if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-141"]="# CHAPTER I\n\nAbout thirty years ago Miss Maria Ward, of Huntingdon, with only seven thousand pounds, had the good luck to captivate Sir Thomas Bertram, of Mansfield Park, in the county of Northampton, and to be thereby raised to the rank of a baronet’s lady, with all the comforts and consequences of an handsome house and large income. All Huntingdon exclaimed on the greatness of the match, and her uncle, the lawyer, himself, allowed her to be at least three thousand pounds short of any equitable claim to it. She had two sisters to be benefited by her elevation; and such of their acquaintance as thought Miss Ward and Miss Frances quite as handsome as Miss Maria, did not scruple to predict their marrying with almost equal advantage. But there certainly are not so many men of large fortune in the world as there are pretty women to deserve them. Miss Ward, at the end of half a dozen years, found herself obliged to be attached to the Rev. Mr. Norris, a friend of her brother-in-law, with scarcely any private fortune, and Miss Frances fared yet worse. Miss Ward’s match, indeed, when it came to the point, was not contemptible: Sir Thomas being happily able to give his friend an income in the living of Mansfield; and Mr. and Mrs. Norris began their career of conjugal felicity with very little less than a thousand a year. But Miss Frances married, in the common phrase, to disoblige her family, and by fixing on a lieutenant of marines, without education, fortune, or connexions, did it very thoroughly. She could hardly have made a more untoward choice. Sir Thomas Bertram had interest, which, from principle as well as pride—from a general wish of doing right, and a desire of seeing all that were connected with him in situations of respectability, he would have been glad to exert for the advantage of Lady Bertram’s sister; but her husband’s profession was such as no interest could reach; and before he had time to devise any other method of assisting them, an absolute breach between the sisters had taken place. It was the natural result of the conduct of each party, and such as a very imprudent marriage almost always produces. To save herself from useless remonstrance, Mrs. Price never wrote to her family on the subject till actually married. Lady Bertram, who was a woman of very tranquil feelings, and a temper remarkably easy and indolent, would have contented herself with merely giving up her sister, and thinking no more of the matter; but Mrs. Norris had a spirit of activity, which could not be satisfied till she had written a long and angry letter to Fanny, to point out the folly of her conduct, and threaten her with all its possible ill consequences. Mrs. Price, in her turn, was injured and angry; and an answer, which comprehended each sister in its bitterness, and bestowed such very disrespectful reflections on the pride of Sir Thomas as Mrs. Norris could not possibly keep to herself, put an end to all intercourse between them for a considerable period.\n\nTheir homes were so distant, and the circles in which they moved so distinct, as almost to preclude the means of ever hearing of each other’s existence during the eleven following years, or, at least, to make it very wonderful to Sir Thomas that Mrs. Norris should ever have it in her power to tell them, as she now and then did, in an angry voice, that Fanny had got another child. By the end of eleven years, however, Mrs. Price could no longer afford to cherish pride or resentment, or to lose one connexion that might possibly assist her. A large and still increasing family, an husband disabled for active service, but not the less equal to company and good liquor, and a very small income to supply their wants, made her eager to regain the friends she had so carelessly sacrificed; and she addressed Lady Bertram in a letter which spoke so much contrition and despondence, such a superfluity of children, and such a want of almost everything else, as could not but dispose them all to a reconciliation. She was preparing for her ninth lying-in; and after bewailing the circumstance, and imploring their countenance as sponsors to the expected child, she could not conceal how important she felt they might be to the future maintenance of the eight already in being. Her eldest was a boy of ten years old, a fine spirited fellow, who longed to be out in the world; but what could she do? Was there any chance of his being hereafter useful to Sir Thomas in the concerns of his West Indian property? No situation would be beneath him; or what did Sir Thomas think of Woolwich? or how could a boy be sent out to the East?\n\nThe letter was not unproductive. It re-established peace and kindness. Sir Thomas sent friendly advice and professions, Lady Bertram dispatched money and baby-linen, and Mrs. Norris wrote the letters.\n\nSuch were its immediate effects, and within a twelvemonth a more important advantage to Mrs. Price resulted from it. Mrs. Norris was often observing to the others that she could not get her poor sister and her family out of her head, and that, much as they had all done for her, she seemed to be wanting to do more; and at length she could not but own it to be her wish that poor Mrs. Price should be relieved from the charge and expense of one child entirely out of her great number. “What if they were among them to undertake the care of her eldest daughter, a girl now nine years old, of an age to require more attention than her poor mother could possibly give? The trouble and expense of it to them would be nothing, compared with the benevolence of the action.” Lady Bertram agreed with her instantly. “I think we cannot do better,” said she; “let us send for the child.”\n\nSir Thomas could not give so instantaneous and unqualified a consent. He debated and hesitated;—it was a serious charge;—a girl so brought up must be adequately provided for, or there would be cruelty instead of kindness in taking her from her family. He thought of his own four children, of his two sons, of cousins in love, etc.;—but no sooner had he deliberately begun to state his objections, than Mrs. Norris interrupted him with a reply to them all, whether stated or not.\n\n“My dear Sir Thomas, I perfectly comprehend you, and do justice to the generosity and delicacy of your notions, which indeed are quite of a piece with your general conduct; and I entirely agree with you in the main as to the propriety of doing everything one could by way of providing for a child one had in a manner taken into one’s own hands; and I am sure I should be the last person in the world to withhold my mite upon such an occasion. Having no children of my own, who should I look to in any little matter I may ever have to bestow, but the children of my sisters?—and I am sure Mr. Norris is too just—but you know I am a woman of few words and professions. Do not let us be frightened from a good deed by a trifle. Give a girl an education, and introduce her properly into the world, and ten to one but she has the means of settling well, without farther expense to anybody. A niece of ours, Sir Thomas, I may say, or at least of yours, would not grow up in this neighbourhood without many advantages. I don’t say she would be so handsome as her cousins. I dare say she would not; but she would be introduced into the society of this country under such very favourable circumstances as, in all human probability, would get her a creditable establishment. You are thinking of your sons—but do not you know that, of all things upon earth, that is the least likely to happen, brought up as they would be, always together like brothers and sisters? It is morally impossible. I never knew an instance of it. It is, in fact, the only sure way of providing against the connexion. Suppose her a pretty girl, and seen by Tom or Edmund for the first time seven years hence, and I dare say there would be mischief. The very idea of her having been suffered to grow up at a distance from us all in poverty and neglect, would be enough to make either of the dear, sweet-tempered boys in love with her. But breed her up with them from this time, and suppose her even to have the beauty of an angel, and she will never be more to either than a sister.”\n\n“There is a great deal of truth in what you say,” replied Sir Thomas, “and far be it from me to throw any fanciful impediment in the way of a plan which would be so consistent with the relative situations of each. I only meant to observe that it ought not to be lightly engaged in, and that to make it really serviceable to Mrs. Price, and creditable to ourselves, we must secure to the child, or consider ourselves engaged to secure to her hereafter, as circumstances may arise, the provision of a gentlewoman, if no such establishment should offer as you are so sanguine in expecting.”\n\n“I thoroughly understand you,” cried Mrs. Norris, “you are everything that is generous and considerate, and I am sure we shall never disagree on this point. Whatever I can do, as you well know, I am always ready enough to do for the good of those I love; and, though I could never feel for this little girl the hundredth part of the regard I bear your own dear children, nor consider her, in any respect, so much my own, I should hate myself if I were capable of neglecting her. Is not she a sister’s child? and could I bear to see her want while I had a bit of bread to give her? My dear Sir Thomas, with all my faults I have a warm heart; and, poor as I am, would rather deny myself the necessaries of life than do an ungenerous thing. So, if you are not against it, I will write to my poor sister tomorrow, and make the proposal; and, as soon as matters are settled, I will engage to get the child to Mansfield; you shall have no trouble about it. My own trouble, you know, I never regard. I will send Nanny to London on purpose, and she may have a bed at her cousin the saddler’s, and the child be appointed to meet her there. They may easily get her from Portsmouth to town by the coach, under the care of any creditable person that may chance to be going. I dare say there is always some reputable tradesman’s wife or other going up.”\n\nExcept to the attack on Nanny’s cousin, Sir Thomas no longer made any objection, and a more respectable, though less economical rendezvous being accordingly substituted, everything was considered as settled, and the pleasures of so benevolent a scheme were already enjoyed. The division of gratifying sensations ought not, in strict justice, to have been equal; for Sir Thomas was fully resolved to be the real and consistent patron of the selected child, and Mrs. Norris had not the least intention of being at any expense whatever in her maintenance. As far as walking, talking, and contriving reached, she was thoroughly benevolent, and nobody knew better how to dictate liberality to others; but her love of money was equal to her love of directing, and she knew quite as well how to save her own as to spend that of her friends. Having married on a narrower income than she had been used to look forward to, she had, from the first, fancied a very strict line of economy necessary; and what was begun as a matter of prudence, soon grew into a matter of choice, as an object of that needful solicitude which there were no children to supply. Had there been a family to provide for, Mrs. Norris might never have saved her money; but having no care of that kind, there was nothing to impede her frugality, or lessen the comfort of making a yearly addition to an income which they had never lived up to. Under this infatuating principle, counteracted by no real affection for her sister, it was impossible for her to aim at more than the credit of projecting and arranging so expensive a charity; though perhaps she might so little know herself as to walk home to the Parsonage, after this conversation, in the happy belief of being the most liberal-minded sister and aunt in the world.\n\nWhen the subject was brought forward again, her views were more fully explained; and, in reply to Lady Bertram’s calm inquiry of “Where shall the child come to first, sister, to you or to us?” Sir Thomas heard with some surprise that it would be totally out of Mrs. Norris’s power to take any share in the personal charge of her. He had been considering her as a particularly welcome addition at the Parsonage, as a desirable companion to an aunt who had no children of her own; but he found himself wholly mistaken. Mrs. Norris was sorry to say that the little girl’s staying with them, at least as things then were, was quite out of the question. Poor Mr. Norris’s indifferent state of health made it an impossibility: he could no more bear the noise of a child than he could fly; if, indeed, he should ever get well of his gouty complaints, it would be a different matter: she should then be glad to take her turn, and think nothing of the inconvenience; but just now, poor Mr. Norris took up every moment of her time, and the very mention of such a thing she was sure would distract him.\n\n“Then she had better come to us,” said Lady Bertram, with the utmost composure. After a short pause Sir Thomas added with dignity, “Yes, let her home be in this house. We will endeavour to do our duty by her, and she will, at least, have the advantage of companions of her own age, and of a regular instructress.”\n\n“Very true,” cried Mrs. Norris, “which are both very important considerations; and it will be just the same to Miss Lee whether she has three girls to teach, or only two—there can be no difference. I only wish I could be more useful; but you see I do all in my power. I am not one of those that spare their own trouble; and Nanny shall fetch her, however it may put me to inconvenience to have my chief counsellor away for three days. I suppose, sister, you will put the child in the little white attic, near the old nurseries. It will be much the best place for her, so near Miss Lee, and not far from the girls, and close by the housemaids, who could either of them help to dress her, you know, and take care of her clothes, for I suppose you would not think it fair to expect Ellis to wait on her as well as the others. Indeed, I do not see that you could possibly place her anywhere else.”\n\nLady Bertram made no opposition.\n\n“I hope she will prove a well-disposed girl,” continued Mrs. Norris, “and be sensible of her uncommon good fortune in having such friends.”\n\n“Should her disposition be really bad,” said Sir Thomas, “we must not, for our own children’s sake, continue her in the family; but there is no reason to expect so great an evil. We shall probably see much to wish altered in her, and must prepare ourselves for gross ignorance, some meanness of opinions, and very distressing vulgarity of manner; but these are not incurable faults; nor, I trust, can they be dangerous for her associates. Had my daughters been younger than herself, I should have considered the introduction of such a companion as a matter of very serious moment; but, as it is, I hope there can be nothing to fear for them, and everything to hope for her, from the association.”\n\n“That is exactly what I think,” cried Mrs. Norris, “and what I was saying to my husband this morning. It will be an education for the child, said I, only being with her cousins; if Miss Lee taught her nothing, she would learn to be good and clever from them.”\n\n“I hope she will not tease my poor pug,” said Lady Bertram; “I have but just got Julia to leave it alone.”\n\n“There will be some difficulty in our way, Mrs. Norris,” observed Sir Thomas, “as to the distinction proper to be made between the girls as they grow up: how to preserve in the minds of my daughters the consciousness of what they are, without making them think too lowly of their cousin; and how, without depressing her spirits too far, to make her remember that she is not a Miss Bertram. I should wish to see them very good friends, and would, on no account, authorise in my girls the smallest degree of arrogance towards their relation; but still they cannot be equals. Their rank, fortune, rights, and expectations will always be different. It is a point of great delicacy, and you must assist us in our endeavours to choose exactly the right line of conduct.”\n\nMrs. Norris was quite at his service; and though she perfectly agreed with him as to its being a most difficult thing, encouraged him to hope that between them it would be easily managed.\n\nIt will be readily believed that Mrs. Norris did not write to her sister in vain. Mrs. Price seemed rather surprised that a girl should be fixed on, when she had so many fine boys, but accepted the offer most thankfully, assuring them of her daughter’s being a very well-disposed, good-humoured girl, and trusting they would never have cause to throw her off. She spoke of her farther as somewhat delicate and puny, but was sanguine in the hope of her being materially better for change of air. Poor woman! she probably thought change of air might agree with many of her children.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\nThe little girl performed her long journey in safety; and at Northampton was met by Mrs. Norris, who thus regaled in the credit of being foremost to welcome her, and in the importance of leading her in to the others, and recommending her to their kindness.\n\nFanny Price was at this time just ten years old, and though there might not be much in her first appearance to captivate, there was, at least, nothing to disgust her relations. She was small of her age, with no glow of complexion, nor any other striking beauty; exceedingly timid and shy, and shrinking from notice; but her air, though awkward, was not vulgar, her voice was sweet, and when she spoke her countenance was pretty. Sir Thomas and Lady Bertram received her very kindly; and Sir Thomas, seeing how much she needed encouragement, tried to be all that was conciliating: but he had to work against a most untoward gravity of deportment; and Lady Bertram, without taking half so much trouble, or speaking one word where he spoke ten, by the mere aid of a good-humoured smile, became immediately the less awful character of the two.\n\nThe young people were all at home, and sustained their share in the introduction very well, with much good humour, and no embarrassment, at least on the part of the sons, who, at seventeen and sixteen, and tall of their age, had all the grandeur of men in the eyes of their little cousin. The two girls were more at a loss from being younger and in greater awe of their father, who addressed them on the occasion with rather an injudicious particularity. But they were too much used to company and praise to have anything like natural shyness; and their confidence increasing from their cousin’s total want of it, they were soon able to take a full survey of her face and her frock in easy indifference.\n\nThey were a remarkably fine family, the sons very well-looking, the daughters decidedly handsome, and all of them well-grown and forward of their age, which produced as striking a difference between the cousins in person, as education had given to their address; and no one would have supposed the girls so nearly of an age as they really were. There were in fact but two years between the youngest and Fanny. Julia Bertram was only twelve, and Maria but a year older. The little visitor meanwhile was as unhappy as possible. Afraid of everybody, ashamed of herself, and longing for the home she had left, she knew not how to look up, and could scarcely speak to be heard, or without crying. Mrs. Norris had been talking to her the whole way from Northampton of her wonderful good fortune, and the extraordinary degree of gratitude and good behaviour which it ought to produce, and her consciousness of misery was therefore increased by the idea of its being a wicked thing for her not to be happy. The fatigue, too, of so long a journey, became soon no trifling evil. In vain were the well-meant condescensions of Sir Thomas, and all the officious prognostications of Mrs. Norris that she would be a good girl; in vain did Lady Bertram smile and make her sit on the sofa with herself and pug, and vain was even the sight of a gooseberry tart towards giving her comfort; she could scarcely swallow two mouthfuls before tears interrupted her, and sleep seeming to be her likeliest friend, she was taken to finish her sorrows in bed.\n\n“This is not a very promising beginning,” said Mrs. Norris, when Fanny had left the room. “After all that I said to her as we came along, I thought she would have behaved better; I told her how much might depend upon her acquitting herself well at first. I wish there may not be a little sulkiness of temper—her poor mother had a good deal; but we must make allowances for such a child—and I do not know that her being sorry to leave her home is really against her, for, with all its faults, it was her home, and she cannot as yet understand how much she has changed for the better; but then there is moderation in all things.”\n\nIt required a longer time, however, than Mrs. Norris was inclined to allow, to reconcile Fanny to the novelty of Mansfield Park, and the separation from everybody she had been used to. Her feelings were very acute, and too little understood to be properly attended to. Nobody meant to be unkind, but nobody put themselves out of their way to secure her comfort.\n\nThe holiday allowed to the Miss Bertrams the next day, on purpose to afford leisure for getting acquainted with, and entertaining their young cousin, produced little union. They could not but hold her cheap on finding that she had but two sashes, and had never learned French; and when they perceived her to be little struck with the duet they were so good as to play, they could do no more than make her a generous present of some of their least valued toys, and leave her to herself, while they adjourned to whatever might be the favourite holiday sport of the moment, making artificial flowers or wasting gold paper.\n\nFanny, whether near or from her cousins, whether in the schoolroom, the drawing-room, or the shrubbery, was equally forlorn, finding something to fear in every person and place. She was disheartened by Lady Bertram’s silence, awed by Sir Thomas’s grave looks, and quite overcome by Mrs. Norris’s admonitions. Her elder cousins mortified her by reflections on her size, and abashed her by noticing her shyness: Miss Lee wondered at her ignorance, and the maid-servants sneered at her clothes; and when to these sorrows was added the idea of the brothers and sisters among whom she had always been important as playfellow, instructress, and nurse, the despondence that sunk her little heart was severe.\n\nThe grandeur of the house astonished, but could not console her. The rooms were too large for her to move in with ease: whatever she touched she expected to injure, and she crept about in constant terror of something or other; often retreating towards her own chamber to cry; and the little girl who was spoken of in the drawing-room when she left it at night as seeming so desirably sensible of her peculiar good fortune, ended every day’s sorrows by sobbing herself to sleep. A week had passed in this way, and no suspicion of it conveyed by her quiet passive manner, when she was found one morning by her cousin Edmund, the youngest of the sons, sitting crying on the attic stairs.\n\n“My dear little cousin,” said he, with all the gentleness of an excellent nature, “what can be the matter?” And sitting down by her, he was at great pains to overcome her shame in being so surprised, and persuade her to speak openly. Was she ill? or was anybody angry with her? or had she quarrelled with Maria and Julia? or was she puzzled about anything in her lesson that he could explain? Did she, in short, want anything he could possibly get her, or do for her? For a long while no answer could be obtained beyond a “no, no—not at all—no, thank you”; but he still persevered; and no sooner had he begun to revert to her own home, than her increased sobs explained to him where the grievance lay. He tried to console her.\n\n“You are sorry to leave Mama, my dear little Fanny,” said he, “which shows you to be a very good girl; but you must remember that you are with relations and friends, who all love you, and wish to make you happy. Let us walk out in the park, and you shall tell me all about your brothers and sisters.”\n\nOn pursuing the subject, he found that, dear as all these brothers and sisters generally were, there was one among them who ran more in her thoughts than the rest. It was William whom she talked of most, and wanted most to see. William, the eldest, a year older than herself, her constant companion and friend; her advocate with her mother (of whom he was the darling) in every distress. “William did not like she should come away; he had told her he should miss her very much indeed.” “But William will write to you, I dare say.” “Yes, he had promised he would, but he had told her to write first.” “And when shall you do it?” She hung her head and answered hesitatingly, “she did not know; she had not any paper.”\n\n“If that be all your difficulty, I will furnish you with paper and every other material, and you may write your letter whenever you choose. Would it make you happy to write to William?”\n\n“Yes, very.”\n\n“Then let it be done now. Come with me into the breakfast-room, we shall find everything there, and be sure of having the room to ourselves.”\n\n“But, cousin, will it go to the post?”\n\n“Yes, depend upon me it shall: it shall go with the other letters; and, as your uncle will frank it, it will cost William nothing.”\n\n“My uncle!” repeated Fanny, with a frightened look.\n\n“Yes, when you have written the letter, I will take it to my father to frank.”\n\nFanny thought it a bold measure, but offered no further resistance; and they went together into the breakfast-room, where Edmund prepared her paper, and ruled her lines with all the goodwill that her brother could himself have felt, and probably with somewhat more exactness. He continued with her the whole time of her writing, to assist her with his penknife or his orthography, as either were wanted; and added to these attentions, which she felt very much, a kindness to her brother which delighted her beyond all the rest. He wrote with his own hand his love to his cousin William, and sent him half a guinea under the seal. Fanny’s feelings on the occasion were such as she believed herself incapable of expressing; but her countenance and a few artless words fully conveyed all their gratitude and delight, and her cousin began to find her an interesting object. He talked to her more, and, from all that she said, was convinced of her having an affectionate heart, and a strong desire of doing right; and he could perceive her to be farther entitled to attention by great sensibility of her situation, and great timidity. He had never knowingly given her pain, but he now felt that she required more positive kindness; and with that view endeavoured, in the first place, to lessen her fears of them all, and gave her especially a great deal of good advice as to playing with Maria and Julia, and being as merry as possible.\n\nFrom this day Fanny grew more comfortable. She felt that she had a friend, and the kindness of her cousin Edmund gave her better spirits with everybody else. The place became less strange, and the people less formidable; and if there were some amongst them whom she could not cease to fear, she began at least to know their ways, and to catch the best manner of conforming to them. The little rusticities and awkwardnesses which had at first made grievous inroads on the tranquillity of all, and not least of herself, necessarily wore away, and she was no longer materially afraid to appear before her uncle, nor did her aunt Norris’s voice make her start very much. To her cousins she became occasionally an acceptable companion. Though unworthy, from inferiority of age and strength, to be their constant associate, their pleasures and schemes were sometimes of a nature to make a third very useful, especially when that third was of an obliging, yielding temper; and they could not but own, when their aunt inquired into her faults, or their brother Edmund urged her claims to their kindness, that “Fanny was good-natured enough.”\n\nEdmund was uniformly kind himself; and she had nothing worse to endure on the part of Tom than that sort of merriment which a young man of seventeen will always think fair with a child of ten. He was just entering into life, full of spirits, and with all the liberal dispositions of an eldest son, who feels born only for expense and enjoyment. His kindness to his little cousin was consistent with his situation and rights: he made her some very pretty presents, and laughed at her.\n\nAs her appearance and spirits improved, Sir Thomas and Mrs. Norris thought with greater satisfaction of their benevolent plan; and it was pretty soon decided between them that, though far from clever, she showed a tractable disposition, and seemed likely to give them little trouble. A mean opinion of her abilities was not confined to them. Fanny could read, work, and write, but she had been taught nothing more; and as her cousins found her ignorant of many things with which they had been long familiar, they thought her prodigiously stupid, and for the first two or three weeks were continually bringing some fresh report of it into the drawing-room. “Dear mama, only think, my cousin cannot put the map of Europe together—or my cousin cannot tell the principal rivers in Russia—or, she never heard of Asia Minor—or she does not know the difference between water-colours and crayons!—How strange!—Did you ever hear anything so stupid?”\n\n“My dear,” their considerate aunt would reply, “it is very bad, but you must not expect everybody to be as forward and quick at learning as yourself.”\n\n“But, aunt, she is really so very ignorant!—Do you know, we asked her last night which way she would go to get to Ireland; and she said, she should cross to the Isle of Wight. She thinks of nothing but the Isle of Wight, and she calls it the Island, as if there were no other island in the world. I am sure I should have been ashamed of myself, if I had not known better long before I was so old as she is. I cannot remember the time when I did not know a great deal that she has not the least notion of yet. How long ago it is, aunt, since we used to repeat the chronological order of the kings of England, with the dates of their accession, and most of the principal events of their reigns!”\n\n“Yes,” added the other; “and of the Roman emperors as low as Severus; besides a great deal of the heathen mythology, and all the metals, semi-metals, planets, and distinguished philosophers.”\n\n“Very true indeed, my dears, but you are blessed with wonderful memories, and your poor cousin has probably none at all. There is a vast deal of difference in memories, as well as in everything else, and therefore you must make allowance for your cousin, and pity her deficiency. And remember that, if you are ever so forward and clever yourselves, you should always be modest; for, much as you know already, there is a great deal more for you to learn.”\n\n“Yes, I know there is, till I am seventeen. But I must tell you another thing of Fanny, so odd and so stupid. Do you know, she says she does not want to learn either music or drawing.”\n\n“To be sure, my dear, that is very stupid indeed, and shows a great want of genius and emulation. But, all things considered, I do not know whether it is not as well that it should be so, for, though you know (owing to me) your papa and mama are so good as to bring her up with you, it is not at all necessary that she should be as accomplished as you are;—on the contrary, it is much more desirable that there should be a difference.”\n\nSuch were the counsels by which Mrs. Norris assisted to form her nieces’ minds; and it is not very wonderful that, with all their promising talents and early information, they should be entirely deficient in the less common acquirements of self-knowledge, generosity and humility. In everything but disposition they were admirably taught. Sir Thomas did not know what was wanting, because, though a truly anxious father, he was not outwardly affectionate, and the reserve of his manner repressed all the flow of their spirits before him.\n\nTo the education of her daughters Lady Bertram paid not the smallest attention. She had not time for such cares. She was a woman who spent her days in sitting, nicely dressed, on a sofa, doing some long piece of needlework, of little use and no beauty, thinking more of her pug than her children, but very indulgent to the latter when it did not put herself to inconvenience, guided in everything important by Sir Thomas, and in smaller concerns by her sister. Had she possessed greater leisure for the service of her girls, she would probably have supposed it unnecessary, for they were under the care of a governess, with proper masters, and could want nothing more. As for Fanny’s being stupid at learning, “she could only say it was very unlucky, but some people were stupid, and Fanny must take more pains: she did not know what else was to be done; and, except her being so dull, she must add she saw no harm in the poor little thing, and always found her very handy and quick in carrying messages, and fetching what she wanted.”\n\nFanny, with all her faults of ignorance and timidity, was fixed at Mansfield Park, and learning to transfer in its favour much of her attachment to her former home, grew up there not unhappily among her cousins. There was no positive ill-nature in Maria or Julia; and though Fanny was often mortified by their treatment of her, she thought too lowly of her own claims to feel injured by it.\n\nFrom about the time of her entering the family, Lady Bertram, in consequence of a little ill-health, and a great deal of indolence, gave up the house in town, which she had been used to occupy every spring, and remained wholly in the country, leaving Sir Thomas to attend his duty in Parliament, with whatever increase or diminution of comfort might arise from her absence. In the country, therefore, the Miss Bertrams continued to exercise their memories, practise their duets, and grow tall and womanly: and their father saw them becoming in person, manner, and accomplishments, everything that could satisfy his anxiety. His eldest son was careless and extravagant, and had already given him much uneasiness; but his other children promised him nothing but good. His daughters, he felt, while they retained the name of Bertram, must be giving it new grace, and in quitting it, he trusted, would extend its respectable alliances; and the character of Edmund, his strong good sense and uprightness of mind, bid most fairly for utility, honour, and happiness to himself and all his connexions. He was to be a clergyman.\n\nAmid the cares and the complacency which his own children suggested, Sir Thomas did not forget to do what he could for the children of Mrs. Price: he assisted her liberally in the education and disposal of her sons as they became old enough for a determinate pursuit; and Fanny, though almost totally separated from her family, was sensible of the truest satisfaction in hearing of any kindness towards them, or of anything at all promising in their situation or conduct. Once, and once only, in the course of many years, had she the happiness of being with William. Of the rest she saw nothing: nobody seemed to think of her ever going amongst them again, even for a visit, nobody at home seemed to want her; but William determining, soon after her removal, to be a sailor, was invited to spend a week with his sister in Northamptonshire before he went to sea. Their eager affection in meeting, their exquisite delight in being together, their hours of happy mirth, and moments of serious conference, may be imagined; as well as the sanguine views and spirits of the boy even to the last, and the misery of the girl when he left her. Luckily the visit happened in the Christmas holidays, when she could directly look for comfort to her cousin Edmund; and he told her such charming things of what William was to do, and be hereafter, in consequence of his profession, as made her gradually admit that the separation might have some use. Edmund’s friendship never failed her: his leaving Eton for Oxford made no change in his kind dispositions, and only afforded more frequent opportunities of proving them. Without any display of doing more than the rest, or any fear of doing too much, he was always true to her interests, and considerate of her feelings, trying to make her good qualities understood, and to conquer the diffidence which prevented their being more apparent; giving her advice, consolation, and encouragement.\n\nKept back as she was by everybody else, his single support could not bring her forward; but his attentions were otherwise of the highest importance in assisting the improvement of her mind, and extending its pleasures. He knew her to be clever, to have a quick apprehension as well as good sense, and a fondness for reading, which, properly directed, must be an education in itself. Miss Lee taught her French, and heard her read the daily portion of history; but he recommended the books which charmed her leisure hours, he encouraged her taste, and corrected her judgment: he made reading useful by talking to her of what she read, and heightened its attraction by judicious praise. In return for such services she loved him better than anybody in the world except William: her heart was divided between the two.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\nThe first event of any importance in the family was the death of Mr. Norris, which happened when Fanny was about fifteen, and necessarily introduced alterations and novelties. Mrs. Norris, on quitting the Parsonage, removed first to the Park, and afterwards to a small house of Sir Thomas’s in the village, and consoled herself for the loss of her husband by considering that she could do very well without him; and for her reduction of income by the evident necessity of stricter economy.\n\nThe living was hereafter for Edmund; and, had his uncle died a few years sooner, it would have been duly given to some friend to hold till he were old enough for orders. But Tom’s extravagance had, previous to that event, been so great as to render a different disposal of the next presentation necessary, and the younger brother must help to pay for the pleasures of the elder. There was another family living actually held for Edmund; but though this circumstance had made the arrangement somewhat easier to Sir Thomas’s conscience, he could not but feel it to be an act of injustice, and he earnestly tried to impress his eldest son with the same conviction, in the hope of its producing a better effect than anything he had yet been able to say or do.\n\n“I blush for you, Tom,” said he, in his most dignified manner; “I blush for the expedient which I am driven on, and I trust I may pity your feelings as a brother on the occasion. You have robbed Edmund for ten, twenty, thirty years, perhaps for life, of more than half the income which ought to be his. It may hereafter be in my power, or in yours (I hope it will), to procure him better preferment; but it must not be forgotten that no benefit of that sort would have been beyond his natural claims on us, and that nothing can, in fact, be an equivalent for the certain advantage which he is now obliged to forego through the urgency of your debts.”\n\nTom listened with some shame and some sorrow; but escaping as quickly as possible, could soon with cheerful selfishness reflect, firstly, that he had not been half so much in debt as some of his friends; secondly, that his father had made a most tiresome piece of work of it; and, thirdly, that the future incumbent, whoever he might be, would, in all probability, die very soon.\n\nOn Mr. Norris’s death the presentation became the right of a Dr. Grant, who came consequently to reside at Mansfield; and on proving to be a hearty man of forty-five, seemed likely to disappoint Mr. Bertram’s calculations. But “no, he was a short-necked, apoplectic sort of fellow, and, plied well with good things, would soon pop off.”\n\nHe had a wife about fifteen years his junior, but no children; and they entered the neighbourhood with the usual fair report of being very respectable, agreeable people.\n\nThe time was now come when Sir Thomas expected his sister-in-law to claim her share in their niece, the change in Mrs. Norris’s situation, and the improvement in Fanny’s age, seeming not merely to do away any former objection to their living together, but even to give it the most decided eligibility; and as his own circumstances were rendered less fair than heretofore, by some recent losses on his West India estate, in addition to his eldest son’s extravagance, it became not undesirable to himself to be relieved from the expense of her support, and the obligation of her future provision. In the fullness of his belief that such a thing must be, he mentioned its probability to his wife; and the first time of the subject’s occurring to her again happening to be when Fanny was present, she calmly observed to her, “So, Fanny, you are going to leave us, and live with my sister. How shall you like it?”\n\nFanny was too much surprised to do more than repeat her aunt’s words, “Going to leave you?”\n\n“Yes, my dear; why should you be astonished? You have been five years with us, and my sister always meant to take you when Mr. Norris died. But you must come up and tack on my patterns all the same.”\n\nThe news was as disagreeable to Fanny as it had been unexpected. She had never received kindness from her aunt Norris, and could not love her.\n\n“I shall be very sorry to go away,” said she, with a faltering voice.\n\n“Yes, I dare say you will; that’s natural enough. I suppose you have had as little to vex you since you came into this house as any creature in the world.”\n\n“I hope I am not ungrateful, aunt,” said Fanny modestly.\n\n“No, my dear; I hope not. I have always found you a very good girl.”\n\n“And am I never to live here again?”\n\n“Never, my dear; but you are sure of a comfortable home. It can make very little difference to you, whether you are in one house or the other.”\n\nFanny left the room with a very sorrowful heart; she could not feel the difference to be so small, she could not think of living with her aunt with anything like satisfaction. As soon as she met with Edmund she told him her distress.\n\n“Cousin,” said she, “something is going to happen which I do not like at all; and though you have often persuaded me into being reconciled to things that I disliked at first, you will not be able to do it now. I am going to live entirely with my aunt Norris.”\n\n“Indeed!”\n\n“Yes; my aunt Bertram has just told me so. It is quite settled. I am to leave Mansfield Park, and go to the White House, I suppose, as soon as she is removed there.”\n\n“Well, Fanny, and if the plan were not unpleasant to you, I should call it an excellent one.”\n\n“Oh, cousin!”\n\n“It has everything else in its favour. My aunt is acting like a sensible woman in wishing for you. She is choosing a friend and companion exactly where she ought, and I am glad her love of money does not interfere. You will be what you ought to be to her. I hope it does not distress you very much, Fanny?”\n\n“Indeed it does: I cannot like it. I love this house and everything in it: I shall love nothing there. You know how uncomfortable I feel with her.”\n\n“I can say nothing for her manner to you as a child; but it was the same with us all, or nearly so. She never knew how to be pleasant to children. But you are now of an age to be treated better; I think she is behaving better already; and when you are her only companion, you must be important to her.”\n\n“I can never be important to any one.”\n\n“What is to prevent you?”\n\n“Everything. My situation, my foolishness and awkwardness.”\n\n“As to your foolishness and awkwardness, my dear Fanny, believe me, you never have a shadow of either, but in using the words so improperly. There is no reason in the world why you should not be important where you are known. You have good sense, and a sweet temper, and I am sure you have a grateful heart, that could never receive kindness without wishing to return it. I do not know any better qualifications for a friend and companion.”\n\n“You are too kind,” said Fanny, colouring at such praise; “how shall I ever thank you as I ought, for thinking so well of me. Oh! cousin, if I am to go away, I shall remember your goodness to the last moment of my life.”\n\n“Why, indeed, Fanny, I should hope to be remembered at such a distance as the White House. You speak as if you were going two hundred miles off instead of only across the park; but you will belong to us almost as much as ever. The two families will be meeting every day in the year. The only difference will be that, living with your aunt, you will necessarily be brought forward as you ought to be. Here there are too many whom you can hide behind; but with her you will be forced to speak for yourself.”\n\n“Oh! do not say so.”\n\n“I must say it, and say it with pleasure. Mrs. Norris is much better fitted than my mother for having the charge of you now. She is of a temper to do a great deal for anybody she really interests herself about, and she will force you to do justice to your natural powers.”\n\nFanny sighed, and said, “I cannot see things as you do; but I ought to believe you to be right rather than myself, and I am very much obliged to you for trying to reconcile me to what must be. If I could suppose my aunt really to care for me, it would be delightful to feel myself of consequence to anybody. Here, I know, I am of none, and yet I love the place so well.”\n\n“The place, Fanny, is what you will not quit, though you quit the house. You will have as free a command of the park and gardens as ever. Even your constant little heart need not take fright at such a nominal change. You will have the same walks to frequent, the same library to choose from, the same people to look at, the same horse to ride.”\n\n“Very true. Yes, dear old grey pony! Ah! cousin, when I remember how much I used to dread riding, what terrors it gave me to hear it talked of as likely to do me good (oh! how I have trembled at my uncle’s opening his lips if horses were talked of), and then think of the kind pains you took to reason and persuade me out of my fears, and convince me that I should like it after a little while, and feel how right you proved to be, I am inclined to hope you may always prophesy as well.”\n\n“And I am quite convinced that your being with Mrs. Norris will be as good for your mind as riding has been for your health, and as much for your ultimate happiness too.”\n\nSo ended their discourse, which, for any very appropriate service it could render Fanny, might as well have been spared, for Mrs. Norris had not the smallest intention of taking her. It had never occurred to her, on the present occasion, but as a thing to be carefully avoided. To prevent its being expected, she had fixed on the smallest habitation which could rank as genteel among the buildings of Mansfield parish, the White House being only just large enough to receive herself and her servants, and allow a spare room for a friend, of which she made a very particular point. The spare rooms at the Parsonage had never been wanted, but the absolute necessity of a spare room for a friend was now never forgotten. Not all her precautions, however, could save her from being suspected of something better; or, perhaps, her very display of the importance of a spare room might have misled Sir Thomas to suppose it really intended for Fanny. Lady Bertram soon brought the matter to a certainty by carelessly observing to Mrs. Norris—\n\n“I think, sister, we need not keep Miss Lee any longer, when Fanny goes to live with you.”\n\nMrs. Norris almost started. “Live with me, dear Lady Bertram! what do you mean?”\n\n“Is she not to live with you? I thought you had settled it with Sir Thomas.”\n\n“Me! never. I never spoke a syllable about it to Sir Thomas, nor he to me. Fanny live with me! the last thing in the world for me to think of, or for anybody to wish that really knows us both. Good heaven! what could I do with Fanny? Me! a poor, helpless, forlorn widow, unfit for anything, my spirits quite broke down; what could I do with a girl at her time of life? A girl of fifteen! the very age of all others to need most attention and care, and put the cheerfullest spirits to the test! Sure Sir Thomas could not seriously expect such a thing! Sir Thomas is too much my friend. Nobody that wishes me well, I am sure, would propose it. How came Sir Thomas to speak to you about it?”\n\n“Indeed, I do not know. I suppose he thought it best.”\n\n“But what did he say? He could not say he wished me to take Fanny. I am sure in his heart he could not wish me to do it.”\n\n“No; he only said he thought it very likely; and I thought so too. We both thought it would be a comfort to you. But if you do not like it, there is no more to be said. She is no encumbrance here.”\n\n“Dear sister, if you consider my unhappy state, how can she be any comfort to me? Here am I, a poor desolate widow, deprived of the best of husbands, my health gone in attending and nursing him, my spirits still worse, all my peace in this world destroyed, with hardly enough to support me in the rank of a gentlewoman, and enable me to live so as not to disgrace the memory of the dear departed—what possible comfort could I have in taking such a charge upon me as Fanny? If I could wish it for my own sake, I would not do so unjust a thing by the poor girl. She is in good hands, and sure of doing well. I must struggle through my sorrows and difficulties as I can.”\n\n“Then you will not mind living by yourself quite alone?”\n\n“Dear Lady Bertram! what am I fit for but solitude? Now and then I shall hope to have a friend in my little cottage (I shall always have a bed for a friend); but the most part of my future days will be spent in utter seclusion. If I can but make both ends meet, that's all I ask for.”\n\n“I hope, sister, things are not so very bad with you neither considering Sir Thomas says you will have six hundred a year.”\n\n“Lady Bertram, I do not complain. I know I cannot live as I have done, but I must retrench where I can, and learn to be a better manager. I have been a liberal housekeeper enough, but I shall not be ashamed to practise economy now. My situation is as much altered as my income. A great many things were due from poor Mr. Norris, as clergyman of the parish, that cannot be expected from me. It is unknown how much was consumed in our kitchen by odd comers and goers. At the White House, matters must be better looked after. I must live within my income, or I shall be miserable; and I own it would give me great satisfaction to be able to do rather more, to lay by a little at the end of the year.”\n\n“I dare say you will. You always do, don’t you?”\n\n“My object, Lady Bertram, is to be of use to those that come after me. It is for your children’s good that I wish to be richer. I have nobody else to care for, but I should be very glad to think I could leave a little trifle among them worth their having.”\n\n“You are very good, but do not trouble yourself about them. They are sure of being well provided for. Sir Thomas will take care of that.”\n\n“Why, you know, Sir Thomas’s means will be rather straitened if the Antigua estate is to make such poor returns.”\n\n“Oh! that will soon be settled. Sir Thomas has been writing about it, I know.”\n\n“Well, Lady Bertram,” said Mrs. Norris, moving to go, “I can only say that my sole desire is to be of use to your family: and so, if Sir Thomas should ever speak again about my taking Fanny, you will be able to say that my health and spirits put it quite out of the question; besides that, I really should not have a bed to give her, for I must keep a spare room for a friend.”\n\nLady Bertram repeated enough of this conversation to her husband to convince him how much he had mistaken his sister-in-law’s views; and she was from that moment perfectly safe from all expectation, or the slightest allusion to it from him. He could not but wonder at her refusing to do anything for a niece whom she had been so forward to adopt; but, as she took early care to make him, as well as Lady Bertram, understand that whatever she possessed was designed for their family, he soon grew reconciled to a distinction which, at the same time that it was advantageous and complimentary to them, would enable him better to provide for Fanny himself.\n\nFanny soon learnt how unnecessary had been her fears of a removal; and her spontaneous, untaught felicity on the discovery, conveyed some consolation to Edmund for his disappointment in what he had expected to be so essentially serviceable to her. Mrs. Norris took possession of the White House, the Grants arrived at the Parsonage, and these events over, everything at Mansfield went on for some time as usual.\n\nThe Grants showing a disposition to be friendly and sociable, gave great satisfaction in the main among their new acquaintance. They had their faults, and Mrs. Norris soon found them out. The Doctor was very fond of eating, and would have a good dinner every day; and Mrs. Grant, instead of contriving to gratify him at little expense, gave her cook as high wages as they did at Mansfield Park, and was scarcely ever seen in her offices. Mrs. Norris could not speak with any temper of such grievances, nor of the quantity of butter and eggs that were regularly consumed in the house. “Nobody loved plenty and hospitality more than herself; nobody more hated pitiful doings; the Parsonage, she believed, had never been wanting in comforts of any sort, had never borne a bad character in her time, but this was a way of going on that she could not understand. A fine lady in a country parsonage was quite out of place. Her store-room, she thought, might have been good enough for Mrs. Grant to go into. Inquire where she would, she could not find out that Mrs. Grant had ever had more than five thousand pounds.”\n\nLady Bertram listened without much interest to this sort of invective. She could not enter into the wrongs of an economist, but she felt all the injuries of beauty in Mrs. Grant’s being so well settled in life without being handsome, and expressed her astonishment on that point almost as often, though not so diffusely, as Mrs. Norris discussed the other.\n\nThese opinions had been hardly canvassed a year before another event arose of such importance in the family, as might fairly claim some place in the thoughts and conversation of the ladies. Sir Thomas found it expedient to go to Antigua himself, for the better arrangement of his affairs, and he took his eldest son with him, in the hope of detaching him from some bad connexions at home. They left England with the probability of being nearly a twelvemonth absent.\n\nThe necessity of the measure in a pecuniary light, and the hope of its utility to his son, reconciled Sir Thomas to the effort of quitting the rest of his family, and of leaving his daughters to the direction of others at their present most interesting time of life. He could not think Lady Bertram quite equal to supply his place with them, or rather, to perform what should have been her own; but, in Mrs. Norris’s watchful attention, and in Edmund’s judgment, he had sufficient confidence to make him go without fears for their conduct.\n\nLady Bertram did not at all like to have her husband leave her; but she was not disturbed by any alarm for his safety, or solicitude for his comfort, being one of those persons who think nothing can be dangerous, or difficult, or fatiguing to anybody but themselves.\n\nThe Miss Bertrams were much to be pitied on the occasion: not for their sorrow, but for their want of it. Their father was no object of love to them; he had never seemed the friend of their pleasures, and his absence was unhappily most welcome. They were relieved by it from all restraint; and without aiming at one gratification that would probably have been forbidden by Sir Thomas, they felt themselves immediately at their own disposal, and to have every indulgence within their reach. Fanny’s relief, and her consciousness of it, were quite equal to her cousins’; but a more tender nature suggested that her feelings were ungrateful, and she really grieved because she could not grieve. “Sir Thomas, who had done so much for her and her brothers, and who was gone perhaps never to return! that she should see him go without a tear! it was a shameful insensibility.” He had said to her, moreover, on the very last morning, that he hoped she might see William again in the course of the ensuing winter, and had charged her to write and invite him to Mansfield as soon as the squadron to which he belonged should be known to be in England. “This was so thoughtful and kind!” and would he only have smiled upon her, and called her “my dear Fanny,” while he said it, every former frown or cold address might have been forgotten. But he had ended his speech in a way to sink her in sad mortification, by adding, “If William does come to Mansfield, I hope you may be able to convince him that the many years which have passed since you parted have not been spent on your side entirely without improvement; though, I fear, he must find his sister at sixteen in some respects too much like his sister at ten.” She cried bitterly over this reflection when her uncle was gone; and her cousins, on seeing her with red eyes, set her down as a hypocrite.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\nTom Bertram had of late spent so little of his time at home that he could be only nominally missed; and Lady Bertram was soon astonished to find how very well they did even without his father, how well Edmund could supply his place in carving, talking to the steward, writing to the attorney, settling with the servants, and equally saving her from all possible fatigue or exertion in every particular but that of directing her letters.\n\nThe earliest intelligence of the travellers’ safe arrival at Antigua, after a favourable voyage, was received; though not before Mrs. Norris had been indulging in very dreadful fears, and trying to make Edmund participate them whenever she could get him alone; and as she depended on being the first person made acquainted with any fatal catastrophe, she had already arranged the manner of breaking it to all the others, when Sir Thomas’s assurances of their both being alive and well made it necessary to lay by her agitation and affectionate preparatory speeches for a while.\n\nThe winter came and passed without their being called for; the accounts continued perfectly good; and Mrs. Norris, in promoting gaieties for her nieces, assisting their toilets, displaying their accomplishments, and looking about for their future husbands, had so much to do as, in addition to all her own household cares, some interference in those of her sister, and Mrs. Grant’s wasteful doings to overlook, left her very little occasion to be occupied in fears for the absent.\n\nThe Miss Bertrams were now fully established among the belles of the neighbourhood; and as they joined to beauty and brilliant acquirements a manner naturally easy, and carefully formed to general civility and obligingness, they possessed its favour as well as its admiration. Their vanity was in such good order that they seemed to be quite free from it, and gave themselves no airs; while the praises attending such behaviour, secured and brought round by their aunt, served to strengthen them in believing they had no faults.\n\nLady Bertram did not go into public with her daughters. She was too indolent even to accept a mother’s gratification in witnessing their success and enjoyment at the expense of any personal trouble, and the charge was made over to her sister, who desired nothing better than a post of such honourable representation, and very thoroughly relished the means it afforded her of mixing in society without having horses to hire.\n\nFanny had no share in the festivities of the season; but she enjoyed being avowedly useful as her aunt’s companion when they called away the rest of the family; and, as Miss Lee had left Mansfield, she naturally became everything to Lady Bertram during the night of a ball or a party. She talked to her, listened to her, read to her; and the tranquillity of such evenings, her perfect security in such a tête-à-tête from any sound of unkindness, was unspeakably welcome to a mind which had seldom known a pause in its alarms or embarrassments. As to her cousins’ gaieties, she loved to hear an account of them, especially of the balls, and whom Edmund had danced with; but thought too lowly of her own situation to imagine she should ever be admitted to the same, and listened, therefore, without an idea of any nearer concern in them. Upon the whole, it was a comfortable winter to her; for though it brought no William to England, the never-failing hope of his arrival was worth much.\n\nThe ensuing spring deprived her of her valued friend, the old grey pony; and for some time she was in danger of feeling the loss in her health as well as in her affections; for in spite of the acknowledged importance of her riding on horse-back, no measures were taken for mounting her again, “because,” as it was observed by her aunts, “she might ride one of her cousin’s horses at any time when they did not want them,” and as the Miss Bertrams regularly wanted their horses every fine day, and had no idea of carrying their obliging manners to the sacrifice of any real pleasure, that time, of course, never came. They took their cheerful rides in the fine mornings of April and May; and Fanny either sat at home the whole day with one aunt, or walked beyond her strength at the instigation of the other: Lady Bertram holding exercise to be as unnecessary for everybody as it was unpleasant to herself; and Mrs. Norris, who was walking all day, thinking everybody ought to walk as much. Edmund was absent at this time, or the evil would have been earlier remedied. When he returned, to understand how Fanny was situated, and perceived its ill effects, there seemed with him but one thing to be done; and that “Fanny must have a horse” was the resolute declaration with which he opposed whatever could be urged by the supineness of his mother, or the economy of his aunt, to make it appear unimportant. Mrs. Norris could not help thinking that some steady old thing might be found among the numbers belonging to the Park that would do vastly well; or that one might be borrowed of the steward; or that perhaps Dr. Grant might now and then lend them the pony he sent to the post. She could not but consider it as absolutely unnecessary, and even improper, that Fanny should have a regular lady’s horse of her own, in the style of her cousins. She was sure Sir Thomas had never intended it: and she must say that, to be making such a purchase in his absence, and adding to the great expenses of his stable, at a time when a large part of his income was unsettled, seemed to her very unjustifiable. “Fanny must have a horse,” was Edmund’s only reply. Mrs. Norris could not see it in the same light. Lady Bertram did: she entirely agreed with her son as to the necessity of it, and as to its being considered necessary by his father; she only pleaded against there being any hurry; she only wanted him to wait till Sir Thomas’s return, and then Sir Thomas might settle it all himself. He would be at home in September, and where would be the harm of only waiting till September?\n\nThough Edmund was much more displeased with his aunt than with his mother, as evincing least regard for her niece, he could not help paying more attention to what she said; and at length determined on a method of proceeding which would obviate the risk of his father’s thinking he had done too much, and at the same time procure for Fanny the immediate means of exercise, which he could not bear she should be without. He had three horses of his own, but not one that would carry a woman. Two of them were hunters; the third, a useful road-horse: this third he resolved to exchange for one that his cousin might ride; he knew where such a one was to be met with; and having once made up his mind, the whole business was soon completed. The new mare proved a treasure; with a very little trouble she became exactly calculated for the purpose, and Fanny was then put in almost full possession of her. She had not supposed before that anything could ever suit her like the old grey pony; but her delight in Edmund’s mare was far beyond any former pleasure of the sort; and the addition it was ever receiving in the consideration of that kindness from which her pleasure sprung, was beyond all her words to express. She regarded her cousin as an example of everything good and great, as possessing worth which no one but herself could ever appreciate, and as entitled to such gratitude from her as no feelings could be strong enough to pay. Her sentiments towards him were compounded of all that was respectful, grateful, confiding, and tender.\n\nAs the horse continued in name, as well as fact, the property of Edmund, Mrs. Norris could tolerate its being for Fanny’s use; and had Lady Bertram ever thought about her own objection again, he might have been excused in her eyes for not waiting till Sir Thomas’s return in September, for when September came Sir Thomas was still abroad, and without any near prospect of finishing his business. Unfavourable circumstances had suddenly arisen at a moment when he was beginning to turn all his thoughts towards England; and the very great uncertainty in which everything was then involved determined him on sending home his son, and waiting the final arrangement by himself. Tom arrived safely, bringing an excellent account of his father’s health; but to very little purpose, as far as Mrs. Norris was concerned. Sir Thomas’s sending away his son seemed to her so like a parent’s care, under the influence of a foreboding of evil to himself, that she could not help feeling dreadful presentiments; and as the long evenings of autumn came on, was so terribly haunted by these ideas, in the sad solitariness of her cottage, as to be obliged to take daily refuge in the dining-room of the Park. The return of winter engagements, however, was not without its effect; and in the course of their progress, her mind became so pleasantly occupied in superintending the fortunes of her eldest niece, as tolerably to quiet her nerves. “If poor Sir Thomas were fated never to return, it would be peculiarly consoling to see their dear Maria well married,” she very often thought; always when they were in the company of men of fortune, and particularly on the introduction of a young man who had recently succeeded to one of the largest estates and finest places in the country.\n\nMr. Rushworth was from the first struck with the beauty of Miss Bertram, and, being inclined to marry, soon fancied himself in love. He was a heavy young man, with not more than common sense; but as there was nothing disagreeable in his figure or address, the young lady was well pleased with her conquest. Being now in her twenty-first year, Maria Bertram was beginning to think matrimony a duty; and as a marriage with Mr. Rushworth would give her the enjoyment of a larger income than her father’s, as well as ensure her the house in town, which was now a prime object, it became, by the same rule of moral obligation, her evident duty to marry Mr. Rushworth if she could. Mrs. Norris was most zealous in promoting the match, by every suggestion and contrivance likely to enhance its desirableness to either party; and, among other means, by seeking an intimacy with the gentleman’s mother, who at present lived with him, and to whom she even forced Lady Bertram to go through ten miles of indifferent road to pay a morning visit. It was not long before a good understanding took place between this lady and herself. Mrs. Rushworth acknowledged herself very desirous that her son should marry, and declared that of all the young ladies she had ever seen, Miss Bertram seemed, by her amiable qualities and accomplishments, the best adapted to make him happy. Mrs. Norris accepted the compliment, and admired the nice discernment of character which could so well distinguish merit. Maria was indeed the pride and delight of them all—perfectly faultless—an angel; and, of course, so surrounded by admirers, must be difficult in her choice: but yet, as far as Mrs. Norris could allow herself to decide on so short an acquaintance, Mr. Rushworth appeared precisely the young man to deserve and attach her.\n\nAfter dancing with each other at a proper number of balls, the young people justified these opinions, and an engagement, with a due reference to the absent Sir Thomas, was entered into, much to the satisfaction of their respective families, and of the general lookers-on of the neighbourhood, who had, for many weeks past, felt the expediency of Mr. Rushworth’s marrying Miss Bertram.\n\nIt was some months before Sir Thomas’s consent could be received; but, in the meanwhile, as no one felt a doubt of his most cordial pleasure in the connexion, the intercourse of the two families was carried on without restraint, and no other attempt made at secrecy than Mrs. Norris’s talking of it everywhere as a matter not to be talked of at present.\n\nEdmund was the only one of the family who could see a fault in the business; but no representation of his aunt’s could induce him to find Mr. Rushworth a desirable companion. He could allow his sister to be the best judge of her own happiness, but he was not pleased that her happiness should centre in a large income; nor could he refrain from often saying to himself, in Mr. Rushworth’s company—“If this man had not twelve thousand a year, he would be a very stupid fellow.”\n\nSir Thomas, however, was truly happy in the prospect of an alliance so unquestionably advantageous, and of which he heard nothing but the perfectly good and agreeable. It was a connexion exactly of the right sort—in the same county, and the same interest—and his most hearty concurrence was conveyed as soon as possible. He only conditioned that the marriage should not take place before his return, which he was again looking eagerly forward to. He wrote in April, and had strong hopes of settling everything to his entire satisfaction, and leaving Antigua before the end of the summer.\n\nSuch was the state of affairs in the month of July; and Fanny had just reached her eighteenth year, when the society of the village received an addition in the brother and sister of Mrs. Grant, a Mr. and Miss Crawford, the children of her mother by a second marriage. They were young people of fortune. The son had a good estate in Norfolk, the daughter twenty thousand pounds. As children, their sister had been always very fond of them; but, as her own marriage had been soon followed by the death of their common parent, which left them to the care of a brother of their father, of whom Mrs. Grant knew nothing, she had scarcely seen them since. In their uncle’s house they had found a kind home. Admiral and Mrs. Crawford, though agreeing in nothing else, were united in affection for these children, or, at least, were no farther adverse in their feelings than that each had their favourite, to whom they showed the greatest fondness of the two. The Admiral delighted in the boy, Mrs. Crawford doted on the girl; and it was the lady’s death which now obliged her protegee, after some months’ further trial at her uncle’s house, to find another home. Admiral Crawford was a man of vicious conduct, who chose, instead of retaining his niece, to bring his mistress under his own roof; and to this Mrs. Grant was indebted for her sister’s proposal of coming to her, a measure quite as welcome on one side as it could be expedient on the other; for Mrs. Grant, having by this time run through the usual resources of ladies residing in the country without a family of children—having more than filled her favourite sitting-room with pretty furniture, and made a choice collection of plants and poultry—was very much in want of some variety at home. The arrival, therefore, of a sister whom she had always loved, and now hoped to retain with her as long as she remained single, was highly agreeable; and her chief anxiety was lest Mansfield should not satisfy the habits of a young woman who had been mostly used to London.\n\nMiss Crawford was not entirely free from similar apprehensions, though they arose principally from doubts of her sister’s style of living and tone of society; and it was not till after she had tried in vain to persuade her brother to settle with her at his own country house, that she could resolve to hazard herself among her other relations. To anything like a permanence of abode, or limitation of society, Henry Crawford had, unluckily, a great dislike: he could not accommodate his sister in an article of such importance; but he escorted her, with the utmost kindness, into Northamptonshire, and as readily engaged to fetch her away again, at half an hour’s notice, whenever she were weary of the place.\n\nThe meeting was very satisfactory on each side. Miss Crawford found a sister without preciseness or rusticity, a sister’s husband who looked the gentleman, and a house commodious and well fitted up; and Mrs. Grant received in those whom she hoped to love better than ever a young man and woman of very prepossessing appearance. Mary Crawford was remarkably pretty; Henry, though not handsome, had air and countenance; the manners of both were lively and pleasant, and Mrs. Grant immediately gave them credit for everything else. She was delighted with each, but Mary was her dearest object; and having never been able to glory in beauty of her own, she thoroughly enjoyed the power of being proud of her sister’s. She had not waited her arrival to look out for a suitable match for her: she had fixed on Tom Bertram; the eldest son of a baronet was not too good for a girl of twenty thousand pounds, with all the elegance and accomplishments which Mrs. Grant foresaw in her; and being a warm-hearted, unreserved woman, Mary had not been three hours in the house before she told her what she had planned.\n\nMiss Crawford was glad to find a family of such consequence so very near them, and not at all displeased either at her sister’s early care, or the choice it had fallen on. Matrimony was her object, provided she could marry well: and having seen Mr. Bertram in town, she knew that objection could no more be made to his person than to his situation in life. While she treated it as a joke, therefore, she did not forget to think of it seriously. The scheme was soon repeated to Henry.\n\n“And now,” added Mrs. Grant, “I have thought of something to make it complete. I should dearly love to settle you both in this country; and therefore, Henry, you shall marry the youngest Miss Bertram, a nice, handsome, good-humoured, accomplished girl, who will make you very happy.”\n\nHenry bowed and thanked her.\n\n“My dear sister,” said Mary, “if you can persuade him into anything of the sort, it will be a fresh matter of delight to me to find myself allied to anybody so clever, and I shall only regret that you have not half a dozen daughters to dispose of. If you can persuade Henry to marry, you must have the address of a Frenchwoman. All that English abilities can do has been tried already. I have three very particular friends who have been all dying for him in their turn; and the pains which they, their mothers (very clever women), as well as my dear aunt and myself, have taken to reason, coax, or trick him into marrying, is inconceivable! He is the most horrible flirt that can be imagined. If your Miss Bertrams do not like to have their hearts broke, let them avoid Henry.”\n\n“My dear brother, I will not believe this of you.”\n\n“No, I am sure you are too good. You will be kinder than Mary. You will allow for the doubts of youth and inexperience. I am of a cautious temper, and unwilling to risk my happiness in a hurry. Nobody can think more highly of the matrimonial state than myself. I consider the blessing of a wife as most justly described in those discreet lines of the poet—‘Heaven’s last best gift.’”\n\n“There, Mrs. Grant, you see how he dwells on one word, and only look at his smile. I assure you he is very detestable; the Admiral’s lessons have quite spoiled him.”\n\n“I pay very little regard,” said Mrs. Grant, “to what any young person says on the subject of marriage. If they profess a disinclination for it, I only set it down that they have not yet seen the right person.”\n\nDr. Grant laughingly congratulated Miss Crawford on feeling no disinclination to the state herself.\n\n“Oh yes! I am not at all ashamed of it. I would have everybody marry if they can do it properly: I do not like to have people throw themselves away; but everybody should marry as soon as they can do it to advantage.”\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\nThe young people were pleased with each other from the first. On each side there was much to attract, and their acquaintance soon promised as early an intimacy as good manners would warrant. Miss Crawford’s beauty did her no disservice with the Miss Bertrams. They were too handsome themselves to dislike any woman for being so too, and were almost as much charmed as their brothers with her lively dark eye, clear brown complexion, and general prettiness. Had she been tall, full formed, and fair, it might have been more of a trial: but as it was, there could be no comparison; and she was most allowably a sweet, pretty girl, while they were the finest young women in the country.\n\nHer brother was not handsome: no, when they first saw him he was absolutely plain, black and plain; but still he was the gentleman, with a pleasing address. The second meeting proved him not so very plain: he was plain, to be sure, but then he had so much countenance, and his teeth were so good, and he was so well made, that one soon forgot he was plain; and after a third interview, after dining in company with him at the Parsonage, he was no longer allowed to be called so by anybody. He was, in fact, the most agreeable young man the sisters had ever known, and they were equally delighted with him. Miss Bertram’s engagement made him in equity the property of Julia, of which Julia was fully aware; and before he had been at Mansfield a week, she was quite ready to be fallen in love with.\n\nMaria’s notions on the subject were more confused and indistinct. She did not want to see or understand. “There could be no harm in her liking an agreeable man—everybody knew her situation—Mr. Crawford must take care of himself.” Mr. Crawford did not mean to be in any danger! the Miss Bertrams were worth pleasing, and were ready to be pleased; and he began with no object but of making them like him. He did not want them to die of love; but with sense and temper which ought to have made him judge and feel better, he allowed himself great latitude on such points.\n\n“I like your Miss Bertrams exceedingly, sister,” said he, as he returned from attending them to their carriage after the said dinner visit; “they are very elegant, agreeable girls.”\n\n“So they are indeed, and I am delighted to hear you say it. But you like Julia best.”\n\n“Oh yes! I like Julia best.”\n\n“But do you really? for Miss Bertram is in general thought the handsomest.”\n\n“So I should suppose. She has the advantage in every feature, and I prefer her countenance; but I like Julia best; Miss Bertram is certainly the handsomest, and I have found her the most agreeable, but I shall always like Julia best, because you order me.”\n\n“I shall not talk to you, Henry, but I know you will like her best at last.”\n\n“Do not I tell you that I like her best at first?”\n\n“And besides, Miss Bertram is engaged. Remember that, my dear brother. Her choice is made.”\n\n“Yes, and I like her the better for it. An engaged woman is always more agreeable than a disengaged. She is satisfied with herself. Her cares are over, and she feels that she may exert all her powers of pleasing without suspicion. All is safe with a lady engaged: no harm can be done.”\n\n“Why, as to that, Mr. Rushworth is a very good sort of young man, and it is a great match for her.”\n\n“But Miss Bertram does not care three straws for him; that is your opinion of your intimate friend. I do not subscribe to it. I am sure Miss Bertram is very much attached to Mr. Rushworth. I could see it in her eyes, when he was mentioned. I think too well of Miss Bertram to suppose she would ever give her hand without her heart.”\n\n“Mary, how shall we manage him?”\n\n“We must leave him to himself, I believe. Talking does no good. He will be taken in at last.”\n\n“But I would not have him taken in; I would not have him duped; I would have it all fair and honourable.”\n\n“Oh dear! let him stand his chance and be taken in. It will do just as well. Everybody is taken in at some period or other.”\n\n“Not always in marriage, dear Mary.”\n\n“In marriage especially. With all due respect to such of the present company as chance to be married, my dear Mrs. Grant, there is not one in a hundred of either sex who is not taken in when they marry. Look where I will, I see that it is so; and I feel that it must be so, when I consider that it is, of all transactions, the one in which people expect most from others, and are least honest themselves.”\n\n“Ah! You have been in a bad school for matrimony, in Hill Street.”\n\n“My poor aunt had certainly little cause to love the state; but, however, speaking from my own observation, it is a manoeuvring business. I know so many who have married in the full expectation and confidence of some one particular advantage in the connexion, or accomplishment, or good quality in the person, who have found themselves entirely deceived, and been obliged to put up with exactly the reverse. What is this but a take in?”\n\n“My dear child, there must be a little imagination here. I beg your pardon, but I cannot quite believe you. Depend upon it, you see but half. You see the evil, but you do not see the consolation. There will be little rubs and disappointments everywhere, and we are all apt to expect too much; but then, if one scheme of happiness fails, human nature turns to another; if the first calculation is wrong, we make a second better: we find comfort somewhere—and those evil-minded observers, dearest Mary, who make much of a little, are more taken in and deceived than the parties themselves.”\n\n“Well done, sister! I honour your esprit du corps. When I am a wife, I mean to be just as staunch myself; and I wish my friends in general would be so too. It would save me many a heartache.”\n\n“You are as bad as your brother, Mary; but we will cure you both. Mansfield shall cure you both, and without any taking in. Stay with us, and we will cure you.”\n\nThe Crawfords, without wanting to be cured, were very willing to stay. Mary was satisfied with the Parsonage as a present home, and Henry equally ready to lengthen his visit. He had come, intending to spend only a few days with them; but Mansfield promised well, and there was nothing to call him elsewhere. It delighted Mrs. Grant to keep them both with her, and Dr. Grant was exceedingly well contented to have it so: a talking pretty young woman like Miss Crawford is always pleasant society to an indolent, stay-at-home man; and Mr. Crawford’s being his guest was an excuse for drinking claret every day.\n\nThe Miss Bertrams’ admiration of Mr. Crawford was more rapturous than anything which Miss Crawford’s habits made her likely to feel. She acknowledged, however, that the Mr. Bertrams were very fine young men, that two such young men were not often seen together even in London, and that their manners, particularly those of the eldest, were very good. He had been much in London, and had more liveliness and gallantry than Edmund, and must, therefore, be preferred; and, indeed, his being the eldest was another strong claim. She had felt an early presentiment that she should like the eldest best. She knew it was her way.\n\nTom Bertram must have been thought pleasant, indeed, at any rate; he was the sort of young man to be generally liked, his agreeableness was of the kind to be oftener found agreeable than some endowments of a higher stamp, for he had easy manners, excellent spirits, a large acquaintance, and a great deal to say; and the reversion of Mansfield Park, and a baronetcy, did no harm to all this. Miss Crawford soon felt that he and his situation might do. She looked about her with due consideration, and found almost everything in his favour: a park, a real park, five miles round, a spacious modern-built house, so well placed and well screened as to deserve to be in any collection of engravings of gentlemen’s seats in the kingdom, and wanting only to be completely new furnished—pleasant sisters, a quiet mother, and an agreeable man himself—with the advantage of being tied up from much gaming at present by a promise to his father, and of being Sir Thomas hereafter. It might do very well; she believed she should accept him; and she began accordingly to interest herself a little about the horse which he had to run at the B—— races.\n\nThese races were to call him away not long after their acquaintance began; and as it appeared that the family did not, from his usual goings on, expect him back again for many weeks, it would bring his passion to an early proof. Much was said on his side to induce her to attend the races, and schemes were made for a large party to them, with all the eagerness of inclination, but it would only do to be talked of.\n\nAnd Fanny, what was she doing and thinking all this while? and what was her opinion of the newcomers? Few young ladies of eighteen could be less called on to speak their opinion than Fanny. In a quiet way, very little attended to, she paid her tribute of admiration to Miss Crawford’s beauty; but as she still continued to think Mr. Crawford very plain, in spite of her two cousins having repeatedly proved the contrary, she never mentioned him. The notice, which she excited herself, was to this effect. “I begin now to understand you all, except Miss Price,” said Miss Crawford, as she was walking with the Mr. Bertrams. “Pray, is she out, or is she not? I am puzzled. She dined at the Parsonage, with the rest of you, which seemed like being out; and yet she says so little, that I can hardly suppose she is.”\n\nEdmund, to whom this was chiefly addressed, replied, “I believe I know what you mean, but I will not undertake to answer the question. My cousin is grown up. She has the age and sense of a woman, but the outs and not outs are beyond me.”\n\n“And yet, in general, nothing can be more easily ascertained. The distinction is so broad. Manners as well as appearance are, generally speaking, so totally different. Till now, I could not have supposed it possible to be mistaken as to a girl’s being out or not. A girl not out has always the same sort of dress: a close bonnet, for instance; looks very demure, and never says a word. You may smile, but it is so, I assure you; and except that it is sometimes carried a little too far, it is all very proper. Girls should be quiet and modest. The most objectionable part is, that the alteration of manners on being introduced into company is frequently too sudden. They sometimes pass in such very little time from reserve to quite the opposite—to confidence! That is the faulty part of the present system. One does not like to see a girl of eighteen or nineteen so immediately up to every thing—and perhaps when one has seen her hardly able to speak the year before. Mr. Bertram, I dare say you have sometimes met with such changes.”\n\n“I believe I have, but this is hardly fair; I see what you are at. You are quizzing me and Miss Anderson.”\n\n“No, indeed. Miss Anderson! I do not know who or what you mean. I am quite in the dark. But I will quiz you with a great deal of pleasure, if you will tell me what about.”\n\n“Ah! you carry it off very well, but I cannot be quite so far imposed on. You must have had Miss Anderson in your eye, in describing an altered young lady. You paint too accurately for mistake. It was exactly so. The Andersons of Baker Street. We were speaking of them the other day, you know. Edmund, you have heard me mention Charles Anderson. The circumstance was precisely as this lady has represented it. When Anderson first introduced me to his family, about two years ago, his sister was not out, and I could not get her to speak to me. I sat there an hour one morning waiting for Anderson, with only her and a little girl or two in the room, the governess being sick or run away, and the mother in and out every moment with letters of business, and I could hardly get a word or a look from the young lady—nothing like a civil answer—she screwed up her mouth, and turned from me with such an air! I did not see her again for a twelvemonth. She was then out. I met her at Mrs. Holford’s, and did not recollect her. She came up to me, claimed me as an acquaintance, stared me out of countenance; and talked and laughed till I did not know which way to look. I felt that I must be the jest of the room at the time, and Miss Crawford, it is plain, has heard the story.”\n\n“And a very pretty story it is, and with more truth in it, I dare say, than does credit to Miss Anderson. It is too common a fault. Mothers certainly have not yet got quite the right way of managing their daughters. I do not know where the error lies. I do not pretend to set people right, but I do see that they are often wrong.”\n\n“Those who are showing the world what female manners should be,” said Mr. Bertram gallantly, “are doing a great deal to set them right.”\n\n“The error is plain enough,” said the less courteous Edmund; “such girls are ill brought up. They are given wrong notions from the beginning. They are always acting upon motives of vanity, and there is no more real modesty in their behaviour before they appear in public than afterwards.”\n\n“I do not know,” replied Miss Crawford hesitatingly. “Yes, I cannot agree with you there. It is certainly the modestest part of the business. It is much worse to have girls not out give themselves the same airs and take the same liberties as if they were, which I have seen done. That is worse than anything—quite disgusting!”\n\n“Yes, that is very inconvenient indeed,” said Mr. Bertram. “It leads one astray; one does not know what to do. The close bonnet and demure air you describe so well (and nothing was ever juster), tell one what is expected; but I got into a dreadful scrape last year from the want of them. I went down to Ramsgate for a week with a friend last September, just after my return from the West Indies. My friend Sneyd—you have heard me speak of Sneyd, Edmund—his father, and mother, and sisters, were there, all new to me. When we reached Albion Place they were out; we went after them, and found them on the pier: Mrs. and the two Miss Sneyds, with others of their acquaintance. I made my bow in form; and as Mrs. Sneyd was surrounded by men, attached myself to one of her daughters, walked by her side all the way home, and made myself as agreeable as I could; the young lady perfectly easy in her manners, and as ready to talk as to listen. I had not a suspicion that I could be doing anything wrong. They looked just the same: both well-dressed, with veils and parasols like other girls; but I afterwards found that I had been giving all my attention to the youngest, who was not out, and had most excessively offended the eldest. Miss Augusta ought not to have been noticed for the next six months; and Miss Sneyd, I believe, has never forgiven me.”\n\n“That was bad indeed. Poor Miss Sneyd. Though I have no younger sister, I feel for her. To be neglected before one’s time must be very vexatious; but it was entirely the mother’s fault. Miss Augusta should have been with her governess. Such half-and-half doings never prosper. But now I must be satisfied about Miss Price. Does she go to balls? Does she dine out every where, as well as at my sister’s?”\n\n“No,” replied Edmund; “I do not think she has ever been to a ball. My mother seldom goes into company herself, and dines nowhere but with Mrs. Grant, and Fanny stays at home with her.”\n\n“Oh! then the point is clear. Miss Price is not out.”\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\nMr. Bertram set off for————, and Miss Crawford was prepared to find a great chasm in their society, and to miss him decidedly in the meetings which were now becoming almost daily between the families; and on their all dining together at the Park soon after his going, she retook her chosen place near the bottom of the table, fully expecting to feel a most melancholy difference in the change of masters. It would be a very flat business, she was sure. In comparison with his brother, Edmund would have nothing to say. The soup would be sent round in a most spiritless manner, wine drank without any smiles or agreeable trifling, and the venison cut up without supplying one pleasant anecdote of any former haunch, or a single entertaining story, about “my friend such a one.” She must try to find amusement in what was passing at the upper end of the table, and in observing Mr. Rushworth, who was now making his appearance at Mansfield for the first time since the Crawfords’ arrival. He had been visiting a friend in the neighbouring county, and that friend having recently had his grounds laid out by an improver, Mr. Rushworth was returned with his head full of the subject, and very eager to be improving his own place in the same way; and though not saying much to the purpose, could talk of nothing else. The subject had been already handled in the drawing-room; it was revived in the dining-parlour. Miss Bertram’s attention and opinion was evidently his chief aim; and though her deportment showed rather conscious superiority than any solicitude to oblige him, the mention of Sotherton Court, and the ideas attached to it, gave her a feeling of complacency, which prevented her from being very ungracious.\n\n“I wish you could see Compton,” said he; “it is the most complete thing! I never saw a place so altered in my life. I told Smith I did not know where I was. The approach now, is one of the finest things in the country: you see the house in the most surprising manner. I declare, when I got back to Sotherton yesterday, it looked like a prison—quite a dismal old prison.”\n\n“Oh, for shame!” cried Mrs. Norris. “A prison indeed? Sotherton Court is the noblest old place in the world.”\n\n“It wants improvement, ma’am, beyond anything. I never saw a place that wanted so much improvement in my life; and it is so forlorn that I do not know what can be done with it.”\n\n“No wonder that Mr. Rushworth should think so at present,” said Mrs. Grant to Mrs. Norris, with a smile; “but depend upon it, Sotherton will have every improvement in time which his heart can desire.”\n\n“I must try to do something with it,” said Mr. Rushworth, “but I do not know what. I hope I shall have some good friend to help me.”\n\n“Your best friend upon such an occasion,” said Miss Bertram calmly, “would be Mr. Repton, I imagine.”\n\n“That is what I was thinking of. As he has done so well by Smith, I think I had better have him at once. His terms are five guineas a day.”\n\n“Well, and if they were ten,” cried Mrs. Norris, “I am sure you need not regard it. The expense need not be any impediment. If I were you, I should not think of the expense. I would have everything done in the best style, and made as nice as possible. Such a place as Sotherton Court deserves everything that taste and money can do. You have space to work upon there, and grounds that will well reward you. For my own part, if I had anything within the fiftieth part of the size of Sotherton, I should be always planting and improving, for naturally I am excessively fond of it. It would be too ridiculous for me to attempt anything where I am now, with my little half acre. It would be quite a burlesque. But if I had more room, I should take a prodigious delight in improving and planting. We did a vast deal in that way at the Parsonage: we made it quite a different place from what it was when we first had it. You young ones do not remember much about it, perhaps; but if dear Sir Thomas were here, he could tell you what improvements we made: and a great deal more would have been done, but for poor Mr. Norris’s sad state of health. He could hardly ever get out, poor man, to enjoy anything, and that disheartened me from doing several things that Sir Thomas and I used to talk of. If it had not been for that, we should have carried on the garden wall, and made the plantation to shut out the churchyard, just as Dr. Grant has done. We were always doing something as it was. It was only the spring twelvemonth before Mr. Norris’s death that we put in the apricot against the stable wall, which is now grown such a noble tree, and getting to such perfection, sir,” addressing herself then to Dr. Grant.\n\n“The tree thrives well, beyond a doubt, madam,” replied Dr. Grant. “The soil is good; and I never pass it without regretting that the fruit should be so little worth the trouble of gathering.”\n\n“Sir, it is a Moor Park, we bought it as a Moor Park, and it cost us—that is, it was a present from Sir Thomas, but I saw the bill—and I know it cost seven shillings, and was charged as a Moor Park.”\n\n“You were imposed on, ma’am,” replied Dr. Grant: “these potatoes have as much the flavour of a Moor Park apricot as the fruit from that tree. It is an insipid fruit at the best; but a good apricot is eatable, which none from my garden are.”\n\n“The truth is, ma’am,” said Mrs. Grant, pretending to whisper across the table to Mrs. Norris, “that Dr. Grant hardly knows what the natural taste of our apricot is: he is scarcely ever indulged with one, for it is so valuable a fruit; with a little assistance, and ours is such a remarkably large, fair sort, that what with early tarts and preserves, my cook contrives to get them all.”\n\nMrs. Norris, who had begun to redden, was appeased; and, for a little while, other subjects took place of the improvements of Sotherton. Dr. Grant and Mrs. Norris were seldom good friends; their acquaintance had begun in dilapidations, and their habits were totally dissimilar.\n\nAfter a short interruption Mr. Rushworth began again. “Smith’s place is the admiration of all the country; and it was a mere nothing before Repton took it in hand. I think I shall have Repton.”\n\n“Mr. Rushworth,” said Lady Bertram, “if I were you, I would have a very pretty shrubbery. One likes to get out into a shrubbery in fine weather.”\n\nMr. Rushworth was eager to assure her ladyship of his acquiescence, and tried to make out something complimentary; but, between his submission to her taste, and his having always intended the same himself, with the superadded objects of professing attention to the comfort of ladies in general, and of insinuating that there was one only whom he was anxious to please, he grew puzzled, and Edmund was glad to put an end to his speech by a proposal of wine. Mr. Rushworth, however, though not usually a great talker, had still more to say on the subject next his heart. “Smith has not much above a hundred acres altogether in his grounds, which is little enough, and makes it more surprising that the place can have been so improved. Now, at Sotherton we have a good seven hundred, without reckoning the water meadows; so that I think, if so much could be done at Compton, we need not despair. There have been two or three fine old trees cut down, that grew too near the house, and it opens the prospect amazingly, which makes me think that Repton, or anybody of that sort, would certainly have the avenue at Sotherton down: the avenue that leads from the west front to the top of the hill, you know,” turning to Miss Bertram particularly as he spoke. But Miss Bertram thought it most becoming to reply—\n\n“The avenue! Oh! I do not recollect it. I really know very little of Sotherton.”\n\nFanny, who was sitting on the other side of Edmund, exactly opposite Miss Crawford, and who had been attentively listening, now looked at him, and said in a low voice—\n\n“Cut down an avenue! What a pity! Does it not make you think of Cowper? ‘Ye fallen avenues, once more I mourn your fate unmerited.’”\n\nHe smiled as he answered, “I am afraid the avenue stands a bad chance, Fanny.”\n\n“I should like to see Sotherton before it is cut down, to see the place as it is now, in its old state; but I do not suppose I shall.”\n\n“Have you never been there? No, you never can; and, unluckily, it is out of distance for a ride. I wish we could contrive it.”\n\n“Oh! it does not signify. Whenever I do see it, you will tell me how it has been altered.”\n\n“I collect,” said Miss Crawford, “that Sotherton is an old place, and a place of some grandeur. In any particular style of building?”\n\n“The house was built in Elizabeth’s time, and is a large, regular, brick building; heavy, but respectable looking, and has many good rooms. It is ill placed. It stands in one of the lowest spots of the park; in that respect, unfavourable for improvement. But the woods are fine, and there is a stream, which, I dare say, might be made a good deal of. Mr. Rushworth is quite right, I think, in meaning to give it a modern dress, and I have no doubt that it will be all done extremely well.”\n\nMiss Crawford listened with submission, and said to herself, “He is a well-bred man; he makes the best of it.”\n\n“I do not wish to influence Mr. Rushworth,” he continued; “but, had I a place to new fashion, I should not put myself into the hands of an improver. I would rather have an inferior degree of beauty, of my own choice, and acquired progressively. I would rather abide by my own blunders than by his.”\n\n“You would know what you were about, of course; but that would not suit me. I have no eye or ingenuity for such matters, but as they are before me; and had I a place of my own in the country, I should be most thankful to any Mr. Repton who would undertake it, and give me as much beauty as he could for my money; and I should never look at it till it was complete.”\n\n“It would be delightful to me to see the progress of it all,” said Fanny.\n\n“Ay, you have been brought up to it. It was no part of my education; and the only dose I ever had, being administered by not the first favourite in the world, has made me consider improvements in hand as the greatest of nuisances. Three years ago the Admiral, my honoured uncle, bought a cottage at Twickenham for us all to spend our summers in; and my aunt and I went down to it quite in raptures; but it being excessively pretty, it was soon found necessary to be improved, and for three months we were all dirt and confusion, without a gravel walk to step on, or a bench fit for use. I would have everything as complete as possible in the country, shrubberies and flower-gardens, and rustic seats innumerable: but it must all be done without my care. Henry is different; he loves to be doing.”\n\nEdmund was sorry to hear Miss Crawford, whom he was much disposed to admire, speak so freely of her uncle. It did not suit his sense of propriety, and he was silenced, till induced by further smiles and liveliness to put the matter by for the present.\n\n“Mr. Bertram,” said she, “I have tidings of my harp at last. I am assured that it is safe at Northampton; and there it has probably been these ten days, in spite of the solemn assurances we have so often received to the contrary.” Edmund expressed his pleasure and surprise. “The truth is, that our inquiries were too direct; we sent a servant, we went ourselves: this will not do seventy miles from London; but this morning we heard of it in the right way. It was seen by some farmer, and he told the miller, and the miller told the butcher, and the butcher’s son-in-law left word at the shop.”\n\n“I am very glad that you have heard of it, by whatever means, and hope there will be no further delay.”\n\n“I am to have it to-morrow; but how do you think it is to be conveyed? Not by a wagon or cart: oh no! nothing of that kind could be hired in the village. I might as well have asked for porters and a handbarrow.”\n\n“You would find it difficult, I dare say, just now, in the middle of a very late hay harvest, to hire a horse and cart?”\n\n“I was astonished to find what a piece of work was made of it! To want a horse and cart in the country seemed impossible, so I told my maid to speak for one directly; and as I cannot look out of my dressing-closet without seeing one farmyard, nor walk in the shrubbery without passing another, I thought it would be only ask and have, and was rather grieved that I could not give the advantage to all. Guess my surprise, when I found that I had been asking the most unreasonable, most impossible thing in the world; had offended all the farmers, all the labourers, all the hay in the parish! As for Dr. Grant’s bailiff, I believe I had better keep out of his way; and my brother-in-law himself, who is all kindness in general, looked rather black upon me when he found what I had been at.”\n\n“You could not be expected to have thought on the subject before; but when you do think of it, you must see the importance of getting in the grass. The hire of a cart at any time might not be so easy as you suppose: our farmers are not in the habit of letting them out; but, in harvest, it must be quite out of their power to spare a horse.”\n\n“I shall understand all your ways in time; but, coming down with the true London maxim, that everything is to be got with money, I was a little embarrassed at first by the sturdy independence of your country customs. However, I am to have my harp fetched to-morrow. Henry, who is good-nature itself, has offered to fetch it in his barouche. Will it not be honourably conveyed?”\n\nEdmund spoke of the harp as his favourite instrument, and hoped to be soon allowed to hear her. Fanny had never heard the harp at all, and wished for it very much.\n\n“I shall be most happy to play to you both,” said Miss Crawford; “at least as long as you can like to listen: probably much longer, for I dearly love music myself, and where the natural taste is equal the player must always be best off, for she is gratified in more ways than one. Now, Mr. Bertram, if you write to your brother, I entreat you to tell him that my harp is come: he heard so much of my misery about it. And you may say, if you please, that I shall prepare my most plaintive airs against his return, in compassion to his feelings, as I know his horse will lose.”\n\n“If I write, I will say whatever you wish me; but I do not, at present, foresee any occasion for writing.”\n\n“No, I dare say, nor if he were to be gone a twelvemonth, would you ever write to him, nor he to you, if it could be helped. The occasion would never be foreseen. What strange creatures brothers are! You would not write to each other but upon the most urgent necessity in the world; and when obliged to take up the pen to say that such a horse is ill, or such a relation dead, it is done in the fewest possible words. You have but one style among you. I know it perfectly. Henry, who is in every other respect exactly what a brother should be, who loves me, consults me, confides in me, and will talk to me by the hour together, has never yet turned the page in a letter; and very often it is nothing more than—‘Dear Mary, I am just arrived. Bath seems full, and everything as usual. Yours sincerely.’ That is the true manly style; that is a complete brother’s letter.”\n\n“When they are at a distance from all their family,” said Fanny, colouring for William’s sake, “they can write long letters.”\n\n“Miss Price has a brother at sea,” said Edmund, “whose excellence as a correspondent makes her think you too severe upon us.”\n\n“At sea, has she? In the king’s service, of course?”\n\nFanny would rather have had Edmund tell the story, but his determined silence obliged her to relate her brother’s situation: her voice was animated in speaking of his profession, and the foreign stations he had been on; but she could not mention the number of years that he had been absent without tears in her eyes. Miss Crawford civilly wished him an early promotion.\n\n“Do you know anything of my cousin’s captain?” said Edmund; “Captain Marshall? You have a large acquaintance in the navy, I conclude?”\n\n“Among admirals, large enough; but,” with an air of grandeur, “we know very little of the inferior ranks. Post-captains may be very good sort of men, but they do not belong to us. Of various admirals I could tell you a great deal: of them and their flags, and the gradation of their pay, and their bickerings and jealousies. But, in general, I can assure you that they are all passed over, and all very ill used. Certainly, my home at my uncle’s brought me acquainted with a circle of admirals. Of Rears and Vices I saw enough. Now do not be suspecting me of a pun, I entreat.”\n\nEdmund again felt grave, and only replied, “It is a noble profession.”\n\n“Yes, the profession is well enough under two circumstances: if it make the fortune, and there be discretion in spending it; but, in short, it is not a favourite profession of mine. It has never worn an amiable form to me.”\n\nEdmund reverted to the harp, and was again very happy in the prospect of hearing her play.\n\nThe subject of improving grounds, meanwhile, was still under consideration among the others; and Mrs. Grant could not help addressing her brother, though it was calling his attention from Miss Julia Bertram.\n\n“My dear Henry, have you nothing to say? You have been an improver yourself, and from what I hear of Everingham, it may vie with any place in England. Its natural beauties, I am sure, are great. Everingham, as it used to be, was perfect in my estimation: such a happy fall of ground, and such timber! What would I not give to see it again!”\n\n“Nothing could be so gratifying to me as to hear your opinion of it,” was his answer; “but I fear there would be some disappointment: you would not find it equal to your present ideas. In extent, it is a mere nothing; you would be surprised at its insignificance; and, as for improvement, there was very little for me to do—too little: I should like to have been busy much longer.”\n\n“You are fond of the sort of thing?” said Julia.\n\n“Excessively; but what with the natural advantages of the ground, which pointed out, even to a very young eye, what little remained to be done, and my own consequent resolutions, I had not been of age three months before Everingham was all that it is now. My plan was laid at Westminster, a little altered, perhaps, at Cambridge, and at one-and-twenty executed. I am inclined to envy Mr. Rushworth for having so much happiness yet before him. I have been a devourer of my own.”\n\n“Those who see quickly, will resolve quickly, and act quickly,” said Julia. “You can never want employment. Instead of envying Mr. Rushworth, you should assist him with your opinion.”\n\nMrs. Grant, hearing the latter part of this speech, enforced it warmly, persuaded that no judgment could be equal to her brother’s; and as Miss Bertram caught at the idea likewise, and gave it her full support, declaring that, in her opinion, it was infinitely better to consult with friends and disinterested advisers, than immediately to throw the business into the hands of a professional man, Mr. Rushworth was very ready to request the favour of Mr. Crawford’s assistance; and Mr. Crawford, after properly depreciating his own abilities, was quite at his service in any way that could be useful. Mr. Rushworth then began to propose Mr. Crawford’s doing him the honour of coming over to Sotherton, and taking a bed there; when Mrs. Norris, as if reading in her two nieces’ minds their little approbation of a plan which was to take Mr. Crawford away, interposed with an amendment.\n\n“There can be no doubt of Mr. Crawford’s willingness; but why should not more of us go? Why should not we make a little party? Here are many that would be interested in your improvements, my dear Mr. Rushworth, and that would like to hear Mr. Crawford’s opinion on the spot, and that might be of some small use to you with their opinions; and, for my own part, I have been long wishing to wait upon your good mother again; nothing but having no horses of my own could have made me so remiss; but now I could go and sit a few hours with Mrs. Rushworth, while the rest of you walked about and settled things, and then we could all return to a late dinner here, or dine at Sotherton, just as might be most agreeable to your mother, and have a pleasant drive home by moonlight. I dare say Mr. Crawford would take my two nieces and me in his barouche, and Edmund can go on horseback, you know, sister, and Fanny will stay at home with you.”\n\nLady Bertram made no objection; and every one concerned in the going was forward in expressing their ready concurrence, excepting Edmund, who heard it all and said nothing.\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\n“Well, Fanny, and how do you like Miss Crawford now?” said Edmund the next day, after thinking some time on the subject himself. “How did you like her yesterday?”\n\n“Very well—very much. I like to hear her talk. She entertains me; and she is so extremely pretty, that I have great pleasure in looking at her.”\n\n“It is her countenance that is so attractive. She has a wonderful play of feature! But was there nothing in her conversation that struck you, Fanny, as not quite right?”\n\n“Oh yes! she ought not to have spoken of her uncle as she did. I was quite astonished. An uncle with whom she has been living so many years, and who, whatever his faults may be, is so very fond of her brother, treating him, they say, quite like a son. I could not have believed it!”\n\n“I thought you would be struck. It was very wrong; very indecorous.”\n\n“And very ungrateful, I think.”\n\n“Ungrateful is a strong word. I do not know that her uncle has any claim to her gratitude; his wife certainly had; and it is the warmth of her respect for her aunt’s memory which misleads her here. She is awkwardly circumstanced. With such warm feelings and lively spirits it must be difficult to do justice to her affection for Mrs. Crawford, without throwing a shade on the Admiral. I do not pretend to know which was most to blame in their disagreements, though the Admiral’s present conduct might incline one to the side of his wife; but it is natural and amiable that Miss Crawford should acquit her aunt entirely. I do not censure her opinions; but there certainly is impropriety in making them public.”\n\n“Do not you think,” said Fanny, after a little consideration, “that this impropriety is a reflection itself upon Mrs. Crawford, as her niece has been entirely brought up by her? She cannot have given her right notions of what was due to the Admiral.”\n\n“That is a fair remark. Yes, we must suppose the faults of the niece to have been those of the aunt; and it makes one more sensible of the disadvantages she has been under. But I think her present home must do her good. Mrs. Grant’s manners are just what they ought to be. She speaks of her brother with a very pleasing affection.”\n\n“Yes, except as to his writing her such short letters. She made me almost laugh; but I cannot rate so very highly the love or good-nature of a brother who will not give himself the trouble of writing anything worth reading to his sisters, when they are separated. I am sure William would never have used me so, under any circumstances. And what right had she to suppose that you would not write long letters when you were absent?”\n\n“The right of a lively mind, Fanny, seizing whatever may contribute to its own amusement or that of others; perfectly allowable, when untinctured by ill-humour or roughness; and there is not a shadow of either in the countenance or manner of Miss Crawford: nothing sharp, or loud, or coarse. She is perfectly feminine, except in the instances we have been speaking of. There she cannot be justified. I am glad you saw it all as I did.”\n\nHaving formed her mind and gained her affections, he had a good chance of her thinking like him; though at this period, and on this subject, there began now to be some danger of dissimilarity, for he was in a line of admiration of Miss Crawford, which might lead him where Fanny could not follow. Miss Crawford’s attractions did not lessen. The harp arrived, and rather added to her beauty, wit, and good-humour; for she played with the greatest obligingness, with an expression and taste which were peculiarly becoming, and there was something clever to be said at the close of every air. Edmund was at the Parsonage every day, to be indulged with his favourite instrument: one morning secured an invitation for the next; for the lady could not be unwilling to have a listener, and every thing was soon in a fair train.\n\nA young woman, pretty, lively, with a harp as elegant as herself, and both placed near a window, cut down to the ground, and opening on a little lawn, surrounded by shrubs in the rich foliage of summer, was enough to catch any man’s heart. The season, the scene, the air, were all favourable to tenderness and sentiment. Mrs. Grant and her tambour frame were not without their use: it was all in harmony; and as everything will turn to account when love is once set going, even the sandwich tray, and Dr. Grant doing the honours of it, were worth looking at. Without studying the business, however, or knowing what he was about, Edmund was beginning, at the end of a week of such intercourse, to be a good deal in love; and to the credit of the lady it may be added that, without his being a man of the world or an elder brother, without any of the arts of flattery or the gaieties of small talk, he began to be agreeable to her. She felt it to be so, though she had not foreseen, and could hardly understand it; for he was not pleasant by any common rule: he talked no nonsense; he paid no compliments; his opinions were unbending, his attentions tranquil and simple. There was a charm, perhaps, in his sincerity, his steadiness, his integrity, which Miss Crawford might be equal to feel, though not equal to discuss with herself. She did not think very much about it, however: he pleased her for the present; she liked to have him near her; it was enough.\n\nFanny could not wonder that Edmund was at the Parsonage every morning; she would gladly have been there too, might she have gone in uninvited and unnoticed, to hear the harp; neither could she wonder that, when the evening stroll was over, and the two families parted again, he should think it right to attend Mrs. Grant and her sister to their home, while Mr. Crawford was devoted to the ladies of the Park; but she thought it a very bad exchange; and if Edmund were not there to mix the wine and water for her, would rather go without it than not. She was a little surprised that he could spend so many hours with Miss Crawford, and not see more of the sort of fault which he had already observed, and of which she was almost always reminded by a something of the same nature whenever she was in her company; but so it was. Edmund was fond of speaking to her of Miss Crawford, but he seemed to think it enough that the Admiral had since been spared; and she scrupled to point out her own remarks to him, lest it should appear like ill-nature. The first actual pain which Miss Crawford occasioned her was the consequence of an inclination to learn to ride, which the former caught, soon after her being settled at Mansfield, from the example of the young ladies at the Park, and which, when Edmund’s acquaintance with her increased, led to his encouraging the wish, and the offer of his own quiet mare for the purpose of her first attempts, as the best fitted for a beginner that either stable could furnish. No pain, no injury, however, was designed by him to his cousin in this offer: she was not to lose a day’s exercise by it. The mare was only to be taken down to the Parsonage half an hour before her ride were to begin; and Fanny, on its being first proposed, so far from feeling slighted, was almost over-powered with gratitude that he should be asking her leave for it.\n\nMiss Crawford made her first essay with great credit to herself, and no inconvenience to Fanny. Edmund, who had taken down the mare and presided at the whole, returned with it in excellent time, before either Fanny or the steady old coachman, who always attended her when she rode without her cousins, were ready to set forward. The second day’s trial was not so guiltless. Miss Crawford’s enjoyment of riding was such that she did not know how to leave off. Active and fearless, and though rather small, strongly made, she seemed formed for a horsewoman; and to the pure genuine pleasure of the exercise, something was probably added in Edmund’s attendance and instructions, and something more in the conviction of very much surpassing her sex in general by her early progress, to make her unwilling to dismount. Fanny was ready and waiting, and Mrs. Norris was beginning to scold her for not being gone, and still no horse was announced, no Edmund appeared. To avoid her aunt, and look for him, she went out.\n\nThe houses, though scarcely half a mile apart, were not within sight of each other; but, by walking fifty yards from the hall door, she could look down the park, and command a view of the Parsonage and all its demesnes, gently rising beyond the village road; and in Dr. Grant’s meadow she immediately saw the group—Edmund and Miss Crawford both on horse-back, riding side by side, Dr. and Mrs. Grant, and Mr. Crawford, with two or three grooms, standing about and looking on. A happy party it appeared to her, all interested in one object: cheerful beyond a doubt, for the sound of merriment ascended even to her. It was a sound which did not make her cheerful; she wondered that Edmund should forget her, and felt a pang. She could not turn her eyes from the meadow; she could not help watching all that passed. At first Miss Crawford and her companion made the circuit of the field, which was not small, at a foot’s pace; then, at her apparent suggestion, they rose into a canter; and to Fanny’s timid nature it was most astonishing to see how well she sat. After a few minutes they stopped entirely. Edmund was close to her; he was speaking to her; he was evidently directing her management of the bridle; he had hold of her hand; she saw it, or the imagination supplied what the eye could not reach. She must not wonder at all this; what could be more natural than that Edmund should be making himself useful, and proving his good-nature by any one? She could not but think, indeed, that Mr. Crawford might as well have saved him the trouble; that it would have been particularly proper and becoming in a brother to have done it himself; but Mr. Crawford, with all his boasted good-nature, and all his coachmanship, probably knew nothing of the matter, and had no active kindness in comparison of Edmund. She began to think it rather hard upon the mare to have such double duty; if she were forgotten, the poor mare should be remembered.\n\nHer feelings for one and the other were soon a little tranquillised by seeing the party in the meadow disperse, and Miss Crawford still on horseback, but attended by Edmund on foot, pass through a gate into the lane, and so into the park, and make towards the spot where she stood. She began then to be afraid of appearing rude and impatient; and walked to meet them with a great anxiety to avoid the suspicion.\n\n“My dear Miss Price,” said Miss Crawford, as soon as she was at all within hearing, “I am come to make my own apologies for keeping you waiting; but I have nothing in the world to say for myself—I knew it was very late, and that I was behaving extremely ill; and therefore, if you please, you must forgive me. Selfishness must always be forgiven, you know, because there is no hope of a cure.”\n\nFanny’s answer was extremely civil, and Edmund added his conviction that she could be in no hurry. “For there is more than time enough for my cousin to ride twice as far as she ever goes,” said he, “and you have been promoting her comfort by preventing her from setting off half an hour sooner: clouds are now coming up, and she will not suffer from the heat as she would have done then. I wish you may not be fatigued by so much exercise. I wish you had saved yourself this walk home.”\n\n“No part of it fatigues me but getting off this horse, I assure you,” said she, as she sprang down with his help; “I am very strong. Nothing ever fatigues me but doing what I do not like. Miss Price, I give way to you with a very bad grace; but I sincerely hope you will have a pleasant ride, and that I may have nothing but good to hear of this dear, delightful, beautiful animal.”\n\nThe old coachman, who had been waiting about with his own horse, now joining them, Fanny was lifted on hers, and they set off across another part of the park; her feelings of discomfort not lightened by seeing, as she looked back, that the others were walking down the hill together to the village; nor did her attendant do her much good by his comments on Miss Crawford’s great cleverness as a horse-woman, which he had been watching with an interest almost equal to her own.\n\n“It is a pleasure to see a lady with such a good heart for riding!” said he. “I never see one sit a horse better. She did not seem to have a thought of fear. Very different from you, miss, when you first began, six years ago come next Easter. Lord bless you! how you did tremble when Sir Thomas first had you put on!”\n\nIn the drawing-room Miss Crawford was also celebrated. Her merit in being gifted by Nature with strength and courage was fully appreciated by the Miss Bertrams; her delight in riding was like their own; her early excellence in it was like their own, and they had great pleasure in praising it.\n\n“I was sure she would ride well,” said Julia; “she has the make for it. Her figure is as neat as her brother’s.”\n\n“Yes,” added Maria, “and her spirits are as good, and she has the same energy of character. I cannot but think that good horsemanship has a great deal to do with the mind.”\n\nWhen they parted at night Edmund asked Fanny whether she meant to ride the next day.\n\n“No, I do not know—not if you want the mare,” was her answer.\n\n“I do not want her at all for myself,” said he; “but whenever you are next inclined to stay at home, I think Miss Crawford would be glad to have her a longer time—for a whole morning, in short. She has a great desire to get as far as Mansfield Common: Mrs. Grant has been telling her of its fine views, and I have no doubt of her being perfectly equal to it. But any morning will do for this. She would be extremely sorry to interfere with you. It would be very wrong if she did. She rides only for pleasure; you for health.”\n\n“I shall not ride to-morrow, certainly,” said Fanny; “I have been out very often lately, and would rather stay at home. You know I am strong enough now to walk very well.”\n\nEdmund looked pleased, which must be Fanny’s comfort, and the ride to Mansfield Common took place the next morning: the party included all the young people but herself, and was much enjoyed at the time, and doubly enjoyed again in the evening discussion. A successful scheme of this sort generally brings on another; and the having been to Mansfield Common disposed them all for going somewhere else the day after. There were many other views to be shewn; and though the weather was hot, there were shady lanes wherever they wanted to go. A young party is always provided with a shady lane. Four fine mornings successively were spent in this manner, in shewing the Crawfords the country, and doing the honours of its finest spots. Everything answered; it was all gaiety and good-humour, the heat only supplying inconvenience enough to be talked of with pleasure—till the fourth day, when the happiness of one of the party was exceedingly clouded. Miss Bertram was the one. Edmund and Julia were invited to dine at the Parsonage, and she was excluded. It was meant and done by Mrs. Grant, with perfect good-humour, on Mr. Rushworth’s account, who was partly expected at the Park that day; but it was felt as a very grievous injury, and her good manners were severely taxed to conceal her vexation and anger till she reached home. As Mr. Rushworth did not come, the injury was increased, and she had not even the relief of shewing her power over him; she could only be sullen to her mother, aunt, and cousin, and throw as great a gloom as possible over their dinner and dessert.\n\nBetween ten and eleven Edmund and Julia walked into the drawing-room, fresh with the evening air, glowing and cheerful, the very reverse of what they found in the three ladies sitting there, for Maria would scarcely raise her eyes from her book, and Lady Bertram was half-asleep; and even Mrs. Norris, discomposed by her niece’s ill-humour, and having asked one or two questions about the dinner, which were not immediately attended to, seemed almost determined to say no more. For a few minutes the brother and sister were too eager in their praise of the night and their remarks on the stars, to think beyond themselves; but when the first pause came, Edmund, looking around, said, “But where is Fanny? Is she gone to bed?”\n\n“No, not that I know of,” replied Mrs. Norris; “she was here a moment ago.”\n\nHer own gentle voice speaking from the other end of the room, which was a very long one, told them that she was on the sofa. Mrs. Norris began scolding.\n\n“That is a very foolish trick, Fanny, to be idling away all the evening upon a sofa. Why cannot you come and sit here, and employ yourself as we do? If you have no work of your own, I can supply you from the poor basket. There is all the new calico, that was bought last week, not touched yet. I am sure I almost broke my back by cutting it out. You should learn to think of other people; and, take my word for it, it is a shocking trick for a young person to be always lolling upon a sofa.”\n\nBefore half this was said, Fanny was returned to her seat at the table, and had taken up her work again; and Julia, who was in high good-humour, from the pleasures of the day, did her the justice of exclaiming, “I must say, ma’am, that Fanny is as little upon the sofa as anybody in the house.”\n\n“Fanny,” said Edmund, after looking at her attentively, “I am sure you have the headache.”\n\nShe could not deny it, but said it was not very bad.\n\n“I can hardly believe you,” he replied; “I know your looks too well. How long have you had it?”\n\n“Since a little before dinner. It is nothing but the heat.”\n\n“Did you go out in the heat?”\n\n“Go out! to be sure she did,” said Mrs. Norris: “would you have her stay within such a fine day as this? Were not we all out? Even your mother was out to-day for above an hour.”\n\n“Yes, indeed, Edmund,” added her ladyship, who had been thoroughly awakened by Mrs. Norris’s sharp reprimand to Fanny; “I was out above an hour. I sat three-quarters of an hour in the flower-garden, while Fanny cut the roses; and very pleasant it was, I assure you, but very hot. It was shady enough in the alcove, but I declare I quite dreaded the coming home again.”\n\n“Fanny has been cutting roses, has she?”\n\n“Yes, and I am afraid they will be the last this year. Poor thing! She found it hot enough; but they were so full-blown that one could not wait.”\n\n“There was no help for it, certainly,” rejoined Mrs. Norris, in a rather softened voice; “but I question whether her headache might not be caught then, sister. There is nothing so likely to give it as standing and stooping in a hot sun; but I dare say it will be well to-morrow. Suppose you let her have your aromatic vinegar; I always forget to have mine filled.”\n\n“She has got it,” said Lady Bertram; “she has had it ever since she came back from your house the second time.”\n\n“What!” cried Edmund; “has she been walking as well as cutting roses; walking across the hot park to your house, and doing it twice, ma’am? No wonder her head aches.”\n\nMrs. Norris was talking to Julia, and did not hear.\n\n“I was afraid it would be too much for her,” said Lady Bertram; “but when the roses were gathered, your aunt wished to have them, and then you know they must be taken home.”\n\n“But were there roses enough to oblige her to go twice?”\n\n“No; but they were to be put into the spare room to dry; and, unluckily, Fanny forgot to lock the door of the room and bring away the key, so she was obliged to go again.”\n\nEdmund got up and walked about the room, saying, “And could nobody be employed on such an errand but Fanny? Upon my word, ma’am, it has been a very ill-managed business.”\n\n“I am sure I do not know how it was to have been done better,” cried Mrs. Norris, unable to be longer deaf; “unless I had gone myself, indeed; but I cannot be in two places at once; and I was talking to Mr. Green at that very time about your mother’s dairymaid, by her desire, and had promised John Groom to write to Mrs. Jefferies about his son, and the poor fellow was waiting for me half an hour. I think nobody can justly accuse me of sparing myself upon any occasion, but really I cannot do everything at once. And as for Fanny’s just stepping down to my house for me—it is not much above a quarter of a mile—I cannot think I was unreasonable to ask it. How often do I pace it three times a day, early and late, ay, and in all weathers too, and say nothing about it?”\n\n“I wish Fanny had half your strength, ma’am.”\n\n“If Fanny would be more regular in her exercise, she would not be knocked up so soon. She has not been out on horseback now this long while, and I am persuaded that, when she does not ride, she ought to walk. If she had been riding before, I should not have asked it of her. But I thought it would rather do her good after being stooping among the roses; for there is nothing so refreshing as a walk after a fatigue of that kind; and though the sun was strong, it was not so very hot. Between ourselves, Edmund,” nodding significantly at his mother, “it was cutting the roses, and dawdling about in the flower-garden, that did the mischief.”\n\n“I am afraid it was, indeed,” said the more candid Lady Bertram, who had overheard her; “I am very much afraid she caught the headache there, for the heat was enough to kill anybody. It was as much as I could bear myself. Sitting and calling to Pug, and trying to keep him from the flower-beds, was almost too much for me.”\n\nEdmund said no more to either lady; but going quietly to another table, on which the supper-tray yet remained, brought a glass of Madeira to Fanny, and obliged her to drink the greater part. She wished to be able to decline it; but the tears, which a variety of feelings created, made it easier to swallow than to speak.\n\nVexed as Edmund was with his mother and aunt, he was still more angry with himself. His own forgetfulness of her was worse than anything which they had done. Nothing of this would have happened had she been properly considered; but she had been left four days together without any choice of companions or exercise, and without any excuse for avoiding whatever her unreasonable aunts might require. He was ashamed to think that for four days together she had not had the power of riding, and very seriously resolved, however unwilling he must be to check a pleasure of Miss Crawford’s, that it should never happen again.\n\nFanny went to bed with her heart as full as on the first evening of her arrival at the Park. The state of her spirits had probably had its share in her indisposition; for she had been feeling neglected, and been struggling against discontent and envy for some days past. As she leant on the sofa, to which she had retreated that she might not be seen, the pain of her mind had been much beyond that in her head; and the sudden change which Edmund’s kindness had then occasioned, made her hardly know how to support herself.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII\n\nFanny’s rides recommenced the very next day; and as it was a pleasant fresh-feeling morning, less hot than the weather had lately been, Edmund trusted that her losses, both of health and pleasure, would be soon made good. While she was gone Mr. Rushworth arrived, escorting his mother, who came to be civil and to shew her civility especially, in urging the execution of the plan for visiting Sotherton, which had been started a fortnight before, and which, in consequence of her subsequent absence from home, had since lain dormant. Mrs. Norris and her nieces were all well pleased with its revival, and an early day was named and agreed to, provided Mr. Crawford should be disengaged: the young ladies did not forget that stipulation, and though Mrs. Norris would willingly have answered for his being so, they would neither authorise the liberty nor run the risk; and at last, on a hint from Miss Bertram, Mr. Rushworth discovered that the properest thing to be done was for him to walk down to the Parsonage directly, and call on Mr. Crawford, and inquire whether Wednesday would suit him or not.\n\nBefore his return Mrs. Grant and Miss Crawford came in. Having been out some time, and taken a different route to the house, they had not met him. Comfortable hopes, however, were given that he would find Mr. Crawford at home. The Sotherton scheme was mentioned of course. It was hardly possible, indeed, that anything else should be talked of, for Mrs. Norris was in high spirits about it; and Mrs. Rushworth, a well-meaning, civil, prosing, pompous woman, who thought nothing of consequence, but as it related to her own and her son’s concerns, had not yet given over pressing Lady Bertram to be of the party. Lady Bertram constantly declined it; but her placid manner of refusal made Mrs. Rushworth still think she wished to come, till Mrs. Norris’s more numerous words and louder tone convinced her of the truth.\n\n“The fatigue would be too much for my sister, a great deal too much, I assure you, my dear Mrs. Rushworth. Ten miles there, and ten back, you know. You must excuse my sister on this occasion, and accept of our two dear girls and myself without her. Sotherton is the only place that could give her a wish to go so far, but it cannot be, indeed. She will have a companion in Fanny Price, you know, so it will all do very well; and as for Edmund, as he is not here to speak for himself, I will answer for his being most happy to join the party. He can go on horseback, you know.”\n\nMrs. Rushworth being obliged to yield to Lady Bertram’s staying at home, could only be sorry. “The loss of her ladyship’s company would be a great drawback, and she should have been extremely happy to have seen the young lady too, Miss Price, who had never been at Sotherton yet, and it was a pity she should not see the place.”\n\n“You are very kind, you are all kindness, my dear madam,” cried Mrs. Norris; “but as to Fanny, she will have opportunities in plenty of seeing Sotherton. She has time enough before her; and her going now is quite out of the question. Lady Bertram could not possibly spare her.”\n\n“Oh no! I cannot do without Fanny.”\n\nMrs. Rushworth proceeded next, under the conviction that everybody must be wanting to see Sotherton, to include Miss Crawford in the invitation; and though Mrs. Grant, who had not been at the trouble of visiting Mrs. Rushworth, on her coming into the neighbourhood, civilly declined it on her own account, she was glad to secure any pleasure for her sister; and Mary, properly pressed and persuaded, was not long in accepting her share of the civility. Mr. Rushworth came back from the Parsonage successful; and Edmund made his appearance just in time to learn what had been settled for Wednesday, to attend Mrs. Rushworth to her carriage, and walk half-way down the park with the two other ladies.\n\nOn his return to the breakfast-room, he found Mrs. Norris trying to make up her mind as to whether Miss Crawford’s being of the party were desirable or not, or whether her brother’s barouche would not be full without her. The Miss Bertrams laughed at the idea, assuring her that the barouche would hold four perfectly well, independent of the box, on which one might go with him.\n\n“But why is it necessary,” said Edmund, “that Crawford’s carriage, or his only, should be employed? Why is no use to be made of my mother’s chaise? I could not, when the scheme was first mentioned the other day, understand why a visit from the family were not to be made in the carriage of the family.”\n\n“What!” cried Julia: “go boxed up three in a postchaise in this weather, when we may have seats in a barouche! No, my dear Edmund, that will not quite do.”\n\n“Besides,” said Maria, “I know that Mr. Crawford depends upon taking us. After what passed at first, he would claim it as a promise.”\n\n“And, my dear Edmund,” added Mrs. Norris, “taking out two carriages when one will do, would be trouble for nothing; and, between ourselves, coachman is not very fond of the roads between this and Sotherton: he always complains bitterly of the narrow lanes scratching his carriage, and you know one should not like to have dear Sir Thomas, when he comes home, find all the varnish scratched off.”\n\n“That would not be a very handsome reason for using Mr. Crawford’s,” said Maria; “but the truth is, that Wilcox is a stupid old fellow, and does not know how to drive. I will answer for it that we shall find no inconvenience from narrow roads on Wednesday.”\n\n“There is no hardship, I suppose, nothing unpleasant,” said Edmund, “in going on the barouche box.”\n\n“Unpleasant!” cried Maria: “oh dear! I believe it would be generally thought the favourite seat. There can be no comparison as to one’s view of the country. Probably Miss Crawford will choose the barouche-box herself.”\n\n“There can be no objection, then, to Fanny’s going with you; there can be no doubt of your having room for her.”\n\n“Fanny!” repeated Mrs. Norris; “my dear Edmund, there is no idea of her going with us. She stays with her aunt. I told Mrs. Rushworth so. She is not expected.”\n\n“You can have no reason, I imagine, madam,” said he, addressing his mother, “for wishing Fanny not to be of the party, but as it relates to yourself, to your own comfort. If you could do without her, you would not wish to keep her at home?”\n\n“To be sure not, but I cannot do without her.”\n\n“You can, if I stay at home with you, as I mean to do.”\n\nThere was a general cry out at this. “Yes,” he continued, “there is no necessity for my going, and I mean to stay at home. Fanny has a great desire to see Sotherton. I know she wishes it very much. She has not often a gratification of the kind, and I am sure, ma’am, you would be glad to give her the pleasure now?”\n\n“Oh yes! very glad, if your aunt sees no objection.”\n\nMrs. Norris was very ready with the only objection which could remain—their having positively assured Mrs. Rushworth that Fanny could not go, and the very strange appearance there would consequently be in taking her, which seemed to her a difficulty quite impossible to be got over. It must have the strangest appearance! It would be something so very unceremonious, so bordering on disrespect for Mrs. Rushworth, whose own manners were such a pattern of good-breeding and attention, that she really did not feel equal to it. Mrs. Norris had no affection for Fanny, and no wish of procuring her pleasure at any time; but her opposition to Edmund now, arose more from partiality for her own scheme, because it was her own, than from anything else. She felt that she had arranged everything extremely well, and that any alteration must be for the worse. When Edmund, therefore, told her in reply, as he did when she would give him the hearing, that she need not distress herself on Mrs. Rushworth’s account, because he had taken the opportunity, as he walked with her through the hall, of mentioning Miss Price as one who would probably be of the party, and had directly received a very sufficient invitation for his cousin, Mrs. Norris was too much vexed to submit with a very good grace, and would only say, “Very well, very well, just as you chuse, settle it your own way, I am sure I do not care about it.”\n\n“It seems very odd,” said Maria, “that you should be staying at home instead of Fanny.”\n\n“I am sure she ought to be very much obliged to you,” added Julia, hastily leaving the room as she spoke, from a consciousness that she ought to offer to stay at home herself.\n\n“Fanny will feel quite as grateful as the occasion requires,” was Edmund’s only reply, and the subject dropt.\n\nFanny’s gratitude, when she heard the plan, was, in fact, much greater than her pleasure. She felt Edmund’s kindness with all, and more than all, the sensibility which he, unsuspicious of her fond attachment, could be aware of; but that he should forego any enjoyment on her account gave her pain, and her own satisfaction in seeing Sotherton would be nothing without him.\n\nThe next meeting of the two Mansfield families produced another alteration in the plan, and one that was admitted with general approbation. Mrs. Grant offered herself as companion for the day to Lady Bertram in lieu of her son, and Dr. Grant was to join them at dinner. Lady Bertram was very well pleased to have it so, and the young ladies were in spirits again. Even Edmund was very thankful for an arrangement which restored him to his share of the party; and Mrs. Norris thought it an excellent plan, and had it at her tongue’s end, and was on the point of proposing it, when Mrs. Grant spoke.\n\nWednesday was fine, and soon after breakfast the barouche arrived, Mr. Crawford driving his sisters; and as everybody was ready, there was nothing to be done but for Mrs. Grant to alight and the others to take their places. The place of all places, the envied seat, the post of honour, was unappropriated. To whose happy lot was it to fall? While each of the Miss Bertrams were meditating how best, and with the most appearance of obliging the others, to secure it, the matter was settled by Mrs. Grant’s saying, as she stepped from the carriage, “As there are five of you, it will be better that one should sit with Henry; and as you were saying lately that you wished you could drive, Julia, I think this will be a good opportunity for you to take a lesson.”\n\nHappy Julia! Unhappy Maria! The former was on the barouche-box in a moment, the latter took her seat within, in gloom and mortification; and the carriage drove off amid the good wishes of the two remaining ladies, and the barking of Pug in his mistress’s arms.\n\nTheir road was through a pleasant country; and Fanny, whose rides had never been extensive, was soon beyond her knowledge, and was very happy in observing all that was new, and admiring all that was pretty. She was not often invited to join in the conversation of the others, nor did she desire it. Her own thoughts and reflections were habitually her best companions; and, in observing the appearance of the country, the bearings of the roads, the difference of soil, the state of the harvest, the cottages, the cattle, the children, she found entertainment that could only have been heightened by having Edmund to speak to of what she felt. That was the only point of resemblance between her and the lady who sat by her: in everything but a value for Edmund, Miss Crawford was very unlike her. She had none of Fanny’s delicacy of taste, of mind, of feeling; she saw Nature, inanimate Nature, with little observation; her attention was all for men and women, her talents for the light and lively. In looking back after Edmund, however, when there was any stretch of road behind them, or when he gained on them in ascending a considerable hill, they were united, and a “there he is” broke at the same moment from them both, more than once.\n\nFor the first seven miles Miss Bertram had very little real comfort: her prospect always ended in Mr. Crawford and her sister sitting side by side, full of conversation and merriment; and to see only his expressive profile as he turned with a smile to Julia, or to catch the laugh of the other, was a perpetual source of irritation, which her own sense of propriety could but just smooth over. When Julia looked back, it was with a countenance of delight, and whenever she spoke to them, it was in the highest spirits: “her view of the country was charming, she wished they could all see it,” etc.; but her only offer of exchange was addressed to Miss Crawford, as they gained the summit of a long hill, and was not more inviting than this: “Here is a fine burst of country. I wish you had my seat, but I dare say you will not take it, let me press you ever so much;” and Miss Crawford could hardly answer before they were moving again at a good pace.\n\nWhen they came within the influence of Sotherton associations, it was better for Miss Bertram, who might be said to have two strings to her bow. She had Rushworth feelings, and Crawford feelings, and in the vicinity of Sotherton the former had considerable effect. Mr. Rushworth’s consequence was hers. She could not tell Miss Crawford that “those woods belonged to Sotherton,” she could not carelessly observe that “she believed that it was now all Mr. Rushworth’s property on each side of the road,” without elation of heart; and it was a pleasure to increase with their approach to the capital freehold mansion, and ancient manorial residence of the family, with all its rights of court-leet and court-baron.\n\n“Now we shall have no more rough road, Miss Crawford; our difficulties are over. The rest of the way is such as it ought to be. Mr. Rushworth has made it since he succeeded to the estate. Here begins the village. Those cottages are really a disgrace. The church spire is reckoned remarkably handsome. I am glad the church is not so close to the great house as often happens in old places. The annoyance of the bells must be terrible. There is the parsonage: a tidy-looking house, and I understand the clergyman and his wife are very decent people. Those are almshouses, built by some of the family. To the right is the steward’s house; he is a very respectable man. Now we are coming to the lodge-gates; but we have nearly a mile through the park still. It is not ugly, you see, at this end; there is some fine timber, but the situation of the house is dreadful. We go down hill to it for half a mile, and it is a pity, for it would not be an ill-looking place if it had a better approach.”\n\nMiss Crawford was not slow to admire; she pretty well guessed Miss Bertram’s feelings, and made it a point of honour to promote her enjoyment to the utmost. Mrs. Norris was all delight and volubility; and even Fanny had something to say in admiration, and might be heard with complacency. Her eye was eagerly taking in everything within her reach; and after being at some pains to get a view of the house, and observing that “it was a sort of building which she could not look at but with respect,” she added, “Now, where is the avenue? The house fronts the east, I perceive. The avenue, therefore, must be at the back of it. Mr. Rushworth talked of the west front.”\n\n“Yes, it is exactly behind the house; begins at a little distance, and ascends for half a mile to the extremity of the grounds. You may see something of it here—something of the more distant trees. It is oak entirely.”\n\nMiss Bertram could now speak with decided information of what she had known nothing about when Mr. Rushworth had asked her opinion; and her spirits were in as happy a flutter as vanity and pride could furnish, when they drove up to the spacious stone steps before the principal entrance.\n\n# CHAPTER IX\n\nMr. Rushworth was at the door to receive his fair lady; and the whole party were welcomed by him with due attention. In the drawing-room they were met with equal cordiality by the mother, and Miss Bertram had all the distinction with each that she could wish. After the business of arriving was over, it was first necessary to eat, and the doors were thrown open to admit them through one or two intermediate rooms into the appointed dining-parlour, where a collation was prepared with abundance and elegance. Much was said, and much was ate, and all went well. The particular object of the day was then considered. How would Mr. Crawford like, in what manner would he chuse, to take a survey of the grounds? Mr. Rushworth mentioned his curricle. Mr. Crawford suggested the greater desirableness of some carriage which might convey more than two. “To be depriving themselves of the advantage of other eyes and other judgments, might be an evil even beyond the loss of present pleasure.”\n\nMrs. Rushworth proposed that the chaise should be taken also; but this was scarcely received as an amendment: the young ladies neither smiled nor spoke. Her next proposition, of shewing the house to such of them as had not been there before, was more acceptable, for Miss Bertram was pleased to have its size displayed, and all were glad to be doing something.\n\nThe whole party rose accordingly, and under Mrs. Rushworth’s guidance were shewn through a number of rooms, all lofty, and many large, and amply furnished in the taste of fifty years back, with shining floors, solid mahogany, rich damask, marble, gilding, and carving, each handsome in its way. Of pictures there were abundance, and some few good, but the larger part were family portraits, no longer anything to anybody but Mrs. Rushworth, who had been at great pains to learn all that the housekeeper could teach, and was now almost equally well qualified to shew the house. On the present occasion she addressed herself chiefly to Miss Crawford and Fanny, but there was no comparison in the willingness of their attention; for Miss Crawford, who had seen scores of great houses, and cared for none of them, had only the appearance of civilly listening, while Fanny, to whom everything was almost as interesting as it was new, attended with unaffected earnestness to all that Mrs. Rushworth could relate of the family in former times, its rise and grandeur, regal visits and loyal efforts, delighted to connect anything with history already known, or warm her imagination with scenes of the past.\n\nThe situation of the house excluded the possibility of much prospect from any of the rooms; and while Fanny and some of the others were attending Mrs. Rushworth, Henry Crawford was looking grave and shaking his head at the windows. Every room on the west front looked across a lawn to the beginning of the avenue immediately beyond tall iron palisades and gates.\n\nHaving visited many more rooms than could be supposed to be of any other use than to contribute to the window-tax, and find employment for housemaids, “Now,” said Mrs. Rushworth, “we are coming to the chapel, which properly we ought to enter from above, and look down upon; but as we are quite among friends, I will take you in this way, if you will excuse me.”\n\nThey entered. Fanny’s imagination had prepared her for something grander than a mere spacious, oblong room, fitted up for the purpose of devotion: with nothing more striking or more solemn than the profusion of mahogany, and the crimson velvet cushions appearing over the ledge of the family gallery above. “I am disappointed,” said she, in a low voice, to Edmund. “This is not my idea of a chapel. There is nothing awful here, nothing melancholy, nothing grand. Here are no aisles, no arches, no inscriptions, no banners. No banners, cousin, to be ‘blown by the night wind of heaven.’ No signs that a ‘Scottish monarch sleeps below.’”\n\n“You forget, Fanny, how lately all this has been built, and for how confined a purpose, compared with the old chapels of castles and monasteries. It was only for the private use of the family. They have been buried, I suppose, in the parish church. There you must look for the banners and the achievements.”\n\n“It was foolish of me not to think of all that; but I am disappointed.”\n\nMrs. Rushworth began her relation. “This chapel was fitted up as you see it, in James the Second’s time. Before that period, as I understand, the pews were only wainscot; and there is some reason to think that the linings and cushions of the pulpit and family seat were only purple cloth; but this is not quite certain. It is a handsome chapel, and was formerly in constant use both morning and evening. Prayers were always read in it by the domestic chaplain, within the memory of many; but the late Mr. Rushworth left it off.”\n\n“Every generation has its improvements,” said Miss Crawford, with a smile, to Edmund.\n\nMrs. Rushworth was gone to repeat her lesson to Mr. Crawford; and Edmund, Fanny, and Miss Crawford remained in a cluster together.\n\n“It is a pity,” cried Fanny, “that the custom should have been discontinued. It was a valuable part of former times. There is something in a chapel and chaplain so much in character with a great house, with one’s ideas of what such a household should be! A whole family assembling regularly for the purpose of prayer is fine!”\n\n“Very fine indeed,” said Miss Crawford, laughing. “It must do the heads of the family a great deal of good to force all the poor housemaids and footmen to leave business and pleasure, and say their prayers here twice a day, while they are inventing excuses themselves for staying away.”\n\n“That is hardly Fanny’s idea of a family assembling,” said Edmund. “If the master and mistress do not attend themselves, there must be more harm than good in the custom.”\n\n“At any rate, it is safer to leave people to their own devices on such subjects. Everybody likes to go their own way—to chuse their own time and manner of devotion. The obligation of attendance, the formality, the restraint, the length of time—altogether it is a formidable thing, and what nobody likes; and if the good people who used to kneel and gape in that gallery could have foreseen that the time would ever come when men and women might lie another ten minutes in bed, when they woke with a headache, without danger of reprobation, because chapel was missed, they would have jumped with joy and envy. Cannot you imagine with what unwilling feelings the former belles of the house of Rushworth did many a time repair to this chapel? The young Mrs. Eleanors and Mrs. Bridgets—starched up into seeming piety, but with heads full of something very different—especially if the poor chaplain were not worth looking at—and, in those days, I fancy parsons were very inferior even to what they are now.”\n\nFor a few moments she was unanswered. Fanny coloured and looked at Edmund, but felt too angry for speech; and he needed a little recollection before he could say, “Your lively mind can hardly be serious even on serious subjects. You have given us an amusing sketch, and human nature cannot say it was not so. We must all feel at times the difficulty of fixing our thoughts as we could wish; but if you are supposing it a frequent thing, that is to say, a weakness grown into a habit from neglect, what could be expected from the private devotions of such persons? Do you think the minds which are suffered, which are indulged in wanderings in a chapel, would be more collected in a closet?”\n\n“Yes, very likely. They would have two chances at least in their favour. There would be less to distract the attention from without, and it would not be tried so long.”\n\n“The mind which does not struggle against itself under one circumstance, would find objects to distract it in the other, I believe; and the influence of the place and of example may often rouse better feelings than are begun with. The greater length of the service, however, I admit to be sometimes too hard a stretch upon the mind. One wishes it were not so; but I have not yet left Oxford long enough to forget what chapel prayers are.”\n\nWhile this was passing, the rest of the party being scattered about the chapel, Julia called Mr. Crawford’s attention to her sister, by saying, “Do look at Mr. Rushworth and Maria, standing side by side, exactly as if the ceremony were going to be performed. Have not they completely the air of it?”\n\nMr. Crawford smiled his acquiescence, and stepping forward to Maria, said, in a voice which she only could hear, “I do not like to see Miss Bertram so near the altar.”\n\nStarting, the lady instinctively moved a step or two, but recovering herself in a moment, affected to laugh, and asked him, in a tone not much louder, “If he would give her away?”\n\n“I am afraid I should do it very awkwardly,” was his reply, with a look of meaning.\n\nJulia, joining them at the moment, carried on the joke.\n\n“Upon my word, it is really a pity that it should not take place directly, if we had but a proper licence, for here we are altogether, and nothing in the world could be more snug and pleasant.” And she talked and laughed about it with so little caution as to catch the comprehension of Mr. Rushworth and his mother, and expose her sister to the whispered gallantries of her lover, while Mrs. Rushworth spoke with proper smiles and dignity of its being a most happy event to her whenever it took place.\n\n“If Edmund were but in orders!” cried Julia, and running to where he stood with Miss Crawford and Fanny: “My dear Edmund, if you were but in orders now, you might perform the ceremony directly. How unlucky that you are not ordained; Mr. Rushworth and Maria are quite ready.”\n\nMiss Crawford’s countenance, as Julia spoke, might have amused a disinterested observer. She looked almost aghast under the new idea she was receiving. Fanny pitied her. “How distressed she will be at what she said just now,” passed across her mind.\n\n“Ordained!” said Miss Crawford; “what, are you to be a clergyman?”\n\n“Yes; I shall take orders soon after my father’s return—probably at Christmas.”\n\nMiss Crawford, rallying her spirits, and recovering her complexion, replied only, “If I had known this before, I would have spoken of the cloth with more respect,” and turned the subject.\n\nThe chapel was soon afterwards left to the silence and stillness which reigned in it, with few interruptions, throughout the year. Miss Bertram, displeased with her sister, led the way, and all seemed to feel that they had been there long enough.\n\nThe lower part of the house had been now entirely shewn, and Mrs. Rushworth, never weary in the cause, would have proceeded towards the principal staircase, and taken them through all the rooms above, if her son had not interposed with a doubt of there being time enough. “For if,” said he, with the sort of self-evident proposition which many a clearer head does not always avoid, “we are too long going over the house, we shall not have time for what is to be done out of doors. It is past two, and we are to dine at five.”\n\nMrs. Rushworth submitted; and the question of surveying the grounds, with the who and the how, was likely to be more fully agitated, and Mrs. Norris was beginning to arrange by what junction of carriages and horses most could be done, when the young people, meeting with an outward door, temptingly open on a flight of steps which led immediately to turf and shrubs, and all the sweets of pleasure-grounds, as by one impulse, one wish for air and liberty, all walked out.\n\n“Suppose we turn down here for the present,” said Mrs. Rushworth, civilly taking the hint and following them. “Here are the greatest number of our plants, and here are the curious pheasants.”\n\n“Query,” said Mr. Crawford, looking round him, “whether we may not find something to employ us here before we go farther? I see walls of great promise. Mr. Rushworth, shall we summon a council on this lawn?”\n\n“James,” said Mrs. Rushworth to her son, “I believe the wilderness will be new to all the party. The Miss Bertrams have never seen the wilderness yet.”\n\nNo objection was made, but for some time there seemed no inclination to move in any plan, or to any distance. All were attracted at first by the plants or the pheasants, and all dispersed about in happy independence. Mr. Crawford was the first to move forward to examine the capabilities of that end of the house. The lawn, bounded on each side by a high wall, contained beyond the first planted area a bowling-green, and beyond the bowling-green a long terrace walk, backed by iron palisades, and commanding a view over them into the tops of the trees of the wilderness immediately adjoining. It was a good spot for fault-finding. Mr. Crawford was soon followed by Miss Bertram and Mr. Rushworth; and when, after a little time, the others began to form into parties, these three were found in busy consultation on the terrace by Edmund, Miss Crawford, and Fanny, who seemed as naturally to unite, and who, after a short participation of their regrets and difficulties, left them and walked on. The remaining three, Mrs. Rushworth, Mrs. Norris, and Julia, were still far behind; for Julia, whose happy star no longer prevailed, was obliged to keep by the side of Mrs. Rushworth, and restrain her impatient feet to that lady’s slow pace, while her aunt, having fallen in with the housekeeper, who was come out to feed the pheasants, was lingering behind in gossip with her. Poor Julia, the only one out of the nine not tolerably satisfied with their lot, was now in a state of complete penance, and as different from the Julia of the barouche-box as could well be imagined. The politeness which she had been brought up to practise as a duty made it impossible for her to escape; while the want of that higher species of self-command, that just consideration of others, that knowledge of her own heart, that principle of right, which had not formed any essential part of her education, made her miserable under it.\n\n“This is insufferably hot,” said Miss Crawford, when they had taken one turn on the terrace, and were drawing a second time to the door in the middle which opened to the wilderness. “Shall any of us object to being comfortable? Here is a nice little wood, if one can but get into it. What happiness if the door should not be locked! but of course it is; for in these great places the gardeners are the only people who can go where they like.”\n\nThe door, however, proved not to be locked, and they were all agreed in turning joyfully through it, and leaving the unmitigated glare of day behind. A considerable flight of steps landed them in the wilderness, which was a planted wood of about two acres, and though chiefly of larch and laurel, and beech cut down, and though laid out with too much regularity, was darkness and shade, and natural beauty, compared with the bowling-green and the terrace. They all felt the refreshment of it, and for some time could only walk and admire. At length, after a short pause, Miss Crawford began with, “So you are to be a clergyman, Mr. Bertram. This is rather a surprise to me.”\n\n“Why should it surprise you? You must suppose me designed for some profession, and might perceive that I am neither a lawyer, nor a soldier, nor a sailor.”\n\n“Very true; but, in short, it had not occurred to me. And you know there is generally an uncle or a grandfather to leave a fortune to the second son.”\n\n“A very praiseworthy practice,” said Edmund, “but not quite universal. I am one of the exceptions, and being one, must do something for myself.”\n\n“But why are you to be a clergyman? I thought that was always the lot of the youngest, where there were many to chuse before him.”\n\n“Do you think the church itself never chosen, then?”\n\n“Never is a black word. But yes, in the never of conversation, which means not very often, I do think it. For what is to be done in the church? Men love to distinguish themselves, and in either of the other lines distinction may be gained, but not in the church. A clergyman is nothing.”\n\n“The nothing of conversation has its gradations, I hope, as well as the never. A clergyman cannot be high in state or fashion. He must not head mobs, or set the ton in dress. But I cannot call that situation nothing which has the charge of all that is of the first importance to mankind, individually or collectively considered, temporally and eternally, which has the guardianship of religion and morals, and consequently of the manners which result from their influence. No one here can call the office nothing. If the man who holds it is so, it is by the neglect of his duty, by foregoing its just importance, and stepping out of his place to appear what he ought not to appear.”\n\n“You assign greater consequence to the clergyman than one has been used to hear given, or than I can quite comprehend. One does not see much of this influence and importance in society, and how can it be acquired where they are so seldom seen themselves? How can two sermons a week, even supposing them worth hearing, supposing the preacher to have the sense to prefer Blair’s to his own, do all that you speak of? govern the conduct and fashion the manners of a large congregation for the rest of the week? One scarcely sees a clergyman out of his pulpit.”\n\n“You are speaking of London, I am speaking of the nation at large.”\n\n“The metropolis, I imagine, is a pretty fair sample of the rest.”\n\n“Not, I should hope, of the proportion of virtue to vice throughout the kingdom. We do not look in great cities for our best morality. It is not there that respectable people of any denomination can do most good; and it certainly is not there that the influence of the clergy can be most felt. A fine preacher is followed and admired; but it is not in fine preaching only that a good clergyman will be useful in his parish and his neighbourhood, where the parish and neighbourhood are of a size capable of knowing his private character, and observing his general conduct, which in London can rarely be the case. The clergy are lost there in the crowds of their parishioners. They are known to the largest part only as preachers. And with regard to their influencing public manners, Miss Crawford must not misunderstand me, or suppose I mean to call them the arbiters of good-breeding, the regulators of refinement and courtesy, the masters of the ceremonies of life. The manners I speak of might rather be called conduct, perhaps, the result of good principles; the effect, in short, of those doctrines which it is their duty to teach and recommend; and it will, I believe, be everywhere found, that as the clergy are, or are not what they ought to be, so are the rest of the nation.”\n\n“Certainly,” said Fanny, with gentle earnestness.\n\n“There,” cried Miss Crawford, “you have quite convinced Miss Price already.”\n\n“I wish I could convince Miss Crawford too.”\n\n“I do not think you ever will,” said she, with an arch smile; “I am just as much surprised now as I was at first that you should intend to take orders. You really are fit for something better. Come, do change your mind. It is not too late. Go into the law.”\n\n“Go into the law! With as much ease as I was told to go into this wilderness.”\n\n“Now you are going to say something about law being the worst wilderness of the two, but I forestall you; remember, I have forestalled you.”\n\n“You need not hurry when the object is only to prevent my saying a bon mot, for there is not the least wit in my nature. I am a very matter-of-fact, plain-spoken being, and may blunder on the borders of a repartee for half an hour together without striking it out.”\n\nA general silence succeeded. Each was thoughtful. Fanny made the first interruption by saying, “I wonder that I should be tired with only walking in this sweet wood; but the next time we come to a seat, if it is not disagreeable to you, I should be glad to sit down for a little while.”\n\n“My dear Fanny,” cried Edmund, immediately drawing her arm within his, “how thoughtless I have been! I hope you are not very tired. Perhaps,” turning to Miss Crawford, “my other companion may do me the honour of taking an arm.”\n\n“Thank you, but I am not at all tired.” She took it, however, as she spoke, and the gratification of having her do so, of feeling such a connexion for the first time, made him a little forgetful of Fanny. “You scarcely touch me,” said he. “You do not make me of any use. What a difference in the weight of a woman’s arm from that of a man! At Oxford I have been a good deal used to have a man lean on me for the length of a street, and you are only a fly in the comparison.”\n\n“I am really not tired, which I almost wonder at; for we must have walked at least a mile in this wood. Do not you think we have?”\n\n“Not half a mile,” was his sturdy answer; for he was not yet so much in love as to measure distance, or reckon time, with feminine lawlessness.\n\n“Oh! you do not consider how much we have wound about. We have taken such a very serpentine course, and the wood itself must be half a mile long in a straight line, for we have never seen the end of it yet since we left the first great path.”\n\n“But if you remember, before we left that first great path, we saw directly to the end of it. We looked down the whole vista, and saw it closed by iron gates, and it could not have been more than a furlong in length.”\n\n“Oh! I know nothing of your furlongs, but I am sure it is a very long wood, and that we have been winding in and out ever since we came into it; and therefore, when I say that we have walked a mile in it, I must speak within compass.”\n\n“We have been exactly a quarter of an hour here,” said Edmund, taking out his watch. “Do you think we are walking four miles an hour?”\n\n“Oh! do not attack me with your watch. A watch is always too fast or too slow. I cannot be dictated to by a watch.”\n\nA few steps farther brought them out at the bottom of the very walk they had been talking of; and standing back, well shaded and sheltered, and looking over a ha-ha into the park, was a comfortable-sized bench, on which they all sat down.\n\n“I am afraid you are very tired, Fanny,” said Edmund, observing her; “why would not you speak sooner? This will be a bad day’s amusement for you if you are to be knocked up. Every sort of exercise fatigues her so soon, Miss Crawford, except riding.”\n\n“How abominable in you, then, to let me engross her horse as I did all last week! I am ashamed of you and of myself, but it shall never happen again.”\n\n“Your attentiveness and consideration makes me more sensible of my own neglect. Fanny’s interest seems in safer hands with you than with me.”\n\n“That she should be tired now, however, gives me no surprise; for there is nothing in the course of one’s duties so fatiguing as what we have been doing this morning: seeing a great house, dawdling from one room to another, straining one’s eyes and one’s attention, hearing what one does not understand, admiring what one does not care for. It is generally allowed to be the greatest bore in the world, and Miss Price has found it so, though she did not know it.”\n\n“I shall soon be rested,” said Fanny; “to sit in the shade on a fine day, and look upon verdure, is the most perfect refreshment.”\n\nAfter sitting a little while Miss Crawford was up again. “I must move,” said she; “resting fatigues me. I have looked across the ha-ha till I am weary. I must go and look through that iron gate at the same view, without being able to see it so well.”\n\nEdmund left the seat likewise. “Now, Miss Crawford, if you will look up the walk, you will convince yourself that it cannot be half a mile long, or half half a mile.”\n\n“It is an immense distance,” said she; “I see that with a glance.”\n\nHe still reasoned with her, but in vain. She would not calculate, she would not compare. She would only smile and assert. The greatest degree of rational consistency could not have been more engaging, and they talked with mutual satisfaction. At last it was agreed that they should endeavour to determine the dimensions of the wood by walking a little more about it. They would go to one end of it, in the line they were then in—for there was a straight green walk along the bottom by the side of the ha-ha—and perhaps turn a little way in some other direction, if it seemed likely to assist them, and be back in a few minutes. Fanny said she was rested, and would have moved too, but this was not suffered. Edmund urged her remaining where she was with an earnestness which she could not resist, and she was left on the bench to think with pleasure of her cousin’s care, but with great regret that she was not stronger. She watched them till they had turned the corner, and listened till all sound of them had ceased.\n\n# CHAPTER X\n\nA quarter of an hour, twenty minutes, passed away, and Fanny was still thinking of Edmund, Miss Crawford, and herself, without interruption from any one. She began to be surprised at being left so long, and to listen with an anxious desire of hearing their steps and their voices again. She listened, and at length she heard; she heard voices and feet approaching; but she had just satisfied herself that it was not those she wanted, when Miss Bertram, Mr. Rushworth, and Mr. Crawford issued from the same path which she had trod herself, and were before her.\n\n“Miss Price all alone” and “My dear Fanny, how comes this?” were the first salutations. She told her story. “Poor dear Fanny,” cried her cousin, “how ill you have been used by them! You had better have staid with us.”\n\nThen seating herself with a gentleman on each side, she resumed the conversation which had engaged them before, and discussed the possibility of improvements with much animation. Nothing was fixed on; but Henry Crawford was full of ideas and projects, and, generally speaking, whatever he proposed was immediately approved, first by her, and then by Mr. Rushworth, whose principal business seemed to be to hear the others, and who scarcely risked an original thought of his own beyond a wish that they had seen his friend Smith’s place.\n\nAfter some minutes spent in this way, Miss Bertram, observing the iron gate, expressed a wish of passing through it into the park, that their views and their plans might be more comprehensive. It was the very thing of all others to be wished, it was the best, it was the only way of proceeding with any advantage, in Henry Crawford’s opinion; and he directly saw a knoll not half a mile off, which would give them exactly the requisite command of the house. Go therefore they must to that knoll, and through that gate; but the gate was locked. Mr. Rushworth wished he had brought the key; he had been very near thinking whether he should not bring the key; he was determined he would never come without the key again; but still this did not remove the present evil. They could not get through; and as Miss Bertram’s inclination for so doing did by no means lessen, it ended in Mr. Rushworth’s declaring outright that he would go and fetch the key. He set off accordingly.\n\n“It is undoubtedly the best thing we can do now, as we are so far from the house already,” said Mr. Crawford, when he was gone.\n\n“Yes, there is nothing else to be done. But now, sincerely, do not you find the place altogether worse than you expected?”\n\n“No, indeed, far otherwise. I find it better, grander, more complete in its style, though that style may not be the best. And to tell you the truth,” speaking rather lower, “I do not think that I shall ever see Sotherton again with so much pleasure as I do now. Another summer will hardly improve it to me.”\n\nAfter a moment’s embarrassment the lady replied, “You are too much a man of the world not to see with the eyes of the world. If other people think Sotherton improved, I have no doubt that you will.”\n\n“I am afraid I am not quite so much the man of the world as might be good for me in some points. My feelings are not quite so evanescent, nor my memory of the past under such easy dominion as one finds to be the case with men of the world.”\n\nThis was followed by a short silence. Miss Bertram began again. “You seemed to enjoy your drive here very much this morning. I was glad to see you so well entertained. You and Julia were laughing the whole way.”\n\n“Were we? Yes, I believe we were; but I have not the least recollection at what. Oh! I believe I was relating to her some ridiculous stories of an old Irish groom of my uncle’s. Your sister loves to laugh.”\n\n“You think her more light-hearted than I am?”\n\n“More easily amused,” he replied; “consequently, you know,” smiling, “better company. I could not have hoped to entertain you with Irish anecdotes during a ten miles’ drive.”\n\n“Naturally, I believe, I am as lively as Julia, but I have more to think of now.”\n\n“You have, undoubtedly; and there are situations in which very high spirits would denote insensibility. Your prospects, however, are too fair to justify want of spirits. You have a very smiling scene before you.”\n\n“Do you mean literally or figuratively? Literally, I conclude. Yes, certainly, the sun shines, and the park looks very cheerful. But unluckily that iron gate, that ha-ha, give me a feeling of restraint and hardship. ‘I cannot get out,’ as the starling said.” As she spoke, and it was with expression, she walked to the gate: he followed her. “Mr. Rushworth is so long fetching this key!”\n\n“And for the world you would not get out without the key and without Mr. Rushworth’s authority and protection, or I think you might with little difficulty pass round the edge of the gate, here, with my assistance; I think it might be done, if you really wished to be more at large, and could allow yourself to think it not prohibited.”\n\n“Prohibited! nonsense! I certainly can get out that way, and I will. Mr. Rushworth will be here in a moment, you know; we shall not be out of sight.”\n\n“Or if we are, Miss Price will be so good as to tell him that he will find us near that knoll: the grove of oak on the knoll.”\n\nFanny, feeling all this to be wrong, could not help making an effort to prevent it. “You will hurt yourself, Miss Bertram,” she cried; “you will certainly hurt yourself against those spikes; you will tear your gown; you will be in danger of slipping into the ha-ha. You had better not go.”\n\nHer cousin was safe on the other side while these words were spoken, and, smiling with all the good-humour of success, she said, “Thank you, my dear Fanny, but I and my gown are alive and well, and so good-bye.”\n\nFanny was again left to her solitude, and with no increase of pleasant feelings, for she was sorry for almost all that she had seen and heard, astonished at Miss Bertram, and angry with Mr. Crawford. By taking a circuitous, and, as it appeared to her, very unreasonable direction to the knoll, they were soon beyond her eye; and for some minutes longer she remained without sight or sound of any companion. She seemed to have the little wood all to herself. She could almost have thought that Edmund and Miss Crawford had left it, but that it was impossible for Edmund to forget her so entirely.\n\nShe was again roused from disagreeable musings by sudden footsteps: somebody was coming at a quick pace down the principal walk. She expected Mr. Rushworth, but it was Julia, who, hot and out of breath, and with a look of disappointment, cried out on seeing her, “Heyday! Where are the others? I thought Maria and Mr. Crawford were with you.”\n\nFanny explained.\n\n“A pretty trick, upon my word! I cannot see them anywhere,” looking eagerly into the park. “But they cannot be very far off, and I think I am equal to as much as Maria, even without help.”\n\n“But, Julia, Mr. Rushworth will be here in a moment with the key. Do wait for Mr. Rushworth.”\n\n“Not I, indeed. I have had enough of the family for one morning. Why, child, I have but this moment escaped from his horrible mother. Such a penance as I have been enduring, while you were sitting here so composed and so happy! It might have been as well, perhaps, if you had been in my place, but you always contrive to keep out of these scrapes.”\n\nThis was a most unjust reflection, but Fanny could allow for it, and let it pass: Julia was vexed, and her temper was hasty; but she felt that it would not last, and therefore, taking no notice, only asked her if she had not seen Mr. Rushworth.\n\n“Yes, yes, we saw him. He was posting away as if upon life and death, and could but just spare time to tell us his errand, and where you all were.”\n\n“It is a pity he should have so much trouble for nothing.”\n\n“That is Miss Maria’s concern. I am not obliged to punish myself for her sins. The mother I could not avoid, as long as my tiresome aunt was dancing about with the housekeeper, but the son I can get away from.”\n\nAnd she immediately scrambled across the fence, and walked away, not attending to Fanny’s last question of whether she had seen anything of Miss Crawford and Edmund. The sort of dread in which Fanny now sat of seeing Mr. Rushworth prevented her thinking so much of their continued absence, however, as she might have done. She felt that he had been very ill-used, and was quite unhappy in having to communicate what had passed. He joined her within five minutes after Julia’s exit; and though she made the best of the story, he was evidently mortified and displeased in no common degree. At first he scarcely said anything; his looks only expressed his extreme surprise and vexation, and he walked to the gate and stood there, without seeming to know what to do.\n\n“They desired me to stay—my cousin Maria charged me to say that you would find them at that knoll, or thereabouts.”\n\n“I do not believe I shall go any farther,” said he sullenly; “I see nothing of them. By the time I get to the knoll they may be gone somewhere else. I have had walking enough.”\n\nAnd he sat down with a most gloomy countenance by Fanny.\n\n“I am very sorry,” said she; “it is very unlucky.” And she longed to be able to say something more to the purpose.\n\nAfter an interval of silence, “I think they might as well have staid for me,” said he.\n\n“Miss Bertram thought you would follow her.”\n\n“I should not have had to follow her if she had staid.”\n\nThis could not be denied, and Fanny was silenced. After another pause, he went on—“Pray, Miss Price, are you such a great admirer of this Mr. Crawford as some people are? For my part, I can see nothing in him.”\n\n“I do not think him at all handsome.”\n\n“Handsome! Nobody can call such an undersized man handsome. He is not five foot nine. I should not wonder if he is not more than five foot eight. I think he is an ill-looking fellow. In my opinion, these Crawfords are no addition at all. We did very well without them.”\n\nA small sigh escaped Fanny here, and she did not know how to contradict him.\n\n“If I had made any difficulty about fetching the key, there might have been some excuse, but I went the very moment she said she wanted it.”\n\n“Nothing could be more obliging than your manner, I am sure, and I dare say you walked as fast as you could; but still it is some distance, you know, from this spot to the house, quite into the house; and when people are waiting, they are bad judges of time, and every half minute seems like five.”\n\nHe got up and walked to the gate again, and “wished he had had the key about him at the time.” Fanny thought she discerned in his standing there an indication of relenting, which encouraged her to another attempt, and she said, therefore, “It is a pity you should not join them. They expected to have a better view of the house from that part of the park, and will be thinking how it may be improved; and nothing of that sort, you know, can be settled without you.”\n\nShe found herself more successful in sending away than in retaining a companion. Mr. Rushworth was worked on. “Well,” said he, “if you really think I had better go: it would be foolish to bring the key for nothing.” And letting himself out, he walked off without farther ceremony.\n\nFanny’s thoughts were now all engrossed by the two who had left her so long ago, and getting quite impatient, she resolved to go in search of them. She followed their steps along the bottom walk, and had just turned up into another, when the voice and the laugh of Miss Crawford once more caught her ear; the sound approached, and a few more windings brought them before her. They were just returned into the wilderness from the park, to which a sidegate, not fastened, had tempted them very soon after their leaving her, and they had been across a portion of the park into the very avenue which Fanny had been hoping the whole morning to reach at last, and had been sitting down under one of the trees. This was their history. It was evident that they had been spending their time pleasantly, and were not aware of the length of their absence. Fanny’s best consolation was in being assured that Edmund had wished for her very much, and that he should certainly have come back for her, had she not been tired already; but this was not quite sufficient to do away with the pain of having been left a whole hour, when he had talked of only a few minutes, nor to banish the sort of curiosity she felt to know what they had been conversing about all that time; and the result of the whole was to her disappointment and depression, as they prepared by general agreement to return to the house.\n\nOn reaching the bottom of the steps to the terrace, Mrs. Rushworth and Mrs. Norris presented themselves at the top, just ready for the wilderness, at the end of an hour and a half from their leaving the house. Mrs. Norris had been too well employed to move faster. Whatever cross-accidents had occurred to intercept the pleasures of her nieces, she had found a morning of complete enjoyment; for the housekeeper, after a great many courtesies on the subject of pheasants, had taken her to the dairy, told her all about their cows, and given her the receipt for a famous cream cheese; and since Julia’s leaving them they had been met by the gardener, with whom she had made a most satisfactory acquaintance, for she had set him right as to his grandson’s illness, convinced him that it was an ague, and promised him a charm for it; and he, in return, had shewn her all his choicest nursery of plants, and actually presented her with a very curious specimen of heath.\n\nOn this rencontre they all returned to the house together, there to lounge away the time as they could with sofas, and chit-chat, and Quarterly Reviews, till the return of the others, and the arrival of dinner. It was late before the Miss Bertrams and the two gentlemen came in, and their ramble did not appear to have been more than partially agreeable, or at all productive of anything useful with regard to the object of the day. By their own accounts they had been all walking after each other, and the junction which had taken place at last seemed, to Fanny’s observation, to have been as much too late for re-establishing harmony, as it confessedly had been for determining on any alteration. She felt, as she looked at Julia and Mr. Rushworth, that hers was not the only dissatisfied bosom amongst them: there was gloom on the face of each. Mr. Crawford and Miss Bertram were much more gay, and she thought that he was taking particular pains, during dinner, to do away any little resentment of the other two, and restore general good-humour.\n\nDinner was soon followed by tea and coffee, a ten miles’ drive home allowed no waste of hours; and from the time of their sitting down to table, it was a quick succession of busy nothings till the carriage came to the door, and Mrs. Norris, having fidgeted about, and obtained a few pheasants’ eggs and a cream cheese from the housekeeper, and made abundance of civil speeches to Mrs. Rushworth, was ready to lead the way. At the same moment Mr. Crawford, approaching Julia, said, “I hope I am not to lose my companion, unless she is afraid of the evening air in so exposed a seat.” The request had not been foreseen, but was very graciously received, and Julia’s day was likely to end almost as well as it began. Miss Bertram had made up her mind to something different, and was a little disappointed; but her conviction of being really the one preferred comforted her under it, and enabled her to receive Mr. Rushworth’s parting attentions as she ought. He was certainly better pleased to hand her into the barouche than to assist her in ascending the box, and his complacency seemed confirmed by the arrangement.\n\n“Well, Fanny, this has been a fine day for you, upon my word,” said Mrs. Norris, as they drove through the park. “Nothing but pleasure from beginning to end! I am sure you ought to be very much obliged to your aunt Bertram and me for contriving to let you go. A pretty good day’s amusement you have had!”\n\nMaria was just discontented enough to say directly, “I think you have done pretty well yourself, ma’am. Your lap seems full of good things, and here is a basket of something between us which has been knocking my elbow unmercifully.”\n\n“My dear, it is only a beautiful little heath, which that nice old gardener would make me take; but if it is in your way, I will have it in my lap directly. There, Fanny, you shall carry that parcel for me; take great care of it: do not let it fall; it is a cream cheese, just like the excellent one we had at dinner. Nothing would satisfy that good old Mrs. Whitaker, but my taking one of the cheeses. I stood out as long as I could, till the tears almost came into her eyes, and I knew it was just the sort that my sister would be delighted with. That Mrs. Whitaker is a treasure! She was quite shocked when I asked her whether wine was allowed at the second table, and she has turned away two housemaids for wearing white gowns. Take care of the cheese, Fanny. Now I can manage the other parcel and the basket very well.”\n\n“What else have you been spunging?” said Maria, half-pleased that Sotherton should be so complimented.\n\n“Spunging, my dear! It is nothing but four of those beautiful pheasants’ eggs, which Mrs. Whitaker would quite force upon me: she would not take a denial. She said it must be such an amusement to me, as she understood I lived quite alone, to have a few living creatures of that sort; and so to be sure it will. I shall get the dairymaid to set them under the first spare hen, and if they come to good I can have them moved to my own house and borrow a coop; and it will be a great delight to me in my lonely hours to attend to them. And if I have good luck, your mother shall have some.”\n\nIt was a beautiful evening, mild and still, and the drive was as pleasant as the serenity of Nature could make it; but when Mrs. Norris ceased speaking, it was altogether a silent drive to those within. Their spirits were in general exhausted; and to determine whether the day had afforded most pleasure or pain, might occupy the meditations of almost all.\n\n# CHAPTER XI\n\nThe day at Sotherton, with all its imperfections, afforded the Miss Bertrams much more agreeable feelings than were derived from the letters from Antigua, which soon afterwards reached Mansfield. It was much pleasanter to think of Henry Crawford than of their father; and to think of their father in England again within a certain period, which these letters obliged them to do, was a most unwelcome exercise.\n\nNovember was the black month fixed for his return. Sir Thomas wrote of it with as much decision as experience and anxiety could authorise. His business was so nearly concluded as to justify him in proposing to take his passage in the September packet, and he consequently looked forward with the hope of being with his beloved family again early in November.\n\nMaria was more to be pitied than Julia; for to her the father brought a husband, and the return of the friend most solicitous for her happiness would unite her to the lover, on whom she had chosen that happiness should depend. It was a gloomy prospect, and all she could do was to throw a mist over it, and hope when the mist cleared away she should see something else. It would hardly be early in November, there were generally delays, a bad passage or something; that favouring something which everybody who shuts their eyes while they look, or their understandings while they reason, feels the comfort of. It would probably be the middle of November at least; the middle of November was three months off. Three months comprised thirteen weeks. Much might happen in thirteen weeks.\n\nSir Thomas would have been deeply mortified by a suspicion of half that his daughters felt on the subject of his return, and would hardly have found consolation in a knowledge of the interest it excited in the breast of another young lady. Miss Crawford, on walking up with her brother to spend the evening at Mansfield Park, heard the good news; and though seeming to have no concern in the affair beyond politeness, and to have vented all her feelings in a quiet congratulation, heard it with an attention not so easily satisfied. Mrs. Norris gave the particulars of the letters, and the subject was dropt; but after tea, as Miss Crawford was standing at an open window with Edmund and Fanny looking out on a twilight scene, while the Miss Bertrams, Mr. Rushworth, and Henry Crawford were all busy with candles at the pianoforte, she suddenly revived it by turning round towards the group, and saying, “How happy Mr. Rushworth looks! He is thinking of November.”\n\nEdmund looked round at Mr. Rushworth too, but had nothing to say.\n\n“Your father’s return will be a very interesting event.”\n\n“It will, indeed, after such an absence; an absence not only long, but including so many dangers.”\n\n“It will be the forerunner also of other interesting events: your sister’s marriage, and your taking orders.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Don’t be affronted,” said she, laughing, “but it does put me in mind of some of the old heathen heroes, who, after performing great exploits in a foreign land, offered sacrifices to the gods on their safe return.”\n\n“There is no sacrifice in the case,” replied Edmund, with a serious smile, and glancing at the pianoforte again; “it is entirely her own doing.”\n\n“Oh yes I know it is. I was merely joking. She has done no more than what every young woman would do; and I have no doubt of her being extremely happy. My other sacrifice, of course, you do not understand.”\n\n“My taking orders, I assure you, is quite as voluntary as Maria’s marrying.”\n\n“It is fortunate that your inclination and your father’s convenience should accord so well. There is a very good living kept for you, I understand, hereabouts.”\n\n“Which you suppose has biassed me?”\n\n“But that I am sure it has not,” cried Fanny.\n\n“Thank you for your good word, Fanny, but it is more than I would affirm myself. On the contrary, the knowing that there was such a provision for me probably did bias me. Nor can I think it wrong that it should. There was no natural disinclination to be overcome, and I see no reason why a man should make a worse clergyman for knowing that he will have a competence early in life. I was in safe hands. I hope I should not have been influenced myself in a wrong way, and I am sure my father was too conscientious to have allowed it. I have no doubt that I was biased, but I think it was blamelessly.”\n\n“It is the same sort of thing,” said Fanny, after a short pause, “as for the son of an admiral to go into the navy, or the son of a general to be in the army, and nobody sees anything wrong in that. Nobody wonders that they should prefer the line where their friends can serve them best, or suspects them to be less in earnest in it than they appear.”\n\n“No, my dear Miss Price, and for reasons good. The profession, either navy or army, is its own justification. It has everything in its favour: heroism, danger, bustle, fashion. Soldiers and sailors are always acceptable in society. Nobody can wonder that men are soldiers and sailors.”\n\n“But the motives of a man who takes orders with the certainty of preferment may be fairly suspected, you think?” said Edmund. “To be justified in your eyes, he must do it in the most complete uncertainty of any provision.”\n\n“What! take orders without a living! No; that is madness indeed; absolute madness.”\n\n“Shall I ask you how the church is to be filled, if a man is neither to take orders with a living nor without? No; for you certainly would not know what to say. But I must beg some advantage to the clergyman from your own argument. As he cannot be influenced by those feelings which you rank highly as temptation and reward to the soldier and sailor in their choice of a profession, as heroism, and noise, and fashion, are all against him, he ought to be less liable to the suspicion of wanting sincerity or good intentions in the choice of his.”\n\n“Oh! no doubt he is very sincere in preferring an income ready made, to the trouble of working for one; and has the best intentions of doing nothing all the rest of his days but eat, drink, and grow fat. It is indolence, Mr. Bertram, indeed. Indolence and love of ease; a want of all laudable ambition, of taste for good company, or of inclination to take the trouble of being agreeable, which make men clergymen. A clergyman has nothing to do but be slovenly and selfish—read the newspaper, watch the weather, and quarrel with his wife. His curate does all the work, and the business of his own life is to dine.”\n\n“There are such clergymen, no doubt, but I think they are not so common as to justify Miss Crawford in esteeming it their general character. I suspect that in this comprehensive and (may I say) commonplace censure, you are not judging from yourself, but from prejudiced persons, whose opinions you have been in the habit of hearing. It is impossible that your own observation can have given you much knowledge of the clergy. You can have been personally acquainted with very few of a set of men you condemn so conclusively. You are speaking what you have been told at your uncle’s table.”\n\n“I speak what appears to me the general opinion; and where an opinion is general, it is usually correct. Though I have not seen much of the domestic lives of clergymen, it is seen by too many to leave any deficiency of information.”\n\n“Where any one body of educated men, of whatever denomination, are condemned indiscriminately, there must be a deficiency of information, or (smiling) of something else. Your uncle, and his brother admirals, perhaps knew little of clergymen beyond the chaplains whom, good or bad, they were always wishing away.”\n\n“Poor William! He has met with great kindness from the chaplain of the Antwerp,” was a tender apostrophe of Fanny’s, very much to the purpose of her own feelings if not of the conversation.\n\n“I have been so little addicted to take my opinions from my uncle,” said Miss Crawford, “that I can hardly suppose—and since you push me so hard, I must observe, that I am not entirely without the means of seeing what clergymen are, being at this present time the guest of my own brother, Dr. Grant. And though Dr. Grant is most kind and obliging to me, and though he is really a gentleman, and, I dare say, a good scholar and clever, and often preaches good sermons, and is very respectable, I see him to be an indolent, selfish bon vivant, who must have his palate consulted in everything; who will not stir a finger for the convenience of any one; and who, moreover, if the cook makes a blunder, is out of humour with his excellent wife. To own the truth, Henry and I were partly driven out this very evening by a disappointment about a green goose, which he could not get the better of. My poor sister was forced to stay and bear it.”\n\n“I do not wonder at your disapprobation, upon my word. It is a great defect of temper, made worse by a very faulty habit of self-indulgence; and to see your sister suffering from it must be exceedingly painful to such feelings as yours. Fanny, it goes against us. We cannot attempt to defend Dr. Grant.”\n\n“No,” replied Fanny, “but we need not give up his profession for all that; because, whatever profession Dr. Grant had chosen, he would have taken a—not a good temper into it; and as he must, either in the navy or army, have had a great many more people under his command than he has now, I think more would have been made unhappy by him as a sailor or soldier than as a clergyman. Besides, I cannot but suppose that whatever there may be to wish otherwise in Dr. Grant would have been in a greater danger of becoming worse in a more active and worldly profession, where he would have had less time and obligation—where he might have escaped that knowledge of himself, the frequency, at least, of that knowledge which it is impossible he should escape as he is now. A man—a sensible man like Dr. Grant, cannot be in the habit of teaching others their duty every week, cannot go to church twice every Sunday, and preach such very good sermons in so good a manner as he does, without being the better for it himself. It must make him think; and I have no doubt that he oftener endeavours to restrain himself than he would if he had been anything but a clergyman.”\n\n“We cannot prove to the contrary, to be sure; but I wish you a better fate, Miss Price, than to be the wife of a man whose amiableness depends upon his own sermons; for though he may preach himself into a good-humour every Sunday, it will be bad enough to have him quarrelling about green geese from Monday morning till Saturday night.”\n\n“I think the man who could often quarrel with Fanny,” said Edmund affectionately, “must be beyond the reach of any sermons.”\n\nFanny turned farther into the window; and Miss Crawford had only time to say, in a pleasant manner, “I fancy Miss Price has been more used to deserve praise than to hear it”; when, being earnestly invited by the Miss Bertrams to join in a glee, she tripped off to the instrument, leaving Edmund looking after her in an ecstasy of admiration of all her many virtues, from her obliging manners down to her light and graceful tread.\n\n“There goes good-humour, I am sure,” said he presently. “There goes a temper which would never give pain! How well she walks! and how readily she falls in with the inclination of others! joining them the moment she is asked. What a pity,” he added, after an instant’s reflection, “that she should have been in such hands!”\n\nFanny agreed to it, and had the pleasure of seeing him continue at the window with her, in spite of the expected glee; and of having his eyes soon turned, like hers, towards the scene without, where all that was solemn, and soothing, and lovely, appeared in the brilliancy of an unclouded night, and the contrast of the deep shade of the woods. Fanny spoke her feelings. “Here’s harmony!” said she; “here’s repose! Here’s what may leave all painting and all music behind, and what poetry only can attempt to describe! Here’s what may tranquillise every care, and lift the heart to rapture! When I look out on such a night as this, I feel as if there could be neither wickedness nor sorrow in the world; and there certainly would be less of both if the sublimity of Nature were more attended to, and people were carried more out of themselves by contemplating such a scene.”\n\n“I like to hear your enthusiasm, Fanny. It is a lovely night, and they are much to be pitied who have not been taught to feel, in some degree, as you do; who have not, at least, been given a taste for Nature in early life. They lose a great deal.”\n\n“You taught me to think and feel on the subject, cousin.”\n\n“I had a very apt scholar. There’s Arcturus looking very bright.”\n\n“Yes, and the Bear. I wish I could see Cassiopeia.”\n\n“We must go out on the lawn for that. Should you be afraid?”\n\n“Not in the least. It is a great while since we have had any star-gazing.”\n\n“Yes; I do not know how it has happened.” The glee began. “We will stay till this is finished, Fanny,” said he, turning his back on the window; and as it advanced, she had the mortification of seeing him advance too, moving forward by gentle degrees towards the instrument, and when it ceased, he was close by the singers, among the most urgent in requesting to hear the glee again.\n\nFanny sighed alone at the window till scolded away by Mrs. Norris’s threats of catching cold.\n\n# CHAPTER XII\n\nSir Thomas was to return in November, and his eldest son had duties to call him earlier home. The approach of September brought tidings of Mr. Bertram, first in a letter to the gamekeeper and then in a letter to Edmund; and by the end of August he arrived himself, to be gay, agreeable, and gallant again as occasion served, or Miss Crawford demanded; to tell of races and Weymouth, and parties and friends, to which she might have listened six weeks before with some interest, and altogether to give her the fullest conviction, by the power of actual comparison, of her preferring his younger brother.\n\nIt was very vexatious, and she was heartily sorry for it; but so it was; and so far from now meaning to marry the elder, she did not even want to attract him beyond what the simplest claims of conscious beauty required: his lengthened absence from Mansfield, without anything but pleasure in view, and his own will to consult, made it perfectly clear that he did not care about her; and his indifference was so much more than equalled by her own, that were he now to step forth the owner of Mansfield Park, the Sir Thomas complete, which he was to be in time, she did not believe she could accept him.\n\nThe season and duties which brought Mr. Bertram back to Mansfield took Mr. Crawford into Norfolk. Everingham could not do without him in the beginning of September. He went for a fortnight—a fortnight of such dullness to the Miss Bertrams as ought to have put them both on their guard, and made even Julia admit, in her jealousy of her sister, the absolute necessity of distrusting his attentions, and wishing him not to return; and a fortnight of sufficient leisure, in the intervals of shooting and sleeping, to have convinced the gentleman that he ought to keep longer away, had he been more in the habit of examining his own motives, and of reflecting to what the indulgence of his idle vanity was tending; but, thoughtless and selfish from prosperity and bad example, he would not look beyond the present moment. The sisters, handsome, clever, and encouraging, were an amusement to his sated mind; and finding nothing in Norfolk to equal the social pleasures of Mansfield, he gladly returned to it at the time appointed, and was welcomed thither quite as gladly by those whom he came to trifle with further.\n\nMaria, with only Mr. Rushworth to attend to her, and doomed to the repeated details of his day’s sport, good or bad, his boast of his dogs, his jealousy of his neighbours, his doubts of their qualifications, and his zeal after poachers, subjects which will not find their way to female feelings without some talent on one side or some attachment on the other, had missed Mr. Crawford grievously; and Julia, unengaged and unemployed, felt all the right of missing him much more. Each sister believed herself the favourite. Julia might be justified in so doing by the hints of Mrs. Grant, inclined to credit what she wished, and Maria by the hints of Mr. Crawford himself. Everything returned into the same channel as before his absence; his manners being to each so animated and agreeable as to lose no ground with either, and just stopping short of the consistence, the steadiness, the solicitude, and the warmth which might excite general notice.\n\nFanny was the only one of the party who found anything to dislike; but since the day at Sotherton, she could never see Mr. Crawford with either sister without observation, and seldom without wonder or censure; and had her confidence in her own judgment been equal to her exercise of it in every other respect, had she been sure that she was seeing clearly, and judging candidly, she would probably have made some important communications to her usual confidant. As it was, however, she only hazarded a hint, and the hint was lost. “I am rather surprised,” said she, “that Mr. Crawford should come back again so soon, after being here so long before, full seven weeks; for I had understood he was so very fond of change and moving about, that I thought something would certainly occur, when he was once gone, to take him elsewhere. He is used to much gayer places than Mansfield.”\n\n“It is to his credit,” was Edmund’s answer; “and I dare say it gives his sister pleasure. She does not like his unsettled habits.”\n\n“What a favourite he is with my cousins!”\n\n“Yes, his manners to women are such as must please. Mrs. Grant, I believe, suspects him of a preference for Julia; I have never seen much symptom of it, but I wish it may be so. He has no faults but what a serious attachment would remove.”\n\n“If Miss Bertram were not engaged,” said Fanny cautiously, “I could sometimes almost think that he admired her more than Julia.”\n\n“Which is, perhaps, more in favour of his liking Julia best, than you, Fanny, may be aware; for I believe it often happens that a man, before he has quite made up his own mind, will distinguish the sister or intimate friend of the woman he is really thinking of more than the woman herself. Crawford has too much sense to stay here if he found himself in any danger from Maria; and I am not at all afraid for her, after such a proof as she has given that her feelings are not strong.”\n\nFanny supposed she must have been mistaken, and meant to think differently in future; but with all that submission to Edmund could do, and all the help of the coinciding looks and hints which she occasionally noticed in some of the others, and which seemed to say that Julia was Mr. Crawford’s choice, she knew not always what to think. She was privy, one evening, to the hopes of her aunt Norris on the subject, as well as to her feelings, and the feelings of Mrs. Rushworth, on a point of some similarity, and could not help wondering as she listened; and glad would she have been not to be obliged to listen, for it was while all the other young people were dancing, and she sitting, most unwillingly, among the chaperons at the fire, longing for the re-entrance of her elder cousin, on whom all her own hopes of a partner then depended. It was Fanny’s first ball, though without the preparation or splendour of many a young lady’s first ball, being the thought only of the afternoon, built on the late acquisition of a violin player in the servants’ hall, and the possibility of raising five couple with the help of Mrs. Grant and a new intimate friend of Mr. Bertram’s just arrived on a visit. It had, however, been a very happy one to Fanny through four dances, and she was quite grieved to be losing even a quarter of an hour. While waiting and wishing, looking now at the dancers and now at the door, this dialogue between the two above-mentioned ladies was forced on her—\n\n“I think, ma’am,” said Mrs. Norris, her eyes directed towards Mr. Rushworth and Maria, who were partners for the second time, “we shall see some happy faces again now.”\n\n“Yes, ma’am, indeed,” replied the other, with a stately simper, “there will be some satisfaction in looking on now, and I think it was rather a pity they should have been obliged to part. Young folks in their situation should be excused complying with the common forms. I wonder my son did not propose it.”\n\n“I dare say he did, ma’am. Mr. Rushworth is never remiss. But dear Maria has such a strict sense of propriety, so much of that true delicacy which one seldom meets with nowadays, Mrs. Rushworth—that wish of avoiding particularity! Dear ma’am, only look at her face at this moment; how different from what it was the two last dances!”\n\nMiss Bertram did indeed look happy, her eyes were sparkling with pleasure, and she was speaking with great animation, for Julia and her partner, Mr. Crawford, were close to her; they were all in a cluster together. How she had looked before, Fanny could not recollect, for she had been dancing with Edmund herself, and had not thought about her.\n\nMrs. Norris continued, “It is quite delightful, ma’am, to see young people so properly happy, so well suited, and so much the thing! I cannot but think of dear Sir Thomas’s delight. And what do you say, ma’am, to the chance of another match? Mr. Rushworth has set a good example, and such things are very catching.”\n\nMrs. Rushworth, who saw nothing but her son, was quite at a loss.\n\n“The couple above, ma’am. Do you see no symptoms there?”\n\n“Oh dear! Miss Julia and Mr. Crawford. Yes, indeed, a very pretty match. What is his property?”\n\n“Four thousand a year.”\n\n“Very well. Those who have not more must be satisfied with what they have. Four thousand a year is a pretty estate, and he seems a very genteel, steady young man, so I hope Miss Julia will be very happy.”\n\n“It is not a settled thing, ma’am, yet. We only speak of it among friends. But I have very little doubt it will be. He is growing extremely particular in his attentions.”\n\nFanny could listen no farther. Listening and wondering were all suspended for a time, for Mr. Bertram was in the room again; and though feeling it would be a great honour to be asked by him, she thought it must happen. He came towards their little circle; but instead of asking her to dance, drew a chair near her, and gave her an account of the present state of a sick horse, and the opinion of the groom, from whom he had just parted. Fanny found that it was not to be, and in the modesty of her nature immediately felt that she had been unreasonable in expecting it. When he had told of his horse, he took a newspaper from the table, and looking over it, said in a languid way, “If you want to dance, Fanny, I will stand up with you.” With more than equal civility the offer was declined; she did not wish to dance. “I am glad of it,” said he, in a much brisker tone, and throwing down the newspaper again, “for I am tired to death. I only wonder how the good people can keep it up so long. They had need be all in love, to find any amusement in such folly; and so they are, I fancy. If you look at them you may see they are so many couple of lovers—all but Yates and Mrs. Grant—and, between ourselves, she, poor woman, must want a lover as much as any one of them. A desperate dull life hers must be with the doctor,” making a sly face as he spoke towards the chair of the latter, who proving, however, to be close at his elbow, made so instantaneous a change of expression and subject necessary, as Fanny, in spite of everything, could hardly help laughing at. “A strange business this in America, Dr. Grant! What is your opinion? I always come to you to know what I am to think of public matters.”\n\n“My dear Tom,” cried his aunt soon afterwards, “as you are not dancing, I dare say you will have no objection to join us in a rubber; shall you?” Then leaving her seat, and coming to him to enforce the proposal, added in a whisper, “We want to make a table for Mrs. Rushworth, you know. Your mother is quite anxious about it, but cannot very well spare time to sit down herself, because of her fringe. Now, you and I and Dr. Grant will just do; and though we play but half-crowns, you know, you may bet half-guineas with him.”\n\n“I should be most happy,” replied he aloud, and jumping up with alacrity, “it would give me the greatest pleasure; but that I am this moment going to dance. Come, Fanny,” taking her hand, “do not be dawdling any longer, or the dance will be over.”\n\nFanny was led off very willingly, though it was impossible for her to feel much gratitude towards her cousin, or distinguish, as he certainly did, between the selfishness of another person and his own.\n\n“A pretty modest request upon my word,” he indignantly exclaimed as they walked away. “To want to nail me to a card-table for the next two hours with herself and Dr. Grant, who are always quarrelling, and that poking old woman, who knows no more of whist than of algebra. I wish my good aunt would be a little less busy! And to ask me in such a way too! without ceremony, before them all, so as to leave me no possibility of refusing. That is what I dislike most particularly. It raises my spleen more than anything, to have the pretence of being asked, of being given a choice, and at the same time addressed in such a way as to oblige one to do the very thing, whatever it be! If I had not luckily thought of standing up with you I could not have got out of it. It is a great deal too bad. But when my aunt has got a fancy in her head, nothing can stop her.”\n\n# CHAPTER XIII\n\nThe Honourable John Yates, this new friend, had not much to recommend him beyond habits of fashion and expense, and being the younger son of a lord with a tolerable independence; and Sir Thomas would probably have thought his introduction at Mansfield by no means desirable. Mr. Bertram’s acquaintance with him had begun at Weymouth, where they had spent ten days together in the same society, and the friendship, if friendship it might be called, had been proved and perfected by Mr. Yates’s being invited to take Mansfield in his way, whenever he could, and by his promising to come; and he did come rather earlier than had been expected, in consequence of the sudden breaking-up of a large party assembled for gaiety at the house of another friend, which he had left Weymouth to join. He came on the wings of disappointment, and with his head full of acting, for it had been a theatrical party; and the play in which he had borne a part was within two days of representation, when the sudden death of one of the nearest connexions of the family had destroyed the scheme and dispersed the performers. To be so near happiness, so near fame, so near the long paragraph in praise of the private theatricals at Ecclesford, the seat of the Right Hon. Lord Ravenshaw, in Cornwall, which would of course have immortalised the whole party for at least a twelvemonth! and being so near, to lose it all, was an injury to be keenly felt, and Mr. Yates could talk of nothing else. Ecclesford and its theatre, with its arrangements and dresses, rehearsals and jokes, was his never-failing subject, and to boast of the past his only consolation.\n\nHappily for him, a love of the theatre is so general, an itch for acting so strong among young people, that he could hardly out-talk the interest of his hearers. From the first casting of the parts to the epilogue it was all bewitching, and there were few who did not wish to have been a party concerned, or would have hesitated to try their skill. The play had been Lovers’ Vows, and Mr. Yates was to have been Count Cassel. “A trifling part,” said he, “and not at all to my taste, and such a one as I certainly would not accept again; but I was determined to make no difficulties. Lord Ravenshaw and the duke had appropriated the only two characters worth playing before I reached Ecclesford; and though Lord Ravenshaw offered to resign his to me, it was impossible to take it, you know. I was sorry for him that he should have so mistaken his powers, for he was no more equal to the Baron—a little man with a weak voice, always hoarse after the first ten minutes. It must have injured the piece materially; but I was resolved to make no difficulties. Sir Henry thought the duke not equal to Frederick, but that was because Sir Henry wanted the part himself; whereas it was certainly in the best hands of the two. I was surprised to see Sir Henry such a stick. Luckily the strength of the piece did not depend upon him. Our Agatha was inimitable, and the duke was thought very great by many. And upon the whole, it would certainly have gone off wonderfully.”\n\n“It was a hard case, upon my word”; and, “I do think you were very much to be pitied,” were the kind responses of listening sympathy.\n\n“It is not worth complaining about; but to be sure the poor old dowager could not have died at a worse time; and it is impossible to help wishing that the news could have been suppressed for just the three days we wanted. It was but three days; and being only a grandmother, and all happening two hundred miles off, I think there would have been no great harm, and it was suggested, I know; but Lord Ravenshaw, who I suppose is one of the most correct men in England, would not hear of it.”\n\n“An afterpiece instead of a comedy,” said Mr. Bertram. “Lovers’ Vows were at an end, and Lord and Lady Ravenshaw left to act My Grandmother by themselves. Well, the jointure may comfort him; and perhaps, between friends, he began to tremble for his credit and his lungs in the Baron, and was not sorry to withdraw; and to make you amends, Yates, I think we must raise a little theatre at Mansfield, and ask you to be our manager.”\n\nThis, though the thought of the moment, did not end with the moment; for the inclination to act was awakened, and in no one more strongly than in him who was now master of the house; and who, having so much leisure as to make almost any novelty a certain good, had likewise such a degree of lively talents and comic taste, as were exactly adapted to the novelty of acting. The thought returned again and again. “Oh for the Ecclesford theatre and scenery to try something with.” Each sister could echo the wish; and Henry Crawford, to whom, in all the riot of his gratifications it was yet an untasted pleasure, was quite alive at the idea. “I really believe,” said he, “I could be fool enough at this moment to undertake any character that ever was written, from Shylock or Richard III down to the singing hero of a farce in his scarlet coat and cocked hat. I feel as if I could be anything or everything; as if I could rant and storm, or sigh or cut capers, in any tragedy or comedy in the English language. Let us be doing something. Be it only half a play, an act, a scene; what should prevent us? Not these countenances, I am sure,” looking towards the Miss Bertrams; “and for a theatre, what signifies a theatre? We shall be only amusing ourselves. Any room in this house might suffice.”\n\n“We must have a curtain,” said Tom Bertram; “a few yards of green baize for a curtain, and perhaps that may be enough.”\n\n“Oh, quite enough,” cried Mr. Yates, “with only just a side wing or two run up, doors in flat, and three or four scenes to be let down; nothing more would be necessary on such a plan as this. For mere amusement among ourselves we should want nothing more.”\n\n“I believe we must be satisfied with less,” said Maria. “There would not be time, and other difficulties would arise. We must rather adopt Mr. Crawford’s views, and make the performance, not the theatre, our object. Many parts of our best plays are independent of scenery.”\n\n“Nay,” said Edmund, who began to listen with alarm. “Let us do nothing by halves. If we are to act, let it be in a theatre completely fitted up with pit, boxes, and gallery, and let us have a play entire from beginning to end; so as it be a German play, no matter what, with a good tricking, shifting afterpiece, and a figure-dance, and a hornpipe, and a song between the acts. If we do not outdo Ecclesford, we do nothing.”\n\n“Now, Edmund, do not be disagreeable,” said Julia. “Nobody loves a play better than you do, or can have gone much farther to see one.”\n\n“True, to see real acting, good hardened real acting; but I would hardly walk from this room to the next to look at the raw efforts of those who have not been bred to the trade: a set of gentlemen and ladies, who have all the disadvantages of education and decorum to struggle through.”\n\nAfter a short pause, however, the subject still continued, and was discussed with unabated eagerness, every one’s inclination increasing by the discussion, and a knowledge of the inclination of the rest; and though nothing was settled but that Tom Bertram would prefer a comedy, and his sisters and Henry Crawford a tragedy, and that nothing in the world could be easier than to find a piece which would please them all, the resolution to act something or other seemed so decided as to make Edmund quite uncomfortable. He was determined to prevent it, if possible, though his mother, who equally heard the conversation which passed at table, did not evince the least disapprobation.\n\nThe same evening afforded him an opportunity of trying his strength. Maria, Julia, Henry Crawford, and Mr. Yates were in the billiard-room. Tom, returning from them into the drawing-room, where Edmund was standing thoughtfully by the fire, while Lady Bertram was on the sofa at a little distance, and Fanny close beside her arranging her work, thus began as he entered—“Such a horribly vile billiard-table as ours is not to be met with, I believe, above ground. I can stand it no longer, and I think, I may say, that nothing shall ever tempt me to it again; but one good thing I have just ascertained: it is the very room for a theatre, precisely the shape and length for it; and the doors at the farther end, communicating with each other, as they may be made to do in five minutes, by merely moving the bookcase in my father’s room, is the very thing we could have desired, if we had sat down to wish for it; and my father’s room will be an excellent greenroom. It seems to join the billiard-room on purpose.”\n\n“You are not serious, Tom, in meaning to act?” said Edmund, in a low voice, as his brother approached the fire.\n\n“Not serious! never more so, I assure you. What is there to surprise you in it?”\n\n“I think it would be very wrong. In a general light, private theatricals are open to some objections, but as we are circumstanced, I must think it would be highly injudicious, and more than injudicious to attempt anything of the kind. It would shew great want of feeling on my father’s account, absent as he is, and in some degree of constant danger; and it would be imprudent, I think, with regard to Maria, whose situation is a very delicate one, considering everything, extremely delicate.”\n\n“You take up a thing so seriously! as if we were going to act three times a week till my father’s return, and invite all the country. But it is not to be a display of that sort. We mean nothing but a little amusement among ourselves, just to vary the scene, and exercise our powers in something new. We want no audience, no publicity. We may be trusted, I think, in chusing some play most perfectly unexceptionable; and I can conceive no greater harm or danger to any of us in conversing in the elegant written language of some respectable author than in chattering in words of our own. I have no fears and no scruples. And as to my father’s being absent, it is so far from an objection, that I consider it rather as a motive; for the expectation of his return must be a very anxious period to my mother; and if we can be the means of amusing that anxiety, and keeping up her spirits for the next few weeks, I shall think our time very well spent, and so, I am sure, will he. It is a very anxious period for her.”\n\nAs he said this, each looked towards their mother. Lady Bertram, sunk back in one corner of the sofa, the picture of health, wealth, ease, and tranquillity, was just falling into a gentle doze, while Fanny was getting through the few difficulties of her work for her.\n\nEdmund smiled and shook his head.\n\n“By Jove! this won’t do,” cried Tom, throwing himself into a chair with a hearty laugh. “To be sure, my dear mother, your anxiety—I was unlucky there.”\n\n“What is the matter?” asked her ladyship, in the heavy tone of one half-roused; “I was not asleep.”\n\n“Oh dear, no, ma’am, nobody suspected you! Well, Edmund,” he continued, returning to the former subject, posture, and voice, as soon as Lady Bertram began to nod again, “but this I will maintain, that we shall be doing no harm.”\n\n“I cannot agree with you; I am convinced that my father would totally disapprove it.”\n\n“And I am convinced to the contrary. Nobody is fonder of the exercise of talent in young people, or promotes it more, than my father, and for anything of the acting, spouting, reciting kind, I think he has always a decided taste. I am sure he encouraged it in us as boys. How many a time have we mourned over the dead body of Julius Caesar, and to be’d and not to be’d, in this very room, for his amusement? And I am sure, my name was Norval, every evening of my life through one Christmas holidays.”\n\n“It was a very different thing. You must see the difference yourself. My father wished us, as schoolboys, to speak well, but he would never wish his grown-up daughters to be acting plays. His sense of decorum is strict.”\n\n“I know all that,” said Tom, displeased. “I know my father as well as you do; and I’ll take care that his daughters do nothing to distress him. Manage your own concerns, Edmund, and I’ll take care of the rest of the family.”\n\n“If you are resolved on acting,” replied the persevering Edmund, “I must hope it will be in a very small and quiet way; and I think a theatre ought not to be attempted. It would be taking liberties with my father’s house in his absence which could not be justified.”\n\n“For everything of that nature I will be answerable,” said Tom, in a decided tone. “His house shall not be hurt. I have quite as great an interest in being careful of his house as you can have; and as to such alterations as I was suggesting just now, such as moving a bookcase, or unlocking a door, or even as using the billiard-room for the space of a week without playing at billiards in it, you might just as well suppose he would object to our sitting more in this room, and less in the breakfast-room, than we did before he went away, or to my sister’s pianoforte being moved from one side of the room to the other. Absolute nonsense!”\n\n“The innovation, if not wrong as an innovation, will be wrong as an expense.”\n\n“Yes, the expense of such an undertaking would be prodigious! Perhaps it might cost a whole twenty pounds. Something of a theatre we must have undoubtedly, but it will be on the simplest plan: a green curtain and a little carpenter’s work, and that’s all; and as the carpenter’s work may be all done at home by Christopher Jackson himself, it will be too absurd to talk of expense; and as long as Jackson is employed, everything will be right with Sir Thomas. Don’t imagine that nobody in this house can see or judge but yourself. Don’t act yourself, if you do not like it, but don’t expect to govern everybody else.”\n\n“No, as to acting myself,” said Edmund, “that I absolutely protest against.”\n\nTom walked out of the room as he said it, and Edmund was left to sit down and stir the fire in thoughtful vexation.\n\nFanny, who had heard it all, and borne Edmund company in every feeling throughout the whole, now ventured to say, in her anxiety to suggest some comfort, “Perhaps they may not be able to find any play to suit them. Your brother’s taste and your sisters’ seem very different.”\n\n“I have no hope there, Fanny. If they persist in the scheme, they will find something. I shall speak to my sisters and try to dissuade them, and that is all I can do.”\n\n“I should think my aunt Norris would be on your side.”\n\n“I dare say she would, but she has no influence with either Tom or my sisters that could be of any use; and if I cannot convince them myself, I shall let things take their course, without attempting it through her. Family squabbling is the greatest evil of all, and we had better do anything than be altogether by the ears.”\n\nHis sisters, to whom he had an opportunity of speaking the next morning, were quite as impatient of his advice, quite as unyielding to his representation, quite as determined in the cause of pleasure, as Tom. Their mother had no objection to the plan, and they were not in the least afraid of their father’s disapprobation. There could be no harm in what had been done in so many respectable families, and by so many women of the first consideration; and it must be scrupulousness run mad that could see anything to censure in a plan like theirs, comprehending only brothers and sisters and intimate friends, and which would never be heard of beyond themselves. Julia did seem inclined to admit that Maria’s situation might require particular caution and delicacy—but that could not extend to her—she was at liberty; and Maria evidently considered her engagement as only raising her so much more above restraint, and leaving her less occasion than Julia to consult either father or mother. Edmund had little to hope, but he was still urging the subject when Henry Crawford entered the room, fresh from the Parsonage, calling out, “No want of hands in our theatre, Miss Bertram. No want of understrappers: my sister desires her love, and hopes to be admitted into the company, and will be happy to take the part of any old duenna or tame confidante, that you may not like to do yourselves.”\n\nMaria gave Edmund a glance, which meant, “What say you now? Can we be wrong if Mary Crawford feels the same?” And Edmund, silenced, was obliged to acknowledge that the charm of acting might well carry fascination to the mind of genius; and with the ingenuity of love, to dwell more on the obliging, accommodating purport of the message than on anything else.\n\nThe scheme advanced. Opposition was vain; and as to Mrs. Norris, he was mistaken in supposing she would wish to make any. She started no difficulties that were not talked down in five minutes by her eldest nephew and niece, who were all-powerful with her; and as the whole arrangement was to bring very little expense to anybody, and none at all to herself, as she foresaw in it all the comforts of hurry, bustle, and importance, and derived the immediate advantage of fancying herself obliged to leave her own house, where she had been living a month at her own cost, and take up her abode in theirs, that every hour might be spent in their service, she was, in fact, exceedingly delighted with the project.\n\n# CHAPTER XIV\n\nFanny seemed nearer being right than Edmund had supposed. The business of finding a play that would suit everybody proved to be no trifle; and the carpenter had received his orders and taken his measurements, had suggested and removed at least two sets of difficulties, and having made the necessity of an enlargement of plan and expense fully evident, was already at work, while a play was still to seek. Other preparations were also in hand. An enormous roll of green baize had arrived from Northampton, and been cut out by Mrs. Norris (with a saving by her good management of full three-quarters of a yard), and was actually forming into a curtain by the housemaids, and still the play was wanting; and as two or three days passed away in this manner, Edmund began almost to hope that none might ever be found.\n\nThere were, in fact, so many things to be attended to, so many people to be pleased, so many best characters required, and, above all, such a need that the play should be at once both tragedy and comedy, that there did seem as little chance of a decision as anything pursued by youth and zeal could hold out.\n\nOn the tragic side were the Miss Bertrams, Henry Crawford, and Mr. Yates; on the comic, Tom Bertram, not quite alone, because it was evident that Mary Crawford’s wishes, though politely kept back, inclined the same way: but his determinateness and his power seemed to make allies unnecessary; and, independent of this great irreconcilable difference, they wanted a piece containing very few characters in the whole, but every character first-rate, and three principal women. All the best plays were run over in vain. Neither Hamlet, nor Macbeth, nor Othello, nor Douglas, nor The Gamester, presented anything that could satisfy even the tragedians; and The Rivals, The School for Scandal, Wheel of Fortune, Heir at Law, and a long et cetera, were successively dismissed with yet warmer objections. No piece could be proposed that did not supply somebody with a difficulty, and on one side or the other it was a continual repetition of, “Oh no, that will never do! Let us have no ranting tragedies. Too many characters. Not a tolerable woman’s part in the play. Anything but that, my dear Tom. It would be impossible to fill it up. One could not expect anybody to take such a part. Nothing but buffoonery from beginning to end. That might do, perhaps, but for the low parts. If I must give my opinion, I have always thought it the most insipid play in the English language. I do not wish to make objections; I shall be happy to be of any use, but I think we could not chuse worse.”\n\nFanny looked on and listened, not unamused to observe the selfishness which, more or less disguised, seemed to govern them all, and wondering how it would end. For her own gratification she could have wished that something might be acted, for she had never seen even half a play, but everything of higher consequence was against it.\n\n“This will never do,” said Tom Bertram at last. “We are wasting time most abominably. Something must be fixed on. No matter what, so that something is chosen. We must not be so nice. A few characters too many must not frighten us. We must double them. We must descend a little. If a part is insignificant, the greater our credit in making anything of it. From this moment I make no difficulties. I take any part you chuse to give me, so as it be comic. Let it but be comic, I condition for nothing more.”\n\nFor about the fifth time he then proposed the Heir at Law, doubting only whether to prefer Lord Duberley or Dr. Pangloss for himself; and very earnestly, but very unsuccessfully, trying to persuade the others that there were some fine tragic parts in the rest of the Dramatis Personæ.\n\nThe pause which followed this fruitless effort was ended by the same speaker, who, taking up one of the many volumes of plays that lay on the table, and turning it over, suddenly exclaimed—“Lovers’ Vows! And why should not Lovers’ Vows do for us as well as for the Ravenshaws? How came it never to be thought of before? It strikes me as if it would do exactly. What say you all? Here are two capital tragic parts for Yates and Crawford, and here is the rhyming Butler for me, if nobody else wants it; a trifling part, but the sort of thing I should not dislike, and, as I said before, I am determined to take anything and do my best. And as for the rest, they may be filled up by anybody. It is only Count Cassel and Anhalt.”\n\nThe suggestion was generally welcome. Everybody was growing weary of indecision, and the first idea with everybody was, that nothing had been proposed before so likely to suit them all. Mr. Yates was particularly pleased: he had been sighing and longing to do the Baron at Ecclesford, had grudged every rant of Lord Ravenshaw’s, and been forced to re-rant it all in his own room. The storm through Baron Wildenheim was the height of his theatrical ambition; and with the advantage of knowing half the scenes by heart already, he did now, with the greatest alacrity, offer his services for the part. To do him justice, however, he did not resolve to appropriate it; for remembering that there was some very good ranting-ground in Frederick, he professed an equal willingness for that. Henry Crawford was ready to take either. Whichever Mr. Yates did not chuse would perfectly satisfy him, and a short parley of compliment ensued. Miss Bertram, feeling all the interest of an Agatha in the question, took on her to decide it, by observing to Mr. Yates that this was a point in which height and figure ought to be considered, and that his being the tallest, seemed to fit him peculiarly for the Baron. She was acknowledged to be quite right, and the two parts being accepted accordingly, she was certain of the proper Frederick. Three of the characters were now cast, besides Mr. Rushworth, who was always answered for by Maria as willing to do anything; when Julia, meaning, like her sister, to be Agatha, began to be scrupulous on Miss Crawford’s account.\n\n“This is not behaving well by the absent,” said she. “Here are not women enough. Amelia and Agatha may do for Maria and me, but here is nothing for your sister, Mr. Crawford.”\n\nMr. Crawford desired that might not be thought of: he was very sure his sister had no wish of acting but as she might be useful, and that she would not allow herself to be considered in the present case. But this was immediately opposed by Tom Bertram, who asserted the part of Amelia to be in every respect the property of Miss Crawford, if she would accept it. “It falls as naturally, as necessarily to her,” said he, “as Agatha does to one or other of my sisters. It can be no sacrifice on their side, for it is highly comic.”\n\nA short silence followed. Each sister looked anxious; for each felt the best claim to Agatha, and was hoping to have it pressed on her by the rest. Henry Crawford, who meanwhile had taken up the play, and with seeming carelessness was turning over the first act, soon settled the business.\n\n“I must entreat Miss Julia Bertram,” said he, “not to engage in the part of Agatha, or it will be the ruin of all my solemnity. You must not, indeed you must not” (turning to her). “I could not stand your countenance dressed up in woe and paleness. The many laughs we have had together would infallibly come across me, and Frederick and his knapsack would be obliged to run away.”\n\nPleasantly, courteously, it was spoken; but the manner was lost in the matter to Julia’s feelings. She saw a glance at Maria which confirmed the injury to herself: it was a scheme, a trick; she was slighted, Maria was preferred; the smile of triumph which Maria was trying to suppress shewed how well it was understood; and before Julia could command herself enough to speak, her brother gave his weight against her too, by saying, “Oh yes! Maria must be Agatha. Maria will be the best Agatha. Though Julia fancies she prefers tragedy, I would not trust her in it. There is nothing of tragedy about her. She has not the look of it. Her features are not tragic features, and she walks too quick, and speaks too quick, and would not keep her countenance. She had better do the old countrywoman: the Cottager’s wife; you had, indeed, Julia. Cottager’s wife is a very pretty part, I assure you. The old lady relieves the high-flown benevolence of her husband with a good deal of spirit. You shall be Cottager’s wife.”\n\n“Cottager’s wife!” cried Mr. Yates. “What are you talking of? The most trivial, paltry, insignificant part; the merest commonplace; not a tolerable speech in the whole. Your sister do that! It is an insult to propose it. At Ecclesford the governess was to have done it. We all agreed that it could not be offered to anybody else. A little more justice, Mr. Manager, if you please. You do not deserve the office, if you cannot appreciate the talents of your company a little better.”\n\n“Why, as to that, my good friend, till I and my company have really acted there must be some guesswork; but I mean no disparagement to Julia. We cannot have two Agathas, and we must have one Cottager’s wife; and I am sure I set her the example of moderation myself in being satisfied with the old Butler. If the part is trifling she will have more credit in making something of it; and if she is so desperately bent against everything humorous, let her take Cottager’s speeches instead of Cottager’s wife’s, and so change the parts all through; he is solemn and pathetic enough, I am sure. It could make no difference in the play, and as for Cottager himself, when he has got his wife’s speeches, I would undertake him with all my heart.”\n\n“With all your partiality for Cottager’s wife,” said Henry Crawford, “it will be impossible to make anything of it fit for your sister, and we must not suffer her good-nature to be imposed on. We must not allow her to accept the part. She must not be left to her own complaisance. Her talents will be wanted in Amelia. Amelia is a character more difficult to be well represented than even Agatha. I consider Amelia is the most difficult character in the whole piece. It requires great powers, great nicety, to give her playfulness and simplicity without extravagance. I have seen good actresses fail in the part. Simplicity, indeed, is beyond the reach of almost every actress by profession. It requires a delicacy of feeling which they have not. It requires a gentlewoman—a Julia Bertram. You will undertake it, I hope?” turning to her with a look of anxious entreaty, which softened her a little; but while she hesitated what to say, her brother again interposed with Miss Crawford’s better claim.\n\n“No, no, Julia must not be Amelia. It is not at all the part for her. She would not like it. She would not do well. She is too tall and robust. Amelia should be a small, light, girlish, skipping figure. It is fit for Miss Crawford, and Miss Crawford only. She looks the part, and I am persuaded will do it admirably.”\n\nWithout attending to this, Henry Crawford continued his supplication. “You must oblige us,” said he, “indeed you must. When you have studied the character, I am sure you will feel it suit you. Tragedy may be your choice, but it will certainly appear that comedy chuses you. You will be to visit me in prison with a basket of provisions; you will not refuse to visit me in prison? I think I see you coming in with your basket.”\n\nThe influence of his voice was felt. Julia wavered; but was he only trying to soothe and pacify her, and make her overlook the previous affront? She distrusted him. The slight had been most determined. He was, perhaps, but at treacherous play with her. She looked suspiciously at her sister; Maria’s countenance was to decide it: if she were vexed and alarmed—but Maria looked all serenity and satisfaction, and Julia well knew that on this ground Maria could not be happy but at her expense. With hasty indignation, therefore, and a tremulous voice, she said to him, “You do not seem afraid of not keeping your countenance when I come in with a basket of provisions—though one might have supposed—but it is only as Agatha that I was to be so overpowering!” She stopped—Henry Crawford looked rather foolish, and as if he did not know what to say. Tom Bertram began again—\n\n“Miss Crawford must be Amelia. She will be an excellent Amelia.”\n\n“Do not be afraid of my wanting the character,” cried Julia, with angry quickness: “I am not to be Agatha, and I am sure I will do nothing else; and as to Amelia, it is of all parts in the world the most disgusting to me. I quite detest her. An odious, little, pert, unnatural, impudent girl. I have always protested against comedy, and this is comedy in its worst form.” And so saying, she walked hastily out of the room, leaving awkward feelings to more than one, but exciting small compassion in any except Fanny, who had been a quiet auditor of the whole, and who could not think of her as under the agitations of jealousy without great pity.\n\nA short silence succeeded her leaving them; but her brother soon returned to business and Lovers’ Vows, and was eagerly looking over the play, with Mr. Yates’s help, to ascertain what scenery would be necessary—while Maria and Henry Crawford conversed together in an under-voice, and the declaration with which she began of, “I am sure I would give up the part to Julia most willingly, but that though I shall probably do it very ill, I feel persuaded she would do it worse,” was doubtless receiving all the compliments it called for.\n\nWhen this had lasted some time, the division of the party was completed by Tom Bertram and Mr. Yates walking off together to consult farther in the room now beginning to be called the Theatre, and Miss Bertram’s resolving to go down to the Parsonage herself with the offer of Amelia to Miss Crawford; and Fanny remained alone.\n\nThe first use she made of her solitude was to take up the volume which had been left on the table, and begin to acquaint herself with the play of which she had heard so much. Her curiosity was all awake, and she ran through it with an eagerness which was suspended only by intervals of astonishment, that it could be chosen in the present instance, that it could be proposed and accepted in a private theatre! Agatha and Amelia appeared to her in their different ways so totally improper for home representation—the situation of one, and the language of the other, so unfit to be expressed by any woman of modesty, that she could hardly suppose her cousins could be aware of what they were engaging in; and longed to have them roused as soon as possible by the remonstrance which Edmund would certainly make.\n\n# CHAPTER XV\n\nMiss Crawford accepted the part very readily; and soon after Miss Bertram’s return from the Parsonage, Mr. Rushworth arrived, and another character was consequently cast. He had the offer of Count Cassel and Anhalt, and at first did not know which to chuse, and wanted Miss Bertram to direct him; but upon being made to understand the different style of the characters, and which was which, and recollecting that he had once seen the play in London, and had thought Anhalt a very stupid fellow, he soon decided for the Count. Miss Bertram approved the decision, for the less he had to learn the better; and though she could not sympathise in his wish that the Count and Agatha might be to act together, nor wait very patiently while he was slowly turning over the leaves with the hope of still discovering such a scene, she very kindly took his part in hand, and curtailed every speech that admitted being shortened; besides pointing out the necessity of his being very much dressed, and chusing his colours. Mr. Rushworth liked the idea of his finery very well, though affecting to despise it; and was too much engaged with what his own appearance would be to think of the others, or draw any of those conclusions, or feel any of that displeasure which Maria had been half prepared for.\n\nThus much was settled before Edmund, who had been out all the morning, knew anything of the matter; but when he entered the drawing-room before dinner, the buzz of discussion was high between Tom, Maria, and Mr. Yates; and Mr. Rushworth stepped forward with great alacrity to tell him the agreeable news.\n\n“We have got a play,” said he. “It is to be Lovers’ Vows; and I am to be Count Cassel, and am to come in first with a blue dress and a pink satin cloak, and afterwards am to have another fine fancy suit, by way of a shooting-dress. I do not know how I shall like it.”\n\nFanny’s eyes followed Edmund, and her heart beat for him as she heard this speech, and saw his look, and felt what his sensations must be.\n\n“Lovers’ Vows!” in a tone of the greatest amazement, was his only reply to Mr. Rushworth, and he turned towards his brother and sisters as if hardly doubting a contradiction.\n\n“Yes,” cried Mr. Yates. “After all our debatings and difficulties, we find there is nothing that will suit us altogether so well, nothing so unexceptionable, as Lovers’ Vows. The wonder is that it should not have been thought of before. My stupidity was abominable, for here we have all the advantage of what I saw at Ecclesford; and it is so useful to have anything of a model! We have cast almost every part.”\n\n“But what do you do for women?” said Edmund gravely, and looking at Maria.\n\nMaria blushed in spite of herself as she answered, “I take the part which Lady Ravenshaw was to have done, and” (with a bolder eye) “Miss Crawford is to be Amelia.”\n\n“I should not have thought it the sort of play to be so easily filled up, with us,” replied Edmund, turning away to the fire, where sat his mother, aunt, and Fanny, and seating himself with a look of great vexation.\n\nMr. Rushworth followed him to say, “I come in three times, and have two-and-forty speeches. That’s something, is not it? But I do not much like the idea of being so fine. I shall hardly know myself in a blue dress and a pink satin cloak.”\n\nEdmund could not answer him. In a few minutes Mr. Bertram was called out of the room to satisfy some doubts of the carpenter; and being accompanied by Mr. Yates, and followed soon afterwards by Mr. Rushworth, Edmund almost immediately took the opportunity of saying, “I cannot, before Mr. Yates, speak what I feel as to this play, without reflecting on his friends at Ecclesford; but I must now, my dear Maria, tell you, that I think it exceedingly unfit for private representation, and that I hope you will give it up. I cannot but suppose you will when you have read it carefully over. Read only the first act aloud to either your mother or aunt, and see how you can approve it. It will not be necessary to send you to your father’s judgment, I am convinced.”\n\n“We see things very differently,” cried Maria. “I am perfectly acquainted with the play, I assure you; and with a very few omissions, and so forth, which will be made, of course, I can see nothing objectionable in it; and I am not the only young woman you find who thinks it very fit for private representation.”\n\n“I am sorry for it,” was his answer; “but in this matter it is you who are to lead. You must set the example. If others have blundered, it is your place to put them right, and shew them what true delicacy is. In all points of decorum your conduct must be law to the rest of the party.”\n\nThis picture of her consequence had some effect, for no one loved better to lead than Maria; and with far more good-humour she answered, “I am much obliged to you, Edmund; you mean very well, I am sure: but I still think you see things too strongly; and I really cannot undertake to harangue all the rest upon a subject of this kind. There would be the greatest indecorum, I think.”\n\n“Do you imagine that I could have such an idea in my head? No; let your conduct be the only harangue. Say that, on examining the part, you feel yourself unequal to it; that you find it requiring more exertion and confidence than you can be supposed to have. Say this with firmness, and it will be quite enough. All who can distinguish will understand your motive. The play will be given up, and your delicacy honoured as it ought.”\n\n“Do not act anything improper, my dear,” said Lady Bertram. “Sir Thomas would not like it.—Fanny, ring the bell; I must have my dinner.—To be sure, Julia is dressed by this time.”\n\n“I am convinced, madam,” said Edmund, preventing Fanny, “that Sir Thomas would not like it.”\n\n“There, my dear, do you hear what Edmund says?”\n\n“If I were to decline the part,” said Maria, with renewed zeal, “Julia would certainly take it.”\n\n“What!” cried Edmund, “if she knew your reasons!”\n\n“Oh! she might think the difference between us—the difference in our situations—that she need not be so scrupulous as I might feel necessary. I am sure she would argue so. No; you must excuse me; I cannot retract my consent; it is too far settled, everybody would be so disappointed, Tom would be quite angry; and if we are so very nice, we shall never act anything.”\n\n“I was just going to say the very same thing,” said Mrs. Norris. “If every play is to be objected to, you will act nothing, and the preparations will be all so much money thrown away, and I am sure that would be a discredit to us all. I do not know the play; but, as Maria says, if there is anything a little too warm (and it is so with most of them) it can be easily left out. We must not be over-precise, Edmund. As Mr. Rushworth is to act too, there can be no harm. I only wish Tom had known his own mind when the carpenters began, for there was the loss of half a day’s work about those side-doors. The curtain will be a good job, however. The maids do their work very well, and I think we shall be able to send back some dozens of the rings. There is no occasion to put them so very close together. I am of some use, I hope, in preventing waste and making the most of things. There should always be one steady head to superintend so many young ones. I forgot to tell Tom of something that happened to me this very day. I had been looking about me in the poultry-yard, and was just coming out, when who should I see but Dick Jackson making up to the servants’ hall-door with two bits of deal board in his hand, bringing them to father, you may be sure; mother had chanced to send him of a message to father, and then father had bid him bring up them two bits of board, for he could not no how do without them. I knew what all this meant, for the servants’ dinner-bell was ringing at the very moment over our heads; and as I hate such encroaching people (the Jacksons are very encroaching, I have always said so: just the sort of people to get all they can), I said to the boy directly (a great lubberly fellow of ten years old, you know, who ought to be ashamed of himself), ‘I’ll take the boards to your father, Dick, so get you home again as fast as you can.’ The boy looked very silly, and turned away without offering a word, for I believe I might speak pretty sharp; and I dare say it will cure him of coming marauding about the house for one while. I hate such greediness—so good as your father is to the family, employing the man all the year round!”\n\nNobody was at the trouble of an answer; the others soon returned; and Edmund found that to have endeavoured to set them right must be his only satisfaction.\n\nDinner passed heavily. Mrs. Norris related again her triumph over Dick Jackson, but neither play nor preparation were otherwise much talked of, for Edmund’s disapprobation was felt even by his brother, though he would not have owned it. Maria, wanting Henry Crawford’s animating support, thought the subject better avoided. Mr. Yates, who was trying to make himself agreeable to Julia, found her gloom less impenetrable on any topic than that of his regret at her secession from their company; and Mr. Rushworth, having only his own part and his own dress in his head, had soon talked away all that could be said of either.\n\nBut the concerns of the theatre were suspended only for an hour or two: there was still a great deal to be settled; and the spirits of evening giving fresh courage, Tom, Maria, and Mr. Yates, soon after their being reassembled in the drawing-room, seated themselves in committee at a separate table, with the play open before them, and were just getting deep in the subject when a most welcome interruption was given by the entrance of Mr. and Miss Crawford, who, late and dark and dirty as it was, could not help coming, and were received with the most grateful joy.\n\n“Well, how do you go on?” and “What have you settled?” and “Oh! we can do nothing without you,” followed the first salutations; and Henry Crawford was soon seated with the other three at the table, while his sister made her way to Lady Bertram, and with pleasant attention was complimenting her. “I must really congratulate your ladyship,” said she, “on the play being chosen; for though you have borne it with exemplary patience, I am sure you must be sick of all our noise and difficulties. The actors may be glad, but the bystanders must be infinitely more thankful for a decision; and I do sincerely give you joy, madam, as well as Mrs. Norris, and everybody else who is in the same predicament,” glancing half fearfully, half slyly, beyond Fanny to Edmund.\n\nShe was very civilly answered by Lady Bertram, but Edmund said nothing. His being only a bystander was not disclaimed. After continuing in chat with the party round the fire a few minutes, Miss Crawford returned to the party round the table; and standing by them, seemed to interest herself in their arrangements till, as if struck by a sudden recollection, she exclaimed, “My good friends, you are most composedly at work upon these cottages and alehouses, inside and out; but pray let me know my fate in the meanwhile. Who is to be Anhalt? What gentleman among you am I to have the pleasure of making love to?”\n\nFor a moment no one spoke; and then many spoke together to tell the same melancholy truth, that they had not yet got any Anhalt. “Mr. Rushworth was to be Count Cassel, but no one had yet undertaken Anhalt.”\n\n“I had my choice of the parts,” said Mr. Rushworth; “but I thought I should like the Count best, though I do not much relish the finery I am to have.”\n\n“You chose very wisely, I am sure,” replied Miss Crawford, with a brightened look; “Anhalt is a heavy part.”\n\n“The Count has two-and-forty speeches,” returned Mr. Rushworth, “which is no trifle.”\n\n“I am not at all surprised,” said Miss Crawford, after a short pause, “at this want of an Anhalt. Amelia deserves no better. Such a forward young lady may well frighten the men.”\n\n“I should be but too happy in taking the part, if it were possible,” cried Tom; “but, unluckily, the Butler and Anhalt are in together. I will not entirely give it up, however; I will try what can be done—I will look it over again.”\n\n“Your brother should take the part,” said Mr. Yates, in a low voice. “Do not you think he would?”\n\n“I shall not ask him,” replied Tom, in a cold, determined manner.\n\nMiss Crawford talked of something else, and soon afterwards rejoined the party at the fire.\n\n“They do not want me at all,” said she, seating herself. “I only puzzle them, and oblige them to make civil speeches. Mr. Edmund Bertram, as you do not act yourself, you will be a disinterested adviser; and, therefore, I apply to you. What shall we do for an Anhalt? Is it practicable for any of the others to double it? What is your advice?”\n\n“My advice,” said he calmly, “is that you change the play.”\n\n“I should have no objection,” she replied; “for though I should not particularly dislike the part of Amelia if well supported, that is, if everything went well, I shall be sorry to be an inconvenience; but as they do not chuse to hear your advice at that table” (looking round), “it certainly will not be taken.”\n\nEdmund said no more.\n\n“If any part could tempt you to act, I suppose it would be Anhalt,” observed the lady archly, after a short pause; “for he is a clergyman, you know.”\n\n“That circumstance would by no means tempt me,” he replied, “for I should be sorry to make the character ridiculous by bad acting. It must be very difficult to keep Anhalt from appearing a formal, solemn lecturer; and the man who chuses the profession itself is, perhaps, one of the last who would wish to represent it on the stage.”\n\nMiss Crawford was silenced, and with some feelings of resentment and mortification, moved her chair considerably nearer the tea-table, and gave all her attention to Mrs. Norris, who was presiding there.\n\n“Fanny,” cried Tom Bertram, from the other table, where the conference was eagerly carrying on, and the conversation incessant, “we want your services.”\n\nFanny was up in a moment, expecting some errand; for the habit of employing her in that way was not yet overcome, in spite of all that Edmund could do.\n\n“Oh! we do not want to disturb you from your seat. We do not want your present services. We shall only want you in our play. You must be Cottager’s wife.”\n\n“Me!” cried Fanny, sitting down again with a most frightened look. “Indeed you must excuse me. I could not act anything if you were to give me the world. No, indeed, I cannot act.”\n\n“Indeed, but you must, for we cannot excuse you. It need not frighten you: it is a nothing of a part, a mere nothing, not above half a dozen speeches altogether, and it will not much signify if nobody hears a word you say; so you may be as creep-mouse as you like, but we must have you to look at.”\n\n“If you are afraid of half a dozen speeches,” cried Mr. Rushworth, “what would you do with such a part as mine? I have forty-two to learn.”\n\n“It is not that I am afraid of learning by heart,” said Fanny, shocked to find herself at that moment the only speaker in the room, and to feel that almost every eye was upon her; “but I really cannot act.”\n\n“Yes, yes, you can act well enough for us. Learn your part, and we will teach you all the rest. You have only two scenes, and as I shall be Cottager, I’ll put you in and push you about, and you will do it very well, I’ll answer for it.”\n\n“No, indeed, Mr. Bertram, you must excuse me. You cannot have an idea. It would be absolutely impossible for me. If I were to undertake it, I should only disappoint you.”\n\n“Phoo! Phoo! Do not be so shamefaced. You’ll do it very well. Every allowance will be made for you. We do not expect perfection. You must get a brown gown, and a white apron, and a mob cap, and we must make you a few wrinkles, and a little of the crowsfoot at the corner of your eyes, and you will be a very proper, little old woman.”\n\n“You must excuse me, indeed you must excuse me,” cried Fanny, growing more and more red from excessive agitation, and looking distressfully at Edmund, who was kindly observing her; but unwilling to exasperate his brother by interference, gave her only an encouraging smile. Her entreaty had no effect on Tom: he only said again what he had said before; and it was not merely Tom, for the requisition was now backed by Maria, and Mr. Crawford, and Mr. Yates, with an urgency which differed from his but in being more gentle or more ceremonious, and which altogether was quite overpowering to Fanny; and before she could breathe after it, Mrs. Norris completed the whole by thus addressing her in a whisper at once angry and audible—“What a piece of work here is about nothing: I am quite ashamed of you, Fanny, to make such a difficulty of obliging your cousins in a trifle of this sort—so kind as they are to you! Take the part with a good grace, and let us hear no more of the matter, I entreat.”\n\n“Do not urge her, madam,” said Edmund. “It is not fair to urge her in this manner. You see she does not like to act. Let her chuse for herself, as well as the rest of us. Her judgment may be quite as safely trusted. Do not urge her any more.”\n\n“I am not going to urge her,” replied Mrs. Norris sharply; “but I shall think her a very obstinate, ungrateful girl, if she does not do what her aunt and cousins wish her—very ungrateful, indeed, considering who and what she is.”\n\nEdmund was too angry to speak; but Miss Crawford, looking for a moment with astonished eyes at Mrs. Norris, and then at Fanny, whose tears were beginning to shew themselves, immediately said, with some keenness, “I do not like my situation: this place is too hot for me,” and moved away her chair to the opposite side of the table, close to Fanny, saying to her, in a kind, low whisper, as she placed herself, “Never mind, my dear Miss Price, this is a cross evening: everybody is cross and teasing, but do not let us mind them”; and with pointed attention continued to talk to her and endeavour to raise her spirits, in spite of being out of spirits herself. By a look at her brother she prevented any farther entreaty from the theatrical board, and the really good feelings by which she was almost purely governed were rapidly restoring her to all the little she had lost in Edmund’s favour.\n\nFanny did not love Miss Crawford; but she felt very much obliged to her for her present kindness; and when, from taking notice of her work, and wishing she could work as well, and begging for the pattern, and supposing Fanny was now preparing for her appearance, as of course she would come out when her cousin was married, Miss Crawford proceeded to inquire if she had heard lately from her brother at sea, and said that she had quite a curiosity to see him, and imagined him a very fine young man, and advised Fanny to get his picture drawn before he went to sea again—she could not help admitting it to be very agreeable flattery, or help listening, and answering with more animation than she had intended.\n\nThe consultation upon the play still went on, and Miss Crawford’s attention was first called from Fanny by Tom Bertram’s telling her, with infinite regret, that he found it absolutely impossible for him to undertake the part of Anhalt in addition to the Butler: he had been most anxiously trying to make it out to be feasible, but it would not do; he must give it up. “But there will not be the smallest difficulty in filling it,” he added. “We have but to speak the word; we may pick and chuse. I could name, at this moment, at least six young men within six miles of us, who are wild to be admitted into our company, and there are one or two that would not disgrace us: I should not be afraid to trust either of the Olivers or Charles Maddox. Tom Oliver is a very clever fellow, and Charles Maddox is as gentlemanlike a man as you will see anywhere, so I will take my horse early to-morrow morning and ride over to Stoke, and settle with one of them.”\n\nWhile he spoke, Maria was looking apprehensively round at Edmund in full expectation that he must oppose such an enlargement of the plan as this: so contrary to all their first protestations; but Edmund said nothing. After a moment’s thought, Miss Crawford calmly replied, “As far as I am concerned, I can have no objection to anything that you all think eligible. Have I ever seen either of the gentlemen? Yes, Mr. Charles Maddox dined at my sister’s one day, did not he, Henry? A quiet-looking young man. I remember him. Let him be applied to, if you please, for it will be less unpleasant to me than to have a perfect stranger.”\n\nCharles Maddox was to be the man. Tom repeated his resolution of going to him early on the morrow; and though Julia, who had scarcely opened her lips before, observed, in a sarcastic manner, and with a glance first at Maria and then at Edmund, that “the Mansfield theatricals would enliven the whole neighbourhood exceedingly,” Edmund still held his peace, and shewed his feelings only by a determined gravity.\n\n“I am not very sanguine as to our play,” said Miss Crawford, in an undervoice to Fanny, after some consideration; “and I can tell Mr. Maddox that I shall shorten some of his speeches, and a great many of my own, before we rehearse together. It will be very disagreeable, and by no means what I expected.”\n\n# CHAPTER XVI\n\nIt was not in Miss Crawford’s power to talk Fanny into any real forgetfulness of what had passed. When the evening was over, she went to bed full of it, her nerves still agitated by the shock of such an attack from her cousin Tom, so public and so persevered in, and her spirits sinking under her aunt’s unkind reflection and reproach. To be called into notice in such a manner, to hear that it was but the prelude to something so infinitely worse, to be told that she must do what was so impossible as to act; and then to have the charge of obstinacy and ingratitude follow it, enforced with such a hint at the dependence of her situation, had been too distressing at the time to make the remembrance when she was alone much less so, especially with the superadded dread of what the morrow might produce in continuation of the subject. Miss Crawford had protected her only for the time; and if she were applied to again among themselves with all the authoritative urgency that Tom and Maria were capable of, and Edmund perhaps away, what should she do? She fell asleep before she could answer the question, and found it quite as puzzling when she awoke the next morning. The little white attic, which had continued her sleeping-room ever since her first entering the family, proving incompetent to suggest any reply, she had recourse, as soon as she was dressed, to another apartment more spacious and more meet for walking about in and thinking, and of which she had now for some time been almost equally mistress. It had been their school-room; so called till the Miss Bertrams would not allow it to be called so any longer, and inhabited as such to a later period. There Miss Lee had lived, and there they had read and written, and talked and laughed, till within the last three years, when she had quitted them. The room had then become useless, and for some time was quite deserted, except by Fanny, when she visited her plants, or wanted one of the books, which she was still glad to keep there, from the deficiency of space and accommodation in her little chamber above: but gradually, as her value for the comforts of it increased, she had added to her possessions, and spent more of her time there; and having nothing to oppose her, had so naturally and so artlessly worked herself into it, that it was now generally admitted to be hers. The East room, as it had been called ever since Maria Bertram was sixteen, was now considered Fanny’s, almost as decidedly as the white attic: the smallness of the one making the use of the other so evidently reasonable that the Miss Bertrams, with every superiority in their own apartments which their own sense of superiority could demand, were entirely approving it; and Mrs. Norris, having stipulated for there never being a fire in it on Fanny’s account, was tolerably resigned to her having the use of what nobody else wanted, though the terms in which she sometimes spoke of the indulgence seemed to imply that it was the best room in the house.\n\nThe aspect was so favourable that even without a fire it was habitable in many an early spring and late autumn morning to such a willing mind as Fanny’s; and while there was a gleam of sunshine she hoped not to be driven from it entirely, even when winter came. The comfort of it in her hours of leisure was extreme. She could go there after anything unpleasant below, and find immediate consolation in some pursuit, or some train of thought at hand. Her plants, her books—of which she had been a collector from the first hour of her commanding a shilling—her writing-desk, and her works of charity and ingenuity, were all within her reach; or if indisposed for employment, if nothing but musing would do, she could scarcely see an object in that room which had not an interesting remembrance connected with it. Everything was a friend, or bore her thoughts to a friend; and though there had been sometimes much of suffering to her; though her motives had often been misunderstood, her feelings disregarded, and her comprehension undervalued; though she had known the pains of tyranny, of ridicule, and neglect, yet almost every recurrence of either had led to something consolatory: her aunt Bertram had spoken for her, or Miss Lee had been encouraging, or, what was yet more frequent or more dear, Edmund had been her champion and her friend: he had supported her cause or explained her meaning, he had told her not to cry, or had given her some proof of affection which made her tears delightful; and the whole was now so blended together, so harmonised by distance, that every former affliction had its charm. The room was most dear to her, and she would not have changed its furniture for the handsomest in the house, though what had been originally plain had suffered all the ill-usage of children; and its greatest elegancies and ornaments were a faded footstool of Julia’s work, too ill done for the drawing-room, three transparencies, made in a rage for transparencies, for the three lower panes of one window, where Tintern Abbey held its station between a cave in Italy and a moonlight lake in Cumberland, a collection of family profiles, thought unworthy of being anywhere else, over the mantelpiece, and by their side, and pinned against the wall, a small sketch of a ship sent four years ago from the Mediterranean by William, with H.M.S. Antwerp at the bottom, in letters as tall as the mainmast.\n\nTo this nest of comforts Fanny now walked down to try its influence on an agitated, doubting spirit, to see if by looking at Edmund’s profile she could catch any of his counsel, or by giving air to her geraniums she might inhale a breeze of mental strength herself. But she had more than fears of her own perseverance to remove: she had begun to feel undecided as to what she ought to do; and as she walked round the room her doubts were increasing. Was she right in refusing what was so warmly asked, so strongly wished for—what might be so essential to a scheme on which some of those to whom she owed the greatest complaisance had set their hearts? Was it not ill-nature, selfishness, and a fear of exposing herself? And would Edmund’s judgment, would his persuasion of Sir Thomas’s disapprobation of the whole, be enough to justify her in a determined denial in spite of all the rest? It would be so horrible to her to act that she was inclined to suspect the truth and purity of her own scruples; and as she looked around her, the claims of her cousins to being obliged were strengthened by the sight of present upon present that she had received from them. The table between the windows was covered with work-boxes and netting-boxes which had been given her at different times, principally by Tom; and she grew bewildered as to the amount of the debt which all these kind remembrances produced. A tap at the door roused her in the midst of this attempt to find her way to her duty, and her gentle “Come in” was answered by the appearance of one, before whom all her doubts were wont to be laid. Her eyes brightened at the sight of Edmund.\n\n“Can I speak with you, Fanny, for a few minutes?” said he.\n\n“Yes, certainly.”\n\n“I want to consult. I want your opinion.”\n\n“My opinion!” she cried, shrinking from such a compliment, highly as it gratified her.\n\n“Yes, your advice and opinion. I do not know what to do. This acting scheme gets worse and worse, you see. They have chosen almost as bad a play as they could, and now, to complete the business, are going to ask the help of a young man very slightly known to any of us. This is the end of all the privacy and propriety which was talked about at first. I know no harm of Charles Maddox; but the excessive intimacy which must spring from his being admitted among us in this manner is highly objectionable, the more than intimacy—the familiarity. I cannot think of it with any patience; and it does appear to me an evil of such magnitude as must, if possible, be prevented. Do not you see it in the same light?”\n\n“Yes; but what can be done? Your brother is so determined.”\n\n“There is but one thing to be done, Fanny. I must take Anhalt myself. I am well aware that nothing else will quiet Tom.”\n\nFanny could not answer him.\n\n“It is not at all what I like,” he continued. “No man can like being driven into the appearance of such inconsistency. After being known to oppose the scheme from the beginning, there is absurdity in the face of my joining them now, when they are exceeding their first plan in every respect; but I can think of no other alternative. Can you, Fanny?”\n\n“No,” said Fanny slowly, “not immediately, but—”\n\n“But what? I see your judgment is not with me. Think it a little over. Perhaps you are not so much aware as I am of the mischief that may, of the unpleasantness that must arise from a young man’s being received in this manner: domesticated among us; authorised to come at all hours, and placed suddenly on a footing which must do away all restraints. To think only of the licence which every rehearsal must tend to create. It is all very bad! Put yourself in Miss Crawford’s place, Fanny. Consider what it would be to act Amelia with a stranger. She has a right to be felt for, because she evidently feels for herself. I heard enough of what she said to you last night to understand her unwillingness to be acting with a stranger; and as she probably engaged in the part with different expectations—perhaps without considering the subject enough to know what was likely to be—it would be ungenerous, it would be really wrong to expose her to it. Her feelings ought to be respected. Does it not strike you so, Fanny? You hesitate.”\n\n“I am sorry for Miss Crawford; but I am more sorry to see you drawn in to do what you had resolved against, and what you are known to think will be disagreeable to my uncle. It will be such a triumph to the others!”\n\n“They will not have much cause of triumph when they see how infamously I act. But, however, triumph there certainly will be, and I must brave it. But if I can be the means of restraining the publicity of the business, of limiting the exhibition, of concentrating our folly, I shall be well repaid. As I am now, I have no influence, I can do nothing: I have offended them, and they will not hear me; but when I have put them in good-humour by this concession, I am not without hopes of persuading them to confine the representation within a much smaller circle than they are now in the high road for. This will be a material gain. My object is to confine it to Mrs. Rushworth and the Grants. Will not this be worth gaining?”\n\n“Yes, it will be a great point.”\n\n“But still it has not your approbation. Can you mention any other measure by which I have a chance of doing equal good?”\n\n“No, I cannot think of anything else.”\n\n“Give me your approbation, then, Fanny. I am not comfortable without it.”\n\n“Oh, cousin!”\n\n“If you are against me, I ought to distrust myself, and yet—But it is absolutely impossible to let Tom go on in this way, riding about the country in quest of anybody who can be persuaded to act—no matter whom: the look of a gentleman is to be enough. I thought you would have entered more into Miss Crawford’s feelings.”\n\n“No doubt she will be very glad. It must be a great relief to her,” said Fanny, trying for greater warmth of manner.\n\n“She never appeared more amiable than in her behaviour to you last night. It gave her a very strong claim on my goodwill.”\n\n“She was very kind, indeed, and I am glad to have her spared”...\n\nShe could not finish the generous effusion. Her conscience stopt her in the middle, but Edmund was satisfied.\n\n“I shall walk down immediately after breakfast,” said he, “and am sure of giving pleasure there. And now, dear Fanny, I will not interrupt you any longer. You want to be reading. But I could not be easy till I had spoken to you, and come to a decision. Sleeping or waking, my head has been full of this matter all night. It is an evil, but I am certainly making it less than it might be. If Tom is up, I shall go to him directly and get it over, and when we meet at breakfast we shall be all in high good-humour at the prospect of acting the fool together with such unanimity. You, in the meanwhile, will be taking a trip into China, I suppose. How does Lord Macartney go on?”—opening a volume on the table and then taking up some others. “And here are Crabbe’s Tales, and the Idler, at hand to relieve you, if you tire of your great book. I admire your little establishment exceedingly; and as soon as I am gone, you will empty your head of all this nonsense of acting, and sit comfortably down to your table. But do not stay here to be cold.”\n\nHe went; but there was no reading, no China, no composure for Fanny. He had told her the most extraordinary, the most inconceivable, the most unwelcome news; and she could think of nothing else. To be acting! After all his objections—objections so just and so public! After all that she had heard him say, and seen him look, and known him to be feeling. Could it be possible? Edmund so inconsistent! Was he not deceiving himself? Was he not wrong? Alas! it was all Miss Crawford’s doing. She had seen her influence in every speech, and was miserable. The doubts and alarms as to her own conduct, which had previously distressed her, and which had all slept while she listened to him, were become of little consequence now. This deeper anxiety swallowed them up. Things should take their course; she cared not how it ended. Her cousins might attack, but could hardly tease her. She was beyond their reach; and if at last obliged to yield—no matter—it was all misery now.\n\n# CHAPTER XVII\n\nIt was, indeed, a triumphant day to Mr. Bertram and Maria. Such a victory over Edmund’s discretion had been beyond their hopes, and was most delightful. There was no longer anything to disturb them in their darling project, and they congratulated each other in private on the jealous weakness to which they attributed the change, with all the glee of feelings gratified in every way. Edmund might still look grave, and say he did not like the scheme in general, and must disapprove the play in particular; their point was gained: he was to act, and he was driven to it by the force of selfish inclinations only. Edmund had descended from that moral elevation which he had maintained before, and they were both as much the better as the happier for the descent.\n\nThey behaved very well, however, to him on the occasion, betraying no exultation beyond the lines about the corners of the mouth, and seemed to think it as great an escape to be quit of the intrusion of Charles Maddox, as if they had been forced into admitting him against their inclination. “To have it quite in their own family circle was what they had particularly wished. A stranger among them would have been the destruction of all their comfort”; and when Edmund, pursuing that idea, gave a hint of his hope as to the limitation of the audience, they were ready, in the complaisance of the moment, to promise anything. It was all good-humour and encouragement. Mrs. Norris offered to contrive his dress, Mr. Yates assured him that Anhalt’s last scene with the Baron admitted a good deal of action and emphasis, and Mr. Rushworth undertook to count his speeches.\n\n“Perhaps,” said Tom, “Fanny may be more disposed to oblige us now. Perhaps you may persuade her.”\n\n“No, she is quite determined. She certainly will not act.”\n\n“Oh! very well.” And not another word was said; but Fanny felt herself again in danger, and her indifference to the danger was beginning to fail her already.\n\nThere were not fewer smiles at the Parsonage than at the Park on this change in Edmund; Miss Crawford looked very lovely in hers, and entered with such an instantaneous renewal of cheerfulness into the whole affair as could have but one effect on him. “He was certainly right in respecting such feelings; he was glad he had determined on it.” And the morning wore away in satisfactions very sweet, if not very sound. One advantage resulted from it to Fanny: at the earnest request of Miss Crawford, Mrs. Grant had, with her usual good-humour, agreed to undertake the part for which Fanny had been wanted; and this was all that occurred to gladden her heart during the day; and even this, when imparted by Edmund, brought a pang with it, for it was Miss Crawford to whom she was obliged—it was Miss Crawford whose kind exertions were to excite her gratitude, and whose merit in making them was spoken of with a glow of admiration. She was safe; but peace and safety were unconnected here. Her mind had been never farther from peace. She could not feel that she had done wrong herself, but she was disquieted in every other way. Her heart and her judgment were equally against Edmund’s decision: she could not acquit his unsteadiness, and his happiness under it made her wretched. She was full of jealousy and agitation. Miss Crawford came with looks of gaiety which seemed an insult, with friendly expressions towards herself which she could hardly answer calmly. Everybody around her was gay and busy, prosperous and important; each had their object of interest, their part, their dress, their favourite scene, their friends and confederates: all were finding employment in consultations and comparisons, or diversion in the playful conceits they suggested. She alone was sad and insignificant: she had no share in anything; she might go or stay; she might be in the midst of their noise, or retreat from it to the solitude of the East room, without being seen or missed. She could almost think anything would have been preferable to this. Mrs. Grant was of consequence: her good-nature had honourable mention; her taste and her time were considered; her presence was wanted; she was sought for, and attended, and praised; and Fanny was at first in some danger of envying her the character she had accepted. But reflection brought better feelings, and shewed her that Mrs. Grant was entitled to respect, which could never have belonged to her; and that, had she received even the greatest, she could never have been easy in joining a scheme which, considering only her uncle, she must condemn altogether.\n\nFanny’s heart was not absolutely the only saddened one amongst them, as she soon began to acknowledge to herself. Julia was a sufferer too, though not quite so blamelessly.\n\nHenry Crawford had trifled with her feelings; but she had very long allowed and even sought his attentions, with a jealousy of her sister so reasonable as ought to have been their cure; and now that the conviction of his preference for Maria had been forced on her, she submitted to it without any alarm for Maria’s situation, or any endeavour at rational tranquillity for herself. She either sat in gloomy silence, wrapt in such gravity as nothing could subdue, no curiosity touch, no wit amuse; or allowing the attentions of Mr. Yates, was talking with forced gaiety to him alone, and ridiculing the acting of the others.\n\nFor a day or two after the affront was given, Henry Crawford had endeavoured to do it away by the usual attack of gallantry and compliment, but he had not cared enough about it to persevere against a few repulses; and becoming soon too busy with his play to have time for more than one flirtation, he grew indifferent to the quarrel, or rather thought it a lucky occurrence, as quietly putting an end to what might ere long have raised expectations in more than Mrs. Grant. She was not pleased to see Julia excluded from the play, and sitting by disregarded; but as it was not a matter which really involved her happiness, as Henry must be the best judge of his own, and as he did assure her, with a most persuasive smile, that neither he nor Julia had ever had a serious thought of each other, she could only renew her former caution as to the elder sister, entreat him not to risk his tranquillity by too much admiration there, and then gladly take her share in anything that brought cheerfulness to the young people in general, and that did so particularly promote the pleasure of the two so dear to her.\n\n“I rather wonder Julia is not in love with Henry,” was her observation to Mary.\n\n“I dare say she is,” replied Mary coldly. “I imagine both sisters are.”\n\n“Both! no, no, that must not be. Do not give him a hint of it. Think of Mr. Rushworth!”\n\n“You had better tell Miss Bertram to think of Mr. Rushworth. It may do her some good. I often think of Mr. Rushworth’s property and independence, and wish them in other hands; but I never think of him. A man might represent the county with such an estate; a man might escape a profession and represent the county.”\n\n“I dare say he will be in parliament soon. When Sir Thomas comes, I dare say he will be in for some borough, but there has been nobody to put him in the way of doing anything yet.”\n\n“Sir Thomas is to achieve many mighty things when he comes home,” said Mary, after a pause. “Do you remember Hawkins Browne’s ‘Address to Tobacco,’ in imitation of Pope?—\n\nBlest leaf! whose aromatic gales dispense To Templars modesty, to Parsons sense.\n\nI will parody them—\n\nBlest Knight! whose dictatorial looks dispense To Children affluence, to Rushworth sense.\n\nWill not that do, Mrs. Grant? Everything seems to depend upon Sir Thomas’s return.”\n\n“You will find his consequence very just and reasonable when you see him in his family, I assure you. I do not think we do so well without him. He has a fine dignified manner, which suits the head of such a house, and keeps everybody in their place. Lady Bertram seems more of a cipher now than when he is at home; and nobody else can keep Mrs. Norris in order. But, Mary, do not fancy that Maria Bertram cares for Henry. I am sure Julia does not, or she would not have flirted as she did last night with Mr. Yates; and though he and Maria are very good friends, I think she likes Sotherton too well to be inconstant.”\n\n“I would not give much for Mr. Rushworth’s chance if Henry stept in before the articles were signed.”\n\n“If you have such a suspicion, something must be done; and as soon as the play is all over, we will talk to him seriously and make him know his own mind; and if he means nothing, we will send him off, though he is Henry, for a time.”\n\nJulia did suffer, however, though Mrs. Grant discerned it not, and though it escaped the notice of many of her own family likewise. She had loved, she did love still, and she had all the suffering which a warm temper and a high spirit were likely to endure under the disappointment of a dear, though irrational hope, with a strong sense of ill-usage. Her heart was sore and angry, and she was capable only of angry consolations. The sister with whom she was used to be on easy terms was now become her greatest enemy: they were alienated from each other; and Julia was not superior to the hope of some distressing end to the attentions which were still carrying on there, some punishment to Maria for conduct so shameful towards herself as well as towards Mr. Rushworth. With no material fault of temper, or difference of opinion, to prevent their being very good friends while their interests were the same, the sisters, under such a trial as this, had not affection or principle enough to make them merciful or just, to give them honour or compassion. Maria felt her triumph, and pursued her purpose, careless of Julia; and Julia could never see Maria distinguished by Henry Crawford without trusting that it would create jealousy, and bring a public disturbance at last.\n\nFanny saw and pitied much of this in Julia; but there was no outward fellowship between them. Julia made no communication, and Fanny took no liberties. They were two solitary sufferers, or connected only by Fanny’s consciousness.\n\nThe inattention of the two brothers and the aunt to Julia’s discomposure, and their blindness to its true cause, must be imputed to the fullness of their own minds. They were totally preoccupied. Tom was engrossed by the concerns of his theatre, and saw nothing that did not immediately relate to it. Edmund, between his theatrical and his real part, between Miss Crawford’s claims and his own conduct, between love and consistency, was equally unobservant; and Mrs. Norris was too busy in contriving and directing the general little matters of the company, superintending their various dresses with economical expedient, for which nobody thanked her, and saving, with delighted integrity, half a crown here and there to the absent Sir Thomas, to have leisure for watching the behaviour, or guarding the happiness of his daughters.\n\n# CHAPTER XVIII\n\nEverything was now in a regular train: theatre, actors, actresses, and dresses, were all getting forward; but though no other great impediments arose, Fanny found, before many days were past, that it was not all uninterrupted enjoyment to the party themselves, and that she had not to witness the continuance of such unanimity and delight as had been almost too much for her at first. Everybody began to have their vexation. Edmund had many. Entirely against his judgment, a scene-painter arrived from town, and was at work, much to the increase of the expenses, and, what was worse, of the eclat of their proceedings; and his brother, instead of being really guided by him as to the privacy of the representation, was giving an invitation to every family who came in his way. Tom himself began to fret over the scene-painter’s slow progress, and to feel the miseries of waiting. He had learned his part—all his parts, for he took every trifling one that could be united with the Butler, and began to be impatient to be acting; and every day thus unemployed was tending to increase his sense of the insignificance of all his parts together, and make him more ready to regret that some other play had not been chosen.\n\nFanny, being always a very courteous listener, and often the only listener at hand, came in for the complaints and the distresses of most of them. She knew that Mr. Yates was in general thought to rant dreadfully; that Mr. Yates was disappointed in Henry Crawford; that Tom Bertram spoke so quick he would be unintelligible; that Mrs. Grant spoiled everything by laughing; that Edmund was behindhand with his part, and that it was misery to have anything to do with Mr. Rushworth, who was wanting a prompter through every speech. She knew, also, that poor Mr. Rushworth could seldom get anybody to rehearse with him: his complaint came before her as well as the rest; and so decided to her eye was her cousin Maria’s avoidance of him, and so needlessly often the rehearsal of the first scene between her and Mr. Crawford, that she had soon all the terror of other complaints from him. So far from being all satisfied and all enjoying, she found everybody requiring something they had not, and giving occasion of discontent to the others. Everybody had a part either too long or too short; nobody would attend as they ought; nobody would remember on which side they were to come in; nobody but the complainer would observe any directions.\n\nFanny believed herself to derive as much innocent enjoyment from the play as any of them; Henry Crawford acted well, and it was a pleasure to her to creep into the theatre, and attend the rehearsal of the first act, in spite of the feelings it excited in some speeches for Maria. Maria, she also thought, acted well, too well; and after the first rehearsal or two, Fanny began to be their only audience; and sometimes as prompter, sometimes as spectator, was often very useful. As far as she could judge, Mr. Crawford was considerably the best actor of all: he had more confidence than Edmund, more judgment than Tom, more talent and taste than Mr. Yates. She did not like him as a man, but she must admit him to be the best actor, and on this point there were not many who differed from her. Mr. Yates, indeed, exclaimed against his tameness and insipidity; and the day came at last, when Mr. Rushworth turned to her with a black look, and said, “Do you think there is anything so very fine in all this? For the life and soul of me, I cannot admire him; and, between ourselves, to see such an undersized, little, mean-looking man, set up for a fine actor, is very ridiculous in my opinion.”\n\nFrom this moment there was a return of his former jealousy, which Maria, from increasing hopes of Crawford, was at little pains to remove; and the chances of Mr. Rushworth’s ever attaining to the knowledge of his two-and-forty speeches became much less. As to his ever making anything tolerable of them, nobody had the smallest idea of that except his mother; she, indeed, regretted that his part was not more considerable, and deferred coming over to Mansfield till they were forward enough in their rehearsal to comprehend all his scenes; but the others aspired at nothing beyond his remembering the catchword, and the first line of his speech, and being able to follow the prompter through the rest. Fanny, in her pity and kindheartedness, was at great pains to teach him how to learn, giving him all the helps and directions in her power, trying to make an artificial memory for him, and learning every word of his part herself, but without his being much the forwarder.\n\nMany uncomfortable, anxious, apprehensive feelings she certainly had; but with all these, and other claims on her time and attention, she was as far from finding herself without employment or utility amongst them, as without a companion in uneasiness; quite as far from having no demand on her leisure as on her compassion. The gloom of her first anticipations was proved to have been unfounded. She was occasionally useful to all; she was perhaps as much at peace as any.\n\nThere was a great deal of needlework to be done, moreover, in which her help was wanted; and that Mrs. Norris thought her quite as well off as the rest, was evident by the manner in which she claimed it—“Come, Fanny,” she cried, “these are fine times for you, but you must not be always walking from one room to the other, and doing the lookings-on at your ease, in this way; I want you here. I have been slaving myself till I can hardly stand, to contrive Mr. Rushworth’s cloak without sending for any more satin; and now I think you may give me your help in putting it together. There are but three seams; you may do them in a trice. It would be lucky for me if I had nothing but the executive part to do. You are best off, I can tell you: but if nobody did more than you, we should not get on very fast.”\n\nFanny took the work very quietly, without attempting any defence; but her kinder aunt Bertram observed on her behalf—\n\n“One cannot wonder, sister, that Fanny should be delighted: it is all new to her, you know; you and I used to be very fond of a play ourselves, and so am I still; and as soon as I am a little more at leisure, I mean to look in at their rehearsals too. What is the play about, Fanny? you have never told me.”\n\n“Oh! sister, pray do not ask her now; for Fanny is not one of those who can talk and work at the same time. It is about Lovers’ Vows.”\n\n“I believe,” said Fanny to her aunt Bertram, “there will be three acts rehearsed to-morrow evening, and that will give you an opportunity of seeing all the actors at once.”\n\n“You had better stay till the curtain is hung,” interposed Mrs. Norris; “the curtain will be hung in a day or two—there is very little sense in a play without a curtain—and I am much mistaken if you do not find it draw up into very handsome festoons.”\n\nLady Bertram seemed quite resigned to waiting. Fanny did not share her aunt’s composure: she thought of the morrow a great deal, for if the three acts were rehearsed, Edmund and Miss Crawford would then be acting together for the first time; the third act would bring a scene between them which interested her most particularly, and which she was longing and dreading to see how they would perform. The whole subject of it was love—a marriage of love was to be described by the gentleman, and very little short of a declaration of love be made by the lady.\n\nShe had read and read the scene again with many painful, many wondering emotions, and looked forward to their representation of it as a circumstance almost too interesting. She did not believe they had yet rehearsed it, even in private.\n\nThe morrow came, the plan for the evening continued, and Fanny’s consideration of it did not become less agitated. She worked very diligently under her aunt’s directions, but her diligence and her silence concealed a very absent, anxious mind; and about noon she made her escape with her work to the East room, that she might have no concern in another, and, as she deemed it, most unnecessary rehearsal of the first act, which Henry Crawford was just proposing, desirous at once of having her time to herself, and of avoiding the sight of Mr. Rushworth. A glimpse, as she passed through the hall, of the two ladies walking up from the Parsonage made no change in her wish of retreat, and she worked and meditated in the East room, undisturbed, for a quarter of an hour, when a gentle tap at the door was followed by the entrance of Miss Crawford.\n\n“Am I right? Yes; this is the East room. My dear Miss Price, I beg your pardon, but I have made my way to you on purpose to entreat your help.”\n\nFanny, quite surprised, endeavoured to shew herself mistress of the room by her civilities, and looked at the bright bars of her empty grate with concern.\n\n“Thank you; I am quite warm, very warm. Allow me to stay here a little while, and do have the goodness to hear me my third act. I have brought my book, and if you would but rehearse it with me, I should be so obliged! I came here to-day intending to rehearse it with Edmund—by ourselves—against the evening, but he is not in the way; and if he were, I do not think I could go through it with him, till I have hardened myself a little; for really there is a speech or two. You will be so good, won’t you?”\n\nFanny was most civil in her assurances, though she could not give them in a very steady voice.\n\n“Have you ever happened to look at the part I mean?” continued Miss Crawford, opening her book. “Here it is. I did not think much of it at first—but, upon my word. There, look at that speech, and that, and that. How am I ever to look him in the face and say such things? Could you do it? But then he is your cousin, which makes all the difference. You must rehearse it with me, that I may fancy you him, and get on by degrees. You have a look of his sometimes.”\n\n“Have I? I will do my best with the greatest readiness; but I must read the part, for I can say very little of it.”\n\n“None of it, I suppose. You are to have the book, of course. Now for it. We must have two chairs at hand for you to bring forward to the front of the stage. There—very good school-room chairs, not made for a theatre, I dare say; much more fitted for little girls to sit and kick their feet against when they are learning a lesson. What would your governess and your uncle say to see them used for such a purpose? Could Sir Thomas look in upon us just now, he would bless himself, for we are rehearsing all over the house. Yates is storming away in the dining-room. I heard him as I came upstairs, and the theatre is engaged of course by those indefatigable rehearsers, Agatha and Frederick. If they are not perfect, I shall be surprised. By the bye, I looked in upon them five minutes ago, and it happened to be exactly at one of the times when they were trying not to embrace, and Mr. Rushworth was with me. I thought he began to look a little queer, so I turned it off as well as I could, by whispering to him, ‘We shall have an excellent Agatha; there is something so maternal in her manner, so completely maternal in her voice and countenance.’ Was not that well done of me? He brightened up directly. Now for my soliloquy.”\n\nShe began, and Fanny joined in with all the modest feeling which the idea of representing Edmund was so strongly calculated to inspire; but with looks and voice so truly feminine as to be no very good picture of a man. With such an Anhalt, however, Miss Crawford had courage enough; and they had got through half the scene, when a tap at the door brought a pause, and the entrance of Edmund, the next moment, suspended it all.\n\nSurprise, consciousness, and pleasure appeared in each of the three on this unexpected meeting; and as Edmund was come on the very same business that had brought Miss Crawford, consciousness and pleasure were likely to be more than momentary in them. He too had his book, and was seeking Fanny, to ask her to rehearse with him, and help him to prepare for the evening, without knowing Miss Crawford to be in the house; and great was the joy and animation of being thus thrown together, of comparing schemes, and sympathising in praise of Fanny’s kind offices.\n\nShe could not equal them in their warmth. Her spirits sank under the glow of theirs, and she felt herself becoming too nearly nothing to both to have any comfort in having been sought by either. They must now rehearse together. Edmund proposed, urged, entreated it, till the lady, not very unwilling at first, could refuse no longer, and Fanny was wanted only to prompt and observe them. She was invested, indeed, with the office of judge and critic, and earnestly desired to exercise it and tell them all their faults; but from doing so every feeling within her shrank—she could not, would not, dared not attempt it: had she been otherwise qualified for criticism, her conscience must have restrained her from venturing at disapprobation. She believed herself to feel too much of it in the aggregate for honesty or safety in particulars. To prompt them must be enough for her; and it was sometimes more than enough; for she could not always pay attention to the book. In watching them she forgot herself; and, agitated by the increasing spirit of Edmund’s manner, had once closed the page and turned away exactly as he wanted help. It was imputed to very reasonable weariness, and she was thanked and pitied; but she deserved their pity more than she hoped they would ever surmise. At last the scene was over, and Fanny forced herself to add her praise to the compliments each was giving the other; and when again alone and able to recall the whole, she was inclined to believe their performance would, indeed, have such nature and feeling in it as must ensure their credit, and make it a very suffering exhibition to herself. Whatever might be its effect, however, she must stand the brunt of it again that very day.\n\nThe first regular rehearsal of the three first acts was certainly to take place in the evening: Mrs. Grant and the Crawfords were engaged to return for that purpose as soon as they could after dinner; and every one concerned was looking forward with eagerness. There seemed a general diffusion of cheerfulness on the occasion. Tom was enjoying such an advance towards the end; Edmund was in spirits from the morning’s rehearsal, and little vexations seemed everywhere smoothed away. All were alert and impatient; the ladies moved soon, the gentlemen soon followed them, and with the exception of Lady Bertram, Mrs. Norris, and Julia, everybody was in the theatre at an early hour; and having lighted it up as well as its unfinished state admitted, were waiting only the arrival of Mrs. Grant and the Crawfords to begin.\n\nThey did not wait long for the Crawfords, but there was no Mrs. Grant. She could not come. Dr. Grant, professing an indisposition, for which he had little credit with his fair sister-in-law, could not spare his wife.\n\n“Dr. Grant is ill,” said she, with mock solemnity. “He has been ill ever since he did not eat any of the pheasant today. He fancied it tough, sent away his plate, and has been suffering ever since”.\n\nHere was disappointment! Mrs. Grant’s non-attendance was sad indeed. Her pleasant manners and cheerful conformity made her always valuable amongst them; but now she was absolutely necessary. They could not act, they could not rehearse with any satisfaction without her. The comfort of the whole evening was destroyed. What was to be done? Tom, as Cottager, was in despair. After a pause of perplexity, some eyes began to be turned towards Fanny, and a voice or two to say, “If Miss Price would be so good as to read the part.” She was immediately surrounded by supplications; everybody asked it; even Edmund said, “Do, Fanny, if it is not very disagreeable to you.”\n\nBut Fanny still hung back. She could not endure the idea of it. Why was not Miss Crawford to be applied to as well? Or why had not she rather gone to her own room, as she had felt to be safest, instead of attending the rehearsal at all? She had known it would irritate and distress her; she had known it her duty to keep away. She was properly punished.\n\n“You have only to read the part,” said Henry Crawford, with renewed entreaty.\n\n“And I do believe she can say every word of it,” added Maria, “for she could put Mrs. Grant right the other day in twenty places. Fanny, I am sure you know the part.”\n\nFanny could not say she did not; and as they all persevered, as Edmund repeated his wish, and with a look of even fond dependence on her good-nature, she must yield. She would do her best. Everybody was satisfied; and she was left to the tremors of a most palpitating heart, while the others prepared to begin.\n\nThey did begin; and being too much engaged in their own noise to be struck by an unusual noise in the other part of the house, had proceeded some way when the door of the room was thrown open, and Julia, appearing at it, with a face all aghast, exclaimed, “My father is come! He is in the hall at this moment.”\n\n# CHAPTER XIX\n\nHow is the consternation of the party to be described? To the greater number it was a moment of absolute horror. Sir Thomas in the house! All felt the instantaneous conviction. Not a hope of imposition or mistake was harboured anywhere. Julia’s looks were an evidence of the fact that made it indisputable; and after the first starts and exclamations, not a word was spoken for half a minute: each with an altered countenance was looking at some other, and almost each was feeling it a stroke the most unwelcome, most ill-timed, most appalling! Mr. Yates might consider it only as a vexatious interruption for the evening, and Mr. Rushworth might imagine it a blessing; but every other heart was sinking under some degree of self-condemnation or undefined alarm, every other heart was suggesting, “What will become of us? what is to be done now?” It was a terrible pause; and terrible to every ear were the corroborating sounds of opening doors and passing footsteps.\n\nJulia was the first to move and speak again. Jealousy and bitterness had been suspended: selfishness was lost in the common cause; but at the moment of her appearance, Frederick was listening with looks of devotion to Agatha’s narrative, and pressing her hand to his heart; and as soon as she could notice this, and see that, in spite of the shock of her words, he still kept his station and retained her sister’s hand, her wounded heart swelled again with injury, and looking as red as she had been white before, she turned out of the room, saying, “I need not be afraid of appearing before him.”\n\nHer going roused the rest; and at the same moment the two brothers stepped forward, feeling the necessity of doing something. A very few words between them were sufficient. The case admitted no difference of opinion: they must go to the drawing-room directly. Maria joined them with the same intent, just then the stoutest of the three; for the very circumstance which had driven Julia away was to her the sweetest support. Henry Crawford’s retaining her hand at such a moment, a moment of such peculiar proof and importance, was worth ages of doubt and anxiety. She hailed it as an earnest of the most serious determination, and was equal even to encounter her father. They walked off, utterly heedless of Mr. Rushworth’s repeated question of, “Shall I go too? Had not I better go too? Will not it be right for me to go too?” but they were no sooner through the door than Henry Crawford undertook to answer the anxious inquiry, and, encouraging him by all means to pay his respects to Sir Thomas without delay, sent him after the others with delighted haste.\n\nFanny was left with only the Crawfords and Mr. Yates. She had been quite overlooked by her cousins; and as her own opinion of her claims on Sir Thomas’s affection was much too humble to give her any idea of classing herself with his children, she was glad to remain behind and gain a little breathing-time. Her agitation and alarm exceeded all that was endured by the rest, by the right of a disposition which not even innocence could keep from suffering. She was nearly fainting: all her former habitual dread of her uncle was returning, and with it compassion for him and for almost every one of the party on the development before him, with solicitude on Edmund’s account indescribable. She had found a seat, where in excessive trembling she was enduring all these fearful thoughts, while the other three, no longer under any restraint, were giving vent to their feelings of vexation, lamenting over such an unlooked-for premature arrival as a most untoward event, and without mercy wishing poor Sir Thomas had been twice as long on his passage, or were still in Antigua.\n\nThe Crawfords were more warm on the subject than Mr. Yates, from better understanding the family, and judging more clearly of the mischief that must ensue. The ruin of the play was to them a certainty: they felt the total destruction of the scheme to be inevitably at hand; while Mr. Yates considered it only as a temporary interruption, a disaster for the evening, and could even suggest the possibility of the rehearsal being renewed after tea, when the bustle of receiving Sir Thomas were over, and he might be at leisure to be amused by it. The Crawfords laughed at the idea; and having soon agreed on the propriety of their walking quietly home and leaving the family to themselves, proposed Mr. Yates’s accompanying them and spending the evening at the Parsonage. But Mr. Yates, having never been with those who thought much of parental claims, or family confidence, could not perceive that anything of the kind was necessary; and therefore, thanking them, said, “he preferred remaining where he was, that he might pay his respects to the old gentleman handsomely since he was come; and besides, he did not think it would be fair by the others to have everybody run away.”\n\nFanny was just beginning to collect herself, and to feel that if she staid longer behind it might seem disrespectful, when this point was settled, and being commissioned with the brother and sister’s apology, saw them preparing to go as she quitted the room herself to perform the dreadful duty of appearing before her uncle.\n\nToo soon did she find herself at the drawing-room door; and after pausing a moment for what she knew would not come, for a courage which the outside of no door had ever supplied to her, she turned the lock in desperation, and the lights of the drawing-room, and all the collected family, were before her. As she entered, her own name caught her ear. Sir Thomas was at that moment looking round him, and saying, “But where is Fanny? Why do not I see my little Fanny?”—and on perceiving her, came forward with a kindness which astonished and penetrated her, calling her his dear Fanny, kissing her affectionately, and observing with decided pleasure how much she was grown! Fanny knew not how to feel, nor where to look. She was quite oppressed. He had never been so kind, so very kind to her in his life. His manner seemed changed, his voice was quick from the agitation of joy; and all that had been awful in his dignity seemed lost in tenderness. He led her nearer the light and looked at her again—inquired particularly after her health, and then, correcting himself, observed that he need not inquire, for her appearance spoke sufficiently on that point. A fine blush having succeeded the previous paleness of her face, he was justified in his belief of her equal improvement in health and beauty. He inquired next after her family, especially William: and his kindness altogether was such as made her reproach herself for loving him so little, and thinking his return a misfortune; and when, on having courage to lift her eyes to his face, she saw that he was grown thinner, and had the burnt, fagged, worn look of fatigue and a hot climate, every tender feeling was increased, and she was miserable in considering how much unsuspected vexation was probably ready to burst on him.\n\nSir Thomas was indeed the life of the party, who at his suggestion now seated themselves round the fire. He had the best right to be the talker; and the delight of his sensations in being again in his own house, in the centre of his family, after such a separation, made him communicative and chatty in a very unusual degree; and he was ready to give every information as to his voyage, and answer every question of his two sons almost before it was put. His business in Antigua had latterly been prosperously rapid, and he came directly from Liverpool, having had an opportunity of making his passage thither in a private vessel, instead of waiting for the packet; and all the little particulars of his proceedings and events, his arrivals and departures, were most promptly delivered, as he sat by Lady Bertram and looked with heartfelt satisfaction on the faces around him—interrupting himself more than once, however, to remark on his good fortune in finding them all at home—coming unexpectedly as he did—all collected together exactly as he could have wished, but dared not depend on. Mr. Rushworth was not forgotten: a most friendly reception and warmth of hand-shaking had already met him, and with pointed attention he was now included in the objects most intimately connected with Mansfield. There was nothing disagreeable in Mr. Rushworth’s appearance, and Sir Thomas was liking him already.\n\nBy not one of the circle was he listened to with such unbroken, unalloyed enjoyment as by his wife, who was really extremely happy to see him, and whose feelings were so warmed by his sudden arrival as to place her nearer agitation than she had been for the last twenty years. She had been almost fluttered for a few minutes, and still remained so sensibly animated as to put away her work, move Pug from her side, and give all her attention and all the rest of her sofa to her husband. She had no anxieties for anybody to cloud her pleasure: her own time had been irreproachably spent during his absence: she had done a great deal of carpet-work, and made many yards of fringe; and she would have answered as freely for the good conduct and useful pursuits of all the young people as for her own. It was so agreeable to her to see him again, and hear him talk, to have her ear amused and her whole comprehension filled by his narratives, that she began particularly to feel how dreadfully she must have missed him, and how impossible it would have been for her to bear a lengthened absence.\n\nMrs. Norris was by no means to be compared in happiness to her sister. Not that she was incommoded by many fears of Sir Thomas’s disapprobation when the present state of his house should be known, for her judgment had been so blinded that, except by the instinctive caution with which she had whisked away Mr. Rushworth’s pink satin cloak as her brother-in-law entered, she could hardly be said to shew any sign of alarm; but she was vexed by the manner of his return. It had left her nothing to do. Instead of being sent for out of the room, and seeing him first, and having to spread the happy news through the house, Sir Thomas, with a very reasonable dependence, perhaps, on the nerves of his wife and children, had sought no confidant but the butler, and had been following him almost instantaneously into the drawing-room. Mrs. Norris felt herself defrauded of an office on which she had always depended, whether his arrival or his death were to be the thing unfolded; and was now trying to be in a bustle without having anything to bustle about, and labouring to be important where nothing was wanted but tranquillity and silence. Would Sir Thomas have consented to eat, she might have gone to the housekeeper with troublesome directions, and insulted the footmen with injunctions of despatch; but Sir Thomas resolutely declined all dinner: he would take nothing, nothing till tea came—he would rather wait for tea. Still Mrs. Norris was at intervals urging something different; and in the most interesting moment of his passage to England, when the alarm of a French privateer was at the height, she burst through his recital with the proposal of soup. “Sure, my dear Sir Thomas, a basin of soup would be a much better thing for you than tea. Do have a basin of soup.”\n\nSir Thomas could not be provoked. “Still the same anxiety for everybody’s comfort, my dear Mrs. Norris,” was his answer. “But indeed I would rather have nothing but tea.”\n\n“Well, then, Lady Bertram, suppose you speak for tea directly; suppose you hurry Baddeley a little; he seems behindhand to-night.” She carried this point, and Sir Thomas’s narrative proceeded.\n\nAt length there was a pause. His immediate communications were exhausted, and it seemed enough to be looking joyfully around him, now at one, now at another of the beloved circle; but the pause was not long: in the elation of her spirits Lady Bertram became talkative, and what were the sensations of her children upon hearing her say, “How do you think the young people have been amusing themselves lately, Sir Thomas? They have been acting. We have been all alive with acting.”\n\n“Indeed! and what have you been acting?”\n\n“Oh! they’ll tell you all about it.”\n\n“The all will soon be told,” cried Tom hastily, and with affected unconcern; “but it is not worth while to bore my father with it now. You will hear enough of it to-morrow, sir. We have just been trying, by way of doing something, and amusing my mother, just within the last week, to get up a few scenes, a mere trifle. We have had such incessant rains almost since October began, that we have been nearly confined to the house for days together. I have hardly taken out a gun since the 3rd. Tolerable sport the first three days, but there has been no attempting anything since. The first day I went over Mansfield Wood, and Edmund took the copses beyond Easton, and we brought home six brace between us, and might each have killed six times as many, but we respect your pheasants, sir, I assure you, as much as you could desire. I do not think you will find your woods by any means worse stocked than they were. I never saw Mansfield Wood so full of pheasants in my life as this year. I hope you will take a day’s sport there yourself, sir, soon.”\n\nFor the present the danger was over, and Fanny’s sick feelings subsided; but when tea was soon afterwards brought in, and Sir Thomas, getting up, said that he found that he could not be any longer in the house without just looking into his own dear room, every agitation was returning. He was gone before anything had been said to prepare him for the change he must find there; and a pause of alarm followed his disappearance. Edmund was the first to speak—\n\n“Something must be done,” said he.\n\n“It is time to think of our visitors,” said Maria, still feeling her hand pressed to Henry Crawford’s heart, and caring little for anything else. “Where did you leave Miss Crawford, Fanny?”\n\nFanny told of their departure, and delivered their message.\n\n“Then poor Yates is all alone,” cried Tom. “I will go and fetch him. He will be no bad assistant when it all comes out.”\n\nTo the theatre he went, and reached it just in time to witness the first meeting of his father and his friend. Sir Thomas had been a good deal surprised to find candles burning in his room; and on casting his eye round it, to see other symptoms of recent habitation and a general air of confusion in the furniture. The removal of the bookcase from before the billiard-room door struck him especially, but he had scarcely more than time to feel astonished at all this, before there were sounds from the billiard-room to astonish him still farther. Some one was talking there in a very loud accent; he did not know the voice—more than talking—almost hallooing. He stepped to the door, rejoicing at that moment in having the means of immediate communication, and, opening it, found himself on the stage of a theatre, and opposed to a ranting young man, who appeared likely to knock him down backwards. At the very moment of Yates perceiving Sir Thomas, and giving perhaps the very best start he had ever given in the whole course of his rehearsals, Tom Bertram entered at the other end of the room; and never had he found greater difficulty in keeping his countenance. His father’s looks of solemnity and amazement on this his first appearance on any stage, and the gradual metamorphosis of the impassioned Baron Wildenheim into the well-bred and easy Mr. Yates, making his bow and apology to Sir Thomas Bertram, was such an exhibition, such a piece of true acting, as he would not have lost upon any account. It would be the last—in all probability—the last scene on that stage; but he was sure there could not be a finer. The house would close with the greatest eclat.\n\nThere was little time, however, for the indulgence of any images of merriment. It was necessary for him to step forward, too, and assist the introduction, and with many awkward sensations he did his best. Sir Thomas received Mr. Yates with all the appearance of cordiality which was due to his own character, but was really as far from pleased with the necessity of the acquaintance as with the manner of its commencement. Mr. Yates’s family and connexions were sufficiently known to him to render his introduction as the “particular friend,” another of the hundred particular friends of his son, exceedingly unwelcome; and it needed all the felicity of being again at home, and all the forbearance it could supply, to save Sir Thomas from anger on finding himself thus bewildered in his own house, making part of a ridiculous exhibition in the midst of theatrical nonsense, and forced in so untoward a moment to admit the acquaintance of a young man whom he felt sure of disapproving, and whose easy indifference and volubility in the course of the first five minutes seemed to mark him the most at home of the two.\n\nTom understood his father’s thoughts, and heartily wishing he might be always as well disposed to give them but partial expression, began to see, more clearly than he had ever done before, that there might be some ground of offence, that there might be some reason for the glance his father gave towards the ceiling and stucco of the room; and that when he inquired with mild gravity after the fate of the billiard-table, he was not proceeding beyond a very allowable curiosity. A few minutes were enough for such unsatisfactory sensations on each side; and Sir Thomas having exerted himself so far as to speak a few words of calm approbation in reply to an eager appeal of Mr. Yates, as to the happiness of the arrangement, the three gentlemen returned to the drawing-room together, Sir Thomas with an increase of gravity which was not lost on all.\n\n“I come from your theatre,” said he composedly, as he sat down; “I found myself in it rather unexpectedly. Its vicinity to my own room—but in every respect, indeed, it took me by surprise, as I had not the smallest suspicion of your acting having assumed so serious a character. It appears a neat job, however, as far as I could judge by candlelight, and does my friend Christopher Jackson credit.” And then he would have changed the subject, and sipped his coffee in peace over domestic matters of a calmer hue; but Mr. Yates, without discernment to catch Sir Thomas’s meaning, or diffidence, or delicacy, or discretion enough to allow him to lead the discourse while he mingled among the others with the least obtrusiveness himself, would keep him on the topic of the theatre, would torment him with questions and remarks relative to it, and finally would make him hear the whole history of his disappointment at Ecclesford. Sir Thomas listened most politely, but found much to offend his ideas of decorum, and confirm his ill-opinion of Mr. Yates’s habits of thinking, from the beginning to the end of the story; and when it was over, could give him no other assurance of sympathy than what a slight bow conveyed.\n\n“This was, in fact, the origin of our acting,” said Tom, after a moment’s thought. “My friend Yates brought the infection from Ecclesford, and it spread—as those things always spread, you know, sir—the faster, probably, from your having so often encouraged the sort of thing in us formerly. It was like treading old ground again.”\n\nMr. Yates took the subject from his friend as soon as possible, and immediately gave Sir Thomas an account of what they had done and were doing: told him of the gradual increase of their views, the happy conclusion of their first difficulties, and present promising state of affairs; relating everything with so blind an interest as made him not only totally unconscious of the uneasy movements of many of his friends as they sat, the change of countenance, the fidget, the hem! of unquietness, but prevented him even from seeing the expression of the face on which his own eyes were fixed—from seeing Sir Thomas’s dark brow contract as he looked with inquiring earnestness at his daughters and Edmund, dwelling particularly on the latter, and speaking a language, a remonstrance, a reproof, which he felt at his heart. Not less acutely was it felt by Fanny, who had edged back her chair behind her aunt’s end of the sofa, and, screened from notice herself, saw all that was passing before her. Such a look of reproach at Edmund from his father she could never have expected to witness; and to feel that it was in any degree deserved was an aggravation indeed. Sir Thomas’s look implied, “On your judgment, Edmund, I depended; what have you been about?” She knelt in spirit to her uncle, and her bosom swelled to utter, “Oh, not to him! Look so to all the others, but not to him!”\n\nMr. Yates was still talking. “To own the truth, Sir Thomas, we were in the middle of a rehearsal when you arrived this evening. We were going through the three first acts, and not unsuccessfully upon the whole. Our company is now so dispersed, from the Crawfords being gone home, that nothing more can be done to-night; but if you will give us the honour of your company to-morrow evening, I should not be afraid of the result. We bespeak your indulgence, you understand, as young performers; we bespeak your indulgence.”\n\n“My indulgence shall be given, sir,” replied Sir Thomas gravely, “but without any other rehearsal.” And with a relenting smile, he added, “I come home to be happy and indulgent.” Then turning away towards any or all of the rest, he tranquilly said, “Mr. and Miss Crawford were mentioned in my last letters from Mansfield. Do you find them agreeable acquaintance?”\n\nTom was the only one at all ready with an answer, but he being entirely without particular regard for either, without jealousy either in love or acting, could speak very handsomely of both. “Mr. Crawford was a most pleasant, gentleman-like man; his sister a sweet, pretty, elegant, lively girl.”\n\nMr. Rushworth could be silent no longer. “I do not say he is not gentleman-like, considering; but you should tell your father he is not above five feet eight, or he will be expecting a well-looking man.”\n\nSir Thomas did not quite understand this, and looked with some surprise at the speaker.\n\n“If I must say what I think,” continued Mr. Rushworth, “in my opinion it is very disagreeable to be always rehearsing. It is having too much of a good thing. I am not so fond of acting as I was at first. I think we are a great deal better employed, sitting comfortably here among ourselves, and doing nothing.”\n\nSir Thomas looked again, and then replied with an approving smile, “I am happy to find our sentiments on this subject so much the same. It gives me sincere satisfaction. That I should be cautious and quick-sighted, and feel many scruples which my children do not feel, is perfectly natural; and equally so that my value for domestic tranquillity, for a home which shuts out noisy pleasures, should much exceed theirs. But at your time of life to feel all this, is a most favourable circumstance for yourself, and for everybody connected with you; and I am sensible of the importance of having an ally of such weight.”\n\nSir Thomas meant to be giving Mr. Rushworth’s opinion in better words than he could find himself. He was aware that he must not expect a genius in Mr. Rushworth; but as a well-judging, steady young man, with better notions than his elocution would do justice to, he intended to value him very highly. It was impossible for many of the others not to smile. Mr. Rushworth hardly knew what to do with so much meaning; but by looking, as he really felt, most exceedingly pleased with Sir Thomas’s good opinion, and saying scarcely anything, he did his best towards preserving that good opinion a little longer.\n\n# CHAPTER XX\n\nEdmund’s first object the next morning was to see his father alone, and give him a fair statement of the whole acting scheme, defending his own share in it as far only as he could then, in a soberer moment, feel his motives to deserve, and acknowledging, with perfect ingenuousness, that his concession had been attended with such partial good as to make his judgment in it very doubtful. He was anxious, while vindicating himself, to say nothing unkind of the others: but there was only one amongst them whose conduct he could mention without some necessity of defence or palliation. “We have all been more or less to blame,” said he, “every one of us, excepting Fanny. Fanny is the only one who has judged rightly throughout; who has been consistent. Her feelings have been steadily against it from first to last. She never ceased to think of what was due to you. You will find Fanny everything you could wish.”\n\nSir Thomas saw all the impropriety of such a scheme among such a party, and at such a time, as strongly as his son had ever supposed he must; he felt it too much, indeed, for many words; and having shaken hands with Edmund, meant to try to lose the disagreeable impression, and forget how much he had been forgotten himself as soon as he could, after the house had been cleared of every object enforcing the remembrance, and restored to its proper state. He did not enter into any remonstrance with his other children: he was more willing to believe they felt their error than to run the risk of investigation. The reproof of an immediate conclusion of everything, the sweep of every preparation, would be sufficient.\n\nThere was one person, however, in the house, whom he could not leave to learn his sentiments merely through his conduct. He could not help giving Mrs. Norris a hint of his having hoped that her advice might have been interposed to prevent what her judgment must certainly have disapproved. The young people had been very inconsiderate in forming the plan; they ought to have been capable of a better decision themselves; but they were young; and, excepting Edmund, he believed, of unsteady characters; and with greater surprise, therefore, he must regard her acquiescence in their wrong measures, her countenance of their unsafe amusements, than that such measures and such amusements should have been suggested. Mrs. Norris was a little confounded and as nearly being silenced as ever she had been in her life; for she was ashamed to confess having never seen any of the impropriety which was so glaring to Sir Thomas, and would not have admitted that her influence was insufficient—that she might have talked in vain. Her only resource was to get out of the subject as fast as possible, and turn the current of Sir Thomas’s ideas into a happier channel. She had a great deal to insinuate in her own praise as to general attention to the interest and comfort of his family, much exertion and many sacrifices to glance at in the form of hurried walks and sudden removals from her own fireside, and many excellent hints of distrust and economy to Lady Bertram and Edmund to detail, whereby a most considerable saving had always arisen, and more than one bad servant been detected. But her chief strength lay in Sotherton. Her greatest support and glory was in having formed the connexion with the Rushworths. There she was impregnable. She took to herself all the credit of bringing Mr. Rushworth’s admiration of Maria to any effect. “If I had not been active,” said she, “and made a point of being introduced to his mother, and then prevailed on my sister to pay the first visit, I am as certain as I sit here that nothing would have come of it; for Mr. Rushworth is the sort of amiable modest young man who wants a great deal of encouragement, and there were girls enough on the catch for him if we had been idle. But I left no stone unturned. I was ready to move heaven and earth to persuade my sister, and at last I did persuade her. You know the distance to Sotherton; it was in the middle of winter, and the roads almost impassable, but I did persuade her.”\n\n“I know how great, how justly great, your influence is with Lady Bertram and her children, and am the more concerned that it should not have been—”\n\n“My dear Sir Thomas, if you had seen the state of the roads that day! I thought we should never have got through them, though we had the four horses of course; and poor old coachman would attend us, out of his great love and kindness, though he was hardly able to sit the box on account of the rheumatism which I had been doctoring him for ever since Michaelmas. I cured him at last; but he was very bad all the winter—and this was such a day, I could not help going to him up in his room before we set off to advise him not to venture: he was putting on his wig; so I said, ‘Coachman, you had much better not go; your Lady and I shall be very safe; you know how steady Stephen is, and Charles has been upon the leaders so often now, that I am sure there is no fear.’ But, however, I soon found it would not do; he was bent upon going, and as I hate to be worrying and officious, I said no more; but my heart quite ached for him at every jolt, and when we got into the rough lanes about Stoke, where, what with frost and snow upon beds of stones, it was worse than anything you can imagine, I was quite in an agony about him. And then the poor horses too! To see them straining away! You know how I always feel for the horses. And when we got to the bottom of Sandcroft Hill, what do you think I did? You will laugh at me; but I got out and walked up. I did indeed. It might not be saving them much, but it was something, and I could not bear to sit at my ease and be dragged up at the expense of those noble animals. I caught a dreadful cold, but that I did not regard. My object was accomplished in the visit.”\n\n“I hope we shall always think the acquaintance worth any trouble that might be taken to establish it. There is nothing very striking in Mr. Rushworth’s manners, but I was pleased last night with what appeared to be his opinion on one subject: his decided preference of a quiet family party to the bustle and confusion of acting. He seemed to feel exactly as one could wish.”\n\n“Yes, indeed, and the more you know of him the better you will like him. He is not a shining character, but he has a thousand good qualities; and is so disposed to look up to you, that I am quite laughed at about it, for everybody considers it as my doing. ‘Upon my word, Mrs. Norris,’ said Mrs. Grant the other day, ‘if Mr. Rushworth were a son of your own, he could not hold Sir Thomas in greater respect.’”\n\nSir Thomas gave up the point, foiled by her evasions, disarmed by her flattery; and was obliged to rest satisfied with the conviction that where the present pleasure of those she loved was at stake, her kindness did sometimes overpower her judgment.\n\nIt was a busy morning with him. Conversation with any of them occupied but a small part of it. He had to reinstate himself in all the wonted concerns of his Mansfield life: to see his steward and his bailiff; to examine and compute, and, in the intervals of business, to walk into his stables and his gardens, and nearest plantations; but active and methodical, he had not only done all this before he resumed his seat as master of the house at dinner, he had also set the carpenter to work in pulling down what had been so lately put up in the billiard-room, and given the scene-painter his dismissal long enough to justify the pleasing belief of his being then at least as far off as Northampton. The scene-painter was gone, having spoilt only the floor of one room, ruined all the coachman’s sponges, and made five of the under-servants idle and dissatisfied; and Sir Thomas was in hopes that another day or two would suffice to wipe away every outward memento of what had been, even to the destruction of every unbound copy of Lovers’ Vows in the house, for he was burning all that met his eye.\n\nMr. Yates was beginning now to understand Sir Thomas’s intentions, though as far as ever from understanding their source. He and his friend had been out with their guns the chief of the morning, and Tom had taken the opportunity of explaining, with proper apologies for his father’s particularity, what was to be expected. Mr. Yates felt it as acutely as might be supposed. To be a second time disappointed in the same way was an instance of very severe ill-luck; and his indignation was such, that had it not been for delicacy towards his friend, and his friend’s youngest sister, he believed he should certainly attack the baronet on the absurdity of his proceedings, and argue him into a little more rationality. He believed this very stoutly while he was in Mansfield Wood, and all the way home; but there was a something in Sir Thomas, when they sat round the same table, which made Mr. Yates think it wiser to let him pursue his own way, and feel the folly of it without opposition. He had known many disagreeable fathers before, and often been struck with the inconveniences they occasioned, but never, in the whole course of his life, had he seen one of that class so unintelligibly moral, so infamously tyrannical as Sir Thomas. He was not a man to be endured but for his children’s sake, and he might be thankful to his fair daughter Julia that Mr. Yates did yet mean to stay a few days longer under his roof.\n\nThe evening passed with external smoothness, though almost every mind was ruffled; and the music which Sir Thomas called for from his daughters helped to conceal the want of real harmony. Maria was in a good deal of agitation. It was of the utmost consequence to her that Crawford should now lose no time in declaring himself, and she was disturbed that even a day should be gone by without seeming to advance that point. She had been expecting to see him the whole morning, and all the evening, too, was still expecting him. Mr. Rushworth had set off early with the great news for Sotherton; and she had fondly hoped for such an immediate eclaircissement as might save him the trouble of ever coming back again. But they had seen no one from the Parsonage, not a creature, and had heard no tidings beyond a friendly note of congratulation and inquiry from Mrs. Grant to Lady Bertram. It was the first day for many, many weeks, in which the families had been wholly divided. Four-and-twenty hours had never passed before, since August began, without bringing them together in some way or other. It was a sad, anxious day; and the morrow, though differing in the sort of evil, did by no means bring less. A few moments of feverish enjoyment were followed by hours of acute suffering. Henry Crawford was again in the house: he walked up with Dr. Grant, who was anxious to pay his respects to Sir Thomas, and at rather an early hour they were ushered into the breakfast-room, where were most of the family. Sir Thomas soon appeared, and Maria saw with delight and agitation the introduction of the man she loved to her father. Her sensations were indefinable, and so were they a few minutes afterwards upon hearing Henry Crawford, who had a chair between herself and Tom, ask the latter in an undervoice whether there were any plans for resuming the play after the present happy interruption (with a courteous glance at Sir Thomas), because, in that case, he should make a point of returning to Mansfield at any time required by the party: he was going away immediately, being to meet his uncle at Bath without delay; but if there were any prospect of a renewal of Lovers’ Vows, he should hold himself positively engaged, he should break through every other claim, he should absolutely condition with his uncle for attending them whenever he might be wanted. The play should not be lost by his absence.\n\n“From Bath, Norfolk, London, York, wherever I may be,” said he; “I will attend you from any place in England, at an hour’s notice.”\n\nIt was well at that moment that Tom had to speak, and not his sister. He could immediately say with easy fluency, “I am sorry you are going; but as to our play, that is all over—entirely at an end” (looking significantly at his father). “The painter was sent off yesterday, and very little will remain of the theatre to-morrow. I knew how that would be from the first. It is early for Bath. You will find nobody there.”\n\n“It is about my uncle’s usual time.”\n\n“When do you think of going?”\n\n“I may, perhaps, get as far as Banbury to-day.”\n\n“Whose stables do you use at Bath?” was the next question; and while this branch of the subject was under discussion, Maria, who wanted neither pride nor resolution, was preparing to encounter her share of it with tolerable calmness.\n\nTo her he soon turned, repeating much of what he had already said, with only a softened air and stronger expressions of regret. But what availed his expressions or his air? He was going, and, if not voluntarily going, voluntarily intending to stay away; for, excepting what might be due to his uncle, his engagements were all self-imposed. He might talk of necessity, but she knew his independence. The hand which had so pressed hers to his heart! the hand and the heart were alike motionless and passive now! Her spirit supported her, but the agony of her mind was severe. She had not long to endure what arose from listening to language which his actions contradicted, or to bury the tumult of her feelings under the restraint of society; for general civilities soon called his notice from her, and the farewell visit, as it then became openly acknowledged, was a very short one. He was gone—he had touched her hand for the last time, he had made his parting bow, and she might seek directly all that solitude could do for her. Henry Crawford was gone, gone from the house, and within two hours afterwards from the parish; and so ended all the hopes his selfish vanity had raised in Maria and Julia Bertram.\n\nJulia could rejoice that he was gone. His presence was beginning to be odious to her; and if Maria gained him not, she was now cool enough to dispense with any other revenge. She did not want exposure to be added to desertion. Henry Crawford gone, she could even pity her sister.\n\nWith a purer spirit did Fanny rejoice in the intelligence. She heard it at dinner, and felt it a blessing. By all the others it was mentioned with regret; and his merits honoured with due gradation of feeling—from the sincerity of Edmund’s too partial regard, to the unconcern of his mother speaking entirely by rote. Mrs. Norris began to look about her, and wonder that his falling in love with Julia had come to nothing; and could almost fear that she had been remiss herself in forwarding it; but with so many to care for, how was it possible for even her activity to keep pace with her wishes?\n\nAnother day or two, and Mr. Yates was gone likewise. In his departure Sir Thomas felt the chief interest: wanting to be alone with his family, the presence of a stranger superior to Mr. Yates must have been irksome; but of him, trifling and confident, idle and expensive, it was every way vexatious. In himself he was wearisome, but as the friend of Tom and the admirer of Julia he became offensive. Sir Thomas had been quite indifferent to Mr. Crawford’s going or staying: but his good wishes for Mr. Yates’s having a pleasant journey, as he walked with him to the hall-door, were given with genuine satisfaction. Mr. Yates had staid to see the destruction of every theatrical preparation at Mansfield, the removal of everything appertaining to the play: he left the house in all the soberness of its general character; and Sir Thomas hoped, in seeing him out of it, to be rid of the worst object connected with the scheme, and the last that must be inevitably reminding him of its existence.\n\nMrs. Norris contrived to remove one article from his sight that might have distressed him. The curtain, over which she had presided with such talent and such success, went off with her to her cottage, where she happened to be particularly in want of green baize.\n\n# CHAPTER XXI\n\nSir Thomas’s return made a striking change in the ways of the family, independent of Lovers’ Vows. Under his government, Mansfield was an altered place. Some members of their society sent away, and the spirits of many others saddened—it was all sameness and gloom compared with the past—a sombre family party rarely enlivened. There was little intercourse with the Parsonage. Sir Thomas, drawing back from intimacies in general, was particularly disinclined, at this time, for any engagements but in one quarter. The Rushworths were the only addition to his own domestic circle which he could solicit.\n\nEdmund did not wonder that such should be his father’s feelings, nor could he regret anything but the exclusion of the Grants. “But they,” he observed to Fanny, “have a claim. They seem to belong to us; they seem to be part of ourselves. I could wish my father were more sensible of their very great attention to my mother and sisters while he was away. I am afraid they may feel themselves neglected. But the truth is, that my father hardly knows them. They had not been here a twelvemonth when he left England. If he knew them better, he would value their society as it deserves; for they are in fact exactly the sort of people he would like. We are sometimes a little in want of animation among ourselves: my sisters seem out of spirits, and Tom is certainly not at his ease. Dr. and Mrs. Grant would enliven us, and make our evenings pass away with more enjoyment even to my father.”\n\n“Do you think so?” said Fanny: “in my opinion, my uncle would not like any addition. I think he values the very quietness you speak of, and that the repose of his own family circle is all he wants. And it does not appear to me that we are more serious than we used to be—I mean before my uncle went abroad. As well as I can recollect, it was always much the same. There was never much laughing in his presence; or, if there is any difference, it is not more, I think, than such an absence has a tendency to produce at first. There must be a sort of shyness; but I cannot recollect that our evenings formerly were ever merry, except when my uncle was in town. No young people’s are, I suppose, when those they look up to are at home”.\n\n“I believe you are right, Fanny,” was his reply, after a short consideration. “I believe our evenings are rather returned to what they were, than assuming a new character. The novelty was in their being lively. Yet, how strong the impression that only a few weeks will give! I have been feeling as if we had never lived so before.”\n\n“I suppose I am graver than other people,” said Fanny. “The evenings do not appear long to me. I love to hear my uncle talk of the West Indies. I could listen to him for an hour together. It entertains me more than many other things have done; but then I am unlike other people, I dare say.”\n\n“Why should you dare say that?” (smiling). “Do you want to be told that you are only unlike other people in being more wise and discreet? But when did you, or anybody, ever get a compliment from me, Fanny? Go to my father if you want to be complimented. He will satisfy you. Ask your uncle what he thinks, and you will hear compliments enough: and though they may be chiefly on your person, you must put up with it, and trust to his seeing as much beauty of mind in time.”\n\nSuch language was so new to Fanny that it quite embarrassed her.\n\n“Your uncle thinks you very pretty, dear Fanny—and that is the long and the short of the matter. Anybody but myself would have made something more of it, and anybody but you would resent that you had not been thought very pretty before; but the truth is, that your uncle never did admire you till now—and now he does. Your complexion is so improved!—and you have gained so much countenance!—and your figure—nay, Fanny, do not turn away about it—it is but an uncle. If you cannot bear an uncle’s admiration, what is to become of you? You must really begin to harden yourself to the idea of being worth looking at. You must try not to mind growing up into a pretty woman.”\n\n“Oh! don’t talk so, don’t talk so,” cried Fanny, distressed by more feelings than he was aware of; but seeing that she was distressed, he had done with the subject, and only added more seriously—\n\n“Your uncle is disposed to be pleased with you in every respect; and I only wish you would talk to him more. You are one of those who are too silent in the evening circle.”\n\n“But I do talk to him more than I used. I am sure I do. Did not you hear me ask him about the slave-trade last night?”\n\n“I did—and was in hopes the question would be followed up by others. It would have pleased your uncle to be inquired of farther.”\n\n“And I longed to do it—but there was such a dead silence! And while my cousins were sitting by without speaking a word, or seeming at all interested in the subject, I did not like—I thought it would appear as if I wanted to set myself off at their expense, by shewing a curiosity and pleasure in his information which he must wish his own daughters to feel.”\n\n“Miss Crawford was very right in what she said of you the other day: that you seemed almost as fearful of notice and praise as other women were of neglect. We were talking of you at the Parsonage, and those were her words. She has great discernment. I know nobody who distinguishes characters better. For so young a woman it is remarkable! She certainly understands you better than you are understood by the greater part of those who have known you so long; and with regard to some others, I can perceive, from occasional lively hints, the unguarded expressions of the moment, that she could define many as accurately, did not delicacy forbid it. I wonder what she thinks of my father! She must admire him as a fine-looking man, with most gentlemanlike, dignified, consistent manners; but perhaps, having seen him so seldom, his reserve may be a little repulsive. Could they be much together, I feel sure of their liking each other. He would enjoy her liveliness and she has talents to value his powers. I wish they met more frequently! I hope she does not suppose there is any dislike on his side.”\n\n“She must know herself too secure of the regard of all the rest of you,” said Fanny, with half a sigh, “to have any such apprehension. And Sir Thomas’s wishing just at first to be only with his family, is so very natural, that she can argue nothing from that. After a little while, I dare say, we shall be meeting again in the same sort of way, allowing for the difference of the time of year.”\n\n“This is the first October that she has passed in the country since her infancy. I do not call Tunbridge or Cheltenham the country; and November is a still more serious month, and I can see that Mrs. Grant is very anxious for her not finding Mansfield dull as winter comes on.”\n\nFanny could have said a great deal, but it was safer to say nothing, and leave untouched all Miss Crawford’s resources—her accomplishments, her spirits, her importance, her friends, lest it should betray her into any observations seemingly unhandsome. Miss Crawford’s kind opinion of herself deserved at least a grateful forbearance, and she began to talk of something else.\n\n“To-morrow, I think, my uncle dines at Sotherton, and you and Mr. Bertram too. We shall be quite a small party at home. I hope my uncle may continue to like Mr. Rushworth.”\n\n“That is impossible, Fanny. He must like him less after to-morrow’s visit, for we shall be five hours in his company. I should dread the stupidity of the day, if there were not a much greater evil to follow—the impression it must leave on Sir Thomas. He cannot much longer deceive himself. I am sorry for them all, and would give something that Rushworth and Maria had never met.”\n\nIn this quarter, indeed, disappointment was impending over Sir Thomas. Not all his good-will for Mr. Rushworth, not all Mr. Rushworth’s deference for him, could prevent him from soon discerning some part of the truth—that Mr. Rushworth was an inferior young man, as ignorant in business as in books, with opinions in general unfixed, and without seeming much aware of it himself.\n\nHe had expected a very different son-in-law; and beginning to feel grave on Maria’s account, tried to understand her feelings. Little observation there was necessary to tell him that indifference was the most favourable state they could be in. Her behaviour to Mr. Rushworth was careless and cold. She could not, did not like him. Sir Thomas resolved to speak seriously to her. Advantageous as would be the alliance, and long standing and public as was the engagement, her happiness must not be sacrificed to it. Mr. Rushworth had, perhaps, been accepted on too short an acquaintance, and, on knowing him better, she was repenting.\n\nWith solemn kindness Sir Thomas addressed her: told her his fears, inquired into her wishes, entreated her to be open and sincere, and assured her that every inconvenience should be braved, and the connexion entirely given up, if she felt herself unhappy in the prospect of it. He would act for her and release her. Maria had a moment’s struggle as she listened, and only a moment’s: when her father ceased, she was able to give her answer immediately, decidedly, and with no apparent agitation. She thanked him for his great attention, his paternal kindness, but he was quite mistaken in supposing she had the smallest desire of breaking through her engagement, or was sensible of any change of opinion or inclination since her forming it. She had the highest esteem for Mr. Rushworth’s character and disposition, and could not have a doubt of her happiness with him.\n\nSir Thomas was satisfied; too glad to be satisfied, perhaps, to urge the matter quite so far as his judgment might have dictated to others. It was an alliance which he could not have relinquished without pain; and thus he reasoned. Mr. Rushworth was young enough to improve. Mr. Rushworth must and would improve in good society; and if Maria could now speak so securely of her happiness with him, speaking certainly without the prejudice, the blindness of love, she ought to be believed. Her feelings, probably, were not acute; he had never supposed them to be so; but her comforts might not be less on that account; and if she could dispense with seeing her husband a leading, shining character, there would certainly be everything else in her favour. A well-disposed young woman, who did not marry for love, was in general but the more attached to her own family; and the nearness of Sotherton to Mansfield must naturally hold out the greatest temptation, and would, in all probability, be a continual supply of the most amiable and innocent enjoyments. Such and such-like were the reasonings of Sir Thomas, happy to escape the embarrassing evils of a rupture, the wonder, the reflections, the reproach that must attend it; happy to secure a marriage which would bring him such an addition of respectability and influence, and very happy to think anything of his daughter’s disposition that was most favourable for the purpose.\n\nTo her the conference closed as satisfactorily as to him. She was in a state of mind to be glad that she had secured her fate beyond recall: that she had pledged herself anew to Sotherton; that she was safe from the possibility of giving Crawford the triumph of governing her actions, and destroying her prospects; and retired in proud resolve, determined only to behave more cautiously to Mr. Rushworth in future, that her father might not be again suspecting her.\n\nHad Sir Thomas applied to his daughter within the first three or four days after Henry Crawford’s leaving Mansfield, before her feelings were at all tranquillised, before she had given up every hope of him, or absolutely resolved on enduring his rival, her answer might have been different; but after another three or four days, when there was no return, no letter, no message, no symptom of a softened heart, no hope of advantage from separation, her mind became cool enough to seek all the comfort that pride and self revenge could give.\n\nHenry Crawford had destroyed her happiness, but he should not know that he had done it; he should not destroy her credit, her appearance, her prosperity, too. He should not have to think of her as pining in the retirement of Mansfield for him, rejecting Sotherton and London, independence and splendour, for his sake. Independence was more needful than ever; the want of it at Mansfield more sensibly felt. She was less and less able to endure the restraint which her father imposed. The liberty which his absence had given was now become absolutely necessary. She must escape from him and Mansfield as soon as possible, and find consolation in fortune and consequence, bustle and the world, for a wounded spirit. Her mind was quite determined, and varied not.\n\nTo such feelings delay, even the delay of much preparation, would have been an evil, and Mr. Rushworth could hardly be more impatient for the marriage than herself. In all the important preparations of the mind she was complete: being prepared for matrimony by an hatred of home, restraint, and tranquillity; by the misery of disappointed affection, and contempt of the man she was to marry. The rest might wait. The preparations of new carriages and furniture might wait for London and spring, when her own taste could have fairer play.\n\nThe principals being all agreed in this respect, it soon appeared that a very few weeks would be sufficient for such arrangements as must precede the wedding.\n\nMrs. Rushworth was quite ready to retire, and make way for the fortunate young woman whom her dear son had selected; and very early in November removed herself, her maid, her footman, and her chariot, with true dowager propriety, to Bath, there to parade over the wonders of Sotherton in her evening parties; enjoying them as thoroughly, perhaps, in the animation of a card-table, as she had ever done on the spot; and before the middle of the same month the ceremony had taken place which gave Sotherton another mistress.\n\nIt was a very proper wedding. The bride was elegantly dressed; the two bridesmaids were duly inferior; her father gave her away; her mother stood with salts in her hand, expecting to be agitated; her aunt tried to cry; and the service was impressively read by Dr. Grant. Nothing could be objected to when it came under the discussion of the neighbourhood, except that the carriage which conveyed the bride and bridegroom and Julia from the church-door to Sotherton was the same chaise which Mr. Rushworth had used for a twelvemonth before. In everything else the etiquette of the day might stand the strictest investigation.\n\nIt was done, and they were gone. Sir Thomas felt as an anxious father must feel, and was indeed experiencing much of the agitation which his wife had been apprehensive of for herself, but had fortunately escaped. Mrs. Norris, most happy to assist in the duties of the day, by spending it at the Park to support her sister’s spirits, and drinking the health of Mr. and Mrs. Rushworth in a supernumerary glass or two, was all joyous delight; for she had made the match; she had done everything; and no one would have supposed, from her confident triumph, that she had ever heard of conjugal infelicity in her life, or could have the smallest insight into the disposition of the niece who had been brought up under her eye.\n\nThe plan of the young couple was to proceed, after a few days, to Brighton, and take a house there for some weeks. Every public place was new to Maria, and Brighton is almost as gay in winter as in summer. When the novelty of amusement there was over, it would be time for the wider range of London.\n\nJulia was to go with them to Brighton. Since rivalry between the sisters had ceased, they had been gradually recovering much of their former good understanding; and were at least sufficiently friends to make each of them exceedingly glad to be with the other at such a time. Some other companion than Mr. Rushworth was of the first consequence to his lady; and Julia was quite as eager for novelty and pleasure as Maria, though she might not have struggled through so much to obtain them, and could better bear a subordinate situation.\n\nTheir departure made another material change at Mansfield, a chasm which required some time to fill up. The family circle became greatly contracted; and though the Miss Bertrams had latterly added little to its gaiety, they could not but be missed. Even their mother missed them; and how much more their tenderhearted cousin, who wandered about the house, and thought of them, and felt for them, with a degree of affectionate regret which they had never done much to deserve!\n\n# CHAPTER XXII\n\nFanny’s consequence increased on the departure of her cousins. Becoming, as she then did, the only young woman in the drawing-room, the only occupier of that interesting division of a family in which she had hitherto held so humble a third, it was impossible for her not to be more looked at, more thought of and attended to, than she had ever been before; and “Where is Fanny?” became no uncommon question, even without her being wanted for any one’s convenience.\n\nNot only at home did her value increase, but at the Parsonage too. In that house, which she had hardly entered twice a year since Mr. Norris’s death, she became a welcome, an invited guest, and in the gloom and dirt of a November day, most acceptable to Mary Crawford. Her visits there, beginning by chance, were continued by solicitation. Mrs. Grant, really eager to get any change for her sister, could, by the easiest self-deceit, persuade herself that she was doing the kindest thing by Fanny, and giving her the most important opportunities of improvement in pressing her frequent calls.\n\nFanny, having been sent into the village on some errand by her aunt Norris, was overtaken by a heavy shower close to the Parsonage; and being descried from one of the windows endeavouring to find shelter under the branches and lingering leaves of an oak just beyond their premises, was forced, though not without some modest reluctance on her part, to come in. A civil servant she had withstood; but when Dr. Grant himself went out with an umbrella, there was nothing to be done but to be very much ashamed, and to get into the house as fast as possible; and to poor Miss Crawford, who had just been contemplating the dismal rain in a very desponding state of mind, sighing over the ruin of all her plan of exercise for that morning, and of every chance of seeing a single creature beyond themselves for the next twenty-four hours, the sound of a little bustle at the front door, and the sight of Miss Price dripping with wet in the vestibule, was delightful. The value of an event on a wet day in the country was most forcibly brought before her. She was all alive again directly, and among the most active in being useful to Fanny, in detecting her to be wetter than she would at first allow, and providing her with dry clothes; and Fanny, after being obliged to submit to all this attention, and to being assisted and waited on by mistresses and maids, being also obliged, on returning downstairs, to be fixed in their drawing-room for an hour while the rain continued, the blessing of something fresh to see and think of was thus extended to Miss Crawford, and might carry on her spirits to the period of dressing and dinner.\n\nThe two sisters were so kind to her, and so pleasant, that Fanny might have enjoyed her visit could she have believed herself not in the way, and could she have foreseen that the weather would certainly clear at the end of the hour, and save her from the shame of having Dr. Grant’s carriage and horses out to take her home, with which she was threatened. As to anxiety for any alarm that her absence in such weather might occasion at home, she had nothing to suffer on that score; for as her being out was known only to her two aunts, she was perfectly aware that none would be felt, and that in whatever cottage aunt Norris might chuse to establish her during the rain, her being in such cottage would be indubitable to aunt Bertram.\n\nIt was beginning to look brighter, when Fanny, observing a harp in the room, asked some questions about it, which soon led to an acknowledgment of her wishing very much to hear it, and a confession, which could hardly be believed, of her having never yet heard it since its being in Mansfield. To Fanny herself it appeared a very simple and natural circumstance. She had scarcely ever been at the Parsonage since the instrument’s arrival, there had been no reason that she should; but Miss Crawford, calling to mind an early expressed wish on the subject, was concerned at her own neglect; and “Shall I play to you now?” and “What will you have?” were questions immediately following with the readiest good-humour.\n\nShe played accordingly; happy to have a new listener, and a listener who seemed so much obliged, so full of wonder at the performance, and who shewed herself not wanting in taste. She played till Fanny’s eyes, straying to the window on the weather’s being evidently fair, spoke what she felt must be done.\n\n“Another quarter of an hour,” said Miss Crawford, “and we shall see how it will be. Do not run away the first moment of its holding up. Those clouds look alarming.”\n\n“But they are passed over,” said Fanny. “I have been watching them. This weather is all from the south.”\n\n“South or north, I know a black cloud when I see it; and you must not set forward while it is so threatening. And besides, I want to play something more to you—a very pretty piece—and your cousin Edmund’s prime favourite. You must stay and hear your cousin’s favourite.”\n\nFanny felt that she must; and though she had not waited for that sentence to be thinking of Edmund, such a memento made her particularly awake to his idea, and she fancied him sitting in that room again and again, perhaps in the very spot where she sat now, listening with constant delight to the favourite air, played, as it appeared to her, with superior tone and expression; and though pleased with it herself, and glad to like whatever was liked by him, she was more sincerely impatient to go away at the conclusion of it than she had been before; and on this being evident, she was so kindly asked to call again, to take them in her walk whenever she could, to come and hear more of the harp, that she felt it necessary to be done, if no objection arose at home.\n\nSuch was the origin of the sort of intimacy which took place between them within the first fortnight after the Miss Bertrams’ going away—an intimacy resulting principally from Miss Crawford’s desire of something new, and which had little reality in Fanny’s feelings. Fanny went to her every two or three days: it seemed a kind of fascination: she could not be easy without going, and yet it was without loving her, without ever thinking like her, without any sense of obligation for being sought after now when nobody else was to be had; and deriving no higher pleasure from her conversation than occasional amusement, and that often at the expense of her judgment, when it was raised by pleasantry on people or subjects which she wished to be respected. She went, however, and they sauntered about together many an half-hour in Mrs. Grant’s shrubbery, the weather being unusually mild for the time of year, and venturing sometimes even to sit down on one of the benches now comparatively unsheltered, remaining there perhaps till, in the midst of some tender ejaculation of Fanny’s on the sweets of so protracted an autumn, they were forced, by the sudden swell of a cold gust shaking down the last few yellow leaves about them, to jump up and walk for warmth.\n\n“This is pretty, very pretty,” said Fanny, looking around her as they were thus sitting together one day; “every time I come into this shrubbery I am more struck with its growth and beauty. Three years ago, this was nothing but a rough hedgerow along the upper side of the field, never thought of as anything, or capable of becoming anything; and now it is converted into a walk, and it would be difficult to say whether most valuable as a convenience or an ornament; and perhaps, in another three years, we may be forgetting—almost forgetting what it was before. How wonderful, how very wonderful the operations of time, and the changes of the human mind!” And following the latter train of thought, she soon afterwards added: “If any one faculty of our nature may be called more wonderful than the rest, I do think it is memory. There seems something more speakingly incomprehensible in the powers, the failures, the inequalities of memory, than in any other of our intelligences. The memory is sometimes so retentive, so serviceable, so obedient; at others, so bewildered and so weak; and at others again, so tyrannic, so beyond control! We are, to be sure, a miracle every way; but our powers of recollecting and of forgetting do seem peculiarly past finding out.”\n\nMiss Crawford, untouched and inattentive, had nothing to say; and Fanny, perceiving it, brought back her own mind to what she thought must interest.\n\n“It may seem impertinent in me to praise, but I must admire the taste Mrs. Grant has shewn in all this. There is such a quiet simplicity in the plan of the walk! Not too much attempted!”\n\n“Yes,” replied Miss Crawford carelessly, “it does very well for a place of this sort. One does not think of extent here; and between ourselves, till I came to Mansfield, I had not imagined a country parson ever aspired to a shrubbery, or anything of the kind.”\n\n“I am so glad to see the evergreens thrive!” said Fanny, in reply. “My uncle’s gardener always says the soil here is better than his own, and so it appears from the growth of the laurels and evergreens in general. The evergreen! How beautiful, how welcome, how wonderful the evergreen! When one thinks of it, how astonishing a variety of nature! In some countries we know the tree that sheds its leaf is the variety, but that does not make it less amazing that the same soil and the same sun should nurture plants differing in the first rule and law of their existence. You will think me rhapsodising; but when I am out of doors, especially when I am sitting out of doors, I am very apt to get into this sort of wondering strain. One cannot fix one’s eyes on the commonest natural production without finding food for a rambling fancy.”\n\n“To say the truth,” replied Miss Crawford, “I am something like the famous Doge at the court of Lewis XIV.; and may declare that I see no wonder in this shrubbery equal to seeing myself in it. If anybody had told me a year ago that this place would be my home, that I should be spending month after month here, as I have done, I certainly should not have believed them. I have now been here nearly five months; and, moreover, the quietest five months I ever passed.”\n\n“Too quiet for you, I believe.”\n\n“I should have thought so theoretically myself, but,” and her eyes brightened as she spoke, “take it all and all, I never spent so happy a summer. But then,” with a more thoughtful air and lowered voice, “there is no saying what it may lead to.”\n\nFanny’s heart beat quick, and she felt quite unequal to surmising or soliciting anything more. Miss Crawford, however, with renewed animation, soon went on—\n\n“I am conscious of being far better reconciled to a country residence than I had ever expected to be. I can even suppose it pleasant to spend half the year in the country, under certain circumstances, very pleasant. An elegant, moderate-sized house in the centre of family connexions; continual engagements among them; commanding the first society in the neighbourhood; looked up to, perhaps, as leading it even more than those of larger fortune, and turning from the cheerful round of such amusements to nothing worse than a tête-à-tête with the person one feels most agreeable in the world. There is nothing frightful in such a picture, is there, Miss Price? One need not envy the new Mrs. Rushworth with such a home as that.”\n\n“Envy Mrs. Rushworth!” was all that Fanny attempted to say. “Come, come, it would be very un-handsome in us to be severe on Mrs. Rushworth, for I look forward to our owing her a great many gay, brilliant, happy hours. I expect we shall be all very much at Sotherton another year. Such a match as Miss Bertram has made is a public blessing; for the first pleasures of Mr. Rushworth’s wife must be to fill her house, and give the best balls in the country.”\n\nFanny was silent, and Miss Crawford relapsed into thoughtfulness, till suddenly looking up at the end of a few minutes, she exclaimed, “Ah! here he is.” It was not Mr. Rushworth, however, but Edmund, who then appeared walking towards them with Mrs. Grant. “My sister and Mr. Bertram. I am so glad your eldest cousin is gone, that he may be Mr. Bertram again. There is something in the sound of Mr. Edmund Bertram so formal, so pitiful, so younger-brother-like, that I detest it.”\n\n“How differently we feel!” cried Fanny. “To me, the sound of Mr. Bertram is so cold and nothing-meaning, so entirely without warmth or character! It just stands for a gentleman, and that’s all. But there is nobleness in the name of Edmund. It is a name of heroism and renown; of kings, princes, and knights; and seems to breathe the spirit of chivalry and warm affections.”\n\n“I grant you the name is good in itself, and Lord Edmund or Sir Edmund sound delightfully; but sink it under the chill, the annihilation of a Mr., and Mr. Edmund is no more than Mr. John or Mr. Thomas. Well, shall we join and disappoint them of half their lecture upon sitting down out of doors at this time of year, by being up before they can begin?”\n\nEdmund met them with particular pleasure. It was the first time of his seeing them together since the beginning of that better acquaintance which he had been hearing of with great satisfaction. A friendship between two so very dear to him was exactly what he could have wished: and to the credit of the lover’s understanding, be it stated, that he did not by any means consider Fanny as the only, or even as the greater gainer by such a friendship.\n\n“Well,” said Miss Crawford, “and do you not scold us for our imprudence? What do you think we have been sitting down for but to be talked to about it, and entreated and supplicated never to do so again?”\n\n“Perhaps I might have scolded,” said Edmund, “if either of you had been sitting down alone; but while you do wrong together, I can overlook a great deal.”\n\n“They cannot have been sitting long,” cried Mrs. Grant, “for when I went up for my shawl I saw them from the staircase window, and then they were walking.”\n\n“And really,” added Edmund, “the day is so mild, that your sitting down for a few minutes can be hardly thought imprudent. Our weather must not always be judged by the calendar. We may sometimes take greater liberties in November than in May.”\n\n“Upon my word,” cried Miss Crawford, “you are two of the most disappointing and unfeeling kind friends I ever met with! There is no giving you a moment’s uneasiness. You do not know how much we have been suffering, nor what chills we have felt! But I have long thought Mr. Bertram one of the worst subjects to work on, in any little manoeuvre against common sense, that a woman could be plagued with. I had very little hope of him from the first; but you, Mrs. Grant, my sister, my own sister, I think I had a right to alarm you a little.”\n\n“Do not flatter yourself, my dearest Mary. You have not the smallest chance of moving me. I have my alarms, but they are quite in a different quarter; and if I could have altered the weather, you would have had a good sharp east wind blowing on you the whole time—for here are some of my plants which Robert will leave out because the nights are so mild, and I know the end of it will be, that we shall have a sudden change of weather, a hard frost setting in all at once, taking everybody (at least Robert) by surprise, and I shall lose every one; and what is worse, cook has just been telling me that the turkey, which I particularly wished not to be dressed till Sunday, because I know how much more Dr. Grant would enjoy it on Sunday after the fatigues of the day, will not keep beyond to-morrow. These are something like grievances, and make me think the weather most unseasonably close.”\n\n“The sweets of housekeeping in a country village!” said Miss Crawford archly. “Commend me to the nurseryman and the poulterer.”\n\n“My dear child, commend Dr. Grant to the deanery of Westminster or St. Paul’s, and I should be as glad of your nurseryman and poulterer as you could be. But we have no such people in Mansfield. What would you have me do?”\n\n“Oh! you can do nothing but what you do already: be plagued very often, and never lose your temper.”\n\n“Thank you; but there is no escaping these little vexations, Mary, live where we may; and when you are settled in town and I come to see you, I dare say I shall find you with yours, in spite of the nurseryman and the poulterer, perhaps on their very account. Their remoteness and unpunctuality, or their exorbitant charges and frauds, will be drawing forth bitter lamentations.”\n\n“I mean to be too rich to lament or to feel anything of the sort. A large income is the best recipe for happiness I ever heard of. It certainly may secure all the myrtle and turkey part of it.”\n\n“You intend to be very rich?” said Edmund, with a look which, to Fanny’s eye, had a great deal of serious meaning.\n\n“To be sure. Do not you? Do not we all?”\n\n“I cannot intend anything which it must be so completely beyond my power to command. Miss Crawford may chuse her degree of wealth. She has only to fix on her number of thousands a year, and there can be no doubt of their coming. My intentions are only not to be poor.”\n\n“By moderation and economy, and bringing down your wants to your income, and all that. I understand you—and a very proper plan it is for a person at your time of life, with such limited means and indifferent connexions. What can you want but a decent maintenance? You have not much time before you; and your relations are in no situation to do anything for you, or to mortify you by the contrast of their own wealth and consequence. Be honest and poor, by all means—but I shall not envy you; I do not much think I shall even respect you. I have a much greater respect for those that are honest and rich.”\n\n“Your degree of respect for honesty, rich or poor, is precisely what I have no manner of concern with. I do not mean to be poor. Poverty is exactly what I have determined against. Honesty, in the something between, in the middle state of worldly circumstances, is all that I am anxious for your not looking down on.”\n\n“But I do look down upon it, if it might have been higher. I must look down upon anything contented with obscurity when it might rise to distinction.”\n\n“But how may it rise? How may my honesty at least rise to any distinction?”\n\nThis was not so very easy a question to answer, and occasioned an “Oh!” of some length from the fair lady before she could add, “You ought to be in parliament, or you should have gone into the army ten years ago.”\n\n“That is not much to the purpose now; and as to my being in parliament, I believe I must wait till there is an especial assembly for the representation of younger sons who have little to live on. No, Miss Crawford,” he added, in a more serious tone, “there are distinctions which I should be miserable if I thought myself without any chance—absolutely without chance or possibility of obtaining—but they are of a different character.”\n\nA look of consciousness as he spoke, and what seemed a consciousness of manner on Miss Crawford’s side as she made some laughing answer, was sorrowfull food for Fanny’s observation; and finding herself quite unable to attend as she ought to Mrs. Grant, by whose side she was now following the others, she had nearly resolved on going home immediately, and only waited for courage to say so, when the sound of the great clock at Mansfield Park, striking three, made her feel that she had really been much longer absent than usual, and brought the previous self-inquiry of whether she should take leave or not just then, and how, to a very speedy issue. With undoubting decision she directly began her adieus; and Edmund began at the same time to recollect that his mother had been inquiring for her, and that he had walked down to the Parsonage on purpose to bring her back.\n\nFanny’s hurry increased; and without in the least expecting Edmund’s attendance, she would have hastened away alone; but the general pace was quickened, and they all accompanied her into the house, through which it was necessary to pass. Dr. Grant was in the vestibule, and as they stopt to speak to him she found, from Edmund’s manner, that he did mean to go with her. He too was taking leave. She could not but be thankful. In the moment of parting, Edmund was invited by Dr. Grant to eat his mutton with him the next day; and Fanny had barely time for an unpleasant feeling on the occasion, when Mrs. Grant, with sudden recollection, turned to her and asked for the pleasure of her company too. This was so new an attention, so perfectly new a circumstance in the events of Fanny’s life, that she was all surprise and embarrassment; and while stammering out her great obligation, and her “but she did not suppose it would be in her power,” was looking at Edmund for his opinion and help. But Edmund, delighted with her having such an happiness offered, and ascertaining with half a look, and half a sentence, that she had no objection but on her aunt’s account, could not imagine that his mother would make any difficulty of sparing her, and therefore gave his decided open advice that the invitation should be accepted; and though Fanny would not venture, even on his encouragement, to such a flight of audacious independence, it was soon settled, that if nothing were heard to the contrary, Mrs. Grant might expect her.\n\n“And you know what your dinner will be,” said Mrs. Grant, smiling—“the turkey, and I assure you a very fine one; for, my dear,” turning to her husband, “cook insists upon the turkey’s being dressed to-morrow.”\n\n“Very well, very well,” cried Dr. Grant, “all the better; I am glad to hear you have anything so good in the house. But Miss Price and Mr. Edmund Bertram, I dare say, would take their chance. We none of us want to hear the bill of fare. A friendly meeting, and not a fine dinner, is all we have in view. A turkey, or a goose, or a leg of mutton, or whatever you and your cook chuse to give us.”\n\nThe two cousins walked home together; and, except in the immediate discussion of this engagement, which Edmund spoke of with the warmest satisfaction, as so particularly desirable for her in the intimacy which he saw with so much pleasure established, it was a silent walk; for having finished that subject, he grew thoughtful and indisposed for any other.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIII\n\n“But why should Mrs. Grant ask Fanny?” said Lady Bertram. “How came she to think of asking Fanny? Fanny never dines there, you know, in this sort of way. I cannot spare her, and I am sure she does not want to go. Fanny, you do not want to go, do you?”\n\n“If you put such a question to her,” cried Edmund, preventing his cousin’s speaking, “Fanny will immediately say No; but I am sure, my dear mother, she would like to go; and I can see no reason why she should not.”\n\n“I cannot imagine why Mrs. Grant should think of asking her? She never did before. She used to ask your sisters now and then, but she never asked Fanny.”\n\n“If you cannot do without me, ma’am—” said Fanny, in a self-denying tone.\n\n“But my mother will have my father with her all the evening.”\n\n“To be sure, so I shall.”\n\n“Suppose you take my father’s opinion, ma’am.”\n\n“That’s well thought of. So I will, Edmund. I will ask Sir Thomas, as soon as he comes in, whether I can do without her.”\n\n“As you please, ma’am, on that head; but I meant my father’s opinion as to the propriety of the invitation’s being accepted or not; and I think he will consider it a right thing by Mrs. Grant, as well as by Fanny, that being the first invitation it should be accepted.”\n\n“I do not know. We will ask him. But he will be very much surprised that Mrs. Grant should ask Fanny at all.”\n\nThere was nothing more to be said, or that could be said to any purpose, till Sir Thomas were present; but the subject involving, as it did, her own evening’s comfort for the morrow, was so much uppermost in Lady Bertram’s mind, that half an hour afterwards, on his looking in for a minute in his way from his plantation to his dressing-room, she called him back again, when he had almost closed the door, with “Sir Thomas, stop a moment—I have something to say to you.”\n\nHer tone of calm languor, for she never took the trouble of raising her voice, was always heard and attended to; and Sir Thomas came back. Her story began; and Fanny immediately slipped out of the room; for to hear herself the subject of any discussion with her uncle was more than her nerves could bear. She was anxious, she knew—more anxious perhaps than she ought to be—for what was it after all whether she went or staid? but if her uncle were to be a great while considering and deciding, and with very grave looks, and those grave looks directed to her, and at last decide against her, she might not be able to appear properly submissive and indifferent. Her cause, meanwhile, went on well. It began, on Lady Bertram’s part, with—“I have something to tell you that will surprise you. Mrs. Grant has asked Fanny to dinner.”\n\n“Well,” said Sir Thomas, as if waiting more to accomplish the surprise.\n\n“Edmund wants her to go. But how can I spare her?”\n\n“She will be late,” said Sir Thomas, taking out his watch; “but what is your difficulty?”\n\nEdmund found himself obliged to speak and fill up the blanks in his mother’s story. He told the whole; and she had only to add, “So strange! for Mrs. Grant never used to ask her.”\n\n“But is it not very natural,” observed Edmund, “that Mrs. Grant should wish to procure so agreeable a visitor for her sister?”\n\n“Nothing can be more natural,” said Sir Thomas, after a short deliberation; “nor, were there no sister in the case, could anything, in my opinion, be more natural. Mrs. Grant’s shewing civility to Miss Price, to Lady Bertram’s niece, could never want explanation. The only surprise I can feel is, that this should be the first time of its being paid. Fanny was perfectly right in giving only a conditional answer. She appears to feel as she ought. But as I conclude that she must wish to go, since all young people like to be together, I can see no reason why she should be denied the indulgence.”\n\n“But can I do without her, Sir Thomas?”\n\n“Indeed I think you may.”\n\n“She always makes tea, you know, when my sister is not here.”\n\n“Your sister, perhaps, may be prevailed on to spend the day with us, and I shall certainly be at home.”\n\n“Very well, then, Fanny may go, Edmund.”\n\nThe good news soon followed her. Edmund knocked at her door in his way to his own.\n\n“Well, Fanny, it is all happily settled, and without the smallest hesitation on your uncle’s side. He had but one opinion. You are to go.”\n\n“Thank you, I am so glad,” was Fanny’s instinctive reply; though when she had turned from him and shut the door, she could not help feeling, “And yet why should I be glad? for am I not certain of seeing or hearing something there to pain me?”\n\nIn spite of this conviction, however, she was glad. Simple as such an engagement might appear in other eyes, it had novelty and importance in hers, for excepting the day at Sotherton, she had scarcely ever dined out before; and though now going only half a mile, and only to three people, still it was dining out, and all the little interests of preparation were enjoyments in themselves. She had neither sympathy nor assistance from those who ought to have entered into her feelings and directed her taste; for Lady Bertram never thought of being useful to anybody, and Mrs. Norris, when she came on the morrow, in consequence of an early call and invitation from Sir Thomas, was in a very ill humour, and seemed intent only on lessening her niece’s pleasure, both present and future, as much as possible.\n\n“Upon my word, Fanny, you are in high luck to meet with such attention and indulgence! You ought to be very much obliged to Mrs. Grant for thinking of you, and to your aunt for letting you go, and you ought to look upon it as something extraordinary; for I hope you are aware that there is no real occasion for your going into company in this sort of way, or ever dining out at all; and it is what you must not depend upon ever being repeated. Nor must you be fancying that the invitation is meant as any particular compliment to you; the compliment is intended to your uncle and aunt and me. Mrs. Grant thinks it a civility due to us to take a little notice of you, or else it would never have come into her head, and you may be very certain that, if your cousin Julia had been at home, you would not have been asked at all.”\n\nMrs. Norris had now so ingeniously done away all Mrs. Grant’s part of the favour, that Fanny, who found herself expected to speak, could only say that she was very much obliged to her aunt Bertram for sparing her, and that she was endeavouring to put her aunt’s evening work in such a state as to prevent her being missed.\n\n“Oh! depend upon it, your aunt can do very well without you, or you would not be allowed to go. I shall be here, so you may be quite easy about your aunt. And I hope you will have a very agreeable day, and find it all mighty delightful. But I must observe that five is the very awkwardest of all possible numbers to sit down to table; and I cannot but be surprised that such an elegant lady as Mrs. Grant should not contrive better! And round their enormous great wide table, too, which fills up the room so dreadfully! Had the doctor been contented to take my dining-table when I came away, as anybody in their senses would have done, instead of having that absurd new one of his own, which is wider, literally wider than the dinner-table here, how infinitely better it would have been! and how much more he would have been respected! for people are never respected when they step out of their proper sphere. Remember that, Fanny. Five—only five to be sitting round that table. However, you will have dinner enough on it for ten, I dare say.”\n\nMrs. Norris fetched breath, and went on again.\n\n“The nonsense and folly of people’s stepping out of their rank and trying to appear above themselves, makes me think it right to give you a hint, Fanny, now that you are going into company without any of us; and I do beseech and entreat you not to be putting yourself forward, and talking and giving your opinion as if you were one of your cousins—as if you were dear Mrs. Rushworth or Julia. That will never do, believe me. Remember, wherever you are, you must be the lowest and last; and though Miss Crawford is in a manner at home at the Parsonage, you are not to be taking place of her. And as to coming away at night, you are to stay just as long as Edmund chuses. Leave him to settle that.”\n\n“Yes, ma’am, I should not think of anything else.”\n\n“And if it should rain, which I think exceedingly likely, for I never saw it more threatening for a wet evening in my life, you must manage as well as you can, and not be expecting the carriage to be sent for you. I certainly do not go home to-night, and, therefore, the carriage will not be out on my account; so you must make up your mind to what may happen, and take your things accordingly.”\n\nHer niece thought it perfectly reasonable. She rated her own claims to comfort as low even as Mrs. Norris could; and when Sir Thomas soon afterwards, just opening the door, said, “Fanny, at what time would you have the carriage come round?” she felt a degree of astonishment which made it impossible for her to speak.\n\n“My dear Sir Thomas!” cried Mrs. Norris, red with anger, “Fanny can walk.”\n\n“Walk!” repeated Sir Thomas, in a tone of most unanswerable dignity, and coming farther into the room. “My niece walk to a dinner engagement at this time of the year! Will twenty minutes after four suit you?”\n\n“Yes, sir,” was Fanny’s humble answer, given with the feelings almost of a criminal towards Mrs. Norris; and not bearing to remain with her in what might seem a state of triumph, she followed her uncle out of the room, having staid behind him only long enough to hear these words spoken in angry agitation—\n\n“Quite unnecessary! a great deal too kind! But Edmund goes; true, it is upon Edmund’s account. I observed he was hoarse on Thursday night.”\n\nBut this could not impose on Fanny. She felt that the carriage was for herself, and herself alone: and her uncle’s consideration of her, coming immediately after such representations from her aunt, cost her some tears of gratitude when she was alone.\n\nThe coachman drove round to a minute; another minute brought down the gentleman; and as the lady had, with a most scrupulous fear of being late, been many minutes seated in the drawing-room, Sir Thomas saw them off in as good time as his own correctly punctual habits required.\n\n“Now I must look at you, Fanny,” said Edmund, with the kind smile of an affectionate brother, “and tell you how I like you; and as well as I can judge by this light, you look very nicely indeed. What have you got on?”\n\n“The new dress that my uncle was so good as to give me on my cousin’s marriage. I hope it is not too fine; but I thought I ought to wear it as soon as I could, and that I might not have such another opportunity all the winter. I hope you do not think me too fine.”\n\n“A woman can never be too fine while she is all in white. No, I see no finery about you; nothing but what is perfectly proper. Your gown seems very pretty. I like these glossy spots. Has not Miss Crawford a gown something the same?”\n\nIn approaching the Parsonage they passed close by the stable-yard and coach-house.\n\n“Heyday!” said Edmund, “here’s company, here’s a carriage! who have they got to meet us?” And letting down the side-glass to distinguish, “’Tis Crawford’s, Crawford’s barouche, I protest! There are his own two men pushing it back into its old quarters. He is here, of course. This is quite a surprise, Fanny. I shall be very glad to see him.”\n\nThere was no occasion, there was no time for Fanny to say how very differently she felt; but the idea of having such another to observe her was a great increase of the trepidation with which she performed the very awful ceremony of walking into the drawing-room.\n\nIn the drawing-room Mr. Crawford certainly was, having been just long enough arrived to be ready for dinner; and the smiles and pleased looks of the three others standing round him, shewed how welcome was his sudden resolution of coming to them for a few days on leaving Bath. A very cordial meeting passed between him and Edmund; and with the exception of Fanny, the pleasure was general; and even to her there might be some advantage in his presence, since every addition to the party must rather forward her favourite indulgence of being suffered to sit silent and unattended to. She was soon aware of this herself; for though she must submit, as her own propriety of mind directed, in spite of her aunt Norris’s opinion, to being the principal lady in company, and to all the little distinctions consequent thereon, she found, while they were at table, such a happy flow of conversation prevailing, in which she was not required to take any part—there was so much to be said between the brother and sister about Bath, so much between the two young men about hunting, so much of politics between Mr. Crawford and Dr. Grant, and of everything and all together between Mr. Crawford and Mrs. Grant, as to leave her the fairest prospect of having only to listen in quiet, and of passing a very agreeable day. She could not compliment the newly arrived gentleman, however, with any appearance of interest, in a scheme for extending his stay at Mansfield, and sending for his hunters from Norfolk, which, suggested by Dr. Grant, advised by Edmund, and warmly urged by the two sisters, was soon in possession of his mind, and which he seemed to want to be encouraged even by her to resolve on. Her opinion was sought as to the probable continuance of the open weather, but her answers were as short and indifferent as civility allowed. She could not wish him to stay, and would much rather not have him speak to her.\n\nHer two absent cousins, especially Maria, were much in her thoughts on seeing him; but no embarrassing remembrance affected his spirits. Here he was again on the same ground where all had passed before, and apparently as willing to stay and be happy without the Miss Bertrams, as if he had never known Mansfield in any other state. She heard them spoken of by him only in a general way, till they were all re-assembled in the drawing-room, when Edmund, being engaged apart in some matter of business with Dr. Grant, which seemed entirely to engross them, and Mrs. Grant occupied at the tea-table, he began talking of them with more particularity to his other sister. With a significant smile, which made Fanny quite hate him, he said, “So! Rushworth and his fair bride are at Brighton, I understand; happy man!”\n\n“Yes, they have been there about a fortnight, Miss Price, have they not? And Julia is with them.”\n\n“And Mr. Yates, I presume, is not far off.”\n\n“Mr. Yates! Oh! we hear nothing of Mr. Yates. I do not imagine he figures much in the letters to Mansfield Park; do you, Miss Price? I think my friend Julia knows better than to entertain her father with Mr. Yates.”\n\n“Poor Rushworth and his two-and-forty speeches!” continued Crawford. “Nobody can ever forget them. Poor fellow! I see him now—his toil and his despair. Well, I am much mistaken if his lovely Maria will ever want him to make two-and-forty speeches to her”; adding, with a momentary seriousness, “She is too good for him—much too good.” And then changing his tone again to one of gentle gallantry, and addressing Fanny, he said, “You were Mr. Rushworth’s best friend. Your kindness and patience can never be forgotten, your indefatigable patience in trying to make it possible for him to learn his part—in trying to give him a brain which nature had denied—to mix up an understanding for him out of the superfluity of your own! He might not have sense enough himself to estimate your kindness, but I may venture to say that it had honour from all the rest of the party.”\n\nFanny coloured, and said nothing.\n\n“It is as a dream, a pleasant dream!” he exclaimed, breaking forth again, after a few minutes’ musing. “I shall always look back on our theatricals with exquisite pleasure. There was such an interest, such an animation, such a spirit diffused. Everybody felt it. We were all alive. There was employment, hope, solicitude, bustle, for every hour of the day. Always some little objection, some little doubt, some little anxiety to be got over. I never was happier.”\n\nWith silent indignation Fanny repeated to herself, “Never happier!—never happier than when doing what you must know was not justifiable!—never happier than when behaving so dishonourably and unfeelingly! Oh! what a corrupted mind!”\n\n“We were unlucky, Miss Price,” he continued, in a lower tone, to avoid the possibility of being heard by Edmund, and not at all aware of her feelings, “we certainly were very unlucky. Another week, only one other week, would have been enough for us. I think if we had had the disposal of events—if Mansfield Park had had the government of the winds just for a week or two, about the equinox, there would have been a difference. Not that we would have endangered his safety by any tremendous weather—but only by a steady contrary wind, or a calm. I think, Miss Price, we would have indulged ourselves with a week’s calm in the Atlantic at that season.”\n\nHe seemed determined to be answered; and Fanny, averting her face, said, with a firmer tone than usual, “As far as I am concerned, sir, I would not have delayed his return for a day. My uncle disapproved it all so entirely when he did arrive, that in my opinion everything had gone quite far enough.”\n\nShe had never spoken so much at once to him in her life before, and never so angrily to any one; and when her speech was over, she trembled and blushed at her own daring. He was surprised; but after a few moments’ silent consideration of her, replied in a calmer, graver tone, and as if the candid result of conviction, “I believe you are right. It was more pleasant than prudent. We were getting too noisy.” And then turning the conversation, he would have engaged her on some other subject, but her answers were so shy and reluctant that he could not advance in any.\n\nMiss Crawford, who had been repeatedly eyeing Dr. Grant and Edmund, now observed, “Those gentlemen must have some very interesting point to discuss.”\n\n“The most interesting in the world,” replied her brother—“how to make money; how to turn a good income into a better. Dr. Grant is giving Bertram instructions about the living he is to step into so soon. I find he takes orders in a few weeks. They were at it in the dining-parlour. I am glad to hear Bertram will be so well off. He will have a very pretty income to make ducks and drakes with, and earned without much trouble. I apprehend he will not have less than seven hundred a year. Seven hundred a year is a fine thing for a younger brother; and as of course he will still live at home, it will be all for his menus plaisirs; and a sermon at Christmas and Easter, I suppose, will be the sum total of sacrifice.”\n\nHis sister tried to laugh off her feelings by saying, “Nothing amuses me more than the easy manner with which everybody settles the abundance of those who have a great deal less than themselves. You would look rather blank, Henry, if your menus plaisirs were to be limited to seven hundred a year.”\n\n“Perhaps I might; but all that you know is entirely comparative. Birthright and habit must settle the business. Bertram is certainly well off for a cadet of even a baronet’s family. By the time he is four or five and twenty he will have seven hundred a year, and nothing to do for it.”\n\nMiss Crawford could have said that there would be a something to do and to suffer for it, which she could not think lightly of; but she checked herself and let it pass; and tried to look calm and unconcerned when the two gentlemen shortly afterwards joined them.\n\n“Bertram,” said Henry Crawford, “I shall make a point of coming to Mansfield to hear you preach your first sermon. I shall come on purpose to encourage a young beginner. When is it to be? Miss Price, will not you join me in encouraging your cousin? Will not you engage to attend with your eyes steadily fixed on him the whole time—as I shall do—not to lose a word; or only looking off just to note down any sentence preeminently beautiful? We will provide ourselves with tablets and a pencil. When will it be? You must preach at Mansfield, you know, that Sir Thomas and Lady Bertram may hear you.”\n\n“I shall keep clear of you, Crawford, as long as I can,” said Edmund; “for you would be more likely to disconcert me, and I should be more sorry to see you trying at it than almost any other man.”\n\n“Will he not feel this?” thought Fanny. “No, he can feel nothing as he ought.”\n\nThe party being now all united, and the chief talkers attracting each other, she remained in tranquillity; and as a whist-table was formed after tea—formed really for the amusement of Dr. Grant, by his attentive wife, though it was not to be supposed so—and Miss Crawford took her harp, she had nothing to do but to listen; and her tranquillity remained undisturbed the rest of the evening, except when Mr. Crawford now and then addressed to her a question or observation, which she could not avoid answering. Miss Crawford was too much vexed by what had passed to be in a humour for anything but music. With that she soothed herself and amused her friend.\n\nThe assurance of Edmund’s being so soon to take orders, coming upon her like a blow that had been suspended, and still hoped uncertain and at a distance, was felt with resentment and mortification. She was very angry with him. She had thought her influence more. She had begun to think of him; she felt that she had, with great regard, with almost decided intentions; but she would now meet him with his own cool feelings. It was plain that he could have no serious views, no true attachment, by fixing himself in a situation which he must know she would never stoop to. She would learn to match him in his indifference. She would henceforth admit his attentions without any idea beyond immediate amusement. If he could so command his affections, hers should do her no harm.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIV\n\nHenry Crawford had quite made up his mind by the next morning to give another fortnight to Mansfield, and having sent for his hunters, and written a few lines of explanation to the Admiral, he looked round at his sister as he sealed and threw the letter from him, and seeing the coast clear of the rest of the family, said, with a smile, “And how do you think I mean to amuse myself, Mary, on the days that I do not hunt? I am grown too old to go out more than three times a week; but I have a plan for the intermediate days, and what do you think it is?”\n\n“To walk and ride with me, to be sure.”\n\n“Not exactly, though I shall be happy to do both, but that would be exercise only to my body, and I must take care of my mind. Besides, that would be all recreation and indulgence, without the wholesome alloy of labour, and I do not like to eat the bread of idleness. No, my plan is to make Fanny Price in love with me.”\n\n“Fanny Price! Nonsense! No, no. You ought to be satisfied with her two cousins.”\n\n“But I cannot be satisfied without Fanny Price, without making a small hole in Fanny Price’s heart. You do not seem properly aware of her claims to notice. When we talked of her last night, you none of you seemed sensible of the wonderful improvement that has taken place in her looks within the last six weeks. You see her every day, and therefore do not notice it; but I assure you she is quite a different creature from what she was in the autumn. She was then merely a quiet, modest, not plain-looking girl, but she is now absolutely pretty. I used to think she had neither complexion nor countenance; but in that soft skin of hers, so frequently tinged with a blush as it was yesterday, there is decided beauty; and from what I observed of her eyes and mouth, I do not despair of their being capable of expression enough when she has anything to express. And then, her air, her manner, her tout ensemble, is so indescribably improved! She must be grown two inches, at least, since October.”\n\n“Phoo! phoo! This is only because there were no tall women to compare her with, and because she has got a new gown, and you never saw her so well dressed before. She is just what she was in October, believe me. The truth is, that she was the only girl in company for you to notice, and you must have a somebody. I have always thought her pretty—not strikingly pretty—but ‘pretty enough,’ as people say; a sort of beauty that grows on one. Her eyes should be darker, but she has a sweet smile; but as for this wonderful degree of improvement, I am sure it may all be resolved into a better style of dress, and your having nobody else to look at; and therefore, if you do set about a flirtation with her, you never will persuade me that it is in compliment to her beauty, or that it proceeds from anything but your own idleness and folly.”\n\nHer brother gave only a smile to this accusation, and soon afterwards said, “I do not quite know what to make of Miss Fanny. I do not understand her. I could not tell what she would be at yesterday. What is her character? Is she solemn? Is she queer? Is she prudish? Why did she draw back and look so grave at me? I could hardly get her to speak. I never was so long in company with a girl in my life, trying to entertain her, and succeed so ill! Never met with a girl who looked so grave on me! I must try to get the better of this. Her looks say, ‘I will not like you, I am determined not to like you’; and I say she shall.”\n\n“Foolish fellow! And so this is her attraction after all! This it is, her not caring about you, which gives her such a soft skin, and makes her so much taller, and produces all these charms and graces! I do desire that you will not be making her really unhappy; a little love, perhaps, may animate and do her good, but I will not have you plunge her deep, for she is as good a little creature as ever lived, and has a great deal of feeling.”\n\n“It can be but for a fortnight,” said Henry; “and if a fortnight can kill her, she must have a constitution which nothing could save. No, I will not do her any harm, dear little soul! I only want her to look kindly on me, to give me smiles as well as blushes, to keep a chair for me by herself wherever we are, and be all animation when I take it and talk to her; to think as I think, be interested in all my possessions and pleasures, try to keep me longer at Mansfield, and feel when I go away that she shall be never happy again. I want nothing more.”\n\n“Moderation itself!” said Mary. “I can have no scruples now. Well, you will have opportunities enough of endeavouring to recommend yourself, for we are a great deal together.”\n\nAnd without attempting any farther remonstrance, she left Fanny to her fate, a fate which, had not Fanny’s heart been guarded in a way unsuspected by Miss Crawford, might have been a little harder than she deserved; for although there doubtless are such unconquerable young ladies of eighteen (or one should not read about them) as are never to be persuaded into love against their judgment by all that talent, manner, attention, and flattery can do, I have no inclination to believe Fanny one of them, or to think that with so much tenderness of disposition, and so much taste as belonged to her, she could have escaped heart-whole from the courtship (though the courtship only of a fortnight) of such a man as Crawford, in spite of there being some previous ill opinion of him to be overcome, had not her affection been engaged elsewhere. With all the security which love of another and disesteem of him could give to the peace of mind he was attacking, his continued attentions—continued, but not obtrusive, and adapting themselves more and more to the gentleness and delicacy of her character—obliged her very soon to dislike him less than formerly. She had by no means forgotten the past, and she thought as ill of him as ever; but she felt his powers: he was entertaining; and his manners were so improved, so polite, so seriously and blamelessly polite, that it was impossible not to be civil to him in return.\n\nA very few days were enough to effect this; and at the end of those few days, circumstances arose which had a tendency rather to forward his views of pleasing her, inasmuch as they gave her a degree of happiness which must dispose her to be pleased with everybody. William, her brother, the so long absent and dearly loved brother, was in England again. She had a letter from him herself, a few hurried happy lines, written as the ship came up Channel, and sent into Portsmouth with the first boat that left the Antwerp at anchor in Spithead; and when Crawford walked up with the newspaper in his hand, which he had hoped would bring the first tidings, he found her trembling with joy over this letter, and listening with a glowing, grateful countenance to the kind invitation which her uncle was most collectedly dictating in reply.\n\nIt was but the day before that Crawford had made himself thoroughly master of the subject, or had in fact become at all aware of her having such a brother, or his being in such a ship, but the interest then excited had been very properly lively, determining him on his return to town to apply for information as to the probable period of the Antwerp’s return from the Mediterranean, etc.; and the good luck which attended his early examination of ship news the next morning seemed the reward of his ingenuity in finding out such a method of pleasing her, as well as of his dutiful attention to the Admiral, in having for many years taken in the paper esteemed to have the earliest naval intelligence. He proved, however, to be too late. All those fine first feelings, of which he had hoped to be the exciter, were already given. But his intention, the kindness of his intention, was thankfully acknowledged: quite thankfully and warmly, for she was elevated beyond the common timidity of her mind by the flow of her love for William.\n\nThis dear William would soon be amongst them. There could be no doubt of his obtaining leave of absence immediately, for he was still only a midshipman; and as his parents, from living on the spot, must already have seen him, and be seeing him perhaps daily, his direct holidays might with justice be instantly given to the sister, who had been his best correspondent through a period of seven years, and the uncle who had done most for his support and advancement; and accordingly the reply to her reply, fixing a very early day for his arrival, came as soon as possible; and scarcely ten days had passed since Fanny had been in the agitation of her first dinner-visit, when she found herself in an agitation of a higher nature, watching in the hall, in the lobby, on the stairs, for the first sound of the carriage which was to bring her a brother.\n\nIt came happily while she was thus waiting; and there being neither ceremony nor fearfulness to delay the moment of meeting, she was with him as he entered the house, and the first minutes of exquisite feeling had no interruption and no witnesses, unless the servants chiefly intent upon opening the proper doors could be called such. This was exactly what Sir Thomas and Edmund had been separately conniving at, as each proved to the other by the sympathetic alacrity with which they both advised Mrs. Norris’s continuing where she was, instead of rushing out into the hall as soon as the noises of the arrival reached them.\n\nWilliam and Fanny soon shewed themselves; and Sir Thomas had the pleasure of receiving, in his protégé, certainly a very different person from the one he had equipped seven years ago, but a young man of an open, pleasant countenance, and frank, unstudied, but feeling and respectful manners, and such as confirmed him his friend.\n\nIt was long before Fanny could recover from the agitating happiness of such an hour as was formed by the last thirty minutes of expectation, and the first of fruition; it was some time even before her happiness could be said to make her happy, before the disappointment inseparable from the alteration of person had vanished, and she could see in him the same William as before, and talk to him, as her heart had been yearning to do through many a past year. That time, however, did gradually come, forwarded by an affection on his side as warm as her own, and much less encumbered by refinement or self-distrust. She was the first object of his love, but it was a love which his stronger spirits, and bolder temper, made it as natural for him to express as to feel. On the morrow they were walking about together with true enjoyment, and every succeeding morrow renewed a tête-à-tête which Sir Thomas could not but observe with complacency, even before Edmund had pointed it out to him.\n\nExcepting the moments of peculiar delight, which any marked or unlooked-for instance of Edmund’s consideration of her in the last few months had excited, Fanny had never known so much felicity in her life, as in this unchecked, equal, fearless intercourse with the brother and friend who was opening all his heart to her, telling her all his hopes and fears, plans, and solicitudes respecting that long thought of, dearly earned, and justly valued blessing of promotion; who could give her direct and minute information of the father and mother, brothers and sisters, of whom she very seldom heard; who was interested in all the comforts and all the little hardships of her home at Mansfield; ready to think of every member of that home as she directed, or differing only by a less scrupulous opinion, and more noisy abuse of their aunt Norris, and with whom (perhaps the dearest indulgence of the whole) all the evil and good of their earliest years could be gone over again, and every former united pain and pleasure retraced with the fondest recollection. An advantage this, a strengthener of love, in which even the conjugal tie is beneath the fraternal. Children of the same family, the same blood, with the same first associations and habits, have some means of enjoyment in their power, which no subsequent connexions can supply; and it must be by a long and unnatural estrangement, by a divorce which no subsequent connexion can justify, if such precious remains of the earliest attachments are ever entirely outlived. Too often, alas! it is so. Fraternal love, sometimes almost everything, is at others worse than nothing. But with William and Fanny Price it was still a sentiment in all its prime and freshness, wounded by no opposition of interest, cooled by no separate attachment, and feeling the influence of time and absence only in its increase.\n\nAn affection so amiable was advancing each in the opinion of all who had hearts to value anything good. Henry Crawford was as much struck with it as any. He honoured the warm-hearted, blunt fondness of the young sailor, which led him to say, with his hands stretched towards Fanny’s head, “Do you know, I begin to like that queer fashion already, though when I first heard of such things being done in England, I could not believe it; and when Mrs. Brown, and the other women at the Commissioner’s at Gibraltar, appeared in the same trim, I thought they were mad; but Fanny can reconcile me to anything”; and saw, with lively admiration, the glow of Fanny’s cheek, the brightness of her eye, the deep interest, the absorbed attention, while her brother was describing any of the imminent hazards, or terrific scenes, which such a period at sea must supply.\n\nIt was a picture which Henry Crawford had moral taste enough to value. Fanny’s attractions increased—increased twofold; for the sensibility which beautified her complexion and illumined her countenance was an attraction in itself. He was no longer in doubt of the capabilities of her heart. She had feeling, genuine feeling. It would be something to be loved by such a girl, to excite the first ardours of her young unsophisticated mind! She interested him more than he had foreseen. A fortnight was not enough. His stay became indefinite.\n\nWilliam was often called on by his uncle to be the talker. His recitals were amusing in themselves to Sir Thomas, but the chief object in seeking them was to understand the reciter, to know the young man by his histories; and he listened to his clear, simple, spirited details with full satisfaction, seeing in them the proof of good principles, professional knowledge, energy, courage, and cheerfulness, everything that could deserve or promise well. Young as he was, William had already seen a great deal. He had been in the Mediterranean; in the West Indies; in the Mediterranean again; had been often taken on shore by the favour of his captain, and in the course of seven years had known every variety of danger which sea and war together could offer. With such means in his power he had a right to be listened to; and though Mrs. Norris could fidget about the room, and disturb everybody in quest of two needlefuls of thread or a second-hand shirt button, in the midst of her nephew’s account of a shipwreck or an engagement, everybody else was attentive; and even Lady Bertram could not hear of such horrors unmoved, or without sometimes lifting her eyes from her work to say, “Dear me! how disagreeable! I wonder anybody can ever go to sea.”\n\nTo Henry Crawford they gave a different feeling. He longed to have been at sea, and seen and done and suffered as much. His heart was warmed, his fancy fired, and he felt the highest respect for a lad who, before he was twenty, had gone through such bodily hardships and given such proofs of mind. The glory of heroism, of usefulness, of exertion, of endurance, made his own habits of selfish indulgence appear in shameful contrast; and he wished he had been a William Price, distinguishing himself and working his way to fortune and consequence with so much self-respect and happy ardour, instead of what he was!\n\nThe wish was rather eager than lasting. He was roused from the reverie of retrospection and regret produced by it, by some inquiry from Edmund as to his plans for the next day’s hunting; and he found it was as well to be a man of fortune at once with horses and grooms at his command. In one respect it was better, as it gave him the means of conferring a kindness where he wished to oblige. With spirits, courage, and curiosity up to anything, William expressed an inclination to hunt; and Crawford could mount him without the slightest inconvenience to himself, and with only some scruples to obviate in Sir Thomas, who knew better than his nephew the value of such a loan, and some alarms to reason away in Fanny. She feared for William; by no means convinced by all that he could relate of his own horsemanship in various countries, of the scrambling parties in which he had been engaged, the rough horses and mules he had ridden, or his many narrow escapes from dreadful falls, that he was at all equal to the management of a high-fed hunter in an English fox-chase; nor till he returned safe and well, without accident or discredit, could she be reconciled to the risk, or feel any of that obligation to Mr. Crawford for lending the horse which he had fully intended it should produce. When it was proved, however, to have done William no harm, she could allow it to be a kindness, and even reward the owner with a smile when the animal was one minute tendered to his use again; and the next, with the greatest cordiality, and in a manner not to be resisted, made over to his use entirely so long as he remained in Northamptonshire.\n\n# CHAPTER XXV\n\nThe intercourse of the two families was at this period more nearly restored to what it had been in the autumn, than any member of the old intimacy had thought ever likely to be again. The return of Henry Crawford, and the arrival of William Price, had much to do with it, but much was still owing to Sir Thomas’s more than toleration of the neighbourly attempts at the Parsonage. His mind, now disengaged from the cares which had pressed on him at first, was at leisure to find the Grants and their young inmates really worth visiting; and though infinitely above scheming or contriving for any the most advantageous matrimonial establishment that could be among the apparent possibilities of any one most dear to him, and disdaining even as a littleness the being quick-sighted on such points, he could not avoid perceiving, in a grand and careless way, that Mr. Crawford was somewhat distinguishing his niece—nor perhaps refrain (though unconsciously) from giving a more willing assent to invitations on that account.\n\nHis readiness, however, in agreeing to dine at the Parsonage, when the general invitation was at last hazarded, after many debates and many doubts as to whether it were worth while, “because Sir Thomas seemed so ill inclined, and Lady Bertram was so indolent!” proceeded from good-breeding and goodwill alone, and had nothing to do with Mr. Crawford, but as being one in an agreeable group: for it was in the course of that very visit that he first began to think that any one in the habit of such idle observations would have thought that Mr. Crawford was the admirer of Fanny Price.\n\nThe meeting was generally felt to be a pleasant one, being composed in a good proportion of those who would talk and those who would listen; and the dinner itself was elegant and plentiful, according to the usual style of the Grants, and too much according to the usual habits of all to raise any emotion except in Mrs. Norris, who could never behold either the wide table or the number of dishes on it with patience, and who did always contrive to experience some evil from the passing of the servants behind her chair, and to bring away some fresh conviction of its being impossible among so many dishes but that some must be cold.\n\nIn the evening it was found, according to the predetermination of Mrs. Grant and her sister, that after making up the whist-table there would remain sufficient for a round game, and everybody being as perfectly complying and without a choice as on such occasions they always are, speculation was decided on almost as soon as whist; and Lady Bertram soon found herself in the critical situation of being applied to for her own choice between the games, and being required either to draw a card for whist or not. She hesitated. Luckily Sir Thomas was at hand.\n\n“What shall I do, Sir Thomas? Whist and speculation; which will amuse me most?”\n\nSir Thomas, after a moment’s thought, recommended speculation. He was a whist player himself, and perhaps might feel that it would not much amuse him to have her for a partner.\n\n“Very well,” was her ladyship’s contented answer; “then speculation, if you please, Mrs. Grant. I know nothing about it, but Fanny must teach me.”\n\nHere Fanny interposed, however, with anxious protestations of her own equal ignorance; she had never played the game nor seen it played in her life; and Lady Bertram felt a moment’s indecision again; but upon everybody’s assuring her that nothing could be so easy, that it was the easiest game on the cards, and Henry Crawford’s stepping forward with a most earnest request to be allowed to sit between her ladyship and Miss Price, and teach them both, it was so settled; and Sir Thomas, Mrs. Norris, and Dr. and Mrs. Grant being seated at the table of prime intellectual state and dignity, the remaining six, under Miss Crawford’s direction, were arranged round the other. It was a fine arrangement for Henry Crawford, who was close to Fanny, and with his hands full of business, having two persons’ cards to manage as well as his own; for though it was impossible for Fanny not to feel herself mistress of the rules of the game in three minutes, he had yet to inspirit her play, sharpen her avarice, and harden her heart, which, especially in any competition with William, was a work of some difficulty; and as for Lady Bertram, he must continue in charge of all her fame and fortune through the whole evening; and if quick enough to keep her from looking at her cards when the deal began, must direct her in whatever was to be done with them to the end of it.\n\nHe was in high spirits, doing everything with happy ease, and preeminent in all the lively turns, quick resources, and playful impudence that could do honour to the game; and the round table was altogether a very comfortable contrast to the steady sobriety and orderly silence of the other.\n\nTwice had Sir Thomas inquired into the enjoyment and success of his lady, but in vain; no pause was long enough for the time his measured manner needed; and very little of her state could be known till Mrs. Grant was able, at the end of the first rubber, to go to her and pay her compliments.\n\n“I hope your ladyship is pleased with the game.”\n\n“Oh dear, yes! very entertaining indeed. A very odd game. I do not know what it is all about. I am never to see my cards; and Mr. Crawford does all the rest.”\n\n“Bertram,” said Crawford, some time afterwards, taking the opportunity of a little languor in the game, “I have never told you what happened to me yesterday in my ride home.” They had been hunting together, and were in the midst of a good run, and at some distance from Mansfield, when his horse being found to have flung a shoe, Henry Crawford had been obliged to give up, and make the best of his way back. “I told you I lost my way after passing that old farmhouse with the yew-trees, because I can never bear to ask; but I have not told you that, with my usual luck—for I never do wrong without gaining by it—I found myself in due time in the very place which I had a curiosity to see. I was suddenly, upon turning the corner of a steepish downy field, in the midst of a retired little village between gently rising hills; a small stream before me to be forded, a church standing on a sort of knoll to my right—which church was strikingly large and handsome for the place, and not a gentleman or half a gentleman’s house to be seen excepting one—to be presumed the Parsonage—within a stone’s throw of the said knoll and church. I found myself, in short, in Thornton Lacey.”\n\n“It sounds like it,” said Edmund; “but which way did you turn after passing Sewell’s farm?”\n\n“I answer no such irrelevant and insidious questions; though were I to answer all that you could put in the course of an hour, you would never be able to prove that it was not Thornton Lacey—for such it certainly was.”\n\n“You inquired, then?”\n\n“No, I never inquire. But I told a man mending a hedge that it was Thornton Lacey, and he agreed to it.”\n\n“You have a good memory. I had forgotten having ever told you half so much of the place.”\n\nThornton Lacey was the name of his impending living, as Miss Crawford well knew; and her interest in a negotiation for William Price’s knave increased.\n\n“Well,” continued Edmund, “and how did you like what you saw?”\n\n“Very much indeed. You are a lucky fellow. There will be work for five summers at least before the place is liveable.”\n\n“No, no, not so bad as that. The farmyard must be moved, I grant you; but I am not aware of anything else. The house is by no means bad, and when the yard is removed, there may be a very tolerable approach to it.”\n\n“The farmyard must be cleared away entirely, and planted up to shut out the blacksmith’s shop. The house must be turned to front the east instead of the north—the entrance and principal rooms, I mean, must be on that side, where the view is really very pretty; I am sure it may be done. And there must be your approach, through what is at present the garden. You must make a new garden at what is now the back of the house; which will be giving it the best aspect in the world, sloping to the south-east. The ground seems precisely formed for it. I rode fifty yards up the lane, between the church and the house, in order to look about me; and saw how it might all be. Nothing can be easier. The meadows beyond what will be the garden, as well as what now is, sweeping round from the lane I stood in to the north-east, that is, to the principal road through the village, must be all laid together, of course; very pretty meadows they are, finely sprinkled with timber. They belong to the living, I suppose; if not, you must purchase them. Then the stream—something must be done with the stream; but I could not quite determine what. I had two or three ideas.”\n\n“And I have two or three ideas also,” said Edmund, “and one of them is, that very little of your plan for Thornton Lacey will ever be put in practice. I must be satisfied with rather less ornament and beauty. I think the house and premises may be made comfortable, and given the air of a gentleman’s residence, without any very heavy expense, and that must suffice me; and, I hope, may suffice all who care about me.”\n\nMiss Crawford, a little suspicious and resentful of a certain tone of voice, and a certain half-look attending the last expression of his hope, made a hasty finish of her dealings with William Price; and securing his knave at an exorbitant rate, exclaimed, “There, I will stake my last like a woman of spirit. No cold prudence for me. I am not born to sit still and do nothing. If I lose the game, it shall not be from not striving for it.”\n\nThe game was hers, and only did not pay her for what she had given to secure it. Another deal proceeded, and Crawford began again about Thornton Lacey.\n\n“My plan may not be the best possible: I had not many minutes to form it in; but you must do a good deal. The place deserves it, and you will find yourself not satisfied with much less than it is capable of. (Excuse me, your ladyship must not see your cards. There, let them lie just before you.) The place deserves it, Bertram. You talk of giving it the air of a gentleman’s residence. That will be done by the removal of the farmyard; for, independent of that terrible nuisance, I never saw a house of the kind which had in itself so much the air of a gentleman’s residence, so much the look of a something above a mere parsonage-house—above the expenditure of a few hundreds a year. It is not a scrambling collection of low single rooms, with as many roofs as windows; it is not cramped into the vulgar compactness of a square farmhouse: it is a solid, roomy, mansion-like looking house, such as one might suppose a respectable old country family had lived in from generation to generation, through two centuries at least, and were now spending from two to three thousand a year in.” Miss Crawford listened, and Edmund agreed to this. “The air of a gentleman’s residence, therefore, you cannot but give it, if you do anything. But it is capable of much more. (Let me see, Mary; Lady Bertram bids a dozen for that queen; no, no, a dozen is more than it is worth. Lady Bertram does not bid a dozen. She will have nothing to say to it. Go on, go on.) By some such improvements as I have suggested (I do not really require you to proceed upon my plan, though, by the bye, I doubt anybody’s striking out a better) you may give it a higher character. You may raise it into a place. From being the mere gentleman’s residence, it becomes, by judicious improvement, the residence of a man of education, taste, modern manners, good connexions. All this may be stamped on it; and that house receive such an air as to make its owner be set down as the great landholder of the parish by every creature travelling the road; especially as there is no real squire’s house to dispute the point—a circumstance, between ourselves, to enhance the value of such a situation in point of privilege and independence beyond all calculation. You think with me, I hope” (turning with a softened voice to Fanny). “Have you ever seen the place?”\n\nFanny gave a quick negative, and tried to hide her interest in the subject by an eager attention to her brother, who was driving as hard a bargain, and imposing on her as much as he could; but Crawford pursued with “No, no, you must not part with the queen. You have bought her too dearly, and your brother does not offer half her value. No, no, sir, hands off, hands off. Your sister does not part with the queen. She is quite determined. The game will be yours,” turning to her again; “it will certainly be yours.”\n\n“And Fanny had much rather it were William’s,” said Edmund, smiling at her. “Poor Fanny! not allowed to cheat herself as she wishes!”\n\n“Mr. Bertram,” said Miss Crawford, a few minutes afterwards, “you know Henry to be such a capital improver, that you cannot possibly engage in anything of the sort at Thornton Lacey without accepting his help. Only think how useful he was at Sotherton! Only think what grand things were produced there by our all going with him one hot day in August to drive about the grounds, and see his genius take fire. There we went, and there we came home again; and what was done there is not to be told!”\n\nFanny’s eyes were turned on Crawford for a moment with an expression more than grave—even reproachful; but on catching his, were instantly withdrawn. With something of consciousness he shook his head at his sister, and laughingly replied, “I cannot say there was much done at Sotherton; but it was a hot day, and we were all walking after each other, and bewildered.” As soon as a general buzz gave him shelter, he added, in a low voice, directed solely at Fanny, “I should be sorry to have my powers of planning judged of by the day at Sotherton. I see things very differently now. Do not think of me as I appeared then.”\n\nSotherton was a word to catch Mrs. Norris, and being just then in the happy leisure which followed securing the odd trick by Sir Thomas’s capital play and her own against Dr. and Mrs. Grant’s great hands, she called out, in high good-humour, “Sotherton! Yes, that is a place, indeed, and we had a charming day there. William, you are quite out of luck; but the next time you come, I hope dear Mr. and Mrs. Rushworth will be at home, and I am sure I can answer for your being kindly received by both. Your cousins are not of a sort to forget their relations, and Mr. Rushworth is a most amiable man. They are at Brighton now, you know; in one of the best houses there, as Mr. Rushworth’s fine fortune gives them a right to be. I do not exactly know the distance, but when you get back to Portsmouth, if it is not very far off, you ought to go over and pay your respects to them; and I could send a little parcel by you that I want to get conveyed to your cousins.”\n\n“I should be very happy, aunt; but Brighton is almost by Beachey Head; and if I could get so far, I could not expect to be welcome in such a smart place as that—poor scrubby midshipman as I am.”\n\nMrs. Norris was beginning an eager assurance of the affability he might depend on, when she was stopped by Sir Thomas’s saying with authority, “I do not advise your going to Brighton, William, as I trust you may soon have more convenient opportunities of meeting; but my daughters would be happy to see their cousins anywhere; and you will find Mr. Rushworth most sincerely disposed to regard all the connexions of our family as his own.”\n\n“I would rather find him private secretary to the First Lord than anything else,” was William’s only answer, in an undervoice, not meant to reach far, and the subject dropped.\n\nAs yet Sir Thomas had seen nothing to remark in Mr. Crawford’s behaviour; but when the whist-table broke up at the end of the second rubber, and leaving Dr. Grant and Mrs. Norris to dispute over their last play, he became a looker-on at the other, he found his niece the object of attentions, or rather of professions, of a somewhat pointed character.\n\nHenry Crawford was in the first glow of another scheme about Thornton Lacey; and not being able to catch Edmund’s ear, was detailing it to his fair neighbour with a look of considerable earnestness. His scheme was to rent the house himself the following winter, that he might have a home of his own in that neighbourhood; and it was not merely for the use of it in the hunting-season (as he was then telling her), though that consideration had certainly some weight, feeling as he did that, in spite of all Dr. Grant’s very great kindness, it was impossible for him and his horses to be accommodated where they now were without material inconvenience; but his attachment to that neighbourhood did not depend upon one amusement or one season of the year: he had set his heart upon having a something there that he could come to at any time, a little homestall at his command, where all the holidays of his year might be spent, and he might find himself continuing, improving, and perfecting that friendship and intimacy with the Mansfield Park family which was increasing in value to him every day. Sir Thomas heard and was not offended. There was no want of respect in the young man’s address; and Fanny’s reception of it was so proper and modest, so calm and uninviting, that he had nothing to censure in her. She said little, assented only here and there, and betrayed no inclination either of appropriating any part of the compliment to herself, or of strengthening his views in favour of Northamptonshire. Finding by whom he was observed, Henry Crawford addressed himself on the same subject to Sir Thomas, in a more everyday tone, but still with feeling.\n\n“I want to be your neighbour, Sir Thomas, as you have, perhaps, heard me telling Miss Price. May I hope for your acquiescence, and for your not influencing your son against such a tenant?”\n\nSir Thomas, politely bowing, replied, “It is the only way, sir, in which I could not wish you established as a permanent neighbour; but I hope, and believe, that Edmund will occupy his own house at Thornton Lacey. Edmund, am I saying too much?”\n\nEdmund, on this appeal, had first to hear what was going on; but, on understanding the question, was at no loss for an answer.\n\n“Certainly, sir, I have no idea but of residence. But, Crawford, though I refuse you as a tenant, come to me as a friend. Consider the house as half your own every winter, and we will add to the stables on your own improved plan, and with all the improvements of your improved plan that may occur to you this spring.”\n\n“We shall be the losers,” continued Sir Thomas. “His going, though only eight miles, will be an unwelcome contraction of our family circle; but I should have been deeply mortified if any son of mine could reconcile himself to doing less. It is perfectly natural that you should not have thought much on the subject, Mr. Crawford. But a parish has wants and claims which can be known only by a clergyman constantly resident, and which no proxy can be capable of satisfying to the same extent. Edmund might, in the common phrase, do the duty of Thornton, that is, he might read prayers and preach, without giving up Mansfield Park: he might ride over every Sunday, to a house nominally inhabited, and go through divine service; he might be the clergyman of Thornton Lacey every seventh day, for three or four hours, if that would content him. But it will not. He knows that human nature needs more lessons than a weekly sermon can convey; and that if he does not live among his parishioners, and prove himself, by constant attention, their well-wisher and friend, he does very little either for their good or his own.”\n\nMr. Crawford bowed his acquiescence.\n\n“I repeat again,” added Sir Thomas, “that Thornton Lacey is the only house in the neighbourhood in which I should not be happy to wait on Mr. Crawford as occupier.”\n\nMr. Crawford bowed his thanks.\n\n“Sir Thomas,” said Edmund, “undoubtedly understands the duty of a parish priest. We must hope his son may prove that he knows it too.”\n\nWhatever effect Sir Thomas’s little harangue might really produce on Mr. Crawford, it raised some awkward sensations in two of the others, two of his most attentive listeners—Miss Crawford and Fanny. One of whom, having never before understood that Thornton was so soon and so completely to be his home, was pondering with downcast eyes on what it would be not to see Edmund every day; and the other, startled from the agreeable fancies she had been previously indulging on the strength of her brother’s description, no longer able, in the picture she had been forming of a future Thornton, to shut out the church, sink the clergyman, and see only the respectable, elegant, modernised, and occasional residence of a man of independent fortune, was considering Sir Thomas, with decided ill-will, as the destroyer of all this, and suffering the more from that involuntary forbearance which his character and manner commanded, and from not daring to relieve herself by a single attempt at throwing ridicule on his cause.\n\nAll the agreeable of her speculation was over for that hour. It was time to have done with cards, if sermons prevailed; and she was glad to find it necessary to come to a conclusion, and be able to refresh her spirits by a change of place and neighbour.\n\nThe chief of the party were now collected irregularly round the fire, and waiting the final break-up. William and Fanny were the most detached. They remained together at the otherwise deserted card-table, talking very comfortably, and not thinking of the rest, till some of the rest began to think of them. Henry Crawford’s chair was the first to be given a direction towards them, and he sat silently observing them for a few minutes; himself, in the meanwhile, observed by Sir Thomas, who was standing in chat with Dr. Grant.\n\n“This is the assembly night,” said William. “If I were at Portsmouth I should be at it, perhaps.”\n\n“But you do not wish yourself at Portsmouth, William?”\n\n“No, Fanny, that I do not. I shall have enough of Portsmouth and of dancing too, when I cannot have you. And I do not know that there would be any good in going to the assembly, for I might not get a partner. The Portsmouth girls turn up their noses at anybody who has not a commission. One might as well be nothing as a midshipman. One is nothing, indeed. You remember the Gregorys; they are grown up amazing fine girls, but they will hardly speak to me, because Lucy is courted by a lieutenant.”\n\n“Oh! shame, shame! But never mind it, William” (her own cheeks in a glow of indignation as she spoke). “It is not worth minding. It is no reflection on you; it is no more than what the greatest admirals have all experienced, more or less, in their time. You must think of that, you must try to make up your mind to it as one of the hardships which fall to every sailor’s share, like bad weather and hard living, only with this advantage, that there will be an end to it, that there will come a time when you will have nothing of that sort to endure. When you are a lieutenant! only think, William, when you are a lieutenant, how little you will care for any nonsense of this kind.”\n\n“I begin to think I shall never be a lieutenant, Fanny. Everybody gets made but me.”\n\n“Oh! my dear William, do not talk so; do not be so desponding. My uncle says nothing, but I am sure he will do everything in his power to get you made. He knows, as well as you do, of what consequence it is.”\n\nShe was checked by the sight of her uncle much nearer to them than she had any suspicion of, and each found it necessary to talk of something else.\n\n“Are you fond of dancing, Fanny?”\n\n“Yes, very; only I am soon tired.”\n\n“I should like to go to a ball with you and see you dance. Have you never any balls at Northampton? I should like to see you dance, and I’d dance with you if you would, for nobody would know who I was here, and I should like to be your partner once more. We used to jump about together many a time, did not we? when the hand-organ was in the street? I am a pretty good dancer in my way, but I dare say you are a better.” And turning to his uncle, who was now close to them, “Is not Fanny a very good dancer, sir?”\n\nFanny, in dismay at such an unprecedented question, did not know which way to look, or how to be prepared for the answer. Some very grave reproof, or at least the coldest expression of indifference, must be coming to distress her brother, and sink her to the ground. But, on the contrary, it was no worse than, “I am sorry to say that I am unable to answer your question. I have never seen Fanny dance since she was a little girl; but I trust we shall both think she acquits herself like a gentlewoman when we do see her, which, perhaps, we may have an opportunity of doing ere long.”\n\n“I have had the pleasure of seeing your sister dance, Mr. Price,” said Henry Crawford, leaning forward, “and will engage to answer every inquiry which you can make on the subject, to your entire satisfaction. But I believe” (seeing Fanny looked distressed) “it must be at some other time. There is one person in company who does not like to have Miss Price spoken of.”\n\nTrue enough, he had once seen Fanny dance; and it was equally true that he would now have answered for her gliding about with quiet, light elegance, and in admirable time; but, in fact, he could not for the life of him recall what her dancing had been, and rather took it for granted that she had been present than remembered anything about her.\n\nHe passed, however, for an admirer of her dancing; and Sir Thomas, by no means displeased, prolonged the conversation on dancing in general, and was so well engaged in describing the balls of Antigua, and listening to what his nephew could relate of the different modes of dancing which had fallen within his observation, that he had not heard his carriage announced, and was first called to the knowledge of it by the bustle of Mrs. Norris.\n\n“Come, Fanny, Fanny, what are you about? We are going. Do not you see your aunt is going? Quick, quick! I cannot bear to keep good old Wilcox waiting. You should always remember the coachman and horses. My dear Sir Thomas, we have settled it that the carriage should come back for you, and Edmund and William.”\n\nSir Thomas could not dissent, as it had been his own arrangement, previously communicated to his wife and sister; but that seemed forgotten by Mrs. Norris, who must fancy that she settled it all herself.\n\nFanny’s last feeling in the visit was disappointment: for the shawl which Edmund was quietly taking from the servant to bring and put round her shoulders was seized by Mr. Crawford’s quicker hand, and she was obliged to be indebted to his more prominent attention.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVI\n\nWilliam’s desire of seeing Fanny dance made more than a momentary impression on his uncle. The hope of an opportunity, which Sir Thomas had then given, was not given to be thought of no more. He remained steadily inclined to gratify so amiable a feeling; to gratify anybody else who might wish to see Fanny dance, and to give pleasure to the young people in general; and having thought the matter over, and taken his resolution in quiet independence, the result of it appeared the next morning at breakfast, when, after recalling and commending what his nephew had said, he added, “I do not like, William, that you should leave Northamptonshire without this indulgence. It would give me pleasure to see you both dance. You spoke of the balls at Northampton. Your cousins have occasionally attended them; but they would not altogether suit us now. The fatigue would be too much for your aunt. I believe we must not think of a Northampton ball. A dance at home would be more eligible; and if—”\n\n“Ah, my dear Sir Thomas!” interrupted Mrs. Norris, “I knew what was coming. I knew what you were going to say. If dear Julia were at home, or dearest Mrs. Rushworth at Sotherton, to afford a reason, an occasion for such a thing, you would be tempted to give the young people a dance at Mansfield. I know you would. If they were at home to grace the ball, a ball you would have this very Christmas. Thank your uncle, William, thank your uncle!”\n\n“My daughters,” replied Sir Thomas, gravely interposing, “have their pleasures at Brighton, and I hope are very happy; but the dance which I think of giving at Mansfield will be for their cousins. Could we be all assembled, our satisfaction would undoubtedly be more complete, but the absence of some is not to debar the others of amusement.”\n\nMrs. Norris had not another word to say. She saw decision in his looks, and her surprise and vexation required some minutes’ silence to be settled into composure. A ball at such a time! His daughters absent and herself not consulted! There was comfort, however, soon at hand. She must be the doer of everything: Lady Bertram would of course be spared all thought and exertion, and it would all fall upon her. She should have to do the honours of the evening; and this reflection quickly restored so much of her good-humour as enabled her to join in with the others, before their happiness and thanks were all expressed.\n\nEdmund, William, and Fanny did, in their different ways, look and speak as much grateful pleasure in the promised ball as Sir Thomas could desire. Edmund’s feelings were for the other two. His father had never conferred a favour or shewn a kindness more to his satisfaction.\n\nLady Bertram was perfectly quiescent and contented, and had no objections to make. Sir Thomas engaged for its giving her very little trouble; and she assured him “that she was not at all afraid of the trouble; indeed, she could not imagine there would be any.”\n\nMrs. Norris was ready with her suggestions as to the rooms he would think fittest to be used, but found it all prearranged; and when she would have conjectured and hinted about the day, it appeared that the day was settled too. Sir Thomas had been amusing himself with shaping a very complete outline of the business; and as soon as she would listen quietly, could read his list of the families to be invited, from whom he calculated, with all necessary allowance for the shortness of the notice, to collect young people enough to form twelve or fourteen couple: and could detail the considerations which had induced him to fix on the 22nd as the most eligible day. William was required to be at Portsmouth on the 24th; the 22nd would therefore be the last day of his visit; but where the days were so few it would be unwise to fix on any earlier. Mrs. Norris was obliged to be satisfied with thinking just the same, and with having been on the point of proposing the 22nd herself, as by far the best day for the purpose.\n\nThe ball was now a settled thing, and before the evening a proclaimed thing to all whom it concerned. Invitations were sent with despatch, and many a young lady went to bed that night with her head full of happy cares as well as Fanny. To her the cares were sometimes almost beyond the happiness; for young and inexperienced, with small means of choice and no confidence in her own taste, the “how she should be dressed” was a point of painful solicitude; and the almost solitary ornament in her possession, a very pretty amber cross which William had brought her from Sicily, was the greatest distress of all, for she had nothing but a bit of ribbon to fasten it to; and though she had worn it in that manner once, would it be allowable at such a time in the midst of all the rich ornaments which she supposed all the other young ladies would appear in? And yet not to wear it! William had wanted to buy her a gold chain too, but the purchase had been beyond his means, and therefore not to wear the cross might be mortifying him. These were anxious considerations; enough to sober her spirits even under the prospect of a ball given principally for her gratification.\n\nThe preparations meanwhile went on, and Lady Bertram continued to sit on her sofa without any inconvenience from them. She had some extra visits from the housekeeper, and her maid was rather hurried in making up a new dress for her: Sir Thomas gave orders, and Mrs. Norris ran about; but all this gave her no trouble, and as she had foreseen, “there was, in fact, no trouble in the business.”\n\nEdmund was at this time particularly full of cares: his mind being deeply occupied in the consideration of two important events now at hand, which were to fix his fate in life—ordination and matrimony—events of such a serious character as to make the ball, which would be very quickly followed by one of them, appear of less moment in his eyes than in those of any other person in the house. On the 23rd he was going to a friend near Peterborough, in the same situation as himself, and they were to receive ordination in the course of the Christmas week. Half his destiny would then be determined, but the other half might not be so very smoothly wooed. His duties would be established, but the wife who was to share, and animate, and reward those duties, might yet be unattainable. He knew his own mind, but he was not always perfectly assured of knowing Miss Crawford’s. There were points on which they did not quite agree; there were moments in which she did not seem propitious; and though trusting altogether to her affection, so far as to be resolved—almost resolved—on bringing it to a decision within a very short time, as soon as the variety of business before him were arranged, and he knew what he had to offer her, he had many anxious feelings, many doubting hours as to the result. His conviction of her regard for him was sometimes very strong; he could look back on a long course of encouragement, and she was as perfect in disinterested attachment as in everything else. But at other times doubt and alarm intermingled with his hopes; and when he thought of her acknowledged disinclination for privacy and retirement, her decided preference of a London life, what could he expect but a determined rejection? unless it were an acceptance even more to be deprecated, demanding such sacrifices of situation and employment on his side as conscience must forbid.\n\nThe issue of all depended on one question. Did she love him well enough to forego what had used to be essential points? Did she love him well enough to make them no longer essential? And this question, which he was continually repeating to himself, though oftenest answered with a “Yes,” had sometimes its “No.”\n\nMiss Crawford was soon to leave Mansfield, and on this circumstance the “no” and the “yes” had been very recently in alternation. He had seen her eyes sparkle as she spoke of the dear friend’s letter, which claimed a long visit from her in London, and of the kindness of Henry, in engaging to remain where he was till January, that he might convey her thither; he had heard her speak of the pleasure of such a journey with an animation which had “no” in every tone. But this had occurred on the first day of its being settled, within the first hour of the burst of such enjoyment, when nothing but the friends she was to visit was before her. He had since heard her express herself differently, with other feelings, more chequered feelings: he had heard her tell Mrs. Grant that she should leave her with regret; that she began to believe neither the friends nor the pleasures she was going to were worth those she left behind; and that though she felt she must go, and knew she should enjoy herself when once away, she was already looking forward to being at Mansfield again. Was there not a “yes” in all this?\n\nWith such matters to ponder over, and arrange, and re-arrange, Edmund could not, on his own account, think very much of the evening which the rest of the family were looking forward to with a more equal degree of strong interest. Independent of his two cousins’ enjoyment in it, the evening was to him of no higher value than any other appointed meeting of the two families might be. In every meeting there was a hope of receiving farther confirmation of Miss Crawford’s attachment; but the whirl of a ballroom, perhaps, was not particularly favourable to the excitement or expression of serious feelings. To engage her early for the two first dances was all the command of individual happiness which he felt in his power, and the only preparation for the ball which he could enter into, in spite of all that was passing around him on the subject, from morning till night.\n\nThursday was the day of the ball; and on Wednesday morning Fanny, still unable to satisfy herself as to what she ought to wear, determined to seek the counsel of the more enlightened, and apply to Mrs. Grant and her sister, whose acknowledged taste would certainly bear her blameless; and as Edmund and William were gone to Northampton, and she had reason to think Mr. Crawford likewise out, she walked down to the Parsonage without much fear of wanting an opportunity for private discussion; and the privacy of such a discussion was a most important part of it to Fanny, being more than half-ashamed of her own solicitude.\n\nShe met Miss Crawford within a few yards of the Parsonage, just setting out to call on her, and as it seemed to her that her friend, though obliged to insist on turning back, was unwilling to lose her walk, she explained her business at once, and observed, that if she would be so kind as to give her opinion, it might be all talked over as well without doors as within. Miss Crawford appeared gratified by the application, and after a moment’s thought, urged Fanny’s returning with her in a much more cordial manner than before, and proposed their going up into her room, where they might have a comfortable coze, without disturbing Dr. and Mrs. Grant, who were together in the drawing-room. It was just the plan to suit Fanny; and with a great deal of gratitude on her side for such ready and kind attention, they proceeded indoors, and upstairs, and were soon deep in the interesting subject. Miss Crawford, pleased with the appeal, gave her all her best judgment and taste, made everything easy by her suggestions, and tried to make everything agreeable by her encouragement. The dress being settled in all its grander parts—“But what shall you have by way of necklace?” said Miss Crawford. “Shall not you wear your brother’s cross?” And as she spoke she was undoing a small parcel, which Fanny had observed in her hand when they met. Fanny acknowledged her wishes and doubts on this point: she did not know how either to wear the cross, or to refrain from wearing it. She was answered by having a small trinket-box placed before her, and being requested to chuse from among several gold chains and necklaces. Such had been the parcel with which Miss Crawford was provided, and such the object of her intended visit: and in the kindest manner she now urged Fanny’s taking one for the cross and to keep for her sake, saying everything she could think of to obviate the scruples which were making Fanny start back at first with a look of horror at the proposal.\n\n“You see what a collection I have,” said she; “more by half than I ever use or think of. I do not offer them as new. I offer nothing but an old necklace. You must forgive the liberty, and oblige me.”\n\nFanny still resisted, and from her heart. The gift was too valuable. But Miss Crawford persevered, and argued the case with so much affectionate earnestness through all the heads of William and the cross, and the ball, and herself, as to be finally successful. Fanny found herself obliged to yield, that she might not be accused of pride or indifference, or some other littleness; and having with modest reluctance given her consent, proceeded to make the selection. She looked and looked, longing to know which might be least valuable; and was determined in her choice at last, by fancying there was one necklace more frequently placed before her eyes than the rest. It was of gold, prettily worked; and though Fanny would have preferred a longer and a plainer chain as more adapted for her purpose, she hoped, in fixing on this, to be chusing what Miss Crawford least wished to keep. Miss Crawford smiled her perfect approbation; and hastened to complete the gift by putting the necklace round her, and making her see how well it looked. Fanny had not a word to say against its becomingness, and, excepting what remained of her scruples, was exceedingly pleased with an acquisition so very apropos. She would rather, perhaps, have been obliged to some other person. But this was an unworthy feeling. Miss Crawford had anticipated her wants with a kindness which proved her a real friend. “When I wear this necklace I shall always think of you,” said she, “and feel how very kind you were.”\n\n“You must think of somebody else too, when you wear that necklace,” replied Miss Crawford. “You must think of Henry, for it was his choice in the first place. He gave it to me, and with the necklace I make over to you all the duty of remembering the original giver. It is to be a family remembrancer. The sister is not to be in your mind without bringing the brother too.”\n\nFanny, in great astonishment and confusion, would have returned the present instantly. To take what had been the gift of another person, of a brother too, impossible! it must not be! and with an eagerness and embarrassment quite diverting to her companion, she laid down the necklace again on its cotton, and seemed resolved either to take another or none at all. Miss Crawford thought she had never seen a prettier consciousness. “My dear child,” said she, laughing, “what are you afraid of? Do you think Henry will claim the necklace as mine, and fancy you did not come honestly by it? or are you imagining he would be too much flattered by seeing round your lovely throat an ornament which his money purchased three years ago, before he knew there was such a throat in the world? or perhaps”—looking archly—“you suspect a confederacy between us, and that what I am now doing is with his knowledge and at his desire?”\n\nWith the deepest blushes Fanny protested against such a thought.\n\n“Well, then,” replied Miss Crawford more seriously, but without at all believing her, “to convince me that you suspect no trick, and are as unsuspicious of compliment as I have always found you, take the necklace and say no more about it. Its being a gift of my brother’s need not make the smallest difference in your accepting it, as I assure you it makes none in my willingness to part with it. He is always giving me something or other. I have such innumerable presents from him that it is quite impossible for me to value or for him to remember half. And as for this necklace, I do not suppose I have worn it six times: it is very pretty, but I never think of it; and though you would be most heartily welcome to any other in my trinket-box, you have happened to fix on the very one which, if I have a choice, I would rather part with and see in your possession than any other. Say no more against it, I entreat you. Such a trifle is not worth half so many words.”\n\nFanny dared not make any farther opposition; and with renewed but less happy thanks accepted the necklace again, for there was an expression in Miss Crawford’s eyes which she could not be satisfied with.\n\nIt was impossible for her to be insensible of Mr. Crawford’s change of manners. She had long seen it. He evidently tried to please her: he was gallant, he was attentive, he was something like what he had been to her cousins: he wanted, she supposed, to cheat her of her tranquillity as he had cheated them; and whether he might not have some concern in this necklace—she could not be convinced that he had not, for Miss Crawford, complaisant as a sister, was careless as a woman and a friend.\n\nReflecting and doubting, and feeling that the possession of what she had so much wished for did not bring much satisfaction, she now walked home again, with a change rather than a diminution of cares since her treading that path before.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVII\n\nOn reaching home Fanny went immediately upstairs to deposit this unexpected acquisition, this doubtful good of a necklace, in some favourite box in the East room, which held all her smaller treasures; but on opening the door, what was her surprise to find her cousin Edmund there writing at the table! Such a sight having never occurred before, was almost as wonderful as it was welcome.\n\n“Fanny,” said he directly, leaving his seat and his pen, and meeting her with something in his hand, “I beg your pardon for being here. I came to look for you, and after waiting a little while in hope of your coming in, was making use of your inkstand to explain my errand. You will find the beginning of a note to yourself; but I can now speak my business, which is merely to beg your acceptance of this little trifle—a chain for William’s cross. You ought to have had it a week ago, but there has been a delay from my brother’s not being in town by several days so soon as I expected; and I have only just now received it at Northampton. I hope you will like the chain itself, Fanny. I endeavoured to consult the simplicity of your taste; but, at any rate, I know you will be kind to my intentions, and consider it, as it really is, a token of the love of one of your oldest friends.”\n\nAnd so saying, he was hurrying away, before Fanny, overpowered by a thousand feelings of pain and pleasure, could attempt to speak; but quickened by one sovereign wish, she then called out, “Oh! cousin, stop a moment, pray stop!”\n\nHe turned back.\n\n“I cannot attempt to thank you,” she continued, in a very agitated manner; “thanks are out of the question. I feel much more than I can possibly express. Your goodness in thinking of me in such a way is beyond—”\n\n“If that is all you have to say, Fanny” smiling and turning away again.\n\n“No, no, it is not. I want to consult you.”\n\nAlmost unconsciously she had now undone the parcel he had just put into her hand, and seeing before her, in all the niceness of jewellers’ packing, a plain gold chain, perfectly simple and neat, she could not help bursting forth again, “Oh, this is beautiful indeed! This is the very thing, precisely what I wished for! This is the only ornament I have ever had a desire to possess. It will exactly suit my cross. They must and shall be worn together. It comes, too, in such an acceptable moment. Oh, cousin, you do not know how acceptable it is.”\n\n“My dear Fanny, you feel these things a great deal too much. I am most happy that you like the chain, and that it should be here in time for to-morrow; but your thanks are far beyond the occasion. Believe me, I have no pleasure in the world superior to that of contributing to yours. No, I can safely say, I have no pleasure so complete, so unalloyed. It is without a drawback.”\n\nUpon such expressions of affection Fanny could have lived an hour without saying another word; but Edmund, after waiting a moment, obliged her to bring down her mind from its heavenly flight by saying, “But what is it that you want to consult me about?”\n\nIt was about the necklace, which she was now most earnestly longing to return, and hoped to obtain his approbation of her doing. She gave the history of her recent visit, and now her raptures might well be over; for Edmund was so struck with the circumstance, so delighted with what Miss Crawford had done, so gratified by such a coincidence of conduct between them, that Fanny could not but admit the superior power of one pleasure over his own mind, though it might have its drawback. It was some time before she could get his attention to her plan, or any answer to her demand of his opinion: he was in a reverie of fond reflection, uttering only now and then a few half-sentences of praise; but when he did awake and understand, he was very decided in opposing what she wished.\n\n“Return the necklace! No, my dear Fanny, upon no account. It would be mortifying her severely. There can hardly be a more unpleasant sensation than the having anything returned on our hands which we have given with a reasonable hope of its contributing to the comfort of a friend. Why should she lose a pleasure which she has shewn herself so deserving of?”\n\n“If it had been given to me in the first instance,” said Fanny, “I should not have thought of returning it; but being her brother’s present, is not it fair to suppose that she would rather not part with it, when it is not wanted?”\n\n“She must not suppose it not wanted, not acceptable, at least: and its having been originally her brother’s gift makes no difference; for as she was not prevented from offering, nor you from taking it on that account, it ought not to prevent you from keeping it. No doubt it is handsomer than mine, and fitter for a ballroom.”\n\n“No, it is not handsomer, not at all handsomer in its way, and, for my purpose, not half so fit. The chain will agree with William’s cross beyond all comparison better than the necklace.”\n\n“For one night, Fanny, for only one night, if it be a sacrifice; I am sure you will, upon consideration, make that sacrifice rather than give pain to one who has been so studious of your comfort. Miss Crawford’s attentions to you have been—not more than you were justly entitled to—I am the last person to think that could be, but they have been invariable; and to be returning them with what must have something the air of ingratitude, though I know it could never have the meaning, is not in your nature, I am sure. Wear the necklace, as you are engaged to do, to-morrow evening, and let the chain, which was not ordered with any reference to the ball, be kept for commoner occasions. This is my advice. I would not have the shadow of a coolness between the two whose intimacy I have been observing with the greatest pleasure, and in whose characters there is so much general resemblance in true generosity and natural delicacy as to make the few slight differences, resulting principally from situation, no reasonable hindrance to a perfect friendship. I would not have the shadow of a coolness arise,” he repeated, his voice sinking a little, “between the two dearest objects I have on earth.”\n\nHe was gone as he spoke; and Fanny remained to tranquillise herself as she could. She was one of his two dearest—that must support her. But the other: the first! She had never heard him speak so openly before, and though it told her no more than what she had long perceived, it was a stab, for it told of his own convictions and views. They were decided. He would marry Miss Crawford. It was a stab, in spite of every long-standing expectation; and she was obliged to repeat again and again, that she was one of his two dearest, before the words gave her any sensation. Could she believe Miss Crawford to deserve him, it would be—oh, how different would it be—how far more tolerable! But he was deceived in her: he gave her merits which she had not; her faults were what they had ever been, but he saw them no longer. Till she had shed many tears over this deception, Fanny could not subdue her agitation; and the dejection which followed could only be relieved by the influence of fervent prayers for his happiness.\n\nIt was her intention, as she felt it to be her duty, to try to overcome all that was excessive, all that bordered on selfishness, in her affection for Edmund. To call or to fancy it a loss, a disappointment, would be a presumption for which she had not words strong enough to satisfy her own humility. To think of him as Miss Crawford might be justified in thinking, would in her be insanity. To her he could be nothing under any circumstances; nothing dearer than a friend. Why did such an idea occur to her even enough to be reprobated and forbidden? It ought not to have touched on the confines of her imagination. She would endeavour to be rational, and to deserve the right of judging of Miss Crawford’s character, and the privilege of true solicitude for him by a sound intellect and an honest heart.\n\nShe had all the heroism of principle, and was determined to do her duty; but having also many of the feelings of youth and nature, let her not be much wondered at, if, after making all these good resolutions on the side of self-government, she seized the scrap of paper on which Edmund had begun writing to her, as a treasure beyond all her hopes, and reading with the tenderest emotion these words, “My very dear Fanny, you must do me the favour to accept” locked it up with the chain, as the dearest part of the gift. It was the only thing approaching to a letter which she had ever received from him; she might never receive another; it was impossible that she ever should receive another so perfectly gratifying in the occasion and the style. Two lines more prized had never fallen from the pen of the most distinguished author—never more completely blessed the researches of the fondest biographer. The enthusiasm of a woman’s love is even beyond the biographer’s. To her, the handwriting itself, independent of anything it may convey, is a blessedness. Never were such characters cut by any other human being as Edmund’s commonest handwriting gave! This specimen, written in haste as it was, had not a fault; and there was a felicity in the flow of the first four words, in the arrangement of “My very dear Fanny,” which she could have looked at for ever.\n\nHaving regulated her thoughts and comforted her feelings by this happy mixture of reason and weakness, she was able in due time to go down and resume her usual employments near her aunt Bertram, and pay her the usual observances without any apparent want of spirits.\n\nThursday, predestined to hope and enjoyment, came; and opened with more kindness to Fanny than such self-willed, unmanageable days often volunteer, for soon after breakfast a very friendly note was brought from Mr. Crawford to William, stating that as he found himself obliged to go to London on the morrow for a few days, he could not help trying to procure a companion; and therefore hoped that if William could make up his mind to leave Mansfield half a day earlier than had been proposed, he would accept a place in his carriage. Mr. Crawford meant to be in town by his uncle’s accustomary late dinner-hour, and William was invited to dine with him at the Admiral’s. The proposal was a very pleasant one to William himself, who enjoyed the idea of travelling post with four horses, and such a good-humoured, agreeable friend; and, in likening it to going up with despatches, was saying at once everything in favour of its happiness and dignity which his imagination could suggest; and Fanny, from a different motive, was exceedingly pleased; for the original plan was that William should go up by the mail from Northampton the following night, which would not have allowed him an hour’s rest before he must have got into a Portsmouth coach; and though this offer of Mr. Crawford’s would rob her of many hours of his company, she was too happy in having William spared from the fatigue of such a journey, to think of anything else. Sir Thomas approved of it for another reason. His nephew’s introduction to Admiral Crawford might be of service. The Admiral, he believed, had interest. Upon the whole, it was a very joyous note. Fanny’s spirits lived on it half the morning, deriving some accession of pleasure from its writer being himself to go away.\n\nAs for the ball, so near at hand, she had too many agitations and fears to have half the enjoyment in anticipation which she ought to have had, or must have been supposed to have by the many young ladies looking forward to the same event in situations more at ease, but under circumstances of less novelty, less interest, less peculiar gratification, than would be attributed to her. Miss Price, known only by name to half the people invited, was now to make her first appearance, and must be regarded as the queen of the evening. Who could be happier than Miss Price? But Miss Price had not been brought up to the trade of coming out; and had she known in what light this ball was, in general, considered respecting her, it would very much have lessened her comfort by increasing the fears she already had of doing wrong and being looked at. To dance without much observation or any extraordinary fatigue, to have strength and partners for about half the evening, to dance a little with Edmund, and not a great deal with Mr. Crawford, to see William enjoy himself, and be able to keep away from her aunt Norris, was the height of her ambition, and seemed to comprehend her greatest possibility of happiness. As these were the best of her hopes, they could not always prevail; and in the course of a long morning, spent principally with her two aunts, she was often under the influence of much less sanguine views. William, determined to make this last day a day of thorough enjoyment, was out snipe-shooting; Edmund, she had too much reason to suppose, was at the Parsonage; and left alone to bear the worrying of Mrs. Norris, who was cross because the housekeeper would have her own way with the supper, and whom she could not avoid though the housekeeper might, Fanny was worn down at last to think everything an evil belonging to the ball, and when sent off with a parting worry to dress, moved as languidly towards her own room, and felt as incapable of happiness as if she had been allowed no share in it.\n\nAs she walked slowly upstairs she thought of yesterday; it had been about the same hour that she had returned from the Parsonage, and found Edmund in the East room. “Suppose I were to find him there again to-day!” said she to herself, in a fond indulgence of fancy.\n\n“Fanny,” said a voice at that moment near her. Starting and looking up, she saw, across the lobby she had just reached, Edmund himself, standing at the head of a different staircase. He came towards her. “You look tired and fagged, Fanny. You have been walking too far.”\n\n“No, I have not been out at all.”\n\n“Then you have had fatigues within doors, which are worse. You had better have gone out.”\n\nFanny, not liking to complain, found it easiest to make no answer; and though he looked at her with his usual kindness, she believed he had soon ceased to think of her countenance. He did not appear in spirits: something unconnected with her was probably amiss. They proceeded upstairs together, their rooms being on the same floor above.\n\n“I come from Dr. Grant’s,” said Edmund presently. “You may guess my errand there, Fanny.” And he looked so conscious, that Fanny could think but of one errand, which turned her too sick for speech. “I wished to engage Miss Crawford for the two first dances,” was the explanation that followed, and brought Fanny to life again, enabling her, as she found she was expected to speak, to utter something like an inquiry as to the result.\n\n“Yes,” he answered, “she is engaged to me; but” (with a smile that did not sit easy) “she says it is to be the last time that she ever will dance with me. She is not serious. I think, I hope, I am sure she is not serious; but I would rather not hear it. She never has danced with a clergyman, she says, and she never will. For my own sake, I could wish there had been no ball just at—I mean not this very week, this very day; to-morrow I leave home.”\n\nFanny struggled for speech, and said, “I am very sorry that anything has occurred to distress you. This ought to be a day of pleasure. My uncle meant it so.”\n\n“Oh yes, yes! and it will be a day of pleasure. It will all end right. I am only vexed for a moment. In fact, it is not that I consider the ball as ill-timed; what does it signify? But, Fanny,” stopping her, by taking her hand, and speaking low and seriously, “you know what all this means. You see how it is; and could tell me, perhaps better than I could tell you, how and why I am vexed. Let me talk to you a little. You are a kind, kind listener. I have been pained by her manner this morning, and cannot get the better of it. I know her disposition to be as sweet and faultless as your own, but the influence of her former companions makes her seem—gives to her conversation, to her professed opinions, sometimes a tinge of wrong. She does not think evil, but she speaks it, speaks it in playfulness; and though I know it to be playfulness, it grieves me to the soul.”\n\n“The effect of education,” said Fanny gently.\n\nEdmund could not but agree to it. “Yes, that uncle and aunt! They have injured the finest mind; for sometimes, Fanny, I own to you, it does appear more than manner: it appears as if the mind itself was tainted.”\n\nFanny imagined this to be an appeal to her judgment, and therefore, after a moment’s consideration, said, “If you only want me as a listener, cousin, I will be as useful as I can; but I am not qualified for an adviser. Do not ask advice of me. I am not competent.”\n\n“You are right, Fanny, to protest against such an office, but you need not be afraid. It is a subject on which I should never ask advice; it is the sort of subject on which it had better never be asked; and few, I imagine, do ask it, but when they want to be influenced against their conscience. I only want to talk to you.”\n\n“One thing more. Excuse the liberty; but take care how you talk to me. Do not tell me anything now, which hereafter you may be sorry for. The time may come—”\n\nThe colour rushed into her cheeks as she spoke.\n\n“Dearest Fanny!” cried Edmund, pressing her hand to his lips with almost as much warmth as if it had been Miss Crawford’s, “you are all considerate thought! But it is unnecessary here. The time will never come. No such time as you allude to will ever come. I begin to think it most improbable: the chances grow less and less; and even if it should, there will be nothing to be remembered by either you or me that we need be afraid of, for I can never be ashamed of my own scruples; and if they are removed, it must be by changes that will only raise her character the more by the recollection of the faults she once had. You are the only being upon earth to whom I should say what I have said; but you have always known my opinion of her; you can bear me witness, Fanny, that I have never been blinded. How many a time have we talked over her little errors! You need not fear me; I have almost given up every serious idea of her; but I must be a blockhead indeed, if, whatever befell me, I could think of your kindness and sympathy without the sincerest gratitude.”\n\nHe had said enough to shake the experience of eighteen. He had said enough to give Fanny some happier feelings than she had lately known, and with a brighter look, she answered, “Yes, cousin, I am convinced that you would be incapable of anything else, though perhaps some might not. I cannot be afraid of hearing anything you wish to say. Do not check yourself. Tell me whatever you like.”\n\nThey were now on the second floor, and the appearance of a housemaid prevented any farther conversation. For Fanny’s present comfort it was concluded, perhaps, at the happiest moment: had he been able to talk another five minutes, there is no saying that he might not have talked away all Miss Crawford’s faults and his own despondence. But as it was, they parted with looks on his side of grateful affection, and with some very precious sensations on hers. She had felt nothing like it for hours. Since the first joy from Mr. Crawford’s note to William had worn away, she had been in a state absolutely the reverse; there had been no comfort around, no hope within her. Now everything was smiling. William’s good fortune returned again upon her mind, and seemed of greater value than at first. The ball, too—such an evening of pleasure before her! It was now a real animation; and she began to dress for it with much of the happy flutter which belongs to a ball. All went well: she did not dislike her own looks; and when she came to the necklaces again, her good fortune seemed complete, for upon trial the one given her by Miss Crawford would by no means go through the ring of the cross. She had, to oblige Edmund, resolved to wear it; but it was too large for the purpose. His, therefore, must be worn; and having, with delightful feelings, joined the chain and the cross—those memorials of the two most beloved of her heart, those dearest tokens so formed for each other by everything real and imaginary—and put them round her neck, and seen and felt how full of William and Edmund they were, she was able, without an effort, to resolve on wearing Miss Crawford’s necklace too. She acknowledged it to be right. Miss Crawford had a claim; and when it was no longer to encroach on, to interfere with the stronger claims, the truer kindness of another, she could do her justice even with pleasure to herself. The necklace really looked very well; and Fanny left her room at last, comfortably satisfied with herself and all about her.\n\nHer aunt Bertram had recollected her on this occasion with an unusual degree of wakefulness. It had really occurred to her, unprompted, that Fanny, preparing for a ball, might be glad of better help than the upper housemaid’s, and when dressed herself, she actually sent her own maid to assist her; too late, of course, to be of any use. Mrs. Chapman had just reached the attic floor, when Miss Price came out of her room completely dressed, and only civilities were necessary; but Fanny felt her aunt’s attention almost as much as Lady Bertram or Mrs. Chapman could do themselves.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVIII\n\nHer uncle and both her aunts were in the drawing-room when Fanny went down. To the former she was an interesting object, and he saw with pleasure the general elegance of her appearance, and her being in remarkably good looks. The neatness and propriety of her dress was all that he would allow himself to commend in her presence, but upon her leaving the room again soon afterwards, he spoke of her beauty with very decided praise.\n\n“Yes,” said Lady Bertram, “she looks very well. I sent Chapman to her.”\n\n“Look well! Oh, yes!” cried Mrs. Norris, “she has good reason to look well with all her advantages: brought up in this family as she has been, with all the benefit of her cousins’ manners before her. Only think, my dear Sir Thomas, what extraordinary advantages you and I have been the means of giving her. The very gown you have been taking notice of is your own generous present to her when dear Mrs. Rushworth married. What would she have been if we had not taken her by the hand?”\n\nSir Thomas said no more; but when they sat down to table the eyes of the two young men assured him that the subject might be gently touched again, when the ladies withdrew, with more success. Fanny saw that she was approved; and the consciousness of looking well made her look still better. From a variety of causes she was happy, and she was soon made still happier; for in following her aunts out of the room, Edmund, who was holding open the door, said, as she passed him, “You must dance with me, Fanny; you must keep two dances for me; any two that you like, except the first.” She had nothing more to wish for. She had hardly ever been in a state so nearly approaching high spirits in her life. Her cousins’ former gaiety on the day of a ball was no longer surprising to her; she felt it to be indeed very charming, and was actually practising her steps about the drawing-room as long as she could be safe from the notice of her aunt Norris, who was entirely taken up at first in fresh arranging and injuring the noble fire which the butler had prepared.\n\nHalf an hour followed that would have been at least languid under any other circumstances, but Fanny’s happiness still prevailed. It was but to think of her conversation with Edmund, and what was the restlessness of Mrs. Norris? What were the yawns of Lady Bertram?\n\nThe gentlemen joined them; and soon after began the sweet expectation of a carriage, when a general spirit of ease and enjoyment seemed diffused, and they all stood about and talked and laughed, and every moment had its pleasure and its hope. Fanny felt that there must be a struggle in Edmund’s cheerfulness, but it was delightful to see the effort so successfully made.\n\nWhen the carriages were really heard, when the guests began really to assemble, her own gaiety of heart was much subdued: the sight of so many strangers threw her back into herself; and besides the gravity and formality of the first great circle, which the manners of neither Sir Thomas nor Lady Bertram were of a kind to do away, she found herself occasionally called on to endure something worse. She was introduced here and there by her uncle, and forced to be spoken to, and to curtsey, and speak again. This was a hard duty, and she was never summoned to it without looking at William, as he walked about at his ease in the background of the scene, and longing to be with him.\n\nThe entrance of the Grants and Crawfords was a favourable epoch. The stiffness of the meeting soon gave way before their popular manners and more diffused intimacies: little groups were formed, and everybody grew comfortable. Fanny felt the advantage; and, drawing back from the toils of civility, would have been again most happy, could she have kept her eyes from wandering between Edmund and Mary Crawford. She looked all loveliness—and what might not be the end of it? Her own musings were brought to an end on perceiving Mr. Crawford before her, and her thoughts were put into another channel by his engaging her almost instantly for the first two dances. Her happiness on this occasion was very much à la mortal, finely chequered. To be secure of a partner at first was a most essential good—for the moment of beginning was now growing seriously near; and she so little understood her own claims as to think that if Mr. Crawford had not asked her, she must have been the last to be sought after, and should have received a partner only through a series of inquiry, and bustle, and interference, which would have been terrible; but at the same time there was a pointedness in his manner of asking her which she did not like, and she saw his eye glancing for a moment at her necklace, with a smile—she thought there was a smile—which made her blush and feel wretched. And though there was no second glance to disturb her, though his object seemed then to be only quietly agreeable, she could not get the better of her embarrassment, heightened as it was by the idea of his perceiving it, and had no composure till he turned away to some one else. Then she could gradually rise up to the genuine satisfaction of having a partner, a voluntary partner, secured against the dancing began.\n\nWhen the company were moving into the ballroom, she found herself for the first time near Miss Crawford, whose eyes and smiles were immediately and more unequivocally directed as her brother’s had been, and who was beginning to speak on the subject, when Fanny, anxious to get the story over, hastened to give the explanation of the second necklace: the real chain. Miss Crawford listened; and all her intended compliments and insinuations to Fanny were forgotten: she felt only one thing; and her eyes, bright as they had been before, shewing they could yet be brighter, she exclaimed with eager pleasure, “Did he? Did Edmund? That was like himself. No other man would have thought of it. I honour him beyond expression.” And she looked around as if longing to tell him so. He was not near, he was attending a party of ladies out of the room; and Mrs. Grant coming up to the two girls, and taking an arm of each, they followed with the rest.\n\nFanny’s heart sunk, but there was no leisure for thinking long even of Miss Crawford’s feelings. They were in the ballroom, the violins were playing, and her mind was in a flutter that forbade its fixing on anything serious. She must watch the general arrangements, and see how everything was done.\n\nIn a few minutes Sir Thomas came to her, and asked if she were engaged; and the “Yes, sir; to Mr. Crawford,” was exactly what he had intended to hear. Mr. Crawford was not far off; Sir Thomas brought him to her, saying something which discovered to Fanny, that she was to lead the way and open the ball; an idea that had never occurred to her before. Whenever she had thought of the minutiae of the evening, it had been as a matter of course that Edmund would begin with Miss Crawford; and the impression was so strong, that though her uncle spoke the contrary, she could not help an exclamation of surprise, a hint of her unfitness, an entreaty even to be excused. To be urging her opinion against Sir Thomas’s was a proof of the extremity of the case; but such was her horror at the first suggestion, that she could actually look him in the face and say that she hoped it might be settled otherwise; in vain, however: Sir Thomas smiled, tried to encourage her, and then looked too serious, and said too decidedly, “It must be so, my dear,” for her to hazard another word; and she found herself the next moment conducted by Mr. Crawford to the top of the room, and standing there to be joined by the rest of the dancers, couple after couple, as they were formed.\n\nShe could hardly believe it. To be placed above so many elegant young women! The distinction was too great. It was treating her like her cousins! And her thoughts flew to those absent cousins with most unfeigned and truly tender regret, that they were not at home to take their own place in the room, and have their share of a pleasure which would have been so very delightful to them. So often as she had heard them wish for a ball at home as the greatest of all felicities! And to have them away when it was given—and for her to be opening the ball—and with Mr. Crawford too! She hoped they would not envy her that distinction now; but when she looked back to the state of things in the autumn, to what they had all been to each other when once dancing in that house before, the present arrangement was almost more than she could understand herself.\n\nThe ball began. It was rather honour than happiness to Fanny, for the first dance at least: her partner was in excellent spirits, and tried to impart them to her; but she was a great deal too much frightened to have any enjoyment till she could suppose herself no longer looked at. Young, pretty, and gentle, however, she had no awkwardnesses that were not as good as graces, and there were few persons present that were not disposed to praise her. She was attractive, she was modest, she was Sir Thomas’s niece, and she was soon said to be admired by Mr. Crawford. It was enough to give her general favour. Sir Thomas himself was watching her progress down the dance with much complacency; he was proud of his niece; and without attributing all her personal beauty, as Mrs. Norris seemed to do, to her transplantation to Mansfield, he was pleased with himself for having supplied everything else: education and manners she owed to him.\n\nMiss Crawford saw much of Sir Thomas’s thoughts as he stood, and having, in spite of all his wrongs towards her, a general prevailing desire of recommending herself to him, took an opportunity of stepping aside to say something agreeable of Fanny. Her praise was warm, and he received it as she could wish, joining in it as far as discretion, and politeness, and slowness of speech would allow, and certainly appearing to greater advantage on the subject than his lady did soon afterwards, when Mary, perceiving her on a sofa very near, turned round before she began to dance, to compliment her on Miss Price’s looks.\n\n“Yes, she does look very well,” was Lady Bertram’s placid reply. “Chapman helped her to dress. I sent Chapman to her.” Not but that she was really pleased to have Fanny admired; but she was so much more struck with her own kindness in sending Chapman to her, that she could not get it out of her head.\n\nMiss Crawford knew Mrs. Norris too well to think of gratifying her by commendation of Fanny; to her, it was as the occasion offered—“Ah! ma’am, how much we want dear Mrs. Rushworth and Julia to-night!” and Mrs. Norris paid her with as many smiles and courteous words as she had time for, amid so much occupation as she found for herself in making up card-tables, giving hints to Sir Thomas, and trying to move all the chaperons to a better part of the room.\n\nMiss Crawford blundered most towards Fanny herself in her intentions to please. She meant to be giving her little heart a happy flutter, and filling her with sensations of delightful self-consequence; and, misinterpreting Fanny’s blushes, still thought she must be doing so when she went to her after the two first dances, and said, with a significant look, “Perhaps you can tell me why my brother goes to town to-morrow? He says he has business there, but will not tell me what. The first time he ever denied me his confidence! But this is what we all come to. All are supplanted sooner or later. Now, I must apply to you for information. Pray, what is Henry going for?”\n\nFanny protested her ignorance as steadily as her embarrassment allowed.\n\n“Well, then,” replied Miss Crawford, laughing, “I must suppose it to be purely for the pleasure of conveying your brother, and of talking of you by the way.”\n\nFanny was confused, but it was the confusion of discontent; while Miss Crawford wondered she did not smile, and thought her over-anxious, or thought her odd, or thought her anything rather than insensible of pleasure in Henry’s attentions. Fanny had a good deal of enjoyment in the course of the evening; but Henry’s attentions had very little to do with it. She would much rather not have been asked by him again so very soon, and she wished she had not been obliged to suspect that his previous inquiries of Mrs. Norris, about the supper hour, were all for the sake of securing her at that part of the evening. But it was not to be avoided: he made her feel that she was the object of all; though she could not say that it was unpleasantly done, that there was indelicacy or ostentation in his manner; and sometimes, when he talked of William, he was really not unagreeable, and shewed even a warmth of heart which did him credit. But still his attentions made no part of her satisfaction. She was happy whenever she looked at William, and saw how perfectly he was enjoying himself, in every five minutes that she could walk about with him and hear his account of his partners; she was happy in knowing herself admired; and she was happy in having the two dances with Edmund still to look forward to, during the greatest part of the evening, her hand being so eagerly sought after that her indefinite engagement with him was in continual perspective. She was happy even when they did take place; but not from any flow of spirits on his side, or any such expressions of tender gallantry as had blessed the morning. His mind was fagged, and her happiness sprung from being the friend with whom it could find repose. “I am worn out with civility,” said he. “I have been talking incessantly all night, and with nothing to say. But with you, Fanny, there may be peace. You will not want to be talked to. Let us have the luxury of silence.” Fanny would hardly even speak her agreement. A weariness, arising probably, in great measure, from the same feelings which he had acknowledged in the morning, was peculiarly to be respected, and they went down their two dances together with such sober tranquillity as might satisfy any looker-on that Sir Thomas had been bringing up no wife for his younger son.\n\nThe evening had afforded Edmund little pleasure. Miss Crawford had been in gay spirits when they first danced together, but it was not her gaiety that could do him good: it rather sank than raised his comfort; and afterwards, for he found himself still impelled to seek her again, she had absolutely pained him by her manner of speaking of the profession to which he was now on the point of belonging. They had talked, and they had been silent; he had reasoned, she had ridiculed; and they had parted at last with mutual vexation. Fanny, not able to refrain entirely from observing them, had seen enough to be tolerably satisfied. It was barbarous to be happy when Edmund was suffering. Yet some happiness must and would arise from the very conviction that he did suffer.\n\nWhen her two dances with him were over, her inclination and strength for more were pretty well at an end; and Sir Thomas, having seen her walk rather than dance down the shortening set, breathless, and with her hand at her side, gave his orders for her sitting down entirely. From that time Mr. Crawford sat down likewise.\n\n“Poor Fanny!” cried William, coming for a moment to visit her, and working away his partner’s fan as if for life, “how soon she is knocked up! Why, the sport is but just begun. I hope we shall keep it up these two hours. How can you be tired so soon?”\n\n“So soon! my good friend,” said Sir Thomas, producing his watch with all necessary caution; “it is three o’clock, and your sister is not used to these sort of hours.”\n\n“Well, then, Fanny, you shall not get up to-morrow before I go. Sleep as long as you can, and never mind me.”\n\n“Oh! William.”\n\n“What! Did she think of being up before you set off?”\n\n“Oh! yes, sir,” cried Fanny, rising eagerly from her seat to be nearer her uncle; “I must get up and breakfast with him. It will be the last time, you know; the last morning.”\n\n“You had better not. He is to have breakfasted and be gone by half-past nine. Mr. Crawford, I think you call for him at half-past nine?”\n\nFanny was too urgent, however, and had too many tears in her eyes for denial; and it ended in a gracious “Well, well!” which was permission.\n\n“Yes, half-past nine,” said Crawford to William as the latter was leaving them, “and I shall be punctual, for there will be no kind sister to get up for me.” And in a lower tone to Fanny, “I shall have only a desolate house to hurry from. Your brother will find my ideas of time and his own very different to-morrow.”\n\nAfter a short consideration, Sir Thomas asked Crawford to join the early breakfast party in that house instead of eating alone: he should himself be of it; and the readiness with which his invitation was accepted convinced him that the suspicions whence, he must confess to himself, this very ball had in great measure sprung, were well founded. Mr. Crawford was in love with Fanny. He had a pleasing anticipation of what would be. His niece, meanwhile, did not thank him for what he had just done. She had hoped to have William all to herself the last morning. It would have been an unspeakable indulgence. But though her wishes were overthrown, there was no spirit of murmuring within her. On the contrary, she was so totally unused to have her pleasure consulted, or to have anything take place at all in the way she could desire, that she was more disposed to wonder and rejoice in having carried her point so far, than to repine at the counteraction which followed.\n\nShortly afterward, Sir Thomas was again interfering a little with her inclination, by advising her to go immediately to bed. “Advise” was his word, but it was the advice of absolute power, and she had only to rise, and, with Mr. Crawford’s very cordial adieus, pass quietly away; stopping at the entrance-door, like the Lady of Branxholm Hall, “one moment and no more,” to view the happy scene, and take a last look at the five or six determined couple who were still hard at work; and then, creeping slowly up the principal staircase, pursued by the ceaseless country-dance, feverish with hopes and fears, soup and negus, sore-footed and fatigued, restless and agitated, yet feeling, in spite of everything, that a ball was indeed delightful.\n\nIn thus sending her away, Sir Thomas perhaps might not be thinking merely of her health. It might occur to him that Mr. Crawford had been sitting by her long enough, or he might mean to recommend her as a wife by shewing her persuadableness.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIX\n\nThe ball was over, and the breakfast was soon over too; the last kiss was given, and William was gone. Mr. Crawford had, as he foretold, been very punctual, and short and pleasant had been the meal.\n\nAfter seeing William to the last moment, Fanny walked back to the breakfast-room with a very saddened heart to grieve over the melancholy change; and there her uncle kindly left her to cry in peace, conceiving, perhaps, that the deserted chair of each young man might exercise her tender enthusiasm, and that the remaining cold pork bones and mustard in William’s plate might but divide her feelings with the broken egg-shells in Mr. Crawford’s. She sat and cried con amore as her uncle intended, but it was con amore fraternal and no other. William was gone, and she now felt as if she had wasted half his visit in idle cares and selfish solicitudes unconnected with him.\n\nFanny’s disposition was such that she could never even think of her aunt Norris in the meagreness and cheerlessness of her own small house, without reproaching herself for some little want of attention to her when they had been last together; much less could her feelings acquit her of having done and said and thought everything by William that was due to him for a whole fortnight.\n\nIt was a heavy, melancholy day. Soon after the second breakfast, Edmund bade them good-bye for a week, and mounted his horse for Peterborough, and then all were gone. Nothing remained of last night but remembrances, which she had nobody to share in. She talked to her aunt Bertram—she must talk to somebody of the ball; but her aunt had seen so little of what had passed, and had so little curiosity, that it was heavy work. Lady Bertram was not certain of anybody’s dress or anybody’s place at supper but her own. “She could not recollect what it was that she had heard about one of the Miss Maddoxes, or what it was that Lady Prescott had noticed in Fanny: she was not sure whether Colonel Harrison had been talking of Mr. Crawford or of William when he said he was the finest young man in the room—somebody had whispered something to her; she had forgot to ask Sir Thomas what it could be.” And these were her longest speeches and clearest communications: the rest was only a languid “Yes, yes; very well; did you? did he? I did not see that; I should not know one from the other.” This was very bad. It was only better than Mrs. Norris’s sharp answers would have been; but she being gone home with all the supernumerary jellies to nurse a sick maid, there was peace and good-humour in their little party, though it could not boast much beside.\n\nThe evening was heavy like the day. “I cannot think what is the matter with me,” said Lady Bertram, when the tea-things were removed. “I feel quite stupid. It must be sitting up so late last night. Fanny, you must do something to keep me awake. I cannot work. Fetch the cards; I feel so very stupid.”\n\nThe cards were brought, and Fanny played at cribbage with her aunt till bedtime; and as Sir Thomas was reading to himself, no sounds were heard in the room for the next two hours beyond the reckonings of the game—“And that makes thirty-one; four in hand and eight in crib. You are to deal, ma’am; shall I deal for you?” Fanny thought and thought again of the difference which twenty-four hours had made in that room, and all that part of the house. Last night it had been hope and smiles, bustle and motion, noise and brilliancy, in the drawing-room, and out of the drawing-room, and everywhere. Now it was languor, and all but solitude.\n\nA good night’s rest improved her spirits. She could think of William the next day more cheerfully; and as the morning afforded her an opportunity of talking over Thursday night with Mrs. Grant and Miss Crawford, in a very handsome style, with all the heightenings of imagination, and all the laughs of playfulness which are so essential to the shade of a departed ball, she could afterwards bring her mind without much effort into its everyday state, and easily conform to the tranquillity of the present quiet week.\n\nThey were indeed a smaller party than she had ever known there for a whole day together, and he was gone on whom the comfort and cheerfulness of every family meeting and every meal chiefly depended. But this must be learned to be endured. He would soon be always gone; and she was thankful that she could now sit in the same room with her uncle, hear his voice, receive his questions, and even answer them, without such wretched feelings as she had formerly known.\n\n“We miss our two young men,” was Sir Thomas’s observation on both the first and second day, as they formed their very reduced circle after dinner; and in consideration of Fanny’s swimming eyes, nothing more was said on the first day than to drink their good health; but on the second it led to something farther. William was kindly commended and his promotion hoped for. “And there is no reason to suppose,” added Sir Thomas, “but that his visits to us may now be tolerably frequent. As to Edmund, we must learn to do without him. This will be the last winter of his belonging to us, as he has done.”\n\n“Yes,” said Lady Bertram, “but I wish he was not going away. They are all going away, I think. I wish they would stay at home.”\n\nThis wish was levelled principally at Julia, who had just applied for permission to go to town with Maria; and as Sir Thomas thought it best for each daughter that the permission should be granted, Lady Bertram, though in her own good-nature she would not have prevented it, was lamenting the change it made in the prospect of Julia’s return, which would otherwise have taken place about this time. A great deal of good sense followed on Sir Thomas’s side, tending to reconcile his wife to the arrangement. Everything that a considerate parent ought to feel was advanced for her use; and everything that an affectionate mother must feel in promoting her children’s enjoyment was attributed to her nature. Lady Bertram agreed to it all with a calm “Yes”; and at the end of a quarter of an hour’s silent consideration spontaneously observed, “Sir Thomas, I have been thinking—and I am very glad we took Fanny as we did, for now the others are away we feel the good of it.”\n\nSir Thomas immediately improved this compliment by adding, “Very true. We shew Fanny what a good girl we think her by praising her to her face, she is now a very valuable companion. If we have been kind to her, she is now quite as necessary to us.”\n\n“Yes,” said Lady Bertram presently; “and it is a comfort to think that we shall always have her.”\n\nSir Thomas paused, half smiled, glanced at his niece, and then gravely replied, “She will never leave us, I hope, till invited to some other home that may reasonably promise her greater happiness than she knows here.”\n\n“And that is not very likely to be, Sir Thomas. Who should invite her? Maria might be very glad to see her at Sotherton now and then, but she would not think of asking her to live there; and I am sure she is better off here; and besides, I cannot do without her.”\n\nThe week which passed so quietly and peaceably at the great house in Mansfield had a very different character at the Parsonage. To the young lady, at least, in each family, it brought very different feelings. What was tranquillity and comfort to Fanny was tediousness and vexation to Mary. Something arose from difference of disposition and habit: one so easily satisfied, the other so unused to endure; but still more might be imputed to difference of circumstances. In some points of interest they were exactly opposed to each other. To Fanny’s mind, Edmund’s absence was really, in its cause and its tendency, a relief. To Mary it was every way painful. She felt the want of his society every day, almost every hour, and was too much in want of it to derive anything but irritation from considering the object for which he went. He could not have devised anything more likely to raise his consequence than this week’s absence, occurring as it did at the very time of her brother’s going away, of William Price’s going too, and completing the sort of general break-up of a party which had been so animated. She felt it keenly. They were now a miserable trio, confined within doors by a series of rain and snow, with nothing to do and no variety to hope for. Angry as she was with Edmund for adhering to his own notions, and acting on them in defiance of her (and she had been so angry that they had hardly parted friends at the ball), she could not help thinking of him continually when absent, dwelling on his merit and affection, and longing again for the almost daily meetings they lately had. His absence was unnecessarily long. He should not have planned such an absence—he should not have left home for a week, when her own departure from Mansfield was so near. Then she began to blame herself. She wished she had not spoken so warmly in their last conversation. She was afraid she had used some strong, some contemptuous expressions in speaking of the clergy, and that should not have been. It was ill-bred; it was wrong. She wished such words unsaid with all her heart.\n\nHer vexation did not end with the week. All this was bad, but she had still more to feel when Friday came round again and brought no Edmund; when Saturday came and still no Edmund; and when, through the slight communication with the other family which Sunday produced, she learned that he had actually written home to defer his return, having promised to remain some days longer with his friend.\n\nIf she had felt impatience and regret before—if she had been sorry for what she said, and feared its too strong effect on him—she now felt and feared it all tenfold more. She had, moreover, to contend with one disagreeable emotion entirely new to her—jealousy. His friend Mr. Owen had sisters; he might find them attractive. But, at any rate, his staying away at a time when, according to all preceding plans, she was to remove to London, meant something that she could not bear. Had Henry returned, as he talked of doing, at the end of three or four days, she should now have been leaving Mansfield. It became absolutely necessary for her to get to Fanny and try to learn something more. She could not live any longer in such solitary wretchedness; and she made her way to the Park, through difficulties of walking which she had deemed unconquerable a week before, for the chance of hearing a little in addition, for the sake of at least hearing his name.\n\nThe first half-hour was lost, for Fanny and Lady Bertram were together, and unless she had Fanny to herself she could hope for nothing. But at last Lady Bertram left the room, and then almost immediately Miss Crawford thus began, with a voice as well regulated as she could—“And how do you like your cousin Edmund’s staying away so long? Being the only young person at home, I consider you as the greatest sufferer. You must miss him. Does his staying longer surprise you?”\n\n“I do not know,” said Fanny hesitatingly. “Yes; I had not particularly expected it.”\n\n“Perhaps he will always stay longer than he talks of. It is the general way all young men do.”\n\n“He did not, the only time he went to see Mr. Owen before.”\n\n“He finds the house more agreeable now. He is a very—a very pleasing young man himself, and I cannot help being rather concerned at not seeing him again before I go to London, as will now undoubtedly be the case. I am looking for Henry every day, and as soon as he comes there will be nothing to detain me at Mansfield. I should like to have seen him once more, I confess. But you must give my compliments to him. Yes; I think it must be compliments. Is not there a something wanted, Miss Price, in our language—a something between compliments and—and love—to suit the sort of friendly acquaintance we have had together? So many months’ acquaintance! But compliments may be sufficient here. Was his letter a long one? Does he give you much account of what he is doing? Is it Christmas gaieties that he is staying for?”\n\n“I only heard a part of the letter; it was to my uncle; but I believe it was very short; indeed I am sure it was but a few lines. All that I heard was that his friend had pressed him to stay longer, and that he had agreed to do so. A few days longer, or some days longer; I am not quite sure which.”\n\n“Oh! if he wrote to his father; but I thought it might have been to Lady Bertram or you. But if he wrote to his father, no wonder he was concise. Who could write chat to Sir Thomas? If he had written to you, there would have been more particulars. You would have heard of balls and parties. He would have sent you a description of everything and everybody. How many Miss Owens are there?”\n\n“Three grown up.”\n\n“Are they musical?”\n\n“I do not at all know. I never heard.”\n\n“That is the first question, you know,” said Miss Crawford, trying to appear gay and unconcerned, “which every woman who plays herself is sure to ask about another. But it is very foolish to ask questions about any young ladies—about any three sisters just grown up; for one knows, without being told, exactly what they are: all very accomplished and pleasing, and one very pretty. There is a beauty in every family; it is a regular thing. Two play on the pianoforte, and one on the harp; and all sing, or would sing if they were taught, or sing all the better for not being taught; or something like it.”\n\n“I know nothing of the Miss Owens,” said Fanny calmly.\n\n“You know nothing and you care less, as people say. Never did tone express indifference plainer. Indeed, how can one care for those one has never seen? Well, when your cousin comes back, he will find Mansfield very quiet; all the noisy ones gone, your brother and mine and myself. I do not like the idea of leaving Mrs. Grant now the time draws near. She does not like my going.”\n\nFanny felt obliged to speak. “You cannot doubt your being missed by many,” said she. “You will be very much missed.”\n\nMiss Crawford turned her eye on her, as if wanting to hear or see more, and then laughingly said, “Oh yes! missed as every noisy evil is missed when it is taken away; that is, there is a great difference felt. But I am not fishing; don’t compliment me. If I am missed, it will appear. I may be discovered by those who want to see me. I shall not be in any doubtful, or distant, or unapproachable region.”\n\nNow Fanny could not bring herself to speak, and Miss Crawford was disappointed; for she had hoped to hear some pleasant assurance of her power from one who she thought must know, and her spirits were clouded again.\n\n“The Miss Owens,” said she, soon afterwards; “suppose you were to have one of the Miss Owens settled at Thornton Lacey; how should you like it? Stranger things have happened. I dare say they are trying for it. And they are quite in the right, for it would be a very pretty establishment for them. I do not at all wonder or blame them. It is everybody’s duty to do as well for themselves as they can. Sir Thomas Bertram’s son is somebody; and now he is in their own line. Their father is a clergyman, and their brother is a clergyman, and they are all clergymen together. He is their lawful property; he fairly belongs to them. You don’t speak, Fanny; Miss Price, you don’t speak. But honestly now, do not you rather expect it than otherwise?”\n\n“No,” said Fanny stoutly, “I do not expect it at all.”\n\n“Not at all!” cried Miss Crawford with alacrity. “I wonder at that. But I dare say you know exactly—I always imagine you are—perhaps you do not think him likely to marry at all—or not at present.”\n\n“No, I do not,” said Fanny softly, hoping she did not err either in the belief or the acknowledgment of it.\n\nHer companion looked at her keenly; and gathering greater spirit from the blush soon produced from such a look, only said, “He is best off as he is,” and turned the subject.\n\n# CHAPTER XXX\n\nMiss Crawford’s uneasiness was much lightened by this conversation, and she walked home again in spirits which might have defied almost another week of the same small party in the same bad weather, had they been put to the proof; but as that very evening brought her brother down from London again in quite, or more than quite, his usual cheerfulness, she had nothing farther to try her own. His still refusing to tell her what he had gone for was but the promotion of gaiety; a day before it might have irritated, but now it was a pleasant joke—suspected only of concealing something planned as a pleasant surprise to herself. And the next day did bring a surprise to her. Henry had said he should just go and ask the Bertrams how they did, and be back in ten minutes, but he was gone above an hour; and when his sister, who had been waiting for him to walk with her in the garden, met him at last most impatiently in the sweep, and cried out, “My dear Henry, where can you have been all this time?” he had only to say that he had been sitting with Lady Bertram and Fanny.\n\n“Sitting with them an hour and a half!” exclaimed Mary.\n\nBut this was only the beginning of her surprise.\n\n“Yes, Mary,” said he, drawing her arm within his, and walking along the sweep as if not knowing where he was: “I could not get away sooner; Fanny looked so lovely! I am quite determined, Mary. My mind is entirely made up. Will it astonish you? No: you must be aware that I am quite determined to marry Fanny Price.”\n\nThe surprise was now complete; for, in spite of whatever his consciousness might suggest, a suspicion of his having any such views had never entered his sister’s imagination; and she looked so truly the astonishment she felt, that he was obliged to repeat what he had said, and more fully and more solemnly. The conviction of his determination once admitted, it was not unwelcome. There was even pleasure with the surprise. Mary was in a state of mind to rejoice in a connexion with the Bertram family, and to be not displeased with her brother’s marrying a little beneath him.\n\n“Yes, Mary,” was Henry’s concluding assurance. “I am fairly caught. You know with what idle designs I began; but this is the end of them. I have, I flatter myself, made no inconsiderable progress in her affections; but my own are entirely fixed.”\n\n“Lucky, lucky girl!” cried Mary, as soon as she could speak; “what a match for her! My dearest Henry, this must be my first feeling; but my second, which you shall have as sincerely, is, that I approve your choice from my soul, and foresee your happiness as heartily as I wish and desire it. You will have a sweet little wife; all gratitude and devotion. Exactly what you deserve. What an amazing match for her! Mrs. Norris often talks of her luck; what will she say now? The delight of all the family, indeed! And she has some true friends in it! How they will rejoice! But tell me all about it! Talk to me for ever. When did you begin to think seriously about her?”\n\nNothing could be more impossible than to answer such a question, though nothing could be more agreeable than to have it asked. “How the pleasing plague had stolen on him” he could not say; and before he had expressed the same sentiment with a little variation of words three times over, his sister eagerly interrupted him with, “Ah, my dear Henry, and this is what took you to London! This was your business! You chose to consult the Admiral before you made up your mind.”\n\nBut this he stoutly denied. He knew his uncle too well to consult him on any matrimonial scheme. The Admiral hated marriage, and thought it never pardonable in a young man of independent fortune.\n\n“When Fanny is known to him,” continued Henry, “he will doat on her. She is exactly the woman to do away every prejudice of such a man as the Admiral, for she is exactly such a woman as he thinks does not exist in the world. She is the very impossibility he would describe, if indeed he has now delicacy of language enough to embody his own ideas. But till it is absolutely settled—settled beyond all interference, he shall know nothing of the matter. No, Mary, you are quite mistaken. You have not discovered my business yet.”\n\n“Well, well, I am satisfied. I know now to whom it must relate, and am in no hurry for the rest. Fanny Price! wonderful, quite wonderful! That Mansfield should have done so much for—that you should have found your fate in Mansfield! But you are quite right; you could not have chosen better. There is not a better girl in the world, and you do not want for fortune; and as to her connexions, they are more than good. The Bertrams are undoubtedly some of the first people in this country. She is niece to Sir Thomas Bertram; that will be enough for the world. But go on, go on. Tell me more. What are your plans? Does she know her own happiness?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“What are you waiting for?”\n\n“For—for very little more than opportunity. Mary, she is not like her cousins; but I think I shall not ask in vain.”\n\n“Oh no! you cannot. Were you even less pleasing—supposing her not to love you already (of which, however, I can have little doubt)—you would be safe. The gentleness and gratitude of her disposition would secure her all your own immediately. From my soul I do not think she would marry you without love; that is, if there is a girl in the world capable of being uninfluenced by ambition, I can suppose it her; but ask her to love you, and she will never have the heart to refuse.”\n\nAs soon as her eagerness could rest in silence, he was as happy to tell as she could be to listen; and a conversation followed almost as deeply interesting to her as to himself, though he had in fact nothing to relate but his own sensations, nothing to dwell on but Fanny’s charms. Fanny’s beauty of face and figure, Fanny’s graces of manner and goodness of heart, were the exhaustless theme. The gentleness, modesty, and sweetness of her character were warmly expatiated on; that sweetness which makes so essential a part of every woman’s worth in the judgment of man, that though he sometimes loves where it is not, he can never believe it absent. Her temper he had good reason to depend on and to praise. He had often seen it tried. Was there one of the family, excepting Edmund, who had not in some way or other continually exercised her patience and forbearance? Her affections were evidently strong. To see her with her brother! What could more delightfully prove that the warmth of her heart was equal to its gentleness? What could be more encouraging to a man who had her love in view? Then, her understanding was beyond every suspicion, quick and clear; and her manners were the mirror of her own modest and elegant mind. Nor was this all. Henry Crawford had too much sense not to feel the worth of good principles in a wife, though he was too little accustomed to serious reflection to know them by their proper name; but when he talked of her having such a steadiness and regularity of conduct, such a high notion of honour, and such an observance of decorum as might warrant any man in the fullest dependence on her faith and integrity, he expressed what was inspired by the knowledge of her being well principled and religious.\n\n“I could so wholly and absolutely confide in her,” said he; “and that is what I want.”\n\nWell might his sister, believing as she really did that his opinion of Fanny Price was scarcely beyond her merits, rejoice in her prospects.\n\n“The more I think of it,” she cried, “the more am I convinced that you are doing quite right; and though I should never have selected Fanny Price as the girl most likely to attach you, I am now persuaded she is the very one to make you happy. Your wicked project upon her peace turns out a clever thought indeed. You will both find your good in it.”\n\n“It was bad, very bad in me against such a creature; but I did not know her then; and she shall have no reason to lament the hour that first put it into my head. I will make her very happy, Mary; happier than she has ever yet been herself, or ever seen anybody else. I will not take her from Northamptonshire. I shall let Everingham, and rent a place in this neighbourhood; perhaps Stanwix Lodge. I shall let a seven years’ lease of Everingham. I am sure of an excellent tenant at half a word. I could name three people now, who would give me my own terms and thank me.”\n\n“Ha!” cried Mary; “settle in Northamptonshire! That is pleasant! Then we shall be all together.”\n\nWhen she had spoken it, she recollected herself, and wished it unsaid; but there was no need of confusion; for her brother saw her only as the supposed inmate of Mansfield parsonage, and replied but to invite her in the kindest manner to his own house, and to claim the best right in her.\n\n“You must give us more than half your time,” said he. “I cannot admit Mrs. Grant to have an equal claim with Fanny and myself, for we shall both have a right in you. Fanny will be so truly your sister!”\n\nMary had only to be grateful and give general assurances; but she was now very fully purposed to be the guest of neither brother nor sister many months longer.\n\n“You will divide your year between London and Northamptonshire?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“That’s right; and in London, of course, a house of your own: no longer with the Admiral. My dearest Henry, the advantage to you of getting away from the Admiral before your manners are hurt by the contagion of his, before you have contracted any of his foolish opinions, or learned to sit over your dinner as if it were the best blessing of life! You are not sensible of the gain, for your regard for him has blinded you; but, in my estimation, your marrying early may be the saving of you. To have seen you grow like the Admiral in word or deed, look or gesture, would have broken my heart.”\n\n“Well, well, we do not think quite alike here. The Admiral has his faults, but he is a very good man, and has been more than a father to me. Few fathers would have let me have my own way half so much. You must not prejudice Fanny against him. I must have them love one another.”\n\nMary refrained from saying what she felt, that there could not be two persons in existence whose characters and manners were less accordant: time would discover it to him; but she could not help this reflection on the Admiral. “Henry, I think so highly of Fanny Price, that if I could suppose the next Mrs. Crawford would have half the reason which my poor ill-used aunt had to abhor the very name, I would prevent the marriage, if possible; but I know you: I know that a wife you loved would be the happiest of women, and that even when you ceased to love, she would yet find in you the liberality and good-breeding of a gentleman.”\n\nThe impossibility of not doing everything in the world to make Fanny Price happy, or of ceasing to love Fanny Price, was of course the groundwork of his eloquent answer.\n\n“Had you seen her this morning, Mary,” he continued, “attending with such ineffable sweetness and patience to all the demands of her aunt’s stupidity, working with her, and for her, her colour beautifully heightened as she leant over the work, then returning to her seat to finish a note which she was previously engaged in writing for that stupid woman’s service, and all this with such unpretending gentleness, so much as if it were a matter of course that she was not to have a moment at her own command, her hair arranged as neatly as it always is, and one little curl falling forward as she wrote, which she now and then shook back, and in the midst of all this, still speaking at intervals to me, or listening, and as if she liked to listen, to what I said. Had you seen her so, Mary, you would not have implied the possibility of her power over my heart ever ceasing.”\n\n“My dearest Henry,” cried Mary, stopping short, and smiling in his face, “how glad I am to see you so much in love! It quite delights me. But what will Mrs. Rushworth and Julia say?”\n\n“I care neither what they say nor what they feel. They will now see what sort of woman it is that can attach me, that can attach a man of sense. I wish the discovery may do them any good. And they will now see their cousin treated as she ought to be, and I wish they may be heartily ashamed of their own abominable neglect and unkindness. They will be angry,” he added, after a moment’s silence, and in a cooler tone; “Mrs. Rushworth will be very angry. It will be a bitter pill to her; that is, like other bitter pills, it will have two moments’ ill flavour, and then be swallowed and forgotten; for I am not such a coxcomb as to suppose her feelings more lasting than other women’s, though I was the object of them. Yes, Mary, my Fanny will feel a difference indeed: a daily, hourly difference, in the behaviour of every being who approaches her; and it will be the completion of my happiness to know that I am the doer of it, that I am the person to give the consequence so justly her due. Now she is dependent, helpless, friendless, neglected, forgotten.”\n\n“Nay, Henry, not by all; not forgotten by all; not friendless or forgotten. Her cousin Edmund never forgets her.”\n\n“Edmund! True, I believe he is, generally speaking, kind to her, and so is Sir Thomas in his way; but it is the way of a rich, superior, long-worded, arbitrary uncle. What can Sir Thomas and Edmund together do, what do they do for her happiness, comfort, honour, and dignity in the world, to what I shall do?”\n\n# CHAPTER XXXI\n\nHenry Crawford was at Mansfield Park again the next morning, and at an earlier hour than common visiting warrants. The two ladies were together in the breakfast-room, and, fortunately for him, Lady Bertram was on the very point of quitting it as he entered. She was almost at the door, and not chusing by any means to take so much trouble in vain, she still went on, after a civil reception, a short sentence about being waited for, and a “Let Sir Thomas know” to the servant.\n\nHenry, overjoyed to have her go, bowed and watched her off, and without losing another moment, turned instantly to Fanny, and, taking out some letters, said, with a most animated look, “I must acknowledge myself infinitely obliged to any creature who gives me such an opportunity of seeing you alone: I have been wishing it more than you can have any idea. Knowing as I do what your feelings as a sister are, I could hardly have borne that any one in the house should share with you in the first knowledge of the news I now bring. He is made. Your brother is a lieutenant. I have the infinite satisfaction of congratulating you on your brother’s promotion. Here are the letters which announce it, this moment come to hand. You will, perhaps, like to see them.”\n\nFanny could not speak, but he did not want her to speak. To see the expression of her eyes, the change of her complexion, the progress of her feelings, their doubt, confusion, and felicity, was enough. She took the letters as he gave them. The first was from the Admiral to inform his nephew, in a few words, of his having succeeded in the object he had undertaken, the promotion of young Price, and enclosing two more, one from the Secretary of the First Lord to a friend, whom the Admiral had set to work in the business, the other from that friend to himself, by which it appeared that his lordship had the very great happiness of attending to the recommendation of Sir Charles; that Sir Charles was much delighted in having such an opportunity of proving his regard for Admiral Crawford, and that the circumstance of Mr. William Price’s commission as Second Lieutenant of H.M. Sloop Thrush being made out was spreading general joy through a wide circle of great people.\n\nWhile her hand was trembling under these letters, her eye running from one to the other, and her heart swelling with emotion, Crawford thus continued, with unfeigned eagerness, to express his interest in the event—\n\n“I will not talk of my own happiness,” said he, “great as it is, for I think only of yours. Compared with you, who has a right to be happy? I have almost grudged myself my own prior knowledge of what you ought to have known before all the world. I have not lost a moment, however. The post was late this morning, but there has not been since a moment’s delay. How impatient, how anxious, how wild I have been on the subject, I will not attempt to describe; how severely mortified, how cruelly disappointed, in not having it finished while I was in London! I was kept there from day to day in the hope of it, for nothing less dear to me than such an object would have detained me half the time from Mansfield. But though my uncle entered into my wishes with all the warmth I could desire, and exerted himself immediately, there were difficulties from the absence of one friend, and the engagements of another, which at last I could no longer bear to stay the end of, and knowing in what good hands I left the cause, I came away on Monday, trusting that many posts would not pass before I should be followed by such very letters as these. My uncle, who is the very best man in the world, has exerted himself, as I knew he would, after seeing your brother. He was delighted with him. I would not allow myself yesterday to say how delighted, or to repeat half that the Admiral said in his praise. I deferred it all till his praise should be proved the praise of a friend, as this day does prove it. Now I may say that even I could not require William Price to excite a greater interest, or be followed by warmer wishes and higher commendation, than were most voluntarily bestowed by my uncle after the evening they had passed together.”\n\n“Has this been all your doing, then?” cried Fanny. “Good heaven! how very, very kind! Have you really—was it by your desire? I beg your pardon, but I am bewildered. Did Admiral Crawford apply? How was it? I am stupefied.”\n\nHenry was most happy to make it more intelligible, by beginning at an earlier stage, and explaining very particularly what he had done. His last journey to London had been undertaken with no other view than that of introducing her brother in Hill Street, and prevailing on the Admiral to exert whatever interest he might have for getting him on. This had been his business. He had communicated it to no creature: he had not breathed a syllable of it even to Mary; while uncertain of the issue, he could not have borne any participation of his feelings, but this had been his business; and he spoke with such a glow of what his solicitude had been, and used such strong expressions, was so abounding in the deepest interest, in twofold motives, in views and wishes more than could be told, that Fanny could not have remained insensible of his drift, had she been able to attend; but her heart was so full and her senses still so astonished, that she could listen but imperfectly even to what he told her of William, and saying only when he paused, “How kind! how very kind! Oh, Mr. Crawford, we are infinitely obliged to you! Dearest, dearest William!” She jumped up and moved in haste towards the door, crying out, “I will go to my uncle. My uncle ought to know it as soon as possible.” But this could not be suffered. The opportunity was too fair, and his feelings too impatient. He was after her immediately. “She must not go, she must allow him five minutes longer,” and he took her hand and led her back to her seat, and was in the middle of his farther explanation, before she had suspected for what she was detained. When she did understand it, however, and found herself expected to believe that she had created sensations which his heart had never known before, and that everything he had done for William was to be placed to the account of his excessive and unequalled attachment to her, she was exceedingly distressed, and for some moments unable to speak. She considered it all as nonsense, as mere trifling and gallantry, which meant only to deceive for the hour; she could not but feel that it was treating her improperly and unworthily, and in such a way as she had not deserved; but it was like himself, and entirely of a piece with what she had seen before; and she would not allow herself to shew half the displeasure she felt, because he had been conferring an obligation, which no want of delicacy on his part could make a trifle to her. While her heart was still bounding with joy and gratitude on William’s behalf, she could not be severely resentful of anything that injured only herself; and after having twice drawn back her hand, and twice attempted in vain to turn away from him, she got up, and said only, with much agitation, “Don’t, Mr. Crawford, pray don’t! I beg you would not. This is a sort of talking which is very unpleasant to me. I must go away. I cannot bear it.” But he was still talking on, describing his affection, soliciting a return, and, finally, in words so plain as to bear but one meaning even to her, offering himself, hand, fortune, everything, to her acceptance. It was so; he had said it. Her astonishment and confusion increased; and though still not knowing how to suppose him serious, she could hardly stand. He pressed for an answer.\n\n“No, no, no!” she cried, hiding her face. “This is all nonsense. Do not distress me. I can hear no more of this. Your kindness to William makes me more obliged to you than words can express; but I do not want, I cannot bear, I must not listen to such—No, no, don’t think of me. But you are not thinking of me. I know it is all nothing.”\n\nShe had burst away from him, and at that moment Sir Thomas was heard speaking to a servant in his way towards the room they were in. It was no time for farther assurances or entreaty, though to part with her at a moment when her modesty alone seemed, to his sanguine and preassured mind, to stand in the way of the happiness he sought, was a cruel necessity. She rushed out at an opposite door from the one her uncle was approaching, and was walking up and down the East room in the utmost confusion of contrary feeling, before Sir Thomas’s politeness or apologies were over, or he had reached the beginning of the joyful intelligence which his visitor came to communicate.\n\nShe was feeling, thinking, trembling about everything; agitated, happy, miserable, infinitely obliged, absolutely angry. It was all beyond belief! He was inexcusable, incomprehensible! But such were his habits that he could do nothing without a mixture of evil. He had previously made her the happiest of human beings, and now he had insulted—she knew not what to say, how to class, or how to regard it. She would not have him be serious, and yet what could excuse the use of such words and offers, if they meant but to trifle?\n\nBut William was a lieutenant. That was a fact beyond a doubt, and without an alloy. She would think of it for ever and forget all the rest. Mr. Crawford would certainly never address her so again: he must have seen how unwelcome it was to her; and in that case, how gratefully she could esteem him for his friendship to William!\n\nShe would not stir farther from the East room than the head of the great staircase, till she had satisfied herself of Mr. Crawford’s having left the house; but when convinced of his being gone, she was eager to go down and be with her uncle, and have all the happiness of his joy as well as her own, and all the benefit of his information or his conjectures as to what would now be William’s destination. Sir Thomas was as joyful as she could desire, and very kind and communicative; and she had so comfortable a talk with him about William as to make her feel as if nothing had occurred to vex her, till she found, towards the close, that Mr. Crawford was engaged to return and dine there that very day. This was a most unwelcome hearing, for though he might think nothing of what had passed, it would be quite distressing to her to see him again so soon.\n\nShe tried to get the better of it; tried very hard, as the dinner hour approached, to feel and appear as usual; but it was quite impossible for her not to look most shy and uncomfortable when their visitor entered the room. She could not have supposed it in the power of any concurrence of circumstances to give her so many painful sensations on the first day of hearing of William’s promotion.\n\nMr. Crawford was not only in the room—he was soon close to her. He had a note to deliver from his sister. Fanny could not look at him, but there was no consciousness of past folly in his voice. She opened her note immediately, glad to have anything to do, and happy, as she read it, to feel that the fidgetings of her aunt Norris, who was also to dine there, screened her a little from view.\n\n“MY DEAR FANNY,—for so I may now always call you, to the infinite relief of a tongue that has been stumbling at Miss Price for at least the last six weeks—I cannot let my brother go without sending you a few lines of general congratulation, and giving my most joyful consent and approval. Go on, my dear Fanny, and without fear; there can be no difficulties worth naming. I chuse to suppose that the assurance of my consent will be something; so you may smile upon him with your sweetest smiles this afternoon, and send him back to me even happier than he goes.\n\nYours affectionately, M. C.”\n\nThese were not expressions to do Fanny any good; for though she read in too much haste and confusion to form the clearest judgment of Miss Crawford’s meaning, it was evident that she meant to compliment her on her brother’s attachment, and even to appear to believe it serious. She did not know what to do, or what to think. There was wretchedness in the idea of its being serious; there was perplexity and agitation every way. She was distressed whenever Mr. Crawford spoke to her, and he spoke to her much too often; and she was afraid there was a something in his voice and manner in addressing her very different from what they were when he talked to the others. Her comfort in that day’s dinner was quite destroyed: she could hardly eat anything; and when Sir Thomas good-humouredly observed that joy had taken away her appetite, she was ready to sink with shame, from the dread of Mr. Crawford’s interpretation; for though nothing could have tempted her to turn her eyes to the right hand, where he sat, she felt that his were immediately directed towards her.\n\nShe was more silent than ever. She would hardly join even when William was the subject, for his commission came all from the right hand too, and there was pain in the connexion.\n\nShe thought Lady Bertram sat longer than ever, and began to be in despair of ever getting away; but at last they were in the drawing-room, and she was able to think as she would, while her aunts finished the subject of William’s appointment in their own style.\n\nMrs. Norris seemed as much delighted with the saving it would be to Sir Thomas as with any part of it. “Now William would be able to keep himself, which would make a vast difference to his uncle, for it was unknown how much he had cost his uncle; and, indeed, it would make some difference in her presents too. She was very glad that she had given William what she did at parting, very glad, indeed, that it had been in her power, without material inconvenience, just at that time to give him something rather considerable; that is, for her, with her limited means, for now it would all be useful in helping to fit up his cabin. She knew he must be at some expense, that he would have many things to buy, though to be sure his father and mother would be able to put him in the way of getting everything very cheap; but she was very glad she had contributed her mite towards it.”\n\n“I am glad you gave him something considerable,” said Lady Bertram, with most unsuspicious calmness, “for I gave him only £10.”\n\n“Indeed!” cried Mrs. Norris, reddening. “Upon my word, he must have gone off with his pockets well lined, and at no expense for his journey to London either!”\n\n“Sir Thomas told me £10 would be enough.”\n\nMrs. Norris, being not at all inclined to question its sufficiency, began to take the matter in another point.\n\n“It is amazing,” said she, “how much young people cost their friends, what with bringing them up and putting them out in the world! They little think how much it comes to, or what their parents, or their uncles and aunts, pay for them in the course of the year. Now, here are my sister Price’s children; take them all together, I dare say nobody would believe what a sum they cost Sir Thomas every year, to say nothing of what I do for them.”\n\n“Very true, sister, as you say. But, poor things! they cannot help it; and you know it makes very little difference to Sir Thomas. Fanny, William must not forget my shawl if he goes to the East Indies; and I shall give him a commission for anything else that is worth having. I wish he may go to the East Indies, that I may have my shawl. I think I will have two shawls, Fanny.”\n\nFanny, meanwhile, speaking only when she could not help it, was very earnestly trying to understand what Mr. and Miss Crawford were at. There was everything in the world against their being serious but his words and manner. Everything natural, probable, reasonable, was against it; all their habits and ways of thinking, and all her own demerits. How could she have excited serious attachment in a man who had seen so many, and been admired by so many, and flirted with so many, infinitely her superiors; who seemed so little open to serious impressions, even where pains had been taken to please him; who thought so slightly, so carelessly, so unfeelingly on all such points; who was everything to everybody, and seemed to find no one essential to him? And farther, how could it be supposed that his sister, with all her high and worldly notions of matrimony, would be forwarding anything of a serious nature in such a quarter? Nothing could be more unnatural in either. Fanny was ashamed of her own doubts. Everything might be possible rather than serious attachment, or serious approbation of it toward her. She had quite convinced herself of this before Sir Thomas and Mr. Crawford joined them. The difficulty was in maintaining the conviction quite so absolutely after Mr. Crawford was in the room; for once or twice a look seemed forced on her which she did not know how to class among the common meaning; in any other man, at least, she would have said that it meant something very earnest, very pointed. But she still tried to believe it no more than what he might often have expressed towards her cousins and fifty other women.\n\nShe thought he was wishing to speak to her unheard by the rest. She fancied he was trying for it the whole evening at intervals, whenever Sir Thomas was out of the room, or at all engaged with Mrs. Norris, and she carefully refused him every opportunity.\n\nAt last—it seemed an at last to Fanny’s nervousness, though not remarkably late—he began to talk of going away; but the comfort of the sound was impaired by his turning to her the next moment, and saying, “Have you nothing to send to Mary? No answer to her note? She will be disappointed if she receives nothing from you. Pray write to her, if it be only a line.”\n\n“Oh yes! certainly,” cried Fanny, rising in haste, the haste of embarrassment and of wanting to get away—“I will write directly.”\n\nShe went accordingly to the table, where she was in the habit of writing for her aunt, and prepared her materials without knowing what in the world to say. She had read Miss Crawford’s note only once, and how to reply to anything so imperfectly understood was most distressing. Quite unpractised in such sort of note-writing, had there been time for scruples and fears as to style she would have felt them in abundance: but something must be instantly written; and with only one decided feeling, that of wishing not to appear to think anything really intended, she wrote thus, in great trembling both of spirits and hand—\n\n“I am very much obliged to you, my dear Miss Crawford, for your kind congratulations, as far as they relate to my dearest William. The rest of your note I know means nothing; but I am so unequal to anything of the sort, that I hope you will excuse my begging you to take no farther notice. I have seen too much of Mr. Crawford not to understand his manners; if he understood me as well, he would, I dare say, behave differently. I do not know what I write, but it would be a great favour of you never to mention the subject again. With thanks for the honour of your note,\n\nI remain, dear Miss Crawford, &c., &c.”\n\nThe conclusion was scarcely intelligible from increasing fright, for she found that Mr. Crawford, under pretence of receiving the note, was coming towards her.\n\n“You cannot think I mean to hurry you,” said he, in an undervoice, perceiving the amazing trepidation with which she made up the note, “you cannot think I have any such object. Do not hurry yourself, I entreat.”\n\n“Oh! I thank you; I have quite done, just done; it will be ready in a moment; I am very much obliged to you; if you will be so good as to give that to Miss Crawford.”\n\nThe note was held out, and must be taken; and as she instantly and with averted eyes walked towards the fireplace, where sat the others, he had nothing to do but to go in good earnest.\n\nFanny thought she had never known a day of greater agitation, both of pain and pleasure; but happily the pleasure was not of a sort to die with the day; for every day would restore the knowledge of William’s advancement, whereas the pain, she hoped, would return no more. She had no doubt that her note must appear excessively ill-written, that the language would disgrace a child, for her distress had allowed no arrangement; but at least it would assure them both of her being neither imposed on nor gratified by Mr. Crawford’s attentions.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXII\n\nFanny had by no means forgotten Mr. Crawford when she awoke the next morning; but she remembered the purport of her note, and was not less sanguine as to its effect than she had been the night before. If Mr. Crawford would but go away! That was what she most earnestly desired: go and take his sister with him, as he was to do, and as he returned to Mansfield on purpose to do. And why it was not done already she could not devise, for Miss Crawford certainly wanted no delay. Fanny had hoped, in the course of his yesterday’s visit, to hear the day named; but he had only spoken of their journey as what would take place ere long.\n\nHaving so satisfactorily settled the conviction her note would convey, she could not but be astonished to see Mr. Crawford, as she accidentally did, coming up to the house again, and at an hour as early as the day before. His coming might have nothing to do with her, but she must avoid seeing him if possible; and being then on her way upstairs, she resolved there to remain, during the whole of his visit, unless actually sent for; and as Mrs. Norris was still in the house, there seemed little danger of her being wanted.\n\nShe sat some time in a good deal of agitation, listening, trembling, and fearing to be sent for every moment; but as no footsteps approached the East room, she grew gradually composed, could sit down, and be able to employ herself, and able to hope that Mr. Crawford had come and would go without her being obliged to know anything of the matter.\n\nNearly half an hour had passed, and she was growing very comfortable, when suddenly the sound of a step in regular approach was heard; a heavy step, an unusual step in that part of the house: it was her uncle’s; she knew it as well as his voice; she had trembled at it as often, and began to tremble again, at the idea of his coming up to speak to her, whatever might be the subject. It was indeed Sir Thomas who opened the door and asked if she were there, and if he might come in. The terror of his former occasional visits to that room seemed all renewed, and she felt as if he were going to examine her again in French and English.\n\nShe was all attention, however, in placing a chair for him, and trying to appear honoured; and, in her agitation, had quite overlooked the deficiencies of her apartment, till he, stopping short as he entered, said, with much surprise, “Why have you no fire to-day?”\n\nThere was snow on the ground, and she was sitting in a shawl. She hesitated.\n\n“I am not cold, sir: I never sit here long at this time of year.”\n\n“But you have a fire in general?”\n\n“No, sir.”\n\n“How comes this about? Here must be some mistake. I understood that you had the use of this room by way of making you perfectly comfortable. In your bedchamber I know you cannot have a fire. Here is some great misapprehension which must be rectified. It is highly unfit for you to sit, be it only half an hour a day, without a fire. You are not strong. You are chilly. Your aunt cannot be aware of this.”\n\nFanny would rather have been silent; but being obliged to speak, she could not forbear, in justice to the aunt she loved best, from saying something in which the words “my aunt Norris” were distinguishable.\n\n“I understand,” cried her uncle, recollecting himself, and not wanting to hear more: “I understand. Your aunt Norris has always been an advocate, and very judiciously, for young people’s being brought up without unnecessary indulgences; but there should be moderation in everything. She is also very hardy herself, which of course will influence her in her opinion of the wants of others. And on another account, too, I can perfectly comprehend. I know what her sentiments have always been. The principle was good in itself, but it may have been, and I believe has been, carried too far in your case. I am aware that there has been sometimes, in some points, a misplaced distinction; but I think too well of you, Fanny, to suppose you will ever harbour resentment on that account. You have an understanding which will prevent you from receiving things only in part, and judging partially by the event. You will take in the whole of the past, you will consider times, persons, and probabilities, and you will feel that they were not least your friends who were educating and preparing you for that mediocrity of condition which seemed to be your lot. Though their caution may prove eventually unnecessary, it was kindly meant; and of this you may be assured, that every advantage of affluence will be doubled by the little privations and restrictions that may have been imposed. I am sure you will not disappoint my opinion of you, by failing at any time to treat your aunt Norris with the respect and attention that are due to her. But enough of this. Sit down, my dear. I must speak to you for a few minutes, but I will not detain you long.”\n\nFanny obeyed, with eyes cast down and colour rising. After a moment’s pause, Sir Thomas, trying to suppress a smile, went on.\n\n“You are not aware, perhaps, that I have had a visitor this morning. I had not been long in my own room, after breakfast, when Mr. Crawford was shewn in. His errand you may probably conjecture.”\n\nFanny’s colour grew deeper and deeper; and her uncle, perceiving that she was embarrassed to a degree that made either speaking or looking up quite impossible, turned away his own eyes, and without any farther pause proceeded in his account of Mr. Crawford’s visit.\n\nMr. Crawford’s business had been to declare himself the lover of Fanny, make decided proposals for her, and entreat the sanction of the uncle, who seemed to stand in the place of her parents; and he had done it all so well, so openly, so liberally, so properly, that Sir Thomas, feeling, moreover, his own replies, and his own remarks to have been very much to the purpose, was exceedingly happy to give the particulars of their conversation; and little aware of what was passing in his niece’s mind, conceived that by such details he must be gratifying her far more than himself. He talked, therefore, for several minutes without Fanny’s daring to interrupt him. She had hardly even attained the wish to do it. Her mind was in too much confusion. She had changed her position; and, with her eyes fixed intently on one of the windows, was listening to her uncle in the utmost perturbation and dismay. For a moment he ceased, but she had barely become conscious of it, when, rising from his chair, he said, “And now, Fanny, having performed one part of my commission, and shewn you everything placed on a basis the most assured and satisfactory, I may execute the remainder by prevailing on you to accompany me downstairs, where, though I cannot but presume on having been no unacceptable companion myself, I must submit to your finding one still better worth listening to. Mr. Crawford, as you have perhaps foreseen, is yet in the house. He is in my room, and hoping to see you there.”\n\nThere was a look, a start, an exclamation on hearing this, which astonished Sir Thomas; but what was his increase of astonishment on hearing her exclaim—“Oh! no, sir, I cannot, indeed I cannot go down to him. Mr. Crawford ought to know—he must know that: I told him enough yesterday to convince him; he spoke to me on this subject yesterday, and I told him without disguise that it was very disagreeable to me, and quite out of my power to return his good opinion.”\n\n“I do not catch your meaning,” said Sir Thomas, sitting down again. “Out of your power to return his good opinion? What is all this? I know he spoke to you yesterday, and (as far as I understand) received as much encouragement to proceed as a well-judging young woman could permit herself to give. I was very much pleased with what I collected to have been your behaviour on the occasion; it shewed a discretion highly to be commended. But now, when he has made his overtures so properly, and honourably—what are your scruples now?”\n\n“You are mistaken, sir,” cried Fanny, forced by the anxiety of the moment even to tell her uncle that he was wrong; “you are quite mistaken. How could Mr. Crawford say such a thing? I gave him no encouragement yesterday. On the contrary, I told him, I cannot recollect my exact words, but I am sure I told him that I would not listen to him, that it was very unpleasant to me in every respect, and that I begged him never to talk to me in that manner again. I am sure I said as much as that and more; and I should have said still more, if I had been quite certain of his meaning anything seriously; but I did not like to be, I could not bear to be, imputing more than might be intended. I thought it might all pass for nothing with him.”\n\nShe could say no more; her breath was almost gone.\n\n“Am I to understand,” said Sir Thomas, after a few moments’ silence, “that you mean to refuse Mr. Crawford?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“Refuse him?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“Refuse Mr. Crawford! Upon what plea? For what reason?”\n\n“I—I cannot like him, sir, well enough to marry him.”\n\n“This is very strange!” said Sir Thomas, in a voice of calm displeasure. “There is something in this which my comprehension does not reach. Here is a young man wishing to pay his addresses to you, with everything to recommend him: not merely situation in life, fortune, and character, but with more than common agreeableness, with address and conversation pleasing to everybody. And he is not an acquaintance of to-day; you have now known him some time. His sister, moreover, is your intimate friend, and he has been doing that for your brother, which I should suppose would have been almost sufficient recommendation to you, had there been no other. It is very uncertain when my interest might have got William on. He has done it already.”\n\n“Yes,” said Fanny, in a faint voice, and looking down with fresh shame; and she did feel almost ashamed of herself, after such a picture as her uncle had drawn, for not liking Mr. Crawford.\n\n“You must have been aware,” continued Sir Thomas presently, “you must have been some time aware of a particularity in Mr. Crawford’s manners to you. This cannot have taken you by surprise. You must have observed his attentions; and though you always received them very properly (I have no accusation to make on that head), I never perceived them to be unpleasant to you. I am half inclined to think, Fanny, that you do not quite know your own feelings.”\n\n“Oh yes, sir! indeed I do. His attentions were always—what I did not like.”\n\nSir Thomas looked at her with deeper surprise. “This is beyond me,” said he. “This requires explanation. Young as you are, and having seen scarcely any one, it is hardly possible that your affections—”\n\nHe paused and eyed her fixedly. He saw her lips formed into a no, though the sound was inarticulate, but her face was like scarlet. That, however, in so modest a girl, might be very compatible with innocence; and chusing at least to appear satisfied, he quickly added, “No, no, I know that is quite out of the question; quite impossible. Well, there is nothing more to be said.”\n\nAnd for a few minutes he did say nothing. He was deep in thought. His niece was deep in thought likewise, trying to harden and prepare herself against farther questioning. She would rather die than own the truth; and she hoped, by a little reflection, to fortify herself beyond betraying it.\n\n“Independently of the interest which Mr. Crawford’s choice seemed to justify” said Sir Thomas, beginning again, and very composedly, “his wishing to marry at all so early is recommendatory to me. I am an advocate for early marriages, where there are means in proportion, and would have every young man, with a sufficient income, settle as soon after four-and-twenty as he can. This is so much my opinion, that I am sorry to think how little likely my own eldest son, your cousin, Mr. Bertram, is to marry early; but at present, as far as I can judge, matrimony makes no part of his plans or thoughts. I wish he were more likely to fix.” Here was a glance at Fanny. “Edmund, I consider, from his dispositions and habits, as much more likely to marry early than his brother. He, indeed, I have lately thought, has seen the woman he could love, which, I am convinced, my eldest son has not. Am I right? Do you agree with me, my dear?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\nIt was gently, but it was calmly said, and Sir Thomas was easy on the score of the cousins. But the removal of his alarm did his niece no service: as her unaccountableness was confirmed his displeasure increased; and getting up and walking about the room with a frown, which Fanny could picture to herself, though she dared not lift up her eyes, he shortly afterwards, and in a voice of authority, said, “Have you any reason, child, to think ill of Mr. Crawford’s temper?”\n\n“No, sir.”\n\nShe longed to add, “But of his principles I have”; but her heart sunk under the appalling prospect of discussion, explanation, and probably non-conviction. Her ill opinion of him was founded chiefly on observations, which, for her cousins’ sake, she could scarcely dare mention to their father. Maria and Julia, and especially Maria, were so closely implicated in Mr. Crawford’s misconduct, that she could not give his character, such as she believed it, without betraying them. She had hoped that, to a man like her uncle, so discerning, so honourable, so good, the simple acknowledgment of settled dislike on her side would have been sufficient. To her infinite grief she found it was not.\n\nSir Thomas came towards the table where she sat in trembling wretchedness, and with a good deal of cold sternness, said, “It is of no use, I perceive, to talk to you. We had better put an end to this most mortifying conference. Mr. Crawford must not be kept longer waiting. I will, therefore, only add, as thinking it my duty to mark my opinion of your conduct, that you have disappointed every expectation I had formed, and proved yourself of a character the very reverse of what I had supposed. For I had, Fanny, as I think my behaviour must have shewn, formed a very favourable opinion of you from the period of my return to England. I had thought you peculiarly free from wilfulness of temper, self-conceit, and every tendency to that independence of spirit which prevails so much in modern days, even in young women, and which in young women is offensive and disgusting beyond all common offence. But you have now shewn me that you can be wilful and perverse; that you can and will decide for yourself, without any consideration or deference for those who have surely some right to guide you, without even asking their advice. You have shewn yourself very, very different from anything that I had imagined. The advantage or disadvantage of your family, of your parents, your brothers and sisters, never seems to have had a moment’s share in your thoughts on this occasion. How they might be benefited, how they must rejoice in such an establishment for you, is nothing to you. You think only of yourself, and because you do not feel for Mr. Crawford exactly what a young heated fancy imagines to be necessary for happiness, you resolve to refuse him at once, without wishing even for a little time to consider of it, a little more time for cool consideration, and for really examining your own inclinations; and are, in a wild fit of folly, throwing away from you such an opportunity of being settled in life, eligibly, honourably, nobly settled, as will, probably, never occur to you again. Here is a young man of sense, of character, of temper, of manners, and of fortune, exceedingly attached to you, and seeking your hand in the most handsome and disinterested way; and let me tell you, Fanny, that you may live eighteen years longer in the world without being addressed by a man of half Mr. Crawford’s estate, or a tenth part of his merits. Gladly would I have bestowed either of my own daughters on him. Maria is nobly married; but had Mr. Crawford sought Julia’s hand, I should have given it to him with superior and more heartfelt satisfaction than I gave Maria’s to Mr. Rushworth.” After half a moment’s pause: “And I should have been very much surprised had either of my daughters, on receiving a proposal of marriage at any time which might carry with it only half the eligibility of this, immediately and peremptorily, and without paying my opinion or my regard the compliment of any consultation, put a decided negative on it. I should have been much surprised and much hurt by such a proceeding. I should have thought it a gross violation of duty and respect. You are not to be judged by the same rule. You do not owe me the duty of a child. But, Fanny, if your heart can acquit you of ingratitude—”\n\nHe ceased. Fanny was by this time crying so bitterly that, angry as he was, he would not press that article farther. Her heart was almost broke by such a picture of what she appeared to him; by such accusations, so heavy, so multiplied, so rising in dreadful gradation! Self-willed, obstinate, selfish, and ungrateful. He thought her all this. She had deceived his expectations; she had lost his good opinion. What was to become of her?\n\n“I am very sorry,” said she inarticulately, through her tears, “I am very sorry indeed.”\n\n“Sorry! yes, I hope you are sorry; and you will probably have reason to be long sorry for this day’s transactions.”\n\n“If it were possible for me to do otherwise” said she, with another strong effort; “but I am so perfectly convinced that I could never make him happy, and that I should be miserable myself.”\n\nAnother burst of tears; but in spite of that burst, and in spite of that great black word miserable, which served to introduce it, Sir Thomas began to think a little relenting, a little change of inclination, might have something to do with it; and to augur favourably from the personal entreaty of the young man himself. He knew her to be very timid, and exceedingly nervous; and thought it not improbable that her mind might be in such a state as a little time, a little pressing, a little patience, and a little impatience, a judicious mixture of all on the lover’s side, might work their usual effect on. If the gentleman would but persevere, if he had but love enough to persevere, Sir Thomas began to have hopes; and these reflections having passed across his mind and cheered it, “Well,” said he, in a tone of becoming gravity, but of less anger, “well, child, dry up your tears. There is no use in these tears; they can do no good. You must now come downstairs with me. Mr. Crawford has been kept waiting too long already. You must give him your own answer: we cannot expect him to be satisfied with less; and you only can explain to him the grounds of that misconception of your sentiments, which, unfortunately for himself, he certainly has imbibed. I am totally unequal to it.”\n\nBut Fanny shewed such reluctance, such misery, at the idea of going down to him, that Sir Thomas, after a little consideration, judged it better to indulge her. His hopes from both gentleman and lady suffered a small depression in consequence; but when he looked at his niece, and saw the state of feature and complexion which her crying had brought her into, he thought there might be as much lost as gained by an immediate interview. With a few words, therefore, of no particular meaning, he walked off by himself, leaving his poor niece to sit and cry over what had passed, with very wretched feelings.\n\nHer mind was all disorder. The past, present, future, everything was terrible. But her uncle’s anger gave her the severest pain of all. Selfish and ungrateful! to have appeared so to him! She was miserable for ever. She had no one to take her part, to counsel, or speak for her. Her only friend was absent. He might have softened his father; but all, perhaps all, would think her selfish and ungrateful. She might have to endure the reproach again and again; she might hear it, or see it, or know it to exist for ever in every connexion about her. She could not but feel some resentment against Mr. Crawford; yet, if he really loved her, and were unhappy too! It was all wretchedness together.\n\nIn about a quarter of an hour her uncle returned; she was almost ready to faint at the sight of him. He spoke calmly, however, without austerity, without reproach, and she revived a little. There was comfort, too, in his words, as well as his manner, for he began with, “Mr. Crawford is gone: he has just left me. I need not repeat what has passed. I do not want to add to anything you may now be feeling, by an account of what he has felt. Suffice it, that he has behaved in the most gentlemanlike and generous manner, and has confirmed me in a most favourable opinion of his understanding, heart, and temper. Upon my representation of what you were suffering, he immediately, and with the greatest delicacy, ceased to urge to see you for the present.”\n\nHere Fanny, who had looked up, looked down again. “Of course,” continued her uncle, “it cannot be supposed but that he should request to speak with you alone, be it only for five minutes; a request too natural, a claim too just to be denied. But there is no time fixed; perhaps to-morrow, or whenever your spirits are composed enough. For the present you have only to tranquillise yourself. Check these tears; they do but exhaust you. If, as I am willing to suppose, you wish to shew me any observance, you will not give way to these emotions, but endeavour to reason yourself into a stronger frame of mind. I advise you to go out: the air will do you good; go out for an hour on the gravel; you will have the shrubbery to yourself, and will be the better for air and exercise. And, Fanny” (turning back again for a moment), “I shall make no mention below of what has passed; I shall not even tell your aunt Bertram. There is no occasion for spreading the disappointment; say nothing about it yourself.”\n\nThis was an order to be most joyfully obeyed; this was an act of kindness which Fanny felt at her heart. To be spared from her aunt Norris’s interminable reproaches! he left her in a glow of gratitude. Anything might be bearable rather than such reproaches. Even to see Mr. Crawford would be less overpowering.\n\nShe walked out directly, as her uncle recommended, and followed his advice throughout, as far as she could; did check her tears; did earnestly try to compose her spirits and strengthen her mind. She wished to prove to him that she did desire his comfort, and sought to regain his favour; and he had given her another strong motive for exertion, in keeping the whole affair from the knowledge of her aunts. Not to excite suspicion by her look or manner was now an object worth attaining; and she felt equal to almost anything that might save her from her aunt Norris.\n\nShe was struck, quite struck, when, on returning from her walk and going into the East room again, the first thing which caught her eye was a fire lighted and burning. A fire! it seemed too much; just at that time to be giving her such an indulgence was exciting even painful gratitude. She wondered that Sir Thomas could have leisure to think of such a trifle again; but she soon found, from the voluntary information of the housemaid, who came in to attend it, that so it was to be every day. Sir Thomas had given orders for it.\n\n“I must be a brute, indeed, if I can be really ungrateful!” said she, in soliloquy. “Heaven defend me from being ungrateful!”\n\nShe saw nothing more of her uncle, nor of her aunt Norris, till they met at dinner. Her uncle’s behaviour to her was then as nearly as possible what it had been before; she was sure he did not mean there should be any change, and that it was only her own conscience that could fancy any; but her aunt was soon quarrelling with her; and when she found how much and how unpleasantly her having only walked out without her aunt’s knowledge could be dwelt on, she felt all the reason she had to bless the kindness which saved her from the same spirit of reproach, exerted on a more momentous subject.\n\n“If I had known you were going out, I should have got you just to go as far as my house with some orders for Nanny,” said she, “which I have since, to my very great inconvenience, been obliged to go and carry myself. I could very ill spare the time, and you might have saved me the trouble, if you would only have been so good as to let us know you were going out. It would have made no difference to you, I suppose, whether you had walked in the shrubbery or gone to my house.”\n\n“I recommended the shrubbery to Fanny as the driest place,” said Sir Thomas.\n\n“Oh!” said Mrs. Norris, with a moment’s check, “that was very kind of you, Sir Thomas; but you do not know how dry the path is to my house. Fanny would have had quite as good a walk there, I assure you, with the advantage of being of some use, and obliging her aunt: it is all her fault. If she would but have let us know she was going out but there is a something about Fanny, I have often observed it before—she likes to go her own way to work; she does not like to be dictated to; she takes her own independent walk whenever she can; she certainly has a little spirit of secrecy, and independence, and nonsense, about her, which I would advise her to get the better of.”\n\nAs a general reflection on Fanny, Sir Thomas thought nothing could be more unjust, though he had been so lately expressing the same sentiments himself, and he tried to turn the conversation: tried repeatedly before he could succeed; for Mrs. Norris had not discernment enough to perceive, either now, or at any other time, to what degree he thought well of his niece, or how very far he was from wishing to have his own children’s merits set off by the depreciation of hers. She was talking at Fanny, and resenting this private walk half through the dinner.\n\nIt was over, however, at last; and the evening set in with more composure to Fanny, and more cheerfulness of spirits than she could have hoped for after so stormy a morning; but she trusted, in the first place, that she had done right: that her judgment had not misled her. For the purity of her intentions she could answer; and she was willing to hope, secondly, that her uncle’s displeasure was abating, and would abate farther as he considered the matter with more impartiality, and felt, as a good man must feel, how wretched, and how unpardonable, how hopeless, and how wicked it was to marry without affection.\n\nWhen the meeting with which she was threatened for the morrow was past, she could not but flatter herself that the subject would be finally concluded, and Mr. Crawford once gone from Mansfield, that everything would soon be as if no such subject had existed. She would not, could not believe, that Mr. Crawford’s affection for her could distress him long; his mind was not of that sort. London would soon bring its cure. In London he would soon learn to wonder at his infatuation, and be thankful for the right reason in her which had saved him from its evil consequences.\n\nWhile Fanny’s mind was engaged in these sort of hopes, her uncle was, soon after tea, called out of the room; an occurrence too common to strike her, and she thought nothing of it till the butler reappeared ten minutes afterwards, and advancing decidedly towards herself, said, “Sir Thomas wishes to speak with you, ma’am, in his own room.” Then it occurred to her what might be going on; a suspicion rushed over her mind which drove the colour from her cheeks; but instantly rising, she was preparing to obey, when Mrs. Norris called out, “Stay, stay, Fanny! what are you about? where are you going? don’t be in such a hurry. Depend upon it, it is not you who are wanted; depend upon it, it is me” (looking at the butler); “but you are so very eager to put yourself forward. What should Sir Thomas want you for? It is me, Baddeley, you mean; I am coming this moment. You mean me, Baddeley, I am sure; Sir Thomas wants me, not Miss Price.”\n\nBut Baddeley was stout. “No, ma’am, it is Miss Price; I am certain of its being Miss Price.” And there was a half-smile with the words, which meant, “I do not think you would answer the purpose at all.”\n\nMrs. Norris, much discontented, was obliged to compose herself to work again; and Fanny, walking off in agitating consciousness, found herself, as she anticipated, in another minute alone with Mr. Crawford.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIII\n\nThe conference was neither so short nor so conclusive as the lady had designed. The gentleman was not so easily satisfied. He had all the disposition to persevere that Sir Thomas could wish him. He had vanity, which strongly inclined him in the first place to think she did love him, though she might not know it herself; and which, secondly, when constrained at last to admit that she did know her own present feelings, convinced him that he should be able in time to make those feelings what he wished.\n\nHe was in love, very much in love; and it was a love which, operating on an active, sanguine spirit, of more warmth than delicacy, made her affection appear of greater consequence because it was withheld, and determined him to have the glory, as well as the felicity, of forcing her to love him.\n\nHe would not despair: he would not desist. He had every well-grounded reason for solid attachment; he knew her to have all the worth that could justify the warmest hopes of lasting happiness with her; her conduct at this very time, by speaking the disinterestedness and delicacy of her character (qualities which he believed most rare indeed), was of a sort to heighten all his wishes, and confirm all his resolutions. He knew not that he had a pre-engaged heart to attack. Of that he had no suspicion. He considered her rather as one who had never thought on the subject enough to be in danger; who had been guarded by youth, a youth of mind as lovely as of person; whose modesty had prevented her from understanding his attentions, and who was still overpowered by the suddenness of addresses so wholly unexpected, and the novelty of a situation which her fancy had never taken into account.\n\nMust it not follow of course, that, when he was understood, he should succeed? He believed it fully. Love such as his, in a man like himself, must with perseverance secure a return, and at no great distance; and he had so much delight in the idea of obliging her to love him in a very short time, that her not loving him now was scarcely regretted. A little difficulty to be overcome was no evil to Henry Crawford. He rather derived spirits from it. He had been apt to gain hearts too easily. His situation was new and animating.\n\nTo Fanny, however, who had known too much opposition all her life to find any charm in it, all this was unintelligible. She found that he did mean to persevere; but how he could, after such language from her as she felt herself obliged to use, was not to be understood. She told him that she did not love him, could not love him, was sure she never should love him; that such a change was quite impossible; that the subject was most painful to her; that she must entreat him never to mention it again, to allow her to leave him at once, and let it be considered as concluded for ever. And when farther pressed, had added, that in her opinion their dispositions were so totally dissimilar as to make mutual affection incompatible; and that they were unfitted for each other by nature, education, and habit. All this she had said, and with the earnestness of sincerity; yet this was not enough, for he immediately denied there being anything uncongenial in their characters, or anything unfriendly in their situations; and positively declared, that he would still love, and still hope!\n\nFanny knew her own meaning, but was no judge of her own manner. Her manner was incurably gentle; and she was not aware how much it concealed the sternness of her purpose. Her diffidence, gratitude, and softness made every expression of indifference seem almost an effort of self-denial; seem, at least, to be giving nearly as much pain to herself as to him. Mr. Crawford was no longer the Mr. Crawford who, as the clandestine, insidious, treacherous admirer of Maria Bertram, had been her abhorrence, whom she had hated to see or to speak to, in whom she could believe no good quality to exist, and whose power, even of being agreeable, she had barely acknowledged. He was now the Mr. Crawford who was addressing herself with ardent, disinterested love; whose feelings were apparently become all that was honourable and upright, whose views of happiness were all fixed on a marriage of attachment; who was pouring out his sense of her merits, describing and describing again his affection, proving as far as words could prove it, and in the language, tone, and spirit of a man of talent too, that he sought her for her gentleness and her goodness; and to complete the whole, he was now the Mr. Crawford who had procured William’s promotion!\n\nHere was a change, and here were claims which could not but operate! She might have disdained him in all the dignity of angry virtue, in the grounds of Sotherton, or the theatre at Mansfield Park; but he approached her now with rights that demanded different treatment. She must be courteous, and she must be compassionate. She must have a sensation of being honoured, and whether thinking of herself or her brother, she must have a strong feeling of gratitude. The effect of the whole was a manner so pitying and agitated, and words intermingled with her refusal so expressive of obligation and concern, that to a temper of vanity and hope like Crawford’s, the truth, or at least the strength of her indifference, might well be questionable; and he was not so irrational as Fanny considered him, in the professions of persevering, assiduous, and not desponding attachment which closed the interview.\n\nIt was with reluctance that he suffered her to go; but there was no look of despair in parting to belie his words, or give her hopes of his being less unreasonable than he professed himself.\n\nNow she was angry. Some resentment did arise at a perseverance so selfish and ungenerous. Here was again a want of delicacy and regard for others which had formerly so struck and disgusted her. Here was again a something of the same Mr. Crawford whom she had so reprobated before. How evidently was there a gross want of feeling and humanity where his own pleasure was concerned—And, alas! how always known no principle to supply as a duty what the heart was deficient in. Had her own affections been as free—as perhaps they ought to have been—he never could have engaged them.\n\nSo thought Fanny, in good truth and sober sadness, as she sat musing over that too great indulgence and luxury of a fire upstairs: wondering at the past and present; wondering at what was yet to come, and in a nervous agitation which made nothing clear to her but the persuasion of her being never under any circumstances able to love Mr. Crawford, and the felicity of having a fire to sit over and think of it.\n\nSir Thomas was obliged, or obliged himself, to wait till the morrow for a knowledge of what had passed between the young people. He then saw Mr. Crawford, and received his account. The first feeling was disappointment: he had hoped better things; he had thought that an hour’s entreaty from a young man like Crawford could not have worked so little change on a gentle-tempered girl like Fanny; but there was speedy comfort in the determined views and sanguine perseverance of the lover; and when seeing such confidence of success in the principal, Sir Thomas was soon able to depend on it himself.\n\nNothing was omitted, on his side, of civility, compliment, or kindness, that might assist the plan. Mr. Crawford’s steadiness was honoured, and Fanny was praised, and the connexion was still the most desirable in the world. At Mansfield Park Mr. Crawford would always be welcome; he had only to consult his own judgment and feelings as to the frequency of his visits, at present or in future. In all his niece’s family and friends, there could be but one opinion, one wish on the subject; the influence of all who loved her must incline one way.\n\nEverything was said that could encourage, every encouragement received with grateful joy, and the gentlemen parted the best of friends.\n\nSatisfied that the cause was now on a footing the most proper and hopeful, Sir Thomas resolved to abstain from all farther importunity with his niece, and to shew no open interference. Upon her disposition he believed kindness might be the best way of working. Entreaty should be from one quarter only. The forbearance of her family on a point, respecting which she could be in no doubt of their wishes, might be their surest means of forwarding it. Accordingly, on this principle, Sir Thomas took the first opportunity of saying to her, with a mild gravity, intended to be overcoming, “Well, Fanny, I have seen Mr. Crawford again, and learn from him exactly how matters stand between you. He is a most extraordinary young man, and whatever be the event, you must feel that you have created an attachment of no common character; though, young as you are, and little acquainted with the transient, varying, unsteady nature of love, as it generally exists, you cannot be struck as I am with all that is wonderful in a perseverance of this sort against discouragement. With him it is entirely a matter of feeling: he claims no merit in it; perhaps is entitled to none. Yet, having chosen so well, his constancy has a respectable stamp. Had his choice been less unexceptionable, I should have condemned his persevering.”\n\n“Indeed, sir,” said Fanny, “I am very sorry that Mr. Crawford should continue to—I know that it is paying me a very great compliment, and I feel most undeservedly honoured; but I am so perfectly convinced, and I have told him so, that it never will be in my power—”\n\n“My dear,” interrupted Sir Thomas, “there is no occasion for this. Your feelings are as well known to me as my wishes and regrets must be to you. There is nothing more to be said or done. From this hour the subject is never to be revived between us. You will have nothing to fear, or to be agitated about. You cannot suppose me capable of trying to persuade you to marry against your inclinations. Your happiness and advantage are all that I have in view, and nothing is required of you but to bear with Mr. Crawford’s endeavours to convince you that they may not be incompatible with his. He proceeds at his own risk. You are on safe ground. I have engaged for your seeing him whenever he calls, as you might have done had nothing of this sort occurred. You will see him with the rest of us, in the same manner, and, as much as you can, dismissing the recollection of everything unpleasant. He leaves Northamptonshire so soon, that even this slight sacrifice cannot be often demanded. The future must be very uncertain. And now, my dear Fanny, this subject is closed between us.”\n\nThe promised departure was all that Fanny could think of with much satisfaction. Her uncle’s kind expressions, however, and forbearing manner, were sensibly felt; and when she considered how much of the truth was unknown to him, she believed she had no right to wonder at the line of conduct he pursued. He, who had married a daughter to Mr. Rushworth: romantic delicacy was certainly not to be expected from him. She must do her duty, and trust that time might make her duty easier than it now was.\n\nShe could not, though only eighteen, suppose Mr. Crawford’s attachment would hold out for ever; she could not but imagine that steady, unceasing discouragement from herself would put an end to it in time. How much time she might, in her own fancy, allot for its dominion, is another concern. It would not be fair to inquire into a young lady’s exact estimate of her own perfections.\n\nIn spite of his intended silence, Sir Thomas found himself once more obliged to mention the subject to his niece, to prepare her briefly for its being imparted to her aunts; a measure which he would still have avoided, if possible, but which became necessary from the totally opposite feelings of Mr. Crawford as to any secrecy of proceeding. He had no idea of concealment. It was all known at the Parsonage, where he loved to talk over the future with both his sisters, and it would be rather gratifying to him to have enlightened witnesses of the progress of his success. When Sir Thomas understood this, he felt the necessity of making his own wife and sister-in-law acquainted with the business without delay; though, on Fanny’s account, he almost dreaded the effect of the communication to Mrs. Norris as much as Fanny herself. He deprecated her mistaken but well-meaning zeal. Sir Thomas, indeed, was, by this time, not very far from classing Mrs. Norris as one of those well-meaning people who are always doing mistaken and very disagreeable things.\n\nMrs. Norris, however, relieved him. He pressed for the strictest forbearance and silence towards their niece; she not only promised, but did observe it. She only looked her increased ill-will. Angry she was: bitterly angry; but she was more angry with Fanny for having received such an offer than for refusing it. It was an injury and affront to Julia, who ought to have been Mr. Crawford’s choice; and, independently of that, she disliked Fanny, because she had neglected her; and she would have grudged such an elevation to one whom she had been always trying to depress.\n\nSir Thomas gave her more credit for discretion on the occasion than she deserved; and Fanny could have blessed her for allowing her only to see her displeasure, and not to hear it.\n\nLady Bertram took it differently. She had been a beauty, and a prosperous beauty, all her life; and beauty and wealth were all that excited her respect. To know Fanny to be sought in marriage by a man of fortune, raised her, therefore, very much in her opinion. By convincing her that Fanny was very pretty, which she had been doubting about before, and that she would be advantageously married, it made her feel a sort of credit in calling her niece.\n\n“Well, Fanny,” said she, as soon as they were alone together afterwards, and she really had known something like impatience to be alone with her, and her countenance, as she spoke, had extraordinary animation; “Well, Fanny, I have had a very agreeable surprise this morning. I must just speak of it once, I told Sir Thomas I must once, and then I shall have done. I give you joy, my dear niece.” And looking at her complacently, she added, “Humph, we certainly are a handsome family!”\n\nFanny coloured, and doubted at first what to say; when, hoping to assail her on her vulnerable side, she presently answered—\n\n“My dear aunt, you cannot wish me to do differently from what I have done, I am sure. You cannot wish me to marry; for you would miss me, should not you? Yes, I am sure you would miss me too much for that.”\n\n“No, my dear, I should not think of missing you, when such an offer as this comes in your way. I could do very well without you, if you were married to a man of such good estate as Mr. Crawford. And you must be aware, Fanny, that it is every young woman’s duty to accept such a very unexceptionable offer as this.”\n\nThis was almost the only rule of conduct, the only piece of advice, which Fanny had ever received from her aunt in the course of eight years and a half. It silenced her. She felt how unprofitable contention would be. If her aunt’s feelings were against her, nothing could be hoped from attacking her understanding. Lady Bertram was quite talkative.\n\n“I will tell you what, Fanny,” said she, “I am sure he fell in love with you at the ball; I am sure the mischief was done that evening. You did look remarkably well. Everybody said so. Sir Thomas said so. And you know you had Chapman to help you to dress. I am very glad I sent Chapman to you. I shall tell Sir Thomas that I am sure it was done that evening.” And still pursuing the same cheerful thoughts, she soon afterwards added, “And I will tell you what, Fanny, which is more than I did for Maria: the next time Pug has a litter you shall have a puppy.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIV\n\nEdmund had great things to hear on his return. Many surprises were awaiting him. The first that occurred was not least in interest: the appearance of Henry Crawford and his sister walking together through the village as he rode into it. He had concluded—he had meant them to be far distant. His absence had been extended beyond a fortnight purposely to avoid Miss Crawford. He was returning to Mansfield with spirits ready to feed on melancholy remembrances, and tender associations, when her own fair self was before him, leaning on her brother’s arm, and he found himself receiving a welcome, unquestionably friendly, from the woman whom, two moments before, he had been thinking of as seventy miles off, and as farther, much farther, from him in inclination than any distance could express.\n\nHer reception of him was of a sort which he could not have hoped for, had he expected to see her. Coming as he did from such a purport fulfilled as had taken him away, he would have expected anything rather than a look of satisfaction, and words of simple, pleasant meaning. It was enough to set his heart in a glow, and to bring him home in the properest state for feeling the full value of the other joyful surprises at hand.\n\nWilliam’s promotion, with all its particulars, he was soon master of; and with such a secret provision of comfort within his own breast to help the joy, he found in it a source of most gratifying sensation and unvarying cheerfulness all dinner-time.\n\nAfter dinner, when he and his father were alone, he had Fanny’s history; and then all the great events of the last fortnight, and the present situation of matters at Mansfield were known to him.\n\nFanny suspected what was going on. They sat so much longer than usual in the dining-parlour, that she was sure they must be talking of her; and when tea at last brought them away, and she was to be seen by Edmund again, she felt dreadfully guilty. He came to her, sat down by her, took her hand, and pressed it kindly; and at that moment she thought that, but for the occupation and the scene which the tea-things afforded, she must have betrayed her emotion in some unpardonable excess.\n\nHe was not intending, however, by such action, to be conveying to her that unqualified approbation and encouragement which her hopes drew from it. It was designed only to express his participation in all that interested her, and to tell her that he had been hearing what quickened every feeling of affection. He was, in fact, entirely on his father’s side of the question. His surprise was not so great as his father’s at her refusing Crawford, because, so far from supposing her to consider him with anything like a preference, he had always believed it to be rather the reverse, and could imagine her to be taken perfectly unprepared, but Sir Thomas could not regard the connexion as more desirable than he did. It had every recommendation to him; and while honouring her for what she had done under the influence of her present indifference, honouring her in rather stronger terms than Sir Thomas could quite echo, he was most earnest in hoping, and sanguine in believing, that it would be a match at last, and that, united by mutual affection, it would appear that their dispositions were as exactly fitted to make them blessed in each other, as he was now beginning seriously to consider them. Crawford had been too precipitate. He had not given her time to attach herself. He had begun at the wrong end. With such powers as his, however, and such a disposition as hers, Edmund trusted that everything would work out a happy conclusion. Meanwhile, he saw enough of Fanny’s embarrassment to make him scrupulously guard against exciting it a second time, by any word, or look, or movement.\n\nCrawford called the next day, and on the score of Edmund’s return, Sir Thomas felt himself more than licensed to ask him to stay dinner; it was really a necessary compliment. He staid of course, and Edmund had then ample opportunity for observing how he sped with Fanny, and what degree of immediate encouragement for him might be extracted from her manners; and it was so little, so very, very little—every chance, every possibility of it, resting upon her embarrassment only; if there was not hope in her confusion, there was hope in nothing else—that he was almost ready to wonder at his friend’s perseverance. Fanny was worth it all; he held her to be worth every effort of patience, every exertion of mind, but he did not think he could have gone on himself with any woman breathing, without something more to warm his courage than his eyes could discern in hers. He was very willing to hope that Crawford saw clearer, and this was the most comfortable conclusion for his friend that he could come to from all that he observed to pass before, and at, and after dinner.\n\nIn the evening a few circumstances occurred which he thought more promising. When he and Crawford walked into the drawing-room, his mother and Fanny were sitting as intently and silently at work as if there were nothing else to care for. Edmund could not help noticing their apparently deep tranquillity.\n\n“We have not been so silent all the time,” replied his mother. “Fanny has been reading to me, and only put the book down upon hearing you coming.” And sure enough there was a book on the table which had the air of being very recently closed: a volume of Shakespeare. “She often reads to me out of those books; and she was in the middle of a very fine speech of that man’s—what’s his name, Fanny?—when we heard your footsteps.”\n\nCrawford took the volume. “Let me have the pleasure of finishing that speech to your ladyship,” said he. “I shall find it immediately.” And by carefully giving way to the inclination of the leaves, he did find it, or within a page or two, quite near enough to satisfy Lady Bertram, who assured him, as soon as he mentioned the name of Cardinal Wolsey, that he had got the very speech. Not a look or an offer of help had Fanny given; not a syllable for or against. All her attention was for her work. She seemed determined to be interested by nothing else. But taste was too strong in her. She could not abstract her mind five minutes: she was forced to listen; his reading was capital, and her pleasure in good reading extreme. To good reading, however, she had been long used: her uncle read well, her cousins all, Edmund very well, but in Mr. Crawford’s reading there was a variety of excellence beyond what she had ever met with. The King, the Queen, Buckingham, Wolsey, Cromwell, all were given in turn; for with the happiest knack, the happiest power of jumping and guessing, he could always alight at will on the best scene, or the best speeches of each; and whether it were dignity, or pride, or tenderness, or remorse, or whatever were to be expressed, he could do it with equal beauty. It was truly dramatic. His acting had first taught Fanny what pleasure a play might give, and his reading brought all his acting before her again; nay, perhaps with greater enjoyment, for it came unexpectedly, and with no such drawback as she had been used to suffer in seeing him on the stage with Miss Bertram.\n\nEdmund watched the progress of her attention, and was amused and gratified by seeing how she gradually slackened in the needlework, which at the beginning seemed to occupy her totally: how it fell from her hand while she sat motionless over it, and at last, how the eyes which had appeared so studiously to avoid him throughout the day were turned and fixed on Crawford—fixed on him for minutes, fixed on him, in short, till the attraction drew Crawford’s upon her, and the book was closed, and the charm was broken. Then she was shrinking again into herself, and blushing and working as hard as ever; but it had been enough to give Edmund encouragement for his friend, and as he cordially thanked him, he hoped to be expressing Fanny’s secret feelings too.\n\n“That play must be a favourite with you,” said he; “you read as if you knew it well.”\n\n“It will be a favourite, I believe, from this hour,” replied Crawford; “but I do not think I have had a volume of Shakespeare in my hand before since I was fifteen. I once saw Henry the Eighth acted, or I have heard of it from somebody who did, I am not certain which. But Shakespeare one gets acquainted with without knowing how. It is a part of an Englishman’s constitution. His thoughts and beauties are so spread abroad that one touches them everywhere; one is intimate with him by instinct. No man of any brain can open at a good part of one of his plays without falling into the flow of his meaning immediately.”\n\n“No doubt one is familiar with Shakespeare in a degree,” said Edmund, “from one’s earliest years. His celebrated passages are quoted by everybody; they are in half the books we open, and we all talk Shakespeare, use his similes, and describe with his descriptions; but this is totally distinct from giving his sense as you gave it. To know him in bits and scraps is common enough; to know him pretty thoroughly is, perhaps, not uncommon; but to read him well aloud is no everyday talent.”\n\n“Sir, you do me honour,” was Crawford’s answer, with a bow of mock gravity.\n\nBoth gentlemen had a glance at Fanny, to see if a word of accordant praise could be extorted from her; yet both feeling that it could not be. Her praise had been given in her attention; that must content them.\n\nLady Bertram’s admiration was expressed, and strongly too. “It was really like being at a play,” said she. “I wish Sir Thomas had been here.”\n\nCrawford was excessively pleased. If Lady Bertram, with all her incompetency and languor, could feel this, the inference of what her niece, alive and enlightened as she was, must feel, was elevating.\n\n“You have a great turn for acting, I am sure, Mr. Crawford,” said her ladyship soon afterwards; “and I will tell you what, I think you will have a theatre, some time or other, at your house in Norfolk. I mean when you are settled there. I do indeed. I think you will fit up a theatre at your house in Norfolk.”\n\n“Do you, ma’am?” cried he, with quickness. “No, no, that will never be. Your ladyship is quite mistaken. No theatre at Everingham! Oh no!” And he looked at Fanny with an expressive smile, which evidently meant, “That lady will never allow a theatre at Everingham.”\n\nEdmund saw it all, and saw Fanny so determined not to see it, as to make it clear that the voice was enough to convey the full meaning of the protestation; and such a quick consciousness of compliment, such a ready comprehension of a hint, he thought, was rather favourable than not.\n\nThe subject of reading aloud was farther discussed. The two young men were the only talkers, but they, standing by the fire, talked over the too common neglect of the qualification, the total inattention to it, in the ordinary school-system for boys, the consequently natural, yet in some instances almost unnatural, degree of ignorance and uncouthness of men, of sensible and well-informed men, when suddenly called to the necessity of reading aloud, which had fallen within their notice, giving instances of blunders, and failures with their secondary causes, the want of management of the voice, of proper modulation and emphasis, of foresight and judgment, all proceeding from the first cause: want of early attention and habit; and Fanny was listening again with great entertainment.\n\n“Even in my profession,” said Edmund, with a smile, “how little the art of reading has been studied! how little a clear manner, and good delivery, have been attended to! I speak rather of the past, however, than the present. There is now a spirit of improvement abroad; but among those who were ordained twenty, thirty, forty years ago, the larger number, to judge by their performance, must have thought reading was reading, and preaching was preaching. It is different now. The subject is more justly considered. It is felt that distinctness and energy may have weight in recommending the most solid truths; and besides, there is more general observation and taste, a more critical knowledge diffused than formerly; in every congregation there is a larger proportion who know a little of the matter, and who can judge and criticise.”\n\nEdmund had already gone through the service once since his ordination; and upon this being understood, he had a variety of questions from Crawford as to his feelings and success; questions, which being made, though with the vivacity of friendly interest and quick taste, without any touch of that spirit of banter or air of levity which Edmund knew to be most offensive to Fanny, he had true pleasure in satisfying; and when Crawford proceeded to ask his opinion and give his own as to the properest manner in which particular passages in the service should be delivered, shewing it to be a subject on which he had thought before, and thought with judgment, Edmund was still more and more pleased. This would be the way to Fanny’s heart. She was not to be won by all that gallantry and wit and good-nature together could do; or, at least, she would not be won by them nearly so soon, without the assistance of sentiment and feeling, and seriousness on serious subjects.\n\n“Our liturgy,” observed Crawford, “has beauties, which not even a careless, slovenly style of reading can destroy; but it has also redundancies and repetitions which require good reading not to be felt. For myself, at least, I must confess being not always so attentive as I ought to be” (here was a glance at Fanny); “that nineteen times out of twenty I am thinking how such a prayer ought to be read, and longing to have it to read myself. Did you speak?” stepping eagerly to Fanny, and addressing her in a softened voice; and upon her saying “No,” he added, “Are you sure you did not speak? I saw your lips move. I fancied you might be going to tell me I ought to be more attentive, and not allow my thoughts to wander. Are not you going to tell me so?”\n\n“No, indeed, you know your duty too well for me to—even supposing—”\n\nShe stopt, felt herself getting into a puzzle, and could not be prevailed on to add another word, not by dint of several minutes of supplication and waiting. He then returned to his former station, and went on as if there had been no such tender interruption.\n\n“A sermon, well delivered, is more uncommon even than prayers well read. A sermon, good in itself, is no rare thing. It is more difficult to speak well than to compose well; that is, the rules and trick of composition are oftener an object of study. A thoroughly good sermon, thoroughly well delivered, is a capital gratification. I can never hear such a one without the greatest admiration and respect, and more than half a mind to take orders and preach myself. There is something in the eloquence of the pulpit, when it is really eloquence, which is entitled to the highest praise and honour. The preacher who can touch and affect such an heterogeneous mass of hearers, on subjects limited, and long worn threadbare in all common hands; who can say anything new or striking, anything that rouses the attention without offending the taste, or wearing out the feelings of his hearers, is a man whom one could not, in his public capacity, honour enough. I should like to be such a man.”\n\nEdmund laughed.\n\n“I should indeed. I never listened to a distinguished preacher in my life without a sort of envy. But then, I must have a London audience. I could not preach but to the educated; to those who were capable of estimating my composition. And I do not know that I should be fond of preaching often; now and then, perhaps once or twice in the spring, after being anxiously expected for half a dozen Sundays together; but not for a constancy; it would not do for a constancy.”\n\nHere Fanny, who could not but listen, involuntarily shook her head, and Crawford was instantly by her side again, entreating to know her meaning; and as Edmund perceived, by his drawing in a chair, and sitting down close by her, that it was to be a very thorough attack, that looks and undertones were to be well tried, he sank as quietly as possible into a corner, turned his back, and took up a newspaper, very sincerely wishing that dear little Fanny might be persuaded into explaining away that shake of the head to the satisfaction of her ardent lover; and as earnestly trying to bury every sound of the business from himself in murmurs of his own, over the various advertisements of “A most desirable Estate in South Wales”; “To Parents and Guardians”; and a “Capital season’d Hunter.”\n\nFanny, meanwhile, vexed with herself for not having been as motionless as she was speechless, and grieved to the heart to see Edmund’s arrangements, was trying by everything in the power of her modest, gentle nature, to repulse Mr. Crawford, and avoid both his looks and inquiries; and he, unrepulsable, was persisting in both.\n\n“What did that shake of the head mean?” said he. “What was it meant to express? Disapprobation, I fear. But of what? What had I been saying to displease you? Did you think me speaking improperly, lightly, irreverently on the subject? Only tell me if I was. Only tell me if I was wrong. I want to be set right. Nay, nay, I entreat you; for one moment put down your work. What did that shake of the head mean?”\n\nIn vain was her “Pray, sir, don’t; pray, Mr. Crawford,” repeated twice over; and in vain did she try to move away. In the same low, eager voice, and the same close neighbourhood, he went on, reurging the same questions as before. She grew more agitated and displeased.\n\n“How can you, sir? You quite astonish me; I wonder how you can—”\n\n“Do I astonish you?” said he. “Do you wonder? Is there anything in my present entreaty that you do not understand? I will explain to you instantly all that makes me urge you in this manner, all that gives me an interest in what you look and do, and excites my present curiosity. I will not leave you to wonder long.”\n\nIn spite of herself, she could not help half a smile, but she said nothing.\n\n“You shook your head at my acknowledging that I should not like to engage in the duties of a clergyman always for a constancy. Yes, that was the word. Constancy: I am not afraid of the word. I would spell it, read it, write it with anybody. I see nothing alarming in the word. Did you think I ought?”\n\n“Perhaps, sir,” said Fanny, wearied at last into speaking—“perhaps, sir, I thought it was a pity you did not always know yourself as well as you seemed to do at that moment.”\n\nCrawford, delighted to get her to speak at any rate, was determined to keep it up; and poor Fanny, who had hoped to silence him by such an extremity of reproof, found herself sadly mistaken, and that it was only a change from one object of curiosity and one set of words to another. He had always something to entreat the explanation of. The opportunity was too fair. None such had occurred since his seeing her in her uncle’s room, none such might occur again before his leaving Mansfield. Lady Bertram’s being just on the other side of the table was a trifle, for she might always be considered as only half-awake, and Edmund’s advertisements were still of the first utility.\n\n“Well,” said Crawford, after a course of rapid questions and reluctant answers; “I am happier than I was, because I now understand more clearly your opinion of me. You think me unsteady: easily swayed by the whim of the moment, easily tempted, easily put aside. With such an opinion, no wonder that—But we shall see.—It is not by protestations that I shall endeavour to convince you I am wronged; it is not by telling you that my affections are steady. My conduct shall speak for me; absence, distance, time shall speak for me. They shall prove that, as far as you can be deserved by anybody, I do deserve you. You are infinitely my superior in merit; all that I know. You have qualities which I had not before supposed to exist in such a degree in any human creature. You have some touches of the angel in you beyond what—not merely beyond what one sees, because one never sees anything like it—but beyond what one fancies might be. But still I am not frightened. It is not by equality of merit that you can be won. That is out of the question. It is he who sees and worships your merit the strongest, who loves you most devotedly, that has the best right to a return. There I build my confidence. By that right I do and will deserve you; and when once convinced that my attachment is what I declare it, I know you too well not to entertain the warmest hopes. Yes, dearest, sweetest Fanny. Nay” (seeing her draw back displeased), “forgive me. Perhaps I have as yet no right; but by what other name can I call you? Do you suppose you are ever present to my imagination under any other? No, it is ‘Fanny’ that I think of all day, and dream of all night. You have given the name such reality of sweetness, that nothing else can now be descriptive of you.”\n\nFanny could hardly have kept her seat any longer, or have refrained from at least trying to get away in spite of all the too public opposition she foresaw to it, had it not been for the sound of approaching relief, the very sound which she had been long watching for, and long thinking strangely delayed.\n\nThe solemn procession, headed by Baddeley, of tea-board, urn, and cake-bearers, made its appearance, and delivered her from a grievous imprisonment of body and mind. Mr. Crawford was obliged to move. She was at liberty, she was busy, she was protected.\n\nEdmund was not sorry to be admitted again among the number of those who might speak and hear. But though the conference had seemed full long to him, and though on looking at Fanny he saw rather a flush of vexation, he inclined to hope that so much could not have been said and listened to without some profit to the speaker.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXV\n\nEdmund had determined that it belonged entirely to Fanny to chuse whether her situation with regard to Crawford should be mentioned between them or not; and that if she did not lead the way, it should never be touched on by him; but after a day or two of mutual reserve, he was induced by his father to change his mind, and try what his influence might do for his friend.\n\nA day, and a very early day, was actually fixed for the Crawfords’ departure; and Sir Thomas thought it might be as well to make one more effort for the young man before he left Mansfield, that all his professions and vows of unshaken attachment might have as much hope to sustain them as possible.\n\nSir Thomas was most cordially anxious for the perfection of Mr. Crawford’s character in that point. He wished him to be a model of constancy; and fancied the best means of effecting it would be by not trying him too long.\n\nEdmund was not unwilling to be persuaded to engage in the business; he wanted to know Fanny’s feelings. She had been used to consult him in every difficulty, and he loved her too well to bear to be denied her confidence now; he hoped to be of service to her, he thought he must be of service to her; whom else had she to open her heart to? If she did not need counsel, she must need the comfort of communication. Fanny estranged from him, silent and reserved, was an unnatural state of things; a state which he must break through, and which he could easily learn to think she was wanting him to break through.\n\n“I will speak to her, sir: I will take the first opportunity of speaking to her alone,” was the result of such thoughts as these; and upon Sir Thomas’s information of her being at that very time walking alone in the shrubbery, he instantly joined her.\n\n“I am come to walk with you, Fanny,” said he. “Shall I?” Drawing her arm within his. “It is a long while since we have had a comfortable walk together.”\n\nShe assented to it all rather by look than word. Her spirits were low.\n\n“But, Fanny,” he presently added, “in order to have a comfortable walk, something more is necessary than merely pacing this gravel together. You must talk to me. I know you have something on your mind. I know what you are thinking of. You cannot suppose me uninformed. Am I to hear of it from everybody but Fanny herself?”\n\nFanny, at once agitated and dejected, replied, “If you hear of it from everybody, cousin, there can be nothing for me to tell.”\n\n“Not of facts, perhaps; but of feelings, Fanny. No one but you can tell me them. I do not mean to press you, however. If it is not what you wish yourself, I have done. I had thought it might be a relief.”\n\n“I am afraid we think too differently for me to find any relief in talking of what I feel.”\n\n“Do you suppose that we think differently? I have no idea of it. I dare say that, on a comparison of our opinions, they would be found as much alike as they have been used to be: to the point—I consider Crawford’s proposals as most advantageous and desirable, if you could return his affection. I consider it as most natural that all your family should wish you could return it; but that, as you cannot, you have done exactly as you ought in refusing him. Can there be any disagreement between us here?”\n\n“Oh no! But I thought you blamed me. I thought you were against me. This is such a comfort!”\n\n“This comfort you might have had sooner, Fanny, had you sought it. But how could you possibly suppose me against you? How could you imagine me an advocate for marriage without love? Were I even careless in general on such matters, how could you imagine me so where your happiness was at stake?”\n\n“My uncle thought me wrong, and I knew he had been talking to you.”\n\n“As far as you have gone, Fanny, I think you perfectly right. I may be sorry, I may be surprised—though hardly that, for you had not had time to attach yourself—but I think you perfectly right. Can it admit of a question? It is disgraceful to us if it does. You did not love him; nothing could have justified your accepting him.”\n\nFanny had not felt so comfortable for days and days.\n\n“So far your conduct has been faultless, and they were quite mistaken who wished you to do otherwise. But the matter does not end here. Crawford’s is no common attachment; he perseveres, with the hope of creating that regard which had not been created before. This, we know, must be a work of time. But” (with an affectionate smile) “let him succeed at last, Fanny, let him succeed at last. You have proved yourself upright and disinterested, prove yourself grateful and tender-hearted; and then you will be the perfect model of a woman which I have always believed you born for.”\n\n“Oh! never, never, never! he never will succeed with me.” And she spoke with a warmth which quite astonished Edmund, and which she blushed at the recollection of herself, when she saw his look, and heard him reply, “Never! Fanny!—so very determined and positive! This is not like yourself, your rational self.”\n\n“I mean,” she cried, sorrowfully correcting herself, “that I think I never shall, as far as the future can be answered for; I think I never shall return his regard.”\n\n“I must hope better things. I am aware, more aware than Crawford can be, that the man who means to make you love him (you having due notice of his intentions) must have very uphill work, for there are all your early attachments and habits in battle array; and before he can get your heart for his own use he has to unfasten it from all the holds upon things animate and inanimate, which so many years’ growth have confirmed, and which are considerably tightened for the moment by the very idea of separation. I know that the apprehension of being forced to quit Mansfield will for a time be arming you against him. I wish he had not been obliged to tell you what he was trying for. I wish he had known you as well as I do, Fanny. Between us, I think we should have won you. My theoretical and his practical knowledge together could not have failed. He should have worked upon my plans. I must hope, however, that time, proving him (as I firmly believe it will) to deserve you by his steady affection, will give him his reward. I cannot suppose that you have not the wish to love him—the natural wish of gratitude. You must have some feeling of that sort. You must be sorry for your own indifference.”\n\n“We are so totally unlike,” said Fanny, avoiding a direct answer, “we are so very, very different in all our inclinations and ways, that I consider it as quite impossible we should ever be tolerably happy together, even if I could like him. There never were two people more dissimilar. We have not one taste in common. We should be miserable.”\n\n“You are mistaken, Fanny. The dissimilarity is not so strong. You are quite enough alike. You have tastes in common. You have moral and literary tastes in common. You have both warm hearts and benevolent feelings; and, Fanny, who that heard him read, and saw you listen to Shakespeare the other night, will think you unfitted as companions? You forget yourself: there is a decided difference in your tempers, I allow. He is lively, you are serious; but so much the better: his spirits will support yours. It is your disposition to be easily dejected and to fancy difficulties greater than they are. His cheerfulness will counteract this. He sees difficulties nowhere: and his pleasantness and gaiety will be a constant support to you. Your being so far unlike, Fanny, does not in the smallest degree make against the probability of your happiness together: do not imagine it. I am myself convinced that it is rather a favourable circumstance. I am perfectly persuaded that the tempers had better be unlike: I mean unlike in the flow of the spirits, in the manners, in the inclination for much or little company, in the propensity to talk or to be silent, to be grave or to be gay. Some opposition here is, I am thoroughly convinced, friendly to matrimonial happiness. I exclude extremes, of course; and a very close resemblance in all those points would be the likeliest way to produce an extreme. A counteraction, gentle and continual, is the best safeguard of manners and conduct.”\n\nFull well could Fanny guess where his thoughts were now: Miss Crawford’s power was all returning. He had been speaking of her cheerfully from the hour of his coming home. His avoiding her was quite at an end. He had dined at the Parsonage only the preceding day.\n\nAfter leaving him to his happier thoughts for some minutes, Fanny, feeling it due to herself, returned to Mr. Crawford, and said, “It is not merely in temper that I consider him as totally unsuited to myself; though, in that respect, I think the difference between us too great, infinitely too great: his spirits often oppress me; but there is something in him which I object to still more. I must say, cousin, that I cannot approve his character. I have not thought well of him from the time of the play. I then saw him behaving, as it appeared to me, so very improperly and unfeelingly—I may speak of it now because it is all over—so improperly by poor Mr. Rushworth, not seeming to care how he exposed or hurt him, and paying attentions to my cousin Maria, which—in short, at the time of the play, I received an impression which will never be got over.”\n\n“My dear Fanny,” replied Edmund, scarcely hearing her to the end, “let us not, any of us, be judged by what we appeared at that period of general folly. The time of the play is a time which I hate to recollect. Maria was wrong, Crawford was wrong, we were all wrong together; but none so wrong as myself. Compared with me, all the rest were blameless. I was playing the fool with my eyes open.”\n\n“As a bystander,” said Fanny, “perhaps I saw more than you did; and I do think that Mr. Rushworth was sometimes very jealous.”\n\n“Very possibly. No wonder. Nothing could be more improper than the whole business. I am shocked whenever I think that Maria could be capable of it; but, if she could undertake the part, we must not be surprised at the rest.”\n\n“Before the play, I am much mistaken if Julia did not think he was paying her attentions.”\n\n“Julia! I have heard before from some one of his being in love with Julia; but I could never see anything of it. And, Fanny, though I hope I do justice to my sisters’ good qualities, I think it very possible that they might, one or both, be more desirous of being admired by Crawford, and might shew that desire rather more unguardedly than was perfectly prudent. I can remember that they were evidently fond of his society; and with such encouragement, a man like Crawford, lively, and it may be, a little unthinking, might be led on to—there could be nothing very striking, because it is clear that he had no pretensions: his heart was reserved for you. And I must say, that its being for you has raised him inconceivably in my opinion. It does him the highest honour; it shews his proper estimation of the blessing of domestic happiness and pure attachment. It proves him unspoilt by his uncle. It proves him, in short, everything that I had been used to wish to believe him, and feared he was not.”\n\n“I am persuaded that he does not think, as he ought, on serious subjects.”\n\n“Say, rather, that he has not thought at all upon serious subjects, which I believe to be a good deal the case. How could it be otherwise, with such an education and adviser? Under the disadvantages, indeed, which both have had, is it not wonderful that they should be what they are? Crawford’s feelings, I am ready to acknowledge, have hitherto been too much his guides. Happily, those feelings have generally been good. You will supply the rest; and a most fortunate man he is to attach himself to such a creature—to a woman who, firm as a rock in her own principles, has a gentleness of character so well adapted to recommend them. He has chosen his partner, indeed, with rare felicity. He will make you happy, Fanny; I know he will make you happy; but you will make him everything.”\n\n“I would not engage in such a charge,” cried Fanny, in a shrinking accent; “in such an office of high responsibility!”\n\n“As usual, believing yourself unequal to anything! fancying everything too much for you! Well, though I may not be able to persuade you into different feelings, you will be persuaded into them, I trust. I confess myself sincerely anxious that you may. I have no common interest in Crawford’s well-doing. Next to your happiness, Fanny, his has the first claim on me. You are aware of my having no common interest in Crawford.”\n\nFanny was too well aware of it to have anything to say; and they walked on together some fifty yards in mutual silence and abstraction. Edmund first began again—\n\n“I was very much pleased by her manner of speaking of it yesterday, particularly pleased, because I had not depended upon her seeing everything in so just a light. I knew she was very fond of you; but yet I was afraid of her not estimating your worth to her brother quite as it deserved, and of her regretting that he had not rather fixed on some woman of distinction or fortune. I was afraid of the bias of those worldly maxims, which she has been too much used to hear. But it was very different. She spoke of you, Fanny, just as she ought. She desires the connexion as warmly as your uncle or myself. We had a long talk about it. I should not have mentioned the subject, though very anxious to know her sentiments; but I had not been in the room five minutes before she began introducing it with all that openness of heart, and sweet peculiarity of manner, that spirit and ingenuousness which are so much a part of herself. Mrs. Grant laughed at her for her rapidity.”\n\n“Was Mrs. Grant in the room, then?”\n\n“Yes, when I reached the house I found the two sisters together by themselves; and when once we had begun, we had not done with you, Fanny, till Crawford and Dr. Grant came in.”\n\n“It is above a week since I saw Miss Crawford.”\n\n“Yes, she laments it; yet owns it may have been best. You will see her, however, before she goes. She is very angry with you, Fanny; you must be prepared for that. She calls herself very angry, but you can imagine her anger. It is the regret and disappointment of a sister, who thinks her brother has a right to everything he may wish for, at the first moment. She is hurt, as you would be for William; but she loves and esteems you with all her heart.”\n\n“I knew she would be very angry with me.”\n\n“My dearest Fanny,” cried Edmund, pressing her arm closer to him, “do not let the idea of her anger distress you. It is anger to be talked of rather than felt. Her heart is made for love and kindness, not for resentment. I wish you could have overheard her tribute of praise; I wish you could have seen her countenance, when she said that you should be Henry’s wife. And I observed that she always spoke of you as ‘Fanny,’ which she was never used to do; and it had a sound of most sisterly cordiality.”\n\n“And Mrs. Grant, did she say—did she speak; was she there all the time?”\n\n“Yes, she was agreeing exactly with her sister. The surprise of your refusal, Fanny, seems to have been unbounded. That you could refuse such a man as Henry Crawford seems more than they can understand. I said what I could for you; but in good truth, as they stated the case—you must prove yourself to be in your senses as soon as you can by a different conduct; nothing else will satisfy them. But this is teasing you. I have done. Do not turn away from me.”\n\n“I should have thought,” said Fanny, after a pause of recollection and exertion, “that every woman must have felt the possibility of a man’s not being approved, not being loved by some one of her sex at least, let him be ever so generally agreeable. Let him have all the perfections in the world, I think it ought not to be set down as certain that a man must be acceptable to every woman he may happen to like himself. But, even supposing it is so, allowing Mr. Crawford to have all the claims which his sisters think he has, how was I to be prepared to meet him with any feeling answerable to his own? He took me wholly by surprise. I had not an idea that his behaviour to me before had any meaning; and surely I was not to be teaching myself to like him only because he was taking what seemed very idle notice of me. In my situation, it would have been the extreme of vanity to be forming expectations on Mr. Crawford. I am sure his sisters, rating him as they do, must have thought it so, supposing he had meant nothing. How, then, was I to be—to be in love with him the moment he said he was with me? How was I to have an attachment at his service, as soon as it was asked for? His sisters should consider me as well as him. The higher his deserts, the more improper for me ever to have thought of him. And, and—we think very differently of the nature of women, if they can imagine a woman so very soon capable of returning an affection as this seems to imply.”\n\n“My dear, dear Fanny, now I have the truth. I know this to be the truth; and most worthy of you are such feelings. I had attributed them to you before. I thought I could understand you. You have now given exactly the explanation which I ventured to make for you to your friend and Mrs. Grant, and they were both better satisfied, though your warm-hearted friend was still run away with a little by the enthusiasm of her fondness for Henry. I told them that you were of all human creatures the one over whom habit had most power and novelty least; and that the very circumstance of the novelty of Crawford’s addresses was against him. Their being so new and so recent was all in their disfavour; that you could tolerate nothing that you were not used to; and a great deal more to the same purpose, to give them a knowledge of your character. Miss Crawford made us laugh by her plans of encouragement for her brother. She meant to urge him to persevere in the hope of being loved in time, and of having his addresses most kindly received at the end of about ten years’ happy marriage.”\n\nFanny could with difficulty give the smile that was here asked for. Her feelings were all in revolt. She feared she had been doing wrong: saying too much, overacting the caution which she had been fancying necessary; in guarding against one evil, laying herself open to another; and to have Miss Crawford’s liveliness repeated to her at such a moment, and on such a subject, was a bitter aggravation.\n\nEdmund saw weariness and distress in her face, and immediately resolved to forbear all farther discussion; and not even to mention the name of Crawford again, except as it might be connected with what must be agreeable to her. On this principle, he soon afterwards observed—“They go on Monday. You are sure, therefore, of seeing your friend either to-morrow or Sunday. They really go on Monday; and I was within a trifle of being persuaded to stay at Lessingby till that very day! I had almost promised it. What a difference it might have made! Those five or six days more at Lessingby might have been felt all my life.”\n\n“You were near staying there?”\n\n“Very. I was most kindly pressed, and had nearly consented. Had I received any letter from Mansfield, to tell me how you were all going on, I believe I should certainly have staid; but I knew nothing that had happened here for a fortnight, and felt that I had been away long enough.”\n\n“You spent your time pleasantly there?”\n\n“Yes; that is, it was the fault of my own mind if I did not. They were all very pleasant. I doubt their finding me so. I took uneasiness with me, and there was no getting rid of it till I was in Mansfield again.”\n\n“The Miss Owens—you liked them, did not you?”\n\n“Yes, very well. Pleasant, good-humoured, unaffected girls. But I am spoilt, Fanny, for common female society. Good-humoured, unaffected girls will not do for a man who has been used to sensible women. They are two distinct orders of being. You and Miss Crawford have made me too nice.”\n\nStill, however, Fanny was oppressed and wearied; he saw it in her looks, it could not be talked away; and attempting it no more, he led her directly, with the kind authority of a privileged guardian, into the house.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVI\n\nEdmund now believed himself perfectly acquainted with all that Fanny could tell, or could leave to be conjectured of her sentiments, and he was satisfied. It had been, as he before presumed, too hasty a measure on Crawford’s side, and time must be given to make the idea first familiar, and then agreeable to her. She must be used to the consideration of his being in love with her, and then a return of affection might not be very distant.\n\nHe gave this opinion as the result of the conversation to his father; and recommended there being nothing more said to her: no farther attempts to influence or persuade; but that everything should be left to Crawford’s assiduities, and the natural workings of her own mind.\n\nSir Thomas promised that it should be so. Edmund’s account of Fanny’s disposition he could believe to be just; he supposed she had all those feelings, but he must consider it as very unfortunate that she had; for, less willing than his son to trust to the future, he could not help fearing that if such very long allowances of time and habit were necessary for her, she might not have persuaded herself into receiving his addresses properly before the young man’s inclination for paying them were over. There was nothing to be done, however, but to submit quietly and hope the best.\n\nThe promised visit from “her friend,” as Edmund called Miss Crawford, was a formidable threat to Fanny, and she lived in continual terror of it. As a sister, so partial and so angry, and so little scrupulous of what she said, and in another light so triumphant and secure, she was in every way an object of painful alarm. Her displeasure, her penetration, and her happiness were all fearful to encounter; and the dependence of having others present when they met was Fanny’s only support in looking forward to it. She absented herself as little as possible from Lady Bertram, kept away from the East room, and took no solitary walk in the shrubbery, in her caution to avoid any sudden attack.\n\nShe succeeded. She was safe in the breakfast-room, with her aunt, when Miss Crawford did come; and the first misery over, and Miss Crawford looking and speaking with much less particularity of expression than she had anticipated, Fanny began to hope there would be nothing worse to be endured than a half-hour of moderate agitation. But here she hoped too much; Miss Crawford was not the slave of opportunity. She was determined to see Fanny alone, and therefore said to her tolerably soon, in a low voice, “I must speak to you for a few minutes somewhere”; words that Fanny felt all over her, in all her pulses and all her nerves. Denial was impossible. Her habits of ready submission, on the contrary, made her almost instantly rise and lead the way out of the room. She did it with wretched feelings, but it was inevitable.\n\nThey were no sooner in the hall than all restraint of countenance was over on Miss Crawford’s side. She immediately shook her head at Fanny with arch, yet affectionate reproach, and taking her hand, seemed hardly able to help beginning directly. She said nothing, however, but, “Sad, sad girl! I do not know when I shall have done scolding you,” and had discretion enough to reserve the rest till they might be secure of having four walls to themselves. Fanny naturally turned upstairs, and took her guest to the apartment which was now always fit for comfortable use; opening the door, however, with a most aching heart, and feeling that she had a more distressing scene before her than ever that spot had yet witnessed. But the evil ready to burst on her was at least delayed by the sudden change in Miss Crawford’s ideas; by the strong effect on her mind which the finding herself in the East room again produced.\n\n“Ha!” she cried, with instant animation, “am I here again? The East room! Once only was I in this room before”; and after stopping to look about her, and seemingly to retrace all that had then passed, she added, “Once only before. Do you remember it? I came to rehearse. Your cousin came too; and we had a rehearsal. You were our audience and prompter. A delightful rehearsal. I shall never forget it. Here we were, just in this part of the room: here was your cousin, here was I, here were the chairs. Oh! why will such things ever pass away?”\n\nHappily for her companion, she wanted no answer. Her mind was entirely self-engrossed. She was in a reverie of sweet remembrances.\n\n“The scene we were rehearsing was so very remarkable! The subject of it so very—very—what shall I say? He was to be describing and recommending matrimony to me. I think I see him now, trying to be as demure and composed as Anhalt ought, through the two long speeches. ‘When two sympathetic hearts meet in the marriage state, matrimony may be called a happy life.’ I suppose no time can ever wear out the impression I have of his looks and voice as he said those words. It was curious, very curious, that we should have such a scene to play! If I had the power of recalling any one week of my existence, it should be that week—that acting week. Say what you would, Fanny, it should be that; for I never knew such exquisite happiness in any other. His sturdy spirit to bend as it did! Oh! it was sweet beyond expression. But alas, that very evening destroyed it all. That very evening brought your most unwelcome uncle. Poor Sir Thomas, who was glad to see you? Yet, Fanny, do not imagine I would now speak disrespectfully of Sir Thomas, though I certainly did hate him for many a week. No, I do him justice now. He is just what the head of such a family should be. Nay, in sober sadness, I believe I now love you all.” And having said so, with a degree of tenderness and consciousness which Fanny had never seen in her before, and now thought only too becoming, she turned away for a moment to recover herself. “I have had a little fit since I came into this room, as you may perceive,” said she presently, with a playful smile, “but it is over now; so let us sit down and be comfortable; for as to scolding you, Fanny, which I came fully intending to do, I have not the heart for it when it comes to the point.” And embracing her very affectionately, “Good, gentle Fanny! when I think of this being the last time of seeing you for I do not know how long, I feel it quite impossible to do anything but love you.”\n\nFanny was affected. She had not foreseen anything of this, and her feelings could seldom withstand the melancholy influence of the word “last.” She cried as if she had loved Miss Crawford more than she possibly could; and Miss Crawford, yet farther softened by the sight of such emotion, hung about her with fondness, and said, “I hate to leave you. I shall see no one half so amiable where I am going. Who says we shall not be sisters? I know we shall. I feel that we are born to be connected; and those tears convince me that you feel it too, dear Fanny.”\n\nFanny roused herself, and replying only in part, said, “But you are only going from one set of friends to another. You are going to a very particular friend.”\n\n“Yes, very true. Mrs. Fraser has been my intimate friend for years. But I have not the least inclination to go near her. I can think only of the friends I am leaving: my excellent sister, yourself, and the Bertrams in general. You have all so much more heart among you than one finds in the world at large. You all give me a feeling of being able to trust and confide in you, which in common intercourse one knows nothing of. I wish I had settled with Mrs. Fraser not to go to her till after Easter, a much better time for the visit, but now I cannot put her off. And when I have done with her I must go to her sister, Lady Stornaway, because she was rather my most particular friend of the two, but I have not cared much for her these three years.”\n\nAfter this speech the two girls sat many minutes silent, each thoughtful: Fanny meditating on the different sorts of friendship in the world, Mary on something of less philosophic tendency. She first spoke again.\n\n“How perfectly I remember my resolving to look for you upstairs, and setting off to find my way to the East room, without having an idea whereabouts it was! How well I remember what I was thinking of as I came along, and my looking in and seeing you here sitting at this table at work; and then your cousin’s astonishment, when he opened the door, at seeing me here! To be sure, your uncle’s returning that very evening! There never was anything quite like it.”\n\nAnother short fit of abstraction followed, when, shaking it off, she thus attacked her companion.\n\n“Why, Fanny, you are absolutely in a reverie. Thinking, I hope, of one who is always thinking of you. Oh! that I could transport you for a short time into our circle in town, that you might understand how your power over Henry is thought of there! Oh! the envyings and heartburnings of dozens and dozens; the wonder, the incredulity that will be felt at hearing what you have done! For as to secrecy, Henry is quite the hero of an old romance, and glories in his chains. You should come to London to know how to estimate your conquest. If you were to see how he is courted, and how I am courted for his sake! Now, I am well aware that I shall not be half so welcome to Mrs. Fraser in consequence of his situation with you. When she comes to know the truth she will, very likely, wish me in Northamptonshire again; for there is a daughter of Mr. Fraser, by a first wife, whom she is wild to get married, and wants Henry to take. Oh! she has been trying for him to such a degree. Innocent and quiet as you sit here, you cannot have an idea of the sensation that you will be occasioning, of the curiosity there will be to see you, of the endless questions I shall have to answer! Poor Margaret Fraser will be at me for ever about your eyes and your teeth, and how you do your hair, and who makes your shoes. I wish Margaret were married, for my poor friend’s sake, for I look upon the Frasers to be about as unhappy as most other married people. And yet it was a most desirable match for Janet at the time. We were all delighted. She could not do otherwise than accept him, for he was rich, and she had nothing; but he turns out ill-tempered and exigeant, and wants a young woman, a beautiful young woman of five-and-twenty, to be as steady as himself. And my friend does not manage him well; she does not seem to know how to make the best of it. There is a spirit of irritation which, to say nothing worse, is certainly very ill-bred. In their house I shall call to mind the conjugal manners of Mansfield Parsonage with respect. Even Dr. Grant does shew a thorough confidence in my sister, and a certain consideration for her judgment, which makes one feel there is attachment; but of that I shall see nothing with the Frasers. I shall be at Mansfield for ever, Fanny. My own sister as a wife, Sir Thomas Bertram as a husband, are my standards of perfection. Poor Janet has been sadly taken in, and yet there was nothing improper on her side: she did not run into the match inconsiderately; there was no want of foresight. She took three days to consider of his proposals, and during those three days asked the advice of everybody connected with her whose opinion was worth having, and especially applied to my late dear aunt, whose knowledge of the world made her judgment very generally and deservedly looked up to by all the young people of her acquaintance, and she was decidedly in favour of Mr. Fraser. This seems as if nothing were a security for matrimonial comfort. I have not so much to say for my friend Flora, who jilted a very nice young man in the Blues for the sake of that horrid Lord Stornaway, who has about as much sense, Fanny, as Mr. Rushworth, but much worse-looking, and with a blackguard character. I had my doubts at the time about her being right, for he has not even the air of a gentleman, and now I am sure she was wrong. By the bye, Flora Ross was dying for Henry the first winter she came out. But were I to attempt to tell you of all the women whom I have known to be in love with him, I should never have done. It is you, only you, insensible Fanny, who can think of him with anything like indifference. But are you so insensible as you profess yourself? No, no, I see you are not.”\n\nThere was, indeed, so deep a blush over Fanny’s face at that moment as might warrant strong suspicion in a predisposed mind.\n\n“Excellent creature! I will not tease you. Everything shall take its course. But, dear Fanny, you must allow that you were not so absolutely unprepared to have the question asked as your cousin fancies. It is not possible but that you must have had some thoughts on the subject, some surmises as to what might be. You must have seen that he was trying to please you by every attention in his power. Was not he devoted to you at the ball? And then before the ball, the necklace! Oh! you received it just as it was meant. You were as conscious as heart could desire. I remember it perfectly.”\n\n“Do you mean, then, that your brother knew of the necklace beforehand? Oh! Miss Crawford, that was not fair.”\n\n“Knew of it! It was his own doing entirely, his own thought. I am ashamed to say that it had never entered my head, but I was delighted to act on his proposal for both your sakes.”\n\n“I will not say,” replied Fanny, “that I was not half afraid at the time of its being so, for there was something in your look that frightened me, but not at first; I was as unsuspicious of it at first—indeed, indeed I was. It is as true as that I sit here. And had I had an idea of it, nothing should have induced me to accept the necklace. As to your brother’s behaviour, certainly I was sensible of a particularity: I had been sensible of it some little time, perhaps two or three weeks; but then I considered it as meaning nothing: I put it down as simply being his way, and was as far from supposing as from wishing him to have any serious thoughts of me. I had not, Miss Crawford, been an inattentive observer of what was passing between him and some part of this family in the summer and autumn. I was quiet, but I was not blind. I could not but see that Mr. Crawford amused himself in gallantries which did mean nothing.”\n\n“Ah! I cannot deny it. He has now and then been a sad flirt, and cared very little for the havoc he might be making in young ladies’ affections. I have often scolded him for it, but it is his only fault; and there is this to be said, that very few young ladies have any affections worth caring for. And then, Fanny, the glory of fixing one who has been shot at by so many; of having it in one’s power to pay off the debts of one’s sex! Oh! I am sure it is not in woman’s nature to refuse such a triumph.”\n\nFanny shook her head. “I cannot think well of a man who sports with any woman’s feelings; and there may often be a great deal more suffered than a stander-by can judge of.”\n\n“I do not defend him. I leave him entirely to your mercy, and when he has got you at Everingham, I do not care how much you lecture him. But this I will say, that his fault, the liking to make girls a little in love with him, is not half so dangerous to a wife’s happiness as a tendency to fall in love himself, which he has never been addicted to. And I do seriously and truly believe that he is attached to you in a way that he never was to any woman before; that he loves you with all his heart, and will love you as nearly for ever as possible. If any man ever loved a woman for ever, I think Henry will do as much for you.”\n\nFanny could not avoid a faint smile, but had nothing to say.\n\n“I cannot imagine Henry ever to have been happier,” continued Mary presently, “than when he had succeeded in getting your brother’s commission.”\n\nShe had made a sure push at Fanny’s feelings here.\n\n“Oh! yes. How very, very kind of him.”\n\n“I know he must have exerted himself very much, for I know the parties he had to move. The Admiral hates trouble, and scorns asking favours; and there are so many young men’s claims to be attended to in the same way, that a friendship and energy, not very determined, is easily put by. What a happy creature William must be! I wish we could see him.”\n\nPoor Fanny’s mind was thrown into the most distressing of all its varieties. The recollection of what had been done for William was always the most powerful disturber of every decision against Mr. Crawford; and she sat thinking deeply of it till Mary, who had been first watching her complacently, and then musing on something else, suddenly called her attention by saying: “I should like to sit talking with you here all day, but we must not forget the ladies below, and so good-bye, my dear, my amiable, my excellent Fanny, for though we shall nominally part in the breakfast-parlour, I must take leave of you here. And I do take leave, longing for a happy reunion, and trusting that when we meet again, it will be under circumstances which may open our hearts to each other without any remnant or shadow of reserve.”\n\nA very, very kind embrace, and some agitation of manner, accompanied these words.\n\n“I shall see your cousin in town soon: he talks of being there tolerably soon; and Sir Thomas, I dare say, in the course of the spring; and your eldest cousin, and the Rushworths, and Julia, I am sure of meeting again and again, and all but you. I have two favours to ask, Fanny: one is your correspondence. You must write to me. And the other, that you will often call on Mrs. Grant, and make her amends for my being gone.”\n\nThe first, at least, of these favours Fanny would rather not have been asked; but it was impossible for her to refuse the correspondence; it was impossible for her even not to accede to it more readily than her own judgment authorised. There was no resisting so much apparent affection. Her disposition was peculiarly calculated to value a fond treatment, and from having hitherto known so little of it, she was the more overcome by Miss Crawford’s. Besides, there was gratitude towards her, for having made their tête-à-tête so much less painful than her fears had predicted.\n\nIt was over, and she had escaped without reproaches and without detection. Her secret was still her own; and while that was the case, she thought she could resign herself to almost everything.\n\nIn the evening there was another parting. Henry Crawford came and sat some time with them; and her spirits not being previously in the strongest state, her heart was softened for a while towards him, because he really seemed to feel. Quite unlike his usual self, he scarcely said anything. He was evidently oppressed, and Fanny must grieve for him, though hoping she might never see him again till he were the husband of some other woman.\n\nWhen it came to the moment of parting, he would take her hand, he would not be denied it; he said nothing, however, or nothing that she heard, and when he had left the room, she was better pleased that such a token of friendship had passed.\n\nOn the morrow the Crawfords were gone.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVII\n\nMr. Crawford gone, Sir Thomas’s next object was that he should be missed; and he entertained great hope that his niece would find a blank in the loss of those attentions which at the time she had felt, or fancied, an evil. She had tasted of consequence in its most flattering form; and he did hope that the loss of it, the sinking again into nothing, would awaken very wholesome regrets in her mind. He watched her with this idea; but he could hardly tell with what success. He hardly knew whether there were any difference in her spirits or not. She was always so gentle and retiring that her emotions were beyond his discrimination. He did not understand her: he felt that he did not; and therefore applied to Edmund to tell him how she stood affected on the present occasion, and whether she were more or less happy than she had been.\n\nEdmund did not discern any symptoms of regret, and thought his father a little unreasonable in supposing the first three or four days could produce any.\n\nWhat chiefly surprised Edmund was, that Crawford’s sister, the friend and companion who had been so much to her, should not be more visibly regretted. He wondered that Fanny spoke so seldom of her, and had so little voluntarily to say of her concern at this separation.\n\nAlas! it was this sister, this friend and companion, who was now the chief bane of Fanny’s comfort. If she could have believed Mary’s future fate as unconnected with Mansfield as she was determined the brother’s should be, if she could have hoped her return thither to be as distant as she was much inclined to think his, she would have been light of heart indeed; but the more she recollected and observed, the more deeply was she convinced that everything was now in a fairer train for Miss Crawford’s marrying Edmund than it had ever been before. On his side the inclination was stronger, on hers less equivocal. His objections, the scruples of his integrity, seemed all done away, nobody could tell how; and the doubts and hesitations of her ambition were equally got over—and equally without apparent reason. It could only be imputed to increasing attachment. His good and her bad feelings yielded to love, and such love must unite them. He was to go to town as soon as some business relative to Thornton Lacey were completed—perhaps within a fortnight; he talked of going, he loved to talk of it; and when once with her again, Fanny could not doubt the rest. Her acceptance must be as certain as his offer; and yet there were bad feelings still remaining which made the prospect of it most sorrowful to her, independently, she believed, independently of self.\n\nIn their very last conversation, Miss Crawford, in spite of some amiable sensations, and much personal kindness, had still been Miss Crawford; still shewn a mind led astray and bewildered, and without any suspicion of being so; darkened, yet fancying itself light. She might love, but she did not deserve Edmund by any other sentiment. Fanny believed there was scarcely a second feeling in common between them; and she may be forgiven by older sages for looking on the chance of Miss Crawford’s future improvement as nearly desperate, for thinking that if Edmund’s influence in this season of love had already done so little in clearing her judgment, and regulating her notions, his worth would be finally wasted on her even in years of matrimony.\n\nExperience might have hoped more for any young people so circumstanced, and impartiality would not have denied to Miss Crawford’s nature that participation of the general nature of women which would lead her to adopt the opinions of the man she loved and respected as her own. But as such were Fanny’s persuasions, she suffered very much from them, and could never speak of Miss Crawford without pain.\n\nSir Thomas, meanwhile, went on with his own hopes and his own observations, still feeling a right, by all his knowledge of human nature, to expect to see the effect of the loss of power and consequence on his niece’s spirits, and the past attentions of the lover producing a craving for their return; and he was soon afterwards able to account for his not yet completely and indubitably seeing all this, by the prospect of another visitor, whose approach he could allow to be quite enough to support the spirits he was watching. William had obtained a ten days’ leave of absence, to be given to Northamptonshire, and was coming, the happiest of lieutenants, because the latest made, to shew his happiness and describe his uniform.\n\nHe came; and he would have been delighted to shew his uniform there too, had not cruel custom prohibited its appearance except on duty. So the uniform remained at Portsmouth, and Edmund conjectured that before Fanny had any chance of seeing it, all its own freshness and all the freshness of its wearer’s feelings must be worn away. It would be sunk into a badge of disgrace; for what can be more unbecoming, or more worthless, than the uniform of a lieutenant, who has been a lieutenant a year or two, and sees others made commanders before him? So reasoned Edmund, till his father made him the confidant of a scheme which placed Fanny’s chance of seeing the second lieutenant of H.M.S. Thrush in all his glory in another light.\n\nThis scheme was that she should accompany her brother back to Portsmouth, and spend a little time with her own family. It had occurred to Sir Thomas, in one of his dignified musings, as a right and desirable measure; but before he absolutely made up his mind, he consulted his son. Edmund considered it every way, and saw nothing but what was right. The thing was good in itself, and could not be done at a better time; and he had no doubt of it being highly agreeable to Fanny. This was enough to determine Sir Thomas; and a decisive “then so it shall be” closed that stage of the business; Sir Thomas retiring from it with some feelings of satisfaction, and views of good over and above what he had communicated to his son; for his prime motive in sending her away had very little to do with the propriety of her seeing her parents again, and nothing at all with any idea of making her happy. He certainly wished her to go willingly, but he as certainly wished her to be heartily sick of home before her visit ended; and that a little abstinence from the elegancies and luxuries of Mansfield Park would bring her mind into a sober state, and incline her to a juster estimate of the value of that home of greater permanence, and equal comfort, of which she had the offer.\n\nIt was a medicinal project upon his niece’s understanding, which he must consider as at present diseased. A residence of eight or nine years in the abode of wealth and plenty had a little disordered her powers of comparing and judging. Her father’s house would, in all probability, teach her the value of a good income; and he trusted that she would be the wiser and happier woman, all her life, for the experiment he had devised.\n\nHad Fanny been at all addicted to raptures, she must have had a strong attack of them when she first understood what was intended, when her uncle first made her the offer of visiting the parents, and brothers, and sisters, from whom she had been divided almost half her life; of returning for a couple of months to the scenes of her infancy, with William for the protector and companion of her journey, and the certainty of continuing to see William to the last hour of his remaining on land. Had she ever given way to bursts of delight, it must have been then, for she was delighted, but her happiness was of a quiet, deep, heart-swelling sort; and though never a great talker, she was always more inclined to silence when feeling most strongly. At the moment she could only thank and accept. Afterwards, when familiarised with the visions of enjoyment so suddenly opened, she could speak more largely to William and Edmund of what she felt; but still there were emotions of tenderness that could not be clothed in words. The remembrance of all her earliest pleasures, and of what she had suffered in being torn from them, came over her with renewed strength, and it seemed as if to be at home again would heal every pain that had since grown out of the separation. To be in the centre of such a circle, loved by so many, and more loved by all than she had ever been before; to feel affection without fear or restraint; to feel herself the equal of those who surrounded her; to be at peace from all mention of the Crawfords, safe from every look which could be fancied a reproach on their account. This was a prospect to be dwelt on with a fondness that could be but half acknowledged.\n\nEdmund, too—to be two months from him (and perhaps she might be allowed to make her absence three) must do her good. At a distance, unassailed by his looks or his kindness, and safe from the perpetual irritation of knowing his heart, and striving to avoid his confidence, she should be able to reason herself into a properer state; she should be able to think of him as in London, and arranging everything there, without wretchedness. What might have been hard to bear at Mansfield was to become a slight evil at Portsmouth.\n\nThe only drawback was the doubt of her aunt Bertram’s being comfortable without her. She was of use to no one else; but there she might be missed to a degree that she did not like to think of; and that part of the arrangement was, indeed, the hardest for Sir Thomas to accomplish, and what only he could have accomplished at all.\n\nBut he was master at Mansfield Park. When he had really resolved on any measure, he could always carry it through; and now by dint of long talking on the subject, explaining and dwelling on the duty of Fanny’s sometimes seeing her family, he did induce his wife to let her go; obtaining it rather from submission, however, than conviction, for Lady Bertram was convinced of very little more than that Sir Thomas thought Fanny ought to go, and therefore that she must. In the calmness of her own dressing-room, in the impartial flow of her own meditations, unbiased by his bewildering statements, she could not acknowledge any necessity for Fanny’s ever going near a father and mother who had done without her so long, while she was so useful to herself. And as to the not missing her, which under Mrs. Norris’s discussion was the point attempted to be proved, she set herself very steadily against admitting any such thing.\n\nSir Thomas had appealed to her reason, conscience, and dignity. He called it a sacrifice, and demanded it of her goodness and self-command as such. But Mrs. Norris wanted to persuade her that Fanny could be very well spared—she being ready to give up all her own time to her as requested—and, in short, could not really be wanted or missed.\n\n“That may be, sister,” was all Lady Bertram’s reply. “I dare say you are very right; but I am sure I shall miss her very much.”\n\nThe next step was to communicate with Portsmouth. Fanny wrote to offer herself; and her mother’s answer, though short, was so kind—a few simple lines expressed so natural and motherly a joy in the prospect of seeing her child again, as to confirm all the daughter’s views of happiness in being with her—convincing her that she should now find a warm and affectionate friend in the “mama” who had certainly shewn no remarkable fondness for her formerly; but this she could easily suppose to have been her own fault or her own fancy. She had probably alienated love by the helplessness and fretfulness of a fearful temper, or been unreasonable in wanting a larger share than any one among so many could deserve. Now, when she knew better how to be useful, and how to forbear, and when her mother could be no longer occupied by the incessant demands of a house full of little children, there would be leisure and inclination for every comfort, and they should soon be what mother and daughter ought to be to each other.\n\nWilliam was almost as happy in the plan as his sister. It would be the greatest pleasure to him to have her there to the last moment before he sailed, and perhaps find her there still when he came in from his first cruise. And besides, he wanted her so very much to see the Thrush before she went out of harbour—the Thrush was certainly the finest sloop in the service—and there were several improvements in the dockyard, too, which he quite longed to shew her.\n\nHe did not scruple to add that her being at home for a while would be a great advantage to everybody.\n\n“I do not know how it is,” said he; “but we seem to want some of your nice ways and orderliness at my father’s. The house is always in confusion. You will set things going in a better way, I am sure. You will tell my mother how it all ought to be, and you will be so useful to Susan, and you will teach Betsey, and make the boys love and mind you. How right and comfortable it will all be!”\n\nBy the time Mrs. Price’s answer arrived, there remained but a very few days more to be spent at Mansfield; and for part of one of those days the young travellers were in a good deal of alarm on the subject of their journey, for when the mode of it came to be talked of, and Mrs. Norris found that all her anxiety to save her brother-in-law’s money was vain, and that in spite of her wishes and hints for a less expensive conveyance of Fanny, they were to travel post; when she saw Sir Thomas actually give William notes for the purpose, she was struck with the idea of there being room for a third in the carriage, and suddenly seized with a strong inclination to go with them, to go and see her poor dear sister Price. She proclaimed her thoughts. She must say that she had more than half a mind to go with the young people; it would be such an indulgence to her; she had not seen her poor dear sister Price for more than twenty years; and it would be a help to the young people in their journey to have her older head to manage for them; and she could not help thinking her poor dear sister Price would feel it very unkind of her not to come by such an opportunity.\n\nWilliam and Fanny were horror-struck at the idea.\n\nAll the comfort of their comfortable journey would be destroyed at once. With woeful countenances they looked at each other. Their suspense lasted an hour or two. No one interfered to encourage or dissuade. Mrs. Norris was left to settle the matter by herself; and it ended, to the infinite joy of her nephew and niece, in the recollection that she could not possibly be spared from Mansfield Park at present; that she was a great deal too necessary to Sir Thomas and Lady Bertram for her to be able to answer it to herself to leave them even for a week, and therefore must certainly sacrifice every other pleasure to that of being useful to them.\n\nIt had, in fact, occurred to her, that though taken to Portsmouth for nothing, it would be hardly possible for her to avoid paying her own expenses back again. So her poor dear sister Price was left to all the disappointment of her missing such an opportunity, and another twenty years’ absence, perhaps, begun.\n\nEdmund’s plans were affected by this Portsmouth journey, this absence of Fanny’s. He too had a sacrifice to make to Mansfield Park as well as his aunt. He had intended, about this time, to be going to London; but he could not leave his father and mother just when everybody else of most importance to their comfort was leaving them; and with an effort, felt but not boasted of, he delayed for a week or two longer a journey which he was looking forward to with the hope of its fixing his happiness for ever.\n\nHe told Fanny of it. She knew so much already, that she must know everything. It made the substance of one other confidential discourse about Miss Crawford; and Fanny was the more affected from feeling it to be the last time in which Miss Crawford’s name would ever be mentioned between them with any remains of liberty. Once afterwards she was alluded to by him. Lady Bertram had been telling her niece in the evening to write to her soon and often, and promising to be a good correspondent herself; and Edmund, at a convenient moment, then added in a whisper, “And I shall write to you, Fanny, when I have anything worth writing about, anything to say that I think you will like to hear, and that you will not hear so soon from any other quarter.” Had she doubted his meaning while she listened, the glow in his face, when she looked up at him, would have been decisive.\n\nFor this letter she must try to arm herself. That a letter from Edmund should be a subject of terror! She began to feel that she had not yet gone through all the changes of opinion and sentiment which the progress of time and variation of circumstances occasion in this world of changes. The vicissitudes of the human mind had not yet been exhausted by her.\n\nPoor Fanny! though going as she did willingly and eagerly, the last evening at Mansfield Park must still be wretchedness. Her heart was completely sad at parting. She had tears for every room in the house, much more for every beloved inhabitant. She clung to her aunt, because she would miss her; she kissed the hand of her uncle with struggling sobs, because she had displeased him; and as for Edmund, she could neither speak, nor look, nor think, when the last moment came with him; and it was not till it was over that she knew he was giving her the affectionate farewell of a brother.\n\nAll this passed overnight, for the journey was to begin very early in the morning; and when the small, diminished party met at breakfast, William and Fanny were talked of as already advanced one stage.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVIII\n\nThe novelty of travelling, and the happiness of being with William, soon produced their natural effect on Fanny’s spirits, when Mansfield Park was fairly left behind; and by the time their first stage was ended, and they were to quit Sir Thomas’s carriage, she was able to take leave of the old coachman, and send back proper messages, with cheerful looks.\n\nOf pleasant talk between the brother and sister there was no end. Everything supplied an amusement to the high glee of William’s mind, and he was full of frolic and joke in the intervals of their higher-toned subjects, all of which ended, if they did not begin, in praise of the Thrush, conjectures how she would be employed, schemes for an action with some superior force, which (supposing the first lieutenant out of the way, and William was not very merciful to the first lieutenant) was to give himself the next step as soon as possible, or speculations upon prize-money, which was to be generously distributed at home, with only the reservation of enough to make the little cottage comfortable, in which he and Fanny were to pass all their middle and later life together.\n\nFanny’s immediate concerns, as far as they involved Mr. Crawford, made no part of their conversation. William knew what had passed, and from his heart lamented that his sister’s feelings should be so cold towards a man whom he must consider as the first of human characters; but he was of an age to be all for love, and therefore unable to blame; and knowing her wish on the subject, he would not distress her by the slightest allusion.\n\nShe had reason to suppose herself not yet forgotten by Mr. Crawford. She had heard repeatedly from his sister within the three weeks which had passed since their leaving Mansfield, and in each letter there had been a few lines from himself, warm and determined like his speeches. It was a correspondence which Fanny found quite as unpleasant as she had feared. Miss Crawford’s style of writing, lively and affectionate, was itself an evil, independent of what she was thus forced into reading from the brother’s pen, for Edmund would never rest till she had read the chief of the letter to him; and then she had to listen to his admiration of her language, and the warmth of her attachments. There had, in fact, been so much of message, of allusion, of recollection, so much of Mansfield in every letter, that Fanny could not but suppose it meant for him to hear; and to find herself forced into a purpose of that kind, compelled into a correspondence which was bringing her the addresses of the man she did not love, and obliging her to administer to the adverse passion of the man she did, was cruelly mortifying. Here, too, her present removal promised advantage. When no longer under the same roof with Edmund, she trusted that Miss Crawford would have no motive for writing strong enough to overcome the trouble, and that at Portsmouth their correspondence would dwindle into nothing.\n\nWith such thoughts as these, among ten hundred others, Fanny proceeded in her journey safely and cheerfully, and as expeditiously as could rationally be hoped in the dirty month of February. They entered Oxford, but she could take only a hasty glimpse of Edmund’s college as they passed along, and made no stop anywhere till they reached Newbury, where a comfortable meal, uniting dinner and supper, wound up the enjoyments and fatigues of the day.\n\nThe next morning saw them off again at an early hour; and with no events, and no delays, they regularly advanced, and were in the environs of Portsmouth while there was yet daylight for Fanny to look around her, and wonder at the new buildings. They passed the drawbridge, and entered the town; and the light was only beginning to fail as, guided by William’s powerful voice, they were rattled into a narrow street, leading from the High Street, and drawn up before the door of a small house now inhabited by Mr. Price.\n\nFanny was all agitation and flutter; all hope and apprehension. The moment they stopped, a trollopy-looking maidservant, seemingly in waiting for them at the door, stepped forward, and more intent on telling the news than giving them any help, immediately began with, “The Thrush is gone out of harbour, please sir, and one of the officers has been here to—” She was interrupted by a fine tall boy of eleven years old, who, rushing out of the house, pushed the maid aside, and while William was opening the chaise-door himself, called out, “You are just in time. We have been looking for you this half-hour. The Thrush went out of harbour this morning. I saw her. It was a beautiful sight. And they think she will have her orders in a day or two. And Mr. Campbell was here at four o’clock to ask for you: he has got one of the Thrush’s boats, and is going off to her at six, and hoped you would be here in time to go with him.”\n\nA stare or two at Fanny, as William helped her out of the carriage, was all the voluntary notice which this brother bestowed; but he made no objection to her kissing him, though still entirely engaged in detailing farther particulars of the Thrush’s going out of harbour, in which he had a strong right of interest, being to commence his career of seamanship in her at this very time.\n\nAnother moment and Fanny was in the narrow entrance-passage of the house, and in her mother’s arms, who met her there with looks of true kindness, and with features which Fanny loved the more, because they brought her aunt Bertram’s before her, and there were her two sisters: Susan, a well-grown fine girl of fourteen, and Betsey, the youngest of the family, about five—both glad to see her in their way, though with no advantage of manner in receiving her. But manner Fanny did not want. Would they but love her, she should be satisfied.\n\nShe was then taken into a parlour, so small that her first conviction was of its being only a passage-room to something better, and she stood for a moment expecting to be invited on; but when she saw there was no other door, and that there were signs of habitation before her, she called back her thoughts, reproved herself, and grieved lest they should have been suspected. Her mother, however, could not stay long enough to suspect anything. She was gone again to the street-door, to welcome William. “Oh! my dear William, how glad I am to see you. But have you heard about the Thrush? She is gone out of harbour already; three days before we had any thought of it; and I do not know what I am to do about Sam’s things, they will never be ready in time; for she may have her orders to-morrow, perhaps. It takes me quite unawares. And now you must be off for Spithead too. Campbell has been here, quite in a worry about you; and now what shall we do? I thought to have had such a comfortable evening with you, and here everything comes upon me at once.”\n\nHer son answered cheerfully, telling her that everything was always for the best; and making light of his own inconvenience in being obliged to hurry away so soon.\n\n“To be sure, I had much rather she had stayed in harbour, that I might have sat a few hours with you in comfort; but as there is a boat ashore, I had better go off at once, and there is no help for it. Whereabouts does the Thrush lay at Spithead? Near the Canopus? But no matter; here’s Fanny in the parlour, and why should we stay in the passage? Come, mother, you have hardly looked at your own dear Fanny yet.”\n\nIn they both came, and Mrs. Price having kindly kissed her daughter again, and commented a little on her growth, began with very natural solicitude to feel for their fatigues and wants as travellers.\n\n“Poor dears! how tired you must both be! and now, what will you have? I began to think you would never come. Betsey and I have been watching for you this half-hour. And when did you get anything to eat? And what would you like to have now? I could not tell whether you would be for some meat, or only a dish of tea, after your journey, or else I would have got something ready. And now I am afraid Campbell will be here before there is time to dress a steak, and we have no butcher at hand. It is very inconvenient to have no butcher in the street. We were better off in our last house. Perhaps you would like some tea as soon as it can be got.”\n\nThey both declared they should prefer it to anything. “Then, Betsey, my dear, run into the kitchen and see if Rebecca has put the water on; and tell her to bring in the tea-things as soon as she can. I wish we could get the bell mended; but Betsey is a very handy little messenger.”\n\nBetsey went with alacrity, proud to shew her abilities before her fine new sister.\n\n“Dear me!” continued the anxious mother, “what a sad fire we have got, and I dare say you are both starved with cold. Draw your chair nearer, my dear. I cannot think what Rebecca has been about. I am sure I told her to bring some coals half an hour ago. Susan, you should have taken care of the fire.”\n\n“I was upstairs, mama, moving my things,” said Susan, in a fearless, self-defending tone, which startled Fanny. “You know you had but just settled that my sister Fanny and I should have the other room; and I could not get Rebecca to give me any help.”\n\nFarther discussion was prevented by various bustles: first, the driver came to be paid; then there was a squabble between Sam and Rebecca about the manner of carrying up his sister’s trunk, which he would manage all his own way; and lastly, in walked Mr. Price himself, his own loud voice preceding him, as with something of the oath kind he kicked away his son’s portmanteau and his daughter’s bandbox in the passage, and called out for a candle; no candle was brought, however, and he walked into the room.\n\nFanny with doubting feelings had risen to meet him, but sank down again on finding herself undistinguished in the dusk, and unthought of. With a friendly shake of his son’s hand, and an eager voice, he instantly began—“Ha! welcome back, my boy. Glad to see you. Have you heard the news? The Thrush went out of harbour this morning. Sharp is the word, you see! By G—, you are just in time! The doctor has been here inquiring for you: he has got one of the boats, and is to be off for Spithead by six, so you had better go with him. I have been to Turner’s about your mess; it is all in a way to be done. I should not wonder if you had your orders to-morrow: but you cannot sail with this wind, if you are to cruise to the westward; and Captain Walsh thinks you will certainly have a cruise to the westward, with the Elephant. By G—, I wish you may! But old Scholey was saying, just now, that he thought you would be sent first to the Texel. Well, well, we are ready, whatever happens. But by G—, you lost a fine sight by not being here in the morning to see the Thrush go out of harbour! I would not have been out of the way for a thousand pounds. Old Scholey ran in at breakfast-time, to say she had slipped her moorings and was coming out, I jumped up, and made but two steps to the platform. If ever there was a perfect beauty afloat, she is one; and there she lays at Spithead, and anybody in England would take her for an eight-and-twenty. I was upon the platform two hours this afternoon looking at her. She lays close to the Endymion, between her and the Cleopatra, just to the eastward of the sheer hulk.”\n\n“Ha!” cried William, “that’s just where I should have put her myself. It’s the best berth at Spithead. But here is my sister, sir; here is Fanny,” turning and leading her forward; “it is so dark you do not see her.”\n\nWith an acknowledgment that he had quite forgot her, Mr. Price now received his daughter; and having given her a cordial hug, and observed that she was grown into a woman, and he supposed would be wanting a husband soon, seemed very much inclined to forget her again. Fanny shrunk back to her seat, with feelings sadly pained by his language and his smell of spirits; and he talked on only to his son, and only of the Thrush, though William, warmly interested as he was in that subject, more than once tried to make his father think of Fanny, and her long absence and long journey.\n\nAfter sitting some time longer, a candle was obtained; but as there was still no appearance of tea, nor, from Betsey’s reports from the kitchen, much hope of any under a considerable period, William determined to go and change his dress, and make the necessary preparations for his removal on board directly, that he might have his tea in comfort afterwards.\n\nAs he left the room, two rosy-faced boys, ragged and dirty, about eight and nine years old, rushed into it just released from school, and coming eagerly to see their sister, and tell that the Thrush was gone out of harbour; Tom and Charles. Charles had been born since Fanny’s going away, but Tom she had often helped to nurse, and now felt a particular pleasure in seeing again. Both were kissed very tenderly, but Tom she wanted to keep by her, to try to trace the features of the baby she had loved, and talked to, of his infant preference of herself. Tom, however, had no mind for such treatment: he came home not to stand and be talked to, but to run about and make a noise; and both boys had soon burst from her, and slammed the parlour-door till her temples ached.\n\nShe had now seen all that were at home; there remained only two brothers between herself and Susan, one of whom was a clerk in a public office in London, and the other midshipman on board an Indiaman. But though she had seen all the members of the family, she had not yet heard all the noise they could make. Another quarter of an hour brought her a great deal more. William was soon calling out from the landing-place of the second story for his mother and for Rebecca. He was in distress for something that he had left there, and did not find again. A key was mislaid, Betsey accused of having got at his new hat, and some slight, but essential alteration of his uniform waistcoat, which he had been promised to have done for him, entirely neglected.\n\nMrs. Price, Rebecca, and Betsey all went up to defend themselves, all talking together, but Rebecca loudest, and the job was to be done as well as it could in a great hurry; William trying in vain to send Betsey down again, or keep her from being troublesome where she was; the whole of which, as almost every door in the house was open, could be plainly distinguished in the parlour, except when drowned at intervals by the superior noise of Sam, Tom, and Charles chasing each other up and down stairs, and tumbling about and hallooing.\n\nFanny was almost stunned. The smallness of the house and thinness of the walls brought everything so close to her, that, added to the fatigue of her journey, and all her recent agitation, she hardly knew how to bear it. Within the room all was tranquil enough, for Susan having disappeared with the others, there were soon only her father and herself remaining; and he, taking out a newspaper, the accustomary loan of a neighbour, applied himself to studying it, without seeming to recollect her existence. The solitary candle was held between himself and the paper, without any reference to her possible convenience; but she had nothing to do, and was glad to have the light screened from her aching head, as she sat in bewildered, broken, sorrowful contemplation.\n\nShe was at home. But, alas! it was not such a home, she had not such a welcome, as—she checked herself; she was unreasonable. What right had she to be of importance to her family? She could have none, so long lost sight of! William’s concerns must be dearest, they always had been, and he had every right. Yet to have so little said or asked about herself, to have scarcely an inquiry made after Mansfield! It did pain her to have Mansfield forgotten; the friends who had done so much—the dear, dear friends! But here, one subject swallowed up all the rest. Perhaps it must be so. The destination of the Thrush must be now preeminently interesting. A day or two might shew the difference. She only was to blame. Yet she thought it would not have been so at Mansfield. No, in her uncle’s house there would have been a consideration of times and seasons, a regulation of subject, a propriety, an attention towards everybody which there was not here.\n\nThe only interruption which thoughts like these received for nearly half an hour was from a sudden burst of her father’s, not at all calculated to compose them. At a more than ordinary pitch of thumping and hallooing in the passage, he exclaimed, “Devil take those young dogs! How they are singing out! Ay, Sam’s voice louder than all the rest! That boy is fit for a boatswain. Holla, you there! Sam, stop your confounded pipe, or I shall be after you.”\n\nThis threat was so palpably disregarded, that though within five minutes afterwards the three boys all burst into the room together and sat down, Fanny could not consider it as a proof of anything more than their being for the time thoroughly fagged, which their hot faces and panting breaths seemed to prove, especially as they were still kicking each other’s shins, and hallooing out at sudden starts immediately under their father’s eye.\n\nThe next opening of the door brought something more welcome: it was for the tea-things, which she had begun almost to despair of seeing that evening. Susan and an attendant girl, whose inferior appearance informed Fanny, to her great surprise, that she had previously seen the upper servant, brought in everything necessary for the meal; Susan looking, as she put the kettle on the fire and glanced at her sister, as if divided between the agreeable triumph of shewing her activity and usefulness, and the dread of being thought to demean herself by such an office. “She had been into the kitchen,” she said, “to hurry Sally and help make the toast, and spread the bread and butter, or she did not know when they should have got tea, and she was sure her sister must want something after her journey.”\n\nFanny was very thankful. She could not but own that she should be very glad of a little tea, and Susan immediately set about making it, as if pleased to have the employment all to herself; and with only a little unnecessary bustle, and some few injudicious attempts at keeping her brothers in better order than she could, acquitted herself very well. Fanny’s spirit was as much refreshed as her body; her head and heart were soon the better for such well-timed kindness. Susan had an open, sensible countenance; she was like William, and Fanny hoped to find her like him in disposition and goodwill towards herself.\n\nIn this more placid state of things William reentered, followed not far behind by his mother and Betsey. He, complete in his lieutenant’s uniform, looking and moving all the taller, firmer, and more graceful for it, and with the happiest smile over his face, walked up directly to Fanny, who, rising from her seat, looked at him for a moment in speechless admiration, and then threw her arms round his neck to sob out her various emotions of pain and pleasure.\n\nAnxious not to appear unhappy, she soon recovered herself; and wiping away her tears, was able to notice and admire all the striking parts of his dress; listening with reviving spirits to his cheerful hopes of being on shore some part of every day before they sailed, and even of getting her to Spithead to see the sloop.\n\nThe next bustle brought in Mr. Campbell, the surgeon of the Thrush, a very well-behaved young man, who came to call for his friend, and for whom there was with some contrivance found a chair, and with some hasty washing of the young tea-maker’s, a cup and saucer; and after another quarter of an hour of earnest talk between the gentlemen, noise rising upon noise, and bustle upon bustle, men and boys at last all in motion together, the moment came for setting off; everything was ready, William took leave, and all of them were gone; for the three boys, in spite of their mother’s entreaty, determined to see their brother and Mr. Campbell to the sally-port; and Mr. Price walked off at the same time to carry back his neighbour’s newspaper.\n\nSomething like tranquillity might now be hoped for; and accordingly, when Rebecca had been prevailed on to carry away the tea-things, and Mrs. Price had walked about the room some time looking for a shirt-sleeve, which Betsey at last hunted out from a drawer in the kitchen, the small party of females were pretty well composed, and the mother having lamented again over the impossibility of getting Sam ready in time, was at leisure to think of her eldest daughter and the friends she had come from.\n\nA few inquiries began: but one of the earliest—“How did sister Bertram manage about her servants?” “Was she as much plagued as herself to get tolerable servants?”—soon led her mind away from Northamptonshire, and fixed it on her own domestic grievances, and the shocking character of all the Portsmouth servants, of whom she believed her own two were the very worst, engrossed her completely. The Bertrams were all forgotten in detailing the faults of Rebecca, against whom Susan had also much to depose, and little Betsey a great deal more, and who did seem so thoroughly without a single recommendation, that Fanny could not help modestly presuming that her mother meant to part with her when her year was up.\n\n“Her year!” cried Mrs. Price; “I am sure I hope I shall be rid of her before she has staid a year, for that will not be up till November. Servants are come to such a pass, my dear, in Portsmouth, that it is quite a miracle if one keeps them more than half a year. I have no hope of ever being settled; and if I was to part with Rebecca, I should only get something worse. And yet I do not think I am a very difficult mistress to please; and I am sure the place is easy enough, for there is always a girl under her, and I often do half the work myself.”\n\nFanny was silent; but not from being convinced that there might not be a remedy found for some of these evils. As she now sat looking at Betsey, she could not but think particularly of another sister, a very pretty little girl, whom she had left there not much younger when she went into Northamptonshire, who had died a few years afterwards. There had been something remarkably amiable about her. Fanny in those early days had preferred her to Susan; and when the news of her death had at last reached Mansfield, had for a short time been quite afflicted. The sight of Betsey brought the image of little Mary back again, but she would not have pained her mother by alluding to her for the world. While considering her with these ideas, Betsey, at a small distance, was holding out something to catch her eyes, meaning to screen it at the same time from Susan’s.\n\n“What have you got there, my love?” said Fanny; “come and shew it to me.”\n\nIt was a silver knife. Up jumped Susan, claiming it as her own, and trying to get it away; but the child ran to her mother’s protection, and Susan could only reproach, which she did very warmly, and evidently hoping to interest Fanny on her side. “It was very hard that she was not to have her own knife; it was her own knife; little sister Mary had left it to her upon her deathbed, and she ought to have had it to keep herself long ago. But mama kept it from her, and was always letting Betsey get hold of it; and the end of it would be that Betsey would spoil it, and get it for her own, though mama had promised her that Betsey should not have it in her own hands.”\n\nFanny was quite shocked. Every feeling of duty, honour, and tenderness was wounded by her sister’s speech and her mother’s reply.\n\n“Now, Susan,” cried Mrs. Price, in a complaining voice, “now, how can you be so cross? You are always quarrelling about that knife. I wish you would not be so quarrelsome. Poor little Betsey; how cross Susan is to you! But you should not have taken it out, my dear, when I sent you to the drawer. You know I told you not to touch it, because Susan is so cross about it. I must hide it another time, Betsey. Poor Mary little thought it would be such a bone of contention when she gave it me to keep, only two hours before she died. Poor little soul! she could but just speak to be heard, and she said so prettily, ‘Let sister Susan have my knife, mama, when I am dead and buried.’ Poor little dear! she was so fond of it, Fanny, that she would have it lay by her in bed, all through her illness. It was the gift of her good godmother, old Mrs. Admiral Maxwell, only six weeks before she was taken for death. Poor little sweet creature! Well, she was taken away from evil to come. My own Betsey” (fondling her), “you have not the luck of such a good godmother. Aunt Norris lives too far off to think of such little people as you.”\n\nFanny had indeed nothing to convey from aunt Norris, but a message to say she hoped that her god-daughter was a good girl, and learnt her book. There had been at one moment a slight murmur in the drawing-room at Mansfield Park about sending her a prayer-book; but no second sound had been heard of such a purpose. Mrs. Norris, however, had gone home and taken down two old prayer-books of her husband with that idea; but, upon examination, the ardour of generosity went off. One was found to have too small a print for a child’s eyes, and the other to be too cumbersome for her to carry about.\n\nFanny, fatigued and fatigued again, was thankful to accept the first invitation of going to bed; and before Betsey had finished her cry at being allowed to sit up only one hour extraordinary in honour of sister, she was off, leaving all below in confusion and noise again; the boys begging for toasted cheese, her father calling out for his rum and water, and Rebecca never where she ought to be.\n\nThere was nothing to raise her spirits in the confined and scantily furnished chamber that she was to share with Susan. The smallness of the rooms above and below, indeed, and the narrowness of the passage and staircase, struck her beyond her imagination. She soon learned to think with respect of her own little attic at Mansfield Park, in that house reckoned too small for anybody’s comfort.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIX\n\nCould Sir Thomas have seen all his niece’s feelings, when she wrote her first letter to her aunt, he would not have despaired; for though a good night’s rest, a pleasant morning, the hope of soon seeing William again, and the comparatively quiet state of the house, from Tom and Charles being gone to school, Sam on some project of his own, and her father on his usual lounges, enabled her to express herself cheerfully on the subject of home, there were still, to her own perfect consciousness, many drawbacks suppressed. Could he have seen only half that she felt before the end of a week, he would have thought Mr. Crawford sure of her, and been delighted with his own sagacity.\n\nBefore the week ended, it was all disappointment. In the first place, William was gone. The Thrush had had her orders, the wind had changed, and he was sailed within four days from their reaching Portsmouth; and during those days she had seen him only twice, in a short and hurried way, when he had come ashore on duty. There had been no free conversation, no walk on the ramparts, no visit to the dockyard, no acquaintance with the Thrush, nothing of all that they had planned and depended on. Everything in that quarter failed her, except William’s affection. His last thought on leaving home was for her. He stepped back again to the door to say, “Take care of Fanny, mother. She is tender, and not used to rough it like the rest of us. I charge you, take care of Fanny.”\n\nWilliam was gone: and the home he had left her in was, Fanny could not conceal it from herself, in almost every respect the very reverse of what she could have wished. It was the abode of noise, disorder, and impropriety. Nobody was in their right place, nothing was done as it ought to be. She could not respect her parents as she had hoped. On her father, her confidence had not been sanguine, but he was more negligent of his family, his habits were worse, and his manners coarser, than she had been prepared for. He did not want abilities but he had no curiosity, and no information beyond his profession; he read only the newspaper and the navy-list; he talked only of the dockyard, the harbour, Spithead, and the Motherbank; he swore and he drank, he was dirty and gross. She had never been able to recall anything approaching to tenderness in his former treatment of herself. There had remained only a general impression of roughness and loudness; and now he scarcely ever noticed her, but to make her the object of a coarse joke.\n\nHer disappointment in her mother was greater: there she had hoped much, and found almost nothing. Every flattering scheme of being of consequence to her soon fell to the ground. Mrs. Price was not unkind; but, instead of gaining on her affection and confidence, and becoming more and more dear, her daughter never met with greater kindness from her than on the first day of her arrival. The instinct of nature was soon satisfied, and Mrs. Price’s attachment had no other source. Her heart and her time were already quite full; she had neither leisure nor affection to bestow on Fanny. Her daughters never had been much to her. She was fond of her sons, especially of William, but Betsey was the first of her girls whom she had ever much regarded. To her she was most injudiciously indulgent. William was her pride; Betsey her darling; and John, Richard, Sam, Tom, and Charles occupied all the rest of her maternal solicitude, alternately her worries and her comforts. These shared her heart: her time was given chiefly to her house and her servants. Her days were spent in a kind of slow bustle; all was busy without getting on, always behindhand and lamenting it, without altering her ways; wishing to be an economist, without contrivance or regularity; dissatisfied with her servants, without skill to make them better, and whether helping, or reprimanding, or indulging them, without any power of engaging their respect.\n\nOf her two sisters, Mrs. Price very much more resembled Lady Bertram than Mrs. Norris. She was a manager by necessity, without any of Mrs. Norris’s inclination for it, or any of her activity. Her disposition was naturally easy and indolent, like Lady Bertram’s; and a situation of similar affluence and do-nothingness would have been much more suited to her capacity than the exertions and self-denials of the one which her imprudent marriage had placed her in. She might have made just as good a woman of consequence as Lady Bertram, but Mrs. Norris would have been a more respectable mother of nine children on a small income.\n\nMuch of all this Fanny could not but be sensible of. She might scruple to make use of the words, but she must and did feel that her mother was a partial, ill-judging parent, a dawdle, a slattern, who neither taught nor restrained her children, whose house was the scene of mismanagement and discomfort from beginning to end, and who had no talent, no conversation, no affection towards herself; no curiosity to know her better, no desire of her friendship, and no inclination for her company that could lessen her sense of such feelings.\n\nFanny was very anxious to be useful, and not to appear above her home, or in any way disqualified or disinclined, by her foreign education, from contributing her help to its comforts, and therefore set about working for Sam immediately; and by working early and late, with perseverance and great despatch, did so much that the boy was shipped off at last, with more than half his linen ready. She had great pleasure in feeling her usefulness, but could not conceive how they would have managed without her.\n\nSam, loud and overbearing as he was, she rather regretted when he went, for he was clever and intelligent, and glad to be employed in any errand in the town; and though spurning the remonstrances of Susan, given as they were, though very reasonable in themselves, with ill-timed and powerless warmth, was beginning to be influenced by Fanny’s services and gentle persuasions; and she found that the best of the three younger ones was gone in him: Tom and Charles being at least as many years as they were his juniors distant from that age of feeling and reason, which might suggest the expediency of making friends, and of endeavouring to be less disagreeable. Their sister soon despaired of making the smallest impression on them; they were quite untameable by any means of address which she had spirits or time to attempt. Every afternoon brought a return of their riotous games all over the house; and she very early learned to sigh at the approach of Saturday’s constant half-holiday.\n\nBetsey, too, a spoiled child, trained up to think the alphabet her greatest enemy, left to be with the servants at her pleasure, and then encouraged to report any evil of them, she was almost as ready to despair of being able to love or assist; and of Susan’s temper she had many doubts. Her continual disagreements with her mother, her rash squabbles with Tom and Charles, and petulance with Betsey, were at least so distressing to Fanny that, though admitting they were by no means without provocation, she feared the disposition that could push them to such length must be far from amiable, and from affording any repose to herself.\n\nSuch was the home which was to put Mansfield out of her head, and teach her to think of her cousin Edmund with moderated feelings. On the contrary, she could think of nothing but Mansfield, its beloved inmates, its happy ways. Everything where she now was in full contrast to it. The elegance, propriety, regularity, harmony, and perhaps, above all, the peace and tranquillity of Mansfield, were brought to her remembrance every hour of the day, by the prevalence of everything opposite to them here.\n\nThe living in incessant noise was, to a frame and temper delicate and nervous like Fanny’s, an evil which no superadded elegance or harmony could have entirely atoned for. It was the greatest misery of all. At Mansfield, no sounds of contention, no raised voice, no abrupt bursts, no tread of violence, was ever heard; all proceeded in a regular course of cheerful orderliness; everybody had their due importance; everybody’s feelings were consulted. If tenderness could be ever supposed wanting, good sense and good breeding supplied its place; and as to the little irritations sometimes introduced by aunt Norris, they were short, they were trifling, they were as a drop of water to the ocean, compared with the ceaseless tumult of her present abode. Here everybody was noisy, every voice was loud (excepting, perhaps, her mother’s, which resembled the soft monotony of Lady Bertram’s, only worn into fretfulness). Whatever was wanted was hallooed for, and the servants hallooed out their excuses from the kitchen. The doors were in constant banging, the stairs were never at rest, nothing was done without a clatter, nobody sat still, and nobody could command attention when they spoke.\n\nIn a review of the two houses, as they appeared to her before the end of a week, Fanny was tempted to apply to them Dr. Johnson’s celebrated judgment as to matrimony and celibacy, and say, that though Mansfield Park might have some pains, Portsmouth could have no pleasures.\n\n# CHAPTER XL\n\nFanny was right enough in not expecting to hear from Miss Crawford now at the rapid rate in which their correspondence had begun; Mary’s next letter was after a decidedly longer interval than the last, but she was not right in supposing that such an interval would be felt a great relief to herself. Here was another strange revolution of mind! She was really glad to receive the letter when it did come. In her present exile from good society, and distance from everything that had been wont to interest her, a letter from one belonging to the set where her heart lived, written with affection, and some degree of elegance, was thoroughly acceptable. The usual plea of increasing engagements was made in excuse for not having written to her earlier; “And now that I have begun,” she continued, “my letter will not be worth your reading, for there will be no little offering of love at the end, no three or four lines passionnées from the most devoted H. C. in the world, for Henry is in Norfolk; business called him to Everingham ten days ago, or perhaps he only pretended the call, for the sake of being travelling at the same time that you were. But there he is, and, by the bye, his absence may sufficiently account for any remissness of his sister’s in writing, for there has been no ‘Well, Mary, when do you write to Fanny? Is not it time for you to write to Fanny?’ to spur me on. At last, after various attempts at meeting, I have seen your cousins, ‘dear Julia and dearest Mrs. Rushworth’; they found me at home yesterday, and we were glad to see each other again. We seemed very glad to see each other, and I do really think we were a little. We had a vast deal to say. Shall I tell you how Mrs. Rushworth looked when your name was mentioned? I did not use to think her wanting in self-possession, but she had not quite enough for the demands of yesterday. Upon the whole, Julia was in the best looks of the two, at least after you were spoken of. There was no recovering the complexion from the moment that I spoke of ‘Fanny,’ and spoke of her as a sister should. But Mrs. Rushworth’s day of good looks will come; we have cards for her first party on the 28th. Then she will be in beauty, for she will open one of the best houses in Wimpole Street. I was in it two years ago, when it was Lady Lascelle’s, and prefer it to almost any I know in London, and certainly she will then feel, to use a vulgar phrase, that she has got her pennyworth for her penny. Henry could not have afforded her such a house. I hope she will recollect it, and be satisfied, as well as she may, with moving the queen of a palace, though the king may appear best in the background; and as I have no desire to tease her, I shall never force your name upon her again. She will grow sober by degrees. From all that I hear and guess, Baron Wildenheim’s attentions to Julia continue, but I do not know that he has any serious encouragement. She ought to do better. A poor honourable is no catch, and I cannot imagine any liking in the case, for take away his rants, and the poor baron has nothing. What a difference a vowel makes! If his rents were but equal to his rants! Your cousin Edmund moves slowly; detained, perchance, by parish duties. There may be some old woman at Thornton Lacey to be converted. I am unwilling to fancy myself neglected for a young one. Adieu! my dear sweet Fanny, this is a long letter from London: write me a pretty one in reply to gladden Henry’s eyes, when he comes back, and send me an account of all the dashing young captains whom you disdain for his sake.”\n\nThere was great food for meditation in this letter, and chiefly for unpleasant meditation; and yet, with all the uneasiness it supplied, it connected her with the absent, it told her of people and things about whom she had never felt so much curiosity as now, and she would have been glad to have been sure of such a letter every week. Her correspondence with her aunt Bertram was her only concern of higher interest.\n\nAs for any society in Portsmouth, that could at all make amends for deficiencies at home, there were none within the circle of her father’s and mother’s acquaintance to afford her the smallest satisfaction: she saw nobody in whose favour she could wish to overcome her own shyness and reserve. The men appeared to her all coarse, the women all pert, everybody underbred; and she gave as little contentment as she received from introductions either to old or new acquaintance. The young ladies who approached her at first with some respect, in consideration of her coming from a baronet’s family, were soon offended by what they termed “airs”; for, as she neither played on the pianoforte nor wore fine pelisses, they could, on farther observation, admit no right of superiority.\n\nThe first solid consolation which Fanny received for the evils of home, the first which her judgment could entirely approve, and which gave any promise of durability, was in a better knowledge of Susan, and a hope of being of service to her. Susan had always behaved pleasantly to herself, but the determined character of her general manners had astonished and alarmed her, and it was at least a fortnight before she began to understand a disposition so totally different from her own. Susan saw that much was wrong at home, and wanted to set it right. That a girl of fourteen, acting only on her own unassisted reason, should err in the method of reform, was not wonderful; and Fanny soon became more disposed to admire the natural light of the mind which could so early distinguish justly, than to censure severely the faults of conduct to which it led. Susan was only acting on the same truths, and pursuing the same system, which her own judgment acknowledged, but which her more supine and yielding temper would have shrunk from asserting. Susan tried to be useful, where she could only have gone away and cried; and that Susan was useful she could perceive; that things, bad as they were, would have been worse but for such interposition, and that both her mother and Betsey were restrained from some excesses of very offensive indulgence and vulgarity.\n\nIn every argument with her mother, Susan had in point of reason the advantage, and never was there any maternal tenderness to buy her off. The blind fondness which was for ever producing evil around her she had never known. There was no gratitude for affection past or present to make her better bear with its excesses to the others.\n\nAll this became gradually evident, and gradually placed Susan before her sister as an object of mingled compassion and respect. That her manner was wrong, however, at times very wrong, her measures often ill-chosen and ill-timed, and her looks and language very often indefensible, Fanny could not cease to feel; but she began to hope they might be rectified. Susan, she found, looked up to her and wished for her good opinion; and new as anything like an office of authority was to Fanny, new as it was to imagine herself capable of guiding or informing any one, she did resolve to give occasional hints to Susan, and endeavour to exercise for her advantage the juster notions of what was due to everybody, and what would be wisest for herself, which her own more favoured education had fixed in her.\n\nHer influence, or at least the consciousness and use of it, originated in an act of kindness by Susan, which, after many hesitations of delicacy, she at last worked herself up to. It had very early occurred to her that a small sum of money might, perhaps, restore peace for ever on the sore subject of the silver knife, canvassed as it now was continually, and the riches which she was in possession of herself, her uncle having given her £10 at parting, made her as able as she was willing to be generous. But she was so wholly unused to confer favours, except on the very poor, so unpractised in removing evils, or bestowing kindnesses among her equals, and so fearful of appearing to elevate herself as a great lady at home, that it took some time to determine that it would not be unbecoming in her to make such a present. It was made, however, at last: a silver knife was bought for Betsey, and accepted with great delight, its newness giving it every advantage over the other that could be desired; Susan was established in the full possession of her own, Betsey handsomely declaring that now she had got one so much prettier herself, she should never want that again; and no reproach seemed conveyed to the equally satisfied mother, which Fanny had almost feared to be impossible. The deed thoroughly answered: a source of domestic altercation was entirely done away, and it was the means of opening Susan’s heart to her, and giving her something more to love and be interested in. Susan shewed that she had delicacy: pleased as she was to be mistress of property which she had been struggling for at least two years, she yet feared that her sister’s judgment had been against her, and that a reproof was designed her for having so struggled as to make the purchase necessary for the tranquillity of the house.\n\nHer temper was open. She acknowledged her fears, blamed herself for having contended so warmly; and from that hour Fanny, understanding the worth of her disposition and perceiving how fully she was inclined to seek her good opinion and refer to her judgment, began to feel again the blessing of affection, and to entertain the hope of being useful to a mind so much in need of help, and so much deserving it. She gave advice, advice too sound to be resisted by a good understanding, and given so mildly and considerately as not to irritate an imperfect temper, and she had the happiness of observing its good effects not unfrequently. More was not expected by one who, while seeing all the obligation and expediency of submission and forbearance, saw also with sympathetic acuteness of feeling all that must be hourly grating to a girl like Susan. Her greatest wonder on the subject soon became—not that Susan should have been provoked into disrespect and impatience against her better knowledge—but that so much better knowledge, so many good notions should have been hers at all; and that, brought up in the midst of negligence and error, she should have formed such proper opinions of what ought to be; she, who had had no cousin Edmund to direct her thoughts or fix her principles.\n\nThe intimacy thus begun between them was a material advantage to each. By sitting together upstairs, they avoided a great deal of the disturbance of the house; Fanny had peace, and Susan learned to think it no misfortune to be quietly employed. They sat without a fire; but that was a privation familiar even to Fanny, and she suffered the less because reminded by it of the East room. It was the only point of resemblance. In space, light, furniture, and prospect, there was nothing alike in the two apartments; and she often heaved a sigh at the remembrance of all her books and boxes, and various comforts there. By degrees the girls came to spend the chief of the morning upstairs, at first only in working and talking, but after a few days, the remembrance of the said books grew so potent and stimulative that Fanny found it impossible not to try for books again. There were none in her father’s house; but wealth is luxurious and daring, and some of hers found its way to a circulating library. She became a subscriber; amazed at being anything in propria persona, amazed at her own doings in every way, to be a renter, a chuser of books! And to be having any one’s improvement in view in her choice! But so it was. Susan had read nothing, and Fanny longed to give her a share in her own first pleasures, and inspire a taste for the biography and poetry which she delighted in herself.\n\nIn this occupation she hoped, moreover, to bury some of the recollections of Mansfield, which were too apt to seize her mind if her fingers only were busy; and, especially at this time, hoped it might be useful in diverting her thoughts from pursuing Edmund to London, whither, on the authority of her aunt’s last letter, she knew he was gone. She had no doubt of what would ensue. The promised notification was hanging over her head. The postman’s knock within the neighbourhood was beginning to bring its daily terrors, and if reading could banish the idea for even half an hour, it was something gained.\n\n# CHAPTER XLI\n\nA week was gone since Edmund might be supposed in town, and Fanny had heard nothing of him. There were three different conclusions to be drawn from his silence, between which her mind was in fluctuation; each of them at times being held the most probable. Either his going had been again delayed, or he had yet procured no opportunity of seeing Miss Crawford alone, or he was too happy for letter-writing!\n\nOne morning, about this time, Fanny having now been nearly four weeks from Mansfield, a point which she never failed to think over and calculate every day, as she and Susan were preparing to remove, as usual, upstairs, they were stopped by the knock of a visitor, whom they felt they could not avoid, from Rebecca’s alertness in going to the door, a duty which always interested her beyond any other.\n\nIt was a gentleman’s voice; it was a voice that Fanny was just turning pale about, when Mr. Crawford walked into the room.\n\nGood sense, like hers, will always act when really called upon; and she found that she had been able to name him to her mother, and recall her remembrance of the name, as that of “William’s friend,” though she could not previously have believed herself capable of uttering a syllable at such a moment. The consciousness of his being known there only as William’s friend was some support. Having introduced him, however, and being all reseated, the terrors that occurred of what this visit might lead to were overpowering, and she fancied herself on the point of fainting away.\n\nWhile trying to keep herself alive, their visitor, who had at first approached her with as animated a countenance as ever, was wisely and kindly keeping his eyes away, and giving her time to recover, while he devoted himself entirely to her mother, addressing her, and attending to her with the utmost politeness and propriety, at the same time with a degree of friendliness, of interest at least, which was making his manner perfect.\n\nMrs. Price’s manners were also at their best. Warmed by the sight of such a friend to her son, and regulated by the wish of appearing to advantage before him, she was overflowing with gratitude—artless, maternal gratitude—which could not be unpleasing. Mr. Price was out, which she regretted very much. Fanny was just recovered enough to feel that she could not regret it; for to her many other sources of uneasiness was added the severe one of shame for the home in which he found her. She might scold herself for the weakness, but there was no scolding it away. She was ashamed, and she would have been yet more ashamed of her father than of all the rest.\n\nThey talked of William, a subject on which Mrs. Price could never tire; and Mr. Crawford was as warm in his commendation as even her heart could wish. She felt that she had never seen so agreeable a man in her life; and was only astonished to find that, so great and so agreeable as he was, he should be come down to Portsmouth neither on a visit to the port-admiral, nor the commissioner, nor yet with the intention of going over to the island, nor of seeing the dockyard. Nothing of all that she had been used to think of as the proof of importance, or the employment of wealth, had brought him to Portsmouth. He had reached it late the night before, was come for a day or two, was staying at the Crown, had accidentally met with a navy officer or two of his acquaintance since his arrival, but had no object of that kind in coming.\n\nBy the time he had given all this information, it was not unreasonable to suppose that Fanny might be looked at and spoken to; and she was tolerably able to bear his eye, and hear that he had spent half an hour with his sister the evening before his leaving London; that she had sent her best and kindest love, but had had no time for writing; that he thought himself lucky in seeing Mary for even half an hour, having spent scarcely twenty-four hours in London, after his return from Norfolk, before he set off again; that her cousin Edmund was in town, had been in town, he understood, a few days; that he had not seen him himself, but that he was well, had left them all well at Mansfield, and was to dine, as yesterday, with the Frasers.\n\nFanny listened collectedly, even to the last-mentioned circumstance; nay, it seemed a relief to her worn mind to be at any certainty; and the words, “then by this time it is all settled,” passed internally, without more evidence of emotion than a faint blush.\n\nAfter talking a little more about Mansfield, a subject in which her interest was most apparent, Crawford began to hint at the expediency of an early walk. “It was a lovely morning, and at that season of the year a fine morning so often turned off, that it was wisest for everybody not to delay their exercise”; and such hints producing nothing, he soon proceeded to a positive recommendation to Mrs. Price and her daughters to take their walk without loss of time. Now they came to an understanding. Mrs. Price, it appeared, scarcely ever stirred out of doors, except of a Sunday; she owned she could seldom, with her large family, find time for a walk. “Would she not, then, persuade her daughters to take advantage of such weather, and allow him the pleasure of attending them?” Mrs. Price was greatly obliged and very complying. “Her daughters were very much confined; Portsmouth was a sad place; they did not often get out; and she knew they had some errands in the town, which they would be very glad to do.” And the consequence was, that Fanny, strange as it was—strange, awkward, and distressing—found herself and Susan, within ten minutes, walking towards the High Street with Mr. Crawford.\n\nIt was soon pain upon pain, confusion upon confusion; for they were hardly in the High Street before they met her father, whose appearance was not the better from its being Saturday. He stopt; and, ungentlemanlike as he looked, Fanny was obliged to introduce him to Mr. Crawford. She could not have a doubt of the manner in which Mr. Crawford must be struck. He must be ashamed and disgusted altogether. He must soon give her up, and cease to have the smallest inclination for the match; and yet, though she had been so much wanting his affection to be cured, this was a sort of cure that would be almost as bad as the complaint; and I believe there is scarcely a young lady in the United Kingdoms who would not rather put up with the misfortune of being sought by a clever, agreeable man, than have him driven away by the vulgarity of her nearest relations.\n\nMr. Crawford probably could not regard his future father-in-law with any idea of taking him for a model in dress; but (as Fanny instantly, and to her great relief, discerned) her father was a very different man, a very different Mr. Price in his behaviour to this most highly respected stranger, from what he was in his own family at home. His manners now, though not polished, were more than passable: they were grateful, animated, manly; his expressions were those of an attached father, and a sensible man; his loud tones did very well in the open air, and there was not a single oath to be heard. Such was his instinctive compliment to the good manners of Mr. Crawford; and, be the consequence what it might, Fanny’s immediate feelings were infinitely soothed.\n\nThe conclusion of the two gentlemen’s civilities was an offer of Mr. Price’s to take Mr. Crawford into the dockyard, which Mr. Crawford, desirous of accepting as a favour what was intended as such, though he had seen the dockyard again and again, and hoping to be so much the longer with Fanny, was very gratefully disposed to avail himself of, if the Miss Prices were not afraid of the fatigue; and as it was somehow or other ascertained, or inferred, or at least acted upon, that they were not at all afraid, to the dockyard they were all to go; and but for Mr. Crawford, Mr. Price would have turned thither directly, without the smallest consideration for his daughters’ errands in the High Street. He took care, however, that they should be allowed to go to the shops they came out expressly to visit; and it did not delay them long, for Fanny could so little bear to excite impatience, or be waited for, that before the gentlemen, as they stood at the door, could do more than begin upon the last naval regulations, or settle the number of three-deckers now in commission, their companions were ready to proceed.\n\nThey were then to set forward for the dockyard at once, and the walk would have been conducted—according to Mr. Crawford’s opinion—in a singular manner, had Mr. Price been allowed the entire regulation of it, as the two girls, he found, would have been left to follow, and keep up with them or not, as they could, while they walked on together at their own hasty pace. He was able to introduce some improvement occasionally, though by no means to the extent he wished; he absolutely would not walk away from them; and at any crossing or any crowd, when Mr. Price was only calling out, “Come, girls; come, Fan; come, Sue, take care of yourselves; keep a sharp lookout!” he would give them his particular attendance.\n\nOnce fairly in the dockyard, he began to reckon upon some happy intercourse with Fanny, as they were very soon joined by a brother lounger of Mr. Price’s, who was come to take his daily survey of how things went on, and who must prove a far more worthy companion than himself; and after a time the two officers seemed very well satisfied going about together, and discussing matters of equal and never-failing interest, while the young people sat down upon some timbers in the yard, or found a seat on board a vessel in the stocks which they all went to look at. Fanny was most conveniently in want of rest. Crawford could not have wished her more fatigued or more ready to sit down; but he could have wished her sister away. A quick-looking girl of Susan’s age was the very worst third in the world: totally different from Lady Bertram, all eyes and ears; and there was no introducing the main point before her. He must content himself with being only generally agreeable, and letting Susan have her share of entertainment, with the indulgence, now and then, of a look or hint for the better-informed and conscious Fanny. Norfolk was what he had mostly to talk of: there he had been some time, and everything there was rising in importance from his present schemes. Such a man could come from no place, no society, without importing something to amuse; his journeys and his acquaintance were all of use, and Susan was entertained in a way quite new to her. For Fanny, somewhat more was related than the accidental agreeableness of the parties he had been in. For her approbation, the particular reason of his going into Norfolk at all, at this unusual time of year, was given. It had been real business, relative to the renewal of a lease in which the welfare of a large and—he believed—industrious family was at stake. He had suspected his agent of some underhand dealing; of meaning to bias him against the deserving; and he had determined to go himself, and thoroughly investigate the merits of the case. He had gone, had done even more good than he had foreseen, had been useful to more than his first plan had comprehended, and was now able to congratulate himself upon it, and to feel that in performing a duty, he had secured agreeable recollections for his own mind. He had introduced himself to some tenants whom he had never seen before; he had begun making acquaintance with cottages whose very existence, though on his own estate, had been hitherto unknown to him. This was aimed, and well aimed, at Fanny. It was pleasing to hear him speak so properly; here he had been acting as he ought to do. To be the friend of the poor and the oppressed! Nothing could be more grateful to her; and she was on the point of giving him an approving look, when it was all frightened off by his adding a something too pointed of his hoping soon to have an assistant, a friend, a guide in every plan of utility or charity for Everingham: a somebody that would make Everingham and all about it a dearer object than it had ever been yet.\n\nShe turned away, and wished he would not say such things. She was willing to allow he might have more good qualities than she had been wont to suppose. She began to feel the possibility of his turning out well at last; but he was and must ever be completely unsuited to her, and ought not to think of her.\n\nHe perceived that enough had been said of Everingham, and that it would be as well to talk of something else, and turned to Mansfield. He could not have chosen better; that was a topic to bring back her attention and her looks almost instantly. It was a real indulgence to her to hear or to speak of Mansfield. Now so long divided from everybody who knew the place, she felt it quite the voice of a friend when he mentioned it, and led the way to her fond exclamations in praise of its beauties and comforts, and by his honourable tribute to its inhabitants allowed her to gratify her own heart in the warmest eulogium, in speaking of her uncle as all that was clever and good, and her aunt as having the sweetest of all sweet tempers.\n\nHe had a great attachment to Mansfield himself; he said so; he looked forward with the hope of spending much, very much, of his time there; always there, or in the neighbourhood. He particularly built upon a very happy summer and autumn there this year; he felt that it would be so: he depended upon it; a summer and autumn infinitely superior to the last. As animated, as diversified, as social, but with circumstances of superiority undescribable.\n\n“Mansfield, Sotherton, Thornton Lacey,” he continued; “what a society will be comprised in those houses! And at Michaelmas, perhaps, a fourth may be added: some small hunting-box in the vicinity of everything so dear; for as to any partnership in Thornton Lacey, as Edmund Bertram once good-humouredly proposed, I hope I foresee two objections: two fair, excellent, irresistible objections to that plan.”\n\nFanny was doubly silenced here; though when the moment was passed, could regret that she had not forced herself into the acknowledged comprehension of one half of his meaning, and encouraged him to say something more of his sister and Edmund. It was a subject which she must learn to speak of, and the weakness that shrunk from it would soon be quite unpardonable.\n\nWhen Mr. Price and his friend had seen all that they wished, or had time for, the others were ready to return; and in the course of their walk back, Mr. Crawford contrived a minute’s privacy for telling Fanny that his only business in Portsmouth was to see her; that he was come down for a couple of days on her account, and hers only, and because he could not endure a longer total separation. She was sorry, really sorry; and yet in spite of this and the two or three other things which she wished he had not said, she thought him altogether improved since she had seen him; he was much more gentle, obliging, and attentive to other people’s feelings than he had ever been at Mansfield; she had never seen him so agreeable—so near being agreeable; his behaviour to her father could not offend, and there was something particularly kind and proper in the notice he took of Susan. He was decidedly improved. She wished the next day over, she wished he had come only for one day; but it was not so very bad as she would have expected: the pleasure of talking of Mansfield was so very great!\n\nBefore they parted, she had to thank him for another pleasure, and one of no trivial kind. Her father asked him to do them the honour of taking his mutton with them, and Fanny had time for only one thrill of horror, before he declared himself prevented by a prior engagement. He was engaged to dinner already both for that day and the next; he had met with some acquaintance at the Crown who would not be denied; he should have the honour, however, of waiting on them again on the morrow, etc., and so they parted—Fanny in a state of actual felicity from escaping so horrible an evil!\n\nTo have had him join their family dinner-party, and see all their deficiencies, would have been dreadful! Rebecca’s cookery and Rebecca’s waiting, and Betsey’s eating at table without restraint, and pulling everything about as she chose, were what Fanny herself was not yet enough inured to for her often to make a tolerable meal. She was nice only from natural delicacy, but he had been brought up in a school of luxury and epicurism.\n\n# CHAPTER XLII\n\nThe Prices were just setting off for church the next day when Mr. Crawford appeared again. He came, not to stop, but to join them; he was asked to go with them to the Garrison chapel, which was exactly what he had intended, and they all walked thither together.\n\nThe family were now seen to advantage. Nature had given them no inconsiderable share of beauty, and every Sunday dressed them in their cleanest skins and best attire. Sunday always brought this comfort to Fanny, and on this Sunday she felt it more than ever. Her poor mother now did not look so very unworthy of being Lady Bertram’s sister as she was but too apt to look. It often grieved her to the heart to think of the contrast between them; to think that where nature had made so little difference, circumstances should have made so much, and that her mother, as handsome as Lady Bertram, and some years her junior, should have an appearance so much more worn and faded, so comfortless, so slatternly, so shabby. But Sunday made her a very creditable and tolerably cheerful-looking Mrs. Price, coming abroad with a fine family of children, feeling a little respite of her weekly cares, and only discomposed if she saw her boys run into danger, or Rebecca pass by with a flower in her hat.\n\nIn chapel they were obliged to divide, but Mr. Crawford took care not to be divided from the female branch; and after chapel he still continued with them, and made one in the family party on the ramparts.\n\nMrs. Price took her weekly walk on the ramparts every fine Sunday throughout the year, always going directly after morning service and staying till dinner-time. It was her public place: there she met her acquaintance, heard a little news, talked over the badness of the Portsmouth servants, and wound up her spirits for the six days ensuing.\n\nThither they now went; Mr. Crawford most happy to consider the Miss Prices as his peculiar charge; and before they had been there long, somehow or other, there was no saying how, Fanny could not have believed it, but he was walking between them with an arm of each under his, and she did not know how to prevent or put an end to it. It made her uncomfortable for a time, but yet there were enjoyments in the day and in the view which would be felt.\n\nThe day was uncommonly lovely. It was really March; but it was April in its mild air, brisk soft wind, and bright sun, occasionally clouded for a minute; and everything looked so beautiful under the influence of such a sky, the effects of the shadows pursuing each other on the ships at Spithead and the island beyond, with the ever-varying hues of the sea, now at high water, dancing in its glee and dashing against the ramparts with so fine a sound, produced altogether such a combination of charms for Fanny, as made her gradually almost careless of the circumstances under which she felt them. Nay, had she been without his arm, she would soon have known that she needed it, for she wanted strength for a two hours’ saunter of this kind, coming, as it generally did, upon a week’s previous inactivity. Fanny was beginning to feel the effect of being debarred from her usual regular exercise; she had lost ground as to health since her being in Portsmouth; and but for Mr. Crawford and the beauty of the weather would soon have been knocked up now.\n\nThe loveliness of the day, and of the view, he felt like herself. They often stopt with the same sentiment and taste, leaning against the wall, some minutes, to look and admire; and considering he was not Edmund, Fanny could not but allow that he was sufficiently open to the charms of nature, and very well able to express his admiration. She had a few tender reveries now and then, which he could sometimes take advantage of to look in her face without detection; and the result of these looks was, that though as bewitching as ever, her face was less blooming than it ought to be. She said she was very well, and did not like to be supposed otherwise; but take it all in all, he was convinced that her present residence could not be comfortable, and therefore could not be salutary for her, and he was growing anxious for her being again at Mansfield, where her own happiness, and his in seeing her, must be so much greater.\n\n“You have been here a month, I think?” said he.\n\n“No; not quite a month. It is only four weeks to-morrow since I left Mansfield.”\n\n“You are a most accurate and honest reckoner. I should call that a month.”\n\n“I did not arrive here till Tuesday evening.”\n\n“And it is to be a two months’ visit, is not?”\n\n“Yes. My uncle talked of two months. I suppose it will not be less.”\n\n“And how are you to be conveyed back again? Who comes for you?”\n\n“I do not know. I have heard nothing about it yet from my aunt. Perhaps I may be to stay longer. It may not be convenient for me to be fetched exactly at the two months’ end.”\n\nAfter a moment’s reflection, Mr. Crawford replied, “I know Mansfield, I know its way, I know its faults towards you. I know the danger of your being so far forgotten, as to have your comforts give way to the imaginary convenience of any single being in the family. I am aware that you may be left here week after week, if Sir Thomas cannot settle everything for coming himself, or sending your aunt’s maid for you, without involving the slightest alteration of the arrangements which he may have laid down for the next quarter of a year. This will not do. Two months is an ample allowance; I should think six weeks quite enough. I am considering your sister’s health,” said he, addressing himself to Susan, “which I think the confinement of Portsmouth unfavourable to. She requires constant air and exercise. When you know her as well as I do, I am sure you will agree that she does, and that she ought never to be long banished from the free air and liberty of the country. If, therefore” (turning again to Fanny), “you find yourself growing unwell, and any difficulties arise about your returning to Mansfield, without waiting for the two months to be ended, that must not be regarded as of any consequence, if you feel yourself at all less strong or comfortable than usual, and will only let my sister know it, give her only the slightest hint, she and I will immediately come down, and take you back to Mansfield. You know the ease and the pleasure with which this would be done. You know all that would be felt on the occasion.”\n\nFanny thanked him, but tried to laugh it off.\n\n“I am perfectly serious,” he replied, “as you perfectly know. And I hope you will not be cruelly concealing any tendency to indisposition. Indeed, you shall not; it shall not be in your power; for so long only as you positively say, in every letter to Mary, ‘I am well,’ and I know you cannot speak or write a falsehood, so long only shall you be considered as well.”\n\nFanny thanked him again, but was affected and distressed to a degree that made it impossible for her to say much, or even to be certain of what she ought to say. This was towards the close of their walk. He attended them to the last, and left them only at the door of their own house, when he knew them to be going to dinner, and therefore pretended to be waited for elsewhere.\n\n“I wish you were not so tired,” said he, still detaining Fanny after all the others were in the house—“I wish I left you in stronger health. Is there anything I can do for you in town? I have half an idea of going into Norfolk again soon. I am not satisfied about Maddison. I am sure he still means to impose on me if possible, and get a cousin of his own into a certain mill, which I design for somebody else. I must come to an understanding with him. I must make him know that I will not be tricked on the south side of Everingham, any more than on the north: that I will be master of my own property. I was not explicit enough with him before. The mischief such a man does on an estate, both as to the credit of his employer and the welfare of the poor, is inconceivable. I have a great mind to go back into Norfolk directly, and put everything at once on such a footing as cannot be afterwards swerved from. Maddison is a clever fellow; I do not wish to displace him, provided he does not try to displace me; but it would be simple to be duped by a man who has no right of creditor to dupe me, and worse than simple to let him give me a hard-hearted, griping fellow for a tenant, instead of an honest man, to whom I have given half a promise already. Would it not be worse than simple? Shall I go? Do you advise it?”\n\n“I advise! You know very well what is right.”\n\n“Yes. When you give me your opinion, I always know what is right. Your judgment is my rule of right.”\n\n“Oh, no! do not say so. We have all a better guide in ourselves, if we would attend to it, than any other person can be. Good-bye; I wish you a pleasant journey to-morrow.”\n\n“Is there nothing I can do for you in town?”\n\n“Nothing; I am much obliged to you.”\n\n“Have you no message for anybody?”\n\n“My love to your sister, if you please; and when you see my cousin, my cousin Edmund, I wish you would be so good as to say that I suppose I shall soon hear from him.”\n\n“Certainly; and if he is lazy or negligent, I will write his excuses myself.”\n\nHe could say no more, for Fanny would be no longer detained. He pressed her hand, looked at her, and was gone. He went to while away the next three hours as he could, with his other acquaintance, till the best dinner that a capital inn afforded was ready for their enjoyment, and she turned in to her more simple one immediately.\n\nTheir general fare bore a very different character; and could he have suspected how many privations, besides that of exercise, she endured in her father’s house, he would have wondered that her looks were not much more affected than he found them. She was so little equal to Rebecca’s puddings and Rebecca’s hashes, brought to table, as they all were, with such accompaniments of half-cleaned plates, and not half-cleaned knives and forks, that she was very often constrained to defer her heartiest meal till she could send her brothers in the evening for biscuits and buns. After being nursed up at Mansfield, it was too late in the day to be hardened at Portsmouth; and though Sir Thomas, had he known all, might have thought his niece in the most promising way of being starved, both mind and body, into a much juster value for Mr. Crawford’s good company and good fortune, he would probably have feared to push his experiment farther, lest she might die under the cure.\n\nFanny was out of spirits all the rest of the day. Though tolerably secure of not seeing Mr. Crawford again, she could not help being low. It was parting with somebody of the nature of a friend; and though, in one light, glad to have him gone, it seemed as if she was now deserted by everybody; it was a sort of renewed separation from Mansfield; and she could not think of his returning to town, and being frequently with Mary and Edmund, without feelings so near akin to envy as made her hate herself for having them.\n\nHer dejection had no abatement from anything passing around her; a friend or two of her father’s, as always happened if he was not with them, spent the long, long evening there; and from six o’clock till half-past nine, there was little intermission of noise or grog. She was very low. The wonderful improvement which she still fancied in Mr. Crawford was the nearest to administering comfort of anything within the current of her thoughts. Not considering in how different a circle she had been just seeing him, nor how much might be owing to contrast, she was quite persuaded of his being astonishingly more gentle and regardful of others than formerly. And, if in little things, must it not be so in great? So anxious for her health and comfort, so very feeling as he now expressed himself, and really seemed, might not it be fairly supposed that he would not much longer persevere in a suit so distressing to her?\n\n# CHAPTER XLIII\n\nIt was presumed that Mr. Crawford was travelling back, to London, on the morrow, for nothing more was seen of him at Mr. Price’s; and two days afterwards, it was a fact ascertained to Fanny by the following letter from his sister, opened and read by her, on another account, with the most anxious curiosity:—\n\n“I have to inform you, my dearest Fanny, that Henry has been down to Portsmouth to see you; that he had a delightful walk with you to the dockyard last Saturday, and one still more to be dwelt on the next day, on the ramparts; when the balmy air, the sparkling sea, and your sweet looks and conversation were altogether in the most delicious harmony, and afforded sensations which are to raise ecstasy even in retrospect. This, as well as I understand, is to be the substance of my information. He makes me write, but I do not know what else is to be communicated, except this said visit to Portsmouth, and these two said walks, and his introduction to your family, especially to a fair sister of yours, a fine girl of fifteen, who was of the party on the ramparts, taking her first lesson, I presume, in love. I have not time for writing much, but it would be out of place if I had, for this is to be a mere letter of business, penned for the purpose of conveying necessary information, which could not be delayed without risk of evil. My dear, dear Fanny, if I had you here, how I would talk to you! You should listen to me till you were tired, and advise me till you were still tired more; but it is impossible to put a hundredth part of my great mind on paper, so I will abstain altogether, and leave you to guess what you like. I have no news for you. You have politics, of course; and it would be too bad to plague you with the names of people and parties that fill up my time. I ought to have sent you an account of your cousin’s first party, but I was lazy, and now it is too long ago; suffice it, that everything was just as it ought to be, in a style that any of her connexions must have been gratified to witness, and that her own dress and manners did her the greatest credit. My friend, Mrs. Fraser, is mad for such a house, and it would not make me miserable. I go to Lady Stornaway after Easter; she seems in high spirits, and very happy. I fancy Lord S. is very good-humoured and pleasant in his own family, and I do not think him so very ill-looking as I did—at least, one sees many worse. He will not do by the side of your cousin Edmund. Of the last-mentioned hero, what shall I say? If I avoided his name entirely, it would look suspicious. I will say, then, that we have seen him two or three times, and that my friends here are very much struck with his gentlemanlike appearance. Mrs. Fraser (no bad judge) declares she knows but three men in town who have so good a person, height, and air; and I must confess, when he dined here the other day, there were none to compare with him, and we were a party of sixteen. Luckily there is no distinction of dress nowadays to tell tales, but—but—but Yours affectionately.”\n\n“I had almost forgot (it was Edmund’s fault: he gets into my head more than does me good) one very material thing I had to say from Henry and myself—I mean about our taking you back into Northamptonshire. My dear little creature, do not stay at Portsmouth to lose your pretty looks. Those vile sea-breezes are the ruin of beauty and health. My poor aunt always felt affected if within ten miles of the sea, which the Admiral of course never believed, but I know it was so. I am at your service and Henry’s, at an hour’s notice. I should like the scheme, and we would make a little circuit, and shew you Everingham in our way, and perhaps you would not mind passing through London, and seeing the inside of St. George’s, Hanover Square. Only keep your cousin Edmund from me at such a time: I should not like to be tempted. What a long letter! one word more. Henry, I find, has some idea of going into Norfolk again upon some business that you approve; but this cannot possibly be permitted before the middle of next week; that is, he cannot anyhow be spared till after the 14th, for we have a party that evening. The value of a man like Henry, on such an occasion, is what you can have no conception of; so you must take it upon my word to be inestimable. He will see the Rushworths, which I own I am not sorry for—having a little curiosity, and so I think has he—though he will not acknowledge it.”\n\nThis was a letter to be run through eagerly, to be read deliberately, to supply matter for much reflection, and to leave everything in greater suspense than ever. The only certainty to be drawn from it was, that nothing decisive had yet taken place. Edmund had not yet spoken. How Miss Crawford really felt, how she meant to act, or might act without or against her meaning; whether his importance to her were quite what it had been before the last separation; whether, if lessened, it were likely to lessen more, or to recover itself, were subjects for endless conjecture, and to be thought of on that day and many days to come, without producing any conclusion. The idea that returned the oftenest was that Miss Crawford, after proving herself cooled and staggered by a return to London habits, would yet prove herself in the end too much attached to him to give him up. She would try to be more ambitious than her heart would allow. She would hesitate, she would tease, she would condition, she would require a great deal, but she would finally accept.\n\nThis was Fanny’s most frequent expectation. A house in town—that, she thought, must be impossible. Yet there was no saying what Miss Crawford might not ask. The prospect for her cousin grew worse and worse. The woman who could speak of him, and speak only of his appearance! What an unworthy attachment! To be deriving support from the commendations of Mrs. Fraser! She who had known him intimately half a year! Fanny was ashamed of her. Those parts of the letter which related only to Mr. Crawford and herself, touched her, in comparison, slightly. Whether Mr. Crawford went into Norfolk before or after the 14th was certainly no concern of hers, though, everything considered, she thought he would go without delay. That Miss Crawford should endeavour to secure a meeting between him and Mrs. Rushworth, was all in her worst line of conduct, and grossly unkind and ill-judged; but she hoped he would not be actuated by any such degrading curiosity. He acknowledged no such inducement, and his sister ought to have given him credit for better feelings than her own.\n\nShe was yet more impatient for another letter from town after receiving this than she had been before; and for a few days was so unsettled by it altogether, by what had come, and what might come, that her usual readings and conversation with Susan were much suspended. She could not command her attention as she wished. If Mr. Crawford remembered her message to her cousin, she thought it very likely, most likely, that he would write to her at all events; it would be most consistent with his usual kindness; and till she got rid of this idea, till it gradually wore off, by no letters appearing in the course of three or four days more, she was in a most restless, anxious state.\n\nAt length, a something like composure succeeded. Suspense must be submitted to, and must not be allowed to wear her out, and make her useless. Time did something, her own exertions something more, and she resumed her attentions to Susan, and again awakened the same interest in them.\n\nSusan was growing very fond of her, and though without any of the early delight in books which had been so strong in Fanny, with a disposition much less inclined to sedentary pursuits, or to information for information’s sake, she had so strong a desire of not appearing ignorant, as, with a good clear understanding, made her a most attentive, profitable, thankful pupil. Fanny was her oracle. Fanny’s explanations and remarks were a most important addition to every essay, or every chapter of history. What Fanny told her of former times dwelt more on her mind than the pages of Goldsmith; and she paid her sister the compliment of preferring her style to that of any printed author. The early habit of reading was wanting.\n\nTheir conversations, however, were not always on subjects so high as history or morals. Others had their hour; and of lesser matters, none returned so often, or remained so long between them, as Mansfield Park, a description of the people, the manners, the amusements, the ways of Mansfield Park. Susan, who had an innate taste for the genteel and well-appointed, was eager to hear, and Fanny could not but indulge herself in dwelling on so beloved a theme. She hoped it was not wrong; though, after a time, Susan’s very great admiration of everything said or done in her uncle’s house, and earnest longing to go into Northamptonshire, seemed almost to blame her for exciting feelings which could not be gratified.\n\nPoor Susan was very little better fitted for home than her elder sister; and as Fanny grew thoroughly to understand this, she began to feel that when her own release from Portsmouth came, her happiness would have a material drawback in leaving Susan behind. That a girl so capable of being made everything good should be left in such hands, distressed her more and more. Were she likely to have a home to invite her to, what a blessing it would be! And had it been possible for her to return Mr. Crawford’s regard, the probability of his being very far from objecting to such a measure would have been the greatest increase of all her own comforts. She thought he was really good-tempered, and could fancy his entering into a plan of that sort most pleasantly.\n\n# CHAPTER XLIV\n\nSeven weeks of the two months were very nearly gone, when the one letter, the letter from Edmund, so long expected, was put into Fanny’s hands. As she opened, and saw its length, she prepared herself for a minute detail of happiness and a profusion of love and praise towards the fortunate creature who was now mistress of his fate. These were the contents—\n\n“My Dear Fanny,—Excuse me that I have not written before. Crawford told me that you were wishing to hear from me, but I found it impossible to write from London, and persuaded myself that you would understand my silence. Could I have sent a few happy lines, they should not have been wanting, but nothing of that nature was ever in my power. I am returned to Mansfield in a less assured state than when I left it. My hopes are much weaker. You are probably aware of this already. So very fond of you as Miss Crawford is, it is most natural that she should tell you enough of her own feelings to furnish a tolerable guess at mine. I will not be prevented, however, from making my own communication. Our confidences in you need not clash. I ask no questions. There is something soothing in the idea that we have the same friend, and that whatever unhappy differences of opinion may exist between us, we are united in our love of you. It will be a comfort to me to tell you how things now are, and what are my present plans, if plans I can be said to have. I have been returned since Saturday. I was three weeks in London, and saw her (for London) very often. I had every attention from the Frasers that could be reasonably expected. I dare say I was not reasonable in carrying with me hopes of an intercourse at all like that of Mansfield. It was her manner, however, rather than any unfrequency of meeting. Had she been different when I did see her, I should have made no complaint, but from the very first she was altered: my first reception was so unlike what I had hoped, that I had almost resolved on leaving London again directly. I need not particularise. You know the weak side of her character, and may imagine the sentiments and expressions which were torturing me. She was in high spirits, and surrounded by those who were giving all the support of their own bad sense to her too lively mind. I do not like Mrs. Fraser. She is a cold-hearted, vain woman, who has married entirely from convenience, and though evidently unhappy in her marriage, places her disappointment not to faults of judgment, or temper, or disproportion of age, but to her being, after all, less affluent than many of her acquaintance, especially than her sister, Lady Stornaway, and is the determined supporter of everything mercenary and ambitious, provided it be only mercenary and ambitious enough. I look upon her intimacy with those two sisters as the greatest misfortune of her life and mine. They have been leading her astray for years. Could she be detached from them!—and sometimes I do not despair of it, for the affection appears to me principally on their side. They are very fond of her; but I am sure she does not love them as she loves you. When I think of her great attachment to you, indeed, and the whole of her judicious, upright conduct as a sister, she appears a very different creature, capable of everything noble, and I am ready to blame myself for a too harsh construction of a playful manner. I cannot give her up, Fanny. She is the only woman in the world whom I could ever think of as a wife. If I did not believe that she had some regard for me, of course I should not say this, but I do believe it. I am convinced that she is not without a decided preference. I have no jealousy of any individual. It is the influence of the fashionable world altogether that I am jealous of. It is the habits of wealth that I fear. Her ideas are not higher than her own fortune may warrant, but they are beyond what our incomes united could authorise. There is comfort, however, even here. I could better bear to lose her because not rich enough, than because of my profession. That would only prove her affection not equal to sacrifices, which, in fact, I am scarcely justified in asking; and, if I am refused, that, I think, will be the honest motive. Her prejudices, I trust, are not so strong as they were. You have my thoughts exactly as they arise, my dear Fanny; perhaps they are sometimes contradictory, but it will not be a less faithful picture of my mind. Having once begun, it is a pleasure to me to tell you all I feel. I cannot give her up. Connected as we already are, and, I hope, are to be, to give up Mary Crawford would be to give up the society of some of those most dear to me; to banish myself from the very houses and friends whom, under any other distress, I should turn to for consolation. The loss of Mary I must consider as comprehending the loss of Crawford and of Fanny. Were it a decided thing, an actual refusal, I hope I should know how to bear it, and how to endeavour to weaken her hold on my heart, and in the course of a few years—but I am writing nonsense. Were I refused, I must bear it; and till I am, I can never cease to try for her. This is the truth. The only question is how? What may be the likeliest means? I have sometimes thought of going to London again after Easter, and sometimes resolved on doing nothing till she returns to Mansfield. Even now, she speaks with pleasure of being in Mansfield in June; but June is at a great distance, and I believe I shall write to her. I have nearly determined on explaining myself by letter. To be at an early certainty is a material object. My present state is miserably irksome. Considering everything, I think a letter will be decidedly the best method of explanation. I shall be able to write much that I could not say, and shall be giving her time for reflection before she resolves on her answer, and I am less afraid of the result of reflection than of an immediate hasty impulse; I think I am. My greatest danger would lie in her consulting Mrs. Fraser, and I at a distance unable to help my own cause. A letter exposes to all the evil of consultation, and where the mind is anything short of perfect decision, an adviser may, in an unlucky moment, lead it to do what it may afterwards regret. I must think this matter over a little. This long letter, full of my own concerns alone, will be enough to tire even the friendship of a Fanny. The last time I saw Crawford was at Mrs. Fraser’s party. I am more and more satisfied with all that I see and hear of him. There is not a shadow of wavering. He thoroughly knows his own mind, and acts up to his resolutions: an inestimable quality. I could not see him and my eldest sister in the same room without recollecting what you once told me, and I acknowledge that they did not meet as friends. There was marked coolness on her side. They scarcely spoke. I saw him draw back surprised, and I was sorry that Mrs. Rushworth should resent any former supposed slight to Miss Bertram. You will wish to hear my opinion of Maria’s degree of comfort as a wife. There is no appearance of unhappiness. I hope they get on pretty well together. I dined twice in Wimpole Street, and might have been there oftener, but it is mortifying to be with Rushworth as a brother. Julia seems to enjoy London exceedingly. I had little enjoyment there, but have less here. We are not a lively party. You are very much wanted. I miss you more than I can express. My mother desires her best love, and hopes to hear from you soon. She talks of you almost every hour, and I am sorry to find how many weeks more she is likely to be without you. My father means to fetch you himself, but it will not be till after Easter, when he has business in town. You are happy at Portsmouth, I hope, but this must not be a yearly visit. I want you at home, that I may have your opinion about Thornton Lacey. I have little heart for extensive improvements till I know that it will ever have a mistress. I think I shall certainly write. It is quite settled that the Grants go to Bath; they leave Mansfield on Monday. I am glad of it. I am not comfortable enough to be fit for anybody; but your aunt seems to feel out of luck that such an article of Mansfield news should fall to my pen instead of hers.—Yours ever, my dearest Fanny.”\n\n“I never will, no, I certainly never will wish for a letter again,” was Fanny’s secret declaration as she finished this. “What do they bring but disappointment and sorrow? Not till after Easter! How shall I bear it? And my poor aunt talking of me every hour!”\n\nFanny checked the tendency of these thoughts as well as she could, but she was within half a minute of starting the idea that Sir Thomas was quite unkind, both to her aunt and to herself. As for the main subject of the letter, there was nothing in that to soothe irritation. She was almost vexed into displeasure and anger against Edmund. “There is no good in this delay,” said she. “Why is not it settled? He is blinded, and nothing will open his eyes; nothing can, after having had truths before him so long in vain. He will marry her, and be poor and miserable. God grant that her influence do not make him cease to be respectable!” She looked over the letter again. “‘So very fond of me!’ ’tis nonsense all. She loves nobody but herself and her brother. Her friends leading her astray for years! She is quite as likely to have led them astray. They have all, perhaps, been corrupting one another; but if they are so much fonder of her than she is of them, she is the less likely to have been hurt, except by their flattery. ‘The only woman in the world whom he could ever think of as a wife.’ I firmly believe it. It is an attachment to govern his whole life. Accepted or refused, his heart is wedded to her for ever. ‘The loss of Mary I must consider as comprehending the loss of Crawford and Fanny.’ Edmund, you do not know me. The families would never be connected if you did not connect them! Oh! write, write. Finish it at once. Let there be an end of this suspense. Fix, commit, condemn yourself.”\n\nSuch sensations, however, were too near akin to resentment to be long guiding Fanny’s soliloquies. She was soon more softened and sorrowful. His warm regard, his kind expressions, his confidential treatment, touched her strongly. He was only too good to everybody. It was a letter, in short, which she would not but have had for the world, and which could never be valued enough. This was the end of it.\n\nEverybody at all addicted to letter-writing, without having much to say, which will include a large proportion of the female world at least, must feel with Lady Bertram that she was out of luck in having such a capital piece of Mansfield news as the certainty of the Grants going to Bath, occur at a time when she could make no advantage of it, and will admit that it must have been very mortifying to her to see it fall to the share of her thankless son, and treated as concisely as possible at the end of a long letter, instead of having it to spread over the largest part of a page of her own. For though Lady Bertram rather shone in the epistolary line, having early in her marriage, from the want of other employment, and the circumstance of Sir Thomas’s being in Parliament, got into the way of making and keeping correspondents, and formed for herself a very creditable, common-place, amplifying style, so that a very little matter was enough for her: she could not do entirely without any; she must have something to write about, even to her niece; and being so soon to lose all the benefit of Dr. Grant’s gouty symptoms and Mrs. Grant’s morning calls, it was very hard upon her to be deprived of one of the last epistolary uses she could put them to.\n\nThere was a rich amends, however, preparing for her. Lady Bertram’s hour of good luck came. Within a few days from the receipt of Edmund’s letter, Fanny had one from her aunt, beginning thus—\n\n“My Dear Fanny,—I take up my pen to communicate some very alarming intelligence, which I make no doubt will give you much concern”.\n\nThis was a great deal better than to have to take up the pen to acquaint her with all the particulars of the Grants’ intended journey, for the present intelligence was of a nature to promise occupation for the pen for many days to come, being no less than the dangerous illness of her eldest son, of which they had received notice by express a few hours before.\n\nTom had gone from London with a party of young men to Newmarket, where a neglected fall and a good deal of drinking had brought on a fever; and when the party broke up, being unable to move, had been left by himself at the house of one of these young men to the comforts of sickness and solitude, and the attendance only of servants. Instead of being soon well enough to follow his friends, as he had then hoped, his disorder increased considerably, and it was not long before he thought so ill of himself as to be as ready as his physician to have a letter despatched to Mansfield.\n\n“This distressing intelligence, as you may suppose,” observed her ladyship, after giving the substance of it, “has agitated us exceedingly, and we cannot prevent ourselves from being greatly alarmed and apprehensive for the poor invalid, whose state Sir Thomas fears may be very critical; and Edmund kindly proposes attending his brother immediately, but I am happy to add that Sir Thomas will not leave me on this distressing occasion, as it would be too trying for me. We shall greatly miss Edmund in our small circle, but I trust and hope he will find the poor invalid in a less alarming state than might be apprehended, and that he will be able to bring him to Mansfield shortly, which Sir Thomas proposes should be done, and thinks best on every account, and I flatter myself the poor sufferer will soon be able to bear the removal without material inconvenience or injury. As I have little doubt of your feeling for us, my dear Fanny, under these distressing circumstances, I will write again very soon.”\n\nFanny’s feelings on the occasion were indeed considerably more warm and genuine than her aunt’s style of writing. She felt truly for them all. Tom dangerously ill, Edmund gone to attend him, and the sadly small party remaining at Mansfield, were cares to shut out every other care, or almost every other. She could just find selfishness enough to wonder whether Edmund had written to Miss Crawford before this summons came, but no sentiment dwelt long with her that was not purely affectionate and disinterestedly anxious. Her aunt did not neglect her: she wrote again and again; they were receiving frequent accounts from Edmund, and these accounts were as regularly transmitted to Fanny, in the same diffuse style, and the same medley of trusts, hopes, and fears, all following and producing each other at haphazard. It was a sort of playing at being frightened. The sufferings which Lady Bertram did not see had little power over her fancy; and she wrote very comfortably about agitation, and anxiety, and poor invalids, till Tom was actually conveyed to Mansfield, and her own eyes had beheld his altered appearance. Then a letter which she had been previously preparing for Fanny was finished in a different style, in the language of real feeling and alarm; then she wrote as she might have spoken. “He is just come, my dear Fanny, and is taken upstairs; and I am so shocked to see him, that I do not know what to do. I am sure he has been very ill. Poor Tom! I am quite grieved for him, and very much frightened, and so is Sir Thomas; and how glad I should be if you were here to comfort me. But Sir Thomas hopes he will be better to-morrow, and says we must consider his journey.”\n\nThe real solicitude now awakened in the maternal bosom was not soon over. Tom’s extreme impatience to be removed to Mansfield, and experience those comforts of home and family which had been little thought of in uninterrupted health, had probably induced his being conveyed thither too early, as a return of fever came on, and for a week he was in a more alarming state than ever. They were all very seriously frightened. Lady Bertram wrote her daily terrors to her niece, who might now be said to live upon letters, and pass all her time between suffering from that of to-day and looking forward to to-morrow’s. Without any particular affection for her eldest cousin, her tenderness of heart made her feel that she could not spare him, and the purity of her principles added yet a keener solicitude, when she considered how little useful, how little self-denying his life had (apparently) been.\n\nSusan was her only companion and listener on this, as on more common occasions. Susan was always ready to hear and to sympathise. Nobody else could be interested in so remote an evil as illness in a family above an hundred miles off; not even Mrs. Price, beyond a brief question or two, if she saw her daughter with a letter in her hand, and now and then the quiet observation of, “My poor sister Bertram must be in a great deal of trouble.”\n\nSo long divided and so differently situated, the ties of blood were little more than nothing. An attachment, originally as tranquil as their tempers, was now become a mere name. Mrs. Price did quite as much for Lady Bertram as Lady Bertram would have done for Mrs. Price. Three or four Prices might have been swept away, any or all except Fanny and William, and Lady Bertram would have thought little about it; or perhaps might have caught from Mrs. Norris’s lips the cant of its being a very happy thing and a great blessing to their poor dear sister Price to have them so well provided for.\n\n# CHAPTER XLV\n\nAt about the week’s end from his return to Mansfield, Tom’s immediate danger was over, and he was so far pronounced safe as to make his mother perfectly easy; for being now used to the sight of him in his suffering, helpless state, and hearing only the best, and never thinking beyond what she heard, with no disposition for alarm and no aptitude at a hint, Lady Bertram was the happiest subject in the world for a little medical imposition. The fever was subdued; the fever had been his complaint; of course he would soon be well again. Lady Bertram could think nothing less, and Fanny shared her aunt’s security, till she received a few lines from Edmund, written purposely to give her a clearer idea of his brother’s situation, and acquaint her with the apprehensions which he and his father had imbibed from the physician with respect to some strong hectic symptoms, which seemed to seize the frame on the departure of the fever. They judged it best that Lady Bertram should not be harassed by alarms which, it was to be hoped, would prove unfounded; but there was no reason why Fanny should not know the truth. They were apprehensive for his lungs.\n\nA very few lines from Edmund shewed her the patient and the sickroom in a juster and stronger light than all Lady Bertram’s sheets of paper could do. There was hardly any one in the house who might not have described, from personal observation, better than herself; not one who was not more useful at times to her son. She could do nothing but glide in quietly and look at him; but when able to talk or be talked to, or read to, Edmund was the companion he preferred. His aunt worried him by her cares, and Sir Thomas knew not how to bring down his conversation or his voice to the level of irritation and feebleness. Edmund was all in all. Fanny would certainly believe him so at least, and must find that her estimation of him was higher than ever when he appeared as the attendant, supporter, cheerer of a suffering brother. There was not only the debility of recent illness to assist: there was also, as she now learnt, nerves much affected, spirits much depressed to calm and raise, and her own imagination added that there must be a mind to be properly guided.\n\nThe family were not consumptive, and she was more inclined to hope than fear for her cousin, except when she thought of Miss Crawford; but Miss Crawford gave her the idea of being the child of good luck, and to her selfishness and vanity it would be good luck to have Edmund the only son.\n\nEven in the sick chamber the fortunate Mary was not forgotten. Edmund’s letter had this postscript. “On the subject of my last, I had actually begun a letter when called away by Tom’s illness, but I have now changed my mind, and fear to trust the influence of friends. When Tom is better, I shall go.”\n\nSuch was the state of Mansfield, and so it continued, with scarcely any change, till Easter. A line occasionally added by Edmund to his mother’s letter was enough for Fanny’s information. Tom’s amendment was alarmingly slow.\n\nEaster came particularly late this year, as Fanny had most sorrowfully considered, on first learning that she had no chance of leaving Portsmouth till after it. It came, and she had yet heard nothing of her return—nothing even of the going to London, which was to precede her return. Her aunt often expressed a wish for her, but there was no notice, no message from the uncle on whom all depended. She supposed he could not yet leave his son, but it was a cruel, a terrible delay to her. The end of April was coming on; it would soon be almost three months, instead of two, that she had been absent from them all, and that her days had been passing in a state of penance, which she loved them too well to hope they would thoroughly understand; and who could yet say when there might be leisure to think of or fetch her?\n\nHer eagerness, her impatience, her longings to be with them, were such as to bring a line or two of Cowper’s Tirocinium for ever before her. “With what intense desire she wants her home,” was continually on her tongue, as the truest description of a yearning which she could not suppose any schoolboy’s bosom to feel more keenly.\n\nWhen she had been coming to Portsmouth, she had loved to call it her home, had been fond of saying that she was going home; the word had been very dear to her, and so it still was, but it must be applied to Mansfield. That was now the home. Portsmouth was Portsmouth; Mansfield was home. They had been long so arranged in the indulgence of her secret meditations, and nothing was more consolatory to her than to find her aunt using the same language: “I cannot but say I much regret your being from home at this distressing time, so very trying to my spirits. I trust and hope, and sincerely wish you may never be absent from home so long again,” were most delightful sentences to her. Still, however, it was her private regale. Delicacy to her parents made her careful not to betray such a preference of her uncle’s house. It was always: “When I go back into Northamptonshire, or when I return to Mansfield, I shall do so and so.” For a great while it was so, but at last the longing grew stronger, it overthrew caution, and she found herself talking of what she should do when she went home before she was aware. She reproached herself, coloured, and looked fearfully towards her father and mother. She need not have been uneasy. There was no sign of displeasure, or even of hearing her. They were perfectly free from any jealousy of Mansfield. She was as welcome to wish herself there as to be there.\n\nIt was sad to Fanny to lose all the pleasures of spring. She had not known before what pleasures she had to lose in passing March and April in a town. She had not known before how much the beginnings and progress of vegetation had delighted her. What animation, both of body and mind, she had derived from watching the advance of that season which cannot, in spite of its capriciousness, be unlovely, and seeing its increasing beauties from the earliest flowers in the warmest divisions of her aunt’s garden, to the opening of leaves of her uncle’s plantations, and the glory of his woods. To be losing such pleasures was no trifle; to be losing them, because she was in the midst of closeness and noise, to have confinement, bad air, bad smells, substituted for liberty, freshness, fragrance, and verdure, was infinitely worse: but even these incitements to regret were feeble, compared with what arose from the conviction of being missed by her best friends, and the longing to be useful to those who were wanting her!\n\nCould she have been at home, she might have been of service to every creature in the house. She felt that she must have been of use to all. To all she must have saved some trouble of head or hand; and were it only in supporting the spirits of her aunt Bertram, keeping her from the evil of solitude, or the still greater evil of a restless, officious companion, too apt to be heightening danger in order to enhance her own importance, her being there would have been a general good. She loved to fancy how she could have read to her aunt, how she could have talked to her, and tried at once to make her feel the blessing of what was, and prepare her mind for what might be; and how many walks up and down stairs she might have saved her, and how many messages she might have carried.\n\nIt astonished her that Tom’s sisters could be satisfied with remaining in London at such a time, through an illness which had now, under different degrees of danger, lasted several weeks. They might return to Mansfield when they chose; travelling could be no difficulty to them, and she could not comprehend how both could still keep away. If Mrs. Rushworth could imagine any interfering obligations, Julia was certainly able to quit London whenever she chose. It appeared from one of her aunt’s letters that Julia had offered to return if wanted, but this was all. It was evident that she would rather remain where she was.\n\nFanny was disposed to think the influence of London very much at war with all respectable attachments. She saw the proof of it in Miss Crawford, as well as in her cousins; her attachment to Edmund had been respectable, the most respectable part of her character; her friendship for herself had at least been blameless. Where was either sentiment now? It was so long since Fanny had had any letter from her, that she had some reason to think lightly of the friendship which had been so dwelt on. It was weeks since she had heard anything of Miss Crawford or of her other connexions in town, except through Mansfield, and she was beginning to suppose that she might never know whether Mr. Crawford had gone into Norfolk again or not till they met, and might never hear from his sister any more this spring, when the following letter was received to revive old and create some new sensations—\n\n“Forgive me, my dear Fanny, as soon as you can, for my long silence, and behave as if you could forgive me directly. This is my modest request and expectation, for you are so good, that I depend upon being treated better than I deserve, and I write now to beg an immediate answer. I want to know the state of things at Mansfield Park, and you, no doubt, are perfectly able to give it. One should be a brute not to feel for the distress they are in; and from what I hear, poor Mr. Bertram has a bad chance of ultimate recovery. I thought little of his illness at first. I looked upon him as the sort of person to be made a fuss with, and to make a fuss himself in any trifling disorder, and was chiefly concerned for those who had to nurse him; but now it is confidently asserted that he is really in a decline, that the symptoms are most alarming, and that part of the family, at least, are aware of it. If it be so, I am sure you must be included in that part, that discerning part, and therefore entreat you to let me know how far I have been rightly informed. I need not say how rejoiced I shall be to hear there has been any mistake, but the report is so prevalent that I confess I cannot help trembling. To have such a fine young man cut off in the flower of his days is most melancholy. Poor Sir Thomas will feel it dreadfully. I really am quite agitated on the subject. Fanny, Fanny, I see you smile and look cunning, but, upon my honour, I never bribed a physician in my life. Poor young man! If he is to die, there will be two poor young men less in the world; and with a fearless face and bold voice would I say to any one, that wealth and consequence could fall into no hands more deserving of them. It was a foolish precipitation last Christmas, but the evil of a few days may be blotted out in part. Varnish and gilding hide many stains. It will be but the loss of the Esquire after his name. With real affection, Fanny, like mine, more might be overlooked. Write to me by return of post, judge of my anxiety, and do not trifle with it. Tell me the real truth, as you have it from the fountainhead. And now, do not trouble yourself to be ashamed of either my feelings or your own. Believe me, they are not only natural, they are philanthropic and virtuous. I put it to your conscience, whether ‘Sir Edmund’ would not do more good with all the Bertram property than any other possible ‘Sir.’ Had the Grants been at home I would not have troubled you, but you are now the only one I can apply to for the truth, his sisters not being within my reach. Mrs. R. has been spending the Easter with the Aylmers at Twickenham (as to be sure you know), and is not yet returned; and Julia is with the cousins who live near Bedford Square, but I forget their name and street. Could I immediately apply to either, however, I should still prefer you, because it strikes me that they have all along been so unwilling to have their own amusements cut up, as to shut their eyes to the truth. I suppose Mrs. R.’s Easter holidays will not last much longer; no doubt they are thorough holidays to her. The Aylmers are pleasant people; and her husband away, she can have nothing but enjoyment. I give her credit for promoting his going dutifully down to Bath, to fetch his mother; but how will she and the dowager agree in one house? Henry is not at hand, so I have nothing to say from him. Do not you think Edmund would have been in town again long ago, but for this illness?—Yours ever, Mary.”\n\n“I had actually begun folding my letter when Henry walked in, but he brings no intelligence to prevent my sending it. Mrs. R. knows a decline is apprehended; he saw her this morning: she returns to Wimpole Street to-day; the old lady is come. Now do not make yourself uneasy with any queer fancies because he has been spending a few days at Richmond. He does it every spring. Be assured he cares for nobody but you. At this very moment he is wild to see you, and occupied only in contriving the means for doing so, and for making his pleasure conduce to yours. In proof, he repeats, and more eagerly, what he said at Portsmouth about our conveying you home, and I join him in it with all my soul. Dear Fanny, write directly, and tell us to come. It will do us all good. He and I can go to the Parsonage, you know, and be no trouble to our friends at Mansfield Park. It would really be gratifying to see them all again, and a little addition of society might be of infinite use to them; and as to yourself, you must feel yourself to be so wanted there, that you cannot in conscience—conscientious as you are—keep away, when you have the means of returning. I have not time or patience to give half Henry’s messages; be satisfied that the spirit of each and every one is unalterable affection.”\n\nFanny’s disgust at the greater part of this letter, with her extreme reluctance to bring the writer of it and her cousin Edmund together, would have made her (as she felt) incapable of judging impartially whether the concluding offer might be accepted or not. To herself, individually, it was most tempting. To be finding herself, perhaps within three days, transported to Mansfield, was an image of the greatest felicity, but it would have been a material drawback to be owing such felicity to persons in whose feelings and conduct, at the present moment, she saw so much to condemn: the sister’s feelings, the brother’s conduct, her cold-hearted ambition, his thoughtless vanity. To have him still the acquaintance, the flirt perhaps, of Mrs. Rushworth! She was mortified. She had thought better of him. Happily, however, she was not left to weigh and decide between opposite inclinations and doubtful notions of right; there was no occasion to determine whether she ought to keep Edmund and Mary asunder or not. She had a rule to apply to, which settled everything. Her awe of her uncle, and her dread of taking a liberty with him, made it instantly plain to her what she had to do. She must absolutely decline the proposal. If he wanted, he would send for her; and even to offer an early return was a presumption which hardly anything would have seemed to justify. She thanked Miss Crawford, but gave a decided negative. “Her uncle, she understood, meant to fetch her; and as her cousin’s illness had continued so many weeks without her being thought at all necessary, she must suppose her return would be unwelcome at present, and that she should be felt an encumbrance.”\n\nHer representation of her cousin’s state at this time was exactly according to her own belief of it, and such as she supposed would convey to the sanguine mind of her correspondent the hope of everything she was wishing for. Edmund would be forgiven for being a clergyman, it seemed, under certain conditions of wealth; and this, she suspected, was all the conquest of prejudice which he was so ready to congratulate himself upon. She had only learnt to think nothing of consequence but money.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVI\n\nAs Fanny could not doubt that her answer was conveying a real disappointment, she was rather in expectation, from her knowledge of Miss Crawford’s temper, of being urged again; and though no second letter arrived for the space of a week, she had still the same feeling when it did come.\n\nOn receiving it, she could instantly decide on its containing little writing, and was persuaded of its having the air of a letter of haste and business. Its object was unquestionable; and two moments were enough to start the probability of its being merely to give her notice that they should be in Portsmouth that very day, and to throw her into all the agitation of doubting what she ought to do in such a case. If two moments, however, can surround with difficulties, a third can disperse them; and before she had opened the letter, the possibility of Mr. and Miss Crawford’s having applied to her uncle and obtained his permission was giving her ease. This was the letter—\n\n“A most scandalous, ill-natured rumour has just reached me, and I write, dear Fanny, to warn you against giving the least credit to it, should it spread into the country. Depend upon it, there is some mistake, and that a day or two will clear it up; at any rate, that Henry is blameless, and in spite of a moment’s etourderie, thinks of nobody but you. Say not a word of it; hear nothing, surmise nothing, whisper nothing till I write again. I am sure it will be all hushed up, and nothing proved but Rushworth’s folly. If they are gone, I would lay my life they are only gone to Mansfield Park, and Julia with them. But why would not you let us come for you? I wish you may not repent it.—Yours, etc.”\n\nFanny stood aghast. As no scandalous, ill-natured rumour had reached her, it was impossible for her to understand much of this strange letter. She could only perceive that it must relate to Wimpole Street and Mr. Crawford, and only conjecture that something very imprudent had just occurred in that quarter to draw the notice of the world, and to excite her jealousy, in Miss Crawford’s apprehension, if she heard it. Miss Crawford need not be alarmed for her. She was only sorry for the parties concerned and for Mansfield, if the report should spread so far; but she hoped it might not. If the Rushworths were gone themselves to Mansfield, as was to be inferred from what Miss Crawford said, it was not likely that anything unpleasant should have preceded them, or at least should make any impression.\n\nAs to Mr. Crawford, she hoped it might give him a knowledge of his own disposition, convince him that he was not capable of being steadily attached to any one woman in the world, and shame him from persisting any longer in addressing herself.\n\nIt was very strange! She had begun to think he really loved her, and to fancy his affection for her something more than common; and his sister still said that he cared for nobody else. Yet there must have been some marked display of attentions to her cousin, there must have been some strong indiscretion, since her correspondent was not of a sort to regard a slight one.\n\nVery uncomfortable she was, and must continue, till she heard from Miss Crawford again. It was impossible to banish the letter from her thoughts, and she could not relieve herself by speaking of it to any human being. Miss Crawford need not have urged secrecy with so much warmth; she might have trusted to her sense of what was due to her cousin.\n\nThe next day came and brought no second letter. Fanny was disappointed. She could still think of little else all the morning; but, when her father came back in the afternoon with the daily newspaper as usual, she was so far from expecting any elucidation through such a channel that the subject was for a moment out of her head.\n\nShe was deep in other musing. The remembrance of her first evening in that room, of her father and his newspaper, came across her. No candle was now wanted. The sun was yet an hour and half above the horizon. She felt that she had, indeed, been three months there; and the sun’s rays falling strongly into the parlour, instead of cheering, made her still more melancholy, for sunshine appeared to her a totally different thing in a town and in the country. Here, its power was only a glare: a stifling, sickly glare, serving but to bring forward stains and dirt that might otherwise have slept. There was neither health nor gaiety in sunshine in a town. She sat in a blaze of oppressive heat, in a cloud of moving dust, and her eyes could only wander from the walls, marked by her father’s head, to the table cut and notched by her brothers, where stood the tea-board never thoroughly cleaned, the cups and saucers wiped in streaks, the milk a mixture of motes floating in thin blue, and the bread and butter growing every minute more greasy than even Rebecca’s hands had first produced it. Her father read his newspaper, and her mother lamented over the ragged carpet as usual, while the tea was in preparation, and wished Rebecca would mend it; and Fanny was first roused by his calling out to her, after humphing and considering over a particular paragraph: “What’s the name of your great cousins in town, Fan?”\n\nA moment’s recollection enabled her to say, “Rushworth, sir.”\n\n“And don’t they live in Wimpole Street?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“Then, there’s the devil to pay among them, that’s all! There” (holding out the paper to her); “much good may such fine relations do you. I don’t know what Sir Thomas may think of such matters; he may be too much of the courtier and fine gentleman to like his daughter the less. But, by G—! if she belonged to me, I’d give her the rope’s end as long as I could stand over her. A little flogging for man and woman too would be the best way of preventing such things.”\n\nFanny read to herself that “it was with infinite concern the newspaper had to announce to the world a matrimonial fracas in the family of Mr. R. of Wimpole Street; the beautiful Mrs. R., whose name had not long been enrolled in the lists of Hymen, and who had promised to become so brilliant a leader in the fashionable world, having quitted her husband’s roof in company with the well-known and captivating Mr. C., the intimate friend and associate of Mr. R., and it was not known even to the editor of the newspaper whither they were gone.”\n\n“It is a mistake, sir,” said Fanny instantly; “it must be a mistake, it cannot be true; it must mean some other people.”\n\nShe spoke from the instinctive wish of delaying shame; she spoke with a resolution which sprung from despair, for she spoke what she did not, could not believe herself. It had been the shock of conviction as she read. The truth rushed on her; and how she could have spoken at all, how she could even have breathed, was afterwards matter of wonder to herself.\n\nMr. Price cared too little about the report to make her much answer. “It might be all a lie,” he acknowledged; “but so many fine ladies were going to the devil nowadays that way, that there was no answering for anybody.”\n\n“Indeed, I hope it is not true,” said Mrs. Price plaintively; “it would be so very shocking! If I have spoken once to Rebecca about that carpet, I am sure I have spoke at least a dozen times; have not I, Betsey? And it would not be ten minutes’ work.”\n\nThe horror of a mind like Fanny’s, as it received the conviction of such guilt, and began to take in some part of the misery that must ensue, can hardly be described. At first, it was a sort of stupefaction; but every moment was quickening her perception of the horrible evil. She could not doubt, she dared not indulge a hope, of the paragraph being false. Miss Crawford’s letter, which she had read so often as to make every line her own, was in frightful conformity with it. Her eager defence of her brother, her hope of its being hushed up, her evident agitation, were all of a piece with something very bad; and if there was a woman of character in existence, who could treat as a trifle this sin of the first magnitude, who would try to gloss it over, and desire to have it unpunished, she could believe Miss Crawford to be the woman! Now she could see her own mistake as to who were gone, or said to be gone. It was not Mr. and Mrs. Rushworth; it was Mrs. Rushworth and Mr. Crawford.\n\nFanny seemed to herself never to have been shocked before. There was no possibility of rest. The evening passed without a pause of misery, the night was totally sleepless. She passed only from feelings of sickness to shudderings of horror; and from hot fits of fever to cold. The event was so shocking, that there were moments even when her heart revolted from it as impossible: when she thought it could not be. A woman married only six months ago; a man professing himself devoted, even engaged to another; that other her near relation; the whole family, both families connected as they were by tie upon tie; all friends, all intimate together! It was too horrible a confusion of guilt, too gross a complication of evil, for human nature, not in a state of utter barbarism, to be capable of! yet her judgment told her it was so. His unsettled affections, wavering with his vanity, Maria’s decided attachment, and no sufficient principle on either side, gave it possibility: Miss Crawford’s letter stampt it a fact.\n\nWhat would be the consequence? Whom would it not injure? Whose views might it not affect? Whose peace would it not cut up for ever? Miss Crawford, herself, Edmund; but it was dangerous, perhaps, to tread such ground. She confined herself, or tried to confine herself, to the simple, indubitable family misery which must envelop all, if it were indeed a matter of certified guilt and public exposure. The mother’s sufferings, the father’s; there she paused. Julia’s, Tom’s, Edmund’s; there a yet longer pause. They were the two on whom it would fall most horribly. Sir Thomas’s parental solicitude and high sense of honour and decorum, Edmund’s upright principles, unsuspicious temper, and genuine strength of feeling, made her think it scarcely possible for them to support life and reason under such disgrace; and it appeared to her that, as far as this world alone was concerned, the greatest blessing to every one of kindred with Mrs. Rushworth would be instant annihilation.\n\nNothing happened the next day, or the next, to weaken her terrors. Two posts came in, and brought no refutation, public or private. There was no second letter to explain away the first from Miss Crawford; there was no intelligence from Mansfield, though it was now full time for her to hear again from her aunt. This was an evil omen. She had, indeed, scarcely the shadow of a hope to soothe her mind, and was reduced to so low and wan and trembling a condition, as no mother, not unkind, except Mrs. Price could have overlooked, when the third day did bring the sickening knock, and a letter was again put into her hands. It bore the London postmark, and came from Edmund.\n\n“Dear Fanny,—You know our present wretchedness. May God support you under your share! We have been here two days, but there is nothing to be done. They cannot be traced. You may not have heard of the last blow—Julia’s elopement; she is gone to Scotland with Yates. She left London a few hours before we entered it. At any other time this would have been felt dreadfully. Now it seems nothing; yet it is an heavy aggravation. My father is not overpowered. More cannot be hoped. He is still able to think and act; and I write, by his desire, to propose your returning home. He is anxious to get you there for my mother’s sake. I shall be at Portsmouth the morning after you receive this, and hope to find you ready to set off for Mansfield. My father wishes you to invite Susan to go with you for a few months. Settle it as you like; say what is proper; I am sure you will feel such an instance of his kindness at such a moment! Do justice to his meaning, however I may confuse it. You may imagine something of my present state. There is no end of the evil let loose upon us. You will see me early by the mail.—Yours, etc.”\n\nNever had Fanny more wanted a cordial. Never had she felt such a one as this letter contained. To-morrow! to leave Portsmouth to-morrow! She was, she felt she was, in the greatest danger of being exquisitely happy, while so many were miserable. The evil which brought such good to her! She dreaded lest she should learn to be insensible of it. To be going so soon, sent for so kindly, sent for as a comfort, and with leave to take Susan, was altogether such a combination of blessings as set her heart in a glow, and for a time seemed to distance every pain, and make her incapable of suitably sharing the distress even of those whose distress she thought of most. Julia’s elopement could affect her comparatively but little; she was amazed and shocked; but it could not occupy her, could not dwell on her mind. She was obliged to call herself to think of it, and acknowledge it to be terrible and grievous, or it was escaping her, in the midst of all the agitating pressing joyful cares attending this summons to herself.\n\nThere is nothing like employment, active indispensable employment, for relieving sorrow. Employment, even melancholy, may dispel melancholy, and her occupations were hopeful. She had so much to do, that not even the horrible story of Mrs. Rushworth (now fixed to the last point of certainty), could affect her as it had done before. She had not time to be miserable. Within twenty-four hours she was hoping to be gone; her father and mother must be spoken to, Susan prepared, everything got ready. Business followed business; the day was hardly long enough. The happiness she was imparting, too, happiness very little alloyed by the black communication which must briefly precede it—the joyful consent of her father and mother to Susan’s going with her—the general satisfaction with which the going of both seemed regarded, and the ecstasy of Susan herself, was all serving to support her spirits.\n\nThe affliction of the Bertrams was little felt in the family. Mrs. Price talked of her poor sister for a few minutes, but how to find anything to hold Susan’s clothes, because Rebecca took away all the boxes and spoilt them, was much more in her thoughts: and as for Susan, now unexpectedly gratified in the first wish of her heart, and knowing nothing personally of those who had sinned, or of those who were sorrowing—if she could help rejoicing from beginning to end, it was as much as ought to be expected from human virtue at fourteen.\n\nAs nothing was really left for the decision of Mrs. Price, or the good offices of Rebecca, everything was rationally and duly accomplished, and the girls were ready for the morrow. The advantage of much sleep to prepare them for their journey was impossible. The cousin who was travelling towards them could hardly have less than visited their agitated spirits—one all happiness, the other all varying and indescribable perturbation.\n\nBy eight in the morning Edmund was in the house. The girls heard his entrance from above, and Fanny went down. The idea of immediately seeing him, with the knowledge of what he must be suffering, brought back all her own first feelings. He so near her, and in misery. She was ready to sink as she entered the parlour. He was alone, and met her instantly; and she found herself pressed to his heart with only these words, just articulate, “My Fanny, my only sister; my only comfort now!” She could say nothing; nor for some minutes could he say more.\n\nHe turned away to recover himself, and when he spoke again, though his voice still faltered, his manner shewed the wish of self-command, and the resolution of avoiding any farther allusion. “Have you breakfasted? When shall you be ready? Does Susan go?” were questions following each other rapidly. His great object was to be off as soon as possible. When Mansfield was considered, time was precious; and the state of his own mind made him find relief only in motion. It was settled that he should order the carriage to the door in half an hour. Fanny answered for their having breakfasted and being quite ready in half an hour. He had already ate, and declined staying for their meal. He would walk round the ramparts, and join them with the carriage. He was gone again; glad to get away even from Fanny.\n\nHe looked very ill; evidently suffering under violent emotions, which he was determined to suppress. She knew it must be so, but it was terrible to her.\n\nThe carriage came; and he entered the house again at the same moment, just in time to spend a few minutes with the family, and be a witness—but that he saw nothing—of the tranquil manner in which the daughters were parted with, and just in time to prevent their sitting down to the breakfast-table, which, by dint of much unusual activity, was quite and completely ready as the carriage drove from the door. Fanny’s last meal in her father’s house was in character with her first: she was dismissed from it as hospitably as she had been welcomed.\n\nHow her heart swelled with joy and gratitude as she passed the barriers of Portsmouth, and how Susan’s face wore its broadest smiles, may be easily conceived. Sitting forwards, however, and screened by her bonnet, those smiles were unseen.\n\nThe journey was likely to be a silent one. Edmund’s deep sighs often reached Fanny. Had he been alone with her, his heart must have opened in spite of every resolution; but Susan’s presence drove him quite into himself, and his attempts to talk on indifferent subjects could never be long supported.\n\nFanny watched him with never-failing solicitude, and sometimes catching his eye, revived an affectionate smile, which comforted her; but the first day’s journey passed without her hearing a word from him on the subjects that were weighing him down. The next morning produced a little more. Just before their setting out from Oxford, while Susan was stationed at a window, in eager observation of the departure of a large family from the inn, the other two were standing by the fire; and Edmund, particularly struck by the alteration in Fanny’s looks, and from his ignorance of the daily evils of her father’s house, attributing an undue share of the change, attributing all to the recent event, took her hand, and said in a low, but very expressive tone, “No wonder—you must feel it—you must suffer. How a man who had once loved, could desert you! But yours—your regard was new compared with——Fanny, think of me!”\n\nThe first division of their journey occupied a long day, and brought them, almost knocked up, to Oxford; but the second was over at a much earlier hour. They were in the environs of Mansfield long before the usual dinner-time, and as they approached the beloved place, the hearts of both sisters sank a little. Fanny began to dread the meeting with her aunts and Tom, under so dreadful a humiliation; and Susan to feel with some anxiety, that all her best manners, all her lately acquired knowledge of what was practised here, was on the point of being called into action. Visions of good and ill breeding, of old vulgarisms and new gentilities, were before her; and she was meditating much upon silver forks, napkins, and finger-glasses. Fanny had been everywhere awake to the difference of the country since February; but when they entered the Park her perceptions and her pleasures were of the keenest sort. It was three months, full three months, since her quitting it, and the change was from winter to summer. Her eye fell everywhere on lawns and plantations of the freshest green; and the trees, though not fully clothed, were in that delightful state when farther beauty is known to be at hand, and when, while much is actually given to the sight, more yet remains for the imagination. Her enjoyment, however, was for herself alone. Edmund could not share it. She looked at him, but he was leaning back, sunk in a deeper gloom than ever, and with eyes closed, as if the view of cheerfulness oppressed him, and the lovely scenes of home must be shut out.\n\nIt made her melancholy again; and the knowledge of what must be enduring there, invested even the house, modern, airy, and well situated as it was, with a melancholy aspect.\n\nBy one of the suffering party within they were expected with such impatience as she had never known before. Fanny had scarcely passed the solemn-looking servants, when Lady Bertram came from the drawing-room to meet her; came with no indolent step; and falling on her neck, said, “Dear Fanny! now I shall be comfortable.”\n\n# CHAPTER XLVII\n\nIt had been a miserable party, each of the three believing themselves most miserable. Mrs. Norris, however, as most attached to Maria, was really the greatest sufferer. Maria was her first favourite, the dearest of all; the match had been her own contriving, as she had been wont with such pride of heart to feel and say, and this conclusion of it almost overpowered her.\n\nShe was an altered creature, quieted, stupefied, indifferent to everything that passed. The being left with her sister and nephew, and all the house under her care, had been an advantage entirely thrown away; she had been unable to direct or dictate, or even fancy herself useful. When really touched by affliction, her active powers had been all benumbed; and neither Lady Bertram nor Tom had received from her the smallest support or attempt at support. She had done no more for them than they had done for each other. They had been all solitary, helpless, and forlorn alike; and now the arrival of the others only established her superiority in wretchedness. Her companions were relieved, but there was no good for her. Edmund was almost as welcome to his brother as Fanny to her aunt; but Mrs. Norris, instead of having comfort from either, was but the more irritated by the sight of the person whom, in the blindness of her anger, she could have charged as the daemon of the piece. Had Fanny accepted Mr. Crawford this could not have happened.\n\nSusan too was a grievance. She had not spirits to notice her in more than a few repulsive looks, but she felt her as a spy, and an intruder, and an indigent niece, and everything most odious. By her other aunt, Susan was received with quiet kindness. Lady Bertram could not give her much time, or many words, but she felt her, as Fanny’s sister, to have a claim at Mansfield, and was ready to kiss and like her; and Susan was more than satisfied, for she came perfectly aware that nothing but ill-humour was to be expected from aunt Norris; and was so provided with happiness, so strong in that best of blessings, an escape from many certain evils, that she could have stood against a great deal more indifference than she met with from the others.\n\nShe was now left a good deal to herself, to get acquainted with the house and grounds as she could, and spent her days very happily in so doing, while those who might otherwise have attended to her were shut up, or wholly occupied each with the person quite dependent on them, at this time, for everything like comfort; Edmund trying to bury his own feelings in exertions for the relief of his brother’s, and Fanny devoted to her aunt Bertram, returning to every former office with more than former zeal, and thinking she could never do enough for one who seemed so much to want her.\n\nTo talk over the dreadful business with Fanny, talk and lament, was all Lady Bertram’s consolation. To be listened to and borne with, and hear the voice of kindness and sympathy in return, was everything that could be done for her. To be otherwise comforted was out of the question. The case admitted of no comfort. Lady Bertram did not think deeply, but, guided by Sir Thomas, she thought justly on all important points; and she saw, therefore, in all its enormity, what had happened, and neither endeavoured herself, nor required Fanny to advise her, to think little of guilt and infamy.\n\nHer affections were not acute, nor was her mind tenacious. After a time, Fanny found it not impossible to direct her thoughts to other subjects, and revive some interest in the usual occupations; but whenever Lady Bertram was fixed on the event, she could see it only in one light, as comprehending the loss of a daughter, and a disgrace never to be wiped off.\n\nFanny learnt from her all the particulars which had yet transpired. Her aunt was no very methodical narrator, but with the help of some letters to and from Sir Thomas, and what she already knew herself, and could reasonably combine, she was soon able to understand quite as much as she wished of the circumstances attending the story.\n\nMrs. Rushworth had gone, for the Easter holidays, to Twickenham, with a family whom she had just grown intimate with: a family of lively, agreeable manners, and probably of morals and discretion to suit, for to their house Mr. Crawford had constant access at all times. His having been in the same neighbourhood Fanny already knew. Mr. Rushworth had been gone at this time to Bath, to pass a few days with his mother, and bring her back to town, and Maria was with these friends without any restraint, without even Julia; for Julia had removed from Wimpole Street two or three weeks before, on a visit to some relations of Sir Thomas; a removal which her father and mother were now disposed to attribute to some view of convenience on Mr. Yates’s account. Very soon after the Rushworths’ return to Wimpole Street, Sir Thomas had received a letter from an old and most particular friend in London, who hearing and witnessing a good deal to alarm him in that quarter, wrote to recommend Sir Thomas’s coming to London himself, and using his influence with his daughter to put an end to the intimacy which was already exposing her to unpleasant remarks, and evidently making Mr. Rushworth uneasy.\n\nSir Thomas was preparing to act upon this letter, without communicating its contents to any creature at Mansfield, when it was followed by another, sent express from the same friend, to break to him the almost desperate situation in which affairs then stood with the young people. Mrs. Rushworth had left her husband’s house: Mr. Rushworth had been in great anger and distress to him (Mr. Harding) for his advice; Mr. Harding feared there had been at least very flagrant indiscretion. The maidservant of Mrs. Rushworth, senior, threatened alarmingly. He was doing all in his power to quiet everything, with the hope of Mrs. Rushworth’s return, but was so much counteracted in Wimpole Street by the influence of Mr. Rushworth’s mother, that the worst consequences might be apprehended.\n\nThis dreadful communication could not be kept from the rest of the family. Sir Thomas set off, Edmund would go with him, and the others had been left in a state of wretchedness, inferior only to what followed the receipt of the next letters from London. Everything was by that time public beyond a hope. The servant of Mrs. Rushworth, the mother, had exposure in her power, and supported by her mistress, was not to be silenced. The two ladies, even in the short time they had been together, had disagreed; and the bitterness of the elder against her daughter-in-law might perhaps arise almost as much from the personal disrespect with which she had herself been treated as from sensibility for her son.\n\nHowever that might be, she was unmanageable. But had she been less obstinate, or of less weight with her son, who was always guided by the last speaker, by the person who could get hold of and shut him up, the case would still have been hopeless, for Mrs. Rushworth did not appear again, and there was every reason to conclude her to be concealed somewhere with Mr. Crawford, who had quitted his uncle’s house, as for a journey, on the very day of her absenting herself.\n\nSir Thomas, however, remained yet a little longer in town, in the hope of discovering and snatching her from farther vice, though all was lost on the side of character.\n\nHis present state Fanny could hardly bear to think of. There was but one of his children who was not at this time a source of misery to him. Tom’s complaints had been greatly heightened by the shock of his sister’s conduct, and his recovery so much thrown back by it, that even Lady Bertram had been struck by the difference, and all her alarms were regularly sent off to her husband; and Julia’s elopement, the additional blow which had met him on his arrival in London, though its force had been deadened at the moment, must, she knew, be sorely felt. She saw that it was. His letters expressed how much he deplored it. Under any circumstances it would have been an unwelcome alliance; but to have it so clandestinely formed, and such a period chosen for its completion, placed Julia’s feelings in a most unfavourable light, and severely aggravated the folly of her choice. He called it a bad thing, done in the worst manner, and at the worst time; and though Julia was yet as more pardonable than Maria as folly than vice, he could not but regard the step she had taken as opening the worst probabilities of a conclusion hereafter like her sister’s. Such was his opinion of the set into which she had thrown herself.\n\nFanny felt for him most acutely. He could have no comfort but in Edmund. Every other child must be racking his heart. His displeasure against herself she trusted, reasoning differently from Mrs. Norris, would now be done away. She should be justified. Mr. Crawford would have fully acquitted her conduct in refusing him; but this, though most material to herself, would be poor consolation to Sir Thomas. Her uncle’s displeasure was terrible to her; but what could her justification or her gratitude and attachment do for him? His stay must be on Edmund alone.\n\nShe was mistaken, however, in supposing that Edmund gave his father no present pain. It was of a much less poignant nature than what the others excited; but Sir Thomas was considering his happiness as very deeply involved in the offence of his sister and friend; cut off by it, as he must be, from the woman whom he had been pursuing with undoubted attachment and strong probability of success; and who, in everything but this despicable brother, would have been so eligible a connexion. He was aware of what Edmund must be suffering on his own behalf, in addition to all the rest, when they were in town: he had seen or conjectured his feelings; and, having reason to think that one interview with Miss Crawford had taken place, from which Edmund derived only increased distress, had been as anxious on that account as on others to get him out of town, and had engaged him in taking Fanny home to her aunt, with a view to his relief and benefit, no less than theirs. Fanny was not in the secret of her uncle’s feelings, Sir Thomas not in the secret of Miss Crawford’s character. Had he been privy to her conversation with his son, he would not have wished her to belong to him, though her twenty thousand pounds had been forty.\n\nThat Edmund must be for ever divided from Miss Crawford did not admit of a doubt with Fanny; and yet, till she knew that he felt the same, her own conviction was insufficient. She thought he did, but she wanted to be assured of it. If he would now speak to her with the unreserve which had sometimes been too much for her before, it would be most consoling; but that she found was not to be. She seldom saw him: never alone. He probably avoided being alone with her. What was to be inferred? That his judgment submitted to all his own peculiar and bitter share of this family affliction, but that it was too keenly felt to be a subject of the slightest communication. This must be his state. He yielded, but it was with agonies which did not admit of speech. Long, long would it be ere Miss Crawford’s name passed his lips again, or she could hope for a renewal of such confidential intercourse as had been.\n\nIt was long. They reached Mansfield on Thursday, and it was not till Sunday evening that Edmund began to talk to her on the subject. Sitting with her on Sunday evening—a wet Sunday evening—the very time of all others when, if a friend is at hand, the heart must be opened, and everything told; no one else in the room, except his mother, who, after hearing an affecting sermon, had cried herself to sleep, it was impossible not to speak; and so, with the usual beginnings, hardly to be traced as to what came first, and the usual declaration that if she would listen to him for a few minutes, he should be very brief, and certainly never tax her kindness in the same way again; she need not fear a repetition; it would be a subject prohibited entirely: he entered upon the luxury of relating circumstances and sensations of the first interest to himself, to one of whose affectionate sympathy he was quite convinced.\n\nHow Fanny listened, with what curiosity and concern, what pain and what delight, how the agitation of his voice was watched, and how carefully her own eyes were fixed on any object but himself, may be imagined. The opening was alarming. He had seen Miss Crawford. He had been invited to see her. He had received a note from Lady Stornaway to beg him to call; and regarding it as what was meant to be the last, last interview of friendship, and investing her with all the feelings of shame and wretchedness which Crawford’s sister ought to have known, he had gone to her in such a state of mind, so softened, so devoted, as made it for a few moments impossible to Fanny’s fears that it should be the last. But as he proceeded in his story, these fears were over. She had met him, he said, with a serious—certainly a serious—even an agitated air; but before he had been able to speak one intelligible sentence, she had introduced the subject in a manner which he owned had shocked him. “‘I heard you were in town,’ said she; ‘I wanted to see you. Let us talk over this sad business. What can equal the folly of our two relations?’ I could not answer, but I believe my looks spoke. She felt reproved. Sometimes how quick to feel! With a graver look and voice she then added, ‘I do not mean to defend Henry at your sister’s expense.’ So she began, but how she went on, Fanny, is not fit, is hardly fit to be repeated to you. I cannot recall all her words. I would not dwell upon them if I could. Their substance was great anger at the folly of each. She reprobated her brother’s folly in being drawn on by a woman whom he had never cared for, to do what must lose him the woman he adored; but still more the folly of poor Maria, in sacrificing such a situation, plunging into such difficulties, under the idea of being really loved by a man who had long ago made his indifference clear. Guess what I must have felt. To hear the woman whom—no harsher name than folly given! So voluntarily, so freely, so coolly to canvass it! No reluctance, no horror, no feminine, shall I say, no modest loathings? This is what the world does. For where, Fanny, shall we find a woman whom nature had so richly endowed? Spoilt, spoilt!”\n\nAfter a little reflection, he went on with a sort of desperate calmness. “I will tell you everything, and then have done for ever. She saw it only as folly, and that folly stamped only by exposure. The want of common discretion, of caution: his going down to Richmond for the whole time of her being at Twickenham; her putting herself in the power of a servant; it was the detection, in short—oh, Fanny! it was the detection, not the offence, which she reprobated. It was the imprudence which had brought things to extremity, and obliged her brother to give up every dearer plan in order to fly with her.”\n\nHe stopt. “And what,” said Fanny (believing herself required to speak), “what could you say?”\n\n“Nothing, nothing to be understood. I was like a man stunned. She went on, began to talk of you; yes, then she began to talk of you, regretting, as well she might, the loss of such a—. There she spoke very rationally. But she has always done justice to you. ‘He has thrown away,’ said she, ‘such a woman as he will never see again. She would have fixed him; she would have made him happy for ever.’ My dearest Fanny, I am giving you, I hope, more pleasure than pain by this retrospect of what might have been—but what never can be now. You do not wish me to be silent? If you do, give me but a look, a word, and I have done.”\n\nNo look or word was given.\n\n“Thank God,” said he. “We were all disposed to wonder, but it seems to have been the merciful appointment of Providence that the heart which knew no guile should not suffer. She spoke of you with high praise and warm affection; yet, even here, there was alloy, a dash of evil; for in the midst of it she could exclaim, ‘Why would not she have him? It is all her fault. Simple girl! I shall never forgive her. Had she accepted him as she ought, they might now have been on the point of marriage, and Henry would have been too happy and too busy to want any other object. He would have taken no pains to be on terms with Mrs. Rushworth again. It would have all ended in a regular standing flirtation, in yearly meetings at Sotherton and Everingham.’ Could you have believed it possible? But the charm is broken. My eyes are opened.”\n\n“Cruel!” said Fanny, “quite cruel. At such a moment to give way to gaiety, to speak with lightness, and to you! Absolute cruelty.”\n\n“Cruelty, do you call it? We differ there. No, hers is not a cruel nature. I do not consider her as meaning to wound my feelings. The evil lies yet deeper: in her total ignorance, unsuspiciousness of there being such feelings; in a perversion of mind which made it natural to her to treat the subject as she did. She was speaking only as she had been used to hear others speak, as she imagined everybody else would speak. Hers are not faults of temper. She would not voluntarily give unnecessary pain to any one, and though I may deceive myself, I cannot but think that for me, for my feelings, she would—. Hers are faults of principle, Fanny; of blunted delicacy and a corrupted, vitiated mind. Perhaps it is best for me, since it leaves me so little to regret. Not so, however. Gladly would I submit to all the increased pain of losing her, rather than have to think of her as I do. I told her so.”\n\n“Did you?”\n\n“Yes; when I left her I told her so.”\n\n“How long were you together?”\n\n“Five-and-twenty minutes. Well, she went on to say that what remained now to be done was to bring about a marriage between them. She spoke of it, Fanny, with a steadier voice than I can.” He was obliged to pause more than once as he continued. “‘We must persuade Henry to marry her,’ said she; ‘and what with honour, and the certainty of having shut himself out for ever from Fanny, I do not despair of it. Fanny he must give up. I do not think that even he could now hope to succeed with one of her stamp, and therefore I hope we may find no insuperable difficulty. My influence, which is not small shall all go that way; and when once married, and properly supported by her own family, people of respectability as they are, she may recover her footing in society to a certain degree. In some circles, we know, she would never be admitted, but with good dinners, and large parties, there will always be those who will be glad of her acquaintance; and there is, undoubtedly, more liberality and candour on those points than formerly. What I advise is, that your father be quiet. Do not let him injure his own cause by interference. Persuade him to let things take their course. If by any officious exertions of his, she is induced to leave Henry’s protection, there will be much less chance of his marrying her than if she remain with him. I know how he is likely to be influenced. Let Sir Thomas trust to his honour and compassion, and it may all end well; but if he get his daughter away, it will be destroying the chief hold.’”\n\nAfter repeating this, Edmund was so much affected that Fanny, watching him with silent, but most tender concern, was almost sorry that the subject had been entered on at all. It was long before he could speak again. At last, “Now, Fanny,” said he, “I shall soon have done. I have told you the substance of all that she said. As soon as I could speak, I replied that I had not supposed it possible, coming in such a state of mind into that house as I had done, that anything could occur to make me suffer more, but that she had been inflicting deeper wounds in almost every sentence. That though I had, in the course of our acquaintance, been often sensible of some difference in our opinions, on points, too, of some moment, it had not entered my imagination to conceive the difference could be such as she had now proved it. That the manner in which she treated the dreadful crime committed by her brother and my sister (with whom lay the greater seduction I pretended not to say), but the manner in which she spoke of the crime itself, giving it every reproach but the right; considering its ill consequences only as they were to be braved or overborne by a defiance of decency and impudence in wrong; and last of all, and above all, recommending to us a compliance, a compromise, an acquiescence in the continuance of the sin, on the chance of a marriage which, thinking as I now thought of her brother, should rather be prevented than sought; all this together most grievously convinced me that I had never understood her before, and that, as far as related to mind, it had been the creature of my own imagination, not Miss Crawford, that I had been too apt to dwell on for many months past. That, perhaps, it was best for me; I had less to regret in sacrificing a friendship, feelings, hopes which must, at any rate, have been torn from me now. And yet, that I must and would confess that, could I have restored her to what she had appeared to me before, I would infinitely prefer any increase of the pain of parting, for the sake of carrying with me the right of tenderness and esteem. This is what I said, the purport of it; but, as you may imagine, not spoken so collectedly or methodically as I have repeated it to you. She was astonished, exceedingly astonished—more than astonished. I saw her change countenance. She turned extremely red. I imagined I saw a mixture of many feelings: a great, though short struggle; half a wish of yielding to truths, half a sense of shame, but habit, habit carried it. She would have laughed if she could. It was a sort of laugh, as she answered, ‘A pretty good lecture, upon my word. Was it part of your last sermon? At this rate you will soon reform everybody at Mansfield and Thornton Lacey; and when I hear of you next, it may be as a celebrated preacher in some great society of Methodists, or as a missionary into foreign parts.’ She tried to speak carelessly, but she was not so careless as she wanted to appear. I only said in reply, that from my heart I wished her well, and earnestly hoped that she might soon learn to think more justly, and not owe the most valuable knowledge we could any of us acquire, the knowledge of ourselves and of our duty, to the lessons of affliction, and immediately left the room. I had gone a few steps, Fanny, when I heard the door open behind me. ‘Mr. Bertram,’ said she. I looked back. ‘Mr. Bertram,’ said she, with a smile; but it was a smile ill-suited to the conversation that had passed, a saucy playful smile, seeming to invite in order to subdue me; at least it appeared so to me. I resisted; it was the impulse of the moment to resist, and still walked on. I have since, sometimes, for a moment, regretted that I did not go back, but I know I was right, and such has been the end of our acquaintance. And what an acquaintance has it been! How have I been deceived! Equally in brother and sister deceived! I thank you for your patience, Fanny. This has been the greatest relief, and now we will have done.”\n\nAnd such was Fanny’s dependence on his words, that for five minutes she thought they had done. Then, however, it all came on again, or something very like it, and nothing less than Lady Bertram’s rousing thoroughly up could really close such a conversation. Till that happened, they continued to talk of Miss Crawford alone, and how she had attached him, and how delightful nature had made her, and how excellent she would have been, had she fallen into good hands earlier. Fanny, now at liberty to speak openly, felt more than justified in adding to his knowledge of her real character, by some hint of what share his brother’s state of health might be supposed to have in her wish for a complete reconciliation. This was not an agreeable intimation. Nature resisted it for a while. It would have been a vast deal pleasanter to have had her more disinterested in her attachment; but his vanity was not of a strength to fight long against reason. He submitted to believe that Tom’s illness had influenced her, only reserving for himself this consoling thought, that considering the many counteractions of opposing habits, she had certainly been more attached to him than could have been expected, and for his sake been more near doing right. Fanny thought exactly the same; and they were also quite agreed in their opinion of the lasting effect, the indelible impression, which such a disappointment must make on his mind. Time would undoubtedly abate somewhat of his sufferings, but still it was a sort of thing which he never could get entirely the better of; and as to his ever meeting with any other woman who could—it was too impossible to be named but with indignation. Fanny’s friendship was all that he had to cling to.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVIII\n\nLet other pens dwell on guilt and misery. I quit such odious subjects as soon as I can, impatient to restore everybody, not greatly in fault themselves, to tolerable comfort, and to have done with all the rest.\n\nMy Fanny, indeed, at this very time, I have the satisfaction of knowing, must have been happy in spite of everything. She must have been a happy creature in spite of all that she felt, or thought she felt, for the distress of those around her. She had sources of delight that must force their way. She was returned to Mansfield Park, she was useful, she was beloved; she was safe from Mr. Crawford; and when Sir Thomas came back she had every proof that could be given in his then melancholy state of spirits, of his perfect approbation and increased regard; and happy as all this must make her, she would still have been happy without any of it, for Edmund was no longer the dupe of Miss Crawford.\n\nIt is true that Edmund was very far from happy himself. He was suffering from disappointment and regret, grieving over what was, and wishing for what could never be. She knew it was so, and was sorry; but it was with a sorrow so founded on satisfaction, so tending to ease, and so much in harmony with every dearest sensation, that there are few who might not have been glad to exchange their greatest gaiety for it.\n\nSir Thomas, poor Sir Thomas, a parent, and conscious of errors in his own conduct as a parent, was the longest to suffer. He felt that he ought not to have allowed the marriage; that his daughter’s sentiments had been sufficiently known to him to render him culpable in authorising it; that in so doing he had sacrificed the right to the expedient, and been governed by motives of selfishness and worldly wisdom. These were reflections that required some time to soften; but time will do almost everything; and though little comfort arose on Mrs. Rushworth’s side for the misery she had occasioned, comfort was to be found greater than he had supposed in his other children. Julia’s match became a less desperate business than he had considered it at first. She was humble, and wishing to be forgiven; and Mr. Yates, desirous of being really received into the family, was disposed to look up to him and be guided. He was not very solid; but there was a hope of his becoming less trifling, of his being at least tolerably domestic and quiet; and at any rate, there was comfort in finding his estate rather more, and his debts much less, than he had feared, and in being consulted and treated as the friend best worth attending to. There was comfort also in Tom, who gradually regained his health, without regaining the thoughtlessness and selfishness of his previous habits. He was the better for ever for his illness. He had suffered, and he had learned to think: two advantages that he had never known before; and the self-reproach arising from the deplorable event in Wimpole Street, to which he felt himself accessory by all the dangerous intimacy of his unjustifiable theatre, made an impression on his mind which, at the age of six-and-twenty, with no want of sense or good companions, was durable in its happy effects. He became what he ought to be: useful to his father, steady and quiet, and not living merely for himself.\n\nHere was comfort indeed! and quite as soon as Sir Thomas could place dependence on such sources of good, Edmund was contributing to his father’s ease by improvement in the only point in which he had given him pain before—improvement in his spirits. After wandering about and sitting under trees with Fanny all the summer evenings, he had so well talked his mind into submission as to be very tolerably cheerful again.\n\nThese were the circumstances and the hopes which gradually brought their alleviation to Sir Thomas, deadening his sense of what was lost, and in part reconciling him to himself; though the anguish arising from the conviction of his own errors in the education of his daughters was never to be entirely done away.\n\nToo late he became aware how unfavourable to the character of any young people must be the totally opposite treatment which Maria and Julia had been always experiencing at home, where the excessive indulgence and flattery of their aunt had been continually contrasted with his own severity. He saw how ill he had judged, in expecting to counteract what was wrong in Mrs. Norris by its reverse in himself; clearly saw that he had but increased the evil by teaching them to repress their spirits in his presence so as to make their real disposition unknown to him, and sending them for all their indulgences to a person who had been able to attach them only by the blindness of her affection, and the excess of her praise.\n\nHere had been grievous mismanagement; but, bad as it was, he gradually grew to feel that it had not been the most direful mistake in his plan of education. Something must have been wanting within, or time would have worn away much of its ill effect. He feared that principle, active principle, had been wanting; that they had never been properly taught to govern their inclinations and tempers by that sense of duty which can alone suffice. They had been instructed theoretically in their religion, but never required to bring it into daily practice. To be distinguished for elegance and accomplishments, the authorised object of their youth, could have had no useful influence that way, no moral effect on the mind. He had meant them to be good, but his cares had been directed to the understanding and manners, not the disposition; and of the necessity of self-denial and humility, he feared they had never heard from any lips that could profit them.\n\nBitterly did he deplore a deficiency which now he could scarcely comprehend to have been possible. Wretchedly did he feel, that with all the cost and care of an anxious and expensive education, he had brought up his daughters without their understanding their first duties, or his being acquainted with their character and temper.\n\nThe high spirit and strong passions of Mrs. Rushworth, especially, were made known to him only in their sad result. She was not to be prevailed on to leave Mr. Crawford. She hoped to marry him, and they continued together till she was obliged to be convinced that such hope was vain, and till the disappointment and wretchedness arising from the conviction rendered her temper so bad, and her feelings for him so like hatred, as to make them for a while each other’s punishment, and then induce a voluntary separation.\n\nShe had lived with him to be reproached as the ruin of all his happiness in Fanny, and carried away no better consolation in leaving him than that she had divided them. What can exceed the misery of such a mind in such a situation?\n\nMr. Rushworth had no difficulty in procuring a divorce; and so ended a marriage contracted under such circumstances as to make any better end the effect of good luck not to be reckoned on. She had despised him, and loved another; and he had been very much aware that it was so. The indignities of stupidity, and the disappointments of selfish passion, can excite little pity. His punishment followed his conduct, as did a deeper punishment the deeper guilt of his wife. He was released from the engagement to be mortified and unhappy, till some other pretty girl could attract him into matrimony again, and he might set forward on a second, and, it is to be hoped, more prosperous trial of the state: if duped, to be duped at least with good humour and good luck; while she must withdraw with infinitely stronger feelings to a retirement and reproach which could allow no second spring of hope or character.\n\nWhere she could be placed became a subject of most melancholy and momentous consultation. Mrs. Norris, whose attachment seemed to augment with the demerits of her niece, would have had her received at home and countenanced by them all. Sir Thomas would not hear of it; and Mrs. Norris’s anger against Fanny was so much the greater, from considering her residence there as the motive. She persisted in placing his scruples to her account, though Sir Thomas very solemnly assured her that, had there been no young woman in question, had there been no young person of either sex belonging to him, to be endangered by the society or hurt by the character of Mrs. Rushworth, he would never have offered so great an insult to the neighbourhood as to expect it to notice her. As a daughter, he hoped a penitent one, she should be protected by him, and secured in every comfort, and supported by every encouragement to do right, which their relative situations admitted; but farther than that he could not go. Maria had destroyed her own character, and he would not, by a vain attempt to restore what never could be restored, by affording his sanction to vice, or in seeking to lessen its disgrace, be anywise accessory to introducing such misery in another man’s family as he had known himself.\n\nIt ended in Mrs. Norris’s resolving to quit Mansfield and devote herself to her unfortunate Maria, and in an establishment being formed for them in another country, remote and private, where, shut up together with little society, on one side no affection, on the other no judgment, it may be reasonably supposed that their tempers became their mutual punishment.\n\nMrs. Norris’s removal from Mansfield was the great supplementary comfort of Sir Thomas’s life. His opinion of her had been sinking from the day of his return from Antigua: in every transaction together from that period, in their daily intercourse, in business, or in chat, she had been regularly losing ground in his esteem, and convincing him that either time had done her much disservice, or that he had considerably over-rated her sense, and wonderfully borne with her manners before. He had felt her as an hourly evil, which was so much the worse, as there seemed no chance of its ceasing but with life; she seemed a part of himself that must be borne for ever. To be relieved from her, therefore, was so great a felicity that, had she not left bitter remembrances behind her, there might have been danger of his learning almost to approve the evil which produced such a good.\n\nShe was regretted by no one at Mansfield. She had never been able to attach even those she loved best; and since Mrs. Rushworth’s elopement, her temper had been in a state of such irritation as to make her everywhere tormenting. Not even Fanny had tears for aunt Norris, not even when she was gone for ever.\n\nThat Julia escaped better than Maria was owing, in some measure, to a favourable difference of disposition and circumstance, but in a greater to her having been less the darling of that very aunt, less flattered and less spoilt. Her beauty and acquirements had held but a second place. She had been always used to think herself a little inferior to Maria. Her temper was naturally the easiest of the two; her feelings, though quick, were more controllable, and education had not given her so very hurtful a degree of self-consequence.\n\nShe had submitted the best to the disappointment in Henry Crawford. After the first bitterness of the conviction of being slighted was over, she had been tolerably soon in a fair way of not thinking of him again; and when the acquaintance was renewed in town, and Mr. Rushworth’s house became Crawford’s object, she had had the merit of withdrawing herself from it, and of chusing that time to pay a visit to her other friends, in order to secure herself from being again too much attracted. This had been her motive in going to her cousin’s. Mr. Yates’s convenience had had nothing to do with it. She had been allowing his attentions some time, but with very little idea of ever accepting him; and had not her sister’s conduct burst forth as it did, and her increased dread of her father and of home, on that event, imagining its certain consequence to herself would be greater severity and restraint, made her hastily resolve on avoiding such immediate horrors at all risks, it is probable that Mr. Yates would never have succeeded. She had not eloped with any worse feelings than those of selfish alarm. It had appeared to her the only thing to be done. Maria’s guilt had induced Julia’s folly.\n\nHenry Crawford, ruined by early independence and bad domestic example, indulged in the freaks of a cold-blooded vanity a little too long. Once it had, by an opening undesigned and unmerited, led him into the way of happiness. Could he have been satisfied with the conquest of one amiable woman’s affections, could he have found sufficient exultation in overcoming the reluctance, in working himself into the esteem and tenderness of Fanny Price, there would have been every probability of success and felicity for him. His affection had already done something. Her influence over him had already given him some influence over her. Would he have deserved more, there can be no doubt that more would have been obtained, especially when that marriage had taken place, which would have given him the assistance of her conscience in subduing her first inclination, and brought them very often together. Would he have persevered, and uprightly, Fanny must have been his reward, and a reward very voluntarily bestowed, within a reasonable period from Edmund’s marrying Mary.\n\nHad he done as he intended, and as he knew he ought, by going down to Everingham after his return from Portsmouth, he might have been deciding his own happy destiny. But he was pressed to stay for Mrs. Fraser’s party; his staying was made of flattering consequence, and he was to meet Mrs. Rushworth there. Curiosity and vanity were both engaged, and the temptation of immediate pleasure was too strong for a mind unused to make any sacrifice to right: he resolved to defer his Norfolk journey, resolved that writing should answer the purpose of it, or that its purpose was unimportant, and staid. He saw Mrs. Rushworth, was received by her with a coldness which ought to have been repulsive, and have established apparent indifference between them for ever; but he was mortified, he could not bear to be thrown off by the woman whose smiles had been so wholly at his command: he must exert himself to subdue so proud a display of resentment; it was anger on Fanny’s account; he must get the better of it, and make Mrs. Rushworth Maria Bertram again in her treatment of himself.\n\nIn this spirit he began the attack, and by animated perseverance had soon re-established the sort of familiar intercourse, of gallantry, of flirtation, which bounded his views; but in triumphing over the discretion which, though beginning in anger, might have saved them both, he had put himself in the power of feelings on her side more strong than he had supposed. She loved him; there was no withdrawing attentions avowedly dear to her. He was entangled by his own vanity, with as little excuse of love as possible, and without the smallest inconstancy of mind towards her cousin. To keep Fanny and the Bertrams from a knowledge of what was passing became his first object. Secrecy could not have been more desirable for Mrs. Rushworth’s credit than he felt it for his own. When he returned from Richmond, he would have been glad to see Mrs. Rushworth no more. All that followed was the result of her imprudence; and he went off with her at last, because he could not help it, regretting Fanny even at the moment, but regretting her infinitely more when all the bustle of the intrigue was over, and a very few months had taught him, by the force of contrast, to place a yet higher value on the sweetness of her temper, the purity of her mind, and the excellence of her principles.\n\nThat punishment, the public punishment of disgrace, should in a just measure attend his share of the offence is, we know, not one of the barriers which society gives to virtue. In this world the penalty is less equal than could be wished; but without presuming to look forward to a juster appointment hereafter, we may fairly consider a man of sense, like Henry Crawford, to be providing for himself no small portion of vexation and regret: vexation that must rise sometimes to self-reproach, and regret to wretchedness, in having so requited hospitality, so injured family peace, so forfeited his best, most estimable, and endeared acquaintance, and so lost the woman whom he had rationally as well as passionately loved.\n\nAfter what had passed to wound and alienate the two families, the continuance of the Bertrams and Grants in such close neighbourhood would have been most distressing; but the absence of the latter, for some months purposely lengthened, ended very fortunately in the necessity, or at least the practicability, of a permanent removal. Dr. Grant, through an interest on which he had almost ceased to form hopes, succeeded to a stall in Westminster, which, as affording an occasion for leaving Mansfield, an excuse for residence in London, and an increase of income to answer the expenses of the change, was highly acceptable to those who went and those who staid.\n\nMrs. Grant, with a temper to love and be loved, must have gone with some regret from the scenes and people she had been used to; but the same happiness of disposition must in any place, and any society, secure her a great deal to enjoy, and she had again a home to offer Mary; and Mary had had enough of her own friends, enough of vanity, ambition, love, and disappointment in the course of the last half-year, to be in need of the true kindness of her sister’s heart, and the rational tranquillity of her ways. They lived together; and when Dr. Grant had brought on apoplexy and death, by three great institutionary dinners in one week, they still lived together; for Mary, though perfectly resolved against ever attaching herself to a younger brother again, was long in finding among the dashing representatives, or idle heir-apparents, who were at the command of her beauty, and her £20,000, any one who could satisfy the better taste she had acquired at Mansfield, whose character and manners could authorise a hope of the domestic happiness she had there learned to estimate, or put Edmund Bertram sufficiently out of her head.\n\nEdmund had greatly the advantage of her in this respect. He had not to wait and wish with vacant affections for an object worthy to succeed her in them. Scarcely had he done regretting Mary Crawford, and observing to Fanny how impossible it was that he should ever meet with such another woman, before it began to strike him whether a very different kind of woman might not do just as well, or a great deal better: whether Fanny herself were not growing as dear, as important to him in all her smiles and all her ways, as Mary Crawford had ever been; and whether it might not be a possible, a hopeful undertaking to persuade her that her warm and sisterly regard for him would be foundation enough for wedded love.\n\nI purposely abstain from dates on this occasion, that every one may be at liberty to fix their own, aware that the cure of unconquerable passions, and the transfer of unchanging attachments, must vary much as to time in different people. I only entreat everybody to believe that exactly at the time when it was quite natural that it should be so, and not a week earlier, Edmund did cease to care about Miss Crawford, and became as anxious to marry Fanny as Fanny herself could desire.\n\nWith such a regard for her, indeed, as his had long been, a regard founded on the most endearing claims of innocence and helplessness, and completed by every recommendation of growing worth, what could be more natural than the change? Loving, guiding, protecting her, as he had been doing ever since her being ten years old, her mind in so great a degree formed by his care, and her comfort depending on his kindness, an object to him of such close and peculiar interest, dearer by all his own importance with her than any one else at Mansfield, what was there now to add, but that he should learn to prefer soft light eyes to sparkling dark ones. And being always with her, and always talking confidentially, and his feelings exactly in that favourable state which a recent disappointment gives, those soft light eyes could not be very long in obtaining the pre-eminence.\n\nHaving once set out, and felt that he had done so on this road to happiness, there was nothing on the side of prudence to stop him or make his progress slow; no doubts of her deserving, no fears of opposition of taste, no need of drawing new hopes of happiness from dissimilarity of temper. Her mind, disposition, opinions, and habits wanted no half-concealment, no self-deception on the present, no reliance on future improvement. Even in the midst of his late infatuation, he had acknowledged Fanny’s mental superiority. What must be his sense of it now, therefore? She was of course only too good for him; but as nobody minds having what is too good for them, he was very steadily earnest in the pursuit of the blessing, and it was not possible that encouragement from her should be long wanting. Timid, anxious, doubting as she was, it was still impossible that such tenderness as hers should not, at times, hold out the strongest hope of success, though it remained for a later period to tell him the whole delightful and astonishing truth. His happiness in knowing himself to have been so long the beloved of such a heart, must have been great enough to warrant any strength of language in which he could clothe it to her or to himself; it must have been a delightful happiness. But there was happiness elsewhere which no description can reach. Let no one presume to give the feelings of a young woman on receiving the assurance of that affection of which she has scarcely allowed herself to entertain a hope.\n\nTheir own inclinations ascertained, there were no difficulties behind, no drawback of poverty or parent. It was a match which Sir Thomas’s wishes had even forestalled. Sick of ambitious and mercenary connexions, prizing more and more the sterling good of principle and temper, and chiefly anxious to bind by the strongest securities all that remained to him of domestic felicity, he had pondered with genuine satisfaction on the more than possibility of the two young friends finding their natural consolation in each other for all that had occurred of disappointment to either; and the joyful consent which met Edmund’s application, the high sense of having realised a great acquisition in the promise of Fanny for a daughter, formed just such a contrast with his early opinion on the subject when the poor little girl’s coming had been first agitated, as time is for ever producing between the plans and decisions of mortals, for their own instruction, and their neighbours’ entertainment.\n\nFanny was indeed the daughter that he wanted. His charitable kindness had been rearing a prime comfort for himself. His liberality had a rich repayment, and the general goodness of his intentions by her deserved it. He might have made her childhood happier; but it had been an error of judgment only which had given him the appearance of harshness, and deprived him of her early love; and now, on really knowing each other, their mutual attachment became very strong. After settling her at Thornton Lacey with every kind attention to her comfort, the object of almost every day was to see her there, or to get her away from it.\n\nSelfishly dear as she had long been to Lady Bertram, she could not be parted with willingly by her. No happiness of son or niece could make her wish the marriage. But it was possible to part with her, because Susan remained to supply her place. Susan became the stationary niece, delighted to be so; and equally well adapted for it by a readiness of mind, and an inclination for usefulness, as Fanny had been by sweetness of temper, and strong feelings of gratitude. Susan could never be spared. First as a comfort to Fanny, then as an auxiliary, and last as her substitute, she was established at Mansfield, with every appearance of equal permanency. Her more fearless disposition and happier nerves made everything easy to her there. With quickness in understanding the tempers of those she had to deal with, and no natural timidity to restrain any consequent wishes, she was soon welcome and useful to all; and after Fanny’s removal succeeded so naturally to her influence over the hourly comfort of her aunt, as gradually to become, perhaps, the most beloved of the two. In her usefulness, in Fanny’s excellence, in William’s continued good conduct and rising fame, and in the general well-doing and success of the other members of the family, all assisting to advance each other, and doing credit to his countenance and aid, Sir Thomas saw repeated, and for ever repeated, reason to rejoice in what he had done for them all, and acknowledge the advantages of early hardship and discipline, and the consciousness of being born to struggle and endure.\n\nWith so much true merit and true love, and no want of fortune and friends, the happiness of the married cousins must appear as secure as earthly happiness can be. Equally formed for domestic life, and attached to country pleasures, their home was the home of affection and comfort; and to complete the picture of good, the acquisition of Mansfield living, by the death of Dr. Grant, occurred just after they had been married long enough to begin to want an increase of income, and feel their distance from the paternal abode an inconvenience.\n\nOn that event they removed to Mansfield; and the Parsonage there, which, under each of its two former owners, Fanny had never been able to approach but with some painful sensation of restraint or alarm, soon grew as dear to her heart, and as thoroughly perfect in her eyes, as everything else within the view and patronage of Mansfield Park had long been.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-141"]="# 제1장\n\n지금으로부터 삼십 년쯤 전, 헌팅던의 마리아 워드 양은 겨우 칠천 파운드밖에 지니지 못했으면서도 노샘프턴주 맨스필드 파크의 토머스 버트럼 경의 마음을 사로잡는 행운을 누렸다. 그리하여 준남작 부인의 지위에 올라, 번듯한 저택과 넉넉한 수입이 가져다주는 온갖 안락과 영화를 손에 넣었다. 헌팅던 사람들은 하나같이 그 혼사가 얼마나 대단한지 감탄해 마지않았고, 변호사인 그녀의 삼촌조차 조카가 그만한 자리에 정당히 어울리려면 적어도 삼천 파운드는 모자란다고 인정할 정도였다. 그녀에게는 언니 덕에 함께 팔자를 고칠 여동생이 둘 있었다. 워드 양과 프랜시스 양도 마리아 양 못지않게 아름답다고 여기던 지인들은 그 둘 역시 그에 버금가는 좋은 자리로 시집가리라 서슴없이 점쳤다. 그러나 세상에는 그럴 만한 미인을 다 감당할 만큼 재산 많은 남자가 그리 흔치 않은 법이다. 워드 양은 여섯 해쯤 지난 끝에 형부의 친구인 노리스 목사와 맺어지지 않을 수 없는 처지가 되었는데, 그에게는 사사로운 재산이랄 것이 거의 없었다. 그리고 프랜시스 양은 그보다도 형편이 나빴다. 사실 워드 양의 혼사는 막상 따지고 보면 그리 볼품없지는 않았다. 토머스 경이 다행히 자기 친구에게 맨스필드의 성직록을 마련해 줄 수 있었던 덕분에, 노리스 부부는 한 해 천 파운드에 크게 못 미치지 않는 수입으로 부부의 단란한 삶을 시작했으니 말이다. 그러나 프랜시스 양은 흔한 말로 집안의 뜻을 거스르려 작정하고 혼인했다. 교육도, 재산도, 이렇다 할 연줄도 없는 해병 중위를 고름으로써 그 일을 아주 철저히 해치운 것이다. 그보다 더 딱한 선택을 하기도 어려웠을 것이다. 토머스 버트럼 경에게는 영향력이 있었고, 원칙에서든 자존심에서든—옳게 처신하고 싶은 마음과, 자기와 이어진 이들이 모두 남부끄럽지 않은 처지에 놓이는 것을 보고 싶은 바람에서—처제를 위해 기꺼이 그 힘을 써 주었을 것이다. 그러나 처제 남편의 직업은 어떤 영향력도 미칠 수 없는 것이었고, 다른 도울 방도를 궁리해 볼 겨를도 없이 두 자매 사이에는 완전한 절연이 벌어지고 말았다. 그것은 양쪽의 처신이 빚어낸 자연스러운 결과였으며, 지극히 경솔한 혼인이 거의 언제나 낳는 결말이기도 했다. 프라이스 부인은 쓸데없는 잔소리를 듣지 않으려고 실제로 혼인하기 전까지는 그 일을 두고 집안에 한마디도 적어 보내지 않았다. 버트럼 부인은 감정이 지극히 잔잔하고 성미가 유난히 느긋하고 굼뜬 여자였던지라, 그저 동생을 단념하고 더는 그 일을 마음에 두지 않는 것으로 만족했을 것이다. 그러나 노리스 부인은 가만있지 못하는 활동적인 기질이라, 패니에게 그 처신이 얼마나 어리석은지 조목조목 일러 주고 앞으로 닥칠 온갖 나쁜 결과로 을러대는 긴 노여운 편지를 써 보내고서야 직성이 풀렸다. 프라이스 부인 또한 이번에는 상처받고 화가 났다. 그리하여 두 언니를 싸잡아 신랄하게 몰아붙이고 토머스 경의 자존심을 두고 몹시 무례한 소리까지 늘어놓은 답장이 돌아왔는데, 노리스 부인으로서는 도저히 혼자 삭이고 있을 수 없는 내용이었던지라, 그것으로 두 사람 사이의 왕래는 상당한 기간 뚝 끊기고 말았다.\n\n두 집은 서로 멀리 떨어져 있었고 드나드는 모임도 워낙 달랐던 터라, 그 뒤로 열한 해 동안 서로의 소식을 들을 길조차 거의 없다시피 했다. 아니, 적어도 노리스 부인이 이따금 그러듯 성난 목소리로 패니가 또 아이를 낳았다더라 하고 전해 올 때면, 토머스 경으로서는 그런 소식을 전할 재간이 대체 어디서 났나 싶어 여간 놀랍지 않았다. 그러나 열한 해가 저물 무렵에는 프라이스 부인도 더는 자존심이나 원한을 붙들고 있을 처지가, 혹은 도움이 될지 모를 인연을 하나라도 놓칠 처지가 못 되었다. 식구는 많은 데다 여전히 늘어 가고, 남편은 현역 복무는 못 하게 되었으면서도 사람들과 어울리고 좋은 술을 즐기는 데에는 조금도 뒤지지 않았으며, 그 궁핍을 메울 수입은 지극히 변변찮았으니, 그녀는 제가 그토록 무심히 저버린 벗들을 되찾고 싶어 안달이 났다. 그리하여 그녀는 버트럼 부인에게 편지를 띄웠는데, 어찌나 뉘우침과 낙심이 가득하고, 어찌나 아이는 넘쳐 나며, 그 밖에는 거의 모든 것이 어찌나 모자란지, 읽는 이들이 화해로 마음이 기울지 않을 수 없을 정도였다. 그녀는 아홉 번째 해산을 앞두고 있었다. 그 사정을 한탄하고 곧 태어날 아이의 대부모가 되어 달라 청한 다음에는, 이미 세상에 나온 여덟 아이의 앞날을 건사하는 데에도 그들이 얼마나 큰 힘이 될 수 있을지, 그 기대를 차마 숨기지 못했다. 맏이는 열 살 난 사내아이로, 기상이 늠름해 어서 세상에 나가고 싶어 안달인 아이였다. 그러나 그녀가 무엇을 해 줄 수 있으랴? 훗날 그 아이가 토머스 경의 서인도제도 재산을 돌보는 일에 쓸모가 있을 가망은 없을까? 어떤 자리든 마다하지 않을 것이었다. 아니면 토머스 경은 울리치를 어찌 여기실까? 사내아이 하나를 동인도로 보내자면 또 어찌해야 할까?\n\n그 편지는 헛되지 않았다. 그것은 평화와 정을 되살렸다. 토머스 경은 다정한 조언과 언약을 보냈고, 버트럼 부인은 돈과 갓난아이 옷가지를 부쳤으며, 편지는 노리스 부인이 도맡아 썼다.\n\n편지가 당장 불러온 결과는 그러했다. 그리고 한 해가 채 가기 전에 프라이스 부인에게는 그로부터 더 중대한 이득이 돌아왔다. 노리스 부인은 딱한 동생과 그 식구가 도무지 머리에서 떠나지 않는다고, 다들 그만큼 해 주었건만 저는 아무래도 더 해 주어야 할 것만 같다고 다른 이들에게 노상 말하곤 했다. 그러다 마침내 그 많은 아이들 가운데 하나만이라도 딱한 프라이스 부인의 손에서 온전히 떼어 그 부담과 비용을 덜어 주는 것이 제 바람임을 털어놓지 않을 수 없었다. “우리끼리 그 애의 맏딸을, 지금 아홉 살 난 그 여자애를 맡아 기르면 어떨까요? 딱한 제 어미가 도저히 못 해 줄 만큼 손이 많이 갈 나이인데 말이에요. 우리한테야 그 수고나 비용이 이 착한 일에 견주면 아무것도 아니지 않겠어요.” 버트럼 부인은 그 자리에서 언니 말에 찬동했다. “그보다 더 나은 일은 없겠어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “그 애를 데려오도록 해요.”\n\n토머스 경은 그처럼 선뜻, 그리고 아무 조건 없이 승낙할 수는 없었다. 그는 곰곰이 따지며 망설였다. 그것은 결코 가벼운 책임이 아니었다. 그렇게 길러진 여자애라면 마땅한 앞길을 마련해 주어야지, 그러지 않고서 집에서 데려온다면 친절이 아니라 도리어 잔인한 일이 될 터였다. 그는 제 자식 넷을, 두 아들을, 사촌지간의 연정 따위를 떠올렸다. 그러나 그가 신중하게 반대할 점을 하나씩 꺼내 놓기가 무섭게, 노리스 부인이 말을 끊고 들어와 그가 꺼냈든 아니든 그 모든 것에 미리 답을 해 버렸다.\n\n“토머스 경, 무슨 말씀이신지 저는 훤히 알아듣겠어요. 그 너그럽고 세심한 생각씀씀이야 마땅히 높이 사야지요. 사실 그거야 평소 처신과 조금도 어긋남이 없는 거고요. 손수 거두다시피 한 아이를 위해 할 수 있는 건 다 해 주어야 마땅하다는 말씀에도 저는 큰 틀에서 전적으로 같은 생각이에요. 그리고 이런 일에 제 몫을 아낄 사람으로 치면 세상에 저만 한 사람이 또 없을 거예요. 제게는 자식이 없으니, 언제고 뭐라도 조금 나누어 줄 일이 생기면 제 동생들의 아이 말고 누굴 바라보겠어요? 게다가 노리스 씨도 워낙 반듯한 분이라—하기야 아시다시피 저야 말수도 적고 겉치레도 없는 사람이지만요. 우리, 사소한 일에 겁먹어서 좋은 일을 그르치지는 말아요. 여자애 하나를 잘 가르쳐서 세상에 번듯하게 내보내면, 열에 아홉은 아무에게도 더는 돈 들이지 않고도 좋은 자리에 자리 잡을 길이 열리는 법이거든요. 토머스 경, 우리—적어도 경의 조카딸이라면 이 고장에서 자라나면서 누릴 이점이 한둘이 아닐 거예요. 그야 그 애가 사촌들만큼 예쁘리라고는 말하지 않겠어요. 아마 그렇진 않겠지요. 하지만 이 고장 사교계에 아주 좋은 조건으로 발을 들이게 될 테니, 사람의 일로 헤아리는 한 어엿한 자리를 얻게 될 거예요. 아드님들을 염려하시는 거지요—하지만 세상 무슨 일보다도 바로 그 일이 일어날 가망이 가장 적다는 걸 모르시겠어요? 어려서부터 늘 오누이처럼 붙어 자랄 텐데 말이에요. 그건 도의상 있을 수 없는 일이에요. 저는 그런 예를 여태 한 번도 본 적이 없어요. 사실 그거야말로 그런 인연이 맺어지는 걸 막는 유일하게 확실한 길이지요. 그 애가 예쁜 처녀라 치고, 톰이나 에드먼드가 앞으로 일곱 해쯤 지나 그 애를 처음 본다고 해 봐요, 아마 탈이 나고 말 거예요. 그 애가 우리 모두와 떨어져 가난과 무관심 속에 자라도록 내버려 두었다는 그 생각만으로도, 그 착하고 다정한 두 아이 중 누구라도 그 애한테 마음을 빼앗기고 말걸요. 하지만 지금부터 함께 기르면, 설령 그 애가 천사 같은 미모를 지녔다 한들 두 아이 어느 쪽에게도 누이 이상은 결코 되지 못할 거예요.”\n\n“자네 말에는 옳은 데가 많네.” 토머스 경이 대답했다. “그리고 서로의 처지에 그토록 걸맞은 계획에 내가 공연한 트집을 잡아 훼방을 놓을 생각은 추호도 없네. 다만 내가 짚어 두려던 건, 이 일을 가벼이 시작해서는 안 된다는 것, 그리고 이 일이 프라이스 부인에게 참으로 도움이 되고 우리에게도 떳떳하려면, 자네가 그토록 낙관하는 그런 자리가 나서지 않을 경우에 대비해, 그 아이에게 숙녀다운 앞가림을 마련해 주거나 형편이 닿는 대로 앞으로 그리해 주기로 우리 스스로 다짐해 두어야 한다는 것뿐일세.”\n\n“무슨 말씀인지 잘 알겠어요.” 노리스 부인이 외쳤다. “경께서는 더없이 너그럽고 사려 깊은 분이시니, 이 점에서 우리가 다툴 일은 결코 없을 거예요. 아시다시피 제가 할 수 있는 일이라면 사랑하는 이들을 위해 저는 언제든 발 벗고 나설 준비가 돼 있어요. 물론 이 어린 계집아이한테야 경의 귀한 자녀들한테 품은 정의 백분의 일도 못 느낄 테고, 어느 모로 보나 제 자식처럼 여기지도 못하겠지만, 그렇다고 그 애를 나 몰라라 한다면 저는 스스로가 미워질 거예요. 그 애도 제 동생의 자식이 아니겠어요? 제게 빵 한 조각이라도 있는 한 그 애가 굶주리는 꼴을 어찌 두고 보겠어요? 토머스 경, 저야 흠이야 많아도 마음만은 따뜻한 사람이에요. 그리고 이렇게 가난해도 인색한 짓을 하느니 차라리 제 먹을 것을 굶겠어요. 그러니 경께서 반대만 안 하신다면, 내일 당장 딱한 동생한테 편지를 써서 이 이야기를 꺼내 볼게요. 그리고 일이 매듭지어지는 대로 그 애를 맨스필드로 데려오는 건 제가 맡을게요. 경께서는 그 일로 조금도 수고하실 것 없어요. 아시다시피 제 수고야 저는 도무지 개의치 않으니까요. 내니를 일부러 런던에 보내면, 그 애 사촌인 마구상네에서 하룻밤 묵으면 되고, 아이더러 거기로 마중 나오게 하면 돼요. 포츠머스에서 런던까지야 마침 그리로 가는 점잖은 사람 편에 딸려 역마차로 손쉽게 데려올 수 있을 거예요. 아마 그맘때면 으레 어엿한 장사꾼 아낙이라도 하나쯤 상경하기 마련이거든요.”\n\n내니의 사촌을 걸고넘어진 대목만 빼면 토머스 경은 더 이상 반대하지 않았다. 그리하여 그보다 좀 더 번듯한, 다만 덜 알뜰한 만남의 자리로 대신하기로 하고 모든 일이 매듭지어진 것으로 여겨졌으며, 이토록 자비로운 계획이 주는 흐뭇함은 벌써부터 누려지고 있었다. 엄밀히 따지자면 그 흐뭇한 기분의 몫이 두 사람에게 똑같이 돌아가서는 안 될 일이었다. 토머스 경은 골라 온 그 아이의 참되고 한결같은 후견인이 되기로 굳게 마음먹은 반면, 노리스 부인은 그 아이를 건사하는 데 한 푼이라도 쓸 생각은 눈곱만치도 없었으니 말이다. 걷고, 말하고, 궁리하는 데까지라면 그녀는 더할 나위 없이 자비로웠고, 남더러 후하게 굴라고 이르는 데에는 누구도 그녀를 따를 자가 없었다. 그러나 그녀의 돈 사랑은 남을 부리기 좋아하는 마음에 조금도 뒤지지 않았고, 남의 돈을 쓰는 재주만큼이나 제 돈을 아끼는 재주에도 훤했다. 애초에 바라 마지않던 것보다 옹색한 수입으로 살림을 차린 터라, 그녀는 처음부터 지극히 빠듯한 절약이 꼭 필요하다고 여겼다. 그리고 분별에서 비롯된 그 절약은 이내 취향의 문제로 굳어졌으니, 마침 그 알뜰한 마음을 쏟을 자식조차 없었던 까닭이다. 만약 건사할 식구가 있었더라면 노리스 부인은 결코 돈을 모으지 못했을 것이다. 그러나 그런 걱정거리가 없다 보니 그녀의 절약을 가로막을 것도, 한 번도 그만큼 써 본 적 없는 수입에 해마다 얼마씩을 보태는 즐거움을 덜어 낼 것도 없었다. 동생을 향한 참된 애정으로도 누그러지지 않는 이 홀리는 신조 아래, 그녀가 노릴 수 있는 것이라고는 그토록 비용이 드는 자선을 기획하고 주선했다는 공치사뿐이었다. 하기야 어쩌면 그녀는 스스로를 어찌나 몰랐던지, 이 대화를 마치고 목사관으로 걸어 돌아가는 내내, 제가 세상에서 가장 인심 좋은 언니이자 이모라는 흐뭇한 믿음에 젖어 있었을는지도 모른다.\n\n그 이야기가 다시 나왔을 때, 그녀의 속셈은 한층 낱낱이 드러났다. “그 애가 처음에 어디로 오게 할까요, 언니? 언니 댁으로, 아니면 우리 집으로?” 하는 버트럼 부인의 태연한 물음에, 토머스 경은 뜻밖에도 그 아이를 몸소 건사하는 일에는 노리스 부인이 도무지 한몫 낄 수 없다는 소리를 듣고 적잖이 놀랐다. 그는 그 아이가 목사관으로서는 유난히 반가운 식구가 되리라고, 제 자식 없는 이모에게는 더없이 바람직한 말벗이 되리라고 여겨 오던 참이었다. 그런데 그것이 완전한 오산이었음을 알게 된 것이다. 노리스 부인은 안됐지만 그 계집아이가 자기들과 함께 지내는 것은, 적어도 지금 형편으로는 도무지 될 일이 아니라고 했다. 딱한 노리스 씨의 시원찮은 건강이 그것을 불가능하게 만든다는 것이었다. 그이는 하늘을 날 수 없듯이 아이의 소란도 도무지 견디지 못하니, 정말이지 훗날 통풍만 씻은 듯 나아 준다면야 이야기가 달라지겠고, 그때는 저도 기꺼이 차례를 맡아 그 불편쯤 아무렇지 않게 여기겠지만, 지금 당장은 딱한 노리스 씨가 그녀의 시간을 잠시도 남김없이 잡아먹는 형편이라, 그런 말을 입에 올리기만 해도 그이가 안절부절못할 게 뻔하다는 것이었다.\n\n“그럼 그 애는 우리 집으로 오는 게 낫겠네요.” 버트럼 부인이 더없이 태연하게 말했다. 잠깐 뜸을 들인 뒤 토머스 경이 위엄 있게 덧붙였다. “그래, 그 애의 집은 이 집으로 삼읍시다. 우리가 그 애에게 도리를 다하도록 애써 봅시다. 그러면 적어도 그 애는 또래 벗들과 제대로 된 여자 가정교사를 곁에 두는 이점은 누리게 될 테니.”\n\n“지당하신 말씀이에요.” 노리스 부인이 외쳤다. “둘 다 참으로 중요한 점이지요. 게다가 리 선생한테야 가르칠 여자애가 셋이든 둘이든 매한가지일 테니—다를 게 하나도 없어요. 저야 좀 더 보탬이 못 되는 게 아쉬울 따름이지요. 하지만 보시다시피 저는 제 힘닿는 데까지 다 하고 있잖아요. 저는 제 수고를 아끼는 그런 부류가 아니에요. 사흘씩이나 제 으뜸가는 조언자를 떠나보내는 게 아무리 불편해도, 내니를 시켜 그 애를 데려오게 하겠어요. 언니, 그 애는 옛 육아실 옆 작은 흰 다락방에 두면 되겠네요. 리 선생과도 그토록 가깝고, 아이들과도 멀지 않고, 하녀들과도 바로 붙어 있으니 그 애한테는 더없이 좋은 자리일 거예요. 하녀들 가운데 누구라도 그 애 옷 입는 걸 거들고 옷가지도 건사해 줄 수 있잖아요. 설마 엘리스한테 다른 애들에 더해 그 애 시중까지 들라고 하는 건 온당치 않다 여기실 테니 말이에요. 정말이지 그 애를 달리 어디 둘 데가 있을 것 같지도 않고요.”\n\n버트럼 부인은 아무 이의도 달지 않았다.\n\n“그 애가 심성 고운 아이로 자라 주면 좋겠어요.” 노리스 부인이 말을 이었다. “그리고 이런 후원자들을 둔 제 남다른 복을 제대로 헤아릴 줄 알았으면 하고요.”\n\n“만약 그 애의 심성이 정말로 못됐다면,” 토머스 경이 말했다. “우리 자식들을 위해서라도 그 애를 이 집안에 계속 둘 수는 없겠지. 하지만 그렇게까지 나쁜 일을 예상할 까닭은 없네. 아마 그 애한테서 고쳤으면 싶은 구석이야 많이 보이겠지. 지독한 무지며, 다소 상스러운 소견이며, 몹시 거슬리는 상스러운 몸가짐 따위를 우리도 각오해 두어야 할 걸세. 그러나 그런 건 못 고칠 흠이 아닐뿐더러, 내 믿기로 함께 지내는 아이들에게 해가 될 것도 아닐세. 만약 내 딸들이 그 애보다 어렸더라면, 그런 아이를 곁에 들이는 일을 아주 심각하게 여겼을 걸세. 하지만 지금 형편으로는, 그 사귐에서 내 딸들이 두려워할 것은 아무것도, 그 애가 바랄 것은 모든 것이 있으리라 보네.”\n\n“바로 제 생각이 그래요.” 노리스 부인이 외쳤다. “오늘 아침에도 남편한테 그렇게 말했다니까요. 그저 사촌들과 함께 지내는 것만으로도 그 애한테는 훌륭한 교육이 될 거라고요. 리 선생이 아무것도 안 가르친다 해도, 그 애는 사촌들한테서 착하고 영리해지는 법을 배울 거라고 그랬어요.”\n\n“그 애가 우리 딱한 퍼그를 괴롭히지나 않으면 좋겠는데요.” 버트럼 부인이 말했다. “줄리아가 이제 겨우 그 녀석을 가만 놔두게 됐는데 말이에요.”\n\n“노리스 부인, 우리한테 좀 곤란한 점이 하나 있을 걸세.” 토머스 경이 짚었다. “아이들이 자라면서 그 애와 내 딸들 사이에 마땅히 두어야 할 구별을 어찌 지킬까 하는 문제 말일세. 내 딸들의 마음속에는 저희가 어떤 처지인지에 대한 자각을 심어 주되 사촌을 너무 낮잡아 보지는 않게 하고, 또 그 애의 기를 지나치게 꺾지는 않으면서도 저는 버트럼 집안의 아가씨가 아니라는 걸 잊지 않게 하는 일 말이야. 나야 그 애들이 아주 사이좋은 벗으로 지내는 걸 보고 싶고, 내 딸들이 친척한테 티끌만큼이라도 거만하게 구는 일은 결코 용납하지 않을 걸세. 그렇지만 그래도 그 애들이 동등할 수는 없어. 그 애들의 신분이며 재산이며 권리며 앞날의 기대는 언제까지나 다를 테니 말일세. 이건 여간 미묘한 문제가 아니야. 그러니 자네가 우리를 도와 꼭 알맞은 처신의 길을 골라 주어야 하네.”\n\n노리스 부인은 기꺼이 그를 돕겠다고 나섰다. 그리고 그것이 참으로 어려운 일이라는 데에는 그와 전적으로 뜻을 같이하면서도, 둘이 힘을 합치면 손쉽게 해낼 수 있으리라며 그에게 희망을 북돋아 주었다.\n\n노리스 부인이 동생에게 보낸 편지가 헛되지 않았으리라는 것은 어렵잖게 짐작할 수 있다. 프라이스 부인은 그토록 훤칠한 사내아이가 여럿인데 하필 딸아이를 점찍었다는 데에 다소 의아해하는 눈치였지만, 그 제안을 더없이 고맙게 받아들이며 제 딸이 심성 곱고 성품 밝은 아이라고, 결코 그들이 그 애를 내치고 싶어질 일은 없을 거라고 다짐했다. 그러면서 그 애가 몸이 좀 여리고 자그마하다는 말도 덧붙였으나, 물과 바람이 바뀌면 눈에 띄게 나아지리라는 희망에 부풀어 있었다. 딱한 여자 같으니! 아마도 그녀는 물과 바람이 바뀌면 제 아이들 여럿에게 두루 좋으련만 하고 생각했으리라.\n\n# 제2장\n\n어린 여자아이는 긴 여정을 무사히 마쳤다. 노샘프턴에서는 노리스 부인이 마중을 나왔는데, 그리하여 그녀는 맨 먼저 아이를 맞이하는 공치사를, 그리고 아이를 다른 이들에게 데려가 그들의 친절에 부탁하는 중대한 역할을 흐뭇이 누렸다.\n\n패니 프라이스는 이때 갓 열 살이었다. 첫인상에 사람을 사로잡을 만한 데는 별로 없었을지 몰라도, 적어도 친척들의 눈살을 찌푸리게 할 구석은 없었다. 나이에 비해 몸집이 작고, 혈색도 발그레하지 않았으며, 이렇다 하게 두드러진 아름다움도 없었다. 지독히 겁이 많고 수줍어 남의 눈길을 피해 움츠러들었다. 그러나 그 몸가짐은 어설프기는 해도 상스럽지 않았고, 목소리는 고왔으며, 입을 열면 얼굴이 예뻐 보였다. 토머스 경과 버트럼 부인은 아이를 무척 다정하게 맞아 주었다. 토머스 경은 아이가 얼마나 격려를 필요로 하는지 알아채고 최대한 마음을 누그러뜨려 주려 애썼으나, 도무지 어쩔 수 없이 근엄한 태도와 씨름해야 했다. 반면 버트럼 부인은 그 절반의 수고도 들이지 않고, 그가 열 마디 할 때 한 마디도 하지 않으면서, 그저 사람 좋은 미소 하나로 이내 두 사람 가운데 덜 두려운 쪽이 되었다.\n\n젊은이들은 모두 집에 있었고, 저마다 아이를 맞이하는 몫을 아주 잘 해냈다. 다들 기분 좋게, 조금도 어색해하지 않았는데, 적어도 아들들은 그러했다. 열일곱과 열여섯에 나이에 비해 키도 커서, 어린 사촌의 눈에는 어엿한 어른의 위풍을 다 갖추고 있었다. 두 딸은 나이가 더 어린 데다 아버지를 더 어려워한 탓에 다소 어쩔 줄 몰라 했다. 아버지가 이 자리에서 딸들에게 지나치게 유별난 분부를 했기 때문이다. 그러나 그들은 사람들 틈에 끼고 칭찬받는 데 너무 익숙해 타고난 수줍음 같은 것은 조금도 없었고, 사촌이 그런 것이 전혀 없는 것을 보고 도리어 자신감이 늘어, 이내 느긋한 무관심 속에 아이의 얼굴과 옷차림을 샅샅이 살필 수 있었다.\n\n그들은 참으로 훤칠한 집안이었다. 아들들은 아주 잘생겼고 딸들은 두말없이 아름다웠으며, 하나같이 잘 자라 나이보다 조숙했다. 그리하여 교육이 말씨에 만들어 놓은 것만큼이나 겉모습에서도 사촌들 사이에 뚜렷한 차이가 났고, 그 여자애들이 실제로 그토록 또래인 줄은 아무도 짐작하지 못했을 것이다. 사실 막내와 패니 사이는 두 살 차이밖에 나지 않았다. 줄리아 버트럼이 겨우 열둘, 마리아가 그보다 한 살 위였으니 말이다. 한편 그 어린 손님은 더할 나위 없이 불행했다. 누구든 무서워하고, 스스로가 부끄럽고, 떠나온 집이 사무치게 그리워, 고개를 들 줄도 몰랐고, 남이 알아듣게 말하지도, 울지 않고 말하지도 못했다. 노리스 부인은 노샘프턴에서 오는 내내 아이에게 그 놀라운 행운을, 그리고 그것이 마땅히 자아내야 할 남다른 감사와 착한 행실을 늘어놓았던 터라, 아이의 서러움은 제가 행복해하지 않는 것이 나쁜 짓이라는 생각 때문에 오히려 더 커졌다. 게다가 그토록 긴 여정의 피로도 이내 만만찮은 괴로움이 되었다. 토머스 경의 선의 어린 배려도, 아이가 착한 애가 될 거라는 노리스 부인의 온갖 참견 어린 장담도 다 헛일이었다. 버트럼 부인이 미소를 지으며 아이를 제 곁 퍼그와 함께 소파에 앉힌 것도 헛일이었고, 심지어 구스베리 타르트를 눈앞에 놓아 준 것조차 위로가 되지 못했다. 아이는 두어 입도 채 삼키기 전에 눈물이 복받쳤고, 잠이야말로 가장 미더운 벗인 듯하여, 남은 슬픔은 잠자리에서 마저 풀도록 침대로 데려가졌다.\n\n“그리 앞날이 밝은 시작은 아니네요.” 패니가 방을 나가자 노리스 부인이 말했다. “오는 내내 그렇게 일러 줬으니 좀 더 얌전히 굴 줄 알았는데. 처음에 얼마나 잘하느냐에 얼마나 많은 게 달렸는지 그토록 말해 뒀건만. 성미가 조금 뾰로통한 데가 있는 건 아니어야 할 텐데—제 어미가 그런 게 많았거든요. 하지만 저런 아이한테야 좀 봐줘야지요. 그리고 제 집을 떠나온 걸 서러워하는 게 정말로 그 애 흠이라고는 못 하겠어요. 흠이야 많아도 어쨌든 제 집이었고, 아직은 제 처지가 얼마나 나아졌는지 헤아리지 못하는 거니까요. 하지만 뭐든 정도가 있는 법이지요.”\n\n그러나 패니를 맨스필드 파크라는 낯선 곳과, 여태 익숙하던 모든 이와의 이별에 길들이는 데에는 노리스 부인이 봐주려던 것보다 훨씬 긴 시간이 걸렸다. 아이의 감정은 무척 예민했으나, 제대로 헤아려 보살펴 주기에는 너무 몰이해 속에 있었다. 아무도 매정하게 굴 생각은 없었으나, 아무도 굳이 나서서 아이의 마음을 편하게 해 주려 하지는 않았다.\n\n이튿날 버트럼 자매에게는 어린 사촌과 낯을 익히고 놀아 주라는 뜻에서 특별히 휴일이 주어졌으나, 사이가 가까워지는 일은 거의 없었다. 아이가 장식띠를 두 개밖에 갖고 있지 않고 프랑스어라고는 배운 적 없다는 것을 알고 나자 자매는 아이를 대수롭잖게 여기지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 자기들이 친절히 연주해 준 이중주에 아이가 별로 감동하지 않는 것을 보고는, 안 쓰는 장난감 몇 개를 선심 쓰듯 안겨 주고 저는 혼자 내버려 둔 채, 저희들은 그때그때 좋아하는 휴일 놀이가 무엇이든—조화를 만들거나 금박지를 축내거나—그리로 자리를 옮겨 버렸다.\n\n패니는 사촌들 곁에 있든 떨어져 있든, 공부방에 있든 응접실에 있든 관목숲에 있든 한결같이 외로웠고, 사람마다 곳마다 무언가 무서운 것을 찾아냈다. 버트럼 부인의 침묵에 풀이 죽었고, 토머스 경의 근엄한 표정에 주눅이 들었으며, 노리스 부인의 훈계에는 그만 기가 꺾였다. 손위 사촌들은 아이의 몸집을 두고 이러쿵저러쿵하며 자존심을 상하게 했고, 수줍음을 들추어 무안을 주었다. 리 선생은 아이의 무지를 의아해했고, 하녀들은 아이의 옷차림을 비웃었다. 그리고 이런 서러움에, 늘 놀이동무이자 선생이자 보모로서 제가 소중한 존재였던 동생들 생각까지 더해지자, 어린 가슴을 짓누르는 낙심은 이만저만이 아니었다.\n\n저택의 웅장함은 아이를 놀라게 했으나 위로해 주지는 못했다. 방들은 너무 커서 마음 편히 움직일 수 없었다. 무엇이든 손을 대면 상하게 할까 봐, 아이는 늘 이런저런 두려움에 떨며 조심조심 다녔고, 걸핏하면 울려고 제 방으로 물러나곤 했다. 밤에 응접실을 나설 때면 제 남다른 복을 어지간히 잘 아는 듯싶다는 말을 듣던 그 어린 여자아이는, 날마다의 서러움을 흐느껴 울다 잠드는 것으로 끝맺었다. 이렇게 한 주가 지나도록 그 조용하고 순한 태도로는 아무도 눈치채지 못하고 있었는데, 어느 날 아침 막내아들인 사촌 에드먼드가 다락 계단에 앉아 울고 있는 아이를 발견했다.\n\n“나의 어린 사촌아,” 더없이 선한 천성에서 우러난 부드러움으로 그가 말했다. “대체 무슨 일이니?” 그러고는 아이 곁에 앉아, 이렇게 들킨 것을 부끄러워하는 마음을 애써 달래며 속을 털어놓게 하려고 무진 애를 썼다. 어디가 아픈 거니? 누가 너한테 화라도 냈어? 마리아나 줄리아와 다투기라도 했니? 아니면 공부에서 무언가 모르는 게 있어 내가 설명해 주면 좋겠니? 한마디로, 내가 구해 줄 수 있는 것, 해 줄 수 있는 것이 뭐든 있는 거니? 오랫동안 “아니에요, 아니에요—전혀요—아니에요, 고마워요” 말고는 아무 대답도 얻지 못했다. 그래도 그는 굽히지 않았다. 그리고 아이의 집 이야기로 말머리를 돌리기가 무섭게 부쩍 커진 흐느낌이 서러움의 근원이 어디 있는지 그에게 일러 주었다. 그는 아이를 달래려 애썼다.\n\n“어머니를 떠나온 게 서러운 거구나, 나의 어린 패니야.” 그가 말했다. “그건 네가 참 착한 아이라는 뜻이란다. 하지만 잊지 마라, 너는 지금 너를 다 사랑하고 너를 행복하게 해 주고 싶어 하는 친척들과 벗들 곁에 있단다. 우리 공원으로 나가 걷자꾸나. 그러면서 네 형제자매 이야기를 다 들려주렴.”\n\n이야기를 이어 가면서 그는, 그 형제자매가 다 소중하기는 해도 그중 유독 아이의 마음에 더 자주 떠오르는 이가 하나 있음을 알게 되었다. 아이가 가장 많이 이야기하고 가장 보고 싶어 한 것은 윌리엄이었다. 맏이인 윌리엄은 저보다 한 살 위로, 늘 함께 지낸 동무이자 벗이었고, (어머니가 끔찍이 아끼는 아들이었기에) 무슨 괴로운 일이 있을 때마다 어머니 앞에서 저를 두둔해 주던 이였다. “윌리엄은 제가 떠나오는 걸 싫어했어요. 저를 정말 무척 그리워할 거라고 그랬어요.” “하지만 윌리엄이 너한테 편지를 쓸 거야, 틀림없이.” “네, 그러겠다고 약속했어요. 그런데 저더러 먼저 쓰라고 했어요.” “그럼 언제 쓸 거니?” 아이는 고개를 떨구고 머뭇머뭇 대답했다. “모르겠어요. 종이가 없어서요.”\n\n“그게 다라면, 내가 종이며 필요한 건 뭐든 대 줄 테니, 언제든 네가 쓰고 싶을 때 편지를 쓰렴. 윌리엄한테 편지를 쓰면 기쁘겠니?”\n\n“네, 아주요.”\n\n“그럼 지금 쓰자. 나랑 아침식사방으로 가자. 거기 가면 다 있고, 우리끼리만 있을 수 있을 거야.”\n\n“그런데 사촌 오빠, 이게 우편으로 부쳐질까요?”\n\n“그럼, 나만 믿으렴. 다른 편지들이랑 같이 부칠 거야. 게다가 삼촌이 무료 우송으로 부쳐 주실 테니 윌리엄한테는 한 푼도 들지 않을 거란다.”\n\n“삼촌이요!” 패니가 겁먹은 얼굴로 되뇌었다.\n\n“그래, 네가 편지를 다 쓰면 내가 아버지께 가져가서 우송 서명을 받을 거야.”\n\n패니는 그것이 대담한 짓이다 싶었으나 더는 버티지 않았다. 그렇게 둘은 함께 아침식사방으로 갔고, 거기서 에드먼드는 아이에게 종이를 마련해 주고, 친오빠라도 그만큼밖에 못 냈을 정성으로—아마 그보다는 좀 더 반듯하게—줄을 그어 주었다. 그는 아이가 편지를 쓰는 내내 곁을 지키며, 주머니칼이든 철자든 필요할 때마다 거들어 주었다. 그리고 아이가 무척이나 고맙게 여긴 이런 마음씀에 더해, 그 무엇보다도 아이를 기쁘게 한 오라비를 향한 친절까지 베풀었다. 그는 손수 사촌 윌리엄에게 안부의 말을 적고, 봉인 안에 반 기니를 넣어 부쳤다. 그때 패니의 감정은 스스로도 도저히 말로 옮길 수 없으리라 여겨질 만한 것이었다. 그러나 그 얼굴빛과 몇 마디 꾸밈없는 말이 그 고마움과 기쁨을 낱낱이 전해 주었고, 사촌은 아이가 흥미로운 존재로 여겨지기 시작했다. 그는 아이와 더 많은 이야기를 나누었고, 아이가 하는 모든 말에서 그 애가 정 많은 마음과 옳게 살고자 하는 강한 바람을 지녔음을 확신하게 되었다. 또 제 처지를 예민하게 느끼는 감수성과 큰 소심함으로 미루어, 아이가 마땅히 더 마음을 써 줄 만한 아이임을 알아보았다. 그는 아이에게 알고서 아픔을 준 적이 한 번도 없었으나, 이제 아이에게는 좀 더 적극적인 친절이 필요하다고 느꼈다. 그런 뜻에서 우선 아이가 모두를 두려워하는 마음을 덜어 주려 애썼고, 특히 마리아와 줄리아와 함께 놀며 되도록 즐겁게 지내라고 좋은 말을 많이 해 주었다.\n\n이날부터 패니는 한결 마음이 편해졌다. 제게 벗이 하나 생겼다는 것을 느꼈고, 사촌 에드먼드의 친절 덕에 다른 모든 이 앞에서도 기운이 났다. 그곳은 덜 낯설어졌고 사람들도 덜 두려워졌다. 도무지 무서움을 떨칠 수 없는 이들이 그중 더러 있었다 해도, 적어도 그들의 방식을 알아 가고, 거기 맞추는 가장 좋은 요령을 익히기 시작했다. 처음에는 모두의, 그중에서도 특히 제 마음의 평온을 크게 어지럽히던 그 촌스러움과 어설픔도 자연히 스러져 갔다. 이제 삼촌 앞에 나서는 것이 그다지 무섭지 않았고, 노리스 이모의 목소리에도 그리 크게 움찔하지 않았다. 사촌들에게 아이는 이따금 그런대로 반가운 동무가 되었다. 나이도 어리고 힘도 약해 늘 붙어 다닐 상대로는 모자랐으나, 자매의 놀이며 궁리에는 때때로 셋째가 있으면 요긴한 것들이 있었고, 더구나 그 셋째가 고분고분 잘 맞춰 주는 성미라면 더욱 그러했다. 그래서 이모가 아이의 흠을 캐물을 때나 오라비 에드먼드가 아이에게 친절히 대해 주라고 채근할 때면, 자매도 “패니가 그만하면 심성은 착하다”고 인정하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n에드먼드는 스스로 한결같이 친절했다. 그리고 톰에게서 아이가 견뎌야 했던 것이라고는, 열일곱 청년이 열 살배기 아이를 두고 으레 짓궂어도 괜찮다 여기는 그런 장난기가 고작이었다. 톰은 이제 막 세상에 발을 들이는 참으로, 혈기가 넘치고, 오로지 쓰고 즐기려고 태어난 줄 아는 맏아들 특유의 활수한 기질을 두루 갖추고 있었다. 어린 사촌에게 베푸는 그의 친절은 제 처지며 권리에 걸맞은 것이었다. 아이에게 아주 예쁜 선물을 몇 가지 해 주고는, 아이를 보며 웃었다.\n\n아이의 겉모습과 기색이 나아지자 토머스 경과 노리스 부인은 저희의 자비로운 계획을 한층 흐뭇하게 되새겼다. 그리고 이내 두 사람 사이에서, 아이가 영리하다고는 도저히 못 해도 고분고분한 성미를 보이고 별 말썽을 부릴 것 같지 않다는 결론이 내려졌다. 아이의 능력을 얕잡아 보는 것은 그 둘에게만 국한된 일이 아니었다. 패니는 읽고, 바느질하고, 쓸 줄은 알았으나 그 이상은 아무것도 배운 바가 없었다. 그래서 사촌들은 저희가 진작부터 익숙하던 숱한 것들을 아이가 모르는 것을 보고 아이를 지독히 아둔하다 여겼으며, 처음 두어 주 동안은 응접실에 그런 소식을 새록새록 물어 날랐다. “어머, 어머니, 글쎄 생각해 보세요, 우리 사촌은 유럽 지도를 못 맞춰요—아니, 러시아의 주요 강 이름도 못 대요—아니, 소아시아라는 말은 들어 본 적도 없대요—아니, 수채물감이랑 크레용도 구별 못 해요!—어쩜 그렇게 이상해요!—이렇게 아둔한 애 얘기 들어 보신 적 있으세요?”\n\n“얘들아,” 사려 깊은 이모가 대꾸하곤 했다. “그거 참 딱하구나. 하지만 다들 너희만큼 배움이 빠르고 조숙하기를 바라서는 안 된단다.”\n\n“그렇지만 이모, 그 앤 정말 너무 아무것도 몰라요!—글쎄, 어젯밤에 우리가 아일랜드에 가려면 어느 길로 가겠느냐고 물었더니, 와이트섬을 건너가면 된다지 뭐예요. 온통 와이트섬 생각뿐이라, 세상에 다른 섬이라고는 없는 것처럼 그걸 그냥 ‘그 섬’이라고 불러요. 저라면 그 애 나이가 되기 훨씬 전에 그만한 것도 몰랐다면 정말 부끄러웠을 거예요. 저는 그 애가 아직 티끌만큼도 모르는 걸 많이 알지 못하던 때가 언제였는지 기억도 안 나요. 이모, 우리가 잉글랜드 왕들을 즉위 연도까지 붙여 가며 순서대로, 게다가 치세의 주요 사건들까지 줄줄 외우던 게 벌써 얼마나 오래전 일이에요!”\n\n“맞아요,” 다른 아이가 거들었다. “로마 황제들도 세베루스까지 다 외웠고요. 그것 말고도 이교의 신화며, 온갖 금속과 준금속과 행성과 이름난 철학자들까지 잔뜩 외웠잖아요.”\n\n“참말 그렇지, 얘들아. 하지만 너희는 놀라운 기억력을 복으로 타고났고, 너희 딱한 사촌은 아마 그런 게 도통 없는 거란다. 기억력이란 다른 모든 것과 마찬가지로 사람마다 천차만별이거든. 그러니 사촌을 좀 봐주고, 그 애의 모자람을 가엾이 여겨야 해. 그리고 잊지 마라. 너희 자신이 아무리 조숙하고 영리하더라도 언제나 겸손해야 한단다. 너희가 이미 아는 게 많다 해도, 앞으로 배워야 할 게 훨씬 더 많으니까.”\n\n“네, 열일곱이 될 때까지는 그렇겠지요. 그런데 이모, 패니 얘기 하나 더 해 드릴게요, 어찌나 별나고 아둔한지. 글쎄 그 애는 음악도 그림도 배우고 싶지 않대요.”\n\n“하긴 얘야, 그거야말로 참 아둔한 소리고, 재능도 향상심도 크게 모자란 표시지. 하지만 이래저래 따져 보면, 그편이 차라리 나을는지도 모르겠구나. 왜냐하면, (다 내 덕이지만) 너희 아버지와 어머니가 그토록 착하셔서 그 애를 너희와 함께 길러 주시긴 해도, 그 애가 너희만큼 온갖 재주를 갖출 필요는 조금도 없거든—오히려 서로 차이가 나는 편이 훨씬 바람직하고말고.”\n\n노리스 부인이 조카딸들의 마음을 빚는 데 보탠 훈수란 이런 것들이었다. 그러니 그 촉망받는 재주와 이른 학식에도 불구하고 자매가 자기 성찰이며 너그러움이며 겸손이라는, 그리 흔치 않은 소양에는 아주 결여되어 있었던 것도 그리 놀랄 일이 아니다. 성품만 빼면 그들은 나무랄 데 없이 잘 배웠다. 토머스 경은 무엇이 모자란지 알지 못했다. 참으로 노심초사하는 아버지이기는 했으나 겉으로 다정하지는 못했고, 그 삼가는 태도가 자기 앞에서는 딸들의 활기를 온통 짓눌러 버렸기 때문이다.\n\n버트럼 부인은 딸들의 교육에 눈곱만큼도 마음을 쓰지 않았다. 그런 데 신경 쓸 겨를이 없었다. 그녀는 곱게 차려입고 소파에 앉아, 쓸모도 아름다움도 없는 기다란 바느질감을 붙들고 하루를 보내는 여자였다. 자식보다 제 퍼그를 더 마음에 두면서도, 제게 불편만 끼치지 않는다면 자식들에게는 무척 너그러웠고, 중요한 일은 뭐든 토머스 경에게, 소소한 일은 언니에게 맡겨 이끌려 갔다. 설령 딸들을 위해 쓸 겨를이 더 있었다 한들, 그녀는 아마 그럴 필요가 없다고 여겼을 것이다. 딸들은 가정교사의 보살핌 아래 마땅한 선생들까지 두고 있었으니 더 바랄 게 없었기 때문이다. 패니가 배움이 더디다는 것에 대해서는, “참 안된 일이지만, 원래 아둔한 사람도 있는 법이니 패니가 더 애를 써야지. 달리 어쩌겠느냐. 그리고 그렇게 굼뜬 것만 빼면 그 딱한 것한테 나쁜 데라고는 없고, 심부름을 시키거나 필요한 걸 가져오게 하면 늘 손발이 야무지고 빠르더라”고 할 뿐이었다.\n\n패니는 무지하고 소심하다는 온갖 흠에도 불구하고 맨스필드 파크에 자리를 잡았고, 옛집에 쏟던 정을 상당 부분 이곳으로 옮기는 법을 익혀 가며, 사촌들 틈에서 그런대로 불행하지 않게 자라났다. 마리아나 줄리아에게 대놓고 심술궂은 데는 없었다. 그리고 패니는 그들의 대우에 종종 자존심이 상했으나, 제 몫에 대한 생각이 너무 낮아 그것을 서러워할 줄도 몰랐다.\n\n아이가 이 집안에 들어올 무렵부터, 버트럼 부인은 몸이 좀 약해진 데다 워낙 게을렀던 탓에, 해마다 봄이면 지내던 도시의 집을 접고 시골에만 눌러앉았다. 토머스 경은 홀로 남아 의회의 소임을 보러 다녔고, 아내의 부재로 안락이 늘든 줄든 그것은 그가 감당할 몫이었다. 그리하여 버트럼 자매는 시골에서 계속 기억력을 갈고, 이중주를 익히고, 키가 크며 처녀 티가 나 갔다. 아버지는 딸들이 겉모습에서나 몸가짐에서나 재주에서나, 제 노심초사를 흡족게 할 온갖 모습으로 자라 가는 것을 지켜보았다. 맏아들은 무심하고 헤퍼 벌써부터 그에게 적잖은 근심을 안겼으나, 다른 자식들은 그에게 오로지 좋은 것만을 약속했다. 그는 느꼈다. 딸들은 버트럼이라는 성을 지니는 동안에는 그 이름에 새로운 기품을 더해 줄 것이고, 그 성을 버릴 때에는—그가 믿기로—그 이름의 어엿한 인척을 넓혀 주리라고. 그리고 에드먼드의 인품, 그 굳센 양식과 곧은 마음은 저 자신은 물론 제게 딸린 모든 이에게 쓸모와 명예와 행복을 안겨 줄 가망이 가장 밝았다. 에드먼드는 장차 성직자가 될 참이었다.\n\n제 자식들이 안겨 주는 근심과 흐뭇함 가운데서도, 토머스 경은 프라이스 부인의 아이들을 위해 할 수 있는 일을 잊지 않았다. 그는 그 아들들이 어떤 진로를 정할 나이가 되자, 교육과 앞길을 놓아 주는 데 아낌없이 도왔다. 그리고 패니는 제 식구와 거의 완전히 떨어져 있으면서도, 그들에게 베풀어진 친절이나 그들의 처지나 처신에 조금이라도 밝은 소식을 들으면 더없이 참된 흐뭇함을 느꼈다. 여러 해가 흐르는 동안 단 한 번, 오직 한 번, 아이는 윌리엄과 함께 지내는 기쁨을 누렸다. 나머지 형제자매는 얼굴도 보지 못했다. 아무도 아이가 잠깐 다니러라도 다시 그들 틈에 가는 것을 생각하는 것 같지 않았고, 고향의 누구도 아이를 원하는 것 같지 않았다. 그러나 윌리엄은 아이가 떠난 지 얼마 안 되어 뱃사람이 되기로 마음먹었고, 바다로 나가기 전에 노샘프턴셔에서 누이와 한 주를 지내라는 초대를 받았다. 만났을 때의 애틋한 정, 함께 있는 더없는 기쁨, 즐겁게 웃던 시간과 진지하게 이야기 나누던 순간은 능히 짐작할 수 있으리라. 마지막까지도 식지 않던 소년의 부푼 꿈과 기상도, 그가 떠날 때 소녀가 느낀 서러움도. 다행히 그 방문은 크리스마스 휴가에 이루어져, 아이는 곧장 사촌 에드먼드에게서 위로를 구할 수 있었다. 그는 윌리엄이 그 직업 덕에 앞으로 무슨 일을 하고 어떤 사람이 될지 그토록 멋진 이야기들을 들려주어, 아이가 차츰 그 이별에도 얼마간 쓸모가 있을지 모른다고 여기게 만들었다. 에드먼드의 우정은 한 번도 아이를 저버리지 않았다. 이튼을 떠나 옥스퍼드로 간 뒤에도 그 다정한 마음씨는 조금도 변하지 않았고, 그것을 증명할 기회만 더 잦아졌을 뿐이다. 남보다 더 해 준다고 티를 내는 법도, 너무 많이 해 줄까 봐 사리는 법도 없이, 그는 언제나 아이의 이익에 진심이었고 아이의 마음을 헤아렸으며, 아이의 좋은 점이 남에게 알려지도록, 또 그것이 더 드러나지 못하게 가로막는 그 수줍음을 이겨 내도록 애썼다. 아이에게 조언과 위로와 격려를 아끼지 않으면서.\n\n다른 모든 이가 아이를 뒤로 밀어 두는 터라 그의 홀로 된 뒷받침만으로는 아이를 앞으로 끌어낼 수 없었다. 그러나 그의 마음씀은 다른 면에서, 곧 아이의 정신을 갈고닦고 그 즐거움을 넓혀 주는 데 더없이 중요했다. 그는 아이가 영리하다는 것을, 좋은 분별력에 더해 빠른 이해력을 지녔고, 제대로만 이끌면 그 자체로 하나의 교육이 될 독서 취미를 지녔음을 알아보았다. 리 선생은 아이에게 프랑스어를 가르치고 날마다 정해진 분량의 역사를 읽혔다. 그러나 아이의 한가한 시간을 매혹한 책들을 골라 준 것은 그였다. 그는 아이의 취향을 북돋고 판단을 바로잡아 주었다. 아이가 읽은 것에 대해 함께 이야기함으로써 독서를 유익하게 만들었고, 사려 깊은 칭찬으로 그 매력을 한층 돋우었다. 이런 보살핌에 대한 보답으로 아이는 윌리엄을 빼면 세상 누구보다 그를 사랑했다. 아이의 마음은 그 둘 사이에 나뉘어 있었다.\n\n# 제3장\n\n이 집안에서 처음으로 일어난 이렇다 할 사건은 노리스 씨의 죽음이었다. 패니가 열다섯 살쯤 되었을 때의 일로, 그것은 필연적으로 여러 변화와 새로운 국면을 불러왔다. 노리스 부인은 목사관을 떠나 처음에는 파크로 옮겨 왔다가, 나중에는 마을에 있는 토머스 경의 작은 집으로 거처를 옮겼다. 그러고는 남편을 잃은 슬픔은 그가 없어도 얼마든지 잘 지낼 수 있다는 생각으로, 수입이 줄어든 것은 더 빠듯이 절약해야 할 게 뻔하다는 생각으로 달랬다.\n\n그 성직록은 이후 에드먼드의 몫이 될 것이었다. 만약 그의 삼촌이 몇 해만 일찍 세상을 떠났더라면, 에드먼드가 서품 받을 나이가 될 때까지 어느 벗에게 맡겨 두었을 터였다. 그러나 그 일이 있기 전에 톰의 낭비가 어찌나 심했던지, 다음번 임명권을 달리 처분하지 않을 수 없게 되었고, 결국 동생이 형의 즐거움 값을 함께 치러야 했다. 에드먼드를 위해 실제로 맡아 둔 다른 성직록이 하나 더 있기는 했다. 그러나 그 사정 덕에 토머스 경의 양심이 다소 가벼워지기는 했어도, 그것이 부당한 처사임을 느끼지 않을 수 없었다. 그리하여 그는 맏아들에게도 같은 생각을 절실히 심어 주려 애썼는데, 여태 그가 말이나 행동으로 이룰 수 있었던 그 무엇보다 더 나은 효과가 나기를 바라서였다.\n\n“톰, 너를 생각하면 낯이 뜨겁다.” 그는 더없이 위엄 있는 태도로 말했다. “내가 이런 궁여지책에 내몰린 것이 부끄럽고, 이 마당에 형으로서 네가 느낄 심정은 딱하게 여겨 주마. 너는 십 년, 이십 년, 삼십 년, 어쩌면 평생토록 에드먼드가 마땅히 받아야 할 수입의 절반이 넘는 것을 그 아이에게서 빼앗은 것이다. 훗날 내 힘으로든 네 힘으로든 (부디 그리되기를 바란다) 그 아이에게 더 나은 성직을 마련해 줄 수 있을지도 모르지. 그러나 잊어서는 안 될 것이, 그런 은전이라 한들 어차피 그 아이가 우리에게 당연히 요구할 수 있는 몫을 넘지 않는다는 것, 그리고 지금 네 빚에 쫓겨 그 아이가 부득이 포기해야 하는 이 확실한 이득을 무엇으로도 사실상 대신할 수 없다는 것이다.”\n\n톰은 얼마간 부끄러움과 얼마간 서글픔 속에 귀 기울였다. 그러나 되도록 빨리 그 자리를 벗어나고 나자, 이내 명랑한 이기심으로 이렇게 되새길 수 있었다. 첫째로 자기는 몇몇 친구들 절반만큼도 빚을 지지 않았다는 것, 둘째로 아버지가 이 일을 지독히 성가시게 부풀렸다는 것, 셋째로 다음번 성직 보유자가 누가 되든 십중팔구 머잖아 죽으리라는 것.\n\n노리스 씨가 죽자 그 임명권은 그랜트 박사의 차지가 되었고, 그리하여 그는 맨스필드에 와서 살게 되었다. 그런데 마흔다섯의 정정한 사내로 밝혀지자, 버트럼 씨의 셈속을 어긋나게 할 성싶었다. 그러나 “아니야, 저자는 목이 짧은 게 중풍 걸리기 딱 좋은 부류라, 좋은 걸 잔뜩 먹여 두면 머잖아 뻗어 버릴걸.”\n\n그에게는 저보다 열다섯 살쯤 어린 아내가 있었으나 자식은 없었다. 두 사람은 아주 점잖고 상냥한 사람들이라는 흔한 호평과 함께 이웃에 발을 들였다.\n\n이제 토머스 경이 처형에게 조카딸을 맡을 제 몫을 요구하리라 기대할 때가 되었다. 노리스 부인의 처지가 달라지고 패니의 나이가 찬 것이 함께 지내는 데 대한 종전의 반대를 없앨 뿐 아니라, 오히려 그것을 더없이 마땅한 일로 만드는 듯싶었다. 게다가 맏아들의 낭비에 더해 최근 서인도 영지에서 얼마간 손실을 본 탓에 제 형편이 예전만 못해진 터라, 아이를 부양하는 비용과 앞날을 마련해 줄 의무에서 벗어나는 것이 그 자신에게도 싫지 않은 일이 되었다. 그런 일이 틀림없이 그리되리라 굳게 믿은 나머지 그는 아내에게 그 가망을 넌지시 비쳤다. 그리고 그 이야기가 아내의 머리에 다시 떠오른 것이 마침 패니가 곁에 있을 때였던지라, 부인은 태연하게 아이에게 말했다. “그러니까 패니야, 너는 이제 우리를 떠나 우리 언니와 살게 될 거란다. 어떠니, 마음에 들겠니?”\n\n패니는 너무 놀란 나머지 이모의 말을 되뇌는 것밖에 하지 못했다. “저희를 떠난다고요?”\n\n“그래, 얘야. 놀랄 게 뭐 있니? 너는 우리와 다섯 해를 지냈고, 우리 언니도 노리스 씨가 죽으면 늘 너를 데려갈 작정이었단다. 그래도 여기 올라와서 내 본 뜨는 일은 거들어야 한다, 그건 그거고.”\n\n그 소식은 뜻밖이었던 만큼이나 패니에게는 달갑지 않았다. 아이는 노리스 이모에게서 한 번도 다정한 대접을 받은 적이 없었고, 그녀를 사랑할 수도 없었다.\n\n“떠나게 되면 정말 서운할 거예요.” 아이가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“그래, 그렇겠지. 그거야 당연하고말고. 이 집에 들어온 뒤로 너만큼 속상할 일 없이 지낸 아이도 세상에 또 없을 게다.”\n\n“제가 은혜를 모르는 건 아니었으면 좋겠어요, 이모님.” 패니가 얌전히 말했다.\n\n“아니지, 얘야. 그러길 바란다. 나는 늘 너를 참 착한 아이로 여겨 왔단다.”\n\n“그럼 저는 다시는 여기서 못 사는 건가요?”\n\n“다시는 못 살지, 얘야. 하지만 편안한 집이 어김없이 있잖니. 이 집에 있든 저 집에 있든 너한테야 별 차이도 없을 거다.”\n\n패니는 몹시 서러운 마음으로 방을 나섰다. 그 차이가 그토록 작다고는 도저히 느낄 수 없었고, 이모와 함께 사는 것을 흡족한 마음으로는 도무지 그려 볼 수 없었다. 에드먼드를 만나자마자 아이는 그에게 제 괴로움을 털어놓았다.\n\n“사촌 오빠,” 아이가 말했다. “제가 도무지 싫은 일이 벌어지려고 해요. 오빠는 여태 제가 처음엔 싫어하던 일도 곧잘 받아들이게 해 주셨지만, 이번만은 그러지 못하실 거예요. 저는 아주 노리스 이모하고만 살게 됐어요.”\n\n“정말?”\n\n“네, 버트럼 이모가 방금 그렇게 말씀하셨어요. 아주 정해진 일이래요. 저는 맨스필드 파크를 떠나, 아마 이모가 그리로 옮기시는 대로 화이트하우스로 가게 될 거예요.”\n\n“글쎄, 패니, 네가 그 계획을 싫어하지만 않는다면 나는 아주 훌륭한 계획이라고 하겠는데.”\n\n“어머, 오빠!”\n\n“그것 말고는 죄다 좋은 점뿐이야. 이모가 너를 원하는 건 분별 있는 여자다운 처사지. 벗이자 동무를 꼭 있어야 할 바로 그 자리에서 고르는 거고, 그 돈 욕심이 끼어들지 않아 나는 기쁘다. 너는 이모에게 마땅히 되어야 할 존재가 될 거야. 이 일이 너를 그리 많이 괴롭히지는 않았으면 좋겠구나, 패니?”\n\n“정말 괴로운걸요. 좋아할 수가 없어요. 저는 이 집이, 이 집 안의 모든 게 좋아요. 거기서는 아무것도 좋아하지 못할 거예요. 제가 이모 앞에서 얼마나 불편한지 오빠도 아시잖아요.”\n\n“네가 어렸을 때 이모가 너를 대한 태도야 두둔할 말이 없다. 하지만 그건 우리한테도, 거의 다 마찬가지였어. 이모는 아이들한테 상냥하게 구는 법을 도무지 몰랐거든. 그렇지만 이제 너는 더 나은 대접을 받을 나이가 됐어. 내 보기엔 이모도 벌써 나아지고 있고. 그리고 네가 이모의 유일한 동무가 되면, 너는 이모한테 중요한 사람이 될 수밖에 없어.”\n\n“저는 누구에게도 중요한 사람이 될 수 없어요.”\n\n“무엇이 그걸 막는다는 거니?”\n\n“전부 다요. 제 처지도, 제 어리석음과 어설픔도요.”\n\n“네 어리석음과 어설픔으로 말하자면, 나의 패니야, 정말이지 너한테는 그런 그림자조차 없다. 그런 말을 이렇게 엉뚱하게 갖다 붙일 때 말고는 말이야. 너를 아는 곳에서 네가 중요한 사람이 되지 못할 까닭은 이 세상에 하나도 없어. 너는 좋은 분별력과 고운 성품을 지녔고, 게다가 나는 네가 친절을 받으면 반드시 갚고 싶어 하는 고마운 마음을 지녔다고 믿는다. 벗이자 동무로서 그보다 더 나은 자질을 나는 알지 못해.”\n\n“너무 과분한 말씀이에요.” 그런 칭찬에 얼굴을 붉히며 패니가 말했다. “저를 그토록 좋게 봐 주시니, 제가 어떻게 마땅한 만큼 감사드릴 수 있을까요. 아, 오빠, 제가 떠나야 한다면, 저는 목숨이 다하는 순간까지 오빠의 다정함을 기억할 거예요.”\n\n“원, 패니, 화이트하우스쯤 되는 거리라면 나야 기억되기를 바라도 되겠지. 너는 마치 이백 마일이나 떨어진 데로 가는 것처럼 말하는구나, 고작 공원 하나 건너가는 것뿐인데. 너는 여전히 거의 예전만큼 우리 식구일 거야. 두 집안은 한 해 내내 날마다 만날 테니까. 다른 점이라고는, 이모와 살면 네가 마땅히 그래야 하듯 앞으로 나서게 되리라는 것뿐이야. 여기서는 네가 뒤에 숨을 사람이 너무 많지. 하지만 이모하고 있으면 너는 어쩔 수 없이 네 목소리를 내야 할 거야.”\n\n“아, 그런 말씀 마세요.”\n\n“나는 그 말을 해야겠고, 기꺼이 하겠다. 노리스 이모는 지금 너를 맡기에 우리 어머니보다 훨씬 알맞은 분이야. 이모는 정말로 마음을 두는 사람을 위해서라면 무엇이든 아끼지 않는 성미고, 네가 타고난 능력을 제대로 발휘하도록 다그쳐 줄 거야.”\n\n패니는 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. “저는 오빠처럼 볼 수가 없어요. 그래도 저 자신보다는 오빠가 옳다고 믿어야겠지요. 어쩔 수 없는 일에 마음을 붙이도록 애써 주셔서 정말 고마워요. 이모가 정말로 저를 아껴 준다고 여길 수만 있다면, 누군가에게 소중한 사람이라고 느끼는 건 참 기쁜 일일 거예요. 여기서는, 저도 알지만, 저는 아무에게도 그런 사람이 못 되잖아요. 그런데도 저는 이곳이 이렇게 좋은걸요.”\n\n“패니, 네가 떠나지 않는 건 바로 이곳이야. 집은 떠나도 말이지. 너는 여전히 예전처럼 공원과 정원을 마음껏 누빌 수 있어. 그 한결같은 여린 마음조차 그런 이름뿐인 변화에는 놀랄 것 없단다. 늘 거닐던 그 길, 골라 읽던 그 서재, 바라보던 그 사람들, 타던 그 말이 그대로일 테니까.”\n\n“정말 그래요. 맞아요, 그 정든 늙은 회색 조랑말! 아, 오빠, 제가 말 타기를 얼마나 무서워했는지, 그게 제 몸에 좋을 거라는 말을 듣기만 해도 얼마나 겁이 났는지 (아, 말 이야기만 나오면 삼촌이 입을 여실까 봐 제가 얼마나 떨었던지), 그런데 오빠가 제 두려움을 조곤조곤 타일러 없애 주고 조금만 지나면 좋아하게 될 거라고 다독여 주신 그 다정한 수고를 떠올리면, 그리고 그 말이 얼마나 옳았는지 지금 느끼고 보면, 오빠는 앞으로도 늘 그렇게 잘 내다보시리라 믿고 싶어져요.”\n\n“그리고 나는, 노리스 이모와 지내는 것이 말 타기가 네 몸에 좋았던 만큼이나 네 마음에도 좋을 거라고, 나아가 네 궁극의 행복에도 그만큼 좋을 거라고 굳게 믿는다.”\n\n이렇게 두 사람의 이야기는 끝났다. 그러나 그것이 패니에게 조금이라도 걸맞은 쓸모가 있었느냐 하면, 차라리 안 했어도 그만이었을 것이다. 노리스 부인에게는 아이를 데려갈 뜻이 눈곱만큼도 없었으니 말이다. 이번 일에서 그것은 그녀의 머릿속에 오로지 애써 피해야 할 일로만 떠올랐을 뿐이다. 그런 기대를 받지 않으려고 그녀는 맨스필드 교구의 건물들 가운데 그나마 점잖은 축에 들 만한 것 중 가장 작은 집을 골라 두었다. 화이트하우스는 저와 하인들이 겨우 들어가고 벗을 위한 남는 방 하나를 둘 만한 크기밖에 안 되었는데, 그 남는 방을 그녀는 유난히 강조했다. 목사관 시절에는 남는 방이 한 번도 아쉬운 적 없었건만, 이제는 벗을 위한 남는 방이 꼭 있어야 한다는 것을 한시도 잊는 법이 없었다. 그러나 그 온갖 방비로도 그녀는 더 그럴싸한 속셈을 품고 있다는 의심을 면치 못했다. 아니, 어쩌면 남는 방이 얼마나 중요한지 그리 떠벌린 탓에, 도리어 토머스 경이 그것을 정말 패니를 위한 것이려니 오해했는지도 모른다. 버트럼 부인이 노리스 부인에게 무심코 이렇게 말하는 바람에 그 일은 이내 분명해졌다.\n\n“언니, 패니가 언니랑 살러 가면 리 선생은 더 둘 필요가 없을 것 같아요.”\n\n노리스 부인은 하마터면 소스라칠 뻔했다. “나랑 살다니, 버트럼 부인! 그게 무슨 말이에요?”\n\n“그 애가 언니랑 사는 게 아니었어요? 토머스 경하고 그렇게 정한 줄 알았는데.”\n\n“내가! 천만에. 나는 그 일로 토머스 경한테 한마디도 꺼낸 적 없고, 그이도 나한테 그런 적 없어요. 패니가 나랑 살다니! 세상에 내가 생각이나 할 일도 아니고, 우리 둘을 제대로 아는 사람이라면 누구도 바랄 일이 아니지요. 원, 세상에! 내가 패니를 데리고 뭘 어쩌겠어요? 나 같은 거! 가련하고 힘없고 외로운 과부, 아무짝에도 못 쓸 몸에, 기력도 아주 꺾여 버린 사람이. 그런 나이의 계집아이를 데리고 내가 뭘 하겠어요? 열다섯 살짜리를! 무엇보다도 가장 많은 관심과 보살핌이 필요하고, 아무리 명랑한 사람의 진을 다 빼놓을 바로 그 나이를! 설마 토머스 경이 진심으로 그런 걸 바랐을 리 없어요! 토머스 경은 나한테 더없이 좋은 벗이에요. 나를 위하는 사람이라면 누구도 그런 걸 내놓을 리 없다고요. 대체 토머스 경이 어쩌다 그 얘기를 부인한테 했대요?”\n\n“글쎄, 나도 모르겠어요. 그게 제일 낫다고 여기셨나 보지요.”\n\n“그런데 뭐라고 하셨어요? 내가 패니를 맡아 주기를 바란다고야 하셨을 리 없어요. 진심으로야 그이가 나더러 그러라고 바랐을 리 없다니까요.”\n\n“아뇨. 그저 그럴 법하다고만 하셨어요. 나도 그렇게 여겼고요. 우리 둘 다 그게 언니한테 위안이 될 거라 생각했지요. 하지만 언니가 싫다면 더 할 말은 없어요. 그 앤 여기 있어도 짐이 되진 않으니까요.”\n\n“이보세요, 부인, 내 딱한 처지를 생각하면, 그 애가 나한테 무슨 위안이 되겠어요? 여기 이렇게, 세상에 둘도 없는 남편을 여읜 가련하고 쓸쓸한 과부가, 그이 병간호에 건강까지 잃고, 기력은 그보다 더 못하고, 이 세상 온갖 평안이 다 부서진 채로, 숙녀 체면이나 겨우 지키며 그 정든 고인의 이름에 누가 되지 않을 만큼 살아갈 것도 빠듯한 사람이 앉아 있는데—패니 같은 짐을 떠맡아서 대체 무슨 위안을 얻겠어요? 설령 내 좋자고 그러고 싶더라도, 그 딱한 애한테 그런 못할 짓은 안 하겠어요. 그 앤 좋은 손에 맡겨져 잘될 게 뻔한데. 나는 내 슬픔과 어려움을 그저 나 혼자 힘으로 헤쳐 나가야지요.”\n\n“그럼 언니는 아주 혼자 사는 게 괜찮겠어요?”\n\n“버트럼 부인도 참! 나 같은 게 고독 말고 뭐에 어울리겠어요? 이따금 내 작은 오두막에 벗 하나쯤 맞이하기를 바라기야 하겠지요 (벗을 위한 잠자리는 늘 마련해 둘 거예요). 하지만 앞으로 내 나날은 거의 다 아주 외로이 파묻혀 지낼 거예요. 그저 겨우 수지만 맞출 수 있다면, 내가 바라는 건 그뿐이에요.”\n\n“언니, 그래도 형편이 그렇게까지 나쁘진 않았으면 좋겠어요. 토머스 경 말로는 언니한테 연 육백 파운드가 들어온다던데요.”\n\n“버트럼 부인, 나는 불평하는 게 아니에요. 예전처럼은 못 산다는 걸 나도 알아요. 하지만 아낄 수 있는 데서는 아끼고, 살림을 더 알뜰히 꾸리는 법을 익혀야지요. 나야 지금껏 인심 후한 살림꾼이었지만, 이제 절약한다고 부끄러워할 것 없어요. 내 처지는 수입만큼이나 달라졌으니까요. 딱한 노리스 씨야 교구 목사로서 마땅히 치러야 할 것이 많았지만, 나한테야 그런 걸 바랄 순 없지요. 뜨내기손님들이 우리 부엌에서 축낸 게 얼마인지 아무도 몰라요. 화이트하우스에서는 그런 걸 더 잘 챙겨야 해요. 나는 반드시 수입 안에서 살아야지, 안 그러면 비참해질 거예요. 그리고 솔직히, 그보다 좀 더 해내서 연말에 얼마간 모아 둘 수 있다면 나는 무척 흐뭇하겠어요.”\n\n“그러시겠지요. 언니야 늘 그러시잖아요?”\n\n“버트럼 부인, 내 목적은 내 뒤에 올 사람들에게 보탬이 되는 거예요. 내가 더 넉넉해지고 싶은 건 다 부인네 아이들 잘되라고 그러는 거지요. 나야 달리 마음 쓸 사람도 없으니, 그 애들한테 가질 만한 작은 것이라도 남겨 줄 수 있다고 생각하면 무척 기쁠 거예요.”\n\n“참 고마운 말씀이에요, 언니. 그렇지만 그 애들 일로 애쓰지 마세요. 그 애들이야 넉넉히 앞가림할 게 틀림없으니까요. 토머스 경이 다 챙겨 주실 거예요.”\n\n“아니, 알잖아요, 앤티가 영지가 그렇게 형편없는 수익만 낸다면 토머스 경의 살림도 꽤 빠듯해질 거예요.”\n\n“어머, 그거야 곧 해결될 거예요. 토머스 경이 그 일로 편지를 써 오셨다니까요, 내가 알아요.”\n\n“그럼, 버트럼 부인,” 자리를 뜨려 몸을 일으키며 노리스 부인이 말했다. “나는 그저 이 말만 하겠어요. 내 유일한 바람은 부인네 식구에게 보탬이 되는 것뿐이라고요. 그러니 혹시라도 토머스 경이 다시 나더러 패니를 맡으라는 말을 꺼내시거든, 내 건강과 기력이 도무지 그럴 처지가 못 된다고 부인이 말해 주면 돼요. 게다가 나는 정말이지 그 애한테 내줄 잠자리조차 없어요. 벗을 위한 남는 방은 꼭 비워 둬야 하니까요.”\n\n버트럼 부인은 이 대화를 남편에게 얼추 옮겨 들려주어, 그가 처형의 뜻을 얼마나 잘못 짚었는지 깨닫게 해 주었다. 그 순간부터 노리스 부인은 그런 기대에서도, 그가 그 일을 넌지시라도 비치는 데서도 완전히 안전해졌다. 그는 그토록 앞장서서 거두겠다던 조카딸을 위해 아무것도 하지 않겠다는 처형이 의아하지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 그녀가 일찌감치 그와 버트럼 부인 모두에게 제가 가진 것은 무엇이든 그들 식구를 위한 것이라고 못 박아 둔 터라, 그는 이내 그 처사에 마음을 붙였다. 그것은 그들에게 이롭고 낯이 서는 일인 동시에, 그가 손수 패니를 더 잘 건사할 수 있게 해 주는 일이었으니 말이다.\n\n패니는 이내 옮겨 간다는 제 두려움이 얼마나 부질없었는지 알게 되었다. 그것을 알았을 때 저도 모르게 절로 우러난 그 기쁨은, 아이에게 그토록 요긴하리라 여겼던 일에서 실망한 에드먼드에게도 얼마간 위로가 되었다. 노리스 부인은 화이트하우스를 차지했고, 그랜트 부부는 목사관에 당도했다. 이 일들이 끝나자 맨스필드의 모든 것은 한동안 여느 때처럼 흘러갔다.\n\n그랜트 부부는 다정하고 붙임성 있는 성미를 보여, 대체로 새 이웃들 사이에서 큰 호감을 샀다. 그들에게도 흠은 있었고, 노리스 부인은 이내 그것을 캐냈다. 박사는 먹는 것을 무척 좋아해 날마다 잘 차린 저녁상을 받으려 했고, 그랜트 부인은 적은 돈으로 그를 만족시킬 궁리를 하기는커녕 맨스필드 파크만큼이나 높은 삯을 요리사에게 주면서 정작 제 부엌일에는 좀처럼 모습을 드러내지 않았다. 노리스 부인은 그런 못마땅한 일들을, 또 그 집에서 으레 축나는 버터와 달걀의 양을 두고 도무지 곱게 말할 수 없었다. “풍성함과 후한 대접을 저만큼 사랑하는 사람도 없고, 인색한 짓을 저만큼 미워하는 사람도 없다. 목사관은 제가 있던 시절에는 무슨 안락이든 모자란 적이 없었고 나쁜 소리 한 번 들은 적 없건만, 이런 살림살이는 도무지 이해할 수가 없다. 시골 목사관에 우아한 마님이라니 영 격에 안 맞는다. 제 저장실이면 그랜트 부인이 드나들기에 넉넉했을 것이다. 아무리 캐물어도 그랜트 부인이 오천 파운드 넘게 가져왔다는 소리는 끝내 알아내지 못했다.”\n\n버트럼 부인은 이런 종류의 험담을 별 흥미 없이 들었다. 알뜰한 사람이 겪는 억울함에는 도무지 공감하지 못했으나, 그랜트 부인이 미인도 아니면서 그토록 팔자가 편 것을 보고는 미모가 입은 온갖 손해만은 절절히 느꼈고, 노리스 부인이 다른 쪽을 늘어놓는 만큼 장황하지는 않았어도 그에 못지않게 자주 그 점에 놀라움을 표했다.\n\n이런 의견들을 주고받은 지 채 한 해도 지나기 전에, 이 집안에는 또 다른 사건이 하나 일어났다. 부인들의 생각과 이야깃거리에서 얼마쯤 자리를 차지할 만한 중대한 일이었다. 토머스 경은 제 사업을 더 잘 정리하기 위해 몸소 앤티가로 가는 것이 상책이라 여겼고, 고향에서 얽힌 몇몇 나쁜 교제에서 맏아들을 떼어 놓으려는 바람에 그를 데리고 갔다. 두 사람은 거의 한 해 가까이 자리를 비우게 될 것을 각오하고 잉글랜드를 떠났다.\n\n금전으로 보아 그 조치가 불가피하다는 것과 아들에게 도움이 되리라는 기대가, 토머스 경으로 하여금 남은 식구를 뒤로하고 딸들을 인생에서 가장 미묘한 시기에 남의 손에 맡겨 두는 그 힘든 결단을 받아들이게 했다. 그는 버트럼 부인이 딸들에게 제 자리를 대신하기에는, 아니 마땅히 제 몫이었을 일을 해내기에는 도무지 미덥지 않다고 여겼다. 그러나 노리스 부인의 빈틈없는 보살핌과 에드먼드의 분별력에는 딸들의 처신을 걱정하지 않고 떠날 만큼 충분히 믿음을 두었다.\n\n버트럼 부인은 남편이 저를 두고 떠나는 것이 도무지 마뜩잖았다. 그러나 그의 안위를 걱정하거나 그의 안락을 노심초사하지는 않았으니, 그녀는 이 세상에 오로지 저 자신 말고는 누구에게도 위험하거나 어렵거나 고될 일이 없다고 여기는 그런 부류의 사람이었기 때문이다.\n\n버트럼 자매는 이 일에서 자못 가엾이 여길 만했다. 슬퍼해서가 아니라 슬퍼할 줄 몰라서였다. 아버지는 그들에게 사랑의 대상이 아니었다. 한 번도 그들의 즐거움을 함께해 주는 벗으로 보인 적이 없었고, 그 부재는 딱하게도 더없이 반가운 일이었다. 그 덕에 그들은 온갖 구속에서 풀려났다. 토머스 경이라면 십중팔구 금했을 만한 즐거움을 굳이 하나도 노리지 않고서도, 그들은 이제 저희 마음대로 할 수 있고 온갖 방종이 손닿는 데 있다고 느꼈다. 패니가 느낀 해방감과 그것을 스스로 의식한 정도도 사촌들 못지않았다. 그러나 더 여린 천성이 제 감정이 배은망덕하다고 일러 주었고, 아이는 슬퍼할 수 없다는 바로 그 이유로 정말이지 슬퍼했다. “저와 제 오빠들을 위해 그토록 많은 것을 해 주셨고, 어쩌면 영영 돌아오지 못할지도 모르는 채 떠나신 토머스 경! 그런 분이 떠나시는데 눈물 한 방울 흘리지 못하다니! 이건 부끄러운 무정함이다.” 게다가 그는 바로 그 마지막 아침에, 다가오는 겨울 동안 아이가 윌리엄을 다시 만날 수 있기를 바란다고 말했고, 그가 속한 함대가 잉글랜드에 들어온 것이 알려지는 대로 편지를 써서 그를 맨스필드로 초대하라 일러 두었다. “이 얼마나 사려 깊고 다정한 일인가!” 그리고 그 말을 하면서 그가 아이에게 미소만 지어 주고 “나의 사랑하는 패니”라고만 불러 주었더라도, 예전의 온갖 찌푸림이나 쌀쌀한 말은 다 잊혔을 것이다. 그러나 그는 이렇게 덧붙임으로써 아이를 서글픈 낙심에 빠뜨리며 말을 맺었다. “윌리엄이 맨스필드에 오거든, 너희가 헤어진 뒤로 흘러간 그 여러 해가 네 쪽에서도 아주 발전 없이 흘러가지만은 않았음을 그 애가 믿게 해 줄 수 있으면 좋겠구나. 하기야 걱정이다만, 그 애는 열여섯 된 제 누이가 몇몇 점에서 열 살 적 누이와 너무 닮았다고 여길 게다.” 삼촌이 떠난 뒤 아이는 이 말을 곱씹으며 쓰라리게 울었다. 그리고 사촌들은 아이의 빨개진 눈을 보고는 아이를 위선자로 치부했다.\n\n# 제4장\n\n톰 버트럼은 요즘 들어 집에서 보내는 시간이 하도 적어, 아쉬워한대야 이름뿐이었다. 버트럼 부인은 이내, 아버지 없이도 식구가 얼마나 잘 지내는지, 에드먼드가 고기를 썰고, 집사와 이야기하고, 변호사에게 편지를 쓰고, 하인들과 셈을 치르는 그 자리를 얼마나 잘 대신하는지, 그리고 편지 겉봉에 주소 적는 일만 빼면 온갖 일에서 저를 얼마나 고스란히 수고와 노고에서 벗어나게 해 주는지 알고 놀랐다.\n\n순조로운 항해 끝에 여행자들이 앤티가에 무사히 닿았다는 첫 소식이 당도했다. 다만 그 전에 노리스 부인이 온갖 무시무시한 두려움에 빠져, 에드먼드를 붙들 수 있을 때마다 그것을 함께 나누게 하려 애쓴 뒤의 일이었다. 그녀는 무슨 참변이 있으면 제가 맨 먼저 알게 되리라 믿고, 그것을 다른 이들에게 어떻게 알릴지 그 방식까지 벌써 짜 두었더랬다. 그러던 차에 두 사람 다 살아서 잘 있다는 토머스 경의 확답이 오는 바람에, 그 소동과 정에 겨운 예비 연설은 한동안 접어 두어야 했다.\n\n겨울이 오고 갔으나 그들이 불려 나갈 일은 없었다. 소식은 여전히 더없이 좋았다. 노리스 부인은 조카딸들을 위해 즐길 거리를 마련하고, 몸단장을 거들고, 재주를 뽐내게 하고, 앞으로 맞을 사윗감을 물색하는 데 하도 바빴다. 게다가 제 살림 걱정에, 동생네 살림에 얼마쯤 참견하는 일에, 그랜트 부인의 헤픈 살림살이를 눈여겨보는 일까지 더해지니, 멀리 떠난 이들을 두고 두려움에 잠길 겨를이 거의 없었다.\n\n버트럼 자매는 이제 이웃의 이름난 미인들 사이에 확고히 자리 잡았다. 아름다움과 빛나는 재주에 더해, 타고나기를 느긋하고 두루 예의 바르고 상냥하도록 정성껏 다듬어진 몸가짐까지 갖추었으니, 사람들의 감탄은 물론 호감까지 한 몸에 받았다. 그들의 허영심은 어찌나 잘 다스려져 있던지 도무지 허영이랄 것이 없어 보였고, 조금도 거드름을 피우지 않았다. 한편 그런 처신에 따르는 칭찬은—이모가 챙기고 끌어모아 준 것이었는데—그들이 제게 아무 흠도 없다고 믿는 마음을 한층 굳혀 주었다.\n\n버트럼 부인은 딸들과 함께 사교 자리에 나가지 않았다. 그녀는 딸들의 성공과 즐거움을 지켜보는 어미의 흐뭇함조차, 몸소 수고를 무릅쓰면서까지 누리기에는 너무 게을렀다. 그리하여 그 소임은 언니에게 넘겨졌는데, 언니로 말하면 그토록 명예로운 대리 역할보다 더 바랄 것이 없는 사람이었고, 삯말을 빌리지 않고도 사교계에 낄 수 있는 그 수단을 아주 흡족하게 누렸다.\n\n패니는 그 철의 흥겨운 자리에는 낄 몫이 없었다. 그러나 다른 식구들이 불려 나가고 나면 이모의 말벗으로서 떳떳이 쓸모 있는 것이 즐거웠다. 게다가 리 선생이 맨스필드를 떠난 뒤로는, 무도회나 파티가 있는 밤이면 자연히 버트럼 부인에게 없어서는 안 될 존재가 되었다. 아이는 부인에게 말을 건네고, 부인의 말을 들어 주고, 책을 읽어 주었다. 그런 저녁의 고요함은, 단둘이 마주 앉아 매정한 소리 하나 들려올 리 없는 그 완전한 안심은, 좀처럼 놀라움이나 무안함이 그칠 새 없던 마음에 이루 말할 수 없이 반가운 것이었다. 사촌들의 흥겨운 일에 대해서는, 특히 무도회 이야기며 에드먼드가 누구와 춤을 추었나 하는 이야기를 듣기 좋아했다. 그러나 제 처지를 너무 낮추어 보아, 저도 언젠가 그런 자리에 낄 수 있으리라고는 상상조차 못 했고, 그래서 조금도 제 일로 여기지 않고 그저 듣기만 했다. 대체로 그것은 아이에게 편안한 겨울이었다. 윌리엄을 잉글랜드로 데려다주지는 못했어도, 그가 오리라는 결코 스러지지 않는 희망만으로도 값진 것이 많았기 때문이다.\n\n이어진 봄은 아이에게서 소중한 벗인 늙은 회색 조랑말을 앗아 갔다. 한동안 아이는 그 상실을 정에서뿐 아니라 건강에서도 느낄 처지에 놓였다. 말을 타는 것이 아이에게 중요하다는 것은 다들 인정하면서도, 아이를 다시 말에 태울 조치는 아무도 하지 않았기 때문이다. “왜냐하면,” 이모들이 하는 말로는, “사촌들이 안 탈 때야 언제든 사촌들 말 한 마리를 타면 되니까”였다. 그러나 버트럼 자매는 날씨 좋은 날이면 으레 제 말을 타고 싶어 했고, 그 상냥한 몸가짐을 무슨 진짜 즐거움을 희생하는 데까지 밀고 갈 생각은 조금도 없었으니, 그럴 때란 물론 영영 오지 않았다. 그들은 사월과 오월의 화창한 아침이면 즐거이 말을 달렸다. 그리고 패니는 한 이모와 하루 종일 집에 앉아 있거나, 다른 이모의 부추김에 제 힘에 부치도록 걸어야 했다. 버트럼 부인은 운동이란 저에게 싫은 만큼 누구에게나 쓸데없다 여겼고, 하루 종일 걸어 다니는 노리스 부인은 누구나 저만큼 걸어야 마땅하다 여겼기 때문이다. 이 무렵 에드먼드는 집을 비우고 있었다. 아니었다면 그 폐단은 더 일찍 바로잡혔을 것이다. 그가 돌아와 패니의 처지를 알아채고 그 나쁜 영향을 눈치채자, 그가 보기엔 할 일이 딱 하나뿐이었다. “패니에게는 말이 있어야 한다”는 것이 그가 단호히 내세운 선언이었고, 그것을 대수롭잖게 보이려는 어머니의 게으름이나 이모의 절약이 무슨 말을 들이대든 그는 이 한마디로 맞섰다. 노리스 부인은 파크에 딸린 그 많은 말 가운데 아주 쓸 만한 얌전한 늙은 놈을 하나 찾을 수 있으리라는, 아니면 집사에게 한 마리 빌리면 되리라는, 아니면 어쩌면 그랜트 박사가 이따금 우편용으로 부리는 조랑말을 빌려줄지 모른다는 생각을 떨칠 수 없었다. 그녀는 패니가 사촌들처럼 제 몫의 어엿한 숙녀용 말을 갖는 것을 도무지 쓸데없는 일, 심지어 온당치 못한 일로 여겼다. 토머스 경도 결코 그럴 뜻은 없었으리라 확신했다. 그리고 한마디 하자면, 그가 없는 사이에 그런 것을 사들여, 그의 수입 상당 부분이 불안정한 이 판국에 마구간 비용을 더 늘리는 것은 도무지 정당화할 수 없다는 것이었다. “패니에게는 말이 있어야 합니다.” 이것이 에드먼드의 유일한 대꾸였다. 노리스 부인은 그것을 같은 눈으로 볼 수 없었다. 버트럼 부인은 그리 보았다. 그것이 꼭 필요하다는 데에도, 그것을 그 아버지도 꼭 필요하다 여기리라는 데에도 그녀는 아들과 온전히 뜻을 같이했다. 다만 서두를 것 없다는 것만 내세웠다. 토머스 경이 돌아올 때까지 기다렸다가 그때 토머스 경이 손수 다 정하게 하자는 것뿐이었다. 그이는 구월이면 집에 올 텐데, 구월까지 그저 기다린다고 무슨 해가 되겠느냐는 것이었다.\n\n에드먼드는 조카딸을 조금도 위하지 않는 태도로 보아 어머니보다 이모에게 훨씬 언짢았으나, 그래도 이모의 말에는 더 마음을 쓰지 않을 수 없었다. 그리하여 마침내, 아버지가 저더러 너무 나섰다고 여길 위험은 피하면서도 패니에게는 당장 운동할 방편을 마련해 줄—그것 없이 지내는 것을 그는 도저히 두고 볼 수 없었다—한 가지 방도를 정했다. 그에게는 제 말이 세 마리 있었으나 여자를 태울 만한 것은 하나도 없었다. 둘은 사냥말이었고, 셋째는 쓸모 있는 길말이었다. 그는 이 셋째를 사촌이 탈 만한 말과 바꾸기로 했다. 그런 말을 어디서 구할 수 있는지 그는 알고 있었고, 한번 마음을 정하고 나자 그 일은 이내 끝났다. 새로 온 암말은 보배로 드러났다. 아주 조금 손을 들이자 그 용도에 딱 맞게 되었고, 그리하여 패니는 거의 온전히 그 말을 차지하게 되었다. 아이는 전에는 그 무엇도 늙은 회색 조랑말만큼 제게 맞으리라고는 생각지 못했었다. 그러나 에드먼드의 암말을 향한 기쁨은 그런 종류의 지난 어떤 즐거움도 아득히 넘어섰다. 게다가 그 즐거움이 샘솟은 근원인 그 친절을 헤아리는 마음이 끊임없이 거기에 더해지니, 그것은 아이가 무슨 말로도 다 옮길 수 없는 것이었다. 아이는 사촌을 온갖 선하고 위대한 것의 본보기로, 저 말고는 아무도 알아보지 못할 값진 것을 지닌 사람으로, 그리고 어떤 감정으로도 다 갚기에 부족할 만큼 마땅히 제 감사를 받아야 할 사람으로 우러렀다. 그를 향한 아이의 마음은 존경과 감사와 믿음과 애틋함이 온통 어우러진 것이었다.\n\n그 말이 이름으로나 실제로나 여전히 에드먼드의 것이었으므로, 노리스 부인은 그것을 패니가 쓰는 것쯤은 참아 줄 수 있었다. 그리고 버트럼 부인이 제 이의를 다시 떠올리기라도 했다면, 에드먼드가 구월에 토머스 경이 돌아올 때까지 기다리지 않은 것도 그녀 눈에는 용서받았을 것이다. 정작 구월이 오자 토머스 경은 여전히 외국에 있었고, 일을 마무리 지을 가망도 좀처럼 보이지 않았으니 말이다. 그가 온통 잉글랜드로 마음을 돌리기 시작하던 바로 그때 불리한 사정들이 별안간 생겨났다. 그리고 그때 모든 것이 휩싸인 그 엄청난 불확실함 때문에, 그는 아들만 먼저 집으로 보내고 최종 정리는 저 혼자 하기로 마음먹었다. 톰은 무사히 도착해 아버지의 건강에 대한 아주 좋은 소식을 전해 주었으나, 노리스 부인으로 말하면 그것은 별 소용이 없었다. 토머스 경이 아들을 먼저 떠나보낸 것이 그녀에게는 마치 제 몸에 닥칠 불행을 예감한 부모의 마음처럼 보였던지라, 그녀는 무시무시한 예감을 떨칠 수 없었다. 그리고 가을의 긴 저녁이 다가오자, 오두막의 그 서글픈 외로움 속에서 이런 생각에 하도 끔찍하게 시달린 나머지, 날마다 파크의 식당으로 피신하지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 겨울철 모임들이 돌아온 것은 나름의 효험이 있었다. 그것이 이어지는 동안 그녀의 마음은 맏조카딸의 앞날을 주선하는 데 하도 즐겁게 쏠려, 신경이 그런대로 가라앉았다. “딱한 토머스 경이 영영 돌아오지 못할 운명이라면, 사랑하는 마리아가 좋은 데로 시집가는 것을 보는 게 유난히 위안이 되겠지”라고 그녀는 곧잘 생각했다. 늘 재산 많은 남자들과 자리를 함께할 때면, 특히 최근에 이 고장에서 가장 큰 영지와 가장 훌륭한 저택 하나를 물려받은 어느 젊은이가 소개되었을 때면.\n\n러시워스 씨는 처음부터 버트럼 양의 아름다움에 마음을 빼앗겼고, 혼인할 생각이 있던 터라 이내 스스로 사랑에 빠졌다 여겼다. 그는 그저 그런 분별밖에 없는 둔한 젊은이였다. 그러나 생김새나 말씨에 딱히 거슬리는 데는 없었으므로, 그 젊은 아가씨는 제 정복이 흡족했다. 스물한 살에 접어든 마리아 버트럼은 혼인을 하나의 의무로 여기기 시작하던 참이었다. 게다가 러시워스 씨와의 혼인은 아버지보다 더 큰 수입을 누리게 해 줄뿐더러 이제 가장 탐나는 것이 된 도시의 집까지 마련해 줄 터였으니, 바로 그 도덕적 의무의 논리대로, 될 수만 있다면 러시워스 씨와 혼인하는 것이 그녀의 명백한 도리가 되었다. 노리스 부인은 그 혼사를 성사시키는 데 더없이 열을 올려, 양쪽 모두에게 그것을 더 탐나게 할 만한 온갖 귀띔과 궁리를 다 짜냈다. 그중 한 방편으로, 지금 아들과 함께 사는 그 신사의 어머니와 가까워지려 애썼는데, 그 부인을 아침에 방문하려고 버트럼 부인더러 십 마일이나 되는 험한 길을 가게 억지로 몰아붙이기까지 했다. 오래지 않아 이 부인과 그녀 사이에는 좋은 뜻이 통했다. 러시워스 부인은 아들이 혼인하기를 무척 바란다고 밝혔고, 여태 본 온갖 젊은 아가씨 가운데 버트럼 양이야말로 그 상냥한 자질과 재주로 보아 아들을 행복하게 해 주기에 가장 알맞아 보인다고 단언했다. 노리스 부인은 그 칭찬을 받아들이며, 그토록 사람의 됨됨이를 잘 가려내는 그 안목에 감탄했다. 정말이지 마리아는 그들 모두의 자랑이요 기쁨이었다—흠 하나 없는—천사였다. 그러니 당연히 그토록 숱한 사모하는 이들에 둘러싸여 고르기가 어려울 수밖에. 그러나 그래도 노리스 부인이 그토록 짧은 사귐으로 감히 단정할 수 있는 한, 러시워스 씨야말로 그녀를 얻고 그녀의 마음을 붙들 자격이 있는 바로 그 젊은이로 보였다.\n\n몇 차례 무도회에서 알맞은 만큼 함께 춤을 추고 나자, 그 젊은 남녀는 이런 평판이 옳았음을 증명했다. 그리하여 자리를 비운 토머스 경에게 마땅히 여쭙는다는 조건으로 약혼이 맺어졌고, 이는 각자의 집안은 물론, 지난 여러 주 동안 러시워스 씨가 버트럼 양과 혼인하는 것이 마땅하다 여겨 온 이웃의 뭇 구경꾼들까지 크게 만족시켰다.\n\n토머스 경의 승낙이 오기까지는 몇 달이 걸렸다. 그러나 그사이, 그가 이 인연을 더없이 진심으로 기뻐하리라는 것을 아무도 의심하지 않았으므로, 두 집안의 왕래는 거리낌 없이 이어졌다. 비밀이랄 것도, 노리스 부인이 그것을 지금은 발설해서는 안 될 일이라며 가는 곳마다 떠벌리는 것 말고는 달리 없었다.\n\n이 집안에서 이 일에 흠을 볼 줄 아는 이는 에드먼드뿐이었다. 그러나 이모가 아무리 그럴싸하게 늘어놓아도 그는 러시워스 씨를 반길 만한 벗으로 여길 수 없었다. 누이가 제 행복을 가장 잘 판단할 사람임은 인정할 수 있었으나, 그 행복이 큰 수입에 걸려 있다는 것이 그는 마뜩잖았다. 그래서 러시워스 씨와 함께 있을 때면 “이 사내에게 연 만 이천 파운드가 없었다면, 아주 멍청한 작자에 지나지 않았을 것을” 하고 속으로 곧잘 되뇌지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n그러나 토머스 경은 그토록 두말없이 이로운 혼사의 전망에 참으로 흐뭇했고, 그것에 대해 더없이 좋고 흡족한 소리밖에 듣지 못했다. 그것은 꼭 알맞은 종류의 인연이었다—같은 주, 같은 이해관계였으니 말이다. 그리하여 그의 더없이 진심 어린 동의가 되도록 빨리 전해졌다. 다만 그는 혼례를 제가 돌아오기 전에는 치르지 말라는 조건만 달았는데, 그 귀국을 그는 또다시 애타게 고대하고 있었다. 그는 사월에 편지를 썼고, 모든 일을 제 뜻대로 매듭지어 여름이 가기 전에 앤티가를 떠날 수 있으리라는 큰 희망을 품었다.\n\n칠월의 형편은 이러했다. 그리고 패니가 막 열여덟이 되었을 무렵, 그랜트 부인의 남동생과 여동생인 크로퍼드 남매가 마을 사교계에 새로 더해졌다. 그들은 그랜트 부인의 어머니가 재혼해 낳은 자식들이었다. 그들은 재산 있는 젊은이들이었다. 아들은 노퍽에 좋은 영지가 있었고, 딸은 이만 파운드를 지녔다. 어릴 적 누이는 늘 그들을 무척 귀여워했다. 그러나 제 혼인 뒤 얼마 안 되어 두 사람의 공통된 어버이가 세상을 떠나면서, 그들은 그랜트 부인이 전혀 모르는 그 아버지 쪽 형제의 손에 맡겨졌고, 그리하여 그 뒤로는 그들을 거의 보지 못했다. 삼촌의 집에서 그들은 다정한 보금자리를 얻었다. 크로퍼드 제독과 그 부인은 다른 무엇에서도 뜻이 맞지 않았으나 이 아이들을 향한 애정에서만은 하나였다. 아니, 적어도 각자 더 아끼는 아이가 따로 있어 그 아이에게 둘 중 더한 정을 쏟았다는 것 말고는, 그 마음이 어긋나지 않았다. 제독은 사내아이를 귀여워했고, 크로퍼드 부인은 계집아이를 애지중지했다. 그리고 이제 그 부인이 세상을 떠나는 바람에, 몇 달 더 삼촌 집에서 지내 본 끝에 그녀가 보살피던 그 아가씨는 다른 보금자리를 찾지 않을 수 없게 되었다. 크로퍼드 제독은 행실이 못된 사람이라, 조카딸을 곁에 두는 대신 제 정부를 제집 지붕 밑에 들이는 쪽을 택했다. 그리고 그랜트 부인이 동생에게서 저를 찾아오겠다는 뜻을 받게 된 것은 다 그 덕이었으니, 이 일은 한쪽에는 요긴한 만큼 다른 쪽에는 더없이 반가운 것이었다. 그도 그럴 것이, 그랜트 부인은 이 무렵 자식 없이 시골에 사는 부인네가 으레 기대는 소일거리를 다 써 버린 터라—좋아하는 거실을 예쁜 가구로 넘치도록 채우고, 화초와 가금을 엄선해 갖추어 놓은 터라—집에서 무언가 색다른 것이 몹시 아쉽던 참이었다. 그러니 늘 사랑해 온 데다 이제 미혼으로 있는 한 곁에 붙들어 두려는 여동생이 오는 것은 더없이 반가운 일이었다. 그녀의 가장 큰 걱정은, 대개 런던에 익숙한 젊은 여자에게 맨스필드가 성에 차지 않으면 어쩌나 하는 것뿐이었다.\n\n크로퍼드 양도 비슷한 걱정에서 아주 자유롭지는 않았으나, 그것은 주로 언니의 사는 방식과 사교의 격에 대한 미심쩍음에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그리고 오빠에게 그의 시골집에 저와 함께 자리 잡자고 설득하려다 헛물만 켠 뒤에야, 그녀는 다른 친척들 틈에 저를 내맡겨 볼 마음을 먹을 수 있었다. 한군데 붙박이로 지내거나 사교를 좁히는 따위의 일을 헨리 크로퍼드는 딱하게도 몹시 싫어했다. 그토록 중대한 일에서 그는 누이를 맞춰 줄 수 없었다. 그러나 더없이 다정하게 그녀를 노샘프턴셔까지 바래다주었고, 그녀가 그곳에 싫증이 나거든 언제든 반 시간만 알려 주면 다시 데리러 오마고 선선히 약속했다.\n\n만남은 양쪽 모두에게 매우 흡족했다. 크로퍼드 양은 까다롭지도 촌스럽지도 않은 언니와, 신사다워 보이는 형부와, 넓고 잘 꾸며진 집을 발견했다. 그리고 그랜트 부인은 그 어느 때보다 더 사랑하고 싶던 이들에게서 아주 호감 가는 용모의 젊은 남녀를 맞이했다. 메리 크로퍼드는 눈에 띄게 예뻤다. 헨리는 잘생기지는 않았어도 기품과 인상이 있었다. 두 사람의 몸가짐은 다 발랄하고 유쾌했으니, 그랜트 부인은 이내 그 밖의 모든 것도 다 좋으리라 믿어 주었다. 그녀는 둘 다 마음에 들었으나, 메리가 가장 아끼는 대상이었다. 제 미모를 자랑해 본 적 없는 그녀는 동생의 미모를 자랑스러워할 수 있다는 그 힘을 마음껏 누렸다. 동생이 오기를 기다렸다가 알맞은 혼처를 알아본 것도 아니었다. 그녀는 이미 톰 버트럼을 점찍어 두었다. 그랜트 부인이 동생에게서 내다본 그 온갖 우아함과 재주까지 갖춘 이만 파운드짜리 아가씨에게, 준남작의 맏아들쯤이야 과분할 것이 없었다. 그리고 마음 따뜻하고 스스럼없는 여자인지라, 그랜트 부인은 동생이 집에 온 지 세 시간도 채 안 되어 제가 세운 계획을 동생에게 털어놓았다.\n\n크로퍼드 양은 그토록 지체 있는 집안이 그리 가까이 있는 것이 반가웠고, 언니가 진작 마음 써 준 것도, 그 마음이 향한 상대도 조금도 못마땅하지 않았다. 잘만 시집갈 수 있다면 혼인이야말로 그녀의 목표였다. 그리고 도시에서 버트럼 씨를 본 적이 있는지라, 그 사람됨에는 그 처지에 못지않게 트집 잡을 데가 없음을 알고 있었다. 그러니 그녀는 그것을 농담으로 다루면서도 진지하게 생각해 보기를 잊지 않았다. 그 계획은 이내 헨리에게 되풀이해 전해졌다.\n\n“그리고 이제,” 그랜트 부인이 덧붙였다. “이걸 완벽하게 만들 궁리를 하나 해 뒀단다. 나는 너희 둘 다 이 고장에 자리 잡게 하고 싶어 죽겠거든. 그러니 헨리, 너는 버트럼가의 막내딸과 혼인하렴. 참하고 예쁘고 성품 밝고 재주 있는 아가씨라, 너를 아주 행복하게 해 줄 거야.”\n\n헨리는 고개를 숙여 고맙다고 했다.\n\n“언니,” 메리가 말했다. “만약 언니가 저 사람을 그런 일에 설득해 낼 수만 있다면, 나는 그렇게 영리한 분과 인척이 되는 게 새삼 기쁠 거예요. 다만 언니한테 시집보낼 딸이 대여섯이나 되지 않는 게 아쉬울 뿐이지요. 헨리를 혼인하도록 설득할 수 있다면, 언니는 프랑스 여자 뺨치는 수완이 있는 게 틀림없어요. 잉글랜드 여자의 온갖 재주로 할 수 있는 건 이미 다 해 봤거든요. 저한테는 아주 각별한 친구가 셋 있는데, 하나씩 차례로 저이한테 죽고 못 살았어요. 그 애들이며, 그 애들 어머니들(아주 영리한 부인들이지요)이며, 저희 이모와 저까지 나서서, 저이를 타이르고 어르고 꾀어 혼인시키려고 들인 공은 이루 말할 수도 없어요! 저이는 상상할 수 있는 가장 지독한 바람둥이예요. 언니네 버트럼 자매가 가슴 찢기기 싫거든, 헨리를 멀리하라고 하세요.”\n\n“얘, 나는 너에 대해 그런 말은 안 믿는다.”\n\n“그럼요, 누님은 워낙 착하시니까요. 누님은 메리보다 너그러우실 거예요. 젊음과 미숙함이 품는 망설임쯤은 봐주실 테니까요. 저는 조심스러운 성미라, 서둘러 제 행복을 걸고 싶지 않을 뿐이에요. 혼인이라는 것을 저보다 더 높이 사는 사람도 없어요. 저는 아내라는 축복이 시인의 그 신중한 구절에 더없이 옳게 그려져 있다고 여겨요—‘하늘이 마지막으로 준 가장 좋은 선물’이라고요.”\n\n“보세요, 언니, 저이가 한 단어에 어찌나 힘을 주는지, 저 미소 좀 보시라니까요. 정말이지 저이는 얄미워 죽겠어요. 제독의 가르침이 저이를 아주 버려 놓았다니까요.”\n\n“나는 별로 개의치 않는단다,” 그랜트 부인이 말했다. “젊은 사람이 혼인을 두고 무슨 말을 하든 말이야. 싫다고 큰소리치면, 나는 그저 아직 제짝을 못 만난 게로구나 여길 뿐이지.”\n\n그랜트 박사는 웃으며, 크로퍼드 양은 정작 혼인이라는 것에 아무 거부감이 없어 다행이라고 짓궂게 축하했다.\n\n“어머, 그럼요! 저는 조금도 부끄럽지 않아요. 저는 누구든 제대로만 할 수 있다면 다 혼인해야 한다고 봐요. 사람이 저를 함부로 내던지는 건 싫어요. 하지만 누구든 이롭게 할 수 있는 한 되도록 빨리 혼인해야지요.”\n\n# 제5장\n\n젊은이들은 처음부터 서로 마음에 들어 했다. 양쪽 다 서로를 끌 만한 것이 많았고, 그들의 사귐은 이내 예의가 허락하는 한 그만큼 이른 친밀로 나아갈 조짐을 보였다. 크로퍼드 양의 미모는 버트럼 자매에게 조금도 해가 되지 않았다. 그들 자신이 워낙 아름다워, 다른 여자가 아름답다고 미워할 사람들이 아니었고, 그 발랄한 검은 눈과 맑은 갈색 살결과 전반적인 예쁨에 오라비들 못지않게 반했다. 만약 그녀가 키가 크고 몸이 풍만하고 살결이 희었더라면 좀 더 견디기 어려웠을지 모른다. 그러나 실상은 견줄 것이 못 되었다. 그녀는 나무랄 데 없이 사랑스럽고 예쁜 아가씨였고, 그들은 이 고장에서 가장 훤칠한 젊은 여자들이었으니 말이다.\n\n그녀의 오빠는 잘생기지 않았다. 아니, 처음 보았을 때 그는 영락없이 못생긴, 거무튀튀하고 못생긴 사람이었다. 그래도 그는 신사였고 말씨가 상냥했다. 두 번째 만남에서는 그가 그리 못생기지는 않았음이 드러났다. 물론 못생기기는 했다. 그러나 인상이 워낙 좋고 치아가 워낙 고르고 몸매가 워낙 잘 잡혀 있어, 이내 그가 못생겼다는 것을 잊게 되었다. 그리고 세 번째로 만나, 목사관에서 함께 저녁을 든 뒤로는, 아무도 그를 더 이상 그리 부르도록 허락하지 않았다. 실로 그는 자매가 여태 알던 가장 호감 가는 젊은이였고, 그들은 그에게 똑같이 반했다. 버트럼 양이 약혼한 몸이었으므로 형평상 그는 줄리아의 몫이 되었고, 줄리아는 그것을 잘 알고 있었다. 그리하여 그가 맨스필드에 온 지 한 주도 되기 전에, 그녀는 얼마든지 사랑에 빠질 준비가 되어 있었다.\n\n이 일에 대한 마리아의 생각은 더 흐릿하고 어정쩡했다. 그녀는 보려고도 알려고도 하지 않았다. “호감 가는 남자를 좋아한다고 해가 될 것은 없다—누구나 제 처지를 아니까—크로퍼드 씨가 알아서 몸을 사려야지.” 크로퍼드 씨는 조금도 위험에 빠질 생각이 없었다! 버트럼 자매는 마음을 사 둘 만했고, 또 기꺼이 마음을 내줄 태세였다. 그리고 그는 그저 그들이 저를 좋아하게 만드는 것 말고는 다른 속셈 없이 시작했다. 그들이 사랑에 겨워 애태우기를 바란 것은 아니었다. 그러나 마땅히 그를 더 옳게 판단하고 느끼게 했어야 할 분별과 성정을 지녔으면서도, 그는 그런 점에서 스스로에게 큰 여유를 허락했다.\n\n“누님네 버트럼 자매가 저는 무척 마음에 들어요, 누님.” 앞서 말한 그 저녁 방문 뒤, 그들을 마차까지 배웅하고 돌아오며 그가 말했다. “아주 우아하고 호감 가는 아가씨들이에요.”\n\n“정말 그렇지. 네가 그렇게 말해 주니 나도 기쁘구나. 그런데 너는 줄리아가 제일 좋지.”\n\n“아, 그럼요! 저는 줄리아가 제일 좋아요.”\n\n“그런데 정말 그러니? 버트럼 양이 대개 더 예쁘다고들 하던데.”\n\n“저도 그러리라 짐작해요. 이목구비 하나하나가 다 낫고, 저도 그 얼굴이 더 좋아요. 그래도 저는 줄리아가 제일 좋아요. 버트럼 양이 분명 더 예쁘고, 제가 만나 보니 더 호감도 가지만, 그래도 저는 언제나 줄리아가 제일 좋을 거예요. 누님이 그러라고 명하시니까요.”\n\n“너하고는 말을 안 하겠다, 헨리. 하지만 나는 안다, 너는 끝내 그 애를 제일 좋아하게 될 거야.”\n\n“제일 처음부터 제가 그 애를 제일 좋아한다고 말씀드리잖아요?”\n\n“게다가 버트럼 양은 약혼한 몸이야. 그걸 잊지 마라, 헨리. 그 애는 이미 짝을 정했어.”\n\n“네, 그래서 그 애가 더 좋은걸요. 약혼한 여자는 언제나 약혼 안 한 여자보다 더 호감이 가거든요. 스스로에게 흡족해하니까요. 근심은 끝났고, 이제 의심받을 걱정 없이 사람을 끌 온갖 재간을 마음껏 부려도 된다고 느끼거든요. 약혼한 아가씨라면 만사가 안전하지요. 아무 탈도 날 게 없어요.”\n\n“하기야 그 점으로 말하면, 러시워스 씨도 아주 착한 젊은이고, 그 애한테는 대단한 혼처지.”\n\n“하지만 버트럼 양은 그 사람을 지푸라기만큼도 마음에 두지 않아요. 그게 바로 누님이 그 절친한 친구를 두고 내리는 평이지요. 저는 거기 동의하지 않아요. 저는 버트럼 양이 러시워스 씨를 무척 사모한다고 확신해요. 그 사람 이야기가 나왔을 때 그 애 눈에서 그걸 봤거든요. 저는 버트럼 양을 워낙 좋게 여겨서, 그 애가 마음 없이 손을 내줄 사람이라고는 생각지 않아요.”\n\n“메리, 우리 저 애를 어떻게 다루면 좋을까?”\n\n“그냥 저 사람 하는 대로 내버려 둬야 할 거예요. 말해 봤자 소용없어요. 저 사람도 끝내 넘어가고 말걸요.”\n\n“하지만 나는 저 애가 속아 넘어가는 건 싫구나. 저 애가 속는 것도, 그런 건 원치 않아. 나는 다 정정당당하고 떳떳했으면 좋겠어.”\n\n“어머, 참! 그냥 저 사람도 운에 맡겨 두고 넘어가게 놔둬요. 그래도 매한가지예요. 사람은 누구나 언젠가는 다 속아 넘어가는걸요.”\n\n“혼인에서 늘 그런 건 아니란다, 메리.”\n\n“혼인에서는 유난히 그렇지요. 지금 이 자리에 마침 혼인하신 분들께는 죄송하지만, 그랜트 부인, 남자든 여자든 혼인할 때 속아 넘어가지 않는 사람은 백에 하나도 없어요. 어딜 둘러봐도 정말 그렇다는 게 보여요. 게다가 곰곰이 생각해 보면 그럴 수밖에 없다는 걸 느껴요. 세상 온갖 거래 가운데 사람들이 상대에게 가장 많은 걸 바라면서 정작 저 자신은 가장 정직하지 못한 게 바로 혼인이니까요. 이게 속임수가 아니고 뭐겠어요?”\n\n“아! 너는 힐 스트리트에서 혼인에 관한 나쁜 학교를 다닌 거로구나.”\n\n“딱한 이모야 분명 그 처지를 사랑할 이유가 별로 없었지요. 하지만 그야 어떻든, 제가 직접 겪어 본 바로 말하면, 혼인이란 술수를 부리는 일이에요. 저는 이 인연에서 어떤 특별한 이득이나, 상대의 어떤 재주나, 어떤 좋은 자질을 잔뜩 기대하고 믿고서 혼인했다가, 완전히 속았음을 알고 정반대의 것을 고스란히 감내해야 했던 사람을 아주 많이 알아요. 이게 속임수가 아니면 뭐예요?”\n\n“얘야, 여기엔 얼마간 상상이 섞였을 게다. 미안하다만, 나는 네 말을 다 믿을 수는 없구나. 정말이지 너는 절반밖에 못 보는 거야. 나쁜 것은 보면서 위안은 못 보는 거지. 어디에나 자잘한 마찰과 실망은 있기 마련이고, 우리는 다들 너무 많은 걸 바라기 십상이란다. 하지만 그러다 행복의 한 계획이 어그러지면, 사람의 본성은 또 다른 계획으로 돌아서지. 첫 번째 셈이 틀리면 두 번째로 더 나은 셈을 하고 말이야. 우리는 어디선가 위안을 찾아내는 법이란다. 그리고 얘, 사랑하는 메리야, 작은 것을 크게 부풀리는 그 심술궂은 구경꾼들이야말로 정작 당사자들보다 더 속고 홀린 사람들이지.”\n\n“잘하셨어요, 언니! 제 편을 감싸는 그 의리, 참으로 존경스러워요. 저도 아내가 되면 꼭 그만큼 든든히 버티려고요. 그리고 제 친구들도 다들 그래 주면 좋겠어요. 그러면 저도 가슴앓이를 많이 덜 텐데.”\n\n“너도 네 오라비 못지않게 고약하구나, 메리. 하지만 우리가 너희 둘 다 고쳐 줄게. 맨스필드가 너희 둘 다 고쳐 줄 거야, 아무도 속이는 일 없이 말이지. 우리와 함께 지내려무나, 우리가 너희를 고쳐 줄 테니.”\n\n크로퍼드 남매는 고쳐지기를 바라지는 않았어도 기꺼이 머물려 했다. 메리는 목사관을 당장의 거처로 삼는 데 만족했고, 헨리도 그에 못지않게 선선히 머무는 기간을 늘렸다. 그는 며칠만 함께 지낼 작정으로 왔었다. 그러나 맨스필드가 좋은 조짐을 보였고, 딱히 그를 다른 데로 부르는 일도 없었다. 두 사람을 곁에 붙들어 둘 수 있어 그랜트 부인은 기뻤고, 그랜트 박사도 그리되는 것이 더없이 흡족했다. 크로퍼드 양처럼 말 잘하고 예쁜 젊은 여자는 게으르고 집에만 붙어 있는 남자에게 언제나 즐거운 벗이었고, 크로퍼드 씨가 손님으로 있다는 것은 날마다 클라레를 마실 좋은 핑곗거리였으니 말이다.\n\n버트럼 자매가 크로퍼드 씨에게 품은 감탄은, 크로퍼드 양의 습성으로 미루어 그녀가 느낄 법한 그 무엇보다 훨씬 뜨거웠다. 그래도 그녀는 버트럼가 형제가 아주 훤칠한 젊은이들이라는 것, 그런 젊은이 둘이 나란히 있는 것은 런던에서도 흔히 볼 수 없다는 것, 그 몸가짐, 특히 맏이의 몸가짐이 아주 좋다는 것은 인정했다. 그는 런던에서 오래 지낸 데다 에드먼드보다 활기와 멋이 넘쳤으니, 마땅히 그가 더 나은 셈이었다. 게다가 실로 그가 맏이라는 것이 또 하나의 강력한 이유였다. 그녀는 진작부터 제가 맏이를 제일 좋아하게 되리라는 예감이 들었었다. 그게 제 방식임을 그녀는 알고 있었다.\n\n톰 버트럼은 어차피 누가 봐도 유쾌한 사람으로 여겨졌을 것이다. 그는 대체로 사람들이 좋아하는 부류의 젊은이였고, 그 호감은 더 고상한 부류의 어떤 재능보다 오히려 더 자주 호감을 사는 그런 종류였다. 몸가짐이 느긋하고, 기색이 좋고, 아는 사람이 많고, 할 말이 많았으니 말이다. 게다가 맨스필드 파크의 상속권과 준남작 작위도 이 모든 것에 해가 될 리 없었다. 크로퍼드 양은 이내 그와 그의 처지라면 괜찮겠다고 느꼈다. 그녀는 마땅한 헤아림으로 주위를 둘러보았고, 거의 모든 것이 그에게 유리함을 알았다. 둘레가 오 마일에 이르는 진짜 파크, 널찍하고 근래에 지은 저택—자리도 좋고 가림막도 좋아 이 왕국의 신사 저택 판화집이라면 어디에 실려도 손색없을 만한, 그저 온전히 새로 꾸미기만 하면 될 저택—상냥한 시누이들, 조용한 시어머니, 그리고 호감 가는 사내 그 자신까지. 게다가 당장은 아버지와의 약속에 매여 크게 노름을 못 한다는 이점, 앞으로 토머스 경이 되리라는 이점까지 있었다. 아주 괜찮을 성싶었다. 그를 받아들이게 될 것 같았다. 그리하여 그녀는 그가 B── 경마에 내보낼 말에 대해 조금씩 관심을 두기 시작했다.\n\n그 경마는 사귐이 시작된 지 얼마 안 되어 그를 불러 갈 참이었다. 그리고 그가 평소 하던 대로라면 식구들이 여러 주 동안은 그가 돌아오리라 기대하지 않는 눈치였으므로, 그것은 그의 열정을 일찌감치 시험대에 올릴 터였다. 그는 그녀에게 경마를 보러 오라고 부추기려 여러 말을 했고, 큰 무리를 지어 함께 가자는 궁리를, 온갖 들뜬 마음으로 세웠다. 그러나 그것은 그저 말로 그칠 수밖에 없었다.\n\n그런데 패니는, 이 모든 동안 무엇을 하고 무엇을 생각하고 있었을까? 그리고 그 새로 온 이들에 대한 그녀의 생각은 어떠했을까? 열여덟 살 아가씨 가운데 패니만큼 제 생각을 말할 일이 없는 이도 드물었다. 남의 눈에 거의 띄지 않는 조용한 방식으로, 그녀는 크로퍼드 양의 미모에 감탄의 뜻을 바쳤다. 그러나 두 사촌이 거듭 아니라고 증명해 보였건만 여전히 크로퍼드 씨를 아주 못생겼다고 여겼으므로, 그에 대해서는 한 번도 입에 올리지 않았다. 그녀가 정작 불러일으킨 주목이란 이런 것이었다. “이제 여러분을 다 알아 가겠는데, 프라이스 양만은 잘 모르겠네요.” 버트럼가 형제와 함께 걸으며 크로퍼드 양이 말했다. “저 아가씨는 사교계에 나온 건가요, 아닌가요? 헷갈리네요. 여러분과 함께 목사관에서 저녁을 들었으니 나온 것 같기도 한데, 워낙 말이 없어서 나왔다고는 도무지 보기 어렵거든요.”\n\n주로 이 말을 들은 에드먼드가 대답했다. “무슨 말씀인지는 알 것 같습니다만, 저로서는 그 물음에 답을 못 하겠군요. 제 사촌은 다 컸어요. 여자로서의 나이와 분별을 갖추었지요. 하지만 나왔느니 안 나왔느니 하는 건 제 소관 밖입니다.”\n\n“그렇지만 대개는 그보다 더 쉽게 알 수 있는 게 없는걸요. 그 구별이 워낙 뚜렷하잖아요. 대체로 겉모습만이 아니라 몸가짐까지 판이하게 다르니까요. 여태 저는 어떤 아가씨가 나왔는지 안 나왔는지를 두고 사람이 틀릴 수 있으리라고는 생각지 못했어요. 사교계에 안 나온 아가씨는 늘 같은 차림새예요. 이를테면 꼭 여미는 보닛을 쓰고요. 아주 얌전해 보이고 한마디도 안 하지요. 웃으셔도 좋지만, 정말 그렇답니다. 그리고 이따금 좀 지나칠 때가 있다는 것만 빼면, 그것도 다 지당한 일이에요. 아가씨란 조용하고 겸손해야 하니까요. 가장 못마땅한 대목은, 사교계에 나오면서 몸가짐이 달라지는 것이 흔히 너무 급작스럽다는 거예요. 더러는 아주 짧은 새에 삼가는 태도에서 정반대로—당당함으로! 넘어가 버리거든요. 바로 그게 지금 방식의 흠이지요. 열여덟, 열아홉 아가씨가 그렇게 대뜸 세상만사에 능란해진 걸 보면 마뜩잖거든요—그것도 어쩌면 한 해 전에는 말도 제대로 못 하는 걸 봤는데 말이에요. 버트럼 씨, 씨도 아마 그런 변화를 더러 겪어 보셨겠지요.”\n\n“겪어 본 것 같습니다만, 이건 좀 공평치 않은데요. 무슨 속셈인지 알겠어요. 저와 앤더슨 양을 놀리시는 거군요.”\n\n“아니에요, 정말로. 앤더슨 양이라니! 누구를, 무슨 말씀을 하시는지 저는 도무지 모르겠어요. 완전히 깜깜하다니까요. 하지만 무엇에 관해서인지 말씀만 해 주시면, 얼마든지 기꺼이 놀려 드릴게요.”\n\n“아! 시치미를 아주 잘 떼시는군요. 하지만 그렇게까지 속아 넘어갈 저는 아니에요. 달라진 아가씨를 그려 보이실 때 앤더슨 양을 염두에 두셨던 게 분명해요. 틀릴 수 없을 만큼 정확하게 그리셨거든요. 딱 그랬어요. 베이커 스트리트의 앤더슨가 말이에요. 며칠 전에 그 집 이야기를 했잖아요. 에드먼드, 너도 내가 찰스 앤더슨 얘기하는 걸 들었지. 사정은 이 아가씨가 그려 보인 것과 꼭 같았어. 앤더슨이 두 해쯤 전에 처음 나를 제 식구에게 소개했을 때, 그 누이는 아직 사교계에 안 나온 몸이라, 나는 그 애한테서 도무지 말 한마디를 끌어낼 수 없었어. 어느 날 아침 나는 앤더슨을 기다리며 한 시간이나 거기 앉아 있었는데, 방에는 그 애와 어린 계집아이 한둘뿐이었지. 가정교사는 병이 났거나 달아났고, 어머니는 볼일 편지를 들고 쉴 새 없이 들락거리는데, 나는 그 아가씨한테서 말 한마디, 눈길 한 번을 좀처럼 못 얻었어—예의 바른 대답 비슷한 것도 없이—입을 꾹 다물고 어찌나 쌀쌀맞게 나한테서 몸을 돌리던지! 그러고는 열두 달을 못 봤어. 그때는 그 애가 사교계에 나온 뒤였지. 홀퍼드 부인 댁에서 만났는데, 나는 그 애를 알아보지도 못했어. 그런데 그 애가 나한테 다가와서는 아는 사이라며 나를 뚫어져라 쳐다봐 무안하게 만들고, 어찌나 떠들고 웃어 대는지 나는 어디를 봐야 할지 몰랐다니까. 나는 그때 내가 온 방의 웃음거리가 된 게 틀림없다고 느꼈어. 그리고 크로퍼드 양이 그 이야기를 들은 게 분명하고요.”\n\n“아주 재미난 이야기네요. 게다가 아마 앤더슨 양의 체면에 좋을 것보다 훨씬 더 사실에 가깝겠지요. 그건 너무 흔한 흠이에요. 어머니들이 아직 딸을 다루는 제대로 된 방법을 도무지 못 익힌 게 분명해요. 어디에 잘못이 있는지는 저도 모르겠어요. 남을 바로잡겠다고 나설 생각은 없어요. 그래도 그들이 자주 틀린다는 건 정말 보이거든요.”\n\n“여자의 몸가짐이 마땅히 어떠해야 하는지 세상에 보여 주는 이들이야말로,” 버트럼 씨가 정중하게 말했다. “그것을 바로잡는 데 크게 이바지하는 것이지요.”\n\n“잘못은 뻔합니다,” 덜 정중한 에드먼드가 말했다. “그런 아가씨들은 잘못 길러진 거예요. 처음부터 그릇된 관념을 주입받은 겁니다. 늘 허영을 동기로 삼아 행동하니, 사교계에 나오기 전이라고 그 처신에 나온 뒤보다 더 참된 겸손이 있는 것도 아니지요.”\n\n“글쎄요,” 크로퍼드 양이 망설이며 대답했다. “아니요, 그 점에서는 동의할 수 없어요. 그거야말로 분명 그 일에서 가장 얌전한 대목인걸요. 사교계에 안 나온 아가씨가 나온 것처럼 똑같이 거드름을 피우고 똑같이 제멋대로 구는 게 훨씬 더 나빠요. 제가 그런 걸 본 적이 있거든요. 그거야말로 무엇보다 나쁘고—정말 역겨워요!”\n\n“그래요, 그건 정말 곤란하지요,” 버트럼 씨가 말했다. “사람을 헷갈리게 하니까요. 어찌해야 할지 모르게 되거든요. 아가씨가 그토록 잘 그려 보이신 그 꼭 여민 보닛과 얌전한 태도는 (그보다 더 옳은 말도 없었지만) 무엇이 요구되는지를 일러 주지요. 그런데 저는 지난해에 바로 그것이 없어서 지독한 곤경에 빠졌어요. 지난 구월, 서인도에서 막 돌아온 직후에 친구 하나와 한 주 예정으로 램스게이트에 내려갔거든요. 제 친구 스니드—에드먼드, 너도 내가 스니드 얘기하는 걸 들었지—그 친구 아버지와 어머니와 누이들이 거기 있었는데, 다 처음 보는 사람들이었어. 우리가 앨비언 플레이스에 닿았을 때 그들은 외출 중이었지. 우리는 뒤따라가서 부두에서 그들을 찾았어. 스니드 부인과 두 스니드 양, 그리고 그들의 다른 지인 몇이 있었어. 나는 격식대로 인사를 했지. 그런데 스니드 부인은 남자들에게 둘러싸여 있어서, 나는 그 딸 하나에게 붙어 집까지 내내 그 곁에서 걸으며 되도록 살갑게 굴었어. 그 아가씨는 몸가짐이 아주 편안하고, 듣는 만큼이나 말도 잘하더군. 내가 뭔가 잘못하고 있으리라고는 짐작조차 못 했지. 둘 다 똑같아 보였거든. 둘 다 잘 차려입고, 다른 아가씨들처럼 베일을 쓰고 양산을 들고 있었으니까. 그런데 나중에 알고 보니, 나는 온통 막내에게만 마음을 쏟았던 거야. 그 애는 아직 사교계에 안 나온 몸이었고, 그 바람에 맏이의 심기를 아주 몹시 거스르고 만 거지. 오거스타 양은 앞으로 여섯 달은 주목받아서는 안 될 몸이었거든. 그리고 스니드 양은, 아마 나를 끝내 용서하지 않았을 거야.”\n\n“그거 정말 딱했네요. 가엾은 스니드 양. 저는 여동생이 없지만 그 심정이 딱해요. 제때가 오기도 전에 밀려나는 건 여간 속상한 일이 아닐 테니까요. 하지만 그건 온전히 어머니 잘못이에요. 오거스타 양은 가정교사와 함께 있었어야지요. 그런 어정쩡한 짓은 결코 잘될 리 없어요. 그런데 이제 저는 프라이스 양에 대해 확실히 알아야겠어요. 그분은 무도회에 가나요? 제 언니 댁만이 아니라 여기저기 저녁 초대에도 다니나요?”\n\n“아뇨,” 에드먼드가 대답했다. “제 사촌은 무도회에 가 본 적이 한 번도 없을 겁니다. 어머니가 좀처럼 남과 어울리러 나가지 않으시고, 그랜트 부인 댁 말고는 어디서도 저녁을 안 드시거든요. 그래서 패니는 어머니와 함께 집에 있습니다.”\n\n“아! 그럼 분명하네요. 프라이스 양은 아직 사교계에 안 나왔군요.”\n\n# 제6장\n\n버트럼 씨는 ──로 떠났고, 크로퍼드 양은 이제 두 집안 사이에서 거의 날마다 벌어지다시피 하는 모임에 크나큰 공허가 생기고, 그가 없는 것을 여실히 아쉬워하게 되리라 미리 각오했다. 그가 떠나고 얼마 안 되어 다 함께 공원 저택에서 저녁을 들게 되었을 때, 그녀는 식탁 아래쪽 제가 정해 둔 자리를 다시 차지하고서, 주인이 바뀐 탓에 더없이 서글픈 차이를 느끼게 되리라 잔뜩 벼르고 있었다. 분명 아주 맥빠지는 자리가 될 터였다. 그의 아우로 말하면, 그 형에 견주어 에드먼드는 할 말이 아무것도 없을 것이었다. 수프는 더없이 김빠진 태도로 돌려질 것이고, 포도주는 웃음도 정겨운 농담도 없이 마셔질 것이며, 사슴고기는 지난날 어느 넓적다리 살에 얽힌 유쾌한 일화 하나, “내 아무개 친구”에 관한 재미난 이야기 하나 없이 썰릴 것이었다. 그녀는 식탁 위쪽에서 오가는 이야기에서, 그리고 크로퍼드 남매가 온 이래 처음으로 맨스필드에 얼굴을 내민 러시워스 씨를 지켜보는 데서 재미를 찾아보는 수밖에 없었다. 그는 이웃 고을의 한 친구를 찾아갔다 온 참이었는데, 그 친구가 근래에 조경가를 시켜 제 정원을 새로 꾸민 터라, 러시워스 씨는 머릿속이 온통 그 생각으로 가득 차 돌아와서는 제 땅도 꼭 그렇게 손보고 싶어 안달이었다. 그리고 딱히 요령 있는 말은 못 하면서도, 오로지 그 이야기밖에 할 줄을 몰랐다. 그 화제는 이미 응접실에서 다뤄졌던 것이, 이제 식당에서 되살아났다. 버트럼 양의 관심과 의견을 얻는 것이 그의 주된 목표임은 뻔했다. 비록 그녀의 태도는 그의 비위를 맞추려는 마음이라기보다 은근한 우월감을 내비치는 쪽이었으나, 소더턴 코트와 거기 딸린 여러 상념이 입에 오르자 그녀는 흐뭇한 기분이 들어, 아주 매몰차게 굴지는 않았다.\n\n“콤프턴을 한번 보셨어야 하는데요.” 그가 말했다. “그야말로 완벽하답니다! 저는 여태 그렇게 확 달라진 곳을 본 적이 없어요. 스미스한테 여기가 어딘지 도무지 모르겠다고 했다니까요. 이제 그 진입로는 이 고장에서 손꼽히는 절경이에요. 저택이 어찌나 놀라운 방식으로 눈앞에 나타나는지요. 정말이지, 어제 소더턴에 돌아와 보니 꼭 감옥 같더군요—음침하기 짝이 없는 낡은 감옥 말이에요.”\n\n“어머, 저런 망측한 소리를!” 노리스 부인이 외쳤다. “감옥이라니요? 소더턴 코트야말로 세상에서 가장 고귀한 옛 저택인데.”\n\n“손볼 데가 이만저만이 아니에요, 부인. 저는 그토록 손볼 데가 많은 곳을 여태 본 적이 없어요. 게다가 어찌나 스산한지 도무지 어찌해야 좋을지 모르겠어요.”\n\n“러시워스 씨가 지금 그리 여기시는 것도 무리는 아니지요.” 그랜트 부인이 노리스 부인에게 미소 지으며 말했다. “하지만 두고 보세요, 소더턴은 머잖아 그분 마음이 바라는 온갖 손질을 다 받게 될 테니까요.”\n\n“저도 뭐라도 좀 해 봐야지요.” 러시워스 씨가 말했다. “그런데 뭘 해야 할지 모르겠어요. 저를 도와줄 좋은 벗이 하나 있으면 좋겠는데.”\n\n“그런 일에 가장 좋은 벗이라면,” 버트럼 양이 차분히 말했다. “아마 렙턴 씨겠지요.”\n\n“저도 바로 그 생각을 하고 있었어요. 스미스네를 그토록 잘 해 놓았으니, 아무래도 그 사람을 당장 부르는 게 낫겠어요. 그 사람 삯은 하루 오 기니랍니다.”\n\n“아니, 설령 열 기니라 한들,” 노리스 부인이 외쳤다. “댁이야 그런 걸 개의할 까닭이 조금도 없지요. 비용이 걸림돌이 될 일은 없어요. 나 같으면 비용 따위는 생각도 안 하겠어요. 뭐든 가장 훌륭한 격조로, 될 수 있는 한 근사하게 해 놓을 거예요. 소더턴 코트만 한 곳이라면 안목과 돈이 해 줄 수 있는 건 뭐든 누릴 만하지요. 거기엔 손댈 여지가 넉넉하고, 애쓴 보람을 톡톡히 돌려줄 땅이 있으니까요. 나로 말하면, 만약 소더턴의 오십분의 일만 한 땅이라도 있었다면, 나는 늘 나무를 심고 가꾸며 지냈을 거예요. 워낙 내가 그런 걸 몹시 좋아하는 사람이라서요. 지금 내가 있는 이 자잘한 반 에이커 땅에서 뭘 해 보겠다고 나서는 건 너무 우스운 일이지요. 그야말로 익살극이 될 거예요. 하지만 자리가 더 넓었다면, 나는 가꾸고 심는 데서 엄청난 즐거움을 누렸을 거예요. 우리도 목사관에서 그런 일을 어마어마하게 했답니다. 처음 그 집을 맡았을 때와는 아주 딴판인 곳으로 만들어 놓았지요. 젊은 사람들이야 그 시절 일을 잘 기억 못 하겠지만, 만약 사랑하는 토머스 경이 여기 계셨다면 우리가 어떤 손질을 했는지 다 일러 주셨을 거예요. 게다가 훨씬 더 많은 일을 했을 텐데, 딱한 노리스 목사의 병약한 몸 탓에 그러질 못했지요. 그 딱한 양반은 좀처럼 밖에 나가 뭘 즐길 수조차 없었고, 바로 그 때문에 나도 토머스 경과 늘 이야기하던 여러 일을 할 마음이 꺾이고 말았어요. 만약 그것만 아니었다면, 우리는 정원 담을 죽 이어 쌓고, 그랜트 박사가 해 놓은 것처럼 교회 묘지를 가리는 나무숲을 만들었을 거예요. 그래도 우리는 늘 뭔가를 하고 있었지요. 노리스 목사가 세상을 뜨기 꼭 한 해 전 봄에야 마구간 담에 살구나무를 심었는데, 그게 지금은 그토록 훌륭한 나무로 자라 그리도 탐스럽게 여물었답니다, 박사님.” 그러면서 그녀는 그랜트 박사에게로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n“그 나무가 잘 자라는 건 두말할 것 없지요, 부인.” 그랜트 박사가 대답했다. “흙이 좋으니까요. 그리고 저는 그 앞을 지날 때마다, 그 열매가 따는 수고에 견줘 그토록 값어치가 없다는 게 안타깝답니다.”\n\n“박사님, 그건 무어 파크 살구예요. 우리가 무어 파크로 사들였고, 값도—아니, 그건 토머스 경이 선물로 주신 거지만, 나는 계산서를 봤어요—분명 일곱 실링을 치렀고, 무어 파크로 값이 매겨져 있었다니까요.”\n\n“속아 사신 겁니다, 부인.” 그랜트 박사가 대답했다. “저 감자들이 그 나무 열매만큼이나 무어 파크 살구 맛을 낼걸요. 그건 아무리 잘 익어도 밍밍한 열매예요. 하지만 좋은 살구라면 먹을 만은 하지요—우리 정원 것은 하나도 그렇지가 못하지만요.”\n\n“실은 말이에요, 부인,” 그랜트 부인이 식탁 너머 노리스 부인에게 소곤대는 시늉을 하며 말했다. “그랜트 박사는 우리 살구의 본디 맛이 어떤지 잘 모른답니다. 워낙 귀한 열매라 그이한테는 좀처럼 하나도 안 돌아가거든요. 손을 좀 보태면, 게다가 우리 것은 유난히 크고 때깔 고운 품종이라, 이르게 만드는 파이며 설탕절임에 우리 요리사가 그걸 죄다 차지해 버리고 만답니다.”\n\n얼굴이 붉으락해지기 시작하던 노리스 부인은 이내 누그러졌다. 그리고 한동안은 소더턴 손질 이야기 대신 다른 화제가 자리를 차지했다. 그랜트 박사와 노리스 부인은 좀처럼 사이좋은 벗이 못 되었다. 그 둘의 사귐은 애초에 파손 배상 다툼으로 시작된 데다, 습성마저 판이했으니 말이다.\n\n잠깐 이야기가 끊긴 뒤 러시워스 씨가 다시 입을 열었다. “스미스네 땅은 온 고장의 감탄거리예요. 그런데 렙턴이 손대기 전에는 그야말로 아무것도 아니었어요. 아무래도 저도 렙턴을 부를까 봐요.”\n\n“러시워스 씨,” 버트럼 부인이 말했다. “나 같으면 아주 예쁜 관목숲을 하나 두겠어요. 날씨 좋을 때 관목숲으로 나가 거니는 게 참 좋거든요.”\n\n러시워스 씨는 부인의 뜻에 따르겠노라 얼른 다짐하며 무언가 알랑거리는 말을 지어내려 애썼다. 그러나 그녀의 취향에 순순히 따르겠다는 마음과, 실은 저도 애초에 똑같이 작정하고 있었다는 마음, 게다가 여인네들 전반의 안락에 마음 쓰는 척하려는 속셈과, 정작 저가 기쁘게 해 드리고 싶은 이는 오직 한 사람뿐임을 은근히 내비치려는 속셈까지 겹쳐, 그는 그만 갈피를 잃고 말았다. 에드먼드는 포도주를 권하며 그의 말을 끊어 주게 되어 다행스러웠다. 그렇지만 러시워스 씨는 여느 때 말수가 많은 편은 아니었어도, 제 가슴에 가장 가까운 이 화제에 대해서만은 아직도 할 말이 남아 있었다. “스미스네 땅은 죄다 합쳐도 백 에이커 남짓밖에 안 돼요. 그거야 얼마 안 되는 거지요. 그러니 그런 땅이 그토록 잘 꾸며졌다는 게 더더욱 놀라운 일이에요. 그런데 우리 소더턴에는 물대는 풀밭은 빼고도 실히 칠백 에이커는 되거든요. 그러니 콤프턴에서 그만큼 해낼 수 있었다면, 우리라고 절망할 것 없다 싶어요. 저택에 너무 가까이 자라던 멋진 고목 두어 그루를 베어 냈더니 전망이 놀랍도록 트이더군요. 그걸 보고 저는, 렙턴이든 그런 부류의 누구든 소더턴의 그 가로수길도 틀림없이 베어 버리라고 하겠구나 싶었어요. 서쪽 정면에서 언덕 꼭대기까지 이어지는 그 가로수길 말이에요.” 이 말을 하며 그는 유독 버트럼 양 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다. 그러나 버트럼 양은 이렇게 대답하는 것이 가장 어울린다고 여겼다—\n\n“그 가로수길이요! 어머! 저는 기억이 안 나는데요. 저는 정말이지 소더턴을 잘 몰라요.”\n\n에드먼드의 맞은편, 크로퍼드 양의 바로 정면에 앉아 줄곧 귀 기울여 듣고 있던 패니가, 이제 그를 바라보며 나직한 목소리로 말했다—\n\n“가로수길을 베어 낸다니! 어쩜 그리 안타까울까! 그러니 쿠퍼가 떠오르지 않아요? ‘쓰러진 가로수길들이여, 내 다시 그대들의 부당한 운명을 애도하노라.’”\n\n그가 미소 지으며 대답했다. “안됐지만 그 가로수길은 가망이 없어 보이는구나, 패니.”\n\n“저는 그 길이 베어지기 전에 소더턴을 한번 보고 싶어요. 지금 그대로, 옛 모습 그대로 있는 그곳을요. 하지만 아마 못 보겠지요.”\n\n“거기 한 번도 가 본 적이 없지? 그래, 갈 수가 없겠구나. 하필이면 말을 타고 다녀오기엔 너무 멀거든. 어떻게든 마련해 볼 수 있으면 좋으련만.”\n\n“아! 괜찮아요. 언제든 제가 그곳을 보게 되면, 오빠가 어떻게 달라졌는지 말해 주실 거잖아요.”\n\n“제가 알기로,” 크로퍼드 양이 말했다. “소더턴은 오래된 곳이고, 얼마간 웅장한 데가 있는 곳이지요. 어떤 특정한 양식으로 지은 건가요?”\n\n“그 저택은 엘리자베스 시대에 지어진, 크고 반듯한 벽돌 건물입니다. 육중하지만 기품 있어 보이고, 좋은 방이 많지요. 자리가 안 좋아요. 파크에서 가장 낮은 곳 가운데 하나에 서 있거든요. 그 점에서는 손보기에 불리하지요. 하지만 숲이 훌륭하고, 개울도 하나 있는데, 감히 말하건대 그걸 잘만 살리면 크게 쓸모가 있을 겁니다. 러시워스 씨가 그곳을 현대풍으로 꾸미려는 것은 아주 옳은 생각이라고 봅니다. 그리고 그 모든 게 더없이 훌륭하게 될 거라고 저는 믿어 의심치 않아요.”\n\n크로퍼드 양은 고분고분 귀 기울여 듣고는, 속으로 이렇게 뇌었다. “예의 바른 사람이야. 좋은 쪽으로 잘 둘러대는군.”\n\n“저는 러시워스 씨를 좌우할 마음은 없습니다.” 그가 말을 이었다. “하지만 만약 저한테 새로 꾸밀 땅이 있다면, 저 자신을 조경가의 손에 맡기지는 않겠어요. 차라리 좀 못한 아름다움일망정 제가 스스로 골라, 조금씩 손수 이뤄 가는 편을 택하겠습니다. 남의 실수를 견디느니 차라리 제 실수를 견디는 편이 나으니까요.”\n\n“댁이야 물론 뭘 하는지 훤히 아시겠지요. 하지만 저한테는 그게 안 맞아요. 저는 그런 일에 눈썰미도 재간도 없어서, 그저 눈앞에 다 차려진 것만 볼 줄 알거든요. 만약 저한테 시골에 제 땅이 있다면, 저는 그걸 맡아 줄 렙턴 씨 같은 사람이라면 누구에게든 더없이 고마워하며, 제 돈으로 뽑아낼 수 있는 만큼의 아름다움을 다 얻어 낼 거예요. 그리고 그게 다 완성되기 전에는 거들떠보지도 않을 거고요.”\n\n“저라면 그 모든 게 되어 가는 과정을 지켜보는 게 즐거울 텐데요.” 패니가 말했다.\n\n“아, 아가씬 그렇게 자라 왔으니까요. 그건 제 교육에는 들어 있지 않던 거예요. 게다가 제가 여태 받아 본 단 한 번의 처방이란 것도, 세상에서 그리 좋아하는 사람도 아닌 이의 손에서 내려진 것이라, 저는 한창 손보는 중인 땅을 세상 무엇보다 성가신 것으로 여기게 되었답니다. 삼 년 전에 제 존경하는 삼촌인 제독께서 우리 온 식구가 여름을 나라고 트위크넘에 별장을 하나 사셨어요. 이모와 저는 아주 신이 나서 내려갔지요. 그런데 그 집이 하도 예쁘다 보니, 이내 손을 봐야겠다는 얘기가 나왔고, 석 달 동안 우리는 온통 흙먼지와 어수선함 속에서, 밟고 다닐 자갈길 하나, 앉을 만한 벤치 하나 없이 지냈답니다. 저도 시골집이라면 관목숲이며 화원이며 소박한 걸상까지 이루 셀 수 없이, 뭐든 될 수 있는 한 완벽하게 갖추고 싶어요. 다만 그 모든 게 제 손을 번거롭게 하지 않고 되어야 한다는 거지요. 헨리는 달라요. 그 앤 손수 하는 걸 좋아하거든요.”\n\n에드먼드는 자기가 무척 우러러보고 싶은 크로퍼드 양이 제 삼촌을 그토록 스스럼없이 입에 올리는 것을 듣고 안타까웠다. 그것은 그의 예의 관념에 맞지 않았고, 그는 입을 다물고 말았다. 그러다 그녀의 잇따른 미소와 발랄함에 이끌려, 그 일은 일단 접어 두게 되었다.\n\n“버트럼 씨,” 그녀가 말했다. “드디어 제 하프 소식을 들었어요. 노샘프턴에 무사히 있다지 뭐예요. 그동안 그렇지 않다는 엄숙한 장담을 그토록 여러 번 받았는데도, 아마 열흘째 거기 있었나 봐요.” 에드먼드는 기쁘고도 놀랍다고 했다. “실은 우리가 너무 대놓고 수소문한 탓이었어요. 하인을 보내기도 하고, 우리가 몸소 나서기도 했으니까요. 런던에서 칠십 마일이나 떨어진 데서는 그런 식으론 안 되는 거였어요. 그런데 오늘 아침에야 제대로 된 경로로 그 소식을 들었지 뭐예요. 어떤 농부가 그걸 봤는데, 그가 방앗간 주인한테 말하고, 방앗간 주인이 푸주한한테 말하고, 푸주한의 사위가 가게에 말을 남긴 거예요.”\n\n“무슨 수로든 소식을 들으셨다니 정말 다행이에요. 이제 더는 지체되지 않기를 바랍니다.”\n\n“내일이면 받게 돼요. 그런데 그게 어떻게 옮겨질지 아세요? 짐수레나 달구지로가 아니에요. 아, 천만에요! 이 마을에서는 그런 건 하나도 못 빌리거든요. 차라리 짐꾼과 손수레를 구해 달라 하는 게 나았을 거예요.”\n\n“지금이야 아마 어려우실 겁니다. 한창 늦은 건초 수확철이라, 말과 달구지를 빌리기가 말이죠?”\n\n“그게 얼마나 큰일인지 알고는 어찌나 기가 막히던지요! 시골에서 말과 달구지가 없다는 게 도무지 있을 수 없는 일 같아서, 저는 하녀더러 당장 하나 부탁해 보라고 일렀어요. 제 화장방에서는 어느 쪽을 내다봐도 농가 마당 하나가 보이고, 관목숲을 거닐어도 또 다른 마당 하나를 지나치게 되니, 그저 청하기만 하면 곧 얻으려니 여겼거든요. 오히려 모두에게 그 혜택을 나눠 주지 못하는 게 좀 서운할 지경이었지요. 그런데 알고 보니 제가 세상에서 가장 얼토당토않고 가장 될 법도 없는 걸 청하고 있었다는 걸 알았을 때, 제가 얼마나 놀랐을지 짐작해 보세요. 온 동네 농부들이며 일꾼들이며, 교구 안의 건초란 건초는 죄다 화나게 하고 만 거예요! 그랜트 박사네 집사로 말하면, 아무래도 그 사람 눈에 안 띄게 다니는 게 낫겠어요. 게다가 평소엔 더없이 친절한 제 형부마저, 제가 무슨 짓을 했는지 알고는 저를 꽤나 못마땅한 낯으로 보더라니까요.”\n\n“미리 그런 데까지 생각하실 수야 없었겠지요. 하지만 막상 생각해 보시면, 풀을 거둬들이는 일이 얼마나 중한지 아실 겁니다. 달구지를 빌리는 건 어느 때고 생각만큼 쉬운 일이 아니에요. 우리 고장 농부들은 그걸 세놓는 버릇이 없거든요. 더구나 수확철이면 말 한 마리를 내주는 건 도무지 그들 형편에 있을 수 없는 일이지요.”\n\n“차차 이곳 사정을 다 알게 되겠지요. 하지만 무엇이든 돈이면 다 얻는다는 그 진짜배기 런던식 격언을 품고 내려온 저로서는, 처음엔 이곳 시골 관습의 그 억센 자립심에 좀 당황했답니다. 어쨌거나 내일이면 제 하프를 가져오게 됐어요. 더없이 착한 천성 그 자체인 헨리가 제 사륜마차로 실어다 주겠다고 나섰거든요. 이만하면 아주 떳떳하게 모셔 오는 셈이지요?”\n\n에드먼드는 하프가 자기가 가장 좋아하는 악기라며, 어서 그녀의 연주를 듣게 되기를 바란다고 했다. 패니는 하프 소리를 아예 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없어, 몹시 듣고 싶어 했다.\n\n“두 분 다에게 기꺼이 연주해 드릴게요.” 크로퍼드 양이 말했다. “적어도 두 분이 듣고 싶어 하시는 동안은요. 아마 그보다 훨씬 오래겠지만요. 저 자신이 음악을 끔찍이 사랑하는 데다, 타고난 취향이 서로 맞먹을 땐 연주하는 쪽이 언제나 더 득을 보는 법이니까요. 여러 가지로 흡족하거든요. 참, 버트럼 씨, 혹시 형님께 편지를 쓰시거든, 제발 제 하프가 왔다고 전해 주세요. 그이가 그 일로 제가 애태우는 소리를 그토록 많이 들었으니까요. 그리고 괜찮으시면, 제가 그이의 심정을 헤아려, 돌아오실 때에 대비해 가장 구슬픈 가락을 준비해 두겠다고도 전해 주세요. 그이 말이 질 게 뻔하다는 걸 저도 아니까요.”\n\n“편지를 쓰게 되면, 원하시는 건 뭐든 전하지요. 하지만 지금으로선 편지를 쓸 일이 딱히 생길 것 같지가 않군요.”\n\n“그렇겠지요. 설령 그이가 열두 달을 떠나 있다 한들, 되도록 피할 수만 있다면 형님도 아우도 서로 편지 한 장 안 쓸걸요. 쓸 일이란 도무지 생기지 않을 테니까요. 오라비란 참 이상한 족속이에요! 세상에 더없이 다급한 일이 아니고서는 서로 편지 한 장 안 쓰지요. 그러다 어쩔 수 없이 붓을 들어야 할 때도—어느 말이 병들었다느니 어느 친척이 죽었다느니 하는 소식조차—되도록 몇 마디 안 되게 적고 마니까요. 오라비들끼리는 문체가 딱 하나뿐이에요. 저는 그걸 훤히 안답니다. 헨리는 다른 모든 점에서는 오라비가 마땅히 그래야 할 그대로예요. 저를 아끼고, 저와 의논하고, 저를 믿고, 몇 시간이고 저와 이야기를 나누지요. 그런데도 여태 편지에서 한 장을 넘겨 본 적이 없어요. 게다가 그나마도 흔히 이런 게 다예요—‘메리 누나에게. 방금 도착했어. 배스는 붐비는 것 같고, 다 여느 때와 같아. 이만 총총.’ 그게 바로 진짜 사내다운 문체예요. 그게 완벽한 오라비의 편지고요.”\n\n“식구들과 다 멀리 떨어져 있을 때는,” 윌리엄을 생각해 얼굴을 붉히며 패니가 말했다. “긴 편지를 쓰기도 해요.”\n\n“프라이스 양에게는 바다에 나가 있는 오라비가 하나 있는데,” 에드먼드가 말했다. “그 사람이 편지를 어찌나 잘 쓰는지, 이 애는 크로퍼드 양이 우리를 너무 매몰차게 나무란다고 여긴답니다.”\n\n“바다에 나가 있다고요? 물론 임금님을 섬기는 몸이겠지요?”\n\n패니는 차라리 에드먼드가 그 이야기를 해 주었으면 했으나, 그가 한사코 입을 다무는 통에 하는 수 없이 제 오라비의 처지를 들려주게 되었다. 그의 직업과, 그가 나가 있던 외국의 여러 주둔지를 말할 때 그녀의 목소리는 생기가 돌았다. 그러나 그가 떠나 있은 햇수를 말할 때는 눈에 눈물이 맺히지 않을 수 없었다. 크로퍼드 양은 예의 바르게 그가 어서 진급하기를 빌어 주었다.\n\n“제 사촌의 함장에 대해 뭐 아시는 게 있나요?” 에드먼드가 말했다. “마셜 대령 말입니다. 크로퍼드 양은 해군에 아는 이가 많으시겠지요?”\n\n“제독들 중에는 꽤 많지요. 하지만,” 그녀가 짐짓 도도한 낯빛으로, “아래 계급 사람들은 저희가 잘 몰라요. 함장 나부랭이야 아주 훌륭한 축일지 몰라도, 저희 부류에는 못 끼거든요. 여러 제독에 대해서라면 얼마든지 이야기해 드릴 수 있어요. 그분들과 그 기함들, 봉급의 등급, 그리고 그분들끼리의 아웅다웅과 시샘까지요. 하지만 대체로 말씀드리자면, 그분들은 다들 진급에서 밀려나 아주 홀대받고 있답니다. 하기야 삼촌 댁이 제 집이었으니, 저는 자연히 제독들과 두루 알고 지내게 되었지요. ‘후위 제독’과 ‘부제독’이라면 물리도록 봤답니다. 자, 제발 제가 무슨 상스러운 말장난이라도 한 걸로 의심하진 말아 주세요.”\n\n에드먼드는 다시금 정색이 되어, 그저 이렇게만 대꾸했다. “고귀한 직업이지요.”\n\n“네, 두 가지 조건만 갖춰지면 그 직업도 그런대로 괜찮아요. 재산을 이루게 해 주고, 그걸 쓰는 데 분별이 있다면 말이지요. 하지만 한마디로, 제가 좋아하는 직업은 못 돼요. 저한테는 한 번도 정다운 모습으로 다가온 적이 없거든요.”\n\n에드먼드는 다시 하프 이야기로 돌아갔고, 그녀의 연주를 들을 생각에 또 한 번 무척 즐거워했다.\n\n그러는 동안에도 땅을 손질하는 이야기는 다른 이들 사이에서 여전히 오가고 있었다. 그리고 그랜트 부인은, 비록 그 아우의 관심을 줄리아 버트럼 양에게서 돌려놓는 일이 되긴 했으나, 동생에게 말을 건네지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n“얘, 헨리, 너는 할 말이 없니? 너도 손질을 해 본 사람이잖니. 게다가 내가 에버링엄에 대해 들은 바로는, 그곳이 잉글랜드의 어느 땅과 견주어도 손색없다던데. 타고난 아름다움이 대단하다고 나는 확신한다. 예전의 에버링엄은 내 눈엔 그야말로 완벽했어. 그 흐뭇하게 흘러내리는 지형이며, 그 나무들이라니! 그곳을 다시 볼 수만 있다면 뭔들 못 내주겠니!”\n\n“누님의 평을 듣는 것만큼 저를 기쁘게 하는 것도 없겠지요.” 그가 대답했다. “하지만 아마 좀 실망하실 겁니다. 지금 누님이 그리시는 것만 못하실 테니까요. 넓이로 치면 그야말로 아무것도 아니에요. 그 보잘것없음에 놀라실걸요. 그리고 손질로 말하면, 제가 할 일이 아주 조금밖에—너무 적었어요. 저는 훨씬 더 오래 바삐 지냈으면 싶었는데요.”\n\n“그런 일을 좋아하나요?” 줄리아가 말했다.\n\n“아주 좋아하지요. 하지만 땅이 타고난 이점 덕에, 아주 어린 눈에도 이제 손볼 게 얼마 안 남았다는 게 훤히 드러났고, 저도 그에 따라 마음을 굳혔던 터라, 저는 성년이 된 지 석 달도 안 되어 에버링엄을 지금 이 모습 그대로 다 만들어 놓았답니다. 제 구상은 웨스트민스터에서 세워져, 케임브리지에서 조금 손질되었다가, 스물한 살에 실행에 옮겨졌지요. 저는 아직 그토록 많은 즐거움이 앞에 남아 있는 러시워스 씨가 부럽다는 생각마저 들어요. 저는 제 몫의 즐거움을 벌써 다 게걸스레 먹어 치웠으니까요.”\n\n“빨리 보는 사람은 빨리 마음을 정하고 빨리 행동하는 법이지요.” 줄리아가 말했다. “댁은 결코 할 일이 없어 아쉬울 사람이 아니에요. 러시워스 씨를 부러워할 게 아니라, 댁의 견해로 그분을 도와드려야지요.”\n\n이 말의 뒷부분을 들은 그랜트 부인은, 제 아우만 한 판단력이 다시없으리라 믿고 그 말에 힘껏 맞장구를 쳤다. 그리고 버트럼 양도 그 생각을 냉큼 붙들어 전적으로 거들면서, 이런 일은 곧바로 전문가의 손에 내던지기보다 벗들이며 사심 없는 조언자들과 의논하는 편이 한없이 낫다고 제 의견을 밝히자, 러시워스 씨는 크로퍼드 씨의 도움을 청하는 호의를 베풀어 달라고 얼른 부탁했다. 그리고 크로퍼드 씨는 제 재주를 짐짓 겸손하게 깎아내린 뒤, 도움이 될 만한 일이라면 어떤 식으로든 기꺼이 나서겠다고 했다. 이윽고 러시워스 씨가, 크로퍼드 씨더러 소더턴으로 왕림하여 하룻밤 묵어 주는 영광을 베풀어 달라 청하려 하는데, 노리스 부인이 마치 두 조카딸의 마음속에서 크로퍼드 씨를 데려가 버릴 그 계획에 대한 언짢음을 읽어 낸 듯, 수정안을 들고 끼어들었다.\n\n“크로퍼드 씨가 기꺼이 나서 주실 거야 의심할 것 없지요. 그런데 우리 중에 몇이 더 못 갈 것도 없잖아요? 다 함께 조촐한 나들이를 하면 어때요? 여기 댁의 손질 이야기에 관심 있어 할 사람이 많잖아요, 우리 러시워스 씨. 그 자리에서 크로퍼드 씨의 견해를 듣고 싶어 할 사람도 많고, 저마다 제 의견으로 댁에게 얼마간 보탬이 될 사람도 많고요. 나로 말하면, 진작부터 댁의 어지신 어머님을 다시 한번 뵈러 가고 싶던 참이었어요. 내 소유의 말이 없다는 것 말고는 그토록 소홀했을 까닭이 없었지요. 하지만 이제라면 나는 가서 러시워스 부인과 몇 시간 앉아 있고, 그동안 나머지 분들은 두루 거닐며 이런저런 것을 정하면 되겠네요. 그런 다음 다 함께 여기로 돌아와 늦은 저녁을 들든지, 아니면 댁의 어머님께 가장 편하실 대로 소더턴에서 저녁을 들고, 달빛 아래 기분 좋게 마차로 돌아오면 되고요. 아마 크로퍼드 씨가 내 두 조카딸과 나를 그 사륜마차에 태워 주실 테고, 에드먼드는 말을 타고 가면 되지 않겠니, 언니. 그리고 패니는 언니 곁에 남아 집을 지키고요.”\n\n버트럼 부인은 아무 이의도 달지 않았다. 그리고 그 나들이에 관계된 이들은 모두 앞다투어 선뜻 찬성의 뜻을 밝혔다. 다만 에드먼드만은 그 모든 것을 들으면서도 아무 말이 없었다.\n\n# 제7장\n\n“그래, 패니, 이제는 크로퍼드 양이 어떠니?” 이튿날, 그 문제를 한동안 혼자 곰곰이 생각해 본 뒤에 에드먼드가 말했다. “어제는 그분이 마음에 들었어?”\n\n“아주 좋았어요—무척요. 저는 그분이 이야기하는 걸 듣는 게 좋아요. 저를 즐겁게 해 주시거든요. 게다가 얼마나 예쁘신지, 바라보는 것만으로도 큰 기쁨이에요.”\n\n“그 얼굴이 워낙 매력적이지. 표정이 어찌나 오묘하게 살아 움직이는지! 그런데 패니, 그분 이야기 중에 뭔가 좀 옳지 않다 싶어 걸리는 데는 없었니?”\n\n“아, 있었어요! 그분은 삼촌에 대해 그렇게 말씀하시면 안 되는 거였어요. 저는 정말 깜짝 놀랐어요. 그토록 여러 해를 함께 살아온 삼촌이고, 흠이야 어떻든 그분 오빠를 그토록 아껴 꼭 아들처럼 대해 준다는 분인데요. 저는 도저히 믿을 수가 없었어요!”\n\n“네가 놀랄 줄 알았다. 아주 잘못된 일이었어. 몹시 무례한 일이었지.”\n\n“게다가 아주 배은망덕하다고, 저는 생각해요.”\n\n“배은망덕이란 좀 센 말이구나. 나는 그 삼촌이 그분한테 고마움을 받을 권리가 있다고는 생각지 않는다. 그 부인이라면 분명 있었지. 그리고 이모의 기억을 향한 그 뜨거운 존경심이 여기서 그분을 그릇된 길로 이끄는 거야. 그분은 처지가 난처해. 그토록 뜨거운 감정과 발랄한 기질을 지녔으니, 제독을 그늘지게 하지 않고서 크로퍼드 부인을 향한 애정을 온전히 표하기란 어려울 거야. 그 두 분의 불화에서 누가 더 잘못이었는지 나는 안다고 나서진 않겠다. 물론 제독이 지금 하는 행실을 보면 그 부인 편을 들고 싶어지기도 하지만 말이야. 어쨌든 크로퍼드 양이 이모를 온전히 두둔하는 건 자연스럽고 갸륵한 일이야. 나는 그분의 생각을 나무라는 게 아니다. 다만 그걸 남 앞에 드러내는 데에는 분명 온당치 못한 데가 있어.”\n\n“그런데 오빠 생각은 어때요?” 잠깐 생각한 뒤 패니가 말했다. “그렇게 온당치 못한 것 자체가, 그분을 온전히 길러 낸 크로퍼드 부인에게도 흠이 되는 게 아닐까요? 그 부인이 그분한테 제독에게 마땅히 해야 할 도리를 제대로 가르쳐 주지 못한 거잖아요.”\n\n“그건 옳은 지적이다. 그래, 조카딸의 흠은 곧 이모의 흠이었다고 봐야겠지. 그리고 그럴수록 그분이 그동안 어떤 불리한 처지에 있었는지 더 헤아리게 되는구나. 하지만 나는 지금의 집이 그분한테 좋은 영향을 주리라고 본다. 그랜트 부인의 몸가짐은 그야말로 나무랄 데가 없거든. 그 부인은 제 아우 이야기를 참 정겨운 애정으로 하지.”\n\n“네, 아우가 그토록 짧은 편지를 쓴다는 대목만 빼면요. 그 얘기엔 저도 하마터면 웃을 뻔했어요. 하지만 자매가 떨어져 있을 때 읽을 만한 무언가를 써 보내는 수고조차 마다하는 오라비의 사랑이나 착한 마음을, 저는 그리 높이 쳐줄 수가 없어요. 윌리엄이라면 어떤 처지에서도 결코 저한테 그러지 않았을 거예요. 그리고 오빠가 떨어져 있을 때 긴 편지를 안 쓸 거라고, 그분이 무슨 권리로 넘겨짚는 거예요?”\n\n“발랄한 사람의 권리지, 패니. 제 흥이나 남의 흥에 보탬이 될 만한 건 뭐든 붙드는 권리 말이다. 심술이나 거친 데만 배지 않았다면 얼마든지 허물할 게 아니야. 그리고 크로퍼드 양의 얼굴이나 몸가짐에는 그런 그림자가 조금도 없어. 날카롭거나 시끄럽거나 상스러운 데라곤 하나도 없지. 우리가 지금 이야기한 그 몇 가지 경우만 빼면, 그분은 더없이 여자다워. 그 대목만은 두둔할 수가 없지만 말이야. 네가 그걸 나와 똑같이 봐 줘서 기쁘구나.”\n\n그가 그녀의 마음을 빚어 주고 그 애정을 얻어 온 터라, 그녀가 그와 같이 생각하게 될 공산은 컸다. 그렇지만 이 무렵, 이 문제에서만은 이제 어긋남이 생길 위험이 얼마간 싹트기 시작했다. 그가 크로퍼드 양을 우러르는 길로 접어들고 있었는데, 그것은 그를 패니가 따라갈 수 없는 데로 이끌지도 몰랐기 때문이다. 크로퍼드 양의 매력은 줄어들지 않았다. 하프가 도착했고, 오히려 그녀의 아름다움과 재치와 쾌활함을 한층 돋우었다. 그녀가 더없이 상냥하게, 유난히 잘 어울리는 표정과 취향으로 연주했고, 곡이 끝날 때마다 무언가 재치 있는 말이 곁들여졌으니 말이다. 에드먼드는 날마다 목사관에 나가 제가 가장 좋아하는 악기 소리에 흠뻑 젖었다. 하루 아침의 방문이 이튿날의 초대를 확보해 주었다. 그 여인으로서도 들어 줄 이가 있는 것을 마다할 리 없었으니, 이내 만사가 순조로운 흐름을 탔다.\n\n예쁘고 발랄한 젊은 여자가, 제 몸만큼이나 우아한 하프를 곁에 두고, 그 둘이 바닥까지 내려오게 낸 창가에 자리 잡았는데, 그 창은 여름의 짙푸른 잎으로 우거진 관목에 둘러싸인 자그마한 잔디밭으로 열려 있었으니—이만하면 어느 사내의 마음인들 사로잡기에 넉넉했다. 계절도, 풍경도, 공기도, 모두가 다정함과 감상에 이바지했다. 그랜트 부인과 그 자수틀도 쓸모가 없지 않았다. 모든 것이 조화로웠다. 그리고 사랑이 일단 발동하면 무엇이든 다 밑천이 되는 법이라, 샌드위치 쟁반이며 그것을 대접하느라 분주한 그랜트 박사조차 바라볼 만한 것이 되었다. 그러나 딱히 그 일을 궁리한 것도, 제가 무엇을 하고 있는지 안 것도 아닌 채로, 에드먼드는 그런 사귐이 이어진 한 주 끝머리에 이르러 자못 깊은 사랑에 빠져들고 있었다. 그리고 그 여인의 명예를 위해 덧붙이자면, 그가 세상 물정에 밝은 사내도 맏이도 아니고, 알랑거리는 재간이나 잡담의 흥겨움 하나 없었는데도, 그는 차츰 그녀의 마음에 들기 시작했다. 그녀 자신은 그것을 미처 내다보지도 못했고 좀처럼 이해하기도 어려웠으나, 그렇게 느꼈다. 그는 흔한 잣대로 보면 도무지 유쾌한 사람이 아니었으니 말이다. 그는 실없는 소리를 하지 않았고, 입에 발린 칭찬도 하지 않았으며, 제 소신은 굽힐 줄 몰랐고, 남을 살피는 마음씨는 잔잔하고 소박했다. 아마 그 진실함과 한결같음과 곧음에 어떤 매력이 있었으리라. 크로퍼드 양은 그것을 느낄 줄은 알아도, 스스로 그것을 헤아려 따져 볼 줄은 몰랐다. 하지만 그녀는 그에 대해 그리 깊이 생각하지 않았다. 그는 당장 그녀를 즐겁게 해 주었고, 그녀는 그를 곁에 두는 게 좋았다. 그것으로 충분했다.\n\n에드먼드가 날마다 아침이면 목사관에 가 있는 것을 패니는 이상히 여길 수 없었다. 청하지 않아도, 남의 눈에 띄지 않고 들어가 하프 소리를 들을 수만 있었다면, 그녀 자신도 기꺼이 거기 가 있었을 테니 말이다. 또한 저녁 산책이 끝나 두 집안이 다시 헤어질 때, 크로퍼드 씨가 공원 저택의 부인네들을 모시는 동안 그가 그랜트 부인과 그 아우를 집까지 바래다주는 것을 도리로 여기는 것도 이상히 여길 수 없었다. 다만 그녀는 그것이 아주 나쁜 맞바꿈이라고 여겼다. 그리고 에드먼드가 곁에서 포도주에 물을 타 주지 않는다면, 차라리 그걸 안 마시고 말겠다 싶었다. 그가 크로퍼드 양과 그토록 여러 시간을 함께 보내면서도, 저가 이미 지적한 그런 흠—패니 자신은 그녀와 함께 있을 때면 거의 언제나 그와 비슷한 무언가로 새삼 떠올리게 되는 그 흠—을 더 보지 못한다는 게 그녀는 조금 의아했다. 하지만 실상이 그러했다. 에드먼드는 그녀에게 크로퍼드 양 이야기를 즐겨 했으나, 그 뒤로 제독이 험담을 면했다는 것으로 만족하는 눈치였다. 그리고 그녀는 제 소견을 그에게 내비치기를 삼갔다. 그것이 심술처럼 보일까 봐서였다. 크로퍼드 양이 그녀에게 처음으로 실제 아픔을 안긴 것은, 그녀가 승마를 배우고 싶어 한 데서 비롯된 일이었다. 크로퍼드 양은 맨스필드에 자리 잡은 지 얼마 안 되어 공원 저택 아가씨들을 본떠 그 마음을 품게 되었는데, 에드먼드와 가까워지자 그가 그 소망을 부추기게 되었고, 초심자에게는 어느 마구간의 어느 말보다 가장 알맞다며, 그녀의 첫 시도를 위해 제 얌전한 암말을 내주기에 이르렀다. 그러나 이 제의에서 그가 사촌에게 아픔이나 손해를 끼칠 뜻은 조금도 없었다. 그녀는 그로 인해 하루의 운동도 잃을 게 없었으니 말이다. 암말은 그녀의 승마가 시작되기 딱 반 시간 전에만 목사관으로 내려보내기로 되어 있었다. 그리고 패니는 그 제안을 처음 받았을 때, 홀대받았다고 느끼기는커녕, 그가 그 일로 제 허락을 구한다는 것에 고마움이 북받쳐 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다.\n\n크로퍼드 양은 첫 시도를 제 체면이 서게 훌륭히 해냈고, 패니에게는 아무 불편도 끼치지 않았다. 암말을 끌고 내려가 그 모든 것을 주관한 에드먼드는, 패니도, 사촌들 없이 그녀가 말을 탈 때면 늘 곁을 지키던 진중한 늙은 마부도 미처 나설 채비를 갖추기 전에, 넉넉한 시간을 두고 말을 끌고 돌아왔다. 둘째 날의 시도는 그렇게 죄가 없지는 못했다. 크로퍼드 양은 승마가 어찌나 즐거운지 그만둘 줄을 몰랐다. 활달하고 겁이 없는 데다, 몸집은 자그마해도 다부지게 여물어, 그녀는 여자 기수로 타고난 듯했다. 그리고 그 운동 자체의 순수한 즐거움에다, 아마 에드먼드가 곁에서 돌봐 주고 일러 주는 것이 얼마간 보태졌을 테고, 이른 진척으로 여느 제 또래 여자들을 훨씬 앞선다는 확신이 또 얼마간 보태져, 그녀는 말에서 내리기를 꺼렸다. 패니는 채비를 갖추고 기다리고 있었고, 노리스 부인은 어서 가지 않는다고 그녀를 나무라기 시작했건만, 여전히 말이 왔다는 기별도, 에드먼드가 나타날 낌새도 없었다. 이모를 피하고 그를 찾을 겸, 그녀는 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n두 집은 반 마일도 채 떨어져 있지 않았으나 서로 마주 보이지는 않았다. 그래도 현관문에서 오십 야드쯤 걸어 나가면 공원 아래쪽을 굽어보며, 마을 길 너머로 완만히 솟은 목사관과 그 딸린 땅 전부를 한눈에 담을 수 있었다. 그리고 그랜트 박사의 풀밭에서 그녀는 이내 그 무리를 보았다—에드먼드와 크로퍼드 양은 둘 다 말을 타고 나란히 달리고 있었고, 그랜트 박사 부부와 크로퍼드 씨는 마부 두어 명과 함께 둘러서서 구경하고 있었다. 그녀 눈에는 다들 한 가지에 마음을 쏟는 흐뭇한 무리로 보였다. 즐거운 게 분명했다. 그 흥겨운 웃음소리가 그녀 있는 데까지 올라왔으니 말이다. 그것은 그녀를 흥겹게 하는 소리가 아니었다. 그녀는 에드먼드가 저를 잊었다는 게 의아했고, 가슴이 저릿했다. 그녀는 그 풀밭에서 눈을 뗄 수가 없었다. 거기서 벌어지는 모든 것을 지켜보지 않을 수 없었다. 처음에 크로퍼드 양과 그 동행은 작지 않은 그 들판을 걸음걸이로 한 바퀴 돌았다. 이윽고 그녀가 그러자고 청한 듯, 둘은 가벼운 구보로 접어들었다. 그리고 겁 많은 천성의 패니에게는, 그녀가 얼마나 말 위에 잘 앉아 있는지가 더없이 놀라워 보였다. 몇 분 뒤 둘은 완전히 멈춰 섰다. 에드먼드가 그녀 바짝 곁에 있었다. 그가 그녀에게 말을 건네고 있었다. 그가 고삐 다루는 법을 일러 주고 있는 게 분명했다. 그가 그녀의 손을 잡고 있었다. 그녀는 그것을 보았다—아니, 눈이 미치지 못하는 것을 상상이 채워 넣었는지도 모른다. 이 모든 것을 그녀가 이상히 여겨서는 안 될 일이었다. 에드먼드가 남에게 쓸모 있는 사람이 되고 그 착한 마음씨를 아무에게나 보여 주는 것보다 더 자연스러운 일이 어디 있겠는가? 그래도 그녀는, 크로퍼드 씨가 그 수고를 대신 덜어 줄 수도 있었을 텐데, 오라비 된 사람이 몸소 그리하는 것이 유독 온당하고 어울렸을 텐데 하는 생각이 들지 않을 수 없었다. 하지만 크로퍼드 씨는 그토록 자랑하는 착한 마음씨에도, 그 온갖 마차 모는 솜씨에도, 아마 그 일에 대해서는 아무것도 모를뿐더러, 에드먼드에 견주면 몸소 나서는 친절이라곤 없는 사람이었다. 그녀는 그런 이중의 노역을 지는 암말이 좀 딱하다는 생각마저 들기 시작했다. 저는 잊혔을망정, 그 딱한 암말만은 누가 좀 기억해 줘야 마땅했다.\n\n이쪽저쪽을 향한 그녀의 마음은, 풀밭의 무리가 흩어지고, 크로퍼드 양은 여전히 말을 탄 채였으나 에드먼드가 걸어서 곁을 따르며, 문을 지나 좁은 길로, 그리고 공원 안으로 들어서서 그녀가 서 있는 자리 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 보자, 이내 얼마간 가라앉았다. 그러자 이번엔 제가 무례하고 안달하는 것처럼 비칠까 봐 겁이 났다. 그래서 그런 의심을 사지 않으려 잔뜩 마음을 졸이며 그들을 맞으러 걸어갔다.\n\n“프라이스 양,” 목소리가 겨우 닿을 만한 데 이르자마자 크로퍼드 양이 말했다. “기다리게 해 드린 데 대해 제 입으로 사과하러 왔어요. 하지만 저로선 세상에 변명할 말이 하나도 없네요—너무 늦은 줄도, 제가 아주 못되게 굴고 있다는 것도 다 알고 있었으니까요. 그러니 부디 저를 용서해 주세요. 이기심이란 늘 용서받아야 하는 법이잖아요. 고칠 가망이 없는 병이니까요.”\n\n패니의 대답은 더없이 정중했고, 에드먼드도 그녀가 급할 리 없다고 거들었다. “제 사촌이 여느 때 다니는 것보다 두 배는 멀리 타고도 남을 만큼 시간이 넉넉하니까요.” 그가 말했다. “게다가 크로퍼드 양은 이 애가 반 시간 일찍 나서는 걸 막아 준 셈이니, 오히려 이 애의 편안함을 도운 거예요. 이제 구름이 몰려오고 있으니, 그때 나섰다면 겪었을 더위에 시달릴 일이 없을 테니까요. 부디 크로퍼드 양께서나 이렇게 많이 운동하시고 지치지 않으시면 좋겠는데요. 이 집까지 걸어오시는 수고는 안 하셨어도 좋았을 텐데요.”\n\n“그중에 저를 지치게 하는 건 이 말에서 내리는 것뿐이랍니다, 정말이에요.” 그의 도움을 받아 훌쩍 내려서며 그녀가 말했다. “저는 아주 튼튼해요. 저를 지치게 하는 건 오직 하기 싫은 일을 하는 것뿐이거든요. 프라이스 양, 저는 아주 볼썽사나운 꼴로 양보하네요. 하지만 진심으로 즐거운 승마 되시길 빌어요. 그리고 이 사랑스럽고 유쾌하고 아름다운 짐승에 대해서는 좋은 소식만 듣게 되기를요.”\n\n제 말을 데리고 근처에서 기다리던 늙은 마부가 이제 그들과 합류하자, 패니는 제 말에 올려졌고, 그들은 공원의 다른 쪽으로 길을 나섰다. 뒤돌아보니 나머지 사람들이 마을 쪽으로 함께 언덕을 내려가고 있는 것이 보여, 그녀의 언짢은 마음은 좀처럼 가벼워지지 않았다. 게다가 곁을 따르던 마부도, 제 못지않은 관심으로 지켜본 크로퍼드 양의 뛰어난 승마 솜씨를 두고 이런저런 말을 늘어놓아, 그녀에게 별 도움이 되지 못했다.\n\n“저렇게 승마에 마음이 잘 붙는 아가씨를 뵈니 참 흐뭇합니다!” 그가 말했다. “저는 저보다 말을 잘 타는 분을 본 적이 없어요. 두려움이라곤 티끌만큼도 없어 보이더군요. 여섯 해 전, 다가오는 부활절이면 꼭 여섯 해가 되는데, 아가씨가 처음 배우실 때하곤 아주 딴판이었지요. 원, 저런! 토머스 경께서 처음 아가씨를 말에 태우셨을 때, 얼마나 벌벌 떠셨는지요!”\n\n응접실에서도 크로퍼드 양은 마찬가지로 찬사를 받았다. 타고나기를 힘과 용기를 갖추었다는 그 장점을 버트럼가 아가씨들은 한껏 알아주었다. 승마를 즐기는 그 마음은 저희 것과 같았고, 일찌감치 능숙해진 것도 저희와 같았으니, 그들은 그것을 칭찬하는 데서 큰 즐거움을 느꼈다.\n\n“그분이 말을 잘 탈 줄 알았어.” 줄리아가 말했다. “그럴 몸매를 타고났거든. 그 몸매가 오라비만큼이나 야무지잖아.”\n\n“그래,” 마리아가 거들었다. “게다가 기질도 그만큼 좋고, 성격의 활기도 똑같아. 승마를 잘하는 건 아무래도 마음가짐과 크게 상관이 있는 것 같아.”\n\n밤에 헤어질 때 에드먼드는 패니에게 이튿날 말을 탈 생각이냐고 물었다.\n\n“아뇨, 모르겠어요—오빠가 암말을 쓰실 거면 안 탈게요.” 그것이 그녀의 대답이었다.\n\n“나는 그 말이 조금도 필요 없어.” 그가 말했다. “다만 다음번에 네가 집에 있고 싶을 때가 있거든, 그때는 크로퍼드 양이 말을 좀 더 오래—한마디로 아침 내내 쓸 수 있으면 반가워하실 것 같아서 말이야. 그분은 맨스필드 공유지까지 가 보고 싶어 몹시 안달이거든. 그랜트 부인이 그 좋은 경치 이야기를 해 줬대. 그분이 거기까지 너끈히 다녀올 수 있으리라는 건 내가 의심치 않아. 하지만 그건 어느 아침이든 상관없어. 그분은 너를 방해하는 걸 몹시 미안해할 거야. 만약 그런다면 아주 잘못된 일이지. 그분은 그저 즐기려고 타는 거지만, 너는 건강을 위해 타는 거니까.”\n\n“저는 내일은 분명 안 탈래요.” 패니가 말했다. “요즘 너무 자주 나갔으니, 차라리 집에 있는 게 좋아요. 저 이제 아주 잘 걸을 만큼 튼튼한 거 아시잖아요.”\n\n에드먼드는 흡족한 낯빛이었고, 그것이 패니에게는 위안이 될 수밖에 없었다. 그리하여 맨스필드 공유지로의 승마는 이튿날 아침에 이루어졌다. 그 무리에는 그녀만 빼고 젊은 사람이 다 끼었고, 그때도 무척 즐거웠거니와 저녁의 이야기판에서 곱절로 다시 즐거웠다. 이런 종류의 성공한 나들이는 대개 또 다른 나들이를 부르는 법이라, 맨스필드 공유지에 다녀온 것이 그들 모두를 그 이튿날 또 어딘가로 가고 싶게 만들었다. 보여 줄 경치는 그 밖에도 많았다. 그리고 날은 더웠어도 그들이 가고 싶어 하는 데면 어디든 그늘진 오솔길이 있었다. 젊은 무리에게는 언제나 그늘진 오솔길이 마련되어 있는 법이다. 이런 식으로 나흘 아침이 잇따라, 크로퍼드 남매에게 그 고장을 보여 주고 가장 좋은 자리들을 대접하는 데 쓰였다. 만사가 척척 들어맞았다. 온통 흥과 화기뿐이었고, 더위조차 유쾌하게 이야깃거리로 삼을 만큼의 불편만을 안겨 줄 뿐이었다—나흘째 되던 날, 그 무리 가운데 한 사람의 행복이 몹시 흐려지기 전까지는 말이다. 버트럼 양이 바로 그 사람이었다. 에드먼드와 줄리아는 목사관 만찬에 초대받았으나 그녀는 빠졌다. 그것은 그날 공원 저택에 얼마간 들르리라 예상되던 러시워스 씨를 생각해 그랜트 부인이 더없이 좋은 뜻으로 마련한 일이었다. 그러나 그것은 몹시 뼈아픈 상처로 느껴졌고, 그녀는 집에 이를 때까지 제 분함과 노여움을 감추느라 그 고운 몸가짐을 여간 쥐어짜지 않았다. 그런데 러시워스 씨가 오지 않는 바람에 상처는 더 커졌고, 그녀는 그를 쥐고 흔드는 힘을 내보이는 후련함조차 누리지 못했다. 그녀가 할 수 있는 것이라곤 어머니와 이모와 사촌에게 뾰로통하게 굴며, 저녁 만찬과 후식 자리에 될 수 있는 대로 짙은 그늘을 드리우는 것뿐이었다.\n\n열 시에서 열한 시 사이에 에드먼드와 줄리아가 응접실로 들어섰다. 저녁 공기에 상기되어 발그레하니 쾌활한 그 모습은, 거기 앉아 있는 세 부인에게서 그들이 본 것과는 정반대였다. 마리아는 좀처럼 책에서 눈을 떼려 하지 않았고, 버트럼 부인은 반쯤 졸고 있었으며, 노리스 부인마저 조카딸의 언짢은 기색에 심기가 흐트러진 데다, 만찬에 대해 한두 마디 물었으나 곧바로 대꾸를 못 얻은 터라, 이제 더는 입을 열지 않기로 작정한 듯했다. 오누이는 몇 분 동안 그 밤을 기리고 별에 대한 소감을 나누느라 저희 말고 다른 데는 생각이 미치지 못했다. 그러나 처음으로 말이 끊긴 틈에, 에드먼드가 둘러보며 말했다. “그런데 패니는 어디 있어요? 자러 갔나요?”\n\n“아니, 내가 알기론 아니다.” 노리스 부인이 대답했다. “조금 전까지 여기 있었는데.”\n\n아주 긴 방의 저쪽 끝에서 들려온 그녀 자신의 부드러운 목소리가, 그녀가 소파에 있음을 그들에게 알려 주었다. 노리스 부인이 나무라기 시작했다.\n\n“패니, 저녁 내내 소파에서 빈둥거리다니 참 어리석은 버릇이다. 왜 이리 와서 앉아 우리처럼 뭐라도 하지 못하니? 네 일감이 없으면 자선 바구니에서 대 줄 수 있다. 지난주에 사 온 새 옥양목이 그대로 있어. 아직 손도 안 댔지. 그거 재단하느라 내 허리가 부러지는 줄 알았다. 너는 남을 생각할 줄 알아야 해. 그리고 내 말 명심해라, 젊은 사람이 늘 소파에 늘어져 있는 건 참 못된 버릇이야.”\n\n이 말이 절반도 끝나기 전에 패니는 식탁의 제자리로 돌아와 다시 일감을 집어 들었다. 그리고 그날의 즐거움으로 한껏 기분이 좋았던 줄리아가, 그녀를 위해 공정하게 이렇게 외쳐 주었다. “이모, 이 말은 해야겠어요. 패니는 이 집 누구 못지않게 소파에 안 있는 애예요.”\n\n“패니,” 그녀를 유심히 바라본 뒤 에드먼드가 말했다. “너 틀림없이 머리가 아프구나.”\n\n그녀는 그것을 부인할 수 없었으나, 그리 심하지는 않다고 했다.\n\n“나는 도무지 못 믿겠는데.” 그가 대꾸했다. “네 낯빛을 내가 너무 잘 알거든. 언제부터 아팠니?”\n\n“저녁 먹기 조금 전부터요. 그냥 더위 탓이에요.”\n\n“더위 속에 나갔었니?”\n\n“나가다니! 그럼, 나갔고말고.” 노리스 부인이 말했다. “이렇게 좋은 날 저 애를 안에만 있게 할 셈이었니? 우리 모두 나갔잖아? 네 어머니마저 오늘 한 시간 넘게 나가 계셨는데.”\n\n“그래, 정말 그랬단다, 에드먼드.” 노리스 부인이 패니를 매섭게 나무라는 통에 잠이 확 깬 부인이 거들었다. “나도 한 시간 넘게 나가 있었어. 패니가 장미를 자르는 동안 나는 화원에 사십오 분이나 앉아 있었지. 정말이지 참 좋더구나. 그런데 무척 덥긴 했어. 정자 안은 그늘이 넉넉했지만, 다시 집으로 돌아올 일이 어찌나 두렵던지.”\n\n“패니가 장미를 자르고 있었어요?”\n\n“그래, 아마 올해 마지막 장미일 게다. 딱한 것! 저 앤 무척 덥다고 느꼈지. 하지만 워낙 활짝 피어 버려서 더는 미룰 수가 없었단다.”\n\n“어쩔 도리가 없었지, 그야.” 노리스 부인이 좀 누그러진 목소리로 다시 끼어들었다. “하지만 저 애 두통이 바로 그때 든 건 아닌가 싶은데, 동생. 뜨거운 볕 아래 서서 몸을 굽히고 있는 것만큼 두통 나기 쉬운 것도 없거든. 그래도 내일이면 나을 게다. 저 애한테 동생 그 방향 식초를 좀 주지 그래. 나는 늘 내 것 채워 두는 걸 잊어버려서.”\n\n“저 앤 그걸 갖고 있어요.” 버트럼 부인이 말했다. “두 번째로 언니 집에서 돌아온 뒤로 죽 지니고 있는걸.”\n\n“뭐라고요!” 에드먼드가 외쳤다. “그럼 장미를 자른 데다 걷기까지 했다는 거예요? 뜨거운 공원을 가로질러 이모 댁까지, 그것도 두 번씩이나요? 머리가 아플 만도 하지요.”\n\n노리스 부인은 줄리아와 이야기하느라 듣지 못했다.\n\n“저 애한테 너무 무리일까 봐 걱정은 했다.” 버트럼 부인이 말했다. “그런데 장미를 다 자르고 나니 네 이모가 그걸 갖고 싶어 했고, 그러면 또 집까지 가져다줘야 하잖니.”\n\n“그런데 두 번씩이나 다녀와야 할 만큼 장미가 많았어요?”\n\n“아니. 다만 그걸 말리려고 빈방에 넣어 두기로 했는데, 하필 패니가 그 방문 잠그고 열쇠 챙겨 오는 걸 깜박하는 바람에, 다시 다녀올 수밖에 없었단다.”\n\n에드먼드는 일어나 방 안을 서성이며 말했다. “그런 심부름에 패니 말고는 부릴 사람이 없었나요? 정말이지 이모, 일 처리가 너무 서툴렀어요.”\n\n“대체 그걸 어떻게 하면 더 잘할 수 있었는지 나는 도무지 모르겠구나.” 더는 못 들은 척할 수 없게 된 노리스 부인이 외쳤다. “정말이지 내가 몸소 갔다면 몰라도. 하지만 나는 한꺼번에 두 군데 있을 수는 없잖니. 게다가 바로 그때 나는 네 어머니 부탁으로 그 댁 젖짜기 하녀 일 때문에 그린 씨와 이야기하고 있었고, 존 그룸한테는 그 아들 일로 제프리스 부인께 편지를 써 주마 약속한 터라, 그 딱한 사람이 반 시간이나 나를 기다리고 있었단다. 나더러 어느 자리에서든 제 몸 사린다고 나무랄 사람은 없을 게다. 하지만 정말이지 나라고 모든 걸 한꺼번에 할 수는 없어. 그리고 패니가 나 대신 우리 집까지 잠깐 다녀오는 거라면—사분의 일 마일이 조금 넘을 뿐인데—그걸 시킨 게 무리였다고는 도무지 생각지 않는다. 나야말로 이르나 늦으나, 그래, 어떤 날씨에도, 하루 세 번씩 그 길을 오가면서 한마디 군소리도 안 하는데, 그게 몇 번이더냐?”\n\n“패니가 이모 힘의 절반만 있어도 좋겠어요.”\n\n“패니가 운동을 좀 더 규칙적으로 했더라면 그리 쉬 지쳐 나가떨어지진 않았을 게다. 요즘 오래도록 말을 안 탔으니, 나는 저 애가 말을 안 탈 때는 마땅히 걸어야 한다고 본다. 만약 그 전에 말을 탔더라면 나도 그런 걸 안 시켰겠지. 하지만 장미 사이에서 몸을 굽히고 난 뒤라 오히려 저 애한테 좋을 거라 여겼단다. 그런 종류의 고단함 뒤에는 걷기만큼 개운한 게 없거든. 그리고 볕이 세긴 했어도 그리 대단히 덥지는 않았어. 우리끼리 이야기지만, 에드먼드,” 하며 그녀는 그 어머니 쪽으로 의미심장하게 고갯짓을 했다. “저 애를 탈 나게 한 건 장미를 자르고 화원에서 어정거린 일이란다.”\n\n“정말이지 그런 것 같아 걱정이구나.” 그 말을 엿들은, 한결 솔직한 버트럼 부인이 말했다. “저 애가 거기서 두통이 든 게 아닌가 나는 몹시 걱정이야. 그 더위는 누구든 잡을 만했으니까. 나조차 겨우겨우 견딜 지경이었어. 앉아서 퍼그를 부르며 그 녀석이 화단에 못 들어가게 막는 것만으로도 나한테는 벅찰 뻔했다니까.”\n\n에드먼드는 두 부인 어느 쪽에게도 더는 말하지 않았다. 다만 조용히 다른 탁자로 가서, 아직 남아 있던 야식 쟁반에서 마데이라 한 잔을 가져다 패니에게 건네고, 그 대부분을 마시게 했다. 그녀는 그것을 사양할 수 있기를 바랐다. 그러나 갖가지 감정이 자아낸 눈물 탓에, 말을 하기보다 차라리 삼키는 편이 더 수월했다.\n\n에드먼드는 제 어머니와 이모에게 언짢았으나, 저 자신에게는 더욱 화가 났다. 그녀를 잊고 있었던 제 잘못이 그들이 저지른 그 무엇보다 더 나빴다. 그녀가 제대로 배려받았더라면 이런 일은 하나도 일어나지 않았을 것이다. 그런데 그녀는 나흘을 내리, 함께할 동무도 운동도 고를 길 없이, 게다가 그 지각없는 이모들이 무엇을 시키든 그것을 피할 핑계 하나 없이 방치되어 있었다. 그녀가 나흘을 내리 말 탈 기회를 갖지 못했다는 것을 떠올리자 그는 부끄러웠다. 그리고 크로퍼드 양의 즐거움을 가로막는 것이 아무리 내키지 않더라도, 다시는 이런 일이 없게 하리라 아주 단단히 마음먹었다.\n\n패니는 공원에 처음 오던 날 저녁만큼이나 벅찬 가슴을 안고 잠자리에 들었다. 아마 그 심사가 그녀의 몸살에도 한몫했을 것이다. 며칠 전부터 그녀는 방치되었다고 느끼며 불만과 시샘에 맞서 애써 싸워 오던 터였으니 말이다. 남의 눈에 띄지 않으려 물러나 앉았던 그 소파에 기대어 있는 동안, 마음의 아픔은 머리의 아픔을 훨씬 웃돌았다. 그리고 그때 에드먼드의 다정함이 불러온 그 갑작스러운 변화에, 그녀는 제 몸을 어떻게 가누어야 할지조차 알기 어려웠다.\n\n# 제8장\n\n패니의 승마는 바로 이튿날 다시 시작되었다. 그리고 상쾌하고 산뜻한 아침인 데다 요 며칠 날씨보다 덜 더웠으므로, 에드먼드는 건강으로 보나 즐거움으로 보나 그녀의 손실이 이내 메워지리라 믿었다. 그녀가 나가 있는 동안 러시워스 씨가 제 어머니를 모시고 도착했다. 어머니는 예를 갖추러, 특히 그 예의를 보이러 온 것이었는데, 보름 전에 시작되었다가 그 뒤 그녀가 집을 비운 탓에 그동안 잠들어 있던 소더턴 방문 계획을 어서 실행에 옮기자고 채근하는 것이 그 방식이었다. 노리스 부인과 조카딸들은 그 계획이 되살아난 것을 다들 무척 반겼고, 크로퍼드 씨의 형편이 비는 것을 조건으로 이른 날짜가 정해져 합의되었다. 아가씨들은 그 단서를 잊지 않았다. 노리스 부인은 그가 그러리라고 선뜻 장담하려 들었으나, 그들은 그런 무람없는 짓을 허락하지도, 그런 위험을 무릅쓰지도 않으려 했다. 그리하여 마침내 버트럼 양의 귀띔을 받아, 러시워스 씨는 자기가 곧장 목사관으로 걸어 내려가 크로퍼드 씨를 찾아, 수요일이 그에게 괜찮은지 아닌지를 물어보는 것이 가장 온당한 일임을 깨달았다.\n\n그가 돌아오기 전에 그랜트 부인과 크로퍼드 양이 들어왔다. 얼마간 밖에 나가 있었던 데다 다른 길로 저택에 온 터라, 그들은 그를 마주치지 못했다. 그래도 그가 크로퍼드 씨를 집에서 만나게 되리라는 든든한 기대가 오갔다. 소더턴 계획이야 물론 화제에 올랐다. 실로 다른 이야기가 나오기란 좀처럼 있을 수 없는 일이었다. 노리스 부인이 그 일로 잔뜩 들떠 있었고, 게다가 러시워스 부인은—뜻은 착하나 격식만 차리며 장황하게 늘어놓는 젠체하는 여자로, 오직 저와 제 아들의 일에 관계될 때만 무언가를 대수로이 여기는 사람이었는데—여태 버트럼 부인더러 함께 가자고 조르기를 그치지 않고 있었으니 말이다. 버트럼 부인은 줄곧 사양했다. 그러나 그 태평한 거절 태도 탓에 러시워스 부인은 그녀가 여전히 오고 싶어 한다고 여겼고, 마침내 노리스 부인의 더 많은 말수와 더 큰 목소리가 그녀에게 진실을 일깨워 주었다.\n\n“우리 동생한테는 그 여행이 너무 벅찰 거예요, 정말 너무나도요, 러시워스 부인. 거기까지 십 마일, 돌아오는 데 또 십 마일이잖아요. 이번만은 우리 동생을 좀 봐주시고, 동생 없이 우리 사랑스러운 두 딸과 저만으로 만족해 주세요. 소더턴이야말로 동생이 그토록 멀리 가고 싶은 마음이 들 만한 유일한 곳이지만, 정말이지 그럴 수는 없답니다. 아시다시피 동생한테는 패니 프라이스라는 말벗이 있으니, 다 잘될 거예요. 그리고 에드먼드로 말하면, 지금 제 입으로 말할 처지가 아니니 제가 대신 장담하는데, 그 애도 이 나들이에 끼는 걸 더없이 기뻐할 거예요. 아시다시피 그 앤 말을 타고 가면 되고요.”\n\n버트럼 부인이 집에 남는 것에 물러설 수밖에 없게 된 러시워스 부인은 그저 아쉬워할 뿐이었다. “준남작 부인이 함께 못 가시는 건 큰 손실이고, 아직 소더턴에 한 번도 와 본 적 없는 저 젊은 아가씨, 프라이스 양도 뵈었으면 더없이 기뻤을 텐데, 그분이 그곳을 못 보게 되는 게 참 안타깝네요.”\n\n“참 친절하시네요, 온통 친절뿐이세요, 부인.” 노리스 부인이 외쳤다. “하지만 패니로 말하면, 소더턴을 볼 기회야 앞으로 얼마든지 있을 거예요. 그 앤 앞날이 창창하니까요. 그러니 지금 가는 건 도무지 될 일이 아니에요. 버트럼 부인이 그 애를 도저히 내줄 수 없거든요.”\n\n“아, 안 돼요! 나는 패니 없이는 못 지내요.”\n\n러시워스 부인은 다음으로, 누구나 소더턴을 보고 싶어 할 게 틀림없다는 확신에서, 크로퍼드 양까지 초대에 넣으려 했다. 그랜트 부인은 이웃에 이사 온 러시워스 부인을 굳이 찾아가 인사한 적이 없던 터라 제 몫으로는 정중히 사양했으나, 아우를 위해서라면 어떤 즐거움이든 잡아 주고 싶었다. 그리하여 메리는 적당히 조름과 설득을 받고는 오래지 않아 제 몫의 호의를 받아들였다. 러시워스 씨는 목사관에서 성공하여 돌아왔다. 그리고 에드먼드는 마침맞게 나타나, 수요일로 정해진 일을 전해 듣고, 러시워스 부인을 마차까지 배웅한 뒤, 다른 두 부인과 함께 공원을 절반쯤 걸어 내려갔다.\n\n아침식사방으로 돌아온 그는, 노리스 부인이 크로퍼드 양이 이 나들이에 끼는 것이 바람직한지 아닌지, 아니면 그녀가 없어도 그 오라비의 사륜마차가 다 차지는 않을지를 두고 마음을 정하려 애쓰는 것을 보았다. 버트럼가 아가씨들은 그 걱정을 비웃으며, 마부석은 빼고도 사륜마차 안에 넉넉히 넷은 탈 수 있고, 마부석에는 한 사람이 그와 함께 타면 된다고 일러 주었다.\n\n“그런데 왜 꼭,” 에드먼드가 말했다. “크로퍼드의 마차, 그것도 그의 마차만 써야 하는 거지? 어머니 마차는 왜 못 쓰는 거야? 며칠 전 이 계획이 처음 나왔을 때도, 나는 왜 이 집 식구의 방문에 이 집 식구의 마차를 쓰지 않는 건지 도무지 이해가 안 갔거든.”\n\n“뭐라고!” 줄리아가 외쳤다. “이 날씨에 셋이 역마차 안에 갇혀 끼어 가라고, 사륜마차에 자리가 있는데? 아니, 에드먼드, 그건 좀 아니야.”\n\n“게다가,” 마리아가 말했다. “크로퍼드 씨는 우리를 태워 갈 걸로 믿고 있어. 처음에 그런 이야기가 오갔으니, 그 사람은 그걸 약속으로 여기고 당연히 요구할걸.”\n\n“그리고 얘, 에드먼드,” 노리스 부인이 거들었다. “하나면 될 걸 마차를 둘씩 끌고 나가는 건 공연한 수고야. 게다가 우리끼리 얘기지만, 마부가 여기서 소더턴 사이 길을 그리 좋아하질 않아. 좁은 길이 마차를 긁어 댄다고 늘 지독히 투덜대거든. 그리고 사랑하는 토머스 경이 돌아오셨을 때 니스칠이 죄다 긁혀 벗겨진 걸 보시게 하고 싶지야 않잖니.”\n\n“그건 크로퍼드 씨 마차를 쓸 그리 훌륭한 이유는 못 돼.” 마리아가 말했다. “하지만 사실은, 윌콕스가 멍청한 늙은이라 몰 줄을 몰라서 그래. 수요일에 좁은 길 때문에 겪을 불편은 하나도 없을 거라고 내가 장담해.”\n\n“마부석에 타는 게,” 에드먼드가 말했다. “뭐 고생스러울 것도, 언짢을 것도 없겠지.”\n\n“언짢다니!” 마리아가 외쳤다. “어머나! 오히려 다들 제일 좋아하는 자리로 칠걸. 시골 경치를 보는 걸로 치면 견줄 데가 없거든. 아마 크로퍼드 양이 손수 마부석을 고를 거야.”\n\n“그럼 패니가 너희랑 같이 가는 데는 반대할 게 없겠네. 너희한테 그 애 자리가 있는 건 의심할 것도 없고.”\n\n“패니라니!” 노리스 부인이 되뇌었다. “얘 에드먼드, 그 애가 우리랑 같이 간다는 얘기는 아예 없다. 그 앤 이모하고 집에 있어. 내가 러시워스 부인한테 그렇게 말해 뒀어. 그 애는 갈 사람으로 치지도 않았다고.”\n\n“어머니, 제 생각엔,” 그가 제 어머니를 향해 말했다. “어머니가 패니를 이 나들이에서 빼고 싶어 하실 까닭이 없을 것 같아요. 어머니 자신의, 어머니의 편안함에 관계된 것 말고는요. 어머니가 그 애 없이 지내실 수 있다면, 굳이 그 애를 집에 붙들어 두고 싶어 하지는 않으실 거잖아요?”\n\n“물론 아니지. 하지만 나는 그 애 없이는 못 지내.”\n\n“제가 어머니와 함께 집에 남으면 지내실 수 있어요. 저는 그럴 작정이거든요.”\n\n이 말에 여기저기서 반대의 아우성이 터졌다. “그래,” 그가 말을 이었다. “내가 갈 필요는 없어. 나는 집에 남을 참이야. 패니는 소더턴을 몹시 보고 싶어 해. 그 애가 그걸 무척 바란다는 걸 나는 알아. 그 앤 그런 즐거움을 좀처럼 누리지 못하잖아. 그러니 이모, 이번엔 그 애한테 그 기쁨을 안겨 주고 싶으실 거예요, 그렇죠?”\n\n“아, 그럼! 네 이모만 반대하지 않는다면, 나야 무척 기쁘지.”\n\n노리스 부인은 남아 있을 수 있는 단 하나의 반대 이유를 대는 데 아주 재발랐다—패니가 못 간다고 러시워스 부인에게 딱 잘라 장담해 놓았으니, 이제 와 그 애를 데려가면 그 꼴이 여간 이상해 보이지 않으리라는 것이었다. 그녀에게는 그것이 도무지 넘을 수 없는 난관 같았다. 그 꼴이 얼마나 이상해 보이겠는가! 그것은 너무나 무람없고, 그 몸가짐이 좋은 가정교육과 세심함의 본보기와도 같은 러시워스 부인에 대한 결례에 가까운 일이라, 그녀는 정말이지 그럴 엄두가 나지 않았다. 노리스 부인은 패니에게 아무 정도 없었고, 어느 때든 그 애의 즐거움을 마련해 줄 마음도 없었다. 그러나 지금 에드먼드에게 맞서는 것은, 다른 무엇보다 제 계획에 대한 편애에서—그것이 바로 제 계획이었기에—비롯된 것이었다. 그녀는 자기가 모든 것을 더없이 잘 짜 놓았으니, 어떤 변경이든 반드시 더 나빠지는 것이라 느꼈다. 그래서 에드먼드가, 그녀가 제 말을 들어줄 때에 이르러, 러시워스 부인 일로는 마음 졸일 것 없다고—그녀와 함께 현관을 지나며 프라이스 양도 아마 이 나들이에 낄 사람이라고 넌지시 말할 기회를 잡았고, 그 자리에서 사촌을 위한 아주 충분한 초대를 곧바로 받았노라고—대꾸하자, 노리스 부인은 너무 언짢은 나머지 곱게 물러서지 못하고, 그저 이렇게 말할 뿐이었다. “그래, 그래, 네 마음대로 하렴. 네 뜻대로 정하려무나. 나야 정말이지 아무래도 상관없으니까.”\n\n“참 이상하네.” 마리아가 말했다. “패니 대신 오빠가 집에 남다니.”\n\n“그 앤 오빠한테 정말 크게 고마워해야 마땅해.” 줄리아가 덧붙이고는, 저야말로 집에 남겠다고 나서야 마땅하다는 자각에서, 말하기가 무섭게 서둘러 방을 나갔다.\n\n“패니는 이 일에 걸맞은 만큼 고마워할 거야.” 그것이 에드먼드의 유일한 대답이었고, 그 화제는 그것으로 끝났다.\n\n막상 그 계획을 들었을 때 패니의 고마움은 실은 그 기쁨보다 훨씬 컸다. 그녀는 에드먼드의 친절을, 그가—그녀의 애틋한 정을 짐작조차 못 하는 그가—알아챌 수 있는 그 온 감수성으로, 아니 그 이상으로 느꼈다. 그러나 그가 저 때문에 어떤 즐거움을 포기한다는 것은 그녀에게 아픔이었고, 그가 없다면 소더턴을 보는 제 흡족함도 아무것도 아닐 터였다.\n\n두 맨스필드 집안이 다음번에 만났을 때 그 계획에 또 하나의 변경이 생겼는데, 다들 반겨 받아들인 변경이었다. 그랜트 부인이 제 아들 대신 그날 버트럼 부인의 말벗이 되겠다고 자청했고, 그랜트 박사는 만찬 자리에 합류하기로 했다. 버트럼 부인은 그리되는 것을 무척 흡족해했고, 아가씨들은 다시 기운이 났다. 에드먼드마저, 자기를 그 나들이의 한 몫으로 되돌려 준 그 마련을 무척 고마워했다. 그리고 노리스 부인은 그것을 훌륭한 방안이라 여겨 혀끝에 올려 두고 막 제안하려던 참이었는데, 그때 그랜트 부인이 먼저 말을 꺼낸 것이었다.\n\n수요일은 날이 좋았고, 아침식사가 끝나고 얼마 안 되어 사륜마차가 도착했다. 크로퍼드 씨가 누이들을 태우고 몰고 온 것이었다. 다들 채비가 되어 있었으므로, 그랜트 부인이 내리고 나머지가 제자리에 앉는 것 말고는 할 일이 없었다. 그 모든 자리 중의 자리, 뭇사람이 부러워하는 그 자리, 영예의 자리만이 임자가 없었다. 그것은 누구의 복된 몫으로 떨어질 것인가? 버트럼가 두 아가씨가 저마다 어떻게 하면 가장 그럴싸하게, 남을 배려하는 척하며 그 자리를 차지할까 궁리하는 사이, 그랜트 부인이 마차에서 내리며 던진 한마디로 그 문제는 매듭지어졌다. “너희가 다섯이니, 하나는 헨리와 함께 앉는 게 낫겠다. 그리고 줄리아, 요전에 마차를 몰 줄 알면 좋겠다고 했으니, 이번이 한 수 배우기에 좋은 기회일 것 같구나.”\n\n복된 줄리아! 가엾은 마리아! 앞의 사람은 순식간에 마부석에 올랐고, 뒤의 사람은 침울함과 굴욕 속에 안쪽 자리에 앉았다. 그리고 마차는 남은 두 부인의 축원과, 여주인의 품에 안긴 퍼그의 짖는 소리 속에 출발했다.\n\n그들의 길은 정겨운 시골을 가로질렀다. 그리고 승마를 멀리 다녀 본 적이 없던 패니는 이내 제가 아는 데를 벗어났고, 새로운 것을 다 눈여겨보고 예쁜 것을 다 감탄하며 무척 행복해했다. 그녀는 남들의 이야기에 끼라는 청을 자주 받지도 않았고, 그러기를 바라지도 않았다. 제 생각과 사색이 늘 그녀의 가장 좋은 벗이었으니 말이다. 그리고 시골의 모습, 길이 뻗은 방향, 흙의 차이, 수확의 형편, 오두막들, 소들, 아이들을 눈여겨보면서, 그녀는 제가 느낀 것을 에드먼드에게 이야기할 수 있었더라면 한층 더했을 그런 즐거움을 누렸다. 그것이야말로 그녀와, 곁에 앉은 그 여인 사이의 유일한 닮은 점이었다. 에드먼드를 소중히 여기는 것 말고는 모든 면에서 크로퍼드 양은 그녀와 아주 딴판이었다. 그녀에게는 패니와 같은 취향의, 마음의, 감정의 섬세함이 조금도 없었다. 그녀는 자연을, 생명 없는 자연을, 별 눈여겨봄 없이 보았다. 그녀의 관심은 온통 남자와 여자에게 쏠려 있었고, 그 재능은 가볍고 발랄한 쪽에 있었다. 그렇지만 뒤에 길이 얼마간 트여 있을 때나, 그가 제법 높은 언덕을 오르며 그들을 따라잡을 때, 뒤돌아 에드먼드를 찾는 데서만은 둘의 마음이 하나가 되어, “저기 오빠가 있다”는 말이 한꺼번에 두 사람 입에서 터져 나오기를 한두 번이 아니었다.\n\n처음 칠 마일 동안 버트럼 양에게는 마음 편할 구석이 거의 없었다. 그녀의 시야는 늘 크로퍼드 씨와 제 동생이 나란히 앉아 이야기와 웃음에 겨워 있는 데서 끝났다. 그가 미소 지으며 줄리아 쪽으로 돌아설 때 그 생기 넘치는 옆모습만 보이거나, 동생의 웃음소리가 들려오는 것은, 그녀 자신의 예의범절로 겨우 다독일 수 있을 뿐인 끊임없는 짜증의 원천이었다. 줄리아가 뒤돌아볼 때면 그 얼굴은 환희에 차 있었고, 그들에게 말을 걸 때면 더없이 들뜬 목소리였다. “경치가 어찌나 좋은지, 다들 함께 봤으면 좋겠다”는 둥. 그러나 그녀가 자리를 바꿔 주겠다고 내민 유일한 제의는, 긴 언덕 꼭대기에 이르렀을 때 크로퍼드 양에게 건넨 것으로, 이보다 더 마음 동하지 않는 것도 없었다. “여기 경치가 확 트이네요. 크로퍼드 양이 제 자리에 앉으시면 좋을 텐데, 제가 아무리 권해도 안 앉으실 거죠?” 그리고 크로퍼드 양이 미처 대답하기도 전에 마차는 다시 제법 빠르게 달리고 있었다.\n\n소더턴에 얽힌 것들의 자장 안에 들어서자 버트럼 양에게는 형편이 나아졌다. 그녀는 이를테면 활에 시위를 둘 가진 셈이었으니 말이다. 그녀에게는 러시워스에 대한 감정과 크로퍼드에 대한 감정이 있었는데, 소더턴 근방에서는 앞의 것이 자못 힘을 떨쳤다. 러시워스 씨의 지위는 곧 그녀의 것이었다. 그녀는 크로퍼드 양에게 “저 숲이 소더턴에 딸린 것”이라고, “이제 길 양쪽이 다 러시워스 씨 소유일 거라 알고 있다”고 지나가듯 말하면서도 가슴이 부풀지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 그 즐거움은, 온갖 영주 재판권과 장원 재판권까지 딸린, 그 집안의 당당한 자유보유 대저택이자 유서 깊은 장원 저택에 다가갈수록 점점 커져 갔다.\n\n“이제 험한 길은 더 없을 거예요, 크로퍼드 양. 고생은 끝났어요. 나머지 길은 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 좋답니다. 러시워스 씨가 이 영지를 물려받은 뒤로 손봐 놓은 거예요. 여기서부터 마을이 시작돼요. 저 오두막들은 정말 볼썽사납죠. 저 교회 첨탑은 유난히 잘생겼다고들 해요. 오래된 데서 흔히 그러듯 교회가 큰 저택에 바싹 붙어 있지 않아서 다행이에요. 종소리의 성가심이란 여간 끔찍하지 않을 테니까요. 저기 목사관이 있네요. 말끔해 보이는 집이죠. 그 목사와 아내는 아주 반듯한 사람들이라고 들었어요. 저건 그 집안 누군가가 지은 구빈원이에요. 오른쪽은 집사의 집이고요. 아주 존경할 만한 사람이랍니다. 이제 정문에 다다르는데, 그래도 공원을 지나는 데만 아직 거의 일 마일이 남았어요. 이쪽 끝은 보시다시피 흉하진 않죠. 훌륭한 나무들도 좀 있고요. 하지만 저택의 자리가 지독해요. 거기까지 반 마일을 내리막으로 내려가야 하는데, 참 안타까운 일이에요. 진입로만 더 나았어도 흉한 곳은 아니었을 테니까요.”\n\n크로퍼드 양은 지체 없이 감탄했다. 그녀는 버트럼 양의 심정을 제법 알아챘고, 그 즐거움을 한껏 북돋우는 것을 하나의 도리로 삼았다. 노리스 부인은 온통 기쁨과 수다뿐이었다. 패니마저 감탄에 보탤 말이 얼마간 있었고, 흐뭇하게 들어줄 만도 했다. 그녀의 눈은 손닿는 데 있는 모든 것을 열심히 담아 들이고 있었다. 그리고 애써 저택을 눈에 담고는, “저건 존경심 없이는 바라볼 수 없는 종류의 건물”이라고 말한 뒤, 이렇게 덧붙였다. “그런데, 가로수길은 어디 있어요? 저택이 동쪽을 바라보고 있네요. 그럼 가로수길은 그 뒤편에 있어야 할 텐데. 러시워스 씨가 서쪽 정면 이야기를 하셨잖아요.”\n\n“그래, 바로 저택 뒤에 있어. 조금 떨어진 데서 시작해서, 반 마일을 올라가 영지 맨 끝까지 이어지지. 여기서도 얼마간 보일 거야—더 멀리 있는 나무들이 좀. 온통 참나무란다.”\n\n버트럼 양은 이제, 러시워스 씨가 의견을 물었을 때는 아무것도 몰랐던 것을 두고 야무진 지식으로 이야기할 수 있었다. 그리고 정문 앞 널따란 돌계단으로 마차가 올라설 무렵, 그녀의 마음은 허영과 자만이 마련해 줄 수 있는 만큼의 흐뭇한 설렘으로 일렁이고 있었다.\n\n# 제9장\n\n러시워스 씨가 제 어여쁜 아가씨를 맞으러 문간에 나와 있었고, 온 일행은 그의 정중한 대접을 받았다. 응접실에서는 그 어머니가 그에 못지않은 다정함으로 그들을 맞았고, 버트럼 양은 두 사람 모두에게서 바랄 수 있는 온갖 각별한 대우를 받았다. 도착의 절차가 끝나자 우선 먹는 일부터 필요했다. 그리하여 문이 활짝 열리고, 그들은 한두 개의 중간 방을 지나 정해진 식당으로 안내되었는데, 거기엔 푸짐하고 우아한 다과가 차려져 있었다. 말도 많이 오갔고, 먹기도 많이 먹었으며, 만사가 순조로웠다. 이윽고 그날의 특별한 목적이 논의되었다. 크로퍼드 씨는 어떤 식으로 영지를 둘러보고 싶어 할까, 어떤 방식을 고를까? 러시워스 씨는 제 이륜 경마차를 들먹였다. 크로퍼드 씨는 둘보다 많이 태울 수 있는 마차 쪽이 더 바람직하다고 넌지시 일렀다. “다른 눈과 다른 판단의 도움을 스스로 저버리는 것은, 당장의 즐거움을 잃는 것 이상의 손해가 될 수도 있으니까요.”\n\n러시워스 부인은 경마차도 함께 끌고 가자고 제안했다. 그러나 이것은 좀처럼 나아진 안으로 받아들여지지 않았다. 아가씨들은 웃지도 입을 열지도 않았다. 그다음 그녀가 낸 제안, 곧 전에 와 본 적 없는 이들에게 저택을 구경시키자는 것은 좀 더 반겼다. 버트럼 양은 그 규모를 뽐낼 수 있어 흡족했고, 다들 무언가 하게 되어 반가웠던 것이다.\n\n그리하여 온 일행이 일어났고, 러시워스 부인의 안내로 여러 방을 두루 구경했다. 방들은 하나같이 천장이 높고 더러는 널찍했으며, 오십 년 전 취향으로 넉넉하게 꾸며져, 번들거리는 마룻바닥, 묵직한 마호가니, 값진 다마스크 천, 대리석, 금박, 조각 장식이 저마다 나름대로 아름다웠다. 그림도 많았고 더러 좋은 것도 있었으나, 대부분은 가문 초상화라 이제는 러시워스 부인 말고는 누구에게도 아무것도 아니었다. 그녀는 가정부가 가르쳐 줄 수 있는 것을 다 배우느라 무진 애를 쓴 터라, 이제 그 저택을 안내하는 데는 거의 가정부 못지않은 자격을 갖추고 있었다. 이 자리에서 그녀는 주로 크로퍼드 양과 패니에게 이야기를 건넸으나, 두 사람이 귀 기울이는 태도에는 견줄 수 없는 차이가 있었다. 큰 저택이라면 수십 채나 보았고 그중 어느 것에도 마음 두지 않는 크로퍼드 양은 그저 예의상 듣는 시늉만 냈으나, 모든 것이 새로운 만큼이나 흥미로운 패니는, 러시워스 부인이 들려주는 옛날 그 가문의 이야기—그 흥성과 영화, 임금의 행차와 충성스러운 헌신에—꾸밈없는 진지함으로 귀를 기울이며, 이미 아는 역사에 무엇이든 이어 보거나 지난날의 정경으로 제 상상을 데우는 것을 기뻐했다.\n\n저택의 자리 탓에 어느 방에서도 이렇다 할 전망을 바랄 수는 없었다. 그리고 패니와 몇몇이 러시워스 부인을 따르는 동안, 헨리 크로퍼드는 창가에서 심각한 낯으로 고개를 절레절레 흔들고 있었다. 서쪽 정면의 방마다 잔디밭 너머로, 높다란 쇠 울짱과 대문 바로 뒤에서 시작되는 가로수길이 내다보였다.\n\n창문세를 보태고 하녀들에게 일거리를 만들어 주는 것 말고는 달리 쓸모가 있으리라고는 볼 수 없는 방들을 그 밖에도 숱하게 구경한 끝에, 러시워스 부인이 말했다. “자, 이제 예배당으로 갑니다. 본디는 위에서 들어가 내려다봐야 마땅하지만, 우리끼리 정다운 사이니, 괜찮으시다면 이쪽으로 안내할게요.”\n\n그들은 안으로 들어섰다. 패니의 상상은, 그저 널찍하고 길쭉한, 예배를 위해 꾸민 방보다는 좀 더 웅장한 무언가를 그려 두고 있었다. 그러나 마호가니가 그득한 것과, 위층 가족석 난간 너머로 보이는 진홍색 벨벳 방석 말고는, 더 눈에 띄거나 더 엄숙한 것이라곤 없었다. “실망이에요.” 그녀가 에드먼드에게 나직이 말했다. “이건 제가 생각한 예배당이 아니에요. 여긴 두려운 것도, 서글픈 것도, 웅장한 것도 없어요. 측랑도, 아치도, 비문도, 군기도 없고요. 군기가 없어요, 오빠—‘하늘의 밤바람에 나부낄’ 군기 말이에요. ‘스코틀랜드 왕이 그 아래 잠들어 있다’는 자취도 없고요.”\n\n“패니, 이 모든 게 얼마나 근래에 지어졌는지, 그리고 성이나 수도원의 옛 예배당에 비하면 얼마나 좁은 쓰임을 위한 것인지를 잊었구나. 이건 그저 이 집안이 사사로이 쓰려던 것뿐이야. 그 집안 사람들은 아마 교구 교회에 묻혔을 거다. 군기와 문장(紋章)은 거기서 찾아야지.”\n\n“그걸 다 생각지 못하다니 제가 어리석었네요. 그래도 실망스러운걸요.”\n\n러시워스 부인이 이야기를 시작했다. “이 예배당은 보시는 대로 제임스 2세 시대에 꾸며진 거예요. 제가 알기로 그전엔 신도석이 그저 판벽뿐이었대요. 그리고 설교단과 가족석의 안감과 방석도 그저 자줏빛 천이었으리라 짐작할 만한 근거가 좀 있지만, 이건 확실치는 않아요. 아름다운 예배당이고, 예전엔 아침저녁으로 늘 쓰였답니다. 여러 사람의 기억으로는 전속 사제가 늘 여기서 기도를 낭독했다는데, 돌아가신 러시워스 씨가 그걸 그만두셨지요.”\n\n“세대마다 나름의 개량이 있는 법이죠.” 크로퍼드 양이 에드먼드에게 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n러시워스 부인은 크로퍼드 씨에게 다시 그 배운 것을 되풀이하러 가 버렸고, 에드먼드와 패니와 크로퍼드 양은 한데 모여 남았다.\n\n“참 안타까워요.” 패니가 외쳤다. “그 관습이 끊겼다니 말이에요. 그건 옛 시절의 값진 한 부분이었는데. 예배당과 사제에는 큰 저택과, 또 그런 집안이라면 마땅히 이러이러해야 하리라는 관념과 그토록 잘 어울리는 무언가가 있잖아요! 온 집안이 기도를 위해 규칙적으로 모이는 건 참 훌륭한 일이에요!”\n\n“정말 훌륭하고말고요.” 크로퍼드 양이 웃으며 말했다. “딱한 하녀들과 하인들을 죄다 일이며 즐거움에서 붙들어다 하루 두 번씩 여기서 기도를 올리게 강요하는 건, 정작 저희는 빠질 핑계를 궁리하면서 말이죠—그거야 집안 어른들한테 참 이로운 일일 거예요.”\n\n“그건 패니가 생각하는 온 집안이 모이는 모습과는 거리가 멀지요.” 에드먼드가 말했다. “주인 내외가 몸소 참석하지 않는다면, 그 관습에는 이로움보다 해로움이 더 클 테니까요.”\n\n“어쨌든 그런 문제는 사람마다 제 뜻에 맡겨 두는 편이 더 안전해요. 누구나 제 방식대로 가고 싶어 하잖아요—제 시간과 제 방식으로 기도하고 싶어 하고요. 참석의 의무, 그 격식, 그 구속, 그 긴 시간—이 모든 게 합쳐지면 여간 부담스러운 게 아니고, 아무도 좋아하지 않는 거예요. 그리고 저 회랑석에서 무릎 꿇고 하품하던 그 착한 사람들이, 언젠가는 두통을 안고 깨어난 남녀가 예배당에 안 갔다고 지탄받을 걱정 없이 침대에 십 분쯤 더 누워 있어도 되는 시절이 오리라는 걸 미리 알았더라면, 아마 부러움에 겨워 펄쩍 뛰었을 거예요. 러시워스 가문의 지난날 미녀들이 얼마나 여러 번, 얼마나 마지못한 심정으로 이 예배당을 찾았을지 짐작이 안 가세요? 젊은 엘리너 부인이니 브리짓 부인이니 하는 이들이—겉으로는 경건한 척 빳빳이 굳어 있으면서도 머릿속은 그와는 사뭇 다른 것으로 가득 차서 말이에요—더구나 딱한 그 사제가 바라볼 값어치도 없는 위인이라면 더욱요. 게다가 그 시절엔 목사들이 지금보다도 훨씬 못한 축이었을 것 같거든요.”\n\n잠깐 동안 그녀에게 아무도 대꾸하지 않았다. 패니는 얼굴을 붉히며 에드먼드를 바라보았으나 너무 화가 나 말이 나오지 않았다. 그리고 그는 얼마간 마음을 가다듬고 나서야 이렇게 말할 수 있었다. “크로퍼드 양의 그 발랄한 마음은 진지한 문제에서조차 좀처럼 진지해지지 못하는군요. 재미난 그림 한 폭을 보여 주셨고, 인간 본성으로 보아 그렇지 않다고는 못 하겠어요. 우리 모두 이따금 제 생각을 바라는 대로 붙들어 두기 어려운 걸 느끼기 마련이니까요. 하지만 만약 그걸 흔한 일로—말하자면 소홀함에서 버릇으로 굳어 버린 약점으로 여기시는 거라면, 그런 사람들의 사사로운 기도인들 무얼 기대할 수 있겠어요? 예배당에서 이리저리 흐트러지도록 내버려 두고 방치한 그 마음이, 골방에서라고 더 잘 모아질 것 같으세요?”\n\n“네, 그럴 법하죠. 적어도 두 가지는 유리할 테니까요. 밖에서 주의를 흩뜨릴 것이 덜할 테고, 그렇게 오래 시달리지도 않을 테고요.”\n\n“한 상황에서 저 자신과 싸우지 않는 마음이라면, 다른 상황에서도 주의를 흩뜨릴 대상을 찾아내고 말 거예요, 제 생각엔. 그리고 장소와 본보기의 힘은, 처음 품고 온 것보다 더 나은 마음을 곧잘 일깨우기도 하고요. 다만 예배가 더 길다는 것은, 인정하건대 때로 마음에 너무 벅찬 짐이 되기도 하지요. 그렇지 않으면 좋으련만. 하지만 저는 아직 옥스퍼드를 떠난 지 얼마 안 되어, 예배당 기도가 어떤 것인지 잊을 만큼은 안 됐답니다.”\n\n이런 이야기가 오가는 동안, 나머지 일행은 예배당 여기저기 흩어져 있었는데, 줄리아가 크로퍼드 씨의 주의를 제 언니에게 끌며 이렇게 말했다. “러시워스 씨하고 마리아 좀 보세요, 나란히 서 있는 게 꼭 지금 막 예식이라도 올리려는 것 같잖아요. 완전히 그런 분위기 아니에요?”\n\n크로퍼드 씨는 그렇다는 듯 미소 지으며 마리아에게로 다가가, 그녀만 들을 수 있는 목소리로 말했다. “버트럼 양이 제단에 저리 가까이 서 계신 걸 보고 싶지 않은데요.”\n\n흠칫한 그녀는 저도 모르게 한두 걸음 옮겼으나, 이내 마음을 추스르고 웃는 시늉을 하며, 그다지 크지 않은 목소리로 그에게 물었다. “그럼 씨가 저를 신랑에게 넘겨주는 역을 맡아 주시겠어요?”\n\n“아무래도 제가 아주 서툴게 해내지 싶은데요.” 그것이 의미심장한 눈빛과 함께 나온 그의 대답이었다.\n\n마침 그때 합류한 줄리아가 그 농을 이어받았다.\n\n“정말이지, 제대로 된 혼인 허가장만 있다면 지금 당장 못 할 게 뭐람. 참 아쉽네요. 이렇게 다들 한자리에 모였겠다, 세상에 이보다 더 아늑하고 즐거운 일도 없을 텐데요.” 그러고는 어찌나 조심성 없이 그 일을 두고 떠들며 웃어 대는지, 러시워스 씨와 그 어머니까지 알아듣게 만들었고, 제 언니를 애인의 소곤거리는 달콤한 말에 고스란히 노출시켰다. 한편 러시워스 부인은 그것이 언제 이루어지든 저에게는 더없이 경사스러운 일이라고, 걸맞은 미소와 위엄을 갖추어 말했다.\n\n“에드먼드가 서품만 받았어도!” 줄리아가 외치며, 크로퍼드 양과 패니와 함께 서 있는 그에게로 달려갔다. “오빠, 지금 오빠가 서품만 받았어도 당장 그 예식을 올려 줄 수 있을 텐데. 아직 서품을 안 받았다니 어쩜 이리 운이 없담. 러시워스 씨하고 마리아는 채비가 다 됐는데.”\n\n줄리아가 이렇게 말할 때 크로퍼드 양의 낯빛은, 무심한 구경꾼이 봤다면 재미있어했을 만했다. 그녀는 새로 받아 든 그 생각에 거의 아연실색한 표정이었다. 패니는 그녀가 딱했다. ‘방금 제가 한 말에 얼마나 난처해할까’ 하는 생각이 그녀의 머리를 스쳤다.\n\n“서품이라니!” 크로퍼드 양이 말했다. “아니, 성직자가 되시는 거예요?”\n\n“네. 아버지가 돌아오시고 얼마 안 있어 서품을 받을 겁니다—아마 크리스마스쯤에요.”\n\n크로퍼드 양은 마음을 다잡고 낯빛을 되찾아, 그저 이렇게만 대답하고는 화제를 돌렸다. “진작 알았더라면 성직을 좀 더 존중해서 말했을 텐데요.”\n\n예배당은 이내, 한 해 내내 거의 아무 방해도 없이 그 안을 지배하는 그 고요와 정적에 다시 내맡겨졌다. 제 동생이 못마땅한 버트럼 양이 앞장서 나섰고, 다들 여기 오래 있었다 싶은 눈치였다.\n\n이제 저택의 아래층은 다 구경한 셈이었고, 이 일에 지칠 줄 모르는 러시워스 부인은 중앙 계단 쪽으로 나아가 그들을 위층 방까지 죄다 안내하려 했을 것이다. 아들이 시간이 넉넉지 않을까 싶다며 끼어들지만 않았다면 말이다. “왜냐하면,” 하고 그는, 제법 명석한 머리라도 늘 피하지는 못하는 그런 뻔한 소리를 하며 말했다. “저택을 도는 데 너무 오래 걸리면, 밖에서 할 일에 쓸 시간이 없을 테니까요. 두 시가 넘었고, 우리는 다섯 시에 저녁을 들 참이잖아요.”\n\n러시워스 부인은 물러섰다. 그리고 누가 어떻게 영지를 둘러볼 것인가 하는 문제가 좀 더 본격적으로 논의될 참이었고, 노리스 부인이 마차와 말을 어떻게 짜맞추면 가장 많은 걸 해낼 수 있을까 궁리하기 시작했는데, 그때 젊은 사람들이 바깥으로 난 문 하나를 발견했다. 그 문은 잔디와 관목으로, 그리고 유원지의 온갖 향긋함으로 곧장 이어지는 계단 위에 솔깃하게 열려 있었고, 그리하여 다들 하나의 충동에, 바람과 자유를 향한 하나의 소망에 이끌린 듯 밖으로 걸어 나갔다.\n\n“그럼 우선 이쪽으로 내려가 볼까요.” 러시워스 부인이 그 뜻을 정중히 알아채고 그들을 따르며 말했다. “여기 우리 화초가 제일 많고, 진기한 꿩들도 있답니다.”\n\n“그런데,” 크로퍼드 씨가 둘레를 둘러보며 말했다. “더 가기 전에 여기서 우리가 매달릴 만한 무언가를 찾아낼 수 있지 않을까요? 아주 유망한 담들이 보이는데요. 러시워스 씨, 이 잔디밭에서 회의를 한번 열어 볼까요?”\n\n“제임스,” 러시워스 부인이 아들에게 말했다. “아마 그 야생 숲은 온 일행에게 처음일 게다. 버트럼가 아가씨들도 아직 야생 숲을 본 적이 없으니까.”\n\n아무도 반대하지 않았으나, 한동안은 어떤 계획으로든, 어느 만큼의 거리로든 움직일 마음이 없어 보였다. 다들 처음엔 화초나 꿩에 이끌려, 저마다 흐뭇하게 제멋대로 흩어졌다. 크로퍼드 씨가 맨 먼저 저택의 그쪽 끝이 지닌 가능성을 살피러 앞으로 나섰다. 양옆이 높은 담으로 둘린 그 잔디밭은, 맨 처음 화초를 심은 구역 너머에 볼링 그린을 품고 있었고, 볼링 그린 너머에는 긴 테라스 산책로가 있었는데, 그 뒤로는 쇠 울짱이 받치고 서서, 그 너머로 바로 잇닿은 야생 숲의 우듬지를 굽어보게 해 주었다. 흠잡기에 딱 좋은 자리였다. 이내 버트럼 양과 러시워스 씨가 크로퍼드 씨를 뒤따랐다. 그리고 얼마 뒤 나머지도 무리를 이루기 시작했을 때, 이 세 사람은 테라스에서 한창 의논에 열중한 채 에드먼드와 크로퍼드 양과 패니의 눈에 띄었는데, 이 셋은 자연스레 한데 어우러진 듯 보였고, 앞의 세 사람의 아쉬움과 어려움에 잠시 끼어 주고 나서는 그들을 두고 앞서 걸어갔다. 남은 세 사람—러시워스 부인, 노리스 부인, 줄리아—은 여전히 한참 뒤처져 있었다. 복된 별이 더는 비추지 않게 된 줄리아는 러시워스 부인 곁을 지키며 안달하는 발걸음을 그 부인의 느린 걸음에 맞춰야 했고, 그녀의 이모는 꿩에게 모이를 주러 나온 가정부와 마주쳐 그와 수다를 떨며 뒤에 처져 어정거리고 있었으니 말이다. 딱한 줄리아, 아홉 중에 제 몫에 그런대로 만족하지 못한 유일한 사람이었던 그녀는, 이제 완전한 고행의 처지에 놓여, 마부석 위의 그 줄리아와는 상상할 수 있는 한 딴판이었다. 도리로 알고 몸에 익힌 그 예의범절이 그녀로서는 빠져나올 길을 막았고, 한편 그 교육에 이렇다 할 몫으로 들어 있지 않았던 것들—그 더 높은 종류의 자제심, 남을 헤아리는 그 올바른 마음, 제 마음을 아는 그 앎, 옳음의 그 원칙—이 없다 보니, 그녀는 그 처지 아래서 비참했다.\n\n“여긴 참을 수 없이 덥네요.” 테라스를 한 바퀴 돌고 나서, 야생 숲으로 열리는 한복판의 그 문으로 두 번째 다가가던 참에 크로퍼드 양이 말했다. “우리 중에 누구라도 편안해지는 걸 마다할 사람이 있을까요? 여기 들어갈 수만 있다면 아담하고 좋은 숲이 있는데. 저 문이 잠겨 있지 않다면 얼마나 좋을까요! 하지만 뻔히 잠겨 있겠죠. 이런 큰 데서는 정원사들만이 제가 가고 싶은 데를 갈 수 있으니까요.”\n\n그러나 그 문은 잠겨 있지 않은 것으로 드러났고, 그들은 다들 반갑게 그 문으로 돌아 들어가 뒤편의 사정없는 햇빛을 등지는 데 뜻을 모았다. 제법 긴 계단을 내려서자 야생 숲에 이르렀는데, 그것은 이 에이커쯤 되는, 심어 가꾼 숲이었다. 주로 낙엽송과 월계수와 베어 낸 너도밤나무로 이루어졌고 지나치게 반듯하게 배치되기는 했으나, 볼링 그린이며 테라스에 견주면 어둑함과 그늘이었고, 자연스러운 아름다움이었다. 다들 그 서늘함을 느끼며 한동안은 그저 걸으며 감탄할 따름이었다. 마침내 잠깐 뜸을 들인 뒤, 크로퍼드 양이 말문을 열었다. “그러니까 성직자가 되신다는 거군요, 버트럼 씨. 저로선 좀 뜻밖이에요.”\n\n“왜 뜻밖이지요? 제가 어떤 직업을 가지도록 정해졌으리라 짐작하셨을 테고, 제가 변호사도, 군인도, 뱃사람도 아니라는 건 알아채셨을 텐데요.”\n\n“옳은 말씀이에요. 하지만 한마디로, 미처 그 생각이 안 났어요. 게다가 아시다시피 둘째 아들한테는 재산을 물려줄 삼촌이나 할아버지가 대개 하나쯤 있잖아요.”\n\n“아주 칭찬할 만한 관례지요.” 에드먼드가 말했다. “하지만 다 그런 건 아니에요. 저는 그 예외 중 하나고, 예외인 이상 저 스스로 무언가를 해야 하지요.”\n\n“그런데 왜 하필 성직자가 되시는 거예요? 저는 그건 늘, 먼저 고를 사람이 여럿 있을 때 막내한테 돌아가는 몫인 줄 알았거든요.”\n\n“그럼 교회 자체를 택하는 사람은 결코 없다고 생각하세요?”\n\n“‘결코’란 험한 말이죠. 하지만 그래요, ‘그리 자주는 아니다’라는 뜻의 그 대화 속 ‘결코’로라면, 저는 정말 그렇게 생각해요. 교회에서 대체 무얼 할 수 있겠어요? 사람은 두각을 나타내고 싶어 하는데, 다른 어느 길에서든 두각은 얻을 수 있어도 교회에서는 못 얻잖아요. 성직자란 아무것도 아니거든요.”\n\n“그 대화 속 ‘아무것도 아니다’에도, ‘결코’와 마찬가지로 등급이 있기를 바랍니다. 성직자가 권세나 유행에서 높은 자리에 있을 수야 없지요. 군중을 이끌어서도, 옷차림의 유행을 이끌어서도 안 되고요. 하지만 저는, 개인으로 보나 집단으로 보나, 이승으로 보나 영원으로 보나 인류에게 가장 으뜸으로 중한 그 모든 것을 맡고, 종교와 도덕을, 따라서 그 영향에서 비롯되는 처신까지 지키는 그 자리를 아무것도 아니라고는 부를 수 없어요. 여기 있는 누구도 그 직분을 아무것도 아니라고는 못 하지요. 그 자리를 맡은 사람이 아무것도 아니라면, 그건 제 소임을 게을리하고, 그 마땅한 중함을 저버리고, 제 자리를 벗어나 마땅히 보여선 안 될 모습으로 나서기 때문이에요.”\n\n“씨는 사람들이 흔히 들어 온 것보다, 아니 제가 도무지 이해할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 중함을 성직자에게 부여하시는군요. 사교계에서는 그런 영향력이나 중함이 그리 눈에 띄지 않는걸요. 게다가 정작 그들 자신이 그토록 좀처럼 눈에 안 띄는데 그게 어떻게 얻어질 수 있겠어요? 일주일에 설교 두 번으로—그게 들을 만한 설교라 치고, 또 설교자가 제 것보다 블레어의 설교를 골라 읽을 분별이 있다고 쳐도—어떻게 씨가 말씀하시는 그 모든 걸 해낼 수 있겠어요? 남은 한 주 내내 많은 신도의 행실을 다스리고 그 처신을 빚을 수 있겠느냐고요? 설교단 밖에 나온 성직자는 좀처럼 눈에 띄지도 않는데요.”\n\n“크로퍼드 양은 런던을 말씀하시는 거고, 저는 나라 전체를 말하는 거예요.”\n\n“수도야말로 나머지의 꽤 공정한 표본이지 싶은데요.”\n\n“왕국 전역의 미덕과 악덕의 비율로 치자면, 그렇지 않기를 바랍니다. 우리는 가장 훌륭한 도덕을 큰 도시에서 찾지 않아요. 어느 교파의 반듯한 사람이든 가장 큰 선을 베풀 수 있는 곳은 거기가 아니고, 성직자의 영향력이 가장 잘 느껴질 수 있는 곳도 분명 거기가 아니지요. 훌륭한 설교자는 사람들이 따르고 우러르지요. 하지만 좋은 성직자가 제 교구와 이웃에 쓸모 있게 되는 것은 훌륭한 설교로만은 아니에요. 그 교구와 이웃이 그의 사사로운 인품을 알고 그의 일상적 처신을 지켜볼 만한 크기일 때 말이지요—런던에서는 그런 경우가 좀처럼 없지만요. 거기서 성직자는 제 교구민의 무리 속에 파묻혀 버려요. 대부분에게는 그저 설교자로만 알려질 뿐이죠. 그리고 그들이 세상의 처신에 미치는 영향에 대해서라면—크로퍼드 양은 저를 오해하시거나, 제가 그들을 좋은 가정교육의 심판관이니, 세련과 예절의 감독관이니, 인생 의례의 사회자니 하는 뜻으로 말한다고 여기시면 안 됩니다. 제가 말하는 처신이란, 차라리 행실이라 불러야 할지도 몰라요. 좋은 원칙에서 비롯되는 것, 한마디로 그들이 가르치고 권하는 것이 소임인 그 교리가 낳는 결과 말이에요. 그리고 저는, 성직자가 마땅한 모습이냐 아니냐에 따라 나머지 온 나라도 그러하다는 것을, 어디서나 확인하게 되리라 믿어요.”\n\n“정말 그래요.” 패니가 부드러운 진지함으로 말했다.\n\n“저것 봐요.” 크로퍼드 양이 외쳤다. “벌써 프라이스 양은 완전히 설득해 버리셨네요.”\n\n“크로퍼드 양도 설득할 수 있으면 좋겠는데요.”\n\n“그러진 못하실걸요.” 그녀가 짓궂은 미소를 지으며 말했다. “씨가 서품을 받으실 작정이라는 게, 저는 지금도 처음만큼이나 놀라워요. 씨는 정말이지 그보다 나은 일에 어울리는 분이에요. 자, 마음을 바꾸세요. 아직 늦지 않았어요. 법조계로 가세요.”\n\n“법조계로 가라고요! 저더러 이 야생 숲으로 들어가라던 것만큼이나 손쉽게 말씀하시네요.”\n\n“이제 씨는 둘 중에 법이야말로 더 고약한 야생 숲이라는 둥 무슨 말을 하려 하시겠죠. 하지만 제가 먼저 막았어요. 기억하세요, 제가 선수를 쳤다는 걸요.”\n\n“고작 제 재담 하나를 막으려는 거라면 서두르실 것 없어요. 제 천성엔 재치라곤 눈곱만큼도 없으니까요. 저는 아주 무미건조하고 곧이곧대로 말하는 사람이라, 반 시간을 내리 재치 있는 응수의 언저리에서 헤매면서도 끝내 그걸 못 짜낼 수도 있는 위인이거든요.”\n\n이어 다들 말이 없어졌다. 저마다 생각에 잠겼다. 패니가 먼저 그 침묵을 깨며 말했다. “이 향긋한 숲을 걷기만 했는데 지치다니 저도 이상하네요. 그래도 다음번에 앉을 자리가 나오면, 여러분이 괜찮으시다면 잠깐 앉았으면 좋겠어요.”\n\n“나의 패니야.” 에드먼드가 외치며 얼른 그녀의 팔을 제 팔에 끼웠다. “내가 어쩜 이리 무심했을까! 너무 지치진 않았기를 바란다. 어쩌면,” 하고 크로퍼드 양 쪽으로 돌아서며, “제 다른 동행께서 제게 팔을 잡아 주시는 영광을 베풀어 주실지도 모르겠군요.”\n\n“고맙지만 저는 조금도 안 지쳤어요.” 그러면서도 그녀는 그 팔을 잡았고, 그녀가 그리해 준 것이, 그런 이어짐을 처음으로 느낀 그 흡족함이, 그로 하여금 패니를 잠깐 잊게 했다. “거의 얹지도 않으시는군요.” 그가 말했다. “저를 조금도 부리지 않으시네요. 여자의 팔 무게란 남자의 그것과 어찌 이리 다른지! 옥스퍼드에서 저는 한 거리 내내 남자 하나를 제게 기대게 한 적이 여러 번인데, 그에 견주면 크로퍼드 양은 한낱 파리 한 마리에 지나지 않아요.”\n\n“저는 정말 안 지쳤어요. 저 스스로도 신기할 지경이에요. 우리가 이 숲에서 적어도 일 마일은 걸었을 텐데 말이죠. 그렇게 생각지 않으세요?”\n\n“반 마일도 안 됩니다.” 그것이 그의 단호한 대답이었다. 그는 아직 여자다운 제멋대로의 셈법으로 거리를 재거나 시간을 헤아릴 만큼 사랑에 빠지지는 않았던 것이다.\n\n“어머! 우리가 얼마나 이리저리 돌았는지는 생각도 안 하시는군요. 우리가 어찌나 구불구불 돌아왔는데요. 게다가 이 숲 자체도 곧게 재면 반 마일은 될 거예요. 첫 큰길을 벗어난 뒤로 아직 그 끝을 한 번도 못 봤잖아요.”\n\n“하지만 기억하신다면, 그 첫 큰길을 벗어나기 전에 우리는 그 끝까지 곧장 내다봤어요. 그 길 전체를 죽 훑어봤고, 저 끝이 쇠 대문으로 막혀 있는 걸 봤죠. 그러니 길어야 일 펄롱밖에 안 됐을 거예요.”\n\n“어머! 저는 씨의 그 펄롱이란 게 뭔지 몰라요. 하지만 여긴 아주 긴 숲이고, 우리가 들어온 뒤로 줄곧 이리저리 굽이돌았다는 건 분명해요. 그러니 제가 여기서 일 마일을 걸었다고 할 때는, 그것도 넉넉잡아 줄여 말하는 거라고요.”\n\n“우리가 여기 있은 지 딱 십오 분이에요.” 에드먼드가 시계를 꺼내며 말했다. “우리가 시속 사 마일로 걷고 있다고 생각하세요?”\n\n“어머! 그 시계로 저를 몰아붙이지 마세요. 시계란 늘 너무 빠르거나 너무 느린 법이거든요. 저는 시계한테 이래라저래라 지시받을 수는 없어요.”\n\n몇 걸음 더 가자 그들은 바로 그 이야기하던 산책로의 끝머리로 나왔다. 그리고 그늘이 잘 지고 아늑하게 물러앉아, 하하—움푹 팬 경계 도랑—너머로 공원을 굽어보는 자리에, 넉넉한 크기의 벤치가 하나 놓여 있어, 그들은 다 함께 거기 앉았다.\n\n“아무래도 너 많이 지쳤나 보구나, 패니.” 그녀를 살피며 에드먼드가 말했다. “왜 진작 말하지 않았어? 이러다 몸살이라도 나면 오늘 하루가 너한테 고약한 놀이가 되고 말 텐데. 크로퍼드 양, 이 애는 승마만 빼면 어떤 운동이든 이토록 금세 지쳐 버린답니다.”\n\n“그럼 지난주 내내 제가 그랬듯이 이 애 말을 독차지하게 두시다니, 씨도 참 못되셨네요! 씨한테도 저 자신한테도 부끄러워요. 하지만 다시는 그런 일 없을 거예요.”\n\n“크로퍼드 양의 그 세심함과 배려가 제 소홀함을 더욱 뼈저리게 느끼게 하는군요. 패니의 안녕은 저보다 크로퍼드 양의 손에 있는 편이 더 안전한 것 같아요.”\n\n“그래도 이 애가 지금 지친 건 저로선 조금도 놀랍지 않아요. 세상에 사람의 소임 가운데, 오늘 아침 우리가 한 것만큼 고단한 일도 없으니까요. 큰 저택을 구경하고, 이 방 저 방 어정어정 옮겨 다니고, 눈과 주의를 잔뜩 쥐어짜고, 알아듣지도 못하는 걸 듣고, 마음도 없는 걸 감탄하고요. 그거야말로 세상에서 제일가는 따분한 일로 다들 치잖아요. 프라이스 양도 정작 모르는 채로 그걸 겪은 거예요.”\n\n“저는 곧 괜찮아질 거예요.” 패니가 말했다. “좋은 날 그늘에 앉아 푸르름을 바라보는 것만큼 더없이 개운한 것도 없으니까요.”\n\n잠시 앉아 있다가 크로퍼드 양이 다시 일어섰다. “저는 좀 움직여야겠어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “쉬는 게 오히려 저를 지치게 하네요. 저 경계 도랑 너머를 하도 바라봤더니 물렸어요. 가서 저 쇠 대문 사이로 같은 경치를, 이번엔 그만큼 잘 보이지도 않게 내다봐야겠어요.”\n\n에드먼드도 자리에서 일어났다. “자, 크로퍼드 양, 저 산책로를 죽 올려다보시면, 그게 반 마일도, 아니 반의반 마일도 될 수 없다는 걸 스스로 아시게 될 겁니다.”\n\n“어마어마하게 먼걸요.” 그녀가 말했다. “저는 한눈에 그게 보여요.”\n\n그는 여전히 그녀를 조리 있게 설득했으나 헛일이었다. 그녀는 셈하려 들지 않았고, 견주려 들지 않았다. 그저 미소 지으며 우겨 댈 뿐이었다. 더할 나위 없이 이치에 맞는 한결같음이라도 이보다 더 사람을 끌지는 못했을 것이고, 그리하여 둘은 서로 흡족해하며 이야기를 나누었다. 마침내, 숲을 좀 더 거닐어 보며 그 크기를 가늠해 보기로 뜻이 모아졌다. 그들은 지금 있는 방향 그대로 숲의 한쪽 끝까지 가 보기로 했다—하하를 낀 아래쪽으로 곧게 뻗은 푸른 산책로가 있었으니 말이다—그리고 도움이 될 듯싶으면 어느 다른 방향으로 조금 꺾어 보았다가 몇 분 안에 돌아오기로 했다. 패니는 이제 쉬었다며 저도 따라 움직이려 했으나, 그것은 허락되지 않았다. 에드먼드가 그녀에게 그 자리에 그냥 있으라고 어찌나 진지하게 권하는지 그녀는 거스를 수 없었고, 그리하여 벤치에 남겨져, 사촌의 살뜰함을 기쁘게 곱씹으면서도 제가 더 튼튼하지 못한 것을 몹시 아쉬워했다. 그녀는 그들이 모퉁이를 돌 때까지 지켜보았고, 그들의 기척이 다 스러질 때까지 귀를 기울였다.\n\n# 제10장\n\n십오 분, 이십 분이 흘러갔건만, 패니는 여전히 에드먼드와 크로퍼드 양과 저 자신을 생각하고 있었고, 아무도 그녀를 방해하지 않았다. 그녀는 이토록 오래 홀로 남겨진 것이 슬슬 의아해지기 시작했고, 그들의 발소리와 목소리를 다시 듣고 싶은 애타는 마음으로 귀를 기울이기 시작했다. 귀를 기울였고, 마침내 들려왔다. 목소리와 발소리가 다가오는 것을 들었다. 그러나 그것이 제가 바라던 사람들의 것이 아님을 막 확인한 참에, 버트럼 양과 러시워스 씨와 크로퍼드 씨가, 그녀 자신이 밟고 갔던 바로 그 길에서 나와 그녀 앞에 섰다.\n\n“프라이스 양 혼자 있네”와 “나의 패니야, 이게 어찌 된 일이니?”가 첫인사였다. 그녀는 사정을 이야기했다. “가엾은 패니야.” 사촌 언니가 외쳤다. “저 사람들한테 어쩜 그리 푸대접을 받았니! 우리랑 같이 있을 걸 그랬다.”\n\n그러고는 양옆에 신사를 하나씩 두고 앉아, 그녀는 아까 그들이 몰두하던 이야기를 다시 이어받아, 손질의 가능성을 자못 활기차게 논했다. 딱히 정해진 것은 없었다. 그러나 헨리 크로퍼드는 온갖 착상과 구상으로 가득했고, 대체로 그가 무엇을 제안하든 곧바로 받아들여졌다—먼저 그녀가, 다음으로 러시워스 씨가. 러시워스 씨의 주된 소임이란 남들의 말을 듣는 것인 듯했고, 그는 제 친구 스미스의 땅을 봤더라면 좋았을 거라는 바람 말고는 좀처럼 제 독창적인 생각 하나 내놓으려 들지 않았다.\n\n이런 식으로 몇 분을 보낸 뒤, 버트럼 양이 쇠문을 눈여겨보고는, 저희의 시야와 궁리를 좀 더 넓히자며 그 문을 지나 공원으로 나가고 싶다는 뜻을 비쳤다. 헨리 크로퍼드의 생각으로도 그것이야말로 무엇보다 바랄 만한 일이요, 최선이요, 조금이라도 득이 되게 일을 진척시킬 유일한 길이었다. 그리고 그는 곧바로 반 마일도 안 되는 데 있는 둔덕 하나를 알아보았는데, 거기서라면 저택을 살피기에 꼭 알맞은 시야가 나올 터였다. 그러니 그 둔덕으로, 그 문을 지나 가야만 했다. 그러나 문은 잠겨 있었다. 러시워스 씨는 열쇠를 가져올 걸 그랬다고 아쉬워했다. 열쇠를 가져와야 하지 않을까 하는 생각을 하마터면 할 뻔했었노라고. 다시는 열쇠 없이 오지 않으리라 다짐도 했다. 그러나 그렇다고 당장의 곤란이 없어지지는 않았다. 그들은 지나갈 수 없었다. 그리고 버트럼 양이 그리하고 싶은 마음이 조금도 수그러들지 않자, 결국 러시워스 씨가 제가 가서 열쇠를 가져오겠노라 대놓고 선언하는 것으로 끝이 났다. 그리하여 그는 길을 나섰다.\n\n“저택에서 이미 이렇게 멀리 왔으니, 지금으로선 그게 우리가 할 수 있는 가장 좋은 일임에 틀림없지요.” 그가 떠나자 크로퍼드 씨가 말했다.\n\n“네, 달리 어쩔 도리가 없죠. 그런데 이제 솔직히 말씀해 보세요, 이곳이 대체로 기대하셨던 것보다 못하다고 느끼지 않으세요?”\n\n“아뇨, 정말이지 정반대예요. 저는 이곳이 더 낫고, 더 웅장하고, 그 양식에서 더 완성되어 있다고 느껴요—비록 그 양식이 최선은 아닐지 몰라도요. 그리고 솔직히 말씀드리면,” 하고 목소리를 좀 낮추며, “저는 앞으로 소더턴을 지금만큼 큰 즐거움으로 다시 보게 될 일은 없을 것 같아요. 여름이 한 번 더 온다 한들 제게는 이곳을 좀처럼 더 낫게 해 주지 못할 거예요.”\n\n잠깐 당황한 뒤 아가씨가 대답했다. “씨는 너무나 세상 물정에 밝은 분이라 세상의 눈으로 보지 않을 수 없으시겠죠. 남들이 소더턴이 나아졌다고 여기면, 씨도 분명 그러실 거예요.”\n\n“아무래도 저는 어떤 점에서는 제게 이로울 만큼 세상 물정에 밝지는 못한가 봐요. 제 감정은 그리 쉬 스러지지 않고, 지난날에 대한 제 기억도, 세상 물정에 밝은 사람들에게서 흔히 보는 것처럼 그리 만만하게 부려지지가 않거든요.”\n\n뒤이어 잠깐 침묵이 흘렀다. 버트럼 양이 다시 말을 꺼냈다. “오늘 아침 여기 오시는 길이 무척 즐거우셨나 봐요. 그토록 흥겨워하시는 걸 보니 저도 기뻤어요. 씨하고 줄리아는 오는 내내 웃어 대던걸요.”\n\n“우리가요? 네, 그랬던 것 같네요. 그런데 뭐가 그리 우스웠는지는 도무지 기억이 안 나요. 아! 아마 제 삼촌의 늙은 아일랜드 마부에 얽힌 우스꽝스러운 이야기를 들려주고 있었나 봐요. 동생분은 웃기를 좋아하시죠.”\n\n“줄리아가 저보다 더 명랑하다고 보시는 거예요?”\n\n“더 쉽게 즐거워하시죠.” 그가 대답했다. “그러니 아시다시피,” 하고 미소 지으며, “더 좋은 말벗이고요. 십 마일을 오는 동안 아일랜드 일화로 버트럼 양을 즐겁게 해 드리기는 바랄 수 없었을 테니까요.”\n\n“저도 천성으로야 줄리아 못지않게 발랄한 것 같아요. 다만 지금은 생각할 게 더 많은 것뿐이죠.”\n\n“그야 두말할 것 없지요. 게다가 너무 들뜬 기색이 오히려 무신경으로 비칠 처지도 있는 법이고요. 하지만 버트럼 양의 앞날은 기운이 없어도 될 만큼 어둡기는커녕 너무 환하잖아요. 눈앞에 아주 방긋 웃는 경치가 펼쳐져 있으니까요.”\n\n“말 그대로 하시는 거예요, 아니면 비유로요? 말 그대로시겠죠. 네, 그럼요, 해가 비치고 공원은 아주 쾌활해 보여요. 하지만 하필 저 쇠문이며 저 하하가 저한테 구속과 답답함을 느끼게 하네요. ‘난 나갈 수가 없어’, 찌르레기가 말한 것처럼요.” 그렇게 말하며—그것도 감정을 실어—그녀는 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다. 그가 그녀를 뒤따랐다. “러시워스 씨는 이 열쇠를 가지러 가서 왜 이리 오래 걸릴까요!”\n\n“그런데 세상없어도 열쇠 없이, 러시워스 씨의 허락과 보호 없이는 나가지 않으시겠다는 거군요. 아니면 제 도움을 받아 여기 문 가장자리로 별 어려움 없이 돌아 나가실 수도 있을 텐데요. 정말로 좀 더 자유로워지고 싶으시고, 그게 금지된 일은 아니라고 스스로 여기실 수만 있다면, 얼마든지 될 것 같은데요.”\n\n“금지라니! 말도 안 돼요! 저는 분명 저쪽으로 나갈 수 있고, 그럴 거예요. 러시워스 씨도 곧 올 거잖아요. 우린 안 보이는 데로 가는 것도 아니고요.”\n\n“설령 안 보이게 된다 해도, 프라이스 양이 그분께 우리를 저 둔덕 근처에서—그 둔덕의 참나무 숲에서 찾으면 된다고 친절히 일러 주겠지요.”\n\n이 모든 것이 옳지 않다고 느낀 패니는 그것을 막아 보려 애쓰지 않을 수 없었다. “다치실 거예요, 언니.” 그녀가 외쳤다. “저 뾰족한 창살에 부딪혀 꼭 다치실 거예요. 드레스가 찢어질 거고요. 하하에 미끄러져 빠질 위험도 있어요. 안 가시는 게 좋아요.”\n\n이 말이 나오는 동안 사촌 언니는 이미 무사히 반대편에 가 있었고, 성공의 온갖 흐뭇함이 담긴 미소를 지으며 말했다. “고맙다, 나의 패니야. 하지만 나도 내 드레스도 멀쩡히 살아 있단다. 그럼 잘 있어.”\n\n패니는 다시 홀로 남겨졌고, 유쾌한 기분이라곤 조금도 더해지지 않았다. 그녀는 제가 보고 들은 것 거의 전부가 안타까웠고, 버트럼 양에게 놀랐으며, 크로퍼드 씨에게 화가 났으니 말이다. 둔덕으로 가느라 그들은 빙 돌아가는, 그녀 보기엔 무척이나 부질없는 방향을 택했고, 이내 그녀의 눈 밖으로 사라졌다. 그리고 그 뒤로도 몇 분 동안 그녀는 어떤 동행의 모습도 소리도 없이 남아 있었다. 그 작은 숲이 온통 저 혼자 차지인 듯했다. 에드먼드와 크로퍼드 양이 숲을 떠나 버린 게 아닐까 하는 생각마저 들 뻔했으나, 에드먼드가 저를 그토록 까맣게 잊는다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이었다.\n\n그녀는 갑작스러운 발소리에 언짢은 상념에서 다시 깨어났다. 누군가 빠른 걸음으로 큰길을 내려오고 있었다. 그녀는 러시워스 씨이려니 했으나, 줄리아였다. 상기되어 숨을 헐떡이며 실망한 낯빛의 그녀가, 패니를 보고 외쳤다. “아니! 다른 사람들은 어디 있어? 마리아하고 크로퍼드 씨가 너랑 같이 있는 줄 알았는데.”\n\n패니가 설명했다.\n\n“참 얄궂은 짓이네, 정말! 어디에도 안 보이잖아.” 하며 그녀는 공원 안을 열심히 들여다보았다. “하지만 그리 멀리는 못 갔을 거야. 그리고 나도 마리아만큼은—도움 없이도—너끈히 해낼 수 있을 것 같아.”\n\n“그렇지만 줄리아 언니, 러시워스 씨가 곧 열쇠를 가지고 올 거예요. 러시워스 씨를 좀 기다려요.”\n\n“난 안 그래, 정말. 오늘 아침엔 그 집안이라면 신물이 나. 얘, 나는 방금 그 사람의 그 지긋지긋한 어머니한테서 겨우 빠져나온 참이라고. 네가 여기 이렇게 태평하고 행복하게 앉아 있는 동안, 내가 견딘 그 고행이라니! 어쩌면 네가 내 자리에 있었어도 매한가지였겠지만, 넌 늘 이런 곤경은 용케 피해 다니잖아.”\n\n이것은 더없이 부당한 힐난이었으나, 패니는 그것을 너그러이 봐주고 흘려 넘길 수 있었다. 줄리아는 언짢은 데다 성미가 급했다. 그러나 그것이 오래가지 않으리라는 걸 알았기에, 아무 내색 없이 그저 러시워스 씨를 못 봤느냐고 물었다.\n\n“봤어, 봤어. 목숨이라도 걸린 듯이 부리나케 달려가면서, 겨우 짬을 내 제 심부름 얘기하고 너희 다 어디 있는지만 일러 주더라.”\n\n“공연히 그렇게 애만 쓰시게 됐으니 딱하네요.”\n\n“그거야 마리아가 알아서 할 일이지. 내가 걔 죄까지 대신 벌받을 의무는 없잖아. 그 어머니야 내 지긋지긋한 이모가 가정부하고 얼쩐거리는 동안엔 피할 도리가 없었지만, 그 아들이야 얼마든지 떼어 놓을 수 있거든.”\n\n그러고는 그녀가 대뜸 울타리를 기어 넘어 걸어가 버리는 통에, 크로퍼드 양과 에드먼드를 못 봤느냐는 패니의 마지막 물음은 귀에 담기지도 못했다. 그러나 이제 러시워스 씨를 볼 일이 그녀에게 안긴 일종의 두려움 탓에, 그녀는 그 둘이 여태 안 돌아오는 것을 (그러지 않았더라면 그랬을 만큼) 그리 골똘히 생각하지도 못했다. 그녀는 그가 몹시 푸대접을 받았다고 느꼈고, 그동안 벌어진 일을 전해야 한다는 것이 여간 마음 불편하지 않았다. 그는 줄리아가 떠난 지 오 분도 안 되어 그녀에게 왔다. 그녀가 되도록 좋게 이야기를 꾸며 댔건만, 그는 예사롭지 않게 상심하고 언짢은 기색이 역력했다. 처음엔 좀처럼 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그 표정만이 극도의 놀라움과 분함을 드러낼 뿐이었고, 그는 문 쪽으로 걸어가 거기 서서, 무얼 어째야 할지 모르는 듯했다.\n\n“여기 있으라고 하셨어요—마리아 언니가 저더러, 저 둔덕이나 그 근처에서 그분들을 찾으실 수 있을 거라고 전하라 하셨고요.”\n\n“저는 더는 가지 않을 참이에요.” 그가 뾰로통하게 말했다. “아무도 안 보이는걸요. 제가 그 둔덕에 다다를 즈음이면 다른 데로 가 버렸을지도 모르고요. 걸을 만큼 걸었어요.”\n\n그러고는 더없이 침울한 낯으로 패니 곁에 앉았다.\n\n“정말 안됐어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “참 운이 없네요.” 그러면서 그녀는 무언가 좀 더 요긴한 말을 할 수 있기를 간절히 바랐다.\n\n잠깐의 침묵 뒤에, “저를 좀 기다려 줬어도 좋았을 텐데 싶네요.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“버트럼 양은 씨가 뒤따라오실 줄 아셨어요.”\n\n“그 사람이 그냥 있었으면 제가 뒤따라갈 일도 없었겠지요.”\n\n이것은 부인할 수 없는 말이라 패니는 입을 다물었다. 또 한 번 뜸을 들인 뒤, 그가 말을 이었다. “그런데 프라이스 양, 당신도 남들처럼 이 크로퍼드 씨를 그리 대단히 흠모하시나요? 저로선 그 사람한테서 아무것도 못 보겠던데.”\n\n“저는 그분이 잘생겼다고는 전혀 생각지 않아요.”\n\n“잘생겼다니! 저렇게 볼품없이 작은 남자를 누가 잘생겼다 하겠어요. 오 피트 구 인치도 안 돼요. 오 피트 팔 인치도 넘지 않는대도 저는 안 놀랄 겁니다. 제 눈엔 못생긴 사내예요. 제 생각엔 이 크로퍼드 남매는 하나도 보탬이 안 돼요. 우린 그들 없이도 아주 잘 지냈는데.”\n\n여기서 패니는 작은 한숨이 새어 나왔고, 그의 말을 어떻게 반박해야 할지 몰랐다.\n\n“제가 열쇠 가져오는 걸 두고 조금이라도 꾸물댔다면 무슨 핑곗거리라도 됐겠지만, 저는 그 사람이 그게 필요하다고 말한 바로 그 순간에 갔단 말이에요.”\n\n“씨의 태도야 그보다 더 친절할 수 없었지요, 정말요. 그리고 최대한 빨리 걸으셨을 거예요. 하지만 그래도 여기서 저택까지, 그것도 저택 안까지는 제법 거리가 있잖아요. 게다가 사람이 기다릴 때는 시간을 가늠하는 눈이 서툴러지는 법이라, 삼십 초마다 오 분처럼 느껴지거든요.”\n\n그는 일어나 다시 문 쪽으로 걸어가, “그때 열쇠를 몸에 지니고 있었더라면 좋았을 것을” 하고 아쉬워했다. 패니는 그가 거기 서 있는 데서 마음이 누그러지는 낌새를 알아챈 듯싶었고, 그에 힘입어 한 번 더 나서 보았다. 그래서 이렇게 말했다. “그분들과 합류하지 못하시면 딱하잖아요. 그분들은 공원의 그쪽에서 저택이 더 잘 보이리라 기대하며, 어떻게 손보면 좋을지 궁리하고 있을 텐데요. 아시다시피 그런 일은 씨 없이는 아무것도 정할 수 없고요.”\n\n그녀는 동행을 붙들어 두는 것보다 떠나보내는 데 더 재주가 있음을 알게 되었다. 러시워스 씨는 마음이 움직였다. “그럼,” 그가 말했다. “정말 제가 가는 게 낫다고 여기신다면 말이죠. 열쇠를 괜히 가져온 꼴이 되면 어리석은 일일 테니까요.” 그러고는 스스로 문을 열고 나가, 더는 격식 차리는 일 없이 걸어가 버렸다.\n\n이제 패니의 마음은 온통, 저를 그토록 오래 두고 간 그 두 사람에게 쏠렸다. 그리고 도무지 참을 수 없어진 그녀는 그들을 찾아 나서기로 마음먹었다. 그녀가 아래쪽 산책로를 따라 그들의 발자취를 좇아 막 다른 길로 접어들었을 때, 크로퍼드 양의 목소리와 웃음소리가 다시 한번 그녀의 귀를 붙들었다. 그 소리가 가까워지고, 몇 굽이를 더 돌자 그들이 그녀 앞에 나타났다. 그들은 방금 공원에서 야생 숲으로 돌아온 참이었는데, 그녀를 두고 떠난 지 얼마 안 되어 잠기지 않은 옆문 하나가 그들을 꾀어냈던 것이다. 그리하여 그들은 공원의 일부를 가로질러, 패니가 아침 내내 마침내 가 닿기를 바라던 바로 그 가로수길로 들어가, 나무 하나 아래 앉아 있었던 것이다. 이것이 그들의 사연이었다. 그들이 시간을 즐겁게 보냈고 제가 얼마나 오래 자리를 비웠는지 모르고 있다는 것은 뻔했다. 패니에게 가장 큰 위안은, 에드먼드가 저를 무척 보고 싶어 했고, 그녀가 이미 지쳐 있지만 않았더라면 틀림없이 저를 데리러 돌아왔으리라는 다짐을 들은 것이었다. 그러나 그것으로도, 그가 겨우 몇 분이라 해 놓고 꼬박 한 시간을 남겨 두었던 그 아픔을 다 씻어 내기에는, 또 그 긴 시간 내내 그들이 무슨 이야기를 나누었는지 알고 싶은 그 궁금함을 다 쫓아내기에는 넉넉지 못했다. 그리하여 그 모든 것의 끝은, 다들 뜻을 모아 저택으로 돌아갈 채비를 하는 가운데, 그녀에게는 실망과 울적함이었다.\n\n테라스로 오르는 계단 아래에 이르렀을 때, 러시워스 부인과 노리스 부인이 계단 위에 나타났다. 저택을 떠난 지 한 시간 반 만에, 이제 막 야생 숲으로 나설 참이었다. 노리스 부인은 더 빨리 움직이기엔 너무 알차게 시간을 보낸 터였다. 조카딸들의 즐거움을 가로막는 어떤 얄궂은 일들이 벌어졌든 간에, 그녀 자신은 더없이 흡족한 아침을 보냈으니 말이다. 가정부가 꿩을 두고 한참 상냥하게 이야기를 나눈 끝에 그녀를 낙농장으로 데려가, 그 소들에 대해 죄다 일러 주고, 이름난 크림치즈 만드는 법까지 가르쳐 준 것이다. 게다가 줄리아가 그들 곁을 떠난 뒤로는 정원사와 마주쳤는데, 그녀는 그와 더없이 흡족한 사이가 되었다. 그 손자의 병에 대해 그를 바로잡아, 그게 학질이라고 일러 주고 그 부적을 하나 주마 약속했더니, 그가 그 답례로 제가 가꾼 가장 귀한 화초 묘상을 죄다 보여 주고, 실은 아주 진기한 히스 한 포기까지 그녀에게 선물한 것이다.\n\n이 뜻밖의 만남을 계기로 다들 함께 저택으로 돌아가, 나머지 사람들이 돌아오고 만찬이 나올 때까지 소파며 잡담이며 계간 평론지로 되는대로 시간을 흘려보냈다. 버트럼가 아가씨들과 두 신사가 들어온 것은 저물녘이었고, 그들의 산책은 절반쯤이나 유쾌했을 뿐, 그날의 목적에 관해서는 딱히 쓸모 있는 것을 아무것도 낳지 못한 듯했다. 저희 말로는 다들 서로 뒤꽁무니를 좇아 걷기만 했다는데, 마침내 이루어진 그 합류는, 패니가 보기에, 무언가 변경을 정하기에 너무 늦었다고 다들 인정한 것만큼이나, 화기를 되살리기에도 너무 늦었던 듯했다. 줄리아와 러시워스 씨를 바라보며 그녀는, 그들 가운데 언짢은 가슴이 저 혼자만은 아님을 느꼈다. 저마다 얼굴에 그늘이 드리워 있었다. 크로퍼드 씨와 버트럼 양은 훨씬 흥겨웠고, 그녀가 보기에 그는 만찬 내내 다른 두 사람의 자잘한 앙심을 없애고 다시 두루 화기를 돌리려 각별히 애쓰는 듯했다.\n\n만찬에 이어 이내 차와 커피가 나왔다. 집까지 십 마일을 가야 하니 한 시간도 허투루 쓸 수 없었다. 그리하여 식탁에 앉은 때부터, 마차가 문간에 올 때까지는 분주하나 하잘것없는 일들이 빠르게 이어졌다. 그동안 노리스 부인은 부산을 떨며 가정부에게서 꿩알 몇 개와 크림치즈 하나를 얻어 내고, 러시워스 부인에게 정중한 인사말을 한껏 늘어놓은 끝에, 앞장설 채비를 마쳤다. 바로 그때 크로퍼드 씨가 줄리아에게 다가가 말했다. “제 말벗을 잃지 않았으면 하는데요—이렇게 탁 트인 자리에서 저녁 공기가 겁나지만 않으시다면요.” 그 청은 미리 내다본 것은 아니었으나 아주 반갑게 받아들여졌고, 줄리아의 하루는 시작만큼이나 좋게 끝날 성싶었다. 버트럼 양은 뭔가 다른 것을 마음에 두고 있던 터라 좀 실망했으나, 자기야말로 정말로 그가 마음에 둔 사람이라는 확신이 그 아래서 그녀를 다독여, 러시워스 씨의 작별 인사를 마땅한 태도로 받을 수 있게 해 주었다. 그는 확실히 그녀를 마부석에 올려 주는 것보다 사륜마차 안으로 이끌어 주는 편이 더 흡족했고, 그 배치로 그의 만족감은 굳어진 듯했다.\n\n“그래 패니, 오늘 너한텐 참 좋은 날이었지, 정말이지.” 마차가 공원을 지날 때 노리스 부인이 말했다. “처음부터 끝까지 온통 즐거움뿐이었잖니! 너를 보내 주려고 궁리한 네 이모 버트럼 부인과 나한테 너는 정말 크게 고마워해야 마땅해. 참 알찬 하루 놀이를 즐겼구나!”\n\n마리아는 대뜸 이렇게 말할 만큼 딱 그만큼 못마땅해 있었다. “이모야말로 오늘 꽤 잘 챙기신 것 같은데요. 무릎엔 좋은 것들이 가득하시고, 여기 우리 사이엔 무슨 바구니 하나가 아까부터 사정없이 제 팔꿈치를 찧어 대고 있잖아요.”\n\n“얘야, 그건 그저 예쁜 히스 한 포기일 뿐이란다. 그 친절한 늙은 정원사가 기어이 나한테 받으라고 해서. 하지만 그게 거치적거리면 당장 내 무릎에 놓으마. 자, 패니, 이 꾸러미는 네가 좀 들어 다오. 아주 조심해서, 떨어뜨리지 말고. 크림치즈란다, 우리가 저녁에 먹은 그 훌륭한 것하고 꼭 같은 거야. 그 착한 늙은 휘터커 부인이 내가 치즈 하나를 받아 가지 않고는 도무지 만족을 못 하겠다지 뭐냐. 나도 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁해질 때까지 버틸 만큼 버텼단다. 게다가 그게 딱 우리 동생이 좋아할 만한 종류인 걸 알았거든. 그 휘터커 부인은 정말 보배야! 내가 아랫상에서 포도주가 허용되느냐고 물었더니 어찌나 질겁을 하던지. 게다가 흰 드레스를 입었다고 하녀 둘을 내보냈다더구나. 치즈 조심해라, 패니. 이제 나는 다른 꾸러미하고 바구니는 그럭저럭 잘 들 수 있으니.”\n\n“또 뭘 우려내셨어요?” 소더턴이 그토록 칭송받는 게 반쯤은 흐뭇해진 마리아가 말했다.\n\n“우려내다니, 얘야! 그저 저 예쁜 꿩알 네 개일 뿐이야. 휘터커 부인이 기어이 나한테 떠안기려 들었거든. 아무리 마다해도 안 듣더라니까. 내가 아주 혼자 사는 걸로 안다면서, 그런 살아 있는 것 몇 마리를 두는 게 나한테는 참 좋은 소일거리가 될 거라고 하더구나. 하기야 정말 그럴 테지. 젖짜기 하녀한테 첫 번째 빈 암탉 밑에 넣어 두라고 시킬 참이야. 잘 자라면 우리 집으로 옮겨다 닭장 하나를 빌려 두면 되고. 외로운 시간에 그것들을 돌보는 게 나한테는 큰 낙이 될 거야. 그리고 운이 좋으면 네 어머니한테도 좀 나눠 주마.”\n\n아름다운 저녁이었다. 포근하고 고요했으며, 마차 길은 자연의 그 잔잔함이 베풀 수 있는 만큼 유쾌했다. 그러나 노리스 부인이 말을 그치자, 마차 안 사람들에게 그것은 온통 말없는 여정이 되었다. 다들 대체로 기운이 바닥나 있었다. 그리고 그날이 즐거움을 더 안겼는지 아픔을 더 안겼는지를 가리는 일이, 아마 거의 모두의 상념을 차지하고 있었을 것이다.\n\n# 제11장\n\n소더턴에서 보낸 그날은, 온갖 아쉬움에도 불구하고, 버트럼 자매에게 얼마 뒤 맨스필드에 다다른 앤티가발 편지가 안겨 준 것보다 훨씬 더 흐뭇한 감정을 선사했다. 아버지를 생각하기보다 헨리 크로퍼드를 생각하는 편이 한결 즐거웠다. 그리고 이 편지들이 억지로 하게끔 만든 일, 곧 머잖은 어느 시점에 아버지가 다시 잉글랜드에 와 계시리라 생각하는 것은, 더없이 달갑잖은 노릇이었다.\n\n십일월이 그가 돌아오기로 정해진 암울한 달이었다. 토머스 경은 오랜 경험과 노심초사가 허락하는 한 더없이 단호한 어조로 그 일을 적어 보냈다. 그의 볼일은 거의 마무리되어 구월의 우편선으로 배편을 잡을 만하다고 스스로 여길 만했고, 그리하여 그는 십일월 초에는 사랑하는 가족과 다시 함께하리라는 희망을 품고 그날을 손꼽아 기다렸다.\n\n마리아는 줄리아보다 더 가엾은 처지였다. 그녀에게는 아버지가 남편을 데려오는 셈이었고, 그녀의 행복을 누구보다 마음 쓰는 그 벗이 돌아오면 그녀는 스스로 제 행복을 걸기로 택한 그 연인과 맺어질 것이었기 때문이다. 그것은 음울한 앞날이었고, 그녀가 할 수 있는 일이라고는 그 위에 안개를 드리우고서, 안개가 걷히면 뭔가 다른 것이 보이기를 바라는 것뿐이었다. 아무래도 십일월 초는 되기 어려울 터였다. 으레 지체되는 일이 있게 마련이고, 항해가 나쁘다든가 무슨 일이 생긴다든가 하니까. 바라보면서도 눈을 감고, 헤아리면서도 지각을 닫아 버리는 사람이면 누구나 위안으로 삼는, 바로 그 편들어 주는 무슨 일 말이다. 아마 적어도 십일월 중순은 되리라. 십일월 중순이면 석 달이나 남았다. 석 달이면 열세 주였다. 열세 주 사이엔 많은 일이 일어날 수 있었다.\n\n토머스 경은 제 딸들이 그의 귀환을 두고 느끼는 심경의 절반만 짐작했더라도 깊이 상심했을 것이며, 그 소식이 또 다른 젊은 아가씨의 가슴에 불러일으킨 관심을 알았던들 좀처럼 위안을 얻지 못했으리라. 크로퍼드 양은 오라비와 함께 맨스필드 파크로 저녁을 보내러 걸어 올라왔다가 그 반가운 소식을 들었다. 그리고 겉으로는 예의를 차리는 것 말고는 그 일에 아무 관심도 없는 양, 조용한 축하 한마디로 제 감정을 죄다 쏟아 낸 양 보였으나, 실은 그리 쉽게 채워지지 않는 주의를 기울여 그 소식을 들었다. 노리스 부인이 편지의 자초지종을 늘어놓았고, 화제는 그것으로 끝났다. 그러나 차를 마신 뒤, 버트럼 자매와 러시워스 씨와 헨리 크로퍼드가 다들 피아노 곁에서 촛불을 매만지느라 분주한 동안, 크로퍼드 양은 에드먼드와 패니와 함께 열린 창가에 서서 땅거미 지는 풍경을 내다보다가, 문득 그 무리 쪽으로 돌아서며 이렇게 말해 화제를 되살렸다. “러시워스 씨가 어쩜 저리 행복해 보일까요! 십일월을 생각하고 있나 봐요.”\n\n에드먼드도 러시워스 씨를 돌아보았으나, 딱히 할 말이 없었다.\n\n“아버님의 귀환은 무척 뜻깊은 일이 되겠지요.”\n\n“정말 그럴 거예요, 그토록 오래 떠나 계셨으니. 오래일 뿐 아니라 그 숱한 위험까지 무릅쓴 부재였으니 말이에요.”\n\n“게다가 그것은 또 다른 뜻깊은 일들의 전조이기도 하겠고요. 누이분의 혼인이며, 씨가 서품 받으시는 일 말이에요.”\n\n“그렇지요.”\n\n“기분 상해하진 마세요.” 그녀가 웃으며 말했다. “그런데 이 일을 보니 옛 이교의 영웅들이 떠오르네요. 이역에서 큰 공을 세우고는, 무사히 돌아온 것을 두고 신들께 제물을 바치던 그 사람들 말이에요.”\n\n“이 경우엔 제물이랄 게 없는걸요.” 에드먼드가 진지한 미소를 띠고, 다시 피아노 쪽을 흘긋 보며 대꾸했다. “그건 온전히 누이 스스로 택한 일이니까요.”\n\n“아 네, 저도 알아요. 그냥 농담한 거예요. 누이분은 어느 젊은 여자라도 할 법한 일을 했을 뿐이고, 더없이 행복하시리라는 걸 저도 믿어 의심치 않아요. 제가 말한 또 하나의 제물이야, 물론 씨는 못 알아들으시겠지만요.”\n\n“제가 서품 받는 것도, 정말이지 마리아가 시집가는 것 못지않게 제 뜻으로 하는 일이랍니다.”\n\n“씨의 마음과 아버님의 형편이 그토록 잘 들어맞다니 다행이네요. 듣자 하니 이 근방에 씨를 위해 마련해 둔 아주 좋은 성직록이 있다면서요.”\n\n“그것이 제 마음을 기울게 했으리라 여기시는 거군요?”\n\n“하지만 그건 결코 아니에요, 저는 확신해요.” 패니가 외쳤다.\n\n“고맙구나, 패니, 그리 말해 주다니. 하지만 그건 나 스스로도 단언할 수 있는 것 이상이란다. 오히려 그런 성직록이 나를 위해 마련돼 있음을 안 것이 아마도 내 마음을 기울게 했겠지. 그리고 나는 그게 그른 일이라고도 생각지 않아. 애초에 이겨 내야 할 천성의 거리낌이랄 게 없었고, 젊어서 넉넉한 살림이 보장돼 있음을 안다고 해서 사내가 더 못난 성직자가 되어야 할 까닭도 나로선 모르겠어. 나는 안전한 손에 맡겨져 있었던 거야. 나 스스로 그릇된 쪽으로 휘둘리지 않았기를 바라고, 또 우리 아버지께서 그것을 용납하시기엔 너무나 양심 곧은 분이심을 나는 확신해. 내가 마음이 기운 것은 틀림없지만, 그건 나무랄 데 없이 그런 것이라 생각한다.”\n\n“그건 이런 것과 똑같은 일이에요.” 패니가 잠깐 뜸을 들인 뒤 말했다. “제독의 아들이 해군에 들어가거나, 장군의 아들이 육군에 몸담는 것과요. 그런데 아무도 거기서 잘못을 보지 않잖아요. 아무도 그들이 제 벗들이 가장 잘 이끌어 줄 수 있는 길을 택하는 것을 이상히 여기지 않고, 겉보기만큼 진심이 아니지 않을까 의심하지도 않고요.”\n\n“아니에요, 프라이스 양, 게다가 그럴 만한 까닭이 있죠. 해군이든 육군이든 그 직업은 그 자체로 정당함을 지녀요. 온갖 것이 그 편이거든요—영웅다움, 위험, 부산함, 세련됨 말이에요. 군인과 뱃사람은 언제든 사교계에서 환영받지요. 사내가 군인이 되고 뱃사람이 되는 것을 아무도 이상히 여길 수 없어요.”\n\n“하지만 승진이 확실한 채로 서품 받는 사내의 동기는 얼마든지 의심받아 마땅하다, 이 말씀이신가요?” 에드먼드가 말했다. “크로퍼드 양의 눈에 떳떳하려면, 그는 아무 성직록도 기약되지 않은 더없이 막막한 처지에서 그 일을 해야 한다는 거군요.”\n\n“뭐라고요! 성직록도 없이 서품을 받는다고요! 안 되죠, 그거야말로 정말 미친 짓이에요. 순전한 광기죠.”\n\n“그럼 여쭤볼까요, 사내가 성직록을 지니고도 서품을 못 받고 성직록 없이도 못 받는다면, 교회는 대체 무엇으로 채운답니까? 아니, 그만두죠. 크로퍼드 양은 분명 뭐라 답해야 할지 모르실 테니까요. 하지만 저는 크로퍼드 양 자신의 논리에서 성직자에게 유리한 점 하나는 얻어 내야겠어요. 성직자는 크로퍼드 양이 군인과 뱃사람이 직업을 고를 때의 유혹이자 보상이라며 높이 치는 그런 것들—영웅다움이며 떠들썩함이며 세련됨—에 이끌릴 수가 없고, 오히려 그 모두가 그에게 불리하니, 그런 만큼 그는 제 직업을 고르면서 진심이나 선한 뜻이 모자라지 않느냐는 의심을 덜 받아 마땅하지요.”\n\n“오! 물론 그는 애써 벌어야 하는 수고보다 이미 마련된 수입을 마다 않을 만큼 진심이겠죠. 그리고 남은 평생 먹고 마시고 살찌는 것 말고는 아무것도 안 하려는 더없이 갸륵한 뜻을 품었을 테고요. 그건 게으름이에요, 버트럼 씨, 정말이지. 사내를 성직자로 만드는 건 게으름과 안락에 대한 사랑, 갸륵한 야심의 결여, 좋은 벗을 사귈 취향의 결여, 또는 남을 즐겁게 해 주는 수고를 감내할 마음의 결여랍니다. 성직자란 게을러 빠지고 제 잇속만 차리면 그만이죠—신문이나 읽고, 날씨나 살피고, 아내와 다투면서요. 궂은일은 죄다 그의 부목사가 하고, 정작 그 자신의 소임이라곤 만찬을 드는 것뿐이랍니다.”\n\n“그런 성직자가 있는 건 틀림없지요. 하지만 그런 이들이, 크로퍼드 양이 그것을 성직자 일반의 성품으로 여겨도 좋을 만큼 흔하지는 않다고 봐요. 이 폭넓은—그리고, 이런 말씀 드려도 될는지, 뻔한—비난에서 크로퍼드 양은 스스로 판단하시는 게 아니라, 늘 그 의견을 들어 오던 편견에 찬 사람들에게서 판단하시는 게 아닌가 싶어요. 크로퍼드 양 자신의 관찰이 성직자에 대해 그리 많은 앎을 주었을 리가 없거든요. 그토록 단정 지어 단죄하시는 그 부류의 사내들 가운데, 실제로 친분이 있는 이는 아주 적을 수밖에 없으실 테니까요. 크로퍼드 양은 숙부님 식탁에서 들은 이야기를 하고 계신 거예요.”\n\n“저는 제게 세상의 통념으로 여겨지는 바를 말하는 거예요. 그리고 어떤 의견이 두루 퍼져 있다면, 그건 대개 옳지요. 저야 성직자의 가정생활을 그리 많이 보지 못했지만, 그건 너무나 많은 이들이 지켜보는 터라 정보가 모자랄 일이 없거든요.”\n\n“교육받은 사내들의 어떤 집단이든, 그 종파가 무엇이건, 마구잡이로 단죄받는 곳이라면, 거기엔 정보가 모자라거나, 아니면” 하고 그는 미소 지으며 말을 이었다. “다른 무언가가 모자란 게 틀림없어요. 크로퍼드 양의 숙부님과 그 동료 제독들이야, 좋든 나쁘든 늘 눈앞에서 치워 버리고만 싶어 하던 군종 신부들 말고는, 성직자에 대해 아마 아는 게 거의 없으셨을 테고요.”\n\n“가엾은 윌리엄 오빠! 오빠는 앤트워프호의 군종 신부님께 큰 은혜를 입었는걸요.” 이것은 패니가 나직이 내뱉은 다정한 탄사로, 오가던 이야기와는 몰라도 제 마음결에는 더없이 잘 들어맞는 말이었다.\n\n“저는 숙부에게서 제 의견을 얻는 버릇이 워낙 없는 터라,” 크로퍼드 양이 말했다. “그렇게 짐작하실 일은 좀처럼—그리고 씨가 저를 그토록 몰아붙이시니, 한마디 하지 않을 수 없네요. 저라고 성직자가 어떤 사람인지 볼 방도가 아주 없는 건 아니랍니다. 지금 이 순간 바로 제 오라비인 그랜트 박사의 식객으로 있으니까요. 그랜트 박사가 제게 더없이 다정하고 살갑고, 또 정말 신사이며, 감히 말하건대 훌륭한 학자에다 영리하고, 좋은 설교를 곧잘 하며, 아주 존경받을 만한 분이긴 하지만, 제가 보기에 그이는 게으르고 잇속만 차리는 식도락가예요. 매사에 제 입맛부터 챙겨야 하고, 남의 편의를 위해서는 손가락 하나 까딱 않으며, 게다가 요리사가 실수라도 하면 그 훌륭한 아내에게 심통을 부리는 사람이죠. 사실대로 말하면, 헨리와 제가 바로 오늘 저녁 이리로 밀려나다시피 한 것도, 그이가 영 삭이지 못한 새끼 거위 요리에 대한 실망 탓이 얼마간 있어요. 가엾은 언니는 남아서 그걸 고스란히 견뎌야 했고요.”\n\n“저런, 크로퍼드 양이 못마땅해하시는 것도 정말 무리가 아니네요. 그건 성미의 큰 결점이고, 제멋대로 구는 몹쓸 버릇 탓에 한층 나빠진 것이죠. 그리고 언니께서 그 때문에 시달리는 걸 지켜보는 일이, 크로퍼드 양 같은 심성에는 더없이 괴로울 게 틀림없고요. 패니야, 이건 우리한테 불리하구나. 우리로선 그랜트 박사를 감싸 보려야 감쌀 수가 없겠어.”\n\n“그렇긴 해요.” 패니가 대답했다. “하지만 그렇다고 그분의 직업까지 두둔하기를 그만둘 필요는 없어요. 그랜트 박사가 어떤 직업을 택하셨든, 그분은 거기에 그—좋다고는 할 수 없는 성미를 지니고 들어가셨을 테니까요. 그리고 해군이든 육군이든 거기서라면 지금보다 훨씬 많은 사람을 제 밑에 두셨을 테니, 저는 그분이 뱃사람이나 군인이었더라면 성직자일 때보다 더 많은 이를 불행하게 만드셨으리라 생각해요. 게다가 그랜트 박사에게 달리 됐으면 싶은 구석이 무엇이 있든, 그건 더 분주하고 세속적인 직업에서라면 한층 나빠질 위험이 컸으리라고 저는 짐작하지 않을 수 없어요. 그런 데서라면 시간도 의무도 더 적었을 테고—지금의 처지에선 도저히 피할 수 없는 그 자기 성찰을, 적어도 그 잦은 성찰을, 그분은 거기서라면 피해 버릴 수도 있었을 테니까요. 사내가—그랜트 박사처럼 지각 있는 사내가, 주마다 남들에게 그 도리를 가르치고, 주일마다 두 번 교회에 나가, 그토록 좋은 설교를 그토록 훌륭한 태도로 하면서, 정작 자신은 조금도 나아지지 않을 수는 없어요. 그건 그분을 생각하게 만들 게 틀림없고요. 그리고 저는 그분이 성직자 아닌 다른 무엇이었을 때보다, 지금 더 자주 스스로를 다잡으려 애쓰시리라 믿어 의심치 않아요.”\n\n“그 반대를 증명할 길이야 물론 없지요. 하지만 프라이스 양, 저는 양에게 그보다 나은 운명을 빌어 드리고 싶어요. 제 설교에 기대어야 붙임성이 생기는 사내의 아내가 되는 것보다는요. 그런 사내야 주일마다 설교로 제 기분을 좋게 돋운다 한들, 월요일 아침부터 토요일 밤까지 새끼 거위 타령으로 다투고 있을 테니, 그것만으로도 충분히 고약하잖아요.”\n\n“패니와 자주 다툴 수 있는 사내라면,” 에드먼드가 다정하게 말했다. “어떤 설교로도 어찌해 볼 수 없는 위인일 게 틀림없어요.”\n\n패니는 창 쪽으로 더 깊이 몸을 돌렸다. 그리고 크로퍼드 양은 상냥한 투로 “프라이스 양은 칭찬을 듣는 것보다 칭찬받을 만한 일을 하는 데 더 익숙한가 봐요.” 한마디 할 짬밖에 없었으니, 버트럼 자매가 합창에 어울리자고 간곡히 청하는 통에, 그녀는 총총히 악기 쪽으로 걸어갔고, 에드먼드는 그 뒤를 바라보며 그녀의 그 숱한 미덕에—살가운 몸가짐에서부터 가볍고도 우아한 발걸음에 이르기까지—넋을 잃고 감탄해 마지않았다.\n\n“저기 좋은 기질이 가는구나, 나는 확신해.” 이윽고 그가 말했다. “저기 결코 남을 아프게 하지 않을 성미가 가고 있어! 걸음걸이가 어쩜 저리 고울까! 게다가 남의 뜻에 어쩜 저리 선뜻 맞춰 줄까! 청하자마자 곧장 어울려 주다니. 참으로 안타깝구나.” 그는 잠깐 생각에 잠겼다가 덧붙였다. “저 사람이 그런 손에 자라 왔다는 게 말이야!”\n\n패니도 그렇다고 맞장구쳤고, 그토록 기다리던 합창에도 아랑곳없이 그가 그녀와 함께 창가에 그대로 머무는 것을, 그리고 이내 그의 눈길이 그녀의 눈길처럼 바깥 풍경으로 향하는 것을 흐뭇이 지켜보았다. 그 풍경에는 엄숙하고 마음을 다독이고 사랑스러운 온갖 것이, 구름 한 점 없는 밤의 눈부심 속에, 깊은 숲 그늘과 대비를 이루며 펼쳐져 있었다. 패니가 제 감정을 입에 담았다. “여기 조화가 있어요!” 그녀가 말했다. “여기 안식이 있어요! 여기, 온갖 그림과 온갖 음악을 뒤로 제쳐 둘 만한 것, 오직 시만이 그려 보려 애쓸 수 있는 것이 있어요! 여기, 온갖 근심을 잠재우고 마음을 환희로 들어 올릴 만한 것이 있어요! 이런 밤을 내다보고 있으면, 저는 세상에 악도 슬픔도 있을 수 없을 것만 같아요. 그리고 만일 사람들이 자연의 숭고함에 좀 더 마음을 기울이고, 이런 풍경을 바라보며 자기 자신에게서 좀 더 벗어날 수 있다면, 악도 슬픔도 분명 지금보다 줄어들 거예요.”\n\n“네 그런 열정을 듣는 게 나는 좋구나, 패니. 사랑스러운 밤이야. 그리고 어느 정도라도 너처럼 느끼도록 배우지 못한 이들, 적어도 어려서 자연을 사랑하는 취향을 얻지 못한 이들은 참으로 가여운 사람들이지. 그들은 아주 많은 것을 잃는 거란다.”\n\n“오빠가 저에게 그 주제를 두고 생각하고 느끼는 법을 가르쳐 주셨는걸요.”\n\n“나야 아주 총명한 제자를 두었던 거지. 저기 아르크투루스가 무척 밝게 빛나는구나.”\n\n“네, 그리고 큰곰자리도요. 카시오페이아를 볼 수 있으면 좋겠어요.”\n\n“그러려면 잔디밭으로 나가야 해. 무섭진 않겠니?”\n\n“조금도요. 우리가 별 구경을 한 지도 참 오래됐네요.”\n\n“그래, 어쩌다 그리됐는지 모르겠구나.” 합창이 시작되었다. “이게 끝날 때까지만 있자꾸나, 패니.” 그가 창에 등을 돌리며 말했다. 그리고 노래가 무르익어 갈수록, 그녀는 그 또한 슬금슬금 다가가는 것을, 조금씩 조금씩 악기 쪽으로 나아가는 것을 지켜보는 쓰라림을 맛보았다. 노래가 멎었을 때 그는 이미 가수들 바로 곁에 서서, 그 합창을 한 번 더 듣자고 누구보다 간곡히 조르는 축에 끼어 있었다.\n\n패니는 창가에서 홀로 한숨지었고, 마침내 감기 든다는 노리스 부인의 으름장에 등 떠밀려 그 자리를 떠났다.\n\n# 제12장\n\n토머스 경은 십일월에 돌아오기로 되어 있었으나, 그의 맏아들은 볼일이 있어 그보다 일찍 집으로 불려 오게 되었다. 구월이 다가오자 버트럼 씨의 소식이 전해졌는데, 처음에는 사냥터지기에게 보낸 편지로, 다음에는 에드먼드에게 보낸 편지로였다. 그리고 팔월이 저물 무렵 그가 몸소 도착하여, 자리가 마련되거나 크로퍼드 양이 청하는 대로 다시금 명랑하고 상냥하고 늠름하게 굴었다. 경마며 웨이머스며, 여러 모임과 벗들 이야기를 늘어놓았는데, 그것은 육 주 전이었더라면 그녀가 얼마간 흥미를 갖고 들었을 법한 것이었으나, 이제는 실제로 견주어 볼 힘 덕에 그녀가 그 아우를 더 좋아함을 더없이 확실히 굳혀 줄 따름이었다.\n\n그것은 여간 짜증스러운 일이 아니었고, 그녀는 진심으로 그 일이 유감스러웠다. 하지만 사실이 그러했다. 그리고 이제 그 형과 혼인할 마음이 없기는커녕, 그녀는 제 미모를 아는 사람이라면 으레 지니는 더없이 소박한 자존심이 요구하는 것 이상으로 그를 끌어당기고 싶은 마음조차 없었다. 딱히 즐길 거리밖에는 아무런 볼일도 없이, 오로지 제 뜻만 좇아 그토록 오래 맨스필드를 비운 것으로 미루어, 그가 그녀에게 마음이 없음은 더없이 분명했다. 그리고 그 무심함은 그녀 자신의 무심함으로 넘치도록 되갚아지고 있었으니, 설령 그가 지금 당장 맨스필드 파크의 주인으로, 머잖아 그리될 완전한 토머스 경으로 나선다 한들, 그녀는 자신이 그를 받아들일 수 있으리라고 믿지 않았다.\n\n버트럼 씨를 맨스필드로 도로 데려온 그 철과 볼일이, 크로퍼드 씨를 노퍽으로 데려갔다. 에버링엄은 구월 초에 그가 없어서는 안 될 형편이었다. 그는 두 주 예정으로 떠났다—그것은 버트럼 자매 둘 다 마땅히 경계심을 품었어야 할, 그리고 언니를 시샘하던 줄리아마저 그의 살가움을 미덥잖게 여기고 그가 돌아오지 않기를 바라는 것이 더없이 마땅함을 인정하게 했어야 할, 그런 지루한 두 주였다. 또한 그것은, 그가 제 동기를 살피고 제 게으른 허영을 방치하는 것이 무엇으로 치닫는지 곰곰이 되짚어 보는 버릇이 좀 더 있었더라면, 사냥과 잠자기 사이사이의 짬만으로도, 자신이 좀 더 오래 떨어져 있어야 함을 깨닫기에 넉넉한 여가이기도 했다. 그러나 넉넉한 살림과 나쁜 본보기 탓에 무심하고 잇속만 차리는 그는, 지금 이 순간 너머를 내다보려 하지 않았다. 아름답고 영리하며 부추기기까지 하는 그 자매는, 물릴 대로 물린 그의 마음에 심심풀이가 되어 주었다. 그리고 노퍽에서는 맨스필드의 사교적 즐거움에 맞먹을 만한 것을 도무지 찾지 못하자, 그는 흔쾌히 정해진 때에 그리로 돌아왔고, 그가 더 희롱해 볼 작정으로 다가간 이들에게서 꼭 그만큼 흔쾌한 환대를 받았다.\n\n마리아는 곁에 러시워스 씨밖에 없었고, 그날 사냥이 잘됐네 못됐네 하는 시시콜콜한 이야기며, 제 개들에 대한 자랑이며, 이웃들에 대한 시샘이며, 그들의 자격을 미심쩍어하는 소리며, 밀렵꾼을 잡겠다는 열의를 되풀이해 들어야 하는 신세였는데—이런 화제란 말하는 쪽에 얼마간 재주가 있거나 듣는 쪽에 얼마간 정이 있지 않고서는 여자의 마음에 좀처럼 파고들지 못하는 법이라—그녀는 크로퍼드 씨를 사무치게 그리워했다. 그리고 매인 데도 없고 할 일도 없는 줄리아는, 그를 그리워할 권리를 한층 더 온전히 누렸다. 자매는 저마다 제가 그의 마음에 든 쪽이라 믿었다. 줄리아는 제가 바라는 바를 곧이곧대로 믿고 싶어 하는 그랜트 부인의 넌지시 건네는 말에 그리 믿을 만한 구석이 있었고, 마리아는 크로퍼드 씨 자신이 넌지시 건네는 말에 그러했다. 모든 것이 그가 떠나기 전과 똑같은 물길로 되돌아왔다. 그의 태도가 두 사람 저마다에게 어찌나 활기차고 상냥하던지 어느 쪽의 마음도 잃지 않으면서도, 뭇사람의 눈길을 끌 만한 한결같음이며 진득함이며 지극한 정성이며 뜨거움에는 딱 미치지 못한 채 멈추어 있었다.\n\n이 무리 가운데 못마땅한 구석을 찾아낸 사람은 패니뿐이었다. 소더턴에서 보낸 그날 이후로, 그녀는 크로퍼드 씨가 두 자매 중 누구와 함께 있는 것을 눈여겨보지 않고서는 볼 수 없었고, 미심쩍어하거나 나무라는 마음 없이 보는 일도 드물었다. 그리고 만일 그녀가 다른 모든 일에서 제 판단을 행사하는 만큼 그 판단에 자신이 있었더라면, 자신이 똑똑히 보고 있고 공정히 헤아리고 있음을 확신했더라면, 그녀는 아마 늘 속내를 털어놓던 사람에게 뭔가 중요한 이야기를 건넸을 것이다. 그러나 실은 넌지시 한마디 흘려 보는 것에 그쳤고, 그 말은 흘려지고 말았다. “저는 좀 놀랐어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “크로퍼드 씨가 이렇게 빨리 다시 돌아온 게요. 앞서 이곳에 그토록 오래, 꼬박 일곱 주나 머물다 갔으면서 말이에요. 저는 그분이 변화와 여기저기 돌아다니는 걸 워낙 좋아하시는 줄 알았거든요. 그래서 그분이 일단 떠나고 나면 분명 무슨 일이 생겨 다른 데로 이끌려 가시리라 생각했어요. 그분은 맨스필드보다 훨씬 흥청거리는 데에 익숙하시잖아요.”\n\n“그거야 그 사람한테 좋은 일이지.” 에드먼드가 대답했다. “그리고 아마 그 누이도 반가워할 거야. 누이는 그 사람의 진득하지 못한 버릇을 못마땅해하거든.”\n\n“사촌 언니들이 어쩜 그리 그분을 좋아할까요!”\n\n“그래, 그 사람이 여자를 대하는 태도야 마음에 들 수밖에 없지. 그랜트 부인은 그 사람이 줄리아를 마음에 두고 있다고 짐작하는 모양이더라. 나야 그런 낌새를 별로 못 봤지만, 정말 그랬으면 싶구나. 진지한 정만 붙으면 씻은 듯 사라질 흠 말고는, 그 사람한테 딱히 흠잡을 데가 없거든.”\n\n“버트럼 언니가 약혼한 몸만 아니라면,” 패니가 조심스레 말했다. “저는 이따금 거의 그분이 줄리아보다 언니를 더 마음에 들어 하시는 게 아닌가 싶기도 해요.”\n\n“그건 아마, 패니 네가 아는 것보다 오히려 그 사람이 줄리아를 가장 좋아한다는 쪽에 더 무게를 실어 주는 얘기일지도 몰라. 사내란 아직 제 마음을 온전히 정하기 전에는, 정작 마음에 두고 있는 여자보다 그 여자의 자매나 가까운 벗을 더 눈에 띄게 대하는 일이 흔하거든. 크로퍼드는 마리아에게서 조금이라도 위험을 느꼈다면 여기 머물 만큼 분별없는 사람이 아니야. 그리고 마리아가 제 감정이 그리 깊지 않음을 그토록 확실히 보여 준 마당에, 나는 그 애가 조금도 걱정되지 않는구나.”\n\n패니는 자기가 잘못 본 것이 틀림없다 여기고, 앞으로는 달리 생각하기로 마음먹었다. 그러나 에드먼드에게 고분고분 따르려는 온갖 노력에도, 또 이따금 다른 몇몇에게서 눈치챈, 줄리아가 크로퍼드 씨의 선택인 듯 말해 주는 그 아귀 맞는 눈빛과 언질들의 온갖 도움에도, 그녀는 무엇을 어찌 생각해야 할지 늘 알 수 있는 것은 아니었다. 어느 날 저녁, 그녀는 이모 노리스가 그 일을 두고 품은 바람과 심경을, 또 그와 얼마쯤 비슷한 대목에 대한 러시워스 부인의 심경까지 곁에서 알게 되었고, 그 이야기를 들으며 의아해하지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 굳이 듣지 않아도 되었다면 얼마나 좋았으랴, 그때 다른 젊은이들은 모두 춤을 추는데 그녀만이 더없이 마지못해 난롯가 보호자들 틈에 끼어 앉아, 파트너를 구할 제 온갖 희망이 걸려 있던 손위 사촌이 다시 들어오기만을 애타게 기다리고 있었으니 말이다. 그것은 패니의 첫 무도회였다. 하기야 여느 아가씨의 첫 무도회 같은 채비도 화려함도 없이, 그저 그날 오후에야 떠오른 착상이었고, 하인방에 근래 바이올린 켜는 이가 하나 들어온 것과, 그랜트 부인 및 버트럼 씨를 찾아와 방금 도착한 새 벗의 힘을 빌리면 다섯 쌍쯤은 세울 수 있으리라는 데에 기대어 꾸며진 것이었다. 그래도 그것은 네 곡을 추는 동안 패니에게 더없이 행복한 무도회였으니, 그녀는 단 십오 분이라도 그것을 놓치는 것이 못내 서러웠다. 기다리고 바라며, 춤추는 이들을 보았다 문을 보았다 하는 사이, 앞서 말한 두 부인 사이의 이 대화가 그녀의 귀에 억지로 밀려들었다—\n\n“제 생각엔 말이에요, 부인,” 두 번째로 짝이 된 러시워스 씨와 마리아 쪽으로 눈길을 두며 노리스 부인이 말했다. “이제 다시 행복한 얼굴들을 좀 보게 될 것 같네요.”\n\n“네, 정말 그래요, 부인.” 상대가 점잔 빼는 미소를 지으며 대꾸했다. “이제는 지켜보는 데도 얼마간 흐뭇함이 있겠어요. 그리고 저 둘이 굳이 떨어져야 했다니 좀 딱한 노릇이지 싶어요. 저런 처지의 젊은것들이야 흔한 격식쯤은 안 지켜도 눈감아 줄 만하잖아요. 우리 아들이 그러자고 청하지 않은 게 이상하네요.”\n\n“분명 청했을 거예요, 부인. 러시워스 씨야 그런 데 소홀할 분이 아니지요. 하지만 우리 사랑스러운 마리아는 예의범절을 어찌나 깍듯이 여기는지, 요즘 좀처럼 보기 힘든 그 참된 조심성을 지녔거든요, 러시워스 부인—남들 눈에 유별나 보이는 걸 피하려는 그 마음 말이에요! 부인, 지금 이 순간 저 애 얼굴만 좀 보세요. 앞서 두 곡을 출 때와 어쩜 저리 다를까요!”\n\n버트럼 양은 과연 행복해 보였으니, 그 눈은 기쁨으로 반짝였고 자못 활기차게 이야기하고 있었다. 줄리아와 그 파트너인 크로퍼드 씨가 가까이 있었기 때문이다. 다들 한데 모여 있었다. 앞서 그녀가 어떤 얼굴이었는지 패니로서는 도무지 떠올릴 수 없었으니, 그때 그녀 자신은 에드먼드와 춤을 추느라 마리아에게는 마음을 쓰지 않았던 것이다.\n\n노리스 부인이 말을 이었다. “젊은것들이 저리 마땅하게 행복하고, 저리 잘 어울리고, 저리 격에 딱 맞는 걸 보니 참으로 흐뭇하네요, 부인! 사랑하는 토머스 경께서 얼마나 기뻐하실지 생각하지 않을 수가 없어요. 그런데 부인은, 또 한 쌍이 맺어질 가망에 대해서는 어찌 보세요? 러시워스 씨가 좋은 본보기를 보였으니, 그런 일이란 참 옮아붙기 쉽거든요.”\n\n오로지 제 아들밖에는 눈에 들어오지 않던 러시워스 부인은 어리둥절할 따름이었다.\n\n“저 위쪽 한 쌍 말이에요, 부인. 저기 아무 낌새도 안 보이세요?”\n\n“어머나! 줄리아 양과 크로퍼드 씨 말이군요. 네, 정말이지 아주 어여쁜 짝이에요. 그이 재산은 얼마나 되나요?”\n\n“연 사천 파운드요.”\n\n“그럼 됐네요. 그보다 더 못 가진 사람들이야 제 가진 것에 만족해야지요. 연 사천 파운드면 넉넉한 재산이고, 게다가 아주 점잖고 진득한 젊은이로 보이니, 줄리아 양이 무척 행복하기를 바라요.”\n\n“아직 정해진 일은 아니에요, 부인. 그저 우리끼리만 하는 얘기지요. 하지만 저는 그리되리라는 걸 거의 의심치 않아요. 그이가 마음 쓰는 태가 갈수록 유별나지고 있거든요.”\n\n패니는 더는 들을 수가 없었다. 듣는 것도 의아해하는 것도 한동안 죄다 멈추었으니, 버트럼 씨가 다시 방에 들어와 있었던 것이다. 그리고 그에게 청을 받는 것이 크나큰 영광이리라 느끼면서도, 그녀는 그리되고야 말리라 여겼다. 그가 그들의 작은 무리 쪽으로 다가왔다. 그러나 그녀에게 춤을 청하기는커녕, 그는 그녀 곁으로 의자를 끌어당겨 앉아, 병든 말의 지금 상태며 방금 헤어진 마부의 소견을 늘어놓았다. 패니는 그리될 일이 아님을 알아챘고, 천성이 겸손한지라 그것을 기대한 제가 당치 않았음을 곧바로 느꼈다. 말 이야기를 마치자 그는 탁자에서 신문을 집어 들어 훑어보며 나른한 투로 말했다. “춤이 추고 싶으면, 패니, 내가 상대해 주마.” 그 못지않은 예의로 그녀는 그 청을 사양했으니, 춤출 마음이 없었던 것이다. “그거 잘됐구나.” 그가 한결 활기찬 어조로 신문을 다시 내던지며 말했다. “나야 죽도록 지쳤거든. 저 착한 사람들이 어찌 저리 오래 버티는지 그저 신기할 따름이야. 저런 어리석은 짓에서 재미를 찾으려면 다들 사랑에 빠져 있어야 할 텐데—내 짐작엔 정말 그런가 봐. 저들을 봐라, 죄다 연인들 아니냐—예이츠와 그랜트 부인만 빼고 말이야—그런데 우리끼리 얘기지만, 저 딱한 부인도 어느 누구 못지않게 연인이 아쉬울 게 틀림없어. 저 박사와 살자면 지독히도 따분한 삶일 테니.” 이렇게 말하며 그는 그랜트 박사의 의자 쪽으로 짓궂은 표정을 지어 보였는데, 하필 박사가 바로 그의 팔꿈치 곁에 와 있는 게 드러나는 바람에, 그토록 잽싸게 낯빛과 화제를 바꾸지 않을 수 없었으니, 패니는 온갖 사정에도 불구하고 웃음을 참기 어려웠다. “아메리카에서 벌어진 이 일이 참 야릇하지요, 그랜트 박사님! 어찌 보십니까? 저는 세상일을 어찌 생각해야 할지 알고 싶을 때면 늘 박사님을 찾는답니다.”\n\n“얘야, 톰,” 이윽고 그의 이모가 외쳤다. “네가 춤을 안 추고 있으니, 우리랑 카드 한 판 어울려도 싫달 것 없겠지, 그렇지?” 그러고는 자리에서 일어나 그에게 다가와 그 청을 밀어붙이며, 나직이 덧붙였다. “러시워스 부인 자리를 만들어 드려야 하거든. 네 어머니가 어찌나 마음을 쓰는지, 그렇다고 몸소 앉아 계실 짬은 못 내는구나, 술 장식 뜨느라 말이다. 그러니 너하고 나하고 그랜트 박사면 딱 맞겠어. 그리고 우리야 반 크라운짜리 판이지만, 알다시피 박사하고는 반 기니씩 걸어도 되고.”\n\n“더없이 기쁘겠어요.” 그가 냉큼 벌떡 일어서며 소리 내어 대답했다. “그야말로 큰 즐거움이겠지만, 지금 막 춤추러 가려던 참이라서요. 자, 패니,” 하며 그녀의 손을 잡고, “더는 꾸물대지 마라, 안 그러면 춤이 끝나 버릴 테니.”\n\n패니는 아주 기꺼이 이끌려 갔다. 하기야 사촌 오빠에게 그리 큰 고마움을 느끼기도, 그가 분명 그러했듯이 남의 잇속과 제 잇속을 가려내기도 어려운 노릇이었지만.\n\n“정말이지 어지간히도 겸손한 청이더군.” 그들이 걸어 나오는데 그가 분해하며 외쳤다. “앞으로 두 시간 동안 나를 카드판에 못 박아 두겠다는 거야, 늘 티격태격하는 저 이모하고 그랜트 박사, 게다가 휘스트를 대수학만큼이나 모르는 저 참견쟁이 노파하고 말이지. 우리 착한 이모가 좀 덜 부산했으면 좋겠다! 게다가 청하는 꼴이라니! 격식도 없이, 다들 보는 앞에서, 나더러 거절할 여지라곤 조금도 안 남기고 말이야. 바로 그게 내가 무엇보다 질색하는 거란다. 청을 받는, 고를 여지를 주는 시늉을 하면서, 동시에 그게 무엇이든 바로 그 일을 하지 않을 수 없게끔 몰아붙이는 것—그것만큼 내 부아를 돋우는 게 없어! 다행히 너하고 춤출 생각을 못 했더라면 나는 거기서 빠져나오지도 못했을 거야. 정말 너무한 노릇이지. 하지만 이모가 일단 무슨 생각을 머리에 담으면, 무엇으로도 못 말린다니까.”\n\n# 제13장\n\n지체 높으신 존 예이츠 씨, 이 새로운 벗은, 유행과 낭비의 버릇, 그리고 그런대로 넉넉한 독립 재산을 지닌 어느 귀족의 작은아들이라는 것 말고는 딱히 내세울 게 없는 사람이었다. 그러니 토머스 경이라면 아마 그가 맨스필드에 발을 들이는 것을 결코 바람직하게 여기지 않았으리라. 버트럼 씨가 그와 알게 된 것은 웨이머스에서였는데, 거기서 둘은 같은 무리에 끼어 열흘을 함께 보냈고, 그 우정은—우정이라 부를 수 있다면—예이츠 씨가 틈나는 대로 언제든 오는 길에 맨스필드에 들르라는 청을 받고, 또 그가 오겠노라 약속함으로써 굳게 다져진 터였다. 그리고 그는 예상보다 다소 일찍 왔는데, 그가 웨이머스를 떠나 합류하러 갔던 또 다른 벗의 집에서, 흥겹게 놀자고 모인 큰 무리가 갑작스레 흩어진 탓이었다. 그는 실망의 날개를 달고, 머릿속은 온통 연극 생각으로 가득 찬 채 왔으니, 그 모임이 연극 모임이었기 때문이다. 그가 한 배역을 맡은 그 연극은 이틀 뒤면 상연될 참이었는데, 그 집안의 가장 가까운 친척 하나가 갑자기 세상을 떠나는 바람에 그 계획이 무너지고 배우들이 뿔뿔이 흩어지고 만 것이다. 그토록 행복에 가까이, 그토록 명성에 가까이, 콘월에 있는 지체 높으신 레이븐쇼 경의 저택 에클스포드에서 열린 그 사가(私家) 연극을 기리는 긴 기사—물론 온 무리를 적어도 열두 달은 불멸의 이름으로 남겨 주었을 그 기사—에 그토록 가까이 다가갔다가! 그토록 가까이서 그 모든 것을 잃었으니, 그것은 사무치게 느껴질 만한 손해였고, 예이츠 씨는 그 이야기밖에 할 줄을 몰랐다. 에클스포드와 그 극장, 그 무대 장치며 의상이며 연습이며 우스갯소리가 그의 마르지 않는 화제였고, 지난날을 자랑하는 것이 그의 유일한 위안이었다.\n\n그로서는 다행스럽게도, 연극에 대한 사랑이란 워낙 두루 퍼진 것이고 연기에 대한 갈망이란 젊은이들 사이에서 워낙 강렬한 것이라, 그가 아무리 떠들어도 듣는 이들의 흥미를 눌러 이기지는 못했다. 배역을 처음 정하는 대목에서부터 맺음말에 이르기까지 그 모두가 홀리는 듯했고, 그 일에 한몫 끼었더라면 싶지 않은 이도, 제 솜씨를 시험해 보기를 망설일 이도 거의 없었다. 그 연극은 「연인들의 맹세」였고, 예이츠 씨는 카셀 백작을 맡기로 되어 있었다. “대수롭잖은 배역이었지요.” 그가 말했다. “도무지 제 취향도 아니고, 다시는 결코 안 맡을 그런 배역이었어요. 하지만 저는 아무 트집도 안 잡기로 마음먹었지요. 레이븐쇼 경과 공작이 제가 에클스포드에 닿기도 전에 맡을 만한 배역 둘을 몽땅 차지해 버렸거든요. 레이븐쇼 경이 제게 제 배역을 넘기겠다고 하긴 했지만, 아시다시피 그걸 받을 수야 없는 노릇이었죠. 저는 그분이 딱했어요, 제 역량을 그토록 잘못 가늠하셨으니 말이에요. 그분은 남작 역에는 도무지 어울리지 않았거든요—목소리가 가냘픈 자그마한 분이라, 처음 십 분만 지나면 늘 목이 쉬어 버렸죠. 그게 작품을 크게 망쳤을 게 틀림없어요. 하지만 저는 아무 트집도 안 잡기로 작정했으니까요. 헨리 경은 공작이 프레더릭 역에 못 미친다고 여겼지만, 그건 헨리 경이 그 배역을 제가 탐냈기 때문이었어요. 실은 둘 중 더 나은 쪽 손에 들어간 게 분명했는데 말이에요. 헨리 경이 그토록 뻣뻣한 사람인 줄은 저도 놀랐어요. 다행히 작품의 힘이 그이한테 달려 있진 않았죠. 우리 아가사는 흉내 낼 수 없을 만큼 훌륭했고, 공작도 많은 이들이 아주 대단하다고 여겼어요. 그리고 대체로, 분명 놀랍도록 잘 굴러갔을 거예요.”\n\n“정말이지 딱한 일이었어요” 라거나, “저는 정말 당신이 무척 안됐다고 생각해요” 하는 것이, 귀 기울여 동정하는 이들의 다정한 대꾸였다.\n\n“불평할 만한 일은 못 되지요. 하지만 그 딱한 노부인이 하필 그보다 더 나쁜 때에 돌아가실 수도 없었을 거예요. 그리고 우리한테 꼭 필요했던 사흘 동안만이라도 그 부고를 눌러 둘 수 있었더라면 하는 바람을 도무지 떨칠 수가 없어요. 고작 사흘이었거든요. 게다가 한낱 할머니일 뿐이고, 그 모든 일이 이백 마일이나 떨어진 데서 벌어졌으니, 저는 별 탈이 없었으리라 봐요. 그런 말이 나오기도 했고요, 저도 알아요. 하지만 아마 잉글랜드에서 가장 반듯한 사람 축에 들 레이븐쇼 경이 그 말을 들으려고도 하지 않았죠.”\n\n“희극 대신 막간극이라니.” 버트럼 씨가 말했다. “「연인들의 맹세」는 막을 내리고, 레이븐쇼 경 내외분만 남아 「우리 할머니」를 손수 연기하게 되셨군요. 뭐, 과부 재산이 그분을 위로해 드릴 테고요. 그리고 어쩌면, 우리끼리 얘기지만, 그분은 남작 역을 하다 제 체면과 제 폐가 어찌 될까 떨리기 시작해서, 물러나는 게 싫지 않았는지도 모르죠. 그리고 예이츠, 당신 손해를 메워 드리는 뜻에서, 아무래도 우리가 맨스필드에 자그마한 극장을 하나 세우고 당신더러 우리 감독을 맡아 달라 청해야겠어요.”\n\n이것은 그 순간의 착상이었으나 그 순간으로 끝나지 않았다. 연기에 대한 욕구가 깨어난 것이다. 게다가 그 누구보다도 지금 이 집의 주인 된 이의 마음에 가장 강하게 깨어났으니, 그는 웬만한 새것이면 무엇이든 틀림없는 즐거움이 될 만큼 여가가 넘쳤고, 게다가 연기라는 새것에 꼭 들어맞는, 발랄한 재주와 희극적 취향까지 그만큼 지니고 있었다. 그 생각은 자꾸만 되살아났다. “아, 뭔가 시험해 볼 에클스포드의 극장과 무대만 있다면.” 자매도 저마다 그 바람에 맞장구쳤다. 그리고 온갖 향락의 소란 속에서도 이것만은 아직 맛보지 못한 즐거움이던 헨리 크로퍼드는, 그 착상에 자못 생기가 돌았다. “저는 정말이지,” 그가 말했다. “지금 이 순간 여태 쓰인 어떤 배역이든 맡을 만큼 얼마든지 바보가 될 수 있을 것 같아요. 샤일록이나 리처드 3세에서부터, 진홍색 상의에 삼각모를 쓴 소극의 노래하는 주인공에 이르기까지 말이에요. 저는 무엇이든, 아니 무엇이든 다 될 수 있을 것만 같아요. 영어로 쓰인 어떤 비극에서든 희극에서든, 고래고래 외치고 사납게 몰아치거나, 한숨짓거나, 익살스레 펄쩍펄쩍 뛸 수 있을 것 같거든요. 뭐라도 좀 해 봅시다. 그게 연극 반 편이든, 한 막이든, 한 장면이든 말이에요. 우리를 막을 게 뭐가 있겠어요? 이 얼굴들은 분명 아니겠지요.” 하며 버트럼 자매 쪽을 바라보았다. “그리고 극장으로 말하면, 극장이 무슨 대수예요? 우리끼리 즐기자는 것뿐인걸요. 이 집 어느 방이든 넉넉할 거예요.”\n\n“막은 있어야겠죠.” 톰 버트럼이 말했다. “막으로 쓸 초록색 나사 몇 야드면, 그거면 어쩌면 넉넉할지도 몰라요.”\n\n“오, 넉넉하고말고요.” 예이츠 씨가 외쳤다. “옆 날개 한둘만 세우고, 문은 평면에 그려 넣고, 배경 서너 개만 내려뜨리면 돼요. 이런 계획에야 그 이상은 아무것도 필요 없지요. 그저 우리끼리 즐기는 거라면 더 바랄 게 없어요.”\n\n“아무래도 우리는 그보다 덜한 걸로 만족해야 할 거예요.” 마리아가 말했다. “시간도 넉넉지 않을 테고, 다른 어려움도 생길 테니까요. 차라리 크로퍼드 씨의 생각을 좇아, 극장이 아니라 상연 자체를 우리 목표로 삼는 게 낫겠어요. 우리가 아는 훌륭한 연극들도 많은 대목이 무대 장치와 상관없이 되잖아요.”\n\n“아니지.” 에드먼드가 말했는데, 그는 슬며시 마음이 놓이지 않아 귀를 기울이기 시작한 참이었다. “우리 이왕 할 거면 어설프게 하진 맙시다. 정 연기를 할 거라면, 평토간이며 박스석이며 위층 관람석까지 완벽하게 갖춘 극장에서 하고, 처음부터 끝까지 온전한 연극을 한 편 올립시다. 그것도 독일 연극으로다가, 뭐든 상관없으니, 잘 속고 잘 뒤바뀌는 막간극에, 무용에, 호른파이프 춤에, 막 사이사이 노래까지 곁들여서요. 에클스포드를 능가하지 못할 바에야, 우리는 아무것도 안 하는 거나 마찬가지죠.”\n\n“얘, 에드먼드, 심술궂게 굴지 마.” 줄리아가 말했다. “오빠만큼 연극을 좋아하는 사람도 없고, 오빠만큼 연극 하나 보겠다고 멀리까지 가 본 사람도 없잖아.”\n\n“맞아, 진짜 연기, 잘 다져진 훌륭한 진짜 연기를 보는 거라면 말이지. 하지만 그 일에 이골이 나도록 배운 적 없는 이들의 설익은 시도를 보자고는, 나는 이 방에서 옆방까지도 걸어가고 싶지 않아. 교양이며 예의범절이라는 온갖 걸림돌을 헤치고 나가야 하는 신사 숙녀들 한 무리 말이야.”\n\n그러나 잠깐 말이 끊긴 뒤에도 그 화제는 여전히 이어졌고, 조금도 수그러들지 않는 열의로 논의되었다. 논의가 오갈수록, 또 서로의 마음을 알게 될수록 저마다의 욕구가 부풀어 갔다. 그리고 톰 버트럼은 희극을 좋아하고 그 누이들과 헨리 크로퍼드는 비극을 좋아한다는 것, 그리고 그들 모두의 마음에 들 작품을 찾는 것보다 세상에 더 쉬운 일은 없으리라는 것 말고는 아무것도 정해지지 않았으나, 무엇이 됐든 연극을 하고야 말겠다는 결의만은 어찌나 굳어 보이던지 에드먼드는 여간 불편하지 않았다. 그는 가능하다면 그것을 막기로 마음먹었다. 비록 그 어머니는 식탁에서 오간 그 대화를 똑같이 들었으면서도 조금도 못마땅한 기색을 보이지 않았지만.\n\n바로 그날 저녁, 그에게 제 힘을 시험해 볼 기회가 찾아왔다. 마리아와 줄리아와 헨리 크로퍼드와 예이츠 씨는 당구실에 있었다. 톰이 그들 곁을 떠나 응접실로 돌아오니, 에드먼드는 난롯가에 서서 생각에 잠겨 있었고, 조금 떨어진 소파에는 버트럼 부인이, 그리고 그 바로 곁에는 패니가 부인의 일감을 매만지며 앉아 있었다. 톰은 들어서면서 이렇게 말문을 열었다—“우리 것처럼 지독히도 형편없는 당구대는, 이 지상 어디에서도 다시 못 만날 거야. 나는 더는 못 참겠어. 그리고 이젠, 다시는 무엇으로도 나를 그리로 꾈 수 없을 거라고 말할 수 있을 것 같아. 하지만 방금 한 가지 좋은 걸 확인했지. 그 방이야말로 극장으로 딱 맞는 방이라는 거야. 그 모양이며 길이가 극장에 꼭 알맞거든. 게다가 저 안쪽 끝의 문들은, 아버지 방의 책장만 옮기면 오 분 만에 서로 통하게 만들 수 있는데, 우리가 앉아서 하나 빌자고 궁리했다 한들 바로 그걸 빌었을 만큼 안성맞춤이야. 그리고 아버지 방은 훌륭한 분장실이 될 거고. 마치 일부러 그러려고 당구실에 붙어 있는 것 같다니까.”\n\n“톰, 설마 진심으로 연기를 할 작정은 아니겠지?” 형이 난롯가로 다가오자 에드먼드가 나직한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“진심이 아니라니! 이보다 더 진심일 수도 없어, 정말이야. 거기 뭐 놀랄 게 있다고?”\n\n“내 생각엔 그건 아주 잘못된 일이야. 일반적으로 봐도 사가 연극이란 얼마간 탈 잡힐 구석이 있는데, 우리 처지에서는 그런 걸 시도하는 게 몹시 지각없는 짓, 아니 지각없는 것 이상이라고 여길 수밖에 없어. 그건 아버지를 생각하면 크게 무정한 짓이 될 거야, 저리 떠나 계신 데다 어느 정도는 늘 위험 속에 계시는데 말이지. 게다가 내 생각엔 마리아를 생각해도 경솔한 짓이야. 그 애 처지가, 이것저것 다 헤아려 보면 아주 미묘하잖아, 지극히 미묘하고 말이야.”\n\n“넌 뭐든 어찌 그리 심각하게 받아들이니! 우리가 무슨 아버지 돌아오실 때까지 한 주에 세 번씩 연기를 하고, 온 고을 사람을 다 부르기라도 하는 것처럼 말이야. 하지만 그런 구경거리를 벌이자는 게 아니야. 우리끼리 그저 자그마한 재미를 좀 보자는 것뿐이지, 분위기나 좀 바꾸고 새로운 데에 우리 재주나 좀 부려 보자는 거라고. 우리는 관객도, 소문나는 것도 원치 않아. 흠잡을 데라곤 도무지 없는 더없이 온당한 연극을 고르는 일쯤이야 우리한테 맡겨도 된다고 봐. 그리고 나로선, 어느 존경받는 작가의 우아하게 쓰인 글을 입에 올리는 것이, 우리 제 말로 지껄이는 것보다 우리 누구에게 더 큰 해나 위험이 되리라곤 도무지 상상이 안 가는걸. 나는 아무 두려움도, 아무 거리낌도 없어. 그리고 아버지가 안 계신 걸로 말하면, 그건 반대할 거리이기는커녕, 나는 오히려 하나의 명분이라고 봐. 아버지 돌아오시기를 기다리는 게 어머니껜 무척 애태우는 시간일 게 틀림없거든. 그러니 우리가 그 애태움을 달래 드리고 앞으로 몇 주간 어머니 기운을 북돋아 드릴 수만 있다면, 나는 우리 시간을 아주 잘 쓴 거라 여길 테고, 아버지도 분명 그리 여기실 거야. 어머니껜 정말이지 애태우는 시간이니까.”\n\n그가 이 말을 하자, 둘은 저마다 어머니 쪽을 바라보았다. 버트럼 부인은 소파 한구석에 파묻히듯 기대앉아, 건강과 부와 안락과 평온의 표본 그 자체인 모습으로, 막 살포시 졸음에 빠져들고 있었고, 그동안 패니는 부인을 위해 그 일감의 자잘한 어려운 대목들을 손봐 주고 있었다.\n\n에드먼드는 빙그레 웃으며 고개를 저었다.\n\n“이런! 이건 안 되겠군.” 톰이 껄껄 웃으며 의자에 털썩 몸을 던지고 외쳤다. “하기야, 어머니, 어머니의 그 애태움이라니—내가 하필 그 대목에서 재수가 없었네.”\n\n“무슨 일이냐?” 반쯤 깬 사람 특유의 묵직한 목소리로 부인이 물었다. “나 안 자고 있었다.”\n\n“아유, 그럼요, 어머니, 아무도 그렇게 안 여겼어요!” 그는 버트럼 부인이 다시 꾸벅꾸벅 졸기 시작하자, 곧바로 앞서의 화제와 자세와 목소리로 돌아가 말을 이었다. “하지만 이것만은 내가 우겨야겠어, 우리가 아무 해도 끼치지 않으리라는 거 말이야.”\n\n“난 네 말에 동의 못 해. 아버지라면 그걸 완전히 못마땅해하시리라고 난 확신해.”\n\n“난 그 반대라고 확신하는데. 젊은이가 재주를 부리는 걸 우리 아버지만큼 좋아하고 북돋아 주는 분도 없어. 그리고 연기니 대사 읊기니 낭송이니 하는 부류라면, 아버지는 늘 뚜렷한 취향을 지니셨다고 봐. 우리가 어릴 적에도 아버지가 그걸 부추기셨던 게 분명해. 우리가 바로 이 방에서, 아버지 즐거우시라고 율리우스 카이사르의 주검을 두고 몇 번이나 슬퍼했고, ‘죽느냐 사느냐’를 읊어 댔던가? 그리고 어느 성탄 연휴 내내, 내 평생 저녁마다 ‘내 이름은 노발이었다’를 외웠던 게 분명하다니까.”\n\n“그건 아주 다른 얘기야. 너도 그 차이를 알 만하잖아. 아버지는 우리가 학생일 때 말을 잘하기를 바라셨지, 다 큰 딸들이 연극을 하기를 바라시진 않을 거야. 아버지의 예의 관념은 엄격하니까.”\n\n“그쯤이야 나도 다 알아.” 톰이 언짢은 투로 말했다. “나도 너 못지않게 아버지를 잘 알고, 아버지 딸들이 아버지 언짢게 할 짓은 하나도 못 하게 내가 단속할 거야. 네 일이나 챙겨, 에드먼드, 나머지 식구는 내가 챙길 테니.”\n\n“정 연기를 할 작정이라면,” 끈덕진 에드먼드가 대꾸했다. “부디 아주 자그맣고 조용하게 하기를 바랄 뿐이야. 그리고 극장까지 꾸미려 드는 건 안 된다고 봐. 그건 아버지 안 계신 새 아버지 집에 함부로 손대는 짓이라, 도무지 떳떳할 수가 없어.”\n\n“그런 일이라면 뭐든 내가 책임질게.” 톰이 단호한 어조로 말했다. “아버지 집은 상하게 하지 않아. 아버지 집을 아끼는 마음이야 나도 너 못지않게 크다고. 그리고 방금 내가 말한 그런 손질로 말하면—책장 하나 옮기고, 문 하나 여는 거, 아니 당구도 안 치면서 한 주간 당구실을 쓰는 정도 가지고—그걸 아버지가 반대하실 거라 여긴다면, 아버지 떠나시기 전보다 우리가 이 방에 더 앉아 있고 아침식사방엔 덜 앉아 있는 걸 반대하시리라거나, 누이 피아노를 방 이쪽에서 저쪽으로 옮기는 걸 반대하시리라고 여기는 거나 매한가지야. 순 말도 안 되는 소리지!”\n\n“새로 벌이는 그 일이, 벌이는 것 자체로는 그르지 않다 쳐도, 비용 면에서는 그를 거야.”\n\n“그래, 그런 일을 벌이는 비용이야 어마어마하겠지! 어쩌면 이십 파운드나 들지도 몰라. 극장 비슷한 거야 틀림없이 있어야겠지만, 더없이 단출한 계획으로 할 거야. 초록색 막 하나에 자잘한 목수 일 조금, 그게 다야. 그리고 목수 일이야 크리스토퍼 잭슨이 몸소 집에서 다 할 수 있으니, 비용 얘기를 꺼내는 것 자체가 우스운 노릇이지. 그리고 잭슨을 쓰는 한, 토머스 경께는 뭐든 다 괜찮을 거야. 이 집에서 너 말고는 아무도 보거나 판단할 줄 모른다고 여기지 마. 하기 싫으면 너나 연기를 하지 마, 그렇다고 나머지 사람들까지 죄다 네 뜻대로 부리려 들진 말고.”\n\n“아니, 나 자신이 연기하는 걸로 말하면,” 에드먼드가 말했다. “그것만은 내가 단연코 반대야.”\n\n톰은 그 말이 떨어지기 무섭게 방을 나가 버렸고, 에드먼드는 홀로 남아 앉아, 생각에 잠긴 언짢음 속에 난롯불을 뒤적였다.\n\n그 모든 것을 다 들었고, 그동안 내내 온갖 감정에서 에드먼드와 마음을 같이해 온 패니가, 이제 무슨 위로라도 건네려는 안타까운 마음에 조심스레 입을 뗐다. “어쩌면 그분들 마음에 맞는 연극을 못 찾을지도 몰라요. 오빠 형과 언니들의 취향이 아주 다른 것 같으니까요.”\n\n“그건 기대할 게 못 돼, 패니. 그 사람들이 이 계획을 밀어붙인다면, 뭐라도 찾아낼 거야. 나야 누이들한테 얘기해서 말려 보긴 하겠지만, 내가 할 수 있는 건 그게 다야.”\n\n“노리스 이모라면 오빠 편을 드실 것 같은데요.”\n\n“그러시겠지. 하지만 이모는 톰한테도 누이들한테도 쓸모 있을 만한 영향력이 없어. 그리고 내가 스스로 그들을 설득하지 못한다면, 이모를 통해 애써 보려 들지 않고 그냥 일이 흘러가는 대로 두겠어. 집안 다툼이야말로 무엇보다 나쁜 화근이니, 아예 서로 으르렁대느니 차라리 무슨 짓이든 하는 게 나아.”\n\n이튿날 아침 그가 이야기를 나눌 기회를 얻은 그 누이들은, 톰과 꼭 마찬가지로 그의 충고를 못 견뎌 했고, 꼭 마찬가지로 그의 말에 굽히지 않았으며, 꼭 마찬가지로 즐거움을 좇는 일에 뜻이 굳었다. 어머니는 그 계획에 반대가 없었고, 그들은 아버지가 못마땅해하실까 조금도 겁내지 않았다. 그토록 많은 존경받는 집안에서, 그토록 많은 지체 높은 여인들이 해 온 일에 무슨 해가 있을 리 없었다. 그리고 오누이와 가까운 벗들만 끼고, 저희 말고는 결코 소문날 일 없는 이런 계획에서 나무랄 구석을 찾아낼 수 있다면, 그거야말로 미쳐 날뛰는 결벽일 따름이었다. 줄리아는 과연 마리아의 처지가 각별한 조심과 신중을 요하리라는 것은 인정하는 눈치였다—하지만 그게 저한테까지 미칠 수는 없었다—저는 자유로운 몸이었으니까. 그리고 마리아는, 제 약혼이 오히려 자신을 그만큼 더 구속 위로 들어 올려 줄 뿐이고, 줄리아보다 아버지든 어머니든 여쭐 일이 더 적게 만들어 준다고 여기는 게 분명했다. 에드먼드는 기대할 게 별로 없었으나, 그래도 여전히 그 화제를 밀어붙이고 있는데, 그때 헨리 크로퍼드가 목사관에서 막 건너와 방으로 들어서며 소리쳤다. “우리 극장엔 일손 걱정 없어요, 버트럼 양. 잔심부름꾼 걱정도 없고요. 제 누이가 안부를 전하면서, 저희 무리에 끼워 주기를 바란다지 뭡니까. 그리고 여러분이 손수 맡기 싫은 늙은 시녀 역이든 얌전한 말벗 역이든 뭐든 기꺼이 맡겠답니다.”\n\n마리아가 에드먼드에게 흘긋 눈길을 던졌는데, 그것은 이런 뜻이었다. “이제 뭐라고 할래? 메리 크로퍼드도 똑같이 여긴다면 우리가 틀렸을 리 없잖아?” 그리고 에드먼드는, 할 말을 잃은 채, 연기의 매력이 천재의 마음까지 능히 홀릴 수 있음을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 사랑에 빠진 이 특유의 눈속임으로, 그 전갈에서 다른 무엇보다도 그 살갑고 너그러운 뜻에만 마음을 두게 되었다.\n\n계획은 진척되었다. 반대는 헛일이었다. 그리고 노리스 부인으로 말하면, 그녀가 반대하고 싶어 하리라 여긴 것은 그의 오산이었다. 그녀가 꺼낸 어려움이란 것도, 그녀에게 절대적인 힘을 지닌 맏조카와 맏조카딸에게 오 분이면 죄다 눌려 버리는 것들뿐이었다. 게다가 그 온갖 채비가 누구에게도 비용이 거의 안 들고 그녀 자신에게는 한 푼도 안 드는 데다, 그 안에서 부산 떨고 법석대고 거들먹거릴 온갖 재미를 미리 내다보았고, 또 그동안 제 돈으로 한 달을 지내 온 제집을 떠나 그들의 집에 눌러앉아, 매시간을 그들 시중에 바쳐야 한다고 여기는 데서 오는 당장의 이득까지 챙겼으니, 사실 그녀는 그 계획이 여간 반갑지 않았다.\n\n# 제14장\n\n패니의 짐작은 에드먼드가 여긴 것보다 옳은 쪽에 더 가까운 듯했다. 누구에게나 맞을 연극을 찾는 일은 결코 사소한 노릇이 아님이 드러났다. 목수는 지시를 받고 치수를 재었으며, 적어도 두 차례 어려움을 이렇게 저렇게 내놓았다 물렸다 했고, 계획과 비용을 늘려야 할 필요를 낱낱이 분명히 해 둔 채 이미 일에 착수했건만, 연극은 여전히 찾는 중이었다. 다른 채비들도 진행 중이었다. 노샘프턴에서 초록색 나사가 어마어마하게 한 두루마리 도착해 노리스 부인 손에 재단되었고(그녀의 알뜰한 살림솜씨 덕에 꼬박 사분의 삼 야드나 아꼈다), 실제로 하녀들 손에서 막으로 짜여 가고 있었으나, 그래도 연극은 없었다. 그리고 이런 식으로 이삼 일이 흘러가자, 에드먼드는 어쩌면 끝내 아무 연극도 못 찾을지 모른다는 희망을 슬며시 품기 시작했다.\n\n사실 챙겨야 할 것이 어찌나 많고, 비위를 맞춰야 할 사람이 어찌나 많고, 요구되는 으뜸 배역이 어찌나 많은지, 게다가 무엇보다도 그 연극이 비극이면서 동시에 희극이어야 한다는 요구까지 있었으니, 젊음과 열의가 좇는 그 어떤 일 못지않게 결말이 날 가망은 희박해 보였다.\n\n비극 편에는 버트럼 자매와 헨리 크로퍼드와 예이츠 씨가 있었고, 희극 편에는 톰 버트럼이 있었는데, 아주 혼자만은 아니었으니 메리 크로퍼드의 바람도—비록 정중히 감추어져 있었으나—같은 쪽으로 기운 것이 분명했기 때문이다. 그러나 톰의 단호함과 힘은 딱히 동지를 필요로 하지 않는 듯했다. 그리고 이 크나큰 화해 불가능한 차이는 차치하고라도, 그들이 원하는 것은 전체 배역 수는 아주 적으면서 배역 하나하나는 일급인, 그러면서 주연 여성이 셋인 작품이었다. 훌륭한 연극이란 연극은 죄다 헛되이 훑어보았다. 「햄릿」도, 「맥베스」도, 「오셀로」도, 「더글러스」도, 「노름꾼」도, 비극 편 사람들조차 만족시킬 만한 것은 하나도 내놓지 못했다. 그리고 「연적들」이며 「스캔들 학교」며 「운명의 수레바퀴」며 「법정 상속인」이며, 그 밖에 길게 이어지는 목록이, 잇따라 한층 더 뜨거운 반대에 부딪혀 물려졌다. 누군가에게 곤란거리를 안기지 않는 작품이란 하나도 내놓을 수 없었으니, 이쪽에서든 저쪽에서든 이런 말이 끝없이 되풀이되었다. “아, 안 돼요, 그건 도무지 안 돼요! 고래고래 외치는 비극은 그만둡시다. 배역이 너무 많아요. 그 연극엔 봐줄 만한 여자 배역이 하나도 없어요. 그것만 빼고 뭐든요, 톰. 그건 채울 도리가 없을 거예요. 그런 배역을 누가 맡으리라 기대할 수도 없고요. 처음부터 끝까지 익살뿐이잖아요. 그건 어쩌면 될지도 모르겠는데, 저 천한 배역들만 아니면요. 정 제 의견을 말해야 한다면, 저는 늘 그게 영어로 쓰인 연극 중에 가장 맹숭맹숭한 작품이라 여겨 왔어요. 저야 반대하고 싶진 않아요. 무슨 쓸모든 되면 기쁘겠지만, 이보다 더 나쁘게 고를 수는 없을 것 같네요.”\n\n패니는 지켜보며 귀 기울였는데, 더하거나 덜하게 감춰진 채 그들 모두를 다스리는 듯한 그 이기심을 살피는 것이 아주 재미없지는 않았고, 이 일이 어찌 끝날까 궁금했다. 제 즐거움을 위해서라면 그녀는 무엇이든 상연되기를 바랄 수도 있었으니, 여태 연극이라곤 반 편도 본 적이 없었던 것이다. 그러나 그보다 더 중대한 온갖 것이 그것을 반대하고 있었다.\n\n“이건 도무지 안 되겠어요.” 마침내 톰 버트럼이 말했다. “우리가 시간을 지독히도 허비하고 있어요. 뭐라도 하나 정해야 해요. 뭐가 됐든 상관없으니, 하나만 고릅시다. 우리가 이렇게 까다롭게 굴어선 안 돼요. 배역 몇 개쯤 남아돈다고 겁먹을 것도 없고요. 그럼 배역을 겹쳐 맡으면 되지요. 눈높이를 좀 낮춥시다. 배역이 하찮으면, 그걸 그럴싸하게 살려 내는 우리 공이 그만큼 더 큰 거고요. 이 순간부터 나는 아무 트집도 안 잡겠어요. 여러분이 주는 배역이면 뭐든 맡겠어요, 희극이기만 하다면 말이죠. 희극이기만 하면 돼요, 그 밖에는 아무 조건도 안 걸겠어요.”\n\n그러고서 그는 다섯 번째쯤 「법정 상속인」을 내놓으며, 다만 자기가 두벌리 경을 맡을지 팽글로스 박사를 맡을지만 망설였다. 그리고 나머지 등장인물 가운데도 그럴듯한 비극 배역이 더러 있다고 다른 이들을 설득하려 무척 애썼으나, 무척 헛수고였다.\n\n이 헛된 노력 뒤에 이어진 침묵을 깬 것도 같은 사람이었으니, 그는 탁자에 놓인 숱한 연극 책 가운데 하나를 집어 들어 뒤적이다가, 불쑥 외쳤다—“「연인들의 맹세」! 이게 레이븐쇼 사람들한테 맞았다면 우리한테는 왜 못 맞겠어? 어째서 여태 이 생각을 못 했지? 딱 들어맞을 것 같은데. 다들 어떻게 생각해요? 여기 예이츠와 크로퍼드한테 딱 좋은 훌륭한 비극 배역이 둘 있고, 여기 나한테는—달리 원하는 사람이 없다면—운문 읊는 집사가 있어요. 대수롭잖은 배역이지만 내가 싫어하지 않을 그런 것이고, 앞서 말했듯이 나는 뭐든 맡아 최선을 다하기로 작정했으니까요. 그리고 나머지야 아무나 채우면 돼요. 카셀 백작과 안할트만 남으니까요.”\n\n그 제안은 두루 환영받았다. 다들 우유부단함에 진력이 나던 참이었고, 저마다 처음 든 생각은 이제껏 이토록 모두에게 맞을 성싶은 것이 나온 적이 없다는 것이었다. 예이츠 씨가 유난히 기뻐했다. 그는 에클스포드에서 남작 역을 하고 싶어 한숨지으며 애태우던 터라, 레이븐쇼 경의 열변 한 대목 한 대목이 아까웠고, 제 방에서 그것을 죄다 혼자 되뇌어 열변을 토할 수밖에 없었던 것이다. 빌덴하임 남작으로 폭풍처럼 몰아치는 것이야말로 그의 연극적 야망의 극치였고, 이미 장면 절반을 외고 있다는 이점까지 더해, 그는 이제 더없이 잽싸게 그 배역에 제 몫을 하겠노라 나섰다. 그러나 공평히 말하자면, 그가 그것을 제 것으로 독차지하기로 마음먹은 것은 아니었으니, 프레더릭에도 열변을 토할 만한 아주 좋은 대목이 있음을 떠올리고는, 그 역도 똑같이 기꺼이 맡겠노라 밝혔다. 헨리 크로퍼드는 어느 쪽이든 맡을 태세였다. 예이츠 씨가 고르지 않는 쪽이면 무엇이든 흡족했으니, 짤막한 겸양의 실랑이가 오갔다. 아가사의 온갖 관심을 절로 느끼던 버트럼 양이, 예이츠 씨에게 이건 키와 몸집을 헤아려야 할 대목이니, 그가 가장 훤칠하고 보면 남작 역에 유난히 어울리는 듯하다고 이르며, 그 일을 결판내는 소임을 스스로 떠맡았다. 그녀 말이 지당하다 인정되어 두 배역이 그에 따라 정해지니, 그녀는 마땅한 프레더릭이 나올 것을 확신하게 되었다. 이제 러시워스 씨 말고도 세 배역이 정해진 셈이었는데—러시워스 씨야 뭐든 기꺼이 하겠다고 늘 마리아가 대신 장담해 주었다—그때 언니와 마찬가지로 아가사가 될 작정이던 줄리아가, 크로퍼드 양을 생각하며 께름칙해하기 시작했다.\n\n“이건 자리에 없는 사람한테 도리가 아니에요.” 그녀가 말했다. “여기 여자 배역이 넉넉지 않잖아요. 어밀리아와 아가사야 마리아와 제가 맡으면 되겠지만, 여기 크로퍼드 씨의 누이한테 돌아갈 게 없어요.”\n\n크로퍼드 씨는 그런 건 염려 말라고 했다. 제 누이는 쓸모가 있을 때 말고는 연기하고 싶은 마음이 조금도 없으며, 이런 경우라면 저를 헤아려 주기를 결코 바라지 않으리라 확신한다는 것이었다. 그러나 이 말은 곧바로 톰 버트럼의 반대에 부딪혔으니, 그는 어밀리아 역이야말로 크로퍼드 양이 맡아만 준다면 모든 면에서 그녀의 몫이라고 우겼다. “그 역이 그녀한테 딱 어울리게, 딱 마땅하게 돌아가는 게,” 그가 말했다. “아가사가 내 누이 둘 중 하나한테 그런 것과 매한가지예요. 그건 누이들 쪽에서 양보랄 것도 없어요, 지극히 희극적인 배역이니까요.”\n\n짧은 침묵이 뒤따랐다. 자매는 저마다 마음을 졸였으니, 저마다 아가사에 가장 정당한 권리가 있다 느꼈고, 나머지 사람들이 저한테 그 역을 떠맡겨 주기를 바라고 있었던 것이다. 그새 연극 책을 집어 들고 짐짓 무심한 척 제1막을 뒤적이던 헨리 크로퍼드가, 이내 그 일을 매듭지었다.\n\n“줄리아 버트럼 양께 간곡히 청하건대,” 그가 말했다. “아가사 역은 맡지 말아 주세요, 안 그러면 제 근엄함이 죄다 무너지고 말 테니까요. 정말이지 그러시면 안 됩니다.” 하며 그녀 쪽으로 돌아앉았다. “슬픔과 창백함으로 꾸민 그 얼굴을, 저는 도저히 마주 볼 수가 없어요. 우리가 함께 웃어 댄 그 숱한 웃음이 어김없이 떠오를 테고, 그럼 프레더릭도 그의 배낭도 걸음아 날 살려라 도망칠밖에요.”\n\n유쾌하고 정중하게 건넨 말이었다. 그러나 그 말투는 줄리아의 심경에는 그 속뜻에 묻혀 사라지고 말았다. 그녀는 마리아 쪽으로 던진 눈짓 하나를 보았고, 그것이 제가 입은 상처를 확인시켜 주었다. 그건 하나의 꿍꿍이, 하나의 술책이었다. 저는 업신여겨졌고, 마리아가 뽑힌 것이다. 마리아가 애써 누르려던 그 득의의 미소가, 그것이 얼마나 뻔히 오간 일인지 여실히 드러냈다. 그리고 줄리아가 말을 꺼낼 만큼 저를 다스리기도 전에, 그녀의 오라비마저 이렇게 말해 그녀에게 불리한 쪽으로 힘을 보탰다. “아 그래! 마리아가 아가사를 맡아야지. 마리아가 가장 나은 아가사일 거야. 줄리아는 제가 비극을 더 좋아한다 여기지만, 나는 그 애한테 비극을 못 맡기겠어. 그 애한텐 비극다운 데가 하나도 없거든. 비극다운 낯빛이 아니야. 이목구비가 비극적인 이목구비가 아니고, 걸음도 너무 빠르고, 말도 너무 빠르고, 얼굴 표정도 지그시 못 지킬걸. 차라리 늙은 시골 아낙을 하는 게 나아. 소작인의 아내 말이야. 정말 그게 낫겠어, 줄리아. 소작인의 아내도 아주 어여쁜 배역이야, 정말이라니까. 그 늙은 아낙이 제 남편의 드높은 자비심을 자못 활기차게 받쳐 주거든. 넌 소작인의 아내를 해라.”\n\n“소작인의 아내라니!” 예이츠 씨가 외쳤다. “무슨 말씀이세요? 더없이 하찮고 보잘것없고 시시한 배역인데. 그야말로 흔해 빠진 것이고, 통틀어 봐줄 만한 대사 한 줄 없어요. 누이분더러 그걸 하라니! 그런 걸 내놓다니 모욕이에요. 에클스포드에서는 가정교사가 맡기로 돼 있었어요. 그건 다른 누구한테도 권할 수 없다고 우리 모두 뜻을 모았고요. 감독 나리, 부디 좀 더 공평하게 하시죠. 제 무리의 재능을 그보다 조금이라도 더 알아볼 줄 모른다면, 그 소임을 맡을 자격이 없어요.”\n\n“뭐, 그거야 이보게,” 톰이 말했다. “나와 내 무리가 실제로 연기를 해 보기 전엔 얼마간 어림짐작일 수밖에 없죠. 하지만 나는 줄리아를 깎아내릴 뜻은 없어요. 아가사를 둘 둘 수는 없고, 소작인의 아내는 하나 있어야 하잖아요. 그리고 나야말로 저 늙은 집사 역에 만족함으로써 스스로 절제의 본을 보였다고 봐요. 배역이 하찮으면 그걸 그럴싸하게 살려 내는 데서 그 애 공이 더 커질 테고요. 그리고 그 애가 우스운 것이라면 뭐든 그토록 기를 쓰고 마다한다면, 소작인의 아내 대사 대신 소작인 대사를 맡아서 배역을 통째로 맞바꾸면 돼요. 그 사람은 충분히 근엄하고 애처로우니까요, 정말이지. 연극엔 아무 지장도 없을 테고, 소작인 본인으로 말하면, 그가 제 아내 대사를 얻고 나면, 나야 기꺼이 그 역을 맡고말고요.”\n\n“당신이 아무리 소작인의 아내를 아끼신다 해도,” 헨리 크로퍼드가 말했다. “그걸 누이분한테 어울리게 살려 낼 도리는 없을 거예요. 그리고 우리가 그 착한 성품에 그런 짐을 지우게 둬선 안 되죠. 그 배역을 맡도록 놔둬선 안 됩니다. 제 고분고분함에 저를 내맡기게 둬선 안 돼요. 누이분의 재능은 어밀리아에 쓰여야 해요. 어밀리아는 아가사보다도 오히려 제대로 그려 내기가 더 어려운 인물이거든요. 저는 어밀리아야말로 이 작품 전체에서 가장 어려운 인물이라고 봐요. 지나침 없이 그 장난기와 천진함을 살려 내려면 대단한 역량과 대단한 섬세함이 필요하죠. 저는 훌륭한 여배우들이 그 역에서 실패하는 걸 봤어요. 천진함이란 정말이지 이름난 여배우들도 거의 다 미치지 못하는 경지랍니다. 그건 그들에게 없는 감정의 섬세함을 요구하거든요. 그건 귀한 집 규수를 요구해요—줄리아 버트럼 같은 분을요. 맡아 주실 거죠, 부디?” 하며 그녀 쪽으로 애타게 간청하는 눈빛을 돌리니, 그녀의 마음이 조금 누그러졌다. 그러나 그녀가 뭐라 할지 망설이는 사이, 그녀의 오라비가 다시 끼어들어 크로퍼드 양이 더 마땅하다는 쪽을 들었다.\n\n“아니, 아니, 줄리아는 어밀리아를 하면 안 돼. 그 애한텐 도무지 안 맞는 배역이야. 그 애가 좋아하지도 않을 거고. 잘하지도 못할 거야. 그 앤 키가 너무 크고 다부지거든. 어밀리아는 자그맣고 가뿐하고 소녀 같고 깡충거리는 몸매라야 해. 그건 크로퍼드 양한테 어울려, 오직 크로퍼드 양한테만. 그녀가 딱 그 배역다워 보이고, 틀림없이 훌륭하게 해낼 거라고 나는 믿어.”\n\n이에 아랑곳없이 헨리 크로퍼드는 계속 간청했다. “저희를 위해 꼭 좀 들어주세요,” 그가 말했다. “정말이지 그러셔야 해요. 그 인물을 익히고 나면, 틀림없이 그게 당신께 잘 맞는다고 느끼실 거예요. 비극이 당신 선택일지 몰라도, 분명 희극이 당신을 선택한다는 게 드러날 테니까요. 당신은 먹을거리를 담은 바구니를 들고 감옥으로 저를 찾아오시게 될 거예요. 감옥으로 저를 찾아오기를 마다하진 않으시겠죠? 바구니를 들고 들어오시는 당신 모습이 눈에 선한 것 같아요.”\n\n그 목소리의 힘이 전해졌다. 줄리아는 흔들렸다. 그러나 그는 그저 저를 달래고 어르어, 앞서의 모욕을 눈감게 하려는 것뿐이 아닐까? 그녀는 그를 믿지 못했다. 그 홀대는 더없이 작심한 것이었다. 그는 어쩌면 저를 상대로 음흉한 장난을 치고 있을 뿐인지도 몰랐다. 그녀는 미심쩍은 눈으로 언니를 보았다. 마리아의 낯빛이 그것을 결판내 줄 터였다. 만일 언니가 언짢고 놀란 기색이라면—그러나 마리아는 온통 평온과 흡족함의 낯빛이었고, 이 대목에서 마리아가 기뻐할 수 있는 것은 오직 제 희생 위에서뿐임을 줄리아는 잘 알고 있었다. 그리하여 그녀는 발끈한 노기와 떨리는 목소리로 그에게 말했다. “제가 먹을거리 바구니를 들고 들어올 땐 얼굴 표정 못 지킬까 봐 겁나진 않으시나 봐요—누구는 그리 짐작했을 법도 한데—하지만 제가 그토록 압도적인 건 오직 아가사일 때뿐이었군요!” 그녀는 말을 멈추었다—헨리 크로퍼드는 어쩐지 멋쩍은 낯으로, 뭐라 해야 할지 모르는 듯했다. 톰 버트럼이 다시 입을 열었다—\n\n“크로퍼드 양이 어밀리아를 맡아야 해요. 훌륭한 어밀리아가 될 거예요.”\n\n“내가 그 배역을 탐낼까 봐 겁낼 것 없어요.” 줄리아가 노기 서린 잽싼 말투로 외쳤다. “나는 아가사는 안 할 거고, 그 밖엔 아무것도 안 할 게 분명하니까요. 그리고 어밀리아로 말하면, 세상 온갖 배역 가운데 나한테 가장 진저리 나는 거예요. 나는 그 여자가 아주 질색이야. 밉살스럽고 자그마하고 되바라지고 부자연스럽고 뻔뻔한 계집애라니. 나는 늘 희극을 반대해 왔는데, 이건 희극 중에서도 가장 고약한 꼴이잖아.” 그러고서 그녀는 총총히 방을 나가 버려, 한 사람 넘게 어색한 심경을 남겼으나, 패니 말고는 딱히 누구의 동정도 자아내지 못했다. 패니는 그 모든 것을 조용히 지켜본 이로서, 줄리아가 시샘에 휩싸여 있다고 여기니 크나큰 연민 없이는 그녀를 바라볼 수 없었던 것이다.\n\n그녀가 자리를 뜨자 짧은 침묵이 이어졌다. 그러나 그녀의 오라비는 이내 본론과 「연인들의 맹세」로 돌아가, 예이츠 씨의 도움을 받아 어떤 무대 장치가 필요할지 알아내려 열심히 연극을 훑어보았다—그동안 마리아와 헨리 크로퍼드는 나직한 목소리로 이야기를 나누었고, 마리아가 “저야 그 배역을 정말 기꺼이 줄리아한테 넘겨줬을 거예요, 다만 제가 아무리 서툴게 한다 해도 그 애는 저보다 더 서툴게 하리라 믿어질 뿐이지요.” 하고 꺼낸 그 말은, 틀림없이 그에 걸맞은 온갖 찬사를 받고 있었다.\n\n이것이 얼마간 이어진 뒤, 톰 버트럼과 예이츠 씨가 이제 ‘극장’이라 불리기 시작한 그 방으로 함께 자리를 옮겨 더 의논하고, 버트럼 양은 어밀리아 역을 크로퍼드 양에게 권하러 몸소 목사관으로 내려가기로 하면서, 무리의 흩어짐이 마무리되었다. 그리고 패니는 홀로 남았다.\n\n그 홀로 있는 시간을 그녀가 맨 처음 쓴 방식은, 탁자에 남겨진 그 책을 집어 들어, 그토록 말이 많던 그 연극과 낯을 익히기 시작하는 것이었다. 호기심이 온통 깨어나, 그녀는 열띤 마음으로 그것을 훑어 내려갔는데, 그 열의는 오직 놀라움의 순간들에만 멈칫거렸다—이런 마당에 이런 것이 뽑히다니, 사가 극장에서 이런 것이 내놓아지고 받아들여지다니! 아가사와 어밀리아는 저마다 다른 식으로 그녀에게 집안 상연에는 도무지 온당치 않아 보였다—한쪽의 처지와 다른 한쪽의 대사가, 정숙한 여자라면 누구도 입에 담기에 너무나 부적절해서, 그녀는 사촌들이 자기네가 뛰어드는 일이 무엇인지 알고나 있는지 도무지 짐작할 수 없었다. 그래서 에드먼드가 틀림없이 할 그 간언으로 어서 그들이 정신 차리기를 애타게 바랐다.\n\n# 제15장\n\n크로퍼드 양은 그 배역을 아주 선뜻 받아들였다. 그리고 버트럼 양이 목사관에서 돌아온 지 얼마 안 되어 러시워스 씨가 왔고, 그리하여 또 한 배역이 정해졌다. 그는 카셀 백작과 안할트를 맡으라는 권유를 받았는데, 처음에는 어느 쪽을 고를지 몰라 버트럼 양이 정해 주기를 바랐다. 그러나 두 인물의 서로 다른 결이며 어느 것이 어느 것인지 일러 주자, 또 언젠가 런던에서 그 연극을 본 적이 있는데 안할트가 참 멍청한 녀석이라 여겼던 것을 떠올리고는, 이내 백작으로 마음을 정했다. 버트럼 양은 그 결정을 반겼으니, 그가 외울 게 적을수록 좋았기 때문이다. 그리고 그녀는 백작과 아가사가 함께 연기할 수 있으면 좋겠다는 그의 바람에는 공감할 수 없었고, 그가 그런 장면을 여태 찾을 수 있으려나 하는 기대로 책장을 느릿느릿 넘기는 동안 그리 참을성 있게 기다려 주지도 못했지만, 그래도 그의 배역을 아주 친절히 손수 맡아, 줄여도 될 만한 대사는 죄다 잘라 냈다. 게다가 그가 아주 잘 차려입어야 한다는 점을 짚어 주고 그의 옷 빛깔까지 골라 주었다. 러시워스 씨는 겉으로는 그런 치장을 업신여기는 척했으나, 실은 제 화려한 차림새라는 생각이 아주 마음에 들었다. 그리고 온통 제 겉모습이 어떠할지에만 정신이 팔려, 남들 생각을 하거나, 마리아가 얼마쯤 각오했던 그런 결론을 이끌어 내거나, 그런 언짢음을 느끼거나 할 겨를이 없었다.\n\n이만큼이 정해진 것은, 아침 내내 밖에 나가 있던 에드먼드가 그 일을 조금이라도 알기 전이었다. 그러나 그가 만찬 전에 응접실에 들어서니, 톰과 마리아와 예이츠 씨 사이에 한창 왁자한 논의가 오가고 있었고, 러시워스 씨가 그 반가운 소식을 전하려 자못 잽싸게 앞으로 나섰다.\n\n“우리 연극을 정했어요.” 그가 말했다. “「연인들의 맹세」로 하기로 했고요, 저는 카셀 백작을 맡아서, 처음엔 파란 옷에 분홍 새틴 망토를 걸치고 나오고, 나중엔 사냥 복장 삼아 또 다른 멋들어진 옷을 입기로 했어요. 이게 제 마음에 들지는 모르겠네요.”\n\n패니의 눈은 에드먼드를 좇았고, 이 말을 듣고 그의 낯빛을 보며 그의 심경이 어떠할지 헤아리자, 그녀의 가슴은 그를 위해 두근거렸다.\n\n“「연인들의 맹세」라니!” 더없이 아연실색한 어조로 내뱉은 이 한마디가, 러시워스 씨에게 건넨 그의 유일한 대꾸였다. 그리고 그는 부정의 대답을 거의 의심치 않는 듯, 형과 누이들 쪽으로 돌아섰다.\n\n“그렇답니다.” 예이츠 씨가 외쳤다. “우리가 그토록 갑론을박하고 애먹은 끝에, 「연인들의 맹세」만큼 우리 모두에게 딱 맞는 것도, 흠잡을 데 없는 것도 없다는 걸 알아냈어요. 어째서 진작 이 생각을 못 했는지 그게 놀라울 따름이에요. 내 아둔함이 지독했지요, 이렇게 내가 에클스포드에서 본 것을 몽땅 유리하게 써먹을 수 있는데 말이에요. 게다가 본보기가 될 게 하나라도 있다는 건 참 요긴하거든요! 우리는 거의 모든 배역을 정했어요.”\n\n“그런데 여자 배역은 어떻게 하고?” 에드먼드가 심각하게, 마리아를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n마리아는 저도 모르게 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다. “내가 레이븐쇼 부인이 맡기로 돼 있던 배역을 맡고,” 그러고는 (한결 대담한 눈빛으로) “크로퍼드 양이 어밀리아를 맡아.”\n\n“나라면 이게, 우리끼리 그리 쉽게 채울 수 있는 그런 연극이라곤 생각지 못했을 텐데.” 에드먼드가 대꾸하며, 어머니와 이모와 패니가 앉아 있는 난롯가로 돌아서서, 잔뜩 언짢은 낯빛으로 자리에 앉았다.\n\n러시워스 씨가 그를 뒤따라와 말했다. “저는 세 번 등장하고, 대사가 마흔두 마디예요. 그쯤이면 대단하죠, 안 그래요? 하지만 그렇게 곱게 차려입는다는 건 그리 내키지 않네요. 파란 옷에 분홍 새틴 망토를 걸치면 나도 나를 못 알아볼 것 같아요.”\n\n에드먼드는 그에게 대꾸할 수 없었다. 몇 분 뒤 버트럼 씨가 목수의 미심쩍은 대목을 풀어 주러 방 밖으로 불려 나갔고, 예이츠 씨가 그를 따라나서고 이내 러시워스 씨까지 뒤따라 나가자, 에드먼드는 거의 곧바로 기회를 잡아 이렇게 말했다. “예이츠 씨 앞에서는, 에클스포드에 있는 그 벗들을 흉보지 않고서는 이 연극에 대해 내가 느끼는 바를 말할 수가 없어. 하지만 이제, 마리아야, 너한테는 말해야겠다. 나는 이게 사가 상연에는 지극히 부적절하다고 보고, 네가 이걸 그만두기를 바란다. 네가 이걸 찬찬히 읽어 보고 나면 틀림없이 그러리라고 나는 믿어 의심치 않아. 어머니든 이모든 앞에서 제1막만이라도 소리 내어 읽어 보고, 네가 그걸 옳다 여길 수 있는지 봐. 굳이 너를 아버지의 판단 앞에까지 데려갈 필요는 없으리라고 나는 확신한다.”\n\n“우리는 세상을 아주 다르게 보는구나.” 마리아가 외쳤다. “나는 이 연극을 훤히 꿰고 있어, 정말이야. 그리고 물론 손볼 몇 군데만 빼내면, 나는 이게 흠잡을 데라곤 없다고 봐. 그리고 이게 사가 상연에 아주 알맞다고 여기는 젊은 여자가 나 하나만은 아니야.”\n\n“그건 유감이구나.” 그가 대답했다. “하지만 이 일에서는 네가 앞장서야 해. 네가 본을 보여야 한다고. 남들이 실수했다면, 그들을 바로잡고 참된 조심성이 무엇인지 보여 주는 게 네 몫이야. 예의범절의 모든 대목에서 네 처신이 나머지 사람들에게 법이 되어야 해.”\n\n제 위상을 그려 보이는 이 말은 얼마간 효과가 있었으니, 마리아만큼 앞장서기를 좋아하는 사람도 없었기 때문이다. 그리하여 그녀는 한결 누그러진 목소리로 대답했다. “고마워, 에드먼드. 오빠 뜻이 참 좋다는 건 나도 알아. 하지만 그래도 나는 오빠가 세상을 너무 지나치게 본다고 생각해. 그리고 나로선 정말이지 이런 일로 나머지 사람들을 죄다 붙잡고 일장 연설을 늘어놓을 수는 없어. 그거야말로 더없이 무례한 짓일 거야, 내 생각엔.”\n\n“내가 그런 생각을 머릿속에 품었을 성싶으냐? 아니야. 오직 네 처신만이 네 연설이 되게 하렴. 그 배역을 살펴보니 네 힘에 부치더라고, 네가 지녔음직한 것보다 더 큰 노력과 자신감을 요구하는 것 같더라고 말해. 이걸 단호하게 말하면, 그걸로 충분할 거야. 분별할 줄 아는 이라면 누구나 네 속뜻을 알아들을 테고. 연극은 접힐 테고, 네 조심성은 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 기려질 거야.”\n\n“온당찮은 건 하지 마라, 얘야.” 버트럼 부인이 말했다. “토머스 경께서 좋아하지 않으실 게다.—패니, 종을 울려라. 나 만찬을 들어야겠구나.—하기야, 줄리아는 이맘때면 옷을 다 차려입었겠지.”\n\n“저는 확신해요, 어머니,” 에드먼드가 패니를 앞질러 말했다. “토머스 경께서 좋아하지 않으시리란 걸요.”\n\n“거봐라, 얘야, 에드먼드가 하는 말 들었지?”\n\n“내가 그 배역을 마다하면,” 마리아가 새삼 열을 올리며 말했다. “줄리아가 틀림없이 맡을 거야.”\n\n“뭐라고!” 에드먼드가 외쳤다. “그 애가 네 이유를 안다면 말이지!”\n\n“아! 그 앤 우리 사이의 차이를—우리 처지의 차이를—떠올리겠지. 나야 필요하다고 느낄 만한 그 조심성을 그 앤 굳이 안 지켜도 된다고 말이야. 그 앤 분명 그렇게 우겨 댈 거야. 아니, 오빠, 나를 봐줘. 나는 승낙을 물릴 수 없어. 너무 멀리 정해져 버렸고, 다들 무척 실망할 테고, 톰은 아주 화를 낼 거야. 그리고 우리가 이렇게 까다롭게 굴다간, 우리는 아무것도 못 하게 될걸.”\n\n“나도 딱 그 말을 하려던 참이었어요.” 노리스 부인이 말했다. “연극이란 연극마다 다 트집을 잡을 양이면, 너희는 아무것도 못 할 테고, 그 채비들도 죄다 돈만 내다 버린 꼴이 될 테고, 그거야말로 우리 모두한테 망신일 게 분명해요. 나야 그 연극을 모르지만, 마리아 말마따나 조금 지나치게 야한 데가 있다면(대개 다 그렇지요) 그거야 손쉽게 빼내면 그만이에요. 우리가 너무 깐깐하게 굴어선 안 돼요, 에드먼드. 러시워스 씨도 함께 연기하니, 해로울 게 없어요. 다만 목수들이 일을 시작할 때 톰이 제 마음을 진작 정했더라면 싶어요, 그 옆문들 때문에 반나절 품이 날아갔거든요. 그래도 막은 잘된 일이지요. 하녀들이 일을 아주 잘하니, 고리는 몇 다스쯤 되돌려 보낼 수 있을 것 같아요. 그걸 그렇게 촘촘히 달 것까진 없거든요. 나도 그래도 얼마쯤 쓸모가 있는가 봐요, 허투루 쓰는 걸 막고 뭐든 알뜰히 굴리는 데는 말이에요. 이렇게 많은 젊은것들을 건사하자면 진득한 어른 하나쯤은 늘 있어야 하는 법이지요. 아 참, 오늘 나한테 있었던 일을 톰한테 이르는 걸 깜빡했네. 내가 닭장을 둘러보고 막 나오는데, 글쎄 딕 잭슨이 손에 전나무 판자 두 쪽을 들고 하인방 문 쪽으로 다가가지 뭐예요, 틀림없이 제 아비한테 갖다주려는 거였지요. 제 어미가 마침 그 애를 시켜 아비한테 뭘 전하게 했는데, 그러고 나서 아비가 그 판자 두 쪽을 가져오라고 시킨 거예요, 그게 없인 도무지 일을 못 하겠다면서요. 나야 이게 다 무슨 속셈인지 훤히 알았지요, 바로 그 순간 우리 머리 위에서 하인들 저녁 종이 울리고 있었으니까요. 그리고 나는 그렇게 야금야금 파고드는 사람들이 질색이라(잭슨네가 아주 그런 축이지요, 늘 그리 말해 왔어요, 딱 얻어 낼 수 있는 건 죄다 얻어 내는 그런 부류거든요), 그 애한테 대뜸 말했어요(알다시피 열 살배기 덩치만 큰 굼벵이 녀석인데, 제 딴엔 부끄러운 줄 알아야지요), ‘판자는 내가 네 아비한테 갖다줄 테니, 딕, 넌 어서 집으로 냉큼 돌아가거라.’ 그 애는 아주 멍한 낯으로, 한마디도 없이 돌아서더군요, 아마 내가 꽤나 매섭게 말했나 봐요. 그리고 이걸로 한동안은 그 애가 집 근처로 얼쩡대며 노략질하는 버릇이 고쳐지지 싶어요. 나는 그런 게걸스러움이 질색이에요—너희 아버지가 그 집안에 얼마나 잘해 주는데, 일 년 내내 그 사람을 부려 주면서 말이지요!”\n\n아무도 굳이 대꾸하려 들지 않았다. 이내 다른 이들이 돌아왔고, 에드먼드는 그들을 바로잡으려 애쓴 것으로 만족하는 수밖에 없음을 깨달았다.\n\n만찬은 무겁게 흘러갔다. 노리스 부인은 딕 잭슨을 이긴 무용담을 또 한 번 늘어놓았으나, 그것 말고는 연극도 채비도 딱히 크게 입에 오르지 않았다. 에드먼드의 못마땅함을 그 형마저도, 비록 인정하려 들진 않았으나, 느끼고 있었기 때문이다. 마리아는 헨리 크로퍼드의 북돋는 힘이 없으니 그 화제는 피하는 게 낫다 여겼다. 줄리아의 비위를 맞춰 보려던 예이츠 씨는, 그녀가 저희 무리에서 빠져나간 것을 자기가 아쉬워한다는 화제 말고는, 그 어떤 화제로도 그녀의 우울이 그리 뚫기 어렵지 않음을 알았다. 그리고 오로지 제 배역과 제 옷차림밖에 머릿속에 없던 러시워스 씨는, 그 둘에 대해 할 수 있는 말은 이내 죄다 지껄여 버린 뒤였다.\n\n그러나 극장에 얽힌 일들은 겨우 한두 시간 미뤄졌을 뿐이었다. 아직도 정할 것이 잔뜩 있었던 것이다. 그리고 저녁의 활기가 새 용기를 북돋우자, 톰과 마리아와 예이츠 씨는, 응접실에 다시 모이고 얼마 안 되어, 따로 놓인 탁자에 위원회처럼 둘러앉아 연극을 펼쳐 놓고 막 그 일에 깊이 빠져들던 참이었는데, 그때 크로퍼드 남매가 들어와 더없이 반가운 훼방을 놓았다. 늦은 데다 어둡고 길이 질척였건만 그들은 오지 않고는 못 배겼고, 더없이 고마운 기쁨 속에 맞아들여졌다.\n\n“자, 어떻게 돼 가요?” 그리고 “뭘 정하셨어요?” 그리고 “아! 당신들 없인 우리가 아무것도 못 해요” 하는 말들이 첫인사에 뒤따랐다. 그리고 헨리 크로퍼드는 이내 나머지 셋과 탁자에 둘러앉았고, 그동안 그 누이는 버트럼 부인에게로 다가가, 상냥한 마음씨로 부인에게 인사치레를 건넸다. “정말이지 부인께 축하 말씀 드려야겠어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “연극이 정해진 걸 두고요. 부인께서야 본보기가 될 만큼 참을성 있게 견뎌 오셨지만, 저희의 그 온갖 소란과 옥신각신에 진저리가 나셨을 게 틀림없거든요. 배우들이야 기쁘겠지만, 곁에서 지켜보는 이들은 결판이 난 것을 그보다 한없이 더 고마워할 게 분명하지요. 그러니 저는 진심으로 부인께 기쁨을 빌어 드려요, 노리스 부인께도, 또 같은 처지에 계신 다른 모든 분께도요.” 하며 패니 너머로 에드먼드 쪽을, 반은 겁먹은 듯 반은 짓궂은 듯 흘긋 보았다.\n\n버트럼 부인은 아주 정중하게 대답했으나, 에드먼드는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 자기가 그저 곁에서 지켜보는 사람일 뿐이라는 것을 그는 부인하지 않았다. 난롯가 무리와 몇 분 더 담소를 나눈 뒤, 크로퍼드 양은 탁자 쪽 무리에게로 돌아가, 그 곁에 서서 그들의 채비에 관심을 두는 듯하더니, 문득 무언가 떠오른 듯 외쳤다. “여러분, 이 오두막이며 술집이며 안팎을 참으로 태연히도 손보고 계시네요. 하지만 그동안 부디 제 운명이나 좀 알려 주세요. 안할트는 누가 맡죠? 제가 여러분 중 어느 신사분께 사랑을 고백하는 기쁨을 누리게 되나요?”\n\n한순간 아무도 입을 열지 않았다. 그러다 여럿이 한꺼번에 입을 열어 같은 서글픈 진실을 전했으니, 아직 안할트를 못 구했다는 것이었다. “러시워스 씨가 카셀 백작을 맡기로 했지만, 안할트는 아직 아무도 안 맡았어요.”\n\n“저는 배역을 고를 수 있었지요.” 러시워스 씨가 말했다. “하지만 백작이 가장 마음에 들 것 같았어요, 걸치게 될 그 화려한 차림새는 그리 내키지 않지만요.”\n\n“아주 현명하게 고르셨네요, 정말이에요.” 크로퍼드 양이 환해진 낯빛으로 대답했다. “안할트는 따분한 배역이거든요.”\n\n“백작은 대사가 마흔두 마디예요.” 러시워스 씨가 되받았다. “그거 만만찮은 거죠.”\n\n“안할트가 없다는 게 저는 조금도 놀랍지 않네요.” 크로퍼드 양이 잠깐 뜸을 들인 뒤 말했다. “어밀리아한텐 그보다 나은 상대가 아깝지요. 그렇게 들이대는 아가씨라면 사내들을 겁줄 만도 하고요.”\n\n“가능하다면야 저야 그 배역을 맡아 더없이 기쁘겠지만,” 톰이 외쳤다. “안타깝게도 집사와 안할트가 같은 장면에 함께 나오지 뭡니까. 그래도 아주 포기하진 않겠어요. 어떻게 해볼 수 있는지 궁리해 보죠—다시 한번 훑어볼게요.”\n\n“당신 아우님이 그 배역을 맡으면 되겠네요.” 예이츠 씨가 나직한 목소리로 말했다. “그러지 않을까요?”\n\n“난 그 앤 안 부를 거예요.” 톰이 싸늘하고 단호한 투로 대답했다.\n\n크로퍼드 양은 다른 이야기를 꺼냈고, 이내 난롯가 무리에게로 다시 돌아왔다.\n\n“저 사람들은 저를 도무지 원하질 않네요.” 그녀가 자리에 앉으며 말했다. “저는 그저 저들을 곤란하게 하고, 인사치레 대꾸나 하게 만들 뿐이에요. 에드먼드 버트럼 씨, 씨는 몸소 연기를 안 하시니 사심 없는 조언자가 되어 주시겠죠. 그래서 씨께 여쭙는 거예요. 안할트는 어떡하면 좋을까요? 다른 이들 중 누가 그 역을 겹쳐 맡는 게 될 만한가요? 씨 생각은 어떠세요?”\n\n“제 조언은,” 그가 차분히 말했다. “연극을 바꾸시라는 거예요.”\n\n“저야 반대할 게 없어요.” 그녀가 대답했다. “받쳐 주기만 잘한다면, 그러니까 모든 게 잘 돌아가기만 한다면, 저는 어밀리아 역이 딱히 싫진 않지만, 걸림돌이 되는 건 미안하니까요. 하지만 저 탁자에서는 씨 조언을 들으려 하질 않으니,” 하고 둘러보며, “그건 분명 받아들여지지 않을 거예요.”\n\n에드먼드는 더는 말하지 않았다.\n\n“정 어떤 배역이 씨더러 연기하게 꾈 수 있다면, 아마 안할트겠지요.” 잠깐 뜸을 들인 뒤 그 아가씨가 짓궂게 넌지시 일렀다. “그 사람은, 아시다시피, 성직자니까요.”\n\n“그런 사정이야 결코 저를 꾀지 못할 거예요.” 그가 대답했다. “서툰 연기로 그 인물을 우스꽝스럽게 만드는 건 저로선 마음 아픈 일일 테니까요. 안할트가 격식 차리는 근엄한 설교꾼처럼 보이지 않게 하기란 무척 어려울 게 틀림없어요. 그리고 그 직업 자체를 택한 사람이야말로, 아마 그것을 무대 위에 그려 보이고 싶어 할 마지막 축에 드는 사람일 거고요.”\n\n크로퍼드 양은 말문이 막혔고, 얼마쯤 분하고 무안한 심경으로 제 의자를 찻상 쪽으로 훨씬 가까이 옮겨, 거기서 좌장 노릇을 하던 노리스 부인에게 온통 마음을 쏟았다.\n\n“패니,” 톰 버트럼이 저쪽 탁자에서 외쳤는데, 거기서는 회의가 열심히 이어지고 이야기가 끊이지 않았다. “네 도움이 필요해.”\n\n패니는 무슨 심부름이려니 하고 냉큼 일어섰다. 그런 식으로 그녀를 부려 먹는 버릇이, 에드먼드가 아무리 애를 써도 아직 사라지지 않았던 것이다.\n\n“아! 네 자리에서 일으키려는 게 아니야. 지금 당장 네 도움이 필요한 건 아니고. 우리 연극에서 네가 좀 필요할 뿐이야. 넌 소작인의 아내를 맡아야 해.”\n\n“저요!” 패니가 더없이 겁에 질린 낯으로 다시 주저앉으며 외쳤다. “정말이지 봐주세요. 온 세상을 다 준다 해도 저는 아무것도 연기 못 해요. 아니, 정말, 저는 연기를 못 해요.”\n\n“아니, 넌 해야 해, 우리가 널 봐줄 수가 없거든. 겁낼 것 없어. 아무것도 아닌 배역이야, 그야말로 아무것도 아니지, 통틀어 대여섯 마디도 안 되고, 네 말을 아무도 못 알아들어도 별 상관없어. 그러니 원하는 만큼 생쥐처럼 기어들어가는 목소리로 해도 되지만, 그래도 우린 바라볼 네가 있어야 해.”\n\n“대여섯 마디가 겁난다면,” 러시워스 씨가 외쳤다. “프라이스 양은 내 것 같은 배역은 어쩌시려고요? 나는 마흔두 마디를 외워야 하는데.”\n\n“외우는 게 겁나서가 아니에요.” 패니가 말했는데, 그 순간 방 안에서 저 혼자만 말하고 있고 거의 모든 눈이 저를 향해 있음을 깨닫고는 소스라친 채였다. “다만 저는 정말이지 연기를 못 해요.”\n\n“아니야, 아니야, 넌 우리한테는 충분히 잘할 수 있어. 네 배역만 외워, 나머지는 우리가 죄다 가르쳐 줄게. 넌 장면이 둘뿐이고, 내가 소작인을 맡을 테니, 내가 널 넣어 주고 이리저리 떠밀어 줄게, 그럼 아주 잘해낼 거야, 내가 장담하지.”\n\n“안 돼요, 정말, 오빠, 부디 절 봐주세요. 오빠는 짐작도 못 하실 거예요. 저로선 도무지 될 수 없는 일이에요. 제가 그걸 맡는다면, 오빠들을 실망시키기밖에 더 하겠어요.”\n\n“쯧! 쯧! 그렇게 부끄럼 타지 마. 넌 아주 잘할 거야. 너한텐 뭐든 다 봐줄 거고. 우리가 완벽함을 바라는 것도 아니잖아. 넌 갈색 가운에 흰 앞치마에 헐렁한 실내 모자를 구하면 되고, 우리가 네 얼굴에 주름 몇 개랑 눈가에 잔주름을 좀 그려 넣으면, 넌 아주 그럴싸한 자그마한 노파가 될 거야.”\n\n“봐주세요, 정말이지 봐주세요.” 패니가 외쳤는데, 지나친 동요로 점점 더 얼굴이 빨개졌고, 저를 친절히 지켜보던 에드먼드 쪽을 애처롭게 바라보았다. 그러나 에드먼드는 끼어들어 형을 노엽게 하고 싶지 않아, 그녀에게 그저 북돋는 미소만 지어 보였다. 그녀의 애원은 톰에게 아무 소용이 없었으니, 그는 앞서 한 말을 되풀이할 뿐이었다. 그리고 톰만이 아니었다. 이제 그 요구를 마리아와 크로퍼드 씨와 예이츠 씨까지 거들었는데, 톰과는 그저 좀 더 부드럽거나 좀 더 정중하다는 것만 다를 뿐인 다그침이었고, 그것이 죄다 합쳐지니 패니로선 도무지 당해 낼 수가 없었다. 그리고 그녀가 그 뒤에 채 숨 돌릴 새도 없이, 노리스 부인이 노기 서리면서도 다 들리게 이렇게 속삭여 그 모든 것에 쐐기를 박았다—“이게 다 아무것도 아닌 일로 무슨 소란이니. 나는 네가 정말 부끄럽구나, 패니, 이런 사소한 일로 사촌들 청을 들어주는 걸 이토록 어렵게 만들다니—너한테 그리 잘해 주는 사람들인데! 얌전히 그 배역을 맡고, 이 일로 더는 말 안 나오게 하렴, 부탁이다.”\n\n“그 애를 다그치지 마세요, 이모.” 에드먼드가 말했다. “이런 식으로 그 애를 다그치는 건 온당치 않아요. 보시다시피 그 애는 연기하기를 싫어하잖아요. 우리 나머지 사람들처럼 그 애도 제 뜻대로 고르게 두세요. 그 애 판단도 얼마든지 믿어도 좋으니까요. 더는 그 애를 다그치지 마세요.”\n\n“난 다그칠 생각 없어요.” 노리스 부인이 쏘아붙였다. “하지만 그 애가 제 이모와 사촌들이 바라는 걸 안 한다면, 나는 그 애가 아주 고집 세고 은혜 모르는 계집애라 여길 거예요—정말이지 아주 배은망덕하지요, 그 애가 누구이고 어떤 처지인지 생각하면 말이에요.”\n\n에드먼드는 너무 화가 나 말을 잇지 못했다. 그러나 크로퍼드 양이 한순간 놀란 눈으로 노리스 부인을, 다음엔 눈물이 비치기 시작한 패니를 보더니, 곧바로 다소 날카롭게 말했다. “제 자리가 마음에 안 드네요. 여긴 저한테 너무 더워요.” 그러고는 제 의자를 탁자 맞은편, 패니 바로 곁으로 옮기며, 자리를 잡으면서 그녀에게 친절하고 나직한 목소리로 속삭였다. “괘념치 마세요, 프라이스 양, 오늘 저녁은 다들 심사가 뒤틀린 날이에요. 다들 심통을 부리고 못살게 굴지만, 우리는 저 사람들 신경 쓰지 말아요.” 그리고 그녀는 저 자신도 기분이 언짢으면서도, 각별한 마음씀으로 그녀에게 계속 말을 붙이며 기운을 북돋우려 애썼다. 제 오라비에게 눈짓 한 번으로 그녀는 연극 위원회에서 더 다그치는 것을 막았고, 그녀를 거의 오롯이 다스리는 그 참으로 착한 심성이, 에드먼드의 호감에서 그녀가 잃었던 그 얼마간을 빠르게 되돌려 주고 있었다.\n\n패니는 크로퍼드 양을 좋아하진 않았다. 그러나 이번의 친절에는 무척 고마움을 느꼈다. 그리고 크로퍼드 양이 그녀의 일감을 눈여겨보며 자기도 그만큼 잘하면 좋겠다고 하고, 본을 좀 알려 달라 청하고, 패니가 이제 사교계 나들이를 채비하는 모양이라며—사촌 언니가 시집가면 그녀도 물론 사교계에 나올 테니—여기더니, 이어서 바다에 나가 있는 오라비에게서 요즘 소식을 들었느냐 묻고, 그를 꼭 한번 보고 싶어 못 견디겠다며 아주 훤칠한 젊은이일 거라 그려 보고, 그가 다시 바다로 나가기 전에 그 초상을 그려 두라 권하기까지 하자—그녀는 그것이 아주 흐뭇한 아첨임을 인정하지 않을 수 없었고, 귀 기울이지 않을 수도, 제가 작정한 것보다 한결 생기 있게 대꾸하지 않을 수도 없었다.\n\n연극에 관한 의논은 여전히 이어졌고, 크로퍼드 양의 주의가 패니에게서 처음으로 돌려진 것은, 톰 버트럼이 그녀에게 한없는 아쉬움을 담아, 집사에 더해 안할트 역까지 맡기란 도무지 불가능하겠다고 말했을 때였다. 그는 그것이 될 만한 일임을 애타게 밝혀 보려 했으나 도무지 안 되겠으니, 접을 수밖에 없다는 것이었다. “하지만 그걸 채우는 데는 조금의 어려움도 없을 거예요.” 그가 덧붙였다. “한마디만 하면 되니까요. 골라잡을 수 있죠. 지금 이 순간에도, 우리 무리에 끼고 싶어 안달인 젊은이를 여기서 육 마일 안에 적어도 여섯은 댈 수 있어요. 그중 한둘은 우리한테 창피를 주지도 않을 거고요. 올리버 형제든 찰스 매독스든 나는 믿고 맡길 수 있어요. 톰 올리버는 아주 영리한 녀석이고, 찰스 매독스는 어디서든 보기 드물게 신사다운 사내니까요. 그러니 내일 아침 일찌감치 말을 몰아 스토크로 건너가서, 그중 하나와 매듭짓고 오겠어요.”\n\n그가 말하는 동안, 마리아는 근심스레 에드먼드 쪽을 둘러보며, 그가 이렇게 계획을 키우는 것을—처음에 다들 그토록 손사래 치던 것과는 딴판인 이 일을—틀림없이 반대하리라 잔뜩 벼르고 있었다. 그러나 에드먼드는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 잠깐 생각한 뒤, 크로퍼드 양이 차분히 대답했다. “저로 말하면, 여러분 모두가 적당하다 여기는 것이라면 무엇에도 반대할 게 없어요. 제가 그 신사분들 중 누구라도 본 적이 있던가요? 아, 찰스 매독스 씨가 언젠가 언니 댁에서 만찬을 든 적이 있죠, 안 그래, 헨리? 얌전해 보이는 젊은이였어요. 저는 그이가 기억나요. 부디 그이를 부르시죠, 생판 낯선 사람보다야 저한텐 덜 거북할 테니까요.”\n\n찰스 매독스가 그 사람으로 정해졌다. 톰은 이튿날 아침 일찍 그를 찾아가겠다는 결심을 되풀이했다. 그리고 그때껏 좀처럼 입을 열지 않던 줄리아가, 먼저 마리아를, 다음엔 에드먼드를 흘긋 보며 빈정대는 투로 “맨스필드 연극이 온 동네를 아주 들썩하게 하겠네” 하고 한마디 했으나, 에드먼드는 여전히 잠자코 있었고, 오직 작심한 듯한 근엄함으로만 제 심경을 드러냈다.\n\n“나는 우리 연극이 그리 잘될 것 같지가 않아요.” 크로퍼드 양이 얼마간 생각한 뒤, 패니에게 나직한 목소리로 말했다. “그리고 매독스 씨한테는, 함께 연습하기 전에 그 사람 대사도 얼마쯤, 내 대사도 아주 많이 줄이겠다고 일러 둬야겠어요. 아주 언짢은 노릇이 될 테고, 결코 내가 바라던 게 아니에요.”\n\n# 제16장\n\n크로퍼드 양의 힘으로도 패니가 그동안 있었던 일을 정말로 잊게 만들 수는 없었다. 저녁이 끝나자 그녀는 그 일로 가슴이 미어진 채 잠자리에 들었으니, 사촌 톰에게서 그토록 공공연히, 그토록 집요하게 몰아세워진 충격에 신경은 아직도 떨렸고, 이모의 매정한 지청구와 나무람에 기가 죽어 있었다. 그런 식으로 뭇사람의 눈길 앞에 불려 나오고, 그것이 한없이 더 고약한 일의 서막에 지나지 않는다는 소리를 듣고, 연기처럼 도무지 될 수 없는 일을 해야 한다는 소리를 듣고, 그러고 나서 고집과 배은망덕이라는 나무람이 뒤따르고, 그것도 제 얹혀사는 처지를 넌지시 들먹이는 말로 힘주어 다그쳐진 것은—그 순간에도 너무나 괴로운 일이었던지라, 홀로 있을 때 그것을 떠올리는 일이 그리 덜 괴로울 수도 없었으니, 더구나 이튿날이 그 화제를 이어받아 무엇을 몰고 올지 모른다는 두려움까지 더해진 터였다. 크로퍼드 양은 그저 그때만 그녀를 감싸 주었을 뿐이었다. 그러니 만일 톰과 마리아가 능히 부릴 만한 그 위압적인 다그침으로 저희끼리 다시 그녀에게 청해 오는데, 에드먼드마저 자리에 없다면, 그녀는 어찌해야 할까? 그 물음에 답하기도 전에 그녀는 잠이 들었고, 이튿날 아침 깨어나서도 그것이 여전히 갈피를 잡을 수 없는 물음임을 알았다. 이 집안에 처음 든 이래로 줄곧 그녀의 침실이던 그 자그마한 흰 다락방이 아무런 답도 일러 주지 못하자, 그녀는 옷을 차려입기 무섭게 좀 더 널찍하고 이리저리 거닐며 생각하기에 좀 더 알맞은, 그리고 이제 얼마 전부터 거의 그녀가 마찬가지로 안주인 노릇을 해 온 다른 방으로 갔다. 그곳은 그들의 공부방이었다—버트럼 자매가 더는 그렇게 부르지 못하게 할 때까지 그리 불렸고, 그 뒤로도 한동안은 공부방으로 쓰였다. 거기서 리 선생이 지냈고, 거기서 그들은 읽고 쓰고, 이야기하고 웃었으니, 리 선생이 그들 곁을 떠난 삼 년 전까지 그러했다. 그 뒤로 그 방은 쓸모가 없어져 한동안 아주 버려져 있었으나, 오직 패니만이, 제 화초를 보살피러 오거나, 위층 제 자그마한 방의 공간과 세간이 모자라 여태 거기 두는 것이 반가운 책을 한 권 찾으러 올 때 드나들었다. 그러다 차츰, 그 방의 안락함을 소중히 여기는 마음이 커지면서, 그녀는 제 세간을 늘리고 그곳에서 더 많은 시간을 보냈다. 그리고 그녀를 막을 것이 아무것도 없으니, 어찌나 자연스럽고 어찌나 스스럼없이 그 방에 스며들었던지, 이제는 그곳이 그녀의 것임이 두루 인정되었다. 마리아 버트럼이 열여섯이던 때부터 줄곧 그리 불려 온 그 동쪽 방은, 이제 흰 다락방과 거의 다름없이 뚜렷하게 패니의 것으로 여겨졌다. 한쪽이 워낙 비좁으니 다른 쪽을 쓰는 것이 너무나 마땅해 보였던 터라, 제 우월감이 요구할 만한 온갖 우월함을 제 방에 갖춘 버트럼 자매도 그것을 온전히 못마땅해하지 않았다. 그리고 노리스 부인은 패니를 위해 그 방에 결코 불을 때지 않는다는 조건을 못박아 둔 뒤로는, 아무도 원치 않는 것을 그녀가 쓰는 것쯤은 그런대로 눈감아 주었다—하기야 그녀가 이따금 그 관대함을 두고 하는 말투를 들으면, 마치 그 방이 집 안에서 가장 좋은 방이기라도 한 듯싶었지만.\n\n방향이 어찌나 좋던지, 불을 안 때도 초봄이며 늦가을의 여러 아침이면 패니처럼 기꺼운 마음에는 지낼 만했다. 그리고 한 줄기 햇살이라도 비치는 한, 그녀는 겨울이 와도 그 방에서 아주 쫓겨나지는 않기를 바랐다. 한가한 시간에 그 방이 주는 위안은 더할 나위 없었다. 아래층에서 언짢은 일이 있으면 그녀는 그리로 가서, 손닿는 데 있는 무슨 소일거리나 어떤 생각의 실마리에서 곧바로 위안을 얻을 수 있었다. 제 화초, 제 책들—단돈 한 실링이라도 부릴 수 있게 된 첫 순간부터 그녀는 책을 모아 왔다—제 필기 책상, 그리고 자선과 손재주로 만든 제 일감이 죄다 손닿는 데 있었다. 아니면 무언가에 손댈 마음이 안 나고 그저 골똘히 생각에 잠기는 것 말고는 아무것도 하기 싫을 때면, 그 방에서 흥미로운 추억 하나 얽혀 있지 않은 물건이라곤 좀처럼 눈에 띄지 않았다. 무엇이든 다 벗이거나, 아니면 그녀의 생각을 어느 벗에게로 실어 갔다. 그리고 이따금 크나큰 괴로움을 겪었더라도, 제 속뜻이 곧잘 오해받고, 제 감정이 무시되고, 제 이해력이 얕잡아 보였더라도, 또 억압과 조롱과 홀대의 아픔을 겪어 왔더라도, 그 어느 것이 되풀이될 때면 거의 언제나 무언가 위로가 되는 일이 뒤따랐다. 버트럼 이모가 그녀를 두둔해 주었거나, 리 선생이 북돋아 주었거나, 아니면 그보다 더 잦고 더 살가운 일로, 에드먼드가 그녀의 편이자 벗이 되어 주었던 것이다. 그는 그녀를 대변해 주거나 그녀의 속뜻을 풀어 주었고, 울지 말라 일러 주거나, 눈물마저 흐뭇하게 만드는 어떤 다정함의 증표를 건네주었다. 그리하여 그 모든 것이 이제는 하나로 녹아들어, 세월의 거리를 두고 어찌나 어우러졌던지, 지난날의 온갖 시련마저 저마다 정겨움을 띠었다. 그 방은 그녀에게 더없이 소중했으니, 그녀는 그 세간을 집 안에서 가장 훤한 것과도 바꾸려 들지 않았을 것이다—본디 수수했던 것이 아이들의 온갖 험한 손길을 다 겪었는데도 말이다. 그리고 그 방의 가장 큰 우아함이자 장식이란, 응접실에 두기엔 너무 서툴게 된 줄리아의 솜씨로 만든 빛바랜 발받침 하나와, 비침 그림이 한창 유행하던 때에 만든 투명 그림 셋—이것은 어느 창의 아래쪽 세 칸에 끼워져, 이탈리아의 어느 동굴과 컴벌랜드의 어느 달빛 어린 호수 사이에 틴턴 수도원이 자리 잡고 있었다—과, 달리 어디 둘 데가 없다 여겨져 벽난로 선반 위에 놓인 집안사람들의 옆모습 초상 한 무더기와, 그리고 그 곁, 벽에 핀으로 꽂힌, 사 년 전 윌리엄이 지중해에서 보내온 배 한 척의 자그마한 스케치—맨 아래에는 큰돛대만큼이나 큼직한 글자로 ‘국왕 폐하의 함선 앤트워프호’라 적혀 있었다—가 다였다.\n\n이 아늑한 위안의 보금자리로 패니는 이제 걸어 내려가, 그것이 흔들리고 미심쩍은 마음에 어떤 힘을 미치는지 시험해 보려 했다. 에드먼드의 옆모습 초상을 바라보노라면 그의 충고를 조금이나마 얻을 수 있을까, 제 제라늄에 바깥바람을 쐬어 주노라면 저 자신도 마음의 기운을 한 줄기 들이마실 수 있을까 해서였다. 그러나 그녀가 걷어 내야 할 것은 제 굳센 마음에 대한 두려움만이 아니었다. 그녀는 자신이 마땅히 어찌해야 옳은지조차 갈피를 못 잡기 시작한 터였다. 그리고 방을 이리저리 거니는 사이 그 의심은 점점 커져 갔다. 그토록 뜨겁게 청해지고 그토록 간절히 바라진 것을—제가 누구보다 고분고분해야 할 이들 가운데 몇몇이 마음을 쏟은 계획에 그토록 요긴할지 모를 것을—마다한 것이 과연 옳은 일이었을까? 그것은 심술이요 이기심이요, 제 모습을 드러내기 두려워하는 마음이 아니었을까? 그리고 에드먼드의 판단이, 이 모든 일을 토머스 경이 못마땅해하시리라는 그의 확신이, 나머지 모두를 거스르고 굳이 마다하는 것을 떳떳하게 해 줄 만큼 충분한 근거가 될까? 연기를 한다는 것이 그녀에게는 너무나 끔찍한 일인지라, 그녀는 제 거리낌이 참되고 순수한 것인지 의심하고 싶어졌다. 그리고 방 안을 둘러보니, 그들에게 신세를 졌다는 사촌들의 명분이, 그녀가 그들에게서 받은 선물 위에 또 선물이 놓인 광경에 힘입어 더욱 굳세어졌다. 두 창 사이의 탁자에는 여러 때에 걸쳐, 주로 톰이 준 반짇고리며 그물뜨기 상자가 잔뜩 놓여 있었다. 그리고 이 온갖 다정한 정표가 자아내는 그 빚이 얼마나 되는지 헤아리다 그녀는 어리둥절해졌다. 이렇게 제 도리를 찾아가려 애쓰는 와중에 문 두드리는 소리가 그녀를 일깨웠고, 그녀의 나직한 “들어오세요”에 답해 나타난 것은, 그 앞에서라면 그녀의 온갖 의심이 으레 내려놓아지곤 하던 사람이었다. 에드먼드를 보자 그녀의 눈이 환해졌다.\n\n“패니, 잠깐 얘기 좀 할 수 있을까?” 그가 말했다.\n\n“네, 물론이죠.”\n\n“의논하고 싶어서. 네 의견이 듣고 싶구나.”\n\n“제 의견을요!” 그녀는 그런 치켜세움에 몸을 움츠리며 외쳤으나, 그것이 여간 흐뭇하지 않았다.\n\n“그래, 네 조언과 의견 말이야. 어찌해야 할지 모르겠구나. 이 연극 소동이 갈수록 나빠지는 게 보이잖니. 저들은 고를 수 있는 가장 나쁜 축에 드는 연극을 골랐고, 이제 그걸로도 모자라, 우리 누구도 거의 모르는 젊은이한테 도움을 청하려 하고 있어. 이게 바로 처음에 그토록 떠들어 대던 그 은밀함이며 온당함의 끝이야. 찰스 매독스한테 나쁜 감정이야 없지만, 그를 이런 식으로 우리 틈에 끼워 들이면 반드시 생겨날 그 지나친 가까움이 몹시 못마땅해—가까움 이상의 것, 그 허물없음 말이야. 나는 도무지 그것을 참고 볼 수가 없어. 그리고 그건 어떻게든 막아야 할 만큼 큰 화근으로 내겐 보이는구나. 너도 그걸 같은 눈으로 보지 않니?”\n\n“네, 하지만 어쩔 수 있겠어요? 오빠 형은 저리 뜻이 굳은걸요.”\n\n“할 수 있는 일은 딱 하나야, 패니. 내가 안할트를 직접 맡는 거지. 그 밖엔 무엇으로도 톰을 잠재우지 못하리란 걸 나는 잘 알아.”\n\n패니는 그에게 대꾸할 수 없었다.\n\n“내가 좋아서 하는 일은 결코 아니야.” 그가 말을 이었다. “그런 앞뒤 안 맞는 꼴로 내몰리는 걸 좋아할 사내는 없지. 처음부터 그 계획에 반대한 걸로 알려진 마당에, 저들이 처음 계획을 온갖 면에서 넘어서고 있는 지금 와서 그들에 끼어든다는 건 우스운 노릇이야. 하지만 달리 마땅한 수가 도무지 떠오르질 않는구나. 넌 떠오르니, 패니?”\n\n“아뇨.” 패니가 천천히 말했다. “당장은요, 하지만—”\n\n“하지만 뭐? 네 판단이 내 편이 아닌 게 보이는구나. 좀 더 곰곰이 생각해 봐. 젊은이가 이런 식으로 받아들여져—우리 틈에 눌러앉아, 아무 때나 드나들 자격을 얻고, 온갖 삼감을 없애 버릴 처지에 불쑥 놓이는 데서—생길 수 있는 해악이며, 반드시 생길 언짢음을 넌 아마 나만큼은 못 헤아리는가 보구나. 연습이란 연습마다 자아낼 그 방종만 생각해 봐도 그래. 죄다 아주 고약한 일이야! 크로퍼드 양의 처지가 되어 봐, 패니. 낯선 사람과 어밀리아를 연기한다는 게 어떨지 헤아려 보라고. 그녀는 마땅히 헤아려질 자격이 있어, 스스로도 그것을 절실히 느끼는 게 분명하니까. 어젯밤 그녀가 너한테 한 말을 나도 충분히 들어서, 그녀가 낯선 사람과 연기하기를 꺼리는 걸 알겠더구나. 그리고 그녀는 아마 다른 기대를 품고 그 배역을 맡았을 테니—어쩌면 무슨 일이 벌어질지 알 만큼 충분히 헤아려 보지도 못한 채 말이야—그녀를 그런 일에 내맡기는 건 야박한 짓이요, 정말이지 그른 일일 거야. 그녀의 감정은 마땅히 존중받아야 해. 너도 그렇게 여겨지지 않니, 패니? 넌 망설이는구나.”\n\n“저는 크로퍼드 양이 안됐어요. 하지만 오빠가 스스로 반대하기로 마음먹었던 일, 삼촌께서 언짢아하시리라 오빠가 여기는 걸로 알려진 일에 끌려드는 걸 보는 게 더 안타까워요. 그건 다른 사람들한텐 얼마나 큰 승리가 되겠어요!”\n\n“내가 얼마나 형편없이 연기하는지 보면 저들도 그리 우쭐할 거리가 없을 거야. 하지만 아무튼 승리감이야 분명 있을 테고, 나는 그걸 무릅써야지. 그래도 내가 이 일이 소문나는 걸 막고, 그 구경거리를 좁히고, 우리 어리석음을 한데 그러모으는 수단이 될 수 있다면, 나는 충분히 값을 치른 셈이야. 지금 이대로라면 나는 아무 영향력도 없고, 아무것도 못 해. 저들의 비위를 거슬렀으니, 저들은 내 말을 안 들어. 하지만 이렇게 양보해 저들을 흐뭇하게 해 놓고 나면, 저들이 지금 냅다 치닫고 있는 것보다 훨씬 좁은 테두리 안으로 상연을 가두도록 설득할 가망이 없지 않아. 이건 실질적인 소득이 될 거야. 내 바람은 그것을 러시워스 부인과 그랜트 내외로만 한정하는 거란다. 이만하면 얻어 볼 만하지 않니?”\n\n“네, 그건 대단한 대목이 되겠어요.”\n\n“그래도 여전히 네 찬성은 못 얻었구나. 내가 그만큼 좋은 일을 해낼 가망이 있는 다른 방도를 하나라도 대 볼 수 있겠니?”\n\n“아뇨, 달리는 아무것도 떠오르지 않아요.”\n\n“그럼 찬성해 다오, 패니. 그게 없으면 나는 마음이 편치 않구나.”\n\n“아, 오빠!”\n\n“네가 나를 반대한다면, 나는 나 자신을 미덥잖게 여겨야 마땅하겠지, 그런데도—하지만 톰이 이런 식으로 계속하게 두는 건 도무지 안 될 일이야, 연기하도록 꾈 수 있는 사람이면 누구든 찾아 온 고장을 말 타고 쏘다니게—그게 누구든 상관없이, 신사처럼 보이기만 하면 그만이라니. 나는 네가 크로퍼드 양의 심정에 좀 더 마음을 실어 줄 줄 알았는데.”\n\n“틀림없이 그녀는 아주 기뻐할 거예요. 그녀한텐 큰 안도가 될 게 분명하고요.” 패니가 좀 더 살가운 투를 내보려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n“어젯밤 너를 대하는 그 태도만큼 그녀가 사랑스러워 보인 적은 없었어. 그 일로 그녀는 내 호의를 살 아주 강한 명분을 얻었지.”\n\n“정말이지 그녀는 아주 친절했어요. 그래서 저는 그녀가 그 일을 면하게 돼 기뻐요”…\n\n그녀는 그 너그러운 말을 끝맺지 못했다. 양심이 그 한복판에서 그녀를 멈춰 세웠던 것이다. 그러나 에드먼드는 흡족해했다.\n\n“나는 아침 먹고 곧장 내려가겠어.” 그가 말했다. “그리고 거기서 틀림없이 기쁨을 안겨 주겠지. 자, 이제, 패니야, 더는 널 방해하지 않으마. 넌 책을 읽고 싶을 테니. 하지만 너한테 말을 건네고 마음을 정하기 전엔 도무지 편할 수가 없었어. 자나 깨나 밤새 이 일로 머릿속이 가득했거든. 이건 화근이지만, 그래도 나는 분명 그것을 될 수 있는 것보다는 작게 만들고 있는 거야. 톰이 일어났으면 곧장 그리로 가서 이 일을 끝내 버려야지. 그럼 우리가 아침상에서 만날 때쯤엔, 그토록 한마음으로 함께 바보짓을 하게 됐다는 기대에 다들 잔뜩 흐뭇해져 있을 거야. 그동안 너는, 아마 중국으로 여행이나 떠나 있겠구나. 매카트니 경은 어찌 돼 가니?”—하며 그는 탁자 위의 책 한 권을 펼치고는 다른 몇 권을 집어 들었다. “그리고 여기, 그 큰 책이 물리거든 널 달래 줄 크래브의 「이야기 시집」과 「빈둥거리는 사람」도 손닿는 데 있구나. 나는 네 이 자그마한 살림살이가 여간 대견하지 않아. 그리고 내가 가고 나면, 너는 이 연기니 뭐니 하는 시답잖은 생각을 머릿속에서 다 비우고, 네 탁자 앞에 편안히 앉겠지. 하지만 여기서 몸 얼도록 있지는 마라.”\n\n그는 갔다. 그러나 패니에게는 독서도, 중국도, 마음의 평온도 없었다. 그는 그녀에게 더없이 뜻밖의, 더없이 있을 수 없는, 더없이 달갑잖은 소식을 전한 것이다. 그리고 그녀는 다른 아무것도 생각할 수 없었다. 연기를 하다니! 그토록 반대해 놓고—그토록 옳고 그토록 공공연한 반대였는데! 그가 하는 말을 죄다 듣고, 그의 낯빛을 보고, 그의 심경을 알고 나서도. 이게 있을 수 있는 일일까? 에드먼드가 그토록 앞뒤가 안 맞다니! 그는 스스로를 속이고 있는 게 아닐까? 그가 그른 게 아닐까? 아아! 이 모두가 크로퍼드 양이 한 일이었다. 그녀는 그의 말 한마디 한마디에서 그 여자의 영향을 보았고, 비참했다. 앞서 그녀를 괴롭혔던, 그리고 그의 말을 듣는 동안 죄다 잠들어 있던, 제 처신에 대한 의심과 불안은 이제 대수롭지 않은 것이 되었다. 이 한층 깊은 근심이 그것을 통째로 삼켜 버렸다. 일은 흘러가는 대로 흘러가라지. 그녀는 그것이 어찌 끝나든 개의치 않았다. 사촌들이 몰아세울지언정, 이제 그녀를 성가시게 할 수는 거의 없었다. 그녀는 그들의 손이 닿지 않는 데 있었다. 그리고 끝내 굽혀야만 한다 해도—상관없었다—이제 모든 것이 비참했으니까.\n\n# 제17장\n\n그날은 참으로 버트럼 씨와 마리아에게 승리의 날이었다. 에드먼드의 분별을 이렇게 이겨 내다니 그들의 바람을 넘어서는 일이었고, 더없이 통쾌했다. 이제 그들의 애지중지하는 계획을 어지럽힐 것은 아무것도 없었고, 그들은 그 변심을 시샘에서 온 약점 탓으로 돌리며, 온갖 식으로 흡족해진 감정의 온갖 신명으로 저희끼리 은근히 축하를 주고받았다. 에드먼드가 여전히 근엄한 낯을 하고, 그 계획이 대체로 마음에 안 든다느니 그 연극만은 못마땅해할 수밖에 없다느니 해도 좋았다. 그들의 목적은 이루어진 것이다. 그가 연기를 하게 됐고, 그것도 오로지 이기적인 마음의 힘에 떠밀려서였으니 말이다. 에드먼드는 앞서 지켜 온 그 도덕적 높이에서 내려온 것이고, 그들은 그가 내려온 만큼 더 흐뭇해졌을 뿐 아니라 더 떳떳해지기까지 했다.\n\n그래도 그들은 이 일에서 그에게 아주 잘 처신했으니, 입가에 어린 잔주름 말고는 어떤 우쭐함도 내비치지 않았고, 찰스 매독스가 끼어드는 것을 면하게 된 것을 마치 저희 뜻과 달리 억지로 그를 받아들이게 될 뻔하다 벗어난 양 여기는 듯했다. “이 일을 온전히 저희 집안 울타리 안에 두는 것이야말로 저희가 각별히 바라던 바였다. 낯선 이가 끼면 저희의 온갖 편안함이 무너졌을 것이다.” 그리고 에드먼드가 그 생각을 이어받아 관객을 제한했으면 하는 제 바람을 넌지시 비치자, 그들은 그 순간의 고분고분함으로 무엇이든 약속할 태세였다. 온통 흐뭇함과 북돋움이었다. 노리스 부인은 그의 의상을 지어 주겠다 나섰고, 예이츠 씨는 안할트가 남작과 함께하는 마지막 장면에 몸짓과 힘줌이 자못 들어갈 만하다고 그를 안심시켰으며, 러시워스 씨는 그의 대사를 세어 주는 소임을 떠맡았다.\n\n“어쩌면,” 톰이 말했다. “이제 패니가 우리 청을 좀 더 들어줄 마음이 생겼을지도 몰라. 네가 그 앨 설득할 수 있을지도 모르고.”\n\n“아니, 그 앤 마음을 굳혔어. 절대 연기 안 할 거야.”\n\n“아! 그래 알았어.” 그러고는 더 아무 말이 없었다. 그러나 패니는 다시 위험에 처했음을 느꼈고, 그 위험에 대한 그녀의 무심함은 벌써 무너지기 시작하고 있었다.\n\n에드먼드의 이 변심을 두고, 목사관에서도 저택에서 못지않게 미소가 오갔다. 크로퍼드 양은 미소 지으니 무척 사랑스러워 보였고, 이 온 소동에 이내 되살아난 명랑함으로 뛰어드니 그것이 그에게 미치는 효과는 오직 하나뿐일 수밖에 없었다. “그가 그런 감정을 존중한 것은 분명 옳았다. 그리 마음먹기를 잘했다.” 그리하여 그 아침은, 아주 건실하지는 않아도 아주 달콤한 흡족함 속에 흘러갔다. 그 일에서 패니에게 이로운 점이 하나 나왔으니, 크로퍼드 양의 간곡한 청에 그랜트 부인이 여느 때의 그 좋은 성품으로 패니가 맡을 뻔했던 배역을 떠맡기로 한 것이다. 그리고 그것이 그날 하루 그녀의 마음을 기쁘게 한 전부였다. 그런데 이것마저도, 에드먼드의 입을 통해 전해지자 아픔 한 자락을 함께 몰고 왔으니, 그녀가 신세를 진 것이 다름 아닌 크로퍼드 양이요—고마움을 자아낼 그 친절한 수고를 베푼 것이 크로퍼드 양이요, 그 수고를 베푼 공이 감탄에 겨운 목소리로 이야기되었기 때문이다. 그녀는 안전했다. 그러나 여기서는 평온과 안전이 서로 이어져 있지 않았다. 그녀의 마음이 평온에서 이토록 멀었던 적이 없었다. 그녀는 자신이 그른 짓을 했다고는 느낄 수 없었으나, 그 밖의 온갖 면에서 마음이 편치 않았다. 그녀의 마음도 판단도 똑같이 에드먼드의 결정에 반대했다. 그녀는 그의 흔들림을 두둔할 수 없었고, 그 흔들림 속에서도 그가 행복해하는 것이 그녀를 비참하게 했다. 그녀는 시샘과 동요로 가득했다. 크로퍼드 양이 명랑한 낯빛으로 다가오는데 그것이 모욕처럼 느껴졌고, 저를 향해 다정한 말을 건네는데 그녀는 좀처럼 차분히 대꾸할 수가 없었다. 그녀 둘레의 모든 이가 명랑하고 분주하고 잘나가고 대단했다. 저마다 관심거리가, 제 배역이, 제 의상이, 좋아하는 장면이, 제 벗과 한패가 있었다. 다들 의논하고 견주는 데서 할 일을 찾거나, 서로 던지는 장난스러운 착상에서 재미를 찾고 있었다. 오직 그녀만이 서글프고 하찮았다. 그녀는 어느 것에도 낄 몫이 없었다. 가도 그만 있어도 그만이었다. 그 소란 한복판에 있든, 거기서 물러나 동쪽 방의 외로움으로 숨어들든, 눈에 띄지도 아쉬워하는 이도 없었다. 이것보다야 무엇이든 나았으리라는 생각마저 들 지경이었다. 그랜트 부인은 대단한 사람이었다. 그녀의 좋은 성품은 명예로이 입에 올랐고, 그녀의 취향과 시간이 헤아려졌으며, 그녀가 있어 주기를 바랐다. 사람들은 그녀를 찾고, 떠받들고, 칭찬했다. 그리하여 패니는 처음엔 그녀가 맡은 그 배역을 시샘할 위험이 얼마간 있었다. 그러나 곰곰이 생각하니 한결 나은 마음이 들었고, 그랜트 부인은 존경받을 자격이 있으나 그것은 결코 자기에게 돌아올 수 있는 것이 아니었음을, 또 설령 자기가 그 가장 큰 존경을 받았다 한들, 오직 삼촌만 생각해도 온통 나무랄 수밖에 없는 그런 계획에 끼어들고서는 결코 마음이 편할 수 없었으리라는 것을 깨달았다.\n\n그들 가운데 서글퍼진 마음이 오롯이 패니의 것만은 아니었으니, 이내 그녀도 스스로 인정하기 시작했다. 줄리아 또한 괴로워하는 사람이었다—다만 그리 나무랄 데 없이 그런 것은 아니었지만.\n\n헨리 크로퍼드는 그녀의 감정을 가지고 놀았다. 그러나 그녀는 아주 오랫동안 그의 살가움을 받아들이고 심지어 구하기까지 했으니, 언니에 대한 시샘도 마땅히 그 병을 고쳐 주었어야 할 만큼 이치에 닿는 것이었다. 그리고 이제 그가 마리아를 더 좋아한다는 확신이 억지로 들이밀어진 마당에, 그녀는 마리아의 처지를 걱정하지도, 저 자신을 위해 사리에 맞는 평온을 되찾으려 애쓰지도 않은 채 그것에 굴복했다. 그녀는 무엇으로도 누그러뜨릴 수 없고, 어떤 호기심으로도 건드릴 수 없고, 어떤 재담으로도 즐겁게 할 수 없는 그런 침울함에 싸여 음울한 침묵 속에 앉아 있거나, 아니면 예이츠 씨의 살가움을 받아 주며 그 사람하고만 억지 명랑함으로 이야기하고 다른 이들의 연기를 비웃었다.\n\n모욕이 있고 나서 하루 이틀, 헨리 크로퍼드는 여느 때의 그 정중함과 인사치레의 공세로 그것을 지워 버리려 애썼다. 그러나 몇 번 퇴짜를 무릅쓰고 밀어붙일 만큼 그 일에 마음을 쓰지는 않았다. 그리고 이내 제 연극에 너무 바빠 시시덕거림 하나에 쓸 시간밖에 없게 되자, 그는 그 다툼에 무심해졌다—아니, 오히려 그것을 다행스러운 일로 여겼으니, 머잖아 그랜트 부인 아닌 이의 마음에도 기대를 부풀렸을 법한 것을 조용히 끝내 준 셈이었기 때문이다. 그랜트 부인은 줄리아가 연극에서 밀려나 홀대받은 채 곁에 앉아 있는 것이 반갑지 않았다. 그러나 그것이 정말로 제 행복에 얽힌 일도 아니고, 헨리야말로 제 마음을 가장 잘 아는 이일 테고, 게다가 그가 더없이 그럴싸한 미소를 지으며 저도 줄리아도 서로를 진지하게 마음에 둔 적은 한 번도 없었노라 장담하니, 그녀는 그저 언니 쪽에 대한 앞서의 경계를 새삼 되뇌며, 거기서 너무 지나치게 반한 나머지 제 평온을 위태롭게 하지 말라 당부하는 수밖에 없었다. 그러고는 젊은이들 전반에 명랑함을 안기는 일, 특히 저에게 그토록 소중한 두 사람의 즐거움을 그토록 북돋는 일이라면 무엇에든 기꺼이 제 몫을 거들었다.\n\n“줄리아가 헨리한테 반하지 않은 게 오히려 이상하구나.” 이것이 메리에게 건넨 그녀의 소견이었다.\n\n“반했을걸요.” 메리가 쌀쌀맞게 대답했다. “제 짐작엔 두 자매 다 그런 것 같은데요.”\n\n“둘 다라니! 안 돼, 안 돼, 그건 안 될 말이지. 그 사람한텐 그런 낌새도 내비치지 마라. 러시워스 씨를 생각해야지!”\n\n“차라리 버트럼 양더러 러시워스 씨를 생각하라고 하시는 게 낫겠어요. 그 편이 그 여자한텐 좀 이로울지도 모르니까요. 저야 러시워스 씨의 재산과 독립된 처지를 곧잘 떠올리며 그게 다른 사람 손에 있으면 좋겠다 싶지만, 그 사람 자체를 떠올리는 일은 결코 없어요. 그만한 재산이면 사내가 주(州)를 대표할 수도 있겠죠. 직업 하나 안 가지고도 주를 대표할 수 있고요.”\n\n“머잖아 그이가 의회에 들어가긴 할 거야. 토머스 경이 오시면, 아마 어느 선거구로든 들어가겠지, 다만 여태 그이한테 무슨 일이든 하는 길을 터 준 사람이 없었을 뿐이야.”\n\n“토머스 경은 집에 오시면 숱한 대단한 일을 이루실 참이지.” 잠깐 뜸을 들인 뒤 메리가 말했다. “포프를 흉내 낸 호킨스 브라운의 「담배에게 부치는 글」을 기억하세요?—\n\n축복받은 잎이여! 그 향긋한 바람이 법학도에게 겸손을, 목사에게 분별을 나누어 주는도다.\n\n제가 이렇게 패러디해 볼게요—\n\n축복받은 기사여! 그 위엄 서린 눈길이 자녀에게 풍요를, 러시워스에게 분별을 나누어 주는도다.\n\n어때요, 그랜트 부인? 모든 게 토머스 경의 귀환에 달린 것 같지 않아요?”\n\n“그이를 그 집안 속에서 보면, 그이의 그 무게가 아주 지당하고 사리에 맞는다는 걸 알게 될 게다, 정말이야. 나는 그이 없이 우리가 그리 잘 지내는 것 같지가 않구나. 그이한텐 그런 집안의 가장에 걸맞은 기품 있고 위엄 서린 태도가 있어서, 모두를 제자리에 붙들어 두거든. 버트럼 부인은 그이가 집에 있을 때보다 지금 오히려 더 있으나 마나 한 사람 같고, 노리스 부인을 다잡을 사람도 그이 말곤 아무도 없어. 하지만 메리, 마리아 버트럼이 헨리한테 마음이 있다고 넘겨짚진 마라. 줄리아가 아닌 건 분명해, 그렇지 않고서야 어젯밤 예이츠 씨하고 그렇게 시시덕거렸을 리 없지. 그리고 헨리와 마리아가 아주 좋은 벗이긴 해도, 그 애는 소더턴을 너무 좋아해서 마음이 변할 것 같진 않구나.”\n\n“헨리가 계약서에 도장 찍기 전에 끼어든다면, 저는 러시워스 씨 쪽엔 별로 걸지 않겠어요.”\n\n“네가 정 그런 의심이 든다면, 무슨 수를 써야겠구나. 연극이 다 끝나는 대로 그이한테 진지하게 이야기해서 제 마음을 똑똑히 알게 만들자. 그리고 아무 뜻도 없는 거라면, 헨리라 해도 한동안 멀리 떠나보내는 거지.”\n\n그래도 줄리아는 정말로 괴로워하고 있었다, 그랜트 부인은 그것을 알아채지 못했고 제 식구 여럿의 눈길마저 그것을 비껴갔지만. 그녀는 사랑했고, 여전히 사랑하고 있었으며, 비록 사리에 안 맞을지언정 애틋한 희망이 어그러진 데다 홀대당했다는 강한 느낌까지 겹쳤을 때 뜨거운 기질과 드높은 자존심이 으레 견뎌야 할 온갖 괴로움을 고스란히 겪고 있었다. 그녀의 마음은 쓰라리고 성이 나 있었고, 그녀는 오직 성난 위안밖에 누릴 줄 몰랐다. 여느 때 스스럼없이 지내던 언니가 이제 그녀의 가장 큰 원수가 되었다. 둘은 서로 멀어졌다. 그리고 줄리아는, 저기서 아직도 이어지는 그 살가움에 무언가 괴로운 끝장이 나기를, 저 자신에게뿐 아니라 러시워스 씨에게도 그토록 부끄러운 처신을 한 마리아에게 무슨 벌이 내리기를 바라는 마음을 넘어서지 못했다. 이해관계가 같을 때는 아주 좋은 벗이 되지 못하게 막을 만한 이렇다 할 기질의 흠도, 의견의 차이도 없었건만, 이런 시련 앞에서 그 자매는 서로에게 자비롭거나 공정할 만큼, 서로에게 존중이나 연민을 베풀 만큼 정도 원칙도 지니지 못했다. 마리아는 제 승리를 만끽하며 줄리아에는 아랑곳없이 제 뜻을 밀고 나갔다. 그리고 줄리아는 마리아가 헨리 크로퍼드에게 남달리 대접받는 것을 볼 때마다, 그것이 시샘을 자아내 끝내 공공연한 소동을 몰고 오리라 믿어 마지않았다.\n\n패니는 줄리아의 이런 모습을 많이 보았고 많이 딱해했다. 그러나 둘 사이에는 겉으로 드러난 벗함이 없었다. 줄리아는 아무것도 털어놓지 않았고, 패니도 함부로 다가서지 않았다. 그들은 두 외로운 괴로워하는 이였거나, 오직 패니의 마음속에서만 이어져 있었다.\n\n두 오라비와 이모가 줄리아의 심란함에 무심하고 그 참된 까닭에 눈멀어 있었던 것은, 저마다 제 마음이 꽉 차 있던 탓으로 돌릴 수밖에 없다. 그들은 온통 딴 데 정신이 팔려 있었다. 톰은 제 극장 일에 골몰해, 거기에 곧바로 얽히지 않은 것은 아무것도 눈에 들어오지 않았다. 에드먼드는 무대 위의 제 배역과 실제 제 처지 사이에서, 크로퍼드 양의 몫과 제 처신 사이에서, 사랑과 한결같음 사이에서, 마찬가지로 무엇 하나 살피지 못했다. 그리고 노리스 부인은, 그 무리의 자잘한 일들을 죄다 꾸리고 지시하고, 아무도 고마워하지 않는 알뜰한 임기응변으로 그들의 갖가지 의상을 감독하며, 흐뭇한 올곧음으로 여기서 반 크라운 저기서 반 크라운 아껴 떠나 있는 토머스 경을 위해 모으느라 너무 바빠, 그 딸들의 처신을 지켜보거나 그 행복을 지켜 줄 겨를이 없었다.\n\n# 제18장\n\n이제 모든 것이 착착 굴러가고 있었다. 극장도, 남녀 배우들도, 의상도 죄다 진척되어 갔다. 그러나 다른 큰 걸림돌은 생기지 않았어도, 며칠 지나지 않아 패니는, 그것이 무리 자신들에게도 온통 방해 없는 즐거움만은 아님을, 그리고 처음엔 저로선 벅찰 지경이던 그런 한마음과 신명이 이어지는 꼴을 자기가 지켜보지 않아도 됨을 알게 되었다. 저마다 짜증거리가 생기기 시작했다. 에드먼드에겐 많았다. 그의 판단과는 온통 어긋나게, 무대 배경 화가가 도시에서 와서 일에 매달렸으니, 비용이 크게 불어난 데다, 더 나쁘게는 그들이 벌이는 일의 요란함까지 커졌다. 그리고 그의 형은, 상연을 은밀히 하는 문제에서 정말로 그의 뜻에 이끌리기는커녕, 오는 길에 마주친 집안마다 초대장을 돌리고 있었다. 톰 자신도 무대 화가의 더딘 작업에 애를 태우고, 기다림의 괴로움을 느끼기 시작했다. 그는 제 배역을—제 모든 배역을 다 외웠으니, 집사와 겹칠 만한 자잘한 배역은 죄다 맡았던 것이다—그리고 어서 연기하고 싶어 안달이 나기 시작했다. 그리하여 이렇게 할 일 없이 흘러가는 나날은, 제 모든 배역을 다 합쳐도 하찮다는 느낌을 키우고, 다른 어떤 연극을 고르지 않은 것을 한결 아쉬워하게 만들 뿐이었다.\n\n늘 아주 정중히 귀 기울여 주고, 게다가 흔히 곁에서 들어 줄 유일한 사람이던 패니에게로, 그들 대부분의 불평과 하소연이 흘러들었다. 그녀는 예이츠 씨가 대체로 지독하게 고래고래 외친다고 여겨진다는 것을, 예이츠 씨가 헨리 크로퍼드에게 실망했다는 것을, 톰 버트럼이 말을 너무 빨리 해서 알아들을 수가 없으리라는 것을, 그랜트 부인이 웃어 대는 통에 다 망친다는 것을, 에드먼드가 제 배역에 뒤처졌다는 것을, 그리고 대사마다 대사를 일러 줄 사람이 있어야 하는 러시워스 씨와 무슨 일이든 함께하는 것이 곤욕이라는 것을 알았다. 또한 그녀는, 딱한 러시워스 씨가 함께 연습해 줄 사람을 좀처럼 못 구한다는 것도 알았다. 그의 하소연도 다른 이들의 것과 함께 그녀 앞에 놓였던 것이다. 그리고 사촌 마리아가 그를 피하는 것이 그녀 눈에는 어찌나 뚜렷하고, 그녀와 크로퍼드 씨 사이의 첫 장면 연습이 어찌나 쓸데없이 잦던지, 이내 그녀는 그에게서도 또 다른 하소연이 나올까 봐 온통 겁이 났다. 다들 흡족해하고 다들 즐기기는커녕, 그녀가 보니 저마다 저한테 없는 무언가를 요구하고, 남들에게 불만거리를 안기고 있었다. 저마다 배역이 너무 길거나 너무 짧았다. 아무도 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 주의를 기울이지 않았고, 아무도 자기가 어느 쪽에서 등장해야 하는지 기억하지 못했으며, 하소연하는 당사자 말고는 아무도 어떤 지시도 지키지 않았다.\n\n패니는 자기도 그들 누구 못지않게 그 연극에서 죄 없는 즐거움을 얻고 있다고 여겼다. 헨리 크로퍼드는 연기를 잘했고, 마리아를 위한 몇몇 대사가 자아내는 감정에도 불구하고, 극장으로 살그머니 기어들어 제1막 연습을 지켜보는 것이 그녀에게는 즐거움이었다. 마리아도 연기를 잘한다고, 아니 너무 잘한다고 그녀는 생각했다. 그리고 첫 한두 번의 연습이 지나자, 패니는 그들의 유일한 관객이 되기 시작했고, 때로는 대사를 일러 주는 이로, 때로는 구경꾼으로, 곧잘 아주 요긴했다. 그녀가 판단할 수 있는 한, 크로퍼드 씨가 단연 모두 가운데 가장 나은 배우였다. 그는 에드먼드보다 자신감이 있고, 톰보다 판단력이 있고, 예이츠 씨보다 재주와 취향이 있었다. 그녀는 사내로서 그를 좋아하지 않았으나, 가장 나은 배우임은 인정할 수밖에 없었고, 이 점에서는 그녀와 생각이 다른 이가 많지 않았다. 하기야 예이츠 씨는 그의 밋밋함과 맹숭맹숭함을 소리 높여 나무랐다. 그리고 마침내 그날이 왔으니, 러시워스 씨가 시커먼 낯으로 그녀에게 돌아서서 이렇게 말했다. “이게 다 뭐 그리 대단할 게 있다고 여기세요? 나는 죽어도 그 사람을 못 우러러보겠어요. 그리고 우리끼리 얘기지만, 저렇게 왜소하고 자그마하고 볼품없는 사내가 훌륭한 배우인 척 나서는 걸 보면, 내 생각엔 아주 우스운 노릇이에요.”\n\n이 순간부터 그의 예전 시샘이 되살아났는데, 크로퍼드에 대한 기대가 커져 가던 마리아는 그것을 지우려 딱히 애쓰지 않았다. 그리하여 러시워스 씨가 그 마흔두 마디 대사를 익히는 경지에 이를 가망은 훨씬 줄어들었다. 그가 그것을 봐줄 만하게 해낼 가망으로 말하면, 그 어머니 말고는 아무도 그런 생각을 손톱만큼도 품지 않았다. 정말이지 그 어머니는 아들 배역이 좀 더 크지 않은 것을 아쉬워하며, 연습이 그의 모든 장면을 알아볼 만큼 진척될 때까지 맨스필드로 건너오기를 미루었다. 그러나 다른 이들이 바라는 것은, 그가 받는말과 제 대사 첫 줄만 기억하고 나머지는 대사 일러 주는 이를 따라갈 수 있는 정도, 그 이상은 아무것도 없었다. 딱한 마음과 착한 심성으로 패니는, 그에게 외우는 법을 가르치느라 크게 애를 썼다. 제 힘이 닿는 온갖 도움과 지시를 주고, 그를 위해 인위적인 기억을 만들어 주려 애쓰며, 제가 그의 배역을 한 마디 한 마디 다 외우기까지 했으나, 정작 그는 그리 나아지지 않았다.\n\n불편하고 불안하고 조마조마한 감정을 그녀는 분명 많이 지녔다. 그러나 이 모든 것에다, 제 시간과 마음을 앗아 가는 다른 일들까지 있었으니, 그녀는 그들 틈에서 할 일이나 쓸모가 없기는커녕, 언짢음 속의 벗이 없지 않은 것과 꼭 마찬가지였다. 제 연민에 못지않게 제 한가로움에도 요구가 없지 않았던 것이다. 처음의 예상이 안긴 그 우울함은 근거 없는 것이었음이 드러났다. 그녀는 이따금 모두에게 쓸모가 있었고, 어쩌면 누구 못지않게 마음이 편안했다.\n\n게다가 해야 할 바느질이 잔뜩 있었고, 거기에 그녀의 손이 필요했다. 그리고 노리스 부인이 그녀를 나머지 사람들 못지않게 팔자 좋다 여기고 있음은, 그녀에게 도움을 요구하는 태도에서 여실히 드러났다—“이리 온, 패니,” 그녀가 외쳤다. “너한테야 참 좋은 시절이겠지만, 그렇게 늘 이 방 저 방 거닐며 태평하게 구경이나 하고 있어선 안 되지. 나는 네가 여기 필요하다. 나는 새틴을 더 안 들이고 러시워스 씨 망토를 지어 보려고 서 있지도 못할 만큼 뼈 빠지게 일해 왔어. 그러니 이제 네가 이걸 꿰매 맞추는 걸 좀 거들어도 되겠구나. 솔기가 셋뿐이야, 넌 눈 깜짝할 새에 해낼 게다. 나야 이 손 놀리는 일만 하면 되면 오죽 좋으련만. 정말이지 네가 제일 팔자 좋아. 하지만 너만큼만 하는 사람뿐이라면, 우리 일은 그리 빨리 못 나갈 거다.”\n\n패니는 아무런 항변도 하지 않고 그 일감을 아주 얌전히 받아 들었다. 그러나 좀 더 다정한 버트럼 이모가 그녀를 두둔해 한마디 했다—\n\n“패니가 좋아하는 것도 무리가 아니지요, 언니. 그 앤 이게 다 처음이잖아요. 언니랑 나도 예전엔 연극을 아주 좋아했고, 나야 지금도 그렇고요. 그리고 나도 좀 더 한가해지는 대로 그 애들 연습을 들여다볼 참이에요. 무슨 연극이니, 패니? 나한텐 통 얘기 안 해 줬잖니.”\n\n“아이참, 지금은 그 애한테 묻지 마세요. 패니는 이야기하면서 동시에 일하는 그런 축이 못 되니까요. 「연인들의 맹세」에 관한 거예요.”\n\n“제 생각엔, 이모,” 패니가 버트럼 이모에게 말했다. “내일 저녁에 세 막을 연습할 것 같아요. 그럼 이모께서 배우들을 한꺼번에 다 보실 수 있을 거예요.”\n\n“막이 걸릴 때까지 기다리시는 게 낫겠어요.” 노리스 부인이 끼어들었다. “막이야 하루 이틀이면 걸릴 테고—막도 없이 연극이랄 게 무슨 소용이겠어요—그게 아주 곱게 꽃 줄 모양으로 걷어 올려지는 걸 못 보신다면 내가 크게 잘못 안 거고요.”\n\n버트럼 부인은 기다리는 것을 아주 순순히 받아들이는 듯했다. 패니는 이모의 그 태연함을 함께 나누지 못했다. 그녀는 내일 일을 무척 많이 생각했으니, 만일 세 막을 연습한다면 그때 에드먼드와 크로퍼드 양이 처음으로 함께 연기할 것이었기 때문이다. 제3막에는 둘 사이의 한 장면이 나오는데, 그것이 그녀에게는 각별히 마음이 쓰였고, 그들이 그것을 어떻게 해낼지 보고 싶으면서도 보기가 두려웠다. 그 장면의 온 주제는 사랑이었다—사랑의 결혼을 신사가 그려 내야 했고, 사랑의 고백에 조금 못 미치는 말을 숙녀가 해야 했던 것이다.\n\n그녀는 그 장면을 몇 번이고 거듭 읽었으니, 괴롭고도 의아한 온갖 감정에 휩싸인 채였다. 그리고 그들이 그것을 그려 보이는 것을, 거의 너무나 마음 졸이게 하는 일로 여기며 기다렸다. 그녀는 그들이 아직, 은밀히조차도, 그것을 연습하지 않았으리라 믿었다.\n\n이튿날이 왔고, 저녁 계획은 그대로였으며, 그것을 헤아리는 패니의 마음은 조금도 덜 심란해지지 않았다. 그녀는 이모의 지시대로 아주 부지런히 일했으나, 그 부지런함과 침묵은 자못 넋 나가고 불안한 마음을 감추고 있었다. 그리고 정오 무렵, 그녀는 일감을 들고 동쪽 방으로 달아났으니, 헨리 크로퍼드가 막 제안하던 또 한 번의, 그리고 그녀가 보기엔 더없이 쓸데없는 제1막 연습에 낄 일이 없게 하려는 것이었고, 제 시간을 저 혼자 갖고 싶은 마음과 러시워스 씨를 마주치기 싫은 마음에서였다. 현관을 지나며 두 부인이 목사관에서 걸어 올라오는 것을 얼핏 보았어도 물러나려는 그녀의 마음은 바뀌지 않았고, 그녀는 동쪽 방에서 방해받지 않고 십오 분쯤 일하며 생각에 잠겼는데, 그때 문을 살며시 두드리는 소리에 뒤이어 크로퍼드 양이 들어섰다.\n\n“여기 맞나요? 네, 여기가 동쪽 방이군요. 프라이스 양, 미안하지만, 도움을 청하려고 일부러 이렇게 찾아왔어요.”\n\n패니는 몹시 놀랐으나, 정중한 태도로 이 방의 안주인임을 내보이려 애쓰며, 불기 없는 제 난로의 번들거리는 살대를 근심스레 바라보았다.\n\n“고마워요. 나 아주 따뜻해요, 아주 따뜻해. 잠깐 여기 있게 해 주고, 부디 내 제3막을 좀 들어 줘요. 책을 가져왔으니, 나하고 이걸 연습만 해 준다면 얼마나 고마울지! 오늘 여기 온 건 저녁에 대비해 에드먼드하고—우리 둘이서만—이걸 연습할 작정이었는데, 그가 안 보이네요. 게다가 설령 그가 있다 한들, 나는 조금 마음을 단단히 다잡기 전엔 그 사람하고는 이걸 끝까지 해내지 못할 것 같아요. 정말이지 대사가 한두 마디 좀 그런 게 있거든요. 그렇게 좀 해 줄 거죠?”\n\n패니는 더없이 정중하게 그러겠노라 했으나, 그 말을 그리 차분한 목소리로 하지는 못했다.\n\n“혹시 내가 말하는 그 대목을 본 적이 있나요?” 크로퍼드 양이 책을 펼치며 말을 이었다. “여기예요. 처음엔 별생각이 없었는데—어머나, 정말. 자, 이 대사를 봐요, 그리고 이것도, 이것도. 내가 어떻게 그 사람 얼굴을 마주 보고 이런 말을 한담? 프라이스 양이라면 할 수 있겠어요? 하기야 그 사람은 프라이스 양의 사촌이니, 그게 사정이 아주 다르긴 하죠. 나하고 이걸 연습해 줘야 해요, 그래야 내가 프라이스 양을 그 사람이라 여기고 차츰 익숙해질 테니. 이따금 프라이스 양한텐 그 사람을 닮은 데가 있거든요.”\n\n“제가요? 더없이 기꺼이 최선을 다할게요. 하지만 저는 그 대목을 읽어야 해요, 외운 게 얼마 없거든요.”\n\n“하나도 없겠죠, 아마. 물론 책은 프라이스 양이 들어요. 자, 시작해 볼까요. 무대 앞으로 끌어다 놓을 의자가 둘 손닿는 데 있어야 해요. 저기—아주 좋은 공부방 의자네요, 극장용으로 만든 건 아닐 테고. 어린 계집애들이 공부하면서 앉아 발을 툭툭 차 대기에나 훨씬 어울리는 것들이죠. 프라이스 양네 가정교사와 삼촌이 이것들이 이런 데 쓰이는 걸 보면 뭐라 하실까요? 토머스 경께서 지금 우리를 들여다보신다면, 성호를 그으실 거예요, 우리가 온 집 안 곳곳에서 연습을 하고 있으니. 예이츠는 식당에서 폭풍처럼 몰아치고 있어요. 올라오는 길에 그 소리를 들었죠. 그리고 극장이야 물론 그 지칠 줄 모르는 연습꾼들, 아가사와 프레더릭이 차지하고 있고요. 그 둘이 완벽하지 않다면야 내가 놀랄 거예요. 그나저나 오 분 전에 그들을 들여다봤는데, 마침 딱 서로 껴안지 않으려고 애쓰는 대목이었고, 러시워스 씨가 나랑 같이 있었지 뭐예요. 그 사람 낯빛이 좀 야릇해지는 것 같기에, 나는 되도록 잘 둘러대느라 그 사람한테 속삭였죠. ‘우리 아가사는 참 훌륭하겠어요, 태도에 어쩜 저리 모성이 어렸을까요, 목소리며 낯빛에 어쩜 저리 온통 모성이 넘칠까요.’ 나 참 잘하지 않았어요? 그 사람 낯빛이 대번에 환해지더군요. 자, 이제 내 독백을 해 볼게요.”\n\n그녀가 시작했고, 패니도 에드먼드를 그려 낸다는 생각이 그토록 강하게 불러일으키는 온갖 조심스러운 감정으로 어울려 들었다—그러나 그 낯빛과 목소리가 너무나 참으로 여성스러워 사내를 그려 보이는 그림으로는 그리 좋지 못했다. 그래도 그런 안할트를 상대로도 크로퍼드 양은 넉넉히 용기를 냈고, 둘이 그 장면의 절반쯤을 해냈을 때, 문 두드리는 소리에 잠깐 멈추게 되었고, 다음 순간 에드먼드가 들어서면서 그 모든 것이 멎었다.\n\n이 뜻밖의 만남에 세 사람 저마다 놀라움과 겸연쩍음과 반가움을 내비쳤다. 그리고 에드먼드가 크로퍼드 양을 데려온 것과 똑같은 볼일로 온 터라, 그 둘의 겸연쩍음과 반가움은 한순간에 그치지 않을 성싶었다. 그도 제 책을 들고, 저녁에 대비해 함께 연습하며 채비를 도와 달라 청하려 패니를 찾던 참이었으니, 크로퍼드 양이 집에 와 있는 줄은 몰랐던 것이다. 그리하여 이렇게 한자리에 모이고, 서로의 궁리를 견주고, 패니의 친절한 수고를 함께 기려 주는 데서 오는 기쁨과 활기가 컸다.\n\n그녀는 그들의 그 뜨거움을 따라갈 수 없었다. 그들의 열기에 그녀의 기운은 가라앉았고, 어느 쪽에게든 너무나 아무것도 아닌 존재가 되어 가는 자신을 느꼈으니, 어느 한쪽에게 찾아진 것에서도 아무런 위안을 얻지 못했다. 이제 그들은 함께 연습해야 했다. 에드먼드가 제안하고, 다그치고, 간청하니, 처음엔 그리 마다하지 않던 그 숙녀도 더는 거절하지 못했고, 패니는 그저 대사를 일러 주고 그들을 지켜보는 데에만 필요했다. 정말이지 그녀는 심판이자 비평가의 소임을 맡아, 그것을 발휘해 그들의 온갖 잘못을 죄다 일러 달라는 간곡한 청까지 받았다. 그러나 그리하는 것에는 그녀 안의 온갖 감정이 움츠러들었다—그녀는 할 수 없었고, 하려 하지 않았고, 감히 엄두도 못 냈다. 설령 그녀가 달리 비평할 자격이 있었다 한들, 양심이 그녀를 막아 감히 못마땅함을 입에 올리지 못하게 했을 것이다. 그녀는 통틀어 못마땅함을 너무 많이 느낀 나머지, 낱낱이 짚기엔 정직하지도 안전하지도 못하리라 여겼다. 그들에게 대사를 일러 주는 것만으로도 그녀에겐 충분했다. 아니, 때로는 충분하고도 남았다. 늘 책에 주의를 기울일 수는 없었기 때문이다. 그들을 지켜보다 그녀는 저 자신을 잊었고, 에드먼드의 태도에 점점 어리는 열정에 마음이 흔들려, 한번은 그가 도움이 필요한 바로 그 대목에서 책장을 덮고 고개를 돌려 버렸다. 그것은 아주 그럴 만한 피로 탓으로 여겨져, 그녀는 고맙다는 말과 함께 안쓰러운 대접을 받았다. 그러나 그녀는 그들이 짐작하리라 바라는 것보다 더 그 안쓰러움을 받아 마땅했다. 마침내 그 장면이 끝났고, 패니는 서로에게 찬사를 건네는 그들 틈에 억지로 제 칭찬을 보탰다. 그리고 다시 홀로 남아 그 모든 것을 되새길 수 있게 되자, 그녀는 그들의 연기가 정말이지 그들의 명성을 확실히 해 줄 만한, 그러면서 저 자신에게는 지켜보기 너무나 괴로운 구경거리가 될 만한 그런 자연스러움과 감정을 담게 되리라 믿고 싶어졌다. 그러나 그것이 어떤 효과를 내든, 그녀는 바로 그날 다시 한번 그 정면을 견뎌 내야 했다.\n\n세 첫 막의 첫 정식 연습이 저녁에 열리기로 분명히 정해졌다. 그랜트 부인과 크로퍼드 남매는 그 일로 만찬 뒤 되도록 빨리 돌아오기로 약속돼 있었고, 관계된 이는 저마다 열띤 마음으로 그때를 기다렸다. 이 일로 명랑함이 두루 번지는 듯했다. 톰은 이렇게 끝을 향해 나아가는 것을 즐겼고, 에드먼드는 아침 연습으로 기운이 났으며, 자잘한 짜증거리는 곳곳에서 다 씻겨 나간 듯했다. 다들 팔팔하고 안달이 났다. 부인들이 이내 자리를 옮겼고 신사들이 곧 뒤따랐으니, 버트럼 부인과 노리스 부인과 줄리아만 빼고 모두가 이른 시각에 극장에 모여, 아직 다 안 된 상태나마 되는 대로 불을 밝혀 두고, 그랜트 부인과 크로퍼드 남매가 오기만을 기다려 시작하려 하고 있었다.\n\n크로퍼드 남매는 오래 기다릴 것 없었으나, 그랜트 부인은 오지 않았다. 그녀는 올 수 없었다. 그랜트 박사가, 그 어여쁜 처제에게는 좀처럼 믿기지 않는 몸살을 핑계로, 제 아내를 내줄 수 없다는 것이었다.\n\n“그랜트 박사가 편찮으시대요.” 그녀가 짐짓 근엄한 척 말했다. “오늘 꿩고기를 한 점도 안 드신 뒤로 죽 편찮으시다지 뭐예요. 질기다 싶어 접시를 물리시더니, 그 뒤로 죽 앓고 계신답니다.”\n\n이 무슨 낭패인가! 그랜트 부인의 불참은 정말이지 서글픈 일이었다. 그 상냥한 태도와 명랑한 어울림이 그녀를 늘 그들 틈에서 값진 사람으로 만들었으나, 이제 그녀는 없어서는 안 될 사람이었다. 그녀 없이는 연기할 수도, 만족스레 연습할 수도 없었다. 온 저녁의 즐거움이 무너졌다. 어찌해야 할까? 소작인 역인 톰은 절망에 빠졌다. 어리둥절한 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 몇몇 눈길이 패니 쪽으로 돌려지기 시작했고, 한둘의 목소리가 이렇게 말했다. “프라이스 양이 그 배역을 읽어 주기만 한다면.” 그녀는 이내 온갖 간청에 둘러싸였다. 다들 그것을 청했고, 에드먼드마저 이렇게 말했다. “해 다오, 패니, 정 몹시 싫은 게 아니라면.”\n\n그러나 패니는 여전히 뒷걸음질쳤다. 그녀는 그런 생각을 견딜 수 없었다. 어째서 크로퍼드 양한텐 청하지 않는 걸까? 아니, 애초에 어째서 연습을 지켜보고 있을 게 아니라, 저로선 가장 안전하다 느꼈던 대로 제 방으로 가 있지 않았던가? 그것이 자기를 애태우고 괴롭게 하리라는 걸 알았으면서, 멀리 떨어져 있는 게 제 도리임을 알았으면서. 그녀는 마땅한 벌을 받은 셈이었다.\n\n“그저 그 배역을 읽어 주기만 하면 돼요.” 헨리 크로퍼드가 새삼 간청하며 말했다.\n\n“그리고 나는 그 애가 한 마디 한 마디 다 외운다고 믿어요.” 마리아가 덧붙였다. “며칠 전에도 그랜트 부인이 스무 군데나 틀린 걸 바로잡아 줬거든요. 패니, 넌 그 배역 알고 있을 거야, 틀림없이.”\n\n패니는 모른다고 할 수 없었다. 그리고 다들 끈덕지게 조르고, 에드먼드가 제 바람을 되풀이하며, 게다가 그녀의 착한 성품에 애틋하게 기대는 듯한 눈빛까지 하니, 그녀는 굽힐 수밖에 없었다. 최선을 다하기로 했다. 다들 흡족해했고, 그녀는 더없이 두방망이질하는 가슴의 떨림에 내맡겨진 채, 다른 이들이 시작할 채비를 하는 것을 지켜보았다.\n\n그들은 정말로 시작했다. 그리고 저희 소란에 너무 열중한 나머지 집 다른 편에서 나는 심상찮은 소리를 알아채지 못한 채 얼마쯤 나아갔는데, 그때 방문이 벌컥 열리며 줄리아가 나타나, 얼굴이 온통 새파랗게 질린 채 외쳤다. “아버지가 오셨어! 지금 이 순간 현관에 계셔.”\n\n# 제19장\n\n그 무리의 경악을 어찌 말로 그리랴? 대다수에게 그것은 순전한 공포의 순간이었다. 토머스 경이 집에 오시다니! 다들 그 사실을 순식간에 확신했다. 잘못 안 것이거나 착각이려니 하는 희망은 어디에도 품어지지 않았다. 줄리아의 낯빛이 그 사실의 증거였으니 반박할 여지가 없었다. 그리고 처음의 소스라침과 탄성이 지난 뒤, 반 분 동안 아무도 한마디 말이 없었다. 저마다 낯빛이 달라진 채 다른 누군가를 바라보았고, 거의 저마다 그것을 더없이 달갑잖고, 더없이 때 나쁘고, 더없이 소름 끼치는 일격으로 느끼고 있었다! 예이츠 씨야 그것을 그저 그날 저녁을 망치는 성가신 훼방쯤으로 여겼을 테고, 러시워스 씨야 그것을 축복으로 여겼을지 모른다. 그러나 그 밖의 모든 가슴은 어느 정도의 자책이나 막연한 불안에 짓눌려 가라앉고 있었고, 그 밖의 모든 가슴이 이렇게 뇌고 있었다. “우리는 어찌 되는 걸까? 이제 어떡하지?” 그것은 끔찍한 정적이었다. 그리고 문 열리는 소리며 지나가는 발소리가 그 사실을 뒷받침하는 것이, 모든 귀에 끔찍했다.\n\n다시 움직이고 말을 꺼낸 것은 줄리아가 처음이었다. 시샘과 쓰라림은 잦아든 참이었다. 이기심은 다 함께 걸린 이 판국에 묻혀 버렸다. 그러나 그녀가 나타난 그 순간, 프레더릭은 아가사의 넋두리에 헌신 어린 눈빛으로 귀 기울이며 그 손을 제 가슴에 꼭 눌러 대고 있었다. 그리고 그녀가 이것을 알아채고, 제 말이 안긴 충격에도 불구하고 그가 여전히 제자리를 지키며 언니의 손을 붙들고 있음을 보게 되자, 그녀의 상처 입은 마음이 다시 서러움으로 부풀어 올랐고, 아까 창백했던 만큼이나 새빨개진 얼굴로 방을 나서며 이렇게 말했다. “나야 그 앞에 나서기가 겁날 것 없지.”\n\n그녀가 나가자 나머지가 정신을 차렸다. 그리고 바로 그 순간 두 오라비가 앞으로 나섰으니, 무언가 해야 함을 느낀 것이다. 둘 사이에 아주 몇 마디면 충분했다. 이 판국에는 의견이 갈릴 여지가 없었다. 당장 응접실로 가야 했다. 마리아도 같은 뜻으로 그들에 어울렸으니, 그때만은 셋 가운데 가장 담대했다. 줄리아를 몰아낸 바로 그 사정이 그녀에게는 더없이 달콤한 버팀목이었기 때문이다. 헨리 크로퍼드가 그런 순간에, 그토록 유별난 증표이자 중대한 순간에 제 손을 붙들어 준 것은, 오랜 세월의 의심과 근심에 맞먹을 값어치가 있었다. 그녀는 그것을 더없이 진지한 결심의 징표로 반겼고, 아버지를 마주할 배포마저 갖추었다. 그들은 걸어 나갔으니, 러시워스 씨가 “저도 갈까요? 저도 가는 게 낫지 않을까요? 저도 가는 게 옳지 않겠어요?” 하고 거듭 묻는 것에는 도무지 아랑곳없었다. 그러나 그들이 문을 나서기 무섭게 헨리 크로퍼드가 그 애타는 물음에 답을 해 주었으니, 지체 없이 토머스 경께 문안 인사를 드리라고 온갖 말로 부추기며, 그를 흐뭇하게 서둘러 다른 이들 뒤로 보냈다.\n\n패니는 크로퍼드 남매와 예이츠 씨만 남은 자리에 남겨졌다. 사촌들은 그녀를 아주 잊고 있었다. 그리고 토머스 경의 애정에 제가 무슨 권리가 있으랴 하는 그녀 자신의 생각은 너무나 겸손해, 자기를 그의 자식들과 한데 묶을 엄두조차 나지 않았던 터라, 그녀는 뒤에 남아 잠깐 숨 돌릴 짬을 얻는 것이 반가웠다. 그녀의 동요와 불안은 나머지 사람들이 겪는 그 모든 것을 넘어섰으니, 죄 없음으로도 괴로움을 면치 못하는 그 천성 탓이었다. 그녀는 거의 까무러칠 지경이었다. 삼촌에 대한 예전의 그 몸에 밴 두려움이 죄다 되살아났고, 그와 더불어 그와 이 무리 거의 모두에 대한 연민이, 그의 눈앞에 드러날 사태를 두고 밀려들었으며, 에드먼드를 걱정하는 마음은 이루 말할 수 없었다. 그녀는 자리 하나를 찾아, 심하게 떨며 이 온갖 무서운 생각을 견디고 있었고, 그동안 다른 세 사람은 이제 아무 거리낌 없이 짜증스러운 감정을 쏟아 내며, 이토록 뜻밖에 이르디이른 도착을 더없이 고약한 일이라 한탄하고, 딱한 토머스 경이 항해에 곱절은 더 걸렸더라면, 아니면 여태 앤티가에 계셨더라면 하고 무자비하게 바랐다.\n\n크로퍼드 남매는 예이츠 씨보다 이 일에 더 열을 올렸으니, 그 집안을 더 잘 아는 데다 반드시 뒤따를 낭패를 더 또렷이 헤아렸기 때문이다. 그들에게 연극의 파탄은 정해진 일이었다. 이 계획의 완전한 무너짐이 코앞에 닥쳤음을 그들은 느꼈다. 반면 예이츠 씨는 그것을 그저 한때의 훼방, 그날 저녁의 낭패쯤으로 여겨, 토머스 경을 맞는 부산함이 끝나고 그가 한가로이 즐길 만해지면 차를 마신 뒤 연습을 다시 시작할 수 있으리라는 가능성마저 내비쳤다. 크로퍼드 남매는 그 생각을 비웃었다. 그리고 조용히 걸어서 집으로 돌아가 그 식구끼리 있게 두는 것이 마땅하다고 이내 뜻을 모은 뒤, 예이츠 씨더러 저희와 함께 가서 목사관에서 저녁을 보내자고 청했다. 그러나 예이츠 씨는 부모의 도리며 집안의 사사로운 정 따위를 대수로이 여기는 이들과 어울려 본 적이 없던지라, 그런 것이 무슨 필요가 있는지 도무지 알아채지 못했다. 그리하여 그는 그들에게 고맙다면서 이렇게 말했다. “나야 그 어른이 오셨으니 이왕이면 여기 남아 그분께 번듯하게 문안이나 드리는 게 낫겠어요. 게다가 다들 달아나 버리면 남은 사람들한테 도리가 아니지 싶고요.”\n\n이 문제가 매듭지어질 무렵 패니는 막 정신을 추스르기 시작했고, 더 오래 뒤에 남아 있다간 무례해 보일지 모른다고 느끼던 참이었다. 그리하여 남매의 사과를 전할 소임을 맡은 채, 그녀는 삼촌 앞에 나서는 그 무서운 도리를 다하러 몸소 방을 나서며, 그들이 떠날 채비를 하는 것을 보았다.\n\n그녀는 너무나 이르게도 응접실 문 앞에 다다랐다. 그리고 결코 오지 않을 것을—어느 문 바깥에서도 여태 그녀에게 준 적 없는 용기를—잠시 기다린 뒤, 자포자기하는 심정으로 자물쇠를 돌리니, 응접실의 불빛과 한자리에 모인 온 식구가 그녀 앞에 있었다. 들어서는데 제 이름이 귀에 들어왔다. 마침 그때 토머스 경이 둘레를 둘러보며 이렇게 말하고 있었다. “그런데 패니는 어디 있느냐? 어째서 우리 어린 패니가 안 보이지?”—그러고는 그녀를 알아보자, 그녀를 놀라게 하고 가슴에 사무치게 하는 다정함으로 다가와, 그녀를 사랑하는 패니라 부르며 정겹게 입 맞추고, 어쩜 이리 많이 컸느냐고 뚜렷한 기쁨으로 눈여겨보았다! 패니는 어찌 느껴야 할지, 어디를 보아야 할지 몰랐다. 그녀는 온통 짓눌린 심정이었다. 그가 평생 그녀에게 그토록 다정한, 그토록 유난히 다정한 적이 없었다. 그의 태도는 달라진 듯했고, 목소리는 기쁨의 설렘으로 빨랐으며, 그 위엄에서 두렵던 모든 것이 다정함에 녹아 사라진 듯했다. 그는 그녀를 불빛 가까이로 이끌어 다시 바라보며—그녀의 건강을 각별히 물었다가, 이내 스스로를 바로잡아, 그럴 것 없다고, 그녀의 안색이 그 점을 넉넉히 말해 준다고 하였다. 앞서 창백했던 그녀의 얼굴에 이제 고운 홍조가 돌았으니, 그녀가 건강도 아름다움도 똑같이 나아졌다는 그의 믿음이 옳았다. 이어 그는 그녀의 식구를, 특히 윌리엄을 물었다. 그리고 그의 다정함이 통틀어 어찌나 크던지, 그녀는 그를 그토록 조금밖에 사랑하지 않고 그의 귀환을 불행으로 여긴 저 자신을 나무라게 되었다. 그리고 용기를 내어 그의 얼굴로 눈을 들어 올렸을 때, 그가 부쩍 여위고, 피로와 무더운 날씨에 그을리고 초췌하고 지친 낯빛임을 보게 되자, 온갖 다정한 감정이 더해졌고, 짐작지 못한 얼마나 많은 짜증거리가 곧 그에게 터져 나오려 하는지 헤아리니 그녀는 비참했다.\n\n토머스 경은 과연 그 무리의 활기였으니, 그의 권유에 다들 이제 난롯가에 둘러앉았다. 이야기하는 이가 될 자격이야 그에게 가장 있었다. 그리고 그토록 오래 떨어져 있다가 다시 제집에, 제 식구 한복판에 있게 된 그 감격이, 그를 여느 때와 아주 다르게 말 많고 수다스럽게 만들었다. 그는 제 항해에 대해 무엇이든 알려 줄 태세였고, 두 아들의 어떤 물음에도 그것이 채 나오기도 전에 답할 태세였다. 앤티가에서의 그의 볼일은 근래 순조롭게 척척 진행된 터라, 그는 우편선을 기다리는 대신 사사로운 배편으로 리버풀까지 건너올 기회를 얻어 곧장 거기서 온 것이었다. 그리고 그가 겪은 일과 사건, 도착과 출발의 자잘한 사연이 죄다 냉큼냉큼 전해졌으니, 그는 버트럼 부인 곁에 앉아 둘레의 얼굴들을 진심 어린 흡족함으로 바라보며—다만 한 번 넘게 스스로 말을 끊고, 이렇게 뜻밖에 들이닥쳤는데도 다들 집에 있는 걸 보게 된 제 복을 두고 한마디씩 했다—바라 마지않았으나 감히 믿지는 못했던 그대로, 다들 꼭 한자리에 모여 있었으니 말이다. 러시워스 씨도 잊히지 않았다. 더없이 다정한 맞이와 뜨거운 악수가 이미 그를 맞았고, 이제 그는 각별한 마음씀으로 맨스필드와 가장 가까이 얽힌 이들 축에 끼었다. 러시워스 씨의 겉모습에는 언짢을 데가 없었고, 토머스 경은 벌써 그가 마음에 들기 시작했다.\n\n그 자리의 누구도 그의 아내만큼 끊김 없고 티 없는 즐거움으로 그의 말에 귀 기울이지 않았으니, 그녀는 그를 보게 되어 정말이지 더없이 행복했고, 그의 갑작스러운 도착에 감정이 어찌나 달아올랐던지 지난 이십 년 새 어느 때보다도 설렘에 가까웠다. 그녀는 몇 분 동안 거의 가슴이 두근거릴 지경이었고, 여전히 그토록 뚜렷이 생기가 돌아, 일감을 밀어 두고, 곁에서 퍼그를 옮기고, 온 주의와 소파의 나머지 자리를 죄다 남편에게 내주었다. 그녀의 즐거움을 흐릴 만한 근심이라곤 누구를 위해서도 없었다. 그가 없는 동안 그녀 자신의 시간은 나무랄 데 없이 보내진 터였다. 그녀는 양탄자 일감을 잔뜩 했고, 술 장식도 몇 야드나 떴다. 그러니 그녀는 젊은이들 모두의 바른 처신과 알찬 소일거리에 대해서도 제 것 못지않게 선뜻 장담했을 것이다. 그를 다시 보고 그가 이야기하는 것을 듣고, 그 이야기에 귀가 즐겁고 온 이해가 가득 차는 것이 그녀에게는 어찌나 흐뭇하던지, 그녀는 자기가 그를 얼마나 지독히 그리워했음이 틀림없는지, 오랜 이별이라면 자기가 얼마나 견디지 못했을지를 유난히 절감하기 시작했다.\n\n노리스 부인은 그 행복으로 말하면 결코 그 아우에 견줄 바가 못 되었다. 집의 지금 형편이 알려지면 토머스 경이 못마땅해하시리라는 두려움에 그녀가 시달렸다는 것이 아니다. 그녀의 판단이 어찌나 눈멀어 있었던지, 형부가 들어설 때 러시워스 씨의 분홍 새틴 망토를 본능적인 조심성으로 잽싸게 치워 버린 것 말고는, 딱히 놀란 기색이라곤 보였다 할 게 거의 없었다. 다만 그녀는 그가 돌아온 그 방식에 속이 상했다. 그것이 그녀에게 할 일을 하나도 남겨 주지 않았던 것이다. 방 밖으로 불려 나가 그를 먼저 뵙고 그 반가운 소식을 온 집 안에 퍼뜨리는 대신, 토머스 경은—아마도 제 아내와 자식들의 신경을 어지간히 믿었던지—집사 말고는 아무 심부름꾼도 찾지 않고, 거의 곧바로 그를 뒤따라 응접실로 들어와 버린 것이다. 노리스 부인은, 그의 도착이든 그의 죽음이든 무엇이 알려질 일이건 간에 늘 저한테 맡겨졌던 그 소임을 빼앗긴 기분이었다. 그래서 이제 부산 떨 거리라곤 아무것도 없이 부산을 떨어 보려 애쓰고, 평온과 침묵 말고는 아무것도 필요치 않은 자리에서 대단한 사람이 되어 보려 안간힘을 썼다. 토머스 경이 뭘 좀 들겠다고만 했어도, 그녀는 성가신 지시를 잔뜩 안고 가정부에게 갔을 것이요, 어서 서두르라는 분부로 하인들을 들볶았을 것이다. 그러나 토머스 경은 어떤 만찬도 딱 잘라 마다했다. 아무것도 안 들겠다고, 차가 나올 때까지 아무것도—차라리 차를 기다리겠노라 했다. 그래도 노리스 부인은 틈틈이 뭔가 다른 것을 자꾸 권했다. 그리고 그가 잉글랜드로 건너오던 이야기의 가장 마음 졸이는 대목, 프랑스 사략선의 위협이 극에 달한 대목에서, 그녀는 수프를 권하며 그의 이야기를 뚫고 들어왔다. “그러지 마시고, 토머스 경, 차보다야 수프 한 대접이 경께 훨씬 나을 거예요. 수프 한 대접 드셔 보세요.”\n\n토머스 경은 성을 낼 수가 없었다. “여전히 모두의 편안함을 그리 걱정하시는구려, 노리스 부인.” 이것이 그의 대답이었다. “하지만 정말이지 나는 차 말고는 아무것도 안 드는 게 좋겠소.”\n\n“그럼, 버트럼 부인, 얼른 차를 좀 청하시지요. 배들리를 좀 재촉하시고요. 오늘 밤 그 사람 굼떠 보이네요.” 그녀는 이 대목을 관철했고, 토머스 경의 이야기가 이어졌다.\n\n마침내 말이 잠깐 끊겼다. 그가 당장 전할 이야기는 바닥났고, 이제 그 사랑하는 무리를 이 사람 저 사람 즐거이 둘러보는 것만으로도 넉넉한 듯했다. 그러나 그 침묵은 길지 않았다. 들뜬 기분에 버트럼 부인이 말이 많아진 것이다. 그리고 그녀가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었을 때 그 자식들의 심경이 어떠했으랴. “요즘 젊은것들이 어떻게 지내는 줄 아세요, 여보? 그 애들이 연기를 하고 있었답니다. 온 집안이 연기로 들썩였어요.”\n\n“그래? 그럼 무슨 연기를 했소?”\n\n“아유! 그건 그 애들이 다 말씀드릴 거예요.”\n\n“그 ‘다’야 곧 말씀드릴 수 있죠.” 톰이 짐짓 태연한 척 서둘러 외쳤다. “하지만 지금 그걸로 아버지를 지루하게 해 드릴 것까진 없어요. 내일이면 실컷 들으실 테니까요, 아버지. 저희는 그저 뭐라도 좀 하면서 어머니를 즐겁게 해 드려 볼까 하고, 바로 지난 한 주 새에, 장면 몇 개를 꾸며 봤을 뿐이에요, 그야말로 사소한 거죠. 시월이 시작될 무렵부터 비가 어찌나 쉴 새 없이 쏟아지던지, 며칠씩 집에만 갇혀 지내다시피 했거든요. 저는 3일 이후로 총 한번 제대로 못 들었어요. 첫 사흘은 그런대로 사냥이 됐는데, 그 뒤론 뭘 해 볼 엄두도 못 냈고요. 첫날 저는 맨스필드 숲을 돌고 에드먼드는 이스턴 너머 잡목림을 맡아서, 둘이 여섯 쌍을 잡아 왔는데, 저마다 그 여섯 배는 잡을 수 있었지만, 아버지 꿩을 아버지가 바라시는 만큼이나 아낀답니다, 정말이에요. 아버지 숲이 예전보다 조금이라도 덜 찼다고는 여기지 않으실 거예요. 저는 평생 올해처럼 맨스필드 숲이 꿩으로 그득한 걸 본 적이 없어요. 조만간 아버지도 몸소 거기서 하루 사냥을 즐기시면 좋겠어요.”\n\n이번만은 위험이 지나갔고, 패니의 메스꺼운 심정도 가라앉았다. 그러나 이내 차가 들어오고, 토머스 경이 일어서며 사랑하는 제 방을 잠깐 들여다보지 않고는 더는 집에 있을 수가 없겠다고 하자, 온갖 동요가 되살아났다. 그가 거기서 마주할 그 변화에 대비시킬 어떤 말도 건네기 전에 그는 가 버렸고, 그가 사라진 뒤로 불안한 정적이 흘렀다. 먼저 입을 연 것은 에드먼드였다—\n\n“무슨 수를 내야 해.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“이제 손님들 생각을 해야지.” 마리아가 말했는데, 여전히 제 손이 헨리 크로퍼드의 가슴에 눌려 있는 느낌에, 다른 것에는 도무지 마음이 없었다. “크로퍼드 양은 어디 두고 왔니, 패니?”\n\n패니는 그들이 떠난 것을 이르고, 그들의 전갈을 전했다.\n\n“그럼 딱한 예이츠가 혼자겠구나.” 톰이 외쳤다. “내가 가서 데려와야지. 이 일이 죄다 드러날 때 그 사람이 그리 나쁜 조력자는 아닐 테니.”\n\n그는 극장으로 갔고, 마침 제 아버지와 제 벗의 첫 만남을 지켜보기 딱 알맞은 때에 다다랐다. 토머스 경은 제 방에 촛불이 켜져 있는 것을 보고 자못 놀란 참이었다. 그리고 방을 둘러보다, 근래 누가 지낸 다른 흔적들과 세간의 온통 어수선한 기운을 보았다. 당구실 문 앞에서 책장이 치워진 것이 유난히 그의 눈에 띄었으나, 이 모든 것에 놀랄 겨를도 채 없이, 당구실 쪽에서 그를 한층 더 놀라게 할 소리가 들려왔다. 누군가 거기서 아주 큰 소리로 지껄이고 있었다. 그는 그 목소리를 몰랐다—지껄인다기보다는—거의 고래고래 외치는 것이었다. 그는 지금 곧바로 이야기를 나눌 방도가 생긴 것을 그 순간 반기며 그 문으로 다가갔고, 문을 여니 어느 극장 무대 위에 서 있는 자신을 발견했다. 그리고 그 앞에는 열변을 토하는 젊은이 하나가, 금방이라도 그를 뒤로 넘어뜨릴 듯이 서 있었다. 예이츠가 토머스 경을 알아채고, 아마 제 온 연습을 통틀어 가장 멋진 소스라침을 내보인 바로 그 순간, 톰 버트럼이 방 저쪽 끝으로 들어섰다. 그리고 그는 낯빛을 지키기가 그토록 어려운 적이 없었다. 어느 무대에든 처음 오르는 이 순간, 아버지의 근엄하고 아연한 낯빛과, 격정에 찬 빌덴하임 남작이 토머스 버트럼 경께 절하고 사과하는 점잖고 태연한 예이츠 씨로 차츰 탈바꿈해 가는 꼴은, 그 무엇을 준다 해도 놓치고 싶지 않을 그런 구경거리, 그런 진짜 연기 한 판이었다. 그것은 아마 그 무대에서의 마지막—십중팔구—마지막 장면일 터였다. 그러나 이보다 더 멋진 장면은 없으리라고 그는 확신했다. 극장은 더없는 요란함 속에 문을 닫게 될 것이었다.\n\n그러나 어떤 유쾌한 상상에 잠겨 있을 겨를은 거의 없었다. 그도 앞으로 나서서 소개를 거들어야 했고, 온갖 어색한 심경으로 그는 최선을 다했다. 토머스 경은 제 사람됨에 걸맞은 온갖 다정한 겉모습으로 예이츠 씨를 맞았으나, 이 사귐이 필요하다는 것도, 그것이 시작된 방식도 실은 조금도 반갑지 않았다. 예이츠 씨의 집안과 인연은 그가 익히 아는 바라, 아들의 그 백 명이나 되는 ‘각별한 벗’ 가운데 또 하나로 그를 ‘각별한 벗’이라며 소개하는 것이 여간 달갑지 않았다. 그리고 제집에서 이렇게 어리둥절해진 채, 연극이라는 시답잖은 것 한복판에서 우스꽝스러운 구경거리의 한 부분이 되고, 그토록 고약한 순간에 못마땅해할 게 뻔한 젊은이의 사귐을 받아들이도록 내몰린 것에 화를 내지 않으려면—게다가 처음 오 분 새 그 젊은이의 태연한 무심함과 청산유수 같은 말솜씨는 마치 그가 둘 중 더 제집처럼 편안한 사람인 양 보이게 했으니—다시 집에 있다는 그 온갖 행복과, 그것이 안겨 줄 수 있는 온갖 인내가 죄다 필요했다.\n\n톰은 아버지의 속내를 알아챘고, 아버지가 늘 이렇듯 그것을 어지간히만 내비칠 마음이기를 진심으로 바라며, 여태 그 어느 때보다 또렷이 깨닫기 시작했다. 성이 날 만한 구석이 얼마쯤 있을 수 있음을, 아버지가 방의 천장과 회벽 장식 쪽으로 던진 눈길에 얼마쯤 까닭이 있을 수 있음을, 그리고 아버지가 당구대의 운명을 나직하고 근엄하게 물었을 때 그것이 결코 얼마든지 봐줄 만한 궁금함을 넘어선 게 아님을 말이다. 이런 언짢은 심경이 양쪽에 오가는 데는 몇 분이면 넉넉했다. 그리고 토머스 경이, 이 마련이 얼마나 흡족하냐는 예이츠 씨의 들뜬 호소에 답해 차분한 인정의 말을 몇 마디 건네는 데까지 애쓴 뒤, 세 신사는 함께 응접실로 돌아왔으니, 토머스 경은 한층 더 근엄해진 채였고 그것은 모두의 눈에 띄지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n“자네 극장에 다녀왔네.” 그가 자리에 앉으며 차분히 말했다. “거기 있는 나 자신을 뜻밖에도 발견했지 뭔가. 내 방과 그토록 가까이 붙어 있으니—하기야 어느 모로 보나 정말이지 나를 깜짝 놀라게 했네, 자네들 연기가 그토록 진지한 꼴을 갖췄으리라곤 손톱만큼도 짐작 못 했으니. 그래도 촛불 아래 내가 판단할 수 있는 한, 말끔한 솜씨더군. 내 벗 크리스토퍼 잭슨의 이름값을 하는 일이고.” 그러고서 그는 화제를 돌려, 좀 더 차분한 빛깔의 집안일을 두고 편안히 커피를 홀짝이려 했다. 그러나 예이츠 씨는 토머스 경의 속뜻을 알아챌 눈치도, 저 자신은 되도록 나서지 않고 다른 이들 틈에 섞이면서 그가 이야기를 이끌게 놔둘 만한 삼감이나 조심이나 분별도 없던지라, 그를 자꾸만 극장 이야기에 붙들어 두고, 그것에 얽힌 물음과 소감으로 그를 들볶다가, 끝내는 에클스포드에서 겪은 제 낭패의 온 사연을 그에게 다 들려주고야 말았다. 토머스 경은 더없이 정중하게 귀 기울였으나, 그 이야기의 처음부터 끝까지, 제 예의 관념을 거스르고 예이츠 씨의 사고방식에 대한 제 나쁜 인상을 굳히는 대목이 숱하게 있음을 알았다. 그리고 그것이 끝났을 때, 그가 건넬 수 있는 공감의 표시란 가벼운 목례가 전하는 것 말고는 없었다.\n\n“이게 사실 저희 연기의 발단이었어요.” 톰이 잠깐 생각한 뒤 말했다. “제 벗 예이츠가 에클스포드에서 그 열병을 옮겨 왔고, 그게 번진 거죠—그런 건 늘 그렇게 번지잖아요, 아버지—아마 아버지께서 예전에 저희한테 그런 걸 그토록 자주 부추기셨던 탓에 더 빨리 번졌을 테고요. 마치 옛 땅을 다시 밟는 것 같았거든요.”\n\n예이츠 씨는 되도록 빨리 벗에게서 그 화제를 넘겨받아, 저희가 무엇을 했고 무엇을 하고 있는지 곧바로 토머스 경에게 늘어놓았다. 저희 구상이 차츰 커진 것이며, 처음의 어려움이 흐뭇하게 매듭지어진 것이며, 지금 일이 잘 풀려 가는 형편을 이야기하는데, 어찌나 눈먼 흥에 겨웠던지, 앉아 있던 제 벗 여럿의 불편한 몸놀림이며 달라진 낯빛이며 안절부절못하는 몸짓이며 편찮은 헛기침을 도무지 알아채지 못했을 뿐 아니라, 정작 제 눈이 박힌 그 얼굴의 표정마저—토머스 경이 제 딸들과 에드먼드를, 특히 에드먼드를 캐묻듯 진지하게 바라보며 그 검은 눈썹을 찌푸리는 것을, 그리고 에드먼드가 가슴으로 절감한 어떤 말을, 어떤 간언을, 어떤 나무람을 건네는 것을—보지 못하게 했다. 그것을 못지않게 예리하게 느낀 것은 패니였으니, 그녀는 이모가 앉은 소파 끄트머리 뒤로 제 의자를 물려, 저 자신은 눈에 안 띄게 가린 채, 눈앞에 벌어지는 그 모든 것을 보고 있었다. 아버지가 에드먼드에게 던지는 그런 나무람의 눈빛을 그녀는 결코 지켜보게 되리라 예상치 못했을 것이다. 그리고 그것이 얼마쯤이라도 마땅한 것임을 느끼는 것은 정말이지 더한 괴로움이었다. 토머스 경의 눈빛은 이렇게 말하고 있었다. “에드먼드야, 나는 네 판단을 믿었다. 대체 무슨 일을 벌인 게냐?” 그녀는 마음속으로 삼촌 앞에 무릎 꿇었고, 이렇게 외치고 싶어 가슴이 부풀었다. “아, 그이한텐 안 돼요! 다른 모두한텐 그리 보셔도, 그이한텐 안 돼요!”\n\n예이츠 씨는 여전히 이야기하고 있었다. “사실대로 말씀드리면, 토머스 경, 오늘 저녁 오셨을 때 저희는 연습 한복판이었어요. 첫 세 막을 죽 해 나가던 참이었는데, 대체로 그리 나쁘지 않았지요. 이제 크로퍼드 남매가 집으로 가 버려 저희 무리가 흩어진 터라 오늘 밤은 더 못 하겠지만, 내일 저녁 왕림해 주시는 영광을 베풀어 주신다면, 저는 그 결과가 겁나지 않아요. 저희야 풋내기 연기자들이니, 부디 너그러이 봐주십사 청합니다, 아시겠지요. 부디 너그러이 봐주십사 청해요.”\n\n“너그러이 봐주겠네.” 토머스 경이 근엄하게 대답했다. “하지만 더 연습은 없이 말일세.” 그러고는 누그러지는 미소로 덧붙였다. “나는 행복하고 너그럽게 지내려고 집에 온 거라네.” 그런 다음 나머지 사람들 아무에게나, 아니 다 함께 향해 돌아서며, 그는 차분히 말했다. “맨스필드에서 온 지난 편지들에 크로퍼드 남매 이야기가 있더구나. 너희 보기에 사귈 만한 이들이더냐?”\n\n냉큼 대답할 이는 톰뿐이었으나, 그는 그 남매 어느 쪽에도 딱히 각별한 마음이 없고, 사랑에서든 연기에서든 시샘이 없던지라, 둘 다에 대해 아주 번듯하게 말할 수 있었다. “크로퍼드 씨는 더없이 유쾌하고 신사다운 사람이고, 그 누이는 사랑스럽고 예쁘고 우아하고 발랄한 아가씨입니다.”\n\n러시워스 씨는 더는 잠자코 있을 수 없었다. “뭐, 그 사람이 처지를 봐서 신사답지 않다는 건 아니지만, 아버님께 말씀드리는 게 좋겠어요, 그 사람 키가 오 피트 팔 인치도 안 된다고요, 안 그러면 아버님이 훤칠한 사내를 기대하실 테니까요.”\n\n토머스 경은 이 말을 잘 알아듣지 못하고, 다소 놀란 눈으로 말한 이를 바라보았다.\n\n“제 생각을 굳이 말씀드려야 한다면,” 러시워스 씨가 말을 이었다. “제 소견으론 늘 연습만 하고 있는 게 아주 언짢은 노릇이에요. 좋은 것도 너무 지나치니까요. 저는 처음만큼 연기가 좋지가 않아요. 이렇게 우리끼리 편안히 앉아 아무것도 안 하는 게 훨씬 나은 소일이라고 봐요.”\n\n토머스 경은 다시 그를 바라보더니, 흡족한 미소로 대답했다. “이 일에서 우리 생각이 그토록 같다니 기쁘네. 참으로 흐뭇한 일이야. 내가 조심스럽고 눈이 밝아, 내 자식들이 못 느끼는 여러 거리낌을 느끼는 것이야 지극히 당연한 노릇이지. 마찬가지로 집안의 평온을, 시끄러운 즐거움을 물리치는 가정을 아끼는 내 마음이 그 애들 것보다 훨씬 큰 것도 당연하고. 하지만 자네 나이에 이 모든 걸 느낀다는 건 자네 자신에게도, 자네와 얽힌 모든 이에게도 더없이 좋은 일이야. 그리고 나는 이토록 무게 있는 한편을 얻는 것이 얼마나 중요한지 잘 안다네.”\n\n토머스 경은 러시워스 씨의 의견을 그 자신이 찾아낼 수 있는 것보다 더 나은 말로 대신 표현해 주려던 것이었다. 그는 러시워스 씨에게서 천재를 기대해선 안 됨을 알고 있었다. 그러나 판단이 바르고 진득한 젊은이로서, 제 말솜씨가 다 담아내지 못할 만큼 좋은 생각을 지녔다 여겨, 그를 아주 높이 사려는 마음이었다. 다른 여럿은 웃음을 참기 어려웠다. 러시워스 씨는 그토록 많은 속뜻을 어찌해야 좋을지 잘 몰랐다. 그러나 토머스 경의 좋은 평가에 정말로 느낀 그대로 더없이 흡족해하는 낯빛을 하고, 거의 아무 말도 안 함으로써, 그는 그 좋은 평가를 조금이라도 더 오래 지키려 제 딴엔 최선을 다했다.\n\n# 제20장\n\n이튿날 아침 에드먼드의 첫째 목표는 아버지를 단둘이 뵙고, 그 연극 소동 전부를 낱낱이 정직하게 아뢰는 것이었다. 자기가 거기 낀 몫은, 그때 좀 더 냉정해진 순간에 제 동기가 그럴 만하다 느껴지는 데까지만 두둔하고, 제 양보가 하도 어중간한 좋은 결과만 가져온 탓에 그 판단이 자못 미심쩍어졌음을 더없이 솔직하게 인정했다. 그는 저 자신을 변호하면서도 남들에 대해서는 매정한 말을 하지 않으려 애썼다. 그러나 그들 가운데 얼마간의 변호나 두둔 없이 그 처신을 입에 올릴 수 있는 이는 오직 한 사람뿐이었다. “저희 모두가 어느 정도는 잘못했어요.” 그가 말했다. “패니만 빼고 저희 하나하나가요. 패니야말로 처음부터 끝까지 옳게 판단한 유일한 사람이고, 한결같았던 유일한 사람이에요. 그 애 마음은 처음부터 끝까지 줄곧 이 일에 반대였어요. 그 앤 아버지께 마땅히 지켜야 할 도리를 한시도 잊지 않았고요. 아버지께서 바라실 만한 모든 것을 패니에게서 보실 거예요.”\n\n토머스 경은 그런 무리 사이에서, 그런 때에 벌어진 그런 계획의 온갖 온당찮음을, 그 아들이 여태 그러리라 짐작한 그대로 절실히 알아보았다. 정말이지 그는 그것을 여러 말로 옮기기엔 너무 크게 느꼈다. 그리하여 에드먼드와 악수를 나눈 뒤, 그 언짢은 인상을 떨쳐 버리고, 저 자신이 얼마나 잊혔던가를 되도록 빨리 잊으려 마음먹었다—그 기억을 강요하는 온갖 것을 집에서 치워 없애고 집을 제 마땅한 꼴로 되돌린 다음에 말이다. 그는 다른 자식들과는 어떤 언쟁도 벌이지 않았다. 캐물어 들어가는 위험을 무릅쓰기보다는, 그들이 제 잘못을 느끼고 있으리라 믿는 편을 택했다. 모든 것을 당장 끝맺고 온갖 채비를 싹 쓸어 없애는 그 나무람이면 충분할 터였다.\n\n그러나 집 안에는, 그저 제 처신을 통해서만 그의 심경을 알게 두어서는 안 될 사람이 하나 있었다. 그는 노리스 부인에게, 그녀의 판단이라면 분명 못마땅해했을 그 일을 막는 데 그녀의 조언이 끼어들었기를 바랐노라고 넌지시 이르지 않을 수 없었다. 젊은것들이야 그 계획을 세우면서 아주 경솔했다. 저희 스스로 더 나은 판단을 내릴 줄 알았어야 마땅했지만, 어리기도 했고, 에드먼드만 빼면 그가 보기에 진득하지 못한 성품들이었다. 그러니 그런 방책과 그런 놀이가 내놓아졌다는 것보다, 그녀가 그들의 그릇된 방책을 순순히 받아들이고 그 위태로운 놀이를 편들어 준 것을 그는 한층 더 놀랍게 여길 수밖에 없었다. 노리스 부인은 조금 당황했고, 평생 그 어느 때만큼이나 말문이 막힐 뻔했다. 토머스 경에게는 그토록 훤히 드러나던 그 온갖 온당찮음을 저는 하나도 못 봤노라 고백하기가 부끄러웠고, 제 영향력이 모자랐다고—제 말이 헛일이었을지 모른다고—인정하려 들지도 않았기 때문이다. 그녀의 유일한 방도는 되도록 빨리 그 화제에서 빠져나와, 토머스 경의 생각의 물길을 좀 더 반가운 쪽으로 돌리는 것이었다. 그녀는 그의 집안의 이익과 안락에 두루 마음 쓴 것을 두고 제 자랑을 넌지시 늘어놓을 거리가 잔뜩 있었고, 부랴부랴 걸어 다니고 제 난롯가를 훌쩍 떠나온 꼴로 내비칠 온갖 수고와 희생이 많았으며, 버트럼 부인과 에드먼드에게 건넨, 사람을 못 미더워하고 알뜰히 굴라는 훌륭한 언질도 낱낱이 늘어놓을 게 많았으니, 그 덕에 늘 여간 큰 절약이 이뤄졌고 몹쓸 하인도 한둘 넘게 들통났다는 것이었다. 그러나 그녀의 가장 큰 힘은 소더턴에 있었다. 그녀의 가장 큰 버팀목이자 자랑은 러시워스가와 인연을 맺어 준 데 있었다. 거기서만은 그녀가 난공불락이었다. 러시워스 씨가 마리아에게 품은 흠모를 결실로 이끈 공을 그녀는 죄다 제 것으로 삼았다. “내가 발 벗고 나서지 않았더라면,” 그녀가 말했다. “기어이 그이 어머니한테 소개받고, 그런 다음 내 아우를 구슬려 먼저 방문하게 만들지 않았더라면, 여기 이렇게 앉아 있는 게 확실한 만큼이나 확실히, 이 일은 아무것도 안 됐을 거예요. 러시워스 씨야 크게 부추겨 줘야 하는 그런 상냥하고 수줍은 젊은이인 데다, 우리가 손 놓고 있었더라면 그이를 낚아채려는 계집애가 얼마든지 있었으니까요. 하지만 나는 온갖 수를 다 썼어요. 내 아우를 설득하겠다고 하늘과 땅이라도 움직일 태세였고, 마침내 정말 설득해 냈지요. 소더턴까지 거리가 얼마나 되는지 아시잖아요. 한겨울인 데다 길은 거의 못 다닐 지경이었지만, 나는 기어이 아우를 설득했다니까요.”\n\n“부인이 버트럼 부인과 그 아이들에게 미치는 힘이 얼마나 큰지, 얼마나 마땅히 큰지 나는 잘 아오. 그래서 더욱 안타깝구려, 그 힘이 그만—”\n\n“아이참, 토머스 경, 그날 길 꼴을 보셨어야 하는데! 물론 말은 네 마리를 매었는데도, 나는 우리가 도무지 그 길을 못 빠져나갈 줄 알았어요. 게다가 딱한 늙은 마부가, 미가엘 축일 무렵부터 내가 줄곧 약을 지어 봐준 그 류머티즘 때문에 마부석에 앉아 있기도 힘든 지경인데도, 그 크나큰 정과 친절로 기어이 우리를 모시겠다지 뭐예요. 나야 끝내 그 사람을 고쳐 주긴 했지만, 겨우내 아주 심했거든요—그런데 하필 그런 날이라, 나는 떠나기 전에 그 사람 방으로 올라가 나서지 말라고 일러 주지 않을 수가 없었어요. 마침 가발을 쓰고 있기에, 내가 그랬죠. ‘마부, 자넨 안 가는 게 훨씬 낫겠네. 마님하고 나는 아주 무사할 테니. 스티븐이 얼마나 진득한지 자네도 알잖나. 그리고 찰스도 이젠 선두 말을 하도 자주 몰아 봐서, 아무 걱정 없을 게 분명하네.’ 하지만 이내 그게 안 되겠다는 걸 알았지요. 그 사람은 갈 작정으로 단단히 벼르고 있었고, 나야 남을 들볶고 참견하는 게 질색이라, 더는 아무 말도 안 했어요. 그래도 덜컹거릴 때마다 그 사람 생각에 내 가슴이 아주 저렸고, 스토크 근처 험한 샛길에 들어서니, 돌바닥 위에 서리며 눈이 쌓여 상상도 못 할 만큼 고약했는데, 나는 그 사람 걱정에 아주 애가 탔지요. 게다가 딱한 말들은 또 어떻고요! 그놈들이 안간힘을 쓰며 끄는 걸 보다니! 내가 늘 말들을 얼마나 딱해하는지 아시잖아요. 그리고 샌드크로프트 언덕 아래에 이르렀을 때, 내가 어쨌게요? 나를 비웃으시겠지만, 나는 내려서 걸어 올라갔어요. 정말 그랬다니까요. 그놈들한테 큰 덜음은 안 됐을지 몰라도, 그래도 얼마간은 됐고, 나는 그 갸륵한 짐승들을 부려 편히 앉아 끌려 올라가는 걸 도무지 견딜 수가 없었거든요. 나는 지독한 감기에 걸렸지만, 그거야 개의치 않았어요. 그 방문에서 내 목적은 이뤄졌으니까요.”\n\n“그 인연을 맺느라 든 어떤 수고에도 우리가 늘 그만한 값어치가 있었다 여기게 되기를 바라오. 러시워스 씨의 태도에 딱히 눈에 띄는 데야 없지만, 나는 어젯밤 그가 한 가지 일에 대해 내비친 듯한 의견이 마음에 들었소. 연기의 소란과 어수선함보다 조용한 가족 모임을 뚜렷이 더 좋아한다는 것 말이오. 바로 바랄 만한 그대로 느끼는 듯하더군.”\n\n“그럼요, 정말이지, 그이를 알면 알수록 더 좋아하시게 될 거예요. 빛나는 사람은 못 되지만, 좋은 자질을 천 가지나 지녔고, 게다가 경을 어찌나 우러러보는지, 다들 그걸 내가 한 일이라 여기는 바람에 나는 아주 놀림을 받는답니다. 며칠 전에도 그랜트 부인이 그랬어요. ‘정말이지, 노리스 부인, 러시워스 씨가 부인 친아들이라 한들 토머스 경을 이보다 더 우러러볼 수는 없겠어요.’”\n\n토머스 경은 그 발뺌에 밀리고 그 아첨에 무장을 빼앗겨 그 대목을 접었다. 그리고 그녀가 사랑하는 이들의 당장의 즐거움이 걸린 자리에서는, 그 다정함이 때로 그 판단을 눌러 버리곤 한다는 확신으로 만족하는 수밖에 없었다.\n\n그에게는 분주한 아침이었다. 그들 누구와 이야기하는 것은 그 아침의 자그마한 한 토막을 차지했을 뿐이다. 그는 제 맨스필드 생활의 그 익은 일들에 죄다 다시 몸담아야 했다. 집사와 마름을 만나고, 살피고 셈하고, 일하는 짬짬이 마구간과 정원, 가장 가까운 농원으로 걸어 나가야 했다. 그러나 부지런하고 빈틈없던지라, 그는 만찬 자리에서 집주인으로 도로 앉기 전에 이 모든 것을 다 했을 뿐 아니라, 목수를 시켜 얼마 전 당구실에 세운 것을 헐게 하고, 무대 화가에게는 이제 적어도 노샘프턴만큼은 멀리 가 있으리라는 흐뭇한 믿음이 정당할 만큼 진작 물러가라 했다. 무대 화가는 떠났으니, 방 하나의 바닥만 망쳐 놓고, 마부의 해면을 죄다 못 쓰게 만들고, 아랫사람 다섯을 게으르고 불만스럽게 만들어 놓은 채였다. 그리고 토머스 경은 하루 이틀이면 지난 일의 온갖 겉 흔적을 다 지워 없애기에 넉넉하리라 바랐으니, 집 안에 있는 「연인들의 맹세」의 제본 안 된 낱권까지 죄다 없애기에 이르러—그는 눈에 띄는 것을 죄다 태우고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n예이츠 씨는 이제 토머스 경의 뜻을 알아차리기 시작했으나, 그 뿌리를 헤아리기는 여전히 까마득했다. 그와 그 벗은 아침의 대부분을 총을 들고 밖에 나가 있었고, 톰은 그 기회를 잡아, 아버지의 별난 성미에 대해 마땅한 사과를 곁들여, 앞으로 어찌 될지를 일러 주었다. 예이츠 씨는 짐작할 만한 그대로 그것을 절실히 느꼈다. 같은 식으로 두 번째 낭패를 보는 것은 여간 혹독한 불운의 사례가 아니었다. 그리고 그 분함이 어찌나 컸던지, 제 벗과 그 벗의 막내 누이에 대한 조심만 아니었더라면, 그는 분명 그 준남작의 처사가 얼마나 어처구니없는지를 두고 그에게 대들어, 좀 더 사리에 맞게 굴도록 따져 댔으리라 믿었다. 그는 맨스필드 숲에 있는 동안, 그리고 집으로 오는 내내 이것을 아주 굳게 믿었다. 그러나 다 함께 한 식탁에 둘러앉으니, 토머스 경에게는 무언가가 있어, 예이츠 씨로 하여금 그가 제 뜻대로 하도록 두고, 대들지 않고 그 어리석음을 스스로 깨닫게 하는 편이 더 슬기롭겠다 여기게 했다. 그는 전에도 언짢은 아버지를 숱하게 봐 왔고, 그들이 안기는 불편에 곧잘 놀라기도 했으나, 평생 통틀어 토머스 경만큼 그토록 알 수 없이 도덕적이고, 그토록 지독하게 폭군 같은 그런 부류의 아버지는 본 적이 없었다. 그는 오직 그 자식들을 봐서나 견딜 만한 사람이었고, 예이츠 씨가 그래도 며칠 더 그 지붕 아래 묵을 작정인 것은 그 어여쁜 딸 줄리아 덕이라 여겨도 좋을 만했다.\n\n저녁은 겉으로는 매끄럽게 흘러갔으나, 거의 모든 마음이 헝클어져 있었다. 그리고 토머스 경이 딸들에게 청한 음악이 참된 화합의 부재를 감추는 데 한몫했다. 마리아는 자못 동요하고 있었다. 크로퍼드가 이제 지체 없이 마음을 밝히는 것이 그녀에게는 더없이 중대한 일이었고, 그 대목을 앞당기는 기미도 없이 하루가 그냥 지나갔다는 것이 그녀를 뒤흔들었다. 그녀는 아침 내내 그를 볼 줄로 기다렸고, 저녁 내내도 여전히 그를 기다렸다. 러시워스 씨는 그 대단한 소식을 안고 아침 일찍 소더턴으로 떠난 터였다. 그리고 그녀는 그가 굳이 다시 돌아올 수고를 덜어 줄 만한 그런 즉각적인 결판을 은근히 바랐던 것이다. 그러나 그들은 목사관에서 아무도, 어느 하나도 보지 못했고, 그랜트 부인이 버트럼 부인에게 보낸 다정한 축하와 안부의 쪽지 말고는 아무 기별도 듣지 못했다. 두 집안이 온전히 갈라진 것은 여러 주 만에 처음이었다. 팔월이 시작된 이래로, 이런저런 식으로든 그들을 한자리에 모으지 않고 스물네 시간이 지난 적은 여태 없었다. 서글프고 애타는 하루였다. 그리고 이튿날은, 화의 종류는 달랐어도, 결코 덜 하지 않았다. 몇 순간의 들뜬 즐거움에 여러 시간의 시린 괴로움이 뒤따랐다. 헨리 크로퍼드가 다시 집에 왔다. 그는 토머스 경께 문안을 드리고 싶어 안달인 그랜트 박사와 함께 걸어 올라왔고, 자못 이른 시각에 둘은 아침식사방으로 안내되었으니, 거기엔 식구 대부분이 있었다. 이내 토머스 경이 나타났고, 마리아는 제가 사랑하는 사내가 아버지에게 소개되는 것을 기쁨과 동요 속에 지켜보았다. 그녀의 심경은 이루 말할 수 없었고, 몇 분 뒤 헨리 크로퍼드—그녀와 톰 사이의 의자에 앉아 있던—가 톰에게 나직한 목소리로, 이 반가운 훼방(토머스 경 쪽으로 정중히 눈길을 던지며)이 지난 뒤에 연극을 다시 할 계획이 있느냐고 묻는 것을 들었을 때에도 그러했다. 만일 그렇다면, 그는 이 무리가 필요로 하는 언제든 맨스필드로 돌아오기를 반드시 지키겠다는 것이었다. 그는 곧장 떠나야 했으니, 지체 없이 배스에서 삼촌을 만나기로 돼 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 만일 「연인들의 맹세」를 다시 할 가망이 조금이라도 있다면, 그는 스스로 단단히 매인 몸으로 여겨, 다른 어떤 볼일도 마다하고, 필요할 때면 언제든 그들에게 오도록 삼촌과 기어이 담판을 짓겠다는 것이었다. 자기가 없어서 연극이 무산돼서는 안 된다는 것이었다.\n\n“배스든 노퍽이든 런던이든 요크든, 내가 어디 있든,” 그가 말했다. “잉글랜드 어느 곳에서든 한 시간 전 기별이면 달려오겠어요.”\n\n그 순간 말해야 할 사람이 그 누이가 아니라 톰이었던 것이 다행이었다. 그는 곧바로 술술 편하게 말할 수 있었다. “가신다니 아쉽네요. 하지만 우리 연극으로 말하면, 그건 다 끝났어요—아주 끝장이 났죠.” (아버지를 뜻있게 바라보며.) “화가는 어제 돌려보냈고, 내일이면 극장이라곤 거의 남지 않을 거예요. 나야 처음부터 그리될 줄 알았죠. 배스에 가기엔 이르네요. 거긴 아무도 없을걸요.”\n\n“우리 삼촌이 늘 가시는 때쯤이라서요.”\n\n“언제 떠날 생각이에요?”\n\n“오늘은 아마 밴버리까지는 갈 수 있겠죠.”\n\n“배스에선 누구 마구간을 쓰세요?” 하는 것이 다음 물음이었다. 그리고 이 곁가지 화제가 오가는 동안, 자존심도 결단력도 모자라지 않던 마리아는, 제 몫의 그것을 그런대로 차분히 마주할 채비를 하고 있었다.\n\n이내 그가 그녀에게로 돌아서서, 앞서 이미 한 말을 상당 부분 되풀이했으니, 다만 좀 더 누그러진 태도와 좀 더 강한 아쉬움의 표현을 곁들였을 뿐이었다. 그러나 그의 표현이며 태도가 무슨 소용이랴? 그는 떠나는 것이고, 제 발로 떠나는 게 아니라면 제 뜻으로 멀리 떨어져 있기로 작정한 것이었으니, 삼촌에게 마땅히 다해야 할 것 말고는 그의 볼일이란 죄다 스스로 지운 것이었기 때문이다. 그는 어쩔 수 없는 노릇이라 말할지 몰라도, 그녀는 그가 얼마나 자유로운 몸인지 알고 있었다. 그토록 제 손을 제 가슴에 눌러 대던 그 손! 이제 그 손도 그 마음도 똑같이 미동도 없이 무심했다! 배포가 그녀를 지탱해 주었으나, 그 마음의 괴로움은 혹독했다. 그의 행동이 뒤엎는 그 말을 듣는 데서 오는 것을 견디거나, 사교의 삼감 아래 제 감정의 소용돌이를 파묻어 두거나 할 일은 오래가지 않았다. 이내 두루 오가는 인사치레가 그의 주의를 그녀에게서 불러냈고, 그때 대놓고 작별 방문임이 드러난 그 방문은 아주 짧은 것이었다. 그는 갔다—마지막으로 그녀의 손을 스치고, 작별의 절을 하고 나니, 그녀는 곧바로 홀로 있음이 저에게 해 줄 수 있는 모든 것을 찾아도 되었다. 헨리 크로퍼드는 갔으니, 집에서 갔고, 그 두 시간도 안 되어 그 교구에서도 떠났다. 그리하여 그의 이기적인 허영이 마리아와 줄리아 버트럼의 마음에 불러일으킨 온갖 희망이 끝났다.\n\n줄리아는 그가 간 것을 기뻐할 수 있었다. 그의 존재가 그녀에게는 밉살스러워지기 시작하던 참이었다. 그리고 마리아가 그를 얻지 못한다면, 그녀는 이제 다른 어떤 복수도 없이 지낼 만큼 마음이 식어 있었다. 그녀는 버림받은 데다 망신까지 더해지기를 바라진 않았다. 헨리 크로퍼드가 가고 나니, 그녀는 언니를 딱해할 수조차 있었다.\n\n좀 더 순수한 마음으로 패니는 그 소식을 기뻐했다. 그녀는 만찬 자리에서 그것을 듣고 축복으로 여겼다. 다른 모든 이는 그것을 아쉬움으로 입에 올렸다. 그리고 그의 장점이, 에드먼드의 너무 편드는 진심 어린 정에서부터, 그저 입에 붙은 대로 지껄이는 그 어머니의 무심함에 이르기까지, 저마다 다른 정도의 감정으로 기려졌다. 노리스 부인은 둘레를 두리번거리며, 그가 줄리아에게 반한 것이 아무것도 안 된 것을 의아해하기 시작했고, 자기가 그걸 앞당기는 데 소홀했던 게 아닐까 하는 두려움마저 들 뻔했다. 하지만 마음 써야 할 이가 그토록 많은데, 아무리 그녀의 부지런함인들 어찌 제 바람을 다 좇을 수 있었으랴?\n\n하루 이틀 더 지나, 예이츠 씨도 마찬가지로 떠났다. 그가 떠나는 데에 토머스 경은 가장 큰 관심을 느꼈다. 식구끼리만 있고 싶던 차라, 예이츠 씨보다 나은 낯선 이가 있었어도 성가셨을 텐데, 하물며 실없고 뻔뻔하고 게으르고 돈이나 쓰는 그 사람으로 말하면 어느 모로 보나 짜증스러웠다. 그 자체로도 그는 진력나는 사람이었으나, 톰의 벗이자 줄리아의 흠모자로서는 밉살스러워졌다. 토머스 경은 크로퍼드 씨가 가든 남든 아주 무심했으나, 예이츠 씨를 현관까지 배웅하며 즐거운 여행을 비는 그의 덕담은 진심 어린 흡족함으로 건네진 것이었다. 예이츠 씨는 맨스필드의 온갖 연극 채비가 헐리고, 연극에 딸린 온갖 것이 치워지는 것까지 다 보고 남아 있었다. 그는 그 집이 그 두루 진중한 본색을 온전히 되찾은 채로 떠났다. 그리고 토머스 경은 그가 집을 나서는 것을 보며, 그 계획에 얽힌 가장 고약한 것이자, 그것이 있었음을 어쩔 수 없이 떠올리게 할 마지막 것을 떨쳐 버리기를 바랐다.\n\n노리스 부인은 그를 괴롭혔을 법한 물건 하나를 용케 그의 눈앞에서 치웠다. 그토록 재주껏, 그토록 훌륭히 도맡아 지어 낸 그 막이 그녀를 따라 그녀의 오두막으로 갔으니, 마침 그녀가 초록색 나사가 유난히 아쉽던 참이었기 때문이다.\n\n# 제21장\n\n토머스 경의 귀환은, 「연인들의 맹세」와는 별개로, 그 집안의 방식에 뚜렷한 변화를 가져왔다. 그의 다스림 아래 맨스필드는 달라진 곳이 되었다. 그들 사교 무리의 몇몇은 떠나보내지고, 다른 여럿의 기운은 시들해졌으니—지난날에 견주면 온통 한결같음과 우울뿐이었다—좀처럼 활기를 띠는 일 없는 침침한 가족 모임이었다. 목사관과의 왕래는 뜸했다. 대체로 가까운 사귐에서 물러나 있던 토머스 경은, 이 무렵 오직 한 곳 말고는 어떤 만남도 유난히 내키지 않아 했다. 러시워스가만이 그가 청할 만한, 제 집안 울타리에 더할 유일한 손님이었다.\n\n에드먼드는 아버지의 심경이 그러한 것을 의아해하지 않았고, 그랜트 내외가 배제된 것 말고는 아무것도 아쉬워하지 않았다. “하지만 그분들은,” 그가 패니에게 말했다. “마땅한 명분이 있어. 그분들은 우리한테 딸린 사람들 같고, 우리 자신의 한 부분 같거든. 나는 아버지가, 당신 안 계신 동안 그분들이 어머니와 누이들한테 베푼 그 크나큰 마음씀을 좀 더 헤아려 주셨으면 싶어. 그분들이 홀대받는다고 느끼실까 봐 걱정이야. 하지만 사실 아버지는 그분들을 거의 모르셔. 아버지가 잉글랜드를 떠나실 때 그분들은 여기 온 지 일 년도 안 됐거든. 아버지가 그분들을 더 잘 아셨더라면, 그 사귐을 마땅한 만큼 값지게 여기셨을 텐데. 실은 아버지가 딱 좋아하실 만한 그런 분들이니까. 우리끼리는 이따금 활기가 좀 아쉽잖아. 누이들은 기운이 없어 보이고, 톰도 분명 마음이 편치 않고. 그랜트 박사 내외라면 우리한테 생기를 돋우고, 아버지한테도 저녁을 한결 즐겁게 보내게 해 드릴 텐데.”\n\n“그렇게 생각하세요?” 패니가 말했다. “제 생각엔, 삼촌은 누가 끼는 걸 도무지 안 좋아하실 것 같아요. 삼촌은 오빠가 말하는 바로 그 조용함을 값지게 여기시고, 당신 가족 울타리의 그 평온이야말로 원하시는 전부인 것 같아요. 그리고 우리가 예전보다 더 진중해졌다고는 제겐 여겨지지 않아요—삼촌이 외국 나가시기 전 말이에요. 제가 기억하는 한, 늘 거의 마찬가지였어요. 삼촌 앞에서 크게 웃는 일은 결코 없었죠. 아니, 차이가 좀 있다면, 그건 그런 오랜 부재가 처음엔 으레 자아내는 것보다 더하진 않다고 봐요. 얼마간 서먹함이야 있을 수밖에요. 하지만 예전에 우리 저녁이 즐거웠던 적이 있다고는, 삼촌이 도시에 계실 때 말고는 도무지 기억이 안 나요. 우러러보는 어른이 집에 계실 때 즐거운 젊은이는 없을 것 같아요.”\n\n“네 말이 옳은 것 같구나, 패니.” 잠깐 생각한 뒤 그가 대답했다. “우리 저녁이 새로운 빛깔을 띠었다기보다 오히려 예전 모습으로 돌아간 거라고 봐. 새로웠던 건 그 저녁이 활기찼다는 거고. 그런데 겨우 몇 주가 남기는 인상이 어찌 그리 강한지! 나는 마치 우리가 전엔 이렇게 지낸 적이 없었던 것처럼 느껴 왔거든.”\n\n“나야 남들보다 더 진중한가 봐요.” 패니가 말했다. “저녁이 제겐 길게 느껴지지 않아요. 저는 삼촌이 서인도제도 이야기하시는 걸 듣는 게 좋아요. 한 시간이고 내리 들을 수 있어요. 다른 많은 것보다 그게 저를 더 즐겁게 해 주거든요. 하기야 저는 남들하고 다르겠죠, 아마.”\n\n“어째서 감히 그런 말을 하지?” (미소 지으며.) “남들과 다른 게 오직 더 슬기롭고 신중하다는 점뿐이라는 말이 듣고 싶은 거니? 그런데 너든 누구든, 나한테서 칭찬을 들은 적이 언제 있었던가, 패니? 칭찬받고 싶으면 아버지한테 가 봐. 아버지가 흡족하게 해 주실 거야. 삼촌한테 어찌 생각하시느냐 여쭤봐, 그럼 실컷 칭찬을 들을 거다. 그게 주로 네 겉모습에 대한 거일지 몰라도, 넌 그쯤 참아야 하고, 삼촌이 머잖아 네 마음의 아름다움도 그만큼 알아보시리라 믿어야지.”\n\n그런 말은 패니에게 너무나 낯설어 그녀를 여간 겸연쩍게 하지 않았다.\n\n“삼촌은 네가 아주 예쁘다고 여기신단다, 패니야—요컨대 그게 다야. 나 말고 다른 누구라도 그걸 더 부풀렸을 테고, 너 말고 다른 누구라도 전엔 예쁘다고 여겨지지 않았던 걸 서운해했겠지. 하지만 사실 삼촌은 이제껏 널 한 번도 예쁘다 여긴 적이 없다가—이제 그리 여기시는 거야. 네 얼굴빛이 어찌나 좋아졌는지!—게다가 얼마나 낯빛이 살아났는지!—그리고 네 몸매도—아니, 패니, 그런 얘기에 고개 돌리지 마라—한낱 삼촌인걸. 삼촌의 감탄도 못 견딘다면, 넌 대체 어쩌려고 그러니? 넌 정말이지 바라볼 값어치가 있는 사람이라는 생각에 익숙해지기 시작해야 해. 예쁜 여자로 자라나는 걸 아무렇지 않게 여기도록 애써 봐.”\n\n“아! 그런 말씀 마세요, 그런 말씀 마세요.” 패니가 외쳤는데, 그가 아는 것보다 더 많은 감정에 괴로웠던 것이다. 그러나 그녀가 괴로워하는 것을 보고, 그는 그 화제를 접고, 그저 좀 더 진지하게 덧붙였다—\n\n“삼촌은 어느 모로 보나 너를 흐뭇해하실 마음이시고, 나는 그저 네가 삼촌한테 좀 더 이야기했으면 싶을 뿐이야. 넌 저녁 자리에서 너무 말이 없는 축에 들거든.”\n\n“하지만 저는 예전보다는 삼촌한테 더 이야기하는걸요. 정말 그래요. 어젯밤에 제가 삼촌한테 노예 무역에 대해 여쭙는 거 못 들으셨어요?”\n\n“들었지—그리고 그 물음에 다른 물음들이 이어지기를 바라던 참이었어. 삼촌은 더 여쭤보면 흐뭇해하셨을 텐데.”\n\n“저도 그러고 싶었어요—그런데 어찌나 쥐 죽은 듯 조용하던지! 게다가 사촌들이 한마디도 없이, 그 화제에 도무지 관심도 없는 듯이 곁에 앉아 있는데, 저는 내키지가 않았어요—삼촌이 당신 딸들도 마땅히 느꼈으면 하실 그런 관심과 즐거움을 제가 내보이며, 사촌들을 깎아내려 저를 돋보이려는 것처럼 보일까 봐서요.”\n\n“크로퍼드 양이 며칠 전에 너를 두고 한 말이 아주 옳았어. 너는 다른 여자들이 홀대를 두려워하는 만큼이나 눈길과 칭찬을 두려워하는 것 같다고 하더구나. 우리가 목사관에서 네 얘기를 하고 있었는데, 그게 그녀 말이었어. 그녀는 사람 보는 눈이 대단해. 사람의 됨됨이를 그녀보다 더 잘 가려내는 이를 나는 몰라. 그토록 젊은 여자로선 놀라운 일이지! 그녀는 분명 너를, 널 그토록 오래 알아 온 사람 대부분보다 더 잘 이해하고 있어. 그리고 몇몇 다른 사람들에 대해서도, 이따금 던지는 생기 넘치는 언질이며 무심코 튀어나오는 그 순간의 말에서 나는 알아챌 수 있는데, 그녀는 조심성만 아니라면 숱한 사람을 그만큼 정확히 짚어 낼 수 있을 거야. 그녀가 우리 아버지를 어찌 여길지 궁금하구나! 더없이 신사답고 위엄 있고 한결같은 태도를 지닌 훤칠한 분으로 감탄할 게 틀림없어. 하지만 어쩌면, 아버지를 그토록 드물게 뵈어서, 그 과묵함이 좀 거슬릴지도 몰라. 두 분이 자주 함께 계실 수 있다면, 나는 서로 좋아하시게 될 거라 확신해. 아버지는 그녀의 활기를 즐기실 테고, 그녀는 아버지의 역량을 값지게 여길 재주가 있으니까. 두 분이 좀 더 자주 만나시면 좋겠어! 그녀가 아버지 쪽에 무슨 미움이 있다고 여기지는 않았으면 하고.”\n\n“그녀는 오빠들 나머지 모두의 호의를 너무나 굳게 확신하고 있을 테니,” 패니가 반쯤 한숨지으며 말했다. “그런 걱정은 조금도 안 할 거예요. 그리고 토머스 경이 처음엔 그저 가족하고만 있고 싶어 하시는 거야 너무나 자연스러운 일이니, 그녀도 거기서 무얼 넘겨짚진 못하겠죠. 얼마쯤 지나면, 아마 철이 바뀐 걸 감안하고 보면, 우리도 다시 예전 같은 식으로 만나게 될 거예요.”\n\n“그녀가 어릴 적 이후로 시골에서 시월을 보내는 건 이번이 처음이야. 나야 턴브리지나 첼트넘을 시골이라 부르진 않으니까. 게다가 십일월은 한층 더 진중한 달이지. 그리고 나는 그랜트 부인이, 겨울이 다가오면서 그녀가 맨스필드를 따분하게 여기지 않을까 무척 애태우는 걸 알겠어.”\n\n패니는 할 말이 아주 많았으나, 아무 말도 안 하고, 크로퍼드 양의 온갖 밑천—그 재주며 활기며 대단함이며 벗들—을 죄다 건드리지 않은 채 두는 편이 더 안전했으니, 자칫 야박해 보일 만한 소견을 저도 모르게 내비칠까 봐서였다. 크로퍼드 양이 저에 대해 품은 다정한 생각은 적어도 고마운 참음을 받을 만했고, 그녀는 다른 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다.\n\n“내일은 삼촌이 소더턴에서 만찬을 드시는 것 같아요, 오빠하고 버트럼 오빠도요. 우리끼리 집에 남으면 아주 단출한 자리가 되겠네요. 삼촌이 러시워스 씨를 계속 좋아하셨으면 좋겠어요.”\n\n“그건 불가능해, 패니. 내일 방문을 다녀오면 아버지는 그이를 덜 좋아하시게 될걸, 다섯 시간이나 함께 있을 테니까. 뒤따를 훨씬 더 큰 화만 아니라면—그게 토머스 경한테 남길 인상 말이야—나는 그날의 그 따분함이 벌써 겁날 지경이야. 아버지도 더는 오래 스스로를 못 속이실 거야. 나는 그들 모두가 안됐고, 러시워스와 마리아가 아예 만나지 않았더라면 하고 무엇이든 내놓고 싶은 심정이야.”\n\n정말이지 이 대목에서 실망이 토머스 경 위에 드리우고 있었다. 러시워스 씨에 대한 그의 온갖 호의도, 그에 대한 러시워스 씨의 온갖 굽실거림도, 그가 이내 그 진실의 일부를 알아차리는 것을 막지는 못했다—러시워스 씨가 못난 젊은이라는 것, 책에 어두운 만큼이나 세상일에도 어둡고, 대체로 의견이 붙박이지 못했으며, 정작 저 자신은 그것을 그리 알지도 못하는 듯하다는 것을 말이다.\n\n그는 아주 다른 사위를 기대했었다. 그리고 마리아를 생각하니 마음이 무거워지기 시작해, 그녀의 심경을 헤아려 보려 했다. 무심함이야말로 그들이 놓일 수 있는 가장 나은 상태임을 알아채는 데는 별로 살필 것도 없었다. 러시워스 씨를 대하는 그녀의 태도는 무심하고 싸늘했다. 그녀는 그를 좋아할 수 없었고, 좋아하지 않았다. 토머스 경은 그녀에게 진지하게 이야기하기로 마음먹었다. 이 혼사가 아무리 득이 되고, 그 약혼이 아무리 오래되고 공공연한 것일지라도, 그녀의 행복이 거기에 희생되어서는 안 되었다. 러시워스 씨는 어쩌면 너무 짧은 사귐 끝에 받아들여진 것이고, 그를 더 잘 알게 되자 그녀가 뉘우치고 있는지도 몰랐다.\n\n근엄한 다정함으로 토머스 경은 그녀에게 말을 건넸다. 제 두려움을 이르고, 그녀의 바람을 물었으며, 터놓고 솔직히 말해 달라 간청하고, 만일 그녀가 이 앞날에 스스로 불행하다 느낀다면 어떤 불편도 무릅쓰고 이 인연을 아주 접겠노라 다짐해 주었다. 그녀를 위해 나서서 그녀를 풀어 주겠다는 것이었다. 마리아는 듣는 동안 한순간 갈등했으나, 오직 한순간뿐이었다. 아버지가 말을 마치자 그녀는 곧바로, 단호하게, 겉으로는 아무 동요도 없이 대답할 수 있었다. 그녀는 그 크나큰 마음씀과 아버지다운 다정함에 고마워했으나, 자기가 이 약혼을 깨뜨리려는 마음이 손톱만큼이라도 있다거나, 이를 맺은 이래 무슨 생각이나 마음의 변화를 느낀다고 여기신다면 아주 잘못 아신 거라 했다. 그녀는 러시워스 씨의 사람됨과 성품을 더없이 높이 사며, 그와 함께할 제 행복을 의심할 여지가 없다는 것이었다.\n\n토머스 경은 흡족했다. 어쩌면 흡족해하고 싶은 마음이 너무 커서, 남들에게라면 제 판단이 시켰음직한 만큼 그 일을 파고들지 못했다. 이는 그가 아픔 없이는 놓아 버릴 수 없는 혼사였다. 그리하여 그는 이렇게 헤아렸다. 러시워스 씨는 나아질 만큼 젊다. 러시워스 씨는 좋은 사교계에서 반드시, 또 틀림없이 나아질 것이다. 그리고 마리아가 이제 그와 함께할 제 행복을 그토록 굳게 말할 수 있다면, 그것도 분명 사랑의 편견이나 눈멂 없이 말하는 것이라면, 마땅히 믿어 줘야 한다. 그녀의 감정은 아마 예민하지 않았고, 그는 여태 그것을 예민하다 여긴 적도 없었다. 그러나 그 때문에 그녀의 안락이 덜해지지는 않을 것이었다. 그리고 만일 그녀가 제 남편이 앞장서는 빛나는 인물인 걸 굳이 안 봐도 된다면, 그 밖의 모든 것은 분명 그녀에게 유리할 터였다. 사랑 때문에 시집가는 게 아닌 심성 고운 젊은 여자는 대개 제 친정에 오히려 더 정이 붙는 법이었다. 그리고 소더턴이 맨스필드에 가까운 것은 자연히 더없이 큰 유혹이 되어 줄 테고, 십중팔구 더없이 상냥하고 죄 없는 즐거움을 끊임없이 대 줄 것이었다. 토머스 경의 헤아림은 이러이러했으니, 파혼에 딸릴 겸연쩍은 화들과 그에 따를 뒷말이며 이러쿵저러쿵 소견이며 나무람을 면하게 되어 흐뭇했고, 이토록 존경과 영향력을 더해 줄 혼사를 확보하게 되어 흐뭇했으며, 제 딸의 성품에 대해 그 목적에 가장 유리한 것이라면 무엇이든 믿을 수 있어 아주 흐뭇했다.\n\n그녀에게도 그 이야기는 그에게 못지않게 흡족하게 끝났다. 그녀는 제 운명을 돌이킬 수 없게 붙들어 둔 것이—소더턴에 새로이 몸을 맹세한 것이, 크로퍼드에게 제 행동을 주무르고 제 앞날을 무너뜨리는 승리를 안겨 줄 가능성에서 안전해진 것이—반가운 마음이었다. 그리하여 그녀는, 오직 앞으로 러시워스 씨를 좀 더 조심스레 대하여 아버지가 다시 저를 의심하지 않게 하리라 마음먹으며, 도도한 결의 속에 물러났다.\n\n만일 토머스 경이, 헨리 크로퍼드가 맨스필드를 떠난 뒤 처음 사나흘 안에, 그녀의 감정이 조금이라도 가라앉기 전에, 그녀가 그에 대한 온갖 희망을 접거나 그 연적을 견디기로 아주 마음먹기 전에 딸에게 물었더라면, 그녀의 대답은 달랐을지도 모른다. 그러나 또 사나흘이 지나, 아무 소식도, 편지도, 전갈도, 마음이 누그러진 기미도, 헤어진 데서 무슨 득을 볼 희망도 없게 되자, 그녀의 마음은 자존심과 자기 복수가 줄 수 있는 온갖 위안을 찾을 만큼 싸늘해졌다.\n\n헨리 크로퍼드는 그녀의 행복을 무너뜨렸다. 그러나 자기가 그리했음을 그가 알게 해서는 안 되었다. 그가 그녀의 체면과 겉모습과 잘나감까지 무너뜨리게 해서는 안 되었다. 그가 그녀를, 그를 못 잊어 맨스필드의 은둔 속에 시들어 가는 여자로, 그를 위해 소더턴과 런던을, 독립과 화려함을 마다한 여자로 떠올리게 해서는 안 되었다. 독립이 그 어느 때보다 절실했고, 맨스필드에서 그것이 없는 것이 한층 사무치게 느껴졌다. 그녀는 아버지가 지우는 그 구속을 점점 더 견디기 어려워졌다. 그가 없던 동안 누린 그 자유가 이제 없어서는 안 될 것이 되어 있었다. 그녀는 되도록 빨리 그와 맨스필드에서 벗어나, 상처 입은 마음을 위해 재물과 지체, 부산함과 세상에서 위안을 찾아야 했다. 그녀의 마음은 아주 굳어져 흔들리지 않았다.\n\n그런 심경에는 지체가, 채비를 많이 하느라 지체되는 것마저도 화였을 테고, 러시워스 씨도 그녀 자신보다 혼인을 더 안달할 수는 없을 지경이었다. 마음의 온갖 중요한 채비에서 그녀는 다 갖춰져 있었으니—집과 구속과 평온에 대한 미움으로, 어그러진 정의 괴로움과 제가 시집갈 사내에 대한 경멸로, 혼인 채비를 마친 셈이었다. 나머지야 기다려도 좋았다. 새 마차와 세간의 채비는, 제 취향이 좀 더 마음껏 놀 수 있을 런던과 봄까지 기다려도 좋았다.\n\n당사자들이 이 점에서 다들 뜻을 같이하니, 혼례에 앞서야 할 그런 채비에는 아주 몇 주면 넉넉하리라는 것이 이내 드러났다.\n\n러시워스 부인은 물러나, 제 사랑하는 아들이 고른 그 복 많은 젊은 여자에게 자리를 내줄 채비가 아주 되어 있었다. 그리하여 십일월 아주 초에, 참된 미망인다운 법도로 저 자신과 제 하녀와 하인과 사륜마차를 배스로 옮겨, 거기서 저녁 모임마다 소더턴의 온갖 놀라운 것을 늘어놓으며 뽐냈다. 어쩌면 그것을 카드판의 활기 속에서, 정작 그곳에 있을 때만큼이나 톡톡히 즐겼으리라. 그리고 그달 중순도 되기 전에, 소더턴에 또 한 사람의 안주인을 안겨 준 그 예식이 치러졌다.\n\n아주 격에 맞는 혼례였다. 신부는 우아하게 차려입었고, 두 들러리는 격에 맞게 그만 못했으며, 아버지가 신부를 넘겨주었고, 어머니는 손에 각성제를 든 채 마음이 흔들리기를 기다리며 서 있었으며, 이모는 울어 보려 애썼고, 예식은 그랜트 박사가 사뭇 엄숙하게 낭독했다. 온 동네의 입방아에 오를 때에도 트집 잡힐 데라곤 없었으니, 다만 신부와 신랑과 줄리아를 교회 문에서 소더턴까지 실어 나른 마차가 러시워스 씨가 그전 일 년간 타던 바로 그 마차였다는 것뿐이었다. 그 밖의 모든 것에서 그날의 격식은 더없이 까다로운 검사도 견뎌 낼 만했다.\n\n일은 치러졌고, 그들은 떠났다. 토머스 경은 애타는 아버지라면 으레 느낄 법한 것을 느꼈으니, 정말이지 제 아내가 저 자신에 대해 미리 걱정했다가 다행히 면한 그 동요의 상당 부분을 겪고 있었다. 노리스 부인은, 그날의 소임을 거들려는 마음에 더없이 흡족해, 공원 저택에서 하루를 보내며 그 아우의 기운을 북돋고, 여분의 잔을 한둘 기울여 러시워스 부부의 건강을 빌며, 온통 신나는 기쁨에 겨웠다. 그녀가 이 혼사를 맺었으니, 그녀가 죄다 해냈으니 말이다. 그리고 그 자신만만한 득의를 보고서는, 그녀가 평생 부부의 불화라는 것을 들어 본 적이나 있는지, 제 눈앞에서 자란 그 조카딸의 성품에 대해 손톱만큼의 통찰이라도 지녔는지, 아무도 짐작하지 못했을 것이다.\n\n젊은 부부의 계획은, 며칠 뒤 브라이턴으로 가서 거기 몇 주간 집을 한 채 빌리는 것이었다. 마리아에게는 사람 모이는 곳이란 곳이 죄다 새로웠고, 브라이턴은 겨울에도 여름 못지않게 흥청거렸다. 거기서의 새로운 놀이가 시들해지면, 그때는 좀 더 넓은 런던으로 나갈 때일 터였다.\n\n줄리아도 그들과 함께 브라이턴으로 가기로 되어 있었다. 자매 사이의 겨룸이 그친 뒤로, 그들은 차츰 예전의 그 좋은 사이를 상당 부분 되찾아 가고 있었고, 적어도 이런 때에 서로 함께 있는 것이 서로에게 여간 반가울 만큼은 벗이 되어 있었다. 러시워스 씨 말고 다른 길동무가 그 부인에게는 무엇보다 요긴했다. 그리고 줄리아도 마리아 못지않게 새로움과 즐거움에 목말라 있었으니—하기야 그것을 얻으려고 그토록 애를 쓰지는 않았을 테고, 아랫자리도 좀 더 잘 견딜 수 있었지만.\n\n그들이 떠나자 맨스필드에 또 한 번 이렇다 할 변화가, 메우는 데 얼마간 시간이 걸릴 빈자리가 생겼다. 가족 울타리가 크게 오그라들었다. 그리고 버트럼 자매가 근래 그 명랑함에 별로 보탠 게 없다 해도, 그들이 아쉽지 않을 수 없었다. 그 어머니마저 그들을 아쉬워했다. 하물며 그 마음 여린 사촌은 얼마나 더했으랴. 그 애는 집 안을 이리저리 거닐며, 그들이 여태 딱히 받을 만한 짓을 한 적 없는 그런 애틋한 아쉬움으로 그들을 생각하고 그들을 안타까워했으니 말이다.\n\n# 제22장\n\n사촌들이 떠나자 패니의 위상이 높아졌다. 그때 그녀가 그리되었듯, 응접실의 유일한 젊은 여자가 되고, 여태 그토록 하찮은 셋째 자리나 지키던 그 흥미로운 가족의 한 갈래를 홀로 차지하는 이가 되니, 여태 그 어느 때보다 더 눈길을 받고, 더 생각되고, 더 마음 쓰이지 않을 수가 없었다. 그리고 “패니는 어디 있느냐?”가, 누구의 편의를 위해 그녀가 필요하지 않을 때조차, 드물지 않은 물음이 되었다.\n\n집에서만 그 값어치가 높아진 것이 아니라, 목사관에서도 그러했다. 노리스 씨가 죽은 뒤로 일 년에 두 번도 채 발을 들이지 않던 그 집에서, 그녀는 반가운, 청함받는 손님이 되었고, 침침하고 질척한 십일월의 어느 날, 메리 크로퍼드에게 더없이 반가운 사람이 되었다. 처음엔 우연히 시작된 그녀의 그곳 방문은, 이제 청함을 받아 이어졌다. 제 아우를 위해 무슨 변화든 얻어 주고 싶어 정말로 안달하던 그랜트 부인은, 더없이 손쉬운 자기기만으로, 패니에게 자주 들르기를 채근하는 것이 그 애한테 더없이 친절한 일을 해 주고 더없이 요긴한 성장의 기회를 주는 거라고 저를 설득할 수 있었다.\n\n패니는 노리스 이모의 심부름으로 마을에 갔다가, 목사관 가까이에서 소나기를 만났다. 그리고 그 집 울타리 바로 너머의 참나무 가지와 아직 매달린 잎사귀 아래로 비를 피하려 애쓰는 것이 어느 창에서 발견되니, 얼마쯤 얌전한 사양이 없지 않았으나 안으로 들어오지 않을 수 없었다. 정중한 하인은 물리쳤으나, 그랜트 박사가 몸소 우산을 들고 나오자, 여간 부끄러워하며 되도록 빨리 집 안으로 들어가는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없었다. 그리고 마침 더없이 낙담한 마음으로 그 음울한 비를 바라보며, 그날 아침의 운동 계획이 죄다 틀어진 것과 앞으로 스물네 시간 동안 저희 말고 살아 있는 사람 하나 볼 가망이 다 사라진 것을 한숨짓던 딱한 크로퍼드 양에게, 현관에서 조그맣게 부산 떠는 소리와 물이 뚝뚝 듣는 채로 현관홀에 선 프라이스 양의 모습은 반가운 것이었다. 시골의 비 오는 날에 무슨 일이 벌어진다는 것의 값어치가 그녀 앞에 더없이 절실하게 다가왔다. 그녀는 대번에 다시 생기가 돌아, 패니에게 쓸모가 되는 데, 그녀가 처음엔 인정하려 들지 않던 것보다 더 젖어 있음을 알아내고 마른 옷을 마련해 주는 데 누구보다 부지런했다. 그리고 패니는, 이 온갖 마음씀에 따르고 안주인들과 하녀들의 시중과 거듦을 받지 않을 수 없었던 데다, 아래층으로 내려와서는 비가 그치지 않는 동안 한 시간이나 그들의 응접실에 붙들려 있지 않을 수 없었으니, 새로이 보고 생각할 무언가가 주는 그 복이 이렇게 크로퍼드 양에게로 넓혀져, 옷을 갈아입고 만찬을 들 때까지 그녀의 기운을 이어 줄 만했다.\n\n두 자매가 그녀에게 어찌나 친절하고 어찌나 상냥하던지, 만일 자기가 거치적거리지 않는다고 믿을 수 있었더라면, 또 한 시간 끝에는 날이 분명 개어, 자기를 집에 데려다주려고 그랜트 박사의 마차와 말을 내는—그녀를 으르던—그 창피를 면하게 되리라 미리 알았더라면, 패니는 그 방문을 즐길 수도 있었으리라. 이런 날씨에 제가 나가 있는 것이 집에 무슨 걱정을 자아낼까 하는 근심으로 말하면, 그녀는 그 점에서는 괴로워할 것이 없었다. 그녀가 나온 것을 아는 이라곤 두 이모뿐이라, 아무 걱정도 없으리란 것을, 그리고 노리스 이모가 비 오는 동안 그녀를 어느 오두막에 붙들어 두든, 그녀가 그런 오두막에 있으리란 것을 버트럼 이모는 의심치 않으리란 것을 그녀는 훤히 알았기 때문이다.\n\n날이 밝아지기 시작하는데, 패니가 방 안의 하프를 눈여겨보고 그것에 대해 몇 마디 물으니, 이내 그것을 무척 듣고 싶어 한다는 고백과, 좀처럼 믿기지 않는—맨스필드에 온 이래 아직 한 번도 그것을 들어 본 적이 없다는—고백으로 이어졌다. 패니 자신에게야 그것은 아주 소박하고 자연스러운 일로 여겨졌다. 악기가 온 뒤로 그녀는 목사관에 거의 온 적이 없었고, 올 까닭도 없었던 것이다. 그러나 크로퍼드 양은 그 일을 두고 진작 밝혔던 바람을 떠올리고는, 제 소홀함이 마음에 걸렸다. 그리하여 “지금 연주해 드릴까요?”와 “뭘 듣고 싶어요?”가 더없이 선선한 명랑함으로 곧바로 뒤따르는 물음이었다.\n\n그리하여 그녀는 연주했다. 새 청중을, 그것도 그토록 고마워하고 그토록 그 연주에 감탄이 가득하며 안목이 모자라지 않음을 내보이는 청중을 얻어 흐뭇해하며. 그녀는, 날이 뚜렷이 개자 창 쪽으로 눈길을 옮긴 패니의 눈이 이제 마땅히 해야 할 일을 말할 때까지 연주했다.\n\n“십오 분만 더 있어요.” 크로퍼드 양이 말했다. “그럼 어떻게 될지 알게 될 테니. 비가 그치는 첫 순간에 냅다 달아나진 말아요. 저 구름이 심상찮아 보이니까요.”\n\n“하지만 저건 지나갔어요.” 패니가 말했다. “제가 죽 지켜봤거든요. 이 날씨는 죄다 남쪽에서 온 거예요.”\n\n“남쪽이든 북쪽이든, 나는 시커먼 구름은 보면 알아요. 저렇게 을러대는 동안엔 나서면 안 돼요. 게다가 나는 양한테 뭘 좀 더 연주해 주고 싶어요—아주 어여쁜 곡을요—양의 사촌 에드먼드가 무엇보다 좋아하는 곡이랍니다. 남아서 사촌이 좋아하는 곡을 들어야 해요.”\n\n패니는 그래야만 함을 느꼈다. 그리고 에드먼드를 생각하느라 굳이 그 말을 기다린 것은 아니었으나, 그런 상기가 그녀를 유난히 그의 생각에 깨어 있게 했다. 그리하여 그녀는 그가 그 방에 몇 번이고 앉아 있는 것을, 어쩌면 지금 그녀가 앉은 바로 그 자리에서, 그 좋아하는 가락을—그녀가 보기엔 더없이 빼어난 음색과 표현으로 연주된 그 가락을—한결같은 즐거움으로 듣고 있는 것을 그려 보았다. 그리고 저 자신도 그것이 마음에 들고, 그가 좋아하는 것이라면 무엇이든 좋아하게 되어 기뻤으나, 그녀는 그것이 끝나자 전보다 더 진심으로 어서 가고 싶어 안달이 났다. 그리고 이것이 뻔히 드러나자, 다시 들르라고, 걸음 나설 때면 언제든 들러 달라고, 와서 하프를 더 들으라고 어찌나 친절히 청함을 받던지, 집에서 반대만 없다면 그리해야겠다 싶었다.\n\n버트럼 자매가 떠난 뒤 처음 두 주 사이에 그들 사이에 생겨난 그런 친밀함의 발단이 이러했다—주로 새로운 것에 대한 크로퍼드 양의 욕구에서 비롯한, 그리고 패니의 심정에는 별로 실질이 없는 친밀함이었다. 패니는 이삼 일에 한 번씩 그녀에게 갔다. 그것은 일종의 홀림 같았다. 가지 않고는 마음이 편치 않았으나, 그러면서도 그녀를 사랑하는 것도, 그녀처럼 생각하는 것도 아니었고, 아무도 달리 없을 때 이제 찾아진다는 것에 무슨 신세 진 마음이 있는 것도 아니었다. 또 그녀의 이야기에서, 이따금의 재미 말고는 더 높은 즐거움을 얻지도 못했으니, 그마저도 흔히 제 판단을 저버려야 얻는 것이었다—그녀가 존중받았으면 하는 사람이나 화제를 두고 우스갯소리로 그 재미가 돋을 때면 말이다. 그래도 그녀는 갔고, 둘은 그랜트 부인의 관목 산책로에서 여러 번 반 시간씩 함께 거닐었으니, 철에 걸맞잖게 날이 유난히 포근했던 것이다. 그리고 이제 견주어 보면 가림막도 없다시피 한 벤치 하나에 이따금 감히 앉아 보기도 했는데, 어쩌면 그토록 길어진 가을의 달콤함을 두고 패니가 무슨 다정한 탄사를 내뱉는 한복판에, 둘레의 마지막 노란 잎사귀 몇을 떨어뜨리며 문득 부풀어 오른 찬 돌풍에 떠밀려, 벌떡 일어나 몸을 덥히려 걸어야 할 때까지 거기 머무르곤 했다.\n\n“이거 참 예쁘네요, 참 예뻐요.” 어느 날 둘이 이렇게 함께 앉아 있는데 패니가 둘레를 둘러보며 말했다. “이 산책로에 올 때마다 나는 그 자람과 아름다움에 더 놀라게 돼요. 삼 년 전만 해도 이건 밭 위쪽을 따라 난 거친 산울타리에 지나지 않았고, 무엇이 되리라 여겨지지도, 무엇이 될 수도 없어 보였는데, 이제 산책로로 바뀌었고, 편의로서 더 값진지 장식으로서 더 값진지 말하기 어려울 정도예요. 그리고 어쩌면 또 삼 년 뒤에는, 우리가 이게 전엔 무엇이었는지 잊게—거의 잊게 될지도 몰라요. 세월의 작용이며 사람 마음의 변화가 어찌 이리 놀라운지, 어찌 이리 참으로 놀라운지!” 그리고 뒤엣것의 갈래를 좇아, 그녀는 이내 덧붙였다. “우리 천성의 어느 한 능력이 나머지보다 더 놀랍다 불릴 만하다면, 나는 그게 기억이라고 봐요. 기억의 그 힘, 그 어긋남, 그 들쭉날쭉함에는, 우리의 다른 어떤 지력에서보다 더 사무치게 알 수 없는 무언가가 있는 것 같거든요. 기억은 때로 그토록 오래 담아 두고, 그토록 쓸모 있고, 그토록 순종하는가 하면, 또 때로는 그토록 갈피를 못 잡고 그토록 약하며, 또 어떤 때는 그토록 폭군 같고 그토록 걷잡을 수 없어요! 우리야 물론 어느 모로 보나 하나의 기적이지만, 우리가 떠올리고 잊는 그 힘만은 유난히 헤아릴 길이 없는 것 같아요.”\n\n크로퍼드 양은 마음이 동하지도, 귀 기울이지도 않아 할 말이 없었다. 그리고 패니는 그것을 알아채고, 흥미로우리라 여겨지는 것으로 제 마음을 되돌렸다.\n\n“내가 칭찬하는 게 주제넘어 보일지 모르지만, 그래도 나는 이 모든 데서 그랜트 부인이 내보인 안목에 감탄하지 않을 수가 없어요. 이 산책로의 짜임에는 어찌나 고요한 소박함이 어렸는지! 너무 욕심부리지 않은 것 말이에요!”\n\n“그래요,” 크로퍼드 양이 심드렁하게 대답했다. “이만한 곳에야 이 정도면 딱 좋죠. 여기선 넓이 같은 건 생각도 안 하니까요. 그리고 우리끼리 얘기지만, 나는 맨스필드에 오기 전엔 시골 목사가 관목 산책로 같은 걸 언감생심 바라리라곤 상상도 못 했어요.”\n\n“상록수들이 잘 자라는 걸 보니 정말 기뻐요!” 패니가 대꾸했다. “삼촌네 정원사는 늘 여기 흙이 자기 것보다 낫다고 하는데, 월계수며 상록수들이 두루 자란 걸 보면 정말 그런가 봐요. 상록수라니! 상록수는 어찌 이리 아름답고, 어찌 이리 반갑고, 어찌 이리 놀라운지! 생각해 보면, 자연의 다채로움이 어찌 이리 놀라운지! 어떤 나라에선 잎을 떨구는 나무가 되레 별종이라는 걸 우리가 알지만, 그렇다고 같은 흙과 같은 해가 그 존재의 첫 법칙과 규범에서부터 다른 초목을 길러 낸다는 게 덜 놀라운 건 아니에요. 나를 시나 읊는다 여기시겠지만, 나는 바깥에 나와 있을 때면, 특히 바깥에 앉아 있을 때면, 곧잘 이런 놀라운 상념에 빠져들거든요. 아무리 흔한 자연의 산물에라도 눈길을 두면 어김없이 이런저런 공상의 먹이를 얻게 되니까요.”\n\n“솔직히 말하면,” 크로퍼드 양이 대답했다. “나는 루이 14세 궁정의 그 유명한 도제와 좀 비슷해서, 이 산책로에서 나 자신을 보고 있는 것만큼 놀라운 건 없다고 말할 수 있어요. 일 년 전에 누가 나한테, 이곳이 내 집이 되고, 내가 여기서 이렇게 달이면 달마다 지내게 될 거라고 했다면, 나는 분명 안 믿었을 거예요. 나는 이제 여기 온 지 다섯 달이 다 돼 가는데, 게다가 내 평생 가장 조용한 다섯 달이었어요.”\n\n“양한텐 너무 조용하겠죠, 아마.”\n\n“나도 이치로야 그리 여겼을 텐데,” 하며 그녀는 말하는 사이 눈빛이 환해졌다. “죄다 통틀어 보면, 나는 이토록 행복한 여름을 보낸 적이 없어요. 하지만,” 하며 좀 더 생각에 잠긴 태도로 목소리를 낮추어, “이게 무엇으로 이어질지는 아무도 모르죠.”\n\n패니의 가슴이 빠르게 뛰었고, 그녀는 그 이상 무엇을 넘겨짚거나 캐물을 엄두가 도무지 나지 않았다. 그러나 크로퍼드 양이, 새로이 생기를 띠고 이내 말을 이었다—\n\n“시골살이에 내가 여태 예상했던 것보다 훨씬 더 잘 화해했다는 걸 나 스스로도 알겠어요. 어떤 형편에서라면, 일 년의 절반을 시골에서 보내는 것이 즐거우리라고까지, 아주 즐거우리라고까지 여길 수 있거든요. 집안 인연들 한복판의 우아하고 알맞은 크기의 집, 그들끼리 끊이지 않는 모임, 이웃에서 으뜸가는 사교계를 거느리고, 어쩌면 더 큰 재산을 지닌 이들보다도 오히려 그것을 이끄는 사람으로 우러러보이고, 그런 흥겨운 놀이의 나날에서 돌아서 봐야 세상에서 가장 마음 맞는 사람과 단둘이 마주 앉는 것보다 더 나쁠 게 없는 삶. 그런 그림에 무서울 게 하나도 없잖아요, 안 그래요, 프라이스 양? 그런 집만 있다면 새로 된 러시워스 부인을 부러워할 것도 없죠.”\n\n“러시워스 부인을 부러워하다니요!”가 패니가 하려던 말의 전부였다. “자, 자, 러시워스 부인한테 매몰차게 구는 건 우리한테 아주 야박한 짓이겠죠. 나는 우리가 그이 덕에 흥겹고 빛나고 행복한 시간을 아주 많이 누리게 되기를 바라거든요. 내년엔 우리가 다들 소더턴에 아주 자주 가 있게 될 것 같아요. 버트럼 양이 한 것 같은 혼사는 세상에 두루 복이 되는 일이에요. 러시워스 씨 아내의 첫 즐거움은 제 집을 가득 채우고, 이 고장에서 가장 좋은 무도회를 여는 것일 테니까요.”\n\n패니는 잠자코 있었고, 크로퍼드 양은 다시 생각에 잠겼다가, 몇 분 뒤 문득 고개를 들며 외쳤다. “아! 저기 그이가 오네.” 그러나 그것은 러시워스 씨가 아니라 에드먼드였으니, 그때 그가 그랜트 부인과 함께 그들 쪽으로 걸어오는 것이 보였다. “우리 언니하고 버트럼 씨네요. 양의 맏사촌이 가서 정말 기뻐요, 그이가 다시 버트럼 씨가 될 수 있으니까. ‘에드먼드 버트럼 씨’라는 소리엔 어쩐지 그토록 격식 차리고, 그토록 딱하고, 그토록 아우 같은 데가 있어서, 나는 그게 질색이거든요.”\n\n“우리 느낌이 어쩜 그리 다를까요!” 패니가 외쳤다. “나한텐 ‘버트럼 씨’라는 소리가 그토록 차갑고 아무 뜻도 없이, 그토록 온통 온기도 개성도 없이 들려요! 그냥 한 신사를 가리킬 뿐이지, 그게 다예요. 하지만 에드먼드라는 이름엔 고귀함이 있어요. 그건 영웅다움과 명성의 이름, 왕과 왕자와 기사의 이름이고, 기사도와 뜨거운 정의 기운을 내뿜는 것 같거든요.”\n\n“이름 자체야 좋다고 인정하죠, ‘에드먼드 공(公)’이나 ‘에드먼드 경(卿)’은 근사하게 들리고요. 하지만 그걸 ‘씨’라는 그 서늘함, 그 소멸 아래로 가라앉히면, ‘에드먼드 씨’는 ‘존 씨’나 ‘토머스 씨’와 다를 게 없어요. 자, 우리 저들이 시작하기도 전에 일어서서, 이맘때 바깥에 앉아 있는 걸 두고 늘어놓을 잔소리의 절반을 김빠지게 해 줄까요?”\n\n에드먼드는 각별한 반가움으로 그들을 맞았다. 그가 크나큰 흡족함으로 전해 듣던 그 한결 나아진 사귐이 시작된 이래, 그가 그 둘이 함께 있는 것을 보는 건 처음이었다. 저에게 그토록 소중한 두 사람 사이의 우정이야말로 그가 바랄 만한 바로 그것이었다. 그리고 그 사랑에 빠진 이의 헤아림을 위해 덧붙여 두자면, 그는 결코 패니를 그런 우정에서 유일한, 아니 더 큰 득을 보는 쪽으로조차 여기지 않았다.\n\n“자,” 크로퍼드 양이 말했다. “우리가 경솔했다고 나무라지 않으시나요? 우리가 대체 뭐 하러 앉아 있었겠어요, 그걸로 잔소리를 듣고, 다시는 그러지 말라고 사정사정 애원받으려는 것 말고요?”\n\n“나무랐을지도 모르죠,” 에드먼드가 말했다. “두 분 중 하나가 혼자 앉아 있었더라면요. 하지만 둘이 함께 나쁜 짓을 하고 있으니, 나는 많은 걸 눈감아 줄 수 있어요.”\n\n“오래 앉아 있었을 리 없어요,” 그랜트 부인이 외쳤다. “내가 숄을 가지러 올라갔을 때 계단 창으로 둘을 봤는데, 그땐 걷고 있었거든요.”\n\n“게다가 정말이지,” 에드먼드가 덧붙였다. “날이 하도 포근해서, 두 분이 몇 분 앉아 있었다고 경솔하달 것도 없어요. 우리 날씨를 늘 달력으로만 판단할 순 없죠. 우린 때로 오월보다 십일월에 더 마음대로 굴어도 되는걸요.”\n\n“원, 참,” 크로퍼드 양이 외쳤다. “두 분은 내가 여태 만난 다정한 벗들 중에 가장 실망스럽고 무정한 축이에요! 잠시도 마음 졸이게 해 드릴 수가 없다니까요. 우리가 얼마나 고생했는지, 얼마나 오슬오슬 떨었는지 모르시죠! 하지만 나는 진작부터, 상식에 맞서 무슨 자그마한 술수를 부리는 데는 버트럼 씨야말로 여자가 상대해야 할 가장 골치 아픈 상대 중 하나라고 여겨 왔어요. 저이한테야 처음부터 별 기대를 안 했죠. 하지만 언니, 우리 언니, 내 친언니, 언니는 내가 좀 놀라게 해 줄 권리쯤은 있다고 봤는데.”\n\n“김칫국부터 마시지 마라, 사랑하는 메리야. 나를 흔들 가망은 눈곱만큼도 없으니까. 나도 걱정거리야 있지만, 그건 아주 딴 데 있단다. 그리고 내가 날씨를 바꿀 수 있었다면, 넌 그 내내 매섭게 날카로운 동풍을 맞았을 거야—여기 내 화초 몇 개가 있는데, 로버트가 밤이 하도 포근하다고 자꾸 밖에 내놓거든. 그리고 나는 그 끝이 어찌 될지 알아. 날씨가 문득 바뀌어 갑자기 된서리가 한꺼번에 내려, 모두를(적어도 로버트를) 깜짝 놀라게 하고, 나는 화초를 하나도 남김없이 잃을 거야. 게다가 더 나쁜 건, 요리사가 방금 그러는데, 내가 일요일까지는 굳이 손질하지 말라고 한 그 칠면조—그랜트 박사가 그날의 고단함 끝에 일요일에 먹으면 얼마나 더 맛있어할지 아니까—그게 내일을 넘기지 못한다는 거야. 이런 게 얼추 걱정거리라는 거고, 그래서 나는 이 날씨가 철에 안 맞게 무덥다 여기는 거란다.”\n\n“시골 마을에서 살림하는 재미란!” 크로퍼드 양이 짓궂게 말했다. “나야 묘목상이랑 새 장수가 최고예요.”\n\n“얘야, 그랜트 박사를 웨스트민스터나 세인트폴의 주임사제 자리에 앉혀 주렴, 그럼 나도 너 못지않게 그 묘목상이랑 새 장수를 반길 테니. 하지만 맨스필드엔 그런 사람들이 없어. 나더러 어쩌라는 거니?”\n\n“아! 언니야 지금 하는 것 말고 달리 할 게 없죠. 툭하면 시달리면서도, 결코 성은 안 내는 거요.”\n\n“고맙구나. 하지만 이런 자잘한 짜증거리야 어디 살든 벗어날 수 없단다, 메리. 그리고 네가 도시에 자리 잡고 내가 널 보러 가면, 아마 넌 묘목상이랑 새 장수가 있는데도 네 나름의 짜증거리를 안고 있을 거야, 어쩌면 바로 그 사람들 탓에 말이지. 그들이 멀리 있고 시간을 안 지킨다거나, 터무니없이 바가지 씌우고 속인다거나 하는 게 쓰디쓴 한탄을 자아낼 테니까.”\n\n“나는 그런 걸 한탄하거나 느끼거나 할 것도 없이 아주 부자가 될 참이에요. 큰 수입이야말로 내가 들어 본 행복의 가장 좋은 비법이니까요. 그거면 분명 도금양이며 칠면조며 하는 부분은 죄다 챙길 수 있죠.”\n\n“아주 부자가 될 작정이오?” 에드먼드가 말했는데, 그 눈빛에는 패니의 눈에 자못 진지한 뜻이 담긴 듯했다.\n\n“그럼요. 안 그러세요? 우리 다 그렇지 않나요?”\n\n“내 힘으로 도무지 어찌해 볼 수 없는 게 뻔한 걸 작정할 순 없죠. 크로퍼드 양이야 원하는 만큼의 재산을 고를 수 있어요. 연 수입 몇천 파운드를 정하기만 하면, 그게 들어올 건 의심할 여지가 없으니까요. 내 작정은 그저 가난하지 않겠다는 것뿐이에요.”\n\n“절제와 알뜰함으로, 씀씀이를 수입에 맞춰 낮추고 어쩌고 하면서요. 무슨 말인지 알겠어요—그리고 그건 씨 나이에, 그토록 빤한 살림에 변변찮은 연줄을 지닌 사람한텐 아주 걸맞은 계획이죠. 씨가 그럭저럭한 생계 말고 뭘 더 바라겠어요? 씨 앞엔 시간도 많지 않고, 씨 친척들도 씨를 위해 뭘 해 줄 처지도, 제 재산과 지체를 견주며 씨를 무안케 할 처지도 못 되잖아요. 부디 정직하게, 그리고 가난하게 사세요—하지만 나는 씨를 부러워하진 않을 거예요. 심지어 씨를 존경할 것 같지도 않고요. 나는 정직하면서 부유한 이들을 훨씬 더 존경하거든요.”\n\n“정직함을 두고, 부유하든 가난하든, 크로퍼드 양이 얼마나 존경하느냐 하는 것은 나와는 도무지 아무 상관이 없는 일이에요. 나는 가난할 생각이 없어요. 가난이야말로 내가 딱 안 하기로 작정한 것이거든요. 다만 그 사이의 무언가, 세상 형편의 중간 자리에 있는 정직함, 그것만은 크로퍼드 양이 얕보지 않았으면 하고 바랄 뿐이에요.”\n\n“하지만 나는 그걸 얕본답니다, 더 높이 오를 수 있었을 거라면요. 나는 두각을 나타낼 수 있는데도 무명에 만족하는 것이라면 무엇이든 얕볼 수밖에 없어요.”\n\n“하지만 어떻게 오를 수 있죠? 적어도 내 정직함이 어떻게 두각을 나타낼 수 있나요?”\n\n이건 그리 쉬운 물음이 아니었고, 그 어여쁜 숙녀는 이렇게 덧붙이기 전에 자못 긴 “아!” 소리를 냈다. “씨는 의회에 있어야 하는데, 아니면 십 년 전에 군대에 갔어야 했어요.”\n\n“그건 지금 와선 별 소용이 없죠. 그리고 내가 의회에 드는 걸로 말하면, 아무래도 나는 먹고살 게 별로 없는 작은아들들을 대표하는 특별 의회가 생길 때까지 기다려야 할까 봐요. 아니에요, 크로퍼드 양,” 그가 좀 더 진지한 어조로 덧붙였다. “내가 조금의 가망도—얻을 가망이나 가능성이 아주 없다고 여긴다면 비참해질 그런 두각이 정말 있긴 있어요—하지만 그건 결이 다른 것이랍니다.”\n\n그가 말하는 사이의 그 뜻있는 낯빛, 그리고 크로퍼드 양이 무언가 웃음 섞인 대답을 하며 지은 뜻있는 듯한 태도는, 패니가 지켜보기에 서글픈 먹이였다. 그리고 이제 그랜트 부인 곁에 붙어 다른 이들을 뒤따르며, 그 부인에게 마땅히 기울여야 할 만큼 주의를 도무지 기울일 수 없음을 깨닫자, 그녀는 곧장 집으로 가기로 거의 마음먹고, 그 말을 꺼낼 용기만 기다리고 있었는데, 그때 맨스필드 파크의 큰 시계가 세 시를 치는 소리에, 자기가 정말이지 여느 때보다 훨씬 오래 나와 있었음을 느꼈고, 지금 작별을 고할지 말지, 또 어떻게 할지 하는 앞서의 자문이 아주 빠르게 매듭지어졌다. 조금의 망설임도 없는 결단으로 그녀는 곧바로 작별 인사를 시작했다. 그리고 에드먼드도 그때 어머니가 그녀를 찾던 것을, 그리고 자기가 바로 그녀를 데려오려고 목사관으로 걸어 내려왔음을 떠올리기 시작했다.\n\n패니의 서두름이 커졌다. 그리고 에드먼드가 함께해 주리라곤 조금도 기대하지 않은 채, 그녀는 혼자 서둘러 떠나려 했다. 그러나 다들 걸음을 재촉해, 지나가야만 하는 그 집 안으로 그녀와 함께 들어갔다. 그랜트 박사가 현관홀에 있었고, 그에게 인사하려 걸음을 멈춘 사이 그녀는 에드먼드의 태도로 미루어 그가 정말로 저와 함께 갈 작정임을 알았다. 그도 작별을 고하던 참이었다. 그녀는 고맙지 않을 수 없었다. 헤어지려는 순간, 에드먼드는 그랜트 박사에게서 이튿날 함께 양고기를 들자는 청을 받았다. 그리고 패니가 그 일로 언짢은 기분에 잠길 겨를도 없이, 그랜트 부인이 문득 떠오른 듯 그녀에게 돌아서서 그녀도 함께해 주는 기쁨을 청했다. 이것은 너무나 새로운 마음씀이요, 패니의 삶에서 일어난 일들 가운데 너무나 온전히 새로운 일이라, 그녀는 온통 놀랍고 겸연쩍었다. 그리고 크나큰 고마움과, “하지만 제가 그럴 수 있을 것 같지 않은데요” 하는 말을 더듬더듬 내놓는 사이, 그녀는 에드먼드의 의견과 도움을 구하려 그를 바라보고 있었다. 그러나 에드먼드는 그녀에게 그런 행복이 주어진 것을 반기며, 반쯤의 눈짓과 반쯤의 말로 그녀가 이모 때문 말고는 반대할 것이 없음을 확인하고서, 어머니가 그녀를 내주는 데 무슨 어려움을 두시리라곤 상상할 수 없어, 그 청을 받아들이라는 제 뜻을 분명하고 거리낌 없이 밝혔다. 그리고 패니는, 그의 북돋움에도 그런 대담하고 자유로운 비약에는 감히 나서지 못했으나, 딴말이 들려오지 않는 한 그랜트 부인이 그녀를 기다려도 좋다는 것으로 이내 매듭지어졌다.\n\n“저녁으로 뭘 드시게 될지 아시겠죠,” 그랜트 부인이 미소 지으며 말했다—“칠면조예요, 게다가 정말 아주 좋은 놈이랍니다. 여보,” 하며 남편에게 돌아서서, “요리사가 칠면조는 내일 손질해야 한다고 우기거든요.”\n\n“좋아, 좋아,” 그랜트 박사가 외쳤다. “그럴수록 좋지. 집에 그렇게 좋은 게 있다니 반갑구려. 하지만 프라이스 양하고 에드먼드 버트럼 씨야, 아마 뭐가 나오든 마다 않을 거요. 우린 아무도 차림표를 듣고 싶지 않아요. 정다운 모임이지, 훌륭한 만찬이 아니라, 그게 우리가 바라는 전부니까. 칠면조든 거위든 양 다리든, 당신하고 요리사가 뭘 주든 말이오.”\n\n두 사촌은 함께 집으로 걸어갔다. 그리고 에드먼드가 이 초대를—그토록 즐겁게 이뤄지는 것을 지켜본 그 친밀함 속에서 그녀에게 그토록 각별히 바람직한 일이라며 더없이 뜨거운 흡족함으로 이야기한—당장 두고 이야기한 것 말고는, 말 없는 걸음이었다. 그 화제를 끝내고 나자 그가 생각에 잠겨 다른 어떤 이야기도 내키지 않아 했기 때문이다.\n\n# 제23장\n\n“그런데 그랜트 부인이 어째서 패니를 부른다니?” 버트럼 부인이 말했다. “어쩌다 패니를 부를 생각을 했을까? 패니는 이런 식으로 거기서 만찬을 든 적이 없잖니. 나는 이 애 없이는 못 지내고, 이 애도 분명 가고 싶어 하지 않을 게다. 패니, 넌 가고 싶지 않지, 그렇지?”\n\n“그렇게 물으시면,” 사촌이 말하려는 것을 앞질러 에드먼드가 외쳤다. “패니는 곧바로 아니라고 할 거예요. 하지만 어머니, 저는 이 애가 가고 싶어 하리라고 확신해요. 그리고 못 갈 까닭이 하나도 없다고 봐요.”\n\n“그랜트 부인이 어째서 이 애를 부를 생각을 했는지 도무지 모르겠구나. 전엔 한 번도 안 그랬는데. 이따금 네 누이들은 불렀어도, 패니는 결코 안 불렀잖니.”\n\n“이모가 저 없이 못 지내신다면—” 패니가 저를 내려놓는 투로 말했다.\n\n“하지만 어머닌 저녁 내내 아버지가 곁에 계실 거잖아요.”\n\n“하기야, 그렇겠구나.”\n\n“아버지 의견을 여쭤보시죠, 어머니.”\n\n“그거 좋은 생각이구나. 그러마, 에드먼드. 토머스 경이 들어오는 대로, 이 애 없이 내가 지낼 수 있을지 여쭤봐야겠다.”\n\n“그 점이야 어머니 마음대로 하세요. 하지만 제가 말한 건 이 초대를 받아들이는 게 온당한지 아버지 의견을 여쭤보시란 거였어요. 그리고 저는 아버지가, 패니한테도 그랜트 부인한테도, 이게 첫 초대인 만큼 받아들이는 게 옳은 일이라 여기실 거라고 봐요.”\n\n“글쎄다. 여쭤보자꾸나. 하지만 그랜트 부인이 패니를 부른다는 것 자체에 아주 놀라실 게다.”\n\n토머스 경이 있을 때까지는 더 할 말도, 무슨 소용 있게 할 수 있는 말도 없었다. 그러나 그 화제는, 내일 저녁 제 편안함이 걸린 일인지라, 버트럼 부인의 마음에 어찌나 크게 자리 잡았던지, 반 시간 뒤 그가 농원에서 옷방으로 가는 길에 잠깐 들여다보자, 그가 거의 문을 닫으려는 참에 그녀가 그를 도로 불러 세웠다. “토머스 경, 잠깐만요—드릴 말씀이 있어요.”\n\n그 차분하고 나른한 어조는—그녀는 결코 목소리를 높이는 수고를 하지 않았으므로—늘 들리고 새겨졌다. 그리하여 토머스 경은 돌아왔다. 그녀의 이야기가 시작되었고, 패니는 곧바로 방을 빠져나갔다. 자기가 삼촌과 무슨 의논거리가 되는 것을 듣는 일은 그 신경으로는 견딜 수 없는 것이었기 때문이다. 그녀는 마음이 졸였다—어쩌면 마땅한 것보다 더 졸였다—따지고 보면 자기가 가든 남든 그게 무슨 대수랴? 그러나 만일 삼촌이 오래오래 곰곰이 따지고 정하며, 그것도 아주 근엄한 낯빛으로, 그 근엄한 눈길을 저에게 두고 있다가, 끝내 저에게 불리하게 정한다면, 그녀는 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 고분고분하고 무심한 낯을 지어 보이지 못할지도 몰랐다. 그러는 사이 그녀의 청은 잘 굴러가고 있었다. 그것은 버트럼 부인 쪽에서 이렇게 시작되었다—“당신을 놀라게 할 얘기가 하나 있어요. 그랜트 부인이 패니를 만찬에 불렀지 뭐예요.”\n\n“그래서?” 토머스 경이, 그 놀라움을 더 채우기를 기다리기라도 하는 듯 말했다.\n\n“에드먼드는 이 애가 가기를 바라요. 그런데 내가 어떻게 이 애 없이 지내죠?”\n\n“늦겠구려.” 토머스 경이 시계를 꺼내며 말했다. “그런데 무엇이 곤란하다는 거요?”\n\n에드먼드는 어머니 이야기의 빈 데를 메우려 나서 말할 수밖에 없음을 알았다. 그가 전부를 이야기했고, 그녀는 그저 이렇게 덧붙일 뿐이었다. “참 이상하죠! 그랜트 부인이 이 애를 부른 적이 없었는데.”\n\n“하지만 아주 자연스러운 일 아닌가요,” 에드먼드가 말했다. “그랜트 부인이 제 아우를 위해 그토록 반가운 손님을 마련해 주고 싶어 하는 것이요?”\n\n“그보다 더 자연스러운 일도 없지.” 잠깐 헤아린 뒤 토머스 경이 말했다. “설령 이 일에 아우가 없다 한들, 내 보기엔 그보다 더 자연스러운 일도 없을 거요. 그랜트 부인이 프라이스 양한테, 버트럼 부인의 조카딸한테 예의를 차리는 데야 무슨 설명이 필요하겠소. 내가 놀랄 만한 건 오직, 이게 그걸 차린 첫 번째라는 거지. 패니가 조건부 대답만 한 건 아주 옳았소. 이 애는 마땅한 대로 느끼는 모양이오. 하지만 젊은이들이란 다 함께 어울리기를 좋아하는 법이니 이 애도 가고 싶어 하는 게 틀림없다고 나는 보오. 그러니 이 즐거움을 마다케 할 까닭이 하나도 없구려.”\n\n“하지만 내가 이 애 없이 지낼 수 있을까요, 토머스 경?”\n\n“정말이지 지낼 수 있을 거요.”\n\n“아우가 여기 없을 땐 늘 이 애가 차를 내잖아요.”\n\n“아우님더러 우리와 하루 보내자고 청하면 응할지도 모르고, 나도 분명 집에 있을 테니.”\n\n“그럼 됐네요, 패니는 가도 돼요, 에드먼드.”\n\n그 반가운 소식이 이내 그녀를 뒤따랐다. 에드먼드가 제 방으로 가는 길에 그녀의 문을 두드린 것이다.\n\n“자, 패니, 다 잘 정해졌어. 삼촌 쪽에서 조금의 망설임도 없이 말이야. 삼촌은 오직 한 가지 의견뿐이셨어. 넌 가게 됐다.”\n\n“고마워요, 정말 너무 기뻐요.”가 패니의 절로 나온 대답이었다. 그러나 그에게서 돌아서서 문을 닫고 나자, 그녀는 이런 생각을 떨칠 수 없었다. “그런데 왜 기뻐하지? 거기서 나를 아프게 할 무언가를 보거나 듣게 될 게 뻔하지 않나?”\n\n그러나 이런 확신에도 불구하고 그녀는 기뻤다. 그런 약속이 남들 눈에는 아무리 대수롭잖아 보일지언정, 그녀의 눈에는 새롭고 중대했다. 소더턴에서의 그날 말고는 여태 바깥에서 만찬을 든 적이 거의 없었기 때문이다. 그리고 이제 겨우 반 마일을 가는 것이고, 겨우 세 사람한테 가는 것이었으나, 그래도 그것은 바깥 만찬이었고, 채비의 온갖 자잘한 재미가 그 자체로 즐거움이었다. 그녀의 감정에 마땅히 함께해 주고 그 취향을 이끌어 주었어야 할 이들에게서는 공감도 도움도 없었다. 버트럼 부인이야 누구에게든 쓸모가 될 생각을 결코 하지 않았고, 노리스 부인은, 이튿날 토머스 경의 이른 부름과 초대로 왔을 때, 심사가 아주 뒤틀린 채, 오직 제 조카딸의 즐거움을, 지금 것이든 앞으로의 것이든, 되도록 깎아내리는 데만 골몰한 듯했다.\n\n“정말이지, 패니, 넌 이런 마음씀과 은혜를 입다니 팔자가 늘어졌구나! 너를 생각해 준 그랜트 부인한테도, 너를 보내 주는 이모한테도 아주 고맙게 여겨야 마땅하고, 이걸 무슨 대단한 일로 여겨야 해. 왜냐하면 나는 네가 이런 식으로 남들 자리에 끼거나, 애당초 바깥 만찬을 들거나 할 진짜 까닭은 하나도 없다는 걸 잘 알고 있기를 바라거든. 그리고 이건 앞으로 또 되풀이되리라 기대해서도 안 되는 일이야. 또 이 초대가 너한테 무슨 각별한 찬사로 뜻해진 거라 넘겨짚어서도 안 돼. 그 찬사는 네 삼촌하고 이모하고 나한테 뜻해진 거니까. 그랜트 부인은 너를 좀 챙기는 게 우리한테 마땅한 예의라 여긴 거고, 그렇잖았으면 애당초 그런 생각도 안 떠올랐을 게다. 그리고 네 사촌 줄리아가 집에 있었더라면 넌 애당초 안 불렸을 게 아주 분명하고 말이지.”\n\n노리스 부인이 이제 그랜트 부인 몫의 호의를 어찌나 감쪽같이 지워 버렸던지, 무슨 말이든 해야 할 것 같던 패니는, 그저 저를 내주신 버트럼 이모한테 아주 고맙다는 것과, 이모의 저녁 일감을 저 없어도 아쉽잖게끔 미리 손봐 두려 애쓰고 있다는 말밖에 할 수 없었다.\n\n“아! 걱정 마라, 네 이모야 너 없이도 아주 잘 지내지, 안 그랬으면 널 보내 주지도 않았을 게다. 내가 여기 있을 테니, 이모 걱정일랑 아주 놓으렴. 그리고 나는 네가 아주 즐거운 하루를 보내고, 그 모든 게 무척 재미나기를 바란다. 하지만 나는 한마디 안 할 수가 없구나, 다섯이란 식탁에 둘러앉기엔 있을 수 있는 수 중에 가장 어정쩡한 수라는 거 말이다. 그리고 그랜트 부인처럼 우아한 부인이 어째서 더 잘 헤아리지 못했는지 놀랍지 않을 수가 없어! 게다가 그 방을 지독히도 꽉 채우는 그 엄청나게 크고 널찍한 식탁을 빙 둘러서라니! 그 박사가 내가 떠나올 때, 제정신인 사람이라면 누구나 그랬을 것처럼, 내 식탁을 가져갔더라면—그 터무니없는 제 새 것, 여기 식탁보다 넓은, 말 그대로 넓은 그것 대신에 말이야—얼마나 한없이 나았을까! 그리고 얼마나 더 존경받았을까! 사람이란 제 마땅한 분수를 벗어나면 결코 존경받는 법이 없거든. 그걸 명심해라, 패니. 다섯이라니—고작 다섯이 그 식탁을 둘러앉다니. 하기야 그 위엔 열 사람 몫 만찬이 차려질 게다, 아마.”\n\n노리스 부인은 숨을 돌리고 다시 말을 이었다.\n\n“사람들이 제 신분을 벗어나 제 분수 위로 올라 보이려 드는 그 어리석고 미련한 짓을 보면, 나는 너한테 한마디 일러 주는 게 옳다 싶구나, 패니, 이제 네가 우리 중 아무도 없이 남들 자리에 끼러 가는 마당이니 말이다. 그리고 나는 네가 나서지 말고, 마치 네 사촌들 중 하나인 양—마치 사랑하는 러시워스 부인이나 줄리아인 양—이러쿵저러쿵 네 의견을 늘어놓지 말기를 간곡히 빌고 또 빈다. 그런 짓은 결코 안 될 말이야, 내 말 믿어라. 명심해, 네가 어디 있든, 넌 가장 낮고 가장 마지막이어야 해. 그리고 크로퍼드 양이야 목사관에서 어느 모로 제 집이나 다름없다지만, 넌 그녀보다 앞자리를 차지해선 안 돼. 그리고 밤에 돌아오는 것으로 말하면, 넌 에드먼드가 정하는 딱 그만큼만 있어야 해. 그건 그 애더러 정하게 두렴.”\n\n“네, 이모, 저야 달리 생각도 안 해요.”\n\n“그리고 만일 비가 온다면, 나는 그럴 가망이 아주 크다고 보는데, 내 평생 비 오는 저녁 낌새가 이보다 더한 걸 본 적이 없거든—넌 되도록 알아서 하고, 마차를 보내 주려니 기대해선 안 된다. 나는 오늘 밤 분명 집에 안 가니까, 마차가 나 때문에 나갈 일은 없어. 그러니 넌 무슨 일이 생길지 각오하고, 거기 맞춰 채비를 하렴.”\n\n그녀의 조카딸은 그것이 아주 지당하다 여겼다. 제가 누릴 편안함에 대한 요구를 그녀는 노리스 부인 못지않게 낮게 매겼다. 그리고 이내 토머스 경이 문을 막 열며 “패니, 마차를 몇 시에 대령하면 좋겠느냐?” 하고 말하자, 그녀는 도무지 말이 안 나올 만큼 놀랐다.\n\n“아이참, 토머스 경!” 노리스 부인이 노기로 벌게져 외쳤다. “패니는 걸어가면 돼요.”\n\n“걸어가?” 토머스 경이 도무지 대꾸할 수 없는 위엄의 어조로 되뇌며, 방 안으로 좀 더 들어섰다. “내 조카딸이 이맘때 만찬 약속에 걸어가다니! 네 시 이십 분이면 되겠느냐?”\n\n“네, 삼촌.”이 패니의 겸손한 대답이었는데, 노리스 부인에게는 거의 죄인 같은 심정으로 건네진 것이었다. 그리고 승리한 것처럼 보일 만한 상태로 그녀와 함께 남아 있기가 괴로워, 그녀는 삼촌을 따라 방을 나갔으니, 노기에 찬 동요 속에 이런 말이 오가는 것을 들을 만큼만 그의 뒤에 머물렀다—\n\n“도무지 필요 없는 일인데! 지나치게 친절하시지! 하지만 에드먼드가 가니까, 그래, 에드먼드 때문이지. 목요일 밤에 그 애가 목이 쉰 걸 내가 봤거든.”\n\n그러나 이것은 패니를 속일 수 없었다. 그녀는 그 마차가 저를 위한, 오직 저 하나를 위한 것임을 느꼈다. 그리고 이모의 그런 말들 바로 뒤에 이어진 삼촌의 그 마음씀은, 홀로 있을 때 그녀에게서 고마움의 눈물 몇 방울을 자아냈다.\n\n마부는 딱 시간 맞춰 마차를 몰아 왔다. 또 일 분 만에 신사가 내려왔다. 그리고 그 숙녀는 늦을까 봐 더없이 노심초사하며 벌써 몇 분이나 응접실에 앉아 있던 터라, 토머스 경은 제 정확히 시간 지키는 버릇이 요구하는 만큼 넉넉한 때에 그들을 배웅했다.\n\n“자, 이제 널 좀 봐야겠다, 패니.” 에드먼드가 정 많은 오라비의 친절한 미소로 말했다. “그리고 네가 얼마나 마음에 드는지 말해 줘야지. 이 불빛으로 내가 판단할 수 있는 한, 넌 정말이지 아주 곱구나. 뭘 입은 거니?”\n\n“사촌 언니 혼인 때 삼촌이 고맙게도 주신 그 새 옷이에요. 너무 화려하진 않았으면 좋겠는데, 되도록 빨리 입어야겠다 싶었어요, 겨우내 이런 기회가 또 없을 것 같아서요. 저를 너무 화려하다 여기진 않으셨으면 해요.”\n\n“온통 하양으로 차려입은 여자는 결코 지나치게 화려할 수 없어. 아니, 네게 화려한 데라곤 안 보이는걸. 더없이 온당한 것 말고는 아무것도 없어. 네 가운이 아주 예뻐 보인다. 이 반들거리는 점무늬가 마음에 들어. 크로퍼드 양한테도 이거하고 좀 비슷한 가운이 있지 않니?”\n\n목사관에 다가가며 그들은 마구간 마당과 마차 창고 바로 옆을 지났다.\n\n“이런!” 에드먼드가 말했다. “손님이 있네, 마차가 있어! 우릴 맞으러 누굴 데려왔지?” 그러고는 알아보려 옆 유리창을 내리며, “크로퍼드 거야, 크로퍼드의 사륜 포장마차라니까! 그 사람네 하인 둘이 그걸 제자리로 도로 밀어 넣고 있어. 물론 그 사람이 온 거지. 이거 뜻밖인걸, 패니. 나야 그 사람을 보게 돼 아주 반가워.”\n\n자기가 얼마나 아주 다르게 느끼는지 패니가 말할 겨를도, 말할 짬도 없었다. 그러나 저를 지켜볼 그런 사람이 또 하나 있다는 생각은, 응접실로 걸어 들어가는 그 더없이 두려운 의식을 치르는 그녀의 떨림을 크게 키웠다.\n\n응접실에 크로퍼드 씨는 과연 있었으니, 만찬을 들 채비가 될 만큼만 딱 방금 도착한 참이었다. 그리고 그를 둘러싼 나머지 세 사람의 미소와 흐뭇한 낯빛은, 배스를 떠나며 며칠 그들에게 오기로 한 그의 갑작스러운 결심이 얼마나 반가운지 여실히 드러냈다. 그와 에드먼드 사이에 아주 정다운 인사가 오갔다. 그리고 패니만 빼고 그 기쁨은 두루 퍼진 것이었다. 그리고 그녀에게조차 그가 온 것에 얼마간 이로운 점이 있었을지 모르니, 무리에 사람이 하나 더 늘 때마다 오히려, 잠자코 아무도 챙기지 않는 채로 앉아 있게 두어지는 그 애지중지하는 즐거움이 더 앞당겨졌기 때문이다. 그녀 자신도 이내 그것을 알아챘다. 노리스 이모의 의견에도 불구하고, 제 마음의 온당함이 이끄는 대로, 그 자리의 주된 숙녀가 되는 것과 그에 따르는 온갖 자잘한 대접에 따라야 했으나, 식탁에 앉아 있는 동안 그녀가 낄 필요가 없는 그토록 흐뭇한 이야기의 흐름이 오가는 것을 알았기 때문이다—남매 사이엔 배스를 두고 할 이야기가 그토록 많았고, 두 젊은 사내 사이엔 사냥을 두고 그토록 많았으며, 크로퍼드 씨와 그랜트 박사 사이엔 정치 이야기가 그토록 많았고, 크로퍼드 씨와 그랜트 부인 사이엔 온갖 것과 모든 것이 뒤섞여 그토록 많았으니, 그녀에게는 그저 조용히 듣기만 하며 아주 즐거운 하루를 보낼 더없이 좋은 형편이 남겨진 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 새로 도착한 그 신사에게, 맨스필드 체류를 늘리고 노퍽에서 제 사냥말들을 불러오라는 계획에 대해 무슨 관심 있는 낯빛으로도 맞장구쳐 줄 수가 없었다. 그랜트 박사가 내놓고 에드먼드가 권하고 두 자매가 뜨겁게 다그친 그 계획은 이내 그의 마음을 사로잡았고, 그는 그녀에게서까지 마음을 굳히도록 부추김을 받고 싶어 하는 듯했다. 날씨가 계속 맑을 가망을 두고 그녀의 의견이 구해졌으나, 그녀의 대답은 예의가 허락하는 한 짧고 무심했다. 그녀는 그가 머물기를 바랄 수 없었고, 차라리 그가 저에게 말을 걸지 않았으면 하는 마음이 훨씬 컸다.\n\n그를 보니 떠나 있는 두 사촌이, 특히 마리아가 그녀 마음에 많이 떠올랐다. 그러나 그 어떤 겸연쩍은 기억도 그의 기분을 건드리지 않았다. 여기 그가 다시, 그 모든 일이 벌어졌던 바로 그 자리에 와 있었고, 마치 맨스필드를 다른 어떤 상태로도 알아 본 적이 없다는 듯, 버트럼 자매 없이도 기꺼이 머물러 행복해하는 듯했다. 그녀는 그가 그들을 그저 두루뭉술하게 입에 올리는 것만 듣다가, 다들 응접실에 다시 모였을 때—에드먼드가 그랜트 박사와 무슨 볼일로 따로 떨어져, 그것에 온통 골몰한 듯 보이고, 그랜트 부인은 찻상에 매여 있을 때—그가 다른 누이에게 그들을 좀 더 자세히 이야기하기 시작하는 것을 들었다. 패니가 그를 여간 밉지 않게 만드는 뜻있는 미소를 지으며, 그가 말했다. “그래! 러시워스와 그 어여쁜 신부가 브라이턴에 가 있다면서, 복 많은 사내지!”\n\n“네, 거기 간 지 두 주쯤 됐죠, 프라이스 양, 안 그래요? 그리고 줄리아도 같이 있고요.”\n\n“그리고 예이츠 씨도, 짐작건대 멀지 않은 데 있겠지.”\n\n“예이츠 씨라니! 아! 우린 예이츠 씨 소식은 하나도 못 들어요. 그 사람이 맨스필드 파크로 오는 편지에 자주 오르내리진 않을걸요. 안 그래요, 프라이스 양? 내 벗 줄리아가 예이츠 씨 얘기로 제 아버지를 즐겁게 해 드릴 만큼 철없진 않을 것 같은데.”\n\n“딱한 러시워스와 그 마흔두 마디 대사라니!” 크로퍼드가 말을 이었다. “아무도 그걸 잊을 수 없을 거야. 딱한 친구! 지금도 눈에 선해—그 안간힘과 그 절망 말이야. 뭐, 그 사랑스러운 마리아가 그더러 저한테 마흔두 마디 대사를 늘어놓아 달라 바랄 일은 결코 없으리라는 데, 내가 크게 틀리지 않을 거야.” 하며 그는 한순간 진지해져 덧붙였다. “그 여잔 그 사내한테 과분해—너무나 과분하지.” 그러고는 다시 어조를 부드러운 정중함으로 바꾸어, 패니에게 향해 말했다. “프라이스 양이 러시워스 씨의 가장 좋은 벗이었죠. 양의 친절과 참을성은 결코 잊힐 수 없을 거예요, 그가 제 배역을 익힐 수 있게 만들어 주려던 그 지칠 줄 모르는 참을성 말이에요—자연이 안 준 두뇌를 그에게 주려던, 양 자신의 넘치는 데서 이해력을 덜어 그에게 섞어 주려던 그 참을성이요! 그 사람이야 제 딴엔 양의 친절을 헤아릴 분별이 없었을지 몰라도, 나머지 모두한테선 그게 기림을 받았다고 나는 감히 말할 수 있어요.”\n\n패니는 얼굴을 붉히고 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n“그건 한 편의 꿈, 즐거운 꿈 같아요!” 몇 분 생각에 잠겼다가 그가 다시 터뜨리듯 외쳤다. “나는 우리 연극을 늘 더없는 즐거움으로 돌아보게 될 거예요. 그런 흥, 그런 활기, 그런 기운이 두루 퍼졌었죠. 다들 그걸 느꼈어요. 우린 다 살아 있었고요. 하루의 매시간마다 할 일이, 희망이, 마음 졸임이, 부산함이 있었어요. 늘 넘어야 할 자그마한 걸림돌이, 자그마한 미심쩍음이, 자그마한 근심이 있었죠. 나는 그때보다 더 행복한 적이 없어요.”\n\n말없는 분함으로 패니는 속으로 되뇌었다. “더 행복한 적이 없다니!—떳떳잖은 줄 뻔히 알았을 짓을 하면서보다 더 행복한 적이 없다니!—그토록 부끄럽고 무정하게 굴면서보다 더 행복한 적이 없다니! 아! 어쩜 저리 썩어 빠진 마음일까!”\n\n“우린 운이 나빴어요, 프라이스 양.” 그가, 에드먼드에게 들릴세라 좀 더 목소리를 낮추어, 그리고 그녀의 심경은 조금도 알아채지 못한 채 말을 이었다. “우린 분명 아주 운이 나빴죠. 한 주만 더, 딱 한 주만 더 있었어도 우리한텐 넉넉했을 거예요. 내 생각엔, 우리가 일들을 마음대로 할 수 있었더라면—맨스필드 파크가 딱 한두 주, 주야평분 무렵의 바람을 다스릴 수 있었더라면, 사정이 달랐을 거예요. 무슨 무시무시한 날씨로 그분 안전을 위태롭게 하자는 게 아니라—그저 한결같은 맞바람이나 잔잔한 바람으로요. 내 생각엔, 프라이스 양, 우린 그 철에 대서양에서 한 주쯤 잔잔한 바람을 누렸을 거예요.”\n\n그는 기어이 대답을 들을 작정인 듯했다. 그리고 패니는 얼굴을 돌린 채, 여느 때보다 단단한 어조로 말했다. “저로 말하면요, 씨, 저는 그분의 귀환을 하루도 늦추지 않았을 거예요. 삼촌이 도착하셨을 때 그 모든 걸 어찌나 온통 못마땅해하셨던지, 제 생각엔 모든 게 이미 갈 데까지 충분히 갔던걸요.”\n\n그녀가 평생 그에게 한 번에 그토록 많이 말한 적이 없었고, 누구에게든 그토록 성나게 말한 적도 없었다. 그리고 제 말이 끝나자 그녀는 제 대담함에 떨며 얼굴을 붉혔다. 그는 놀랐다. 그러나 몇 순간 말없이 그녀를 헤아리더니, 좀 더 차분하고 진중한 어조로, 마치 확신에서 우러난 솔직한 결론인 양 대답했다. “양 말이 옳은 것 같아요. 슬기롭기보다 즐거운 쪽이었죠. 우린 너무 시끄러워지고 있었어요.” 그러고는 화제를 돌려, 그녀를 다른 이야기에 끌어들이려 했으나, 그녀의 대답이 어찌나 수줍고 마지못한 것이던지 그는 어느 쪽으로도 나아갈 수 없었다.\n\n그랜트 박사와 에드먼드를 거듭 눈여겨보던 크로퍼드 양이, 이제 소견을 냈다. “저 신사분들은 뭔가 아주 흥미로운 걸 의논하고 있나 봐요.”\n\n“세상에서 가장 흥미로운 거지.” 그 오라비가 대답했다. “돈 버는 법, 좋은 수입을 더 나은 수입으로 굴리는 법 말이야. 그랜트 박사가 버트럼한테, 곧 물려받을 그 성직록에 대해 일러 주고 있는 거야. 알아보니 몇 주 뒤면 서품을 받는다더군. 식당에서도 그 얘길 하고 있더라니까. 버트럼이 그리 넉넉해진다니 반갑구먼. 물수제비 뜨듯 펑펑 써도 될 아주 짭짤한 수입을, 그것도 별 수고 없이 얻는 셈이니까. 내 짐작엔 연 칠백 파운드는 못 되진 않을 거야. 연 칠백 파운드면 작은아들한테야 근사한 거지. 그리고 물론 그는 여전히 집에서 지낼 테니, 그게 죄다 제 자잘한 즐길거리에 쓰일 테고. 성탄과 부활절에 설교 한 번씩, 그게 아마 바치는 것의 전부일 거야.”\n\n그 누이는 이렇게 말하며 제 감정을 웃어넘기려 했다. “저마다 저보다 훨씬 덜 가진 이들의 넉넉함을 태평하게 셈해 대는 그 태도만큼 나를 웃기는 것도 없어. 오빠도 좀 난감한 낯빛이 될걸, 오빠 자잘한 즐길거리가 연 칠백 파운드로 묶인다면 말이야.”\n\n“그럴지도 모르지. 하지만 그거야 다 알다시피 순전히 견주기 나름이야. 타고난 권리와 버릇이 그걸 정하는 거지. 버트럼은 준남작 집안의 차남치고도 분명 넉넉한 형편이야. 스물네댓 살이 될 무렵이면 연 칠백 파운드가 생기고, 그걸 위해 할 일이라곤 아무것도 없을 테니.”\n\n크로퍼드 양은, 그것을 위해 하고 견뎌야 할 무언가가 있으리라고, 그리고 그것을 자기는 가볍게 여길 수 없다고 말할 수도 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 스스로를 다잡아 그냥 넘겼다. 그리고 이내 두 신사가 그들에게 합류하자 차분하고 무심한 낯을 지으려 애썼다.\n\n“버트럼,” 헨리 크로퍼드가 말했다. “나는 자네 첫 설교를 들으러 꼭 맨스필드에 오겠네. 풋내기를 북돋아 주려고 일부러 올 거야. 언제인가? 프라이스 양, 양도 나와 함께 사촌을 북돋아 주지 않겠어요? 그 내내 그에게 눈을 지그시 박고—나처럼 말이에요—한마디도 놓치지 않기로 약속하지 않겠어요? 아니면 유난히 아름다운 문장을 받아 적으려 잠깐 눈을 떼는 것만 빼고요? 우리 메모판이랑 연필을 챙겨 갑시다. 언제인가? 토머스 경과 버트럼 부인이 들으실 수 있게, 자네 맨스필드에서 설교해야 하네, 알겠나.”\n\n“나는 되도록 오래 자네를 피할 걸세, 크로퍼드.” 에드먼드가 말했다. “자네야말로 나를 당황케 하기 십상일 테고, 나는 다른 거의 누구보다도 자네가 그러려 드는 걸 보는 게 더 싫을 테니까.”\n\n“저이는 이걸 못 느낄까?” 패니는 생각했다. “아니, 저이는 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 아무것도 못 느끼지.”\n\n이제 무리가 다 모이고, 말 많은 이들끼리 서로 끌리니, 그녀는 평온 속에 남았다. 그리고 차를 마신 뒤 휘스트 판이 벌어지자—겉으론 그리 안 보였으나 실은 세심한 아내가 그랜트 박사를 즐겁게 해 주려 벌인 판이었다—크로퍼드 양이 제 하프를 잡으니, 그녀는 그저 듣기만 하면 되었다. 그리고 그날 저녁 내내 그녀의 평온은 흐트러지지 않았으니, 다만 크로퍼드 씨가 이따금 그녀에게 물음이나 소견을 건네어 그녀가 대답하지 않을 수 없을 때만 빼고서였다. 크로퍼드 양은 그동안 있었던 일에 너무 속이 상해 음악 말고는 아무것도 할 기분이 아니었다. 그녀는 그것으로 저를 달래고 제 벗을 즐겁게 했다.\n\n에드먼드가 그토록 곧 서품을 받으리라는 그 확실한 소식은, 미뤄져 있던, 그러면서 여전히 불확실하고 먼 것이기를 바라던 일격처럼 그녀에게 닥쳐, 분함과 무안함으로 느껴졌다. 그녀는 그에게 몹시 화가 났다. 그녀는 제 영향력이 더 크다 여겼었다. 그녀는 정말로 그를 마음에 두기 시작했었다. 자기가 그러기 시작했음을, 크나큰 정으로, 거의 마음을 굳힌 뜻으로 그랬음을 그녀는 느꼈다. 그러나 이제 그녀는 그의 그 싸늘한 감정으로 그를 맞을 참이었다. 자기가 결코 몸 굽혀 받아들이지 않으리란 걸 뻔히 알 그런 처지에 스스로를 붙박음으로써, 그가 아무런 진지한 뜻도, 참된 정도 품을 수 없음이 분명했다. 그녀는 그의 무심함에 맞먹게끔 배우려 했다. 이제부터 그녀는 당장의 재미 말고는 아무 생각 없이 그의 살가움을 받아들이려 했다. 만일 그가 그토록 제 정을 다스릴 수 있다면, 그녀의 정도 그녀를 해쳐선 안 되었다.\n\n# 제24장\n\n헨리 크로퍼드는 이튿날 아침이면 벌써 맨스필드에 두 주를 더 보내기로 마음을 아주 굳혔다. 그리고 제 사냥말들을 부르러 사람을 보내고, 제독에게 몇 줄 해명을 적어 봉인해 편지를 던져 놓고는, 나머지 식구가 물러난 것을 보고 누이 쪽을 돌아보며 미소 띤 얼굴로 말했다. “그래, 사냥 안 하는 날에 내가 뭘 하며 지낼 작정인 것 같아, 메리? 나야 이제 한 주에 세 번 넘게 나가기엔 너무 늙었거든. 그런데 그 사이사이 날들을 위한 계획이 하나 있는데, 그게 뭘 것 같아?”\n\n“나랑 걷고 말 타는 거겠지, 뻔하잖아.”\n\n“그건 아니야, 물론 둘 다 기꺼이 하겠지만. 하지만 그거야 내 몸에만 운동이 될 뿐이고, 나는 내 마음도 돌봐야 하거든. 게다가 그거야 죄다 놀이요 방종일 뿐, 노동이라는 이로운 섞임이 없잖아. 나는 게으름의 빵을 먹기는 싫어. 아니지, 내 계획은 패니 프라이스가 나한테 반하게 만드는 거야.”\n\n“패니 프라이스라니! 말도 안 돼! 안 돼, 안 돼. 오빤 그 사촌 둘로 만족해야지.”\n\n“하지만 나는 패니 프라이스 없인 만족 못 해, 패니 프라이스 마음에 자그마한 구멍 하나 못 내고는 말이야. 넌 그 애가 눈여겨볼 만하다는 걸 제대로 모르는 것 같더구나. 어젯밤 그 애 얘길 할 때, 너희 중 누구도 지난 육 주 새 그 애 얼굴에 일어난 그 놀라운 변화를 못 알아채는 것 같던데. 넌 그 앨 날마다 보니까 못 알아채는 거야. 하지만 정말이지 그 앤 가을과는 아주 딴사람이야. 그때야 그저 얌전하고 조신한, 못생기진 않은 계집애였지만, 지금은 정말이지 예뻐. 나는 그 애가 얼굴빛도 낯빛도 없다 여겼었어. 그런데 어제처럼 그토록 자주 홍조가 어리는 그 보드라운 살결엔 분명한 아름다움이 있고, 내가 살펴본 그 애 눈과 입으로 미루어, 뭔가 드러낼 게 있을 땐 넉넉히 표현해 낼 만하리라는 걸 나는 의심치 않아. 게다가 그 애 분위기며 몸가짐이며 전체 인상이 이루 말할 수 없이 나아졌어! 시월 이후로 적어도 두 인치는 컸을 거야.”\n\n“쳇, 쳇! 그건 그저 견줄 만한 키 큰 여자가 없었던 데다, 그 애가 새 가운을 얻어서, 오빠가 그렇게 잘 차려입은 걸 전엔 못 봤기 때문일 뿐이야. 그 앤 시월 그대로라니까, 내 말 믿어. 사실인즉, 그 애가 오빠 눈에 띌 유일한 계집애였고, 오빤 누군가는 있어야 하는 사람이거든. 나야 늘 그 애가 예쁘다 여겼어—눈에 확 띄게 예쁜 건 아니지만—사람들 말마따나 ‘그만하면 예쁜’, 두고 볼수록 정드는 그런 아름다움 말이야. 눈이 좀 더 짙었으면 싶지만, 미소가 참하지. 그런데 이 놀라운 변화로 말하면, 그건 죄다 더 나은 옷차림새랑 오빠가 달리 볼 사람이 없었던 거로 풀이될 게 분명해. 그러니 오빠가 정 그 애랑 시시덕거릴 작정이라면, 그게 그 애 아름다움에 대한 찬사라거나 오빠 게으름과 어리석음 말고 다른 데서 나온 거라고는, 나를 결코 못 믿게 만들 거야.”\n\n그 오라비는 이 나무람에 그저 미소만 짓더니, 이내 말했다. “나는 패니 양을 도무지 어찌 봐야 할지 모르겠어. 그 앨 통 이해 못 하겠거든. 어제 그 애가 대체 뭘 하려는 건지 알 수가 없었어. 그 앤 성품이 어떻지? 근엄한가? 별난가? 내숭인가? 왜 뒷걸음질 치며 나한테 그리 근엄한 낯을 하지? 도무지 말을 시킬 수가 없었어. 평생 계집애를 즐겁게 해 주려고 그토록 오래 함께 있어 보고 그토록 형편없이 실패한 적이 없어! 나한테 그토록 근엄한 낯을 하는 계집애는 만나 본 적이 없다니까! 나는 이걸 기어이 이겨 내야겠어. 그 애 낯빛은 이렇게 말하거든. ‘난 당신을 안 좋아할 거예요, 안 좋아하기로 작정했어요.’ 그럼 나는 말하지, 좋아하게 만들겠다고.”\n\n“어리석은 사람! 그러니까 이게 결국 그 애 매력이었구먼! 바로 이거야, 그 애가 오빠한테 무심한 게 그 애 살결을 그토록 보드랍게 하고, 그 애 키를 그토록 크게 하고, 이 온갖 매력과 우아함을 다 자아낸 거라고! 나는 정말이지 오빠가 그 앨 진짜로 불행하게 만들진 말기를 바라. 어쩌면 자그마한 사랑이야 그 애한테 생기를 주고 이로울지도 모르지, 하지만 그 앨 깊이 빠뜨리는 건 안 돼. 그 앤 세상에 둘도 없이 착한 애고, 정도 아주 많거든.”\n\n“고작 두 주뿐인걸.” 헨리가 말했다. “그리고 두 주에 그 애가 죽는다면, 그야 무엇으로도 살릴 수 없는 체질인 거지. 아니야, 나는 그 앨 조금도 해치지 않아, 그 사랑스러운 것을! 나는 그저 그 애가 나를 다정하게 봐 주고, 홍조뿐 아니라 미소도 지어 주고, 우리가 어디 있든 제 곁에 나를 위한 의자 하나 맡아 두고, 내가 그 자리에 앉아 말을 걸면 온통 생기가 돌기를 바랄 뿐이야. 나처럼 생각하고, 내 온갖 소유물과 즐거움에 관심을 두고, 나를 맨스필드에 좀 더 붙들어 두려 애쓰고, 내가 떠나면 다시는 결코 행복할 수 없으리라 느끼기를 말이야. 그 이상은 아무것도 안 바라.”\n\n“절제의 화신일세!” 메리가 말했다. “이젠 나도 아무 거리낌이 없네. 뭐, 오빠 자신을 잘 보여 줄 기회야 얼마든지 있을 거야, 우리가 워낙 자주 함께 있으니까.”\n\n그러고는 더 나무라 볼 것도 없이, 그녀는 패니를 그 운명에 맡겨 두었다. 만일 패니의 마음이 크로퍼드 양은 짐작도 못 할 방식으로 지켜지고 있지 않았더라면, 그것은 그 애가 마땅히 받을 것보다 조금 더 가혹한 운명이었을지 모른다. 재주와 태도와 마음씀과 아첨이 할 수 있는 그 온갖 것으로도, 제 판단을 거슬러 사랑에 빠지도록 결코 설득되지 않는 그런 굴복할 줄 모르는 열여덟 살 아가씨가 분명 있기야 하겠지만(안 그러면 그런 이야기를 읽을 리도 없을 테니), 나는 패니를 그런 축이라 믿고 싶지도, 그토록 다정한 성품과 그토록 고운 취향을 지닌 그 애가—비록 두 주뿐인 구애일망정—크로퍼드 같은 사내의 구애에서, 게다가 그에 대한 미리 품은 나쁜 인상까지 이겨 내야 하는 마당에, 만일 그 애의 정이 다른 데 매여 있지 않았더라면 마음 온전히 벗어날 수 있었으리라 여기고 싶지도 않다. 다른 이에 대한 사랑과 그에 대한 낮은 평가가 그가 공략하는 그 마음의 평온에 줄 수 있는 온갖 방패에도 불구하고, 그의 끊임없는 마음씀은—끊임없되 주제넘지 않고, 그 애 성품의 부드러움과 섬세함에 점점 더 맞춰 가는 그 마음씀은—이내 그녀로 하여금 예전보다 그를 덜 싫어하게 만들었다. 그녀는 결코 지난 일을 잊지 않았고, 여전히 그를 나쁘게 여겼다. 그러나 그녀는 그의 역량을 느꼈다. 그는 재미났고, 그 태도가 어찌나 나아지고, 어찌나 정중하고, 어찌나 진지하고 나무랄 데 없이 정중하던지, 답례로 예의를 차리지 않을 수가 없었다.\n\n아주 며칠이면 이렇게 되기에 넉넉했다. 그리고 그 며칠 끝에, 오히려 그녀를 기쁘게 하려는 그의 뜻을 앞당길 만한 일이 생겼으니, 그것이 그녀에게 온갖 사람을 다 반기게 할 만한 정도의 행복을 안겨 주었기 때문이다. 그녀의 오라비 윌리엄이, 그토록 오래 떠나 있던 애틋이 사랑하는 오라비가 다시 잉글랜드에 돌아온 것이다. 그녀는 그에게서 몸소 편지를 받았으니, 배가 해협을 거슬러 올라올 때 적어, 스핏헤드에 닻 내린 앤트워프호를 떠난 첫 보트 편에 포츠머스로 부친, 서둘러 쓴 행복한 몇 줄이었다. 그리고 크로퍼드가, 첫 소식을 전해 주기를 바랐던 신문을 손에 들고 걸어 올라왔을 때, 그는 그녀가 이 편지 위로 기쁨에 떨며, 삼촌이 더없이 침착하게 불러 주며 답장을 받아 적게 하던 그 다정한 초대를, 발갛게 상기되어 고마움 어린 낯빛으로 듣고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n크로퍼드가 이 일을 훤히 꿰게 된 것은, 아니 사실 그녀에게 그런 오라비가 있다는 것도, 그가 그런 배에 타고 있다는 것도 조금이라도 알게 된 것은 바로 그 전날이었다. 그러나 그때 인 관심은 아주 그럴싸하게 생생해서, 그는 도시로 돌아가면 앤트워프호가 지중해에서 돌아올 대략의 시기 따위를 알아보기로 마음먹었다. 그리고 이튿날 아침 일찍 배 소식을 살핀 그 행운은, 그녀를 기쁘게 할 그런 방도를 찾아낸 제 꾀바름에 대한, 그리고 오랜 세월 해군 소식이 가장 빠르다는 평판의 그 신문을 받아 본, 제독에 대한 제 갸륵한 마음씀에 대한 보답인 듯했다. 그러나 그는 너무 늦은 것으로 드러났다. 그가 불러일으키는 이가 되기를 바랐던 그 온갖 고운 첫 감정은 이미 다 주어져 있었다. 그러나 그의 뜻, 그 뜻의 친절함은 고맙게 받아들여졌다—아주 고맙고 따뜻하게 말이다. 윌리엄에 대한 사랑이 밀려들어 그녀가 여느 때의 그 소심함을 넘어 한껏 들떠 있었기 때문이다.\n\n이 사랑하는 윌리엄이 이내 그들 틈에 오게 될 것이었다. 그가 곧바로 휴가를 얻으리라는 데는 의심의 여지가 없었으니, 그는 아직 사관후보생일 뿐이었기 때문이다. 그리고 그 부모야 마침 그곳에 사니 벌써 그를 봤을 테고 어쩌면 날마다 보고 있을 테니, 그의 곧장의 휴일은 마땅히 곧바로 그 누이에게—일곱 해 세월 내내 그의 가장 좋은 편지 벗이었던 누이에게—그리고 그를 건사하고 앞길을 열어 주는 데 누구보다 애쓴 그 삼촌에게 주어질 만했다. 그리하여 그의 도착일을 아주 이르게 잡은, 그녀의 답장에 대한 답장이 되도록 빨리 왔다. 그리고 패니가 첫 만찬 방문의 동요에 잠겼던 이래 열흘도 채 지나지 않아, 그녀는 한층 높은 종류의 동요에 잠겨, 오라비를 데려올 마차의 첫 소리를 들으려 현관홀에서, 로비에서, 계단에서 지켜보고 있는 자신을 발견했다.\n\n그녀가 이렇게 기다리는 동안 그것이 반갑게 왔다. 그리고 만남의 순간을 늦출 격식도 두려움도 없던지라, 그가 집에 들어설 때 그녀는 그와 함께 있었고, 더없이 애틋한 감정의 첫 몇 분은 아무 훼방도 아무 목격자도 없었으니, 알맞은 문들을 여는 데만 골몰한 하인들을 목격자라 부를 수 있다면 몰라도 말이다. 이것이야말로 토머스 경과 에드먼드가 저마다 넌지시 눈감아 준 바로 그것이었다. 도착의 소음이 들려오자마자 노리스 부인이 현관홀로 뛰쳐나가려는 것을 둘 다, 서로에게 그 마음 맞는 잽싼 태도로 증명하듯, 있던 자리에 그대로 있으라 권했던 것이다.\n\n이내 윌리엄과 패니가 모습을 드러냈다. 그리고 토머스 경은 제 피후견인으로, 분명 일곱 해 전 그가 채비시켜 보낸 사람과는 아주 딴사람을, 그러나 트이고 유쾌한 낯빛에, 꾸밈없고 스스럼없되 정 있고 공손한 태도를 지닌, 그리하여 그를 제 편이라 굳혀 주는 그런 젊은이를 맞는 기쁨을 누렸다.\n\n마지막 삼십 분의 기다림과 첫 삼십 분의 이룸으로 이뤄진 그런 한때의 그 벅찬 행복에서 패니가 헤어나기까지는 오래 걸렸다. 그 행복이 그녀를 행복하게 한다 할 만해지기까지도, 사람이 달라진 데서 오는 어쩔 수 없는 실망이 스러져 그 안에서 예전과 같은 윌리엄을 알아보고, 지난 여러 해 마음이 사무치게 바라 오던 대로 그와 이야기할 수 있게 되기까지도 얼마간 시간이 걸렸다. 그러나 그때는 차츰 왔으니, 그녀 못지않게 따뜻하면서 세련됨이나 자기 불신에 훨씬 덜 얽매인 그의 정에 힘입어서였다. 그녀는 그의 사랑의 첫째 대상이었으나, 그것은 그의 더 굳센 기상과 더 대담한 기질이 느끼기 만큼이나 표현하기도 자연스럽게 만든 그런 사랑이었다. 이튿날 그들은 참된 즐거움 속에 함께 거닐었고, 뒤이은 아침마다 단둘의 시간이 새로이 이어졌으니, 그것을 토머스 경은, 에드먼드가 그에게 짚어 주기도 전에, 흐뭇하게 지켜보지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n지난 몇 달 사이 에드먼드가 그녀를 헤아려 준 그 어떤 두드러지거나 뜻밖의 일이 자아낸 각별한 기쁨의 순간들을 빼고는, 패니는 평생 이토록 큰 행복을 알아 본 적이 없었으니, 저에게 온 마음을 열어 보이는 이 오라비이자 벗과의 이 거리낌 없고 대등하고 두려움 없는 사귐에서 누린 것 같은 행복은 말이다. 그는 그토록 오래 그려 오고, 값지게 얻어 내고, 마땅히 값진 그 승진의 축복에 얽힌 제 온갖 희망과 두려움, 계획과 마음 졸임을 그녀에게 다 털어놓았다. 그는 그녀가 좀처럼 소식 듣지 못하던 아버지와 어머니, 형제자매에 대해 곧바로 낱낱이 알려 줄 수 있었다. 그는 맨스필드 그녀 집의 온갖 편안함과 온갖 자잘한 고생에 관심을 두었고, 그 집 식구 하나하나를 그녀가 이끄는 대로 생각할 채비가 되어 있었으며—다만 노리스 이모에 대해서만은 좀 덜 삼가는 의견과 좀 더 시끄러운 험담으로 달랐을 뿐이다—그리고 그와 더불어(어쩌면 그 모든 것 가운데 가장 애틋한 즐거움으로) 그들 가장 어린 시절의 온갖 궂은 일과 좋은 일을 다시 되짚고, 지난날 함께 나눈 온갖 아픔과 즐거움을 더없이 정겨운 추억으로 되밟을 수 있었다. 이것이야말로 하나의 이점이요, 사랑을 굳혀 주는 것이니, 여기서는 부부의 인연조차 남매의 그것 아래에 있다. 같은 집안, 같은 피, 같은 첫 기억과 버릇을 지닌 자식들에게는, 훗날 그 어떤 인연으로도 대신할 수 없는 즐거움의 방도가 그 손안에 얼마간 있다. 그리고 그토록 값진, 가장 이른 정의 잔재가 아주 스러져 버리는 일이 있다면, 그것은 오직 길고 부자연스러운 소원함으로, 훗날 그 어떤 인연으로도 정당화할 수 없는 갈라섬으로써일 것이다. 아아, 너무나 자주 그러하기는 하다! 남매의 사랑이란, 때로 거의 전부이다가도, 다른 때는 없느니만 못하다. 그러나 윌리엄과 패니 프라이스에게 그것은 여전히 그 온전한 한창과 싱그러움 속의 정이었으니, 이해의 다툼으로 상하지도, 따로 든 정으로 식지도 않고, 세월과 헤어짐의 영향이라곤 오직 그것이 자라나는 데서만 느껴지는 것이었다.\n\n그토록 상냥한 정은, 무엇이든 좋은 것을 값지게 여길 마음을 지닌 모든 이의 눈에 그 둘을 저마다 돋보이게 했다. 헨리 크로퍼드도 누구 못지않게 그것에 감동했다. 그는 그 젊은 뱃사람의 따뜻하고 무뚝뚝한 다정함을 우러렀으니, 그 다정함에 이끌려 그는 패니의 머리 쪽으로 두 손을 뻗으며 이렇게 말하곤 했다. “있잖아요, 나 벌써 그 야릇한 유행이 좋아지기 시작했어요. 잉글랜드에서 그런 걸 한다는 소릴 처음 들었을 땐 도무지 못 믿었고, 지브롤터 판무관 댁의 브라운 부인이랑 다른 여자들이 똑같은 매무새로 나타났을 땐 다들 실성했나 싶었는데, 패니한테라면 나는 뭐든 정이 든다니까요.” 그리고 그는, 그런 바다 생활이 으레 안겨 줄 절박한 위험이나 무시무시한 장면을 그녀의 오라비가 그려 보일 때, 발갛게 상기된 패니의 뺨, 반짝이는 그 눈, 그 깊은 관심, 그 넋 나간 몰두를 생기 넘치는 감탄으로 지켜보았다.\n\n그것은 헨리 크로퍼드가 값지게 여길 만한 도덕적 안목을 지닌 그림이었다. 패니의 매력이 커졌다—곱절로 커졌다. 그녀의 얼굴빛을 아름답게 하고 낯빛을 환히 밝히는 그 감수성이 그 자체로 하나의 매력이었기 때문이다. 그는 이제 그녀 마음의 역량을 의심하지 않았다. 그녀에게는 정이, 참된 정이 있었다. 그런 계집애에게 사랑받는다는 것, 그 젊고 순박한 마음의 첫 열정을 불러일으킨다는 것은 대단한 일일 터였다! 그녀는 그가 미리 내다본 것보다 더 그의 마음을 끌었다. 두 주로는 넉넉지 않았다. 그의 체류가 기약 없어졌다.\n\n윌리엄은 삼촌에게서 곧잘 이야기꾼이 되어 달라는 청을 받았다. 그의 이야기는 그 자체로 토머스 경에게 재미났으나, 그것을 구하는 주된 목적은 이야기하는 이를 헤아리는 것, 그 젊은이를 그 살아온 이야기로 알아 가는 것이었다. 그리고 그는 그 또렷하고 소박하고 생기 넘치는 자초지종에 온전한 흡족함으로 귀 기울였으니, 거기서 훌륭한 원칙, 직업의 지식, 활력, 용기, 명랑함의 증거를, 무엇이든 값지거나 앞날이 밝을 만한 그 모든 것을 보았기 때문이다. 어린 나이에도 윌리엄은 벌써 많은 것을 보았다. 그는 지중해에도, 서인도제도에도, 다시 지중해에도 있었다. 함장의 배려로 곧잘 뭍에도 올랐고, 일곱 해 동안 바다와 전쟁이 함께 안길 수 있는 온갖 위험을 다 알았다. 제 손에 그런 밑천을 지녔으니 그는 귀 기울여 들을 만한 자격이 있었다. 그리고 조카가 난파나 교전을 이야기하는 한복판에, 노리스 부인이 방 안을 부산 떨며 실 두 바늘어치나 헌 셔츠 단추 하나를 찾느라 모두를 어지럽힐 수는 있었어도, 그 밖의 모두는 귀를 기울였다. 그리고 버트럼 부인마저 그런 무서운 이야기를 아무렇지 않게 들을 수는 없어, 이따금 일감에서 눈을 들어 이렇게 말하곤 했다. “저런! 어쩜 그리 끔찍할까! 어떻게 바다에 나가는 사람이 다 있나 몰라.”\n\n헨리 크로퍼드에게 그것은 다른 감정을 주었다. 그는 저도 바다에 나가, 그만큼 보고 행하고 겪었더라면 하고 사무치게 바랐다. 그의 마음이 데워지고, 그의 상상이 불붙었으며, 스무 살도 되기 전에 그런 육신의 고생을 다 겪고 그런 심지의 증거를 다 내보인 젊은이에게 더없는 존경을 느꼈다. 영웅다움의, 쓸모의, 애씀의, 견딤의 그 영광은, 제 이기적인 방종의 버릇을 부끄러운 대비 속에 드러나게 했다. 그리고 그는 지금의 저 대신, 그토록 큰 자긍심과 행복한 열의로 스스로를 돋보이며 재물과 지체로 제 길을 열어 가는 윌리엄 프라이스였더라면 하고 바랐다!\n\n그 바람은 오래가기보다 자못 뜨거운 쪽이었다. 그것이 자아낸 회고와 뉘우침의 몽상에서 그는, 이튿날 사냥 계획을 묻는 에드먼드의 물음에 깨어났다. 그리고 그는 그냥 당장 말과 마부를 제 마음대로 부리는 재산가인 편이 낫다는 걸 알았다. 한 가지 점에서 그것은 더 나았으니, 은혜를 베풀고 싶은 데 친절을 베풀 방도를 주었기 때문이다. 무엇에든 나설 기상과 용기와 호기심을 지닌 윌리엄이 사냥하고 싶은 뜻을 내비쳤다. 그리고 크로퍼드는 저에게는 조금의 불편도 없이 그를 말에 태워 줄 수 있었으니, 다만 조카보다 그런 대여의 값어치를 더 잘 아는 토머스 경에게서 얼마간의 거리낌을 없애 주고, 패니에게서 얼마간의 불안을 다독여 없애 주면 되었다. 그녀는 윌리엄을 걱정했다. 여러 나라에서의 제 승마 솜씨며, 저도 낀 그 우당탕 몰려다닌 사냥이며, 저가 탔던 거친 말과 노새며, 무서운 낙마를 아슬아슬하게 여러 번 면한 이야기를 그가 아무리 늘어놓아도, 그가 잉글랜드 여우 사냥에서 잘 먹인 사냥말을 다룰 만하다고는 조금도 믿기지 않았던 것이다. 그리고 그가 아무 사고도 망신도 없이 무사히 잘 돌아오기 전까지는, 그녀는 그 위험과 화해할 수도, 그 말을 빌려준 데 대해 크로퍼드 씨가 마땅히 자아내리라 작정했던 그 고마움을 조금도 느낄 수도 없었다. 그러나 그것이 윌리엄에게 아무 해도 끼치지 않았음이 드러나자, 그녀는 그것이 친절임을 인정할 수 있었다. 그리하여 그 짐승이 한순간 다시 윌리엄의 쓰임에 내밀어지고, 다음 순간 더없이 정답게, 마다할 수 없는 태도로, 그가 노샘프턴셔에 머무는 동안은 죄다 그의 쓰임에 넘겨졌을 때, 그녀는 미소로 그 주인에게 보답하기까지 했다.\n\n# 제25장\n\n이 무렵 두 집안의 왕래는, 옛 친분을 나누던 이들 가운데 누구도 다시 그리되리라 좀처럼 여기지 못했을 만큼, 가을 시절의 모습에 거의 되돌아와 있었다. 헨리 크로퍼드의 귀환과 윌리엄 프라이스의 도착이 거기에 큰 몫을 했으나, 목사관 쪽의 이웃다운 다가섬을 토머스 경이 그저 견뎌 주는 정도를 넘어선 데에도 여전히 힘입은 바가 컸다. 처음 그를 짓누르던 근심에서 이제 벗어난 그의 마음은, 그랜트 부부와 그 집에 머무는 젊은이들이 정말이지 찾아볼 만한 이들임을 알아볼 여유가 생긴 것이다. 그리고 그는 저에게 가장 소중한 이 누구를 위해서든 눈앞에 그럴싸한 가능성으로 떠오를 법한 그 어떤 더없이 유리한 혼처를 꾀하거나 도모하는 일 따위는 한없이 낮게 보았고, 그런 점에 눈치가 빠른 것조차 좀스러운 짓이라 업신여겼으되, 크로퍼드 씨가 제 조카딸을 얼마간 각별히 대하고 있음을 대범하고 무심한 투로나마 알아채지 않을 수는 없었고—아마 그 까닭에 (저도 모르게) 초대에 한결 기꺼이 응하는 것을 삼가지도 못했으리라.\n\n그러나 “토머스 경은 워낙 내키지 않아 하시고, 버트럼 부인은 워낙 게으르시니!” 그럴 만한 값어치가 있을까를 두고 숱한 실랑이와 숱한 망설임 끝에 마침내 저녁 초대라는 것이 한번 무릅써졌을 때, 목사관에서 만찬을 들자는 데 그가 선뜻 응한 것은 오직 좋은 교양과 호의에서 나온 것일 뿐, 크로퍼드 씨와는 그저 유쾌한 무리의 한 사람이라는 것 말고는 아무 상관이 없었다. 다름 아닌 바로 그 방문의 자리에서 그는, 그런 한가로운 눈여겨보기를 버릇 삼은 이라면 누구든 크로퍼드 씨가 패니 프라이스를 흠모하는 이라고 여겼으리라는 생각을 비로소 처음으로 품게 되었으니 말이다.\n\n그 모임은 대체로 유쾌한 자리로 여겨졌으니, 말할 이와 들을 이가 알맞게 어우러진 까닭이었다. 그리고 만찬 자체는 그랜트가의 여느 방식대로 우아하고 푸짐했으며, 하나같이 여느 습관에 너무 익은 것이라 노리스 부인 말고는 누구에게도 아무 감흥을 일으키지 못했다. 그녀로 말하면 넓은 식탁도, 그 위에 오른 접시 수도 도무지 진득하니 바라보지 못하는 사람이었고, 제 의자 뒤로 하인들이 오가는 데서 늘 무슨 언짢은 일을 겪어 내고야 마는 사람이었으며, 이토록 접시가 많으니 그중 몇은 식지 않을 도리가 없다는 새로운 확신 하나를 꼭 챙겨 오는 사람이었다.\n\n저녁이 되자, 그랜트 부인과 그 아우가 미리 짜 둔 대로, 휘스트 탁자를 꾸리고도 원탁 놀이에 넉넉할 만큼이 남으리라는 것이 드러났다. 그리고 이런 자리에서 다들 늘 그러하듯 저마다 더없이 고분고분하고 이렇다 할 취향도 없던 터라, 휘스트가 정해지기 무섭게 스페큘레이션이 정해졌다. 그리하여 버트럼 부인은, 두 놀이 가운데 어느 쪽이 좋으냐는 물음을 받고 휘스트에 낄 패를 뽑을지 말지를 정해야 하는, 자못 아슬아슬한 처지에 이내 놓이고 말았다. 그녀는 머뭇거렸다. 다행히 토머스 경이 곁에 있었다.\n\n“제가 어쩌면 좋을까요, 토머스 경? 휘스트하고 스페큘레이션 중에, 어느 게 저를 제일 즐겁게 해 줄까요?”\n\n토머스 경은 잠깐 생각하더니 스페큘레이션을 권했다. 그 자신이 휘스트를 즐기는 사람이었으니, 아마 그녀를 짝으로 두어서는 그리 재미나지 못하리라 느꼈던 것이리라.\n\n“그럼 됐어요.” 마님은 흡족한 낯으로 대답했다. “그럼 스페큘레이션으로 해요, 그랜트 부인, 그렇게 해 주세요. 나야 그게 뭔지 도통 모르지만, 패니가 가르쳐 주면 되니까요.”\n\n그러나 여기서 패니가, 저 역시 그에 못지않게 아무것도 모른다며 애타게 손사래를 치며 끼어들었다. 그녀는 평생 그 놀이를 해 본 적도, 누가 하는 것을 본 적도 없었던 것이다. 그러자 버트럼 부인은 다시 한순간 갈팡질팡했다. 그러나 그보다 쉬운 것은 없다고, 카드놀이 가운데 가장 쉬운 놀이라고 다들 장담하고, 헨리 크로퍼드가 나서서 마님과 프라이스 양 사이에 앉아 두 분을 다 가르치게 해 달라고 더없이 간곡히 청하니, 일은 그리 정해졌다. 그리하여 토머스 경과 노리스 부인, 그랜트 박사 부부가 가장 지적이고 위엄 있는 탁자에 자리 잡고, 나머지 여섯은 크로퍼드 양의 지휘 아래 다른 탁자에 둘러앉았다. 헨리 크로퍼드에게는 참으로 좋은 배치였으니, 그는 패니 곁에 딱 붙어 있었고, 제 것 말고도 두 사람 몫의 패까지 건사하느라 손이 잔뜩 바빴다. 패니가 삼 분 만에 그 놀이의 규칙을 제 손아귀에 넣지 않기란 어림없는 일이었으나, 그래도 그는 그녀의 판을 북돋우고, 욕심을 벼려 주고, 마음을 모질게 다잡아 주어야 했는데—특히 윌리엄과 겨루는 판에서는—그것이 여간 애먹는 일이 아니었다. 그리고 버트럼 부인으로 말하면, 저녁 내내 그녀의 명예와 재물을 죄다 도맡아야 했다. 패를 돌리기 시작할 제 그녀가 제 패를 들여다보지 못하게 재빨리 막고서, 놀이가 끝날 때까지 그 패로 무엇을 해야 할지 일일이 이끌어 주어야 했던 것이다.\n\n그는 잔뜩 신이 나서, 무엇이든 흐뭇하리만치 손쉽게 해냈고, 놀이의 흥을 돋우는 온갖 발랄한 재치며 재빠른 임기응변이며 장난스러운 뻔뻔함에서 단연 두드러졌다. 그리하여 그 원탁은 여느 탁자의 진득한 진지함과 정연한 침묵과는 사뭇 편안한 대조를 이루었다.\n\n토머스 경은 제 아내가 얼마나 즐거워하고 잘 풀리는지를 두 번이나 물었으나 허사였다. 그의 느긋하고 재는 말투가 필요로 하는 만큼 놀이가 오래 멈추는 법이 없었던 것이다. 그리하여 첫 판이 끝날 무렵 그랜트 부인이 그녀에게 다가가 덕담을 건넬 수 있게 되기까지는, 그녀의 형편이 어떤지 좀처럼 알 길이 없었다.\n\n“마님, 이 놀이가 마음에 드시는지요.”\n\n“아이참, 그럼요! 정말이지 무척 재미나요. 참으로 별난 놀이예요. 나야 이게 다 무슨 영문인지 통 모르겠지만요. 나는 도무지 내 패를 볼 일이 없고, 나머지는 죄다 크로퍼드 씨가 해 주니까요.”\n\n“버트럼,” 하고 얼마 뒤 크로퍼드가, 놀이가 잠시 느슨해진 틈을 타서 말했다. “어제 집으로 말 타고 돌아오는 길에 나한테 무슨 일이 있었는지 자네한테 아직 얘기 안 했지.” 그들은 함께 사냥을 나갔다가, 한창 좋은 몰이 중에 맨스필드에서 얼마쯤 떨어진 곳에 이르렀을 때 그의 말이 편자 하나를 벗은 것이 드러나, 헨리 크로퍼드는 하는 수 없이 사냥을 접고 되도록 잘 되짚어 돌아와야 했던 것이다. “내가 그 주목 나무 있는 낡은 농가를 지나서 길을 잃었다고 자네한테 말했지, 나야 도무지 길 묻는 걸 못 견디니까. 하지만 이 얘긴 안 했어. 늘 그렇듯 운수 좋게도—나는 잘못을 저지르고서 덕을 못 본 적이 없거든—얼마 안 가 다름 아닌, 내가 못내 궁금해하던 바로 그곳에 이르러 있더라니까. 제법 가파른 잔풀 언덕배기 모롱이를 도니, 문득 나지막이 솟은 언덕들 사이 외진 자그마한 마을 한복판이지 뭔가. 앞에는 걸어서 건널 작은 시내가 있고, 오른편에는 자그마한 둔덕 위에 교회가 서 있는데—그 교회가 이런 시골치고는 유난히 크고 훤칠하고, 신사도 반쪽짜리 신사도 사는 집이라곤 하나 말고는 통 보이지 않더군—그 하나는 방금 말한 그 둔덕과 교회에서 돌 던지면 닿을 만한 데에 있으니, 목사관이려니 싶었지. 요컨대 나는 손턴 레이시에 와 있더라 이 말일세.”\n\n“그런 것 같군.” 에드먼드가 말했다. “한데 자네, 슈얼네 농장을 지나서는 어느 쪽으로 돌았나?”\n\n“나는 그런 뜬금없고 얄궂은 물음엔 대답 안 하네. 하기야 자네가 한 시간 동안 던질 수 있는 물음에 내가 다 대답한들, 그게 손턴 레이시가 아니었다는 걸 자네는 결코 증명 못 할 테니까—틀림없이 손턴 레이시였거든.”\n\n“그럼 물어봤단 말인가?”\n\n“아니, 나는 절대 안 물어. 다만 울타리를 손보던 사내한테 여기가 손턴 레이시라고 일러 줬더니, 그자가 그렇다고 맞장구치더군.”\n\n“기억력이 참 좋군. 나는 자네한테 그곳 얘길 그 절반이라도 해 준 적이 있다는 걸 까맣게 잊고 있었네.”\n\n손턴 레이시란 곧 그에게 떨어질 성직록의 이름이었으니, 크로퍼드 양은 이를 잘 알고 있었다. 그리하여 윌리엄 프라이스의 잭을 둘러싼 흥정에 그녀의 관심이 부쩍 커졌다.\n\n“그래,” 에드먼드가 말을 이었다. “그래서 자네가 본 게 마음에 들던가?”\n\n“정말이지 무척 들더군. 자네는 운 좋은 사람이야. 그곳이 사람 살 만해지려면 적어도 다섯 해 여름은 손봐야 할걸.”\n\n“아니, 아냐, 그리 심하진 않아. 헛간 마당을 옮겨야 하는 거야 나도 인정하네. 하지만 그 밖엔 딱히 걸릴 게 없는걸. 집은 결코 나쁘지 않고, 마당만 치우고 나면 제법 봐줄 만한 진입로도 낼 수 있을 거야.”\n\n“헛간 마당은 몽땅 쓸어 없애고, 대장간이 안 보이게 나무를 심어 가려야 하네. 집은 북쪽 대신 동쪽을 바라보게 돌려야 하고—내 말은, 현관하고 주요한 방들이 그쪽으로 나야 한다는 거야. 그쪽 경치가 정말이지 무척 예쁘거든. 틀림없이 그리할 수 있어. 그리고 자네 진입로는 바로 거기, 지금 정원인 데를 지나게 내야 해. 지금 집 뒤편인 데다 새 정원을 만들어야 하는데, 그러면 세상 둘도 없이 좋은 방향을 얻게 되지, 남동쪽으로 비스듬히 트인 자리 말일세. 땅이 딱 그러라고 생긴 것 같더군. 나는 둘러보려고 교회와 집 사이 오솔길을 오십 야드쯤 말 타고 올라가 봤는데, 그 모든 게 어찌 될 수 있을지 훤히 보이더라니까. 더없이 쉬운 일이야. 앞으로 정원이 될 데 너머의 풀밭도, 지금 정원인 데 너머의 풀밭도, 내가 서 있던 그 오솔길에서 북동쪽으로, 그러니까 마을을 가로지르는 큰길 쪽으로 휘돌아 나가는 그것들은 물론 죄다 하나로 묶어야 하지. 참 예쁜 풀밭들이야, 나무가 곱게 점점이 박혀서 말일세. 그것들은 성직록에 딸린 땅일 테지. 아니라면 자네가 사들여야 하고. 그다음엔 그 시내—시내는 뭔가 손을 봐야 하는데, 다만 뭘 어찌해야 할지는 딱 못 정하겠더군. 두어 가지 생각이 있긴 했네.”\n\n“그리고 나도 두어 가지 생각이 있네.” 에드먼드가 말했다. “그중 하나는, 손턴 레이시를 위한 자네 계획 가운데 실제로 이뤄질 건 거의 없으리라는 거야. 나는 그보다 훨씬 덜 꾸미고 덜 아름다운 걸로 만족해야 하네. 나는 그 집과 딸린 터를, 그리 큰돈 안 들이고도 편안하게, 그리고 신사가 사는 집다운 기품을 갖추게 할 수 있으리라 보고, 그거면 내겐 넉넉해. 그리고 바라건대 나를 아끼는 이들 누구에게든 그거면 넉넉하기를.”\n\n크로퍼드 양은, 그 마지막 바람의 어떤 목소리 빛깔과, 그에 딸린 어떤 곁눈질에 얼마간 미심쩍고 서운한 마음이 들어, 윌리엄 프라이스와의 흥정을 서둘러 끝맺어 버렸다. 그러고는 터무니없는 값을 치르고 그의 잭을 손에 넣더니 외쳤다. “자, 나는 기개 있는 여자답게 마지막 밑천을 걸겠어요. 차가운 신중함 따위는 내게 어울리지 않아요. 나는 가만히 앉아 아무것도 안 하도록 태어난 사람이 아니거든요. 내가 이 판을 진다면, 그건 애써 겨루지 않아서는 아닐 거예요.”\n\n그 판은 그녀의 것이 되었으나, 그것을 손에 넣으려고 그녀가 치른 값을 갚아 주지는 못했다. 다음 패가 돌려졌고, 크로퍼드는 다시 손턴 레이시 얘기를 꺼냈다.\n\n“내 계획이 될 수 있는 최선은 아닐지도 몰라. 그걸 짤 겨를이 몇 분도 없었으니까. 하지만 자네는 손을 꽤 많이 대야 하네. 그곳은 그럴 만하고, 자네도 그곳이 낼 수 있는 것에 한참 못 미치는 걸로는 결코 만족 못 하게 될 거야. (실례합니다, 마님, 패를 보시면 안 됩니다. 자, 그냥 앞에 그대로 엎어 두세요.) 그곳은 그럴 만해, 버트럼. 자네는 신사가 사는 집다운 기품을 준다는 얘길 하지. 그거야 헛간 마당을 치우기만 하면 절로 되는 걸세. 그 지긋지긋한 눈엣가시만 빼놓고 보면, 나는 그만큼 그 자체로 신사가 사는 집다운 기품을 지닌, 그만큼 한낱 목사관 이상의—한 해 몇백 파운드 쓰는 살림 이상의 것으로 보이는 그런 집을 본 적이 없거든. 그건 낮은 단칸방들을 창문 수만큼이나 많은 지붕으로 어수선하게 이어 붙인 그런 집이 아니야. 네모난 농가의 촌스러운 옹색함에 오그라든 집도 아니고. 그건 튼튼하고 널찍한, 저택 같은 풍채의 집이라, 점잖은 오랜 시골 명문이 대대로, 적어도 두 세기 내내 살아오면서 이제 한 해에 이삼천 파운드씩 들이는 그런 집이려니 싶은 그런 집일세.” 크로퍼드 양이 귀 기울여 들었고, 에드먼드도 이에 수긍했다. “그러니 자네가 무언가 손을 대기만 하면, 신사가 사는 집다운 기품이야 안 줄 수가 없어. 하지만 그건 그보다 훨씬 더한 것도 될 수 있네. (어디 보자, 메리. 버트럼 부인이 저 퀸에 열둘을 부르시는군. 아니야, 아냐, 열둘은 그 값어치보다 많아. 버트럼 부인은 열둘을 안 부르셔. 그 퀸엔 볼일이 없으시고 말고. 계속해, 계속.) 내가 내놓은 것 같은 그런 손질 몇 가지로 (자네더러 정말 내 계획대로 하라는 건 아니야. 하기야 누가 더 나은 걸 짜낼 수 있을지는 못 미덥지만) 자네는 그곳에 한층 높은 격을 줄 수 있어. 그걸 하나의 명소로 끌어올릴 수 있는 거지. 한낱 신사의 집이던 것이, 안목 있는 손질을 거치면 교양과 취향과 요즘풍 몸가짐과 좋은 인연을 갖춘 사람의 거처가 되는 걸세. 이 모두를 거기에 새겨 넣을 수 있어. 그리하여 그 집이 어떤 풍채를 얻으면, 그 길을 지나는 이라면 누구나 그 주인을 이 교구의 큰 지주로 여기게 되지. 더구나 그걸 두고 다툴 진짜 향사의 저택도 없으니 말이야—우리끼리 얘기지만, 이건 그런 자리의 값어치를 특권과 독립이라는 점에서 이루 헤아릴 수 없이 높여 줄 사정이거든. 양도 나와 같은 생각이시겠지요.” (부드러워진 목소리로 패니 쪽을 돌아보며.) “그곳을 본 적 있으세요?”\n\n패니는 얼른 아니라고 대답하고는, 이 화제에 쏠린 제 관심을 감추려 오라비 쪽에 열심히 마음을 쏟았다. 오라비는 더없이 야박한 흥정을 붙이며 있는 힘껏 그녀에게서 뜯어내려 하고 있었다. 그러나 크로퍼드는 말을 이었다. “안 돼요, 안 돼, 퀸을 내놓으시면 안 됩니다. 너무 비싸게 사셨고, 이 오라비는 그 값의 절반도 못 부르고 있잖아요. 아냐, 아냐, 손 떼시게, 손 떼. 자네 누이는 퀸을 안 내놓아. 아주 단단히 마음먹으셨으니. 이 판은 양의 것이 될 겁니다.” 하고 다시 그녀 쪽을 돌아보며, “틀림없이 양의 것이 될 거예요.”\n\n“한데 패니는 차라리 그게 윌리엄 것이 되기를 훨씬 바랄걸.” 에드먼드가 그녀에게 미소 지으며 말했다. “가엾은 패니! 제 바람대로 제 손해를 보게 놔두질 않으니!”\n\n“버트럼 씨,” 하고 몇 분 뒤 크로퍼드 양이 말했다. “댁도 아시다시피 헨리가 워낙 대단한 손질꾼이라, 손턴 레이시에서 그런 일을 벌이시면서 이이 도움을 안 받으실 수야 없을 거예요. 소더턴에서 이이가 얼마나 쓸모 있었는지 생각만 해 보세요! 팔월 어느 무더운 날, 우리 다 함께 이이를 따라나서 그 영지를 돌아보며 이이의 재주가 불붙는 걸 지켜본 덕에, 거기서 얼마나 굉장한 것들이 나왔는지 생각만 해 보시라니까요. 우리는 거기로 갔다가 다시 집으로 돌아왔지요. 그리고 거기서 이뤄진 것은 이루 말로 다 못 할 정도예요!”\n\n패니의 눈길이 한순간, 근엄함을 넘어—나무람마저 어린 낯빛으로 크로퍼드에게로 향했다. 그러나 그의 눈길과 마주치자 이내 거두어졌다. 그는 얼마간 켕기는 기색으로 누이를 향해 고개를 젓고는, 웃으며 대꾸했다. “소더턴에서 이뤄진 게 많다고는 못 하겠는걸. 그날은 무덥고, 우리 다 서로 뒤를 쫓아 걷느라 정신이 없었으니까.” 이내 두런거리는 소리가 일어 그를 가려 주자, 그는 오로지 패니만을 향해 나직한 목소리로 덧붙였다. “나의 꾸미는 솜씨를 소더턴에서의 그날로 가늠하시게 되면 나야 서운하지요. 지금은 사뭇 다르게 본답니다. 그때 비친 모습으로 나를 여기지는 마세요.”\n\n소더턴은 노리스 부인의 귀를 잡아채는 낱말이었다. 마침 토머스 경의 멋진 솜씨와 제 솜씨로 그랜트 박사 부부의 좋은 패를 눌러 승부를 가른 그 한 끗을 따낸 뒤 찾아온 흐뭇한 여유에 잠겨 있던 터라, 그녀는 자못 기분 좋게 큰 소리로 외쳤다. “소더턴! 그럼, 거긴 정말이지 대단한 곳이지, 우리가 거기서 참 좋은 하루를 보냈고 말이야. 윌리엄, 너는 운이 참 없구나. 하지만 다음에 오거든, 그때는 착한 러시워스 내외가 집에 있으면 좋겠구나. 두 사람한테 네가 다정한 대접을 받으리라는 건 내가 장담하마. 네 사촌들은 제 피붙이를 잊을 사람들이 아니고, 러시워스 씨는 더없이 상냥한 분이거든. 그이들이 지금은 브라이턴에 가 있단다, 알겠니. 거기서도 손꼽히는 좋은 집 가운데 하나에 말이야. 러시워스 씨의 넉넉한 재산이 그럴 자격을 주니까. 거리가 얼마나 되는지 나야 정확힌 모르지만, 네가 포츠머스로 돌아가거든, 그리 멀지만 않다면 마땅히 건너가 문안을 여쭈어야지. 그리고 나도 네 사촌들한테 꼭 전하고픈 자그마한 꾸러미 하나를 네 편에 부칠 수 있을 테고.”\n\n“저야 무척 기쁘겠지요, 이모. 하지만 브라이턴은 비치 헤드 바로 옆이나 다름없는걸요. 게다가 제가 거기까지 갈 수 있다 한들, 그토록 번드르르한 곳에서 환대받기를 바랄 수야 없지요—보잘것없는 초라한 사관후보생인 제가 말이에요.”\n\n노리스 부인이, 그가 믿어도 좋을 그 살가운 대접을 열띠게 장담하려던 참인데, 토머스 경이 위엄 있게 말하며 그녀를 막았다. “나는 자네가 브라이턴에 가는 건 권하지 않네, 윌리엄. 머잖아 더 편한 자리에서 만날 기회가 생기리라 믿으니 말일세. 하지만 내 딸들은 어디서든 제 사촌을 반겨 볼 테고, 자네도 러시워스 씨가 우리 집안의 온갖 인연을 제 것처럼 여기려는 더없이 진실한 사람임을 알게 될 걸세.”\n\n“저야 그이가 그 무엇보다도 차라리 해군장관의 개인 비서로나 있어 주면 좋겠는데요.” 이것이 윌리엄의 유일한 대꾸였는데, 나직한 소리라 멀리까지 닿으라고 한 말은 아니었다. 그리고 그 화제는 그것으로 흐지부지되었다.\n\n여태 토머스 경은 크로퍼드 씨의 행동에서 이렇다 할 것을 알아채지 못했다. 그러나 둘째 판이 끝나 휘스트 탁자가 파하고, 그랜트 박사와 노리스 부인이 저희 마지막 판을 두고 옥신각신하게 내버려 둔 채 그가 다른 탁자의 구경꾼이 되었을 때, 그는 제 조카딸이 얼마간 노골적인 빛깔을 띤 마음씀의—아니, 차라리 공언(公言)의—과녁이 되어 있음을 알아챘다.\n\n헨리 크로퍼드는 손턴 레이시를 두고 또 다른 계획에 막 달아오른 참이었다. 에드먼드의 귀를 잡을 수가 없자, 그는 자못 진지한 낯빛으로 그것을 제 아리따운 옆자리 이에게 낱낱이 늘어놓고 있었다. 그의 계획인즉, 이듬해 겨울 그 집을 제가 직접 세내어 그 근방에 제 집을 하나 두겠다는 것이었다. 그것은 (그가 그때 그녀에게 말하고 있던 대로) 그저 사냥철에 쓰려는 것만은 아니었다—하기야 그 생각도 분명 얼마간 무게가 있기는 했으니, 그는 그랜트 박사의 그 크나큰 친절에도 불구하고, 지금 머무는 곳에서는 저와 제 말들을 큰 불편 없이 건사하기가 도무지 어렵다고 느끼고 있었던 것이다. 그러나 그 근방에 대한 그의 애착은 어느 한 가지 놀이나 어느 한 철에 달린 것이 아니었다. 그는 언제든 찾아올 수 있는 무언가를, 제 뜻대로 쓸 수 있는 자그마한 보금자리 하나를 그곳에 두어, 한 해의 온갖 휴일을 거기서 보내고, 날마다 그 값어치를 더해 가는 맨스필드 파크 식구와의 그 우정과 친분을 이어 가고 두터이 하며 온전케 해 가는 저 자신을 보게 되기를 마음속으로 굳게 바라고 있었다. 토머스 경은 이를 듣고도 언짢아하지 않았다. 그 젊은이의 말투에는 무례함이 조금도 없었고, 패니가 그것을 받아들이는 품이 어찌나 온당하고 겸손하며, 어찌나 차분하고 부추기는 데가 없던지, 그로서는 그녀에게서 나무랄 것이 하나도 없었다. 그녀는 말을 아꼈고, 여기저기서 그저 맞장구만 쳤을 뿐, 그 찬사의 어느 대목이든 제 것으로 삼으려는 기색도, 노샘프턴셔에 마음 두라는 그의 뜻을 북돋우려는 기색도 조금도 내비치지 않았다. 누가 저를 지켜보고 있는지를 알아챈 헨리 크로퍼드는, 같은 화제를 두고 이번에는 좀 더 예사로운 투로, 그러나 여전히 정을 담아 토머스 경에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n“저는 경의 이웃이 되고 싶습니다, 토머스 경. 어쩌면 제가 프라이스 양께 그리 말하는 걸 들으셨겠지요. 경께서 승낙해 주시기를, 그리고 그런 세입자를 마다하라고 아드님을 부추기지 않으시기를 바라도 되겠습니까?”\n\n토머스 경은 정중히 목례하며 대답했다. “여보게, 자네를 이곳의 붙박이 이웃으로 삼고 싶지 않은 길은 오직 이 한 가지뿐일세. 나는 에드먼드가 손턴 레이시의 제집에 들어가 살리라 바라고, 또 그리 믿으니 말이야. 에드먼드, 내가 지나친 말을 하는 겐가?”\n\n에드먼드는 이렇게 불려서, 우선 무슨 얘기가 오가고 있는지부터 들어야 했다. 그러나 그 물음을 알아듣고 나자 대답이 궁하지 않았다.\n\n“그야 물론이지요, 아버지, 저는 거기 사는 것 말고는 다른 생각이 없습니다. 하지만 크로퍼드, 나는 자네를 세입자로는 마다해도, 벗으로는 오게. 겨울마다 그 집을 반은 자네 것이라 여기게. 그리고 우리는 자네가 손질한 그 계획대로, 게다가 이번 봄에 자네한테 떠오를 온갖 손질까지 더해서 마구간을 늘리세.”\n\n“우리로선 손해일세.” 토머스 경이 말을 이었다. “저 아이가 떠나는 건 겨우 팔 마일이라 해도 우리 식구 울타리가 달갑잖게 오그라드는 일이니 말이야. 하지만 내 아들 가운데 누구라도 그보다 못한 일에 스스로 만족한다면 나는 깊이 부끄러웠을 걸세. 자네가 이 문제를 별로 깊이 생각해 보지 못한 거야 지극히 자연스러운 일이야, 크로퍼드 씨. 그러나 교구에는 늘 붙박여 사는 성직자라야만 알 수 있는, 그리고 어떤 대리인도 그만큼 채워 줄 수 없는 그런 필요와 요구가 있는 법일세. 에드먼드가 흔한 말로 손턴의 소임을 볼 수야 있겠지, 그러니까 맨스필드 파크를 떠나지 않고도 기도를 읽고 설교를 할 수야 있겠지. 이름뿐인 집에 일요일마다 말 타고 건너가 예배를 치를 수야 있고, 이레마다 서너 시간씩 손턴 레이시의 성직자가 될 수야 있겠지, 그거로 흡족하다면 말이야. 하지만 그거로는 안 될 걸세. 저 아이는 사람의 본성이 주간 설교 한 번으로 전할 수 있는 것보다 더 많은 가르침을 필요로 함을 알고, 또 제가 제 교구민들 틈에 살며 한결같은 마음씀으로 저 자신이 그들을 위하고 아끼는 벗임을 증명해 보이지 않고서는, 그들의 이로움을 위해서든 제 이로움을 위해서든 하는 것이 지극히 하찮음을 아니까.”\n\n크로퍼드 씨가 목례로 수긍했다.\n\n“다시 한번 말하네만,” 하고 토머스 경이 덧붙였다. “손턴 레이시야말로 이 근방에서 크로퍼드 씨를 세입자로 맞아 반기지 않을 유일한 집일세.”\n\n크로퍼드 씨가 목례로 고마움을 표했다.\n\n“토머스 경께서는,” 에드먼드가 말했다. “교구 사제의 소임을 틀림없이 꿰고 계시지요. 그 아드님도 저 역시 그걸 안다는 걸 증명해 보이기를 우리는 바라야겠고요.”\n\n토머스 경의 그 자그마한 일장 연설이 크로퍼드 씨에게 실제로 무슨 효험을 냈든 간에, 그것은 다른 두 사람—더없이 열심히 귀 기울이던 두 사람, 곧 크로퍼드 양과 패니에게 얼마간 거북한 심경을 불러일으켰다. 그 하나는, 손턴이 그토록 이르고 그토록 온전히 그의 보금자리가 되리라는 것을 여태 미처 몰랐던지라, 날마다 에드먼드를 못 보게 된다는 것이 어떤 것일지를 눈을 내리깐 채 곱씹고 있었다. 그리고 다른 하나는, 앞서 제 오라비의 이야기에 기대어 흐뭇한 공상에 잠겨 있던 참에 화들짝 놀라, 저 자신이 그려 오던 미래의 손턴 그림에서 이제는 교회를 지워 내고 성직자를 가라앉힌 채 오직 재산이 넉넉한 독립한 사내가 이따금 머무는, 점잖고 우아하고 요즘풍으로 손질된 거처만을 볼 수 없게 되었으니, 토머스 경을 이 모두를 허문 이로 여겨 뚜렷한 미움을 품고 있었다. 게다가 그의 사람됨과 태도가 자아내는 그 어쩔 수 없는 삼감 탓에, 그리고 그의 논지에 감히 비웃음 한번 던져 제 속을 풀지도 못하는 탓에 그녀는 한층 더 괴로웠다.\n\n그녀의 스페큘레이션이 지녔던 온갖 즐거움이 그 시각으로 다한 셈이었다. 설교가 판을 치는 마당이라면 카드도 그만둘 때였고, 그녀는 이만 끝맺어야겠다 싶은 것이 다행스러웠으니, 자리와 옆 사람을 바꾸어 제 기분을 되살릴 수 있게 된 까닭이었다.\n\n이제 일행의 주된 이들은 난롯가에 들쭉날쭉 모여, 마지막 파장을 기다리고 있었다. 윌리엄과 패니가 가장 동떨어져 있었다. 그 둘은 이제 아무도 없는 카드 탁자에 함께 남아, 남들 생각은 하지도 않은 채 더없이 편안히 이야기를 나누었는데, 마침내 남은 이들 가운데 몇이 그 둘을 눈여겨보기 시작했다. 헨리 크로퍼드의 의자가 맨 먼저 그 둘 쪽으로 방향을 틀었고, 그는 잠깐 말없이 그들을 지켜보며 앉아 있었다. 그러는 사이 그 자신은, 그랜트 박사와 담소하며 서 있던 토머스 경에게 지켜보이고 있었다.\n\n“오늘이 무도회 날이야.” 윌리엄이 말했다. “내가 포츠머스에 있었으면 아마 거기 가 있었겠지.”\n\n“하지만 넌 포츠머스에 있고 싶은 건 아니잖아, 윌리엄?”\n\n“그럼, 패니, 있고 싶지 않고말고. 포츠머스도, 춤도, 너 없이 지내야 할 때면 나야 실컷 겪을 텐데 뭐. 게다가 무도회에 간들 딱히 좋을 것 같지도 않아, 짝을 못 구할지도 모르니까. 포츠머스 계집애들은 임관장 없는 사람은 누구든 콧대를 세우고 깔봐. 사관후보생쯤이야 아무것도 아닌 거나 매한가지지. 사실 아무것도 아니고 말이야. 너 그레고리네 알지. 놀랄 만큼 고운 처녀들로 자랐는데, 나한테는 말도 잘 안 걸려, 루시가 중위한테 구애를 받고 있다고 말이야.”\n\n“어머! 창피해라, 창피해! 하지만 마음 쓰지 마, 윌리엄.” (이 말을 하는 그녀의 두 뺨이 분한 마음에 발갛게 달아올랐다.) “그런 거 마음 둘 값어치도 없어. 너를 두고 하는 얘기도 아니잖아. 그건 그저 이름난 제독들도 저마다 한창때에 다들 더러 겪어 낸 것에 지나지 않아. 그걸 생각해야지, 궂은 날씨나 고된 살림처럼 뱃사람이라면 누구나 겪는 고생거리의 하나려니 하고 마음을 다잡아야 해. 다만 이런 좋은 점이 있잖아, 거기엔 끝이 있고, 그런 걸 하나도 견딜 필요 없는 때가 오리라는 것 말이야. 네가 중위가 되면! 생각만 해 봐, 윌리엄, 네가 중위가 되면 이런 부질없는 일 따위야 얼마나 아무렇지 않겠어.”\n\n“나는 아무래도 영영 중위가 못 될 것 같아, 패니. 다들 임관하는데 나만 못 하니.”\n\n“어머! 사랑하는 윌리엄, 그런 말 마. 그렇게 낙심하지 좀 마. 삼촌이 아무 말씀 안 하셔도, 나는 삼촌이 너를 임관시키려고 온 힘을 다 쓰시리라 믿어. 삼촌도 너만큼이나 그게 얼마나 중한 일인지 아시거든.”\n\n그녀는, 저희가 짐작한 것보다 훨씬 가까이에 삼촌이 와 있는 것을 보고 말문이 막혔다. 그리하여 둘 다 다른 얘기를 꺼낼 밖에 없었다.\n\n“넌 춤추는 거 좋아하니, 패니?”\n\n“응, 아주. 다만 나는 금세 지쳐 버려.”\n\n“나는 너랑 무도회에 가서 네가 춤추는 걸 보고 싶어. 노샘프턴에서는 무도회 같은 거 한 번도 없었니? 나는 네가 춤추는 걸 보고 싶고, 네가 춰 준다면 나도 너랑 추고 싶어. 여기선 아무도 내가 누군지 모를 테고, 나야 다시 한번 네 짝이 되고 싶으니까. 우리 예전엔 곧잘 함께 팔짝팔짝 뛰놀았잖아, 안 그래? 거리에 손풍금이 나왔을 때 말이야. 나도 내 나름으론 제법 춤을 추지만, 아마 네가 나보다 나을 거야.” 그러고는 이제 저희 가까이에 와 있는 삼촌 쪽을 돌아보며, “패니가 아주 춤을 잘 추지 않나요, 삼촌?”\n\n패니는 이런 뜻밖의 물음에 당황해, 어디를 봐야 할지도, 그 대답을 어찌 맞을지도 몰랐다. 무언가 자못 근엄한 나무람이, 아니면 적어도 더없이 싸늘한 무관심의 말이 나와 제 오라비를 곤란케 하고 저를 땅속으로 꺼지게 만들 것만 같았다. 그러나 뜻밖에도 그것은 이보다 나쁠 게 없었다. “유감스럽지만 나는 자네 물음엔 답해 줄 수가 없구먼. 패니가 어릴 적 이래로 나는 그 애가 춤추는 걸 본 적이 없으니 말이야. 하지만 우리 둘 다 언젠가 그 애를 보게 되면 그 애가 숙녀답게 잘해 내리라 믿네. 그리고 머잖아 그럴 기회가 우리에게 있을지도 모르지.”\n\n“저는 댁의 누이가 춤추는 걸 본 즐거움이 있습니다, 프라이스 씨.” 하고 헨리 크로퍼드가 몸을 앞으로 기울이며 말했다. “그러니 그 일을 두고 자네가 물을 수 있는 무엇이든, 자네가 온전히 흡족하도록 답해 드리기로 하지요. 다만 아무래도” (패니가 괴로워하는 낯빛인 것을 보고) “그건 언제 다른 때라야겠어요. 이 자리에 프라이스 양 얘기가 오르내리는 걸 달가워하지 않는 이가 한 사람 있으니까요.”\n\n과연 그는 언젠가 패니가 춤추는 것을 본 적이 있었다. 그리고 그녀가 나긋하고 가뿐한 우아함으로, 흠잡을 데 없는 박자에 맞추어 미끄러지듯 돌더라고 이제 장담해 준 것 또한 못지않게 사실이었다. 그러나 실은 그녀의 춤이 어떠했는지 죽어도 도무지 떠올릴 수가 없었고, 그녀에 관해 무엇을 기억한다기보다 그저 그녀가 그 자리에 있었으려니 지레짐작한 것에 가까웠다.\n\n그래도 그는 그녀의 춤을 흠모하는 이로 통했다. 그리고 토머스 경은 조금도 언짢아하기는커녕 춤 일반을 두고 이야기를 길게 이어 가, 안티과의 무도회를 늘어놓고 여러 다른 춤 방식에 대해—그 조카가 제 눈으로 본 대로 들려줄 수 있는 것을 듣는 데 어찌나 흠뻑 빠져 있던지, 제 마차가 왔다는 전갈도 못 듣고, 노리스 부인의 부산 떠는 소리에야 비로소 그것을 알아챘다.\n\n“자, 패니, 패니, 뭘 하고 있는 게냐? 우리 가야지. 이모가 가는 게 안 보이니? 어서, 어서! 나는 착한 늙은 윌콕스를 기다리게 하는 걸 못 견딘단다. 너는 늘 마부하고 말들을 생각해야 하는 게야. 토머스 경, 마차가 경하고 에드먼드하고 윌리엄을 모시러 되돌아오도록 우리가 정해 뒀어요.”\n\n토머스 경은 반대할 수가 없었으니, 그것은 애초에 저 자신이 미리 제 아내와 처형에게 일러 둔 채비였던 까닭이다. 그러나 노리스 부인은 그것을 잊은 듯, 그 모두를 저 혼자 정했다고 여기는 모양이었다.\n\n이 방문에서 패니의 마지막 심경은 서운함이었다. 에드먼드가 하인에게서 조용히 숄을 받아 가져다 제 어깨에 둘러 주려던 것을, 크로퍼드 씨의 더 잰 손이 낚아채는 바람에, 그녀는 그의 더 도드라진 마음씀에 신세를 지지 않을 수 없었던 것이다.\n\n# 제26장\n\n패니가 춤추는 것을 보고 싶다는 윌리엄의 바람은 그 삼촌에게 한순간 스치는 인상 이상의 것을 남겼다. 토머스 경이 그때 내비친 기회에 대한 그 바람은, 그저 한번 말해 보고 마는 것이 아니었다. 그는 그토록 갸륵한 마음을 흐뭇하게 채워 주고 싶은 뜻을, 패니가 춤추는 것을 보고 싶어 할 다른 누구든 흐뭇하게 채워 주고 젊은이들 모두를 즐겁게 해 주고 싶은 뜻을 한결같이 품고 있었다. 그리하여 그 일을 곰곰 헤아리고 조용히 혼자 마음을 정한 끝에, 그 결실이 이튿날 아침 식사 자리에서 드러났으니, 조카가 한 말을 다시 떠올려 추어 준 뒤 그가 이렇게 덧붙인 것이다. “나는 자네가 이런 호사 한번 못 누리고 노샘프턴셔를 떠나는 게 마음에 안 드네, 윌리엄. 나야 자네들 둘이 춤추는 걸 보면 즐겁겠어. 자네가 노샘프턴의 무도회 얘길 했지. 자네 사촌들도 더러 거기 다녀오긴 했네만, 지금 우리한테는 썩 어울리진 않을 걸세. 자네 이모한테는 그 수고가 너무 벅찰 테니까. 아무래도 노샘프턴 무도회는 생각지 말아야겠어. 집에서 춤판을 벌이는 편이 더 나을 테고. 그리고 만일—”\n\n“아, 토머스 경!” 노리스 부인이 가로막았다. “무슨 말이 나올지 나야 알았지요. 무슨 말을 하려는지 다 알았어요. 만일 착한 줄리아가 집에 있거나, 더없이 소중한 러시워스 부인이 소더턴에 있어서 그런 일에 무슨 명분이나 구실이 되어 준다면, 경께서는 맨스필드에서 젊은이들한테 춤판을 벌여 주고 싶어지실 게 뻔해요. 나는 다 안다니까요. 그 애들이 집에 있어 그 무도회를 빛내 준다면, 바로 이번 크리스마스에라도 무도회를 여실 거예요. 삼촌한테 고맙다고 해라, 윌리엄, 삼촌한테 고맙다고 해!”\n\n“내 딸들은,” 하고 토머스 경이 근엄하게 끼어들며 대답했다. “브라이턴에서 저희 즐거움을 누리고 있고, 나야 그 아이들이 무척 행복하기를 바라오. 하지만 내가 맨스필드에서 벌일까 하는 그 춤판은 그 사촌들을 위한 것이오. 우리 모두가 한자리에 모일 수 있다면 우리의 흡족함이야 물론 더 온전하겠지만, 더러 없는 이가 있다 해서 다른 이들이 즐거움을 못 누릴 것은 아니지요.”\n\n노리스 부인은 더 할 말이 없었다. 그녀는 그의 낯빛에서 결심을 읽었고, 그 놀라움과 언짢음이 평온으로 가라앉으려면 몇 분간의 침묵이 필요했다. 하필 이런 때에 무도회라니! 제 딸들은 없고, 저와는 의논 한마디 없이! 그러나 이내 위안이 다가왔다. 자기가 온갖 일을 도맡는 사람이 되어야 할 터였다. 버트럼 부인이야 물론 온갖 궁리와 수고에서 면제될 테고, 그 모두가 자기 몫으로 떨어질 것이었다. 그날 저녁의 안주인 노릇을 저가 해야 할 터였다. 이 생각이 이내 그녀의 좋은 기분을 넉넉히 되살려, 다른 이들의 행복과 감사가 다 쏟아져 나오기 전에 그녀도 그 자리에 어울릴 수 있게 되었다.\n\n에드먼드와 윌리엄과 패니는 저마다 다른 결로, 약속된 무도회에 대해 토머스 경이 바랄 만한 만큼의 고마운 기쁨을 낯빛과 말로 드러냈다. 에드먼드의 심정은 다른 두 사람을 위한 것이었다. 그의 아버지가 이토록 그의 마음에 흡족한 은혜를 베풀거나 친절을 보인 적은 여태 없었다.\n\n버트럼 부인은 더없이 잠잠하고 흡족해, 이렇다 할 반대가 없었다. 토머스 경이 그 일로 그녀를 거의 성가시게 하지 않겠노라 다짐하니, 그녀는 그에게 “나야 그 수고쯤 조금도 두렵지 않아요. 정말이지 수고랄 게 있을 성싶지도 않은걸요”라고 장담했다.\n\n노리스 부인은 그가 어느 방을 쓰는 게 가장 알맞다 여길지를 두고 훈수를 둘 채비를 하고 있었으나, 그 모두가 이미 짜여 있음을 알았다. 그리고 그녀가 날짜를 두고 넘겨짚어 넌지시 일러 보려 하니, 날짜 또한 정해져 있는 것으로 드러났다. 토머스 경은 그 일의 아주 완전한 얼개를 잡아 보며 재미를 붙이던 참이었다. 그리하여 그녀가 잠자코 듣기만 하면, 그는 초대할 집안의 목록을 죽 읽어 줄 수 있었으니, 그것들로부터 그는 촉박한 통지에 대한 온갖 넉넉한 셈속까지 헤아려 열두 쌍이나 열네 쌍을 이룰 만큼의 젊은이를 그러모을 셈이었다. 또 22일을 가장 알맞은 날로 정하게 된 여러 사정을 낱낱이 일러 줄 수도 있었다. 윌리엄은 24일에 포츠머스에 가 있어야 했다. 그러니 22일이 그가 머무는 마지막 날이 될 터였다. 하지만 이토록 날수가 얼마 안 되는 마당에 그보다 이른 날로 정하는 것은 어리석은 일이었으리라. 노리스 부인은 하는 수 없이, 저 역시 꼭 같은 생각이었다는 것과, 저야말로 22일이 이 일에 단연 가장 좋은 날이라며 막 내놓으려던 참이었다는 것으로 만족해야 했다.\n\n무도회는 이제 정해진 일이 되었고, 저녁이 되기 전에 그와 관련된 모든 이에게 공표된 일이 되었다. 초대장이 부랴부랴 부쳐졌고, 그날 밤 패니 말고도 여러 아가씨가 행복한 걱정으로 머릿속이 가득한 채 잠자리에 들었다. 패니에게는 그 걱정이 때로 행복을 거의 넘어서기까지 했다. 어리고 세상 모르는 데다 고를 밑천도 적고 제 취향에 자신도 없던 그녀로서는, “어떻게 차려입어야 하나” 하는 것이 애타는 근심거리였던 것이다. 그리고 그녀가 지닌 거의 유일한 장신구, 곧 윌리엄이 시칠리아에서 가져다준 무척 예쁜 호박 십자가가 무엇보다 큰 시름이었다. 그것을 매달 것이라곤 리본 조각 하나밖에 없었던 까닭이다. 언젠가 그런 식으로 걸어 본 적이야 있었으나, 다른 아가씨들이 죄다 걸치고 나오리라 싶은 그 온갖 화려한 장신구 한복판에서, 이런 자리에 그리해도 될 노릇일까? 그렇다고 그것을 안 걸 수도 없고! 윌리엄은 금줄도 하나 사 주고 싶어 했으나, 그것은 그의 형편을 넘어서는 일이었다. 그러니 그 십자가를 안 거는 것은 그의 마음을 아프게 하는 일일지도 몰랐다. 이것들은 애태우는 근심거리였으니, 주로 저를 즐겁게 해 주려고 열리는 무도회를 앞두고서도 그녀의 마음을 가라앉히기에 넉넉했다.\n\n그러는 사이 채비는 착착 진행되었고, 버트럼 부인은 그 무엇에도 조금의 성가심 없이 여전히 소파에 앉아 있었다. 가정부가 몇 번 더 다녀갔고, 그녀의 하녀는 새 옷을 짓느라 좀 바삐 서둘렀다. 토머스 경은 지시를 내리고 노리스 부인은 이리저리 뛰어다녔다. 그러나 이 모두가 그녀에게는 아무 수고도 아니었으니, 그녀가 미리 내다본 대로 “정말이지 이 일엔 수고랄 게 하나도 없었다.”\n\n에드먼드는 이 무렵 유난히 근심으로 가득 차 있었다. 그의 마음은 이제 눈앞에 닥친, 삶에서 제 운명을 정하게 될 두 중대사—서품과 혼인—을 헤아리는 데 깊이 잠겨 있었다. 그것은 자못 진중한 일이라, 그중 하나가 이내 뒤따를 그 무도회가 그의 눈에는 이 집 안 다른 누구의 눈에서보다 대수롭잖게 비쳤다. 23일에 그는 저와 같은 처지인, 피터버러 근방의 한 벗에게로 갈 참이었고, 둘은 크리스마스 주간 동안 서품을 받기로 되어 있었다. 그리하면 그의 운명 절반은 정해질 터였다. 그러나 나머지 절반은 그리 술술 구애되지 않을지도 몰랐다. 그의 소임이야 자리 잡히겠으나, 그 소임을 함께 나누고 살려 내고 갚아 줄 아내는 아직 얻지 못할지도 몰랐다. 그는 제 마음이야 알았으나, 크로퍼드 양의 마음을 안다고는 늘 온전히 확신하지는 못했다. 둘이 딱 맞지 않는 점들이 있었고, 그녀가 미덥잖아 보이는 순간들도 있었다. 그리고 그녀의 애정을 온통 믿어, 눈앞에 놓인 여러 일이 정리되어 그가 그녀에게 무엇을 내놓을 수 있는지를 알게 되는 대로 이내 그것을 결판 지으리라 마음먹기까지—거의 마음먹기까지—했으면서도, 그 결말을 두고 애타는 심경과 미심쩍은 시각이 숱했다. 그를 향한 그녀의 마음에 대한 확신이 때로는 아주 굳었다. 그는 오랜 세월 이어진 격려를 되짚어 볼 수 있었고, 그녀는 다른 무엇에서나 그러하듯 사심 없는 애착에서도 나무랄 데가 없었다. 그러나 또 어느 때는 의심과 불안이 그의 바람에 뒤섞였다. 홀로 지냄과 은둔에 대한 그녀의 드러내 놓은 싫증을, 런던살이에 대한 그녀의 뚜렷한 선호를 떠올릴 제, 그가 단호한 거절 말고 무엇을 바랄 수 있었으랴? 아니면 그보다 더 못마땅해할 만한 수락, 곧 그의 편에서 양심이 금할 만한 그런 처지와 소임의 희생을 요구하는 수락 말고는.\n\n이 모두의 결말이 오직 한 물음에 달려 있었다. 그녀는 예전엔 없어선 안 될 것이던 것들을 버릴 만큼 그를 깊이 사랑하는가? 그것들을 더는 없어선 안 될 것이 아니게 만들 만큼 그를 깊이 사랑하는가? 그리고 이 물음은, 그가 끊임없이 저 자신에게 되뇌던 이 물음은, 흔히 “그렇다”로 대답되면서도 때로 “아니다”가 나오기도 했다.\n\n크로퍼드 양은 머잖아 맨스필드를 떠날 참이었고, 이 사정을 두고 “아니다”와 “그렇다”가 바로 요즈음 번갈아 오르내렸다. 그는 그녀가, 런던에서 오래 머물다 가라고 조르는 그 소중한 벗의 편지 얘기를 할 때, 그리고 저를 거기까지 데려다주려고 정월까지 이곳에 남기로 한 헨리의 친절 얘기를 할 때 두 눈이 반짝이는 것을 보았다. 또 그런 여행의 즐거움을 두고 말투마다 “아니다”가 배어 있는 그런 들뜬 목소리로 그녀가 이야기하는 것을 들었다. 그러나 이것은 그 일이 정해진 첫날, 그런 즐거움이 왈칵 터진 첫 시각 동안, 찾아갈 벗들 말고는 눈앞에 아무것도 없을 때 일어난 일이었다. 그 뒤로 그는 그녀가 사뭇 다르게, 다른 심경으로, 더 얼룩덜룩한 심경으로 저를 드러내는 것을 들었다. 그녀가 그랜트 부인에게, 이곳을 떠나기가 서운하다고, 저가 찾아가는 벗도 즐거움도 이곳에 두고 가는 것만 못하지 싶기 시작한다고, 그리고 가야 하는 줄 알고 일단 떠나면 즐겁게 지내리라는 것도 알지만 벌써부터 다시 맨스필드로 돌아올 날을 손꼽고 있다고 말하는 것을 들었던 것이다. 이 모든 데에 “그렇다”가 있지 않은가?\n\n이런 일들을 곰곰 헤아리고, 짜고, 다시 짜야 했던 터라, 에드먼드는 저 자신을 위해서는, 나머지 식구가 한결 고른 정도의 큰 관심으로 손꼽아 기다리는 그 저녁을 그리 대단히 여길 수가 없었다. 두 사촌이 거기서 누릴 즐거움을 빼면, 그 저녁은 그에게 두 집안이 정해 놓은 여느 만남보다 더 값진 것이 아니었다. 어느 만남에서든 크로퍼드 양의 애착을 한층 굳혀 줄 것을 받으리라는 바람이 있기는 했다. 그러나 무도회장의 그 소용돌이는, 아마도 진중한 심경을 북돋우거나 드러내기에 딱히 이롭지는 않았다. 처음 두 곡의 춤을 위해 그녀를 일찌감치 붙잡아 두는 것, 그것이 저 홀로 누릴 수 있는 행복이라 여겨지는 전부였고, 아침부터 밤까지 그 화제를 두고 온 둘레에서 벌어지는 그 온갖 일에도 불구하고 그가 마음 쓸 수 있는 그 무도회에 대한 유일한 채비였다.\n\n무도회 날은 목요일이었다. 그리고 수요일 아침, 무엇을 입어야 할지 여태 도무지 마음을 정하지 못한 패니는, 좀 더 밝은 눈을 가진 이의 조언을 구하기로, 그랜트 부인과 그 아우에게 여쭈어 보기로 마음먹었다. 그들의 널리 인정받는 취향이라면 저를 나무랄 데 없이 이끌어 주리라 여긴 것이다. 그리고 에드먼드와 윌리엄은 노샘프턴에 가 있고 크로퍼드 씨도 나가고 없다 여길 만한 까닭이 있던 터라, 그녀는 오붓하게 의논할 틈이 없을까 하는 걱정 없이 목사관으로 걸어 내려갔다. 그런 의논의 은밀함이야말로 패니에게는 무엇보다 중한 대목이었으니, 그녀는 제 안달을 절반 넘게 부끄러워하고 있었던 까닭이다.\n\n그녀는 목사관에서 몇 발짝 안 되는 데서 크로퍼드 양과 마주쳤는데, 마침 저를 찾아 나서던 참이었다. 그리고 벗이 되돌아가자고 우기지 않을 수 없으면서도 산책을 놓치기는 싫어하는 듯 보이던지라, 그녀는 대뜸 제 볼일을 밝히고, 그토록 친절히 의견을 들려주실 참이라면 그 이야기야 문 안에서든 문 밖에서든 다 나눌 수 있으리라고 말했다. 크로퍼드 양은 그 부탁이 흐뭇한 듯, 잠깐 생각한 뒤, 전보다 훨씬 다정한 투로 패니에게 저와 함께 돌아가자고 조르며, 응접실에 함께 있는 그랜트 박사 부부를 성가시게 하지 않고 오붓하게 정담을 나눌 수 있도록 제 방으로 올라가자고 청했다. 그것은 패니에게 꼭 맞는 셈이었다. 그리하여 그토록 선선하고 친절한 마음씀에 저로서는 크나큰 고마움을 느끼며, 그들은 집 안으로, 위층으로 올라가 이내 그 흥미로운 화제에 깊이 빠져들었다. 그 부탁이 반가웠던 크로퍼드 양은 제 온갖 좋은 판단과 취향을 다 쏟아, 이런저런 제안으로 만사를 손쉽게 해 주고, 격려로 만사를 흐뭇하게 해 주려 애썼다. 옷차림의 굵직한 대목이 다 정해지자—“그런데 목걸이로는 뭘 하실 참이에요?” 크로퍼드 양이 말했다. “오라버니의 십자가를 안 거실 거예요?” 그러면서 그녀는 자그마한 꾸러미 하나를 풀고 있었는데, 그것은 마주쳤을 때 패니가 그녀 손에 든 것을 눈여겨보았던 것이었다. 패니는 이 점에 대한 제 바람과 망설임을 털어놓았다. 그 십자가를 어찌 걸어야 할지도, 그렇다고 어찌 안 걸고 있어야 할지도 모르겠다고. 그러자 자그마한 패물 상자 하나가 그녀 앞에 놓이고, 여러 금줄과 목걸이 가운데서 하나 고르라는 청이 대답으로 돌아왔다. 크로퍼드 양이 챙겨 온 꾸러미가 바로 그것이었고, 그녀가 찾아오려던 목적 또한 바로 그것이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 이제 더없이 친절한 투로, 그 십자가에 쓰고 또 제 정표로 간직하도록 하나 가지라며 패니를 졸랐다. 처음에는 그 제안에 질겁한 낯빛으로 뒷걸음질 치게 만든 그 거리낌을 덜어 주려고, 떠오르는 온갖 말을 다 늘어놓으면서.\n\n“내가 얼마나 많이 모아 뒀는지 보이죠.” 그녀가 말했다. “쓰거나 눈길 주는 것보다 곱절은 많아요. 새것으로 드리는 게 아니에요. 낡은 목걸이 하나 드릴 뿐인걸요. 이 주제넘음을 용서하고, 내 청을 들어줘요.”\n\n패니는 여전히, 진심으로 마다했다. 그 선물은 너무 값진 것이었다. 그러나 크로퍼드 양은 물러서지 않고, 윌리엄이며 십자가며 무도회며 저 자신에 이르기까지 온갖 갈래를 짚어 가며 어찌나 다정하고 간곡히 그 일을 파고들었던지, 끝내 뜻을 이루고야 말았다. 패니는 하는 수 없이 굽히고 말았으니, 자칫 오만이나 무심함이나 그 밖의 어떤 좀스러움으로 몰릴까 저어한 것이다. 그리고 얌전히 못 이기는 척 승낙한 뒤, 고르는 일에 들어갔다. 그녀는 어느 것이 가장 값이 덜 나갈까 알고 싶어 보고 또 보았다. 그러다 마침내, 다른 것들보다 제 눈앞에 더 자주 놓이는 목걸이가 하나 있는 성싶어 그것으로 마음을 정했다. 그것은 금으로 된, 곱게 세공된 것이었다. 그리고 패니야 제 쓰임에 더 알맞도록 좀 더 길고 수수한 줄을 골랐으련만, 이것으로 정하면 크로퍼드 양이 가장 간직하고 싶어 하지 않을 것을 고르는 셈이 되기를 바랐다. 크로퍼드 양은 흡족한 웃음으로 온전히 찬동하고는, 서둘러 그 목걸이를 그녀에게 둘러 주고 그것이 얼마나 잘 어울리는지 보여 주며 그 선물을 마무리 지었다. 패니는 그 어울림에 대해서는 한마디도 흠잡을 말이 없었고, 남은 거리낌만 빼면, 이토록 때맞은 얻음에 더없이 흐뭇했다. 그녀는 어쩌면 다른 누군가에게 신세를 졌더라면 싶기도 했다. 그러나 이것은 못난 심정이었다. 크로퍼드 양은 저를 참된 벗임을 증명하는 그런 친절로 제 아쉬움을 미리 헤아려 준 것이었다. “이 목걸이를 걸 때면 나는 언제나 크로퍼드 양을 생각하고, 그때 얼마나 친절하셨는지 떠올릴 거예요.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“그 목걸이를 걸 때면 다른 사람도 생각해야 해요.” 크로퍼드 양이 대꾸했다. “헨리를 생각해야 하죠, 애초에 그이가 고른 거니까요. 그이가 나한테 준 거예요. 그러니 이 목걸이와 더불어, 맨 처음 준 사람을 기억할 온갖 몫까지 죄다 그쪽한테 넘길게요. 이건 집안 정표가 되는 셈이에요. 오라비를 함께 떠올리지 않고서는 누이도 마음에 두어선 안 되는 거죠.”\n\n패니는 몹시 놀라고 얼떨떨하여 그 선물을 당장 돌려주려 했다. 남의 것이던 것을, 게다가 오라비의 것이던 것을 받다니, 어림없는 일이었다! 그래선 안 되었다! 그리하여 그녀는, 벗의 눈에는 자못 우스꽝스러운 그 간절함과 당황함으로, 목걸이를 다시 솜 위에 내려놓고, 다른 것을 고르든 아니면 아무것도 안 받든 하겠다는 듯 굳게 마음먹은 듯 보였다. 크로퍼드 양은 이보다 더 어여쁜 켕김은 본 적이 없다 싶었다. “얘도 참,” 그녀가 웃으며 말했다. “뭐가 그리 무서워요? 헨리가 이 목걸이를 제 것이라 우기며, 그쪽이 떳떳잖게 손에 넣었다고 여길까 봐 그래요? 아니면 그이가, 삼 년 전 이 세상에 이런 목이 있는 줄도 모르던 시절에 제 돈으로 산 장신구가 그쪽 고운 목에 둘려 있는 걸 보고 너무 우쭐할까 봐 그러는 거예요? 아니면 어쩌면”—짓궂게 곁눈질하며—“우리 둘이 짬짜미했다고, 지금 내가 하는 일이 그이도 알고 그이가 바라서 하는 일이라고 의심하는 거예요?”\n\n패니는 더없이 새빨개진 얼굴로 그런 생각을 극구 부인했다.\n\n“그럼, 됐어요.” 크로퍼드 양이 좀 더 진지하게, 그러면서도 그녀의 말을 조금도 믿지는 않으며 대꾸했다. “그쪽이 무슨 수작을 의심하는 게 아니라는 걸, 그리고 내가 늘 봐 온 대로 아첨 따위엔 의심할 줄 모른다는 걸 나한테 증명하려거든, 그냥 이 목걸이를 받고 더는 그 얘기 말아요. 이게 내 오라비의 선물이었다 해서 받는 데 조금도 거리낄 것 없어요, 이걸 내놓는 내 마음에도 그게 아무 상관 없다고 장담하니까요. 그이는 늘 나한테 이것저것 주거든요. 그이한테서 받은 선물이 하도 많아서 나는 그 절반도 값을 매길 수도, 그이는 그 절반도 기억할 수도 없을 지경이에요. 그리고 이 목걸이로 말하면, 나야 여섯 번도 안 걸어 봤지 싶어요. 무척 예쁘긴 해도 나는 통 눈길도 안 준다니까요. 내 패물 상자에 든 다른 어떤 것이든 진심으로 반겨 드렸겠지만, 그쪽은 마침, 내게 고를 여지가 있다면 다른 무엇보다도 기꺼이 내놓고 그쪽 손에 있는 걸 보고 싶은 바로 그것을 고른 거예요. 더는 마다하지 말아요, 정말 부탁이에요. 이런 하찮은 것에 이만큼 많은 말은 그 절반도 아깝잖아요.”\n\n패니는 감히 더는 맞설 수 없었다. 그리하여 새로운, 그러나 덜 기쁜 고마움과 함께 그 목걸이를 다시 받았으니, 크로퍼드 양의 눈에 도무지 흡족해할 수 없는 어떤 빛이 어려 있었던 까닭이다.\n\n크로퍼드 씨의 달라진 태도를 그녀가 못 느낄 리 없었다. 그녀는 진작부터 그것을 보아 왔다. 그는 분명 그녀의 마음에 들려 애썼다. 그는 정중했고, 자상했고, 예전에 제 사촌들을 대하던 것과 얼추 비슷했다. 그는, 짐작건대, 사촌들에게서 그리했던 것처럼 그녀에게서도 그 평온을 앗아 가려는 것이었다. 그리고 그가 이 목걸이에 얼마간 얽혀 있지나 않을까—그녀는 그렇지 않다고 믿을 수가 없었으니, 크로퍼드 양은 누이로서는 살가워도 여자로서, 벗으로서는 무심했던 까닭이다.\n\n곰곰 헤아리고 미심쩍어하며, 그토록 바라 마지않던 것을 손에 넣고도 그리 큰 흡족함이 따르지 않음을 느끼면서, 그녀는 이제 집으로 다시 걸어갔다. 아까 이 길을 밟던 때에 견주어 근심이 줄었다기보다 그저 결이 바뀐 채로.\n\n# 제27장\n\n집에 이르자 패니는 이 뜻밖의 얻음을, 이 미덥잖은 목걸이라는 좋은 것을 제 자잘한 보물을 죄다 간직해 둔 동쪽 방 어느 아끼는 상자에 넣어 두려고 곧장 위층으로 올라갔다. 그러나 문을 열고 보니, 사촌 에드먼드가 거기 탁자에 앉아 무언가를 쓰고 있는 것이 아닌가! 여태 한 번도 없던 그런 광경이라, 그것은 반가운 만큼이나 놀라운 것이었다.\n\n“패니,” 그가 자리와 펜을 떠나 손에 무언가를 든 채 그녀를 맞으며 이내 말했다. “여기 있어서 미안하구나. 널 찾으러 왔다가, 네가 들어오려니 하고 얼마쯤 기다리던 참에, 네 잉크병을 빌려 내 볼일을 적어 두던 중이었어. 너한테 쓰다 만 쪽지를 보게 될 게다. 하지만 이제 내 볼일을 직접 말할 수 있겠구나. 다름이 아니라 이 자그마한 것을 받아 달라 청하려던 거야—윌리엄의 십자가에 쓸 줄이란다. 일주일 전에 벌써 네 손에 있어야 했는데, 내 아우가 내 짐작보다 며칠이나 늦게 시내에 들어오는 바람에 늦어졌지 뭐냐. 나도 방금 노샘프턴에서야 받았어. 이 줄 자체도 네 마음에 들면 좋겠구나, 패니. 나야 네 수수한 취향에 맞추려 애써 봤다만, 아무튼 너는 내 뜻을 곱게 봐 주고, 이것을 그 참모습 그대로, 곧 네 가장 오랜 벗 가운데 하나가 보내는 사랑의 정표로 여겨 줄 줄 안다.”\n\n그러고서 그는 서둘러 가려 했다. 온갖 아픔과 기쁨의 감정에 짓눌린 패니가 미처 말을 꺼내 보기도 전에. 그러나 한 가지 절실한 바람에 다급해져, 그녀는 이내 소리쳤다. “아! 오빠, 잠깐만요, 제발 멈춰 봐요!”\n\n그가 되돌아섰다.\n\n“고맙다는 말도 못 하겠어요.” 그녀가 몹시 떨리는 투로 말을 이었다. “고맙다는 말로는 어림도 없어요. 저는 도저히 말로 옮길 수 없는 것보다 훨씬 더 많이 느끼고 있어요. 저를 이런 식으로 생각해 주신 그 고운 마음은 이루—”\n\n“그게 할 말 다라면, 패니” 하고 미소 지으며 다시 돌아서려 했다.\n\n“아뇨, 아뇨, 아니에요. 의논드리고 싶어서요.”\n\n거의 저도 모르게 그녀는 어느새 그가 방금 손에 쥐여 준 꾸러미를 풀어, 보석상의 그 정갈한 포장 그대로, 눈앞에 더없이 수수하고 말끔한 민 금줄 하나가 놓인 것을 보고는, 다시금 왈칵 터뜨리지 않을 수 없었다. “어머, 정말 곱기도 해라! 이게 바로 그거예요, 딱 제가 바라던 그것이에요! 이건 제가 가지고 싶다고 여겨 본 유일한 장신구예요. 제 십자가에 꼭 어울리겠어요. 둘은 반드시, 꼭 함께 걸어야 해요. 게다가 이토록 반가운 때에 오다니. 아, 오빠, 이게 얼마나 반가운지 오빠는 모르실 거예요.”\n\n“패니야, 넌 이런 것들을 너무 크게 느끼는구나. 이 줄이 네 마음에 들고, 내일에 맞춰 여기 닿아서 나야 더없이 기쁘다만, 네 고마움은 이런 일에 견주면 한참 과하다. 정말이지 나는 네 기쁨에 보탬이 되는 것보다 더한 즐거움이 이 세상에 없단다. 아니, 나는 떳떳이 말할 수 있어, 이보다 더 온전하고 티 없는 즐거움은 내게 없다고. 이건 아무 흠도 없는 즐거움이거든.”\n\n이런 애정 어린 말에 패니는 한마디도 더 안 하고 한 시간이라도 살 수 있었으련만, 에드먼드가 잠깐 기다리다가, “그런데 나한테 의논하고 싶다는 게 뭐냐?” 하고 물어, 하는 수 없이 그녀의 마음을 그 하늘 높이 나는 데서 끌어내려야 했다.\n\n그것은 그 목걸이에 관한 것이었으니, 그녀는 이제 그것을 더없이 간절히 돌려주고 싶어 그리하는 데 그의 찬동을 얻기를 바랐다. 그녀는 조금 전 방문의 자초지종을 들려주었고, 이제 그녀의 황홀함은 다할 만도 했다. 에드먼드가 그 사정에 어찌나 감복하고, 크로퍼드 양이 한 일에 어찌나 기뻐하고, 둘의 처사가 그토록 딱 맞아떨어진 데 어찌나 흐뭇해했던지, 패니로서는 그의 마음속에서 어느 한 즐거움이—비록 흠이 있을지언정—제 것보다 더 힘이 세다는 것을 인정하지 않을 수 없었던 것이다. 그가 그녀의 생각에 귀 기울여 주고, 의견을 청한 데 대해 무슨 대답이라도 해 주기까지는 한참이 걸렸다. 그는 다정한 상념의 몽롱함에 잠겨, 이따금 반 토막짜리 칭찬의 말만 몇 마디 뇌고 있었던 것이다. 그러나 마침내 정신을 차려 알아듣고 나자, 그는 그녀가 바라는 바에 아주 단호히 반대했다.\n\n“목걸이를 돌려줘! 안 돼, 패니야, 절대 안 될 말이다. 그건 그분에게 몹시 무안을 주는 일이야. 벗의 안락에 보탬이 되기를 바라는 응당한 마음으로 준 것을 되돌려받는 것보다 더 언짢은 심경도 좀처럼 없거든. 그분이 그럴 만한 사람임을 스스로 그토록 잘 보여 준 마당에, 어째서 그 즐거움을 잃어야 하겠니?”\n\n“애초에 저한테 준 것이었다면,” 패니가 말했다. “저도 돌려줄 생각은 안 했을 거예요. 하지만 그건 오라비의 선물이니, 그게 쓸데없어진 마당이면 오히려 크로퍼드 양이 그걸 내놓고 싶지 않으리라 여기는 게 온당하지 않을까요?”\n\n“그분은 그걸 쓸데없다고, 적어도 안 받는다고 여겨선 안 돼. 그리고 그게 원래 오라비의 선물이었다는 건 아무 상관 없어. 그 때문에 그분이 내놓는 걸 삼가지도, 네가 받는 걸 삼가지도 않은 마당이면, 그 때문에 네가 간직하는 걸 삼가서도 안 되지. 그야 물론 내 것보다 더 곱고, 무도회장엔 더 어울릴 테고.”\n\n“아뇨, 더 곱지 않아요, 그 나름으론 조금도 더 곱지 않고, 제 쓰임엔 그 절반도 안 어울려요. 이 줄이야말로 윌리엄의 십자가엔 그 목걸이보다 비할 데 없이 잘 어울리는걸요.”\n\n“하룻밤이잖니, 패니, 단 하룻밤이야. 그게 정말 희생이라 해도 말이다. 나는 네가 곰곰 생각해 보면, 그토록 정성껏 네 안락을 헤아려 준 이에게 아픔을 주느니 차라리 그 희생을 치를 줄 안다. 크로퍼드 양이 너한테 베푼 마음씀은—네가 마땅히 받을 것 이상은 아니야, 그게 그럴 수 있으리라곤 나야말로 결코 생각지 않을 사람이지만—아무튼 그건 한결같았어. 그런데 그걸, 결코 그런 뜻일 리는 없다는 걸 나도 알지만, 아무래도 배은망덕한 낌새를 얼마간 띨 수밖에 없는 것으로 갚는다는 건, 네 성품에 맞지 않는 일이야, 나는 그리 믿는다. 약속한 대로 그 목걸이를 내일 저녁에 걸고, 무도회를 염두에 두고 맞춘 게 아닌 이 줄은 좀 더 예사로운 자리를 위해 간직해 두려무나. 이게 내 조언이야. 나는, 그 친분을 더없이 큰 즐거움으로 지켜보아 온 두 사람 사이에, 냉랭함의 그림자 하나 어리는 것도 원치 않아. 그 두 사람의 성품에는 참된 너그러움과 타고난 섬세함에서 두루 닮은 데가 하도 많아, 주로 처지에서 비롯한 그 몇 안 되는 사소한 다름쯤은 온전한 벗됨을 가로막을 이렇다 할 걸림돌이 못 되거든. 나는 냉랭함의 그림자 하나 이는 것도 원치 않는다.” 그가 목소리를 조금 낮추며 되뇌었다. “이 세상에서 내게 가장 소중한 두 사람 사이에.”\n\n그는 말하면서 이미 가 버렸다. 그리고 패니는 남아서 되도록 제 마음을 가라앉히려 했다. 그녀는 그의 가장 소중한 두 사람 가운데 하나였다—그것이 그녀를 지탱해 줄 터였다. 그러나 다른 하나, 그 첫째가는 하나라니! 그가 이토록 터놓고 말하는 것을 그녀는 여태 들어 본 적이 없었다. 그리고 그것이 그녀가 진작부터 알아채 온 것 말고 더 새로운 것을 일러 준 것은 아니었으나, 그것은 하나의 비수였다. 그의 굳은 확신과 뜻을 일러 준 까닭이다. 그것들은 정해져 있었다. 그는 크로퍼드 양과 혼인할 것이었다. 그것은, 오래 품어 온 온갖 짐작에도 불구하고, 하나의 비수였다. 그리고 그녀는 그 말이 무슨 느낌을 주기까지, 저가 그의 가장 소중한 두 사람 가운데 하나라는 것을 몇 번이고 되뇌어야 했다. 크로퍼드 양이 그에게 어울린다고 믿을 수만 있었다면, 그것은—아, 얼마나 달랐으랴—얼마나 훨씬 견딜 만했으랴! 그러나 그는 그녀를 잘못 보고 있었다. 그는 그녀에게 있지도 않은 미덕을 부여했고, 그녀의 허물은 여전히 예전 그대로건만 그는 이제 그것을 보지 못했다. 이 그릇된 봄을 두고 숱한 눈물을 흘리고서야 패니는 겨우 그 동요를 다스릴 수 있었다. 그리고 뒤따른 그 낙담은, 오직 그의 행복을 위한 뜨거운 기도의 힘으로만 덜어질 수 있었다.\n\n에드먼드를 향한 제 애정에서 지나친 것, 이기심에 가까운 것을 죄다 이겨 내려 애쓰는 것, 그것이 제 도리라 여긴 그녀의 뜻이었다. 그것을 잃음이니 실망이니 부르거나 여기는 것은, 제 겸손함으로는 도저히 걸맞은 말을 찾을 수 없을 만큼 주제넘은 짓이었다. 크로퍼드 양이라면 그럴 만도 한 식으로 그를 생각하는 것은, 그녀에게는 실성한 짓일 터였다. 그녀에게 그는 그 어떤 처지에서도 아무것도 될 수 없었다. 벗보다 더 소중한 그 무엇도 될 수 없었다. 어째서 그런 생각이, 나무라고 금해야 할 만큼이나마 떠오르기라도 했단 말인가? 그것은 제 상상의 언저리조차 스쳐서는 안 될 것이었다. 그녀는 분별 있게 처신하려 애쓰고, 건전한 지성과 정직한 마음으로 크로퍼드 양의 성품을 가늠할 자격과 그를 향한 참된 근심의 특권을 얻으려 애쓸 것이었다.\n\n그녀에게는 지조의 온갖 굳셈이 있었고, 제 도리를 다하기로 굳게 마음먹었다. 그러나 젊음과 천성의 감정 또한 많이 지녔던지라, 자기 다스림의 편에서 이 온갖 갸륵한 결심을 다 한 뒤에, 에드먼드가 저에게 쓰기 시작한 그 쪽지 한 조각을 제 온갖 바람을 넘어선 보물인 양 움켜쥐고, “나의 더없이 사랑하는 패니, 부디 이것을 받아 주는 은혜를”이라는 이 몇 마디를 더없이 애틋한 감격으로 읽고는, 그것을 그 선물의 가장 소중한 부분으로 여겨 그 줄과 함께 넣어 잠가 두었다 한들, 그녀를 그리 크게 나무랄 것은 없으리라. 그것은 그가 그녀에게 보낸, 편지랄 만한 것에 가장 가까운 유일한 것이었다. 그녀는 다시는 못 받을지도 몰랐다. 이토록 그 계기와 문체에서 온전히 흐뭇한 것은 다시는 결코 못 받을 것이었다. 이보다 더 값진 두 줄은, 그 어떤 이름난 작가의 펜에서도 떨어진 적이 없었고—그 어떤 애틋한 전기 작가의 뒤짐에도 이보다 더 온전한 복이 내린 적이 없었다. 여인의 사랑이 지닌 그 열정은 전기 작가의 것마저 넘어선다. 그녀에게는, 그것이 무엇을 전하든 그와는 상관없이, 그 글씨체 자체가 하나의 축복이었다. 에드먼드의 그 예사로운 글씨체가 그어 낸 것 같은 그런 글자를 그린 사람은 여태 아무도 없었다! 아무리 서둘러 쓴 것이라 해도, 이 본보기에는 흠 하나 없었다. 그리고 첫 네 마디의 그 흐름에, “나의 더없이 사랑하는 패니”라는 그 짜임에는, 그녀가 영영 바라보고 있어도 좋을 만한 어떤 어울림이 깃들어 있었다.\n\n이렇게 이성과 여림의 그 행복한 뒤섞임으로 제 생각을 다스리고 제 감정을 다독인 뒤, 그녀는 이윽고 아래층으로 내려가, 버트럼 이모 곁에서 여느 일거리를 다시 하고, 이렇다 하게 기운 없는 티도 안 내며 여느 시중을 그녀에게 들 수 있었다.\n\n바람과 즐거움을 위해 예정된 그 목요일이 왔다. 그날은, 이런 제멋대로에 걷잡을 수 없는 날들이 곧잘 그러는 것보다 더 살갑게 패니에게 열렸다. 아침 식사가 끝나고 얼마 안 되어 크로퍼드 씨가 윌리엄에게 보낸 무척 다정한 쪽지 하나가 전해진 것이다. 그 내용인즉, 자기가 며칠간 볼일로 이튿날 런던에 가지 않을 수 없게 되었으니 아무래도 길동무를 하나 구하려 애써 보지 않을 수 없다고, 그러니 윌리엄이 마음먹고 애초에 정한 것보다 반나절 일찍 맨스필드를 떠날 수만 있다면 제 마차에 한자리 받아 주기를 바란다는 것이었다. 크로퍼드 씨는 제 삼촌이 늘 드는 늦은 만찬 시각에 맞춰 시내에 닿을 참이었고, 윌리엄은 제독 댁에서 그와 함께 만찬을 들자고 초대받았다. 그 제안은 윌리엄 자신에게 무척 반가운 것이었으니, 네 마리 말이 끄는 역마차로 그토록 서글서글하고 유쾌한 벗과 함께 여행한다는 생각이 즐거웠던 것이다. 그리고 그것을 급보를 전하러 상경하는 데 견주며, 그는 제 상상이 떠올려 줄 수 있는 온갖 그 행복과 위엄에 대한 말을 대번에 다 늘어놓았다. 그리고 패니 또한, 다른 까닭에서 더없이 기뻤다. 애초의 계획은 윌리엄이 이튿날 밤 노샘프턴에서 우편마차로 상경하는 것이었는데, 그러면 그가 포츠머스행 마차에 오르기까지 한 시간도 쉴 틈이 없었으련만, 크로퍼드 씨의 이 제안이 비록 그의 벗 노릇을 할 여러 시간을 그녀에게서 앗아 갈지언정, 그녀는 윌리엄이 그런 여정의 고단함을 면하게 된 것이 너무 기뻐 다른 것은 생각할 겨를도 없었다. 토머스 경은 또 다른 까닭으로 그것을 반겼다. 조카가 크로퍼드 제독에게 소개되는 것이 도움이 될지도 몰랐던 것이다. 그 제독에게는, 그가 믿기로, 힘이 있었다. 대체로 그것은 무척 기쁜 쪽지였다. 패니의 기운은 그날 아침 절반을 그것에 기대어 살았으니, 그것을 쓴 이 자신이 떠난다는 데서 얼마간 더 큰 즐거움까지 얻었다.\n\n이토록 코앞에 닥친 무도회로 말하면, 그녀는 마땅히 누렸어야 할, 또는 같은 자리를 손꼽아 기다리는 여러 아가씨—그녀보다 더 마음 편한 처지이되, 그녀에게 돌려질 만한 것보다는 덜 새롭고 덜 흥미롭고 덜 각별한 흐뭇함의 사정에 놓인 그 아가씨들이 응당 누리려니 여겨질 만한, 그 미리 그리는 즐거움의 절반도 누리기엔 너무 많은 동요와 두려움에 시달렸다. 초대받은 이들 절반에게 이름만 알려져 있던 프라이스 양이 이제 처음으로 모습을 드러내는 것이었고, 그 저녁의 여왕으로 여겨질 참이었다. 프라이스 양보다 더 행복할 이가 어디 있으랴? 그러나 프라이스 양은 사교계에 나서는 일에 익도록 자라지 못했다. 그리고 저를 두고 이 무도회가 대체로 어떤 눈으로 여겨지는지 그녀가 알았더라면, 그녀가 이미 품고 있던, 잘못 처신하고 남의 눈에 띄는 데 대한 두려움만 키워 그 마음의 편안함을 크게 줄였으리라. 그리 눈에 띄지도 유난히 고단하지도 않게 춤추는 것, 저녁 절반쯤 버틸 기력과 짝이 있는 것, 에드먼드와는 얼마간 추되 크로퍼드 씨와는 많이는 안 추는 것, 윌리엄이 즐거워하는 것을 보는 것, 그리고 노리스 이모에게서 멀찍이 떨어져 있을 수 있는 것, 그것이 그녀 야심의 극치였고, 그녀가 누릴 수 있는 가장 큰 행복의 가능성을 다 아우르는 듯 보였다. 이것들이 그녀 바람의 최선이었던지라, 그것들이 늘 이길 수는 없었다. 그리하여 주로 두 이모와 함께 보낸 긴 아침나절 내내, 그녀는 흔히 그보다 훨씬 덜 밝은 심경에 잠겨 있었다. 이 마지막 날을 실컷 즐기는 날로 삼기로 굳게 마음먹은 윌리엄은 밖에 나가 도요새를 잡고 있었다. 에드먼드는, 그녀가 짐작할 까닭이 너무 많았듯, 목사관에 가 있었다. 그리고 홀로 남아, 가정부가 저녁상을 제 뜻대로 하려 든다고 심통이 난 노리스 부인의 볶아 댐을 견뎌야 했으니—가정부야 그녀를 피할 수 있었을망정 그녀로서는 그럴 수 없었던지라—패니는 끝내 진이 빠져, 무엇이든 죄다 이 무도회에 딸린 재앙이려니 여기게 되었다. 그리하여 마지막 볶아 댐과 함께 옷을 갈아입으라 내보내지자, 축 처져 제 방으로 걸음을 옮기며, 마치 저에게는 그 몫이 조금도 허락되지 않은 양 행복이라곤 도무지 느낄 수 없는 심정이었다.\n\n천천히 위층으로 오르며 그녀는 어제를 떠올렸다. 목사관에서 돌아와 동쪽 방에서 에드먼드를 본 것이 얼추 이맘때였던 것이다. “오늘 또 거기서 오빠를 보게 된다면!” 그녀는 다정한 공상에 젖어 혼자 뇌었다.\n\n“패니,” 하고 바로 그때 그녀 가까이서 한 목소리가 났다. 흠칫하며 올려다보니, 방금 다다른 로비 건너편에, 다른 계단 위쪽에 에드먼드가 서 있는 것이 보였다. 그가 그녀 쪽으로 다가왔다. “지치고 고단해 보이는구나, 패니. 너무 멀리 걸었나 보다.”\n\n“아뇨, 저는 아예 안 나갔는걸요.”\n\n“그럼 집 안에서 고단한 일을 겪었구나, 그게 더 나쁜데. 차라리 나갔다 오는 게 나을 뻔했다.”\n\n패니는 하소연하기가 싫어, 아무 대답도 안 하는 편이 가장 낫다 싶었다. 그리고 그가 여느 때의 그 친절로 그녀를 바라보기는 했으나, 그녀는 그가 이내 제 낯빛에 대한 생각을 그친 줄로 여겼다. 그는 기운이 없어 보였다. 아마 그녀와 상관없는 무언가가 잘못된 모양이었다. 둘은 함께 위층으로 올라갔으니, 그들의 방은 위층 같은 층에 있었다.\n\n“그랜트 박사 댁에 다녀오는 길이야.” 이내 에드먼드가 말했다. “내 거기 볼일이 뭔지야 넌 짐작하겠지, 패니.” 그리고 그의 낯빛이 어찌나 켕기는 듯하던지, 패니는 오직 한 가지 볼일밖에 떠올릴 수 없었고, 그것이 그녀를 너무 메스껍게 만들어 말도 나오지 않았다. “처음 두 곡의 춤을 위해 크로퍼드 양을 붙잡아 두고 싶어서.” 이것이 뒤따른 설명이었고, 이것이 패니를 다시 살려 내어, 저더러 말하라는 기색인 줄 알아채고 그 결말이 어찌 되었는지 묻는 것 비슷한 무언가를 뇌게 해 주었다.\n\n“응,” 그가 대답했다. “나한테 약속했어. 하지만” (썩 편치 않은 미소를 띠며) “나랑 춤추는 건 이번이 마지막이 될 거라는구나. 진심은 아니야. 나는 그녀가 진심이 아니라 생각하고, 바라고, 확신해. 하지만 그런 말은 안 듣느니만 못하지. 성직자하고는 여태 춤춰 본 적이 없고, 앞으로도 결코 안 추겠다는 거야. 나로서야, 하필 이런—그러니까 하필 이번 주에, 바로 오늘 무도회가 없었더라면 싶어. 내일이면 나는 집을 떠나니까.”\n\n패니는 애써 말을 짜내어 이렇게 말했다. “무언가 오빠를 괴롭히는 일이 생겼다니 저도 무척 안됐어요. 오늘은 즐거운 날이어야 하는데. 삼촌도 그리 되라고 여신 건데요.”\n\n“아 그럼, 그럼! 그리고 즐거운 날이 될 거야. 다 잘 끝날 테고. 나야 잠깐 언짢을 뿐이지. 실은 무도회를 때 안 맞는 것으로 여겨서가 아니야. 그게 무슨 대수겠니? 하지만, 패니,” 하고 그녀의 손을 잡아 걸음을 멈춰 세우며, 나직하고 진지하게 말했다. “너는 이 모든 게 무슨 뜻인지 알잖니. 어찌 된 영문인지 너는 알고 있어. 그리고 내가 어떻게, 왜 언짢은지, 어쩌면 내가 너한테 말해 줄 수 있는 것보다 더 잘 나한테 말해 줄 수 있겠지. 잠깐 너랑 얘기 좀 하자. 너는 참으로, 참으로 다정한 들을 이거든. 나는 오늘 아침 그녀의 태도에 마음이 아팠고, 그걸 도무지 떨쳐 낼 수가 없어. 나는 그녀의 성품이 너만큼이나 곱고 흠 없는 줄 알아. 하지만 예전 벗들의 물듦이 그녀를—그녀의 말에, 그녀가 내세우는 견해에 이따금 그릇된 빛깔을 얼마간 입히는 거야. 그녀는 악을 품는 게 아니라, 그걸 입에 담는 거야, 장난삼아 담는 거지. 그리고 나야 그게 장난인 줄 알지만, 그게 내 넋까지 아프게 하는구나.”\n\n“교육 탓이지요.” 패니가 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n에드먼드도 그에 수긍하지 않을 수 없었다. “그래, 그 삼촌에 그 숙모라니! 그들이 더없이 고운 마음을 상하게 한 거야. 이따금은 말이다, 패니, 너한테 털어놓거니와, 그게 태도 이상으로 보이거든. 마음 자체가 물든 것처럼 보인단 말이야.”\n\n패니는 이것을 제 판단을 구하는 것이려니 여겨, 잠깐 생각한 뒤에 말했다. “저를 그저 들을 이로만 원하시는 거라면, 오빠, 저야 되도록 쓸모가 되겠어요. 하지만 저는 조언자로는 어울리지 않아요. 저한테 조언을 구하진 마세요. 저는 그럴 깜냥이 못 돼요.”\n\n“그런 소임을 마다하는 건 옳아, 패니. 하지만 겁낼 것 없어. 이건 내가 결코 조언을 구할 만한 일이 아니야. 아예 조언 같은 건 안 구하느니만 못한 그런 일이지. 그리고 내 짐작에, 이런 걸 조언 구하는 이는 거의 없어, 제 양심에 거스르도록 부추김받고 싶을 때 말고는. 나야 그저 너랑 얘기하고 싶을 뿐이야.”\n\n“한 가지만 더요. 주제넘음을 용서하세요. 하지만 저한테 어떻게 말씀하실지 조심하세요. 지금 무언가, 나중에 후회하실 만한 말은 저한테 하지 마세요. 그때가 올지도—”\n\n이 말을 하며 그녀의 두 뺨에 홍조가 왈칵 밀려들었다.\n\n“더없이 사랑하는 패니야!” 에드먼드가, 마치 크로퍼드 양의 손이기라도 한 듯 그에 못지않은 뜨거움으로 그녀의 손을 제 입술에 대며 외쳤다. “너는 온통 사려 깊은 마음뿐이구나! 하지만 여기선 그럴 것 없어. 그런 때는 결코 안 와. 네가 넌지시 말하는 그런 때는 결코 오지 않아. 나는 그게 더없이 있음 직하지 않다고 여기기 시작했어. 그 가망은 갈수록 작아지거든. 그리고 설령 그런 때가 온다 해도, 너든 나든 두려워할 만한 것이라곤 기억에 남을 게 하나도 없을 거야. 나는 결코 내 거리낌을 부끄러워할 일이 없을 테고, 그것이 걷힌다면 그건 오직, 그녀가 한때 지녔던 허물을 되새김으로써 오히려 그녀의 됨됨이를 더욱 돋보이게 할 그런 변화로써일 테니까. 내가 지금 한 이런 말을 할 사람은 이 세상에 오직 너뿐이야. 하지만 너는 늘 그녀에 대한 내 생각을 알고 있었지. 너는 내 증인이 되어 줄 수 있어, 패니, 내가 결코 눈멀지 않았다는 걸. 우리가 그녀의 자잘한 잘못들을 두고 얼마나 여러 번 얘기했더냐! 너는 나를 두려워할 것 없다. 나는 그녀에 대한 온갖 진지한 생각을 거의 다 접었으니까. 하지만 나한테 무슨 일이 닥치든 간에, 네 친절과 살핌을 더없이 진실한 고마움 없이 떠올릴 수 있다면, 나야말로 정녕 멍청이일 테지.”\n\n그는 열여덟 살의 경험을 뒤흔들 만큼 충분히 말한 셈이었다. 그는 패니에게 요즈음 알던 것보다 더 행복한 심경을 안겨 줄 만큼 충분히 말한 셈이었다. 그리하여 한결 밝아진 낯빛으로 그녀는 대답했다. “네, 오빠, 저는 확신해요, 오빠라면 결코 다른 짓은 못 하실 거라고요, 어쩌면 그러지 못할 이도 더러 있겠지만요. 오빠가 하고픈 무슨 말이든 듣는 게 저는 두렵지 않아요. 스스로를 억누르지 마세요. 하고픈 건 뭐든 저한테 말씀하세요.”\n\n이제 둘은 삼 층에 이르렀고, 하녀 하나가 나타나는 바람에 더는 이야기를 나누지 못했다. 패니의 그때 마음의 편안함을 위해서는, 어쩌면 그것이 가장 행복한 순간에 끝맺어진 셈이었다. 그가 오 분만 더 얘기할 수 있었더라면, 크로퍼드 양의 온갖 허물과 제 낙담을 죄다 말로 풀어 없앴을지도 모를 노릇이었다. 그러나 사정이 그러했던지라, 둘은 그의 편에서는 고마운 애정 어린 낯빛으로, 그녀의 편에서는 자못 소중한 심경으로 헤어졌다. 그녀는 여러 시간 동안 이런 것을 조금도 느껴 본 적이 없었다. 크로퍼드 씨가 윌리엄에게 보낸 쪽지에서 온 그 첫 기쁨이 스러진 뒤로, 그녀는 정반대의 상태에 있었다. 둘레엔 아무 위안도 없고, 마음속엔 아무 바람도 없었다. 이제 만사가 방긋 웃고 있었다. 윌리엄의 행운이 다시금 그녀의 마음에 되돌아와, 처음보다 더 값진 것으로 보였다. 무도회 또한—눈앞에 그런 즐거운 저녁이 펼쳐져 있다니! 이제 그것은 참된 설렘이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 무도회에 딸린 그 행복한 두근거림을 한껏 안고 무도회를 위해 옷을 갈아입기 시작했다. 만사가 잘 풀렸다. 그녀는 제 모습이 싫지 않았다. 그리고 다시 목걸이들을 만지작거리게 되었을 때, 그녀의 행운은 온전해 보였다. 걸어 보니 크로퍼드 양이 준 것은 도무지 그 십자가의 고리를 통과하지 못했던 것이다. 그녀는 에드먼드의 뜻을 받들어 그것을 걸기로 마음먹었었으나, 그 쓰임엔 너무 컸다. 그러니 그의 것을 걸어야 했다. 그리하여 더없이 흐뭇한 심경으로 그 줄과 십자가를—제 마음에 가장 사랑하는 두 사람의 그 정표를, 온갖 실제와 상상으로 서로에게 그토록 꼭 맞게 지어진 그 더없이 소중한 표시를—이어 목에 둘렀다. 그리고 그것들이 얼마나 윌리엄과 에드먼드로 가득 차 있는지를 보고 느끼고 나니, 그녀는 애쓸 것도 없이 크로퍼드 양의 목걸이 또한 걸기로 마음먹을 수 있었다. 그녀는 그리하는 것이 옳음을 인정했다. 크로퍼드 양에게도 응당한 몫이 있었다. 그리고 그것이 더는 다른 이의 더 굳센 몫과 더 참된 친절을 침범하거나 방해하지 않게 된 마당이니, 그녀는 스스로 기꺼운 마음으로도 크로퍼드 양을 온당히 대할 수 있었다. 그 목걸이는 정말이지 무척 잘 어울렸다. 그리하여 패니는 마침내 제 방을 나섰으니, 저 자신에게도 제 둘레의 모든 것에도 흐뭇이 흡족한 채였다.\n\n버트럼 이모는 이번 일에 뜻밖에도 정신을 바짝 차려 그녀를 챙겼다. 그녀는 누가 시키지도 않았는데, 무도회를 채비하는 패니가 위층 하녀보다 더 나은 손길을 반길지도 모르겠다는 생각이 정말로 들어, 제가 옷을 다 갈아입고 나자 실제로 제 하녀를 그녀에게 거들라고 보냈다—물론 아무 쓸모도 못 될 만큼 너무 늦었지만. 채프먼 부인이 다락층에 막 이르렀을 때 프라이스 양이 옷을 다 갖춰 입고 제 방에서 나오니, 예의치레나 오갈밖에 없었다. 그러나 패니는 버트럼 부인이나 채프먼 부인 자신들이 느낄 만한 것에 거의 못지않게 이모의 마음씀을 느꼈다.\n\n# 제28장\n\n패니가 내려갔을 때 삼촌과 두 이모가 응접실에 있었다. 삼촌에게 그녀는 눈여겨볼 만한 대상이었으니, 그는 그 차림새의 두루 우아함과, 그녀가 유난히 고와 보이는 것을 흐뭇이 바라보았다. 그녀 앞에서 그가 스스로 칭찬을 허락한 것이라곤 그 옷차림의 정갈함과 온당함뿐이었으나, 이내 그녀가 다시 방을 나가자 그는 그 아름다움을 자못 뚜렷한 칭찬으로 입에 올렸다.\n\n“그럼요,” 버트럼 부인이 말했다. “저 애가 참 곱네요. 내가 채프먼을 저 애한테 보냈거든요.”\n\n“곱기만 하겠어요! 아이참, 그럼요!” 노리스 부인이 외쳤다. “저만한 온갖 덕을 입었으니 고와 보일 만도 하지요. 이 집안에서 이렇게 자랐으니 말이에요, 눈앞에서 사촌들의 몸가짐을 죄다 본받는 그 온갖 덕을 누리면서요. 생각 좀 해 보세요, 토머스 경, 경과 내가 저 애한테 얼마나 유별난 덕을 베풀어 주는 노릇을 했는지요. 경께서 지금 눈여겨보신 바로 그 가운도, 소중한 러시워스 부인이 시집갈 때 경께서 저 애한테 손수 내리신 후한 선물이잖아요. 우리가 저 애를 거두지 않았더라면 저 애가 대체 어찌 됐겠어요?”\n\n토머스 경은 더는 말이 없었다. 그러나 다들 식탁에 앉자, 두 젊은이의 눈길이 그에게, 부인들이 물러난 뒤 그 화제를 다시 살며시 건드려 보면 더 잘 통하리라는 것을 일러 주었다. 패니는 저가 좋게 여겨지는 것을 알았고, 곱게 보인다는 자각이 그녀를 한층 더 곱게 만들었다. 여러 까닭으로 그녀는 행복했고, 이내 한층 더 행복해졌다. 이모들을 따라 방을 나서는데, 문을 열어 잡고 있던 에드먼드가 그녀가 지나갈 제 이렇게 말한 것이다. “나랑 춤춰야 한다, 패니. 나를 위해 두 곡을 남겨 둬. 네가 좋은 아무 두 곡이든, 첫 곡만 빼고.” 그녀는 더 바랄 것이 없었다. 그녀는 평생 이토록 들뜸에 가까운 상태에 있어 본 적이 좀처럼 없었다. 무도회 날 사촌들이 예전에 보이던 그 들뜸이 이제는 조금도 놀랍지 않았다. 그것이 정말이지 무척 사랑스러운 것임을 그녀는 느꼈고, 실제로 응접실 여기저기서 춤 스텝을 밟아 보고 있었으니—처음엔 집사가 마련해 둔 그 훌륭한 불을 새로 헤집어 도리어 망치는 데 온통 정신이 팔려 있던 노리스 이모의 눈을 피할 수 있는 동안 말이다.\n\n이어진 반 시간은, 다른 어떤 사정에서였다면 적어도 맥없었을 시간이었으나, 패니의 행복이 여전히 이겼다. 그저 에드먼드와의 그 이야기를 떠올리기만 하면 될 뿐, 노리스 부인의 안절부절이 다 무엇이랴? 버트럼 부인의 하품이 다 무엇이랴?\n\n신사들이 그들과 어울렸다. 그리고 이내 마차를 기다리는 그 달콤한 설렘이 시작되니, 두루 편안하고 즐거운 기운이 감도는 듯하여, 다들 여기저기 둘러서서 이야기하고 웃었으며, 매 순간에 저마다의 즐거움과 저마다의 바람이 깃들었다. 패니는 에드먼드의 그 명랑함 속에 얼마간 안간힘이 있으려니 느꼈으나, 그 애씀이 그토록 감쪽같이 이루어지는 것을 보는 것은 흐뭇한 일이었다.\n\n마차 소리가 정말로 들리고, 손님들이 정말로 모여들기 시작하자, 그녀 자신의 들뜬 마음은 자못 사그라들었다. 그토록 많은 낯선 이의 모습이 그녀를 다시 제 속으로 움츠러들게 만든 것이다. 게다가 토머스 경도 버트럼 부인도 그 태도로는 걷어 낼 만한 종류가 못 되는, 처음 그 큰 무리의 그 근엄함과 격식스러움에 더해, 그녀는 이따금 그보다 더한 것을 견뎌 내라 불려 나오는 자신을 발견했다. 그녀는 삼촌 손에 이끌려 여기저기 소개되었고, 하는 수 없이 말을 걸어 받고, 무릎 굽혀 인사하고, 다시 말해야 했다. 이것은 고된 소임이었고, 이 소임에 불려 나올 때마다 그녀는, 그 광경의 뒤편에서 느긋이 거니는 윌리엄을 바라보며 그의 곁에 있고 싶어 하지 않은 적이 없었다.\n\n그랜트 부부와 크로퍼드 남매의 등장은 반가운 전환점이었다. 만남의 그 뻣뻣함은 그들의 서글서글한 태도와 한결 폭넓은 친분 앞에서 이내 누그러졌다. 자그마한 무리들이 이루어지고, 다들 편안해졌다. 패니는 그 덕을 느꼈다. 그리고 예의차림의 고역에서 물러나, 에드먼드와 메리 크로퍼드 사이를 오가는 제 눈길만 붙잡아 둘 수 있었더라면 다시금 더없이 행복했으련만. 그녀는 온통 사랑스러워 보였다—그러니 그 끝이 무엇인들 안 되겠는가? 크로퍼드 씨가 제 앞에 있음을 알아채면서 그녀 자신의 상념은 끝이 났고, 그가 거의 대번에 그녀를 처음 두 곡의 춤에 붙잡으니 그녀의 생각은 다른 물길로 돌려졌다. 이번 일에서 그녀의 행복은 자못 인간다운 것이라, 곱게 얼룩덜룩했다. 우선 짝을 확보해 둔다는 것은 더없이 요긴한 좋음이었다—시작할 순간이 이제 자못 가까이 다가오고 있었던 까닭이다. 게다가 그녀는 제 값어치를 하도 몰라, 크로퍼드 씨가 청하지 않았더라면 저야말로 가장 늦게까지 찾는 이 없는 사람이 되었으리라고, 그리하여 이런저런 수소문과 부산과 참견을 거쳐서야 겨우 짝을 얻었으리라고, 그것이 얼마나 끔찍했으랴 여겼다. 그러나 그와 동시에 그가 청하는 태도에는 그녀가 달갑잖은 어떤 노골적임이 있었고, 그녀는 그의 눈길이 한순간 제 목걸이를 스치는 것을, 어떤 미소와 함께—미소가 있는 것 같았다—그리하는 것을 보았으니, 그것이 그녀를 얼굴 붉히고 비참하게 만들었다. 그리고 그녀를 어지럽힐 두 번째 눈길이 없었을망정, 그때 그의 뜻은 그저 조용히 상냥하게 굴려는 것뿐인 듯했을망정, 그녀는 그 당혹감을 이겨 낼 수가 없었으니—그가 그것을 알아챈다는 생각에 그 당혹감이 더 커진 까닭이라—그가 다른 누군가에게로 돌아설 때까지 도무지 마음을 가라앉히지 못했다. 그러고서야 그녀는, 춤이 시작되기 전에 짝을, 그것도 자청한 짝을 확보해 두었다는 그 진정한 흡족함으로 차츰 올라설 수 있었다.\n\n무리가 무도회장으로 옮겨 갈 즈음, 그녀는 난생처음 크로퍼드 양 가까이 서게 되었다. 크로퍼드 양의 눈길과 미소는 대번에, 그 오라비의 것이 그러했던 것보다 한결 더 또렷하게 그녀에게로 쏠렸고, 그녀가 그 이야기를 막 꺼내려던 참에, 그 얘기를 어서 끝내고 싶어 안달하던 패니가 서둘러 두 번째 목걸이의—곧 진짜 그 줄의—사연을 밝혔다. 크로퍼드 양은 귀 기울여 들었다. 그리하여 패니에게 건네려던 그 온갖 찬사며 넌지시 비추려던 말이 죄다 잊히고, 그녀는 오직 한 가지만을 느꼈다. 그리고 아까도 밝던 두 눈이 더 밝아질 수 있음을 보여 주며, 그녀는 벅찬 기쁨으로 외쳤다. “그이가요? 에드먼드가요? 딱 그이다운 일이네요. 다른 남자라면 그런 생각도 못 했을 거예요. 나는 이루 말 못 하게 그이를 우러러봐요.” 그러고는 마치 당장 그에게 그리 말하고 싶어 못 견디는 듯 둘레를 둘러보았다. 그는 가까이에 없었다. 그는 부인들 한 무리를 방 밖으로 배웅하는 중이었다. 그리고 그랜트 부인이 두 아가씨에게 다가와 저마다 한쪽 팔을 끼니, 그들은 나머지 이들과 함께 뒤따라갔다.\n\n패니는 가슴이 내려앉았으나, 크로퍼드 양의 심경마저 오래 헤아릴 겨를은 없었다. 그들은 무도회장에 있었고, 바이올린이 연주되고 있었으며, 그녀의 마음은 무언가 진중한 데 붙박일 수 없게 하는 그런 설렘에 잠겨 있었다. 그녀는 두루 어떻게 짜이는지를 지켜보며 만사가 어찌 돌아가는지 봐야 했다.\n\n몇 분 만에 토머스 경이 그녀에게 와서 짝이 정해졌느냐고 물으니, “네, 삼촌. 크로퍼드 씨와요”라는 그 대답은 바로 그가 들으려던 그대로였다. 크로퍼드 씨는 멀리 있지 않았다. 토머스 경이 그를 그녀에게 데려오며 무슨 말인가를 했는데, 그것으로 패니는 자기가 앞장서서 무도회의 첫 춤을 열어야 함을 알아챘다. 여태 한 번도 떠올려 본 적 없는 생각이었다. 그날 저녁의 시시콜콜한 대목을 그려 볼 때마다, 그녀는 에드먼드가 크로퍼드 양과 첫 춤을 시작하려니 여기는 것을 당연시했다. 그리고 그 인상이 어찌나 강했던지, 삼촌이 그 반대로 말하는데도 그녀는 놀라 소리치지 않을 수 없었고, 제가 그럴 자격이 못 됨을 넌지시 비추고, 부디 봐 달라 애원까지 하지 않을 수 없었다. 토머스 경의 뜻을 거슬러 제 의견을 우겨 대는 것은 이 일이 그만큼 절박했다는 증거였다. 그러나 그 첫 제안에 그녀가 어찌나 질겁했던지, 그녀는 실제로 그의 얼굴을 마주 보며 부디 달리 정해지기를 바란다고 말할 수 있었다. 그러나 허사였다. 토머스 경은 미소 지으며 그녀를 북돋우려 하다가, 이내 너무 진지한 낯빛이 되어 너무 단호하게 “그리되어야 한다, 얘야” 하고 말하니, 그녀는 감히 한마디도 더 못 붙였다. 그리하여 그녀는 다음 순간 크로퍼드 씨의 안내로 방 위쪽으로 이끌려, 거기 서서 짝지어 이루어지는 대로 한 쌍 한 쌍 다른 춤꾼들이 합류하기를 기다리는 자신을 발견했다.\n\n그녀는 도무지 믿을 수가 없었다. 그토록 우아한 아가씨를 그토록 많이 제치고 윗자리에 서다니! 그 각별함은 너무나 큰 것이었다. 그것은 그녀를 제 사촌들처럼 대우하는 것이었다! 그리하여 그녀의 생각은, 그 자리에 없는 사촌들에게로, 더없이 꾸밈없고 참으로 애틋한 서운함과 함께 날아갔다—그들이 집에 있어 이 방에서 제 자리를 차지하고, 그들에게 그토록 무척 즐거웠을 그 즐거움을 나누지 못하다니. 그들이 집에서 무도회 여는 것을 온갖 복 가운데 으뜸이라며 얼마나 자주 바라던가! 그런데 그것이 열리는 마당에 그들은 없고—그것도 그녀가 무도회를 열다니—게다가 크로퍼드 씨와 함께라니! 그녀는 그들이 지금 이 각별함을 시샘하지 않기를 바랐다. 그러나 가을의 그 형편을, 언젠가 예전에 이 집에서 함께 춤추던 때에 다들 서로에게 어떤 사이였던가를 되돌아보니, 지금 이 짜임은 그녀 자신조차 도무지 헤아리기 어려운 것이었다.\n\n무도회가 시작되었다. 적어도 첫 춤 동안은 그것이 패니에게 행복이라기보다 차라리 영광이었다. 그녀의 짝은 잔뜩 신이 나서 그 신명을 그녀에게도 옮겨 주려 했으나, 그녀는 저가 더는 남의 눈에 안 띈다 여길 수 있게 되기까지는 조금의 즐거움도 누리기엔 너무 겁에 질려 있었다. 그러나 젊고 예쁘고 상냥했던지라, 그녀에게는 우아함이나 진배없는 어색함밖에 없었고, 그 자리에 있는 이들 가운데 그녀를 칭찬할 마음이 없는 이는 드물었다. 그녀는 사람을 끌었고, 겸손했으며, 토머스 경의 조카딸이었고, 게다가 이내 크로퍼드 씨가 흠모한다는 말까지 돌았다. 그것이면 두루 호감을 사기에 넉넉했다. 토머스 경 자신도 자못 흐뭇하게 그녀가 춤을 따라 내려가는 모습을 지켜보았다. 그는 제 조카딸이 자랑스러웠다. 그리고 노리스 부인이 그러는 듯 그녀의 온갖 몸매의 아름다움을 죄다 맨스필드로 옮겨 심긴 덕으로 돌리지는 않았으되, 그 밖의 모든 것을 대 주었다는 데 스스로 흐뭇해했다. 교육도 몸가짐도 그녀는 그에게 힘입은 것이었다.\n\n크로퍼드 양은 서 있는 토머스 경의 속내를 많이 알아챘다. 그리고 그가 저에게 저지른 온갖 그릇됨에도 불구하고 그의 마음에 들고 싶은 마음이 두루 앞섰던지라, 슬쩍 옆으로 비켜서서 패니에 대해 무언가 흐뭇한 말을 건넬 기회를 잡았다. 그녀의 칭찬은 따뜻했고, 그는 그녀가 바랄 만한 대로 그것을 받아들여, 신중함과 예의와 느린 말투가 허락하는 데까지 거기에 맞장구쳤다. 그리하여 그는 이 화제에서, 이내 그의 부인이 보인 것보다 확실히 한결 나아 보였으니, 메리가 바로 곁 소파에 있는 그녀를 알아채고, 춤추기 전에 몸을 돌려 프라이스 양의 고운 모습을 두고 그녀에게 찬사를 건넸을 때 말이다.\n\n“그럼요, 저 애가 참 고와 보이네요.” 버트럼 부인의 잔잔한 대답이었다. “채프먼이 저 애 옷 입는 걸 거들었어요. 내가 채프먼을 저 애한테 보냈거든요.” 패니가 칭찬받는 것이 정말로 기쁘지 않아서가 아니었다. 다만 채프먼을 그녀에게 보냈다는 제 친절에 어찌나 더 사무쳤던지, 그것을 도무지 머릿속에서 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었던 것이다.\n\n크로퍼드 양은 노리스 부인을 너무 잘 알기에 패니를 칭찬하여 그녀를 흐뭇하게 할 생각은 하지 않았다. 그녀에게는 그저 기회 닿는 대로—“아! 부인, 오늘 밤 그 소중한 러시워스 부인하고 줄리아가 얼마나 아쉬운지 몰라요!” 하는 정도였고, 노리스 부인은 카드 탁자를 꾸리고, 토머스 경에게 훈수를 두고, 샤프롱들을 죄다 방의 더 나은 자리로 옮기려 애쓰느라 저 스스로 잔뜩 일거리를 찾아낸 그 분주함 속에서, 짬 나는 대로 미소와 정중한 말을 되도록 많이 그녀에게 갚아 주었다.\n\n크로퍼드 양은 남을 기쁘게 하려는 뜻에서 다름 아닌 패니 자신에게 가장 크게 헛짚었다. 그녀는 그 자그마한 마음에 행복한 두근거림을 안겨 주고, 흐뭇한 자부심으로 그녀를 채워 주려던 것이었다. 그리하여 패니의 홍조를 잘못 새겨, 처음 두 곡의 춤이 끝난 뒤 그녀에게 가서 의미심장한 눈길로 이렇게 말했을 때에도 저가 그리하고 있으려니 여전히 여겼다. “혹시 우리 오라비가 왜 내일 시내에 가는지 그쪽은 말해 줄 수 있어요? 거기 볼일이 있다면서도 그게 뭔지는 말을 안 해요. 나한테 속내를 안 밝힌 건 이번이 처음이라니까요! 하지만 우리 다 결국 이 꼴이 되는 거죠. 다들 조만간 뒷전으로 밀려나는 거예요. 자, 이제 나는 그쪽한테 알아봐야겠어요. 부디 좀 말해 줘요, 헨리가 뭐 하러 가는 거예요?”\n\n패니는 그 당혹감이 허락하는 한 한결같이 저는 모른다고 우겼다.\n\n“그럼, 됐어요.” 크로퍼드 양이 웃으며 대꾸했다. “그저 그쪽 오라비를 태워다 주는 즐거움 때문에, 그리고 가는 길에 그쪽 얘기를 하려는 것 때문이려니 여길밖에요.”\n\n패니는 얼떨떨했으나, 그것은 못마땅함에서 온 얼떨떨함이었다. 한편 크로퍼드 양은 그녀가 웃지 않는 것을 의아해하며, 그녀가 지나치게 조바심 낸다 여기거나, 별나다 여기거나, 아무튼 헨리의 마음씀에서 즐거움을 못 느낀다는 것 말고 다른 무엇이든으로 여겼다. 패니는 그날 저녁 내내 자못 큰 즐거움을 누렸다. 그러나 헨리의 마음씀은 그것과 거의 상관이 없었다. 그녀는 그가 그토록 이내 또다시 청해 오지 않았더라면 훨씬 좋았겠다 싶었고, 저녁 식사 시각에 대해 그가 앞서 노리스 부인에게 물어본 것이 죄다 그 저녁의 그 대목에 저를 붙잡아 두기 위한 것이었으리라 의심하지 않을 수 없게 된 것이 싫었다. 그러나 그것은 피할 길이 없었다. 그는 그녀에게 저가 온갖 것의 과녁임을 느끼게 만들었다. 하기야 그가 그것을 언짢게 굴었다고는, 그의 태도에 무례함이나 뽐냄이 있었다고는 그녀도 말할 수 없었다. 그리고 이따금 그가 윌리엄 얘기를 할 때면 그는 정말이지 밉지 않았고, 그의 값어치를 돋우는 따뜻한 마음마저 내비쳤다. 그러나 그래도 그의 마음씀은 그녀의 흡족함에 아무 몫도 하지 못했다. 그녀는 윌리엄을 바라보며 그가 얼마나 온전히 즐거워하는지 볼 때마다 행복했고, 오 분씩이나마 그와 거닐며 그가 제 짝들에 대해 늘어놓는 이야기를 들을 수 있을 때마다 행복했다. 그녀는 저가 흠모받는 줄 아는 것이 행복했다. 그리고 저녁의 대부분 동안 에드먼드와 출 두 곡의 춤이 아직 앞에 남아 있다는 것이 행복했으니, 그녀의 손이 하도 열심히 청해진 터라 그와의 그 정해지지 않은 약속이 내내 눈앞에 아른거렸던 것이다. 그녀는 그 두 곡이 정작 이루어질 때에도 행복했다. 다만 그것은 그의 편의 어떤 들뜬 신명에서도, 아침을 축복해 준 그런 다정한 정겨움의 말에서도 온 것이 아니었다. 그의 마음은 지쳐 있었고, 그녀의 행복은 그 마음이 안식을 찾을 수 있는 벗이 되어 주는 데서 우러났다. “나는 예의차림에 진이 다 빠졌구나.” 그가 말했다. “밤새 쉴 새 없이 지껄였는데, 할 말이라곤 아무것도 없었어. 하지만 너하고는, 패니, 평온이 있을 수 있지. 너는 말을 걸어 달라 바라지 않을 테니까. 우리 침묵의 호사를 누리자.” 패니는 그러자는 말조차 좀처럼 입 밖에 내지 못했다. 아마 대체로, 그가 아침에 털어놓은 바로 그 심경에서 우러난 그 피로는 각별히 존중받을 만한 것이었다. 그리하여 둘은 함께 그 두 곡의 춤을 자못 차분한 고요 속에 추어 내려갔으니, 그 어떤 구경꾼이라도 토머스 경이 작은아들을 위해 아내를 길러 온 것이 아님을 흡족히 여길 만한 그런 고요였다.\n\n그날 저녁은 에드먼드에게 별 즐거움을 주지 못했다. 크로퍼드 양은 둘이 처음 함께 춤출 때 명랑한 신명에 젖어 있었으나, 그를 이롭게 할 수 있는 것은 그녀의 그 명랑함이 아니었다. 그것은 그의 마음을 북돋우기보다 도리어 가라앉혔다. 그리고 그 뒤로도, 그는 여전히 그녀를 다시 찾지 않을 수 없는 자신을 발견했으니, 그녀는 그가 이제 막 몸담으려는 그 직업을 두고 지껄이는 태도로 그를 아주 아프게 했다. 둘은 이야기했고, 둘은 잠잠했다. 그는 조리 있게 따졌고, 그녀는 비웃었다. 그리하여 둘은 끝내 서로 언짢은 채로 헤어졌다. 그들을 지켜보는 것을 도무지 삼갈 수 없던 패니는, 웬만큼 흡족할 만한 것을 넉넉히 보았다. 에드먼드가 괴로워하는데 행복해한다는 것은 야박한 짓이었다. 그러나 그가 정말로 괴로워한다는 바로 그 확신에서 어떤 행복이 일지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n그와의 두 곡의 춤이 끝나자, 더 추고픈 마음도 기력도 거의 다한 상태였다. 그리고 토머스 경은, 짧아지는 춤 대열을 따라 그녀가 춤춘다기보다 그저 걷는 것을, 숨이 차 손을 옆구리에 댄 채 그러는 것을 보고, 아주 앉아 있으라 분부를 내렸다. 그때부터 크로퍼드 씨도 마찬가지로 앉았다.\n\n“가엾은 패니!” 잠깐 그녀를 찾아온 윌리엄이, 죽어라 제 짝의 부채를 부쳐 대며 외쳤다. “벌써 이렇게 나가떨어지다니! 아니, 흥은 이제 막 시작인데. 나야 이 두어 시간은 계속됐으면 하는데. 어떻게 이리 금세 지치니?”\n\n“이리 금세라니! 이 사람아,” 토머스 경이 필요한 만큼 조심스레 회중시계를 꺼내며 말했다. “세 시일세. 그리고 자네 누이는 이런 시각이 몸에 배지 않았어.”\n\n“그럼, 됐어, 패니, 넌 내일 내가 떠나기 전에 일어나지 마. 잘 수 있는 만큼 실컷 자고, 나는 신경 쓰지 말고.”\n\n“어머! 윌리엄.”\n\n“뭐라고! 자네 떠나기 전에 이 애가 일어날 생각을 했단 말인가?”\n\n“아! 그럼요, 삼촌.” 패니가 삼촌 가까이 가려고 냉큼 자리에서 일어나며 외쳤다. “저는 일어나서 오빠하고 아침을 먹어야 해요. 마지막인걸요, 아시잖아요. 마지막 아침이란 말이에요.”\n\n“그러지 않는 게 좋겠다. 저 애는 아홉 시 반이면 벌써 아침을 먹고 떠날 참이야. 크로퍼드 씨, 아마 자네가 아홉 시 반에 그를 데리러 오지?”\n\n그러나 패니가 너무 애타게 매달렸고, 눈에 눈물이 하도 그렁그렁해 마다할 수가 없었다. 그리하여 그것은 너그러운 “그래, 그래!”로 끝났으니, 그것이 곧 허락이었다.\n\n“그래, 아홉 시 반일세.” 윌리엄이 그들을 떠날 참에 크로퍼드가 그에게 말했다. “나야 어김없을 걸세, 나를 위해 일어나 줄 살가운 누이 하나 없을 테니까.” 그러고는 나직한 소리로 패니에게, “나야 그저 쓸쓸한 집에서 서둘러 나올 뿐이겠지요. 댁의 오라비는 내일 시각에 대한 내 생각과 제 생각이 사뭇 다름을 알게 될 겁니다.”\n\n잠깐 궁리한 끝에, 토머스 경은 크로퍼드에게 혼자 먹지 말고 이 집의 이른 아침 식사 자리에 함께하라 청했다. 저 자신도 거기 낄 참이었다. 그리고 그 초대가 선뜻 받아들여지는 것을 보고, 그는—저 자신에게 털어놓지 않을 수 없거니와—실은 바로 이 무도회가 대체로 거기서 비롯한 그 의심이 근거 있는 것이었음을 확신했다. 크로퍼드 씨는 패니를 사랑하고 있었다. 그는 앞으로 어찌 될지를 흐뭇이 내다보았다. 한편 그의 조카딸은 그가 방금 한 일을 고마워하지 않았다. 그녀는 마지막 아침을 온통 윌리엄만 차지하고 싶었던 것이다. 그것은 이루 말할 수 없는 호사였으련만. 그러나 제 바람이 뒤엎어졌어도 그녀 안에는 투덜대는 기색이 없었다. 오히려 제 즐거움이 헤아려지거나 제가 바랄 만한 식으로 무언가가 이루어지는 데 하도 익지 않았던지라, 그녀는 그 뒤따른 어긋남을 두고 원망하기보다, 제 뜻을 그만큼이나 관철한 것을 도리어 놀라워하고 기뻐하는 쪽이었다.\n\n이내 토머스 경은 다시 그녀더러 당장 잠자리에 들라 이르며 그녀의 마음에 얼마간 참견했다. 그의 말은 “이른다”였으나, 그것은 절대 권력의 이름이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 그저 일어나, 크로퍼드 씨의 더없이 다정한 작별 인사를 받으며 조용히 물러날밖에 없었다. 브랭섬 홀의 귀부인처럼 현관문에 멈춰 서서 “한순간, 그뿐” 그 행복한 광경을 바라보고, 아직 열심히 춤에 매달린 대여섯 쌍의 굳센 이들을 마지막으로 한번 눈에 담고서. 그런 다음 그칠 줄 모르는 컨트리 댄스 가락에 쫓기며 큰 계단을 천천히 기어오르니—바람과 두려움으로, 수프와 니거스로 달아오르고, 발이 아프고 고단하며, 안절부절 들뜬 채였으나, 그 모든 것에도 불구하고 무도회란 정말이지 흐뭇한 것이라 느끼면서.\n\n그녀를 이렇게 올려 보내면서 토머스 경은 아마 그저 그녀의 건강만 생각한 것은 아니었으리라. 크로퍼드 씨가 그녀 곁에 앉아 있은 지 이미 오래라는 생각이 들었을지도 모르고, 아니면 그녀가 얼마나 순순한지를 보여 줌으로써 그녀를 아내감으로 천거하려는 뜻이었는지도 모른다.\n\n# 제29장\n\n무도회가 끝났고, 아침 식사도 이내 끝났다. 마지막 입맞춤이 오갔고, 윌리엄은 떠났다. 크로퍼드 씨는 제가 예고한 대로 무척 어김없었고, 식사는 짧고도 즐거웠다.\n\n윌리엄을 마지막 순간까지 배웅한 뒤, 패니는 그 서글픈 변화를 슬퍼하려 자못 시름겨운 마음으로 아침 식사방으로 되걸어 돌아갔다. 그리고 거기서 삼촌은 친절히도 그녀를 홀로 두어 마음껏 울게 해 주었으니, 아마 두 젊은이의 저마다 비워진 의자가 그녀의 애틋한 감회를 불러일으키려니, 그리고 윌리엄의 접시에 남은 차가운 돼지고기 뼈다귀와 겨자가 크로퍼드 씨의 접시에 남은 깨진 달걀 껍데기와 그녀의 심경을 나눠 가질 따름이려니 여긴 것이리라. 그녀는 삼촌이 뜻한 대로 정을 담아 앉아 울었으나, 그것은 오누이의 정을 담은 울음이었을 뿐 다른 어떤 것도 아니었다. 윌리엄은 떠났다. 그리하여 그녀는 이제, 그와 상관없는 부질없는 근심과 이기적인 안달로 그의 방문 절반을 허비해 버린 것만 같았다.\n\n패니의 성미란, 노리스 이모가 제 그 옹색하고 스산한 자그마한 집에서 지내는 것을 떠올릴 때면, 지난번 함께 있을 때 조금이나마 마음을 덜 써 드린 것을 두고 스스로를 나무라지 않고는 도무지 못 배기는 그런 성미였다. 하물며 그녀의 심경으로는, 꼬박 두 주 동안 윌리엄에게 마땅히 해야 할 온갖 것을 다 하고 다 말하고 다 생각했노라 저를 놓아줄 수야 더더욱 없었다.\n\n그날은 무겁고 서글픈 날이었다. 두 번째 아침 식사가 끝나고 얼마 안 되어, 에드먼드가 한 주 동안의 작별을 고하고 피터버러를 향해 말에 올랐으니, 이제 다들 떠나 버렸다. 지난밤에서 남은 것이라곤 추억뿐이었는데, 그것을 나눌 사람이 그녀에겐 아무도 없었다. 그녀는 버트럼 이모와 이야기했다—무도회 얘기를 누군가와는 해야 했던 것이다. 그러나 이모는 무슨 일이 있었는지 워낙 본 것이 적고 워낙 궁금해하는 것도 없던지라, 그것은 무거운 노릇이었다. 버트럼 부인은 저 자신의 것 말고는 누구의 옷차림도, 누구의 저녁 식사 자리도 딱히 기억하지 못했다. “그 매독스 양네 가운데 하나에 대해 무슨 얘길 들었는지도, 프레스콧 부인이 패니의 무엇을 눈여겨봤는지도 도무지 떠올릴 수가 없구나. 해리슨 대령이 방 안에서 가장 훤한 젊은이라고 한 게 크로퍼드 씨를 두고 한 말인지 윌리엄을 두고 한 말인지도 잘 모르겠고—누군가 나한테 뭐라고 소곤거렸는데, 그게 뭘 수 있을지 토머스 경께 여쭤보는 걸 깜빡했지 뭐냐.” 그리고 이것들이 그녀의 가장 긴 말이자 가장 또렷한 전달이었다. 나머지는 그저 맥없는 “그래, 그래. 아주 좋아. 그랬니? 그이가? 나야 그건 못 봤다. 나는 이것저것 분간을 못 하겠구나”뿐이었다. 이것은 자못 딱한 노릇이었다. 그저 노리스 부인의 그 톡 쏘는 대꾸보다 나을 따름이었다. 그러나 그 부인은 남아도는 젤리를 죄다 싸 들고 앓는 하녀를 돌보러 집에 간 터라, 이 자그마한 무리에는—달리 내세울 것은 별로 없었을망정—평온과 화기가 감돌았다.\n\n그날 저녁도 낮처럼 무거웠다. “나는 대체 내가 왜 이러는지 모르겠구나.” 찻그릇이 치워지자 버트럼 부인이 말했다. “머리가 아주 멍하다. 어젯밤 그토록 늦게까지 앉아 있어서 그런 게 틀림없어. 패니, 나 안 졸게 뭐라도 좀 해 다오. 나는 바느질도 못 하겠다. 카드를 좀 가져오렴. 머리가 하도 멍해서.”\n\n카드가 나왔고, 패니는 잠자리에 들 때까지 이모와 크리비지를 했다. 그리고 토머스 경은 혼자 책을 읽고 있던 터라, 그다음 두 시간 동안 그 방에서는 놀이의 셈 말고는 아무 소리도 들리지 않았다—“그래서 그게 서른하나예요. 손패에 넷, 크립에 여덟이고요. 이모가 돌리실 차례예요. 제가 대신 돌려 드릴까요?” 패니는, 스물네 시간이 그 방과 그 집의 그 온 자락에 빚어낸 그 차이를 곱씹고 또 곱씹었다. 어젯밤엔 응접실에서도, 응접실 밖에서도, 어디에서도 온통 바람과 미소, 부산과 움직임, 소란과 눈부심이었다. 이제는 나른함이요, 홀로임이나 진배없었다.\n\n밤새 잘 쉬고 나니 그녀의 기운이 나아졌다. 이튿날 그녀는 윌리엄을 한결 밝게 떠올릴 수 있었다. 그리고 그날 아침이, 떠난 무도회의 그 여운에 그토록 요긴한 온갖 상상의 부풀림과 온갖 장난스러운 웃음을 곁들여, 무척 그럴듯한 투로 목요일 밤을 두고 그랜트 부인과 크로퍼드 양과 도란도란 얘기할 기회를 마련해 준 덕에, 그녀는 그 뒤로 별 애씀 없이 제 마음을 여느 상태로 되돌리고, 지금 이 조용한 한 주의 고요에 손쉽게 순응할 수 있었다.\n\n그들은 정말이지, 그녀가 여태 그곳에서 온종일 함께 지내며 알던 것보다 더 단출한 무리였고, 어느 가족 모임이든 어느 끼니든 그 편안함과 화기가 주로 기대던 그이는 떠나고 없었다. 그러나 이것은 견디는 법을 익혀야 할 일이었다. 그는 머잖아 늘 떠나 있게 될 것이었다. 그리고 그녀는, 이제 삼촌과 같은 방에 앉아 그 목소리를 듣고 그 물음을 받고 심지어 그에 대답까지 하면서도, 예전에 알던 그런 비참한 심경이 들지 않는 것이 고마웠다.\n\n“우리 두 젊은이가 아쉽구려.” 이것이 첫날에도 둘째 날에도, 만찬 뒤 자못 단출해진 무리를 이룰 때 토머스 경이 한 소감이었다. 그리고 패니의 그렁그렁한 눈을 헤아려, 첫날엔 그저 그들의 건강을 비는 것 말고는 더 아무 말도 안 나왔다. 그러나 둘째 날엔 그것이 무언가 더한 데로 이어졌다. 윌리엄이 다정하게 칭찬받고, 그 진급이 빌어졌다. “그리고 이제 그가 우리를 찾아오는 것이 웬만큼 잦아지리라 여겨도,” 토머스 경이 덧붙였다. “그르다 할 까닭이 없소. 에드먼드로 말하면, 우리는 그 없이 지내는 법을 익혀야 하오. 여태 그래 온 것처럼 그가 우리 식구로 있는 것은 이번이 마지막 겨울이 될 테니까.”\n\n“그래요,” 버트럼 부인이 말했다. “하지만 나는 그 애가 떠나지 않았으면 좋겠어요. 다들 떠나는 것 같아요. 다들 집에 있어 주면 좋겠는데.”\n\n이 바람은 주로 줄리아를 겨냥한 것이었으니, 그녀가 방금 마리아와 함께 시내에 가게 해 달라 청한 참이었던 것이다. 그리고 토머스 경은 그 허락을 내리는 것이 두 딸 저마다에게 가장 낫다 여겼던지라, 버트럼 부인은 제 그 착한 성미로야 그것을 막지는 않았을망정, 안 그랬으면 이맘때쯤 이루어졌을 줄리아의 귀가 전망에 그것이 빚어낸 그 변화를 서운해하고 있었다. 토머스 경의 편에서 자못 이치에 맞는 말이 뒤따라, 그 채비에 아내를 수긍시키려 했다. 사려 깊은 어버이라면 마땅히 느껴야 할 온갖 것이 그녀가 쓰라고 내밀어졌고, 정 많은 어머니라면 자식의 즐거움을 북돋우며 반드시 느낄 온갖 것이 그녀의 천성으로 돌려졌다. 버트럼 부인은 잔잔한 “그래요”로 그 모두에 수긍했다. 그리고 십오 분간 말없이 곰곰 생각한 끝에 스스로 이렇게 뇌었다. “토머스 경, 나는 곰곰 생각해 봤는데—우리가 패니를 그렇게 데려온 게 참 잘한 일이다 싶어요. 이제 다른 애들이 다 떠나고 나니 그 덕을 느끼거든요.”\n\n토머스 경은 이 칭찬을 대번에 이렇게 덧붙여 돋우었다. “참으로 맞는 말이오. 우리가 패니를 얼마나 착한 아이로 여기는지 그 애 앞에서 칭찬해 보여 준들, 그 애는 이제 자못 값진 벗이니 말이오. 우리가 그 애에게 친절을 베풀었다면, 이제 그 애는 우리에게 그에 못지않게 없어선 안 될 사람이오.”\n\n“그래요.” 이내 버트럼 부인이 말했다. “그리고 우리가 언제까지나 그 애를 곁에 둘 거라 생각하면 마음이 놓여요.”\n\n토머스 경은 잠깐 뜸을 들이더니, 반쯤 미소 짓고, 조카딸을 슬쩍 보고는, 이내 근엄하게 대답했다. “그 애가 여기서 아는 것보다 응당 더 큰 행복을 약속할 만한 다른 집으로 청함받기 전에는, 그 애가 우리를 떠나는 일이 결코 없기를 나는 바라오.”\n\n“그리고 그럴 성싶지도 않은걸요, 토머스 경. 누가 그 애를 청하겠어요? 마리아야 이따금 소더턴에서 그 애를 반가이 볼지 몰라도, 거기 와서 살라고 할 생각이야 안 할 거예요. 그리고 그 애는 여기가 더 나은 게 분명하고요. 게다가 나는 그 애 없인 못 지내는걸요.”\n\n맨스필드의 큰 저택에서는 그토록 조용하고 평온하게 지나간 그 한 주가, 목사관에서는 자못 다른 빛깔을 띠었다. 적어도 두 집안 저마다의 젊은 아가씨에게는, 그것이 자못 다른 심경을 안겨 주었다. 패니에게는 고요와 편안함이던 것이, 메리에게는 지루함과 언짢음이었다. 얼마간은 성미와 습성의 다름에서 비롯한 것이었다—한쪽은 그토록 쉬 흡족해하고, 다른 쪽은 견디는 데 그토록 익지 않았으니 말이다. 그러나 그보다 더 크게는 처지의 다름에 돌릴 만한 것이었다. 몇몇 관심사에서 둘은 딱 정반대로 맞서 있었다. 패니의 마음으로는, 에드먼드의 부재가 그 까닭에서나 그 결에서나 정말이지 안도였다. 메리에게 그것은 어느 모로 보나 괴로움이었다. 그녀는 날마다, 거의 매시간 그의 벗함이 아쉬웠고, 그것이 하도 아쉬웠던지라 그가 떠난 목적을 헤아려 보아야 짜증밖에는 아무것도 얻지 못했다. 그가 제 값어치를 돋우기에 이보다 더 그럴듯한 것은 궁리해 낼 수 없었으리라—바로 그녀의 오라비가 떠나고 윌리엄 프라이스도 떠나던 바로 그때에 겹쳐 일어나, 그토록 신명 나던 무리의 그 두루 흩어짐 같은 것을 채워 완성한 이번 주의 부재 말이다. 그녀는 그것을 뼈저리게 느꼈다. 그들은 이제, 잇단 비와 눈에 집 안에 갇혀, 할 일도 없고 바랄 만한 변화도 없는 비참한 세 사람이었다. 제 생각을 고집하며 그녀를 거슬러 그대로 밀어붙인 에드먼드에게 화가 났으면서도(그녀는 어찌나 화가 났던지 무도회에서 둘은 벗으로서 헤어졌다고도 할 수 없을 지경이었다), 그녀는 그가 없는 동안 끊임없이 그를 떠올리며, 그의 미덕과 애정에 마음이 머물고, 요즈음 거의 날마다 나누던 그 만남을 다시금 그리워하지 않을 수 없었다. 그의 부재는 쓸데없이 길었다. 그는 그런 부재를 계획해서는 안 되는 것이었다—제가 맨스필드를 떠날 날이 그토록 가까운 마당에, 한 주씩이나 집을 비워서는 안 되는 것이었다. 그러다 그녀는 저 자신을 나무라기 시작했다. 지난번 대화에서 그토록 발끈하며 말하지 말았어야 했다 싶었다. 성직자를 두고 말하면서 자기가 무슨 모진, 무슨 업신여기는 표현을 쓰지나 않았을까 두려웠고, 그건 그러지 말았어야 했다. 그건 무례한 짓이었고, 그른 짓이었다. 그녀는 그런 말을 안 한 것이었으면 하고 진심으로 바랐다.\n\n그녀의 언짢음은 그 한 주로 끝나지 않았다. 이 모두가 나빴으나, 금요일이 다시 돌아오도록 에드먼드가 안 오고, 토요일이 오도록 여전히 에드먼드가 없고, 일요일이 빚어낸 그 다른 집안과의 짧은 왕래를 통해, 그가 그 벗과 며칠 더 머물기로 약속하여 실제로 집에 편지를 써 귀가를 미뤘음을 알게 되니, 그녀는 더한 것을 느껴야 했다.\n\n앞서 조바심과 후회를 느꼈다면—제가 한 말을 뉘우치고 그것이 그에게 너무 세게 미쳤을까 두려워했다면—그녀는 이제 그 모두를 열 곱절 더 느끼고 두려워했다. 게다가 그녀는, 저에게 아주 낯선 언짢은 감정 하나와도 씨름해야 했으니—바로 질투였다. 그의 벗 오언 씨에게는 누이들이 있었다. 그가 그들을 매력적으로 여길지도 몰랐다. 그러나 아무튼, 앞선 온갖 계획대로라면 제가 런던으로 옮겨 갈 바로 그때에 그가 이렇게 떨어져 있다는 것은, 그녀로선 견딜 수 없는 무언가를 뜻했다. 헨리가 제 말대로 사나흘 만에 돌아왔더라면, 그녀는 이제 맨스필드를 떠나고 있었을 터였다. 그녀로선 어떻게든 패니에게 가서 무언가 더 알아보려 애쓰는 것이 아주 절실해졌다. 이런 외로운 비참함 속에 더는 살 수가 없었던 것이다. 그리하여 그녀는, 한 주 전만 해도 도저히 못 이기리라 여겼던 걷기의 온갖 험한 고생을 무릅쓰고 저택으로 향했으니, 무언가 조금이라도 더 들을 요행을 위해, 적어도 그의 이름이나마 들어 보기 위해서였다.\n\n첫 반 시간은 허비되었다. 패니와 버트럼 부인이 함께 있었고, 패니를 저 혼자 차지하지 못하는 한 아무것도 바랄 수 없었던 것이다. 그러나 마침내 버트럼 부인이 방을 나가니, 이내 크로퍼드 양이 되도록 가다듬은 목소리로 이렇게 말문을 열었다—“그래, 그쪽은 사촌 에드먼드가 이렇게 오래 떨어져 있는 게 어때요? 집에 있는 젊은 사람이라곤 그쪽뿐이니, 나는 그쪽이 가장 괴로운 사람이려니 여겨요. 그이가 아쉽겠죠. 그이가 더 오래 머무는 게 놀랍진 않아요?”\n\n“모르겠어요.” 패니가 머뭇거리며 말했다. “네, 딱히 그럴 줄은 몰랐어요.”\n\n“어쩌면 그이는 늘 말한 것보다 더 오래 머무는지도 몰라요. 젊은 남자들이야 다들 그러는 게 예사니까요.”\n\n“전에 오언 씨를 뵈러 갔던 그 한 번은 안 그러셨는걸요.”\n\n“지금은 그 집이 더 마음에 드는 거죠. 그이 자신도 무척—무척 서글서글한 젊은이잖아요. 그러니 내가 런던에 가기 전에—이제 틀림없이 그리될 텐데—그이를 다시 못 보게 되는 게 나야 자못 서운하지 않을 수 없어요. 나는 날마다 헨리를 기다리는데, 그이가 오는 대로 나를 맨스필드에 붙잡아 둘 건 아무것도 없거든요. 솔직히 그이를 한 번 더 봤으면 싶어요. 하지만 그쪽이 그이한테 내 안부를 전해 줘요. 그래요, 아무래도 안부라야겠죠. 프라이스 양, 우리말에는 뭔가 아쉬운 게 있지 않아요—우리가 함께 나눈 이런 정겨운 사귐에 딱 맞는, 안부와—그리고—사랑 사이의 어떤 것 말이에요? 그 여러 달의 사귐이잖아요! 하지만 여기선 안부면 넉넉하겠죠. 그이 편지가 길었어요? 자기가 뭘 하고 지내는지 많이 적어 주던가요? 크리스마스 흥청거림 때문에 머무는 거예요?”\n\n“저는 편지의 한 대목만 들었어요. 삼촌한테 온 거였거든요. 하지만 무척 짧았지 싶어요. 정말이지 몇 줄뿐이었던 게 분명해요. 제가 들은 거라곤 벗이 더 머물라 졸랐고 그러기로 하셨다는 것뿐이에요. 며칠 더인지, 얼마간 더인지, 어느 쪽인지는 잘 모르겠고요.”\n\n“아! 아버지한테 쓴 거라면. 나는 버트럼 부인이나 그쪽한테 온 건가 했는데. 하지만 아버지한테 쓴 거라면 짧은 것도 이상할 게 없죠. 누가 토머스 경한테 수다를 적어 보내겠어요? 그쪽한테 썼다면 시시콜콜한 게 더 많았을 텐데. 무도회며 모임 얘기를 들었겠죠. 그이가 온갖 것과 온갖 사람에 대한 묘사를 그쪽한테 적어 보냈을 거예요. 오언 양이 몇이나 돼요?”\n\n“다 큰 게 셋이에요.”\n\n“음악을 하나요?”\n\n“도무지 몰라요. 들어 본 적이 없어서요.”\n\n“그게 첫 물음이잖아요.” 크로퍼드 양이 명랑하고 아무렇지 않은 척하며 말했다. “저 스스로 연주하는 여자라면 누구나 남을 두고 꼭 묻고 마는 첫 물음 말이에요. 하지만 어떤 아가씨들이든—이제 막 다 큰 세 자매든 두고 이러쿵저러쿵 묻는 건 참으로 어리석은 짓이죠. 굳이 안 들어도 그들이 어떤지 딱 아니까요. 죄다 무척 교양 있고 상냥하고, 하나는 무척 예쁘죠. 어느 집안에나 미인이 하나씩 있잖아요. 그게 으레 그런 거예요. 둘은 피아노포르테를 치고 하나는 하프를 켜죠. 그리고 다들 노래하거나, 배웠으면 노래했거나, 안 배운 덕에 오히려 더 잘 노래하거나—뭐 그 비슷한 거고요.”\n\n“저는 오언 양네에 대해선 아무것도 몰라요.” 패니가 차분히 말했다.\n\n“아무것도 모르고, 사람들 말마따나 관심은 그보다 더 없다는 거네요. 무심함을 이보다 더 또렷이 드러낸 말투도 없었어요. 하기야 한 번도 못 본 이를 어찌 마음 쓰겠어요? 자, 그쪽 사촌이 돌아오면 맨스필드가 무척 조용한 걸 알게 될 거예요. 그 시끄러운 이들이 죄다 떠났으니까요, 그쪽 오라비하고 내 오라비하고 나 말이에요. 나는 이제 때가 다가오니 그랜트 부인을 두고 떠나는 게 싫어요. 그이도 내가 가는 걸 안 좋아하고요.”\n\n패니는 무언가 말해야 할 것 같았다. “여러 사람이 크로퍼드 양을 아쉬워하리란 건 의심할 여지가 없어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “무척 아쉬워할 거예요.”\n\n크로퍼드 양은, 더 듣거나 보고 싶은 듯 그녀에게 눈길을 돌리더니, 이내 웃으며 말했다. “아 그럼요! 시끄러운 골칫거리가 하나 없어질 때 아쉬워하는 그런 식으로 아쉬워하겠죠. 그러니까, 크게 달라진 게 느껴진다는 거예요. 하지만 나는 미끼를 던지는 게 아니에요. 나한테 빈말은 마요. 내가 정말 아쉬워지면 그야 드러날 테니까요. 나야 나를 보고 싶어 하는 이들이 찾아낼 수 있어요. 나는 무슨 미덥잖거나 멀거나 다가갈 수 없는 데에 있진 않을 테니까요.”\n\n이제 패니는 도무지 말을 꺼낼 수가 없었고, 크로퍼드 양은 실망했다. 응당 알 만한 사람이라 여긴 이에게서 제 힘에 대한 무슨 흐뭇한 다짐을 듣기를 바랐던 것인데, 그녀의 기운은 다시금 흐려졌다.\n\n“그 오언 양네 말인데요,” 이내 그녀가 말했다. “그쪽, 만일 그 오언 양 가운데 하나가 손턴 레이시에 자리 잡게 된다면, 어떨 것 같아요? 더한 일도 있어 왔잖아요. 나야 그들이 그걸 노리고 있지 싶어요. 그리고 그들이야 아주 옳죠, 그들한텐 자못 그럴듯한 자리 잡음일 테니까요. 나는 그들을 조금도 의아해하거나 나무라지 않아요. 저마다 제 형편을 되도록 잘 챙기는 게 누구에게나 도리니까요. 토머스 버트럼 경의 아들이면 어엿한 사람이고, 게다가 이제 그이가 그들과 같은 길에 들어섰죠. 그들 아버지도 성직자, 오라비도 성직자, 다 함께 성직자니까요. 그이는 그들의 정당한 소유물이에요. 마땅히 그들 몫인 거죠. 말이 없으시네요, 패니. 프라이스 양, 아무 말이 없어요. 하지만 이제 솔직히, 오히려 그리되리라 짐작하는 편 아니에요?”\n\n“아뇨.” 패니가 야무지게 말했다. “저는 조금도 그리 짐작 안 해요.”\n\n“조금도라니!” 크로퍼드 양이 냉큼 외쳤다. “그것참 뜻밖이네. 하지만 나야 그쪽이 딱 알고 있으려니—나는 늘 그쪽이라면—어쩌면 그쪽은 그이가 아예 혼인할 것 같지도 않다고 여기나 봐요—아니면 적어도 지금은요.”\n\n“네, 안 그럴 것 같아요.” 패니가, 그 믿음에서든 그것을 인정하는 데서든 저가 그르지 않기를 바라며 나직이 말했다.\n\n그녀의 벗은 그녀를 뚫어져라 바라보았다. 그리고 그런 눈길에 이내 어린 홍조에서 한결 큰 기운을 얻어, 그저 “지금 그대로가 그이한텐 가장 좋죠” 하고는 화제를 돌렸다.\n\n# 제30장\n\n크로퍼드 양의 불안은 이 대화로 자못 가벼워졌다. 그리하여 그녀는, 만일 시험해 봤더라면 이 궂은 날씨 속에서 이 단출한 무리와 또 한 주쯤 더 지내는 것도 너끈히 버텨 냈을 만한 기운으로 다시 집으로 걸어갔다. 그러나 바로 그날 저녁 그 오라비가 런던에서, 여느 때 못지않은—아니, 여느 때 이상의 명랑함을 안고 내려온 터라, 그녀는 제 기운을 더 시험할 일이 없었다. 그가 무엇 하러 갔었는지 여전히 안 밝히는 것도 그저 그 명랑함을 돋울 뿐이었다. 하루 전이라면 짜증스러웠을 테지만, 이제 그것은 유쾌한 농이었다—저 자신을 위한 유쾌한 놀람거리로 짜 둔 무언가를 감추고 있으려니 짐작되었을 뿐이니 말이다. 그리고 이튿날 그것이 정말로 그녀에게 놀람을 안겨 주었다. 헨리는 그저 버트럼가에 들러 안부나 여쭙고 십 분 만에 돌아오겠노라 했건만, 한 시간이 넘도록 돌아오지 않았다. 그리고 함께 정원을 거닐려 그를 기다리던 누이가 마침내 진입로에서 못내 애타게 그를 맞으며 “사랑하는 오빠, 이 오래도록 대체 어디 있었던 거야?” 하고 외치니, 그는 그저 버트럼 부인과 패니와 함께 앉아 있었노라 말할밖에 없었다.\n\n“그이들이랑 한 시간 반이나 앉아 있었다고!” 메리가 외쳤다.\n\n그러나 이것은 그녀 놀람의 시작일 뿐이었다.\n\n“그래, 메리.” 그가 누이의 팔을 제 팔에 끼고, 마치 제가 어디 있는지도 모르는 양 진입로를 따라 걸으며 말했다. “나야 더 일찍은 못 떠났어. 패니가 어찌나 사랑스러워 보이던지! 나는 아주 마음을 정했어, 메리. 내 결심은 온전히 굳었다니까. 놀랄 테지? 아니지, 넌 알 만도 해, 내가 패니 프라이스와 혼인하기로 아주 마음을 굳혔다는 걸.”\n\n이제 놀람은 온전해졌다. 그의 마음속에서야 무엇을 넌지시 일러 주었든 간에, 그에게 그런 뜻이 있으리라는 짐작은 그 누이의 상상에 한 번도 든 적이 없었던 것이다. 그리하여 그녀가 느끼는 그 놀라움을 어찌나 참으로 고스란히 낯빛에 드러냈던지, 그는 하는 수 없이 제가 한 말을 더 낱낱이, 더 엄숙하게 다시 해야 했다. 그의 결심이 참됨이 일단 받아들여지자, 그것은 달갑잖은 것이 아니었다. 그 놀라움에는 즐거움마저 섞여 있었다. 메리는 버트럼 집안과 이어지는 것을 반길 만한 심경이었고, 오라비가 저보다 조금 낮은 데로 장가드는 것을 언짢아하지 않을 심경이었다.\n\n“그래, 메리.” 헨리의 마무리 다짐이었다. “나는 아주 사로잡혔어. 넌 내가 어떤 한가한 심보로 시작했는지 알잖아. 하지만 이게 그 끝이야. 나는, 스스로 좋게 봐주자면, 그 애 마음속에 제법 적잖은 진척을 이뤘어. 그리고 내 마음은 온전히 굳었고.”\n\n“운 좋은, 운 좋은 애로구나!” 겨우 말이 나오자마자 메리가 외쳤다. “그 애한테 이 얼마나 대단한 짝인지! 사랑하는 오빠, 이게 내 첫 심정이겠지. 하지만 못지않게 진심으로 들려줄 내 둘째 심정은, 나는 온 넋으로 오빠의 선택을 반기고, 내가 바라고 비는 만큼이나 진심으로 오빠의 행복을 내다본다는 거야. 오빠는 곱고 자그마한 아내를 얻을 거야, 온통 고마움과 정성뿐인 아내를. 딱 오빠가 받을 만한 사람이지. 그 애한테는 이 얼마나 놀라운 짝인지! 노리스 부인이 툭하면 그 애 운수 얘기를 하던데, 이제 뭐라 할까? 정말이지 온 집안의 기쁨이겠지! 게다가 그 집엔 그 애한테 참된 벗들도 좀 있잖아! 그들이 얼마나 기뻐할까! 하지만 죄다 얘기해 줘! 나한테 끝없이 말해 줘. 언제부터 그 애를 진지하게 생각하기 시작한 거야?”\n\n이런 물음에 대답하는 것만큼 못 할 노릇도 없었으나, 이런 물음을 받는 것만큼 흐뭇한 일도 없었다. “그 달콤한 홍역이 어떻게 제게 스며들었는지” 그는 말할 수 없었다. 그리하여 그가 같은 심정을 말만 조금씩 바꿔 세 번이나 되뇌기도 전에, 누이가 못내 애타게 그를 가로막았다. “아, 사랑하는 오빠, 그러니까 이게 오빠를 런던에 가게 한 거구나! 이게 오빠 볼일이었어! 마음을 굳히기 전에 제독하고 의논하려 했던 거지.”\n\n그러나 이것을 그는 딱 잘라 부인했다. 그는 제 삼촌을 너무 잘 알기에 어떤 혼사 문제로든 그와 의논할 사람이 아니었다. 제독은 혼인을 몹시 싫어했고, 재산이 넉넉한 젊은이가 혼인하는 것은 결코 용서할 수 없는 일이라 여겼다.\n\n“패니를 알게 되면,” 헨리가 말을 이었다. “삼촌은 그 애한테 홀딱 반할 거야. 그 애야말로 제독 같은 사람의 온갖 편견을 걷어 낼 딱 그런 여자거든. 그 애가 바로, 삼촌이 세상엔 없다고 여기는 딱 그런 여자니까. 삼촌이 만일 이제 제 생각을 담아낼 만큼 섬세한 말솜씨가 있다면, 그가 그려 보일 바로 그 있을 수 없는 여자란 말이야. 하지만 그 일이 아주 정해지기 전엔—어떤 참견으로도 못 뒤엎게 정해지기 전엔, 삼촌은 이 일에 대해 아무것도 몰라야 해. 아니지, 메리, 넌 아주 잘못 짚었어. 아직 내 볼일을 못 알아냈다니까.”\n\n“됐어, 됐어, 나는 흡족해. 이제 이게 누구에 관한 일인지 아니까, 나머지야 서두를 것 없어. 패니 프라이스라니! 놀라워, 정말 놀라워! 맨스필드가 그토록 큰일을 해내다니—오빠가 맨스필드에서 제 운명을 찾다니! 하지만 아주 옳은 선택이야. 이보다 더 잘 고를 수야 없었어. 세상에 그보다 나은 애는 없고, 오빠야 재산이 아쉬운 것도 아니지. 그리고 그 애 인연으로 말하면, 좋은 것 이상이잖아. 버트럼가야 두말할 것 없이 이 나라 최고 명문 축에 드니까. 그 애는 토머스 버트럼 경의 조카딸이야. 그거면 세상엔 넉넉하지. 하지만 계속해, 계속. 더 얘기해 줘. 오빠 계획은 뭐야? 그 애는 제 행복을 알아?”\n\n“아니.”\n\n“뭘 기다리는 건데?”\n\n“그저—그저 기회 말고는 거의 아무것도 안 기다려. 메리, 그 애는 제 사촌들과는 달라. 하지만 나는 헛되이 청하진 않을 것 같아.”\n\n“아 그럼! 헛될 리가. 오빠가 설령 덜 서글서글하다 해도—그 애가 아직 오빠를 사랑하지 않는다 쳐도(하기야 나야 그건 거의 의심 안 하지만)—오빠는 안전할 거야. 그 애 성미의 그 상냥함과 고마움이 그 애를 대번에 죄다 오빠 것으로 만들어 줄 테니까. 나는 온 넋으로 그 애가 사랑 없이는 오빠와 혼인 안 할 거라 믿어. 그러니까, 세상에 야심에 휘둘리지 않을 수 있는 애가 있다면 그게 그 애려니 여길 수 있다는 거야. 하지만 그 애더러 오빠를 사랑해 달라 청해 봐, 그럼 그 애는 결코 거절할 마음이 안 들 테니까.”\n\n그녀의 열띤 마음이 침묵 속에 잦아들자마자, 그는 그녀가 듣고 싶어 하는 만큼이나 기꺼이 얘기했다. 그리하여 뒤이은 대화는, 실은 그가 제 심경 말고는 들려줄 것도 없고 패니의 매력 말고는 머무를 것도 없었건만, 그에게 못지않게 그녀에게도 자못 깊이 흥미로웠다. 패니의 얼굴과 몸매의 아름다움, 패니의 태도의 우아함과 마음의 착함이 다함 없는 화제였다. 그녀 성품의 상냥함과 겸손함과 고움이 뜨겁게 늘어놓아졌다—사내의 눈에는 온갖 여자의 값어치에서 그토록 요긴한 몫을 이루는 그 고움, 그것이 없는 데서 이따금 사랑에 빠질지언정 그것이 없다고는 결코 믿지 못하게 하는 그 고움 말이다. 그녀의 성미로 말하면 그는 그것에 기대고 또 그것을 칭찬할 만한 까닭이 넉넉했다. 그는 그것이 시험당하는 것을 곧잘 봐 왔던 것이다. 에드먼드만 빼고 그 집안에서, 어떤 식으로든 끊임없이 그녀의 인내와 참을성을 부려먹지 않은 이가 있던가? 그녀의 정은 분명 두터웠다. 오라비와 함께 있는 그 모습을 보라! 그녀 마음의 그 뜨거움이 그 상냥함에 못지않음을 이보다 더 흐뭇하게 증명할 것이 무엇이랴? 그녀의 사랑을 눈앞에 둔 사내에게 이보다 더 든든할 것이 무엇이랴? 게다가 그녀의 슬기는 온갖 의심을 넘어, 재빠르고 맑았다. 그리고 그녀의 태도는 그녀 자신의 겸손하고 우아한 마음을 비추는 거울이었다. 이뿐만이 아니었다. 헨리 크로퍼드는, 진지한 성찰에는 너무 안 익어 그것들을 제 이름으로 부를 줄은 몰랐을망정, 아내에게 있는 좋은 지조의 값어치를 못 느낄 만큼 어리석지는 않았다. 그리하여 그가 그녀에 대해, 어떤 사내라도 그 신의와 성실에 온전히 기대어도 좋을 만한 그런 처신의 진득함과 한결같음, 그런 드높은 명예심, 그런 예절 지킴을 지녔노라 말할 때, 그는 그녀가 지조 바르고 신심 깊은 사람임을 아는 데서 우러난 것을 드러낸 셈이었다.\n\n“나는 그 애한테 그토록 온전히, 그토록 남김없이 마음을 맡길 수 있어.” 그가 말했다. “그리고 그게 바로 내가 바라는 거야.”\n\n그 누이가, 패니 프라이스에 대한 그의 평가가 그녀의 값어치를 좀처럼 넘어서지 않는다고 정말로 믿었던 만큼, 그녀의 앞날을 반기는 것도 마땅했다.\n\n“생각하면 할수록,” 그녀가 외쳤다. “오빠가 아주 옳은 일을 하고 있다는 확신이 더 들어. 그리고 나야 패니 프라이스를 오빠를 사로잡을 성싶은 애로는 결코 고르지 않았을 테지만, 이제 나는 그 애가 오빠를 행복하게 해 줄 바로 그 애라 믿어. 그 애 평온을 노린 오빠의 그 못된 심보가 알고 보니 정말 영리한 착상이었네. 둘 다 거기서 제 좋음을 찾을 테니까.”\n\n“그런 애한테 그리 굴었으니 못됐지, 아주 못됐어. 하지만 그땐 그 애를 몰랐고, 그 애는 그런 생각을 처음 내 머리에 넣어 준 그때를 슬퍼할 까닭이 없을 거야. 나는 그 애를 아주 행복하게 해 줄 테니까, 메리. 그 애가 여태 저 스스로 누린 것보다, 남에게서 본 그 무엇보다 더 행복하게. 나는 그 애를 노샘프턴셔에서 데려가지 않을 거야. 에버링엄을 세놓고, 이 근방에 자리 하나를 세낼 참이야. 어쩌면 스탠윅스 로지가 좋겠지. 에버링엄은 칠 년 임대로 내놓을 거고. 나야 한마디만 하면 훌륭한 세입자를 얻는 건 떼어 놓은 당상이야. 지금도 내 조건대로 다 내주고 오히려 나한테 고마워할 사람을 셋은 댈 수 있다니까.”\n\n“어머!” 메리가 외쳤다. “노샘프턴셔에 자리를 잡는다니! 그거 반갑네! 그럼 우리 다 함께 있게 되겠구나.”\n\n그리 말하고 나서 그녀는 정신이 들어, 안 한 말이었으면 싶었다. 그러나 얼떨떨해할 것 없었다. 그 오라비는 그녀를 그저 맨스필드 목사관에 머무는 사람으로만 보고, 그저 더없이 다정한 투로 그녀를 제집으로 청하고 그녀에 대한 으뜸가는 권리를 주장하려 대꾸했을 뿐이니 말이다.\n\n“넌 네 시간의 절반 넘게를 우리한테 줘야 해.” 그가 말했다. “나는 그랜트 부인이 나하고 패니와 대등한 권리를 갖는 건 인정 못 해. 우리 둘 다 너에 대한 권리를 가질 테니까. 패니는 정말이지 네 언니가 될 테고!”\n\n메리는 그저 고마워하며 두루뭉술한 다짐이나 할밖에 없었다. 그러나 그녀는 이제, 오라비의 손님이든 올케의 손님이든 그리 여러 달 더는 안 되겠다고 자못 단단히 마음먹고 있었다.\n\n“오빠는 한 해를 런던과 노샘프턴셔로 나눠 지낼 거야?”\n\n“그래.”\n\n“그게 옳아. 그리고 런던에선, 물론, 오빠 자신의 집을 두고. 더는 제독하고 함께가 아니라. 사랑하는 오빠, 오빠 몸가짐이 제독의 그 못된 물듦에 상하기 전에, 오빠가 제독의 그 어리석은 소견을 하나라도 옮기거나, 만찬 앞에 마치 그게 인생 최고의 복인 양 눌러앉는 걸 배우기 전에 제독한테서 벗어나는 게 얼마나 오빠한테 득인지! 오빠야 그 득을 못 느끼겠지, 그이를 아끼는 마음에 눈이 멀었으니까. 하지만 내가 보기엔, 오빠가 일찍 혼인하는 게 오빠를 구하는 길일지도 몰라. 오빠가 말이나 행동에서, 눈빛이나 몸짓에서 제독을 닮아 가는 걸 봤다면 나는 가슴이 무너졌을 거야.”\n\n“됐어, 됐어, 이 점에선 우리 생각이 아주 같진 않구나. 제독한테야 흠이 있지만, 그이는 아주 좋은 사람이고, 나한텐 아버지 이상이었어. 나를 그 절반만큼이라도 내 뜻대로 하게 놔둘 아버지야 드물지. 넌 패니가 그이한테 편견을 갖게 만들면 안 돼. 나는 둘이 서로 아끼게 해야겠어.”\n\n메리는 제가 느낀 바를 입 밖에 내는 것을 삼갔으니, 곧 그 성품과 몸가짐이 그보다 덜 맞을 두 사람은 세상에 없으리라는 것이었다. 시간이 그것을 그에게 일러 줄 터였다. 그러나 그녀는 제독을 두고 이 소감만은 뇌지 않을 수 없었다. “오빠, 나는 패니 프라이스를 하도 높이 봐서, 만일 다음번 크로퍼드 부인이, 학대받은 우리 딱한 숙모가 그 이름 자체를 몸서리치게 미워한 그 까닭의 절반이라도 갖게 되리라 짐작할 수 있다면, 나는 되도록 이 혼인을 막을 거야. 하지만 나는 오빠를 알아. 오빠가 사랑하는 아내라면 세상에 둘도 없이 행복한 여자가 되리라는 걸, 그리고 오빠가 설령 사랑을 그친다 해도 아내는 오빠한테서 신사다운 너그러움과 좋은 교양은 여전히 찾으리라는 걸 나는 알거든.”\n\n패니 프라이스를 행복하게 하려고 세상 온갖 일을 다 안 할 도리가 없다는 것, 또는 패니 프라이스를 사랑하기를 그칠 도리가 없다는 것이, 물론 그의 그 청산유수 같은 대답의 바탕이었다.\n\n“오늘 아침 그 애를 봤어야 하는데, 메리.” 그가 말을 이었다. “이모의 그 아둔함이 요구하는 온갖 것을 그토록 이루 말할 수 없는 고움과 참을성으로 받들며, 그 이모와 함께, 또 그 이모를 위해 바느질을 하고, 일감 위로 몸을 숙일 제 그 낯빛이 곱게 발그레 상기되고, 그러다 다시 제자리로 돌아가, 그 아둔한 여자의 시중을 들려 앞서 쓰고 있던 쪽지를 마저 쓰는데, 이 모든 걸 그토록 꾸밈없는 상냥함으로, 마치 제 뜻대로 쓸 순간이 한 톨도 없는 게 지극히 당연한 일인 양 하면서, 머리칼은 늘 그렇듯 정갈하게 매만져지고, 자그마한 곱슬머리 한 가닥이 쓸 때마다 앞으로 흘러내리면 이따금 뒤로 넘기고, 그 와중에도 여전히 틈틈이 나한테 말을 걸거나, 내 말을 듣는데—마치 듣는 게 즐거운 양 듣는 그 모습을 봤어야 하는데. 그런 그 애를 봤더라면, 메리, 내 마음을 사로잡은 그 애 힘이 언젠가 그칠 수 있으리라곤 넌 넌지시라도 못 비쳤을 거야.”\n\n“사랑하는 오빠,” 메리가 우뚝 멈춰 서서 그의 얼굴을 보며 미소 지으며 외쳤다. “오빠가 이토록 사랑에 빠진 걸 보니 얼마나 기쁜지! 아주 신이 나. 하지만 러시워스 부인하고 줄리아는 뭐라 할까?”\n\n“나야 그들이 뭐라 하든 뭘 느끼든 상관없어. 이제 그들도, 나를 사로잡을 수 있는, 지각 있는 사내를 사로잡을 수 있는 여자가 어떤 여자인지 알게 될 거야. 그 깨달음이 그들한테 무슨 득이라도 되면 좋겠군. 그리고 이제 그들도 제 사촌이 마땅히 받아야 할 대접을 받는 걸 보게 될 테고, 나야 그들이 제 그 몹쓸 소홀함과 야박함을 진심으로 부끄러워하면 좋겠어. 화를 낼 테지.” 그가 잠깐 잠잠하다 한결 차가운 투로 덧붙였다. “러시워스 부인은 무척 화를 낼 거야. 그 여자한텐 쓴 약이겠지. 그러니까, 다른 쓴 약이 그렇듯 잠깐 두어 순간 고약한 맛이 나다가, 이내 삼켜지고 잊힐 거란 말이야. 나야 비록 내가 그 감정의 과녁이었을망정, 그 여자의 감정이 다른 여자들 것보다 더 오래가리라 여길 만큼 자만한 사내는 아니거든. 그래, 메리, 내 패니는 정말이지 달라짐을 느낄 거야. 저한테 다가오는 뭇사람의 처신에서 날마다, 시시각각의 달라짐을. 그리고 그걸 이룬 이가 바로 나임을, 그토록 마땅히 그 애 몫인 대접을 안겨 줄 이가 바로 나임을 아는 것이 내 행복을 온전케 할 거야. 지금 그 애는 얹혀사는, 힘없는, 벗 없는, 소홀히 여겨지는, 잊힌 사람이야.”\n\n“아냐, 오빠, 다한테는 아니야. 다한테 잊힌 건 아니야. 벗 없거나 잊힌 게 아니라고. 사촌 에드먼드는 결코 그 애를 잊지 않아.”\n\n“에드먼드! 하기야, 나도 그가 대체로는 그 애한테 친절하다고 믿어. 그리고 토머스 경도 그 나름으론 그렇지. 하지만 그건 부유하고, 잘나고, 말 많고, 제멋대로인 삼촌의 방식일 뿐이야. 토머스 경과 에드먼드가 힘을 합친들, 세상에서 그 애의 행복과 안락과 명예와 위엄을 위해 대체 무얼 할 수 있으며, 무얼 하고 있느냐, 내가 할 것에 견주면 말이야?”\n\n# 제31장\n\n헨리 크로퍼드는 이튿날 아침 다시 맨스필드 파크에 왔는데, 여느 방문이 허락될 만한 것보다 이른 시각이었다. 두 부인이 아침 식사방에 함께 있었고, 그에게는 다행스럽게도 버트럼 부인이 그가 들어설 바로 그 참에 막 방을 나서려는 중이었다. 그녀는 거의 문간에 이르러 있던 터라, 그 수고를 도무지 헛되이 하기는 싫어, 정중히 그를 맞고, 저를 기다리는 이가 있다는 짤막한 말과, 하인에게 “토머스 경께 알리게” 하는 분부를 남기고는 그대로 나갔다.\n\n헨리는 그녀가 나가는 것이 못내 반가워, 목례하며 그녀를 배웅하고는, 한순간도 더 허비하지 않고 대번에 패니에게로 돌아서서, 편지 몇 통을 꺼내며 더없이 생기 어린 낯빛으로 말했다. “저에게 이렇게 프라이스 양을 단둘이 만날 기회를 주는 이한테, 저는 이루 헤아릴 수 없이 신세 진 사람임을 인정해야겠습니다. 저는 양이 짐작도 못 할 만큼 이걸 바라 왔거든요. 누이로서 양의 심경이 어떠할지 제가 아는 만큼, 제가 지금 가져온 이 소식을 이 집 안 누구든 양과 먼저 나눈다는 건 저로선 도무지 견딜 수 없었을 겁니다. 그가 임관됐어요. 양의 오라비가 중위가 됐습니다. 저는 양의 오라비의 진급을 축하드리는 이루 말할 수 없는 흡족함을 누리고 있어요. 여기 그걸 알리는 편지들이 있습니다, 방금 막 손에 들어온 것들이에요. 아마 양도 보고 싶으시겠지요.”\n\n패니는 말을 할 수가 없었으나, 그도 그녀가 말하기를 바라지 않았다. 그녀 눈의 그 표정을, 낯빛의 그 바뀜을, 그 심경의 오르내림을, 그 미심쩍음과 얼떨떨함과 벅찬 기쁨을 보는 것으로 넉넉했다. 그녀는 그가 건네는 대로 편지들을 받았다. 첫 통은 제독이 조카에게, 저가 떠맡은 일—곧 젊은 프라이스의 진급—을 이뤄 냈음을 몇 마디로 알리는 것이었고, 거기에 두 통이 더 동봉되어 있었다. 하나는 해군장관의 비서가, 제독이 이 일에 나서게 한 한 벗에게 보낸 것이었고, 다른 하나는 그 벗이 제독에게 보낸 것이었다. 그것들로 미루어, 장관께서는 찰스 경의 천거를 받들어 주는 크나큰 기쁨을 누리셨고, 찰스 경은 크로퍼드 제독에 대한 제 마음을 증명할 그런 기회를 얻은 것을 무척 기뻐했으며, 윌리엄 프라이스 씨가 국왕 폐하의 슬루프함 스러시호의 차석 중위 임명장을 받게 된 그 사정이 여러 높은 분들의 넓은 무리에 두루 기쁨을 퍼뜨리고 있음이 드러났다.\n\n이 편지들 아래 그녀의 손이 떨리고, 그 눈이 이것에서 저것으로 오가고, 그 가슴이 벅차오르는 동안, 크로퍼드는 이 일에 대한 제 관심을 꾸밈없이 열띠게 이렇게 이어 나갔다—\n\n“제 행복은 얘기하지 않겠습니다.” 그가 말했다. “그게 아무리 크더라도요, 저는 오직 양의 행복만 생각하니까요. 양에 견주어, 누가 행복할 자격이 있겠습니까? 저는 양이 온 세상 누구보다 먼저 알아야 할 것을 저 혼자 앞서 알고 있었다는 게 제 자신한테 거의 미울 지경이었어요. 그래도 저는 한순간도 허비하지 않았습니다. 오늘 아침 우편이 늦었지만, 그 뒤로는 한순간의 지체도 없었어요. 이 일로 제가 얼마나 조바심 나고, 얼마나 애타고, 얼마나 미칠 것 같았는지는 이루 그려 내지 않으렵니다. 런던에 있는 동안 이걸 마무리 짓지 못한 게 얼마나 사무치게 분하고, 얼마나 모질게 실망스러웠는지도요! 저는 그 바람에 하루하루 거기 붙들려 있었어요. 이만큼 소중한 일이 아니고서야 저를 그 절반의 시간이나마 맨스필드에서 떼어 놓았을 리 없거든요. 하지만 제 삼촌이 제가 바랄 수 있는 온갖 열의로 제 바람에 나서 주고 곧바로 애써 주긴 했으되, 한 벗은 부재중이고 또 한 벗은 볼일에 매인 탓에 어려움이 있었고, 끝내 저는 그 마무리를 지켜보려 더 머무는 걸 도무지 견딜 수 없었습니다. 그리하여 그 일을 얼마나 좋은 손에 맡겨 두는지를 알기에, 저는 월요일에 떠나왔어요, 바로 이런 편지들이 여러 우편도 지나기 전에 저를 뒤따라오리라 믿으면서요. 세상에 둘도 없이 좋은 사람인 제 삼촌은, 제가 그러리라 알았던 대로, 양의 오라비를 보고 나서 애써 주었습니다. 삼촌은 그가 몹시 마음에 들었어요. 어제는 얼마나 마음에 들어 했는지 차마 말하지도, 제독이 그를 칭찬한 말의 절반도 옮기지도 못하게 저 자신을 다잡았지요. 그 칭찬이 벗의 칭찬임이 증명될 때까지 그 모두를 미뤄 두었던 겁니다, 오늘이 정말로 그걸 증명해 주듯이요. 이제야 저는 말할 수 있어요, 저조차도 윌리엄 프라이스가 그보다 더 큰 관심을 불러일으키거나, 그보다 더 따뜻한 축원과 더 드높은 칭찬이 뒤따르기를 바랄 수 없다고요—함께 보낸 그 저녁 뒤 제 삼촌이 더없이 자진해서 베푼 것보다 말입니다.”\n\n“그럼 이게 죄다 크로퍼드 씨가 하신 일이란 말이에요?” 패니가 외쳤다. “세상에! 어쩌면 이토록, 이토록 친절하세요! 정말로—크로퍼드 씨 바람으로 그리된 거예요? 죄송해요, 저는 정신이 하나도 없어요. 크로퍼드 제독께서 청하셨어요? 어찌 된 일이에요? 저는 얼이 다 빠졌어요.”\n\n헨리는, 좀 더 앞선 대목에서부터 시작해 제가 한 일을 아주 낱낱이 밝히며 그것을 더 알아듣기 쉽게 해 주는 것이 더없이 기뻤다. 그가 지난번 런던에 간 것은, 오로지 그녀의 오라비를 힐 스트리트에 소개하고, 그를 밀어 주는 데 제독에게 무슨 힘이 있든 그것을 써 달라 설득하려는 것 말고는 다른 뜻이 없었다. 이것이 그의 볼일이었다. 그는 그것을 아무한테도 알리지 않았다. 메리에게조차 한마디도 발설하지 않았다. 그 결말이 미덥지 않은 동안엔 제 심경을 조금도 나눌 수가 없었던 것이다. 아무튼 이것이 그의 볼일이었다. 그리고 그는 제 마음씀이 어떠했는지를 그토록 달아오른 투로 이야기하며, 그토록 세찬 표현을 쓰고, 더없이 깊은 관심에, 두 겹의 동기에, 이루 말할 수 없이 많은 뜻과 바람에 그토록 넘쳐 났던지라, 만일 그녀가 새겨들을 수 있었더라면 그의 속뜻을 도무지 모른 채로 있을 수 없었으리라. 그러나 그녀의 가슴이 하도 벅차고 정신이 여전히 하도 얼떨떨한 터라, 그녀는 그가 윌리엄에 대해 들려주는 것조차 어설프게밖에 못 듣고, 그가 말을 멈출 때면 그저 “어쩌면 이토록 친절하세요! 아, 크로퍼드 씨, 우리는 크로퍼드 씨한테 이루 헤아릴 수 없이 신세를 졌어요! 사랑하는, 사랑하는 윌리엄!” 할 뿐이었다. 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 서둘러 문 쪽으로 옮겨 가며 외쳤다. “저는 삼촌한테 가야겠어요. 삼촌이 되도록 빨리 이걸 아셔야 해요.” 그러나 이것은 용납될 수 없었다. 기회는 너무 좋았고, 그의 심경은 너무 조바심 났다. 그는 대번에 그녀를 뒤쫓았다. “그녀는 가면 안 된다, 오 분만 더 저에게 내주어야 한다”며, 그는 그녀의 손을 잡아 다시 자리로 이끌었고, 그녀가 무엇 때문에 붙들렸는지 미처 짐작도 하기 전에 벌써 그 더한 설명의 한복판에 있었다. 그러나 그녀가 마침내 그것을 알아듣고, 저가 그의 마음에 여태 한 번도 알지 못한 심경을 자아냈다는 것과, 그가 윌리엄을 위해 한 온갖 일이 저에 대한 그의 지나치고 견줄 데 없는 애착의 몫으로 돌려지리라는 것을 믿으라 기대받는 자신을 발견하니, 그녀는 몹시 괴로워 한동안 말을 하지 못했다. 그녀는 그것을 죄다 헛소리로, 그저 그 한때를 속이려는 실없는 희롱이요 정겨움으로 여겼다. 그가 저를 온당찮고 되잖게, 저가 받을 만하지 않은 그런 식으로 대하고 있다고 느끼지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 그것은 딱 그다웠고, 앞서 본 것과 온전히 한 결이었다. 그리고 그녀는 제가 느낀 언짢음의 절반도 내비치려 하지 않았으니, 그가 은혜를 베푼 참이라, 그의 편의 어떤 무례함으로도 그것을 그녀에게 하찮은 것으로 만들 수는 없었던 까닭이다. 윌리엄을 위한 기쁨과 고마움으로 여전히 가슴이 뛰는 동안, 그녀는 오직 저 자신만을 해치는 것에는 모질게 노여워할 수가 없었다. 그리하여 두 번 손을 빼고, 두 번 헛되이 그에게서 돌아서려 애쓴 끝에, 그녀는 일어나 몹시 떨리는 투로 이렇게만 말했다. “그러지 마세요, 크로퍼드 씨, 제발 그러지 마세요! 부디 그러지 마시길 청해요. 이런 얘기는 제게 무척 언짢아요. 저는 가야겠어요. 도무지 견딜 수가 없어요.” 그러나 그는 여전히 얘기를 이어 가며, 제 애정을 그려 보이고, 화답을 청하고, 마침내 그녀에게조차 오직 한 가지 뜻으로밖에 들릴 수 없을 만큼 뚜렷한 말로, 저 자신을, 그 손을, 그 재산을, 그 온갖 것을 그녀가 받아 주기를 청했다. 그러했다. 그가 그리 말한 것이다. 그녀의 놀라움과 얼떨떨함이 더해 갔다. 그리고 그를 진심이라 여길 길을 여전히 몰랐으면서도, 그녀는 도무지 서 있기조차 힘들었다. 그가 대답을 재촉했다.\n\n“안 돼요, 안 돼요, 안 돼요!” 그녀가 얼굴을 가리며 외쳤다. “이건 죄다 헛소리예요. 저를 괴롭히지 마세요. 이런 얘긴 더 못 듣겠어요. 윌리엄에 대한 크로퍼드 씨의 친절은 말로 다 못 할 만큼 저를 신세 지게 했지만, 저는 그런—원치 않고, 견딜 수 없고, 들어서도 안 돼요. 아뇨, 아뇨, 저를 마음에 두지 마세요. 하지만 크로퍼드 씨는 정말로 저를 마음에 두는 게 아니잖아요. 저는 이게 죄다 아무것도 아닌 줄 알아요.”\n\n그녀는 그에게서 왈칵 벗어난 참이었고, 바로 그때 토머스 경이 그들이 있는 방으로 오는 길에 하인에게 말하는 소리가 들렸다. 더 다짐하거나 애원할 때가 아니었다. 하기야 그의 그 낙관적이고 미리 굳어진 마음에는 오직 그녀의 겸손함만이 제가 좇는 행복을 가로막는 듯 보이던 그 순간에 그녀와 헤어져야 한다는 것이 모진 노릇이긴 했다. 그녀는 삼촌이 다가오는 문과 맞은편 문으로 뛰쳐나가, 토머스 경의 예의차림이나 사과가 끝나기도, 그가 손님이 전하러 온 그 기쁜 소식의 첫머리에 이르기도 전에, 벌써 서로 어긋난 심경의 더없는 얼떨떨함 속에 동쪽 방을 이리저리 거닐고 있었다.\n\n그녀는 온갖 것을 두고 느끼고, 생각하고, 떨었다. 들뜨고, 행복하고, 비참하고, 이루 헤아릴 수 없이 신세 졌고, 아주 노여웠다. 그 모두가 도무지 믿기지 않았다! 그는 용서할 수도, 헤아릴 수도 없는 사람이었다! 그러나 악의 뒤섞임 없이는 아무것도 못 하는 것이 그의 버릇이었다. 그는 앞서 그녀를 세상에 둘도 없이 행복한 사람으로 만들어 놓고, 이제 그녀를 모욕한—그녀는 무어라 말할지, 어찌 갈래 지을지, 어찌 여길지 알 수 없었다. 그녀는 그가 진심이기를 바라지 않았으되, 그렇다고 그런 말과 청이 그저 희롱하려는 것이었다면 대체 무엇으로 변명이 되겠는가?\n\n그러나 윌리엄은 중위였다. 그것은 의심할 여지 없는, 티 하나 없는 사실이었다. 그녀는 그것을 영영 생각하며 나머지는 죄다 잊으리라. 크로퍼드 씨는 분명 다시는 그녀에게 그리 말하지 않을 것이었다. 그것이 그녀에게 얼마나 달갑잖은지 그도 보았을 게 틀림없었다. 그러면 그녀는 윌리엄을 향한 그의 우정에 대해 얼마나 고맙게 그를 기릴 수 있으랴!\n\n그녀는 크로퍼드 씨가 집을 떠났음을 확인할 때까지는 큰 계단 위쪽에서 더는 동쪽 방을 벗어나려 하지 않았다. 그러나 그가 갔음을 확신하자, 그녀는 얼른 아래층으로 내려가 삼촌과 함께 있으며, 저 자신의 기쁨뿐 아니라 그 기쁨까지 죄다 누리고, 이제 윌리엄이 어디로 배치될지에 대한 그의 소식이나 그의 짐작에서 온갖 이로움을 얻고 싶어졌다. 토머스 경은 그녀가 바랄 만큼 기뻐했고, 무척 친절하고 스스럼없이 이야기를 나누어 주었다. 그리하여 그녀는 윌리엄에 대해 그와 어찌나 흐뭇하게 얘기를 나누었던지, 저를 언짢게 할 일이라곤 아무것도 없었던 것처럼 느껴질 지경이었다—그 끝머리에 이르러, 크로퍼드 씨가 바로 그날 다시 와서 여기서 만찬을 들기로 되어 있음을 알기 전까지는 말이다. 이것은 더없이 달갑잖은 소식이었으니, 그야 지난 일을 아무렇지 않게 여길지 몰라도, 그를 그토록 이내 다시 보는 것이 그녀로선 자못 괴로운 노릇이었던 것이다.\n\n그녀는 그것을 이겨 내려 애썼다. 만찬 시각이 다가오자, 여느 때처럼 느끼고 여느 때처럼 보이려 무척 애썼다. 그러나 손님이 방에 들어설 때 더없이 수줍고 거북한 낯을 안 하기란 그녀로선 도무지 어림없는 일이었다. 그녀는 윌리엄의 진급 소식을 들은 첫날에 저에게 이토록 많은 괴로운 심경을 안겨 줄 만한 사정의 겹침이 있으리라곤 여겨 본 적이 없었다.\n\n크로퍼드 씨는 그저 방에 있는 정도가 아니라—이내 그녀 바로 곁에 있었다. 그는 제 누이에게서 전할 쪽지 하나를 가지고 있었다. 패니는 그를 볼 수가 없었으나, 그의 목소리에는 지난 어리석음에 대한 켕김이 조금도 없었다. 그녀는 무엇이든 할 일이 있는 게 반가워 대번에 제 쪽지를 펼쳤고, 읽어 가면서, 이 자리에서 함께 만찬을 들 노리스 이모의 그 부산함이 저를 남의 눈에서 얼마쯤 가려 준다는 것이 다행스러웠다.\n\n“사랑하는 패니에게—이제 나는 언제나 그쪽을 이렇게 불러도 되겠네요. 적어도 지난 여섯 주 동안 프라이스 양이라 하며 걸리적거리던 이 혀가 이루 말할 수 없이 놓이게 말이에요—그쪽한테 몇 줄 두루 축하의 말과, 더없이 기쁜 내 승낙과 찬동을 보내지 않고는 오라비를 보낼 수가 없어요. 나아가요, 사랑하는 패니, 두려워 말고요. 이렇다 하게 걸릴 만한 어려움이라곤 없을 테니까요. 나는 내 승낙의 다짐이 무언가 힘이 되려니 여기려 해요. 그러니 그쪽은 오늘 오후 더없이 고운 미소로 그이한테 방긋 웃어 주고, 떠날 때보다 더 행복한 채로 그이를 내게 돌려보내 줘요.\n\n애정을 담아, M. C.”\n\n이런 것은 패니에게 조금도 좋을 것이 없는 말이었다. 그야 그녀가 크로퍼드 양의 뜻을 더없이 또렷이 헤아리기엔 너무 서둘러 얼떨떨한 채로 읽기는 했으나, 그녀가 오라비의 애착을 두고 저를 추어 주려는 뜻인 것, 심지어 그것을 진심이라 믿는 척하려는 뜻인 것만은 뻔했다. 그녀는 어찌해야 할지도, 무어라 여겨야 할지도 몰랐다. 그것이 진심이라는 생각엔 비참함이 있었고, 어느 모로 보나 당혹과 동요가 있었다. 크로퍼드 씨가 그녀에게 말을 걸 때마다 그녀는 괴로웠는데, 그는 너무나 자주 그녀에게 말을 걸었다. 그리고 그녀는 그가 저에게 건네는 목소리와 태도에, 남들에게 말할 때와는 자못 다른 무언가가 있는 것 같아 두려웠다. 그날 만찬에서 그녀의 편안함은 아주 무너져 버렸다. 그녀는 무엇이든 거의 먹지 못했다. 그리고 토머스 경이 기분 좋게, 기쁨이 그녀의 입맛을 앗아 갔구나 하고 말하니, 그녀는 크로퍼드 씨가 그것을 어찌 새길까 하는 두려움에 부끄러워 땅속으로 꺼질 것만 같았다. 무엇으로도 그녀는 그가 앉은 오른편으로 눈을 돌리도록 꾈 수 없었을망정, 그의 눈이 대번에 저를 향하고 있음을 느꼈던 것이다.\n\n그녀는 여느 때보다 더 말이 없었다. 윌리엄이 화제일 때조차 그녀는 좀처럼 끼려 하지 않았으니, 그의 임관 또한 죄다 그 오른편에서 왔던 데다, 그 얽힘에는 아픔이 있었던 까닭이다.\n\n그녀는 버트럼 부인이 여느 때보다 더 오래 앉아 있는 것 같아, 도무지 자리를 뜰 수 없으리라 절망하기 시작했다. 그러나 마침내 그들은 응접실에 있었고, 두 이모가 저희 나름의 방식으로 윌리엄의 임명이라는 화제를 마저 이어 가는 동안, 그녀는 제 뜻대로 생각할 수 있었다.\n\n노리스 부인은 그것이 토머스 경에게 안겨 줄 절약을, 그 무슨 대목 못지않게 반기는 듯했다. “이제 윌리엄이 제 앞가림을 할 수 있을 테니, 그것이 그 삼촌한텐 크나큰 차이가 될 거예요. 그 애가 삼촌한테 얼마나 돈을 쓰이게 했는지는 아무도 모르니까요. 그리고 정말이지, 그건 내 선물에도 얼마간 차이가 될 거예요. 나는 헤어질 때 윌리엄한테 그만큼 준 게 참 다행이에요. 정말이지, 하필 그때에 큰 불편 없이 제법 두둑한 것을 그 애한테 줄 형편이 됐던 게 참 다행이라니까요. 그러니까, 나 형편, 이 빠듯한 살림으로 치면 말이에요. 이제 그게 죄다 그 애 선실 차리는 데 보탬이 될 테니까요. 그 애가 얼마간 돈이 들 게 분명하고, 살 것도 많을 거예요. 하기야 그 애 부모야 뭐든 아주 싸게 살 길을 대 줄 수 있겠지만요. 아무튼 나는 거기에 내 몫의 조그만 보탬을 보탠 게 참 다행이에요.”\n\n“그 애한테 두둑한 걸 좀 준 게 다행이에요, 언니.” 버트럼 부인이 조금도 의심 없는 차분함으로 말했다. “나는 그 애한테 10파운드밖에 안 줬거든요.”\n\n“어머!” 노리스 부인이 얼굴을 붉히며 외쳤다. “원, 그럼 그 애는 주머니가 두둑한 채로 떠났겠네요, 게다가 런던 가는 여비도 안 들이고요!”\n\n“토머스 경이 10파운드면 넉넉하다고 하셨어요.”\n\n노리스 부인은 그것이 넉넉했는지를 따질 생각은 조금도 없던지라, 그 일을 다른 쪽으로 몰아 가기 시작했다.\n\n“놀라운 일이라니까요,” 그녀가 말했다. “젊은 것들이 그 피붙이한테 돈이 얼마나 드는지, 길러 주고 세상에 내보내 주고 하느라 말이에요! 그것들은 그게 얼마나 되는지, 제 부모나 삼촌 이모가 한 해에 저희 몫으로 얼마를 치르는지 도통 생각을 안 해요. 자, 여기 내 동생 프라이스네 아이들만 봐도, 죄다 합쳐 놓으면, 토머스 경이 해마다 그것들한테 얼마나 큰돈을 쓰는지 아무도 안 믿을걸요. 내가 그것들한테 해 주는 건 말할 것도 없고요.”\n\n“지당한 말이에요, 언니, 언니 말대로예요. 하지만 딱한 것들이지! 어쩔 수 없는 노릇이고요. 게다가 언니도 알다시피 토머스 경한텐 별 차이도 없잖아요. 패니, 윌리엄이 동인도에 가거든 내 숄을 잊으면 안 된다. 그리고 다른 살 만한 것이 있으면 그 애한테 부탁을 하나 주겠다. 나야 내 숄을 얻게 그 애가 동인도에 갔으면 싶구나. 아무래도 숄을 두 장 얻어야겠다, 패니.”\n\n한편 패니는, 어쩔 수 없을 때 말고는 말을 아끼며, 크로퍼드 씨와 크로퍼드 양이 무슨 속셈인지 몹시 애써 헤아려 보고 있었다. 그의 말과 태도만 빼면, 그들이 진심일 리 없다는 것이 세상 모든 것이었다. 자연스럽고 있음 직하고 이치에 맞는 온갖 것이 그것에 어긋났다. 그들의 온갖 버릇과 사고방식이, 그리고 그녀 자신의 온갖 못남이. 그토록 많은 여자를 봐 왔고, 그토록 많은 여자에게 흠모받았고, 저보다 한없이 나은 그토록 많은 여자와 시시덕거려 온 사내가—애써 비위를 맞춰 준 데서조차 진지한 인상엔 그토록 무디어 보이던 사내가, 그런 온갖 점을 그토록 대수롭잖게, 그토록 무심하게, 그토록 몰인정하게 여기던 사내가, 뭇사람에게 온갖 것이면서 정작 저에게 없어선 안 될 이는 하나도 없어 보이던 사내가, 어떻게 저에게서 진지한 애착을 품었단 말인가? 게다가, 혼인에 대한 그 온갖 드높고 세속적인 소견을 지닌 그 누이가, 이런 데를 두고 무슨 진지한 일을 밀어붙이고 있으리라고 어찌 여길 수 있단 말인가? 둘 중 누구에게든 이보다 더 부자연스러운 일은 없었다. 패니는 제 의심이 부끄러웠다. 진지한 애착이나 그에 대한 진지한 찬동이 저를 향한 것이라기보다는, 다른 무엇이든 다 그럴싸했다. 그녀는 토머스 경과 크로퍼드 씨가 그들과 합류하기 전에 이것을 아주 굳게 스스로 믿게 되었다. 어려움은 크로퍼드 씨가 방에 든 뒤 그 확신을 그토록 온전히 지키는 데 있었다. 한두 번, 여느 뜻으로 갈래 지을 줄 모를 어떤 눈길이 그녀에게 던져지는 것 같았던 것이다. 적어도 다른 어떤 사내였다면, 그것이 무언가 무척 진지하고 무척 노골적인 것을 뜻한다고 그녀는 말했으리라. 그러나 그녀는 여전히 그것을, 그가 제 사촌들이나 다른 쉰 명의 여자에게 곧잘 내비쳤을 법한 것에 지나지 않는다 믿으려 애썼다.\n\n그녀는 그가 남들 못 듣게 저에게 말하고 싶어 한다 여겼다. 그가 저녁 내내 틈틈이, 토머스 경이 방을 나가거나 노리스 부인과 무언가에 매여 있을 때면 언제고 그리하려 애쓴다 여겼고, 그녀는 그에게 그 기회를 하나하나 조심스레 안 주었다.\n\n마침내—딱히 아주 늦은 것은 아니었으나 패니의 안절부절에는 마침내로 여겨졌다—그가 갈 얘기를 꺼내기 시작했다. 그러나 그 소리의 편안함은, 그가 다음 순간 그녀 쪽으로 돌아서서 이렇게 말하는 바람에 상하고 말았다. “메리한테 보낼 것 없으세요? 그 애 쪽지에 답이 없으세요? 그 애가 양한테서 아무것도 못 받으면 실망할 텐데요. 부디 한 줄이라도 좋으니 그 애한테 써 주세요.”\n\n“아, 네! 그럼요.” 패니가 서둘러 일어나며 외쳤으니, 그것은 당혹함과 어서 벗어나고픈 마음의 서두름이었다. “당장 쓸게요.”\n\n그리하여 그녀는, 이모를 위해 늘 글을 쓰던 그 탁자로 가서, 세상에 무어라 써야 할지도 모른 채 필기구를 마련했다. 그녀는 크로퍼드 양의 쪽지를 딱 한 번 읽었을 뿐이라, 그토록 어설프게밖에 못 헤아린 것에 어찌 답할지가 더없이 괴로웠다. 이런 쪽지 쓰기에 도무지 익지 않았던 터라, 문체를 두고 거리끼고 두려워할 겨를이 있었더라면 그런 마음을 잔뜩 느꼈으련만. 그러나 무언가를 당장 써야 했다. 그리하여 오직 한 가지 굳은 심경, 곧 무언가 정말로 뜻한 것이 있다고 여기는 티를 안 내고 싶은 심경만을 안고, 그녀는 정신도 손도 몹시 떨며 이렇게 썼다—\n\n“친절한 축하의 말씀, 그것이 사랑하는 윌리엄에 관한 것인 한, 크로퍼드 양, 저는 무척 감사드려요. 나머지 쪽지 말씀은 아무 뜻도 아닌 줄 압니다만, 저는 그런 것에 도무지 감당이 안 되는 사람이라, 부디 더는 마음 쓰지 말아 주시기를 청하는 저를 용서해 주시기 바라요. 저는 크로퍼드 씨를 너무 많이 보아 그 태도를 모르지 않아요. 그이도 저를 그만큼 안다면, 감히 말하건대, 달리 처신하시겠지요. 제가 무얼 쓰는지도 모르겠지만, 다시는 이 화제를 입에 올리지 말아 주시면 크나큰 은혜가 되겠어요. 쪽지의 영광에 감사드리며,\n\n그럼 이만 줄여요, 크로퍼드 양, 운운.”\n\n그 맺음말은 점점 커지는 두려움에 좀처럼 알아볼 수도 없었으니, 크로퍼드 씨가 쪽지를 받는다는 핑계로 제 쪽으로 오고 있음을 알아챈 까닭이다.\n\n“제가 양을 재촉하려는 게 아닌 줄 아시겠지요.” 그녀가 그 쪽지를 접으며 보이는 그 놀라운 떨림을 알아채고, 그가 나직한 소리로 말했다. “제게 그런 뜻이 있다곤 여기지 마세요. 서두르지 마시길 청해요.”\n\n“아! 고맙습니다. 저는 아주 다 됐어요, 방금 됐어요. 곧 준비될 거예요. 무척 감사드려요. 그럼, 이걸 크로퍼드 양께 좀 전해 주시겠어요.”\n\n쪽지가 내밀어졌으니, 받지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 그녀가 대번에 눈을 돌린 채 다른 이들이 앉은 난롯가 쪽으로 걸어가니, 그로서는 정말로 떠나는 것 말고는 달리 할 것이 없었다.\n\n패니는, 아픔에서든 기쁨에서든 이보다 더 크게 마음 흔들린 날을 여태 겪어 본 적이 없다 싶었다. 그러나 다행히 그 기쁨은 하루와 함께 스러질 종류가 아니었으니, 날마다 윌리엄의 출세에 대한 앎이 되살아날 것이기 때문이었다. 반면 그 아픔은, 그녀가 바라건대, 다시는 돌아오지 않을 것이었다. 그녀는 제 쪽지가 몹시 서투르게 쓰인 것으로 보이리라는 것, 그 말씨가 아이가 썼대도 부끄러울 지경이리라는 것을 의심치 않았다. 그녀의 괴로움이 도무지 다듬을 겨를을 안 준 까닭이다. 그러나 적어도 그것은, 저가 크로퍼드 씨의 마음씀에 속아 넘어간 것도 우쭐한 것도 아님을 두 사람 모두에게 확실히 일러 줄 터였다.\n\n# 제32장\n\n이튿날 아침 잠에서 깼을 때 패니는 크로퍼드 씨를 결코 잊고 있지 않았다. 다만 그녀는 제 쪽지의 취지를 떠올렸고, 그 효험에 대해 간밤 못지않게 낙관하고 있었다. 크로퍼드 씨가 그저 떠나 주기만 한다면! 그것이 그녀가 더없이 간절히 바라는 것이었다. 하기로 되어 있는 대로, 또 하려고 일부러 맨스필드에 돌아온 그대로, 떠나 주기를, 제 누이를 데리고 떠나 주기를. 그리고 그것이 왜 아직 안 이뤄졌는지 그녀는 헤아릴 수가 없었으니, 크로퍼드 양이야 분명 지체를 조금도 바라지 않았던 것이다. 패니는 어제 그의 방문 중에 그 날짜가 정해지는 것을 듣기를 바랐다. 그러나 그는 그 여행을 그저 머잖아 있을 일로만 얘기했을 뿐이었다.\n\n제 쪽지가 전할 확신을 그토록 흡족하게 마무리 지어 놓은 터라, 그녀는 우연히 그리되었듯 크로퍼드 씨가 다시 저택 쪽으로, 게다가 어제만큼이나 이른 시각에 올라오는 것을 보고 놀라지 않을 수 없었다. 그의 방문이야 저와 아무 상관 없을지도 몰랐으나, 되도록 그를 안 마주쳐야 했다. 그리하여 마침 위층으로 오르던 참이라, 그녀는 정말로 부름을 받지 않는 한 그의 방문 내내 거기 남아 있기로 마음먹었다. 그리고 노리스 부인이 아직 집에 있던 터라, 저가 필요해질 위험은 별로 없어 보였다.\n\n그녀는 한동안 자못 마음 졸이며, 귀 기울이고, 떨며, 시시각각 부름을 받을까 두려워하며 앉아 있었다. 그러나 동쪽 방으로 다가오는 발소리가 없자, 차츰 마음이 가라앉아, 자리에 앉을 수 있었고, 무언가에 마음을 쏟을 수 있었으며, 크로퍼드 씨가 왔다가 저는 이 일에 대해 아무것도 알 필요 없이 가 주기를 바랄 수 있게 되었다.\n\n거의 반 시간이 지나 그녀가 자못 편안해지던 참에, 문득 규칙적으로 다가오는 발소리가 들렸다. 무거운 발소리, 이 집의 이 자락에서는 예사롭지 않은 발소리였다. 삼촌의 것이었다. 그녀는 그 목소리만큼이나 그것을 잘 알았다. 그토록 자주 그 소리에 떨었던지라, 화제가 무엇이든 간에 그가 저에게 말하러 올라온다는 생각에 다시 떨기 시작했다. 과연 문을 열고 저가 거기 있느냐고, 들어가도 되겠느냐고 물은 것은 토머스 경이었다. 예전에 그가 이따금 그 방을 찾던 때의 그 두려움이 죄다 되살아나는 듯했고, 그녀는 그가 다시 저를 프랑스어와 영어로 시험하려는 것만 같았다.\n\n그러나 그녀는, 그를 위해 의자를 놓고 영광스러워하는 티를 내려 온통 마음을 쏟느라, 그 동요 속에 제 방의 부족함을 아주 놓치고 있었다. 그가 들어서다 우뚝 멈춰, 자못 놀라며 “오늘은 왜 불을 안 땠느냐?” 하고 말하기까지는.\n\n땅에는 눈이 있었고, 그녀는 숄을 걸치고 앉아 있었다. 그녀는 머뭇거렸다.\n\n“춥지 않아요, 삼촌. 저는 이맘때면 여기 오래 안 앉아 있거든요.”\n\n“하지만 여느 때는 불을 때지?”\n\n“아뇨, 삼촌.”\n\n“어찌 된 영문이냐? 여기 무슨 착오가 있는 게 분명하다. 나는 네가 아주 편안하게 지내라고 이 방을 쓰게 한 걸로 알고 있는데. 네 침실에야 불을 땔 수 없다는 걸 나도 안다. 여기 무슨 큰 오해가 있으니 바로잡아야겠다. 하루 겨우 반 시간이라 해도, 네가 불도 없이 앉아 있다니 이건 도무지 온당치 않아. 너는 튼튼하지 못하다. 추위를 타지. 네 이모가 이걸 모르고 있는 게 틀림없어.”\n\n패니는 차라리 잠자코 있고 싶었다. 그러나 말을 하지 않을 수 없게 되자, 그녀는 저가 가장 사랑하는 이모를 위하는 마음에서, “노리스 이모” 하는 말이 알아들을 수 있게 섞인 무언가를 뇌지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n“알겠다.” 삼촌이 정신을 차리고, 더는 듣고 싶지 않다는 듯 외쳤다. “알겠어. 네 노리스 이모는 늘, 그리고 자못 사려 깊게, 젊은 것들을 쓸데없는 응석 없이 기르자는 편이었지. 하지만 무엇에든 알맞음이라는 게 있어야 하는 법이다. 게다가 그이 자신이 워낙 튼튼하니, 그것이 물론 남의 아쉬움을 헤아리는 그이의 소견에 영향을 미치겠지. 또 다른 까닭으로도, 나는 훤히 짐작이 간다. 나는 그이의 심경이 늘 어떠했는지 알아. 그 원칙 자체야 좋은 것이었으나, 네 경우엔 지나치게 밀어붙여졌을 수도 있고, 나는 정말 그리되었다고 믿는다. 나도 이따금 어떤 점들에서 잘못 놓인 차별이 있어 왔음을 안다. 하지만 나는 너를 워낙 좋게 여기니, 얘야, 네가 그 때문에 언제고 원한을 품으리라곤 여기지 않는다. 너에게는, 무엇이든 그 일부만 받아들여 그 결말만으로 치우쳐 판단하는 것을 막아 줄 슬기가 있어. 너는 지난 일을 온전히 헤아려, 때와 사람과 있음 직함을 헤아릴 테고, 네 몫인 듯 보이던 그 어중간한 처지에 맞게 너를 가르치고 채비시킨 이들이야말로 네 벗 아닌 것이 결코 아니었음을 느낄 게다. 그들의 조심스러움이 끝내 쓸데없는 것으로 드러날지언정, 그것은 좋은 뜻에서 나온 것이었어. 그리고 이건 확신해도 좋다, 넉넉함의 온갖 이점은, 지워졌을지 모를 그 자잘한 결핍과 제약으로 곱절이 되리라는 걸. 나는 네가 언제고 노리스 이모를 마땅한 존경과 마음씀으로 대하기를 저버려, 너에 대한 내 좋은 생각을 실망시키지 않으리라 믿는다. 하지만 이 얘긴 이만하자. 앉거라, 얘야. 몇 분 너하고 얘기해야겠다만, 오래 붙들진 않으마.”\n\n패니는 눈을 내리깔고 낯빛을 붉히며 따랐다. 잠깐 뜸을 들인 뒤, 토머스 경은 미소를 누르려 애쓰며 말을 이었다.\n\n“너는 아마 모르겠지만, 오늘 아침 나한테 손님이 왔었다. 아침을 먹고 내 방에 든 지 얼마 안 되어 크로퍼드 씨가 안내되어 들어왔지. 그가 온 볼일이야 너도 아마 짐작할 게다.”\n\n패니의 낯빛이 점점 더 짙어졌다. 그리고 삼촌은, 그녀가 말하는 것도 고개를 드는 것도 도무지 못 할 만큼 얼떨떨해하는 것을 알아채고, 제 눈길을 돌려, 더는 뜸 들이지 않고 크로퍼드 씨의 방문에 대한 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n크로퍼드 씨의 볼일은, 저가 패니를 사랑하는 이임을 밝히고, 그녀에게 뚜렷한 청혼을 하고, 그녀 부모를 대신하는 듯한 삼촌의 허락을 청하는 것이었다. 그리고 그가 그 모두를 어찌나 훌륭하게, 어찌나 터놓고, 어찌나 넉넉하게, 어찌나 온당하게 해냈던지, 게다가 제 대답과 제 소감이 자못 요긴했다고 느낀 토머스 경은, 그들 대화의 시시콜콜한 대목을 들려주는 것이 더없이 기뻤다. 그리고 조카딸의 마음속에 무엇이 오가는지는 거의 알지 못한 채, 이런 낱낱의 이야기로 저 자신보다 그녀를 한결 더 흐뭇하게 해 주고 있으려니 여겼다. 그리하여 그는 여러 분 동안 이야기했고, 패니는 감히 그를 가로막지 못했다. 그녀는 그러고픈 바람에조차 좀처럼 이르지 못했다. 그녀의 마음은 너무 얼떨떨했던 것이다. 그녀는 자세를 바꾸어, 두 눈을 창문 하나에 골똘히 박은 채, 더없는 심란함과 낙담 속에 삼촌의 말을 듣고 있었다. 한순간 그가 말을 멈췄으나, 그녀가 그것을 미처 알아채기도 전에, 그는 의자에서 일어나며 말했다. “자, 이제, 얘야, 내 소임의 한몫을 다하고 온갖 것을 더없이 확실하고 흡족한 바탕 위에 놓아 보여 주었으니, 나머지 몫은 너를 데리고 아래층으로 내려감으로써 다하마. 하기야 나 자신이 달갑잖은 말벗은 아니었으려니 여기지 않을 수 없다만, 아래층엔 귀 기울일 값어치가 더한 이가 있음을 나도 받아들여야겠지. 크로퍼드 씨가, 너도 아마 짐작했겠지만, 아직 집에 있단다. 그는 내 방에 있으면서, 거기서 너를 보기를 바라고 있어.”\n\n이 말을 듣자 어떤 낯빛, 어떤 흠칫함, 어떤 외침이 일어 토머스 경을 놀라게 했다. 그러나 그녀가 이렇게 외치는 것을 들었을 때 그 놀라움이 얼마나 커졌으랴—“아! 안 돼요, 삼촌. 저는 못 해요, 정말이지 그이한테 내려갈 수 없어요. 크로퍼드 씨도 알아야 해요—틀림없이 알 거예요. 어제 저는 그이를 납득시킬 만큼 넉넉히 말했거든요. 그이가 어제 이 얘기를 저한테 꺼냈을 때, 저는 그게 제게 무척 언짢고, 그이의 호의에 화답하는 건 제 힘에 도무지 벅찬 일이라고 숨김없이 말했어요.”\n\n“무슨 말인지 못 알아듣겠구나.” 토머스 경이 다시 앉으며 말했다. “그의 호의에 화답하는 게 네 힘에 벅차다니? 이게 다 무슨 소리냐? 나는 그가 어제 너한테 말했고, (내가 알기로는) 사려 깊은 젊은 여자가 스스로 허락할 만한 만큼의 격려를 받아 나아갔다고 알고 있다. 나는 그 자리에서 네가 어찌 처신했다는 것을 전해 듣고 무척 흐뭇했어. 그건 크게 기릴 만한 신중함을 보여 준 것이었지. 그런데 이제, 그가 그토록 온당하고 떳떳하게 그 뜻을 밝힌 마당에—이제 와서 무슨 거리낌이란 말이냐?”\n\n“오해하셨어요, 삼촌.” 패니가, 그 순간의 애타는 마음에 못 이겨 삼촌이 틀렸다는 말까지 하며 외쳤다. “아주 오해하셨어요. 크로퍼드 씨가 어떻게 그런 말을 할 수 있죠? 저는 어제 그이한테 아무 격려도 안 했어요. 오히려 저는, 정확한 말은 떠오르지 않지만, 그이 말을 듣지 않겠다고, 그건 어느 모로 보나 제게 무척 언짢다고, 다시는 그런 식으로 제게 말하지 말아 달라 청한다고 분명 말했어요. 분명 그만큼, 아니 그 이상 말했어요. 그리고 그이가 무언가 진지하게 뜻하는 게 확실하기만 했다면 더 말했을 거예요. 하지만 저는 뜻하지 않았을지 모를 것을 넘겨짚는 게 싫었고, 도무지 못 할 짓이었어요. 저는 이게 죄다 그이한테는 아무것도 아닌 일로 넘어가려니 여겼거든요.”\n\n그녀는 더 말할 수 없었다. 숨이 거의 다한 것이다.\n\n“그러니까,” 잠깐 잠잠하던 토머스 경이 말했다. “네가 크로퍼드 씨를 마다하겠다는 뜻으로 알아들으면 되겠느냐?”\n\n“네, 삼촌.”\n\n“그를 마다한다고?”\n\n“네, 삼촌.”\n\n“크로퍼드 씨를 마다한다니! 무슨 구실로? 무슨 까닭으로?”\n\n“저는—저는 그이를, 혼인할 만큼 좋아할 수가 없어요, 삼촌.”\n\n“이건 참 이상하구나!” 토머스 경이 차분한 언짢음의 목소리로 말했다. “여기엔 내 헤아림이 미치지 못하는 무언가가 있어. 여기 한 젊은이가 있어 너에게 구애하고 싶어 하는데, 그를 천거할 온갖 것을 다 갖췄지. 삶의 처지며 재산이며 사람됨뿐 아니라, 여느 이상의 서글서글함에, 뭇사람의 마음에 드는 언변과 말솜씨까지. 게다가 그는 오늘 처음 안 사이도 아니야. 너도 이제 그를 얼마간 알아 왔지. 더구나 그 누이는 네 절친한 벗이고, 그는 네 오라비를 위해 그런 일을 해 주었어. 다른 게 아무것도 없었다 해도 너에게는 거의 넉넉한 천거가 되었으려니 나는 여기는데. 내 힘으로 윌리엄을 밀어 주기까지야 언제일지 자못 아득했다. 그가 벌써 그걸 해낸 거야.”\n\n“네.” 패니가 새삼 부끄러워 눈을 내리깔며 가냘픈 목소리로 말했다. 그리고 삼촌이 그려 보인 그런 그림 뒤에, 크로퍼드 씨를 안 좋아한다는 것이 그녀는 정말이지 저 자신이 거의 부끄러웠다.\n\n“너도 알고 있었을 게다,” 토머스 경이 이내 말을 이었다. “너에 대한 크로퍼드 씨의 태도에 어떤 각별함이 있음을 얼마 전부터 알고 있었을 게야. 이게 너를 놀라게 했을 리 없어. 너도 그의 마음씀을 눈여겨봤을 테지. 그리고 네가 늘 그것을 무척 온당하게 받아들이긴 했으나(그 점에 대해선 나도 나무랄 게 없다), 나는 그것이 너에게 언짢은 것이라곤 한 번도 못 느꼈어. 나는 아무래도, 얘야, 네가 네 심경을 잘 모르는 게 아닌가 싶기도 하구나.”\n\n“아, 아녜요, 삼촌! 정말이지 저는 알아요. 그이의 마음씀은 늘—제가 싫어하던 것이었어요.”\n\n토머스 경은 한결 깊은 놀라움으로 그녀를 바라보았다. “이건 내 헤아림을 넘어서는구나.” 그가 말했다. “이건 설명이 있어야겠다. 너처럼 어리고, 좀처럼 아무도 만나 본 적이 없는 처지에, 네 정이—”\n\n그는 말을 멈추고 그녀를 뚫어져라 바라보았다. 소리는 또렷하지 않았으나 그녀의 입술이 아니라는 모양을 짓는 것이 보였는데, 그 얼굴은 진홍빛 같았다. 그러나 그것이, 이토록 얌전한 처녀에게라면 순진함과도 얼마든지 어울릴 만했다. 그리하여 적어도 흡족한 척이라도 하기로 마음먹고, 그는 얼른 덧붙였다. “아니지, 아니야, 그건 아주 있을 수 없는 일임을 나도 안다. 아주 어림없는 일이지. 자, 더 할 말이 없구나.”\n\n그리고 몇 분간 그는 정말 아무 말도 안 했다. 그는 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 조카딸 역시 골똘히 생각에 잠겨, 더 캐물음에 맞서 저를 다잡고 채비시키려 애쓰고 있었다. 그녀는 그 진실을 털어놓느니 차라리 죽는 편이 나았다. 그리고 조금 곰곰 생각해 그것을 발설하지 않도록 저를 든든히 하기를 바랐다.\n\n“크로퍼드 씨의 선택이 정당해 보이게 하는 그 관심과는 별개로,” 토머스 경이 다시, 아주 차분하게 말문을 열었다. “그가 이토록 일찍 혼인하고 싶어 한다는 것 자체가 내겐 천거할 만한 점이다. 나는 형편이 그에 걸맞은 데서라면 이른 혼인을 지지하는 사람이라, 넉넉한 수입이 있는 젊은이라면 누구든 스물넷을 넘기고 되도록 빨리 자리 잡기를 바란다. 이게 워낙 내 소견인지라, 내 맏아들, 네 사촌 버트럼 씨가 일찍 혼인할 성싶지 않다는 걸 생각하면 나는 서운하구나. 하지만 지금으로선, 내가 판단할 수 있는 한, 혼인은 그의 계획에도 생각에도 없어. 나는 그가 좀 더 자리 잡을 성싶으면 싶다.” 여기서 패니를 슬쩍 보았다. “에드먼드는, 내가 보기에, 그 성미와 버릇으로 미루어 제 형보다 훨씬 일찍 혼인할 성싶다. 그 아이는, 정말이지, 근래 내 생각엔, 사랑할 만한 여자를 본 듯한데, 내 맏아들은 그러지 못했다고 나는 믿는다. 내 생각이 옳으냐? 너도 나랑 같은 생각이냐, 얘야?”\n\n“네, 삼촌.”\n\n그것은 나직했으나 차분히 뇐 말이었고, 토머스 경은 그 사촌들 문제에 관해서는 마음이 놓였다. 그러나 그 불안이 걷혔다 해서 조카딸에게 이로울 것은 없었다. 그녀의 도무지 알 수 없음이 굳어질수록 그의 언짢음은 커졌던 것이다. 그리하여 일어나, 패니가 감히 눈을 들지는 못해도 능히 떠올릴 수 있는 그 찌푸린 얼굴로 방 안을 거닐다가, 이내 위엄 있는 목소리로 그는 말했다. “얘야, 너는 크로퍼드 씨의 성미를 나쁘게 여길 만한 무슨 까닭이라도 있느냐?”\n\n“아뇨, 삼촌.”\n\n그녀는 “하지만 그이의 지조는 나쁘게 여겨요” 하고 덧붙이고 싶어 못 견뎠다. 그러나 그 논의와 설명과, 아마도 끝내 납득 못 시킴이라는 그 소름 끼치는 전망에 그녀의 가슴이 내려앉았다. 그에 대한 그녀의 나쁜 생각은 주로 눈으로 본 것들에 바탕한 것이었는데, 그것은 사촌들을 위해서라도 그들 아버지에게 감히 입에 올릴 수 없는 것이었다. 마리아와 줄리아가, 특히 마리아가 크로퍼드 씨의 그릇된 처신에 어찌나 깊이 얽혀 있던지, 그녀는 그들을 팔지 않고서는 제가 믿는 그대로의 그의 사람됨을 밝힐 수가 없었다. 그녀는, 삼촌처럼 그토록 눈 밝고, 그토록 떳떳하고, 그토록 좋은 사람에게라면, 제 편에서 굳어진 싫음을 그저 밝히는 것만으로 넉넉하려니 바랐다. 그런데 그렇지 않음을 알게 되어 이루 말할 수 없이 서러웠다.\n\n토머스 경은 그녀가 떨리는 비참함 속에 앉아 있는 탁자 쪽으로 다가와, 자못 싸늘한 엄함으로 말했다. “너한테 말해 봐야 소용없구나, 알겠다. 이 더없이 무안한 대면은 그만 끝내는 게 낫겠다. 크로퍼드 씨를 더 오래 기다리게 해선 안 되니까. 그러니 나는, 네 처신에 대한 내 생각을 밝히는 게 내 도리라 여겨, 이 말만 덧붙이마. 너는 내가 품었던 온갖 기대를 저버렸고, 내가 짐작한 것과는 정반대인 사람임을 스스로 드러냈다. 나는 정말이지, 얘야, 내 처신이 보여 주었을 줄 안다만, 잉글랜드에 돌아온 그 무렵부터 너를 자못 좋게 여겨 왔거든. 나는 네가 성미의 고집스러움에서도, 자만에서도, 요즘 세상에 그토록 판을 치는, 젊은 여자에게조차 판을 치는, 그리고 젊은 여자에게 있으면 여느 거슬림을 넘어 거슬리고 역겨운 그런 방자한 기질에서도 유난히 벗어나 있다 여겨 왔어. 그런데 너는 이제, 네가 고집스럽고 비뚤어질 수 있음을, 너를 이끌 얼마간의 권리가 분명 있는 이들에 대한 아무런 헤아림도 존중도 없이, 아니 그들 조언을 구하지조차 않고서, 네 스스로 정할 수 있고 또 정하려 든다는 걸 내게 보여 주었다. 너는 내가 그려 본 온갖 것과는 아주, 아주 다른 사람임을 스스로 드러낸 거야. 네 집안의, 네 부모와 형제자매의 이로움이나 해로움은, 이번 일에 네 생각 속에 한순간의 몫도 차지한 적이 없어 보인다. 그들이 어찌 덕을 볼지, 그들이 너의 이런 자리 잡음을 얼마나 반길지는 너한테는 아무것도 아닌 거야. 너는 오직 너 자신만 생각하지. 그리고 젊은 달뜬 공상이 행복에 꼭 있어야 한다고 여기는 바로 그것을 크로퍼드 씨에게 못 느낀다 해서, 잠깐 생각해 볼 겨를조차, 차분히 헤아리고 네 마음을 정말로 살펴볼 겨를조차 바라지 않고 대뜸 그를 마다하기로 마음먹었어. 그리하여 어리석음의 사나운 발작 속에, 삶에서 자리 잡을—번듯하게, 떳떳하게, 훌륭하게 자리 잡을 그런 기회를, 아마 다시는 네게 오지 않을 그런 기회를 스스로 내던지고 있는 거다. 여기 지각과 사람됨과 성미와 몸가짐과 재산을 갖춘 젊은이가 있어, 너에게 몹시 애착하며 더없이 훤칠하고 사심 없는 방식으로 네 손을 청하고 있어. 그리고 얘야, 내 너한테 이르거니와, 너는 세상에서 열여덟 해를 더 살아도 크로퍼드 씨 재산의 절반, 그 미덕의 십분의 일을 가진 사내한테서도 구애받지 못할 수 있어. 나야 기꺼이 내 두 딸 가운데 누구라도 그에게 주었을 게다. 마리아는 번듯하게 시집갔다만, 크로퍼드 씨가 줄리아의 손을 청했더라면, 나는 마리아를 러시워스 씨에게 준 것보다 더 크고 더 진심 어린 흡족함으로 줄리아를 그에게 주었을 거야.” 잠깐 뜸을 들인 뒤. “그리고 내 딸 가운데 누구라도, 언제고 이것의 절반의 걸맞음밖에 못 지닌 청혼을 받고서, 내 생각이나 내 뜻에 의논 한마디의 예도 갖추지 않고 대뜸, 딱 잘라 그것을 마다했더라면, 나는 무척 놀랐을 거야. 나는 그런 처사에 무척 놀라고 무척 상했을 게다. 나는 그것을 도리와 존중을 몹시 저버린 것으로 여겼을 거야. 너야 같은 잣대로 잴 건 아니다. 너는 나한테 자식으로서의 도리를 진 건 아니니까. 하지만, 얘야, 네 마음이 너를 배은망덕에서 놓아줄 수 있다면—”\n\n그는 말을 그쳤다. 이때쯤 패니가 어찌나 서럽게 울던지, 그는 아무리 화가 났어도 그 대목을 더 밀어붙이려 하지 않았다. 그녀의 가슴은, 저가 그에게 어떻게 비쳤는가 하는 그런 그림에, 그토록 무겁고, 그토록 겹겹이 쌓이고, 그토록 소름 끼치는 층층으로 높아 가는 그런 나무람에 거의 무너져 버렸다! 제멋대로에, 고집스럽고, 이기적이고, 배은망덕하다니. 그가 그녀를 이 모두로 여긴 것이다. 그녀는 그의 기대를 저버렸다. 그녀는 그의 좋은 생각을 잃었다. 그녀는 앞으로 어찌 될 것인가?\n\n“정말 죄송해요.” 그녀가 눈물 사이로 더듬거리며 말했다. “정말이지 너무 죄송해요.”\n\n“죄송하다고! 그래, 죄송하기를 바란다. 그리고 너는 아마 오늘 벌어진 일을 두고 오래도록 죄송해할 까닭을 갖게 되겠지.”\n\n“제가 달리 어찌할 수만 있다면요.” 그녀가 또 한 번 안간힘을 쓰며 말했다. “하지만 저는 결코 그이를 행복하게 못 하리라는 것과, 저 자신이 비참해지리라는 것을 너무나 온전히 확신해서요.”\n\n또 한 번 눈물이 터졌다. 그러나 그 터짐에도 불구하고, 또 그 터짐의 서두가 된 그 크고 시커먼 낱말 비참에도 불구하고, 토머스 경은 어쩌면 얼마간의 누그러짐, 얼마간의 마음이 돌아섬이 여기 얽혀 있을지도 모른다고, 그리고 그 젊은이 자신의 직접적인 간청에서 좋은 조짐을 점칠 만하다고 여기기 시작했다. 그는 그녀가 무척 소심하고 몹시 예민함을 알았다. 그리하여 그녀의 마음이, 조금의 겨를과, 조금의 조름과, 조금의 참을성과, 조금의 조바심—사랑하는 이의 편에서 이 모두를 사려 깊게 뒤섞으면 늘 그렇듯 효험을 낼 만한 그런 상태에 있을지도 모른다 여겼다. 그 신사가 그저 물러서지만 않는다면, 물러서지 않을 만큼의 사랑만 그에게 있다면, 토머스 경은 바람이 생기기 시작했다. 그리고 이런 생각이 마음을 스쳐 그것을 밝혀 준 터라, “자,” 하고 그는 걸맞은 근엄함이되 노여움은 덜한 투로 말했다. “자, 얘야, 눈물을 거두어라. 이런 눈물은 소용없다. 아무 도움도 못 돼. 이제 너는 나랑 아래층으로 내려가야 한다. 크로퍼드 씨를 벌써 너무 오래 기다리게 했어. 너는 그에게 네 답을 몸소 주어야 해. 그가 그보다 못한 것으로 흡족해하기를 바랄 수야 없으니까. 그리고 오직 너만이, 그가 딱하게도 분명 품어 버린 네 심경에 대한 그 오해의 근거를 그에게 밝혀 줄 수 있어. 나는 도무지 그럴 깜냥이 못 돼.”\n\n그러나 그에게 내려간다는 생각에 패니가 어찌나 꺼리는 기색, 어찌나 비참한 기색을 보이던지, 토머스 경은 잠깐 헤아린 끝에 그녀의 뜻을 받아 주는 편이 낫겠다 여겼다. 그 신사와 아가씨 둘 모두에게 건 그의 바람이 그로 인해 얼마간 꺾였다. 그러나 조카딸을 보고, 울음이 그녀의 이목구비와 낯빛을 어떤 꼴로 만들어 놨는지를 보니, 그는 당장의 대면으로 얻는 것 못지않게 잃는 것도 있으리라 여겼다. 그리하여 별 뜻 없는 몇 마디를 남기고, 그는 혼자 걸어 나가, 딱한 조카딸을 남겨, 자못 비참한 심경으로 앉아 지난 일을 두고 울게 내버려 두었다.\n\n그녀의 마음은 온통 어지러웠다. 지난 일도, 지금도, 앞날도, 온갖 것이 끔찍했다. 그러나 삼촌의 노여움이 그 무엇보다 모진 아픔을 안겨 주었다. 이기적이고 배은망덕하다니! 그에게 그리 비쳤다니! 그녀는 영영 비참했다. 그녀 편을 들어 주고, 조언해 주고, 대신 말해 줄 이가 아무도 없었다. 그녀의 유일한 벗은 떠나 있었다. 그라면 제 아버지를 누그러뜨렸을지도 몰랐다. 그러나 다들, 어쩌면 다들 그녀를 이기적이고 배은망덕하다 여기리라. 그녀는 그 나무람을 몇 번이고 견뎌야 할지도 몰랐다. 그것을 듣거나, 보거나, 제 둘레의 온갖 인연 속에 그것이 영영 있음을 알아야 할지도 몰랐다. 그녀는 크로퍼드 씨에게 얼마간 원망이 들지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 만일 그가 정말로 저를 사랑하고, 그 또한 불행하다면! 그것은 죄다 한데 뒤엉킨 비참함이었다.\n\n십오 분쯤 지나 삼촌이 돌아왔다. 그녀는 그를 보자 거의 까무러칠 지경이었다. 그러나 그는 차분하게, 매섭지도 나무라지도 않고 말했고, 그녀는 조금 되살아났다. 그의 태도뿐 아니라 그 말에도 위안이 있었으니, 그가 이렇게 시작한 것이다. “크로퍼드 씨는 갔다. 방금 나한테서 떠났어. 무슨 일이 있었는지 되풀이할 것 없다. 그가 무엇을 느꼈는지 들려주어 네가 지금 느낄지 모를 것에 보태고 싶지도 않고. 이것만 알면 된다, 그가 더없이 신사답고 너그럽게 처신했다는 것, 그리고 그 슬기와 마음과 성미에 대한 더없이 좋은 생각을 내게 굳혀 주었다는 것. 네가 얼마나 괴로워하는지를 내가 전하니, 그는 대번에, 더없는 섬세함으로, 지금 너를 보자 조르기를 그쳤다.”\n\n이때 고개를 들었던 패니가 다시 고개를 숙였다. “물론,” 삼촌이 말을 이었다. “그가 단 오 분이라도 너하고 단둘이 얘기하기를 청하는 거야 짐작 못 할 바 없지. 마다하기엔 너무 자연스러운 청이요, 너무 온당한 요구야. 하지만 정해진 때는 없다. 어쩌면 내일이든, 아니면 네 기분이 웬만큼 가라앉을 때든. 지금으로선 너는 그저 마음을 다스리기만 하면 돼. 이 눈물을 거두어라. 이건 너를 지치게 할 뿐이야. 만일, 내가 기꺼이 믿고 싶은 대로, 네가 나한테 무슨 마음씀이라도 보이고 싶거든, 이런 감정에 지지 말고, 좀 더 굳센 마음가짐이 되도록 스스로를 다잡아 봐라. 밖에 나가 보라 이르마. 바람이 너한테 이로울 게다. 한 시간쯤 자갈길로 나가 보렴. 관목 산책로가 온통 네 차지일 테고, 바람과 운동으로 나아질 거야. 그리고, 얘야” (잠깐 다시 돌아서며). “나는 아래층에서 무슨 일이 있었는지 입에 안 올리마. 버트럼 이모한테조차 말 안 할 거야. 이 실망스러운 일을 퍼뜨릴 까닭이 없으니, 너도 그 얘긴 아무한테도 하지 마라.”\n\n이것은 더없이 기쁘게 따를 분부였다. 이것은 패니가 마음으로 사무치게 느낀 친절의 처사였다. 노리스 이모의 그 끝없는 나무람을 면할 수 있다니! 그는 그녀를 고마움에 달아오른 채로 두고 떠났다. 그런 나무람만 아니라면 무엇이든 견딜 만했다. 크로퍼드 씨를 보는 것조차 그보다는 덜 벅찰 터였다.\n\n그녀는 삼촌이 이른 대로 곧장 밖에 나가, 되도록 그 조언을 낱낱이 따랐다. 정말로 눈물을 거두었고, 정말로 애써 제 마음을 다스리고 제 마음가짐을 든든히 하려 했다. 그녀는 그에게 저가 정말로 그의 편안함을 바라고 그의 호의를 되찾으려 애쓴다는 것을 증명하고 싶었다. 게다가 그는 이 온 일을 두 이모가 모르게 함으로써 그녀에게 애쓸 또 하나의 굳센 동기를 주었다. 낯빛이나 태도로 의심을 사지 않는 것이 이제 이룰 만한 값어치 있는 목표였다. 그리고 그녀는 노리스 이모에게서 저를 구해 줄 것이라면 무엇이든 거의 다 해낼 수 있을 것 같았다.\n\n그녀가 산책에서 돌아와 다시 동쪽 방에 들어갔을 때, 맨 처음 눈에 든 것이 지펴져 타오르는 불이라, 그녀는 놀랐다, 아주 놀랐다. 불이라니! 너무한 것 같았다. 하필 이런 때에 저에게 이런 호사를 베풀다니, 아프기까지 한 고마움이 일었다. 토머스 경이 이런 사소한 것을 다시 헤아릴 겨를이 있었다는 것이 의아했다. 그러나 그녀는, 불을 살피러 들어온 하녀가 자진해 알려 준 덕에, 이제 날마다 그리하기로 되어 있음을 이내 알았다. 토머스 경이 그리하라 분부를 내린 것이었다.\n\n“정말이지 내가 참으로 배은망덕할 수 있다면 나는 짐승이나 다름없어!” 그녀는 혼잣말로 뇌었다. “배은망덕한 사람이 되지 않게 하늘이여 지켜 주소서!”\n\n그녀는 만찬 자리에서 마주치기까지 삼촌도, 노리스 이모도 더는 보지 못했다. 그때 그녀에 대한 삼촌의 처신은 되도록 예전 그대로였다. 그가 무슨 달라짐이 있게 할 뜻은 없다는 것, 그리고 어떤 달라짐이든 오직 제 양심이 지어내는 것일 뿐임을 그녀는 확신했다. 그러나 이모는 이내 그녀와 다투기 시작했다. 그리고 이모 모르게 그저 산책 나갔다는 것 하나를 두고 얼마나 오래, 얼마나 언짢게 물고 늘어질 수 있는지를 보고 나니, 훨씬 더 중대한 일에서 이 같은 나무람의 기세를 저에게서 막아 준 그 친절을 감사할 온갖 까닭이 그녀에게 있음을 절감했다.\n\n“네가 나갈 줄 알았으면, 내니한테 줄 분부 몇 가지를 들려 그저 우리 집까지만이라도 다녀오라 했을 텐데.” 이모가 말했다. “그걸 그 뒤로 내가 몸소 큰 불편을 무릅쓰고 가서 전해야 했지 뭐냐. 나는 그럴 겨를이 도무지 없었는데, 네가 그저 나간다고 알려 주는 친절만 베풀었어도 그 수고를 덜어 줬을 것 아니냐. 관목 산책로를 거닐든 우리 집엘 가든 너한테야 아무 차이도 없었을 텐데 말이다.”\n\n“내가 패니한테 관목 산책로가 가장 마른 데라고 권했소.” 토머스 경이 말했다.\n\n“어머!” 노리스 부인이 한순간 멈칫하며 말했다. “참으로 친절도 하셔라, 토머스 경. 하지만 우리 집으로 가는 길이 얼마나 마른지는 경께서 모르셔서요. 정말이지 패니는 거기서도 꼭 그만큼 좋은 산책을 했을 거예요, 게다가 무언가 쓸모가 되고 제 이모를 도왔을 덕까지 보태서요. 다 그 애 잘못이에요. 그저 나간다고 우리한테 알려 주기만 했어도—하지만 패니한테는 뭔가가 있어요, 나는 진작부터 곧잘 봐 왔는데—제멋대로 일을 하고 싶어 하고, 지시받는 걸 안 좋아하고, 틈만 나면 제 혼자 방자한 산책을 나서죠. 정말이지 그 애한테는 은밀함이며 방자함이며 하는 시답잖은 기질이 얼마간 있어서, 나는 그 애더러 그걸 좀 고치라 이르고 싶어요.”\n\n패니에 대한 두루뭉술한 평으로 치면, 토머스 경은 이보다 더 부당한 것은 없으리라 여겼다—저 자신도 방금 그토록 같은 심경을 밝힌 참이면서도. 그리하여 그는 화제를 돌리려 했다. 몇 번이고 애쓴 끝에야 겨우 뜻을 이뤘으니, 노리스 부인에게는, 지금이든 다른 어느 때든, 그가 조카딸을 얼마나 좋게 여기는지, 그리고 제 자식들의 미덕을 조카딸을 깎아내려 돋보이게 하고 싶은 마음에서 그가 얼마나 멀리 있는지를 알아챌 만한 눈이 없었던 것이다. 그녀는 만찬의 절반 내내 패니를 겨냥해 말하며 이 은밀한 산책을 두고 언짢아하고 있었다.\n\n그러나 마침내 그것도 끝났다. 그리고 그 저녁은, 그토록 사나웠던 아침 뒤에 그녀가 바랄 수 있었던 것보다 더 큰 평온으로, 더 큰 명랑함으로 패니에게 찾아들었다. 그러나 그녀는 무엇보다 먼저, 제가 옳게 했다고, 제 판단이 저를 그르치지 않았다고 믿었다. 제 뜻의 순수함이야 그녀가 장담할 수 있었다. 그리고 그녀는 둘째로, 삼촌의 언짢음이 가라앉고 있으며, 그가 이 일을 좀 더 공평하게 헤아려 보고, 좋은 사람이라면 마땅히 느낄 대로, 애정 없이 혼인하는 것이 얼마나 비참하고, 얼마나 용서 못 할 일이고, 얼마나 가망 없고, 얼마나 사악한 일인지를 느끼면 더 가라앉으리라 기꺼이 바랐다.\n\n이튿날 겁내던 그 대면이 지나고 나면, 그녀는 이 화제가 끝내 마무리되고, 크로퍼드 씨가 일단 맨스필드를 떠나면 이내 만사가 마치 그런 화제란 애초에 없었던 것처럼 되리라 여기지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀는, 저에 대한 크로퍼드 씨의 애정이 그를 오래 괴롭힐 수 있으리라곤 믿으려 하지도, 믿을 수도 없었다. 그의 마음은 그런 부류가 아니었다. 런던이 이내 그 병을 고쳐 줄 터였다. 런던에서 그는 이내 제 홀림을 의아해하는 법을 배우고, 그 나쁜 결말에서 저를 구해 준 그녀의 그 옳은 분별을 고마워하게 될 터였다.\n\n패니의 마음이 이런 종류의 바람에 잠겨 있는 동안, 삼촌은 차를 든 뒤 얼마 안 되어 방 밖으로 불려 나갔다. 눈에 띄기엔 너무 흔한 일이라, 그녀는 그것을 대수롭잖게 여겼는데, 십 분 뒤 집사가 다시 나타나 결연히 그녀 쪽으로 다가와 “토머스 경께서 아가씨와 당신 방에서 얘기하고 싶어 하십니다” 하고 말했다. 그제야 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 그녀에게 떠올랐다. 어떤 짐작이 그녀의 마음을 왈칵 스쳐 두 뺨에서 핏기를 앗아 갔다. 그러나 그녀가 대번에 일어나 따르려 채비하는데, 노리스 부인이 소리쳤다. “가만, 가만있어, 패니! 뭘 하는 게냐? 어딜 가? 그리 서두르지 마라. 틀림없이 필요한 건 네가 아니야. 틀림없이 나지” (집사를 바라보며). “한데 너는 어찌 그리 나서지 못해 안달이니. 토머스 경이 뭐 하러 널 찾겠어? 나겠지, 배들리, 자네 말은. 곧 가겠네. 나 말이지, 배들리, 분명해. 토머스 경이 찾는 건 나야, 프라이스 양이 아니라.”\n\n그러나 배들리는 꿋꿋했다. “아닙니다, 부인. 프라이스 양이십니다. 프라이스 양이 틀림없습니다.” 그리고 그 말에는 반쯤의 미소가 어렸으니, 그것은 “부인께서는 그 볼일에 도무지 안 맞으실 듯한데요” 하는 뜻이었다.\n\n노리스 부인은 몹시 못마땅한 채, 하는 수 없이 마음을 다잡아 다시 바느질에 들었다. 그리고 패니는 마음 졸이는 심경으로 걸어 나가, 저가 짐작한 대로, 잠시 뒤 크로퍼드 씨와 단둘이 있는 자신을 발견했다.\n\n# 제33장\n\n그 대면은 그 아가씨가 짜 둔 것만큼 짧지도, 결판나지도 않았다. 그 신사는 그리 쉽게 흡족해하지 않았다. 그에게는 토머스 경이 바랄 만한 온갖 물러서지 않을 기질이 있었다. 그에게는 허영심이 있어, 우선 그것이 그로 하여금, 비록 그녀 자신은 모를지언정 그녀가 저를 사랑한다고 강하게 여기게 했다. 그리고 둘째로, 그녀가 제 지금 심경을 스스로 알고 있음을 마침내 인정할 수밖에 없게 되었을 때, 그 허영심은 그로 하여금, 머잖아 그 심경을 제가 바라는 대로 만들 수 있으리라 확신하게 했다.\n\n그는 사랑에 빠져 있었다, 몹시 깊이. 그리고 그것은, 섬세함보다 뜨거움이 더한, 활달하고 낙천적인 마음에 작용하여, 얻지 못하기에 그녀의 정을 도리어 더 값진 것으로 보이게 하고, 그로 하여금 그녀를 억지로라도 사랑에 빠지게 만드는 그 행복뿐 아니라 그 영광까지 누리기로 마음먹게 한 그런 사랑이었다.\n\n그는 단념하려 하지 않았다. 물러서려 하지 않았다. 그에게는 굳건한 애착을 뒷받침할 온갖 근거가 넉넉했다. 그녀와 더불어 이어질 행복에 더없이 뜨거운 바람을 품어도 좋을 만한 온갖 값어치를 그녀가 지녔음을 그는 알았다. 바로 이때의 그녀의 처신은, 그 성품의 사심 없음과 섬세함(그가 정말이지 더없이 드물다 믿는 자질)을 말해 줌으로써, 그의 온갖 바람을 돋우고 그의 온갖 결심을 굳혀 줄 만한 것이었다. 그는 저가 이미 매인 마음을 공략하고 있다는 것을 몰랐다. 그것은 조금도 의심하지 못했다. 그는 그녀를, 이런 문제를 위험에 빠질 만큼 충분히 생각해 본 적이 없는 사람으로, 젊음—몸매만큼이나 사랑스러운 마음의 젊음으로 지켜져 온 사람으로, 그 겸손함 탓에 그의 마음씀을 못 알아챈 사람으로, 그리고 그토록 온전히 뜻밖인 구애의 그 갑작스러움과, 제 공상에 한 번도 넣어 본 적 없는 처지의 그 낯섦에 여전히 짓눌린 사람으로 여겼다.\n\n그러니 그가 이해받게 되면 뜻을 이루리라는 것이 물론 따라 나오지 않겠는가? 그는 그것을 온전히 믿었다. 그와 같은 사내의, 그와 같은 사랑이라면, 물러서지 않고 밀어붙이면 반드시 화답을 얻어 내되 그리 멀지 않은 데서 얻어 내리라. 그리고 그는 머잖아 그녀가 저를 사랑하게 만든다는 생각에 어찌나 큰 기쁨을 느꼈던지, 그녀가 지금 저를 사랑하지 않는다는 것이 좀처럼 아쉽지도 않았다. 이겨 낼 자그마한 어려움쯤은 헨리 크로퍼드에게 조금도 나쁜 것이 아니었다. 그는 오히려 거기서 기운을 얻었다. 그는 마음을 너무 쉬 얻는 데 익어 온 사람이었다. 그의 처지는 새롭고 신나는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 패니로 말하면, 평생 너무 많은 거스름을 겪어 거기서 아무 매력도 못 느끼는 사람이라, 이 모두가 도무지 알 수 없는 노릇이었다. 그녀는 그가 정말 물러서지 않을 작정임을 알았다. 그러나 저가 하지 않을 수 없다 여긴 그런 말을 한 뒤에 그가 어찌 그럴 수 있는지는 도무지 알 수 없었다. 그녀는 그에게, 저는 그를 사랑하지 않는다고, 사랑할 수 없다고, 결코 사랑하게 되지 않으리라 확신한다고, 그런 변화란 아주 어림없는 일이라고, 이 화제가 저에게 더없이 괴롭다고, 부디 다시는 그것을 입에 올리지 말고 당장 저를 떠나게 해 주기를, 그리하여 그것이 영영 끝난 일로 여겨지기를 청한다고 말했다. 그리고 더 조름을 받자, 제 생각에 둘의 성미는 아주 딴판이라 서로의 정이 어우러질 수 없다고, 둘은 천성으로도 교육으로도 버릇으로도 서로에게 어울리지 않는다고 덧붙였다. 이 모두를 그녀는 진심의 간절함으로 말했다. 그러나 이것으로도 넉넉하지 않았으니, 그가 대번에 둘의 성품에 안 맞는 것이 있다는 것도, 둘의 처지에 못마땅한 것이 있다는 것도 부인하고, 저는 여전히 사랑하겠노라, 여전히 바라겠노라 딱 잘라 선언한 것이다!\n\n패니는 제 뜻이 무엇인지야 알았으나, 제 태도를 가늠할 줄은 몰랐다. 그녀의 태도는 도무지 고칠 수 없이 상냥했다. 그리고 그것이 제 뜻의 그 단호함을 얼마나 감추는지 그녀는 알지 못했다. 그녀의 수줍음과 고마움과 부드러움은, 무심함의 온갖 표현마저 거의 자기 부정의 안간힘처럼—적어도 그에게 못지않게 저 자신에게도 그만큼 아픔을 주는 것처럼 보이게 했다. 크로퍼드 씨는 이제, 마리아 버트럼의 그 은밀하고 음험하고 배신 어린 흠모자로서 그녀가 몸서리치던, 보기도 말하기도 싫어하던, 그에게 무슨 좋은 자질이 있으리라곤 믿을 수 없던, 심지어 상냥할 수 있는 그 역량조차 좀처럼 인정하지 않던 그 크로퍼드 씨가 아니었다. 그는 이제, 뜨겁고 사심 없는 사랑으로 저에게 구애하는 크로퍼드 씨였다. 그 심경이 겉보기에 온통 떳떳하고 올곧은 것이 된, 그 행복의 뜻이 온통 애정으로 맺어지는 혼인에 붙박인, 그녀의 값어치에 대한 제 마음을 쏟아 내고, 제 애정을 그리고 또 그려 보이고, 말로써 증명할 수 있는 데까지, 게다가 재주 있는 사내의 말투와 목소리와 기백으로, 저가 그녀를 그 상냥함과 착함 때문에 구한다는 것을 증명하는 크로퍼드 씨였다. 그리고 이 온갖 것을 마무리 짓듯, 그는 이제 윌리엄의 진급을 이뤄 준 그 크로퍼드 씨였던 것이다!\n\n여기 하나의 달라짐이 있었고, 여기 작용하지 않을 수 없는 몫들이 있었다! 그녀는 노여운 덕의 온갖 위엄으로, 소더턴의 뜰에서나 맨스필드 파크의 극장에서라면 그를 업신여겼을지 모른다. 그러나 그는 이제 다른 대접을 요구하는 권리를 지니고 그녀에게 다가왔다. 그녀는 정중해야 했고, 딱하게 여겨야 했다. 영광스럽다는 느낌이 들지 않을 수 없었고, 저 자신을 생각하든 제 오라비를 생각하든 굳센 고마움을 느끼지 않을 수 없었다. 그 온갖 것의 결과로 그녀의 태도는 자못 애처롭고 마음 졸이는 것이 되었고, 그녀의 마다함에 뒤섞인 말은 신세 진 마음과 걱정을 자못 잘 드러내는 것이 되어, 크로퍼드처럼 허영과 바람에 찬 성미에는 그녀의 무심함의 참됨, 아니 적어도 그 굳셈이 자못 미덥잖게 여겨질 만했다. 그리하여 이 대면을 마무리 지은 그 물러서지 않고 끈질기며 낙심 없는 애착의 다짐에서, 그는 패니가 여긴 것만큼 얼빠진 사람이 아니었다.\n\n그가 그녀를 가게 놓아준 것은 마지못한 일이었다. 그러나 헤어질 제 그의 낯빛에는, 그의 말을 무색케 하거나 그가 스스로 자처한 것보다 덜 억지스러우리라는 바람을 그녀에게 줄 만한 절망의 기색이 조금도 없었다.\n\n이제 그녀는 화가 났다. 그토록 이기적이고 야박한 끈질김에 얼마간 원망이 일었다. 여기 또다시, 예전에 그토록 그녀를 놀라게 하고 역겹게 하던, 섬세함과 남에 대한 헤아림의 부족이 있었다. 여기 또다시, 앞서 그토록 그녀가 나무라던 그 똑같은 크로퍼드 씨의 무언가가 있었다. 제 즐거움이 걸린 데서는 얼마나 뻔하게 정과 인정의 큰 부족이 있던지—그리고, 아아! 마음이 모자란 것을 도리로써 채워 줄 아무런 지조도 없음을 얼마나 늘 알던지. 만일 그녀 자신의 정이—어쩌면 마땅히 그러했어야 할 만큼—자유로웠더라면, 그는 결코 그것을 사로잡을 수 없었으리라.\n\n위층의 그 너무 과분하고 호사스러운 불을 두고 곰곰 앉아, 지난 일과 지금 일을 의아해하며, 아직 올 일을 의아해하며, 어떤 처지에서도 결코 크로퍼드 씨를 사랑할 수 없으리라는 그 확신과, 그 곁에 앉아 그것을 생각할 불이 있다는 그 복 말고는 아무것도 또렷하지 않은 그 마음 졸이는 심경 속에서, 패니는 정녕 진실되고 차분한 서글픔으로 이렇게 생각했다.\n\n토머스 경은 두 젊은이 사이에 무슨 일이 있었는지를 알기까지 이튿날까지 기다려야 했다, 아니 스스로 기다렸다. 그리하여 그는 크로퍼드 씨를 보고 그 이야기를 들었다. 첫 심경은 실망이었다. 그는 더 나은 것을 바랐던 것이다. 그는 크로퍼드 같은 젊은이의 한 시간 간청이 패니 같은 상냥한 성미의 아가씨에게 그토록 작은 변화밖에 못 냈을 리 없다 여겼다. 그러나 그 사랑하는 이의 굳은 뜻과 낙천적인 끈질김에 이내 위안이 있었다. 그리하여 그 당사자에게서 그런 성공의 확신을 보고 나니, 토머스 경 자신도 이내 거기에 기댈 수 있게 되었다.\n\n그의 편에서는, 그 계획을 도울 만한 예의도, 찬사도, 친절도 하나 빠뜨림이 없었다. 크로퍼드 씨의 진득함이 기려지고, 패니가 칭찬받았으며, 그 인연은 여전히 세상 둘도 없이 바람직한 것이었다. 맨스필드 파크에서 크로퍼드 씨는 늘 반가운 손님일 터였다. 지금이든 앞으로든 얼마나 자주 찾아올지는 그저 제 판단과 심경에 물어보기만 하면 될 일이었다. 조카딸의 온 집안과 벗들 사이에, 이 일에 대해서는 오직 한 가지 생각, 한 가지 바람밖에 있을 수 없었다. 그녀를 사랑하는 모든 이의 뜻이 한 방향으로 기울 수밖에 없었다.\n\n북돋울 만한 온갖 것이 말해졌고, 온갖 북돋움이 고마운 기쁨으로 받아들여졌으며, 두 신사는 더없이 좋은 벗으로 헤어졌다.\n\n이 일이 이제 더없이 온당하고 바람직한 발판 위에 놓였음에 흡족한 토머스 경은, 조카딸에게 더는 조르기를 삼가고, 드러내 놓고 끼어들지 않기로 마음먹었다. 그녀의 성미에는 아무래도 친절이 가장 잘 통하는 길이려니 그는 믿었다. 조름은 오직 한 편에서만 있어야 했다. 그녀가 그들 바람을 조금도 의심할 수 없는 그런 대목에서 집안이 참고 물러나는 것이야말로 그것을 밀어붙일 가장 확실한 방편일지도 몰랐다. 그리하여 이 원칙에 따라, 토머스 경은 첫 기회를 잡아, 마음을 돌려놓으려는 뜻으로 부드러운 근엄함을 담아 그녀에게 말했다. “자, 패니, 나는 크로퍼드 씨를 다시 만나, 너희 사이가 어찌 됐는지를 그에게서 소상히 들었다. 그는 더없이 유별난 젊은이야. 그리고 그 결말이 무엇이든, 너는 여느 것과는 다른 애착을 자아냈음을 느껴야 해. 하기야 너처럼 어리고, 사랑이란 것이 대개 그렇듯 그 덧없고 변덕스럽고 미덥지 못한 본성을 잘 모르는 처지에, 낙심 앞에서도 물러서지 않는 이런 끈질김의 그 온갖 놀라움에 나만큼 감복할 수야 없겠지만. 그에게 그것은 온전히 마음의 일이야. 그는 거기서 아무 공도 내세우지 않는다. 어쩌면 내세울 만한 것도 없겠지. 그러나 그토록 잘 골랐으니, 그 한결같음에는 존경할 만한 도장이 찍힌 셈이다. 만일 그의 선택이 덜 나무랄 데 없었다면, 나는 그가 물러서지 않는 것을 나무랐을 게야.”\n\n“정말이지, 삼촌,” 패니가 말했다. “크로퍼드 씨가 계속 그러시니 저는 무척 죄송해요—그게 저한테 자못 큰 찬사임을 알고, 저는 분에 넘치는 영광으로 느끼지만, 저는 너무나 온전히 확신하고 있고, 그이한테도 그리 말했어요, 결코 제 힘에 닿는 일이 아니라고—”\n\n“얘야,” 토머스 경이 가로막았다. “그럴 것 없다. 네 심경은, 내 바람과 서운함이 너에게 그러할 것이 틀림없듯, 나한테도 훤히 알려져 있어. 더 말하거나 할 것이 없다. 이 시각부터 이 화제는 우리 사이에 결코 다시 꺼내지 않으마. 너는 두려워하거나 마음 졸일 것이 없어. 내가 너를 네 뜻에 거슬러 혼인시키려 설득할 사람이라곤 여기지 못할 게다. 나는 오직 네 행복과 이로움만을 눈에 두고 있고, 너한테 바라는 것이라곤, 그 심경이 제 심경과 어우러질 수 없는 것은 아님을 너에게 납득시키려는 크로퍼드 씨의 애씀을 참아 주는 것 말고는 아무것도 없다. 그는 제 위험을 무릅쓰고 나아가는 거야. 너는 안전한 땅에 있고. 이런 일이 애초에 없었더라도 네가 그리했을 그대로, 그가 찾아올 때면 언제든 네가 그를 만나 주기로 나는 다짐해 뒀다. 너는 우리 나머지 식구와 함께, 같은 식으로 그를 만나고, 되도록 온갖 언짢은 기억은 떨쳐 버리려무나. 그는 머잖아 노샘프턴셔를 떠나니, 이런 자그마한 희생조차 자주 요구되지는 않을 거야. 앞날은 자못 미덥지 못한 법이고. 자, 이제, 얘야, 이 화제는 우리 사이에 닫혔다.”\n\n약속된 떠남만이 패니가 자못 흡족한 마음으로 헤아릴 수 있는 전부였다. 그러나 삼촌의 친절한 말과 참고 물러나는 태도는 사무치게 느껴졌다. 그리고 그 진실이 얼마나 많이 그에게 알려지지 않았는가를 헤아려 보니, 그녀는 그가 좇는 그 처신의 갈래를 두고 의아해할 자격이 없다 여겼다. 딸을 러시워스 씨에게 시집보낸 그에게서 낭만적인 섬세함이야 분명 바랄 수 없는 노릇이었다. 그녀는 제 도리를 다하고, 시간이 그 도리를 지금보다 수월하게 해 주기를 믿을밖에 없었다.\n\n그녀는, 겨우 열여덟이면서도, 크로퍼드 씨의 애착이 영영 버티리라곤 여길 수 없었다. 저 편에서 한결같이 그침 없이 낙심시키면 머잖아 그것을 끝장내리라 여기지 않을 수 없었다. 그것이 판치는 기간을 제 공상 속에 얼마나 잡아 두었는지는 또 다른 문제다. 젊은 아가씨가 제 완벽함을 두고 얼마나 정확히 어림하는지 캐묻는 것은 온당치 않으리라.\n\n말을 아끼려던 뜻에도 불구하고, 토머스 경은 이 화제를 다시 한번 조카딸에게 꺼내, 그것이 두 이모에게 전해지리라는 것을 짤막이 채비시키지 않을 수 없는 자신을 발견했다. 그것은 되도록 여전히 피하고 싶은 조처였으나, 일 처리를 은밀히 하는 데 대한 크로퍼드 씨의 아주 정반대인 심경 탓에 어쩔 수 없어진 것이었다. 그는 감출 생각이 조금도 없었다. 그것은 목사관에 죄다 알려져 있었으니, 거기서 그는 두 누이와 함께 앞날을 두고 얘기하기를 즐겼고, 제 성공의 진척을 지켜볼 밝은 눈의 증인들이 있는 것이 오히려 흐뭇했던 것이다. 이를 알게 되자 토머스 경은 지체 없이 제 아내와 처형에게 이 일을 알릴 필요를 느꼈다. 그러나 패니를 생각하면, 노리스 부인에게 이를 전했을 때의 그 여파가 패니 자신 못지않게 거의 두려웠다. 그는 그녀의 그릇된, 그러나 선의에서 나온 그 열성을 못마땅해했다. 정말이지 토머스 경은, 이때쯤 노리스 부인을, 늘 그릇되고 자못 언짢은 일을 하고야 마는 그런 선의의 사람들 축에 넣는 데서 그리 멀지 않았다.\n\n그러나 노리스 부인은 그를 놓이게 해 주었다. 그가 조카딸에게 더없이 엄히 참고 잠잠할 것을 조르니, 그녀는 약속했을 뿐 아니라 정말로 그것을 지켰다. 그녀는 그저 더해진 미움을 낯빛에 담을 뿐이었다. 화가 났다, 몹시 화가 났다. 그러나 그녀는 패니가 그런 청혼을 마다한 것보다 그런 청혼을 받았다는 것에 더 화가 났다. 그것은, 마땅히 크로퍼드 씨의 선택이 되었어야 할 줄리아에 대한 손상이요 모욕이었다. 그리고 그와는 별개로, 그녀는 패니를 소홀히 여겨 왔던지라 패니를 싫어했다. 그리고 늘 짓눌러 오려 애쓴 이가 그렇게 올라서는 것을 그녀는 아까워했으리라.\n\n토머스 경은 이번 일에서 그녀에게 마땅한 것보다 더 큰 분별을 인정해 주었다. 그리고 패니는, 이모가 그 언짢음을 그저 낯빛으로 보이게만 하고 소리 내어 듣게는 하지 않은 것을 두고 그녀를 축복할 수도 있었다.\n\n버트럼 부인은 그것을 다르게 받아들였다. 그녀는 평생 미인이었고, 잘나가는 미인이었다. 그리고 미모와 재물이야말로 그녀의 존중을 자아내는 전부였다. 그러니 패니가 재산 있는 사내에게 청혼받았음을 아는 것은 그녀의 눈에 그녀를 자못 높여 놓았다. 그것은, 앞서 미심쩍어하던 패니가 정말 무척 예쁘다는 것과, 그 애가 이롭게 시집가리라는 것을 그녀에게 납득시킴으로써, 그 애를 제 조카딸이라 부르는 데 일종의 뿌듯함을 느끼게 했다.\n\n“자, 패니,” 그 뒤 둘만 남게 되자마자 그녀가 말했으니, 그녀는 정말이지 그녀와 단둘이 있고파 조바심 비슷한 것마저 알던 참이었고, 말할 제 그 낯빛에는 유별난 생기가 어려 있었다. “자, 패니, 나는 오늘 아침 자못 반가운 놀람을 겪었다. 딱 한 번만 이 얘길 해야겠어, 토머스 경한테도 딱 한 번만 하겠다 했으니, 그러고 나면 그만하마. 축하한다, 사랑하는 조카야.” 그리고 흐뭇이 그녀를 바라보며 덧붙였다. “흠, 우리는 확실히 잘생긴 집안이야!”\n\n패니는 얼굴을 붉히며 처음엔 무어라 할지 몰랐다. 그러다 이모의 약한 데를 파고들 요량으로, 이내 대답했다—\n\n“이모, 이모야 제가 한 것과 달리하기를 바라진 않으실 거예요, 분명. 이모는 제가 시집가기를 바라진 않으실 거예요. 제가 없으면 이모가 아쉬우실 테니까요, 안 그래요? 네, 이모는 저를 아주 아쉬워하셔서 그러진 못하실 거라 저는 믿어요.”\n\n“아니다, 얘야, 이런 청혼이 네 앞에 온 마당에 너를 아쉬워할 생각은 안 할 거란다. 네가 크로퍼드 씨처럼 좋은 재산을 가진 사내한테 시집간다면 나야 너 없이도 아주 잘 지낼 수 있어. 그리고 너도 알아야 한다, 패니, 이토록 나무랄 데 없는 청혼을 받아들이는 게 젊은 여자라면 누구나의 도리라는 걸.”\n\n이것은 여덟 해 반 동안 패니가 이모에게서 받아 본, 거의 유일한 처신의 규칙이자 유일한 조언이었다. 그것이 그녀를 잠잠하게 만들었다. 그녀는 다투는 것이 얼마나 부질없을지 절감했다. 이모의 심경이 저를 거스르는 마당이면, 그 슬기를 파고들어 봐야 아무것도 바랄 수 없었다. 버트럼 부인은 자못 수다스러워졌다.\n\n“내 너한테 이르마, 패니,” 그녀가 말했다. “나는 그이가 무도회에서 너한테 반한 게 틀림없다 싶어. 그 사달이 그날 저녁에 난 게 분명해. 너는 정말 유난히 고와 보였거든. 다들 그리 말했지. 토머스 경도 그리 말했고. 그리고 너도 알다시피 채프먼이 네 옷 입는 걸 거들었잖니. 채프먼을 너한테 보낸 게 참 다행이야. 토머스 경한테, 그게 그날 저녁에 벌어진 게 틀림없다고 말해야겠다.” 그리고 여전히 그 명랑한 생각을 좇으며, 이내 덧붙였다. “그리고 내 너한테 이르마, 패니, 이건 마리아한테 해 준 것보다 더한 건데—다음번에 퍼그가 새끼를 낳으면 너한테 강아지 한 마리 주마.”\n\n# 제34장\n\n에드먼드는 돌아와서 들을 굉장한 일들이 있었다. 숱한 놀람이 그를 기다리고 있었다. 맨 처음 벌어진 것이 그 흥미로 치면 결코 뒤지지 않았으니, 그가 말을 타고 마을에 들어설 제 헨리 크로퍼드와 그 누이가 함께 마을을 걷고 있는 모습이었다. 그는 그들이 멀리 떠났으려니 여겼다—떠나 있기를 바랐던 것이다. 그의 부재는 크로퍼드 양을 피하려고 일부러 두 주 넘게 늘어졌다. 그는 서글픈 추억과 애틋한 상념을 먹고 살 채비가 된 마음으로 맨스필드로 돌아오던 참인데, 바로 그 아리따운 그녀 자신이 제 오라비의 팔에 기댄 채 눈앞에 있었다. 그리하여 그는, 두 순간 전만 해도 칠십 마일이나 떨어져 있으려니, 아니 그 어떤 거리로도 다 나타낼 수 없을 만큼 마음으로는 훨씬, 훨씬 더 멀리 있으려니 여기던 그 여인에게서, 두말할 것 없이 정겨운 환대를 받는 자신을 발견했다.\n\n그녀가 그를 맞이하는 품은, 설령 그가 그녀를 볼 줄 알았다 해도 바랄 수 없었을 그런 것이었다. 그를 떠나게 했던 그런 볼일을 다 이루고 돌아오는 마당이라, 그는 흡족한 낯빛이며 소박하고 정다운 뜻의 말 따위는 무엇보다도 가장 기대하지 않았으련만. 그것은 그의 가슴을 달아오르게 하기에, 그리고 눈앞에 놓인 다른 기쁜 놀람들의 온전한 값어치를 느끼기에 더없이 알맞은 상태로 그를 집에 데려오기에 넉넉했다.\n\n윌리엄의 진급을 그 온갖 시시콜콜한 대목까지 그는 이내 훤히 알게 되었다. 그리고 그 기쁨을 도울 그런 은밀한 위안의 밑천을 제 가슴에 지닌 터라, 그는 그 안에서 만찬 내내 더없이 흐뭇한 심경과 한결같은 명랑함의 샘을 찾아냈다.\n\n만찬 뒤, 그와 아버지가 단둘이 있게 되자, 그는 패니의 사연을 들었다. 그리하여 지난 두 주의 온갖 큰 사건과, 맨스필드의 지금 형편이 그에게 다 알려졌다.\n\n패니는 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 짐작했다. 그들이 여느 때보다 하도 오래 식당에 앉아 있어, 그녀는 그들이 제 얘기를 하고 있는 게 틀림없다 여겼다. 그리하여 마침내 차가 그들을 데려 나와 다시 에드먼드에게 저를 보이게 되었을 때, 그녀는 몹시 죄스러웠다. 그가 그녀에게 다가와, 곁에 앉아, 손을 잡고 다정하게 꼭 눌러 주었다. 그 순간 그녀는, 찻그릇이 마련해 준 그 일거리와 그 광경이 아니었더라면 저가 용서 못 할 지나침으로 제 심경을 드러내고 말았으리라 여겼다.\n\n그러나 그는 그런 행동으로, 그녀의 바람이 거기서 끌어낸 그런 온전한 찬동과 북돋움을 전하려던 것이 아니었다. 그것은 그저 저를 흥미로워하는 온갖 것에 저도 한몫 낀다는 것을 나타내고, 온갖 애정을 북돋우는 것을 저가 들었노라 그녀에게 일러 주려던 것뿐이었다. 그는 실은 이 문제에서 온전히 제 아버지 편이었다. 그녀가 크로퍼드를 마다한 데 대한 그의 놀라움은 아버지의 것만큼 크지 않았으니, 그녀가 그를 어떤 마음에 든다는 것 비슷하게라도 여긴다고는 여기기는커녕 늘 오히려 그 반대라 믿어 온 데다, 그녀가 아주 채비 안 된 채로 당했으려니 여길 수 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 토머스 경도 그만큼 이 인연을 바람직하게 여길 수는 없었다. 그에게는 그것이 온갖 천거할 점을 갖춘 것이었다. 그리하여 지금의 무심함에 이끌려 그녀가 한 일을 두고 그녀를 기리면서—토머스 경이 온전히 맞장구칠 수 있는 것보다 다소 세찬 말로 그녀를 기리면서—그는 끝내 그것이 맺어지리라 더없이 간절히 바라고 낙천적으로 믿었고, 서로의 정으로 맺어지면 둘의 성미가, 그가 이제 진지하게 여기기 시작한 그대로, 서로에게서 복 받게 할 만큼 꼭 맞아떨어짐이 드러나리라 여겼다. 크로퍼드는 너무 성급했다. 그는 그녀에게 정 붙일 겨를을 주지 않았다. 그는 엉뚱한 데서 시작한 것이다. 그러나 그와 같은 역량과 그녀와 같은 성미라면, 온갖 것이 행복한 결말로 풀리리라 에드먼드는 믿었다. 한편 그는 패니의 당혹함을 넉넉히 보았던지라, 무슨 말이나 눈길이나 몸짓으로도 그것을 두 번째로 자아내지 않도록 각별히 조심했다.\n\n크로퍼드가 이튿날 찾아왔고, 에드먼드가 돌아온 참이라 토머스 경은 그에게 만찬을 들고 가라 청할 자격이 넉넉하다 느꼈다. 그것은 정말이지 응당한 예의였다. 그는 물론 머물렀고, 그리하여 에드먼드는 그가 패니에게서 어찌 나아가는지, 그녀의 태도에서 그를 위한 얼마간의 당장의 북돋움이 짜낼 수 있는지를 지켜볼 넉넉한 기회를 얻었다. 그런데 그것이 어찌나 적던지, 어찌나 무척, 무척이나 적던지—그 온갖 낌새, 온갖 가망이 오직 그녀의 당혹함에만 걸려 있었으니, 그녀의 얼떨떨함에 바람이 없다면 다른 어디에도 바람이 없었던지라—그는 제 벗의 끈질김을 의아해할 지경이었다. 패니는 그 온갖 값어치가 있었다. 그는 그녀가 온갖 참을성의 애씀, 온갖 마음의 수고를 들일 값어치가 있다 여겼다. 그러나 그는, 제 눈이 그녀의 눈에서 알아볼 수 있는 것 이상으로 제 용기를 데워 줄 무언가 없이는, 세상 어떤 여자와도 저 자신은 밀고 나가지 못했으리라 여겼다. 그는 크로퍼드가 더 밝게 보기를 기꺼이 바랐고, 이것이야말로 만찬 전과 만찬 중과 만찬 뒤에 오가는 것을 죄다 지켜보고 그가 제 벗을 위해 이를 수 있는 가장 마음 놓이는 결론이었다.\n\n저녁에 그가 좀 더 조짐 좋다 여긴 몇 가지 일이 벌어졌다. 그와 크로퍼드가 응접실로 걸어 들어갔을 때, 그의 어머니와 패니는 마치 달리 마음 쓸 것이라곤 아무것도 없는 양 골똘히, 잠잠히 바느질을 하며 앉아 있었다. 에드먼드는 그들의 겉보기에 깊은 그 고요를 눈여겨보지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n“우리가 내내 이리 잠잠했던 건 아니란다.” 그의 어머니가 대꾸했다. “패니가 나한테 책을 읽어 주다가, 네가 오는 소리를 듣고서야 책을 내려놨을 뿐이야.” 과연 탁자 위에는 방금 막 덮은 듯한 책 한 권이 놓여 있었으니, 셰익스피어 한 권이었다. “저 애가 곧잘 저 책들을 나한테 읽어 준단다. 그리고 그 사람의—그 이름이 뭐더라, 패니?—어느 아주 훌륭한 대사 한복판에 있던 참에 우리가 네 발소리를 들은 거야.”\n\n크로퍼드가 그 책을 집어 들었다. “그 대사를 마저 읽어 드리는 즐거움을 제게 주시지요, 마님.” 그가 말했다. “이내 찾아내겠습니다.” 그리고 책장이 저절로 넘어가려는 쪽을 조심스레 따라가, 그는 정말로 그것을 찾아냈으니, 한두 쪽 안팎으로 버트럼 부인을 흡족게 하기엔 더없이 가까웠다. 그가 울지 추기경의 이름을 대자마자 그녀는 그가 바로 그 대사를 찾았노라 장담했다. 패니는 눈길 한번, 돕겠다는 말 한마디 건네지 않았다. 편들거나 반대하는 한 마디도 없었다. 그녀의 온 마음은 제 바느질에 쏠려 있었다. 그녀는 달리 아무것에도 흥미 없기로 굳게 마음먹은 듯했다. 그러나 취향이 그녀 안에서 너무 셌다. 그녀는 오 분도 제 마음을 딴 데 둘 수 없었다. 하는 수 없이 귀를 기울였다. 그의 낭독은 빼어났고, 좋은 낭독에서 그녀가 얻는 즐거움은 지극했다. 그러나 좋은 낭독에야 그녀는 진작부터 익어 있었다. 삼촌도 잘 읽었고, 사촌들도 다들, 에드먼드는 아주 잘 읽었다. 그러나 크로퍼드 씨의 낭독에는 그녀가 여태 만나 본 것을 넘어서는 여러 빼어남이 있었다. 왕과 왕비와 버킹엄과 울지와 크롬웰이 차례로 살아났다. 더없이 절묘한 재간, 건너뛰고 짐작해 내는 더없이 절묘한 힘으로, 그는 언제든 마음대로 각자의 가장 좋은 장면이나 가장 좋은 대사에 내려앉을 수 있었던 것이다. 그리고 위엄이든 오만이든 애틋함이든 뉘우침이든, 무엇을 나타내야 하든 그는 그것을 한결같은 아름다움으로 해낼 수 있었다. 그것은 정녕 극적이었다. 연극이 어떤 즐거움을 줄 수 있는지를 그의 연기가 처음 패니에게 가르쳐 주었었는데, 그의 낭독이 그 온갖 연기를 다시 그녀 앞에 데려온 것이다. 아니, 어쩌면 더 큰 즐거움과 함께였으니, 그것이 뜻밖에 왔고, 무대 위에서 그를 버트럼 양과 함께 보며 겪곤 하던 그런 씁쓸함이 없었기 때문이다.\n\n에드먼드는 그녀의 마음이 쏠려 가는 것을 지켜보았고, 처음엔 온통 그녀를 붙들고 있는 듯하던 바느질이 어떻게 차츰 늘어지는지, 어떻게 그녀가 그것을 붙들고 꼼짝 않고 앉은 채 그것이 손에서 떨어지는지, 그리고 마침내 온종일 그토록 애써 저를 피하는 듯하던 그 눈이 어떻게 크로퍼드에게로 돌아가 박히는지—몇 분씩 그에게 박히는지, 요컨대 그 끌림이 크로퍼드의 눈길을 그녀에게로 끌어당겨 책이 덮이고 그 홀림이 깨질 때까지 그에게 박혀 있는지를 보며 재미나고 흐뭇했다. 그러자 그녀는 다시 제 안으로 움츠러들어, 여느 때 못지않게 얼굴을 붉히며 부지런히 바느질을 했다. 그러나 그것은 에드먼드에게 제 벗을 위한 북돋움을 주기에 넉넉했다. 그리하여 그가 진심으로 그에게 고마움을 표하며, 그는 패니의 은밀한 심경 또한 나타내고 있기를 바랐다.\n\n“그 극이 자네가 좋아하는 것인가 보군.” 그가 말했다. “아주 잘 아는 것처럼 읽는 걸 보니.”\n\n“이 시각부터는 좋아하는 게 될 것 같네.” 크로퍼드가 대꾸했다. “하지만 열다섯 살 이후로 셰익스피어를 손에 든 적이 있는지 모르겠어. 언젠가 「헨리 8세」가 상연되는 걸 봤든지, 아니면 본 누군가한테서 들었든지, 어느 쪽인지도 확실치 않네. 하지만 셰익스피어야 어떻게 알게 되는지도 모른 채 알게 되는 법이지. 그건 잉글랜드 사람 됨됨이의 한 부분이니까. 그의 생각과 아름다움이 워낙 널리 퍼져 있어 어디서나 그것에 닿게 되거든. 본능으로 그와 친해지는 거야. 웬만큼 머리 있는 사람이라면 누구든 그의 극 가운데 좋은 대목을 펼치기만 하면 대번에 그 뜻의 흐름에 젖어 들지 않을 수 없지.”\n\n“누구든 얼마간 셰익스피어에 익어 있는 거야 두말할 것 없지.” 에드먼드가 말했다. “어릴 적부터 말이야. 그의 그 이름난 구절들은 누구나 인용하고, 우리가 펼치는 책 절반에 그것들이 들어 있고, 우리 다 셰익스피어로 말하고, 그의 비유를 쓰고, 그의 묘사로 묘사하지. 하지만 이건 자네가 그 뜻을 살려 낸 것과는 아주 딴것이야. 그를 토막토막 조각조각 아는 거야 흔한 일이고, 그를 제법 속속들이 아는 것도 어쩌면 드물지 않지. 하지만 그를 소리 내어 잘 읽는 건 여느 재주가 아닐세.”\n\n“선생, 과찬일세.” 짐짓 근엄한 목례와 함께한 크로퍼드의 대답이었다.\n\n두 신사 모두 패니를 슬쩍 보았으니, 그녀에게서 맞장구치는 칭찬 한마디라도 짜낼 수 있을까 해서였다. 그러면서도 둘 다 그럴 수 없으리라 느꼈다. 그녀의 칭찬은 그 골똘함으로 이미 주어진 셈이었다. 그것으로 그들은 만족해야 했다.\n\n버트럼 부인의 감탄이 드러났다, 그것도 세차게. “정말이지 연극을 보는 것 같았어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “토머스 경이 여기 계셨더라면.”\n\n크로퍼드는 몹시 기뻤다. 버트럼 부인이, 그 온갖 무능함과 나른함에도 이것을 느낄 수 있다면, 그 조카딸이—그토록 생기 있고 밝은 이가—느낄 것에 대한 짐작은 마음을 들뜨게 하는 것이었다.\n\n“분명 크로퍼드 씨는 연기에 아주 재주가 있어요.” 이내 마님이 말했다. “그리고 내 이르리다, 아무래도 언젠가 노퍽의 그 댁에 극장을 하나 두시겠어요. 거기 자리 잡은 뒤에 말이에요. 정말 그럴 것 같아요. 노퍽 그 댁에 극장을 하나 꾸미시겠어요.”\n\n“그러겠습니까, 마님?” 그가 얼른 외쳤다. “아뇨, 아뇨, 그런 일은 결코 없을 겁니다. 마님께서 아주 잘못 짚으셨어요. 에버링엄에 극장이라니요! 아, 안 됩니다!” 그러고는 뜻 어린 미소로 패니를 바라보았는데, 그것은 분명 “저 아가씨가 에버링엄에 극장을 결코 허락 안 할 겁니다” 하는 뜻이었다.\n\n에드먼드는 그것을 죄다 보았고, 패니가 어찌나 그것을 안 보려 굳게 마음먹었던지, 그 목소리만으로도 그 항변의 온전한 뜻을 전하기에 넉넉함이 드러났다. 그리고 그런 재빠른 찬사의 알아챔, 그런 얼른 넌지시 비침을 알아들음은, 그가 여기기에 오히려 좋은 조짐이었다.\n\n소리 내어 읽는 일에 대한 화제가 더 이어졌다. 두 젊은이만이 이야기꾼이었으나, 그들은 난롯가에 서서, 이 소양이 너무 흔히 소홀히 여겨지는 것을, 사내아이들의 여느 학교 교육에서 그것이 통 마음 쓰이지 않는 것을, 그리하여 여느 자연스러운, 그러나 더러는 거의 부자연스러운 정도의, 지각 있고 박식한 사내들의 무지와 어설픔을—소리 내어 읽어야 할 필요에 문득 부딪혔을 때 저희 눈으로 본 그런 것을 두고 이야기했다. 실수와 실패의 사례를 그 부차적 원인들과 함께 들며—목소리를 다루지 못함, 알맞은 억양과 강조를 못 함, 미리 내다보고 판단하지 못함, 이 모두가 그 첫 원인에서 비롯함, 곧 어려서 마음 쓰고 버릇 들이지 못함에서 비롯함을 이야기했다. 그리고 패니는 다시금 자못 재미나게 귀 기울이고 있었다.\n\n“내 직업에서조차,” 에드먼드가 미소 지으며 말했다. “읽는 기예가 얼마나 안 익혀졌던지! 또렷한 태도와 좋은 전달이 얼마나 안 마음 쓰였던지! 하기야 나는 지금보다 지난날을 두고 하는 말일세만. 이제는 나아지려는 기운이 두루 퍼져 있어. 하지만 이삼십 년, 사십 년 전에 서품받은 이들 가운데 태반은, 그 하는 걸로 미루어 보건대, 읽는 건 읽는 거고 설교는 설교라 여겼던 게 틀림없어. 이제는 달라졌지. 이 문제가 더 온당히 여겨지거든. 또렷함과 힘참이 더없이 굳건한 진리를 천거하는 데 무게를 실을 수 있음이 느껴지는 거야. 게다가 예전보다 두루 눈여겨봄과 안목이, 더 까다로운 앎이 널리 퍼져 있어. 어느 회중에나 이 일을 조금이라도 알아 판단하고 따질 줄 아는 이의 비율이 더 커졌지.”\n\n에드먼드는 서품 뒤 이미 한 번 예배를 치른 참이었다. 그리고 이것이 알려지자, 그는 크로퍼드에게서 그 심경과 성과에 대한 여러 물음을 받았다. 그 물음들은, 정겨운 관심과 재빠른 안목의 발랄함으로 던져지되, 에드먼드가 패니에게 더없이 거슬리는 줄 아는 그 놀림의 기색이나 가벼운 태도라곤 조금도 없이 던져진 것이라, 그는 그에 답하는 것이 정말로 즐거웠다. 그리고 크로퍼드가 나아가, 예배 가운데 특정 대목을 어떻게 전하는 것이 가장 온당한지 제 의견을 청하고 제 것을 내놓으며, 그것이 저가 앞서 생각해 본, 그것도 분별 있게 생각해 본 문제임을 보이니, 에드먼드는 더욱더 기뻤다. 이것이야말로 패니의 마음에 이르는 길일 터였다. 그녀는 정겨움과 재치와 착한 성미가 한데 힘써 할 수 있는 온갖 것으로는 얻어질 사람이 아니었다. 아니, 적어도 진지한 화제에서의 심경과 정과 진지함의 도움 없이는 그리 이내 얻어질 사람이 아니었다.\n\n“우리 전례문에는,” 크로퍼드가 소감을 밝혔다. “아무리 무심하고 데면데면한 낭독으로도 망칠 수 없는 아름다움이 있지. 하지만 좋은 낭독이라야 안 느껴지는 군더더기와 되풀이 또한 있고. 나 자신으로 말하면, 적어도, 마땅한 만큼 늘 마음을 쏟지는 못함을 인정해야겠네” (여기서 패니를 슬쩍 보았다). “스무 번에 열아홉 번은 이런 기도를 어떻게 읽어야 할까 궁리하며, 그걸 내가 직접 읽어 봤으면 하고 못 견디거든. 뭐라 하셨어요?” 얼른 패니에게 다가서서 부드러워진 목소리로 그녀에게 말을 건네더니, 그녀가 “아뇨” 하자 그가 덧붙였다. “정말 아무 말도 안 하셨어요? 입술이 움직이는 걸 봤는데. 나더러 마음을 더 쏟아야 한다고, 딴생각이 나돌게 놔둬선 안 된다고 이르시려나 했지요. 그리 이르시려는 것 아니에요?”\n\n“아뇨, 정말이지, 그이는 제 도리를 너무 잘 아셔서 제가—설령 그렇다 쳐도—”\n\n그녀는 말을 그쳤다. 저가 헷갈리는 데로 빠져드는 것을 느꼈고, 몇 분간의 애원과 기다림으로도 한마디도 더 붙이도록 구슬려지지 않았다. 그리하여 그는 아까 자리로 돌아가, 마치 그런 애틋한 끼어듦이라곤 없었던 양 말을 이었다.\n\n“설교를 잘 전하는 건 기도를 잘 읽는 것보다 더욱 드물지. 그 자체로 좋은 설교야 드문 게 아니야. 잘 짓기보다 잘 말하기가 더 어려운 법이거든. 그러니까, 짓는 법과 요령이 더 자주 익힘의 대상이 된다는 거지. 속속들이 좋은 설교를 속속들이 잘 전하면, 그건 더없는 흐뭇함이야. 나는 그런 걸 듣고서 더없는 감복과 존경 없이, 나도 성직을 받아 직접 설교하고 싶은 마음을 절반 넘게 품지 않은 적이 없어. 강단의 웅변에는, 그것이 정말로 웅변일 때는, 더없는 칭찬과 영광을 받을 만한 무언가가 있어. 그토록 갖가지 뒤섞인 청중을, 그것도 뻔하고 온갖 예사로운 손에서 오래 낡을 대로 낡은 화제를 두고 감동시키고 움직일 수 있는 이, 안목을 거스르거나 청중의 감정을 지치게 하지 않으면서 무언가 새롭거나 눈에 띄는 것을, 마음을 흔드는 무언가를 말할 수 있는 이, 그런 이야말로 그 공적인 소임에서 아무리 기려도 모자랄 사람이지. 나는 그런 사람이 되고 싶어.”\n\n에드먼드가 웃었다.\n\n“정말 그러고 싶다니까. 나는 평생 빼어난 설교자의 말을 들으며 일종의 부러움 없이 들어 본 적이 없어. 하지만 그러려면 나는 런던 청중이 있어야 해. 교양 있는 이들, 내 지은 것을 가늠할 줄 아는 이들 말고는 설교할 수가 없거든. 그리고 자주 설교하기를 좋아할 것 같지도 않아. 이따금, 어쩌면 봄에 한두 번, 여섯 주 일요일 내내 애타게 기다려진 끝에라면 몰라도. 늘 하는 건 안 돼. 그건 늘 할 노릇이 못 되지.”\n\n이때 귀 기울이지 않을 수 없던 패니가 저도 모르게 고개를 저었고, 크로퍼드는 대번에 다시 그녀 곁에 와서 그 뜻을 알려 달라 졸랐다. 그리고 그가 의자를 끌어와 그녀 바로 곁에 앉는 것을 보고, 에드먼드는 이것이 자못 철저한 공략이 되리라는 것을, 눈길과 낮은 목소리가 실컷 시험되리라는 것을 알아챈지라, 그는 되도록 조용히 한구석으로 물러나, 등을 돌리고 신문을 집어 들며, 정녕 진심으로 그 사랑스러운 어린 패니가 그 고개 저음을 그 뜨거운 사랑하는 이가 흡족하도록 잘 풀어 설명하도록 구슬려지기를 바랐다. 그리고 “사우스웨일스의 더없이 탐나는 영지”와 “부모와 후견인께”와 “잘 길든 훌륭한 사냥말”이라는 갖가지 광고를 두고 제 나름의 웅얼거림으로 그 일의 온갖 소리를 저 자신에게서 묻어 버리려 못지않게 애썼다.\n\n한편 패니는, 말이 없던 만큼 몸도 꼼짝 않고 있지 못한 것을 두고 저 자신에게 언짢아하고, 에드먼드의 그 채비를 보고 가슴 저리게 서러워하며, 제 겸손하고 상냥한 천성이 할 수 있는 온갖 것으로 크로퍼드 씨를 물리치고 그의 눈길도 물음도 다 피하려 애쓰고 있었다. 그리고 그는, 물리쳐지지 않은 채, 그 둘 다를 고집하고 있었다.\n\n“그 고개 저음이 무슨 뜻이었어요?” 그가 말했다. “뭘 나타내려던 거예요? 못마땅함이려니 싶은데. 하지만 무엇에 대한? 내가 무슨 말을 해서 양의 비위를 거슬렀나요? 내가 이 화제를 두고 온당찮게, 가볍게, 불경하게 말한다 여기셨어요? 그랬는지만 말해 줘요. 내가 틀렸는지만 말해 줘요. 나는 바로잡히고 싶어요. 아뇨, 아뇨, 부탁이에요. 한순간만 바느질을 내려놔요. 그 고개 저음이 무슨 뜻이었어요?”\n\n두 번이나 되풀이한 그녀의 “제발, 그러지 마세요. 제발, 크로퍼드 씨” 도 허사였고, 자리를 뜨려 애써 봐도 허사였다. 그 똑같이 낮고 열띤 목소리로, 그 똑같이 바짝 붙은 채, 그는 아까 그 물음들을 다시 조르며 말을 이었다. 그녀는 더 마음 졸이고 언짢아졌다.\n\n“어쩜 그러세요? 정말 기가 막혀요. 대체 어쩜 그럴 수—”\n\n“내가 기가 막히게 해요?” 그가 말했다. “의아해요? 지금 내 이 간청에 양이 못 알아들을 게 뭐가 있어요? 내가 이런 식으로 양을 조르게 하는 온갖 것을, 양의 낯빛과 행동에 내가 관심을 갖게 하는 온갖 것을, 지금 내 이 궁금함을 자아내는 온갖 것을 당장 양한테 설명해 드리죠. 오래 의아해하게 놔두진 않겠어요.”\n\n저도 모르게 그녀는 반쯤의 미소를 짓지 않을 수 없었으나, 아무 말도 안 했다.\n\n“내가, 성직자의 소임을 늘 한결같이 맡고 싶지는 않다고 인정한 데다 양이 고개를 저었죠. 그래요, 그게 그 낱말이었어요. 한결같음. 나는 그 낱말이 두렵지 않아요. 나는 누구 앞에서든 그걸 또박또박 말하고, 읽고, 쓸 수 있어요. 나는 그 낱말에서 겁날 게 조금도 안 보이는데. 내가 그래야 마땅하다 여기셨어요?”\n\n“어쩌면요,” 마침내 지쳐 입을 연 패니가 말했다. “어쩌면 저는, 그이가 그 순간엔 그토록 저를 잘 아는 듯하시더니 늘 그렇게 저를 알진 못하시는 게 딱하다 여겼는지도 몰라요.”\n\n크로퍼드는, 아무튼 그녀가 입을 열게 만든 것이 기뻐, 그것을 이어 가기로 굳게 마음먹었다. 그리하여 그런 극단의 나무람으로 그를 잠잠케 하리라 바랐던 딱한 패니는, 저가 서글피 잘못 짚었음을, 그리고 그것이 그저 한 궁금함의 대상과 한 무리의 말에서 다른 것으로 옮아간 것일 뿐임을 알았다. 그에게는 늘 설명을 졸라 댈 무언가가 있었다. 기회는 너무 좋았다. 삼촌 방에서 그녀를 본 이래 이런 기회는 없었고, 맨스필드를 떠나기 전에 이런 기회가 다시 올 리 없었다. 버트럼 부인이 탁자 바로 건너편에 있는 것쯤은 대수롭잖았으니, 그녀야 늘 반쯤 조는 사람으로만 여기면 됐고, 에드먼드의 광고들도 여전히 더없이 요긴했던 것이다.\n\n“자,” 재빠른 물음과 마지못한 대답이 한바탕 오간 뒤 크로퍼드가 말했다. “나는 아까보다 더 행복해요, 이제 나에 대한 양의 생각을 더 또렷이 알았으니까요. 양은 나를 미덥지 못하다고, 그때그때의 변덕에 쉬 휘둘리고, 쉬 홀리고, 쉬 밀려나는 사람이라 여기시죠. 그런 생각이라면, 놀랄 것도 없이—하지만 두고 봅시다.—내가 억울하다는 걸 양한테 납득시키려 애쓰는 건 항변으로가 아니에요. 내 정이 굳건하다고 양한테 말함으로써가 아니고요. 내 처신이 나를 대신해 말할 겁니다. 떨어져 있음이, 거리가, 시간이 나를 대신해 말할 거예요. 그것들이 증명할 겁니다, 누구에게든 양이 받을 만한 그 만큼은 나도 양을 받을 만함을. 양은 값어치에서 나보다 한없이 나은 사람이에요. 그거야 다 알아요. 양한테는, 어떤 사람한테도 그만한 정도로 있으리라곤 여태 여기지 못한 자질들이 있어요. 양한테는 천사 같은 데가 얼마간 있어요, 그저 눈에 보이는 것을 넘어서—그런 건 눈에 보이지도 않으니까요—아니, 사람이 있으려니 상상하는 것마저 넘어서요. 하지만 그래도 나는 겁나지 않아요. 양은 값어치가 대등해서 얻어질 사람이 아니에요. 그건 어림없는 일이죠. 양의 값어치를 가장 세차게 알아보고 우러르는 이, 양을 가장 헌신적으로 사랑하는 이야말로 화답받을 가장 큰 권리를 지녔죠. 거기에 나는 내 확신을 세워요. 그 권리로 나는 양을 받을 만하고, 또 받을 만해질 겁니다. 그리고 내 애착이 내가 말한 그대로임을 일단 확신하게 되면, 나는 양을 너무 잘 알기에 더없이 뜨거운 바람을 품지 않을 수 없어요. 그래요, 더없이 사랑하고 더없이 고운 패니. 아뇨” (그녀가 언짢아 물러나는 것을 보고), “용서해요. 어쩌면 나한테 아직 그럴 권리는 없겠지요. 하지만 달리 무슨 이름으로 양을 부르겠어요? 양이 내 상상 속에 다른 어떤 이름으로 떠오르리라 여기세요? 아니에요, 내가 온종일 생각하고 밤새 꿈꾸는 건 바로 ‘패니’예요. 양은 그 이름에 그토록 참된 고움을 입혀 놔서, 이제 다른 무엇으로도 양을 그려 낼 수가 없어요.”\n\n패니는, 다가오는 구원의 소리가—그녀가 오래 기다려 온, 그리고 이상하게도 늦어진다 오래 여기던 바로 그 소리가 아니었더라면, 도무지 자리를 더 지키지도, 저가 내다본 그 온갖 너무 남의 눈에 띄는 반대를 무릅쓰고라도 자리를 뜨려 애써 보기를 삼가지도 못했으리라.\n\n배들리를 앞세운, 찻쟁반과 주전자와 케이크를 나르는 이들의 그 엄숙한 행렬이 모습을 드러내, 그녀를 몸과 마음의 그 괴로운 갇힘에서 풀어 주었다. 크로퍼드는 하는 수 없이 자리를 옮겨야 했다. 그녀는 놓여났고, 바빠졌고, 지켜졌다.\n\n에드먼드는 다시 말하고 들을 수 있는 이들 축에 끼게 된 것이 싫지 않았다. 그러나 그 대면이 그에게는 자못 길게 여겨졌음에도, 또 패니를 보니 오히려 언짢음의 홍조가 어렸음에도, 그는 그토록 많은 말이 오가고 들렸으니 그 말한 이에게 얼마간 득이 없지는 않았으리라 기꺼이 바랐다.\n\n# 제35장\n\n에드먼드는, 크로퍼드에 관한 제 처지를 둘 사이에 입에 올릴지 말지는 온전히 패니가 고를 몫이라고, 그리고 그녀가 먼저 물꼬를 트지 않으면 저는 결코 그것을 건드리지 않으리라 마음먹은 터였다. 그러나 하루 이틀 서로 삼가며 지낸 끝에, 그는 아버지의 권함에 마음을 바꾸어, 제 힘이 벗을 위해 무엇을 할 수 있을지 시험해 보게 되었다.\n\n크로퍼드 남매의 떠날 날이, 그것도 자못 이른 날이 실제로 정해졌다. 그리하여 토머스 경은, 그 젊은이가 맨스필드를 떠나기 전에 그를 위해 한 번 더 애써 두는 것이, 그 흔들림 없는 애착의 온갖 다짐과 맹세를 되도록 오래 떠받쳐 줄 바람이 되리라 여겼다.\n\n토머스 경은 그 점에서 크로퍼드 씨의 사람됨이 온전하기를 더없이 진심으로 바랐다. 그는 그가 한결같음의 본보기이기를 바랐고, 그것을 이룰 가장 좋은 방편은 그를 너무 오래 시험하지 않는 것이려니 여겼다.\n\n에드먼드는 이 일을 맡으라 설득당하는 것이 싫지 않았다. 그는 패니의 심경을 알고 싶었다. 그녀는 온갖 어려움에서 그와 의논하는 데 익어 있었고, 그는 그녀를 너무 사랑해 이제 그녀의 속내를 못 듣는 것을 견딜 수 없었다. 그는 그녀에게 쓸모가 되기를 바랐고, 저가 그녀에게 쓸모가 되어야 한다 여겼다. 달리 누구에게 그녀가 마음을 열 수 있으랴? 조언이 필요치 않다면, 털어놓음의 위안이라도 필요할 터였다. 저에게서 멀어져, 잠잠하고 삼가는 패니는 부자연스러운 형편이었다. 그가 깨뜨려야 할, 그리고 그녀가 깨뜨려 주기를 바라고 있으려니 쉬 여길 만한 그런 형편이었다.\n\n“제가 그 애한테 말해 보지요, 아버지. 단둘이 얘기할 첫 기회를 잡겠습니다.” 이런 생각들의 결말이 그러했다. 그리고 그녀가 바로 그때 관목 산책로에서 혼자 거닐고 있다는 토머스 경의 일러 줌에, 그는 대번에 그녀에게 다가갔다.\n\n“너하고 걸으려고 왔다, 패니.” 그가 말했다. “그래도 되지?” 그녀의 팔을 제 팔에 끼며. “우리가 함께 오붓하게 걸어 본 지가 오래됐구나.”\n\n그녀는 말보다는 낯빛으로 그 모두에 응했다. 그녀의 기운은 가라앉아 있었다.\n\n“하지만, 패니,” 그가 이내 덧붙였다. “오붓하게 걸으려면, 이 자갈길을 그저 함께 거니는 것 말고 무언가가 더 있어야 해. 넌 나한테 얘기해야 한다. 네 마음에 뭔가 걸린 게 있는 걸 나는 알아. 네가 무슨 생각을 하는지 나는 알고. 내가 아무것도 모르리라곤 여기지 못할 게다. 나는 이걸 패니 자신 말고 다른 온갖 사람한테서 들어야 하겠니?”\n\n패니는, 마음 졸이면서도 풀 죽은 채 대답했다. “다른 온갖 사람한테서 들으신다면, 오빠, 제가 얘기할 건 아무것도 없겠네요.”\n\n“사실이야 아마 그렇겠지. 하지만 심경은, 패니. 그건 너 말고 아무도 나한테 못 얘기해. 하지만 나야 너를 조를 뜻은 없다. 이게 네가 바라는 게 아니라면 나는 그만하마. 나는 이게 얼마간 위안이 되지 않을까 여겼거든.”\n\n“아무래도 우리 생각이 너무 달라서, 제가 느끼는 걸 얘기해 봐야 아무 위안도 못 찾을 것 같아요.”\n\n“우리 생각이 다르다고 여기니? 나는 그런 줄 몰랐다. 감히 말하건대, 우리 의견을 견줘 보면 늘 그래 왔던 만큼이나 서로 닮았을걸. 요점을 말하면—나는 크로퍼드의 청혼을, 네가 그 정에 화답할 수만 있다면, 더없이 이롭고 바람직한 것으로 여긴다. 나는 네 온 식구가 네가 화답할 수 있기를 바라는 게 더없이 자연스럽다 여기고. 하지만 네가 그럴 수 없는 마당이니, 그를 마다한 것은 네가 마땅히 해야 할 바로 그대로 한 거야. 여기 우리 사이에 무슨 어긋남이 있을 수 있겠니?”\n\n“어머, 아뇨! 하지만 저는 오빠가 저를 나무라시는 줄 알았어요. 오빠가 저를 거스르시는 줄 알았고요. 이거 참 위안이 돼요!”\n\n“이 위안을 넌 더 일찍 얻을 수도 있었어, 패니, 네가 구했더라면. 하지만 어떻게 나를 너를 거스르는 사람이라 여길 수 있었니? 어떻게 나를 사랑 없는 혼인을 지지하는 사람이라 여겼어? 설령 내가 그런 일에 두루 무심하다 해도, 네 행복이 걸린 데서야 어떻게 나를 그러리라 여기겠니?”\n\n“삼촌은 제가 틀렸다 여기셨고, 저는 삼촌이 오빠하고 얘기하신 걸 알았거든요.”\n\n“네가 여태 한 데까지로 치면, 패니, 나는 네가 아주 옳다 여긴다. 서운할지도, 놀랄지도 몰라—하기야 그건 아니지, 넌 정 붙일 겨를이 없었으니까—하지만 나는 네가 아주 옳다 여겨. 여기에 무슨 물음의 여지가 있을 수 있니? 있다면 그건 우리한테 부끄러운 일이야. 너는 그를 사랑하지 않았어. 무엇으로도 네가 그를 받아들이는 게 정당화될 수 없었지.”\n\n패니는 여러 날, 여러 날 동안 이토록 마음 편해 본 적이 없었다.\n\n“여태 네 처신은 흠 없었어. 그리고 네가 달리하기를 바란 이들은 아주 잘못 짚은 거고. 하지만 일이 여기서 끝나진 않아. 크로퍼드의 것은 여느 애착이 아니야. 그는 물러서지 않지, 앞서 생겨나지 않은 그 정을 자아내리라는 바람으로 말이야. 이건, 우리도 알다시피, 시간의 일일 수밖에 없어. 하지만” (애정 어린 미소와 함께) “끝내 그가 뜻을 이루게 해 줘라, 패니, 끝내 이루게 해 줘. 넌 저가 올곧고 사심 없음을 증명해 보였으니, 이제 저가 고맙고 정 많음을 증명해 보이렴. 그러면 넌, 내가 늘 네가 그러라고 태어났다 믿어 온 그 완벽한 여인의 본보기가 될 게다.”\n\n“어머! 결코, 결코, 결코요! 그이는 결코 저한테 뜻을 못 이룰 거예요.” 그리고 그녀가 어찌나 뜨겁게 말했던지 에드먼드는 자못 놀랐고, 그녀는 그의 낯빛을 보고, 그리고 그가 “결코라니! 패니!—그토록 단단히 마음먹고 딱 잘라 말하다니! 이건 너답지 않구나, 분별 있는 너답지가 않아” 하고 대꾸하는 것을 듣고, 저 자신을 떠올리며 얼굴을 붉혔다.\n\n“제 말은,” 그녀가 서글피 저를 고치며 외쳤다. “앞날을 장담할 수 있는 한, 저는 결코 그러지 않으리라 여긴다는 거예요. 저는 결코 그이의 정에 화답하지 않으리라 여겨요.”\n\n“나는 더 나은 것을 바랄밖에 없다. 나는 크로퍼드가 알 수 있는 것보다 더 잘 알아, 너로 하여금 저를 사랑하게 만들려는 사내는 (그 뜻을 네가 미리 알고 있으니) 자못 가파른 일을 해야 한다는 걸. 네 온갖 어릴 적 애착과 버릇이 진을 치고 맞서 있으니 말이야. 그리고 그가 네 마음을 제 것으로 삼기 전에, 그는 그것을, 그토록 여러 해의 자라남이 굳혀 온, 그리고 헤어짐이라는 바로 그 생각에 지금 자못 단단히 죄인, 살아 있고 살아 있지 않은 온갖 것에 대한 그 붙듦에서 죄다 풀어내야 하거든. 나는 맨스필드를 떠나야 한다는 두려움이 한동안 너를 그에게 맞서 무장시키리라는 걸 안다. 나는 그가 저가 뭘 노리는지 너한테 말해야 했던 게 아니었으면 싶어. 나는 그가 나만큼 너를 잘 알았으면 싶고, 패니. 우리 둘이 힘을 합쳤다면 우리가 너를 얻었으리라 나는 여긴다. 내 이론적인 앎과 그의 실전 앎이 합쳐졌으면 실패할 리 없었지. 그가 내 계획대로 움직였어야 했어. 하지만 나는 바랄밖에 없다, 시간이, 그가 (내가 굳게 믿듯) 그 굳건한 애정으로 너를 받을 만한 사람임을 증명해, 그에게 그 보답을 주기를. 나는 네가 그를 사랑하고픈 바람이—고마움에서 우러나는 그 자연스러운 바람이 없으리라곤 여길 수 없다. 넌 그런 마음이 얼마간은 있을 게야. 넌 네 무심함이 미안할 게고.”\n\n“우리는 너무나 딴판이에요,” 패니가 곧바른 대답을 피하며 말했다. “우리는 온갖 성향과 방식에서 너무나, 너무나 달라서, 저는 설령 제가 그이를 좋아할 수 있다 해도 우리가 함께 웬만큼이나마 행복할 수 있으리라곤 아주 어림없다 여겨요. 이보다 더 딴판인 두 사람은 없었을 거예요. 우리한테는 통하는 취향이 하나도 없거든요. 우리는 비참해질 거예요.”\n\n“네가 잘못 짚은 거야, 패니. 그 딴판임이 그리 세지 않아. 너희는 충분히 닮았어. 너희한테는 통하는 취향이 있어. 도덕적, 문학적 취향이 통하지. 너희 둘 다 따뜻한 마음과 인정 어린 심경을 지녔고. 그리고, 패니, 요전 밤 그가 읽는 걸 듣고 네가 셰익스피어에 귀 기울이는 걸 본 이가, 너희가 짝으로 안 어울린다 여기겠니? 넌 네 자신을 잊는구나. 너희 성미에 뚜렷한 다름이 있다는 건 나도 인정해. 그는 발랄하고, 넌 진지하지. 하지만 그럴수록 더 좋아. 그의 신명이 네 것을 떠받쳐 줄 테니까. 쉬 풀 죽고 어려움을 실제보다 크게 여기는 게 네 성미야. 그의 명랑함이 이걸 눅여 줄 거야. 그는 어디서도 어려움을 안 보거든. 그리고 그의 유쾌함과 발랄함이 너한테 한결같은 떠받침이 될 게다. 너희가 그토록 딴판이라는 게, 패니, 함께 행복할 가망을 조금도 해치지 않아. 그렇게 여기지 마라. 나 자신은 그게 오히려 좋은 사정이라 확신해. 나는 성미란 서로 다른 편이 낫다고 온전히 믿어. 내 말은, 신명의 흐름에서, 몸가짐에서, 사람 많은 데를 좋아하고 적은 데를 좋아하는 데서, 말하고 싶어 하고 잠잠하고 싶어 하는 데서, 근엄하고 싶어 하고 명랑하고 싶어 하는 데서 서로 다른 편이 낫다는 거야. 여기서 얼마간 반대되는 게, 나는 속속들이 확신하는데, 혼인의 행복에 이롭거든. 물론 극단은 뺀 얘기고. 그 온갖 점에서 아주 바짝 닮으면 오히려 극단을 낳기 십상이야. 부드럽고 끊임없는 눅임이야말로 몸가짐과 처신의 가장 좋은 방패지.”\n\n패니는 이제 그의 생각이 어디에 가 있는지 넉넉히 짐작할 수 있었다. 크로퍼드 양의 힘이 죄다 되살아나고 있었다. 그는 집에 돌아온 그 시각부터 그녀를 명랑하게 입에 올려 왔다. 그녀를 피하던 것은 아주 끝났다. 그는 바로 그 전날 목사관에서 만찬을 든 참이었다.\n\n몇 분 그를 그 더 행복한 생각에 맡겨 둔 뒤, 패니는 저 자신을 위해 마땅한 일이다 여겨 크로퍼드 씨 얘기로 돌아가 말했다. “제가 그이를 저한테 아주 안 어울린다 여기는 건 그저 성미에서만이 아니에요. 하기야 그 점에서도 저는 우리 사이의 다름이 너무 크다, 한없이 크다 여기지만요. 그이의 신명은 곧잘 저를 짓눌러요. 하지만 그이한테는 제가 더더욱 못마땅해하는 무언가가 있어요. 저는 말해야겠어요, 오빠, 저는 그이의 사람됨을 좋게 볼 수가 없어요. 저는 그 연극 때부터 그이를 좋게 여기지 않았어요. 그때 저는 그이가, 제 눈에는, 그토록 무척 온당찮고 몰인정하게 처신하는 걸 봤거든요—이제는 다 지난 일이니 말할 수 있어요—딱한 러시워스 씨한테 그토록 온당찮게, 그이를 어찌 망신 주고 아프게 하는지는 아랑곳 않는 듯, 그리고 사촌 마리아한테 마음을 쓰는 걸—요컨대, 그 연극 때 저는 결코 지워지지 않을 인상을 받았어요.”\n\n“패니야,” 에드먼드가 그녀 말을 끝까지 듣지도 않다시피 하며 대꾸했다. “우리 누구도, 그 두루 어리석던 시절에 우리가 어떻게 비쳤는가로 재이지 말자꾸나. 그 연극 때야말로 내가 떠올리기 싫은 때야. 마리아도 틀렸고, 크로퍼드도 틀렸고, 우리 다 함께 틀렸어. 하지만 나만큼 틀린 이는 없었지. 나에 견주면 나머지는 다 흠 없었어. 나야말로 두 눈 멀쩡히 뜨고 바보짓을 하고 있었으니까.”\n\n“곁에서 지켜보는 이로서,” 패니가 말했다. “어쩌면 저는 오빠보다 더 많이 봤는지도 몰라요. 그리고 저는 정말 러시워스 씨가 이따금 무척 질투했다 여겨요.”\n\n“그야 얼마든지 그랬겠지. 놀랄 것도 없어. 그 온 일보다 더 온당찮은 건 없었으니까. 나는 마리아가 그런 짓을 할 수 있었다는 걸 떠올릴 때마다 소름이 끼쳐. 하지만 그 애가 그 배역을 맡을 수 있었다면, 나머지야 놀랄 게 없지.”\n\n“연극 전엔, 줄리아가 그이가 저한테 마음 쓴다 여긴 게 아니라면 제가 아주 잘못 본 거예요.”\n\n“줄리아라니! 나도 전에 누군가한테서 그가 줄리아한테 반했다는 소릴 들은 적이 있어. 하지만 나는 그런 낌새를 통 못 봤거든. 그리고, 패니, 나야 내 누이들의 좋은 점을 온당히 봐주고 싶다만, 그 애들이, 하나든 둘 다든, 크로퍼드한테 흠모받고 싶어 하고, 그 마음을 아주 신중한 것보다 다소 부주의하게 내비쳤을 수도 있다 여긴다. 나는 그 애들이 분명 그의 벗함을 좋아하던 걸 떠올릴 수 있어. 그리고 그런 북돋움이 있으면, 크로퍼드 같은 사내, 발랄하고, 어쩌면 얼마간 생각 없는 사내가 이끌려—뭐 그리 눈에 띌 건 없었을 거야, 그가 아무 속셈이 없었던 게 뻔하니까. 그의 마음은 너를 위해 남겨져 있었거든. 그리고 나는 말해야겠어, 그게 너를 위한 것이었다는 게 내 눈에 그를 이루 헤아릴 수 없이 높여 놨다고. 그건 그에게 더없는 영광을 안겨. 그건 그가 가정의 행복과 순수한 애착의 복을 온당히 가늠함을 보여 주지. 그건 그가 삼촌한테 물들지 않았음을 증명하고. 요컨대 그건 그가, 내가 늘 그러리라 믿고 싶어 하면서도 아닐까 두려워하던 온갖 것임을 증명해.”\n\n“저는 그이가 진지한 화제를 두고 마땅히 생각해야 할 만큼 생각하지 않는다 믿어요.”\n\n“차라리 그가 진지한 화제를 두고 아예 생각해 본 적이 없다고 하려무나. 나는 그게 대체로 그렇다고 믿어. 그런 교육과 조언자로야 어찌 달랐겠니? 정말이지 둘 다 겪어 온 그 불리함 아래서, 그들이 지금 그만한 사람인 게 오히려 놀랍지 않으냐? 크로퍼드의 심경이 여태 너무 그의 길잡이 노릇을 해 왔다는 건 나도 기꺼이 인정한다. 다행히 그 심경이 대체로 좋은 것이긴 했지. 나머지야 네가 채워 줄 게고. 그리고 그런 사람한테—제 지조에서 바위처럼 굳으면서도, 그 지조를 천거하기에 그토록 잘 맞는 상냥한 성품을 지닌 여인한테 정 붙이니, 그는 더없이 복 받은 사내야. 그는 정말이지 드문 복으로 제 짝을 골랐어. 그는 너를 행복하게 해 줄 거다, 패니. 나는 그가 너를 행복하게 해 주리란 걸 알아. 하지만 너는 그를 온갖 것이게 만들어 줄 게야.”\n\n“저는 그런 임무를 맡진 않겠어요,” 패니가 움츠러드는 어조로 외쳤다. “그런 막중한 소임을요!”\n\n“여느 때처럼, 저는 아무것도 감당 못 한다 여기는구나! 온갖 게 저한텐 너무 벅차다 여기고! 자, 내가 널 다른 심경으로 설득하진 못할지언정, 넌 거기로 설득되고 말 게다, 나는 믿는다. 나는 정말이지 네가 그러기를 진심으로 바란다고 털어놓으마. 나는 크로퍼드가 잘되는 데 여느 관심이 아닌 관심을 갖고 있어. 네 행복 다음으로, 패니, 그의 행복이 나한테 으뜸가는 몫을 차지하거든. 내가 크로퍼드한테 여느 관심 아닌 관심이 있다는 걸 넌 알잖니.”\n\n패니는 그것을 너무 잘 알기에 아무 할 말이 없었다. 그리하여 둘은 서로 잠잠히 상념에 잠겨 오십 야드쯤 함께 걸었다. 에드먼드가 먼저 다시 입을 열었다—\n\n“나는 어제 그녀가 그것을 얘기하는 태도에 무척 기뻤어, 유난히 기뻤지, 그녀가 온갖 것을 그토록 온당한 눈으로 보리라곤 기대 못 했으니까. 나는 그녀가 너를 무척 좋아하는 줄이야 알았어. 하지만 그래도 그녀가 그 오라비에 대한 네 값어치를 마땅한 만큼 가늠 못 하고, 오히려 그가 지체 높거나 재산 있는 어떤 여자한테 마음 붙이지 않은 걸 서운해하지나 않을까 두려웠거든. 그녀가 너무 익히 들어 온 그 세속적 격언들에 치우치지나 않을까 두려웠지. 하지만 그건 아주 딴판이었어. 그녀는 너를, 패니, 딱 마땅한 대로 얘기했어. 그녀는 이 인연을, 네 삼촌이나 나만큼이나 뜨겁게 바라. 우리는 그것을 두고 오래 얘기했지. 나야 그녀의 심경을 무척 알고 싶으면서도 그 화제를 먼저 꺼내진 않았을 텐데, 방에 든 지 오 분도 안 되어 그녀가 그 온갖 툭 트인 마음과, 그토록 그녀다운 그 고운 남다른 태도와, 그 기백과 꾸밈없음으로 그것을 꺼내기 시작하더구나. 그랜트 부인이 그녀의 그 성급함을 두고 웃었어.”\n\n“그럼 그랜트 부인도 방에 있었어요?”\n\n“그래, 내가 그 집에 이르니 두 자매가 단둘이 함께 있더라. 그리고 일단 시작하니, 크로퍼드하고 그랜트 박사가 들어올 때까지 우리는 네 얘기를 끝내지 못했어, 패니.”\n\n“제가 크로퍼드 양을 못 본 지 일주일이 넘었어요.”\n\n“그래, 그녀도 그걸 서운해해. 하지만 그게 나았을지도 모른다고 인정하지. 아무튼 그녀가 떠나기 전에 넌 그녀를 보게 될 거야. 그녀는 너한테 무척 화가 나 있어, 패니. 넌 그 각오를 해야 해. 자기가 무척 화났다고 말은 하지만, 그 화가 어떤 건지야 넌 짐작할 수 있잖니. 그건, 제 오라비가 바라는 온갖 것을 그 첫 순간에 얻을 권리가 있다 여기는 누이의 서운함과 실망이야. 그녀는 상처받았어, 네가 윌리엄을 위해서라면 그럴 것처럼. 하지만 그녀는 온 마음으로 너를 사랑하고 존중해.”\n\n“저는 그녀가 저한테 무척 화가 나 있으리란 걸 알았어요.”\n\n“패니야,” 에드먼드가 그녀의 팔을 저에게 더 바짝 끌어당기며 외쳤다. “그녀의 화라는 생각에 너무 마음 졸이지 마라. 그건 느껴진다기보다 말로 하는 화야. 그녀의 마음은 사랑과 친절을 위해 지어졌지, 원한을 위해 지어진 게 아니거든. 나는 네가 그녀의 그 칭찬의 헌사를 엿들었더라면 싶어. 네가 반드시 헨리의 아내가 되어야 한다고 말할 때 그녀의 낯빛을 봤더라면 싶고. 그리고 나는 그녀가 늘 너를 ‘패니’라 부르는 걸 눈여겨봤는데, 여태 안 그러던 그녀가 말이야. 그건 더없이 누이다운 정겨움의 울림이 있었어.”\n\n“그런데 그랜트 부인은, 뭐라고—뭐라 말했어요? 내내 거기 있었고요?”\n\n“그래, 그녀는 제 아우와 딱 같은 생각이었어. 네가 마다한 데 대한 놀라움은, 패니, 끝이 없었던 모양이야. 네가 헨리 크로퍼드 같은 사내를 마다할 수 있었다는 게 그들로선 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 일인가 봐. 나는 너를 위해 할 수 있는 말을 했지. 하지만 정말이지, 그들이 그 일을 말하는 걸 들으니—너는 되도록 빨리 다른 처신으로 네가 제정신임을 증명해 보여야겠다. 그거 말곤 아무것도 그들을 흡족게 못 할 테니까. 하지만 이건 너를 놀리는 거구나. 나는 그만하마. 나한테서 돌아서진 마라.”\n\n“저는 여겼어야 하지 않을까 싶어요,” 패니가 떠올리고 애쓰느라 잠깐 뜸을 들인 뒤 말했다. “한 사내가, 아무리 두루 상냥하다 해도, 적어도 제 여자들 가운데 누군가한테는 인정받지 못하고 사랑받지 못할 수도 있음을 여자라면 누구나 느꼈어야 하지 않을까 하고요. 그이가 세상 온갖 완벽함을 다 갖췄다 해도, 한 사내가 저 좋은 대로 마음에 든 여자마다에게 반드시 받아들여져야 한다고 못 박아 둬선 안 된다 여겨요. 하지만 설령 그렇다 쳐도, 크로퍼드 씨한테 그 누이들이 여기는 온갖 자격이 다 있다 쳐도, 제가 무슨 수로 그이의 것에 걸맞은 심경으로 그이를 맞을 채비가 돼 있었겠어요? 그이는 저를 아주 뜻밖에 덮쳤어요. 저는 앞서 저한테 대한 그이의 처신에 무슨 뜻이 있으리라곤 생각도 못 했고요. 그리고 정말이지, 그이가 자못 실없어 보이는 눈길을 저한테 주었다 해서 제가 스스로 그이를 좋아하도록 다잡아야 할 건 아니잖아요. 제 처지에, 크로퍼드 씨한테 기대를 품는다는 건 극단의 허영일 테고요. 그이가 아무 뜻도 없었다 치면, 그이를 그렇게나 높이 치는 그 누이들도 분명 그리 여겼을 거예요. 그런데 어떻게 제가—그이가 저를 사랑한다 말한 그 순간에 그이를 사랑하고 있어야 했겠어요? 어떻게 청받자마자 대령할 애착을 지니고 있어야 했겠어요? 그이의 누이들은 그이뿐 아니라 저도 헤아려야 해요. 그이가 나을수록, 제가 언제고 그이를 마음에 둔다는 건 더 온당찮은 일이고요. 그리고, 그리고—여자가 이토록 이내 정에 화답할 수 있으리라 여긴다면, 이게 넌지시 그런 뜻인 듯한데, 우리는 여자의 본성을 아주 다르게 보는 거예요.”\n\n“패니야, 사랑하는 패니야, 이제 나는 진실을 얻었다. 나는 이게 진실임을 알아. 그리고 그런 심경이야말로 더없이 너다워. 나는 앞서 그것을 네 것으로 돌렸었어. 나는 너를 알 수 있으려니 여겼거든. 너는 이제, 내가 감히 너를 대신해 네 벗과 그랜트 부인에게 해 준 그 설명을 딱 그대로 해 줬어. 그리고 둘 다 더 흡족해했지, 하기야 네 따뜻한 벗은 헨리를 아끼는 그 열정에 여전히 얼마쯤 휩쓸리긴 했지만. 나는 그들한테, 온갖 사람 가운데 버릇이 가장 큰 힘을 미치고 새것이 가장 작은 힘을 미치는 이가 바로 너라고, 그러니 크로퍼드의 구애가 새롭다는 바로 그 사정이 그에게 불리하다고 말했어. 그것이 그토록 새롭고 그토록 근래인 게 죄다 그에게 안 좋다고, 넌 익지 않은 것이라곤 아무것도 못 견딘다고 말이야. 그리고 그들한테 네 성품을 알려 주려고 같은 취지의 말을 잔뜩 더 했지. 크로퍼드 양은 제 오라비를 북돋울 계획으로 우리를 웃겼어. 그녀는 그더러, 머잖아 사랑받게 되리라는 바람으로, 그리고 한 십 년쯤 행복한 혼인 끝에 그 구애가 더없이 살갑게 받아들여지리라는 바람으로 물러서지 말라 조를 작정이었거든.”\n\n패니는 여기서 청해진 그 미소를 좀처럼 짓기 어려웠다. 그녀의 심경은 온통 곤두서 있었다. 그녀는 제가 잘못하고 있었나 두려웠다. 너무 많이 말하고, 필요하려니 여긴 그 조심을 지나치게 부린 건 아닌가, 한 화를 막다가 다른 화에 저를 내맡긴 건 아닌가 하고. 그리고 하필 이런 순간에, 이런 화제로 크로퍼드 양의 발랄함을 되풀이해 듣는 것은 쓰라린 보탬이었다.\n\n에드먼드는 그녀의 낯에서 지침과 괴로움을 보고, 대번에 더는 아무 논의도 삼가기로, 그리고 반드시 그녀에게 흐뭇할 것과 얽힌 경우 말고는 크로퍼드의 이름조차 다시 입에 안 올리기로 마음먹었다. 이 원칙에 따라 그는 이내 이렇게 뇌었다—“그들은 월요일에 떠나. 그러니 넌 틀림없이 내일이든 일요일이든 네 벗을 보게 될 거야. 그들은 정말 월요일에 가거든. 그리고 나는 바로 그날까지 레싱비에 머물도록 설득당할 뻔했지 뭐냐! 거의 약속할 뻔했어. 그랬으면 얼마나 달랐을까! 레싱비에서의 그 대엿새가 평생 사무쳤을지도 몰라.”\n\n“거기 머물 뻔했어요?”\n\n“그럼. 무척 살갑게 조름받아 거의 승낙할 뻔했지. 만일 맨스필드에서 너희가 다들 어찌 지내는지 알려 주는 편지라도 받았더라면, 나는 분명 머물렀을 거야. 하지만 나는 여기서 두 주 동안 무슨 일이 있었는지 아무것도 몰랐고, 이만하면 오래 떠나 있었다 느꼈거든.”\n\n“거기서 즐겁게 지내셨어요?”\n\n“그래. 그러니까, 안 그랬다면 그건 내 마음 탓이지. 그들은 다들 무척 유쾌했어. 그들이 나를 그리 여겼을지는 모르겠다만. 나야 마음의 불편을 지고 갔고, 다시 맨스필드에 오기까지는 그것을 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었으니까.”\n\n“그 오언 양들—좋으셨죠?”\n\n“그래, 아주. 유쾌하고 성미 좋고 꾸밈없는 아가씨들이야. 하지만 나는, 패니, 여느 여자들과의 사귐엔 버려진 몸이야. 성미 좋고 꾸밈없는 아가씨들이야, 지각 있는 여자들에 익은 사내한텐 안 맞거든. 그 둘은 딴 부류의 존재야. 너하고 크로퍼드 양이 나를 너무 까다롭게 만들어 놨어.”\n\n그래도 패니는 여전히 짓눌리고 지쳐 있었다. 그는 그녀의 낯빛에서 그것을 보았고, 그것은 말로 씻어 낼 수 없었다. 그리하여 더는 그리 애쓰지 않고, 그는 그녀를, 그럴 자격을 지닌 보호자의 다정한 위엄으로, 곧장 집 안으로 이끌었다.\n\n# 제36장\n\n에드먼드는 이제, 패니가 제 심경에 대해 말해 줄 수 있는, 또는 짐작에 맡겨 둘 수 있는 온갖 것을 저가 훤히 안다고 믿었고, 흡족했다. 그것은, 그가 앞서 넘겨짚은 대로, 크로퍼드 편에서 너무 성급한 조처였고, 그 생각을 그녀에게 우선 익숙하게, 그다음 흐뭇하게 만들려면 시간이 주어져야 했다. 그녀는 그가 저를 사랑한다는 것을 헤아리는 데 익어야 했고, 그러면 정으로 화답하기까지가 그리 멀지 않을지도 몰랐다.\n\n그는 이 소견을 그 대화의 결말로 아버지에게 전했다. 그리고 그녀에게 더는 아무 말도 안 하기를, 영향을 미치거나 설득하려는 시도를 더는 안 하기를, 다만 온갖 것을 크로퍼드의 부지런함과 그녀 자신의 마음의 자연스러운 움직임에 맡겨 두기를 권했다.\n\n토머스 경은 그리하마고 약속했다. 패니의 성미에 대한 에드먼드의 이야기는 옳다 믿을 만했다. 그는 그녀에게 그 온갖 심경이 있으려니 여겼으나, 그것이 있다는 것을 자못 딱한 일로 여기지 않을 수 없었다. 아들만큼 앞날을 믿지는 못하던 그로서는, 그녀에게 그토록 오랜 시간과 버릇의 여유가 필요하다면, 그 젊은이가 구애하고픈 마음이 다하기 전에 그녀가 스스로 그 구애를 온당히 받아들이도록 설득되지 못할까 두렵지 않을 수 없었던 것이다. 그러나 조용히 따르고 잘되기를 바라는 것 말고는 달리 할 것이 없었다.\n\n에드먼드가 “그녀의 벗”이라 부른 크로퍼드 양의 그 약속된 방문은 패니에게 무시무시한 위협이었고, 그녀는 끊임없는 두려움 속에 살았다. 누이로서 그토록 치우치고 그토록 화나 있으며 제 말에 그토록 거리낌이 없는 데다, 또 다른 면으로는 그토록 의기양양하고 든든한 그녀는, 어느 모로 보나 괴로운 두려움의 대상이었다. 그녀의 언짢음도, 그녀의 꿰뚫어 봄도, 그녀의 행복도 죄다 맞닥뜨리기 두려운 것이었다. 그리하여 만날 때 다른 이들이 곁에 있으리라는 것에 기대는 것만이, 그것을 앞두고 패니가 지닌 유일한 버팀이었다. 그녀는 어떤 갑작스러운 공략도 피하려는 조심에서, 되도록 버트럼 부인 곁을 떠나지 않고, 동쪽 방을 멀리하고, 관목 산책로에서 혼자 걷지 않았다.\n\n그녀는 뜻을 이뤘다. 크로퍼드 양이 정말로 왔을 때 그녀는 이모와 함께 아침 식사방에 안전히 있었다. 그리고 첫 괴로움이 지나고, 크로퍼드 양이 그녀가 짐작한 것보다 훨씬 덜 노골적인 표정으로 보고 말하니, 패니는 웬만한 동요의 반 시간 말고는 견뎌 낼 더 나쁜 것이 없으려니 바라기 시작했다. 그러나 여기서 그녀는 너무 많이 바랐다. 크로퍼드 양은 기회의 노예가 아니었다. 그녀는 패니를 단둘이 보기로 굳게 마음먹은지라, 자못 이내 나직한 목소리로 그녀에게 말했다. “어디서든 몇 분 그쪽하고 얘기해야겠어요.” 패니가 온몸으로, 온 맥박과 온 신경으로 느낀 말이었다. 마다하기란 어림없었다. 오히려 얼른 따르는 그 버릇 탓에 그녀는 거의 대번에 일어나 앞장서 방을 나갔다. 그녀는 비참한 심경으로 그리했으나, 피할 수 없는 일이었다.\n\n현관홀에 나서기 무섭게 크로퍼드 양 편에서는 낯빛의 온갖 삼감이 다했다. 그녀는 대번에 짓궂으면서도 정겨운 나무람으로 패니에게 고개를 젓고, 손을 잡더니, 좀처럼 곧바로 시작하지 않고는 못 배기는 듯했다. 그러나 그녀는 그저 “못된, 못된 사람 같으니! 언제쯤 그쪽 잔소리를 다 할지 모르겠어요” 하고 말할 뿐, 넉 벽이 온전히 저희만의 것이 될 때까지 나머지는 아껴 둘 만한 분별은 있었다. 패니는 자연스레 위층으로 향해, 손님을 이제 늘 편안히 쓸 만해진 그 방으로 데려갔다. 그러나 더없이 아픈 마음으로 문을 열며, 저 앞에 그곳이 여태 지켜본 그 어떤 것보다 더 괴로운 장면이 놓여 있음을 느끼면서였다. 그러나 그녀에게 막 터지려던 그 화는, 크로퍼드 양의 생각이 문득 바뀐 덕에, 다시 동쪽 방에 있는 자신을 발견한 것이 그녀 마음에 미친 세찬 영향 덕에 적어도 미뤄졌다.\n\n“어머!” 그녀가 대번에 생기를 띠며 외쳤다. “내가 여기 다시 왔네요? 동쪽 방! 전에 딱 한 번 이 방에 와 봤어요.” 그리고 둘레를 둘러보려, 그리고 그때 오간 온갖 것을 되짚어 보려는 듯 멈춰 섰다가, 그녀가 덧붙였다. “전에 딱 한 번. 기억나요? 나는 연습하러 왔었죠. 그쪽 사촌도 왔고, 우리는 연습을 했어요. 그쪽은 우리 청중이자 대사 알려 주는 이였고요. 즐거운 연습이었어요. 나는 결코 못 잊을 거예요. 우리가 여기 있었죠, 바로 이 자리에. 여기 그쪽 사촌이, 여기 내가, 여기 의자들이 있었어요. 아! 어째서 이런 것들이 다 지나가 버려야 하죠?”\n\n그녀의 벗에게는 다행히도, 그녀는 아무 대답도 바라지 않았다. 그녀의 마음은 온통 저에게만 쏠려 있었다. 그녀는 달콤한 추억의 몽롱함에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n“우리가 연습하던 그 장면은 정말이지 어찌나 남다르던지! 그 화제가 정말이지 그토록—그토록—뭐라 할까요? 그이는 나한테 혼인을 그려 보이고 권하기로 되어 있었죠. 나는 지금도 그이가 보이는 것 같아요, 그 두 긴 대사 내내 안할트답게 그토록 얌전하고 차분하려 애쓰던 모습이요. ‘두 마음 맞는 이가 혼인의 상태에서 만나면, 혼인은 행복한 삶이라 불릴 만하지요.’ 그이가 그 말을 할 때의 그 낯빛과 목소리에 대한 내 인상은 어떤 시간으로도 결코 못 지울 거예요. 우리가 그런 장면을 연기하게 됐다는 게 참 묘했어요, 아주 묘했죠! 만일 내가 내 삶의 어느 한 주라도 되불러 올 힘이 있다면, 그건 그 한 주여야 해요—그 연기하던 한 주. 뭐라 하시든, 패니, 그건 그 주여야 해요. 나는 다른 어디서도 그토록 지극한 행복을 알아 본 적이 없거든요. 그이의 그 뻣뻣한 기개가 그렇게나 꺾이다니! 아! 그건 이루 말할 수 없이 달콤했어요. 하지만 아아, 바로 그날 저녁이 그 모두를 무너뜨렸죠. 바로 그날 저녁이 그쪽의 그 더없이 달갑잖은 삼촌을 데려왔으니까요. 딱한 토머스 경, 그가 온 걸 반긴 이가 누구였겠어요? 하지만, 패니, 내가 지금 토머스 경을 함부로 말하려니 여기진 마요, 하기야 나야 분명 몇 주씩이나 그를 미워했지만요. 아니에요, 나는 이제 그를 온당히 봐요. 그는 그런 집안의 가장이 마땅히 그래야 할 딱 그대로예요. 아니, 차분한 서글픔으로 말하건대, 나는 이제 그쪽 식구 모두를 사랑하지 싶어요.” 그리고 그리 말하고서, 패니가 여태 그녀에게서 본 적 없는, 그리고 이제 너무나 어울린다 여긴 그런 애틋함과 켕김을 담아, 그녀는 저를 추스르려 한순간 돌아섰다. “이 방에 들어온 뒤로 내가 잠깐 발작을 겪었죠, 보시다시피.” 그녀가 이내 장난스러운 미소로 말했다. “하지만 이제 지났어요. 그러니 우리 앉아서 편안히 있어요. 잔소리로 말하면, 패니, 나는 그걸 하려고 잔뜩 벼르고 왔지만, 막상 닥치니 도무지 그럴 마음이 안 나거든요.” 그리고 그녀를 자못 정겹게 끌어안으며, “착하고 상냥한 패니! 이게 얼마나 오래일지 모를 그쪽을 보는 마지막이라는 걸 생각하면, 나는 그쪽을 사랑하는 것 말고는 아무것도 도무지 못 하겠어요.”\n\n패니는 마음이 움직였다. 그녀는 이런 것을 조금도 내다보지 못했고, 그녀의 심경은 “마지막”이라는 낱말의 그 서글픈 힘을 좀처럼 못 버텼다. 그녀는 마치 도무지 그럴 수 없을 만큼 크로퍼드 양을 사랑하기라도 한 양 울었다. 그리고 그런 심경을 보고 한결 더 누그러진 크로퍼드 양은, 그녀에게 다정히 매달리며 말했다. “나는 그쪽을 두고 가는 게 싫어요. 내가 가는 데선 이 반만큼 상냥한 이도 못 볼 테니까요. 우리가 자매가 안 되리라고 누가 그래요? 나는 우리가 그리되리란 걸 알아요. 나는 우리가 이어질 운명으로 태어났음을 느껴요. 그리고 그 눈물이, 그쪽도 그걸 느낀다는 걸 나한테 확신시켜 줘요, 사랑하는 패니.”\n\n패니는 정신을 차리고, 그저 절반만 대꾸하며 말했다. “하지만 크로퍼드 양은 그저 한 무리의 벗에게서 다른 무리의 벗에게로 가는 거잖아요. 아주 각별한 벗한테 가시는 거고요.”\n\n“그래요, 참 맞는 말이에요. 프레이저 부인은 여러 해 내 절친한 벗이었죠. 하지만 나는 그이 곁에 갈 마음이 조금도 없어요. 나는 오직 내가 두고 가는 벗들만 생각할 수 있거든요. 훌륭한 우리 언니, 그쪽, 그리고 버트럼가 사람들 다요. 그쪽들한테는 온 세상에서 찾을 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 마음이 있어요. 그쪽들 모두가 나한테, 그쪽들을 믿고 마음을 터놓을 수 있다는 느낌을 줘요, 여느 사귐에선 도무지 알 수 없는 그런 느낌 말이에요. 나는 프레이저 부인하고 부활절 뒤에나 가기로 약속해 뒀더라면 싶어요, 방문엔 훨씬 나은 때니까요. 하지만 이제 와서 미룰 순 없죠. 그리고 그이하고 볼일이 끝나면 나는 그 언니 스토너웨이 부인한테 가야 해요, 그이가 둘 중에선 오히려 내 가장 각별한 벗이었으니까요. 하지만 이 삼 년간 나는 그이한테도 별 마음이 없었어요.”\n\n이 말 뒤에 두 아가씨는 저마다 생각에 잠긴 채 여러 분 잠잠히 앉아 있었다. 패니는 세상의 갖가지 우정을 곰곰 헤아렸고, 메리는 좀 덜 철학적인 것을 헤아렸다. 그녀가 먼저 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n“내가 그쪽을 위층에서 찾아보기로 마음먹고, 어디쯤인지도 모른 채 동쪽 방으로 가는 길을 찾아 나선 걸 어찌나 또렷이 기억하는지! 걸어오면서 내가 무슨 생각을 했는지, 안을 들여다보고 그쪽이 이 탁자에 앉아 바느질하는 걸 본 걸, 그다음 그쪽 사촌이 문을 열고 여기 있는 나를 보고 얼마나 놀랐는지를 얼마나 또렷이 기억하는지! 정말이지, 그쪽 삼촌이 바로 그날 저녁에 돌아오다니! 이런 일은 여태 도무지 없었어요.”\n\n또 한 번 잠깐의 상념이 뒤따랐고, 그것을 떨쳐 낸 그녀는 이렇게 제 벗을 공략했다.\n\n“아니, 패니, 그쪽 아주 몽롱해 있네요. 나야 그쪽 생각을 늘 하는 이를 생각하고 있으려니 바라지만. 아! 잠시라도 그쪽을 시내의 우리 무리로 데려갈 수 있다면, 헨리에 대한 그쪽 힘이 거기서 어떻게 여겨지는지 그쪽이 알 수 있을 텐데! 아! 수십 명이 넘는 이들의 그 시샘과 배 아파함, 그쪽이 한 일을 듣고 느낄 그 놀라움과 못 믿음이라니! 은밀함으로 말하면, 헨리는 아주 옛 이야기 속 사내 주인공이라, 제 사슬을 자랑스러워하거든요. 그쪽은 런던에 와서 제 정복이 어떤 값어치인지 가늠해 봐야 해요. 그가 어떻게 떠받들리는지, 그리고 그이 덕에 내가 어떻게 떠받들리는지 그쪽이 본다면! 이제 나는, 그이가 그쪽과 그런 처지가 된 탓에 내가 프레이저 부인한테 그 반만큼도 반갑지 않으리란 걸 잘 알아요. 그이가 진실을 알게 되면, 아마 나를 다시 노샘프턴셔로 보내고 싶어 할걸요. 프레이저 씨한테 전처소생 딸이 하나 있는데, 그이가 그 애를 시집보내려고 안달이 나서 헨리더러 데려가라 하거든요. 아! 그이가 그이를 얻으려고 어찌나 애를 썼는지. 여기 이토록 순진하고 조용히 앉아 있는 그쪽은, 그쪽이 일으킬 그 파장이, 그쪽을 보려는 그 궁금함이, 내가 답해야 할 그 끝없는 물음들이 어떨지 짐작도 못 할 거예요! 딱한 마거릿 프레이저는 그쪽 눈이며 이며, 머리 어떻게 하는지, 신발은 누가 만드는지를 두고 영영 나를 들볶겠죠. 나는, 딱한 내 벗을 위해 마거릿이 시집갔으면 싶어요. 프레이저네야말로 여느 부부만큼이나 불행하지 싶으니까요. 하지만 그때야 재닛한테는 더없이 바람직한 짝이었죠. 우리 다 기뻐했고요. 그이는 그를 받아들일밖에 없었어요, 그가 부유하고 그이는 가진 게 없었으니까요. 그런데 그가 알고 보니 성미가 고약하고 까다로워서, 스물다섯의 젊은 여자, 아름다운 젊은 여자더러 저만큼이나 진득하라고 해요. 그리고 내 벗은 그를 잘 다루질 못해요. 그걸 어떻게 잘 넘겨야 할지 도무지 모르는 것 같아요. 좋게 말해도 분명 무척 무례한, 짜증의 기운이 있죠. 그 집에서 나는 맨스필드 목사관의 부부 사이를 존경심으로 떠올리게 될 거예요. 그랜트 박사조차도 우리 언니를 온전히 믿고, 언니 판단을 얼마간 헤아려 줘서, 정말 애착이 있구나 느끼게 하거든요. 하지만 프레이저네선 그런 걸 조금도 못 보겠죠. 나는 영영 맨스필드에 마음이 가 있을 거예요, 패니. 아내로선 우리 언니가, 남편으론 토머스 버트럼 경이 내 완벽함의 잣대예요. 딱한 재닛은 서글피 속아 넘어갔죠, 그러면서도 제 편에선 온당찮은 게 없었어요. 그이는 경솔하게 그 혼인에 뛰어든 게 아니거든요. 앞을 못 내다본 것도 아니고요. 그이는 그의 청혼을 사흘 두고 생각했고, 그 사흘 동안 제 지인들 가운데 의견 들을 만한 이는 죄다 붙잡고 조언을 구했어요. 특히 돌아가신 우리 소중한 숙모한테 여쭸는데, 세상 물정에 밝아 그 판단이 지인인 젊은이들 사이에 두루, 또 마땅히 우러러졌던 분이죠. 그런데 그분이 딱 프레이저 씨 편이었어요. 이건 마치 무엇으로도 부부의 편안함을 장담할 수 없다는 것 같잖아요. 내 벗 플로라로 말하면 나는 그리 할 말이 없어요. 그 애는 근위 기병대의 아주 좋은 젊은이를 그 끔찍한 스토너웨이 경 때문에 차 버렸거든요. 그자는, 패니, 러시워스 씨만큼이나 지각이 있는데 훨씬 더 못생겼고, 게다가 불한당 같은 사람이에요. 나야 그때 그 애가 옳은지 미심쩍었어요, 그자는 신사다운 풍채조차 없으니까요. 그리고 이제 나는 그 애가 틀렸다고 확신해요. 그건 그렇고, 플로라 로스는 사교계에 나온 첫 겨울에 헨리한테 죽고 못 살았죠. 하지만 내가 알기로 그이한테 반한 여자들을 죄다 그쪽한테 말하려 든다면, 나는 영영 끝이 없을 거예요. 그이를 무언가 무심함 비슷한 걸로 생각할 수 있는 건 그쪽뿐이에요, 오직 그쪽, 무정한 패니뿐. 하지만 그쪽, 정말 스스로 말하는 만큼 무정해요? 아뇨, 아뇨, 아닌 게 보이는걸요.”\n\n과연 그 순간 패니의 얼굴엔 자못 짙은 홍조가 어려, 미리 그리 여기는 마음에라면 강한 의심을 품을 만도 했다.\n\n“훌륭한 사람 같으니! 그쪽을 놀리진 않을게요. 온갖 게 제 갈 길로 가겠죠. 하지만, 사랑하는 패니, 그쪽 사촌이 여기는 만큼 그쪽이 그 물음을 받을 채비가 아주 안 돼 있던 건 아니었다는 건 인정해야 해요. 그쪽이 이 일을 두고 얼마간 생각이, 어찌 될까 하는 얼마간의 짐작이 없었을 리 없어요. 그이가 제 힘닿는 온갖 마음씀으로 그쪽 마음에 들려 애쓰는 걸 그쪽도 봤을 게 분명하고요. 무도회에서 그이가 그쪽한테 홀딱 빠져 있지 않았어요? 그리고 무도회 전엔, 그 목걸이! 아! 그쪽은 그걸 딱 그 뜻대로 받았어요. 그쪽은 바랄 나위 없이 다 알고 있었죠. 나는 그걸 아주 또렷이 기억해요.”\n\n“그럼 크로퍼드 양은, 오라버니가 그 목걸이를 미리 알고 있었다는 말씀이에요? 아! 크로퍼드 양, 그건 온당치 않았어요.”\n\n“알고 있었냐고요! 그건 온전히 그이가 한 일, 그이 생각이었어요. 부끄럽지만 나야 그런 생각이 통 안 들었는데, 나는 그쪽들 둘 다를 위해 그이 제안대로 해서 기뻤죠.”\n\n“저는 말 안 하겠어요,” 패니가 대꾸했다. “그때 저도 그런 게 아닐까 절반쯤 겁나지 않았다고는요. 크로퍼드 양의 낯빛에 저를 겁먹게 하는 무언가가 있었으니까요, 하지만 처음엔 아니었어요. 처음엔 저는 그걸 조금도 의심 안 했어요—정말, 정말 그랬어요. 제가 여기 앉아 있는 것만큼이나 참이에요. 그리고 제가 그걸 짐작이라도 했다면, 무엇으로도 저는 그 목걸이를 받지 않았을 거예요. 오라버니의 처신으로 말하면, 분명 저는 어떤 각별함을 느끼긴 했어요. 얼마 전부터, 어쩌면 두세 주 전부터 그걸 느꼈죠. 하지만 그때 저는 그게 아무 뜻도 아니려니 여겼어요. 그저 그이의 방식이려니 여겼고, 그이가 저를 진지하게 생각하리라곤 바라기는커녕 짐작도 안 했어요. 크로퍼드 양, 저는 여름과 가을에 그이와 이 집안 몇몇 사이에 오간 걸 무심히 보고만 있던 사람이 아니거든요. 저는 조용했지만 눈멀진 않았어요. 크로퍼드 씨가 아무 뜻도 없는 정겨움으로 재미 삼는 걸 저는 볼밖에 없었죠.”\n\n“아! 나도 그건 부인 못 해요. 그이는 이따금 딱한 바람둥이였고, 아가씨들 마음에 저지를지 모를 그 난장은 별로 아랑곳 안 했죠. 나도 곧잘 그걸 두고 그이를 나무랐어요. 하지만 그게 그이의 유일한 흠이에요. 그리고 이런 말은 할 수 있죠, 마음 쓸 값어치 있는 정을 지닌 아가씨는 아주 드물다고요. 그리고, 패니, 그토록 많은 이의 표적이던 이를 붙잡는 그 영광이라니. 제 성(性)의 빚을 갚을 힘을 지닌다는 그 영광! 아! 나는 그런 승리를 마다하는 건 여자의 본성에 없는 일이라 확신해요.”\n\n패니는 고개를 저었다. “저는 어떤 여자의 마음이든 가지고 노는 사내를 좋게 볼 수가 없어요. 그리고 곁에서 지켜보는 이가 가늠할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 많이 겪어 내는 경우가 곧잘 있고요.”\n\n“나는 그이를 편들지 않아요. 그이를 온전히 그쪽 자비에 맡길게요. 그이가 그쪽을 에버링엄에 데려가면, 그쪽이 아무리 그이를 나무라도 나는 상관 안 해요. 하지만 이건 말하겠어요, 그이의 흠, 곧 아가씨들이 저한테 얼마간 반하게 만들기를 좋아하는 그 흠은, 저 스스로 사랑에 빠지는 성향—그이가 여태 한 번도 빠진 적 없는 그 성향의 절반만큼도 아내의 행복에 위험하지 않다고요. 그리고 나는 정말이지 진심으로 믿어요, 그이가 여태 어떤 여자한테도 그런 적 없는 방식으로 그쪽한테 애착하고 있음을, 그이가 온 마음으로 그쪽을 사랑하고, 되도록 영원에 가깝도록 그쪽을 사랑하리라는 걸. 어떤 사내든 여자를 영원히 사랑한 적이 있다면, 나는 헨리가 그쪽한테 그만큼 할 거라 여겨요.”\n\n패니는 어렴풋한 미소를 피할 수 없었으나, 아무 할 말이 없었다.\n\n“나는 헨리가 그쪽 오라비의 임관을 얻어 내는 데 성공했을 때보다 더 행복한 적이 있었으리라곤 상상할 수 없어요.” 이내 메리가 말을 이었다.\n\n그녀는 여기서 패니의 심경을 확실히 찔렀다.\n\n“어머! 네. 어쩌면 그토록, 그토록 친절하세요.”\n\n“나는 그이가 무척 애썼을 게 분명하다는 걸 알아요, 그이가 움직여야 했던 이들을 아니까요. 제독은 성가신 걸 싫어하고 청탁 넣는 걸 업신여기죠. 게다가 같은 식으로 봐줘야 할 젊은이들의 청이 하도 많으니, 그리 단단하지 못한 우정과 열의야 쉬 밀려나거든요. 윌리엄은 얼마나 행복한 사람일까! 우리가 그를 볼 수 있으면 싶네요.”\n\n딱한 패니의 마음은 그 온갖 갈래 가운데 가장 괴로운 것에 던져졌다. 윌리엄을 위해 이뤄진 일을 떠올리는 것은 크로퍼드 씨에 대한 온갖 굳은 마음을 늘 가장 세차게 흔드는 것이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 그것을 골똘히 생각하며 앉아 있었는데, 처음엔 흐뭇이 그녀를 지켜보다가 그다음 딴것을 곰곰 헤아리던 메리가 문득 이렇게 말하며 그녀의 정신을 불러 세웠다. “나는 여기서 온종일 그쪽하고 앉아 얘기하고 싶지만, 아래층 부인들을 잊어선 안 되겠죠. 그러니 잘 있어요, 사랑하는, 상냥한, 훌륭한 나의 패니. 우리가 이름으로야 아침 식사방에서 헤어지겠지만, 나는 여기서 그쪽과 작별해야겠어요. 그리고 나는 정말 작별해요, 행복한 재회를 못내 바라며, 우리가 다시 만날 때는 삼감의 자취도 그림자도 없이 서로에게 마음을 열 수 있는 그런 사정에서이기를 믿으며.”\n\n자못, 자못 다정한 포옹과 얼마간의 마음 졸이는 태도가 이 말에 뒤따랐다.\n\n“나는 머잖아 시내에서 그쪽 사촌을 볼 거예요. 그이가 제법 이내 거기 가겠다니까요. 그리고 토머스 경도, 아무래도 봄 동안에는요. 그리고 그쪽 맏사촌이랑 러시워스 내외랑 줄리아는 몇 번이고 다시 만날 게 분명해요, 그쪽만 빼고 다요. 나는 부탁이 둘 있어요, 패니. 하나는 그쪽 편지예요. 나한테 꼭 편지해요. 그리고 다른 하나는, 그쪽이 곧잘 그랜트 부인을 찾아가, 내가 떠난 걸 그이한테 갚아 주는 거예요.”\n\n이 부탁 가운데 적어도 첫째는 패니가 차라리 안 받았으면 싶은 것이었다. 그러나 편지 왕래를 마다하기란 그녀로선 어림없었다. 제 판단이 허락하는 것보다 더 선선히 그것에 응하지 않기조차 어림없었다. 그토록 짙어 보이는 애정을 거스를 길이 없었던 것이다. 그녀의 성미는 다정한 대접을 유난히 값지게 여기도록 되어 있었고, 여태 그런 것을 워낙 못 알고 지낸 터라, 크로퍼드 양의 그것에 더욱 사로잡혔다. 게다가, 저희 단둘의 이야기를 제 두려움이 점친 것보다 훨씬 덜 괴롭게 해 준 데 대한 고마움도 있었다.\n\n그것은 끝났고, 그녀는 나무람도 들킴도 없이 벗어났다. 그녀의 비밀은 여전히 저만의 것이었다. 그리고 그러한 한, 그녀는 거의 온갖 것에 저를 내맡길 수 있으리라 여겼다.\n\n저녁에 또 한 번의 작별이 있었다. 헨리 크로퍼드가 와서 한동안 그들과 앉아 있었다. 그리고 그녀의 기운이 앞서 그리 굳센 상태가 아니던 터라, 그가 정말로 무언가 느끼는 듯 보여, 그녀의 마음은 한동안 그를 향해 누그러졌다. 여느 저와는 아주 딴판으로, 그는 좀처럼 아무 말도 안 했다. 그는 분명 짓눌려 있었다. 그리고 패니는, 그를 다시는 다른 여자의 남편이 되기 전엔 안 보기를 바라면서도, 그를 안쓰러워할밖에 없었다.\n\n헤어질 순간이 오자, 그는 기어이 그녀의 손을 잡으려 했고, 그것을 마다하게 놔두지 않았다. 그러나 그는 아무 말도, 적어도 그녀가 들은 말은 아무것도 안 했다. 그리고 그가 방을 떠나자, 그녀는 그런 우정의 정표가 오간 것이 오히려 흐뭇했다.\n\n이튿날 크로퍼드 남매는 떠났다.\n\n# 제37장\n\n크로퍼드 씨가 떠나자, 토머스 경의 다음 목표는 그가 아쉬워지는 것이었다. 그리하여 그는, 그 무렵 조카딸이 나쁜 것으로 느꼈거나 여겼던 그 마음씀을 잃은 데서 그녀가 허전함을 찾아내리라 크게 바랐다. 그녀는 그 값어치를 더없이 우쭐한 꼴로 맛본 참이었다. 그러니 그는, 그것을 잃음이, 다시 아무것도 아닌 데로 가라앉음이 그녀 마음에 자못 이로운 뉘우침을 일깨우기를 정말 바랐다. 그는 이런 생각으로 그녀를 지켜보았다. 그러나 얼마나 성과가 있는지 그로선 좀처럼 알 수 없었다. 그녀의 기운에 무슨 다름이 있는지 없는지조차 그는 좀처럼 알지 못했다. 그녀는 늘 하도 상냥하고 물러나 있는지라 그 심경이 그의 분간을 넘어섰던 것이다. 그는 그녀를 헤아리지 못했다. 그리하여 그는 헤아리지 못함을 느끼고, 그런 까닭에 에드먼드에게, 그녀가 이번 일에서 어찌 느끼고 있는지, 예전보다 더 행복한지 덜 행복한지를 일러 달라 청했다.\n\n에드먼드는 뉘우침의 낌새라곤 조금도 알아채지 못했고, 처음 사나흘로 무슨 뉘우침이 생기리라 여기는 아버지가 다소 무리하다 여겼다.\n\n에드먼드가 무엇보다 놀란 것은, 크로퍼드의 누이, 곧 그녀에게 그토록 큰 존재이던 그 벗이자 동무가 더 눈에 띄게 아쉬워지지 않는다는 것이었다. 그는 패니가 그녀에 대해 그토록 드물게 말하고, 이 헤어짐에 대한 저의 마음을 자진해 얘기하는 것이 그토록 적은 것이 의아했다.\n\n아아! 이제 패니의 편안함에 으뜸가는 골칫거리가 된 것이 바로 이 누이, 이 벗이자 동무였다. 만일 메리의 앞날의 운명이, 저가 그 오라비의 것은 그러하기를 굳게 마음먹은 만큼이나 맨스필드와 상관없다 믿을 수 있었더라면, 만일 그녀가 그리로 돌아올 날이, 저가 그의 것은 그러하리라 자못 여기고 싶은 만큼이나 멀다 바랄 수 있었더라면, 그녀는 정말이지 마음이 가벼웠으련만. 그러나 떠올리고 지켜볼수록, 크로퍼드 양이 에드먼드와 혼인하는 쪽으로 이제 온갖 것이 여태 없던 만큼이나 더 순조로운 궤도에 올랐음을 그녀는 더 깊이 확신했다. 그의 편에서 그 마음이 더 굳세어졌고, 그녀의 편에서 덜 애매해졌다. 그의 반대, 곧 그 성실함의 거리낌은 어찌 된 영문인지 죄다 사라진 듯했다. 그리고 그녀의 야심의 미심쩍음과 머뭇거림 또한 마찬가지로—그리고 마찬가지로 이렇다 할 까닭 없이 걷혔다. 그것은 오직 짙어 가는 애착에 돌릴밖에 없었다. 그의 좋은 심경과 그녀의 나쁜 심경이 사랑에 굽혔고, 그런 사랑이라면 둘을 맺어 줄 수밖에 없었다. 그는 손턴 레이시에 관한 얼마간의 볼일이 끝나는 대로—어쩌면 두 주 안에 시내로 갈 참이었다. 그는 갈 얘기를 했고, 그 얘기를 즐겨 했다. 그리고 다시 한번 그녀와 함께하면 나머지야 패니로선 의심할 나위 없었다. 그녀의 승낙은 그의 청혼만큼이나 틀림없을 터였다. 그런데도 여전히 남은 나쁜 심경이 있어, 그것의 전망을 그녀에게 더없이 서글픈 것으로 만들었으니—그녀가 믿기로는, 저 자신과는 별개로 서글픈 것이었다.\n\n바로 그 지난번 대화에서, 크로퍼드 양은 얼마간의 상냥한 심경과 자못 큰 인간적 친절에도 불구하고 여전히 크로퍼드 양이었다. 여전히 잘못 이끌려 갈피 못 잡으면서도 그런 줄은 조금도 의심 못 하는 마음을, 어두워졌으면서도 스스로 밝은 줄 여기는 마음을 드러냈다. 그녀는 사랑할지언정, 다른 어떤 심경으로도 에드먼드를 받을 만하지 않았다. 패니는 둘 사이에 통하는 두 번째 심경이 좀처럼 없다 믿었다. 그리고 그녀가, 크로퍼드 양이 앞으로 나아질 가망을 거의 가망 없다 보는 것을, 만일 이 사랑의 철에 에드먼드의 힘이 그녀의 판단을 맑히고 그 소견을 다잡는 데 이미 그토록 조금밖에 못 했다면 그 값어치가 혼인의 세월 속에서조차 끝내 그녀에게 헛되이 낭비되리라 여기는 것을, 더 나이 든 현인들은 용서해 줄지도 모른다.\n\n경험이라면 그런 처지의 젊은이들에게 더 많이 바랐을지도 모르고, 공평함이라면 크로퍼드 양의 천성에서, 저가 사랑하고 존경하는 사내의 의견을 제 것으로 받아들이게 할 그 여자의 두루 있는 천성의 몫을 부인하지 않았으리라. 그러나 패니의 믿음이 그러했던지라, 그녀는 그것으로 무척 괴로워했고, 아픔 없이는 결코 크로퍼드 양을 입에 올릴 수 없었다.\n\n한편 토머스 경은 제 나름의 바람과 제 나름의 지켜봄을 이어 갔다. 여전히 인간 본성에 대한 그 온갖 앎으로, 힘과 값어치를 잃은 것이 조카딸의 기운에 미친 영향을, 그리고 그 사랑하던 이의 지난 마음씀이 그 되돌아옴에 대한 갈망을 자아내는 것을 볼 자격이 저에게 있다 느꼈다. 그리고 그는 이내, 이 모두를 아직 온전히, 틀림없이 못 보는 까닭을, 또 한 사람의 손님이 온다는 전망으로 풀어 낼 수 있게 되었으니, 그 다가옴은 그가 지켜보던 그 기운을 떠받치기에 아주 넉넉하다 여길 만한 것이었다. 윌리엄이 노샘프턴셔에서 쓸 열흘의 휴가를 얻어, 세상에 둘도 없이 행복한 중위—갓 임관된 중위인지라 가장 행복한 중위로서, 제 행복을 보이고 제 제복을 그려 보이러 오는 것이었다.\n\n그는 왔다. 그리고 그는 여기서도 제 제복을 보이면 기뻤으련만, 잔인한 관례가 근무 중 말고는 그 차림을 금했다. 그리하여 제복은 포츠머스에 남았고, 에드먼드는 패니가 그것을 볼 낌새라도 있기 전에 그 온갖 새것 냄새며 그것을 입은 이의 온갖 새것 같은 심경이 다 닳아 없어지리라 넘겨짚었다. 그것은 부끄러움의 표지로 가라앉을 터였다. 중위가 된 지 한두 해에, 남들이 저보다 먼저 함장이 되는 것을 보는 중위의 제복보다 더 안 어울리고 더 값없는 것이 무엇이랴? 에드먼드는 이렇게 헤아렸으니, 그의 아버지가 그로 하여금, 국왕 폐하의 배 스러시호의 차석 중위가 그 온갖 영광에 싸인 것을 패니가 볼 낌새를 딴 눈으로 보게 한 한 계획의 속내를 그에게 털어놓기까지는 그러했다.\n\n이 계획인즉, 그녀가 오라비를 따라 포츠머스로 돌아가, 제 식구와 얼마간의 시간을 보낸다는 것이었다. 그것은, 위엄 어린 상념에 잠긴 어느 때에 토머스 경에게 옳고 바람직한 조처로 떠오른 것이었다. 그러나 아주 마음을 굳히기 전에 그는 아들과 의논했다. 에드먼드는 그것을 어느 모로나 헤아려 보고, 옳은 것 말고는 아무것도 보지 못했다. 그 일은 그 자체로 좋았고, 이보다 나은 때에 할 수도 없었다. 그리고 그는 그것이 패니에게 더없이 흐뭇한 일임을 의심치 않았다. 이것으로 토머스 경은 마음을 정하기에 넉넉했다. 그리하여 단호한 “그럼 그리하지”가 그 일의 그 단계를 매듭지었다. 토머스 경은 얼마간의 흡족한 심경과, 아들에게 전한 것 이상의 좋은 속셈을 지닌 채 거기서 물러났다. 그녀를 떠나보내는 그의 으뜸가는 동기는 그녀가 다시 부모를 보는 것의 온당함과는 별로 상관이 없었고, 그녀를 행복하게 해 준다는 생각과는 조금도 상관이 없었던 것이다. 그는 분명 그녀가 기꺼이 가기를 바랐으나, 그에 못지않게 분명, 그 방문이 끝나기 전에 그녀가 집에 진저리 나기를 바랐다. 그리고 맨스필드 파크의 우아함과 호사를 얼마간 삼가는 것이 그녀의 마음을 차분한 상태로 만들어, 그녀에게 청해진, 더 오래가고 못지않게 편안한 그 보금자리의 값어치를 더 올바로 가늠하도록 기울게 하기를 바랐다.\n\n그것은 조카딸의 슬기를 겨냥한 처방이었으니, 그는 그것을 지금으로선 병들었다 여길밖에 없었다. 여덟아홉 해를 재물과 넉넉함의 거처에서 지낸 것이 그녀의 견주고 판단하는 힘을 얼마간 어지럽혀 놓은 것이다. 아버지의 집이 아마 그녀에게 넉넉한 수입의 값어치를 가르쳐 줄 터였다. 그리고 그는, 저가 궁리해 낸 이 시험 덕에 그녀가 평생 더 지혜롭고 더 행복한 여자가 되리라 믿었다.\n\n패니가 조금이라도 황홀에 빠지는 사람이었더라면, 무엇이 뜻해졌는지를 처음 알아들었을 때—삼촌이 처음 그녀에게, 거의 반평생 떨어져 지낸 그 부모와 형제자매를 만나 볼 것을, 윌리엄을 그 여정의 보호자이자 동무로 삼아 두어 달 어린 시절의 그 자리로 돌아갈 것을, 그리고 윌리엄이 뭍에 남아 있는 마지막 시각까지 계속 그를 볼 수 있으리라는 확실함을 내밀었을 때—그녀는 그 황홀에 세게 사로잡혔을 게 틀림없다. 그녀가 언제고 벅찬 기쁨에 몸을 내맡긴 적이 있다면 그것은 그때였을 게 틀림없으니, 그녀는 기뻤던 것이다. 그러나 그녀의 행복은 조용하고 깊은, 가슴 벅찬 종류의 것이었다. 그리고 그녀는 원래 말이 많은 사람은 아니었으되, 가장 세차게 느낄 때면 늘 더 잠잠해지는 쪽이었다. 그 순간 그녀는 그저 고마워하며 받아들일밖에 없었다. 그 뒤로, 그토록 문득 열린 그 즐거움의 그림에 익숙해지자, 그녀는 제가 느낀 바를 윌리엄과 에드먼드에게 더 넉넉히 얘기할 수 있었다. 그러나 그래도 말로 옷 입힐 수 없는 애틋한 심경들이 있었다. 제 온갖 가장 어릴 적 즐거움에 대한 기억과, 그것에서 뜯겨 나며 겪은 것에 대한 기억이 새로운 힘으로 그녀를 덮쳐 왔고, 다시 집에 있으면 그 헤어짐에서 그 뒤로 자라난 온갖 아픔이 다 나을 것만 같았다. 그런 무리의 한가운데에 있는 것, 그토록 많은 이에게 사랑받고, 여태 있어 본 것보다 다들에게 더 사랑받는 것, 두려움도 삼감도 없이 정을 느끼는 것, 저 자신이 저를 둘러싼 이들과 대등함을 느끼는 것, 크로퍼드가에 대한 온갖 언급에서 놓여나 평온한 것, 그들 때문에 나무람이려니 여겨질 만한 온갖 눈길에서 벗어나 안전한 것. 이것은 절반밖에 인정 못 할 애틋함으로 마음 두고픈 전망이었다.\n\n에드먼드도—그에게서 두 달 떨어져 있는 것이 (어쩌면 그녀는 그 부재를 석 달로 늘리도록 허락받을 수도 있으리라) 그녀에게 이로울 게 틀림없었다. 멀리 떨어져, 그의 낯빛이나 친절에 공략당하지 않고, 그의 마음을 아는 그 끊임없는 짜증과, 그의 속내를 피하려 애쓰는 것에서 안전한 곳에서라면, 그녀는 더 온당한 상태로 스스로를 다잡을 수 있으리라. 그녀는 그가 런던에서 온갖 것을 마련하고 있으려니, 비참함 없이 그를 생각할 수 있으리라. 맨스필드에서라면 견디기 힘들었을 것이 포츠머스에서는 자그마한 화가 되려 하고 있었다.\n\n단 하나 걸리는 것은 버트럼 이모가 그녀 없이 편안할까 하는 미심쩍음이었다. 그녀는 다른 누구에게도 쓸모가 없었다. 그러나 거기서는 그녀가, 생각하기 싫을 만큼이나 아쉬워질지도 몰랐다. 그리고 그 채비의 그 대목이야말로 정말이지 토머스 경이 이뤄 내기에 가장 어려운 것이었고, 오직 그만이 조금이라도 이뤄 낼 수 있는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 그는 맨스필드 파크의 주인이었다. 어떤 조처든 정말로 마음을 굳히면 그는 늘 그것을 밀고 나갈 수 있었다. 그리하여 이제, 그 화제를 두고 오래 얘기하고, 패니가 이따금 제 식구를 보는 것의 도리를 설명하고 되풀이한 끝에, 그는 아내로 하여금 그녀를 보내게 만들었다. 다만 확신에서라기보다 순종에서 얻어 낸 것이었으니, 버트럼 부인은 토머스 경이 패니가 가야 마땅하다 여기니 그리해야 한다는 것 말고는 좀처럼 확신하는 것이 없었기 때문이다. 제 옷방의 그 고요 속에서, 그의 어리둥절케 하는 말에 치우치지 않은 제 상념의 공평한 흐름 속에서라면, 그녀는 패니가, 저에게 그토록 쓸모 있으면서, 그토록 오래 저 없이 지내 온 아버지와 어머니 곁에 언제고 갈 무슨 필요가 있다고는 도무지 인정할 수 없었다. 그리고 그녀가 안 아쉬워지리라는 것—노리스 부인의 논의 아래 증명되려던 그 요점에 대해서는, 그녀는 그런 것을 인정하기를 아주 꿋꿋이 마다했다.\n\n토머스 경은 그녀의 이성과 양심과 위엄에 호소했다. 그는 그것을 희생이라 부르고, 그녀의 착함과 자제심에 그런 것으로서 그것을 요구했다. 그러나 노리스 부인은 그녀에게, 패니는 얼마든지 없어도 된다고—저 자신이 청받은 대로 제 온갖 시간을 그녀에게 다 바칠 채비가 돼 있으니—요컨대 정말로 필요하지도 아쉬워지지도 않으리라 설득하려 했다.\n\n“그럴지도 모르지, 언니.” 이것이 버트럼 부인의 대꾸의 전부였다. “아마 언니 말이 아주 옳겠죠. 하지만 나는 분명 그 애가 무척 아쉬울 거예요.”\n\n다음 단계는 포츠머스에 알리는 것이었다. 패니가 편지를 써서 저를 보내겠다 청했다. 그리고 어머니의 답장은, 비록 짧았으나 어찌나 다정하던지—제 아이를 다시 볼 전망에 그토록 자연스럽고 어머니다운 기쁨을 몇 마디 소박한 줄로 담아, 어머니와 함께한다는 것에 대한 딸의 온갖 행복의 그림을 죄다 굳혀 주었다—앞서 저에게 이렇다 할 애정을 보인 적이 분명 없던 그 “엄마”에게서 이제 따뜻하고 정겨운 벗을 찾으리라 그녀를 확신시켰다. 그러나 이것은, 제 잘못이었거나 제 상상이었으려니 그녀는 쉬 여길 수 있었다. 그녀는 아마 겁 많은 성미의 그 무력함과 짜증으로 사랑을 멀어지게 했거나, 그토록 많은 이 가운데 누구도 받을 만한 것보다 더 큰 몫을 바라는 무리한 짓을 했으리라. 이제, 어떻게 쓸모가 되고 어떻게 참는지를 더 잘 알게 되었을 때, 그리고 어머니가 더는 어린것들로 가득한 집의 끊임없는 요구에 매이지 않게 되었을 때라면, 온갖 편안함을 위한 여유와 마음이 있을 터였고, 둘은 이내 어머니와 딸이 서로에게 마땅히 그래야 할 사이가 될 터였다.\n\n윌리엄은 그 계획에 제 누이 못지않게 행복했다. 그로서는, 출항 전 마지막 순간까지 그녀를 거기 두는 것, 그리고 어쩌면 첫 순항에서 돌아왔을 때 그녀를 여전히 거기서 보는 것이 더없는 즐거움일 터였다. 게다가 그는 스러시호가 항구를 나서기 전에 그녀가 그것을 몹시도 봤으면 싶었다—스러시호야말로 분명 해군에서 가장 훌륭한 슬루프함이었다—그리고 조선소에도 그가 그녀에게 무척 보여 주고픈 손질이 여럿 있었다.\n\n그는 그녀가 얼마간 집에 있는 것이 모두에게 큰 득이 되리라는 말을 덧붙이기를 서슴지 않았다.\n\n“어찌 된 영문인지 모르겠어.” 그가 말했다. “하지만 아버지 댁엔 네 그 알뜰한 방식이랑 정연함이 얼마쯤 아쉬운 것 같아. 집이 늘 어수선하거든. 넌 분명 만사를 더 나은 식으로 굴러가게 해 놓을 거야. 넌 어머니한테 온갖 게 어때야 하는지 일러 줄 테고, 수전한테 그토록 쓸모가 될 테고, 벳시를 가르칠 테고, 사내애들이 널 사랑하고 따르게 만들 거야. 그게 다 얼마나 반듯하고 편안할까!”\n\n프라이스 부인의 답장이 이를 무렵엔, 맨스필드에서 보낼 날이 아주 며칠밖에 안 남아 있었다. 그리고 그 며칠 가운데 한 날의 얼마 동안, 젊은 나그네들은 제 여정을 두고 자못 마음을 졸였다. 그 방식이 얘기되기에 이르러, 형부의 돈을 아끼려던 제 온갖 안달이 헛됨을, 패니를 덜 비싼 편으로 데려가라는 제 바람과 넌지시 비침에도 불구하고 그들이 역마차로 가리라는 것을 노리스 부인이 알았을 때, 토머스 경이 실제로 그 비용으로 윌리엄에게 지폐를 주는 것을 그녀가 보았을 때, 그녀는 마차에 세 번째 자리가 있다는 생각에 사로잡혀, 문득 그들과 함께 가고픈, 딱한 소중한 동생 프라이스를 보러 가고픈 강한 마음에 휩싸인 것이다. 그녀는 제 생각을 떠벌렸다. 절반 넘게 젊은이들과 함께 가고픈 마음이 있노라 말해야겠다고, 저한테는 그것이 그런 호사일 테고, 딱한 소중한 동생 프라이스를 이십 년 넘게 못 봤으며, 저의 나이 든 지혜가 그들을 대신해 챙겨 주면 젊은이들 여정에 도움이 될 테고, 딱한 소중한 동생 프라이스가 이런 기회에 제가 안 오는 걸 무척 야박하게 여기지 않을까 여기지 않을 수 없다고.\n\n윌리엄과 패니는 그 생각에 소름이 끼쳤다.\n\n그 오붓한 여정의 온갖 편안함이 대번에 무너질 판이었다. 애처로운 낯빛으로 둘은 서로를 바라보았다. 그 마음 졸임이 한두 시간 이어졌다. 아무도 부추기거나 말리려 끼어들지 않았다. 노리스 부인은 그 일을 저 혼자 정하도록 내버려졌다. 그리고 그것은, 조카와 조카딸의 이루 말할 수 없는 기쁨으로, 저가 지금 맨스필드 파크에서 도무지 빠질 수 없다는 것—토머스 경과 버트럼 부인에게 저가 너무나 없어선 안 될 사람이라, 한 주라도 그들을 떠나는 것은 스스로에게 떳떳할 수 없으니, 반드시 그들에게 쓸모가 되는 즐거움을 위해 다른 온갖 즐거움을 희생해야 한다는 것을 떠올리는 것으로 끝났다.\n\n실은, 공짜로 포츠머스에 데려다진다 해도, 되돌아오는 제 비용은 도무지 안 치를 수 없으리라는 생각이 그녀에게 떠올랐던 것이다. 그리하여 딱한 소중한 동생 프라이스는 그런 기회를 놓치는 온갖 실망에 내맡겨졌고, 어쩌면 또 이십 년의 못 봄이 시작되었다.\n\n에드먼드의 계획도 이 포츠머스 여정에, 이 패니의 부재에 영향받았다. 그 역시 이모와 마찬가지로 맨스필드 파크에 바칠 희생이 있었다. 그는 이맘때쯤 런던에 갈 작정이었다. 그러나 그들의 편안함에 가장 중한 다른 온갖 이가 그들을 떠나는 바로 그때에 아버지와 어머니를 떠날 수는 없었다. 그리하여 자랑삼진 않았으되 느껴진 애씀으로, 그는 제 행복을 영영 굳혀 주리라는 바람으로 손꼽아 기다리던 그 여정을 한두 주 더 미뤘다.\n\n그는 그것을 패니에게 말했다. 그녀가 이미 하도 많이 아는지라, 그녀는 온갖 것을 알밖에 없었다. 그것이 크로퍼드 양에 관한 또 한 번의 은밀한 이야기의 알맹이를 이뤘다. 그리고 패니는, 크로퍼드 양의 이름이 조금이나마 스스럼없이 둘 사이에 언제고 입에 오르는 마지막이려니 느껴 한결 더 마음이 움직였다. 그 뒤 한 번 그가 그녀를 넌지시 입에 올렸다. 버트럼 부인이 저녁에 조카딸에게 이내 자주 편지하라 이르며 저 자신도 좋은 편지 벗이 되겠노라 약속하고 있었는데, 에드먼드가 알맞은 순간에 이렇게 속삭여 덧붙인 것이다. “그리고 내가 너한테 편지하마, 패니, 무언가 적을 값어치 있는 게, 네가 듣고 싶어 하려니 싶은, 그리고 다른 데선 그리 이내 못 들을 무언가 할 말이 생기면.” 듣는 동안 그의 뜻을 미심쩍어했다 한들, 그녀가 그를 올려다볼 제 그의 낯에 어린 그 달아오름이 그것을 못 박아 주었으리라.\n\n이 편지를 위해 그녀는 저를 다잡으려 애써야 했다. 에드먼드의 편지가 두려움의 대상이 되다니! 그녀는, 이 바뀜의 세상에서 시간의 흐름과 사정의 달라짐이 자아내는 그 온갖 생각과 심경의 바뀜을 저가 아직 다 겪지 않았음을 느끼기 시작했다. 인간 마음의 그 부침은 그녀에게서 아직 다하지 않은 것이었다.\n\n딱한 패니! 그녀가 그토록 기꺼이, 못내 가는 것이었음에도, 맨스필드 파크에서의 마지막 저녁은 그래도 비참함일밖에 없었다. 헤어질 제 그녀의 마음은 온통 서글펐다. 그녀는 집 안 방마다에 눈물을 흘렸고, 사랑하는 식구마다에는 훨씬 더 그러했다. 그녀는 이모에게 매달렸으니, 이모가 아쉬워질 것이기 때문이었다. 그녀는 삼촌의 손에 흐느낌을 삼키며 입 맞췄으니, 그를 언짢게 했기 때문이었다. 그리고 에드먼드로 말하면, 그와의 마지막 순간이 왔을 때 그녀는 말할 수도, 볼 수도, 생각할 수도 없었다. 그리고 그것이 지나고 나서야, 그녀는 그가 오라비의 정겨운 작별을 저에게 건네고 있었음을 알았다.\n\n이 모두가 밤새 오갔으니, 여정이 아침 아주 일찍 시작되기로 되어 있었기 때문이다. 그리하여 그 작고 단출해진 무리가 아침 식사에 모였을 때, 윌리엄과 패니는 이미 한 참(站)을 나아간 것으로 이야기되었다.\n\n# 제38장\n\n여행의 새로움과 윌리엄과 함께한다는 행복은, 맨스필드 파크가 아주 뒤로 물러나자 이내 패니의 기운에 그 자연스러운 효험을 냈다. 그리하여 첫 참(站)이 끝나 토머스 경의 마차를 떠나게 될 무렵, 그녀는 늙은 마부에게 작별을 고하고 밝은 낯빛으로 온당한 인사말을 돌려보낼 수 있었다.\n\n오누이 사이의 정겨운 얘기에는 끝이 없었다. 온갖 것이 잔뜩 신난 윌리엄의 마음에 재밋거리가 되었고, 그는 좀 더 높은 결의 화제 사이사이에 장난과 농이 넘쳤다. 그 화제들은, 시작이야 그렇지 않더라도, 죄다 스러시호에 대한 칭찬으로, 그것이 어찌 쓰일까 하는 짐작으로, 어떤 우세한 적과 한판 붙을 궁리로(선임 중위가 자리를 비운다 치면—윌리엄은 선임 중위에게 그리 인정을 베풀지 않았다—되도록 빨리 제 진급을 이룰 요량이었다), 아니면 나포 상금에 대한 억측으로 끝이 났다. 그 상금은 집에 넉넉히 나눠 주되, 그와 패니가 앞으로 중년과 노년을 죄다 함께 보낼 그 자그마한 오두막을 편안하게 꾸밀 만큼만 남겨 둘 참이었다.\n\n패니의 당장의 걱정거리는, 크로퍼드 씨와 얽힌 한, 그들 대화의 아무 몫도 되지 않았다. 윌리엄은 무슨 일이 있었는지 알았고, 저가 인간의 됨됨이 가운데 으뜸으로 여길밖에 없는 사내에게 누이의 심경이 그토록 차가운 것을 진심으로 안타까워했다. 그러나 그는 온통 사랑 편일 나이라 나무랄 줄을 몰랐다. 그리고 이 일에 대한 그녀의 바람을 알기에, 그는 조금도 넌지시 비쳐 그녀를 괴롭히지 않으려 했다.\n\n그녀는 아직 크로퍼드 씨에게 잊히지 않았으려니 여길 만한 까닭이 있었다. 맨스필드를 떠난 뒤 지난 세 주 동안 그녀는 그 누이에게서 여러 번 소식을 받았고, 편지마다 그 자신에게서 온 몇 줄이, 그의 말투처럼 뜨겁고 단호한 몇 줄이 들어 있었던 것이다. 그것은 패니가 두려워한 것 못지않게 언짢게 여긴 편지 왕래였다. 크로퍼드 양의 그 발랄하고 정겨운 글투 자체가, 그녀가 이렇게 하는 수 없이 그 오라비의 펜에서 읽어야 하는 것과는 별개로 하나의 화였다. 에드먼드가 그녀에게 그 편지의 알맹이를 제게 읽어 주기까지는 좀처럼 가만있지 않았던 까닭이다. 그러면 그녀는 그가 그녀의 말솜씨와 그 애착의 뜨거움을 감탄하는 것을 들어야 했다. 실은, 편지마다 안부와 넌지시 비침과 추억이, 맨스필드에 대한 얘기가 어찌나 많던지, 패니는 그것이 그더러 들으라고 뜻해진 것이려니 여기지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 그런 종류의 목적에 억지로 끌려 들어가, 사랑하지 않는 사내의 구애를 저에게 가져다주고 사랑하는 사내의 어긋난 정을 저더러 시중들게 하는 편지 왕래에 떠밀린 자신을 발견하는 것은 모질게 무안한 노릇이었다. 여기서도 그녀의 이번 떠남은 이로움을 약속했다. 더는 에드먼드와 한 지붕 아래 있지 않게 되면, 크로퍼드 양이 그 수고를 무릅쓸 만큼 강한 편지 쓸 동기가 없어지리라, 그리고 포츠머스에서는 그들 편지 왕래가 아무것도 아닌 것으로 시들어 버리리라 그녀는 믿었다.\n\n이런 생각들을 천 가지 다른 생각들 틈에 품고서, 패니는 안전하고 밝게, 그리고 진창투성이 이월에 이치에 맞게 바랄 수 있는 만큼 빠르게 여정을 나아갔다. 그들은 옥스퍼드에 들어섰으나, 지나는 길에 그녀는 에드먼드의 대학을 그저 서둘러 흘깃 볼 수 있었을 뿐이었다. 그리고 뉴베리에 이르기까지 어디서도 멈추지 않았는데, 거기서 만찬과 저녁을 합친 편안한 한 끼가 그날의 즐거움과 고단함을 마무리 지었다.\n\n이튿날 아침 그들은 다시 이른 시각에 길을 나섰다. 그리고 아무 일도, 아무 지체도 없이 꾸준히 나아가, 아직 패니가 둘레를 둘러보고 새 건물들을 신기해할 만한 대낮 빛이 남아 있는 동안 포츠머스 어귀에 이르렀다. 그들은 도개교를 지나 시내에 들어섰다. 그리고 빛이 겨우 스러지기 시작할 무렵, 윌리엄의 우렁찬 목소리에 이끌려, 그들은 하이 스트리트에서 갈라진 좁은 골목으로 덜컹거리며 들어가, 이제 프라이스 씨가 사는 자그마한 집 문 앞에 멎었다.\n\n패니는 온통 마음 졸이며 설레었다. 온통 바람과 두려움이었다. 그들이 멎는 순간, 문간에서 그들을 기다리던 듯한, 헤벌쭉해 보이는 하녀 하나가 앞으로 나와, 무슨 거들 생각보다 소식 전할 마음이 더 앞선지, 대번에 이렇게 말문을 열었다. “스러시호는 항구를 나갔어요, 나리. 그리고 사관 한 분이 여기 오셔서—” 그녀는 열한 살쯤 된 훤칠하고 잘생긴 사내아이에게 말이 잘렸으니, 그 아이가 집에서 뛰쳐나와 하녀를 밀치고, 윌리엄이 손수 마차 문을 열고 있는 사이에 소리친 것이다. “딱 맞춰 왔네. 우리가 반 시간이나 형을 기다렸어. 스러시호는 오늘 아침에 항구를 나갔어. 내가 봤어. 참 멋진 광경이었어. 그리고 하루 이틀이면 출항 명령이 떨어질 거래. 그리고 캠벨 씨가 네 시에 형을 찾으러 오셨어. 스러시호 보트를 하나 얻으셔서 여섯 시에 배로 나가는데, 형이 제때 와서 같이 가면 좋겠다고 하셨어.”\n\n윌리엄이 그녀를 마차에서 내려 주는 동안, 이 아우가 자진해 보인 아는 체라곤 패니를 한두 번 빤히 쳐다본 것이 다였다. 그러나 그는 그녀가 제게 입 맞추는 것에는 반대하지 않았다. 하기야 여전히 온통, 저에게 강한 관심의 권리가 있는—바로 이때에 그 배에서 뱃사람 노릇을 시작할 참인 그 스러시호의 항구 나섬에 대해 더 낱낱이 늘어놓는 데 팔려 있긴 했지만.\n\n또 한순간이 지나 패니는 집의 그 좁은 현관 통로에 들어, 어머니의 품에 안겼다. 어머니는 참된 다정함의 낯빛으로, 그리고 버트럼 이모의 얼굴을 눈앞에 떠올려 주어 패니가 더욱 사랑한 그 이목구비로 거기서 그녀를 맞았다. 그리고 거기 그녀의 두 누이가 있었다. 잘 자란 훤한 열넷 처녀 수전과, 집안의 막내로 다섯 살쯤 된 벳시였다—둘 다 저희 나름으로 그녀를 반가워했으나, 맞이하는 태도에 이렇다 할 좋은 데는 없었다. 그러나 태도야 패니가 바라는 것이 아니었다. 그들이 그저 저를 사랑해 주기만 한다면 그녀는 흡족할 터였다.\n\n그녀는 이내 어느 응접실로 안내되었는데, 하도 작아 처음엔 이것이 그저 더 나은 어디로 가는 통로 방이려니 확신하고, 한순간 더 안으로 청받기를 기다리며 서 있었다. 그러나 다른 문이 없는 것을, 그리고 눈앞에 사람 사는 흔적이 있는 것을 보고, 그녀는 생각을 거두고 저를 나무라며, 그들이 그것을 눈치챘을까 봐 서러워했다. 그러나 어머니는 무엇을 눈치챌 만큼 오래 머물지 못했다. 그녀는 윌리엄을 맞으러 다시 거리 쪽 문으로 가 버린 것이다. “아, 사랑하는 윌리엄, 널 보니 얼마나 반가운지. 그런데 스러시호 소식 들었니? 벌써 항구를 나갔단다. 우리가 생각도 못 한 사흘이나 앞당겨서 말이야. 그래서 나는 샘 물건들을 어쩌면 좋을지 모르겠구나, 제때 도무지 채비가 안 될 텐데. 어쩌면 내일 출항 명령이 떨어질지도 모르니까. 아주 느닷없이 닥친 일이야. 그리고 이제 너도 스핏헤드로 나가야 하고. 캠벨이 여기 와서 너 때문에 잔뜩 안달하더라. 그래 이제 어쩌면 좋으냐? 나는 너하고 그토록 오붓한 저녁을 보내려 했는데, 온갖 게 한꺼번에 나한테 닥치는구나.”\n\n아들은 밝게 대답하며, 온갖 것은 늘 잘되게 마련이라고, 그리고 저가 이토록 이내 서둘러 떠나야 하는 제 불편을 대수롭잖게 넘겼다.\n\n“물론 저야 그 배가 항구에 머물러서 어머니하고 몇 시간 편히 앉아 있었으면 훨씬 좋았겠죠. 하지만 뭍에 보트가 있으니 당장 나가는 게 낫겠어요, 어쩔 도리가 없죠. 스러시호가 스핏헤드 어디쯤에 있어요? 캐노퍼스호 근처예요? 하지만 상관없어요. 여기 응접실에 패니가 있는데, 우리가 왜 통로에 서 있어야 해요? 자, 어머니, 어머니의 그 소중한 패니를 아직 제대로 보지도 못하셨잖아요.”\n\n둘이 함께 들어왔고, 프라이스 부인은 딸에게 다정히 다시 입 맞추고 그 자란 것을 조금 두고 말하더니, 자못 자연스러운 걱정으로 나그네인 그들의 고단함과 아쉬움을 헤아리기 시작했다.\n\n“딱한 것들! 둘 다 얼마나 지쳤겠니! 자, 이제 뭘 좀 들려? 나는 너희가 영영 안 오려나 했다. 벳시하고 나는 반 시간이나 너희를 기다렸어. 그래 언제 뭘 좀 먹었니? 그리고 이제 뭘 들고 싶어? 여행 뒤에 고기를 좀 들지, 아니면 그냥 차 한 잔이면 될지 알 수가 없어서 미리 뭘 채비 못 했다. 그리고 이제 스테이크를 구울 짬도 나기 전에 캠벨이 올까 봐 걱정이구나, 게다가 근처에 고깃간도 없고. 골목에 고깃간이 없으니 참 불편해. 지난번 집이 그래도 나았지. 아무래도 차가 되는 대로 좀 마시는 게 좋겠구나.”\n\n둘 다 무엇보다도 그게 좋겠다 했다. “그럼, 벳시야, 부엌에 가서 리베카가 물을 올렸는지 보렴. 그리고 되도록 빨리 찻그릇을 들여오라 일러라. 초인종을 고칠 수 있으면 좋겠는데, 그래도 벳시가 아주 야무진 자그마한 심부름꾼이지.”\n\n벳시는 냉큼 갔으니, 훤한 새 언니 앞에서 제 재간을 보이는 것이 우쭐했던 것이다.\n\n“어쩌나!” 걱정 많은 어머니가 말을 이었다. “불이 이토록 시원찮으니, 너희 둘 다 추위에 얼었겠다. 의자를 좀 더 가까이 당기렴, 얘야. 리베카가 대체 뭘 하고 있었는지 모르겠다. 분명 반 시간 전에 석탄을 좀 가져오라 일렀는데. 수전, 네가 불을 좀 봤어야지.”\n\n“저는 위층에서 제 물건을 옮기고 있었어요, 엄마.” 수전이, 패니를 흠칫하게 하는 겁 없이 저를 두둔하는 투로 말했다. “패니 언니하고 제가 저쪽 방을 쓰기로 방금 막 정하셨잖아요. 그리고 저는 리베카한테 도무지 거들어 달랄 수가 없었고요.”\n\n더 실랑이할 새도 없이 여러 소동이 벌어졌다. 먼저 마부가 삯을 받으러 왔고, 그다음 누이의 여행 가방을 어떻게 위층으로 나를지를 두고 샘과 리베카 사이에 옥신각신이 있었는데, 샘은 죄다 제 뜻대로 하려 들었다. 그리고 끝으로, 프라이스 씨 자신이 걸어 들어왔으니, 그 우렁찬 목소리가 그보다 앞섰다. 무슨 욕지거리 비슷한 걸 내뱉으며 통로에서 아들의 여행 가방과 딸의 모자 상자를 걷어차고 초를 가져오라 소리친 것이다. 그러나 초는 안 나왔고, 그는 방으로 걸어 들어왔다.\n\n패니는 미심쩍은 심경으로 일어나 그를 맞으러 갔으나, 어둑함 속에 저가 눈에도 안 띄고 생각조차 안 됨을 알고 다시 주저앉았다. 아들과 정겹게 악수를 나누고, 열띤 목소리로, 그는 대번에 말문을 열었다. “허! 잘 돌아왔다, 얘야. 반갑구나. 소식 들었니? 스러시호가 오늘 아침 항구를 나갔어. 서둘러야 한다, 알겠니! 이런, 제기랄, 딱 맞춰 왔구나! 군의관이 여기 와서 널 찾더라. 보트를 하나 얻어서 여섯 시엔 스핏헤드로 나간다니, 너도 그이랑 같이 가는 게 낫겠다. 네 식사비 때문에 터너네도 다녀왔다, 그럭저럭 될 판이야. 내일 출항 명령이 떨어져도 놀랄 것 없겠다. 하지만 서쪽으로 순항할 거라면 이 바람으론 못 뜬다. 그리고 월시 함장은 네가 엘리펀트호랑 서쪽 순항을 분명 하게 되리라 여기더라. 이런, 제기랄, 그리됐으면 좋겠다! 하지만 늙은 숄리는 방금, 네가 먼저 텍설로 보내지지 싶다더라. 뭐, 됐어, 무슨 일이 있든 우리야 채비가 됐으니. 하지만 이런 제기랄, 넌 아침에 여기 없어서 스러시호가 항구 나서는 그 멋진 광경을 놓쳤구나! 천 파운드를 준대도 나는 그 자리를 안 비웠을 게다. 늙은 숄리가 아침 먹을 참에 뛰어들어와 그 배가 닻줄을 풀고 나오고 있다길래, 나는 벌떡 일어나 두 걸음 만에 관측대로 갔지. 물 위에 완벽한 미인이 떠 있었던 적이 있다면, 그 배가 바로 그거다. 그리고 그게 스핏헤드에 누워 있는데, 잉글랜드 사람이라면 누구든 28문함으로 볼 게다. 나는 오늘 오후 두 시간이나 관측대에서 그 배를 봤다. 그건 엔디미언호 바로 곁, 그 배랑 클레오파트라호 사이, 폐선 기중선 바로 동쪽에 누워 있어.”\n\n“허!” 윌리엄이 외쳤다. “바로 거기가 저라도 그 배를 뒀을 데예요. 스핏헤드에서 제일 좋은 정박지죠. 그런데 여기 제 누이가 있어요, 아버지. 여기 패니예요.” 하며 돌아서서 그녀를 앞으로 이끌었다. “너무 어두워서 안 보이시는군요.”\n\n그녀를 아주 잊고 있었다는 걸 인정하며, 프라이스 씨는 이제 딸을 맞았다. 그리고 다정히 한번 껴안고, 다 큰 여자가 됐구나, 아마 머잖아 남편이 필요하겠구나 하더니, 이내 다시 그녀를 잊으려 자못 마음이 기우는 듯했다. 패니는 그의 말투와 술 냄새에 서글피 아파진 심경으로 제자리로 움츠러들었다. 그리고 그는 오직 아들에게만, 오직 스러시호에 대해서만 얘기를 이어 갔다. 하기야 윌리엄이, 저 역시 그 화제에 뜨겁게 끌렸으면서도, 한 번 넘게 아버지가 패니를, 그 오랜 떠나 있음과 먼 여정을 생각하게 하려 애쓰긴 했지만.\n\n한동안 더 앉아 있으니 초가 하나 나왔다. 그러나 여전히 차는 나올 낌새가 없고, 벳시가 부엌에서 전하는 바로도 상당한 시간 안에는 차가 나올 가망이 별로 없던지라, 윌리엄은 가서 옷을 갈아입고 배로 옮겨 갈 채비를 곧바로 해 두어, 그 뒤에 차를 편히 마시기로 마음먹었다.\n\n그가 방을 나서자, 여덟아홉 살쯤 된, 누더기에 지저분한 두 볼그레한 사내아이가 방금 학교에서 풀려나 방으로 뛰어들었다. 누이를 얼른 보고 스러시호가 항구를 나갔다는 걸 전하러 온 것이었으니, 톰과 찰스였다. 찰스는 패니가 떠난 뒤에 태어났으나, 톰은 그녀가 곧잘 어르며 돌봐 주었던지라, 이제 다시 보는 것이 자못 각별히 반가웠다. 둘 다 무척 애틋하게 입맞춤을 받았으나, 톰은 저가 사랑하고 말도 걸어 주던 그 아기의 이목구비를 더듬어 보려, 그 아기가 어릴 적 저를 따르던 것을 얘기하려 곁에 붙들어 두고 싶었다. 그러나 톰은 그런 대접에 마음이 없었다. 그는 서서 말 들으려 온 것이 아니라 뛰어다니며 소란 피우러 온 것이었다. 그리하여 두 아이는 이내 그녀에게서 왈칵 벗어나, 그녀의 관자놀이가 지끈거리도록 응접실 문을 쾅 닫아 댔다.\n\n이제 그녀는 집에 있는 이는 다 본 셈이었다. 저와 수전 사이엔 오라비 둘만 남았는데, 하나는 런던의 관청 서기였고, 다른 하나는 동인도 무역선의 사관후보생이었다. 그러나 그녀가 식구를 다 본 것이긴 해도, 그들이 낼 수 있는 온갖 소란을 아직 다 들은 것은 아니었다. 또 한 십오 분이 그녀에게 훨씬 더 많은 것을 안겨 주었다. 윌리엄이 이내 이 층 층계참에서 어머니와 리베카를 불러 대기 시작했다. 그가 거기 두고 온 무언가를 못 찾아 애를 태우고 있었던 것이다. 열쇠가 어디 놓였는지 모르게 됐고, 벳시가 그의 새 모자를 만졌다는 혐의를 받았으며, 해 주기로 약속받았던, 사소하나 요긴한 제복 조끼 손질이 아주 소홀히 되어 있었다.\n\n프라이스 부인과 리베카와 벳시가 다들 저를 두둔하러 위층으로 올라가 죄다 한꺼번에 떠들었는데, 리베카가 가장 큰 소리였다. 그리고 그 일은 그 큰 서두름 속에 되도록 해내야 했다. 윌리엄은 헛되이 벳시를 다시 아래로 보내거나, 있는 데서 성가시게 굴지 않게 하려 애썼다. 이 온갖 것이, 집 안 거의 모든 문이 열려 있던지라, 응접실에서 또렷이 분간되었다—샘과 톰과 찰스가 계단을 오르내리며 서로 쫓고, 뒹굴고, 고함치는 그 더 큰 소란에 이따금 묻힐 때만 빼면.\n\n패니는 거의 얼이 빠질 지경이었다. 집의 작음과 벽의 얇음이 온갖 것을 그토록 그녀에게 바짝 들이밀어, 여정의 고단함과 그 온갖 근래의 마음 졸임에 더해, 그녀는 도무지 그것을 어찌 견딜지 몰랐다. 방 안은 웬만큼 고요했으니, 수전이 다른 이들과 함께 사라져, 이내 아버지와 그녀만 남았기 때문이다. 그리고 그는, 이웃에게서 늘 빌려 오는 신문을 꺼내, 그녀의 존재는 떠올리는 기색도 없이 그것을 읽는 데 몰두했다. 그 외로운 초 하나가 그와 신문 사이에, 그녀의 있을 법한 편의는 조금도 헤아리지 않은 채 놓여 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 할 일이 없었고, 얼떨떨하고 부서지고 서글픈 상념에 잠겨 앉은 채, 지끈거리는 머리에서 그 불빛이 가려진 것이 오히려 다행스러웠다.\n\n그녀는 집에 있었다. 그러나, 아아! 그것은 그런 집이 아니었고, 그녀는 그런 환대를 받은 게 아니었다—그녀는 저를 다잡았다. 저가 무리한 것이다. 저가 제 식구에게 중한 사람일 무슨 권리가 있단 말인가? 그토록 오래 잊혀 있었으니, 아무 권리도 없을밖에! 윌리엄의 일이 가장 소중한 게 마땅했다, 늘 그래 왔고, 그에게는 온갖 권리가 있었다. 그러나 저 자신에 대해서는 그토록 묻고 말하는 것이 적다니, 맨스필드에 대해서는 물음 하나 좀처럼 없다니! 맨스필드가 잊히는 것이 그녀는 정말 아팠다. 그토록 많은 것을 해 준 그 벗들이—사랑하는, 사랑하는 벗들이! 그러나 여기서는 한 가지 화제가 나머지를 죄다 삼켜 버렸다. 어쩌면 그럴밖에 없으리라. 스러시호가 어디로 갈지가 이제 무엇보다 흥미로울밖에 없었다. 하루 이틀이면 그 다름이 드러날지도 몰랐다. 저 혼자 탓할 일이었다. 그러나 그녀는 맨스필드에서라면 이렇지 않았으리라 여겼다. 아니, 삼촌 댁에서라면 때와 철에 대한 헤아림이, 화제의 다스림이, 온당함이, 여기엔 없는 뭇사람에 대한 마음씀이 있었으리라.\n\n이런 생각들이 거의 반 시간 동안 받은 유일한 끼어듦은, 그것들을 가라앉히기엔 도무지 알맞잖은, 아버지의 문득 터진 외침에서였다. 통로에서 여느 때보다 더한 쿵쾅거림과 고함이 일자, 그가 소리친 것이다. “저 강아지 같은 것들을 악마가 잡아가라지! 어찌 저리 짖어 대는지! 아무렴, 샘 목소리가 나머지보다 다 크구나! 저 녀석은 갑판장 감이야. 이봐, 거기! 샘, 그 망할 놈의 피리 소릴 멈춰라, 안 그러면 내가 쫓아간다.”\n\n이 으름장이 어찌나 뻔히 무시되었던지, 오 분도 안 되어 세 아이가 다 함께 방으로 왈칵 들어와 앉기는 했으나, 패니는 그것을 그들이 한동안 아주 지쳤다는 것 이상의 무슨 증거로도 여길 수 없었다. 그들의 달아오른 얼굴과 헐떡이는 숨이 그것을 증명하는 듯했으니, 더구나 그들은 여전히 아버지 눈앞에서 서로의 정강이를 걷어차고 문득문득 고함을 질러 댔던 것이다.\n\n다음번 문이 열릴 땐 좀 더 반가운 것이 들어왔다. 찻그릇이었으니, 그녀는 그날 저녁 그것을 볼 것을 거의 단념하기 시작한 참이었다. 수전과, 그 못한 행색으로 미루어 패니가 크게 놀라며 앞서 본 것이 위층 하녀였음을 알게 해 준 시중드는 계집아이가, 그 끼니에 필요한 온갖 것을 들여왔다. 수전은 주전자를 불에 올리고 누이를 슬쩍 보며, 제 부지런함과 쓸모를 보이는 흐뭇한 우쭐함과, 그런 일로 저를 낮춘다 여겨질까 하는 두려움 사이에서 갈리는 듯한 낯빛이었다. “부엌에 다녀왔어요,” 그녀가 말했다. “샐리를 재촉하고 토스트 만드는 걸 거들고 버터빵을 발라 놓으려고요. 안 그럼 언제 차가 될지 몰라서요. 그리고 언니가 여행 뒤에 뭐라도 들고 싶을 게 분명하니까요.”\n\n패니는 무척 고마웠다. 그녀는 차를 좀 마시면 무척 반갑겠다는 것을 인정할밖에 없었고, 수전은 마치 그 일거리를 온통 저 혼자 맡은 것이 기쁜 양 대번에 차 끓이는 데 나섰다. 그리고 얼마간의 쓸데없는 부산과, 제가 할 수 있는 것보다 오라비들을 더 잘 잡도리하려는 몇 번의 서투른 시도만 빼면, 자못 잘 해냈다. 패니의 기운은 몸만큼이나 되살아났다. 그녀의 머리와 마음은 그런 때맞은 친절 덕에 이내 나아졌다. 수전은 툭 트인, 지각 있는 낯빛이었다. 그녀는 윌리엄을 닮았고, 패니는 성미와 저에 대한 호의에서도 그녀가 그를 닮았기를 바랐다.\n\n이렇게 좀 더 잔잔해진 형편에 윌리엄이 다시 들어왔고, 그 뒤 멀지 않게 어머니와 벳시가 뒤따랐다. 그는 중위 제복을 온전히 갖춰 입어, 그 덕에 더 훤칠하고 더 다부지고 더 우아해 보이고 움직이며, 더없이 행복한 미소를 낯에 띤 채 곧장 패니에게로 걸어왔다. 그녀는 자리에서 일어나 한순간 말없는 감탄으로 그를 바라보더니, 그의 목에 팔을 두르고 아픔과 기쁨의 갖가지 심경을 흐느껴 쏟아 냈다.\n\n불행해 보이지 않으려 애쓰며, 그녀는 이내 저를 추슬렀다. 그리고 눈물을 닦고서, 그의 차림에서 눈에 띄는 온갖 대목을 눈여겨보고 감탄할 수 있었다. 출항 전 날마다 얼마간 뭍에 있으리라는, 심지어 그녀를 스핏헤드로 데려가 그 슬루프함을 보여 주리라는 그의 밝은 바람을, 되살아나는 기운으로 들으면서.\n\n다음 소동은 스러시호의 군의관 캠벨 씨를 들여보냈다. 자못 반듯한 젊은이로, 제 벗을 부르러 온 것이었다. 그를 위해 이리저리 궁리해 의자 하나가 마련되었고, 젊은 차 끓이는 이가 서둘러 씻은 잔과 받침이 마련되었다. 그리고 신사들 사이에 또 한 십오 분의 열띤 얘기가 오간 뒤—소란 위에 소란이, 부산 위에 부산이 일고, 사내들과 아이들이 마침내 다 함께 움직이는 가운데—떠날 순간이 왔다. 온갖 것이 채비되었고, 윌리엄이 작별을 고했으며, 그들 모두가 떠났다. 세 아이가 어머니의 간청에도 아랑곳없이 오라비와 캠벨 씨를 성문까지 배웅하기로 굳게 마음먹었고, 프라이스 씨도 그때 함께 걸어 나가 이웃의 신문을 돌려주었던 것이다.\n\n이제 고요 비슷한 것을 바랄 만도 했다. 그리하여 리베카가 겨우 설득되어 찻그릇을 치우고, 프라이스 부인이 한동안 방을 이리저리 거닐며 셔츠 소매 한 짝을 찾았으나 벳시가 마침내 부엌 서랍에서 찾아내니, 그 자그마한 여자들의 무리는 웬만큼 가라앉았다. 그리고 어머니는 샘을 제때 채비시키기가 도무지 어렵다는 것을 다시 한번 한탄하고 나서야, 맏딸과 그녀가 떠나온 벗들을 생각할 여유가 생겼다.\n\n몇 가지 물음이 시작되었다. 그러나 가장 이른 것 가운데 하나—“버트럼 언니는 하인들을 어떻게 건사하니?” “쓸 만한 하인 구하기가 나만큼이나 애먹니?”—가 이내 그 마음을 노샘프턴셔에서 데려가, 제 살림의 시름에, 포츠머스 하인이란 하인은 죄다 고약하다는 데 붙박아 놓았다. 그리고 그중에서도 제 두 하인이 딱 최악이려니 여기는 그 마음이 그녀를 온통 사로잡았다. 리베카의 흠을 낱낱이 늘어놓는 데 버트럼가는 죄다 잊혔으니, 리베카에 대해서는 수전도 고할 것이 많고 어린 벳시는 훨씬 더 많았으며, 리베카는 정말이지 그토록 온전히 천거할 점 하나 없어 보이던지라, 패니는 어머니가 그 한 해가 차면 그녀를 내보낼 셈이려니 조심스레 넘겨짚지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n“한 해라니!” 프라이스 부인이 외쳤다. “정말이지 나는 그게 한 해가 차기 전에 그 애를 떼어 내면 좋겠다, 그 한 해는 십일월이나 돼야 차거든. 얘야, 포츠머스에선 하인이란 게 이 지경에 이르러서, 반년 넘게 데리고 있으면 그게 아주 기적이란다. 나는 도무지 자리 잡을 가망이 없어. 그리고 리베카를 내보낸들 더 못한 것이나 얻을 뿐이지. 그래도 나는 내가 그리 비위 맞추기 어려운 안주인이라곤 여기지 않아. 그리고 이 자리야 분명 넉넉히 수월해, 늘 그 애 밑에 계집아이가 하나 있고, 나도 곧잘 일의 절반을 손수 하니까.”\n\n패니는 잠잠했다. 그러나 이 화들 가운데 얼마간에는 고칠 길이 있을 수도 있음을 믿지 못해서가 아니었다. 이제 벳시를 바라보고 앉은 그녀는, 또 다른 누이를, 저가 노샘프턴셔로 갈 때 그리 어리지 않은 나이로 거기 두고 온, 자못 예쁜 어린 계집아이를, 몇 해 뒤에 죽은 그 아이를 각별히 생각하지 않을 수 없었다. 그 아이에게는 유난히 사랑스러운 데가 있었다. 그 어릴 적 패니는 그 아이를 수전보다 더 좋아했다. 그리고 그 아이의 죽음 소식이 마침내 맨스필드에 이르렀을 때, 한동안 자못 애통해했었다. 벳시를 보니 어린 메리의 모습이 다시 떠올랐으나, 무엇을 준대도 그녀는 그 아이를 넌지시 입에 올려 어머니를 아프게 하지 않으려 했다. 이런 생각으로 그 아이를 헤아리는 동안, 벳시가 조금 떨어진 데서, 수전에게서는 가리려 하면서 무언가를 그녀의 눈에 띄게 내밀고 있었다.\n\n“거기 뭘 들고 있니, 얘야?” 패니가 말했다. “이리 와서 나한테 보여 주렴.”\n\n그것은 은칼이었다. 수전이 벌떡 일어나 제 것이라 우기며 빼앗으려 했으나, 아이는 어머니의 보호막으로 달아났고, 수전은 그저 나무랄밖에 없었는데, 자못 발끈하며 그리했다. 그러면서 분명 패니가 제 편이 되어 주기를 바라는 눈치였다. “제 칼을 못 갖다니 너무해요. 그건 제 칼이에요. 어린 메리 언니가 죽으면서 저한테 남긴 건데, 진작 제가 간직해야 마땅했다고요. 그런데 엄마가 그걸 저한테서 두고, 늘 벳시가 손대게 놔둬요. 그러다 끝내는 벳시가 그걸 망가뜨리고 제 것으로 삼을 거예요. 엄마가 벳시 손엔 안 쥐여 주겠다고 약속해 놓고선.”\n\n패니는 자못 충격받았다. 누이의 말과 어머니의 대답에, 도리와 명예와 애틋함의 온갖 심경이 상했다.\n\n“아니, 수전,” 프라이스 부인이 투정 부리는 목소리로 외쳤다. “아니, 어쩜 그리 심술을 부리니? 넌 늘 그 칼을 두고 다투는구나. 제발 그리 다투려 들지 마라. 딱한 어린 벳시, 수전이 너한테 어찌 저리 심술을 부리는지! 하지만 너도 그걸 꺼내지 말았어야지, 얘야, 내가 서랍에 심부름을 보냈을 때 말이야. 손대지 말라 일렀잖니, 수전이 그걸 두고 하도 심술을 부리니까. 다음번엔 그걸 숨겨 둬야겠다, 벳시. 딱한 메리는 그걸 나한테 간직하라 주면서, 죽기 겨우 두 시간 전에 주면서, 그게 이런 다툼거리가 될 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐을 게다. 딱한 것! 그 애는 겨우 들릴 만큼밖에 말을 못 하면서도, 어찌나 곱게 말했는지, ‘엄마, 내가 죽어 묻히면 수전 언니 칼 주세요’ 했단다. 딱하고 사랑스러운 것! 그 애가 그걸 어찌나 아꼈는지, 패니, 앓는 내내 잠자리 곁에 두려 했단다. 그건 그 애의 착한 대모, 늙은 맥스웰 제독 부인이 준 선물이야, 그 애가 죽음에 붙들리기 겨우 여섯 주 전에 말이다. 딱하고 고운 어린것! 뭐, 그 애는 앞으로 올 화를 피해 데려가진 게지. 우리 벳시야” (아이를 어루만지며), “너는 그런 좋은 대모의 복이 없구나. 노리스 이모야 너 같은 조그만 것들을 생각하기엔 너무 멀리 사시니.”\n\n패니가 정말이지 노리스 이모에게서 전할 것이라곤, 제 대녀가 착한 아이여서 책을 잘 배우기를 바란다는 안부의 말뿐이었다. 한순간 맨스필드 파크 응접실에서 그 애에게 기도서를 보내자는 얘기가 잠깐 나온 적은 있었으나, 그런 뜻이 두 번 다시 들리진 않았다. 그래도 노리스 부인은 그 생각으로 집에 가서 남편의 낡은 기도서 두 권을 내렸다. 그러나 살펴본 끝에 그 너그러움의 열의가 식었다. 하나는 아이 눈엔 활자가 너무 작았고, 다른 하나는 아이가 들고 다니기엔 너무 거추장스러웠던 것이다.\n\n패니는 지치고 또 지친지라, 잠자리에 들라는 첫 청을 고맙게 받아들였다. 그리고 언니를 기리는 뜻에서 딱 한 시간 더 앉아 있게 허락받은 것을 두고 벳시가 우는 것이 채 끝나기도 전에, 그녀는 자리를 떴다. 아래층을 다시 어수선함과 소란에 남겨 둔 채로였으니, 아이들은 구운 치즈를 달라 조르고, 아버지는 럼주에 물 탄 것을 내오라 소리치고, 리베카는 있어야 할 자리에 통 없었다.\n\n수전과 함께 쓰기로 된 그 옹색하고 세간 변변찮은 방에는 그녀의 기운을 돋울 것이 아무것도 없었다. 정말이지 위아래 방들의 작음과, 통로와 계단의 좁음이 그녀의 상상을 넘어 사무쳤다. 그녀는 이내, 맨스필드 파크의 제 자그마한 다락방을—그 집에선 누구의 편안함에도 너무 작다 여겨지던 그 방을 존경심으로 떠올리게 되었다.\n\n# 제39장\n\n토머스 경이, 조카딸이 이모에게 첫 편지를 쓸 때의 그 온갖 심경을 다 볼 수 있었더라면, 그는 절망하지 않았으리라. 밤새 잘 쉰 것과, 유쾌한 아침과, 이내 다시 윌리엄을 볼 바람과, 톰과 찰스가 학교에 가고 샘이 제 무슨 일로 나가고 아버지가 여느 어슬렁거림에 나선 덕에 집이 견주어 조용해진 것이, 그녀로 하여금 집에 대해 밝게 저를 드러낼 수 있게 했으되, 그녀 자신은 훤히 아는 대로 여전히 눌러 둔 언짢은 대목이 많았던 것이다. 그가 한 주가 채 가기 전에 그녀가 느낀 것의 절반만이라도 볼 수 있었더라면, 그는 크로퍼드 씨가 그녀를 확실히 얻으리라 여기고, 제 슬기로움에 흐뭇해했으리라.\n\n한 주가 가기도 전에 그것은 죄다 실망이었다. 우선, 윌리엄이 떠났다. 스러시호에 출항 명령이 떨어지고 바람이 바뀌어, 그는 그들이 포츠머스에 이른 지 나흘 만에 배를 타고 떠났다. 그리고 그 며칠 동안 그녀는 그가 근무차 뭍에 왔을 때 짧고 서두르는 식으로 딱 두 번밖에 그를 못 봤다. 스스럼없는 얘기도, 성벽 위 산책도, 조선소 구경도, 스러시호와 낯익어지는 것도, 그들이 짜고 기대던 그 온갖 것이 하나도 없었다. 윌리엄의 정 말고는 그 방면의 온갖 것이 그녀를 저버렸다. 집을 떠날 제 그의 마지막 생각은 그녀를 위한 것이었다. 그는 문간으로 다시 되걸어 와 말했다. “패니를 잘 돌봐 주세요, 어머니. 그 애는 여려서, 우리 나머지처럼 거칠게 부대끼는 데 익지 않았어요. 제가 당부드려요, 패니를 잘 돌봐 주세요.”\n\n윌리엄은 떠났다. 그리고 그가 그녀를 남겨 두고 간 그 집은, 패니가 저 자신에게 감출 수 없었듯, 거의 어느 모로 보나 저가 바랐을 것과 정반대였다. 그것은 소란과 무질서와 온당찮음의 거처였다. 아무도 제자리에 있지 않았고, 아무것도 마땅한 대로 되지 않았다. 그녀는 바라던 만큼 제 부모를 존경할 수가 없었다. 아버지에 대해서는 그녀의 믿음이 그리 크지 않았으나, 그는 그녀가 각오한 것보다 제 식구에게 더 무심하고, 버릇이 더 나쁘고, 태도가 더 거칠었다. 그에게 재간이 없는 것은 아니었으나, 호기심도, 제 직업 너머의 앎도 없었다. 그는 신문과 해군 명부만 읽었고, 조선소와 항구와 스핏헤드와 마더뱅크 얘기만 했다. 그는 욕을 했고 술을 마셨으며, 지저분하고 거칠었다. 그녀는 예전에 저를 대하던 그의 태도에서 애틋함 비슷한 것이라곤 좀처럼 떠올릴 수 없었다. 남은 것이라곤 거칢과 시끄러움의 두루뭉술한 인상뿐이었다. 그리고 이제 그는 좀처럼 그녀를 아는 체하지 않았으니, 거친 농의 과녁으로 삼을 때 말고는.\n\n어머니에 대한 실망은 더 컸다. 거기에 그녀는 많이 바랐으나, 거의 아무것도 못 찾았다. 어머니에게 중한 사람이 되려던 온갖 우쭐한 궁리가 이내 무너졌다. 프라이스 부인은 야박하진 않았다. 그러나 딸은, 그 애정과 믿음을 얻어 점점 더 소중한 사람이 되기는커녕, 도착한 첫날보다 더 큰 친절을 그녀에게서 받아 본 적이 없었다. 천성의 본능은 이내 채워졌고, 프라이스 부인의 정에는 다른 샘이 없었다. 그녀의 마음도 시간도 이미 아주 가득 차 있었다. 그녀에게는 패니에게 베풀 여유도 정도 없었다. 딸들은 그녀에게 여태 큰 존재가 아니었다. 그녀는 아들들을, 특히 윌리엄을 아꼈으나, 벳시야말로 그녀가 여태 크게 마음 쓴 첫 딸이었다. 그 아이에게 그녀는 더없이 분별없이 무르게 굴었다. 윌리엄은 그녀의 자랑이요, 벳시는 그녀의 귀염둥이였다. 그리고 존과 리처드와 샘과 톰과 찰스가 그 나머지 온갖 어미로서의 마음씀을 차지해, 번갈아 그녀의 시름이자 위안이 되었다. 이들이 그녀의 마음을 나눠 가졌다. 그녀의 시간은 주로 살림과 하인들에게 주어졌다. 그녀의 나날은 일종의 굼뜬 부산함 속에 흘렀다. 온통 바쁘되 나아가는 것은 없고, 늘 뒤처지고 그것을 한탄하되 제 방식은 안 바꾸고, 알뜰해지고 싶어 하되 궁리도 규모도 없고, 하인들에게 못마땅해하되 그들을 낫게 할 재간은 없고, 거들든 나무라든 무르게 굴든 그들의 존경을 얻어 낼 힘이라곤 없었다.\n\n두 언니 가운데 프라이스 부인은 노리스 부인보다 버트럼 부인을 훨씬 더 닮았다. 그녀는 어쩔 수 없이 살림꾼이었을 뿐, 노리스 부인의 그런 성향도, 그런 부지런함도 조금도 없었다. 그녀의 성미는 천성으로 느긋하고 게을렀으니, 버트럼 부인처럼 말이다. 그리고 비슷하게 넉넉하고 아무것도 안 해도 되는 처지가, 그녀의 경솔한 혼인이 그녀를 밀어 넣은 그 처지의 애씀과 자기 부정보다 그녀의 깜냥에 훨씬 더 잘 맞았으리라. 그녀도 버트럼 부인만큼이나 훌륭한 지체 높은 여자가 되었을 수 있고, 노리스 부인이라면 적은 수입으로 아홉 아이의 더 존경할 만한 어머니가 되었으리라.\n\n이 모두의 많은 것을 패니는 못 느낄 수 없었다. 그녀는 그 낱말을 쓰기를 거리꼈을지 모르나, 어머니가 치우치고 판단 그른 어버이임을, 자식을 가르치지도 잡도리하지도 않는 굼뜨고 지저분한 사람임을, 그 집이 처음부터 끝까지 잘못 건사됨과 불편함의 무대임을, 그리고 저를 향해 재간도 말솜씨도 정도 없는 사람임을—저를 더 알고픈 호기심도, 저의 우정을 바라는 마음도, 그런 심경을 덜어 줄 만한 벗함에 대한 기울음도 없는 사람임을 느낄밖에 없었고 또 느꼈다.\n\n패니는 몹시도 쓸모가 되고 싶었고, 제 집을 얕보는 티를 내거나 이국의 교육 탓에 그 편안함에 도움을 보태기에 어울리지 않거나 내키지 않는 티를 어떤 식으로든 안 내고 싶었다. 그리하여 그녀는 대번에 샘을 위해 바느질에 나섰고, 이르나 늦으나 물러섬 없이 바지런히 바느질해 하도 많이 해낸지라, 그 아이는 끝내 제 속옷 절반 넘게 채비된 채로 배에 실려 갔다. 그녀는 제 쓸모를 느끼는 것이 크게 기뻤으나, 저 없이 그들이 어찌 꾸려 갔을지 상상할 수 없었다.\n\n샘은 시끄럽고 제멋대로였으되, 떠날 때 그녀는 오히려 그를 아쉬워했다. 그가 영리하고 슬기롭고, 시내의 어떤 심부름이든 기꺼이 맡았기 때문이다. 그리고 그는, 그 자체로는 자못 이치에 맞으나 때 안 맞고 힘없는 열의로 건네지는 수전의 나무람은 뿌리치면서도, 패니의 시중과 상냥한 구슬림에는 영향받기 시작하던 참이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 세 어린 것 가운데 가장 나은 이가 그와 함께 가 버렸음을 알았다. 톰과 찰스는, 벗을 사귀고 덜 얄미워지려 애쓰는 것이 이롭겠다는 생각이 들 만한 그 감정과 이성의 나이에서, 적어도 그들이 그보다 어린 햇수만큼 멀었던 것이다. 누이는 이내 그들에게는 조금의 감화도 줄 수 없으리라 단념했다. 그들은 그녀가 시도할 기운이나 겨를이 있는 어떤 방식으로도 도무지 길들일 수 없었다. 오후마다 온 집 안에 그들의 난장 놀이가 되돌아왔다. 그리고 그녀는 아주 일찍부터 토요일의 그 어김없는 반공일이 다가오는 데 한숨짓기를 배웠다.\n\n벳시 또한, 버릇없이 자라, 글자를 제 가장 큰 원수로 여기도록 길들여지고, 제 좋을 대로 하인들과 어울리게 내버려졌다가 그다음 그들의 무슨 나쁜 짓이든 일러바치라 부추김받은 그 아이라, 그녀는 사랑하거나 도와줄 수 있을까 하는 데서 거의 그만큼 단념할 지경이었다. 그리고 수전의 성미에 대해서는 미심쩍은 게 많았다. 어머니와의 끊임없는 다툼, 톰과 찰스와의 불끈하는 옥신각신, 벳시에 대한 심술이 적어도 패니에게는 어찌나 괴롭던지, 그것들이 결코 부아를 돋우는 일 없이 그런 것은 아님을 인정하면서도, 그녀는 그것들을 그토록 극단으로 밀어붙일 수 있는 그 성미가 상냥함과는, 그리고 저 자신에게 무슨 안식을 줌과는 거리가 멀지 않을까 두려웠다.\n\n이것이 바로, 맨스필드를 그녀 머리에서 몰아내고 사촌 에드먼드를 누그러진 심경으로 생각하도록 가르치기로 되어 있던 그 집이었다. 오히려 그녀는 맨스필드 말고는, 그 사랑하는 식구들 말고는, 그 행복한 방식들 말고는 아무것도 생각할 수 없었다. 그녀가 지금 있는 데의 온갖 것이 그것과 온전한 대조를 이뤘다. 맨스필드의 그 우아함, 온당함, 정연함, 어우러짐, 그리고 어쩌면 무엇보다도 그 평온과 고요가, 여기서는 그와 정반대인 온갖 것이 판을 치는 탓에, 하루의 시각마다 그녀의 기억에 되불려 왔다.\n\n끊임없는 소란 속에 사는 것은, 패니처럼 여리고 예민한 몸과 성미에는, 아무리 우아함이나 어우러짐을 더한들 온전히 갚아 줄 수 없는 화였다. 그것이 무엇보다 큰 비참함이었다. 맨스필드에서는 다툼의 소리도, 높아진 목소리도, 문득 터지는 소리도, 우악스러운 발소리도 결코 들리지 않았다. 온갖 것이 밝은 정연함의 규칙적인 흐름으로 나아갔다. 다들 저마다의 마땅한 중함을 지녔고, 다들의 심경이 헤아려졌다. 애틋함이 모자란다 여겨질 만한 데가 언제고 있다면, 좋은 분별과 좋은 교양이 그 자리를 채웠다. 그리고 노리스 이모가 이따금 일으키는 그 자잘한 짜증으로 말하면, 그것들은 짧았고, 사소했으며, 지금 거처의 그 그침 없는 소란에 견주면 바다에 물 한 방울이었다. 여기서는 다들 시끄러웠고, 목소리마다 컸다(어쩌면 어머니의 것만 빼고. 그것은 버트럼 부인의 부드러운 단조로움을 닮았으되, 다만 짜증으로 닳아 있었다). 무엇이 필요하든 고함쳐 불렀고, 하인들은 부엌에서 제 변명을 고함쳐 댔다. 문은 끊임없이 쾅쾅거렸고, 계단은 결코 가만있지 않았으며, 무엇 하나 달그락거림 없이 되지 않았고, 아무도 가만 앉아 있지 않았으며, 아무도 말할 때 남의 귀를 붙들 수 없었다.\n\n한 주가 채 가기 전에 그녀에게 비친 대로 그 두 집을 견주어 보며, 패니는 혼인과 독신에 대한 존슨 박사의 그 이름난 판단을 거기에 갖다 대어, 맨스필드 파크에는 얼마간 고통이 있을지언정 포츠머스에는 아무 즐거움도 없다고 말하고픈 마음이 들었다.\n\n# 제40장\n\n이제 크로퍼드 양에게서, 그들 편지 왕래가 시작될 때의 그 빠른 속도로 소식이 오리라 기대하지 않은 것은 패니가 옳았다. 메리의 다음 편지는 지난번보다 확실히 더 긴 사이를 두고 왔다. 그러나 그런 사이가 저에게 큰 안도로 느껴지리라 여긴 것은 옳지 않았다. 여기 또 한 번 마음의 야릇한 뒤집힘이 있었으니! 그녀는 그 편지가 정작 오니 정말로 반가웠던 것이다. 좋은 사교에서 이렇게 쫓겨나, 저를 흥미롭게 하곤 하던 온갖 것에서 이토록 멀리 떨어진 지금, 제 마음이 살고 있는 그 무리에 속한 이가 애정과 얼마간의 우아함을 담아 쓴 편지는 더없이 반가웠다. 더 일찍 못 쓴 것에 대한 변명으로 볼일이 많아진다는 그 흔한 핑계가 대어졌다. “그리고 이제 시작하고 보니,” 그녀는 말을 이었다. “내 편지는 그쪽이 읽을 값어치가 없겠어요. 끝에 자그마한 사랑의 예물이, 세상에 둘도 없이 헌신적인 H. C.에게서 온 열정 어린(passionnées) 서너 줄이 없을 테니까요. 헨리가 노퍽에 있거든요. 열흘 전에 볼일로 에버링엄에 불려 갔어요, 아니면 그저 볼일이 있는 척했는지도 모르죠, 그쪽이 여행할 그 무렵에 저도 여행하려고 말이에요. 아무튼 그이는 거기 있고, 그건 그렇고, 그이가 없으니 그 누이의 편지 게으름도 넉넉히 설명이 되겠죠. 나를 재촉할 ‘그래, 메리, 언제 패니한테 편지 쓸 거야? 이젠 패니한테 편지할 때 아니야?’ 하는 소리가 없었으니까요. 마침내, 몇 번의 만남 시도 끝에, 나는 그쪽 사촌들, ‘소중한 줄리아와 더없이 소중한 러시워스 부인’을 봤어요. 그들이 어제 집으로 나를 찾아왔고, 우리는 다시 만나 반가웠죠. 우리는 서로 만나 아주 반가운 듯했고, 나는 정말이지 우리가 얼마간은 반가웠다 여겨요. 우리는 나눌 얘기가 아주 많았어요. 그쪽 이름이 나왔을 때 러시워스 부인이 어떤 낯빛이던지 얘기해 줄까요? 나는 여태 그이가 침착함이 모자란 사람이라곤 여기지 않았는데, 어제의 그 시련엔 그게 좀 모자랐어요. 대체로 둘 중엔 줄리아가 더 나은 낯빛이었죠, 적어도 그쪽 얘기가 나온 뒤엔요. 내가 ‘패니’ 얘기를 꺼내고, 누이라면 마땅히 그럴 만한 투로 그 얘길 한 그 순간부터, 그 낯빛은 도무지 돌아오질 않더군요. 하지만 러시워스 부인이 고운 낯빛을 뽐낼 날이 올 거예요. 우리는 28일 그이의 첫 모임 초대장을 받았거든요. 그때는 그이도 아름다움에 싸이겠죠, 윔폴 스트리트에서도 손꼽히는 집 가운데 하나를 열 테니까요. 나는 이 년 전 그게 래슬 부인 댁이던 시절에 거기 가 봤는데, 내가 런던에서 아는 거의 어느 집보다 더 좋아해요. 그리고 그때는 그이도 분명, 속된 표현을 쓰자면, 제 한 푼으로 한 푼어치를 제대로 얻었다 느끼겠죠. 헨리는 그이한테 그런 집을 마련해 줄 수 없었을 거예요. 나는 그이가 그걸 떠올려, 왕(王)은 뒤편에 있을 때 가장 돋보일지언정 궁전의 여왕으로 나서는 데 되도록 흡족해하기를 바라요. 그리고 나는 그이를 괴롭힐 마음이 없으니, 다시는 그쪽 이름을 그이한테 억지로 들이밀지 않을게요. 그이도 차츰 차분해지겠죠. 내가 듣고 짐작하는 온갖 것으로 미루어, 빌덴하임 남작의 줄리아에 대한 마음씀은 이어지지만, 그이가 무슨 진지한 부추김을 받는지는 모르겠어요. 그 앤 더 잘해야 해요. 가난한 귀족 자제(honourable)는 좋은 신랑감이 못 되죠. 그리고 나는 이 경우에 무슨 정 같은 건 도무지 상상이 안 돼요. 그이의 허풍(rants)을 빼면 딱한 그 남작한테는 아무것도 안 남거든요. 모음 하나가 이런 차이를 내다니! 그이의 지대(rents)가 그 허풍(rants)만큼만 됐어도! 그쪽 사촌 에드먼드는 느릿느릿 움직여요. 어쩌면 교구 소임에 붙들렸겠죠. 손턴 레이시에 개종시킬 노파라도 하나 있나 봐요. 나는 젊은 여자 때문에 내가 뒷전이 됐다곤 여기고 싶지 않네요. 안녕! 사랑하고 고운 나의 패니, 이건 런던에서 온 긴 편지예요. 답장으로 헨리가 돌아왔을 때 그이 눈을 즐겁게 할 예쁜 편지 하나 써 보내 줘요. 그리고 그쪽이 그이를 위해 업신여기는 그 온갖 늠름한 젊은 함장들 얘기도 적어 보내 주고요.”\n\n이 편지에는 곰곰 헤아릴 거리가, 주로 언짢게 헤아릴 거리가 많았다. 그러면서도 그것이 안겨 준 그 온갖 불편함에도 불구하고, 그것은 그녀를 떠나 있는 이들과 이어 주었고, 지금만큼 궁금해 본 적 없는 사람들과 일들을 그녀에게 일러 주었다. 그리하여 그녀는 이런 편지를 다달이가 아니라 매주 받으리라는 확신이 있었더라면 반가웠으련만. 버트럼 이모와의 편지 왕래가 그보다 더 큰 관심의 유일한 일이었다.\n\n포츠머스에서 사귈 만한, 집의 부족함을 조금이나마 갚아 줄 만한 사교로 말하면, 아버지와 어머니의 지인 무리 안에는 그녀에게 조금의 흡족함이라도 줄 이가 하나도 없었다. 그녀가 저의 수줍음과 삼감을 무릅쓰고픈 마음이 들 만한 이는 아무도 안 보였다. 그녀 눈에 사내들은 죄다 거칠고, 여자들은 죄다 건방지고, 다들 교양 없어 보였다. 그리하여 옛 지인이든 새 지인이든 소개받아, 그녀는 받은 것만큼이나 준 흡족함도 적었다. 처음에 준남작 집안 출신임을 헤아려 얼마간 존경을 담아 그녀에게 다가온 아가씨들은, 이내 그들이 “거드름”이라 부르는 것에 언짢아했다. 그녀가 피아노포르테를 치지도 고운 펠리스를 걸치지도 않으니, 더 지켜본 끝에 그들은 그녀에게 무슨 우위의 권리도 인정할 수 없었던 것이다.\n\n패니가 집의 화를 두고 받은 첫 든든한 위안, 그녀의 판단이 온전히 찬동할 수 있는 첫 위안, 그리고 조금이나마 오래갈 조짐을 준 첫 위안은, 수전을 더 잘 알게 된 것과 그녀에게 쓸모가 되리라는 바람에 있었다. 수전은 늘 저에게 유쾌하게 굴었으나, 그 두루뭉술한 태도의 단호한 결이 그녀를 놀라게 하고 겁먹게 했던지라, 저와는 그토록 온전히 다른 성미를 헤아리기 시작하기까지 적어도 두 주가 걸렸다. 수전은 집에 잘못된 게 많음을 보고, 그것을 바로잡고 싶어 했다. 열넷 계집아이가, 오직 저 혼자의 도움 없는 이성만으로 움직이며, 그 고침의 방식에서 그른다는 것이야 놀랄 일이 아니었다. 그리하여 패니는 이내, 그것이 이끈 처신의 흠을 모질게 나무라기보다, 그토록 일찍부터 옳은 것을 분간할 줄 아는 그 마음의 타고난 빛을 감탄하는 쪽으로 더 기울었다. 수전은 그저, 그녀 자신의 판단도 인정하나 좀 더 늘어지고 굽히는 그녀의 성미로는 감히 내세우기를 움츠렸을 그 똑같은 진리에 따라 움직이며, 그 똑같은 방식을 좇고 있을 뿐이었다. 그녀라면 그저 자리를 떠 울밖에 없었을 데서 수전은 쓸모가 되려 애썼다. 그리고 수전이 쓸모가 됨을, 사정이 아무리 나빴어도 그런 끼어듦이 없었더라면 더 나빴으리라는 것을, 어머니와 벳시 둘 다 자못 거슬리는 무름과 상스러움의 몇몇 극단에서 억눌려 있음을 그녀는 알아볼 수 있었다.\n\n어머니와의 어떤 다툼에서든 수전은 이치에서 우세했고, 그녀를 달래 물릴 어미로서의 애틋함이라곤 여태 없었다. 제 둘레에 늘 화를 낳는 그 눈먼 무름을 그녀는 한 번도 받아 본 적이 없었다. 남들에게 베풀어지는 그 지나침을 더 잘 참게 해 줄, 지난 또는 지금의 애정에 대한 고마움이라곤 없었던 것이다.\n\n이 모두가 차츰 뚜렷해지며, 차츰 수전을 언니 앞에 딱함과 존경이 뒤섞인 대상으로 세워 놓았다. 그러나 그 태도가 그르다는 것, 이따금 자못 그르다는 것, 그 방편이 곧잘 잘못 골라지고 때 안 맞는다는 것, 그 낯빛과 말이 자못 흔히 두둔할 수 없다는 것을 패니는 못 느낄 수 없었다. 그러나 그녀는 그것들이 바로잡힐 수 있으리라 바라기 시작했다. 수전이 저를 우러러보며 저의 좋은 평가를 바란다는 것을 그녀는 알았다. 그리고 권위의 소임 비슷한 것이 패니에게 아무리 낯설고, 저가 누구를 이끌거나 일깨울 수 있다고 여기는 것이 아무리 낯설었어도, 그녀는 수전에게 이따금 넌지시 일러 주기로, 그리고 저의 더 나은 교육이 저에게 박아 준 그 올바른 소견—누구에게나 마땅한 것이 무엇인지, 그리고 저 자신에게 가장 지혜로운 것이 무엇인지에 대한 소견을 그녀의 이로움을 위해 써 보기로 마음먹었다.\n\n그녀의 감화, 아니 적어도 그것의 자각과 씀은, 여러 번의 섬세한 망설임 끝에 마침내 그녀가 스스로를 다잡아 해낸 수전을 위한 친절의 처사에서 비롯했다. 이제 끊임없이 입에 오르내리는 그 은칼이라는 아픈 화제를 두고, 자그마한 돈이 어쩌면 영영 평화를 되찾아 줄지도 모른다는 생각이 그녀에게 아주 일찍 떠올랐고, 삼촌이 헤어질 때 준 10파운드로 저 자신이 가진 그 재물이, 너그러워지고픈 마음만큼이나 너그러워질 수 있게 해 주었던 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 아주 가난한 이 말고는 은혜를 베풀어 본 적이 하도 없고, 제 또래 사이에서 화를 없애거나 친절을 베푸는 데 하도 안 익고, 집에서 저를 대단한 마님인 양 치켜세우는 티를 낼까 봐 하도 두려운지라, 그런 선물을 하는 것이 저에게 안 어울리는 일은 아니라 마음먹기까지 얼마간이 걸렸다. 그러나 마침내 그것은 이루어졌다. 벳시를 위해 은칼 하나를 샀고, 그것은 크나큰 기쁨으로 받아들여졌으니, 그 새것임이 바랄 만한 온갖 점에서 그것을 다른 것보다 낫게 했다. 수전은 제 것을 온전히 차지하게 되었고, 벳시는 이제 제가 훨씬 더 예쁜 걸 얻었으니 다시는 저것을 바라지 않겠다고 훤칠히 선언했다. 그리고 못지않게 흡족한 어머니에게도 아무 나무람이 전해지지 않은 듯했으니, 패니가 그것은 거의 어림없으려니 여기던 일이었다. 그 처사는 속속들이 통했다. 집안 다툼의 한 샘이 아주 없어졌고, 그것은 수전이 그녀에게 마음을 여는 방편이 되어, 그녀에게 사랑하고 관심 둘 무언가를 더 안겨 주었다. 수전은 저에게 섬세함이 있음을 보여 주었다. 적어도 두 해나 다투어 온 그 소유물의 임자가 된 것이 기쁘면서도, 그녀는 언니의 판단이 저를 거슬렀을까 봐, 집의 고요를 위해 그 사들임이 필요해질 만큼 다툰 데 대한 꾸지람이 저에게 뜻해졌을까 봐 두려웠던 것이다.\n\n그녀의 성미는 툭 트여 있었다. 그녀는 제 두려움을 털어놓고, 그토록 발끈하며 다툰 것을 두고 저를 나무랐다. 그리하여 그 시각부터 패니는, 그녀의 성미의 값어치를 헤아리고, 그녀가 저의 좋은 평가를 구하고 저의 판단에 기대려 얼마나 온전히 기울어 있는지를 알아채고서, 다시금 애정의 복을 느끼기 시작했고, 그토록 도움이 아쉬우면서 그토록 도움을 받을 만한 마음에 쓸모가 되리라는 바람을 품기 시작했다. 그녀는 조언을 했으니, 좋은 슬기라면 거스를 수 없을 만큼 온당하되, 미흡한 성미를 돋우지 않도록 그토록 부드럽고 사려 깊게 건네진 조언이었다. 그리고 그녀는 그 좋은 효험을 드물잖게 지켜보는 행복을 누렸다. 굽히고 참는 것의 온갖 마땅함과 이로움을 보면서도, 수전 같은 아이에게 시시각각 거슬릴 온갖 것을 못지않게 정겹고 예민한 심경으로 본 이가 그보다 더 바라지는 않았다. 이 일에 대한 그녀의 가장 큰 놀라움은 이내—수전이 제 더 나은 앎을 거슬러 무례와 조바심으로 부아 돋음을 당했다는 것이 아니라, 그토록 더 나은 앎이, 그토록 많은 좋은 소견이 대체 그녀에게 있었다는 것이 되었다. 그리고 무심함과 그릇됨의 한복판에서 자라, 마땅히 어때야 하는가에 대한 그토록 올바른 소견을 지었다는 것—제 생각을 이끌거나 제 지조를 박아 줄 사촌 에드먼드 하나 없던 그녀가 말이다.\n\n이렇게 둘 사이에 시작된 친밀함은 저마다에게 큰 이로움이었다. 위층에서 함께 앉아 지냄으로써 그들은 집의 소란을 크게 피했다. 패니는 평온을 얻었고, 수전은 조용히 일하는 것이 불행이 아님을 배웠다. 그들은 불도 없이 앉아 있었다. 그러나 그것은 패니에게조차 익은 결핍이었고, 그것이 동쪽 방을 떠올려 준 덕에 그녀는 덜 괴로웠다. 그것이 유일하게 닮은 점이었다. 넓이도, 빛도, 세간도, 내다보이는 경치도, 두 방에는 닮은 것이 하나도 없었다. 그리하여 그녀는 곧잘, 거기 있던 제 온갖 책과 상자와 갖가지 편안함을 떠올리며 한숨을 내쉬었다. 차츰 두 아가씨는 아침의 대부분을 위층에서 보내게 되었으니, 처음엔 그저 바느질하고 얘기하는 것뿐이었으나, 며칠 뒤엔 그 책들에 대한 기억이 하도 세차고 부추기는 것이 되어, 패니는 다시 책을 구해 보지 않을 수 없었다. 아버지의 집엔 책이 하나도 없었다. 그러나 재물이란 호사스럽고 대담한 것이라, 그녀의 재물 얼마가 대출 문고로 흘러 들어갔다. 그녀는 회원이 되었으니, 제 이름으로 어엿한 무언가가 된다는 데 놀라고, 어느 모로 보나 제가 하는 일에 놀라며—빌리는 이가, 책을 고르는 이가 되다니! 게다가 그 고름에 누군가의 나아짐을 눈에 두고 있다니! 그러나 정말 그러했다. 수전은 아무것도 읽은 것이 없었고, 패니는 저의 그 첫 즐거움을 그녀에게 나눠 주어, 저 스스로 좋아하는 전기와 시에 대한 취향을 불어넣어 주고 싶었다.\n\n게다가 이 일로 그녀는 맨스필드에 대한 기억 얼마간을 묻어 두기를 바랐으니, 그 기억은 손가락만 바쁠 때면 너무 쉬 그녀의 마음을 사로잡곤 했던 것이다. 그리고 특히 이 무렵, 그녀의 생각이 에드먼드를 좇아 런던으로 가는 것을 돌려놓는 데 이것이 쓸모가 되기를 바랐다. 이모의 지난번 편지를 근거로, 그녀는 그가 거기로 갔음을 알았던 것이다. 그녀는 뒤이어 무슨 일이 생길지 의심치 않았다. 그 약속된 알림이 그녀의 머리 위에 걸려 있었다. 근방에 울리는 우체부의 문 두드림이 나날의 두려움을 가져오기 시작했고, 만일 책 읽기가 반 시간만이라도 그 생각을 몰아낼 수 있다면, 그것만으로도 얻는 바가 있는 셈이었다.\n\n# 제41장\n\n에드먼드가 시내에 있으려니 여겨질 만한 때로부터 한 주가 지났으나, 패니는 그에 대한 아무 소식도 못 들었다. 그의 잠잠함에서 이끌어 낼 만한 결론이 셋 있었으니, 그녀의 마음은 그 사이에서 오르내렸고, 저마다 때때로 가장 그럴싸한 것으로 여겨졌다. 그의 상경이 또 미뤄졌거나, 아직 크로퍼드 양을 단둘이 만날 기회를 못 얻었거나, 아니면 편지 쓰기엔 너무 행복하거나!\n\n이 무렵 어느 아침, 패니가 이제 맨스필드를 떠난 지 거의 네 주가 된 참—그녀가 날마다 어김없이 곱씹고 헤아리던 그 대목이었다—에, 그녀와 수전이 여느 때처럼 위층으로 올라가려 채비하는데, 손님의 문 두드림에 걸음이 멎었다. 리베카가 문으로 가는 데 어찌나 잽싸던지—그것은 여느 무엇보다도 늘 그녀의 관심을 끄는 소임이었다—그들은 그 손님을 피할 수 없으려니 느꼈다.\n\n신사의 목소리였다. 패니가 막 창백해지려던 그 목소리였는데, 이내 크로퍼드 씨가 방으로 걸어 들어왔다.\n\n그녀 같은 좋은 분별은 정말로 부름받으면 늘 움직이는 법이라, 그녀는 저가 그를 어머니에게 소개하며 “윌리엄의 벗”이라는 그 이름을 어머니에게 되떠올려 줄 수 있었음을 알았다. 하기야 앞서라면 그런 순간에 한마디라도 뇔 수 있으리라곤 저 스스로 믿지 못했으련만. 그가 여기서 그저 윌리엄의 벗으로만 알려져 있다는 자각이 얼마간 버팀이 되었다. 그러나 그를 소개하고 다들 다시 자리에 앉고 나니, 이 방문이 무엇으로 이어질지에 대한 두려움이 밀려들어, 그녀는 저가 막 까무러칠 것만 같았다.\n\n저를 다잡으려 애쓰는 동안, 처음엔 여느 때 못지않게 생기 어린 낯빛으로 그녀에게 다가왔던 그 손님은, 슬기롭고도 친절하게 눈길을 돌려 그녀에게 추스를 겨를을 주고는, 온통 그녀 어머니에게만 몸을 바쳐, 더없는 예의와 온당함으로, 그리고 동시에 그의 태도를 온전케 하는 얼마간의 다정함—적어도 관심을 담아 그녀에게 말을 걸고 시중을 들었다.\n\n프라이스 부인의 태도 또한 더없이 좋았다. 제 아들에게 그런 벗이 있음을 보고 데워지고, 그 앞에서 잘 보이려는 바람에 다잡혀, 그녀는 고마움이—꾸밈없는 어미의 고마움이 넘쳐흘렀는데, 그것은 밉지 않은 것이었다. 프라이스 씨는 나가고 없었으니, 그녀는 그것을 무척 아쉬워했다. 패니는 겨우 추슬러 저는 그것을 아쉬워할 수 없음을 느낄 만큼 되어 있었다. 그녀의 여러 다른 불편함의 샘에, 그가 저를 발견한 이 집에 대한 부끄러움이라는 모진 것이 더해진 까닭이다. 그녀는 그 여림을 두고 저를 나무랄 수야 있었으나, 나무란다고 그것이 없어지진 않았다. 그녀는 부끄러웠고, 나머지 온갖 것보다 아버지가 훨씬 더 부끄러웠으리라.\n\n그들은 윌리엄 얘기를 했는데, 프라이스 부인은 그 화제라면 결코 물릴 줄 몰랐다. 그리고 크로퍼드 씨는 그녀의 마음이 바랄 만한 만큼이나 뜨겁게 그를 칭찬했다. 그녀는 저가 평생 이토록 상냥한 사내를 본 적이 없다 느꼈다. 다만 그토록 훌륭하고 그토록 상냥한 그가 포츠머스에 내려온 것이 항구 제독을 뵈러 온 것도, 감독관을 뵈러 온 것도, 섬으로 건너갈 뜻에서도, 조선소를 보러 온 것도 아님을 알고 그저 놀랄 뿐이었다. 그녀가 중함의 증거로, 또는 재물의 쓰임으로 여기곤 하던 그 온갖 것 가운데 무엇도 그를 포츠머스로 데려온 것이 아니었다. 그는 간밤 늦게 이르러, 하루 이틀 머물 참으로 크라운 여관에 묵고 있었고, 도착한 뒤 우연히 아는 해군 사관 한둘을 만났으나, 그런 종류의 목적으로 온 것은 아니었다.\n\n이 온갖 소식을 다 전할 무렵, 이제 패니가 눈길받고 말을 걸어져도 무리가 아니었다. 그리고 그녀는 그의 눈을 웬만큼 견디며, 그가 런던을 떠나기 전날 저녁 그 누이와 반 시간을 보냈다는 것을, 그녀가 더없이 다정한 사랑의 안부를 전했으나 편지 쓸 겨를은 없었다는 것을, 노퍽에서 돌아온 뒤 다시 길을 나서기까지 런던에서 채 스물네 시간도 못 보낸지라 메리를 반 시간이라도 본 것을 저는 운 좋게 여긴다는 것을, 그녀의 사촌 에드먼드가 시내에 있다는 것을—그가 알기로 며칠째 시내에 있다는 것을, 저는 그를 직접 보진 못했으나 그는 잘 있고 맨스필드의 온 식구를 잘 있는 채로 두고 왔으며, 어제처럼 프레이저네에서 만찬을 들기로 되어 있다는 것을 들었다.\n\n패니는 차분히 들었으니, 마지막에 언급된 그 사정까지도 그러했다. 아니, 무엇에든 확실함에 이르는 것이 그녀의 지친 마음에 오히려 안도인 듯했다. 그리하여 “그럼 이때쯤엔 죄다 정해졌겠구나” 하는 말이, 어렴풋한 홍조 말고는 이렇다 할 심경의 드러남 없이 속으로 지나갔다.\n\n맨스필드에 대해 조금 더 얘기하고 나서—그녀의 관심이 더없이 뚜렷한 화제였다—크로퍼드는 이른 산책이 좋겠다고 넌지시 비치기 시작했다. “고운 아침이고, 이맘때는 고운 아침이 하도 자주 흐려지니, 누구든 운동을 미루지 않는 게 가장 지혜롭다”는 것이었다. 그리고 그런 넌지시 비침이 아무것도 못 낳자, 그는 이내 프라이스 부인과 그 딸들에게 지체 없이 산책 나가라고 대놓고 권하기에 이르렀다. 이제 그들은 서로 통하게 되었다. 프라이스 부인은, 보아하니 일요일 말고는 좀처럼 문밖에 나서지 않았다. 대식구를 거느린지라 산책할 겨를을 좀처럼 못 낸다고 그녀는 인정했다. “그럼 딸들더러 이런 날씨를 누리라 이르고, 저에게 그들을 모시는 즐거움을 허락해 주지 않으시겠어요?” 프라이스 부인은 크게 고마워하며 자못 선선히 응했다. “딸들이 자못 갇혀 지내요. 포츠머스는 딱한 곳이라, 자주 못 나가거든요. 그리고 그 애들이 시내에 볼일이 좀 있어, 기꺼이 하려던 참인 걸 나도 안답니다.” 그리하여 그 결과로, 패니는—야릇하게도, 야릇하고 거북하고 괴롭게도—십 분도 안 되어 저와 수전이 크로퍼드 씨와 함께 하이 스트리트 쪽으로 걷고 있음을 알았다.\n\n이내 아픔 위에 아픔이, 얼떨떨함 위에 얼떨떨함이었다. 그들이 하이 스트리트에 채 들어서기도 전에 아버지와 마주쳤는데, 그 행색은 토요일인 탓에 나을 것이 없었던 것이다. 그가 멈춰 섰다. 그리고 그가 아무리 신사답잖아 보였어도, 패니는 하는 수 없이 그를 크로퍼드 씨에게 소개해야 했다. 크로퍼드 씨가 어떤 인상을 받을지 그녀는 의심할 나위가 없었다. 그는 아주 부끄럽고 역겨울 게 틀림없었다. 그는 이내 그녀를 단념하고 그 혼사에 대한 조금의 마음도 그칠 게 틀림없었다. 그런데도, 그녀가 그토록 그의 정이 낫기를 바라 왔으면서도, 이것은 그 병만큼이나 나쁠 뻔한 그런 종류의 나음이었다. 그리고 나는 믿거니와, 영리하고 상냥한 사내에게 구애받는 불운을 참아 내느니 차라리 그이가 제 가장 가까운 피붙이의 상스러움에 쫓겨 가게 하고 싶은 아가씨는 온 연합왕국에 좀처럼 없으리라.\n\n크로퍼드 씨는 아마 제 장래 장인을 옷차림의 본보기로 삼을 생각으로야 볼 수 없었으리라. 그러나 (패니가 대번에, 그리고 크게 안도하며 알아챈 대로) 그녀의 아버지는, 이 더없이 존경받는 낯선 이를 대하는 처신에서, 집에서 제 식구를 대하던 것과는 아주 딴사람, 아주 딴 프라이스 씨였다. 그의 태도는 이제, 세련되진 않았어도 웬만큼을 넘었다. 고맙게 여기고, 생기 있고, 사내다웠다. 그의 말은 정 많은 아버지, 지각 있는 사내의 말이었다. 그 우렁찬 목소리도 트인 데선 자못 어울렸고, 욕지거리 한마디 들리지 않았다. 이것이 크로퍼드 씨의 좋은 태도에 대한 그의 본능적인 예의였다. 그리고 그 결과가 무엇이든 간에, 패니의 당장의 심경은 이루 말할 수 없이 다독여졌다.\n\n두 신사의 예의차림의 끝은, 크로퍼드 씨를 조선소로 데려가겠다는 프라이스 씨의 청이었다. 크로퍼드 씨는, 조선소야 몇 번이고 봤으면서도, 그런 뜻으로 베풀어진 것을 은혜로 받고 싶고, 그만큼 더 오래 패니와 함께하기를 바라, 만일 프라이스 양들이 그 고단함을 겁내지만 않는다면 자못 고맙게 그것을 누리려 했다. 그리고 어찌어찌 그들이 조금도 안 겁낸다는 것이 확인되었는지 넘겨짚어졌는지, 아니면 적어도 그런 셈 치고, 조선소로 다들 가기로 되었다. 그리고 크로퍼드 씨만 아니었다면, 프라이스 씨는 딸들의 하이 스트리트 볼일은 조금도 헤아리지 않고 곧장 그리로 돌아섰으리라. 그러나 크로퍼드 씨는, 그들이 일부러 들르러 나온 가게에 들르게 되도록 마음 썼다. 그것이 그들을 오래 지체시키진 않았으니, 패니는 남을 조바심 나게 하거나 기다리게 하는 것을 하도 못 견뎌, 신사들이 문간에 서서 최근 해군 규정 얘기를 겨우 꺼내거나 지금 취역 중인 삼층 갑판함의 수를 채 셈하기도 전에, 그들의 일행은 나아갈 채비가 되었던 것이다.\n\n그리하여 그들은 곧바로 조선소로 나서기로 했다. 그리고 크로퍼드 씨가 보기에, 만일 프라이스 씨에게 그 산책의 온전한 조율이 맡겨졌더라면 그것은 자못 별난 식으로 이뤄졌으리라. 두 아가씨는, 그가 보니, 뒤에 남겨져 저희 힘닿는 대로 따라오든 말든 하게 내버려지고, 그동안 그들 둘은 저희 서두르는 걸음으로 함께 걸어갈 판이었던 것이다. 그는 이따금 얼마간 손을 볼 수 있었으니, 바라는 만큼은 결코 못 됐지만. 그는 결코 그들에게서 앞서 걸어가려 하지 않았고, 어떤 건널목이나 어떤 인파에서, 프라이스 씨가 그저 “이리 와, 얘들아. 이리, 팬. 이리, 수, 조심들 해라. 눈 똑바로 뜨고!” 하고 소리칠 뿐일 때, 그는 그들에게 각별히 시중을 들었다.\n\n일단 조선소에 제대로 들어서자, 그는 패니와의 얼마간 행복한 어울림을 기대하기 시작했다. 이내 프라이스 씨의 어슬렁거리는 벗 하나가 그들과 합류했는데, 일이 어찌 돌아가는지 날마다 둘러보러 온 이로, 그 자신보다 훨씬 값진 말벗임이 틀림없었던 것이다. 그리하여 얼마 뒤 두 사관은 함께 돌아다니며 서로 대등하고 결코 물리지 않는 관심사를 논하는 데 자못 흡족해 보였고, 그동안 젊은이들은 뜰의 목재 위에 앉거나, 다들 보러 간, 선대에 오른 배 위에서 앉을 자리를 찾았다. 패니는 마침 쉬어야 할 참이었다. 크로퍼드는 그녀가 그보다 더 지쳐 앉을 채비가 더 되어 있기를 바랄 수 없었으련만, 그녀 누이가 없어졌으면 하고는 바랄 만했다. 수전 또래의 눈치 빠른 계집아이는 세상에 둘도 없이 나쁜 세 번째였다. 버트럼 부인과는 아주 딴판으로, 온통 눈과 귀뿐이라, 그 앞에서 요점을 꺼낼 수가 없었다. 그는 그저 두루 상냥하게 굴며, 수전에게도 그 즐거움의 몫을 나눠 주고, 이따금 더 밝고 다 아는 패니에게 눈길이나 넌지시 비침을 던지는 것으로 만족할밖에 없었다. 노퍽이 그가 주로 얘기할 것이었다. 거기서 그는 한동안 지냈고, 거기의 온갖 것이 그의 지금 궁리로 중해지고 있었다. 그런 사내라면 어느 곳, 어느 사교에서도 즐길 거리를 얼마간 안 가져오고는 못 왔을 터라, 그의 여행과 그의 지인이 죄다 쓸모가 되었고, 수전은 저에게 아주 낯선 식으로 즐거워졌다. 패니에게는, 그가 어울린 무리의 우연한 유쾌함보다 얼마간 더한 것이 들려졌다. 그녀의 찬동을 얻으려, 이 여느 때 아닌 철에 그가 대체 왜 노퍽에 갔는지 그 각별한 까닭이 주어졌다. 그것은 정말 볼일이었으니, 크고 또—그가 믿기로—부지런한 한 집안의 안녕이 걸린 임대차의 갱신에 관한 것이었다. 그는 제 관리인이 무슨 뒷거래를 하는 게, 저를 마땅한 이에게서 치우치게 할 셈인 게 미심쩍었다. 그리하여 몸소 가서 그 일의 옳고 그름을 속속들이 살피기로 마음먹었다. 그는 갔고, 저가 내다본 것보다 더 큰 좋은 일을 했으며, 애초 계획이 아우른 것보다 더 많은 이에게 쓸모가 되었다. 그리고 이제 그것을 두고 스스로 뿌듯해하며, 한 도리를 다함으로써 제 마음에 흐뭇한 기억을 마련해 두었다 느낄 수 있었다. 그는 여태 한 번도 못 본 세입자 몇에게 저를 소개했고, 제 영지에 있으면서도 여태 그 존재조차 몰랐던 오두막들과 낯을 트기 시작했다. 이것은 패니를 겨냥한, 그것도 잘 겨냥한 것이었다. 그가 그토록 온당하게 말하는 것을 듣는 것은 흐뭇한 일이었다. 여기서 그는 마땅히 해야 할 대로 처신하고 있었다. 가난하고 억눌린 이의 벗이 된다니! 그녀에게 이보다 더 반가운 것은 없었다. 그리하여 그녀가 막 찬동하는 눈길을 그에게 주려는데, 그가 무언가 너무 노골적인 것을—머잖아 에버링엄을 위한 온갖 쓸모나 자선의 계획에서 조력자를, 벗을, 길잡이를 두기를 바란다는 것을, 에버링엄과 그 온갖 것을 여태보다 더 소중한 것으로 만들어 줄 누군가를 두기를 바란다는 것을 덧붙이는 바람에, 그 눈길이 죄다 겁먹어 달아나 버렸다.\n\n그녀는 고개를 돌리고, 그가 그런 말을 안 했으면 싶었다. 그녀는 그에게 저가 여기던 것보다 더 많은 좋은 자질이 있을지도 모른다고 기꺼이 인정했다. 그녀는 그가 끝내 잘될 가망이 있음을 느끼기 시작했다. 그러나 그는 저에게 아주 안 어울렸고, 앞으로도 늘 그럴 게 틀림없었으며, 저를 마음에 두어선 안 되었다.\n\n그는 에버링엄 얘기는 넉넉히 했음을, 그리고 다른 얘기로 옮기는 게 나으리라는 것을 알아채고 맨스필드로 돌아섰다. 이보다 더 잘 고를 수야 없었다. 그것은 거의 대번에 그녀의 관심과 낯빛을 되돌릴 화제였다. 맨스필드에 대해 듣거나 말하는 것은 그녀에게 정말이지 호사였다. 이제 그곳을 아는 온갖 이와 그토록 오래 떨어져 있던 터라, 그가 그것을 입에 올리니 그녀는 그것을 자못 벗의 목소리로 느꼈다. 그리고 그가 그 아름다움과 편안함을 기리는 그녀의 애틋한 탄사로 이끌고, 그곳 식구들에 대한 그의 떳떳한 헌사로써 그녀로 하여금—삼촌을 온통 영리하고 착한 이로, 이모를 온갖 고운 성미 가운데 가장 고운 성미를 지닌 이로 말하며—더없이 뜨거운 찬사로 제 마음을 흐뭇하게 하도록 해 주었다.\n\n그 자신도 맨스필드에 크나큰 애착이 있었다. 그가 그리 말했다. 그는 제 시간의 많은, 아주 많은 부분을 거기서 보내기를 바라며 내다보았다. 늘 거기서든, 그 근방에서든. 그는 특히 올해 거기서의 자못 행복한 여름과 가을에 기대를 걸었다. 그리되리라 느꼈고, 그것에 기댔다. 지난번보다 한없이 나은 여름과 가을. 그만큼 생기 있고, 그만큼 다채롭고, 그만큼 어울리되, 이루 말할 수 없이 나은 사정을 갖춘.\n\n“맨스필드, 소더턴, 손턴 레이시,” 그가 말을 이었다. “그 집들에 얼마나 대단한 사교가 담기겠어요! 그리고 미가엘 축일쯤엔, 어쩌면 넷째가 더해질지도 모르죠. 그토록 소중한 온갖 것의 근방에 자그마한 사냥 별장 하나가요. 언젠가 에드먼드 버트럼이 기분 좋게 내놨던 대로 손턴 레이시를 함께 쓰는 것으로 말하면, 나는 두 가지 반대를 미리 내다보지 싶거든요. 그 계획에 대한, 두 가지 어여쁘고, 훌륭하고, 뿌리칠 수 없는 반대를요.”\n\n패니는 여기서 두 겹으로 말문이 막혔다. 그러나 그 순간이 지나고 나니, 그의 뜻의 절반이나마 알아들었다고 스스로 억지로 인정해 그로 하여금 그 누이와 에드먼드에 대해 무언가 더 말하도록 북돋우지 못한 것이 아쉬웠다. 그것은 그녀가 얘기하는 법을 익혀야 할 화제였고, 그것에서 움츠러드는 그 여림은 이내 아주 용서 못 할 것이 될 터였다.\n\n프라이스 씨와 그 벗이 보고 싶은, 또는 볼 겨를이 있는 온갖 것을 다 보고 나자, 나머지도 돌아갈 채비가 되었다. 그리고 되걸어 돌아오는 길에, 크로퍼드 씨는 잠깐의 오붓함을 마련해 패니에게, 포츠머스에서 제 유일한 볼일은 그녀를 보는 것이라고, 오직 그녀 때문에, 오로지 그녀 때문에 하루 이틀 내려왔다고, 더 긴 온전한 헤어짐을 도무지 견딜 수 없어서라고 말했다. 그녀는 서운했다, 정말 서운했다. 그러면서도 이것과, 그가 안 했으면 싶은 두세 가지 다른 것에도 불구하고, 그녀는 그가 저번에 본 이래로 대체로 나아졌다 여겼다. 그는 맨스필드에서 여태 그러던 것보다 훨씬 더 상냥하고, 곰살맞고, 남의 심경에 자상했다. 그녀는 그가 이토록 상냥한 것을—이토록 상냥함에 가까운 것을 본 적이 없었다. 그녀의 아버지를 대하는 그의 처신은 거슬릴 수 없었고, 그가 수전을 아는 체하는 데는 유난히 친절하고 온당한 무언가가 있었다. 그는 확실히 나아졌다. 그녀는 이튿날이 지나 버렸으면, 그가 딱 하루만 왔더라면 싶었다. 그러나 그것은 그녀가 겁냈을 만큼 그리 나쁘진 않았다. 맨스필드 얘기를 하는 즐거움이 하도 컸던 것이다!\n\n헤어지기 전에, 그녀는 또 하나의 즐거움을, 그것도 사소하지 않은 즐거움을 두고 그에게 고마워해야 했다. 아버지가 그에게 함께 양고기 만찬을 드는 영광을 베풀어 달라 청하니, 패니는 딱 한 번의 소름 끼치는 전율을 느낄 겨를밖에 없었는데, 이내 그가 먼저 약속이 있어 못 하게 되었다고 밝혔다. 그는 그날과 이튿날 둘 다 이미 만찬 약속이 있었다. 크라운에서 몇몇 지인을 만났는데 도무지 마다할 수 없다는 것이었다. 그래도 이튿날 다시 그들을 뵈러 오는 영광을 누리겠노라 운운하고, 그리하여 그들은 헤어졌다—패니는 그토록 소름 끼치는 화를 면한 데서 온 정말이지 벅찬 기쁨에 잠긴 채!\n\n그가 그들의 집안 만찬 자리에 끼어 그들의 온갖 부족함을 다 보았더라면 끔찍했으리라! 리베카의 요리와 리베카의 시중, 그리고 벳시가 식탁에서 거리낌 없이 먹으며 온갖 것을 제멋대로 잡아당기는 것은, 패니 자신조차 아직 웬만한 끼니를 곧잘 들 만큼은 익지 못한 것들이었다. 그녀는 그저 타고난 섬세함에서 까다로울 뿐이었으나, 그는 호사와 미식의 학교에서 자란 사람이었다.\n\n# 제42장\n\n이튿날 프라이스네가 막 교회로 나서려는데 크로퍼드 씨가 다시 나타났다. 그는 머물러 있으려는 게 아니라 그들과 함께하러 온 것이었다. 그는 수비대 예배당까지 함께 가자 청받았는데, 그것이야말로 딱 그가 뜻하던 바였고, 그리하여 다들 함께 그리로 걸어갔다.\n\n이제 그 집안이 돋보이게 보였다. 자연은 그들에게 적잖은 아름다움의 몫을 주었고, 일요일마다 그들을 가장 깨끗한 살결과 가장 좋은 옷차림으로 꾸며 놓았다. 일요일은 늘 이 위안을 패니에게 안겨 주었고, 이 일요일에 그녀는 그것을 여느 때보다 더 느꼈다. 그녀의 딱한 어머니는 이제, 자못 곧잘 그리 보이곤 하던 만큼 버트럼 부인의 동생답잖아 보이진 않았다. 그녀는 둘 사이의 대조를 생각하면 곧잘 가슴이 저렸다. 자연이 그토록 작은 다름밖에 안 낸 데에 사정이 그토록 큰 다름을 냈다는 것을, 버트럼 부인만큼 고운 데다 몇 살 손아래인 어머니가 그토록 훨씬 더 닳고 바랜, 그토록 스산하고, 그토록 지저분하고, 그토록 초라한 행색이라는 것을 생각하면. 그러나 일요일은 그녀를 자못 어엿하고 웬만큼 명랑해 보이는 프라이스 부인으로 만들었다. 훤한 아이들 무리를 데리고 나서서, 한 주의 시름에서 잠깐 놓여난 것을 느끼며, 그저 제 아들들이 위험에 뛰어드는 것을 보거나 리베카가 모자에 꽃을 꽂고 지나가는 것을 볼 때만 심기가 흐트러지는.\n\n예배당에서 그들은 하는 수 없이 갈라져야 했으나, 크로퍼드 씨는 여자들 쪽에서 갈라지지 않도록 마음을 썼다. 그리고 예배가 끝난 뒤에도 그는 여전히 그들과 함께해, 성벽 위 가족 무리의 한 사람이 되었다.\n\n프라이스 부인은 한 해 내내 고운 일요일마다 성벽 위로 주간 산책을 했으니, 늘 아침 예배 직후에 가서 만찬 시각까지 머물렀다. 그것은 그녀의 사교 자리였다. 거기서 그녀는 지인을 만나고, 소식을 조금 듣고, 포츠머스 하인들의 고약함을 두고 얘기하고, 뒤이은 엿새를 위해 제 기운을 돋우었다.\n\n이제 그들은 그리로 갔다. 크로퍼드 씨는 프라이스 양들을 제 각별한 소임으로 여기는 것이 더없이 행복했다. 그리고 거기 이른 지 얼마 안 되어, 어찌 된 영문인지—어찌 그리됐는지 알 길이 없고 패니로선 믿을 수도 없었으나—그는 저마다의 팔을 제 팔에 낀 채 그들 사이를 걷고 있었고, 그녀는 그것을 막거나 끝낼 길을 몰랐다. 그것이 한동안 그녀를 거북하게 했으나, 그래도 그날에도 그 경치에도 느껴질 만한 즐거움이 있었다.\n\n그날은 유난히 고왔다. 정말 삼월이긴 했다. 그러나 그 포근한 공기와, 상쾌하고 부드러운 바람과, 이따금 잠깐씩 구름 낀 밝은 햇살에서는 사월이었다. 그리고 그런 하늘 아래 온갖 것이 하도 아름다워 보였다. 스핏헤드의 배들과 그 너머 섬 위로 서로 쫓는 그림자의 어우러짐이, 지금 만조에 이른 바다의 그 늘 바뀌는 빛깔과 함께, 흥에 겨워 춤추며 그토록 고운 소리로 성벽에 부딪히니, 그 모든 것이 어우러져 패니에게 그런 매력의 어우러짐을 자아내, 그녀로 하여금 그것을 느끼는 처지에 차츰 거의 무심해지게 했다. 아니, 그의 팔이 없었더라면 그녀는 이내 저에게 그것이 필요함을 알았으리라. 대개 그러하듯 한 주의 앞선 무기력 끝에 온 이런 종류의 두 시간 어슬렁 산책을 견딜 기력이 그녀에겐 없었던 것이다. 패니는 여느 규칙적인 운동을 못 하게 된 것의 여파를 느끼기 시작하고 있었다. 포츠머스에 온 이래 그녀는 건강이 나빠졌고, 크로퍼드 씨와 그 좋은 날씨가 아니었다면 이제 이내 나가떨어졌으리라.\n\n그날과 그 경치의 고움을 그도 그녀처럼 느꼈다. 그들은 곧잘 같은 심경과 안목으로, 벽에 기댄 채 몇 분씩 멈춰 서서 바라보고 감탄했다. 그리고 그가 에드먼드가 아님을 감안하면, 패니는 그가 자연의 매력에 웬만큼 열려 있고, 제 감탄을 자못 잘 나타낼 줄 안다는 것을 인정할밖에 없었다. 그녀는 이따금 얼마간 애틋한 상념에 젖었는데, 그는 이따금 그 틈을 타 들키지 않고 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다볼 수 있었다. 그리고 그 들여다봄의 결과인즉, 그녀의 얼굴이 여느 때 못지않게 홀리는 것이면서도, 마땅한 만큼 화사하진 않다는 것이었다. 그녀는 아주 잘 있노라 말했고, 달리 여겨지기를 싫어했다. 그러나 이 모두를 아울러 보면, 그는 그녀의 지금 거처가 편안할 리 없고 그러니 그녀에게 이로울 리 없다 확신했다. 그리하여 그녀가 다시 맨스필드에 있게 되기를 애타게 바라기 시작했으니, 거기서라면 그녀 자신의 행복도, 그녀를 보는 저의 행복도 훨씬 클 게 틀림없었다.\n\n“여기 온 지 한 달 됐죠?” 그가 말했다.\n\n“아뇨, 한 달이 채 안 됐어요. 맨스필드를 떠난 지 내일이면 겨우 네 주예요.”\n\n“참으로 정확하고 정직한 셈꾼이시네요. 나라면 그걸 한 달이라 부르겠는데.”\n\n“저는 화요일 저녁에야 여기 이르렀는걸요.”\n\n“그리고 두 달 방문이 될 참이죠, 아닌가요?”\n\n“네. 삼촌이 두 달을 얘기하셨어요. 그보다 짧진 않을 것 같아요.”\n\n“그리고 어떻게 다시 데려가지죠? 누가 데리러 오나요?”\n\n“모르겠어요. 아직 이모한테서 그에 대해선 아무 말도 못 들었어요. 어쩌면 더 오래 머물게 될지도 모르죠. 딱 두 달 끝에 데리러 오는 게 형편에 안 맞을 수도 있고요.”\n\n잠깐 헤아린 뒤, 크로퍼드 씨가 대답했다. “나는 맨스필드를 알아요, 그 방식을 알고, 양을 향한 그 흠도 알아요. 나는 양이 그토록 까맣게 잊혀, 양의 편안함이 그 집안 어느 한 사람의 상상 속 편의에 밀려나게 될 위험을 알아요. 나는 알아요, 토머스 경이 몸소 오거나 이모의 하녀를 양한테 보내는 일을, 제가 앞으로 석 달간 짜 둔 채비를 조금도 안 바꾸고서는 마련하지 못하면, 양이 이 주 저 주 여기 남겨질 수도 있다는 걸. 이래선 안 돼요. 두 달이면 넉넉한 몫이에요. 나라면 여섯 주면 아주 넉넉하다 여기겠어요. 나는 양의 누이 건강을 헤아리고 있어요.” 하며 그가 수전에게 몸을 돌려 말했다. “포츠머스에 갇혀 있는 게 그이 건강에 안 좋다 여겨서요. 그이는 끊임없는 바람과 운동이 필요해요. 나만큼 그이를 알게 되면, 그이가 그렇다는 것과, 그이가 결코 시골의 트인 바람과 자유에서 오래 쫓겨나 있어선 안 된다는 것에 그쪽도 분명 수긍할 거예요. 그러니 만일” (다시 패니에게 돌아서며) “양이 몸이 나빠지는 걸 느끼고, 두 달이 다 차기를 기다리지 않고 맨스필드로 돌아가는 데 무슨 어려움이 생기거든, 그건 조금도 대수로이 여겨선 안 돼요. 여느 때보다 조금이라도 덜 튼튼하거나 덜 편안하다 느껴지거든, 그저 내 누이한테만 알려요, 그이한테 아주 살짝만 넌지시 비치기만 하면, 그이하고 나는 대번에 내려와 양을 맨스필드로 데려갈 거예요. 이게 얼마나 손쉽고 즐겁게 이뤄질지 양도 알잖아요. 그 자리에서 무엇이 느껴질지 양도 다 알고요.”\n\n패니는 그에게 고마워했으나, 그것을 웃어넘기려 했다.\n\n“나는 아주 진지해요,” 그가 대꾸했다. “양도 아주 잘 알듯이. 그리고 나는 양이 몸이 안 좋아지는 낌새를 모질게 감추지 않기를 바라요. 정말이지, 그리해선 안 돼요. 그건 양의 힘에 닿는 일이 되지 못할 겁니다. 양이 메리한테 보내는 편지마다 대놓고 ‘나는 잘 있어요’ 하는 동안에만—그리고 나는 양이 거짓을 말하거나 쓸 수 없음을 아니까요—오직 그동안에만 양이 잘 있다 여겨질 테니까요.”\n\n패니는 다시 그에게 고마워했으나, 어찌나 마음이 움직이고 괴로웠던지 많은 말을 하기도, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 가늠하기조차 어림없었다. 이것은 그들 산책의 끝머리 무렵이었다. 그는 마지막까지 그들에게 시중을 들다가, 그들이 만찬을 들러 간다는 것을 알자, 어디 다른 데서 저를 기다린다는 척하며, 그들 집 문간에서야 그들과 헤어졌다.\n\n“양이 그토록 지치지 않았으면 싶어요,” 다른 이들이 다 집에 든 뒤에도 여전히 패니를 붙들며 그가 말했다. “양을 더 튼튼한 채로 두고 가면 싶고요. 시내에서 양을 위해 뭐 해 줄 게 없나요? 나는 머잖아 다시 노퍽에 갈까 하는 마음이 절반쯤 있어요. 매디슨이 못 미더워서요. 그자는 여전히 되도록 나를 속여서 제 사촌을 어느 방앗간에 앉히려는 게 분명하거든요, 내가 딴 사람한테 주려는 방앗간에 말이에요. 나는 그자와 담판을 지어야 해요. 나는 에버링엄 북쪽에서든 남쪽에서든 결코 속아 넘어가지 않으리라는 걸, 내가 내 재산의 주인이 되리라는 걸 그자한테 알려야 해요. 앞서 나는 그자한테 충분히 딱 잘라 말하지 못했어요. 그런 자가 영지에서 저지르는 해악은, 그 주인의 신망으로 보나 가난한 이의 안녕으로 보나 이루 헤아릴 수 없어요. 나는 곧장 노퍽으로 돌아가, 나중에 어긋날 수 없는 발판 위에 온갖 것을 대번에 올려놓고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같아요. 매디슨은 영리한 자예요. 나는 그자가 나를 밀어내려 들지만 않는다면 그자를 밀어낼 마음은 없어요. 하지만 나를 속일 채권자의 권리도 없는 자한테 속아 넘어가는 건 어리석은 짓이고, 이미 절반쯤 약속을 준 정직한 사내 대신, 그자가 나한테 모질고 인색한 자를 세입자로 안기게 놔두는 건 어리석음보다 더한 짓이죠. 어리석음보다 더한 짓 아니겠어요? 갈까요? 그리하라 권하시겠어요?”\n\n“제가 권하다니요! 크로퍼드 씨야말로 뭐가 옳은지 잘 아시잖아요.”\n\n“그럼요. 양이 내게 의견을 주면, 나는 늘 뭐가 옳은지 알거든요. 양의 판단이 내 옳음의 잣대예요.”\n\n“어머, 아녜요! 그런 말씀 마세요. 우리 다, 마음만 기울인다면, 다른 누구보다 더 나은 길잡이를 저 자신 안에 지니고 있는걸요. 안녕히 가세요. 내일 즐거운 여행 되시길 빌어요.”\n\n“시내에서 양을 위해 해 줄 게 정말 없어요?”\n\n“없어요. 무척 감사드려요.”\n\n“누구한테 전할 말도 없고요?”\n\n“누이한테 제 사랑을 전해 주세요, 그렇게 해 주시면. 그리고 제 사촌을, 사촌 에드먼드를 보시거든, 부디 이내 그이한테서 소식을 들으려니 여긴다고 좀 전해 주시면 좋겠어요.”\n\n“그럼요. 그리고 그가 게으르거나 소홀하거든, 그의 변명은 내가 직접 써 드리죠.”\n\n그는 더 말할 수 없었으니, 패니가 더는 붙들리려 하지 않았던 것이다. 그는 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐고, 그녀를 바라보고, 갔다. 그는 뒤이은 세 시간을 다른 지인들과 함께 되도록 흘려보내러 갔으니, 훌륭한 여관이 내놓을 수 있는 가장 좋은 만찬이 그들이 즐길 채비가 될 때까지였다. 그리고 그녀는 대번에 제 더 소박한 만찬으로 들어갔다.\n\n그들의 여느 끼니는 자못 다른 빛깔이었다. 그리고 그가, 운동 말고도 그녀가 아버지 집에서 얼마나 많은 결핍을 견디는지 짐작할 수 있었더라면, 그녀의 낯빛이 저가 본 것보다 훨씬 더 축나지 않은 것을 의아해했으리라. 그녀는 리베카의 푸딩과 리베카의 해시—죄다 반쯤 닦인 접시와, 절반도 안 닦인 나이프와 포크를 곁들여 식탁에 오르는 그것들을 도무지 감당 못 해, 자못 곧잘 제 가장 든든한 끼니를, 저녁에 오라비들을 시켜 비스킷과 빵을 사 올 수 있을 때까지 미룰밖에 없었다. 맨스필드에서 곱게 길러진 뒤라, 이제 와 포츠머스에서 단련되기엔 너무 늦은 것이다. 그리고 토머스 경이, 온갖 것을 알았더라면, 조카딸이 몸도 마음도 굶주려 크로퍼드 씨의 좋은 벗함과 좋은 운수를 훨씬 더 올바로 값매기게 될 더없이 그럴듯한 길에 들어섰다 여겼을지언정, 그는 아마 그 처방으로 그녀가 죽을까 봐 그 시험을 더 밀어붙이기를 두려워했으리라.\n\n패니는 그날 나머지 내내 기운이 없었다. 크로퍼드 씨를 다시 안 볼 것을 웬만큼 확신하면서도, 그녀는 처지는 것을 어쩔 수 없었다. 그것은 벗의 성질을 띤 누군가와 헤어지는 것이었다. 그리고 한 면으로는 그가 간 것이 반가우면서도, 이제 저가 모두에게 버려진 것만 같았다. 그것은 일종의 새삼스러운 맨스필드와의 헤어짐이었다. 그리고 그가 시내로 돌아가 메리와 에드먼드와 곧잘 함께하리라는 것을, 그런 마음을 품은 저 자신이 미울 만큼 시샘에 아주 가까운 심경 없이는 그녀는 생각할 수 없었다.\n\n그녀의 풀 죽음은 제 둘레에 벌어지는 무엇으로도 덜어지지 않았다. 아버지의 벗 한둘이, 그가 함께 있지 않을 때면 늘 그러듯, 거기서 그 길고 긴 저녁을 보냈다. 그리고 여섯 시부터 아홉 시 반까지, 소란과 그로그가 좀처럼 끊이지 않았다. 그녀는 자못 기운이 없었다. 그녀가 여전히 크로퍼드 씨에게서 그리는 그 놀라운 나아짐이, 그녀 생각의 흐름 안에서 위안을 줄 것에 가장 가까운 것이었다. 저가 방금 얼마나 다른 무리에서 그를 봤는지도, 그것이 얼마나 대조에 힘입은 것일 수 있는지도 헤아리지 않은 채, 그녀는 그가 예전보다 놀랄 만큼 더 상냥하고 남을 더 헤아린다고 아주 믿어 버렸다. 그리고 사소한 것에서 그렇다면, 큰 것에서도 그럴밖에 없지 않겠는가? 그녀의 건강과 편안함을 그토록 애타게 걱정하고, 이제 스스로 나타낸 대로, 또 정말로 그리 보인 대로 그토록 마음 깊은 사람이라면, 그가 그녀에게 그토록 괴로운 그 구애를 더는 오래 밀어붙이지 않으리라 여겨도 온당하지 않겠는가?\n\n# 제43장\n\n크로퍼드 씨가 이튿날 런던으로 되돌아가는 여정에 올랐으려니 여겨졌으니, 프라이스 씨 댁에서 그의 모습이 더는 보이지 않았던 것이다. 그리고 이틀 뒤, 그것은 그 누이에게서 온 다음 편지로 패니에게 확인된 사실이 되었다. 그 편지는 다른 까닭에서 그녀가 더없이 애타는 궁금함으로 뜯어 읽은 것이었다—\n\n“사랑하는 패니, 그쪽한테 알려야겠어요. 헨리가 그쪽을 보러 포츠머스에 내려갔다는 걸요. 지난 토요일 그이가 그쪽하고 조선소까지 즐거운 산책을 했고, 이튿날엔 성벽 위에서 그보다 더 마음에 담아 둘 만한 산책을 했다는 걸요. 그때 그 향긋한 공기와, 반짝이는 바다와, 그쪽의 고운 낯빛과 이야기가 다 함께 더없이 감미로운 어우러짐을 이루어, 되돌아 떠올리기만 해도 황홀을 자아낼 만한 심경을 안겨 주었다는 걸요. 이게, 내가 알아듣기로는, 내 알림의 알맹이가 될 참이에요. 그이가 나더러 쓰게 하는데, 그이가 포츠머스에 다녀간 이 일과, 이 두 번의 산책과, 그이가 그쪽 식구한테 소개된 것—특히 그쪽의 어여쁜 누이, 성벽 위 무리에 낀 열다섯 살 훤한 아가씨, 짐작건대 사랑의 첫 가르침을 받던 그 아가씨한테 소개된 것 말고 달리 뭘 전해야 할지 나는 모르겠어요. 나는 길게 쓸 겨를이 없어요, 하지만 겨를이 있다 해도 어울리지 않을 거예요. 이건 그저 볼일 편지, 미룰 경우 화의 위험이 있는 그 요긴한 알림을 전할 목적으로 쓰인 편지가 될 참이니까요. 사랑하는, 사랑하는 패니, 그쪽이 여기 있다면 내가 얼마나 그쪽한테 얘기할지! 그쪽은 지칠 때까지 내 말을 들어야 하고, 그보다 더 지칠 때까지 나한테 조언해야 할 거예요. 하지만 내 그 대단한 마음의 백분의 일도 종이에 옮기는 건 어림없으니, 나는 아예 삼가고, 그쪽더러 좋을 대로 짐작하게 놔둘게요. 그쪽한테 전할 소식이 없어요. 물론 그쪽엔 정치가 있죠. 그리고 내 시간을 채우는 사람과 무리의 이름으로 그쪽을 성가시게 하는 건 너무 못할 노릇이겠고요. 그쪽 사촌의 첫 모임 얘기를 보냈어야 했는데, 게을렀고 이젠 너무 오래된 얘기예요. 이것만 알면 돼요, 온갖 게 딱 마땅한 대로였고, 그이의 인연이라면 누구든 지켜보며 흐뭇해했을 그런 격이었으며, 그이 자신의 옷차림과 태도가 그이한테 더없는 명예를 안겼다는 걸요. 내 벗 프레이저 부인은 그런 집이라면 사족을 못 쓰는데, 나라도 그런 집이 있으면 비참하진 않겠어요. 나는 부활절 뒤에 스토너웨이 부인한테 가요. 그이는 자못 신나고 무척 행복해 보여요. S 경은 제 식구 사이에선 자못 서글서글하고 유쾌한가 봐요. 그리고 나는 그이가 여기던 만큼 그리 못생겼다곤 여기지 않게 됐어요—적어도, 더 못한 이도 많으니까요. 그이야 그쪽 사촌 에드먼드 곁에선 어림없죠. 방금 말한 그 주인공으로 말하면, 뭐라 할까요? 내가 그이 이름을 아예 피하면 미심쩍어 보일 테니. 그럼 이렇게 말하죠, 우리가 그이를 두세 번 봤고, 여기 내 벗들이 그이의 신사다운 풍채에 자못 감복했다고요. 프레이저 부인은(못 볼 눈이 아니에요) 시내에서 그만큼 좋은 인물과 키와 풍채를 지닌 남자를 셋밖에 모른다 단언해요. 그리고 나도 인정하건대, 요전에 그이가 여기서 만찬을 들었을 때, 그이에 견줄 이가 하나도 없었어요, 우리가 열여섯 명 무리였는데도요. 다행히 요즘은 속내를 드러낼 옷차림의 차등이 없지만, 하지만—하지만—하지만 애정을 담아.”\n\n“깜빡 잊을 뻔했네요(에드먼드 탓이에요, 그이가 내게 이로운 것보다 더 자꾸 머릿속에 떠올라서요) 헨리와 나한테서 전할 자못 요긴한 것 하나를요—우리가 그쪽을 노샘프턴셔로 데려가는 것 말이에요. 사랑하는 어린것, 포츠머스에 남아 그 어여쁜 낯빛을 잃지 말아요. 그 고약한 바닷바람은 아름다움과 건강을 망쳐요. 우리 딱한 숙모는 바다 십 마일 안이면 늘 몸이 안 좋았거든요, 제독이야 물론 결코 안 믿었지만, 나는 정말 그랬다는 걸 알아요. 한 시간 전 통지면 나는 그쪽과 헨리를 위해 대령이에요. 나는 그 계획이 마음에 들 거예요, 그리고 우리가 조금 에둘러, 가는 길에 그쪽한테 에버링엄을 보여 줄 거예요. 어쩌면 그쪽도 런던을 지나 하노버 스퀘어의 세인트 조지 교회 안을 구경하는 걸 마다하지 않겠죠. 다만 그럴 때 그쪽 사촌 에드먼드는 내게서 떼어 놔요. 나는 유혹받고 싶지 않으니까요. 편지가 어찌 이리 긴지! 한마디만 더요. 헨리가, 알고 보니, 그쪽이 찬동하는 무슨 볼일로 다시 노퍽에 갈까 하는 생각이 좀 있대요. 하지만 이건 다음 주 중반 전엔 도무지 허락될 수 없어요. 그러니까, 그이는 14일 전엔 어떻게도 뺄 수 없거든요, 우리가 그날 저녁 모임이 있으니까요. 이런 자리에서 헨리 같은 남자의 값어치란 그쪽이 도무지 짐작도 못 할 것이니, 이루 헤아릴 수 없다는 내 말을 믿을밖에요. 그이가 러시워스 내외를 볼 텐데, 나야 그게 싫지 않다고 인정해요—얼마간 궁금하거든요, 그이도 그렇지 싶고요—그이야 인정 안 하겠지만.”\n\n이것은 못내 죽 훑어보고, 찬찬히 읽고, 곰곰 헤아릴 거리를 잔뜩 주며, 온갖 것을 여느 때보다 더 큰 마음 졸임 속에 남겨 두는 편지였다. 거기서 이끌어 낼 유일한 확실함은, 아직 결판나는 일이 아무것도 없었다는 것이었다. 에드먼드는 아직 말을 안 꺼낸 것이다. 크로퍼드 양이 정말 어떻게 느끼는지, 어떻게 처신하려 하는지, 아니면 제 뜻과 상관없이 또는 그에 거슬러 어떻게 처신할지, 그가 그녀에게 중한 것이 지난번 헤어짐 전과 딱 같은지, 만일 덜해졌다면 더 덜해질 성싶은지 아니면 다시 회복될 성싶은지—이런 것이 끝없는 짐작거리였고, 그날에도 앞으로 올 여러 날에도 아무 결론도 못 낸 채 헤아려질 것이었다. 가장 자주 되돌아오는 생각은, 크로퍼드 양이 런던 생활로 돌아가 저가 식고 흔들렸음을 드러낸 끝에도, 끝내는 그를 단념하기엔 그에게 너무 애착함을 드러내리라는 것이었다. 그녀는 제 마음이 허락하는 것보다 더 야심 차려 애쓰리라. 그녀는 머뭇거리고, 애태우고, 조건을 걸고, 많은 것을 요구하되, 끝내 받아들이리라.\n\n이것이 패니의 가장 잦은 짐작이었다. 시내의 집—그것은, 그녀 생각에, 어림없을 게 틀림없었다. 그러나 크로퍼드 양이 무엇을 요구 안 할지야 알 길이 없었다. 사촌의 앞날이 점점 더 나빠졌다. 그를 두고, 오직 그의 풍채만을 말할 수 있는 여자라니! 이 얼마나 값없는 애착인가! 프레이저 부인의 칭찬에서 힘을 얻다니! 그를 반년이나 가까이 알아 온 그녀가! 패니는 그녀가 부끄러웠다. 오직 크로퍼드 씨와 저 자신에 관한 편지의 그 대목들은, 견주어 보면 그녀를 얕게 건드렸다. 크로퍼드 씨가 14일 전에 노퍽에 가든 뒤에 가든 분명 그녀가 상관할 바 아니었으나, 온갖 것을 헤아려 보면 그녀는 그가 지체 없이 가리라 여겼다. 크로퍼드 양이 그와 러시워스 부인의 만남을 주선하려 애쓴다는 것은, 죄다 그녀의 가장 나쁜 처신의 결이었고, 몹시 야박하고 판단 그른 것이었다. 그러나 그녀는 그가 그런 천한 궁금함에 움직이지 않기를 바랐다. 그는 그런 부추김을 인정하지 않았고, 그 누이는 제 것보다 더 나은 심경을 그에게 인정해 주었어야 했다.\n\n이 편지를 받고 나니 그녀는 앞서보다 더욱, 시내에서 또 한 통의 편지가 오기를 애타게 기다렸다. 그리고 며칠 동안은 그것으로, 온 것과 올지 모를 것으로 하도 마음이 어지러워, 여느 수전과의 책 읽기와 얘기가 자못 뜸해졌다. 그녀는 바라는 만큼 제 마음을 다잡을 수 없었다. 만일 크로퍼드 씨가 사촌한테 전하라던 제 말을 기억한다면, 그가 무슨 일이 있어도 저한테 편지하리라는 것이 자못 그럴싸하다, 더없이 그럴싸하다 그녀는 여겼다. 그것이 그의 여느 친절과 더없이 걸맞을 터였다. 그리고 이 생각을 떨쳐 내기까지, 사나흘이 더 지나도 아무 편지가 안 나타나는 덕에 그것이 차츰 닳아 없어지기까지, 그녀는 더없이 안절부절, 애타는 상태에 있었다.\n\n마침내, 평온 비슷한 것이 뒤이었다. 마음 졸임은 받아들여야 했고, 그것이 저를 지치게 해 쓸모없이 만들도록 놔둬선 안 되었다. 시간이 얼마간 해 주었고, 저 자신의 애씀이 얼마간 더 해 주었다. 그리하여 그녀는 수전에 대한 마음씀을 다시 이어, 다시금 그 안에 같은 관심을 일깨웠다.\n\n수전은 그녀를 무척 좋아하게 되어 갔다. 그리고 패니에게 그토록 강했던 어릴 적의 책에 대한 그 즐거움은 조금도 없고, 앉아서 하는 일이나 앎을 위한 앎에는 훨씬 덜 기우는 성미였으되, 그녀는 무지해 보이지 않으려는 마음이 하도 강해, 좋고 맑은 슬기와 더불어 더없이 마음 쏟고 얻는 것 많고 고마워하는 학생이 되었다. 패니는 그녀의 신탁이었다. 패니의 설명과 소견은 어느 논설이든, 어느 역사의 장이든 더없이 요긴한 보탬이었다. 패니가 옛날 얘기를 들려준 것이 골드스미스의 책장보다 그녀 마음에 더 오래 남았다. 그리고 그녀는 인쇄된 어느 작가의 문체보다 언니의 문체를 더 좋아하는 것으로 언니에게 찬사를 바쳤다. 어릴 적 책 읽는 버릇이 없었던 것이다.\n\n그러나 그들의 이야기가 늘 역사나 도덕만큼 높은 화제인 것은 아니었다. 다른 것들도 저마다의 때가 있었다. 그리고 좀 더 사소한 것 가운데, 맨스필드 파크—그 사람들, 그 태도, 그 즐길 거리, 맨스필드 파크의 방식에 대한 묘사만큼 자주 되돌아오거나 둘 사이에 오래 머무는 것은 없었다. 점잖고 잘 갖춰진 것에 대한 타고난 취향이 있던 수전은 못내 듣고 싶어 했고, 패니는 그토록 사랑하는 화제에 마음 두는 데 저를 내맡길밖에 없었다. 그녀는 그것이 그른 짓이 아니기를 바랐다. 하기야 얼마 뒤, 삼촌 집에서 말해지거나 이뤄진 온갖 것에 대한 수전의 그 자못 큰 감탄과, 노샘프턴셔에 가고픈 간절한 바람은, 채워질 수 없는 심경을 자아냈다며 거의 그녀를 나무라는 듯도 했지만.\n\n딱한 수전도 언니 못지않게 집에 어울리지 못했다. 그리고 패니가 이것을 속속들이 헤아리게 되면서, 저 자신이 포츠머스에서 풀려날 때가 오면 제 행복에 수전을 뒤에 두고 가는 것이 큰 흠이 되리라 느끼기 시작했다. 온갖 좋은 것이 될 만한 그 재능 있는 아이가 그런 손에 남겨져야 한다는 것이 그녀를 점점 더 괴롭혔다. 만일 그녀가 그 애를 청할 집을 갖게 될 성싶다면, 그 얼마나 큰 복이랴! 그리고 만일 그녀가 크로퍼드 씨의 정에 화답하는 것이 있음 직한 일이었다면, 그가 그런 조처를 마다하기는커녕 아주 반길 성싶다는 것이 그녀 자신의 온갖 편안함에 가장 큰 보탬이 되었으리라. 그녀는 그가 정말로 성미 좋은 사람이라 여겼고, 그가 그런 종류의 계획에 더없이 흔쾌히 나서는 것을 그려 볼 수 있었다.\n\n# 제44장\n\n두 달 가운데 일곱 주가 거의 다 갔을 무렵, 그토록 오래 기다리던 그 한 통의 편지, 에드먼드의 편지가 패니의 손에 들어왔다. 뜯어 그 길이를 보며, 그녀는 행복의 낱낱과, 이제 그의 운명의 임자가 된 그 운 좋은 이를 향한 넘치는 사랑과 칭찬에 대한 채비를 했다. 그 내용은 이러했다—\n\n“사랑하는 패니에게—진작 편지 못 한 걸 용서해 다오. 크로퍼드가 네가 내 소식을 듣고 싶어 한다고 일러 줬다만, 나는 런던에서는 도무지 쓸 수가 없더구나. 그리고 네가 내 잠잠함을 헤아려 주려니 스스로를 다독였지. 행복한 몇 줄이라도 보낼 수 있었다면 그것을 아끼지 않았으련만, 그런 종류의 것은 아무것도 내 힘에 닿지 않았어. 나는 떠날 때보다 덜 확신하는 상태로 맨스필드에 돌아왔다. 내 바람은 자못 약해졌어. 너도 아마 이미 알고 있겠지. 크로퍼드 양이 너를 그토록 아끼니, 그녀가 제 심경을 너한테 넉넉히 얘기해 내 심경도 웬만큼 짐작하게 해 주는 게 더없이 자연스러운 일이니까. 하지만 나는 그렇다 해서 내 얘기를 못 하진 않으련다. 너를 향한 우리 둘의 속내가 부딪힐 것 없어. 나는 아무것도 안 물으마. 우리한테 같은 벗이 있다는 것, 그리고 우리 사이에 무슨 불행한 의견의 다름이 있을지언정 너를 사랑하는 데선 우리가 하나라는 그 생각에는 뭔가 위안이 있구나. 지금 형편이 어떤지, 그리고 내 지금 계획이—계획이랄 게 있다면—무엇인지 너한테 얘기하는 게 나한텐 위안이 될 게다. 나는 토요일부터 돌아와 있어. 나는 런던에 세 주 있었고, (런던치고는) 그녀를 자못 자주 봤다. 프레이저네에서 온당히 기대할 만한 온갖 마음씀을 다 받았고. 정말이지 맨스필드에서 같은 어울림을 바라며 간 건 내가 온당치 못했나 보다. 하지만 그건 만남이 뜸해서라기보다 그녀의 태도였어. 정작 봤을 때 그녀가 달랐다면 나야 아무 불평도 안 했겠지만, 맨 처음부터 그녀는 달라져 있었어. 첫 맞이가 내가 바라던 것과 하도 딴판이라, 나는 하마터면 곧장 런던을 다시 떠나기로 마음먹을 뻔했지. 낱낱이 옮길 것도 없다. 너는 그녀 성품의 약한 데를 알 테니, 나를 괴롭히던 그 말과 심경을 짐작할 수 있겠지. 그녀는 자못 들떠 있었고, 저희 나쁜 분별의 온갖 뒷받침을 그녀의 너무 발랄한 마음에 대 주는 이들에 둘러싸여 있었어. 나는 프레이저 부인이 싫다. 그 여자는 냉정하고 허영에 찬 여자로, 온전히 편의로 시집간 데다, 제 혼인이 분명 불행하면서도 그 실망을 판단이나 성미의 흠이나 나이의 안 맞음이 아니라, 결국 저가 여러 지인, 특히 그 언니 스토너웨이 부인보다 덜 넉넉한 탓으로 돌리며, 돈만 되고 야심 차기만 하다면 무엇이든 그 돈 밝히고 야심 찬 것을 굳게 편드는 여자야. 나는 그녀가 그 두 자매와 가깝게 지내는 것을 그녀 삶과 내 삶의 가장 큰 불운으로 여긴다. 그들은 여러 해 그녀를 잘못 이끌어 왔어. 그녀가 그들한테서 떨어질 수만 있다면!—그리고 나는 이따금 그것을 단념하지 않는데, 그 애정이 내가 보기엔 주로 그들 편에 있는 듯하기 때문이야. 그들은 그녀를 무척 아껴. 하지만 나는 그녀가 너를 사랑하는 만큼 그들을 사랑하진 않는다고 확신해. 정말이지 너를 향한 그녀의 그 큰 애착과, 누이로서 보인 그 온전히 사려 깊고 올곧은 처신을 떠올리면, 그녀는 온갖 고귀한 것을 할 수 있는 아주 딴사람으로 보이고, 나는 장난스러운 태도를 너무 모질게 새긴 나 자신을 나무랄 마음마저 든다. 나는 그녀를 단념할 수 없어, 패니. 그녀는 이 세상에서 내가 아내로 여길 수 있는 유일한 여자야. 그녀가 나를 얼마간 마음에 두고 있다 믿지 않았다면 물론 이런 말은 안 했겠지만, 나는 정말 그리 믿는다. 나는 그녀가 뚜렷한 마음이 없진 않다고 확신해. 나는 어떤 한 사람을 두고 질투하는 게 아니야. 내가 질투하는 건 그 유행하는 세상 전체의 물듦이야. 내가 두려운 건 재물의 버릇이고. 그녀의 생각은 제 재산이 감당할 만한 것보다 높진 않아, 다만 우리 수입을 합친 것으로 뒷받침할 수 있는 것을 넘어서지. 하지만 여기에도 위안은 있어. 나는 그녀를 내 직업 탓에 잃느니, 넉넉지 못한 탓에 잃는 걸 차라리 더 잘 견딜 수 있거든. 앞엣것은 그녀의 정이 희생만큼 크지 못함을 증명할 뿐일 텐데, 실은 내가 그런 희생을 청할 자격도 좀처럼 없고. 그리고 만일 내가 거절당한다면, 그게, 내 생각엔, 정직한 이유일 거야. 그녀의 편견은, 나는 믿는데, 예전만큼 세진 않아. 사랑하는 패니, 너는 내 생각을 그 이는 그대로 다 받는 셈이다. 어쩌면 이따금 앞뒤가 안 맞겠지만, 그렇다 해서 내 마음의 덜 참된 그림은 아닐 게야. 일단 시작하고 보니, 내가 느끼는 걸 죄다 너한테 얘기하는 게 나한텐 즐겁구나. 나는 그녀를 단념할 수 없어. 우리가 이미 이어져 있고, 또 그러기를 바라는 마당이니, 메리 크로퍼드를 단념한다는 건 내게 가장 소중한 몇몇의 사귐을 단념하는 것이요, 다른 어떤 괴로움에서든 위안을 얻으러 찾아갈 바로 그 집들과 벗들에게서 나 자신을 쫓아내는 일이 될 게다. 나는 메리를 잃는 것을 크로퍼드와 패니를 잃는 것까지 아우르는 것으로 여길밖에 없어. 그게 결판난 일이라면, 정말 거절이라면, 나는 그것을 견디는 법을, 그리고 그녀가 내 마음을 붙든 힘을 약하게 하려 애쓰는 법을, 그리고 몇 해가 지나면—하지만 나는 실없는 소릴 쓰고 있구나. 거절당한다면 나는 견뎌야겠지. 그리고 거절당하기까지, 나는 결코 그녀를 얻으려는 걸 그칠 수 없다. 이게 진실이야. 남은 물음은 오직 어떻게 하느냐지. 무엇이 가장 그럴싸한 방편일까? 나는 이따금 부활절 뒤 다시 런던에 갈까 생각했고, 이따금 그녀가 맨스필드로 돌아올 때까지 아무것도 안 하기로 마음먹었어. 지금도 그녀는 유월에 맨스필드에 있을 걸 즐겁게 얘기해. 하지만 유월은 아직 멀고, 아무래도 나는 그녀한테 편지를 쓸 것 같다. 나는 편지로 내 뜻을 밝히기로 거의 마음을 정했어. 일찍 확실함에 이르는 게 요긴한 일이니까. 내 지금 처지는 비참하리만치 진력나. 온갖 것을 헤아려 보면, 편지가 뜻을 밝히는 데 단연 가장 좋은 방편일 것 같다. 나는 말로는 못 할 것을 많이 쓸 수 있을 테고, 그녀가 답을 정하기 전에 곰곰 생각할 겨를을 줄 테니까. 그리고 나는 문득 서두르는 충동의 결말보다 곰곰 생각한 결말이 덜 두렵거든. 그런 것 같아. 내 가장 큰 위험은 그녀가 프레이저 부인과 의논하고, 내가 멀리 떨어져 내 편을 돕지 못하는 데 있겠지. 편지란 의논의 온갖 화에 노출되는 것이고, 마음이 온전한 결심에 조금이라도 못 미치는 데선, 조언자가 하필 나쁜 순간에 나중에 뉘우칠 짓을 하도록 이끌 수도 있으니까. 이 일은 조금 더 곰곰 생각해 봐야겠다. 온통 내 걱정거리로만 가득한 이 긴 편지는 패니의 우정마저 지치게 하기에 넉넉하겠지. 크로퍼드를 마지막 본 건 프레이저 부인의 모임에서였어. 나는 그에 대해 보고 듣는 온갖 것에 점점 더 흡족해. 흔들림의 그림자 하나 없어. 그는 제 마음을 속속들이 알고, 제 결심대로 처신해. 값을 매길 수 없는 자질이지. 나는 그와 내 맏누이가 한방에 있는 걸 보며, 언젠가 네가 나한테 한 말을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었어. 그리고 인정하건대, 둘은 벗으로 만나진 않더구나. 그녀 편에 뚜렷한 냉랭함이 있었어. 둘은 좀처럼 말을 안 했지. 나는 그가 놀라 물러서는 걸 봤고, 러시워스 부인이 예전에 버트럼 양한테 있었으려니 여기는 무슨 무시를 원망한다는 게 나는 서운했다. 너는 아내로서 마리아가 얼마나 편안한지 내 생각을 듣고 싶겠지. 불행해 보이는 데는 없어. 나는 둘이 제법 잘 지내기를 바란다. 나는 윔폴 스트리트에서 두 번 만찬을 들었고, 더 자주 갈 수도 있었지만, 러시워스와 처남으로 함께 있는 건 무안한 노릇이야. 줄리아는 런던을 몹시 즐기는 듯하고. 나는 거기서 즐거움이 별로 없었는데, 여기선 더 없구나. 우리는 발랄한 무리가 못 돼. 네가 무척 아쉬워. 나는 이루 말할 수 없이 너를 그리워한다. 어머니가 더없는 사랑을 전하시며, 이내 네 소식을 듣기를 바라셔. 어머니는 거의 매시간 네 얘기를 하시고, 앞으로 몇 주나 더 너 없이 지내실 성싶은지를 아니 나도 서글프구나. 아버지가 몸소 너를 데리러 갈 참이지만, 그건 부활절 뒤, 아버지가 시내에 볼일이 있을 때라야 될 거야. 너는 포츠머스에서 행복하기를 바란다만, 이건 해마다 하는 방문이 되어선 안 돼. 나는 손턴 레이시에 대한 네 의견을 듣게 네가 집에 있으면 싶어. 나는 그곳이 언제고 안주인을 갖게 될지 알기 전엔 크게 손볼 마음이 별로 안 나. 아무래도 나는 분명 편지를 쓸 것 같다. 그랜트 부부가 바스로 가는 건 아주 정해졌어. 월요일에 맨스필드를 떠나. 나야 그게 반갑다. 나는 누구한테든 어울릴 만큼 마음이 편치 못하니까. 하지만 네 이모는, 맨스필드 소식의 이런 요긴한 대목이 당신 펜 대신 내 펜에 떨어진 걸 운수 없게 여기시는 눈치야.—언제나 네 것인, 사랑하는 패니.”\n\n“나는 결코, 아니, 나는 분명 다시는 편지를 바라지 않을 거야.” 이것이 이 편지를 다 읽으며 패니가 속으로 한 다짐이었다. “편지가 실망과 슬픔 말고 뭘 가져다준담? 부활절 뒤에나라니! 어떻게 견디지? 게다가 딱한 이모가 매시간 내 얘기를 하신다니!”\n\n패니는 이런 생각의 쏠림을 되도록 다잡았으나, 토머스 경이 이모에게도 저 자신에게도 자못 야박하다는 생각을 떠올리기 반 분 전까지 갔다. 편지의 주된 화제로 말하면, 거기엔 짜증을 다독일 것이 아무것도 없었다. 그녀는 에드먼드에게 언짢음과 노여움이 일 만큼 거의 부아가 났다. “이 미룸엔 좋을 게 없어.” 그녀는 뇌었다. “왜 정하지 않는담? 그이는 눈이 멀었고, 무엇으로도 그 눈을 못 뜨게 해. 그토록 오래 진실을 헛되이 눈앞에 뒀는데도, 무엇으로도 못 하지. 그이는 그녀와 혼인해 가난하고 비참해질 거야. 하느님, 그녀의 물듦이 그이를 어엿하지 못하게 만들지만 말게 하소서!” 그녀는 편지를 다시 훑어보았다. “‘나를 그토록 아끼니!’ 죄다 실없는 소리야. 그녀는 저 자신과 제 오라비 말고는 아무도 사랑 안 해. 그녀의 벗들이 여러 해 그녀를 잘못 이끌었다니! 그녀가 그들을 잘못 이끌었을 가망도 딱 그만큼 크지. 어쩌면 그들 다 서로를 물들여 왔을 테지만, 만일 그녀가 그들을 아끼는 것보다 그들이 그녀를 그토록 더 아낀다면, 그녀는 그들의 아첨 말고는 상처받았을 가망이 덜하지. ‘이 세상에서 그이가 아내로 여길 수 있는 유일한 여자.’ 나는 굳게 믿어. 그건 그이의 온 삶을 다스릴 애착이야. 받아들여지든 거절당하든, 그이 마음은 영영 그녀와 맺어졌어. ‘나는 메리를 잃는 것을 크로퍼드와 패니를 잃는 것까지 아우르는 것으로 여길밖에 없어.’ 에드먼드 오빠, 오빤 나를 몰라요. 오빠가 잇지 않는다면 그 집안들은 결코 이어지지 않을 텐데! 아! 쓰세요, 쓰세요. 당장 끝내 버려요. 이 마음 졸임을 끝내요. 정하고, 매이고, 스스로에게 벌을 내리세요.”\n\n그러나 이런 심경은 너무나 원망에 가까운 것이라 패니의 혼잣말을 오래 이끌지 못했다. 그녀는 이내 한결 누그러지고 서글퍼졌다. 그의 뜨거운 마음, 그의 다정한 말, 그의 속내를 터놓는 대우가 그녀를 세차게 건드렸다. 그는 다들에게 너무 좋기만 한 사람이었다. 요컨대 그것은 무엇을 준대도 그녀가 안 받았을 리 없는 편지, 아무리 값매겨도 모자랄 편지였다. 이것이 그 끝이었다.\n\n할 말은 별로 없으면서 편지 쓰기에 어지간히 빠진 이라면 누구나—적어도 여자들 세상의 큰 몫이 여기 들 텐데—버트럼 부인이, 그랜트 부부가 바스로 가는 것이 확실하다는 그런 요긴한 맨스필드 소식 한 대목이 하필 저가 그것을 써먹을 수 없는 때에 벌어진 것을 운수 없게 여긴 심정에 공감할 게 틀림없다. 그리고 그것이, 저 자신의 한 쪽의 가장 큰 부분에 펼쳐 쓸 거리가 되는 대신, 그 고마움 모르는 아들의 몫으로 떨어져 긴 편지 끝에 되도록 짤막하게 다뤄지는 것을 보는 것이 그녀에게 자못 무안한 노릇이었으리라는 것도 인정하리라. 버트럼 부인은 편지 쓰는 방면에서 오히려 좀 빛났으니, 혼인 초에, 달리 할 일이 없고 토머스 경이 의회에 있던 사정으로, 편지 벗을 만들고 지키는 데 들어, 저 나름의 자못 어엿하고 판에 박힌, 부풀리는 문체를 지어 내, 아주 자그마한 거리만으로도 넉넉했던 것이다. 그래도 그녀는 아무 거리 없이는 도무지 못 했다. 조카딸에게조차 쓸 무언가가 있어야 했다. 그런데 이내 그랜트 박사의 통풍 증세와 그랜트 부인의 아침 방문이라는 그 온갖 덕을 잃게 될 참이라, 그것들에 써먹을 수 있는 마지막 편지용 쓰임 하나를 빼앗기는 것이 그녀에겐 자못 딱한 노릇이었다.\n\n그러나 그녀에게는 넉넉한 보상이 마련되고 있었다. 버트럼 부인의 운수 좋은 때가 온 것이다. 에드먼드의 편지를 받은 지 며칠 안 되어, 패니는 이모에게서 이렇게 시작하는 편지를 한 통 받았다—\n\n“사랑하는 패니에게—나는 자못 놀라운 소식을 전하려 펜을 든다. 그게 너한테 큰 걱정을 안기리라는 걸 나는 의심치 않는다.”\n\n이것은 그랜트 부부가 잡은 여정의 온갖 낱낱을 알리려 펜을 드는 것보다 훨씬 나았다. 지금 소식은 앞으로 여러 날 펜에 일거리를 약속하는 성질의 것이었으니, 다름 아닌 맏아들의 위중한 병이었고, 몇 시간 전 속달로 그 기별을 받은 것이었다.\n\n톰은 젊은이 무리와 함께 런던에서 뉴마켓으로 갔는데, 거기서 소홀히 넘긴 낙마와 자못 많은 술이 열병을 불러온 것이다. 그리고 무리가 흩어질 때, 움직일 수 없던 터라, 이 젊은이들 가운데 하나의 집에 홀로 남겨져, 병과 외로움의 그 편안함과, 오직 하인들의 시중에 내맡겨졌다. 그때 바란 대로 이내 나아 벗들을 뒤따르기는커녕, 그의 병은 자못 깊어졌고, 오래지 않아 스스로도 몹시 위중하다 여겨, 제 의사 못지않게 맨스필드로 편지를 부칠 채비가 되었다.\n\n“이 괴로운 소식은, 너도 짐작하다시피,” 마님이 그 알맹이를 전한 뒤 뇌었다. “우리를 몹시 마음 졸이게 했다. 그리고 우리는 그 딱한 병자 걱정에 크게 놀라고 두려워하지 않을 수 없구나. 토머스 경은 그 애 상태가 자못 위태로울까 두려워하신다. 그리고 에드먼드가 친절하게도 당장 제 형을 보살피러 가겠다는데, 그래도 나는 이 말을 기쁘게 덧붙이마, 토머스 경은 이 괴로운 때에 나를 두고 떠나진 않으신다는 걸, 그게 나한텐 너무 힘든 일일 테니까. 우리 자그마한 무리에서 에드먼드가 몹시 아쉽겠지만, 나는 그 애가 그 딱한 병자를 두려워하던 것보단 덜 놀라운 상태로 보게 되기를, 그리고 이내 그 애를 맨스필드로 데려올 수 있기를 믿고 바란다. 토머스 경도 그리하자 하시고, 어느 모로 보나 그게 낫다 여기시고, 나도 그 딱한 이가 이내 큰 불편이나 해 없이 그 옮김을 견딜 수 있으려니 스스로 좋게 여기고 있어. 사랑하는 패니, 이 괴로운 사정에 네가 우리를 안쓰러워하리라는 걸 나는 거의 의심치 않으니, 이내 다시 쓰마.”\n\n이 일에 대한 패니의 심경은 정말이지 이모의 글투보다 훨씬 더 뜨겁고 참되었다. 그녀는 그들 모두를 진심으로 안쓰러워했다. 톰이 위중하고, 에드먼드가 그를 보살피러 가고, 서글피 단출해진 무리가 맨스필드에 남았다는 것이, 다른 온갖 걱정을, 아니 거의 온갖 걱정을 몰아낼 만한 근심거리였다. 그녀는 겨우, 이 부름이 오기 전에 에드먼드가 크로퍼드 양에게 편지를 썼을지 궁금해할 만큼의 이기심을 낼 수 있었으나, 순전히 정겹고 사심 없이 애타는 것이 아닌 심경은 무엇도 그녀에게 오래 머물지 않았다. 이모는 그녀를 소홀히 하지 않았다. 몇 번이고 다시 썼다. 그들은 에드먼드에게서 잦은 소식을 받고 있었고, 그 소식은 꼬박꼬박 패니에게로 전해졌으니, 같은 장황한 문체로, 같은 믿음과 바람과 두려움의 뒤범벅으로, 죄다 되는대로 서로 뒤따르고 서로를 낳으며 전해졌다. 그것은 일종의 겁먹은 척 놀이였다. 버트럼 부인이 못 본 그 고통은 그녀의 상상에 별 힘을 못 미쳤다. 그리하여 그녀는 마음 졸임과 애탐과 딱한 병자를 두고 자못 편안히 써 내려갔으니, 톰이 정말로 맨스필드로 옮겨져 저 자신의 눈이 그 달라진 행색을 보기까지는 그러했다. 그러자 앞서 패니에게 쓰려고 채비하던 편지가 다른 문체로, 참된 심경과 놀라움의 말로 마무리되었다. 그러자 그녀는 말하듯 썼다. “그 애가 방금 왔다, 사랑하는 패니, 그리고 위층으로 옮겨졌어. 그런데 그 애를 보니 나는 하도 놀라 어찌할 바를 모르겠구나. 그 애가 무척 아팠던 게 틀림없어. 딱한 톰! 나는 그 애가 자못 서럽고, 몹시 두렵다. 토머스 경도 그러시고. 그리고 네가 여기 있어 나를 위로해 준다면 얼마나 좋을까. 하지만 토머스 경은 그 애가 내일이면 나아지리라 바라시고, 우리가 그 애 여정을 헤아려 봐야 한다 하신다.”\n\n이제 어미의 가슴에 깨어난 그 참된 근심은 이내 지나가지 않았다. 톰이 맨스필드로 옮겨져, 끊이지 않는 건강 속에선 별로 생각지 않던 집과 식구의 그 편안함을 누리기를 몹시도 조바심 낸 것이, 아마 그를 너무 일찍 그리로 옮기게 만들었으리라. 열병이 다시 도져, 한 주 동안 그는 여느 때보다 더 놀라운 상태에 있었던 것이다. 다들 자못 심각하게 겁먹었다. 버트럼 부인은 나날의 두려움을 조카딸에게 써 보냈고, 조카딸은 이제 편지에 기대어 산다 해도 좋을 지경이라, 온 시간을 오늘의 편지에 괴로워하는 것과 내일의 편지를 손꼽아 기다리는 것 사이에서 보냈다. 맏사촌에게 이렇다 할 정이 있는 것도 아니었으나, 마음의 애틋함이 그녀로 하여금 그를 잃을 수 없다 느끼게 했고, 그의 삶이 (겉보기에) 얼마나 쓸모없고 얼마나 자기 부정 없는 것이었나를 헤아리면 그 지조의 순수함이 한층 더 예리한 근심을 더했다.\n\n수전이, 이 일에서도 좀 더 예사로운 일에서와 마찬가지로, 그녀의 유일한 동무이자 들을 이였다. 수전은 늘 기꺼이 들어 주고 함께 안타까워했다. 백 마일도 넘게 떨어진 집안의 병이라는 그토록 먼 화에 관심을 둘 이는 달리 아무도 없었다. 프라이스 부인조차, 딸이 손에 편지를 든 것을 보면 짤막한 물음 한둘과, 이따금 “딱한 버트럼 언니가 큰 시름에 잠겼겠구나” 하는 잔잔한 소감 말고는 그러지 않았다.\n\n그토록 오래 갈라지고 그토록 다르게 처한 터라, 핏줄의 정이란 아무것도 아닌 것이나 다름없었다. 애초에 그들 성미만큼이나 잔잔하던 애착이 이제 한낱 이름이 되어 있었다. 프라이스 부인이 버트럼 부인을 위해 한 것은 버트럼 부인이 프라이스 부인을 위해 했을 만큼과 딱 같았다. 프라이스네 서넛쯤은 쓸려 갔을지도, 패니와 윌리엄만 빼고 누구든 아니 죄다 그랬을지도 모르나, 버트럼 부인은 그것을 별로 마음에 두지 않았으리라. 아니 어쩌면, 그들이 그렇게 잘 건사되었으니 딱한 소중한 동생 프라이스한테는 자못 다행한 일이요 큰 복이라는 노리스 부인의 입버릇을 옮겨 받았을지도 모른다.\n\n# 제45장\n\n맨스필드로 돌아온 지 한 주쯤 되었을 무렵, 톰의 당장의 위험은 지나갔고, 어머니를 아주 놓이게 할 만큼 그는 웬만큼 안전하다고 판정되었다. 이제 그의 그 앓는 무력한 모습을 보는 데 익고, 가장 좋은 소식만 듣고, 들은 것 너머로는 결코 생각지 않으며, 놀랄 기질도 없고 넌지시 비침을 알아챌 눈치도 없던 버트럼 부인은, 얼마간의 의술적 눈속임에 세상 둘도 없이 알맞은 대상이었던 것이다. 열이 잡혔다. 열이 그의 병이었다. 그러니 물론 그는 이내 다시 나을 터였다. 버트럼 부인은 그보다 못한 것을 도무지 생각할 수 없었고, 패니도 이모의 안심을 함께 나누었다—에드먼드에게서, 그 형의 처지에 대한 더 또렷한 그림을 주고, 열이 물러갈 제 몸을 사로잡는 듯한 어떤 심한 소모열 증세에 대해 그와 그 아버지가 의사에게서 얻은 그 두려움을 그녀에게 알리려 일부러 쓴 몇 줄을 받기까지는. 그들은, 근거 없는 것으로 드러나기를 바라야 할 그런 놀라움으로 버트럼 부인을 시달리게 하지 않는 것이 가장 낫다 여겼다. 그러나 패니가 진실을 몰라야 할 까닭은 없었다. 그들은 그의 폐를 두려워했다.\n\n에드먼드의 아주 몇 줄이, 버트럼 부인의 온갖 종이보다 더 올바르고 더 세찬 빛으로 그 병자와 병실을 그녀에게 보여 주었다. 집 안에서, 저 자신보다 눈으로 본 대로 더 잘 그려 낼 수 없을 이가 좀처럼 없었다. 이따금 그 아들에게 저보다 더 쓸모가 되지 않는 이가 하나도 없었다. 그녀야 조용히 미끄러져 들어가 그를 바라보는 것 말고는 아무것도 못 했으리라. 그러나 말을 하거나 말을 걸어지거나 책을 읽어질 만할 때면, 에드먼드가 그가 좋아하는 동무였다. 이모는 그 마음씀으로 그를 성가시게 했고, 토머스 경은 제 말이나 제 목소리를 그 짜증과 힘없음의 눈높이로 낮출 줄을 몰랐다. 에드먼드가 전부였다. 패니는 적어도 그가 그러하리라 분명 믿었을 테고, 그가 앓는 형의 시중꾼이자 떠받침이자 북돋우는 이로 나선 모습을 보면 그에 대한 제 평가가 여느 때보다 더 높아짐을 알밖에 없었다. 도울 것이라곤 근래 병의 그 쇠약함만이 아니었다. 그녀가 이제 알게 된 대로, 자못 상한 신경과, 다독이고 돋워야 할 자못 가라앉은 기운도 있었다. 그리고 그녀 자신의 상상은, 온당히 이끌어야 할 마음도 있으려니 하고 덧붙였다.\n\n그 집안은 폐병 내림이 아니었다. 그리하여 그녀는, 크로퍼드 양을 생각할 때만 빼면, 사촌을 두고 두려워하기보다 바라는 쪽으로 더 기울었다. 그러나 크로퍼드 양은 그녀에게 운수 좋은 아이라는 인상을 주었고, 그녀의 이기심과 허영에는 에드먼드가 외아들이 되는 것이 운수 좋은 일일 터였다.\n\n병실에서마저 그 운 좋은 메리는 잊히지 않았다. 에드먼드의 편지에는 이런 추신이 있었다. “지난번 편지의 그 일에 대해서는, 나는 정말로 편지를 쓰기 시작했다가 톰의 병으로 불려 갔어. 하지만 이제 마음을 바꿔, 벗들의 물듦에 맡기기가 두렵구나. 톰이 나아지면, 내가 가마.”\n\n맨스필드의 형편은 이러했고, 부활절까지 좀처럼 아무 달라짐 없이 그대로 이어졌다. 에드먼드가 어머니의 편지에 이따금 덧붙인 한 줄이 패니의 소식으로 넉넉했다. 톰의 나아짐은 놀랍도록 더뎠다.\n\n올해는 부활절이 유난히 늦게 왔으니, 그 뒤라야 포츠머스를 떠날 낌새가 있음을 처음 알았을 때 패니가 더없이 서글피 헤아린 대로였다. 그것이 왔으되, 그녀는 아직 제 돌아감에 대해서는 아무것도—돌아감에 앞설 런던행에 대해서조차 아무것도 듣지 못했다. 이모는 곧잘 그녀를 바라는 뜻을 밝혔으나, 온갖 것이 걸린 그 삼촌에게서는 아무 기별도, 아무 전갈도 없었다. 그녀는 그가 아직 아들 곁을 못 떠나려니 여겼으나, 그것은 그녀에게 모진, 소름 끼치는 미룸이었다. 사월 말이 다가오고 있었다. 이내 그녀가 그들 모두에게서 떠나 있은 지가 두 달이 아니라 거의 석 달이 될 참이었고, 그녀의 나날이, 그들이 속속들이 헤아려 주기를 바라기엔 그녀가 그들을 너무 사랑하는 그런 고행의 상태로 흘러온 지가 그리될 참이었다. 그런데 그녀를 생각하거나 데리러 올 겨를이 언제 있을지 아직 누가 말할 수 있으랴?\n\n그들과 함께하고픈 그녀의 못내 애타는 마음, 그 조바심, 그 그리움은, 쿠퍼의 「티로키니움」의 한두 행을 영영 그녀 눈앞에 데려올 만한 것이었다. “얼마나 간절한 열망으로 제 집을 그리는가”가 그녀 혀끝에 노상 맴돌았으니, 어떤 학생의 가슴인들 이보다 더 뼈저리게 느끼리라곤 여길 수 없는 그 그리움을 더없이 참되게 그린 것이었다.\n\n포츠머스로 오던 무렵, 그녀는 그것을 제 집이라 부르기를 좋아했고, 집에 간다고 말하기를 즐겼다. 그 낱말이 그녀에게 자못 소중했고, 지금도 여전히 그러했으나, 그것은 맨스필드에 갖다 붙여야 했다. 그곳이 이제 집이었다. 포츠머스는 포츠머스요, 맨스필드가 집이었다. 그녀의 은밀한 상념에 젖어 오래전부터 그렇게 정해져 있었다. 그리고 이모가 같은 말을 쓰는 것을 보는 것보다 그녀에게 더 위안이 되는 것은 없었다. “이 괴로운 때에, 내 기운에 그토록 힘겨운 이때에 네가 집을 떠나 있는 게 나는 자못 서운하다 안 할 수 없구나. 나는 네가 다시는 이토록 오래 집을 떠나 있지 않기를 믿고 바라고 진심으로 빈다”는 그녀에게 더없이 흐뭇한 문장이었다. 그래도 그것은 그녀 혼자만의 즐김이었다. 부모에 대한 섬세함이, 삼촌 집을 이렇게 더 좋아함을 드러내지 않도록 그녀를 조심시켰다. 늘 이랬다. “노샘프턴셔로 돌아가면, 아니 맨스필드로 돌아가면, 저는 이러이러하게 할 거예요.” 오래도록 그러했으나, 마침내 그리움이 더 세져 조심을 뒤엎었고, 그녀는 저도 모르는 새 집에 가면 무엇을 하겠다는 얘기를 하고 있는 자신을 발견했다. 그녀는 저를 나무라며, 얼굴을 붉히고, 두려운 눈으로 아버지와 어머니를 바라보았다. 마음 졸일 것 없었다. 언짢은 기색은커녕 그녀 말을 듣는 기색조차 없었다. 그들은 맨스필드에 대한 어떤 시샘에서도 아주 벗어나 있었다. 그녀가 거기 있고 싶어 하는 것도 거기 있는 것만큼이나 반가운 일이었다.\n\n봄의 온갖 즐거움을 잃는 것이 패니에겐 서글펐다. 그녀는 삼월과 사월을 도시에서 지내며 잃을 즐거움이 저에게 얼마나 있었는지 앞서 몰랐다. 초목이 돋고 자라는 것이 저를 얼마나 기쁘게 했는지 앞서 몰랐다. 그 변덕에도 불구하고 사랑스럽지 않을 수 없는 그 철이 나아가는 것을 지켜보며, 이모 정원의 가장 볕 좋은 자락의 가장 이른 꽃부터 삼촌이 심은 나무들의 잎이 벌어짐과 그 숲의 영광에 이르기까지 그 더해 가는 아름다움을 보며 그녀가 몸과 마음 모두에서 얻어 낸 그 생기라니. 그런 즐거움을 잃는 것은 대수롭잖은 일이 아니었다. 그것을, 갇힘과 소란 한복판에 있는 탓에 잃는다는 것, 자유와 싱그러움과 향기와 푸르름 대신 갇힘과 나쁜 공기와 나쁜 냄새를 안는다는 것은 한없이 더 나빴다. 그러나 이런 아쉬움을 부추기는 것들조차, 가장 좋은 벗들에게 아쉬워지고 있다는 확신에서, 그리고 저를 아쉬워하는 이들에게 쓸모가 되고픈 그리움에서 우러나는 것에 견주면 힘없는 것이었다!\n\n집에 있을 수 있었다면, 그녀는 집 안 온갖 이에게 쓸모가 되었으리라. 다들에게 쓸모가 되었으리라 그녀는 느꼈다. 다들에게 머리나 손의 수고를 얼마간 덜어 주었으리라. 그리고 그것이 그저 버트럼 이모의 기운을 떠받쳐, 이모를 외로움의 화에서, 아니 제 중함을 돋우려 위험을 키우기 십상인 안절부절 참견 많은 동무라는 더 큰 화에서 지켜 주는 것뿐이었대도, 그녀가 거기 있는 것이 두루 좋은 일이었으리라. 그녀는 어떻게 이모에게 책을 읽어 주고, 어떻게 이모와 얘기하고, 있는 것의 복을 느끼게 하는 동시에 있을지 모를 것에 대한 마음의 채비를 시키려 애쓸 수 있었을지, 그리고 얼마나 많은 계단 오르내림을 이모에게 덜어 주고 얼마나 많은 심부름을 대신했을지 그려 보기를 좋아했다.\n\n톰의 누이들이 하필 이런 때에, 이제 저마다 다른 정도의 위험 아래 여러 주째 이어진 병 내내 런던에 남아 있으면서 흡족할 수 있다는 것이 그녀는 놀라웠다. 그들이야 마음만 먹으면 맨스필드로 돌아올 수 있었다. 그들에게 여행이야 어려울 게 없었다. 그런데 어떻게 둘 다 여전히 멀찍이 있을 수 있는지 그녀는 헤아릴 수 없었다. 러시워스 부인이야 무슨 발목 잡는 의무를 떠올릴 수 있다 쳐도, 줄리아는 분명 마음만 먹으면 언제든 런던을 떠날 수 있었다. 이모의 편지 하나로 미루어, 줄리아가 필요하다면 돌아오겠다 한 것 같았으나, 그것이 다였다. 그녀가 있는 데 그대로 남고 싶어 하는 것이 뻔했다.\n\n패니는 런던의 물듦이 온갖 어엿한 애착과 자못 다투는 것이라 여기는 쪽으로 기울었다. 그녀는 그 증거를 사촌들에게서뿐 아니라 크로퍼드 양에게서도 보았다. 에드먼드에 대한 그녀의 애착은 어엿한 것이었고, 그 성품에서 가장 어엿한 대목이었다. 저에 대한 그녀의 우정도 적어도 흠 없는 것이었다. 그런데 이제 그 어느 심경이 어디에 있는가? 패니가 그녀에게서 편지를 받은 지가 하도 오래라, 그토록 마음 두던 그 우정을 대수롭잖게 여길 만한 까닭이 얼마간 있었다. 맨스필드를 통하지 않고서는 크로퍼드 양이나 시내의 다른 인연에 대해 무엇도 못 들은 지가 여러 주였다. 그리하여 그녀는, 크로퍼드 씨가 다시 노퍽에 갔는지 안 갔는지도 만나기 전엔 영영 모를지도 모르고, 그 누이에게서 이 봄엔 다시는 소식을 못 들을지도 모른다 여기기 시작하던 참에, 옛 심경을 되살리고 새 심경을 얼마간 자아내는 다음 편지를 받았다—\n\n“사랑하는 패니, 내 오랜 잠잠함을 되도록 빨리 용서하고, 당장 용서할 수 있는 양 처신해 줘요. 이게 내 얌전한 청이자 기대예요. 그쪽이 하도 착해서, 나는 내가 받을 만한 것보다 더 나은 대접을 받으려니 믿거든요. 그리고 나는 이제 당장의 답을 청하려 쓰는 거예요. 나는 맨스필드 파크의 형편을 알고 싶은데, 그쪽이야 두말할 것 없이 그걸 아주 잘 전할 수 있겠죠. 그들이 처한 그 괴로움을 안쓰러워하지 않는다면 짐승이나 다름없을 거예요. 그리고 내가 듣기로, 딱한 버트럼 씨는 끝내 회복될 가망이 나쁘대요. 나는 처음엔 그이 병을 대수롭잖게 여겼어요. 그이를 무슨 사소한 탈에도 야단법석 떨어 주게 되고 저 스스로도 야단법석 떨 그런 부류로 봤고, 주로 그이를 돌봐야 할 이들이 안쓰러웠거든요. 하지만 이제 그이가 정말 쇠약해지고 있다고, 증세가 더없이 놀랍다고, 적어도 집안의 일부는 그걸 안다고 다들 자신 있게 말해요. 만일 그렇다면, 그쪽도 분명 그 일부에, 그 눈 밝은 일부에 들어 있을 게 틀림없으니, 부디 내가 얼마나 옳게 전해 들은 건지 알려 주기를 청해요. 무슨 착오가 있었다는 걸 들으면 내가 얼마나 기쁠지야 말할 것도 없지만, 그 소문이 하도 파다해서, 솔직히 나는 떨지 않을 수 없어요. 그토록 훤한 젊은이가 한창때에 꺾이다니 더없이 서글픈 일이죠. 딱한 토머스 경이 그걸 끔찍이 느끼실 거예요. 나는 정말 이 일로 자못 마음이 졸여요. 패니, 패니, 그쪽이 웃으며 짓궂은 낯을 하는 게 보이지만, 맹세코 나는 평생 의사한테 뇌물 준 적이 없어요. 딱한 젊은이! 만일 그이가 죽는다면, 세상에 딱한 젊은이가 둘 줄어드는 거고요. 그리고 나는 겁 없는 얼굴과 대담한 목소리로 누구에게든 말하겠어요, 재물과 지체가 그보다 더 받을 만한 손에는 떨어질 수 없다고요. 지난 크리스마스엔 어리석은 성급함이었지만, 며칠의 화쯤은 얼마간 지워질 수 있죠. 니스칠과 금박은 숱한 얼룩을 가리니까요. 그건 그저 이름 뒤의 ‘에스콰이어(Esq.)’를 잃는 것뿐일 테고요. 내 것 같은 참된 정이 있으면, 패니, 더 많은 게 눈감아질 수 있어요. 회신 우편으로 나한테 편지해서, 내 애탐을 헤아려 그것을 가벼이 여기지 말아 줘요. 그쪽이 그 원천에서 얻은 대로, 참된 진실을 나한테 일러 줘요. 그리고 이제, 내 심경이든 그쪽 심경이든 부끄러워하려 애쓰지 말아요. 내 말을 믿어요, 그건 자연스러울 뿐 아니라 박애롭고 덕스러운 거예요. 그쪽 양심에 물어봐요, ‘에드먼드 경’이 다른 어떤 있을 법한 ‘경’보다 버트럼가의 온 재산으로 더 많은 좋은 일을 하지 않겠는지를요. 그랜트 부부가 집에 있었으면 그쪽을 성가시게 안 했겠지만, 이제 진실을 물어볼 이는 그쪽뿐이에요, 그이 누이들은 내 손이 닿는 데 없으니까요. R 부인은 트위크넘의 에일머네에서 부활절을 보내는 중이고(물론 그쪽도 알겠지만) 아직 안 돌아왔어요. 줄리아는 베드퍼드 스퀘어 근처에 사는 사촌들과 있는데, 그 이름과 거리는 잊어버렸네요. 하지만 둘 중 누구에게든 당장 물어볼 수 있다 해도, 나는 여전히 그쪽이 더 좋아요. 그들 다 저희 즐길 거리가 깨지는 게 하도 싫어 진실에 눈감아 온 것 같다는 생각이 들거든요. R 부인의 부활절 휴가야 그리 오래는 안 가겠죠. 두말할 것 없이 그이한텐 온전한 휴가일 테고요. 에일머네는 유쾌한 사람들이에요. 게다가 남편이 없으니 그이야 즐거움밖에 없겠죠. 나는 그이가 남편더러 도리껏 바스로 내려가 제 어머니를 모셔 오게 부추긴 걸 갸륵히 봐요. 하지만 그이하고 그 노부인이 한집에서 어떻게 지낼까요? 헨리는 곁에 없어서, 그이한테서 전할 말은 없어요. 이 병만 아니었으면 에드먼드가 진작 다시 시내에 와 있었으리라 여기지 않아요?—언제나 그쪽의, 메리.”\n\n“막 편지를 접기 시작하는데 헨리가 걸어 들어왔지만, 그이가 이걸 못 부치게 할 소식을 가져오진 않네요. R 부인은 쇠약해지고 있다고 걱정한다는 걸 알아요. 그이가 오늘 아침 그이를 봤거든요. 그이는 오늘 윔폴 스트리트로 돌아가요. 노부인이 왔고요. 자, 그이가 리치먼드에서 며칠 보냈다고 무슨 야릇한 공상으로 마음 졸이진 말아요. 그이는 봄마다 그래요. 그이가 그쪽 말고는 아무도 마음에 안 둔다는 걸 믿어요. 바로 이 순간에도 그이는 그쪽을 보고파 안달이고, 오직 그럴 방편을, 그리고 제 즐거움이 그쪽 즐거움에 보탬이 되게 할 방편을 궁리하는 데만 매여 있어요. 그 증거로, 그이는 포츠머스에서 우리가 그쪽을 집에 데려간다던 그 말을 되풀이해요, 더 못내 애타게요. 그리고 나도 온 넋으로 거기 함께해요. 사랑하는 패니, 당장 편지해서 우리더러 오라고 해 줘요. 그게 우리 다한테 좋을 거예요. 그이하고 나는 목사관에 묵으면 되니까요, 알잖아요, 맨스필드 파크의 우리 벗들한테 폐 끼칠 것도 없고요. 그들 다 다시 보는 게 정말 흐뭇하겠고, 사교가 조금 보태지면 그들한테 한없이 쓸모가 될 거예요. 그리고 그쪽 자신으로 말하면, 거기서 저가 그토록 아쉬워지고 있음을 느낄 게 틀림없으니, 돌아갈 방편이 있는 마당에—그토록 양심 바른 그쪽이—양심상 멀찍이 있을 수 없을 거예요. 나는 헨리의 전할 말을 절반이라도 옮길 겨를도 참을성도 없어요. 그 하나하나의 알맹이가 변함없는 애정이라는 걸로 만족해 줘요.”\n\n이 편지의 대부분에 대한 패니의 역겨움은, 그것을 쓴 이와 사촌 에드먼드를 한데 데려오는 것에 대한 그녀의 극심한 꺼림과 더불어, (그녀가 느끼기에) 그 맺음말의 청을 받아들여도 될지 아닐지를 공평하게 판단할 수 없게 만들었으리라. 저 자신으로만 치면, 그것은 더없이 마음 끌리는 것이었다. 어쩌면 사흘 안에 맨스필드로 옮겨진 자신을 발견한다는 것은 더없는 행복의 그림이었으나, 그런 행복을, 그 심경과 처신에서 지금 이 순간 나무랄 것이 하도 많이 보이는 이들—누이의 그 심경, 오라비의 그 처신, 그녀의 냉정한 야심, 그의 생각 없는 허영—에게 빚진다는 것은 큰 흠이 되었으리라. 그가 여전히 러시워스 부인의 지인, 어쩌면 그 시시덕거리는 상대라니! 그녀는 무안했다. 그녀는 그를 더 좋게 여겨 왔던 것이다. 그러나 다행히, 그녀는 서로 어긋난 마음과 옳음에 대한 미심쩍은 소견 사이에서 재고 정할 처지에 놓이지 않았다. 에드먼드와 메리를 떼어 놓아야 할지 말지를 정할 까닭이 없었다. 그녀에게는 온갖 것을 매듭지어 줄, 갖다 댈 규칙이 있었던 것이다. 삼촌에 대한 경외와, 그에게 주제넘게 구는 데 대한 두려움이, 저가 무엇을 해야 할지를 대번에 뚜렷하게 해 주었다. 그녀는 그 제안을 아주 마다해야 했다. 그가 원하면 저를 데리러 보낼 터였다. 그리고 일찍 돌아가겠다고 나서는 것조차, 무엇으로도 좀처럼 정당화될 성싶지 않은 주제넘음이었다. 그녀는 크로퍼드 양에게 고마워하되, 딱 잘라 아니라고 했다. “제가 알기로, 삼촌이 저를 데리러 오실 참이에요. 그리고 사촌의 병이 그토록 여러 주 이어지도록 제가 조금도 필요하다 여겨지지 않았으니, 제 돌아감이 지금으로선 반갑지 않으려니, 제가 짐스럽게 느껴지려니 여길밖에요.”\n\n이때 사촌의 상태에 대한 그녀의 전함은 저 자신이 믿는 그대로였고, 편지 벗의 그 낙천적인 마음에 그녀가 바라는 온갖 것에 대한 바람을 전할 만한 것이려니 그녀가 여기는 그대로였다. 에드먼드는, 보아하니, 얼마간의 재물이라는 조건 아래서라면 성직자인 것을 용서받을 터였다. 그리고 이것이, 그녀가 짐작기에, 그가 그토록 선뜻 스스로 축하하던 그 편견의 극복이라는 것의 전부였다. 그녀는 그저 돈 말고는 아무것도 중하게 여기지 않는 법을 배웠을 뿐이었다.\n\n# 제46장\n\n제 답장이 정말 실망을 전하고 있음을 패니가 의심할 수 없던 터라, 그녀는 크로퍼드 양의 성미를 아는 만큼, 다시 조름받으려니 오히려 기대하는 쪽이었다. 그리고 한 주 동안 두 번째 편지가 안 왔으되, 그것이 정작 왔을 때에도 그녀는 여전히 같은 심경이었다.\n\n그것을 받자, 그녀는 그 안에 글이 별로 없음을 대번에 알 수 있었고, 그것이 서두르는 볼일 편지의 낌새를 띠고 있음을 짐작했다. 그 목적은 의심할 나위 없었다. 그리고 두 순간이면, 그것이 그저 그들이 바로 그날 포츠머스에 오리라는 알림일 뿐이려니 하는 가망을 떠올려, 그런 경우 저가 무엇을 해야 할지 미심쩍어하는 온갖 마음 졸임에 그녀를 빠뜨리기에 넉넉했다. 그러나 두 순간이 어려움으로 에워쌀 수 있다면, 세 번째 순간은 그것을 흩어 버릴 수 있는 법이라, 편지를 뜯기 전에, 크로퍼드 남매가 삼촌에게 청해 그 허락을 얻었을 가망이 그녀를 놓이게 했다. 편지는 이러했다—\n\n“더없이 망측하고 심술궂은 소문이 방금 나한테 이르렀어요. 그래서, 사랑하는 패니, 그게 시골까지 퍼지거든 조금도 그걸 곧이듣지 말라 그쪽한테 일러 두려 쓰는 거예요. 믿어요, 무슨 착오가 있는 거고, 하루 이틀이면 밝혀질 거예요. 아무튼 헨리는 흠 없고, 한순간의 경솔(étourderie)에도 불구하고 그쪽 말고는 아무도 마음에 안 둬요. 이 얘긴 한마디도 마요. 내가 다시 쓸 때까지 아무것도 듣지도, 넘겨짚지도, 소곤거리지도 마요. 나는 이게 죄다 쉬쉬 덮이고, 러시워스의 어리석음 말고는 아무것도 드러나지 않으리라 확신해요. 그들이 떠났다면, 나는 목숨을 걸고 그들이 그저 맨스필드 파크로, 줄리아와 함께 간 것뿐이라 장담해요. 그런데 왜 우리더러 그쪽을 데리러 가게 안 했어요? 그쪽이 그걸 뉘우치지 않으면 싶네요.—그럼 이만.”\n\n패니는 얼이 빠진 채 서 있었다. 아무런 망측하고 심술궂은 소문도 그녀에게 이르지 않은지라, 이 야릇한 편지를 많이 헤아리기란 어림없었다. 그녀는 그것이 윔폴 스트리트와 크로퍼드 씨에 관한 것일 게 틀림없다는 것만 알아챌 수 있었고, 그 방면에서 방금 자못 경솔한 무슨 일이 벌어져 세상의 눈길을 끌고, 크로퍼드 양이 두렵기로는, 저가 그것을 들으면 저의 질투를 돋우려니 하는 것만 넘겨짚을 수 있었다. 크로퍼드 양이 저 때문에 놀랄 것 없었다. 그녀는 그저 그 관련된 이들이, 그리고 그 소문이 그토록 멀리 퍼진다면 맨스필드가 안쓰러울 뿐이었으나, 그러지 않기를 바랐다. 크로퍼드 양의 말로 미루어질 대로 러시워스 내외 자신이 맨스필드로 갔다면, 무슨 언짢은 일이 그들에 앞섰거나, 적어도 무슨 인상을 남겼을 성싶진 않았다.\n\n크로퍼드 씨로 말하면, 그녀는 그것이 그에게 제 성미를 알게 해 주기를, 그가 세상 어떤 한 여자에게도 굳게 애착할 깜냥이 못 됨을 그에게 납득시키기를, 그리하여 그가 저에게 구애하기를 더는 못 밀어붙이도록 부끄럽게 하기를 바랐다.\n\n참 야릇했다! 그녀는 그가 정말로 저를 사랑한다 여기기 시작했고, 저에 대한 그의 정을 여느 것 이상의 무언가로 그려 보기 시작하던 참이었다. 그리고 그 누이도 여전히 그가 다른 누구도 마음에 안 둔다 했다. 그러나 사촌에게 무슨 뚜렷한 마음씀의 드러냄이 있었던 게 틀림없었고, 무슨 큰 경솔함이 있었던 게 틀림없었으니, 그녀의 편지 벗은 사소한 것을 대수로이 여길 부류가 아니었던 까닭이다.\n\n그녀는 크로퍼드 양에게서 다시 소식을 들을 때까지 자못 거북했고, 또 그럴밖에 없었다. 그 편지를 생각에서 몰아내기란 어림없었고, 그것을 어떤 사람에게든 얘기하여 저를 덜 수도 없었다. 크로퍼드 양이 그토록 뜨겁게 은밀함을 조를 것 없었다. 사촌에게 마땅한 것을 헤아리는 그녀의 분별에 기대어도 됐을 것을.\n\n이튿날이 왔으나 두 번째 편지는 오지 않았다. 패니는 실망했다. 그녀는 여전히 아침 내내 딴것은 거의 생각할 수 없었다. 그러나 아버지가 오후에 여느 때처럼 그날 신문을 들고 돌아왔을 때, 그녀는 그런 통로로 무슨 밝힘이 있으리라곤 하도 기대하지 않던 터라 그 화제가 한순간 머리에서 떠나 있었다.\n\n그녀는 딴 상념에 깊이 잠겨 있었다. 그 방에서의 첫 저녁, 아버지와 그 신문에 대한 기억이 그녀를 스쳐 갔다. 이제는 초가 필요 없었다. 해가 아직 지평선 위 한 시간 반 남짓 있었다. 그녀는 정말이지 저가 거기 석 달 있었음을 느꼈다. 그리고 응접실로 세차게 쏟아지는 햇살은, 명랑하게 하기는커녕 그녀를 한층 더 서글프게 했으니, 햇빛이 그녀에겐 도시에서와 시골에서 아주 딴것으로 보였던 까닭이다. 여기서 그 힘은 그저 눈부심일 뿐이었다. 숨 막히고 병약한 눈부심, 안 그랬으면 잠들어 있었을 얼룩과 때를 드러낼 뿐인 눈부심이었다. 도시의 햇빛에는 건강도 명랑함도 없었다. 그녀는 짓누르는 열기의 이글거림 속에, 떠도는 먼지의 구름 속에 앉아 있었고, 그녀의 눈은 그저, 아버지의 머리 자국이 밴 벽에서, 오라비들이 베고 새긴 탁자로 헤맬 수 있을 뿐이었다. 거기엔 결코 속속들이 닦인 적 없는 찻쟁반과, 줄무늬로 훔쳐진 잔과 받침과, 묽은 푸른빛에 티끌이 떠도는 뒤범벅인 우유와, 리베카의 손이 처음 만들어 낸 것보다 시시각각 더 번드르르해지는 버터빵이 놓여 있었다. 아버지는 신문을 읽었고, 어머니는 차가 준비되는 동안 여느 때처럼 해진 양탄자를 두고 한탄하며 리베카가 그걸 좀 기웠으면 했다. 그러다 패니는, 아버지가 어느 대목을 두고 흠흠거리며 곰곰 살핀 뒤 저에게 소리쳐 부르는 데 비로소 정신이 들었다. “시내에 사는 그 대단한 네 사촌 이름이 뭐냐, 팬?”\n\n잠깐 떠올려 그녀는 말할 수 있었다. “러시워스예요, 아버지.”\n\n“그리고 그들이 윔폴 스트리트에 살지 않니?”\n\n“네, 아버지.”\n\n“그럼, 그들 사이에 큰 사달이 났구나, 그게 다야! 자” (그녀에게 신문을 내밀며). “그런 대단한 친척들이 너한테 참 좋기도 하겠다. 토머스 경이 이런 일을 어찌 여길지야 모르겠다만, 그이야 너무 궁정 신하에 점잖은 신사라 제 딸을 덜 좋아하진 못하겠지. 하지만, 이런 제기랄! 그 애가 내 딸이었다면, 나는 지켜설 수 있는 한 밧줄 끝으로 매질을 했을 게다. 남자든 여자든 매질을 좀 하는 게 이런 일을 막는 가장 좋은 길이야.”\n\n패니는 혼자 읽어 내려갔다, “윔폴 스트리트의 R 씨 집안에 혼인상의 불화(fracas)가 있었음을 세상에 알리게 되어 신문은 한없이 마음이 무겁다. 히멘의 명부에 그 이름이 오른 지 얼마 안 된, 그리고 유행하는 세상에서 그토록 빛나는 선도자가 되리라 여겨지던 아리따운 R 부인이, R 씨의 절친한 벗이자 동료인, 널리 알려진 매혹적인 C 씨와 함께 제 남편의 지붕을 떠났으며, 그들이 어디로 갔는지는 신문 편집자조차 알지 못한다.”\n\n“착오예요, 아버지.” 패니가 대번에 말했다. “착오가 틀림없어요. 참일 리 없어요. 다른 누군가를 두고 하는 얘기가 틀림없어요.”\n\n그녀는 부끄러움을 미루려는 본능적인 바람에서 말했고, 절망에서 우러난 결연함으로 말했으니, 저 자신도 믿지 않는, 믿을 수 없는 것을 말했기 때문이다. 읽는 동안 그것은 확신의 충격이었다. 진실이 그녀에게 밀려들었다. 그리고 그녀가 대체 어떻게 조금이라도 말할 수 있었는지, 어떻게 숨이라도 쉴 수 있었는지가 뒷날 저 자신에게 놀라운 일이었다.\n\n프라이스 씨는 그 소문을 하도 대수롭잖게 여겨 그녀에게 이렇다 할 대꾸를 하지 않았다. “죄다 거짓말일 수도 있지.” 그가 인정했다. “하지만 요즘 그토록 많은 대단한 부인들이 그런 식으로 마귀한테 끌려가니, 누구든 장담할 수가 없어.”\n\n“정말이지, 참이 아니면 좋겠어요.” 프라이스 부인이 하소연하듯 말했다. “그렇다면 너무나 망측하잖아요! 그 양탄자를 두고 리베카한테 한 번이라도 말했다면, 분명 적어도 열두 번은 말했을 거예요. 안 그러니, 벳시? 게다가 십 분도 안 걸릴 일인데.”\n\n그런 죄에 대한 확신을 받아들이며, 뒤이을 비참함의 일부를 헤아리기 시작하는, 패니 같은 마음의 그 소름은 좀처럼 그려 낼 수 없다. 처음엔 일종의 얼떨떨함이었다. 그러나 매 순간이 그 소름 끼치는 화에 대한 그녀의 알아챔을 재우쳤다. 그녀는 그 대목이 거짓이라는 것을 의심할 수도, 감히 바랄 수도 없었다. 하도 자주 읽어 한 줄 한 줄 제 것으로 만든 크로퍼드 양의 편지가, 그것과 소름 끼치도록 들어맞았다. 그 오라비를 못내 두둔한 것, 그것이 쉬쉬 덮이기를 바란 것, 그 뻔한 마음 졸임이 죄다 무언가 자못 나쁜 것과 한 결이었다. 그리고 이 으뜸가는 크기의 죄를 대수롭잖게 여기고, 그것을 얼버무리려 애쓰고, 그것이 벌받지 않기를 바랄 수 있는, 됨됨이 있는 여자가 세상에 있다면, 크로퍼드 양이 바로 그 여자려니 그녀는 믿을 수 있었다! 이제 그녀는 누가 떠났는지, 아니 떠났다고 말해지는지에 대한 제 착각을 알 수 있었다. 그것은 러시워스 내외가 아니었다. 그것은 러시워스 부인과 크로퍼드 씨였다.\n\n패니는 저 자신에게, 여태 한 번도 이토록 충격받은 적이 없는 것만 같았다. 쉴 가망이 없었다. 그 저녁은 비참함의 멈춤 없이 지나갔고, 그 밤은 아주 잠 못 이루는 밤이었다. 그녀는 그저 메스꺼움의 심경에서 소름의 몸서리로, 열병의 뜨거운 발작에서 차가운 발작으로 오갈 뿐이었다. 그 일은 하도 충격적이어서, 그녀의 마음이 그것을 어림없는 일로 여겨 거부하는 순간마저 있었으니, 그럴 리 없다 여기던 순간이었다. 시집간 지 겨우 여섯 달 된 여자, 스스로 다른 이에게 헌신한다, 심지어 약혼했다고까지 공언하는 사내, 그 다른 이가 그의 가까운 피붙이, 온 집안, 서로 얽히고설킨 두 집안, 죄다 벗이요 죄다 서로 가까운 사이라니! 그것은, 아주 야만의 상태에 있지 않은 인간의 본성이 저지를 수 있기엔 너무나 소름 끼치는 죄의 뒤엉킴이요, 너무나 흉악한 화의 뒤얽힘이었다! 그러나 그녀의 판단은 그것이 그러하다고 일러 주었다. 그의 그 미덥지 못한 정, 허영과 함께 흔들리는 그 정과, 마리아의 뚜렷한 애착과, 어느 편에도 넉넉한 지조가 없음이 그것을 있음 직하게 했다. 크로퍼드 양의 편지가 그것을 사실로 못 박았다.\n\n그 결말은 무엇일까? 그것이 누구를 안 다치게 할까? 그것이 누구의 뜻에 영향 안 미칠까? 그것이 누구의 평온을 영영 도려내지 않을까? 크로퍼드 양, 저 자신, 에드먼드. 그러나 어쩌면, 그런 땅을 밟는 것은 위험한 일이었다. 그녀는, 만일 그것이 정말 확인된 죄요 세상에 드러남의 문제라면 다들을 에워쌀 그 단순하고 의심할 나위 없는 집안의 비참함에 저를 붙들었다, 아니 붙들려 애썼다. 어머니의 고통, 아버지의 고통. 거기서 그녀는 멈췄다. 줄리아의, 톰의, 에드먼드의. 거기서 한층 더 긴 멈춤이었다. 그것이 가장 소름 끼치게 떨어질 두 사람이 그들이었다. 토머스 경의 어버이로서의 근심과 드높은 명예심과 예절 관념이, 에드먼드의 올곧은 지조와 의심할 줄 모르는 성미와 참된 심경의 굳셈이, 그런 치욕 아래 그들이 목숨과 이성을 지탱하기란 좀처럼 어림없으려니 그녀에게 여겨지게 했다. 그리고 이 세상 하나만 놓고 보면, 러시워스 부인과 피붙이인 이 저마다에게 가장 큰 복은 당장의 없어짐이려니 그녀에게 보였다.\n\n이튿날에도, 그 이튿날에도 그녀의 두려움을 약하게 할 아무 일도 일어나지 않았다. 우편이 두 번 들어왔으나, 세상에든 사사로이든 아무 반박도 가져오지 않았다. 크로퍼드 양의 첫 편지를 풀어 없앨 두 번째 편지가 없었다. 이제 이모에게서 다시 소식을 들을 때가 꽉 찼는데도, 맨스필드에서 아무 기별이 없었다. 이것은 나쁜 조짐이었다. 정말이지 그녀는 마음을 다독일 바람의 그림자조차 좀처럼 없었고, 프라이스 부인 말고는 야박하지 않은 어떤 어머니라도 그냥 지나칠 수 없었을 만큼 그토록 기운 빠지고 파리하고 떨리는 상태로 내몰려 있었는데, 사흘째 되던 날 정말로 그 메스꺼운 문 두드림이 오고, 편지가 다시 그녀의 손에 놓였다. 그것은 런던 소인이 찍혀 있었고, 에드먼드에게서 온 것이었다.\n\n“사랑하는 패니에게—너는 우리의 지금 비참함을 알겠지. 네 몫의 그것 아래서 하느님이 너를 붙들어 주시기를! 우리는 여기 이틀 있었지만, 할 수 있는 게 아무것도 없구나. 그들의 자취를 좇을 수가 없어. 너는 아직 마지막 타격은 못 들었겠지—줄리아의 사랑의 도피 말이야. 그 애는 예이츠와 스코틀랜드로 갔다. 우리가 런던에 들어서기 몇 시간 전에 런던을 떠났어. 다른 어느 때였다면 이건 끔찍이 사무쳤겠지. 지금은 아무것도 아닌 것 같지만, 그래도 무거운 보탬이야. 아버지는 무너지진 않으셨다. 그 이상은 바랄 수 없지. 아버지는 아직 생각하고 처신하실 수 있어. 그리고 나는, 아버지의 뜻으로, 네가 집에 돌아올 것을 청하려 쓴다. 아버지는 어머니를 위해 너를 거기 데려오고 싶어 하셔. 나는 네가 이걸 받은 이튿날 아침 포츠머스에 이를 테고, 네가 맨스필드로 떠날 채비가 돼 있기를 바란다. 아버지는 네가 수전더러 몇 달 함께 가자 청하기를 바라셔. 네 좋을 대로 정하고, 온당한 말을 하렴. 이런 순간에 이런 아버지의 친절을 너는 분명 느낄 게다! 내가 아무리 그 뜻을 어지럽혀 전하더라도, 그 뜻만은 온당히 새겨 줘. 너는 내 지금 상태를 얼마간 그려 볼 수 있겠지. 우리에게 풀려난 화는 끝이 없어. 너는 이른 우편마차로 오는 나를 곧 보게 될 게다.—그럼 이만.”\n\n패니가 이토록 원기 돋울 것을 바란 적이 없었다. 이 편지가 담은 그런 것을 그녀가 느낀 적이 없었다. 내일! 내일 포츠머스를 떠난다니! 그녀는, 그토록 많은 이가 비참한데, 저는 지극히 행복해질 크나큰 위험에 처해 있음을—그렇다고 느꼈다. 그녀에게 그런 좋음을 가져다준 그 화라니! 그녀는 저가 그것에 무뎌지는 법을 배울까 두려웠다. 그토록 이내 떠나게 된 것, 그토록 친절히 부름받은 것, 위안으로 부름받은 것, 게다가 수전을 데려가도 좋다는 허락까지—그것은 죄다, 그녀의 마음을 달아오르게 하고, 한동안 온갖 아픔을 멀리 밀어내는 듯, 그녀가 가장 마음 쓰는 이들의 괴로움조차 걸맞게 나눌 수 없게 만드는 그런 복의 어우러짐이었다. 줄리아의 사랑의 도피는 견주어 보면 그녀에게 별로 영향 못 미쳤다. 그녀는 놀라고 충격받았으나, 그것은 그녀를 사로잡을 수도, 그녀 마음에 머물 수도 없었다. 그녀는 억지로 저를 다잡아 그것을 생각하고, 그것이 끔찍하고 서러운 일임을 인정할밖에 없었으니, 안 그러면 저 자신을 향한 이 부름에 딸린 그 온갖 마음 졸이고 다급하고 기쁜 걱정거리 한복판에서 그것이 그녀에게서 새어 나가곤 했던 것이다.\n\n슬픔을 더는 데는 일거리, 활발하고 없어선 안 될 일거리만 한 것이 없다. 일거리는, 서글픈 일거리마저도 서글픔을 흩어 버릴 수 있으니, 그녀의 할 일은 바람에 찬 것이었다. 할 일이 하도 많아, 러시워스 부인의 그 소름 끼치는 사연(이제 확실함의 마지막 지점에 못 박힌)조차 앞서만큼 그녀에게 영향 미치지 못했다. 그녀는 비참해할 겨를이 없었다. 스물네 시간 안에 그녀는 떠나기를 바라고 있었다. 아버지와 어머니에게 말해야 했고, 수전을 채비시켜야 했고, 온갖 것을 준비해야 했다. 볼일에 볼일이 뒤따랐다. 하루가 도무지 넉넉지 않았다. 게다가 그녀가 나누어 주는 그 행복도—그에 앞서 짤막이 전해야 할 그 시커먼 소식으로도 거의 흐려지지 않는 행복도, 수전이 저와 함께 가는 데 대한 아버지와 어머니의 기쁜 승낙도, 둘의 떠남이 두루 흐뭇하게 여겨지는 듯한 것도, 수전 자신의 황홀함도, 죄다 그녀의 기운을 떠받쳐 주고 있었다.\n\n버트럼가의 괴로움은 그 집안에서 별로 느껴지지 않았다. 프라이스 부인은 몇 분 제 딱한 언니 얘기를 했으나, 리베카가 상자를 죄다 가져가 망쳐 놓은 탓에 수전의 옷을 담을 무언가를 어떻게 찾을까 하는 것이 그녀 생각에 훨씬 더 많이 있었다. 그리고 수전으로 말하면, 이제 뜻밖에 제 마음의 첫 바람이 채워진 데다, 죄지은 이들도 서러워하는 이들도 몸소 아는 바 없으니—처음부터 끝까지 기뻐하기를 억누를 수 있었다면, 그것만으로도 열넷의 인간의 덕에서 바랄 만한 것을 다한 셈이었다.\n\n정말이지 프라이스 부인의 결정이나 리베카의 도움에 맡겨진 것이 아무것도 없던지라, 온갖 것이 이치에 맞게 마땅히 이뤄졌고, 두 아가씨는 이튿날을 위한 채비가 되었다. 여정을 위해 그들을 다잡아 줄 넉넉한 잠이란 어림없었다. 그들을 향해 오고 있는 사촌이, 마음 졸이는 그들의 심경을—하나는 온통 행복이요, 다른 하나는 온통 변덕스럽고 이루 말할 수 없는 어지러움인 그 심경을 좀처럼 찾아들지 않았을 리 없었으니 말이다.\n\n아침 여덟 시에 에드먼드가 집에 들었다. 두 아가씨는 위에서 그가 들어오는 것을 들었고, 패니가 내려갔다. 그가 무엇을 겪고 있을지 아는 채로 그를 당장 본다는 생각이, 그녀 자신의 그 온갖 첫 심경을 되불러 왔다. 그가 그토록 저 가까이에, 그것도 비참함 속에 있다니. 그녀는 응접실에 들어서며 주저앉을 것만 같았다. 그는 혼자였고, 대번에 그녀를 맞았다. 그리고 그녀는, 겨우 또렷한 이 몇 마디만과 함께 그의 가슴에 꼭 안긴 자신을 발견했다. “내 패니, 내 하나뿐인 누이. 이제 내 하나뿐인 위안!” 그녀는 아무 말도 할 수 없었다. 몇 분 동안은 그도 더 말할 수 없었다.\n\n그는 저를 추스르려 돌아섰다. 그리고 다시 말할 때, 그 목소리는 여전히 떨렸으되, 그 태도는 자제하려는 바람과, 더는 그 일을 넌지시 비치지 않으려는 결심을 보였다. “아침 먹었니? 언제 채비되겠어? 수전도 가고?”가 잇달아 재빨리 뒤따른 물음이었다. 그의 큰 목표는 되도록 빨리 떠나는 것이었다. 맨스필드를 헤아리면 시간이 소중했다. 그리고 그 자신의 마음 상태가 그로 하여금 오직 움직임에서만 안도를 찾게 했다. 그가 반 시간 안에 마차를 문으로 대게 하는 것으로 정해졌다. 패니는 그들이 아침을 먹고 반 시간 안에 아주 채비되어 있으리라 장담했다. 그는 이미 먹은 터라 그들의 끼니를 위해 머물기를 마다했다. 그는 성벽을 한 바퀴 돌다가 마차와 함께 그들과 합류하려 했다. 그는 다시 나갔다. 패니에게서마저 벗어나는 것이 반가운 채였다.\n\n그는 자못 아파 보였다. 분명 억누르기로 굳게 마음먹은 세찬 심경 아래 괴로워하고 있었다. 그녀는 그럴밖에 없음을 알았으나, 그것이 그녀에겐 끔찍했다.\n\n마차가 왔다. 그리고 그가 바로 그 순간 다시 집에 들었으니, 마침 식구와 몇 분을 보내고, 딸들이 얼마나 잔잔한 태도로 떠나보내지는지의—다만 그는 아무것도 못 봤지만—증인이 되기에 딱 맞춰서였고, 또 그들이 아침 식탁에 앉는 것을 막기에 딱 맞춰서였다. 그 식탁은, 자못 유별난 부지런함으로, 마차가 문에서 떠날 제 아주 온전히 차려져 있었다. 아버지 집에서의 패니의 마지막 끼니는 그 첫 끼니와 한 결이었으니, 그녀는 맞아들여졌던 것만큼이나 살갑게 그 자리에서 내보내졌다.\n\n포츠머스의 성문을 지날 제 그녀의 마음이 기쁨과 고마움으로 얼마나 벅찼는지, 수전의 얼굴이 얼마나 활짝 웃음을 띠었는지는 쉬 그려 볼 수 있다. 그러나 앞쪽에 앉아 제 보닛에 가려, 그 웃음은 안 보였다.\n\n그 여정은 잠잠한 것이 될 성싶었다. 에드먼드의 깊은 한숨이 곧잘 패니에게 닿았다. 그가 그녀와 단둘이 있었다면, 그의 마음은 온갖 결심에도 불구하고 열렸을 게 틀림없었다. 그러나 수전이 있어 그를 아주 제 속으로 몰아넣었고, 대수롭잖은 화제로 얘기해 보려는 그의 애씀은 결코 오래 이어지지 못했다.\n\n패니는 그침 없는 근심으로 그를 지켜보며, 이따금 그의 눈길과 마주쳐 정겨운 미소를 되살렸고, 그것이 그녀를 위로했다. 그러나 첫날 여정은, 그를 짓누르는 그 일에 대해 그에게서 한마디도 못 들은 채 지나갔다. 이튿날 아침이 조금 더 안겨 주었다. 옥스퍼드를 막 떠나기 전, 수전이 여관에서 한 대식구가 떠나는 것을 못내 지켜보며 창가에 서 있는 동안, 다른 둘은 난롯가에 서 있었다. 그리고 에드먼드는, 패니의 낯빛의 달라짐에 유난히 사무쳐, 그리고 그녀 아버지 집의 나날의 화를 모르는 탓에 그 달라짐의 지나친 몫을—죄다 그 근래의 일 탓으로 돌리며, 그녀의 손을 잡고 나직하나 자못 뜻 어린 투로 말했다. “놀랄 것 없지—너는 사무칠밖에—너는 괴로울밖에. 한때 사랑했던 사내가 어찌 너를 저버릴 수 있단 말이냐! 하지만 너의—너의 정은 저것에 견주면 새것이었지——패니, 나를 생각해 다오!”\n\n그들 여정의 첫 자락은 긴 하루를 차지하며, 거의 나가떨어진 그들을 옥스퍼드에 데려다주었다. 그러나 둘째 자락은 훨씬 이른 시각에 끝났다. 그들은 여느 만찬 시각보다 한참 전에 맨스필드 어귀에 이르렀고, 그 사랑하는 곳에 다가갈 제 두 자매의 마음이 다 얼마간 내려앉았다. 패니는 그런 소름 끼치는 치욕 아래 이모들과 톰을 마주하는 것이 두려워지기 시작했고, 수전은 얼마간의 마음 졸임으로, 제 온갖 가장 좋은 몸가짐이, 여기서 행해지는 것에 대해 근래 얻은 온갖 앎이 막 부려질 참임을 느끼기 시작했다. 좋은 교양과 나쁜 교양의, 옛 상스러움과 새 점잖음의 그림이 그녀 눈앞에 있었고, 그녀는 은 포크와 냅킨과 핑거볼을 자못 곰곰 헤아리고 있었다. 패니는 이월 이래 어디서나 시골의 그 달라짐에 눈떠 있었다. 그러나 그들이 저택에 들어서니 그녀의 알아챔과 즐거움이 더없이 예리한 종류의 것이 되었다. 그녀가 그곳을 떠난 지 석 달, 꼬박 석 달이었고, 그 바뀜은 겨울에서 여름으로였다. 그녀의 눈은 어디서나 더없이 싱그러운 초록의 잔디밭과 나무숲에 닿았다. 그리고 나무들은, 온전히 옷 입지는 않았으되, 더한 아름다움이 코앞에 있음을 알 만한, 그리고 눈에 실제로 많은 것이 주어지면서도 상상에 더 많은 것이 남아 있는 그 흐뭇한 상태에 있었다. 그러나 그녀의 즐거움은 저 혼자만의 것이었다. 에드먼드는 그것을 나눌 수 없었다. 그녀가 그를 바라보니, 그는 여느 때보다 더 깊은 침울함에 잠겨 뒤로 기댄 채, 마치 그 명랑한 경치가 저를 짓누르고 집의 그 사랑스러운 광경을 물리쳐야 하는 양 눈을 감고 있었다.\n\n그것이 그녀를 다시금 서글프게 했다. 그리고 거기서 무엇이 견뎌지고 있을지 아는 것이, 요즘풍에 바람 잘 통하고 자리 좋은 그 집마저도 서글픈 낯빛으로 뒤덮었다.\n\n안에서 괴로워하는 무리 가운데 한 사람은 그녀를 여태 알던 적 없는 그런 못내 애타는 마음으로 기다리고 있었다. 패니가 근엄해 보이는 하인들을 채 지나기도 전에, 버트럼 부인이 그녀를 맞으러 응접실에서 나왔다. 게으른 걸음이 아니게 나와, 그녀 목에 매달리며 말했다. “사랑하는 패니! 이제 나는 편안하겠구나.”\n\n# 제47장\n\n그것은 비참한 무리였으니, 셋이 저마다 저 자신을 가장 비참하다 여겼다. 그러나 노리스 부인은 마리아에게 가장 애착했던지라 정말이지 가장 크게 괴로워하는 이였다. 마리아는 그녀의 첫째가는 귀염둥이, 온갖 이 가운데 가장 소중한 이였다. 그 혼사는, 그녀가 그런 뿌듯한 마음으로 곧잘 느끼고 말하곤 했듯, 저 자신이 꾸민 것이었고, 그것의 이런 결말이 그녀를 거의 무너뜨렸다.\n\n그녀는 달라진 사람이었으니, 잠잠해지고, 얼떨떨해지고, 벌어지는 온갖 것에 무심했다. 아우와 조카와 함께 남겨져 온 집을 제 손에 맡은 것은 아주 헛되이 던져진 이점이었다. 그녀는 지휘하거나 지시할 수도, 심지어 저가 쓸모 있다 여길 수조차 없었던 것이다. 정말로 괴로움에 사무치니 그녀의 활발한 힘이 죄다 마비되었다. 그리하여 버트럼 부인도 톰도 그녀에게서 조금의 떠받침도, 떠받침의 시늉도 받지 못했다. 그녀가 그들을 위해 한 것은 그들이 서로를 위해 한 것 이상이 아니었다. 그들은 다 함께 외롭고, 힘없고, 쓸쓸했다. 그리고 이제 다른 이들의 도착은 그저 비참함에서 그녀의 우위를 굳혀 놓을 뿐이었다. 그녀의 동무들은 놓였으나, 그녀에게는 아무 좋음이 없었다. 에드먼드는 그 형에게 패니가 이모에게 그러한 것에 거의 못지않게 반가웠으나, 노리스 부인은 어느 쪽에서도 위안을 얻기는커녕, 제 노여움의 눈멂 속에 이 일의 악마라 몰아붙일 수도 있었을 그 사람을 보는 데 그저 더 부아만 돋을 뿐이었다. 패니가 크로퍼드 씨를 받아들였다면 이런 일은 일어날 수 없었으리라.\n\n수전 또한 성가심이었다. 그녀는 그녀를 몇 번의 못마땅한 눈길 이상으로 아는 체할 기운이 없었으나, 그녀를 첩자로, 침입자로, 궁색한 조카딸로, 온갖 더없이 밉살스러운 것으로 느꼈다. 다른 이모에게서 수전은 조용한 친절로 맞아졌다. 버트럼 부인은 그녀에게 많은 시간이나 많은 말을 줄 수 없었으나, 그녀를 패니의 누이로서 맨스필드에 응당한 몫이 있다 느껴, 기꺼이 입 맞추고 좋아하려 했다. 그리고 수전은 그 이상으로 흡족했으니, 노리스 이모에게서는 심술 말고는 바랄 게 없음을 아주 잘 알고 온 데다, 하도 행복으로 채워지고, 그 온갖 복 가운데 으뜸인 것—숱한 확실한 화에서 벗어남에 하도 든든해, 다른 이들에게서 받은 것보다 훨씬 더한 무심함에도 맞설 수 있었을 것이기 때문이다.\n\n그녀는 이제 자못 저 혼자에 내맡겨져, 되도록 집과 그 땅과 낯을 트게 되었고, 그리하며 나날을 자못 행복하게 보냈다. 한편 안 그랬으면 그녀를 챙겼을 이들은 갇혀 있거나, 이 무렵 편안함 비슷한 온갖 것을 저에게 온전히 기대는 그 사람에게 저마다 온통 매여 있었다. 에드먼드는 형의 괴로움을 덜려는 애씀에 제 심경을 묻으려 애썼고, 패니는 버트럼 이모에게 몸을 바쳐, 예전의 온갖 소임에 예전 이상의 열의로 돌아가, 저를 그토록 많이 아쉬워하는 듯한 이를 위해서라면 아무리 해도 모자라다 여겼다.\n\n그 소름 끼치는 일을 패니와 함께 얘기하고, 얘기하고 한탄하는 것이 버트럼 부인의 유일한 위안이었다. 귀 기울여지고 참아지며, 그 답례로 친절과 공감의 목소리를 듣는 것이 그녀를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 전부였다. 달리 위로받기란 어림없었다. 그 일은 아무 위안도 허락하지 않았다. 버트럼 부인은 깊이 생각하지 못했으나, 토머스 경에게 이끌려 온갖 중요한 점에서 올바로 생각했다. 그리하여 그녀는 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 그 온갖 흉악함 그대로 보았고, 죄와 불명예를 대수롭잖게 여기려 스스로 애쓰지도, 패니더러 그리 이끌라 요구하지도 않았다.\n\n그녀의 정은 예리하지 않았고, 그 마음은 질기지 않았다. 얼마 뒤 패니는 그녀의 생각을 다른 화제로 돌려 여느 일거리에 얼마간 관심을 되살리는 것이 어림없지 않음을 알았다. 그러나 버트럼 부인이 그 일에 마음이 붙박일 때면 언제나, 그녀는 그것을 오직 한 빛으로만—딸을 잃음과, 결코 씻길 수 없는 치욕을 아우르는 것으로만 볼 수 있었다.\n\n패니는 그녀에게서, 아직 밝혀진 온갖 낱낱을 다 알게 되었다. 이모는 자못 조리 있는 이야기꾼은 아니었으나, 토머스 경에게 오간 몇몇 편지의 도움과, 저 자신이 이미 아는 것과, 웬만큼 짜맞출 수 있는 것으로, 그녀는 이내 그 사연에 딸린 사정을 저가 바라는 만큼 훤히 헤아릴 수 있었다.\n\n러시워스 부인은 부활절 휴가 동안, 마침 가까워진 한 집안과 함께 트위크넘에 가 있었다. 발랄하고 상냥한 태도의 집안, 그리고 아마 거기 걸맞은 품행과 분별의 집안이었으니, 그들 집엔 크로퍼드 씨가 언제든 늘 드나들었던 것이다. 그가 같은 근방에 있었다는 것은 패니도 이미 알았다. 러시워스 씨는 이때 바스에 가 있었으니, 며칠 제 어머니와 지내고 시내로 모셔 오려던 것이었고, 마리아는 이 벗들과 아무런 잡도리 없이, 줄리아조차 없이 있었다. 줄리아가 두세 주 전 윔폴 스트리트를 떠나 토머스 경의 어느 친척을 찾아간 것인데, 그 떠남을 이제 그 부모는 예이츠 씨에 얽힌 무슨 편의를 노린 것으로 돌리는 쪽이었다. 러시워스 내외가 윔폴 스트리트로 돌아온 지 얼마 안 되어, 토머스 경은 런던의 오랜 더없이 각별한 벗에게서 편지를 한 통 받았다. 그는 그 방면에서 그를 놀라게 할 만한 것을 자못 많이 듣고 목격한지라, 토머스 경이 몸소 런던에 와서 딸에게 힘을 써, 이미 그녀를 언짢은 입길에 오르게 하고 분명 러시워스 씨를 거북하게 만들고 있는 그 가까움을 끝내라 권하려 쓴 것이었다.\n\n토머스 경은 그 내용을 맨스필드의 어떤 사람에게도 알리지 않고 이 편지대로 움직이려 채비하던 참에, 같은 벗에게서 속달로 부쳐진 또 한 통이 뒤따랐다. 그때 두 젊은이 사이의 사정이 놓인 그 거의 절망적인 처지를 그에게 알리려는 것이었다. 러시워스 부인이 제 남편의 집을 떠났고, 러시워스 씨가 크게 노하고 괴로워하며 그이(하딩 씨)에게 조언을 구했다는 것, 하딩 씨가 적어도 자못 노골적인 경솔함이 있었으려니 두려워한다는 것이었다. 러시워스 노부인의 하녀가 놀랍게도 위협을 해 왔다. 그는 러시워스 부인의 돌아옴을 바라며 온갖 것을 잠재우려 제 힘닿는 데까지 하고 있었으나, 윔폴 스트리트에서 러시워스 씨 어머니의 영향에 하도 방해받아, 가장 나쁜 결말이 두려워질 만했다.\n\n이 소름 끼치는 소식은 나머지 식구에게 감출 수 없었다. 토머스 경이 떠났고, 에드먼드가 함께 가려 했으며, 나머지는 비참함의 상태에 남겨졌으니, 런던에서 온 다음 편지들을 받은 뒤 뒤따른 것에만 못한 비참함이었다. 그때쯤엔 온갖 것이 바랄 나위 없이 세상에 드러나 있었다. 어머니 러시워스 부인의 하인이 폭로할 힘을 쥐고 있었고, 제 안주인의 뒷받침을 받아 잠재워질 수 없었다. 두 부인은 함께 있은 그 짧은 동안에조차 틀어졌다. 그리고 며느리에 대한 손위 쪽의 그 쓰라림은, 어쩌면 제 아들을 위하는 마음에서만큼이나, 저 자신이 받은 그 대접의 무시에서 우러난 것이었으리라.\n\n그것이 어떠했든, 그녀는 다룰 수 없는 이였다. 그러나 그녀가 덜 고집스럽거나, 늘 마지막에 말한 이에게, 저를 붙들어 가둘 수 있는 이에게 이끌리는 아들에게 무게가 덜했다 해도, 그 일은 여전히 가망 없었으리라. 러시워스 부인이 다시는 나타나지 않았고, 그녀가 어딘가에 크로퍼드 씨와 함께 숨어 있으려니 여길 온갖 까닭이 있었기 때문이다. 그는 바로 그녀가 몸을 감춘 그날, 여행이라도 가는 양 삼촌 집을 떠났던 것이다.\n\n그래도 토머스 경은, 됨됨이의 면에서야 죄다 잃었을망정, 그녀를 찾아내어 더한 악덕에서 낚아채기를 바라며 시내에 얼마간 더 머물렀다.\n\n그의 지금 상태를 패니는 좀처럼 생각을 견딜 수 없었다. 그의 자식 가운데 이때 그에게 비참함의 샘이 아닌 이는 딱 하나뿐이었다. 톰의 병은 누이의 처신이 준 충격으로 자못 깊어졌고, 그 나음이 그로 인해 자못 뒷걸음질 쳤던지라, 버트럼 부인조차 그 달라짐에 사무쳐, 그 온갖 놀라움을 꼬박꼬박 남편에게 부쳐 보냈다. 그리고 줄리아의 사랑의 도피, 곧 런던에 이르러 그를 맞은 그 보탠 타격은, 비록 그 순간엔 그 힘이 무디어졌을망정, 뼈저리게 사무칠 게 틀림없음을 그녀는 알았다. 그녀는 그러함을 보았다. 그의 편지가 그가 그것을 얼마나 개탄하는지를 드러냈다. 어떤 사정에서든 그것은 달갑잖은 인연이었으련만, 그것이 그토록 은밀히 맺어지고, 그것을 이루는 데 그런 때가 골라진 것은, 줄리아의 심경을 더없이 안 좋은 빛으로 놓았고 그 고름의 어리석음을 모질게 더했다. 그는 그것을 나쁜 일이라, 가장 나쁜 식으로, 가장 나쁜 때에 저질러진 것이라 불렀다. 그리고 줄리아가 아직, 악덕보다 어리석음이 그렇듯 마리아보다 더 용서할 만하긴 했으나, 그는 그녀가 밟은 그 걸음을 두고, 훗날 그 언니와 같은 결말의 가장 나쁜 가망을 여는 것으로 여길밖에 없었다. 그녀가 뛰어든 그 무리에 대한 그의 생각이 그러했다.\n\n패니는 그를 더없이 뼈저리게 안쓰러워했다. 그에게는 에드먼드 말고는 아무 위안이 있을 수 없었다. 다른 자식마다 그의 마음을 쥐어뜯을 게 틀림없었다. 저에 대한 그의 언짢음은, 노리스 부인과 다르게 헤아리건대, 이제 사라지려니 그녀는 믿었다. 그녀는 옳았음이 밝혀질 터였다. 크로퍼드 씨가 그를 마다한 그녀의 처신을 온전히 옳다 해 줬을 터였다. 그러나 이것은, 저 자신에게야 더없이 요긴할지언정, 토머스 경에게는 시원찮은 위안일 터였다. 삼촌의 언짢음이 그녀에겐 끔찍했으나, 그녀의 떳떳함이나 그녀의 고마움과 애착이 그를 위해 무엇을 할 수 있으랴? 그가 기댈 곳은 오직 에드먼드뿐일 게 틀림없었다.\n\n그러나 에드먼드가 제 아버지에게 지금 아무 아픔도 안 준다 여긴 것은 그녀의 착각이었다. 그것은 다른 이들이 자아낸 것보다 훨씬 덜 저미는 성질의 것이었으나, 토머스 경은 그의 행복이 제 누이와 벗의 죄에 자못 깊이 얽혀 있다 여기고 있었다. 그가 그로 인해, 의심할 나위 없는 애착과 성공의 큰 가망으로 좇아 오던 그 여자에게서—이 천한 오라비만 빼면 자못 어울리는 인연이었을 그 여자에게서 떼어져야 할 게 틀림없었으니 말이다. 그는, 그들이 시내에 있을 때 에드먼드가 그 온갖 나머지에 더해 제 몫으로 무엇을 겪고 있을지 알고 있었다. 그는 그의 심경을 보았거나 넘겨짚었고, 크로퍼드 양과의 한 번의 만남이 있었으며 에드먼드가 거기서 더한 괴로움만 얻었으려니 여길 만한 까닭이 있던지라, 다른 까닭 못지않게 그 까닭으로도 그를 시내에서 데리고 나오기를 애타게 바라, 그를 시켜 패니를 이모에게 데려가게 했으니, 그들의 안도와 이로움 못지않게 그의 안도와 이로움을 뜻해서였다. 패니는 삼촌의 심경의 속내를 몰랐고, 토머스 경은 크로퍼드 양의 됨됨이의 속내를 몰랐다. 그가 그녀와 제 아들의 대화를 알았더라면, 그녀의 이만 파운드가 사만이었다 해도 그녀가 그의 사람이 되기를 바라지 않았으리라.\n\n에드먼드가 크로퍼드 양과 영영 갈라져야 한다는 것은 패니에게 의심의 여지가 없었다. 그러면서도, 그가 같은 생각임을 알기까지는 저 자신의 확신이 넉넉지 않았다. 그가 그러려니 여겼으나, 그것을 확인받고 싶었다. 만일 그가 이제, 앞서 이따금 그녀에게 너무 벅차던 그 스스럼없음으로 저에게 얘기해 준다면 더없는 위안일 터였다. 그러나 그것은 안 될 일임을 그녀는 알았다. 그녀는 그를 좀처럼 못 봤고, 결코 단둘이서는 못 봤다. 그는 아마 그녀와 단둘이 있는 것을 피했으리라. 무엇을 미루어 알 수 있었으랴? 그의 판단은 이 집안 괴로움에서 제 몫인 그 온갖 남다르고 쓰라린 것을 다 받아들였으되, 그것이 조금이나마 얘기할 화제가 되기엔 너무 뼈저리게 사무친다는 것이었다. 이것이 그의 상태일 게 틀림없었다. 그는 굽혔으되, 그것은 말을 허락하지 않는 고통과 함께였다. 크로퍼드 양의 이름이 다시 그의 입에 오르거나, 그녀가 예전 같은 그런 속내를 터놓는 사귐의 되살아남을 바랄 수 있기까지는, 오래고 오랜 세월이 걸릴 터였다.\n\n정말이지 오래였다. 그들은 목요일에 맨스필드에 이르렀는데, 에드먼드가 그 일을 두고 그녀에게 얘기하기 시작한 것은 일요일 저녁에야였다. 일요일 저녁—비 오는 일요일 저녁, 곧 벗이 곁에 있으면 마음이 열려 온갖 것이 말해질밖에 없는, 온갖 것 가운데 바로 그런 때에 그녀와 함께 앉아, 방엔 그 어머니 말고 아무도 없이—그 어머니는 사무치는 설교를 들은 뒤 울다 잠들었으니—말하지 않기란 어림없었다. 그리하여, 무엇이 먼저였는지 좀처럼 좇을 수 없는 여느 서두와, 만일 그녀가 몇 분 저에게 귀 기울여 준다면 저는 자못 짤막하겠고 결코 다시는 같은 식으로 그녀의 친절을 부리지 않으리라는—되풀이는 두려워 말라는, 아주 금해진 화제가 되리라는 그 여느 다짐과 함께, 그는 저 자신에게 더없이 흥미로운 사정과 심경을, 그 정겨운 공감을 아주 확신하는 이에게 늘어놓는 호사에 들었다.\n\n패니가 어떤 궁금함과 걱정으로, 어떤 아픔과 어떤 기쁨으로 귀 기울였는지, 그의 목소리의 떨림이 어떻게 지켜졌는지, 그리고 그녀 자신의 눈이 어떻게 그가 아닌 다른 무엇에라도 애써 박혔는지는 그려 볼 수 있으리라. 그 서두가 놀라웠다. 그가 크로퍼드 양을 봤다는 것이었다. 그녀를 보라 청받았다는 것이었다. 그는 스토너웨이 부인에게서 들러 달라는 쪽지를 받았고, 그것을 우정의 마지막, 마지막 만남으로 뜻해진 것으로 여기며, 크로퍼드의 누이라면 응당 알았어야 할 그 온갖 부끄러움과 비참함의 심경을 그녀에게 입혀, 그토록 누그러지고 그토록 헌신적인 마음 상태로 그녀에게 갔던지라, 패니의 두려움으로선 그것이 마지막이라는 것이 잠깐 어림없어졌다. 그러나 그가 이야기를 이어 가자, 이 두려움은 걷혔다. 그녀는 그를, 진지한—분명 진지한, 심지어 마음 졸이는 낯빛으로 맞았노라 그가 말했다. 그러나 그가 알아들을 만한 한 문장을 채 말하기도 전에, 그녀가 그 화제를, 저를 충격받게 했노라 그가 인정한 그런 식으로 꺼냈다. “‘시내에 오셨다는 걸 들었어요.’ 그녀가 말했어. ‘당신을 보고 싶었죠. 이 서글픈 일을 함께 얘기해요. 우리 두 피붙이의 어리석음에 견줄 게 뭐가 있겠어요?’ 나는 대답할 수 없었지만, 내 낯빛이 말했으리라 믿는다. 그녀는 나무람받았다 느꼈어. 이따금 어찌나 재빨리 느끼던지! 그녀는 이내 한결 근엄한 낯빛과 목소리로 덧붙였어. ‘당신 누이를 희생시켜 헨리를 두둔하려는 건 아니에요.’ 그렇게 시작했지만, 패니야, 그녀가 어떻게 이어 갔는지는 너한테 옮기기에 맞잖다, 좀처럼 맞잖아. 나는 그녀의 온갖 말을 다 떠올릴 수 없어. 떠올릴 수 있다 해도 거기 마음 두고 싶지 않고. 그 알맹이는 저마다의 어리석음에 대한 큰 노여움이었어. 그녀는, 저가 한 번도 마음 두지 않은 여자한테 끌려, 저가 흠모하던 여자를 잃을밖에 없는 짓을 저지른 제 오라비의 어리석음을 나무랐어. 하지만 그보다 더, 그토록 좋은 처지를 희생하고 그런 어려움에 뛰어든 딱한 마리아의 어리석음을—진작 제 무심함을 뚜렷이 밝힌 사내한테 정말로 사랑받고 있다는 생각에서 그리한 어리석음을 나무랐지. 내가 무엇을 느꼈을지 짐작해 보렴. 어리석음 말고는 더 모진 이름 하나 붙이지 않고서—그 여자를 그토록 스스로, 그토록 거리낌 없이, 그토록 차갑게 도마에 올리는 걸 듣는다는 걸! 아무 꺼림도, 아무 소름도, 아무 여자다운, 뭐랄까, 아무 얌전한 역겨움도 없이! 이게 세상이 하는 짓이야. 패니, 자연이 그토록 넉넉히 갖춰 준 여자를 우리가 대체 어디서 찾겠니? 망가졌어, 망가졌다니까!”\n\n잠깐 헤아린 뒤, 그는 일종의 절망적인 차분함으로 말을 이었다. “너한테 죄다 얘기하고, 그러고 나면 영영 그만하마. 그녀는 그것을 오직 어리석음으로만, 그것도 오직 드러남으로만 못 박힌 어리석음으로만 봤어. 여느 분별의, 조심의 모자람—그가 그녀가 트위크넘에 있는 내내 리치먼드로 내려간 것, 그녀가 저를 하인의 손아귀에 내맡긴 것. 요컨대 그건 들킴이었어—아, 패니! 그녀가 나무란 건 들킴이었지, 죄가 아니었어. 일을 극단으로 몰아 그 오라비로 하여금 더 소중한 온갖 계획을 버리고 그녀와 함께 달아나게 만든 그 경솔함이었단 말이다.”\n\n그가 말을 멈췄다. “그래서,” 패니가 (저더러 말하라는 줄 여기며) 말했다. “뭐라고—뭐라고 하셨어요, 오빠?”\n\n“아무 말도, 알아들을 만한 아무 말도 못 했어. 나는 얼이 빠진 사람 같았지. 그녀는 말을 이었고, 네 얘길 꺼내기 시작했어. 그래, 그때 그녀가 네 얘길 꺼내며, 마땅히 그럴 만하게, 그런—을 잃은 것을 서운해했어. 거기서 그녀는 자못 조리 있게 말했지. 하지만 그녀는 늘 너를 온당히 봐줬어. ‘그이는,’ 그녀가 말했어. ‘다시는 못 볼 그런 여자를 내던진 거예요. 그 여자라면 그이를 붙들었을 거고, 그이를 영영 행복하게 해 줬을 거예요.’ 사랑하는 패니야, 나는 있을 수 있었던—그러나 이제 결코 있을 수 없는 것을 이렇게 되짚음으로써, 너한테 아픔보다 기쁨을 더 주고 있기를 바란다. 내가 잠잠하기를 바라진 않지? 만일 그렇다면 눈길이든 말 한마디든 다오, 그럼 나는 그만하마.”\n\n아무 눈길도 말도 주어지지 않았다.\n\n“하느님 고맙게도,” 그가 말했다. “우리 다 의아해할 만했지만, 아무 속임수도 모르던 그 마음이 괴로워하지 않은 건 아무래도 섭리의 자비로운 정하심이었나 봐. 그녀는 너를 드높은 칭찬과 따뜻한 정으로 얘기했어. 그러나 여기서마저 티가, 얼마간의 악의 섞임이 있었으니, 그 한복판에서 그녀가 이렇게 외칠 수 있었거든. ‘왜 그 여자는 그이를 안 받았을까요? 죄다 그 여자 잘못이에요. 어리석은 애 같으니! 나는 결코 그 애를 용서 못 해요. 마땅히 그래야 할 대로 그이를 받아들였다면, 그들은 지금쯤 혼인을 코앞에 뒀을 테고, 헨리는 너무 행복하고 너무 바빠서 다른 어떤 대상도 바라지 않았을 거예요. 그이는 러시워스 부인과 다시 가까워지려 애쓰지 않았을 거고요. 그건 죄다 예사로운 붙박이 시시덕거림으로, 소더턴과 에버링엄에서의 해마다의 만남으로 끝났을 텐데.’ 이게 있을 수 있는 일이라 믿을 수 있었겠니? 하지만 그 홀림은 깨졌어. 내 눈이 뜨였다.”\n\n“모질어요!” 패니가 말했다. “정말 모질어요. 하필 그런 순간에 명랑함에 젖어, 가벼이 말하다니, 그것도 오빠한테! 아주 모진 짓이에요.”\n\n“모진 짓이라고? 거기선 우리 생각이 다르구나. 아니야, 그녀의 천성은 모질지 않아. 나는 그녀가 내 심경을 다치게 할 뜻이었다곤 여기지 않는다. 그 화는 훨씬 더 깊은 데 있어. 그런 심경이 있다는 것을 그녀가 아주 모르고, 조금도 짐작 못 한다는 데, 그 화제를 그런 식으로 다루는 걸 그녀에게 자연스럽게 만든 그 뒤틀린 마음에 말이다. 그녀는 그저 남들이 말하는 걸 익히 들어 온 대로, 다른 누구든 다 그리 말하리라 여기는 대로 말했을 뿐이야. 그녀의 것은 성미의 흠이 아니야. 그녀는 누구에게든 쓸데없는 아픔을 자진해 주진 않을 거야. 그리고 내가 스스로를 속이는지 몰라도, 나로서는, 내 심경을 위해서라면 그녀가—라고 여길밖에 없어. 그녀의 것은 지조의 흠이야, 패니. 무디어진 섬세함과, 물들고 상한 마음의 흠 말이다. 어쩌면 나한텐 그게 가장 나은지도 모르지, 서운해할 게 그토록 적게 남으니까. 하지만 그렇지도 않아. 나는 그녀를 지금 이리 여겨야 하느니, 차라리 그녀를 잃는 온갖 더한 아픔에 기꺼이 굽히겠어. 나는 그녀한테 그리 말했다.”\n\n“그러셨어요?”\n\n“그래. 그녀를 떠날 때 그리 말했어.”\n\n“얼마나 함께 계셨어요?”\n\n“이십오 분. 자, 그녀는 이어서, 이제 남은 할 일은 둘을 혼인시키는 것이라 말했어. 그녀는 그것을, 패니, 나보다 더 진득한 목소리로 얘기했지.” 그는 말을 이으며 한 번 넘게 멈춰야 했다. “‘우리는 헨리더러 그 여자와 혼인하라 설득해야 해요.’ 그녀가 말했어. ‘그리고 명예로 보나, 저가 패니한테서 영영 스스로를 막아 버린 게 확실한 걸로 보나, 나는 그걸 단념하지 않아요. 패니는 그이가 단념할밖에 없죠. 나는 그이조차 이제 그 애 같은 됨됨이의 여자한테 뜻을 이루기를 바랄 순 없으리라 여기니, 넘을 수 없는 어려움은 없으려니 바라요. 작지 않은 내 영향력이 죄다 그리로 갈 거예요. 그리고 일단 혼인하고, 어엿한 사람들인 제 집안의 온당한 뒷받침을 받으면, 그 여자도 사교계에서 얼마간 제 발판을 되찾을 수 있어요. 어떤 무리에선, 우리도 알다시피, 결코 안 받아들여지겠지만, 좋은 만찬과 큰 모임이 있으면 늘 그 여자와 낯을 트고 반길 이들이 있기 마련이죠. 그리고 이런 점에선 두말할 것 없이 예전보다 더 너그럽고 트인 데가 있고요. 내가 권하는 건, 당신 아버지가 가만히 계시는 거예요. 참견으로 제 일을 그르치게 놔두지 말아요. 일이 제 갈 길로 가게 두라고 설득해요. 만일 그이가 무슨 부산한 애씀으로 그 여자를 헨리의 보호에서 떠나게 만들면, 그이가 그 여자와 혼인할 가망은 그 여자가 그이한테 남는 것보다 훨씬 줄어들어요. 나는 그이가 어떻게 휘둘리기 쉬운지 알거든요. 토머스 경더러 제 명예와 연민에 기대게 놔두면 다 잘 끝날 수도 있어요. 하지만 그이가 딸을 데려가면, 그건 그 으뜸가는 붙듦을 무너뜨리는 거예요.’”\n\n이것을 되풀이하고 나서 에드먼드가 하도 사무쳐하니, 잠잠하나 더없이 애틋한 근심으로 그를 지켜보던 패니는 이 화제를 아예 꺼낸 것이 거의 서러울 지경이었다. 그가 다시 말할 수 있기까지 한참이 걸렸다. 마침내, “자, 패니야,” 그가 말했다. “이제 이내 그만하마. 나는 너한테 그녀가 한 온갖 말의 알맹이를 얘기했다. 나는 겨우 말할 수 있게 되자, 내가 그런 마음 상태로 그 집에 들어와서, 나를 더 괴롭게 할 무슨 일이 생기리라곤 여기지 못했건만, 그녀가 거의 문장마다 더 깊은 상처를 입히고 있었노라 대꾸했어. 우리가 사귀는 동안 나도 곧잘 우리 의견에, 그것도 얼마간 중한 점들에서 얼마간 다름이 있음을 느끼긴 했으나, 그 다름이 그녀가 이제 증명한 그런 것일 수 있으리라곤 내 상상에 떠오르지 않았노라고. 제 오라비와 내 누이가 저지른 그 소름 끼치는 죄(누가 더 크게 꾀었는지야 나는 말하지 않으련다만)를 다루는 그 태도, 곧 그 죄 자체를 두고 말하며 온갖 나무람을 다 하되 옳은 나무람만은 안 하고, 그 나쁜 결말을, 오직 그름에서의 뻔뻔함과 예절 무시로써 무릅쓰거나 눌러 내야 할 것으로만 여기고, 그리고 무엇보다 맨 마지막으로, 이제 내가 그 오라비를 여기는 대로라면 오히려 막아야 할 혼인의 가망을 걸고, 그 죄가 이어지는 걸 순순히 받아들이고 타협하고 눈감으라 우리에게 권한 그 태도—이 모두가 함께, 나는 여태 그녀를 결코 헤아린 적이 없었음을, 그리고 마음에 관한 한, 내가 지난 여러 달 너무 쉬 마음 둔 것은 크로퍼드 양이 아니라 내 상상이 지어낸 이였음을 더없이 서럽게 나를 확신시켰노라고. 어쩌면 나한텐 그게 가장 나은지도 모른다고, 아무튼 이제 나한테서 뜯겨 나갈밖에 없었던 우정과 심경과 바람을 희생하는 데 서운해할 게 덜하니까. 그러면서도, 만일 그녀를 앞서 나한테 비쳤던 그 모습으로 되돌릴 수 있다면, 애틋함과 존경의 권리를 지니고 가기 위해, 나는 헤어짐의 아픔이 아무리 더해져도 한없이 그편이 낫겠노라 인정할밖에, 인정하고 싶노라고. 이게 내가 한 말이야, 그 취지지. 하지만 짐작하다시피, 내가 너한테 되풀이한 것처럼 그리 차분하거나 조리 있게 말하진 못했어. 그녀는 놀랐어, 몹시 놀랐지—놀람 이상이었어. 나는 그녀의 낯빛이 바뀌는 걸 봤다. 그녀는 몹시 붉어졌어. 나는 여러 심경이 뒤섞인 걸 본 것 같았어. 크나큰, 그러나 짧은 몸부림, 진실에 굽히고픈 마음 절반, 부끄러움의 자각 절반이. 하지만 버릇이, 버릇이 이겼어. 그녀는 웃을 수 있었다면 웃었을 거야. 일종의 웃음 비슷한 것과 함께 그녀가 대답했지. ‘참으로 훌륭한 설교로군요, 정말. 지난번 설교의 한 대목이었나요? 이대로라면 당신은 이내 맨스필드와 손턴 레이시의 온갖 사람을 뜯어고치겠어요. 그리고 다음에 당신 소식을 듣거든, 그건 무슨 대단한 감리교 무리의 이름난 설교자로서든, 외국으로 가는 선교사로서든이겠죠.’ 그녀는 아무렇지 않게 말하려 했지만, 보이려던 만큼 아무렇지 않진 않았어. 나는 그저 답으로, 진심으로 그녀가 잘되기를 빈다고, 그녀가 이내 더 올바로 생각하는 법을 배워, 우리 누구나 얻을 수 있는 가장 값진 앎—저 자신과 제 도리에 대한 앎을 괴로움의 가르침에 빚지지 않기를 간절히 바란다고 말하고, 대번에 방을 나섰어. 몇 걸음 갔을 때, 패니야, 뒤에서 문이 열리는 소리가 들렸다. ‘버트럼 씨.’ 그녀가 말했어. 나는 돌아봤지. ‘버트럼 씨.’ 그녀가 미소와 함께 말했어. 하지만 그건 방금 오간 대화엔 안 맞는 미소, 나를 굴복시키려 꾀는 듯한 앙큼하고 장난스러운 미소였어. 적어도 나한텐 그리 보였다. 나는 뿌리쳤어. 뿌리치는 게 그 순간의 충동이었고, 그대로 걸어갔어. 그 뒤로 이따금, 한순간, 돌아가지 않은 걸 서운해한 적도 있지만, 내가 옳았음을 나는 안다. 그리고 우리 사귐의 끝이 그러했어. 그런데 그게 대체 무슨 사귐이었더냐! 내가 얼마나 속았는지! 오라비에게든 누이에게든 똑같이 속았어! 네 참을성이 고맙다, 패니. 이게 더없이 큰 위안이었어. 자, 이제 그만하자.”\n\n그리고 그의 말에 대한 패니의 믿음이 하도 컸던지라, 오 분 동안 그녀는 그들이 정말 그만한 줄 여겼다. 그러나 그러다 그것이 죄다 다시 밀려왔으니, 아니 그와 자못 비슷한 것이 밀려왔으니, 버트럼 부인이 속속들이 깨어나는 것 말고는 그런 대화를 정말로 끝맺을 수 없었다. 그리되기까지, 그들은 오직 크로퍼드 양만을, 그녀가 그를 어떻게 사로잡았는지, 자연이 그녀를 얼마나 흐뭇하게 지어냈는지, 좀 더 일찍 좋은 손에 들었더라면 그녀가 얼마나 훌륭했을지를 얘기해 갔다. 이제 터놓고 말할 수 있게 된 패니는, 톰의 건강 상태가 온전한 화해에 대한 그녀의 바람에 얼마나 몫을 했으려니 여겨질지를 얼마간 넌지시 비침으로써, 그녀의 참된 됨됨이에 대한 그의 앎에 보태는 것이 떳떳함 이상이라 느꼈다. 이것은 달가운 알림이 아니었다. 천성이 한동안 그것에 맞섰다. 그녀의 애착이 좀 더 사심 없는 것이었더라면 훨씬 더 흐뭇했으련만. 그러나 그의 허영은 이치에 맞서 오래 싸울 만큼 세지 않았다. 그는 톰의 병이 그녀에게 영향을 미쳤음을 믿는 데 굽혔으니, 다만 저 자신을 위해 이 위안 어린 생각만은 남겨 두었다—맞서는 버릇의 그 숱한 어긋냄을 헤아리면, 그녀는 분명 바랄 수 있었던 것보다 그를 더 애착했고, 그를 위해 옳은 일을 하는 데 더 가까이 갔었다는 생각을. 패니도 딱 같은 생각이었다. 그리고 그런 실망이 그의 마음에 남길 게 틀림없는 그 오래가는 여파, 그 지워지지 않는 자국에 대한 소견에서도 둘은 아주 뜻이 같았다. 시간이 두말할 것 없이 그의 괴로움을 얼마간 덜어 줄 테지만, 그래도 그것은 그가 결코 아주 이겨 낼 수 없는 그런 종류의 것이었다. 그리고 그가 언제고—할 수 있는 다른 어떤 여자를 만나는 것으로 말하면—그것은 노여움 없이는 입에 담기엔 너무 어림없는 일이었다. 패니의 우정이 그가 매달릴 수 있는 전부였다.\n\n# 제48장\n\n죄와 비참함에는 다른 펜들이 마음을 두게 하자. 나는 그런 밉살스러운 화제는 되도록 빨리 접으련다, 저 자신에게 크게 잘못이 없는 이들을 죄다 웬만한 편안함으로 되돌리고 나머지는 죄다 끝내고 싶어 못내 조바심 나니 말이다.\n\n정말이지 내 패니는, 바로 이때에, 그 온갖 것에도 불구하고 행복했을 게 틀림없음을 나는 흡족히 안다. 그녀는 제 둘레 사람들의 괴로움을 두고 저가 느낀, 아니 느낀다 여긴 그 온갖 것에도 불구하고 행복한 이였을 게 틀림없다. 그녀에게는 스스로 뚫고 나올밖에 없는 즐거움의 샘들이 있었다. 그녀는 맨스필드 파크로 돌아왔고, 쓸모가 되었고, 사랑받았다. 크로퍼드 씨에게서 안전했다. 그리고 토머스 경이 돌아왔을 때, 그녀는 그때의 그 서글픈 마음 상태에서 그가 줄 수 있는 온갖 증거로, 그의 온전한 찬동과 더해진 마음을 얻었다. 이 온갖 것이 그녀를 아무리 행복하게 했을지언정, 그녀는 그중 아무것도 없이도 여전히 행복했으리라, 에드먼드가 더는 크로퍼드 양의 속는 이가 아니었으니 말이다.\n\n정말이지 에드먼드 자신은 행복과는 자못 거리가 멀었다. 그는 실망과 서운함에 괴로워하며, 있었던 것을 슬퍼하고 결코 있을 수 없는 것을 바랐다. 그녀는 그러함을 알았고 안쓰러웠다. 그러나 그것은, 흡족함에 그토록 바탕하고, 안도로 그토록 기울고, 온갖 더없이 소중한 심경과 그토록 어우러진 슬픔이라, 저의 가장 큰 명랑함을 그것과 바꾸어도 좋아하지 않을 이가 드물었으리라.\n\n토머스 경은, 딱한 토머스 경은, 어버이로서, 그리고 어버이로서 제 처신의 잘못을 자각하는 이로서, 가장 오래 괴로워하는 이였다. 그는 그 혼사를 허락하지 말았어야 했다 느꼈다. 딸의 심경이 저에게 넉넉히 알려져 있었으니 그것을 승낙한 데 저의 죄가 있다고, 그리함으로써 저가 옳음을 편의에 희생하고 이기심과 세속적 슬기의 동기에 휘둘렸다고 느꼈다. 이것은 누그러지려면 얼마간의 시간이 걸릴 반성이었다. 그러나 시간은 거의 온갖 것을 해내는 법이라, 러시워스 부인 쪽에서는 그녀가 자아낸 비참함에 대해 위안이 별로 일지 않았을망정, 다른 자식들에게서는 그가 여긴 것보다 더 큰 위안을 찾을 만했다. 줄리아의 혼사는 그가 처음에 여긴 것보다 덜 절망적인 일이 되었다. 그녀는 겸손해져 용서받고 싶어 했다. 그리고 예이츠 씨는, 정말로 집안에 받아들여지고 싶어, 그를 우러러보며 이끌려는 쪽이었다. 그는 자못 진득하지는 못했으나, 덜 실없어지고, 적어도 웬만큼은 가정적이고 조용해지리라는 바람이 있었다. 그리고 아무튼, 그의 재산이 그가 두려워한 것보다 오히려 좀 더 많고 그의 빚이 훨씬 적음을 알게 된 데, 그리고 귀 기울일 값어치가 가장 큰 벗으로 의논받고 대접받는 데 위안이 있었다. 톰에게도 위안이 있었으니, 그는 차츰 건강을 되찾되 예전 버릇의 그 생각 없음과 이기심은 되찾지 않았다. 그는 제 병 덕에 영영 나아졌다. 그는 괴로워했고, 생각하는 법을 배웠으니—여태 한 번도 알지 못한 두 이점이었다. 그리고 윔폴 스트리트의 그 개탄스러운 일—저가 두둔할 수 없는 그 연극의 온갖 위험한 가까움으로 저 자신이 한몫 거들었다 느낀 그 일에서 우러난 자책은, 지각이나 좋은 벗이 모자라지 않은 스물여섯의 나이에, 그 흐뭇한 여파가 오래가는 그런 자국을 그의 마음에 남겼다. 그는 마땅히 되어야 할 사람이 되었다. 제 아버지에게 쓸모가 되고, 진득하고 조용하며, 그저 저 자신만을 위해 살지 않는 사람이.\n\n여기 정말 위안이 있었다! 그리고 토머스 경이 그런 좋음의 샘들에 기댈 수 있게 되기 무섭게, 에드먼드는, 앞서 그에게 아픔을 주던 유일한 점—곧 그의 기운의 나아짐으로 아버지의 편안함에 보태고 있었다. 여름 저녁 내내 패니와 함께 거닐고 나무 아래 앉아 있은 끝에, 그는 제 마음을 자못 잘 다독여 굽히게 하여 다시 웬만큼 명랑해졌다.\n\n이런 것들이, 차츰 토머스 경에게 그 덜어짐을 가져다주어, 잃은 것에 대한 그의 사무침을 무디게 하고, 그를 얼마간 저 자신과 화해시킨 사정이자 바람이었다. 하기야 딸들의 교육에서 저 자신이 저지른 잘못에 대한 확신에서 우러난 그 고통은 결코 아주 없어질 수 없었지만.\n\n너무 늦게야 그는, 마리아와 줄리아가 집에서 늘 겪던 그 아주 정반대인 대우—이모의 지나친 무름과 아첨이 저 자신의 엄함과 끊임없이 대비되던 그것이, 어떤 젊은이의 됨됨이에든 얼마나 안 좋을 게 틀림없는지를 깨달았다. 그는 노리스 부인에게 잘못된 것을 저 자신의 그 반대로 눅이려 여긴 것이 얼마나 그릇된 판단이었는지를 보았다. 그가 딸들더러 제 앞에서 기운을 억누르게 가르쳐 그 참된 성미를 저에게 모르게 만들고, 오직 제 정의 눈멂과 제 칭찬의 지나침으로만 그들을 붙들 수 있던 사람에게 온갖 응석을 부리러 보냄으로써, 그저 그 화를 키웠을 뿐임을 또렷이 보았다.\n\n여기 서러운 잘못된 건사가 있었다. 그러나 그것이 아무리 나빴어도, 그는 차츰 그것이 제 교육 계획에서 가장 끔찍한 잘못은 아니었음을 느끼게 되었다. 무언가가 안에서 모자랐던 게 틀림없었으니, 안 그랬다면 시간이 그 나쁜 여파의 많은 것을 닳아 없앴으리라. 그는 지조가, 살아 움직이는 지조가 모자랐던 게 아닌가 두려웠다. 그들이, 오직 그것만으로 넉넉한 그 도리의 자각으로 제 성향과 성미를 다스리도록 제대로 가르쳐진 적이 없었던 게 아닌가 두려웠다. 그들은 제 신앙을 이론으로야 배웠으되, 그것을 날마다 실천하도록 요구받은 적이 결코 없었다. 그들 젊음의 그 정해진 목표인, 우아함과 교양으로 돋보이는 것은, 그 방면에 아무 쓸모 있는 영향도, 마음에 아무 도덕적 효험도 못 미쳤으리라. 그는 그들이 착하기를 뜻했으나, 그의 마음씀은 성미가 아니라 슬기와 몸가짐으로 쏠렸다. 그리고 자기 부정과 겸손의 필요에 대해서는, 그들이 저희에게 이로울 만한 어떤 입에서도 들어 본 적이 없는 게 아닌가 그는 두려웠다.\n\n이제 그로서는 좀처럼 있을 수 있었으리라 헤아리기도 어려운 그 모자람을 그는 쓰라리게 개탄했다. 애타고 값비싼 교육의 그 온갖 비용과 마음씀에도, 저가 딸들을 그 첫째가는 도리도 헤아리지 못하게, 그리고 저 자신은 그들의 됨됨이와 성미를 알지도 못하게 길렀음을 그는 비참하게 느꼈다.\n\n특히 러시워스 부인의 그 드높은 기개와 세찬 열정은, 오직 그 서글픈 결말에서야 그에게 알려졌다. 그녀는 크로퍼드 씨를 떠나도록 구슬려지지 않았다. 그녀는 그와 혼인하기를 바랐고, 둘은 함께 지냈다—그런 바람이 헛됨을 그녀가 하는 수 없이 확신하기까지, 그리고 그 확신에서 우러난 실망과 비참함이 그녀의 성미를 하도 나쁘게, 그에 대한 그녀의 심경을 미움과 하도 비슷하게 만들어, 한동안 둘이 서로의 벌이 되게 하고, 이내 자진한 헤어짐을 불러오기까지.\n\n그녀는 그와 살면서 패니를 향한 그의 온갖 행복을 무너뜨린 원흉으로 나무람받았고, 그를 떠나며 저가 둘을 갈라놓았다는 것 말고는 더 나은 위안을 가져가지 못했다. 그런 처지의 그런 마음의 비참함에 견줄 것이 무엇이랴?\n\n러시워스 씨는 이혼을 얻어 내는 데 아무 어려움이 없었다. 그리하여, 더 나은 결말이라면 무엇이든 기대할 수 없는 요행의 덕일 만한 그런 사정 아래 맺어진 혼인이 끝났다. 그녀는 그를 업신여기고 다른 이를 사랑했으며, 그도 그러함을 자못 잘 알고 있었다. 어리석음의 그 수모와 이기적 열정의 그 실망은 좀처럼 딱함을 자아낼 수 없다. 그의 벌은 그의 처신을 뒤따랐고, 더 깊은 벌이 그 아내의 더 깊은 죄를 뒤따랐듯이. 그는 그 약속에서 풀려나, 무안하고 불행한 채로 있다가, 어떤 다른 예쁜 처녀가 다시 그를 혼인으로 꾈 수 있을 때까지—그리하여 그 상태에 대한 두 번째의, 그리고 바라건대 더 잘되는 시도에 나설 수 있을 때까지 그리 지낼 터였다. 속더라도 적어도 좋은 기분과 좋은 운으로 속을 터였다. 반면 그녀는 한없이 더 세찬 심경을 안고, 바람이나 됨됨이의 두 번째 봄을 허락하지 않을 은둔과 나무람으로 물러날밖에 없었다.\n\n그녀를 어디에 둘까가 더없이 서글프고 중대한 의논거리가 되었다. 노리스 부인은, 그 정이 조카딸의 흠과 함께 더해지는 듯한 이라, 그녀가 집에 받아들여져 다들의 두둔을 받게 했으리라. 토머스 경은 그것을 들으려 하지 않았다. 그리고 노리스 부인의 패니에 대한 노여움은, 그녀가 거기 사는 것을 그 동기로 여겨 한층 더 커졌다. 그녀는 그의 거리낌을 그녀 탓으로 돌리기를 고집했다. 하기야 토머스 경은, 만일 문제 될 젊은 여자가 없었더라면, 러시워스 부인의 사귐에 위험해지거나 그 됨됨이에 해를 입을 만한, 저에게 딸린 어떤 성(性)의 젊은 사람도 없었더라면, 그가 이웃더러 그녀를 아는 체하기를 바라는 그런 큰 모욕을 결코 이 고장에 안겼을 리 없다고 자못 엄숙히 그녀에게 다짐했지만. 딸로서, 그가 바라건대 뉘우치는 딸로서, 그녀는 그의 보호를 받고, 온갖 편안함을 누리고, 그들의 처지가 허락하는 한 옳게 처신하라는 온갖 북돋움의 뒷받침을 받을 터였다. 그러나 그 이상으로는 그가 나아갈 수 없었다. 마리아는 제 됨됨이를 스스로 무너뜨렸고, 그는, 결코 되돌릴 수 없는 것을 되돌리려는 헛된 시도로써, 악덕에 제 승인을 보탬으로써, 아니 그 치욕을 덜려 애씀으로써, 저 자신이 겪었던 것 같은 그런 비참함을 다른 이의 집안에 들이는 데 어떤 식으로든 한몫 거들지 않으려 했다.\n\n그것은 노리스 부인이 맨스필드를 떠나 제 불운한 마리아에게 몸을 바치기로 마음먹는 것으로, 그리고 그들을 위해 다른 고장, 멀고 외진 곳에 거처가 마련되는 것으로 끝났다. 거기서 사교라곤 별로 없이 함께 갇혀, 한쪽엔 정이 없고 다른 쪽엔 분별이 없으니, 그 성미가 서로의 벌이 되었으려니 웬만큼 여길 만하다.\n\n노리스 부인이 맨스필드를 떠난 것은 토머스 경의 삶에 더해진 큰 위안이었다. 그녀에 대한 그의 생각은 앤티가에서 돌아온 날부터 가라앉고 있었다. 그때부터 함께한 온갖 일에서, 나날의 사귐에서, 볼일에서든 잡담에서든, 그녀는 그의 존중에서 꼬박꼬박 자리를 잃으며, 그로 하여금 시간이 그녀에게 큰 해를 끼쳤거나, 아니면 그가 앞서 그녀의 지각을 자못 지나치게 값매기고 그녀의 태도를 놀랍도록 참아 왔던 것임을 확신하게 했다. 그는 그녀를 시시각각의 화로 느껴 왔는데, 그것이 목숨과 함께라야 그칠 낌새밖에 없어 보이니 한층 더 나빴다. 그녀는 영영 견뎌야 할 저 자신의 일부인 듯했다. 그러니 그녀에게서 놓이는 것이 하도 큰 기쁨이라, 만일 그녀가 쓰라린 기억을 뒤에 남기지 않았더라면, 그가 그런 좋음을 낳은 그 화를 거의 갸륵히 여기는 법을 배울 위험마저 있었으리라.\n\n맨스필드에서 그녀를 아쉬워한 이는 아무도 없었다. 그녀는 저가 가장 사랑한 이들조차 붙들지 못했다. 그리고 러시워스 부인의 사랑의 도피 이래로, 그녀의 성미는 하도 부아 돋은 상태라 어디서나 사람을 볶아 댔다. 패니조차 노리스 이모를 위해 흘릴 눈물이 없었으니, 그녀가 영영 떠났을 때조차 그러했다.\n\n줄리아가 마리아보다 더 잘 벗어난 것은, 얼마간은 성미와 사정의 다행한 다름 덕이었으나, 더 크게는 그녀가 바로 그 이모의 귀염둥이가 덜었던 것, 덜 아첨받고 덜 버릇 나빠진 것 덕이었다. 그녀의 미모와 재주는 둘째 자리를 차지했을 뿐이었다. 그녀는 늘 저를 마리아보다 조금 못하다 여기는 데 익어 있었다. 그녀의 성미는 천성으로 둘 중 가장 느긋했고, 그 심경은, 재빠르되 더 다스릴 만했으며, 교육이 그녀에게 그토록 해로운 정도의 자부심을 주지는 않았다.\n\n그녀는 헨리 크로퍼드에 대한 실망에 가장 잘 굽혔다. 무시당했다는 확신의 그 첫 쓰라림이 지나자, 그녀는 웬만큼 이내 그를 다시 생각지 않을 순탄한 길에 들어섰다. 그리고 시내에서 그 사귐이 되살아나 러시워스 씨의 집이 크로퍼드의 목표가 되었을 때, 그녀는 거기서 저를 물리고, 다시 너무 끌리는 데서 저를 지키려 하필 그때를 잡아 다른 벗들을 찾아가는 갸륵함이 있었다. 이것이 그녀가 사촌에게 간 동기였다. 예이츠 씨의 편의야 그것과 아무 상관이 없었다. 그녀는 한동안 그의 마음씀을 받아 주고 있긴 했으나, 언제고 그를 받아들일 생각은 별로 없었다. 그리고 만일 그녀 언니의 처신이 그렇게 터지지 않았더라면, 그리고 그 일에 대한 아버지와 집에 대한 그녀의 더해진 두려움—그것이 저 자신에게 낳을 확실한 결말은 더한 엄함과 잡도리이려니 여겨, 무슨 위험을 무릅쓰고라도 그런 당장의 소름을 피하기로 서둘러 마음먹게 만든 그 두려움이 아니었더라면, 아마 예이츠 씨는 결코 뜻을 못 이뤘으리라. 그녀는 이기적인 놀람 말고는 더 나쁜 심경으로 도피한 것이 아니었다. 그것이 그녀에겐 할 수 있는 유일한 일로 보였다. 마리아의 죄가 줄리아의 어리석음을 불러온 것이다.\n\n헨리 크로퍼드는, 이른 독립과 나쁜 집안의 본보기에 망가져, 냉혈의 허영의 그 변덕을 조금 너무 오래 부렸다. 언젠가 그것은, 뜻하지도 받을 만하지도 않은 열림으로써, 그를 행복의 길로 이끈 적이 있었다. 만일 그가 한 상냥한 여자의 정을 얻은 것으로 흡족할 수 있었다면, 만일 패니 프라이스의 꺼림을 이겨 내어 그 존경과 애틋함으로 파고드는 데서 넉넉한 우쭐함을 찾을 수 있었다면, 그에게는 성공과 행복의 온갖 가망이 있었으리라. 그의 정은 이미 얼마간을 이룬 참이었다. 그에 대한 그녀의 힘이 이미 그에게 그녀에 대한 얼마간의 힘을 준 것이다. 만일 그가 더 받을 만했더라면, 더 많은 것이 얻어졌으리라는 데 의심의 여지가 없다, 특히 그 혼인이 이뤄져 그가 그녀의 첫 성향을 눅이는 데 그녀 양심의 도움을 얻고, 둘을 자못 자주 함께 있게 했을 때는. 만일 그가, 그것도 올곧게 물러서지 않았더라면, 패니는 그의 보답이 될밖에 없었으리라, 그것도 에드먼드가 메리와 혼인하고 얼마 안 되어 자못 자진해 주어진 보답이.\n\n만일 그가 뜻한 대로, 또 마땅히 해야 할 줄 안 대로, 포츠머스에서 돌아온 뒤 에버링엄으로 내려갔더라면, 그는 저 자신의 행복한 운명을 정하고 있었을지도 모른다. 그러나 그는 프레이저 부인의 모임을 위해 머물라 조름받았다. 그의 머묾은 우쭐한 중함을 띠었고, 그는 거기서 러시워스 부인을 만나게 되어 있었다. 궁금함과 허영이 둘 다 걸렸고, 당장의 즐거움의 유혹이, 옳음을 위해 무슨 희생도 하는 데 안 익은 마음에는 너무 셌다. 그는 노퍽행을 미루기로, 편지가 그 목적을 대신하리라, 아니 그 목적이 대수롭잖으리라 마음먹고, 머물렀다. 그는 러시워스 부인을 봤고, 그녀에게서, 마땅히 그를 밀쳐 내고 둘 사이에 겉보기 무심함을 영영 세웠어야 할 그런 냉랭함으로 맞아졌다. 그러나 그는 무안했다. 그 미소가 그토록 온전히 제 손아귀에 있던 여자에게 내쳐지는 것을 그는 견딜 수 없었다. 그는 그토록 오만하게 드러난 원망을 눅이려 애써야 했다. 그것은 패니 때문에 난 노여움이었다. 그는 그것을 이겨 내어, 저 자신을 대하는 태도에서 러시워스 부인을 다시 마리아 버트럼으로 만들어야 했다.\n\n이런 마음으로 그는 공략을 시작했고, 생기 어린 끈질김으로 이내, 제 뜻이 그은 그 한계인 그런 격의 없는 사귐, 정겨움, 시시덕거림을 다시 세워 놓았다. 그러나 노여움에서 비롯했으되 둘 다를 구할 수도 있었을 그 분별을 이기고 우쭐하는 사이에, 그는 저가 여긴 것보다 더 세찬 그녀 편 심경의 손아귀에 저를 내맡기고 말았다. 그녀는 그를 사랑했다. 그녀에게 대놓고 소중한 그 마음씀을 거둘 길이 없었다. 그는 되도록 사랑의 변명은 없이, 그리고 그녀 사촌을 향한 마음의 조금의 변덕도 없이, 저 자신의 허영에 얽혀 들었다. 무슨 일이 벌어지는지를 패니와 버트럼가가 모르게 하는 것이 그의 첫째가는 목표가 되었다. 은밀함은 러시워스 부인의 명예를 위해서보다 저 자신을 위해 그가 더 바랄 만한 것이었다. 리치먼드에서 돌아왔을 때, 그는 러시워스 부인을 다시는 안 봤으면 싶었으리라. 그 뒤에 이어진 온갖 것은 그녀의 경솔함의 결과였다. 그리고 그는 끝내 그녀와 함께 달아났으니, 그럴밖에 없었기 때문이요, 그 순간에도 패니를 서운해하되, 그 밀회의 온갖 소란이 끝나고 아주 몇 달이 대조의 힘으로 그에게 그녀 성미의 고움과 그 마음의 순수함과 그 지조의 훌륭함을 한층 더 높이 값매기도록 가르쳤을 때 한없이 더 그녀를 서운해했다.\n\n벌이, 곧 치욕이라는 세상의 벌이 그의 죄의 몫에 마땅한 만큼 따라야 한다는 것은, 우리가 알듯, 세상이 덕에 세워 주는 방벽 가운데 하나가 아니다. 이 세상에서 그 벌은 바랄 만한 것보다 덜 고르다. 그러나 훗날의 더 올바른 정하심을 감히 내다보려 들지 않더라도, 우리는 헨리 크로퍼드 같은 지각 있는 사내가 저 자신에게 적잖은 언짢음과 서운함을 마련해 두고 있으려니 온당히 여길 만하다—그토록 후한 대접을 그리 갚고, 집안의 평화를 그리 해치고, 저의 가장 좋고 가장 값지고 가장 정든 사귐을 그리 잃고, 저가 열정으로만큼이나 이치로도 사랑한 여자를 그리 잃음으로써, 때로 자책으로까지 치솟을밖에 없는 언짢음과 비참함으로까지 치솟을 서운함을.\n\n두 집안을 다치게 하고 멀어지게 한 그 일이 있고 나서, 버트럼가와 그랜트가가 그토록 바짝 붙은 이웃으로 이어 지내는 것은 더없이 괴로웠으리라. 그러나 몇 달 일부러 늘어진 그랜트가의 부재가, 더없이 다행하게도 아주 옮겨 가는 것의 필요, 아니 적어도 그 될 만함으로 끝났다. 그랜트 박사는, 이제 거의 바람을 접다시피 한 어떤 연줄로, 웨스트민스터의 참사회원 자리를 이어받았는데, 그것은 맨스필드를 떠날 계기가 되고, 런던에 살 구실이 되고, 그 옮김의 비용을 감당할 수입의 늘음이 되는지라, 떠나는 이들에게나 남는 이들에게나 더없이 반가웠다.\n\n그랜트 부인은, 사랑하고 사랑받을 성미라, 익숙하던 그 자리와 사람들을 얼마간 서운해하며 떠났을 게 틀림없다. 그러나 그 성미의 그 행복함은 어느 곳, 어느 사교에서든 그녀에게 누릴 것을 자못 많이 마련해 줄 게 틀림없었다. 그리고 그녀에게는 다시 메리에게 내밀 집이 있었다. 그리고 메리는, 지난 반년 사이에 제 벗들에도, 허영과 야심과 사랑과 실망에도 진력난지라, 그 언니 마음의 참된 친절과 그 방식의 이치에 맞는 고요가 아쉬웠다. 둘은 함께 살았다. 그리고 그랜트 박사가 한 주에 세 번의 큰 공식 만찬으로 졸중과 죽음을 불러왔을 때에도, 둘은 여전히 함께 살았다. 메리는, 다시는 결코 작은아들에게 정 붙이지 않기로 아주 굳게 마음먹었으면서도, 제 미모와 이만 파운드가 좌우할 수 있는 그 늠름한 명사들이나 한가한 상속자들 가운데서, 저가 맨스필드에서 얻은 그 더 나은 취향을 채워 줄 이, 그 됨됨이와 몸가짐이 저가 거기서 값매기는 법을 배운 그 가정의 행복에 대한 바람을 뒷받침해 줄 이, 아니 에드먼드 버트럼을 제 머리에서 넉넉히 몰아내 줄 이를 찾기까지 오래 걸렸던 것이다.\n\n에드먼드는 이 점에서 그녀보다 자못 유리했다. 그는 그녀를 이을 만한 대상을 텅 빈 정으로 기다리고 바랄 필요가 없었다. 그가 메리 크로퍼드를 서운해하기를 겨우 마치고, 그런 다른 여자를 만나기란 얼마나 어림없는지를 패니에게 뇌기 무섭게, 자못 다른 종류의 여자도 딱 그만큼 잘, 아니 훨씬 더 잘 어울리지 않을까—패니 자신이, 그 온갖 미소와 그 온갖 방식에서, 메리 크로퍼드가 여태 그러했던 만큼이나 저에게 소중하고 중해지고 있지 않은가—그리고 그를 향한 그녀의 따뜻하고 누이다운 마음이 부부의 사랑의 넉넉한 바탕이 되리라 그녀를 설득하는 것이 있음 직한, 바람 있는 일이 아닐까 하는 생각이 그를 치기 시작한 것이다.\n\n나는 이 자리에서 날짜를 일부러 안 밝히려는데, 이겨 낼 수 없는 열정의 나음과 변치 않는 애착의 옮김이 사람마다 그 시간이 자못 다를 게 틀림없음을 알기에, 다들 저 나름의 날짜를 정할 수 있도록이다. 나는 그저 다들에게 이것만 믿기를 청한다, 딱 그리되는 게 지극히 자연스러운 그때에, 그보다 한 주도 이르지 않게, 에드먼드가 정말로 크로퍼드 양에 대한 마음을 그쳤고, 패니 자신이 바랄 만한 만큼이나 패니와 혼인하기를 못내 바라게 되었다는 것을.\n\n정말이지 그의 것처럼 오래된, 순진함과 힘없음이라는 더없이 정든 몫에 바탕하고, 자라 가는 값어치의 온갖 천거로 온전해진 그런 마음이라면, 그 바뀜보다 더 자연스러운 것이 무엇이랴? 그녀가 열 살이던 때부터 줄곧 해 온 대로 그녀를 사랑하고 이끌고 지켜 주며, 그녀의 마음이 그토록 크게 그의 마음씀으로 지어지고, 그녀의 편안함이 그의 친절에 기대고, 그에게 그토록 바짝하고 남다른 관심의 대상이며, 그녀와 얽힌 저 자신의 온갖 중함으로 하여 맨스필드의 다른 누구보다 소중했던 그녀이니, 이제 더할 것이 무엇이랴, 그가 반짝이는 검은 눈보다 부드러운 옅은 눈을 더 좋아하는 법을 배우는 것 말고는. 그리고 늘 그녀와 함께 있고, 늘 속내를 터놓고 얘기하며, 그의 심경이 근래의 실망이 주는 딱 그 좋은 상태에 있으니, 그 부드러운 옅은 눈이 으뜸을 차지하기까지는 그리 오래 걸릴 수 없었다.\n\n일단 나서서, 저가 이 행복의 길에 나섰음을 느끼고 나니, 그를 멈추거나 그 나아감을 더디게 할 만한 것이 신중함 편에는 아무것도 없었다. 그녀가 받을 만함에 대한 미심쩍음도, 취향이 맞설까 하는 두려움도, 성미의 다름에서 행복의 새 바람을 끌어낼 필요도 없었다. 그녀의 마음도, 성미도, 소견도, 버릇도, 절반의 감춤도, 지금에 대한 자기기만도, 앞날의 나아짐에 대한 기댐도 필요치 않았다. 그 지난 홀림 한복판에서마저 그는 패니의 마음의 나음을 인정했었다. 그러니 이제 그것에 대한 그의 사무침이 어떠했으랴? 그녀야 물론 그에게는 너무 과분한 사람일 뿐이었으나, 저에게 과분한 것을 갖는 걸 언짢아하는 이는 아무도 없는 법이라, 그는 자못 진득이 그 복을 좇는 데 진심이었고, 그녀에게서 오는 북돋움이 오래 모자랄 리 없었다. 그녀가 아무리 소심하고 애타고 미심쩍어했어도, 그녀 같은 그런 애틋함이 이따금 성공의 더없이 굳센 바람을 내비치지 않기란 여전히 어림없었다—하기야 그 온전히 흐뭇하고 놀라운 진실을 죄다 그에게 일러 주는 것은 좀 더 나중의 몫으로 남았지만. 그토록 오래 그런 마음의 사랑받는 이였음을 아는 그의 행복은, 그가 그것을 그녀에게든 저 자신에게든 옷 입힐 수 있는 어떤 세찬 말이라도 마땅케 할 만큼 컸을 게 틀림없다. 그것은 흐뭇한 행복이었을 게 틀림없다. 그러나 어떤 묘사로도 닿을 수 없는 행복이 다른 데 있었다. 좀처럼 스스로 바람을 품도록 허락한 적조차 없는 그 정에 대한 다짐을 받는 젊은 여자의 심경을 감히 누구도 옮기려 들지 말라.\n\n저마다의 마음이 확인되니, 뒤에는 아무 어려움도, 가난이나 부모라는 아무 걸림도 없었다. 그것은 토머스 경의 바람이 오히려 미리 앞질러 놓은 혼사였다. 야심 차고 돈 밝히는 인연에 진력나고, 지조와 성미라는 참된 좋음을 점점 더 값지게 여기며, 저에게 남은 가정의 온갖 행복을 가장 굳센 담보로 붙들어 매기를 무엇보다 애타게 바라던 그는, 그 두 젊은 벗이 저마다에게 닥친 온갖 실망에 대한 자연스러운 위안을 서로에게서 찾는 것의 그 있음 직함 이상의 것을 참된 흡족함으로 곰곰 헤아려 온 터였다. 그리하여 에드먼드의 청을 맞은 그 기쁜 승낙은, 패니를 며느리로 약속받음으로써 큰 얻음을 이뤘다는 그 드높은 사무침은, 딱한 어린 계집아이의 옴이 처음 논의되던 무렵 이 일에 대한 그의 첫 생각과 딱 그런 대조를 이뤘으니—시간이 인간의 계획과 결정 사이에, 저희 자신의 깨우침과 그 이웃의 재미를 위해 영영 지어내는 그런 대조를.\n\n패니는 정말이지 그가 바라던 딸이었다. 그의 자비로운 친절은 저 자신을 위한 으뜸가는 위안을 길러 온 셈이었다. 그의 너그러움은 넉넉한 갚음을 받았고, 그녀를 향한 그의 뜻의 두루 착함이 그럴 만했다. 그가 그녀의 어린 시절을 더 행복하게 해 줄 수도 있었으련만, 그에게 엄함의 겉모습을 씌우고 그녀의 어릴 적 사랑을 앗아 간 것은 오직 판단의 잘못이었을 뿐이었다. 그리고 이제, 서로를 정말로 알게 되니, 그들 서로의 애착은 자못 굳어졌다. 그녀를 손턴 레이시에 그 편안함에 대한 온갖 다정한 마음씀과 함께 자리 잡게 한 뒤, 거의 날마다의 목표가 거기서 그녀를 보거나, 그녀를 거기서 데려오는 것이었다.\n\n버트럼 부인에게 그녀가 오래도록 그토록 이기적으로 소중했던지라, 그녀로서는 그녀를 기꺼이 떠나보낼 수 없었다. 아들이나 조카딸의 어떤 행복도 그녀로 하여금 그 혼인을 바라게 할 수 없었다. 그러나 그녀를 떠나보내는 것이 될 만했으니, 수전이 남아 그 자리를 채웠기 때문이다. 수전이 붙박이 조카딸이 되었고, 그리되는 것이 기뻤다. 그리고 패니가 성미의 고움과 굳센 고마움의 심경으로 그에 알맞았던 것과 꼭 같이, 그녀도 재빠른 마음과 쓸모가 되려는 성향으로 그에 알맞았다. 수전은 결코 없어선 안 될 이가 되었다. 처음엔 패니의 위안으로, 다음엔 조력자로, 끝으로 그 자리를 잇는 이로, 그녀는 못지않게 붙박일 온갖 낌새와 함께 맨스필드에 자리 잡았다. 그녀의 더 겁 없는 성미와 더 행복한 신경이 거기서 그녀에게 온갖 것을 손쉽게 해 주었다. 상대해야 할 이들의 성미를 알아채는 재빠름과, 그에 따른 어떤 바람도 억누를 타고난 소심함이 없던지라, 그녀는 이내 다들에게 반가운, 쓸모 있는 이가 되었다. 그리고 패니가 떠난 뒤 이모의 시시각각의 편안함에 대한 그 힘을 하도 자연스레 이어받아, 차츰 어쩌면 둘 중 더 사랑받는 이가 되었다. 그녀의 쓸모에서, 패니의 훌륭함에서, 윌리엄의 이어지는 좋은 처신과 오르는 명성에서, 그리고 다들 서로를 밀어 주고 그의 보살핌과 도움에 값한, 다른 식구들의 두루 잘됨과 성공에서, 토머스 경은, 저가 그들 모두를 위해 한 일을 기뻐하고, 이른 고생과 단련의 이점을, 그리고 애쓰고 견디도록 태어났다는 자각의 이점을 인정할 까닭을 거듭, 영영 거듭 보았다.\n\n그토록 많은 참된 값어치와 참된 사랑이 있고, 재산과 벗이 모자라지 않으니, 혼인한 두 사촌의 행복은 이 세상의 행복이 그럴 수 있는 만큼이나 든든해 보일밖에 없다. 둘 다 똑같이 가정의 삶에 걸맞고 시골의 즐거움에 정든지라, 그들의 집은 애정과 편안함의 집이었다. 그리고 그 좋음의 그림을 온전케 하듯, 그랜트 박사의 죽음으로 맨스필드 성직록을 얻은 것이, 그들이 수입의 늘음을 아쉬워하기 시작하고 아버지 집과의 거리를 불편으로 느낄 만큼 혼인한 지 오래된 바로 그때에 일어났다.\n\n그 일로 그들은 맨스필드로 옮겨 갔다. 그리고 거기 목사관은, 앞선 두 임자 저마다의 시절에 패니가 얼마간의 괴로운 잡힘이나 놀람의 심경 없이는 결코 다가갈 수 없던 그 목사관은, 이내 그녀의 마음에, 맨스필드 파크의 눈길과 두둔 안의 다른 온갖 것이 오래도록 그러했던 것만큼이나 소중하고, 그녀의 눈에 그만큼 속속들이 온전한 것이 되었다.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-141"]=[[["# CHAPTER I", "# 제1장"]], [["About thirty years ago Miss Maria Ward, of Huntingdon, with only seven thousand pounds, had the good luck to captivate Sir Thomas Bertram, of Mansfield Park, in the county of Northampton, and to be thereby raised to the rank of a baronet’s lady, with all the comforts and consequences of an handsome house and large income.", "지금으로부터 삼십 년쯤 전, 헌팅던의 마리아 워드 양은 겨우 칠천 파운드밖에 지니지 못했으면서도 노샘프턴주 맨스필드 파크의 토머스 버트럼 경의 마음을 사로잡는 행운을 누렸다. 그리하여 준남작 부인의 지위에 올라, 번듯한 저택과 넉넉한 수입이 가져다주는 온갖 안락과 영화를 손에 넣었다."], ["All Huntingdon exclaimed on the greatness of the match, and her uncle, the lawyer, himself, allowed her to be at least three thousand pounds short of any equitable claim to it.", "헌팅던 사람들은 하나같이 그 혼사가 얼마나 대단한지 감탄해 마지않았고, 변호사인 그녀의 삼촌조차 조카가 그만한 자리에 정당히 어울리려면 적어도 삼천 파운드는 모자란다고 인정할 정도였다."], ["She had two sisters to be benefited by her elevation; and such of their acquaintance as thought Miss Ward and Miss Frances quite as handsome as Miss Maria, did not scruple to predict their marrying with almost equal advantage.", "그녀에게는 언니 덕에 함께 팔자를 고칠 여동생이 둘 있었다. 워드 양과 프랜시스 양도 마리아 양 못지않게 아름답다고 여기던 지인들은 그 둘 역시 그에 버금가는 좋은 자리로 시집가리라 서슴없이 점쳤다."], ["But there certainly are not so many men of large fortune in the world as there are pretty women to deserve them.", "그러나 세상에는 그럴 만한 미인을 다 감당할 만큼 재산 많은 남자가 그리 흔치 않은 법이다."], ["Miss Ward, at the end of half a dozen years, found herself obliged to be attached to the Rev. Mr.", "워드 양은 여섯 해쯤 지난 끝에 형부의 친구인 노리스 목사와 맺어지지 않을 수 없는 처지가 되었는데, 그에게는 사사로운 재산이랄 것이 거의 없었다."], ["Norris, a friend of her brother-in-law, with scarcely any private fortune, and Miss Frances fared yet worse.", "그리고 프랜시스 양은 그보다도 형편이 나빴다. 사실 워드 양의 혼사는 막상 따지고 보면 그리 볼품없지는 않았다."], ["Miss Ward’s match, indeed, when it came to the point, was not contemptible: Sir Thomas being happily able to give his friend an income in the living of Mansfield; and Mr. and Mrs.", "토머스 경이 다행히 자기 친구에게 맨스필드의 성직록을 마련해 줄 수 있었던 덕분에, 노리스 부부는 한 해 천 파운드에 크게 못 미치지 않는 수입으로 부부의 단란한 삶을 시작했으니 말이다."], ["Norris began their career of conjugal felicity with very little less than a thousand a year.", "그러나 프랜시스 양은 흔한 말로 집안의 뜻을 거스르려 작정하고 혼인했다."], ["But Miss Frances married, in the common phrase, to disoblige her family, and by fixing on a lieutenant of marines, without education, fortune, or connexions, did it very thoroughly.", "교육도, 재산도, 이렇다 할 연줄도 없는 해병 중위를 고름으로써 그 일을 아주 철저히 해치운 것이다."], ["She could hardly have made a more untoward choice.", "그보다 더 딱한 선택을 하기도 어려웠을 것이다."], ["Sir Thomas Bertram had interest, which, from principle as well as pride—from a general wish of doing right, and a desire of seeing all that were connected with him in situations of respectability, he would have been glad to exert for the advantage of Lady Bertram’s sister; but her husband’s profession was such as no interest could reach; and before he had time to devise any other method of assisting them, an absolute breach between the sisters had taken place.", "토머스 버트럼 경에게는 영향력이 있었고, 원칙에서든 자존심에서든—옳게 처신하고 싶은 마음과, 자기와 이어진 이들이 모두 남부끄럽지 않은 처지에 놓이는 것을 보고 싶은 바람에서—처제를 위해 기꺼이 그 힘을 써 주었을 것이다. 그러나 처제 남편의 직업은 어떤 영향력도 미칠 수 없는 것이었고, 다른 도울 방도를 궁리해 볼 겨를도 없이 두 자매 사이에는 완전한 절연이 벌어지고 말았다."], ["It was the natural result of the conduct of each party, and such as a very imprudent marriage almost always produces.", "그것은 양쪽의 처신이 빚어낸 자연스러운 결과였으며, 지극히 경솔한 혼인이 거의 언제나 낳는 결말이기도 했다."], ["To save herself from useless remonstrance, Mrs. Price never wrote to her family on the subject till actually married.", "프라이스 부인은 쓸데없는 잔소리를 듣지 않으려고 실제로 혼인하기 전까지는 그 일을 두고 집안에 한마디도 적어 보내지 않았다."], ["Lady Bertram, who was a woman of very tranquil feelings, and a temper remarkably easy and indolent, would have contented herself with merely giving up her sister, and thinking no more of the matter; but Mrs.", "버트럼 부인은 감정이 지극히 잔잔하고 성미가 유난히 느긋하고 굼뜬 여자였던지라, 그저 동생을 단념하고 더는 그 일을 마음에 두지 않는 것으로 만족했을 것이다. 그러나 노리스 부인은 가만있지 못하는 활동적인 기질이라, 패니에게 그 처신이 얼마나 어리석은지 조목조목 일러 주고 앞으로 닥칠 온갖 나쁜 결과로 을러대는 긴 노여운 편지를 써 보내고서야 직성이 풀렸다."], ["Norris had a spirit of activity, which could not be satisfied till she had written a long and angry letter to Fanny, to point out the folly of her conduct, and threaten her with all its possible ill consequences. Mrs.", "프라이스 부인 또한 이번에는 상처받고 화가 났다."], ["Price, in her turn, was injured and angry; and an answer, which comprehended each sister in its bitterness, and bestowed such very disrespectful reflections on the pride of Sir Thomas as Mrs. Norris could not possibly keep to herself, put an end to all intercourse between them for a considerable period.", "그리하여 두 언니를 싸잡아 신랄하게 몰아붙이고 토머스 경의 자존심을 두고 몹시 무례한 소리까지 늘어놓은 답장이 돌아왔는데, 노리스 부인으로서는 도저히 혼자 삭이고 있을 수 없는 내용이었던지라, 그것으로 두 사람 사이의 왕래는 상당한 기간 뚝 끊기고 말았다."]], [["Their homes were so distant, and the circles in which they moved so distinct, as almost to preclude the means of ever hearing of each other’s existence during the eleven following years, or, at least, to make it very wonderful to Sir Thomas that Mrs.", "두 집은 서로 멀리 떨어져 있었고 드나드는 모임도 워낙 달랐던 터라, 그 뒤로 열한 해 동안 서로의 소식을 들을 길조차 거의 없다시피 했다."], ["Norris should ever have it in her power to tell them, as she now and then did, in an angry voice, that Fanny had got another child. By the end of eleven years, however, Mrs.", "아니, 적어도 노리스 부인이 이따금 그러듯 성난 목소리로 패니가 또 아이를 낳았다더라 하고 전해 올 때면, 토머스 경으로서는 그런 소식을 전할 재간이 대체 어디서 났나 싶어 여간 놀랍지 않았다."], ["Price could no longer afford to cherish pride or resentment, or to lose one connexion that might possibly assist her.", "그러나 열한 해가 저물 무렵에는 프라이스 부인도 더는 자존심이나 원한을 붙들고 있을 처지가, 혹은 도움이 될지 모를 인연을 하나라도 놓칠 처지가 못 되었다."], ["A large and still increasing family, an husband disabled for active service, but not the less equal to company and good liquor, and a very small income to supply their wants, made her eager to regain the friends she had so carelessly sacrificed; and she addressed Lady Bertram in a letter which spoke so much contrition and despondence, such a superfluity of children, and such a want of almost everything else, as could not but dispose them all to a reconciliation.", "식구는 많은 데다 여전히 늘어 가고, 남편은 현역 복무는 못 하게 되었으면서도 사람들과 어울리고 좋은 술을 즐기는 데에는 조금도 뒤지지 않았으며, 그 궁핍을 메울 수입은 지극히 변변찮았으니, 그녀는 제가 그토록 무심히 저버린 벗들을 되찾고 싶어 안달이 났다. 그리하여 그녀는 버트럼 부인에게 편지를 띄웠는데, 어찌나 뉘우침과 낙심이 가득하고, 어찌나 아이는 넘쳐 나며, 그 밖에는 거의 모든 것이 어찌나 모자란지, 읽는 이들이 화해로 마음이 기울지 않을 수 없을 정도였다."], ["She was preparing for her ninth lying-in; and after bewailing the circumstance, and imploring their countenance as sponsors to the expected child, she could not conceal how important she felt they might be to the future maintenance of the eight already in being.", "그녀는 아홉 번째 해산을 앞두고 있었다. 그 사정을 한탄하고 곧 태어날 아이의 대부모가 되어 달라 청한 다음에는, 이미 세상에 나온 여덟 아이의 앞날을 건사하는 데에도 그들이 얼마나 큰 힘이 될 수 있을지, 그 기대를 차마 숨기지 못했다."], ["Her eldest was a boy of ten years old, a fine spirited fellow, who longed to be out in the world; but what could she do?", "맏이는 열 살 난 사내아이로, 기상이 늠름해 어서 세상에 나가고 싶어 안달인 아이였다. 그러나 그녀가 무엇을 해 줄 수 있으랴?"], ["Was there any chance of his being hereafter useful to Sir Thomas in the concerns of his West Indian property?", "훗날 그 아이가 토머스 경의 서인도제도 재산을 돌보는 일에 쓸모가 있을 가망은 없을까?"], ["No situation would be beneath him; or what did Sir Thomas think of Woolwich?", "어떤 자리든 마다하지 않을 것이었다. 아니면 토머스 경은 울리치를 어찌 여기실까?"], ["or how could a boy be sent out to the East?", "사내아이 하나를 동인도로 보내자면 또 어찌해야 할까?"]], [["The letter was not unproductive.", "그 편지는 헛되지 않았다."], ["It re-established peace and kindness.", "그것은 평화와 정을 되살렸다."], ["Sir Thomas sent friendly advice and professions, Lady Bertram dispatched money and baby-linen, and Mrs. Norris wrote the letters.", "토머스 경은 다정한 조언과 언약을 보냈고, 버트럼 부인은 돈과 갓난아이 옷가지를 부쳤으며, 편지는 노리스 부인이 도맡아 썼다."]], [["Such were its immediate effects, and within a twelvemonth a more important advantage to Mrs. Price resulted from it.", "편지가 당장 불러온 결과는 그러했다."], ["Mrs. Norris was often observing to the others that she could not get her poor sister and her family out of her head, and that, much as they had all done for her, she seemed to be wanting to do more; and at length she could not but own it to be her wish that poor Mrs.", "그리고 한 해가 채 가기 전에 프라이스 부인에게는 그로부터 더 중대한 이득이 돌아왔다."], ["Price should be relieved from the charge and expense of one child entirely out of her great number.", "노리스 부인은 딱한 동생과 그 식구가 도무지 머리에서 떠나지 않는다고, 다들 그만큼 해 주었건만 저는 아무래도 더 해 주어야 할 것만 같다고 다른 이들에게 노상 말하곤 했다."], ["“What if they were among them to undertake the care of her eldest daughter, a girl now nine years old, of an age to require more attention than her poor mother could possibly give?", "그러다 마침내 그 많은 아이들 가운데 하나만이라도 딱한 프라이스 부인의 손에서 온전히 떼어 그 부담과 비용을 덜어 주는 것이 제 바람임을 털어놓지 않을 수 없었다. “우리끼리 그 애의 맏딸을, 지금 아홉 살 난 그 여자애를 맡아 기르면 어떨까요?"], ["The trouble and expense of it to them would be nothing, compared with the benevolence of the action.”", "딱한 제 어미가 도저히 못 해 줄 만큼 손이 많이 갈 나이인데 말이에요. 우리한테야 그 수고나 비용이 이 착한 일에 견주면 아무것도 아니지 않겠어요.”"], ["Lady Bertram agreed with her instantly.", "버트럼 부인은 그 자리에서 언니 말에 찬동했다."], ["“I think we cannot do better,”", "“그보다 더 나은 일은 없겠어요.”"], ["said she; “let us send for the child.”", "그녀가 말했다. “그 애를 데려오도록 해요.”"]], [["Sir Thomas could not give so instantaneous and unqualified a consent.", "토머스 경은 그처럼 선뜻, 그리고 아무 조건 없이 승낙할 수는 없었다."], ["He debated and hesitated;—it was a serious charge;—a girl so brought up must be adequately provided for, or there would be cruelty instead of kindness in taking her from her family.", "그는 곰곰이 따지며 망설였다. 그것은 결코 가벼운 책임이 아니었다."], ["He thought of his own four children, of his two sons, of cousins in love, etc.;—but no sooner had he deliberately begun to state his objections, than Mrs.", "그렇게 길러진 여자애라면 마땅한 앞길을 마련해 주어야지, 그러지 않고서 집에서 데려온다면 친절이 아니라 도리어 잔인한 일이 될 터였다. 그는 제 자식 넷을, 두 아들을, 사촌지간의 연정 따위를 떠올렸다."], ["Norris interrupted him with a reply to them all, whether stated or not.", "그러나 그가 신중하게 반대할 점을 하나씩 꺼내 놓기가 무섭게, 노리스 부인이 말을 끊고 들어와 그가 꺼냈든 아니든 그 모든 것에 미리 답을 해 버렸다."]], [["“My dear Sir Thomas, I perfectly comprehend you, and do justice to the generosity and delicacy of your notions, which indeed are quite of a piece with your general conduct; and I entirely agree with you in the main as to the propriety of doing everything one could by way of providing for a child one had in a manner taken into one’s own hands; and I am sure I should be the last person in the world to withhold my mite upon such an occasion.", "“토머스 경, 무슨 말씀이신지 저는 훤히 알아듣겠어요. 그 너그럽고 세심한 생각씀씀이야 마땅히 높이 사야지요."], ["Having no children of my own, who should I look to in any little matter I may ever have to bestow, but the children of my sisters?—and I am sure Mr.", "사실 그거야 평소 처신과 조금도 어긋남이 없는 거고요. 손수 거두다시피 한 아이를 위해 할 수 있는 건 다 해 주어야 마땅하다는 말씀에도 저는 큰 틀에서 전적으로 같은 생각이에요."], ["Norris is too just—but you know I am a woman of few words and professions.", "그리고 이런 일에 제 몫을 아낄 사람으로 치면 세상에 저만 한 사람이 또 없을 거예요."], ["Do not let us be frightened from a good deed by a trifle.", "제게는 자식이 없으니, 언제고 뭐라도 조금 나누어 줄 일이 생기면 제 동생들의 아이 말고 누굴 바라보겠어요?"], ["Give a girl an education, and introduce her properly into the world, and ten to one but she has the means of settling well, without farther expense to anybody.", "게다가 노리스 씨도 워낙 반듯한 분이라—하기야 아시다시피 저야 말수도 적고 겉치레도 없는 사람이지만요. 우리, 사소한 일에 겁먹어서 좋은 일을 그르치지는 말아요."], ["A niece of ours, Sir Thomas, I may say, or at least of yours, would not grow up in this neighbourhood without many advantages.", "여자애 하나를 잘 가르쳐서 세상에 번듯하게 내보내면, 열에 아홉은 아무에게도 더는 돈 들이지 않고도 좋은 자리에 자리 잡을 길이 열리는 법이거든요. 토머스 경, 우리—적어도 경의 조카딸이라면 이 고장에서 자라나면서 누릴 이점이 한둘이 아닐 거예요."], ["I don’t say she would be so handsome as her cousins.", "그야 그 애가 사촌들만큼 예쁘리라고는 말하지 않겠어요."], ["I dare say she would not; but she would be introduced into the society of this country under such very favourable circumstances as, in all human probability, would get her a creditable establishment.", "아마 그렇진 않겠지요. 하지만 이 고장 사교계에 아주 좋은 조건으로 발을 들이게 될 테니, 사람의 일로 헤아리는 한 어엿한 자리를 얻게 될 거예요."], ["You are thinking of your sons—but do not you know that, of all things upon earth, that is the least likely to happen, brought up as they would be, always together like brothers and sisters?", "아드님들을 염려하시는 거지요—하지만 세상 무슨 일보다도 바로 그 일이 일어날 가망이 가장 적다는 걸 모르시겠어요? 어려서부터 늘 오누이처럼 붙어 자랄 텐데 말이에요."], ["It is morally impossible.", "그건 도의상 있을 수 없는 일이에요."], ["I never knew an instance of it.", "저는 그런 예를 여태 한 번도 본 적이 없어요."], ["It is, in fact, the only sure way of providing against the connexion.", "사실 그거야말로 그런 인연이 맺어지는 걸 막는 유일하게 확실한 길이지요."], ["Suppose her a pretty girl, and seen by Tom or Edmund for the first time seven years hence, and I dare say there would be mischief.", "그 애가 예쁜 처녀라 치고, 톰이나 에드먼드가 앞으로 일곱 해쯤 지나 그 애를 처음 본다고 해 봐요, 아마 탈이 나고 말 거예요."], ["The very idea of her having been suffered to grow up at a distance from us all in poverty and neglect, would be enough to make either of the dear, sweet-tempered boys in love with her.", "그 애가 우리 모두와 떨어져 가난과 무관심 속에 자라도록 내버려 두었다는 그 생각만으로도, 그 착하고 다정한 두 아이 중 누구라도 그 애한테 마음을 빼앗기고 말걸요."], ["But breed her up with them from this time, and suppose her even to have the beauty of an angel, and she will never be more to either than a sister.”", "하지만 지금부터 함께 기르면, 설령 그 애가 천사 같은 미모를 지녔다 한들 두 아이 어느 쪽에게도 누이 이상은 결코 되지 못할 거예요.”"]], [["“There is a great deal of truth in what you say,”", "“자네 말에는 옳은 데가 많네.”"], ["replied Sir Thomas, “and far be it from me to throw any fanciful impediment in the way of a plan which would be so consistent with the relative situations of each.", "토머스 경이 대답했다. “그리고 서로의 처지에 그토록 걸맞은 계획에 내가 공연한 트집을 잡아 훼방을 놓을 생각은 추호도 없네."], ["I only meant to observe that it ought not to be lightly engaged in, and that to make it really serviceable to Mrs. Price, and creditable to ourselves, we must secure to the child, or consider ourselves engaged to secure to her hereafter, as circumstances may arise, the provision of a gentlewoman, if no such establishment should offer as you are so sanguine in expecting.”", "다만 내가 짚어 두려던 건, 이 일을 가벼이 시작해서는 안 된다는 것, 그리고 이 일이 프라이스 부인에게 참으로 도움이 되고 우리에게도 떳떳하려면, 자네가 그토록 낙관하는 그런 자리가 나서지 않을 경우에 대비해, 그 아이에게 숙녀다운 앞가림을 마련해 주거나 형편이 닿는 대로 앞으로 그리해 주기로 우리 스스로 다짐해 두어야 한다는 것뿐일세.”"]], [["“I thoroughly understand you,” cried Mrs.", "“무슨 말씀인지 잘 알겠어요.”"], ["Norris, “you are everything that is generous and considerate, and I am sure we shall never disagree on this point.", "노리스 부인이 외쳤다. “경께서는 더없이 너그럽고 사려 깊은 분이시니, 이 점에서 우리가 다툴 일은 결코 없을 거예요."], ["Whatever I can do, as you well know, I am always ready enough to do for the good of those I love; and, though I could never feel for this little girl the hundredth part of the regard I bear your own dear children, nor consider her, in any respect, so much my own, I should hate myself if I were capable of neglecting her.", "아시다시피 제가 할 수 있는 일이라면 사랑하는 이들을 위해 저는 언제든 발 벗고 나설 준비가 돼 있어요. 물론 이 어린 계집아이한테야 경의 귀한 자녀들한테 품은 정의 백분의 일도 못 느낄 테고, 어느 모로 보나 제 자식처럼 여기지도 못하겠지만, 그렇다고 그 애를 나 몰라라 한다면 저는 스스로가 미워질 거예요."], ["Is not she a sister’s child?", "그 애도 제 동생의 자식이 아니겠어요?"], ["and could I bear to see her want while I had a bit of bread to give her?", "제게 빵 한 조각이라도 있는 한 그 애가 굶주리는 꼴을 어찌 두고 보겠어요?"], ["My dear Sir Thomas, with all my faults I have a warm heart; and, poor as I am, would rather deny myself the necessaries of life than do an ungenerous thing.", "토머스 경, 저야 흠이야 많아도 마음만은 따뜻한 사람이에요. 그리고 이렇게 가난해도 인색한 짓을 하느니 차라리 제 먹을 것을 굶겠어요."], ["So, if you are not against it, I will write to my poor sister tomorrow, and make the proposal; and, as soon as matters are settled, I will engage to get the child to Mansfield; you shall have no trouble about it.", "그러니 경께서 반대만 안 하신다면, 내일 당장 딱한 동생한테 편지를 써서 이 이야기를 꺼내 볼게요. 그리고 일이 매듭지어지는 대로 그 애를 맨스필드로 데려오는 건 제가 맡을게요."], ["My own trouble, you know, I never regard.", "경께서는 그 일로 조금도 수고하실 것 없어요."], ["I will send Nanny to London on purpose, and she may have a bed at her cousin the saddler’s, and the child be appointed to meet her there.", "아시다시피 제 수고야 저는 도무지 개의치 않으니까요. 내니를 일부러 런던에 보내면, 그 애 사촌인 마구상네에서 하룻밤 묵으면 되고, 아이더러 거기로 마중 나오게 하면 돼요."], ["They may easily get her from Portsmouth to town by the coach, under the care of any creditable person that may chance to be going.", "포츠머스에서 런던까지야 마침 그리로 가는 점잖은 사람 편에 딸려 역마차로 손쉽게 데려올 수 있을 거예요."], ["I dare say there is always some reputable tradesman’s wife or other going up.”", "아마 그맘때면 으레 어엿한 장사꾼 아낙이라도 하나쯤 상경하기 마련이거든요.”"]], [["Except to the attack on Nanny’s cousin, Sir Thomas no longer made any objection, and a more respectable, though less economical rendezvous being accordingly substituted, everything was considered as settled, and the pleasures of so benevolent a scheme were already enjoyed.", "내니의 사촌을 걸고넘어진 대목만 빼면 토머스 경은 더 이상 반대하지 않았다. 그리하여 그보다 좀 더 번듯한, 다만 덜 알뜰한 만남의 자리로 대신하기로 하고 모든 일이 매듭지어진 것으로 여겨졌으며, 이토록 자비로운 계획이 주는 흐뭇함은 벌써부터 누려지고 있었다."], ["The division of gratifying sensations ought not, in strict justice, to have been equal; for Sir Thomas was fully resolved to be the real and consistent patron of the selected child, and Mrs.", "엄밀히 따지자면 그 흐뭇한 기분의 몫이 두 사람에게 똑같이 돌아가서는 안 될 일이었다. 토머스 경은 골라 온 그 아이의 참되고 한결같은 후견인이 되기로 굳게 마음먹은 반면, 노리스 부인은 그 아이를 건사하는 데 한 푼이라도 쓸 생각은 눈곱만치도 없었으니 말이다."], ["Norris had not the least intention of being at any expense whatever in her maintenance.", "걷고, 말하고, 궁리하는 데까지라면 그녀는 더할 나위 없이 자비로웠고, 남더러 후하게 굴라고 이르는 데에는 누구도 그녀를 따를 자가 없었다."], ["As far as walking, talking, and contriving reached, she was thoroughly benevolent, and nobody knew better how to dictate liberality to others; but her love of money was equal to her love of directing, and she knew quite as well how to save her own as to spend that of her friends.", "그러나 그녀의 돈 사랑은 남을 부리기 좋아하는 마음에 조금도 뒤지지 않았고, 남의 돈을 쓰는 재주만큼이나 제 돈을 아끼는 재주에도 훤했다. 애초에 바라 마지않던 것보다 옹색한 수입으로 살림을 차린 터라, 그녀는 처음부터 지극히 빠듯한 절약이 꼭 필요하다고 여겼다."], ["Having married on a narrower income than she had been used to look forward to, she had, from the first, fancied a very strict line of economy necessary; and what was begun as a matter of prudence, soon grew into a matter of choice, as an object of that needful solicitude which there were no children to supply.", "그리고 분별에서 비롯된 그 절약은 이내 취향의 문제로 굳어졌으니, 마침 그 알뜰한 마음을 쏟을 자식조차 없었던 까닭이다. 만약 건사할 식구가 있었더라면 노리스 부인은 결코 돈을 모으지 못했을 것이다."], ["Had there been a family to provide for, Mrs. Norris might never have saved her money; but having no care of that kind, there was nothing to impede her frugality, or lessen the comfort of making a yearly addition to an income which they had never lived up to.", "그러나 그런 걱정거리가 없다 보니 그녀의 절약을 가로막을 것도, 한 번도 그만큼 써 본 적 없는 수입에 해마다 얼마씩을 보태는 즐거움을 덜어 낼 것도 없었다."], ["Under this infatuating principle, counteracted by no real affection for her sister, it was impossible for her to aim at more than the credit of projecting and arranging so expensive a charity; though perhaps she might so little know herself as to walk home to the Parsonage, after this conversation, in the happy belief of being the most liberal-minded sister and aunt in the world.", "동생을 향한 참된 애정으로도 누그러지지 않는 이 홀리는 신조 아래, 그녀가 노릴 수 있는 것이라고는 그토록 비용이 드는 자선을 기획하고 주선했다는 공치사뿐이었다. 하기야 어쩌면 그녀는 스스로를 어찌나 몰랐던지, 이 대화를 마치고 목사관으로 걸어 돌아가는 내내, 제가 세상에서 가장 인심 좋은 언니이자 이모라는 흐뭇한 믿음에 젖어 있었을는지도 모른다."]], [["When the subject was brought forward again, her views were more fully explained; and, in reply to Lady Bertram’s calm inquiry of “Where shall the child come to first, sister, to you or to us?”", "그 이야기가 다시 나왔을 때, 그녀의 속셈은 한층 낱낱이 드러났다."], ["Sir Thomas heard with some surprise that it would be totally out of Mrs.", "“그 애가 처음에 어디로 오게 할까요, 언니?"], ["Norris’s power to take any share in the personal charge of her.", "언니 댁으로, 아니면 우리 집으로?”"], ["He had been considering her as a particularly welcome addition at the Parsonage, as a desirable companion to an aunt who had no children of her own; but he found himself wholly mistaken. Mrs.", "하는 버트럼 부인의 태연한 물음에, 토머스 경은 뜻밖에도 그 아이를 몸소 건사하는 일에는 노리스 부인이 도무지 한몫 낄 수 없다는 소리를 듣고 적잖이 놀랐다."], ["Norris was sorry to say that the little girl’s staying with them, at least as things then were, was quite out of the question. Poor Mr.", "그는 그 아이가 목사관으로서는 유난히 반가운 식구가 되리라고, 제 자식 없는 이모에게는 더없이 바람직한 말벗이 되리라고 여겨 오던 참이었다."], ["Norris’s indifferent state of health made it an impossibility: he could no more bear the noise of a child than he could fly; if, indeed, he should ever get well of his gouty complaints, it would be a different matter: she should then be glad to take her turn, and think nothing of the inconvenience; but just now, poor Mr.", "그런데 그것이 완전한 오산이었음을 알게 된 것이다. 노리스 부인은 안됐지만 그 계집아이가 자기들과 함께 지내는 것은, 적어도 지금 형편으로는 도무지 될 일이 아니라고 했다."], ["Norris took up every moment of her time, and the very mention of such a thing she was sure would distract him.", "딱한 노리스 씨의 시원찮은 건강이 그것을 불가능하게 만든다는 것이었다. 그이는 하늘을 날 수 없듯이 아이의 소란도 도무지 견디지 못하니, 정말이지 훗날 통풍만 씻은 듯 나아 준다면야 이야기가 달라지겠고, 그때는 저도 기꺼이 차례를 맡아 그 불편쯤 아무렇지 않게 여기겠지만, 지금 당장은 딱한 노리스 씨가 그녀의 시간을 잠시도 남김없이 잡아먹는 형편이라, 그런 말을 입에 올리기만 해도 그이가 안절부절못할 게 뻔하다는 것이었다."]], [["“Then she had better come to us,”", "“그럼 그 애는 우리 집으로 오는 게 낫겠네요.”"], ["said Lady Bertram, with the utmost composure.", "버트럼 부인이 더없이 태연하게 말했다."], ["After a short pause Sir Thomas added with dignity, “Yes, let her home be in this house.", "잠깐 뜸을 들인 뒤 토머스 경이 위엄 있게 덧붙였다. “그래, 그 애의 집은 이 집으로 삼읍시다."], ["We will endeavour to do our duty by her, and she will, at least, have the advantage of companions of her own age, and of a regular instructress.”", "우리가 그 애에게 도리를 다하도록 애써 봅시다. 그러면 적어도 그 애는 또래 벗들과 제대로 된 여자 가정교사를 곁에 두는 이점은 누리게 될 테니.”"]], [["“Very true,”", "“지당하신 말씀이에요.”"], ["cried Mrs.", "노리스 부인이 외쳤다."], ["Norris, “which are both very important considerations; and it will be just the same to Miss Lee whether she has three girls to teach, or only two—there can be no difference.", "“둘 다 참으로 중요한 점이지요. 게다가 리 선생한테야 가르칠 여자애가 셋이든 둘이든 매한가지일 테니—다를 게 하나도 없어요."], ["I only wish I could be more useful; but you see I do all in my power.", "저야 좀 더 보탬이 못 되는 게 아쉬울 따름이지요. 하지만 보시다시피 저는 제 힘닿는 데까지 다 하고 있잖아요."], ["I am not one of those that spare their own trouble; and Nanny shall fetch her, however it may put me to inconvenience to have my chief counsellor away for three days.", "저는 제 수고를 아끼는 그런 부류가 아니에요. 사흘씩이나 제 으뜸가는 조언자를 떠나보내는 게 아무리 불편해도, 내니를 시켜 그 애를 데려오게 하겠어요."], ["I suppose, sister, you will put the child in the little white attic, near the old nurseries.", "언니, 그 애는 옛 육아실 옆 작은 흰 다락방에 두면 되겠네요. 리 선생과도 그토록 가깝고, 아이들과도 멀지 않고, 하녀들과도 바로 붙어 있으니 그 애한테는 더없이 좋은 자리일 거예요."], ["It will be much the best place for her, so near Miss Lee, and not far from the girls, and close by the housemaids, who could either of them help to dress her, you know, and take care of her clothes, for I suppose you would not think it fair to expect Ellis to wait on her as well as the others.", "하녀들 가운데 누구라도 그 애 옷 입는 걸 거들고 옷가지도 건사해 줄 수 있잖아요. 설마 엘리스한테 다른 애들에 더해 그 애 시중까지 들라고 하는 건 온당치 않다 여기실 테니 말이에요."], ["Indeed, I do not see that you could possibly place her anywhere else.”", "정말이지 그 애를 달리 어디 둘 데가 있을 것 같지도 않고요.”"]], [["Lady Bertram made no opposition.", "버트럼 부인은 아무 이의도 달지 않았다."]], [["“I hope she will prove a well-disposed girl,”", "“그 애가 심성 고운 아이로 자라 주면 좋겠어요.”"], ["continued Mrs.", "노리스 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["Norris, “and be sensible of her uncommon good fortune in having such friends.”", "“그리고 이런 후원자들을 둔 제 남다른 복을 제대로 헤아릴 줄 알았으면 하고요.”"]], [["“Should her disposition be really bad,” said Sir Thomas, “we must not, for our own children’s sake, continue her in the family; but there is no reason to expect so great an evil. We shall probably see much to wish altered in her, and must prepare ourselves for gross ignorance, some meanness of opinions, and very distressing vulgarity of manner; but these are not incurable faults; nor, I trust, can they be dangerous for her associates. Had my daughters been younger than herself, I should have considered the introduction of such a companion as a matter of very serious moment; but, as it is, I hope there can be nothing to fear for them, and everything to hope for her, from the association.”", "“만약 그 애의 심성이 정말로 못됐다면,” 토머스 경이 말했다. “우리 자식들을 위해서라도 그 애를 이 집안에 계속 둘 수는 없겠지. 하지만 그렇게까지 나쁜 일을 예상할 까닭은 없네. 아마 그 애한테서 고쳤으면 싶은 구석이야 많이 보이겠지. 지독한 무지며, 다소 상스러운 소견이며, 몹시 거슬리는 상스러운 몸가짐 따위를 우리도 각오해 두어야 할 걸세. 그러나 그런 건 못 고칠 흠이 아닐뿐더러, 내 믿기로 함께 지내는 아이들에게 해가 될 것도 아닐세. 만약 내 딸들이 그 애보다 어렸더라면, 그런 아이를 곁에 들이는 일을 아주 심각하게 여겼을 걸세. 하지만 지금 형편으로는, 그 사귐에서 내 딸들이 두려워할 것은 아무것도, 그 애가 바랄 것은 모든 것이 있으리라 보네.”"]], [["“That is exactly what I think,” cried Mrs.", "“바로 제 생각이 그래요.”"], ["Norris, “and what I was saying to my husband this morning.", "노리스 부인이 외쳤다. “오늘 아침에도 남편한테 그렇게 말했다니까요."], ["It will be an education for the child, said I, only being with her cousins; if Miss Lee taught her nothing, she would learn to be good and clever from them.”", "그저 사촌들과 함께 지내는 것만으로도 그 애한테는 훌륭한 교육이 될 거라고요. 리 선생이 아무것도 안 가르친다 해도, 그 애는 사촌들한테서 착하고 영리해지는 법을 배울 거라고 그랬어요.”"]], [["“I hope she will not tease my poor pug,”", "“그 애가 우리 딱한 퍼그를 괴롭히지나 않으면 좋겠는데요.”"], ["said Lady Bertram; “I have but just got Julia to leave it alone.”", "버트럼 부인이 말했다. “줄리아가 이제 겨우 그 녀석을 가만 놔두게 됐는데 말이에요.”"]], [["“There will be some difficulty in our way, Mrs.", "“노리스 부인, 우리한테 좀 곤란한 점이 하나 있을 걸세.”"], ["Norris,”", "토머스 경이 짚었다."], ["observed Sir Thomas, “as to the distinction proper to be made between the girls as they grow up: how to preserve in the minds of my daughters the consciousness of what they are, without making them think too lowly of their cousin; and how, without depressing her spirits too far, to make her remember that she is not a Miss Bertram.", "“아이들이 자라면서 그 애와 내 딸들 사이에 마땅히 두어야 할 구별을 어찌 지킬까 하는 문제 말일세. 내 딸들의 마음속에는 저희가 어떤 처지인지에 대한 자각을 심어 주되 사촌을 너무 낮잡아 보지는 않게 하고, 또 그 애의 기를 지나치게 꺾지는 않으면서도 저는 버트럼 집안의 아가씨가 아니라는 걸 잊지 않게 하는 일 말이야."], ["I should wish to see them very good friends, and would, on no account, authorise in my girls the smallest degree of arrogance towards their relation; but still they cannot be equals.", "나야 그 애들이 아주 사이좋은 벗으로 지내는 걸 보고 싶고, 내 딸들이 친척한테 티끌만큼이라도 거만하게 구는 일은 결코 용납하지 않을 걸세. 그렇지만 그래도 그 애들이 동등할 수는 없어."], ["Their rank, fortune, rights, and expectations will always be different.", "그 애들의 신분이며 재산이며 권리며 앞날의 기대는 언제까지나 다를 테니 말일세."], ["It is a point of great delicacy, and you must assist us in our endeavours to choose exactly the right line of conduct.”", "이건 여간 미묘한 문제가 아니야. 그러니 자네가 우리를 도와 꼭 알맞은 처신의 길을 골라 주어야 하네.”"]], [["Mrs.", "노리스 부인은 기꺼이 그를 돕겠다고 나섰다."], ["Norris was quite at his service; and though she perfectly agreed with him as to its being a most difficult thing, encouraged him to hope that between them it would be easily managed.", "그리고 그것이 참으로 어려운 일이라는 데에는 그와 전적으로 뜻을 같이하면서도, 둘이 힘을 합치면 손쉽게 해낼 수 있으리라며 그에게 희망을 북돋아 주었다."]], [["It will be readily believed that Mrs. Norris did not write to her sister in vain.", "노리스 부인이 동생에게 보낸 편지가 헛되지 않았으리라는 것은 어렵잖게 짐작할 수 있다."], ["Mrs. Price seemed rather surprised that a girl should be fixed on, when she had so many fine boys, but accepted the offer most thankfully, assuring them of her daughter’s being a very well-disposed, good-humoured girl, and trusting they would never have cause to throw her off.", "프라이스 부인은 그토록 훤칠한 사내아이가 여럿인데 하필 딸아이를 점찍었다는 데에 다소 의아해하는 눈치였지만, 그 제안을 더없이 고맙게 받아들이며 제 딸이 심성 곱고 성품 밝은 아이라고, 결코 그들이 그 애를 내치고 싶어질 일은 없을 거라고 다짐했다."], ["She spoke of her farther as somewhat delicate and puny, but was sanguine in the hope of her being materially better for change of air.", "그러면서 그 애가 몸이 좀 여리고 자그마하다는 말도 덧붙였으나, 물과 바람이 바뀌면 눈에 띄게 나아지리라는 희망에 부풀어 있었다. 딱한 여자 같으니!"], ["Poor woman! she probably thought change of air might agree with many of her children.", "아마도 그녀는 물과 바람이 바뀌면 제 아이들 여럿에게 두루 좋으련만 하고 생각했으리라."]], [["# CHAPTER II", "# 제2장"]], [["The little girl performed her long journey in safety; and at Northampton was met by Mrs.", "어린 여자아이는 긴 여정을 무사히 마쳤다."], ["Norris, who thus regaled in the credit of being foremost to welcome her, and in the importance of leading her in to the others, and recommending her to their kindness.", "노샘프턴에서는 노리스 부인이 마중을 나왔는데, 그리하여 그녀는 맨 먼저 아이를 맞이하는 공치사를, 그리고 아이를 다른 이들에게 데려가 그들의 친절에 부탁하는 중대한 역할을 흐뭇이 누렸다."]], [["Fanny Price was at this time just ten years old, and though there might not be much in her first appearance to captivate, there was, at least, nothing to disgust her relations. She was small of her age, with no glow of complexion, nor any other striking beauty; exceedingly timid and shy, and shrinking from notice; but her air, though awkward, was not vulgar, her voice was sweet, and when she spoke her countenance was pretty. Sir Thomas and Lady Bertram received her very kindly; and Sir Thomas, seeing how much she needed encouragement, tried to be all that was conciliating: but he had to work against a most untoward gravity of deportment; and Lady Bertram, without taking half so much trouble, or speaking one word where he spoke ten, by the mere aid of a good-humoured smile, became immediately the less awful character of the two.", "패니 프라이스는 이때 갓 열 살이었다. 첫인상에 사람을 사로잡을 만한 데는 별로 없었을지 몰라도, 적어도 친척들의 눈살을 찌푸리게 할 구석은 없었다. 나이에 비해 몸집이 작고, 혈색도 발그레하지 않았으며, 이렇다 하게 두드러진 아름다움도 없었다. 지독히 겁이 많고 수줍어 남의 눈길을 피해 움츠러들었다. 그러나 그 몸가짐은 어설프기는 해도 상스럽지 않았고, 목소리는 고왔으며, 입을 열면 얼굴이 예뻐 보였다. 토머스 경과 버트럼 부인은 아이를 무척 다정하게 맞아 주었다. 토머스 경은 아이가 얼마나 격려를 필요로 하는지 알아채고 최대한 마음을 누그러뜨려 주려 애썼으나, 도무지 어쩔 수 없이 근엄한 태도와 씨름해야 했다. 반면 버트럼 부인은 그 절반의 수고도 들이지 않고, 그가 열 마디 할 때 한 마디도 하지 않으면서, 그저 사람 좋은 미소 하나로 이내 두 사람 가운데 덜 두려운 쪽이 되었다."]], [["The young people were all at home, and sustained their share in the introduction very well, with much good humour, and no embarrassment, at least on the part of the sons, who, at seventeen and sixteen, and tall of their age, had all the grandeur of men in the eyes of their little cousin.", "젊은이들은 모두 집에 있었고, 저마다 아이를 맞이하는 몫을 아주 잘 해냈다. 다들 기분 좋게, 조금도 어색해하지 않았는데, 적어도 아들들은 그러했다."], ["The two girls were more at a loss from being younger and in greater awe of their father, who addressed them on the occasion with rather an injudicious particularity.", "열일곱과 열여섯에 나이에 비해 키도 커서, 어린 사촌의 눈에는 어엿한 어른의 위풍을 다 갖추고 있었다. 두 딸은 나이가 더 어린 데다 아버지를 더 어려워한 탓에 다소 어쩔 줄 몰라 했다."], ["But they were too much used to company and praise to have anything like natural shyness; and their confidence increasing from their cousin’s total want of it, they were soon able to take a full survey of her face and her frock in easy indifference.", "아버지가 이 자리에서 딸들에게 지나치게 유별난 분부를 했기 때문이다. 그러나 그들은 사람들 틈에 끼고 칭찬받는 데 너무 익숙해 타고난 수줍음 같은 것은 조금도 없었고, 사촌이 그런 것이 전혀 없는 것을 보고 도리어 자신감이 늘어, 이내 느긋한 무관심 속에 아이의 얼굴과 옷차림을 샅샅이 살필 수 있었다."]], [["They were a remarkably fine family, the sons very well-looking, the daughters decidedly handsome, and all of them well-grown and forward of their age, which produced as striking a difference between the cousins in person, as education had given to their address; and no one would have supposed the girls so nearly of an age as they really were.", "그들은 참으로 훤칠한 집안이었다. 아들들은 아주 잘생겼고 딸들은 두말없이 아름다웠으며, 하나같이 잘 자라 나이보다 조숙했다."], ["There were in fact but two years between the youngest and Fanny.", "그리하여 교육이 말씨에 만들어 놓은 것만큼이나 겉모습에서도 사촌들 사이에 뚜렷한 차이가 났고, 그 여자애들이 실제로 그토록 또래인 줄은 아무도 짐작하지 못했을 것이다."], ["Julia Bertram was only twelve, and Maria but a year older.", "사실 막내와 패니 사이는 두 살 차이밖에 나지 않았다."], ["The little visitor meanwhile was as unhappy as possible.", "줄리아 버트럼이 겨우 열둘, 마리아가 그보다 한 살 위였으니 말이다."], ["Afraid of everybody, ashamed of herself, and longing for the home she had left, she knew not how to look up, and could scarcely speak to be heard, or without crying.", "한편 그 어린 손님은 더할 나위 없이 불행했다. 누구든 무서워하고, 스스로가 부끄럽고, 떠나온 집이 사무치게 그리워, 고개를 들 줄도 몰랐고, 남이 알아듣게 말하지도, 울지 않고 말하지도 못했다."], ["Mrs. Norris had been talking to her the whole way from Northampton of her wonderful good fortune, and the extraordinary degree of gratitude and good behaviour which it ought to produce, and her consciousness of misery was therefore increased by the idea of its being a wicked thing for her not to be happy.", "노리스 부인은 노샘프턴에서 오는 내내 아이에게 그 놀라운 행운을, 그리고 그것이 마땅히 자아내야 할 남다른 감사와 착한 행실을 늘어놓았던 터라, 아이의 서러움은 제가 행복해하지 않는 것이 나쁜 짓이라는 생각 때문에 오히려 더 커졌다."], ["The fatigue, too, of so long a journey, became soon no trifling evil.", "게다가 그토록 긴 여정의 피로도 이내 만만찮은 괴로움이 되었다."], ["In vain were the well-meant condescensions of Sir Thomas, and all the officious prognostications of Mrs.", "토머스 경의 선의 어린 배려도, 아이가 착한 애가 될 거라는 노리스 부인의 온갖 참견 어린 장담도 다 헛일이었다."], ["Norris that she would be a good girl; in vain did Lady Bertram smile and make her sit on the sofa with herself and pug, and vain was even the sight of a gooseberry tart towards giving her comfort; she could scarcely swallow two mouthfuls before tears interrupted her, and sleep seeming to be her likeliest friend, she was taken to finish her sorrows in bed.", "버트럼 부인이 미소를 지으며 아이를 제 곁 퍼그와 함께 소파에 앉힌 것도 헛일이었고, 심지어 구스베리 타르트를 눈앞에 놓아 준 것조차 위로가 되지 못했다. 아이는 두어 입도 채 삼키기 전에 눈물이 복받쳤고, 잠이야말로 가장 미더운 벗인 듯하여, 남은 슬픔은 잠자리에서 마저 풀도록 침대로 데려가졌다."]], [["“This is not a very promising beginning,”", "“그리 앞날이 밝은 시작은 아니네요.” 패니가 방을 나가자 노리스 부인이 말했다."], ["said Mrs.", "“오는 내내 그렇게 일러 줬으니 좀 더 얌전히 굴 줄 알았는데."], ["Norris, when Fanny had left the room.", "처음에 얼마나 잘하느냐에 얼마나 많은 게 달렸는지 그토록 말해 뒀건만. 성미가 조금 뾰로통한 데가 있는 건 아니어야 할 텐데—제 어미가 그런 게 많았거든요."], ["“After all that I said to her as we came along, I thought she would have behaved better; I told her how much might depend upon her acquitting herself well at first.", "하지만 저런 아이한테야 좀 봐줘야지요. 그리고 제 집을 떠나온 걸 서러워하는 게 정말로 그 애 흠이라고는 못 하겠어요."], ["I wish there may not be a little sulkiness of temper—her poor mother had a good deal; but we must make allowances for such a child—and I do not know that her being sorry to leave her home is really against her, for, with all its faults, it was her home, and she cannot as yet understand how much she has changed for the better; but then there is moderation in all things.”", "흠이야 많아도 어쨌든 제 집이었고, 아직은 제 처지가 얼마나 나아졌는지 헤아리지 못하는 거니까요. 하지만 뭐든 정도가 있는 법이지요.”"]], [["It required a longer time, however, than Mrs. Norris was inclined to allow, to reconcile Fanny to the novelty of Mansfield Park, and the separation from everybody she had been used to.", "그러나 패니를 맨스필드 파크라는 낯선 곳과, 여태 익숙하던 모든 이와의 이별에 길들이는 데에는 노리스 부인이 봐주려던 것보다 훨씬 긴 시간이 걸렸다."], ["Her feelings were very acute, and too little understood to be properly attended to.", "아이의 감정은 무척 예민했으나, 제대로 헤아려 보살펴 주기에는 너무 몰이해 속에 있었다."], ["Nobody meant to be unkind, but nobody put themselves out of their way to secure her comfort.", "아무도 매정하게 굴 생각은 없었으나, 아무도 굳이 나서서 아이의 마음을 편하게 해 주려 하지는 않았다."]], [["The holiday allowed to the Miss Bertrams the next day, on purpose to afford leisure for getting acquainted with, and entertaining their young cousin, produced little union.", "이튿날 버트럼 자매에게는 어린 사촌과 낯을 익히고 놀아 주라는 뜻에서 특별히 휴일이 주어졌으나, 사이가 가까워지는 일은 거의 없었다."], ["They could not but hold her cheap on finding that she had but two sashes, and had never learned French; and when they perceived her to be little struck with the duet they were so good as to play, they could do no more than make her a generous present of some of their least valued toys, and leave her to herself, while they adjourned to whatever might be the favourite holiday sport of the moment, making artificial flowers or wasting gold paper.", "아이가 장식띠를 두 개밖에 갖고 있지 않고 프랑스어라고는 배운 적 없다는 것을 알고 나자 자매는 아이를 대수롭잖게 여기지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 자기들이 친절히 연주해 준 이중주에 아이가 별로 감동하지 않는 것을 보고는, 안 쓰는 장난감 몇 개를 선심 쓰듯 안겨 주고 저는 혼자 내버려 둔 채, 저희들은 그때그때 좋아하는 휴일 놀이가 무엇이든—조화를 만들거나 금박지를 축내거나—그리로 자리를 옮겨 버렸다."]], [["Fanny, whether near or from her cousins, whether in the schoolroom, the drawing-room, or the shrubbery, was equally forlorn, finding something to fear in every person and place.", "패니는 사촌들 곁에 있든 떨어져 있든, 공부방에 있든 응접실에 있든 관목숲에 있든 한결같이 외로웠고, 사람마다 곳마다 무언가 무서운 것을 찾아냈다. 버트럼 부인의 침묵에 풀이 죽었고, 토머스 경의 근엄한 표정에 주눅이 들었으며, 노리스 부인의 훈계에는 그만 기가 꺾였다."], ["She was disheartened by Lady Bertram’s silence, awed by Sir Thomas’s grave looks, and quite overcome by Mrs. Norris’s admonitions.", "손위 사촌들은 아이의 몸집을 두고 이러쿵저러쿵하며 자존심을 상하게 했고, 수줍음을 들추어 무안을 주었다."], ["Her elder cousins mortified her by reflections on her size, and abashed her by noticing her shyness: Miss Lee wondered at her ignorance, and the maid-servants sneered at her clothes; and when to these sorrows was added the idea of the brothers and sisters among whom she had always been important as playfellow, instructress, and nurse, the despondence that sunk her little heart was severe.", "리 선생은 아이의 무지를 의아해했고, 하녀들은 아이의 옷차림을 비웃었다. 그리고 이런 서러움에, 늘 놀이동무이자 선생이자 보모로서 제가 소중한 존재였던 동생들 생각까지 더해지자, 어린 가슴을 짓누르는 낙심은 이만저만이 아니었다."]], [["The grandeur of the house astonished, but could not console her.", "저택의 웅장함은 아이를 놀라게 했으나 위로해 주지는 못했다. 방들은 너무 커서 마음 편히 움직일 수 없었다."], ["The rooms were too large for her to move in with ease: whatever she touched she expected to injure, and she crept about in constant terror of something or other; often retreating towards her own chamber to cry; and the little girl who was spoken of in the drawing-room when she left it at night as seeming so desirably sensible of her peculiar good fortune, ended every day’s sorrows by sobbing herself to sleep.", "무엇이든 손을 대면 상하게 할까 봐, 아이는 늘 이런저런 두려움에 떨며 조심조심 다녔고, 걸핏하면 울려고 제 방으로 물러나곤 했다. 밤에 응접실을 나설 때면 제 남다른 복을 어지간히 잘 아는 듯싶다는 말을 듣던 그 어린 여자아이는, 날마다의 서러움을 흐느껴 울다 잠드는 것으로 끝맺었다."], ["A week had passed in this way, and no suspicion of it conveyed by her quiet passive manner, when she was found one morning by her cousin Edmund, the youngest of the sons, sitting crying on the attic stairs.", "이렇게 한 주가 지나도록 그 조용하고 순한 태도로는 아무도 눈치채지 못하고 있었는데, 어느 날 아침 막내아들인 사촌 에드먼드가 다락 계단에 앉아 울고 있는 아이를 발견했다."]], [["“My dear little cousin,”", "“나의 어린 사촌아,”"], ["said he, with all the gentleness of an excellent nature, “what can be the matter?”", "더없이 선한 천성에서 우러난 부드러움으로 그가 말했다. “대체 무슨 일이니?”"], ["And sitting down by her, he was at great pains to overcome her shame in being so surprised, and persuade her to speak openly.", "그러고는 아이 곁에 앉아, 이렇게 들킨 것을 부끄러워하는 마음을 애써 달래며 속을 털어놓게 하려고 무진 애를 썼다."], ["Was she ill?", "어디가 아픈 거니?"], ["or was anybody angry with her?", "누가 너한테 화라도 냈어?"], ["or had she quarrelled with Maria and Julia?", "마리아나 줄리아와 다투기라도 했니?"], ["or was she puzzled about anything in her lesson that he could explain?", "아니면 공부에서 무언가 모르는 게 있어 내가 설명해 주면 좋겠니? 한마디로, 내가 구해 줄 수 있는 것, 해 줄 수 있는 것이 뭐든 있는 거니?"], ["Did she, in short, want anything he could possibly get her, or do for her?", "오랫동안 “아니에요, 아니에요—전혀요—아니에요, 고마워요” 말고는 아무 대답도 얻지 못했다."], ["For a long while no answer could be obtained beyond a “no, no—not at all—no, thank you”; but he still persevered; and no sooner had he begun to revert to her own home, than her increased sobs explained to him where the grievance lay.", "그래도 그는 굽히지 않았다. 그리고 아이의 집 이야기로 말머리를 돌리기가 무섭게 부쩍 커진 흐느낌이 서러움의 근원이 어디 있는지 그에게 일러 주었다."], ["He tried to console her.", "그는 아이를 달래려 애썼다."]], [["“You are sorry to leave Mama, my dear little Fanny,”", "“어머니를 떠나온 게 서러운 거구나, 나의 어린 패니야.” 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “which shows you to be a very good girl; but you must remember that you are with relations and friends, who all love you, and wish to make you happy.", "“그건 네가 참 착한 아이라는 뜻이란다. 하지만 잊지 마라, 너는 지금 너를 다 사랑하고 너를 행복하게 해 주고 싶어 하는 친척들과 벗들 곁에 있단다."], ["Let us walk out in the park, and you shall tell me all about your brothers and sisters.”", "우리 공원으로 나가 걷자꾸나. 그러면서 네 형제자매 이야기를 다 들려주렴.”"]], [["On pursuing the subject, he found that, dear as all these brothers and sisters generally were, there was one among them who ran more in her thoughts than the rest.", "이야기를 이어 가면서 그는, 그 형제자매가 다 소중하기는 해도 그중 유독 아이의 마음에 더 자주 떠오르는 이가 하나 있음을 알게 되었다."], ["It was William whom she talked of most, and wanted most to see.", "아이가 가장 많이 이야기하고 가장 보고 싶어 한 것은 윌리엄이었다."], ["William, the eldest, a year older than herself, her constant companion and friend; her advocate with her mother (of whom he was the darling) in every distress.", "맏이인 윌리엄은 저보다 한 살 위로, 늘 함께 지낸 동무이자 벗이었고, (어머니가 끔찍이 아끼는 아들이었기에) 무슨 괴로운 일이 있을 때마다 어머니 앞에서 저를 두둔해 주던 이였다. “윌리엄은 제가 떠나오는 걸 싫어했어요."], ["“William did not like she should come away; he had told her he should miss her very much indeed.”", "저를 정말 무척 그리워할 거라고 그랬어요.” “하지만 윌리엄이 너한테 편지를 쓸 거야, 틀림없이.”"], ["“But William will write to you, I dare say.”", "“네, 그러겠다고 약속했어요."], ["“Yes, he had promised he would, but he had told her to write first.”", "그런데 저더러 먼저 쓰라고 했어요.” “그럼 언제 쓸 거니?”"], ["“And when shall you do it?”", "아이는 고개를 떨구고 머뭇머뭇 대답했다."], ["She hung her head and answered hesitatingly, “she did not know; she had not any paper.”", "“모르겠어요. 종이가 없어서요.”"]], [["“If that be all your difficulty, I will furnish you with paper and every other material, and you may write your letter whenever you choose.", "“그게 다라면, 내가 종이며 필요한 건 뭐든 대 줄 테니, 언제든 네가 쓰고 싶을 때 편지를 쓰렴."], ["Would it make you happy to write to William?”", "윌리엄한테 편지를 쓰면 기쁘겠니?”"]], [["“Yes, very.”", "“네, 아주요.”"]], [["“Then let it be done now.", "“그럼 지금 쓰자."], ["Come with me into the breakfast-room, we shall find everything there, and be sure of having the room to ourselves.”", "나랑 아침식사방으로 가자. 거기 가면 다 있고, 우리끼리만 있을 수 있을 거야.”"]], [["“But, cousin, will it go to the post?”", "“그런데 사촌 오빠, 이게 우편으로 부쳐질까요?”"]], [["“Yes, depend upon me it shall: it shall go with the other letters; and, as your uncle will frank it, it will cost William nothing.”", "“그럼, 나만 믿으렴. 다른 편지들이랑 같이 부칠 거야. 게다가 삼촌이 무료 우송으로 부쳐 주실 테니 윌리엄한테는 한 푼도 들지 않을 거란다.”"]], [["“My uncle!”", "“삼촌이요!”"], ["repeated Fanny, with a frightened look.", "패니가 겁먹은 얼굴로 되뇌었다."]], [["“Yes, when you have written the letter, I will take it to my father to frank.”", "“그래, 네가 편지를 다 쓰면 내가 아버지께 가져가서 우송 서명을 받을 거야.”"]], [["Fanny thought it a bold measure, but offered no further resistance; and they went together into the breakfast-room, where Edmund prepared her paper, and ruled her lines with all the goodwill that her brother could himself have felt, and probably with somewhat more exactness.", "패니는 그것이 대담한 짓이다 싶었으나 더는 버티지 않았다. 그렇게 둘은 함께 아침식사방으로 갔고, 거기서 에드먼드는 아이에게 종이를 마련해 주고, 친오빠라도 그만큼밖에 못 냈을 정성으로—아마 그보다는 좀 더 반듯하게—줄을 그어 주었다."], ["He continued with her the whole time of her writing, to assist her with his penknife or his orthography, as either were wanted; and added to these attentions, which she felt very much, a kindness to her brother which delighted her beyond all the rest.", "그는 아이가 편지를 쓰는 내내 곁을 지키며, 주머니칼이든 철자든 필요할 때마다 거들어 주었다. 그리고 아이가 무척이나 고맙게 여긴 이런 마음씀에 더해, 그 무엇보다도 아이를 기쁘게 한 오라비를 향한 친절까지 베풀었다."], ["He wrote with his own hand his love to his cousin William, and sent him half a guinea under the seal.", "그는 손수 사촌 윌리엄에게 안부의 말을 적고, 봉인 안에 반 기니를 넣어 부쳤다."], ["Fanny’s feelings on the occasion were such as she believed herself incapable of expressing; but her countenance and a few artless words fully conveyed all their gratitude and delight, and her cousin began to find her an interesting object.", "그때 패니의 감정은 스스로도 도저히 말로 옮길 수 없으리라 여겨질 만한 것이었다. 그러나 그 얼굴빛과 몇 마디 꾸밈없는 말이 그 고마움과 기쁨을 낱낱이 전해 주었고, 사촌은 아이가 흥미로운 존재로 여겨지기 시작했다."], ["He talked to her more, and, from all that she said, was convinced of her having an affectionate heart, and a strong desire of doing right; and he could perceive her to be farther entitled to attention by great sensibility of her situation, and great timidity.", "그는 아이와 더 많은 이야기를 나누었고, 아이가 하는 모든 말에서 그 애가 정 많은 마음과 옳게 살고자 하는 강한 바람을 지녔음을 확신하게 되었다. 또 제 처지를 예민하게 느끼는 감수성과 큰 소심함으로 미루어, 아이가 마땅히 더 마음을 써 줄 만한 아이임을 알아보았다."], ["He had never knowingly given her pain, but he now felt that she required more positive kindness; and with that view endeavoured, in the first place, to lessen her fears of them all, and gave her especially a great deal of good advice as to playing with Maria and Julia, and being as merry as possible.", "그는 아이에게 알고서 아픔을 준 적이 한 번도 없었으나, 이제 아이에게는 좀 더 적극적인 친절이 필요하다고 느꼈다. 그런 뜻에서 우선 아이가 모두를 두려워하는 마음을 덜어 주려 애썼고, 특히 마리아와 줄리아와 함께 놀며 되도록 즐겁게 지내라고 좋은 말을 많이 해 주었다."]], [["From this day Fanny grew more comfortable.", "이날부터 패니는 한결 마음이 편해졌다."], ["She felt that she had a friend, and the kindness of her cousin Edmund gave her better spirits with everybody else.", "제게 벗이 하나 생겼다는 것을 느꼈고, 사촌 에드먼드의 친절 덕에 다른 모든 이 앞에서도 기운이 났다."], ["The place became less strange, and the people less formidable; and if there were some amongst them whom she could not cease to fear, she began at least to know their ways, and to catch the best manner of conforming to them.", "그곳은 덜 낯설어졌고 사람들도 덜 두려워졌다. 도무지 무서움을 떨칠 수 없는 이들이 그중 더러 있었다 해도, 적어도 그들의 방식을 알아 가고, 거기 맞추는 가장 좋은 요령을 익히기 시작했다."], ["The little rusticities and awkwardnesses which had at first made grievous inroads on the tranquillity of all, and not least of herself, necessarily wore away, and she was no longer materially afraid to appear before her uncle, nor did her aunt Norris’s voice make her start very much.", "처음에는 모두의, 그중에서도 특히 제 마음의 평온을 크게 어지럽히던 그 촌스러움과 어설픔도 자연히 스러져 갔다. 이제 삼촌 앞에 나서는 것이 그다지 무섭지 않았고, 노리스 이모의 목소리에도 그리 크게 움찔하지 않았다."], ["To her cousins she became occasionally an acceptable companion.", "사촌들에게 아이는 이따금 그런대로 반가운 동무가 되었다."], ["Though unworthy, from inferiority of age and strength, to be their constant associate, their pleasures and schemes were sometimes of a nature to make a third very useful, especially when that third was of an obliging, yielding temper; and they could not but own, when their aunt inquired into her faults, or their brother Edmund urged her claims to their kindness, that “Fanny was good-natured enough.”", "나이도 어리고 힘도 약해 늘 붙어 다닐 상대로는 모자랐으나, 자매의 놀이며 궁리에는 때때로 셋째가 있으면 요긴한 것들이 있었고, 더구나 그 셋째가 고분고분 잘 맞춰 주는 성미라면 더욱 그러했다. 그래서 이모가 아이의 흠을 캐물을 때나 오라비 에드먼드가 아이에게 친절히 대해 주라고 채근할 때면, 자매도 “패니가 그만하면 심성은 착하다”고 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["Edmund was uniformly kind himself; and she had nothing worse to endure on the part of Tom than that sort of merriment which a young man of seventeen will always think fair with a child of ten.", "에드먼드는 스스로 한결같이 친절했다. 그리고 톰에게서 아이가 견뎌야 했던 것이라고는, 열일곱 청년이 열 살배기 아이를 두고 으레 짓궂어도 괜찮다 여기는 그런 장난기가 고작이었다."], ["He was just entering into life, full of spirits, and with all the liberal dispositions of an eldest son, who feels born only for expense and enjoyment.", "톰은 이제 막 세상에 발을 들이는 참으로, 혈기가 넘치고, 오로지 쓰고 즐기려고 태어난 줄 아는 맏아들 특유의 활수한 기질을 두루 갖추고 있었다."], ["His kindness to his little cousin was consistent with his situation and rights: he made her some very pretty presents, and laughed at her.", "어린 사촌에게 베푸는 그의 친절은 제 처지며 권리에 걸맞은 것이었다. 아이에게 아주 예쁜 선물을 몇 가지 해 주고는, 아이를 보며 웃었다."]], [["As her appearance and spirits improved, Sir Thomas and Mrs.", "아이의 겉모습과 기색이 나아지자 토머스 경과 노리스 부인은 저희의 자비로운 계획을 한층 흐뭇하게 되새겼다."], ["Norris thought with greater satisfaction of their benevolent plan; and it was pretty soon decided between them that, though far from clever, she showed a tractable disposition, and seemed likely to give them little trouble.", "그리고 이내 두 사람 사이에서, 아이가 영리하다고는 도저히 못 해도 고분고분한 성미를 보이고 별 말썽을 부릴 것 같지 않다는 결론이 내려졌다."], ["A mean opinion of her abilities was not confined to them.", "아이의 능력을 얕잡아 보는 것은 그 둘에게만 국한된 일이 아니었다."], ["Fanny could read, work, and write, but she had been taught nothing more; and as her cousins found her ignorant of many things with which they had been long familiar, they thought her prodigiously stupid, and for the first two or three weeks were continually bringing some fresh report of it into the drawing-room.", "패니는 읽고, 바느질하고, 쓸 줄은 알았으나 그 이상은 아무것도 배운 바가 없었다. 그래서 사촌들은 저희가 진작부터 익숙하던 숱한 것들을 아이가 모르는 것을 보고 아이를 지독히 아둔하다 여겼으며, 처음 두어 주 동안은 응접실에 그런 소식을 새록새록 물어 날랐다."], ["“Dear mama, only think, my cousin cannot put the map of Europe together—or my cousin cannot tell the principal rivers in Russia—or, she never heard of Asia Minor—or she does not know the difference between water-colours and crayons!—How strange!—Did you ever hear anything so stupid?”", "“어머, 어머니, 글쎄 생각해 보세요, 우리 사촌은 유럽 지도를 못 맞춰요—아니, 러시아의 주요 강 이름도 못 대요—아니, 소아시아라는 말은 들어 본 적도 없대요—아니, 수채물감이랑 크레용도 구별 못 해요!—어쩜 그렇게 이상해요!—이렇게 아둔한 애 얘기 들어 보신 적 있으세요?”"]], [["“My dear,”", "“얘들아,” 사려 깊은 이모가 대꾸하곤 했다."], ["their considerate aunt would reply, “it is very bad, but you must not expect everybody to be as forward and quick at learning as yourself.”", "“그거 참 딱하구나. 하지만 다들 너희만큼 배움이 빠르고 조숙하기를 바라서는 안 된단다.”"]], [["“But, aunt, she is really so very ignorant!—Do you know, we asked her last night which way she would go to get to Ireland; and she said, she should cross to the Isle of Wight.", "“그렇지만 이모, 그 앤 정말 너무 아무것도 몰라요!—글쎄, 어젯밤에 우리가 아일랜드에 가려면 어느 길로 가겠느냐고 물었더니, 와이트섬을 건너가면 된다지 뭐예요."], ["She thinks of nothing but the Isle of Wight, and she calls it the Island, as if there were no other island in the world.", "온통 와이트섬 생각뿐이라, 세상에 다른 섬이라고는 없는 것처럼 그걸 그냥 ‘그 섬’이라고 불러요."], ["I am sure I should have been ashamed of myself, if I had not known better long before I was so old as she is.", "저라면 그 애 나이가 되기 훨씬 전에 그만한 것도 몰랐다면 정말 부끄러웠을 거예요."], ["I cannot remember the time when I did not know a great deal that she has not the least notion of yet.", "저는 그 애가 아직 티끌만큼도 모르는 걸 많이 알지 못하던 때가 언제였는지 기억도 안 나요."], ["How long ago it is, aunt, since we used to repeat the chronological order of the kings of England, with the dates of their accession, and most of the principal events of their reigns!”", "이모, 우리가 잉글랜드 왕들을 즉위 연도까지 붙여 가며 순서대로, 게다가 치세의 주요 사건들까지 줄줄 외우던 게 벌써 얼마나 오래전 일이에요!”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“맞아요,” 다른 아이가 거들었다."], ["added the other; “and of the Roman emperors as low as Severus; besides a great deal of the heathen mythology, and all the metals, semi-metals, planets, and distinguished philosophers.”", "“로마 황제들도 세베루스까지 다 외웠고요. 그것 말고도 이교의 신화며, 온갖 금속과 준금속과 행성과 이름난 철학자들까지 잔뜩 외웠잖아요.”"]], [["“Very true indeed, my dears, but you are blessed with wonderful memories, and your poor cousin has probably none at all. There is a vast deal of difference in memories, as well as in everything else, and therefore you must make allowance for your cousin, and pity her deficiency. And remember that, if you are ever so forward and clever yourselves, you should always be modest; for, much as you know already, there is a great deal more for you to learn.”", "“참말 그렇지, 얘들아. 하지만 너희는 놀라운 기억력을 복으로 타고났고, 너희 딱한 사촌은 아마 그런 게 도통 없는 거란다. 기억력이란 다른 모든 것과 마찬가지로 사람마다 천차만별이거든. 그러니 사촌을 좀 봐주고, 그 애의 모자람을 가엾이 여겨야 해. 그리고 잊지 마라. 너희 자신이 아무리 조숙하고 영리하더라도 언제나 겸손해야 한단다. 너희가 이미 아는 게 많다 해도, 앞으로 배워야 할 게 훨씬 더 많으니까.”"]], [["“Yes, I know there is, till I am seventeen.", "“네, 열일곱이 될 때까지는 그렇겠지요."], ["But I must tell you another thing of Fanny, so odd and so stupid.", "그런데 이모, 패니 얘기 하나 더 해 드릴게요, 어찌나 별나고 아둔한지."], ["Do you know, she says she does not want to learn either music or drawing.”", "글쎄 그 애는 음악도 그림도 배우고 싶지 않대요.”"]], [["“To be sure, my dear, that is very stupid indeed, and shows a great want of genius and emulation.", "“하긴 얘야, 그거야말로 참 아둔한 소리고, 재능도 향상심도 크게 모자란 표시지."], ["But, all things considered, I do not know whether it is not as well that it should be so, for, though you know (owing to me) your papa and mama are so good as to bring her up with you, it is not at all necessary that she should be as accomplished as you are;—on the contrary, it is much more desirable that there should be a difference.”", "하지만 이래저래 따져 보면, 그편이 차라리 나을는지도 모르겠구나. 왜냐하면, (다 내 덕이지만) 너희 아버지와 어머니가 그토록 착하셔서 그 애를 너희와 함께 길러 주시긴 해도, 그 애가 너희만큼 온갖 재주를 갖출 필요는 조금도 없거든—오히려 서로 차이가 나는 편이 훨씬 바람직하고말고.”"]], [["Such were the counsels by which Mrs.", "노리스 부인이 조카딸들의 마음을 빚는 데 보탠 훈수란 이런 것들이었다."], ["Norris assisted to form her nieces’ minds; and it is not very wonderful that, with all their promising talents and early information, they should be entirely deficient in the less common acquirements of self-knowledge, generosity and humility.", "그러니 그 촉망받는 재주와 이른 학식에도 불구하고 자매가 자기 성찰이며 너그러움이며 겸손이라는, 그리 흔치 않은 소양에는 아주 결여되어 있었던 것도 그리 놀랄 일이 아니다."], ["In everything but disposition they were admirably taught.", "성품만 빼면 그들은 나무랄 데 없이 잘 배웠다."], ["Sir Thomas did not know what was wanting, because, though a truly anxious father, he was not outwardly affectionate, and the reserve of his manner repressed all the flow of their spirits before him.", "토머스 경은 무엇이 모자란지 알지 못했다. 참으로 노심초사하는 아버지이기는 했으나 겉으로 다정하지는 못했고, 그 삼가는 태도가 자기 앞에서는 딸들의 활기를 온통 짓눌러 버렸기 때문이다."]], [["To the education of her daughters Lady Bertram paid not the smallest attention.", "버트럼 부인은 딸들의 교육에 눈곱만큼도 마음을 쓰지 않았다. 그런 데 신경 쓸 겨를이 없었다."], ["She had not time for such cares.", "그녀는 곱게 차려입고 소파에 앉아, 쓸모도 아름다움도 없는 기다란 바느질감을 붙들고 하루를 보내는 여자였다."], ["She was a woman who spent her days in sitting, nicely dressed, on a sofa, doing some long piece of needlework, of little use and no beauty, thinking more of her pug than her children, but very indulgent to the latter when it did not put herself to inconvenience, guided in everything important by Sir Thomas, and in smaller concerns by her sister.", "자식보다 제 퍼그를 더 마음에 두면서도, 제게 불편만 끼치지 않는다면 자식들에게는 무척 너그러웠고, 중요한 일은 뭐든 토머스 경에게, 소소한 일은 언니에게 맡겨 이끌려 갔다. 설령 딸들을 위해 쓸 겨를이 더 있었다 한들, 그녀는 아마 그럴 필요가 없다고 여겼을 것이다."], ["Had she possessed greater leisure for the service of her girls, she would probably have supposed it unnecessary, for they were under the care of a governess, with proper masters, and could want nothing more.", "딸들은 가정교사의 보살핌 아래 마땅한 선생들까지 두고 있었으니 더 바랄 게 없었기 때문이다. 패니가 배움이 더디다는 것에 대해서는, “참 안된 일이지만, 원래 아둔한 사람도 있는 법이니 패니가 더 애를 써야지."], ["As for Fanny’s being stupid at learning, “she could only say it was very unlucky, but some people were stupid, and Fanny must take more pains: she did not know what else was to be done; and, except her being so dull, she must add she saw no harm in the poor little thing, and always found her very handy and quick in carrying messages, and fetching what she wanted.”", "달리 어쩌겠느냐. 그리고 그렇게 굼뜬 것만 빼면 그 딱한 것한테 나쁜 데라고는 없고, 심부름을 시키거나 필요한 걸 가져오게 하면 늘 손발이 야무지고 빠르더라”고 할 뿐이었다."]], [["Fanny, with all her faults of ignorance and timidity, was fixed at Mansfield Park, and learning to transfer in its favour much of her attachment to her former home, grew up there not unhappily among her cousins.", "패니는 무지하고 소심하다는 온갖 흠에도 불구하고 맨스필드 파크에 자리를 잡았고, 옛집에 쏟던 정을 상당 부분 이곳으로 옮기는 법을 익혀 가며, 사촌들 틈에서 그런대로 불행하지 않게 자라났다."], ["There was no positive ill-nature in Maria or Julia; and though Fanny was often mortified by their treatment of her, she thought too lowly of her own claims to feel injured by it.", "마리아나 줄리아에게 대놓고 심술궂은 데는 없었다. 그리고 패니는 그들의 대우에 종종 자존심이 상했으나, 제 몫에 대한 생각이 너무 낮아 그것을 서러워할 줄도 몰랐다."]], [["From about the time of her entering the family, Lady Bertram, in consequence of a little ill-health, and a great deal of indolence, gave up the house in town, which she had been used to occupy every spring, and remained wholly in the country, leaving Sir Thomas to attend his duty in Parliament, with whatever increase or diminution of comfort might arise from her absence.", "아이가 이 집안에 들어올 무렵부터, 버트럼 부인은 몸이 좀 약해진 데다 워낙 게을렀던 탓에, 해마다 봄이면 지내던 도시의 집을 접고 시골에만 눌러앉았다. 토머스 경은 홀로 남아 의회의 소임을 보러 다녔고, 아내의 부재로 안락이 늘든 줄든 그것은 그가 감당할 몫이었다."], ["In the country, therefore, the Miss Bertrams continued to exercise their memories, practise their duets, and grow tall and womanly: and their father saw them becoming in person, manner, and accomplishments, everything that could satisfy his anxiety.", "그리하여 버트럼 자매는 시골에서 계속 기억력을 갈고, 이중주를 익히고, 키가 크며 처녀 티가 나 갔다. 아버지는 딸들이 겉모습에서나 몸가짐에서나 재주에서나, 제 노심초사를 흡족게 할 온갖 모습으로 자라 가는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["His eldest son was careless and extravagant, and had already given him much uneasiness; but his other children promised him nothing but good.", "맏아들은 무심하고 헤퍼 벌써부터 그에게 적잖은 근심을 안겼으나, 다른 자식들은 그에게 오로지 좋은 것만을 약속했다. 그는 느꼈다."], ["His daughters, he felt, while they retained the name of Bertram, must be giving it new grace, and in quitting it, he trusted, would extend its respectable alliances; and the character of Edmund, his strong good sense and uprightness of mind, bid most fairly for utility, honour, and happiness to himself and all his connexions.", "딸들은 버트럼이라는 성을 지니는 동안에는 그 이름에 새로운 기품을 더해 줄 것이고, 그 성을 버릴 때에는—그가 믿기로—그 이름의 어엿한 인척을 넓혀 주리라고. 그리고 에드먼드의 인품, 그 굳센 양식과 곧은 마음은 저 자신은 물론 제게 딸린 모든 이에게 쓸모와 명예와 행복을 안겨 줄 가망이 가장 밝았다."], ["He was to be a clergyman.", "에드먼드는 장차 성직자가 될 참이었다."]], [["Amid the cares and the complacency which his own children suggested, Sir Thomas did not forget to do what he could for the children of Mrs.", "제 자식들이 안겨 주는 근심과 흐뭇함 가운데서도, 토머스 경은 프라이스 부인의 아이들을 위해 할 수 있는 일을 잊지 않았다."], ["Price: he assisted her liberally in the education and disposal of her sons as they became old enough for a determinate pursuit; and Fanny, though almost totally separated from her family, was sensible of the truest satisfaction in hearing of any kindness towards them, or of anything at all promising in their situation or conduct.", "그는 그 아들들이 어떤 진로를 정할 나이가 되자, 교육과 앞길을 놓아 주는 데 아낌없이 도왔다. 그리고 패니는 제 식구와 거의 완전히 떨어져 있으면서도, 그들에게 베풀어진 친절이나 그들의 처지나 처신에 조금이라도 밝은 소식을 들으면 더없이 참된 흐뭇함을 느꼈다."], ["Once, and once only, in the course of many years, had she the happiness of being with William.", "여러 해가 흐르는 동안 단 한 번, 오직 한 번, 아이는 윌리엄과 함께 지내는 기쁨을 누렸다. 나머지 형제자매는 얼굴도 보지 못했다."], ["Of the rest she saw nothing: nobody seemed to think of her ever going amongst them again, even for a visit, nobody at home seemed to want her; but William determining, soon after her removal, to be a sailor, was invited to spend a week with his sister in Northamptonshire before he went to sea.", "아무도 아이가 잠깐 다니러라도 다시 그들 틈에 가는 것을 생각하는 것 같지 않았고, 고향의 누구도 아이를 원하는 것 같지 않았다. 그러나 윌리엄은 아이가 떠난 지 얼마 안 되어 뱃사람이 되기로 마음먹었고, 바다로 나가기 전에 노샘프턴셔에서 누이와 한 주를 지내라는 초대를 받았다."], ["Their eager affection in meeting, their exquisite delight in being together, their hours of happy mirth, and moments of serious conference, may be imagined; as well as the sanguine views and spirits of the boy even to the last, and the misery of the girl when he left her.", "만났을 때의 애틋한 정, 함께 있는 더없는 기쁨, 즐겁게 웃던 시간과 진지하게 이야기 나누던 순간은 능히 짐작할 수 있으리라. 마지막까지도 식지 않던 소년의 부푼 꿈과 기상도, 그가 떠날 때 소녀가 느낀 서러움도."], ["Luckily the visit happened in the Christmas holidays, when she could directly look for comfort to her cousin Edmund; and he told her such charming things of what William was to do, and be hereafter, in consequence of his profession, as made her gradually admit that the separation might have some use.", "다행히 그 방문은 크리스마스 휴가에 이루어져, 아이는 곧장 사촌 에드먼드에게서 위로를 구할 수 있었다. 그는 윌리엄이 그 직업 덕에 앞으로 무슨 일을 하고 어떤 사람이 될지 그토록 멋진 이야기들을 들려주어, 아이가 차츰 그 이별에도 얼마간 쓸모가 있을지 모른다고 여기게 만들었다."], ["Edmund’s friendship never failed her: his leaving Eton for Oxford made no change in his kind dispositions, and only afforded more frequent opportunities of proving them.", "에드먼드의 우정은 한 번도 아이를 저버리지 않았다. 이튼을 떠나 옥스퍼드로 간 뒤에도 그 다정한 마음씨는 조금도 변하지 않았고, 그것을 증명할 기회만 더 잦아졌을 뿐이다."], ["Without any display of doing more than the rest, or any fear of doing too much, he was always true to her interests, and considerate of her feelings, trying to make her good qualities understood, and to conquer the diffidence which prevented their being more apparent; giving her advice, consolation, and encouragement.", "남보다 더 해 준다고 티를 내는 법도, 너무 많이 해 줄까 봐 사리는 법도 없이, 그는 언제나 아이의 이익에 진심이었고 아이의 마음을 헤아렸으며, 아이의 좋은 점이 남에게 알려지도록, 또 그것이 더 드러나지 못하게 가로막는 그 수줍음을 이겨 내도록 애썼다. 아이에게 조언과 위로와 격려를 아끼지 않으면서."]], [["Kept back as she was by everybody else, his single support could not bring her forward; but his attentions were otherwise of the highest importance in assisting the improvement of her mind, and extending its pleasures. He knew her to be clever, to have a quick apprehension as well as good sense, and a fondness for reading, which, properly directed, must be an education in itself. Miss Lee taught her French, and heard her read the daily portion of history; but he recommended the books which charmed her leisure hours, he encouraged her taste, and corrected her judgment: he made reading useful by talking to her of what she read, and heightened its attraction by judicious praise. In return for such services she loved him better than anybody in the world except William: her heart was divided between the two.", "다른 모든 이가 아이를 뒤로 밀어 두는 터라 그의 홀로 된 뒷받침만으로는 아이를 앞으로 끌어낼 수 없었다. 그러나 그의 마음씀은 다른 면에서, 곧 아이의 정신을 갈고닦고 그 즐거움을 넓혀 주는 데 더없이 중요했다. 그는 아이가 영리하다는 것을, 좋은 분별력에 더해 빠른 이해력을 지녔고, 제대로만 이끌면 그 자체로 하나의 교육이 될 독서 취미를 지녔음을 알아보았다. 리 선생은 아이에게 프랑스어를 가르치고 날마다 정해진 분량의 역사를 읽혔다. 그러나 아이의 한가한 시간을 매혹한 책들을 골라 준 것은 그였다. 그는 아이의 취향을 북돋고 판단을 바로잡아 주었다. 아이가 읽은 것에 대해 함께 이야기함으로써 독서를 유익하게 만들었고, 사려 깊은 칭찬으로 그 매력을 한층 돋우었다. 이런 보살핌에 대한 보답으로 아이는 윌리엄을 빼면 세상 누구보다 그를 사랑했다. 아이의 마음은 그 둘 사이에 나뉘어 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER III", "# 제3장"]], [["The first event of any importance in the family was the death of Mr.", "이 집안에서 처음으로 일어난 이렇다 할 사건은 노리스 씨의 죽음이었다."], ["Norris, which happened when Fanny was about fifteen, and necessarily introduced alterations and novelties. Mrs.", "패니가 열다섯 살쯤 되었을 때의 일로, 그것은 필연적으로 여러 변화와 새로운 국면을 불러왔다."], ["Norris, on quitting the Parsonage, removed first to the Park, and afterwards to a small house of Sir Thomas’s in the village, and consoled herself for the loss of her husband by considering that she could do very well without him; and for her reduction of income by the evident necessity of stricter economy.", "노리스 부인은 목사관을 떠나 처음에는 파크로 옮겨 왔다가, 나중에는 마을에 있는 토머스 경의 작은 집으로 거처를 옮겼다. 그러고는 남편을 잃은 슬픔은 그가 없어도 얼마든지 잘 지낼 수 있다는 생각으로, 수입이 줄어든 것은 더 빠듯이 절약해야 할 게 뻔하다는 생각으로 달랬다."]], [["The living was hereafter for Edmund; and, had his uncle died a few years sooner, it would have been duly given to some friend to hold till he were old enough for orders.", "그 성직록은 이후 에드먼드의 몫이 될 것이었다. 만약 그의 삼촌이 몇 해만 일찍 세상을 떠났더라면, 에드먼드가 서품 받을 나이가 될 때까지 어느 벗에게 맡겨 두었을 터였다."], ["But Tom’s extravagance had, previous to that event, been so great as to render a different disposal of the next presentation necessary, and the younger brother must help to pay for the pleasures of the elder.", "그러나 그 일이 있기 전에 톰의 낭비가 어찌나 심했던지, 다음번 임명권을 달리 처분하지 않을 수 없게 되었고, 결국 동생이 형의 즐거움 값을 함께 치러야 했다. 에드먼드를 위해 실제로 맡아 둔 다른 성직록이 하나 더 있기는 했다."], ["There was another family living actually held for Edmund; but though this circumstance had made the arrangement somewhat easier to Sir Thomas’s conscience, he could not but feel it to be an act of injustice, and he earnestly tried to impress his eldest son with the same conviction, in the hope of its producing a better effect than anything he had yet been able to say or do.", "그러나 그 사정 덕에 토머스 경의 양심이 다소 가벼워지기는 했어도, 그것이 부당한 처사임을 느끼지 않을 수 없었다. 그리하여 그는 맏아들에게도 같은 생각을 절실히 심어 주려 애썼는데, 여태 그가 말이나 행동으로 이룰 수 있었던 그 무엇보다 더 나은 효과가 나기를 바라서였다."]], [["“I blush for you, Tom,”", "“톰, 너를 생각하면 낯이 뜨겁다.”"], ["said he, in his most dignified manner; “I blush for the expedient which I am driven on, and I trust I may pity your feelings as a brother on the occasion.", "그는 더없이 위엄 있는 태도로 말했다. “내가 이런 궁여지책에 내몰린 것이 부끄럽고, 이 마당에 형으로서 네가 느낄 심정은 딱하게 여겨 주마."], ["You have robbed Edmund for ten, twenty, thirty years, perhaps for life, of more than half the income which ought to be his.", "너는 십 년, 이십 년, 삼십 년, 어쩌면 평생토록 에드먼드가 마땅히 받아야 할 수입의 절반이 넘는 것을 그 아이에게서 빼앗은 것이다."], ["It may hereafter be in my power, or in yours (I hope it will), to procure him better preferment; but it must not be forgotten that no benefit of that sort would have been beyond his natural claims on us, and that nothing can, in fact, be an equivalent for the certain advantage which he is now obliged to forego through the urgency of your debts.”", "훗날 내 힘으로든 네 힘으로든 (부디 그리되기를 바란다) 그 아이에게 더 나은 성직을 마련해 줄 수 있을지도 모르지. 그러나 잊어서는 안 될 것이, 그런 은전이라 한들 어차피 그 아이가 우리에게 당연히 요구할 수 있는 몫을 넘지 않는다는 것, 그리고 지금 네 빚에 쫓겨 그 아이가 부득이 포기해야 하는 이 확실한 이득을 무엇으로도 사실상 대신할 수 없다는 것이다.”"]], [["Tom listened with some shame and some sorrow; but escaping as quickly as possible, could soon with cheerful selfishness reflect, firstly, that he had not been half so much in debt as some of his friends; secondly, that his father had made a most tiresome piece of work of it; and, thirdly, that the future incumbent, whoever he might be, would, in all probability, die very soon.", "톰은 얼마간 부끄러움과 얼마간 서글픔 속에 귀 기울였다. 그러나 되도록 빨리 그 자리를 벗어나고 나자, 이내 명랑한 이기심으로 이렇게 되새길 수 있었다. 첫째로 자기는 몇몇 친구들 절반만큼도 빚을 지지 않았다는 것, 둘째로 아버지가 이 일을 지독히 성가시게 부풀렸다는 것, 셋째로 다음번 성직 보유자가 누가 되든 십중팔구 머잖아 죽으리라는 것."]], [["On Mr. Norris’s death the presentation became the right of a Dr.", "노리스 씨가 죽자 그 임명권은 그랜트 박사의 차지가 되었고, 그리하여 그는 맨스필드에 와서 살게 되었다."], ["Grant, who came consequently to reside at Mansfield; and on proving to be a hearty man of forty-five, seemed likely to disappoint Mr.", "그런데 마흔다섯의 정정한 사내로 밝혀지자, 버트럼 씨의 셈속을 어긋나게 할 성싶었다."], ["Bertram’s calculations. But “no, he was a short-necked, apoplectic sort of fellow, and, plied well with good things, would soon pop off.”", "그러나 “아니야, 저자는 목이 짧은 게 중풍 걸리기 딱 좋은 부류라, 좋은 걸 잔뜩 먹여 두면 머잖아 뻗어 버릴걸.”"]], [["He had a wife about fifteen years his junior, but no children; and they entered the neighbourhood with the usual fair report of being very respectable, agreeable people.", "그에게는 저보다 열다섯 살쯤 어린 아내가 있었으나 자식은 없었다. 두 사람은 아주 점잖고 상냥한 사람들이라는 흔한 호평과 함께 이웃에 발을 들였다."]], [["The time was now come when Sir Thomas expected his sister-in-law to claim her share in their niece, the change in Mrs.", "이제 토머스 경이 처형에게 조카딸을 맡을 제 몫을 요구하리라 기대할 때가 되었다. 노리스 부인의 처지가 달라지고 패니의 나이가 찬 것이 함께 지내는 데 대한 종전의 반대를 없앨 뿐 아니라, 오히려 그것을 더없이 마땅한 일로 만드는 듯싶었다."], ["Norris’s situation, and the improvement in Fanny’s age, seeming not merely to do away any former objection to their living together, but even to give it the most decided eligibility; and as his own circumstances were rendered less fair than heretofore, by some recent losses on his West India estate, in addition to his eldest son’s extravagance, it became not undesirable to himself to be relieved from the expense of her support, and the obligation of her future provision.", "게다가 맏아들의 낭비에 더해 최근 서인도 영지에서 얼마간 손실을 본 탓에 제 형편이 예전만 못해진 터라, 아이를 부양하는 비용과 앞날을 마련해 줄 의무에서 벗어나는 것이 그 자신에게도 싫지 않은 일이 되었다. 그런 일이 틀림없이 그리되리라 굳게 믿은 나머지 그는 아내에게 그 가망을 넌지시 비쳤다."], ["In the fullness of his belief that such a thing must be, he mentioned its probability to his wife; and the first time of the subject’s occurring to her again happening to be when Fanny was present, she calmly observed to her, “So, Fanny, you are going to leave us, and live with my sister.", "그리고 그 이야기가 아내의 머리에 다시 떠오른 것이 마침 패니가 곁에 있을 때였던지라, 부인은 태연하게 아이에게 말했다. “그러니까 패니야, 너는 이제 우리를 떠나 우리 언니와 살게 될 거란다."], ["How shall you like it?”", "어떠니, 마음에 들겠니?”"]], [["Fanny was too much surprised to do more than repeat her aunt’s words, “Going to leave you?”", "패니는 너무 놀란 나머지 이모의 말을 되뇌는 것밖에 하지 못했다. “저희를 떠난다고요?”"]], [["“Yes, my dear; why should you be astonished?", "“그래, 얘야. 놀랄 게 뭐 있니?"], ["You have been five years with us, and my sister always meant to take you when Mr.", "너는 우리와 다섯 해를 지냈고, 우리 언니도 노리스 씨가 죽으면 늘 너를 데려갈 작정이었단다."], ["Norris died. But you must come up and tack on my patterns all the same.”", "그래도 여기 올라와서 내 본 뜨는 일은 거들어야 한다, 그건 그거고.”"]], [["The news was as disagreeable to Fanny as it had been unexpected.", "그 소식은 뜻밖이었던 만큼이나 패니에게는 달갑지 않았다."], ["She had never received kindness from her aunt Norris, and could not love her.", "아이는 노리스 이모에게서 한 번도 다정한 대접을 받은 적이 없었고, 그녀를 사랑할 수도 없었다."]], [["“I shall be very sorry to go away,”", "“떠나게 되면 정말 서운할 거예요.”"], ["said she, with a faltering voice.", "아이가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I dare say you will; that’s natural enough.", "“그래, 그렇겠지. 그거야 당연하고말고."], ["I suppose you have had as little to vex you since you came into this house as any creature in the world.”", "이 집에 들어온 뒤로 너만큼 속상할 일 없이 지낸 아이도 세상에 또 없을 게다.”"]], [["“I hope I am not ungrateful, aunt,”", "“제가 은혜를 모르는 건 아니었으면 좋겠어요, 이모님.”"], ["said Fanny modestly.", "패니가 얌전히 말했다."]], [["“No, my dear; I hope not.", "“아니지, 얘야. 그러길 바란다."], ["I have always found you a very good girl.”", "나는 늘 너를 참 착한 아이로 여겨 왔단다.”"]], [["“And am I never to live here again?”", "“그럼 저는 다시는 여기서 못 사는 건가요?”"]], [["“Never, my dear; but you are sure of a comfortable home.", "“다시는 못 살지, 얘야. 하지만 편안한 집이 어김없이 있잖니."], ["It can make very little difference to you, whether you are in one house or the other.”", "이 집에 있든 저 집에 있든 너한테야 별 차이도 없을 거다.”"]], [["Fanny left the room with a very sorrowful heart; she could not feel the difference to be so small, she could not think of living with her aunt with anything like satisfaction.", "패니는 몹시 서러운 마음으로 방을 나섰다. 그 차이가 그토록 작다고는 도저히 느낄 수 없었고, 이모와 함께 사는 것을 흡족한 마음으로는 도무지 그려 볼 수 없었다."], ["As soon as she met with Edmund she told him her distress.", "에드먼드를 만나자마자 아이는 그에게 제 괴로움을 털어놓았다."]], [["“Cousin,”", "“사촌 오빠,” 아이가 말했다."], ["said she, “something is going to happen which I do not like at all; and though you have often persuaded me into being reconciled to things that I disliked at first, you will not be able to do it now.", "“제가 도무지 싫은 일이 벌어지려고 해요. 오빠는 여태 제가 처음엔 싫어하던 일도 곧잘 받아들이게 해 주셨지만, 이번만은 그러지 못하실 거예요."], ["I am going to live entirely with my aunt Norris.”", "저는 아주 노리스 이모하고만 살게 됐어요.”"]], [["“Indeed!”", "“정말?”"]], [["“Yes; my aunt Bertram has just told me so.", "“네, 버트럼 이모가 방금 그렇게 말씀하셨어요."], ["It is quite settled.", "아주 정해진 일이래요."], ["I am to leave Mansfield Park, and go to the White House, I suppose, as soon as she is removed there.”", "저는 맨스필드 파크를 떠나, 아마 이모가 그리로 옮기시는 대로 화이트하우스로 가게 될 거예요.”"]], [["“Well, Fanny, and if the plan were not unpleasant to you, I should call it an excellent one.”", "“글쎄, 패니, 네가 그 계획을 싫어하지만 않는다면 나는 아주 훌륭한 계획이라고 하겠는데.”"]], [["“Oh, cousin!”", "“어머, 오빠!”"]], [["“It has everything else in its favour.", "“그것 말고는 죄다 좋은 점뿐이야."], ["My aunt is acting like a sensible woman in wishing for you.", "이모가 너를 원하는 건 분별 있는 여자다운 처사지."], ["She is choosing a friend and companion exactly where she ought, and I am glad her love of money does not interfere.", "벗이자 동무를 꼭 있어야 할 바로 그 자리에서 고르는 거고, 그 돈 욕심이 끼어들지 않아 나는 기쁘다."], ["You will be what you ought to be to her.", "너는 이모에게 마땅히 되어야 할 존재가 될 거야."], ["I hope it does not distress you very much, Fanny?”", "이 일이 너를 그리 많이 괴롭히지는 않았으면 좋겠구나, 패니?”"]], [["“Indeed it does: I cannot like it.", "“정말 괴로운걸요. 좋아할 수가 없어요."], ["I love this house and everything in it: I shall love nothing there.", "저는 이 집이, 이 집 안의 모든 게 좋아요. 거기서는 아무것도 좋아하지 못할 거예요."], ["You know how uncomfortable I feel with her.”", "제가 이모 앞에서 얼마나 불편한지 오빠도 아시잖아요.”"]], [["“I can say nothing for her manner to you as a child; but it was the same with us all, or nearly so.", "“네가 어렸을 때 이모가 너를 대한 태도야 두둔할 말이 없다. 하지만 그건 우리한테도, 거의 다 마찬가지였어."], ["She never knew how to be pleasant to children.", "이모는 아이들한테 상냥하게 구는 법을 도무지 몰랐거든. 그렇지만 이제 너는 더 나은 대접을 받을 나이가 됐어."], ["But you are now of an age to be treated better; I think she is behaving better already; and when you are her only companion, you must be important to her.”", "내 보기엔 이모도 벌써 나아지고 있고. 그리고 네가 이모의 유일한 동무가 되면, 너는 이모한테 중요한 사람이 될 수밖에 없어.”"]], [["“I can never be important to any one.”", "“저는 누구에게도 중요한 사람이 될 수 없어요.”"]], [["“What is to prevent you?”", "“무엇이 그걸 막는다는 거니?”"]], [["“Everything.", "“전부 다요."], ["My situation, my foolishness and awkwardness.”", "제 처지도, 제 어리석음과 어설픔도요.”"]], [["“As to your foolishness and awkwardness, my dear Fanny, believe me, you never have a shadow of either, but in using the words so improperly.", "“네 어리석음과 어설픔으로 말하자면, 나의 패니야, 정말이지 너한테는 그런 그림자조차 없다. 그런 말을 이렇게 엉뚱하게 갖다 붙일 때 말고는 말이야."], ["There is no reason in the world why you should not be important where you are known.", "너를 아는 곳에서 네가 중요한 사람이 되지 못할 까닭은 이 세상에 하나도 없어."], ["You have good sense, and a sweet temper, and I am sure you have a grateful heart, that could never receive kindness without wishing to return it.", "너는 좋은 분별력과 고운 성품을 지녔고, 게다가 나는 네가 친절을 받으면 반드시 갚고 싶어 하는 고마운 마음을 지녔다고 믿는다."], ["I do not know any better qualifications for a friend and companion.”", "벗이자 동무로서 그보다 더 나은 자질을 나는 알지 못해.”"]], [["“You are too kind,”", "“너무 과분한 말씀이에요.”"], ["said Fanny, colouring at such praise; “how shall I ever thank you as I ought, for thinking so well of me.", "그런 칭찬에 얼굴을 붉히며 패니가 말했다. “저를 그토록 좋게 봐 주시니, 제가 어떻게 마땅한 만큼 감사드릴 수 있을까요."], ["Oh! cousin, if I am to go away, I shall remember your goodness to the last moment of my life.”", "아, 오빠, 제가 떠나야 한다면, 저는 목숨이 다하는 순간까지 오빠의 다정함을 기억할 거예요.”"]], [["“Why, indeed, Fanny, I should hope to be remembered at such a distance as the White House.", "“원, 패니, 화이트하우스쯤 되는 거리라면 나야 기억되기를 바라도 되겠지."], ["You speak as if you were going two hundred miles off instead of only across the park; but you will belong to us almost as much as ever.", "너는 마치 이백 마일이나 떨어진 데로 가는 것처럼 말하는구나, 고작 공원 하나 건너가는 것뿐인데. 너는 여전히 거의 예전만큼 우리 식구일 거야."], ["The two families will be meeting every day in the year.", "두 집안은 한 해 내내 날마다 만날 테니까."], ["The only difference will be that, living with your aunt, you will necessarily be brought forward as you ought to be.", "다른 점이라고는, 이모와 살면 네가 마땅히 그래야 하듯 앞으로 나서게 되리라는 것뿐이야."], ["Here there are too many whom you can hide behind; but with her you will be forced to speak for yourself.”", "여기서는 네가 뒤에 숨을 사람이 너무 많지. 하지만 이모하고 있으면 너는 어쩔 수 없이 네 목소리를 내야 할 거야.”"]], [["“Oh! do not say so.”", "“아, 그런 말씀 마세요.”"]], [["“I must say it, and say it with pleasure. Mrs.", "“나는 그 말을 해야겠고, 기꺼이 하겠다."], ["Norris is much better fitted than my mother for having the charge of you now.", "노리스 이모는 지금 너를 맡기에 우리 어머니보다 훨씬 알맞은 분이야."], ["She is of a temper to do a great deal for anybody she really interests herself about, and she will force you to do justice to your natural powers.”", "이모는 정말로 마음을 두는 사람을 위해서라면 무엇이든 아끼지 않는 성미고, 네가 타고난 능력을 제대로 발휘하도록 다그쳐 줄 거야.”"]], [["Fanny sighed, and said, “I cannot see things as you do; but I ought to believe you to be right rather than myself, and I am very much obliged to you for trying to reconcile me to what must be. If I could suppose my aunt really to care for me, it would be delightful to feel myself of consequence to anybody. Here, I know, I am of none, and yet I love the place so well.”", "패니는 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. “저는 오빠처럼 볼 수가 없어요. 그래도 저 자신보다는 오빠가 옳다고 믿어야겠지요. 어쩔 수 없는 일에 마음을 붙이도록 애써 주셔서 정말 고마워요. 이모가 정말로 저를 아껴 준다고 여길 수만 있다면, 누군가에게 소중한 사람이라고 느끼는 건 참 기쁜 일일 거예요. 여기서는, 저도 알지만, 저는 아무에게도 그런 사람이 못 되잖아요. 그런데도 저는 이곳이 이렇게 좋은걸요.”"]], [["“The place, Fanny, is what you will not quit, though you quit the house.", "“패니, 네가 떠나지 않는 건 바로 이곳이야. 집은 떠나도 말이지."], ["You will have as free a command of the park and gardens as ever.", "너는 여전히 예전처럼 공원과 정원을 마음껏 누빌 수 있어."], ["Even your constant little heart need not take fright at such a nominal change.", "그 한결같은 여린 마음조차 그런 이름뿐인 변화에는 놀랄 것 없단다."], ["You will have the same walks to frequent, the same library to choose from, the same people to look at, the same horse to ride.”", "늘 거닐던 그 길, 골라 읽던 그 서재, 바라보던 그 사람들, 타던 그 말이 그대로일 테니까.”"]], [["“Very true.", "“정말 그래요."], ["Yes, dear old grey pony! Ah!", "맞아요, 그 정든 늙은 회색 조랑말!"], ["cousin, when I remember how much I used to dread riding, what terrors it gave me to hear it talked of as likely to do me good (oh! how I have trembled at my uncle’s opening his lips if horses were talked of), and then think of the kind pains you took to reason and persuade me out of my fears, and convince me that I should like it after a little while, and feel how right you proved to be, I am inclined to hope you may always prophesy as well.”", "아, 오빠, 제가 말 타기를 얼마나 무서워했는지, 그게 제 몸에 좋을 거라는 말을 듣기만 해도 얼마나 겁이 났는지 (아, 말 이야기만 나오면 삼촌이 입을 여실까 봐 제가 얼마나 떨었던지), 그런데 오빠가 제 두려움을 조곤조곤 타일러 없애 주고 조금만 지나면 좋아하게 될 거라고 다독여 주신 그 다정한 수고를 떠올리면, 그리고 그 말이 얼마나 옳았는지 지금 느끼고 보면, 오빠는 앞으로도 늘 그렇게 잘 내다보시리라 믿고 싶어져요.”"]], [["“And I am quite convinced that your being with Mrs. Norris will be as good for your mind as riding has been for your health, and as much for your ultimate happiness too.”", "“그리고 나는, 노리스 이모와 지내는 것이 말 타기가 네 몸에 좋았던 만큼이나 네 마음에도 좋을 거라고, 나아가 네 궁극의 행복에도 그만큼 좋을 거라고 굳게 믿는다.”"]], [["So ended their discourse, which, for any very appropriate service it could render Fanny, might as well have been spared, for Mrs.", "이렇게 두 사람의 이야기는 끝났다. 그러나 그것이 패니에게 조금이라도 걸맞은 쓸모가 있었느냐 하면, 차라리 안 했어도 그만이었을 것이다."], ["Norris had not the smallest intention of taking her.", "노리스 부인에게는 아이를 데려갈 뜻이 눈곱만큼도 없었으니 말이다."], ["It had never occurred to her, on the present occasion, but as a thing to be carefully avoided.", "이번 일에서 그것은 그녀의 머릿속에 오로지 애써 피해야 할 일로만 떠올랐을 뿐이다."], ["To prevent its being expected, she had fixed on the smallest habitation which could rank as genteel among the buildings of Mansfield parish, the White House being only just large enough to receive herself and her servants, and allow a spare room for a friend, of which she made a very particular point.", "그런 기대를 받지 않으려고 그녀는 맨스필드 교구의 건물들 가운데 그나마 점잖은 축에 들 만한 것 중 가장 작은 집을 골라 두었다. 화이트하우스는 저와 하인들이 겨우 들어가고 벗을 위한 남는 방 하나를 둘 만한 크기밖에 안 되었는데, 그 남는 방을 그녀는 유난히 강조했다."], ["The spare rooms at the Parsonage had never been wanted, but the absolute necessity of a spare room for a friend was now never forgotten.", "목사관 시절에는 남는 방이 한 번도 아쉬운 적 없었건만, 이제는 벗을 위한 남는 방이 꼭 있어야 한다는 것을 한시도 잊는 법이 없었다."], ["Not all her precautions, however, could save her from being suspected of something better; or, perhaps, her very display of the importance of a spare room might have misled Sir Thomas to suppose it really intended for Fanny.", "그러나 그 온갖 방비로도 그녀는 더 그럴싸한 속셈을 품고 있다는 의심을 면치 못했다. 아니, 어쩌면 남는 방이 얼마나 중요한지 그리 떠벌린 탓에, 도리어 토머스 경이 그것을 정말 패니를 위한 것이려니 오해했는지도 모른다."], ["Lady Bertram soon brought the matter to a certainty by carelessly observing to Mrs. Norris—", "버트럼 부인이 노리스 부인에게 무심코 이렇게 말하는 바람에 그 일은 이내 분명해졌다."]], [["“I think, sister, we need not keep Miss Lee any longer, when Fanny goes to live with you.”", "“언니, 패니가 언니랑 살러 가면 리 선생은 더 둘 필요가 없을 것 같아요.”"]], [["Mrs. Norris almost started.", "노리스 부인은 하마터면 소스라칠 뻔했다."], ["“Live with me, dear Lady Bertram!", "“나랑 살다니, 버트럼 부인!"], ["what do you mean?”", "그게 무슨 말이에요?”"]], [["“Is she not to live with you?", "“그 애가 언니랑 사는 게 아니었어요?"], ["I thought you had settled it with Sir Thomas.”", "토머스 경하고 그렇게 정한 줄 알았는데.”"]], [["“Me!", "“내가!"], ["never.", "천만에."], ["I never spoke a syllable about it to Sir Thomas, nor he to me.", "나는 그 일로 토머스 경한테 한마디도 꺼낸 적 없고, 그이도 나한테 그런 적 없어요."], ["Fanny live with me!", "패니가 나랑 살다니!"], ["the last thing in the world for me to think of, or for anybody to wish that really knows us both.", "세상에 내가 생각이나 할 일도 아니고, 우리 둘을 제대로 아는 사람이라면 누구도 바랄 일이 아니지요."], ["Good heaven!", "원, 세상에!"], ["what could I do with Fanny? Me!", "내가 패니를 데리고 뭘 어쩌겠어요?"], ["a poor, helpless, forlorn widow, unfit for anything, my spirits quite broke down; what could I do with a girl at her time of life?", "나 같은 거! 가련하고 힘없고 외로운 과부, 아무짝에도 못 쓸 몸에, 기력도 아주 꺾여 버린 사람이."], ["A girl of fifteen!", "그런 나이의 계집아이를 데리고 내가 뭘 하겠어요?"], ["the very age of all others to need most attention and care, and put the cheerfullest spirits to the test!", "열다섯 살짜리를! 무엇보다도 가장 많은 관심과 보살핌이 필요하고, 아무리 명랑한 사람의 진을 다 빼놓을 바로 그 나이를!"], ["Sure Sir Thomas could not seriously expect such a thing!", "설마 토머스 경이 진심으로 그런 걸 바랐을 리 없어요!"], ["Sir Thomas is too much my friend.", "토머스 경은 나한테 더없이 좋은 벗이에요."], ["Nobody that wishes me well, I am sure, would propose it.", "나를 위하는 사람이라면 누구도 그런 걸 내놓을 리 없다고요."], ["How came Sir Thomas to speak to you about it?”", "대체 토머스 경이 어쩌다 그 얘기를 부인한테 했대요?”"]], [["“Indeed, I do not know.", "“글쎄, 나도 모르겠어요."], ["I suppose he thought it best.”", "그게 제일 낫다고 여기셨나 보지요.”"]], [["“But what did he say?", "“그런데 뭐라고 하셨어요?"], ["He could not say he wished me to take Fanny.", "내가 패니를 맡아 주기를 바란다고야 하셨을 리 없어요."], ["I am sure in his heart he could not wish me to do it.”", "진심으로야 그이가 나더러 그러라고 바랐을 리 없다니까요.”"]], [["“No; he only said he thought it very likely; and I thought so too.", "“아뇨. 그저 그럴 법하다고만 하셨어요."], ["We both thought it would be a comfort to you.", "나도 그렇게 여겼고요. 우리 둘 다 그게 언니한테 위안이 될 거라 생각했지요."], ["But if you do not like it, there is no more to be said.", "하지만 언니가 싫다면 더 할 말은 없어요."], ["She is no encumbrance here.”", "그 앤 여기 있어도 짐이 되진 않으니까요.”"]], [["“Dear sister, if you consider my unhappy state, how can she be any comfort to me?", "“이보세요, 부인, 내 딱한 처지를 생각하면, 그 애가 나한테 무슨 위안이 되겠어요?"], ["Here am I, a poor desolate widow, deprived of the best of husbands, my health gone in attending and nursing him, my spirits still worse, all my peace in this world destroyed, with hardly enough to support me in the rank of a gentlewoman, and enable me to live so as not to disgrace the memory of the dear departed—what possible comfort could I have in taking such a charge upon me as Fanny?", "여기 이렇게, 세상에 둘도 없는 남편을 여읜 가련하고 쓸쓸한 과부가, 그이 병간호에 건강까지 잃고, 기력은 그보다 더 못하고, 이 세상 온갖 평안이 다 부서진 채로, 숙녀 체면이나 겨우 지키며 그 정든 고인의 이름에 누가 되지 않을 만큼 살아갈 것도 빠듯한 사람이 앉아 있는데—패니 같은 짐을 떠맡아서 대체 무슨 위안을 얻겠어요?"], ["If I could wish it for my own sake, I would not do so unjust a thing by the poor girl.", "설령 내 좋자고 그러고 싶더라도, 그 딱한 애한테 그런 못할 짓은 안 하겠어요."], ["She is in good hands, and sure of doing well.", "그 앤 좋은 손에 맡겨져 잘될 게 뻔한데."], ["I must struggle through my sorrows and difficulties as I can.”", "나는 내 슬픔과 어려움을 그저 나 혼자 힘으로 헤쳐 나가야지요.”"]], [["“Then you will not mind living by yourself quite alone?”", "“그럼 언니는 아주 혼자 사는 게 괜찮겠어요?”"]], [["“Dear Lady Bertram!", "“버트럼 부인도 참!"], ["what am I fit for but solitude?", "나 같은 게 고독 말고 뭐에 어울리겠어요?"], ["Now and then I shall hope to have a friend in my little cottage (I shall always have a bed for a friend); but the most part of my future days will be spent in utter seclusion.", "이따금 내 작은 오두막에 벗 하나쯤 맞이하기를 바라기야 하겠지요 (벗을 위한 잠자리는 늘 마련해 둘 거예요). 하지만 앞으로 내 나날은 거의 다 아주 외로이 파묻혀 지낼 거예요."], ["If I can but make both ends meet, that's all I ask for.”", "그저 겨우 수지만 맞출 수 있다면, 내가 바라는 건 그뿐이에요.”"]], [["“I hope, sister, things are not so very bad with you neither considering Sir Thomas says you will have six hundred a year.”", "“언니, 그래도 형편이 그렇게까지 나쁘진 않았으면 좋겠어요. 토머스 경 말로는 언니한테 연 육백 파운드가 들어온다던데요.”"]], [["“Lady Bertram, I do not complain.", "“버트럼 부인, 나는 불평하는 게 아니에요."], ["I know I cannot live as I have done, but I must retrench where I can, and learn to be a better manager.", "예전처럼은 못 산다는 걸 나도 알아요. 하지만 아낄 수 있는 데서는 아끼고, 살림을 더 알뜰히 꾸리는 법을 익혀야지요."], ["I have been a liberal housekeeper enough, but I shall not be ashamed to practise economy now.", "나야 지금껏 인심 후한 살림꾼이었지만, 이제 절약한다고 부끄러워할 것 없어요."], ["My situation is as much altered as my income.", "내 처지는 수입만큼이나 달라졌으니까요."], ["A great many things were due from poor Mr. Norris, as clergyman of the parish, that cannot be expected from me.", "딱한 노리스 씨야 교구 목사로서 마땅히 치러야 할 것이 많았지만, 나한테야 그런 걸 바랄 순 없지요."], ["It is unknown how much was consumed in our kitchen by odd comers and goers.", "뜨내기손님들이 우리 부엌에서 축낸 게 얼마인지 아무도 몰라요."], ["At the White House, matters must be better looked after.", "화이트하우스에서는 그런 걸 더 잘 챙겨야 해요."], ["I must live within my income, or I shall be miserable; and I own it would give me great satisfaction to be able to do rather more, to lay by a little at the end of the year.”", "나는 반드시 수입 안에서 살아야지, 안 그러면 비참해질 거예요. 그리고 솔직히, 그보다 좀 더 해내서 연말에 얼마간 모아 둘 수 있다면 나는 무척 흐뭇하겠어요.”"]], [["“I dare say you will.", "“그러시겠지요."], ["You always do, don’t you?”", "언니야 늘 그러시잖아요?”"]], [["“My object, Lady Bertram, is to be of use to those that come after me.", "“버트럼 부인, 내 목적은 내 뒤에 올 사람들에게 보탬이 되는 거예요."], ["It is for your children’s good that I wish to be richer.", "내가 더 넉넉해지고 싶은 건 다 부인네 아이들 잘되라고 그러는 거지요."], ["I have nobody else to care for, but I should be very glad to think I could leave a little trifle among them worth their having.”", "나야 달리 마음 쓸 사람도 없으니, 그 애들한테 가질 만한 작은 것이라도 남겨 줄 수 있다고 생각하면 무척 기쁠 거예요.”"]], [["“You are very good, but do not trouble yourself about them.", "“참 고마운 말씀이에요, 언니. 그렇지만 그 애들 일로 애쓰지 마세요."], ["They are sure of being well provided for.", "그 애들이야 넉넉히 앞가림할 게 틀림없으니까요."], ["Sir Thomas will take care of that.”", "토머스 경이 다 챙겨 주실 거예요.”"]], [["“Why, you know, Sir Thomas’s means will be rather straitened if the Antigua estate is to make such poor returns.”", "“아니, 알잖아요, 앤티가 영지가 그렇게 형편없는 수익만 낸다면 토머스 경의 살림도 꽤 빠듯해질 거예요.”"]], [["“Oh! that will soon be settled.", "“어머, 그거야 곧 해결될 거예요."], ["Sir Thomas has been writing about it, I know.”", "토머스 경이 그 일로 편지를 써 오셨다니까요, 내가 알아요.”"]], [["“Well, Lady Bertram,” said Mrs. Norris, moving to go, “I can only say that my sole desire is to be of use to your family: and so, if Sir Thomas should ever speak again about my taking Fanny, you will be able to say that my health and spirits put it quite out of the question; besides that, I really should not have a bed to give her, for I must keep a spare room for a friend.”", "“그럼, 버트럼 부인,” 자리를 뜨려 몸을 일으키며 노리스 부인이 말했다. “나는 그저 이 말만 하겠어요. 내 유일한 바람은 부인네 식구에게 보탬이 되는 것뿐이라고요. 그러니 혹시라도 토머스 경이 다시 나더러 패니를 맡으라는 말을 꺼내시거든, 내 건강과 기력이 도무지 그럴 처지가 못 된다고 부인이 말해 주면 돼요. 게다가 나는 정말이지 그 애한테 내줄 잠자리조차 없어요. 벗을 위한 남는 방은 꼭 비워 둬야 하니까요.”"]], [["Lady Bertram repeated enough of this conversation to her husband to convince him how much he had mistaken his sister-in-law’s views; and she was from that moment perfectly safe from all expectation, or the slightest allusion to it from him. He could not but wonder at her refusing to do anything for a niece whom she had been so forward to adopt; but, as she took early care to make him, as well as Lady Bertram, understand that whatever she possessed was designed for their family, he soon grew reconciled to a distinction which, at the same time that it was advantageous and complimentary to them, would enable him better to provide for Fanny himself.", "버트럼 부인은 이 대화를 남편에게 얼추 옮겨 들려주어, 그가 처형의 뜻을 얼마나 잘못 짚었는지 깨닫게 해 주었다. 그 순간부터 노리스 부인은 그런 기대에서도, 그가 그 일을 넌지시라도 비치는 데서도 완전히 안전해졌다. 그는 그토록 앞장서서 거두겠다던 조카딸을 위해 아무것도 하지 않겠다는 처형이 의아하지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 그녀가 일찌감치 그와 버트럼 부인 모두에게 제가 가진 것은 무엇이든 그들 식구를 위한 것이라고 못 박아 둔 터라, 그는 이내 그 처사에 마음을 붙였다. 그것은 그들에게 이롭고 낯이 서는 일인 동시에, 그가 손수 패니를 더 잘 건사할 수 있게 해 주는 일이었으니 말이다."]], [["Fanny soon learnt how unnecessary had been her fears of a removal; and her spontaneous, untaught felicity on the discovery, conveyed some consolation to Edmund for his disappointment in what he had expected to be so essentially serviceable to her.", "패니는 이내 옮겨 간다는 제 두려움이 얼마나 부질없었는지 알게 되었다. 그것을 알았을 때 저도 모르게 절로 우러난 그 기쁨은, 아이에게 그토록 요긴하리라 여겼던 일에서 실망한 에드먼드에게도 얼마간 위로가 되었다."], ["Mrs.", "노리스 부인은 화이트하우스를 차지했고, 그랜트 부부는 목사관에 당도했다."], ["Norris took possession of the White House, the Grants arrived at the Parsonage, and these events over, everything at Mansfield went on for some time as usual.", "이 일들이 끝나자 맨스필드의 모든 것은 한동안 여느 때처럼 흘러갔다."]], [["The Grants showing a disposition to be friendly and sociable, gave great satisfaction in the main among their new acquaintance.", "그랜트 부부는 다정하고 붙임성 있는 성미를 보여, 대체로 새 이웃들 사이에서 큰 호감을 샀다."], ["They had their faults, and Mrs. Norris soon found them out.", "그들에게도 흠은 있었고, 노리스 부인은 이내 그것을 캐냈다."], ["The Doctor was very fond of eating, and would have a good dinner every day; and Mrs. Grant, instead of contriving to gratify him at little expense, gave her cook as high wages as they did at Mansfield Park, and was scarcely ever seen in her offices.", "박사는 먹는 것을 무척 좋아해 날마다 잘 차린 저녁상을 받으려 했고, 그랜트 부인은 적은 돈으로 그를 만족시킬 궁리를 하기는커녕 맨스필드 파크만큼이나 높은 삯을 요리사에게 주면서 정작 제 부엌일에는 좀처럼 모습을 드러내지 않았다."], ["Mrs. Norris could not speak with any temper of such grievances, nor of the quantity of butter and eggs that were regularly consumed in the house.", "노리스 부인은 그런 못마땅한 일들을, 또 그 집에서 으레 축나는 버터와 달걀의 양을 두고 도무지 곱게 말할 수 없었다."], ["“Nobody loved plenty and hospitality more than herself; nobody more hated pitiful doings; the Parsonage, she believed, had never been wanting in comforts of any sort, had never borne a bad character in her time, but this was a way of going on that she could not understand.", "“풍성함과 후한 대접을 저만큼 사랑하는 사람도 없고, 인색한 짓을 저만큼 미워하는 사람도 없다. 목사관은 제가 있던 시절에는 무슨 안락이든 모자란 적이 없었고 나쁜 소리 한 번 들은 적 없건만, 이런 살림살이는 도무지 이해할 수가 없다."], ["A fine lady in a country parsonage was quite out of place.", "시골 목사관에 우아한 마님이라니 영 격에 안 맞는다."], ["Her store-room, she thought, might have been good enough for Mrs. Grant to go into.", "제 저장실이면 그랜트 부인이 드나들기에 넉넉했을 것이다."], ["Inquire where she would, she could not find out that Mrs. Grant had ever had more than five thousand pounds.”", "아무리 캐물어도 그랜트 부인이 오천 파운드 넘게 가져왔다는 소리는 끝내 알아내지 못했다.”"]], [["Lady Bertram listened without much interest to this sort of invective. She could not enter into the wrongs of an economist, but she felt all the injuries of beauty in Mrs.", "버트럼 부인은 이런 종류의 험담을 별 흥미 없이 들었다."], ["Grant’s being so well settled in life without being handsome, and expressed her astonishment on that point almost as often, though not so diffusely, as Mrs. Norris discussed the other.", "알뜰한 사람이 겪는 억울함에는 도무지 공감하지 못했으나, 그랜트 부인이 미인도 아니면서 그토록 팔자가 편 것을 보고는 미모가 입은 온갖 손해만은 절절히 느꼈고, 노리스 부인이 다른 쪽을 늘어놓는 만큼 장황하지는 않았어도 그에 못지않게 자주 그 점에 놀라움을 표했다."]], [["These opinions had been hardly canvassed a year before another event arose of such importance in the family, as might fairly claim some place in the thoughts and conversation of the ladies.", "이런 의견들을 주고받은 지 채 한 해도 지나기 전에, 이 집안에는 또 다른 사건이 하나 일어났다. 부인들의 생각과 이야깃거리에서 얼마쯤 자리를 차지할 만한 중대한 일이었다."], ["Sir Thomas found it expedient to go to Antigua himself, for the better arrangement of his affairs, and he took his eldest son with him, in the hope of detaching him from some bad connexions at home.", "토머스 경은 제 사업을 더 잘 정리하기 위해 몸소 앤티가로 가는 것이 상책이라 여겼고, 고향에서 얽힌 몇몇 나쁜 교제에서 맏아들을 떼어 놓으려는 바람에 그를 데리고 갔다."], ["They left England with the probability of being nearly a twelvemonth absent.", "두 사람은 거의 한 해 가까이 자리를 비우게 될 것을 각오하고 잉글랜드를 떠났다."]], [["The necessity of the measure in a pecuniary light, and the hope of its utility to his son, reconciled Sir Thomas to the effort of quitting the rest of his family, and of leaving his daughters to the direction of others at their present most interesting time of life.", "금전으로 보아 그 조치가 불가피하다는 것과 아들에게 도움이 되리라는 기대가, 토머스 경으로 하여금 남은 식구를 뒤로하고 딸들을 인생에서 가장 미묘한 시기에 남의 손에 맡겨 두는 그 힘든 결단을 받아들이게 했다."], ["He could not think Lady Bertram quite equal to supply his place with them, or rather, to perform what should have been her own; but, in Mrs.", "그는 버트럼 부인이 딸들에게 제 자리를 대신하기에는, 아니 마땅히 제 몫이었을 일을 해내기에는 도무지 미덥지 않다고 여겼다."], ["Norris’s watchful attention, and in Edmund’s judgment, he had sufficient confidence to make him go without fears for their conduct.", "그러나 노리스 부인의 빈틈없는 보살핌과 에드먼드의 분별력에는 딸들의 처신을 걱정하지 않고 떠날 만큼 충분히 믿음을 두었다."]], [["Lady Bertram did not at all like to have her husband leave her; but she was not disturbed by any alarm for his safety, or solicitude for his comfort, being one of those persons who think nothing can be dangerous, or difficult, or fatiguing to anybody but themselves.", "버트럼 부인은 남편이 저를 두고 떠나는 것이 도무지 마뜩잖았다. 그러나 그의 안위를 걱정하거나 그의 안락을 노심초사하지는 않았으니, 그녀는 이 세상에 오로지 저 자신 말고는 누구에게도 위험하거나 어렵거나 고될 일이 없다고 여기는 그런 부류의 사람이었기 때문이다."]], [["The Miss Bertrams were much to be pitied on the occasion: not for their sorrow, but for their want of it.", "버트럼 자매는 이 일에서 자못 가엾이 여길 만했다. 슬퍼해서가 아니라 슬퍼할 줄 몰라서였다."], ["Their father was no object of love to them; he had never seemed the friend of their pleasures, and his absence was unhappily most welcome.", "아버지는 그들에게 사랑의 대상이 아니었다. 한 번도 그들의 즐거움을 함께해 주는 벗으로 보인 적이 없었고, 그 부재는 딱하게도 더없이 반가운 일이었다."], ["They were relieved by it from all restraint; and without aiming at one gratification that would probably have been forbidden by Sir Thomas, they felt themselves immediately at their own disposal, and to have every indulgence within their reach.", "그 덕에 그들은 온갖 구속에서 풀려났다. 토머스 경이라면 십중팔구 금했을 만한 즐거움을 굳이 하나도 노리지 않고서도, 그들은 이제 저희 마음대로 할 수 있고 온갖 방종이 손닿는 데 있다고 느꼈다."], ["Fanny’s relief, and her consciousness of it, were quite equal to her cousins’; but a more tender nature suggested that her feelings were ungrateful, and she really grieved because she could not grieve.", "패니가 느낀 해방감과 그것을 스스로 의식한 정도도 사촌들 못지않았다. 그러나 더 여린 천성이 제 감정이 배은망덕하다고 일러 주었고, 아이는 슬퍼할 수 없다는 바로 그 이유로 정말이지 슬퍼했다."], ["“Sir Thomas, who had done so much for her and her brothers, and who was gone perhaps never to return!", "“저와 제 오빠들을 위해 그토록 많은 것을 해 주셨고, 어쩌면 영영 돌아오지 못할지도 모르는 채 떠나신 토머스 경!"], ["that she should see him go without a tear!", "그런 분이 떠나시는데 눈물 한 방울 흘리지 못하다니!"], ["it was a shameful insensibility.”", "이건 부끄러운 무정함이다.”"], ["He had said to her, moreover, on the very last morning, that he hoped she might see William again in the course of the ensuing winter, and had charged her to write and invite him to Mansfield as soon as the squadron to which he belonged should be known to be in England.", "게다가 그는 바로 그 마지막 아침에, 다가오는 겨울 동안 아이가 윌리엄을 다시 만날 수 있기를 바란다고 말했고, 그가 속한 함대가 잉글랜드에 들어온 것이 알려지는 대로 편지를 써서 그를 맨스필드로 초대하라 일러 두었다. “이 얼마나 사려 깊고 다정한 일인가!”"], ["“This was so thoughtful and kind!” and would he only have smiled upon her, and called her “my dear Fanny,”", "그리고 그 말을 하면서 그가 아이에게 미소만 지어 주고 “나의 사랑하는 패니”라고만 불러 주었더라도, 예전의 온갖 찌푸림이나 쌀쌀한 말은 다 잊혔을 것이다."], ["while he said it, every former frown or cold address might have been forgotten.", "그러나 그는 이렇게 덧붙임으로써 아이를 서글픈 낙심에 빠뜨리며 말을 맺었다."], ["But he had ended his speech in a way to sink her in sad mortification, by adding, “If William does come to Mansfield, I hope you may be able to convince him that the many years which have passed since you parted have not been spent on your side entirely without improvement; though, I fear, he must find his sister at sixteen in some respects too much like his sister at ten.”", "“윌리엄이 맨스필드에 오거든, 너희가 헤어진 뒤로 흘러간 그 여러 해가 네 쪽에서도 아주 발전 없이 흘러가지만은 않았음을 그 애가 믿게 해 줄 수 있으면 좋겠구나. 하기야 걱정이다만, 그 애는 열여섯 된 제 누이가 몇몇 점에서 열 살 적 누이와 너무 닮았다고 여길 게다.”"], ["She cried bitterly over this reflection when her uncle was gone; and her cousins, on seeing her with red eyes, set her down as a hypocrite.", "삼촌이 떠난 뒤 아이는 이 말을 곱씹으며 쓰라리게 울었다. 그리고 사촌들은 아이의 빨개진 눈을 보고는 아이를 위선자로 치부했다."]], [["# CHAPTER IV", "# 제4장"]], [["Tom Bertram had of late spent so little of his time at home that he could be only nominally missed; and Lady Bertram was soon astonished to find how very well they did even without his father, how well Edmund could supply his place in carving, talking to the steward, writing to the attorney, settling with the servants, and equally saving her from all possible fatigue or exertion in every particular but that of directing her letters.", "톰 버트럼은 요즘 들어 집에서 보내는 시간이 하도 적어, 아쉬워한대야 이름뿐이었다. 버트럼 부인은 이내, 아버지 없이도 식구가 얼마나 잘 지내는지, 에드먼드가 고기를 썰고, 집사와 이야기하고, 변호사에게 편지를 쓰고, 하인들과 셈을 치르는 그 자리를 얼마나 잘 대신하는지, 그리고 편지 겉봉에 주소 적는 일만 빼면 온갖 일에서 저를 얼마나 고스란히 수고와 노고에서 벗어나게 해 주는지 알고 놀랐다."]], [["The earliest intelligence of the travellers’ safe arrival at Antigua, after a favourable voyage, was received; though not before Mrs.", "순조로운 항해 끝에 여행자들이 앤티가에 무사히 닿았다는 첫 소식이 당도했다. 다만 그 전에 노리스 부인이 온갖 무시무시한 두려움에 빠져, 에드먼드를 붙들 수 있을 때마다 그것을 함께 나누게 하려 애쓴 뒤의 일이었다."], ["Norris had been indulging in very dreadful fears, and trying to make Edmund participate them whenever she could get him alone; and as she depended on being the first person made acquainted with any fatal catastrophe, she had already arranged the manner of breaking it to all the others, when Sir Thomas’s assurances of their both being alive and well made it necessary to lay by her agitation and affectionate preparatory speeches for a while.", "그녀는 무슨 참변이 있으면 제가 맨 먼저 알게 되리라 믿고, 그것을 다른 이들에게 어떻게 알릴지 그 방식까지 벌써 짜 두었더랬다. 그러던 차에 두 사람 다 살아서 잘 있다는 토머스 경의 확답이 오는 바람에, 그 소동과 정에 겨운 예비 연설은 한동안 접어 두어야 했다."]], [["The winter came and passed without their being called for; the accounts continued perfectly good; and Mrs.", "겨울이 오고 갔으나 그들이 불려 나갈 일은 없었다. 소식은 여전히 더없이 좋았다."], ["Norris, in promoting gaieties for her nieces, assisting their toilets, displaying their accomplishments, and looking about for their future husbands, had so much to do as, in addition to all her own household cares, some interference in those of her sister, and Mrs.", "노리스 부인은 조카딸들을 위해 즐길 거리를 마련하고, 몸단장을 거들고, 재주를 뽐내게 하고, 앞으로 맞을 사윗감을 물색하는 데 하도 바빴다."], ["Grant’s wasteful doings to overlook, left her very little occasion to be occupied in fears for the absent.", "게다가 제 살림 걱정에, 동생네 살림에 얼마쯤 참견하는 일에, 그랜트 부인의 헤픈 살림살이를 눈여겨보는 일까지 더해지니, 멀리 떠난 이들을 두고 두려움에 잠길 겨를이 거의 없었다."]], [["The Miss Bertrams were now fully established among the belles of the neighbourhood; and as they joined to beauty and brilliant acquirements a manner naturally easy, and carefully formed to general civility and obligingness, they possessed its favour as well as its admiration.", "버트럼 자매는 이제 이웃의 이름난 미인들 사이에 확고히 자리 잡았다. 아름다움과 빛나는 재주에 더해, 타고나기를 느긋하고 두루 예의 바르고 상냥하도록 정성껏 다듬어진 몸가짐까지 갖추었으니, 사람들의 감탄은 물론 호감까지 한 몸에 받았다."], ["Their vanity was in such good order that they seemed to be quite free from it, and gave themselves no airs; while the praises attending such behaviour, secured and brought round by their aunt, served to strengthen them in believing they had no faults.", "그들의 허영심은 어찌나 잘 다스려져 있던지 도무지 허영이랄 것이 없어 보였고, 조금도 거드름을 피우지 않았다. 한편 그런 처신에 따르는 칭찬은—이모가 챙기고 끌어모아 준 것이었는데—그들이 제게 아무 흠도 없다고 믿는 마음을 한층 굳혀 주었다."]], [["Lady Bertram did not go into public with her daughters.", "버트럼 부인은 딸들과 함께 사교 자리에 나가지 않았다."], ["She was too indolent even to accept a mother’s gratification in witnessing their success and enjoyment at the expense of any personal trouble, and the charge was made over to her sister, who desired nothing better than a post of such honourable representation, and very thoroughly relished the means it afforded her of mixing in society without having horses to hire.", "그녀는 딸들의 성공과 즐거움을 지켜보는 어미의 흐뭇함조차, 몸소 수고를 무릅쓰면서까지 누리기에는 너무 게을렀다. 그리하여 그 소임은 언니에게 넘겨졌는데, 언니로 말하면 그토록 명예로운 대리 역할보다 더 바랄 것이 없는 사람이었고, 삯말을 빌리지 않고도 사교계에 낄 수 있는 그 수단을 아주 흡족하게 누렸다."]], [["Fanny had no share in the festivities of the season; but she enjoyed being avowedly useful as her aunt’s companion when they called away the rest of the family; and, as Miss Lee had left Mansfield, she naturally became everything to Lady Bertram during the night of a ball or a party. She talked to her, listened to her, read to her; and the tranquillity of such evenings, her perfect security in such a tête-à-tête from any sound of unkindness, was unspeakably welcome to a mind which had seldom known a pause in its alarms or embarrassments. As to her cousins’ gaieties, she loved to hear an account of them, especially of the balls, and whom Edmund had danced with; but thought too lowly of her own situation to imagine she should ever be admitted to the same, and listened, therefore, without an idea of any nearer concern in them. Upon the whole, it was a comfortable winter to her; for though it brought no William to England, the never-failing hope of his arrival was worth much.", "패니는 그 철의 흥겨운 자리에는 낄 몫이 없었다. 그러나 다른 식구들이 불려 나가고 나면 이모의 말벗으로서 떳떳이 쓸모 있는 것이 즐거웠다. 게다가 리 선생이 맨스필드를 떠난 뒤로는, 무도회나 파티가 있는 밤이면 자연히 버트럼 부인에게 없어서는 안 될 존재가 되었다. 아이는 부인에게 말을 건네고, 부인의 말을 들어 주고, 책을 읽어 주었다. 그런 저녁의 고요함은, 단둘이 마주 앉아 매정한 소리 하나 들려올 리 없는 그 완전한 안심은, 좀처럼 놀라움이나 무안함이 그칠 새 없던 마음에 이루 말할 수 없이 반가운 것이었다. 사촌들의 흥겨운 일에 대해서는, 특히 무도회 이야기며 에드먼드가 누구와 춤을 추었나 하는 이야기를 듣기 좋아했다. 그러나 제 처지를 너무 낮추어 보아, 저도 언젠가 그런 자리에 낄 수 있으리라고는 상상조차 못 했고, 그래서 조금도 제 일로 여기지 않고 그저 듣기만 했다. 대체로 그것은 아이에게 편안한 겨울이었다. 윌리엄을 잉글랜드로 데려다주지는 못했어도, 그가 오리라는 결코 스러지지 않는 희망만으로도 값진 것이 많았기 때문이다."]], [["The ensuing spring deprived her of her valued friend, the old grey pony; and for some time she was in danger of feeling the loss in her health as well as in her affections; for in spite of the acknowledged importance of her riding on horse-back, no measures were taken for mounting her again, “because,”", "이어진 봄은 아이에게서 소중한 벗인 늙은 회색 조랑말을 앗아 갔다. 한동안 아이는 그 상실을 정에서뿐 아니라 건강에서도 느낄 처지에 놓였다."], ["as it was observed by her aunts, “she might ride one of her cousin’s horses at any time when they did not want them,”", "말을 타는 것이 아이에게 중요하다는 것은 다들 인정하면서도, 아이를 다시 말에 태울 조치는 아무도 하지 않았기 때문이다."], ["and as the Miss Bertrams regularly wanted their horses every fine day, and had no idea of carrying their obliging manners to the sacrifice of any real pleasure, that time, of course, never came.", "“왜냐하면,” 이모들이 하는 말로는, “사촌들이 안 탈 때야 언제든 사촌들 말 한 마리를 타면 되니까”였다."], ["They took their cheerful rides in the fine mornings of April and May; and Fanny either sat at home the whole day with one aunt, or walked beyond her strength at the instigation of the other: Lady Bertram holding exercise to be as unnecessary for everybody as it was unpleasant to herself; and Mrs.", "그러나 버트럼 자매는 날씨 좋은 날이면 으레 제 말을 타고 싶어 했고, 그 상냥한 몸가짐을 무슨 진짜 즐거움을 희생하는 데까지 밀고 갈 생각은 조금도 없었으니, 그럴 때란 물론 영영 오지 않았다. 그들은 사월과 오월의 화창한 아침이면 즐거이 말을 달렸다."], ["Norris, who was walking all day, thinking everybody ought to walk as much.", "그리고 패니는 한 이모와 하루 종일 집에 앉아 있거나, 다른 이모의 부추김에 제 힘에 부치도록 걸어야 했다."], ["Edmund was absent at this time, or the evil would have been earlier remedied.", "버트럼 부인은 운동이란 저에게 싫은 만큼 누구에게나 쓸데없다 여겼고, 하루 종일 걸어 다니는 노리스 부인은 누구나 저만큼 걸어야 마땅하다 여겼기 때문이다. 이 무렵 에드먼드는 집을 비우고 있었다."], ["When he returned, to understand how Fanny was situated, and perceived its ill effects, there seemed with him but one thing to be done; and that “Fanny must have a horse”", "아니었다면 그 폐단은 더 일찍 바로잡혔을 것이다. 그가 돌아와 패니의 처지를 알아채고 그 나쁜 영향을 눈치채자, 그가 보기엔 할 일이 딱 하나뿐이었다."], ["was the resolute declaration with which he opposed whatever could be urged by the supineness of his mother, or the economy of his aunt, to make it appear unimportant. Mrs.", "“패니에게는 말이 있어야 한다”는 것이 그가 단호히 내세운 선언이었고, 그것을 대수롭잖게 보이려는 어머니의 게으름이나 이모의 절약이 무슨 말을 들이대든 그는 이 한마디로 맞섰다."], ["Norris could not help thinking that some steady old thing might be found among the numbers belonging to the Park that would do vastly well; or that one might be borrowed of the steward; or that perhaps Dr. Grant might now and then lend them the pony he sent to the post.", "노리스 부인은 파크에 딸린 그 많은 말 가운데 아주 쓸 만한 얌전한 늙은 놈을 하나 찾을 수 있으리라는, 아니면 집사에게 한 마리 빌리면 되리라는, 아니면 어쩌면 그랜트 박사가 이따금 우편용으로 부리는 조랑말을 빌려줄지 모른다는 생각을 떨칠 수 없었다."], ["She could not but consider it as absolutely unnecessary, and even improper, that Fanny should have a regular lady’s horse of her own, in the style of her cousins.", "그녀는 패니가 사촌들처럼 제 몫의 어엿한 숙녀용 말을 갖는 것을 도무지 쓸데없는 일, 심지어 온당치 못한 일로 여겼다. 토머스 경도 결코 그럴 뜻은 없었으리라 확신했다."], ["She was sure Sir Thomas had never intended it: and she must say that, to be making such a purchase in his absence, and adding to the great expenses of his stable, at a time when a large part of his income was unsettled, seemed to her very unjustifiable.", "그리고 한마디 하자면, 그가 없는 사이에 그런 것을 사들여, 그의 수입 상당 부분이 불안정한 이 판국에 마구간 비용을 더 늘리는 것은 도무지 정당화할 수 없다는 것이었다. “패니에게는 말이 있어야 합니다.”"], ["“Fanny must have a horse,”", "이것이 에드먼드의 유일한 대꾸였다."], ["was Edmund’s only reply. Mrs.", "노리스 부인은 그것을 같은 눈으로 볼 수 없었다."], ["Norris could not see it in the same light.", "버트럼 부인은 그리 보았다."], ["Lady Bertram did: she entirely agreed with her son as to the necessity of it, and as to its being considered necessary by his father; she only pleaded against there being any hurry; she only wanted him to wait till Sir Thomas’s return, and then Sir Thomas might settle it all himself.", "그것이 꼭 필요하다는 데에도, 그것을 그 아버지도 꼭 필요하다 여기리라는 데에도 그녀는 아들과 온전히 뜻을 같이했다. 다만 서두를 것 없다는 것만 내세웠다."], ["He would be at home in September, and where would be the harm of only waiting till September?", "토머스 경이 돌아올 때까지 기다렸다가 그때 토머스 경이 손수 다 정하게 하자는 것뿐이었다. 그이는 구월이면 집에 올 텐데, 구월까지 그저 기다린다고 무슨 해가 되겠느냐는 것이었다."]], [["Though Edmund was much more displeased with his aunt than with his mother, as evincing least regard for her niece, he could not help paying more attention to what she said; and at length determined on a method of proceeding which would obviate the risk of his father’s thinking he had done too much, and at the same time procure for Fanny the immediate means of exercise, which he could not bear she should be without.", "에드먼드는 조카딸을 조금도 위하지 않는 태도로 보아 어머니보다 이모에게 훨씬 언짢았으나, 그래도 이모의 말에는 더 마음을 쓰지 않을 수 없었다. 그리하여 마침내, 아버지가 저더러 너무 나섰다고 여길 위험은 피하면서도 패니에게는 당장 운동할 방편을 마련해 줄—그것 없이 지내는 것을 그는 도저히 두고 볼 수 없었다—한 가지 방도를 정했다."], ["He had three horses of his own, but not one that would carry a woman.", "그에게는 제 말이 세 마리 있었으나 여자를 태울 만한 것은 하나도 없었다. 둘은 사냥말이었고, 셋째는 쓸모 있는 길말이었다."], ["Two of them were hunters; the third, a useful road-horse: this third he resolved to exchange for one that his cousin might ride; he knew where such a one was to be met with; and having once made up his mind, the whole business was soon completed.", "그는 이 셋째를 사촌이 탈 만한 말과 바꾸기로 했다. 그런 말을 어디서 구할 수 있는지 그는 알고 있었고, 한번 마음을 정하고 나자 그 일은 이내 끝났다."], ["The new mare proved a treasure; with a very little trouble she became exactly calculated for the purpose, and Fanny was then put in almost full possession of her.", "새로 온 암말은 보배로 드러났다. 아주 조금 손을 들이자 그 용도에 딱 맞게 되었고, 그리하여 패니는 거의 온전히 그 말을 차지하게 되었다."], ["She had not supposed before that anything could ever suit her like the old grey pony; but her delight in Edmund’s mare was far beyond any former pleasure of the sort; and the addition it was ever receiving in the consideration of that kindness from which her pleasure sprung, was beyond all her words to express.", "아이는 전에는 그 무엇도 늙은 회색 조랑말만큼 제게 맞으리라고는 생각지 못했었다. 그러나 에드먼드의 암말을 향한 기쁨은 그런 종류의 지난 어떤 즐거움도 아득히 넘어섰다."], ["She regarded her cousin as an example of everything good and great, as possessing worth which no one but herself could ever appreciate, and as entitled to such gratitude from her as no feelings could be strong enough to pay.", "게다가 그 즐거움이 샘솟은 근원인 그 친절을 헤아리는 마음이 끊임없이 거기에 더해지니, 그것은 아이가 무슨 말로도 다 옮길 수 없는 것이었다. 아이는 사촌을 온갖 선하고 위대한 것의 본보기로, 저 말고는 아무도 알아보지 못할 값진 것을 지닌 사람으로, 그리고 어떤 감정으로도 다 갚기에 부족할 만큼 마땅히 제 감사를 받아야 할 사람으로 우러렀다."], ["Her sentiments towards him were compounded of all that was respectful, grateful, confiding, and tender.", "그를 향한 아이의 마음은 존경과 감사와 믿음과 애틋함이 온통 어우러진 것이었다."]], [["As the horse continued in name, as well as fact, the property of Edmund, Mrs.", "그 말이 이름으로나 실제로나 여전히 에드먼드의 것이었으므로, 노리스 부인은 그것을 패니가 쓰는 것쯤은 참아 줄 수 있었다."], ["Norris could tolerate its being for Fanny’s use; and had Lady Bertram ever thought about her own objection again, he might have been excused in her eyes for not waiting till Sir Thomas’s return in September, for when September came Sir Thomas was still abroad, and without any near prospect of finishing his business.", "그리고 버트럼 부인이 제 이의를 다시 떠올리기라도 했다면, 에드먼드가 구월에 토머스 경이 돌아올 때까지 기다리지 않은 것도 그녀 눈에는 용서받았을 것이다. 정작 구월이 오자 토머스 경은 여전히 외국에 있었고, 일을 마무리 지을 가망도 좀처럼 보이지 않았으니 말이다."], ["Unfavourable circumstances had suddenly arisen at a moment when he was beginning to turn all his thoughts towards England; and the very great uncertainty in which everything was then involved determined him on sending home his son, and waiting the final arrangement by himself.", "그가 온통 잉글랜드로 마음을 돌리기 시작하던 바로 그때 불리한 사정들이 별안간 생겨났다. 그리고 그때 모든 것이 휩싸인 그 엄청난 불확실함 때문에, 그는 아들만 먼저 집으로 보내고 최종 정리는 저 혼자 하기로 마음먹었다."], ["Tom arrived safely, bringing an excellent account of his father’s health; but to very little purpose, as far as Mrs. Norris was concerned.", "톰은 무사히 도착해 아버지의 건강에 대한 아주 좋은 소식을 전해 주었으나, 노리스 부인으로 말하면 그것은 별 소용이 없었다."], ["Sir Thomas’s sending away his son seemed to her so like a parent’s care, under the influence of a foreboding of evil to himself, that she could not help feeling dreadful presentiments; and as the long evenings of autumn came on, was so terribly haunted by these ideas, in the sad solitariness of her cottage, as to be obliged to take daily refuge in the dining-room of the Park.", "토머스 경이 아들을 먼저 떠나보낸 것이 그녀에게는 마치 제 몸에 닥칠 불행을 예감한 부모의 마음처럼 보였던지라, 그녀는 무시무시한 예감을 떨칠 수 없었다. 그리고 가을의 긴 저녁이 다가오자, 오두막의 그 서글픈 외로움 속에서 이런 생각에 하도 끔찍하게 시달린 나머지, 날마다 파크의 식당으로 피신하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["The return of winter engagements, however, was not without its effect; and in the course of their progress, her mind became so pleasantly occupied in superintending the fortunes of her eldest niece, as tolerably to quiet her nerves.", "그러나 겨울철 모임들이 돌아온 것은 나름의 효험이 있었다. 그것이 이어지는 동안 그녀의 마음은 맏조카딸의 앞날을 주선하는 데 하도 즐겁게 쏠려, 신경이 그런대로 가라앉았다."], ["“If poor Sir Thomas were fated never to return, it would be peculiarly consoling to see their dear Maria well married,”", "“딱한 토머스 경이 영영 돌아오지 못할 운명이라면, 사랑하는 마리아가 좋은 데로 시집가는 것을 보는 게 유난히 위안이 되겠지”라고 그녀는 곧잘 생각했다."], ["she very often thought; always when they were in the company of men of fortune, and particularly on the introduction of a young man who had recently succeeded to one of the largest estates and finest places in the country.", "늘 재산 많은 남자들과 자리를 함께할 때면, 특히 최근에 이 고장에서 가장 큰 영지와 가장 훌륭한 저택 하나를 물려받은 어느 젊은이가 소개되었을 때면."]], [["Mr. Rushworth was from the first struck with the beauty of Miss Bertram, and, being inclined to marry, soon fancied himself in love.", "러시워스 씨는 처음부터 버트럼 양의 아름다움에 마음을 빼앗겼고, 혼인할 생각이 있던 터라 이내 스스로 사랑에 빠졌다 여겼다."], ["He was a heavy young man, with not more than common sense; but as there was nothing disagreeable in his figure or address, the young lady was well pleased with her conquest.", "그는 그저 그런 분별밖에 없는 둔한 젊은이였다. 그러나 생김새나 말씨에 딱히 거슬리는 데는 없었으므로, 그 젊은 아가씨는 제 정복이 흡족했다."], ["Being now in her twenty-first year, Maria Bertram was beginning to think matrimony a duty; and as a marriage with Mr.", "스물한 살에 접어든 마리아 버트럼은 혼인을 하나의 의무로 여기기 시작하던 참이었다."], ["Rushworth would give her the enjoyment of a larger income than her father’s, as well as ensure her the house in town, which was now a prime object, it became, by the same rule of moral obligation, her evident duty to marry Mr. Rushworth if she could.", "게다가 러시워스 씨와의 혼인은 아버지보다 더 큰 수입을 누리게 해 줄뿐더러 이제 가장 탐나는 것이 된 도시의 집까지 마련해 줄 터였으니, 바로 그 도덕적 의무의 논리대로, 될 수만 있다면 러시워스 씨와 혼인하는 것이 그녀의 명백한 도리가 되었다."], ["Mrs. Norris was most zealous in promoting the match, by every suggestion and contrivance likely to enhance its desirableness to either party; and, among other means, by seeking an intimacy with the gentleman’s mother, who at present lived with him, and to whom she even forced Lady Bertram to go through ten miles of indifferent road to pay a morning visit.", "노리스 부인은 그 혼사를 성사시키는 데 더없이 열을 올려, 양쪽 모두에게 그것을 더 탐나게 할 만한 온갖 귀띔과 궁리를 다 짜냈다."], ["It was not long before a good understanding took place between this lady and herself. Mrs.", "그중 한 방편으로, 지금 아들과 함께 사는 그 신사의 어머니와 가까워지려 애썼는데, 그 부인을 아침에 방문하려고 버트럼 부인더러 십 마일이나 되는 험한 길을 가게 억지로 몰아붙이기까지 했다."], ["Rushworth acknowledged herself very desirous that her son should marry, and declared that of all the young ladies she had ever seen, Miss Bertram seemed, by her amiable qualities and accomplishments, the best adapted to make him happy.", "오래지 않아 이 부인과 그녀 사이에는 좋은 뜻이 통했다. 러시워스 부인은 아들이 혼인하기를 무척 바란다고 밝혔고, 여태 본 온갖 젊은 아가씨 가운데 버트럼 양이야말로 그 상냥한 자질과 재주로 보아 아들을 행복하게 해 주기에 가장 알맞아 보인다고 단언했다."], ["Mrs. Norris accepted the compliment, and admired the nice discernment of character which could so well distinguish merit.", "노리스 부인은 그 칭찬을 받아들이며, 그토록 사람의 됨됨이를 잘 가려내는 그 안목에 감탄했다."], ["Maria was indeed the pride and delight of them all—perfectly faultless—an angel; and, of course, so surrounded by admirers, must be difficult in her choice: but yet, as far as Mrs.", "정말이지 마리아는 그들 모두의 자랑이요 기쁨이었다—흠 하나 없는—천사였다. 그러니 당연히 그토록 숱한 사모하는 이들에 둘러싸여 고르기가 어려울 수밖에."], ["Norris could allow herself to decide on so short an acquaintance, Mr. Rushworth appeared precisely the young man to deserve and attach her.", "그러나 그래도 노리스 부인이 그토록 짧은 사귐으로 감히 단정할 수 있는 한, 러시워스 씨야말로 그녀를 얻고 그녀의 마음을 붙들 자격이 있는 바로 그 젊은이로 보였다."]], [["After dancing with each other at a proper number of balls, the young people justified these opinions, and an engagement, with a due reference to the absent Sir Thomas, was entered into, much to the satisfaction of their respective families, and of the general lookers-on of the neighbourhood, who had, for many weeks past, felt the expediency of Mr.", "몇 차례 무도회에서 알맞은 만큼 함께 춤을 추고 나자, 그 젊은 남녀는 이런 평판이 옳았음을 증명했다."], ["Rushworth’s marrying Miss Bertram.", "그리하여 자리를 비운 토머스 경에게 마땅히 여쭙는다는 조건으로 약혼이 맺어졌고, 이는 각자의 집안은 물론, 지난 여러 주 동안 러시워스 씨가 버트럼 양과 혼인하는 것이 마땅하다 여겨 온 이웃의 뭇 구경꾼들까지 크게 만족시켰다."]], [["It was some months before Sir Thomas’s consent could be received; but, in the meanwhile, as no one felt a doubt of his most cordial pleasure in the connexion, the intercourse of the two families was carried on without restraint, and no other attempt made at secrecy than Mrs.", "토머스 경의 승낙이 오기까지는 몇 달이 걸렸다. 그러나 그사이, 그가 이 인연을 더없이 진심으로 기뻐하리라는 것을 아무도 의심하지 않았으므로, 두 집안의 왕래는 거리낌 없이 이어졌다."], ["Norris’s talking of it everywhere as a matter not to be talked of at present.", "비밀이랄 것도, 노리스 부인이 그것을 지금은 발설해서는 안 될 일이라며 가는 곳마다 떠벌리는 것 말고는 달리 없었다."]], [["Edmund was the only one of the family who could see a fault in the business; but no representation of his aunt’s could induce him to find Mr.", "이 집안에서 이 일에 흠을 볼 줄 아는 이는 에드먼드뿐이었다. 그러나 이모가 아무리 그럴싸하게 늘어놓아도 그는 러시워스 씨를 반길 만한 벗으로 여길 수 없었다."], ["Rushworth a desirable companion. He could allow his sister to be the best judge of her own happiness, but he was not pleased that her happiness should centre in a large income; nor could he refrain from often saying to himself, in Mr.", "누이가 제 행복을 가장 잘 판단할 사람임은 인정할 수 있었으나, 그 행복이 큰 수입에 걸려 있다는 것이 그는 마뜩잖았다."], ["Rushworth’s company—“If this man had not twelve thousand a year, he would be a very stupid fellow.”", "그래서 러시워스 씨와 함께 있을 때면 “이 사내에게 연 만 이천 파운드가 없었다면, 아주 멍청한 작자에 지나지 않았을 것을” 하고 속으로 곧잘 되뇌지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["Sir Thomas, however, was truly happy in the prospect of an alliance so unquestionably advantageous, and of which he heard nothing but the perfectly good and agreeable.", "그러나 토머스 경은 그토록 두말없이 이로운 혼사의 전망에 참으로 흐뭇했고, 그것에 대해 더없이 좋고 흡족한 소리밖에 듣지 못했다."], ["It was a connexion exactly of the right sort—in the same county, and the same interest—and his most hearty concurrence was conveyed as soon as possible.", "그것은 꼭 알맞은 종류의 인연이었다—같은 주, 같은 이해관계였으니 말이다. 그리하여 그의 더없이 진심 어린 동의가 되도록 빨리 전해졌다."], ["He only conditioned that the marriage should not take place before his return, which he was again looking eagerly forward to.", "다만 그는 혼례를 제가 돌아오기 전에는 치르지 말라는 조건만 달았는데, 그 귀국을 그는 또다시 애타게 고대하고 있었다."], ["He wrote in April, and had strong hopes of settling everything to his entire satisfaction, and leaving Antigua before the end of the summer.", "그는 사월에 편지를 썼고, 모든 일을 제 뜻대로 매듭지어 여름이 가기 전에 앤티가를 떠날 수 있으리라는 큰 희망을 품었다."]], [["Such was the state of affairs in the month of July; and Fanny had just reached her eighteenth year, when the society of the village received an addition in the brother and sister of Mrs.", "칠월의 형편은 이러했다. 그리고 패니가 막 열여덟이 되었을 무렵, 그랜트 부인의 남동생과 여동생인 크로퍼드 남매가 마을 사교계에 새로 더해졌다."], ["Grant, a Mr. and Miss Crawford, the children of her mother by a second marriage.", "그들은 그랜트 부인의 어머니가 재혼해 낳은 자식들이었다."], ["They were young people of fortune.", "그들은 재산 있는 젊은이들이었다."], ["The son had a good estate in Norfolk, the daughter twenty thousand pounds.", "아들은 노퍽에 좋은 영지가 있었고, 딸은 이만 파운드를 지녔다."], ["As children, their sister had been always very fond of them; but, as her own marriage had been soon followed by the death of their common parent, which left them to the care of a brother of their father, of whom Mrs.", "어릴 적 누이는 늘 그들을 무척 귀여워했다. 그러나 제 혼인 뒤 얼마 안 되어 두 사람의 공통된 어버이가 세상을 떠나면서, 그들은 그랜트 부인이 전혀 모르는 그 아버지 쪽 형제의 손에 맡겨졌고, 그리하여 그 뒤로는 그들을 거의 보지 못했다."], ["Grant knew nothing, she had scarcely seen them since.", "삼촌의 집에서 그들은 다정한 보금자리를 얻었다."], ["In their uncle’s house they had found a kind home. Admiral and Mrs.", "크로퍼드 제독과 그 부인은 다른 무엇에서도 뜻이 맞지 않았으나 이 아이들을 향한 애정에서만은 하나였다."], ["Crawford, though agreeing in nothing else, were united in affection for these children, or, at least, were no farther adverse in their feelings than that each had their favourite, to whom they showed the greatest fondness of the two.", "아니, 적어도 각자 더 아끼는 아이가 따로 있어 그 아이에게 둘 중 더한 정을 쏟았다는 것 말고는, 그 마음이 어긋나지 않았다. 제독은 사내아이를 귀여워했고, 크로퍼드 부인은 계집아이를 애지중지했다."], ["The Admiral delighted in the boy, Mrs. Crawford doted on the girl; and it was the lady’s death which now obliged her protegee, after some months’ further trial at her uncle’s house, to find another home.", "그리고 이제 그 부인이 세상을 떠나는 바람에, 몇 달 더 삼촌 집에서 지내 본 끝에 그녀가 보살피던 그 아가씨는 다른 보금자리를 찾지 않을 수 없게 되었다."], ["Admiral Crawford was a man of vicious conduct, who chose, instead of retaining his niece, to bring his mistress under his own roof; and to this Mrs.", "크로퍼드 제독은 행실이 못된 사람이라, 조카딸을 곁에 두는 대신 제 정부를 제집 지붕 밑에 들이는 쪽을 택했다."], ["Grant was indebted for her sister’s proposal of coming to her, a measure quite as welcome on one side as it could be expedient on the other; for Mrs.", "그리고 그랜트 부인이 동생에게서 저를 찾아오겠다는 뜻을 받게 된 것은 다 그 덕이었으니, 이 일은 한쪽에는 요긴한 만큼 다른 쪽에는 더없이 반가운 것이었다."], ["Grant, having by this time run through the usual resources of ladies residing in the country without a family of children—having more than filled her favourite sitting-room with pretty furniture, and made a choice collection of plants and poultry—was very much in want of some variety at home.", "그도 그럴 것이, 그랜트 부인은 이 무렵 자식 없이 시골에 사는 부인네가 으레 기대는 소일거리를 다 써 버린 터라—좋아하는 거실을 예쁜 가구로 넘치도록 채우고, 화초와 가금을 엄선해 갖추어 놓은 터라—집에서 무언가 색다른 것이 몹시 아쉽던 참이었다."], ["The arrival, therefore, of a sister whom she had always loved, and now hoped to retain with her as long as she remained single, was highly agreeable; and her chief anxiety was lest Mansfield should not satisfy the habits of a young woman who had been mostly used to London.", "그러니 늘 사랑해 온 데다 이제 미혼으로 있는 한 곁에 붙들어 두려는 여동생이 오는 것은 더없이 반가운 일이었다. 그녀의 가장 큰 걱정은, 대개 런던에 익숙한 젊은 여자에게 맨스필드가 성에 차지 않으면 어쩌나 하는 것뿐이었다."]], [["Miss Crawford was not entirely free from similar apprehensions, though they arose principally from doubts of her sister’s style of living and tone of society; and it was not till after she had tried in vain to persuade her brother to settle with her at his own country house, that she could resolve to hazard herself among her other relations. To anything like a permanence of abode, or limitation of society, Henry Crawford had, unluckily, a great dislike: he could not accommodate his sister in an article of such importance; but he escorted her, with the utmost kindness, into Northamptonshire, and as readily engaged to fetch her away again, at half an hour’s notice, whenever she were weary of the place.", "크로퍼드 양도 비슷한 걱정에서 아주 자유롭지는 않았으나, 그것은 주로 언니의 사는 방식과 사교의 격에 대한 미심쩍음에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그리고 오빠에게 그의 시골집에 저와 함께 자리 잡자고 설득하려다 헛물만 켠 뒤에야, 그녀는 다른 친척들 틈에 저를 내맡겨 볼 마음을 먹을 수 있었다. 한군데 붙박이로 지내거나 사교를 좁히는 따위의 일을 헨리 크로퍼드는 딱하게도 몹시 싫어했다. 그토록 중대한 일에서 그는 누이를 맞춰 줄 수 없었다. 그러나 더없이 다정하게 그녀를 노샘프턴셔까지 바래다주었고, 그녀가 그곳에 싫증이 나거든 언제든 반 시간만 알려 주면 다시 데리러 오마고 선선히 약속했다."]], [["The meeting was very satisfactory on each side.", "만남은 양쪽 모두에게 매우 흡족했다."], ["Miss Crawford found a sister without preciseness or rusticity, a sister’s husband who looked the gentleman, and a house commodious and well fitted up; and Mrs.", "크로퍼드 양은 까다롭지도 촌스럽지도 않은 언니와, 신사다워 보이는 형부와, 넓고 잘 꾸며진 집을 발견했다."], ["Grant received in those whom she hoped to love better than ever a young man and woman of very prepossessing appearance.", "그리고 그랜트 부인은 그 어느 때보다 더 사랑하고 싶던 이들에게서 아주 호감 가는 용모의 젊은 남녀를 맞이했다. 메리 크로퍼드는 눈에 띄게 예뻤다."], ["Mary Crawford was remarkably pretty; Henry, though not handsome, had air and countenance; the manners of both were lively and pleasant, and Mrs.", "헨리는 잘생기지는 않았어도 기품과 인상이 있었다. 두 사람의 몸가짐은 다 발랄하고 유쾌했으니, 그랜트 부인은 이내 그 밖의 모든 것도 다 좋으리라 믿어 주었다."], ["Grant immediately gave them credit for everything else.", "그녀는 둘 다 마음에 들었으나, 메리가 가장 아끼는 대상이었다."], ["She was delighted with each, but Mary was her dearest object; and having never been able to glory in beauty of her own, she thoroughly enjoyed the power of being proud of her sister’s.", "제 미모를 자랑해 본 적 없는 그녀는 동생의 미모를 자랑스러워할 수 있다는 그 힘을 마음껏 누렸다. 동생이 오기를 기다렸다가 알맞은 혼처를 알아본 것도 아니었다."], ["She had not waited her arrival to look out for a suitable match for her: she had fixed on Tom Bertram; the eldest son of a baronet was not too good for a girl of twenty thousand pounds, with all the elegance and accomplishments which Mrs.", "그녀는 이미 톰 버트럼을 점찍어 두었다. 그랜트 부인이 동생에게서 내다본 그 온갖 우아함과 재주까지 갖춘 이만 파운드짜리 아가씨에게, 준남작의 맏아들쯤이야 과분할 것이 없었다."], ["Grant foresaw in her; and being a warm-hearted, unreserved woman, Mary had not been three hours in the house before she told her what she had planned.", "그리고 마음 따뜻하고 스스럼없는 여자인지라, 그랜트 부인은 동생이 집에 온 지 세 시간도 채 안 되어 제가 세운 계획을 동생에게 털어놓았다."]], [["Miss Crawford was glad to find a family of such consequence so very near them, and not at all displeased either at her sister’s early care, or the choice it had fallen on.", "크로퍼드 양은 그토록 지체 있는 집안이 그리 가까이 있는 것이 반가웠고, 언니가 진작 마음 써 준 것도, 그 마음이 향한 상대도 조금도 못마땅하지 않았다."], ["Matrimony was her object, provided she could marry well: and having seen Mr.", "잘만 시집갈 수 있다면 혼인이야말로 그녀의 목표였다."], ["Bertram in town, she knew that objection could no more be made to his person than to his situation in life.", "그리고 도시에서 버트럼 씨를 본 적이 있는지라, 그 사람됨에는 그 처지에 못지않게 트집 잡을 데가 없음을 알고 있었다."], ["While she treated it as a joke, therefore, she did not forget to think of it seriously.", "그러니 그녀는 그것을 농담으로 다루면서도 진지하게 생각해 보기를 잊지 않았다."], ["The scheme was soon repeated to Henry.", "그 계획은 이내 헨리에게 되풀이해 전해졌다."]], [["“And now,”", "“그리고 이제,”"], ["added Mrs.", "그랜트 부인이 덧붙였다."], ["Grant, “I have thought of something to make it complete.", "“이걸 완벽하게 만들 궁리를 하나 해 뒀단다. 나는 너희 둘 다 이 고장에 자리 잡게 하고 싶어 죽겠거든."], ["I should dearly love to settle you both in this country; and therefore, Henry, you shall marry the youngest Miss Bertram, a nice, handsome, good-humoured, accomplished girl, who will make you very happy.”", "그러니 헨리, 너는 버트럼가의 막내딸과 혼인하렴. 참하고 예쁘고 성품 밝고 재주 있는 아가씨라, 너를 아주 행복하게 해 줄 거야.”"]], [["Henry bowed and thanked her.", "헨리는 고개를 숙여 고맙다고 했다."]], [["“My dear sister,”", "“언니,” 메리가 말했다."], ["said Mary, “if you can persuade him into anything of the sort, it will be a fresh matter of delight to me to find myself allied to anybody so clever, and I shall only regret that you have not half a dozen daughters to dispose of.", "“만약 언니가 저 사람을 그런 일에 설득해 낼 수만 있다면, 나는 그렇게 영리한 분과 인척이 되는 게 새삼 기쁠 거예요. 다만 언니한테 시집보낼 딸이 대여섯이나 되지 않는 게 아쉬울 뿐이지요."], ["If you can persuade Henry to marry, you must have the address of a Frenchwoman.", "헨리를 혼인하도록 설득할 수 있다면, 언니는 프랑스 여자 뺨치는 수완이 있는 게 틀림없어요."], ["All that English abilities can do has been tried already.", "잉글랜드 여자의 온갖 재주로 할 수 있는 건 이미 다 해 봤거든요."], ["I have three very particular friends who have been all dying for him in their turn; and the pains which they, their mothers (very clever women), as well as my dear aunt and myself, have taken to reason, coax, or trick him into marrying, is inconceivable!", "저한테는 아주 각별한 친구가 셋 있는데, 하나씩 차례로 저이한테 죽고 못 살았어요. 그 애들이며, 그 애들 어머니들(아주 영리한 부인들이지요)이며, 저희 이모와 저까지 나서서, 저이를 타이르고 어르고 꾀어 혼인시키려고 들인 공은 이루 말할 수도 없어요!"], ["He is the most horrible flirt that can be imagined.", "저이는 상상할 수 있는 가장 지독한 바람둥이예요."], ["If your Miss Bertrams do not like to have their hearts broke, let them avoid Henry.”", "언니네 버트럼 자매가 가슴 찢기기 싫거든, 헨리를 멀리하라고 하세요.”"]], [["“My dear brother, I will not believe this of you.”", "“얘, 나는 너에 대해 그런 말은 안 믿는다.”"]], [["“No, I am sure you are too good.", "“그럼요, 누님은 워낙 착하시니까요."], ["You will be kinder than Mary.", "누님은 메리보다 너그러우실 거예요."], ["You will allow for the doubts of youth and inexperience.", "젊음과 미숙함이 품는 망설임쯤은 봐주실 테니까요."], ["I am of a cautious temper, and unwilling to risk my happiness in a hurry.", "저는 조심스러운 성미라, 서둘러 제 행복을 걸고 싶지 않을 뿐이에요."], ["Nobody can think more highly of the matrimonial state than myself.", "혼인이라는 것을 저보다 더 높이 사는 사람도 없어요."], ["I consider the blessing of a wife as most justly described in those discreet lines of the poet—‘Heaven’s last best gift.’”", "저는 아내라는 축복이 시인의 그 신중한 구절에 더없이 옳게 그려져 있다고 여겨요—‘하늘이 마지막으로 준 가장 좋은 선물’이라고요.”"]], [["“There, Mrs. Grant, you see how he dwells on one word, and only look at his smile.", "“보세요, 언니, 저이가 한 단어에 어찌나 힘을 주는지, 저 미소 좀 보시라니까요."], ["I assure you he is very detestable; the Admiral’s lessons have quite spoiled him.”", "정말이지 저이는 얄미워 죽겠어요. 제독의 가르침이 저이를 아주 버려 놓았다니까요.”"]], [["“I pay very little regard,” said Mrs.", "“나는 별로 개의치 않는단다,”"], ["Grant, “to what any young person says on the subject of marriage.", "그랜트 부인이 말했다. “젊은 사람이 혼인을 두고 무슨 말을 하든 말이야."], ["If they profess a disinclination for it, I only set it down that they have not yet seen the right person.”", "싫다고 큰소리치면, 나는 그저 아직 제짝을 못 만난 게로구나 여길 뿐이지.”"]], [["Dr. Grant laughingly congratulated Miss Crawford on feeling no disinclination to the state herself.", "그랜트 박사는 웃으며, 크로퍼드 양은 정작 혼인이라는 것에 아무 거부감이 없어 다행이라고 짓궂게 축하했다."]], [["“Oh yes!", "“어머, 그럼요!"], ["I am not at all ashamed of it.", "저는 조금도 부끄럽지 않아요. 저는 누구든 제대로만 할 수 있다면 다 혼인해야 한다고 봐요."], ["I would have everybody marry if they can do it properly: I do not like to have people throw themselves away; but everybody should marry as soon as they can do it to advantage.”", "사람이 저를 함부로 내던지는 건 싫어요. 하지만 누구든 이롭게 할 수 있는 한 되도록 빨리 혼인해야지요.”"]], [["# CHAPTER V", "# 제5장"]], [["The young people were pleased with each other from the first.", "젊은이들은 처음부터 서로 마음에 들어 했다."], ["On each side there was much to attract, and their acquaintance soon promised as early an intimacy as good manners would warrant.", "양쪽 다 서로를 끌 만한 것이 많았고, 그들의 사귐은 이내 예의가 허락하는 한 그만큼 이른 친밀로 나아갈 조짐을 보였다."], ["Miss Crawford’s beauty did her no disservice with the Miss Bertrams.", "크로퍼드 양의 미모는 버트럼 자매에게 조금도 해가 되지 않았다."], ["They were too handsome themselves to dislike any woman for being so too, and were almost as much charmed as their brothers with her lively dark eye, clear brown complexion, and general prettiness.", "그들 자신이 워낙 아름다워, 다른 여자가 아름답다고 미워할 사람들이 아니었고, 그 발랄한 검은 눈과 맑은 갈색 살결과 전반적인 예쁨에 오라비들 못지않게 반했다. 만약 그녀가 키가 크고 몸이 풍만하고 살결이 희었더라면 좀 더 견디기 어려웠을지 모른다."], ["Had she been tall, full formed, and fair, it might have been more of a trial: but as it was, there could be no comparison; and she was most allowably a sweet, pretty girl, while they were the finest young women in the country.", "그러나 실상은 견줄 것이 못 되었다. 그녀는 나무랄 데 없이 사랑스럽고 예쁜 아가씨였고, 그들은 이 고장에서 가장 훤칠한 젊은 여자들이었으니 말이다."]], [["Her brother was not handsome: no, when they first saw him he was absolutely plain, black and plain; but still he was the gentleman, with a pleasing address. The second meeting proved him not so very plain: he was plain, to be sure, but then he had so much countenance, and his teeth were so good, and he was so well made, that one soon forgot he was plain; and after a third interview, after dining in company with him at the Parsonage, he was no longer allowed to be called so by anybody. He was, in fact, the most agreeable young man the sisters had ever known, and they were equally delighted with him. Miss Bertram’s engagement made him in equity the property of Julia, of which Julia was fully aware; and before he had been at Mansfield a week, she was quite ready to be fallen in love with.", "그녀의 오빠는 잘생기지 않았다. 아니, 처음 보았을 때 그는 영락없이 못생긴, 거무튀튀하고 못생긴 사람이었다. 그래도 그는 신사였고 말씨가 상냥했다. 두 번째 만남에서는 그가 그리 못생기지는 않았음이 드러났다. 물론 못생기기는 했다. 그러나 인상이 워낙 좋고 치아가 워낙 고르고 몸매가 워낙 잘 잡혀 있어, 이내 그가 못생겼다는 것을 잊게 되었다. 그리고 세 번째로 만나, 목사관에서 함께 저녁을 든 뒤로는, 아무도 그를 더 이상 그리 부르도록 허락하지 않았다. 실로 그는 자매가 여태 알던 가장 호감 가는 젊은이였고, 그들은 그에게 똑같이 반했다. 버트럼 양이 약혼한 몸이었으므로 형평상 그는 줄리아의 몫이 되었고, 줄리아는 그것을 잘 알고 있었다. 그리하여 그가 맨스필드에 온 지 한 주도 되기 전에, 그녀는 얼마든지 사랑에 빠질 준비가 되어 있었다."]], [["Maria’s notions on the subject were more confused and indistinct.", "이 일에 대한 마리아의 생각은 더 흐릿하고 어정쩡했다."], ["She did not want to see or understand.", "그녀는 보려고도 알려고도 하지 않았다."], ["“There could be no harm in her liking an agreeable man—everybody knew her situation—Mr. Crawford must take care of himself.”", "“호감 가는 남자를 좋아한다고 해가 될 것은 없다—누구나 제 처지를 아니까—크로퍼드 씨가 알아서 몸을 사려야지.”"], ["Mr. Crawford did not mean to be in any danger!", "크로퍼드 씨는 조금도 위험에 빠질 생각이 없었다!"], ["the Miss Bertrams were worth pleasing, and were ready to be pleased; and he began with no object but of making them like him.", "버트럼 자매는 마음을 사 둘 만했고, 또 기꺼이 마음을 내줄 태세였다. 그리고 그는 그저 그들이 저를 좋아하게 만드는 것 말고는 다른 속셈 없이 시작했다."], ["He did not want them to die of love; but with sense and temper which ought to have made him judge and feel better, he allowed himself great latitude on such points.", "그들이 사랑에 겨워 애태우기를 바란 것은 아니었다. 그러나 마땅히 그를 더 옳게 판단하고 느끼게 했어야 할 분별과 성정을 지녔으면서도, 그는 그런 점에서 스스로에게 큰 여유를 허락했다."]], [["“I like your Miss Bertrams exceedingly, sister,”", "“누님네 버트럼 자매가 저는 무척 마음에 들어요, 누님.”"], ["said he, as he returned from attending them to their carriage after the said dinner visit; “they are very elegant, agreeable girls.”", "앞서 말한 그 저녁 방문 뒤, 그들을 마차까지 배웅하고 돌아오며 그가 말했다. “아주 우아하고 호감 가는 아가씨들이에요.”"]], [["“So they are indeed, and I am delighted to hear you say it.", "“정말 그렇지. 네가 그렇게 말해 주니 나도 기쁘구나."], ["But you like Julia best.”", "그런데 너는 줄리아가 제일 좋지.”"]], [["“Oh yes!", "“아, 그럼요!"], ["I like Julia best.”", "저는 줄리아가 제일 좋아요.”"]], [["“But do you really?", "“그런데 정말 그러니?"], ["for Miss Bertram is in general thought the handsomest.”", "버트럼 양이 대개 더 예쁘다고들 하던데.”"]], [["“So I should suppose. She has the advantage in every feature, and I prefer her countenance; but I like Julia best; Miss Bertram is certainly the handsomest, and I have found her the most agreeable, but I shall always like Julia best, because you order me.”", "“저도 그러리라 짐작해요. 이목구비 하나하나가 다 낫고, 저도 그 얼굴이 더 좋아요. 그래도 저는 줄리아가 제일 좋아요. 버트럼 양이 분명 더 예쁘고, 제가 만나 보니 더 호감도 가지만, 그래도 저는 언제나 줄리아가 제일 좋을 거예요. 누님이 그러라고 명하시니까요.”"]], [["“I shall not talk to you, Henry, but I know you will like her best at last.”", "“너하고는 말을 안 하겠다, 헨리. 하지만 나는 안다, 너는 끝내 그 애를 제일 좋아하게 될 거야.”"]], [["“Do not I tell you that I like her best at first?”", "“제일 처음부터 제가 그 애를 제일 좋아한다고 말씀드리잖아요?”"]], [["“And besides, Miss Bertram is engaged.", "“게다가 버트럼 양은 약혼한 몸이야."], ["Remember that, my dear brother.", "그걸 잊지 마라, 헨리."], ["Her choice is made.”", "그 애는 이미 짝을 정했어.”"]], [["“Yes, and I like her the better for it.", "“네, 그래서 그 애가 더 좋은걸요."], ["An engaged woman is always more agreeable than a disengaged.", "약혼한 여자는 언제나 약혼 안 한 여자보다 더 호감이 가거든요."], ["She is satisfied with herself.", "스스로에게 흡족해하니까요."], ["Her cares are over, and she feels that she may exert all her powers of pleasing without suspicion.", "근심은 끝났고, 이제 의심받을 걱정 없이 사람을 끌 온갖 재간을 마음껏 부려도 된다고 느끼거든요."], ["All is safe with a lady engaged: no harm can be done.”", "약혼한 아가씨라면 만사가 안전하지요. 아무 탈도 날 게 없어요.”"]], [["“Why, as to that, Mr. Rushworth is a very good sort of young man, and it is a great match for her.”", "“하기야 그 점으로 말하면, 러시워스 씨도 아주 착한 젊은이고, 그 애한테는 대단한 혼처지.”"]], [["“But Miss Bertram does not care three straws for him; that is your opinion of your intimate friend.", "“하지만 버트럼 양은 그 사람을 지푸라기만큼도 마음에 두지 않아요. 그게 바로 누님이 그 절친한 친구를 두고 내리는 평이지요."], ["I do not subscribe to it.", "저는 거기 동의하지 않아요."], ["I am sure Miss Bertram is very much attached to Mr.", "저는 버트럼 양이 러시워스 씨를 무척 사모한다고 확신해요."], ["Rushworth. I could see it in her eyes, when he was mentioned.", "그 사람 이야기가 나왔을 때 그 애 눈에서 그걸 봤거든요."], ["I think too well of Miss Bertram to suppose she would ever give her hand without her heart.”", "저는 버트럼 양을 워낙 좋게 여겨서, 그 애가 마음 없이 손을 내줄 사람이라고는 생각지 않아요.”"]], [["“Mary, how shall we manage him?”", "“메리, 우리 저 애를 어떻게 다루면 좋을까?”"]], [["“We must leave him to himself, I believe.", "“그냥 저 사람 하는 대로 내버려 둬야 할 거예요."], ["Talking does no good.", "말해 봤자 소용없어요."], ["He will be taken in at last.”", "저 사람도 끝내 넘어가고 말걸요.”"]], [["“But I would not have him taken in; I would not have him duped; I would have it all fair and honourable.”", "“하지만 나는 저 애가 속아 넘어가는 건 싫구나. 저 애가 속는 것도, 그런 건 원치 않아. 나는 다 정정당당하고 떳떳했으면 좋겠어.”"]], [["“Oh dear!", "“어머, 참!"], ["let him stand his chance and be taken in.", "그냥 저 사람도 운에 맡겨 두고 넘어가게 놔둬요."], ["It will do just as well.", "그래도 매한가지예요."], ["Everybody is taken in at some period or other.”", "사람은 누구나 언젠가는 다 속아 넘어가는걸요.”"]], [["“Not always in marriage, dear Mary.”", "“혼인에서 늘 그런 건 아니란다, 메리.”"]], [["“In marriage especially.", "“혼인에서는 유난히 그렇지요."], ["With all due respect to such of the present company as chance to be married, my dear Mrs.", "지금 이 자리에 마침 혼인하신 분들께는 죄송하지만, 그랜트 부인, 남자든 여자든 혼인할 때 속아 넘어가지 않는 사람은 백에 하나도 없어요."], ["Grant, there is not one in a hundred of either sex who is not taken in when they marry.", "어딜 둘러봐도 정말 그렇다는 게 보여요. 게다가 곰곰이 생각해 보면 그럴 수밖에 없다는 걸 느껴요."], ["Look where I will, I see that it is so; and I feel that it must be so, when I consider that it is, of all transactions, the one in which people expect most from others, and are least honest themselves.”", "세상 온갖 거래 가운데 사람들이 상대에게 가장 많은 걸 바라면서 정작 저 자신은 가장 정직하지 못한 게 바로 혼인이니까요. 이게 속임수가 아니고 뭐겠어요?”"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["You have been in a bad school for matrimony, in Hill Street.”", "너는 힐 스트리트에서 혼인에 관한 나쁜 학교를 다닌 거로구나.”"]], [["“My poor aunt had certainly little cause to love the state; but, however, speaking from my own observation, it is a manoeuvring business.", "“딱한 이모야 분명 그 처지를 사랑할 이유가 별로 없었지요. 하지만 그야 어떻든, 제가 직접 겪어 본 바로 말하면, 혼인이란 술수를 부리는 일이에요."], ["I know so many who have married in the full expectation and confidence of some one particular advantage in the connexion, or accomplishment, or good quality in the person, who have found themselves entirely deceived, and been obliged to put up with exactly the reverse.", "저는 이 인연에서 어떤 특별한 이득이나, 상대의 어떤 재주나, 어떤 좋은 자질을 잔뜩 기대하고 믿고서 혼인했다가, 완전히 속았음을 알고 정반대의 것을 고스란히 감내해야 했던 사람을 아주 많이 알아요."], ["What is this but a take in?”", "이게 속임수가 아니면 뭐예요?”"]], [["“My dear child, there must be a little imagination here.", "“얘야, 여기엔 얼마간 상상이 섞였을 게다. 미안하다만, 나는 네 말을 다 믿을 수는 없구나."], ["I beg your pardon, but I cannot quite believe you.", "정말이지 너는 절반밖에 못 보는 거야. 나쁜 것은 보면서 위안은 못 보는 거지."], ["Depend upon it, you see but half.", "어디에나 자잘한 마찰과 실망은 있기 마련이고, 우리는 다들 너무 많은 걸 바라기 십상이란다."], ["You see the evil, but you do not see the consolation.", "하지만 그러다 행복의 한 계획이 어그러지면, 사람의 본성은 또 다른 계획으로 돌아서지. 첫 번째 셈이 틀리면 두 번째로 더 나은 셈을 하고 말이야."], ["There will be little rubs and disappointments everywhere, and we are all apt to expect too much; but then, if one scheme of happiness fails, human nature turns to another; if the first calculation is wrong, we make a second better: we find comfort somewhere—and those evil-minded observers, dearest Mary, who make much of a little, are more taken in and deceived than the parties themselves.”", "우리는 어디선가 위안을 찾아내는 법이란다. 그리고 얘, 사랑하는 메리야, 작은 것을 크게 부풀리는 그 심술궂은 구경꾼들이야말로 정작 당사자들보다 더 속고 홀린 사람들이지.”"]], [["“Well done, sister!", "“잘하셨어요, 언니!"], ["I honour your esprit du corps.", "제 편을 감싸는 그 의리, 참으로 존경스러워요."], ["When I am a wife, I mean to be just as staunch myself; and I wish my friends in general would be so too.", "저도 아내가 되면 꼭 그만큼 든든히 버티려고요. 그리고 제 친구들도 다들 그래 주면 좋겠어요."], ["It would save me many a heartache.”", "그러면 저도 가슴앓이를 많이 덜 텐데.”"]], [["“You are as bad as your brother, Mary; but we will cure you both.", "“너도 네 오라비 못지않게 고약하구나, 메리. 하지만 우리가 너희 둘 다 고쳐 줄게."], ["Mansfield shall cure you both, and without any taking in.", "맨스필드가 너희 둘 다 고쳐 줄 거야, 아무도 속이는 일 없이 말이지."], ["Stay with us, and we will cure you.”", "우리와 함께 지내려무나, 우리가 너희를 고쳐 줄 테니.”"]], [["The Crawfords, without wanting to be cured, were very willing to stay.", "크로퍼드 남매는 고쳐지기를 바라지는 않았어도 기꺼이 머물려 했다."], ["Mary was satisfied with the Parsonage as a present home, and Henry equally ready to lengthen his visit.", "메리는 목사관을 당장의 거처로 삼는 데 만족했고, 헨리도 그에 못지않게 선선히 머무는 기간을 늘렸다."], ["He had come, intending to spend only a few days with them; but Mansfield promised well, and there was nothing to call him elsewhere.", "그는 며칠만 함께 지낼 작정으로 왔었다. 그러나 맨스필드가 좋은 조짐을 보였고, 딱히 그를 다른 데로 부르는 일도 없었다."], ["It delighted Mrs. Grant to keep them both with her, and Dr.", "두 사람을 곁에 붙들어 둘 수 있어 그랜트 부인은 기뻤고, 그랜트 박사도 그리되는 것이 더없이 흡족했다."], ["Grant was exceedingly well contented to have it so: a talking pretty young woman like Miss Crawford is always pleasant society to an indolent, stay-at-home man; and Mr. Crawford’s being his guest was an excuse for drinking claret every day.", "크로퍼드 양처럼 말 잘하고 예쁜 젊은 여자는 게으르고 집에만 붙어 있는 남자에게 언제나 즐거운 벗이었고, 크로퍼드 씨가 손님으로 있다는 것은 날마다 클라레를 마실 좋은 핑곗거리였으니 말이다."]], [["The Miss Bertrams’ admiration of Mr. Crawford was more rapturous than anything which Miss Crawford’s habits made her likely to feel.", "버트럼 자매가 크로퍼드 씨에게 품은 감탄은, 크로퍼드 양의 습성으로 미루어 그녀가 느낄 법한 그 무엇보다 훨씬 뜨거웠다."], ["She acknowledged, however, that the Mr. Bertrams were very fine young men, that two such young men were not often seen together even in London, and that their manners, particularly those of the eldest, were very good.", "그래도 그녀는 버트럼가 형제가 아주 훤칠한 젊은이들이라는 것, 그런 젊은이 둘이 나란히 있는 것은 런던에서도 흔히 볼 수 없다는 것, 그 몸가짐, 특히 맏이의 몸가짐이 아주 좋다는 것은 인정했다."], ["He had been much in London, and had more liveliness and gallantry than Edmund, and must, therefore, be preferred; and, indeed, his being the eldest was another strong claim.", "그는 런던에서 오래 지낸 데다 에드먼드보다 활기와 멋이 넘쳤으니, 마땅히 그가 더 나은 셈이었다. 게다가 실로 그가 맏이라는 것이 또 하나의 강력한 이유였다."], ["She had felt an early presentiment that she should like the eldest best.", "그녀는 진작부터 제가 맏이를 제일 좋아하게 되리라는 예감이 들었었다."], ["She knew it was her way.", "그게 제 방식임을 그녀는 알고 있었다."]], [["Tom Bertram must have been thought pleasant, indeed, at any rate; he was the sort of young man to be generally liked, his agreeableness was of the kind to be oftener found agreeable than some endowments of a higher stamp, for he had easy manners, excellent spirits, a large acquaintance, and a great deal to say; and the reversion of Mansfield Park, and a baronetcy, did no harm to all this. Miss Crawford soon felt that he and his situation might do. She looked about her with due consideration, and found almost everything in his favour: a park, a real park, five miles round, a spacious modern-built house, so well placed and well screened as to deserve to be in any collection of engravings of gentlemen’s seats in the kingdom, and wanting only to be completely new furnished—pleasant sisters, a quiet mother, and an agreeable man himself—with the advantage of being tied up from much gaming at present by a promise to his father, and of being Sir Thomas hereafter. It might do very well; she believed she should accept him; and she began accordingly to interest herself a little about the horse which he had to run at the B—— races.", "톰 버트럼은 어차피 누가 봐도 유쾌한 사람으로 여겨졌을 것이다. 그는 대체로 사람들이 좋아하는 부류의 젊은이였고, 그 호감은 더 고상한 부류의 어떤 재능보다 오히려 더 자주 호감을 사는 그런 종류였다. 몸가짐이 느긋하고, 기색이 좋고, 아는 사람이 많고, 할 말이 많았으니 말이다. 게다가 맨스필드 파크의 상속권과 준남작 작위도 이 모든 것에 해가 될 리 없었다. 크로퍼드 양은 이내 그와 그의 처지라면 괜찮겠다고 느꼈다. 그녀는 마땅한 헤아림으로 주위를 둘러보았고, 거의 모든 것이 그에게 유리함을 알았다. 둘레가 오 마일에 이르는 진짜 파크, 널찍하고 근래에 지은 저택—자리도 좋고 가림막도 좋아 이 왕국의 신사 저택 판화집이라면 어디에 실려도 손색없을 만한, 그저 온전히 새로 꾸미기만 하면 될 저택—상냥한 시누이들, 조용한 시어머니, 그리고 호감 가는 사내 그 자신까지. 게다가 당장은 아버지와의 약속에 매여 크게 노름을 못 한다는 이점, 앞으로 토머스 경이 되리라는 이점까지 있었다. 아주 괜찮을 성싶었다. 그를 받아들이게 될 것 같았다. 그리하여 그녀는 그가 B── 경마에 내보낼 말에 대해 조금씩 관심을 두기 시작했다."]], [["These races were to call him away not long after their acquaintance began; and as it appeared that the family did not, from his usual goings on, expect him back again for many weeks, it would bring his passion to an early proof.", "그 경마는 사귐이 시작된 지 얼마 안 되어 그를 불러 갈 참이었다. 그리고 그가 평소 하던 대로라면 식구들이 여러 주 동안은 그가 돌아오리라 기대하지 않는 눈치였으므로, 그것은 그의 열정을 일찌감치 시험대에 올릴 터였다."], ["Much was said on his side to induce her to attend the races, and schemes were made for a large party to them, with all the eagerness of inclination, but it would only do to be talked of.", "그는 그녀에게 경마를 보러 오라고 부추기려 여러 말을 했고, 큰 무리를 지어 함께 가자는 궁리를, 온갖 들뜬 마음으로 세웠다. 그러나 그것은 그저 말로 그칠 수밖에 없었다."]], [["And Fanny, what was she doing and thinking all this while?", "그런데 패니는, 이 모든 동안 무엇을 하고 무엇을 생각하고 있었을까?"], ["and what was her opinion of the newcomers?", "그리고 그 새로 온 이들에 대한 그녀의 생각은 어떠했을까?"], ["Few young ladies of eighteen could be less called on to speak their opinion than Fanny.", "열여덟 살 아가씨 가운데 패니만큼 제 생각을 말할 일이 없는 이도 드물었다."], ["In a quiet way, very little attended to, she paid her tribute of admiration to Miss Crawford’s beauty; but as she still continued to think Mr.", "남의 눈에 거의 띄지 않는 조용한 방식으로, 그녀는 크로퍼드 양의 미모에 감탄의 뜻을 바쳤다."], ["Crawford very plain, in spite of her two cousins having repeatedly proved the contrary, she never mentioned him.", "그러나 두 사촌이 거듭 아니라고 증명해 보였건만 여전히 크로퍼드 씨를 아주 못생겼다고 여겼으므로, 그에 대해서는 한 번도 입에 올리지 않았다."], ["The notice, which she excited herself, was to this effect.", "그녀가 정작 불러일으킨 주목이란 이런 것이었다."], ["“I begin now to understand you all, except Miss Price,”", "“이제 여러분을 다 알아 가겠는데, 프라이스 양만은 잘 모르겠네요.”"], ["said Miss Crawford, as she was walking with the Mr.", "버트럼가 형제와 함께 걸으며 크로퍼드 양이 말했다."], ["Bertrams. “Pray, is she out, or is she not?", "“저 아가씨는 사교계에 나온 건가요, 아닌가요?"], ["I am puzzled.", "헷갈리네요."], ["She dined at the Parsonage, with the rest of you, which seemed like being out; and yet she says so little, that I can hardly suppose she is.”", "여러분과 함께 목사관에서 저녁을 들었으니 나온 것 같기도 한데, 워낙 말이 없어서 나왔다고는 도무지 보기 어렵거든요.”"]], [["Edmund, to whom this was chiefly addressed, replied, “I believe I know what you mean, but I will not undertake to answer the question.", "주로 이 말을 들은 에드먼드가 대답했다. “무슨 말씀인지는 알 것 같습니다만, 저로서는 그 물음에 답을 못 하겠군요."], ["My cousin is grown up.", "제 사촌은 다 컸어요."], ["She has the age and sense of a woman, but the outs and not outs are beyond me.”", "여자로서의 나이와 분별을 갖추었지요. 하지만 나왔느니 안 나왔느니 하는 건 제 소관 밖입니다.”"]], [["“And yet, in general, nothing can be more easily ascertained.", "“그렇지만 대개는 그보다 더 쉽게 알 수 있는 게 없는걸요."], ["The distinction is so broad.", "그 구별이 워낙 뚜렷하잖아요."], ["Manners as well as appearance are, generally speaking, so totally different.", "대체로 겉모습만이 아니라 몸가짐까지 판이하게 다르니까요."], ["Till now, I could not have supposed it possible to be mistaken as to a girl’s being out or not.", "여태 저는 어떤 아가씨가 나왔는지 안 나왔는지를 두고 사람이 틀릴 수 있으리라고는 생각지 못했어요. 사교계에 안 나온 아가씨는 늘 같은 차림새예요."], ["A girl not out has always the same sort of dress: a close bonnet, for instance; looks very demure, and never says a word.", "이를테면 꼭 여미는 보닛을 쓰고요. 아주 얌전해 보이고 한마디도 안 하지요."], ["You may smile, but it is so, I assure you; and except that it is sometimes carried a little too far, it is all very proper.", "웃으셔도 좋지만, 정말 그렇답니다. 그리고 이따금 좀 지나칠 때가 있다는 것만 빼면, 그것도 다 지당한 일이에요."], ["Girls should be quiet and modest.", "아가씨란 조용하고 겸손해야 하니까요."], ["The most objectionable part is, that the alteration of manners on being introduced into company is frequently too sudden.", "가장 못마땅한 대목은, 사교계에 나오면서 몸가짐이 달라지는 것이 흔히 너무 급작스럽다는 거예요."], ["They sometimes pass in such very little time from reserve to quite the opposite—to confidence!", "더러는 아주 짧은 새에 삼가는 태도에서 정반대로—당당함으로! 넘어가 버리거든요."], ["That is the faulty part of the present system.", "바로 그게 지금 방식의 흠이지요."], ["One does not like to see a girl of eighteen or nineteen so immediately up to every thing—and perhaps when one has seen her hardly able to speak the year before.", "열여덟, 열아홉 아가씨가 그렇게 대뜸 세상만사에 능란해진 걸 보면 마뜩잖거든요—그것도 어쩌면 한 해 전에는 말도 제대로 못 하는 걸 봤는데 말이에요."], ["Mr. Bertram, I dare say you have sometimes met with such changes.”", "버트럼 씨, 씨도 아마 그런 변화를 더러 겪어 보셨겠지요.”"]], [["“I believe I have, but this is hardly fair; I see what you are at.", "“겪어 본 것 같습니다만, 이건 좀 공평치 않은데요. 무슨 속셈인지 알겠어요."], ["You are quizzing me and Miss Anderson.”", "저와 앤더슨 양을 놀리시는 거군요.”"]], [["“No, indeed.", "“아니에요, 정말로."], ["Miss Anderson!", "앤더슨 양이라니!"], ["I do not know who or what you mean.", "누구를, 무슨 말씀을 하시는지 저는 도무지 모르겠어요."], ["I am quite in the dark.", "완전히 깜깜하다니까요."], ["But I will quiz you with a great deal of pleasure, if you will tell me what about.”", "하지만 무엇에 관해서인지 말씀만 해 주시면, 얼마든지 기꺼이 놀려 드릴게요.”"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["you carry it off very well, but I cannot be quite so far imposed on.", "시치미를 아주 잘 떼시는군요. 하지만 그렇게까지 속아 넘어갈 저는 아니에요."], ["You must have had Miss Anderson in your eye, in describing an altered young lady.", "달라진 아가씨를 그려 보이실 때 앤더슨 양을 염두에 두셨던 게 분명해요."], ["You paint too accurately for mistake.", "틀릴 수 없을 만큼 정확하게 그리셨거든요."], ["It was exactly so.", "딱 그랬어요."], ["The Andersons of Baker Street.", "베이커 스트리트의 앤더슨가 말이에요."], ["We were speaking of them the other day, you know.", "며칠 전에 그 집 이야기를 했잖아요."], ["Edmund, you have heard me mention Charles Anderson.", "에드먼드, 너도 내가 찰스 앤더슨 얘기하는 걸 들었지."], ["The circumstance was precisely as this lady has represented it.", "사정은 이 아가씨가 그려 보인 것과 꼭 같았어."], ["When Anderson first introduced me to his family, about two years ago, his sister was not out, and I could not get her to speak to me.", "앤더슨이 두 해쯤 전에 처음 나를 제 식구에게 소개했을 때, 그 누이는 아직 사교계에 안 나온 몸이라, 나는 그 애한테서 도무지 말 한마디를 끌어낼 수 없었어."], ["I sat there an hour one morning waiting for Anderson, with only her and a little girl or two in the room, the governess being sick or run away, and the mother in and out every moment with letters of business, and I could hardly get a word or a look from the young lady—nothing like a civil answer—she screwed up her mouth, and turned from me with such an air!", "어느 날 아침 나는 앤더슨을 기다리며 한 시간이나 거기 앉아 있었는데, 방에는 그 애와 어린 계집아이 한둘뿐이었지. 가정교사는 병이 났거나 달아났고, 어머니는 볼일 편지를 들고 쉴 새 없이 들락거리는데, 나는 그 아가씨한테서 말 한마디, 눈길 한 번을 좀처럼 못 얻었어—예의 바른 대답 비슷한 것도 없이—입을 꾹 다물고 어찌나 쌀쌀맞게 나한테서 몸을 돌리던지!"], ["I did not see her again for a twelvemonth.", "그러고는 열두 달을 못 봤어."], ["She was then out. I met her at Mrs.", "그때는 그 애가 사교계에 나온 뒤였지."], ["Holford’s, and did not recollect her.", "홀퍼드 부인 댁에서 만났는데, 나는 그 애를 알아보지도 못했어."], ["She came up to me, claimed me as an acquaintance, stared me out of countenance; and talked and laughed till I did not know which way to look.", "그런데 그 애가 나한테 다가와서는 아는 사이라며 나를 뚫어져라 쳐다봐 무안하게 만들고, 어찌나 떠들고 웃어 대는지 나는 어디를 봐야 할지 몰랐다니까."], ["I felt that I must be the jest of the room at the time, and Miss Crawford, it is plain, has heard the story.”", "나는 그때 내가 온 방의 웃음거리가 된 게 틀림없다고 느꼈어. 그리고 크로퍼드 양이 그 이야기를 들은 게 분명하고요.”"]], [["“And a very pretty story it is, and with more truth in it, I dare say, than does credit to Miss Anderson.", "“아주 재미난 이야기네요. 게다가 아마 앤더슨 양의 체면에 좋을 것보다 훨씬 더 사실에 가깝겠지요."], ["It is too common a fault.", "그건 너무 흔한 흠이에요."], ["Mothers certainly have not yet got quite the right way of managing their daughters.", "어머니들이 아직 딸을 다루는 제대로 된 방법을 도무지 못 익힌 게 분명해요."], ["I do not know where the error lies.", "어디에 잘못이 있는지는 저도 모르겠어요."], ["I do not pretend to set people right, but I do see that they are often wrong.”", "남을 바로잡겠다고 나설 생각은 없어요. 그래도 그들이 자주 틀린다는 건 정말 보이거든요.”"]], [["“Those who are showing the world what female manners should be,” said Mr.", "“여자의 몸가짐이 마땅히 어떠해야 하는지 세상에 보여 주는 이들이야말로,”"], ["Bertram gallantly, “are doing a great deal to set them right.”", "버트럼 씨가 정중하게 말했다. “그것을 바로잡는 데 크게 이바지하는 것이지요.”"]], [["“The error is plain enough,”", "“잘못은 뻔합니다,”"], ["said the less courteous Edmund; “such girls are ill brought up.", "덜 정중한 에드먼드가 말했다. “그런 아가씨들은 잘못 길러진 거예요."], ["They are given wrong notions from the beginning.", "처음부터 그릇된 관념을 주입받은 겁니다."], ["They are always acting upon motives of vanity, and there is no more real modesty in their behaviour before they appear in public than afterwards.”", "늘 허영을 동기로 삼아 행동하니, 사교계에 나오기 전이라고 그 처신에 나온 뒤보다 더 참된 겸손이 있는 것도 아니지요.”"]], [["“I do not know,”", "“글쎄요,”"], ["replied Miss Crawford hesitatingly.", "크로퍼드 양이 망설이며 대답했다."], ["“Yes, I cannot agree with you there.", "“아니요, 그 점에서는 동의할 수 없어요."], ["It is certainly the modestest part of the business.", "그거야말로 분명 그 일에서 가장 얌전한 대목인걸요."], ["It is much worse to have girls not out give themselves the same airs and take the same liberties as if they were, which I have seen done.", "사교계에 안 나온 아가씨가 나온 것처럼 똑같이 거드름을 피우고 똑같이 제멋대로 구는 게 훨씬 더 나빠요. 제가 그런 걸 본 적이 있거든요."], ["That is worse than anything—quite disgusting!”", "그거야말로 무엇보다 나쁘고—정말 역겨워요!”"]], [["“Yes, that is very inconvenient indeed,”", "“그래요, 그건 정말 곤란하지요,”"], ["said Mr. Bertram.", "버트럼 씨가 말했다."], ["“It leads one astray; one does not know what to do.", "“사람을 헷갈리게 하니까요. 어찌해야 할지 모르게 되거든요."], ["The close bonnet and demure air you describe so well (and nothing was ever juster), tell one what is expected; but I got into a dreadful scrape last year from the want of them.", "아가씨가 그토록 잘 그려 보이신 그 꼭 여민 보닛과 얌전한 태도는 (그보다 더 옳은 말도 없었지만) 무엇이 요구되는지를 일러 주지요. 그런데 저는 지난해에 바로 그것이 없어서 지독한 곤경에 빠졌어요."], ["I went down to Ramsgate for a week with a friend last September, just after my return from the West Indies.", "지난 구월, 서인도에서 막 돌아온 직후에 친구 하나와 한 주 예정으로 램스게이트에 내려갔거든요. 제 친구 스니드—에드먼드, 너도 내가 스니드 얘기하는 걸 들었지—그 친구 아버지와 어머니와 누이들이 거기 있었는데, 다 처음 보는 사람들이었어."], ["My friend Sneyd—you have heard me speak of Sneyd, Edmund—his father, and mother, and sisters, were there, all new to me.", "우리가 앨비언 플레이스에 닿았을 때 그들은 외출 중이었지. 우리는 뒤따라가서 부두에서 그들을 찾았어."], ["When we reached Albion Place they were out; we went after them, and found them on the pier: Mrs.", "스니드 부인과 두 스니드 양, 그리고 그들의 다른 지인 몇이 있었어. 나는 격식대로 인사를 했지."], ["and the two Miss Sneyds, with others of their acquaintance. I made my bow in form; and as Mrs.", "그런데 스니드 부인은 남자들에게 둘러싸여 있어서, 나는 그 딸 하나에게 붙어 집까지 내내 그 곁에서 걸으며 되도록 살갑게 굴었어."], ["Sneyd was surrounded by men, attached myself to one of her daughters, walked by her side all the way home, and made myself as agreeable as I could; the young lady perfectly easy in her manners, and as ready to talk as to listen.", "그 아가씨는 몸가짐이 아주 편안하고, 듣는 만큼이나 말도 잘하더군. 내가 뭔가 잘못하고 있으리라고는 짐작조차 못 했지."], ["I had not a suspicion that I could be doing anything wrong.", "둘 다 똑같아 보였거든. 둘 다 잘 차려입고, 다른 아가씨들처럼 베일을 쓰고 양산을 들고 있었으니까."], ["They looked just the same: both well-dressed, with veils and parasols like other girls; but I afterwards found that I had been giving all my attention to the youngest, who was not out, and had most excessively offended the eldest.", "그런데 나중에 알고 보니, 나는 온통 막내에게만 마음을 쏟았던 거야. 그 애는 아직 사교계에 안 나온 몸이었고, 그 바람에 맏이의 심기를 아주 몹시 거스르고 만 거지."], ["Miss Augusta ought not to have been noticed for the next six months; and Miss Sneyd, I believe, has never forgiven me.”", "오거스타 양은 앞으로 여섯 달은 주목받아서는 안 될 몸이었거든. 그리고 스니드 양은, 아마 나를 끝내 용서하지 않았을 거야.”"]], [["“That was bad indeed.", "“그거 정말 딱했네요."], ["Poor Miss Sneyd.", "가엾은 스니드 양."], ["Though I have no younger sister, I feel for her.", "저는 여동생이 없지만 그 심정이 딱해요."], ["To be neglected before one’s time must be very vexatious; but it was entirely the mother’s fault.", "제때가 오기도 전에 밀려나는 건 여간 속상한 일이 아닐 테니까요. 하지만 그건 온전히 어머니 잘못이에요."], ["Miss Augusta should have been with her governess.", "오거스타 양은 가정교사와 함께 있었어야지요."], ["Such half-and-half doings never prosper.", "그런 어정쩡한 짓은 결코 잘될 리 없어요."], ["But now I must be satisfied about Miss Price.", "그런데 이제 저는 프라이스 양에 대해 확실히 알아야겠어요."], ["Does she go to balls?", "그분은 무도회에 가나요?"], ["Does she dine out every where, as well as at my sister’s?”", "제 언니 댁만이 아니라 여기저기 저녁 초대에도 다니나요?”"]], [["“No,”", "“아뇨,”"], ["replied Edmund; “I do not think she has ever been to a ball.", "에드먼드가 대답했다. “제 사촌은 무도회에 가 본 적이 한 번도 없을 겁니다."], ["My mother seldom goes into company herself, and dines nowhere but with Mrs.", "어머니가 좀처럼 남과 어울리러 나가지 않으시고, 그랜트 부인 댁 말고는 어디서도 저녁을 안 드시거든요."], ["Grant, and Fanny stays at home with her.”", "그래서 패니는 어머니와 함께 집에 있습니다.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["then the point is clear.", "그럼 분명하네요."], ["Miss Price is not out.”", "프라이스 양은 아직 사교계에 안 나왔군요.”"]], [["# CHAPTER VI", "# 제6장"]], [["Mr. Bertram set off for————, and Miss Crawford was prepared to find a great chasm in their society, and to miss him decidedly in the meetings which were now becoming almost daily between the families; and on their all dining together at the Park soon after his going, she retook her chosen place near the bottom of the table, fully expecting to feel a most melancholy difference in the change of masters.", "버트럼 씨는 ──로 떠났고, 크로퍼드 양은 이제 두 집안 사이에서 거의 날마다 벌어지다시피 하는 모임에 크나큰 공허가 생기고, 그가 없는 것을 여실히 아쉬워하게 되리라 미리 각오했다."], ["It would be a very flat business, she was sure. In comparison with his brother, Edmund would have nothing to say.", "그가 떠나고 얼마 안 되어 다 함께 공원 저택에서 저녁을 들게 되었을 때, 그녀는 식탁 아래쪽 제가 정해 둔 자리를 다시 차지하고서, 주인이 바뀐 탓에 더없이 서글픈 차이를 느끼게 되리라 잔뜩 벼르고 있었다."], ["The soup would be sent round in a most spiritless manner, wine drank without any smiles or agreeable trifling, and the venison cut up without supplying one pleasant anecdote of any former haunch, or a single entertaining story, about “my friend such a one.”", "분명 아주 맥빠지는 자리가 될 터였다. 그의 아우로 말하면, 그 형에 견주어 에드먼드는 할 말이 아무것도 없을 것이었다."], ["She must try to find amusement in what was passing at the upper end of the table, and in observing Mr. Rushworth, who was now making his appearance at Mansfield for the first time since the Crawfords’ arrival.", "수프는 더없이 김빠진 태도로 돌려질 것이고, 포도주는 웃음도 정겨운 농담도 없이 마셔질 것이며, 사슴고기는 지난날 어느 넓적다리 살에 얽힌 유쾌한 일화 하나, “내 아무개 친구”에 관한 재미난 이야기 하나 없이 썰릴 것이었다."], ["He had been visiting a friend in the neighbouring county, and that friend having recently had his grounds laid out by an improver, Mr.", "그녀는 식탁 위쪽에서 오가는 이야기에서, 그리고 크로퍼드 남매가 온 이래 처음으로 맨스필드에 얼굴을 내민 러시워스 씨를 지켜보는 데서 재미를 찾아보는 수밖에 없었다."], ["Rushworth was returned with his head full of the subject, and very eager to be improving his own place in the same way; and though not saying much to the purpose, could talk of nothing else.", "그는 이웃 고을의 한 친구를 찾아갔다 온 참이었는데, 그 친구가 근래에 조경가를 시켜 제 정원을 새로 꾸민 터라, 러시워스 씨는 머릿속이 온통 그 생각으로 가득 차 돌아와서는 제 땅도 꼭 그렇게 손보고 싶어 안달이었다. 그리고 딱히 요령 있는 말은 못 하면서도, 오로지 그 이야기밖에 할 줄을 몰랐다."], ["The subject had been already handled in the drawing-room; it was revived in the dining-parlour.", "그 화제는 이미 응접실에서 다뤄졌던 것이, 이제 식당에서 되살아났다."], ["Miss Bertram’s attention and opinion was evidently his chief aim; and though her deportment showed rather conscious superiority than any solicitude to oblige him, the mention of Sotherton Court, and the ideas attached to it, gave her a feeling of complacency, which prevented her from being very ungracious.", "버트럼 양의 관심과 의견을 얻는 것이 그의 주된 목표임은 뻔했다. 비록 그녀의 태도는 그의 비위를 맞추려는 마음이라기보다 은근한 우월감을 내비치는 쪽이었으나, 소더턴 코트와 거기 딸린 여러 상념이 입에 오르자 그녀는 흐뭇한 기분이 들어, 아주 매몰차게 굴지는 않았다."]], [["“I wish you could see Compton,”", "“콤프턴을 한번 보셨어야 하는데요.”"], ["said he; “it is the most complete thing!", "그가 말했다. “그야말로 완벽하답니다!"], ["I never saw a place so altered in my life.", "저는 여태 그렇게 확 달라진 곳을 본 적이 없어요."], ["I told Smith I did not know where I was.", "스미스한테 여기가 어딘지 도무지 모르겠다고 했다니까요."], ["The approach now, is one of the finest things in the country: you see the house in the most surprising manner.", "이제 그 진입로는 이 고장에서 손꼽히는 절경이에요. 저택이 어찌나 놀라운 방식으로 눈앞에 나타나는지요."], ["I declare, when I got back to Sotherton yesterday, it looked like a prison—quite a dismal old prison.”", "정말이지, 어제 소더턴에 돌아와 보니 꼭 감옥 같더군요—음침하기 짝이 없는 낡은 감옥 말이에요.”"]], [["“Oh, for shame!” cried Mrs.", "“어머, 저런 망측한 소리를!”"], ["Norris. “A prison indeed?", "노리스 부인이 외쳤다."], ["Sotherton Court is the noblest old place in the world.”", "“감옥이라니요? 소더턴 코트야말로 세상에서 가장 고귀한 옛 저택인데.”"]], [["“It wants improvement, ma’am, beyond anything.", "“손볼 데가 이만저만이 아니에요, 부인."], ["I never saw a place that wanted so much improvement in my life; and it is so forlorn that I do not know what can be done with it.”", "저는 그토록 손볼 데가 많은 곳을 여태 본 적이 없어요. 게다가 어찌나 스산한지 도무지 어찌해야 좋을지 모르겠어요.”"]], [["“No wonder that Mr. Rushworth should think so at present,”", "“러시워스 씨가 지금 그리 여기시는 것도 무리는 아니지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Grant to Mrs.", "그랜트 부인이 노리스 부인에게 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["Norris, with a smile; “but depend upon it, Sotherton will have every improvement in time which his heart can desire.”", "“하지만 두고 보세요, 소더턴은 머잖아 그분 마음이 바라는 온갖 손질을 다 받게 될 테니까요.”"]], [["“I must try to do something with it,”", "“저도 뭐라도 좀 해 봐야지요.” 러시워스 씨가 말했다."], ["said Mr. Rushworth, “but I do not know what.", "“그런데 뭘 해야 할지 모르겠어요."], ["I hope I shall have some good friend to help me.”", "저를 도와줄 좋은 벗이 하나 있으면 좋겠는데.”"]], [["“Your best friend upon such an occasion,”", "“그런 일에 가장 좋은 벗이라면,”"], ["said Miss Bertram calmly, “would be Mr.", "버트럼 양이 차분히 말했다."], ["Repton, I imagine.”", "“아마 렙턴 씨겠지요.”"]], [["“That is what I was thinking of.", "“저도 바로 그 생각을 하고 있었어요."], ["As he has done so well by Smith, I think I had better have him at once.", "스미스네를 그토록 잘 해 놓았으니, 아무래도 그 사람을 당장 부르는 게 낫겠어요."], ["His terms are five guineas a day.”", "그 사람 삯은 하루 오 기니랍니다.”"]], [["“Well, and if they were ten,” cried Mrs.", "“아니, 설령 열 기니라 한들,”"], ["Norris, “I am sure you need not regard it.", "노리스 부인이 외쳤다. “댁이야 그런 걸 개의할 까닭이 조금도 없지요."], ["The expense need not be any impediment.", "비용이 걸림돌이 될 일은 없어요."], ["If I were you, I should not think of the expense.", "나 같으면 비용 따위는 생각도 안 하겠어요."], ["I would have everything done in the best style, and made as nice as possible.", "뭐든 가장 훌륭한 격조로, 될 수 있는 한 근사하게 해 놓을 거예요."], ["Such a place as Sotherton Court deserves everything that taste and money can do.", "소더턴 코트만 한 곳이라면 안목과 돈이 해 줄 수 있는 건 뭐든 누릴 만하지요."], ["You have space to work upon there, and grounds that will well reward you.", "거기엔 손댈 여지가 넉넉하고, 애쓴 보람을 톡톡히 돌려줄 땅이 있으니까요."], ["For my own part, if I had anything within the fiftieth part of the size of Sotherton, I should be always planting and improving, for naturally I am excessively fond of it.", "나로 말하면, 만약 소더턴의 오십분의 일만 한 땅이라도 있었다면, 나는 늘 나무를 심고 가꾸며 지냈을 거예요. 워낙 내가 그런 걸 몹시 좋아하는 사람이라서요."], ["It would be too ridiculous for me to attempt anything where I am now, with my little half acre.", "지금 내가 있는 이 자잘한 반 에이커 땅에서 뭘 해 보겠다고 나서는 건 너무 우스운 일이지요."], ["It would be quite a burlesque.", "그야말로 익살극이 될 거예요."], ["But if I had more room, I should take a prodigious delight in improving and planting.", "하지만 자리가 더 넓었다면, 나는 가꾸고 심는 데서 엄청난 즐거움을 누렸을 거예요."], ["We did a vast deal in that way at the Parsonage: we made it quite a different place from what it was when we first had it.", "우리도 목사관에서 그런 일을 어마어마하게 했답니다. 처음 그 집을 맡았을 때와는 아주 딴판인 곳으로 만들어 놓았지요."], ["You young ones do not remember much about it, perhaps; but if dear Sir Thomas were here, he could tell you what improvements we made: and a great deal more would have been done, but for poor Mr.", "젊은 사람들이야 그 시절 일을 잘 기억 못 하겠지만, 만약 사랑하는 토머스 경이 여기 계셨다면 우리가 어떤 손질을 했는지 다 일러 주셨을 거예요. 게다가 훨씬 더 많은 일을 했을 텐데, 딱한 노리스 목사의 병약한 몸 탓에 그러질 못했지요."], ["Norris’s sad state of health. He could hardly ever get out, poor man, to enjoy anything, and that disheartened me from doing several things that Sir Thomas and I used to talk of.", "그 딱한 양반은 좀처럼 밖에 나가 뭘 즐길 수조차 없었고, 바로 그 때문에 나도 토머스 경과 늘 이야기하던 여러 일을 할 마음이 꺾이고 말았어요."], ["If it had not been for that, we should have carried on the garden wall, and made the plantation to shut out the churchyard, just as Dr. Grant has done.", "만약 그것만 아니었다면, 우리는 정원 담을 죽 이어 쌓고, 그랜트 박사가 해 놓은 것처럼 교회 묘지를 가리는 나무숲을 만들었을 거예요."], ["We were always doing something as it was.", "그래도 우리는 늘 뭔가를 하고 있었지요."], ["It was only the spring twelvemonth before Mr. Norris’s death that we put in the apricot against the stable wall, which is now grown such a noble tree, and getting to such perfection, sir,”", "노리스 목사가 세상을 뜨기 꼭 한 해 전 봄에야 마구간 담에 살구나무를 심었는데, 그게 지금은 그토록 훌륭한 나무로 자라 그리도 탐스럽게 여물었답니다, 박사님.”"], ["addressing herself then to Dr. Grant.", "그러면서 그녀는 그랜트 박사에게로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“The tree thrives well, beyond a doubt, madam,”", "“그 나무가 잘 자라는 건 두말할 것 없지요, 부인.”"], ["replied Dr. Grant.", "그랜트 박사가 대답했다."], ["“The soil is good; and I never pass it without regretting that the fruit should be so little worth the trouble of gathering.”", "“흙이 좋으니까요. 그리고 저는 그 앞을 지날 때마다, 그 열매가 따는 수고에 견줘 그토록 값어치가 없다는 게 안타깝답니다.”"]], [["“Sir, it is a Moor Park, we bought it as a Moor Park, and it cost us—that is, it was a present from Sir Thomas, but I saw the bill—and I know it cost seven shillings, and was charged as a Moor Park.”", "“박사님, 그건 무어 파크 살구예요. 우리가 무어 파크로 사들였고, 값도—아니, 그건 토머스 경이 선물로 주신 거지만, 나는 계산서를 봤어요—분명 일곱 실링을 치렀고, 무어 파크로 값이 매겨져 있었다니까요.”"]], [["“You were imposed on, ma’am,” replied Dr.", "“속아 사신 겁니다, 부인.”"], ["Grant: “these potatoes have as much the flavour of a Moor Park apricot as the fruit from that tree.", "그랜트 박사가 대답했다. “저 감자들이 그 나무 열매만큼이나 무어 파크 살구 맛을 낼걸요."], ["It is an insipid fruit at the best; but a good apricot is eatable, which none from my garden are.”", "그건 아무리 잘 익어도 밍밍한 열매예요. 하지만 좋은 살구라면 먹을 만은 하지요—우리 정원 것은 하나도 그렇지가 못하지만요.”"]], [["“The truth is, ma’am,”", "“실은 말이에요, 부인,”"], ["said Mrs. Grant, pretending to whisper across the table to Mrs.", "그랜트 부인이 식탁 너머 노리스 부인에게 소곤대는 시늉을 하며 말했다."], ["Norris, “that Dr.", "“그랜트 박사는 우리 살구의 본디 맛이 어떤지 잘 모른답니다."], ["Grant hardly knows what the natural taste of our apricot is: he is scarcely ever indulged with one, for it is so valuable a fruit; with a little assistance, and ours is such a remarkably large, fair sort, that what with early tarts and preserves, my cook contrives to get them all.”", "워낙 귀한 열매라 그이한테는 좀처럼 하나도 안 돌아가거든요. 손을 좀 보태면, 게다가 우리 것은 유난히 크고 때깔 고운 품종이라, 이르게 만드는 파이며 설탕절임에 우리 요리사가 그걸 죄다 차지해 버리고 만답니다.”"]], [["Mrs. Norris, who had begun to redden, was appeased; and, for a little while, other subjects took place of the improvements of Sotherton.", "얼굴이 붉으락해지기 시작하던 노리스 부인은 이내 누그러졌다."], ["Dr. Grant and Mrs.", "그리고 한동안은 소더턴 손질 이야기 대신 다른 화제가 자리를 차지했다."], ["Norris were seldom good friends; their acquaintance had begun in dilapidations, and their habits were totally dissimilar.", "그랜트 박사와 노리스 부인은 좀처럼 사이좋은 벗이 못 되었다. 그 둘의 사귐은 애초에 파손 배상 다툼으로 시작된 데다, 습성마저 판이했으니 말이다."]], [["After a short interruption Mr. Rushworth began again.", "잠깐 이야기가 끊긴 뒤 러시워스 씨가 다시 입을 열었다."], ["“Smith’s place is the admiration of all the country; and it was a mere nothing before Repton took it in hand.", "“스미스네 땅은 온 고장의 감탄거리예요. 그런데 렙턴이 손대기 전에는 그야말로 아무것도 아니었어요."], ["I think I shall have Repton.”", "아무래도 저도 렙턴을 부를까 봐요.”"]], [["“Mr. Rushworth,”", "“러시워스 씨,”"], ["said Lady Bertram, “if I were you, I would have a very pretty shrubbery.", "버트럼 부인이 말했다. “나 같으면 아주 예쁜 관목숲을 하나 두겠어요."], ["One likes to get out into a shrubbery in fine weather.”", "날씨 좋을 때 관목숲으로 나가 거니는 게 참 좋거든요.”"]], [["Mr.", "러시워스 씨는 부인의 뜻에 따르겠노라 얼른 다짐하며 무언가 알랑거리는 말을 지어내려 애썼다."], ["Rushworth was eager to assure her ladyship of his acquiescence, and tried to make out something complimentary; but, between his submission to her taste, and his having always intended the same himself, with the superadded objects of professing attention to the comfort of ladies in general, and of insinuating that there was one only whom he was anxious to please, he grew puzzled, and Edmund was glad to put an end to his speech by a proposal of wine.", "그러나 그녀의 취향에 순순히 따르겠다는 마음과, 실은 저도 애초에 똑같이 작정하고 있었다는 마음, 게다가 여인네들 전반의 안락에 마음 쓰는 척하려는 속셈과, 정작 저가 기쁘게 해 드리고 싶은 이는 오직 한 사람뿐임을 은근히 내비치려는 속셈까지 겹쳐, 그는 그만 갈피를 잃고 말았다. 에드먼드는 포도주를 권하며 그의 말을 끊어 주게 되어 다행스러웠다."], ["Mr. Rushworth, however, though not usually a great talker, had still more to say on the subject next his heart.", "그렇지만 러시워스 씨는 여느 때 말수가 많은 편은 아니었어도, 제 가슴에 가장 가까운 이 화제에 대해서만은 아직도 할 말이 남아 있었다."], ["“Smith has not much above a hundred acres altogether in his grounds, which is little enough, and makes it more surprising that the place can have been so improved.", "“스미스네 땅은 죄다 합쳐도 백 에이커 남짓밖에 안 돼요. 그거야 얼마 안 되는 거지요."], ["Now, at Sotherton we have a good seven hundred, without reckoning the water meadows; so that I think, if so much could be done at Compton, we need not despair.", "그러니 그런 땅이 그토록 잘 꾸며졌다는 게 더더욱 놀라운 일이에요. 그런데 우리 소더턴에는 물대는 풀밭은 빼고도 실히 칠백 에이커는 되거든요."], ["There have been two or three fine old trees cut down, that grew too near the house, and it opens the prospect amazingly, which makes me think that Repton, or anybody of that sort, would certainly have the avenue at Sotherton down: the avenue that leads from the west front to the top of the hill, you know,”", "그러니 콤프턴에서 그만큼 해낼 수 있었다면, 우리라고 절망할 것 없다 싶어요. 저택에 너무 가까이 자라던 멋진 고목 두어 그루를 베어 냈더니 전망이 놀랍도록 트이더군요."], ["turning to Miss Bertram particularly as he spoke.", "그걸 보고 저는, 렙턴이든 그런 부류의 누구든 소더턴의 그 가로수길도 틀림없이 베어 버리라고 하겠구나 싶었어요. 서쪽 정면에서 언덕 꼭대기까지 이어지는 그 가로수길 말이에요.”"], ["But Miss Bertram thought it most becoming to reply—", "이 말을 하며 그는 유독 버트럼 양 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다. 그러나 버트럼 양은 이렇게 대답하는 것이 가장 어울린다고 여겼다—"]], [["“The avenue! Oh!", "“그 가로수길이요!"], ["I do not recollect it.", "어머! 저는 기억이 안 나는데요."], ["I really know very little of Sotherton.”", "저는 정말이지 소더턴을 잘 몰라요.”"]], [["Fanny, who was sitting on the other side of Edmund, exactly opposite Miss Crawford, and who had been attentively listening, now looked at him, and said in a low voice—", "에드먼드의 맞은편, 크로퍼드 양의 바로 정면에 앉아 줄곧 귀 기울여 듣고 있던 패니가, 이제 그를 바라보며 나직한 목소리로 말했다—"]], [["“Cut down an avenue!", "“가로수길을 베어 낸다니!"], ["What a pity!", "어쩜 그리 안타까울까!"], ["Does it not make you think of Cowper?", "그러니 쿠퍼가 떠오르지 않아요?"], ["‘Ye fallen avenues, once more I mourn your fate unmerited.’”", "‘쓰러진 가로수길들이여, 내 다시 그대들의 부당한 운명을 애도하노라.’”"]], [["He smiled as he answered, “I am afraid the avenue stands a bad chance, Fanny.”", "그가 미소 지으며 대답했다. “안됐지만 그 가로수길은 가망이 없어 보이는구나, 패니.”"]], [["“I should like to see Sotherton before it is cut down, to see the place as it is now, in its old state; but I do not suppose I shall.”", "“저는 그 길이 베어지기 전에 소더턴을 한번 보고 싶어요. 지금 그대로, 옛 모습 그대로 있는 그곳을요. 하지만 아마 못 보겠지요.”"]], [["“Have you never been there?", "“거기 한 번도 가 본 적이 없지?"], ["No, you never can; and, unluckily, it is out of distance for a ride.", "그래, 갈 수가 없겠구나. 하필이면 말을 타고 다녀오기엔 너무 멀거든."], ["I wish we could contrive it.”", "어떻게든 마련해 볼 수 있으면 좋으련만.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["it does not signify.", "괜찮아요."], ["Whenever I do see it, you will tell me how it has been altered.”", "언제든 제가 그곳을 보게 되면, 오빠가 어떻게 달라졌는지 말해 주실 거잖아요.”"]], [["“I collect,”", "“제가 알기로,”"], ["said Miss Crawford, “that Sotherton is an old place, and a place of some grandeur.", "크로퍼드 양이 말했다. “소더턴은 오래된 곳이고, 얼마간 웅장한 데가 있는 곳이지요."], ["In any particular style of building?”", "어떤 특정한 양식으로 지은 건가요?”"]], [["“The house was built in Elizabeth’s time, and is a large, regular, brick building; heavy, but respectable looking, and has many good rooms.", "“그 저택은 엘리자베스 시대에 지어진, 크고 반듯한 벽돌 건물입니다. 육중하지만 기품 있어 보이고, 좋은 방이 많지요."], ["It is ill placed.", "자리가 안 좋아요."], ["It stands in one of the lowest spots of the park; in that respect, unfavourable for improvement.", "파크에서 가장 낮은 곳 가운데 하나에 서 있거든요. 그 점에서는 손보기에 불리하지요."], ["But the woods are fine, and there is a stream, which, I dare say, might be made a good deal of. Mr.", "하지만 숲이 훌륭하고, 개울도 하나 있는데, 감히 말하건대 그걸 잘만 살리면 크게 쓸모가 있을 겁니다."], ["Rushworth is quite right, I think, in meaning to give it a modern dress, and I have no doubt that it will be all done extremely well.”", "러시워스 씨가 그곳을 현대풍으로 꾸미려는 것은 아주 옳은 생각이라고 봅니다. 그리고 그 모든 게 더없이 훌륭하게 될 거라고 저는 믿어 의심치 않아요.”"]], [["Miss Crawford listened with submission, and said to herself, “He is a well-bred man; he makes the best of it.”", "크로퍼드 양은 고분고분 귀 기울여 듣고는, 속으로 이렇게 뇌었다. “예의 바른 사람이야. 좋은 쪽으로 잘 둘러대는군.”"]], [["“I do not wish to influence Mr. Rushworth,”", "“저는 러시워스 씨를 좌우할 마음은 없습니다.”"], ["he continued; “but, had I a place to new fashion, I should not put myself into the hands of an improver.", "그가 말을 이었다. “하지만 만약 저한테 새로 꾸밀 땅이 있다면, 저 자신을 조경가의 손에 맡기지는 않겠어요."], ["I would rather have an inferior degree of beauty, of my own choice, and acquired progressively.", "차라리 좀 못한 아름다움일망정 제가 스스로 골라, 조금씩 손수 이뤄 가는 편을 택하겠습니다."], ["I would rather abide by my own blunders than by his.”", "남의 실수를 견디느니 차라리 제 실수를 견디는 편이 나으니까요.”"]], [["“You would know what you were about, of course; but that would not suit me.", "“댁이야 물론 뭘 하는지 훤히 아시겠지요. 하지만 저한테는 그게 안 맞아요."], ["I have no eye or ingenuity for such matters, but as they are before me; and had I a place of my own in the country, I should be most thankful to any Mr.", "저는 그런 일에 눈썰미도 재간도 없어서, 그저 눈앞에 다 차려진 것만 볼 줄 알거든요."], ["Repton who would undertake it, and give me as much beauty as he could for my money; and I should never look at it till it was complete.”", "만약 저한테 시골에 제 땅이 있다면, 저는 그걸 맡아 줄 렙턴 씨 같은 사람이라면 누구에게든 더없이 고마워하며, 제 돈으로 뽑아낼 수 있는 만큼의 아름다움을 다 얻어 낼 거예요. 그리고 그게 다 완성되기 전에는 거들떠보지도 않을 거고요.”"]], [["“It would be delightful to me to see the progress of it all,”", "“저라면 그 모든 게 되어 가는 과정을 지켜보는 게 즐거울 텐데요.”"], ["said Fanny.", "패니가 말했다."]], [["“Ay, you have been brought up to it.", "“아, 아가씬 그렇게 자라 왔으니까요. 그건 제 교육에는 들어 있지 않던 거예요."], ["It was no part of my education; and the only dose I ever had, being administered by not the first favourite in the world, has made me consider improvements in hand as the greatest of nuisances.", "게다가 제가 여태 받아 본 단 한 번의 처방이란 것도, 세상에서 그리 좋아하는 사람도 아닌 이의 손에서 내려진 것이라, 저는 한창 손보는 중인 땅을 세상 무엇보다 성가신 것으로 여기게 되었답니다. 삼 년 전에 제 존경하는 삼촌인 제독께서 우리 온 식구가 여름을 나라고 트위크넘에 별장을 하나 사셨어요."], ["Three years ago the Admiral, my honoured uncle, bought a cottage at Twickenham for us all to spend our summers in; and my aunt and I went down to it quite in raptures; but it being excessively pretty, it was soon found necessary to be improved, and for three months we were all dirt and confusion, without a gravel walk to step on, or a bench fit for use.", "이모와 저는 아주 신이 나서 내려갔지요. 그런데 그 집이 하도 예쁘다 보니, 이내 손을 봐야겠다는 얘기가 나왔고, 석 달 동안 우리는 온통 흙먼지와 어수선함 속에서, 밟고 다닐 자갈길 하나, 앉을 만한 벤치 하나 없이 지냈답니다."], ["I would have everything as complete as possible in the country, shrubberies and flower-gardens, and rustic seats innumerable: but it must all be done without my care.", "저도 시골집이라면 관목숲이며 화원이며 소박한 걸상까지 이루 셀 수 없이, 뭐든 될 수 있는 한 완벽하게 갖추고 싶어요. 다만 그 모든 게 제 손을 번거롭게 하지 않고 되어야 한다는 거지요."], ["Henry is different; he loves to be doing.”", "헨리는 달라요. 그 앤 손수 하는 걸 좋아하거든요.”"]], [["Edmund was sorry to hear Miss Crawford, whom he was much disposed to admire, speak so freely of her uncle.", "에드먼드는 자기가 무척 우러러보고 싶은 크로퍼드 양이 제 삼촌을 그토록 스스럼없이 입에 올리는 것을 듣고 안타까웠다."], ["It did not suit his sense of propriety, and he was silenced, till induced by further smiles and liveliness to put the matter by for the present.", "그것은 그의 예의 관념에 맞지 않았고, 그는 입을 다물고 말았다. 그러다 그녀의 잇따른 미소와 발랄함에 이끌려, 그 일은 일단 접어 두게 되었다."]], [["“Mr. Bertram,”", "“버트럼 씨,”"], ["said she, “I have tidings of my harp at last.", "그녀가 말했다. “드디어 제 하프 소식을 들었어요."], ["I am assured that it is safe at Northampton; and there it has probably been these ten days, in spite of the solemn assurances we have so often received to the contrary.”", "노샘프턴에 무사히 있다지 뭐예요. 그동안 그렇지 않다는 엄숙한 장담을 그토록 여러 번 받았는데도, 아마 열흘째 거기 있었나 봐요.”"], ["Edmund expressed his pleasure and surprise.", "에드먼드는 기쁘고도 놀랍다고 했다. “실은 우리가 너무 대놓고 수소문한 탓이었어요."], ["“The truth is, that our inquiries were too direct; we sent a servant, we went ourselves: this will not do seventy miles from London; but this morning we heard of it in the right way.", "하인을 보내기도 하고, 우리가 몸소 나서기도 했으니까요. 런던에서 칠십 마일이나 떨어진 데서는 그런 식으론 안 되는 거였어요."], ["It was seen by some farmer, and he told the miller, and the miller told the butcher, and the butcher’s son-in-law left word at the shop.”", "그런데 오늘 아침에야 제대로 된 경로로 그 소식을 들었지 뭐예요. 어떤 농부가 그걸 봤는데, 그가 방앗간 주인한테 말하고, 방앗간 주인이 푸주한한테 말하고, 푸주한의 사위가 가게에 말을 남긴 거예요.”"]], [["“I am very glad that you have heard of it, by whatever means, and hope there will be no further delay.”", "“무슨 수로든 소식을 들으셨다니 정말 다행이에요. 이제 더는 지체되지 않기를 바랍니다.”"]], [["“I am to have it to-morrow; but how do you think it is to be conveyed?", "“내일이면 받게 돼요. 그런데 그게 어떻게 옮겨질지 아세요?"], ["Not by a wagon or cart: oh no!", "짐수레나 달구지로가 아니에요."], ["nothing of that kind could be hired in the village.", "아, 천만에요! 이 마을에서는 그런 건 하나도 못 빌리거든요."], ["I might as well have asked for porters and a handbarrow.”", "차라리 짐꾼과 손수레를 구해 달라 하는 게 나았을 거예요.”"]], [["“You would find it difficult, I dare say, just now, in the middle of a very late hay harvest, to hire a horse and cart?”", "“지금이야 아마 어려우실 겁니다. 한창 늦은 건초 수확철이라, 말과 달구지를 빌리기가 말이죠?”"]], [["“I was astonished to find what a piece of work was made of it!", "“그게 얼마나 큰일인지 알고는 어찌나 기가 막히던지요!"], ["To want a horse and cart in the country seemed impossible, so I told my maid to speak for one directly; and as I cannot look out of my dressing-closet without seeing one farmyard, nor walk in the shrubbery without passing another, I thought it would be only ask and have, and was rather grieved that I could not give the advantage to all.", "시골에서 말과 달구지가 없다는 게 도무지 있을 수 없는 일 같아서, 저는 하녀더러 당장 하나 부탁해 보라고 일렀어요. 제 화장방에서는 어느 쪽을 내다봐도 농가 마당 하나가 보이고, 관목숲을 거닐어도 또 다른 마당 하나를 지나치게 되니, 그저 청하기만 하면 곧 얻으려니 여겼거든요."], ["Guess my surprise, when I found that I had been asking the most unreasonable, most impossible thing in the world; had offended all the farmers, all the labourers, all the hay in the parish!", "오히려 모두에게 그 혜택을 나눠 주지 못하는 게 좀 서운할 지경이었지요. 그런데 알고 보니 제가 세상에서 가장 얼토당토않고 가장 될 법도 없는 걸 청하고 있었다는 걸 알았을 때, 제가 얼마나 놀랐을지 짐작해 보세요."], ["As for Dr.", "온 동네 농부들이며 일꾼들이며, 교구 안의 건초란 건초는 죄다 화나게 하고 만 거예요!"], ["Grant’s bailiff, I believe I had better keep out of his way; and my brother-in-law himself, who is all kindness in general, looked rather black upon me when he found what I had been at.”", "그랜트 박사네 집사로 말하면, 아무래도 그 사람 눈에 안 띄게 다니는 게 낫겠어요. 게다가 평소엔 더없이 친절한 제 형부마저, 제가 무슨 짓을 했는지 알고는 저를 꽤나 못마땅한 낯으로 보더라니까요.”"]], [["“You could not be expected to have thought on the subject before; but when you do think of it, you must see the importance of getting in the grass. The hire of a cart at any time might not be so easy as you suppose: our farmers are not in the habit of letting them out; but, in harvest, it must be quite out of their power to spare a horse.”", "“미리 그런 데까지 생각하실 수야 없었겠지요. 하지만 막상 생각해 보시면, 풀을 거둬들이는 일이 얼마나 중한지 아실 겁니다. 달구지를 빌리는 건 어느 때고 생각만큼 쉬운 일이 아니에요. 우리 고장 농부들은 그걸 세놓는 버릇이 없거든요. 더구나 수확철이면 말 한 마리를 내주는 건 도무지 그들 형편에 있을 수 없는 일이지요.”"]], [["“I shall understand all your ways in time; but, coming down with the true London maxim, that everything is to be got with money, I was a little embarrassed at first by the sturdy independence of your country customs.", "“차차 이곳 사정을 다 알게 되겠지요. 하지만 무엇이든 돈이면 다 얻는다는 그 진짜배기 런던식 격언을 품고 내려온 저로서는, 처음엔 이곳 시골 관습의 그 억센 자립심에 좀 당황했답니다."], ["However, I am to have my harp fetched to-morrow.", "어쨌거나 내일이면 제 하프를 가져오게 됐어요."], ["Henry, who is good-nature itself, has offered to fetch it in his barouche.", "더없이 착한 천성 그 자체인 헨리가 제 사륜마차로 실어다 주겠다고 나섰거든요."], ["Will it not be honourably conveyed?”", "이만하면 아주 떳떳하게 모셔 오는 셈이지요?”"]], [["Edmund spoke of the harp as his favourite instrument, and hoped to be soon allowed to hear her.", "에드먼드는 하프가 자기가 가장 좋아하는 악기라며, 어서 그녀의 연주를 듣게 되기를 바란다고 했다."], ["Fanny had never heard the harp at all, and wished for it very much.", "패니는 하프 소리를 아예 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없어, 몹시 듣고 싶어 했다."]], [["“I shall be most happy to play to you both,”", "“두 분 다에게 기꺼이 연주해 드릴게요.” 크로퍼드 양이 말했다."], ["said Miss Crawford; “at least as long as you can like to listen: probably much longer, for I dearly love music myself, and where the natural taste is equal the player must always be best off, for she is gratified in more ways than one.", "“적어도 두 분이 듣고 싶어 하시는 동안은요. 아마 그보다 훨씬 오래겠지만요."], ["Now, Mr.", "저 자신이 음악을 끔찍이 사랑하는 데다, 타고난 취향이 서로 맞먹을 땐 연주하는 쪽이 언제나 더 득을 보는 법이니까요. 여러 가지로 흡족하거든요."], ["Bertram, if you write to your brother, I entreat you to tell him that my harp is come: he heard so much of my misery about it.", "참, 버트럼 씨, 혹시 형님께 편지를 쓰시거든, 제발 제 하프가 왔다고 전해 주세요. 그이가 그 일로 제가 애태우는 소리를 그토록 많이 들었으니까요."], ["And you may say, if you please, that I shall prepare my most plaintive airs against his return, in compassion to his feelings, as I know his horse will lose.”", "그리고 괜찮으시면, 제가 그이의 심정을 헤아려, 돌아오실 때에 대비해 가장 구슬픈 가락을 준비해 두겠다고도 전해 주세요. 그이 말이 질 게 뻔하다는 걸 저도 아니까요.”"]], [["“If I write, I will say whatever you wish me; but I do not, at present, foresee any occasion for writing.”", "“편지를 쓰게 되면, 원하시는 건 뭐든 전하지요. 하지만 지금으로선 편지를 쓸 일이 딱히 생길 것 같지가 않군요.”"]], [["“No, I dare say, nor if he were to be gone a twelvemonth, would you ever write to him, nor he to you, if it could be helped.", "“그렇겠지요. 설령 그이가 열두 달을 떠나 있다 한들, 되도록 피할 수만 있다면 형님도 아우도 서로 편지 한 장 안 쓸걸요."], ["The occasion would never be foreseen.", "쓸 일이란 도무지 생기지 않을 테니까요. 오라비란 참 이상한 족속이에요!"], ["What strange creatures brothers are!", "세상에 더없이 다급한 일이 아니고서는 서로 편지 한 장 안 쓰지요."], ["You would not write to each other but upon the most urgent necessity in the world; and when obliged to take up the pen to say that such a horse is ill, or such a relation dead, it is done in the fewest possible words.", "그러다 어쩔 수 없이 붓을 들어야 할 때도—어느 말이 병들었다느니 어느 친척이 죽었다느니 하는 소식조차—되도록 몇 마디 안 되게 적고 마니까요. 오라비들끼리는 문체가 딱 하나뿐이에요."], ["You have but one style among you.", "저는 그걸 훤히 안답니다. 헨리는 다른 모든 점에서는 오라비가 마땅히 그래야 할 그대로예요."], ["I know it perfectly.", "저를 아끼고, 저와 의논하고, 저를 믿고, 몇 시간이고 저와 이야기를 나누지요."], ["Henry, who is in every other respect exactly what a brother should be, who loves me, consults me, confides in me, and will talk to me by the hour together, has never yet turned the page in a letter; and very often it is nothing more than—‘Dear Mary, I am just arrived.", "그런데도 여태 편지에서 한 장을 넘겨 본 적이 없어요. 게다가 그나마도 흔히 이런 게 다예요—‘메리 누나에게."], ["Bath seems full, and everything as usual.", "방금 도착했어. 배스는 붐비는 것 같고, 다 여느 때와 같아."], ["Yours sincerely.’ That is the true manly style; that is a complete brother’s letter.”", "이만 총총.’ 그게 바로 진짜 사내다운 문체예요. 그게 완벽한 오라비의 편지고요.”"]], [["“When they are at a distance from all their family,”", "“식구들과 다 멀리 떨어져 있을 때는,”"], ["said Fanny, colouring for William’s sake, “they can write long letters.”", "윌리엄을 생각해 얼굴을 붉히며 패니가 말했다. “긴 편지를 쓰기도 해요.”"]], [["“Miss Price has a brother at sea,”", "“프라이스 양에게는 바다에 나가 있는 오라비가 하나 있는데,”"], ["said Edmund, “whose excellence as a correspondent makes her think you too severe upon us.”", "에드먼드가 말했다. “그 사람이 편지를 어찌나 잘 쓰는지, 이 애는 크로퍼드 양이 우리를 너무 매몰차게 나무란다고 여긴답니다.”"]], [["“At sea, has she?", "“바다에 나가 있다고요?"], ["In the king’s service, of course?”", "물론 임금님을 섬기는 몸이겠지요?”"]], [["Fanny would rather have had Edmund tell the story, but his determined silence obliged her to relate her brother’s situation: her voice was animated in speaking of his profession, and the foreign stations he had been on; but she could not mention the number of years that he had been absent without tears in her eyes.", "패니는 차라리 에드먼드가 그 이야기를 해 주었으면 했으나, 그가 한사코 입을 다무는 통에 하는 수 없이 제 오라비의 처지를 들려주게 되었다. 그의 직업과, 그가 나가 있던 외국의 여러 주둔지를 말할 때 그녀의 목소리는 생기가 돌았다."], ["Miss Crawford civilly wished him an early promotion.", "그러나 그가 떠나 있은 햇수를 말할 때는 눈에 눈물이 맺히지 않을 수 없었다. 크로퍼드 양은 예의 바르게 그가 어서 진급하기를 빌어 주었다."]], [["“Do you know anything of my cousin’s captain?”", "“제 사촌의 함장에 대해 뭐 아시는 게 있나요?”"], ["said Edmund; “Captain Marshall?", "에드먼드가 말했다. “마셜 대령 말입니다."], ["You have a large acquaintance in the navy, I conclude?”", "크로퍼드 양은 해군에 아는 이가 많으시겠지요?”"]], [["“Among admirals, large enough; but,”", "“제독들 중에는 꽤 많지요. 하지만,”"], ["with an air of grandeur, “we know very little of the inferior ranks.", "그녀가 짐짓 도도한 낯빛으로, “아래 계급 사람들은 저희가 잘 몰라요."], ["Post-captains may be very good sort of men, but they do not belong to us.", "함장 나부랭이야 아주 훌륭한 축일지 몰라도, 저희 부류에는 못 끼거든요."], ["Of various admirals I could tell you a great deal: of them and their flags, and the gradation of their pay, and their bickerings and jealousies.", "여러 제독에 대해서라면 얼마든지 이야기해 드릴 수 있어요. 그분들과 그 기함들, 봉급의 등급, 그리고 그분들끼리의 아웅다웅과 시샘까지요."], ["But, in general, I can assure you that they are all passed over, and all very ill used.", "하지만 대체로 말씀드리자면, 그분들은 다들 진급에서 밀려나 아주 홀대받고 있답니다."], ["Certainly, my home at my uncle’s brought me acquainted with a circle of admirals.", "하기야 삼촌 댁이 제 집이었으니, 저는 자연히 제독들과 두루 알고 지내게 되었지요."], ["Of Rears and Vices I saw enough.", "‘후위 제독’과 ‘부제독’이라면 물리도록 봤답니다."], ["Now do not be suspecting me of a pun, I entreat.”", "자, 제발 제가 무슨 상스러운 말장난이라도 한 걸로 의심하진 말아 주세요.”"]], [["Edmund again felt grave, and only replied, “It is a noble profession.”", "에드먼드는 다시금 정색이 되어, 그저 이렇게만 대꾸했다. “고귀한 직업이지요.”"]], [["“Yes, the profession is well enough under two circumstances: if it make the fortune, and there be discretion in spending it; but, in short, it is not a favourite profession of mine.", "“네, 두 가지 조건만 갖춰지면 그 직업도 그런대로 괜찮아요. 재산을 이루게 해 주고, 그걸 쓰는 데 분별이 있다면 말이지요."], ["It has never worn an amiable form to me.”", "하지만 한마디로, 제가 좋아하는 직업은 못 돼요. 저한테는 한 번도 정다운 모습으로 다가온 적이 없거든요.”"]], [["Edmund reverted to the harp, and was again very happy in the prospect of hearing her play.", "에드먼드는 다시 하프 이야기로 돌아갔고, 그녀의 연주를 들을 생각에 또 한 번 무척 즐거워했다."]], [["The subject of improving grounds, meanwhile, was still under consideration among the others; and Mrs.", "그러는 동안에도 땅을 손질하는 이야기는 다른 이들 사이에서 여전히 오가고 있었다."], ["Grant could not help addressing her brother, though it was calling his attention from Miss Julia Bertram.", "그리고 그랜트 부인은, 비록 그 아우의 관심을 줄리아 버트럼 양에게서 돌려놓는 일이 되긴 했으나, 동생에게 말을 건네지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“My dear Henry, have you nothing to say?", "“얘, 헨리, 너는 할 말이 없니?"], ["You have been an improver yourself, and from what I hear of Everingham, it may vie with any place in England.", "너도 손질을 해 본 사람이잖니. 게다가 내가 에버링엄에 대해 들은 바로는, 그곳이 잉글랜드의 어느 땅과 견주어도 손색없다던데."], ["Its natural beauties, I am sure, are great.", "타고난 아름다움이 대단하다고 나는 확신한다."], ["Everingham, as it used to be, was perfect in my estimation: such a happy fall of ground, and such timber!", "예전의 에버링엄은 내 눈엔 그야말로 완벽했어. 그 흐뭇하게 흘러내리는 지형이며, 그 나무들이라니!"], ["What would I not give to see it again!”", "그곳을 다시 볼 수만 있다면 뭔들 못 내주겠니!”"]], [["“Nothing could be so gratifying to me as to hear your opinion of it,” was his answer; “but I fear there would be some disappointment: you would not find it equal to your present ideas. In extent, it is a mere nothing; you would be surprised at its insignificance; and, as for improvement, there was very little for me to do—too little: I should like to have been busy much longer.”", "“누님의 평을 듣는 것만큼 저를 기쁘게 하는 것도 없겠지요.” 그가 대답했다. “하지만 아마 좀 실망하실 겁니다. 지금 누님이 그리시는 것만 못하실 테니까요. 넓이로 치면 그야말로 아무것도 아니에요. 그 보잘것없음에 놀라실걸요. 그리고 손질로 말하면, 제가 할 일이 아주 조금밖에—너무 적었어요. 저는 훨씬 더 오래 바삐 지냈으면 싶었는데요.”"]], [["“You are fond of the sort of thing?”", "“그런 일을 좋아하나요?”"], ["said Julia.", "줄리아가 말했다."]], [["“Excessively; but what with the natural advantages of the ground, which pointed out, even to a very young eye, what little remained to be done, and my own consequent resolutions, I had not been of age three months before Everingham was all that it is now.", "“아주 좋아하지요. 하지만 땅이 타고난 이점 덕에, 아주 어린 눈에도 이제 손볼 게 얼마 안 남았다는 게 훤히 드러났고, 저도 그에 따라 마음을 굳혔던 터라, 저는 성년이 된 지 석 달도 안 되어 에버링엄을 지금 이 모습 그대로 다 만들어 놓았답니다."], ["My plan was laid at Westminster, a little altered, perhaps, at Cambridge, and at one-and-twenty executed.", "제 구상은 웨스트민스터에서 세워져, 케임브리지에서 조금 손질되었다가, 스물한 살에 실행에 옮겨졌지요."], ["I am inclined to envy Mr. Rushworth for having so much happiness yet before him.", "저는 아직 그토록 많은 즐거움이 앞에 남아 있는 러시워스 씨가 부럽다는 생각마저 들어요."], ["I have been a devourer of my own.”", "저는 제 몫의 즐거움을 벌써 다 게걸스레 먹어 치웠으니까요.”"]], [["“Those who see quickly, will resolve quickly, and act quickly,”", "“빨리 보는 사람은 빨리 마음을 정하고 빨리 행동하는 법이지요.”"], ["said Julia.", "줄리아가 말했다."], ["“You can never want employment. Instead of envying Mr.", "“댁은 결코 할 일이 없어 아쉬울 사람이 아니에요."], ["Rushworth, you should assist him with your opinion.”", "러시워스 씨를 부러워할 게 아니라, 댁의 견해로 그분을 도와드려야지요.”"]], [["Mrs. Grant, hearing the latter part of this speech, enforced it warmly, persuaded that no judgment could be equal to her brother’s; and as Miss Bertram caught at the idea likewise, and gave it her full support, declaring that, in her opinion, it was infinitely better to consult with friends and disinterested advisers, than immediately to throw the business into the hands of a professional man, Mr. Rushworth was very ready to request the favour of Mr. Crawford’s assistance; and Mr. Crawford, after properly depreciating his own abilities, was quite at his service in any way that could be useful. Mr. Rushworth then began to propose Mr. Crawford’s doing him the honour of coming over to Sotherton, and taking a bed there; when Mrs. Norris, as if reading in her two nieces’ minds their little approbation of a plan which was to take Mr. Crawford away, interposed with an amendment.", "이 말의 뒷부분을 들은 그랜트 부인은, 제 아우만 한 판단력이 다시없으리라 믿고 그 말에 힘껏 맞장구를 쳤다. 그리고 버트럼 양도 그 생각을 냉큼 붙들어 전적으로 거들면서, 이런 일은 곧바로 전문가의 손에 내던지기보다 벗들이며 사심 없는 조언자들과 의논하는 편이 한없이 낫다고 제 의견을 밝히자, 러시워스 씨는 크로퍼드 씨의 도움을 청하는 호의를 베풀어 달라고 얼른 부탁했다. 그리고 크로퍼드 씨는 제 재주를 짐짓 겸손하게 깎아내린 뒤, 도움이 될 만한 일이라면 어떤 식으로든 기꺼이 나서겠다고 했다. 이윽고 러시워스 씨가, 크로퍼드 씨더러 소더턴으로 왕림하여 하룻밤 묵어 주는 영광을 베풀어 달라 청하려 하는데, 노리스 부인이 마치 두 조카딸의 마음속에서 크로퍼드 씨를 데려가 버릴 그 계획에 대한 언짢음을 읽어 낸 듯, 수정안을 들고 끼어들었다."]], [["“There can be no doubt of Mr.", "“크로퍼드 씨가 기꺼이 나서 주실 거야 의심할 것 없지요."], ["Crawford’s willingness; but why should not more of us go?", "그런데 우리 중에 몇이 더 못 갈 것도 없잖아요?"], ["Why should not we make a little party?", "다 함께 조촐한 나들이를 하면 어때요?"], ["Here are many that would be interested in your improvements, my dear Mr.", "여기 댁의 손질 이야기에 관심 있어 할 사람이 많잖아요, 우리 러시워스 씨. 그 자리에서 크로퍼드 씨의 견해를 듣고 싶어 할 사람도 많고, 저마다 제 의견으로 댁에게 얼마간 보탬이 될 사람도 많고요."], ["Rushworth, and that would like to hear Mr.", "나로 말하면, 진작부터 댁의 어지신 어머님을 다시 한번 뵈러 가고 싶던 참이었어요."], ["Crawford’s opinion on the spot, and that might be of some small use to you with their opinions; and, for my own part, I have been long wishing to wait upon your good mother again; nothing but having no horses of my own could have made me so remiss; but now I could go and sit a few hours with Mrs.", "내 소유의 말이 없다는 것 말고는 그토록 소홀했을 까닭이 없었지요. 하지만 이제라면 나는 가서 러시워스 부인과 몇 시간 앉아 있고, 그동안 나머지 분들은 두루 거닐며 이런저런 것을 정하면 되겠네요."], ["Rushworth, while the rest of you walked about and settled things, and then we could all return to a late dinner here, or dine at Sotherton, just as might be most agreeable to your mother, and have a pleasant drive home by moonlight. I dare say Mr.", "그런 다음 다 함께 여기로 돌아와 늦은 저녁을 들든지, 아니면 댁의 어머님께 가장 편하실 대로 소더턴에서 저녁을 들고, 달빛 아래 기분 좋게 마차로 돌아오면 되고요."], ["Crawford would take my two nieces and me in his barouche, and Edmund can go on horseback, you know, sister, and Fanny will stay at home with you.”", "아마 크로퍼드 씨가 내 두 조카딸과 나를 그 사륜마차에 태워 주실 테고, 에드먼드는 말을 타고 가면 되지 않겠니, 언니. 그리고 패니는 언니 곁에 남아 집을 지키고요.”"]], [["Lady Bertram made no objection; and every one concerned in the going was forward in expressing their ready concurrence, excepting Edmund, who heard it all and said nothing.", "버트럼 부인은 아무 이의도 달지 않았다. 그리고 그 나들이에 관계된 이들은 모두 앞다투어 선뜻 찬성의 뜻을 밝혔다. 다만 에드먼드만은 그 모든 것을 들으면서도 아무 말이 없었다."]], [["# CHAPTER VII", "# 제7장"]], [["“Well, Fanny, and how do you like Miss Crawford now?”", "“그래, 패니, 이제는 크로퍼드 양이 어떠니?”"], ["said Edmund the next day, after thinking some time on the subject himself.", "이튿날, 그 문제를 한동안 혼자 곰곰이 생각해 본 뒤에 에드먼드가 말했다."], ["“How did you like her yesterday?”", "“어제는 그분이 마음에 들었어?”"]], [["“Very well—very much.", "“아주 좋았어요—무척요."], ["I like to hear her talk.", "저는 그분이 이야기하는 걸 듣는 게 좋아요."], ["She entertains me; and she is so extremely pretty, that I have great pleasure in looking at her.”", "저를 즐겁게 해 주시거든요. 게다가 얼마나 예쁘신지, 바라보는 것만으로도 큰 기쁨이에요.”"]], [["“It is her countenance that is so attractive.", "“그 얼굴이 워낙 매력적이지."], ["She has a wonderful play of feature!", "표정이 어찌나 오묘하게 살아 움직이는지!"], ["But was there nothing in her conversation that struck you, Fanny, as not quite right?”", "그런데 패니, 그분 이야기 중에 뭔가 좀 옳지 않다 싶어 걸리는 데는 없었니?”"]], [["“Oh yes!", "“아, 있었어요!"], ["she ought not to have spoken of her uncle as she did.", "그분은 삼촌에 대해 그렇게 말씀하시면 안 되는 거였어요."], ["I was quite astonished.", "저는 정말 깜짝 놀랐어요."], ["An uncle with whom she has been living so many years, and who, whatever his faults may be, is so very fond of her brother, treating him, they say, quite like a son.", "그토록 여러 해를 함께 살아온 삼촌이고, 흠이야 어떻든 그분 오빠를 그토록 아껴 꼭 아들처럼 대해 준다는 분인데요."], ["I could not have believed it!”", "저는 도저히 믿을 수가 없었어요!”"]], [["“I thought you would be struck.", "“네가 놀랄 줄 알았다."], ["It was very wrong; very indecorous.”", "아주 잘못된 일이었어. 몹시 무례한 일이었지.”"]], [["“And very ungrateful, I think.”", "“게다가 아주 배은망덕하다고, 저는 생각해요.”"]], [["“Ungrateful is a strong word.", "“배은망덕이란 좀 센 말이구나."], ["I do not know that her uncle has any claim to her gratitude; his wife certainly had; and it is the warmth of her respect for her aunt’s memory which misleads her here.", "나는 그 삼촌이 그분한테 고마움을 받을 권리가 있다고는 생각지 않는다. 그 부인이라면 분명 있었지."], ["She is awkwardly circumstanced.", "그리고 이모의 기억을 향한 그 뜨거운 존경심이 여기서 그분을 그릇된 길로 이끄는 거야."], ["With such warm feelings and lively spirits it must be difficult to do justice to her affection for Mrs.", "그분은 처지가 난처해. 그토록 뜨거운 감정과 발랄한 기질을 지녔으니, 제독을 그늘지게 하지 않고서 크로퍼드 부인을 향한 애정을 온전히 표하기란 어려울 거야."], ["Crawford, without throwing a shade on the Admiral.", "그 두 분의 불화에서 누가 더 잘못이었는지 나는 안다고 나서진 않겠다."], ["I do not pretend to know which was most to blame in their disagreements, though the Admiral’s present conduct might incline one to the side of his wife; but it is natural and amiable that Miss Crawford should acquit her aunt entirely.", "물론 제독이 지금 하는 행실을 보면 그 부인 편을 들고 싶어지기도 하지만 말이야. 어쨌든 크로퍼드 양이 이모를 온전히 두둔하는 건 자연스럽고 갸륵한 일이야."], ["I do not censure her opinions; but there certainly is impropriety in making them public.”", "나는 그분의 생각을 나무라는 게 아니다. 다만 그걸 남 앞에 드러내는 데에는 분명 온당치 못한 데가 있어.”"]], [["“Do not you think,”", "“그런데 오빠 생각은 어때요?”"], ["said Fanny, after a little consideration, “that this impropriety is a reflection itself upon Mrs.", "잠깐 생각한 뒤 패니가 말했다. “그렇게 온당치 못한 것 자체가, 그분을 온전히 길러 낸 크로퍼드 부인에게도 흠이 되는 게 아닐까요?"], ["Crawford, as her niece has been entirely brought up by her? She cannot have given her right notions of what was due to the Admiral.”", "그 부인이 그분한테 제독에게 마땅히 해야 할 도리를 제대로 가르쳐 주지 못한 거잖아요.”"]], [["“That is a fair remark.", "“그건 옳은 지적이다."], ["Yes, we must suppose the faults of the niece to have been those of the aunt; and it makes one more sensible of the disadvantages she has been under.", "그래, 조카딸의 흠은 곧 이모의 흠이었다고 봐야겠지. 그리고 그럴수록 그분이 그동안 어떤 불리한 처지에 있었는지 더 헤아리게 되는구나."], ["But I think her present home must do her good. Mrs.", "하지만 나는 지금의 집이 그분한테 좋은 영향을 주리라고 본다."], ["Grant’s manners are just what they ought to be.", "그랜트 부인의 몸가짐은 그야말로 나무랄 데가 없거든."], ["She speaks of her brother with a very pleasing affection.”", "그 부인은 제 아우 이야기를 참 정겨운 애정으로 하지.”"]], [["“Yes, except as to his writing her such short letters.", "“네, 아우가 그토록 짧은 편지를 쓴다는 대목만 빼면요."], ["She made me almost laugh; but I cannot rate so very highly the love or good-nature of a brother who will not give himself the trouble of writing anything worth reading to his sisters, when they are separated.", "그 얘기엔 저도 하마터면 웃을 뻔했어요. 하지만 자매가 떨어져 있을 때 읽을 만한 무언가를 써 보내는 수고조차 마다하는 오라비의 사랑이나 착한 마음을, 저는 그리 높이 쳐줄 수가 없어요."], ["I am sure William would never have used me so, under any circumstances.", "윌리엄이라면 어떤 처지에서도 결코 저한테 그러지 않았을 거예요."], ["And what right had she to suppose that you would not write long letters when you were absent?”", "그리고 오빠가 떨어져 있을 때 긴 편지를 안 쓸 거라고, 그분이 무슨 권리로 넘겨짚는 거예요?”"]], [["“The right of a lively mind, Fanny, seizing whatever may contribute to its own amusement or that of others; perfectly allowable, when untinctured by ill-humour or roughness; and there is not a shadow of either in the countenance or manner of Miss Crawford: nothing sharp, or loud, or coarse.", "“발랄한 사람의 권리지, 패니. 제 흥이나 남의 흥에 보탬이 될 만한 건 뭐든 붙드는 권리 말이다."], ["She is perfectly feminine, except in the instances we have been speaking of.", "심술이나 거친 데만 배지 않았다면 얼마든지 허물할 게 아니야. 그리고 크로퍼드 양의 얼굴이나 몸가짐에는 그런 그림자가 조금도 없어."], ["There she cannot be justified.", "날카롭거나 시끄럽거나 상스러운 데라곤 하나도 없지. 우리가 지금 이야기한 그 몇 가지 경우만 빼면, 그분은 더없이 여자다워."], ["I am glad you saw it all as I did.”", "그 대목만은 두둔할 수가 없지만 말이야. 네가 그걸 나와 똑같이 봐 줘서 기쁘구나.”"]], [["Having formed her mind and gained her affections, he had a good chance of her thinking like him; though at this period, and on this subject, there began now to be some danger of dissimilarity, for he was in a line of admiration of Miss Crawford, which might lead him where Fanny could not follow. Miss Crawford’s attractions did not lessen. The harp arrived, and rather added to her beauty, wit, and good-humour; for she played with the greatest obligingness, with an expression and taste which were peculiarly becoming, and there was something clever to be said at the close of every air. Edmund was at the Parsonage every day, to be indulged with his favourite instrument: one morning secured an invitation for the next; for the lady could not be unwilling to have a listener, and every thing was soon in a fair train.", "그가 그녀의 마음을 빚어 주고 그 애정을 얻어 온 터라, 그녀가 그와 같이 생각하게 될 공산은 컸다. 그렇지만 이 무렵, 이 문제에서만은 이제 어긋남이 생길 위험이 얼마간 싹트기 시작했다. 그가 크로퍼드 양을 우러르는 길로 접어들고 있었는데, 그것은 그를 패니가 따라갈 수 없는 데로 이끌지도 몰랐기 때문이다. 크로퍼드 양의 매력은 줄어들지 않았다. 하프가 도착했고, 오히려 그녀의 아름다움과 재치와 쾌활함을 한층 돋우었다. 그녀가 더없이 상냥하게, 유난히 잘 어울리는 표정과 취향으로 연주했고, 곡이 끝날 때마다 무언가 재치 있는 말이 곁들여졌으니 말이다. 에드먼드는 날마다 목사관에 나가 제가 가장 좋아하는 악기 소리에 흠뻑 젖었다. 하루 아침의 방문이 이튿날의 초대를 확보해 주었다. 그 여인으로서도 들어 줄 이가 있는 것을 마다할 리 없었으니, 이내 만사가 순조로운 흐름을 탔다."]], [["A young woman, pretty, lively, with a harp as elegant as herself, and both placed near a window, cut down to the ground, and opening on a little lawn, surrounded by shrubs in the rich foliage of summer, was enough to catch any man’s heart.", "예쁘고 발랄한 젊은 여자가, 제 몸만큼이나 우아한 하프를 곁에 두고, 그 둘이 바닥까지 내려오게 낸 창가에 자리 잡았는데, 그 창은 여름의 짙푸른 잎으로 우거진 관목에 둘러싸인 자그마한 잔디밭으로 열려 있었으니—이만하면 어느 사내의 마음인들 사로잡기에 넉넉했다. 계절도, 풍경도, 공기도, 모두가 다정함과 감상에 이바지했다."], ["The season, the scene, the air, were all favourable to tenderness and sentiment.", "그랜트 부인과 그 자수틀도 쓸모가 없지 않았다. 모든 것이 조화로웠다."], ["Mrs. Grant and her tambour frame were not without their use: it was all in harmony; and as everything will turn to account when love is once set going, even the sandwich tray, and Dr.", "그리고 사랑이 일단 발동하면 무엇이든 다 밑천이 되는 법이라, 샌드위치 쟁반이며 그것을 대접하느라 분주한 그랜트 박사조차 바라볼 만한 것이 되었다."], ["Grant doing the honours of it, were worth looking at.", "그러나 딱히 그 일을 궁리한 것도, 제가 무엇을 하고 있는지 안 것도 아닌 채로, 에드먼드는 그런 사귐이 이어진 한 주 끝머리에 이르러 자못 깊은 사랑에 빠져들고 있었다. 그리고 그 여인의 명예를 위해 덧붙이자면, 그가 세상 물정에 밝은 사내도 맏이도 아니고, 알랑거리는 재간이나 잡담의 흥겨움 하나 없었는데도, 그는 차츰 그녀의 마음에 들기 시작했다."], ["Without studying the business, however, or knowing what he was about, Edmund was beginning, at the end of a week of such intercourse, to be a good deal in love; and to the credit of the lady it may be added that, without his being a man of the world or an elder brother, without any of the arts of flattery or the gaieties of small talk, he began to be agreeable to her.", "그녀 자신은 그것을 미처 내다보지도 못했고 좀처럼 이해하기도 어려웠으나, 그렇게 느꼈다. 그는 흔한 잣대로 보면 도무지 유쾌한 사람이 아니었으니 말이다."], ["She felt it to be so, though she had not foreseen, and could hardly understand it; for he was not pleasant by any common rule: he talked no nonsense; he paid no compliments; his opinions were unbending, his attentions tranquil and simple.", "그는 실없는 소리를 하지 않았고, 입에 발린 칭찬도 하지 않았으며, 제 소신은 굽힐 줄 몰랐고, 남을 살피는 마음씨는 잔잔하고 소박했다. 아마 그 진실함과 한결같음과 곧음에 어떤 매력이 있었으리라."], ["There was a charm, perhaps, in his sincerity, his steadiness, his integrity, which Miss Crawford might be equal to feel, though not equal to discuss with herself.", "크로퍼드 양은 그것을 느낄 줄은 알아도, 스스로 그것을 헤아려 따져 볼 줄은 몰랐다. 하지만 그녀는 그에 대해 그리 깊이 생각하지 않았다."], ["She did not think very much about it, however: he pleased her for the present; she liked to have him near her; it was enough.", "그는 당장 그녀를 즐겁게 해 주었고, 그녀는 그를 곁에 두는 게 좋았다. 그것으로 충분했다."]], [["Fanny could not wonder that Edmund was at the Parsonage every morning; she would gladly have been there too, might she have gone in uninvited and unnoticed, to hear the harp; neither could she wonder that, when the evening stroll was over, and the two families parted again, he should think it right to attend Mrs.", "에드먼드가 날마다 아침이면 목사관에 가 있는 것을 패니는 이상히 여길 수 없었다. 청하지 않아도, 남의 눈에 띄지 않고 들어가 하프 소리를 들을 수만 있었다면, 그녀 자신도 기꺼이 거기 가 있었을 테니 말이다."], ["Grant and her sister to their home, while Mr.", "또한 저녁 산책이 끝나 두 집안이 다시 헤어질 때, 크로퍼드 씨가 공원 저택의 부인네들을 모시는 동안 그가 그랜트 부인과 그 아우를 집까지 바래다주는 것을 도리로 여기는 것도 이상히 여길 수 없었다. 다만 그녀는 그것이 아주 나쁜 맞바꿈이라고 여겼다."], ["Crawford was devoted to the ladies of the Park; but she thought it a very bad exchange; and if Edmund were not there to mix the wine and water for her, would rather go without it than not.", "그리고 에드먼드가 곁에서 포도주에 물을 타 주지 않는다면, 차라리 그걸 안 마시고 말겠다 싶었다. 그가 크로퍼드 양과 그토록 여러 시간을 함께 보내면서도, 저가 이미 지적한 그런 흠—패니 자신은 그녀와 함께 있을 때면 거의 언제나 그와 비슷한 무언가로 새삼 떠올리게 되는 그 흠—을 더 보지 못한다는 게 그녀는 조금 의아했다."], ["She was a little surprised that he could spend so many hours with Miss Crawford, and not see more of the sort of fault which he had already observed, and of which she was almost always reminded by a something of the same nature whenever she was in her company; but so it was.", "하지만 실상이 그러했다. 에드먼드는 그녀에게 크로퍼드 양 이야기를 즐겨 했으나, 그 뒤로 제독이 험담을 면했다는 것으로 만족하는 눈치였다."], ["Edmund was fond of speaking to her of Miss Crawford, but he seemed to think it enough that the Admiral had since been spared; and she scrupled to point out her own remarks to him, lest it should appear like ill-nature.", "그리고 그녀는 제 소견을 그에게 내비치기를 삼갔다. 그것이 심술처럼 보일까 봐서였다."], ["The first actual pain which Miss Crawford occasioned her was the consequence of an inclination to learn to ride, which the former caught, soon after her being settled at Mansfield, from the example of the young ladies at the Park, and which, when Edmund’s acquaintance with her increased, led to his encouraging the wish, and the offer of his own quiet mare for the purpose of her first attempts, as the best fitted for a beginner that either stable could furnish.", "크로퍼드 양이 그녀에게 처음으로 실제 아픔을 안긴 것은, 그녀가 승마를 배우고 싶어 한 데서 비롯된 일이었다. 크로퍼드 양은 맨스필드에 자리 잡은 지 얼마 안 되어 공원 저택 아가씨들을 본떠 그 마음을 품게 되었는데, 에드먼드와 가까워지자 그가 그 소망을 부추기게 되었고, 초심자에게는 어느 마구간의 어느 말보다 가장 알맞다며, 그녀의 첫 시도를 위해 제 얌전한 암말을 내주기에 이르렀다."], ["No pain, no injury, however, was designed by him to his cousin in this offer: she was not to lose a day’s exercise by it.", "그러나 이 제의에서 그가 사촌에게 아픔이나 손해를 끼칠 뜻은 조금도 없었다. 그녀는 그로 인해 하루의 운동도 잃을 게 없었으니 말이다."], ["The mare was only to be taken down to the Parsonage half an hour before her ride were to begin; and Fanny, on its being first proposed, so far from feeling slighted, was almost over-powered with gratitude that he should be asking her leave for it.", "암말은 그녀의 승마가 시작되기 딱 반 시간 전에만 목사관으로 내려보내기로 되어 있었다. 그리고 패니는 그 제안을 처음 받았을 때, 홀대받았다고 느끼기는커녕, 그가 그 일로 제 허락을 구한다는 것에 고마움이 북받쳐 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다."]], [["Miss Crawford made her first essay with great credit to herself, and no inconvenience to Fanny.", "크로퍼드 양은 첫 시도를 제 체면이 서게 훌륭히 해냈고, 패니에게는 아무 불편도 끼치지 않았다."], ["Edmund, who had taken down the mare and presided at the whole, returned with it in excellent time, before either Fanny or the steady old coachman, who always attended her when she rode without her cousins, were ready to set forward.", "암말을 끌고 내려가 그 모든 것을 주관한 에드먼드는, 패니도, 사촌들 없이 그녀가 말을 탈 때면 늘 곁을 지키던 진중한 늙은 마부도 미처 나설 채비를 갖추기 전에, 넉넉한 시간을 두고 말을 끌고 돌아왔다."], ["The second day’s trial was not so guiltless.", "둘째 날의 시도는 그렇게 죄가 없지는 못했다."], ["Miss Crawford’s enjoyment of riding was such that she did not know how to leave off.", "크로퍼드 양은 승마가 어찌나 즐거운지 그만둘 줄을 몰랐다."], ["Active and fearless, and though rather small, strongly made, she seemed formed for a horsewoman; and to the pure genuine pleasure of the exercise, something was probably added in Edmund’s attendance and instructions, and something more in the conviction of very much surpassing her sex in general by her early progress, to make her unwilling to dismount.", "활달하고 겁이 없는 데다, 몸집은 자그마해도 다부지게 여물어, 그녀는 여자 기수로 타고난 듯했다. 그리고 그 운동 자체의 순수한 즐거움에다, 아마 에드먼드가 곁에서 돌봐 주고 일러 주는 것이 얼마간 보태졌을 테고, 이른 진척으로 여느 제 또래 여자들을 훨씬 앞선다는 확신이 또 얼마간 보태져, 그녀는 말에서 내리기를 꺼렸다."], ["Fanny was ready and waiting, and Mrs. Norris was beginning to scold her for not being gone, and still no horse was announced, no Edmund appeared.", "패니는 채비를 갖추고 기다리고 있었고, 노리스 부인은 어서 가지 않는다고 그녀를 나무라기 시작했건만, 여전히 말이 왔다는 기별도, 에드먼드가 나타날 낌새도 없었다."], ["To avoid her aunt, and look for him, she went out.", "이모를 피하고 그를 찾을 겸, 그녀는 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["The houses, though scarcely half a mile apart, were not within sight of each other; but, by walking fifty yards from the hall door, she could look down the park, and command a view of the Parsonage and all its demesnes, gently rising beyond the village road; and in Dr.", "두 집은 반 마일도 채 떨어져 있지 않았으나 서로 마주 보이지는 않았다. 그래도 현관문에서 오십 야드쯤 걸어 나가면 공원 아래쪽을 굽어보며, 마을 길 너머로 완만히 솟은 목사관과 그 딸린 땅 전부를 한눈에 담을 수 있었다."], ["Grant’s meadow she immediately saw the group—Edmund and Miss Crawford both on horse-back, riding side by side, Dr.", "그리고 그랜트 박사의 풀밭에서 그녀는 이내 그 무리를 보았다—에드먼드와 크로퍼드 양은 둘 다 말을 타고 나란히 달리고 있었고, 그랜트 박사 부부와 크로퍼드 씨는 마부 두어 명과 함께 둘러서서 구경하고 있었다. 그녀 눈에는 다들 한 가지에 마음을 쏟는 흐뭇한 무리로 보였다."], ["and Mrs. Grant, and Mr.", "즐거운 게 분명했다."], ["Crawford, with two or three grooms, standing about and looking on.", "그 흥겨운 웃음소리가 그녀 있는 데까지 올라왔으니 말이다."], ["A happy party it appeared to her, all interested in one object: cheerful beyond a doubt, for the sound of merriment ascended even to her.", "그것은 그녀를 흥겹게 하는 소리가 아니었다. 그녀는 에드먼드가 저를 잊었다는 게 의아했고, 가슴이 저릿했다."], ["It was a sound which did not make her cheerful; she wondered that Edmund should forget her, and felt a pang.", "그녀는 그 풀밭에서 눈을 뗄 수가 없었다. 거기서 벌어지는 모든 것을 지켜보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["She could not turn her eyes from the meadow; she could not help watching all that passed.", "처음에 크로퍼드 양과 그 동행은 작지 않은 그 들판을 걸음걸이로 한 바퀴 돌았다."], ["At first Miss Crawford and her companion made the circuit of the field, which was not small, at a foot’s pace; then, at her apparent suggestion, they rose into a canter; and to Fanny’s timid nature it was most astonishing to see how well she sat.", "이윽고 그녀가 그러자고 청한 듯, 둘은 가벼운 구보로 접어들었다. 그리고 겁 많은 천성의 패니에게는, 그녀가 얼마나 말 위에 잘 앉아 있는지가 더없이 놀라워 보였다."], ["After a few minutes they stopped entirely.", "몇 분 뒤 둘은 완전히 멈춰 섰다. 에드먼드가 그녀 바짝 곁에 있었다."], ["Edmund was close to her; he was speaking to her; he was evidently directing her management of the bridle; he had hold of her hand; she saw it, or the imagination supplied what the eye could not reach.", "그가 그녀에게 말을 건네고 있었다. 그가 고삐 다루는 법을 일러 주고 있는 게 분명했다."], ["She must not wonder at all this; what could be more natural than that Edmund should be making himself useful, and proving his good-nature by any one?", "그가 그녀의 손을 잡고 있었다. 그녀는 그것을 보았다—아니, 눈이 미치지 못하는 것을 상상이 채워 넣었는지도 모른다."], ["She could not but think, indeed, that Mr.", "이 모든 것을 그녀가 이상히 여겨서는 안 될 일이었다."], ["Crawford might as well have saved him the trouble; that it would have been particularly proper and becoming in a brother to have done it himself; but Mr.", "에드먼드가 남에게 쓸모 있는 사람이 되고 그 착한 마음씨를 아무에게나 보여 주는 것보다 더 자연스러운 일이 어디 있겠는가? 그래도 그녀는, 크로퍼드 씨가 그 수고를 대신 덜어 줄 수도 있었을 텐데, 오라비 된 사람이 몸소 그리하는 것이 유독 온당하고 어울렸을 텐데 하는 생각이 들지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Crawford, with all his boasted good-nature, and all his coachmanship, probably knew nothing of the matter, and had no active kindness in comparison of Edmund.", "하지만 크로퍼드 씨는 그토록 자랑하는 착한 마음씨에도, 그 온갖 마차 모는 솜씨에도, 아마 그 일에 대해서는 아무것도 모를뿐더러, 에드먼드에 견주면 몸소 나서는 친절이라곤 없는 사람이었다."], ["She began to think it rather hard upon the mare to have such double duty; if she were forgotten, the poor mare should be remembered.", "그녀는 그런 이중의 노역을 지는 암말이 좀 딱하다는 생각마저 들기 시작했다. 저는 잊혔을망정, 그 딱한 암말만은 누가 좀 기억해 줘야 마땅했다."]], [["Her feelings for one and the other were soon a little tranquillised by seeing the party in the meadow disperse, and Miss Crawford still on horseback, but attended by Edmund on foot, pass through a gate into the lane, and so into the park, and make towards the spot where she stood.", "이쪽저쪽을 향한 그녀의 마음은, 풀밭의 무리가 흩어지고, 크로퍼드 양은 여전히 말을 탄 채였으나 에드먼드가 걸어서 곁을 따르며, 문을 지나 좁은 길로, 그리고 공원 안으로 들어서서 그녀가 서 있는 자리 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 보자, 이내 얼마간 가라앉았다."], ["She began then to be afraid of appearing rude and impatient; and walked to meet them with a great anxiety to avoid the suspicion.", "그러자 이번엔 제가 무례하고 안달하는 것처럼 비칠까 봐 겁이 났다. 그래서 그런 의심을 사지 않으려 잔뜩 마음을 졸이며 그들을 맞으러 걸어갔다."]], [["“My dear Miss Price,” said Miss Crawford, as soon as she was at all within hearing, “I am come to make my own apologies for keeping you waiting; but I have nothing in the world to say for myself—I knew it was very late, and that I was behaving extremely ill; and therefore, if you please, you must forgive me. Selfishness must always be forgiven, you know, because there is no hope of a cure.”", "“프라이스 양,” 목소리가 겨우 닿을 만한 데 이르자마자 크로퍼드 양이 말했다. “기다리게 해 드린 데 대해 제 입으로 사과하러 왔어요. 하지만 저로선 세상에 변명할 말이 하나도 없네요—너무 늦은 줄도, 제가 아주 못되게 굴고 있다는 것도 다 알고 있었으니까요. 그러니 부디 저를 용서해 주세요. 이기심이란 늘 용서받아야 하는 법이잖아요. 고칠 가망이 없는 병이니까요.”"]], [["Fanny’s answer was extremely civil, and Edmund added his conviction that she could be in no hurry.", "패니의 대답은 더없이 정중했고, 에드먼드도 그녀가 급할 리 없다고 거들었다."], ["“For there is more than time enough for my cousin to ride twice as far as she ever goes,”", "“제 사촌이 여느 때 다니는 것보다 두 배는 멀리 타고도 남을 만큼 시간이 넉넉하니까요.” 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “and you have been promoting her comfort by preventing her from setting off half an hour sooner: clouds are now coming up, and she will not suffer from the heat as she would have done then.", "“게다가 크로퍼드 양은 이 애가 반 시간 일찍 나서는 걸 막아 준 셈이니, 오히려 이 애의 편안함을 도운 거예요. 이제 구름이 몰려오고 있으니, 그때 나섰다면 겪었을 더위에 시달릴 일이 없을 테니까요."], ["I wish you may not be fatigued by so much exercise.", "부디 크로퍼드 양께서나 이렇게 많이 운동하시고 지치지 않으시면 좋겠는데요."], ["I wish you had saved yourself this walk home.”", "이 집까지 걸어오시는 수고는 안 하셨어도 좋았을 텐데요.”"]], [["“No part of it fatigues me but getting off this horse, I assure you,”", "“그중에 저를 지치게 하는 건 이 말에서 내리는 것뿐이랍니다, 정말이에요.” 그의 도움을 받아 훌쩍 내려서며 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, as she sprang down with his help; “I am very strong.", "“저는 아주 튼튼해요. 저를 지치게 하는 건 오직 하기 싫은 일을 하는 것뿐이거든요."], ["Nothing ever fatigues me but doing what I do not like.", "프라이스 양, 저는 아주 볼썽사나운 꼴로 양보하네요."], ["Miss Price, I give way to you with a very bad grace; but I sincerely hope you will have a pleasant ride, and that I may have nothing but good to hear of this dear, delightful, beautiful animal.”", "하지만 진심으로 즐거운 승마 되시길 빌어요. 그리고 이 사랑스럽고 유쾌하고 아름다운 짐승에 대해서는 좋은 소식만 듣게 되기를요.”"]], [["The old coachman, who had been waiting about with his own horse, now joining them, Fanny was lifted on hers, and they set off across another part of the park; her feelings of discomfort not lightened by seeing, as she looked back, that the others were walking down the hill together to the village; nor did her attendant do her much good by his comments on Miss Crawford’s great cleverness as a horse-woman, which he had been watching with an interest almost equal to her own.", "제 말을 데리고 근처에서 기다리던 늙은 마부가 이제 그들과 합류하자, 패니는 제 말에 올려졌고, 그들은 공원의 다른 쪽으로 길을 나섰다. 뒤돌아보니 나머지 사람들이 마을 쪽으로 함께 언덕을 내려가고 있는 것이 보여, 그녀의 언짢은 마음은 좀처럼 가벼워지지 않았다. 게다가 곁을 따르던 마부도, 제 못지않은 관심으로 지켜본 크로퍼드 양의 뛰어난 승마 솜씨를 두고 이런저런 말을 늘어놓아, 그녀에게 별 도움이 되지 못했다."]], [["“It is a pleasure to see a lady with such a good heart for riding!”", "“저렇게 승마에 마음이 잘 붙는 아가씨를 뵈니 참 흐뭇합니다!” 그가 말했다."], ["said he. “I never see one sit a horse better.", "“저는 저보다 말을 잘 타는 분을 본 적이 없어요."], ["She did not seem to have a thought of fear.", "두려움이라곤 티끌만큼도 없어 보이더군요."], ["Very different from you, miss, when you first began, six years ago come next Easter.", "여섯 해 전, 다가오는 부활절이면 꼭 여섯 해가 되는데, 아가씨가 처음 배우실 때하곤 아주 딴판이었지요."], ["Lord bless you!", "원, 저런!"], ["how you did tremble when Sir Thomas first had you put on!”", "토머스 경께서 처음 아가씨를 말에 태우셨을 때, 얼마나 벌벌 떠셨는지요!”"]], [["In the drawing-room Miss Crawford was also celebrated.", "응접실에서도 크로퍼드 양은 마찬가지로 찬사를 받았다."], ["Her merit in being gifted by Nature with strength and courage was fully appreciated by the Miss Bertrams; her delight in riding was like their own; her early excellence in it was like their own, and they had great pleasure in praising it.", "타고나기를 힘과 용기를 갖추었다는 그 장점을 버트럼가 아가씨들은 한껏 알아주었다. 승마를 즐기는 그 마음은 저희 것과 같았고, 일찌감치 능숙해진 것도 저희와 같았으니, 그들은 그것을 칭찬하는 데서 큰 즐거움을 느꼈다."]], [["“I was sure she would ride well,”", "“그분이 말을 잘 탈 줄 알았어.”"], ["said Julia; “she has the make for it.", "줄리아가 말했다. “그럴 몸매를 타고났거든."], ["Her figure is as neat as her brother’s.”", "그 몸매가 오라비만큼이나 야무지잖아.”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“그래,”"], ["added Maria, “and her spirits are as good, and she has the same energy of character.", "마리아가 거들었다. “게다가 기질도 그만큼 좋고, 성격의 활기도 똑같아."], ["I cannot but think that good horsemanship has a great deal to do with the mind.”", "승마를 잘하는 건 아무래도 마음가짐과 크게 상관이 있는 것 같아.”"]], [["When they parted at night Edmund asked Fanny whether she meant to ride the next day.", "밤에 헤어질 때 에드먼드는 패니에게 이튿날 말을 탈 생각이냐고 물었다."]], [["“No, I do not know—not if you want the mare,”", "“아뇨, 모르겠어요—오빠가 암말을 쓰실 거면 안 탈게요.”"], ["was her answer.", "그것이 그녀의 대답이었다."]], [["“I do not want her at all for myself,”", "“나는 그 말이 조금도 필요 없어.”"], ["said he; “but whenever you are next inclined to stay at home, I think Miss Crawford would be glad to have her a longer time—for a whole morning, in short.", "그가 말했다. “다만 다음번에 네가 집에 있고 싶을 때가 있거든, 그때는 크로퍼드 양이 말을 좀 더 오래—한마디로 아침 내내 쓸 수 있으면 반가워하실 것 같아서 말이야."], ["She has a great desire to get as far as Mansfield Common: Mrs.", "그분은 맨스필드 공유지까지 가 보고 싶어 몹시 안달이거든."], ["Grant has been telling her of its fine views, and I have no doubt of her being perfectly equal to it.", "그랜트 부인이 그 좋은 경치 이야기를 해 줬대. 그분이 거기까지 너끈히 다녀올 수 있으리라는 건 내가 의심치 않아."], ["But any morning will do for this.", "하지만 그건 어느 아침이든 상관없어."], ["She would be extremely sorry to interfere with you.", "그분은 너를 방해하는 걸 몹시 미안해할 거야."], ["It would be very wrong if she did.", "만약 그런다면 아주 잘못된 일이지."], ["She rides only for pleasure; you for health.”", "그분은 그저 즐기려고 타는 거지만, 너는 건강을 위해 타는 거니까.”"]], [["“I shall not ride to-morrow, certainly,”", "“저는 내일은 분명 안 탈래요.”"], ["said Fanny; “I have been out very often lately, and would rather stay at home.", "패니가 말했다. “요즘 너무 자주 나갔으니, 차라리 집에 있는 게 좋아요."], ["You know I am strong enough now to walk very well.”", "저 이제 아주 잘 걸을 만큼 튼튼한 거 아시잖아요.”"]], [["Edmund looked pleased, which must be Fanny’s comfort, and the ride to Mansfield Common took place the next morning: the party included all the young people but herself, and was much enjoyed at the time, and doubly enjoyed again in the evening discussion.", "에드먼드는 흡족한 낯빛이었고, 그것이 패니에게는 위안이 될 수밖에 없었다. 그리하여 맨스필드 공유지로의 승마는 이튿날 아침에 이루어졌다."], ["A successful scheme of this sort generally brings on another; and the having been to Mansfield Common disposed them all for going somewhere else the day after.", "그 무리에는 그녀만 빼고 젊은 사람이 다 끼었고, 그때도 무척 즐거웠거니와 저녁의 이야기판에서 곱절로 다시 즐거웠다. 이런 종류의 성공한 나들이는 대개 또 다른 나들이를 부르는 법이라, 맨스필드 공유지에 다녀온 것이 그들 모두를 그 이튿날 또 어딘가로 가고 싶게 만들었다."], ["There were many other views to be shewn; and though the weather was hot, there were shady lanes wherever they wanted to go.", "보여 줄 경치는 그 밖에도 많았다. 그리고 날은 더웠어도 그들이 가고 싶어 하는 데면 어디든 그늘진 오솔길이 있었다."], ["A young party is always provided with a shady lane.", "젊은 무리에게는 언제나 그늘진 오솔길이 마련되어 있는 법이다."], ["Four fine mornings successively were spent in this manner, in shewing the Crawfords the country, and doing the honours of its finest spots.", "이런 식으로 나흘 아침이 잇따라, 크로퍼드 남매에게 그 고장을 보여 주고 가장 좋은 자리들을 대접하는 데 쓰였다. 만사가 척척 들어맞았다."], ["Everything answered; it was all gaiety and good-humour, the heat only supplying inconvenience enough to be talked of with pleasure—till the fourth day, when the happiness of one of the party was exceedingly clouded.", "온통 흥과 화기뿐이었고, 더위조차 유쾌하게 이야깃거리로 삼을 만큼의 불편만을 안겨 줄 뿐이었다—나흘째 되던 날, 그 무리 가운데 한 사람의 행복이 몹시 흐려지기 전까지는 말이다."], ["Miss Bertram was the one.", "버트럼 양이 바로 그 사람이었다."], ["Edmund and Julia were invited to dine at the Parsonage, and she was excluded.", "에드먼드와 줄리아는 목사관 만찬에 초대받았으나 그녀는 빠졌다."], ["It was meant and done by Mrs. Grant, with perfect good-humour, on Mr.", "그것은 그날 공원 저택에 얼마간 들르리라 예상되던 러시워스 씨를 생각해 그랜트 부인이 더없이 좋은 뜻으로 마련한 일이었다."], ["Rushworth’s account, who was partly expected at the Park that day; but it was felt as a very grievous injury, and her good manners were severely taxed to conceal her vexation and anger till she reached home. As Mr.", "그러나 그것은 몹시 뼈아픈 상처로 느껴졌고, 그녀는 집에 이를 때까지 제 분함과 노여움을 감추느라 그 고운 몸가짐을 여간 쥐어짜지 않았다."], ["Rushworth did not come, the injury was increased, and she had not even the relief of shewing her power over him; she could only be sullen to her mother, aunt, and cousin, and throw as great a gloom as possible over their dinner and dessert.", "그런데 러시워스 씨가 오지 않는 바람에 상처는 더 커졌고, 그녀는 그를 쥐고 흔드는 힘을 내보이는 후련함조차 누리지 못했다. 그녀가 할 수 있는 것이라곤 어머니와 이모와 사촌에게 뾰로통하게 굴며, 저녁 만찬과 후식 자리에 될 수 있는 대로 짙은 그늘을 드리우는 것뿐이었다."]], [["Between ten and eleven Edmund and Julia walked into the drawing-room, fresh with the evening air, glowing and cheerful, the very reverse of what they found in the three ladies sitting there, for Maria would scarcely raise her eyes from her book, and Lady Bertram was half-asleep; and even Mrs.", "열 시에서 열한 시 사이에 에드먼드와 줄리아가 응접실로 들어섰다. 저녁 공기에 상기되어 발그레하니 쾌활한 그 모습은, 거기 앉아 있는 세 부인에게서 그들이 본 것과는 정반대였다."], ["Norris, discomposed by her niece’s ill-humour, and having asked one or two questions about the dinner, which were not immediately attended to, seemed almost determined to say no more.", "마리아는 좀처럼 책에서 눈을 떼려 하지 않았고, 버트럼 부인은 반쯤 졸고 있었으며, 노리스 부인마저 조카딸의 언짢은 기색에 심기가 흐트러진 데다, 만찬에 대해 한두 마디 물었으나 곧바로 대꾸를 못 얻은 터라, 이제 더는 입을 열지 않기로 작정한 듯했다. 오누이는 몇 분 동안 그 밤을 기리고 별에 대한 소감을 나누느라 저희 말고 다른 데는 생각이 미치지 못했다."], ["For a few minutes the brother and sister were too eager in their praise of the night and their remarks on the stars, to think beyond themselves; but when the first pause came, Edmund, looking around, said, “But where is Fanny?", "그러나 처음으로 말이 끊긴 틈에, 에드먼드가 둘러보며 말했다. “그런데 패니는 어디 있어요?"], ["Is she gone to bed?”", "자러 갔나요?”"]], [["“No, not that I know of,”", "“아니, 내가 알기론 아니다.”"], ["replied Mrs.", "노리스 부인이 대답했다."], ["Norris; “she was here a moment ago.”", "“조금 전까지 여기 있었는데.”"]], [["Her own gentle voice speaking from the other end of the room, which was a very long one, told them that she was on the sofa.", "아주 긴 방의 저쪽 끝에서 들려온 그녀 자신의 부드러운 목소리가, 그녀가 소파에 있음을 그들에게 알려 주었다."], ["Mrs. Norris began scolding.", "노리스 부인이 나무라기 시작했다."]], [["“That is a very foolish trick, Fanny, to be idling away all the evening upon a sofa.", "“패니, 저녁 내내 소파에서 빈둥거리다니 참 어리석은 버릇이다."], ["Why cannot you come and sit here, and employ yourself as we do?", "왜 이리 와서 앉아 우리처럼 뭐라도 하지 못하니?"], ["If you have no work of your own, I can supply you from the poor basket.", "네 일감이 없으면 자선 바구니에서 대 줄 수 있다."], ["There is all the new calico, that was bought last week, not touched yet.", "지난주에 사 온 새 옥양목이 그대로 있어. 아직 손도 안 댔지."], ["I am sure I almost broke my back by cutting it out.", "그거 재단하느라 내 허리가 부러지는 줄 알았다."], ["You should learn to think of other people; and, take my word for it, it is a shocking trick for a young person to be always lolling upon a sofa.”", "너는 남을 생각할 줄 알아야 해. 그리고 내 말 명심해라, 젊은 사람이 늘 소파에 늘어져 있는 건 참 못된 버릇이야.”"]], [["Before half this was said, Fanny was returned to her seat at the table, and had taken up her work again; and Julia, who was in high good-humour, from the pleasures of the day, did her the justice of exclaiming, “I must say, ma’am, that Fanny is as little upon the sofa as anybody in the house.”", "이 말이 절반도 끝나기 전에 패니는 식탁의 제자리로 돌아와 다시 일감을 집어 들었다. 그리고 그날의 즐거움으로 한껏 기분이 좋았던 줄리아가, 그녀를 위해 공정하게 이렇게 외쳐 주었다. “이모, 이 말은 해야겠어요. 패니는 이 집 누구 못지않게 소파에 안 있는 애예요.”"]], [["“Fanny,”", "“패니,”"], ["said Edmund, after looking at her attentively, “I am sure you have the headache.”", "그녀를 유심히 바라본 뒤 에드먼드가 말했다. “너 틀림없이 머리가 아프구나.”"]], [["She could not deny it, but said it was not very bad.", "그녀는 그것을 부인할 수 없었으나, 그리 심하지는 않다고 했다."]], [["“I can hardly believe you,”", "“나는 도무지 못 믿겠는데.”"], ["he replied; “I know your looks too well.", "그가 대꾸했다. “네 낯빛을 내가 너무 잘 알거든."], ["How long have you had it?”", "언제부터 아팠니?”"]], [["“Since a little before dinner.", "“저녁 먹기 조금 전부터요."], ["It is nothing but the heat.”", "그냥 더위 탓이에요.”"]], [["“Did you go out in the heat?”", "“더위 속에 나갔었니?”"]], [["“Go out!", "“나가다니!"], ["to be sure she did,” said Mrs.", "그럼, 나갔고말고.”"], ["Norris: “would you have her stay within such a fine day as this?", "노리스 부인이 말했다. “이렇게 좋은 날 저 애를 안에만 있게 할 셈이었니?"], ["Were not we all out?", "우리 모두 나갔잖아?"], ["Even your mother was out to-day for above an hour.”", "네 어머니마저 오늘 한 시간 넘게 나가 계셨는데.”"]], [["“Yes, indeed, Edmund,”", "“그래, 정말 그랬단다, 에드먼드.”"], ["added her ladyship, who had been thoroughly awakened by Mrs.", "노리스 부인이 패니를 매섭게 나무라는 통에 잠이 확 깬 부인이 거들었다."], ["Norris’s sharp reprimand to Fanny; “I was out above an hour.", "“나도 한 시간 넘게 나가 있었어."], ["I sat three-quarters of an hour in the flower-garden, while Fanny cut the roses; and very pleasant it was, I assure you, but very hot.", "패니가 장미를 자르는 동안 나는 화원에 사십오 분이나 앉아 있었지. 정말이지 참 좋더구나."], ["It was shady enough in the alcove, but I declare I quite dreaded the coming home again.”", "그런데 무척 덥긴 했어. 정자 안은 그늘이 넉넉했지만, 다시 집으로 돌아올 일이 어찌나 두렵던지.”"]], [["“Fanny has been cutting roses, has she?”", "“패니가 장미를 자르고 있었어요?”"]], [["“Yes, and I am afraid they will be the last this year.", "“그래, 아마 올해 마지막 장미일 게다."], ["Poor thing!", "딱한 것!"], ["She found it hot enough; but they were so full-blown that one could not wait.”", "저 앤 무척 덥다고 느꼈지. 하지만 워낙 활짝 피어 버려서 더는 미룰 수가 없었단다.”"]], [["“There was no help for it, certainly,” rejoined Mrs.", "“어쩔 도리가 없었지, 그야.”"], ["Norris, in a rather softened voice; “but I question whether her headache might not be caught then, sister.", "노리스 부인이 좀 누그러진 목소리로 다시 끼어들었다. “하지만 저 애 두통이 바로 그때 든 건 아닌가 싶은데, 동생."], ["There is nothing so likely to give it as standing and stooping in a hot sun; but I dare say it will be well to-morrow.", "뜨거운 볕 아래 서서 몸을 굽히고 있는 것만큼 두통 나기 쉬운 것도 없거든. 그래도 내일이면 나을 게다."], ["Suppose you let her have your aromatic vinegar; I always forget to have mine filled.”", "저 애한테 동생 그 방향 식초를 좀 주지 그래. 나는 늘 내 것 채워 두는 걸 잊어버려서.”"]], [["“She has got it,”", "“저 앤 그걸 갖고 있어요.”"], ["said Lady Bertram; “she has had it ever since she came back from your house the second time.”", "버트럼 부인이 말했다. “두 번째로 언니 집에서 돌아온 뒤로 죽 지니고 있는걸.”"]], [["“What!”", "“뭐라고요!” 에드먼드가 외쳤다."], ["cried Edmund; “has she been walking as well as cutting roses; walking across the hot park to your house, and doing it twice, ma’am?", "“그럼 장미를 자른 데다 걷기까지 했다는 거예요? 뜨거운 공원을 가로질러 이모 댁까지, 그것도 두 번씩이나요?"], ["No wonder her head aches.”", "머리가 아플 만도 하지요.”"]], [["Mrs. Norris was talking to Julia, and did not hear.", "노리스 부인은 줄리아와 이야기하느라 듣지 못했다."]], [["“I was afraid it would be too much for her,”", "“저 애한테 너무 무리일까 봐 걱정은 했다.”"], ["said Lady Bertram; “but when the roses were gathered, your aunt wished to have them, and then you know they must be taken home.”", "버트럼 부인이 말했다. “그런데 장미를 다 자르고 나니 네 이모가 그걸 갖고 싶어 했고, 그러면 또 집까지 가져다줘야 하잖니.”"]], [["“But were there roses enough to oblige her to go twice?”", "“그런데 두 번씩이나 다녀와야 할 만큼 장미가 많았어요?”"]], [["“No; but they were to be put into the spare room to dry; and, unluckily, Fanny forgot to lock the door of the room and bring away the key, so she was obliged to go again.”", "“아니. 다만 그걸 말리려고 빈방에 넣어 두기로 했는데, 하필 패니가 그 방문 잠그고 열쇠 챙겨 오는 걸 깜박하는 바람에, 다시 다녀올 수밖에 없었단다.”"]], [["Edmund got up and walked about the room, saying, “And could nobody be employed on such an errand but Fanny?", "에드먼드는 일어나 방 안을 서성이며 말했다. “그런 심부름에 패니 말고는 부릴 사람이 없었나요?"], ["Upon my word, ma’am, it has been a very ill-managed business.”", "정말이지 이모, 일 처리가 너무 서툴렀어요.”"]], [["“I am sure I do not know how it was to have been done better,” cried Mrs.", "“대체 그걸 어떻게 하면 더 잘할 수 있었는지 나는 도무지 모르겠구나.”"], ["Norris, unable to be longer deaf; “unless I had gone myself, indeed; but I cannot be in two places at once; and I was talking to Mr.", "더는 못 들은 척할 수 없게 된 노리스 부인이 외쳤다. “정말이지 내가 몸소 갔다면 몰라도."], ["Green at that very time about your mother’s dairymaid, by her desire, and had promised John Groom to write to Mrs.", "하지만 나는 한꺼번에 두 군데 있을 수는 없잖니. 게다가 바로 그때 나는 네 어머니 부탁으로 그 댁 젖짜기 하녀 일 때문에 그린 씨와 이야기하고 있었고, 존 그룸한테는 그 아들 일로 제프리스 부인께 편지를 써 주마 약속한 터라, 그 딱한 사람이 반 시간이나 나를 기다리고 있었단다."], ["Jefferies about his son, and the poor fellow was waiting for me half an hour.", "나더러 어느 자리에서든 제 몸 사린다고 나무랄 사람은 없을 게다."], ["I think nobody can justly accuse me of sparing myself upon any occasion, but really I cannot do everything at once.", "하지만 정말이지 나라고 모든 걸 한꺼번에 할 수는 없어."], ["And as for Fanny’s just stepping down to my house for me—it is not much above a quarter of a mile—I cannot think I was unreasonable to ask it.", "그리고 패니가 나 대신 우리 집까지 잠깐 다녀오는 거라면—사분의 일 마일이 조금 넘을 뿐인데—그걸 시킨 게 무리였다고는 도무지 생각지 않는다."], ["How often do I pace it three times a day, early and late, ay, and in all weathers too, and say nothing about it?”", "나야말로 이르나 늦으나, 그래, 어떤 날씨에도, 하루 세 번씩 그 길을 오가면서 한마디 군소리도 안 하는데, 그게 몇 번이더냐?”"]], [["“I wish Fanny had half your strength, ma’am.”", "“패니가 이모 힘의 절반만 있어도 좋겠어요.”"]], [["“If Fanny would be more regular in her exercise, she would not be knocked up so soon.", "“패니가 운동을 좀 더 규칙적으로 했더라면 그리 쉬 지쳐 나가떨어지진 않았을 게다."], ["She has not been out on horseback now this long while, and I am persuaded that, when she does not ride, she ought to walk.", "요즘 오래도록 말을 안 탔으니, 나는 저 애가 말을 안 탈 때는 마땅히 걸어야 한다고 본다. 만약 그 전에 말을 탔더라면 나도 그런 걸 안 시켰겠지."], ["If she had been riding before, I should not have asked it of her.", "하지만 장미 사이에서 몸을 굽히고 난 뒤라 오히려 저 애한테 좋을 거라 여겼단다."], ["But I thought it would rather do her good after being stooping among the roses; for there is nothing so refreshing as a walk after a fatigue of that kind; and though the sun was strong, it was not so very hot.", "그런 종류의 고단함 뒤에는 걷기만큼 개운한 게 없거든. 그리고 볕이 세긴 했어도 그리 대단히 덥지는 않았어."], ["Between ourselves, Edmund,”", "우리끼리 이야기지만, 에드먼드,”"], ["nodding significantly at his mother, “it was cutting the roses, and dawdling about in the flower-garden, that did the mischief.”", "하며 그녀는 그 어머니 쪽으로 의미심장하게 고갯짓을 했다. “저 애를 탈 나게 한 건 장미를 자르고 화원에서 어정거린 일이란다.”"]], [["“I am afraid it was, indeed,”", "“정말이지 그런 것 같아 걱정이구나.”"], ["said the more candid Lady Bertram, who had overheard her; “I am very much afraid she caught the headache there, for the heat was enough to kill anybody.", "그 말을 엿들은, 한결 솔직한 버트럼 부인이 말했다. “저 애가 거기서 두통이 든 게 아닌가 나는 몹시 걱정이야."], ["It was as much as I could bear myself.", "그 더위는 누구든 잡을 만했으니까."], ["Sitting and calling to Pug, and trying to keep him from the flower-beds, was almost too much for me.”", "나조차 겨우겨우 견딜 지경이었어. 앉아서 퍼그를 부르며 그 녀석이 화단에 못 들어가게 막는 것만으로도 나한테는 벅찰 뻔했다니까.”"]], [["Edmund said no more to either lady; but going quietly to another table, on which the supper-tray yet remained, brought a glass of Madeira to Fanny, and obliged her to drink the greater part.", "에드먼드는 두 부인 어느 쪽에게도 더는 말하지 않았다. 다만 조용히 다른 탁자로 가서, 아직 남아 있던 야식 쟁반에서 마데이라 한 잔을 가져다 패니에게 건네고, 그 대부분을 마시게 했다."], ["She wished to be able to decline it; but the tears, which a variety of feelings created, made it easier to swallow than to speak.", "그녀는 그것을 사양할 수 있기를 바랐다. 그러나 갖가지 감정이 자아낸 눈물 탓에, 말을 하기보다 차라리 삼키는 편이 더 수월했다."]], [["Vexed as Edmund was with his mother and aunt, he was still more angry with himself.", "에드먼드는 제 어머니와 이모에게 언짢았으나, 저 자신에게는 더욱 화가 났다."], ["His own forgetfulness of her was worse than anything which they had done.", "그녀를 잊고 있었던 제 잘못이 그들이 저지른 그 무엇보다 더 나빴다."], ["Nothing of this would have happened had she been properly considered; but she had been left four days together without any choice of companions or exercise, and without any excuse for avoiding whatever her unreasonable aunts might require.", "그녀가 제대로 배려받았더라면 이런 일은 하나도 일어나지 않았을 것이다. 그런데 그녀는 나흘을 내리, 함께할 동무도 운동도 고를 길 없이, 게다가 그 지각없는 이모들이 무엇을 시키든 그것을 피할 핑계 하나 없이 방치되어 있었다."], ["He was ashamed to think that for four days together she had not had the power of riding, and very seriously resolved, however unwilling he must be to check a pleasure of Miss Crawford’s, that it should never happen again.", "그녀가 나흘을 내리 말 탈 기회를 갖지 못했다는 것을 떠올리자 그는 부끄러웠다. 그리고 크로퍼드 양의 즐거움을 가로막는 것이 아무리 내키지 않더라도, 다시는 이런 일이 없게 하리라 아주 단단히 마음먹었다."]], [["Fanny went to bed with her heart as full as on the first evening of her arrival at the Park.", "패니는 공원에 처음 오던 날 저녁만큼이나 벅찬 가슴을 안고 잠자리에 들었다."], ["The state of her spirits had probably had its share in her indisposition; for she had been feeling neglected, and been struggling against discontent and envy for some days past.", "아마 그 심사가 그녀의 몸살에도 한몫했을 것이다. 며칠 전부터 그녀는 방치되었다고 느끼며 불만과 시샘에 맞서 애써 싸워 오던 터였으니 말이다."], ["As she leant on the sofa, to which she had retreated that she might not be seen, the pain of her mind had been much beyond that in her head; and the sudden change which Edmund’s kindness had then occasioned, made her hardly know how to support herself.", "남의 눈에 띄지 않으려 물러나 앉았던 그 소파에 기대어 있는 동안, 마음의 아픔은 머리의 아픔을 훨씬 웃돌았다. 그리고 그때 에드먼드의 다정함이 불러온 그 갑작스러운 변화에, 그녀는 제 몸을 어떻게 가누어야 할지조차 알기 어려웠다."]], [["# CHAPTER VIII", "# 제8장"]], [["Fanny’s rides recommenced the very next day; and as it was a pleasant fresh-feeling morning, less hot than the weather had lately been, Edmund trusted that her losses, both of health and pleasure, would be soon made good.", "패니의 승마는 바로 이튿날 다시 시작되었다. 그리고 상쾌하고 산뜻한 아침인 데다 요 며칠 날씨보다 덜 더웠으므로, 에드먼드는 건강으로 보나 즐거움으로 보나 그녀의 손실이 이내 메워지리라 믿었다."], ["While she was gone Mr. Rushworth arrived, escorting his mother, who came to be civil and to shew her civility especially, in urging the execution of the plan for visiting Sotherton, which had been started a fortnight before, and which, in consequence of her subsequent absence from home, had since lain dormant.", "그녀가 나가 있는 동안 러시워스 씨가 제 어머니를 모시고 도착했다."], ["Mrs. Norris and her nieces were all well pleased with its revival, and an early day was named and agreed to, provided Mr.", "어머니는 예를 갖추러, 특히 그 예의를 보이러 온 것이었는데, 보름 전에 시작되었다가 그 뒤 그녀가 집을 비운 탓에 그동안 잠들어 있던 소더턴 방문 계획을 어서 실행에 옮기자고 채근하는 것이 그 방식이었다."], ["Crawford should be disengaged: the young ladies did not forget that stipulation, and though Mrs.", "노리스 부인과 조카딸들은 그 계획이 되살아난 것을 다들 무척 반겼고, 크로퍼드 씨의 형편이 비는 것을 조건으로 이른 날짜가 정해져 합의되었다."], ["Norris would willingly have answered for his being so, they would neither authorise the liberty nor run the risk; and at last, on a hint from Miss Bertram, Mr.", "아가씨들은 그 단서를 잊지 않았다. 노리스 부인은 그가 그러리라고 선뜻 장담하려 들었으나, 그들은 그런 무람없는 짓을 허락하지도, 그런 위험을 무릅쓰지도 않으려 했다."], ["Rushworth discovered that the properest thing to be done was for him to walk down to the Parsonage directly, and call on Mr. Crawford, and inquire whether Wednesday would suit him or not.", "그리하여 마침내 버트럼 양의 귀띔을 받아, 러시워스 씨는 자기가 곧장 목사관으로 걸어 내려가 크로퍼드 씨를 찾아, 수요일이 그에게 괜찮은지 아닌지를 물어보는 것이 가장 온당한 일임을 깨달았다."]], [["Before his return Mrs. Grant and Miss Crawford came in.", "그가 돌아오기 전에 그랜트 부인과 크로퍼드 양이 들어왔다."], ["Having been out some time, and taken a different route to the house, they had not met him.", "얼마간 밖에 나가 있었던 데다 다른 길로 저택에 온 터라, 그들은 그를 마주치지 못했다."], ["Comfortable hopes, however, were given that he would find Mr. Crawford at home.", "그래도 그가 크로퍼드 씨를 집에서 만나게 되리라는 든든한 기대가 오갔다."], ["The Sotherton scheme was mentioned of course.", "소더턴 계획이야 물론 화제에 올랐다."], ["It was hardly possible, indeed, that anything else should be talked of, for Mrs.", "실로 다른 이야기가 나오기란 좀처럼 있을 수 없는 일이었다."], ["Norris was in high spirits about it; and Mrs. Rushworth, a well-meaning, civil, prosing, pompous woman, who thought nothing of consequence, but as it related to her own and her son’s concerns, had not yet given over pressing Lady Bertram to be of the party.", "노리스 부인이 그 일로 잔뜩 들떠 있었고, 게다가 러시워스 부인은—뜻은 착하나 격식만 차리며 장황하게 늘어놓는 젠체하는 여자로, 오직 저와 제 아들의 일에 관계될 때만 무언가를 대수로이 여기는 사람이었는데—여태 버트럼 부인더러 함께 가자고 조르기를 그치지 않고 있었으니 말이다."], ["Lady Bertram constantly declined it; but her placid manner of refusal made Mrs.", "버트럼 부인은 줄곧 사양했다."], ["Rushworth still think she wished to come, till Mrs. Norris’s more numerous words and louder tone convinced her of the truth.", "그러나 그 태평한 거절 태도 탓에 러시워스 부인은 그녀가 여전히 오고 싶어 한다고 여겼고, 마침내 노리스 부인의 더 많은 말수와 더 큰 목소리가 그녀에게 진실을 일깨워 주었다."]], [["“The fatigue would be too much for my sister, a great deal too much, I assure you, my dear Mrs.", "“우리 동생한테는 그 여행이 너무 벅찰 거예요, 정말 너무나도요, 러시워스 부인."], ["Rushworth. Ten miles there, and ten back, you know.", "거기까지 십 마일, 돌아오는 데 또 십 마일이잖아요."], ["You must excuse my sister on this occasion, and accept of our two dear girls and myself without her.", "이번만은 우리 동생을 좀 봐주시고, 동생 없이 우리 사랑스러운 두 딸과 저만으로 만족해 주세요."], ["Sotherton is the only place that could give her a wish to go so far, but it cannot be, indeed.", "소더턴이야말로 동생이 그토록 멀리 가고 싶은 마음이 들 만한 유일한 곳이지만, 정말이지 그럴 수는 없답니다."], ["She will have a companion in Fanny Price, you know, so it will all do very well; and as for Edmund, as he is not here to speak for himself, I will answer for his being most happy to join the party.", "아시다시피 동생한테는 패니 프라이스라는 말벗이 있으니, 다 잘될 거예요. 그리고 에드먼드로 말하면, 지금 제 입으로 말할 처지가 아니니 제가 대신 장담하는데, 그 애도 이 나들이에 끼는 걸 더없이 기뻐할 거예요."], ["He can go on horseback, you know.”", "아시다시피 그 앤 말을 타고 가면 되고요.”"]], [["Mrs. Rushworth being obliged to yield to Lady Bertram’s staying at home, could only be sorry.", "버트럼 부인이 집에 남는 것에 물러설 수밖에 없게 된 러시워스 부인은 그저 아쉬워할 뿐이었다."], ["“The loss of her ladyship’s company would be a great drawback, and she should have been extremely happy to have seen the young lady too, Miss Price, who had never been at Sotherton yet, and it was a pity she should not see the place.”", "“준남작 부인이 함께 못 가시는 건 큰 손실이고, 아직 소더턴에 한 번도 와 본 적 없는 저 젊은 아가씨, 프라이스 양도 뵈었으면 더없이 기뻤을 텐데, 그분이 그곳을 못 보게 되는 게 참 안타깝네요.”"]], [["“You are very kind, you are all kindness, my dear madam,”", "“참 친절하시네요, 온통 친절뿐이세요, 부인.” 노리스 부인이 외쳤다."], ["cried Mrs. Norris; “but as to Fanny, she will have opportunities in plenty of seeing Sotherton.", "“하지만 패니로 말하면, 소더턴을 볼 기회야 앞으로 얼마든지 있을 거예요."], ["She has time enough before her; and her going now is quite out of the question.", "그 앤 앞날이 창창하니까요. 그러니 지금 가는 건 도무지 될 일이 아니에요."], ["Lady Bertram could not possibly spare her.”", "버트럼 부인이 그 애를 도저히 내줄 수 없거든요.”"]], [["“Oh no!", "“아, 안 돼요!"], ["I cannot do without Fanny.”", "나는 패니 없이는 못 지내요.”"]], [["Mrs. Rushworth proceeded next, under the conviction that everybody must be wanting to see Sotherton, to include Miss Crawford in the invitation; and though Mrs.", "러시워스 부인은 다음으로, 누구나 소더턴을 보고 싶어 할 게 틀림없다는 확신에서, 크로퍼드 양까지 초대에 넣으려 했다."], ["Grant, who had not been at the trouble of visiting Mrs. Rushworth, on her coming into the neighbourhood, civilly declined it on her own account, she was glad to secure any pleasure for her sister; and Mary, properly pressed and persuaded, was not long in accepting her share of the civility.", "그랜트 부인은 이웃에 이사 온 러시워스 부인을 굳이 찾아가 인사한 적이 없던 터라 제 몫으로는 정중히 사양했으나, 아우를 위해서라면 어떤 즐거움이든 잡아 주고 싶었다."], ["Mr. Rushworth came back from the Parsonage successful; and Edmund made his appearance just in time to learn what had been settled for Wednesday, to attend Mrs.", "그리하여 메리는 적당히 조름과 설득을 받고는 오래지 않아 제 몫의 호의를 받아들였다."], ["Rushworth to her carriage, and walk half-way down the park with the two other ladies.", "러시워스 씨는 목사관에서 성공하여 돌아왔다. 그리고 에드먼드는 마침맞게 나타나, 수요일로 정해진 일을 전해 듣고, 러시워스 부인을 마차까지 배웅한 뒤, 다른 두 부인과 함께 공원을 절반쯤 걸어 내려갔다."]], [["On his return to the breakfast-room, he found Mrs. Norris trying to make up her mind as to whether Miss Crawford’s being of the party were desirable or not, or whether her brother’s barouche would not be full without her.", "아침식사방으로 돌아온 그는, 노리스 부인이 크로퍼드 양이 이 나들이에 끼는 것이 바람직한지 아닌지, 아니면 그녀가 없어도 그 오라비의 사륜마차가 다 차지는 않을지를 두고 마음을 정하려 애쓰는 것을 보았다."], ["The Miss Bertrams laughed at the idea, assuring her that the barouche would hold four perfectly well, independent of the box, on which one might go with him.", "버트럼가 아가씨들은 그 걱정을 비웃으며, 마부석은 빼고도 사륜마차 안에 넉넉히 넷은 탈 수 있고, 마부석에는 한 사람이 그와 함께 타면 된다고 일러 주었다."]], [["“But why is it necessary,”", "“그런데 왜 꼭,”"], ["said Edmund, “that Crawford’s carriage, or his only, should be employed?", "에드먼드가 말했다. “크로퍼드의 마차, 그것도 그의 마차만 써야 하는 거지?"], ["Why is no use to be made of my mother’s chaise?", "어머니 마차는 왜 못 쓰는 거야?"], ["I could not, when the scheme was first mentioned the other day, understand why a visit from the family were not to be made in the carriage of the family.”", "며칠 전 이 계획이 처음 나왔을 때도, 나는 왜 이 집 식구의 방문에 이 집 식구의 마차를 쓰지 않는 건지 도무지 이해가 안 갔거든.”"]], [["“What!”", "“뭐라고!”"], ["cried Julia: “go boxed up three in a postchaise in this weather, when we may have seats in a barouche!", "줄리아가 외쳤다. “이 날씨에 셋이 역마차 안에 갇혀 끼어 가라고, 사륜마차에 자리가 있는데?"], ["No, my dear Edmund, that will not quite do.”", "아니, 에드먼드, 그건 좀 아니야.”"]], [["“Besides,”", "“게다가,”"], ["said Maria, “I know that Mr.", "마리아가 말했다."], ["Crawford depends upon taking us.", "“크로퍼드 씨는 우리를 태워 갈 걸로 믿고 있어."], ["After what passed at first, he would claim it as a promise.”", "처음에 그런 이야기가 오갔으니, 그 사람은 그걸 약속으로 여기고 당연히 요구할걸.”"]], [["“And, my dear Edmund,”", "“그리고 얘, 에드먼드,” 노리스 부인이 거들었다."], ["added Mrs.", "“하나면 될 걸 마차를 둘씩 끌고 나가는 건 공연한 수고야. 게다가 우리끼리 얘기지만, 마부가 여기서 소더턴 사이 길을 그리 좋아하질 않아."], ["Norris, “taking out two carriages when one will do, would be trouble for nothing; and, between ourselves, coachman is not very fond of the roads between this and Sotherton: he always complains bitterly of the narrow lanes scratching his carriage, and you know one should not like to have dear Sir Thomas, when he comes home, find all the varnish scratched off.”", "좁은 길이 마차를 긁어 댄다고 늘 지독히 투덜대거든. 그리고 사랑하는 토머스 경이 돌아오셨을 때 니스칠이 죄다 긁혀 벗겨진 걸 보시게 하고 싶지야 않잖니.”"]], [["“That would not be a very handsome reason for using Mr. Crawford’s,”", "“그건 크로퍼드 씨 마차를 쓸 그리 훌륭한 이유는 못 돼.”"], ["said Maria; “but the truth is, that Wilcox is a stupid old fellow, and does not know how to drive.", "마리아가 말했다. “하지만 사실은, 윌콕스가 멍청한 늙은이라 몰 줄을 몰라서 그래."], ["I will answer for it that we shall find no inconvenience from narrow roads on Wednesday.”", "수요일에 좁은 길 때문에 겪을 불편은 하나도 없을 거라고 내가 장담해.”"]], [["“There is no hardship, I suppose, nothing unpleasant,”", "“마부석에 타는 게,” 에드먼드가 말했다."], ["said Edmund, “in going on the barouche box.”", "“뭐 고생스러울 것도, 언짢을 것도 없겠지.”"]], [["“Unpleasant!”", "“언짢다니!”"], ["cried Maria: “oh dear!", "마리아가 외쳤다."], ["I believe it would be generally thought the favourite seat.", "“어머나! 오히려 다들 제일 좋아하는 자리로 칠걸."], ["There can be no comparison as to one’s view of the country.", "시골 경치를 보는 걸로 치면 견줄 데가 없거든."], ["Probably Miss Crawford will choose the barouche-box herself.”", "아마 크로퍼드 양이 손수 마부석을 고를 거야.”"]], [["“There can be no objection, then, to Fanny’s going with you; there can be no doubt of your having room for her.”", "“그럼 패니가 너희랑 같이 가는 데는 반대할 게 없겠네. 너희한테 그 애 자리가 있는 건 의심할 것도 없고.”"]], [["“Fanny!”", "“패니라니!”"], ["repeated Mrs.", "노리스 부인이 되뇌었다."], ["Norris; “my dear Edmund, there is no idea of her going with us.", "“얘 에드먼드, 그 애가 우리랑 같이 간다는 얘기는 아예 없다. 그 앤 이모하고 집에 있어."], ["She stays with her aunt. I told Mrs.", "내가 러시워스 부인한테 그렇게 말해 뒀어."], ["Rushworth so. She is not expected.”", "그 애는 갈 사람으로 치지도 않았다고.”"]], [["“You can have no reason, I imagine, madam,”", "“어머니, 제 생각엔,” 그가 제 어머니를 향해 말했다."], ["said he, addressing his mother, “for wishing Fanny not to be of the party, but as it relates to yourself, to your own comfort.", "“어머니가 패니를 이 나들이에서 빼고 싶어 하실 까닭이 없을 것 같아요. 어머니 자신의, 어머니의 편안함에 관계된 것 말고는요."], ["If you could do without her, you would not wish to keep her at home?”", "어머니가 그 애 없이 지내실 수 있다면, 굳이 그 애를 집에 붙들어 두고 싶어 하지는 않으실 거잖아요?”"]], [["“To be sure not, but I cannot do without her.”", "“물론 아니지. 하지만 나는 그 애 없이는 못 지내.”"]], [["“You can, if I stay at home with you, as I mean to do.”", "“제가 어머니와 함께 집에 남으면 지내실 수 있어요. 저는 그럴 작정이거든요.”"]], [["There was a general cry out at this.", "이 말에 여기저기서 반대의 아우성이 터졌다."], ["“Yes,”", "“그래,”"], ["he continued, “there is no necessity for my going, and I mean to stay at home.", "그가 말을 이었다. “내가 갈 필요는 없어."], ["Fanny has a great desire to see Sotherton.", "나는 집에 남을 참이야. 패니는 소더턴을 몹시 보고 싶어 해."], ["I know she wishes it very much.", "그 애가 그걸 무척 바란다는 걸 나는 알아."], ["She has not often a gratification of the kind, and I am sure, ma’am, you would be glad to give her the pleasure now?”", "그 앤 그런 즐거움을 좀처럼 누리지 못하잖아. 그러니 이모, 이번엔 그 애한테 그 기쁨을 안겨 주고 싶으실 거예요, 그렇죠?”"]], [["“Oh yes!", "“아, 그럼!"], ["very glad, if your aunt sees no objection.”", "네 이모만 반대하지 않는다면, 나야 무척 기쁘지.”"]], [["Mrs. Norris was very ready with the only objection which could remain—their having positively assured Mrs.", "노리스 부인은 남아 있을 수 있는 단 하나의 반대 이유를 대는 데 아주 재발랐다—패니가 못 간다고 러시워스 부인에게 딱 잘라 장담해 놓았으니, 이제 와 그 애를 데려가면 그 꼴이 여간 이상해 보이지 않으리라는 것이었다."], ["Rushworth that Fanny could not go, and the very strange appearance there would consequently be in taking her, which seemed to her a difficulty quite impossible to be got over.", "그녀에게는 그것이 도무지 넘을 수 없는 난관 같았다."], ["It must have the strangest appearance!", "그 꼴이 얼마나 이상해 보이겠는가!"], ["It would be something so very unceremonious, so bordering on disrespect for Mrs. Rushworth, whose own manners were such a pattern of good-breeding and attention, that she really did not feel equal to it.", "그것은 너무나 무람없고, 그 몸가짐이 좋은 가정교육과 세심함의 본보기와도 같은 러시워스 부인에 대한 결례에 가까운 일이라, 그녀는 정말이지 그럴 엄두가 나지 않았다."], ["Mrs. Norris had no affection for Fanny, and no wish of procuring her pleasure at any time; but her opposition to Edmund now, arose more from partiality for her own scheme, because it was her own, than from anything else.", "노리스 부인은 패니에게 아무 정도 없었고, 어느 때든 그 애의 즐거움을 마련해 줄 마음도 없었다."], ["She felt that she had arranged everything extremely well, and that any alteration must be for the worse.", "그러나 지금 에드먼드에게 맞서는 것은, 다른 무엇보다 제 계획에 대한 편애에서—그것이 바로 제 계획이었기에—비롯된 것이었다."], ["When Edmund, therefore, told her in reply, as he did when she would give him the hearing, that she need not distress herself on Mrs.", "그녀는 자기가 모든 것을 더없이 잘 짜 놓았으니, 어떤 변경이든 반드시 더 나빠지는 것이라 느꼈다."], ["Rushworth’s account, because he had taken the opportunity, as he walked with her through the hall, of mentioning Miss Price as one who would probably be of the party, and had directly received a very sufficient invitation for his cousin, Mrs.", "그래서 에드먼드가, 그녀가 제 말을 들어줄 때에 이르러, 러시워스 부인 일로는 마음 졸일 것 없다고—그녀와 함께 현관을 지나며 프라이스 양도 아마 이 나들이에 낄 사람이라고 넌지시 말할 기회를 잡았고, 그 자리에서 사촌을 위한 아주 충분한 초대를 곧바로 받았노라고—대꾸하자, 노리스 부인은 너무 언짢은 나머지 곱게 물러서지 못하고, 그저 이렇게 말할 뿐이었다. “그래, 그래, 네 마음대로 하렴."], ["Norris was too much vexed to submit with a very good grace, and would only say, “Very well, very well, just as you chuse, settle it your own way, I am sure I do not care about it.”", "네 뜻대로 정하려무나. 나야 정말이지 아무래도 상관없으니까.”"]], [["“It seems very odd,”", "“참 이상하네.”"], ["said Maria, “that you should be staying at home instead of Fanny.”", "마리아가 말했다. “패니 대신 오빠가 집에 남다니.”"]], [["“I am sure she ought to be very much obliged to you,”", "“그 앤 오빠한테 정말 크게 고마워해야 마땅해.”"], ["added Julia, hastily leaving the room as she spoke, from a consciousness that she ought to offer to stay at home herself.", "줄리아가 덧붙이고는, 저야말로 집에 남겠다고 나서야 마땅하다는 자각에서, 말하기가 무섭게 서둘러 방을 나갔다."]], [["“Fanny will feel quite as grateful as the occasion requires,”", "“패니는 이 일에 걸맞은 만큼 고마워할 거야.”"], ["was Edmund’s only reply, and the subject dropt.", "그것이 에드먼드의 유일한 대답이었고, 그 화제는 그것으로 끝났다."]], [["Fanny’s gratitude, when she heard the plan, was, in fact, much greater than her pleasure.", "막상 그 계획을 들었을 때 패니의 고마움은 실은 그 기쁨보다 훨씬 컸다."], ["She felt Edmund’s kindness with all, and more than all, the sensibility which he, unsuspicious of her fond attachment, could be aware of; but that he should forego any enjoyment on her account gave her pain, and her own satisfaction in seeing Sotherton would be nothing without him.", "그녀는 에드먼드의 친절을, 그가—그녀의 애틋한 정을 짐작조차 못 하는 그가—알아챌 수 있는 그 온 감수성으로, 아니 그 이상으로 느꼈다. 그러나 그가 저 때문에 어떤 즐거움을 포기한다는 것은 그녀에게 아픔이었고, 그가 없다면 소더턴을 보는 제 흡족함도 아무것도 아닐 터였다."]], [["The next meeting of the two Mansfield families produced another alteration in the plan, and one that was admitted with general approbation. Mrs.", "두 맨스필드 집안이 다음번에 만났을 때 그 계획에 또 하나의 변경이 생겼는데, 다들 반겨 받아들인 변경이었다."], ["Grant offered herself as companion for the day to Lady Bertram in lieu of her son, and Dr. Grant was to join them at dinner.", "그랜트 부인이 제 아들 대신 그날 버트럼 부인의 말벗이 되겠다고 자청했고, 그랜트 박사는 만찬 자리에 합류하기로 했다."], ["Lady Bertram was very well pleased to have it so, and the young ladies were in spirits again.", "버트럼 부인은 그리되는 것을 무척 흡족해했고, 아가씨들은 다시 기운이 났다."], ["Even Edmund was very thankful for an arrangement which restored him to his share of the party; and Mrs.", "에드먼드마저, 자기를 그 나들이의 한 몫으로 되돌려 준 그 마련을 무척 고마워했다."], ["Norris thought it an excellent plan, and had it at her tongue’s end, and was on the point of proposing it, when Mrs. Grant spoke.", "그리고 노리스 부인은 그것을 훌륭한 방안이라 여겨 혀끝에 올려 두고 막 제안하려던 참이었는데, 그때 그랜트 부인이 먼저 말을 꺼낸 것이었다."]], [["Wednesday was fine, and soon after breakfast the barouche arrived, Mr.", "수요일은 날이 좋았고, 아침식사가 끝나고 얼마 안 되어 사륜마차가 도착했다."], ["Crawford driving his sisters; and as everybody was ready, there was nothing to be done but for Mrs.", "크로퍼드 씨가 누이들을 태우고 몰고 온 것이었다. 다들 채비가 되어 있었으므로, 그랜트 부인이 내리고 나머지가 제자리에 앉는 것 말고는 할 일이 없었다."], ["Grant to alight and the others to take their places. The place of all places, the envied seat, the post of honour, was unappropriated.", "그 모든 자리 중의 자리, 뭇사람이 부러워하는 그 자리, 영예의 자리만이 임자가 없었다."], ["To whose happy lot was it to fall?", "그것은 누구의 복된 몫으로 떨어질 것인가?"], ["While each of the Miss Bertrams were meditating how best, and with the most appearance of obliging the others, to secure it, the matter was settled by Mrs.", "버트럼가 두 아가씨가 저마다 어떻게 하면 가장 그럴싸하게, 남을 배려하는 척하며 그 자리를 차지할까 궁리하는 사이, 그랜트 부인이 마차에서 내리며 던진 한마디로 그 문제는 매듭지어졌다."], ["Grant’s saying, as she stepped from the carriage, “As there are five of you, it will be better that one should sit with Henry; and as you were saying lately that you wished you could drive, Julia, I think this will be a good opportunity for you to take a lesson.”", "“너희가 다섯이니, 하나는 헨리와 함께 앉는 게 낫겠다. 그리고 줄리아, 요전에 마차를 몰 줄 알면 좋겠다고 했으니, 이번이 한 수 배우기에 좋은 기회일 것 같구나.”"]], [["Happy Julia!", "복된 줄리아!"], ["Unhappy Maria!", "가엾은 마리아!"], ["The former was on the barouche-box in a moment, the latter took her seat within, in gloom and mortification; and the carriage drove off amid the good wishes of the two remaining ladies, and the barking of Pug in his mistress’s arms.", "앞의 사람은 순식간에 마부석에 올랐고, 뒤의 사람은 침울함과 굴욕 속에 안쪽 자리에 앉았다. 그리고 마차는 남은 두 부인의 축원과, 여주인의 품에 안긴 퍼그의 짖는 소리 속에 출발했다."]], [["Their road was through a pleasant country; and Fanny, whose rides had never been extensive, was soon beyond her knowledge, and was very happy in observing all that was new, and admiring all that was pretty.", "그들의 길은 정겨운 시골을 가로질렀다. 그리고 승마를 멀리 다녀 본 적이 없던 패니는 이내 제가 아는 데를 벗어났고, 새로운 것을 다 눈여겨보고 예쁜 것을 다 감탄하며 무척 행복해했다."], ["She was not often invited to join in the conversation of the others, nor did she desire it.", "그녀는 남들의 이야기에 끼라는 청을 자주 받지도 않았고, 그러기를 바라지도 않았다. 제 생각과 사색이 늘 그녀의 가장 좋은 벗이었으니 말이다."], ["Her own thoughts and reflections were habitually her best companions; and, in observing the appearance of the country, the bearings of the roads, the difference of soil, the state of the harvest, the cottages, the cattle, the children, she found entertainment that could only have been heightened by having Edmund to speak to of what she felt.", "그리고 시골의 모습, 길이 뻗은 방향, 흙의 차이, 수확의 형편, 오두막들, 소들, 아이들을 눈여겨보면서, 그녀는 제가 느낀 것을 에드먼드에게 이야기할 수 있었더라면 한층 더했을 그런 즐거움을 누렸다. 그것이야말로 그녀와, 곁에 앉은 그 여인 사이의 유일한 닮은 점이었다."], ["That was the only point of resemblance between her and the lady who sat by her: in everything but a value for Edmund, Miss Crawford was very unlike her.", "에드먼드를 소중히 여기는 것 말고는 모든 면에서 크로퍼드 양은 그녀와 아주 딴판이었다. 그녀에게는 패니와 같은 취향의, 마음의, 감정의 섬세함이 조금도 없었다."], ["She had none of Fanny’s delicacy of taste, of mind, of feeling; she saw Nature, inanimate Nature, with little observation; her attention was all for men and women, her talents for the light and lively.", "그녀는 자연을, 생명 없는 자연을, 별 눈여겨봄 없이 보았다. 그녀의 관심은 온통 남자와 여자에게 쏠려 있었고, 그 재능은 가볍고 발랄한 쪽에 있었다."], ["In looking back after Edmund, however, when there was any stretch of road behind them, or when he gained on them in ascending a considerable hill, they were united, and a “there he is” broke at the same moment from them both, more than once.", "그렇지만 뒤에 길이 얼마간 트여 있을 때나, 그가 제법 높은 언덕을 오르며 그들을 따라잡을 때, 뒤돌아 에드먼드를 찾는 데서만은 둘의 마음이 하나가 되어, “저기 오빠가 있다”는 말이 한꺼번에 두 사람 입에서 터져 나오기를 한두 번이 아니었다."]], [["For the first seven miles Miss Bertram had very little real comfort: her prospect always ended in Mr.", "처음 칠 마일 동안 버트럼 양에게는 마음 편할 구석이 거의 없었다."], ["Crawford and her sister sitting side by side, full of conversation and merriment; and to see only his expressive profile as he turned with a smile to Julia, or to catch the laugh of the other, was a perpetual source of irritation, which her own sense of propriety could but just smooth over.", "그녀의 시야는 늘 크로퍼드 씨와 제 동생이 나란히 앉아 이야기와 웃음에 겨워 있는 데서 끝났다. 그가 미소 지으며 줄리아 쪽으로 돌아설 때 그 생기 넘치는 옆모습만 보이거나, 동생의 웃음소리가 들려오는 것은, 그녀 자신의 예의범절로 겨우 다독일 수 있을 뿐인 끊임없는 짜증의 원천이었다."], ["When Julia looked back, it was with a countenance of delight, and whenever she spoke to them, it was in the highest spirits: “her view of the country was charming, she wished they could all see it,”", "줄리아가 뒤돌아볼 때면 그 얼굴은 환희에 차 있었고, 그들에게 말을 걸 때면 더없이 들뜬 목소리였다. “경치가 어찌나 좋은지, 다들 함께 봤으면 좋겠다”는 둥."], ["etc.; but her only offer of exchange was addressed to Miss Crawford, as they gained the summit of a long hill, and was not more inviting than this: “Here is a fine burst of country.", "그러나 그녀가 자리를 바꿔 주겠다고 내민 유일한 제의는, 긴 언덕 꼭대기에 이르렀을 때 크로퍼드 양에게 건넨 것으로, 이보다 더 마음 동하지 않는 것도 없었다. “여기 경치가 확 트이네요."], ["I wish you had my seat, but I dare say you will not take it, let me press you ever so much;”", "크로퍼드 양이 제 자리에 앉으시면 좋을 텐데, 제가 아무리 권해도 안 앉으실 거죠?”"], ["and Miss Crawford could hardly answer before they were moving again at a good pace.", "그리고 크로퍼드 양이 미처 대답하기도 전에 마차는 다시 제법 빠르게 달리고 있었다."]], [["When they came within the influence of Sotherton associations, it was better for Miss Bertram, who might be said to have two strings to her bow.", "소더턴에 얽힌 것들의 자장 안에 들어서자 버트럼 양에게는 형편이 나아졌다. 그녀는 이를테면 활에 시위를 둘 가진 셈이었으니 말이다."], ["She had Rushworth feelings, and Crawford feelings, and in the vicinity of Sotherton the former had considerable effect.", "그녀에게는 러시워스에 대한 감정과 크로퍼드에 대한 감정이 있었는데, 소더턴 근방에서는 앞의 것이 자못 힘을 떨쳤다."], ["Mr. Rushworth’s consequence was hers.", "러시워스 씨의 지위는 곧 그녀의 것이었다."], ["She could not tell Miss Crawford that “those woods belonged to Sotherton,” she could not carelessly observe that “she believed that it was now all Mr.", "그녀는 크로퍼드 양에게 “저 숲이 소더턴에 딸린 것”이라고, “이제 길 양쪽이 다 러시워스 씨 소유일 거라 알고 있다”고 지나가듯 말하면서도 가슴이 부풀지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Rushworth’s property on each side of the road,” without elation of heart; and it was a pleasure to increase with their approach to the capital freehold mansion, and ancient manorial residence of the family, with all its rights of court-leet and court-baron.", "그리고 그 즐거움은, 온갖 영주 재판권과 장원 재판권까지 딸린, 그 집안의 당당한 자유보유 대저택이자 유서 깊은 장원 저택에 다가갈수록 점점 커져 갔다."]], [["“Now we shall have no more rough road, Miss Crawford; our difficulties are over.", "“이제 험한 길은 더 없을 거예요, 크로퍼드 양. 고생은 끝났어요."], ["The rest of the way is such as it ought to be. Mr.", "나머지 길은 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 좋답니다."], ["Rushworth has made it since he succeeded to the estate.", "러시워스 씨가 이 영지를 물려받은 뒤로 손봐 놓은 거예요."], ["Here begins the village.", "여기서부터 마을이 시작돼요."], ["Those cottages are really a disgrace.", "저 오두막들은 정말 볼썽사납죠."], ["The church spire is reckoned remarkably handsome.", "저 교회 첨탑은 유난히 잘생겼다고들 해요."], ["I am glad the church is not so close to the great house as often happens in old places.", "오래된 데서 흔히 그러듯 교회가 큰 저택에 바싹 붙어 있지 않아서 다행이에요."], ["The annoyance of the bells must be terrible.", "종소리의 성가심이란 여간 끔찍하지 않을 테니까요. 저기 목사관이 있네요."], ["There is the parsonage: a tidy-looking house, and I understand the clergyman and his wife are very decent people.", "말끔해 보이는 집이죠. 그 목사와 아내는 아주 반듯한 사람들이라고 들었어요."], ["Those are almshouses, built by some of the family.", "저건 그 집안 누군가가 지은 구빈원이에요."], ["To the right is the steward’s house; he is a very respectable man.", "오른쪽은 집사의 집이고요. 아주 존경할 만한 사람이랍니다."], ["Now we are coming to the lodge-gates; but we have nearly a mile through the park still.", "이제 정문에 다다르는데, 그래도 공원을 지나는 데만 아직 거의 일 마일이 남았어요. 이쪽 끝은 보시다시피 흉하진 않죠."], ["It is not ugly, you see, at this end; there is some fine timber, but the situation of the house is dreadful.", "훌륭한 나무들도 좀 있고요. 하지만 저택의 자리가 지독해요."], ["We go down hill to it for half a mile, and it is a pity, for it would not be an ill-looking place if it had a better approach.”", "거기까지 반 마일을 내리막으로 내려가야 하는데, 참 안타까운 일이에요. 진입로만 더 나았어도 흉한 곳은 아니었을 테니까요.”"]], [["Miss Crawford was not slow to admire; she pretty well guessed Miss Bertram’s feelings, and made it a point of honour to promote her enjoyment to the utmost.", "크로퍼드 양은 지체 없이 감탄했다. 그녀는 버트럼 양의 심정을 제법 알아챘고, 그 즐거움을 한껏 북돋우는 것을 하나의 도리로 삼았다."], ["Mrs. Norris was all delight and volubility; and even Fanny had something to say in admiration, and might be heard with complacency.", "노리스 부인은 온통 기쁨과 수다뿐이었다."], ["Her eye was eagerly taking in everything within her reach; and after being at some pains to get a view of the house, and observing that “it was a sort of building which she could not look at but with respect,”", "패니마저 감탄에 보탤 말이 얼마간 있었고, 흐뭇하게 들어줄 만도 했다. 그녀의 눈은 손닿는 데 있는 모든 것을 열심히 담아 들이고 있었다."], ["she added, “Now, where is the avenue?", "그리고 애써 저택을 눈에 담고는, “저건 존경심 없이는 바라볼 수 없는 종류의 건물”이라고 말한 뒤, 이렇게 덧붙였다."], ["The house fronts the east, I perceive.", "“그런데, 가로수길은 어디 있어요?"], ["The avenue, therefore, must be at the back of it.", "저택이 동쪽을 바라보고 있네요. 그럼 가로수길은 그 뒤편에 있어야 할 텐데."], ["Mr. Rushworth talked of the west front.”", "러시워스 씨가 서쪽 정면 이야기를 하셨잖아요.”"]], [["“Yes, it is exactly behind the house; begins at a little distance, and ascends for half a mile to the extremity of the grounds.", "“그래, 바로 저택 뒤에 있어. 조금 떨어진 데서 시작해서, 반 마일을 올라가 영지 맨 끝까지 이어지지."], ["You may see something of it here—something of the more distant trees.", "여기서도 얼마간 보일 거야—더 멀리 있는 나무들이 좀."], ["It is oak entirely.”", "온통 참나무란다.”"]], [["Miss Bertram could now speak with decided information of what she had known nothing about when Mr.", "버트럼 양은 이제, 러시워스 씨가 의견을 물었을 때는 아무것도 몰랐던 것을 두고 야무진 지식으로 이야기할 수 있었다."], ["Rushworth had asked her opinion; and her spirits were in as happy a flutter as vanity and pride could furnish, when they drove up to the spacious stone steps before the principal entrance.", "그리고 정문 앞 널따란 돌계단으로 마차가 올라설 무렵, 그녀의 마음은 허영과 자만이 마련해 줄 수 있는 만큼의 흐뭇한 설렘으로 일렁이고 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER IX", "# 제9장"]], [["Mr. Rushworth was at the door to receive his fair lady; and the whole party were welcomed by him with due attention.", "러시워스 씨가 제 어여쁜 아가씨를 맞으러 문간에 나와 있었고, 온 일행은 그의 정중한 대접을 받았다."], ["In the drawing-room they were met with equal cordiality by the mother, and Miss Bertram had all the distinction with each that she could wish.", "응접실에서는 그 어머니가 그에 못지않은 다정함으로 그들을 맞았고, 버트럼 양은 두 사람 모두에게서 바랄 수 있는 온갖 각별한 대우를 받았다."], ["After the business of arriving was over, it was first necessary to eat, and the doors were thrown open to admit them through one or two intermediate rooms into the appointed dining-parlour, where a collation was prepared with abundance and elegance.", "도착의 절차가 끝나자 우선 먹는 일부터 필요했다. 그리하여 문이 활짝 열리고, 그들은 한두 개의 중간 방을 지나 정해진 식당으로 안내되었는데, 거기엔 푸짐하고 우아한 다과가 차려져 있었다."], ["Much was said, and much was ate, and all went well.", "말도 많이 오갔고, 먹기도 많이 먹었으며, 만사가 순조로웠다."], ["The particular object of the day was then considered.", "이윽고 그날의 특별한 목적이 논의되었다."], ["How would Mr. Crawford like, in what manner would he chuse, to take a survey of the grounds?", "크로퍼드 씨는 어떤 식으로 영지를 둘러보고 싶어 할까, 어떤 방식을 고를까?"], ["Mr. Rushworth mentioned his curricle.", "러시워스 씨는 제 이륜 경마차를 들먹였다."], ["Mr. Crawford suggested the greater desirableness of some carriage which might convey more than two.", "크로퍼드 씨는 둘보다 많이 태울 수 있는 마차 쪽이 더 바람직하다고 넌지시 일렀다."], ["“To be depriving themselves of the advantage of other eyes and other judgments, might be an evil even beyond the loss of present pleasure.”", "“다른 눈과 다른 판단의 도움을 스스로 저버리는 것은, 당장의 즐거움을 잃는 것 이상의 손해가 될 수도 있으니까요.”"]], [["Mrs.", "러시워스 부인은 경마차도 함께 끌고 가자고 제안했다."], ["Rushworth proposed that the chaise should be taken also; but this was scarcely received as an amendment: the young ladies neither smiled nor spoke.", "그러나 이것은 좀처럼 나아진 안으로 받아들여지지 않았다. 아가씨들은 웃지도 입을 열지도 않았다."], ["Her next proposition, of shewing the house to such of them as had not been there before, was more acceptable, for Miss Bertram was pleased to have its size displayed, and all were glad to be doing something.", "그다음 그녀가 낸 제안, 곧 전에 와 본 적 없는 이들에게 저택을 구경시키자는 것은 좀 더 반겼다. 버트럼 양은 그 규모를 뽐낼 수 있어 흡족했고, 다들 무언가 하게 되어 반가웠던 것이다."]], [["The whole party rose accordingly, and under Mrs.", "그리하여 온 일행이 일어났고, 러시워스 부인의 안내로 여러 방을 두루 구경했다."], ["Rushworth’s guidance were shewn through a number of rooms, all lofty, and many large, and amply furnished in the taste of fifty years back, with shining floors, solid mahogany, rich damask, marble, gilding, and carving, each handsome in its way.", "방들은 하나같이 천장이 높고 더러는 널찍했으며, 오십 년 전 취향으로 넉넉하게 꾸며져, 번들거리는 마룻바닥, 묵직한 마호가니, 값진 다마스크 천, 대리석, 금박, 조각 장식이 저마다 나름대로 아름다웠다. 그림도 많았고 더러 좋은 것도 있었으나, 대부분은 가문 초상화라 이제는 러시워스 부인 말고는 누구에게도 아무것도 아니었다."], ["Of pictures there were abundance, and some few good, but the larger part were family portraits, no longer anything to anybody but Mrs.", "그녀는 가정부가 가르쳐 줄 수 있는 것을 다 배우느라 무진 애를 쓴 터라, 이제 그 저택을 안내하는 데는 거의 가정부 못지않은 자격을 갖추고 있었다."], ["Rushworth, who had been at great pains to learn all that the housekeeper could teach, and was now almost equally well qualified to shew the house.", "이 자리에서 그녀는 주로 크로퍼드 양과 패니에게 이야기를 건넸으나, 두 사람이 귀 기울이는 태도에는 견줄 수 없는 차이가 있었다."], ["On the present occasion she addressed herself chiefly to Miss Crawford and Fanny, but there was no comparison in the willingness of their attention; for Miss Crawford, who had seen scores of great houses, and cared for none of them, had only the appearance of civilly listening, while Fanny, to whom everything was almost as interesting as it was new, attended with unaffected earnestness to all that Mrs. Rushworth could relate of the family in former times, its rise and grandeur, regal visits and loyal efforts, delighted to connect anything with history already known, or warm her imagination with scenes of the past.", "큰 저택이라면 수십 채나 보았고 그중 어느 것에도 마음 두지 않는 크로퍼드 양은 그저 예의상 듣는 시늉만 냈으나, 모든 것이 새로운 만큼이나 흥미로운 패니는, 러시워스 부인이 들려주는 옛날 그 가문의 이야기—그 흥성과 영화, 임금의 행차와 충성스러운 헌신에—꾸밈없는 진지함으로 귀를 기울이며, 이미 아는 역사에 무엇이든 이어 보거나 지난날의 정경으로 제 상상을 데우는 것을 기뻐했다."]], [["The situation of the house excluded the possibility of much prospect from any of the rooms; and while Fanny and some of the others were attending Mrs.", "저택의 자리 탓에 어느 방에서도 이렇다 할 전망을 바랄 수는 없었다."], ["Rushworth, Henry Crawford was looking grave and shaking his head at the windows.", "그리고 패니와 몇몇이 러시워스 부인을 따르는 동안, 헨리 크로퍼드는 창가에서 심각한 낯으로 고개를 절레절레 흔들고 있었다."], ["Every room on the west front looked across a lawn to the beginning of the avenue immediately beyond tall iron palisades and gates.", "서쪽 정면의 방마다 잔디밭 너머로, 높다란 쇠 울짱과 대문 바로 뒤에서 시작되는 가로수길이 내다보였다."]], [["Having visited many more rooms than could be supposed to be of any other use than to contribute to the window-tax, and find employment for housemaids, “Now,” said Mrs.", "창문세를 보태고 하녀들에게 일거리를 만들어 주는 것 말고는 달리 쓸모가 있으리라고는 볼 수 없는 방들을 그 밖에도 숱하게 구경한 끝에, 러시워스 부인이 말했다."], ["Rushworth, “we are coming to the chapel, which properly we ought to enter from above, and look down upon; but as we are quite among friends, I will take you in this way, if you will excuse me.”", "“자, 이제 예배당으로 갑니다. 본디는 위에서 들어가 내려다봐야 마땅하지만, 우리끼리 정다운 사이니, 괜찮으시다면 이쪽으로 안내할게요.”"]], [["They entered.", "그들은 안으로 들어섰다."], ["Fanny’s imagination had prepared her for something grander than a mere spacious, oblong room, fitted up for the purpose of devotion: with nothing more striking or more solemn than the profusion of mahogany, and the crimson velvet cushions appearing over the ledge of the family gallery above.", "패니의 상상은, 그저 널찍하고 길쭉한, 예배를 위해 꾸민 방보다는 좀 더 웅장한 무언가를 그려 두고 있었다. 그러나 마호가니가 그득한 것과, 위층 가족석 난간 너머로 보이는 진홍색 벨벳 방석 말고는, 더 눈에 띄거나 더 엄숙한 것이라곤 없었다."], ["“I am disappointed,”", "“실망이에요.”"], ["said she, in a low voice, to Edmund.", "그녀가 에드먼드에게 나직이 말했다."], ["“This is not my idea of a chapel.", "“이건 제가 생각한 예배당이 아니에요."], ["There is nothing awful here, nothing melancholy, nothing grand.", "여긴 두려운 것도, 서글픈 것도, 웅장한 것도 없어요."], ["Here are no aisles, no arches, no inscriptions, no banners.", "측랑도, 아치도, 비문도, 군기도 없고요."], ["No banners, cousin, to be ‘blown by the night wind of heaven.’ No signs that a ‘Scottish monarch sleeps below.’”", "군기가 없어요, 오빠—‘하늘의 밤바람에 나부낄’ 군기 말이에요. ‘스코틀랜드 왕이 그 아래 잠들어 있다’는 자취도 없고요.”"]], [["“You forget, Fanny, how lately all this has been built, and for how confined a purpose, compared with the old chapels of castles and monasteries.", "“패니, 이 모든 게 얼마나 근래에 지어졌는지, 그리고 성이나 수도원의 옛 예배당에 비하면 얼마나 좁은 쓰임을 위한 것인지를 잊었구나."], ["It was only for the private use of the family.", "이건 그저 이 집안이 사사로이 쓰려던 것뿐이야."], ["They have been buried, I suppose, in the parish church.", "그 집안 사람들은 아마 교구 교회에 묻혔을 거다."], ["There you must look for the banners and the achievements.”", "군기와 문장(紋章)은 거기서 찾아야지.”"]], [["“It was foolish of me not to think of all that; but I am disappointed.”", "“그걸 다 생각지 못하다니 제가 어리석었네요. 그래도 실망스러운걸요.”"]], [["Mrs. Rushworth began her relation.", "러시워스 부인이 이야기를 시작했다."], ["“This chapel was fitted up as you see it, in James the Second’s time.", "“이 예배당은 보시는 대로 제임스 2세 시대에 꾸며진 거예요."], ["Before that period, as I understand, the pews were only wainscot; and there is some reason to think that the linings and cushions of the pulpit and family seat were only purple cloth; but this is not quite certain.", "제가 알기로 그전엔 신도석이 그저 판벽뿐이었대요. 그리고 설교단과 가족석의 안감과 방석도 그저 자줏빛 천이었으리라 짐작할 만한 근거가 좀 있지만, 이건 확실치는 않아요."], ["It is a handsome chapel, and was formerly in constant use both morning and evening.", "아름다운 예배당이고, 예전엔 아침저녁으로 늘 쓰였답니다."], ["Prayers were always read in it by the domestic chaplain, within the memory of many; but the late Mr. Rushworth left it off.”", "여러 사람의 기억으로는 전속 사제가 늘 여기서 기도를 낭독했다는데, 돌아가신 러시워스 씨가 그걸 그만두셨지요.”"]], [["“Every generation has its improvements,”", "“세대마다 나름의 개량이 있는 법이죠.”"], ["said Miss Crawford, with a smile, to Edmund.", "크로퍼드 양이 에드먼드에게 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["Mrs. Rushworth was gone to repeat her lesson to Mr. Crawford; and Edmund, Fanny, and Miss Crawford remained in a cluster together.", "러시워스 부인은 크로퍼드 씨에게 다시 그 배운 것을 되풀이하러 가 버렸고, 에드먼드와 패니와 크로퍼드 양은 한데 모여 남았다."]], [["“It is a pity,”", "“참 안타까워요.”"], ["cried Fanny, “that the custom should have been discontinued.", "패니가 외쳤다. “그 관습이 끊겼다니 말이에요."], ["It was a valuable part of former times.", "그건 옛 시절의 값진 한 부분이었는데."], ["There is something in a chapel and chaplain so much in character with a great house, with one’s ideas of what such a household should be!", "예배당과 사제에는 큰 저택과, 또 그런 집안이라면 마땅히 이러이러해야 하리라는 관념과 그토록 잘 어울리는 무언가가 있잖아요!"], ["A whole family assembling regularly for the purpose of prayer is fine!”", "온 집안이 기도를 위해 규칙적으로 모이는 건 참 훌륭한 일이에요!”"]], [["“Very fine indeed,”", "“정말 훌륭하고말고요.”"], ["said Miss Crawford, laughing.", "크로퍼드 양이 웃으며 말했다."], ["“It must do the heads of the family a great deal of good to force all the poor housemaids and footmen to leave business and pleasure, and say their prayers here twice a day, while they are inventing excuses themselves for staying away.”", "“딱한 하녀들과 하인들을 죄다 일이며 즐거움에서 붙들어다 하루 두 번씩 여기서 기도를 올리게 강요하는 건, 정작 저희는 빠질 핑계를 궁리하면서 말이죠—그거야 집안 어른들한테 참 이로운 일일 거예요.”"]], [["“That is hardly Fanny’s idea of a family assembling,” said Edmund.", "“그건 패니가 생각하는 온 집안이 모이는 모습과는 거리가 멀지요.”"], ["“If the master and mistress do not attend themselves, there must be more harm than good in the custom.”", "에드먼드가 말했다. “주인 내외가 몸소 참석하지 않는다면, 그 관습에는 이로움보다 해로움이 더 클 테니까요.”"]], [["“At any rate, it is safer to leave people to their own devices on such subjects.", "“어쨌든 그런 문제는 사람마다 제 뜻에 맡겨 두는 편이 더 안전해요. 누구나 제 방식대로 가고 싶어 하잖아요—제 시간과 제 방식으로 기도하고 싶어 하고요."], ["Everybody likes to go their own way—to chuse their own time and manner of devotion.", "참석의 의무, 그 격식, 그 구속, 그 긴 시간—이 모든 게 합쳐지면 여간 부담스러운 게 아니고, 아무도 좋아하지 않는 거예요."], ["The obligation of attendance, the formality, the restraint, the length of time—altogether it is a formidable thing, and what nobody likes; and if the good people who used to kneel and gape in that gallery could have foreseen that the time would ever come when men and women might lie another ten minutes in bed, when they woke with a headache, without danger of reprobation, because chapel was missed, they would have jumped with joy and envy.", "그리고 저 회랑석에서 무릎 꿇고 하품하던 그 착한 사람들이, 언젠가는 두통을 안고 깨어난 남녀가 예배당에 안 갔다고 지탄받을 걱정 없이 침대에 십 분쯤 더 누워 있어도 되는 시절이 오리라는 걸 미리 알았더라면, 아마 부러움에 겨워 펄쩍 뛰었을 거예요. 러시워스 가문의 지난날 미녀들이 얼마나 여러 번, 얼마나 마지못한 심정으로 이 예배당을 찾았을지 짐작이 안 가세요?"], ["Cannot you imagine with what unwilling feelings the former belles of the house of Rushworth did many a time repair to this chapel? The young Mrs.", "젊은 엘리너 부인이니 브리짓 부인이니 하는 이들이—겉으로는 경건한 척 빳빳이 굳어 있으면서도 머릿속은 그와는 사뭇 다른 것으로 가득 차서 말이에요—더구나 딱한 그 사제가 바라볼 값어치도 없는 위인이라면 더욱요."], ["Eleanors and Mrs. Bridgets—starched up into seeming piety, but with heads full of something very different—especially if the poor chaplain were not worth looking at—and, in those days, I fancy parsons were very inferior even to what they are now.”", "게다가 그 시절엔 목사들이 지금보다도 훨씬 못한 축이었을 것 같거든요.”"]], [["For a few moments she was unanswered.", "잠깐 동안 그녀에게 아무도 대꾸하지 않았다."], ["Fanny coloured and looked at Edmund, but felt too angry for speech; and he needed a little recollection before he could say, “Your lively mind can hardly be serious even on serious subjects.", "패니는 얼굴을 붉히며 에드먼드를 바라보았으나 너무 화가 나 말이 나오지 않았다. 그리고 그는 얼마간 마음을 가다듬고 나서야 이렇게 말할 수 있었다."], ["You have given us an amusing sketch, and human nature cannot say it was not so.", "“크로퍼드 양의 그 발랄한 마음은 진지한 문제에서조차 좀처럼 진지해지지 못하는군요. 재미난 그림 한 폭을 보여 주셨고, 인간 본성으로 보아 그렇지 않다고는 못 하겠어요."], ["We must all feel at times the difficulty of fixing our thoughts as we could wish; but if you are supposing it a frequent thing, that is to say, a weakness grown into a habit from neglect, what could be expected from the private devotions of such persons?", "우리 모두 이따금 제 생각을 바라는 대로 붙들어 두기 어려운 걸 느끼기 마련이니까요. 하지만 만약 그걸 흔한 일로—말하자면 소홀함에서 버릇으로 굳어 버린 약점으로 여기시는 거라면, 그런 사람들의 사사로운 기도인들 무얼 기대할 수 있겠어요?"], ["Do you think the minds which are suffered, which are indulged in wanderings in a chapel, would be more collected in a closet?”", "예배당에서 이리저리 흐트러지도록 내버려 두고 방치한 그 마음이, 골방에서라고 더 잘 모아질 것 같으세요?”"]], [["“Yes, very likely.", "“네, 그럴 법하죠."], ["They would have two chances at least in their favour.", "적어도 두 가지는 유리할 테니까요."], ["There would be less to distract the attention from without, and it would not be tried so long.”", "밖에서 주의를 흩뜨릴 것이 덜할 테고, 그렇게 오래 시달리지도 않을 테고요.”"]], [["“The mind which does not struggle against itself under one circumstance, would find objects to distract it in the other, I believe; and the influence of the place and of example may often rouse better feelings than are begun with.", "“한 상황에서 저 자신과 싸우지 않는 마음이라면, 다른 상황에서도 주의를 흩뜨릴 대상을 찾아내고 말 거예요, 제 생각엔. 그리고 장소와 본보기의 힘은, 처음 품고 온 것보다 더 나은 마음을 곧잘 일깨우기도 하고요."], ["The greater length of the service, however, I admit to be sometimes too hard a stretch upon the mind.", "다만 예배가 더 길다는 것은, 인정하건대 때로 마음에 너무 벅찬 짐이 되기도 하지요. 그렇지 않으면 좋으련만."], ["One wishes it were not so; but I have not yet left Oxford long enough to forget what chapel prayers are.”", "하지만 저는 아직 옥스퍼드를 떠난 지 얼마 안 되어, 예배당 기도가 어떤 것인지 잊을 만큼은 안 됐답니다.”"]], [["While this was passing, the rest of the party being scattered about the chapel, Julia called Mr. Crawford’s attention to her sister, by saying, “Do look at Mr.", "이런 이야기가 오가는 동안, 나머지 일행은 예배당 여기저기 흩어져 있었는데, 줄리아가 크로퍼드 씨의 주의를 제 언니에게 끌며 이렇게 말했다."], ["Rushworth and Maria, standing side by side, exactly as if the ceremony were going to be performed.", "“러시워스 씨하고 마리아 좀 보세요, 나란히 서 있는 게 꼭 지금 막 예식이라도 올리려는 것 같잖아요."], ["Have not they completely the air of it?”", "완전히 그런 분위기 아니에요?”"]], [["Mr.", "크로퍼드 씨는 그렇다는 듯 미소 지으며 마리아에게로 다가가, 그녀만 들을 수 있는 목소리로 말했다."], ["Crawford smiled his acquiescence, and stepping forward to Maria, said, in a voice which she only could hear, “I do not like to see Miss Bertram so near the altar.”", "“버트럼 양이 제단에 저리 가까이 서 계신 걸 보고 싶지 않은데요.”"]], [["Starting, the lady instinctively moved a step or two, but recovering herself in a moment, affected to laugh, and asked him, in a tone not much louder, “If he would give her away?”", "흠칫한 그녀는 저도 모르게 한두 걸음 옮겼으나, 이내 마음을 추스르고 웃는 시늉을 하며, 그다지 크지 않은 목소리로 그에게 물었다. “그럼 씨가 저를 신랑에게 넘겨주는 역을 맡아 주시겠어요?”"]], [["“I am afraid I should do it very awkwardly,”", "“아무래도 제가 아주 서툴게 해내지 싶은데요.”"], ["was his reply, with a look of meaning.", "그것이 의미심장한 눈빛과 함께 나온 그의 대답이었다."]], [["Julia, joining them at the moment, carried on the joke.", "마침 그때 합류한 줄리아가 그 농을 이어받았다."]], [["“Upon my word, it is really a pity that it should not take place directly, if we had but a proper licence, for here we are altogether, and nothing in the world could be more snug and pleasant.”", "“정말이지, 제대로 된 혼인 허가장만 있다면 지금 당장 못 할 게 뭐람. 참 아쉽네요."], ["And she talked and laughed about it with so little caution as to catch the comprehension of Mr.", "이렇게 다들 한자리에 모였겠다, 세상에 이보다 더 아늑하고 즐거운 일도 없을 텐데요.”"], ["Rushworth and his mother, and expose her sister to the whispered gallantries of her lover, while Mrs.", "그러고는 어찌나 조심성 없이 그 일을 두고 떠들며 웃어 대는지, 러시워스 씨와 그 어머니까지 알아듣게 만들었고, 제 언니를 애인의 소곤거리는 달콤한 말에 고스란히 노출시켰다."], ["Rushworth spoke with proper smiles and dignity of its being a most happy event to her whenever it took place.", "한편 러시워스 부인은 그것이 언제 이루어지든 저에게는 더없이 경사스러운 일이라고, 걸맞은 미소와 위엄을 갖추어 말했다."]], [["“If Edmund were but in orders!”", "“에드먼드가 서품만 받았어도!”"], ["cried Julia, and running to where he stood with Miss Crawford and Fanny: “My dear Edmund, if you were but in orders now, you might perform the ceremony directly.", "줄리아가 외치며, 크로퍼드 양과 패니와 함께 서 있는 그에게로 달려갔다. “오빠, 지금 오빠가 서품만 받았어도 당장 그 예식을 올려 줄 수 있을 텐데."], ["How unlucky that you are not ordained; Mr.", "아직 서품을 안 받았다니 어쩜 이리 운이 없담."], ["Rushworth and Maria are quite ready.”", "러시워스 씨하고 마리아는 채비가 다 됐는데.”"]], [["Miss Crawford’s countenance, as Julia spoke, might have amused a disinterested observer.", "줄리아가 이렇게 말할 때 크로퍼드 양의 낯빛은, 무심한 구경꾼이 봤다면 재미있어했을 만했다."], ["She looked almost aghast under the new idea she was receiving.", "그녀는 새로 받아 든 그 생각에 거의 아연실색한 표정이었다."], ["Fanny pitied her.", "패니는 그녀가 딱했다."], ["“How distressed she will be at what she said just now,” passed across her mind.", "‘방금 제가 한 말에 얼마나 난처해할까’ 하는 생각이 그녀의 머리를 스쳤다."]], [["“Ordained!”", "“서품이라니!”"], ["said Miss Crawford; “what, are you to be a clergyman?”", "크로퍼드 양이 말했다. “아니, 성직자가 되시는 거예요?”"]], [["“Yes; I shall take orders soon after my father’s return—probably at Christmas.”", "“네. 아버지가 돌아오시고 얼마 안 있어 서품을 받을 겁니다—아마 크리스마스쯤에요.”"]], [["Miss Crawford, rallying her spirits, and recovering her complexion, replied only, “If I had known this before, I would have spoken of the cloth with more respect,”", "크로퍼드 양은 마음을 다잡고 낯빛을 되찾아, 그저 이렇게만 대답하고는 화제를 돌렸다."], ["and turned the subject.", "“진작 알았더라면 성직을 좀 더 존중해서 말했을 텐데요.”"]], [["The chapel was soon afterwards left to the silence and stillness which reigned in it, with few interruptions, throughout the year.", "예배당은 이내, 한 해 내내 거의 아무 방해도 없이 그 안을 지배하는 그 고요와 정적에 다시 내맡겨졌다."], ["Miss Bertram, displeased with her sister, led the way, and all seemed to feel that they had been there long enough.", "제 동생이 못마땅한 버트럼 양이 앞장서 나섰고, 다들 여기 오래 있었다 싶은 눈치였다."]], [["The lower part of the house had been now entirely shewn, and Mrs.", "이제 저택의 아래층은 다 구경한 셈이었고, 이 일에 지칠 줄 모르는 러시워스 부인은 중앙 계단 쪽으로 나아가 그들을 위층 방까지 죄다 안내하려 했을 것이다."], ["Rushworth, never weary in the cause, would have proceeded towards the principal staircase, and taken them through all the rooms above, if her son had not interposed with a doubt of there being time enough.", "아들이 시간이 넉넉지 않을까 싶다며 끼어들지만 않았다면 말이다."], ["“For if,”", "“왜냐하면,”"], ["said he, with the sort of self-evident proposition which many a clearer head does not always avoid, “we are too long going over the house, we shall not have time for what is to be done out of doors.", "하고 그는, 제법 명석한 머리라도 늘 피하지는 못하는 그런 뻔한 소리를 하며 말했다. “저택을 도는 데 너무 오래 걸리면, 밖에서 할 일에 쓸 시간이 없을 테니까요."], ["It is past two, and we are to dine at five.”", "두 시가 넘었고, 우리는 다섯 시에 저녁을 들 참이잖아요.”"]], [["Mrs. Rushworth submitted; and the question of surveying the grounds, with the who and the how, was likely to be more fully agitated, and Mrs.", "러시워스 부인은 물러섰다."], ["Norris was beginning to arrange by what junction of carriages and horses most could be done, when the young people, meeting with an outward door, temptingly open on a flight of steps which led immediately to turf and shrubs, and all the sweets of pleasure-grounds, as by one impulse, one wish for air and liberty, all walked out.", "그리고 누가 어떻게 영지를 둘러볼 것인가 하는 문제가 좀 더 본격적으로 논의될 참이었고, 노리스 부인이 마차와 말을 어떻게 짜맞추면 가장 많은 걸 해낼 수 있을까 궁리하기 시작했는데, 그때 젊은 사람들이 바깥으로 난 문 하나를 발견했다. 그 문은 잔디와 관목으로, 그리고 유원지의 온갖 향긋함으로 곧장 이어지는 계단 위에 솔깃하게 열려 있었고, 그리하여 다들 하나의 충동에, 바람과 자유를 향한 하나의 소망에 이끌린 듯 밖으로 걸어 나갔다."]], [["“Suppose we turn down here for the present,”", "“그럼 우선 이쪽으로 내려가 볼까요.”"], ["said Mrs. Rushworth, civilly taking the hint and following them.", "러시워스 부인이 그 뜻을 정중히 알아채고 그들을 따르며 말했다."], ["“Here are the greatest number of our plants, and here are the curious pheasants.”", "“여기 우리 화초가 제일 많고, 진기한 꿩들도 있답니다.”"]], [["“Query,” said Mr.", "“그런데,”"], ["Crawford, looking round him, “whether we may not find something to employ us here before we go farther?", "크로퍼드 씨가 둘레를 둘러보며 말했다. “더 가기 전에 여기서 우리가 매달릴 만한 무언가를 찾아낼 수 있지 않을까요?"], ["I see walls of great promise.", "아주 유망한 담들이 보이는데요."], ["Mr. Rushworth, shall we summon a council on this lawn?”", "러시워스 씨, 이 잔디밭에서 회의를 한번 열어 볼까요?”"]], [["“James,” said Mrs.", "“제임스,”"], ["Rushworth to her son, “I believe the wilderness will be new to all the party.", "러시워스 부인이 아들에게 말했다. “아마 그 야생 숲은 온 일행에게 처음일 게다."], ["The Miss Bertrams have never seen the wilderness yet.”", "버트럼가 아가씨들도 아직 야생 숲을 본 적이 없으니까.”"]], [["No objection was made, but for some time there seemed no inclination to move in any plan, or to any distance.", "아무도 반대하지 않았으나, 한동안은 어떤 계획으로든, 어느 만큼의 거리로든 움직일 마음이 없어 보였다."], ["All were attracted at first by the plants or the pheasants, and all dispersed about in happy independence. Mr.", "다들 처음엔 화초나 꿩에 이끌려, 저마다 흐뭇하게 제멋대로 흩어졌다."], ["Crawford was the first to move forward to examine the capabilities of that end of the house.", "크로퍼드 씨가 맨 먼저 저택의 그쪽 끝이 지닌 가능성을 살피러 앞으로 나섰다."], ["The lawn, bounded on each side by a high wall, contained beyond the first planted area a bowling-green, and beyond the bowling-green a long terrace walk, backed by iron palisades, and commanding a view over them into the tops of the trees of the wilderness immediately adjoining.", "양옆이 높은 담으로 둘린 그 잔디밭은, 맨 처음 화초를 심은 구역 너머에 볼링 그린을 품고 있었고, 볼링 그린 너머에는 긴 테라스 산책로가 있었는데, 그 뒤로는 쇠 울짱이 받치고 서서, 그 너머로 바로 잇닿은 야생 숲의 우듬지를 굽어보게 해 주었다."], ["It was a good spot for fault-finding.", "흠잡기에 딱 좋은 자리였다."], ["Mr. Crawford was soon followed by Miss Bertram and Mr.", "이내 버트럼 양과 러시워스 씨가 크로퍼드 씨를 뒤따랐다."], ["Rushworth; and when, after a little time, the others began to form into parties, these three were found in busy consultation on the terrace by Edmund, Miss Crawford, and Fanny, who seemed as naturally to unite, and who, after a short participation of their regrets and difficulties, left them and walked on. The remaining three, Mrs.", "그리고 얼마 뒤 나머지도 무리를 이루기 시작했을 때, 이 세 사람은 테라스에서 한창 의논에 열중한 채 에드먼드와 크로퍼드 양과 패니의 눈에 띄었는데, 이 셋은 자연스레 한데 어우러진 듯 보였고, 앞의 세 사람의 아쉬움과 어려움에 잠시 끼어 주고 나서는 그들을 두고 앞서 걸어갔다."], ["Rushworth, Mrs. Norris, and Julia, were still far behind; for Julia, whose happy star no longer prevailed, was obliged to keep by the side of Mrs.", "남은 세 사람—러시워스 부인, 노리스 부인, 줄리아—은 여전히 한참 뒤처져 있었다."], ["Rushworth, and restrain her impatient feet to that lady’s slow pace, while her aunt, having fallen in with the housekeeper, who was come out to feed the pheasants, was lingering behind in gossip with her.", "복된 별이 더는 비추지 않게 된 줄리아는 러시워스 부인 곁을 지키며 안달하는 발걸음을 그 부인의 느린 걸음에 맞춰야 했고, 그녀의 이모는 꿩에게 모이를 주러 나온 가정부와 마주쳐 그와 수다를 떨며 뒤에 처져 어정거리고 있었으니 말이다."], ["Poor Julia, the only one out of the nine not tolerably satisfied with their lot, was now in a state of complete penance, and as different from the Julia of the barouche-box as could well be imagined.", "딱한 줄리아, 아홉 중에 제 몫에 그런대로 만족하지 못한 유일한 사람이었던 그녀는, 이제 완전한 고행의 처지에 놓여, 마부석 위의 그 줄리아와는 상상할 수 있는 한 딴판이었다."], ["The politeness which she had been brought up to practise as a duty made it impossible for her to escape; while the want of that higher species of self-command, that just consideration of others, that knowledge of her own heart, that principle of right, which had not formed any essential part of her education, made her miserable under it.", "도리로 알고 몸에 익힌 그 예의범절이 그녀로서는 빠져나올 길을 막았고, 한편 그 교육에 이렇다 할 몫으로 들어 있지 않았던 것들—그 더 높은 종류의 자제심, 남을 헤아리는 그 올바른 마음, 제 마음을 아는 그 앎, 옳음의 그 원칙—이 없다 보니, 그녀는 그 처지 아래서 비참했다."]], [["“This is insufferably hot,”", "“여긴 참을 수 없이 덥네요.”"], ["said Miss Crawford, when they had taken one turn on the terrace, and were drawing a second time to the door in the middle which opened to the wilderness.", "테라스를 한 바퀴 돌고 나서, 야생 숲으로 열리는 한복판의 그 문으로 두 번째 다가가던 참에 크로퍼드 양이 말했다."], ["“Shall any of us object to being comfortable?", "“우리 중에 누구라도 편안해지는 걸 마다할 사람이 있을까요?"], ["Here is a nice little wood, if one can but get into it.", "여기 들어갈 수만 있다면 아담하고 좋은 숲이 있는데."], ["What happiness if the door should not be locked!", "저 문이 잠겨 있지 않다면 얼마나 좋을까요!"], ["but of course it is; for in these great places the gardeners are the only people who can go where they like.”", "하지만 뻔히 잠겨 있겠죠. 이런 큰 데서는 정원사들만이 제가 가고 싶은 데를 갈 수 있으니까요.”"]], [["The door, however, proved not to be locked, and they were all agreed in turning joyfully through it, and leaving the unmitigated glare of day behind.", "그러나 그 문은 잠겨 있지 않은 것으로 드러났고, 그들은 다들 반갑게 그 문으로 돌아 들어가 뒤편의 사정없는 햇빛을 등지는 데 뜻을 모았다."], ["A considerable flight of steps landed them in the wilderness, which was a planted wood of about two acres, and though chiefly of larch and laurel, and beech cut down, and though laid out with too much regularity, was darkness and shade, and natural beauty, compared with the bowling-green and the terrace.", "제법 긴 계단을 내려서자 야생 숲에 이르렀는데, 그것은 이 에이커쯤 되는, 심어 가꾼 숲이었다. 주로 낙엽송과 월계수와 베어 낸 너도밤나무로 이루어졌고 지나치게 반듯하게 배치되기는 했으나, 볼링 그린이며 테라스에 견주면 어둑함과 그늘이었고, 자연스러운 아름다움이었다."], ["They all felt the refreshment of it, and for some time could only walk and admire.", "다들 그 서늘함을 느끼며 한동안은 그저 걸으며 감탄할 따름이었다."], ["At length, after a short pause, Miss Crawford began with, “So you are to be a clergyman, Mr.", "마침내 잠깐 뜸을 들인 뒤, 크로퍼드 양이 말문을 열었다. “그러니까 성직자가 되신다는 거군요, 버트럼 씨."], ["Bertram. This is rather a surprise to me.”", "저로선 좀 뜻밖이에요.”"]], [["“Why should it surprise you?", "“왜 뜻밖이지요?"], ["You must suppose me designed for some profession, and might perceive that I am neither a lawyer, nor a soldier, nor a sailor.”", "제가 어떤 직업을 가지도록 정해졌으리라 짐작하셨을 테고, 제가 변호사도, 군인도, 뱃사람도 아니라는 건 알아채셨을 텐데요.”"]], [["“Very true; but, in short, it had not occurred to me.", "“옳은 말씀이에요. 하지만 한마디로, 미처 그 생각이 안 났어요."], ["And you know there is generally an uncle or a grandfather to leave a fortune to the second son.”", "게다가 아시다시피 둘째 아들한테는 재산을 물려줄 삼촌이나 할아버지가 대개 하나쯤 있잖아요.”"]], [["“A very praiseworthy practice,”", "“아주 칭찬할 만한 관례지요.”"], ["said Edmund, “but not quite universal.", "에드먼드가 말했다. “하지만 다 그런 건 아니에요."], ["I am one of the exceptions, and being one, must do something for myself.”", "저는 그 예외 중 하나고, 예외인 이상 저 스스로 무언가를 해야 하지요.”"]], [["“But why are you to be a clergyman?", "“그런데 왜 하필 성직자가 되시는 거예요?"], ["I thought that was always the lot of the youngest, where there were many to chuse before him.”", "저는 그건 늘, 먼저 고를 사람이 여럿 있을 때 막내한테 돌아가는 몫인 줄 알았거든요.”"]], [["“Do you think the church itself never chosen, then?”", "“그럼 교회 자체를 택하는 사람은 결코 없다고 생각하세요?”"]], [["“Never is a black word.", "“‘결코’란 험한 말이죠."], ["But yes, in the never of conversation, which means not very often, I do think it.", "하지만 그래요, ‘그리 자주는 아니다’라는 뜻의 그 대화 속 ‘결코’로라면, 저는 정말 그렇게 생각해요."], ["For what is to be done in the church?", "교회에서 대체 무얼 할 수 있겠어요?"], ["Men love to distinguish themselves, and in either of the other lines distinction may be gained, but not in the church.", "사람은 두각을 나타내고 싶어 하는데, 다른 어느 길에서든 두각은 얻을 수 있어도 교회에서는 못 얻잖아요."], ["A clergyman is nothing.”", "성직자란 아무것도 아니거든요.”"]], [["“The nothing of conversation has its gradations, I hope, as well as the never.", "“그 대화 속 ‘아무것도 아니다’에도, ‘결코’와 마찬가지로 등급이 있기를 바랍니다."], ["A clergyman cannot be high in state or fashion.", "성직자가 권세나 유행에서 높은 자리에 있을 수야 없지요."], ["He must not head mobs, or set the ton in dress.", "군중을 이끌어서도, 옷차림의 유행을 이끌어서도 안 되고요."], ["But I cannot call that situation nothing which has the charge of all that is of the first importance to mankind, individually or collectively considered, temporally and eternally, which has the guardianship of religion and morals, and consequently of the manners which result from their influence.", "하지만 저는, 개인으로 보나 집단으로 보나, 이승으로 보나 영원으로 보나 인류에게 가장 으뜸으로 중한 그 모든 것을 맡고, 종교와 도덕을, 따라서 그 영향에서 비롯되는 처신까지 지키는 그 자리를 아무것도 아니라고는 부를 수 없어요."], ["No one here can call the office nothing.", "여기 있는 누구도 그 직분을 아무것도 아니라고는 못 하지요."], ["If the man who holds it is so, it is by the neglect of his duty, by foregoing its just importance, and stepping out of his place to appear what he ought not to appear.”", "그 자리를 맡은 사람이 아무것도 아니라면, 그건 제 소임을 게을리하고, 그 마땅한 중함을 저버리고, 제 자리를 벗어나 마땅히 보여선 안 될 모습으로 나서기 때문이에요.”"]], [["“You assign greater consequence to the clergyman than one has been used to hear given, or than I can quite comprehend.", "“씨는 사람들이 흔히 들어 온 것보다, 아니 제가 도무지 이해할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 중함을 성직자에게 부여하시는군요."], ["One does not see much of this influence and importance in society, and how can it be acquired where they are so seldom seen themselves?", "사교계에서는 그런 영향력이나 중함이 그리 눈에 띄지 않는걸요. 게다가 정작 그들 자신이 그토록 좀처럼 눈에 안 띄는데 그게 어떻게 얻어질 수 있겠어요?"], ["How can two sermons a week, even supposing them worth hearing, supposing the preacher to have the sense to prefer Blair’s to his own, do all that you speak of?", "일주일에 설교 두 번으로—그게 들을 만한 설교라 치고, 또 설교자가 제 것보다 블레어의 설교를 골라 읽을 분별이 있다고 쳐도—어떻게 씨가 말씀하시는 그 모든 걸 해낼 수 있겠어요?"], ["govern the conduct and fashion the manners of a large congregation for the rest of the week?", "남은 한 주 내내 많은 신도의 행실을 다스리고 그 처신을 빚을 수 있겠느냐고요?"], ["One scarcely sees a clergyman out of his pulpit.”", "설교단 밖에 나온 성직자는 좀처럼 눈에 띄지도 않는데요.”"]], [["“You are speaking of London, I am speaking of the nation at large.”", "“크로퍼드 양은 런던을 말씀하시는 거고, 저는 나라 전체를 말하는 거예요.”"]], [["“The metropolis, I imagine, is a pretty fair sample of the rest.”", "“수도야말로 나머지의 꽤 공정한 표본이지 싶은데요.”"]], [["“Not, I should hope, of the proportion of virtue to vice throughout the kingdom.", "“왕국 전역의 미덕과 악덕의 비율로 치자면, 그렇지 않기를 바랍니다."], ["We do not look in great cities for our best morality.", "우리는 가장 훌륭한 도덕을 큰 도시에서 찾지 않아요."], ["It is not there that respectable people of any denomination can do most good; and it certainly is not there that the influence of the clergy can be most felt.", "어느 교파의 반듯한 사람이든 가장 큰 선을 베풀 수 있는 곳은 거기가 아니고, 성직자의 영향력이 가장 잘 느껴질 수 있는 곳도 분명 거기가 아니지요. 훌륭한 설교자는 사람들이 따르고 우러르지요."], ["A fine preacher is followed and admired; but it is not in fine preaching only that a good clergyman will be useful in his parish and his neighbourhood, where the parish and neighbourhood are of a size capable of knowing his private character, and observing his general conduct, which in London can rarely be the case.", "하지만 좋은 성직자가 제 교구와 이웃에 쓸모 있게 되는 것은 훌륭한 설교로만은 아니에요. 그 교구와 이웃이 그의 사사로운 인품을 알고 그의 일상적 처신을 지켜볼 만한 크기일 때 말이지요—런던에서는 그런 경우가 좀처럼 없지만요."], ["The clergy are lost there in the crowds of their parishioners.", "거기서 성직자는 제 교구민의 무리 속에 파묻혀 버려요."], ["They are known to the largest part only as preachers.", "대부분에게는 그저 설교자로만 알려질 뿐이죠."], ["And with regard to their influencing public manners, Miss Crawford must not misunderstand me, or suppose I mean to call them the arbiters of good-breeding, the regulators of refinement and courtesy, the masters of the ceremonies of life.", "그리고 그들이 세상의 처신에 미치는 영향에 대해서라면—크로퍼드 양은 저를 오해하시거나, 제가 그들을 좋은 가정교육의 심판관이니, 세련과 예절의 감독관이니, 인생 의례의 사회자니 하는 뜻으로 말한다고 여기시면 안 됩니다. 제가 말하는 처신이란, 차라리 행실이라 불러야 할지도 몰라요."], ["The manners I speak of might rather be called conduct, perhaps, the result of good principles; the effect, in short, of those doctrines which it is their duty to teach and recommend; and it will, I believe, be everywhere found, that as the clergy are, or are not what they ought to be, so are the rest of the nation.”", "좋은 원칙에서 비롯되는 것, 한마디로 그들이 가르치고 권하는 것이 소임인 그 교리가 낳는 결과 말이에요. 그리고 저는, 성직자가 마땅한 모습이냐 아니냐에 따라 나머지 온 나라도 그러하다는 것을, 어디서나 확인하게 되리라 믿어요.”"]], [["“Certainly,”", "“정말 그래요.”"], ["said Fanny, with gentle earnestness.", "패니가 부드러운 진지함으로 말했다."]], [["“There,”", "“저것 봐요.”"], ["cried Miss Crawford, “you have quite convinced Miss Price already.”", "크로퍼드 양이 외쳤다. “벌써 프라이스 양은 완전히 설득해 버리셨네요.”"]], [["“I wish I could convince Miss Crawford too.”", "“크로퍼드 양도 설득할 수 있으면 좋겠는데요.”"]], [["“I do not think you ever will,”", "“그러진 못하실걸요.”"], ["said she, with an arch smile; “I am just as much surprised now as I was at first that you should intend to take orders.", "그녀가 짓궂은 미소를 지으며 말했다. “씨가 서품을 받으실 작정이라는 게, 저는 지금도 처음만큼이나 놀라워요."], ["You really are fit for something better.", "씨는 정말이지 그보다 나은 일에 어울리는 분이에요."], ["Come, do change your mind.", "자, 마음을 바꾸세요."], ["It is not too late.", "아직 늦지 않았어요."], ["Go into the law.”", "법조계로 가세요.”"]], [["“Go into the law!", "“법조계로 가라고요!"], ["With as much ease as I was told to go into this wilderness.”", "저더러 이 야생 숲으로 들어가라던 것만큼이나 손쉽게 말씀하시네요.”"]], [["“Now you are going to say something about law being the worst wilderness of the two, but I forestall you; remember, I have forestalled you.”", "“이제 씨는 둘 중에 법이야말로 더 고약한 야생 숲이라는 둥 무슨 말을 하려 하시겠죠. 하지만 제가 먼저 막았어요. 기억하세요, 제가 선수를 쳤다는 걸요.”"]], [["“You need not hurry when the object is only to prevent my saying a bon mot, for there is not the least wit in my nature.", "“고작 제 재담 하나를 막으려는 거라면 서두르실 것 없어요. 제 천성엔 재치라곤 눈곱만큼도 없으니까요."], ["I am a very matter-of-fact, plain-spoken being, and may blunder on the borders of a repartee for half an hour together without striking it out.”", "저는 아주 무미건조하고 곧이곧대로 말하는 사람이라, 반 시간을 내리 재치 있는 응수의 언저리에서 헤매면서도 끝내 그걸 못 짜낼 수도 있는 위인이거든요.”"]], [["A general silence succeeded.", "이어 다들 말이 없어졌다. 저마다 생각에 잠겼다."], ["Each was thoughtful.", "패니가 먼저 그 침묵을 깨며 말했다."], ["Fanny made the first interruption by saying, “I wonder that I should be tired with only walking in this sweet wood; but the next time we come to a seat, if it is not disagreeable to you, I should be glad to sit down for a little while.”", "“이 향긋한 숲을 걷기만 했는데 지치다니 저도 이상하네요. 그래도 다음번에 앉을 자리가 나오면, 여러분이 괜찮으시다면 잠깐 앉았으면 좋겠어요.”"]], [["“My dear Fanny,”", "“나의 패니야.”"], ["cried Edmund, immediately drawing her arm within his, “how thoughtless I have been!", "에드먼드가 외치며 얼른 그녀의 팔을 제 팔에 끼웠다. “내가 어쩜 이리 무심했을까!"], ["I hope you are not very tired.", "너무 지치진 않았기를 바란다."], ["Perhaps,”", "어쩌면,”"], ["turning to Miss Crawford, “my other companion may do me the honour of taking an arm.”", "하고 크로퍼드 양 쪽으로 돌아서며, “제 다른 동행께서 제게 팔을 잡아 주시는 영광을 베풀어 주실지도 모르겠군요.”"]], [["“Thank you, but I am not at all tired.”", "“고맙지만 저는 조금도 안 지쳤어요.”"], ["She took it, however, as she spoke, and the gratification of having her do so, of feeling such a connexion for the first time, made him a little forgetful of Fanny.", "그러면서도 그녀는 그 팔을 잡았고, 그녀가 그리해 준 것이, 그런 이어짐을 처음으로 느낀 그 흡족함이, 그로 하여금 패니를 잠깐 잊게 했다."], ["“You scarcely touch me,”", "“거의 얹지도 않으시는군요.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“You do not make me of any use.", "“저를 조금도 부리지 않으시네요."], ["What a difference in the weight of a woman’s arm from that of a man!", "여자의 팔 무게란 남자의 그것과 어찌 이리 다른지!"], ["At Oxford I have been a good deal used to have a man lean on me for the length of a street, and you are only a fly in the comparison.”", "옥스퍼드에서 저는 한 거리 내내 남자 하나를 제게 기대게 한 적이 여러 번인데, 그에 견주면 크로퍼드 양은 한낱 파리 한 마리에 지나지 않아요.”"]], [["“I am really not tired, which I almost wonder at; for we must have walked at least a mile in this wood.", "“저는 정말 안 지쳤어요. 저 스스로도 신기할 지경이에요."], ["Do not you think we have?”", "우리가 이 숲에서 적어도 일 마일은 걸었을 텐데 말이죠. 그렇게 생각지 않으세요?”"]], [["“Not half a mile,”", "“반 마일도 안 됩니다.”"], ["was his sturdy answer; for he was not yet so much in love as to measure distance, or reckon time, with feminine lawlessness.", "그것이 그의 단호한 대답이었다. 그는 아직 여자다운 제멋대로의 셈법으로 거리를 재거나 시간을 헤아릴 만큼 사랑에 빠지지는 않았던 것이다."]], [["“Oh!", "“어머!"], ["you do not consider how much we have wound about.", "우리가 얼마나 이리저리 돌았는지는 생각도 안 하시는군요. 우리가 어찌나 구불구불 돌아왔는데요."], ["We have taken such a very serpentine course, and the wood itself must be half a mile long in a straight line, for we have never seen the end of it yet since we left the first great path.”", "게다가 이 숲 자체도 곧게 재면 반 마일은 될 거예요. 첫 큰길을 벗어난 뒤로 아직 그 끝을 한 번도 못 봤잖아요.”"]], [["“But if you remember, before we left that first great path, we saw directly to the end of it.", "“하지만 기억하신다면, 그 첫 큰길을 벗어나기 전에 우리는 그 끝까지 곧장 내다봤어요."], ["We looked down the whole vista, and saw it closed by iron gates, and it could not have been more than a furlong in length.”", "그 길 전체를 죽 훑어봤고, 저 끝이 쇠 대문으로 막혀 있는 걸 봤죠. 그러니 길어야 일 펄롱밖에 안 됐을 거예요.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“어머! 저는 씨의 그 펄롱이란 게 뭔지 몰라요."], ["I know nothing of your furlongs, but I am sure it is a very long wood, and that we have been winding in and out ever since we came into it; and therefore, when I say that we have walked a mile in it, I must speak within compass.”", "하지만 여긴 아주 긴 숲이고, 우리가 들어온 뒤로 줄곧 이리저리 굽이돌았다는 건 분명해요. 그러니 제가 여기서 일 마일을 걸었다고 할 때는, 그것도 넉넉잡아 줄여 말하는 거라고요.”"]], [["“We have been exactly a quarter of an hour here,”", "“우리가 여기 있은 지 딱 십오 분이에요.”"], ["said Edmund, taking out his watch.", "에드먼드가 시계를 꺼내며 말했다."], ["“Do you think we are walking four miles an hour?”", "“우리가 시속 사 마일로 걷고 있다고 생각하세요?”"]], [["“Oh!", "“어머!"], ["do not attack me with your watch.", "그 시계로 저를 몰아붙이지 마세요."], ["A watch is always too fast or too slow.", "시계란 늘 너무 빠르거나 너무 느린 법이거든요."], ["I cannot be dictated to by a watch.”", "저는 시계한테 이래라저래라 지시받을 수는 없어요.”"]], [["A few steps farther brought them out at the bottom of the very walk they had been talking of; and standing back, well shaded and sheltered, and looking over a ha-ha into the park, was a comfortable-sized bench, on which they all sat down.", "몇 걸음 더 가자 그들은 바로 그 이야기하던 산책로의 끝머리로 나왔다. 그리고 그늘이 잘 지고 아늑하게 물러앉아, 하하—움푹 팬 경계 도랑—너머로 공원을 굽어보는 자리에, 넉넉한 크기의 벤치가 하나 놓여 있어, 그들은 다 함께 거기 앉았다."]], [["“I am afraid you are very tired, Fanny,”", "“아무래도 너 많이 지쳤나 보구나, 패니.”"], ["said Edmund, observing her; “why would not you speak sooner?", "그녀를 살피며 에드먼드가 말했다. “왜 진작 말하지 않았어?"], ["This will be a bad day’s amusement for you if you are to be knocked up.", "이러다 몸살이라도 나면 오늘 하루가 너한테 고약한 놀이가 되고 말 텐데."], ["Every sort of exercise fatigues her so soon, Miss Crawford, except riding.”", "크로퍼드 양, 이 애는 승마만 빼면 어떤 운동이든 이토록 금세 지쳐 버린답니다.”"]], [["“How abominable in you, then, to let me engross her horse as I did all last week!", "“그럼 지난주 내내 제가 그랬듯이 이 애 말을 독차지하게 두시다니, 씨도 참 못되셨네요!"], ["I am ashamed of you and of myself, but it shall never happen again.”", "씨한테도 저 자신한테도 부끄러워요. 하지만 다시는 그런 일 없을 거예요.”"]], [["“Your attentiveness and consideration makes me more sensible of my own neglect.", "“크로퍼드 양의 그 세심함과 배려가 제 소홀함을 더욱 뼈저리게 느끼게 하는군요."], ["Fanny’s interest seems in safer hands with you than with me.”", "패니의 안녕은 저보다 크로퍼드 양의 손에 있는 편이 더 안전한 것 같아요.”"]], [["“That she should be tired now, however, gives me no surprise; for there is nothing in the course of one’s duties so fatiguing as what we have been doing this morning: seeing a great house, dawdling from one room to another, straining one’s eyes and one’s attention, hearing what one does not understand, admiring what one does not care for. It is generally allowed to be the greatest bore in the world, and Miss Price has found it so, though she did not know it.”", "“그래도 이 애가 지금 지친 건 저로선 조금도 놀랍지 않아요. 세상에 사람의 소임 가운데, 오늘 아침 우리가 한 것만큼 고단한 일도 없으니까요. 큰 저택을 구경하고, 이 방 저 방 어정어정 옮겨 다니고, 눈과 주의를 잔뜩 쥐어짜고, 알아듣지도 못하는 걸 듣고, 마음도 없는 걸 감탄하고요. 그거야말로 세상에서 제일가는 따분한 일로 다들 치잖아요. 프라이스 양도 정작 모르는 채로 그걸 겪은 거예요.”"]], [["“I shall soon be rested,”", "“저는 곧 괜찮아질 거예요.”"], ["said Fanny; “to sit in the shade on a fine day, and look upon verdure, is the most perfect refreshment.”", "패니가 말했다. “좋은 날 그늘에 앉아 푸르름을 바라보는 것만큼 더없이 개운한 것도 없으니까요.”"]], [["After sitting a little while Miss Crawford was up again.", "잠시 앉아 있다가 크로퍼드 양이 다시 일어섰다. “저는 좀 움직여야겠어요.”"], ["“I must move,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she; “resting fatigues me.", "“쉬는 게 오히려 저를 지치게 하네요."], ["I have looked across the ha-ha till I am weary.", "저 경계 도랑 너머를 하도 바라봤더니 물렸어요."], ["I must go and look through that iron gate at the same view, without being able to see it so well.”", "가서 저 쇠 대문 사이로 같은 경치를, 이번엔 그만큼 잘 보이지도 않게 내다봐야겠어요.”"]], [["Edmund left the seat likewise.", "에드먼드도 자리에서 일어났다."], ["“Now, Miss Crawford, if you will look up the walk, you will convince yourself that it cannot be half a mile long, or half half a mile.”", "“자, 크로퍼드 양, 저 산책로를 죽 올려다보시면, 그게 반 마일도, 아니 반의반 마일도 될 수 없다는 걸 스스로 아시게 될 겁니다.”"]], [["“It is an immense distance,”", "“어마어마하게 먼걸요.”"], ["said she; “I see that with a glance.”", "그녀가 말했다. “저는 한눈에 그게 보여요.”"]], [["He still reasoned with her, but in vain.", "그는 여전히 그녀를 조리 있게 설득했으나 헛일이었다."], ["She would not calculate, she would not compare.", "그녀는 셈하려 들지 않았고, 견주려 들지 않았다."], ["She would only smile and assert.", "그저 미소 지으며 우겨 댈 뿐이었다."], ["The greatest degree of rational consistency could not have been more engaging, and they talked with mutual satisfaction.", "더할 나위 없이 이치에 맞는 한결같음이라도 이보다 더 사람을 끌지는 못했을 것이고, 그리하여 둘은 서로 흡족해하며 이야기를 나누었다."], ["At last it was agreed that they should endeavour to determine the dimensions of the wood by walking a little more about it.", "마침내, 숲을 좀 더 거닐어 보며 그 크기를 가늠해 보기로 뜻이 모아졌다."], ["They would go to one end of it, in the line they were then in—for there was a straight green walk along the bottom by the side of the ha-ha—and perhaps turn a little way in some other direction, if it seemed likely to assist them, and be back in a few minutes.", "그들은 지금 있는 방향 그대로 숲의 한쪽 끝까지 가 보기로 했다—하하를 낀 아래쪽으로 곧게 뻗은 푸른 산책로가 있었으니 말이다—그리고 도움이 될 듯싶으면 어느 다른 방향으로 조금 꺾어 보았다가 몇 분 안에 돌아오기로 했다."], ["Fanny said she was rested, and would have moved too, but this was not suffered.", "패니는 이제 쉬었다며 저도 따라 움직이려 했으나, 그것은 허락되지 않았다."], ["Edmund urged her remaining where she was with an earnestness which she could not resist, and she was left on the bench to think with pleasure of her cousin’s care, but with great regret that she was not stronger.", "에드먼드가 그녀에게 그 자리에 그냥 있으라고 어찌나 진지하게 권하는지 그녀는 거스를 수 없었고, 그리하여 벤치에 남겨져, 사촌의 살뜰함을 기쁘게 곱씹으면서도 제가 더 튼튼하지 못한 것을 몹시 아쉬워했다."], ["She watched them till they had turned the corner, and listened till all sound of them had ceased.", "그녀는 그들이 모퉁이를 돌 때까지 지켜보았고, 그들의 기척이 다 스러질 때까지 귀를 기울였다."]], [["# CHAPTER X", "# 제10장"]], [["A quarter of an hour, twenty minutes, passed away, and Fanny was still thinking of Edmund, Miss Crawford, and herself, without interruption from any one.", "십오 분, 이십 분이 흘러갔건만, 패니는 여전히 에드먼드와 크로퍼드 양과 저 자신을 생각하고 있었고, 아무도 그녀를 방해하지 않았다."], ["She began to be surprised at being left so long, and to listen with an anxious desire of hearing their steps and their voices again.", "그녀는 이토록 오래 홀로 남겨진 것이 슬슬 의아해지기 시작했고, 그들의 발소리와 목소리를 다시 듣고 싶은 애타는 마음으로 귀를 기울이기 시작했다."], ["She listened, and at length she heard; she heard voices and feet approaching; but she had just satisfied herself that it was not those she wanted, when Miss Bertram, Mr.", "귀를 기울였고, 마침내 들려왔다. 목소리와 발소리가 다가오는 것을 들었다."], ["Rushworth, and Mr. Crawford issued from the same path which she had trod herself, and were before her.", "그러나 그것이 제가 바라던 사람들의 것이 아님을 막 확인한 참에, 버트럼 양과 러시워스 씨와 크로퍼드 씨가, 그녀 자신이 밟고 갔던 바로 그 길에서 나와 그녀 앞에 섰다."]], [["“Miss Price all alone” and “My dear Fanny, how comes this?”", "“프라이스 양 혼자 있네”와 “나의 패니야, 이게 어찌 된 일이니?”가 첫인사였다."], ["were the first salutations.", "그녀는 사정을 이야기했다."], ["She told her story.", "“가엾은 패니야.”"], ["“Poor dear Fanny,”", "사촌 언니가 외쳤다."], ["cried her cousin, “how ill you have been used by them!", "“저 사람들한테 어쩜 그리 푸대접을 받았니!"], ["You had better have staid with us.”", "우리랑 같이 있을 걸 그랬다.”"]], [["Then seating herself with a gentleman on each side, she resumed the conversation which had engaged them before, and discussed the possibility of improvements with much animation.", "그러고는 양옆에 신사를 하나씩 두고 앉아, 그녀는 아까 그들이 몰두하던 이야기를 다시 이어받아, 손질의 가능성을 자못 활기차게 논했다. 딱히 정해진 것은 없었다."], ["Nothing was fixed on; but Henry Crawford was full of ideas and projects, and, generally speaking, whatever he proposed was immediately approved, first by her, and then by Mr.", "그러나 헨리 크로퍼드는 온갖 착상과 구상으로 가득했고, 대체로 그가 무엇을 제안하든 곧바로 받아들여졌다—먼저 그녀가, 다음으로 러시워스 씨가."], ["Rushworth, whose principal business seemed to be to hear the others, and who scarcely risked an original thought of his own beyond a wish that they had seen his friend Smith’s place.", "러시워스 씨의 주된 소임이란 남들의 말을 듣는 것인 듯했고, 그는 제 친구 스미스의 땅을 봤더라면 좋았을 거라는 바람 말고는 좀처럼 제 독창적인 생각 하나 내놓으려 들지 않았다."]], [["After some minutes spent in this way, Miss Bertram, observing the iron gate, expressed a wish of passing through it into the park, that their views and their plans might be more comprehensive.", "이런 식으로 몇 분을 보낸 뒤, 버트럼 양이 쇠문을 눈여겨보고는, 저희의 시야와 궁리를 좀 더 넓히자며 그 문을 지나 공원으로 나가고 싶다는 뜻을 비쳤다. 헨리 크로퍼드의 생각으로도 그것이야말로 무엇보다 바랄 만한 일이요, 최선이요, 조금이라도 득이 되게 일을 진척시킬 유일한 길이었다."], ["It was the very thing of all others to be wished, it was the best, it was the only way of proceeding with any advantage, in Henry Crawford’s opinion; and he directly saw a knoll not half a mile off, which would give them exactly the requisite command of the house.", "그리고 그는 곧바로 반 마일도 안 되는 데 있는 둔덕 하나를 알아보았는데, 거기서라면 저택을 살피기에 꼭 알맞은 시야가 나올 터였다. 그러니 그 둔덕으로, 그 문을 지나 가야만 했다."], ["Go therefore they must to that knoll, and through that gate; but the gate was locked.", "그러나 문은 잠겨 있었다. 러시워스 씨는 열쇠를 가져올 걸 그랬다고 아쉬워했다."], ["Mr. Rushworth wished he had brought the key; he had been very near thinking whether he should not bring the key; he was determined he would never come without the key again; but still this did not remove the present evil.", "열쇠를 가져와야 하지 않을까 하는 생각을 하마터면 할 뻔했었노라고."], ["They could not get through; and as Miss Bertram’s inclination for so doing did by no means lessen, it ended in Mr.", "다시는 열쇠 없이 오지 않으리라 다짐도 했다. 그러나 그렇다고 당장의 곤란이 없어지지는 않았다."], ["Rushworth’s declaring outright that he would go and fetch the key.", "그들은 지나갈 수 없었다. 그리고 버트럼 양이 그리하고 싶은 마음이 조금도 수그러들지 않자, 결국 러시워스 씨가 제가 가서 열쇠를 가져오겠노라 대놓고 선언하는 것으로 끝이 났다."], ["He set off accordingly.", "그리하여 그는 길을 나섰다."]], [["“It is undoubtedly the best thing we can do now, as we are so far from the house already,”", "“저택에서 이미 이렇게 멀리 왔으니, 지금으로선 그게 우리가 할 수 있는 가장 좋은 일임에 틀림없지요.”"], ["said Mr. Crawford, when he was gone.", "그가 떠나자 크로퍼드 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, there is nothing else to be done.", "“네, 달리 어쩔 도리가 없죠."], ["But now, sincerely, do not you find the place altogether worse than you expected?”", "그런데 이제 솔직히 말씀해 보세요, 이곳이 대체로 기대하셨던 것보다 못하다고 느끼지 않으세요?”"]], [["“No, indeed, far otherwise.", "“아뇨, 정말이지 정반대예요."], ["I find it better, grander, more complete in its style, though that style may not be the best.", "저는 이곳이 더 낫고, 더 웅장하고, 그 양식에서 더 완성되어 있다고 느껴요—비록 그 양식이 최선은 아닐지 몰라도요."], ["And to tell you the truth,”", "그리고 솔직히 말씀드리면,”"], ["speaking rather lower, “I do not think that I shall ever see Sotherton again with so much pleasure as I do now.", "하고 목소리를 좀 낮추며, “저는 앞으로 소더턴을 지금만큼 큰 즐거움으로 다시 보게 될 일은 없을 것 같아요."], ["Another summer will hardly improve it to me.”", "여름이 한 번 더 온다 한들 제게는 이곳을 좀처럼 더 낫게 해 주지 못할 거예요.”"]], [["After a moment’s embarrassment the lady replied, “You are too much a man of the world not to see with the eyes of the world.", "잠깐 당황한 뒤 아가씨가 대답했다. “씨는 너무나 세상 물정에 밝은 분이라 세상의 눈으로 보지 않을 수 없으시겠죠."], ["If other people think Sotherton improved, I have no doubt that you will.”", "남들이 소더턴이 나아졌다고 여기면, 씨도 분명 그러실 거예요.”"]], [["“I am afraid I am not quite so much the man of the world as might be good for me in some points.", "“아무래도 저는 어떤 점에서는 제게 이로울 만큼 세상 물정에 밝지는 못한가 봐요."], ["My feelings are not quite so evanescent, nor my memory of the past under such easy dominion as one finds to be the case with men of the world.”", "제 감정은 그리 쉬 스러지지 않고, 지난날에 대한 제 기억도, 세상 물정에 밝은 사람들에게서 흔히 보는 것처럼 그리 만만하게 부려지지가 않거든요.”"]], [["This was followed by a short silence.", "뒤이어 잠깐 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["Miss Bertram began again.", "버트럼 양이 다시 말을 꺼냈다."], ["“You seemed to enjoy your drive here very much this morning.", "“오늘 아침 여기 오시는 길이 무척 즐거우셨나 봐요."], ["I was glad to see you so well entertained.", "그토록 흥겨워하시는 걸 보니 저도 기뻤어요."], ["You and Julia were laughing the whole way.”", "씨하고 줄리아는 오는 내내 웃어 대던걸요.”"]], [["“Were we?", "“우리가요?"], ["Yes, I believe we were; but I have not the least recollection at what.", "네, 그랬던 것 같네요. 그런데 뭐가 그리 우스웠는지는 도무지 기억이 안 나요."], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["I believe I was relating to her some ridiculous stories of an old Irish groom of my uncle’s.", "아마 제 삼촌의 늙은 아일랜드 마부에 얽힌 우스꽝스러운 이야기를 들려주고 있었나 봐요."], ["Your sister loves to laugh.”", "동생분은 웃기를 좋아하시죠.”"]], [["“You think her more light-hearted than I am?”", "“줄리아가 저보다 더 명랑하다고 보시는 거예요?”"]], [["“More easily amused,”", "“더 쉽게 즐거워하시죠.”"], ["he replied; “consequently, you know,”", "그가 대답했다. “그러니 아시다시피,”"], ["smiling, “better company.", "하고 미소 지으며, “더 좋은 말벗이고요."], ["I could not have hoped to entertain you with Irish anecdotes during a ten miles’ drive.”", "십 마일을 오는 동안 아일랜드 일화로 버트럼 양을 즐겁게 해 드리기는 바랄 수 없었을 테니까요.”"]], [["“Naturally, I believe, I am as lively as Julia, but I have more to think of now.”", "“저도 천성으로야 줄리아 못지않게 발랄한 것 같아요. 다만 지금은 생각할 게 더 많은 것뿐이죠.”"]], [["“You have, undoubtedly; and there are situations in which very high spirits would denote insensibility.", "“그야 두말할 것 없지요. 게다가 너무 들뜬 기색이 오히려 무신경으로 비칠 처지도 있는 법이고요."], ["Your prospects, however, are too fair to justify want of spirits.", "하지만 버트럼 양의 앞날은 기운이 없어도 될 만큼 어둡기는커녕 너무 환하잖아요."], ["You have a very smiling scene before you.”", "눈앞에 아주 방긋 웃는 경치가 펼쳐져 있으니까요.”"]], [["“Do you mean literally or figuratively?", "“말 그대로 하시는 거예요, 아니면 비유로요?"], ["Literally, I conclude.", "말 그대로시겠죠."], ["Yes, certainly, the sun shines, and the park looks very cheerful.", "네, 그럼요, 해가 비치고 공원은 아주 쾌활해 보여요."], ["But unluckily that iron gate, that ha-ha, give me a feeling of restraint and hardship.", "하지만 하필 저 쇠문이며 저 하하가 저한테 구속과 답답함을 느끼게 하네요."], ["‘I cannot get out,’ as the starling said.”", "‘난 나갈 수가 없어’, 찌르레기가 말한 것처럼요.”"], ["As she spoke, and it was with expression, she walked to the gate: he followed her.", "그렇게 말하며—그것도 감정을 실어—그녀는 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다. 그가 그녀를 뒤따랐다."], ["“Mr. Rushworth is so long fetching this key!”", "“러시워스 씨는 이 열쇠를 가지러 가서 왜 이리 오래 걸릴까요!”"]], [["“And for the world you would not get out without the key and without Mr.", "“그런데 세상없어도 열쇠 없이, 러시워스 씨의 허락과 보호 없이는 나가지 않으시겠다는 거군요."], ["Rushworth’s authority and protection, or I think you might with little difficulty pass round the edge of the gate, here, with my assistance; I think it might be done, if you really wished to be more at large, and could allow yourself to think it not prohibited.”", "아니면 제 도움을 받아 여기 문 가장자리로 별 어려움 없이 돌아 나가실 수도 있을 텐데요. 정말로 좀 더 자유로워지고 싶으시고, 그게 금지된 일은 아니라고 스스로 여기실 수만 있다면, 얼마든지 될 것 같은데요.”"]], [["“Prohibited!", "“금지라니!"], ["nonsense!", "말도 안 돼요!"], ["I certainly can get out that way, and I will. Mr.", "저는 분명 저쪽으로 나갈 수 있고, 그럴 거예요."], ["Rushworth will be here in a moment, you know; we shall not be out of sight.”", "러시워스 씨도 곧 올 거잖아요. 우린 안 보이는 데로 가는 것도 아니고요.”"]], [["“Or if we are, Miss Price will be so good as to tell him that he will find us near that knoll: the grove of oak on the knoll.”", "“설령 안 보이게 된다 해도, 프라이스 양이 그분께 우리를 저 둔덕 근처에서—그 둔덕의 참나무 숲에서 찾으면 된다고 친절히 일러 주겠지요.”"]], [["Fanny, feeling all this to be wrong, could not help making an effort to prevent it.", "이 모든 것이 옳지 않다고 느낀 패니는 그것을 막아 보려 애쓰지 않을 수 없었다. “다치실 거예요, 언니.”"], ["“You will hurt yourself, Miss Bertram,”", "그녀가 외쳤다. “저 뾰족한 창살에 부딪혀 꼭 다치실 거예요."], ["she cried; “you will certainly hurt yourself against those spikes; you will tear your gown; you will be in danger of slipping into the ha-ha.", "드레스가 찢어질 거고요. 하하에 미끄러져 빠질 위험도 있어요."], ["You had better not go.”", "안 가시는 게 좋아요.”"]], [["Her cousin was safe on the other side while these words were spoken, and, smiling with all the good-humour of success, she said, “Thank you, my dear Fanny, but I and my gown are alive and well, and so good-bye.”", "이 말이 나오는 동안 사촌 언니는 이미 무사히 반대편에 가 있었고, 성공의 온갖 흐뭇함이 담긴 미소를 지으며 말했다. “고맙다, 나의 패니야. 하지만 나도 내 드레스도 멀쩡히 살아 있단다. 그럼 잘 있어.”"]], [["Fanny was again left to her solitude, and with no increase of pleasant feelings, for she was sorry for almost all that she had seen and heard, astonished at Miss Bertram, and angry with Mr.", "패니는 다시 홀로 남겨졌고, 유쾌한 기분이라곤 조금도 더해지지 않았다. 그녀는 제가 보고 들은 것 거의 전부가 안타까웠고, 버트럼 양에게 놀랐으며, 크로퍼드 씨에게 화가 났으니 말이다."], ["Crawford. By taking a circuitous, and, as it appeared to her, very unreasonable direction to the knoll, they were soon beyond her eye; and for some minutes longer she remained without sight or sound of any companion.", "둔덕으로 가느라 그들은 빙 돌아가는, 그녀 보기엔 무척이나 부질없는 방향을 택했고, 이내 그녀의 눈 밖으로 사라졌다."], ["She seemed to have the little wood all to herself.", "그리고 그 뒤로도 몇 분 동안 그녀는 어떤 동행의 모습도 소리도 없이 남아 있었다."], ["She could almost have thought that Edmund and Miss Crawford had left it, but that it was impossible for Edmund to forget her so entirely.", "그 작은 숲이 온통 저 혼자 차지인 듯했다. 에드먼드와 크로퍼드 양이 숲을 떠나 버린 게 아닐까 하는 생각마저 들 뻔했으나, 에드먼드가 저를 그토록 까맣게 잊는다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이었다."]], [["She was again roused from disagreeable musings by sudden footsteps: somebody was coming at a quick pace down the principal walk.", "그녀는 갑작스러운 발소리에 언짢은 상념에서 다시 깨어났다. 누군가 빠른 걸음으로 큰길을 내려오고 있었다."], ["She expected Mr.", "그녀는 러시워스 씨이려니 했으나, 줄리아였다."], ["Rushworth, but it was Julia, who, hot and out of breath, and with a look of disappointment, cried out on seeing her, “Heyday!", "상기되어 숨을 헐떡이며 실망한 낯빛의 그녀가, 패니를 보고 외쳤다. “아니!"], ["Where are the others?", "다른 사람들은 어디 있어?"], ["I thought Maria and Mr. Crawford were with you.”", "마리아하고 크로퍼드 씨가 너랑 같이 있는 줄 알았는데.”"]], [["Fanny explained.", "패니가 설명했다."]], [["“A pretty trick, upon my word!", "“참 얄궂은 짓이네, 정말!"], ["I cannot see them anywhere,”", "어디에도 안 보이잖아.”"], ["looking eagerly into the park.", "하며 그녀는 공원 안을 열심히 들여다보았다."], ["“But they cannot be very far off, and I think I am equal to as much as Maria, even without help.”", "“하지만 그리 멀리는 못 갔을 거야. 그리고 나도 마리아만큼은—도움 없이도—너끈히 해낼 수 있을 것 같아.”"]], [["“But, Julia, Mr. Rushworth will be here in a moment with the key.", "“그렇지만 줄리아 언니, 러시워스 씨가 곧 열쇠를 가지고 올 거예요."], ["Do wait for Mr. Rushworth.”", "러시워스 씨를 좀 기다려요.”"]], [["“Not I, indeed.", "“난 안 그래, 정말."], ["I have had enough of the family for one morning.", "오늘 아침엔 그 집안이라면 신물이 나."], ["Why, child, I have but this moment escaped from his horrible mother.", "얘, 나는 방금 그 사람의 그 지긋지긋한 어머니한테서 겨우 빠져나온 참이라고."], ["Such a penance as I have been enduring, while you were sitting here so composed and so happy!", "네가 여기 이렇게 태평하고 행복하게 앉아 있는 동안, 내가 견딘 그 고행이라니!"], ["It might have been as well, perhaps, if you had been in my place, but you always contrive to keep out of these scrapes.”", "어쩌면 네가 내 자리에 있었어도 매한가지였겠지만, 넌 늘 이런 곤경은 용케 피해 다니잖아.”"]], [["This was a most unjust reflection, but Fanny could allow for it, and let it pass: Julia was vexed, and her temper was hasty; but she felt that it would not last, and therefore, taking no notice, only asked her if she had not seen Mr.", "이것은 더없이 부당한 힐난이었으나, 패니는 그것을 너그러이 봐주고 흘려 넘길 수 있었다. 줄리아는 언짢은 데다 성미가 급했다."], ["Rushworth.", "그러나 그것이 오래가지 않으리라는 걸 알았기에, 아무 내색 없이 그저 러시워스 씨를 못 봤느냐고 물었다."]], [["“Yes, yes, we saw him.", "“봤어, 봤어."], ["He was posting away as if upon life and death, and could but just spare time to tell us his errand, and where you all were.”", "목숨이라도 걸린 듯이 부리나케 달려가면서, 겨우 짬을 내 제 심부름 얘기하고 너희 다 어디 있는지만 일러 주더라.”"]], [["“It is a pity he should have so much trouble for nothing.”", "“공연히 그렇게 애만 쓰시게 됐으니 딱하네요.”"]], [["“That is Miss Maria’s concern.", "“그거야 마리아가 알아서 할 일이지."], ["I am not obliged to punish myself for her sins.", "내가 걔 죄까지 대신 벌받을 의무는 없잖아."], ["The mother I could not avoid, as long as my tiresome aunt was dancing about with the housekeeper, but the son I can get away from.”", "그 어머니야 내 지긋지긋한 이모가 가정부하고 얼쩐거리는 동안엔 피할 도리가 없었지만, 그 아들이야 얼마든지 떼어 놓을 수 있거든.”"]], [["And she immediately scrambled across the fence, and walked away, not attending to Fanny’s last question of whether she had seen anything of Miss Crawford and Edmund.", "그러고는 그녀가 대뜸 울타리를 기어 넘어 걸어가 버리는 통에, 크로퍼드 양과 에드먼드를 못 봤느냐는 패니의 마지막 물음은 귀에 담기지도 못했다."], ["The sort of dread in which Fanny now sat of seeing Mr. Rushworth prevented her thinking so much of their continued absence, however, as she might have done.", "그러나 이제 러시워스 씨를 볼 일이 그녀에게 안긴 일종의 두려움 탓에, 그녀는 그 둘이 여태 안 돌아오는 것을 (그러지 않았더라면 그랬을 만큼) 그리 골똘히 생각하지도 못했다."], ["She felt that he had been very ill-used, and was quite unhappy in having to communicate what had passed.", "그녀는 그가 몹시 푸대접을 받았다고 느꼈고, 그동안 벌어진 일을 전해야 한다는 것이 여간 마음 불편하지 않았다."], ["He joined her within five minutes after Julia’s exit; and though she made the best of the story, he was evidently mortified and displeased in no common degree.", "그는 줄리아가 떠난 지 오 분도 안 되어 그녀에게 왔다. 그녀가 되도록 좋게 이야기를 꾸며 댔건만, 그는 예사롭지 않게 상심하고 언짢은 기색이 역력했다."], ["At first he scarcely said anything; his looks only expressed his extreme surprise and vexation, and he walked to the gate and stood there, without seeming to know what to do.", "처음엔 좀처럼 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그 표정만이 극도의 놀라움과 분함을 드러낼 뿐이었고, 그는 문 쪽으로 걸어가 거기 서서, 무얼 어째야 할지 모르는 듯했다."]], [["“They desired me to stay—my cousin Maria charged me to say that you would find them at that knoll, or thereabouts.”", "“여기 있으라고 하셨어요—마리아 언니가 저더러, 저 둔덕이나 그 근처에서 그분들을 찾으실 수 있을 거라고 전하라 하셨고요.”"]], [["“I do not believe I shall go any farther,”", "“저는 더는 가지 않을 참이에요.”"], ["said he sullenly; “I see nothing of them.", "그가 뾰로통하게 말했다. “아무도 안 보이는걸요."], ["By the time I get to the knoll they may be gone somewhere else.", "제가 그 둔덕에 다다를 즈음이면 다른 데로 가 버렸을지도 모르고요."], ["I have had walking enough.”", "걸을 만큼 걸었어요.”"]], [["And he sat down with a most gloomy countenance by Fanny.", "그러고는 더없이 침울한 낯으로 패니 곁에 앉았다."]], [["“I am very sorry,”", "“정말 안됐어요.”"], ["said she; “it is very unlucky.”", "그녀가 말했다. “참 운이 없네요.”"], ["And she longed to be able to say something more to the purpose.", "그러면서 그녀는 무언가 좀 더 요긴한 말을 할 수 있기를 간절히 바랐다."]], [["After an interval of silence, “I think they might as well have staid for me,”", "잠깐의 침묵 뒤에, “저를 좀 기다려 줬어도 좋았을 텐데 싶네요.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Miss Bertram thought you would follow her.”", "“버트럼 양은 씨가 뒤따라오실 줄 아셨어요.”"]], [["“I should not have had to follow her if she had staid.”", "“그 사람이 그냥 있었으면 제가 뒤따라갈 일도 없었겠지요.”"]], [["This could not be denied, and Fanny was silenced.", "이것은 부인할 수 없는 말이라 패니는 입을 다물었다."], ["After another pause, he went on—“Pray, Miss Price, are you such a great admirer of this Mr.", "또 한 번 뜸을 들인 뒤, 그가 말을 이었다. “그런데 프라이스 양, 당신도 남들처럼 이 크로퍼드 씨를 그리 대단히 흠모하시나요?"], ["Crawford as some people are? For my part, I can see nothing in him.”", "저로선 그 사람한테서 아무것도 못 보겠던데.”"]], [["“I do not think him at all handsome.”", "“저는 그분이 잘생겼다고는 전혀 생각지 않아요.”"]], [["“Handsome!", "“잘생겼다니!"], ["Nobody can call such an undersized man handsome.", "저렇게 볼품없이 작은 남자를 누가 잘생겼다 하겠어요."], ["He is not five foot nine.", "오 피트 구 인치도 안 돼요."], ["I should not wonder if he is not more than five foot eight.", "오 피트 팔 인치도 넘지 않는대도 저는 안 놀랄 겁니다."], ["I think he is an ill-looking fellow.", "제 눈엔 못생긴 사내예요."], ["In my opinion, these Crawfords are no addition at all.", "제 생각엔 이 크로퍼드 남매는 하나도 보탬이 안 돼요."], ["We did very well without them.”", "우린 그들 없이도 아주 잘 지냈는데.”"]], [["A small sigh escaped Fanny here, and she did not know how to contradict him.", "여기서 패니는 작은 한숨이 새어 나왔고, 그의 말을 어떻게 반박해야 할지 몰랐다."]], [["“If I had made any difficulty about fetching the key, there might have been some excuse, but I went the very moment she said she wanted it.”", "“제가 열쇠 가져오는 걸 두고 조금이라도 꾸물댔다면 무슨 핑곗거리라도 됐겠지만, 저는 그 사람이 그게 필요하다고 말한 바로 그 순간에 갔단 말이에요.”"]], [["“Nothing could be more obliging than your manner, I am sure, and I dare say you walked as fast as you could; but still it is some distance, you know, from this spot to the house, quite into the house; and when people are waiting, they are bad judges of time, and every half minute seems like five.”", "“씨의 태도야 그보다 더 친절할 수 없었지요, 정말요. 그리고 최대한 빨리 걸으셨을 거예요. 하지만 그래도 여기서 저택까지, 그것도 저택 안까지는 제법 거리가 있잖아요. 게다가 사람이 기다릴 때는 시간을 가늠하는 눈이 서툴러지는 법이라, 삼십 초마다 오 분처럼 느껴지거든요.”"]], [["He got up and walked to the gate again, and “wished he had had the key about him at the time.” Fanny thought she discerned in his standing there an indication of relenting, which encouraged her to another attempt, and she said, therefore, “It is a pity you should not join them. They expected to have a better view of the house from that part of the park, and will be thinking how it may be improved; and nothing of that sort, you know, can be settled without you.”", "그는 일어나 다시 문 쪽으로 걸어가, “그때 열쇠를 몸에 지니고 있었더라면 좋았을 것을” 하고 아쉬워했다. 패니는 그가 거기 서 있는 데서 마음이 누그러지는 낌새를 알아챈 듯싶었고, 그에 힘입어 한 번 더 나서 보았다. 그래서 이렇게 말했다. “그분들과 합류하지 못하시면 딱하잖아요. 그분들은 공원의 그쪽에서 저택이 더 잘 보이리라 기대하며, 어떻게 손보면 좋을지 궁리하고 있을 텐데요. 아시다시피 그런 일은 씨 없이는 아무것도 정할 수 없고요.”"]], [["She found herself more successful in sending away than in retaining a companion. Mr.", "그녀는 동행을 붙들어 두는 것보다 떠나보내는 데 더 재주가 있음을 알게 되었다."], ["Rushworth was worked on.", "러시워스 씨는 마음이 움직였다. “그럼,”"], ["“Well,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “if you really think I had better go: it would be foolish to bring the key for nothing.”", "“정말 제가 가는 게 낫다고 여기신다면 말이죠. 열쇠를 괜히 가져온 꼴이 되면 어리석은 일일 테니까요.”"], ["And letting himself out, he walked off without farther ceremony.", "그러고는 스스로 문을 열고 나가, 더는 격식 차리는 일 없이 걸어가 버렸다."]], [["Fanny’s thoughts were now all engrossed by the two who had left her so long ago, and getting quite impatient, she resolved to go in search of them.", "이제 패니의 마음은 온통, 저를 그토록 오래 두고 간 그 두 사람에게 쏠렸다. 그리고 도무지 참을 수 없어진 그녀는 그들을 찾아 나서기로 마음먹었다."], ["She followed their steps along the bottom walk, and had just turned up into another, when the voice and the laugh of Miss Crawford once more caught her ear; the sound approached, and a few more windings brought them before her.", "그녀가 아래쪽 산책로를 따라 그들의 발자취를 좇아 막 다른 길로 접어들었을 때, 크로퍼드 양의 목소리와 웃음소리가 다시 한번 그녀의 귀를 붙들었다. 그 소리가 가까워지고, 몇 굽이를 더 돌자 그들이 그녀 앞에 나타났다."], ["They were just returned into the wilderness from the park, to which a sidegate, not fastened, had tempted them very soon after their leaving her, and they had been across a portion of the park into the very avenue which Fanny had been hoping the whole morning to reach at last, and had been sitting down under one of the trees.", "그들은 방금 공원에서 야생 숲으로 돌아온 참이었는데, 그녀를 두고 떠난 지 얼마 안 되어 잠기지 않은 옆문 하나가 그들을 꾀어냈던 것이다. 그리하여 그들은 공원의 일부를 가로질러, 패니가 아침 내내 마침내 가 닿기를 바라던 바로 그 가로수길로 들어가, 나무 하나 아래 앉아 있었던 것이다."], ["This was their history.", "이것이 그들의 사연이었다."], ["It was evident that they had been spending their time pleasantly, and were not aware of the length of their absence.", "그들이 시간을 즐겁게 보냈고 제가 얼마나 오래 자리를 비웠는지 모르고 있다는 것은 뻔했다. 패니에게 가장 큰 위안은, 에드먼드가 저를 무척 보고 싶어 했고, 그녀가 이미 지쳐 있지만 않았더라면 틀림없이 저를 데리러 돌아왔으리라는 다짐을 들은 것이었다."], ["Fanny’s best consolation was in being assured that Edmund had wished for her very much, and that he should certainly have come back for her, had she not been tired already; but this was not quite sufficient to do away with the pain of having been left a whole hour, when he had talked of only a few minutes, nor to banish the sort of curiosity she felt to know what they had been conversing about all that time; and the result of the whole was to her disappointment and depression, as they prepared by general agreement to return to the house.", "그러나 그것으로도, 그가 겨우 몇 분이라 해 놓고 꼬박 한 시간을 남겨 두었던 그 아픔을 다 씻어 내기에는, 또 그 긴 시간 내내 그들이 무슨 이야기를 나누었는지 알고 싶은 그 궁금함을 다 쫓아내기에는 넉넉지 못했다. 그리하여 그 모든 것의 끝은, 다들 뜻을 모아 저택으로 돌아갈 채비를 하는 가운데, 그녀에게는 실망과 울적함이었다."]], [["On reaching the bottom of the steps to the terrace, Mrs.", "테라스로 오르는 계단 아래에 이르렀을 때, 러시워스 부인과 노리스 부인이 계단 위에 나타났다."], ["Rushworth and Mrs.", "저택을 떠난 지 한 시간 반 만에, 이제 막 야생 숲으로 나설 참이었다."], ["Norris presented themselves at the top, just ready for the wilderness, at the end of an hour and a half from their leaving the house.", "노리스 부인은 더 빨리 움직이기엔 너무 알차게 시간을 보낸 터였다. 조카딸들의 즐거움을 가로막는 어떤 얄궂은 일들이 벌어졌든 간에, 그녀 자신은 더없이 흡족한 아침을 보냈으니 말이다."], ["Mrs. Norris had been too well employed to move faster.", "가정부가 꿩을 두고 한참 상냥하게 이야기를 나눈 끝에 그녀를 낙농장으로 데려가, 그 소들에 대해 죄다 일러 주고, 이름난 크림치즈 만드는 법까지 가르쳐 준 것이다."], ["Whatever cross-accidents had occurred to intercept the pleasures of her nieces, she had found a morning of complete enjoyment; for the housekeeper, after a great many courtesies on the subject of pheasants, had taken her to the dairy, told her all about their cows, and given her the receipt for a famous cream cheese; and since Julia’s leaving them they had been met by the gardener, with whom she had made a most satisfactory acquaintance, for she had set him right as to his grandson’s illness, convinced him that it was an ague, and promised him a charm for it; and he, in return, had shewn her all his choicest nursery of plants, and actually presented her with a very curious specimen of heath.", "게다가 줄리아가 그들 곁을 떠난 뒤로는 정원사와 마주쳤는데, 그녀는 그와 더없이 흡족한 사이가 되었다. 그 손자의 병에 대해 그를 바로잡아, 그게 학질이라고 일러 주고 그 부적을 하나 주마 약속했더니, 그가 그 답례로 제가 가꾼 가장 귀한 화초 묘상을 죄다 보여 주고, 실은 아주 진기한 히스 한 포기까지 그녀에게 선물한 것이다."]], [["On this rencontre they all returned to the house together, there to lounge away the time as they could with sofas, and chit-chat, and Quarterly Reviews, till the return of the others, and the arrival of dinner.", "이 뜻밖의 만남을 계기로 다들 함께 저택으로 돌아가, 나머지 사람들이 돌아오고 만찬이 나올 때까지 소파며 잡담이며 계간 평론지로 되는대로 시간을 흘려보냈다."], ["It was late before the Miss Bertrams and the two gentlemen came in, and their ramble did not appear to have been more than partially agreeable, or at all productive of anything useful with regard to the object of the day.", "버트럼가 아가씨들과 두 신사가 들어온 것은 저물녘이었고, 그들의 산책은 절반쯤이나 유쾌했을 뿐, 그날의 목적에 관해서는 딱히 쓸모 있는 것을 아무것도 낳지 못한 듯했다."], ["By their own accounts they had been all walking after each other, and the junction which had taken place at last seemed, to Fanny’s observation, to have been as much too late for re-establishing harmony, as it confessedly had been for determining on any alteration. She felt, as she looked at Julia and Mr.", "저희 말로는 다들 서로 뒤꽁무니를 좇아 걷기만 했다는데, 마침내 이루어진 그 합류는, 패니가 보기에, 무언가 변경을 정하기에 너무 늦었다고 다들 인정한 것만큼이나, 화기를 되살리기에도 너무 늦었던 듯했다."], ["Rushworth, that hers was not the only dissatisfied bosom amongst them: there was gloom on the face of each. Mr.", "줄리아와 러시워스 씨를 바라보며 그녀는, 그들 가운데 언짢은 가슴이 저 혼자만은 아님을 느꼈다."], ["Crawford and Miss Bertram were much more gay, and she thought that he was taking particular pains, during dinner, to do away any little resentment of the other two, and restore general good-humour.", "저마다 얼굴에 그늘이 드리워 있었다. 크로퍼드 씨와 버트럼 양은 훨씬 흥겨웠고, 그녀가 보기에 그는 만찬 내내 다른 두 사람의 자잘한 앙심을 없애고 다시 두루 화기를 돌리려 각별히 애쓰는 듯했다."]], [["Dinner was soon followed by tea and coffee, a ten miles’ drive home allowed no waste of hours; and from the time of their sitting down to table, it was a quick succession of busy nothings till the carriage came to the door, and Mrs.", "만찬에 이어 이내 차와 커피가 나왔다. 집까지 십 마일을 가야 하니 한 시간도 허투루 쓸 수 없었다."], ["Norris, having fidgeted about, and obtained a few pheasants’ eggs and a cream cheese from the housekeeper, and made abundance of civil speeches to Mrs.", "그리하여 식탁에 앉은 때부터, 마차가 문간에 올 때까지는 분주하나 하잘것없는 일들이 빠르게 이어졌다. 그동안 노리스 부인은 부산을 떨며 가정부에게서 꿩알 몇 개와 크림치즈 하나를 얻어 내고, 러시워스 부인에게 정중한 인사말을 한껏 늘어놓은 끝에, 앞장설 채비를 마쳤다."], ["Rushworth, was ready to lead the way. At the same moment Mr.", "바로 그때 크로퍼드 씨가 줄리아에게 다가가 말했다."], ["Crawford, approaching Julia, said, “I hope I am not to lose my companion, unless she is afraid of the evening air in so exposed a seat.”", "“제 말벗을 잃지 않았으면 하는데요—이렇게 탁 트인 자리에서 저녁 공기가 겁나지만 않으시다면요.”"], ["The request had not been foreseen, but was very graciously received, and Julia’s day was likely to end almost as well as it began.", "그 청은 미리 내다본 것은 아니었으나 아주 반갑게 받아들여졌고, 줄리아의 하루는 시작만큼이나 좋게 끝날 성싶었다."], ["Miss Bertram had made up her mind to something different, and was a little disappointed; but her conviction of being really the one preferred comforted her under it, and enabled her to receive Mr. Rushworth’s parting attentions as she ought.", "버트럼 양은 뭔가 다른 것을 마음에 두고 있던 터라 좀 실망했으나, 자기야말로 정말로 그가 마음에 둔 사람이라는 확신이 그 아래서 그녀를 다독여, 러시워스 씨의 작별 인사를 마땅한 태도로 받을 수 있게 해 주었다."], ["He was certainly better pleased to hand her into the barouche than to assist her in ascending the box, and his complacency seemed confirmed by the arrangement.", "그는 확실히 그녀를 마부석에 올려 주는 것보다 사륜마차 안으로 이끌어 주는 편이 더 흡족했고, 그 배치로 그의 만족감은 굳어진 듯했다."]], [["“Well, Fanny, this has been a fine day for you, upon my word,”", "“그래 패니, 오늘 너한텐 참 좋은 날이었지, 정말이지.”"], ["said Mrs. Norris, as they drove through the park.", "마차가 공원을 지날 때 노리스 부인이 말했다."], ["“Nothing but pleasure from beginning to end!", "“처음부터 끝까지 온통 즐거움뿐이었잖니!"], ["I am sure you ought to be very much obliged to your aunt Bertram and me for contriving to let you go.", "너를 보내 주려고 궁리한 네 이모 버트럼 부인과 나한테 너는 정말 크게 고마워해야 마땅해."], ["A pretty good day’s amusement you have had!”", "참 알찬 하루 놀이를 즐겼구나!”"]], [["Maria was just discontented enough to say directly, “I think you have done pretty well yourself, ma’am.", "마리아는 대뜸 이렇게 말할 만큼 딱 그만큼 못마땅해 있었다. “이모야말로 오늘 꽤 잘 챙기신 것 같은데요."], ["Your lap seems full of good things, and here is a basket of something between us which has been knocking my elbow unmercifully.”", "무릎엔 좋은 것들이 가득하시고, 여기 우리 사이엔 무슨 바구니 하나가 아까부터 사정없이 제 팔꿈치를 찧어 대고 있잖아요.”"]], [["“My dear, it is only a beautiful little heath, which that nice old gardener would make me take; but if it is in your way, I will have it in my lap directly.", "“얘야, 그건 그저 예쁜 히스 한 포기일 뿐이란다. 그 친절한 늙은 정원사가 기어이 나한테 받으라고 해서."], ["There, Fanny, you shall carry that parcel for me; take great care of it: do not let it fall; it is a cream cheese, just like the excellent one we had at dinner.", "하지만 그게 거치적거리면 당장 내 무릎에 놓으마. 자, 패니, 이 꾸러미는 네가 좀 들어 다오."], ["Nothing would satisfy that good old Mrs.", "아주 조심해서, 떨어뜨리지 말고."], ["Whitaker, but my taking one of the cheeses.", "크림치즈란다, 우리가 저녁에 먹은 그 훌륭한 것하고 꼭 같은 거야."], ["I stood out as long as I could, till the tears almost came into her eyes, and I knew it was just the sort that my sister would be delighted with.", "그 착한 늙은 휘터커 부인이 내가 치즈 하나를 받아 가지 않고는 도무지 만족을 못 하겠다지 뭐냐. 나도 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁해질 때까지 버틸 만큼 버텼단다."], ["That Mrs. Whitaker is a treasure!", "게다가 그게 딱 우리 동생이 좋아할 만한 종류인 걸 알았거든."], ["She was quite shocked when I asked her whether wine was allowed at the second table, and she has turned away two housemaids for wearing white gowns.", "그 휘터커 부인은 정말 보배야! 내가 아랫상에서 포도주가 허용되느냐고 물었더니 어찌나 질겁을 하던지."], ["Take care of the cheese, Fanny.", "게다가 흰 드레스를 입었다고 하녀 둘을 내보냈다더구나."], ["Now I can manage the other parcel and the basket very well.”", "치즈 조심해라, 패니. 이제 나는 다른 꾸러미하고 바구니는 그럭저럭 잘 들 수 있으니.”"]], [["“What else have you been spunging?”", "“또 뭘 우려내셨어요?”"], ["said Maria, half-pleased that Sotherton should be so complimented.", "소더턴이 그토록 칭송받는 게 반쯤은 흐뭇해진 마리아가 말했다."]], [["“Spunging, my dear!", "“우려내다니, 얘야!"], ["It is nothing but four of those beautiful pheasants’ eggs, which Mrs.", "그저 저 예쁜 꿩알 네 개일 뿐이야."], ["Whitaker would quite force upon me: she would not take a denial.", "휘터커 부인이 기어이 나한테 떠안기려 들었거든. 아무리 마다해도 안 듣더라니까."], ["She said it must be such an amusement to me, as she understood I lived quite alone, to have a few living creatures of that sort; and so to be sure it will.", "내가 아주 혼자 사는 걸로 안다면서, 그런 살아 있는 것 몇 마리를 두는 게 나한테는 참 좋은 소일거리가 될 거라고 하더구나. 하기야 정말 그럴 테지."], ["I shall get the dairymaid to set them under the first spare hen, and if they come to good I can have them moved to my own house and borrow a coop; and it will be a great delight to me in my lonely hours to attend to them.", "젖짜기 하녀한테 첫 번째 빈 암탉 밑에 넣어 두라고 시킬 참이야. 잘 자라면 우리 집으로 옮겨다 닭장 하나를 빌려 두면 되고."], ["And if I have good luck, your mother shall have some.”", "외로운 시간에 그것들을 돌보는 게 나한테는 큰 낙이 될 거야. 그리고 운이 좋으면 네 어머니한테도 좀 나눠 주마.”"]], [["It was a beautiful evening, mild and still, and the drive was as pleasant as the serenity of Nature could make it; but when Mrs.", "아름다운 저녁이었다. 포근하고 고요했으며, 마차 길은 자연의 그 잔잔함이 베풀 수 있는 만큼 유쾌했다."], ["Norris ceased speaking, it was altogether a silent drive to those within.", "그러나 노리스 부인이 말을 그치자, 마차 안 사람들에게 그것은 온통 말없는 여정이 되었다."], ["Their spirits were in general exhausted; and to determine whether the day had afforded most pleasure or pain, might occupy the meditations of almost all.", "다들 대체로 기운이 바닥나 있었다. 그리고 그날이 즐거움을 더 안겼는지 아픔을 더 안겼는지를 가리는 일이, 아마 거의 모두의 상념을 차지하고 있었을 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XI", "# 제11장"]], [["The day at Sotherton, with all its imperfections, afforded the Miss Bertrams much more agreeable feelings than were derived from the letters from Antigua, which soon afterwards reached Mansfield.", "소더턴에서 보낸 그날은, 온갖 아쉬움에도 불구하고, 버트럼 자매에게 얼마 뒤 맨스필드에 다다른 앤티가발 편지가 안겨 준 것보다 훨씬 더 흐뭇한 감정을 선사했다."], ["It was much pleasanter to think of Henry Crawford than of their father; and to think of their father in England again within a certain period, which these letters obliged them to do, was a most unwelcome exercise.", "아버지를 생각하기보다 헨리 크로퍼드를 생각하는 편이 한결 즐거웠다. 그리고 이 편지들이 억지로 하게끔 만든 일, 곧 머잖은 어느 시점에 아버지가 다시 잉글랜드에 와 계시리라 생각하는 것은, 더없이 달갑잖은 노릇이었다."]], [["November was the black month fixed for his return.", "십일월이 그가 돌아오기로 정해진 암울한 달이었다."], ["Sir Thomas wrote of it with as much decision as experience and anxiety could authorise.", "토머스 경은 오랜 경험과 노심초사가 허락하는 한 더없이 단호한 어조로 그 일을 적어 보냈다."], ["His business was so nearly concluded as to justify him in proposing to take his passage in the September packet, and he consequently looked forward with the hope of being with his beloved family again early in November.", "그의 볼일은 거의 마무리되어 구월의 우편선으로 배편을 잡을 만하다고 스스로 여길 만했고, 그리하여 그는 십일월 초에는 사랑하는 가족과 다시 함께하리라는 희망을 품고 그날을 손꼽아 기다렸다."]], [["Maria was more to be pitied than Julia; for to her the father brought a husband, and the return of the friend most solicitous for her happiness would unite her to the lover, on whom she had chosen that happiness should depend.", "마리아는 줄리아보다 더 가엾은 처지였다. 그녀에게는 아버지가 남편을 데려오는 셈이었고, 그녀의 행복을 누구보다 마음 쓰는 그 벗이 돌아오면 그녀는 스스로 제 행복을 걸기로 택한 그 연인과 맺어질 것이었기 때문이다."], ["It was a gloomy prospect, and all she could do was to throw a mist over it, and hope when the mist cleared away she should see something else.", "그것은 음울한 앞날이었고, 그녀가 할 수 있는 일이라고는 그 위에 안개를 드리우고서, 안개가 걷히면 뭔가 다른 것이 보이기를 바라는 것뿐이었다. 아무래도 십일월 초는 되기 어려울 터였다."], ["It would hardly be early in November, there were generally delays, a bad passage or something; that favouring something which everybody who shuts their eyes while they look, or their understandings while they reason, feels the comfort of.", "으레 지체되는 일이 있게 마련이고, 항해가 나쁘다든가 무슨 일이 생긴다든가 하니까. 바라보면서도 눈을 감고, 헤아리면서도 지각을 닫아 버리는 사람이면 누구나 위안으로 삼는, 바로 그 편들어 주는 무슨 일 말이다."], ["It would probably be the middle of November at least; the middle of November was three months off.", "아마 적어도 십일월 중순은 되리라. 십일월 중순이면 석 달이나 남았다."], ["Three months comprised thirteen weeks.", "석 달이면 열세 주였다."], ["Much might happen in thirteen weeks.", "열세 주 사이엔 많은 일이 일어날 수 있었다."]], [["Sir Thomas would have been deeply mortified by a suspicion of half that his daughters felt on the subject of his return, and would hardly have found consolation in a knowledge of the interest it excited in the breast of another young lady.", "토머스 경은 제 딸들이 그의 귀환을 두고 느끼는 심경의 절반만 짐작했더라도 깊이 상심했을 것이며, 그 소식이 또 다른 젊은 아가씨의 가슴에 불러일으킨 관심을 알았던들 좀처럼 위안을 얻지 못했으리라. 크로퍼드 양은 오라비와 함께 맨스필드 파크로 저녁을 보내러 걸어 올라왔다가 그 반가운 소식을 들었다."], ["Miss Crawford, on walking up with her brother to spend the evening at Mansfield Park, heard the good news; and though seeming to have no concern in the affair beyond politeness, and to have vented all her feelings in a quiet congratulation, heard it with an attention not so easily satisfied.", "그리고 겉으로는 예의를 차리는 것 말고는 그 일에 아무 관심도 없는 양, 조용한 축하 한마디로 제 감정을 죄다 쏟아 낸 양 보였으나, 실은 그리 쉽게 채워지지 않는 주의를 기울여 그 소식을 들었다. 노리스 부인이 편지의 자초지종을 늘어놓았고, 화제는 그것으로 끝났다."], ["Mrs. Norris gave the particulars of the letters, and the subject was dropt; but after tea, as Miss Crawford was standing at an open window with Edmund and Fanny looking out on a twilight scene, while the Miss Bertrams, Mr.", "그러나 차를 마신 뒤, 버트럼 자매와 러시워스 씨와 헨리 크로퍼드가 다들 피아노 곁에서 촛불을 매만지느라 분주한 동안, 크로퍼드 양은 에드먼드와 패니와 함께 열린 창가에 서서 땅거미 지는 풍경을 내다보다가, 문득 그 무리 쪽으로 돌아서며 이렇게 말해 화제를 되살렸다."], ["Rushworth, and Henry Crawford were all busy with candles at the pianoforte, she suddenly revived it by turning round towards the group, and saying, “How happy Mr. Rushworth looks!", "“러시워스 씨가 어쩜 저리 행복해 보일까요!"], ["He is thinking of November.”", "십일월을 생각하고 있나 봐요.”"]], [["Edmund looked round at Mr. Rushworth too, but had nothing to say.", "에드먼드도 러시워스 씨를 돌아보았으나, 딱히 할 말이 없었다."]], [["“Your father’s return will be a very interesting event.”", "“아버님의 귀환은 무척 뜻깊은 일이 되겠지요.”"]], [["“It will, indeed, after such an absence; an absence not only long, but including so many dangers.”", "“정말 그럴 거예요, 그토록 오래 떠나 계셨으니. 오래일 뿐 아니라 그 숱한 위험까지 무릅쓴 부재였으니 말이에요.”"]], [["“It will be the forerunner also of other interesting events: your sister’s marriage, and your taking orders.”", "“게다가 그것은 또 다른 뜻깊은 일들의 전조이기도 하겠고요. 누이분의 혼인이며, 씨가 서품 받으시는 일 말이에요.”"]], [["“Yes.”", "“그렇지요.”"]], [["“Don’t be affronted,”", "“기분 상해하진 마세요.” 그녀가 웃으며 말했다."], ["said she, laughing, “but it does put me in mind of some of the old heathen heroes, who, after performing great exploits in a foreign land, offered sacrifices to the gods on their safe return.”", "“그런데 이 일을 보니 옛 이교의 영웅들이 떠오르네요. 이역에서 큰 공을 세우고는, 무사히 돌아온 것을 두고 신들께 제물을 바치던 그 사람들 말이에요.”"]], [["“There is no sacrifice in the case,”", "“이 경우엔 제물이랄 게 없는걸요.”"], ["replied Edmund, with a serious smile, and glancing at the pianoforte again; “it is entirely her own doing.”", "에드먼드가 진지한 미소를 띠고, 다시 피아노 쪽을 흘긋 보며 대꾸했다. “그건 온전히 누이 스스로 택한 일이니까요.”"]], [["“Oh yes I know it is.", "“아 네, 저도 알아요."], ["I was merely joking.", "그냥 농담한 거예요."], ["She has done no more than what every young woman would do; and I have no doubt of her being extremely happy.", "누이분은 어느 젊은 여자라도 할 법한 일을 했을 뿐이고, 더없이 행복하시리라는 걸 저도 믿어 의심치 않아요."], ["My other sacrifice, of course, you do not understand.”", "제가 말한 또 하나의 제물이야, 물론 씨는 못 알아들으시겠지만요.”"]], [["“My taking orders, I assure you, is quite as voluntary as Maria’s marrying.”", "“제가 서품 받는 것도, 정말이지 마리아가 시집가는 것 못지않게 제 뜻으로 하는 일이랍니다.”"]], [["“It is fortunate that your inclination and your father’s convenience should accord so well.", "“씨의 마음과 아버님의 형편이 그토록 잘 들어맞다니 다행이네요."], ["There is a very good living kept for you, I understand, hereabouts.”", "듣자 하니 이 근방에 씨를 위해 마련해 둔 아주 좋은 성직록이 있다면서요.”"]], [["“Which you suppose has biassed me?”", "“그것이 제 마음을 기울게 했으리라 여기시는 거군요?”"]], [["“But that I am sure it has not,”", "“하지만 그건 결코 아니에요, 저는 확신해요.”"], ["cried Fanny.", "패니가 외쳤다."]], [["“Thank you for your good word, Fanny, but it is more than I would affirm myself.", "“고맙구나, 패니, 그리 말해 주다니. 하지만 그건 나 스스로도 단언할 수 있는 것 이상이란다."], ["On the contrary, the knowing that there was such a provision for me probably did bias me.", "오히려 그런 성직록이 나를 위해 마련돼 있음을 안 것이 아마도 내 마음을 기울게 했겠지."], ["Nor can I think it wrong that it should.", "그리고 나는 그게 그른 일이라고도 생각지 않아."], ["There was no natural disinclination to be overcome, and I see no reason why a man should make a worse clergyman for knowing that he will have a competence early in life.", "애초에 이겨 내야 할 천성의 거리낌이랄 게 없었고, 젊어서 넉넉한 살림이 보장돼 있음을 안다고 해서 사내가 더 못난 성직자가 되어야 할 까닭도 나로선 모르겠어. 나는 안전한 손에 맡겨져 있었던 거야."], ["I was in safe hands. I hope I should not have been influenced myself in a wrong way, and I am sure my father was too conscientious to have allowed it.", "나 스스로 그릇된 쪽으로 휘둘리지 않았기를 바라고, 또 우리 아버지께서 그것을 용납하시기엔 너무나 양심 곧은 분이심을 나는 확신해."], ["I have no doubt that I was biased, but I think it was blamelessly.”", "내가 마음이 기운 것은 틀림없지만, 그건 나무랄 데 없이 그런 것이라 생각한다.”"]], [["“It is the same sort of thing,”", "“그건 이런 것과 똑같은 일이에요.”"], ["said Fanny, after a short pause, “as for the son of an admiral to go into the navy, or the son of a general to be in the army, and nobody sees anything wrong in that.", "패니가 잠깐 뜸을 들인 뒤 말했다. “제독의 아들이 해군에 들어가거나, 장군의 아들이 육군에 몸담는 것과요."], ["Nobody wonders that they should prefer the line where their friends can serve them best, or suspects them to be less in earnest in it than they appear.”", "그런데 아무도 거기서 잘못을 보지 않잖아요. 아무도 그들이 제 벗들이 가장 잘 이끌어 줄 수 있는 길을 택하는 것을 이상히 여기지 않고, 겉보기만큼 진심이 아니지 않을까 의심하지도 않고요.”"]], [["“No, my dear Miss Price, and for reasons good.", "“아니에요, 프라이스 양, 게다가 그럴 만한 까닭이 있죠."], ["The profession, either navy or army, is its own justification.", "해군이든 육군이든 그 직업은 그 자체로 정당함을 지녀요."], ["It has everything in its favour: heroism, danger, bustle, fashion.", "온갖 것이 그 편이거든요—영웅다움, 위험, 부산함, 세련됨 말이에요."], ["Soldiers and sailors are always acceptable in society.", "군인과 뱃사람은 언제든 사교계에서 환영받지요."], ["Nobody can wonder that men are soldiers and sailors.”", "사내가 군인이 되고 뱃사람이 되는 것을 아무도 이상히 여길 수 없어요.”"]], [["“But the motives of a man who takes orders with the certainty of preferment may be fairly suspected, you think?”", "“하지만 승진이 확실한 채로 서품 받는 사내의 동기는 얼마든지 의심받아 마땅하다, 이 말씀이신가요?” 에드먼드가 말했다."], ["said Edmund. “To be justified in your eyes, he must do it in the most complete uncertainty of any provision.”", "“크로퍼드 양의 눈에 떳떳하려면, 그는 아무 성직록도 기약되지 않은 더없이 막막한 처지에서 그 일을 해야 한다는 거군요.”"]], [["“What!", "“뭐라고요!"], ["take orders without a living!", "성직록도 없이 서품을 받는다고요!"], ["No; that is madness indeed; absolute madness.”", "안 되죠, 그거야말로 정말 미친 짓이에요. 순전한 광기죠.”"]], [["“Shall I ask you how the church is to be filled, if a man is neither to take orders with a living nor without?", "“그럼 여쭤볼까요, 사내가 성직록을 지니고도 서품을 못 받고 성직록 없이도 못 받는다면, 교회는 대체 무엇으로 채운답니까? 아니, 그만두죠."], ["No; for you certainly would not know what to say.", "크로퍼드 양은 분명 뭐라 답해야 할지 모르실 테니까요."], ["But I must beg some advantage to the clergyman from your own argument.", "하지만 저는 크로퍼드 양 자신의 논리에서 성직자에게 유리한 점 하나는 얻어 내야겠어요."], ["As he cannot be influenced by those feelings which you rank highly as temptation and reward to the soldier and sailor in their choice of a profession, as heroism, and noise, and fashion, are all against him, he ought to be less liable to the suspicion of wanting sincerity or good intentions in the choice of his.”", "성직자는 크로퍼드 양이 군인과 뱃사람이 직업을 고를 때의 유혹이자 보상이라며 높이 치는 그런 것들—영웅다움이며 떠들썩함이며 세련됨—에 이끌릴 수가 없고, 오히려 그 모두가 그에게 불리하니, 그런 만큼 그는 제 직업을 고르면서 진심이나 선한 뜻이 모자라지 않느냐는 의심을 덜 받아 마땅하지요.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“오!"], ["no doubt he is very sincere in preferring an income ready made, to the trouble of working for one; and has the best intentions of doing nothing all the rest of his days but eat, drink, and grow fat.", "물론 그는 애써 벌어야 하는 수고보다 이미 마련된 수입을 마다 않을 만큼 진심이겠죠. 그리고 남은 평생 먹고 마시고 살찌는 것 말고는 아무것도 안 하려는 더없이 갸륵한 뜻을 품었을 테고요."], ["It is indolence, Mr. Bertram, indeed.", "그건 게으름이에요, 버트럼 씨, 정말이지."], ["Indolence and love of ease; a want of all laudable ambition, of taste for good company, or of inclination to take the trouble of being agreeable, which make men clergymen.", "사내를 성직자로 만드는 건 게으름과 안락에 대한 사랑, 갸륵한 야심의 결여, 좋은 벗을 사귈 취향의 결여, 또는 남을 즐겁게 해 주는 수고를 감내할 마음의 결여랍니다."], ["A clergyman has nothing to do but be slovenly and selfish—read the newspaper, watch the weather, and quarrel with his wife.", "성직자란 게을러 빠지고 제 잇속만 차리면 그만이죠—신문이나 읽고, 날씨나 살피고, 아내와 다투면서요."], ["His curate does all the work, and the business of his own life is to dine.”", "궂은일은 죄다 그의 부목사가 하고, 정작 그 자신의 소임이라곤 만찬을 드는 것뿐이랍니다.”"]], [["“There are such clergymen, no doubt, but I think they are not so common as to justify Miss Crawford in esteeming it their general character.", "“그런 성직자가 있는 건 틀림없지요. 하지만 그런 이들이, 크로퍼드 양이 그것을 성직자 일반의 성품으로 여겨도 좋을 만큼 흔하지는 않다고 봐요."], ["I suspect that in this comprehensive and (may I say) commonplace censure, you are not judging from yourself, but from prejudiced persons, whose opinions you have been in the habit of hearing.", "이 폭넓은—그리고, 이런 말씀 드려도 될는지, 뻔한—비난에서 크로퍼드 양은 스스로 판단하시는 게 아니라, 늘 그 의견을 들어 오던 편견에 찬 사람들에게서 판단하시는 게 아닌가 싶어요."], ["It is impossible that your own observation can have given you much knowledge of the clergy.", "크로퍼드 양 자신의 관찰이 성직자에 대해 그리 많은 앎을 주었을 리가 없거든요."], ["You can have been personally acquainted with very few of a set of men you condemn so conclusively.", "그토록 단정 지어 단죄하시는 그 부류의 사내들 가운데, 실제로 친분이 있는 이는 아주 적을 수밖에 없으실 테니까요."], ["You are speaking what you have been told at your uncle’s table.”", "크로퍼드 양은 숙부님 식탁에서 들은 이야기를 하고 계신 거예요.”"]], [["“I speak what appears to me the general opinion; and where an opinion is general, it is usually correct.", "“저는 제게 세상의 통념으로 여겨지는 바를 말하는 거예요. 그리고 어떤 의견이 두루 퍼져 있다면, 그건 대개 옳지요."], ["Though I have not seen much of the domestic lives of clergymen, it is seen by too many to leave any deficiency of information.”", "저야 성직자의 가정생활을 그리 많이 보지 못했지만, 그건 너무나 많은 이들이 지켜보는 터라 정보가 모자랄 일이 없거든요.”"]], [["“Where any one body of educated men, of whatever denomination, are condemned indiscriminately, there must be a deficiency of information, or (smiling) of something else.", "“교육받은 사내들의 어떤 집단이든, 그 종파가 무엇이건, 마구잡이로 단죄받는 곳이라면, 거기엔 정보가 모자라거나, 아니면” 하고 그는 미소 지으며 말을 이었다."], ["Your uncle, and his brother admirals, perhaps knew little of clergymen beyond the chaplains whom, good or bad, they were always wishing away.”", "“다른 무언가가 모자란 게 틀림없어요. 크로퍼드 양의 숙부님과 그 동료 제독들이야, 좋든 나쁘든 늘 눈앞에서 치워 버리고만 싶어 하던 군종 신부들 말고는, 성직자에 대해 아마 아는 게 거의 없으셨을 테고요.”"]], [["“Poor William!", "“가엾은 윌리엄 오빠!"], ["He has met with great kindness from the chaplain of the Antwerp,”", "오빠는 앤트워프호의 군종 신부님께 큰 은혜를 입었는걸요.”"], ["was a tender apostrophe of Fanny’s, very much to the purpose of her own feelings if not of the conversation.", "이것은 패니가 나직이 내뱉은 다정한 탄사로, 오가던 이야기와는 몰라도 제 마음결에는 더없이 잘 들어맞는 말이었다."]], [["“I have been so little addicted to take my opinions from my uncle,”", "“저는 숙부에게서 제 의견을 얻는 버릇이 워낙 없는 터라,” 크로퍼드 양이 말했다."], ["said Miss Crawford, “that I can hardly suppose—and since you push me so hard, I must observe, that I am not entirely without the means of seeing what clergymen are, being at this present time the guest of my own brother, Dr.", "“그렇게 짐작하실 일은 좀처럼—그리고 씨가 저를 그토록 몰아붙이시니, 한마디 하지 않을 수 없네요. 저라고 성직자가 어떤 사람인지 볼 방도가 아주 없는 건 아니랍니다."], ["Grant. And though Dr.", "지금 이 순간 바로 제 오라비인 그랜트 박사의 식객으로 있으니까요."], ["Grant is most kind and obliging to me, and though he is really a gentleman, and, I dare say, a good scholar and clever, and often preaches good sermons, and is very respectable, I see him to be an indolent, selfish bon vivant, who must have his palate consulted in everything; who will not stir a finger for the convenience of any one; and who, moreover, if the cook makes a blunder, is out of humour with his excellent wife.", "그랜트 박사가 제게 더없이 다정하고 살갑고, 또 정말 신사이며, 감히 말하건대 훌륭한 학자에다 영리하고, 좋은 설교를 곧잘 하며, 아주 존경받을 만한 분이긴 하지만, 제가 보기에 그이는 게으르고 잇속만 차리는 식도락가예요. 매사에 제 입맛부터 챙겨야 하고, 남의 편의를 위해서는 손가락 하나 까딱 않으며, 게다가 요리사가 실수라도 하면 그 훌륭한 아내에게 심통을 부리는 사람이죠."], ["To own the truth, Henry and I were partly driven out this very evening by a disappointment about a green goose, which he could not get the better of.", "사실대로 말하면, 헨리와 제가 바로 오늘 저녁 이리로 밀려나다시피 한 것도, 그이가 영 삭이지 못한 새끼 거위 요리에 대한 실망 탓이 얼마간 있어요."], ["My poor sister was forced to stay and bear it.”", "가엾은 언니는 남아서 그걸 고스란히 견뎌야 했고요.”"]], [["“I do not wonder at your disapprobation, upon my word.", "“저런, 크로퍼드 양이 못마땅해하시는 것도 정말 무리가 아니네요."], ["It is a great defect of temper, made worse by a very faulty habit of self-indulgence; and to see your sister suffering from it must be exceedingly painful to such feelings as yours.", "그건 성미의 큰 결점이고, 제멋대로 구는 몹쓸 버릇 탓에 한층 나빠진 것이죠. 그리고 언니께서 그 때문에 시달리는 걸 지켜보는 일이, 크로퍼드 양 같은 심성에는 더없이 괴로울 게 틀림없고요."], ["Fanny, it goes against us.", "패니야, 이건 우리한테 불리하구나."], ["We cannot attempt to defend Dr. Grant.”", "우리로선 그랜트 박사를 감싸 보려야 감쌀 수가 없겠어.”"]], [["“No,”", "“그렇긴 해요.”"], ["replied Fanny, “but we need not give up his profession for all that; because, whatever profession Dr.", "패니가 대답했다. “하지만 그렇다고 그분의 직업까지 두둔하기를 그만둘 필요는 없어요."], ["Grant had chosen, he would have taken a—not a good temper into it; and as he must, either in the navy or army, have had a great many more people under his command than he has now, I think more would have been made unhappy by him as a sailor or soldier than as a clergyman.", "그랜트 박사가 어떤 직업을 택하셨든, 그분은 거기에 그—좋다고는 할 수 없는 성미를 지니고 들어가셨을 테니까요. 그리고 해군이든 육군이든 거기서라면 지금보다 훨씬 많은 사람을 제 밑에 두셨을 테니, 저는 그분이 뱃사람이나 군인이었더라면 성직자일 때보다 더 많은 이를 불행하게 만드셨으리라 생각해요."], ["Besides, I cannot but suppose that whatever there may be to wish otherwise in Dr.", "게다가 그랜트 박사에게 달리 됐으면 싶은 구석이 무엇이 있든, 그건 더 분주하고 세속적인 직업에서라면 한층 나빠질 위험이 컸으리라고 저는 짐작하지 않을 수 없어요."], ["Grant would have been in a greater danger of becoming worse in a more active and worldly profession, where he would have had less time and obligation—where he might have escaped that knowledge of himself, the frequency, at least, of that knowledge which it is impossible he should escape as he is now. A man—a sensible man like Dr.", "그런 데서라면 시간도 의무도 더 적었을 테고—지금의 처지에선 도저히 피할 수 없는 그 자기 성찰을, 적어도 그 잦은 성찰을, 그분은 거기서라면 피해 버릴 수도 있었을 테니까요."], ["Grant, cannot be in the habit of teaching others their duty every week, cannot go to church twice every Sunday, and preach such very good sermons in so good a manner as he does, without being the better for it himself.", "사내가—그랜트 박사처럼 지각 있는 사내가, 주마다 남들에게 그 도리를 가르치고, 주일마다 두 번 교회에 나가, 그토록 좋은 설교를 그토록 훌륭한 태도로 하면서, 정작 자신은 조금도 나아지지 않을 수는 없어요."], ["It must make him think; and I have no doubt that he oftener endeavours to restrain himself than he would if he had been anything but a clergyman.”", "그건 그분을 생각하게 만들 게 틀림없고요. 그리고 저는 그분이 성직자 아닌 다른 무엇이었을 때보다, 지금 더 자주 스스로를 다잡으려 애쓰시리라 믿어 의심치 않아요.”"]], [["“We cannot prove to the contrary, to be sure; but I wish you a better fate, Miss Price, than to be the wife of a man whose amiableness depends upon his own sermons; for though he may preach himself into a good-humour every Sunday, it will be bad enough to have him quarrelling about green geese from Monday morning till Saturday night.”", "“그 반대를 증명할 길이야 물론 없지요. 하지만 프라이스 양, 저는 양에게 그보다 나은 운명을 빌어 드리고 싶어요. 제 설교에 기대어야 붙임성이 생기는 사내의 아내가 되는 것보다는요. 그런 사내야 주일마다 설교로 제 기분을 좋게 돋운다 한들, 월요일 아침부터 토요일 밤까지 새끼 거위 타령으로 다투고 있을 테니, 그것만으로도 충분히 고약하잖아요.”"]], [["“I think the man who could often quarrel with Fanny,”", "“패니와 자주 다툴 수 있는 사내라면,” 에드먼드가 다정하게 말했다."], ["said Edmund affectionately, “must be beyond the reach of any sermons.”", "“어떤 설교로도 어찌해 볼 수 없는 위인일 게 틀림없어요.”"]], [["Fanny turned farther into the window; and Miss Crawford had only time to say, in a pleasant manner, “I fancy Miss Price has been more used to deserve praise than to hear it”; when, being earnestly invited by the Miss Bertrams to join in a glee, she tripped off to the instrument, leaving Edmund looking after her in an ecstasy of admiration of all her many virtues, from her obliging manners down to her light and graceful tread.", "패니는 창 쪽으로 더 깊이 몸을 돌렸다. 그리고 크로퍼드 양은 상냥한 투로 “프라이스 양은 칭찬을 듣는 것보다 칭찬받을 만한 일을 하는 데 더 익숙한가 봐요.” 한마디 할 짬밖에 없었으니, 버트럼 자매가 합창에 어울리자고 간곡히 청하는 통에, 그녀는 총총히 악기 쪽으로 걸어갔고, 에드먼드는 그 뒤를 바라보며 그녀의 그 숱한 미덕에—살가운 몸가짐에서부터 가볍고도 우아한 발걸음에 이르기까지—넋을 잃고 감탄해 마지않았다."]], [["“There goes good-humour, I am sure,”", "“저기 좋은 기질이 가는구나, 나는 확신해.”"], ["said he presently.", "이윽고 그가 말했다."], ["“There goes a temper which would never give pain!", "“저기 결코 남을 아프게 하지 않을 성미가 가고 있어!"], ["How well she walks!", "걸음걸이가 어쩜 저리 고울까!"], ["and how readily she falls in with the inclination of others!", "게다가 남의 뜻에 어쩜 저리 선뜻 맞춰 줄까!"], ["joining them the moment she is asked.", "청하자마자 곧장 어울려 주다니."], ["What a pity,”", "참으로 안타깝구나.”"], ["he added, after an instant’s reflection, “that she should have been in such hands!”", "그는 잠깐 생각에 잠겼다가 덧붙였다. “저 사람이 그런 손에 자라 왔다는 게 말이야!”"]], [["Fanny agreed to it, and had the pleasure of seeing him continue at the window with her, in spite of the expected glee; and of having his eyes soon turned, like hers, towards the scene without, where all that was solemn, and soothing, and lovely, appeared in the brilliancy of an unclouded night, and the contrast of the deep shade of the woods.", "패니도 그렇다고 맞장구쳤고, 그토록 기다리던 합창에도 아랑곳없이 그가 그녀와 함께 창가에 그대로 머무는 것을, 그리고 이내 그의 눈길이 그녀의 눈길처럼 바깥 풍경으로 향하는 것을 흐뭇이 지켜보았다. 그 풍경에는 엄숙하고 마음을 다독이고 사랑스러운 온갖 것이, 구름 한 점 없는 밤의 눈부심 속에, 깊은 숲 그늘과 대비를 이루며 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["Fanny spoke her feelings.", "패니가 제 감정을 입에 담았다."], ["“Here’s harmony!”", "“여기 조화가 있어요!”"], ["said she; “here’s repose!", "그녀가 말했다. “여기 안식이 있어요!"], ["Here’s what may leave all painting and all music behind, and what poetry only can attempt to describe!", "여기, 온갖 그림과 온갖 음악을 뒤로 제쳐 둘 만한 것, 오직 시만이 그려 보려 애쓸 수 있는 것이 있어요!"], ["Here’s what may tranquillise every care, and lift the heart to rapture!", "여기, 온갖 근심을 잠재우고 마음을 환희로 들어 올릴 만한 것이 있어요!"], ["When I look out on such a night as this, I feel as if there could be neither wickedness nor sorrow in the world; and there certainly would be less of both if the sublimity of Nature were more attended to, and people were carried more out of themselves by contemplating such a scene.”", "이런 밤을 내다보고 있으면, 저는 세상에 악도 슬픔도 있을 수 없을 것만 같아요. 그리고 만일 사람들이 자연의 숭고함에 좀 더 마음을 기울이고, 이런 풍경을 바라보며 자기 자신에게서 좀 더 벗어날 수 있다면, 악도 슬픔도 분명 지금보다 줄어들 거예요.”"]], [["“I like to hear your enthusiasm, Fanny.", "“네 그런 열정을 듣는 게 나는 좋구나, 패니."], ["It is a lovely night, and they are much to be pitied who have not been taught to feel, in some degree, as you do; who have not, at least, been given a taste for Nature in early life.", "사랑스러운 밤이야. 그리고 어느 정도라도 너처럼 느끼도록 배우지 못한 이들, 적어도 어려서 자연을 사랑하는 취향을 얻지 못한 이들은 참으로 가여운 사람들이지."], ["They lose a great deal.”", "그들은 아주 많은 것을 잃는 거란다.”"]], [["“You taught me to think and feel on the subject, cousin.”", "“오빠가 저에게 그 주제를 두고 생각하고 느끼는 법을 가르쳐 주셨는걸요.”"]], [["“I had a very apt scholar.", "“나야 아주 총명한 제자를 두었던 거지."], ["There’s Arcturus looking very bright.”", "저기 아르크투루스가 무척 밝게 빛나는구나.”"]], [["“Yes, and the Bear.", "“네, 그리고 큰곰자리도요."], ["I wish I could see Cassiopeia.”", "카시오페이아를 볼 수 있으면 좋겠어요.”"]], [["“We must go out on the lawn for that.", "“그러려면 잔디밭으로 나가야 해."], ["Should you be afraid?”", "무섭진 않겠니?”"]], [["“Not in the least.", "“조금도요."], ["It is a great while since we have had any star-gazing.”", "우리가 별 구경을 한 지도 참 오래됐네요.”"]], [["“Yes; I do not know how it has happened.”", "“그래, 어쩌다 그리됐는지 모르겠구나.”"], ["The glee began.", "합창이 시작되었다."], ["“We will stay till this is finished, Fanny,”", "“이게 끝날 때까지만 있자꾸나, 패니.” 그가 창에 등을 돌리며 말했다."], ["said he, turning his back on the window; and as it advanced, she had the mortification of seeing him advance too, moving forward by gentle degrees towards the instrument, and when it ceased, he was close by the singers, among the most urgent in requesting to hear the glee again.", "그리고 노래가 무르익어 갈수록, 그녀는 그 또한 슬금슬금 다가가는 것을, 조금씩 조금씩 악기 쪽으로 나아가는 것을 지켜보는 쓰라림을 맛보았다. 노래가 멎었을 때 그는 이미 가수들 바로 곁에 서서, 그 합창을 한 번 더 듣자고 누구보다 간곡히 조르는 축에 끼어 있었다."]], [["Fanny sighed alone at the window till scolded away by Mrs. Norris’s threats of catching cold.", "패니는 창가에서 홀로 한숨지었고, 마침내 감기 든다는 노리스 부인의 으름장에 등 떠밀려 그 자리를 떠났다."]], [["# CHAPTER XII", "# 제12장"]], [["Sir Thomas was to return in November, and his eldest son had duties to call him earlier home.", "토머스 경은 십일월에 돌아오기로 되어 있었으나, 그의 맏아들은 볼일이 있어 그보다 일찍 집으로 불려 오게 되었다."], ["The approach of September brought tidings of Mr.", "구월이 다가오자 버트럼 씨의 소식이 전해졌는데, 처음에는 사냥터지기에게 보낸 편지로, 다음에는 에드먼드에게 보낸 편지로였다."], ["Bertram, first in a letter to the gamekeeper and then in a letter to Edmund; and by the end of August he arrived himself, to be gay, agreeable, and gallant again as occasion served, or Miss Crawford demanded; to tell of races and Weymouth, and parties and friends, to which she might have listened six weeks before with some interest, and altogether to give her the fullest conviction, by the power of actual comparison, of her preferring his younger brother.", "그리고 팔월이 저물 무렵 그가 몸소 도착하여, 자리가 마련되거나 크로퍼드 양이 청하는 대로 다시금 명랑하고 상냥하고 늠름하게 굴었다. 경마며 웨이머스며, 여러 모임과 벗들 이야기를 늘어놓았는데, 그것은 육 주 전이었더라면 그녀가 얼마간 흥미를 갖고 들었을 법한 것이었으나, 이제는 실제로 견주어 볼 힘 덕에 그녀가 그 아우를 더 좋아함을 더없이 확실히 굳혀 줄 따름이었다."]], [["It was very vexatious, and she was heartily sorry for it; but so it was; and so far from now meaning to marry the elder, she did not even want to attract him beyond what the simplest claims of conscious beauty required: his lengthened absence from Mansfield, without anything but pleasure in view, and his own will to consult, made it perfectly clear that he did not care about her; and his indifference was so much more than equalled by her own, that were he now to step forth the owner of Mansfield Park, the Sir Thomas complete, which he was to be in time, she did not believe she could accept him.", "그것은 여간 짜증스러운 일이 아니었고, 그녀는 진심으로 그 일이 유감스러웠다. 하지만 사실이 그러했다. 그리고 이제 그 형과 혼인할 마음이 없기는커녕, 그녀는 제 미모를 아는 사람이라면 으레 지니는 더없이 소박한 자존심이 요구하는 것 이상으로 그를 끌어당기고 싶은 마음조차 없었다. 딱히 즐길 거리밖에는 아무런 볼일도 없이, 오로지 제 뜻만 좇아 그토록 오래 맨스필드를 비운 것으로 미루어, 그가 그녀에게 마음이 없음은 더없이 분명했다. 그리고 그 무심함은 그녀 자신의 무심함으로 넘치도록 되갚아지고 있었으니, 설령 그가 지금 당장 맨스필드 파크의 주인으로, 머잖아 그리될 완전한 토머스 경으로 나선다 한들, 그녀는 자신이 그를 받아들일 수 있으리라고 믿지 않았다."]], [["The season and duties which brought Mr.", "버트럼 씨를 맨스필드로 도로 데려온 그 철과 볼일이, 크로퍼드 씨를 노퍽으로 데려갔다."], ["Bertram back to Mansfield took Mr.", "에버링엄은 구월 초에 그가 없어서는 안 될 형편이었다."], ["Crawford into Norfolk. Everingham could not do without him in the beginning of September.", "그는 두 주 예정으로 떠났다—그것은 버트럼 자매 둘 다 마땅히 경계심을 품었어야 할, 그리고 언니를 시샘하던 줄리아마저 그의 살가움을 미덥잖게 여기고 그가 돌아오지 않기를 바라는 것이 더없이 마땅함을 인정하게 했어야 할, 그런 지루한 두 주였다."], ["He went for a fortnight—a fortnight of such dullness to the Miss Bertrams as ought to have put them both on their guard, and made even Julia admit, in her jealousy of her sister, the absolute necessity of distrusting his attentions, and wishing him not to return; and a fortnight of sufficient leisure, in the intervals of shooting and sleeping, to have convinced the gentleman that he ought to keep longer away, had he been more in the habit of examining his own motives, and of reflecting to what the indulgence of his idle vanity was tending; but, thoughtless and selfish from prosperity and bad example, he would not look beyond the present moment.", "또한 그것은, 그가 제 동기를 살피고 제 게으른 허영을 방치하는 것이 무엇으로 치닫는지 곰곰이 되짚어 보는 버릇이 좀 더 있었더라면, 사냥과 잠자기 사이사이의 짬만으로도, 자신이 좀 더 오래 떨어져 있어야 함을 깨닫기에 넉넉한 여가이기도 했다. 그러나 넉넉한 살림과 나쁜 본보기 탓에 무심하고 잇속만 차리는 그는, 지금 이 순간 너머를 내다보려 하지 않았다."], ["The sisters, handsome, clever, and encouraging, were an amusement to his sated mind; and finding nothing in Norfolk to equal the social pleasures of Mansfield, he gladly returned to it at the time appointed, and was welcomed thither quite as gladly by those whom he came to trifle with further.", "아름답고 영리하며 부추기기까지 하는 그 자매는, 물릴 대로 물린 그의 마음에 심심풀이가 되어 주었다. 그리고 노퍽에서는 맨스필드의 사교적 즐거움에 맞먹을 만한 것을 도무지 찾지 못하자, 그는 흔쾌히 정해진 때에 그리로 돌아왔고, 그가 더 희롱해 볼 작정으로 다가간 이들에게서 꼭 그만큼 흔쾌한 환대를 받았다."]], [["Maria, with only Mr. Rushworth to attend to her, and doomed to the repeated details of his day’s sport, good or bad, his boast of his dogs, his jealousy of his neighbours, his doubts of their qualifications, and his zeal after poachers, subjects which will not find their way to female feelings without some talent on one side or some attachment on the other, had missed Mr.", "마리아는 곁에 러시워스 씨밖에 없었고, 그날 사냥이 잘됐네 못됐네 하는 시시콜콜한 이야기며, 제 개들에 대한 자랑이며, 이웃들에 대한 시샘이며, 그들의 자격을 미심쩍어하는 소리며, 밀렵꾼을 잡겠다는 열의를 되풀이해 들어야 하는 신세였는데—이런 화제란 말하는 쪽에 얼마간 재주가 있거나 듣는 쪽에 얼마간 정이 있지 않고서는 여자의 마음에 좀처럼 파고들지 못하는 법이라—그녀는 크로퍼드 씨를 사무치게 그리워했다."], ["Crawford grievously; and Julia, unengaged and unemployed, felt all the right of missing him much more. Each sister believed herself the favourite.", "그리고 매인 데도 없고 할 일도 없는 줄리아는, 그를 그리워할 권리를 한층 더 온전히 누렸다."], ["Julia might be justified in so doing by the hints of Mrs.", "자매는 저마다 제가 그의 마음에 든 쪽이라 믿었다."], ["Grant, inclined to credit what she wished, and Maria by the hints of Mr. Crawford himself.", "줄리아는 제가 바라는 바를 곧이곧대로 믿고 싶어 하는 그랜트 부인의 넌지시 건네는 말에 그리 믿을 만한 구석이 있었고, 마리아는 크로퍼드 씨 자신이 넌지시 건네는 말에 그러했다."], ["Everything returned into the same channel as before his absence; his manners being to each so animated and agreeable as to lose no ground with either, and just stopping short of the consistence, the steadiness, the solicitude, and the warmth which might excite general notice.", "모든 것이 그가 떠나기 전과 똑같은 물길로 되돌아왔다. 그의 태도가 두 사람 저마다에게 어찌나 활기차고 상냥하던지 어느 쪽의 마음도 잃지 않으면서도, 뭇사람의 눈길을 끌 만한 한결같음이며 진득함이며 지극한 정성이며 뜨거움에는 딱 미치지 못한 채 멈추어 있었다."]], [["Fanny was the only one of the party who found anything to dislike; but since the day at Sotherton, she could never see Mr.", "이 무리 가운데 못마땅한 구석을 찾아낸 사람은 패니뿐이었다. 소더턴에서 보낸 그날 이후로, 그녀는 크로퍼드 씨가 두 자매 중 누구와 함께 있는 것을 눈여겨보지 않고서는 볼 수 없었고, 미심쩍어하거나 나무라는 마음 없이 보는 일도 드물었다."], ["Crawford with either sister without observation, and seldom without wonder or censure; and had her confidence in her own judgment been equal to her exercise of it in every other respect, had she been sure that she was seeing clearly, and judging candidly, she would probably have made some important communications to her usual confidant.", "그리고 만일 그녀가 다른 모든 일에서 제 판단을 행사하는 만큼 그 판단에 자신이 있었더라면, 자신이 똑똑히 보고 있고 공정히 헤아리고 있음을 확신했더라면, 그녀는 아마 늘 속내를 털어놓던 사람에게 뭔가 중요한 이야기를 건넸을 것이다. 그러나 실은 넌지시 한마디 흘려 보는 것에 그쳤고, 그 말은 흘려지고 말았다."], ["As it was, however, she only hazarded a hint, and the hint was lost.", "“저는 좀 놀랐어요.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["“I am rather surprised,” said she, “that Mr.", "“크로퍼드 씨가 이렇게 빨리 다시 돌아온 게요."], ["Crawford should come back again so soon, after being here so long before, full seven weeks; for I had understood he was so very fond of change and moving about, that I thought something would certainly occur, when he was once gone, to take him elsewhere.", "앞서 이곳에 그토록 오래, 꼬박 일곱 주나 머물다 갔으면서 말이에요. 저는 그분이 변화와 여기저기 돌아다니는 걸 워낙 좋아하시는 줄 알았거든요."], ["He is used to much gayer places than Mansfield.”", "그래서 그분이 일단 떠나고 나면 분명 무슨 일이 생겨 다른 데로 이끌려 가시리라 생각했어요. 그분은 맨스필드보다 훨씬 흥청거리는 데에 익숙하시잖아요.”"]], [["“It is to his credit,”", "“그거야 그 사람한테 좋은 일이지.”"], ["was Edmund’s answer; “and I dare say it gives his sister pleasure.", "에드먼드가 대답했다. “그리고 아마 그 누이도 반가워할 거야."], ["She does not like his unsettled habits.”", "누이는 그 사람의 진득하지 못한 버릇을 못마땅해하거든.”"]], [["“What a favourite he is with my cousins!”", "“사촌 언니들이 어쩜 그리 그분을 좋아할까요!”"]], [["“Yes, his manners to women are such as must please. Mrs.", "“그래, 그 사람이 여자를 대하는 태도야 마음에 들 수밖에 없지."], ["Grant, I believe, suspects him of a preference for Julia; I have never seen much symptom of it, but I wish it may be so.", "그랜트 부인은 그 사람이 줄리아를 마음에 두고 있다고 짐작하는 모양이더라. 나야 그런 낌새를 별로 못 봤지만, 정말 그랬으면 싶구나."], ["He has no faults but what a serious attachment would remove.”", "진지한 정만 붙으면 씻은 듯 사라질 흠 말고는, 그 사람한테 딱히 흠잡을 데가 없거든.”"]], [["“If Miss Bertram were not engaged,”", "“버트럼 언니가 약혼한 몸만 아니라면,”"], ["said Fanny cautiously, “I could sometimes almost think that he admired her more than Julia.”", "패니가 조심스레 말했다. “저는 이따금 거의 그분이 줄리아보다 언니를 더 마음에 들어 하시는 게 아닌가 싶기도 해요.”"]], [["“Which is, perhaps, more in favour of his liking Julia best, than you, Fanny, may be aware; for I believe it often happens that a man, before he has quite made up his own mind, will distinguish the sister or intimate friend of the woman he is really thinking of more than the woman herself.", "“그건 아마, 패니 네가 아는 것보다 오히려 그 사람이 줄리아를 가장 좋아한다는 쪽에 더 무게를 실어 주는 얘기일지도 몰라. 사내란 아직 제 마음을 온전히 정하기 전에는, 정작 마음에 두고 있는 여자보다 그 여자의 자매나 가까운 벗을 더 눈에 띄게 대하는 일이 흔하거든."], ["Crawford has too much sense to stay here if he found himself in any danger from Maria; and I am not at all afraid for her, after such a proof as she has given that her feelings are not strong.”", "크로퍼드는 마리아에게서 조금이라도 위험을 느꼈다면 여기 머물 만큼 분별없는 사람이 아니야. 그리고 마리아가 제 감정이 그리 깊지 않음을 그토록 확실히 보여 준 마당에, 나는 그 애가 조금도 걱정되지 않는구나.”"]], [["Fanny supposed she must have been mistaken, and meant to think differently in future; but with all that submission to Edmund could do, and all the help of the coinciding looks and hints which she occasionally noticed in some of the others, and which seemed to say that Julia was Mr.", "패니는 자기가 잘못 본 것이 틀림없다 여기고, 앞으로는 달리 생각하기로 마음먹었다. 그러나 에드먼드에게 고분고분 따르려는 온갖 노력에도, 또 이따금 다른 몇몇에게서 눈치챈, 줄리아가 크로퍼드 씨의 선택인 듯 말해 주는 그 아귀 맞는 눈빛과 언질들의 온갖 도움에도, 그녀는 무엇을 어찌 생각해야 할지 늘 알 수 있는 것은 아니었다."], ["Crawford’s choice, she knew not always what to think. She was privy, one evening, to the hopes of her aunt Norris on the subject, as well as to her feelings, and the feelings of Mrs.", "어느 날 저녁, 그녀는 이모 노리스가 그 일을 두고 품은 바람과 심경을, 또 그와 얼마쯤 비슷한 대목에 대한 러시워스 부인의 심경까지 곁에서 알게 되었고, 그 이야기를 들으며 의아해하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Rushworth, on a point of some similarity, and could not help wondering as she listened; and glad would she have been not to be obliged to listen, for it was while all the other young people were dancing, and she sitting, most unwillingly, among the chaperons at the fire, longing for the re-entrance of her elder cousin, on whom all her own hopes of a partner then depended.", "그리고 굳이 듣지 않아도 되었다면 얼마나 좋았으랴, 그때 다른 젊은이들은 모두 춤을 추는데 그녀만이 더없이 마지못해 난롯가 보호자들 틈에 끼어 앉아, 파트너를 구할 제 온갖 희망이 걸려 있던 손위 사촌이 다시 들어오기만을 애타게 기다리고 있었으니 말이다. 그것은 패니의 첫 무도회였다."], ["It was Fanny’s first ball, though without the preparation or splendour of many a young lady’s first ball, being the thought only of the afternoon, built on the late acquisition of a violin player in the servants’ hall, and the possibility of raising five couple with the help of Mrs. Grant and a new intimate friend of Mr.", "하기야 여느 아가씨의 첫 무도회 같은 채비도 화려함도 없이, 그저 그날 오후에야 떠오른 착상이었고, 하인방에 근래 바이올린 켜는 이가 하나 들어온 것과, 그랜트 부인 및 버트럼 씨를 찾아와 방금 도착한 새 벗의 힘을 빌리면 다섯 쌍쯤은 세울 수 있으리라는 데에 기대어 꾸며진 것이었다."], ["Bertram’s just arrived on a visit. It had, however, been a very happy one to Fanny through four dances, and she was quite grieved to be losing even a quarter of an hour.", "그래도 그것은 네 곡을 추는 동안 패니에게 더없이 행복한 무도회였으니, 그녀는 단 십오 분이라도 그것을 놓치는 것이 못내 서러웠다."], ["While waiting and wishing, looking now at the dancers and now at the door, this dialogue between the two above-mentioned ladies was forced on her—", "기다리고 바라며, 춤추는 이들을 보았다 문을 보았다 하는 사이, 앞서 말한 두 부인 사이의 이 대화가 그녀의 귀에 억지로 밀려들었다—"]], [["“I think, ma’am,” said Mrs.", "“제 생각엔 말이에요, 부인,”"], ["Norris, her eyes directed towards Mr.", "두 번째로 짝이 된 러시워스 씨와 마리아 쪽으로 눈길을 두며 노리스 부인이 말했다."], ["Rushworth and Maria, who were partners for the second time, “we shall see some happy faces again now.”", "“이제 다시 행복한 얼굴들을 좀 보게 될 것 같네요.”"]], [["“Yes, ma’am, indeed,”", "“네, 정말 그래요, 부인.”"], ["replied the other, with a stately simper, “there will be some satisfaction in looking on now, and I think it was rather a pity they should have been obliged to part.", "상대가 점잔 빼는 미소를 지으며 대꾸했다. “이제는 지켜보는 데도 얼마간 흐뭇함이 있겠어요."], ["Young folks in their situation should be excused complying with the common forms.", "그리고 저 둘이 굳이 떨어져야 했다니 좀 딱한 노릇이지 싶어요. 저런 처지의 젊은것들이야 흔한 격식쯤은 안 지켜도 눈감아 줄 만하잖아요."], ["I wonder my son did not propose it.”", "우리 아들이 그러자고 청하지 않은 게 이상하네요.”"]], [["“I dare say he did, ma’am.", "“분명 청했을 거예요, 부인."], ["Mr. Rushworth is never remiss.", "러시워스 씨야 그런 데 소홀할 분이 아니지요."], ["But dear Maria has such a strict sense of propriety, so much of that true delicacy which one seldom meets with nowadays, Mrs. Rushworth—that wish of avoiding particularity!", "하지만 우리 사랑스러운 마리아는 예의범절을 어찌나 깍듯이 여기는지, 요즘 좀처럼 보기 힘든 그 참된 조심성을 지녔거든요, 러시워스 부인—남들 눈에 유별나 보이는 걸 피하려는 그 마음 말이에요!"], ["Dear ma’am, only look at her face at this moment; how different from what it was the two last dances!”", "부인, 지금 이 순간 저 애 얼굴만 좀 보세요. 앞서 두 곡을 출 때와 어쩜 저리 다를까요!”"]], [["Miss Bertram did indeed look happy, her eyes were sparkling with pleasure, and she was speaking with great animation, for Julia and her partner, Mr.", "버트럼 양은 과연 행복해 보였으니, 그 눈은 기쁨으로 반짝였고 자못 활기차게 이야기하고 있었다."], ["Crawford, were close to her; they were all in a cluster together.", "줄리아와 그 파트너인 크로퍼드 씨가 가까이 있었기 때문이다."], ["How she had looked before, Fanny could not recollect, for she had been dancing with Edmund herself, and had not thought about her.", "다들 한데 모여 있었다. 앞서 그녀가 어떤 얼굴이었는지 패니로서는 도무지 떠올릴 수 없었으니, 그때 그녀 자신은 에드먼드와 춤을 추느라 마리아에게는 마음을 쓰지 않았던 것이다."]], [["Mrs. Norris continued, “It is quite delightful, ma’am, to see young people so properly happy, so well suited, and so much the thing!", "노리스 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["I cannot but think of dear Sir Thomas’s delight.", "“젊은것들이 저리 마땅하게 행복하고, 저리 잘 어울리고, 저리 격에 딱 맞는 걸 보니 참으로 흐뭇하네요, 부인!"], ["And what do you say, ma’am, to the chance of another match? Mr.", "사랑하는 토머스 경께서 얼마나 기뻐하실지 생각하지 않을 수가 없어요."], ["Rushworth has set a good example, and such things are very catching.”", "그런데 부인은, 또 한 쌍이 맺어질 가망에 대해서는 어찌 보세요? 러시워스 씨가 좋은 본보기를 보였으니, 그런 일이란 참 옮아붙기 쉽거든요.”"]], [["Mrs. Rushworth, who saw nothing but her son, was quite at a loss.", "오로지 제 아들밖에는 눈에 들어오지 않던 러시워스 부인은 어리둥절할 따름이었다."]], [["“The couple above, ma’am.", "“저 위쪽 한 쌍 말이에요, 부인."], ["Do you see no symptoms there?”", "저기 아무 낌새도 안 보이세요?”"]], [["“Oh dear!", "“어머나!"], ["Miss Julia and Mr. Crawford.", "줄리아 양과 크로퍼드 씨 말이군요."], ["Yes, indeed, a very pretty match.", "네, 정말이지 아주 어여쁜 짝이에요."], ["What is his property?”", "그이 재산은 얼마나 되나요?”"]], [["“Four thousand a year.”", "“연 사천 파운드요.”"]], [["“Very well.", "“그럼 됐네요."], ["Those who have not more must be satisfied with what they have.", "그보다 더 못 가진 사람들이야 제 가진 것에 만족해야지요."], ["Four thousand a year is a pretty estate, and he seems a very genteel, steady young man, so I hope Miss Julia will be very happy.”", "연 사천 파운드면 넉넉한 재산이고, 게다가 아주 점잖고 진득한 젊은이로 보이니, 줄리아 양이 무척 행복하기를 바라요.”"]], [["“It is not a settled thing, ma’am, yet.", "“아직 정해진 일은 아니에요, 부인."], ["We only speak of it among friends.", "그저 우리끼리만 하는 얘기지요."], ["But I have very little doubt it will be.", "하지만 저는 그리되리라는 걸 거의 의심치 않아요."], ["He is growing extremely particular in his attentions.”", "그이가 마음 쓰는 태가 갈수록 유별나지고 있거든요.”"]], [["Fanny could listen no farther.", "패니는 더는 들을 수가 없었다."], ["Listening and wondering were all suspended for a time, for Mr.", "듣는 것도 의아해하는 것도 한동안 죄다 멈추었으니, 버트럼 씨가 다시 방에 들어와 있었던 것이다."], ["Bertram was in the room again; and though feeling it would be a great honour to be asked by him, she thought it must happen.", "그리고 그에게 청을 받는 것이 크나큰 영광이리라 느끼면서도, 그녀는 그리되고야 말리라 여겼다."], ["He came towards their little circle; but instead of asking her to dance, drew a chair near her, and gave her an account of the present state of a sick horse, and the opinion of the groom, from whom he had just parted.", "그가 그들의 작은 무리 쪽으로 다가왔다. 그러나 그녀에게 춤을 청하기는커녕, 그는 그녀 곁으로 의자를 끌어당겨 앉아, 병든 말의 지금 상태며 방금 헤어진 마부의 소견을 늘어놓았다."], ["Fanny found that it was not to be, and in the modesty of her nature immediately felt that she had been unreasonable in expecting it.", "패니는 그리될 일이 아님을 알아챘고, 천성이 겸손한지라 그것을 기대한 제가 당치 않았음을 곧바로 느꼈다."], ["When he had told of his horse, he took a newspaper from the table, and looking over it, said in a languid way, “If you want to dance, Fanny, I will stand up with you.”", "말 이야기를 마치자 그는 탁자에서 신문을 집어 들어 훑어보며 나른한 투로 말했다. “춤이 추고 싶으면, 패니, 내가 상대해 주마.”"], ["With more than equal civility the offer was declined; she did not wish to dance.", "그 못지않은 예의로 그녀는 그 청을 사양했으니, 춤출 마음이 없었던 것이다."], ["“I am glad of it,”", "“그거 잘됐구나.”"], ["said he, in a much brisker tone, and throwing down the newspaper again, “for I am tired to death.", "그가 한결 활기찬 어조로 신문을 다시 내던지며 말했다. “나야 죽도록 지쳤거든."], ["I only wonder how the good people can keep it up so long.", "저 착한 사람들이 어찌 저리 오래 버티는지 그저 신기할 따름이야."], ["They had need be all in love, to find any amusement in such folly; and so they are, I fancy.", "저런 어리석은 짓에서 재미를 찾으려면 다들 사랑에 빠져 있어야 할 텐데—내 짐작엔 정말 그런가 봐."], ["If you look at them you may see they are so many couple of lovers—all but Yates and Mrs. Grant—and, between ourselves, she, poor woman, must want a lover as much as any one of them.", "저들을 봐라, 죄다 연인들 아니냐—예이츠와 그랜트 부인만 빼고 말이야—그런데 우리끼리 얘기지만, 저 딱한 부인도 어느 누구 못지않게 연인이 아쉬울 게 틀림없어."], ["A desperate dull life hers must be with the doctor,”", "저 박사와 살자면 지독히도 따분한 삶일 테니.”"], ["making a sly face as he spoke towards the chair of the latter, who proving, however, to be close at his elbow, made so instantaneous a change of expression and subject necessary, as Fanny, in spite of everything, could hardly help laughing at.", "이렇게 말하며 그는 그랜트 박사의 의자 쪽으로 짓궂은 표정을 지어 보였는데, 하필 박사가 바로 그의 팔꿈치 곁에 와 있는 게 드러나는 바람에, 그토록 잽싸게 낯빛과 화제를 바꾸지 않을 수 없었으니, 패니는 온갖 사정에도 불구하고 웃음을 참기 어려웠다."], ["“A strange business this in America, Dr. Grant!", "“아메리카에서 벌어진 이 일이 참 야릇하지요, 그랜트 박사님!"], ["What is your opinion?", "어찌 보십니까?"], ["I always come to you to know what I am to think of public matters.”", "저는 세상일을 어찌 생각해야 할지 알고 싶을 때면 늘 박사님을 찾는답니다.”"]], [["“My dear Tom,”", "“얘야, 톰,”"], ["cried his aunt soon afterwards, “as you are not dancing, I dare say you will have no objection to join us in a rubber; shall you?”", "이윽고 그의 이모가 외쳤다. “네가 춤을 안 추고 있으니, 우리랑 카드 한 판 어울려도 싫달 것 없겠지, 그렇지?”"], ["Then leaving her seat, and coming to him to enforce the proposal, added in a whisper, “We want to make a table for Mrs.", "그러고는 자리에서 일어나 그에게 다가와 그 청을 밀어붙이며, 나직이 덧붙였다. “러시워스 부인 자리를 만들어 드려야 하거든."], ["Rushworth, you know. Your mother is quite anxious about it, but cannot very well spare time to sit down herself, because of her fringe.", "네 어머니가 어찌나 마음을 쓰는지, 그렇다고 몸소 앉아 계실 짬은 못 내는구나, 술 장식 뜨느라 말이다."], ["Now, you and I and Dr.", "그러니 너하고 나하고 그랜트 박사면 딱 맞겠어."], ["Grant will just do; and though we play but half-crowns, you know, you may bet half-guineas with him.”", "그리고 우리야 반 크라운짜리 판이지만, 알다시피 박사하고는 반 기니씩 걸어도 되고.”"]], [["“I should be most happy,”", "“더없이 기쁘겠어요.”"], ["replied he aloud, and jumping up with alacrity, “it would give me the greatest pleasure; but that I am this moment going to dance.", "그가 냉큼 벌떡 일어서며 소리 내어 대답했다. “그야말로 큰 즐거움이겠지만, 지금 막 춤추러 가려던 참이라서요."], ["Come, Fanny,”", "자, 패니,”"], ["taking her hand, “do not be dawdling any longer, or the dance will be over.”", "하며 그녀의 손을 잡고, “더는 꾸물대지 마라, 안 그러면 춤이 끝나 버릴 테니.”"]], [["Fanny was led off very willingly, though it was impossible for her to feel much gratitude towards her cousin, or distinguish, as he certainly did, between the selfishness of another person and his own.", "패니는 아주 기꺼이 이끌려 갔다. 하기야 사촌 오빠에게 그리 큰 고마움을 느끼기도, 그가 분명 그러했듯이 남의 잇속과 제 잇속을 가려내기도 어려운 노릇이었지만."]], [["“A pretty modest request upon my word,”", "“정말이지 어지간히도 겸손한 청이더군.”"], ["he indignantly exclaimed as they walked away.", "그들이 걸어 나오는데 그가 분해하며 외쳤다."], ["“To want to nail me to a card-table for the next two hours with herself and Dr. Grant, who are always quarrelling, and that poking old woman, who knows no more of whist than of algebra.", "“앞으로 두 시간 동안 나를 카드판에 못 박아 두겠다는 거야, 늘 티격태격하는 저 이모하고 그랜트 박사, 게다가 휘스트를 대수학만큼이나 모르는 저 참견쟁이 노파하고 말이지."], ["I wish my good aunt would be a little less busy!", "우리 착한 이모가 좀 덜 부산했으면 좋겠다!"], ["And to ask me in such a way too!", "게다가 청하는 꼴이라니!"], ["without ceremony, before them all, so as to leave me no possibility of refusing.", "격식도 없이, 다들 보는 앞에서, 나더러 거절할 여지라곤 조금도 안 남기고 말이야."], ["That is what I dislike most particularly.", "바로 그게 내가 무엇보다 질색하는 거란다."], ["It raises my spleen more than anything, to have the pretence of being asked, of being given a choice, and at the same time addressed in such a way as to oblige one to do the very thing, whatever it be!", "청을 받는, 고를 여지를 주는 시늉을 하면서, 동시에 그게 무엇이든 바로 그 일을 하지 않을 수 없게끔 몰아붙이는 것—그것만큼 내 부아를 돋우는 게 없어!"], ["If I had not luckily thought of standing up with you I could not have got out of it.", "다행히 너하고 춤출 생각을 못 했더라면 나는 거기서 빠져나오지도 못했을 거야."], ["It is a great deal too bad.", "정말 너무한 노릇이지."], ["But when my aunt has got a fancy in her head, nothing can stop her.”", "하지만 이모가 일단 무슨 생각을 머리에 담으면, 무엇으로도 못 말린다니까.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XIII", "# 제13장"]], [["The Honourable John Yates, this new friend, had not much to recommend him beyond habits of fashion and expense, and being the younger son of a lord with a tolerable independence; and Sir Thomas would probably have thought his introduction at Mansfield by no means desirable.", "지체 높으신 존 예이츠 씨, 이 새로운 벗은, 유행과 낭비의 버릇, 그리고 그런대로 넉넉한 독립 재산을 지닌 어느 귀족의 작은아들이라는 것 말고는 딱히 내세울 게 없는 사람이었다. 그러니 토머스 경이라면 아마 그가 맨스필드에 발을 들이는 것을 결코 바람직하게 여기지 않았으리라."], ["Mr. Bertram’s acquaintance with him had begun at Weymouth, where they had spent ten days together in the same society, and the friendship, if friendship it might be called, had been proved and perfected by Mr.", "버트럼 씨가 그와 알게 된 것은 웨이머스에서였는데, 거기서 둘은 같은 무리에 끼어 열흘을 함께 보냈고, 그 우정은—우정이라 부를 수 있다면—예이츠 씨가 틈나는 대로 언제든 오는 길에 맨스필드에 들르라는 청을 받고, 또 그가 오겠노라 약속함으로써 굳게 다져진 터였다."], ["Yates’s being invited to take Mansfield in his way, whenever he could, and by his promising to come; and he did come rather earlier than had been expected, in consequence of the sudden breaking-up of a large party assembled for gaiety at the house of another friend, which he had left Weymouth to join.", "그리고 그는 예상보다 다소 일찍 왔는데, 그가 웨이머스를 떠나 합류하러 갔던 또 다른 벗의 집에서, 흥겹게 놀자고 모인 큰 무리가 갑작스레 흩어진 탓이었다. 그는 실망의 날개를 달고, 머릿속은 온통 연극 생각으로 가득 찬 채 왔으니, 그 모임이 연극 모임이었기 때문이다."], ["He came on the wings of disappointment, and with his head full of acting, for it had been a theatrical party; and the play in which he had borne a part was within two days of representation, when the sudden death of one of the nearest connexions of the family had destroyed the scheme and dispersed the performers.", "그가 한 배역을 맡은 그 연극은 이틀 뒤면 상연될 참이었는데, 그 집안의 가장 가까운 친척 하나가 갑자기 세상을 떠나는 바람에 그 계획이 무너지고 배우들이 뿔뿔이 흩어지고 만 것이다."], ["To be so near happiness, so near fame, so near the long paragraph in praise of the private theatricals at Ecclesford, the seat of the Right Hon. Lord Ravenshaw, in Cornwall, which would of course have immortalised the whole party for at least a twelvemonth!", "그토록 행복에 가까이, 그토록 명성에 가까이, 콘월에 있는 지체 높으신 레이븐쇼 경의 저택 에클스포드에서 열린 그 사가(私家) 연극을 기리는 긴 기사—물론 온 무리를 적어도 열두 달은 불멸의 이름으로 남겨 주었을 그 기사—에 그토록 가까이 다가갔다가!"], ["and being so near, to lose it all, was an injury to be keenly felt, and Mr. Yates could talk of nothing else.", "그토록 가까이서 그 모든 것을 잃었으니, 그것은 사무치게 느껴질 만한 손해였고, 예이츠 씨는 그 이야기밖에 할 줄을 몰랐다."], ["Ecclesford and its theatre, with its arrangements and dresses, rehearsals and jokes, was his never-failing subject, and to boast of the past his only consolation.", "에클스포드와 그 극장, 그 무대 장치며 의상이며 연습이며 우스갯소리가 그의 마르지 않는 화제였고, 지난날을 자랑하는 것이 그의 유일한 위안이었다."]], [["Happily for him, a love of the theatre is so general, an itch for acting so strong among young people, that he could hardly out-talk the interest of his hearers.", "그로서는 다행스럽게도, 연극에 대한 사랑이란 워낙 두루 퍼진 것이고 연기에 대한 갈망이란 젊은이들 사이에서 워낙 강렬한 것이라, 그가 아무리 떠들어도 듣는 이들의 흥미를 눌러 이기지는 못했다."], ["From the first casting of the parts to the epilogue it was all bewitching, and there were few who did not wish to have been a party concerned, or would have hesitated to try their skill.", "배역을 처음 정하는 대목에서부터 맺음말에 이르기까지 그 모두가 홀리는 듯했고, 그 일에 한몫 끼었더라면 싶지 않은 이도, 제 솜씨를 시험해 보기를 망설일 이도 거의 없었다. 그 연극은 「연인들의 맹세」였고, 예이츠 씨는 카셀 백작을 맡기로 되어 있었다."], ["The play had been Lovers’ Vows, and Mr.", "“대수롭잖은 배역이었지요.” 그가 말했다."], ["Yates was to have been Count Cassel. “A trifling part,”", "“도무지 제 취향도 아니고, 다시는 결코 안 맡을 그런 배역이었어요."], ["said he, “and not at all to my taste, and such a one as I certainly would not accept again; but I was determined to make no difficulties.", "하지만 저는 아무 트집도 안 잡기로 마음먹었지요. 레이븐쇼 경과 공작이 제가 에클스포드에 닿기도 전에 맡을 만한 배역 둘을 몽땅 차지해 버렸거든요."], ["Lord Ravenshaw and the duke had appropriated the only two characters worth playing before I reached Ecclesford; and though Lord Ravenshaw offered to resign his to me, it was impossible to take it, you know.", "레이븐쇼 경이 제게 제 배역을 넘기겠다고 하긴 했지만, 아시다시피 그걸 받을 수야 없는 노릇이었죠. 저는 그분이 딱했어요, 제 역량을 그토록 잘못 가늠하셨으니 말이에요."], ["I was sorry for him that he should have so mistaken his powers, for he was no more equal to the Baron—a little man with a weak voice, always hoarse after the first ten minutes.", "그분은 남작 역에는 도무지 어울리지 않았거든요—목소리가 가냘픈 자그마한 분이라, 처음 십 분만 지나면 늘 목이 쉬어 버렸죠."], ["It must have injured the piece materially; but I was resolved to make no difficulties.", "그게 작품을 크게 망쳤을 게 틀림없어요. 하지만 저는 아무 트집도 안 잡기로 작정했으니까요."], ["Sir Henry thought the duke not equal to Frederick, but that was because Sir Henry wanted the part himself; whereas it was certainly in the best hands of the two.", "헨리 경은 공작이 프레더릭 역에 못 미친다고 여겼지만, 그건 헨리 경이 그 배역을 제가 탐냈기 때문이었어요. 실은 둘 중 더 나은 쪽 손에 들어간 게 분명했는데 말이에요."], ["I was surprised to see Sir Henry such a stick.", "헨리 경이 그토록 뻣뻣한 사람인 줄은 저도 놀랐어요."], ["Luckily the strength of the piece did not depend upon him.", "다행히 작품의 힘이 그이한테 달려 있진 않았죠."], ["Our Agatha was inimitable, and the duke was thought very great by many.", "우리 아가사는 흉내 낼 수 없을 만큼 훌륭했고, 공작도 많은 이들이 아주 대단하다고 여겼어요."], ["And upon the whole, it would certainly have gone off wonderfully.”", "그리고 대체로, 분명 놀랍도록 잘 굴러갔을 거예요.”"]], [["“It was a hard case, upon my word”; and, “I do think you were very much to be pitied,”", "“정말이지 딱한 일이었어요” 라거나, “저는 정말 당신이 무척 안됐다고 생각해요”"], ["were the kind responses of listening sympathy.", "하는 것이, 귀 기울여 동정하는 이들의 다정한 대꾸였다."]], [["“It is not worth complaining about; but to be sure the poor old dowager could not have died at a worse time; and it is impossible to help wishing that the news could have been suppressed for just the three days we wanted. It was but three days; and being only a grandmother, and all happening two hundred miles off, I think there would have been no great harm, and it was suggested, I know; but Lord Ravenshaw, who I suppose is one of the most correct men in England, would not hear of it.”", "“불평할 만한 일은 못 되지요. 하지만 그 딱한 노부인이 하필 그보다 더 나쁜 때에 돌아가실 수도 없었을 거예요. 그리고 우리한테 꼭 필요했던 사흘 동안만이라도 그 부고를 눌러 둘 수 있었더라면 하는 바람을 도무지 떨칠 수가 없어요. 고작 사흘이었거든요. 게다가 한낱 할머니일 뿐이고, 그 모든 일이 이백 마일이나 떨어진 데서 벌어졌으니, 저는 별 탈이 없었으리라 봐요. 그런 말이 나오기도 했고요, 저도 알아요. 하지만 아마 잉글랜드에서 가장 반듯한 사람 축에 들 레이븐쇼 경이 그 말을 들으려고도 하지 않았죠.”"]], [["“An afterpiece instead of a comedy,”", "“희극 대신 막간극이라니.”"], ["said Mr. Bertram.", "버트럼 씨가 말했다."], ["“Lovers’ Vows were at an end, and Lord and Lady Ravenshaw left to act My Grandmother by themselves.", "“「연인들의 맹세」는 막을 내리고, 레이븐쇼 경 내외분만 남아 「우리 할머니」를 손수 연기하게 되셨군요. 뭐, 과부 재산이 그분을 위로해 드릴 테고요."], ["Well, the jointure may comfort him; and perhaps, between friends, he began to tremble for his credit and his lungs in the Baron, and was not sorry to withdraw; and to make you amends, Yates, I think we must raise a little theatre at Mansfield, and ask you to be our manager.”", "그리고 어쩌면, 우리끼리 얘기지만, 그분은 남작 역을 하다 제 체면과 제 폐가 어찌 될까 떨리기 시작해서, 물러나는 게 싫지 않았는지도 모르죠. 그리고 예이츠, 당신 손해를 메워 드리는 뜻에서, 아무래도 우리가 맨스필드에 자그마한 극장을 하나 세우고 당신더러 우리 감독을 맡아 달라 청해야겠어요.”"]], [["This, though the thought of the moment, did not end with the moment; for the inclination to act was awakened, and in no one more strongly than in him who was now master of the house; and who, having so much leisure as to make almost any novelty a certain good, had likewise such a degree of lively talents and comic taste, as were exactly adapted to the novelty of acting.", "이것은 그 순간의 착상이었으나 그 순간으로 끝나지 않았다. 연기에 대한 욕구가 깨어난 것이다."], ["The thought returned again and again.", "게다가 그 누구보다도 지금 이 집의 주인 된 이의 마음에 가장 강하게 깨어났으니, 그는 웬만한 새것이면 무엇이든 틀림없는 즐거움이 될 만큼 여가가 넘쳤고, 게다가 연기라는 새것에 꼭 들어맞는, 발랄한 재주와 희극적 취향까지 그만큼 지니고 있었다."], ["“Oh for the Ecclesford theatre and scenery to try something with.”", "그 생각은 자꾸만 되살아났다. “아, 뭔가 시험해 볼 에클스포드의 극장과 무대만 있다면.”"], ["Each sister could echo the wish; and Henry Crawford, to whom, in all the riot of his gratifications it was yet an untasted pleasure, was quite alive at the idea.", "자매도 저마다 그 바람에 맞장구쳤다. 그리고 온갖 향락의 소란 속에서도 이것만은 아직 맛보지 못한 즐거움이던 헨리 크로퍼드는, 그 착상에 자못 생기가 돌았다."], ["“I really believe,”", "“저는 정말이지,” 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I could be fool enough at this moment to undertake any character that ever was written, from Shylock or Richard III down to the singing hero of a farce in his scarlet coat and cocked hat.", "“지금 이 순간 여태 쓰인 어떤 배역이든 맡을 만큼 얼마든지 바보가 될 수 있을 것 같아요. 샤일록이나 리처드 3세에서부터, 진홍색 상의에 삼각모를 쓴 소극의 노래하는 주인공에 이르기까지 말이에요."], ["I feel as if I could be anything or everything; as if I could rant and storm, or sigh or cut capers, in any tragedy or comedy in the English language.", "저는 무엇이든, 아니 무엇이든 다 될 수 있을 것만 같아요. 영어로 쓰인 어떤 비극에서든 희극에서든, 고래고래 외치고 사납게 몰아치거나, 한숨짓거나, 익살스레 펄쩍펄쩍 뛸 수 있을 것 같거든요."], ["Let us be doing something.", "뭐라도 좀 해 봅시다."], ["Be it only half a play, an act, a scene; what should prevent us?", "그게 연극 반 편이든, 한 막이든, 한 장면이든 말이에요. 우리를 막을 게 뭐가 있겠어요?"], ["Not these countenances, I am sure,”", "이 얼굴들은 분명 아니겠지요.”"], ["looking towards the Miss Bertrams; “and for a theatre, what signifies a theatre?", "하며 버트럼 자매 쪽을 바라보았다. “그리고 극장으로 말하면, 극장이 무슨 대수예요?"], ["We shall be only amusing ourselves.", "우리끼리 즐기자는 것뿐인걸요."], ["Any room in this house might suffice.”", "이 집 어느 방이든 넉넉할 거예요.”"]], [["“We must have a curtain,”", "“막은 있어야겠죠.”"], ["said Tom Bertram; “a few yards of green baize for a curtain, and perhaps that may be enough.”", "톰 버트럼이 말했다. “막으로 쓸 초록색 나사 몇 야드면, 그거면 어쩌면 넉넉할지도 몰라요.”"]], [["“Oh, quite enough,” cried Mr.", "“오, 넉넉하고말고요.”"], ["Yates, “with only just a side wing or two run up, doors in flat, and three or four scenes to be let down; nothing more would be necessary on such a plan as this.", "예이츠 씨가 외쳤다. “옆 날개 한둘만 세우고, 문은 평면에 그려 넣고, 배경 서너 개만 내려뜨리면 돼요."], ["For mere amusement among ourselves we should want nothing more.”", "이런 계획에야 그 이상은 아무것도 필요 없지요. 그저 우리끼리 즐기는 거라면 더 바랄 게 없어요.”"]], [["“I believe we must be satisfied with less,”", "“아무래도 우리는 그보다 덜한 걸로 만족해야 할 거예요.”"], ["said Maria.", "마리아가 말했다."], ["“There would not be time, and other difficulties would arise.", "“시간도 넉넉지 않을 테고, 다른 어려움도 생길 테니까요."], ["We must rather adopt Mr. Crawford’s views, and make the performance, not the theatre, our object.", "차라리 크로퍼드 씨의 생각을 좇아, 극장이 아니라 상연 자체를 우리 목표로 삼는 게 낫겠어요."], ["Many parts of our best plays are independent of scenery.”", "우리가 아는 훌륭한 연극들도 많은 대목이 무대 장치와 상관없이 되잖아요.”"]], [["“Nay,”", "“아니지.”"], ["said Edmund, who began to listen with alarm.", "에드먼드가 말했는데, 그는 슬며시 마음이 놓이지 않아 귀를 기울이기 시작한 참이었다."], ["“Let us do nothing by halves.", "“우리 이왕 할 거면 어설프게 하진 맙시다."], ["If we are to act, let it be in a theatre completely fitted up with pit, boxes, and gallery, and let us have a play entire from beginning to end; so as it be a German play, no matter what, with a good tricking, shifting afterpiece, and a figure-dance, and a hornpipe, and a song between the acts.", "정 연기를 할 거라면, 평토간이며 박스석이며 위층 관람석까지 완벽하게 갖춘 극장에서 하고, 처음부터 끝까지 온전한 연극을 한 편 올립시다. 그것도 독일 연극으로다가, 뭐든 상관없으니, 잘 속고 잘 뒤바뀌는 막간극에, 무용에, 호른파이프 춤에, 막 사이사이 노래까지 곁들여서요."], ["If we do not outdo Ecclesford, we do nothing.”", "에클스포드를 능가하지 못할 바에야, 우리는 아무것도 안 하는 거나 마찬가지죠.”"]], [["“Now, Edmund, do not be disagreeable,”", "“얘, 에드먼드, 심술궂게 굴지 마.”"], ["said Julia.", "줄리아가 말했다."], ["“Nobody loves a play better than you do, or can have gone much farther to see one.”", "“오빠만큼 연극을 좋아하는 사람도 없고, 오빠만큼 연극 하나 보겠다고 멀리까지 가 본 사람도 없잖아.”"]], [["“True, to see real acting, good hardened real acting; but I would hardly walk from this room to the next to look at the raw efforts of those who have not been bred to the trade: a set of gentlemen and ladies, who have all the disadvantages of education and decorum to struggle through.”", "“맞아, 진짜 연기, 잘 다져진 훌륭한 진짜 연기를 보는 거라면 말이지. 하지만 그 일에 이골이 나도록 배운 적 없는 이들의 설익은 시도를 보자고는, 나는 이 방에서 옆방까지도 걸어가고 싶지 않아. 교양이며 예의범절이라는 온갖 걸림돌을 헤치고 나가야 하는 신사 숙녀들 한 무리 말이야.”"]], [["After a short pause, however, the subject still continued, and was discussed with unabated eagerness, every one’s inclination increasing by the discussion, and a knowledge of the inclination of the rest; and though nothing was settled but that Tom Bertram would prefer a comedy, and his sisters and Henry Crawford a tragedy, and that nothing in the world could be easier than to find a piece which would please them all, the resolution to act something or other seemed so decided as to make Edmund quite uncomfortable. He was determined to prevent it, if possible, though his mother, who equally heard the conversation which passed at table, did not evince the least disapprobation.", "그러나 잠깐 말이 끊긴 뒤에도 그 화제는 여전히 이어졌고, 조금도 수그러들지 않는 열의로 논의되었다. 논의가 오갈수록, 또 서로의 마음을 알게 될수록 저마다의 욕구가 부풀어 갔다. 그리고 톰 버트럼은 희극을 좋아하고 그 누이들과 헨리 크로퍼드는 비극을 좋아한다는 것, 그리고 그들 모두의 마음에 들 작품을 찾는 것보다 세상에 더 쉬운 일은 없으리라는 것 말고는 아무것도 정해지지 않았으나, 무엇이 됐든 연극을 하고야 말겠다는 결의만은 어찌나 굳어 보이던지 에드먼드는 여간 불편하지 않았다. 그는 가능하다면 그것을 막기로 마음먹었다. 비록 그 어머니는 식탁에서 오간 그 대화를 똑같이 들었으면서도 조금도 못마땅한 기색을 보이지 않았지만."]], [["The same evening afforded him an opportunity of trying his strength.", "바로 그날 저녁, 그에게 제 힘을 시험해 볼 기회가 찾아왔다. 마리아와 줄리아와 헨리 크로퍼드와 예이츠 씨는 당구실에 있었다."], ["Maria, Julia, Henry Crawford, and Mr.", "톰이 그들 곁을 떠나 응접실로 돌아오니, 에드먼드는 난롯가에 서서 생각에 잠겨 있었고, 조금 떨어진 소파에는 버트럼 부인이, 그리고 그 바로 곁에는 패니가 부인의 일감을 매만지며 앉아 있었다. 톰은 들어서면서 이렇게 말문을 열었다—“우리 것처럼 지독히도 형편없는 당구대는, 이 지상 어디에서도 다시 못 만날 거야."], ["Yates were in the billiard-room.", "나는 더는 못 참겠어. 그리고 이젠, 다시는 무엇으로도 나를 그리로 꾈 수 없을 거라고 말할 수 있을 것 같아."], ["Tom, returning from them into the drawing-room, where Edmund was standing thoughtfully by the fire, while Lady Bertram was on the sofa at a little distance, and Fanny close beside her arranging her work, thus began as he entered—“Such a horribly vile billiard-table as ours is not to be met with, I believe, above ground.", "하지만 방금 한 가지 좋은 걸 확인했지. 그 방이야말로 극장으로 딱 맞는 방이라는 거야."], ["I can stand it no longer, and I think, I may say, that nothing shall ever tempt me to it again; but one good thing I have just ascertained: it is the very room for a theatre, precisely the shape and length for it; and the doors at the farther end, communicating with each other, as they may be made to do in five minutes, by merely moving the bookcase in my father’s room, is the very thing we could have desired, if we had sat down to wish for it; and my father’s room will be an excellent greenroom.", "그 모양이며 길이가 극장에 꼭 알맞거든. 게다가 저 안쪽 끝의 문들은, 아버지 방의 책장만 옮기면 오 분 만에 서로 통하게 만들 수 있는데, 우리가 앉아서 하나 빌자고 궁리했다 한들 바로 그걸 빌었을 만큼 안성맞춤이야."], ["It seems to join the billiard-room on purpose.”", "그리고 아버지 방은 훌륭한 분장실이 될 거고. 마치 일부러 그러려고 당구실에 붙어 있는 것 같다니까.”"]], [["“You are not serious, Tom, in meaning to act?”", "“톰, 설마 진심으로 연기를 할 작정은 아니겠지?”"], ["said Edmund, in a low voice, as his brother approached the fire.", "형이 난롯가로 다가오자 에드먼드가 나직한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Not serious!", "“진심이 아니라니!"], ["never more so, I assure you.", "이보다 더 진심일 수도 없어, 정말이야."], ["What is there to surprise you in it?”", "거기 뭐 놀랄 게 있다고?”"]], [["“I think it would be very wrong.", "“내 생각엔 그건 아주 잘못된 일이야."], ["In a general light, private theatricals are open to some objections, but as we are circumstanced, I must think it would be highly injudicious, and more than injudicious to attempt anything of the kind.", "일반적으로 봐도 사가 연극이란 얼마간 탈 잡힐 구석이 있는데, 우리 처지에서는 그런 걸 시도하는 게 몹시 지각없는 짓, 아니 지각없는 것 이상이라고 여길 수밖에 없어. 그건 아버지를 생각하면 크게 무정한 짓이 될 거야, 저리 떠나 계신 데다 어느 정도는 늘 위험 속에 계시는데 말이지."], ["It would shew great want of feeling on my father’s account, absent as he is, and in some degree of constant danger; and it would be imprudent, I think, with regard to Maria, whose situation is a very delicate one, considering everything, extremely delicate.”", "게다가 내 생각엔 마리아를 생각해도 경솔한 짓이야. 그 애 처지가, 이것저것 다 헤아려 보면 아주 미묘하잖아, 지극히 미묘하고 말이야.”"]], [["“You take up a thing so seriously!", "“넌 뭐든 어찌 그리 심각하게 받아들이니!"], ["as if we were going to act three times a week till my father’s return, and invite all the country.", "우리가 무슨 아버지 돌아오실 때까지 한 주에 세 번씩 연기를 하고, 온 고을 사람을 다 부르기라도 하는 것처럼 말이야."], ["But it is not to be a display of that sort.", "하지만 그런 구경거리를 벌이자는 게 아니야."], ["We mean nothing but a little amusement among ourselves, just to vary the scene, and exercise our powers in something new.", "우리끼리 그저 자그마한 재미를 좀 보자는 것뿐이지, 분위기나 좀 바꾸고 새로운 데에 우리 재주나 좀 부려 보자는 거라고."], ["We want no audience, no publicity.", "우리는 관객도, 소문나는 것도 원치 않아."], ["We may be trusted, I think, in chusing some play most perfectly unexceptionable; and I can conceive no greater harm or danger to any of us in conversing in the elegant written language of some respectable author than in chattering in words of our own.", "흠잡을 데라곤 도무지 없는 더없이 온당한 연극을 고르는 일쯤이야 우리한테 맡겨도 된다고 봐. 그리고 나로선, 어느 존경받는 작가의 우아하게 쓰인 글을 입에 올리는 것이, 우리 제 말로 지껄이는 것보다 우리 누구에게 더 큰 해나 위험이 되리라곤 도무지 상상이 안 가는걸."], ["I have no fears and no scruples.", "나는 아무 두려움도, 아무 거리낌도 없어. 그리고 아버지가 안 계신 걸로 말하면, 그건 반대할 거리이기는커녕, 나는 오히려 하나의 명분이라고 봐."], ["And as to my father’s being absent, it is so far from an objection, that I consider it rather as a motive; for the expectation of his return must be a very anxious period to my mother; and if we can be the means of amusing that anxiety, and keeping up her spirits for the next few weeks, I shall think our time very well spent, and so, I am sure, will he.", "아버지 돌아오시기를 기다리는 게 어머니껜 무척 애태우는 시간일 게 틀림없거든. 그러니 우리가 그 애태움을 달래 드리고 앞으로 몇 주간 어머니 기운을 북돋아 드릴 수만 있다면, 나는 우리 시간을 아주 잘 쓴 거라 여길 테고, 아버지도 분명 그리 여기실 거야."], ["It is a very anxious period for her.”", "어머니껜 정말이지 애태우는 시간이니까.”"]], [["As he said this, each looked towards their mother.", "그가 이 말을 하자, 둘은 저마다 어머니 쪽을 바라보았다."], ["Lady Bertram, sunk back in one corner of the sofa, the picture of health, wealth, ease, and tranquillity, was just falling into a gentle doze, while Fanny was getting through the few difficulties of her work for her.", "버트럼 부인은 소파 한구석에 파묻히듯 기대앉아, 건강과 부와 안락과 평온의 표본 그 자체인 모습으로, 막 살포시 졸음에 빠져들고 있었고, 그동안 패니는 부인을 위해 그 일감의 자잘한 어려운 대목들을 손봐 주고 있었다."]], [["Edmund smiled and shook his head.", "에드먼드는 빙그레 웃으며 고개를 저었다."]], [["“By Jove!", "“이런!"], ["this won’t do,”", "이건 안 되겠군.”"], ["cried Tom, throwing himself into a chair with a hearty laugh.", "톰이 껄껄 웃으며 의자에 털썩 몸을 던지고 외쳤다."], ["“To be sure, my dear mother, your anxiety—I was unlucky there.”", "“하기야, 어머니, 어머니의 그 애태움이라니—내가 하필 그 대목에서 재수가 없었네.”"]], [["“What is the matter?”", "“무슨 일이냐?”"], ["asked her ladyship, in the heavy tone of one half-roused; “I was not asleep.”", "반쯤 깬 사람 특유의 묵직한 목소리로 부인이 물었다. “나 안 자고 있었다.”"]], [["“Oh dear, no, ma’am, nobody suspected you! Well, Edmund,”", "“아유, 그럼요, 어머니, 아무도 그렇게 안 여겼어요!”"], ["he continued, returning to the former subject, posture, and voice, as soon as Lady Bertram began to nod again, “but this I will maintain, that we shall be doing no harm.”", "그는 버트럼 부인이 다시 꾸벅꾸벅 졸기 시작하자, 곧바로 앞서의 화제와 자세와 목소리로 돌아가 말을 이었다. “하지만 이것만은 내가 우겨야겠어, 우리가 아무 해도 끼치지 않으리라는 거 말이야.”"]], [["“I cannot agree with you; I am convinced that my father would totally disapprove it.”", "“난 네 말에 동의 못 해. 아버지라면 그걸 완전히 못마땅해하시리라고 난 확신해.”"]], [["“And I am convinced to the contrary.", "“난 그 반대라고 확신하는데."], ["Nobody is fonder of the exercise of talent in young people, or promotes it more, than my father, and for anything of the acting, spouting, reciting kind, I think he has always a decided taste.", "젊은이가 재주를 부리는 걸 우리 아버지만큼 좋아하고 북돋아 주는 분도 없어. 그리고 연기니 대사 읊기니 낭송이니 하는 부류라면, 아버지는 늘 뚜렷한 취향을 지니셨다고 봐."], ["I am sure he encouraged it in us as boys.", "우리가 어릴 적에도 아버지가 그걸 부추기셨던 게 분명해."], ["How many a time have we mourned over the dead body of Julius Caesar, and to be’d and not to be’d, in this very room, for his amusement?", "우리가 바로 이 방에서, 아버지 즐거우시라고 율리우스 카이사르의 주검을 두고 몇 번이나 슬퍼했고, ‘죽느냐 사느냐’를 읊어 댔던가?"], ["And I am sure, my name was Norval, every evening of my life through one Christmas holidays.”", "그리고 어느 성탄 연휴 내내, 내 평생 저녁마다 ‘내 이름은 노발이었다’를 외웠던 게 분명하다니까.”"]], [["“It was a very different thing.", "“그건 아주 다른 얘기야."], ["You must see the difference yourself.", "너도 그 차이를 알 만하잖아."], ["My father wished us, as schoolboys, to speak well, but he would never wish his grown-up daughters to be acting plays.", "아버지는 우리가 학생일 때 말을 잘하기를 바라셨지, 다 큰 딸들이 연극을 하기를 바라시진 않을 거야."], ["His sense of decorum is strict.”", "아버지의 예의 관념은 엄격하니까.”"]], [["“I know all that,”", "“그쯤이야 나도 다 알아.”"], ["said Tom, displeased.", "톰이 언짢은 투로 말했다."], ["“I know my father as well as you do; and I’ll take care that his daughters do nothing to distress him.", "“나도 너 못지않게 아버지를 잘 알고, 아버지 딸들이 아버지 언짢게 할 짓은 하나도 못 하게 내가 단속할 거야."], ["Manage your own concerns, Edmund, and I’ll take care of the rest of the family.”", "네 일이나 챙겨, 에드먼드, 나머지 식구는 내가 챙길 테니.”"]], [["“If you are resolved on acting,”", "“정 연기를 할 작정이라면,”"], ["replied the persevering Edmund, “I must hope it will be in a very small and quiet way; and I think a theatre ought not to be attempted.", "끈덕진 에드먼드가 대꾸했다. “부디 아주 자그맣고 조용하게 하기를 바랄 뿐이야."], ["It would be taking liberties with my father’s house in his absence which could not be justified.”", "그리고 극장까지 꾸미려 드는 건 안 된다고 봐. 그건 아버지 안 계신 새 아버지 집에 함부로 손대는 짓이라, 도무지 떳떳할 수가 없어.”"]], [["“For everything of that nature I will be answerable,”", "“그런 일이라면 뭐든 내가 책임질게.”"], ["said Tom, in a decided tone.", "톰이 단호한 어조로 말했다."], ["“His house shall not be hurt.", "“아버지 집은 상하게 하지 않아."], ["I have quite as great an interest in being careful of his house as you can have; and as to such alterations as I was suggesting just now, such as moving a bookcase, or unlocking a door, or even as using the billiard-room for the space of a week without playing at billiards in it, you might just as well suppose he would object to our sitting more in this room, and less in the breakfast-room, than we did before he went away, or to my sister’s pianoforte being moved from one side of the room to the other.", "아버지 집을 아끼는 마음이야 나도 너 못지않게 크다고. 그리고 방금 내가 말한 그런 손질로 말하면—책장 하나 옮기고, 문 하나 여는 거, 아니 당구도 안 치면서 한 주간 당구실을 쓰는 정도 가지고—그걸 아버지가 반대하실 거라 여긴다면, 아버지 떠나시기 전보다 우리가 이 방에 더 앉아 있고 아침식사방엔 덜 앉아 있는 걸 반대하시리라거나, 누이 피아노를 방 이쪽에서 저쪽으로 옮기는 걸 반대하시리라고 여기는 거나 매한가지야."], ["Absolute nonsense!”", "순 말도 안 되는 소리지!”"]], [["“The innovation, if not wrong as an innovation, will be wrong as an expense.”", "“새로 벌이는 그 일이, 벌이는 것 자체로는 그르지 않다 쳐도, 비용 면에서는 그를 거야.”"]], [["“Yes, the expense of such an undertaking would be prodigious!", "“그래, 그런 일을 벌이는 비용이야 어마어마하겠지! 어쩌면 이십 파운드나 들지도 몰라."], ["Perhaps it might cost a whole twenty pounds.", "극장 비슷한 거야 틀림없이 있어야겠지만, 더없이 단출한 계획으로 할 거야."], ["Something of a theatre we must have undoubtedly, but it will be on the simplest plan: a green curtain and a little carpenter’s work, and that’s all; and as the carpenter’s work may be all done at home by Christopher Jackson himself, it will be too absurd to talk of expense; and as long as Jackson is employed, everything will be right with Sir Thomas.", "초록색 막 하나에 자잘한 목수 일 조금, 그게 다야. 그리고 목수 일이야 크리스토퍼 잭슨이 몸소 집에서 다 할 수 있으니, 비용 얘기를 꺼내는 것 자체가 우스운 노릇이지."], ["Don’t imagine that nobody in this house can see or judge but yourself.", "그리고 잭슨을 쓰는 한, 토머스 경께는 뭐든 다 괜찮을 거야."], ["Don’t act yourself, if you do not like it, but don’t expect to govern everybody else.”", "이 집에서 너 말고는 아무도 보거나 판단할 줄 모른다고 여기지 마. 하기 싫으면 너나 연기를 하지 마, 그렇다고 나머지 사람들까지 죄다 네 뜻대로 부리려 들진 말고.”"]], [["“No, as to acting myself,”", "“아니, 나 자신이 연기하는 걸로 말하면,”"], ["said Edmund, “that I absolutely protest against.”", "에드먼드가 말했다. “그것만은 내가 단연코 반대야.”"]], [["Tom walked out of the room as he said it, and Edmund was left to sit down and stir the fire in thoughtful vexation.", "톰은 그 말이 떨어지기 무섭게 방을 나가 버렸고, 에드먼드는 홀로 남아 앉아, 생각에 잠긴 언짢음 속에 난롯불을 뒤적였다."]], [["Fanny, who had heard it all, and borne Edmund company in every feeling throughout the whole, now ventured to say, in her anxiety to suggest some comfort, “Perhaps they may not be able to find any play to suit them.", "그 모든 것을 다 들었고, 그동안 내내 온갖 감정에서 에드먼드와 마음을 같이해 온 패니가, 이제 무슨 위로라도 건네려는 안타까운 마음에 조심스레 입을 뗐다. “어쩌면 그분들 마음에 맞는 연극을 못 찾을지도 몰라요."], ["Your brother’s taste and your sisters’ seem very different.”", "오빠 형과 언니들의 취향이 아주 다른 것 같으니까요.”"]], [["“I have no hope there, Fanny.", "“그건 기대할 게 못 돼, 패니."], ["If they persist in the scheme, they will find something.", "그 사람들이 이 계획을 밀어붙인다면, 뭐라도 찾아낼 거야."], ["I shall speak to my sisters and try to dissuade them, and that is all I can do.”", "나야 누이들한테 얘기해서 말려 보긴 하겠지만, 내가 할 수 있는 건 그게 다야.”"]], [["“I should think my aunt Norris would be on your side.”", "“노리스 이모라면 오빠 편을 드실 것 같은데요.”"]], [["“I dare say she would, but she has no influence with either Tom or my sisters that could be of any use; and if I cannot convince them myself, I shall let things take their course, without attempting it through her.", "“그러시겠지. 하지만 이모는 톰한테도 누이들한테도 쓸모 있을 만한 영향력이 없어."], ["Family squabbling is the greatest evil of all, and we had better do anything than be altogether by the ears.”", "그리고 내가 스스로 그들을 설득하지 못한다면, 이모를 통해 애써 보려 들지 않고 그냥 일이 흘러가는 대로 두겠어. 집안 다툼이야말로 무엇보다 나쁜 화근이니, 아예 서로 으르렁대느니 차라리 무슨 짓이든 하는 게 나아.”"]], [["His sisters, to whom he had an opportunity of speaking the next morning, were quite as impatient of his advice, quite as unyielding to his representation, quite as determined in the cause of pleasure, as Tom.", "이튿날 아침 그가 이야기를 나눌 기회를 얻은 그 누이들은, 톰과 꼭 마찬가지로 그의 충고를 못 견뎌 했고, 꼭 마찬가지로 그의 말에 굽히지 않았으며, 꼭 마찬가지로 즐거움을 좇는 일에 뜻이 굳었다. 어머니는 그 계획에 반대가 없었고, 그들은 아버지가 못마땅해하실까 조금도 겁내지 않았다."], ["Their mother had no objection to the plan, and they were not in the least afraid of their father’s disapprobation.", "그토록 많은 존경받는 집안에서, 그토록 많은 지체 높은 여인들이 해 온 일에 무슨 해가 있을 리 없었다."], ["There could be no harm in what had been done in so many respectable families, and by so many women of the first consideration; and it must be scrupulousness run mad that could see anything to censure in a plan like theirs, comprehending only brothers and sisters and intimate friends, and which would never be heard of beyond themselves.", "그리고 오누이와 가까운 벗들만 끼고, 저희 말고는 결코 소문날 일 없는 이런 계획에서 나무랄 구석을 찾아낼 수 있다면, 그거야말로 미쳐 날뛰는 결벽일 따름이었다. 줄리아는 과연 마리아의 처지가 각별한 조심과 신중을 요하리라는 것은 인정하는 눈치였다—하지만 그게 저한테까지 미칠 수는 없었다—저는 자유로운 몸이었으니까."], ["Julia did seem inclined to admit that Maria’s situation might require particular caution and delicacy—but that could not extend to her—she was at liberty; and Maria evidently considered her engagement as only raising her so much more above restraint, and leaving her less occasion than Julia to consult either father or mother.", "그리고 마리아는, 제 약혼이 오히려 자신을 그만큼 더 구속 위로 들어 올려 줄 뿐이고, 줄리아보다 아버지든 어머니든 여쭐 일이 더 적게 만들어 준다고 여기는 게 분명했다. 에드먼드는 기대할 게 별로 없었으나, 그래도 여전히 그 화제를 밀어붙이고 있는데, 그때 헨리 크로퍼드가 목사관에서 막 건너와 방으로 들어서며 소리쳤다."], ["Edmund had little to hope, but he was still urging the subject when Henry Crawford entered the room, fresh from the Parsonage, calling out, “No want of hands in our theatre, Miss Bertram.", "“우리 극장엔 일손 걱정 없어요, 버트럼 양. 잔심부름꾼 걱정도 없고요."], ["No want of understrappers: my sister desires her love, and hopes to be admitted into the company, and will be happy to take the part of any old duenna or tame confidante, that you may not like to do yourselves.”", "제 누이가 안부를 전하면서, 저희 무리에 끼워 주기를 바란다지 뭡니까. 그리고 여러분이 손수 맡기 싫은 늙은 시녀 역이든 얌전한 말벗 역이든 뭐든 기꺼이 맡겠답니다.”"]], [["Maria gave Edmund a glance, which meant, “What say you now?", "마리아가 에드먼드에게 흘긋 눈길을 던졌는데, 그것은 이런 뜻이었다. “이제 뭐라고 할래?"], ["Can we be wrong if Mary Crawford feels the same?”", "메리 크로퍼드도 똑같이 여긴다면 우리가 틀렸을 리 없잖아?”"], ["And Edmund, silenced, was obliged to acknowledge that the charm of acting might well carry fascination to the mind of genius; and with the ingenuity of love, to dwell more on the obliging, accommodating purport of the message than on anything else.", "그리고 에드먼드는, 할 말을 잃은 채, 연기의 매력이 천재의 마음까지 능히 홀릴 수 있음을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 사랑에 빠진 이 특유의 눈속임으로, 그 전갈에서 다른 무엇보다도 그 살갑고 너그러운 뜻에만 마음을 두게 되었다."]], [["The scheme advanced.", "계획은 진척되었다."], ["Opposition was vain; and as to Mrs.", "반대는 헛일이었다."], ["Norris, he was mistaken in supposing she would wish to make any.", "그리고 노리스 부인으로 말하면, 그녀가 반대하고 싶어 하리라 여긴 것은 그의 오산이었다."], ["She started no difficulties that were not talked down in five minutes by her eldest nephew and niece, who were all-powerful with her; and as the whole arrangement was to bring very little expense to anybody, and none at all to herself, as she foresaw in it all the comforts of hurry, bustle, and importance, and derived the immediate advantage of fancying herself obliged to leave her own house, where she had been living a month at her own cost, and take up her abode in theirs, that every hour might be spent in their service, she was, in fact, exceedingly delighted with the project.", "그녀가 꺼낸 어려움이란 것도, 그녀에게 절대적인 힘을 지닌 맏조카와 맏조카딸에게 오 분이면 죄다 눌려 버리는 것들뿐이었다. 게다가 그 온갖 채비가 누구에게도 비용이 거의 안 들고 그녀 자신에게는 한 푼도 안 드는 데다, 그 안에서 부산 떨고 법석대고 거들먹거릴 온갖 재미를 미리 내다보았고, 또 그동안 제 돈으로 한 달을 지내 온 제집을 떠나 그들의 집에 눌러앉아, 매시간을 그들 시중에 바쳐야 한다고 여기는 데서 오는 당장의 이득까지 챙겼으니, 사실 그녀는 그 계획이 여간 반갑지 않았다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIV", "# 제14장"]], [["Fanny seemed nearer being right than Edmund had supposed.", "패니의 짐작은 에드먼드가 여긴 것보다 옳은 쪽에 더 가까운 듯했다."], ["The business of finding a play that would suit everybody proved to be no trifle; and the carpenter had received his orders and taken his measurements, had suggested and removed at least two sets of difficulties, and having made the necessity of an enlargement of plan and expense fully evident, was already at work, while a play was still to seek.", "누구에게나 맞을 연극을 찾는 일은 결코 사소한 노릇이 아님이 드러났다. 목수는 지시를 받고 치수를 재었으며, 적어도 두 차례 어려움을 이렇게 저렇게 내놓았다 물렸다 했고, 계획과 비용을 늘려야 할 필요를 낱낱이 분명히 해 둔 채 이미 일에 착수했건만, 연극은 여전히 찾는 중이었다."], ["Other preparations were also in hand. An enormous roll of green baize had arrived from Northampton, and been cut out by Mrs.", "다른 채비들도 진행 중이었다."], ["Norris (with a saving by her good management of full three-quarters of a yard), and was actually forming into a curtain by the housemaids, and still the play was wanting; and as two or three days passed away in this manner, Edmund began almost to hope that none might ever be found.", "노샘프턴에서 초록색 나사가 어마어마하게 한 두루마리 도착해 노리스 부인 손에 재단되었고(그녀의 알뜰한 살림솜씨 덕에 꼬박 사분의 삼 야드나 아꼈다), 실제로 하녀들 손에서 막으로 짜여 가고 있었으나, 그래도 연극은 없었다. 그리고 이런 식으로 이삼 일이 흘러가자, 에드먼드는 어쩌면 끝내 아무 연극도 못 찾을지 모른다는 희망을 슬며시 품기 시작했다."]], [["There were, in fact, so many things to be attended to, so many people to be pleased, so many best characters required, and, above all, such a need that the play should be at once both tragedy and comedy, that there did seem as little chance of a decision as anything pursued by youth and zeal could hold out.", "사실 챙겨야 할 것이 어찌나 많고, 비위를 맞춰야 할 사람이 어찌나 많고, 요구되는 으뜸 배역이 어찌나 많은지, 게다가 무엇보다도 그 연극이 비극이면서 동시에 희극이어야 한다는 요구까지 있었으니, 젊음과 열의가 좇는 그 어떤 일 못지않게 결말이 날 가망은 희박해 보였다."]], [["On the tragic side were the Miss Bertrams, Henry Crawford, and Mr.", "비극 편에는 버트럼 자매와 헨리 크로퍼드와 예이츠 씨가 있었고, 희극 편에는 톰 버트럼이 있었는데, 아주 혼자만은 아니었으니 메리 크로퍼드의 바람도—비록 정중히 감추어져 있었으나—같은 쪽으로 기운 것이 분명했기 때문이다."], ["Yates; on the comic, Tom Bertram, not quite alone, because it was evident that Mary Crawford’s wishes, though politely kept back, inclined the same way: but his determinateness and his power seemed to make allies unnecessary; and, independent of this great irreconcilable difference, they wanted a piece containing very few characters in the whole, but every character first-rate, and three principal women.", "그러나 톰의 단호함과 힘은 딱히 동지를 필요로 하지 않는 듯했다. 그리고 이 크나큰 화해 불가능한 차이는 차치하고라도, 그들이 원하는 것은 전체 배역 수는 아주 적으면서 배역 하나하나는 일급인, 그러면서 주연 여성이 셋인 작품이었다."], ["All the best plays were run over in vain.", "훌륭한 연극이란 연극은 죄다 헛되이 훑어보았다."], ["Neither Hamlet, nor Macbeth, nor Othello, nor Douglas, nor The Gamester, presented anything that could satisfy even the tragedians; and The Rivals, The School for Scandal, Wheel of Fortune, Heir at Law, and a long et cetera, were successively dismissed with yet warmer objections.", "「햄릿」도, 「맥베스」도, 「오셀로」도, 「더글러스」도, 「노름꾼」도, 비극 편 사람들조차 만족시킬 만한 것은 하나도 내놓지 못했다. 그리고 「연적들」이며 「스캔들 학교」며 「운명의 수레바퀴」며 「법정 상속인」이며, 그 밖에 길게 이어지는 목록이, 잇따라 한층 더 뜨거운 반대에 부딪혀 물려졌다."], ["No piece could be proposed that did not supply somebody with a difficulty, and on one side or the other it was a continual repetition of, “Oh no, that will never do!", "누군가에게 곤란거리를 안기지 않는 작품이란 하나도 내놓을 수 없었으니, 이쪽에서든 저쪽에서든 이런 말이 끝없이 되풀이되었다. “아, 안 돼요, 그건 도무지 안 돼요!"], ["Let us have no ranting tragedies.", "고래고래 외치는 비극은 그만둡시다."], ["Too many characters.", "배역이 너무 많아요."], ["Not a tolerable woman’s part in the play.", "그 연극엔 봐줄 만한 여자 배역이 하나도 없어요."], ["Anything but that, my dear Tom.", "그것만 빼고 뭐든요, 톰."], ["It would be impossible to fill it up.", "그건 채울 도리가 없을 거예요."], ["One could not expect anybody to take such a part.", "그런 배역을 누가 맡으리라 기대할 수도 없고요."], ["Nothing but buffoonery from beginning to end.", "처음부터 끝까지 익살뿐이잖아요."], ["That might do, perhaps, but for the low parts.", "그건 어쩌면 될지도 모르겠는데, 저 천한 배역들만 아니면요."], ["If I must give my opinion, I have always thought it the most insipid play in the English language.", "정 제 의견을 말해야 한다면, 저는 늘 그게 영어로 쓰인 연극 중에 가장 맹숭맹숭한 작품이라 여겨 왔어요."], ["I do not wish to make objections; I shall be happy to be of any use, but I think we could not chuse worse.”", "저야 반대하고 싶진 않아요. 무슨 쓸모든 되면 기쁘겠지만, 이보다 더 나쁘게 고를 수는 없을 것 같네요.”"]], [["Fanny looked on and listened, not unamused to observe the selfishness which, more or less disguised, seemed to govern them all, and wondering how it would end.", "패니는 지켜보며 귀 기울였는데, 더하거나 덜하게 감춰진 채 그들 모두를 다스리는 듯한 그 이기심을 살피는 것이 아주 재미없지는 않았고, 이 일이 어찌 끝날까 궁금했다."], ["For her own gratification she could have wished that something might be acted, for she had never seen even half a play, but everything of higher consequence was against it.", "제 즐거움을 위해서라면 그녀는 무엇이든 상연되기를 바랄 수도 있었으니, 여태 연극이라곤 반 편도 본 적이 없었던 것이다. 그러나 그보다 더 중대한 온갖 것이 그것을 반대하고 있었다."]], [["“This will never do,”", "“이건 도무지 안 되겠어요.”"], ["said Tom Bertram at last.", "마침내 톰 버트럼이 말했다."], ["“We are wasting time most abominably.", "“우리가 시간을 지독히도 허비하고 있어요."], ["Something must be fixed on.", "뭐라도 하나 정해야 해요."], ["No matter what, so that something is chosen.", "뭐가 됐든 상관없으니, 하나만 고릅시다."], ["We must not be so nice.", "우리가 이렇게 까다롭게 굴어선 안 돼요."], ["A few characters too many must not frighten us.", "배역 몇 개쯤 남아돈다고 겁먹을 것도 없고요."], ["We must double them.", "그럼 배역을 겹쳐 맡으면 되지요."], ["We must descend a little.", "눈높이를 좀 낮춥시다."], ["If a part is insignificant, the greater our credit in making anything of it.", "배역이 하찮으면, 그걸 그럴싸하게 살려 내는 우리 공이 그만큼 더 큰 거고요."], ["From this moment I make no difficulties.", "이 순간부터 나는 아무 트집도 안 잡겠어요."], ["I take any part you chuse to give me, so as it be comic.", "여러분이 주는 배역이면 뭐든 맡겠어요, 희극이기만 하다면 말이죠."], ["Let it but be comic, I condition for nothing more.”", "희극이기만 하면 돼요, 그 밖에는 아무 조건도 안 걸겠어요.”"]], [["For about the fifth time he then proposed the Heir at Law, doubting only whether to prefer Lord Duberley or Dr.", "그러고서 그는 다섯 번째쯤 「법정 상속인」을 내놓으며, 다만 자기가 두벌리 경을 맡을지 팽글로스 박사를 맡을지만 망설였다."], ["Pangloss for himself; and very earnestly, but very unsuccessfully, trying to persuade the others that there were some fine tragic parts in the rest of the Dramatis Personæ.", "그리고 나머지 등장인물 가운데도 그럴듯한 비극 배역이 더러 있다고 다른 이들을 설득하려 무척 애썼으나, 무척 헛수고였다."]], [["The pause which followed this fruitless effort was ended by the same speaker, who, taking up one of the many volumes of plays that lay on the table, and turning it over, suddenly exclaimed—“Lovers’ Vows!", "이 헛된 노력 뒤에 이어진 침묵을 깬 것도 같은 사람이었으니, 그는 탁자에 놓인 숱한 연극 책 가운데 하나를 집어 들어 뒤적이다가, 불쑥 외쳤다—“「연인들의 맹세」!"], ["And why should not Lovers’ Vows do for us as well as for the Ravenshaws?", "이게 레이븐쇼 사람들한테 맞았다면 우리한테는 왜 못 맞겠어?"], ["How came it never to be thought of before?", "어째서 여태 이 생각을 못 했지?"], ["It strikes me as if it would do exactly.", "딱 들어맞을 것 같은데."], ["What say you all?", "다들 어떻게 생각해요?"], ["Here are two capital tragic parts for Yates and Crawford, and here is the rhyming Butler for me, if nobody else wants it; a trifling part, but the sort of thing I should not dislike, and, as I said before, I am determined to take anything and do my best.", "여기 예이츠와 크로퍼드한테 딱 좋은 훌륭한 비극 배역이 둘 있고, 여기 나한테는—달리 원하는 사람이 없다면—운문 읊는 집사가 있어요. 대수롭잖은 배역이지만 내가 싫어하지 않을 그런 것이고, 앞서 말했듯이 나는 뭐든 맡아 최선을 다하기로 작정했으니까요."], ["And as for the rest, they may be filled up by anybody.", "그리고 나머지야 아무나 채우면 돼요."], ["It is only Count Cassel and Anhalt.”", "카셀 백작과 안할트만 남으니까요.”"]], [["The suggestion was generally welcome.", "그 제안은 두루 환영받았다."], ["Everybody was growing weary of indecision, and the first idea with everybody was, that nothing had been proposed before so likely to suit them all.", "다들 우유부단함에 진력이 나던 참이었고, 저마다 처음 든 생각은 이제껏 이토록 모두에게 맞을 성싶은 것이 나온 적이 없다는 것이었다. 예이츠 씨가 유난히 기뻐했다."], ["Mr. Yates was particularly pleased: he had been sighing and longing to do the Baron at Ecclesford, had grudged every rant of Lord Ravenshaw’s, and been forced to re-rant it all in his own room.", "그는 에클스포드에서 남작 역을 하고 싶어 한숨지으며 애태우던 터라, 레이븐쇼 경의 열변 한 대목 한 대목이 아까웠고, 제 방에서 그것을 죄다 혼자 되뇌어 열변을 토할 수밖에 없었던 것이다."], ["The storm through Baron Wildenheim was the height of his theatrical ambition; and with the advantage of knowing half the scenes by heart already, he did now, with the greatest alacrity, offer his services for the part.", "빌덴하임 남작으로 폭풍처럼 몰아치는 것이야말로 그의 연극적 야망의 극치였고, 이미 장면 절반을 외고 있다는 이점까지 더해, 그는 이제 더없이 잽싸게 그 배역에 제 몫을 하겠노라 나섰다."], ["To do him justice, however, he did not resolve to appropriate it; for remembering that there was some very good ranting-ground in Frederick, he professed an equal willingness for that.", "그러나 공평히 말하자면, 그가 그것을 제 것으로 독차지하기로 마음먹은 것은 아니었으니, 프레더릭에도 열변을 토할 만한 아주 좋은 대목이 있음을 떠올리고는, 그 역도 똑같이 기꺼이 맡겠노라 밝혔다."], ["Henry Crawford was ready to take either. Whichever Mr.", "헨리 크로퍼드는 어느 쪽이든 맡을 태세였다."], ["Yates did not chuse would perfectly satisfy him, and a short parley of compliment ensued.", "예이츠 씨가 고르지 않는 쪽이면 무엇이든 흡족했으니, 짤막한 겸양의 실랑이가 오갔다."], ["Miss Bertram, feeling all the interest of an Agatha in the question, took on her to decide it, by observing to Mr. Yates that this was a point in which height and figure ought to be considered, and that his being the tallest, seemed to fit him peculiarly for the Baron.", "아가사의 온갖 관심을 절로 느끼던 버트럼 양이, 예이츠 씨에게 이건 키와 몸집을 헤아려야 할 대목이니, 그가 가장 훤칠하고 보면 남작 역에 유난히 어울리는 듯하다고 이르며, 그 일을 결판내는 소임을 스스로 떠맡았다."], ["She was acknowledged to be quite right, and the two parts being accepted accordingly, she was certain of the proper Frederick.", "그녀 말이 지당하다 인정되어 두 배역이 그에 따라 정해지니, 그녀는 마땅한 프레더릭이 나올 것을 확신하게 되었다."], ["Three of the characters were now cast, besides Mr. Rushworth, who was always answered for by Maria as willing to do anything; when Julia, meaning, like her sister, to be Agatha, began to be scrupulous on Miss Crawford’s account.", "이제 러시워스 씨 말고도 세 배역이 정해진 셈이었는데—러시워스 씨야 뭐든 기꺼이 하겠다고 늘 마리아가 대신 장담해 주었다—그때 언니와 마찬가지로 아가사가 될 작정이던 줄리아가, 크로퍼드 양을 생각하며 께름칙해하기 시작했다."]], [["“This is not behaving well by the absent,”", "“이건 자리에 없는 사람한테 도리가 아니에요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Here are not women enough.", "“여기 여자 배역이 넉넉지 않잖아요."], ["Amelia and Agatha may do for Maria and me, but here is nothing for your sister, Mr. Crawford.”", "어밀리아와 아가사야 마리아와 제가 맡으면 되겠지만, 여기 크로퍼드 씨의 누이한테 돌아갈 게 없어요.”"]], [["Mr. Crawford desired that might not be thought of: he was very sure his sister had no wish of acting but as she might be useful, and that she would not allow herself to be considered in the present case.", "크로퍼드 씨는 그런 건 염려 말라고 했다."], ["But this was immediately opposed by Tom Bertram, who asserted the part of Amelia to be in every respect the property of Miss Crawford, if she would accept it.", "제 누이는 쓸모가 있을 때 말고는 연기하고 싶은 마음이 조금도 없으며, 이런 경우라면 저를 헤아려 주기를 결코 바라지 않으리라 확신한다는 것이었다. 그러나 이 말은 곧바로 톰 버트럼의 반대에 부딪혔으니, 그는 어밀리아 역이야말로 크로퍼드 양이 맡아만 준다면 모든 면에서 그녀의 몫이라고 우겼다."], ["“It falls as naturally, as necessarily to her,”", "“그 역이 그녀한테 딱 어울리게, 딱 마땅하게 돌아가는 게,”"], ["said he, “as Agatha does to one or other of my sisters.", "그가 말했다. “아가사가 내 누이 둘 중 하나한테 그런 것과 매한가지예요."], ["It can be no sacrifice on their side, for it is highly comic.”", "그건 누이들 쪽에서 양보랄 것도 없어요, 지극히 희극적인 배역이니까요.”"]], [["A short silence followed.", "짧은 침묵이 뒤따랐다."], ["Each sister looked anxious; for each felt the best claim to Agatha, and was hoping to have it pressed on her by the rest.", "자매는 저마다 마음을 졸였으니, 저마다 아가사에 가장 정당한 권리가 있다 느꼈고, 나머지 사람들이 저한테 그 역을 떠맡겨 주기를 바라고 있었던 것이다."], ["Henry Crawford, who meanwhile had taken up the play, and with seeming carelessness was turning over the first act, soon settled the business.", "그새 연극 책을 집어 들고 짐짓 무심한 척 제1막을 뒤적이던 헨리 크로퍼드가, 이내 그 일을 매듭지었다."]], [["“I must entreat Miss Julia Bertram,”", "“줄리아 버트럼 양께 간곡히 청하건대,”"], ["said he, “not to engage in the part of Agatha, or it will be the ruin of all my solemnity.", "그가 말했다. “아가사 역은 맡지 말아 주세요, 안 그러면 제 근엄함이 죄다 무너지고 말 테니까요."], ["You must not, indeed you must not”", "정말이지 그러시면 안 됩니다.”"], ["(turning to her).", "하며 그녀 쪽으로 돌아앉았다."], ["“I could not stand your countenance dressed up in woe and paleness.", "“슬픔과 창백함으로 꾸민 그 얼굴을, 저는 도저히 마주 볼 수가 없어요."], ["The many laughs we have had together would infallibly come across me, and Frederick and his knapsack would be obliged to run away.”", "우리가 함께 웃어 댄 그 숱한 웃음이 어김없이 떠오를 테고, 그럼 프레더릭도 그의 배낭도 걸음아 날 살려라 도망칠밖에요.”"]], [["Pleasantly, courteously, it was spoken; but the manner was lost in the matter to Julia’s feelings.", "유쾌하고 정중하게 건넨 말이었다. 그러나 그 말투는 줄리아의 심경에는 그 속뜻에 묻혀 사라지고 말았다."], ["She saw a glance at Maria which confirmed the injury to herself: it was a scheme, a trick; she was slighted, Maria was preferred; the smile of triumph which Maria was trying to suppress shewed how well it was understood; and before Julia could command herself enough to speak, her brother gave his weight against her too, by saying, “Oh yes!", "그녀는 마리아 쪽으로 던진 눈짓 하나를 보았고, 그것이 제가 입은 상처를 확인시켜 주었다. 그건 하나의 꿍꿍이, 하나의 술책이었다."], ["Maria must be Agatha. Maria will be the best Agatha.", "저는 업신여겨졌고, 마리아가 뽑힌 것이다."], ["Though Julia fancies she prefers tragedy, I would not trust her in it.", "마리아가 애써 누르려던 그 득의의 미소가, 그것이 얼마나 뻔히 오간 일인지 여실히 드러냈다. 그리고 줄리아가 말을 꺼낼 만큼 저를 다스리기도 전에, 그녀의 오라비마저 이렇게 말해 그녀에게 불리한 쪽으로 힘을 보탰다."], ["There is nothing of tragedy about her.", "“아 그래! 마리아가 아가사를 맡아야지."], ["She has not the look of it.", "마리아가 가장 나은 아가사일 거야."], ["Her features are not tragic features, and she walks too quick, and speaks too quick, and would not keep her countenance.", "줄리아는 제가 비극을 더 좋아한다 여기지만, 나는 그 애한테 비극을 못 맡기겠어. 그 애한텐 비극다운 데가 하나도 없거든."], ["She had better do the old countrywoman: the Cottager’s wife; you had, indeed, Julia.", "비극다운 낯빛이 아니야. 이목구비가 비극적인 이목구비가 아니고, 걸음도 너무 빠르고, 말도 너무 빠르고, 얼굴 표정도 지그시 못 지킬걸."], ["Cottager’s wife is a very pretty part, I assure you.", "차라리 늙은 시골 아낙을 하는 게 나아. 소작인의 아내 말이야."], ["The old lady relieves the high-flown benevolence of her husband with a good deal of spirit.", "정말 그게 낫겠어, 줄리아. 소작인의 아내도 아주 어여쁜 배역이야, 정말이라니까."], ["You shall be Cottager’s wife.”", "그 늙은 아낙이 제 남편의 드높은 자비심을 자못 활기차게 받쳐 주거든. 넌 소작인의 아내를 해라.”"]], [["“Cottager’s wife!”", "“소작인의 아내라니!”"], ["cried Mr. Yates.", "예이츠 씨가 외쳤다."], ["“What are you talking of?", "“무슨 말씀이세요?"], ["The most trivial, paltry, insignificant part; the merest commonplace; not a tolerable speech in the whole.", "더없이 하찮고 보잘것없고 시시한 배역인데. 그야말로 흔해 빠진 것이고, 통틀어 봐줄 만한 대사 한 줄 없어요."], ["Your sister do that!", "누이분더러 그걸 하라니!"], ["It is an insult to propose it.", "그런 걸 내놓다니 모욕이에요."], ["At Ecclesford the governess was to have done it.", "에클스포드에서는 가정교사가 맡기로 돼 있었어요."], ["We all agreed that it could not be offered to anybody else.", "그건 다른 누구한테도 권할 수 없다고 우리 모두 뜻을 모았고요."], ["A little more justice, Mr. Manager, if you please.", "감독 나리, 부디 좀 더 공평하게 하시죠."], ["You do not deserve the office, if you cannot appreciate the talents of your company a little better.”", "제 무리의 재능을 그보다 조금이라도 더 알아볼 줄 모른다면, 그 소임을 맡을 자격이 없어요.”"]], [["“Why, as to that, my good friend, till I and my company have really acted there must be some guesswork; but I mean no disparagement to Julia. We cannot have two Agathas, and we must have one Cottager’s wife; and I am sure I set her the example of moderation myself in being satisfied with the old Butler. If the part is trifling she will have more credit in making something of it; and if she is so desperately bent against everything humorous, let her take Cottager’s speeches instead of Cottager’s wife’s, and so change the parts all through; he is solemn and pathetic enough, I am sure. It could make no difference in the play, and as for Cottager himself, when he has got his wife’s speeches, I would undertake him with all my heart.”", "“뭐, 그거야 이보게,” 톰이 말했다. “나와 내 무리가 실제로 연기를 해 보기 전엔 얼마간 어림짐작일 수밖에 없죠. 하지만 나는 줄리아를 깎아내릴 뜻은 없어요. 아가사를 둘 둘 수는 없고, 소작인의 아내는 하나 있어야 하잖아요. 그리고 나야말로 저 늙은 집사 역에 만족함으로써 스스로 절제의 본을 보였다고 봐요. 배역이 하찮으면 그걸 그럴싸하게 살려 내는 데서 그 애 공이 더 커질 테고요. 그리고 그 애가 우스운 것이라면 뭐든 그토록 기를 쓰고 마다한다면, 소작인의 아내 대사 대신 소작인 대사를 맡아서 배역을 통째로 맞바꾸면 돼요. 그 사람은 충분히 근엄하고 애처로우니까요, 정말이지. 연극엔 아무 지장도 없을 테고, 소작인 본인으로 말하면, 그가 제 아내 대사를 얻고 나면, 나야 기꺼이 그 역을 맡고말고요.”"]], [["“With all your partiality for Cottager’s wife,”", "“당신이 아무리 소작인의 아내를 아끼신다 해도,” 헨리 크로퍼드가 말했다."], ["said Henry Crawford, “it will be impossible to make anything of it fit for your sister, and we must not suffer her good-nature to be imposed on.", "“그걸 누이분한테 어울리게 살려 낼 도리는 없을 거예요. 그리고 우리가 그 착한 성품에 그런 짐을 지우게 둬선 안 되죠."], ["We must not allow her to accept the part.", "그 배역을 맡도록 놔둬선 안 됩니다."], ["She must not be left to her own complaisance.", "제 고분고분함에 저를 내맡기게 둬선 안 돼요."], ["Her talents will be wanted in Amelia.", "누이분의 재능은 어밀리아에 쓰여야 해요."], ["Amelia is a character more difficult to be well represented than even Agatha.", "어밀리아는 아가사보다도 오히려 제대로 그려 내기가 더 어려운 인물이거든요."], ["I consider Amelia is the most difficult character in the whole piece.", "저는 어밀리아야말로 이 작품 전체에서 가장 어려운 인물이라고 봐요."], ["It requires great powers, great nicety, to give her playfulness and simplicity without extravagance.", "지나침 없이 그 장난기와 천진함을 살려 내려면 대단한 역량과 대단한 섬세함이 필요하죠."], ["I have seen good actresses fail in the part.", "저는 훌륭한 여배우들이 그 역에서 실패하는 걸 봤어요."], ["Simplicity, indeed, is beyond the reach of almost every actress by profession.", "천진함이란 정말이지 이름난 여배우들도 거의 다 미치지 못하는 경지랍니다."], ["It requires a delicacy of feeling which they have not.", "그건 그들에게 없는 감정의 섬세함을 요구하거든요."], ["It requires a gentlewoman—a Julia Bertram.", "그건 귀한 집 규수를 요구해요—줄리아 버트럼 같은 분을요."], ["You will undertake it, I hope?”", "맡아 주실 거죠, 부디?”"], ["turning to her with a look of anxious entreaty, which softened her a little; but while she hesitated what to say, her brother again interposed with Miss Crawford’s better claim.", "하며 그녀 쪽으로 애타게 간청하는 눈빛을 돌리니, 그녀의 마음이 조금 누그러졌다. 그러나 그녀가 뭐라 할지 망설이는 사이, 그녀의 오라비가 다시 끼어들어 크로퍼드 양이 더 마땅하다는 쪽을 들었다."]], [["“No, no, Julia must not be Amelia.", "“아니, 아니, 줄리아는 어밀리아를 하면 안 돼."], ["It is not at all the part for her.", "그 애한텐 도무지 안 맞는 배역이야."], ["She would not like it.", "그 애가 좋아하지도 않을 거고."], ["She would not do well.", "잘하지도 못할 거야."], ["She is too tall and robust.", "그 앤 키가 너무 크고 다부지거든."], ["Amelia should be a small, light, girlish, skipping figure.", "어밀리아는 자그맣고 가뿐하고 소녀 같고 깡충거리는 몸매라야 해."], ["It is fit for Miss Crawford, and Miss Crawford only.", "그건 크로퍼드 양한테 어울려, 오직 크로퍼드 양한테만."], ["She looks the part, and I am persuaded will do it admirably.”", "그녀가 딱 그 배역다워 보이고, 틀림없이 훌륭하게 해낼 거라고 나는 믿어.”"]], [["Without attending to this, Henry Crawford continued his supplication.", "이에 아랑곳없이 헨리 크로퍼드는 계속 간청했다. “저희를 위해 꼭 좀 들어주세요,”"], ["“You must oblige us,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “indeed you must.", "“정말이지 그러셔야 해요."], ["When you have studied the character, I am sure you will feel it suit you.", "그 인물을 익히고 나면, 틀림없이 그게 당신께 잘 맞는다고 느끼실 거예요."], ["Tragedy may be your choice, but it will certainly appear that comedy chuses you.", "비극이 당신 선택일지 몰라도, 분명 희극이 당신을 선택한다는 게 드러날 테니까요."], ["You will be to visit me in prison with a basket of provisions; you will not refuse to visit me in prison?", "당신은 먹을거리를 담은 바구니를 들고 감옥으로 저를 찾아오시게 될 거예요. 감옥으로 저를 찾아오기를 마다하진 않으시겠죠?"], ["I think I see you coming in with your basket.”", "바구니를 들고 들어오시는 당신 모습이 눈에 선한 것 같아요.”"]], [["The influence of his voice was felt.", "그 목소리의 힘이 전해졌다."], ["Julia wavered; but was he only trying to soothe and pacify her, and make her overlook the previous affront?", "줄리아는 흔들렸다. 그러나 그는 그저 저를 달래고 어르어, 앞서의 모욕을 눈감게 하려는 것뿐이 아닐까?"], ["She distrusted him.", "그녀는 그를 믿지 못했다."], ["The slight had been most determined.", "그 홀대는 더없이 작심한 것이었다."], ["He was, perhaps, but at treacherous play with her.", "그는 어쩌면 저를 상대로 음흉한 장난을 치고 있을 뿐인지도 몰랐다. 그녀는 미심쩍은 눈으로 언니를 보았다."], ["She looked suspiciously at her sister; Maria’s countenance was to decide it: if she were vexed and alarmed—but Maria looked all serenity and satisfaction, and Julia well knew that on this ground Maria could not be happy but at her expense.", "마리아의 낯빛이 그것을 결판내 줄 터였다. 만일 언니가 언짢고 놀란 기색이라면—그러나 마리아는 온통 평온과 흡족함의 낯빛이었고, 이 대목에서 마리아가 기뻐할 수 있는 것은 오직 제 희생 위에서뿐임을 줄리아는 잘 알고 있었다."], ["With hasty indignation, therefore, and a tremulous voice, she said to him, “You do not seem afraid of not keeping your countenance when I come in with a basket of provisions—though one might have supposed—but it is only as Agatha that I was to be so overpowering!”", "그리하여 그녀는 발끈한 노기와 떨리는 목소리로 그에게 말했다. “제가 먹을거리 바구니를 들고 들어올 땐 얼굴 표정 못 지킬까 봐 겁나진 않으시나 봐요—누구는 그리 짐작했을 법도 한데—하지만 제가 그토록 압도적인 건 오직 아가사일 때뿐이었군요!”"], ["She stopped—Henry Crawford looked rather foolish, and as if he did not know what to say.", "그녀는 말을 멈추었다—헨리 크로퍼드는 어쩐지 멋쩍은 낯으로, 뭐라 해야 할지 모르는 듯했다."], ["Tom Bertram began again—", "톰 버트럼이 다시 입을 열었다—"]], [["“Miss Crawford must be Amelia.", "“크로퍼드 양이 어밀리아를 맡아야 해요."], ["She will be an excellent Amelia.”", "훌륭한 어밀리아가 될 거예요.”"]], [["“Do not be afraid of my wanting the character,”", "“내가 그 배역을 탐낼까 봐 겁낼 것 없어요.” 줄리아가 노기 서린 잽싼 말투로 외쳤다."], ["cried Julia, with angry quickness: “I am not to be Agatha, and I am sure I will do nothing else; and as to Amelia, it is of all parts in the world the most disgusting to me.", "“나는 아가사는 안 할 거고, 그 밖엔 아무것도 안 할 게 분명하니까요. 그리고 어밀리아로 말하면, 세상 온갖 배역 가운데 나한테 가장 진저리 나는 거예요."], ["I quite detest her.", "나는 그 여자가 아주 질색이야."], ["An odious, little, pert, unnatural, impudent girl.", "밉살스럽고 자그마하고 되바라지고 부자연스럽고 뻔뻔한 계집애라니."], ["I have always protested against comedy, and this is comedy in its worst form.”", "나는 늘 희극을 반대해 왔는데, 이건 희극 중에서도 가장 고약한 꼴이잖아.”"], ["And so saying, she walked hastily out of the room, leaving awkward feelings to more than one, but exciting small compassion in any except Fanny, who had been a quiet auditor of the whole, and who could not think of her as under the agitations of jealousy without great pity.", "그러고서 그녀는 총총히 방을 나가 버려, 한 사람 넘게 어색한 심경을 남겼으나, 패니 말고는 딱히 누구의 동정도 자아내지 못했다. 패니는 그 모든 것을 조용히 지켜본 이로서, 줄리아가 시샘에 휩싸여 있다고 여기니 크나큰 연민 없이는 그녀를 바라볼 수 없었던 것이다."]], [["A short silence succeeded her leaving them; but her brother soon returned to business and Lovers’ Vows, and was eagerly looking over the play, with Mr.", "그녀가 자리를 뜨자 짧은 침묵이 이어졌다."], ["Yates’s help, to ascertain what scenery would be necessary—while Maria and Henry Crawford conversed together in an under-voice, and the declaration with which she began of, “I am sure I would give up the part to Julia most willingly, but that though I shall probably do it very ill, I feel persuaded she would do it worse,”", "그러나 그녀의 오라비는 이내 본론과 「연인들의 맹세」로 돌아가, 예이츠 씨의 도움을 받아 어떤 무대 장치가 필요할지 알아내려 열심히 연극을 훑어보았다—그동안 마리아와 헨리 크로퍼드는 나직한 목소리로 이야기를 나누었고, 마리아가 “저야 그 배역을 정말 기꺼이 줄리아한테 넘겨줬을 거예요, 다만 제가 아무리 서툴게 한다 해도 그 애는 저보다 더 서툴게 하리라 믿어질 뿐이지요.”"], ["was doubtless receiving all the compliments it called for.", "하고 꺼낸 그 말은, 틀림없이 그에 걸맞은 온갖 찬사를 받고 있었다."]], [["When this had lasted some time, the division of the party was completed by Tom Bertram and Mr.", "이것이 얼마간 이어진 뒤, 톰 버트럼과 예이츠 씨가 이제 ‘극장’이라 불리기 시작한 그 방으로 함께 자리를 옮겨 더 의논하고, 버트럼 양은 어밀리아 역을 크로퍼드 양에게 권하러 몸소 목사관으로 내려가기로 하면서, 무리의 흩어짐이 마무리되었다."], ["Yates walking off together to consult farther in the room now beginning to be called the Theatre, and Miss Bertram’s resolving to go down to the Parsonage herself with the offer of Amelia to Miss Crawford; and Fanny remained alone.", "그리고 패니는 홀로 남았다."]], [["The first use she made of her solitude was to take up the volume which had been left on the table, and begin to acquaint herself with the play of which she had heard so much.", "그 홀로 있는 시간을 그녀가 맨 처음 쓴 방식은, 탁자에 남겨진 그 책을 집어 들어, 그토록 말이 많던 그 연극과 낯을 익히기 시작하는 것이었다."], ["Her curiosity was all awake, and she ran through it with an eagerness which was suspended only by intervals of astonishment, that it could be chosen in the present instance, that it could be proposed and accepted in a private theatre!", "호기심이 온통 깨어나, 그녀는 열띤 마음으로 그것을 훑어 내려갔는데, 그 열의는 오직 놀라움의 순간들에만 멈칫거렸다—이런 마당에 이런 것이 뽑히다니, 사가 극장에서 이런 것이 내놓아지고 받아들여지다니!"], ["Agatha and Amelia appeared to her in their different ways so totally improper for home representation—the situation of one, and the language of the other, so unfit to be expressed by any woman of modesty, that she could hardly suppose her cousins could be aware of what they were engaging in; and longed to have them roused as soon as possible by the remonstrance which Edmund would certainly make.", "아가사와 어밀리아는 저마다 다른 식으로 그녀에게 집안 상연에는 도무지 온당치 않아 보였다—한쪽의 처지와 다른 한쪽의 대사가, 정숙한 여자라면 누구도 입에 담기에 너무나 부적절해서, 그녀는 사촌들이 자기네가 뛰어드는 일이 무엇인지 알고나 있는지 도무지 짐작할 수 없었다. 그래서 에드먼드가 틀림없이 할 그 간언으로 어서 그들이 정신 차리기를 애타게 바랐다."]], [["# CHAPTER XV", "# 제15장"]], [["Miss Crawford accepted the part very readily; and soon after Miss Bertram’s return from the Parsonage, Mr.", "크로퍼드 양은 그 배역을 아주 선뜻 받아들였다. 그리고 버트럼 양이 목사관에서 돌아온 지 얼마 안 되어 러시워스 씨가 왔고, 그리하여 또 한 배역이 정해졌다."], ["Rushworth arrived, and another character was consequently cast.", "그는 카셀 백작과 안할트를 맡으라는 권유를 받았는데, 처음에는 어느 쪽을 고를지 몰라 버트럼 양이 정해 주기를 바랐다. 그러나 두 인물의 서로 다른 결이며 어느 것이 어느 것인지 일러 주자, 또 언젠가 런던에서 그 연극을 본 적이 있는데 안할트가 참 멍청한 녀석이라 여겼던 것을 떠올리고는, 이내 백작으로 마음을 정했다."], ["He had the offer of Count Cassel and Anhalt, and at first did not know which to chuse, and wanted Miss Bertram to direct him; but upon being made to understand the different style of the characters, and which was which, and recollecting that he had once seen the play in London, and had thought Anhalt a very stupid fellow, he soon decided for the Count.", "버트럼 양은 그 결정을 반겼으니, 그가 외울 게 적을수록 좋았기 때문이다. 그리고 그녀는 백작과 아가사가 함께 연기할 수 있으면 좋겠다는 그의 바람에는 공감할 수 없었고, 그가 그런 장면을 여태 찾을 수 있으려나 하는 기대로 책장을 느릿느릿 넘기는 동안 그리 참을성 있게 기다려 주지도 못했지만, 그래도 그의 배역을 아주 친절히 손수 맡아, 줄여도 될 만한 대사는 죄다 잘라 냈다."], ["Miss Bertram approved the decision, for the less he had to learn the better; and though she could not sympathise in his wish that the Count and Agatha might be to act together, nor wait very patiently while he was slowly turning over the leaves with the hope of still discovering such a scene, she very kindly took his part in hand, and curtailed every speech that admitted being shortened; besides pointing out the necessity of his being very much dressed, and chusing his colours. Mr.", "게다가 그가 아주 잘 차려입어야 한다는 점을 짚어 주고 그의 옷 빛깔까지 골라 주었다."], ["Rushworth liked the idea of his finery very well, though affecting to despise it; and was too much engaged with what his own appearance would be to think of the others, or draw any of those conclusions, or feel any of that displeasure which Maria had been half prepared for.", "러시워스 씨는 겉으로는 그런 치장을 업신여기는 척했으나, 실은 제 화려한 차림새라는 생각이 아주 마음에 들었다. 그리고 온통 제 겉모습이 어떠할지에만 정신이 팔려, 남들 생각을 하거나, 마리아가 얼마쯤 각오했던 그런 결론을 이끌어 내거나, 그런 언짢음을 느끼거나 할 겨를이 없었다."]], [["Thus much was settled before Edmund, who had been out all the morning, knew anything of the matter; but when he entered the drawing-room before dinner, the buzz of discussion was high between Tom, Maria, and Mr.", "이만큼이 정해진 것은, 아침 내내 밖에 나가 있던 에드먼드가 그 일을 조금이라도 알기 전이었다."], ["Yates; and Mr. Rushworth stepped forward with great alacrity to tell him the agreeable news.", "그러나 그가 만찬 전에 응접실에 들어서니, 톰과 마리아와 예이츠 씨 사이에 한창 왁자한 논의가 오가고 있었고, 러시워스 씨가 그 반가운 소식을 전하려 자못 잽싸게 앞으로 나섰다."]], [["“We have got a play,” said he.", "“우리 연극을 정했어요.”"], ["“It is to be Lovers’ Vows; and I am to be Count Cassel, and am to come in first with a blue dress and a pink satin cloak, and afterwards am to have another fine fancy suit, by way of a shooting-dress.", "그가 말했다. “「연인들의 맹세」로 하기로 했고요, 저는 카셀 백작을 맡아서, 처음엔 파란 옷에 분홍 새틴 망토를 걸치고 나오고, 나중엔 사냥 복장 삼아 또 다른 멋들어진 옷을 입기로 했어요."], ["I do not know how I shall like it.”", "이게 제 마음에 들지는 모르겠네요.”"]], [["Fanny’s eyes followed Edmund, and her heart beat for him as she heard this speech, and saw his look, and felt what his sensations must be.", "패니의 눈은 에드먼드를 좇았고, 이 말을 듣고 그의 낯빛을 보며 그의 심경이 어떠할지 헤아리자, 그녀의 가슴은 그를 위해 두근거렸다."]], [["“Lovers’ Vows!”", "“「연인들의 맹세」라니!”"], ["in a tone of the greatest amazement, was his only reply to Mr.", "더없이 아연실색한 어조로 내뱉은 이 한마디가, 러시워스 씨에게 건넨 그의 유일한 대꾸였다."], ["Rushworth, and he turned towards his brother and sisters as if hardly doubting a contradiction.", "그리고 그는 부정의 대답을 거의 의심치 않는 듯, 형과 누이들 쪽으로 돌아섰다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“그렇답니다.”"], ["cried Mr. Yates.", "예이츠 씨가 외쳤다."], ["“After all our debatings and difficulties, we find there is nothing that will suit us altogether so well, nothing so unexceptionable, as Lovers’ Vows.", "“우리가 그토록 갑론을박하고 애먹은 끝에, 「연인들의 맹세」만큼 우리 모두에게 딱 맞는 것도, 흠잡을 데 없는 것도 없다는 걸 알아냈어요."], ["The wonder is that it should not have been thought of before.", "어째서 진작 이 생각을 못 했는지 그게 놀라울 따름이에요."], ["My stupidity was abominable, for here we have all the advantage of what I saw at Ecclesford; and it is so useful to have anything of a model!", "내 아둔함이 지독했지요, 이렇게 내가 에클스포드에서 본 것을 몽땅 유리하게 써먹을 수 있는데 말이에요. 게다가 본보기가 될 게 하나라도 있다는 건 참 요긴하거든요!"], ["We have cast almost every part.”", "우리는 거의 모든 배역을 정했어요.”"]], [["“But what do you do for women?”", "“그런데 여자 배역은 어떻게 하고?”"], ["said Edmund gravely, and looking at Maria.", "에드먼드가 심각하게, 마리아를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["Maria blushed in spite of herself as she answered, “I take the part which Lady Ravenshaw was to have done, and”", "마리아는 저도 모르게 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다. “내가 레이븐쇼 부인이 맡기로 돼 있던 배역을 맡고,”"], ["(with a bolder eye) “Miss Crawford is to be Amelia.”", "그러고는 (한결 대담한 눈빛으로) “크로퍼드 양이 어밀리아를 맡아.”"]], [["“I should not have thought it the sort of play to be so easily filled up, with us,”", "“나라면 이게, 우리끼리 그리 쉽게 채울 수 있는 그런 연극이라곤 생각지 못했을 텐데.”"], ["replied Edmund, turning away to the fire, where sat his mother, aunt, and Fanny, and seating himself with a look of great vexation.", "에드먼드가 대꾸하며, 어머니와 이모와 패니가 앉아 있는 난롯가로 돌아서서, 잔뜩 언짢은 낯빛으로 자리에 앉았다."]], [["Mr. Rushworth followed him to say, “I come in three times, and have two-and-forty speeches.", "러시워스 씨가 그를 뒤따라와 말했다."], ["That’s something, is not it?", "“저는 세 번 등장하고, 대사가 마흔두 마디예요."], ["But I do not much like the idea of being so fine.", "그쯤이면 대단하죠, 안 그래요? 하지만 그렇게 곱게 차려입는다는 건 그리 내키지 않네요."], ["I shall hardly know myself in a blue dress and a pink satin cloak.”", "파란 옷에 분홍 새틴 망토를 걸치면 나도 나를 못 알아볼 것 같아요.”"]], [["Edmund could not answer him. In a few minutes Mr.", "에드먼드는 그에게 대꾸할 수 없었다."], ["Bertram was called out of the room to satisfy some doubts of the carpenter; and being accompanied by Mr. Yates, and followed soon afterwards by Mr.", "몇 분 뒤 버트럼 씨가 목수의 미심쩍은 대목을 풀어 주러 방 밖으로 불려 나갔고, 예이츠 씨가 그를 따라나서고 이내 러시워스 씨까지 뒤따라 나가자, 에드먼드는 거의 곧바로 기회를 잡아 이렇게 말했다."], ["Rushworth, Edmund almost immediately took the opportunity of saying, “I cannot, before Mr.", "“예이츠 씨 앞에서는, 에클스포드에 있는 그 벗들을 흉보지 않고서는 이 연극에 대해 내가 느끼는 바를 말할 수가 없어."], ["Yates, speak what I feel as to this play, without reflecting on his friends at Ecclesford; but I must now, my dear Maria, tell you, that I think it exceedingly unfit for private representation, and that I hope you will give it up.", "하지만 이제, 마리아야, 너한테는 말해야겠다. 나는 이게 사가 상연에는 지극히 부적절하다고 보고, 네가 이걸 그만두기를 바란다."], ["I cannot but suppose you will when you have read it carefully over.", "네가 이걸 찬찬히 읽어 보고 나면 틀림없이 그러리라고 나는 믿어 의심치 않아."], ["Read only the first act aloud to either your mother or aunt, and see how you can approve it.", "어머니든 이모든 앞에서 제1막만이라도 소리 내어 읽어 보고, 네가 그걸 옳다 여길 수 있는지 봐."], ["It will not be necessary to send you to your father’s judgment, I am convinced.”", "굳이 너를 아버지의 판단 앞에까지 데려갈 필요는 없으리라고 나는 확신한다.”"]], [["“We see things very differently,”", "“우리는 세상을 아주 다르게 보는구나.” 마리아가 외쳤다."], ["cried Maria.", "“나는 이 연극을 훤히 꿰고 있어, 정말이야."], ["“I am perfectly acquainted with the play, I assure you; and with a very few omissions, and so forth, which will be made, of course, I can see nothing objectionable in it; and I am not the only young woman you find who thinks it very fit for private representation.”", "그리고 물론 손볼 몇 군데만 빼내면, 나는 이게 흠잡을 데라곤 없다고 봐. 그리고 이게 사가 상연에 아주 알맞다고 여기는 젊은 여자가 나 하나만은 아니야.”"]], [["“I am sorry for it,”", "“그건 유감이구나.”"], ["was his answer; “but in this matter it is you who are to lead.", "그가 대답했다. “하지만 이 일에서는 네가 앞장서야 해."], ["You must set the example.", "네가 본을 보여야 한다고."], ["If others have blundered, it is your place to put them right, and shew them what true delicacy is.", "남들이 실수했다면, 그들을 바로잡고 참된 조심성이 무엇인지 보여 주는 게 네 몫이야."], ["In all points of decorum your conduct must be law to the rest of the party.”", "예의범절의 모든 대목에서 네 처신이 나머지 사람들에게 법이 되어야 해.”"]], [["This picture of her consequence had some effect, for no one loved better to lead than Maria; and with far more good-humour she answered, “I am much obliged to you, Edmund; you mean very well, I am sure: but I still think you see things too strongly; and I really cannot undertake to harangue all the rest upon a subject of this kind. There would be the greatest indecorum, I think.”", "제 위상을 그려 보이는 이 말은 얼마간 효과가 있었으니, 마리아만큼 앞장서기를 좋아하는 사람도 없었기 때문이다. 그리하여 그녀는 한결 누그러진 목소리로 대답했다. “고마워, 에드먼드. 오빠 뜻이 참 좋다는 건 나도 알아. 하지만 그래도 나는 오빠가 세상을 너무 지나치게 본다고 생각해. 그리고 나로선 정말이지 이런 일로 나머지 사람들을 죄다 붙잡고 일장 연설을 늘어놓을 수는 없어. 그거야말로 더없이 무례한 짓일 거야, 내 생각엔.”"]], [["“Do you imagine that I could have such an idea in my head?", "“내가 그런 생각을 머릿속에 품었을 성싶으냐? 아니야."], ["No; let your conduct be the only harangue.", "오직 네 처신만이 네 연설이 되게 하렴."], ["Say that, on examining the part, you feel yourself unequal to it; that you find it requiring more exertion and confidence than you can be supposed to have.", "그 배역을 살펴보니 네 힘에 부치더라고, 네가 지녔음직한 것보다 더 큰 노력과 자신감을 요구하는 것 같더라고 말해."], ["Say this with firmness, and it will be quite enough.", "이걸 단호하게 말하면, 그걸로 충분할 거야."], ["All who can distinguish will understand your motive.", "분별할 줄 아는 이라면 누구나 네 속뜻을 알아들을 테고."], ["The play will be given up, and your delicacy honoured as it ought.”", "연극은 접힐 테고, 네 조심성은 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 기려질 거야.”"]], [["“Do not act anything improper, my dear,”", "“온당찮은 건 하지 마라, 얘야.”"], ["said Lady Bertram.", "버트럼 부인이 말했다."], ["“Sir Thomas would not like it.—Fanny, ring the bell; I must have my dinner.—To be sure, Julia is dressed by this time.”", "“토머스 경께서 좋아하지 않으실 게다.—패니, 종을 울려라. 나 만찬을 들어야겠구나.—하기야, 줄리아는 이맘때면 옷을 다 차려입었겠지.”"]], [["“I am convinced, madam,”", "“저는 확신해요, 어머니,”"], ["said Edmund, preventing Fanny, “that Sir Thomas would not like it.”", "에드먼드가 패니를 앞질러 말했다. “토머스 경께서 좋아하지 않으시리란 걸요.”"]], [["“There, my dear, do you hear what Edmund says?”", "“거봐라, 얘야, 에드먼드가 하는 말 들었지?”"]], [["“If I were to decline the part,”", "“내가 그 배역을 마다하면,”"], ["said Maria, with renewed zeal, “Julia would certainly take it.”", "마리아가 새삼 열을 올리며 말했다. “줄리아가 틀림없이 맡을 거야.”"]], [["“What!”", "“뭐라고!”"], ["cried Edmund, “if she knew your reasons!”", "에드먼드가 외쳤다. “그 애가 네 이유를 안다면 말이지!”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아! 그 앤 우리 사이의 차이를—우리 처지의 차이를—떠올리겠지."], ["she might think the difference between us—the difference in our situations—that she need not be so scrupulous as I might feel necessary.", "나야 필요하다고 느낄 만한 그 조심성을 그 앤 굳이 안 지켜도 된다고 말이야. 그 앤 분명 그렇게 우겨 댈 거야."], ["I am sure she would argue so.", "아니, 오빠, 나를 봐줘. 나는 승낙을 물릴 수 없어."], ["No; you must excuse me; I cannot retract my consent; it is too far settled, everybody would be so disappointed, Tom would be quite angry; and if we are so very nice, we shall never act anything.”", "너무 멀리 정해져 버렸고, 다들 무척 실망할 테고, 톰은 아주 화를 낼 거야. 그리고 우리가 이렇게 까다롭게 굴다간, 우리는 아무것도 못 하게 될걸.”"]], [["“I was just going to say the very same thing,”", "“나도 딱 그 말을 하려던 참이었어요.”"], ["said Mrs. Norris.", "노리스 부인이 말했다."], ["“If every play is to be objected to, you will act nothing, and the preparations will be all so much money thrown away, and I am sure that would be a discredit to us all.", "“연극이란 연극마다 다 트집을 잡을 양이면, 너희는 아무것도 못 할 테고, 그 채비들도 죄다 돈만 내다 버린 꼴이 될 테고, 그거야말로 우리 모두한테 망신일 게 분명해요."], ["I do not know the play; but, as Maria says, if there is anything a little too warm (and it is so with most of them) it can be easily left out.", "나야 그 연극을 모르지만, 마리아 말마따나 조금 지나치게 야한 데가 있다면(대개 다 그렇지요) 그거야 손쉽게 빼내면 그만이에요."], ["We must not be over-precise, Edmund. As Mr.", "우리가 너무 깐깐하게 굴어선 안 돼요, 에드먼드."], ["Rushworth is to act too, there can be no harm.", "러시워스 씨도 함께 연기하니, 해로울 게 없어요."], ["I only wish Tom had known his own mind when the carpenters began, for there was the loss of half a day’s work about those side-doors.", "다만 목수들이 일을 시작할 때 톰이 제 마음을 진작 정했더라면 싶어요, 그 옆문들 때문에 반나절 품이 날아갔거든요."], ["The curtain will be a good job, however.", "그래도 막은 잘된 일이지요."], ["The maids do their work very well, and I think we shall be able to send back some dozens of the rings.", "하녀들이 일을 아주 잘하니, 고리는 몇 다스쯤 되돌려 보낼 수 있을 것 같아요."], ["There is no occasion to put them so very close together.", "그걸 그렇게 촘촘히 달 것까진 없거든요."], ["I am of some use, I hope, in preventing waste and making the most of things.", "나도 그래도 얼마쯤 쓸모가 있는가 봐요, 허투루 쓰는 걸 막고 뭐든 알뜰히 굴리는 데는 말이에요."], ["There should always be one steady head to superintend so many young ones.", "이렇게 많은 젊은것들을 건사하자면 진득한 어른 하나쯤은 늘 있어야 하는 법이지요."], ["I forgot to tell Tom of something that happened to me this very day.", "아 참, 오늘 나한테 있었던 일을 톰한테 이르는 걸 깜빡했네."], ["I had been looking about me in the poultry-yard, and was just coming out, when who should I see but Dick Jackson making up to the servants’ hall-door with two bits of deal board in his hand, bringing them to father, you may be sure; mother had chanced to send him of a message to father, and then father had bid him bring up them two bits of board, for he could not no how do without them.", "내가 닭장을 둘러보고 막 나오는데, 글쎄 딕 잭슨이 손에 전나무 판자 두 쪽을 들고 하인방 문 쪽으로 다가가지 뭐예요, 틀림없이 제 아비한테 갖다주려는 거였지요. 제 어미가 마침 그 애를 시켜 아비한테 뭘 전하게 했는데, 그러고 나서 아비가 그 판자 두 쪽을 가져오라고 시킨 거예요, 그게 없인 도무지 일을 못 하겠다면서요."], ["I knew what all this meant, for the servants’ dinner-bell was ringing at the very moment over our heads; and as I hate such encroaching people (the Jacksons are very encroaching, I have always said so: just the sort of people to get all they can), I said to the boy directly (a great lubberly fellow of ten years old, you know, who ought to be ashamed of himself), ‘I’ll take the boards to your father, Dick, so get you home again as fast as you can.’ The boy looked very silly, and turned away without offering a word, for I believe I might speak pretty sharp; and I dare say it will cure him of coming marauding about the house for one while.", "나야 이게 다 무슨 속셈인지 훤히 알았지요, 바로 그 순간 우리 머리 위에서 하인들 저녁 종이 울리고 있었으니까요. 그리고 나는 그렇게 야금야금 파고드는 사람들이 질색이라(잭슨네가 아주 그런 축이지요, 늘 그리 말해 왔어요, 딱 얻어 낼 수 있는 건 죄다 얻어 내는 그런 부류거든요), 그 애한테 대뜸 말했어요(알다시피 열 살배기 덩치만 큰 굼벵이 녀석인데, 제 딴엔 부끄러운 줄 알아야지요), ‘판자는 내가 네 아비한테 갖다줄 테니, 딕, 넌 어서 집으로 냉큼 돌아가거라.’ 그 애는 아주 멍한 낯으로, 한마디도 없이 돌아서더군요, 아마 내가 꽤나 매섭게 말했나 봐요."], ["I hate such greediness—so good as your father is to the family, employing the man all the year round!”", "그리고 이걸로 한동안은 그 애가 집 근처로 얼쩡대며 노략질하는 버릇이 고쳐지지 싶어요. 나는 그런 게걸스러움이 질색이에요—너희 아버지가 그 집안에 얼마나 잘해 주는데, 일 년 내내 그 사람을 부려 주면서 말이지요!”"]], [["Nobody was at the trouble of an answer; the others soon returned; and Edmund found that to have endeavoured to set them right must be his only satisfaction.", "아무도 굳이 대꾸하려 들지 않았다. 이내 다른 이들이 돌아왔고, 에드먼드는 그들을 바로잡으려 애쓴 것으로 만족하는 수밖에 없음을 깨달았다."]], [["Dinner passed heavily. Mrs.", "만찬은 무겁게 흘러갔다."], ["Norris related again her triumph over Dick Jackson, but neither play nor preparation were otherwise much talked of, for Edmund’s disapprobation was felt even by his brother, though he would not have owned it.", "노리스 부인은 딕 잭슨을 이긴 무용담을 또 한 번 늘어놓았으나, 그것 말고는 연극도 채비도 딱히 크게 입에 오르지 않았다. 에드먼드의 못마땅함을 그 형마저도, 비록 인정하려 들진 않았으나, 느끼고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Maria, wanting Henry Crawford’s animating support, thought the subject better avoided.", "마리아는 헨리 크로퍼드의 북돋는 힘이 없으니 그 화제는 피하는 게 낫다 여겼다."], ["Mr. Yates, who was trying to make himself agreeable to Julia, found her gloom less impenetrable on any topic than that of his regret at her secession from their company; and Mr.", "줄리아의 비위를 맞춰 보려던 예이츠 씨는, 그녀가 저희 무리에서 빠져나간 것을 자기가 아쉬워한다는 화제 말고는, 그 어떤 화제로도 그녀의 우울이 그리 뚫기 어렵지 않음을 알았다."], ["Rushworth, having only his own part and his own dress in his head, had soon talked away all that could be said of either.", "그리고 오로지 제 배역과 제 옷차림밖에 머릿속에 없던 러시워스 씨는, 그 둘에 대해 할 수 있는 말은 이내 죄다 지껄여 버린 뒤였다."]], [["But the concerns of the theatre were suspended only for an hour or two: there was still a great deal to be settled; and the spirits of evening giving fresh courage, Tom, Maria, and Mr.", "그러나 극장에 얽힌 일들은 겨우 한두 시간 미뤄졌을 뿐이었다. 아직도 정할 것이 잔뜩 있었던 것이다."], ["Yates, soon after their being reassembled in the drawing-room, seated themselves in committee at a separate table, with the play open before them, and were just getting deep in the subject when a most welcome interruption was given by the entrance of Mr.", "그리고 저녁의 활기가 새 용기를 북돋우자, 톰과 마리아와 예이츠 씨는, 응접실에 다시 모이고 얼마 안 되어, 따로 놓인 탁자에 위원회처럼 둘러앉아 연극을 펼쳐 놓고 막 그 일에 깊이 빠져들던 참이었는데, 그때 크로퍼드 남매가 들어와 더없이 반가운 훼방을 놓았다."], ["and Miss Crawford, who, late and dark and dirty as it was, could not help coming, and were received with the most grateful joy.", "늦은 데다 어둡고 길이 질척였건만 그들은 오지 않고는 못 배겼고, 더없이 고마운 기쁨 속에 맞아들여졌다."]], [["“Well, how do you go on?”", "“자, 어떻게 돼 가요?”"], ["and “What have you settled?”", "그리고 “뭘 정하셨어요?”"], ["and “Oh!", "그리고 “아!"], ["we can do nothing without you,”", "당신들 없인 우리가 아무것도 못 해요”"], ["followed the first salutations; and Henry Crawford was soon seated with the other three at the table, while his sister made her way to Lady Bertram, and with pleasant attention was complimenting her.", "하는 말들이 첫인사에 뒤따랐다. 그리고 헨리 크로퍼드는 이내 나머지 셋과 탁자에 둘러앉았고, 그동안 그 누이는 버트럼 부인에게로 다가가, 상냥한 마음씨로 부인에게 인사치레를 건넸다."], ["“I must really congratulate your ladyship,”", "“정말이지 부인께 축하 말씀 드려야겠어요.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “on the play being chosen; for though you have borne it with exemplary patience, I am sure you must be sick of all our noise and difficulties.", "“연극이 정해진 걸 두고요. 부인께서야 본보기가 될 만큼 참을성 있게 견뎌 오셨지만, 저희의 그 온갖 소란과 옥신각신에 진저리가 나셨을 게 틀림없거든요."], ["The actors may be glad, but the bystanders must be infinitely more thankful for a decision; and I do sincerely give you joy, madam, as well as Mrs.", "배우들이야 기쁘겠지만, 곁에서 지켜보는 이들은 결판이 난 것을 그보다 한없이 더 고마워할 게 분명하지요. 그러니 저는 진심으로 부인께 기쁨을 빌어 드려요, 노리스 부인께도, 또 같은 처지에 계신 다른 모든 분께도요.”"], ["Norris, and everybody else who is in the same predicament,” glancing half fearfully, half slyly, beyond Fanny to Edmund.", "하며 패니 너머로 에드먼드 쪽을, 반은 겁먹은 듯 반은 짓궂은 듯 흘긋 보았다."]], [["She was very civilly answered by Lady Bertram, but Edmund said nothing.", "버트럼 부인은 아주 정중하게 대답했으나, 에드먼드는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["His being only a bystander was not disclaimed.", "자기가 그저 곁에서 지켜보는 사람일 뿐이라는 것을 그는 부인하지 않았다."], ["After continuing in chat with the party round the fire a few minutes, Miss Crawford returned to the party round the table; and standing by them, seemed to interest herself in their arrangements till, as if struck by a sudden recollection, she exclaimed, “My good friends, you are most composedly at work upon these cottages and alehouses, inside and out; but pray let me know my fate in the meanwhile.", "난롯가 무리와 몇 분 더 담소를 나눈 뒤, 크로퍼드 양은 탁자 쪽 무리에게로 돌아가, 그 곁에 서서 그들의 채비에 관심을 두는 듯하더니, 문득 무언가 떠오른 듯 외쳤다. “여러분, 이 오두막이며 술집이며 안팎을 참으로 태연히도 손보고 계시네요."], ["Who is to be Anhalt?", "하지만 그동안 부디 제 운명이나 좀 알려 주세요."], ["What gentleman among you am I to have the pleasure of making love to?”", "안할트는 누가 맡죠? 제가 여러분 중 어느 신사분께 사랑을 고백하는 기쁨을 누리게 되나요?”"]], [["For a moment no one spoke; and then many spoke together to tell the same melancholy truth, that they had not yet got any Anhalt.", "한순간 아무도 입을 열지 않았다. 그러다 여럿이 한꺼번에 입을 열어 같은 서글픈 진실을 전했으니, 아직 안할트를 못 구했다는 것이었다."], ["“Mr. Rushworth was to be Count Cassel, but no one had yet undertaken Anhalt.”", "“러시워스 씨가 카셀 백작을 맡기로 했지만, 안할트는 아직 아무도 안 맡았어요.”"]], [["“I had my choice of the parts,” said Mr.", "“저는 배역을 고를 수 있었지요.”"], ["Rushworth; “but I thought I should like the Count best, though I do not much relish the finery I am to have.”", "러시워스 씨가 말했다. “하지만 백작이 가장 마음에 들 것 같았어요, 걸치게 될 그 화려한 차림새는 그리 내키지 않지만요.”"]], [["“You chose very wisely, I am sure,”", "“아주 현명하게 고르셨네요, 정말이에요.”"], ["replied Miss Crawford, with a brightened look; “Anhalt is a heavy part.”", "크로퍼드 양이 환해진 낯빛으로 대답했다. “안할트는 따분한 배역이거든요.”"]], [["“The Count has two-and-forty speeches,”", "“백작은 대사가 마흔두 마디예요.”"], ["returned Mr.", "러시워스 씨가 되받았다."], ["Rushworth, “which is no trifle.”", "“그거 만만찮은 거죠.”"]], [["“I am not at all surprised,”", "“안할트가 없다는 게 저는 조금도 놀랍지 않네요.”"], ["said Miss Crawford, after a short pause, “at this want of an Anhalt.", "크로퍼드 양이 잠깐 뜸을 들인 뒤 말했다."], ["Amelia deserves no better.", "“어밀리아한텐 그보다 나은 상대가 아깝지요."], ["Such a forward young lady may well frighten the men.”", "그렇게 들이대는 아가씨라면 사내들을 겁줄 만도 하고요.”"]], [["“I should be but too happy in taking the part, if it were possible,”", "“가능하다면야 저야 그 배역을 맡아 더없이 기쁘겠지만,” 톰이 외쳤다."], ["cried Tom; “but, unluckily, the Butler and Anhalt are in together.", "“안타깝게도 집사와 안할트가 같은 장면에 함께 나오지 뭡니까."], ["I will not entirely give it up, however; I will try what can be done—I will look it over again.”", "그래도 아주 포기하진 않겠어요. 어떻게 해볼 수 있는지 궁리해 보죠—다시 한번 훑어볼게요.”"]], [["“Your brother should take the part,”", "“당신 아우님이 그 배역을 맡으면 되겠네요.”"], ["said Mr. Yates, in a low voice.", "예이츠 씨가 나직한 목소리로 말했다."], ["“Do not you think he would?”", "“그러지 않을까요?”"]], [["“I shall not ask him,”", "“난 그 앤 안 부를 거예요.”"], ["replied Tom, in a cold, determined manner.", "톰이 싸늘하고 단호한 투로 대답했다."]], [["Miss Crawford talked of something else, and soon afterwards rejoined the party at the fire.", "크로퍼드 양은 다른 이야기를 꺼냈고, 이내 난롯가 무리에게로 다시 돌아왔다."]], [["“They do not want me at all,”", "“저 사람들은 저를 도무지 원하질 않네요.”"], ["said she, seating herself.", "그녀가 자리에 앉으며 말했다."], ["“I only puzzle them, and oblige them to make civil speeches. Mr.", "“저는 그저 저들을 곤란하게 하고, 인사치레 대꾸나 하게 만들 뿐이에요."], ["Edmund Bertram, as you do not act yourself, you will be a disinterested adviser; and, therefore, I apply to you.", "에드먼드 버트럼 씨, 씨는 몸소 연기를 안 하시니 사심 없는 조언자가 되어 주시겠죠. 그래서 씨께 여쭙는 거예요."], ["What shall we do for an Anhalt?", "안할트는 어떡하면 좋을까요?"], ["Is it practicable for any of the others to double it?", "다른 이들 중 누가 그 역을 겹쳐 맡는 게 될 만한가요?"], ["What is your advice?”", "씨 생각은 어떠세요?”"]], [["“My advice,”", "“제 조언은,”"], ["said he calmly, “is that you change the play.”", "그가 차분히 말했다. “연극을 바꾸시라는 거예요.”"]], [["“I should have no objection,”", "“저야 반대할 게 없어요.” 그녀가 대답했다."], ["she replied; “for though I should not particularly dislike the part of Amelia if well supported, that is, if everything went well, I shall be sorry to be an inconvenience; but as they do not chuse to hear your advice at that table”", "“받쳐 주기만 잘한다면, 그러니까 모든 게 잘 돌아가기만 한다면, 저는 어밀리아 역이 딱히 싫진 않지만, 걸림돌이 되는 건 미안하니까요. 하지만 저 탁자에서는 씨 조언을 들으려 하질 않으니,”"], ["(looking round), “it certainly will not be taken.”", "하고 둘러보며, “그건 분명 받아들여지지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["Edmund said no more.", "에드먼드는 더는 말하지 않았다."]], [["“If any part could tempt you to act, I suppose it would be Anhalt,”", "“정 어떤 배역이 씨더러 연기하게 꾈 수 있다면, 아마 안할트겠지요.”"], ["observed the lady archly, after a short pause; “for he is a clergyman, you know.”", "잠깐 뜸을 들인 뒤 그 아가씨가 짓궂게 넌지시 일렀다. “그 사람은, 아시다시피, 성직자니까요.”"]], [["“That circumstance would by no means tempt me,”", "“그런 사정이야 결코 저를 꾀지 못할 거예요.”"], ["he replied, “for I should be sorry to make the character ridiculous by bad acting.", "그가 대답했다. “서툰 연기로 그 인물을 우스꽝스럽게 만드는 건 저로선 마음 아픈 일일 테니까요."], ["It must be very difficult to keep Anhalt from appearing a formal, solemn lecturer; and the man who chuses the profession itself is, perhaps, one of the last who would wish to represent it on the stage.”", "안할트가 격식 차리는 근엄한 설교꾼처럼 보이지 않게 하기란 무척 어려울 게 틀림없어요. 그리고 그 직업 자체를 택한 사람이야말로, 아마 그것을 무대 위에 그려 보이고 싶어 할 마지막 축에 드는 사람일 거고요.”"]], [["Miss Crawford was silenced, and with some feelings of resentment and mortification, moved her chair considerably nearer the tea-table, and gave all her attention to Mrs. Norris, who was presiding there.", "크로퍼드 양은 말문이 막혔고, 얼마쯤 분하고 무안한 심경으로 제 의자를 찻상 쪽으로 훨씬 가까이 옮겨, 거기서 좌장 노릇을 하던 노리스 부인에게 온통 마음을 쏟았다."]], [["“Fanny,”", "“패니,”"], ["cried Tom Bertram, from the other table, where the conference was eagerly carrying on, and the conversation incessant, “we want your services.”", "톰 버트럼이 저쪽 탁자에서 외쳤는데, 거기서는 회의가 열심히 이어지고 이야기가 끊이지 않았다. “네 도움이 필요해.”"]], [["Fanny was up in a moment, expecting some errand; for the habit of employing her in that way was not yet overcome, in spite of all that Edmund could do.", "패니는 무슨 심부름이려니 하고 냉큼 일어섰다. 그런 식으로 그녀를 부려 먹는 버릇이, 에드먼드가 아무리 애를 써도 아직 사라지지 않았던 것이다."]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["we do not want to disturb you from your seat.", "네 자리에서 일으키려는 게 아니야."], ["We do not want your present services.", "지금 당장 네 도움이 필요한 건 아니고."], ["We shall only want you in our play.", "우리 연극에서 네가 좀 필요할 뿐이야."], ["You must be Cottager’s wife.”", "넌 소작인의 아내를 맡아야 해.”"]], [["“Me!”", "“저요!”"], ["cried Fanny, sitting down again with a most frightened look.", "패니가 더없이 겁에 질린 낯으로 다시 주저앉으며 외쳤다."], ["“Indeed you must excuse me.", "“정말이지 봐주세요."], ["I could not act anything if you were to give me the world.", "온 세상을 다 준다 해도 저는 아무것도 연기 못 해요."], ["No, indeed, I cannot act.”", "아니, 정말, 저는 연기를 못 해요.”"]], [["“Indeed, but you must, for we cannot excuse you.", "“아니, 넌 해야 해, 우리가 널 봐줄 수가 없거든. 겁낼 것 없어."], ["It need not frighten you: it is a nothing of a part, a mere nothing, not above half a dozen speeches altogether, and it will not much signify if nobody hears a word you say; so you may be as creep-mouse as you like, but we must have you to look at.”", "아무것도 아닌 배역이야, 그야말로 아무것도 아니지, 통틀어 대여섯 마디도 안 되고, 네 말을 아무도 못 알아들어도 별 상관없어. 그러니 원하는 만큼 생쥐처럼 기어들어가는 목소리로 해도 되지만, 그래도 우린 바라볼 네가 있어야 해.”"]], [["“If you are afraid of half a dozen speeches,”", "“대여섯 마디가 겁난다면,” 러시워스 씨가 외쳤다."], ["cried Mr. Rushworth, “what would you do with such a part as mine?", "“프라이스 양은 내 것 같은 배역은 어쩌시려고요?"], ["I have forty-two to learn.”", "나는 마흔두 마디를 외워야 하는데.”"]], [["“It is not that I am afraid of learning by heart,”", "“외우는 게 겁나서가 아니에요.”"], ["said Fanny, shocked to find herself at that moment the only speaker in the room, and to feel that almost every eye was upon her; “but I really cannot act.”", "패니가 말했는데, 그 순간 방 안에서 저 혼자만 말하고 있고 거의 모든 눈이 저를 향해 있음을 깨닫고는 소스라친 채였다. “다만 저는 정말이지 연기를 못 해요.”"]], [["“Yes, yes, you can act well enough for us.", "“아니야, 아니야, 넌 우리한테는 충분히 잘할 수 있어."], ["Learn your part, and we will teach you all the rest.", "네 배역만 외워, 나머지는 우리가 죄다 가르쳐 줄게."], ["You have only two scenes, and as I shall be Cottager, I’ll put you in and push you about, and you will do it very well, I’ll answer for it.”", "넌 장면이 둘뿐이고, 내가 소작인을 맡을 테니, 내가 널 넣어 주고 이리저리 떠밀어 줄게, 그럼 아주 잘해낼 거야, 내가 장담하지.”"]], [["“No, indeed, Mr. Bertram, you must excuse me.", "“안 돼요, 정말, 오빠, 부디 절 봐주세요."], ["You cannot have an idea.", "오빠는 짐작도 못 하실 거예요."], ["It would be absolutely impossible for me.", "저로선 도무지 될 수 없는 일이에요."], ["If I were to undertake it, I should only disappoint you.”", "제가 그걸 맡는다면, 오빠들을 실망시키기밖에 더 하겠어요.”"]], [["“Phoo!", "“쯧!"], ["Phoo!", "쯧!"], ["Do not be so shamefaced.", "그렇게 부끄럼 타지 마."], ["You’ll do it very well.", "넌 아주 잘할 거야."], ["Every allowance will be made for you.", "너한텐 뭐든 다 봐줄 거고."], ["We do not expect perfection.", "우리가 완벽함을 바라는 것도 아니잖아."], ["You must get a brown gown, and a white apron, and a mob cap, and we must make you a few wrinkles, and a little of the crowsfoot at the corner of your eyes, and you will be a very proper, little old woman.”", "넌 갈색 가운에 흰 앞치마에 헐렁한 실내 모자를 구하면 되고, 우리가 네 얼굴에 주름 몇 개랑 눈가에 잔주름을 좀 그려 넣으면, 넌 아주 그럴싸한 자그마한 노파가 될 거야.”"]], [["“You must excuse me, indeed you must excuse me,”", "“봐주세요, 정말이지 봐주세요.”"], ["cried Fanny, growing more and more red from excessive agitation, and looking distressfully at Edmund, who was kindly observing her; but unwilling to exasperate his brother by interference, gave her only an encouraging smile.", "패니가 외쳤는데, 지나친 동요로 점점 더 얼굴이 빨개졌고, 저를 친절히 지켜보던 에드먼드 쪽을 애처롭게 바라보았다. 그러나 에드먼드는 끼어들어 형을 노엽게 하고 싶지 않아, 그녀에게 그저 북돋는 미소만 지어 보였다."], ["Her entreaty had no effect on Tom: he only said again what he had said before; and it was not merely Tom, for the requisition was now backed by Maria, and Mr.", "그녀의 애원은 톰에게 아무 소용이 없었으니, 그는 앞서 한 말을 되풀이할 뿐이었다. 그리고 톰만이 아니었다."], ["Crawford, and Mr. Yates, with an urgency which differed from his but in being more gentle or more ceremonious, and which altogether was quite overpowering to Fanny; and before she could breathe after it, Mrs.", "이제 그 요구를 마리아와 크로퍼드 씨와 예이츠 씨까지 거들었는데, 톰과는 그저 좀 더 부드럽거나 좀 더 정중하다는 것만 다를 뿐인 다그침이었고, 그것이 죄다 합쳐지니 패니로선 도무지 당해 낼 수가 없었다."], ["Norris completed the whole by thus addressing her in a whisper at once angry and audible—“What a piece of work here is about nothing: I am quite ashamed of you, Fanny, to make such a difficulty of obliging your cousins in a trifle of this sort—so kind as they are to you!", "그리고 그녀가 그 뒤에 채 숨 돌릴 새도 없이, 노리스 부인이 노기 서리면서도 다 들리게 이렇게 속삭여 그 모든 것에 쐐기를 박았다—“이게 다 아무것도 아닌 일로 무슨 소란이니. 나는 네가 정말 부끄럽구나, 패니, 이런 사소한 일로 사촌들 청을 들어주는 걸 이토록 어렵게 만들다니—너한테 그리 잘해 주는 사람들인데!"], ["Take the part with a good grace, and let us hear no more of the matter, I entreat.”", "얌전히 그 배역을 맡고, 이 일로 더는 말 안 나오게 하렴, 부탁이다.”"]], [["“Do not urge her, madam,”", "“그 애를 다그치지 마세요, 이모.”"], ["said Edmund.", "에드먼드가 말했다."], ["“It is not fair to urge her in this manner.", "“이런 식으로 그 애를 다그치는 건 온당치 않아요."], ["You see she does not like to act.", "보시다시피 그 애는 연기하기를 싫어하잖아요."], ["Let her chuse for herself, as well as the rest of us.", "우리 나머지 사람들처럼 그 애도 제 뜻대로 고르게 두세요."], ["Her judgment may be quite as safely trusted.", "그 애 판단도 얼마든지 믿어도 좋으니까요."], ["Do not urge her any more.”", "더는 그 애를 다그치지 마세요.”"]], [["“I am not going to urge her,”", "“난 다그칠 생각 없어요.”"], ["replied Mrs.", "노리스 부인이 쏘아붙였다."], ["Norris sharply; “but I shall think her a very obstinate, ungrateful girl, if she does not do what her aunt and cousins wish her—very ungrateful, indeed, considering who and what she is.”", "“하지만 그 애가 제 이모와 사촌들이 바라는 걸 안 한다면, 나는 그 애가 아주 고집 세고 은혜 모르는 계집애라 여길 거예요—정말이지 아주 배은망덕하지요, 그 애가 누구이고 어떤 처지인지 생각하면 말이에요.”"]], [["Edmund was too angry to speak; but Miss Crawford, looking for a moment with astonished eyes at Mrs. Norris, and then at Fanny, whose tears were beginning to shew themselves, immediately said, with some keenness, “I do not like my situation: this place is too hot for me,” and moved away her chair to the opposite side of the table, close to Fanny, saying to her, in a kind, low whisper, as she placed herself, “Never mind, my dear Miss Price, this is a cross evening: everybody is cross and teasing, but do not let us mind them”; and with pointed attention continued to talk to her and endeavour to raise her spirits, in spite of being out of spirits herself. By a look at her brother she prevented any farther entreaty from the theatrical board, and the really good feelings by which she was almost purely governed were rapidly restoring her to all the little she had lost in Edmund’s favour.", "에드먼드는 너무 화가 나 말을 잇지 못했다. 그러나 크로퍼드 양이 한순간 놀란 눈으로 노리스 부인을, 다음엔 눈물이 비치기 시작한 패니를 보더니, 곧바로 다소 날카롭게 말했다. “제 자리가 마음에 안 드네요. 여긴 저한테 너무 더워요.” 그러고는 제 의자를 탁자 맞은편, 패니 바로 곁으로 옮기며, 자리를 잡으면서 그녀에게 친절하고 나직한 목소리로 속삭였다. “괘념치 마세요, 프라이스 양, 오늘 저녁은 다들 심사가 뒤틀린 날이에요. 다들 심통을 부리고 못살게 굴지만, 우리는 저 사람들 신경 쓰지 말아요.” 그리고 그녀는 저 자신도 기분이 언짢으면서도, 각별한 마음씀으로 그녀에게 계속 말을 붙이며 기운을 북돋우려 애썼다. 제 오라비에게 눈짓 한 번으로 그녀는 연극 위원회에서 더 다그치는 것을 막았고, 그녀를 거의 오롯이 다스리는 그 참으로 착한 심성이, 에드먼드의 호감에서 그녀가 잃었던 그 얼마간을 빠르게 되돌려 주고 있었다."]], [["Fanny did not love Miss Crawford; but she felt very much obliged to her for her present kindness; and when, from taking notice of her work, and wishing she could work as well, and begging for the pattern, and supposing Fanny was now preparing for her appearance, as of course she would come out when her cousin was married, Miss Crawford proceeded to inquire if she had heard lately from her brother at sea, and said that she had quite a curiosity to see him, and imagined him a very fine young man, and advised Fanny to get his picture drawn before he went to sea again—she could not help admitting it to be very agreeable flattery, or help listening, and answering with more animation than she had intended.", "패니는 크로퍼드 양을 좋아하진 않았다. 그러나 이번의 친절에는 무척 고마움을 느꼈다. 그리고 크로퍼드 양이 그녀의 일감을 눈여겨보며 자기도 그만큼 잘하면 좋겠다고 하고, 본을 좀 알려 달라 청하고, 패니가 이제 사교계 나들이를 채비하는 모양이라며—사촌 언니가 시집가면 그녀도 물론 사교계에 나올 테니—여기더니, 이어서 바다에 나가 있는 오라비에게서 요즘 소식을 들었느냐 묻고, 그를 꼭 한번 보고 싶어 못 견디겠다며 아주 훤칠한 젊은이일 거라 그려 보고, 그가 다시 바다로 나가기 전에 그 초상을 그려 두라 권하기까지 하자—그녀는 그것이 아주 흐뭇한 아첨임을 인정하지 않을 수 없었고, 귀 기울이지 않을 수도, 제가 작정한 것보다 한결 생기 있게 대꾸하지 않을 수도 없었다."]], [["The consultation upon the play still went on, and Miss Crawford’s attention was first called from Fanny by Tom Bertram’s telling her, with infinite regret, that he found it absolutely impossible for him to undertake the part of Anhalt in addition to the Butler: he had been most anxiously trying to make it out to be feasible, but it would not do; he must give it up.", "연극에 관한 의논은 여전히 이어졌고, 크로퍼드 양의 주의가 패니에게서 처음으로 돌려진 것은, 톰 버트럼이 그녀에게 한없는 아쉬움을 담아, 집사에 더해 안할트 역까지 맡기란 도무지 불가능하겠다고 말했을 때였다. 그는 그것이 될 만한 일임을 애타게 밝혀 보려 했으나 도무지 안 되겠으니, 접을 수밖에 없다는 것이었다."], ["“But there will not be the smallest difficulty in filling it,”", "“하지만 그걸 채우는 데는 조금의 어려움도 없을 거예요.” 그가 덧붙였다."], ["he added.", "“한마디만 하면 되니까요."], ["“We have but to speak the word; we may pick and chuse.", "골라잡을 수 있죠. 지금 이 순간에도, 우리 무리에 끼고 싶어 안달인 젊은이를 여기서 육 마일 안에 적어도 여섯은 댈 수 있어요."], ["I could name, at this moment, at least six young men within six miles of us, who are wild to be admitted into our company, and there are one or two that would not disgrace us: I should not be afraid to trust either of the Olivers or Charles Maddox.", "그중 한둘은 우리한테 창피를 주지도 않을 거고요. 올리버 형제든 찰스 매독스든 나는 믿고 맡길 수 있어요."], ["Tom Oliver is a very clever fellow, and Charles Maddox is as gentlemanlike a man as you will see anywhere, so I will take my horse early to-morrow morning and ride over to Stoke, and settle with one of them.”", "톰 올리버는 아주 영리한 녀석이고, 찰스 매독스는 어디서든 보기 드물게 신사다운 사내니까요. 그러니 내일 아침 일찌감치 말을 몰아 스토크로 건너가서, 그중 하나와 매듭짓고 오겠어요.”"]], [["While he spoke, Maria was looking apprehensively round at Edmund in full expectation that he must oppose such an enlargement of the plan as this: so contrary to all their first protestations; but Edmund said nothing.", "그가 말하는 동안, 마리아는 근심스레 에드먼드 쪽을 둘러보며, 그가 이렇게 계획을 키우는 것을—처음에 다들 그토록 손사래 치던 것과는 딴판인 이 일을—틀림없이 반대하리라 잔뜩 벼르고 있었다. 그러나 에드먼드는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["After a moment’s thought, Miss Crawford calmly replied, “As far as I am concerned, I can have no objection to anything that you all think eligible.", "잠깐 생각한 뒤, 크로퍼드 양이 차분히 대답했다. “저로 말하면, 여러분 모두가 적당하다 여기는 것이라면 무엇에도 반대할 게 없어요."], ["Have I ever seen either of the gentlemen? Yes, Mr.", "제가 그 신사분들 중 누구라도 본 적이 있던가요?"], ["Charles Maddox dined at my sister’s one day, did not he, Henry?", "아, 찰스 매독스 씨가 언젠가 언니 댁에서 만찬을 든 적이 있죠, 안 그래, 헨리?"], ["A quiet-looking young man.", "얌전해 보이는 젊은이였어요."], ["I remember him.", "저는 그이가 기억나요."], ["Let him be applied to, if you please, for it will be less unpleasant to me than to have a perfect stranger.”", "부디 그이를 부르시죠, 생판 낯선 사람보다야 저한텐 덜 거북할 테니까요.”"]], [["Charles Maddox was to be the man.", "찰스 매독스가 그 사람으로 정해졌다."], ["Tom repeated his resolution of going to him early on the morrow; and though Julia, who had scarcely opened her lips before, observed, in a sarcastic manner, and with a glance first at Maria and then at Edmund, that “the Mansfield theatricals would enliven the whole neighbourhood exceedingly,”", "톰은 이튿날 아침 일찍 그를 찾아가겠다는 결심을 되풀이했다. 그리고 그때껏 좀처럼 입을 열지 않던 줄리아가, 먼저 마리아를, 다음엔 에드먼드를 흘긋 보며 빈정대는 투로 “맨스필드 연극이 온 동네를 아주 들썩하게 하겠네”"], ["Edmund still held his peace, and shewed his feelings only by a determined gravity.", "하고 한마디 했으나, 에드먼드는 여전히 잠자코 있었고, 오직 작심한 듯한 근엄함으로만 제 심경을 드러냈다."]], [["“I am not very sanguine as to our play,”", "“나는 우리 연극이 그리 잘될 것 같지가 않아요.”"], ["said Miss Crawford, in an undervoice to Fanny, after some consideration; “and I can tell Mr.", "크로퍼드 양이 얼마간 생각한 뒤, 패니에게 나직한 목소리로 말했다."], ["Maddox that I shall shorten some of his speeches, and a great many of my own, before we rehearse together.", "“그리고 매독스 씨한테는, 함께 연습하기 전에 그 사람 대사도 얼마쯤, 내 대사도 아주 많이 줄이겠다고 일러 둬야겠어요."], ["It will be very disagreeable, and by no means what I expected.”", "아주 언짢은 노릇이 될 테고, 결코 내가 바라던 게 아니에요.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XVI", "# 제16장"]], [["It was not in Miss Crawford’s power to talk Fanny into any real forgetfulness of what had passed.", "크로퍼드 양의 힘으로도 패니가 그동안 있었던 일을 정말로 잊게 만들 수는 없었다."], ["When the evening was over, she went to bed full of it, her nerves still agitated by the shock of such an attack from her cousin Tom, so public and so persevered in, and her spirits sinking under her aunt’s unkind reflection and reproach.", "저녁이 끝나자 그녀는 그 일로 가슴이 미어진 채 잠자리에 들었으니, 사촌 톰에게서 그토록 공공연히, 그토록 집요하게 몰아세워진 충격에 신경은 아직도 떨렸고, 이모의 매정한 지청구와 나무람에 기가 죽어 있었다."], ["To be called into notice in such a manner, to hear that it was but the prelude to something so infinitely worse, to be told that she must do what was so impossible as to act; and then to have the charge of obstinacy and ingratitude follow it, enforced with such a hint at the dependence of her situation, had been too distressing at the time to make the remembrance when she was alone much less so, especially with the superadded dread of what the morrow might produce in continuation of the subject.", "그런 식으로 뭇사람의 눈길 앞에 불려 나오고, 그것이 한없이 더 고약한 일의 서막에 지나지 않는다는 소리를 듣고, 연기처럼 도무지 될 수 없는 일을 해야 한다는 소리를 듣고, 그러고 나서 고집과 배은망덕이라는 나무람이 뒤따르고, 그것도 제 얹혀사는 처지를 넌지시 들먹이는 말로 힘주어 다그쳐진 것은—그 순간에도 너무나 괴로운 일이었던지라, 홀로 있을 때 그것을 떠올리는 일이 그리 덜 괴로울 수도 없었으니, 더구나 이튿날이 그 화제를 이어받아 무엇을 몰고 올지 모른다는 두려움까지 더해진 터였다."], ["Miss Crawford had protected her only for the time; and if she were applied to again among themselves with all the authoritative urgency that Tom and Maria were capable of, and Edmund perhaps away, what should she do?", "크로퍼드 양은 그저 그때만 그녀를 감싸 주었을 뿐이었다. 그러니 만일 톰과 마리아가 능히 부릴 만한 그 위압적인 다그침으로 저희끼리 다시 그녀에게 청해 오는데, 에드먼드마저 자리에 없다면, 그녀는 어찌해야 할까?"], ["She fell asleep before she could answer the question, and found it quite as puzzling when she awoke the next morning.", "그 물음에 답하기도 전에 그녀는 잠이 들었고, 이튿날 아침 깨어나서도 그것이 여전히 갈피를 잡을 수 없는 물음임을 알았다."], ["The little white attic, which had continued her sleeping-room ever since her first entering the family, proving incompetent to suggest any reply, she had recourse, as soon as she was dressed, to another apartment more spacious and more meet for walking about in and thinking, and of which she had now for some time been almost equally mistress.", "이 집안에 처음 든 이래로 줄곧 그녀의 침실이던 그 자그마한 흰 다락방이 아무런 답도 일러 주지 못하자, 그녀는 옷을 차려입기 무섭게 좀 더 널찍하고 이리저리 거닐며 생각하기에 좀 더 알맞은, 그리고 이제 얼마 전부터 거의 그녀가 마찬가지로 안주인 노릇을 해 온 다른 방으로 갔다."], ["It had been their school-room; so called till the Miss Bertrams would not allow it to be called so any longer, and inhabited as such to a later period.", "그곳은 그들의 공부방이었다—버트럼 자매가 더는 그렇게 부르지 못하게 할 때까지 그리 불렸고, 그 뒤로도 한동안은 공부방으로 쓰였다."], ["There Miss Lee had lived, and there they had read and written, and talked and laughed, till within the last three years, when she had quitted them.", "거기서 리 선생이 지냈고, 거기서 그들은 읽고 쓰고, 이야기하고 웃었으니, 리 선생이 그들 곁을 떠난 삼 년 전까지 그러했다."], ["The room had then become useless, and for some time was quite deserted, except by Fanny, when she visited her plants, or wanted one of the books, which she was still glad to keep there, from the deficiency of space and accommodation in her little chamber above: but gradually, as her value for the comforts of it increased, she had added to her possessions, and spent more of her time there; and having nothing to oppose her, had so naturally and so artlessly worked herself into it, that it was now generally admitted to be hers.", "그 뒤로 그 방은 쓸모가 없어져 한동안 아주 버려져 있었으나, 오직 패니만이, 제 화초를 보살피러 오거나, 위층 제 자그마한 방의 공간과 세간이 모자라 여태 거기 두는 것이 반가운 책을 한 권 찾으러 올 때 드나들었다. 그러다 차츰, 그 방의 안락함을 소중히 여기는 마음이 커지면서, 그녀는 제 세간을 늘리고 그곳에서 더 많은 시간을 보냈다."], ["The East room, as it had been called ever since Maria Bertram was sixteen, was now considered Fanny’s, almost as decidedly as the white attic: the smallness of the one making the use of the other so evidently reasonable that the Miss Bertrams, with every superiority in their own apartments which their own sense of superiority could demand, were entirely approving it; and Mrs.", "그리고 그녀를 막을 것이 아무것도 없으니, 어찌나 자연스럽고 어찌나 스스럼없이 그 방에 스며들었던지, 이제는 그곳이 그녀의 것임이 두루 인정되었다. 마리아 버트럼이 열여섯이던 때부터 줄곧 그리 불려 온 그 동쪽 방은, 이제 흰 다락방과 거의 다름없이 뚜렷하게 패니의 것으로 여겨졌다."], ["Norris, having stipulated for there never being a fire in it on Fanny’s account, was tolerably resigned to her having the use of what nobody else wanted, though the terms in which she sometimes spoke of the indulgence seemed to imply that it was the best room in the house.", "한쪽이 워낙 비좁으니 다른 쪽을 쓰는 것이 너무나 마땅해 보였던 터라, 제 우월감이 요구할 만한 온갖 우월함을 제 방에 갖춘 버트럼 자매도 그것을 온전히 못마땅해하지 않았다. 그리고 노리스 부인은 패니를 위해 그 방에 결코 불을 때지 않는다는 조건을 못박아 둔 뒤로는, 아무도 원치 않는 것을 그녀가 쓰는 것쯤은 그런대로 눈감아 주었다—하기야 그녀가 이따금 그 관대함을 두고 하는 말투를 들으면, 마치 그 방이 집 안에서 가장 좋은 방이기라도 한 듯싶었지만."]], [["The aspect was so favourable that even without a fire it was habitable in many an early spring and late autumn morning to such a willing mind as Fanny’s; and while there was a gleam of sunshine she hoped not to be driven from it entirely, even when winter came.", "방향이 어찌나 좋던지, 불을 안 때도 초봄이며 늦가을의 여러 아침이면 패니처럼 기꺼운 마음에는 지낼 만했다. 그리고 한 줄기 햇살이라도 비치는 한, 그녀는 겨울이 와도 그 방에서 아주 쫓겨나지는 않기를 바랐다."], ["The comfort of it in her hours of leisure was extreme.", "한가한 시간에 그 방이 주는 위안은 더할 나위 없었다. 아래층에서 언짢은 일이 있으면 그녀는 그리로 가서, 손닿는 데 있는 무슨 소일거리나 어떤 생각의 실마리에서 곧바로 위안을 얻을 수 있었다."], ["She could go there after anything unpleasant below, and find immediate consolation in some pursuit, or some train of thought at hand.", "제 화초, 제 책들—단돈 한 실링이라도 부릴 수 있게 된 첫 순간부터 그녀는 책을 모아 왔다—제 필기 책상, 그리고 자선과 손재주로 만든 제 일감이 죄다 손닿는 데 있었다. 아니면 무언가에 손댈 마음이 안 나고 그저 골똘히 생각에 잠기는 것 말고는 아무것도 하기 싫을 때면, 그 방에서 흥미로운 추억 하나 얽혀 있지 않은 물건이라곤 좀처럼 눈에 띄지 않았다."], ["Her plants, her books—of which she had been a collector from the first hour of her commanding a shilling—her writing-desk, and her works of charity and ingenuity, were all within her reach; or if indisposed for employment, if nothing but musing would do, she could scarcely see an object in that room which had not an interesting remembrance connected with it.", "무엇이든 다 벗이거나, 아니면 그녀의 생각을 어느 벗에게로 실어 갔다. 그리고 이따금 크나큰 괴로움을 겪었더라도, 제 속뜻이 곧잘 오해받고, 제 감정이 무시되고, 제 이해력이 얕잡아 보였더라도, 또 억압과 조롱과 홀대의 아픔을 겪어 왔더라도, 그 어느 것이 되풀이될 때면 거의 언제나 무언가 위로가 되는 일이 뒤따랐다."], ["Everything was a friend, or bore her thoughts to a friend; and though there had been sometimes much of suffering to her; though her motives had often been misunderstood, her feelings disregarded, and her comprehension undervalued; though she had known the pains of tyranny, of ridicule, and neglect, yet almost every recurrence of either had led to something consolatory: her aunt Bertram had spoken for her, or Miss Lee had been encouraging, or, what was yet more frequent or more dear, Edmund had been her champion and her friend: he had supported her cause or explained her meaning, he had told her not to cry, or had given her some proof of affection which made her tears delightful; and the whole was now so blended together, so harmonised by distance, that every former affliction had its charm.", "버트럼 이모가 그녀를 두둔해 주었거나, 리 선생이 북돋아 주었거나, 아니면 그보다 더 잦고 더 살가운 일로, 에드먼드가 그녀의 편이자 벗이 되어 주었던 것이다. 그는 그녀를 대변해 주거나 그녀의 속뜻을 풀어 주었고, 울지 말라 일러 주거나, 눈물마저 흐뭇하게 만드는 어떤 다정함의 증표를 건네주었다."], ["The room was most dear to her, and she would not have changed its furniture for the handsomest in the house, though what had been originally plain had suffered all the ill-usage of children; and its greatest elegancies and ornaments were a faded footstool of Julia’s work, too ill done for the drawing-room, three transparencies, made in a rage for transparencies, for the three lower panes of one window, where Tintern Abbey held its station between a cave in Italy and a moonlight lake in Cumberland, a collection of family profiles, thought unworthy of being anywhere else, over the mantelpiece, and by their side, and pinned against the wall, a small sketch of a ship sent four years ago from the Mediterranean by William, with H.M.S.", "그리하여 그 모든 것이 이제는 하나로 녹아들어, 세월의 거리를 두고 어찌나 어우러졌던지, 지난날의 온갖 시련마저 저마다 정겨움을 띠었다. 그 방은 그녀에게 더없이 소중했으니, 그녀는 그 세간을 집 안에서 가장 훤한 것과도 바꾸려 들지 않았을 것이다—본디 수수했던 것이 아이들의 온갖 험한 손길을 다 겪었는데도 말이다."], ["Antwerp at the bottom, in letters as tall as the mainmast.", "그리고 그 방의 가장 큰 우아함이자 장식이란, 응접실에 두기엔 너무 서툴게 된 줄리아의 솜씨로 만든 빛바랜 발받침 하나와, 비침 그림이 한창 유행하던 때에 만든 투명 그림 셋—이것은 어느 창의 아래쪽 세 칸에 끼워져, 이탈리아의 어느 동굴과 컴벌랜드의 어느 달빛 어린 호수 사이에 틴턴 수도원이 자리 잡고 있었다—과, 달리 어디 둘 데가 없다 여겨져 벽난로 선반 위에 놓인 집안사람들의 옆모습 초상 한 무더기와, 그리고 그 곁, 벽에 핀으로 꽂힌, 사 년 전 윌리엄이 지중해에서 보내온 배 한 척의 자그마한 스케치—맨 아래에는 큰돛대만큼이나 큼직한 글자로 ‘국왕 폐하의 함선 앤트워프호’라 적혀 있었다—가 다였다."]], [["To this nest of comforts Fanny now walked down to try its influence on an agitated, doubting spirit, to see if by looking at Edmund’s profile she could catch any of his counsel, or by giving air to her geraniums she might inhale a breeze of mental strength herself.", "이 아늑한 위안의 보금자리로 패니는 이제 걸어 내려가, 그것이 흔들리고 미심쩍은 마음에 어떤 힘을 미치는지 시험해 보려 했다. 에드먼드의 옆모습 초상을 바라보노라면 그의 충고를 조금이나마 얻을 수 있을까, 제 제라늄에 바깥바람을 쐬어 주노라면 저 자신도 마음의 기운을 한 줄기 들이마실 수 있을까 해서였다."], ["But she had more than fears of her own perseverance to remove: she had begun to feel undecided as to what she ought to do; and as she walked round the room her doubts were increasing.", "그러나 그녀가 걷어 내야 할 것은 제 굳센 마음에 대한 두려움만이 아니었다. 그녀는 자신이 마땅히 어찌해야 옳은지조차 갈피를 못 잡기 시작한 터였다."], ["Was she right in refusing what was so warmly asked, so strongly wished for—what might be so essential to a scheme on which some of those to whom she owed the greatest complaisance had set their hearts?", "그리고 방을 이리저리 거니는 사이 그 의심은 점점 커져 갔다. 그토록 뜨겁게 청해지고 그토록 간절히 바라진 것을—제가 누구보다 고분고분해야 할 이들 가운데 몇몇이 마음을 쏟은 계획에 그토록 요긴할지 모를 것을—마다한 것이 과연 옳은 일이었을까?"], ["Was it not ill-nature, selfishness, and a fear of exposing herself?", "그것은 심술이요 이기심이요, 제 모습을 드러내기 두려워하는 마음이 아니었을까?"], ["And would Edmund’s judgment, would his persuasion of Sir Thomas’s disapprobation of the whole, be enough to justify her in a determined denial in spite of all the rest?", "그리고 에드먼드의 판단이, 이 모든 일을 토머스 경이 못마땅해하시리라는 그의 확신이, 나머지 모두를 거스르고 굳이 마다하는 것을 떳떳하게 해 줄 만큼 충분한 근거가 될까?"], ["It would be so horrible to her to act that she was inclined to suspect the truth and purity of her own scruples; and as she looked around her, the claims of her cousins to being obliged were strengthened by the sight of present upon present that she had received from them.", "연기를 한다는 것이 그녀에게는 너무나 끔찍한 일인지라, 그녀는 제 거리낌이 참되고 순수한 것인지 의심하고 싶어졌다. 그리고 방 안을 둘러보니, 그들에게 신세를 졌다는 사촌들의 명분이, 그녀가 그들에게서 받은 선물 위에 또 선물이 놓인 광경에 힘입어 더욱 굳세어졌다."], ["The table between the windows was covered with work-boxes and netting-boxes which had been given her at different times, principally by Tom; and she grew bewildered as to the amount of the debt which all these kind remembrances produced.", "두 창 사이의 탁자에는 여러 때에 걸쳐, 주로 톰이 준 반짇고리며 그물뜨기 상자가 잔뜩 놓여 있었다. 그리고 이 온갖 다정한 정표가 자아내는 그 빚이 얼마나 되는지 헤아리다 그녀는 어리둥절해졌다."], ["A tap at the door roused her in the midst of this attempt to find her way to her duty, and her gentle “Come in” was answered by the appearance of one, before whom all her doubts were wont to be laid.", "이렇게 제 도리를 찾아가려 애쓰는 와중에 문 두드리는 소리가 그녀를 일깨웠고, 그녀의 나직한 “들어오세요”에 답해 나타난 것은, 그 앞에서라면 그녀의 온갖 의심이 으레 내려놓아지곤 하던 사람이었다."], ["Her eyes brightened at the sight of Edmund.", "에드먼드를 보자 그녀의 눈이 환해졌다."]], [["“Can I speak with you, Fanny, for a few minutes?”", "“패니, 잠깐 얘기 좀 할 수 있을까?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, certainly.”", "“네, 물론이죠.”"]], [["“I want to consult.", "“의논하고 싶어서."], ["I want your opinion.”", "네 의견이 듣고 싶구나.”"]], [["“My opinion!”", "“제 의견을요!”"], ["she cried, shrinking from such a compliment, highly as it gratified her.", "그녀는 그런 치켜세움에 몸을 움츠리며 외쳤으나, 그것이 여간 흐뭇하지 않았다."]], [["“Yes, your advice and opinion.", "“그래, 네 조언과 의견 말이야."], ["I do not know what to do.", "어찌해야 할지 모르겠구나."], ["This acting scheme gets worse and worse, you see.", "이 연극 소동이 갈수록 나빠지는 게 보이잖니."], ["They have chosen almost as bad a play as they could, and now, to complete the business, are going to ask the help of a young man very slightly known to any of us.", "저들은 고를 수 있는 가장 나쁜 축에 드는 연극을 골랐고, 이제 그걸로도 모자라, 우리 누구도 거의 모르는 젊은이한테 도움을 청하려 하고 있어."], ["This is the end of all the privacy and propriety which was talked about at first.", "이게 바로 처음에 그토록 떠들어 대던 그 은밀함이며 온당함의 끝이야."], ["I know no harm of Charles Maddox; but the excessive intimacy which must spring from his being admitted among us in this manner is highly objectionable, the more than intimacy—the familiarity.", "찰스 매독스한테 나쁜 감정이야 없지만, 그를 이런 식으로 우리 틈에 끼워 들이면 반드시 생겨날 그 지나친 가까움이 몹시 못마땅해—가까움 이상의 것, 그 허물없음 말이야."], ["I cannot think of it with any patience; and it does appear to me an evil of such magnitude as must, if possible, be prevented.", "나는 도무지 그것을 참고 볼 수가 없어. 그리고 그건 어떻게든 막아야 할 만큼 큰 화근으로 내겐 보이는구나."], ["Do not you see it in the same light?”", "너도 그걸 같은 눈으로 보지 않니?”"]], [["“Yes; but what can be done?", "“네, 하지만 어쩔 수 있겠어요?"], ["Your brother is so determined.”", "오빠 형은 저리 뜻이 굳은걸요.”"]], [["“There is but one thing to be done, Fanny.", "“할 수 있는 일은 딱 하나야, 패니."], ["I must take Anhalt myself.", "내가 안할트를 직접 맡는 거지."], ["I am well aware that nothing else will quiet Tom.”", "그 밖엔 무엇으로도 톰을 잠재우지 못하리란 걸 나는 잘 알아.”"]], [["Fanny could not answer him.", "패니는 그에게 대꾸할 수 없었다."]], [["“It is not at all what I like,” he continued.", "“내가 좋아서 하는 일은 결코 아니야.”"], ["“No man can like being driven into the appearance of such inconsistency.", "그가 말을 이었다. “그런 앞뒤 안 맞는 꼴로 내몰리는 걸 좋아할 사내는 없지."], ["After being known to oppose the scheme from the beginning, there is absurdity in the face of my joining them now, when they are exceeding their first plan in every respect; but I can think of no other alternative.", "처음부터 그 계획에 반대한 걸로 알려진 마당에, 저들이 처음 계획을 온갖 면에서 넘어서고 있는 지금 와서 그들에 끼어든다는 건 우스운 노릇이야. 하지만 달리 마땅한 수가 도무지 떠오르질 않는구나."], ["Can you, Fanny?”", "넌 떠오르니, 패니?”"]], [["“No,”", "“아뇨.”"], ["said Fanny slowly, “not immediately, but—”", "패니가 천천히 말했다. “당장은요, 하지만—”"]], [["“But what?", "“하지만 뭐?"], ["I see your judgment is not with me.", "네 판단이 내 편이 아닌 게 보이는구나."], ["Think it a little over.", "좀 더 곰곰이 생각해 봐."], ["Perhaps you are not so much aware as I am of the mischief that may, of the unpleasantness that must arise from a young man’s being received in this manner: domesticated among us; authorised to come at all hours, and placed suddenly on a footing which must do away all restraints.", "젊은이가 이런 식으로 받아들여져—우리 틈에 눌러앉아, 아무 때나 드나들 자격을 얻고, 온갖 삼감을 없애 버릴 처지에 불쑥 놓이는 데서—생길 수 있는 해악이며, 반드시 생길 언짢음을 넌 아마 나만큼은 못 헤아리는가 보구나."], ["To think only of the licence which every rehearsal must tend to create.", "연습이란 연습마다 자아낼 그 방종만 생각해 봐도 그래."], ["It is all very bad!", "죄다 아주 고약한 일이야!"], ["Put yourself in Miss Crawford’s place, Fanny.", "크로퍼드 양의 처지가 되어 봐, 패니."], ["Consider what it would be to act Amelia with a stranger.", "낯선 사람과 어밀리아를 연기한다는 게 어떨지 헤아려 보라고."], ["She has a right to be felt for, because she evidently feels for herself.", "그녀는 마땅히 헤아려질 자격이 있어, 스스로도 그것을 절실히 느끼는 게 분명하니까."], ["I heard enough of what she said to you last night to understand her unwillingness to be acting with a stranger; and as she probably engaged in the part with different expectations—perhaps without considering the subject enough to know what was likely to be—it would be ungenerous, it would be really wrong to expose her to it.", "어젯밤 그녀가 너한테 한 말을 나도 충분히 들어서, 그녀가 낯선 사람과 연기하기를 꺼리는 걸 알겠더구나. 그리고 그녀는 아마 다른 기대를 품고 그 배역을 맡았을 테니—어쩌면 무슨 일이 벌어질지 알 만큼 충분히 헤아려 보지도 못한 채 말이야—그녀를 그런 일에 내맡기는 건 야박한 짓이요, 정말이지 그른 일일 거야."], ["Her feelings ought to be respected.", "그녀의 감정은 마땅히 존중받아야 해."], ["Does it not strike you so, Fanny?", "너도 그렇게 여겨지지 않니, 패니?"], ["You hesitate.”", "넌 망설이는구나.”"]], [["“I am sorry for Miss Crawford; but I am more sorry to see you drawn in to do what you had resolved against, and what you are known to think will be disagreeable to my uncle.", "“저는 크로퍼드 양이 안됐어요. 하지만 오빠가 스스로 반대하기로 마음먹었던 일, 삼촌께서 언짢아하시리라 오빠가 여기는 걸로 알려진 일에 끌려드는 걸 보는 게 더 안타까워요."], ["It will be such a triumph to the others!”", "그건 다른 사람들한텐 얼마나 큰 승리가 되겠어요!”"]], [["“They will not have much cause of triumph when they see how infamously I act.", "“내가 얼마나 형편없이 연기하는지 보면 저들도 그리 우쭐할 거리가 없을 거야."], ["But, however, triumph there certainly will be, and I must brave it.", "하지만 아무튼 승리감이야 분명 있을 테고, 나는 그걸 무릅써야지."], ["But if I can be the means of restraining the publicity of the business, of limiting the exhibition, of concentrating our folly, I shall be well repaid.", "그래도 내가 이 일이 소문나는 걸 막고, 그 구경거리를 좁히고, 우리 어리석음을 한데 그러모으는 수단이 될 수 있다면, 나는 충분히 값을 치른 셈이야. 지금 이대로라면 나는 아무 영향력도 없고, 아무것도 못 해."], ["As I am now, I have no influence, I can do nothing: I have offended them, and they will not hear me; but when I have put them in good-humour by this concession, I am not without hopes of persuading them to confine the representation within a much smaller circle than they are now in the high road for.", "저들의 비위를 거슬렀으니, 저들은 내 말을 안 들어. 하지만 이렇게 양보해 저들을 흐뭇하게 해 놓고 나면, 저들이 지금 냅다 치닫고 있는 것보다 훨씬 좁은 테두리 안으로 상연을 가두도록 설득할 가망이 없지 않아."], ["This will be a material gain.", "이건 실질적인 소득이 될 거야."], ["My object is to confine it to Mrs. Rushworth and the Grants.", "내 바람은 그것을 러시워스 부인과 그랜트 내외로만 한정하는 거란다."], ["Will not this be worth gaining?”", "이만하면 얻어 볼 만하지 않니?”"]], [["“Yes, it will be a great point.”", "“네, 그건 대단한 대목이 되겠어요.”"]], [["“But still it has not your approbation.", "“그래도 여전히 네 찬성은 못 얻었구나."], ["Can you mention any other measure by which I have a chance of doing equal good?”", "내가 그만큼 좋은 일을 해낼 가망이 있는 다른 방도를 하나라도 대 볼 수 있겠니?”"]], [["“No, I cannot think of anything else.”", "“아뇨, 달리는 아무것도 떠오르지 않아요.”"]], [["“Give me your approbation, then, Fanny.", "“그럼 찬성해 다오, 패니."], ["I am not comfortable without it.”", "그게 없으면 나는 마음이 편치 않구나.”"]], [["“Oh, cousin!”", "“아, 오빠!”"]], [["“If you are against me, I ought to distrust myself, and yet—But it is absolutely impossible to let Tom go on in this way, riding about the country in quest of anybody who can be persuaded to act—no matter whom: the look of a gentleman is to be enough.", "“네가 나를 반대한다면, 나는 나 자신을 미덥잖게 여겨야 마땅하겠지, 그런데도—하지만 톰이 이런 식으로 계속하게 두는 건 도무지 안 될 일이야, 연기하도록 꾈 수 있는 사람이면 누구든 찾아 온 고장을 말 타고 쏘다니게—그게 누구든 상관없이, 신사처럼 보이기만 하면 그만이라니."], ["I thought you would have entered more into Miss Crawford’s feelings.”", "나는 네가 크로퍼드 양의 심정에 좀 더 마음을 실어 줄 줄 알았는데.”"]], [["“No doubt she will be very glad.", "“틀림없이 그녀는 아주 기뻐할 거예요."], ["It must be a great relief to her,”", "그녀한텐 큰 안도가 될 게 분명하고요.”"], ["said Fanny, trying for greater warmth of manner.", "패니가 좀 더 살가운 투를 내보려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["“She never appeared more amiable than in her behaviour to you last night.", "“어젯밤 너를 대하는 그 태도만큼 그녀가 사랑스러워 보인 적은 없었어."], ["It gave her a very strong claim on my goodwill.”", "그 일로 그녀는 내 호의를 살 아주 강한 명분을 얻었지.”"]], [["“She was very kind, indeed, and I am glad to have her spared”...", "“정말이지 그녀는 아주 친절했어요. 그래서 저는 그녀가 그 일을 면하게 돼 기뻐요”…"]], [["She could not finish the generous effusion.", "그녀는 그 너그러운 말을 끝맺지 못했다."], ["Her conscience stopt her in the middle, but Edmund was satisfied.", "양심이 그 한복판에서 그녀를 멈춰 세웠던 것이다. 그러나 에드먼드는 흡족해했다."]], [["“I shall walk down immediately after breakfast,”", "“나는 아침 먹고 곧장 내려가겠어.” 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “and am sure of giving pleasure there.", "“그리고 거기서 틀림없이 기쁨을 안겨 주겠지."], ["And now, dear Fanny, I will not interrupt you any longer.", "자, 이제, 패니야, 더는 널 방해하지 않으마."], ["You want to be reading.", "넌 책을 읽고 싶을 테니."], ["But I could not be easy till I had spoken to you, and come to a decision.", "하지만 너한테 말을 건네고 마음을 정하기 전엔 도무지 편할 수가 없었어."], ["Sleeping or waking, my head has been full of this matter all night.", "자나 깨나 밤새 이 일로 머릿속이 가득했거든."], ["It is an evil, but I am certainly making it less than it might be.", "이건 화근이지만, 그래도 나는 분명 그것을 될 수 있는 것보다는 작게 만들고 있는 거야."], ["If Tom is up, I shall go to him directly and get it over, and when we meet at breakfast we shall be all in high good-humour at the prospect of acting the fool together with such unanimity.", "톰이 일어났으면 곧장 그리로 가서 이 일을 끝내 버려야지. 그럼 우리가 아침상에서 만날 때쯤엔, 그토록 한마음으로 함께 바보짓을 하게 됐다는 기대에 다들 잔뜩 흐뭇해져 있을 거야."], ["You, in the meanwhile, will be taking a trip into China, I suppose.", "그동안 너는, 아마 중국으로 여행이나 떠나 있겠구나."], ["How does Lord Macartney go on?”—opening a volume on the table and then taking up some others.", "매카트니 경은 어찌 돼 가니?”—하며 그는 탁자 위의 책 한 권을 펼치고는 다른 몇 권을 집어 들었다."], ["“And here are Crabbe’s Tales, and the Idler, at hand to relieve you, if you tire of your great book.", "“그리고 여기, 그 큰 책이 물리거든 널 달래 줄 크래브의 「이야기 시집」과 「빈둥거리는 사람」도 손닿는 데 있구나."], ["I admire your little establishment exceedingly; and as soon as I am gone, you will empty your head of all this nonsense of acting, and sit comfortably down to your table.", "나는 네 이 자그마한 살림살이가 여간 대견하지 않아. 그리고 내가 가고 나면, 너는 이 연기니 뭐니 하는 시답잖은 생각을 머릿속에서 다 비우고, 네 탁자 앞에 편안히 앉겠지."], ["But do not stay here to be cold.”", "하지만 여기서 몸 얼도록 있지는 마라.”"]], [["He went; but there was no reading, no China, no composure for Fanny.", "그는 갔다. 그러나 패니에게는 독서도, 중국도, 마음의 평온도 없었다."], ["He had told her the most extraordinary, the most inconceivable, the most unwelcome news; and she could think of nothing else.", "그는 그녀에게 더없이 뜻밖의, 더없이 있을 수 없는, 더없이 달갑잖은 소식을 전한 것이다. 그리고 그녀는 다른 아무것도 생각할 수 없었다."], ["To be acting!", "연기를 하다니!"], ["After all his objections—objections so just and so public!", "그토록 반대해 놓고—그토록 옳고 그토록 공공연한 반대였는데!"], ["After all that she had heard him say, and seen him look, and known him to be feeling.", "그가 하는 말을 죄다 듣고, 그의 낯빛을 보고, 그의 심경을 알고 나서도."], ["Could it be possible?", "이게 있을 수 있는 일일까?"], ["Edmund so inconsistent!", "에드먼드가 그토록 앞뒤가 안 맞다니!"], ["Was he not deceiving himself?", "그는 스스로를 속이고 있는 게 아닐까?"], ["Was he not wrong?", "그가 그른 게 아닐까?"], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["it was all Miss Crawford’s doing.", "이 모두가 크로퍼드 양이 한 일이었다."], ["She had seen her influence in every speech, and was miserable.", "그녀는 그의 말 한마디 한마디에서 그 여자의 영향을 보았고, 비참했다."], ["The doubts and alarms as to her own conduct, which had previously distressed her, and which had all slept while she listened to him, were become of little consequence now.", "앞서 그녀를 괴롭혔던, 그리고 그의 말을 듣는 동안 죄다 잠들어 있던, 제 처신에 대한 의심과 불안은 이제 대수롭지 않은 것이 되었다."], ["This deeper anxiety swallowed them up.", "이 한층 깊은 근심이 그것을 통째로 삼켜 버렸다."], ["Things should take their course; she cared not how it ended.", "일은 흘러가는 대로 흘러가라지. 그녀는 그것이 어찌 끝나든 개의치 않았다."], ["Her cousins might attack, but could hardly tease her.", "사촌들이 몰아세울지언정, 이제 그녀를 성가시게 할 수는 거의 없었다."], ["She was beyond their reach; and if at last obliged to yield—no matter—it was all misery now.", "그녀는 그들의 손이 닿지 않는 데 있었다. 그리고 끝내 굽혀야만 한다 해도—상관없었다—이제 모든 것이 비참했으니까."]], [["# CHAPTER XVII", "# 제17장"]], [["It was, indeed, a triumphant day to Mr. Bertram and Maria.", "그날은 참으로 버트럼 씨와 마리아에게 승리의 날이었다."], ["Such a victory over Edmund’s discretion had been beyond their hopes, and was most delightful.", "에드먼드의 분별을 이렇게 이겨 내다니 그들의 바람을 넘어서는 일이었고, 더없이 통쾌했다."], ["There was no longer anything to disturb them in their darling project, and they congratulated each other in private on the jealous weakness to which they attributed the change, with all the glee of feelings gratified in every way.", "이제 그들의 애지중지하는 계획을 어지럽힐 것은 아무것도 없었고, 그들은 그 변심을 시샘에서 온 약점 탓으로 돌리며, 온갖 식으로 흡족해진 감정의 온갖 신명으로 저희끼리 은근히 축하를 주고받았다."], ["Edmund might still look grave, and say he did not like the scheme in general, and must disapprove the play in particular; their point was gained: he was to act, and he was driven to it by the force of selfish inclinations only.", "에드먼드가 여전히 근엄한 낯을 하고, 그 계획이 대체로 마음에 안 든다느니 그 연극만은 못마땅해할 수밖에 없다느니 해도 좋았다. 그들의 목적은 이루어진 것이다."], ["Edmund had descended from that moral elevation which he had maintained before, and they were both as much the better as the happier for the descent.", "그가 연기를 하게 됐고, 그것도 오로지 이기적인 마음의 힘에 떠밀려서였으니 말이다. 에드먼드는 앞서 지켜 온 그 도덕적 높이에서 내려온 것이고, 그들은 그가 내려온 만큼 더 흐뭇해졌을 뿐 아니라 더 떳떳해지기까지 했다."]], [["They behaved very well, however, to him on the occasion, betraying no exultation beyond the lines about the corners of the mouth, and seemed to think it as great an escape to be quit of the intrusion of Charles Maddox, as if they had been forced into admitting him against their inclination.", "그래도 그들은 이 일에서 그에게 아주 잘 처신했으니, 입가에 어린 잔주름 말고는 어떤 우쭐함도 내비치지 않았고, 찰스 매독스가 끼어드는 것을 면하게 된 것을 마치 저희 뜻과 달리 억지로 그를 받아들이게 될 뻔하다 벗어난 양 여기는 듯했다. “이 일을 온전히 저희 집안 울타리 안에 두는 것이야말로 저희가 각별히 바라던 바였다."], ["“To have it quite in their own family circle was what they had particularly wished.", "낯선 이가 끼면 저희의 온갖 편안함이 무너졌을 것이다.”"], ["A stranger among them would have been the destruction of all their comfort”; and when Edmund, pursuing that idea, gave a hint of his hope as to the limitation of the audience, they were ready, in the complaisance of the moment, to promise anything. It was all good-humour and encouragement.", "그리고 에드먼드가 그 생각을 이어받아 관객을 제한했으면 하는 제 바람을 넌지시 비치자, 그들은 그 순간의 고분고분함으로 무엇이든 약속할 태세였다."], ["Mrs. Norris offered to contrive his dress, Mr.", "온통 흐뭇함과 북돋움이었다."], ["Yates assured him that Anhalt’s last scene with the Baron admitted a good deal of action and emphasis, and Mr. Rushworth undertook to count his speeches.", "노리스 부인은 그의 의상을 지어 주겠다 나섰고, 예이츠 씨는 안할트가 남작과 함께하는 마지막 장면에 몸짓과 힘줌이 자못 들어갈 만하다고 그를 안심시켰으며, 러시워스 씨는 그의 대사를 세어 주는 소임을 떠맡았다."]], [["“Perhaps,”", "“어쩌면,”"], ["said Tom, “Fanny may be more disposed to oblige us now.", "톰이 말했다. “이제 패니가 우리 청을 좀 더 들어줄 마음이 생겼을지도 몰라."], ["Perhaps you may persuade her.”", "네가 그 앨 설득할 수 있을지도 모르고.”"]], [["“No, she is quite determined.", "“아니, 그 앤 마음을 굳혔어."], ["She certainly will not act.”", "절대 연기 안 할 거야.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["very well.”", "그래 알았어.”"], ["And not another word was said; but Fanny felt herself again in danger, and her indifference to the danger was beginning to fail her already.", "그러고는 더 아무 말이 없었다. 그러나 패니는 다시 위험에 처했음을 느꼈고, 그 위험에 대한 그녀의 무심함은 벌써 무너지기 시작하고 있었다."]], [["There were not fewer smiles at the Parsonage than at the Park on this change in Edmund; Miss Crawford looked very lovely in hers, and entered with such an instantaneous renewal of cheerfulness into the whole affair as could have but one effect on him.", "에드먼드의 이 변심을 두고, 목사관에서도 저택에서 못지않게 미소가 오갔다. 크로퍼드 양은 미소 지으니 무척 사랑스러워 보였고, 이 온 소동에 이내 되살아난 명랑함으로 뛰어드니 그것이 그에게 미치는 효과는 오직 하나뿐일 수밖에 없었다."], ["“He was certainly right in respecting such feelings; he was glad he had determined on it.”", "“그가 그런 감정을 존중한 것은 분명 옳았다. 그리 마음먹기를 잘했다.”"], ["And the morning wore away in satisfactions very sweet, if not very sound.", "그리하여 그 아침은, 아주 건실하지는 않아도 아주 달콤한 흡족함 속에 흘러갔다."], ["One advantage resulted from it to Fanny: at the earnest request of Miss Crawford, Mrs.", "그 일에서 패니에게 이로운 점이 하나 나왔으니, 크로퍼드 양의 간곡한 청에 그랜트 부인이 여느 때의 그 좋은 성품으로 패니가 맡을 뻔했던 배역을 떠맡기로 한 것이다."], ["Grant had, with her usual good-humour, agreed to undertake the part for which Fanny had been wanted; and this was all that occurred to gladden her heart during the day; and even this, when imparted by Edmund, brought a pang with it, for it was Miss Crawford to whom she was obliged—it was Miss Crawford whose kind exertions were to excite her gratitude, and whose merit in making them was spoken of with a glow of admiration.", "그리고 그것이 그날 하루 그녀의 마음을 기쁘게 한 전부였다. 그런데 이것마저도, 에드먼드의 입을 통해 전해지자 아픔 한 자락을 함께 몰고 왔으니, 그녀가 신세를 진 것이 다름 아닌 크로퍼드 양이요—고마움을 자아낼 그 친절한 수고를 베푼 것이 크로퍼드 양이요, 그 수고를 베푼 공이 감탄에 겨운 목소리로 이야기되었기 때문이다."], ["She was safe; but peace and safety were unconnected here.", "그녀는 안전했다. 그러나 여기서는 평온과 안전이 서로 이어져 있지 않았다."], ["Her mind had been never farther from peace.", "그녀의 마음이 평온에서 이토록 멀었던 적이 없었다. 그녀는 자신이 그른 짓을 했다고는 느낄 수 없었으나, 그 밖의 온갖 면에서 마음이 편치 않았다."], ["She could not feel that she had done wrong herself, but she was disquieted in every other way.", "그녀의 마음도 판단도 똑같이 에드먼드의 결정에 반대했다. 그녀는 그의 흔들림을 두둔할 수 없었고, 그 흔들림 속에서도 그가 행복해하는 것이 그녀를 비참하게 했다."], ["Her heart and her judgment were equally against Edmund’s decision: she could not acquit his unsteadiness, and his happiness under it made her wretched.", "그녀는 시샘과 동요로 가득했다. 크로퍼드 양이 명랑한 낯빛으로 다가오는데 그것이 모욕처럼 느껴졌고, 저를 향해 다정한 말을 건네는데 그녀는 좀처럼 차분히 대꾸할 수가 없었다."], ["She was full of jealousy and agitation.", "그녀 둘레의 모든 이가 명랑하고 분주하고 잘나가고 대단했다."], ["Miss Crawford came with looks of gaiety which seemed an insult, with friendly expressions towards herself which she could hardly answer calmly.", "저마다 관심거리가, 제 배역이, 제 의상이, 좋아하는 장면이, 제 벗과 한패가 있었다. 다들 의논하고 견주는 데서 할 일을 찾거나, 서로 던지는 장난스러운 착상에서 재미를 찾고 있었다."], ["Everybody around her was gay and busy, prosperous and important; each had their object of interest, their part, their dress, their favourite scene, their friends and confederates: all were finding employment in consultations and comparisons, or diversion in the playful conceits they suggested.", "오직 그녀만이 서글프고 하찮았다. 그녀는 어느 것에도 낄 몫이 없었다."], ["She alone was sad and insignificant: she had no share in anything; she might go or stay; she might be in the midst of their noise, or retreat from it to the solitude of the East room, without being seen or missed.", "가도 그만 있어도 그만이었다. 그 소란 한복판에 있든, 거기서 물러나 동쪽 방의 외로움으로 숨어들든, 눈에 띄지도 아쉬워하는 이도 없었다."], ["She could almost think anything would have been preferable to this. Mrs.", "이것보다야 무엇이든 나았으리라는 생각마저 들 지경이었다."], ["Grant was of consequence: her good-nature had honourable mention; her taste and her time were considered; her presence was wanted; she was sought for, and attended, and praised; and Fanny was at first in some danger of envying her the character she had accepted.", "그랜트 부인은 대단한 사람이었다. 그녀의 좋은 성품은 명예로이 입에 올랐고, 그녀의 취향과 시간이 헤아려졌으며, 그녀가 있어 주기를 바랐다."], ["But reflection brought better feelings, and shewed her that Mrs.", "사람들은 그녀를 찾고, 떠받들고, 칭찬했다."], ["Grant was entitled to respect, which could never have belonged to her; and that, had she received even the greatest, she could never have been easy in joining a scheme which, considering only her uncle, she must condemn altogether.", "그리하여 패니는 처음엔 그녀가 맡은 그 배역을 시샘할 위험이 얼마간 있었다. 그러나 곰곰이 생각하니 한결 나은 마음이 들었고, 그랜트 부인은 존경받을 자격이 있으나 그것은 결코 자기에게 돌아올 수 있는 것이 아니었음을, 또 설령 자기가 그 가장 큰 존경을 받았다 한들, 오직 삼촌만 생각해도 온통 나무랄 수밖에 없는 그런 계획에 끼어들고서는 결코 마음이 편할 수 없었으리라는 것을 깨달았다."]], [["Fanny’s heart was not absolutely the only saddened one amongst them, as she soon began to acknowledge to herself.", "그들 가운데 서글퍼진 마음이 오롯이 패니의 것만은 아니었으니, 이내 그녀도 스스로 인정하기 시작했다."], ["Julia was a sufferer too, though not quite so blamelessly.", "줄리아 또한 괴로워하는 사람이었다—다만 그리 나무랄 데 없이 그런 것은 아니었지만."]], [["Henry Crawford had trifled with her feelings; but she had very long allowed and even sought his attentions, with a jealousy of her sister so reasonable as ought to have been their cure; and now that the conviction of his preference for Maria had been forced on her, she submitted to it without any alarm for Maria’s situation, or any endeavour at rational tranquillity for herself.", "헨리 크로퍼드는 그녀의 감정을 가지고 놀았다. 그러나 그녀는 아주 오랫동안 그의 살가움을 받아들이고 심지어 구하기까지 했으니, 언니에 대한 시샘도 마땅히 그 병을 고쳐 주었어야 할 만큼 이치에 닿는 것이었다."], ["She either sat in gloomy silence, wrapt in such gravity as nothing could subdue, no curiosity touch, no wit amuse; or allowing the attentions of Mr.", "그리고 이제 그가 마리아를 더 좋아한다는 확신이 억지로 들이밀어진 마당에, 그녀는 마리아의 처지를 걱정하지도, 저 자신을 위해 사리에 맞는 평온을 되찾으려 애쓰지도 않은 채 그것에 굴복했다."], ["Yates, was talking with forced gaiety to him alone, and ridiculing the acting of the others.", "그녀는 무엇으로도 누그러뜨릴 수 없고, 어떤 호기심으로도 건드릴 수 없고, 어떤 재담으로도 즐겁게 할 수 없는 그런 침울함에 싸여 음울한 침묵 속에 앉아 있거나, 아니면 예이츠 씨의 살가움을 받아 주며 그 사람하고만 억지 명랑함으로 이야기하고 다른 이들의 연기를 비웃었다."]], [["For a day or two after the affront was given, Henry Crawford had endeavoured to do it away by the usual attack of gallantry and compliment, but he had not cared enough about it to persevere against a few repulses; and becoming soon too busy with his play to have time for more than one flirtation, he grew indifferent to the quarrel, or rather thought it a lucky occurrence, as quietly putting an end to what might ere long have raised expectations in more than Mrs.", "모욕이 있고 나서 하루 이틀, 헨리 크로퍼드는 여느 때의 그 정중함과 인사치레의 공세로 그것을 지워 버리려 애썼다. 그러나 몇 번 퇴짜를 무릅쓰고 밀어붙일 만큼 그 일에 마음을 쓰지는 않았다."], ["Grant.", "그리고 이내 제 연극에 너무 바빠 시시덕거림 하나에 쓸 시간밖에 없게 되자, 그는 그 다툼에 무심해졌다—아니, 오히려 그것을 다행스러운 일로 여겼으니, 머잖아 그랜트 부인 아닌 이의 마음에도 기대를 부풀렸을 법한 것을 조용히 끝내 준 셈이었기 때문이다. 그랜트 부인은 줄리아가 연극에서 밀려나 홀대받은 채 곁에 앉아 있는 것이 반갑지 않았다."], ["She was not pleased to see Julia excluded from the play, and sitting by disregarded; but as it was not a matter which really involved her happiness, as Henry must be the best judge of his own, and as he did assure her, with a most persuasive smile, that neither he nor Julia had ever had a serious thought of each other, she could only renew her former caution as to the elder sister, entreat him not to risk his tranquillity by too much admiration there, and then gladly take her share in anything that brought cheerfulness to the young people in general, and that did so particularly promote the pleasure of the two so dear to her.", "그러나 그것이 정말로 제 행복에 얽힌 일도 아니고, 헨리야말로 제 마음을 가장 잘 아는 이일 테고, 게다가 그가 더없이 그럴싸한 미소를 지으며 저도 줄리아도 서로를 진지하게 마음에 둔 적은 한 번도 없었노라 장담하니, 그녀는 그저 언니 쪽에 대한 앞서의 경계를 새삼 되뇌며, 거기서 너무 지나치게 반한 나머지 제 평온을 위태롭게 하지 말라 당부하는 수밖에 없었다. 그러고는 젊은이들 전반에 명랑함을 안기는 일, 특히 저에게 그토록 소중한 두 사람의 즐거움을 그토록 북돋는 일이라면 무엇에든 기꺼이 제 몫을 거들었다."]], [["“I rather wonder Julia is not in love with Henry,”", "“줄리아가 헨리한테 반하지 않은 게 오히려 이상하구나.”"], ["was her observation to Mary.", "이것이 메리에게 건넨 그녀의 소견이었다."]], [["“I dare say she is,”", "“반했을걸요.”"], ["replied Mary coldly.", "메리가 쌀쌀맞게 대답했다."], ["“I imagine both sisters are.”", "“제 짐작엔 두 자매 다 그런 것 같은데요.”"]], [["“Both!", "“둘 다라니!"], ["no, no, that must not be.", "안 돼, 안 돼, 그건 안 될 말이지."], ["Do not give him a hint of it.", "그 사람한텐 그런 낌새도 내비치지 마라."], ["Think of Mr. Rushworth!”", "러시워스 씨를 생각해야지!”"]], [["“You had better tell Miss Bertram to think of Mr. Rushworth.", "“차라리 버트럼 양더러 러시워스 씨를 생각하라고 하시는 게 낫겠어요."], ["It may do her some good. I often think of Mr.", "그 편이 그 여자한텐 좀 이로울지도 모르니까요."], ["Rushworth’s property and independence, and wish them in other hands; but I never think of him.", "저야 러시워스 씨의 재산과 독립된 처지를 곧잘 떠올리며 그게 다른 사람 손에 있으면 좋겠다 싶지만, 그 사람 자체를 떠올리는 일은 결코 없어요."], ["A man might represent the county with such an estate; a man might escape a profession and represent the county.”", "그만한 재산이면 사내가 주(州)를 대표할 수도 있겠죠. 직업 하나 안 가지고도 주를 대표할 수 있고요.”"]], [["“I dare say he will be in parliament soon.", "“머잖아 그이가 의회에 들어가긴 할 거야."], ["When Sir Thomas comes, I dare say he will be in for some borough, but there has been nobody to put him in the way of doing anything yet.”", "토머스 경이 오시면, 아마 어느 선거구로든 들어가겠지, 다만 여태 그이한테 무슨 일이든 하는 길을 터 준 사람이 없었을 뿐이야.”"]], [["“Sir Thomas is to achieve many mighty things when he comes home,”", "“토머스 경은 집에 오시면 숱한 대단한 일을 이루실 참이지.”"], ["said Mary, after a pause.", "잠깐 뜸을 들인 뒤 메리가 말했다."], ["“Do you remember Hawkins Browne’s ‘Address to Tobacco,’ in imitation of Pope?—", "“포프를 흉내 낸 호킨스 브라운의 「담배에게 부치는 글」을 기억하세요?—"]], [["Blest leaf!", "축복받은 잎이여!"], ["whose aromatic gales dispense To Templars modesty, to Parsons sense.", "그 향긋한 바람이 법학도에게 겸손을, 목사에게 분별을 나누어 주는도다."]], [["I will parody them—", "제가 이렇게 패러디해 볼게요—"]], [["Blest Knight!", "축복받은 기사여!"], ["whose dictatorial looks dispense To Children affluence, to Rushworth sense.", "그 위엄 서린 눈길이 자녀에게 풍요를, 러시워스에게 분별을 나누어 주는도다."]], [["Will not that do, Mrs.", "어때요, 그랜트 부인?"], ["Grant? Everything seems to depend upon Sir Thomas’s return.”", "모든 게 토머스 경의 귀환에 달린 것 같지 않아요?”"]], [["“You will find his consequence very just and reasonable when you see him in his family, I assure you.", "“그이를 그 집안 속에서 보면, 그이의 그 무게가 아주 지당하고 사리에 맞는다는 걸 알게 될 게다, 정말이야."], ["I do not think we do so well without him.", "나는 그이 없이 우리가 그리 잘 지내는 것 같지가 않구나."], ["He has a fine dignified manner, which suits the head of such a house, and keeps everybody in their place.", "그이한텐 그런 집안의 가장에 걸맞은 기품 있고 위엄 서린 태도가 있어서, 모두를 제자리에 붙들어 두거든."], ["Lady Bertram seems more of a cipher now than when he is at home; and nobody else can keep Mrs. Norris in order.", "버트럼 부인은 그이가 집에 있을 때보다 지금 오히려 더 있으나 마나 한 사람 같고, 노리스 부인을 다잡을 사람도 그이 말곤 아무도 없어."], ["But, Mary, do not fancy that Maria Bertram cares for Henry.", "하지만 메리, 마리아 버트럼이 헨리한테 마음이 있다고 넘겨짚진 마라."], ["I am sure Julia does not, or she would not have flirted as she did last night with Mr.", "줄리아가 아닌 건 분명해, 그렇지 않고서야 어젯밤 예이츠 씨하고 그렇게 시시덕거렸을 리 없지."], ["Yates; and though he and Maria are very good friends, I think she likes Sotherton too well to be inconstant.”", "그리고 헨리와 마리아가 아주 좋은 벗이긴 해도, 그 애는 소더턴을 너무 좋아해서 마음이 변할 것 같진 않구나.”"]], [["“I would not give much for Mr. Rushworth’s chance if Henry stept in before the articles were signed.”", "“헨리가 계약서에 도장 찍기 전에 끼어든다면, 저는 러시워스 씨 쪽엔 별로 걸지 않겠어요.”"]], [["“If you have such a suspicion, something must be done; and as soon as the play is all over, we will talk to him seriously and make him know his own mind; and if he means nothing, we will send him off, though he is Henry, for a time.”", "“네가 정 그런 의심이 든다면, 무슨 수를 써야겠구나. 연극이 다 끝나는 대로 그이한테 진지하게 이야기해서 제 마음을 똑똑히 알게 만들자. 그리고 아무 뜻도 없는 거라면, 헨리라 해도 한동안 멀리 떠나보내는 거지.”"]], [["Julia did suffer, however, though Mrs. Grant discerned it not, and though it escaped the notice of many of her own family likewise.", "그래도 줄리아는 정말로 괴로워하고 있었다, 그랜트 부인은 그것을 알아채지 못했고 제 식구 여럿의 눈길마저 그것을 비껴갔지만."], ["She had loved, she did love still, and she had all the suffering which a warm temper and a high spirit were likely to endure under the disappointment of a dear, though irrational hope, with a strong sense of ill-usage.", "그녀는 사랑했고, 여전히 사랑하고 있었으며, 비록 사리에 안 맞을지언정 애틋한 희망이 어그러진 데다 홀대당했다는 강한 느낌까지 겹쳤을 때 뜨거운 기질과 드높은 자존심이 으레 견뎌야 할 온갖 괴로움을 고스란히 겪고 있었다. 그녀의 마음은 쓰라리고 성이 나 있었고, 그녀는 오직 성난 위안밖에 누릴 줄 몰랐다."], ["Her heart was sore and angry, and she was capable only of angry consolations.", "여느 때 스스럼없이 지내던 언니가 이제 그녀의 가장 큰 원수가 되었다."], ["The sister with whom she was used to be on easy terms was now become her greatest enemy: they were alienated from each other; and Julia was not superior to the hope of some distressing end to the attentions which were still carrying on there, some punishment to Maria for conduct so shameful towards herself as well as towards Mr.", "둘은 서로 멀어졌다. 그리고 줄리아는, 저기서 아직도 이어지는 그 살가움에 무언가 괴로운 끝장이 나기를, 저 자신에게뿐 아니라 러시워스 씨에게도 그토록 부끄러운 처신을 한 마리아에게 무슨 벌이 내리기를 바라는 마음을 넘어서지 못했다."], ["Rushworth. With no material fault of temper, or difference of opinion, to prevent their being very good friends while their interests were the same, the sisters, under such a trial as this, had not affection or principle enough to make them merciful or just, to give them honour or compassion.", "이해관계가 같을 때는 아주 좋은 벗이 되지 못하게 막을 만한 이렇다 할 기질의 흠도, 의견의 차이도 없었건만, 이런 시련 앞에서 그 자매는 서로에게 자비롭거나 공정할 만큼, 서로에게 존중이나 연민을 베풀 만큼 정도 원칙도 지니지 못했다."], ["Maria felt her triumph, and pursued her purpose, careless of Julia; and Julia could never see Maria distinguished by Henry Crawford without trusting that it would create jealousy, and bring a public disturbance at last.", "마리아는 제 승리를 만끽하며 줄리아에는 아랑곳없이 제 뜻을 밀고 나갔다. 그리고 줄리아는 마리아가 헨리 크로퍼드에게 남달리 대접받는 것을 볼 때마다, 그것이 시샘을 자아내 끝내 공공연한 소동을 몰고 오리라 믿어 마지않았다."]], [["Fanny saw and pitied much of this in Julia; but there was no outward fellowship between them.", "패니는 줄리아의 이런 모습을 많이 보았고 많이 딱해했다. 그러나 둘 사이에는 겉으로 드러난 벗함이 없었다."], ["Julia made no communication, and Fanny took no liberties.", "줄리아는 아무것도 털어놓지 않았고, 패니도 함부로 다가서지 않았다."], ["They were two solitary sufferers, or connected only by Fanny’s consciousness.", "그들은 두 외로운 괴로워하는 이였거나, 오직 패니의 마음속에서만 이어져 있었다."]], [["The inattention of the two brothers and the aunt to Julia’s discomposure, and their blindness to its true cause, must be imputed to the fullness of their own minds.", "두 오라비와 이모가 줄리아의 심란함에 무심하고 그 참된 까닭에 눈멀어 있었던 것은, 저마다 제 마음이 꽉 차 있던 탓으로 돌릴 수밖에 없다."], ["They were totally preoccupied.", "그들은 온통 딴 데 정신이 팔려 있었다."], ["Tom was engrossed by the concerns of his theatre, and saw nothing that did not immediately relate to it.", "톰은 제 극장 일에 골몰해, 거기에 곧바로 얽히지 않은 것은 아무것도 눈에 들어오지 않았다."], ["Edmund, between his theatrical and his real part, between Miss Crawford’s claims and his own conduct, between love and consistency, was equally unobservant; and Mrs.", "에드먼드는 무대 위의 제 배역과 실제 제 처지 사이에서, 크로퍼드 양의 몫과 제 처신 사이에서, 사랑과 한결같음 사이에서, 마찬가지로 무엇 하나 살피지 못했다."], ["Norris was too busy in contriving and directing the general little matters of the company, superintending their various dresses with economical expedient, for which nobody thanked her, and saving, with delighted integrity, half a crown here and there to the absent Sir Thomas, to have leisure for watching the behaviour, or guarding the happiness of his daughters.", "그리고 노리스 부인은, 그 무리의 자잘한 일들을 죄다 꾸리고 지시하고, 아무도 고마워하지 않는 알뜰한 임기응변으로 그들의 갖가지 의상을 감독하며, 흐뭇한 올곧음으로 여기서 반 크라운 저기서 반 크라운 아껴 떠나 있는 토머스 경을 위해 모으느라 너무 바빠, 그 딸들의 처신을 지켜보거나 그 행복을 지켜 줄 겨를이 없었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XVIII", "# 제18장"]], [["Everything was now in a regular train: theatre, actors, actresses, and dresses, were all getting forward; but though no other great impediments arose, Fanny found, before many days were past, that it was not all uninterrupted enjoyment to the party themselves, and that she had not to witness the continuance of such unanimity and delight as had been almost too much for her at first.", "이제 모든 것이 착착 굴러가고 있었다. 극장도, 남녀 배우들도, 의상도 죄다 진척되어 갔다."], ["Everybody began to have their vexation.", "그러나 다른 큰 걸림돌은 생기지 않았어도, 며칠 지나지 않아 패니는, 그것이 무리 자신들에게도 온통 방해 없는 즐거움만은 아님을, 그리고 처음엔 저로선 벅찰 지경이던 그런 한마음과 신명이 이어지는 꼴을 자기가 지켜보지 않아도 됨을 알게 되었다. 저마다 짜증거리가 생기기 시작했다."], ["Edmund had many.", "에드먼드에겐 많았다."], ["Entirely against his judgment, a scene-painter arrived from town, and was at work, much to the increase of the expenses, and, what was worse, of the eclat of their proceedings; and his brother, instead of being really guided by him as to the privacy of the representation, was giving an invitation to every family who came in his way.", "그의 판단과는 온통 어긋나게, 무대 배경 화가가 도시에서 와서 일에 매달렸으니, 비용이 크게 불어난 데다, 더 나쁘게는 그들이 벌이는 일의 요란함까지 커졌다. 그리고 그의 형은, 상연을 은밀히 하는 문제에서 정말로 그의 뜻에 이끌리기는커녕, 오는 길에 마주친 집안마다 초대장을 돌리고 있었다."], ["Tom himself began to fret over the scene-painter’s slow progress, and to feel the miseries of waiting.", "톰 자신도 무대 화가의 더딘 작업에 애를 태우고, 기다림의 괴로움을 느끼기 시작했다."], ["He had learned his part—all his parts, for he took every trifling one that could be united with the Butler, and began to be impatient to be acting; and every day thus unemployed was tending to increase his sense of the insignificance of all his parts together, and make him more ready to regret that some other play had not been chosen.", "그는 제 배역을—제 모든 배역을 다 외웠으니, 집사와 겹칠 만한 자잘한 배역은 죄다 맡았던 것이다—그리고 어서 연기하고 싶어 안달이 나기 시작했다. 그리하여 이렇게 할 일 없이 흘러가는 나날은, 제 모든 배역을 다 합쳐도 하찮다는 느낌을 키우고, 다른 어떤 연극을 고르지 않은 것을 한결 아쉬워하게 만들 뿐이었다."]], [["Fanny, being always a very courteous listener, and often the only listener at hand, came in for the complaints and the distresses of most of them. She knew that Mr.", "늘 아주 정중히 귀 기울여 주고, 게다가 흔히 곁에서 들어 줄 유일한 사람이던 패니에게로, 그들 대부분의 불평과 하소연이 흘러들었다."], ["Yates was in general thought to rant dreadfully; that Mr. Yates was disappointed in Henry Crawford; that Tom Bertram spoke so quick he would be unintelligible; that Mrs.", "그녀는 예이츠 씨가 대체로 지독하게 고래고래 외친다고 여겨진다는 것을, 예이츠 씨가 헨리 크로퍼드에게 실망했다는 것을, 톰 버트럼이 말을 너무 빨리 해서 알아들을 수가 없으리라는 것을, 그랜트 부인이 웃어 대는 통에 다 망친다는 것을, 에드먼드가 제 배역에 뒤처졌다는 것을, 그리고 대사마다 대사를 일러 줄 사람이 있어야 하는 러시워스 씨와 무슨 일이든 함께하는 것이 곤욕이라는 것을 알았다."], ["Grant spoiled everything by laughing; that Edmund was behindhand with his part, and that it was misery to have anything to do with Mr.", "또한 그녀는, 딱한 러시워스 씨가 함께 연습해 줄 사람을 좀처럼 못 구한다는 것도 알았다."], ["Rushworth, who was wanting a prompter through every speech. She knew, also, that poor Mr.", "그의 하소연도 다른 이들의 것과 함께 그녀 앞에 놓였던 것이다."], ["Rushworth could seldom get anybody to rehearse with him: his complaint came before her as well as the rest; and so decided to her eye was her cousin Maria’s avoidance of him, and so needlessly often the rehearsal of the first scene between her and Mr. Crawford, that she had soon all the terror of other complaints from him.", "그리고 사촌 마리아가 그를 피하는 것이 그녀 눈에는 어찌나 뚜렷하고, 그녀와 크로퍼드 씨 사이의 첫 장면 연습이 어찌나 쓸데없이 잦던지, 이내 그녀는 그에게서도 또 다른 하소연이 나올까 봐 온통 겁이 났다."], ["So far from being all satisfied and all enjoying, she found everybody requiring something they had not, and giving occasion of discontent to the others.", "다들 흡족해하고 다들 즐기기는커녕, 그녀가 보니 저마다 저한테 없는 무언가를 요구하고, 남들에게 불만거리를 안기고 있었다. 저마다 배역이 너무 길거나 너무 짧았다."], ["Everybody had a part either too long or too short; nobody would attend as they ought; nobody would remember on which side they were to come in; nobody but the complainer would observe any directions.", "아무도 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 주의를 기울이지 않았고, 아무도 자기가 어느 쪽에서 등장해야 하는지 기억하지 못했으며, 하소연하는 당사자 말고는 아무도 어떤 지시도 지키지 않았다."]], [["Fanny believed herself to derive as much innocent enjoyment from the play as any of them; Henry Crawford acted well, and it was a pleasure to her to creep into the theatre, and attend the rehearsal of the first act, in spite of the feelings it excited in some speeches for Maria.", "패니는 자기도 그들 누구 못지않게 그 연극에서 죄 없는 즐거움을 얻고 있다고 여겼다. 헨리 크로퍼드는 연기를 잘했고, 마리아를 위한 몇몇 대사가 자아내는 감정에도 불구하고, 극장으로 살그머니 기어들어 제1막 연습을 지켜보는 것이 그녀에게는 즐거움이었다."], ["Maria, she also thought, acted well, too well; and after the first rehearsal or two, Fanny began to be their only audience; and sometimes as prompter, sometimes as spectator, was often very useful.", "마리아도 연기를 잘한다고, 아니 너무 잘한다고 그녀는 생각했다. 그리고 첫 한두 번의 연습이 지나자, 패니는 그들의 유일한 관객이 되기 시작했고, 때로는 대사를 일러 주는 이로, 때로는 구경꾼으로, 곧잘 아주 요긴했다."], ["As far as she could judge, Mr.", "그녀가 판단할 수 있는 한, 크로퍼드 씨가 단연 모두 가운데 가장 나은 배우였다."], ["Crawford was considerably the best actor of all: he had more confidence than Edmund, more judgment than Tom, more talent and taste than Mr. Yates.", "그는 에드먼드보다 자신감이 있고, 톰보다 판단력이 있고, 예이츠 씨보다 재주와 취향이 있었다."], ["She did not like him as a man, but she must admit him to be the best actor, and on this point there were not many who differed from her. Mr.", "그녀는 사내로서 그를 좋아하지 않았으나, 가장 나은 배우임은 인정할 수밖에 없었고, 이 점에서는 그녀와 생각이 다른 이가 많지 않았다."], ["Yates, indeed, exclaimed against his tameness and insipidity; and the day came at last, when Mr.", "하기야 예이츠 씨는 그의 밋밋함과 맹숭맹숭함을 소리 높여 나무랐다."], ["Rushworth turned to her with a black look, and said, “Do you think there is anything so very fine in all this?", "그리고 마침내 그날이 왔으니, 러시워스 씨가 시커먼 낯으로 그녀에게 돌아서서 이렇게 말했다. “이게 다 뭐 그리 대단할 게 있다고 여기세요?"], ["For the life and soul of me, I cannot admire him; and, between ourselves, to see such an undersized, little, mean-looking man, set up for a fine actor, is very ridiculous in my opinion.”", "나는 죽어도 그 사람을 못 우러러보겠어요. 그리고 우리끼리 얘기지만, 저렇게 왜소하고 자그마하고 볼품없는 사내가 훌륭한 배우인 척 나서는 걸 보면, 내 생각엔 아주 우스운 노릇이에요.”"]], [["From this moment there was a return of his former jealousy, which Maria, from increasing hopes of Crawford, was at little pains to remove; and the chances of Mr.", "이 순간부터 그의 예전 시샘이 되살아났는데, 크로퍼드에 대한 기대가 커져 가던 마리아는 그것을 지우려 딱히 애쓰지 않았다. 그리하여 러시워스 씨가 그 마흔두 마디 대사를 익히는 경지에 이를 가망은 훨씬 줄어들었다."], ["Rushworth’s ever attaining to the knowledge of his two-and-forty speeches became much less.", "그가 그것을 봐줄 만하게 해낼 가망으로 말하면, 그 어머니 말고는 아무도 그런 생각을 손톱만큼도 품지 않았다."], ["As to his ever making anything tolerable of them, nobody had the smallest idea of that except his mother; she, indeed, regretted that his part was not more considerable, and deferred coming over to Mansfield till they were forward enough in their rehearsal to comprehend all his scenes; but the others aspired at nothing beyond his remembering the catchword, and the first line of his speech, and being able to follow the prompter through the rest.", "정말이지 그 어머니는 아들 배역이 좀 더 크지 않은 것을 아쉬워하며, 연습이 그의 모든 장면을 알아볼 만큼 진척될 때까지 맨스필드로 건너오기를 미루었다. 그러나 다른 이들이 바라는 것은, 그가 받는말과 제 대사 첫 줄만 기억하고 나머지는 대사 일러 주는 이를 따라갈 수 있는 정도, 그 이상은 아무것도 없었다."], ["Fanny, in her pity and kindheartedness, was at great pains to teach him how to learn, giving him all the helps and directions in her power, trying to make an artificial memory for him, and learning every word of his part herself, but without his being much the forwarder.", "딱한 마음과 착한 심성으로 패니는, 그에게 외우는 법을 가르치느라 크게 애를 썼다. 제 힘이 닿는 온갖 도움과 지시를 주고, 그를 위해 인위적인 기억을 만들어 주려 애쓰며, 제가 그의 배역을 한 마디 한 마디 다 외우기까지 했으나, 정작 그는 그리 나아지지 않았다."]], [["Many uncomfortable, anxious, apprehensive feelings she certainly had; but with all these, and other claims on her time and attention, she was as far from finding herself without employment or utility amongst them, as without a companion in uneasiness; quite as far from having no demand on her leisure as on her compassion.", "불편하고 불안하고 조마조마한 감정을 그녀는 분명 많이 지녔다. 그러나 이 모든 것에다, 제 시간과 마음을 앗아 가는 다른 일들까지 있었으니, 그녀는 그들 틈에서 할 일이나 쓸모가 없기는커녕, 언짢음 속의 벗이 없지 않은 것과 꼭 마찬가지였다."], ["The gloom of her first anticipations was proved to have been unfounded.", "제 연민에 못지않게 제 한가로움에도 요구가 없지 않았던 것이다. 처음의 예상이 안긴 그 우울함은 근거 없는 것이었음이 드러났다."], ["She was occasionally useful to all; she was perhaps as much at peace as any.", "그녀는 이따금 모두에게 쓸모가 있었고, 어쩌면 누구 못지않게 마음이 편안했다."]], [["There was a great deal of needlework to be done, moreover, in which her help was wanted; and that Mrs.", "게다가 해야 할 바느질이 잔뜩 있었고, 거기에 그녀의 손이 필요했다."], ["Norris thought her quite as well off as the rest, was evident by the manner in which she claimed it—“Come, Fanny,”", "그리고 노리스 부인이 그녀를 나머지 사람들 못지않게 팔자 좋다 여기고 있음은, 그녀에게 도움을 요구하는 태도에서 여실히 드러났다—“이리 온, 패니,”"], ["she cried, “these are fine times for you, but you must not be always walking from one room to the other, and doing the lookings-on at your ease, in this way; I want you here.", "그녀가 외쳤다. “너한테야 참 좋은 시절이겠지만, 그렇게 늘 이 방 저 방 거닐며 태평하게 구경이나 하고 있어선 안 되지."], ["I have been slaving myself till I can hardly stand, to contrive Mr.", "나는 네가 여기 필요하다."], ["Rushworth’s cloak without sending for any more satin; and now I think you may give me your help in putting it together.", "나는 새틴을 더 안 들이고 러시워스 씨 망토를 지어 보려고 서 있지도 못할 만큼 뼈 빠지게 일해 왔어. 그러니 이제 네가 이걸 꿰매 맞추는 걸 좀 거들어도 되겠구나."], ["There are but three seams; you may do them in a trice.", "솔기가 셋뿐이야, 넌 눈 깜짝할 새에 해낼 게다."], ["It would be lucky for me if I had nothing but the executive part to do.", "나야 이 손 놀리는 일만 하면 되면 오죽 좋으련만."], ["You are best off, I can tell you: but if nobody did more than you, we should not get on very fast.”", "정말이지 네가 제일 팔자 좋아. 하지만 너만큼만 하는 사람뿐이라면, 우리 일은 그리 빨리 못 나갈 거다.”"]], [["Fanny took the work very quietly, without attempting any defence; but her kinder aunt Bertram observed on her behalf—", "패니는 아무런 항변도 하지 않고 그 일감을 아주 얌전히 받아 들었다. 그러나 좀 더 다정한 버트럼 이모가 그녀를 두둔해 한마디 했다—"]], [["“One cannot wonder, sister, that Fanny should be delighted: it is all new to her, you know; you and I used to be very fond of a play ourselves, and so am I still; and as soon as I am a little more at leisure, I mean to look in at their rehearsals too.", "“패니가 좋아하는 것도 무리가 아니지요, 언니. 그 앤 이게 다 처음이잖아요."], ["What is the play about, Fanny?", "언니랑 나도 예전엔 연극을 아주 좋아했고, 나야 지금도 그렇고요. 그리고 나도 좀 더 한가해지는 대로 그 애들 연습을 들여다볼 참이에요."], ["you have never told me.”", "무슨 연극이니, 패니? 나한텐 통 얘기 안 해 줬잖니.”"]], [["“Oh! sister, pray do not ask her now; for Fanny is not one of those who can talk and work at the same time.", "“아이참, 지금은 그 애한테 묻지 마세요."], ["It is about Lovers’ Vows.”", "패니는 이야기하면서 동시에 일하는 그런 축이 못 되니까요. 「연인들의 맹세」에 관한 거예요.”"]], [["“I believe,”", "“제 생각엔, 이모,” 패니가 버트럼 이모에게 말했다."], ["said Fanny to her aunt Bertram, “there will be three acts rehearsed to-morrow evening, and that will give you an opportunity of seeing all the actors at once.”", "“내일 저녁에 세 막을 연습할 것 같아요. 그럼 이모께서 배우들을 한꺼번에 다 보실 수 있을 거예요.”"]], [["“You had better stay till the curtain is hung,”", "“막이 걸릴 때까지 기다리시는 게 낫겠어요.”"], ["interposed Mrs.", "노리스 부인이 끼어들었다."], ["Norris; “the curtain will be hung in a day or two—there is very little sense in a play without a curtain—and I am much mistaken if you do not find it draw up into very handsome festoons.”", "“막이야 하루 이틀이면 걸릴 테고—막도 없이 연극이랄 게 무슨 소용이겠어요—그게 아주 곱게 꽃 줄 모양으로 걷어 올려지는 걸 못 보신다면 내가 크게 잘못 안 거고요.”"]], [["Lady Bertram seemed quite resigned to waiting.", "버트럼 부인은 기다리는 것을 아주 순순히 받아들이는 듯했다. 패니는 이모의 그 태연함을 함께 나누지 못했다."], ["Fanny did not share her aunt’s composure: she thought of the morrow a great deal, for if the three acts were rehearsed, Edmund and Miss Crawford would then be acting together for the first time; the third act would bring a scene between them which interested her most particularly, and which she was longing and dreading to see how they would perform.", "그녀는 내일 일을 무척 많이 생각했으니, 만일 세 막을 연습한다면 그때 에드먼드와 크로퍼드 양이 처음으로 함께 연기할 것이었기 때문이다. 제3막에는 둘 사이의 한 장면이 나오는데, 그것이 그녀에게는 각별히 마음이 쓰였고, 그들이 그것을 어떻게 해낼지 보고 싶으면서도 보기가 두려웠다."], ["The whole subject of it was love—a marriage of love was to be described by the gentleman, and very little short of a declaration of love be made by the lady.", "그 장면의 온 주제는 사랑이었다—사랑의 결혼을 신사가 그려 내야 했고, 사랑의 고백에 조금 못 미치는 말을 숙녀가 해야 했던 것이다."]], [["She had read and read the scene again with many painful, many wondering emotions, and looked forward to their representation of it as a circumstance almost too interesting.", "그녀는 그 장면을 몇 번이고 거듭 읽었으니, 괴롭고도 의아한 온갖 감정에 휩싸인 채였다. 그리고 그들이 그것을 그려 보이는 것을, 거의 너무나 마음 졸이게 하는 일로 여기며 기다렸다."], ["She did not believe they had yet rehearsed it, even in private.", "그녀는 그들이 아직, 은밀히조차도, 그것을 연습하지 않았으리라 믿었다."]], [["The morrow came, the plan for the evening continued, and Fanny’s consideration of it did not become less agitated.", "이튿날이 왔고, 저녁 계획은 그대로였으며, 그것을 헤아리는 패니의 마음은 조금도 덜 심란해지지 않았다."], ["She worked very diligently under her aunt’s directions, but her diligence and her silence concealed a very absent, anxious mind; and about noon she made her escape with her work to the East room, that she might have no concern in another, and, as she deemed it, most unnecessary rehearsal of the first act, which Henry Crawford was just proposing, desirous at once of having her time to herself, and of avoiding the sight of Mr.", "그녀는 이모의 지시대로 아주 부지런히 일했으나, 그 부지런함과 침묵은 자못 넋 나가고 불안한 마음을 감추고 있었다. 그리고 정오 무렵, 그녀는 일감을 들고 동쪽 방으로 달아났으니, 헨리 크로퍼드가 막 제안하던 또 한 번의, 그리고 그녀가 보기엔 더없이 쓸데없는 제1막 연습에 낄 일이 없게 하려는 것이었고, 제 시간을 저 혼자 갖고 싶은 마음과 러시워스 씨를 마주치기 싫은 마음에서였다."], ["Rushworth. A glimpse, as she passed through the hall, of the two ladies walking up from the Parsonage made no change in her wish of retreat, and she worked and meditated in the East room, undisturbed, for a quarter of an hour, when a gentle tap at the door was followed by the entrance of Miss Crawford.", "현관을 지나며 두 부인이 목사관에서 걸어 올라오는 것을 얼핏 보았어도 물러나려는 그녀의 마음은 바뀌지 않았고, 그녀는 동쪽 방에서 방해받지 않고 십오 분쯤 일하며 생각에 잠겼는데, 그때 문을 살며시 두드리는 소리에 뒤이어 크로퍼드 양이 들어섰다."]], [["“Am I right?", "“여기 맞나요?"], ["Yes; this is the East room.", "네, 여기가 동쪽 방이군요."], ["My dear Miss Price, I beg your pardon, but I have made my way to you on purpose to entreat your help.”", "프라이스 양, 미안하지만, 도움을 청하려고 일부러 이렇게 찾아왔어요.”"]], [["Fanny, quite surprised, endeavoured to shew herself mistress of the room by her civilities, and looked at the bright bars of her empty grate with concern.", "패니는 몹시 놀랐으나, 정중한 태도로 이 방의 안주인임을 내보이려 애쓰며, 불기 없는 제 난로의 번들거리는 살대를 근심스레 바라보았다."]], [["“Thank you; I am quite warm, very warm.", "“고마워요. 나 아주 따뜻해요, 아주 따뜻해."], ["Allow me to stay here a little while, and do have the goodness to hear me my third act.", "잠깐 여기 있게 해 주고, 부디 내 제3막을 좀 들어 줘요. 책을 가져왔으니, 나하고 이걸 연습만 해 준다면 얼마나 고마울지!"], ["I have brought my book, and if you would but rehearse it with me, I should be so obliged!", "오늘 여기 온 건 저녁에 대비해 에드먼드하고—우리 둘이서만—이걸 연습할 작정이었는데, 그가 안 보이네요."], ["I came here to-day intending to rehearse it with Edmund—by ourselves—against the evening, but he is not in the way; and if he were, I do not think I could go through it with him, till I have hardened myself a little; for really there is a speech or two.", "게다가 설령 그가 있다 한들, 나는 조금 마음을 단단히 다잡기 전엔 그 사람하고는 이걸 끝까지 해내지 못할 것 같아요. 정말이지 대사가 한두 마디 좀 그런 게 있거든요."], ["You will be so good, won’t you?”", "그렇게 좀 해 줄 거죠?”"]], [["Fanny was most civil in her assurances, though she could not give them in a very steady voice.", "패니는 더없이 정중하게 그러겠노라 했으나, 그 말을 그리 차분한 목소리로 하지는 못했다."]], [["“Have you ever happened to look at the part I mean?”", "“혹시 내가 말하는 그 대목을 본 적이 있나요?”"], ["continued Miss Crawford, opening her book.", "크로퍼드 양이 책을 펼치며 말을 이었다."], ["“Here it is.", "“여기예요."], ["I did not think much of it at first—but, upon my word.", "처음엔 별생각이 없었는데—어머나, 정말."], ["There, look at that speech, and that, and that.", "자, 이 대사를 봐요, 그리고 이것도, 이것도."], ["How am I ever to look him in the face and say such things?", "내가 어떻게 그 사람 얼굴을 마주 보고 이런 말을 한담? 프라이스 양이라면 할 수 있겠어요?"], ["Could you do it? But then he is your cousin, which makes all the difference.", "하기야 그 사람은 프라이스 양의 사촌이니, 그게 사정이 아주 다르긴 하죠."], ["You must rehearse it with me, that I may fancy you him, and get on by degrees.", "나하고 이걸 연습해 줘야 해요, 그래야 내가 프라이스 양을 그 사람이라 여기고 차츰 익숙해질 테니."], ["You have a look of his sometimes.”", "이따금 프라이스 양한텐 그 사람을 닮은 데가 있거든요.”"]], [["“Have I?", "“제가요?"], ["I will do my best with the greatest readiness; but I must read the part, for I can say very little of it.”", "더없이 기꺼이 최선을 다할게요. 하지만 저는 그 대목을 읽어야 해요, 외운 게 얼마 없거든요.”"]], [["“None of it, I suppose.", "“하나도 없겠죠, 아마."], ["You are to have the book, of course. Now for it.", "물론 책은 프라이스 양이 들어요."], ["We must have two chairs at hand for you to bring forward to the front of the stage.", "자, 시작해 볼까요. 무대 앞으로 끌어다 놓을 의자가 둘 손닿는 데 있어야 해요."], ["There—very good school-room chairs, not made for a theatre, I dare say; much more fitted for little girls to sit and kick their feet against when they are learning a lesson.", "저기—아주 좋은 공부방 의자네요, 극장용으로 만든 건 아닐 테고. 어린 계집애들이 공부하면서 앉아 발을 툭툭 차 대기에나 훨씬 어울리는 것들이죠."], ["What would your governess and your uncle say to see them used for such a purpose?", "프라이스 양네 가정교사와 삼촌이 이것들이 이런 데 쓰이는 걸 보면 뭐라 하실까요?"], ["Could Sir Thomas look in upon us just now, he would bless himself, for we are rehearsing all over the house.", "토머스 경께서 지금 우리를 들여다보신다면, 성호를 그으실 거예요, 우리가 온 집 안 곳곳에서 연습을 하고 있으니."], ["Yates is storming away in the dining-room.", "예이츠는 식당에서 폭풍처럼 몰아치고 있어요."], ["I heard him as I came upstairs, and the theatre is engaged of course by those indefatigable rehearsers, Agatha and Frederick.", "올라오는 길에 그 소리를 들었죠. 그리고 극장이야 물론 그 지칠 줄 모르는 연습꾼들, 아가사와 프레더릭이 차지하고 있고요."], ["If they are not perfect, I shall be surprised.", "그 둘이 완벽하지 않다면야 내가 놀랄 거예요."], ["By the bye, I looked in upon them five minutes ago, and it happened to be exactly at one of the times when they were trying not to embrace, and Mr. Rushworth was with me.", "그나저나 오 분 전에 그들을 들여다봤는데, 마침 딱 서로 껴안지 않으려고 애쓰는 대목이었고, 러시워스 씨가 나랑 같이 있었지 뭐예요."], ["I thought he began to look a little queer, so I turned it off as well as I could, by whispering to him, ‘We shall have an excellent Agatha; there is something so maternal in her manner, so completely maternal in her voice and countenance.’ Was not that well done of me?", "그 사람 낯빛이 좀 야릇해지는 것 같기에, 나는 되도록 잘 둘러대느라 그 사람한테 속삭였죠. ‘우리 아가사는 참 훌륭하겠어요, 태도에 어쩜 저리 모성이 어렸을까요, 목소리며 낯빛에 어쩜 저리 온통 모성이 넘칠까요.’ 나 참 잘하지 않았어요?"], ["He brightened up directly.", "그 사람 낯빛이 대번에 환해지더군요."], ["Now for my soliloquy.”", "자, 이제 내 독백을 해 볼게요.”"]], [["She began, and Fanny joined in with all the modest feeling which the idea of representing Edmund was so strongly calculated to inspire; but with looks and voice so truly feminine as to be no very good picture of a man.", "그녀가 시작했고, 패니도 에드먼드를 그려 낸다는 생각이 그토록 강하게 불러일으키는 온갖 조심스러운 감정으로 어울려 들었다—그러나 그 낯빛과 목소리가 너무나 참으로 여성스러워 사내를 그려 보이는 그림으로는 그리 좋지 못했다."], ["With such an Anhalt, however, Miss Crawford had courage enough; and they had got through half the scene, when a tap at the door brought a pause, and the entrance of Edmund, the next moment, suspended it all.", "그래도 그런 안할트를 상대로도 크로퍼드 양은 넉넉히 용기를 냈고, 둘이 그 장면의 절반쯤을 해냈을 때, 문 두드리는 소리에 잠깐 멈추게 되었고, 다음 순간 에드먼드가 들어서면서 그 모든 것이 멎었다."]], [["Surprise, consciousness, and pleasure appeared in each of the three on this unexpected meeting; and as Edmund was come on the very same business that had brought Miss Crawford, consciousness and pleasure were likely to be more than momentary in them.", "이 뜻밖의 만남에 세 사람 저마다 놀라움과 겸연쩍음과 반가움을 내비쳤다. 그리고 에드먼드가 크로퍼드 양을 데려온 것과 똑같은 볼일로 온 터라, 그 둘의 겸연쩍음과 반가움은 한순간에 그치지 않을 성싶었다."], ["He too had his book, and was seeking Fanny, to ask her to rehearse with him, and help him to prepare for the evening, without knowing Miss Crawford to be in the house; and great was the joy and animation of being thus thrown together, of comparing schemes, and sympathising in praise of Fanny’s kind offices.", "그도 제 책을 들고, 저녁에 대비해 함께 연습하며 채비를 도와 달라 청하려 패니를 찾던 참이었으니, 크로퍼드 양이 집에 와 있는 줄은 몰랐던 것이다. 그리하여 이렇게 한자리에 모이고, 서로의 궁리를 견주고, 패니의 친절한 수고를 함께 기려 주는 데서 오는 기쁨과 활기가 컸다."]], [["She could not equal them in their warmth.", "그녀는 그들의 그 뜨거움을 따라갈 수 없었다."], ["Her spirits sank under the glow of theirs, and she felt herself becoming too nearly nothing to both to have any comfort in having been sought by either.", "그들의 열기에 그녀의 기운은 가라앉았고, 어느 쪽에게든 너무나 아무것도 아닌 존재가 되어 가는 자신을 느꼈으니, 어느 한쪽에게 찾아진 것에서도 아무런 위안을 얻지 못했다."], ["They must now rehearse together.", "이제 그들은 함께 연습해야 했다."], ["Edmund proposed, urged, entreated it, till the lady, not very unwilling at first, could refuse no longer, and Fanny was wanted only to prompt and observe them.", "에드먼드가 제안하고, 다그치고, 간청하니, 처음엔 그리 마다하지 않던 그 숙녀도 더는 거절하지 못했고, 패니는 그저 대사를 일러 주고 그들을 지켜보는 데에만 필요했다. 정말이지 그녀는 심판이자 비평가의 소임을 맡아, 그것을 발휘해 그들의 온갖 잘못을 죄다 일러 달라는 간곡한 청까지 받았다."], ["She was invested, indeed, with the office of judge and critic, and earnestly desired to exercise it and tell them all their faults; but from doing so every feeling within her shrank—she could not, would not, dared not attempt it: had she been otherwise qualified for criticism, her conscience must have restrained her from venturing at disapprobation.", "그러나 그리하는 것에는 그녀 안의 온갖 감정이 움츠러들었다—그녀는 할 수 없었고, 하려 하지 않았고, 감히 엄두도 못 냈다. 설령 그녀가 달리 비평할 자격이 있었다 한들, 양심이 그녀를 막아 감히 못마땅함을 입에 올리지 못하게 했을 것이다."], ["She believed herself to feel too much of it in the aggregate for honesty or safety in particulars.", "그녀는 통틀어 못마땅함을 너무 많이 느낀 나머지, 낱낱이 짚기엔 정직하지도 안전하지도 못하리라 여겼다."], ["To prompt them must be enough for her; and it was sometimes more than enough; for she could not always pay attention to the book.", "그들에게 대사를 일러 주는 것만으로도 그녀에겐 충분했다. 아니, 때로는 충분하고도 남았다."], ["In watching them she forgot herself; and, agitated by the increasing spirit of Edmund’s manner, had once closed the page and turned away exactly as he wanted help.", "늘 책에 주의를 기울일 수는 없었기 때문이다. 그들을 지켜보다 그녀는 저 자신을 잊었고, 에드먼드의 태도에 점점 어리는 열정에 마음이 흔들려, 한번은 그가 도움이 필요한 바로 그 대목에서 책장을 덮고 고개를 돌려 버렸다."], ["It was imputed to very reasonable weariness, and she was thanked and pitied; but she deserved their pity more than she hoped they would ever surmise.", "그것은 아주 그럴 만한 피로 탓으로 여겨져, 그녀는 고맙다는 말과 함께 안쓰러운 대접을 받았다. 그러나 그녀는 그들이 짐작하리라 바라는 것보다 더 그 안쓰러움을 받아 마땅했다."], ["At last the scene was over, and Fanny forced herself to add her praise to the compliments each was giving the other; and when again alone and able to recall the whole, she was inclined to believe their performance would, indeed, have such nature and feeling in it as must ensure their credit, and make it a very suffering exhibition to herself.", "마침내 그 장면이 끝났고, 패니는 서로에게 찬사를 건네는 그들 틈에 억지로 제 칭찬을 보탰다. 그리고 다시 홀로 남아 그 모든 것을 되새길 수 있게 되자, 그녀는 그들의 연기가 정말이지 그들의 명성을 확실히 해 줄 만한, 그러면서 저 자신에게는 지켜보기 너무나 괴로운 구경거리가 될 만한 그런 자연스러움과 감정을 담게 되리라 믿고 싶어졌다."], ["Whatever might be its effect, however, she must stand the brunt of it again that very day.", "그러나 그것이 어떤 효과를 내든, 그녀는 바로 그날 다시 한번 그 정면을 견뎌 내야 했다."]], [["The first regular rehearsal of the three first acts was certainly to take place in the evening: Mrs.", "세 첫 막의 첫 정식 연습이 저녁에 열리기로 분명히 정해졌다."], ["Grant and the Crawfords were engaged to return for that purpose as soon as they could after dinner; and every one concerned was looking forward with eagerness.", "그랜트 부인과 크로퍼드 남매는 그 일로 만찬 뒤 되도록 빨리 돌아오기로 약속돼 있었고, 관계된 이는 저마다 열띤 마음으로 그때를 기다렸다."], ["There seemed a general diffusion of cheerfulness on the occasion.", "이 일로 명랑함이 두루 번지는 듯했다."], ["Tom was enjoying such an advance towards the end; Edmund was in spirits from the morning’s rehearsal, and little vexations seemed everywhere smoothed away.", "톰은 이렇게 끝을 향해 나아가는 것을 즐겼고, 에드먼드는 아침 연습으로 기운이 났으며, 자잘한 짜증거리는 곳곳에서 다 씻겨 나간 듯했다."], ["All were alert and impatient; the ladies moved soon, the gentlemen soon followed them, and with the exception of Lady Bertram, Mrs.", "다들 팔팔하고 안달이 났다."], ["Norris, and Julia, everybody was in the theatre at an early hour; and having lighted it up as well as its unfinished state admitted, were waiting only the arrival of Mrs. Grant and the Crawfords to begin.", "부인들이 이내 자리를 옮겼고 신사들이 곧 뒤따랐으니, 버트럼 부인과 노리스 부인과 줄리아만 빼고 모두가 이른 시각에 극장에 모여, 아직 다 안 된 상태나마 되는 대로 불을 밝혀 두고, 그랜트 부인과 크로퍼드 남매가 오기만을 기다려 시작하려 하고 있었다."]], [["They did not wait long for the Crawfords, but there was no Mrs. Grant.", "크로퍼드 남매는 오래 기다릴 것 없었으나, 그랜트 부인은 오지 않았다."], ["She could not come. Dr.", "그녀는 올 수 없었다."], ["Grant, professing an indisposition, for which he had little credit with his fair sister-in-law, could not spare his wife.", "그랜트 박사가, 그 어여쁜 처제에게는 좀처럼 믿기지 않는 몸살을 핑계로, 제 아내를 내줄 수 없다는 것이었다."]], [["“Dr. Grant is ill,”", "“그랜트 박사가 편찮으시대요.”"], ["said she, with mock solemnity.", "그녀가 짐짓 근엄한 척 말했다."], ["“He has been ill ever since he did not eat any of the pheasant today.", "“오늘 꿩고기를 한 점도 안 드신 뒤로 죽 편찮으시다지 뭐예요."], ["He fancied it tough, sent away his plate, and has been suffering ever since”.", "질기다 싶어 접시를 물리시더니, 그 뒤로 죽 앓고 계신답니다.”"]], [["Here was disappointment!", "이 무슨 낭패인가!"], ["Mrs. Grant’s non-attendance was sad indeed.", "그랜트 부인의 불참은 정말이지 서글픈 일이었다."], ["Her pleasant manners and cheerful conformity made her always valuable amongst them; but now she was absolutely necessary.", "그 상냥한 태도와 명랑한 어울림이 그녀를 늘 그들 틈에서 값진 사람으로 만들었으나, 이제 그녀는 없어서는 안 될 사람이었다."], ["They could not act, they could not rehearse with any satisfaction without her.", "그녀 없이는 연기할 수도, 만족스레 연습할 수도 없었다. 온 저녁의 즐거움이 무너졌다."], ["The comfort of the whole evening was destroyed.", "어찌해야 할까? 소작인 역인 톰은 절망에 빠졌다."], ["What was to be done? Tom, as Cottager, was in despair.", "어리둥절한 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 몇몇 눈길이 패니 쪽으로 돌려지기 시작했고, 한둘의 목소리가 이렇게 말했다."], ["After a pause of perplexity, some eyes began to be turned towards Fanny, and a voice or two to say, “If Miss Price would be so good as to read the part.”", "“프라이스 양이 그 배역을 읽어 주기만 한다면.” 그녀는 이내 온갖 간청에 둘러싸였다."], ["She was immediately surrounded by supplications; everybody asked it; even Edmund said, “Do, Fanny, if it is not very disagreeable to you.”", "다들 그것을 청했고, 에드먼드마저 이렇게 말했다. “해 다오, 패니, 정 몹시 싫은 게 아니라면.”"]], [["But Fanny still hung back.", "그러나 패니는 여전히 뒷걸음질쳤다."], ["She could not endure the idea of it.", "그녀는 그런 생각을 견딜 수 없었다."], ["Why was not Miss Crawford to be applied to as well?", "어째서 크로퍼드 양한텐 청하지 않는 걸까?"], ["Or why had not she rather gone to her own room, as she had felt to be safest, instead of attending the rehearsal at all?", "아니, 애초에 어째서 연습을 지켜보고 있을 게 아니라, 저로선 가장 안전하다 느꼈던 대로 제 방으로 가 있지 않았던가?"], ["She had known it would irritate and distress her; she had known it her duty to keep away.", "그것이 자기를 애태우고 괴롭게 하리라는 걸 알았으면서, 멀리 떨어져 있는 게 제 도리임을 알았으면서."], ["She was properly punished.", "그녀는 마땅한 벌을 받은 셈이었다."]], [["“You have only to read the part,”", "“그저 그 배역을 읽어 주기만 하면 돼요.”"], ["said Henry Crawford, with renewed entreaty.", "헨리 크로퍼드가 새삼 간청하며 말했다."]], [["“And I do believe she can say every word of it,”", "“그리고 나는 그 애가 한 마디 한 마디 다 외운다고 믿어요.”"], ["added Maria, “for she could put Mrs.", "마리아가 덧붙였다."], ["Grant right the other day in twenty places.", "“며칠 전에도 그랜트 부인이 스무 군데나 틀린 걸 바로잡아 줬거든요."], ["Fanny, I am sure you know the part.”", "패니, 넌 그 배역 알고 있을 거야, 틀림없이.”"]], [["Fanny could not say she did not; and as they all persevered, as Edmund repeated his wish, and with a look of even fond dependence on her good-nature, she must yield.", "패니는 모른다고 할 수 없었다. 그리고 다들 끈덕지게 조르고, 에드먼드가 제 바람을 되풀이하며, 게다가 그녀의 착한 성품에 애틋하게 기대는 듯한 눈빛까지 하니, 그녀는 굽힐 수밖에 없었다."], ["She would do her best.", "최선을 다하기로 했다."], ["Everybody was satisfied; and she was left to the tremors of a most palpitating heart, while the others prepared to begin.", "다들 흡족해했고, 그녀는 더없이 두방망이질하는 가슴의 떨림에 내맡겨진 채, 다른 이들이 시작할 채비를 하는 것을 지켜보았다."]], [["They did begin; and being too much engaged in their own noise to be struck by an unusual noise in the other part of the house, had proceeded some way when the door of the room was thrown open, and Julia, appearing at it, with a face all aghast, exclaimed, “My father is come!", "그들은 정말로 시작했다. 그리고 저희 소란에 너무 열중한 나머지 집 다른 편에서 나는 심상찮은 소리를 알아채지 못한 채 얼마쯤 나아갔는데, 그때 방문이 벌컥 열리며 줄리아가 나타나, 얼굴이 온통 새파랗게 질린 채 외쳤다."], ["He is in the hall at this moment.”", "“아버지가 오셨어! 지금 이 순간 현관에 계셔.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XIX", "# 제19장"]], [["How is the consternation of the party to be described?", "그 무리의 경악을 어찌 말로 그리랴? 대다수에게 그것은 순전한 공포의 순간이었다."], ["To the greater number it was a moment of absolute horror.", "토머스 경이 집에 오시다니! 다들 그 사실을 순식간에 확신했다."], ["Sir Thomas in the house! All felt the instantaneous conviction.", "잘못 안 것이거나 착각이려니 하는 희망은 어디에도 품어지지 않았다."], ["Not a hope of imposition or mistake was harboured anywhere.", "줄리아의 낯빛이 그 사실의 증거였으니 반박할 여지가 없었다."], ["Julia’s looks were an evidence of the fact that made it indisputable; and after the first starts and exclamations, not a word was spoken for half a minute: each with an altered countenance was looking at some other, and almost each was feeling it a stroke the most unwelcome, most ill-timed, most appalling!", "그리고 처음의 소스라침과 탄성이 지난 뒤, 반 분 동안 아무도 한마디 말이 없었다. 저마다 낯빛이 달라진 채 다른 누군가를 바라보았고, 거의 저마다 그것을 더없이 달갑잖고, 더없이 때 나쁘고, 더없이 소름 끼치는 일격으로 느끼고 있었다!"], ["Mr. Yates might consider it only as a vexatious interruption for the evening, and Mr.", "예이츠 씨야 그것을 그저 그날 저녁을 망치는 성가신 훼방쯤으로 여겼을 테고, 러시워스 씨야 그것을 축복으로 여겼을지 모른다."], ["Rushworth might imagine it a blessing; but every other heart was sinking under some degree of self-condemnation or undefined alarm, every other heart was suggesting, “What will become of us?", "그러나 그 밖의 모든 가슴은 어느 정도의 자책이나 막연한 불안에 짓눌려 가라앉고 있었고, 그 밖의 모든 가슴이 이렇게 뇌고 있었다. “우리는 어찌 되는 걸까?"], ["what is to be done now?”", "이제 어떡하지?”"], ["It was a terrible pause; and terrible to every ear were the corroborating sounds of opening doors and passing footsteps.", "그것은 끔찍한 정적이었다. 그리고 문 열리는 소리며 지나가는 발소리가 그 사실을 뒷받침하는 것이, 모든 귀에 끔찍했다."]], [["Julia was the first to move and speak again. Jealousy and bitterness had been suspended: selfishness was lost in the common cause; but at the moment of her appearance, Frederick was listening with looks of devotion to Agatha’s narrative, and pressing her hand to his heart; and as soon as she could notice this, and see that, in spite of the shock of her words, he still kept his station and retained her sister’s hand, her wounded heart swelled again with injury, and looking as red as she had been white before, she turned out of the room, saying, “I need not be afraid of appearing before him.”", "다시 움직이고 말을 꺼낸 것은 줄리아가 처음이었다. 시샘과 쓰라림은 잦아든 참이었다. 이기심은 다 함께 걸린 이 판국에 묻혀 버렸다. 그러나 그녀가 나타난 그 순간, 프레더릭은 아가사의 넋두리에 헌신 어린 눈빛으로 귀 기울이며 그 손을 제 가슴에 꼭 눌러 대고 있었다. 그리고 그녀가 이것을 알아채고, 제 말이 안긴 충격에도 불구하고 그가 여전히 제자리를 지키며 언니의 손을 붙들고 있음을 보게 되자, 그녀의 상처 입은 마음이 다시 서러움으로 부풀어 올랐고, 아까 창백했던 만큼이나 새빨개진 얼굴로 방을 나서며 이렇게 말했다. “나야 그 앞에 나서기가 겁날 것 없지.”"]], [["Her going roused the rest; and at the same moment the two brothers stepped forward, feeling the necessity of doing something.", "그녀가 나가자 나머지가 정신을 차렸다. 그리고 바로 그 순간 두 오라비가 앞으로 나섰으니, 무언가 해야 함을 느낀 것이다."], ["A very few words between them were sufficient.", "둘 사이에 아주 몇 마디면 충분했다."], ["The case admitted no difference of opinion: they must go to the drawing-room directly.", "이 판국에는 의견이 갈릴 여지가 없었다. 당장 응접실로 가야 했다."], ["Maria joined them with the same intent, just then the stoutest of the three; for the very circumstance which had driven Julia away was to her the sweetest support.", "마리아도 같은 뜻으로 그들에 어울렸으니, 그때만은 셋 가운데 가장 담대했다. 줄리아를 몰아낸 바로 그 사정이 그녀에게는 더없이 달콤한 버팀목이었기 때문이다."], ["Henry Crawford’s retaining her hand at such a moment, a moment of such peculiar proof and importance, was worth ages of doubt and anxiety.", "헨리 크로퍼드가 그런 순간에, 그토록 유별난 증표이자 중대한 순간에 제 손을 붙들어 준 것은, 오랜 세월의 의심과 근심에 맞먹을 값어치가 있었다."], ["She hailed it as an earnest of the most serious determination, and was equal even to encounter her father.", "그녀는 그것을 더없이 진지한 결심의 징표로 반겼고, 아버지를 마주할 배포마저 갖추었다."], ["They walked off, utterly heedless of Mr.", "그들은 걸어 나갔으니, 러시워스 씨가 “저도 갈까요?"], ["Rushworth’s repeated question of, “Shall I go too?", "저도 가는 게 낫지 않을까요?"], ["Had not I better go too?", "저도 가는 게 옳지 않겠어요?”"], ["Will not it be right for me to go too?”", "하고 거듭 묻는 것에는 도무지 아랑곳없었다."], ["but they were no sooner through the door than Henry Crawford undertook to answer the anxious inquiry, and, encouraging him by all means to pay his respects to Sir Thomas without delay, sent him after the others with delighted haste.", "그러나 그들이 문을 나서기 무섭게 헨리 크로퍼드가 그 애타는 물음에 답을 해 주었으니, 지체 없이 토머스 경께 문안 인사를 드리라고 온갖 말로 부추기며, 그를 흐뭇하게 서둘러 다른 이들 뒤로 보냈다."]], [["Fanny was left with only the Crawfords and Mr. Yates.", "패니는 크로퍼드 남매와 예이츠 씨만 남은 자리에 남겨졌다."], ["She had been quite overlooked by her cousins; and as her own opinion of her claims on Sir Thomas’s affection was much too humble to give her any idea of classing herself with his children, she was glad to remain behind and gain a little breathing-time.", "사촌들은 그녀를 아주 잊고 있었다. 그리고 토머스 경의 애정에 제가 무슨 권리가 있으랴 하는 그녀 자신의 생각은 너무나 겸손해, 자기를 그의 자식들과 한데 묶을 엄두조차 나지 않았던 터라, 그녀는 뒤에 남아 잠깐 숨 돌릴 짬을 얻는 것이 반가웠다."], ["Her agitation and alarm exceeded all that was endured by the rest, by the right of a disposition which not even innocence could keep from suffering.", "그녀의 동요와 불안은 나머지 사람들이 겪는 그 모든 것을 넘어섰으니, 죄 없음으로도 괴로움을 면치 못하는 그 천성 탓이었다. 그녀는 거의 까무러칠 지경이었다."], ["She was nearly fainting: all her former habitual dread of her uncle was returning, and with it compassion for him and for almost every one of the party on the development before him, with solicitude on Edmund’s account indescribable.", "삼촌에 대한 예전의 그 몸에 밴 두려움이 죄다 되살아났고, 그와 더불어 그와 이 무리 거의 모두에 대한 연민이, 그의 눈앞에 드러날 사태를 두고 밀려들었으며, 에드먼드를 걱정하는 마음은 이루 말할 수 없었다."], ["She had found a seat, where in excessive trembling she was enduring all these fearful thoughts, while the other three, no longer under any restraint, were giving vent to their feelings of vexation, lamenting over such an unlooked-for premature arrival as a most untoward event, and without mercy wishing poor Sir Thomas had been twice as long on his passage, or were still in Antigua.", "그녀는 자리 하나를 찾아, 심하게 떨며 이 온갖 무서운 생각을 견디고 있었고, 그동안 다른 세 사람은 이제 아무 거리낌 없이 짜증스러운 감정을 쏟아 내며, 이토록 뜻밖에 이르디이른 도착을 더없이 고약한 일이라 한탄하고, 딱한 토머스 경이 항해에 곱절은 더 걸렸더라면, 아니면 여태 앤티가에 계셨더라면 하고 무자비하게 바랐다."]], [["The Crawfords were more warm on the subject than Mr. Yates, from better understanding the family, and judging more clearly of the mischief that must ensue.", "크로퍼드 남매는 예이츠 씨보다 이 일에 더 열을 올렸으니, 그 집안을 더 잘 아는 데다 반드시 뒤따를 낭패를 더 또렷이 헤아렸기 때문이다."], ["The ruin of the play was to them a certainty: they felt the total destruction of the scheme to be inevitably at hand; while Mr.", "그들에게 연극의 파탄은 정해진 일이었다. 이 계획의 완전한 무너짐이 코앞에 닥쳤음을 그들은 느꼈다."], ["Yates considered it only as a temporary interruption, a disaster for the evening, and could even suggest the possibility of the rehearsal being renewed after tea, when the bustle of receiving Sir Thomas were over, and he might be at leisure to be amused by it.", "반면 예이츠 씨는 그것을 그저 한때의 훼방, 그날 저녁의 낭패쯤으로 여겨, 토머스 경을 맞는 부산함이 끝나고 그가 한가로이 즐길 만해지면 차를 마신 뒤 연습을 다시 시작할 수 있으리라는 가능성마저 내비쳤다. 크로퍼드 남매는 그 생각을 비웃었다."], ["The Crawfords laughed at the idea; and having soon agreed on the propriety of their walking quietly home and leaving the family to themselves, proposed Mr.", "그리고 조용히 걸어서 집으로 돌아가 그 식구끼리 있게 두는 것이 마땅하다고 이내 뜻을 모은 뒤, 예이츠 씨더러 저희와 함께 가서 목사관에서 저녁을 보내자고 청했다. 그러나 예이츠 씨는 부모의 도리며 집안의 사사로운 정 따위를 대수로이 여기는 이들과 어울려 본 적이 없던지라, 그런 것이 무슨 필요가 있는지 도무지 알아채지 못했다."], ["Yates’s accompanying them and spending the evening at the Parsonage. But Mr.", "그리하여 그는 그들에게 고맙다면서 이렇게 말했다."], ["Yates, having never been with those who thought much of parental claims, or family confidence, could not perceive that anything of the kind was necessary; and therefore, thanking them, said, “he preferred remaining where he was, that he might pay his respects to the old gentleman handsomely since he was come; and besides, he did not think it would be fair by the others to have everybody run away.”", "“나야 그 어른이 오셨으니 이왕이면 여기 남아 그분께 번듯하게 문안이나 드리는 게 낫겠어요. 게다가 다들 달아나 버리면 남은 사람들한테 도리가 아니지 싶고요.”"]], [["Fanny was just beginning to collect herself, and to feel that if she staid longer behind it might seem disrespectful, when this point was settled, and being commissioned with the brother and sister’s apology, saw them preparing to go as she quitted the room herself to perform the dreadful duty of appearing before her uncle.", "이 문제가 매듭지어질 무렵 패니는 막 정신을 추스르기 시작했고, 더 오래 뒤에 남아 있다간 무례해 보일지 모른다고 느끼던 참이었다. 그리하여 남매의 사과를 전할 소임을 맡은 채, 그녀는 삼촌 앞에 나서는 그 무서운 도리를 다하러 몸소 방을 나서며, 그들이 떠날 채비를 하는 것을 보았다."]], [["Too soon did she find herself at the drawing-room door; and after pausing a moment for what she knew would not come, for a courage which the outside of no door had ever supplied to her, she turned the lock in desperation, and the lights of the drawing-room, and all the collected family, were before her.", "그녀는 너무나 이르게도 응접실 문 앞에 다다랐다. 그리고 결코 오지 않을 것을—어느 문 바깥에서도 여태 그녀에게 준 적 없는 용기를—잠시 기다린 뒤, 자포자기하는 심정으로 자물쇠를 돌리니, 응접실의 불빛과 한자리에 모인 온 식구가 그녀 앞에 있었다."], ["As she entered, her own name caught her ear.", "들어서는데 제 이름이 귀에 들어왔다."], ["Sir Thomas was at that moment looking round him, and saying, “But where is Fanny?", "마침 그때 토머스 경이 둘레를 둘러보며 이렇게 말하고 있었다. “그런데 패니는 어디 있느냐?"], ["Why do not I see my little Fanny?”—and on perceiving her, came forward with a kindness which astonished and penetrated her, calling her his dear Fanny, kissing her affectionately, and observing with decided pleasure how much she was grown!", "어째서 우리 어린 패니가 안 보이지?”—그러고는 그녀를 알아보자, 그녀를 놀라게 하고 가슴에 사무치게 하는 다정함으로 다가와, 그녀를 사랑하는 패니라 부르며 정겹게 입 맞추고, 어쩜 이리 많이 컸느냐고 뚜렷한 기쁨으로 눈여겨보았다!"], ["Fanny knew not how to feel, nor where to look.", "패니는 어찌 느껴야 할지, 어디를 보아야 할지 몰랐다."], ["She was quite oppressed.", "그녀는 온통 짓눌린 심정이었다."], ["He had never been so kind, so very kind to her in his life.", "그가 평생 그녀에게 그토록 다정한, 그토록 유난히 다정한 적이 없었다."], ["His manner seemed changed, his voice was quick from the agitation of joy; and all that had been awful in his dignity seemed lost in tenderness.", "그의 태도는 달라진 듯했고, 목소리는 기쁨의 설렘으로 빨랐으며, 그 위엄에서 두렵던 모든 것이 다정함에 녹아 사라진 듯했다."], ["He led her nearer the light and looked at her again—inquired particularly after her health, and then, correcting himself, observed that he need not inquire, for her appearance spoke sufficiently on that point.", "그는 그녀를 불빛 가까이로 이끌어 다시 바라보며—그녀의 건강을 각별히 물었다가, 이내 스스로를 바로잡아, 그럴 것 없다고, 그녀의 안색이 그 점을 넉넉히 말해 준다고 하였다."], ["A fine blush having succeeded the previous paleness of her face, he was justified in his belief of her equal improvement in health and beauty.", "앞서 창백했던 그녀의 얼굴에 이제 고운 홍조가 돌았으니, 그녀가 건강도 아름다움도 똑같이 나아졌다는 그의 믿음이 옳았다. 이어 그는 그녀의 식구를, 특히 윌리엄을 물었다."], ["He inquired next after her family, especially William: and his kindness altogether was such as made her reproach herself for loving him so little, and thinking his return a misfortune; and when, on having courage to lift her eyes to his face, she saw that he was grown thinner, and had the burnt, fagged, worn look of fatigue and a hot climate, every tender feeling was increased, and she was miserable in considering how much unsuspected vexation was probably ready to burst on him.", "그리고 그의 다정함이 통틀어 어찌나 크던지, 그녀는 그를 그토록 조금밖에 사랑하지 않고 그의 귀환을 불행으로 여긴 저 자신을 나무라게 되었다. 그리고 용기를 내어 그의 얼굴로 눈을 들어 올렸을 때, 그가 부쩍 여위고, 피로와 무더운 날씨에 그을리고 초췌하고 지친 낯빛임을 보게 되자, 온갖 다정한 감정이 더해졌고, 짐작지 못한 얼마나 많은 짜증거리가 곧 그에게 터져 나오려 하는지 헤아리니 그녀는 비참했다."]], [["Sir Thomas was indeed the life of the party, who at his suggestion now seated themselves round the fire.", "토머스 경은 과연 그 무리의 활기였으니, 그의 권유에 다들 이제 난롯가에 둘러앉았다."], ["He had the best right to be the talker; and the delight of his sensations in being again in his own house, in the centre of his family, after such a separation, made him communicative and chatty in a very unusual degree; and he was ready to give every information as to his voyage, and answer every question of his two sons almost before it was put.", "이야기하는 이가 될 자격이야 그에게 가장 있었다. 그리고 그토록 오래 떨어져 있다가 다시 제집에, 제 식구 한복판에 있게 된 그 감격이, 그를 여느 때와 아주 다르게 말 많고 수다스럽게 만들었다."], ["His business in Antigua had latterly been prosperously rapid, and he came directly from Liverpool, having had an opportunity of making his passage thither in a private vessel, instead of waiting for the packet; and all the little particulars of his proceedings and events, his arrivals and departures, were most promptly delivered, as he sat by Lady Bertram and looked with heartfelt satisfaction on the faces around him—interrupting himself more than once, however, to remark on his good fortune in finding them all at home—coming unexpectedly as he did—all collected together exactly as he could have wished, but dared not depend on.", "그는 제 항해에 대해 무엇이든 알려 줄 태세였고, 두 아들의 어떤 물음에도 그것이 채 나오기도 전에 답할 태세였다. 앤티가에서의 그의 볼일은 근래 순조롭게 척척 진행된 터라, 그는 우편선을 기다리는 대신 사사로운 배편으로 리버풀까지 건너올 기회를 얻어 곧장 거기서 온 것이었다."], ["Mr. Rushworth was not forgotten: a most friendly reception and warmth of hand-shaking had already met him, and with pointed attention he was now included in the objects most intimately connected with Mansfield.", "그리고 그가 겪은 일과 사건, 도착과 출발의 자잘한 사연이 죄다 냉큼냉큼 전해졌으니, 그는 버트럼 부인 곁에 앉아 둘레의 얼굴들을 진심 어린 흡족함으로 바라보며—다만 한 번 넘게 스스로 말을 끊고, 이렇게 뜻밖에 들이닥쳤는데도 다들 집에 있는 걸 보게 된 제 복을 두고 한마디씩 했다—바라 마지않았으나 감히 믿지는 못했던 그대로, 다들 꼭 한자리에 모여 있었으니 말이다."], ["There was nothing disagreeable in Mr.", "러시워스 씨도 잊히지 않았다."], ["Rushworth’s appearance, and Sir Thomas was liking him already.", "더없이 다정한 맞이와 뜨거운 악수가 이미 그를 맞았고, 이제 그는 각별한 마음씀으로 맨스필드와 가장 가까이 얽힌 이들 축에 끼었다. 러시워스 씨의 겉모습에는 언짢을 데가 없었고, 토머스 경은 벌써 그가 마음에 들기 시작했다."]], [["By not one of the circle was he listened to with such unbroken, unalloyed enjoyment as by his wife, who was really extremely happy to see him, and whose feelings were so warmed by his sudden arrival as to place her nearer agitation than she had been for the last twenty years.", "그 자리의 누구도 그의 아내만큼 끊김 없고 티 없는 즐거움으로 그의 말에 귀 기울이지 않았으니, 그녀는 그를 보게 되어 정말이지 더없이 행복했고, 그의 갑작스러운 도착에 감정이 어찌나 달아올랐던지 지난 이십 년 새 어느 때보다도 설렘에 가까웠다."], ["She had been almost fluttered for a few minutes, and still remained so sensibly animated as to put away her work, move Pug from her side, and give all her attention and all the rest of her sofa to her husband.", "그녀는 몇 분 동안 거의 가슴이 두근거릴 지경이었고, 여전히 그토록 뚜렷이 생기가 돌아, 일감을 밀어 두고, 곁에서 퍼그를 옮기고, 온 주의와 소파의 나머지 자리를 죄다 남편에게 내주었다. 그녀의 즐거움을 흐릴 만한 근심이라곤 누구를 위해서도 없었다."], ["She had no anxieties for anybody to cloud her pleasure: her own time had been irreproachably spent during his absence: she had done a great deal of carpet-work, and made many yards of fringe; and she would have answered as freely for the good conduct and useful pursuits of all the young people as for her own.", "그가 없는 동안 그녀 자신의 시간은 나무랄 데 없이 보내진 터였다. 그녀는 양탄자 일감을 잔뜩 했고, 술 장식도 몇 야드나 떴다."], ["It was so agreeable to her to see him again, and hear him talk, to have her ear amused and her whole comprehension filled by his narratives, that she began particularly to feel how dreadfully she must have missed him, and how impossible it would have been for her to bear a lengthened absence.", "그러니 그녀는 젊은이들 모두의 바른 처신과 알찬 소일거리에 대해서도 제 것 못지않게 선뜻 장담했을 것이다. 그를 다시 보고 그가 이야기하는 것을 듣고, 그 이야기에 귀가 즐겁고 온 이해가 가득 차는 것이 그녀에게는 어찌나 흐뭇하던지, 그녀는 자기가 그를 얼마나 지독히 그리워했음이 틀림없는지, 오랜 이별이라면 자기가 얼마나 견디지 못했을지를 유난히 절감하기 시작했다."]], [["Mrs. Norris was by no means to be compared in happiness to her sister.", "노리스 부인은 그 행복으로 말하면 결코 그 아우에 견줄 바가 못 되었다."], ["Not that she was incommoded by many fears of Sir Thomas’s disapprobation when the present state of his house should be known, for her judgment had been so blinded that, except by the instinctive caution with which she had whisked away Mr.", "집의 지금 형편이 알려지면 토머스 경이 못마땅해하시리라는 두려움에 그녀가 시달렸다는 것이 아니다. 그녀의 판단이 어찌나 눈멀어 있었던지, 형부가 들어설 때 러시워스 씨의 분홍 새틴 망토를 본능적인 조심성으로 잽싸게 치워 버린 것 말고는, 딱히 놀란 기색이라곤 보였다 할 게 거의 없었다."], ["Rushworth’s pink satin cloak as her brother-in-law entered, she could hardly be said to shew any sign of alarm; but she was vexed by the manner of his return.", "다만 그녀는 그가 돌아온 그 방식에 속이 상했다."], ["It had left her nothing to do.", "그것이 그녀에게 할 일을 하나도 남겨 주지 않았던 것이다."], ["Instead of being sent for out of the room, and seeing him first, and having to spread the happy news through the house, Sir Thomas, with a very reasonable dependence, perhaps, on the nerves of his wife and children, had sought no confidant but the butler, and had been following him almost instantaneously into the drawing-room. Mrs.", "방 밖으로 불려 나가 그를 먼저 뵙고 그 반가운 소식을 온 집 안에 퍼뜨리는 대신, 토머스 경은—아마도 제 아내와 자식들의 신경을 어지간히 믿었던지—집사 말고는 아무 심부름꾼도 찾지 않고, 거의 곧바로 그를 뒤따라 응접실로 들어와 버린 것이다."], ["Norris felt herself defrauded of an office on which she had always depended, whether his arrival or his death were to be the thing unfolded; and was now trying to be in a bustle without having anything to bustle about, and labouring to be important where nothing was wanted but tranquillity and silence.", "노리스 부인은, 그의 도착이든 그의 죽음이든 무엇이 알려질 일이건 간에 늘 저한테 맡겨졌던 그 소임을 빼앗긴 기분이었다. 그래서 이제 부산 떨 거리라곤 아무것도 없이 부산을 떨어 보려 애쓰고, 평온과 침묵 말고는 아무것도 필요치 않은 자리에서 대단한 사람이 되어 보려 안간힘을 썼다."], ["Would Sir Thomas have consented to eat, she might have gone to the housekeeper with troublesome directions, and insulted the footmen with injunctions of despatch; but Sir Thomas resolutely declined all dinner: he would take nothing, nothing till tea came—he would rather wait for tea. Still Mrs.", "토머스 경이 뭘 좀 들겠다고만 했어도, 그녀는 성가신 지시를 잔뜩 안고 가정부에게 갔을 것이요, 어서 서두르라는 분부로 하인들을 들볶았을 것이다."], ["Norris was at intervals urging something different; and in the most interesting moment of his passage to England, when the alarm of a French privateer was at the height, she burst through his recital with the proposal of soup.", "그러나 토머스 경은 어떤 만찬도 딱 잘라 마다했다. 아무것도 안 들겠다고, 차가 나올 때까지 아무것도—차라리 차를 기다리겠노라 했다."], ["“Sure, my dear Sir Thomas, a basin of soup would be a much better thing for you than tea.", "그래도 노리스 부인은 틈틈이 뭔가 다른 것을 자꾸 권했다. 그리고 그가 잉글랜드로 건너오던 이야기의 가장 마음 졸이는 대목, 프랑스 사략선의 위협이 극에 달한 대목에서, 그녀는 수프를 권하며 그의 이야기를 뚫고 들어왔다."], ["Do have a basin of soup.”", "“그러지 마시고, 토머스 경, 차보다야 수프 한 대접이 경께 훨씬 나을 거예요. 수프 한 대접 드셔 보세요.”"]], [["Sir Thomas could not be provoked.", "토머스 경은 성을 낼 수가 없었다."], ["“Still the same anxiety for everybody’s comfort, my dear Mrs.", "“여전히 모두의 편안함을 그리 걱정하시는구려, 노리스 부인.”"], ["Norris,” was his answer.", "이것이 그의 대답이었다."], ["“But indeed I would rather have nothing but tea.”", "“하지만 정말이지 나는 차 말고는 아무것도 안 드는 게 좋겠소.”"]], [["“Well, then, Lady Bertram, suppose you speak for tea directly; suppose you hurry Baddeley a little; he seems behindhand to-night.”", "“그럼, 버트럼 부인, 얼른 차를 좀 청하시지요. 배들리를 좀 재촉하시고요."], ["She carried this point, and Sir Thomas’s narrative proceeded.", "오늘 밤 그 사람 굼떠 보이네요.” 그녀는 이 대목을 관철했고, 토머스 경의 이야기가 이어졌다."]], [["At length there was a pause.", "마침내 말이 잠깐 끊겼다. 그가 당장 전할 이야기는 바닥났고, 이제 그 사랑하는 무리를 이 사람 저 사람 즐거이 둘러보는 것만으로도 넉넉한 듯했다."], ["His immediate communications were exhausted, and it seemed enough to be looking joyfully around him, now at one, now at another of the beloved circle; but the pause was not long: in the elation of her spirits Lady Bertram became talkative, and what were the sensations of her children upon hearing her say, “How do you think the young people have been amusing themselves lately, Sir Thomas?", "그러나 그 침묵은 길지 않았다. 들뜬 기분에 버트럼 부인이 말이 많아진 것이다."], ["They have been acting.", "그리고 그녀가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었을 때 그 자식들의 심경이 어떠했으랴. “요즘 젊은것들이 어떻게 지내는 줄 아세요, 여보?"], ["We have been all alive with acting.”", "그 애들이 연기를 하고 있었답니다. 온 집안이 연기로 들썩였어요.”"]], [["“Indeed!", "“그래?"], ["and what have you been acting?”", "그럼 무슨 연기를 했소?”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아유!"], ["they’ll tell you all about it.”", "그건 그 애들이 다 말씀드릴 거예요.”"]], [["“The all will soon be told,”", "“그 ‘다’야 곧 말씀드릴 수 있죠.”"], ["cried Tom hastily, and with affected unconcern; “but it is not worth while to bore my father with it now.", "톰이 짐짓 태연한 척 서둘러 외쳤다. “하지만 지금 그걸로 아버지를 지루하게 해 드릴 것까진 없어요."], ["You will hear enough of it to-morrow, sir.", "내일이면 실컷 들으실 테니까요, 아버지."], ["We have just been trying, by way of doing something, and amusing my mother, just within the last week, to get up a few scenes, a mere trifle.", "저희는 그저 뭐라도 좀 하면서 어머니를 즐겁게 해 드려 볼까 하고, 바로 지난 한 주 새에, 장면 몇 개를 꾸며 봤을 뿐이에요, 그야말로 사소한 거죠."], ["We have had such incessant rains almost since October began, that we have been nearly confined to the house for days together.", "시월이 시작될 무렵부터 비가 어찌나 쉴 새 없이 쏟아지던지, 며칠씩 집에만 갇혀 지내다시피 했거든요."], ["I have hardly taken out a gun since the 3rd.", "저는 3일 이후로 총 한번 제대로 못 들었어요."], ["Tolerable sport the first three days, but there has been no attempting anything since.", "첫 사흘은 그런대로 사냥이 됐는데, 그 뒤론 뭘 해 볼 엄두도 못 냈고요."], ["The first day I went over Mansfield Wood, and Edmund took the copses beyond Easton, and we brought home six brace between us, and might each have killed six times as many, but we respect your pheasants, sir, I assure you, as much as you could desire.", "첫날 저는 맨스필드 숲을 돌고 에드먼드는 이스턴 너머 잡목림을 맡아서, 둘이 여섯 쌍을 잡아 왔는데, 저마다 그 여섯 배는 잡을 수 있었지만, 아버지 꿩을 아버지가 바라시는 만큼이나 아낀답니다, 정말이에요."], ["I do not think you will find your woods by any means worse stocked than they were.", "아버지 숲이 예전보다 조금이라도 덜 찼다고는 여기지 않으실 거예요."], ["I never saw Mansfield Wood so full of pheasants in my life as this year.", "저는 평생 올해처럼 맨스필드 숲이 꿩으로 그득한 걸 본 적이 없어요."], ["I hope you will take a day’s sport there yourself, sir, soon.”", "조만간 아버지도 몸소 거기서 하루 사냥을 즐기시면 좋겠어요.”"]], [["For the present the danger was over, and Fanny’s sick feelings subsided; but when tea was soon afterwards brought in, and Sir Thomas, getting up, said that he found that he could not be any longer in the house without just looking into his own dear room, every agitation was returning.", "이번만은 위험이 지나갔고, 패니의 메스꺼운 심정도 가라앉았다. 그러나 이내 차가 들어오고, 토머스 경이 일어서며 사랑하는 제 방을 잠깐 들여다보지 않고는 더는 집에 있을 수가 없겠다고 하자, 온갖 동요가 되살아났다."], ["He was gone before anything had been said to prepare him for the change he must find there; and a pause of alarm followed his disappearance.", "그가 거기서 마주할 그 변화에 대비시킬 어떤 말도 건네기 전에 그는 가 버렸고, 그가 사라진 뒤로 불안한 정적이 흘렀다."], ["Edmund was the first to speak—", "먼저 입을 연 것은 에드먼드였다—"]], [["“Something must be done,”", "“무슨 수를 내야 해.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“It is time to think of our visitors,”", "“이제 손님들 생각을 해야지.”"], ["said Maria, still feeling her hand pressed to Henry Crawford’s heart, and caring little for anything else.", "마리아가 말했는데, 여전히 제 손이 헨리 크로퍼드의 가슴에 눌려 있는 느낌에, 다른 것에는 도무지 마음이 없었다."], ["“Where did you leave Miss Crawford, Fanny?”", "“크로퍼드 양은 어디 두고 왔니, 패니?”"]], [["Fanny told of their departure, and delivered their message.", "패니는 그들이 떠난 것을 이르고, 그들의 전갈을 전했다."]], [["“Then poor Yates is all alone,”", "“그럼 딱한 예이츠가 혼자겠구나.”"], ["cried Tom.", "톰이 외쳤다."], ["“I will go and fetch him.", "“내가 가서 데려와야지."], ["He will be no bad assistant when it all comes out.”", "이 일이 죄다 드러날 때 그 사람이 그리 나쁜 조력자는 아닐 테니.”"]], [["To the theatre he went, and reached it just in time to witness the first meeting of his father and his friend.", "그는 극장으로 갔고, 마침 제 아버지와 제 벗의 첫 만남을 지켜보기 딱 알맞은 때에 다다랐다."], ["Sir Thomas had been a good deal surprised to find candles burning in his room; and on casting his eye round it, to see other symptoms of recent habitation and a general air of confusion in the furniture.", "토머스 경은 제 방에 촛불이 켜져 있는 것을 보고 자못 놀란 참이었다. 그리고 방을 둘러보다, 근래 누가 지낸 다른 흔적들과 세간의 온통 어수선한 기운을 보았다."], ["The removal of the bookcase from before the billiard-room door struck him especially, but he had scarcely more than time to feel astonished at all this, before there were sounds from the billiard-room to astonish him still farther.", "당구실 문 앞에서 책장이 치워진 것이 유난히 그의 눈에 띄었으나, 이 모든 것에 놀랄 겨를도 채 없이, 당구실 쪽에서 그를 한층 더 놀라게 할 소리가 들려왔다. 누군가 거기서 아주 큰 소리로 지껄이고 있었다."], ["Some one was talking there in a very loud accent; he did not know the voice—more than talking—almost hallooing.", "그는 그 목소리를 몰랐다—지껄인다기보다는—거의 고래고래 외치는 것이었다."], ["He stepped to the door, rejoicing at that moment in having the means of immediate communication, and, opening it, found himself on the stage of a theatre, and opposed to a ranting young man, who appeared likely to knock him down backwards.", "그는 지금 곧바로 이야기를 나눌 방도가 생긴 것을 그 순간 반기며 그 문으로 다가갔고, 문을 여니 어느 극장 무대 위에 서 있는 자신을 발견했다. 그리고 그 앞에는 열변을 토하는 젊은이 하나가, 금방이라도 그를 뒤로 넘어뜨릴 듯이 서 있었다."], ["At the very moment of Yates perceiving Sir Thomas, and giving perhaps the very best start he had ever given in the whole course of his rehearsals, Tom Bertram entered at the other end of the room; and never had he found greater difficulty in keeping his countenance.", "예이츠가 토머스 경을 알아채고, 아마 제 온 연습을 통틀어 가장 멋진 소스라침을 내보인 바로 그 순간, 톰 버트럼이 방 저쪽 끝으로 들어섰다. 그리고 그는 낯빛을 지키기가 그토록 어려운 적이 없었다."], ["His father’s looks of solemnity and amazement on this his first appearance on any stage, and the gradual metamorphosis of the impassioned Baron Wildenheim into the well-bred and easy Mr. Yates, making his bow and apology to Sir Thomas Bertram, was such an exhibition, such a piece of true acting, as he would not have lost upon any account.", "어느 무대에든 처음 오르는 이 순간, 아버지의 근엄하고 아연한 낯빛과, 격정에 찬 빌덴하임 남작이 토머스 버트럼 경께 절하고 사과하는 점잖고 태연한 예이츠 씨로 차츰 탈바꿈해 가는 꼴은, 그 무엇을 준다 해도 놓치고 싶지 않을 그런 구경거리, 그런 진짜 연기 한 판이었다."], ["It would be the last—in all probability—the last scene on that stage; but he was sure there could not be a finer.", "그것은 아마 그 무대에서의 마지막—십중팔구—마지막 장면일 터였다. 그러나 이보다 더 멋진 장면은 없으리라고 그는 확신했다."], ["The house would close with the greatest eclat.", "극장은 더없는 요란함 속에 문을 닫게 될 것이었다."]], [["There was little time, however, for the indulgence of any images of merriment.", "그러나 어떤 유쾌한 상상에 잠겨 있을 겨를은 거의 없었다."], ["It was necessary for him to step forward, too, and assist the introduction, and with many awkward sensations he did his best.", "그도 앞으로 나서서 소개를 거들어야 했고, 온갖 어색한 심경으로 그는 최선을 다했다."], ["Sir Thomas received Mr. Yates with all the appearance of cordiality which was due to his own character, but was really as far from pleased with the necessity of the acquaintance as with the manner of its commencement.", "토머스 경은 제 사람됨에 걸맞은 온갖 다정한 겉모습으로 예이츠 씨를 맞았으나, 이 사귐이 필요하다는 것도, 그것이 시작된 방식도 실은 조금도 반갑지 않았다."], ["Mr. Yates’s family and connexions were sufficiently known to him to render his introduction as the “particular friend,”", "예이츠 씨의 집안과 인연은 그가 익히 아는 바라, 아들의 그 백 명이나 되는 ‘각별한 벗’ 가운데 또 하나로 그를 ‘각별한 벗’이라며 소개하는 것이 여간 달갑지 않았다."], ["another of the hundred particular friends of his son, exceedingly unwelcome; and it needed all the felicity of being again at home, and all the forbearance it could supply, to save Sir Thomas from anger on finding himself thus bewildered in his own house, making part of a ridiculous exhibition in the midst of theatrical nonsense, and forced in so untoward a moment to admit the acquaintance of a young man whom he felt sure of disapproving, and whose easy indifference and volubility in the course of the first five minutes seemed to mark him the most at home of the two.", "그리고 제집에서 이렇게 어리둥절해진 채, 연극이라는 시답잖은 것 한복판에서 우스꽝스러운 구경거리의 한 부분이 되고, 그토록 고약한 순간에 못마땅해할 게 뻔한 젊은이의 사귐을 받아들이도록 내몰린 것에 화를 내지 않으려면—게다가 처음 오 분 새 그 젊은이의 태연한 무심함과 청산유수 같은 말솜씨는 마치 그가 둘 중 더 제집처럼 편안한 사람인 양 보이게 했으니—다시 집에 있다는 그 온갖 행복과, 그것이 안겨 줄 수 있는 온갖 인내가 죄다 필요했다."]], [["Tom understood his father’s thoughts, and heartily wishing he might be always as well disposed to give them but partial expression, began to see, more clearly than he had ever done before, that there might be some ground of offence, that there might be some reason for the glance his father gave towards the ceiling and stucco of the room; and that when he inquired with mild gravity after the fate of the billiard-table, he was not proceeding beyond a very allowable curiosity.", "톰은 아버지의 속내를 알아챘고, 아버지가 늘 이렇듯 그것을 어지간히만 내비칠 마음이기를 진심으로 바라며, 여태 그 어느 때보다 또렷이 깨닫기 시작했다. 성이 날 만한 구석이 얼마쯤 있을 수 있음을, 아버지가 방의 천장과 회벽 장식 쪽으로 던진 눈길에 얼마쯤 까닭이 있을 수 있음을, 그리고 아버지가 당구대의 운명을 나직하고 근엄하게 물었을 때 그것이 결코 얼마든지 봐줄 만한 궁금함을 넘어선 게 아님을 말이다."], ["A few minutes were enough for such unsatisfactory sensations on each side; and Sir Thomas having exerted himself so far as to speak a few words of calm approbation in reply to an eager appeal of Mr.", "이런 언짢은 심경이 양쪽에 오가는 데는 몇 분이면 넉넉했다."], ["Yates, as to the happiness of the arrangement, the three gentlemen returned to the drawing-room together, Sir Thomas with an increase of gravity which was not lost on all.", "그리고 토머스 경이, 이 마련이 얼마나 흡족하냐는 예이츠 씨의 들뜬 호소에 답해 차분한 인정의 말을 몇 마디 건네는 데까지 애쓴 뒤, 세 신사는 함께 응접실로 돌아왔으니, 토머스 경은 한층 더 근엄해진 채였고 그것은 모두의 눈에 띄지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“I come from your theatre,”", "“자네 극장에 다녀왔네.”"], ["said he composedly, as he sat down; “I found myself in it rather unexpectedly.", "그가 자리에 앉으며 차분히 말했다. “거기 있는 나 자신을 뜻밖에도 발견했지 뭔가."], ["Its vicinity to my own room—but in every respect, indeed, it took me by surprise, as I had not the smallest suspicion of your acting having assumed so serious a character.", "내 방과 그토록 가까이 붙어 있으니—하기야 어느 모로 보나 정말이지 나를 깜짝 놀라게 했네, 자네들 연기가 그토록 진지한 꼴을 갖췄으리라곤 손톱만큼도 짐작 못 했으니."], ["It appears a neat job, however, as far as I could judge by candlelight, and does my friend Christopher Jackson credit.”", "그래도 촛불 아래 내가 판단할 수 있는 한, 말끔한 솜씨더군. 내 벗 크리스토퍼 잭슨의 이름값을 하는 일이고.”"], ["And then he would have changed the subject, and sipped his coffee in peace over domestic matters of a calmer hue; but Mr.", "그러고서 그는 화제를 돌려, 좀 더 차분한 빛깔의 집안일을 두고 편안히 커피를 홀짝이려 했다."], ["Yates, without discernment to catch Sir Thomas’s meaning, or diffidence, or delicacy, or discretion enough to allow him to lead the discourse while he mingled among the others with the least obtrusiveness himself, would keep him on the topic of the theatre, would torment him with questions and remarks relative to it, and finally would make him hear the whole history of his disappointment at Ecclesford.", "그러나 예이츠 씨는 토머스 경의 속뜻을 알아챌 눈치도, 저 자신은 되도록 나서지 않고 다른 이들 틈에 섞이면서 그가 이야기를 이끌게 놔둘 만한 삼감이나 조심이나 분별도 없던지라, 그를 자꾸만 극장 이야기에 붙들어 두고, 그것에 얽힌 물음과 소감으로 그를 들볶다가, 끝내는 에클스포드에서 겪은 제 낭패의 온 사연을 그에게 다 들려주고야 말았다."], ["Sir Thomas listened most politely, but found much to offend his ideas of decorum, and confirm his ill-opinion of Mr.", "토머스 경은 더없이 정중하게 귀 기울였으나, 그 이야기의 처음부터 끝까지, 제 예의 관념을 거스르고 예이츠 씨의 사고방식에 대한 제 나쁜 인상을 굳히는 대목이 숱하게 있음을 알았다."], ["Yates’s habits of thinking, from the beginning to the end of the story; and when it was over, could give him no other assurance of sympathy than what a slight bow conveyed.", "그리고 그것이 끝났을 때, 그가 건넬 수 있는 공감의 표시란 가벼운 목례가 전하는 것 말고는 없었다."]], [["“This was, in fact, the origin of our acting,”", "“이게 사실 저희 연기의 발단이었어요.”"], ["said Tom, after a moment’s thought.", "톰이 잠깐 생각한 뒤 말했다."], ["“My friend Yates brought the infection from Ecclesford, and it spread—as those things always spread, you know, sir—the faster, probably, from your having so often encouraged the sort of thing in us formerly.", "“제 벗 예이츠가 에클스포드에서 그 열병을 옮겨 왔고, 그게 번진 거죠—그런 건 늘 그렇게 번지잖아요, 아버지—아마 아버지께서 예전에 저희한테 그런 걸 그토록 자주 부추기셨던 탓에 더 빨리 번졌을 테고요."], ["It was like treading old ground again.”", "마치 옛 땅을 다시 밟는 것 같았거든요.”"]], [["Mr. Yates took the subject from his friend as soon as possible, and immediately gave Sir Thomas an account of what they had done and were doing: told him of the gradual increase of their views, the happy conclusion of their first difficulties, and present promising state of affairs; relating everything with so blind an interest as made him not only totally unconscious of the uneasy movements of many of his friends as they sat, the change of countenance, the fidget, the hem!", "예이츠 씨는 되도록 빨리 벗에게서 그 화제를 넘겨받아, 저희가 무엇을 했고 무엇을 하고 있는지 곧바로 토머스 경에게 늘어놓았다."], ["of unquietness, but prevented him even from seeing the expression of the face on which his own eyes were fixed—from seeing Sir Thomas’s dark brow contract as he looked with inquiring earnestness at his daughters and Edmund, dwelling particularly on the latter, and speaking a language, a remonstrance, a reproof, which he felt at his heart.", "저희 구상이 차츰 커진 것이며, 처음의 어려움이 흐뭇하게 매듭지어진 것이며, 지금 일이 잘 풀려 가는 형편을 이야기하는데, 어찌나 눈먼 흥에 겨웠던지, 앉아 있던 제 벗 여럿의 불편한 몸놀림이며 달라진 낯빛이며 안절부절못하는 몸짓이며 편찮은 헛기침을 도무지 알아채지 못했을 뿐 아니라, 정작 제 눈이 박힌 그 얼굴의 표정마저—토머스 경이 제 딸들과 에드먼드를, 특히 에드먼드를 캐묻듯 진지하게 바라보며 그 검은 눈썹을 찌푸리는 것을, 그리고 에드먼드가 가슴으로 절감한 어떤 말을, 어떤 간언을, 어떤 나무람을 건네는 것을—보지 못하게 했다."], ["Not less acutely was it felt by Fanny, who had edged back her chair behind her aunt’s end of the sofa, and, screened from notice herself, saw all that was passing before her.", "그것을 못지않게 예리하게 느낀 것은 패니였으니, 그녀는 이모가 앉은 소파 끄트머리 뒤로 제 의자를 물려, 저 자신은 눈에 안 띄게 가린 채, 눈앞에 벌어지는 그 모든 것을 보고 있었다."], ["Such a look of reproach at Edmund from his father she could never have expected to witness; and to feel that it was in any degree deserved was an aggravation indeed.", "아버지가 에드먼드에게 던지는 그런 나무람의 눈빛을 그녀는 결코 지켜보게 되리라 예상치 못했을 것이다. 그리고 그것이 얼마쯤이라도 마땅한 것임을 느끼는 것은 정말이지 더한 괴로움이었다."], ["Sir Thomas’s look implied, “On your judgment, Edmund, I depended; what have you been about?”", "토머스 경의 눈빛은 이렇게 말하고 있었다. “에드먼드야, 나는 네 판단을 믿었다."], ["She knelt in spirit to her uncle, and her bosom swelled to utter, “Oh, not to him!", "대체 무슨 일을 벌인 게냐?” 그녀는 마음속으로 삼촌 앞에 무릎 꿇었고, 이렇게 외치고 싶어 가슴이 부풀었다."], ["Look so to all the others, but not to him!”", "“아, 그이한텐 안 돼요! 다른 모두한텐 그리 보셔도, 그이한텐 안 돼요!”"]], [["Mr. Yates was still talking.", "예이츠 씨는 여전히 이야기하고 있었다."], ["“To own the truth, Sir Thomas, we were in the middle of a rehearsal when you arrived this evening.", "“사실대로 말씀드리면, 토머스 경, 오늘 저녁 오셨을 때 저희는 연습 한복판이었어요."], ["We were going through the three first acts, and not unsuccessfully upon the whole.", "첫 세 막을 죽 해 나가던 참이었는데, 대체로 그리 나쁘지 않았지요."], ["Our company is now so dispersed, from the Crawfords being gone home, that nothing more can be done to-night; but if you will give us the honour of your company to-morrow evening, I should not be afraid of the result.", "이제 크로퍼드 남매가 집으로 가 버려 저희 무리가 흩어진 터라 오늘 밤은 더 못 하겠지만, 내일 저녁 왕림해 주시는 영광을 베풀어 주신다면, 저는 그 결과가 겁나지 않아요."], ["We bespeak your indulgence, you understand, as young performers; we bespeak your indulgence.”", "저희야 풋내기 연기자들이니, 부디 너그러이 봐주십사 청합니다, 아시겠지요. 부디 너그러이 봐주십사 청해요.”"]], [["“My indulgence shall be given, sir,”", "“너그러이 봐주겠네.”"], ["replied Sir Thomas gravely, “but without any other rehearsal.”", "토머스 경이 근엄하게 대답했다. “하지만 더 연습은 없이 말일세.”"], ["And with a relenting smile, he added, “I come home to be happy and indulgent.”", "그러고는 누그러지는 미소로 덧붙였다. “나는 행복하고 너그럽게 지내려고 집에 온 거라네.”"], ["Then turning away towards any or all of the rest, he tranquilly said, “Mr.", "그런 다음 나머지 사람들 아무에게나, 아니 다 함께 향해 돌아서며, 그는 차분히 말했다."], ["and Miss Crawford were mentioned in my last letters from Mansfield.", "“맨스필드에서 온 지난 편지들에 크로퍼드 남매 이야기가 있더구나."], ["Do you find them agreeable acquaintance?”", "너희 보기에 사귈 만한 이들이더냐?”"]], [["Tom was the only one at all ready with an answer, but he being entirely without particular regard for either, without jealousy either in love or acting, could speak very handsomely of both.", "냉큼 대답할 이는 톰뿐이었으나, 그는 그 남매 어느 쪽에도 딱히 각별한 마음이 없고, 사랑에서든 연기에서든 시샘이 없던지라, 둘 다에 대해 아주 번듯하게 말할 수 있었다."], ["“Mr. Crawford was a most pleasant, gentleman-like man; his sister a sweet, pretty, elegant, lively girl.”", "“크로퍼드 씨는 더없이 유쾌하고 신사다운 사람이고, 그 누이는 사랑스럽고 예쁘고 우아하고 발랄한 아가씨입니다.”"]], [["Mr. Rushworth could be silent no longer.", "러시워스 씨는 더는 잠자코 있을 수 없었다."], ["“I do not say he is not gentleman-like, considering; but you should tell your father he is not above five feet eight, or he will be expecting a well-looking man.”", "“뭐, 그 사람이 처지를 봐서 신사답지 않다는 건 아니지만, 아버님께 말씀드리는 게 좋겠어요, 그 사람 키가 오 피트 팔 인치도 안 된다고요, 안 그러면 아버님이 훤칠한 사내를 기대하실 테니까요.”"]], [["Sir Thomas did not quite understand this, and looked with some surprise at the speaker.", "토머스 경은 이 말을 잘 알아듣지 못하고, 다소 놀란 눈으로 말한 이를 바라보았다."]], [["“If I must say what I think,” continued Mr.", "“제 생각을 굳이 말씀드려야 한다면,”"], ["Rushworth, “in my opinion it is very disagreeable to be always rehearsing.", "러시워스 씨가 말을 이었다. “제 소견으론 늘 연습만 하고 있는 게 아주 언짢은 노릇이에요."], ["It is having too much of a good thing.", "좋은 것도 너무 지나치니까요."], ["I am not so fond of acting as I was at first.", "저는 처음만큼 연기가 좋지가 않아요."], ["I think we are a great deal better employed, sitting comfortably here among ourselves, and doing nothing.”", "이렇게 우리끼리 편안히 앉아 아무것도 안 하는 게 훨씬 나은 소일이라고 봐요.”"]], [["Sir Thomas looked again, and then replied with an approving smile, “I am happy to find our sentiments on this subject so much the same.", "토머스 경은 다시 그를 바라보더니, 흡족한 미소로 대답했다. “이 일에서 우리 생각이 그토록 같다니 기쁘네."], ["It gives me sincere satisfaction.", "참으로 흐뭇한 일이야."], ["That I should be cautious and quick-sighted, and feel many scruples which my children do not feel, is perfectly natural; and equally so that my value for domestic tranquillity, for a home which shuts out noisy pleasures, should much exceed theirs.", "내가 조심스럽고 눈이 밝아, 내 자식들이 못 느끼는 여러 거리낌을 느끼는 것이야 지극히 당연한 노릇이지. 마찬가지로 집안의 평온을, 시끄러운 즐거움을 물리치는 가정을 아끼는 내 마음이 그 애들 것보다 훨씬 큰 것도 당연하고."], ["But at your time of life to feel all this, is a most favourable circumstance for yourself, and for everybody connected with you; and I am sensible of the importance of having an ally of such weight.”", "하지만 자네 나이에 이 모든 걸 느낀다는 건 자네 자신에게도, 자네와 얽힌 모든 이에게도 더없이 좋은 일이야. 그리고 나는 이토록 무게 있는 한편을 얻는 것이 얼마나 중요한지 잘 안다네.”"]], [["Sir Thomas meant to be giving Mr. Rushworth’s opinion in better words than he could find himself.", "토머스 경은 러시워스 씨의 의견을 그 자신이 찾아낼 수 있는 것보다 더 나은 말로 대신 표현해 주려던 것이었다."], ["He was aware that he must not expect a genius in Mr.", "그는 러시워스 씨에게서 천재를 기대해선 안 됨을 알고 있었다."], ["Rushworth; but as a well-judging, steady young man, with better notions than his elocution would do justice to, he intended to value him very highly.", "그러나 판단이 바르고 진득한 젊은이로서, 제 말솜씨가 다 담아내지 못할 만큼 좋은 생각을 지녔다 여겨, 그를 아주 높이 사려는 마음이었다."], ["It was impossible for many of the others not to smile. Mr.", "다른 여럿은 웃음을 참기 어려웠다."], ["Rushworth hardly knew what to do with so much meaning; but by looking, as he really felt, most exceedingly pleased with Sir Thomas’s good opinion, and saying scarcely anything, he did his best towards preserving that good opinion a little longer.", "러시워스 씨는 그토록 많은 속뜻을 어찌해야 좋을지 잘 몰랐다. 그러나 토머스 경의 좋은 평가에 정말로 느낀 그대로 더없이 흡족해하는 낯빛을 하고, 거의 아무 말도 안 함으로써, 그는 그 좋은 평가를 조금이라도 더 오래 지키려 제 딴엔 최선을 다했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XX", "# 제20장"]], [["Edmund’s first object the next morning was to see his father alone, and give him a fair statement of the whole acting scheme, defending his own share in it as far only as he could then, in a soberer moment, feel his motives to deserve, and acknowledging, with perfect ingenuousness, that his concession had been attended with such partial good as to make his judgment in it very doubtful.", "이튿날 아침 에드먼드의 첫째 목표는 아버지를 단둘이 뵙고, 그 연극 소동 전부를 낱낱이 정직하게 아뢰는 것이었다. 자기가 거기 낀 몫은, 그때 좀 더 냉정해진 순간에 제 동기가 그럴 만하다 느껴지는 데까지만 두둔하고, 제 양보가 하도 어중간한 좋은 결과만 가져온 탓에 그 판단이 자못 미심쩍어졌음을 더없이 솔직하게 인정했다."], ["He was anxious, while vindicating himself, to say nothing unkind of the others: but there was only one amongst them whose conduct he could mention without some necessity of defence or palliation.", "그는 저 자신을 변호하면서도 남들에 대해서는 매정한 말을 하지 않으려 애썼다. 그러나 그들 가운데 얼마간의 변호나 두둔 없이 그 처신을 입에 올릴 수 있는 이는 오직 한 사람뿐이었다."], ["“We have all been more or less to blame,”", "“저희 모두가 어느 정도는 잘못했어요.”"], ["said he, “every one of us, excepting Fanny.", "그가 말했다. “패니만 빼고 저희 하나하나가요."], ["Fanny is the only one who has judged rightly throughout; who has been consistent.", "패니야말로 처음부터 끝까지 옳게 판단한 유일한 사람이고, 한결같았던 유일한 사람이에요."], ["Her feelings have been steadily against it from first to last.", "그 애 마음은 처음부터 끝까지 줄곧 이 일에 반대였어요."], ["She never ceased to think of what was due to you.", "그 앤 아버지께 마땅히 지켜야 할 도리를 한시도 잊지 않았고요."], ["You will find Fanny everything you could wish.”", "아버지께서 바라실 만한 모든 것을 패니에게서 보실 거예요.”"]], [["Sir Thomas saw all the impropriety of such a scheme among such a party, and at such a time, as strongly as his son had ever supposed he must; he felt it too much, indeed, for many words; and having shaken hands with Edmund, meant to try to lose the disagreeable impression, and forget how much he had been forgotten himself as soon as he could, after the house had been cleared of every object enforcing the remembrance, and restored to its proper state.", "토머스 경은 그런 무리 사이에서, 그런 때에 벌어진 그런 계획의 온갖 온당찮음을, 그 아들이 여태 그러리라 짐작한 그대로 절실히 알아보았다. 정말이지 그는 그것을 여러 말로 옮기기엔 너무 크게 느꼈다."], ["He did not enter into any remonstrance with his other children: he was more willing to believe they felt their error than to run the risk of investigation.", "그리하여 에드먼드와 악수를 나눈 뒤, 그 언짢은 인상을 떨쳐 버리고, 저 자신이 얼마나 잊혔던가를 되도록 빨리 잊으려 마음먹었다—그 기억을 강요하는 온갖 것을 집에서 치워 없애고 집을 제 마땅한 꼴로 되돌린 다음에 말이다. 그는 다른 자식들과는 어떤 언쟁도 벌이지 않았다."], ["The reproof of an immediate conclusion of everything, the sweep of every preparation, would be sufficient.", "캐물어 들어가는 위험을 무릅쓰기보다는, 그들이 제 잘못을 느끼고 있으리라 믿는 편을 택했다. 모든 것을 당장 끝맺고 온갖 채비를 싹 쓸어 없애는 그 나무람이면 충분할 터였다."]], [["There was one person, however, in the house, whom he could not leave to learn his sentiments merely through his conduct.", "그러나 집 안에는, 그저 제 처신을 통해서만 그의 심경을 알게 두어서는 안 될 사람이 하나 있었다. 그는 노리스 부인에게, 그녀의 판단이라면 분명 못마땅해했을 그 일을 막는 데 그녀의 조언이 끼어들었기를 바랐노라고 넌지시 이르지 않을 수 없었다."], ["He could not help giving Mrs.", "젊은것들이야 그 계획을 세우면서 아주 경솔했다."], ["Norris a hint of his having hoped that her advice might have been interposed to prevent what her judgment must certainly have disapproved.", "저희 스스로 더 나은 판단을 내릴 줄 알았어야 마땅했지만, 어리기도 했고, 에드먼드만 빼면 그가 보기에 진득하지 못한 성품들이었다."], ["The young people had been very inconsiderate in forming the plan; they ought to have been capable of a better decision themselves; but they were young; and, excepting Edmund, he believed, of unsteady characters; and with greater surprise, therefore, he must regard her acquiescence in their wrong measures, her countenance of their unsafe amusements, than that such measures and such amusements should have been suggested. Mrs.", "그러니 그런 방책과 그런 놀이가 내놓아졌다는 것보다, 그녀가 그들의 그릇된 방책을 순순히 받아들이고 그 위태로운 놀이를 편들어 준 것을 그는 한층 더 놀랍게 여길 수밖에 없었다."], ["Norris was a little confounded and as nearly being silenced as ever she had been in her life; for she was ashamed to confess having never seen any of the impropriety which was so glaring to Sir Thomas, and would not have admitted that her influence was insufficient—that she might have talked in vain.", "노리스 부인은 조금 당황했고, 평생 그 어느 때만큼이나 말문이 막힐 뻔했다. 토머스 경에게는 그토록 훤히 드러나던 그 온갖 온당찮음을 저는 하나도 못 봤노라 고백하기가 부끄러웠고, 제 영향력이 모자랐다고—제 말이 헛일이었을지 모른다고—인정하려 들지도 않았기 때문이다."], ["Her only resource was to get out of the subject as fast as possible, and turn the current of Sir Thomas’s ideas into a happier channel.", "그녀의 유일한 방도는 되도록 빨리 그 화제에서 빠져나와, 토머스 경의 생각의 물길을 좀 더 반가운 쪽으로 돌리는 것이었다."], ["She had a great deal to insinuate in her own praise as to general attention to the interest and comfort of his family, much exertion and many sacrifices to glance at in the form of hurried walks and sudden removals from her own fireside, and many excellent hints of distrust and economy to Lady Bertram and Edmund to detail, whereby a most considerable saving had always arisen, and more than one bad servant been detected.", "그녀는 그의 집안의 이익과 안락에 두루 마음 쓴 것을 두고 제 자랑을 넌지시 늘어놓을 거리가 잔뜩 있었고, 부랴부랴 걸어 다니고 제 난롯가를 훌쩍 떠나온 꼴로 내비칠 온갖 수고와 희생이 많았으며, 버트럼 부인과 에드먼드에게 건넨, 사람을 못 미더워하고 알뜰히 굴라는 훌륭한 언질도 낱낱이 늘어놓을 게 많았으니, 그 덕에 늘 여간 큰 절약이 이뤄졌고 몹쓸 하인도 한둘 넘게 들통났다는 것이었다."], ["But her chief strength lay in Sotherton.", "그러나 그녀의 가장 큰 힘은 소더턴에 있었다."], ["Her greatest support and glory was in having formed the connexion with the Rushworths.", "그녀의 가장 큰 버팀목이자 자랑은 러시워스가와 인연을 맺어 준 데 있었다."], ["There she was impregnable.", "거기서만은 그녀가 난공불락이었다."], ["She took to herself all the credit of bringing Mr. Rushworth’s admiration of Maria to any effect.", "러시워스 씨가 마리아에게 품은 흠모를 결실로 이끈 공을 그녀는 죄다 제 것으로 삼았다."], ["“If I had not been active,”", "“내가 발 벗고 나서지 않았더라면,”"], ["said she, “and made a point of being introduced to his mother, and then prevailed on my sister to pay the first visit, I am as certain as I sit here that nothing would have come of it; for Mr.", "그녀가 말했다. “기어이 그이 어머니한테 소개받고, 그런 다음 내 아우를 구슬려 먼저 방문하게 만들지 않았더라면, 여기 이렇게 앉아 있는 게 확실한 만큼이나 확실히, 이 일은 아무것도 안 됐을 거예요."], ["Rushworth is the sort of amiable modest young man who wants a great deal of encouragement, and there were girls enough on the catch for him if we had been idle.", "러시워스 씨야 크게 부추겨 줘야 하는 그런 상냥하고 수줍은 젊은이인 데다, 우리가 손 놓고 있었더라면 그이를 낚아채려는 계집애가 얼마든지 있었으니까요."], ["But I left no stone unturned.", "하지만 나는 온갖 수를 다 썼어요."], ["I was ready to move heaven and earth to persuade my sister, and at last I did persuade her.", "내 아우를 설득하겠다고 하늘과 땅이라도 움직일 태세였고, 마침내 정말 설득해 냈지요."], ["You know the distance to Sotherton; it was in the middle of winter, and the roads almost impassable, but I did persuade her.”", "소더턴까지 거리가 얼마나 되는지 아시잖아요. 한겨울인 데다 길은 거의 못 다닐 지경이었지만, 나는 기어이 아우를 설득했다니까요.”"]], [["“I know how great, how justly great, your influence is with Lady Bertram and her children, and am the more concerned that it should not have been—”", "“부인이 버트럼 부인과 그 아이들에게 미치는 힘이 얼마나 큰지, 얼마나 마땅히 큰지 나는 잘 아오. 그래서 더욱 안타깝구려, 그 힘이 그만—”"]], [["“My dear Sir Thomas, if you had seen the state of the roads that day!", "“아이참, 토머스 경, 그날 길 꼴을 보셨어야 하는데!"], ["I thought we should never have got through them, though we had the four horses of course; and poor old coachman would attend us, out of his great love and kindness, though he was hardly able to sit the box on account of the rheumatism which I had been doctoring him for ever since Michaelmas.", "물론 말은 네 마리를 매었는데도, 나는 우리가 도무지 그 길을 못 빠져나갈 줄 알았어요. 게다가 딱한 늙은 마부가, 미가엘 축일 무렵부터 내가 줄곧 약을 지어 봐준 그 류머티즘 때문에 마부석에 앉아 있기도 힘든 지경인데도, 그 크나큰 정과 친절로 기어이 우리를 모시겠다지 뭐예요."], ["I cured him at last; but he was very bad all the winter—and this was such a day, I could not help going to him up in his room before we set off to advise him not to venture: he was putting on his wig; so I said, ‘Coachman, you had much better not go; your Lady and I shall be very safe; you know how steady Stephen is, and Charles has been upon the leaders so often now, that I am sure there is no fear.’ But, however, I soon found it would not do; he was bent upon going, and as I hate to be worrying and officious, I said no more; but my heart quite ached for him at every jolt, and when we got into the rough lanes about Stoke, where, what with frost and snow upon beds of stones, it was worse than anything you can imagine, I was quite in an agony about him.", "나야 끝내 그 사람을 고쳐 주긴 했지만, 겨우내 아주 심했거든요—그런데 하필 그런 날이라, 나는 떠나기 전에 그 사람 방으로 올라가 나서지 말라고 일러 주지 않을 수가 없었어요. 마침 가발을 쓰고 있기에, 내가 그랬죠."], ["And then the poor horses too!", "‘마부, 자넨 안 가는 게 훨씬 낫겠네."], ["To see them straining away!", "마님하고 나는 아주 무사할 테니."], ["You know how I always feel for the horses.", "스티븐이 얼마나 진득한지 자네도 알잖나. 그리고 찰스도 이젠 선두 말을 하도 자주 몰아 봐서, 아무 걱정 없을 게 분명하네.’ 하지만 이내 그게 안 되겠다는 걸 알았지요."], ["And when we got to the bottom of Sandcroft Hill, what do you think I did?", "그 사람은 갈 작정으로 단단히 벼르고 있었고, 나야 남을 들볶고 참견하는 게 질색이라, 더는 아무 말도 안 했어요. 그래도 덜컹거릴 때마다 그 사람 생각에 내 가슴이 아주 저렸고, 스토크 근처 험한 샛길에 들어서니, 돌바닥 위에 서리며 눈이 쌓여 상상도 못 할 만큼 고약했는데, 나는 그 사람 걱정에 아주 애가 탔지요."], ["You will laugh at me; but I got out and walked up.", "게다가 딱한 말들은 또 어떻고요! 그놈들이 안간힘을 쓰며 끄는 걸 보다니!"], ["I did indeed.", "내가 늘 말들을 얼마나 딱해하는지 아시잖아요."], ["It might not be saving them much, but it was something, and I could not bear to sit at my ease and be dragged up at the expense of those noble animals.", "그리고 샌드크로프트 언덕 아래에 이르렀을 때, 내가 어쨌게요? 나를 비웃으시겠지만, 나는 내려서 걸어 올라갔어요."], ["I caught a dreadful cold, but that I did not regard.", "정말 그랬다니까요. 그놈들한테 큰 덜음은 안 됐을지 몰라도, 그래도 얼마간은 됐고, 나는 그 갸륵한 짐승들을 부려 편히 앉아 끌려 올라가는 걸 도무지 견딜 수가 없었거든요."], ["My object was accomplished in the visit.”", "나는 지독한 감기에 걸렸지만, 그거야 개의치 않았어요. 그 방문에서 내 목적은 이뤄졌으니까요.”"]], [["“I hope we shall always think the acquaintance worth any trouble that might be taken to establish it. There is nothing very striking in Mr.", "“그 인연을 맺느라 든 어떤 수고에도 우리가 늘 그만한 값어치가 있었다 여기게 되기를 바라오."], ["Rushworth’s manners, but I was pleased last night with what appeared to be his opinion on one subject: his decided preference of a quiet family party to the bustle and confusion of acting.", "러시워스 씨의 태도에 딱히 눈에 띄는 데야 없지만, 나는 어젯밤 그가 한 가지 일에 대해 내비친 듯한 의견이 마음에 들었소. 연기의 소란과 어수선함보다 조용한 가족 모임을 뚜렷이 더 좋아한다는 것 말이오."], ["He seemed to feel exactly as one could wish.”", "바로 바랄 만한 그대로 느끼는 듯하더군.”"]], [["“Yes, indeed, and the more you know of him the better you will like him.", "“그럼요, 정말이지, 그이를 알면 알수록 더 좋아하시게 될 거예요."], ["He is not a shining character, but he has a thousand good qualities; and is so disposed to look up to you, that I am quite laughed at about it, for everybody considers it as my doing.", "빛나는 사람은 못 되지만, 좋은 자질을 천 가지나 지녔고, 게다가 경을 어찌나 우러러보는지, 다들 그걸 내가 한 일이라 여기는 바람에 나는 아주 놀림을 받는답니다."], ["‘Upon my word, Mrs. Norris,’ said Mrs.", "며칠 전에도 그랜트 부인이 그랬어요."], ["Grant the other day, ‘if Mr. Rushworth were a son of your own, he could not hold Sir Thomas in greater respect.’”", "‘정말이지, 노리스 부인, 러시워스 씨가 부인 친아들이라 한들 토머스 경을 이보다 더 우러러볼 수는 없겠어요.’”"]], [["Sir Thomas gave up the point, foiled by her evasions, disarmed by her flattery; and was obliged to rest satisfied with the conviction that where the present pleasure of those she loved was at stake, her kindness did sometimes overpower her judgment.", "토머스 경은 그 발뺌에 밀리고 그 아첨에 무장을 빼앗겨 그 대목을 접었다. 그리고 그녀가 사랑하는 이들의 당장의 즐거움이 걸린 자리에서는, 그 다정함이 때로 그 판단을 눌러 버리곤 한다는 확신으로 만족하는 수밖에 없었다."]], [["It was a busy morning with him.", "그에게는 분주한 아침이었다."], ["Conversation with any of them occupied but a small part of it.", "그들 누구와 이야기하는 것은 그 아침의 자그마한 한 토막을 차지했을 뿐이다. 그는 제 맨스필드 생활의 그 익은 일들에 죄다 다시 몸담아야 했다."], ["He had to reinstate himself in all the wonted concerns of his Mansfield life: to see his steward and his bailiff; to examine and compute, and, in the intervals of business, to walk into his stables and his gardens, and nearest plantations; but active and methodical, he had not only done all this before he resumed his seat as master of the house at dinner, he had also set the carpenter to work in pulling down what had been so lately put up in the billiard-room, and given the scene-painter his dismissal long enough to justify the pleasing belief of his being then at least as far off as Northampton.", "집사와 마름을 만나고, 살피고 셈하고, 일하는 짬짬이 마구간과 정원, 가장 가까운 농원으로 걸어 나가야 했다. 그러나 부지런하고 빈틈없던지라, 그는 만찬 자리에서 집주인으로 도로 앉기 전에 이 모든 것을 다 했을 뿐 아니라, 목수를 시켜 얼마 전 당구실에 세운 것을 헐게 하고, 무대 화가에게는 이제 적어도 노샘프턴만큼은 멀리 가 있으리라는 흐뭇한 믿음이 정당할 만큼 진작 물러가라 했다."], ["The scene-painter was gone, having spoilt only the floor of one room, ruined all the coachman’s sponges, and made five of the under-servants idle and dissatisfied; and Sir Thomas was in hopes that another day or two would suffice to wipe away every outward memento of what had been, even to the destruction of every unbound copy of Lovers’ Vows in the house, for he was burning all that met his eye.", "무대 화가는 떠났으니, 방 하나의 바닥만 망쳐 놓고, 마부의 해면을 죄다 못 쓰게 만들고, 아랫사람 다섯을 게으르고 불만스럽게 만들어 놓은 채였다. 그리고 토머스 경은 하루 이틀이면 지난 일의 온갖 겉 흔적을 다 지워 없애기에 넉넉하리라 바랐으니, 집 안에 있는 「연인들의 맹세」의 제본 안 된 낱권까지 죄다 없애기에 이르러—그는 눈에 띄는 것을 죄다 태우고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["Mr. Yates was beginning now to understand Sir Thomas’s intentions, though as far as ever from understanding their source.", "예이츠 씨는 이제 토머스 경의 뜻을 알아차리기 시작했으나, 그 뿌리를 헤아리기는 여전히 까마득했다."], ["He and his friend had been out with their guns the chief of the morning, and Tom had taken the opportunity of explaining, with proper apologies for his father’s particularity, what was to be expected.", "그와 그 벗은 아침의 대부분을 총을 들고 밖에 나가 있었고, 톰은 그 기회를 잡아, 아버지의 별난 성미에 대해 마땅한 사과를 곁들여, 앞으로 어찌 될지를 일러 주었다. 예이츠 씨는 짐작할 만한 그대로 그것을 절실히 느꼈다."], ["Mr. Yates felt it as acutely as might be supposed.", "같은 식으로 두 번째 낭패를 보는 것은 여간 혹독한 불운의 사례가 아니었다."], ["To be a second time disappointed in the same way was an instance of very severe ill-luck; and his indignation was such, that had it not been for delicacy towards his friend, and his friend’s youngest sister, he believed he should certainly attack the baronet on the absurdity of his proceedings, and argue him into a little more rationality.", "그리고 그 분함이 어찌나 컸던지, 제 벗과 그 벗의 막내 누이에 대한 조심만 아니었더라면, 그는 분명 그 준남작의 처사가 얼마나 어처구니없는지를 두고 그에게 대들어, 좀 더 사리에 맞게 굴도록 따져 댔으리라 믿었다. 그는 맨스필드 숲에 있는 동안, 그리고 집으로 오는 내내 이것을 아주 굳게 믿었다."], ["He believed this very stoutly while he was in Mansfield Wood, and all the way home; but there was a something in Sir Thomas, when they sat round the same table, which made Mr. Yates think it wiser to let him pursue his own way, and feel the folly of it without opposition.", "그러나 다 함께 한 식탁에 둘러앉으니, 토머스 경에게는 무언가가 있어, 예이츠 씨로 하여금 그가 제 뜻대로 하도록 두고, 대들지 않고 그 어리석음을 스스로 깨닫게 하는 편이 더 슬기롭겠다 여기게 했다."], ["He had known many disagreeable fathers before, and often been struck with the inconveniences they occasioned, but never, in the whole course of his life, had he seen one of that class so unintelligibly moral, so infamously tyrannical as Sir Thomas.", "그는 전에도 언짢은 아버지를 숱하게 봐 왔고, 그들이 안기는 불편에 곧잘 놀라기도 했으나, 평생 통틀어 토머스 경만큼 그토록 알 수 없이 도덕적이고, 그토록 지독하게 폭군 같은 그런 부류의 아버지는 본 적이 없었다."], ["He was not a man to be endured but for his children’s sake, and he might be thankful to his fair daughter Julia that Mr. Yates did yet mean to stay a few days longer under his roof.", "그는 오직 그 자식들을 봐서나 견딜 만한 사람이었고, 예이츠 씨가 그래도 며칠 더 그 지붕 아래 묵을 작정인 것은 그 어여쁜 딸 줄리아 덕이라 여겨도 좋을 만했다."]], [["The evening passed with external smoothness, though almost every mind was ruffled; and the music which Sir Thomas called for from his daughters helped to conceal the want of real harmony.", "저녁은 겉으로는 매끄럽게 흘러갔으나, 거의 모든 마음이 헝클어져 있었다. 그리고 토머스 경이 딸들에게 청한 음악이 참된 화합의 부재를 감추는 데 한몫했다."], ["Maria was in a good deal of agitation.", "마리아는 자못 동요하고 있었다."], ["It was of the utmost consequence to her that Crawford should now lose no time in declaring himself, and she was disturbed that even a day should be gone by without seeming to advance that point.", "크로퍼드가 이제 지체 없이 마음을 밝히는 것이 그녀에게는 더없이 중대한 일이었고, 그 대목을 앞당기는 기미도 없이 하루가 그냥 지나갔다는 것이 그녀를 뒤흔들었다. 그녀는 아침 내내 그를 볼 줄로 기다렸고, 저녁 내내도 여전히 그를 기다렸다."], ["She had been expecting to see him the whole morning, and all the evening, too, was still expecting him. Mr.", "러시워스 씨는 그 대단한 소식을 안고 아침 일찍 소더턴으로 떠난 터였다."], ["Rushworth had set off early with the great news for Sotherton; and she had fondly hoped for such an immediate eclaircissement as might save him the trouble of ever coming back again.", "그리고 그녀는 그가 굳이 다시 돌아올 수고를 덜어 줄 만한 그런 즉각적인 결판을 은근히 바랐던 것이다. 그러나 그들은 목사관에서 아무도, 어느 하나도 보지 못했고, 그랜트 부인이 버트럼 부인에게 보낸 다정한 축하와 안부의 쪽지 말고는 아무 기별도 듣지 못했다."], ["But they had seen no one from the Parsonage, not a creature, and had heard no tidings beyond a friendly note of congratulation and inquiry from Mrs.", "두 집안이 온전히 갈라진 것은 여러 주 만에 처음이었다. 팔월이 시작된 이래로, 이런저런 식으로든 그들을 한자리에 모으지 않고 스물네 시간이 지난 적은 여태 없었다."], ["Grant to Lady Bertram.", "서글프고 애타는 하루였다."], ["It was the first day for many, many weeks, in which the families had been wholly divided.", "그리고 이튿날은, 화의 종류는 달랐어도, 결코 덜 하지 않았다."], ["Four-and-twenty hours had never passed before, since August began, without bringing them together in some way or other.", "몇 순간의 들뜬 즐거움에 여러 시간의 시린 괴로움이 뒤따랐다. 헨리 크로퍼드가 다시 집에 왔다."], ["It was a sad, anxious day; and the morrow, though differing in the sort of evil, did by no means bring less.", "그는 토머스 경께 문안을 드리고 싶어 안달인 그랜트 박사와 함께 걸어 올라왔고, 자못 이른 시각에 둘은 아침식사방으로 안내되었으니, 거기엔 식구 대부분이 있었다."], ["A few moments of feverish enjoyment were followed by hours of acute suffering. Henry Crawford was again in the house: he walked up with Dr.", "이내 토머스 경이 나타났고, 마리아는 제가 사랑하는 사내가 아버지에게 소개되는 것을 기쁨과 동요 속에 지켜보았다."], ["Grant, who was anxious to pay his respects to Sir Thomas, and at rather an early hour they were ushered into the breakfast-room, where were most of the family.", "그녀의 심경은 이루 말할 수 없었고, 몇 분 뒤 헨리 크로퍼드—그녀와 톰 사이의 의자에 앉아 있던—가 톰에게 나직한 목소리로, 이 반가운 훼방(토머스 경 쪽으로 정중히 눈길을 던지며)이 지난 뒤에 연극을 다시 할 계획이 있느냐고 묻는 것을 들었을 때에도 그러했다."], ["Sir Thomas soon appeared, and Maria saw with delight and agitation the introduction of the man she loved to her father.", "만일 그렇다면, 그는 이 무리가 필요로 하는 언제든 맨스필드로 돌아오기를 반드시 지키겠다는 것이었다."], ["Her sensations were indefinable, and so were they a few minutes afterwards upon hearing Henry Crawford, who had a chair between herself and Tom, ask the latter in an undervoice whether there were any plans for resuming the play after the present happy interruption (with a courteous glance at Sir Thomas), because, in that case, he should make a point of returning to Mansfield at any time required by the party: he was going away immediately, being to meet his uncle at Bath without delay; but if there were any prospect of a renewal of Lovers’ Vows, he should hold himself positively engaged, he should break through every other claim, he should absolutely condition with his uncle for attending them whenever he might be wanted.", "그는 곧장 떠나야 했으니, 지체 없이 배스에서 삼촌을 만나기로 돼 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 만일 「연인들의 맹세」를 다시 할 가망이 조금이라도 있다면, 그는 스스로 단단히 매인 몸으로 여겨, 다른 어떤 볼일도 마다하고, 필요할 때면 언제든 그들에게 오도록 삼촌과 기어이 담판을 짓겠다는 것이었다."], ["The play should not be lost by his absence.", "자기가 없어서 연극이 무산돼서는 안 된다는 것이었다."]], [["“From Bath, Norfolk, London, York, wherever I may be,”", "“배스든 노퍽이든 런던이든 요크든, 내가 어디 있든,”"], ["said he; “I will attend you from any place in England, at an hour’s notice.”", "그가 말했다. “잉글랜드 어느 곳에서든 한 시간 전 기별이면 달려오겠어요.”"]], [["It was well at that moment that Tom had to speak, and not his sister.", "그 순간 말해야 할 사람이 그 누이가 아니라 톰이었던 것이 다행이었다. 그는 곧바로 술술 편하게 말할 수 있었다."], ["He could immediately say with easy fluency, “I am sorry you are going; but as to our play, that is all over—entirely at an end”", "“가신다니 아쉽네요. 하지만 우리 연극으로 말하면, 그건 다 끝났어요—아주 끝장이 났죠.”"], ["(looking significantly at his father). “The painter was sent off yesterday, and very little will remain of the theatre to-morrow.", "(아버지를 뜻있게 바라보며.) “화가는 어제 돌려보냈고, 내일이면 극장이라곤 거의 남지 않을 거예요."], ["I knew how that would be from the first.", "나야 처음부터 그리될 줄 알았죠."], ["It is early for Bath.", "배스에 가기엔 이르네요."], ["You will find nobody there.”", "거긴 아무도 없을걸요.”"]], [["“It is about my uncle’s usual time.”", "“우리 삼촌이 늘 가시는 때쯤이라서요.”"]], [["“When do you think of going?”", "“언제 떠날 생각이에요?”"]], [["“I may, perhaps, get as far as Banbury to-day.”", "“오늘은 아마 밴버리까지는 갈 수 있겠죠.”"]], [["“Whose stables do you use at Bath?”", "“배스에선 누구 마구간을 쓰세요?”"], ["was the next question; and while this branch of the subject was under discussion, Maria, who wanted neither pride nor resolution, was preparing to encounter her share of it with tolerable calmness.", "하는 것이 다음 물음이었다. 그리고 이 곁가지 화제가 오가는 동안, 자존심도 결단력도 모자라지 않던 마리아는, 제 몫의 그것을 그런대로 차분히 마주할 채비를 하고 있었다."]], [["To her he soon turned, repeating much of what he had already said, with only a softened air and stronger expressions of regret.", "이내 그가 그녀에게로 돌아서서, 앞서 이미 한 말을 상당 부분 되풀이했으니, 다만 좀 더 누그러진 태도와 좀 더 강한 아쉬움의 표현을 곁들였을 뿐이었다."], ["But what availed his expressions or his air?", "그러나 그의 표현이며 태도가 무슨 소용이랴?"], ["He was going, and, if not voluntarily going, voluntarily intending to stay away; for, excepting what might be due to his uncle, his engagements were all self-imposed.", "그는 떠나는 것이고, 제 발로 떠나는 게 아니라면 제 뜻으로 멀리 떨어져 있기로 작정한 것이었으니, 삼촌에게 마땅히 다해야 할 것 말고는 그의 볼일이란 죄다 스스로 지운 것이었기 때문이다."], ["He might talk of necessity, but she knew his independence.", "그는 어쩔 수 없는 노릇이라 말할지 몰라도, 그녀는 그가 얼마나 자유로운 몸인지 알고 있었다."], ["The hand which had so pressed hers to his heart!", "그토록 제 손을 제 가슴에 눌러 대던 그 손!"], ["the hand and the heart were alike motionless and passive now!", "이제 그 손도 그 마음도 똑같이 미동도 없이 무심했다!"], ["Her spirit supported her, but the agony of her mind was severe.", "배포가 그녀를 지탱해 주었으나, 그 마음의 괴로움은 혹독했다."], ["She had not long to endure what arose from listening to language which his actions contradicted, or to bury the tumult of her feelings under the restraint of society; for general civilities soon called his notice from her, and the farewell visit, as it then became openly acknowledged, was a very short one.", "그의 행동이 뒤엎는 그 말을 듣는 데서 오는 것을 견디거나, 사교의 삼감 아래 제 감정의 소용돌이를 파묻어 두거나 할 일은 오래가지 않았다. 이내 두루 오가는 인사치레가 그의 주의를 그녀에게서 불러냈고, 그때 대놓고 작별 방문임이 드러난 그 방문은 아주 짧은 것이었다."], ["He was gone—he had touched her hand for the last time, he had made his parting bow, and she might seek directly all that solitude could do for her.", "그는 갔다—마지막으로 그녀의 손을 스치고, 작별의 절을 하고 나니, 그녀는 곧바로 홀로 있음이 저에게 해 줄 수 있는 모든 것을 찾아도 되었다."], ["Henry Crawford was gone, gone from the house, and within two hours afterwards from the parish; and so ended all the hopes his selfish vanity had raised in Maria and Julia Bertram.", "헨리 크로퍼드는 갔으니, 집에서 갔고, 그 두 시간도 안 되어 그 교구에서도 떠났다. 그리하여 그의 이기적인 허영이 마리아와 줄리아 버트럼의 마음에 불러일으킨 온갖 희망이 끝났다."]], [["Julia could rejoice that he was gone.", "줄리아는 그가 간 것을 기뻐할 수 있었다."], ["His presence was beginning to be odious to her; and if Maria gained him not, she was now cool enough to dispense with any other revenge.", "그의 존재가 그녀에게는 밉살스러워지기 시작하던 참이었다. 그리고 마리아가 그를 얻지 못한다면, 그녀는 이제 다른 어떤 복수도 없이 지낼 만큼 마음이 식어 있었다."], ["She did not want exposure to be added to desertion.", "그녀는 버림받은 데다 망신까지 더해지기를 바라진 않았다."], ["Henry Crawford gone, she could even pity her sister.", "헨리 크로퍼드가 가고 나니, 그녀는 언니를 딱해할 수조차 있었다."]], [["With a purer spirit did Fanny rejoice in the intelligence.", "좀 더 순수한 마음으로 패니는 그 소식을 기뻐했다. 그녀는 만찬 자리에서 그것을 듣고 축복으로 여겼다."], ["She heard it at dinner, and felt it a blessing.", "다른 모든 이는 그것을 아쉬움으로 입에 올렸다."], ["By all the others it was mentioned with regret; and his merits honoured with due gradation of feeling—from the sincerity of Edmund’s too partial regard, to the unconcern of his mother speaking entirely by rote. Mrs.", "그리고 그의 장점이, 에드먼드의 너무 편드는 진심 어린 정에서부터, 그저 입에 붙은 대로 지껄이는 그 어머니의 무심함에 이르기까지, 저마다 다른 정도의 감정으로 기려졌다."], ["Norris began to look about her, and wonder that his falling in love with Julia had come to nothing; and could almost fear that she had been remiss herself in forwarding it; but with so many to care for, how was it possible for even her activity to keep pace with her wishes?", "노리스 부인은 둘레를 두리번거리며, 그가 줄리아에게 반한 것이 아무것도 안 된 것을 의아해하기 시작했고, 자기가 그걸 앞당기는 데 소홀했던 게 아닐까 하는 두려움마저 들 뻔했다. 하지만 마음 써야 할 이가 그토록 많은데, 아무리 그녀의 부지런함인들 어찌 제 바람을 다 좇을 수 있었으랴?"]], [["Another day or two, and Mr. Yates was gone likewise.", "하루 이틀 더 지나, 예이츠 씨도 마찬가지로 떠났다."], ["In his departure Sir Thomas felt the chief interest: wanting to be alone with his family, the presence of a stranger superior to Mr.", "그가 떠나는 데에 토머스 경은 가장 큰 관심을 느꼈다."], ["Yates must have been irksome; but of him, trifling and confident, idle and expensive, it was every way vexatious.", "식구끼리만 있고 싶던 차라, 예이츠 씨보다 나은 낯선 이가 있었어도 성가셨을 텐데, 하물며 실없고 뻔뻔하고 게으르고 돈이나 쓰는 그 사람으로 말하면 어느 모로 보나 짜증스러웠다."], ["In himself he was wearisome, but as the friend of Tom and the admirer of Julia he became offensive.", "그 자체로도 그는 진력나는 사람이었으나, 톰의 벗이자 줄리아의 흠모자로서는 밉살스러워졌다."], ["Sir Thomas had been quite indifferent to Mr. Crawford’s going or staying: but his good wishes for Mr.", "토머스 경은 크로퍼드 씨가 가든 남든 아주 무심했으나, 예이츠 씨를 현관까지 배웅하며 즐거운 여행을 비는 그의 덕담은 진심 어린 흡족함으로 건네진 것이었다."], ["Yates’s having a pleasant journey, as he walked with him to the hall-door, were given with genuine satisfaction. Mr.", "예이츠 씨는 맨스필드의 온갖 연극 채비가 헐리고, 연극에 딸린 온갖 것이 치워지는 것까지 다 보고 남아 있었다."], ["Yates had staid to see the destruction of every theatrical preparation at Mansfield, the removal of everything appertaining to the play: he left the house in all the soberness of its general character; and Sir Thomas hoped, in seeing him out of it, to be rid of the worst object connected with the scheme, and the last that must be inevitably reminding him of its existence.", "그는 그 집이 그 두루 진중한 본색을 온전히 되찾은 채로 떠났다. 그리고 토머스 경은 그가 집을 나서는 것을 보며, 그 계획에 얽힌 가장 고약한 것이자, 그것이 있었음을 어쩔 수 없이 떠올리게 할 마지막 것을 떨쳐 버리기를 바랐다."]], [["Mrs. Norris contrived to remove one article from his sight that might have distressed him.", "노리스 부인은 그를 괴롭혔을 법한 물건 하나를 용케 그의 눈앞에서 치웠다."], ["The curtain, over which she had presided with such talent and such success, went off with her to her cottage, where she happened to be particularly in want of green baize.", "그토록 재주껏, 그토록 훌륭히 도맡아 지어 낸 그 막이 그녀를 따라 그녀의 오두막으로 갔으니, 마침 그녀가 초록색 나사가 유난히 아쉽던 참이었기 때문이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXI", "# 제21장"]], [["Sir Thomas’s return made a striking change in the ways of the family, independent of Lovers’ Vows.", "토머스 경의 귀환은, 「연인들의 맹세」와는 별개로, 그 집안의 방식에 뚜렷한 변화를 가져왔다."], ["Under his government, Mansfield was an altered place.", "그의 다스림 아래 맨스필드는 달라진 곳이 되었다."], ["Some members of their society sent away, and the spirits of many others saddened—it was all sameness and gloom compared with the past—a sombre family party rarely enlivened.", "그들 사교 무리의 몇몇은 떠나보내지고, 다른 여럿의 기운은 시들해졌으니—지난날에 견주면 온통 한결같음과 우울뿐이었다—좀처럼 활기를 띠는 일 없는 침침한 가족 모임이었다."], ["There was little intercourse with the Parsonage.", "목사관과의 왕래는 뜸했다."], ["Sir Thomas, drawing back from intimacies in general, was particularly disinclined, at this time, for any engagements but in one quarter.", "대체로 가까운 사귐에서 물러나 있던 토머스 경은, 이 무렵 오직 한 곳 말고는 어떤 만남도 유난히 내키지 않아 했다."], ["The Rushworths were the only addition to his own domestic circle which he could solicit.", "러시워스가만이 그가 청할 만한, 제 집안 울타리에 더할 유일한 손님이었다."]], [["Edmund did not wonder that such should be his father’s feelings, nor could he regret anything but the exclusion of the Grants.", "에드먼드는 아버지의 심경이 그러한 것을 의아해하지 않았고, 그랜트 내외가 배제된 것 말고는 아무것도 아쉬워하지 않았다."], ["“But they,”", "“하지만 그분들은,”"], ["he observed to Fanny, “have a claim.", "그가 패니에게 말했다. “마땅한 명분이 있어."], ["They seem to belong to us; they seem to be part of ourselves.", "그분들은 우리한테 딸린 사람들 같고, 우리 자신의 한 부분 같거든."], ["I could wish my father were more sensible of their very great attention to my mother and sisters while he was away.", "나는 아버지가, 당신 안 계신 동안 그분들이 어머니와 누이들한테 베푼 그 크나큰 마음씀을 좀 더 헤아려 주셨으면 싶어. 그분들이 홀대받는다고 느끼실까 봐 걱정이야."], ["I am afraid they may feel themselves neglected.", "하지만 사실 아버지는 그분들을 거의 모르셔."], ["But the truth is, that my father hardly knows them.", "아버지가 잉글랜드를 떠나실 때 그분들은 여기 온 지 일 년도 안 됐거든."], ["They had not been here a twelvemonth when he left England.", "아버지가 그분들을 더 잘 아셨더라면, 그 사귐을 마땅한 만큼 값지게 여기셨을 텐데."], ["If he knew them better, he would value their society as it deserves; for they are in fact exactly the sort of people he would like.", "실은 아버지가 딱 좋아하실 만한 그런 분들이니까. 우리끼리는 이따금 활기가 좀 아쉽잖아."], ["We are sometimes a little in want of animation among ourselves: my sisters seem out of spirits, and Tom is certainly not at his ease. Dr.", "누이들은 기운이 없어 보이고, 톰도 분명 마음이 편치 않고."], ["and Mrs. Grant would enliven us, and make our evenings pass away with more enjoyment even to my father.”", "그랜트 박사 내외라면 우리한테 생기를 돋우고, 아버지한테도 저녁을 한결 즐겁게 보내게 해 드릴 텐데.”"]], [["“Do you think so?”", "“그렇게 생각하세요?”"], ["said Fanny: “in my opinion, my uncle would not like any addition.", "패니가 말했다. “제 생각엔, 삼촌은 누가 끼는 걸 도무지 안 좋아하실 것 같아요."], ["I think he values the very quietness you speak of, and that the repose of his own family circle is all he wants.", "삼촌은 오빠가 말하는 바로 그 조용함을 값지게 여기시고, 당신 가족 울타리의 그 평온이야말로 원하시는 전부인 것 같아요."], ["And it does not appear to me that we are more serious than we used to be—I mean before my uncle went abroad.", "그리고 우리가 예전보다 더 진중해졌다고는 제겐 여겨지지 않아요—삼촌이 외국 나가시기 전 말이에요."], ["As well as I can recollect, it was always much the same.", "제가 기억하는 한, 늘 거의 마찬가지였어요."], ["There was never much laughing in his presence; or, if there is any difference, it is not more, I think, than such an absence has a tendency to produce at first.", "삼촌 앞에서 크게 웃는 일은 결코 없었죠. 아니, 차이가 좀 있다면, 그건 그런 오랜 부재가 처음엔 으레 자아내는 것보다 더하진 않다고 봐요."], ["There must be a sort of shyness; but I cannot recollect that our evenings formerly were ever merry, except when my uncle was in town.", "얼마간 서먹함이야 있을 수밖에요. 하지만 예전에 우리 저녁이 즐거웠던 적이 있다고는, 삼촌이 도시에 계실 때 말고는 도무지 기억이 안 나요."], ["No young people’s are, I suppose, when those they look up to are at home”.", "우러러보는 어른이 집에 계실 때 즐거운 젊은이는 없을 것 같아요.”"]], [["“I believe you are right, Fanny,”", "“네 말이 옳은 것 같구나, 패니.”"], ["was his reply, after a short consideration.", "잠깐 생각한 뒤 그가 대답했다."], ["“I believe our evenings are rather returned to what they were, than assuming a new character.", "“우리 저녁이 새로운 빛깔을 띠었다기보다 오히려 예전 모습으로 돌아간 거라고 봐."], ["The novelty was in their being lively.", "새로웠던 건 그 저녁이 활기찼다는 거고."], ["Yet, how strong the impression that only a few weeks will give!", "그런데 겨우 몇 주가 남기는 인상이 어찌 그리 강한지!"], ["I have been feeling as if we had never lived so before.”", "나는 마치 우리가 전엔 이렇게 지낸 적이 없었던 것처럼 느껴 왔거든.”"]], [["“I suppose I am graver than other people,”", "“나야 남들보다 더 진중한가 봐요.”"], ["said Fanny.", "패니가 말했다."], ["“The evenings do not appear long to me.", "“저녁이 제겐 길게 느껴지지 않아요."], ["I love to hear my uncle talk of the West Indies.", "저는 삼촌이 서인도제도 이야기하시는 걸 듣는 게 좋아요."], ["I could listen to him for an hour together.", "한 시간이고 내리 들을 수 있어요."], ["It entertains me more than many other things have done; but then I am unlike other people, I dare say.”", "다른 많은 것보다 그게 저를 더 즐겁게 해 주거든요. 하기야 저는 남들하고 다르겠죠, 아마.”"]], [["“Why should you dare say that?”", "“어째서 감히 그런 말을 하지?”"], ["(smiling). “Do you want to be told that you are only unlike other people in being more wise and discreet?", "(미소 지으며.) “남들과 다른 게 오직 더 슬기롭고 신중하다는 점뿐이라는 말이 듣고 싶은 거니?"], ["But when did you, or anybody, ever get a compliment from me, Fanny?", "그런데 너든 누구든, 나한테서 칭찬을 들은 적이 언제 있었던가, 패니?"], ["Go to my father if you want to be complimented.", "칭찬받고 싶으면 아버지한테 가 봐."], ["He will satisfy you.", "아버지가 흡족하게 해 주실 거야."], ["Ask your uncle what he thinks, and you will hear compliments enough: and though they may be chiefly on your person, you must put up with it, and trust to his seeing as much beauty of mind in time.”", "삼촌한테 어찌 생각하시느냐 여쭤봐, 그럼 실컷 칭찬을 들을 거다. 그게 주로 네 겉모습에 대한 거일지 몰라도, 넌 그쯤 참아야 하고, 삼촌이 머잖아 네 마음의 아름다움도 그만큼 알아보시리라 믿어야지.”"]], [["Such language was so new to Fanny that it quite embarrassed her.", "그런 말은 패니에게 너무나 낯설어 그녀를 여간 겸연쩍게 하지 않았다."]], [["“Your uncle thinks you very pretty, dear Fanny—and that is the long and the short of the matter.", "“삼촌은 네가 아주 예쁘다고 여기신단다, 패니야—요컨대 그게 다야."], ["Anybody but myself would have made something more of it, and anybody but you would resent that you had not been thought very pretty before; but the truth is, that your uncle never did admire you till now—and now he does.", "나 말고 다른 누구라도 그걸 더 부풀렸을 테고, 너 말고 다른 누구라도 전엔 예쁘다고 여겨지지 않았던 걸 서운해했겠지. 하지만 사실 삼촌은 이제껏 널 한 번도 예쁘다 여긴 적이 없다가—이제 그리 여기시는 거야."], ["Your complexion is so improved!—and you have gained so much countenance!—and your figure—nay, Fanny, do not turn away about it—it is but an uncle.", "네 얼굴빛이 어찌나 좋아졌는지!—게다가 얼마나 낯빛이 살아났는지!—그리고 네 몸매도—아니, 패니, 그런 얘기에 고개 돌리지 마라—한낱 삼촌인걸."], ["If you cannot bear an uncle’s admiration, what is to become of you?", "삼촌의 감탄도 못 견딘다면, 넌 대체 어쩌려고 그러니?"], ["You must really begin to harden yourself to the idea of being worth looking at.", "넌 정말이지 바라볼 값어치가 있는 사람이라는 생각에 익숙해지기 시작해야 해."], ["You must try not to mind growing up into a pretty woman.”", "예쁜 여자로 자라나는 걸 아무렇지 않게 여기도록 애써 봐.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["don’t talk so, don’t talk so,”", "그런 말씀 마세요, 그런 말씀 마세요.”"], ["cried Fanny, distressed by more feelings than he was aware of; but seeing that she was distressed, he had done with the subject, and only added more seriously—", "패니가 외쳤는데, 그가 아는 것보다 더 많은 감정에 괴로웠던 것이다. 그러나 그녀가 괴로워하는 것을 보고, 그는 그 화제를 접고, 그저 좀 더 진지하게 덧붙였다—"]], [["“Your uncle is disposed to be pleased with you in every respect; and I only wish you would talk to him more.", "“삼촌은 어느 모로 보나 너를 흐뭇해하실 마음이시고, 나는 그저 네가 삼촌한테 좀 더 이야기했으면 싶을 뿐이야."], ["You are one of those who are too silent in the evening circle.”", "넌 저녁 자리에서 너무 말이 없는 축에 들거든.”"]], [["“But I do talk to him more than I used.", "“하지만 저는 예전보다는 삼촌한테 더 이야기하는걸요."], ["I am sure I do.", "정말 그래요."], ["Did not you hear me ask him about the slave-trade last night?”", "어젯밤에 제가 삼촌한테 노예 무역에 대해 여쭙는 거 못 들으셨어요?”"]], [["“I did—and was in hopes the question would be followed up by others.", "“들었지—그리고 그 물음에 다른 물음들이 이어지기를 바라던 참이었어."], ["It would have pleased your uncle to be inquired of farther.”", "삼촌은 더 여쭤보면 흐뭇해하셨을 텐데.”"]], [["“And I longed to do it—but there was such a dead silence!", "“저도 그러고 싶었어요—그런데 어찌나 쥐 죽은 듯 조용하던지!"], ["And while my cousins were sitting by without speaking a word, or seeming at all interested in the subject, I did not like—I thought it would appear as if I wanted to set myself off at their expense, by shewing a curiosity and pleasure in his information which he must wish his own daughters to feel.”", "게다가 사촌들이 한마디도 없이, 그 화제에 도무지 관심도 없는 듯이 곁에 앉아 있는데, 저는 내키지가 않았어요—삼촌이 당신 딸들도 마땅히 느꼈으면 하실 그런 관심과 즐거움을 제가 내보이며, 사촌들을 깎아내려 저를 돋보이려는 것처럼 보일까 봐서요.”"]], [["“Miss Crawford was very right in what she said of you the other day: that you seemed almost as fearful of notice and praise as other women were of neglect.", "“크로퍼드 양이 며칠 전에 너를 두고 한 말이 아주 옳았어. 너는 다른 여자들이 홀대를 두려워하는 만큼이나 눈길과 칭찬을 두려워하는 것 같다고 하더구나."], ["We were talking of you at the Parsonage, and those were her words.", "우리가 목사관에서 네 얘기를 하고 있었는데, 그게 그녀 말이었어."], ["She has great discernment.", "그녀는 사람 보는 눈이 대단해."], ["I know nobody who distinguishes characters better.", "사람의 됨됨이를 그녀보다 더 잘 가려내는 이를 나는 몰라."], ["For so young a woman it is remarkable!", "그토록 젊은 여자로선 놀라운 일이지!"], ["She certainly understands you better than you are understood by the greater part of those who have known you so long; and with regard to some others, I can perceive, from occasional lively hints, the unguarded expressions of the moment, that she could define many as accurately, did not delicacy forbid it.", "그녀는 분명 너를, 널 그토록 오래 알아 온 사람 대부분보다 더 잘 이해하고 있어. 그리고 몇몇 다른 사람들에 대해서도, 이따금 던지는 생기 넘치는 언질이며 무심코 튀어나오는 그 순간의 말에서 나는 알아챌 수 있는데, 그녀는 조심성만 아니라면 숱한 사람을 그만큼 정확히 짚어 낼 수 있을 거야."], ["I wonder what she thinks of my father!", "그녀가 우리 아버지를 어찌 여길지 궁금하구나!"], ["She must admire him as a fine-looking man, with most gentlemanlike, dignified, consistent manners; but perhaps, having seen him so seldom, his reserve may be a little repulsive.", "더없이 신사답고 위엄 있고 한결같은 태도를 지닌 훤칠한 분으로 감탄할 게 틀림없어. 하지만 어쩌면, 아버지를 그토록 드물게 뵈어서, 그 과묵함이 좀 거슬릴지도 몰라."], ["Could they be much together, I feel sure of their liking each other.", "두 분이 자주 함께 계실 수 있다면, 나는 서로 좋아하시게 될 거라 확신해."], ["He would enjoy her liveliness and she has talents to value his powers.", "아버지는 그녀의 활기를 즐기실 테고, 그녀는 아버지의 역량을 값지게 여길 재주가 있으니까."], ["I wish they met more frequently!", "두 분이 좀 더 자주 만나시면 좋겠어!"], ["I hope she does not suppose there is any dislike on his side.”", "그녀가 아버지 쪽에 무슨 미움이 있다고 여기지는 않았으면 하고.”"]], [["“She must know herself too secure of the regard of all the rest of you,”", "“그녀는 오빠들 나머지 모두의 호의를 너무나 굳게 확신하고 있을 테니,”"], ["said Fanny, with half a sigh, “to have any such apprehension.", "패니가 반쯤 한숨지으며 말했다. “그런 걱정은 조금도 안 할 거예요."], ["And Sir Thomas’s wishing just at first to be only with his family, is so very natural, that she can argue nothing from that.", "그리고 토머스 경이 처음엔 그저 가족하고만 있고 싶어 하시는 거야 너무나 자연스러운 일이니, 그녀도 거기서 무얼 넘겨짚진 못하겠죠."], ["After a little while, I dare say, we shall be meeting again in the same sort of way, allowing for the difference of the time of year.”", "얼마쯤 지나면, 아마 철이 바뀐 걸 감안하고 보면, 우리도 다시 예전 같은 식으로 만나게 될 거예요.”"]], [["“This is the first October that she has passed in the country since her infancy.", "“그녀가 어릴 적 이후로 시골에서 시월을 보내는 건 이번이 처음이야."], ["I do not call Tunbridge or Cheltenham the country; and November is a still more serious month, and I can see that Mrs.", "나야 턴브리지나 첼트넘을 시골이라 부르진 않으니까. 게다가 십일월은 한층 더 진중한 달이지."], ["Grant is very anxious for her not finding Mansfield dull as winter comes on.”", "그리고 나는 그랜트 부인이, 겨울이 다가오면서 그녀가 맨스필드를 따분하게 여기지 않을까 무척 애태우는 걸 알겠어.”"]], [["Fanny could have said a great deal, but it was safer to say nothing, and leave untouched all Miss Crawford’s resources—her accomplishments, her spirits, her importance, her friends, lest it should betray her into any observations seemingly unhandsome.", "패니는 할 말이 아주 많았으나, 아무 말도 안 하고, 크로퍼드 양의 온갖 밑천—그 재주며 활기며 대단함이며 벗들—을 죄다 건드리지 않은 채 두는 편이 더 안전했으니, 자칫 야박해 보일 만한 소견을 저도 모르게 내비칠까 봐서였다."], ["Miss Crawford’s kind opinion of herself deserved at least a grateful forbearance, and she began to talk of something else.", "크로퍼드 양이 저에 대해 품은 다정한 생각은 적어도 고마운 참음을 받을 만했고, 그녀는 다른 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다."]], [["“To-morrow, I think, my uncle dines at Sotherton, and you and Mr. Bertram too.", "“내일은 삼촌이 소더턴에서 만찬을 드시는 것 같아요, 오빠하고 버트럼 오빠도요."], ["We shall be quite a small party at home.", "우리끼리 집에 남으면 아주 단출한 자리가 되겠네요."], ["I hope my uncle may continue to like Mr. Rushworth.”", "삼촌이 러시워스 씨를 계속 좋아하셨으면 좋겠어요.”"]], [["“That is impossible, Fanny.", "“그건 불가능해, 패니."], ["He must like him less after to-morrow’s visit, for we shall be five hours in his company.", "내일 방문을 다녀오면 아버지는 그이를 덜 좋아하시게 될걸, 다섯 시간이나 함께 있을 테니까."], ["I should dread the stupidity of the day, if there were not a much greater evil to follow—the impression it must leave on Sir Thomas.", "뒤따를 훨씬 더 큰 화만 아니라면—그게 토머스 경한테 남길 인상 말이야—나는 그날의 그 따분함이 벌써 겁날 지경이야."], ["He cannot much longer deceive himself.", "아버지도 더는 오래 스스로를 못 속이실 거야."], ["I am sorry for them all, and would give something that Rushworth and Maria had never met.”", "나는 그들 모두가 안됐고, 러시워스와 마리아가 아예 만나지 않았더라면 하고 무엇이든 내놓고 싶은 심정이야.”"]], [["In this quarter, indeed, disappointment was impending over Sir Thomas. Not all his good-will for Mr. Rushworth, not all Mr. Rushworth’s deference for him, could prevent him from soon discerning some part of the truth—that Mr. Rushworth was an inferior young man, as ignorant in business as in books, with opinions in general unfixed, and without seeming much aware of it himself.", "정말이지 이 대목에서 실망이 토머스 경 위에 드리우고 있었다. 러시워스 씨에 대한 그의 온갖 호의도, 그에 대한 러시워스 씨의 온갖 굽실거림도, 그가 이내 그 진실의 일부를 알아차리는 것을 막지는 못했다—러시워스 씨가 못난 젊은이라는 것, 책에 어두운 만큼이나 세상일에도 어둡고, 대체로 의견이 붙박이지 못했으며, 정작 저 자신은 그것을 그리 알지도 못하는 듯하다는 것을 말이다."]], [["He had expected a very different son-in-law; and beginning to feel grave on Maria’s account, tried to understand her feelings.", "그는 아주 다른 사위를 기대했었다. 그리고 마리아를 생각하니 마음이 무거워지기 시작해, 그녀의 심경을 헤아려 보려 했다."], ["Little observation there was necessary to tell him that indifference was the most favourable state they could be in.", "무심함이야말로 그들이 놓일 수 있는 가장 나은 상태임을 알아채는 데는 별로 살필 것도 없었다."], ["Her behaviour to Mr. Rushworth was careless and cold.", "러시워스 씨를 대하는 그녀의 태도는 무심하고 싸늘했다."], ["She could not, did not like him.", "그녀는 그를 좋아할 수 없었고, 좋아하지 않았다."], ["Sir Thomas resolved to speak seriously to her.", "토머스 경은 그녀에게 진지하게 이야기하기로 마음먹었다."], ["Advantageous as would be the alliance, and long standing and public as was the engagement, her happiness must not be sacrificed to it.", "이 혼사가 아무리 득이 되고, 그 약혼이 아무리 오래되고 공공연한 것일지라도, 그녀의 행복이 거기에 희생되어서는 안 되었다."], ["Mr. Rushworth had, perhaps, been accepted on too short an acquaintance, and, on knowing him better, she was repenting.", "러시워스 씨는 어쩌면 너무 짧은 사귐 끝에 받아들여진 것이고, 그를 더 잘 알게 되자 그녀가 뉘우치고 있는지도 몰랐다."]], [["With solemn kindness Sir Thomas addressed her: told her his fears, inquired into her wishes, entreated her to be open and sincere, and assured her that every inconvenience should be braved, and the connexion entirely given up, if she felt herself unhappy in the prospect of it.", "근엄한 다정함으로 토머스 경은 그녀에게 말을 건넸다. 제 두려움을 이르고, 그녀의 바람을 물었으며, 터놓고 솔직히 말해 달라 간청하고, 만일 그녀가 이 앞날에 스스로 불행하다 느낀다면 어떤 불편도 무릅쓰고 이 인연을 아주 접겠노라 다짐해 주었다."], ["He would act for her and release her.", "그녀를 위해 나서서 그녀를 풀어 주겠다는 것이었다."], ["Maria had a moment’s struggle as she listened, and only a moment’s: when her father ceased, she was able to give her answer immediately, decidedly, and with no apparent agitation.", "마리아는 듣는 동안 한순간 갈등했으나, 오직 한순간뿐이었다. 아버지가 말을 마치자 그녀는 곧바로, 단호하게, 겉으로는 아무 동요도 없이 대답할 수 있었다."], ["She thanked him for his great attention, his paternal kindness, but he was quite mistaken in supposing she had the smallest desire of breaking through her engagement, or was sensible of any change of opinion or inclination since her forming it.", "그녀는 그 크나큰 마음씀과 아버지다운 다정함에 고마워했으나, 자기가 이 약혼을 깨뜨리려는 마음이 손톱만큼이라도 있다거나, 이를 맺은 이래 무슨 생각이나 마음의 변화를 느낀다고 여기신다면 아주 잘못 아신 거라 했다."], ["She had the highest esteem for Mr. Rushworth’s character and disposition, and could not have a doubt of her happiness with him.", "그녀는 러시워스 씨의 사람됨과 성품을 더없이 높이 사며, 그와 함께할 제 행복을 의심할 여지가 없다는 것이었다."]], [["Sir Thomas was satisfied; too glad to be satisfied, perhaps, to urge the matter quite so far as his judgment might have dictated to others.", "토머스 경은 흡족했다. 어쩌면 흡족해하고 싶은 마음이 너무 커서, 남들에게라면 제 판단이 시켰음직한 만큼 그 일을 파고들지 못했다."], ["It was an alliance which he could not have relinquished without pain; and thus he reasoned.", "이는 그가 아픔 없이는 놓아 버릴 수 없는 혼사였다."], ["Mr.", "그리하여 그는 이렇게 헤아렸다."], ["Rushworth was young enough to improve. Mr.", "러시워스 씨는 나아질 만큼 젊다."], ["Rushworth must and would improve in good society; and if Maria could now speak so securely of her happiness with him, speaking certainly without the prejudice, the blindness of love, she ought to be believed.", "러시워스 씨는 좋은 사교계에서 반드시, 또 틀림없이 나아질 것이다. 그리고 마리아가 이제 그와 함께할 제 행복을 그토록 굳게 말할 수 있다면, 그것도 분명 사랑의 편견이나 눈멂 없이 말하는 것이라면, 마땅히 믿어 줘야 한다."], ["Her feelings, probably, were not acute; he had never supposed them to be so; but her comforts might not be less on that account; and if she could dispense with seeing her husband a leading, shining character, there would certainly be everything else in her favour.", "그녀의 감정은 아마 예민하지 않았고, 그는 여태 그것을 예민하다 여긴 적도 없었다. 그러나 그 때문에 그녀의 안락이 덜해지지는 않을 것이었다."], ["A well-disposed young woman, who did not marry for love, was in general but the more attached to her own family; and the nearness of Sotherton to Mansfield must naturally hold out the greatest temptation, and would, in all probability, be a continual supply of the most amiable and innocent enjoyments.", "그리고 만일 그녀가 제 남편이 앞장서는 빛나는 인물인 걸 굳이 안 봐도 된다면, 그 밖의 모든 것은 분명 그녀에게 유리할 터였다. 사랑 때문에 시집가는 게 아닌 심성 고운 젊은 여자는 대개 제 친정에 오히려 더 정이 붙는 법이었다."], ["Such and such-like were the reasonings of Sir Thomas, happy to escape the embarrassing evils of a rupture, the wonder, the reflections, the reproach that must attend it; happy to secure a marriage which would bring him such an addition of respectability and influence, and very happy to think anything of his daughter’s disposition that was most favourable for the purpose.", "그리고 소더턴이 맨스필드에 가까운 것은 자연히 더없이 큰 유혹이 되어 줄 테고, 십중팔구 더없이 상냥하고 죄 없는 즐거움을 끊임없이 대 줄 것이었다. 토머스 경의 헤아림은 이러이러했으니, 파혼에 딸릴 겸연쩍은 화들과 그에 따를 뒷말이며 이러쿵저러쿵 소견이며 나무람을 면하게 되어 흐뭇했고, 이토록 존경과 영향력을 더해 줄 혼사를 확보하게 되어 흐뭇했으며, 제 딸의 성품에 대해 그 목적에 가장 유리한 것이라면 무엇이든 믿을 수 있어 아주 흐뭇했다."]], [["To her the conference closed as satisfactorily as to him.", "그녀에게도 그 이야기는 그에게 못지않게 흡족하게 끝났다."], ["She was in a state of mind to be glad that she had secured her fate beyond recall: that she had pledged herself anew to Sotherton; that she was safe from the possibility of giving Crawford the triumph of governing her actions, and destroying her prospects; and retired in proud resolve, determined only to behave more cautiously to Mr.", "그녀는 제 운명을 돌이킬 수 없게 붙들어 둔 것이—소더턴에 새로이 몸을 맹세한 것이, 크로퍼드에게 제 행동을 주무르고 제 앞날을 무너뜨리는 승리를 안겨 줄 가능성에서 안전해진 것이—반가운 마음이었다."], ["Rushworth in future, that her father might not be again suspecting her.", "그리하여 그녀는, 오직 앞으로 러시워스 씨를 좀 더 조심스레 대하여 아버지가 다시 저를 의심하지 않게 하리라 마음먹으며, 도도한 결의 속에 물러났다."]], [["Had Sir Thomas applied to his daughter within the first three or four days after Henry Crawford’s leaving Mansfield, before her feelings were at all tranquillised, before she had given up every hope of him, or absolutely resolved on enduring his rival, her answer might have been different; but after another three or four days, when there was no return, no letter, no message, no symptom of a softened heart, no hope of advantage from separation, her mind became cool enough to seek all the comfort that pride and self revenge could give.", "만일 토머스 경이, 헨리 크로퍼드가 맨스필드를 떠난 뒤 처음 사나흘 안에, 그녀의 감정이 조금이라도 가라앉기 전에, 그녀가 그에 대한 온갖 희망을 접거나 그 연적을 견디기로 아주 마음먹기 전에 딸에게 물었더라면, 그녀의 대답은 달랐을지도 모른다. 그러나 또 사나흘이 지나, 아무 소식도, 편지도, 전갈도, 마음이 누그러진 기미도, 헤어진 데서 무슨 득을 볼 희망도 없게 되자, 그녀의 마음은 자존심과 자기 복수가 줄 수 있는 온갖 위안을 찾을 만큼 싸늘해졌다."]], [["Henry Crawford had destroyed her happiness, but he should not know that he had done it; he should not destroy her credit, her appearance, her prosperity, too.", "헨리 크로퍼드는 그녀의 행복을 무너뜨렸다. 그러나 자기가 그리했음을 그가 알게 해서는 안 되었다."], ["He should not have to think of her as pining in the retirement of Mansfield for him, rejecting Sotherton and London, independence and splendour, for his sake.", "그가 그녀의 체면과 겉모습과 잘나감까지 무너뜨리게 해서는 안 되었다. 그가 그녀를, 그를 못 잊어 맨스필드의 은둔 속에 시들어 가는 여자로, 그를 위해 소더턴과 런던을, 독립과 화려함을 마다한 여자로 떠올리게 해서는 안 되었다."], ["Independence was more needful than ever; the want of it at Mansfield more sensibly felt.", "독립이 그 어느 때보다 절실했고, 맨스필드에서 그것이 없는 것이 한층 사무치게 느껴졌다."], ["She was less and less able to endure the restraint which her father imposed.", "그녀는 아버지가 지우는 그 구속을 점점 더 견디기 어려워졌다."], ["The liberty which his absence had given was now become absolutely necessary.", "그가 없던 동안 누린 그 자유가 이제 없어서는 안 될 것이 되어 있었다."], ["She must escape from him and Mansfield as soon as possible, and find consolation in fortune and consequence, bustle and the world, for a wounded spirit.", "그녀는 되도록 빨리 그와 맨스필드에서 벗어나, 상처 입은 마음을 위해 재물과 지체, 부산함과 세상에서 위안을 찾아야 했다."], ["Her mind was quite determined, and varied not.", "그녀의 마음은 아주 굳어져 흔들리지 않았다."]], [["To such feelings delay, even the delay of much preparation, would have been an evil, and Mr. Rushworth could hardly be more impatient for the marriage than herself.", "그런 심경에는 지체가, 채비를 많이 하느라 지체되는 것마저도 화였을 테고, 러시워스 씨도 그녀 자신보다 혼인을 더 안달할 수는 없을 지경이었다."], ["In all the important preparations of the mind she was complete: being prepared for matrimony by an hatred of home, restraint, and tranquillity; by the misery of disappointed affection, and contempt of the man she was to marry.", "마음의 온갖 중요한 채비에서 그녀는 다 갖춰져 있었으니—집과 구속과 평온에 대한 미움으로, 어그러진 정의 괴로움과 제가 시집갈 사내에 대한 경멸로, 혼인 채비를 마친 셈이었다."], ["The rest might wait.", "나머지야 기다려도 좋았다."], ["The preparations of new carriages and furniture might wait for London and spring, when her own taste could have fairer play.", "새 마차와 세간의 채비는, 제 취향이 좀 더 마음껏 놀 수 있을 런던과 봄까지 기다려도 좋았다."]], [["The principals being all agreed in this respect, it soon appeared that a very few weeks would be sufficient for such arrangements as must precede the wedding.", "당사자들이 이 점에서 다들 뜻을 같이하니, 혼례에 앞서야 할 그런 채비에는 아주 몇 주면 넉넉하리라는 것이 이내 드러났다."]], [["Mrs.", "러시워스 부인은 물러나, 제 사랑하는 아들이 고른 그 복 많은 젊은 여자에게 자리를 내줄 채비가 아주 되어 있었다. 그리하여 십일월 아주 초에, 참된 미망인다운 법도로 저 자신과 제 하녀와 하인과 사륜마차를 배스로 옮겨, 거기서 저녁 모임마다 소더턴의 온갖 놀라운 것을 늘어놓으며 뽐냈다."], ["Rushworth was quite ready to retire, and make way for the fortunate young woman whom her dear son had selected; and very early in November removed herself, her maid, her footman, and her chariot, with true dowager propriety, to Bath, there to parade over the wonders of Sotherton in her evening parties; enjoying them as thoroughly, perhaps, in the animation of a card-table, as she had ever done on the spot; and before the middle of the same month the ceremony had taken place which gave Sotherton another mistress.", "어쩌면 그것을 카드판의 활기 속에서, 정작 그곳에 있을 때만큼이나 톡톡히 즐겼으리라. 그리고 그달 중순도 되기 전에, 소더턴에 또 한 사람의 안주인을 안겨 준 그 예식이 치러졌다."]], [["It was a very proper wedding.", "아주 격에 맞는 혼례였다."], ["The bride was elegantly dressed; the two bridesmaids were duly inferior; her father gave her away; her mother stood with salts in her hand, expecting to be agitated; her aunt tried to cry; and the service was impressively read by Dr. Grant.", "신부는 우아하게 차려입었고, 두 들러리는 격에 맞게 그만 못했으며, 아버지가 신부를 넘겨주었고, 어머니는 손에 각성제를 든 채 마음이 흔들리기를 기다리며 서 있었으며, 이모는 울어 보려 애썼고, 예식은 그랜트 박사가 사뭇 엄숙하게 낭독했다."], ["Nothing could be objected to when it came under the discussion of the neighbourhood, except that the carriage which conveyed the bride and bridegroom and Julia from the church-door to Sotherton was the same chaise which Mr. Rushworth had used for a twelvemonth before.", "온 동네의 입방아에 오를 때에도 트집 잡힐 데라곤 없었으니, 다만 신부와 신랑과 줄리아를 교회 문에서 소더턴까지 실어 나른 마차가 러시워스 씨가 그전 일 년간 타던 바로 그 마차였다는 것뿐이었다."], ["In everything else the etiquette of the day might stand the strictest investigation.", "그 밖의 모든 것에서 그날의 격식은 더없이 까다로운 검사도 견뎌 낼 만했다."]], [["It was done, and they were gone.", "일은 치러졌고, 그들은 떠났다."], ["Sir Thomas felt as an anxious father must feel, and was indeed experiencing much of the agitation which his wife had been apprehensive of for herself, but had fortunately escaped. Mrs.", "토머스 경은 애타는 아버지라면 으레 느낄 법한 것을 느꼈으니, 정말이지 제 아내가 저 자신에 대해 미리 걱정했다가 다행히 면한 그 동요의 상당 부분을 겪고 있었다."], ["Norris, most happy to assist in the duties of the day, by spending it at the Park to support her sister’s spirits, and drinking the health of Mr. and Mrs.", "노리스 부인은, 그날의 소임을 거들려는 마음에 더없이 흡족해, 공원 저택에서 하루를 보내며 그 아우의 기운을 북돋고, 여분의 잔을 한둘 기울여 러시워스 부부의 건강을 빌며, 온통 신나는 기쁨에 겨웠다."], ["Rushworth in a supernumerary glass or two, was all joyous delight; for she had made the match; she had done everything; and no one would have supposed, from her confident triumph, that she had ever heard of conjugal infelicity in her life, or could have the smallest insight into the disposition of the niece who had been brought up under her eye.", "그녀가 이 혼사를 맺었으니, 그녀가 죄다 해냈으니 말이다. 그리고 그 자신만만한 득의를 보고서는, 그녀가 평생 부부의 불화라는 것을 들어 본 적이나 있는지, 제 눈앞에서 자란 그 조카딸의 성품에 대해 손톱만큼의 통찰이라도 지녔는지, 아무도 짐작하지 못했을 것이다."]], [["The plan of the young couple was to proceed, after a few days, to Brighton, and take a house there for some weeks.", "젊은 부부의 계획은, 며칠 뒤 브라이턴으로 가서 거기 몇 주간 집을 한 채 빌리는 것이었다."], ["Every public place was new to Maria, and Brighton is almost as gay in winter as in summer.", "마리아에게는 사람 모이는 곳이란 곳이 죄다 새로웠고, 브라이턴은 겨울에도 여름 못지않게 흥청거렸다."], ["When the novelty of amusement there was over, it would be time for the wider range of London.", "거기서의 새로운 놀이가 시들해지면, 그때는 좀 더 넓은 런던으로 나갈 때일 터였다."]], [["Julia was to go with them to Brighton.", "줄리아도 그들과 함께 브라이턴으로 가기로 되어 있었다."], ["Since rivalry between the sisters had ceased, they had been gradually recovering much of their former good understanding; and were at least sufficiently friends to make each of them exceedingly glad to be with the other at such a time. Some other companion than Mr.", "자매 사이의 겨룸이 그친 뒤로, 그들은 차츰 예전의 그 좋은 사이를 상당 부분 되찾아 가고 있었고, 적어도 이런 때에 서로 함께 있는 것이 서로에게 여간 반가울 만큼은 벗이 되어 있었다."], ["Rushworth was of the first consequence to his lady; and Julia was quite as eager for novelty and pleasure as Maria, though she might not have struggled through so much to obtain them, and could better bear a subordinate situation.", "러시워스 씨 말고 다른 길동무가 그 부인에게는 무엇보다 요긴했다. 그리고 줄리아도 마리아 못지않게 새로움과 즐거움에 목말라 있었으니—하기야 그것을 얻으려고 그토록 애를 쓰지는 않았을 테고, 아랫자리도 좀 더 잘 견딜 수 있었지만."]], [["Their departure made another material change at Mansfield, a chasm which required some time to fill up.", "그들이 떠나자 맨스필드에 또 한 번 이렇다 할 변화가, 메우는 데 얼마간 시간이 걸릴 빈자리가 생겼다. 가족 울타리가 크게 오그라들었다."], ["The family circle became greatly contracted; and though the Miss Bertrams had latterly added little to its gaiety, they could not but be missed.", "그리고 버트럼 자매가 근래 그 명랑함에 별로 보탠 게 없다 해도, 그들이 아쉽지 않을 수 없었다. 그 어머니마저 그들을 아쉬워했다."], ["Even their mother missed them; and how much more their tenderhearted cousin, who wandered about the house, and thought of them, and felt for them, with a degree of affectionate regret which they had never done much to deserve!", "하물며 그 마음 여린 사촌은 얼마나 더했으랴. 그 애는 집 안을 이리저리 거닐며, 그들이 여태 딱히 받을 만한 짓을 한 적 없는 그런 애틋한 아쉬움으로 그들을 생각하고 그들을 안타까워했으니 말이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXII", "# 제22장"]], [["Fanny’s consequence increased on the departure of her cousins.", "사촌들이 떠나자 패니의 위상이 높아졌다."], ["Becoming, as she then did, the only young woman in the drawing-room, the only occupier of that interesting division of a family in which she had hitherto held so humble a third, it was impossible for her not to be more looked at, more thought of and attended to, than she had ever been before; and “Where is Fanny?”", "그때 그녀가 그리되었듯, 응접실의 유일한 젊은 여자가 되고, 여태 그토록 하찮은 셋째 자리나 지키던 그 흥미로운 가족의 한 갈래를 홀로 차지하는 이가 되니, 여태 그 어느 때보다 더 눈길을 받고, 더 생각되고, 더 마음 쓰이지 않을 수가 없었다."], ["became no uncommon question, even without her being wanted for any one’s convenience.", "그리고 “패니는 어디 있느냐?”가, 누구의 편의를 위해 그녀가 필요하지 않을 때조차, 드물지 않은 물음이 되었다."]], [["Not only at home did her value increase, but at the Parsonage too.", "집에서만 그 값어치가 높아진 것이 아니라, 목사관에서도 그러했다."], ["In that house, which she had hardly entered twice a year since Mr. Norris’s death, she became a welcome, an invited guest, and in the gloom and dirt of a November day, most acceptable to Mary Crawford.", "노리스 씨가 죽은 뒤로 일 년에 두 번도 채 발을 들이지 않던 그 집에서, 그녀는 반가운, 청함받는 손님이 되었고, 침침하고 질척한 십일월의 어느 날, 메리 크로퍼드에게 더없이 반가운 사람이 되었다."], ["Her visits there, beginning by chance, were continued by solicitation.", "처음엔 우연히 시작된 그녀의 그곳 방문은, 이제 청함을 받아 이어졌다."], ["Mrs. Grant, really eager to get any change for her sister, could, by the easiest self-deceit, persuade herself that she was doing the kindest thing by Fanny, and giving her the most important opportunities of improvement in pressing her frequent calls.", "제 아우를 위해 무슨 변화든 얻어 주고 싶어 정말로 안달하던 그랜트 부인은, 더없이 손쉬운 자기기만으로, 패니에게 자주 들르기를 채근하는 것이 그 애한테 더없이 친절한 일을 해 주고 더없이 요긴한 성장의 기회를 주는 거라고 저를 설득할 수 있었다."]], [["Fanny, having been sent into the village on some errand by her aunt Norris, was overtaken by a heavy shower close to the Parsonage; and being descried from one of the windows endeavouring to find shelter under the branches and lingering leaves of an oak just beyond their premises, was forced, though not without some modest reluctance on her part, to come in.", "패니는 노리스 이모의 심부름으로 마을에 갔다가, 목사관 가까이에서 소나기를 만났다. 그리고 그 집 울타리 바로 너머의 참나무 가지와 아직 매달린 잎사귀 아래로 비를 피하려 애쓰는 것이 어느 창에서 발견되니, 얼마쯤 얌전한 사양이 없지 않았으나 안으로 들어오지 않을 수 없었다."], ["A civil servant she had withstood; but when Dr.", "정중한 하인은 물리쳤으나, 그랜트 박사가 몸소 우산을 들고 나오자, 여간 부끄러워하며 되도록 빨리 집 안으로 들어가는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없었다."], ["Grant himself went out with an umbrella, there was nothing to be done but to be very much ashamed, and to get into the house as fast as possible; and to poor Miss Crawford, who had just been contemplating the dismal rain in a very desponding state of mind, sighing over the ruin of all her plan of exercise for that morning, and of every chance of seeing a single creature beyond themselves for the next twenty-four hours, the sound of a little bustle at the front door, and the sight of Miss Price dripping with wet in the vestibule, was delightful.", "그리고 마침 더없이 낙담한 마음으로 그 음울한 비를 바라보며, 그날 아침의 운동 계획이 죄다 틀어진 것과 앞으로 스물네 시간 동안 저희 말고 살아 있는 사람 하나 볼 가망이 다 사라진 것을 한숨짓던 딱한 크로퍼드 양에게, 현관에서 조그맣게 부산 떠는 소리와 물이 뚝뚝 듣는 채로 현관홀에 선 프라이스 양의 모습은 반가운 것이었다."], ["The value of an event on a wet day in the country was most forcibly brought before her.", "시골의 비 오는 날에 무슨 일이 벌어진다는 것의 값어치가 그녀 앞에 더없이 절실하게 다가왔다."], ["She was all alive again directly, and among the most active in being useful to Fanny, in detecting her to be wetter than she would at first allow, and providing her with dry clothes; and Fanny, after being obliged to submit to all this attention, and to being assisted and waited on by mistresses and maids, being also obliged, on returning downstairs, to be fixed in their drawing-room for an hour while the rain continued, the blessing of something fresh to see and think of was thus extended to Miss Crawford, and might carry on her spirits to the period of dressing and dinner.", "그녀는 대번에 다시 생기가 돌아, 패니에게 쓸모가 되는 데, 그녀가 처음엔 인정하려 들지 않던 것보다 더 젖어 있음을 알아내고 마른 옷을 마련해 주는 데 누구보다 부지런했다. 그리고 패니는, 이 온갖 마음씀에 따르고 안주인들과 하녀들의 시중과 거듦을 받지 않을 수 없었던 데다, 아래층으로 내려와서는 비가 그치지 않는 동안 한 시간이나 그들의 응접실에 붙들려 있지 않을 수 없었으니, 새로이 보고 생각할 무언가가 주는 그 복이 이렇게 크로퍼드 양에게로 넓혀져, 옷을 갈아입고 만찬을 들 때까지 그녀의 기운을 이어 줄 만했다."]], [["The two sisters were so kind to her, and so pleasant, that Fanny might have enjoyed her visit could she have believed herself not in the way, and could she have foreseen that the weather would certainly clear at the end of the hour, and save her from the shame of having Dr. Grant’s carriage and horses out to take her home, with which she was threatened.", "두 자매가 그녀에게 어찌나 친절하고 어찌나 상냥하던지, 만일 자기가 거치적거리지 않는다고 믿을 수 있었더라면, 또 한 시간 끝에는 날이 분명 개어, 자기를 집에 데려다주려고 그랜트 박사의 마차와 말을 내는—그녀를 으르던—그 창피를 면하게 되리라 미리 알았더라면, 패니는 그 방문을 즐길 수도 있었으리라."], ["As to anxiety for any alarm that her absence in such weather might occasion at home, she had nothing to suffer on that score; for as her being out was known only to her two aunts, she was perfectly aware that none would be felt, and that in whatever cottage aunt Norris might chuse to establish her during the rain, her being in such cottage would be indubitable to aunt Bertram.", "이런 날씨에 제가 나가 있는 것이 집에 무슨 걱정을 자아낼까 하는 근심으로 말하면, 그녀는 그 점에서는 괴로워할 것이 없었다. 그녀가 나온 것을 아는 이라곤 두 이모뿐이라, 아무 걱정도 없으리란 것을, 그리고 노리스 이모가 비 오는 동안 그녀를 어느 오두막에 붙들어 두든, 그녀가 그런 오두막에 있으리란 것을 버트럼 이모는 의심치 않으리란 것을 그녀는 훤히 알았기 때문이다."]], [["It was beginning to look brighter, when Fanny, observing a harp in the room, asked some questions about it, which soon led to an acknowledgment of her wishing very much to hear it, and a confession, which could hardly be believed, of her having never yet heard it since its being in Mansfield.", "날이 밝아지기 시작하는데, 패니가 방 안의 하프를 눈여겨보고 그것에 대해 몇 마디 물으니, 이내 그것을 무척 듣고 싶어 한다는 고백과, 좀처럼 믿기지 않는—맨스필드에 온 이래 아직 한 번도 그것을 들어 본 적이 없다는—고백으로 이어졌다."], ["To Fanny herself it appeared a very simple and natural circumstance.", "패니 자신에게야 그것은 아주 소박하고 자연스러운 일로 여겨졌다."], ["She had scarcely ever been at the Parsonage since the instrument’s arrival, there had been no reason that she should; but Miss Crawford, calling to mind an early expressed wish on the subject, was concerned at her own neglect; and “Shall I play to you now?”", "악기가 온 뒤로 그녀는 목사관에 거의 온 적이 없었고, 올 까닭도 없었던 것이다. 그러나 크로퍼드 양은 그 일을 두고 진작 밝혔던 바람을 떠올리고는, 제 소홀함이 마음에 걸렸다."], ["and “What will you have?” were questions immediately following with the readiest good-humour.", "그리하여 “지금 연주해 드릴까요?”와 “뭘 듣고 싶어요?”가 더없이 선선한 명랑함으로 곧바로 뒤따르는 물음이었다."]], [["She played accordingly; happy to have a new listener, and a listener who seemed so much obliged, so full of wonder at the performance, and who shewed herself not wanting in taste.", "그리하여 그녀는 연주했다. 새 청중을, 그것도 그토록 고마워하고 그토록 그 연주에 감탄이 가득하며 안목이 모자라지 않음을 내보이는 청중을 얻어 흐뭇해하며."], ["She played till Fanny’s eyes, straying to the window on the weather’s being evidently fair, spoke what she felt must be done.", "그녀는, 날이 뚜렷이 개자 창 쪽으로 눈길을 옮긴 패니의 눈이 이제 마땅히 해야 할 일을 말할 때까지 연주했다."]], [["“Another quarter of an hour,”", "“십오 분만 더 있어요.”"], ["said Miss Crawford, “and we shall see how it will be.", "크로퍼드 양이 말했다. “그럼 어떻게 될지 알게 될 테니."], ["Do not run away the first moment of its holding up.", "비가 그치는 첫 순간에 냅다 달아나진 말아요."], ["Those clouds look alarming.”", "저 구름이 심상찮아 보이니까요.”"]], [["“But they are passed over,”", "“하지만 저건 지나갔어요.”"], ["said Fanny.", "패니가 말했다."], ["“I have been watching them.", "“제가 죽 지켜봤거든요."], ["This weather is all from the south.”", "이 날씨는 죄다 남쪽에서 온 거예요.”"]], [["“South or north, I know a black cloud when I see it; and you must not set forward while it is so threatening.", "“남쪽이든 북쪽이든, 나는 시커먼 구름은 보면 알아요. 저렇게 을러대는 동안엔 나서면 안 돼요."], ["And besides, I want to play something more to you—a very pretty piece—and your cousin Edmund’s prime favourite.", "게다가 나는 양한테 뭘 좀 더 연주해 주고 싶어요—아주 어여쁜 곡을요—양의 사촌 에드먼드가 무엇보다 좋아하는 곡이랍니다."], ["You must stay and hear your cousin’s favourite.”", "남아서 사촌이 좋아하는 곡을 들어야 해요.”"]], [["Fanny felt that she must; and though she had not waited for that sentence to be thinking of Edmund, such a memento made her particularly awake to his idea, and she fancied him sitting in that room again and again, perhaps in the very spot where she sat now, listening with constant delight to the favourite air, played, as it appeared to her, with superior tone and expression; and though pleased with it herself, and glad to like whatever was liked by him, she was more sincerely impatient to go away at the conclusion of it than she had been before; and on this being evident, she was so kindly asked to call again, to take them in her walk whenever she could, to come and hear more of the harp, that she felt it necessary to be done, if no objection arose at home.", "패니는 그래야만 함을 느꼈다. 그리고 에드먼드를 생각하느라 굳이 그 말을 기다린 것은 아니었으나, 그런 상기가 그녀를 유난히 그의 생각에 깨어 있게 했다. 그리하여 그녀는 그가 그 방에 몇 번이고 앉아 있는 것을, 어쩌면 지금 그녀가 앉은 바로 그 자리에서, 그 좋아하는 가락을—그녀가 보기엔 더없이 빼어난 음색과 표현으로 연주된 그 가락을—한결같은 즐거움으로 듣고 있는 것을 그려 보았다. 그리고 저 자신도 그것이 마음에 들고, 그가 좋아하는 것이라면 무엇이든 좋아하게 되어 기뻤으나, 그녀는 그것이 끝나자 전보다 더 진심으로 어서 가고 싶어 안달이 났다. 그리고 이것이 뻔히 드러나자, 다시 들르라고, 걸음 나설 때면 언제든 들러 달라고, 와서 하프를 더 들으라고 어찌나 친절히 청함을 받던지, 집에서 반대만 없다면 그리해야겠다 싶었다."]], [["Such was the origin of the sort of intimacy which took place between them within the first fortnight after the Miss Bertrams’ going away—an intimacy resulting principally from Miss Crawford’s desire of something new, and which had little reality in Fanny’s feelings.", "버트럼 자매가 떠난 뒤 처음 두 주 사이에 그들 사이에 생겨난 그런 친밀함의 발단이 이러했다—주로 새로운 것에 대한 크로퍼드 양의 욕구에서 비롯한, 그리고 패니의 심정에는 별로 실질이 없는 친밀함이었다. 패니는 이삼 일에 한 번씩 그녀에게 갔다."], ["Fanny went to her every two or three days: it seemed a kind of fascination: she could not be easy without going, and yet it was without loving her, without ever thinking like her, without any sense of obligation for being sought after now when nobody else was to be had; and deriving no higher pleasure from her conversation than occasional amusement, and that often at the expense of her judgment, when it was raised by pleasantry on people or subjects which she wished to be respected.", "그것은 일종의 홀림 같았다. 가지 않고는 마음이 편치 않았으나, 그러면서도 그녀를 사랑하는 것도, 그녀처럼 생각하는 것도 아니었고, 아무도 달리 없을 때 이제 찾아진다는 것에 무슨 신세 진 마음이 있는 것도 아니었다."], ["She went, however, and they sauntered about together many an half-hour in Mrs.", "또 그녀의 이야기에서, 이따금의 재미 말고는 더 높은 즐거움을 얻지도 못했으니, 그마저도 흔히 제 판단을 저버려야 얻는 것이었다—그녀가 존중받았으면 하는 사람이나 화제를 두고 우스갯소리로 그 재미가 돋을 때면 말이다."], ["Grant’s shrubbery, the weather being unusually mild for the time of year, and venturing sometimes even to sit down on one of the benches now comparatively unsheltered, remaining there perhaps till, in the midst of some tender ejaculation of Fanny’s on the sweets of so protracted an autumn, they were forced, by the sudden swell of a cold gust shaking down the last few yellow leaves about them, to jump up and walk for warmth.", "그래도 그녀는 갔고, 둘은 그랜트 부인의 관목 산책로에서 여러 번 반 시간씩 함께 거닐었으니, 철에 걸맞잖게 날이 유난히 포근했던 것이다. 그리고 이제 견주어 보면 가림막도 없다시피 한 벤치 하나에 이따금 감히 앉아 보기도 했는데, 어쩌면 그토록 길어진 가을의 달콤함을 두고 패니가 무슨 다정한 탄사를 내뱉는 한복판에, 둘레의 마지막 노란 잎사귀 몇을 떨어뜨리며 문득 부풀어 오른 찬 돌풍에 떠밀려, 벌떡 일어나 몸을 덥히려 걸어야 할 때까지 거기 머무르곤 했다."]], [["“This is pretty, very pretty,”", "“이거 참 예쁘네요, 참 예뻐요.”"], ["said Fanny, looking around her as they were thus sitting together one day; “every time I come into this shrubbery I am more struck with its growth and beauty.", "어느 날 둘이 이렇게 함께 앉아 있는데 패니가 둘레를 둘러보며 말했다. “이 산책로에 올 때마다 나는 그 자람과 아름다움에 더 놀라게 돼요."], ["Three years ago, this was nothing but a rough hedgerow along the upper side of the field, never thought of as anything, or capable of becoming anything; and now it is converted into a walk, and it would be difficult to say whether most valuable as a convenience or an ornament; and perhaps, in another three years, we may be forgetting—almost forgetting what it was before.", "삼 년 전만 해도 이건 밭 위쪽을 따라 난 거친 산울타리에 지나지 않았고, 무엇이 되리라 여겨지지도, 무엇이 될 수도 없어 보였는데, 이제 산책로로 바뀌었고, 편의로서 더 값진지 장식으로서 더 값진지 말하기 어려울 정도예요. 그리고 어쩌면 또 삼 년 뒤에는, 우리가 이게 전엔 무엇이었는지 잊게—거의 잊게 될지도 몰라요."], ["How wonderful, how very wonderful the operations of time, and the changes of the human mind!”", "세월의 작용이며 사람 마음의 변화가 어찌 이리 놀라운지, 어찌 이리 참으로 놀라운지!”"], ["And following the latter train of thought, she soon afterwards added: “If any one faculty of our nature may be called more wonderful than the rest, I do think it is memory.", "그리고 뒤엣것의 갈래를 좇아, 그녀는 이내 덧붙였다. “우리 천성의 어느 한 능력이 나머지보다 더 놀랍다 불릴 만하다면, 나는 그게 기억이라고 봐요."], ["There seems something more speakingly incomprehensible in the powers, the failures, the inequalities of memory, than in any other of our intelligences.", "기억의 그 힘, 그 어긋남, 그 들쭉날쭉함에는, 우리의 다른 어떤 지력에서보다 더 사무치게 알 수 없는 무언가가 있는 것 같거든요."], ["The memory is sometimes so retentive, so serviceable, so obedient; at others, so bewildered and so weak; and at others again, so tyrannic, so beyond control!", "기억은 때로 그토록 오래 담아 두고, 그토록 쓸모 있고, 그토록 순종하는가 하면, 또 때로는 그토록 갈피를 못 잡고 그토록 약하며, 또 어떤 때는 그토록 폭군 같고 그토록 걷잡을 수 없어요!"], ["We are, to be sure, a miracle every way; but our powers of recollecting and of forgetting do seem peculiarly past finding out.”", "우리야 물론 어느 모로 보나 하나의 기적이지만, 우리가 떠올리고 잊는 그 힘만은 유난히 헤아릴 길이 없는 것 같아요.”"]], [["Miss Crawford, untouched and inattentive, had nothing to say; and Fanny, perceiving it, brought back her own mind to what she thought must interest.", "크로퍼드 양은 마음이 동하지도, 귀 기울이지도 않아 할 말이 없었다. 그리고 패니는 그것을 알아채고, 흥미로우리라 여겨지는 것으로 제 마음을 되돌렸다."]], [["“It may seem impertinent in me to praise, but I must admire the taste Mrs. Grant has shewn in all this.", "“내가 칭찬하는 게 주제넘어 보일지 모르지만, 그래도 나는 이 모든 데서 그랜트 부인이 내보인 안목에 감탄하지 않을 수가 없어요."], ["There is such a quiet simplicity in the plan of the walk!", "이 산책로의 짜임에는 어찌나 고요한 소박함이 어렸는지!"], ["Not too much attempted!”", "너무 욕심부리지 않은 것 말이에요!”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“그래요,”"], ["replied Miss Crawford carelessly, “it does very well for a place of this sort.", "크로퍼드 양이 심드렁하게 대답했다. “이만한 곳에야 이 정도면 딱 좋죠."], ["One does not think of extent here; and between ourselves, till I came to Mansfield, I had not imagined a country parson ever aspired to a shrubbery, or anything of the kind.”", "여기선 넓이 같은 건 생각도 안 하니까요. 그리고 우리끼리 얘기지만, 나는 맨스필드에 오기 전엔 시골 목사가 관목 산책로 같은 걸 언감생심 바라리라곤 상상도 못 했어요.”"]], [["“I am so glad to see the evergreens thrive!”", "“상록수들이 잘 자라는 걸 보니 정말 기뻐요!”"], ["said Fanny, in reply.", "패니가 대꾸했다."], ["“My uncle’s gardener always says the soil here is better than his own, and so it appears from the growth of the laurels and evergreens in general.", "“삼촌네 정원사는 늘 여기 흙이 자기 것보다 낫다고 하는데, 월계수며 상록수들이 두루 자란 걸 보면 정말 그런가 봐요."], ["The evergreen!", "상록수라니!"], ["How beautiful, how welcome, how wonderful the evergreen!", "상록수는 어찌 이리 아름답고, 어찌 이리 반갑고, 어찌 이리 놀라운지!"], ["When one thinks of it, how astonishing a variety of nature!", "생각해 보면, 자연의 다채로움이 어찌 이리 놀라운지!"], ["In some countries we know the tree that sheds its leaf is the variety, but that does not make it less amazing that the same soil and the same sun should nurture plants differing in the first rule and law of their existence.", "어떤 나라에선 잎을 떨구는 나무가 되레 별종이라는 걸 우리가 알지만, 그렇다고 같은 흙과 같은 해가 그 존재의 첫 법칙과 규범에서부터 다른 초목을 길러 낸다는 게 덜 놀라운 건 아니에요."], ["You will think me rhapsodising; but when I am out of doors, especially when I am sitting out of doors, I am very apt to get into this sort of wondering strain.", "나를 시나 읊는다 여기시겠지만, 나는 바깥에 나와 있을 때면, 특히 바깥에 앉아 있을 때면, 곧잘 이런 놀라운 상념에 빠져들거든요."], ["One cannot fix one’s eyes on the commonest natural production without finding food for a rambling fancy.”", "아무리 흔한 자연의 산물에라도 눈길을 두면 어김없이 이런저런 공상의 먹이를 얻게 되니까요.”"]], [["“To say the truth,”", "“솔직히 말하면,”"], ["replied Miss Crawford, “I am something like the famous Doge at the court of Lewis XIV.; and may declare that I see no wonder in this shrubbery equal to seeing myself in it.", "크로퍼드 양이 대답했다. “나는 루이 14세 궁정의 그 유명한 도제와 좀 비슷해서, 이 산책로에서 나 자신을 보고 있는 것만큼 놀라운 건 없다고 말할 수 있어요."], ["If anybody had told me a year ago that this place would be my home, that I should be spending month after month here, as I have done, I certainly should not have believed them.", "일 년 전에 누가 나한테, 이곳이 내 집이 되고, 내가 여기서 이렇게 달이면 달마다 지내게 될 거라고 했다면, 나는 분명 안 믿었을 거예요."], ["I have now been here nearly five months; and, moreover, the quietest five months I ever passed.”", "나는 이제 여기 온 지 다섯 달이 다 돼 가는데, 게다가 내 평생 가장 조용한 다섯 달이었어요.”"]], [["“Too quiet for you, I believe.”", "“양한텐 너무 조용하겠죠, 아마.”"]], [["“I should have thought so theoretically myself, but,”", "“나도 이치로야 그리 여겼을 텐데,”"], ["and her eyes brightened as she spoke, “take it all and all, I never spent so happy a summer.", "하며 그녀는 말하는 사이 눈빛이 환해졌다. “죄다 통틀어 보면, 나는 이토록 행복한 여름을 보낸 적이 없어요."], ["But then,”", "하지만,”"], ["with a more thoughtful air and lowered voice, “there is no saying what it may lead to.”", "하며 좀 더 생각에 잠긴 태도로 목소리를 낮추어, “이게 무엇으로 이어질지는 아무도 모르죠.”"]], [["Fanny’s heart beat quick, and she felt quite unequal to surmising or soliciting anything more.", "패니의 가슴이 빠르게 뛰었고, 그녀는 그 이상 무엇을 넘겨짚거나 캐물을 엄두가 도무지 나지 않았다."], ["Miss Crawford, however, with renewed animation, soon went on—", "그러나 크로퍼드 양이, 새로이 생기를 띠고 이내 말을 이었다—"]], [["“I am conscious of being far better reconciled to a country residence than I had ever expected to be.", "“시골살이에 내가 여태 예상했던 것보다 훨씬 더 잘 화해했다는 걸 나 스스로도 알겠어요."], ["I can even suppose it pleasant to spend half the year in the country, under certain circumstances, very pleasant.", "어떤 형편에서라면, 일 년의 절반을 시골에서 보내는 것이 즐거우리라고까지, 아주 즐거우리라고까지 여길 수 있거든요."], ["An elegant, moderate-sized house in the centre of family connexions; continual engagements among them; commanding the first society in the neighbourhood; looked up to, perhaps, as leading it even more than those of larger fortune, and turning from the cheerful round of such amusements to nothing worse than a tête-à-tête with the person one feels most agreeable in the world.", "집안 인연들 한복판의 우아하고 알맞은 크기의 집, 그들끼리 끊이지 않는 모임, 이웃에서 으뜸가는 사교계를 거느리고, 어쩌면 더 큰 재산을 지닌 이들보다도 오히려 그것을 이끄는 사람으로 우러러보이고, 그런 흥겨운 놀이의 나날에서 돌아서 봐야 세상에서 가장 마음 맞는 사람과 단둘이 마주 앉는 것보다 더 나쁠 게 없는 삶."], ["There is nothing frightful in such a picture, is there, Miss Price?", "그런 그림에 무서울 게 하나도 없잖아요, 안 그래요, 프라이스 양?"], ["One need not envy the new Mrs. Rushworth with such a home as that.”", "그런 집만 있다면 새로 된 러시워스 부인을 부러워할 것도 없죠.”"]], [["“Envy Mrs. Rushworth!”", "“러시워스 부인을 부러워하다니요!”가 패니가 하려던 말의 전부였다."], ["was all that Fanny attempted to say. “Come, come, it would be very un-handsome in us to be severe on Mrs.", "“자, 자, 러시워스 부인한테 매몰차게 구는 건 우리한테 아주 야박한 짓이겠죠."], ["Rushworth, for I look forward to our owing her a great many gay, brilliant, happy hours.", "나는 우리가 그이 덕에 흥겹고 빛나고 행복한 시간을 아주 많이 누리게 되기를 바라거든요."], ["I expect we shall be all very much at Sotherton another year.", "내년엔 우리가 다들 소더턴에 아주 자주 가 있게 될 것 같아요."], ["Such a match as Miss Bertram has made is a public blessing; for the first pleasures of Mr.", "버트럼 양이 한 것 같은 혼사는 세상에 두루 복이 되는 일이에요."], ["Rushworth’s wife must be to fill her house, and give the best balls in the country.”", "러시워스 씨 아내의 첫 즐거움은 제 집을 가득 채우고, 이 고장에서 가장 좋은 무도회를 여는 것일 테니까요.”"]], [["Fanny was silent, and Miss Crawford relapsed into thoughtfulness, till suddenly looking up at the end of a few minutes, she exclaimed, “Ah!", "패니는 잠자코 있었고, 크로퍼드 양은 다시 생각에 잠겼다가, 몇 분 뒤 문득 고개를 들며 외쳤다. “아!"], ["here he is.” It was not Mr.", "저기 그이가 오네.”"], ["Rushworth, however, but Edmund, who then appeared walking towards them with Mrs. Grant.", "그러나 그것은 러시워스 씨가 아니라 에드먼드였으니, 그때 그가 그랜트 부인과 함께 그들 쪽으로 걸어오는 것이 보였다."], ["“My sister and Mr. Bertram.", "“우리 언니하고 버트럼 씨네요."], ["I am so glad your eldest cousin is gone, that he may be Mr. Bertram again.", "양의 맏사촌이 가서 정말 기뻐요, 그이가 다시 버트럼 씨가 될 수 있으니까."], ["There is something in the sound of Mr. Edmund Bertram so formal, so pitiful, so younger-brother-like, that I detest it.”", "‘에드먼드 버트럼 씨’라는 소리엔 어쩐지 그토록 격식 차리고, 그토록 딱하고, 그토록 아우 같은 데가 있어서, 나는 그게 질색이거든요.”"]], [["“How differently we feel!”", "“우리 느낌이 어쩜 그리 다를까요!”"], ["cried Fanny.", "패니가 외쳤다."], ["“To me, the sound of Mr. Bertram is so cold and nothing-meaning, so entirely without warmth or character!", "“나한텐 ‘버트럼 씨’라는 소리가 그토록 차갑고 아무 뜻도 없이, 그토록 온통 온기도 개성도 없이 들려요!"], ["It just stands for a gentleman, and that’s all.", "그냥 한 신사를 가리킬 뿐이지, 그게 다예요."], ["But there is nobleness in the name of Edmund.", "하지만 에드먼드라는 이름엔 고귀함이 있어요."], ["It is a name of heroism and renown; of kings, princes, and knights; and seems to breathe the spirit of chivalry and warm affections.”", "그건 영웅다움과 명성의 이름, 왕과 왕자와 기사의 이름이고, 기사도와 뜨거운 정의 기운을 내뿜는 것 같거든요.”"]], [["“I grant you the name is good in itself, and Lord Edmund or Sir Edmund sound delightfully; but sink it under the chill, the annihilation of a Mr., and Mr.", "“이름 자체야 좋다고 인정하죠, ‘에드먼드 공(公)’이나 ‘에드먼드 경(卿)’은 근사하게 들리고요."], ["Edmund is no more than Mr. John or Mr.", "하지만 그걸 ‘씨’라는 그 서늘함, 그 소멸 아래로 가라앉히면, ‘에드먼드 씨’는 ‘존 씨’나 ‘토머스 씨’와 다를 게 없어요."], ["Thomas. Well, shall we join and disappoint them of half their lecture upon sitting down out of doors at this time of year, by being up before they can begin?”", "자, 우리 저들이 시작하기도 전에 일어서서, 이맘때 바깥에 앉아 있는 걸 두고 늘어놓을 잔소리의 절반을 김빠지게 해 줄까요?”"]], [["Edmund met them with particular pleasure.", "에드먼드는 각별한 반가움으로 그들을 맞았다."], ["It was the first time of his seeing them together since the beginning of that better acquaintance which he had been hearing of with great satisfaction.", "그가 크나큰 흡족함으로 전해 듣던 그 한결 나아진 사귐이 시작된 이래, 그가 그 둘이 함께 있는 것을 보는 건 처음이었다."], ["A friendship between two so very dear to him was exactly what he could have wished: and to the credit of the lover’s understanding, be it stated, that he did not by any means consider Fanny as the only, or even as the greater gainer by such a friendship.", "저에게 그토록 소중한 두 사람 사이의 우정이야말로 그가 바랄 만한 바로 그것이었다. 그리고 그 사랑에 빠진 이의 헤아림을 위해 덧붙여 두자면, 그는 결코 패니를 그런 우정에서 유일한, 아니 더 큰 득을 보는 쪽으로조차 여기지 않았다."]], [["“Well,”", "“자,”"], ["said Miss Crawford, “and do you not scold us for our imprudence?", "크로퍼드 양이 말했다. “우리가 경솔했다고 나무라지 않으시나요?"], ["What do you think we have been sitting down for but to be talked to about it, and entreated and supplicated never to do so again?”", "우리가 대체 뭐 하러 앉아 있었겠어요, 그걸로 잔소리를 듣고, 다시는 그러지 말라고 사정사정 애원받으려는 것 말고요?”"]], [["“Perhaps I might have scolded,”", "“나무랐을지도 모르죠,” 에드먼드가 말했다."], ["said Edmund, “if either of you had been sitting down alone; but while you do wrong together, I can overlook a great deal.”", "“두 분 중 하나가 혼자 앉아 있었더라면요. 하지만 둘이 함께 나쁜 짓을 하고 있으니, 나는 많은 걸 눈감아 줄 수 있어요.”"]], [["“They cannot have been sitting long,” cried Mrs.", "“오래 앉아 있었을 리 없어요,”"], ["Grant, “for when I went up for my shawl I saw them from the staircase window, and then they were walking.”", "그랜트 부인이 외쳤다. “내가 숄을 가지러 올라갔을 때 계단 창으로 둘을 봤는데, 그땐 걷고 있었거든요.”"]], [["“And really,”", "“게다가 정말이지,”"], ["added Edmund, “the day is so mild, that your sitting down for a few minutes can be hardly thought imprudent.", "에드먼드가 덧붙였다. “날이 하도 포근해서, 두 분이 몇 분 앉아 있었다고 경솔하달 것도 없어요."], ["Our weather must not always be judged by the calendar.", "우리 날씨를 늘 달력으로만 판단할 순 없죠."], ["We may sometimes take greater liberties in November than in May.”", "우린 때로 오월보다 십일월에 더 마음대로 굴어도 되는걸요.”"]], [["“Upon my word,”", "“원, 참,”"], ["cried Miss Crawford, “you are two of the most disappointing and unfeeling kind friends I ever met with!", "크로퍼드 양이 외쳤다. “두 분은 내가 여태 만난 다정한 벗들 중에 가장 실망스럽고 무정한 축이에요!"], ["There is no giving you a moment’s uneasiness.", "잠시도 마음 졸이게 해 드릴 수가 없다니까요."], ["You do not know how much we have been suffering, nor what chills we have felt!", "우리가 얼마나 고생했는지, 얼마나 오슬오슬 떨었는지 모르시죠!"], ["But I have long thought Mr. Bertram one of the worst subjects to work on, in any little manoeuvre against common sense, that a woman could be plagued with.", "하지만 나는 진작부터, 상식에 맞서 무슨 자그마한 술수를 부리는 데는 버트럼 씨야말로 여자가 상대해야 할 가장 골치 아픈 상대 중 하나라고 여겨 왔어요."], ["I had very little hope of him from the first; but you, Mrs.", "저이한테야 처음부터 별 기대를 안 했죠."], ["Grant, my sister, my own sister, I think I had a right to alarm you a little.”", "하지만 언니, 우리 언니, 내 친언니, 언니는 내가 좀 놀라게 해 줄 권리쯤은 있다고 봤는데.”"]], [["“Do not flatter yourself, my dearest Mary.", "“김칫국부터 마시지 마라, 사랑하는 메리야."], ["You have not the smallest chance of moving me.", "나를 흔들 가망은 눈곱만큼도 없으니까."], ["I have my alarms, but they are quite in a different quarter; and if I could have altered the weather, you would have had a good sharp east wind blowing on you the whole time—for here are some of my plants which Robert will leave out because the nights are so mild, and I know the end of it will be, that we shall have a sudden change of weather, a hard frost setting in all at once, taking everybody (at least Robert) by surprise, and I shall lose every one; and what is worse, cook has just been telling me that the turkey, which I particularly wished not to be dressed till Sunday, because I know how much more Dr.", "나도 걱정거리야 있지만, 그건 아주 딴 데 있단다. 그리고 내가 날씨를 바꿀 수 있었다면, 넌 그 내내 매섭게 날카로운 동풍을 맞았을 거야—여기 내 화초 몇 개가 있는데, 로버트가 밤이 하도 포근하다고 자꾸 밖에 내놓거든."], ["Grant would enjoy it on Sunday after the fatigues of the day, will not keep beyond to-morrow.", "그리고 나는 그 끝이 어찌 될지 알아. 날씨가 문득 바뀌어 갑자기 된서리가 한꺼번에 내려, 모두를(적어도 로버트를) 깜짝 놀라게 하고, 나는 화초를 하나도 남김없이 잃을 거야."], ["These are something like grievances, and make me think the weather most unseasonably close.”", "게다가 더 나쁜 건, 요리사가 방금 그러는데, 내가 일요일까지는 굳이 손질하지 말라고 한 그 칠면조—그랜트 박사가 그날의 고단함 끝에 일요일에 먹으면 얼마나 더 맛있어할지 아니까—그게 내일을 넘기지 못한다는 거야. 이런 게 얼추 걱정거리라는 거고, 그래서 나는 이 날씨가 철에 안 맞게 무덥다 여기는 거란다.”"]], [["“The sweets of housekeeping in a country village!”", "“시골 마을에서 살림하는 재미란!”"], ["said Miss Crawford archly.", "크로퍼드 양이 짓궂게 말했다."], ["“Commend me to the nurseryman and the poulterer.”", "“나야 묘목상이랑 새 장수가 최고예요.”"]], [["“My dear child, commend Dr. Grant to the deanery of Westminster or St.", "“얘야, 그랜트 박사를 웨스트민스터나 세인트폴의 주임사제 자리에 앉혀 주렴, 그럼 나도 너 못지않게 그 묘목상이랑 새 장수를 반길 테니."], ["Paul’s, and I should be as glad of your nurseryman and poulterer as you could be. But we have no such people in Mansfield.", "하지만 맨스필드엔 그런 사람들이 없어."], ["What would you have me do?”", "나더러 어쩌라는 거니?”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["you can do nothing but what you do already: be plagued very often, and never lose your temper.”", "언니야 지금 하는 것 말고 달리 할 게 없죠. 툭하면 시달리면서도, 결코 성은 안 내는 거요.”"]], [["“Thank you; but there is no escaping these little vexations, Mary, live where we may; and when you are settled in town and I come to see you, I dare say I shall find you with yours, in spite of the nurseryman and the poulterer, perhaps on their very account.", "“고맙구나. 하지만 이런 자잘한 짜증거리야 어디 살든 벗어날 수 없단다, 메리."], ["Their remoteness and unpunctuality, or their exorbitant charges and frauds, will be drawing forth bitter lamentations.”", "그리고 네가 도시에 자리 잡고 내가 널 보러 가면, 아마 넌 묘목상이랑 새 장수가 있는데도 네 나름의 짜증거리를 안고 있을 거야, 어쩌면 바로 그 사람들 탓에 말이지. 그들이 멀리 있고 시간을 안 지킨다거나, 터무니없이 바가지 씌우고 속인다거나 하는 게 쓰디쓴 한탄을 자아낼 테니까.”"]], [["“I mean to be too rich to lament or to feel anything of the sort.", "“나는 그런 걸 한탄하거나 느끼거나 할 것도 없이 아주 부자가 될 참이에요."], ["A large income is the best recipe for happiness I ever heard of.", "큰 수입이야말로 내가 들어 본 행복의 가장 좋은 비법이니까요."], ["It certainly may secure all the myrtle and turkey part of it.”", "그거면 분명 도금양이며 칠면조며 하는 부분은 죄다 챙길 수 있죠.”"]], [["“You intend to be very rich?”", "“아주 부자가 될 작정이오?”"], ["said Edmund, with a look which, to Fanny’s eye, had a great deal of serious meaning.", "에드먼드가 말했는데, 그 눈빛에는 패니의 눈에 자못 진지한 뜻이 담긴 듯했다."]], [["“To be sure.", "“그럼요."], ["Do not you?", "안 그러세요?"], ["Do not we all?”", "우리 다 그렇지 않나요?”"]], [["“I cannot intend anything which it must be so completely beyond my power to command.", "“내 힘으로 도무지 어찌해 볼 수 없는 게 뻔한 걸 작정할 순 없죠."], ["Miss Crawford may chuse her degree of wealth.", "크로퍼드 양이야 원하는 만큼의 재산을 고를 수 있어요."], ["She has only to fix on her number of thousands a year, and there can be no doubt of their coming.", "연 수입 몇천 파운드를 정하기만 하면, 그게 들어올 건 의심할 여지가 없으니까요."], ["My intentions are only not to be poor.”", "내 작정은 그저 가난하지 않겠다는 것뿐이에요.”"]], [["“By moderation and economy, and bringing down your wants to your income, and all that.", "“절제와 알뜰함으로, 씀씀이를 수입에 맞춰 낮추고 어쩌고 하면서요."], ["I understand you—and a very proper plan it is for a person at your time of life, with such limited means and indifferent connexions.", "무슨 말인지 알겠어요—그리고 그건 씨 나이에, 그토록 빤한 살림에 변변찮은 연줄을 지닌 사람한텐 아주 걸맞은 계획이죠."], ["What can you want but a decent maintenance?", "씨가 그럭저럭한 생계 말고 뭘 더 바라겠어요?"], ["You have not much time before you; and your relations are in no situation to do anything for you, or to mortify you by the contrast of their own wealth and consequence.", "씨 앞엔 시간도 많지 않고, 씨 친척들도 씨를 위해 뭘 해 줄 처지도, 제 재산과 지체를 견주며 씨를 무안케 할 처지도 못 되잖아요."], ["Be honest and poor, by all means—but I shall not envy you; I do not much think I shall even respect you.", "부디 정직하게, 그리고 가난하게 사세요—하지만 나는 씨를 부러워하진 않을 거예요. 심지어 씨를 존경할 것 같지도 않고요."], ["I have a much greater respect for those that are honest and rich.”", "나는 정직하면서 부유한 이들을 훨씬 더 존경하거든요.”"]], [["“Your degree of respect for honesty, rich or poor, is precisely what I have no manner of concern with.", "“정직함을 두고, 부유하든 가난하든, 크로퍼드 양이 얼마나 존경하느냐 하는 것은 나와는 도무지 아무 상관이 없는 일이에요."], ["I do not mean to be poor.", "나는 가난할 생각이 없어요."], ["Poverty is exactly what I have determined against.", "가난이야말로 내가 딱 안 하기로 작정한 것이거든요."], ["Honesty, in the something between, in the middle state of worldly circumstances, is all that I am anxious for your not looking down on.”", "다만 그 사이의 무언가, 세상 형편의 중간 자리에 있는 정직함, 그것만은 크로퍼드 양이 얕보지 않았으면 하고 바랄 뿐이에요.”"]], [["“But I do look down upon it, if it might have been higher.", "“하지만 나는 그걸 얕본답니다, 더 높이 오를 수 있었을 거라면요."], ["I must look down upon anything contented with obscurity when it might rise to distinction.”", "나는 두각을 나타낼 수 있는데도 무명에 만족하는 것이라면 무엇이든 얕볼 수밖에 없어요.”"]], [["“But how may it rise?", "“하지만 어떻게 오를 수 있죠?"], ["How may my honesty at least rise to any distinction?”", "적어도 내 정직함이 어떻게 두각을 나타낼 수 있나요?”"]], [["This was not so very easy a question to answer, and occasioned an “Oh!”", "이건 그리 쉬운 물음이 아니었고, 그 어여쁜 숙녀는 이렇게 덧붙이기 전에 자못 긴 “아!”"], ["of some length from the fair lady before she could add, “You ought to be in parliament, or you should have gone into the army ten years ago.”", "소리를 냈다. “씨는 의회에 있어야 하는데, 아니면 십 년 전에 군대에 갔어야 했어요.”"]], [["“That is not much to the purpose now; and as to my being in parliament, I believe I must wait till there is an especial assembly for the representation of younger sons who have little to live on.", "“그건 지금 와선 별 소용이 없죠. 그리고 내가 의회에 드는 걸로 말하면, 아무래도 나는 먹고살 게 별로 없는 작은아들들을 대표하는 특별 의회가 생길 때까지 기다려야 할까 봐요."], ["No, Miss Crawford,”", "아니에요, 크로퍼드 양,”"], ["he added, in a more serious tone, “there are distinctions which I should be miserable if I thought myself without any chance—absolutely without chance or possibility of obtaining—but they are of a different character.”", "그가 좀 더 진지한 어조로 덧붙였다. “내가 조금의 가망도—얻을 가망이나 가능성이 아주 없다고 여긴다면 비참해질 그런 두각이 정말 있긴 있어요—하지만 그건 결이 다른 것이랍니다.”"]], [["A look of consciousness as he spoke, and what seemed a consciousness of manner on Miss Crawford’s side as she made some laughing answer, was sorrowfull food for Fanny’s observation; and finding herself quite unable to attend as she ought to Mrs.", "그가 말하는 사이의 그 뜻있는 낯빛, 그리고 크로퍼드 양이 무언가 웃음 섞인 대답을 하며 지은 뜻있는 듯한 태도는, 패니가 지켜보기에 서글픈 먹이였다."], ["Grant, by whose side she was now following the others, she had nearly resolved on going home immediately, and only waited for courage to say so, when the sound of the great clock at Mansfield Park, striking three, made her feel that she had really been much longer absent than usual, and brought the previous self-inquiry of whether she should take leave or not just then, and how, to a very speedy issue.", "그리고 이제 그랜트 부인 곁에 붙어 다른 이들을 뒤따르며, 그 부인에게 마땅히 기울여야 할 만큼 주의를 도무지 기울일 수 없음을 깨닫자, 그녀는 곧장 집으로 가기로 거의 마음먹고, 그 말을 꺼낼 용기만 기다리고 있었는데, 그때 맨스필드 파크의 큰 시계가 세 시를 치는 소리에, 자기가 정말이지 여느 때보다 훨씬 오래 나와 있었음을 느꼈고, 지금 작별을 고할지 말지, 또 어떻게 할지 하는 앞서의 자문이 아주 빠르게 매듭지어졌다."], ["With undoubting decision she directly began her adieus; and Edmund began at the same time to recollect that his mother had been inquiring for her, and that he had walked down to the Parsonage on purpose to bring her back.", "조금의 망설임도 없는 결단으로 그녀는 곧바로 작별 인사를 시작했다. 그리고 에드먼드도 그때 어머니가 그녀를 찾던 것을, 그리고 자기가 바로 그녀를 데려오려고 목사관으로 걸어 내려왔음을 떠올리기 시작했다."]], [["Fanny’s hurry increased; and without in the least expecting Edmund’s attendance, she would have hastened away alone; but the general pace was quickened, and they all accompanied her into the house, through which it was necessary to pass. Dr.", "패니의 서두름이 커졌다."], ["Grant was in the vestibule, and as they stopt to speak to him she found, from Edmund’s manner, that he did mean to go with her. He too was taking leave.", "그리고 에드먼드가 함께해 주리라곤 조금도 기대하지 않은 채, 그녀는 혼자 서둘러 떠나려 했다."], ["She could not but be thankful. In the moment of parting, Edmund was invited by Dr.", "그러나 다들 걸음을 재촉해, 지나가야만 하는 그 집 안으로 그녀와 함께 들어갔다."], ["Grant to eat his mutton with him the next day; and Fanny had barely time for an unpleasant feeling on the occasion, when Mrs.", "그랜트 박사가 현관홀에 있었고, 그에게 인사하려 걸음을 멈춘 사이 그녀는 에드먼드의 태도로 미루어 그가 정말로 저와 함께 갈 작정임을 알았다. 그도 작별을 고하던 참이었다."], ["Grant, with sudden recollection, turned to her and asked for the pleasure of her company too.", "그녀는 고맙지 않을 수 없었다. 헤어지려는 순간, 에드먼드는 그랜트 박사에게서 이튿날 함께 양고기를 들자는 청을 받았다."], ["This was so new an attention, so perfectly new a circumstance in the events of Fanny’s life, that she was all surprise and embarrassment; and while stammering out her great obligation, and her “but she did not suppose it would be in her power,”", "그리고 패니가 그 일로 언짢은 기분에 잠길 겨를도 없이, 그랜트 부인이 문득 떠오른 듯 그녀에게 돌아서서 그녀도 함께해 주는 기쁨을 청했다. 이것은 너무나 새로운 마음씀이요, 패니의 삶에서 일어난 일들 가운데 너무나 온전히 새로운 일이라, 그녀는 온통 놀랍고 겸연쩍었다."], ["was looking at Edmund for his opinion and help.", "그리고 크나큰 고마움과, “하지만 제가 그럴 수 있을 것 같지 않은데요”"], ["But Edmund, delighted with her having such an happiness offered, and ascertaining with half a look, and half a sentence, that she had no objection but on her aunt’s account, could not imagine that his mother would make any difficulty of sparing her, and therefore gave his decided open advice that the invitation should be accepted; and though Fanny would not venture, even on his encouragement, to such a flight of audacious independence, it was soon settled, that if nothing were heard to the contrary, Mrs.", "하는 말을 더듬더듬 내놓는 사이, 그녀는 에드먼드의 의견과 도움을 구하려 그를 바라보고 있었다. 그러나 에드먼드는 그녀에게 그런 행복이 주어진 것을 반기며, 반쯤의 눈짓과 반쯤의 말로 그녀가 이모 때문 말고는 반대할 것이 없음을 확인하고서, 어머니가 그녀를 내주는 데 무슨 어려움을 두시리라곤 상상할 수 없어, 그 청을 받아들이라는 제 뜻을 분명하고 거리낌 없이 밝혔다."], ["Grant might expect her.", "그리고 패니는, 그의 북돋움에도 그런 대담하고 자유로운 비약에는 감히 나서지 못했으나, 딴말이 들려오지 않는 한 그랜트 부인이 그녀를 기다려도 좋다는 것으로 이내 매듭지어졌다."]], [["“And you know what your dinner will be,”", "“저녁으로 뭘 드시게 될지 아시겠죠,”"], ["said Mrs. Grant, smiling—“the turkey, and I assure you a very fine one; for, my dear,”", "그랜트 부인이 미소 지으며 말했다—“칠면조예요, 게다가 정말 아주 좋은 놈이랍니다."], ["turning to her husband, “cook insists upon the turkey’s being dressed to-morrow.”", "여보,” 하며 남편에게 돌아서서, “요리사가 칠면조는 내일 손질해야 한다고 우기거든요.”"]], [["“Very well, very well,”", "“좋아, 좋아,”"], ["cried Dr.", "그랜트 박사가 외쳤다."], ["Grant, “all the better; I am glad to hear you have anything so good in the house.", "“그럴수록 좋지. 집에 그렇게 좋은 게 있다니 반갑구려."], ["But Miss Price and Mr. Edmund Bertram, I dare say, would take their chance.", "하지만 프라이스 양하고 에드먼드 버트럼 씨야, 아마 뭐가 나오든 마다 않을 거요."], ["We none of us want to hear the bill of fare.", "우린 아무도 차림표를 듣고 싶지 않아요."], ["A friendly meeting, and not a fine dinner, is all we have in view.", "정다운 모임이지, 훌륭한 만찬이 아니라, 그게 우리가 바라는 전부니까."], ["A turkey, or a goose, or a leg of mutton, or whatever you and your cook chuse to give us.”", "칠면조든 거위든 양 다리든, 당신하고 요리사가 뭘 주든 말이오.”"]], [["The two cousins walked home together; and, except in the immediate discussion of this engagement, which Edmund spoke of with the warmest satisfaction, as so particularly desirable for her in the intimacy which he saw with so much pleasure established, it was a silent walk; for having finished that subject, he grew thoughtful and indisposed for any other.", "두 사촌은 함께 집으로 걸어갔다. 그리고 에드먼드가 이 초대를—그토록 즐겁게 이뤄지는 것을 지켜본 그 친밀함 속에서 그녀에게 그토록 각별히 바람직한 일이라며 더없이 뜨거운 흡족함으로 이야기한—당장 두고 이야기한 것 말고는, 말 없는 걸음이었다. 그 화제를 끝내고 나자 그가 생각에 잠겨 다른 어떤 이야기도 내키지 않아 했기 때문이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXIII", "# 제23장"]], [["“But why should Mrs. Grant ask Fanny?”", "“그런데 그랜트 부인이 어째서 패니를 부른다니?”"], ["said Lady Bertram.", "버트럼 부인이 말했다."], ["“How came she to think of asking Fanny?", "“어쩌다 패니를 부를 생각을 했을까?"], ["Fanny never dines there, you know, in this sort of way.", "패니는 이런 식으로 거기서 만찬을 든 적이 없잖니."], ["I cannot spare her, and I am sure she does not want to go.", "나는 이 애 없이는 못 지내고, 이 애도 분명 가고 싶어 하지 않을 게다."], ["Fanny, you do not want to go, do you?”", "패니, 넌 가고 싶지 않지, 그렇지?”"]], [["“If you put such a question to her,” cried Edmund, preventing his cousin’s speaking, “Fanny will immediately say No; but I am sure, my dear mother, she would like to go; and I can see no reason why she should not.”", "“그렇게 물으시면,” 사촌이 말하려는 것을 앞질러 에드먼드가 외쳤다. “패니는 곧바로 아니라고 할 거예요. 하지만 어머니, 저는 이 애가 가고 싶어 하리라고 확신해요. 그리고 못 갈 까닭이 하나도 없다고 봐요.”"]], [["“I cannot imagine why Mrs. Grant should think of asking her?", "“그랜트 부인이 어째서 이 애를 부를 생각을 했는지 도무지 모르겠구나."], ["She never did before.", "전엔 한 번도 안 그랬는데."], ["She used to ask your sisters now and then, but she never asked Fanny.”", "이따금 네 누이들은 불렀어도, 패니는 결코 안 불렀잖니.”"]], [["“If you cannot do without me, ma’am—”", "“이모가 저 없이 못 지내신다면—”"], ["said Fanny, in a self-denying tone.", "패니가 저를 내려놓는 투로 말했다."]], [["“But my mother will have my father with her all the evening.”", "“하지만 어머닌 저녁 내내 아버지가 곁에 계실 거잖아요.”"]], [["“To be sure, so I shall.”", "“하기야, 그렇겠구나.”"]], [["“Suppose you take my father’s opinion, ma’am.”", "“아버지 의견을 여쭤보시죠, 어머니.”"]], [["“That’s well thought of.", "“그거 좋은 생각이구나."], ["So I will, Edmund.", "그러마, 에드먼드."], ["I will ask Sir Thomas, as soon as he comes in, whether I can do without her.”", "토머스 경이 들어오는 대로, 이 애 없이 내가 지낼 수 있을지 여쭤봐야겠다.”"]], [["“As you please, ma’am, on that head; but I meant my father’s opinion as to the propriety of the invitation’s being accepted or not; and I think he will consider it a right thing by Mrs.", "“그 점이야 어머니 마음대로 하세요. 하지만 제가 말한 건 이 초대를 받아들이는 게 온당한지 아버지 의견을 여쭤보시란 거였어요."], ["Grant, as well as by Fanny, that being the first invitation it should be accepted.”", "그리고 저는 아버지가, 패니한테도 그랜트 부인한테도, 이게 첫 초대인 만큼 받아들이는 게 옳은 일이라 여기실 거라고 봐요.”"]], [["“I do not know.", "“글쎄다."], ["We will ask him.", "여쭤보자꾸나."], ["But he will be very much surprised that Mrs. Grant should ask Fanny at all.”", "하지만 그랜트 부인이 패니를 부른다는 것 자체에 아주 놀라실 게다.”"]], [["There was nothing more to be said, or that could be said to any purpose, till Sir Thomas were present; but the subject involving, as it did, her own evening’s comfort for the morrow, was so much uppermost in Lady Bertram’s mind, that half an hour afterwards, on his looking in for a minute in his way from his plantation to his dressing-room, she called him back again, when he had almost closed the door, with “Sir Thomas, stop a moment—I have something to say to you.”", "토머스 경이 있을 때까지는 더 할 말도, 무슨 소용 있게 할 수 있는 말도 없었다. 그러나 그 화제는, 내일 저녁 제 편안함이 걸린 일인지라, 버트럼 부인의 마음에 어찌나 크게 자리 잡았던지, 반 시간 뒤 그가 농원에서 옷방으로 가는 길에 잠깐 들여다보자, 그가 거의 문을 닫으려는 참에 그녀가 그를 도로 불러 세웠다. “토머스 경, 잠깐만요—드릴 말씀이 있어요.”"]], [["Her tone of calm languor, for she never took the trouble of raising her voice, was always heard and attended to; and Sir Thomas came back.", "그 차분하고 나른한 어조는—그녀는 결코 목소리를 높이는 수고를 하지 않았으므로—늘 들리고 새겨졌다. 그리하여 토머스 경은 돌아왔다."], ["Her story began; and Fanny immediately slipped out of the room; for to hear herself the subject of any discussion with her uncle was more than her nerves could bear.", "그녀의 이야기가 시작되었고, 패니는 곧바로 방을 빠져나갔다. 자기가 삼촌과 무슨 의논거리가 되는 것을 듣는 일은 그 신경으로는 견딜 수 없는 것이었기 때문이다."], ["She was anxious, she knew—more anxious perhaps than she ought to be—for what was it after all whether she went or staid?", "그녀는 마음이 졸였다—어쩌면 마땅한 것보다 더 졸였다—따지고 보면 자기가 가든 남든 그게 무슨 대수랴?"], ["but if her uncle were to be a great while considering and deciding, and with very grave looks, and those grave looks directed to her, and at last decide against her, she might not be able to appear properly submissive and indifferent.", "그러나 만일 삼촌이 오래오래 곰곰이 따지고 정하며, 그것도 아주 근엄한 낯빛으로, 그 근엄한 눈길을 저에게 두고 있다가, 끝내 저에게 불리하게 정한다면, 그녀는 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 고분고분하고 무심한 낯을 지어 보이지 못할지도 몰랐다."], ["Her cause, meanwhile, went on well.", "그러는 사이 그녀의 청은 잘 굴러가고 있었다."], ["It began, on Lady Bertram’s part, with—“I have something to tell you that will surprise you.", "그것은 버트럼 부인 쪽에서 이렇게 시작되었다—“당신을 놀라게 할 얘기가 하나 있어요."], ["Mrs. Grant has asked Fanny to dinner.”", "그랜트 부인이 패니를 만찬에 불렀지 뭐예요.”"]], [["“Well,”", "“그래서?”"], ["said Sir Thomas, as if waiting more to accomplish the surprise.", "토머스 경이, 그 놀라움을 더 채우기를 기다리기라도 하는 듯 말했다."]], [["“Edmund wants her to go.", "“에드먼드는 이 애가 가기를 바라요."], ["But how can I spare her?”", "그런데 내가 어떻게 이 애 없이 지내죠?”"]], [["“She will be late,”", "“늦겠구려.”"], ["said Sir Thomas, taking out his watch; “but what is your difficulty?”", "토머스 경이 시계를 꺼내며 말했다. “그런데 무엇이 곤란하다는 거요?”"]], [["Edmund found himself obliged to speak and fill up the blanks in his mother’s story.", "에드먼드는 어머니 이야기의 빈 데를 메우려 나서 말할 수밖에 없음을 알았다."], ["He told the whole; and she had only to add, “So strange!", "그가 전부를 이야기했고, 그녀는 그저 이렇게 덧붙일 뿐이었다. “참 이상하죠!"], ["for Mrs. Grant never used to ask her.”", "그랜트 부인이 이 애를 부른 적이 없었는데.”"]], [["“But is it not very natural,”", "“하지만 아주 자연스러운 일 아닌가요,”"], ["observed Edmund, “that Mrs.", "에드먼드가 말했다."], ["Grant should wish to procure so agreeable a visitor for her sister?”", "“그랜트 부인이 제 아우를 위해 그토록 반가운 손님을 마련해 주고 싶어 하는 것이요?”"]], [["“Nothing can be more natural,”", "“그보다 더 자연스러운 일도 없지.”"], ["said Sir Thomas, after a short deliberation; “nor, were there no sister in the case, could anything, in my opinion, be more natural.", "잠깐 헤아린 뒤 토머스 경이 말했다. “설령 이 일에 아우가 없다 한들, 내 보기엔 그보다 더 자연스러운 일도 없을 거요."], ["Mrs. Grant’s shewing civility to Miss Price, to Lady Bertram’s niece, could never want explanation.", "그랜트 부인이 프라이스 양한테, 버트럼 부인의 조카딸한테 예의를 차리는 데야 무슨 설명이 필요하겠소."], ["The only surprise I can feel is, that this should be the first time of its being paid.", "내가 놀랄 만한 건 오직, 이게 그걸 차린 첫 번째라는 거지."], ["Fanny was perfectly right in giving only a conditional answer.", "패니가 조건부 대답만 한 건 아주 옳았소."], ["She appears to feel as she ought.", "이 애는 마땅한 대로 느끼는 모양이오."], ["But as I conclude that she must wish to go, since all young people like to be together, I can see no reason why she should be denied the indulgence.”", "하지만 젊은이들이란 다 함께 어울리기를 좋아하는 법이니 이 애도 가고 싶어 하는 게 틀림없다고 나는 보오. 그러니 이 즐거움을 마다케 할 까닭이 하나도 없구려.”"]], [["“But can I do without her, Sir Thomas?”", "“하지만 내가 이 애 없이 지낼 수 있을까요, 토머스 경?”"]], [["“Indeed I think you may.”", "“정말이지 지낼 수 있을 거요.”"]], [["“She always makes tea, you know, when my sister is not here.”", "“아우가 여기 없을 땐 늘 이 애가 차를 내잖아요.”"]], [["“Your sister, perhaps, may be prevailed on to spend the day with us, and I shall certainly be at home.”", "“아우님더러 우리와 하루 보내자고 청하면 응할지도 모르고, 나도 분명 집에 있을 테니.”"]], [["“Very well, then, Fanny may go, Edmund.”", "“그럼 됐네요, 패니는 가도 돼요, 에드먼드.”"]], [["The good news soon followed her.", "그 반가운 소식이 이내 그녀를 뒤따랐다."], ["Edmund knocked at her door in his way to his own.", "에드먼드가 제 방으로 가는 길에 그녀의 문을 두드린 것이다."]], [["“Well, Fanny, it is all happily settled, and without the smallest hesitation on your uncle’s side.", "“자, 패니, 다 잘 정해졌어. 삼촌 쪽에서 조금의 망설임도 없이 말이야."], ["He had but one opinion.", "삼촌은 오직 한 가지 의견뿐이셨어."], ["You are to go.”", "넌 가게 됐다.”"]], [["“Thank you, I am so glad,”", "“고마워요, 정말 너무 기뻐요.”가 패니의 절로 나온 대답이었다."], ["was Fanny’s instinctive reply; though when she had turned from him and shut the door, she could not help feeling, “And yet why should I be glad?", "그러나 그에게서 돌아서서 문을 닫고 나자, 그녀는 이런 생각을 떨칠 수 없었다. “그런데 왜 기뻐하지?"], ["for am I not certain of seeing or hearing something there to pain me?”", "거기서 나를 아프게 할 무언가를 보거나 듣게 될 게 뻔하지 않나?”"]], [["In spite of this conviction, however, she was glad.", "그러나 이런 확신에도 불구하고 그녀는 기뻤다."], ["Simple as such an engagement might appear in other eyes, it had novelty and importance in hers, for excepting the day at Sotherton, she had scarcely ever dined out before; and though now going only half a mile, and only to three people, still it was dining out, and all the little interests of preparation were enjoyments in themselves.", "그런 약속이 남들 눈에는 아무리 대수롭잖아 보일지언정, 그녀의 눈에는 새롭고 중대했다. 소더턴에서의 그날 말고는 여태 바깥에서 만찬을 든 적이 거의 없었기 때문이다."], ["She had neither sympathy nor assistance from those who ought to have entered into her feelings and directed her taste; for Lady Bertram never thought of being useful to anybody, and Mrs.", "그리고 이제 겨우 반 마일을 가는 것이고, 겨우 세 사람한테 가는 것이었으나, 그래도 그것은 바깥 만찬이었고, 채비의 온갖 자잘한 재미가 그 자체로 즐거움이었다."], ["Norris, when she came on the morrow, in consequence of an early call and invitation from Sir Thomas, was in a very ill humour, and seemed intent only on lessening her niece’s pleasure, both present and future, as much as possible.", "그녀의 감정에 마땅히 함께해 주고 그 취향을 이끌어 주었어야 할 이들에게서는 공감도 도움도 없었다. 버트럼 부인이야 누구에게든 쓸모가 될 생각을 결코 하지 않았고, 노리스 부인은, 이튿날 토머스 경의 이른 부름과 초대로 왔을 때, 심사가 아주 뒤틀린 채, 오직 제 조카딸의 즐거움을, 지금 것이든 앞으로의 것이든, 되도록 깎아내리는 데만 골몰한 듯했다."]], [["“Upon my word, Fanny, you are in high luck to meet with such attention and indulgence!", "“정말이지, 패니, 넌 이런 마음씀과 은혜를 입다니 팔자가 늘어졌구나! 너를 생각해 준 그랜트 부인한테도, 너를 보내 주는 이모한테도 아주 고맙게 여겨야 마땅하고, 이걸 무슨 대단한 일로 여겨야 해."], ["You ought to be very much obliged to Mrs.", "왜냐하면 나는 네가 이런 식으로 남들 자리에 끼거나, 애당초 바깥 만찬을 들거나 할 진짜 까닭은 하나도 없다는 걸 잘 알고 있기를 바라거든."], ["Grant for thinking of you, and to your aunt for letting you go, and you ought to look upon it as something extraordinary; for I hope you are aware that there is no real occasion for your going into company in this sort of way, or ever dining out at all; and it is what you must not depend upon ever being repeated.", "그리고 이건 앞으로 또 되풀이되리라 기대해서도 안 되는 일이야. 또 이 초대가 너한테 무슨 각별한 찬사로 뜻해진 거라 넘겨짚어서도 안 돼."], ["Nor must you be fancying that the invitation is meant as any particular compliment to you; the compliment is intended to your uncle and aunt and me. Mrs.", "그 찬사는 네 삼촌하고 이모하고 나한테 뜻해진 거니까."], ["Grant thinks it a civility due to us to take a little notice of you, or else it would never have come into her head, and you may be very certain that, if your cousin Julia had been at home, you would not have been asked at all.”", "그랜트 부인은 너를 좀 챙기는 게 우리한테 마땅한 예의라 여긴 거고, 그렇잖았으면 애당초 그런 생각도 안 떠올랐을 게다. 그리고 네 사촌 줄리아가 집에 있었더라면 넌 애당초 안 불렸을 게 아주 분명하고 말이지.”"]], [["Mrs. Norris had now so ingeniously done away all Mrs. Grant’s part of the favour, that Fanny, who found herself expected to speak, could only say that she was very much obliged to her aunt Bertram for sparing her, and that she was endeavouring to put her aunt’s evening work in such a state as to prevent her being missed.", "노리스 부인이 이제 그랜트 부인 몫의 호의를 어찌나 감쪽같이 지워 버렸던지, 무슨 말이든 해야 할 것 같던 패니는, 그저 저를 내주신 버트럼 이모한테 아주 고맙다는 것과, 이모의 저녁 일감을 저 없어도 아쉽잖게끔 미리 손봐 두려 애쓰고 있다는 말밖에 할 수 없었다."]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["depend upon it, your aunt can do very well without you, or you would not be allowed to go.", "걱정 마라, 네 이모야 너 없이도 아주 잘 지내지, 안 그랬으면 널 보내 주지도 않았을 게다."], ["I shall be here, so you may be quite easy about your aunt.", "내가 여기 있을 테니, 이모 걱정일랑 아주 놓으렴."], ["And I hope you will have a very agreeable day, and find it all mighty delightful.", "그리고 나는 네가 아주 즐거운 하루를 보내고, 그 모든 게 무척 재미나기를 바란다."], ["But I must observe that five is the very awkwardest of all possible numbers to sit down to table; and I cannot but be surprised that such an elegant lady as Mrs.", "하지만 나는 한마디 안 할 수가 없구나, 다섯이란 식탁에 둘러앉기엔 있을 수 있는 수 중에 가장 어정쩡한 수라는 거 말이다."], ["Grant should not contrive better! And round their enormous great wide table, too, which fills up the room so dreadfully!", "그리고 그랜트 부인처럼 우아한 부인이 어째서 더 잘 헤아리지 못했는지 놀랍지 않을 수가 없어!"], ["Had the doctor been contented to take my dining-table when I came away, as anybody in their senses would have done, instead of having that absurd new one of his own, which is wider, literally wider than the dinner-table here, how infinitely better it would have been!", "게다가 그 방을 지독히도 꽉 채우는 그 엄청나게 크고 널찍한 식탁을 빙 둘러서라니! 그 박사가 내가 떠나올 때, 제정신인 사람이라면 누구나 그랬을 것처럼, 내 식탁을 가져갔더라면—그 터무니없는 제 새 것, 여기 식탁보다 넓은, 말 그대로 넓은 그것 대신에 말이야—얼마나 한없이 나았을까!"], ["and how much more he would have been respected!", "그리고 얼마나 더 존경받았을까!"], ["for people are never respected when they step out of their proper sphere.", "사람이란 제 마땅한 분수를 벗어나면 결코 존경받는 법이 없거든."], ["Remember that, Fanny.", "그걸 명심해라, 패니."], ["Five—only five to be sitting round that table.", "다섯이라니—고작 다섯이 그 식탁을 둘러앉다니."], ["However, you will have dinner enough on it for ten, I dare say.”", "하기야 그 위엔 열 사람 몫 만찬이 차려질 게다, 아마.”"]], [["Mrs. Norris fetched breath, and went on again.", "노리스 부인은 숨을 돌리고 다시 말을 이었다."]], [["“The nonsense and folly of people’s stepping out of their rank and trying to appear above themselves, makes me think it right to give you a hint, Fanny, now that you are going into company without any of us; and I do beseech and entreat you not to be putting yourself forward, and talking and giving your opinion as if you were one of your cousins—as if you were dear Mrs.", "“사람들이 제 신분을 벗어나 제 분수 위로 올라 보이려 드는 그 어리석고 미련한 짓을 보면, 나는 너한테 한마디 일러 주는 게 옳다 싶구나, 패니, 이제 네가 우리 중 아무도 없이 남들 자리에 끼러 가는 마당이니 말이다. 그리고 나는 네가 나서지 말고, 마치 네 사촌들 중 하나인 양—마치 사랑하는 러시워스 부인이나 줄리아인 양—이러쿵저러쿵 네 의견을 늘어놓지 말기를 간곡히 빌고 또 빈다."], ["Rushworth or Julia. That will never do, believe me.", "그런 짓은 결코 안 될 말이야, 내 말 믿어라."], ["Remember, wherever you are, you must be the lowest and last; and though Miss Crawford is in a manner at home at the Parsonage, you are not to be taking place of her.", "명심해, 네가 어디 있든, 넌 가장 낮고 가장 마지막이어야 해. 그리고 크로퍼드 양이야 목사관에서 어느 모로 제 집이나 다름없다지만, 넌 그녀보다 앞자리를 차지해선 안 돼."], ["And as to coming away at night, you are to stay just as long as Edmund chuses.", "그리고 밤에 돌아오는 것으로 말하면, 넌 에드먼드가 정하는 딱 그만큼만 있어야 해."], ["Leave him to settle that.”", "그건 그 애더러 정하게 두렴.”"]], [["“Yes, ma’am, I should not think of anything else.”", "“네, 이모, 저야 달리 생각도 안 해요.”"]], [["“And if it should rain, which I think exceedingly likely, for I never saw it more threatening for a wet evening in my life, you must manage as well as you can, and not be expecting the carriage to be sent for you.", "“그리고 만일 비가 온다면, 나는 그럴 가망이 아주 크다고 보는데, 내 평생 비 오는 저녁 낌새가 이보다 더한 걸 본 적이 없거든—넌 되도록 알아서 하고, 마차를 보내 주려니 기대해선 안 된다."], ["I certainly do not go home to-night, and, therefore, the carriage will not be out on my account; so you must make up your mind to what may happen, and take your things accordingly.”", "나는 오늘 밤 분명 집에 안 가니까, 마차가 나 때문에 나갈 일은 없어. 그러니 넌 무슨 일이 생길지 각오하고, 거기 맞춰 채비를 하렴.”"]], [["Her niece thought it perfectly reasonable.", "그녀의 조카딸은 그것이 아주 지당하다 여겼다."], ["She rated her own claims to comfort as low even as Mrs.", "제가 누릴 편안함에 대한 요구를 그녀는 노리스 부인 못지않게 낮게 매겼다."], ["Norris could; and when Sir Thomas soon afterwards, just opening the door, said, “Fanny, at what time would you have the carriage come round?”", "그리고 이내 토머스 경이 문을 막 열며 “패니, 마차를 몇 시에 대령하면 좋겠느냐?”"], ["she felt a degree of astonishment which made it impossible for her to speak.", "하고 말하자, 그녀는 도무지 말이 안 나올 만큼 놀랐다."]], [["“My dear Sir Thomas!” cried Mrs.", "“아이참, 토머스 경!”"], ["Norris, red with anger, “Fanny can walk.”", "노리스 부인이 노기로 벌게져 외쳤다. “패니는 걸어가면 돼요.”"]], [["“Walk!”", "“걸어가?”"], ["repeated Sir Thomas, in a tone of most unanswerable dignity, and coming farther into the room.", "토머스 경이 도무지 대꾸할 수 없는 위엄의 어조로 되뇌며, 방 안으로 좀 더 들어섰다."], ["“My niece walk to a dinner engagement at this time of the year!", "“내 조카딸이 이맘때 만찬 약속에 걸어가다니!"], ["Will twenty minutes after four suit you?”", "네 시 이십 분이면 되겠느냐?”"]], [["“Yes, sir,” was Fanny’s humble answer, given with the feelings almost of a criminal towards Mrs.", "“네, 삼촌.”이 패니의 겸손한 대답이었는데, 노리스 부인에게는 거의 죄인 같은 심정으로 건네진 것이었다."], ["Norris; and not bearing to remain with her in what might seem a state of triumph, she followed her uncle out of the room, having staid behind him only long enough to hear these words spoken in angry agitation—", "그리고 승리한 것처럼 보일 만한 상태로 그녀와 함께 남아 있기가 괴로워, 그녀는 삼촌을 따라 방을 나갔으니, 노기에 찬 동요 속에 이런 말이 오가는 것을 들을 만큼만 그의 뒤에 머물렀다—"]], [["“Quite unnecessary!", "“도무지 필요 없는 일인데!"], ["a great deal too kind!", "지나치게 친절하시지!"], ["But Edmund goes; true, it is upon Edmund’s account.", "하지만 에드먼드가 가니까, 그래, 에드먼드 때문이지."], ["I observed he was hoarse on Thursday night.”", "목요일 밤에 그 애가 목이 쉰 걸 내가 봤거든.”"]], [["But this could not impose on Fanny.", "그러나 이것은 패니를 속일 수 없었다."], ["She felt that the carriage was for herself, and herself alone: and her uncle’s consideration of her, coming immediately after such representations from her aunt, cost her some tears of gratitude when she was alone.", "그녀는 그 마차가 저를 위한, 오직 저 하나를 위한 것임을 느꼈다. 그리고 이모의 그런 말들 바로 뒤에 이어진 삼촌의 그 마음씀은, 홀로 있을 때 그녀에게서 고마움의 눈물 몇 방울을 자아냈다."]], [["The coachman drove round to a minute; another minute brought down the gentleman; and as the lady had, with a most scrupulous fear of being late, been many minutes seated in the drawing-room, Sir Thomas saw them off in as good time as his own correctly punctual habits required.", "마부는 딱 시간 맞춰 마차를 몰아 왔다. 또 일 분 만에 신사가 내려왔다. 그리고 그 숙녀는 늦을까 봐 더없이 노심초사하며 벌써 몇 분이나 응접실에 앉아 있던 터라, 토머스 경은 제 정확히 시간 지키는 버릇이 요구하는 만큼 넉넉한 때에 그들을 배웅했다."]], [["“Now I must look at you, Fanny,”", "“자, 이제 널 좀 봐야겠다, 패니.” 에드먼드가 정 많은 오라비의 친절한 미소로 말했다."], ["said Edmund, with the kind smile of an affectionate brother, “and tell you how I like you; and as well as I can judge by this light, you look very nicely indeed.", "“그리고 네가 얼마나 마음에 드는지 말해 줘야지. 이 불빛으로 내가 판단할 수 있는 한, 넌 정말이지 아주 곱구나."], ["What have you got on?”", "뭘 입은 거니?”"]], [["“The new dress that my uncle was so good as to give me on my cousin’s marriage.", "“사촌 언니 혼인 때 삼촌이 고맙게도 주신 그 새 옷이에요."], ["I hope it is not too fine; but I thought I ought to wear it as soon as I could, and that I might not have such another opportunity all the winter.", "너무 화려하진 않았으면 좋겠는데, 되도록 빨리 입어야겠다 싶었어요, 겨우내 이런 기회가 또 없을 것 같아서요."], ["I hope you do not think me too fine.”", "저를 너무 화려하다 여기진 않으셨으면 해요.”"]], [["“A woman can never be too fine while she is all in white.", "“온통 하양으로 차려입은 여자는 결코 지나치게 화려할 수 없어."], ["No, I see no finery about you; nothing but what is perfectly proper.", "아니, 네게 화려한 데라곤 안 보이는걸. 더없이 온당한 것 말고는 아무것도 없어."], ["Your gown seems very pretty.", "네 가운이 아주 예뻐 보인다."], ["I like these glossy spots.", "이 반들거리는 점무늬가 마음에 들어."], ["Has not Miss Crawford a gown something the same?”", "크로퍼드 양한테도 이거하고 좀 비슷한 가운이 있지 않니?”"]], [["In approaching the Parsonage they passed close by the stable-yard and coach-house.", "목사관에 다가가며 그들은 마구간 마당과 마차 창고 바로 옆을 지났다."]], [["“Heyday!”", "“이런!”"], ["said Edmund, “here’s company, here’s a carriage!", "에드먼드가 말했다. “손님이 있네, 마차가 있어!"], ["who have they got to meet us?”", "우릴 맞으러 누굴 데려왔지?”"], ["And letting down the side-glass to distinguish, “’Tis Crawford’s, Crawford’s barouche, I protest!", "그러고는 알아보려 옆 유리창을 내리며, “크로퍼드 거야, 크로퍼드의 사륜 포장마차라니까!"], ["There are his own two men pushing it back into its old quarters.", "그 사람네 하인 둘이 그걸 제자리로 도로 밀어 넣고 있어."], ["He is here, of course.", "물론 그 사람이 온 거지."], ["This is quite a surprise, Fanny.", "이거 뜻밖인걸, 패니."], ["I shall be very glad to see him.”", "나야 그 사람을 보게 돼 아주 반가워.”"]], [["There was no occasion, there was no time for Fanny to say how very differently she felt; but the idea of having such another to observe her was a great increase of the trepidation with which she performed the very awful ceremony of walking into the drawing-room.", "자기가 얼마나 아주 다르게 느끼는지 패니가 말할 겨를도, 말할 짬도 없었다. 그러나 저를 지켜볼 그런 사람이 또 하나 있다는 생각은, 응접실로 걸어 들어가는 그 더없이 두려운 의식을 치르는 그녀의 떨림을 크게 키웠다."]], [["In the drawing-room Mr. Crawford certainly was, having been just long enough arrived to be ready for dinner; and the smiles and pleased looks of the three others standing round him, shewed how welcome was his sudden resolution of coming to them for a few days on leaving Bath.", "응접실에 크로퍼드 씨는 과연 있었으니, 만찬을 들 채비가 될 만큼만 딱 방금 도착한 참이었다."], ["A very cordial meeting passed between him and Edmund; and with the exception of Fanny, the pleasure was general; and even to her there might be some advantage in his presence, since every addition to the party must rather forward her favourite indulgence of being suffered to sit silent and unattended to.", "그리고 그를 둘러싼 나머지 세 사람의 미소와 흐뭇한 낯빛은, 배스를 떠나며 며칠 그들에게 오기로 한 그의 갑작스러운 결심이 얼마나 반가운지 여실히 드러냈다. 그와 에드먼드 사이에 아주 정다운 인사가 오갔다."], ["She was soon aware of this herself; for though she must submit, as her own propriety of mind directed, in spite of her aunt Norris’s opinion, to being the principal lady in company, and to all the little distinctions consequent thereon, she found, while they were at table, such a happy flow of conversation prevailing, in which she was not required to take any part—there was so much to be said between the brother and sister about Bath, so much between the two young men about hunting, so much of politics between Mr.", "그리고 패니만 빼고 그 기쁨은 두루 퍼진 것이었다. 그리고 그녀에게조차 그가 온 것에 얼마간 이로운 점이 있었을지 모르니, 무리에 사람이 하나 더 늘 때마다 오히려, 잠자코 아무도 챙기지 않는 채로 앉아 있게 두어지는 그 애지중지하는 즐거움이 더 앞당겨졌기 때문이다."], ["Crawford and Dr. Grant, and of everything and all together between Mr.", "그녀 자신도 이내 그것을 알아챘다."], ["Crawford and Mrs. Grant, as to leave her the fairest prospect of having only to listen in quiet, and of passing a very agreeable day.", "노리스 이모의 의견에도 불구하고, 제 마음의 온당함이 이끄는 대로, 그 자리의 주된 숙녀가 되는 것과 그에 따르는 온갖 자잘한 대접에 따라야 했으나, 식탁에 앉아 있는 동안 그녀가 낄 필요가 없는 그토록 흐뭇한 이야기의 흐름이 오가는 것을 알았기 때문이다—남매 사이엔 배스를 두고 할 이야기가 그토록 많았고, 두 젊은 사내 사이엔 사냥을 두고 그토록 많았으며, 크로퍼드 씨와 그랜트 박사 사이엔 정치 이야기가 그토록 많았고, 크로퍼드 씨와 그랜트 부인 사이엔 온갖 것과 모든 것이 뒤섞여 그토록 많았으니, 그녀에게는 그저 조용히 듣기만 하며 아주 즐거운 하루를 보낼 더없이 좋은 형편이 남겨진 것이다."], ["She could not compliment the newly arrived gentleman, however, with any appearance of interest, in a scheme for extending his stay at Mansfield, and sending for his hunters from Norfolk, which, suggested by Dr.", "그러나 그녀는 새로 도착한 그 신사에게, 맨스필드 체류를 늘리고 노퍽에서 제 사냥말들을 불러오라는 계획에 대해 무슨 관심 있는 낯빛으로도 맞장구쳐 줄 수가 없었다."], ["Grant, advised by Edmund, and warmly urged by the two sisters, was soon in possession of his mind, and which he seemed to want to be encouraged even by her to resolve on.", "그랜트 박사가 내놓고 에드먼드가 권하고 두 자매가 뜨겁게 다그친 그 계획은 이내 그의 마음을 사로잡았고, 그는 그녀에게서까지 마음을 굳히도록 부추김을 받고 싶어 하는 듯했다."], ["Her opinion was sought as to the probable continuance of the open weather, but her answers were as short and indifferent as civility allowed.", "날씨가 계속 맑을 가망을 두고 그녀의 의견이 구해졌으나, 그녀의 대답은 예의가 허락하는 한 짧고 무심했다."], ["She could not wish him to stay, and would much rather not have him speak to her.", "그녀는 그가 머물기를 바랄 수 없었고, 차라리 그가 저에게 말을 걸지 않았으면 하는 마음이 훨씬 컸다."]], [["Her two absent cousins, especially Maria, were much in her thoughts on seeing him; but no embarrassing remembrance affected his spirits.", "그를 보니 떠나 있는 두 사촌이, 특히 마리아가 그녀 마음에 많이 떠올랐다. 그러나 그 어떤 겸연쩍은 기억도 그의 기분을 건드리지 않았다."], ["Here he was again on the same ground where all had passed before, and apparently as willing to stay and be happy without the Miss Bertrams, as if he had never known Mansfield in any other state.", "여기 그가 다시, 그 모든 일이 벌어졌던 바로 그 자리에 와 있었고, 마치 맨스필드를 다른 어떤 상태로도 알아 본 적이 없다는 듯, 버트럼 자매 없이도 기꺼이 머물러 행복해하는 듯했다."], ["She heard them spoken of by him only in a general way, till they were all re-assembled in the drawing-room, when Edmund, being engaged apart in some matter of business with Dr. Grant, which seemed entirely to engross them, and Mrs.", "그녀는 그가 그들을 그저 두루뭉술하게 입에 올리는 것만 듣다가, 다들 응접실에 다시 모였을 때—에드먼드가 그랜트 박사와 무슨 볼일로 따로 떨어져, 그것에 온통 골몰한 듯 보이고, 그랜트 부인은 찻상에 매여 있을 때—그가 다른 누이에게 그들을 좀 더 자세히 이야기하기 시작하는 것을 들었다."], ["Grant occupied at the tea-table, he began talking of them with more particularity to his other sister.", "패니가 그를 여간 밉지 않게 만드는 뜻있는 미소를 지으며, 그가 말했다."], ["With a significant smile, which made Fanny quite hate him, he said, “So!", "“그래!"], ["Rushworth and his fair bride are at Brighton, I understand; happy man!”", "러시워스와 그 어여쁜 신부가 브라이턴에 가 있다면서, 복 많은 사내지!”"]], [["“Yes, they have been there about a fortnight, Miss Price, have they not?", "“네, 거기 간 지 두 주쯤 됐죠, 프라이스 양, 안 그래요?"], ["And Julia is with them.”", "그리고 줄리아도 같이 있고요.”"]], [["“And Mr. Yates, I presume, is not far off.”", "“그리고 예이츠 씨도, 짐작건대 멀지 않은 데 있겠지.”"]], [["“Mr. Yates!", "“예이츠 씨라니!"], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["we hear nothing of Mr. Yates.", "우린 예이츠 씨 소식은 하나도 못 들어요."], ["I do not imagine he figures much in the letters to Mansfield Park; do you, Miss Price?", "그 사람이 맨스필드 파크로 오는 편지에 자주 오르내리진 않을걸요. 안 그래요, 프라이스 양?"], ["I think my friend Julia knows better than to entertain her father with Mr. Yates.”", "내 벗 줄리아가 예이츠 씨 얘기로 제 아버지를 즐겁게 해 드릴 만큼 철없진 않을 것 같은데.”"]], [["“Poor Rushworth and his two-and-forty speeches!”", "“딱한 러시워스와 그 마흔두 마디 대사라니!”"], ["continued Crawford.", "크로퍼드가 말을 이었다."], ["“Nobody can ever forget them.", "“아무도 그걸 잊을 수 없을 거야."], ["Poor fellow!", "딱한 친구!"], ["I see him now—his toil and his despair.", "지금도 눈에 선해—그 안간힘과 그 절망 말이야."], ["Well, I am much mistaken if his lovely Maria will ever want him to make two-and-forty speeches to her”; adding, with a momentary seriousness, “She is too good for him—much too good.”", "뭐, 그 사랑스러운 마리아가 그더러 저한테 마흔두 마디 대사를 늘어놓아 달라 바랄 일은 결코 없으리라는 데, 내가 크게 틀리지 않을 거야.” 하며 그는 한순간 진지해져 덧붙였다."], ["And then changing his tone again to one of gentle gallantry, and addressing Fanny, he said, “You were Mr.", "“그 여잔 그 사내한테 과분해—너무나 과분하지.” 그러고는 다시 어조를 부드러운 정중함으로 바꾸어, 패니에게 향해 말했다."], ["Rushworth’s best friend.", "“프라이스 양이 러시워스 씨의 가장 좋은 벗이었죠."], ["Your kindness and patience can never be forgotten, your indefatigable patience in trying to make it possible for him to learn his part—in trying to give him a brain which nature had denied—to mix up an understanding for him out of the superfluity of your own!", "양의 친절과 참을성은 결코 잊힐 수 없을 거예요, 그가 제 배역을 익힐 수 있게 만들어 주려던 그 지칠 줄 모르는 참을성 말이에요—자연이 안 준 두뇌를 그에게 주려던, 양 자신의 넘치는 데서 이해력을 덜어 그에게 섞어 주려던 그 참을성이요!"], ["He might not have sense enough himself to estimate your kindness, but I may venture to say that it had honour from all the rest of the party.”", "그 사람이야 제 딴엔 양의 친절을 헤아릴 분별이 없었을지 몰라도, 나머지 모두한테선 그게 기림을 받았다고 나는 감히 말할 수 있어요.”"]], [["Fanny coloured, and said nothing.", "패니는 얼굴을 붉히고 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["“It is as a dream, a pleasant dream!”", "“그건 한 편의 꿈, 즐거운 꿈 같아요!”"], ["he exclaimed, breaking forth again, after a few minutes’ musing.", "몇 분 생각에 잠겼다가 그가 다시 터뜨리듯 외쳤다."], ["“I shall always look back on our theatricals with exquisite pleasure.", "“나는 우리 연극을 늘 더없는 즐거움으로 돌아보게 될 거예요."], ["There was such an interest, such an animation, such a spirit diffused.", "그런 흥, 그런 활기, 그런 기운이 두루 퍼졌었죠."], ["Everybody felt it.", "다들 그걸 느꼈어요."], ["We were all alive.", "우린 다 살아 있었고요."], ["There was employment, hope, solicitude, bustle, for every hour of the day.", "하루의 매시간마다 할 일이, 희망이, 마음 졸임이, 부산함이 있었어요."], ["Always some little objection, some little doubt, some little anxiety to be got over.", "늘 넘어야 할 자그마한 걸림돌이, 자그마한 미심쩍음이, 자그마한 근심이 있었죠."], ["I never was happier.”", "나는 그때보다 더 행복한 적이 없어요.”"]], [["With silent indignation Fanny repeated to herself, “Never happier!—never happier than when doing what you must know was not justifiable!—never happier than when behaving so dishonourably and unfeelingly!", "말없는 분함으로 패니는 속으로 되뇌었다. “더 행복한 적이 없다니!—떳떳잖은 줄 뻔히 알았을 짓을 하면서보다 더 행복한 적이 없다니!—그토록 부끄럽고 무정하게 굴면서보다 더 행복한 적이 없다니!"], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["what a corrupted mind!”", "어쩜 저리 썩어 빠진 마음일까!”"]], [["“We were unlucky, Miss Price,”", "“우린 운이 나빴어요, 프라이스 양.”"], ["he continued, in a lower tone, to avoid the possibility of being heard by Edmund, and not at all aware of her feelings, “we certainly were very unlucky.", "그가, 에드먼드에게 들릴세라 좀 더 목소리를 낮추어, 그리고 그녀의 심경은 조금도 알아채지 못한 채 말을 이었다. “우린 분명 아주 운이 나빴죠."], ["Another week, only one other week, would have been enough for us.", "한 주만 더, 딱 한 주만 더 있었어도 우리한텐 넉넉했을 거예요."], ["I think if we had had the disposal of events—if Mansfield Park had had the government of the winds just for a week or two, about the equinox, there would have been a difference.", "내 생각엔, 우리가 일들을 마음대로 할 수 있었더라면—맨스필드 파크가 딱 한두 주, 주야평분 무렵의 바람을 다스릴 수 있었더라면, 사정이 달랐을 거예요."], ["Not that we would have endangered his safety by any tremendous weather—but only by a steady contrary wind, or a calm.", "무슨 무시무시한 날씨로 그분 안전을 위태롭게 하자는 게 아니라—그저 한결같은 맞바람이나 잔잔한 바람으로요."], ["I think, Miss Price, we would have indulged ourselves with a week’s calm in the Atlantic at that season.”", "내 생각엔, 프라이스 양, 우린 그 철에 대서양에서 한 주쯤 잔잔한 바람을 누렸을 거예요.”"]], [["He seemed determined to be answered; and Fanny, averting her face, said, with a firmer tone than usual, “As far as I am concerned, sir, I would not have delayed his return for a day.", "그는 기어이 대답을 들을 작정인 듯했다. 그리고 패니는 얼굴을 돌린 채, 여느 때보다 단단한 어조로 말했다."], ["My uncle disapproved it all so entirely when he did arrive, that in my opinion everything had gone quite far enough.”", "“저로 말하면요, 씨, 저는 그분의 귀환을 하루도 늦추지 않았을 거예요. 삼촌이 도착하셨을 때 그 모든 걸 어찌나 온통 못마땅해하셨던지, 제 생각엔 모든 게 이미 갈 데까지 충분히 갔던걸요.”"]], [["She had never spoken so much at once to him in her life before, and never so angrily to any one; and when her speech was over, she trembled and blushed at her own daring.", "그녀가 평생 그에게 한 번에 그토록 많이 말한 적이 없었고, 누구에게든 그토록 성나게 말한 적도 없었다. 그리고 제 말이 끝나자 그녀는 제 대담함에 떨며 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["He was surprised; but after a few moments’ silent consideration of her, replied in a calmer, graver tone, and as if the candid result of conviction, “I believe you are right.", "그는 놀랐다. 그러나 몇 순간 말없이 그녀를 헤아리더니, 좀 더 차분하고 진중한 어조로, 마치 확신에서 우러난 솔직한 결론인 양 대답했다."], ["It was more pleasant than prudent.", "“양 말이 옳은 것 같아요."], ["We were getting too noisy.”", "슬기롭기보다 즐거운 쪽이었죠."], ["And then turning the conversation, he would have engaged her on some other subject, but her answers were so shy and reluctant that he could not advance in any.", "우린 너무 시끄러워지고 있었어요.” 그러고는 화제를 돌려, 그녀를 다른 이야기에 끌어들이려 했으나, 그녀의 대답이 어찌나 수줍고 마지못한 것이던지 그는 어느 쪽으로도 나아갈 수 없었다."]], [["Miss Crawford, who had been repeatedly eyeing Dr.", "그랜트 박사와 에드먼드를 거듭 눈여겨보던 크로퍼드 양이, 이제 소견을 냈다."], ["Grant and Edmund, now observed, “Those gentlemen must have some very interesting point to discuss.”", "“저 신사분들은 뭔가 아주 흥미로운 걸 의논하고 있나 봐요.”"]], [["“The most interesting in the world,”", "“세상에서 가장 흥미로운 거지.”"], ["replied her brother—“how to make money; how to turn a good income into a better.", "그 오라비가 대답했다. “돈 버는 법, 좋은 수입을 더 나은 수입으로 굴리는 법 말이야."], ["Dr. Grant is giving Bertram instructions about the living he is to step into so soon.", "그랜트 박사가 버트럼한테, 곧 물려받을 그 성직록에 대해 일러 주고 있는 거야."], ["I find he takes orders in a few weeks.", "알아보니 몇 주 뒤면 서품을 받는다더군."], ["They were at it in the dining-parlour.", "식당에서도 그 얘길 하고 있더라니까."], ["I am glad to hear Bertram will be so well off.", "버트럼이 그리 넉넉해진다니 반갑구먼."], ["He will have a very pretty income to make ducks and drakes with, and earned without much trouble.", "물수제비 뜨듯 펑펑 써도 될 아주 짭짤한 수입을, 그것도 별 수고 없이 얻는 셈이니까. 내 짐작엔 연 칠백 파운드는 못 되진 않을 거야."], ["I apprehend he will not have less than seven hundred a year.", "연 칠백 파운드면 작은아들한테야 근사한 거지."], ["Seven hundred a year is a fine thing for a younger brother; and as of course he will still live at home, it will be all for his menus plaisirs; and a sermon at Christmas and Easter, I suppose, will be the sum total of sacrifice.”", "그리고 물론 그는 여전히 집에서 지낼 테니, 그게 죄다 제 자잘한 즐길거리에 쓰일 테고. 성탄과 부활절에 설교 한 번씩, 그게 아마 바치는 것의 전부일 거야.”"]], [["His sister tried to laugh off her feelings by saying, “Nothing amuses me more than the easy manner with which everybody settles the abundance of those who have a great deal less than themselves.", "그 누이는 이렇게 말하며 제 감정을 웃어넘기려 했다. “저마다 저보다 훨씬 덜 가진 이들의 넉넉함을 태평하게 셈해 대는 그 태도만큼 나를 웃기는 것도 없어."], ["You would look rather blank, Henry, if your menus plaisirs were to be limited to seven hundred a year.”", "오빠도 좀 난감한 낯빛이 될걸, 오빠 자잘한 즐길거리가 연 칠백 파운드로 묶인다면 말이야.”"]], [["“Perhaps I might; but all that you know is entirely comparative.", "“그럴지도 모르지. 하지만 그거야 다 알다시피 순전히 견주기 나름이야."], ["Birthright and habit must settle the business.", "타고난 권리와 버릇이 그걸 정하는 거지."], ["Bertram is certainly well off for a cadet of even a baronet’s family.", "버트럼은 준남작 집안의 차남치고도 분명 넉넉한 형편이야."], ["By the time he is four or five and twenty he will have seven hundred a year, and nothing to do for it.”", "스물네댓 살이 될 무렵이면 연 칠백 파운드가 생기고, 그걸 위해 할 일이라곤 아무것도 없을 테니.”"]], [["Miss Crawford could have said that there would be a something to do and to suffer for it, which she could not think lightly of; but she checked herself and let it pass; and tried to look calm and unconcerned when the two gentlemen shortly afterwards joined them.", "크로퍼드 양은, 그것을 위해 하고 견뎌야 할 무언가가 있으리라고, 그리고 그것을 자기는 가볍게 여길 수 없다고 말할 수도 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 스스로를 다잡아 그냥 넘겼다. 그리고 이내 두 신사가 그들에게 합류하자 차분하고 무심한 낯을 지으려 애썼다."]], [["“Bertram,”", "“버트럼,”"], ["said Henry Crawford, “I shall make a point of coming to Mansfield to hear you preach your first sermon.", "헨리 크로퍼드가 말했다. “나는 자네 첫 설교를 들으러 꼭 맨스필드에 오겠네."], ["I shall come on purpose to encourage a young beginner.", "풋내기를 북돋아 주려고 일부러 올 거야."], ["When is it to be?", "언제인가?"], ["Miss Price, will not you join me in encouraging your cousin?", "프라이스 양, 양도 나와 함께 사촌을 북돋아 주지 않겠어요?"], ["Will not you engage to attend with your eyes steadily fixed on him the whole time—as I shall do—not to lose a word; or only looking off just to note down any sentence preeminently beautiful?", "그 내내 그에게 눈을 지그시 박고—나처럼 말이에요—한마디도 놓치지 않기로 약속하지 않겠어요? 아니면 유난히 아름다운 문장을 받아 적으려 잠깐 눈을 떼는 것만 빼고요?"], ["We will provide ourselves with tablets and a pencil.", "우리 메모판이랑 연필을 챙겨 갑시다. 언제인가?"], ["When will it be? You must preach at Mansfield, you know, that Sir Thomas and Lady Bertram may hear you.”", "토머스 경과 버트럼 부인이 들으실 수 있게, 자네 맨스필드에서 설교해야 하네, 알겠나.”"]], [["“I shall keep clear of you, Crawford, as long as I can,”", "“나는 되도록 오래 자네를 피할 걸세, 크로퍼드.”"], ["said Edmund; “for you would be more likely to disconcert me, and I should be more sorry to see you trying at it than almost any other man.”", "에드먼드가 말했다. “자네야말로 나를 당황케 하기 십상일 테고, 나는 다른 거의 누구보다도 자네가 그러려 드는 걸 보는 게 더 싫을 테니까.”"]], [["“Will he not feel this?”", "“저이는 이걸 못 느낄까?”"], ["thought Fanny.", "패니는 생각했다."], ["“No, he can feel nothing as he ought.”", "“아니, 저이는 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 아무것도 못 느끼지.”"]], [["The party being now all united, and the chief talkers attracting each other, she remained in tranquillity; and as a whist-table was formed after tea—formed really for the amusement of Dr.", "이제 무리가 다 모이고, 말 많은 이들끼리 서로 끌리니, 그녀는 평온 속에 남았다."], ["Grant, by his attentive wife, though it was not to be supposed so—and Miss Crawford took her harp, she had nothing to do but to listen; and her tranquillity remained undisturbed the rest of the evening, except when Mr.", "그리고 차를 마신 뒤 휘스트 판이 벌어지자—겉으론 그리 안 보였으나 실은 세심한 아내가 그랜트 박사를 즐겁게 해 주려 벌인 판이었다—크로퍼드 양이 제 하프를 잡으니, 그녀는 그저 듣기만 하면 되었다."], ["Crawford now and then addressed to her a question or observation, which she could not avoid answering.", "그리고 그날 저녁 내내 그녀의 평온은 흐트러지지 않았으니, 다만 크로퍼드 씨가 이따금 그녀에게 물음이나 소견을 건네어 그녀가 대답하지 않을 수 없을 때만 빼고서였다."], ["Miss Crawford was too much vexed by what had passed to be in a humour for anything but music.", "크로퍼드 양은 그동안 있었던 일에 너무 속이 상해 음악 말고는 아무것도 할 기분이 아니었다."], ["With that she soothed herself and amused her friend.", "그녀는 그것으로 저를 달래고 제 벗을 즐겁게 했다."]], [["The assurance of Edmund’s being so soon to take orders, coming upon her like a blow that had been suspended, and still hoped uncertain and at a distance, was felt with resentment and mortification.", "에드먼드가 그토록 곧 서품을 받으리라는 그 확실한 소식은, 미뤄져 있던, 그러면서 여전히 불확실하고 먼 것이기를 바라던 일격처럼 그녀에게 닥쳐, 분함과 무안함으로 느껴졌다. 그녀는 그에게 몹시 화가 났다."], ["She was very angry with him.", "그녀는 제 영향력이 더 크다 여겼었다."], ["She had thought her influence more.", "그녀는 정말로 그를 마음에 두기 시작했었다."], ["She had begun to think of him; she felt that she had, with great regard, with almost decided intentions; but she would now meet him with his own cool feelings.", "자기가 그러기 시작했음을, 크나큰 정으로, 거의 마음을 굳힌 뜻으로 그랬음을 그녀는 느꼈다. 그러나 이제 그녀는 그의 그 싸늘한 감정으로 그를 맞을 참이었다."], ["It was plain that he could have no serious views, no true attachment, by fixing himself in a situation which he must know she would never stoop to.", "자기가 결코 몸 굽혀 받아들이지 않으리란 걸 뻔히 알 그런 처지에 스스로를 붙박음으로써, 그가 아무런 진지한 뜻도, 참된 정도 품을 수 없음이 분명했다."], ["She would learn to match him in his indifference.", "그녀는 그의 무심함에 맞먹게끔 배우려 했다."], ["She would henceforth admit his attentions without any idea beyond immediate amusement.", "이제부터 그녀는 당장의 재미 말고는 아무 생각 없이 그의 살가움을 받아들이려 했다."], ["If he could so command his affections, hers should do her no harm.", "만일 그가 그토록 제 정을 다스릴 수 있다면, 그녀의 정도 그녀를 해쳐선 안 되었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXIV", "# 제24장"]], [["Henry Crawford had quite made up his mind by the next morning to give another fortnight to Mansfield, and having sent for his hunters, and written a few lines of explanation to the Admiral, he looked round at his sister as he sealed and threw the letter from him, and seeing the coast clear of the rest of the family, said, with a smile, “And how do you think I mean to amuse myself, Mary, on the days that I do not hunt? I am grown too old to go out more than three times a week; but I have a plan for the intermediate days, and what do you think it is?”", "헨리 크로퍼드는 이튿날 아침이면 벌써 맨스필드에 두 주를 더 보내기로 마음을 아주 굳혔다. 그리고 제 사냥말들을 부르러 사람을 보내고, 제독에게 몇 줄 해명을 적어 봉인해 편지를 던져 놓고는, 나머지 식구가 물러난 것을 보고 누이 쪽을 돌아보며 미소 띤 얼굴로 말했다. “그래, 사냥 안 하는 날에 내가 뭘 하며 지낼 작정인 것 같아, 메리? 나야 이제 한 주에 세 번 넘게 나가기엔 너무 늙었거든. 그런데 그 사이사이 날들을 위한 계획이 하나 있는데, 그게 뭘 것 같아?”"]], [["“To walk and ride with me, to be sure.”", "“나랑 걷고 말 타는 거겠지, 뻔하잖아.”"]], [["“Not exactly, though I shall be happy to do both, but that would be exercise only to my body, and I must take care of my mind.", "“그건 아니야, 물론 둘 다 기꺼이 하겠지만. 하지만 그거야 내 몸에만 운동이 될 뿐이고, 나는 내 마음도 돌봐야 하거든."], ["Besides, that would be all recreation and indulgence, without the wholesome alloy of labour, and I do not like to eat the bread of idleness.", "게다가 그거야 죄다 놀이요 방종일 뿐, 노동이라는 이로운 섞임이 없잖아. 나는 게으름의 빵을 먹기는 싫어."], ["No, my plan is to make Fanny Price in love with me.”", "아니지, 내 계획은 패니 프라이스가 나한테 반하게 만드는 거야.”"]], [["“Fanny Price!", "“패니 프라이스라니!"], ["Nonsense! No, no.", "말도 안 돼!"], ["You ought to be satisfied with her two cousins.”", "안 돼, 안 돼. 오빤 그 사촌 둘로 만족해야지.”"]], [["“But I cannot be satisfied without Fanny Price, without making a small hole in Fanny Price’s heart.", "“하지만 나는 패니 프라이스 없인 만족 못 해, 패니 프라이스 마음에 자그마한 구멍 하나 못 내고는 말이야."], ["You do not seem properly aware of her claims to notice.", "넌 그 애가 눈여겨볼 만하다는 걸 제대로 모르는 것 같더구나."], ["When we talked of her last night, you none of you seemed sensible of the wonderful improvement that has taken place in her looks within the last six weeks.", "어젯밤 그 애 얘길 할 때, 너희 중 누구도 지난 육 주 새 그 애 얼굴에 일어난 그 놀라운 변화를 못 알아채는 것 같던데."], ["You see her every day, and therefore do not notice it; but I assure you she is quite a different creature from what she was in the autumn.", "넌 그 앨 날마다 보니까 못 알아채는 거야. 하지만 정말이지 그 앤 가을과는 아주 딴사람이야."], ["She was then merely a quiet, modest, not plain-looking girl, but she is now absolutely pretty.", "그때야 그저 얌전하고 조신한, 못생기진 않은 계집애였지만, 지금은 정말이지 예뻐."], ["I used to think she had neither complexion nor countenance; but in that soft skin of hers, so frequently tinged with a blush as it was yesterday, there is decided beauty; and from what I observed of her eyes and mouth, I do not despair of their being capable of expression enough when she has anything to express.", "나는 그 애가 얼굴빛도 낯빛도 없다 여겼었어. 그런데 어제처럼 그토록 자주 홍조가 어리는 그 보드라운 살결엔 분명한 아름다움이 있고, 내가 살펴본 그 애 눈과 입으로 미루어, 뭔가 드러낼 게 있을 땐 넉넉히 표현해 낼 만하리라는 걸 나는 의심치 않아."], ["And then, her air, her manner, her tout ensemble, is so indescribably improved!", "게다가 그 애 분위기며 몸가짐이며 전체 인상이 이루 말할 수 없이 나아졌어!"], ["She must be grown two inches, at least, since October.”", "시월 이후로 적어도 두 인치는 컸을 거야.”"]], [["“Phoo! phoo!", "“쳇, 쳇!"], ["This is only because there were no tall women to compare her with, and because she has got a new gown, and you never saw her so well dressed before.", "그건 그저 견줄 만한 키 큰 여자가 없었던 데다, 그 애가 새 가운을 얻어서, 오빠가 그렇게 잘 차려입은 걸 전엔 못 봤기 때문일 뿐이야."], ["She is just what she was in October, believe me.", "그 앤 시월 그대로라니까, 내 말 믿어."], ["The truth is, that she was the only girl in company for you to notice, and you must have a somebody.", "사실인즉, 그 애가 오빠 눈에 띌 유일한 계집애였고, 오빤 누군가는 있어야 하는 사람이거든."], ["I have always thought her pretty—not strikingly pretty—but ‘pretty enough,’ as people say; a sort of beauty that grows on one.", "나야 늘 그 애가 예쁘다 여겼어—눈에 확 띄게 예쁜 건 아니지만—사람들 말마따나 ‘그만하면 예쁜’, 두고 볼수록 정드는 그런 아름다움 말이야. 눈이 좀 더 짙었으면 싶지만, 미소가 참하지."], ["Her eyes should be darker, but she has a sweet smile; but as for this wonderful degree of improvement, I am sure it may all be resolved into a better style of dress, and your having nobody else to look at; and therefore, if you do set about a flirtation with her, you never will persuade me that it is in compliment to her beauty, or that it proceeds from anything but your own idleness and folly.”", "그런데 이 놀라운 변화로 말하면, 그건 죄다 더 나은 옷차림새랑 오빠가 달리 볼 사람이 없었던 거로 풀이될 게 분명해. 그러니 오빠가 정 그 애랑 시시덕거릴 작정이라면, 그게 그 애 아름다움에 대한 찬사라거나 오빠 게으름과 어리석음 말고 다른 데서 나온 거라고는, 나를 결코 못 믿게 만들 거야.”"]], [["Her brother gave only a smile to this accusation, and soon afterwards said, “I do not quite know what to make of Miss Fanny.", "그 오라비는 이 나무람에 그저 미소만 짓더니, 이내 말했다. “나는 패니 양을 도무지 어찌 봐야 할지 모르겠어."], ["I do not understand her.", "그 앨 통 이해 못 하겠거든."], ["I could not tell what she would be at yesterday.", "어제 그 애가 대체 뭘 하려는 건지 알 수가 없었어."], ["What is her character?", "그 앤 성품이 어떻지?"], ["Is she solemn?", "근엄한가? 별난가?"], ["Is she queer?", "내숭인가?"], ["Is she prudish? Why did she draw back and look so grave at me?", "왜 뒷걸음질 치며 나한테 그리 근엄한 낯을 하지?"], ["I could hardly get her to speak.", "도무지 말을 시킬 수가 없었어."], ["I never was so long in company with a girl in my life, trying to entertain her, and succeed so ill!", "평생 계집애를 즐겁게 해 주려고 그토록 오래 함께 있어 보고 그토록 형편없이 실패한 적이 없어!"], ["Never met with a girl who looked so grave on me!", "나한테 그토록 근엄한 낯을 하는 계집애는 만나 본 적이 없다니까!"], ["I must try to get the better of this.", "나는 이걸 기어이 이겨 내야겠어."], ["Her looks say, ‘I will not like you, I am determined not to like you’; and I say she shall.”", "그 애 낯빛은 이렇게 말하거든. ‘난 당신을 안 좋아할 거예요, 안 좋아하기로 작정했어요.’ 그럼 나는 말하지, 좋아하게 만들겠다고.”"]], [["“Foolish fellow!", "“어리석은 사람!"], ["And so this is her attraction after all!", "그러니까 이게 결국 그 애 매력이었구먼!"], ["This it is, her not caring about you, which gives her such a soft skin, and makes her so much taller, and produces all these charms and graces!", "바로 이거야, 그 애가 오빠한테 무심한 게 그 애 살결을 그토록 보드랍게 하고, 그 애 키를 그토록 크게 하고, 이 온갖 매력과 우아함을 다 자아낸 거라고! 나는 정말이지 오빠가 그 앨 진짜로 불행하게 만들진 말기를 바라."], ["I do desire that you will not be making her really unhappy; a little love, perhaps, may animate and do her good, but I will not have you plunge her deep, for she is as good a little creature as ever lived, and has a great deal of feeling.”", "어쩌면 자그마한 사랑이야 그 애한테 생기를 주고 이로울지도 모르지, 하지만 그 앨 깊이 빠뜨리는 건 안 돼. 그 앤 세상에 둘도 없이 착한 애고, 정도 아주 많거든.”"]], [["“It can be but for a fortnight,”", "“고작 두 주뿐인걸.” 헨리가 말했다."], ["said Henry; “and if a fortnight can kill her, she must have a constitution which nothing could save.", "“그리고 두 주에 그 애가 죽는다면, 그야 무엇으로도 살릴 수 없는 체질인 거지."], ["No, I will not do her any harm, dear little soul!", "아니야, 나는 그 앨 조금도 해치지 않아, 그 사랑스러운 것을!"], ["I only want her to look kindly on me, to give me smiles as well as blushes, to keep a chair for me by herself wherever we are, and be all animation when I take it and talk to her; to think as I think, be interested in all my possessions and pleasures, try to keep me longer at Mansfield, and feel when I go away that she shall be never happy again.", "나는 그저 그 애가 나를 다정하게 봐 주고, 홍조뿐 아니라 미소도 지어 주고, 우리가 어디 있든 제 곁에 나를 위한 의자 하나 맡아 두고, 내가 그 자리에 앉아 말을 걸면 온통 생기가 돌기를 바랄 뿐이야. 나처럼 생각하고, 내 온갖 소유물과 즐거움에 관심을 두고, 나를 맨스필드에 좀 더 붙들어 두려 애쓰고, 내가 떠나면 다시는 결코 행복할 수 없으리라 느끼기를 말이야."], ["I want nothing more.”", "그 이상은 아무것도 안 바라.”"]], [["“Moderation itself!”", "“절제의 화신일세!”"], ["said Mary.", "메리가 말했다."], ["“I can have no scruples now.", "“이젠 나도 아무 거리낌이 없네."], ["Well, you will have opportunities enough of endeavouring to recommend yourself, for we are a great deal together.”", "뭐, 오빠 자신을 잘 보여 줄 기회야 얼마든지 있을 거야, 우리가 워낙 자주 함께 있으니까.”"]], [["And without attempting any farther remonstrance, she left Fanny to her fate, a fate which, had not Fanny’s heart been guarded in a way unsuspected by Miss Crawford, might have been a little harder than she deserved; for although there doubtless are such unconquerable young ladies of eighteen (or one should not read about them) as are never to be persuaded into love against their judgment by all that talent, manner, attention, and flattery can do, I have no inclination to believe Fanny one of them, or to think that with so much tenderness of disposition, and so much taste as belonged to her, she could have escaped heart-whole from the courtship (though the courtship only of a fortnight) of such a man as Crawford, in spite of there being some previous ill opinion of him to be overcome, had not her affection been engaged elsewhere. With all the security which love of another and disesteem of him could give to the peace of mind he was attacking, his continued attentions—continued, but not obtrusive, and adapting themselves more and more to the gentleness and delicacy of her character—obliged her very soon to dislike him less than formerly. She had by no means forgotten the past, and she thought as ill of him as ever; but she felt his powers: he was entertaining; and his manners were so improved, so polite, so seriously and blamelessly polite, that it was impossible not to be civil to him in return.", "그러고는 더 나무라 볼 것도 없이, 그녀는 패니를 그 운명에 맡겨 두었다. 만일 패니의 마음이 크로퍼드 양은 짐작도 못 할 방식으로 지켜지고 있지 않았더라면, 그것은 그 애가 마땅히 받을 것보다 조금 더 가혹한 운명이었을지 모른다. 재주와 태도와 마음씀과 아첨이 할 수 있는 그 온갖 것으로도, 제 판단을 거슬러 사랑에 빠지도록 결코 설득되지 않는 그런 굴복할 줄 모르는 열여덟 살 아가씨가 분명 있기야 하겠지만(안 그러면 그런 이야기를 읽을 리도 없을 테니), 나는 패니를 그런 축이라 믿고 싶지도, 그토록 다정한 성품과 그토록 고운 취향을 지닌 그 애가—비록 두 주뿐인 구애일망정—크로퍼드 같은 사내의 구애에서, 게다가 그에 대한 미리 품은 나쁜 인상까지 이겨 내야 하는 마당에, 만일 그 애의 정이 다른 데 매여 있지 않았더라면 마음 온전히 벗어날 수 있었으리라 여기고 싶지도 않다. 다른 이에 대한 사랑과 그에 대한 낮은 평가가 그가 공략하는 그 마음의 평온에 줄 수 있는 온갖 방패에도 불구하고, 그의 끊임없는 마음씀은—끊임없되 주제넘지 않고, 그 애 성품의 부드러움과 섬세함에 점점 더 맞춰 가는 그 마음씀은—이내 그녀로 하여금 예전보다 그를 덜 싫어하게 만들었다. 그녀는 결코 지난 일을 잊지 않았고, 여전히 그를 나쁘게 여겼다. 그러나 그녀는 그의 역량을 느꼈다. 그는 재미났고, 그 태도가 어찌나 나아지고, 어찌나 정중하고, 어찌나 진지하고 나무랄 데 없이 정중하던지, 답례로 예의를 차리지 않을 수가 없었다."]], [["A very few days were enough to effect this; and at the end of those few days, circumstances arose which had a tendency rather to forward his views of pleasing her, inasmuch as they gave her a degree of happiness which must dispose her to be pleased with everybody.", "아주 며칠이면 이렇게 되기에 넉넉했다. 그리고 그 며칠 끝에, 오히려 그녀를 기쁘게 하려는 그의 뜻을 앞당길 만한 일이 생겼으니, 그것이 그녀에게 온갖 사람을 다 반기게 할 만한 정도의 행복을 안겨 주었기 때문이다."], ["William, her brother, the so long absent and dearly loved brother, was in England again.", "그녀의 오라비 윌리엄이, 그토록 오래 떠나 있던 애틋이 사랑하는 오라비가 다시 잉글랜드에 돌아온 것이다."], ["She had a letter from him herself, a few hurried happy lines, written as the ship came up Channel, and sent into Portsmouth with the first boat that left the Antwerp at anchor in Spithead; and when Crawford walked up with the newspaper in his hand, which he had hoped would bring the first tidings, he found her trembling with joy over this letter, and listening with a glowing, grateful countenance to the kind invitation which her uncle was most collectedly dictating in reply.", "그녀는 그에게서 몸소 편지를 받았으니, 배가 해협을 거슬러 올라올 때 적어, 스핏헤드에 닻 내린 앤트워프호를 떠난 첫 보트 편에 포츠머스로 부친, 서둘러 쓴 행복한 몇 줄이었다. 그리고 크로퍼드가, 첫 소식을 전해 주기를 바랐던 신문을 손에 들고 걸어 올라왔을 때, 그는 그녀가 이 편지 위로 기쁨에 떨며, 삼촌이 더없이 침착하게 불러 주며 답장을 받아 적게 하던 그 다정한 초대를, 발갛게 상기되어 고마움 어린 낯빛으로 듣고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["It was but the day before that Crawford had made himself thoroughly master of the subject, or had in fact become at all aware of her having such a brother, or his being in such a ship, but the interest then excited had been very properly lively, determining him on his return to town to apply for information as to the probable period of the Antwerp’s return from the Mediterranean, etc.; and the good luck which attended his early examination of ship news the next morning seemed the reward of his ingenuity in finding out such a method of pleasing her, as well as of his dutiful attention to the Admiral, in having for many years taken in the paper esteemed to have the earliest naval intelligence.", "크로퍼드가 이 일을 훤히 꿰게 된 것은, 아니 사실 그녀에게 그런 오라비가 있다는 것도, 그가 그런 배에 타고 있다는 것도 조금이라도 알게 된 것은 바로 그 전날이었다. 그러나 그때 인 관심은 아주 그럴싸하게 생생해서, 그는 도시로 돌아가면 앤트워프호가 지중해에서 돌아올 대략의 시기 따위를 알아보기로 마음먹었다."], ["He proved, however, to be too late.", "그리고 이튿날 아침 일찍 배 소식을 살핀 그 행운은, 그녀를 기쁘게 할 그런 방도를 찾아낸 제 꾀바름에 대한, 그리고 오랜 세월 해군 소식이 가장 빠르다는 평판의 그 신문을 받아 본, 제독에 대한 제 갸륵한 마음씀에 대한 보답인 듯했다."], ["All those fine first feelings, of which he had hoped to be the exciter, were already given.", "그러나 그는 너무 늦은 것으로 드러났다. 그가 불러일으키는 이가 되기를 바랐던 그 온갖 고운 첫 감정은 이미 다 주어져 있었다."], ["But his intention, the kindness of his intention, was thankfully acknowledged: quite thankfully and warmly, for she was elevated beyond the common timidity of her mind by the flow of her love for William.", "그러나 그의 뜻, 그 뜻의 친절함은 고맙게 받아들여졌다—아주 고맙고 따뜻하게 말이다. 윌리엄에 대한 사랑이 밀려들어 그녀가 여느 때의 그 소심함을 넘어 한껏 들떠 있었기 때문이다."]], [["This dear William would soon be amongst them. There could be no doubt of his obtaining leave of absence immediately, for he was still only a midshipman; and as his parents, from living on the spot, must already have seen him, and be seeing him perhaps daily, his direct holidays might with justice be instantly given to the sister, who had been his best correspondent through a period of seven years, and the uncle who had done most for his support and advancement; and accordingly the reply to her reply, fixing a very early day for his arrival, came as soon as possible; and scarcely ten days had passed since Fanny had been in the agitation of her first dinner-visit, when she found herself in an agitation of a higher nature, watching in the hall, in the lobby, on the stairs, for the first sound of the carriage which was to bring her a brother.", "이 사랑하는 윌리엄이 이내 그들 틈에 오게 될 것이었다. 그가 곧바로 휴가를 얻으리라는 데는 의심의 여지가 없었으니, 그는 아직 사관후보생일 뿐이었기 때문이다. 그리고 그 부모야 마침 그곳에 사니 벌써 그를 봤을 테고 어쩌면 날마다 보고 있을 테니, 그의 곧장의 휴일은 마땅히 곧바로 그 누이에게—일곱 해 세월 내내 그의 가장 좋은 편지 벗이었던 누이에게—그리고 그를 건사하고 앞길을 열어 주는 데 누구보다 애쓴 그 삼촌에게 주어질 만했다. 그리하여 그의 도착일을 아주 이르게 잡은, 그녀의 답장에 대한 답장이 되도록 빨리 왔다. 그리고 패니가 첫 만찬 방문의 동요에 잠겼던 이래 열흘도 채 지나지 않아, 그녀는 한층 높은 종류의 동요에 잠겨, 오라비를 데려올 마차의 첫 소리를 들으려 현관홀에서, 로비에서, 계단에서 지켜보고 있는 자신을 발견했다."]], [["It came happily while she was thus waiting; and there being neither ceremony nor fearfulness to delay the moment of meeting, she was with him as he entered the house, and the first minutes of exquisite feeling had no interruption and no witnesses, unless the servants chiefly intent upon opening the proper doors could be called such.", "그녀가 이렇게 기다리는 동안 그것이 반갑게 왔다. 그리고 만남의 순간을 늦출 격식도 두려움도 없던지라, 그가 집에 들어설 때 그녀는 그와 함께 있었고, 더없이 애틋한 감정의 첫 몇 분은 아무 훼방도 아무 목격자도 없었으니, 알맞은 문들을 여는 데만 골몰한 하인들을 목격자라 부를 수 있다면 몰라도 말이다."], ["This was exactly what Sir Thomas and Edmund had been separately conniving at, as each proved to the other by the sympathetic alacrity with which they both advised Mrs.", "이것이야말로 토머스 경과 에드먼드가 저마다 넌지시 눈감아 준 바로 그것이었다."], ["Norris’s continuing where she was, instead of rushing out into the hall as soon as the noises of the arrival reached them.", "도착의 소음이 들려오자마자 노리스 부인이 현관홀로 뛰쳐나가려는 것을 둘 다, 서로에게 그 마음 맞는 잽싼 태도로 증명하듯, 있던 자리에 그대로 있으라 권했던 것이다."]], [["William and Fanny soon shewed themselves; and Sir Thomas had the pleasure of receiving, in his protégé, certainly a very different person from the one he had equipped seven years ago, but a young man of an open, pleasant countenance, and frank, unstudied, but feeling and respectful manners, and such as confirmed him his friend.", "이내 윌리엄과 패니가 모습을 드러냈다. 그리고 토머스 경은 제 피후견인으로, 분명 일곱 해 전 그가 채비시켜 보낸 사람과는 아주 딴사람을, 그러나 트이고 유쾌한 낯빛에, 꾸밈없고 스스럼없되 정 있고 공손한 태도를 지닌, 그리하여 그를 제 편이라 굳혀 주는 그런 젊은이를 맞는 기쁨을 누렸다."]], [["It was long before Fanny could recover from the agitating happiness of such an hour as was formed by the last thirty minutes of expectation, and the first of fruition; it was some time even before her happiness could be said to make her happy, before the disappointment inseparable from the alteration of person had vanished, and she could see in him the same William as before, and talk to him, as her heart had been yearning to do through many a past year.", "마지막 삼십 분의 기다림과 첫 삼십 분의 이룸으로 이뤄진 그런 한때의 그 벅찬 행복에서 패니가 헤어나기까지는 오래 걸렸다. 그 행복이 그녀를 행복하게 한다 할 만해지기까지도, 사람이 달라진 데서 오는 어쩔 수 없는 실망이 스러져 그 안에서 예전과 같은 윌리엄을 알아보고, 지난 여러 해 마음이 사무치게 바라 오던 대로 그와 이야기할 수 있게 되기까지도 얼마간 시간이 걸렸다."], ["That time, however, did gradually come, forwarded by an affection on his side as warm as her own, and much less encumbered by refinement or self-distrust.", "그러나 그때는 차츰 왔으니, 그녀 못지않게 따뜻하면서 세련됨이나 자기 불신에 훨씬 덜 얽매인 그의 정에 힘입어서였다."], ["She was the first object of his love, but it was a love which his stronger spirits, and bolder temper, made it as natural for him to express as to feel.", "그녀는 그의 사랑의 첫째 대상이었으나, 그것은 그의 더 굳센 기상과 더 대담한 기질이 느끼기 만큼이나 표현하기도 자연스럽게 만든 그런 사랑이었다."], ["On the morrow they were walking about together with true enjoyment, and every succeeding morrow renewed a tête-à-tête which Sir Thomas could not but observe with complacency, even before Edmund had pointed it out to him.", "이튿날 그들은 참된 즐거움 속에 함께 거닐었고, 뒤이은 아침마다 단둘의 시간이 새로이 이어졌으니, 그것을 토머스 경은, 에드먼드가 그에게 짚어 주기도 전에, 흐뭇하게 지켜보지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["Excepting the moments of peculiar delight, which any marked or unlooked-for instance of Edmund’s consideration of her in the last few months had excited, Fanny had never known so much felicity in her life, as in this unchecked, equal, fearless intercourse with the brother and friend who was opening all his heart to her, telling her all his hopes and fears, plans, and solicitudes respecting that long thought of, dearly earned, and justly valued blessing of promotion; who could give her direct and minute information of the father and mother, brothers and sisters, of whom she very seldom heard; who was interested in all the comforts and all the little hardships of her home at Mansfield; ready to think of every member of that home as she directed, or differing only by a less scrupulous opinion, and more noisy abuse of their aunt Norris, and with whom (perhaps the dearest indulgence of the whole) all the evil and good of their earliest years could be gone over again, and every former united pain and pleasure retraced with the fondest recollection.", "지난 몇 달 사이 에드먼드가 그녀를 헤아려 준 그 어떤 두드러지거나 뜻밖의 일이 자아낸 각별한 기쁨의 순간들을 빼고는, 패니는 평생 이토록 큰 행복을 알아 본 적이 없었으니, 저에게 온 마음을 열어 보이는 이 오라비이자 벗과의 이 거리낌 없고 대등하고 두려움 없는 사귐에서 누린 것 같은 행복은 말이다. 그는 그토록 오래 그려 오고, 값지게 얻어 내고, 마땅히 값진 그 승진의 축복에 얽힌 제 온갖 희망과 두려움, 계획과 마음 졸임을 그녀에게 다 털어놓았다."], ["An advantage this, a strengthener of love, in which even the conjugal tie is beneath the fraternal.", "그는 그녀가 좀처럼 소식 듣지 못하던 아버지와 어머니, 형제자매에 대해 곧바로 낱낱이 알려 줄 수 있었다."], ["Children of the same family, the same blood, with the same first associations and habits, have some means of enjoyment in their power, which no subsequent connexions can supply; and it must be by a long and unnatural estrangement, by a divorce which no subsequent connexion can justify, if such precious remains of the earliest attachments are ever entirely outlived.", "그는 맨스필드 그녀 집의 온갖 편안함과 온갖 자잘한 고생에 관심을 두었고, 그 집 식구 하나하나를 그녀가 이끄는 대로 생각할 채비가 되어 있었으며—다만 노리스 이모에 대해서만은 좀 덜 삼가는 의견과 좀 더 시끄러운 험담으로 달랐을 뿐이다—그리고 그와 더불어(어쩌면 그 모든 것 가운데 가장 애틋한 즐거움으로) 그들 가장 어린 시절의 온갖 궂은 일과 좋은 일을 다시 되짚고, 지난날 함께 나눈 온갖 아픔과 즐거움을 더없이 정겨운 추억으로 되밟을 수 있었다. 이것이야말로 하나의 이점이요, 사랑을 굳혀 주는 것이니, 여기서는 부부의 인연조차 남매의 그것 아래에 있다."], ["Too often, alas! it is so.", "같은 집안, 같은 피, 같은 첫 기억과 버릇을 지닌 자식들에게는, 훗날 그 어떤 인연으로도 대신할 수 없는 즐거움의 방도가 그 손안에 얼마간 있다."], ["Fraternal love, sometimes almost everything, is at others worse than nothing.", "그리고 그토록 값진, 가장 이른 정의 잔재가 아주 스러져 버리는 일이 있다면, 그것은 오직 길고 부자연스러운 소원함으로, 훗날 그 어떤 인연으로도 정당화할 수 없는 갈라섬으로써일 것이다. 아아, 너무나 자주 그러하기는 하다!"], ["But with William and Fanny Price it was still a sentiment in all its prime and freshness, wounded by no opposition of interest, cooled by no separate attachment, and feeling the influence of time and absence only in its increase.", "남매의 사랑이란, 때로 거의 전부이다가도, 다른 때는 없느니만 못하다. 그러나 윌리엄과 패니 프라이스에게 그것은 여전히 그 온전한 한창과 싱그러움 속의 정이었으니, 이해의 다툼으로 상하지도, 따로 든 정으로 식지도 않고, 세월과 헤어짐의 영향이라곤 오직 그것이 자라나는 데서만 느껴지는 것이었다."]], [["An affection so amiable was advancing each in the opinion of all who had hearts to value anything good.", "그토록 상냥한 정은, 무엇이든 좋은 것을 값지게 여길 마음을 지닌 모든 이의 눈에 그 둘을 저마다 돋보이게 했다."], ["Henry Crawford was as much struck with it as any.", "헨리 크로퍼드도 누구 못지않게 그것에 감동했다."], ["He honoured the warm-hearted, blunt fondness of the young sailor, which led him to say, with his hands stretched towards Fanny’s head, “Do you know, I begin to like that queer fashion already, though when I first heard of such things being done in England, I could not believe it; and when Mrs.", "그는 그 젊은 뱃사람의 따뜻하고 무뚝뚝한 다정함을 우러렀으니, 그 다정함에 이끌려 그는 패니의 머리 쪽으로 두 손을 뻗으며 이렇게 말하곤 했다. “있잖아요, 나 벌써 그 야릇한 유행이 좋아지기 시작했어요."], ["Brown, and the other women at the Commissioner’s at Gibraltar, appeared in the same trim, I thought they were mad; but Fanny can reconcile me to anything”; and saw, with lively admiration, the glow of Fanny’s cheek, the brightness of her eye, the deep interest, the absorbed attention, while her brother was describing any of the imminent hazards, or terrific scenes, which such a period at sea must supply.", "잉글랜드에서 그런 걸 한다는 소릴 처음 들었을 땐 도무지 못 믿었고, 지브롤터 판무관 댁의 브라운 부인이랑 다른 여자들이 똑같은 매무새로 나타났을 땐 다들 실성했나 싶었는데, 패니한테라면 나는 뭐든 정이 든다니까요.” 그리고 그는, 그런 바다 생활이 으레 안겨 줄 절박한 위험이나 무시무시한 장면을 그녀의 오라비가 그려 보일 때, 발갛게 상기된 패니의 뺨, 반짝이는 그 눈, 그 깊은 관심, 그 넋 나간 몰두를 생기 넘치는 감탄으로 지켜보았다."]], [["It was a picture which Henry Crawford had moral taste enough to value.", "그것은 헨리 크로퍼드가 값지게 여길 만한 도덕적 안목을 지닌 그림이었다."], ["Fanny’s attractions increased—increased twofold; for the sensibility which beautified her complexion and illumined her countenance was an attraction in itself.", "패니의 매력이 커졌다—곱절로 커졌다. 그녀의 얼굴빛을 아름답게 하고 낯빛을 환히 밝히는 그 감수성이 그 자체로 하나의 매력이었기 때문이다."], ["He was no longer in doubt of the capabilities of her heart.", "그는 이제 그녀 마음의 역량을 의심하지 않았다."], ["She had feeling, genuine feeling.", "그녀에게는 정이, 참된 정이 있었다."], ["It would be something to be loved by such a girl, to excite the first ardours of her young unsophisticated mind!", "그런 계집애에게 사랑받는다는 것, 그 젊고 순박한 마음의 첫 열정을 불러일으킨다는 것은 대단한 일일 터였다!"], ["She interested him more than he had foreseen.", "그녀는 그가 미리 내다본 것보다 더 그의 마음을 끌었다."], ["A fortnight was not enough.", "두 주로는 넉넉지 않았다."], ["His stay became indefinite.", "그의 체류가 기약 없어졌다."]], [["William was often called on by his uncle to be the talker.", "윌리엄은 삼촌에게서 곧잘 이야기꾼이 되어 달라는 청을 받았다."], ["His recitals were amusing in themselves to Sir Thomas, but the chief object in seeking them was to understand the reciter, to know the young man by his histories; and he listened to his clear, simple, spirited details with full satisfaction, seeing in them the proof of good principles, professional knowledge, energy, courage, and cheerfulness, everything that could deserve or promise well.", "그의 이야기는 그 자체로 토머스 경에게 재미났으나, 그것을 구하는 주된 목적은 이야기하는 이를 헤아리는 것, 그 젊은이를 그 살아온 이야기로 알아 가는 것이었다. 그리고 그는 그 또렷하고 소박하고 생기 넘치는 자초지종에 온전한 흡족함으로 귀 기울였으니, 거기서 훌륭한 원칙, 직업의 지식, 활력, 용기, 명랑함의 증거를, 무엇이든 값지거나 앞날이 밝을 만한 그 모든 것을 보았기 때문이다."], ["Young as he was, William had already seen a great deal.", "어린 나이에도 윌리엄은 벌써 많은 것을 보았다."], ["He had been in the Mediterranean; in the West Indies; in the Mediterranean again; had been often taken on shore by the favour of his captain, and in the course of seven years had known every variety of danger which sea and war together could offer.", "그는 지중해에도, 서인도제도에도, 다시 지중해에도 있었다. 함장의 배려로 곧잘 뭍에도 올랐고, 일곱 해 동안 바다와 전쟁이 함께 안길 수 있는 온갖 위험을 다 알았다."], ["With such means in his power he had a right to be listened to; and though Mrs.", "제 손에 그런 밑천을 지녔으니 그는 귀 기울여 들을 만한 자격이 있었다."], ["Norris could fidget about the room, and disturb everybody in quest of two needlefuls of thread or a second-hand shirt button, in the midst of her nephew’s account of a shipwreck or an engagement, everybody else was attentive; and even Lady Bertram could not hear of such horrors unmoved, or without sometimes lifting her eyes from her work to say, “Dear me!", "그리고 조카가 난파나 교전을 이야기하는 한복판에, 노리스 부인이 방 안을 부산 떨며 실 두 바늘어치나 헌 셔츠 단추 하나를 찾느라 모두를 어지럽힐 수는 있었어도, 그 밖의 모두는 귀를 기울였다. 그리고 버트럼 부인마저 그런 무서운 이야기를 아무렇지 않게 들을 수는 없어, 이따금 일감에서 눈을 들어 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["how disagreeable!", "“저런! 어쩜 그리 끔찍할까!"], ["I wonder anybody can ever go to sea.”", "어떻게 바다에 나가는 사람이 다 있나 몰라.”"]], [["To Henry Crawford they gave a different feeling.", "헨리 크로퍼드에게 그것은 다른 감정을 주었다."], ["He longed to have been at sea, and seen and done and suffered as much.", "그는 저도 바다에 나가, 그만큼 보고 행하고 겪었더라면 하고 사무치게 바랐다."], ["His heart was warmed, his fancy fired, and he felt the highest respect for a lad who, before he was twenty, had gone through such bodily hardships and given such proofs of mind.", "그의 마음이 데워지고, 그의 상상이 불붙었으며, 스무 살도 되기 전에 그런 육신의 고생을 다 겪고 그런 심지의 증거를 다 내보인 젊은이에게 더없는 존경을 느꼈다."], ["The glory of heroism, of usefulness, of exertion, of endurance, made his own habits of selfish indulgence appear in shameful contrast; and he wished he had been a William Price, distinguishing himself and working his way to fortune and consequence with so much self-respect and happy ardour, instead of what he was!", "영웅다움의, 쓸모의, 애씀의, 견딤의 그 영광은, 제 이기적인 방종의 버릇을 부끄러운 대비 속에 드러나게 했다. 그리고 그는 지금의 저 대신, 그토록 큰 자긍심과 행복한 열의로 스스로를 돋보이며 재물과 지체로 제 길을 열어 가는 윌리엄 프라이스였더라면 하고 바랐다!"]], [["The wish was rather eager than lasting.", "그 바람은 오래가기보다 자못 뜨거운 쪽이었다."], ["He was roused from the reverie of retrospection and regret produced by it, by some inquiry from Edmund as to his plans for the next day’s hunting; and he found it was as well to be a man of fortune at once with horses and grooms at his command.", "그것이 자아낸 회고와 뉘우침의 몽상에서 그는, 이튿날 사냥 계획을 묻는 에드먼드의 물음에 깨어났다. 그리고 그는 그냥 당장 말과 마부를 제 마음대로 부리는 재산가인 편이 낫다는 걸 알았다."], ["In one respect it was better, as it gave him the means of conferring a kindness where he wished to oblige.", "한 가지 점에서 그것은 더 나았으니, 은혜를 베풀고 싶은 데 친절을 베풀 방도를 주었기 때문이다. 무엇에든 나설 기상과 용기와 호기심을 지닌 윌리엄이 사냥하고 싶은 뜻을 내비쳤다."], ["With spirits, courage, and curiosity up to anything, William expressed an inclination to hunt; and Crawford could mount him without the slightest inconvenience to himself, and with only some scruples to obviate in Sir Thomas, who knew better than his nephew the value of such a loan, and some alarms to reason away in Fanny.", "그리고 크로퍼드는 저에게는 조금의 불편도 없이 그를 말에 태워 줄 수 있었으니, 다만 조카보다 그런 대여의 값어치를 더 잘 아는 토머스 경에게서 얼마간의 거리낌을 없애 주고, 패니에게서 얼마간의 불안을 다독여 없애 주면 되었다. 그녀는 윌리엄을 걱정했다."], ["She feared for William; by no means convinced by all that he could relate of his own horsemanship in various countries, of the scrambling parties in which he had been engaged, the rough horses and mules he had ridden, or his many narrow escapes from dreadful falls, that he was at all equal to the management of a high-fed hunter in an English fox-chase; nor till he returned safe and well, without accident or discredit, could she be reconciled to the risk, or feel any of that obligation to Mr.", "여러 나라에서의 제 승마 솜씨며, 저도 낀 그 우당탕 몰려다닌 사냥이며, 저가 탔던 거친 말과 노새며, 무서운 낙마를 아슬아슬하게 여러 번 면한 이야기를 그가 아무리 늘어놓아도, 그가 잉글랜드 여우 사냥에서 잘 먹인 사냥말을 다룰 만하다고는 조금도 믿기지 않았던 것이다. 그리고 그가 아무 사고도 망신도 없이 무사히 잘 돌아오기 전까지는, 그녀는 그 위험과 화해할 수도, 그 말을 빌려준 데 대해 크로퍼드 씨가 마땅히 자아내리라 작정했던 그 고마움을 조금도 느낄 수도 없었다."], ["Crawford for lending the horse which he had fully intended it should produce.", "그러나 그것이 윌리엄에게 아무 해도 끼치지 않았음이 드러나자, 그녀는 그것이 친절임을 인정할 수 있었다."], ["When it was proved, however, to have done William no harm, she could allow it to be a kindness, and even reward the owner with a smile when the animal was one minute tendered to his use again; and the next, with the greatest cordiality, and in a manner not to be resisted, made over to his use entirely so long as he remained in Northamptonshire.", "그리하여 그 짐승이 한순간 다시 윌리엄의 쓰임에 내밀어지고, 다음 순간 더없이 정답게, 마다할 수 없는 태도로, 그가 노샘프턴셔에 머무는 동안은 죄다 그의 쓰임에 넘겨졌을 때, 그녀는 미소로 그 주인에게 보답하기까지 했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXV", "# 제25장"]], [["The intercourse of the two families was at this period more nearly restored to what it had been in the autumn, than any member of the old intimacy had thought ever likely to be again.", "이 무렵 두 집안의 왕래는, 옛 친분을 나누던 이들 가운데 누구도 다시 그리되리라 좀처럼 여기지 못했을 만큼, 가을 시절의 모습에 거의 되돌아와 있었다."], ["The return of Henry Crawford, and the arrival of William Price, had much to do with it, but much was still owing to Sir Thomas’s more than toleration of the neighbourly attempts at the Parsonage.", "헨리 크로퍼드의 귀환과 윌리엄 프라이스의 도착이 거기에 큰 몫을 했으나, 목사관 쪽의 이웃다운 다가섬을 토머스 경이 그저 견뎌 주는 정도를 넘어선 데에도 여전히 힘입은 바가 컸다. 처음 그를 짓누르던 근심에서 이제 벗어난 그의 마음은, 그랜트 부부와 그 집에 머무는 젊은이들이 정말이지 찾아볼 만한 이들임을 알아볼 여유가 생긴 것이다."], ["His mind, now disengaged from the cares which had pressed on him at first, was at leisure to find the Grants and their young inmates really worth visiting; and though infinitely above scheming or contriving for any the most advantageous matrimonial establishment that could be among the apparent possibilities of any one most dear to him, and disdaining even as a littleness the being quick-sighted on such points, he could not avoid perceiving, in a grand and careless way, that Mr. Crawford was somewhat distinguishing his niece—nor perhaps refrain (though unconsciously) from giving a more willing assent to invitations on that account.", "그리고 그는 저에게 가장 소중한 이 누구를 위해서든 눈앞에 그럴싸한 가능성으로 떠오를 법한 그 어떤 더없이 유리한 혼처를 꾀하거나 도모하는 일 따위는 한없이 낮게 보았고, 그런 점에 눈치가 빠른 것조차 좀스러운 짓이라 업신여겼으되, 크로퍼드 씨가 제 조카딸을 얼마간 각별히 대하고 있음을 대범하고 무심한 투로나마 알아채지 않을 수는 없었고—아마 그 까닭에 (저도 모르게) 초대에 한결 기꺼이 응하는 것을 삼가지도 못했으리라."]], [["His readiness, however, in agreeing to dine at the Parsonage, when the general invitation was at last hazarded, after many debates and many doubts as to whether it were worth while, “because Sir Thomas seemed so ill inclined, and Lady Bertram was so indolent!”", "그러나 “토머스 경은 워낙 내키지 않아 하시고, 버트럼 부인은 워낙 게으르시니!”"], ["proceeded from good-breeding and goodwill alone, and had nothing to do with Mr.", "그럴 만한 값어치가 있을까를 두고 숱한 실랑이와 숱한 망설임 끝에 마침내 저녁 초대라는 것이 한번 무릅써졌을 때, 목사관에서 만찬을 들자는 데 그가 선뜻 응한 것은 오직 좋은 교양과 호의에서 나온 것일 뿐, 크로퍼드 씨와는 그저 유쾌한 무리의 한 사람이라는 것 말고는 아무 상관이 없었다."], ["Crawford, but as being one in an agreeable group: for it was in the course of that very visit that he first began to think that any one in the habit of such idle observations would have thought that Mr. Crawford was the admirer of Fanny Price.", "다름 아닌 바로 그 방문의 자리에서 그는, 그런 한가로운 눈여겨보기를 버릇 삼은 이라면 누구든 크로퍼드 씨가 패니 프라이스를 흠모하는 이라고 여겼으리라는 생각을 비로소 처음으로 품게 되었으니 말이다."]], [["The meeting was generally felt to be a pleasant one, being composed in a good proportion of those who would talk and those who would listen; and the dinner itself was elegant and plentiful, according to the usual style of the Grants, and too much according to the usual habits of all to raise any emotion except in Mrs.", "그 모임은 대체로 유쾌한 자리로 여겨졌으니, 말할 이와 들을 이가 알맞게 어우러진 까닭이었다. 그리고 만찬 자체는 그랜트가의 여느 방식대로 우아하고 푸짐했으며, 하나같이 여느 습관에 너무 익은 것이라 노리스 부인 말고는 누구에게도 아무 감흥을 일으키지 못했다."], ["Norris, who could never behold either the wide table or the number of dishes on it with patience, and who did always contrive to experience some evil from the passing of the servants behind her chair, and to bring away some fresh conviction of its being impossible among so many dishes but that some must be cold.", "그녀로 말하면 넓은 식탁도, 그 위에 오른 접시 수도 도무지 진득하니 바라보지 못하는 사람이었고, 제 의자 뒤로 하인들이 오가는 데서 늘 무슨 언짢은 일을 겪어 내고야 마는 사람이었으며, 이토록 접시가 많으니 그중 몇은 식지 않을 도리가 없다는 새로운 확신 하나를 꼭 챙겨 오는 사람이었다."]], [["In the evening it was found, according to the predetermination of Mrs.", "저녁이 되자, 그랜트 부인과 그 아우가 미리 짜 둔 대로, 휘스트 탁자를 꾸리고도 원탁 놀이에 넉넉할 만큼이 남으리라는 것이 드러났다."], ["Grant and her sister, that after making up the whist-table there would remain sufficient for a round game, and everybody being as perfectly complying and without a choice as on such occasions they always are, speculation was decided on almost as soon as whist; and Lady Bertram soon found herself in the critical situation of being applied to for her own choice between the games, and being required either to draw a card for whist or not.", "그리고 이런 자리에서 다들 늘 그러하듯 저마다 더없이 고분고분하고 이렇다 할 취향도 없던 터라, 휘스트가 정해지기 무섭게 스페큘레이션이 정해졌다. 그리하여 버트럼 부인은, 두 놀이 가운데 어느 쪽이 좋으냐는 물음을 받고 휘스트에 낄 패를 뽑을지 말지를 정해야 하는, 자못 아슬아슬한 처지에 이내 놓이고 말았다."], ["She hesitated.", "그녀는 머뭇거렸다."], ["Luckily Sir Thomas was at hand.", "다행히 토머스 경이 곁에 있었다."]], [["“What shall I do, Sir Thomas?", "“제가 어쩌면 좋을까요, 토머스 경?"], ["Whist and speculation; which will amuse me most?”", "휘스트하고 스페큘레이션 중에, 어느 게 저를 제일 즐겁게 해 줄까요?”"]], [["Sir Thomas, after a moment’s thought, recommended speculation.", "토머스 경은 잠깐 생각하더니 스페큘레이션을 권했다."], ["He was a whist player himself, and perhaps might feel that it would not much amuse him to have her for a partner.", "그 자신이 휘스트를 즐기는 사람이었으니, 아마 그녀를 짝으로 두어서는 그리 재미나지 못하리라 느꼈던 것이리라."]], [["“Very well,”", "“그럼 됐어요.”"], ["was her ladyship’s contented answer; “then speculation, if you please, Mrs.", "마님은 흡족한 낯으로 대답했다. “그럼 스페큘레이션으로 해요, 그랜트 부인, 그렇게 해 주세요."], ["Grant. I know nothing about it, but Fanny must teach me.”", "나야 그게 뭔지 도통 모르지만, 패니가 가르쳐 주면 되니까요.”"]], [["Here Fanny interposed, however, with anxious protestations of her own equal ignorance; she had never played the game nor seen it played in her life; and Lady Bertram felt a moment’s indecision again; but upon everybody’s assuring her that nothing could be so easy, that it was the easiest game on the cards, and Henry Crawford’s stepping forward with a most earnest request to be allowed to sit between her ladyship and Miss Price, and teach them both, it was so settled; and Sir Thomas, Mrs.", "그러나 여기서 패니가, 저 역시 그에 못지않게 아무것도 모른다며 애타게 손사래를 치며 끼어들었다. 그녀는 평생 그 놀이를 해 본 적도, 누가 하는 것을 본 적도 없었던 것이다."], ["Norris, and Dr.", "그러자 버트럼 부인은 다시 한순간 갈팡질팡했다. 그러나 그보다 쉬운 것은 없다고, 카드놀이 가운데 가장 쉬운 놀이라고 다들 장담하고, 헨리 크로퍼드가 나서서 마님과 프라이스 양 사이에 앉아 두 분을 다 가르치게 해 달라고 더없이 간곡히 청하니, 일은 그리 정해졌다."], ["and Mrs.", "그리하여 토머스 경과 노리스 부인, 그랜트 박사 부부가 가장 지적이고 위엄 있는 탁자에 자리 잡고, 나머지 여섯은 크로퍼드 양의 지휘 아래 다른 탁자에 둘러앉았다."], ["Grant being seated at the table of prime intellectual state and dignity, the remaining six, under Miss Crawford’s direction, were arranged round the other.", "헨리 크로퍼드에게는 참으로 좋은 배치였으니, 그는 패니 곁에 딱 붙어 있었고, 제 것 말고도 두 사람 몫의 패까지 건사하느라 손이 잔뜩 바빴다. 패니가 삼 분 만에 그 놀이의 규칙을 제 손아귀에 넣지 않기란 어림없는 일이었으나, 그래도 그는 그녀의 판을 북돋우고, 욕심을 벼려 주고, 마음을 모질게 다잡아 주어야 했는데—특히 윌리엄과 겨루는 판에서는—그것이 여간 애먹는 일이 아니었다."], ["It was a fine arrangement for Henry Crawford, who was close to Fanny, and with his hands full of business, having two persons’ cards to manage as well as his own; for though it was impossible for Fanny not to feel herself mistress of the rules of the game in three minutes, he had yet to inspirit her play, sharpen her avarice, and harden her heart, which, especially in any competition with William, was a work of some difficulty; and as for Lady Bertram, he must continue in charge of all her fame and fortune through the whole evening; and if quick enough to keep her from looking at her cards when the deal began, must direct her in whatever was to be done with them to the end of it.", "그리고 버트럼 부인으로 말하면, 저녁 내내 그녀의 명예와 재물을 죄다 도맡아야 했다. 패를 돌리기 시작할 제 그녀가 제 패를 들여다보지 못하게 재빨리 막고서, 놀이가 끝날 때까지 그 패로 무엇을 해야 할지 일일이 이끌어 주어야 했던 것이다."]], [["He was in high spirits, doing everything with happy ease, and preeminent in all the lively turns, quick resources, and playful impudence that could do honour to the game; and the round table was altogether a very comfortable contrast to the steady sobriety and orderly silence of the other.", "그는 잔뜩 신이 나서, 무엇이든 흐뭇하리만치 손쉽게 해냈고, 놀이의 흥을 돋우는 온갖 발랄한 재치며 재빠른 임기응변이며 장난스러운 뻔뻔함에서 단연 두드러졌다. 그리하여 그 원탁은 여느 탁자의 진득한 진지함과 정연한 침묵과는 사뭇 편안한 대조를 이루었다."]], [["Twice had Sir Thomas inquired into the enjoyment and success of his lady, but in vain; no pause was long enough for the time his measured manner needed; and very little of her state could be known till Mrs.", "토머스 경은 제 아내가 얼마나 즐거워하고 잘 풀리는지를 두 번이나 물었으나 허사였다. 그의 느긋하고 재는 말투가 필요로 하는 만큼 놀이가 오래 멈추는 법이 없었던 것이다."], ["Grant was able, at the end of the first rubber, to go to her and pay her compliments.", "그리하여 첫 판이 끝날 무렵 그랜트 부인이 그녀에게 다가가 덕담을 건넬 수 있게 되기까지는, 그녀의 형편이 어떤지 좀처럼 알 길이 없었다."]], [["“I hope your ladyship is pleased with the game.”", "“마님, 이 놀이가 마음에 드시는지요.”"]], [["“Oh dear, yes!", "“아이참, 그럼요!"], ["very entertaining indeed.", "정말이지 무척 재미나요."], ["A very odd game.", "참으로 별난 놀이예요."], ["I do not know what it is all about.", "나야 이게 다 무슨 영문인지 통 모르겠지만요."], ["I am never to see my cards; and Mr. Crawford does all the rest.”", "나는 도무지 내 패를 볼 일이 없고, 나머지는 죄다 크로퍼드 씨가 해 주니까요.”"]], [["“Bertram,”", "“버트럼,”"], ["said Crawford, some time afterwards, taking the opportunity of a little languor in the game, “I have never told you what happened to me yesterday in my ride home.”", "하고 얼마 뒤 크로퍼드가, 놀이가 잠시 느슨해진 틈을 타서 말했다. “어제 집으로 말 타고 돌아오는 길에 나한테 무슨 일이 있었는지 자네한테 아직 얘기 안 했지.”"], ["They had been hunting together, and were in the midst of a good run, and at some distance from Mansfield, when his horse being found to have flung a shoe, Henry Crawford had been obliged to give up, and make the best of his way back.", "그들은 함께 사냥을 나갔다가, 한창 좋은 몰이 중에 맨스필드에서 얼마쯤 떨어진 곳에 이르렀을 때 그의 말이 편자 하나를 벗은 것이 드러나, 헨리 크로퍼드는 하는 수 없이 사냥을 접고 되도록 잘 되짚어 돌아와야 했던 것이다. “내가 그 주목 나무 있는 낡은 농가를 지나서 길을 잃었다고 자네한테 말했지, 나야 도무지 길 묻는 걸 못 견디니까."], ["“I told you I lost my way after passing that old farmhouse with the yew-trees, because I can never bear to ask; but I have not told you that, with my usual luck—for I never do wrong without gaining by it—I found myself in due time in the very place which I had a curiosity to see.", "하지만 이 얘긴 안 했어. 늘 그렇듯 운수 좋게도—나는 잘못을 저지르고서 덕을 못 본 적이 없거든—얼마 안 가 다름 아닌, 내가 못내 궁금해하던 바로 그곳에 이르러 있더라니까."], ["I was suddenly, upon turning the corner of a steepish downy field, in the midst of a retired little village between gently rising hills; a small stream before me to be forded, a church standing on a sort of knoll to my right—which church was strikingly large and handsome for the place, and not a gentleman or half a gentleman’s house to be seen excepting one—to be presumed the Parsonage—within a stone’s throw of the said knoll and church.", "제법 가파른 잔풀 언덕배기 모롱이를 도니, 문득 나지막이 솟은 언덕들 사이 외진 자그마한 마을 한복판이지 뭔가. 앞에는 걸어서 건널 작은 시내가 있고, 오른편에는 자그마한 둔덕 위에 교회가 서 있는데—그 교회가 이런 시골치고는 유난히 크고 훤칠하고, 신사도 반쪽짜리 신사도 사는 집이라곤 하나 말고는 통 보이지 않더군—그 하나는 방금 말한 그 둔덕과 교회에서 돌 던지면 닿을 만한 데에 있으니, 목사관이려니 싶었지."], ["I found myself, in short, in Thornton Lacey.”", "요컨대 나는 손턴 레이시에 와 있더라 이 말일세.”"]], [["“It sounds like it,”", "“그런 것 같군.”"], ["said Edmund; “but which way did you turn after passing Sewell’s farm?”", "에드먼드가 말했다. “한데 자네, 슈얼네 농장을 지나서는 어느 쪽으로 돌았나?”"]], [["“I answer no such irrelevant and insidious questions; though were I to answer all that you could put in the course of an hour, you would never be able to prove that it was not Thornton Lacey—for such it certainly was.”", "“나는 그런 뜬금없고 얄궂은 물음엔 대답 안 하네. 하기야 자네가 한 시간 동안 던질 수 있는 물음에 내가 다 대답한들, 그게 손턴 레이시가 아니었다는 걸 자네는 결코 증명 못 할 테니까—틀림없이 손턴 레이시였거든.”"]], [["“You inquired, then?”", "“그럼 물어봤단 말인가?”"]], [["“No, I never inquire.", "“아니, 나는 절대 안 물어."], ["But I told a man mending a hedge that it was Thornton Lacey, and he agreed to it.”", "다만 울타리를 손보던 사내한테 여기가 손턴 레이시라고 일러 줬더니, 그자가 그렇다고 맞장구치더군.”"]], [["“You have a good memory.", "“기억력이 참 좋군."], ["I had forgotten having ever told you half so much of the place.”", "나는 자네한테 그곳 얘길 그 절반이라도 해 준 적이 있다는 걸 까맣게 잊고 있었네.”"]], [["Thornton Lacey was the name of his impending living, as Miss Crawford well knew; and her interest in a negotiation for William Price’s knave increased.", "손턴 레이시란 곧 그에게 떨어질 성직록의 이름이었으니, 크로퍼드 양은 이를 잘 알고 있었다. 그리하여 윌리엄 프라이스의 잭을 둘러싼 흥정에 그녀의 관심이 부쩍 커졌다."]], [["“Well,”", "“그래,”"], ["continued Edmund, “and how did you like what you saw?”", "에드먼드가 말을 이었다. “그래서 자네가 본 게 마음에 들던가?”"]], [["“Very much indeed.", "“정말이지 무척 들더군."], ["You are a lucky fellow.", "자네는 운 좋은 사람이야."], ["There will be work for five summers at least before the place is liveable.”", "그곳이 사람 살 만해지려면 적어도 다섯 해 여름은 손봐야 할걸.”"]], [["“No, no, not so bad as that.", "“아니, 아냐, 그리 심하진 않아."], ["The farmyard must be moved, I grant you; but I am not aware of anything else.", "헛간 마당을 옮겨야 하는 거야 나도 인정하네. 하지만 그 밖엔 딱히 걸릴 게 없는걸."], ["The house is by no means bad, and when the yard is removed, there may be a very tolerable approach to it.”", "집은 결코 나쁘지 않고, 마당만 치우고 나면 제법 봐줄 만한 진입로도 낼 수 있을 거야.”"]], [["“The farmyard must be cleared away entirely, and planted up to shut out the blacksmith’s shop.", "“헛간 마당은 몽땅 쓸어 없애고, 대장간이 안 보이게 나무를 심어 가려야 하네."], ["The house must be turned to front the east instead of the north—the entrance and principal rooms, I mean, must be on that side, where the view is really very pretty; I am sure it may be done.", "집은 북쪽 대신 동쪽을 바라보게 돌려야 하고—내 말은, 현관하고 주요한 방들이 그쪽으로 나야 한다는 거야. 그쪽 경치가 정말이지 무척 예쁘거든."], ["And there must be your approach, through what is at present the garden.", "틀림없이 그리할 수 있어. 그리고 자네 진입로는 바로 거기, 지금 정원인 데를 지나게 내야 해."], ["You must make a new garden at what is now the back of the house; which will be giving it the best aspect in the world, sloping to the south-east.", "지금 집 뒤편인 데다 새 정원을 만들어야 하는데, 그러면 세상 둘도 없이 좋은 방향을 얻게 되지, 남동쪽으로 비스듬히 트인 자리 말일세."], ["The ground seems precisely formed for it.", "땅이 딱 그러라고 생긴 것 같더군."], ["I rode fifty yards up the lane, between the church and the house, in order to look about me; and saw how it might all be.", "나는 둘러보려고 교회와 집 사이 오솔길을 오십 야드쯤 말 타고 올라가 봤는데, 그 모든 게 어찌 될 수 있을지 훤히 보이더라니까."], ["Nothing can be easier.", "더없이 쉬운 일이야."], ["The meadows beyond what will be the garden, as well as what now is, sweeping round from the lane I stood in to the north-east, that is, to the principal road through the village, must be all laid together, of course; very pretty meadows they are, finely sprinkled with timber.", "앞으로 정원이 될 데 너머의 풀밭도, 지금 정원인 데 너머의 풀밭도, 내가 서 있던 그 오솔길에서 북동쪽으로, 그러니까 마을을 가로지르는 큰길 쪽으로 휘돌아 나가는 그것들은 물론 죄다 하나로 묶어야 하지. 참 예쁜 풀밭들이야, 나무가 곱게 점점이 박혀서 말일세."], ["They belong to the living, I suppose; if not, you must purchase them.", "그것들은 성직록에 딸린 땅일 테지. 아니라면 자네가 사들여야 하고."], ["Then the stream—something must be done with the stream; but I could not quite determine what.", "그다음엔 그 시내—시내는 뭔가 손을 봐야 하는데, 다만 뭘 어찌해야 할지는 딱 못 정하겠더군."], ["I had two or three ideas.”", "두어 가지 생각이 있긴 했네.”"]], [["“And I have two or three ideas also,”", "“그리고 나도 두어 가지 생각이 있네.”"], ["said Edmund, “and one of them is, that very little of your plan for Thornton Lacey will ever be put in practice.", "에드먼드가 말했다. “그중 하나는, 손턴 레이시를 위한 자네 계획 가운데 실제로 이뤄질 건 거의 없으리라는 거야."], ["I must be satisfied with rather less ornament and beauty.", "나는 그보다 훨씬 덜 꾸미고 덜 아름다운 걸로 만족해야 하네."], ["I think the house and premises may be made comfortable, and given the air of a gentleman’s residence, without any very heavy expense, and that must suffice me; and, I hope, may suffice all who care about me.”", "나는 그 집과 딸린 터를, 그리 큰돈 안 들이고도 편안하게, 그리고 신사가 사는 집다운 기품을 갖추게 할 수 있으리라 보고, 그거면 내겐 넉넉해. 그리고 바라건대 나를 아끼는 이들 누구에게든 그거면 넉넉하기를.”"]], [["Miss Crawford, a little suspicious and resentful of a certain tone of voice, and a certain half-look attending the last expression of his hope, made a hasty finish of her dealings with William Price; and securing his knave at an exorbitant rate, exclaimed, “There, I will stake my last like a woman of spirit.", "크로퍼드 양은, 그 마지막 바람의 어떤 목소리 빛깔과, 그에 딸린 어떤 곁눈질에 얼마간 미심쩍고 서운한 마음이 들어, 윌리엄 프라이스와의 흥정을 서둘러 끝맺어 버렸다. 그러고는 터무니없는 값을 치르고 그의 잭을 손에 넣더니 외쳤다."], ["No cold prudence for me.", "“자, 나는 기개 있는 여자답게 마지막 밑천을 걸겠어요."], ["I am not born to sit still and do nothing.", "차가운 신중함 따위는 내게 어울리지 않아요."], ["If I lose the game, it shall not be from not striving for it.”", "나는 가만히 앉아 아무것도 안 하도록 태어난 사람이 아니거든요. 내가 이 판을 진다면, 그건 애써 겨루지 않아서는 아닐 거예요.”"]], [["The game was hers, and only did not pay her for what she had given to secure it.", "그 판은 그녀의 것이 되었으나, 그것을 손에 넣으려고 그녀가 치른 값을 갚아 주지는 못했다."], ["Another deal proceeded, and Crawford began again about Thornton Lacey.", "다음 패가 돌려졌고, 크로퍼드는 다시 손턴 레이시 얘기를 꺼냈다."]], [["“My plan may not be the best possible: I had not many minutes to form it in; but you must do a good deal.", "“내 계획이 될 수 있는 최선은 아닐지도 몰라. 그걸 짤 겨를이 몇 분도 없었으니까."], ["The place deserves it, and you will find yourself not satisfied with much less than it is capable of.", "하지만 자네는 손을 꽤 많이 대야 하네. 그곳은 그럴 만하고, 자네도 그곳이 낼 수 있는 것에 한참 못 미치는 걸로는 결코 만족 못 하게 될 거야."], ["(Excuse me, your ladyship must not see your cards.", "(실례합니다, 마님, 패를 보시면 안 됩니다."], ["There, let them lie just before you.) The place deserves it, Bertram.", "자, 그냥 앞에 그대로 엎어 두세요.) 그곳은 그럴 만해, 버트럼. 자네는 신사가 사는 집다운 기품을 준다는 얘길 하지."], ["You talk of giving it the air of a gentleman’s residence.", "그거야 헛간 마당을 치우기만 하면 절로 되는 걸세."], ["That will be done by the removal of the farmyard; for, independent of that terrible nuisance, I never saw a house of the kind which had in itself so much the air of a gentleman’s residence, so much the look of a something above a mere parsonage-house—above the expenditure of a few hundreds a year.", "그 지긋지긋한 눈엣가시만 빼놓고 보면, 나는 그만큼 그 자체로 신사가 사는 집다운 기품을 지닌, 그만큼 한낱 목사관 이상의—한 해 몇백 파운드 쓰는 살림 이상의 것으로 보이는 그런 집을 본 적이 없거든. 그건 낮은 단칸방들을 창문 수만큼이나 많은 지붕으로 어수선하게 이어 붙인 그런 집이 아니야."], ["It is not a scrambling collection of low single rooms, with as many roofs as windows; it is not cramped into the vulgar compactness of a square farmhouse: it is a solid, roomy, mansion-like looking house, such as one might suppose a respectable old country family had lived in from generation to generation, through two centuries at least, and were now spending from two to three thousand a year in.”", "네모난 농가의 촌스러운 옹색함에 오그라든 집도 아니고. 그건 튼튼하고 널찍한, 저택 같은 풍채의 집이라, 점잖은 오랜 시골 명문이 대대로, 적어도 두 세기 내내 살아오면서 이제 한 해에 이삼천 파운드씩 들이는 그런 집이려니 싶은 그런 집일세.”"], ["Miss Crawford listened, and Edmund agreed to this.", "크로퍼드 양이 귀 기울여 들었고, 에드먼드도 이에 수긍했다."], ["“The air of a gentleman’s residence, therefore, you cannot but give it, if you do anything.", "“그러니 자네가 무언가 손을 대기만 하면, 신사가 사는 집다운 기품이야 안 줄 수가 없어. 하지만 그건 그보다 훨씬 더한 것도 될 수 있네."], ["But it is capable of much more.", "(어디 보자, 메리."], ["(Let me see, Mary; Lady Bertram bids a dozen for that queen; no, no, a dozen is more than it is worth.", "버트럼 부인이 저 퀸에 열둘을 부르시는군. 아니야, 아냐, 열둘은 그 값어치보다 많아."], ["Lady Bertram does not bid a dozen.", "버트럼 부인은 열둘을 안 부르셔."], ["She will have nothing to say to it.", "그 퀸엔 볼일이 없으시고 말고."], ["Go on, go on.) By some such improvements as I have suggested (I do not really require you to proceed upon my plan, though, by the bye, I doubt anybody’s striking out a better) you may give it a higher character.", "계속해, 계속.) 내가 내놓은 것 같은 그런 손질 몇 가지로 (자네더러 정말 내 계획대로 하라는 건 아니야. 하기야 누가 더 나은 걸 짜낼 수 있을지는 못 미덥지만) 자네는 그곳에 한층 높은 격을 줄 수 있어."], ["You may raise it into a place.", "그걸 하나의 명소로 끌어올릴 수 있는 거지."], ["From being the mere gentleman’s residence, it becomes, by judicious improvement, the residence of a man of education, taste, modern manners, good connexions.", "한낱 신사의 집이던 것이, 안목 있는 손질을 거치면 교양과 취향과 요즘풍 몸가짐과 좋은 인연을 갖춘 사람의 거처가 되는 걸세. 이 모두를 거기에 새겨 넣을 수 있어."], ["All this may be stamped on it; and that house receive such an air as to make its owner be set down as the great landholder of the parish by every creature travelling the road; especially as there is no real squire’s house to dispute the point—a circumstance, between ourselves, to enhance the value of such a situation in point of privilege and independence beyond all calculation.", "그리하여 그 집이 어떤 풍채를 얻으면, 그 길을 지나는 이라면 누구나 그 주인을 이 교구의 큰 지주로 여기게 되지. 더구나 그걸 두고 다툴 진짜 향사의 저택도 없으니 말이야—우리끼리 얘기지만, 이건 그런 자리의 값어치를 특권과 독립이라는 점에서 이루 헤아릴 수 없이 높여 줄 사정이거든."], ["You think with me, I hope”", "양도 나와 같은 생각이시겠지요.”"], ["(turning with a softened voice to Fanny). “Have you ever seen the place?”", "(부드러워진 목소리로 패니 쪽을 돌아보며.) “그곳을 본 적 있으세요?”"]], [["Fanny gave a quick negative, and tried to hide her interest in the subject by an eager attention to her brother, who was driving as hard a bargain, and imposing on her as much as he could; but Crawford pursued with “No, no, you must not part with the queen.", "패니는 얼른 아니라고 대답하고는, 이 화제에 쏠린 제 관심을 감추려 오라비 쪽에 열심히 마음을 쏟았다. 오라비는 더없이 야박한 흥정을 붙이며 있는 힘껏 그녀에게서 뜯어내려 하고 있었다."], ["You have bought her too dearly, and your brother does not offer half her value.", "그러나 크로퍼드는 말을 이었다. “안 돼요, 안 돼, 퀸을 내놓으시면 안 됩니다."], ["No, no, sir, hands off, hands off.", "너무 비싸게 사셨고, 이 오라비는 그 값의 절반도 못 부르고 있잖아요."], ["Your sister does not part with the queen.", "아냐, 아냐, 손 떼시게, 손 떼. 자네 누이는 퀸을 안 내놓아."], ["She is quite determined.", "아주 단단히 마음먹으셨으니."], ["The game will be yours,”", "이 판은 양의 것이 될 겁니다.”"], ["turning to her again; “it will certainly be yours.”", "하고 다시 그녀 쪽을 돌아보며, “틀림없이 양의 것이 될 거예요.”"]], [["“And Fanny had much rather it were William’s,”", "“한데 패니는 차라리 그게 윌리엄 것이 되기를 훨씬 바랄걸.”"], ["said Edmund, smiling at her.", "에드먼드가 그녀에게 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“Poor Fanny!", "“가엾은 패니!"], ["not allowed to cheat herself as she wishes!”", "제 바람대로 제 손해를 보게 놔두질 않으니!”"]], [["“Mr. Bertram,”", "“버트럼 씨,”"], ["said Miss Crawford, a few minutes afterwards, “you know Henry to be such a capital improver, that you cannot possibly engage in anything of the sort at Thornton Lacey without accepting his help.", "하고 몇 분 뒤 크로퍼드 양이 말했다. “댁도 아시다시피 헨리가 워낙 대단한 손질꾼이라, 손턴 레이시에서 그런 일을 벌이시면서 이이 도움을 안 받으실 수야 없을 거예요."], ["Only think how useful he was at Sotherton!", "소더턴에서 이이가 얼마나 쓸모 있었는지 생각만 해 보세요!"], ["Only think what grand things were produced there by our all going with him one hot day in August to drive about the grounds, and see his genius take fire.", "팔월 어느 무더운 날, 우리 다 함께 이이를 따라나서 그 영지를 돌아보며 이이의 재주가 불붙는 걸 지켜본 덕에, 거기서 얼마나 굉장한 것들이 나왔는지 생각만 해 보시라니까요."], ["There we went, and there we came home again; and what was done there is not to be told!”", "우리는 거기로 갔다가 다시 집으로 돌아왔지요. 그리고 거기서 이뤄진 것은 이루 말로 다 못 할 정도예요!”"]], [["Fanny’s eyes were turned on Crawford for a moment with an expression more than grave—even reproachful; but on catching his, were instantly withdrawn.", "패니의 눈길이 한순간, 근엄함을 넘어—나무람마저 어린 낯빛으로 크로퍼드에게로 향했다. 그러나 그의 눈길과 마주치자 이내 거두어졌다."], ["With something of consciousness he shook his head at his sister, and laughingly replied, “I cannot say there was much done at Sotherton; but it was a hot day, and we were all walking after each other, and bewildered.”", "그는 얼마간 켕기는 기색으로 누이를 향해 고개를 젓고는, 웃으며 대꾸했다. “소더턴에서 이뤄진 게 많다고는 못 하겠는걸."], ["As soon as a general buzz gave him shelter, he added, in a low voice, directed solely at Fanny, “I should be sorry to have my powers of planning judged of by the day at Sotherton.", "그날은 무덥고, 우리 다 서로 뒤를 쫓아 걷느라 정신이 없었으니까.” 이내 두런거리는 소리가 일어 그를 가려 주자, 그는 오로지 패니만을 향해 나직한 목소리로 덧붙였다."], ["I see things very differently now.", "“나의 꾸미는 솜씨를 소더턴에서의 그날로 가늠하시게 되면 나야 서운하지요."], ["Do not think of me as I appeared then.”", "지금은 사뭇 다르게 본답니다. 그때 비친 모습으로 나를 여기지는 마세요.”"]], [["Sotherton was a word to catch Mrs.", "소더턴은 노리스 부인의 귀를 잡아채는 낱말이었다."], ["Norris, and being just then in the happy leisure which followed securing the odd trick by Sir Thomas’s capital play and her own against Dr.", "마침 토머스 경의 멋진 솜씨와 제 솜씨로 그랜트 박사 부부의 좋은 패를 눌러 승부를 가른 그 한 끗을 따낸 뒤 찾아온 흐뭇한 여유에 잠겨 있던 터라, 그녀는 자못 기분 좋게 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["and Mrs.", "“소더턴!"], ["Grant’s great hands, she called out, in high good-humour, “Sotherton!", "그럼, 거긴 정말이지 대단한 곳이지, 우리가 거기서 참 좋은 하루를 보냈고 말이야."], ["Yes, that is a place, indeed, and we had a charming day there.", "윌리엄, 너는 운이 참 없구나."], ["William, you are quite out of luck; but the next time you come, I hope dear Mr.", "하지만 다음에 오거든, 그때는 착한 러시워스 내외가 집에 있으면 좋겠구나."], ["and Mrs. Rushworth will be at home, and I am sure I can answer for your being kindly received by both.", "두 사람한테 네가 다정한 대접을 받으리라는 건 내가 장담하마."], ["Your cousins are not of a sort to forget their relations, and Mr. Rushworth is a most amiable man.", "네 사촌들은 제 피붙이를 잊을 사람들이 아니고, 러시워스 씨는 더없이 상냥한 분이거든."], ["They are at Brighton now, you know; in one of the best houses there, as Mr.", "그이들이 지금은 브라이턴에 가 있단다, 알겠니. 거기서도 손꼽히는 좋은 집 가운데 하나에 말이야."], ["Rushworth’s fine fortune gives them a right to be.", "러시워스 씨의 넉넉한 재산이 그럴 자격을 주니까."], ["I do not exactly know the distance, but when you get back to Portsmouth, if it is not very far off, you ought to go over and pay your respects to them; and I could send a little parcel by you that I want to get conveyed to your cousins.”", "거리가 얼마나 되는지 나야 정확힌 모르지만, 네가 포츠머스로 돌아가거든, 그리 멀지만 않다면 마땅히 건너가 문안을 여쭈어야지. 그리고 나도 네 사촌들한테 꼭 전하고픈 자그마한 꾸러미 하나를 네 편에 부칠 수 있을 테고.”"]], [["“I should be very happy, aunt; but Brighton is almost by Beachey Head; and if I could get so far, I could not expect to be welcome in such a smart place as that—poor scrubby midshipman as I am.”", "“저야 무척 기쁘겠지요, 이모. 하지만 브라이턴은 비치 헤드 바로 옆이나 다름없는걸요. 게다가 제가 거기까지 갈 수 있다 한들, 그토록 번드르르한 곳에서 환대받기를 바랄 수야 없지요—보잘것없는 초라한 사관후보생인 제가 말이에요.”"]], [["Mrs.", "노리스 부인이, 그가 믿어도 좋을 그 살가운 대접을 열띠게 장담하려던 참인데, 토머스 경이 위엄 있게 말하며 그녀를 막았다."], ["Norris was beginning an eager assurance of the affability he might depend on, when she was stopped by Sir Thomas’s saying with authority, “I do not advise your going to Brighton, William, as I trust you may soon have more convenient opportunities of meeting; but my daughters would be happy to see their cousins anywhere; and you will find Mr.", "“나는 자네가 브라이턴에 가는 건 권하지 않네, 윌리엄. 머잖아 더 편한 자리에서 만날 기회가 생기리라 믿으니 말일세."], ["Rushworth most sincerely disposed to regard all the connexions of our family as his own.”", "하지만 내 딸들은 어디서든 제 사촌을 반겨 볼 테고, 자네도 러시워스 씨가 우리 집안의 온갖 인연을 제 것처럼 여기려는 더없이 진실한 사람임을 알게 될 걸세.”"]], [["“I would rather find him private secretary to the First Lord than anything else,”", "“저야 그이가 그 무엇보다도 차라리 해군장관의 개인 비서로나 있어 주면 좋겠는데요.”"], ["was William’s only answer, in an undervoice, not meant to reach far, and the subject dropped.", "이것이 윌리엄의 유일한 대꾸였는데, 나직한 소리라 멀리까지 닿으라고 한 말은 아니었다. 그리고 그 화제는 그것으로 흐지부지되었다."]], [["As yet Sir Thomas had seen nothing to remark in Mr. Crawford’s behaviour; but when the whist-table broke up at the end of the second rubber, and leaving Dr.", "여태 토머스 경은 크로퍼드 씨의 행동에서 이렇다 할 것을 알아채지 못했다."], ["Grant and Mrs. Norris to dispute over their last play, he became a looker-on at the other, he found his niece the object of attentions, or rather of professions, of a somewhat pointed character.", "그러나 둘째 판이 끝나 휘스트 탁자가 파하고, 그랜트 박사와 노리스 부인이 저희 마지막 판을 두고 옥신각신하게 내버려 둔 채 그가 다른 탁자의 구경꾼이 되었을 때, 그는 제 조카딸이 얼마간 노골적인 빛깔을 띤 마음씀의—아니, 차라리 공언(公言)의—과녁이 되어 있음을 알아챘다."]], [["Henry Crawford was in the first glow of another scheme about Thornton Lacey; and not being able to catch Edmund’s ear, was detailing it to his fair neighbour with a look of considerable earnestness.", "헨리 크로퍼드는 손턴 레이시를 두고 또 다른 계획에 막 달아오른 참이었다. 에드먼드의 귀를 잡을 수가 없자, 그는 자못 진지한 낯빛으로 그것을 제 아리따운 옆자리 이에게 낱낱이 늘어놓고 있었다."], ["His scheme was to rent the house himself the following winter, that he might have a home of his own in that neighbourhood; and it was not merely for the use of it in the hunting-season (as he was then telling her), though that consideration had certainly some weight, feeling as he did that, in spite of all Dr.", "그의 계획인즉, 이듬해 겨울 그 집을 제가 직접 세내어 그 근방에 제 집을 하나 두겠다는 것이었다. 그것은 (그가 그때 그녀에게 말하고 있던 대로) 그저 사냥철에 쓰려는 것만은 아니었다—하기야 그 생각도 분명 얼마간 무게가 있기는 했으니, 그는 그랜트 박사의 그 크나큰 친절에도 불구하고, 지금 머무는 곳에서는 저와 제 말들을 큰 불편 없이 건사하기가 도무지 어렵다고 느끼고 있었던 것이다."], ["Grant’s very great kindness, it was impossible for him and his horses to be accommodated where they now were without material inconvenience; but his attachment to that neighbourhood did not depend upon one amusement or one season of the year: he had set his heart upon having a something there that he could come to at any time, a little homestall at his command, where all the holidays of his year might be spent, and he might find himself continuing, improving, and perfecting that friendship and intimacy with the Mansfield Park family which was increasing in value to him every day.", "그러나 그 근방에 대한 그의 애착은 어느 한 가지 놀이나 어느 한 철에 달린 것이 아니었다. 그는 언제든 찾아올 수 있는 무언가를, 제 뜻대로 쓸 수 있는 자그마한 보금자리 하나를 그곳에 두어, 한 해의 온갖 휴일을 거기서 보내고, 날마다 그 값어치를 더해 가는 맨스필드 파크 식구와의 그 우정과 친분을 이어 가고 두터이 하며 온전케 해 가는 저 자신을 보게 되기를 마음속으로 굳게 바라고 있었다."], ["Sir Thomas heard and was not offended.", "토머스 경은 이를 듣고도 언짢아하지 않았다."], ["There was no want of respect in the young man’s address; and Fanny’s reception of it was so proper and modest, so calm and uninviting, that he had nothing to censure in her.", "그 젊은이의 말투에는 무례함이 조금도 없었고, 패니가 그것을 받아들이는 품이 어찌나 온당하고 겸손하며, 어찌나 차분하고 부추기는 데가 없던지, 그로서는 그녀에게서 나무랄 것이 하나도 없었다."], ["She said little, assented only here and there, and betrayed no inclination either of appropriating any part of the compliment to herself, or of strengthening his views in favour of Northamptonshire.", "그녀는 말을 아꼈고, 여기저기서 그저 맞장구만 쳤을 뿐, 그 찬사의 어느 대목이든 제 것으로 삼으려는 기색도, 노샘프턴셔에 마음 두라는 그의 뜻을 북돋우려는 기색도 조금도 내비치지 않았다."], ["Finding by whom he was observed, Henry Crawford addressed himself on the same subject to Sir Thomas, in a more everyday tone, but still with feeling.", "누가 저를 지켜보고 있는지를 알아챈 헨리 크로퍼드는, 같은 화제를 두고 이번에는 좀 더 예사로운 투로, 그러나 여전히 정을 담아 토머스 경에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“I want to be your neighbour, Sir Thomas, as you have, perhaps, heard me telling Miss Price.", "“저는 경의 이웃이 되고 싶습니다, 토머스 경. 어쩌면 제가 프라이스 양께 그리 말하는 걸 들으셨겠지요."], ["May I hope for your acquiescence, and for your not influencing your son against such a tenant?”", "경께서 승낙해 주시기를, 그리고 그런 세입자를 마다하라고 아드님을 부추기지 않으시기를 바라도 되겠습니까?”"]], [["Sir Thomas, politely bowing, replied, “It is the only way, sir, in which I could not wish you established as a permanent neighbour; but I hope, and believe, that Edmund will occupy his own house at Thornton Lacey.", "토머스 경은 정중히 목례하며 대답했다. “여보게, 자네를 이곳의 붙박이 이웃으로 삼고 싶지 않은 길은 오직 이 한 가지뿐일세."], ["Edmund, am I saying too much?”", "나는 에드먼드가 손턴 레이시의 제집에 들어가 살리라 바라고, 또 그리 믿으니 말이야. 에드먼드, 내가 지나친 말을 하는 겐가?”"]], [["Edmund, on this appeal, had first to hear what was going on; but, on understanding the question, was at no loss for an answer.", "에드먼드는 이렇게 불려서, 우선 무슨 얘기가 오가고 있는지부터 들어야 했다. 그러나 그 물음을 알아듣고 나자 대답이 궁하지 않았다."]], [["“Certainly, sir, I have no idea but of residence.", "“그야 물론이지요, 아버지, 저는 거기 사는 것 말고는 다른 생각이 없습니다."], ["But, Crawford, though I refuse you as a tenant, come to me as a friend.", "하지만 크로퍼드, 나는 자네를 세입자로는 마다해도, 벗으로는 오게."], ["Consider the house as half your own every winter, and we will add to the stables on your own improved plan, and with all the improvements of your improved plan that may occur to you this spring.”", "겨울마다 그 집을 반은 자네 것이라 여기게. 그리고 우리는 자네가 손질한 그 계획대로, 게다가 이번 봄에 자네한테 떠오를 온갖 손질까지 더해서 마구간을 늘리세.”"]], [["“We shall be the losers,”", "“우리로선 손해일세.”"], ["continued Sir Thomas.", "토머스 경이 말을 이었다."], ["“His going, though only eight miles, will be an unwelcome contraction of our family circle; but I should have been deeply mortified if any son of mine could reconcile himself to doing less.", "“저 아이가 떠나는 건 겨우 팔 마일이라 해도 우리 식구 울타리가 달갑잖게 오그라드는 일이니 말이야. 하지만 내 아들 가운데 누구라도 그보다 못한 일에 스스로 만족한다면 나는 깊이 부끄러웠을 걸세."], ["It is perfectly natural that you should not have thought much on the subject, Mr. Crawford.", "자네가 이 문제를 별로 깊이 생각해 보지 못한 거야 지극히 자연스러운 일이야, 크로퍼드 씨."], ["But a parish has wants and claims which can be known only by a clergyman constantly resident, and which no proxy can be capable of satisfying to the same extent.", "그러나 교구에는 늘 붙박여 사는 성직자라야만 알 수 있는, 그리고 어떤 대리인도 그만큼 채워 줄 수 없는 그런 필요와 요구가 있는 법일세."], ["Edmund might, in the common phrase, do the duty of Thornton, that is, he might read prayers and preach, without giving up Mansfield Park: he might ride over every Sunday, to a house nominally inhabited, and go through divine service; he might be the clergyman of Thornton Lacey every seventh day, for three or four hours, if that would content him.", "에드먼드가 흔한 말로 손턴의 소임을 볼 수야 있겠지, 그러니까 맨스필드 파크를 떠나지 않고도 기도를 읽고 설교를 할 수야 있겠지. 이름뿐인 집에 일요일마다 말 타고 건너가 예배를 치를 수야 있고, 이레마다 서너 시간씩 손턴 레이시의 성직자가 될 수야 있겠지, 그거로 흡족하다면 말이야."], ["But it will not.", "하지만 그거로는 안 될 걸세."], ["He knows that human nature needs more lessons than a weekly sermon can convey; and that if he does not live among his parishioners, and prove himself, by constant attention, their well-wisher and friend, he does very little either for their good or his own.”", "저 아이는 사람의 본성이 주간 설교 한 번으로 전할 수 있는 것보다 더 많은 가르침을 필요로 함을 알고, 또 제가 제 교구민들 틈에 살며 한결같은 마음씀으로 저 자신이 그들을 위하고 아끼는 벗임을 증명해 보이지 않고서는, 그들의 이로움을 위해서든 제 이로움을 위해서든 하는 것이 지극히 하찮음을 아니까.”"]], [["Mr. Crawford bowed his acquiescence.", "크로퍼드 씨가 목례로 수긍했다."]], [["“I repeat again,”", "“다시 한번 말하네만,” 하고 토머스 경이 덧붙였다."], ["added Sir Thomas, “that Thornton Lacey is the only house in the neighbourhood in which I should not be happy to wait on Mr. Crawford as occupier.”", "“손턴 레이시야말로 이 근방에서 크로퍼드 씨를 세입자로 맞아 반기지 않을 유일한 집일세.”"]], [["Mr. Crawford bowed his thanks.", "크로퍼드 씨가 목례로 고마움을 표했다."]], [["“Sir Thomas,”", "“토머스 경께서는,”"], ["said Edmund, “undoubtedly understands the duty of a parish priest.", "에드먼드가 말했다. “교구 사제의 소임을 틀림없이 꿰고 계시지요."], ["We must hope his son may prove that he knows it too.”", "그 아드님도 저 역시 그걸 안다는 걸 증명해 보이기를 우리는 바라야겠고요.”"]], [["Whatever effect Sir Thomas’s little harangue might really produce on Mr.", "토머스 경의 그 자그마한 일장 연설이 크로퍼드 씨에게 실제로 무슨 효험을 냈든 간에, 그것은 다른 두 사람—더없이 열심히 귀 기울이던 두 사람, 곧 크로퍼드 양과 패니에게 얼마간 거북한 심경을 불러일으켰다."], ["Crawford, it raised some awkward sensations in two of the others, two of his most attentive listeners—Miss Crawford and Fanny.", "그 하나는, 손턴이 그토록 이르고 그토록 온전히 그의 보금자리가 되리라는 것을 여태 미처 몰랐던지라, 날마다 에드먼드를 못 보게 된다는 것이 어떤 것일지를 눈을 내리깐 채 곱씹고 있었다."], ["One of whom, having never before understood that Thornton was so soon and so completely to be his home, was pondering with downcast eyes on what it would be not to see Edmund every day; and the other, startled from the agreeable fancies she had been previously indulging on the strength of her brother’s description, no longer able, in the picture she had been forming of a future Thornton, to shut out the church, sink the clergyman, and see only the respectable, elegant, modernised, and occasional residence of a man of independent fortune, was considering Sir Thomas, with decided ill-will, as the destroyer of all this, and suffering the more from that involuntary forbearance which his character and manner commanded, and from not daring to relieve herself by a single attempt at throwing ridicule on his cause.", "그리고 다른 하나는, 앞서 제 오라비의 이야기에 기대어 흐뭇한 공상에 잠겨 있던 참에 화들짝 놀라, 저 자신이 그려 오던 미래의 손턴 그림에서 이제는 교회를 지워 내고 성직자를 가라앉힌 채 오직 재산이 넉넉한 독립한 사내가 이따금 머무는, 점잖고 우아하고 요즘풍으로 손질된 거처만을 볼 수 없게 되었으니, 토머스 경을 이 모두를 허문 이로 여겨 뚜렷한 미움을 품고 있었다. 게다가 그의 사람됨과 태도가 자아내는 그 어쩔 수 없는 삼감 탓에, 그리고 그의 논지에 감히 비웃음 한번 던져 제 속을 풀지도 못하는 탓에 그녀는 한층 더 괴로웠다."]], [["All the agreeable of her speculation was over for that hour.", "그녀의 스페큘레이션이 지녔던 온갖 즐거움이 그 시각으로 다한 셈이었다."], ["It was time to have done with cards, if sermons prevailed; and she was glad to find it necessary to come to a conclusion, and be able to refresh her spirits by a change of place and neighbour.", "설교가 판을 치는 마당이라면 카드도 그만둘 때였고, 그녀는 이만 끝맺어야겠다 싶은 것이 다행스러웠으니, 자리와 옆 사람을 바꾸어 제 기분을 되살릴 수 있게 된 까닭이었다."]], [["The chief of the party were now collected irregularly round the fire, and waiting the final break-up.", "이제 일행의 주된 이들은 난롯가에 들쭉날쭉 모여, 마지막 파장을 기다리고 있었다."], ["William and Fanny were the most detached.", "윌리엄과 패니가 가장 동떨어져 있었다."], ["They remained together at the otherwise deserted card-table, talking very comfortably, and not thinking of the rest, till some of the rest began to think of them.", "그 둘은 이제 아무도 없는 카드 탁자에 함께 남아, 남들 생각은 하지도 않은 채 더없이 편안히 이야기를 나누었는데, 마침내 남은 이들 가운데 몇이 그 둘을 눈여겨보기 시작했다. 헨리 크로퍼드의 의자가 맨 먼저 그 둘 쪽으로 방향을 틀었고, 그는 잠깐 말없이 그들을 지켜보며 앉아 있었다."], ["Henry Crawford’s chair was the first to be given a direction towards them, and he sat silently observing them for a few minutes; himself, in the meanwhile, observed by Sir Thomas, who was standing in chat with Dr. Grant.", "그러는 사이 그 자신은, 그랜트 박사와 담소하며 서 있던 토머스 경에게 지켜보이고 있었다."]], [["“This is the assembly night,”", "“오늘이 무도회 날이야.”"], ["said William.", "윌리엄이 말했다."], ["“If I were at Portsmouth I should be at it, perhaps.”", "“내가 포츠머스에 있었으면 아마 거기 가 있었겠지.”"]], [["“But you do not wish yourself at Portsmouth, William?”", "“하지만 넌 포츠머스에 있고 싶은 건 아니잖아, 윌리엄?”"]], [["“No, Fanny, that I do not.", "“그럼, 패니, 있고 싶지 않고말고."], ["I shall have enough of Portsmouth and of dancing too, when I cannot have you.", "포츠머스도, 춤도, 너 없이 지내야 할 때면 나야 실컷 겪을 텐데 뭐."], ["And I do not know that there would be any good in going to the assembly, for I might not get a partner.", "게다가 무도회에 간들 딱히 좋을 것 같지도 않아, 짝을 못 구할지도 모르니까."], ["The Portsmouth girls turn up their noses at anybody who has not a commission.", "포츠머스 계집애들은 임관장 없는 사람은 누구든 콧대를 세우고 깔봐."], ["One might as well be nothing as a midshipman.", "사관후보생쯤이야 아무것도 아닌 거나 매한가지지."], ["One is nothing, indeed.", "사실 아무것도 아니고 말이야."], ["You remember the Gregorys; they are grown up amazing fine girls, but they will hardly speak to me, because Lucy is courted by a lieutenant.”", "너 그레고리네 알지. 놀랄 만큼 고운 처녀들로 자랐는데, 나한테는 말도 잘 안 걸려, 루시가 중위한테 구애를 받고 있다고 말이야.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“어머!"], ["shame, shame!", "창피해라, 창피해!"], ["But never mind it, William”", "하지만 마음 쓰지 마, 윌리엄.”"], ["(her own cheeks in a glow of indignation as she spoke). “It is not worth minding.", "(이 말을 하는 그녀의 두 뺨이 분한 마음에 발갛게 달아올랐다.) “그런 거 마음 둘 값어치도 없어."], ["It is no reflection on you; it is no more than what the greatest admirals have all experienced, more or less, in their time.", "너를 두고 하는 얘기도 아니잖아. 그건 그저 이름난 제독들도 저마다 한창때에 다들 더러 겪어 낸 것에 지나지 않아."], ["You must think of that, you must try to make up your mind to it as one of the hardships which fall to every sailor’s share, like bad weather and hard living, only with this advantage, that there will be an end to it, that there will come a time when you will have nothing of that sort to endure.", "그걸 생각해야지, 궂은 날씨나 고된 살림처럼 뱃사람이라면 누구나 겪는 고생거리의 하나려니 하고 마음을 다잡아야 해. 다만 이런 좋은 점이 있잖아, 거기엔 끝이 있고, 그런 걸 하나도 견딜 필요 없는 때가 오리라는 것 말이야."], ["When you are a lieutenant!", "네가 중위가 되면!"], ["only think, William, when you are a lieutenant, how little you will care for any nonsense of this kind.”", "생각만 해 봐, 윌리엄, 네가 중위가 되면 이런 부질없는 일 따위야 얼마나 아무렇지 않겠어.”"]], [["“I begin to think I shall never be a lieutenant, Fanny.", "“나는 아무래도 영영 중위가 못 될 것 같아, 패니."], ["Everybody gets made but me.”", "다들 임관하는데 나만 못 하니.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“어머!"], ["my dear William, do not talk so; do not be so desponding.", "사랑하는 윌리엄, 그런 말 마. 그렇게 낙심하지 좀 마."], ["My uncle says nothing, but I am sure he will do everything in his power to get you made.", "삼촌이 아무 말씀 안 하셔도, 나는 삼촌이 너를 임관시키려고 온 힘을 다 쓰시리라 믿어."], ["He knows, as well as you do, of what consequence it is.”", "삼촌도 너만큼이나 그게 얼마나 중한 일인지 아시거든.”"]], [["She was checked by the sight of her uncle much nearer to them than she had any suspicion of, and each found it necessary to talk of something else.", "그녀는, 저희가 짐작한 것보다 훨씬 가까이에 삼촌이 와 있는 것을 보고 말문이 막혔다. 그리하여 둘 다 다른 얘기를 꺼낼 밖에 없었다."]], [["“Are you fond of dancing, Fanny?”", "“넌 춤추는 거 좋아하니, 패니?”"]], [["“Yes, very; only I am soon tired.”", "“응, 아주. 다만 나는 금세 지쳐 버려.”"]], [["“I should like to go to a ball with you and see you dance.", "“나는 너랑 무도회에 가서 네가 춤추는 걸 보고 싶어."], ["Have you never any balls at Northampton?", "노샘프턴에서는 무도회 같은 거 한 번도 없었니?"], ["I should like to see you dance, and I’d dance with you if you would, for nobody would know who I was here, and I should like to be your partner once more.", "나는 네가 춤추는 걸 보고 싶고, 네가 춰 준다면 나도 너랑 추고 싶어. 여기선 아무도 내가 누군지 모를 테고, 나야 다시 한번 네 짝이 되고 싶으니까."], ["We used to jump about together many a time, did not we?", "우리 예전엔 곧잘 함께 팔짝팔짝 뛰놀았잖아, 안 그래?"], ["when the hand-organ was in the street?", "거리에 손풍금이 나왔을 때 말이야."], ["I am a pretty good dancer in my way, but I dare say you are a better.”", "나도 내 나름으론 제법 춤을 추지만, 아마 네가 나보다 나을 거야.”"], ["And turning to his uncle, who was now close to them, “Is not Fanny a very good dancer, sir?”", "그러고는 이제 저희 가까이에 와 있는 삼촌 쪽을 돌아보며, “패니가 아주 춤을 잘 추지 않나요, 삼촌?”"]], [["Fanny, in dismay at such an unprecedented question, did not know which way to look, or how to be prepared for the answer.", "패니는 이런 뜻밖의 물음에 당황해, 어디를 봐야 할지도, 그 대답을 어찌 맞을지도 몰랐다."], ["Some very grave reproof, or at least the coldest expression of indifference, must be coming to distress her brother, and sink her to the ground.", "무언가 자못 근엄한 나무람이, 아니면 적어도 더없이 싸늘한 무관심의 말이 나와 제 오라비를 곤란케 하고 저를 땅속으로 꺼지게 만들 것만 같았다. 그러나 뜻밖에도 그것은 이보다 나쁠 게 없었다."], ["But, on the contrary, it was no worse than, “I am sorry to say that I am unable to answer your question.", "“유감스럽지만 나는 자네 물음엔 답해 줄 수가 없구먼. 패니가 어릴 적 이래로 나는 그 애가 춤추는 걸 본 적이 없으니 말이야."], ["I have never seen Fanny dance since she was a little girl; but I trust we shall both think she acquits herself like a gentlewoman when we do see her, which, perhaps, we may have an opportunity of doing ere long.”", "하지만 우리 둘 다 언젠가 그 애를 보게 되면 그 애가 숙녀답게 잘해 내리라 믿네. 그리고 머잖아 그럴 기회가 우리에게 있을지도 모르지.”"]], [["“I have had the pleasure of seeing your sister dance, Mr. Price,”", "“저는 댁의 누이가 춤추는 걸 본 즐거움이 있습니다, 프라이스 씨.”"], ["said Henry Crawford, leaning forward, “and will engage to answer every inquiry which you can make on the subject, to your entire satisfaction.", "하고 헨리 크로퍼드가 몸을 앞으로 기울이며 말했다. “그러니 그 일을 두고 자네가 물을 수 있는 무엇이든, 자네가 온전히 흡족하도록 답해 드리기로 하지요."], ["But I believe”", "다만 아무래도”"], ["(seeing Fanny looked distressed) “it must be at some other time.", "(패니가 괴로워하는 낯빛인 것을 보고) “그건 언제 다른 때라야겠어요."], ["There is one person in company who does not like to have Miss Price spoken of.”", "이 자리에 프라이스 양 얘기가 오르내리는 걸 달가워하지 않는 이가 한 사람 있으니까요.”"]], [["True enough, he had once seen Fanny dance; and it was equally true that he would now have answered for her gliding about with quiet, light elegance, and in admirable time; but, in fact, he could not for the life of him recall what her dancing had been, and rather took it for granted that she had been present than remembered anything about her.", "과연 그는 언젠가 패니가 춤추는 것을 본 적이 있었다. 그리고 그녀가 나긋하고 가뿐한 우아함으로, 흠잡을 데 없는 박자에 맞추어 미끄러지듯 돌더라고 이제 장담해 준 것 또한 못지않게 사실이었다. 그러나 실은 그녀의 춤이 어떠했는지 죽어도 도무지 떠올릴 수가 없었고, 그녀에 관해 무엇을 기억한다기보다 그저 그녀가 그 자리에 있었으려니 지레짐작한 것에 가까웠다."]], [["He passed, however, for an admirer of her dancing; and Sir Thomas, by no means displeased, prolonged the conversation on dancing in general, and was so well engaged in describing the balls of Antigua, and listening to what his nephew could relate of the different modes of dancing which had fallen within his observation, that he had not heard his carriage announced, and was first called to the knowledge of it by the bustle of Mrs.", "그래도 그는 그녀의 춤을 흠모하는 이로 통했다."], ["Norris.", "그리고 토머스 경은 조금도 언짢아하기는커녕 춤 일반을 두고 이야기를 길게 이어 가, 안티과의 무도회를 늘어놓고 여러 다른 춤 방식에 대해—그 조카가 제 눈으로 본 대로 들려줄 수 있는 것을 듣는 데 어찌나 흠뻑 빠져 있던지, 제 마차가 왔다는 전갈도 못 듣고, 노리스 부인의 부산 떠는 소리에야 비로소 그것을 알아챘다."]], [["“Come, Fanny, Fanny, what are you about?", "“자, 패니, 패니, 뭘 하고 있는 게냐?"], ["We are going.", "우리 가야지."], ["Do not you see your aunt is going?", "이모가 가는 게 안 보이니?"], ["Quick, quick!", "어서, 어서!"], ["I cannot bear to keep good old Wilcox waiting.", "나는 착한 늙은 윌콕스를 기다리게 하는 걸 못 견딘단다."], ["You should always remember the coachman and horses.", "너는 늘 마부하고 말들을 생각해야 하는 게야."], ["My dear Sir Thomas, we have settled it that the carriage should come back for you, and Edmund and William.”", "토머스 경, 마차가 경하고 에드먼드하고 윌리엄을 모시러 되돌아오도록 우리가 정해 뒀어요.”"]], [["Sir Thomas could not dissent, as it had been his own arrangement, previously communicated to his wife and sister; but that seemed forgotten by Mrs.", "토머스 경은 반대할 수가 없었으니, 그것은 애초에 저 자신이 미리 제 아내와 처형에게 일러 둔 채비였던 까닭이다."], ["Norris, who must fancy that she settled it all herself.", "그러나 노리스 부인은 그것을 잊은 듯, 그 모두를 저 혼자 정했다고 여기는 모양이었다."]], [["Fanny’s last feeling in the visit was disappointment: for the shawl which Edmund was quietly taking from the servant to bring and put round her shoulders was seized by Mr.", "이 방문에서 패니의 마지막 심경은 서운함이었다."], ["Crawford’s quicker hand, and she was obliged to be indebted to his more prominent attention.", "에드먼드가 하인에게서 조용히 숄을 받아 가져다 제 어깨에 둘러 주려던 것을, 크로퍼드 씨의 더 잰 손이 낚아채는 바람에, 그녀는 그의 더 도드라진 마음씀에 신세를 지지 않을 수 없었던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXVI", "# 제26장"]], [["William’s desire of seeing Fanny dance made more than a momentary impression on his uncle.", "패니가 춤추는 것을 보고 싶다는 윌리엄의 바람은 그 삼촌에게 한순간 스치는 인상 이상의 것을 남겼다."], ["The hope of an opportunity, which Sir Thomas had then given, was not given to be thought of no more.", "토머스 경이 그때 내비친 기회에 대한 그 바람은, 그저 한번 말해 보고 마는 것이 아니었다."], ["He remained steadily inclined to gratify so amiable a feeling; to gratify anybody else who might wish to see Fanny dance, and to give pleasure to the young people in general; and having thought the matter over, and taken his resolution in quiet independence, the result of it appeared the next morning at breakfast, when, after recalling and commending what his nephew had said, he added, “I do not like, William, that you should leave Northamptonshire without this indulgence.", "그는 그토록 갸륵한 마음을 흐뭇하게 채워 주고 싶은 뜻을, 패니가 춤추는 것을 보고 싶어 할 다른 누구든 흐뭇하게 채워 주고 젊은이들 모두를 즐겁게 해 주고 싶은 뜻을 한결같이 품고 있었다. 그리하여 그 일을 곰곰 헤아리고 조용히 혼자 마음을 정한 끝에, 그 결실이 이튿날 아침 식사 자리에서 드러났으니, 조카가 한 말을 다시 떠올려 추어 준 뒤 그가 이렇게 덧붙인 것이다."], ["It would give me pleasure to see you both dance.", "“나는 자네가 이런 호사 한번 못 누리고 노샘프턴셔를 떠나는 게 마음에 안 드네, 윌리엄."], ["You spoke of the balls at Northampton.", "나야 자네들 둘이 춤추는 걸 보면 즐겁겠어."], ["Your cousins have occasionally attended them; but they would not altogether suit us now.", "자네가 노샘프턴의 무도회 얘길 했지. 자네 사촌들도 더러 거기 다녀오긴 했네만, 지금 우리한테는 썩 어울리진 않을 걸세."], ["The fatigue would be too much for your aunt.", "자네 이모한테는 그 수고가 너무 벅찰 테니까."], ["I believe we must not think of a Northampton ball.", "아무래도 노샘프턴 무도회는 생각지 말아야겠어."], ["A dance at home would be more eligible; and if—”", "집에서 춤판을 벌이는 편이 더 나을 테고. 그리고 만일—”"]], [["“Ah, my dear Sir Thomas!”", "“아, 토머스 경!”"], ["interrupted Mrs.", "노리스 부인이 가로막았다."], ["Norris, “I knew what was coming.", "“무슨 말이 나올지 나야 알았지요."], ["I knew what you were going to say.", "무슨 말을 하려는지 다 알았어요."], ["If dear Julia were at home, or dearest Mrs. Rushworth at Sotherton, to afford a reason, an occasion for such a thing, you would be tempted to give the young people a dance at Mansfield.", "만일 착한 줄리아가 집에 있거나, 더없이 소중한 러시워스 부인이 소더턴에 있어서 그런 일에 무슨 명분이나 구실이 되어 준다면, 경께서는 맨스필드에서 젊은이들한테 춤판을 벌여 주고 싶어지실 게 뻔해요."], ["I know you would.", "나는 다 안다니까요."], ["If they were at home to grace the ball, a ball you would have this very Christmas.", "그 애들이 집에 있어 그 무도회를 빛내 준다면, 바로 이번 크리스마스에라도 무도회를 여실 거예요."], ["Thank your uncle, William, thank your uncle!”", "삼촌한테 고맙다고 해라, 윌리엄, 삼촌한테 고맙다고 해!”"]], [["“My daughters,”", "“내 딸들은,”"], ["replied Sir Thomas, gravely interposing, “have their pleasures at Brighton, and I hope are very happy; but the dance which I think of giving at Mansfield will be for their cousins.", "하고 토머스 경이 근엄하게 끼어들며 대답했다. “브라이턴에서 저희 즐거움을 누리고 있고, 나야 그 아이들이 무척 행복하기를 바라오."], ["Could we be all assembled, our satisfaction would undoubtedly be more complete, but the absence of some is not to debar the others of amusement.”", "하지만 내가 맨스필드에서 벌일까 하는 그 춤판은 그 사촌들을 위한 것이오. 우리 모두가 한자리에 모일 수 있다면 우리의 흡족함이야 물론 더 온전하겠지만, 더러 없는 이가 있다 해서 다른 이들이 즐거움을 못 누릴 것은 아니지요.”"]], [["Mrs. Norris had not another word to say.", "노리스 부인은 더 할 말이 없었다."], ["She saw decision in his looks, and her surprise and vexation required some minutes’ silence to be settled into composure.", "그녀는 그의 낯빛에서 결심을 읽었고, 그 놀라움과 언짢음이 평온으로 가라앉으려면 몇 분간의 침묵이 필요했다."], ["A ball at such a time!", "하필 이런 때에 무도회라니!"], ["His daughters absent and herself not consulted!", "제 딸들은 없고, 저와는 의논 한마디 없이!"], ["There was comfort, however, soon at hand.", "그러나 이내 위안이 다가왔다."], ["She must be the doer of everything: Lady Bertram would of course be spared all thought and exertion, and it would all fall upon her.", "자기가 온갖 일을 도맡는 사람이 되어야 할 터였다. 버트럼 부인이야 물론 온갖 궁리와 수고에서 면제될 테고, 그 모두가 자기 몫으로 떨어질 것이었다."], ["She should have to do the honours of the evening; and this reflection quickly restored so much of her good-humour as enabled her to join in with the others, before their happiness and thanks were all expressed.", "그날 저녁의 안주인 노릇을 저가 해야 할 터였다. 이 생각이 이내 그녀의 좋은 기분을 넉넉히 되살려, 다른 이들의 행복과 감사가 다 쏟아져 나오기 전에 그녀도 그 자리에 어울릴 수 있게 되었다."]], [["Edmund, William, and Fanny did, in their different ways, look and speak as much grateful pleasure in the promised ball as Sir Thomas could desire.", "에드먼드와 윌리엄과 패니는 저마다 다른 결로, 약속된 무도회에 대해 토머스 경이 바랄 만한 만큼의 고마운 기쁨을 낯빛과 말로 드러냈다."], ["Edmund’s feelings were for the other two.", "에드먼드의 심정은 다른 두 사람을 위한 것이었다."], ["His father had never conferred a favour or shewn a kindness more to his satisfaction.", "그의 아버지가 이토록 그의 마음에 흡족한 은혜를 베풀거나 친절을 보인 적은 여태 없었다."]], [["Lady Bertram was perfectly quiescent and contented, and had no objections to make.", "버트럼 부인은 더없이 잠잠하고 흡족해, 이렇다 할 반대가 없었다."], ["Sir Thomas engaged for its giving her very little trouble; and she assured him “that she was not at all afraid of the trouble; indeed, she could not imagine there would be any.”", "토머스 경이 그 일로 그녀를 거의 성가시게 하지 않겠노라 다짐하니, 그녀는 그에게 “나야 그 수고쯤 조금도 두렵지 않아요. 정말이지 수고랄 게 있을 성싶지도 않은걸요”라고 장담했다."]], [["Mrs. Norris was ready with her suggestions as to the rooms he would think fittest to be used, but found it all prearranged; and when she would have conjectured and hinted about the day, it appeared that the day was settled too.", "노리스 부인은 그가 어느 방을 쓰는 게 가장 알맞다 여길지를 두고 훈수를 둘 채비를 하고 있었으나, 그 모두가 이미 짜여 있음을 알았다."], ["Sir Thomas had been amusing himself with shaping a very complete outline of the business; and as soon as she would listen quietly, could read his list of the families to be invited, from whom he calculated, with all necessary allowance for the shortness of the notice, to collect young people enough to form twelve or fourteen couple: and could detail the considerations which had induced him to fix on the 22nd as the most eligible day.", "그리고 그녀가 날짜를 두고 넘겨짚어 넌지시 일러 보려 하니, 날짜 또한 정해져 있는 것으로 드러났다. 토머스 경은 그 일의 아주 완전한 얼개를 잡아 보며 재미를 붙이던 참이었다."], ["William was required to be at Portsmouth on the 24th; the 22nd would therefore be the last day of his visit; but where the days were so few it would be unwise to fix on any earlier.", "그리하여 그녀가 잠자코 듣기만 하면, 그는 초대할 집안의 목록을 죽 읽어 줄 수 있었으니, 그것들로부터 그는 촉박한 통지에 대한 온갖 넉넉한 셈속까지 헤아려 열두 쌍이나 열네 쌍을 이룰 만큼의 젊은이를 그러모을 셈이었다. 또 22일을 가장 알맞은 날로 정하게 된 여러 사정을 낱낱이 일러 줄 수도 있었다."], ["Mrs.", "윌리엄은 24일에 포츠머스에 가 있어야 했다. 그러니 22일이 그가 머무는 마지막 날이 될 터였다."], ["Norris was obliged to be satisfied with thinking just the same, and with having been on the point of proposing the 22nd herself, as by far the best day for the purpose.", "하지만 이토록 날수가 얼마 안 되는 마당에 그보다 이른 날로 정하는 것은 어리석은 일이었으리라. 노리스 부인은 하는 수 없이, 저 역시 꼭 같은 생각이었다는 것과, 저야말로 22일이 이 일에 단연 가장 좋은 날이라며 막 내놓으려던 참이었다는 것으로 만족해야 했다."]], [["The ball was now a settled thing, and before the evening a proclaimed thing to all whom it concerned.", "무도회는 이제 정해진 일이 되었고, 저녁이 되기 전에 그와 관련된 모든 이에게 공표된 일이 되었다. 초대장이 부랴부랴 부쳐졌고, 그날 밤 패니 말고도 여러 아가씨가 행복한 걱정으로 머릿속이 가득한 채 잠자리에 들었다."], ["Invitations were sent with despatch, and many a young lady went to bed that night with her head full of happy cares as well as Fanny.", "패니에게는 그 걱정이 때로 행복을 거의 넘어서기까지 했다. 어리고 세상 모르는 데다 고를 밑천도 적고 제 취향에 자신도 없던 그녀로서는, “어떻게 차려입어야 하나”"], ["To her the cares were sometimes almost beyond the happiness; for young and inexperienced, with small means of choice and no confidence in her own taste, the “how she should be dressed”", "하는 것이 애타는 근심거리였던 것이다. 그리고 그녀가 지닌 거의 유일한 장신구, 곧 윌리엄이 시칠리아에서 가져다준 무척 예쁜 호박 십자가가 무엇보다 큰 시름이었다."], ["was a point of painful solicitude; and the almost solitary ornament in her possession, a very pretty amber cross which William had brought her from Sicily, was the greatest distress of all, for she had nothing but a bit of ribbon to fasten it to; and though she had worn it in that manner once, would it be allowable at such a time in the midst of all the rich ornaments which she supposed all the other young ladies would appear in?", "그것을 매달 것이라곤 리본 조각 하나밖에 없었던 까닭이다. 언젠가 그런 식으로 걸어 본 적이야 있었으나, 다른 아가씨들이 죄다 걸치고 나오리라 싶은 그 온갖 화려한 장신구 한복판에서, 이런 자리에 그리해도 될 노릇일까?"], ["And yet not to wear it!", "그렇다고 그것을 안 걸 수도 없고!"], ["William had wanted to buy her a gold chain too, but the purchase had been beyond his means, and therefore not to wear the cross might be mortifying him.", "윌리엄은 금줄도 하나 사 주고 싶어 했으나, 그것은 그의 형편을 넘어서는 일이었다. 그러니 그 십자가를 안 거는 것은 그의 마음을 아프게 하는 일일지도 몰랐다."], ["These were anxious considerations; enough to sober her spirits even under the prospect of a ball given principally for her gratification.", "이것들은 애태우는 근심거리였으니, 주로 저를 즐겁게 해 주려고 열리는 무도회를 앞두고서도 그녀의 마음을 가라앉히기에 넉넉했다."]], [["The preparations meanwhile went on, and Lady Bertram continued to sit on her sofa without any inconvenience from them.", "그러는 사이 채비는 착착 진행되었고, 버트럼 부인은 그 무엇에도 조금의 성가심 없이 여전히 소파에 앉아 있었다."], ["She had some extra visits from the housekeeper, and her maid was rather hurried in making up a new dress for her: Sir Thomas gave orders, and Mrs.", "가정부가 몇 번 더 다녀갔고, 그녀의 하녀는 새 옷을 짓느라 좀 바삐 서둘렀다. 토머스 경은 지시를 내리고 노리스 부인은 이리저리 뛰어다녔다."], ["Norris ran about; but all this gave her no trouble, and as she had foreseen, “there was, in fact, no trouble in the business.”", "그러나 이 모두가 그녀에게는 아무 수고도 아니었으니, 그녀가 미리 내다본 대로 “정말이지 이 일엔 수고랄 게 하나도 없었다.”"]], [["Edmund was at this time particularly full of cares: his mind being deeply occupied in the consideration of two important events now at hand, which were to fix his fate in life—ordination and matrimony—events of such a serious character as to make the ball, which would be very quickly followed by one of them, appear of less moment in his eyes than in those of any other person in the house.", "에드먼드는 이 무렵 유난히 근심으로 가득 차 있었다. 그의 마음은 이제 눈앞에 닥친, 삶에서 제 운명을 정하게 될 두 중대사—서품과 혼인—을 헤아리는 데 깊이 잠겨 있었다."], ["On the 23rd he was going to a friend near Peterborough, in the same situation as himself, and they were to receive ordination in the course of the Christmas week.", "그것은 자못 진중한 일이라, 그중 하나가 이내 뒤따를 그 무도회가 그의 눈에는 이 집 안 다른 누구의 눈에서보다 대수롭잖게 비쳤다. 23일에 그는 저와 같은 처지인, 피터버러 근방의 한 벗에게로 갈 참이었고, 둘은 크리스마스 주간 동안 서품을 받기로 되어 있었다."], ["Half his destiny would then be determined, but the other half might not be so very smoothly wooed.", "그리하면 그의 운명 절반은 정해질 터였다. 그러나 나머지 절반은 그리 술술 구애되지 않을지도 몰랐다."], ["His duties would be established, but the wife who was to share, and animate, and reward those duties, might yet be unattainable.", "그의 소임이야 자리 잡히겠으나, 그 소임을 함께 나누고 살려 내고 갚아 줄 아내는 아직 얻지 못할지도 몰랐다."], ["He knew his own mind, but he was not always perfectly assured of knowing Miss Crawford’s.", "그는 제 마음이야 알았으나, 크로퍼드 양의 마음을 안다고는 늘 온전히 확신하지는 못했다."], ["There were points on which they did not quite agree; there were moments in which she did not seem propitious; and though trusting altogether to her affection, so far as to be resolved—almost resolved—on bringing it to a decision within a very short time, as soon as the variety of business before him were arranged, and he knew what he had to offer her, he had many anxious feelings, many doubting hours as to the result.", "둘이 딱 맞지 않는 점들이 있었고, 그녀가 미덥잖아 보이는 순간들도 있었다. 그리고 그녀의 애정을 온통 믿어, 눈앞에 놓인 여러 일이 정리되어 그가 그녀에게 무엇을 내놓을 수 있는지를 알게 되는 대로 이내 그것을 결판 지으리라 마음먹기까지—거의 마음먹기까지—했으면서도, 그 결말을 두고 애타는 심경과 미심쩍은 시각이 숱했다."], ["His conviction of her regard for him was sometimes very strong; he could look back on a long course of encouragement, and she was as perfect in disinterested attachment as in everything else.", "그를 향한 그녀의 마음에 대한 확신이 때로는 아주 굳었다. 그는 오랜 세월 이어진 격려를 되짚어 볼 수 있었고, 그녀는 다른 무엇에서나 그러하듯 사심 없는 애착에서도 나무랄 데가 없었다."], ["But at other times doubt and alarm intermingled with his hopes; and when he thought of her acknowledged disinclination for privacy and retirement, her decided preference of a London life, what could he expect but a determined rejection?", "그러나 또 어느 때는 의심과 불안이 그의 바람에 뒤섞였다. 홀로 지냄과 은둔에 대한 그녀의 드러내 놓은 싫증을, 런던살이에 대한 그녀의 뚜렷한 선호를 떠올릴 제, 그가 단호한 거절 말고 무엇을 바랄 수 있었으랴?"], ["unless it were an acceptance even more to be deprecated, demanding such sacrifices of situation and employment on his side as conscience must forbid.", "아니면 그보다 더 못마땅해할 만한 수락, 곧 그의 편에서 양심이 금할 만한 그런 처지와 소임의 희생을 요구하는 수락 말고는."]], [["The issue of all depended on one question.", "이 모두의 결말이 오직 한 물음에 달려 있었다."], ["Did she love him well enough to forego what had used to be essential points?", "그녀는 예전엔 없어선 안 될 것이던 것들을 버릴 만큼 그를 깊이 사랑하는가?"], ["Did she love him well enough to make them no longer essential?", "그것들을 더는 없어선 안 될 것이 아니게 만들 만큼 그를 깊이 사랑하는가?"], ["And this question, which he was continually repeating to himself, though oftenest answered with a “Yes,” had sometimes its “No.”", "그리고 이 물음은, 그가 끊임없이 저 자신에게 되뇌던 이 물음은, 흔히 “그렇다”로 대답되면서도 때로 “아니다”가 나오기도 했다."]], [["Miss Crawford was soon to leave Mansfield, and on this circumstance the “no” and the “yes”", "크로퍼드 양은 머잖아 맨스필드를 떠날 참이었고, 이 사정을 두고 “아니다”와 “그렇다”가 바로 요즈음 번갈아 오르내렸다."], ["had been very recently in alternation. He had seen her eyes sparkle as she spoke of the dear friend’s letter, which claimed a long visit from her in London, and of the kindness of Henry, in engaging to remain where he was till January, that he might convey her thither; he had heard her speak of the pleasure of such a journey with an animation which had “no”", "그는 그녀가, 런던에서 오래 머물다 가라고 조르는 그 소중한 벗의 편지 얘기를 할 때, 그리고 저를 거기까지 데려다주려고 정월까지 이곳에 남기로 한 헨리의 친절 얘기를 할 때 두 눈이 반짝이는 것을 보았다."], ["in every tone. But this had occurred on the first day of its being settled, within the first hour of the burst of such enjoyment, when nothing but the friends she was to visit was before her.", "또 그런 여행의 즐거움을 두고 말투마다 “아니다”가 배어 있는 그런 들뜬 목소리로 그녀가 이야기하는 것을 들었다."], ["He had since heard her express herself differently, with other feelings, more chequered feelings: he had heard her tell Mrs.", "그러나 이것은 그 일이 정해진 첫날, 그런 즐거움이 왈칵 터진 첫 시각 동안, 찾아갈 벗들 말고는 눈앞에 아무것도 없을 때 일어난 일이었다."], ["Grant that she should leave her with regret; that she began to believe neither the friends nor the pleasures she was going to were worth those she left behind; and that though she felt she must go, and knew she should enjoy herself when once away, she was already looking forward to being at Mansfield again.", "그 뒤로 그는 그녀가 사뭇 다르게, 다른 심경으로, 더 얼룩덜룩한 심경으로 저를 드러내는 것을 들었다. 그녀가 그랜트 부인에게, 이곳을 떠나기가 서운하다고, 저가 찾아가는 벗도 즐거움도 이곳에 두고 가는 것만 못하지 싶기 시작한다고, 그리고 가야 하는 줄 알고 일단 떠나면 즐겁게 지내리라는 것도 알지만 벌써부터 다시 맨스필드로 돌아올 날을 손꼽고 있다고 말하는 것을 들었던 것이다."], ["Was there not a “yes” in all this?", "이 모든 데에 “그렇다”가 있지 않은가?"]], [["With such matters to ponder over, and arrange, and re-arrange, Edmund could not, on his own account, think very much of the evening which the rest of the family were looking forward to with a more equal degree of strong interest.", "이런 일들을 곰곰 헤아리고, 짜고, 다시 짜야 했던 터라, 에드먼드는 저 자신을 위해서는, 나머지 식구가 한결 고른 정도의 큰 관심으로 손꼽아 기다리는 그 저녁을 그리 대단히 여길 수가 없었다."], ["Independent of his two cousins’ enjoyment in it, the evening was to him of no higher value than any other appointed meeting of the two families might be.", "두 사촌이 거기서 누릴 즐거움을 빼면, 그 저녁은 그에게 두 집안이 정해 놓은 여느 만남보다 더 값진 것이 아니었다."], ["In every meeting there was a hope of receiving farther confirmation of Miss Crawford’s attachment; but the whirl of a ballroom, perhaps, was not particularly favourable to the excitement or expression of serious feelings.", "어느 만남에서든 크로퍼드 양의 애착을 한층 굳혀 줄 것을 받으리라는 바람이 있기는 했다. 그러나 무도회장의 그 소용돌이는, 아마도 진중한 심경을 북돋우거나 드러내기에 딱히 이롭지는 않았다."], ["To engage her early for the two first dances was all the command of individual happiness which he felt in his power, and the only preparation for the ball which he could enter into, in spite of all that was passing around him on the subject, from morning till night.", "처음 두 곡의 춤을 위해 그녀를 일찌감치 붙잡아 두는 것, 그것이 저 홀로 누릴 수 있는 행복이라 여겨지는 전부였고, 아침부터 밤까지 그 화제를 두고 온 둘레에서 벌어지는 그 온갖 일에도 불구하고 그가 마음 쓸 수 있는 그 무도회에 대한 유일한 채비였다."]], [["Thursday was the day of the ball; and on Wednesday morning Fanny, still unable to satisfy herself as to what she ought to wear, determined to seek the counsel of the more enlightened, and apply to Mrs.", "무도회 날은 목요일이었다. 그리고 수요일 아침, 무엇을 입어야 할지 여태 도무지 마음을 정하지 못한 패니는, 좀 더 밝은 눈을 가진 이의 조언을 구하기로, 그랜트 부인과 그 아우에게 여쭈어 보기로 마음먹었다."], ["Grant and her sister, whose acknowledged taste would certainly bear her blameless; and as Edmund and William were gone to Northampton, and she had reason to think Mr.", "그들의 널리 인정받는 취향이라면 저를 나무랄 데 없이 이끌어 주리라 여긴 것이다."], ["Crawford likewise out, she walked down to the Parsonage without much fear of wanting an opportunity for private discussion; and the privacy of such a discussion was a most important part of it to Fanny, being more than half-ashamed of her own solicitude.", "그리고 에드먼드와 윌리엄은 노샘프턴에 가 있고 크로퍼드 씨도 나가고 없다 여길 만한 까닭이 있던 터라, 그녀는 오붓하게 의논할 틈이 없을까 하는 걱정 없이 목사관으로 걸어 내려갔다. 그런 의논의 은밀함이야말로 패니에게는 무엇보다 중한 대목이었으니, 그녀는 제 안달을 절반 넘게 부끄러워하고 있었던 까닭이다."]], [["She met Miss Crawford within a few yards of the Parsonage, just setting out to call on her, and as it seemed to her that her friend, though obliged to insist on turning back, was unwilling to lose her walk, she explained her business at once, and observed, that if she would be so kind as to give her opinion, it might be all talked over as well without doors as within.", "그녀는 목사관에서 몇 발짝 안 되는 데서 크로퍼드 양과 마주쳤는데, 마침 저를 찾아 나서던 참이었다. 그리고 벗이 되돌아가자고 우기지 않을 수 없으면서도 산책을 놓치기는 싫어하는 듯 보이던지라, 그녀는 대뜸 제 볼일을 밝히고, 그토록 친절히 의견을 들려주실 참이라면 그 이야기야 문 안에서든 문 밖에서든 다 나눌 수 있으리라고 말했다."], ["Miss Crawford appeared gratified by the application, and after a moment’s thought, urged Fanny’s returning with her in a much more cordial manner than before, and proposed their going up into her room, where they might have a comfortable coze, without disturbing Dr. and Mrs.", "크로퍼드 양은 그 부탁이 흐뭇한 듯, 잠깐 생각한 뒤, 전보다 훨씬 다정한 투로 패니에게 저와 함께 돌아가자고 조르며, 응접실에 함께 있는 그랜트 박사 부부를 성가시게 하지 않고 오붓하게 정담을 나눌 수 있도록 제 방으로 올라가자고 청했다."], ["Grant, who were together in the drawing-room.", "그것은 패니에게 꼭 맞는 셈이었다."], ["It was just the plan to suit Fanny; and with a great deal of gratitude on her side for such ready and kind attention, they proceeded indoors, and upstairs, and were soon deep in the interesting subject.", "그리하여 그토록 선선하고 친절한 마음씀에 저로서는 크나큰 고마움을 느끼며, 그들은 집 안으로, 위층으로 올라가 이내 그 흥미로운 화제에 깊이 빠져들었다."], ["Miss Crawford, pleased with the appeal, gave her all her best judgment and taste, made everything easy by her suggestions, and tried to make everything agreeable by her encouragement.", "그 부탁이 반가웠던 크로퍼드 양은 제 온갖 좋은 판단과 취향을 다 쏟아, 이런저런 제안으로 만사를 손쉽게 해 주고, 격려로 만사를 흐뭇하게 해 주려 애썼다."], ["The dress being settled in all its grander parts—“But what shall you have by way of necklace?”", "옷차림의 굵직한 대목이 다 정해지자—“그런데 목걸이로는 뭘 하실 참이에요?”"], ["said Miss Crawford.", "크로퍼드 양이 말했다."], ["“Shall not you wear your brother’s cross?”", "“오라버니의 십자가를 안 거실 거예요?”"], ["And as she spoke she was undoing a small parcel, which Fanny had observed in her hand when they met.", "그러면서 그녀는 자그마한 꾸러미 하나를 풀고 있었는데, 그것은 마주쳤을 때 패니가 그녀 손에 든 것을 눈여겨보았던 것이었다."], ["Fanny acknowledged her wishes and doubts on this point: she did not know how either to wear the cross, or to refrain from wearing it.", "패니는 이 점에 대한 제 바람과 망설임을 털어놓았다. 그 십자가를 어찌 걸어야 할지도, 그렇다고 어찌 안 걸고 있어야 할지도 모르겠다고."], ["She was answered by having a small trinket-box placed before her, and being requested to chuse from among several gold chains and necklaces.", "그러자 자그마한 패물 상자 하나가 그녀 앞에 놓이고, 여러 금줄과 목걸이 가운데서 하나 고르라는 청이 대답으로 돌아왔다. 크로퍼드 양이 챙겨 온 꾸러미가 바로 그것이었고, 그녀가 찾아오려던 목적 또한 바로 그것이었다."], ["Such had been the parcel with which Miss Crawford was provided, and such the object of her intended visit: and in the kindest manner she now urged Fanny’s taking one for the cross and to keep for her sake, saying everything she could think of to obviate the scruples which were making Fanny start back at first with a look of horror at the proposal.", "그리하여 그녀는 이제 더없이 친절한 투로, 그 십자가에 쓰고 또 제 정표로 간직하도록 하나 가지라며 패니를 졸랐다. 처음에는 그 제안에 질겁한 낯빛으로 뒷걸음질 치게 만든 그 거리낌을 덜어 주려고, 떠오르는 온갖 말을 다 늘어놓으면서."]], [["“You see what a collection I have,”", "“내가 얼마나 많이 모아 뒀는지 보이죠.”"], ["said she; “more by half than I ever use or think of.", "그녀가 말했다. “쓰거나 눈길 주는 것보다 곱절은 많아요."], ["I do not offer them as new.", "새것으로 드리는 게 아니에요."], ["I offer nothing but an old necklace.", "낡은 목걸이 하나 드릴 뿐인걸요."], ["You must forgive the liberty, and oblige me.”", "이 주제넘음을 용서하고, 내 청을 들어줘요.”"]], [["Fanny still resisted, and from her heart.", "패니는 여전히, 진심으로 마다했다."], ["The gift was too valuable.", "그 선물은 너무 값진 것이었다."], ["But Miss Crawford persevered, and argued the case with so much affectionate earnestness through all the heads of William and the cross, and the ball, and herself, as to be finally successful.", "그러나 크로퍼드 양은 물러서지 않고, 윌리엄이며 십자가며 무도회며 저 자신에 이르기까지 온갖 갈래를 짚어 가며 어찌나 다정하고 간곡히 그 일을 파고들었던지, 끝내 뜻을 이루고야 말았다."], ["Fanny found herself obliged to yield, that she might not be accused of pride or indifference, or some other littleness; and having with modest reluctance given her consent, proceeded to make the selection.", "패니는 하는 수 없이 굽히고 말았으니, 자칫 오만이나 무심함이나 그 밖의 어떤 좀스러움으로 몰릴까 저어한 것이다. 그리고 얌전히 못 이기는 척 승낙한 뒤, 고르는 일에 들어갔다."], ["She looked and looked, longing to know which might be least valuable; and was determined in her choice at last, by fancying there was one necklace more frequently placed before her eyes than the rest.", "그녀는 어느 것이 가장 값이 덜 나갈까 알고 싶어 보고 또 보았다. 그러다 마침내, 다른 것들보다 제 눈앞에 더 자주 놓이는 목걸이가 하나 있는 성싶어 그것으로 마음을 정했다."], ["It was of gold, prettily worked; and though Fanny would have preferred a longer and a plainer chain as more adapted for her purpose, she hoped, in fixing on this, to be chusing what Miss Crawford least wished to keep.", "그것은 금으로 된, 곱게 세공된 것이었다. 그리고 패니야 제 쓰임에 더 알맞도록 좀 더 길고 수수한 줄을 골랐으련만, 이것으로 정하면 크로퍼드 양이 가장 간직하고 싶어 하지 않을 것을 고르는 셈이 되기를 바랐다."], ["Miss Crawford smiled her perfect approbation; and hastened to complete the gift by putting the necklace round her, and making her see how well it looked.", "크로퍼드 양은 흡족한 웃음으로 온전히 찬동하고는, 서둘러 그 목걸이를 그녀에게 둘러 주고 그것이 얼마나 잘 어울리는지 보여 주며 그 선물을 마무리 지었다."], ["Fanny had not a word to say against its becomingness, and, excepting what remained of her scruples, was exceedingly pleased with an acquisition so very apropos.", "패니는 그 어울림에 대해서는 한마디도 흠잡을 말이 없었고, 남은 거리낌만 빼면, 이토록 때맞은 얻음에 더없이 흐뭇했다."], ["She would rather, perhaps, have been obliged to some other person.", "그녀는 어쩌면 다른 누군가에게 신세를 졌더라면 싶기도 했다."], ["But this was an unworthy feeling.", "그러나 이것은 못난 심정이었다."], ["Miss Crawford had anticipated her wants with a kindness which proved her a real friend.", "크로퍼드 양은 저를 참된 벗임을 증명하는 그런 친절로 제 아쉬움을 미리 헤아려 준 것이었다."], ["“When I wear this necklace I shall always think of you,”", "“이 목걸이를 걸 때면 나는 언제나 크로퍼드 양을 생각하고, 그때 얼마나 친절하셨는지 떠올릴 거예요.”"], ["said she, “and feel how very kind you were.”", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“You must think of somebody else too, when you wear that necklace,”", "“그 목걸이를 걸 때면 다른 사람도 생각해야 해요.”"], ["replied Miss Crawford.", "크로퍼드 양이 대꾸했다."], ["“You must think of Henry, for it was his choice in the first place.", "“헨리를 생각해야 하죠, 애초에 그이가 고른 거니까요."], ["He gave it to me, and with the necklace I make over to you all the duty of remembering the original giver.", "그이가 나한테 준 거예요. 그러니 이 목걸이와 더불어, 맨 처음 준 사람을 기억할 온갖 몫까지 죄다 그쪽한테 넘길게요."], ["It is to be a family remembrancer.", "이건 집안 정표가 되는 셈이에요."], ["The sister is not to be in your mind without bringing the brother too.”", "오라비를 함께 떠올리지 않고서는 누이도 마음에 두어선 안 되는 거죠.”"]], [["Fanny, in great astonishment and confusion, would have returned the present instantly.", "패니는 몹시 놀라고 얼떨떨하여 그 선물을 당장 돌려주려 했다."], ["To take what had been the gift of another person, of a brother too, impossible!", "남의 것이던 것을, 게다가 오라비의 것이던 것을 받다니, 어림없는 일이었다!"], ["it must not be!", "그래선 안 되었다!"], ["and with an eagerness and embarrassment quite diverting to her companion, she laid down the necklace again on its cotton, and seemed resolved either to take another or none at all.", "그리하여 그녀는, 벗의 눈에는 자못 우스꽝스러운 그 간절함과 당황함으로, 목걸이를 다시 솜 위에 내려놓고, 다른 것을 고르든 아니면 아무것도 안 받든 하겠다는 듯 굳게 마음먹은 듯 보였다."], ["Miss Crawford thought she had never seen a prettier consciousness.", "크로퍼드 양은 이보다 더 어여쁜 켕김은 본 적이 없다 싶었다."], ["“My dear child,”", "“얘도 참,”"], ["said she, laughing, “what are you afraid of?", "그녀가 웃으며 말했다. “뭐가 그리 무서워요?"], ["Do you think Henry will claim the necklace as mine, and fancy you did not come honestly by it?", "헨리가 이 목걸이를 제 것이라 우기며, 그쪽이 떳떳잖게 손에 넣었다고 여길까 봐 그래요?"], ["or are you imagining he would be too much flattered by seeing round your lovely throat an ornament which his money purchased three years ago, before he knew there was such a throat in the world?", "아니면 그이가, 삼 년 전 이 세상에 이런 목이 있는 줄도 모르던 시절에 제 돈으로 산 장신구가 그쪽 고운 목에 둘려 있는 걸 보고 너무 우쭐할까 봐 그러는 거예요?"], ["or perhaps”—looking archly—“you suspect a confederacy between us, and that what I am now doing is with his knowledge and at his desire?”", "아니면 어쩌면”—짓궂게 곁눈질하며—“우리 둘이 짬짜미했다고, 지금 내가 하는 일이 그이도 알고 그이가 바라서 하는 일이라고 의심하는 거예요?”"]], [["With the deepest blushes Fanny protested against such a thought.", "패니는 더없이 새빨개진 얼굴로 그런 생각을 극구 부인했다."]], [["“Well, then,”", "“그럼, 됐어요.”"], ["replied Miss Crawford more seriously, but without at all believing her, “to convince me that you suspect no trick, and are as unsuspicious of compliment as I have always found you, take the necklace and say no more about it.", "크로퍼드 양이 좀 더 진지하게, 그러면서도 그녀의 말을 조금도 믿지는 않으며 대꾸했다. “그쪽이 무슨 수작을 의심하는 게 아니라는 걸, 그리고 내가 늘 봐 온 대로 아첨 따위엔 의심할 줄 모른다는 걸 나한테 증명하려거든, 그냥 이 목걸이를 받고 더는 그 얘기 말아요."], ["Its being a gift of my brother’s need not make the smallest difference in your accepting it, as I assure you it makes none in my willingness to part with it.", "이게 내 오라비의 선물이었다 해서 받는 데 조금도 거리낄 것 없어요, 이걸 내놓는 내 마음에도 그게 아무 상관 없다고 장담하니까요."], ["He is always giving me something or other.", "그이는 늘 나한테 이것저것 주거든요."], ["I have such innumerable presents from him that it is quite impossible for me to value or for him to remember half.", "그이한테서 받은 선물이 하도 많아서 나는 그 절반도 값을 매길 수도, 그이는 그 절반도 기억할 수도 없을 지경이에요. 그리고 이 목걸이로 말하면, 나야 여섯 번도 안 걸어 봤지 싶어요."], ["And as for this necklace, I do not suppose I have worn it six times: it is very pretty, but I never think of it; and though you would be most heartily welcome to any other in my trinket-box, you have happened to fix on the very one which, if I have a choice, I would rather part with and see in your possession than any other.", "무척 예쁘긴 해도 나는 통 눈길도 안 준다니까요. 내 패물 상자에 든 다른 어떤 것이든 진심으로 반겨 드렸겠지만, 그쪽은 마침, 내게 고를 여지가 있다면 다른 무엇보다도 기꺼이 내놓고 그쪽 손에 있는 걸 보고 싶은 바로 그것을 고른 거예요."], ["Say no more against it, I entreat you.", "더는 마다하지 말아요, 정말 부탁이에요."], ["Such a trifle is not worth half so many words.”", "이런 하찮은 것에 이만큼 많은 말은 그 절반도 아깝잖아요.”"]], [["Fanny dared not make any farther opposition; and with renewed but less happy thanks accepted the necklace again, for there was an expression in Miss Crawford’s eyes which she could not be satisfied with.", "패니는 감히 더는 맞설 수 없었다. 그리하여 새로운, 그러나 덜 기쁜 고마움과 함께 그 목걸이를 다시 받았으니, 크로퍼드 양의 눈에 도무지 흡족해할 수 없는 어떤 빛이 어려 있었던 까닭이다."]], [["It was impossible for her to be insensible of Mr.", "크로퍼드 씨의 달라진 태도를 그녀가 못 느낄 리 없었다. 그녀는 진작부터 그것을 보아 왔다."], ["Crawford’s change of manners.", "그는 분명 그녀의 마음에 들려 애썼다."], ["She had long seen it.", "그는 정중했고, 자상했고, 예전에 제 사촌들을 대하던 것과 얼추 비슷했다."], ["He evidently tried to please her: he was gallant, he was attentive, he was something like what he had been to her cousins: he wanted, she supposed, to cheat her of her tranquillity as he had cheated them; and whether he might not have some concern in this necklace—she could not be convinced that he had not, for Miss Crawford, complaisant as a sister, was careless as a woman and a friend.", "그는, 짐작건대, 사촌들에게서 그리했던 것처럼 그녀에게서도 그 평온을 앗아 가려는 것이었다. 그리고 그가 이 목걸이에 얼마간 얽혀 있지나 않을까—그녀는 그렇지 않다고 믿을 수가 없었으니, 크로퍼드 양은 누이로서는 살가워도 여자로서, 벗으로서는 무심했던 까닭이다."]], [["Reflecting and doubting, and feeling that the possession of what she had so much wished for did not bring much satisfaction, she now walked home again, with a change rather than a diminution of cares since her treading that path before.", "곰곰 헤아리고 미심쩍어하며, 그토록 바라 마지않던 것을 손에 넣고도 그리 큰 흡족함이 따르지 않음을 느끼면서, 그녀는 이제 집으로 다시 걸어갔다. 아까 이 길을 밟던 때에 견주어 근심이 줄었다기보다 그저 결이 바뀐 채로."]], [["# CHAPTER XXVII", "# 제27장"]], [["On reaching home Fanny went immediately upstairs to deposit this unexpected acquisition, this doubtful good of a necklace, in some favourite box in the East room, which held all her smaller treasures; but on opening the door, what was her surprise to find her cousin Edmund there writing at the table!", "집에 이르자 패니는 이 뜻밖의 얻음을, 이 미덥잖은 목걸이라는 좋은 것을 제 자잘한 보물을 죄다 간직해 둔 동쪽 방 어느 아끼는 상자에 넣어 두려고 곧장 위층으로 올라갔다. 그러나 문을 열고 보니, 사촌 에드먼드가 거기 탁자에 앉아 무언가를 쓰고 있는 것이 아닌가!"], ["Such a sight having never occurred before, was almost as wonderful as it was welcome.", "여태 한 번도 없던 그런 광경이라, 그것은 반가운 만큼이나 놀라운 것이었다."]], [["“Fanny,”", "“패니,”"], ["said he directly, leaving his seat and his pen, and meeting her with something in his hand, “I beg your pardon for being here.", "그가 자리와 펜을 떠나 손에 무언가를 든 채 그녀를 맞으며 이내 말했다. “여기 있어서 미안하구나."], ["I came to look for you, and after waiting a little while in hope of your coming in, was making use of your inkstand to explain my errand.", "널 찾으러 왔다가, 네가 들어오려니 하고 얼마쯤 기다리던 참에, 네 잉크병을 빌려 내 볼일을 적어 두던 중이었어. 너한테 쓰다 만 쪽지를 보게 될 게다."], ["You will find the beginning of a note to yourself; but I can now speak my business, which is merely to beg your acceptance of this little trifle—a chain for William’s cross.", "하지만 이제 내 볼일을 직접 말할 수 있겠구나. 다름이 아니라 이 자그마한 것을 받아 달라 청하려던 거야—윌리엄의 십자가에 쓸 줄이란다."], ["You ought to have had it a week ago, but there has been a delay from my brother’s not being in town by several days so soon as I expected; and I have only just now received it at Northampton.", "일주일 전에 벌써 네 손에 있어야 했는데, 내 아우가 내 짐작보다 며칠이나 늦게 시내에 들어오는 바람에 늦어졌지 뭐냐. 나도 방금 노샘프턴에서야 받았어."], ["I hope you will like the chain itself, Fanny.", "이 줄 자체도 네 마음에 들면 좋겠구나, 패니."], ["I endeavoured to consult the simplicity of your taste; but, at any rate, I know you will be kind to my intentions, and consider it, as it really is, a token of the love of one of your oldest friends.”", "나야 네 수수한 취향에 맞추려 애써 봤다만, 아무튼 너는 내 뜻을 곱게 봐 주고, 이것을 그 참모습 그대로, 곧 네 가장 오랜 벗 가운데 하나가 보내는 사랑의 정표로 여겨 줄 줄 안다.”"]], [["And so saying, he was hurrying away, before Fanny, overpowered by a thousand feelings of pain and pleasure, could attempt to speak; but quickened by one sovereign wish, she then called out, “Oh! cousin, stop a moment, pray stop!”", "그러고서 그는 서둘러 가려 했다. 온갖 아픔과 기쁨의 감정에 짓눌린 패니가 미처 말을 꺼내 보기도 전에. 그러나 한 가지 절실한 바람에 다급해져, 그녀는 이내 소리쳤다. “아! 오빠, 잠깐만요, 제발 멈춰 봐요!”"]], [["He turned back.", "그가 되돌아섰다."]], [["“I cannot attempt to thank you,”", "“고맙다는 말도 못 하겠어요.”"], ["she continued, in a very agitated manner; “thanks are out of the question.", "그녀가 몹시 떨리는 투로 말을 이었다. “고맙다는 말로는 어림도 없어요."], ["I feel much more than I can possibly express.", "저는 도저히 말로 옮길 수 없는 것보다 훨씬 더 많이 느끼고 있어요."], ["Your goodness in thinking of me in such a way is beyond—”", "저를 이런 식으로 생각해 주신 그 고운 마음은 이루—”"]], [["“If that is all you have to say, Fanny”", "“그게 할 말 다라면, 패니”"], ["smiling and turning away again.", "하고 미소 지으며 다시 돌아서려 했다."]], [["“No, no, it is not.", "“아뇨, 아뇨, 아니에요."], ["I want to consult you.”", "의논드리고 싶어서요.”"]], [["Almost unconsciously she had now undone the parcel he had just put into her hand, and seeing before her, in all the niceness of jewellers’ packing, a plain gold chain, perfectly simple and neat, she could not help bursting forth again, “Oh, this is beautiful indeed!", "거의 저도 모르게 그녀는 어느새 그가 방금 손에 쥐여 준 꾸러미를 풀어, 보석상의 그 정갈한 포장 그대로, 눈앞에 더없이 수수하고 말끔한 민 금줄 하나가 놓인 것을 보고는, 다시금 왈칵 터뜨리지 않을 수 없었다. “어머, 정말 곱기도 해라!"], ["This is the very thing, precisely what I wished for!", "이게 바로 그거예요, 딱 제가 바라던 그것이에요!"], ["This is the only ornament I have ever had a desire to possess.", "이건 제가 가지고 싶다고 여겨 본 유일한 장신구예요."], ["It will exactly suit my cross.", "제 십자가에 꼭 어울리겠어요."], ["They must and shall be worn together.", "둘은 반드시, 꼭 함께 걸어야 해요."], ["It comes, too, in such an acceptable moment.", "게다가 이토록 반가운 때에 오다니."], ["Oh, cousin, you do not know how acceptable it is.”", "아, 오빠, 이게 얼마나 반가운지 오빠는 모르실 거예요.”"]], [["“My dear Fanny, you feel these things a great deal too much.", "“패니야, 넌 이런 것들을 너무 크게 느끼는구나."], ["I am most happy that you like the chain, and that it should be here in time for to-morrow; but your thanks are far beyond the occasion.", "이 줄이 네 마음에 들고, 내일에 맞춰 여기 닿아서 나야 더없이 기쁘다만, 네 고마움은 이런 일에 견주면 한참 과하다."], ["Believe me, I have no pleasure in the world superior to that of contributing to yours.", "정말이지 나는 네 기쁨에 보탬이 되는 것보다 더한 즐거움이 이 세상에 없단다."], ["No, I can safely say, I have no pleasure so complete, so unalloyed.", "아니, 나는 떳떳이 말할 수 있어, 이보다 더 온전하고 티 없는 즐거움은 내게 없다고."], ["It is without a drawback.”", "이건 아무 흠도 없는 즐거움이거든.”"]], [["Upon such expressions of affection Fanny could have lived an hour without saying another word; but Edmund, after waiting a moment, obliged her to bring down her mind from its heavenly flight by saying, “But what is it that you want to consult me about?”", "이런 애정 어린 말에 패니는 한마디도 더 안 하고 한 시간이라도 살 수 있었으련만, 에드먼드가 잠깐 기다리다가, “그런데 나한테 의논하고 싶다는 게 뭐냐?” 하고 물어, 하는 수 없이 그녀의 마음을 그 하늘 높이 나는 데서 끌어내려야 했다."]], [["It was about the necklace, which she was now most earnestly longing to return, and hoped to obtain his approbation of her doing.", "그것은 그 목걸이에 관한 것이었으니, 그녀는 이제 그것을 더없이 간절히 돌려주고 싶어 그리하는 데 그의 찬동을 얻기를 바랐다. 그녀는 조금 전 방문의 자초지종을 들려주었고, 이제 그녀의 황홀함은 다할 만도 했다."], ["She gave the history of her recent visit, and now her raptures might well be over; for Edmund was so struck with the circumstance, so delighted with what Miss Crawford had done, so gratified by such a coincidence of conduct between them, that Fanny could not but admit the superior power of one pleasure over his own mind, though it might have its drawback.", "에드먼드가 그 사정에 어찌나 감복하고, 크로퍼드 양이 한 일에 어찌나 기뻐하고, 둘의 처사가 그토록 딱 맞아떨어진 데 어찌나 흐뭇해했던지, 패니로서는 그의 마음속에서 어느 한 즐거움이—비록 흠이 있을지언정—제 것보다 더 힘이 세다는 것을 인정하지 않을 수 없었던 것이다. 그가 그녀의 생각에 귀 기울여 주고, 의견을 청한 데 대해 무슨 대답이라도 해 주기까지는 한참이 걸렸다."], ["It was some time before she could get his attention to her plan, or any answer to her demand of his opinion: he was in a reverie of fond reflection, uttering only now and then a few half-sentences of praise; but when he did awake and understand, he was very decided in opposing what she wished.", "그는 다정한 상념의 몽롱함에 잠겨, 이따금 반 토막짜리 칭찬의 말만 몇 마디 뇌고 있었던 것이다. 그러나 마침내 정신을 차려 알아듣고 나자, 그는 그녀가 바라는 바에 아주 단호히 반대했다."]], [["“Return the necklace!", "“목걸이를 돌려줘!"], ["No, my dear Fanny, upon no account.", "안 돼, 패니야, 절대 안 될 말이다."], ["It would be mortifying her severely.", "그건 그분에게 몹시 무안을 주는 일이야."], ["There can hardly be a more unpleasant sensation than the having anything returned on our hands which we have given with a reasonable hope of its contributing to the comfort of a friend.", "벗의 안락에 보탬이 되기를 바라는 응당한 마음으로 준 것을 되돌려받는 것보다 더 언짢은 심경도 좀처럼 없거든."], ["Why should she lose a pleasure which she has shewn herself so deserving of?”", "그분이 그럴 만한 사람임을 스스로 그토록 잘 보여 준 마당에, 어째서 그 즐거움을 잃어야 하겠니?”"]], [["“If it had been given to me in the first instance,”", "“애초에 저한테 준 것이었다면,” 패니가 말했다."], ["said Fanny, “I should not have thought of returning it; but being her brother’s present, is not it fair to suppose that she would rather not part with it, when it is not wanted?”", "“저도 돌려줄 생각은 안 했을 거예요. 하지만 그건 오라비의 선물이니, 그게 쓸데없어진 마당이면 오히려 크로퍼드 양이 그걸 내놓고 싶지 않으리라 여기는 게 온당하지 않을까요?”"]], [["“She must not suppose it not wanted, not acceptable, at least: and its having been originally her brother’s gift makes no difference; for as she was not prevented from offering, nor you from taking it on that account, it ought not to prevent you from keeping it.", "“그분은 그걸 쓸데없다고, 적어도 안 받는다고 여겨선 안 돼. 그리고 그게 원래 오라비의 선물이었다는 건 아무 상관 없어."], ["No doubt it is handsomer than mine, and fitter for a ballroom.”", "그 때문에 그분이 내놓는 걸 삼가지도, 네가 받는 걸 삼가지도 않은 마당이면, 그 때문에 네가 간직하는 걸 삼가서도 안 되지. 그야 물론 내 것보다 더 곱고, 무도회장엔 더 어울릴 테고.”"]], [["“No, it is not handsomer, not at all handsomer in its way, and, for my purpose, not half so fit.", "“아뇨, 더 곱지 않아요, 그 나름으론 조금도 더 곱지 않고, 제 쓰임엔 그 절반도 안 어울려요."], ["The chain will agree with William’s cross beyond all comparison better than the necklace.”", "이 줄이야말로 윌리엄의 십자가엔 그 목걸이보다 비할 데 없이 잘 어울리는걸요.”"]], [["“For one night, Fanny, for only one night, if it be a sacrifice; I am sure you will, upon consideration, make that sacrifice rather than give pain to one who has been so studious of your comfort.", "“하룻밤이잖니, 패니, 단 하룻밤이야. 그게 정말 희생이라 해도 말이다."], ["Miss Crawford’s attentions to you have been—not more than you were justly entitled to—I am the last person to think that could be, but they have been invariable; and to be returning them with what must have something the air of ingratitude, though I know it could never have the meaning, is not in your nature, I am sure.", "나는 네가 곰곰 생각해 보면, 그토록 정성껏 네 안락을 헤아려 준 이에게 아픔을 주느니 차라리 그 희생을 치를 줄 안다. 크로퍼드 양이 너한테 베푼 마음씀은—네가 마땅히 받을 것 이상은 아니야, 그게 그럴 수 있으리라곤 나야말로 결코 생각지 않을 사람이지만—아무튼 그건 한결같았어."], ["Wear the necklace, as you are engaged to do, to-morrow evening, and let the chain, which was not ordered with any reference to the ball, be kept for commoner occasions.", "그런데 그걸, 결코 그런 뜻일 리는 없다는 걸 나도 알지만, 아무래도 배은망덕한 낌새를 얼마간 띨 수밖에 없는 것으로 갚는다는 건, 네 성품에 맞지 않는 일이야, 나는 그리 믿는다. 약속한 대로 그 목걸이를 내일 저녁에 걸고, 무도회를 염두에 두고 맞춘 게 아닌 이 줄은 좀 더 예사로운 자리를 위해 간직해 두려무나."], ["This is my advice.", "이게 내 조언이야."], ["I would not have the shadow of a coolness between the two whose intimacy I have been observing with the greatest pleasure, and in whose characters there is so much general resemblance in true generosity and natural delicacy as to make the few slight differences, resulting principally from situation, no reasonable hindrance to a perfect friendship.", "나는, 그 친분을 더없이 큰 즐거움으로 지켜보아 온 두 사람 사이에, 냉랭함의 그림자 하나 어리는 것도 원치 않아. 그 두 사람의 성품에는 참된 너그러움과 타고난 섬세함에서 두루 닮은 데가 하도 많아, 주로 처지에서 비롯한 그 몇 안 되는 사소한 다름쯤은 온전한 벗됨을 가로막을 이렇다 할 걸림돌이 못 되거든."], ["I would not have the shadow of a coolness arise,”", "나는 냉랭함의 그림자 하나 이는 것도 원치 않는다.”"], ["he repeated, his voice sinking a little, “between the two dearest objects I have on earth.”", "그가 목소리를 조금 낮추며 되뇌었다. “이 세상에서 내게 가장 소중한 두 사람 사이에.”"]], [["He was gone as he spoke; and Fanny remained to tranquillise herself as she could.", "그는 말하면서 이미 가 버렸다. 그리고 패니는 남아서 되도록 제 마음을 가라앉히려 했다."], ["She was one of his two dearest—that must support her.", "그녀는 그의 가장 소중한 두 사람 가운데 하나였다—그것이 그녀를 지탱해 줄 터였다."], ["But the other: the first!", "그러나 다른 하나, 그 첫째가는 하나라니!"], ["She had never heard him speak so openly before, and though it told her no more than what she had long perceived, it was a stab, for it told of his own convictions and views.", "그가 이토록 터놓고 말하는 것을 그녀는 여태 들어 본 적이 없었다. 그리고 그것이 그녀가 진작부터 알아채 온 것 말고 더 새로운 것을 일러 준 것은 아니었으나, 그것은 하나의 비수였다."], ["They were decided.", "그의 굳은 확신과 뜻을 일러 준 까닭이다."], ["He would marry Miss Crawford.", "그것들은 정해져 있었다. 그는 크로퍼드 양과 혼인할 것이었다."], ["It was a stab, in spite of every long-standing expectation; and she was obliged to repeat again and again, that she was one of his two dearest, before the words gave her any sensation.", "그것은, 오래 품어 온 온갖 짐작에도 불구하고, 하나의 비수였다. 그리고 그녀는 그 말이 무슨 느낌을 주기까지, 저가 그의 가장 소중한 두 사람 가운데 하나라는 것을 몇 번이고 되뇌어야 했다."], ["Could she believe Miss Crawford to deserve him, it would be—oh, how different would it be—how far more tolerable!", "크로퍼드 양이 그에게 어울린다고 믿을 수만 있었다면, 그것은—아, 얼마나 달랐으랴—얼마나 훨씬 견딜 만했으랴!"], ["But he was deceived in her: he gave her merits which she had not; her faults were what they had ever been, but he saw them no longer.", "그러나 그는 그녀를 잘못 보고 있었다. 그는 그녀에게 있지도 않은 미덕을 부여했고, 그녀의 허물은 여전히 예전 그대로건만 그는 이제 그것을 보지 못했다."], ["Till she had shed many tears over this deception, Fanny could not subdue her agitation; and the dejection which followed could only be relieved by the influence of fervent prayers for his happiness.", "이 그릇된 봄을 두고 숱한 눈물을 흘리고서야 패니는 겨우 그 동요를 다스릴 수 있었다. 그리고 뒤따른 그 낙담은, 오직 그의 행복을 위한 뜨거운 기도의 힘으로만 덜어질 수 있었다."]], [["It was her intention, as she felt it to be her duty, to try to overcome all that was excessive, all that bordered on selfishness, in her affection for Edmund.", "에드먼드를 향한 제 애정에서 지나친 것, 이기심에 가까운 것을 죄다 이겨 내려 애쓰는 것, 그것이 제 도리라 여긴 그녀의 뜻이었다."], ["To call or to fancy it a loss, a disappointment, would be a presumption for which she had not words strong enough to satisfy her own humility.", "그것을 잃음이니 실망이니 부르거나 여기는 것은, 제 겸손함으로는 도저히 걸맞은 말을 찾을 수 없을 만큼 주제넘은 짓이었다."], ["To think of him as Miss Crawford might be justified in thinking, would in her be insanity.", "크로퍼드 양이라면 그럴 만도 한 식으로 그를 생각하는 것은, 그녀에게는 실성한 짓일 터였다."], ["To her he could be nothing under any circumstances; nothing dearer than a friend.", "그녀에게 그는 그 어떤 처지에서도 아무것도 될 수 없었다. 벗보다 더 소중한 그 무엇도 될 수 없었다."], ["Why did such an idea occur to her even enough to be reprobated and forbidden?", "어째서 그런 생각이, 나무라고 금해야 할 만큼이나마 떠오르기라도 했단 말인가?"], ["It ought not to have touched on the confines of her imagination.", "그것은 제 상상의 언저리조차 스쳐서는 안 될 것이었다."], ["She would endeavour to be rational, and to deserve the right of judging of Miss Crawford’s character, and the privilege of true solicitude for him by a sound intellect and an honest heart.", "그녀는 분별 있게 처신하려 애쓰고, 건전한 지성과 정직한 마음으로 크로퍼드 양의 성품을 가늠할 자격과 그를 향한 참된 근심의 특권을 얻으려 애쓸 것이었다."]], [["She had all the heroism of principle, and was determined to do her duty; but having also many of the feelings of youth and nature, let her not be much wondered at, if, after making all these good resolutions on the side of self-government, she seized the scrap of paper on which Edmund had begun writing to her, as a treasure beyond all her hopes, and reading with the tenderest emotion these words, “My very dear Fanny, you must do me the favour to accept”", "그녀에게는 지조의 온갖 굳셈이 있었고, 제 도리를 다하기로 굳게 마음먹었다. 그러나 젊음과 천성의 감정 또한 많이 지녔던지라, 자기 다스림의 편에서 이 온갖 갸륵한 결심을 다 한 뒤에, 에드먼드가 저에게 쓰기 시작한 그 쪽지 한 조각을 제 온갖 바람을 넘어선 보물인 양 움켜쥐고, “나의 더없이 사랑하는 패니, 부디 이것을 받아 주는 은혜를”이라는 이 몇 마디를 더없이 애틋한 감격으로 읽고는, 그것을 그 선물의 가장 소중한 부분으로 여겨 그 줄과 함께 넣어 잠가 두었다 한들, 그녀를 그리 크게 나무랄 것은 없으리라."], ["locked it up with the chain, as the dearest part of the gift.", "그것은 그가 그녀에게 보낸, 편지랄 만한 것에 가장 가까운 유일한 것이었다."], ["It was the only thing approaching to a letter which she had ever received from him; she might never receive another; it was impossible that she ever should receive another so perfectly gratifying in the occasion and the style.", "그녀는 다시는 못 받을지도 몰랐다. 이토록 그 계기와 문체에서 온전히 흐뭇한 것은 다시는 결코 못 받을 것이었다."], ["Two lines more prized had never fallen from the pen of the most distinguished author—never more completely blessed the researches of the fondest biographer.", "이보다 더 값진 두 줄은, 그 어떤 이름난 작가의 펜에서도 떨어진 적이 없었고—그 어떤 애틋한 전기 작가의 뒤짐에도 이보다 더 온전한 복이 내린 적이 없었다."], ["The enthusiasm of a woman’s love is even beyond the biographer’s.", "여인의 사랑이 지닌 그 열정은 전기 작가의 것마저 넘어선다."], ["To her, the handwriting itself, independent of anything it may convey, is a blessedness.", "그녀에게는, 그것이 무엇을 전하든 그와는 상관없이, 그 글씨체 자체가 하나의 축복이었다."], ["Never were such characters cut by any other human being as Edmund’s commonest handwriting gave!", "에드먼드의 그 예사로운 글씨체가 그어 낸 것 같은 그런 글자를 그린 사람은 여태 아무도 없었다! 아무리 서둘러 쓴 것이라 해도, 이 본보기에는 흠 하나 없었다."], ["This specimen, written in haste as it was, had not a fault; and there was a felicity in the flow of the first four words, in the arrangement of “My very dear Fanny,” which she could have looked at for ever.", "그리고 첫 네 마디의 그 흐름에, “나의 더없이 사랑하는 패니”라는 그 짜임에는, 그녀가 영영 바라보고 있어도 좋을 만한 어떤 어울림이 깃들어 있었다."]], [["Having regulated her thoughts and comforted her feelings by this happy mixture of reason and weakness, she was able in due time to go down and resume her usual employments near her aunt Bertram, and pay her the usual observances without any apparent want of spirits.", "이렇게 이성과 여림의 그 행복한 뒤섞임으로 제 생각을 다스리고 제 감정을 다독인 뒤, 그녀는 이윽고 아래층으로 내려가, 버트럼 이모 곁에서 여느 일거리를 다시 하고, 이렇다 하게 기운 없는 티도 안 내며 여느 시중을 그녀에게 들 수 있었다."]], [["Thursday, predestined to hope and enjoyment, came; and opened with more kindness to Fanny than such self-willed, unmanageable days often volunteer, for soon after breakfast a very friendly note was brought from Mr.", "바람과 즐거움을 위해 예정된 그 목요일이 왔다. 그날은, 이런 제멋대로에 걷잡을 수 없는 날들이 곧잘 그러는 것보다 더 살갑게 패니에게 열렸다."], ["Crawford to William, stating that as he found himself obliged to go to London on the morrow for a few days, he could not help trying to procure a companion; and therefore hoped that if William could make up his mind to leave Mansfield half a day earlier than had been proposed, he would accept a place in his carriage.", "아침 식사가 끝나고 얼마 안 되어 크로퍼드 씨가 윌리엄에게 보낸 무척 다정한 쪽지 하나가 전해진 것이다. 그 내용인즉, 자기가 며칠간 볼일로 이튿날 런던에 가지 않을 수 없게 되었으니 아무래도 길동무를 하나 구하려 애써 보지 않을 수 없다고, 그러니 윌리엄이 마음먹고 애초에 정한 것보다 반나절 일찍 맨스필드를 떠날 수만 있다면 제 마차에 한자리 받아 주기를 바란다는 것이었다."], ["Mr. Crawford meant to be in town by his uncle’s accustomary late dinner-hour, and William was invited to dine with him at the Admiral’s.", "크로퍼드 씨는 제 삼촌이 늘 드는 늦은 만찬 시각에 맞춰 시내에 닿을 참이었고, 윌리엄은 제독 댁에서 그와 함께 만찬을 들자고 초대받았다."], ["The proposal was a very pleasant one to William himself, who enjoyed the idea of travelling post with four horses, and such a good-humoured, agreeable friend; and, in likening it to going up with despatches, was saying at once everything in favour of its happiness and dignity which his imagination could suggest; and Fanny, from a different motive, was exceedingly pleased; for the original plan was that William should go up by the mail from Northampton the following night, which would not have allowed him an hour’s rest before he must have got into a Portsmouth coach; and though this offer of Mr.", "그 제안은 윌리엄 자신에게 무척 반가운 것이었으니, 네 마리 말이 끄는 역마차로 그토록 서글서글하고 유쾌한 벗과 함께 여행한다는 생각이 즐거웠던 것이다. 그리고 그것을 급보를 전하러 상경하는 데 견주며, 그는 제 상상이 떠올려 줄 수 있는 온갖 그 행복과 위엄에 대한 말을 대번에 다 늘어놓았다."], ["Crawford’s would rob her of many hours of his company, she was too happy in having William spared from the fatigue of such a journey, to think of anything else.", "그리고 패니 또한, 다른 까닭에서 더없이 기뻤다. 애초의 계획은 윌리엄이 이튿날 밤 노샘프턴에서 우편마차로 상경하는 것이었는데, 그러면 그가 포츠머스행 마차에 오르기까지 한 시간도 쉴 틈이 없었으련만, 크로퍼드 씨의 이 제안이 비록 그의 벗 노릇을 할 여러 시간을 그녀에게서 앗아 갈지언정, 그녀는 윌리엄이 그런 여정의 고단함을 면하게 된 것이 너무 기뻐 다른 것은 생각할 겨를도 없었다."], ["Sir Thomas approved of it for another reason.", "토머스 경은 또 다른 까닭으로 그것을 반겼다."], ["His nephew’s introduction to Admiral Crawford might be of service.", "조카가 크로퍼드 제독에게 소개되는 것이 도움이 될지도 몰랐던 것이다."], ["The Admiral, he believed, had interest.", "그 제독에게는, 그가 믿기로, 힘이 있었다."], ["Upon the whole, it was a very joyous note.", "대체로 그것은 무척 기쁜 쪽지였다."], ["Fanny’s spirits lived on it half the morning, deriving some accession of pleasure from its writer being himself to go away.", "패니의 기운은 그날 아침 절반을 그것에 기대어 살았으니, 그것을 쓴 이 자신이 떠난다는 데서 얼마간 더 큰 즐거움까지 얻었다."]], [["As for the ball, so near at hand, she had too many agitations and fears to have half the enjoyment in anticipation which she ought to have had, or must have been supposed to have by the many young ladies looking forward to the same event in situations more at ease, but under circumstances of less novelty, less interest, less peculiar gratification, than would be attributed to her.", "이토록 코앞에 닥친 무도회로 말하면, 그녀는 마땅히 누렸어야 할, 또는 같은 자리를 손꼽아 기다리는 여러 아가씨—그녀보다 더 마음 편한 처지이되, 그녀에게 돌려질 만한 것보다는 덜 새롭고 덜 흥미롭고 덜 각별한 흐뭇함의 사정에 놓인 그 아가씨들이 응당 누리려니 여겨질 만한, 그 미리 그리는 즐거움의 절반도 누리기엔 너무 많은 동요와 두려움에 시달렸다."], ["Miss Price, known only by name to half the people invited, was now to make her first appearance, and must be regarded as the queen of the evening.", "초대받은 이들 절반에게 이름만 알려져 있던 프라이스 양이 이제 처음으로 모습을 드러내는 것이었고, 그 저녁의 여왕으로 여겨질 참이었다."], ["Who could be happier than Miss Price?", "프라이스 양보다 더 행복할 이가 어디 있으랴?"], ["But Miss Price had not been brought up to the trade of coming out; and had she known in what light this ball was, in general, considered respecting her, it would very much have lessened her comfort by increasing the fears she already had of doing wrong and being looked at.", "그러나 프라이스 양은 사교계에 나서는 일에 익도록 자라지 못했다. 그리고 저를 두고 이 무도회가 대체로 어떤 눈으로 여겨지는지 그녀가 알았더라면, 그녀가 이미 품고 있던, 잘못 처신하고 남의 눈에 띄는 데 대한 두려움만 키워 그 마음의 편안함을 크게 줄였으리라."], ["To dance without much observation or any extraordinary fatigue, to have strength and partners for about half the evening, to dance a little with Edmund, and not a great deal with Mr. Crawford, to see William enjoy himself, and be able to keep away from her aunt Norris, was the height of her ambition, and seemed to comprehend her greatest possibility of happiness.", "그리 눈에 띄지도 유난히 고단하지도 않게 춤추는 것, 저녁 절반쯤 버틸 기력과 짝이 있는 것, 에드먼드와는 얼마간 추되 크로퍼드 씨와는 많이는 안 추는 것, 윌리엄이 즐거워하는 것을 보는 것, 그리고 노리스 이모에게서 멀찍이 떨어져 있을 수 있는 것, 그것이 그녀 야심의 극치였고, 그녀가 누릴 수 있는 가장 큰 행복의 가능성을 다 아우르는 듯 보였다."], ["As these were the best of her hopes, they could not always prevail; and in the course of a long morning, spent principally with her two aunts, she was often under the influence of much less sanguine views.", "이것들이 그녀 바람의 최선이었던지라, 그것들이 늘 이길 수는 없었다. 그리하여 주로 두 이모와 함께 보낸 긴 아침나절 내내, 그녀는 흔히 그보다 훨씬 덜 밝은 심경에 잠겨 있었다."], ["William, determined to make this last day a day of thorough enjoyment, was out snipe-shooting; Edmund, she had too much reason to suppose, was at the Parsonage; and left alone to bear the worrying of Mrs.", "이 마지막 날을 실컷 즐기는 날로 삼기로 굳게 마음먹은 윌리엄은 밖에 나가 도요새를 잡고 있었다. 에드먼드는, 그녀가 짐작할 까닭이 너무 많았듯, 목사관에 가 있었다."], ["Norris, who was cross because the housekeeper would have her own way with the supper, and whom she could not avoid though the housekeeper might, Fanny was worn down at last to think everything an evil belonging to the ball, and when sent off with a parting worry to dress, moved as languidly towards her own room, and felt as incapable of happiness as if she had been allowed no share in it.", "그리고 홀로 남아, 가정부가 저녁상을 제 뜻대로 하려 든다고 심통이 난 노리스 부인의 볶아 댐을 견뎌야 했으니—가정부야 그녀를 피할 수 있었을망정 그녀로서는 그럴 수 없었던지라—패니는 끝내 진이 빠져, 무엇이든 죄다 이 무도회에 딸린 재앙이려니 여기게 되었다. 그리하여 마지막 볶아 댐과 함께 옷을 갈아입으라 내보내지자, 축 처져 제 방으로 걸음을 옮기며, 마치 저에게는 그 몫이 조금도 허락되지 않은 양 행복이라곤 도무지 느낄 수 없는 심정이었다."]], [["As she walked slowly upstairs she thought of yesterday; it had been about the same hour that she had returned from the Parsonage, and found Edmund in the East room.", "천천히 위층으로 오르며 그녀는 어제를 떠올렸다. 목사관에서 돌아와 동쪽 방에서 에드먼드를 본 것이 얼추 이맘때였던 것이다."], ["“Suppose I were to find him there again to-day!”", "“오늘 또 거기서 오빠를 보게 된다면!”"], ["said she to herself, in a fond indulgence of fancy.", "그녀는 다정한 공상에 젖어 혼자 뇌었다."]], [["“Fanny,”", "“패니,”"], ["said a voice at that moment near her.", "하고 바로 그때 그녀 가까이서 한 목소리가 났다."], ["Starting and looking up, she saw, across the lobby she had just reached, Edmund himself, standing at the head of a different staircase.", "흠칫하며 올려다보니, 방금 다다른 로비 건너편에, 다른 계단 위쪽에 에드먼드가 서 있는 것이 보였다."], ["He came towards her.", "그가 그녀 쪽으로 다가왔다."], ["“You look tired and fagged, Fanny.", "“지치고 고단해 보이는구나, 패니."], ["You have been walking too far.”", "너무 멀리 걸었나 보다.”"]], [["“No, I have not been out at all.”", "“아뇨, 저는 아예 안 나갔는걸요.”"]], [["“Then you have had fatigues within doors, which are worse.", "“그럼 집 안에서 고단한 일을 겪었구나, 그게 더 나쁜데."], ["You had better have gone out.”", "차라리 나갔다 오는 게 나을 뻔했다.”"]], [["Fanny, not liking to complain, found it easiest to make no answer; and though he looked at her with his usual kindness, she believed he had soon ceased to think of her countenance.", "패니는 하소연하기가 싫어, 아무 대답도 안 하는 편이 가장 낫다 싶었다. 그리고 그가 여느 때의 그 친절로 그녀를 바라보기는 했으나, 그녀는 그가 이내 제 낯빛에 대한 생각을 그친 줄로 여겼다."], ["He did not appear in spirits: something unconnected with her was probably amiss.", "그는 기운이 없어 보였다. 아마 그녀와 상관없는 무언가가 잘못된 모양이었다."], ["They proceeded upstairs together, their rooms being on the same floor above.", "둘은 함께 위층으로 올라갔으니, 그들의 방은 위층 같은 층에 있었다."]], [["“I come from Dr. Grant’s,”", "“그랜트 박사 댁에 다녀오는 길이야.”"], ["said Edmund presently.", "이내 에드먼드가 말했다."], ["“You may guess my errand there, Fanny.”", "“내 거기 볼일이 뭔지야 넌 짐작하겠지, 패니.”"], ["And he looked so conscious, that Fanny could think but of one errand, which turned her too sick for speech.", "그리고 그의 낯빛이 어찌나 켕기는 듯하던지, 패니는 오직 한 가지 볼일밖에 떠올릴 수 없었고, 그것이 그녀를 너무 메스껍게 만들어 말도 나오지 않았다."], ["“I wished to engage Miss Crawford for the two first dances,”", "“처음 두 곡의 춤을 위해 크로퍼드 양을 붙잡아 두고 싶어서.”"], ["was the explanation that followed, and brought Fanny to life again, enabling her, as she found she was expected to speak, to utter something like an inquiry as to the result.", "이것이 뒤따른 설명이었고, 이것이 패니를 다시 살려 내어, 저더러 말하라는 기색인 줄 알아채고 그 결말이 어찌 되었는지 묻는 것 비슷한 무언가를 뇌게 해 주었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“응,”"], ["he answered, “she is engaged to me; but”", "그가 대답했다. “나한테 약속했어."], ["(with a smile that did not sit easy) “she says it is to be the last time that she ever will dance with me.", "하지만” (썩 편치 않은 미소를 띠며) “나랑 춤추는 건 이번이 마지막이 될 거라는구나."], ["She is not serious.", "진심은 아니야."], ["I think, I hope, I am sure she is not serious; but I would rather not hear it.", "나는 그녀가 진심이 아니라 생각하고, 바라고, 확신해. 하지만 그런 말은 안 듣느니만 못하지."], ["She never has danced with a clergyman, she says, and she never will.", "성직자하고는 여태 춤춰 본 적이 없고, 앞으로도 결코 안 추겠다는 거야."], ["For my own sake, I could wish there had been no ball just at—I mean not this very week, this very day; to-morrow I leave home.”", "나로서야, 하필 이런—그러니까 하필 이번 주에, 바로 오늘 무도회가 없었더라면 싶어. 내일이면 나는 집을 떠나니까.”"]], [["Fanny struggled for speech, and said, “I am very sorry that anything has occurred to distress you.", "패니는 애써 말을 짜내어 이렇게 말했다. “무언가 오빠를 괴롭히는 일이 생겼다니 저도 무척 안됐어요."], ["This ought to be a day of pleasure.", "오늘은 즐거운 날이어야 하는데."], ["My uncle meant it so.”", "삼촌도 그리 되라고 여신 건데요.”"]], [["“Oh yes, yes!", "“아 그럼, 그럼!"], ["and it will be a day of pleasure.", "그리고 즐거운 날이 될 거야."], ["It will all end right.", "다 잘 끝날 테고."], ["I am only vexed for a moment.", "나야 잠깐 언짢을 뿐이지."], ["In fact, it is not that I consider the ball as ill-timed; what does it signify?", "실은 무도회를 때 안 맞는 것으로 여겨서가 아니야. 그게 무슨 대수겠니?"], ["But, Fanny,”", "하지만, 패니,”"], ["stopping her, by taking her hand, and speaking low and seriously, “you know what all this means.", "하고 그녀의 손을 잡아 걸음을 멈춰 세우며, 나직하고 진지하게 말했다. “너는 이 모든 게 무슨 뜻인지 알잖니."], ["You see how it is; and could tell me, perhaps better than I could tell you, how and why I am vexed.", "어찌 된 영문인지 너는 알고 있어. 그리고 내가 어떻게, 왜 언짢은지, 어쩌면 내가 너한테 말해 줄 수 있는 것보다 더 잘 나한테 말해 줄 수 있겠지."], ["Let me talk to you a little.", "잠깐 너랑 얘기 좀 하자."], ["You are a kind, kind listener.", "너는 참으로, 참으로 다정한 들을 이거든."], ["I have been pained by her manner this morning, and cannot get the better of it.", "나는 오늘 아침 그녀의 태도에 마음이 아팠고, 그걸 도무지 떨쳐 낼 수가 없어."], ["I know her disposition to be as sweet and faultless as your own, but the influence of her former companions makes her seem—gives to her conversation, to her professed opinions, sometimes a tinge of wrong.", "나는 그녀의 성품이 너만큼이나 곱고 흠 없는 줄 알아. 하지만 예전 벗들의 물듦이 그녀를—그녀의 말에, 그녀가 내세우는 견해에 이따금 그릇된 빛깔을 얼마간 입히는 거야."], ["She does not think evil, but she speaks it, speaks it in playfulness; and though I know it to be playfulness, it grieves me to the soul.”", "그녀는 악을 품는 게 아니라, 그걸 입에 담는 거야, 장난삼아 담는 거지. 그리고 나야 그게 장난인 줄 알지만, 그게 내 넋까지 아프게 하는구나.”"]], [["“The effect of education,”", "“교육 탓이지요.”"], ["said Fanny gently.", "패니가 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["Edmund could not but agree to it.", "에드먼드도 그에 수긍하지 않을 수 없었다. “그래, 그 삼촌에 그 숙모라니!"], ["“Yes, that uncle and aunt!", "그들이 더없이 고운 마음을 상하게 한 거야."], ["They have injured the finest mind; for sometimes, Fanny, I own to you, it does appear more than manner: it appears as if the mind itself was tainted.”", "이따금은 말이다, 패니, 너한테 털어놓거니와, 그게 태도 이상으로 보이거든. 마음 자체가 물든 것처럼 보인단 말이야.”"]], [["Fanny imagined this to be an appeal to her judgment, and therefore, after a moment’s consideration, said, “If you only want me as a listener, cousin, I will be as useful as I can; but I am not qualified for an adviser.", "패니는 이것을 제 판단을 구하는 것이려니 여겨, 잠깐 생각한 뒤에 말했다. “저를 그저 들을 이로만 원하시는 거라면, 오빠, 저야 되도록 쓸모가 되겠어요."], ["Do not ask advice of me.", "하지만 저는 조언자로는 어울리지 않아요. 저한테 조언을 구하진 마세요."], ["I am not competent.”", "저는 그럴 깜냥이 못 돼요.”"]], [["“You are right, Fanny, to protest against such an office, but you need not be afraid.", "“그런 소임을 마다하는 건 옳아, 패니. 하지만 겁낼 것 없어."], ["It is a subject on which I should never ask advice; it is the sort of subject on which it had better never be asked; and few, I imagine, do ask it, but when they want to be influenced against their conscience.", "이건 내가 결코 조언을 구할 만한 일이 아니야. 아예 조언 같은 건 안 구하느니만 못한 그런 일이지."], ["I only want to talk to you.”", "그리고 내 짐작에, 이런 걸 조언 구하는 이는 거의 없어, 제 양심에 거스르도록 부추김받고 싶을 때 말고는. 나야 그저 너랑 얘기하고 싶을 뿐이야.”"]], [["“One thing more.", "“한 가지만 더요."], ["Excuse the liberty; but take care how you talk to me.", "주제넘음을 용서하세요. 하지만 저한테 어떻게 말씀하실지 조심하세요."], ["Do not tell me anything now, which hereafter you may be sorry for.", "지금 무언가, 나중에 후회하실 만한 말은 저한테 하지 마세요."], ["The time may come—”", "그때가 올지도—”"]], [["The colour rushed into her cheeks as she spoke.", "이 말을 하며 그녀의 두 뺨에 홍조가 왈칵 밀려들었다."]], [["“Dearest Fanny!”", "“더없이 사랑하는 패니야!”"], ["cried Edmund, pressing her hand to his lips with almost as much warmth as if it had been Miss Crawford’s, “you are all considerate thought!", "에드먼드가, 마치 크로퍼드 양의 손이기라도 한 듯 그에 못지않은 뜨거움으로 그녀의 손을 제 입술에 대며 외쳤다. “너는 온통 사려 깊은 마음뿐이구나!"], ["But it is unnecessary here.", "하지만 여기선 그럴 것 없어. 그런 때는 결코 안 와."], ["The time will never come.", "네가 넌지시 말하는 그런 때는 결코 오지 않아. 나는 그게 더없이 있음 직하지 않다고 여기기 시작했어."], ["No such time as you allude to will ever come.", "그 가망은 갈수록 작아지거든. 그리고 설령 그런 때가 온다 해도, 너든 나든 두려워할 만한 것이라곤 기억에 남을 게 하나도 없을 거야."], ["I begin to think it most improbable: the chances grow less and less; and even if it should, there will be nothing to be remembered by either you or me that we need be afraid of, for I can never be ashamed of my own scruples; and if they are removed, it must be by changes that will only raise her character the more by the recollection of the faults she once had.", "나는 결코 내 거리낌을 부끄러워할 일이 없을 테고, 그것이 걷힌다면 그건 오직, 그녀가 한때 지녔던 허물을 되새김으로써 오히려 그녀의 됨됨이를 더욱 돋보이게 할 그런 변화로써일 테니까. 내가 지금 한 이런 말을 할 사람은 이 세상에 오직 너뿐이야."], ["You are the only being upon earth to whom I should say what I have said; but you have always known my opinion of her; you can bear me witness, Fanny, that I have never been blinded.", "하지만 너는 늘 그녀에 대한 내 생각을 알고 있었지. 너는 내 증인이 되어 줄 수 있어, 패니, 내가 결코 눈멀지 않았다는 걸."], ["How many a time have we talked over her little errors!", "우리가 그녀의 자잘한 잘못들을 두고 얼마나 여러 번 얘기했더냐! 너는 나를 두려워할 것 없다."], ["You need not fear me; I have almost given up every serious idea of her; but I must be a blockhead indeed, if, whatever befell me, I could think of your kindness and sympathy without the sincerest gratitude.”", "나는 그녀에 대한 온갖 진지한 생각을 거의 다 접었으니까. 하지만 나한테 무슨 일이 닥치든 간에, 네 친절과 살핌을 더없이 진실한 고마움 없이 떠올릴 수 있다면, 나야말로 정녕 멍청이일 테지.”"]], [["He had said enough to shake the experience of eighteen.", "그는 열여덟 살의 경험을 뒤흔들 만큼 충분히 말한 셈이었다. 그는 패니에게 요즈음 알던 것보다 더 행복한 심경을 안겨 줄 만큼 충분히 말한 셈이었다."], ["He had said enough to give Fanny some happier feelings than she had lately known, and with a brighter look, she answered, “Yes, cousin, I am convinced that you would be incapable of anything else, though perhaps some might not.", "그리하여 한결 밝아진 낯빛으로 그녀는 대답했다. “네, 오빠, 저는 확신해요, 오빠라면 결코 다른 짓은 못 하실 거라고요, 어쩌면 그러지 못할 이도 더러 있겠지만요."], ["I cannot be afraid of hearing anything you wish to say.", "오빠가 하고픈 무슨 말이든 듣는 게 저는 두렵지 않아요."], ["Do not check yourself.", "스스로를 억누르지 마세요."], ["Tell me whatever you like.”", "하고픈 건 뭐든 저한테 말씀하세요.”"]], [["They were now on the second floor, and the appearance of a housemaid prevented any farther conversation.", "이제 둘은 삼 층에 이르렀고, 하녀 하나가 나타나는 바람에 더는 이야기를 나누지 못했다. 패니의 그때 마음의 편안함을 위해서는, 어쩌면 그것이 가장 행복한 순간에 끝맺어진 셈이었다."], ["For Fanny’s present comfort it was concluded, perhaps, at the happiest moment: had he been able to talk another five minutes, there is no saying that he might not have talked away all Miss Crawford’s faults and his own despondence.", "그가 오 분만 더 얘기할 수 있었더라면, 크로퍼드 양의 온갖 허물과 제 낙담을 죄다 말로 풀어 없앴을지도 모를 노릇이었다. 그러나 사정이 그러했던지라, 둘은 그의 편에서는 고마운 애정 어린 낯빛으로, 그녀의 편에서는 자못 소중한 심경으로 헤어졌다."], ["But as it was, they parted with looks on his side of grateful affection, and with some very precious sensations on hers.", "그녀는 여러 시간 동안 이런 것을 조금도 느껴 본 적이 없었다. 크로퍼드 씨가 윌리엄에게 보낸 쪽지에서 온 그 첫 기쁨이 스러진 뒤로, 그녀는 정반대의 상태에 있었다."], ["She had felt nothing like it for hours.", "둘레엔 아무 위안도 없고, 마음속엔 아무 바람도 없었다."], ["Since the first joy from Mr.", "이제 만사가 방긋 웃고 있었다."], ["Crawford’s note to William had worn away, she had been in a state absolutely the reverse; there had been no comfort around, no hope within her.", "윌리엄의 행운이 다시금 그녀의 마음에 되돌아와, 처음보다 더 값진 것으로 보였다. 무도회 또한—눈앞에 그런 즐거운 저녁이 펼쳐져 있다니!"], ["Now everything was smiling.", "이제 그것은 참된 설렘이었다."], ["William’s good fortune returned again upon her mind, and seemed of greater value than at first.", "그리하여 그녀는 무도회에 딸린 그 행복한 두근거림을 한껏 안고 무도회를 위해 옷을 갈아입기 시작했다."], ["The ball, too—such an evening of pleasure before her!", "만사가 잘 풀렸다. 그녀는 제 모습이 싫지 않았다."], ["It was now a real animation; and she began to dress for it with much of the happy flutter which belongs to a ball.", "그리고 다시 목걸이들을 만지작거리게 되었을 때, 그녀의 행운은 온전해 보였다."], ["All went well: she did not dislike her own looks; and when she came to the necklaces again, her good fortune seemed complete, for upon trial the one given her by Miss Crawford would by no means go through the ring of the cross.", "걸어 보니 크로퍼드 양이 준 것은 도무지 그 십자가의 고리를 통과하지 못했던 것이다. 그녀는 에드먼드의 뜻을 받들어 그것을 걸기로 마음먹었었으나, 그 쓰임엔 너무 컸다."], ["She had, to oblige Edmund, resolved to wear it; but it was too large for the purpose.", "그러니 그의 것을 걸어야 했다."], ["His, therefore, must be worn; and having, with delightful feelings, joined the chain and the cross—those memorials of the two most beloved of her heart, those dearest tokens so formed for each other by everything real and imaginary—and put them round her neck, and seen and felt how full of William and Edmund they were, she was able, without an effort, to resolve on wearing Miss Crawford’s necklace too.", "그리하여 더없이 흐뭇한 심경으로 그 줄과 십자가를—제 마음에 가장 사랑하는 두 사람의 그 정표를, 온갖 실제와 상상으로 서로에게 그토록 꼭 맞게 지어진 그 더없이 소중한 표시를—이어 목에 둘렀다. 그리고 그것들이 얼마나 윌리엄과 에드먼드로 가득 차 있는지를 보고 느끼고 나니, 그녀는 애쓸 것도 없이 크로퍼드 양의 목걸이 또한 걸기로 마음먹을 수 있었다."], ["She acknowledged it to be right.", "그녀는 그리하는 것이 옳음을 인정했다."], ["Miss Crawford had a claim; and when it was no longer to encroach on, to interfere with the stronger claims, the truer kindness of another, she could do her justice even with pleasure to herself.", "크로퍼드 양에게도 응당한 몫이 있었다. 그리고 그것이 더는 다른 이의 더 굳센 몫과 더 참된 친절을 침범하거나 방해하지 않게 된 마당이니, 그녀는 스스로 기꺼운 마음으로도 크로퍼드 양을 온당히 대할 수 있었다."], ["The necklace really looked very well; and Fanny left her room at last, comfortably satisfied with herself and all about her.", "그 목걸이는 정말이지 무척 잘 어울렸다. 그리하여 패니는 마침내 제 방을 나섰으니, 저 자신에게도 제 둘레의 모든 것에도 흐뭇이 흡족한 채였다."]], [["Her aunt Bertram had recollected her on this occasion with an unusual degree of wakefulness.", "버트럼 이모는 이번 일에 뜻밖에도 정신을 바짝 차려 그녀를 챙겼다."], ["It had really occurred to her, unprompted, that Fanny, preparing for a ball, might be glad of better help than the upper housemaid’s, and when dressed herself, she actually sent her own maid to assist her; too late, of course, to be of any use. Mrs.", "그녀는 누가 시키지도 않았는데, 무도회를 채비하는 패니가 위층 하녀보다 더 나은 손길을 반길지도 모르겠다는 생각이 정말로 들어, 제가 옷을 다 갈아입고 나자 실제로 제 하녀를 그녀에게 거들라고 보냈다—물론 아무 쓸모도 못 될 만큼 너무 늦었지만."], ["Chapman had just reached the attic floor, when Miss Price came out of her room completely dressed, and only civilities were necessary; but Fanny felt her aunt’s attention almost as much as Lady Bertram or Mrs.", "채프먼 부인이 다락층에 막 이르렀을 때 프라이스 양이 옷을 다 갖춰 입고 제 방에서 나오니, 예의치레나 오갈밖에 없었다."], ["Chapman could do themselves.", "그러나 패니는 버트럼 부인이나 채프먼 부인 자신들이 느낄 만한 것에 거의 못지않게 이모의 마음씀을 느꼈다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXVIII", "# 제28장"]], [["Her uncle and both her aunts were in the drawing-room when Fanny went down.", "패니가 내려갔을 때 삼촌과 두 이모가 응접실에 있었다."], ["To the former she was an interesting object, and he saw with pleasure the general elegance of her appearance, and her being in remarkably good looks.", "삼촌에게 그녀는 눈여겨볼 만한 대상이었으니, 그는 그 차림새의 두루 우아함과, 그녀가 유난히 고와 보이는 것을 흐뭇이 바라보았다."], ["The neatness and propriety of her dress was all that he would allow himself to commend in her presence, but upon her leaving the room again soon afterwards, he spoke of her beauty with very decided praise.", "그녀 앞에서 그가 스스로 칭찬을 허락한 것이라곤 그 옷차림의 정갈함과 온당함뿐이었으나, 이내 그녀가 다시 방을 나가자 그는 그 아름다움을 자못 뚜렷한 칭찬으로 입에 올렸다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“그럼요,”"], ["said Lady Bertram, “she looks very well.", "버트럼 부인이 말했다. “저 애가 참 곱네요."], ["I sent Chapman to her.”", "내가 채프먼을 저 애한테 보냈거든요.”"]], [["“Look well!", "“곱기만 하겠어요!"], ["Oh, yes!”", "아이참, 그럼요!”"], ["cried Mrs.", "노리스 부인이 외쳤다."], ["Norris, “she has good reason to look well with all her advantages: brought up in this family as she has been, with all the benefit of her cousins’ manners before her.", "“저만한 온갖 덕을 입었으니 고와 보일 만도 하지요. 이 집안에서 이렇게 자랐으니 말이에요, 눈앞에서 사촌들의 몸가짐을 죄다 본받는 그 온갖 덕을 누리면서요."], ["Only think, my dear Sir Thomas, what extraordinary advantages you and I have been the means of giving her.", "생각 좀 해 보세요, 토머스 경, 경과 내가 저 애한테 얼마나 유별난 덕을 베풀어 주는 노릇을 했는지요."], ["The very gown you have been taking notice of is your own generous present to her when dear Mrs. Rushworth married.", "경께서 지금 눈여겨보신 바로 그 가운도, 소중한 러시워스 부인이 시집갈 때 경께서 저 애한테 손수 내리신 후한 선물이잖아요."], ["What would she have been if we had not taken her by the hand?”", "우리가 저 애를 거두지 않았더라면 저 애가 대체 어찌 됐겠어요?”"]], [["Sir Thomas said no more; but when they sat down to table the eyes of the two young men assured him that the subject might be gently touched again, when the ladies withdrew, with more success.", "토머스 경은 더는 말이 없었다. 그러나 다들 식탁에 앉자, 두 젊은이의 눈길이 그에게, 부인들이 물러난 뒤 그 화제를 다시 살며시 건드려 보면 더 잘 통하리라는 것을 일러 주었다."], ["Fanny saw that she was approved; and the consciousness of looking well made her look still better.", "패니는 저가 좋게 여겨지는 것을 알았고, 곱게 보인다는 자각이 그녀를 한층 더 곱게 만들었다. 여러 까닭으로 그녀는 행복했고, 이내 한층 더 행복해졌다."], ["From a variety of causes she was happy, and she was soon made still happier; for in following her aunts out of the room, Edmund, who was holding open the door, said, as she passed him, “You must dance with me, Fanny; you must keep two dances for me; any two that you like, except the first.”", "이모들을 따라 방을 나서는데, 문을 열어 잡고 있던 에드먼드가 그녀가 지나갈 제 이렇게 말한 것이다. “나랑 춤춰야 한다, 패니."], ["She had nothing more to wish for.", "나를 위해 두 곡을 남겨 둬. 네가 좋은 아무 두 곡이든, 첫 곡만 빼고.”"], ["She had hardly ever been in a state so nearly approaching high spirits in her life.", "그녀는 더 바랄 것이 없었다. 그녀는 평생 이토록 들뜸에 가까운 상태에 있어 본 적이 좀처럼 없었다."], ["Her cousins’ former gaiety on the day of a ball was no longer surprising to her; she felt it to be indeed very charming, and was actually practising her steps about the drawing-room as long as she could be safe from the notice of her aunt Norris, who was entirely taken up at first in fresh arranging and injuring the noble fire which the butler had prepared.", "무도회 날 사촌들이 예전에 보이던 그 들뜸이 이제는 조금도 놀랍지 않았다. 그것이 정말이지 무척 사랑스러운 것임을 그녀는 느꼈고, 실제로 응접실 여기저기서 춤 스텝을 밟아 보고 있었으니—처음엔 집사가 마련해 둔 그 훌륭한 불을 새로 헤집어 도리어 망치는 데 온통 정신이 팔려 있던 노리스 이모의 눈을 피할 수 있는 동안 말이다."]], [["Half an hour followed that would have been at least languid under any other circumstances, but Fanny’s happiness still prevailed.", "이어진 반 시간은, 다른 어떤 사정에서였다면 적어도 맥없었을 시간이었으나, 패니의 행복이 여전히 이겼다."], ["It was but to think of her conversation with Edmund, and what was the restlessness of Mrs. Norris?", "그저 에드먼드와의 그 이야기를 떠올리기만 하면 될 뿐, 노리스 부인의 안절부절이 다 무엇이랴?"], ["What were the yawns of Lady Bertram?", "버트럼 부인의 하품이 다 무엇이랴?"]], [["The gentlemen joined them; and soon after began the sweet expectation of a carriage, when a general spirit of ease and enjoyment seemed diffused, and they all stood about and talked and laughed, and every moment had its pleasure and its hope.", "신사들이 그들과 어울렸다. 그리고 이내 마차를 기다리는 그 달콤한 설렘이 시작되니, 두루 편안하고 즐거운 기운이 감도는 듯하여, 다들 여기저기 둘러서서 이야기하고 웃었으며, 매 순간에 저마다의 즐거움과 저마다의 바람이 깃들었다."], ["Fanny felt that there must be a struggle in Edmund’s cheerfulness, but it was delightful to see the effort so successfully made.", "패니는 에드먼드의 그 명랑함 속에 얼마간 안간힘이 있으려니 느꼈으나, 그 애씀이 그토록 감쪽같이 이루어지는 것을 보는 것은 흐뭇한 일이었다."]], [["When the carriages were really heard, when the guests began really to assemble, her own gaiety of heart was much subdued: the sight of so many strangers threw her back into herself; and besides the gravity and formality of the first great circle, which the manners of neither Sir Thomas nor Lady Bertram were of a kind to do away, she found herself occasionally called on to endure something worse.", "마차 소리가 정말로 들리고, 손님들이 정말로 모여들기 시작하자, 그녀 자신의 들뜬 마음은 자못 사그라들었다. 그토록 많은 낯선 이의 모습이 그녀를 다시 제 속으로 움츠러들게 만든 것이다."], ["She was introduced here and there by her uncle, and forced to be spoken to, and to curtsey, and speak again.", "게다가 토머스 경도 버트럼 부인도 그 태도로는 걷어 낼 만한 종류가 못 되는, 처음 그 큰 무리의 그 근엄함과 격식스러움에 더해, 그녀는 이따금 그보다 더한 것을 견뎌 내라 불려 나오는 자신을 발견했다."], ["This was a hard duty, and she was never summoned to it without looking at William, as he walked about at his ease in the background of the scene, and longing to be with him.", "그녀는 삼촌 손에 이끌려 여기저기 소개되었고, 하는 수 없이 말을 걸어 받고, 무릎 굽혀 인사하고, 다시 말해야 했다. 이것은 고된 소임이었고, 이 소임에 불려 나올 때마다 그녀는, 그 광경의 뒤편에서 느긋이 거니는 윌리엄을 바라보며 그의 곁에 있고 싶어 하지 않은 적이 없었다."]], [["The entrance of the Grants and Crawfords was a favourable epoch.", "그랜트 부부와 크로퍼드 남매의 등장은 반가운 전환점이었다."], ["The stiffness of the meeting soon gave way before their popular manners and more diffused intimacies: little groups were formed, and everybody grew comfortable.", "만남의 그 뻣뻣함은 그들의 서글서글한 태도와 한결 폭넓은 친분 앞에서 이내 누그러졌다. 자그마한 무리들이 이루어지고, 다들 편안해졌다."], ["Fanny felt the advantage; and, drawing back from the toils of civility, would have been again most happy, could she have kept her eyes from wandering between Edmund and Mary Crawford.", "패니는 그 덕을 느꼈다. 그리고 예의차림의 고역에서 물러나, 에드먼드와 메리 크로퍼드 사이를 오가는 제 눈길만 붙잡아 둘 수 있었더라면 다시금 더없이 행복했으련만."], ["She looked all loveliness—and what might not be the end of it?", "그녀는 온통 사랑스러워 보였다—그러니 그 끝이 무엇인들 안 되겠는가?"], ["Her own musings were brought to an end on perceiving Mr. Crawford before her, and her thoughts were put into another channel by his engaging her almost instantly for the first two dances.", "크로퍼드 씨가 제 앞에 있음을 알아채면서 그녀 자신의 상념은 끝이 났고, 그가 거의 대번에 그녀를 처음 두 곡의 춤에 붙잡으니 그녀의 생각은 다른 물길로 돌려졌다."], ["Her happiness on this occasion was very much à la mortal, finely chequered.", "이번 일에서 그녀의 행복은 자못 인간다운 것이라, 곱게 얼룩덜룩했다."], ["To be secure of a partner at first was a most essential good—for the moment of beginning was now growing seriously near; and she so little understood her own claims as to think that if Mr.", "우선 짝을 확보해 둔다는 것은 더없이 요긴한 좋음이었다—시작할 순간이 이제 자못 가까이 다가오고 있었던 까닭이다."], ["Crawford had not asked her, she must have been the last to be sought after, and should have received a partner only through a series of inquiry, and bustle, and interference, which would have been terrible; but at the same time there was a pointedness in his manner of asking her which she did not like, and she saw his eye glancing for a moment at her necklace, with a smile—she thought there was a smile—which made her blush and feel wretched.", "게다가 그녀는 제 값어치를 하도 몰라, 크로퍼드 씨가 청하지 않았더라면 저야말로 가장 늦게까지 찾는 이 없는 사람이 되었으리라고, 그리하여 이런저런 수소문과 부산과 참견을 거쳐서야 겨우 짝을 얻었으리라고, 그것이 얼마나 끔찍했으랴 여겼다. 그러나 그와 동시에 그가 청하는 태도에는 그녀가 달갑잖은 어떤 노골적임이 있었고, 그녀는 그의 눈길이 한순간 제 목걸이를 스치는 것을, 어떤 미소와 함께—미소가 있는 것 같았다—그리하는 것을 보았으니, 그것이 그녀를 얼굴 붉히고 비참하게 만들었다."], ["And though there was no second glance to disturb her, though his object seemed then to be only quietly agreeable, she could not get the better of her embarrassment, heightened as it was by the idea of his perceiving it, and had no composure till he turned away to some one else.", "그리고 그녀를 어지럽힐 두 번째 눈길이 없었을망정, 그때 그의 뜻은 그저 조용히 상냥하게 굴려는 것뿐인 듯했을망정, 그녀는 그 당혹감을 이겨 낼 수가 없었으니—그가 그것을 알아챈다는 생각에 그 당혹감이 더 커진 까닭이라—그가 다른 누군가에게로 돌아설 때까지 도무지 마음을 가라앉히지 못했다."], ["Then she could gradually rise up to the genuine satisfaction of having a partner, a voluntary partner, secured against the dancing began.", "그러고서야 그녀는, 춤이 시작되기 전에 짝을, 그것도 자청한 짝을 확보해 두었다는 그 진정한 흡족함으로 차츰 올라설 수 있었다."]], [["When the company were moving into the ballroom, she found herself for the first time near Miss Crawford, whose eyes and smiles were immediately and more unequivocally directed as her brother’s had been, and who was beginning to speak on the subject, when Fanny, anxious to get the story over, hastened to give the explanation of the second necklace: the real chain.", "무리가 무도회장으로 옮겨 갈 즈음, 그녀는 난생처음 크로퍼드 양 가까이 서게 되었다. 크로퍼드 양의 눈길과 미소는 대번에, 그 오라비의 것이 그러했던 것보다 한결 더 또렷하게 그녀에게로 쏠렸고, 그녀가 그 이야기를 막 꺼내려던 참에, 그 얘기를 어서 끝내고 싶어 안달하던 패니가 서둘러 두 번째 목걸이의—곧 진짜 그 줄의—사연을 밝혔다."], ["Miss Crawford listened; and all her intended compliments and insinuations to Fanny were forgotten: she felt only one thing; and her eyes, bright as they had been before, shewing they could yet be brighter, she exclaimed with eager pleasure, “Did he?", "크로퍼드 양은 귀 기울여 들었다. 그리하여 패니에게 건네려던 그 온갖 찬사며 넌지시 비추려던 말이 죄다 잊히고, 그녀는 오직 한 가지만을 느꼈다."], ["Did Edmund? That was like himself.", "그리고 아까도 밝던 두 눈이 더 밝아질 수 있음을 보여 주며, 그녀는 벅찬 기쁨으로 외쳤다."], ["No other man would have thought of it.", "“그이가요? 에드먼드가요?"], ["I honour him beyond expression.”", "딱 그이다운 일이네요."], ["And she looked around as if longing to tell him so.", "다른 남자라면 그런 생각도 못 했을 거예요. 나는 이루 말 못 하게 그이를 우러러봐요.”"], ["He was not near, he was attending a party of ladies out of the room; and Mrs.", "그러고는 마치 당장 그에게 그리 말하고 싶어 못 견디는 듯 둘레를 둘러보았다. 그는 가까이에 없었다."], ["Grant coming up to the two girls, and taking an arm of each, they followed with the rest.", "그는 부인들 한 무리를 방 밖으로 배웅하는 중이었다. 그리고 그랜트 부인이 두 아가씨에게 다가와 저마다 한쪽 팔을 끼니, 그들은 나머지 이들과 함께 뒤따라갔다."]], [["Fanny’s heart sunk, but there was no leisure for thinking long even of Miss Crawford’s feelings.", "패니는 가슴이 내려앉았으나, 크로퍼드 양의 심경마저 오래 헤아릴 겨를은 없었다."], ["They were in the ballroom, the violins were playing, and her mind was in a flutter that forbade its fixing on anything serious.", "그들은 무도회장에 있었고, 바이올린이 연주되고 있었으며, 그녀의 마음은 무언가 진중한 데 붙박일 수 없게 하는 그런 설렘에 잠겨 있었다."], ["She must watch the general arrangements, and see how everything was done.", "그녀는 두루 어떻게 짜이는지를 지켜보며 만사가 어찌 돌아가는지 봐야 했다."]], [["In a few minutes Sir Thomas came to her, and asked if she were engaged; and the “Yes, sir; to Mr.", "몇 분 만에 토머스 경이 그녀에게 와서 짝이 정해졌느냐고 물으니, “네, 삼촌. 크로퍼드 씨와요”라는 그 대답은 바로 그가 들으려던 그대로였다."], ["Crawford,”", "크로퍼드 씨는 멀리 있지 않았다."], ["was exactly what he had intended to hear. Mr.", "토머스 경이 그를 그녀에게 데려오며 무슨 말인가를 했는데, 그것으로 패니는 자기가 앞장서서 무도회의 첫 춤을 열어야 함을 알아챘다."], ["Crawford was not far off; Sir Thomas brought him to her, saying something which discovered to Fanny, that she was to lead the way and open the ball; an idea that had never occurred to her before.", "여태 한 번도 떠올려 본 적 없는 생각이었다. 그날 저녁의 시시콜콜한 대목을 그려 볼 때마다, 그녀는 에드먼드가 크로퍼드 양과 첫 춤을 시작하려니 여기는 것을 당연시했다."], ["Whenever she had thought of the minutiae of the evening, it had been as a matter of course that Edmund would begin with Miss Crawford; and the impression was so strong, that though her uncle spoke the contrary, she could not help an exclamation of surprise, a hint of her unfitness, an entreaty even to be excused.", "그리고 그 인상이 어찌나 강했던지, 삼촌이 그 반대로 말하는데도 그녀는 놀라 소리치지 않을 수 없었고, 제가 그럴 자격이 못 됨을 넌지시 비추고, 부디 봐 달라 애원까지 하지 않을 수 없었다. 토머스 경의 뜻을 거슬러 제 의견을 우겨 대는 것은 이 일이 그만큼 절박했다는 증거였다."], ["To be urging her opinion against Sir Thomas’s was a proof of the extremity of the case; but such was her horror at the first suggestion, that she could actually look him in the face and say that she hoped it might be settled otherwise; in vain, however: Sir Thomas smiled, tried to encourage her, and then looked too serious, and said too decidedly, “It must be so, my dear,”", "그러나 그 첫 제안에 그녀가 어찌나 질겁했던지, 그녀는 실제로 그의 얼굴을 마주 보며 부디 달리 정해지기를 바란다고 말할 수 있었다. 그러나 허사였다."], ["for her to hazard another word; and she found herself the next moment conducted by Mr.", "토머스 경은 미소 지으며 그녀를 북돋우려 하다가, 이내 너무 진지한 낯빛이 되어 너무 단호하게 “그리되어야 한다, 얘야”"], ["Crawford to the top of the room, and standing there to be joined by the rest of the dancers, couple after couple, as they were formed.", "하고 말하니, 그녀는 감히 한마디도 더 못 붙였다. 그리하여 그녀는 다음 순간 크로퍼드 씨의 안내로 방 위쪽으로 이끌려, 거기 서서 짝지어 이루어지는 대로 한 쌍 한 쌍 다른 춤꾼들이 합류하기를 기다리는 자신을 발견했다."]], [["She could hardly believe it.", "그녀는 도무지 믿을 수가 없었다."], ["To be placed above so many elegant young women!", "그토록 우아한 아가씨를 그토록 많이 제치고 윗자리에 서다니!"], ["The distinction was too great.", "그 각별함은 너무나 큰 것이었다."], ["It was treating her like her cousins!", "그것은 그녀를 제 사촌들처럼 대우하는 것이었다!"], ["And her thoughts flew to those absent cousins with most unfeigned and truly tender regret, that they were not at home to take their own place in the room, and have their share of a pleasure which would have been so very delightful to them.", "그리하여 그녀의 생각은, 그 자리에 없는 사촌들에게로, 더없이 꾸밈없고 참으로 애틋한 서운함과 함께 날아갔다—그들이 집에 있어 이 방에서 제 자리를 차지하고, 그들에게 그토록 무척 즐거웠을 그 즐거움을 나누지 못하다니."], ["So often as she had heard them wish for a ball at home as the greatest of all felicities!", "그들이 집에서 무도회 여는 것을 온갖 복 가운데 으뜸이라며 얼마나 자주 바라던가!"], ["And to have them away when it was given—and for her to be opening the ball—and with Mr. Crawford too!", "그런데 그것이 열리는 마당에 그들은 없고—그것도 그녀가 무도회를 열다니—게다가 크로퍼드 씨와 함께라니!"], ["She hoped they would not envy her that distinction now; but when she looked back to the state of things in the autumn, to what they had all been to each other when once dancing in that house before, the present arrangement was almost more than she could understand herself.", "그녀는 그들이 지금 이 각별함을 시샘하지 않기를 바랐다. 그러나 가을의 그 형편을, 언젠가 예전에 이 집에서 함께 춤추던 때에 다들 서로에게 어떤 사이였던가를 되돌아보니, 지금 이 짜임은 그녀 자신조차 도무지 헤아리기 어려운 것이었다."]], [["The ball began.", "무도회가 시작되었다."], ["It was rather honour than happiness to Fanny, for the first dance at least: her partner was in excellent spirits, and tried to impart them to her; but she was a great deal too much frightened to have any enjoyment till she could suppose herself no longer looked at.", "적어도 첫 춤 동안은 그것이 패니에게 행복이라기보다 차라리 영광이었다. 그녀의 짝은 잔뜩 신이 나서 그 신명을 그녀에게도 옮겨 주려 했으나, 그녀는 저가 더는 남의 눈에 안 띈다 여길 수 있게 되기까지는 조금의 즐거움도 누리기엔 너무 겁에 질려 있었다."], ["Young, pretty, and gentle, however, she had no awkwardnesses that were not as good as graces, and there were few persons present that were not disposed to praise her.", "그러나 젊고 예쁘고 상냥했던지라, 그녀에게는 우아함이나 진배없는 어색함밖에 없었고, 그 자리에 있는 이들 가운데 그녀를 칭찬할 마음이 없는 이는 드물었다."], ["She was attractive, she was modest, she was Sir Thomas’s niece, and she was soon said to be admired by Mr. Crawford.", "그녀는 사람을 끌었고, 겸손했으며, 토머스 경의 조카딸이었고, 게다가 이내 크로퍼드 씨가 흠모한다는 말까지 돌았다."], ["It was enough to give her general favour.", "그것이면 두루 호감을 사기에 넉넉했다."], ["Sir Thomas himself was watching her progress down the dance with much complacency; he was proud of his niece; and without attributing all her personal beauty, as Mrs.", "토머스 경 자신도 자못 흐뭇하게 그녀가 춤을 따라 내려가는 모습을 지켜보았다. 그는 제 조카딸이 자랑스러웠다."], ["Norris seemed to do, to her transplantation to Mansfield, he was pleased with himself for having supplied everything else: education and manners she owed to him.", "그리고 노리스 부인이 그러는 듯 그녀의 온갖 몸매의 아름다움을 죄다 맨스필드로 옮겨 심긴 덕으로 돌리지는 않았으되, 그 밖의 모든 것을 대 주었다는 데 스스로 흐뭇해했다. 교육도 몸가짐도 그녀는 그에게 힘입은 것이었다."]], [["Miss Crawford saw much of Sir Thomas’s thoughts as he stood, and having, in spite of all his wrongs towards her, a general prevailing desire of recommending herself to him, took an opportunity of stepping aside to say something agreeable of Fanny.", "크로퍼드 양은 서 있는 토머스 경의 속내를 많이 알아챘다. 그리고 그가 저에게 저지른 온갖 그릇됨에도 불구하고 그의 마음에 들고 싶은 마음이 두루 앞섰던지라, 슬쩍 옆으로 비켜서서 패니에 대해 무언가 흐뭇한 말을 건넬 기회를 잡았다."], ["Her praise was warm, and he received it as she could wish, joining in it as far as discretion, and politeness, and slowness of speech would allow, and certainly appearing to greater advantage on the subject than his lady did soon afterwards, when Mary, perceiving her on a sofa very near, turned round before she began to dance, to compliment her on Miss Price’s looks.", "그녀의 칭찬은 따뜻했고, 그는 그녀가 바랄 만한 대로 그것을 받아들여, 신중함과 예의와 느린 말투가 허락하는 데까지 거기에 맞장구쳤다. 그리하여 그는 이 화제에서, 이내 그의 부인이 보인 것보다 확실히 한결 나아 보였으니, 메리가 바로 곁 소파에 있는 그녀를 알아채고, 춤추기 전에 몸을 돌려 프라이스 양의 고운 모습을 두고 그녀에게 찬사를 건넸을 때 말이다."]], [["“Yes, she does look very well,”", "“그럼요, 저 애가 참 고와 보이네요.”"], ["was Lady Bertram’s placid reply.", "버트럼 부인의 잔잔한 대답이었다."], ["“Chapman helped her to dress.", "“채프먼이 저 애 옷 입는 걸 거들었어요."], ["I sent Chapman to her.”", "내가 채프먼을 저 애한테 보냈거든요.”"], ["Not but that she was really pleased to have Fanny admired; but she was so much more struck with her own kindness in sending Chapman to her, that she could not get it out of her head.", "패니가 칭찬받는 것이 정말로 기쁘지 않아서가 아니었다. 다만 채프먼을 그녀에게 보냈다는 제 친절에 어찌나 더 사무쳤던지, 그것을 도무지 머릿속에서 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었던 것이다."]], [["Miss Crawford knew Mrs. Norris too well to think of gratifying her by commendation of Fanny; to her, it was as the occasion offered—“Ah!", "크로퍼드 양은 노리스 부인을 너무 잘 알기에 패니를 칭찬하여 그녀를 흐뭇하게 할 생각은 하지 않았다."], ["ma’am, how much we want dear Mrs.", "그녀에게는 그저 기회 닿는 대로—“아!"], ["Rushworth and Julia to-night!” and Mrs.", "부인, 오늘 밤 그 소중한 러시워스 부인하고 줄리아가 얼마나 아쉬운지 몰라요!”"], ["Norris paid her with as many smiles and courteous words as she had time for, amid so much occupation as she found for herself in making up card-tables, giving hints to Sir Thomas, and trying to move all the chaperons to a better part of the room.", "하는 정도였고, 노리스 부인은 카드 탁자를 꾸리고, 토머스 경에게 훈수를 두고, 샤프롱들을 죄다 방의 더 나은 자리로 옮기려 애쓰느라 저 스스로 잔뜩 일거리를 찾아낸 그 분주함 속에서, 짬 나는 대로 미소와 정중한 말을 되도록 많이 그녀에게 갚아 주었다."]], [["Miss Crawford blundered most towards Fanny herself in her intentions to please.", "크로퍼드 양은 남을 기쁘게 하려는 뜻에서 다름 아닌 패니 자신에게 가장 크게 헛짚었다."], ["She meant to be giving her little heart a happy flutter, and filling her with sensations of delightful self-consequence; and, misinterpreting Fanny’s blushes, still thought she must be doing so when she went to her after the two first dances, and said, with a significant look, “Perhaps you can tell me why my brother goes to town to-morrow?", "그녀는 그 자그마한 마음에 행복한 두근거림을 안겨 주고, 흐뭇한 자부심으로 그녀를 채워 주려던 것이었다. 그리하여 패니의 홍조를 잘못 새겨, 처음 두 곡의 춤이 끝난 뒤 그녀에게 가서 의미심장한 눈길로 이렇게 말했을 때에도 저가 그리하고 있으려니 여전히 여겼다."], ["He says he has business there, but will not tell me what.", "“혹시 우리 오라비가 왜 내일 시내에 가는지 그쪽은 말해 줄 수 있어요? 거기 볼일이 있다면서도 그게 뭔지는 말을 안 해요."], ["The first time he ever denied me his confidence!", "나한테 속내를 안 밝힌 건 이번이 처음이라니까요!"], ["But this is what we all come to.", "하지만 우리 다 결국 이 꼴이 되는 거죠."], ["All are supplanted sooner or later.", "다들 조만간 뒷전으로 밀려나는 거예요."], ["Now, I must apply to you for information.", "자, 이제 나는 그쪽한테 알아봐야겠어요."], ["Pray, what is Henry going for?”", "부디 좀 말해 줘요, 헨리가 뭐 하러 가는 거예요?”"]], [["Fanny protested her ignorance as steadily as her embarrassment allowed.", "패니는 그 당혹감이 허락하는 한 한결같이 저는 모른다고 우겼다."]], [["“Well, then,”", "“그럼, 됐어요.”"], ["replied Miss Crawford, laughing, “I must suppose it to be purely for the pleasure of conveying your brother, and of talking of you by the way.”", "크로퍼드 양이 웃으며 대꾸했다. “그저 그쪽 오라비를 태워다 주는 즐거움 때문에, 그리고 가는 길에 그쪽 얘기를 하려는 것 때문이려니 여길밖에요.”"]], [["Fanny was confused, but it was the confusion of discontent; while Miss Crawford wondered she did not smile, and thought her over-anxious, or thought her odd, or thought her anything rather than insensible of pleasure in Henry’s attentions.", "패니는 얼떨떨했으나, 그것은 못마땅함에서 온 얼떨떨함이었다. 한편 크로퍼드 양은 그녀가 웃지 않는 것을 의아해하며, 그녀가 지나치게 조바심 낸다 여기거나, 별나다 여기거나, 아무튼 헨리의 마음씀에서 즐거움을 못 느낀다는 것 말고 다른 무엇이든으로 여겼다."], ["Fanny had a good deal of enjoyment in the course of the evening; but Henry’s attentions had very little to do with it.", "패니는 그날 저녁 내내 자못 큰 즐거움을 누렸다. 그러나 헨리의 마음씀은 그것과 거의 상관이 없었다."], ["She would much rather not have been asked by him again so very soon, and she wished she had not been obliged to suspect that his previous inquiries of Mrs.", "그녀는 그가 그토록 이내 또다시 청해 오지 않았더라면 훨씬 좋았겠다 싶었고, 저녁 식사 시각에 대해 그가 앞서 노리스 부인에게 물어본 것이 죄다 그 저녁의 그 대목에 저를 붙잡아 두기 위한 것이었으리라 의심하지 않을 수 없게 된 것이 싫었다."], ["Norris, about the supper hour, were all for the sake of securing her at that part of the evening.", "그러나 그것은 피할 길이 없었다. 그는 그녀에게 저가 온갖 것의 과녁임을 느끼게 만들었다."], ["But it was not to be avoided: he made her feel that she was the object of all; though she could not say that it was unpleasantly done, that there was indelicacy or ostentation in his manner; and sometimes, when he talked of William, he was really not unagreeable, and shewed even a warmth of heart which did him credit.", "하기야 그가 그것을 언짢게 굴었다고는, 그의 태도에 무례함이나 뽐냄이 있었다고는 그녀도 말할 수 없었다. 그리고 이따금 그가 윌리엄 얘기를 할 때면 그는 정말이지 밉지 않았고, 그의 값어치를 돋우는 따뜻한 마음마저 내비쳤다."], ["But still his attentions made no part of her satisfaction.", "그러나 그래도 그의 마음씀은 그녀의 흡족함에 아무 몫도 하지 못했다."], ["She was happy whenever she looked at William, and saw how perfectly he was enjoying himself, in every five minutes that she could walk about with him and hear his account of his partners; she was happy in knowing herself admired; and she was happy in having the two dances with Edmund still to look forward to, during the greatest part of the evening, her hand being so eagerly sought after that her indefinite engagement with him was in continual perspective.", "그녀는 윌리엄을 바라보며 그가 얼마나 온전히 즐거워하는지 볼 때마다 행복했고, 오 분씩이나마 그와 거닐며 그가 제 짝들에 대해 늘어놓는 이야기를 들을 수 있을 때마다 행복했다. 그녀는 저가 흠모받는 줄 아는 것이 행복했다."], ["She was happy even when they did take place; but not from any flow of spirits on his side, or any such expressions of tender gallantry as had blessed the morning.", "그리고 저녁의 대부분 동안 에드먼드와 출 두 곡의 춤이 아직 앞에 남아 있다는 것이 행복했으니, 그녀의 손이 하도 열심히 청해진 터라 그와의 그 정해지지 않은 약속이 내내 눈앞에 아른거렸던 것이다. 그녀는 그 두 곡이 정작 이루어질 때에도 행복했다."], ["His mind was fagged, and her happiness sprung from being the friend with whom it could find repose.", "다만 그것은 그의 편의 어떤 들뜬 신명에서도, 아침을 축복해 준 그런 다정한 정겨움의 말에서도 온 것이 아니었다. 그의 마음은 지쳐 있었고, 그녀의 행복은 그 마음이 안식을 찾을 수 있는 벗이 되어 주는 데서 우러났다."], ["“I am worn out with civility,” said he.", "“나는 예의차림에 진이 다 빠졌구나.”"], ["“I have been talking incessantly all night, and with nothing to say.", "그가 말했다. “밤새 쉴 새 없이 지껄였는데, 할 말이라곤 아무것도 없었어."], ["But with you, Fanny, there may be peace.", "하지만 너하고는, 패니, 평온이 있을 수 있지."], ["You will not want to be talked to.", "너는 말을 걸어 달라 바라지 않을 테니까."], ["Let us have the luxury of silence.”", "우리 침묵의 호사를 누리자.”"], ["Fanny would hardly even speak her agreement.", "패니는 그러자는 말조차 좀처럼 입 밖에 내지 못했다."], ["A weariness, arising probably, in great measure, from the same feelings which he had acknowledged in the morning, was peculiarly to be respected, and they went down their two dances together with such sober tranquillity as might satisfy any looker-on that Sir Thomas had been bringing up no wife for his younger son.", "아마 대체로, 그가 아침에 털어놓은 바로 그 심경에서 우러난 그 피로는 각별히 존중받을 만한 것이었다. 그리하여 둘은 함께 그 두 곡의 춤을 자못 차분한 고요 속에 추어 내려갔으니, 그 어떤 구경꾼이라도 토머스 경이 작은아들을 위해 아내를 길러 온 것이 아님을 흡족히 여길 만한 그런 고요였다."]], [["The evening had afforded Edmund little pleasure.", "그날 저녁은 에드먼드에게 별 즐거움을 주지 못했다."], ["Miss Crawford had been in gay spirits when they first danced together, but it was not her gaiety that could do him good: it rather sank than raised his comfort; and afterwards, for he found himself still impelled to seek her again, she had absolutely pained him by her manner of speaking of the profession to which he was now on the point of belonging.", "크로퍼드 양은 둘이 처음 함께 춤출 때 명랑한 신명에 젖어 있었으나, 그를 이롭게 할 수 있는 것은 그녀의 그 명랑함이 아니었다. 그것은 그의 마음을 북돋우기보다 도리어 가라앉혔다."], ["They had talked, and they had been silent; he had reasoned, she had ridiculed; and they had parted at last with mutual vexation.", "그리고 그 뒤로도, 그는 여전히 그녀를 다시 찾지 않을 수 없는 자신을 발견했으니, 그녀는 그가 이제 막 몸담으려는 그 직업을 두고 지껄이는 태도로 그를 아주 아프게 했다. 둘은 이야기했고, 둘은 잠잠했다."], ["Fanny, not able to refrain entirely from observing them, had seen enough to be tolerably satisfied.", "그는 조리 있게 따졌고, 그녀는 비웃었다. 그리하여 둘은 끝내 서로 언짢은 채로 헤어졌다."], ["It was barbarous to be happy when Edmund was suffering.", "그들을 지켜보는 것을 도무지 삼갈 수 없던 패니는, 웬만큼 흡족할 만한 것을 넉넉히 보았다."], ["Yet some happiness must and would arise from the very conviction that he did suffer.", "에드먼드가 괴로워하는데 행복해한다는 것은 야박한 짓이었다. 그러나 그가 정말로 괴로워한다는 바로 그 확신에서 어떤 행복이 일지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["When her two dances with him were over, her inclination and strength for more were pretty well at an end; and Sir Thomas, having seen her walk rather than dance down the shortening set, breathless, and with her hand at her side, gave his orders for her sitting down entirely.", "그와의 두 곡의 춤이 끝나자, 더 추고픈 마음도 기력도 거의 다한 상태였다. 그리고 토머스 경은, 짧아지는 춤 대열을 따라 그녀가 춤춘다기보다 그저 걷는 것을, 숨이 차 손을 옆구리에 댄 채 그러는 것을 보고, 아주 앉아 있으라 분부를 내렸다."], ["From that time Mr. Crawford sat down likewise.", "그때부터 크로퍼드 씨도 마찬가지로 앉았다."]], [["“Poor Fanny!”", "“가엾은 패니!”"], ["cried William, coming for a moment to visit her, and working away his partner’s fan as if for life, “how soon she is knocked up!", "잠깐 그녀를 찾아온 윌리엄이, 죽어라 제 짝의 부채를 부쳐 대며 외쳤다. “벌써 이렇게 나가떨어지다니!"], ["Why, the sport is but just begun.", "아니, 흥은 이제 막 시작인데."], ["I hope we shall keep it up these two hours.", "나야 이 두어 시간은 계속됐으면 하는데."], ["How can you be tired so soon?”", "어떻게 이리 금세 지치니?”"]], [["“So soon!", "“이리 금세라니!"], ["my good friend,”", "이 사람아,” 토머스 경이 필요한 만큼 조심스레 회중시계를 꺼내며 말했다."], ["said Sir Thomas, producing his watch with all necessary caution; “it is three o’clock, and your sister is not used to these sort of hours.”", "“세 시일세. 그리고 자네 누이는 이런 시각이 몸에 배지 않았어.”"]], [["“Well, then, Fanny, you shall not get up to-morrow before I go.", "“그럼, 됐어, 패니, 넌 내일 내가 떠나기 전에 일어나지 마."], ["Sleep as long as you can, and never mind me.”", "잘 수 있는 만큼 실컷 자고, 나는 신경 쓰지 말고.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“어머!"], ["William.”", "윌리엄.”"]], [["“What!", "“뭐라고!"], ["Did she think of being up before you set off?”", "자네 떠나기 전에 이 애가 일어날 생각을 했단 말인가?”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["yes, sir,”", "그럼요, 삼촌.”"], ["cried Fanny, rising eagerly from her seat to be nearer her uncle; “I must get up and breakfast with him.", "패니가 삼촌 가까이 가려고 냉큼 자리에서 일어나며 외쳤다. “저는 일어나서 오빠하고 아침을 먹어야 해요."], ["It will be the last time, you know; the last morning.”", "마지막인걸요, 아시잖아요. 마지막 아침이란 말이에요.”"]], [["“You had better not.", "“그러지 않는 게 좋겠다."], ["He is to have breakfasted and be gone by half-past nine.", "저 애는 아홉 시 반이면 벌써 아침을 먹고 떠날 참이야."], ["Mr. Crawford, I think you call for him at half-past nine?”", "크로퍼드 씨, 아마 자네가 아홉 시 반에 그를 데리러 오지?”"]], [["Fanny was too urgent, however, and had too many tears in her eyes for denial; and it ended in a gracious “Well, well!”", "그러나 패니가 너무 애타게 매달렸고, 눈에 눈물이 하도 그렁그렁해 마다할 수가 없었다."], ["which was permission.", "그리하여 그것은 너그러운 “그래, 그래!”로 끝났으니, 그것이 곧 허락이었다."]], [["“Yes, half-past nine,”", "“그래, 아홉 시 반일세.”"], ["said Crawford to William as the latter was leaving them, “and I shall be punctual, for there will be no kind sister to get up for me.”", "윌리엄이 그들을 떠날 참에 크로퍼드가 그에게 말했다. “나야 어김없을 걸세, 나를 위해 일어나 줄 살가운 누이 하나 없을 테니까.”"], ["And in a lower tone to Fanny, “I shall have only a desolate house to hurry from.", "그러고는 나직한 소리로 패니에게, “나야 그저 쓸쓸한 집에서 서둘러 나올 뿐이겠지요."], ["Your brother will find my ideas of time and his own very different to-morrow.”", "댁의 오라비는 내일 시각에 대한 내 생각과 제 생각이 사뭇 다름을 알게 될 겁니다.”"]], [["After a short consideration, Sir Thomas asked Crawford to join the early breakfast party in that house instead of eating alone: he should himself be of it; and the readiness with which his invitation was accepted convinced him that the suspicions whence, he must confess to himself, this very ball had in great measure sprung, were well founded. Mr.", "잠깐 궁리한 끝에, 토머스 경은 크로퍼드에게 혼자 먹지 말고 이 집의 이른 아침 식사 자리에 함께하라 청했다."], ["Crawford was in love with Fanny.", "저 자신도 거기 낄 참이었다."], ["He had a pleasing anticipation of what would be. His niece, meanwhile, did not thank him for what he had just done.", "그리고 그 초대가 선뜻 받아들여지는 것을 보고, 그는—저 자신에게 털어놓지 않을 수 없거니와—실은 바로 이 무도회가 대체로 거기서 비롯한 그 의심이 근거 있는 것이었음을 확신했다."], ["She had hoped to have William all to herself the last morning.", "크로퍼드 씨는 패니를 사랑하고 있었다. 그는 앞으로 어찌 될지를 흐뭇이 내다보았다."], ["It would have been an unspeakable indulgence.", "한편 그의 조카딸은 그가 방금 한 일을 고마워하지 않았다."], ["But though her wishes were overthrown, there was no spirit of murmuring within her.", "그녀는 마지막 아침을 온통 윌리엄만 차지하고 싶었던 것이다. 그것은 이루 말할 수 없는 호사였으련만."], ["On the contrary, she was so totally unused to have her pleasure consulted, or to have anything take place at all in the way she could desire, that she was more disposed to wonder and rejoice in having carried her point so far, than to repine at the counteraction which followed.", "그러나 제 바람이 뒤엎어졌어도 그녀 안에는 투덜대는 기색이 없었다. 오히려 제 즐거움이 헤아려지거나 제가 바랄 만한 식으로 무언가가 이루어지는 데 하도 익지 않았던지라, 그녀는 그 뒤따른 어긋남을 두고 원망하기보다, 제 뜻을 그만큼이나 관철한 것을 도리어 놀라워하고 기뻐하는 쪽이었다."]], [["Shortly afterward, Sir Thomas was again interfering a little with her inclination, by advising her to go immediately to bed. “Advise”", "이내 토머스 경은 다시 그녀더러 당장 잠자리에 들라 이르며 그녀의 마음에 얼마간 참견했다."], ["was his word, but it was the advice of absolute power, and she had only to rise, and, with Mr.", "그의 말은 “이른다”였으나, 그것은 절대 권력의 이름이었다."], ["Crawford’s very cordial adieus, pass quietly away; stopping at the entrance-door, like the Lady of Branxholm Hall, “one moment and no more,”", "그리하여 그녀는 그저 일어나, 크로퍼드 씨의 더없이 다정한 작별 인사를 받으며 조용히 물러날밖에 없었다. 브랭섬 홀의 귀부인처럼 현관문에 멈춰 서서 “한순간, 그뿐”"], ["to view the happy scene, and take a last look at the five or six determined couple who were still hard at work; and then, creeping slowly up the principal staircase, pursued by the ceaseless country-dance, feverish with hopes and fears, soup and negus, sore-footed and fatigued, restless and agitated, yet feeling, in spite of everything, that a ball was indeed delightful.", "그 행복한 광경을 바라보고, 아직 열심히 춤에 매달린 대여섯 쌍의 굳센 이들을 마지막으로 한번 눈에 담고서. 그런 다음 그칠 줄 모르는 컨트리 댄스 가락에 쫓기며 큰 계단을 천천히 기어오르니—바람과 두려움으로, 수프와 니거스로 달아오르고, 발이 아프고 고단하며, 안절부절 들뜬 채였으나, 그 모든 것에도 불구하고 무도회란 정말이지 흐뭇한 것이라 느끼면서."]], [["In thus sending her away, Sir Thomas perhaps might not be thinking merely of her health.", "그녀를 이렇게 올려 보내면서 토머스 경은 아마 그저 그녀의 건강만 생각한 것은 아니었으리라."], ["It might occur to him that Mr. Crawford had been sitting by her long enough, or he might mean to recommend her as a wife by shewing her persuadableness.", "크로퍼드 씨가 그녀 곁에 앉아 있은 지 이미 오래라는 생각이 들었을지도 모르고, 아니면 그녀가 얼마나 순순한지를 보여 줌으로써 그녀를 아내감으로 천거하려는 뜻이었는지도 모른다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXIX", "# 제29장"]], [["The ball was over, and the breakfast was soon over too; the last kiss was given, and William was gone.", "무도회가 끝났고, 아침 식사도 이내 끝났다. 마지막 입맞춤이 오갔고, 윌리엄은 떠났다."], ["Mr. Crawford had, as he foretold, been very punctual, and short and pleasant had been the meal.", "크로퍼드 씨는 제가 예고한 대로 무척 어김없었고, 식사는 짧고도 즐거웠다."]], [["After seeing William to the last moment, Fanny walked back to the breakfast-room with a very saddened heart to grieve over the melancholy change; and there her uncle kindly left her to cry in peace, conceiving, perhaps, that the deserted chair of each young man might exercise her tender enthusiasm, and that the remaining cold pork bones and mustard in William’s plate might but divide her feelings with the broken egg-shells in Mr.", "윌리엄을 마지막 순간까지 배웅한 뒤, 패니는 그 서글픈 변화를 슬퍼하려 자못 시름겨운 마음으로 아침 식사방으로 되걸어 돌아갔다. 그리고 거기서 삼촌은 친절히도 그녀를 홀로 두어 마음껏 울게 해 주었으니, 아마 두 젊은이의 저마다 비워진 의자가 그녀의 애틋한 감회를 불러일으키려니, 그리고 윌리엄의 접시에 남은 차가운 돼지고기 뼈다귀와 겨자가 크로퍼드 씨의 접시에 남은 깨진 달걀 껍데기와 그녀의 심경을 나눠 가질 따름이려니 여긴 것이리라."], ["Crawford’s. She sat and cried con amore as her uncle intended, but it was con amore fraternal and no other.", "그녀는 삼촌이 뜻한 대로 정을 담아 앉아 울었으나, 그것은 오누이의 정을 담은 울음이었을 뿐 다른 어떤 것도 아니었다."], ["William was gone, and she now felt as if she had wasted half his visit in idle cares and selfish solicitudes unconnected with him.", "윌리엄은 떠났다. 그리하여 그녀는 이제, 그와 상관없는 부질없는 근심과 이기적인 안달로 그의 방문 절반을 허비해 버린 것만 같았다."]], [["Fanny’s disposition was such that she could never even think of her aunt Norris in the meagreness and cheerlessness of her own small house, without reproaching herself for some little want of attention to her when they had been last together; much less could her feelings acquit her of having done and said and thought everything by William that was due to him for a whole fortnight.", "패니의 성미란, 노리스 이모가 제 그 옹색하고 스산한 자그마한 집에서 지내는 것을 떠올릴 때면, 지난번 함께 있을 때 조금이나마 마음을 덜 써 드린 것을 두고 스스로를 나무라지 않고는 도무지 못 배기는 그런 성미였다. 하물며 그녀의 심경으로는, 꼬박 두 주 동안 윌리엄에게 마땅히 해야 할 온갖 것을 다 하고 다 말하고 다 생각했노라 저를 놓아줄 수야 더더욱 없었다."]], [["It was a heavy, melancholy day.", "그날은 무겁고 서글픈 날이었다."], ["Soon after the second breakfast, Edmund bade them good-bye for a week, and mounted his horse for Peterborough, and then all were gone.", "두 번째 아침 식사가 끝나고 얼마 안 되어, 에드먼드가 한 주 동안의 작별을 고하고 피터버러를 향해 말에 올랐으니, 이제 다들 떠나 버렸다."], ["Nothing remained of last night but remembrances, which she had nobody to share in.", "지난밤에서 남은 것이라곤 추억뿐이었는데, 그것을 나눌 사람이 그녀에겐 아무도 없었다."], ["She talked to her aunt Bertram—she must talk to somebody of the ball; but her aunt had seen so little of what had passed, and had so little curiosity, that it was heavy work.", "그녀는 버트럼 이모와 이야기했다—무도회 얘기를 누군가와는 해야 했던 것이다. 그러나 이모는 무슨 일이 있었는지 워낙 본 것이 적고 워낙 궁금해하는 것도 없던지라, 그것은 무거운 노릇이었다."], ["Lady Bertram was not certain of anybody’s dress or anybody’s place at supper but her own.", "버트럼 부인은 저 자신의 것 말고는 누구의 옷차림도, 누구의 저녁 식사 자리도 딱히 기억하지 못했다."], ["“She could not recollect what it was that she had heard about one of the Miss Maddoxes, or what it was that Lady Prescott had noticed in Fanny: she was not sure whether Colonel Harrison had been talking of Mr.", "“그 매독스 양네 가운데 하나에 대해 무슨 얘길 들었는지도, 프레스콧 부인이 패니의 무엇을 눈여겨봤는지도 도무지 떠올릴 수가 없구나. 해리슨 대령이 방 안에서 가장 훤한 젊은이라고 한 게 크로퍼드 씨를 두고 한 말인지 윌리엄을 두고 한 말인지도 잘 모르겠고—누군가 나한테 뭐라고 소곤거렸는데, 그게 뭘 수 있을지 토머스 경께 여쭤보는 걸 깜빡했지 뭐냐.”"], ["Crawford or of William when he said he was the finest young man in the room—somebody had whispered something to her; she had forgot to ask Sir Thomas what it could be.”", "그리고 이것들이 그녀의 가장 긴 말이자 가장 또렷한 전달이었다. 나머지는 그저 맥없는 “그래, 그래."], ["And these were her longest speeches and clearest communications: the rest was only a languid “Yes, yes; very well; did you?", "아주 좋아. 그랬니?"], ["did he?", "그이가?"], ["I did not see that; I should not know one from the other.”", "나야 그건 못 봤다. 나는 이것저것 분간을 못 하겠구나”뿐이었다."], ["This was very bad. It was only better than Mrs.", "이것은 자못 딱한 노릇이었다."], ["Norris’s sharp answers would have been; but she being gone home with all the supernumerary jellies to nurse a sick maid, there was peace and good-humour in their little party, though it could not boast much beside.", "그저 노리스 부인의 그 톡 쏘는 대꾸보다 나을 따름이었다. 그러나 그 부인은 남아도는 젤리를 죄다 싸 들고 앓는 하녀를 돌보러 집에 간 터라, 이 자그마한 무리에는—달리 내세울 것은 별로 없었을망정—평온과 화기가 감돌았다."]], [["The evening was heavy like the day.", "그날 저녁도 낮처럼 무거웠다."], ["“I cannot think what is the matter with me,”", "“나는 대체 내가 왜 이러는지 모르겠구나.”"], ["said Lady Bertram, when the tea-things were removed.", "찻그릇이 치워지자 버트럼 부인이 말했다."], ["“I feel quite stupid.", "“머리가 아주 멍하다."], ["It must be sitting up so late last night.", "어젯밤 그토록 늦게까지 앉아 있어서 그런 게 틀림없어."], ["Fanny, you must do something to keep me awake.", "패니, 나 안 졸게 뭐라도 좀 해 다오."], ["I cannot work.", "나는 바느질도 못 하겠다."], ["Fetch the cards; I feel so very stupid.”", "카드를 좀 가져오렴. 머리가 하도 멍해서.”"]], [["The cards were brought, and Fanny played at cribbage with her aunt till bedtime; and as Sir Thomas was reading to himself, no sounds were heard in the room for the next two hours beyond the reckonings of the game—“And that makes thirty-one; four in hand and eight in crib.", "카드가 나왔고, 패니는 잠자리에 들 때까지 이모와 크리비지를 했다. 그리고 토머스 경은 혼자 책을 읽고 있던 터라, 그다음 두 시간 동안 그 방에서는 놀이의 셈 말고는 아무 소리도 들리지 않았다—“그래서 그게 서른하나예요."], ["You are to deal, ma’am; shall I deal for you?”", "손패에 넷, 크립에 여덟이고요. 이모가 돌리실 차례예요."], ["Fanny thought and thought again of the difference which twenty-four hours had made in that room, and all that part of the house.", "제가 대신 돌려 드릴까요?” 패니는, 스물네 시간이 그 방과 그 집의 그 온 자락에 빚어낸 그 차이를 곱씹고 또 곱씹었다."], ["Last night it had been hope and smiles, bustle and motion, noise and brilliancy, in the drawing-room, and out of the drawing-room, and everywhere.", "어젯밤엔 응접실에서도, 응접실 밖에서도, 어디에서도 온통 바람과 미소, 부산과 움직임, 소란과 눈부심이었다."], ["Now it was languor, and all but solitude.", "이제는 나른함이요, 홀로임이나 진배없었다."]], [["A good night’s rest improved her spirits.", "밤새 잘 쉬고 나니 그녀의 기운이 나아졌다."], ["She could think of William the next day more cheerfully; and as the morning afforded her an opportunity of talking over Thursday night with Mrs.", "이튿날 그녀는 윌리엄을 한결 밝게 떠올릴 수 있었다."], ["Grant and Miss Crawford, in a very handsome style, with all the heightenings of imagination, and all the laughs of playfulness which are so essential to the shade of a departed ball, she could afterwards bring her mind without much effort into its everyday state, and easily conform to the tranquillity of the present quiet week.", "그리고 그날 아침이, 떠난 무도회의 그 여운에 그토록 요긴한 온갖 상상의 부풀림과 온갖 장난스러운 웃음을 곁들여, 무척 그럴듯한 투로 목요일 밤을 두고 그랜트 부인과 크로퍼드 양과 도란도란 얘기할 기회를 마련해 준 덕에, 그녀는 그 뒤로 별 애씀 없이 제 마음을 여느 상태로 되돌리고, 지금 이 조용한 한 주의 고요에 손쉽게 순응할 수 있었다."]], [["They were indeed a smaller party than she had ever known there for a whole day together, and he was gone on whom the comfort and cheerfulness of every family meeting and every meal chiefly depended.", "그들은 정말이지, 그녀가 여태 그곳에서 온종일 함께 지내며 알던 것보다 더 단출한 무리였고, 어느 가족 모임이든 어느 끼니든 그 편안함과 화기가 주로 기대던 그이는 떠나고 없었다."], ["But this must be learned to be endured.", "그러나 이것은 견디는 법을 익혀야 할 일이었다."], ["He would soon be always gone; and she was thankful that she could now sit in the same room with her uncle, hear his voice, receive his questions, and even answer them, without such wretched feelings as she had formerly known.", "그는 머잖아 늘 떠나 있게 될 것이었다. 그리고 그녀는, 이제 삼촌과 같은 방에 앉아 그 목소리를 듣고 그 물음을 받고 심지어 그에 대답까지 하면서도, 예전에 알던 그런 비참한 심경이 들지 않는 것이 고마웠다."]], [["“We miss our two young men,”", "“우리 두 젊은이가 아쉽구려.”"], ["was Sir Thomas’s observation on both the first and second day, as they formed their very reduced circle after dinner; and in consideration of Fanny’s swimming eyes, nothing more was said on the first day than to drink their good health; but on the second it led to something farther.", "이것이 첫날에도 둘째 날에도, 만찬 뒤 자못 단출해진 무리를 이룰 때 토머스 경이 한 소감이었다. 그리고 패니의 그렁그렁한 눈을 헤아려, 첫날엔 그저 그들의 건강을 비는 것 말고는 더 아무 말도 안 나왔다."], ["William was kindly commended and his promotion hoped for.", "그러나 둘째 날엔 그것이 무언가 더한 데로 이어졌다."], ["“And there is no reason to suppose,”", "윌리엄이 다정하게 칭찬받고, 그 진급이 빌어졌다."], ["added Sir Thomas, “but that his visits to us may now be tolerably frequent.", "“그리고 이제 그가 우리를 찾아오는 것이 웬만큼 잦아지리라 여겨도,”"], ["As to Edmund, we must learn to do without him.", "토머스 경이 덧붙였다. “그르다 할 까닭이 없소."], ["This will be the last winter of his belonging to us, as he has done.”", "에드먼드로 말하면, 우리는 그 없이 지내는 법을 익혀야 하오. 여태 그래 온 것처럼 그가 우리 식구로 있는 것은 이번이 마지막 겨울이 될 테니까.”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“그래요,”"], ["said Lady Bertram, “but I wish he was not going away.", "버트럼 부인이 말했다. “하지만 나는 그 애가 떠나지 않았으면 좋겠어요."], ["They are all going away, I think.", "다들 떠나는 것 같아요."], ["I wish they would stay at home.”", "다들 집에 있어 주면 좋겠는데.”"]], [["This wish was levelled principally at Julia, who had just applied for permission to go to town with Maria; and as Sir Thomas thought it best for each daughter that the permission should be granted, Lady Bertram, though in her own good-nature she would not have prevented it, was lamenting the change it made in the prospect of Julia’s return, which would otherwise have taken place about this time.", "이 바람은 주로 줄리아를 겨냥한 것이었으니, 그녀가 방금 마리아와 함께 시내에 가게 해 달라 청한 참이었던 것이다. 그리고 토머스 경은 그 허락을 내리는 것이 두 딸 저마다에게 가장 낫다 여겼던지라, 버트럼 부인은 제 그 착한 성미로야 그것을 막지는 않았을망정, 안 그랬으면 이맘때쯤 이루어졌을 줄리아의 귀가 전망에 그것이 빚어낸 그 변화를 서운해하고 있었다."], ["A great deal of good sense followed on Sir Thomas’s side, tending to reconcile his wife to the arrangement.", "토머스 경의 편에서 자못 이치에 맞는 말이 뒤따라, 그 채비에 아내를 수긍시키려 했다. 사려 깊은 어버이라면 마땅히 느껴야 할 온갖 것이 그녀가 쓰라고 내밀어졌고, 정 많은 어머니라면 자식의 즐거움을 북돋우며 반드시 느낄 온갖 것이 그녀의 천성으로 돌려졌다."], ["Everything that a considerate parent ought to feel was advanced for her use; and everything that an affectionate mother must feel in promoting her children’s enjoyment was attributed to her nature.", "버트럼 부인은 잔잔한 “그래요”로 그 모두에 수긍했다. 그리고 십오 분간 말없이 곰곰 생각한 끝에 스스로 이렇게 뇌었다."], ["Lady Bertram agreed to it all with a calm “Yes”; and at the end of a quarter of an hour’s silent consideration spontaneously observed, “Sir Thomas, I have been thinking—and I am very glad we took Fanny as we did, for now the others are away we feel the good of it.”", "“토머스 경, 나는 곰곰 생각해 봤는데—우리가 패니를 그렇게 데려온 게 참 잘한 일이다 싶어요. 이제 다른 애들이 다 떠나고 나니 그 덕을 느끼거든요.”"]], [["Sir Thomas immediately improved this compliment by adding, “Very true.", "토머스 경은 이 칭찬을 대번에 이렇게 덧붙여 돋우었다. “참으로 맞는 말이오."], ["We shew Fanny what a good girl we think her by praising her to her face, she is now a very valuable companion.", "우리가 패니를 얼마나 착한 아이로 여기는지 그 애 앞에서 칭찬해 보여 준들, 그 애는 이제 자못 값진 벗이니 말이오."], ["If we have been kind to her, she is now quite as necessary to us.”", "우리가 그 애에게 친절을 베풀었다면, 이제 그 애는 우리에게 그에 못지않게 없어선 안 될 사람이오.”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“그래요.”"], ["said Lady Bertram presently; “and it is a comfort to think that we shall always have her.”", "이내 버트럼 부인이 말했다. “그리고 우리가 언제까지나 그 애를 곁에 둘 거라 생각하면 마음이 놓여요.”"]], [["Sir Thomas paused, half smiled, glanced at his niece, and then gravely replied, “She will never leave us, I hope, till invited to some other home that may reasonably promise her greater happiness than she knows here.”", "토머스 경은 잠깐 뜸을 들이더니, 반쯤 미소 짓고, 조카딸을 슬쩍 보고는, 이내 근엄하게 대답했다. “그 애가 여기서 아는 것보다 응당 더 큰 행복을 약속할 만한 다른 집으로 청함받기 전에는, 그 애가 우리를 떠나는 일이 결코 없기를 나는 바라오.”"]], [["“And that is not very likely to be, Sir Thomas.", "“그리고 그럴 성싶지도 않은걸요, 토머스 경. 누가 그 애를 청하겠어요?"], ["Who should invite her?", "마리아야 이따금 소더턴에서 그 애를 반가이 볼지 몰라도, 거기 와서 살라고 할 생각이야 안 할 거예요."], ["Maria might be very glad to see her at Sotherton now and then, but she would not think of asking her to live there; and I am sure she is better off here; and besides, I cannot do without her.”", "그리고 그 애는 여기가 더 나은 게 분명하고요. 게다가 나는 그 애 없인 못 지내는걸요.”"]], [["The week which passed so quietly and peaceably at the great house in Mansfield had a very different character at the Parsonage.", "맨스필드의 큰 저택에서는 그토록 조용하고 평온하게 지나간 그 한 주가, 목사관에서는 자못 다른 빛깔을 띠었다."], ["To the young lady, at least, in each family, it brought very different feelings.", "적어도 두 집안 저마다의 젊은 아가씨에게는, 그것이 자못 다른 심경을 안겨 주었다."], ["What was tranquillity and comfort to Fanny was tediousness and vexation to Mary.", "패니에게는 고요와 편안함이던 것이, 메리에게는 지루함과 언짢음이었다."], ["Something arose from difference of disposition and habit: one so easily satisfied, the other so unused to endure; but still more might be imputed to difference of circumstances.", "얼마간은 성미와 습성의 다름에서 비롯한 것이었다—한쪽은 그토록 쉬 흡족해하고, 다른 쪽은 견디는 데 그토록 익지 않았으니 말이다. 그러나 그보다 더 크게는 처지의 다름에 돌릴 만한 것이었다."], ["In some points of interest they were exactly opposed to each other.", "몇몇 관심사에서 둘은 딱 정반대로 맞서 있었다."], ["To Fanny’s mind, Edmund’s absence was really, in its cause and its tendency, a relief.", "패니의 마음으로는, 에드먼드의 부재가 그 까닭에서나 그 결에서나 정말이지 안도였다."], ["To Mary it was every way painful.", "메리에게 그것은 어느 모로 보나 괴로움이었다."], ["She felt the want of his society every day, almost every hour, and was too much in want of it to derive anything but irritation from considering the object for which he went.", "그녀는 날마다, 거의 매시간 그의 벗함이 아쉬웠고, 그것이 하도 아쉬웠던지라 그가 떠난 목적을 헤아려 보아야 짜증밖에는 아무것도 얻지 못했다."], ["He could not have devised anything more likely to raise his consequence than this week’s absence, occurring as it did at the very time of her brother’s going away, of William Price’s going too, and completing the sort of general break-up of a party which had been so animated. She felt it keenly.", "그가 제 값어치를 돋우기에 이보다 더 그럴듯한 것은 궁리해 낼 수 없었으리라—바로 그녀의 오라비가 떠나고 윌리엄 프라이스도 떠나던 바로 그때에 겹쳐 일어나, 그토록 신명 나던 무리의 그 두루 흩어짐 같은 것을 채워 완성한 이번 주의 부재 말이다."], ["They were now a miserable trio, confined within doors by a series of rain and snow, with nothing to do and no variety to hope for.", "그녀는 그것을 뼈저리게 느꼈다. 그들은 이제, 잇단 비와 눈에 집 안에 갇혀, 할 일도 없고 바랄 만한 변화도 없는 비참한 세 사람이었다."], ["Angry as she was with Edmund for adhering to his own notions, and acting on them in defiance of her (and she had been so angry that they had hardly parted friends at the ball), she could not help thinking of him continually when absent, dwelling on his merit and affection, and longing again for the almost daily meetings they lately had.", "제 생각을 고집하며 그녀를 거슬러 그대로 밀어붙인 에드먼드에게 화가 났으면서도(그녀는 어찌나 화가 났던지 무도회에서 둘은 벗으로서 헤어졌다고도 할 수 없을 지경이었다), 그녀는 그가 없는 동안 끊임없이 그를 떠올리며, 그의 미덕과 애정에 마음이 머물고, 요즈음 거의 날마다 나누던 그 만남을 다시금 그리워하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["His absence was unnecessarily long.", "그의 부재는 쓸데없이 길었다."], ["He should not have planned such an absence—he should not have left home for a week, when her own departure from Mansfield was so near.", "그는 그런 부재를 계획해서는 안 되는 것이었다—제가 맨스필드를 떠날 날이 그토록 가까운 마당에, 한 주씩이나 집을 비워서는 안 되는 것이었다."], ["Then she began to blame herself.", "그러다 그녀는 저 자신을 나무라기 시작했다."], ["She wished she had not spoken so warmly in their last conversation.", "지난번 대화에서 그토록 발끈하며 말하지 말았어야 했다 싶었다."], ["She was afraid she had used some strong, some contemptuous expressions in speaking of the clergy, and that should not have been.", "성직자를 두고 말하면서 자기가 무슨 모진, 무슨 업신여기는 표현을 쓰지나 않았을까 두려웠고, 그건 그러지 말았어야 했다."], ["It was ill-bred; it was wrong.", "그건 무례한 짓이었고, 그른 짓이었다."], ["She wished such words unsaid with all her heart.", "그녀는 그런 말을 안 한 것이었으면 하고 진심으로 바랐다."]], [["Her vexation did not end with the week.", "그녀의 언짢음은 그 한 주로 끝나지 않았다."], ["All this was bad, but she had still more to feel when Friday came round again and brought no Edmund; when Saturday came and still no Edmund; and when, through the slight communication with the other family which Sunday produced, she learned that he had actually written home to defer his return, having promised to remain some days longer with his friend.", "이 모두가 나빴으나, 금요일이 다시 돌아오도록 에드먼드가 안 오고, 토요일이 오도록 여전히 에드먼드가 없고, 일요일이 빚어낸 그 다른 집안과의 짧은 왕래를 통해, 그가 그 벗과 며칠 더 머물기로 약속하여 실제로 집에 편지를 써 귀가를 미뤘음을 알게 되니, 그녀는 더한 것을 느껴야 했다."]], [["If she had felt impatience and regret before—if she had been sorry for what she said, and feared its too strong effect on him—she now felt and feared it all tenfold more.", "앞서 조바심과 후회를 느꼈다면—제가 한 말을 뉘우치고 그것이 그에게 너무 세게 미쳤을까 두려워했다면—그녀는 이제 그 모두를 열 곱절 더 느끼고 두려워했다."], ["She had, moreover, to contend with one disagreeable emotion entirely new to her—jealousy. His friend Mr.", "게다가 그녀는, 저에게 아주 낯선 언짢은 감정 하나와도 씨름해야 했으니—바로 질투였다."], ["Owen had sisters; he might find them attractive.", "그의 벗 오언 씨에게는 누이들이 있었다."], ["But, at any rate, his staying away at a time when, according to all preceding plans, she was to remove to London, meant something that she could not bear.", "그가 그들을 매력적으로 여길지도 몰랐다. 그러나 아무튼, 앞선 온갖 계획대로라면 제가 런던으로 옮겨 갈 바로 그때에 그가 이렇게 떨어져 있다는 것은, 그녀로선 견딜 수 없는 무언가를 뜻했다."], ["Had Henry returned, as he talked of doing, at the end of three or four days, she should now have been leaving Mansfield.", "헨리가 제 말대로 사나흘 만에 돌아왔더라면, 그녀는 이제 맨스필드를 떠나고 있었을 터였다."], ["It became absolutely necessary for her to get to Fanny and try to learn something more.", "그녀로선 어떻게든 패니에게 가서 무언가 더 알아보려 애쓰는 것이 아주 절실해졌다."], ["She could not live any longer in such solitary wretchedness; and she made her way to the Park, through difficulties of walking which she had deemed unconquerable a week before, for the chance of hearing a little in addition, for the sake of at least hearing his name.", "이런 외로운 비참함 속에 더는 살 수가 없었던 것이다. 그리하여 그녀는, 한 주 전만 해도 도저히 못 이기리라 여겼던 걷기의 온갖 험한 고생을 무릅쓰고 저택으로 향했으니, 무언가 조금이라도 더 들을 요행을 위해, 적어도 그의 이름이나마 들어 보기 위해서였다."]], [["The first half-hour was lost, for Fanny and Lady Bertram were together, and unless she had Fanny to herself she could hope for nothing.", "첫 반 시간은 허비되었다. 패니와 버트럼 부인이 함께 있었고, 패니를 저 혼자 차지하지 못하는 한 아무것도 바랄 수 없었던 것이다."], ["But at last Lady Bertram left the room, and then almost immediately Miss Crawford thus began, with a voice as well regulated as she could—“And how do you like your cousin Edmund’s staying away so long?", "그러나 마침내 버트럼 부인이 방을 나가니, 이내 크로퍼드 양이 되도록 가다듬은 목소리로 이렇게 말문을 열었다—“그래, 그쪽은 사촌 에드먼드가 이렇게 오래 떨어져 있는 게 어때요?"], ["Being the only young person at home, I consider you as the greatest sufferer.", "집에 있는 젊은 사람이라곤 그쪽뿐이니, 나는 그쪽이 가장 괴로운 사람이려니 여겨요."], ["You must miss him.", "그이가 아쉽겠죠."], ["Does his staying longer surprise you?”", "그이가 더 오래 머무는 게 놀랍진 않아요?”"]], [["“I do not know,”", "“모르겠어요.”"], ["said Fanny hesitatingly.", "패니가 머뭇거리며 말했다."], ["“Yes; I had not particularly expected it.”", "“네, 딱히 그럴 줄은 몰랐어요.”"]], [["“Perhaps he will always stay longer than he talks of.", "“어쩌면 그이는 늘 말한 것보다 더 오래 머무는지도 몰라요."], ["It is the general way all young men do.”", "젊은 남자들이야 다들 그러는 게 예사니까요.”"]], [["“He did not, the only time he went to see Mr. Owen before.”", "“전에 오언 씨를 뵈러 갔던 그 한 번은 안 그러셨는걸요.”"]], [["“He finds the house more agreeable now.", "“지금은 그 집이 더 마음에 드는 거죠."], ["He is a very—a very pleasing young man himself, and I cannot help being rather concerned at not seeing him again before I go to London, as will now undoubtedly be the case.", "그이 자신도 무척—무척 서글서글한 젊은이잖아요. 그러니 내가 런던에 가기 전에—이제 틀림없이 그리될 텐데—그이를 다시 못 보게 되는 게 나야 자못 서운하지 않을 수 없어요."], ["I am looking for Henry every day, and as soon as he comes there will be nothing to detain me at Mansfield.", "나는 날마다 헨리를 기다리는데, 그이가 오는 대로 나를 맨스필드에 붙잡아 둘 건 아무것도 없거든요."], ["I should like to have seen him once more, I confess.", "솔직히 그이를 한 번 더 봤으면 싶어요."], ["But you must give my compliments to him.", "하지만 그쪽이 그이한테 내 안부를 전해 줘요."], ["Yes; I think it must be compliments.", "그래요, 아무래도 안부라야겠죠."], ["Is not there a something wanted, Miss Price, in our language—a something between compliments and—and love—to suit the sort of friendly acquaintance we have had together?", "프라이스 양, 우리말에는 뭔가 아쉬운 게 있지 않아요—우리가 함께 나눈 이런 정겨운 사귐에 딱 맞는, 안부와—그리고—사랑 사이의 어떤 것 말이에요?"], ["So many months’ acquaintance!", "그 여러 달의 사귐이잖아요!"], ["But compliments may be sufficient here.", "하지만 여기선 안부면 넉넉하겠죠."], ["Was his letter a long one?", "그이 편지가 길었어요?"], ["Does he give you much account of what he is doing?", "자기가 뭘 하고 지내는지 많이 적어 주던가요?"], ["Is it Christmas gaieties that he is staying for?”", "크리스마스 흥청거림 때문에 머무는 거예요?”"]], [["“I only heard a part of the letter; it was to my uncle; but I believe it was very short; indeed I am sure it was but a few lines.", "“저는 편지의 한 대목만 들었어요. 삼촌한테 온 거였거든요."], ["All that I heard was that his friend had pressed him to stay longer, and that he had agreed to do so.", "하지만 무척 짧았지 싶어요. 정말이지 몇 줄뿐이었던 게 분명해요."], ["A few days longer, or some days longer; I am not quite sure which.”", "제가 들은 거라곤 벗이 더 머물라 졸랐고 그러기로 하셨다는 것뿐이에요. 며칠 더인지, 얼마간 더인지, 어느 쪽인지는 잘 모르겠고요.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["if he wrote to his father; but I thought it might have been to Lady Bertram or you.", "아버지한테 쓴 거라면. 나는 버트럼 부인이나 그쪽한테 온 건가 했는데."], ["But if he wrote to his father, no wonder he was concise.", "하지만 아버지한테 쓴 거라면 짧은 것도 이상할 게 없죠."], ["Who could write chat to Sir Thomas?", "누가 토머스 경한테 수다를 적어 보내겠어요?"], ["If he had written to you, there would have been more particulars.", "그쪽한테 썼다면 시시콜콜한 게 더 많았을 텐데."], ["You would have heard of balls and parties.", "무도회며 모임 얘기를 들었겠죠."], ["He would have sent you a description of everything and everybody.", "그이가 온갖 것과 온갖 사람에 대한 묘사를 그쪽한테 적어 보냈을 거예요."], ["How many Miss Owens are there?”", "오언 양이 몇이나 돼요?”"]], [["“Three grown up.”", "“다 큰 게 셋이에요.”"]], [["“Are they musical?”", "“음악을 하나요?”"]], [["“I do not at all know.", "“도무지 몰라요."], ["I never heard.”", "들어 본 적이 없어서요.”"]], [["“That is the first question, you know,”", "“그게 첫 물음이잖아요.” 크로퍼드 양이 명랑하고 아무렇지 않은 척하며 말했다."], ["said Miss Crawford, trying to appear gay and unconcerned, “which every woman who plays herself is sure to ask about another.", "“저 스스로 연주하는 여자라면 누구나 남을 두고 꼭 묻고 마는 첫 물음 말이에요. 하지만 어떤 아가씨들이든—이제 막 다 큰 세 자매든 두고 이러쿵저러쿵 묻는 건 참으로 어리석은 짓이죠."], ["But it is very foolish to ask questions about any young ladies—about any three sisters just grown up; for one knows, without being told, exactly what they are: all very accomplished and pleasing, and one very pretty.", "굳이 안 들어도 그들이 어떤지 딱 아니까요. 죄다 무척 교양 있고 상냥하고, 하나는 무척 예쁘죠."], ["There is a beauty in every family; it is a regular thing.", "어느 집안에나 미인이 하나씩 있잖아요. 그게 으레 그런 거예요."], ["Two play on the pianoforte, and one on the harp; and all sing, or would sing if they were taught, or sing all the better for not being taught; or something like it.”", "둘은 피아노포르테를 치고 하나는 하프를 켜죠. 그리고 다들 노래하거나, 배웠으면 노래했거나, 안 배운 덕에 오히려 더 잘 노래하거나—뭐 그 비슷한 거고요.”"]], [["“I know nothing of the Miss Owens,”", "“저는 오언 양네에 대해선 아무것도 몰라요.”"], ["said Fanny calmly.", "패니가 차분히 말했다."]], [["“You know nothing and you care less, as people say.", "“아무것도 모르고, 사람들 말마따나 관심은 그보다 더 없다는 거네요."], ["Never did tone express indifference plainer.", "무심함을 이보다 더 또렷이 드러낸 말투도 없었어요."], ["Indeed, how can one care for those one has never seen?", "하기야 한 번도 못 본 이를 어찌 마음 쓰겠어요?"], ["Well, when your cousin comes back, he will find Mansfield very quiet; all the noisy ones gone, your brother and mine and myself.", "자, 그쪽 사촌이 돌아오면 맨스필드가 무척 조용한 걸 알게 될 거예요. 그 시끄러운 이들이 죄다 떠났으니까요, 그쪽 오라비하고 내 오라비하고 나 말이에요."], ["I do not like the idea of leaving Mrs. Grant now the time draws near.", "나는 이제 때가 다가오니 그랜트 부인을 두고 떠나는 게 싫어요."], ["She does not like my going.”", "그이도 내가 가는 걸 안 좋아하고요.”"]], [["Fanny felt obliged to speak.", "패니는 무언가 말해야 할 것 같았다."], ["“You cannot doubt your being missed by many,” said she.", "“여러 사람이 크로퍼드 양을 아쉬워하리란 건 의심할 여지가 없어요.”"], ["“You will be very much missed.”", "그녀가 말했다. “무척 아쉬워할 거예요.”"]], [["Miss Crawford turned her eye on her, as if wanting to hear or see more, and then laughingly said, “Oh yes!", "크로퍼드 양은, 더 듣거나 보고 싶은 듯 그녀에게 눈길을 돌리더니, 이내 웃으며 말했다. “아 그럼요!"], ["missed as every noisy evil is missed when it is taken away; that is, there is a great difference felt.", "시끄러운 골칫거리가 하나 없어질 때 아쉬워하는 그런 식으로 아쉬워하겠죠. 그러니까, 크게 달라진 게 느껴진다는 거예요."], ["But I am not fishing; don’t compliment me.", "하지만 나는 미끼를 던지는 게 아니에요. 나한테 빈말은 마요."], ["If I am missed, it will appear.", "내가 정말 아쉬워지면 그야 드러날 테니까요."], ["I may be discovered by those who want to see me.", "나야 나를 보고 싶어 하는 이들이 찾아낼 수 있어요."], ["I shall not be in any doubtful, or distant, or unapproachable region.”", "나는 무슨 미덥잖거나 멀거나 다가갈 수 없는 데에 있진 않을 테니까요.”"]], [["Now Fanny could not bring herself to speak, and Miss Crawford was disappointed; for she had hoped to hear some pleasant assurance of her power from one who she thought must know, and her spirits were clouded again.", "이제 패니는 도무지 말을 꺼낼 수가 없었고, 크로퍼드 양은 실망했다. 응당 알 만한 사람이라 여긴 이에게서 제 힘에 대한 무슨 흐뭇한 다짐을 듣기를 바랐던 것인데, 그녀의 기운은 다시금 흐려졌다."]], [["“The Miss Owens,”", "“그 오언 양네 말인데요,”"], ["said she, soon afterwards; “suppose you were to have one of the Miss Owens settled at Thornton Lacey; how should you like it?", "이내 그녀가 말했다. “그쪽, 만일 그 오언 양 가운데 하나가 손턴 레이시에 자리 잡게 된다면, 어떨 것 같아요?"], ["Stranger things have happened.", "더한 일도 있어 왔잖아요."], ["I dare say they are trying for it.", "나야 그들이 그걸 노리고 있지 싶어요."], ["And they are quite in the right, for it would be a very pretty establishment for them.", "그리고 그들이야 아주 옳죠, 그들한텐 자못 그럴듯한 자리 잡음일 테니까요."], ["I do not at all wonder or blame them.", "나는 그들을 조금도 의아해하거나 나무라지 않아요."], ["It is everybody’s duty to do as well for themselves as they can.", "저마다 제 형편을 되도록 잘 챙기는 게 누구에게나 도리니까요."], ["Sir Thomas Bertram’s son is somebody; and now he is in their own line.", "토머스 버트럼 경의 아들이면 어엿한 사람이고, 게다가 이제 그이가 그들과 같은 길에 들어섰죠."], ["Their father is a clergyman, and their brother is a clergyman, and they are all clergymen together.", "그들 아버지도 성직자, 오라비도 성직자, 다 함께 성직자니까요. 그이는 그들의 정당한 소유물이에요."], ["He is their lawful property; he fairly belongs to them.", "마땅히 그들 몫인 거죠. 말이 없으시네요, 패니."], ["You don’t speak, Fanny; Miss Price, you don’t speak.", "프라이스 양, 아무 말이 없어요."], ["But honestly now, do not you rather expect it than otherwise?”", "하지만 이제 솔직히, 오히려 그리되리라 짐작하는 편 아니에요?”"]], [["“No,”", "“아뇨.”"], ["said Fanny stoutly, “I do not expect it at all.”", "패니가 야무지게 말했다. “저는 조금도 그리 짐작 안 해요.”"]], [["“Not at all!”", "“조금도라니!”"], ["cried Miss Crawford with alacrity.", "크로퍼드 양이 냉큼 외쳤다."], ["“I wonder at that.", "“그것참 뜻밖이네."], ["But I dare say you know exactly—I always imagine you are—perhaps you do not think him likely to marry at all—or not at present.”", "하지만 나야 그쪽이 딱 알고 있으려니—나는 늘 그쪽이라면—어쩌면 그쪽은 그이가 아예 혼인할 것 같지도 않다고 여기나 봐요—아니면 적어도 지금은요.”"]], [["“No, I do not,”", "“네, 안 그럴 것 같아요.”"], ["said Fanny softly, hoping she did not err either in the belief or the acknowledgment of it.", "패니가, 그 믿음에서든 그것을 인정하는 데서든 저가 그르지 않기를 바라며 나직이 말했다."]], [["Her companion looked at her keenly; and gathering greater spirit from the blush soon produced from such a look, only said, “He is best off as he is,”", "그녀의 벗은 그녀를 뚫어져라 바라보았다. 그리고 그런 눈길에 이내 어린 홍조에서 한결 큰 기운을 얻어, 그저 “지금 그대로가 그이한텐 가장 좋죠”"], ["and turned the subject.", "하고는 화제를 돌렸다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXX", "# 제30장"]], [["Miss Crawford’s uneasiness was much lightened by this conversation, and she walked home again in spirits which might have defied almost another week of the same small party in the same bad weather, had they been put to the proof; but as that very evening brought her brother down from London again in quite, or more than quite, his usual cheerfulness, she had nothing farther to try her own.", "크로퍼드 양의 불안은 이 대화로 자못 가벼워졌다. 그리하여 그녀는, 만일 시험해 봤더라면 이 궂은 날씨 속에서 이 단출한 무리와 또 한 주쯤 더 지내는 것도 너끈히 버텨 냈을 만한 기운으로 다시 집으로 걸어갔다."], ["His still refusing to tell her what he had gone for was but the promotion of gaiety; a day before it might have irritated, but now it was a pleasant joke—suspected only of concealing something planned as a pleasant surprise to herself.", "그러나 바로 그날 저녁 그 오라비가 런던에서, 여느 때 못지않은—아니, 여느 때 이상의 명랑함을 안고 내려온 터라, 그녀는 제 기운을 더 시험할 일이 없었다. 그가 무엇 하러 갔었는지 여전히 안 밝히는 것도 그저 그 명랑함을 돋울 뿐이었다."], ["And the next day did bring a surprise to her.", "하루 전이라면 짜증스러웠을 테지만, 이제 그것은 유쾌한 농이었다—저 자신을 위한 유쾌한 놀람거리로 짜 둔 무언가를 감추고 있으려니 짐작되었을 뿐이니 말이다. 그리고 이튿날 그것이 정말로 그녀에게 놀람을 안겨 주었다."], ["Henry had said he should just go and ask the Bertrams how they did, and be back in ten minutes, but he was gone above an hour; and when his sister, who had been waiting for him to walk with her in the garden, met him at last most impatiently in the sweep, and cried out, “My dear Henry, where can you have been all this time?”", "헨리는 그저 버트럼가에 들러 안부나 여쭙고 십 분 만에 돌아오겠노라 했건만, 한 시간이 넘도록 돌아오지 않았다. 그리고 함께 정원을 거닐려 그를 기다리던 누이가 마침내 진입로에서 못내 애타게 그를 맞으며 “사랑하는 오빠, 이 오래도록 대체 어디 있었던 거야?”"], ["he had only to say that he had been sitting with Lady Bertram and Fanny.", "하고 외치니, 그는 그저 버트럼 부인과 패니와 함께 앉아 있었노라 말할밖에 없었다."]], [["“Sitting with them an hour and a half!”", "“그이들이랑 한 시간 반이나 앉아 있었다고!”"], ["exclaimed Mary.", "메리가 외쳤다."]], [["But this was only the beginning of her surprise.", "그러나 이것은 그녀 놀람의 시작일 뿐이었다."]], [["“Yes, Mary,”", "“그래, 메리.”"], ["said he, drawing her arm within his, and walking along the sweep as if not knowing where he was: “I could not get away sooner; Fanny looked so lovely!", "그가 누이의 팔을 제 팔에 끼고, 마치 제가 어디 있는지도 모르는 양 진입로를 따라 걸으며 말했다. “나야 더 일찍은 못 떠났어."], ["I am quite determined, Mary.", "패니가 어찌나 사랑스러워 보이던지!"], ["My mind is entirely made up.", "나는 아주 마음을 정했어, 메리."], ["Will it astonish you?", "내 결심은 온전히 굳었다니까."], ["No: you must be aware that I am quite determined to marry Fanny Price.”", "놀랄 테지? 아니지, 넌 알 만도 해, 내가 패니 프라이스와 혼인하기로 아주 마음을 굳혔다는 걸.”"]], [["The surprise was now complete; for, in spite of whatever his consciousness might suggest, a suspicion of his having any such views had never entered his sister’s imagination; and she looked so truly the astonishment she felt, that he was obliged to repeat what he had said, and more fully and more solemnly.", "이제 놀람은 온전해졌다. 그의 마음속에서야 무엇을 넌지시 일러 주었든 간에, 그에게 그런 뜻이 있으리라는 짐작은 그 누이의 상상에 한 번도 든 적이 없었던 것이다."], ["The conviction of his determination once admitted, it was not unwelcome.", "그리하여 그녀가 느끼는 그 놀라움을 어찌나 참으로 고스란히 낯빛에 드러냈던지, 그는 하는 수 없이 제가 한 말을 더 낱낱이, 더 엄숙하게 다시 해야 했다."], ["There was even pleasure with the surprise.", "그의 결심이 참됨이 일단 받아들여지자, 그것은 달갑잖은 것이 아니었다."], ["Mary was in a state of mind to rejoice in a connexion with the Bertram family, and to be not displeased with her brother’s marrying a little beneath him.", "그 놀라움에는 즐거움마저 섞여 있었다. 메리는 버트럼 집안과 이어지는 것을 반길 만한 심경이었고, 오라비가 저보다 조금 낮은 데로 장가드는 것을 언짢아하지 않을 심경이었다."]], [["“Yes, Mary,”", "“그래, 메리.”"], ["was Henry’s concluding assurance.", "헨리의 마무리 다짐이었다."], ["“I am fairly caught.", "“나는 아주 사로잡혔어."], ["You know with what idle designs I began; but this is the end of them.", "넌 내가 어떤 한가한 심보로 시작했는지 알잖아. 하지만 이게 그 끝이야."], ["I have, I flatter myself, made no inconsiderable progress in her affections; but my own are entirely fixed.”", "나는, 스스로 좋게 봐주자면, 그 애 마음속에 제법 적잖은 진척을 이뤘어. 그리고 내 마음은 온전히 굳었고.”"]], [["“Lucky, lucky girl!”", "“운 좋은, 운 좋은 애로구나!”"], ["cried Mary, as soon as she could speak; “what a match for her!", "겨우 말이 나오자마자 메리가 외쳤다. “그 애한테 이 얼마나 대단한 짝인지!"], ["My dearest Henry, this must be my first feeling; but my second, which you shall have as sincerely, is, that I approve your choice from my soul, and foresee your happiness as heartily as I wish and desire it.", "사랑하는 오빠, 이게 내 첫 심정이겠지. 하지만 못지않게 진심으로 들려줄 내 둘째 심정은, 나는 온 넋으로 오빠의 선택을 반기고, 내가 바라고 비는 만큼이나 진심으로 오빠의 행복을 내다본다는 거야."], ["You will have a sweet little wife; all gratitude and devotion.", "오빠는 곱고 자그마한 아내를 얻을 거야, 온통 고마움과 정성뿐인 아내를."], ["Exactly what you deserve.", "딱 오빠가 받을 만한 사람이지."], ["What an amazing match for her! Mrs.", "그 애한테는 이 얼마나 놀라운 짝인지!"], ["Norris often talks of her luck; what will she say now?", "노리스 부인이 툭하면 그 애 운수 얘기를 하던데, 이제 뭐라 할까?"], ["The delight of all the family, indeed!", "정말이지 온 집안의 기쁨이겠지!"], ["And she has some true friends in it!", "게다가 그 집엔 그 애한테 참된 벗들도 좀 있잖아!"], ["How they will rejoice!", "그들이 얼마나 기뻐할까!"], ["But tell me all about it!", "하지만 죄다 얘기해 줘!"], ["Talk to me for ever.", "나한테 끝없이 말해 줘."], ["When did you begin to think seriously about her?”", "언제부터 그 애를 진지하게 생각하기 시작한 거야?”"]], [["Nothing could be more impossible than to answer such a question, though nothing could be more agreeable than to have it asked.", "이런 물음에 대답하는 것만큼 못 할 노릇도 없었으나, 이런 물음을 받는 것만큼 흐뭇한 일도 없었다. “그 달콤한 홍역이 어떻게 제게 스며들었는지”"], ["“How the pleasing plague had stolen on him”", "그는 말할 수 없었다."], ["he could not say; and before he had expressed the same sentiment with a little variation of words three times over, his sister eagerly interrupted him with, “Ah, my dear Henry, and this is what took you to London!", "그리하여 그가 같은 심정을 말만 조금씩 바꿔 세 번이나 되뇌기도 전에, 누이가 못내 애타게 그를 가로막았다. “아, 사랑하는 오빠, 그러니까 이게 오빠를 런던에 가게 한 거구나!"], ["This was your business!", "이게 오빠 볼일이었어!"], ["You chose to consult the Admiral before you made up your mind.”", "마음을 굳히기 전에 제독하고 의논하려 했던 거지.”"]], [["But this he stoutly denied.", "그러나 이것을 그는 딱 잘라 부인했다."], ["He knew his uncle too well to consult him on any matrimonial scheme.", "그는 제 삼촌을 너무 잘 알기에 어떤 혼사 문제로든 그와 의논할 사람이 아니었다."], ["The Admiral hated marriage, and thought it never pardonable in a young man of independent fortune.", "제독은 혼인을 몹시 싫어했고, 재산이 넉넉한 젊은이가 혼인하는 것은 결코 용서할 수 없는 일이라 여겼다."]], [["“When Fanny is known to him,”", "“패니를 알게 되면,” 헨리가 말을 이었다."], ["continued Henry, “he will doat on her.", "“삼촌은 그 애한테 홀딱 반할 거야."], ["She is exactly the woman to do away every prejudice of such a man as the Admiral, for she is exactly such a woman as he thinks does not exist in the world.", "그 애야말로 제독 같은 사람의 온갖 편견을 걷어 낼 딱 그런 여자거든. 그 애가 바로, 삼촌이 세상엔 없다고 여기는 딱 그런 여자니까."], ["She is the very impossibility he would describe, if indeed he has now delicacy of language enough to embody his own ideas.", "삼촌이 만일 이제 제 생각을 담아낼 만큼 섬세한 말솜씨가 있다면, 그가 그려 보일 바로 그 있을 수 없는 여자란 말이야."], ["But till it is absolutely settled—settled beyond all interference, he shall know nothing of the matter.", "하지만 그 일이 아주 정해지기 전엔—어떤 참견으로도 못 뒤엎게 정해지기 전엔, 삼촌은 이 일에 대해 아무것도 몰라야 해."], ["No, Mary, you are quite mistaken.", "아니지, 메리, 넌 아주 잘못 짚었어."], ["You have not discovered my business yet.”", "아직 내 볼일을 못 알아냈다니까.”"]], [["“Well, well, I am satisfied.", "“됐어, 됐어, 나는 흡족해."], ["I know now to whom it must relate, and am in no hurry for the rest.", "이제 이게 누구에 관한 일인지 아니까, 나머지야 서두를 것 없어."], ["Fanny Price!", "패니 프라이스라니!"], ["wonderful, quite wonderful!", "놀라워, 정말 놀라워!"], ["That Mansfield should have done so much for—that you should have found your fate in Mansfield!", "맨스필드가 그토록 큰일을 해내다니—오빠가 맨스필드에서 제 운명을 찾다니!"], ["But you are quite right; you could not have chosen better.", "하지만 아주 옳은 선택이야. 이보다 더 잘 고를 수야 없었어."], ["There is not a better girl in the world, and you do not want for fortune; and as to her connexions, they are more than good.", "세상에 그보다 나은 애는 없고, 오빠야 재산이 아쉬운 것도 아니지. 그리고 그 애 인연으로 말하면, 좋은 것 이상이잖아."], ["The Bertrams are undoubtedly some of the first people in this country.", "버트럼가야 두말할 것 없이 이 나라 최고 명문 축에 드니까."], ["She is niece to Sir Thomas Bertram; that will be enough for the world.", "그 애는 토머스 버트럼 경의 조카딸이야. 그거면 세상엔 넉넉하지."], ["But go on, go on.", "하지만 계속해, 계속."], ["Tell me more.", "더 얘기해 줘."], ["What are your plans?", "오빠 계획은 뭐야?"], ["Does she know her own happiness?”", "그 애는 제 행복을 알아?”"]], [["“No.”", "“아니.”"]], [["“What are you waiting for?”", "“뭘 기다리는 건데?”"]], [["“For—for very little more than opportunity.", "“그저—그저 기회 말고는 거의 아무것도 안 기다려."], ["Mary, she is not like her cousins; but I think I shall not ask in vain.”", "메리, 그 애는 제 사촌들과는 달라. 하지만 나는 헛되이 청하진 않을 것 같아.”"]], [["“Oh no!", "“아 그럼!"], ["you cannot.", "헛될 리가."], ["Were you even less pleasing—supposing her not to love you already (of which, however, I can have little doubt)—you would be safe.", "오빠가 설령 덜 서글서글하다 해도—그 애가 아직 오빠를 사랑하지 않는다 쳐도(하기야 나야 그건 거의 의심 안 하지만)—오빠는 안전할 거야."], ["The gentleness and gratitude of her disposition would secure her all your own immediately.", "그 애 성미의 그 상냥함과 고마움이 그 애를 대번에 죄다 오빠 것으로 만들어 줄 테니까. 나는 온 넋으로 그 애가 사랑 없이는 오빠와 혼인 안 할 거라 믿어."], ["From my soul I do not think she would marry you without love; that is, if there is a girl in the world capable of being uninfluenced by ambition, I can suppose it her; but ask her to love you, and she will never have the heart to refuse.”", "그러니까, 세상에 야심에 휘둘리지 않을 수 있는 애가 있다면 그게 그 애려니 여길 수 있다는 거야. 하지만 그 애더러 오빠를 사랑해 달라 청해 봐, 그럼 그 애는 결코 거절할 마음이 안 들 테니까.”"]], [["As soon as her eagerness could rest in silence, he was as happy to tell as she could be to listen; and a conversation followed almost as deeply interesting to her as to himself, though he had in fact nothing to relate but his own sensations, nothing to dwell on but Fanny’s charms.", "그녀의 열띤 마음이 침묵 속에 잦아들자마자, 그는 그녀가 듣고 싶어 하는 만큼이나 기꺼이 얘기했다. 그리하여 뒤이은 대화는, 실은 그가 제 심경 말고는 들려줄 것도 없고 패니의 매력 말고는 머무를 것도 없었건만, 그에게 못지않게 그녀에게도 자못 깊이 흥미로웠다."], ["Fanny’s beauty of face and figure, Fanny’s graces of manner and goodness of heart, were the exhaustless theme.", "패니의 얼굴과 몸매의 아름다움, 패니의 태도의 우아함과 마음의 착함이 다함 없는 화제였다."], ["The gentleness, modesty, and sweetness of her character were warmly expatiated on; that sweetness which makes so essential a part of every woman’s worth in the judgment of man, that though he sometimes loves where it is not, he can never believe it absent.", "그녀 성품의 상냥함과 겸손함과 고움이 뜨겁게 늘어놓아졌다—사내의 눈에는 온갖 여자의 값어치에서 그토록 요긴한 몫을 이루는 그 고움, 그것이 없는 데서 이따금 사랑에 빠질지언정 그것이 없다고는 결코 믿지 못하게 하는 그 고움 말이다."], ["Her temper he had good reason to depend on and to praise. He had often seen it tried.", "그녀의 성미로 말하면 그는 그것에 기대고 또 그것을 칭찬할 만한 까닭이 넉넉했다."], ["Was there one of the family, excepting Edmund, who had not in some way or other continually exercised her patience and forbearance?", "그는 그것이 시험당하는 것을 곧잘 봐 왔던 것이다. 에드먼드만 빼고 그 집안에서, 어떤 식으로든 끊임없이 그녀의 인내와 참을성을 부려먹지 않은 이가 있던가?"], ["Her affections were evidently strong.", "그녀의 정은 분명 두터웠다."], ["To see her with her brother!", "오라비와 함께 있는 그 모습을 보라!"], ["What could more delightfully prove that the warmth of her heart was equal to its gentleness?", "그녀 마음의 그 뜨거움이 그 상냥함에 못지않음을 이보다 더 흐뭇하게 증명할 것이 무엇이랴?"], ["What could be more encouraging to a man who had her love in view?", "그녀의 사랑을 눈앞에 둔 사내에게 이보다 더 든든할 것이 무엇이랴?"], ["Then, her understanding was beyond every suspicion, quick and clear; and her manners were the mirror of her own modest and elegant mind.", "게다가 그녀의 슬기는 온갖 의심을 넘어, 재빠르고 맑았다. 그리고 그녀의 태도는 그녀 자신의 겸손하고 우아한 마음을 비추는 거울이었다."], ["Nor was this all.", "이뿐만이 아니었다."], ["Henry Crawford had too much sense not to feel the worth of good principles in a wife, though he was too little accustomed to serious reflection to know them by their proper name; but when he talked of her having such a steadiness and regularity of conduct, such a high notion of honour, and such an observance of decorum as might warrant any man in the fullest dependence on her faith and integrity, he expressed what was inspired by the knowledge of her being well principled and religious.", "헨리 크로퍼드는, 진지한 성찰에는 너무 안 익어 그것들을 제 이름으로 부를 줄은 몰랐을망정, 아내에게 있는 좋은 지조의 값어치를 못 느낄 만큼 어리석지는 않았다. 그리하여 그가 그녀에 대해, 어떤 사내라도 그 신의와 성실에 온전히 기대어도 좋을 만한 그런 처신의 진득함과 한결같음, 그런 드높은 명예심, 그런 예절 지킴을 지녔노라 말할 때, 그는 그녀가 지조 바르고 신심 깊은 사람임을 아는 데서 우러난 것을 드러낸 셈이었다."]], [["“I could so wholly and absolutely confide in her,”", "“나는 그 애한테 그토록 온전히, 그토록 남김없이 마음을 맡길 수 있어.”"], ["said he; “and that is what I want.”", "그가 말했다. “그리고 그게 바로 내가 바라는 거야.”"]], [["Well might his sister, believing as she really did that his opinion of Fanny Price was scarcely beyond her merits, rejoice in her prospects.", "그 누이가, 패니 프라이스에 대한 그의 평가가 그녀의 값어치를 좀처럼 넘어서지 않는다고 정말로 믿었던 만큼, 그녀의 앞날을 반기는 것도 마땅했다."]], [["“The more I think of it,”", "“생각하면 할수록,” 그녀가 외쳤다."], ["she cried, “the more am I convinced that you are doing quite right; and though I should never have selected Fanny Price as the girl most likely to attach you, I am now persuaded she is the very one to make you happy.", "“오빠가 아주 옳은 일을 하고 있다는 확신이 더 들어. 그리고 나야 패니 프라이스를 오빠를 사로잡을 성싶은 애로는 결코 고르지 않았을 테지만, 이제 나는 그 애가 오빠를 행복하게 해 줄 바로 그 애라 믿어."], ["Your wicked project upon her peace turns out a clever thought indeed.", "그 애 평온을 노린 오빠의 그 못된 심보가 알고 보니 정말 영리한 착상이었네."], ["You will both find your good in it.”", "둘 다 거기서 제 좋음을 찾을 테니까.”"]], [["“It was bad, very bad in me against such a creature; but I did not know her then; and she shall have no reason to lament the hour that first put it into my head.", "“그런 애한테 그리 굴었으니 못됐지, 아주 못됐어. 하지만 그땐 그 애를 몰랐고, 그 애는 그런 생각을 처음 내 머리에 넣어 준 그때를 슬퍼할 까닭이 없을 거야."], ["I will make her very happy, Mary; happier than she has ever yet been herself, or ever seen anybody else.", "나는 그 애를 아주 행복하게 해 줄 테니까, 메리. 그 애가 여태 저 스스로 누린 것보다, 남에게서 본 그 무엇보다 더 행복하게."], ["I will not take her from Northamptonshire.", "나는 그 애를 노샘프턴셔에서 데려가지 않을 거야."], ["I shall let Everingham, and rent a place in this neighbourhood; perhaps Stanwix Lodge.", "에버링엄을 세놓고, 이 근방에 자리 하나를 세낼 참이야. 어쩌면 스탠윅스 로지가 좋겠지."], ["I shall let a seven years’ lease of Everingham.", "에버링엄은 칠 년 임대로 내놓을 거고."], ["I am sure of an excellent tenant at half a word.", "나야 한마디만 하면 훌륭한 세입자를 얻는 건 떼어 놓은 당상이야."], ["I could name three people now, who would give me my own terms and thank me.”", "지금도 내 조건대로 다 내주고 오히려 나한테 고마워할 사람을 셋은 댈 수 있다니까.”"]], [["“Ha!”", "“어머!”"], ["cried Mary; “settle in Northamptonshire!", "메리가 외쳤다. “노샘프턴셔에 자리를 잡는다니!"], ["That is pleasant!", "그거 반갑네!"], ["Then we shall be all together.”", "그럼 우리 다 함께 있게 되겠구나.”"]], [["When she had spoken it, she recollected herself, and wished it unsaid; but there was no need of confusion; for her brother saw her only as the supposed inmate of Mansfield parsonage, and replied but to invite her in the kindest manner to his own house, and to claim the best right in her.", "그리 말하고 나서 그녀는 정신이 들어, 안 한 말이었으면 싶었다. 그러나 얼떨떨해할 것 없었다. 그 오라비는 그녀를 그저 맨스필드 목사관에 머무는 사람으로만 보고, 그저 더없이 다정한 투로 그녀를 제집으로 청하고 그녀에 대한 으뜸가는 권리를 주장하려 대꾸했을 뿐이니 말이다."]], [["“You must give us more than half your time,” said he.", "“넌 네 시간의 절반 넘게를 우리한테 줘야 해.”"], ["“I cannot admit Mrs.", "그가 말했다."], ["Grant to have an equal claim with Fanny and myself, for we shall both have a right in you.", "“나는 그랜트 부인이 나하고 패니와 대등한 권리를 갖는 건 인정 못 해. 우리 둘 다 너에 대한 권리를 가질 테니까."], ["Fanny will be so truly your sister!”", "패니는 정말이지 네 언니가 될 테고!”"]], [["Mary had only to be grateful and give general assurances; but she was now very fully purposed to be the guest of neither brother nor sister many months longer.", "메리는 그저 고마워하며 두루뭉술한 다짐이나 할밖에 없었다. 그러나 그녀는 이제, 오라비의 손님이든 올케의 손님이든 그리 여러 달 더는 안 되겠다고 자못 단단히 마음먹고 있었다."]], [["“You will divide your year between London and Northamptonshire?”", "“오빠는 한 해를 런던과 노샘프턴셔로 나눠 지낼 거야?”"]], [["“Yes.”", "“그래.”"]], [["“That’s right; and in London, of course, a house of your own: no longer with the Admiral.", "“그게 옳아. 그리고 런던에선, 물론, 오빠 자신의 집을 두고."], ["My dearest Henry, the advantage to you of getting away from the Admiral before your manners are hurt by the contagion of his, before you have contracted any of his foolish opinions, or learned to sit over your dinner as if it were the best blessing of life!", "더는 제독하고 함께가 아니라. 사랑하는 오빠, 오빠 몸가짐이 제독의 그 못된 물듦에 상하기 전에, 오빠가 제독의 그 어리석은 소견을 하나라도 옮기거나, 만찬 앞에 마치 그게 인생 최고의 복인 양 눌러앉는 걸 배우기 전에 제독한테서 벗어나는 게 얼마나 오빠한테 득인지!"], ["You are not sensible of the gain, for your regard for him has blinded you; but, in my estimation, your marrying early may be the saving of you.", "오빠야 그 득을 못 느끼겠지, 그이를 아끼는 마음에 눈이 멀었으니까. 하지만 내가 보기엔, 오빠가 일찍 혼인하는 게 오빠를 구하는 길일지도 몰라."], ["To have seen you grow like the Admiral in word or deed, look or gesture, would have broken my heart.”", "오빠가 말이나 행동에서, 눈빛이나 몸짓에서 제독을 닮아 가는 걸 봤다면 나는 가슴이 무너졌을 거야.”"]], [["“Well, well, we do not think quite alike here.", "“됐어, 됐어, 이 점에선 우리 생각이 아주 같진 않구나."], ["The Admiral has his faults, but he is a very good man, and has been more than a father to me.", "제독한테야 흠이 있지만, 그이는 아주 좋은 사람이고, 나한텐 아버지 이상이었어."], ["Few fathers would have let me have my own way half so much.", "나를 그 절반만큼이라도 내 뜻대로 하게 놔둘 아버지야 드물지."], ["You must not prejudice Fanny against him.", "넌 패니가 그이한테 편견을 갖게 만들면 안 돼."], ["I must have them love one another.”", "나는 둘이 서로 아끼게 해야겠어.”"]], [["Mary refrained from saying what she felt, that there could not be two persons in existence whose characters and manners were less accordant: time would discover it to him; but she could not help this reflection on the Admiral.", "메리는 제가 느낀 바를 입 밖에 내는 것을 삼갔으니, 곧 그 성품과 몸가짐이 그보다 덜 맞을 두 사람은 세상에 없으리라는 것이었다. 시간이 그것을 그에게 일러 줄 터였다."], ["“Henry, I think so highly of Fanny Price, that if I could suppose the next Mrs.", "그러나 그녀는 제독을 두고 이 소감만은 뇌지 않을 수 없었다. “오빠, 나는 패니 프라이스를 하도 높이 봐서, 만일 다음번 크로퍼드 부인이, 학대받은 우리 딱한 숙모가 그 이름 자체를 몸서리치게 미워한 그 까닭의 절반이라도 갖게 되리라 짐작할 수 있다면, 나는 되도록 이 혼인을 막을 거야."], ["Crawford would have half the reason which my poor ill-used aunt had to abhor the very name, I would prevent the marriage, if possible; but I know you: I know that a wife you loved would be the happiest of women, and that even when you ceased to love, she would yet find in you the liberality and good-breeding of a gentleman.”", "하지만 나는 오빠를 알아. 오빠가 사랑하는 아내라면 세상에 둘도 없이 행복한 여자가 되리라는 걸, 그리고 오빠가 설령 사랑을 그친다 해도 아내는 오빠한테서 신사다운 너그러움과 좋은 교양은 여전히 찾으리라는 걸 나는 알거든.”"]], [["The impossibility of not doing everything in the world to make Fanny Price happy, or of ceasing to love Fanny Price, was of course the groundwork of his eloquent answer.", "패니 프라이스를 행복하게 하려고 세상 온갖 일을 다 안 할 도리가 없다는 것, 또는 패니 프라이스를 사랑하기를 그칠 도리가 없다는 것이, 물론 그의 그 청산유수 같은 대답의 바탕이었다."]], [["“Had you seen her this morning, Mary,”", "“오늘 아침 그 애를 봤어야 하는데, 메리.”"], ["he continued, “attending with such ineffable sweetness and patience to all the demands of her aunt’s stupidity, working with her, and for her, her colour beautifully heightened as she leant over the work, then returning to her seat to finish a note which she was previously engaged in writing for that stupid woman’s service, and all this with such unpretending gentleness, so much as if it were a matter of course that she was not to have a moment at her own command, her hair arranged as neatly as it always is, and one little curl falling forward as she wrote, which she now and then shook back, and in the midst of all this, still speaking at intervals to me, or listening, and as if she liked to listen, to what I said.", "그가 말을 이었다. “이모의 그 아둔함이 요구하는 온갖 것을 그토록 이루 말할 수 없는 고움과 참을성으로 받들며, 그 이모와 함께, 또 그 이모를 위해 바느질을 하고, 일감 위로 몸을 숙일 제 그 낯빛이 곱게 발그레 상기되고, 그러다 다시 제자리로 돌아가, 그 아둔한 여자의 시중을 들려 앞서 쓰고 있던 쪽지를 마저 쓰는데, 이 모든 걸 그토록 꾸밈없는 상냥함으로, 마치 제 뜻대로 쓸 순간이 한 톨도 없는 게 지극히 당연한 일인 양 하면서, 머리칼은 늘 그렇듯 정갈하게 매만져지고, 자그마한 곱슬머리 한 가닥이 쓸 때마다 앞으로 흘러내리면 이따금 뒤로 넘기고, 그 와중에도 여전히 틈틈이 나한테 말을 걸거나, 내 말을 듣는데—마치 듣는 게 즐거운 양 듣는 그 모습을 봤어야 하는데."], ["Had you seen her so, Mary, you would not have implied the possibility of her power over my heart ever ceasing.”", "그런 그 애를 봤더라면, 메리, 내 마음을 사로잡은 그 애 힘이 언젠가 그칠 수 있으리라곤 넌 넌지시라도 못 비쳤을 거야.”"]], [["“My dearest Henry,”", "“사랑하는 오빠,”"], ["cried Mary, stopping short, and smiling in his face, “how glad I am to see you so much in love!", "메리가 우뚝 멈춰 서서 그의 얼굴을 보며 미소 지으며 외쳤다. “오빠가 이토록 사랑에 빠진 걸 보니 얼마나 기쁜지!"], ["It quite delights me.", "아주 신이 나."], ["But what will Mrs. Rushworth and Julia say?”", "하지만 러시워스 부인하고 줄리아는 뭐라 할까?”"]], [["“I care neither what they say nor what they feel.", "“나야 그들이 뭐라 하든 뭘 느끼든 상관없어."], ["They will now see what sort of woman it is that can attach me, that can attach a man of sense.", "이제 그들도, 나를 사로잡을 수 있는, 지각 있는 사내를 사로잡을 수 있는 여자가 어떤 여자인지 알게 될 거야."], ["I wish the discovery may do them any good.", "그 깨달음이 그들한테 무슨 득이라도 되면 좋겠군."], ["And they will now see their cousin treated as she ought to be, and I wish they may be heartily ashamed of their own abominable neglect and unkindness.", "그리고 이제 그들도 제 사촌이 마땅히 받아야 할 대접을 받는 걸 보게 될 테고, 나야 그들이 제 그 몹쓸 소홀함과 야박함을 진심으로 부끄러워하면 좋겠어."], ["They will be angry,”", "화를 낼 테지.”"], ["he added, after a moment’s silence, and in a cooler tone; “Mrs.", "그가 잠깐 잠잠하다 한결 차가운 투로 덧붙였다. “러시워스 부인은 무척 화를 낼 거야."], ["Rushworth will be very angry.", "그 여자한텐 쓴 약이겠지."], ["It will be a bitter pill to her; that is, like other bitter pills, it will have two moments’ ill flavour, and then be swallowed and forgotten; for I am not such a coxcomb as to suppose her feelings more lasting than other women’s, though I was the object of them.", "그러니까, 다른 쓴 약이 그렇듯 잠깐 두어 순간 고약한 맛이 나다가, 이내 삼켜지고 잊힐 거란 말이야. 나야 비록 내가 그 감정의 과녁이었을망정, 그 여자의 감정이 다른 여자들 것보다 더 오래가리라 여길 만큼 자만한 사내는 아니거든."], ["Yes, Mary, my Fanny will feel a difference indeed: a daily, hourly difference, in the behaviour of every being who approaches her; and it will be the completion of my happiness to know that I am the doer of it, that I am the person to give the consequence so justly her due.", "그래, 메리, 내 패니는 정말이지 달라짐을 느낄 거야. 저한테 다가오는 뭇사람의 처신에서 날마다, 시시각각의 달라짐을."], ["Now she is dependent, helpless, friendless, neglected, forgotten.”", "그리고 그걸 이룬 이가 바로 나임을, 그토록 마땅히 그 애 몫인 대접을 안겨 줄 이가 바로 나임을 아는 것이 내 행복을 온전케 할 거야. 지금 그 애는 얹혀사는, 힘없는, 벗 없는, 소홀히 여겨지는, 잊힌 사람이야.”"]], [["“Nay, Henry, not by all; not forgotten by all; not friendless or forgotten.", "“아냐, 오빠, 다한테는 아니야. 다한테 잊힌 건 아니야."], ["Her cousin Edmund never forgets her.”", "벗 없거나 잊힌 게 아니라고. 사촌 에드먼드는 결코 그 애를 잊지 않아.”"]], [["“Edmund!", "“에드먼드!"], ["True, I believe he is, generally speaking, kind to her, and so is Sir Thomas in his way; but it is the way of a rich, superior, long-worded, arbitrary uncle.", "하기야, 나도 그가 대체로는 그 애한테 친절하다고 믿어. 그리고 토머스 경도 그 나름으론 그렇지."], ["What can Sir Thomas and Edmund together do, what do they do for her happiness, comfort, honour, and dignity in the world, to what I shall do?”", "하지만 그건 부유하고, 잘나고, 말 많고, 제멋대로인 삼촌의 방식일 뿐이야. 토머스 경과 에드먼드가 힘을 합친들, 세상에서 그 애의 행복과 안락과 명예와 위엄을 위해 대체 무얼 할 수 있으며, 무얼 하고 있느냐, 내가 할 것에 견주면 말이야?”"]], [["# CHAPTER XXXI", "# 제31장"]], [["Henry Crawford was at Mansfield Park again the next morning, and at an earlier hour than common visiting warrants.", "헨리 크로퍼드는 이튿날 아침 다시 맨스필드 파크에 왔는데, 여느 방문이 허락될 만한 것보다 이른 시각이었다."], ["The two ladies were together in the breakfast-room, and, fortunately for him, Lady Bertram was on the very point of quitting it as he entered.", "두 부인이 아침 식사방에 함께 있었고, 그에게는 다행스럽게도 버트럼 부인이 그가 들어설 바로 그 참에 막 방을 나서려는 중이었다."], ["She was almost at the door, and not chusing by any means to take so much trouble in vain, she still went on, after a civil reception, a short sentence about being waited for, and a “Let Sir Thomas know”", "그녀는 거의 문간에 이르러 있던 터라, 그 수고를 도무지 헛되이 하기는 싫어, 정중히 그를 맞고, 저를 기다리는 이가 있다는 짤막한 말과, 하인에게 “토머스 경께 알리게”"], ["to the servant.", "하는 분부를 남기고는 그대로 나갔다."]], [["Henry, overjoyed to have her go, bowed and watched her off, and without losing another moment, turned instantly to Fanny, and, taking out some letters, said, with a most animated look, “I must acknowledge myself infinitely obliged to any creature who gives me such an opportunity of seeing you alone: I have been wishing it more than you can have any idea.", "헨리는 그녀가 나가는 것이 못내 반가워, 목례하며 그녀를 배웅하고는, 한순간도 더 허비하지 않고 대번에 패니에게로 돌아서서, 편지 몇 통을 꺼내며 더없이 생기 어린 낯빛으로 말했다. “저에게 이렇게 프라이스 양을 단둘이 만날 기회를 주는 이한테, 저는 이루 헤아릴 수 없이 신세 진 사람임을 인정해야겠습니다."], ["Knowing as I do what your feelings as a sister are, I could hardly have borne that any one in the house should share with you in the first knowledge of the news I now bring.", "저는 양이 짐작도 못 할 만큼 이걸 바라 왔거든요. 누이로서 양의 심경이 어떠할지 제가 아는 만큼, 제가 지금 가져온 이 소식을 이 집 안 누구든 양과 먼저 나눈다는 건 저로선 도무지 견딜 수 없었을 겁니다."], ["He is made.", "그가 임관됐어요."], ["Your brother is a lieutenant.", "양의 오라비가 중위가 됐습니다."], ["I have the infinite satisfaction of congratulating you on your brother’s promotion.", "저는 양의 오라비의 진급을 축하드리는 이루 말할 수 없는 흡족함을 누리고 있어요."], ["Here are the letters which announce it, this moment come to hand.", "여기 그걸 알리는 편지들이 있습니다, 방금 막 손에 들어온 것들이에요."], ["You will, perhaps, like to see them.”", "아마 양도 보고 싶으시겠지요.”"]], [["Fanny could not speak, but he did not want her to speak.", "패니는 말을 할 수가 없었으나, 그도 그녀가 말하기를 바라지 않았다."], ["To see the expression of her eyes, the change of her complexion, the progress of her feelings, their doubt, confusion, and felicity, was enough.", "그녀 눈의 그 표정을, 낯빛의 그 바뀜을, 그 심경의 오르내림을, 그 미심쩍음과 얼떨떨함과 벅찬 기쁨을 보는 것으로 넉넉했다."], ["She took the letters as he gave them.", "그녀는 그가 건네는 대로 편지들을 받았다."], ["The first was from the Admiral to inform his nephew, in a few words, of his having succeeded in the object he had undertaken, the promotion of young Price, and enclosing two more, one from the Secretary of the First Lord to a friend, whom the Admiral had set to work in the business, the other from that friend to himself, by which it appeared that his lordship had the very great happiness of attending to the recommendation of Sir Charles; that Sir Charles was much delighted in having such an opportunity of proving his regard for Admiral Crawford, and that the circumstance of Mr.", "첫 통은 제독이 조카에게, 저가 떠맡은 일—곧 젊은 프라이스의 진급—을 이뤄 냈음을 몇 마디로 알리는 것이었고, 거기에 두 통이 더 동봉되어 있었다. 하나는 해군장관의 비서가, 제독이 이 일에 나서게 한 한 벗에게 보낸 것이었고, 다른 하나는 그 벗이 제독에게 보낸 것이었다."], ["William Price’s commission as Second Lieutenant of H.M. Sloop Thrush being made out was spreading general joy through a wide circle of great people.", "그것들로 미루어, 장관께서는 찰스 경의 천거를 받들어 주는 크나큰 기쁨을 누리셨고, 찰스 경은 크로퍼드 제독에 대한 제 마음을 증명할 그런 기회를 얻은 것을 무척 기뻐했으며, 윌리엄 프라이스 씨가 국왕 폐하의 슬루프함 스러시호의 차석 중위 임명장을 받게 된 그 사정이 여러 높은 분들의 넓은 무리에 두루 기쁨을 퍼뜨리고 있음이 드러났다."]], [["While her hand was trembling under these letters, her eye running from one to the other, and her heart swelling with emotion, Crawford thus continued, with unfeigned eagerness, to express his interest in the event—", "이 편지들 아래 그녀의 손이 떨리고, 그 눈이 이것에서 저것으로 오가고, 그 가슴이 벅차오르는 동안, 크로퍼드는 이 일에 대한 제 관심을 꾸밈없이 열띠게 이렇게 이어 나갔다—"]], [["“I will not talk of my own happiness,”", "“제 행복은 얘기하지 않겠습니다.” 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “great as it is, for I think only of yours.", "“그게 아무리 크더라도요, 저는 오직 양의 행복만 생각하니까요."], ["Compared with you, who has a right to be happy?", "양에 견주어, 누가 행복할 자격이 있겠습니까?"], ["I have almost grudged myself my own prior knowledge of what you ought to have known before all the world.", "저는 양이 온 세상 누구보다 먼저 알아야 할 것을 저 혼자 앞서 알고 있었다는 게 제 자신한테 거의 미울 지경이었어요."], ["I have not lost a moment, however.", "그래도 저는 한순간도 허비하지 않았습니다."], ["The post was late this morning, but there has not been since a moment’s delay.", "오늘 아침 우편이 늦었지만, 그 뒤로는 한순간의 지체도 없었어요."], ["How impatient, how anxious, how wild I have been on the subject, I will not attempt to describe; how severely mortified, how cruelly disappointed, in not having it finished while I was in London!", "이 일로 제가 얼마나 조바심 나고, 얼마나 애타고, 얼마나 미칠 것 같았는지는 이루 그려 내지 않으렵니다. 런던에 있는 동안 이걸 마무리 짓지 못한 게 얼마나 사무치게 분하고, 얼마나 모질게 실망스러웠는지도요!"], ["I was kept there from day to day in the hope of it, for nothing less dear to me than such an object would have detained me half the time from Mansfield.", "저는 그 바람에 하루하루 거기 붙들려 있었어요. 이만큼 소중한 일이 아니고서야 저를 그 절반의 시간이나마 맨스필드에서 떼어 놓았을 리 없거든요."], ["But though my uncle entered into my wishes with all the warmth I could desire, and exerted himself immediately, there were difficulties from the absence of one friend, and the engagements of another, which at last I could no longer bear to stay the end of, and knowing in what good hands I left the cause, I came away on Monday, trusting that many posts would not pass before I should be followed by such very letters as these.", "하지만 제 삼촌이 제가 바랄 수 있는 온갖 열의로 제 바람에 나서 주고 곧바로 애써 주긴 했으되, 한 벗은 부재중이고 또 한 벗은 볼일에 매인 탓에 어려움이 있었고, 끝내 저는 그 마무리를 지켜보려 더 머무는 걸 도무지 견딜 수 없었습니다. 그리하여 그 일을 얼마나 좋은 손에 맡겨 두는지를 알기에, 저는 월요일에 떠나왔어요, 바로 이런 편지들이 여러 우편도 지나기 전에 저를 뒤따라오리라 믿으면서요."], ["My uncle, who is the very best man in the world, has exerted himself, as I knew he would, after seeing your brother.", "세상에 둘도 없이 좋은 사람인 제 삼촌은, 제가 그러리라 알았던 대로, 양의 오라비를 보고 나서 애써 주었습니다."], ["He was delighted with him.", "삼촌은 그가 몹시 마음에 들었어요."], ["I would not allow myself yesterday to say how delighted, or to repeat half that the Admiral said in his praise.", "어제는 얼마나 마음에 들어 했는지 차마 말하지도, 제독이 그를 칭찬한 말의 절반도 옮기지도 못하게 저 자신을 다잡았지요."], ["I deferred it all till his praise should be proved the praise of a friend, as this day does prove it.", "그 칭찬이 벗의 칭찬임이 증명될 때까지 그 모두를 미뤄 두었던 겁니다, 오늘이 정말로 그걸 증명해 주듯이요."], ["Now I may say that even I could not require William Price to excite a greater interest, or be followed by warmer wishes and higher commendation, than were most voluntarily bestowed by my uncle after the evening they had passed together.”", "이제야 저는 말할 수 있어요, 저조차도 윌리엄 프라이스가 그보다 더 큰 관심을 불러일으키거나, 그보다 더 따뜻한 축원과 더 드높은 칭찬이 뒤따르기를 바랄 수 없다고요—함께 보낸 그 저녁 뒤 제 삼촌이 더없이 자진해서 베푼 것보다 말입니다.”"]], [["“Has this been all your doing, then?”", "“그럼 이게 죄다 크로퍼드 씨가 하신 일이란 말이에요?”"], ["cried Fanny.", "패니가 외쳤다."], ["“Good heaven!", "“세상에!"], ["how very, very kind!", "어쩌면 이토록, 이토록 친절하세요!"], ["Have you really—was it by your desire?", "정말로—크로퍼드 씨 바람으로 그리된 거예요?"], ["I beg your pardon, but I am bewildered.", "죄송해요, 저는 정신이 하나도 없어요."], ["Did Admiral Crawford apply?", "크로퍼드 제독께서 청하셨어요?"], ["How was it?", "어찌 된 일이에요?"], ["I am stupefied.”", "저는 얼이 다 빠졌어요.”"]], [["Henry was most happy to make it more intelligible, by beginning at an earlier stage, and explaining very particularly what he had done.", "헨리는, 좀 더 앞선 대목에서부터 시작해 제가 한 일을 아주 낱낱이 밝히며 그것을 더 알아듣기 쉽게 해 주는 것이 더없이 기뻤다."], ["His last journey to London had been undertaken with no other view than that of introducing her brother in Hill Street, and prevailing on the Admiral to exert whatever interest he might have for getting him on.", "그가 지난번 런던에 간 것은, 오로지 그녀의 오라비를 힐 스트리트에 소개하고, 그를 밀어 주는 데 제독에게 무슨 힘이 있든 그것을 써 달라 설득하려는 것 말고는 다른 뜻이 없었다. 이것이 그의 볼일이었다."], ["This had been his business.", "그는 그것을 아무한테도 알리지 않았다."], ["He had communicated it to no creature: he had not breathed a syllable of it even to Mary; while uncertain of the issue, he could not have borne any participation of his feelings, but this had been his business; and he spoke with such a glow of what his solicitude had been, and used such strong expressions, was so abounding in the deepest interest, in twofold motives, in views and wishes more than could be told, that Fanny could not have remained insensible of his drift, had she been able to attend; but her heart was so full and her senses still so astonished, that she could listen but imperfectly even to what he told her of William, and saying only when he paused, “How kind!", "메리에게조차 한마디도 발설하지 않았다. 그 결말이 미덥지 않은 동안엔 제 심경을 조금도 나눌 수가 없었던 것이다."], ["how very kind! Oh, Mr.", "아무튼 이것이 그의 볼일이었다."], ["Crawford, we are infinitely obliged to you! Dearest, dearest William!”", "그리고 그는 제 마음씀이 어떠했는지를 그토록 달아오른 투로 이야기하며, 그토록 세찬 표현을 쓰고, 더없이 깊은 관심에, 두 겹의 동기에, 이루 말할 수 없이 많은 뜻과 바람에 그토록 넘쳐 났던지라, 만일 그녀가 새겨들을 수 있었더라면 그의 속뜻을 도무지 모른 채로 있을 수 없었으리라."], ["She jumped up and moved in haste towards the door, crying out, “I will go to my uncle.", "그러나 그녀의 가슴이 하도 벅차고 정신이 여전히 하도 얼떨떨한 터라, 그녀는 그가 윌리엄에 대해 들려주는 것조차 어설프게밖에 못 듣고, 그가 말을 멈출 때면 그저 “어쩌면 이토록 친절하세요! 아, 크로퍼드 씨, 우리는 크로퍼드 씨한테 이루 헤아릴 수 없이 신세를 졌어요!"], ["My uncle ought to know it as soon as possible.”", "사랑하는, 사랑하는 윌리엄!” 할 뿐이었다."], ["But this could not be suffered.", "그녀는 벌떡 일어나 서둘러 문 쪽으로 옮겨 가며 외쳤다."], ["The opportunity was too fair, and his feelings too impatient.", "“저는 삼촌한테 가야겠어요. 삼촌이 되도록 빨리 이걸 아셔야 해요.”"], ["He was after her immediately.", "그러나 이것은 용납될 수 없었다."], ["“She must not go, she must allow him five minutes longer,”", "기회는 너무 좋았고, 그의 심경은 너무 조바심 났다. 그는 대번에 그녀를 뒤쫓았다."], ["and he took her hand and led her back to her seat, and was in the middle of his farther explanation, before she had suspected for what she was detained.", "“그녀는 가면 안 된다, 오 분만 더 저에게 내주어야 한다”며, 그는 그녀의 손을 잡아 다시 자리로 이끌었고, 그녀가 무엇 때문에 붙들렸는지 미처 짐작도 하기 전에 벌써 그 더한 설명의 한복판에 있었다. 그러나 그녀가 마침내 그것을 알아듣고, 저가 그의 마음에 여태 한 번도 알지 못한 심경을 자아냈다는 것과, 그가 윌리엄을 위해 한 온갖 일이 저에 대한 그의 지나치고 견줄 데 없는 애착의 몫으로 돌려지리라는 것을 믿으라 기대받는 자신을 발견하니, 그녀는 몹시 괴로워 한동안 말을 하지 못했다."], ["When she did understand it, however, and found herself expected to believe that she had created sensations which his heart had never known before, and that everything he had done for William was to be placed to the account of his excessive and unequalled attachment to her, she was exceedingly distressed, and for some moments unable to speak.", "그녀는 그것을 죄다 헛소리로, 그저 그 한때를 속이려는 실없는 희롱이요 정겨움으로 여겼다. 그가 저를 온당찮고 되잖게, 저가 받을 만하지 않은 그런 식으로 대하고 있다고 느끼지 않을 수 없었다."], ["She considered it all as nonsense, as mere trifling and gallantry, which meant only to deceive for the hour; she could not but feel that it was treating her improperly and unworthily, and in such a way as she had not deserved; but it was like himself, and entirely of a piece with what she had seen before; and she would not allow herself to shew half the displeasure she felt, because he had been conferring an obligation, which no want of delicacy on his part could make a trifle to her.", "그러나 그것은 딱 그다웠고, 앞서 본 것과 온전히 한 결이었다. 그리고 그녀는 제가 느낀 언짢음의 절반도 내비치려 하지 않았으니, 그가 은혜를 베푼 참이라, 그의 편의 어떤 무례함으로도 그것을 그녀에게 하찮은 것으로 만들 수는 없었던 까닭이다."], ["While her heart was still bounding with joy and gratitude on William’s behalf, she could not be severely resentful of anything that injured only herself; and after having twice drawn back her hand, and twice attempted in vain to turn away from him, she got up, and said only, with much agitation, “Don’t, Mr.", "윌리엄을 위한 기쁨과 고마움으로 여전히 가슴이 뛰는 동안, 그녀는 오직 저 자신만을 해치는 것에는 모질게 노여워할 수가 없었다. 그리하여 두 번 손을 빼고, 두 번 헛되이 그에게서 돌아서려 애쓴 끝에, 그녀는 일어나 몹시 떨리는 투로 이렇게만 말했다."], ["Crawford, pray don’t! I beg you would not.", "“그러지 마세요, 크로퍼드 씨, 제발 그러지 마세요!"], ["This is a sort of talking which is very unpleasant to me.", "부디 그러지 마시길 청해요. 이런 얘기는 제게 무척 언짢아요."], ["I must go away.", "저는 가야겠어요."], ["I cannot bear it.”", "도무지 견딜 수가 없어요.”"], ["But he was still talking on, describing his affection, soliciting a return, and, finally, in words so plain as to bear but one meaning even to her, offering himself, hand, fortune, everything, to her acceptance.", "그러나 그는 여전히 얘기를 이어 가며, 제 애정을 그려 보이고, 화답을 청하고, 마침내 그녀에게조차 오직 한 가지 뜻으로밖에 들릴 수 없을 만큼 뚜렷한 말로, 저 자신을, 그 손을, 그 재산을, 그 온갖 것을 그녀가 받아 주기를 청했다. 그러했다."], ["It was so; he had said it.", "그가 그리 말한 것이다."], ["Her astonishment and confusion increased; and though still not knowing how to suppose him serious, she could hardly stand.", "그녀의 놀라움과 얼떨떨함이 더해 갔다. 그리고 그를 진심이라 여길 길을 여전히 몰랐으면서도, 그녀는 도무지 서 있기조차 힘들었다."], ["He pressed for an answer.", "그가 대답을 재촉했다."]], [["“No, no, no!” she cried, hiding her face.", "“안 돼요, 안 돼요, 안 돼요!”"], ["“This is all nonsense.", "그녀가 얼굴을 가리며 외쳤다."], ["Do not distress me.", "“이건 죄다 헛소리예요."], ["I can hear no more of this.", "저를 괴롭히지 마세요. 이런 얘긴 더 못 듣겠어요."], ["Your kindness to William makes me more obliged to you than words can express; but I do not want, I cannot bear, I must not listen to such—No, no, don’t think of me.", "윌리엄에 대한 크로퍼드 씨의 친절은 말로 다 못 할 만큼 저를 신세 지게 했지만, 저는 그런—원치 않고, 견딜 수 없고, 들어서도 안 돼요. 아뇨, 아뇨, 저를 마음에 두지 마세요."], ["But you are not thinking of me.", "하지만 크로퍼드 씨는 정말로 저를 마음에 두는 게 아니잖아요."], ["I know it is all nothing.”", "저는 이게 죄다 아무것도 아닌 줄 알아요.”"]], [["She had burst away from him, and at that moment Sir Thomas was heard speaking to a servant in his way towards the room they were in.", "그녀는 그에게서 왈칵 벗어난 참이었고, 바로 그때 토머스 경이 그들이 있는 방으로 오는 길에 하인에게 말하는 소리가 들렸다."], ["It was no time for farther assurances or entreaty, though to part with her at a moment when her modesty alone seemed, to his sanguine and preassured mind, to stand in the way of the happiness he sought, was a cruel necessity.", "더 다짐하거나 애원할 때가 아니었다. 하기야 그의 그 낙관적이고 미리 굳어진 마음에는 오직 그녀의 겸손함만이 제가 좇는 행복을 가로막는 듯 보이던 그 순간에 그녀와 헤어져야 한다는 것이 모진 노릇이긴 했다."], ["She rushed out at an opposite door from the one her uncle was approaching, and was walking up and down the East room in the utmost confusion of contrary feeling, before Sir Thomas’s politeness or apologies were over, or he had reached the beginning of the joyful intelligence which his visitor came to communicate.", "그녀는 삼촌이 다가오는 문과 맞은편 문으로 뛰쳐나가, 토머스 경의 예의차림이나 사과가 끝나기도, 그가 손님이 전하러 온 그 기쁜 소식의 첫머리에 이르기도 전에, 벌써 서로 어긋난 심경의 더없는 얼떨떨함 속에 동쪽 방을 이리저리 거닐고 있었다."]], [["She was feeling, thinking, trembling about everything; agitated, happy, miserable, infinitely obliged, absolutely angry.", "그녀는 온갖 것을 두고 느끼고, 생각하고, 떨었다. 들뜨고, 행복하고, 비참하고, 이루 헤아릴 수 없이 신세 졌고, 아주 노여웠다."], ["It was all beyond belief!", "그 모두가 도무지 믿기지 않았다!"], ["He was inexcusable, incomprehensible!", "그는 용서할 수도, 헤아릴 수도 없는 사람이었다!"], ["But such were his habits that he could do nothing without a mixture of evil.", "그러나 악의 뒤섞임 없이는 아무것도 못 하는 것이 그의 버릇이었다."], ["He had previously made her the happiest of human beings, and now he had insulted—she knew not what to say, how to class, or how to regard it.", "그는 앞서 그녀를 세상에 둘도 없이 행복한 사람으로 만들어 놓고, 이제 그녀를 모욕한—그녀는 무어라 말할지, 어찌 갈래 지을지, 어찌 여길지 알 수 없었다."], ["She would not have him be serious, and yet what could excuse the use of such words and offers, if they meant but to trifle?", "그녀는 그가 진심이기를 바라지 않았으되, 그렇다고 그런 말과 청이 그저 희롱하려는 것이었다면 대체 무엇으로 변명이 되겠는가?"]], [["But William was a lieutenant.", "그러나 윌리엄은 중위였다."], ["That was a fact beyond a doubt, and without an alloy.", "그것은 의심할 여지 없는, 티 하나 없는 사실이었다. 그녀는 그것을 영영 생각하며 나머지는 죄다 잊으리라."], ["She would think of it for ever and forget all the rest. Mr.", "크로퍼드 씨는 분명 다시는 그녀에게 그리 말하지 않을 것이었다."], ["Crawford would certainly never address her so again: he must have seen how unwelcome it was to her; and in that case, how gratefully she could esteem him for his friendship to William!", "그것이 그녀에게 얼마나 달갑잖은지 그도 보았을 게 틀림없었다. 그러면 그녀는 윌리엄을 향한 그의 우정에 대해 얼마나 고맙게 그를 기릴 수 있으랴!"]], [["She would not stir farther from the East room than the head of the great staircase, till she had satisfied herself of Mr.", "그녀는 크로퍼드 씨가 집을 떠났음을 확인할 때까지는 큰 계단 위쪽에서 더는 동쪽 방을 벗어나려 하지 않았다."], ["Crawford’s having left the house; but when convinced of his being gone, she was eager to go down and be with her uncle, and have all the happiness of his joy as well as her own, and all the benefit of his information or his conjectures as to what would now be William’s destination.", "그러나 그가 갔음을 확신하자, 그녀는 얼른 아래층으로 내려가 삼촌과 함께 있으며, 저 자신의 기쁨뿐 아니라 그 기쁨까지 죄다 누리고, 이제 윌리엄이 어디로 배치될지에 대한 그의 소식이나 그의 짐작에서 온갖 이로움을 얻고 싶어졌다. 토머스 경은 그녀가 바랄 만큼 기뻐했고, 무척 친절하고 스스럼없이 이야기를 나누어 주었다."], ["Sir Thomas was as joyful as she could desire, and very kind and communicative; and she had so comfortable a talk with him about William as to make her feel as if nothing had occurred to vex her, till she found, towards the close, that Mr. Crawford was engaged to return and dine there that very day.", "그리하여 그녀는 윌리엄에 대해 그와 어찌나 흐뭇하게 얘기를 나누었던지, 저를 언짢게 할 일이라곤 아무것도 없었던 것처럼 느껴질 지경이었다—그 끝머리에 이르러, 크로퍼드 씨가 바로 그날 다시 와서 여기서 만찬을 들기로 되어 있음을 알기 전까지는 말이다."], ["This was a most unwelcome hearing, for though he might think nothing of what had passed, it would be quite distressing to her to see him again so soon.", "이것은 더없이 달갑잖은 소식이었으니, 그야 지난 일을 아무렇지 않게 여길지 몰라도, 그를 그토록 이내 다시 보는 것이 그녀로선 자못 괴로운 노릇이었던 것이다."]], [["She tried to get the better of it; tried very hard, as the dinner hour approached, to feel and appear as usual; but it was quite impossible for her not to look most shy and uncomfortable when their visitor entered the room.", "그녀는 그것을 이겨 내려 애썼다. 만찬 시각이 다가오자, 여느 때처럼 느끼고 여느 때처럼 보이려 무척 애썼다."], ["She could not have supposed it in the power of any concurrence of circumstances to give her so many painful sensations on the first day of hearing of William’s promotion.", "그러나 손님이 방에 들어설 때 더없이 수줍고 거북한 낯을 안 하기란 그녀로선 도무지 어림없는 일이었다. 그녀는 윌리엄의 진급 소식을 들은 첫날에 저에게 이토록 많은 괴로운 심경을 안겨 줄 만한 사정의 겹침이 있으리라곤 여겨 본 적이 없었다."]], [["Mr. Crawford was not only in the room—he was soon close to her.", "크로퍼드 씨는 그저 방에 있는 정도가 아니라—이내 그녀 바로 곁에 있었다."], ["He had a note to deliver from his sister.", "그는 제 누이에게서 전할 쪽지 하나를 가지고 있었다."], ["Fanny could not look at him, but there was no consciousness of past folly in his voice.", "패니는 그를 볼 수가 없었으나, 그의 목소리에는 지난 어리석음에 대한 켕김이 조금도 없었다."], ["She opened her note immediately, glad to have anything to do, and happy, as she read it, to feel that the fidgetings of her aunt Norris, who was also to dine there, screened her a little from view.", "그녀는 무엇이든 할 일이 있는 게 반가워 대번에 제 쪽지를 펼쳤고, 읽어 가면서, 이 자리에서 함께 만찬을 들 노리스 이모의 그 부산함이 저를 남의 눈에서 얼마쯤 가려 준다는 것이 다행스러웠다."]], [["“MY DEAR FANNY,—for so I may now always call you, to the infinite relief of a tongue that has been stumbling at Miss Price for at least the last six weeks—I cannot let my brother go without sending you a few lines of general congratulation, and giving my most joyful consent and approval.", "“사랑하는 패니에게—이제 나는 언제나 그쪽을 이렇게 불러도 되겠네요. 적어도 지난 여섯 주 동안 프라이스 양이라 하며 걸리적거리던 이 혀가 이루 말할 수 없이 놓이게 말이에요—그쪽한테 몇 줄 두루 축하의 말과, 더없이 기쁜 내 승낙과 찬동을 보내지 않고는 오라비를 보낼 수가 없어요."], ["Go on, my dear Fanny, and without fear; there can be no difficulties worth naming.", "나아가요, 사랑하는 패니, 두려워 말고요. 이렇다 하게 걸릴 만한 어려움이라곤 없을 테니까요."], ["I chuse to suppose that the assurance of my consent will be something; so you may smile upon him with your sweetest smiles this afternoon, and send him back to me even happier than he goes.", "나는 내 승낙의 다짐이 무언가 힘이 되려니 여기려 해요. 그러니 그쪽은 오늘 오후 더없이 고운 미소로 그이한테 방긋 웃어 주고, 떠날 때보다 더 행복한 채로 그이를 내게 돌려보내 줘요."]], [["Yours affectionately, M.", "애정을 담아, M."], ["C.”", "C.”"]], [["These were not expressions to do Fanny any good; for though she read in too much haste and confusion to form the clearest judgment of Miss Crawford’s meaning, it was evident that she meant to compliment her on her brother’s attachment, and even to appear to believe it serious.", "이런 것은 패니에게 조금도 좋을 것이 없는 말이었다. 그야 그녀가 크로퍼드 양의 뜻을 더없이 또렷이 헤아리기엔 너무 서둘러 얼떨떨한 채로 읽기는 했으나, 그녀가 오라비의 애착을 두고 저를 추어 주려는 뜻인 것, 심지어 그것을 진심이라 믿는 척하려는 뜻인 것만은 뻔했다."], ["She did not know what to do, or what to think.", "그녀는 어찌해야 할지도, 무어라 여겨야 할지도 몰랐다."], ["There was wretchedness in the idea of its being serious; there was perplexity and agitation every way. She was distressed whenever Mr.", "그것이 진심이라는 생각엔 비참함이 있었고, 어느 모로 보나 당혹과 동요가 있었다."], ["Crawford spoke to her, and he spoke to her much too often; and she was afraid there was a something in his voice and manner in addressing her very different from what they were when he talked to the others.", "크로퍼드 씨가 그녀에게 말을 걸 때마다 그녀는 괴로웠는데, 그는 너무나 자주 그녀에게 말을 걸었다. 그리고 그녀는 그가 저에게 건네는 목소리와 태도에, 남들에게 말할 때와는 자못 다른 무언가가 있는 것 같아 두려웠다."], ["Her comfort in that day’s dinner was quite destroyed: she could hardly eat anything; and when Sir Thomas good-humouredly observed that joy had taken away her appetite, she was ready to sink with shame, from the dread of Mr.", "그날 만찬에서 그녀의 편안함은 아주 무너져 버렸다. 그녀는 무엇이든 거의 먹지 못했다."], ["Crawford’s interpretation; for though nothing could have tempted her to turn her eyes to the right hand, where he sat, she felt that his were immediately directed towards her.", "그리고 토머스 경이 기분 좋게, 기쁨이 그녀의 입맛을 앗아 갔구나 하고 말하니, 그녀는 크로퍼드 씨가 그것을 어찌 새길까 하는 두려움에 부끄러워 땅속으로 꺼질 것만 같았다. 무엇으로도 그녀는 그가 앉은 오른편으로 눈을 돌리도록 꾈 수 없었을망정, 그의 눈이 대번에 저를 향하고 있음을 느꼈던 것이다."]], [["She was more silent than ever.", "그녀는 여느 때보다 더 말이 없었다."], ["She would hardly join even when William was the subject, for his commission came all from the right hand too, and there was pain in the connexion.", "윌리엄이 화제일 때조차 그녀는 좀처럼 끼려 하지 않았으니, 그의 임관 또한 죄다 그 오른편에서 왔던 데다, 그 얽힘에는 아픔이 있었던 까닭이다."]], [["She thought Lady Bertram sat longer than ever, and began to be in despair of ever getting away; but at last they were in the drawing-room, and she was able to think as she would, while her aunts finished the subject of William’s appointment in their own style.", "그녀는 버트럼 부인이 여느 때보다 더 오래 앉아 있는 것 같아, 도무지 자리를 뜰 수 없으리라 절망하기 시작했다. 그러나 마침내 그들은 응접실에 있었고, 두 이모가 저희 나름의 방식으로 윌리엄의 임명이라는 화제를 마저 이어 가는 동안, 그녀는 제 뜻대로 생각할 수 있었다."]], [["Mrs. Norris seemed as much delighted with the saving it would be to Sir Thomas as with any part of it. “Now William would be able to keep himself, which would make a vast difference to his uncle, for it was unknown how much he had cost his uncle; and, indeed, it would make some difference in her presents too. She was very glad that she had given William what she did at parting, very glad, indeed, that it had been in her power, without material inconvenience, just at that time to give him something rather considerable; that is, for her, with her limited means, for now it would all be useful in helping to fit up his cabin. She knew he must be at some expense, that he would have many things to buy, though to be sure his father and mother would be able to put him in the way of getting everything very cheap; but she was very glad she had contributed her mite towards it.”", "노리스 부인은 그것이 토머스 경에게 안겨 줄 절약을, 그 무슨 대목 못지않게 반기는 듯했다. “이제 윌리엄이 제 앞가림을 할 수 있을 테니, 그것이 그 삼촌한텐 크나큰 차이가 될 거예요. 그 애가 삼촌한테 얼마나 돈을 쓰이게 했는지는 아무도 모르니까요. 그리고 정말이지, 그건 내 선물에도 얼마간 차이가 될 거예요. 나는 헤어질 때 윌리엄한테 그만큼 준 게 참 다행이에요. 정말이지, 하필 그때에 큰 불편 없이 제법 두둑한 것을 그 애한테 줄 형편이 됐던 게 참 다행이라니까요. 그러니까, 나 형편, 이 빠듯한 살림으로 치면 말이에요. 이제 그게 죄다 그 애 선실 차리는 데 보탬이 될 테니까요. 그 애가 얼마간 돈이 들 게 분명하고, 살 것도 많을 거예요. 하기야 그 애 부모야 뭐든 아주 싸게 살 길을 대 줄 수 있겠지만요. 아무튼 나는 거기에 내 몫의 조그만 보탬을 보탠 게 참 다행이에요.”"]], [["“I am glad you gave him something considerable,”", "“그 애한테 두둑한 걸 좀 준 게 다행이에요, 언니.”"], ["said Lady Bertram, with most unsuspicious calmness, “for I gave him only £10.”", "버트럼 부인이 조금도 의심 없는 차분함으로 말했다. “나는 그 애한테 10파운드밖에 안 줬거든요.”"]], [["“Indeed!”", "“어머!”"], ["cried Mrs. Norris, reddening.", "노리스 부인이 얼굴을 붉히며 외쳤다."], ["“Upon my word, he must have gone off with his pockets well lined, and at no expense for his journey to London either!”", "“원, 그럼 그 애는 주머니가 두둑한 채로 떠났겠네요, 게다가 런던 가는 여비도 안 들이고요!”"]], [["“Sir Thomas told me £10 would be enough.”", "“토머스 경이 10파운드면 넉넉하다고 하셨어요.”"]], [["Mrs. Norris, being not at all inclined to question its sufficiency, began to take the matter in another point.", "노리스 부인은 그것이 넉넉했는지를 따질 생각은 조금도 없던지라, 그 일을 다른 쪽으로 몰아 가기 시작했다."]], [["“It is amazing,”", "“놀라운 일이라니까요,”"], ["said she, “how much young people cost their friends, what with bringing them up and putting them out in the world!", "그녀가 말했다. “젊은 것들이 그 피붙이한테 돈이 얼마나 드는지, 길러 주고 세상에 내보내 주고 하느라 말이에요!"], ["They little think how much it comes to, or what their parents, or their uncles and aunts, pay for them in the course of the year.", "그것들은 그게 얼마나 되는지, 제 부모나 삼촌 이모가 한 해에 저희 몫으로 얼마를 치르는지 도통 생각을 안 해요."], ["Now, here are my sister Price’s children; take them all together, I dare say nobody would believe what a sum they cost Sir Thomas every year, to say nothing of what I do for them.”", "자, 여기 내 동생 프라이스네 아이들만 봐도, 죄다 합쳐 놓으면, 토머스 경이 해마다 그것들한테 얼마나 큰돈을 쓰는지 아무도 안 믿을걸요. 내가 그것들한테 해 주는 건 말할 것도 없고요.”"]], [["“Very true, sister, as you say.", "“지당한 말이에요, 언니, 언니 말대로예요."], ["But, poor things!", "하지만 딱한 것들이지!"], ["they cannot help it; and you know it makes very little difference to Sir Thomas.", "어쩔 수 없는 노릇이고요. 게다가 언니도 알다시피 토머스 경한텐 별 차이도 없잖아요."], ["Fanny, William must not forget my shawl if he goes to the East Indies; and I shall give him a commission for anything else that is worth having.", "패니, 윌리엄이 동인도에 가거든 내 숄을 잊으면 안 된다. 그리고 다른 살 만한 것이 있으면 그 애한테 부탁을 하나 주겠다."], ["I wish he may go to the East Indies, that I may have my shawl.", "나야 내 숄을 얻게 그 애가 동인도에 갔으면 싶구나."], ["I think I will have two shawls, Fanny.”", "아무래도 숄을 두 장 얻어야겠다, 패니.”"]], [["Fanny, meanwhile, speaking only when she could not help it, was very earnestly trying to understand what Mr. and Miss Crawford were at.", "한편 패니는, 어쩔 수 없을 때 말고는 말을 아끼며, 크로퍼드 씨와 크로퍼드 양이 무슨 속셈인지 몹시 애써 헤아려 보고 있었다."], ["There was everything in the world against their being serious but his words and manner.", "그의 말과 태도만 빼면, 그들이 진심일 리 없다는 것이 세상 모든 것이었다."], ["Everything natural, probable, reasonable, was against it; all their habits and ways of thinking, and all her own demerits.", "자연스럽고 있음 직하고 이치에 맞는 온갖 것이 그것에 어긋났다. 그들의 온갖 버릇과 사고방식이, 그리고 그녀 자신의 온갖 못남이."], ["How could she have excited serious attachment in a man who had seen so many, and been admired by so many, and flirted with so many, infinitely her superiors; who seemed so little open to serious impressions, even where pains had been taken to please him; who thought so slightly, so carelessly, so unfeelingly on all such points; who was everything to everybody, and seemed to find no one essential to him?", "그토록 많은 여자를 봐 왔고, 그토록 많은 여자에게 흠모받았고, 저보다 한없이 나은 그토록 많은 여자와 시시덕거려 온 사내가—애써 비위를 맞춰 준 데서조차 진지한 인상엔 그토록 무디어 보이던 사내가, 그런 온갖 점을 그토록 대수롭잖게, 그토록 무심하게, 그토록 몰인정하게 여기던 사내가, 뭇사람에게 온갖 것이면서 정작 저에게 없어선 안 될 이는 하나도 없어 보이던 사내가, 어떻게 저에게서 진지한 애착을 품었단 말인가?"], ["And farther, how could it be supposed that his sister, with all her high and worldly notions of matrimony, would be forwarding anything of a serious nature in such a quarter?", "게다가, 혼인에 대한 그 온갖 드높고 세속적인 소견을 지닌 그 누이가, 이런 데를 두고 무슨 진지한 일을 밀어붙이고 있으리라고 어찌 여길 수 있단 말인가?"], ["Nothing could be more unnatural in either.", "둘 중 누구에게든 이보다 더 부자연스러운 일은 없었다."], ["Fanny was ashamed of her own doubts.", "패니는 제 의심이 부끄러웠다."], ["Everything might be possible rather than serious attachment, or serious approbation of it toward her.", "진지한 애착이나 그에 대한 진지한 찬동이 저를 향한 것이라기보다는, 다른 무엇이든 다 그럴싸했다."], ["She had quite convinced herself of this before Sir Thomas and Mr. Crawford joined them.", "그녀는 토머스 경과 크로퍼드 씨가 그들과 합류하기 전에 이것을 아주 굳게 스스로 믿게 되었다."], ["The difficulty was in maintaining the conviction quite so absolutely after Mr.", "어려움은 크로퍼드 씨가 방에 든 뒤 그 확신을 그토록 온전히 지키는 데 있었다."], ["Crawford was in the room; for once or twice a look seemed forced on her which she did not know how to class among the common meaning; in any other man, at least, she would have said that it meant something very earnest, very pointed.", "한두 번, 여느 뜻으로 갈래 지을 줄 모를 어떤 눈길이 그녀에게 던져지는 것 같았던 것이다. 적어도 다른 어떤 사내였다면, 그것이 무언가 무척 진지하고 무척 노골적인 것을 뜻한다고 그녀는 말했으리라."], ["But she still tried to believe it no more than what he might often have expressed towards her cousins and fifty other women.", "그러나 그녀는 여전히 그것을, 그가 제 사촌들이나 다른 쉰 명의 여자에게 곧잘 내비쳤을 법한 것에 지나지 않는다 믿으려 애썼다."]], [["She thought he was wishing to speak to her unheard by the rest.", "그녀는 그가 남들 못 듣게 저에게 말하고 싶어 한다 여겼다."], ["She fancied he was trying for it the whole evening at intervals, whenever Sir Thomas was out of the room, or at all engaged with Mrs. Norris, and she carefully refused him every opportunity.", "그가 저녁 내내 틈틈이, 토머스 경이 방을 나가거나 노리스 부인과 무언가에 매여 있을 때면 언제고 그리하려 애쓴다 여겼고, 그녀는 그에게 그 기회를 하나하나 조심스레 안 주었다."]], [["At last—it seemed an at last to Fanny’s nervousness, though not remarkably late—he began to talk of going away; but the comfort of the sound was impaired by his turning to her the next moment, and saying, “Have you nothing to send to Mary?", "마침내—딱히 아주 늦은 것은 아니었으나 패니의 안절부절에는 마침내로 여겨졌다—그가 갈 얘기를 꺼내기 시작했다. 그러나 그 소리의 편안함은, 그가 다음 순간 그녀 쪽으로 돌아서서 이렇게 말하는 바람에 상하고 말았다."], ["No answer to her note?", "“메리한테 보낼 것 없으세요?"], ["She will be disappointed if she receives nothing from you.", "그 애 쪽지에 답이 없으세요? 그 애가 양한테서 아무것도 못 받으면 실망할 텐데요."], ["Pray write to her, if it be only a line.”", "부디 한 줄이라도 좋으니 그 애한테 써 주세요.”"]], [["“Oh yes!", "“아, 네!"], ["certainly,”", "그럼요.”"], ["cried Fanny, rising in haste, the haste of embarrassment and of wanting to get away—“I will write directly.”", "패니가 서둘러 일어나며 외쳤으니, 그것은 당혹함과 어서 벗어나고픈 마음의 서두름이었다. “당장 쓸게요.”"]], [["She went accordingly to the table, where she was in the habit of writing for her aunt, and prepared her materials without knowing what in the world to say.", "그리하여 그녀는, 이모를 위해 늘 글을 쓰던 그 탁자로 가서, 세상에 무어라 써야 할지도 모른 채 필기구를 마련했다. 그녀는 크로퍼드 양의 쪽지를 딱 한 번 읽었을 뿐이라, 그토록 어설프게밖에 못 헤아린 것에 어찌 답할지가 더없이 괴로웠다."], ["She had read Miss Crawford’s note only once, and how to reply to anything so imperfectly understood was most distressing.", "이런 쪽지 쓰기에 도무지 익지 않았던 터라, 문체를 두고 거리끼고 두려워할 겨를이 있었더라면 그런 마음을 잔뜩 느꼈으련만."], ["Quite unpractised in such sort of note-writing, had there been time for scruples and fears as to style she would have felt them in abundance: but something must be instantly written; and with only one decided feeling, that of wishing not to appear to think anything really intended, she wrote thus, in great trembling both of spirits and hand—", "그러나 무언가를 당장 써야 했다. 그리하여 오직 한 가지 굳은 심경, 곧 무언가 정말로 뜻한 것이 있다고 여기는 티를 안 내고 싶은 심경만을 안고, 그녀는 정신도 손도 몹시 떨며 이렇게 썼다—"]], [["“I am very much obliged to you, my dear Miss Crawford, for your kind congratulations, as far as they relate to my dearest William.", "“친절한 축하의 말씀, 그것이 사랑하는 윌리엄에 관한 것인 한, 크로퍼드 양, 저는 무척 감사드려요."], ["The rest of your note I know means nothing; but I am so unequal to anything of the sort, that I hope you will excuse my begging you to take no farther notice. I have seen too much of Mr.", "나머지 쪽지 말씀은 아무 뜻도 아닌 줄 압니다만, 저는 그런 것에 도무지 감당이 안 되는 사람이라, 부디 더는 마음 쓰지 말아 주시기를 청하는 저를 용서해 주시기 바라요."], ["Crawford not to understand his manners; if he understood me as well, he would, I dare say, behave differently.", "저는 크로퍼드 씨를 너무 많이 보아 그 태도를 모르지 않아요. 그이도 저를 그만큼 안다면, 감히 말하건대, 달리 처신하시겠지요."], ["I do not know what I write, but it would be a great favour of you never to mention the subject again.", "제가 무얼 쓰는지도 모르겠지만, 다시는 이 화제를 입에 올리지 말아 주시면 크나큰 은혜가 되겠어요."], ["With thanks for the honour of your note,", "쪽지의 영광에 감사드리며,"]], [["I remain, dear Miss Crawford, &c., &c.”", "그럼 이만 줄여요, 크로퍼드 양, 운운.”"]], [["The conclusion was scarcely intelligible from increasing fright, for she found that Mr. Crawford, under pretence of receiving the note, was coming towards her.", "그 맺음말은 점점 커지는 두려움에 좀처럼 알아볼 수도 없었으니, 크로퍼드 씨가 쪽지를 받는다는 핑계로 제 쪽으로 오고 있음을 알아챈 까닭이다."]], [["“You cannot think I mean to hurry you,”", "“제가 양을 재촉하려는 게 아닌 줄 아시겠지요.”"], ["said he, in an undervoice, perceiving the amazing trepidation with which she made up the note, “you cannot think I have any such object.", "그녀가 그 쪽지를 접으며 보이는 그 놀라운 떨림을 알아채고, 그가 나직한 소리로 말했다. “제게 그런 뜻이 있다곤 여기지 마세요."], ["Do not hurry yourself, I entreat.”", "서두르지 마시길 청해요.”"]], [["“Oh! I thank you; I have quite done, just done; it will be ready in a moment; I am very much obliged to you; if you will be so good as to give that to Miss Crawford.”", "“아! 고맙습니다. 저는 아주 다 됐어요, 방금 됐어요. 곧 준비될 거예요. 무척 감사드려요. 그럼, 이걸 크로퍼드 양께 좀 전해 주시겠어요.”"]], [["The note was held out, and must be taken; and as she instantly and with averted eyes walked towards the fireplace, where sat the others, he had nothing to do but to go in good earnest.", "쪽지가 내밀어졌으니, 받지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 그녀가 대번에 눈을 돌린 채 다른 이들이 앉은 난롯가 쪽으로 걸어가니, 그로서는 정말로 떠나는 것 말고는 달리 할 것이 없었다."]], [["Fanny thought she had never known a day of greater agitation, both of pain and pleasure; but happily the pleasure was not of a sort to die with the day; for every day would restore the knowledge of William’s advancement, whereas the pain, she hoped, would return no more.", "패니는, 아픔에서든 기쁨에서든 이보다 더 크게 마음 흔들린 날을 여태 겪어 본 적이 없다 싶었다. 그러나 다행히 그 기쁨은 하루와 함께 스러질 종류가 아니었으니, 날마다 윌리엄의 출세에 대한 앎이 되살아날 것이기 때문이었다."], ["She had no doubt that her note must appear excessively ill-written, that the language would disgrace a child, for her distress had allowed no arrangement; but at least it would assure them both of her being neither imposed on nor gratified by Mr.", "반면 그 아픔은, 그녀가 바라건대, 다시는 돌아오지 않을 것이었다. 그녀는 제 쪽지가 몹시 서투르게 쓰인 것으로 보이리라는 것, 그 말씨가 아이가 썼대도 부끄러울 지경이리라는 것을 의심치 않았다."], ["Crawford’s attentions.", "그녀의 괴로움이 도무지 다듬을 겨를을 안 준 까닭이다. 그러나 적어도 그것은, 저가 크로퍼드 씨의 마음씀에 속아 넘어간 것도 우쭐한 것도 아님을 두 사람 모두에게 확실히 일러 줄 터였다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXII", "# 제32장"]], [["Fanny had by no means forgotten Mr.", "이튿날 아침 잠에서 깼을 때 패니는 크로퍼드 씨를 결코 잊고 있지 않았다."], ["Crawford when she awoke the next morning; but she remembered the purport of her note, and was not less sanguine as to its effect than she had been the night before.", "다만 그녀는 제 쪽지의 취지를 떠올렸고, 그 효험에 대해 간밤 못지않게 낙관하고 있었다. 크로퍼드 씨가 그저 떠나 주기만 한다면!"], ["If Mr. Crawford would but go away!", "그것이 그녀가 더없이 간절히 바라는 것이었다."], ["That was what she most earnestly desired: go and take his sister with him, as he was to do, and as he returned to Mansfield on purpose to do.", "하기로 되어 있는 대로, 또 하려고 일부러 맨스필드에 돌아온 그대로, 떠나 주기를, 제 누이를 데리고 떠나 주기를."], ["And why it was not done already she could not devise, for Miss Crawford certainly wanted no delay.", "그리고 그것이 왜 아직 안 이뤄졌는지 그녀는 헤아릴 수가 없었으니, 크로퍼드 양이야 분명 지체를 조금도 바라지 않았던 것이다."], ["Fanny had hoped, in the course of his yesterday’s visit, to hear the day named; but he had only spoken of their journey as what would take place ere long.", "패니는 어제 그의 방문 중에 그 날짜가 정해지는 것을 듣기를 바랐다. 그러나 그는 그 여행을 그저 머잖아 있을 일로만 얘기했을 뿐이었다."]], [["Having so satisfactorily settled the conviction her note would convey, she could not but be astonished to see Mr. Crawford, as she accidentally did, coming up to the house again, and at an hour as early as the day before.", "제 쪽지가 전할 확신을 그토록 흡족하게 마무리 지어 놓은 터라, 그녀는 우연히 그리되었듯 크로퍼드 씨가 다시 저택 쪽으로, 게다가 어제만큼이나 이른 시각에 올라오는 것을 보고 놀라지 않을 수 없었다."], ["His coming might have nothing to do with her, but she must avoid seeing him if possible; and being then on her way upstairs, she resolved there to remain, during the whole of his visit, unless actually sent for; and as Mrs.", "그의 방문이야 저와 아무 상관 없을지도 몰랐으나, 되도록 그를 안 마주쳐야 했다. 그리하여 마침 위층으로 오르던 참이라, 그녀는 정말로 부름을 받지 않는 한 그의 방문 내내 거기 남아 있기로 마음먹었다."], ["Norris was still in the house, there seemed little danger of her being wanted.", "그리고 노리스 부인이 아직 집에 있던 터라, 저가 필요해질 위험은 별로 없어 보였다."]], [["She sat some time in a good deal of agitation, listening, trembling, and fearing to be sent for every moment; but as no footsteps approached the East room, she grew gradually composed, could sit down, and be able to employ herself, and able to hope that Mr.", "그녀는 한동안 자못 마음 졸이며, 귀 기울이고, 떨며, 시시각각 부름을 받을까 두려워하며 앉아 있었다."], ["Crawford had come and would go without her being obliged to know anything of the matter.", "그러나 동쪽 방으로 다가오는 발소리가 없자, 차츰 마음이 가라앉아, 자리에 앉을 수 있었고, 무언가에 마음을 쏟을 수 있었으며, 크로퍼드 씨가 왔다가 저는 이 일에 대해 아무것도 알 필요 없이 가 주기를 바랄 수 있게 되었다."]], [["Nearly half an hour had passed, and she was growing very comfortable, when suddenly the sound of a step in regular approach was heard; a heavy step, an unusual step in that part of the house: it was her uncle’s; she knew it as well as his voice; she had trembled at it as often, and began to tremble again, at the idea of his coming up to speak to her, whatever might be the subject. It was indeed Sir Thomas who opened the door and asked if she were there, and if he might come in. The terror of his former occasional visits to that room seemed all renewed, and she felt as if he were going to examine her again in French and English.", "거의 반 시간이 지나 그녀가 자못 편안해지던 참에, 문득 규칙적으로 다가오는 발소리가 들렸다. 무거운 발소리, 이 집의 이 자락에서는 예사롭지 않은 발소리였다. 삼촌의 것이었다. 그녀는 그 목소리만큼이나 그것을 잘 알았다. 그토록 자주 그 소리에 떨었던지라, 화제가 무엇이든 간에 그가 저에게 말하러 올라온다는 생각에 다시 떨기 시작했다. 과연 문을 열고 저가 거기 있느냐고, 들어가도 되겠느냐고 물은 것은 토머스 경이었다. 예전에 그가 이따금 그 방을 찾던 때의 그 두려움이 죄다 되살아나는 듯했고, 그녀는 그가 다시 저를 프랑스어와 영어로 시험하려는 것만 같았다."]], [["She was all attention, however, in placing a chair for him, and trying to appear honoured; and, in her agitation, had quite overlooked the deficiencies of her apartment, till he, stopping short as he entered, said, with much surprise, “Why have you no fire to-day?”", "그러나 그녀는, 그를 위해 의자를 놓고 영광스러워하는 티를 내려 온통 마음을 쏟느라, 그 동요 속에 제 방의 부족함을 아주 놓치고 있었다. 그가 들어서다 우뚝 멈춰, 자못 놀라며 “오늘은 왜 불을 안 땠느냐?” 하고 말하기까지는."]], [["There was snow on the ground, and she was sitting in a shawl.", "땅에는 눈이 있었고, 그녀는 숄을 걸치고 앉아 있었다."], ["She hesitated.", "그녀는 머뭇거렸다."]], [["“I am not cold, sir: I never sit here long at this time of year.”", "“춥지 않아요, 삼촌. 저는 이맘때면 여기 오래 안 앉아 있거든요.”"]], [["“But you have a fire in general?”", "“하지만 여느 때는 불을 때지?”"]], [["“No, sir.”", "“아뇨, 삼촌.”"]], [["“How comes this about?", "“어찌 된 영문이냐?"], ["Here must be some mistake.", "여기 무슨 착오가 있는 게 분명하다."], ["I understood that you had the use of this room by way of making you perfectly comfortable.", "나는 네가 아주 편안하게 지내라고 이 방을 쓰게 한 걸로 알고 있는데."], ["In your bedchamber I know you cannot have a fire.", "네 침실에야 불을 땔 수 없다는 걸 나도 안다."], ["Here is some great misapprehension which must be rectified.", "여기 무슨 큰 오해가 있으니 바로잡아야겠다."], ["It is highly unfit for you to sit, be it only half an hour a day, without a fire.", "하루 겨우 반 시간이라 해도, 네가 불도 없이 앉아 있다니 이건 도무지 온당치 않아."], ["You are not strong.", "너는 튼튼하지 못하다."], ["You are chilly.", "추위를 타지."], ["Your aunt cannot be aware of this.”", "네 이모가 이걸 모르고 있는 게 틀림없어.”"]], [["Fanny would rather have been silent; but being obliged to speak, she could not forbear, in justice to the aunt she loved best, from saying something in which the words “my aunt Norris”", "패니는 차라리 잠자코 있고 싶었다. 그러나 말을 하지 않을 수 없게 되자, 그녀는 저가 가장 사랑하는 이모를 위하는 마음에서, “노리스 이모”"], ["were distinguishable.", "하는 말이 알아들을 수 있게 섞인 무언가를 뇌지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“I understand,”", "“알겠다.”"], ["cried her uncle, recollecting himself, and not wanting to hear more: “I understand.", "삼촌이 정신을 차리고, 더는 듣고 싶지 않다는 듯 외쳤다. “알겠어."], ["Your aunt Norris has always been an advocate, and very judiciously, for young people’s being brought up without unnecessary indulgences; but there should be moderation in everything.", "네 노리스 이모는 늘, 그리고 자못 사려 깊게, 젊은 것들을 쓸데없는 응석 없이 기르자는 편이었지. 하지만 무엇에든 알맞음이라는 게 있어야 하는 법이다."], ["She is also very hardy herself, which of course will influence her in her opinion of the wants of others.", "게다가 그이 자신이 워낙 튼튼하니, 그것이 물론 남의 아쉬움을 헤아리는 그이의 소견에 영향을 미치겠지."], ["And on another account, too, I can perfectly comprehend.", "또 다른 까닭으로도, 나는 훤히 짐작이 간다."], ["I know what her sentiments have always been.", "나는 그이의 심경이 늘 어떠했는지 알아."], ["The principle was good in itself, but it may have been, and I believe has been, carried too far in your case.", "그 원칙 자체야 좋은 것이었으나, 네 경우엔 지나치게 밀어붙여졌을 수도 있고, 나는 정말 그리되었다고 믿는다."], ["I am aware that there has been sometimes, in some points, a misplaced distinction; but I think too well of you, Fanny, to suppose you will ever harbour resentment on that account.", "나도 이따금 어떤 점들에서 잘못 놓인 차별이 있어 왔음을 안다. 하지만 나는 너를 워낙 좋게 여기니, 얘야, 네가 그 때문에 언제고 원한을 품으리라곤 여기지 않는다."], ["You have an understanding which will prevent you from receiving things only in part, and judging partially by the event.", "너에게는, 무엇이든 그 일부만 받아들여 그 결말만으로 치우쳐 판단하는 것을 막아 줄 슬기가 있어."], ["You will take in the whole of the past, you will consider times, persons, and probabilities, and you will feel that they were not least your friends who were educating and preparing you for that mediocrity of condition which seemed to be your lot.", "너는 지난 일을 온전히 헤아려, 때와 사람과 있음 직함을 헤아릴 테고, 네 몫인 듯 보이던 그 어중간한 처지에 맞게 너를 가르치고 채비시킨 이들이야말로 네 벗 아닌 것이 결코 아니었음을 느낄 게다."], ["Though their caution may prove eventually unnecessary, it was kindly meant; and of this you may be assured, that every advantage of affluence will be doubled by the little privations and restrictions that may have been imposed.", "그들의 조심스러움이 끝내 쓸데없는 것으로 드러날지언정, 그것은 좋은 뜻에서 나온 것이었어. 그리고 이건 확신해도 좋다, 넉넉함의 온갖 이점은, 지워졌을지 모를 그 자잘한 결핍과 제약으로 곱절이 되리라는 걸."], ["I am sure you will not disappoint my opinion of you, by failing at any time to treat your aunt Norris with the respect and attention that are due to her.", "나는 네가 언제고 노리스 이모를 마땅한 존경과 마음씀으로 대하기를 저버려, 너에 대한 내 좋은 생각을 실망시키지 않으리라 믿는다."], ["But enough of this.", "하지만 이 얘긴 이만하자."], ["Sit down, my dear.", "앉거라, 얘야."], ["I must speak to you for a few minutes, but I will not detain you long.”", "몇 분 너하고 얘기해야겠다만, 오래 붙들진 않으마.”"]], [["Fanny obeyed, with eyes cast down and colour rising.", "패니는 눈을 내리깔고 낯빛을 붉히며 따랐다."], ["After a moment’s pause, Sir Thomas, trying to suppress a smile, went on.", "잠깐 뜸을 들인 뒤, 토머스 경은 미소를 누르려 애쓰며 말을 이었다."]], [["“You are not aware, perhaps, that I have had a visitor this morning.", "“너는 아마 모르겠지만, 오늘 아침 나한테 손님이 왔었다."], ["I had not been long in my own room, after breakfast, when Mr. Crawford was shewn in.", "아침을 먹고 내 방에 든 지 얼마 안 되어 크로퍼드 씨가 안내되어 들어왔지."], ["His errand you may probably conjecture.”", "그가 온 볼일이야 너도 아마 짐작할 게다.”"]], [["Fanny’s colour grew deeper and deeper; and her uncle, perceiving that she was embarrassed to a degree that made either speaking or looking up quite impossible, turned away his own eyes, and without any farther pause proceeded in his account of Mr.", "패니의 낯빛이 점점 더 짙어졌다."], ["Crawford’s visit.", "그리고 삼촌은, 그녀가 말하는 것도 고개를 드는 것도 도무지 못 할 만큼 얼떨떨해하는 것을 알아채고, 제 눈길을 돌려, 더는 뜸 들이지 않고 크로퍼드 씨의 방문에 대한 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["Mr. Crawford’s business had been to declare himself the lover of Fanny, make decided proposals for her, and entreat the sanction of the uncle, who seemed to stand in the place of her parents; and he had done it all so well, so openly, so liberally, so properly, that Sir Thomas, feeling, moreover, his own replies, and his own remarks to have been very much to the purpose, was exceedingly happy to give the particulars of their conversation; and little aware of what was passing in his niece’s mind, conceived that by such details he must be gratifying her far more than himself.", "크로퍼드 씨의 볼일은, 저가 패니를 사랑하는 이임을 밝히고, 그녀에게 뚜렷한 청혼을 하고, 그녀 부모를 대신하는 듯한 삼촌의 허락을 청하는 것이었다."], ["He talked, therefore, for several minutes without Fanny’s daring to interrupt him.", "그리고 그가 그 모두를 어찌나 훌륭하게, 어찌나 터놓고, 어찌나 넉넉하게, 어찌나 온당하게 해냈던지, 게다가 제 대답과 제 소감이 자못 요긴했다고 느낀 토머스 경은, 그들 대화의 시시콜콜한 대목을 들려주는 것이 더없이 기뻤다. 그리고 조카딸의 마음속에 무엇이 오가는지는 거의 알지 못한 채, 이런 낱낱의 이야기로 저 자신보다 그녀를 한결 더 흐뭇하게 해 주고 있으려니 여겼다."], ["She had hardly even attained the wish to do it.", "그리하여 그는 여러 분 동안 이야기했고, 패니는 감히 그를 가로막지 못했다. 그녀는 그러고픈 바람에조차 좀처럼 이르지 못했다."], ["Her mind was in too much confusion.", "그녀의 마음은 너무 얼떨떨했던 것이다."], ["She had changed her position; and, with her eyes fixed intently on one of the windows, was listening to her uncle in the utmost perturbation and dismay.", "그녀는 자세를 바꾸어, 두 눈을 창문 하나에 골똘히 박은 채, 더없는 심란함과 낙담 속에 삼촌의 말을 듣고 있었다. 한순간 그가 말을 멈췄으나, 그녀가 그것을 미처 알아채기도 전에, 그는 의자에서 일어나며 말했다."], ["For a moment he ceased, but she had barely become conscious of it, when, rising from his chair, he said, “And now, Fanny, having performed one part of my commission, and shewn you everything placed on a basis the most assured and satisfactory, I may execute the remainder by prevailing on you to accompany me downstairs, where, though I cannot but presume on having been no unacceptable companion myself, I must submit to your finding one still better worth listening to.", "“자, 이제, 얘야, 내 소임의 한몫을 다하고 온갖 것을 더없이 확실하고 흡족한 바탕 위에 놓아 보여 주었으니, 나머지 몫은 너를 데리고 아래층으로 내려감으로써 다하마. 하기야 나 자신이 달갑잖은 말벗은 아니었으려니 여기지 않을 수 없다만, 아래층엔 귀 기울일 값어치가 더한 이가 있음을 나도 받아들여야겠지."], ["Mr. Crawford, as you have perhaps foreseen, is yet in the house.", "크로퍼드 씨가, 너도 아마 짐작했겠지만, 아직 집에 있단다."], ["He is in my room, and hoping to see you there.”", "그는 내 방에 있으면서, 거기서 너를 보기를 바라고 있어.”"]], [["There was a look, a start, an exclamation on hearing this, which astonished Sir Thomas; but what was his increase of astonishment on hearing her exclaim—“Oh!", "이 말을 듣자 어떤 낯빛, 어떤 흠칫함, 어떤 외침이 일어 토머스 경을 놀라게 했다. 그러나 그녀가 이렇게 외치는 것을 들었을 때 그 놀라움이 얼마나 커졌으랴—“아!"], ["no, sir, I cannot, indeed I cannot go down to him.", "안 돼요, 삼촌. 저는 못 해요, 정말이지 그이한테 내려갈 수 없어요."], ["Mr.", "크로퍼드 씨도 알아야 해요—틀림없이 알 거예요."], ["Crawford ought to know—he must know that: I told him enough yesterday to convince him; he spoke to me on this subject yesterday, and I told him without disguise that it was very disagreeable to me, and quite out of my power to return his good opinion.”", "어제 저는 그이를 납득시킬 만큼 넉넉히 말했거든요. 그이가 어제 이 얘기를 저한테 꺼냈을 때, 저는 그게 제게 무척 언짢고, 그이의 호의에 화답하는 건 제 힘에 도무지 벅찬 일이라고 숨김없이 말했어요.”"]], [["“I do not catch your meaning,”", "“무슨 말인지 못 알아듣겠구나.”"], ["said Sir Thomas, sitting down again.", "토머스 경이 다시 앉으며 말했다."], ["“Out of your power to return his good opinion?", "“그의 호의에 화답하는 게 네 힘에 벅차다니?"], ["What is all this?", "이게 다 무슨 소리냐?"], ["I know he spoke to you yesterday, and (as far as I understand) received as much encouragement to proceed as a well-judging young woman could permit herself to give.", "나는 그가 어제 너한테 말했고, (내가 알기로는) 사려 깊은 젊은 여자가 스스로 허락할 만한 만큼의 격려를 받아 나아갔다고 알고 있다."], ["I was very much pleased with what I collected to have been your behaviour on the occasion; it shewed a discretion highly to be commended.", "나는 그 자리에서 네가 어찌 처신했다는 것을 전해 듣고 무척 흐뭇했어. 그건 크게 기릴 만한 신중함을 보여 준 것이었지."], ["But now, when he has made his overtures so properly, and honourably—what are your scruples now?”", "그런데 이제, 그가 그토록 온당하고 떳떳하게 그 뜻을 밝힌 마당에—이제 와서 무슨 거리낌이란 말이냐?”"]], [["“You are mistaken, sir,”", "“오해하셨어요, 삼촌.”"], ["cried Fanny, forced by the anxiety of the moment even to tell her uncle that he was wrong; “you are quite mistaken.", "패니가, 그 순간의 애타는 마음에 못 이겨 삼촌이 틀렸다는 말까지 하며 외쳤다. “아주 오해하셨어요."], ["How could Mr. Crawford say such a thing?", "크로퍼드 씨가 어떻게 그런 말을 할 수 있죠?"], ["I gave him no encouragement yesterday.", "저는 어제 그이한테 아무 격려도 안 했어요."], ["On the contrary, I told him, I cannot recollect my exact words, but I am sure I told him that I would not listen to him, that it was very unpleasant to me in every respect, and that I begged him never to talk to me in that manner again.", "오히려 저는, 정확한 말은 떠오르지 않지만, 그이 말을 듣지 않겠다고, 그건 어느 모로 보나 제게 무척 언짢다고, 다시는 그런 식으로 제게 말하지 말아 달라 청한다고 분명 말했어요. 분명 그만큼, 아니 그 이상 말했어요."], ["I am sure I said as much as that and more; and I should have said still more, if I had been quite certain of his meaning anything seriously; but I did not like to be, I could not bear to be, imputing more than might be intended.", "그리고 그이가 무언가 진지하게 뜻하는 게 확실하기만 했다면 더 말했을 거예요. 하지만 저는 뜻하지 않았을지 모를 것을 넘겨짚는 게 싫었고, 도무지 못 할 짓이었어요."], ["I thought it might all pass for nothing with him.”", "저는 이게 죄다 그이한테는 아무것도 아닌 일로 넘어가려니 여겼거든요.”"]], [["She could say no more; her breath was almost gone.", "그녀는 더 말할 수 없었다. 숨이 거의 다한 것이다."]], [["“Am I to understand,”", "“그러니까,” 잠깐 잠잠하던 토머스 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Thomas, after a few moments’ silence, “that you mean to refuse Mr. Crawford?”", "“네가 크로퍼드 씨를 마다하겠다는 뜻으로 알아들으면 되겠느냐?”"]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "“네, 삼촌.”"]], [["“Refuse him?”", "“그를 마다한다고?”"]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "“네, 삼촌.”"]], [["“Refuse Mr. Crawford!", "“크로퍼드 씨를 마다한다니!"], ["Upon what plea?", "무슨 구실로?"], ["For what reason?”", "무슨 까닭으로?”"]], [["“I—I cannot like him, sir, well enough to marry him.”", "“저는—저는 그이를, 혼인할 만큼 좋아할 수가 없어요, 삼촌.”"]], [["“This is very strange!”", "“이건 참 이상하구나!”"], ["said Sir Thomas, in a voice of calm displeasure.", "토머스 경이 차분한 언짢음의 목소리로 말했다."], ["“There is something in this which my comprehension does not reach.", "“여기엔 내 헤아림이 미치지 못하는 무언가가 있어."], ["Here is a young man wishing to pay his addresses to you, with everything to recommend him: not merely situation in life, fortune, and character, but with more than common agreeableness, with address and conversation pleasing to everybody.", "여기 한 젊은이가 있어 너에게 구애하고 싶어 하는데, 그를 천거할 온갖 것을 다 갖췄지. 삶의 처지며 재산이며 사람됨뿐 아니라, 여느 이상의 서글서글함에, 뭇사람의 마음에 드는 언변과 말솜씨까지."], ["And he is not an acquaintance of to-day; you have now known him some time.", "게다가 그는 오늘 처음 안 사이도 아니야. 너도 이제 그를 얼마간 알아 왔지."], ["His sister, moreover, is your intimate friend, and he has been doing that for your brother, which I should suppose would have been almost sufficient recommendation to you, had there been no other.", "더구나 그 누이는 네 절친한 벗이고, 그는 네 오라비를 위해 그런 일을 해 주었어. 다른 게 아무것도 없었다 해도 너에게는 거의 넉넉한 천거가 되었으려니 나는 여기는데."], ["It is very uncertain when my interest might have got William on.", "내 힘으로 윌리엄을 밀어 주기까지야 언제일지 자못 아득했다."], ["He has done it already.”", "그가 벌써 그걸 해낸 거야.”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“네.” 패니가 새삼 부끄러워 눈을 내리깔며 가냘픈 목소리로 말했다."], ["said Fanny, in a faint voice, and looking down with fresh shame; and she did feel almost ashamed of herself, after such a picture as her uncle had drawn, for not liking Mr. Crawford.", "그리고 삼촌이 그려 보인 그런 그림 뒤에, 크로퍼드 씨를 안 좋아한다는 것이 그녀는 정말이지 저 자신이 거의 부끄러웠다."]], [["“You must have been aware,”", "“너도 알고 있었을 게다,”"], ["continued Sir Thomas presently, “you must have been some time aware of a particularity in Mr.", "토머스 경이 이내 말을 이었다. “너에 대한 크로퍼드 씨의 태도에 어떤 각별함이 있음을 얼마 전부터 알고 있었을 게야."], ["Crawford’s manners to you.", "이게 너를 놀라게 했을 리 없어."], ["This cannot have taken you by surprise.", "너도 그의 마음씀을 눈여겨봤을 테지."], ["You must have observed his attentions; and though you always received them very properly (I have no accusation to make on that head), I never perceived them to be unpleasant to you.", "그리고 네가 늘 그것을 무척 온당하게 받아들이긴 했으나(그 점에 대해선 나도 나무랄 게 없다), 나는 그것이 너에게 언짢은 것이라곤 한 번도 못 느꼈어."], ["I am half inclined to think, Fanny, that you do not quite know your own feelings.”", "나는 아무래도, 얘야, 네가 네 심경을 잘 모르는 게 아닌가 싶기도 하구나.”"]], [["“Oh yes, sir!", "“아, 아녜요, 삼촌!"], ["indeed I do.", "정말이지 저는 알아요."], ["His attentions were always—what I did not like.”", "그이의 마음씀은 늘—제가 싫어하던 것이었어요.”"]], [["Sir Thomas looked at her with deeper surprise.", "토머스 경은 한결 깊은 놀라움으로 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["“This is beyond me,” said he.", "“이건 내 헤아림을 넘어서는구나.”"], ["“This requires explanation.", "그가 말했다."], ["Young as you are, and having seen scarcely any one, it is hardly possible that your affections—”", "“이건 설명이 있어야겠다. 너처럼 어리고, 좀처럼 아무도 만나 본 적이 없는 처지에, 네 정이—”"]], [["He paused and eyed her fixedly.", "그는 말을 멈추고 그녀를 뚫어져라 바라보았다."], ["He saw her lips formed into a no, though the sound was inarticulate, but her face was like scarlet.", "소리는 또렷하지 않았으나 그녀의 입술이 아니라는 모양을 짓는 것이 보였는데, 그 얼굴은 진홍빛 같았다. 그러나 그것이, 이토록 얌전한 처녀에게라면 순진함과도 얼마든지 어울릴 만했다."], ["That, however, in so modest a girl, might be very compatible with innocence; and chusing at least to appear satisfied, he quickly added, “No, no, I know that is quite out of the question; quite impossible.", "그리하여 적어도 흡족한 척이라도 하기로 마음먹고, 그는 얼른 덧붙였다. “아니지, 아니야, 그건 아주 있을 수 없는 일임을 나도 안다."], ["Well, there is nothing more to be said.”", "아주 어림없는 일이지. 자, 더 할 말이 없구나.”"]], [["And for a few minutes he did say nothing.", "그리고 몇 분간 그는 정말 아무 말도 안 했다."], ["He was deep in thought.", "그는 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["His niece was deep in thought likewise, trying to harden and prepare herself against farther questioning.", "조카딸 역시 골똘히 생각에 잠겨, 더 캐물음에 맞서 저를 다잡고 채비시키려 애쓰고 있었다."], ["She would rather die than own the truth; and she hoped, by a little reflection, to fortify herself beyond betraying it.", "그녀는 그 진실을 털어놓느니 차라리 죽는 편이 나았다. 그리고 조금 곰곰 생각해 그것을 발설하지 않도록 저를 든든히 하기를 바랐다."]], [["“Independently of the interest which Mr. Crawford’s choice seemed to justify”", "“크로퍼드 씨의 선택이 정당해 보이게 하는 그 관심과는 별개로,”"], ["said Sir Thomas, beginning again, and very composedly, “his wishing to marry at all so early is recommendatory to me.", "토머스 경이 다시, 아주 차분하게 말문을 열었다. “그가 이토록 일찍 혼인하고 싶어 한다는 것 자체가 내겐 천거할 만한 점이다."], ["I am an advocate for early marriages, where there are means in proportion, and would have every young man, with a sufficient income, settle as soon after four-and-twenty as he can.", "나는 형편이 그에 걸맞은 데서라면 이른 혼인을 지지하는 사람이라, 넉넉한 수입이 있는 젊은이라면 누구든 스물넷을 넘기고 되도록 빨리 자리 잡기를 바란다."], ["This is so much my opinion, that I am sorry to think how little likely my own eldest son, your cousin, Mr.", "이게 워낙 내 소견인지라, 내 맏아들, 네 사촌 버트럼 씨가 일찍 혼인할 성싶지 않다는 걸 생각하면 나는 서운하구나."], ["Bertram, is to marry early; but at present, as far as I can judge, matrimony makes no part of his plans or thoughts.", "하지만 지금으로선, 내가 판단할 수 있는 한, 혼인은 그의 계획에도 생각에도 없어."], ["I wish he were more likely to fix.”", "나는 그가 좀 더 자리 잡을 성싶으면 싶다.”"], ["Here was a glance at Fanny.", "여기서 패니를 슬쩍 보았다."], ["“Edmund, I consider, from his dispositions and habits, as much more likely to marry early than his brother.", "“에드먼드는, 내가 보기에, 그 성미와 버릇으로 미루어 제 형보다 훨씬 일찍 혼인할 성싶다."], ["He, indeed, I have lately thought, has seen the woman he could love, which, I am convinced, my eldest son has not.", "그 아이는, 정말이지, 근래 내 생각엔, 사랑할 만한 여자를 본 듯한데, 내 맏아들은 그러지 못했다고 나는 믿는다."], ["Am I right?", "내 생각이 옳으냐?"], ["Do you agree with me, my dear?”", "너도 나랑 같은 생각이냐, 얘야?”"]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "“네, 삼촌.”"]], [["It was gently, but it was calmly said, and Sir Thomas was easy on the score of the cousins.", "그것은 나직했으나 차분히 뇐 말이었고, 토머스 경은 그 사촌들 문제에 관해서는 마음이 놓였다. 그러나 그 불안이 걷혔다 해서 조카딸에게 이로울 것은 없었다."], ["But the removal of his alarm did his niece no service: as her unaccountableness was confirmed his displeasure increased; and getting up and walking about the room with a frown, which Fanny could picture to herself, though she dared not lift up her eyes, he shortly afterwards, and in a voice of authority, said, “Have you any reason, child, to think ill of Mr.", "그녀의 도무지 알 수 없음이 굳어질수록 그의 언짢음은 커졌던 것이다. 그리하여 일어나, 패니가 감히 눈을 들지는 못해도 능히 떠올릴 수 있는 그 찌푸린 얼굴로 방 안을 거닐다가, 이내 위엄 있는 목소리로 그는 말했다."], ["Crawford’s temper?”", "“얘야, 너는 크로퍼드 씨의 성미를 나쁘게 여길 만한 무슨 까닭이라도 있느냐?”"]], [["“No, sir.”", "“아뇨, 삼촌.”"]], [["She longed to add, “But of his principles I have”; but her heart sunk under the appalling prospect of discussion, explanation, and probably non-conviction.", "그녀는 “하지만 그이의 지조는 나쁘게 여겨요” 하고 덧붙이고 싶어 못 견뎠다."], ["Her ill opinion of him was founded chiefly on observations, which, for her cousins’ sake, she could scarcely dare mention to their father.", "그러나 그 논의와 설명과, 아마도 끝내 납득 못 시킴이라는 그 소름 끼치는 전망에 그녀의 가슴이 내려앉았다. 그에 대한 그녀의 나쁜 생각은 주로 눈으로 본 것들에 바탕한 것이었는데, 그것은 사촌들을 위해서라도 그들 아버지에게 감히 입에 올릴 수 없는 것이었다."], ["Maria and Julia, and especially Maria, were so closely implicated in Mr. Crawford’s misconduct, that she could not give his character, such as she believed it, without betraying them.", "마리아와 줄리아가, 특히 마리아가 크로퍼드 씨의 그릇된 처신에 어찌나 깊이 얽혀 있던지, 그녀는 그들을 팔지 않고서는 제가 믿는 그대로의 그의 사람됨을 밝힐 수가 없었다."], ["She had hoped that, to a man like her uncle, so discerning, so honourable, so good, the simple acknowledgment of settled dislike on her side would have been sufficient.", "그녀는, 삼촌처럼 그토록 눈 밝고, 그토록 떳떳하고, 그토록 좋은 사람에게라면, 제 편에서 굳어진 싫음을 그저 밝히는 것만으로 넉넉하려니 바랐다."], ["To her infinite grief she found it was not.", "그런데 그렇지 않음을 알게 되어 이루 말할 수 없이 서러웠다."]], [["Sir Thomas came towards the table where she sat in trembling wretchedness, and with a good deal of cold sternness, said, “It is of no use, I perceive, to talk to you.", "토머스 경은 그녀가 떨리는 비참함 속에 앉아 있는 탁자 쪽으로 다가와, 자못 싸늘한 엄함으로 말했다. “너한테 말해 봐야 소용없구나, 알겠다."], ["We had better put an end to this most mortifying conference.", "이 더없이 무안한 대면은 그만 끝내는 게 낫겠다."], ["Mr. Crawford must not be kept longer waiting.", "크로퍼드 씨를 더 오래 기다리게 해선 안 되니까."], ["I will, therefore, only add, as thinking it my duty to mark my opinion of your conduct, that you have disappointed every expectation I had formed, and proved yourself of a character the very reverse of what I had supposed.", "그러니 나는, 네 처신에 대한 내 생각을 밝히는 게 내 도리라 여겨, 이 말만 덧붙이마. 너는 내가 품었던 온갖 기대를 저버렸고, 내가 짐작한 것과는 정반대인 사람임을 스스로 드러냈다."], ["For I had, Fanny, as I think my behaviour must have shewn, formed a very favourable opinion of you from the period of my return to England.", "나는 정말이지, 얘야, 내 처신이 보여 주었을 줄 안다만, 잉글랜드에 돌아온 그 무렵부터 너를 자못 좋게 여겨 왔거든."], ["I had thought you peculiarly free from wilfulness of temper, self-conceit, and every tendency to that independence of spirit which prevails so much in modern days, even in young women, and which in young women is offensive and disgusting beyond all common offence.", "나는 네가 성미의 고집스러움에서도, 자만에서도, 요즘 세상에 그토록 판을 치는, 젊은 여자에게조차 판을 치는, 그리고 젊은 여자에게 있으면 여느 거슬림을 넘어 거슬리고 역겨운 그런 방자한 기질에서도 유난히 벗어나 있다 여겨 왔어."], ["But you have now shewn me that you can be wilful and perverse; that you can and will decide for yourself, without any consideration or deference for those who have surely some right to guide you, without even asking their advice.", "그런데 너는 이제, 네가 고집스럽고 비뚤어질 수 있음을, 너를 이끌 얼마간의 권리가 분명 있는 이들에 대한 아무런 헤아림도 존중도 없이, 아니 그들 조언을 구하지조차 않고서, 네 스스로 정할 수 있고 또 정하려 든다는 걸 내게 보여 주었다."], ["You have shewn yourself very, very different from anything that I had imagined.", "너는 내가 그려 본 온갖 것과는 아주, 아주 다른 사람임을 스스로 드러낸 거야."], ["The advantage or disadvantage of your family, of your parents, your brothers and sisters, never seems to have had a moment’s share in your thoughts on this occasion.", "네 집안의, 네 부모와 형제자매의 이로움이나 해로움은, 이번 일에 네 생각 속에 한순간의 몫도 차지한 적이 없어 보인다."], ["How they might be benefited, how they must rejoice in such an establishment for you, is nothing to you.", "그들이 어찌 덕을 볼지, 그들이 너의 이런 자리 잡음을 얼마나 반길지는 너한테는 아무것도 아닌 거야."], ["You think only of yourself, and because you do not feel for Mr.", "너는 오직 너 자신만 생각하지."], ["Crawford exactly what a young heated fancy imagines to be necessary for happiness, you resolve to refuse him at once, without wishing even for a little time to consider of it, a little more time for cool consideration, and for really examining your own inclinations; and are, in a wild fit of folly, throwing away from you such an opportunity of being settled in life, eligibly, honourably, nobly settled, as will, probably, never occur to you again.", "그리고 젊은 달뜬 공상이 행복에 꼭 있어야 한다고 여기는 바로 그것을 크로퍼드 씨에게 못 느낀다 해서, 잠깐 생각해 볼 겨를조차, 차분히 헤아리고 네 마음을 정말로 살펴볼 겨를조차 바라지 않고 대뜸 그를 마다하기로 마음먹었어. 그리하여 어리석음의 사나운 발작 속에, 삶에서 자리 잡을—번듯하게, 떳떳하게, 훌륭하게 자리 잡을 그런 기회를, 아마 다시는 네게 오지 않을 그런 기회를 스스로 내던지고 있는 거다."], ["Here is a young man of sense, of character, of temper, of manners, and of fortune, exceedingly attached to you, and seeking your hand in the most handsome and disinterested way; and let me tell you, Fanny, that you may live eighteen years longer in the world without being addressed by a man of half Mr.", "여기 지각과 사람됨과 성미와 몸가짐과 재산을 갖춘 젊은이가 있어, 너에게 몹시 애착하며 더없이 훤칠하고 사심 없는 방식으로 네 손을 청하고 있어. 그리고 얘야, 내 너한테 이르거니와, 너는 세상에서 열여덟 해를 더 살아도 크로퍼드 씨 재산의 절반, 그 미덕의 십분의 일을 가진 사내한테서도 구애받지 못할 수 있어."], ["Crawford’s estate, or a tenth part of his merits. Gladly would I have bestowed either of my own daughters on him.", "나야 기꺼이 내 두 딸 가운데 누구라도 그에게 주었을 게다."], ["Maria is nobly married; but had Mr. Crawford sought Julia’s hand, I should have given it to him with superior and more heartfelt satisfaction than I gave Maria’s to Mr.", "마리아는 번듯하게 시집갔다만, 크로퍼드 씨가 줄리아의 손을 청했더라면, 나는 마리아를 러시워스 씨에게 준 것보다 더 크고 더 진심 어린 흡족함으로 줄리아를 그에게 주었을 거야.”"], ["Rushworth.”", "잠깐 뜸을 들인 뒤."], ["After half a moment’s pause: “And I should have been very much surprised had either of my daughters, on receiving a proposal of marriage at any time which might carry with it only half the eligibility of this, immediately and peremptorily, and without paying my opinion or my regard the compliment of any consultation, put a decided negative on it.", "“그리고 내 딸 가운데 누구라도, 언제고 이것의 절반의 걸맞음밖에 못 지닌 청혼을 받고서, 내 생각이나 내 뜻에 의논 한마디의 예도 갖추지 않고 대뜸, 딱 잘라 그것을 마다했더라면, 나는 무척 놀랐을 거야."], ["I should have been much surprised and much hurt by such a proceeding.", "나는 그런 처사에 무척 놀라고 무척 상했을 게다."], ["I should have thought it a gross violation of duty and respect.", "나는 그것을 도리와 존중을 몹시 저버린 것으로 여겼을 거야."], ["You are not to be judged by the same rule.", "너야 같은 잣대로 잴 건 아니다."], ["You do not owe me the duty of a child.", "너는 나한테 자식으로서의 도리를 진 건 아니니까."], ["But, Fanny, if your heart can acquit you of ingratitude—”", "하지만, 얘야, 네 마음이 너를 배은망덕에서 놓아줄 수 있다면—”"]], [["He ceased.", "그는 말을 그쳤다."], ["Fanny was by this time crying so bitterly that, angry as he was, he would not press that article farther.", "이때쯤 패니가 어찌나 서럽게 울던지, 그는 아무리 화가 났어도 그 대목을 더 밀어붙이려 하지 않았다."], ["Her heart was almost broke by such a picture of what she appeared to him; by such accusations, so heavy, so multiplied, so rising in dreadful gradation!", "그녀의 가슴은, 저가 그에게 어떻게 비쳤는가 하는 그런 그림에, 그토록 무겁고, 그토록 겹겹이 쌓이고, 그토록 소름 끼치는 층층으로 높아 가는 그런 나무람에 거의 무너져 버렸다!"], ["Self-willed, obstinate, selfish, and ungrateful.", "제멋대로에, 고집스럽고, 이기적이고, 배은망덕하다니."], ["He thought her all this.", "그가 그녀를 이 모두로 여긴 것이다."], ["She had deceived his expectations; she had lost his good opinion.", "그녀는 그의 기대를 저버렸다. 그녀는 그의 좋은 생각을 잃었다."], ["What was to become of her?", "그녀는 앞으로 어찌 될 것인가?"]], [["“I am very sorry,”", "“정말 죄송해요.”"], ["said she inarticulately, through her tears, “I am very sorry indeed.”", "그녀가 눈물 사이로 더듬거리며 말했다. “정말이지 너무 죄송해요.”"]], [["“Sorry!", "“죄송하다고!"], ["yes, I hope you are sorry; and you will probably have reason to be long sorry for this day’s transactions.”", "그래, 죄송하기를 바란다. 그리고 너는 아마 오늘 벌어진 일을 두고 오래도록 죄송해할 까닭을 갖게 되겠지.”"]], [["“If it were possible for me to do otherwise”", "“제가 달리 어찌할 수만 있다면요.”"], ["said she, with another strong effort; “but I am so perfectly convinced that I could never make him happy, and that I should be miserable myself.”", "그녀가 또 한 번 안간힘을 쓰며 말했다. “하지만 저는 결코 그이를 행복하게 못 하리라는 것과, 저 자신이 비참해지리라는 것을 너무나 온전히 확신해서요.”"]], [["Another burst of tears; but in spite of that burst, and in spite of that great black word miserable, which served to introduce it, Sir Thomas began to think a little relenting, a little change of inclination, might have something to do with it; and to augur favourably from the personal entreaty of the young man himself.", "또 한 번 눈물이 터졌다. 그러나 그 터짐에도 불구하고, 또 그 터짐의 서두가 된 그 크고 시커먼 낱말 비참에도 불구하고, 토머스 경은 어쩌면 얼마간의 누그러짐, 얼마간의 마음이 돌아섬이 여기 얽혀 있을지도 모른다고, 그리고 그 젊은이 자신의 직접적인 간청에서 좋은 조짐을 점칠 만하다고 여기기 시작했다."], ["He knew her to be very timid, and exceedingly nervous; and thought it not improbable that her mind might be in such a state as a little time, a little pressing, a little patience, and a little impatience, a judicious mixture of all on the lover’s side, might work their usual effect on.", "그는 그녀가 무척 소심하고 몹시 예민함을 알았다. 그리하여 그녀의 마음이, 조금의 겨를과, 조금의 조름과, 조금의 참을성과, 조금의 조바심—사랑하는 이의 편에서 이 모두를 사려 깊게 뒤섞으면 늘 그렇듯 효험을 낼 만한 그런 상태에 있을지도 모른다 여겼다."], ["If the gentleman would but persevere, if he had but love enough to persevere, Sir Thomas began to have hopes; and these reflections having passed across his mind and cheered it, “Well,”", "그 신사가 그저 물러서지만 않는다면, 물러서지 않을 만큼의 사랑만 그에게 있다면, 토머스 경은 바람이 생기기 시작했다. 그리고 이런 생각이 마음을 스쳐 그것을 밝혀 준 터라, “자,”"], ["said he, in a tone of becoming gravity, but of less anger, “well, child, dry up your tears.", "하고 그는 걸맞은 근엄함이되 노여움은 덜한 투로 말했다. “자, 얘야, 눈물을 거두어라."], ["There is no use in these tears; they can do no good.", "이런 눈물은 소용없다. 아무 도움도 못 돼."], ["You must now come downstairs with me. Mr.", "이제 너는 나랑 아래층으로 내려가야 한다."], ["Crawford has been kept waiting too long already.", "크로퍼드 씨를 벌써 너무 오래 기다리게 했어. 너는 그에게 네 답을 몸소 주어야 해."], ["You must give him your own answer: we cannot expect him to be satisfied with less; and you only can explain to him the grounds of that misconception of your sentiments, which, unfortunately for himself, he certainly has imbibed.", "그가 그보다 못한 것으로 흡족해하기를 바랄 수야 없으니까. 그리고 오직 너만이, 그가 딱하게도 분명 품어 버린 네 심경에 대한 그 오해의 근거를 그에게 밝혀 줄 수 있어."], ["I am totally unequal to it.”", "나는 도무지 그럴 깜냥이 못 돼.”"]], [["But Fanny shewed such reluctance, such misery, at the idea of going down to him, that Sir Thomas, after a little consideration, judged it better to indulge her.", "그러나 그에게 내려간다는 생각에 패니가 어찌나 꺼리는 기색, 어찌나 비참한 기색을 보이던지, 토머스 경은 잠깐 헤아린 끝에 그녀의 뜻을 받아 주는 편이 낫겠다 여겼다."], ["His hopes from both gentleman and lady suffered a small depression in consequence; but when he looked at his niece, and saw the state of feature and complexion which her crying had brought her into, he thought there might be as much lost as gained by an immediate interview.", "그 신사와 아가씨 둘 모두에게 건 그의 바람이 그로 인해 얼마간 꺾였다. 그러나 조카딸을 보고, 울음이 그녀의 이목구비와 낯빛을 어떤 꼴로 만들어 놨는지를 보니, 그는 당장의 대면으로 얻는 것 못지않게 잃는 것도 있으리라 여겼다."], ["With a few words, therefore, of no particular meaning, he walked off by himself, leaving his poor niece to sit and cry over what had passed, with very wretched feelings.", "그리하여 별 뜻 없는 몇 마디를 남기고, 그는 혼자 걸어 나가, 딱한 조카딸을 남겨, 자못 비참한 심경으로 앉아 지난 일을 두고 울게 내버려 두었다."]], [["Her mind was all disorder.", "그녀의 마음은 온통 어지러웠다."], ["The past, present, future, everything was terrible.", "지난 일도, 지금도, 앞날도, 온갖 것이 끔찍했다."], ["But her uncle’s anger gave her the severest pain of all.", "그러나 삼촌의 노여움이 그 무엇보다 모진 아픔을 안겨 주었다."], ["Selfish and ungrateful!", "이기적이고 배은망덕하다니!"], ["to have appeared so to him!", "그에게 그리 비쳤다니!"], ["She was miserable for ever.", "그녀는 영영 비참했다."], ["She had no one to take her part, to counsel, or speak for her.", "그녀 편을 들어 주고, 조언해 주고, 대신 말해 줄 이가 아무도 없었다."], ["Her only friend was absent.", "그녀의 유일한 벗은 떠나 있었다."], ["He might have softened his father; but all, perhaps all, would think her selfish and ungrateful.", "그라면 제 아버지를 누그러뜨렸을지도 몰랐다. 그러나 다들, 어쩌면 다들 그녀를 이기적이고 배은망덕하다 여기리라."], ["She might have to endure the reproach again and again; she might hear it, or see it, or know it to exist for ever in every connexion about her.", "그녀는 그 나무람을 몇 번이고 견뎌야 할지도 몰랐다. 그것을 듣거나, 보거나, 제 둘레의 온갖 인연 속에 그것이 영영 있음을 알아야 할지도 몰랐다."], ["She could not but feel some resentment against Mr.", "그녀는 크로퍼드 씨에게 얼마간 원망이 들지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Crawford; yet, if he really loved her, and were unhappy too!", "그러나 만일 그가 정말로 저를 사랑하고, 그 또한 불행하다면!"], ["It was all wretchedness together.", "그것은 죄다 한데 뒤엉킨 비참함이었다."]], [["In about a quarter of an hour her uncle returned; she was almost ready to faint at the sight of him.", "십오 분쯤 지나 삼촌이 돌아왔다. 그녀는 그를 보자 거의 까무러칠 지경이었다."], ["He spoke calmly, however, without austerity, without reproach, and she revived a little.", "그러나 그는 차분하게, 매섭지도 나무라지도 않고 말했고, 그녀는 조금 되살아났다."], ["There was comfort, too, in his words, as well as his manner, for he began with, “Mr.", "그의 태도뿐 아니라 그 말에도 위안이 있었으니, 그가 이렇게 시작한 것이다."], ["Crawford is gone: he has just left me.", "“크로퍼드 씨는 갔다. 방금 나한테서 떠났어."], ["I need not repeat what has passed.", "무슨 일이 있었는지 되풀이할 것 없다."], ["I do not want to add to anything you may now be feeling, by an account of what he has felt.", "그가 무엇을 느꼈는지 들려주어 네가 지금 느낄지 모를 것에 보태고 싶지도 않고."], ["Suffice it, that he has behaved in the most gentlemanlike and generous manner, and has confirmed me in a most favourable opinion of his understanding, heart, and temper.", "이것만 알면 된다, 그가 더없이 신사답고 너그럽게 처신했다는 것, 그리고 그 슬기와 마음과 성미에 대한 더없이 좋은 생각을 내게 굳혀 주었다는 것."], ["Upon my representation of what you were suffering, he immediately, and with the greatest delicacy, ceased to urge to see you for the present.”", "네가 얼마나 괴로워하는지를 내가 전하니, 그는 대번에, 더없는 섬세함으로, 지금 너를 보자 조르기를 그쳤다.”"]], [["Here Fanny, who had looked up, looked down again.", "이때 고개를 들었던 패니가 다시 고개를 숙였다. “물론,”"], ["“Of course,”", "삼촌이 말을 이었다."], ["continued her uncle, “it cannot be supposed but that he should request to speak with you alone, be it only for five minutes; a request too natural, a claim too just to be denied.", "“그가 단 오 분이라도 너하고 단둘이 얘기하기를 청하는 거야 짐작 못 할 바 없지. 마다하기엔 너무 자연스러운 청이요, 너무 온당한 요구야."], ["But there is no time fixed; perhaps to-morrow, or whenever your spirits are composed enough.", "하지만 정해진 때는 없다. 어쩌면 내일이든, 아니면 네 기분이 웬만큼 가라앉을 때든."], ["For the present you have only to tranquillise yourself.", "지금으로선 너는 그저 마음을 다스리기만 하면 돼. 이 눈물을 거두어라."], ["Check these tears; they do but exhaust you.", "이건 너를 지치게 할 뿐이야. 만일, 내가 기꺼이 믿고 싶은 대로, 네가 나한테 무슨 마음씀이라도 보이고 싶거든, 이런 감정에 지지 말고, 좀 더 굳센 마음가짐이 되도록 스스로를 다잡아 봐라."], ["If, as I am willing to suppose, you wish to shew me any observance, you will not give way to these emotions, but endeavour to reason yourself into a stronger frame of mind.", "밖에 나가 보라 이르마. 바람이 너한테 이로울 게다."], ["I advise you to go out: the air will do you good; go out for an hour on the gravel; you will have the shrubbery to yourself, and will be the better for air and exercise.", "한 시간쯤 자갈길로 나가 보렴. 관목 산책로가 온통 네 차지일 테고, 바람과 운동으로 나아질 거야."], ["And, Fanny”", "그리고, 얘야”"], ["(turning back again for a moment), “I shall make no mention below of what has passed; I shall not even tell your aunt Bertram.", "(잠깐 다시 돌아서며). “나는 아래층에서 무슨 일이 있었는지 입에 안 올리마."], ["There is no occasion for spreading the disappointment; say nothing about it yourself.”", "버트럼 이모한테조차 말 안 할 거야. 이 실망스러운 일을 퍼뜨릴 까닭이 없으니, 너도 그 얘긴 아무한테도 하지 마라.”"]], [["This was an order to be most joyfully obeyed; this was an act of kindness which Fanny felt at her heart.", "이것은 더없이 기쁘게 따를 분부였다. 이것은 패니가 마음으로 사무치게 느낀 친절의 처사였다."], ["To be spared from her aunt Norris’s interminable reproaches!", "노리스 이모의 그 끝없는 나무람을 면할 수 있다니!"], ["he left her in a glow of gratitude.", "그는 그녀를 고마움에 달아오른 채로 두고 떠났다."], ["Anything might be bearable rather than such reproaches.", "그런 나무람만 아니라면 무엇이든 견딜 만했다."], ["Even to see Mr. Crawford would be less overpowering.", "크로퍼드 씨를 보는 것조차 그보다는 덜 벅찰 터였다."]], [["She walked out directly, as her uncle recommended, and followed his advice throughout, as far as she could; did check her tears; did earnestly try to compose her spirits and strengthen her mind.", "그녀는 삼촌이 이른 대로 곧장 밖에 나가, 되도록 그 조언을 낱낱이 따랐다. 정말로 눈물을 거두었고, 정말로 애써 제 마음을 다스리고 제 마음가짐을 든든히 하려 했다."], ["She wished to prove to him that she did desire his comfort, and sought to regain his favour; and he had given her another strong motive for exertion, in keeping the whole affair from the knowledge of her aunts.", "그녀는 그에게 저가 정말로 그의 편안함을 바라고 그의 호의를 되찾으려 애쓴다는 것을 증명하고 싶었다. 게다가 그는 이 온 일을 두 이모가 모르게 함으로써 그녀에게 애쓸 또 하나의 굳센 동기를 주었다."], ["Not to excite suspicion by her look or manner was now an object worth attaining; and she felt equal to almost anything that might save her from her aunt Norris.", "낯빛이나 태도로 의심을 사지 않는 것이 이제 이룰 만한 값어치 있는 목표였다. 그리고 그녀는 노리스 이모에게서 저를 구해 줄 것이라면 무엇이든 거의 다 해낼 수 있을 것 같았다."]], [["She was struck, quite struck, when, on returning from her walk and going into the East room again, the first thing which caught her eye was a fire lighted and burning.", "그녀가 산책에서 돌아와 다시 동쪽 방에 들어갔을 때, 맨 처음 눈에 든 것이 지펴져 타오르는 불이라, 그녀는 놀랐다, 아주 놀랐다."], ["A fire!", "불이라니!"], ["it seemed too much; just at that time to be giving her such an indulgence was exciting even painful gratitude.", "너무한 것 같았다. 하필 이런 때에 저에게 이런 호사를 베풀다니, 아프기까지 한 고마움이 일었다."], ["She wondered that Sir Thomas could have leisure to think of such a trifle again; but she soon found, from the voluntary information of the housemaid, who came in to attend it, that so it was to be every day.", "토머스 경이 이런 사소한 것을 다시 헤아릴 겨를이 있었다는 것이 의아했다. 그러나 그녀는, 불을 살피러 들어온 하녀가 자진해 알려 준 덕에, 이제 날마다 그리하기로 되어 있음을 이내 알았다."], ["Sir Thomas had given orders for it.", "토머스 경이 그리하라 분부를 내린 것이었다."]], [["“I must be a brute, indeed, if I can be really ungrateful!”", "“정말이지 내가 참으로 배은망덕할 수 있다면 나는 짐승이나 다름없어!”"], ["said she, in soliloquy.", "그녀는 혼잣말로 뇌었다."], ["“Heaven defend me from being ungrateful!”", "“배은망덕한 사람이 되지 않게 하늘이여 지켜 주소서!”"]], [["She saw nothing more of her uncle, nor of her aunt Norris, till they met at dinner. Her uncle’s behaviour to her was then as nearly as possible what it had been before; she was sure he did not mean there should be any change, and that it was only her own conscience that could fancy any; but her aunt was soon quarrelling with her; and when she found how much and how unpleasantly her having only walked out without her aunt’s knowledge could be dwelt on, she felt all the reason she had to bless the kindness which saved her from the same spirit of reproach, exerted on a more momentous subject.", "그녀는 만찬 자리에서 마주치기까지 삼촌도, 노리스 이모도 더는 보지 못했다. 그때 그녀에 대한 삼촌의 처신은 되도록 예전 그대로였다. 그가 무슨 달라짐이 있게 할 뜻은 없다는 것, 그리고 어떤 달라짐이든 오직 제 양심이 지어내는 것일 뿐임을 그녀는 확신했다. 그러나 이모는 이내 그녀와 다투기 시작했다. 그리고 이모 모르게 그저 산책 나갔다는 것 하나를 두고 얼마나 오래, 얼마나 언짢게 물고 늘어질 수 있는지를 보고 나니, 훨씬 더 중대한 일에서 이 같은 나무람의 기세를 저에게서 막아 준 그 친절을 감사할 온갖 까닭이 그녀에게 있음을 절감했다."]], [["“If I had known you were going out, I should have got you just to go as far as my house with some orders for Nanny,”", "“네가 나갈 줄 알았으면, 내니한테 줄 분부 몇 가지를 들려 그저 우리 집까지만이라도 다녀오라 했을 텐데.”"], ["said she, “which I have since, to my very great inconvenience, been obliged to go and carry myself.", "이모가 말했다. “그걸 그 뒤로 내가 몸소 큰 불편을 무릅쓰고 가서 전해야 했지 뭐냐."], ["I could very ill spare the time, and you might have saved me the trouble, if you would only have been so good as to let us know you were going out.", "나는 그럴 겨를이 도무지 없었는데, 네가 그저 나간다고 알려 주는 친절만 베풀었어도 그 수고를 덜어 줬을 것 아니냐."], ["It would have made no difference to you, I suppose, whether you had walked in the shrubbery or gone to my house.”", "관목 산책로를 거닐든 우리 집엘 가든 너한테야 아무 차이도 없었을 텐데 말이다.”"]], [["“I recommended the shrubbery to Fanny as the driest place,”", "“내가 패니한테 관목 산책로가 가장 마른 데라고 권했소.”"], ["said Sir Thomas.", "토머스 경이 말했다."]], [["“Oh!”", "“어머!”"], ["said Mrs.", "노리스 부인이 한순간 멈칫하며 말했다."], ["Norris, with a moment’s check, “that was very kind of you, Sir Thomas; but you do not know how dry the path is to my house.", "“참으로 친절도 하셔라, 토머스 경. 하지만 우리 집으로 가는 길이 얼마나 마른지는 경께서 모르셔서요."], ["Fanny would have had quite as good a walk there, I assure you, with the advantage of being of some use, and obliging her aunt: it is all her fault.", "정말이지 패니는 거기서도 꼭 그만큼 좋은 산책을 했을 거예요, 게다가 무언가 쓸모가 되고 제 이모를 도왔을 덕까지 보태서요. 다 그 애 잘못이에요."], ["If she would but have let us know she was going out but there is a something about Fanny, I have often observed it before—she likes to go her own way to work; she does not like to be dictated to; she takes her own independent walk whenever she can; she certainly has a little spirit of secrecy, and independence, and nonsense, about her, which I would advise her to get the better of.”", "그저 나간다고 우리한테 알려 주기만 했어도—하지만 패니한테는 뭔가가 있어요, 나는 진작부터 곧잘 봐 왔는데—제멋대로 일을 하고 싶어 하고, 지시받는 걸 안 좋아하고, 틈만 나면 제 혼자 방자한 산책을 나서죠. 정말이지 그 애한테는 은밀함이며 방자함이며 하는 시답잖은 기질이 얼마간 있어서, 나는 그 애더러 그걸 좀 고치라 이르고 싶어요.”"]], [["As a general reflection on Fanny, Sir Thomas thought nothing could be more unjust, though he had been so lately expressing the same sentiments himself, and he tried to turn the conversation: tried repeatedly before he could succeed; for Mrs.", "패니에 대한 두루뭉술한 평으로 치면, 토머스 경은 이보다 더 부당한 것은 없으리라 여겼다—저 자신도 방금 그토록 같은 심경을 밝힌 참이면서도. 그리하여 그는 화제를 돌리려 했다."], ["Norris had not discernment enough to perceive, either now, or at any other time, to what degree he thought well of his niece, or how very far he was from wishing to have his own children’s merits set off by the depreciation of hers.", "몇 번이고 애쓴 끝에야 겨우 뜻을 이뤘으니, 노리스 부인에게는, 지금이든 다른 어느 때든, 그가 조카딸을 얼마나 좋게 여기는지, 그리고 제 자식들의 미덕을 조카딸을 깎아내려 돋보이게 하고 싶은 마음에서 그가 얼마나 멀리 있는지를 알아챌 만한 눈이 없었던 것이다."], ["She was talking at Fanny, and resenting this private walk half through the dinner.", "그녀는 만찬의 절반 내내 패니를 겨냥해 말하며 이 은밀한 산책을 두고 언짢아하고 있었다."]], [["It was over, however, at last; and the evening set in with more composure to Fanny, and more cheerfulness of spirits than she could have hoped for after so stormy a morning; but she trusted, in the first place, that she had done right: that her judgment had not misled her. For the purity of her intentions she could answer; and she was willing to hope, secondly, that her uncle’s displeasure was abating, and would abate farther as he considered the matter with more impartiality, and felt, as a good man must feel, how wretched, and how unpardonable, how hopeless, and how wicked it was to marry without affection.", "그러나 마침내 그것도 끝났다. 그리고 그 저녁은, 그토록 사나웠던 아침 뒤에 그녀가 바랄 수 있었던 것보다 더 큰 평온으로, 더 큰 명랑함으로 패니에게 찾아들었다. 그러나 그녀는 무엇보다 먼저, 제가 옳게 했다고, 제 판단이 저를 그르치지 않았다고 믿었다. 제 뜻의 순수함이야 그녀가 장담할 수 있었다. 그리고 그녀는 둘째로, 삼촌의 언짢음이 가라앉고 있으며, 그가 이 일을 좀 더 공평하게 헤아려 보고, 좋은 사람이라면 마땅히 느낄 대로, 애정 없이 혼인하는 것이 얼마나 비참하고, 얼마나 용서 못 할 일이고, 얼마나 가망 없고, 얼마나 사악한 일인지를 느끼면 더 가라앉으리라 기꺼이 바랐다."]], [["When the meeting with which she was threatened for the morrow was past, she could not but flatter herself that the subject would be finally concluded, and Mr. Crawford once gone from Mansfield, that everything would soon be as if no such subject had existed.", "이튿날 겁내던 그 대면이 지나고 나면, 그녀는 이 화제가 끝내 마무리되고, 크로퍼드 씨가 일단 맨스필드를 떠나면 이내 만사가 마치 그런 화제란 애초에 없었던 것처럼 되리라 여기지 않을 수 없었다."], ["She would not, could not believe, that Mr. Crawford’s affection for her could distress him long; his mind was not of that sort.", "그녀는, 저에 대한 크로퍼드 씨의 애정이 그를 오래 괴롭힐 수 있으리라곤 믿으려 하지도, 믿을 수도 없었다."], ["London would soon bring its cure.", "그의 마음은 그런 부류가 아니었다."], ["In London he would soon learn to wonder at his infatuation, and be thankful for the right reason in her which had saved him from its evil consequences.", "런던이 이내 그 병을 고쳐 줄 터였다. 런던에서 그는 이내 제 홀림을 의아해하는 법을 배우고, 그 나쁜 결말에서 저를 구해 준 그녀의 그 옳은 분별을 고마워하게 될 터였다."]], [["While Fanny’s mind was engaged in these sort of hopes, her uncle was, soon after tea, called out of the room; an occurrence too common to strike her, and she thought nothing of it till the butler reappeared ten minutes afterwards, and advancing decidedly towards herself, said, “Sir Thomas wishes to speak with you, ma’am, in his own room.”", "패니의 마음이 이런 종류의 바람에 잠겨 있는 동안, 삼촌은 차를 든 뒤 얼마 안 되어 방 밖으로 불려 나갔다. 눈에 띄기엔 너무 흔한 일이라, 그녀는 그것을 대수롭잖게 여겼는데, 십 분 뒤 집사가 다시 나타나 결연히 그녀 쪽으로 다가와 “토머스 경께서 아가씨와 당신 방에서 얘기하고 싶어 하십니다”"], ["Then it occurred to her what might be going on; a suspicion rushed over her mind which drove the colour from her cheeks; but instantly rising, she was preparing to obey, when Mrs.", "하고 말했다. 그제야 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 그녀에게 떠올랐다."], ["Norris called out, “Stay, stay, Fanny!", "어떤 짐작이 그녀의 마음을 왈칵 스쳐 두 뺨에서 핏기를 앗아 갔다. 그러나 그녀가 대번에 일어나 따르려 채비하는데, 노리스 부인이 소리쳤다."], ["what are you about?", "“가만, 가만있어, 패니!"], ["where are you going?", "뭘 하는 게냐? 어딜 가?"], ["don’t be in such a hurry.", "그리 서두르지 마라."], ["Depend upon it, it is not you who are wanted; depend upon it, it is me”", "틀림없이 필요한 건 네가 아니야. 틀림없이 나지”"], ["(looking at the butler); “but you are so very eager to put yourself forward.", "(집사를 바라보며). “한데 너는 어찌 그리 나서지 못해 안달이니."], ["What should Sir Thomas want you for?", "토머스 경이 뭐 하러 널 찾겠어?"], ["It is me, Baddeley, you mean; I am coming this moment.", "나겠지, 배들리, 자네 말은. 곧 가겠네."], ["You mean me, Baddeley, I am sure; Sir Thomas wants me, not Miss Price.”", "나 말이지, 배들리, 분명해. 토머스 경이 찾는 건 나야, 프라이스 양이 아니라.”"]], [["But Baddeley was stout.", "그러나 배들리는 꿋꿋했다. “아닙니다, 부인."], ["“No, ma’am, it is Miss Price; I am certain of its being Miss Price.”", "프라이스 양이십니다. 프라이스 양이 틀림없습니다.”"], ["And there was a half-smile with the words, which meant, “I do not think you would answer the purpose at all.”", "그리고 그 말에는 반쯤의 미소가 어렸으니, 그것은 “부인께서는 그 볼일에 도무지 안 맞으실 듯한데요” 하는 뜻이었다."]], [["Mrs. Norris, much discontented, was obliged to compose herself to work again; and Fanny, walking off in agitating consciousness, found herself, as she anticipated, in another minute alone with Mr.", "노리스 부인은 몹시 못마땅한 채, 하는 수 없이 마음을 다잡아 다시 바느질에 들었다."], ["Crawford.", "그리고 패니는 마음 졸이는 심경으로 걸어 나가, 저가 짐작한 대로, 잠시 뒤 크로퍼드 씨와 단둘이 있는 자신을 발견했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIII", "# 제33장"]], [["The conference was neither so short nor so conclusive as the lady had designed.", "그 대면은 그 아가씨가 짜 둔 것만큼 짧지도, 결판나지도 않았다."], ["The gentleman was not so easily satisfied.", "그 신사는 그리 쉽게 흡족해하지 않았다."], ["He had all the disposition to persevere that Sir Thomas could wish him.", "그에게는 토머스 경이 바랄 만한 온갖 물러서지 않을 기질이 있었다."], ["He had vanity, which strongly inclined him in the first place to think she did love him, though she might not know it herself; and which, secondly, when constrained at last to admit that she did know her own present feelings, convinced him that he should be able in time to make those feelings what he wished.", "그에게는 허영심이 있어, 우선 그것이 그로 하여금, 비록 그녀 자신은 모를지언정 그녀가 저를 사랑한다고 강하게 여기게 했다. 그리고 둘째로, 그녀가 제 지금 심경을 스스로 알고 있음을 마침내 인정할 수밖에 없게 되었을 때, 그 허영심은 그로 하여금, 머잖아 그 심경을 제가 바라는 대로 만들 수 있으리라 확신하게 했다."]], [["He was in love, very much in love; and it was a love which, operating on an active, sanguine spirit, of more warmth than delicacy, made her affection appear of greater consequence because it was withheld, and determined him to have the glory, as well as the felicity, of forcing her to love him.", "그는 사랑에 빠져 있었다, 몹시 깊이. 그리고 그것은, 섬세함보다 뜨거움이 더한, 활달하고 낙천적인 마음에 작용하여, 얻지 못하기에 그녀의 정을 도리어 더 값진 것으로 보이게 하고, 그로 하여금 그녀를 억지로라도 사랑에 빠지게 만드는 그 행복뿐 아니라 그 영광까지 누리기로 마음먹게 한 그런 사랑이었다."]], [["He would not despair: he would not desist.", "그는 단념하려 하지 않았다. 물러서려 하지 않았다."], ["He had every well-grounded reason for solid attachment; he knew her to have all the worth that could justify the warmest hopes of lasting happiness with her; her conduct at this very time, by speaking the disinterestedness and delicacy of her character (qualities which he believed most rare indeed), was of a sort to heighten all his wishes, and confirm all his resolutions.", "그에게는 굳건한 애착을 뒷받침할 온갖 근거가 넉넉했다. 그녀와 더불어 이어질 행복에 더없이 뜨거운 바람을 품어도 좋을 만한 온갖 값어치를 그녀가 지녔음을 그는 알았다."], ["He knew not that he had a pre-engaged heart to attack.", "바로 이때의 그녀의 처신은, 그 성품의 사심 없음과 섬세함(그가 정말이지 더없이 드물다 믿는 자질)을 말해 줌으로써, 그의 온갖 바람을 돋우고 그의 온갖 결심을 굳혀 줄 만한 것이었다."], ["Of that he had no suspicion.", "그는 저가 이미 매인 마음을 공략하고 있다는 것을 몰랐다."], ["He considered her rather as one who had never thought on the subject enough to be in danger; who had been guarded by youth, a youth of mind as lovely as of person; whose modesty had prevented her from understanding his attentions, and who was still overpowered by the suddenness of addresses so wholly unexpected, and the novelty of a situation which her fancy had never taken into account.", "그것은 조금도 의심하지 못했다. 그는 그녀를, 이런 문제를 위험에 빠질 만큼 충분히 생각해 본 적이 없는 사람으로, 젊음—몸매만큼이나 사랑스러운 마음의 젊음으로 지켜져 온 사람으로, 그 겸손함 탓에 그의 마음씀을 못 알아챈 사람으로, 그리고 그토록 온전히 뜻밖인 구애의 그 갑작스러움과, 제 공상에 한 번도 넣어 본 적 없는 처지의 그 낯섦에 여전히 짓눌린 사람으로 여겼다."]], [["Must it not follow of course, that, when he was understood, he should succeed?", "그러니 그가 이해받게 되면 뜻을 이루리라는 것이 물론 따라 나오지 않겠는가?"], ["He believed it fully.", "그는 그것을 온전히 믿었다."], ["Love such as his, in a man like himself, must with perseverance secure a return, and at no great distance; and he had so much delight in the idea of obliging her to love him in a very short time, that her not loving him now was scarcely regretted.", "그와 같은 사내의, 그와 같은 사랑이라면, 물러서지 않고 밀어붙이면 반드시 화답을 얻어 내되 그리 멀지 않은 데서 얻어 내리라. 그리고 그는 머잖아 그녀가 저를 사랑하게 만든다는 생각에 어찌나 큰 기쁨을 느꼈던지, 그녀가 지금 저를 사랑하지 않는다는 것이 좀처럼 아쉽지도 않았다."], ["A little difficulty to be overcome was no evil to Henry Crawford.", "이겨 낼 자그마한 어려움쯤은 헨리 크로퍼드에게 조금도 나쁜 것이 아니었다."], ["He rather derived spirits from it.", "그는 오히려 거기서 기운을 얻었다."], ["He had been apt to gain hearts too easily.", "그는 마음을 너무 쉬 얻는 데 익어 온 사람이었다."], ["His situation was new and animating.", "그의 처지는 새롭고 신나는 것이었다."]], [["To Fanny, however, who had known too much opposition all her life to find any charm in it, all this was unintelligible.", "그러나 패니로 말하면, 평생 너무 많은 거스름을 겪어 거기서 아무 매력도 못 느끼는 사람이라, 이 모두가 도무지 알 수 없는 노릇이었다."], ["She found that he did mean to persevere; but how he could, after such language from her as she felt herself obliged to use, was not to be understood.", "그녀는 그가 정말 물러서지 않을 작정임을 알았다. 그러나 저가 하지 않을 수 없다 여긴 그런 말을 한 뒤에 그가 어찌 그럴 수 있는지는 도무지 알 수 없었다."], ["She told him that she did not love him, could not love him, was sure she never should love him; that such a change was quite impossible; that the subject was most painful to her; that she must entreat him never to mention it again, to allow her to leave him at once, and let it be considered as concluded for ever.", "그녀는 그에게, 저는 그를 사랑하지 않는다고, 사랑할 수 없다고, 결코 사랑하게 되지 않으리라 확신한다고, 그런 변화란 아주 어림없는 일이라고, 이 화제가 저에게 더없이 괴롭다고, 부디 다시는 그것을 입에 올리지 말고 당장 저를 떠나게 해 주기를, 그리하여 그것이 영영 끝난 일로 여겨지기를 청한다고 말했다."], ["And when farther pressed, had added, that in her opinion their dispositions were so totally dissimilar as to make mutual affection incompatible; and that they were unfitted for each other by nature, education, and habit.", "그리고 더 조름을 받자, 제 생각에 둘의 성미는 아주 딴판이라 서로의 정이 어우러질 수 없다고, 둘은 천성으로도 교육으로도 버릇으로도 서로에게 어울리지 않는다고 덧붙였다."], ["All this she had said, and with the earnestness of sincerity; yet this was not enough, for he immediately denied there being anything uncongenial in their characters, or anything unfriendly in their situations; and positively declared, that he would still love, and still hope!", "이 모두를 그녀는 진심의 간절함으로 말했다. 그러나 이것으로도 넉넉하지 않았으니, 그가 대번에 둘의 성품에 안 맞는 것이 있다는 것도, 둘의 처지에 못마땅한 것이 있다는 것도 부인하고, 저는 여전히 사랑하겠노라, 여전히 바라겠노라 딱 잘라 선언한 것이다!"]], [["Fanny knew her own meaning, but was no judge of her own manner.", "패니는 제 뜻이 무엇인지야 알았으나, 제 태도를 가늠할 줄은 몰랐다."], ["Her manner was incurably gentle; and she was not aware how much it concealed the sternness of her purpose.", "그녀의 태도는 도무지 고칠 수 없이 상냥했다. 그리고 그것이 제 뜻의 그 단호함을 얼마나 감추는지 그녀는 알지 못했다."], ["Her diffidence, gratitude, and softness made every expression of indifference seem almost an effort of self-denial; seem, at least, to be giving nearly as much pain to herself as to him.", "그녀의 수줍음과 고마움과 부드러움은, 무심함의 온갖 표현마저 거의 자기 부정의 안간힘처럼—적어도 그에게 못지않게 저 자신에게도 그만큼 아픔을 주는 것처럼 보이게 했다. 크로퍼드 씨는 이제, 마리아 버트럼의 그 은밀하고 음험하고 배신 어린 흠모자로서 그녀가 몸서리치던, 보기도 말하기도 싫어하던, 그에게 무슨 좋은 자질이 있으리라곤 믿을 수 없던, 심지어 상냥할 수 있는 그 역량조차 좀처럼 인정하지 않던 그 크로퍼드 씨가 아니었다."], ["Mr. Crawford was no longer the Mr.", "그는 이제, 뜨겁고 사심 없는 사랑으로 저에게 구애하는 크로퍼드 씨였다."], ["Crawford who, as the clandestine, insidious, treacherous admirer of Maria Bertram, had been her abhorrence, whom she had hated to see or to speak to, in whom she could believe no good quality to exist, and whose power, even of being agreeable, she had barely acknowledged. He was now the Mr.", "그 심경이 겉보기에 온통 떳떳하고 올곧은 것이 된, 그 행복의 뜻이 온통 애정으로 맺어지는 혼인에 붙박인, 그녀의 값어치에 대한 제 마음을 쏟아 내고, 제 애정을 그리고 또 그려 보이고, 말로써 증명할 수 있는 데까지, 게다가 재주 있는 사내의 말투와 목소리와 기백으로, 저가 그녀를 그 상냥함과 착함 때문에 구한다는 것을 증명하는 크로퍼드 씨였다."], ["Crawford who was addressing herself with ardent, disinterested love; whose feelings were apparently become all that was honourable and upright, whose views of happiness were all fixed on a marriage of attachment; who was pouring out his sense of her merits, describing and describing again his affection, proving as far as words could prove it, and in the language, tone, and spirit of a man of talent too, that he sought her for her gentleness and her goodness; and to complete the whole, he was now the Mr. Crawford who had procured William’s promotion!", "그리고 이 온갖 것을 마무리 짓듯, 그는 이제 윌리엄의 진급을 이뤄 준 그 크로퍼드 씨였던 것이다!"]], [["Here was a change, and here were claims which could not but operate!", "여기 하나의 달라짐이 있었고, 여기 작용하지 않을 수 없는 몫들이 있었다!"], ["She might have disdained him in all the dignity of angry virtue, in the grounds of Sotherton, or the theatre at Mansfield Park; but he approached her now with rights that demanded different treatment.", "그녀는 노여운 덕의 온갖 위엄으로, 소더턴의 뜰에서나 맨스필드 파크의 극장에서라면 그를 업신여겼을지 모른다. 그러나 그는 이제 다른 대접을 요구하는 권리를 지니고 그녀에게 다가왔다."], ["She must be courteous, and she must be compassionate.", "그녀는 정중해야 했고, 딱하게 여겨야 했다."], ["She must have a sensation of being honoured, and whether thinking of herself or her brother, she must have a strong feeling of gratitude.", "영광스럽다는 느낌이 들지 않을 수 없었고, 저 자신을 생각하든 제 오라비를 생각하든 굳센 고마움을 느끼지 않을 수 없었다."], ["The effect of the whole was a manner so pitying and agitated, and words intermingled with her refusal so expressive of obligation and concern, that to a temper of vanity and hope like Crawford’s, the truth, or at least the strength of her indifference, might well be questionable; and he was not so irrational as Fanny considered him, in the professions of persevering, assiduous, and not desponding attachment which closed the interview.", "그 온갖 것의 결과로 그녀의 태도는 자못 애처롭고 마음 졸이는 것이 되었고, 그녀의 마다함에 뒤섞인 말은 신세 진 마음과 걱정을 자못 잘 드러내는 것이 되어, 크로퍼드처럼 허영과 바람에 찬 성미에는 그녀의 무심함의 참됨, 아니 적어도 그 굳셈이 자못 미덥잖게 여겨질 만했다. 그리하여 이 대면을 마무리 지은 그 물러서지 않고 끈질기며 낙심 없는 애착의 다짐에서, 그는 패니가 여긴 것만큼 얼빠진 사람이 아니었다."]], [["It was with reluctance that he suffered her to go; but there was no look of despair in parting to belie his words, or give her hopes of his being less unreasonable than he professed himself.", "그가 그녀를 가게 놓아준 것은 마지못한 일이었다. 그러나 헤어질 제 그의 낯빛에는, 그의 말을 무색케 하거나 그가 스스로 자처한 것보다 덜 억지스러우리라는 바람을 그녀에게 줄 만한 절망의 기색이 조금도 없었다."]], [["Now she was angry.", "이제 그녀는 화가 났다."], ["Some resentment did arise at a perseverance so selfish and ungenerous.", "그토록 이기적이고 야박한 끈질김에 얼마간 원망이 일었다."], ["Here was again a want of delicacy and regard for others which had formerly so struck and disgusted her.", "여기 또다시, 예전에 그토록 그녀를 놀라게 하고 역겹게 하던, 섬세함과 남에 대한 헤아림의 부족이 있었다."], ["Here was again a something of the same Mr. Crawford whom she had so reprobated before.", "여기 또다시, 앞서 그토록 그녀가 나무라던 그 똑같은 크로퍼드 씨의 무언가가 있었다."], ["How evidently was there a gross want of feeling and humanity where his own pleasure was concerned—And, alas!", "제 즐거움이 걸린 데서는 얼마나 뻔하게 정과 인정의 큰 부족이 있던지—그리고, 아아!"], ["how always known no principle to supply as a duty what the heart was deficient in.", "마음이 모자란 것을 도리로써 채워 줄 아무런 지조도 없음을 얼마나 늘 알던지."], ["Had her own affections been as free—as perhaps they ought to have been—he never could have engaged them.", "만일 그녀 자신의 정이—어쩌면 마땅히 그러했어야 할 만큼—자유로웠더라면, 그는 결코 그것을 사로잡을 수 없었으리라."]], [["So thought Fanny, in good truth and sober sadness, as she sat musing over that too great indulgence and luxury of a fire upstairs: wondering at the past and present; wondering at what was yet to come, and in a nervous agitation which made nothing clear to her but the persuasion of her being never under any circumstances able to love Mr. Crawford, and the felicity of having a fire to sit over and think of it.", "위층의 그 너무 과분하고 호사스러운 불을 두고 곰곰 앉아, 지난 일과 지금 일을 의아해하며, 아직 올 일을 의아해하며, 어떤 처지에서도 결코 크로퍼드 씨를 사랑할 수 없으리라는 그 확신과, 그 곁에 앉아 그것을 생각할 불이 있다는 그 복 말고는 아무것도 또렷하지 않은 그 마음 졸이는 심경 속에서, 패니는 정녕 진실되고 차분한 서글픔으로 이렇게 생각했다."]], [["Sir Thomas was obliged, or obliged himself, to wait till the morrow for a knowledge of what had passed between the young people.", "토머스 경은 두 젊은이 사이에 무슨 일이 있었는지를 알기까지 이튿날까지 기다려야 했다, 아니 스스로 기다렸다. 그리하여 그는 크로퍼드 씨를 보고 그 이야기를 들었다."], ["He then saw Mr.", "첫 심경은 실망이었다."], ["Crawford, and received his account.", "그는 더 나은 것을 바랐던 것이다. 그는 크로퍼드 같은 젊은이의 한 시간 간청이 패니 같은 상냥한 성미의 아가씨에게 그토록 작은 변화밖에 못 냈을 리 없다 여겼다."], ["The first feeling was disappointment: he had hoped better things; he had thought that an hour’s entreaty from a young man like Crawford could not have worked so little change on a gentle-tempered girl like Fanny; but there was speedy comfort in the determined views and sanguine perseverance of the lover; and when seeing such confidence of success in the principal, Sir Thomas was soon able to depend on it himself.", "그러나 그 사랑하는 이의 굳은 뜻과 낙천적인 끈질김에 이내 위안이 있었다. 그리하여 그 당사자에게서 그런 성공의 확신을 보고 나니, 토머스 경 자신도 이내 거기에 기댈 수 있게 되었다."]], [["Nothing was omitted, on his side, of civility, compliment, or kindness, that might assist the plan. Mr.", "그의 편에서는, 그 계획을 도울 만한 예의도, 찬사도, 친절도 하나 빠뜨림이 없었다."], ["Crawford’s steadiness was honoured, and Fanny was praised, and the connexion was still the most desirable in the world. At Mansfield Park Mr.", "크로퍼드 씨의 진득함이 기려지고, 패니가 칭찬받았으며, 그 인연은 여전히 세상 둘도 없이 바람직한 것이었다."], ["Crawford would always be welcome; he had only to consult his own judgment and feelings as to the frequency of his visits, at present or in future.", "맨스필드 파크에서 크로퍼드 씨는 늘 반가운 손님일 터였다. 지금이든 앞으로든 얼마나 자주 찾아올지는 그저 제 판단과 심경에 물어보기만 하면 될 일이었다."], ["In all his niece’s family and friends, there could be but one opinion, one wish on the subject; the influence of all who loved her must incline one way.", "조카딸의 온 집안과 벗들 사이에, 이 일에 대해서는 오직 한 가지 생각, 한 가지 바람밖에 있을 수 없었다. 그녀를 사랑하는 모든 이의 뜻이 한 방향으로 기울 수밖에 없었다."]], [["Everything was said that could encourage, every encouragement received with grateful joy, and the gentlemen parted the best of friends.", "북돋울 만한 온갖 것이 말해졌고, 온갖 북돋움이 고마운 기쁨으로 받아들여졌으며, 두 신사는 더없이 좋은 벗으로 헤어졌다."]], [["Satisfied that the cause was now on a footing the most proper and hopeful, Sir Thomas resolved to abstain from all farther importunity with his niece, and to shew no open interference.", "이 일이 이제 더없이 온당하고 바람직한 발판 위에 놓였음에 흡족한 토머스 경은, 조카딸에게 더는 조르기를 삼가고, 드러내 놓고 끼어들지 않기로 마음먹었다."], ["Upon her disposition he believed kindness might be the best way of working.", "그녀의 성미에는 아무래도 친절이 가장 잘 통하는 길이려니 그는 믿었다."], ["Entreaty should be from one quarter only.", "조름은 오직 한 편에서만 있어야 했다."], ["The forbearance of her family on a point, respecting which she could be in no doubt of their wishes, might be their surest means of forwarding it.", "그녀가 그들 바람을 조금도 의심할 수 없는 그런 대목에서 집안이 참고 물러나는 것이야말로 그것을 밀어붙일 가장 확실한 방편일지도 몰랐다."], ["Accordingly, on this principle, Sir Thomas took the first opportunity of saying to her, with a mild gravity, intended to be overcoming, “Well, Fanny, I have seen Mr.", "그리하여 이 원칙에 따라, 토머스 경은 첫 기회를 잡아, 마음을 돌려놓으려는 뜻으로 부드러운 근엄함을 담아 그녀에게 말했다. “자, 패니, 나는 크로퍼드 씨를 다시 만나, 너희 사이가 어찌 됐는지를 그에게서 소상히 들었다."], ["Crawford again, and learn from him exactly how matters stand between you.", "그는 더없이 유별난 젊은이야. 그리고 그 결말이 무엇이든, 너는 여느 것과는 다른 애착을 자아냈음을 느껴야 해."], ["He is a most extraordinary young man, and whatever be the event, you must feel that you have created an attachment of no common character; though, young as you are, and little acquainted with the transient, varying, unsteady nature of love, as it generally exists, you cannot be struck as I am with all that is wonderful in a perseverance of this sort against discouragement.", "하기야 너처럼 어리고, 사랑이란 것이 대개 그렇듯 그 덧없고 변덕스럽고 미덥지 못한 본성을 잘 모르는 처지에, 낙심 앞에서도 물러서지 않는 이런 끈질김의 그 온갖 놀라움에 나만큼 감복할 수야 없겠지만. 그에게 그것은 온전히 마음의 일이야."], ["With him it is entirely a matter of feeling: he claims no merit in it; perhaps is entitled to none.", "그는 거기서 아무 공도 내세우지 않는다. 어쩌면 내세울 만한 것도 없겠지."], ["Yet, having chosen so well, his constancy has a respectable stamp.", "그러나 그토록 잘 골랐으니, 그 한결같음에는 존경할 만한 도장이 찍힌 셈이다."], ["Had his choice been less unexceptionable, I should have condemned his persevering.”", "만일 그의 선택이 덜 나무랄 데 없었다면, 나는 그가 물러서지 않는 것을 나무랐을 게야.”"]], [["“Indeed, sir,”", "“정말이지, 삼촌,”"], ["said Fanny, “I am very sorry that Mr.", "패니가 말했다."], ["Crawford should continue to—I know that it is paying me a very great compliment, and I feel most undeservedly honoured; but I am so perfectly convinced, and I have told him so, that it never will be in my power—”", "“크로퍼드 씨가 계속 그러시니 저는 무척 죄송해요—그게 저한테 자못 큰 찬사임을 알고, 저는 분에 넘치는 영광으로 느끼지만, 저는 너무나 온전히 확신하고 있고, 그이한테도 그리 말했어요, 결코 제 힘에 닿는 일이 아니라고—”"]], [["“My dear,”", "“얘야,”"], ["interrupted Sir Thomas, “there is no occasion for this.", "토머스 경이 가로막았다. “그럴 것 없다."], ["Your feelings are as well known to me as my wishes and regrets must be to you.", "네 심경은, 내 바람과 서운함이 너에게 그러할 것이 틀림없듯, 나한테도 훤히 알려져 있어."], ["There is nothing more to be said or done.", "더 말하거나 할 것이 없다."], ["From this hour the subject is never to be revived between us.", "이 시각부터 이 화제는 우리 사이에 결코 다시 꺼내지 않으마."], ["You will have nothing to fear, or to be agitated about.", "너는 두려워하거나 마음 졸일 것이 없어."], ["You cannot suppose me capable of trying to persuade you to marry against your inclinations.", "내가 너를 네 뜻에 거슬러 혼인시키려 설득할 사람이라곤 여기지 못할 게다."], ["Your happiness and advantage are all that I have in view, and nothing is required of you but to bear with Mr. Crawford’s endeavours to convince you that they may not be incompatible with his.", "나는 오직 네 행복과 이로움만을 눈에 두고 있고, 너한테 바라는 것이라곤, 그 심경이 제 심경과 어우러질 수 없는 것은 아님을 너에게 납득시키려는 크로퍼드 씨의 애씀을 참아 주는 것 말고는 아무것도 없다."], ["He proceeds at his own risk.", "그는 제 위험을 무릅쓰고 나아가는 거야."], ["You are on safe ground.", "너는 안전한 땅에 있고."], ["I have engaged for your seeing him whenever he calls, as you might have done had nothing of this sort occurred.", "이런 일이 애초에 없었더라도 네가 그리했을 그대로, 그가 찾아올 때면 언제든 네가 그를 만나 주기로 나는 다짐해 뒀다."], ["You will see him with the rest of us, in the same manner, and, as much as you can, dismissing the recollection of everything unpleasant.", "너는 우리 나머지 식구와 함께, 같은 식으로 그를 만나고, 되도록 온갖 언짢은 기억은 떨쳐 버리려무나."], ["He leaves Northamptonshire so soon, that even this slight sacrifice cannot be often demanded.", "그는 머잖아 노샘프턴셔를 떠나니, 이런 자그마한 희생조차 자주 요구되지는 않을 거야."], ["The future must be very uncertain.", "앞날은 자못 미덥지 못한 법이고."], ["And now, my dear Fanny, this subject is closed between us.”", "자, 이제, 얘야, 이 화제는 우리 사이에 닫혔다.”"]], [["The promised departure was all that Fanny could think of with much satisfaction.", "약속된 떠남만이 패니가 자못 흡족한 마음으로 헤아릴 수 있는 전부였다."], ["Her uncle’s kind expressions, however, and forbearing manner, were sensibly felt; and when she considered how much of the truth was unknown to him, she believed she had no right to wonder at the line of conduct he pursued.", "그러나 삼촌의 친절한 말과 참고 물러나는 태도는 사무치게 느껴졌다. 그리고 그 진실이 얼마나 많이 그에게 알려지지 않았는가를 헤아려 보니, 그녀는 그가 좇는 그 처신의 갈래를 두고 의아해할 자격이 없다 여겼다."], ["He, who had married a daughter to Mr. Rushworth: romantic delicacy was certainly not to be expected from him.", "딸을 러시워스 씨에게 시집보낸 그에게서 낭만적인 섬세함이야 분명 바랄 수 없는 노릇이었다."], ["She must do her duty, and trust that time might make her duty easier than it now was.", "그녀는 제 도리를 다하고, 시간이 그 도리를 지금보다 수월하게 해 주기를 믿을밖에 없었다."]], [["She could not, though only eighteen, suppose Mr.", "그녀는, 겨우 열여덟이면서도, 크로퍼드 씨의 애착이 영영 버티리라곤 여길 수 없었다."], ["Crawford’s attachment would hold out for ever; she could not but imagine that steady, unceasing discouragement from herself would put an end to it in time.", "저 편에서 한결같이 그침 없이 낙심시키면 머잖아 그것을 끝장내리라 여기지 않을 수 없었다."], ["How much time she might, in her own fancy, allot for its dominion, is another concern.", "그것이 판치는 기간을 제 공상 속에 얼마나 잡아 두었는지는 또 다른 문제다."], ["It would not be fair to inquire into a young lady’s exact estimate of her own perfections.", "젊은 아가씨가 제 완벽함을 두고 얼마나 정확히 어림하는지 캐묻는 것은 온당치 않으리라."]], [["In spite of his intended silence, Sir Thomas found himself once more obliged to mention the subject to his niece, to prepare her briefly for its being imparted to her aunts; a measure which he would still have avoided, if possible, but which became necessary from the totally opposite feelings of Mr.", "말을 아끼려던 뜻에도 불구하고, 토머스 경은 이 화제를 다시 한번 조카딸에게 꺼내, 그것이 두 이모에게 전해지리라는 것을 짤막이 채비시키지 않을 수 없는 자신을 발견했다. 그것은 되도록 여전히 피하고 싶은 조처였으나, 일 처리를 은밀히 하는 데 대한 크로퍼드 씨의 아주 정반대인 심경 탓에 어쩔 수 없어진 것이었다."], ["Crawford as to any secrecy of proceeding.", "그는 감출 생각이 조금도 없었다."], ["He had no idea of concealment. It was all known at the Parsonage, where he loved to talk over the future with both his sisters, and it would be rather gratifying to him to have enlightened witnesses of the progress of his success.", "그것은 목사관에 죄다 알려져 있었으니, 거기서 그는 두 누이와 함께 앞날을 두고 얘기하기를 즐겼고, 제 성공의 진척을 지켜볼 밝은 눈의 증인들이 있는 것이 오히려 흐뭇했던 것이다."], ["When Sir Thomas understood this, he felt the necessity of making his own wife and sister-in-law acquainted with the business without delay; though, on Fanny’s account, he almost dreaded the effect of the communication to Mrs.", "이를 알게 되자 토머스 경은 지체 없이 제 아내와 처형에게 이 일을 알릴 필요를 느꼈다. 그러나 패니를 생각하면, 노리스 부인에게 이를 전했을 때의 그 여파가 패니 자신 못지않게 거의 두려웠다."], ["Norris as much as Fanny herself. He deprecated her mistaken but well-meaning zeal.", "그는 그녀의 그릇된, 그러나 선의에서 나온 그 열성을 못마땅해했다."], ["Sir Thomas, indeed, was, by this time, not very far from classing Mrs. Norris as one of those well-meaning people who are always doing mistaken and very disagreeable things.", "정말이지 토머스 경은, 이때쯤 노리스 부인을, 늘 그릇되고 자못 언짢은 일을 하고야 마는 그런 선의의 사람들 축에 넣는 데서 그리 멀지 않았다."]], [["Mrs. Norris, however, relieved him.", "그러나 노리스 부인은 그를 놓이게 해 주었다."], ["He pressed for the strictest forbearance and silence towards their niece; she not only promised, but did observe it.", "그가 조카딸에게 더없이 엄히 참고 잠잠할 것을 조르니, 그녀는 약속했을 뿐 아니라 정말로 그것을 지켰다."], ["She only looked her increased ill-will.", "그녀는 그저 더해진 미움을 낯빛에 담을 뿐이었다."], ["Angry she was: bitterly angry; but she was more angry with Fanny for having received such an offer than for refusing it.", "화가 났다, 몹시 화가 났다. 그러나 그녀는 패니가 그런 청혼을 마다한 것보다 그런 청혼을 받았다는 것에 더 화가 났다."], ["It was an injury and affront to Julia, who ought to have been Mr.", "그것은, 마땅히 크로퍼드 씨의 선택이 되었어야 할 줄리아에 대한 손상이요 모욕이었다."], ["Crawford’s choice; and, independently of that, she disliked Fanny, because she had neglected her; and she would have grudged such an elevation to one whom she had been always trying to depress.", "그리고 그와는 별개로, 그녀는 패니를 소홀히 여겨 왔던지라 패니를 싫어했다. 그리고 늘 짓눌러 오려 애쓴 이가 그렇게 올라서는 것을 그녀는 아까워했으리라."]], [["Sir Thomas gave her more credit for discretion on the occasion than she deserved; and Fanny could have blessed her for allowing her only to see her displeasure, and not to hear it.", "토머스 경은 이번 일에서 그녀에게 마땅한 것보다 더 큰 분별을 인정해 주었다. 그리고 패니는, 이모가 그 언짢음을 그저 낯빛으로 보이게만 하고 소리 내어 듣게는 하지 않은 것을 두고 그녀를 축복할 수도 있었다."]], [["Lady Bertram took it differently.", "버트럼 부인은 그것을 다르게 받아들였다."], ["She had been a beauty, and a prosperous beauty, all her life; and beauty and wealth were all that excited her respect.", "그녀는 평생 미인이었고, 잘나가는 미인이었다. 그리고 미모와 재물이야말로 그녀의 존중을 자아내는 전부였다."], ["To know Fanny to be sought in marriage by a man of fortune, raised her, therefore, very much in her opinion.", "그러니 패니가 재산 있는 사내에게 청혼받았음을 아는 것은 그녀의 눈에 그녀를 자못 높여 놓았다."], ["By convincing her that Fanny was very pretty, which she had been doubting about before, and that she would be advantageously married, it made her feel a sort of credit in calling her niece.", "그것은, 앞서 미심쩍어하던 패니가 정말 무척 예쁘다는 것과, 그 애가 이롭게 시집가리라는 것을 그녀에게 납득시킴으로써, 그 애를 제 조카딸이라 부르는 데 일종의 뿌듯함을 느끼게 했다."]], [["“Well, Fanny,”", "“자, 패니,”"], ["said she, as soon as they were alone together afterwards, and she really had known something like impatience to be alone with her, and her countenance, as she spoke, had extraordinary animation; “Well, Fanny, I have had a very agreeable surprise this morning.", "그 뒤 둘만 남게 되자마자 그녀가 말했으니, 그녀는 정말이지 그녀와 단둘이 있고파 조바심 비슷한 것마저 알던 참이었고, 말할 제 그 낯빛에는 유별난 생기가 어려 있었다. “자, 패니, 나는 오늘 아침 자못 반가운 놀람을 겪었다."], ["I must just speak of it once, I told Sir Thomas I must once, and then I shall have done.", "딱 한 번만 이 얘길 해야겠어, 토머스 경한테도 딱 한 번만 하겠다 했으니, 그러고 나면 그만하마."], ["I give you joy, my dear niece.”", "축하한다, 사랑하는 조카야.”"], ["And looking at her complacently, she added, “Humph, we certainly are a handsome family!”", "그리고 흐뭇이 그녀를 바라보며 덧붙였다. “흠, 우리는 확실히 잘생긴 집안이야!”"]], [["Fanny coloured, and doubted at first what to say; when, hoping to assail her on her vulnerable side, she presently answered—", "패니는 얼굴을 붉히며 처음엔 무어라 할지 몰랐다. 그러다 이모의 약한 데를 파고들 요량으로, 이내 대답했다—"]], [["“My dear aunt, you cannot wish me to do differently from what I have done, I am sure.", "“이모, 이모야 제가 한 것과 달리하기를 바라진 않으실 거예요, 분명."], ["You cannot wish me to marry; for you would miss me, should not you?", "이모는 제가 시집가기를 바라진 않으실 거예요. 제가 없으면 이모가 아쉬우실 테니까요, 안 그래요?"], ["Yes, I am sure you would miss me too much for that.”", "네, 이모는 저를 아주 아쉬워하셔서 그러진 못하실 거라 저는 믿어요.”"]], [["“No, my dear, I should not think of missing you, when such an offer as this comes in your way.", "“아니다, 얘야, 이런 청혼이 네 앞에 온 마당에 너를 아쉬워할 생각은 안 할 거란다."], ["I could do very well without you, if you were married to a man of such good estate as Mr. Crawford.", "네가 크로퍼드 씨처럼 좋은 재산을 가진 사내한테 시집간다면 나야 너 없이도 아주 잘 지낼 수 있어."], ["And you must be aware, Fanny, that it is every young woman’s duty to accept such a very unexceptionable offer as this.”", "그리고 너도 알아야 한다, 패니, 이토록 나무랄 데 없는 청혼을 받아들이는 게 젊은 여자라면 누구나의 도리라는 걸.”"]], [["This was almost the only rule of conduct, the only piece of advice, which Fanny had ever received from her aunt in the course of eight years and a half.", "이것은 여덟 해 반 동안 패니가 이모에게서 받아 본, 거의 유일한 처신의 규칙이자 유일한 조언이었다. 그것이 그녀를 잠잠하게 만들었다."], ["It silenced her. She felt how unprofitable contention would be.", "그녀는 다투는 것이 얼마나 부질없을지 절감했다."], ["If her aunt’s feelings were against her, nothing could be hoped from attacking her understanding.", "이모의 심경이 저를 거스르는 마당이면, 그 슬기를 파고들어 봐야 아무것도 바랄 수 없었다."], ["Lady Bertram was quite talkative.", "버트럼 부인은 자못 수다스러워졌다."]], [["“I will tell you what, Fanny,”", "“내 너한테 이르마, 패니,” 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “I am sure he fell in love with you at the ball; I am sure the mischief was done that evening.", "“나는 그이가 무도회에서 너한테 반한 게 틀림없다 싶어. 그 사달이 그날 저녁에 난 게 분명해."], ["You did look remarkably well.", "너는 정말 유난히 고와 보였거든."], ["Everybody said so.", "다들 그리 말했지."], ["Sir Thomas said so.", "토머스 경도 그리 말했고."], ["And you know you had Chapman to help you to dress.", "그리고 너도 알다시피 채프먼이 네 옷 입는 걸 거들었잖니."], ["I am very glad I sent Chapman to you.", "채프먼을 너한테 보낸 게 참 다행이야."], ["I shall tell Sir Thomas that I am sure it was done that evening.”", "토머스 경한테, 그게 그날 저녁에 벌어진 게 틀림없다고 말해야겠다.”"], ["And still pursuing the same cheerful thoughts, she soon afterwards added, “And I will tell you what, Fanny, which is more than I did for Maria: the next time Pug has a litter you shall have a puppy.”", "그리고 여전히 그 명랑한 생각을 좇으며, 이내 덧붙였다. “그리고 내 너한테 이르마, 패니, 이건 마리아한테 해 준 것보다 더한 건데—다음번에 퍼그가 새끼를 낳으면 너한테 강아지 한 마리 주마.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIV", "# 제34장"]], [["Edmund had great things to hear on his return.", "에드먼드는 돌아와서 들을 굉장한 일들이 있었다."], ["Many surprises were awaiting him.", "숱한 놀람이 그를 기다리고 있었다."], ["The first that occurred was not least in interest: the appearance of Henry Crawford and his sister walking together through the village as he rode into it.", "맨 처음 벌어진 것이 그 흥미로 치면 결코 뒤지지 않았으니, 그가 말을 타고 마을에 들어설 제 헨리 크로퍼드와 그 누이가 함께 마을을 걷고 있는 모습이었다."], ["He had concluded—he had meant them to be far distant.", "그는 그들이 멀리 떠났으려니 여겼다—떠나 있기를 바랐던 것이다."], ["His absence had been extended beyond a fortnight purposely to avoid Miss Crawford.", "그의 부재는 크로퍼드 양을 피하려고 일부러 두 주 넘게 늘어졌다."], ["He was returning to Mansfield with spirits ready to feed on melancholy remembrances, and tender associations, when her own fair self was before him, leaning on her brother’s arm, and he found himself receiving a welcome, unquestionably friendly, from the woman whom, two moments before, he had been thinking of as seventy miles off, and as farther, much farther, from him in inclination than any distance could express.", "그는 서글픈 추억과 애틋한 상념을 먹고 살 채비가 된 마음으로 맨스필드로 돌아오던 참인데, 바로 그 아리따운 그녀 자신이 제 오라비의 팔에 기댄 채 눈앞에 있었다. 그리하여 그는, 두 순간 전만 해도 칠십 마일이나 떨어져 있으려니, 아니 그 어떤 거리로도 다 나타낼 수 없을 만큼 마음으로는 훨씬, 훨씬 더 멀리 있으려니 여기던 그 여인에게서, 두말할 것 없이 정겨운 환대를 받는 자신을 발견했다."]], [["Her reception of him was of a sort which he could not have hoped for, had he expected to see her.", "그녀가 그를 맞이하는 품은, 설령 그가 그녀를 볼 줄 알았다 해도 바랄 수 없었을 그런 것이었다."], ["Coming as he did from such a purport fulfilled as had taken him away, he would have expected anything rather than a look of satisfaction, and words of simple, pleasant meaning.", "그를 떠나게 했던 그런 볼일을 다 이루고 돌아오는 마당이라, 그는 흡족한 낯빛이며 소박하고 정다운 뜻의 말 따위는 무엇보다도 가장 기대하지 않았으련만."], ["It was enough to set his heart in a glow, and to bring him home in the properest state for feeling the full value of the other joyful surprises at hand.", "그것은 그의 가슴을 달아오르게 하기에, 그리고 눈앞에 놓인 다른 기쁜 놀람들의 온전한 값어치를 느끼기에 더없이 알맞은 상태로 그를 집에 데려오기에 넉넉했다."]], [["William’s promotion, with all its particulars, he was soon master of; and with such a secret provision of comfort within his own breast to help the joy, he found in it a source of most gratifying sensation and unvarying cheerfulness all dinner-time.", "윌리엄의 진급을 그 온갖 시시콜콜한 대목까지 그는 이내 훤히 알게 되었다. 그리고 그 기쁨을 도울 그런 은밀한 위안의 밑천을 제 가슴에 지닌 터라, 그는 그 안에서 만찬 내내 더없이 흐뭇한 심경과 한결같은 명랑함의 샘을 찾아냈다."]], [["After dinner, when he and his father were alone, he had Fanny’s history; and then all the great events of the last fortnight, and the present situation of matters at Mansfield were known to him.", "만찬 뒤, 그와 아버지가 단둘이 있게 되자, 그는 패니의 사연을 들었다. 그리하여 지난 두 주의 온갖 큰 사건과, 맨스필드의 지금 형편이 그에게 다 알려졌다."]], [["Fanny suspected what was going on.", "패니는 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 짐작했다."], ["They sat so much longer than usual in the dining-parlour, that she was sure they must be talking of her; and when tea at last brought them away, and she was to be seen by Edmund again, she felt dreadfully guilty.", "그들이 여느 때보다 하도 오래 식당에 앉아 있어, 그녀는 그들이 제 얘기를 하고 있는 게 틀림없다 여겼다. 그리하여 마침내 차가 그들을 데려 나와 다시 에드먼드에게 저를 보이게 되었을 때, 그녀는 몹시 죄스러웠다."], ["He came to her, sat down by her, took her hand, and pressed it kindly; and at that moment she thought that, but for the occupation and the scene which the tea-things afforded, she must have betrayed her emotion in some unpardonable excess.", "그가 그녀에게 다가와, 곁에 앉아, 손을 잡고 다정하게 꼭 눌러 주었다. 그 순간 그녀는, 찻그릇이 마련해 준 그 일거리와 그 광경이 아니었더라면 저가 용서 못 할 지나침으로 제 심경을 드러내고 말았으리라 여겼다."]], [["He was not intending, however, by such action, to be conveying to her that unqualified approbation and encouragement which her hopes drew from it.", "그러나 그는 그런 행동으로, 그녀의 바람이 거기서 끌어낸 그런 온전한 찬동과 북돋움을 전하려던 것이 아니었다."], ["It was designed only to express his participation in all that interested her, and to tell her that he had been hearing what quickened every feeling of affection.", "그것은 그저 저를 흥미로워하는 온갖 것에 저도 한몫 낀다는 것을 나타내고, 온갖 애정을 북돋우는 것을 저가 들었노라 그녀에게 일러 주려던 것뿐이었다."], ["He was, in fact, entirely on his father’s side of the question.", "그는 실은 이 문제에서 온전히 제 아버지 편이었다."], ["His surprise was not so great as his father’s at her refusing Crawford, because, so far from supposing her to consider him with anything like a preference, he had always believed it to be rather the reverse, and could imagine her to be taken perfectly unprepared, but Sir Thomas could not regard the connexion as more desirable than he did.", "그녀가 크로퍼드를 마다한 데 대한 그의 놀라움은 아버지의 것만큼 크지 않았으니, 그녀가 그를 어떤 마음에 든다는 것 비슷하게라도 여긴다고는 여기기는커녕 늘 오히려 그 반대라 믿어 온 데다, 그녀가 아주 채비 안 된 채로 당했으려니 여길 수 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 토머스 경도 그만큼 이 인연을 바람직하게 여길 수는 없었다."], ["It had every recommendation to him; and while honouring her for what she had done under the influence of her present indifference, honouring her in rather stronger terms than Sir Thomas could quite echo, he was most earnest in hoping, and sanguine in believing, that it would be a match at last, and that, united by mutual affection, it would appear that their dispositions were as exactly fitted to make them blessed in each other, as he was now beginning seriously to consider them.", "그에게는 그것이 온갖 천거할 점을 갖춘 것이었다. 그리하여 지금의 무심함에 이끌려 그녀가 한 일을 두고 그녀를 기리면서—토머스 경이 온전히 맞장구칠 수 있는 것보다 다소 세찬 말로 그녀를 기리면서—그는 끝내 그것이 맺어지리라 더없이 간절히 바라고 낙천적으로 믿었고, 서로의 정으로 맺어지면 둘의 성미가, 그가 이제 진지하게 여기기 시작한 그대로, 서로에게서 복 받게 할 만큼 꼭 맞아떨어짐이 드러나리라 여겼다."], ["Crawford had been too precipitate.", "크로퍼드는 너무 성급했다."], ["He had not given her time to attach herself.", "그는 그녀에게 정 붙일 겨를을 주지 않았다."], ["He had begun at the wrong end.", "그는 엉뚱한 데서 시작한 것이다."], ["With such powers as his, however, and such a disposition as hers, Edmund trusted that everything would work out a happy conclusion.", "그러나 그와 같은 역량과 그녀와 같은 성미라면, 온갖 것이 행복한 결말로 풀리리라 에드먼드는 믿었다."], ["Meanwhile, he saw enough of Fanny’s embarrassment to make him scrupulously guard against exciting it a second time, by any word, or look, or movement.", "한편 그는 패니의 당혹함을 넉넉히 보았던지라, 무슨 말이나 눈길이나 몸짓으로도 그것을 두 번째로 자아내지 않도록 각별히 조심했다."]], [["Crawford called the next day, and on the score of Edmund’s return, Sir Thomas felt himself more than licensed to ask him to stay dinner; it was really a necessary compliment.", "크로퍼드가 이튿날 찾아왔고, 에드먼드가 돌아온 참이라 토머스 경은 그에게 만찬을 들고 가라 청할 자격이 넉넉하다 느꼈다. 그것은 정말이지 응당한 예의였다."], ["He staid of course, and Edmund had then ample opportunity for observing how he sped with Fanny, and what degree of immediate encouragement for him might be extracted from her manners; and it was so little, so very, very little—every chance, every possibility of it, resting upon her embarrassment only; if there was not hope in her confusion, there was hope in nothing else—that he was almost ready to wonder at his friend’s perseverance.", "그는 물론 머물렀고, 그리하여 에드먼드는 그가 패니에게서 어찌 나아가는지, 그녀의 태도에서 그를 위한 얼마간의 당장의 북돋움이 짜낼 수 있는지를 지켜볼 넉넉한 기회를 얻었다. 그런데 그것이 어찌나 적던지, 어찌나 무척, 무척이나 적던지—그 온갖 낌새, 온갖 가망이 오직 그녀의 당혹함에만 걸려 있었으니, 그녀의 얼떨떨함에 바람이 없다면 다른 어디에도 바람이 없었던지라—그는 제 벗의 끈질김을 의아해할 지경이었다."], ["Fanny was worth it all; he held her to be worth every effort of patience, every exertion of mind, but he did not think he could have gone on himself with any woman breathing, without something more to warm his courage than his eyes could discern in hers.", "패니는 그 온갖 값어치가 있었다. 그는 그녀가 온갖 참을성의 애씀, 온갖 마음의 수고를 들일 값어치가 있다 여겼다."], ["He was very willing to hope that Crawford saw clearer, and this was the most comfortable conclusion for his friend that he could come to from all that he observed to pass before, and at, and after dinner.", "그러나 그는, 제 눈이 그녀의 눈에서 알아볼 수 있는 것 이상으로 제 용기를 데워 줄 무언가 없이는, 세상 어떤 여자와도 저 자신은 밀고 나가지 못했으리라 여겼다. 그는 크로퍼드가 더 밝게 보기를 기꺼이 바랐고, 이것이야말로 만찬 전과 만찬 중과 만찬 뒤에 오가는 것을 죄다 지켜보고 그가 제 벗을 위해 이를 수 있는 가장 마음 놓이는 결론이었다."]], [["In the evening a few circumstances occurred which he thought more promising.", "저녁에 그가 좀 더 조짐 좋다 여긴 몇 가지 일이 벌어졌다."], ["When he and Crawford walked into the drawing-room, his mother and Fanny were sitting as intently and silently at work as if there were nothing else to care for.", "그와 크로퍼드가 응접실로 걸어 들어갔을 때, 그의 어머니와 패니는 마치 달리 마음 쓸 것이라곤 아무것도 없는 양 골똘히, 잠잠히 바느질을 하며 앉아 있었다."], ["Edmund could not help noticing their apparently deep tranquillity.", "에드먼드는 그들의 겉보기에 깊은 그 고요를 눈여겨보지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“We have not been so silent all the time,”", "“우리가 내내 이리 잠잠했던 건 아니란다.”"], ["replied his mother.", "그의 어머니가 대꾸했다."], ["“Fanny has been reading to me, and only put the book down upon hearing you coming.”", "“패니가 나한테 책을 읽어 주다가, 네가 오는 소리를 듣고서야 책을 내려놨을 뿐이야.”"], ["And sure enough there was a book on the table which had the air of being very recently closed: a volume of Shakespeare.", "과연 탁자 위에는 방금 막 덮은 듯한 책 한 권이 놓여 있었으니, 셰익스피어 한 권이었다."], ["“She often reads to me out of those books; and she was in the middle of a very fine speech of that man’s—what’s his name, Fanny?—when we heard your footsteps.”", "“저 애가 곧잘 저 책들을 나한테 읽어 준단다. 그리고 그 사람의—그 이름이 뭐더라, 패니?—어느 아주 훌륭한 대사 한복판에 있던 참에 우리가 네 발소리를 들은 거야.”"]], [["Crawford took the volume.", "크로퍼드가 그 책을 집어 들었다."], ["“Let me have the pleasure of finishing that speech to your ladyship,”", "“그 대사를 마저 읽어 드리는 즐거움을 제게 주시지요, 마님.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I shall find it immediately.”", "“이내 찾아내겠습니다.”"], ["And by carefully giving way to the inclination of the leaves, he did find it, or within a page or two, quite near enough to satisfy Lady Bertram, who assured him, as soon as he mentioned the name of Cardinal Wolsey, that he had got the very speech.", "그리고 책장이 저절로 넘어가려는 쪽을 조심스레 따라가, 그는 정말로 그것을 찾아냈으니, 한두 쪽 안팎으로 버트럼 부인을 흡족게 하기엔 더없이 가까웠다. 그가 울지 추기경의 이름을 대자마자 그녀는 그가 바로 그 대사를 찾았노라 장담했다."], ["Not a look or an offer of help had Fanny given; not a syllable for or against.", "패니는 눈길 한번, 돕겠다는 말 한마디 건네지 않았다. 편들거나 반대하는 한 마디도 없었다."], ["All her attention was for her work.", "그녀의 온 마음은 제 바느질에 쏠려 있었다."], ["She seemed determined to be interested by nothing else.", "그녀는 달리 아무것에도 흥미 없기로 굳게 마음먹은 듯했다. 그러나 취향이 그녀 안에서 너무 셌다."], ["But taste was too strong in her.", "그녀는 오 분도 제 마음을 딴 데 둘 수 없었다."], ["She could not abstract her mind five minutes: she was forced to listen; his reading was capital, and her pleasure in good reading extreme.", "하는 수 없이 귀를 기울였다. 그의 낭독은 빼어났고, 좋은 낭독에서 그녀가 얻는 즐거움은 지극했다."], ["To good reading, however, she had been long used: her uncle read well, her cousins all, Edmund very well, but in Mr.", "그러나 좋은 낭독에야 그녀는 진작부터 익어 있었다. 삼촌도 잘 읽었고, 사촌들도 다들, 에드먼드는 아주 잘 읽었다."], ["Crawford’s reading there was a variety of excellence beyond what she had ever met with.", "그러나 크로퍼드 씨의 낭독에는 그녀가 여태 만나 본 것을 넘어서는 여러 빼어남이 있었다. 왕과 왕비와 버킹엄과 울지와 크롬웰이 차례로 살아났다."], ["The King, the Queen, Buckingham, Wolsey, Cromwell, all were given in turn; for with the happiest knack, the happiest power of jumping and guessing, he could always alight at will on the best scene, or the best speeches of each; and whether it were dignity, or pride, or tenderness, or remorse, or whatever were to be expressed, he could do it with equal beauty.", "더없이 절묘한 재간, 건너뛰고 짐작해 내는 더없이 절묘한 힘으로, 그는 언제든 마음대로 각자의 가장 좋은 장면이나 가장 좋은 대사에 내려앉을 수 있었던 것이다. 그리고 위엄이든 오만이든 애틋함이든 뉘우침이든, 무엇을 나타내야 하든 그는 그것을 한결같은 아름다움으로 해낼 수 있었다."], ["It was truly dramatic.", "그것은 정녕 극적이었다."], ["His acting had first taught Fanny what pleasure a play might give, and his reading brought all his acting before her again; nay, perhaps with greater enjoyment, for it came unexpectedly, and with no such drawback as she had been used to suffer in seeing him on the stage with Miss Bertram.", "연극이 어떤 즐거움을 줄 수 있는지를 그의 연기가 처음 패니에게 가르쳐 주었었는데, 그의 낭독이 그 온갖 연기를 다시 그녀 앞에 데려온 것이다. 아니, 어쩌면 더 큰 즐거움과 함께였으니, 그것이 뜻밖에 왔고, 무대 위에서 그를 버트럼 양과 함께 보며 겪곤 하던 그런 씁쓸함이 없었기 때문이다."]], [["Edmund watched the progress of her attention, and was amused and gratified by seeing how she gradually slackened in the needlework, which at the beginning seemed to occupy her totally: how it fell from her hand while she sat motionless over it, and at last, how the eyes which had appeared so studiously to avoid him throughout the day were turned and fixed on Crawford—fixed on him for minutes, fixed on him, in short, till the attraction drew Crawford’s upon her, and the book was closed, and the charm was broken.", "에드먼드는 그녀의 마음이 쏠려 가는 것을 지켜보았고, 처음엔 온통 그녀를 붙들고 있는 듯하던 바느질이 어떻게 차츰 늘어지는지, 어떻게 그녀가 그것을 붙들고 꼼짝 않고 앉은 채 그것이 손에서 떨어지는지, 그리고 마침내 온종일 그토록 애써 저를 피하는 듯하던 그 눈이 어떻게 크로퍼드에게로 돌아가 박히는지—몇 분씩 그에게 박히는지, 요컨대 그 끌림이 크로퍼드의 눈길을 그녀에게로 끌어당겨 책이 덮이고 그 홀림이 깨질 때까지 그에게 박혀 있는지를 보며 재미나고 흐뭇했다. 그러자 그녀는 다시 제 안으로 움츠러들어, 여느 때 못지않게 얼굴을 붉히며 부지런히 바느질을 했다."], ["Then she was shrinking again into herself, and blushing and working as hard as ever; but it had been enough to give Edmund encouragement for his friend, and as he cordially thanked him, he hoped to be expressing Fanny’s secret feelings too.", "그러나 그것은 에드먼드에게 제 벗을 위한 북돋움을 주기에 넉넉했다. 그리하여 그가 진심으로 그에게 고마움을 표하며, 그는 패니의 은밀한 심경 또한 나타내고 있기를 바랐다."]], [["“That play must be a favourite with you,”", "“그 극이 자네가 좋아하는 것인가 보군.”"], ["said he; “you read as if you knew it well.”", "그가 말했다. “아주 잘 아는 것처럼 읽는 걸 보니.”"]], [["“It will be a favourite, I believe, from this hour,”", "“이 시각부터는 좋아하는 게 될 것 같네.”"], ["replied Crawford; “but I do not think I have had a volume of Shakespeare in my hand before since I was fifteen.", "크로퍼드가 대꾸했다. “하지만 열다섯 살 이후로 셰익스피어를 손에 든 적이 있는지 모르겠어."], ["I once saw Henry the Eighth acted, or I have heard of it from somebody who did, I am not certain which.", "언젠가 「헨리 8세」가 상연되는 걸 봤든지, 아니면 본 누군가한테서 들었든지, 어느 쪽인지도 확실치 않네."], ["But Shakespeare one gets acquainted with without knowing how.", "하지만 셰익스피어야 어떻게 알게 되는지도 모른 채 알게 되는 법이지."], ["It is a part of an Englishman’s constitution.", "그건 잉글랜드 사람 됨됨이의 한 부분이니까."], ["His thoughts and beauties are so spread abroad that one touches them everywhere; one is intimate with him by instinct.", "그의 생각과 아름다움이 워낙 널리 퍼져 있어 어디서나 그것에 닿게 되거든. 본능으로 그와 친해지는 거야."], ["No man of any brain can open at a good part of one of his plays without falling into the flow of his meaning immediately.”", "웬만큼 머리 있는 사람이라면 누구든 그의 극 가운데 좋은 대목을 펼치기만 하면 대번에 그 뜻의 흐름에 젖어 들지 않을 수 없지.”"]], [["“No doubt one is familiar with Shakespeare in a degree,”", "“누구든 얼마간 셰익스피어에 익어 있는 거야 두말할 것 없지.”"], ["said Edmund, “from one’s earliest years.", "에드먼드가 말했다. “어릴 적부터 말이야."], ["His celebrated passages are quoted by everybody; they are in half the books we open, and we all talk Shakespeare, use his similes, and describe with his descriptions; but this is totally distinct from giving his sense as you gave it.", "그의 그 이름난 구절들은 누구나 인용하고, 우리가 펼치는 책 절반에 그것들이 들어 있고, 우리 다 셰익스피어로 말하고, 그의 비유를 쓰고, 그의 묘사로 묘사하지. 하지만 이건 자네가 그 뜻을 살려 낸 것과는 아주 딴것이야."], ["To know him in bits and scraps is common enough; to know him pretty thoroughly is, perhaps, not uncommon; but to read him well aloud is no everyday talent.”", "그를 토막토막 조각조각 아는 거야 흔한 일이고, 그를 제법 속속들이 아는 것도 어쩌면 드물지 않지. 하지만 그를 소리 내어 잘 읽는 건 여느 재주가 아닐세.”"]], [["“Sir, you do me honour,”", "“선생, 과찬일세.”"], ["was Crawford’s answer, with a bow of mock gravity.", "짐짓 근엄한 목례와 함께한 크로퍼드의 대답이었다."]], [["Both gentlemen had a glance at Fanny, to see if a word of accordant praise could be extorted from her; yet both feeling that it could not be.", "두 신사 모두 패니를 슬쩍 보았으니, 그녀에게서 맞장구치는 칭찬 한마디라도 짜낼 수 있을까 해서였다. 그러면서도 둘 다 그럴 수 없으리라 느꼈다."], ["Her praise had been given in her attention; that must content them.", "그녀의 칭찬은 그 골똘함으로 이미 주어진 셈이었다. 그것으로 그들은 만족해야 했다."]], [["Lady Bertram’s admiration was expressed, and strongly too.", "버트럼 부인의 감탄이 드러났다, 그것도 세차게."], ["“It was really like being at a play,”", "“정말이지 연극을 보는 것 같았어요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“I wish Sir Thomas had been here.”", "“토머스 경이 여기 계셨더라면.”"]], [["Crawford was excessively pleased.", "크로퍼드는 몹시 기뻤다."], ["If Lady Bertram, with all her incompetency and languor, could feel this, the inference of what her niece, alive and enlightened as she was, must feel, was elevating.", "버트럼 부인이, 그 온갖 무능함과 나른함에도 이것을 느낄 수 있다면, 그 조카딸이—그토록 생기 있고 밝은 이가—느낄 것에 대한 짐작은 마음을 들뜨게 하는 것이었다."]], [["“You have a great turn for acting, I am sure, Mr. Crawford,”", "“분명 크로퍼드 씨는 연기에 아주 재주가 있어요.”"], ["said her ladyship soon afterwards; “and I will tell you what, I think you will have a theatre, some time or other, at your house in Norfolk.", "이내 마님이 말했다. “그리고 내 이르리다, 아무래도 언젠가 노퍽의 그 댁에 극장을 하나 두시겠어요."], ["I mean when you are settled there. I do indeed.", "거기 자리 잡은 뒤에 말이에요."], ["I think you will fit up a theatre at your house in Norfolk.”", "정말 그럴 것 같아요. 노퍽 그 댁에 극장을 하나 꾸미시겠어요.”"]], [["“Do you, ma’am?”", "“그러겠습니까, 마님?”"], ["cried he, with quickness.", "그가 얼른 외쳤다."], ["“No, no, that will never be.", "“아뇨, 아뇨, 그런 일은 결코 없을 겁니다."], ["Your ladyship is quite mistaken.", "마님께서 아주 잘못 짚으셨어요. 에버링엄에 극장이라니요!"], ["No theatre at Everingham! Oh no!”", "아, 안 됩니다!”"], ["And he looked at Fanny with an expressive smile, which evidently meant, “That lady will never allow a theatre at Everingham.”", "그러고는 뜻 어린 미소로 패니를 바라보았는데, 그것은 분명 “저 아가씨가 에버링엄에 극장을 결코 허락 안 할 겁니다” 하는 뜻이었다."]], [["Edmund saw it all, and saw Fanny so determined not to see it, as to make it clear that the voice was enough to convey the full meaning of the protestation; and such a quick consciousness of compliment, such a ready comprehension of a hint, he thought, was rather favourable than not.", "에드먼드는 그것을 죄다 보았고, 패니가 어찌나 그것을 안 보려 굳게 마음먹었던지, 그 목소리만으로도 그 항변의 온전한 뜻을 전하기에 넉넉함이 드러났다. 그리고 그런 재빠른 찬사의 알아챔, 그런 얼른 넌지시 비침을 알아들음은, 그가 여기기에 오히려 좋은 조짐이었다."]], [["The subject of reading aloud was farther discussed.", "소리 내어 읽는 일에 대한 화제가 더 이어졌다. 두 젊은이만이 이야기꾼이었으나, 그들은 난롯가에 서서, 이 소양이 너무 흔히 소홀히 여겨지는 것을, 사내아이들의 여느 학교 교육에서 그것이 통 마음 쓰이지 않는 것을, 그리하여 여느 자연스러운, 그러나 더러는 거의 부자연스러운 정도의, 지각 있고 박식한 사내들의 무지와 어설픔을—소리 내어 읽어야 할 필요에 문득 부딪혔을 때 저희 눈으로 본 그런 것을 두고 이야기했다."], ["The two young men were the only talkers, but they, standing by the fire, talked over the too common neglect of the qualification, the total inattention to it, in the ordinary school-system for boys, the consequently natural, yet in some instances almost unnatural, degree of ignorance and uncouthness of men, of sensible and well-informed men, when suddenly called to the necessity of reading aloud, which had fallen within their notice, giving instances of blunders, and failures with their secondary causes, the want of management of the voice, of proper modulation and emphasis, of foresight and judgment, all proceeding from the first cause: want of early attention and habit; and Fanny was listening again with great entertainment.", "실수와 실패의 사례를 그 부차적 원인들과 함께 들며—목소리를 다루지 못함, 알맞은 억양과 강조를 못 함, 미리 내다보고 판단하지 못함, 이 모두가 그 첫 원인에서 비롯함, 곧 어려서 마음 쓰고 버릇 들이지 못함에서 비롯함을 이야기했다. 그리고 패니는 다시금 자못 재미나게 귀 기울이고 있었다."]], [["“Even in my profession,”", "“내 직업에서조차,”"], ["said Edmund, with a smile, “how little the art of reading has been studied!", "에드먼드가 미소 지으며 말했다. “읽는 기예가 얼마나 안 익혀졌던지!"], ["how little a clear manner, and good delivery, have been attended to!", "또렷한 태도와 좋은 전달이 얼마나 안 마음 쓰였던지!"], ["I speak rather of the past, however, than the present.", "하기야 나는 지금보다 지난날을 두고 하는 말일세만."], ["There is now a spirit of improvement abroad; but among those who were ordained twenty, thirty, forty years ago, the larger number, to judge by their performance, must have thought reading was reading, and preaching was preaching.", "이제는 나아지려는 기운이 두루 퍼져 있어. 하지만 이삼십 년, 사십 년 전에 서품받은 이들 가운데 태반은, 그 하는 걸로 미루어 보건대, 읽는 건 읽는 거고 설교는 설교라 여겼던 게 틀림없어."], ["It is different now.", "이제는 달라졌지."], ["The subject is more justly considered.", "이 문제가 더 온당히 여겨지거든. 또렷함과 힘참이 더없이 굳건한 진리를 천거하는 데 무게를 실을 수 있음이 느껴지는 거야."], ["It is felt that distinctness and energy may have weight in recommending the most solid truths; and besides, there is more general observation and taste, a more critical knowledge diffused than formerly; in every congregation there is a larger proportion who know a little of the matter, and who can judge and criticise.”", "게다가 예전보다 두루 눈여겨봄과 안목이, 더 까다로운 앎이 널리 퍼져 있어. 어느 회중에나 이 일을 조금이라도 알아 판단하고 따질 줄 아는 이의 비율이 더 커졌지.”"]], [["Edmund had already gone through the service once since his ordination; and upon this being understood, he had a variety of questions from Crawford as to his feelings and success; questions, which being made, though with the vivacity of friendly interest and quick taste, without any touch of that spirit of banter or air of levity which Edmund knew to be most offensive to Fanny, he had true pleasure in satisfying; and when Crawford proceeded to ask his opinion and give his own as to the properest manner in which particular passages in the service should be delivered, shewing it to be a subject on which he had thought before, and thought with judgment, Edmund was still more and more pleased. This would be the way to Fanny’s heart. She was not to be won by all that gallantry and wit and good-nature together could do; or, at least, she would not be won by them nearly so soon, without the assistance of sentiment and feeling, and seriousness on serious subjects.", "에드먼드는 서품 뒤 이미 한 번 예배를 치른 참이었다. 그리고 이것이 알려지자, 그는 크로퍼드에게서 그 심경과 성과에 대한 여러 물음을 받았다. 그 물음들은, 정겨운 관심과 재빠른 안목의 발랄함으로 던져지되, 에드먼드가 패니에게 더없이 거슬리는 줄 아는 그 놀림의 기색이나 가벼운 태도라곤 조금도 없이 던져진 것이라, 그는 그에 답하는 것이 정말로 즐거웠다. 그리고 크로퍼드가 나아가, 예배 가운데 특정 대목을 어떻게 전하는 것이 가장 온당한지 제 의견을 청하고 제 것을 내놓으며, 그것이 저가 앞서 생각해 본, 그것도 분별 있게 생각해 본 문제임을 보이니, 에드먼드는 더욱더 기뻤다. 이것이야말로 패니의 마음에 이르는 길일 터였다. 그녀는 정겨움과 재치와 착한 성미가 한데 힘써 할 수 있는 온갖 것으로는 얻어질 사람이 아니었다. 아니, 적어도 진지한 화제에서의 심경과 정과 진지함의 도움 없이는 그리 이내 얻어질 사람이 아니었다."]], [["“Our liturgy,”", "“우리 전례문에는,”"], ["observed Crawford, “has beauties, which not even a careless, slovenly style of reading can destroy; but it has also redundancies and repetitions which require good reading not to be felt.", "크로퍼드가 소감을 밝혔다. “아무리 무심하고 데면데면한 낭독으로도 망칠 수 없는 아름다움이 있지."], ["For myself, at least, I must confess being not always so attentive as I ought to be”", "하지만 좋은 낭독이라야 안 느껴지는 군더더기와 되풀이 또한 있고. 나 자신으로 말하면, 적어도, 마땅한 만큼 늘 마음을 쏟지는 못함을 인정해야겠네”"], ["(here was a glance at Fanny); “that nineteen times out of twenty I am thinking how such a prayer ought to be read, and longing to have it to read myself.", "(여기서 패니를 슬쩍 보았다). “스무 번에 열아홉 번은 이런 기도를 어떻게 읽어야 할까 궁리하며, 그걸 내가 직접 읽어 봤으면 하고 못 견디거든."], ["Did you speak?”", "뭐라 하셨어요?”"], ["stepping eagerly to Fanny, and addressing her in a softened voice; and upon her saying “No,”", "얼른 패니에게 다가서서 부드러워진 목소리로 그녀에게 말을 건네더니, 그녀가 “아뇨”"], ["he added, “Are you sure you did not speak?", "하자 그가 덧붙였다. “정말 아무 말도 안 하셨어요?"], ["I saw your lips move.", "입술이 움직이는 걸 봤는데."], ["I fancied you might be going to tell me I ought to be more attentive, and not allow my thoughts to wander.", "나더러 마음을 더 쏟아야 한다고, 딴생각이 나돌게 놔둬선 안 된다고 이르시려나 했지요."], ["Are not you going to tell me so?”", "그리 이르시려는 것 아니에요?”"]], [["“No, indeed, you know your duty too well for me to—even supposing—”", "“아뇨, 정말이지, 그이는 제 도리를 너무 잘 아셔서 제가—설령 그렇다 쳐도—”"]], [["She stopt, felt herself getting into a puzzle, and could not be prevailed on to add another word, not by dint of several minutes of supplication and waiting.", "그녀는 말을 그쳤다. 저가 헷갈리는 데로 빠져드는 것을 느꼈고, 몇 분간의 애원과 기다림으로도 한마디도 더 붙이도록 구슬려지지 않았다."], ["He then returned to his former station, and went on as if there had been no such tender interruption.", "그리하여 그는 아까 자리로 돌아가, 마치 그런 애틋한 끼어듦이라곤 없었던 양 말을 이었다."]], [["“A sermon, well delivered, is more uncommon even than prayers well read.", "“설교를 잘 전하는 건 기도를 잘 읽는 것보다 더욱 드물지."], ["A sermon, good in itself, is no rare thing.", "그 자체로 좋은 설교야 드문 게 아니야."], ["It is more difficult to speak well than to compose well; that is, the rules and trick of composition are oftener an object of study.", "잘 짓기보다 잘 말하기가 더 어려운 법이거든. 그러니까, 짓는 법과 요령이 더 자주 익힘의 대상이 된다는 거지."], ["A thoroughly good sermon, thoroughly well delivered, is a capital gratification.", "속속들이 좋은 설교를 속속들이 잘 전하면, 그건 더없는 흐뭇함이야."], ["I can never hear such a one without the greatest admiration and respect, and more than half a mind to take orders and preach myself.", "나는 그런 걸 듣고서 더없는 감복과 존경 없이, 나도 성직을 받아 직접 설교하고 싶은 마음을 절반 넘게 품지 않은 적이 없어."], ["There is something in the eloquence of the pulpit, when it is really eloquence, which is entitled to the highest praise and honour.", "강단의 웅변에는, 그것이 정말로 웅변일 때는, 더없는 칭찬과 영광을 받을 만한 무언가가 있어."], ["The preacher who can touch and affect such an heterogeneous mass of hearers, on subjects limited, and long worn threadbare in all common hands; who can say anything new or striking, anything that rouses the attention without offending the taste, or wearing out the feelings of his hearers, is a man whom one could not, in his public capacity, honour enough.", "그토록 갖가지 뒤섞인 청중을, 그것도 뻔하고 온갖 예사로운 손에서 오래 낡을 대로 낡은 화제를 두고 감동시키고 움직일 수 있는 이, 안목을 거스르거나 청중의 감정을 지치게 하지 않으면서 무언가 새롭거나 눈에 띄는 것을, 마음을 흔드는 무언가를 말할 수 있는 이, 그런 이야말로 그 공적인 소임에서 아무리 기려도 모자랄 사람이지."], ["I should like to be such a man.”", "나는 그런 사람이 되고 싶어.”"]], [["Edmund laughed.", "에드먼드가 웃었다."]], [["“I should indeed.", "“정말 그러고 싶다니까."], ["I never listened to a distinguished preacher in my life without a sort of envy.", "나는 평생 빼어난 설교자의 말을 들으며 일종의 부러움 없이 들어 본 적이 없어. 하지만 그러려면 나는 런던 청중이 있어야 해."], ["But then, I must have a London audience.", "교양 있는 이들, 내 지은 것을 가늠할 줄 아는 이들 말고는 설교할 수가 없거든."], ["I could not preach but to the educated; to those who were capable of estimating my composition.", "그리고 자주 설교하기를 좋아할 것 같지도 않아. 이따금, 어쩌면 봄에 한두 번, 여섯 주 일요일 내내 애타게 기다려진 끝에라면 몰라도."], ["And I do not know that I should be fond of preaching often; now and then, perhaps once or twice in the spring, after being anxiously expected for half a dozen Sundays together; but not for a constancy; it would not do for a constancy.”", "늘 하는 건 안 돼. 그건 늘 할 노릇이 못 되지.”"]], [["Here Fanny, who could not but listen, involuntarily shook her head, and Crawford was instantly by her side again, entreating to know her meaning; and as Edmund perceived, by his drawing in a chair, and sitting down close by her, that it was to be a very thorough attack, that looks and undertones were to be well tried, he sank as quietly as possible into a corner, turned his back, and took up a newspaper, very sincerely wishing that dear little Fanny might be persuaded into explaining away that shake of the head to the satisfaction of her ardent lover; and as earnestly trying to bury every sound of the business from himself in murmurs of his own, over the various advertisements of “A most desirable Estate in South Wales”; “To Parents and Guardians”; and a “Capital season’d Hunter.”", "이때 귀 기울이지 않을 수 없던 패니가 저도 모르게 고개를 저었고, 크로퍼드는 대번에 다시 그녀 곁에 와서 그 뜻을 알려 달라 졸랐다. 그리고 그가 의자를 끌어와 그녀 바로 곁에 앉는 것을 보고, 에드먼드는 이것이 자못 철저한 공략이 되리라는 것을, 눈길과 낮은 목소리가 실컷 시험되리라는 것을 알아챈지라, 그는 되도록 조용히 한구석으로 물러나, 등을 돌리고 신문을 집어 들며, 정녕 진심으로 그 사랑스러운 어린 패니가 그 고개 저음을 그 뜨거운 사랑하는 이가 흡족하도록 잘 풀어 설명하도록 구슬려지기를 바랐다. 그리고 “사우스웨일스의 더없이 탐나는 영지”와 “부모와 후견인께”와 “잘 길든 훌륭한 사냥말”이라는 갖가지 광고를 두고 제 나름의 웅얼거림으로 그 일의 온갖 소리를 저 자신에게서 묻어 버리려 못지않게 애썼다."]], [["Fanny, meanwhile, vexed with herself for not having been as motionless as she was speechless, and grieved to the heart to see Edmund’s arrangements, was trying by everything in the power of her modest, gentle nature, to repulse Mr.", "한편 패니는, 말이 없던 만큼 몸도 꼼짝 않고 있지 못한 것을 두고 저 자신에게 언짢아하고, 에드먼드의 그 채비를 보고 가슴 저리게 서러워하며, 제 겸손하고 상냥한 천성이 할 수 있는 온갖 것으로 크로퍼드 씨를 물리치고 그의 눈길도 물음도 다 피하려 애쓰고 있었다."], ["Crawford, and avoid both his looks and inquiries; and he, unrepulsable, was persisting in both.", "그리고 그는, 물리쳐지지 않은 채, 그 둘 다를 고집하고 있었다."]], [["“What did that shake of the head mean?”", "“그 고개 저음이 무슨 뜻이었어요?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“What was it meant to express?", "“뭘 나타내려던 거예요?"], ["Disapprobation, I fear.", "못마땅함이려니 싶은데."], ["But of what?", "하지만 무엇에 대한?"], ["What had I been saying to displease you?", "내가 무슨 말을 해서 양의 비위를 거슬렀나요?"], ["Did you think me speaking improperly, lightly, irreverently on the subject?", "내가 이 화제를 두고 온당찮게, 가볍게, 불경하게 말한다 여기셨어요?"], ["Only tell me if I was.", "그랬는지만 말해 줘요."], ["Only tell me if I was wrong.", "내가 틀렸는지만 말해 줘요."], ["I want to be set right.", "나는 바로잡히고 싶어요."], ["Nay, nay, I entreat you; for one moment put down your work.", "아뇨, 아뇨, 부탁이에요. 한순간만 바느질을 내려놔요."], ["What did that shake of the head mean?”", "그 고개 저음이 무슨 뜻이었어요?”"]], [["In vain was her “Pray, sir, don’t; pray, Mr. Crawford,”", "두 번이나 되풀이한 그녀의 “제발, 그러지 마세요."], ["repeated twice over; and in vain did she try to move away.", "제발, 크로퍼드 씨” 도 허사였고, 자리를 뜨려 애써 봐도 허사였다."], ["In the same low, eager voice, and the same close neighbourhood, he went on, reurging the same questions as before.", "그 똑같이 낮고 열띤 목소리로, 그 똑같이 바짝 붙은 채, 그는 아까 그 물음들을 다시 조르며 말을 이었다."], ["She grew more agitated and displeased.", "그녀는 더 마음 졸이고 언짢아졌다."]], [["“How can you, sir?", "“어쩜 그러세요?"], ["You quite astonish me; I wonder how you can—”", "정말 기가 막혀요. 대체 어쩜 그럴 수—”"]], [["“Do I astonish you?” said he.", "“내가 기가 막히게 해요?”"], ["“Do you wonder?", "그가 말했다."], ["Is there anything in my present entreaty that you do not understand?", "“의아해요? 지금 내 이 간청에 양이 못 알아들을 게 뭐가 있어요?"], ["I will explain to you instantly all that makes me urge you in this manner, all that gives me an interest in what you look and do, and excites my present curiosity.", "내가 이런 식으로 양을 조르게 하는 온갖 것을, 양의 낯빛과 행동에 내가 관심을 갖게 하는 온갖 것을, 지금 내 이 궁금함을 자아내는 온갖 것을 당장 양한테 설명해 드리죠."], ["I will not leave you to wonder long.”", "오래 의아해하게 놔두진 않겠어요.”"]], [["In spite of herself, she could not help half a smile, but she said nothing.", "저도 모르게 그녀는 반쯤의 미소를 짓지 않을 수 없었으나, 아무 말도 안 했다."]], [["“You shook your head at my acknowledging that I should not like to engage in the duties of a clergyman always for a constancy.", "“내가, 성직자의 소임을 늘 한결같이 맡고 싶지는 않다고 인정한 데다 양이 고개를 저었죠."], ["Yes, that was the word.", "그래요, 그게 그 낱말이었어요."], ["Constancy: I am not afraid of the word.", "한결같음. 나는 그 낱말이 두렵지 않아요."], ["I would spell it, read it, write it with anybody.", "나는 누구 앞에서든 그걸 또박또박 말하고, 읽고, 쓸 수 있어요."], ["I see nothing alarming in the word.", "나는 그 낱말에서 겁날 게 조금도 안 보이는데."], ["Did you think I ought?”", "내가 그래야 마땅하다 여기셨어요?”"]], [["“Perhaps, sir,”", "“어쩌면요,”"], ["said Fanny, wearied at last into speaking—“perhaps, sir, I thought it was a pity you did not always know yourself as well as you seemed to do at that moment.”", "마침내 지쳐 입을 연 패니가 말했다. “어쩌면 저는, 그이가 그 순간엔 그토록 저를 잘 아는 듯하시더니 늘 그렇게 저를 알진 못하시는 게 딱하다 여겼는지도 몰라요.”"]], [["Crawford, delighted to get her to speak at any rate, was determined to keep it up; and poor Fanny, who had hoped to silence him by such an extremity of reproof, found herself sadly mistaken, and that it was only a change from one object of curiosity and one set of words to another.", "크로퍼드는, 아무튼 그녀가 입을 열게 만든 것이 기뻐, 그것을 이어 가기로 굳게 마음먹었다. 그리하여 그런 극단의 나무람으로 그를 잠잠케 하리라 바랐던 딱한 패니는, 저가 서글피 잘못 짚었음을, 그리고 그것이 그저 한 궁금함의 대상과 한 무리의 말에서 다른 것으로 옮아간 것일 뿐임을 알았다."], ["He had always something to entreat the explanation of.", "그에게는 늘 설명을 졸라 댈 무언가가 있었다."], ["The opportunity was too fair.", "기회는 너무 좋았다."], ["None such had occurred since his seeing her in her uncle’s room, none such might occur again before his leaving Mansfield.", "삼촌 방에서 그녀를 본 이래 이런 기회는 없었고, 맨스필드를 떠나기 전에 이런 기회가 다시 올 리 없었다."], ["Lady Bertram’s being just on the other side of the table was a trifle, for she might always be considered as only half-awake, and Edmund’s advertisements were still of the first utility.", "버트럼 부인이 탁자 바로 건너편에 있는 것쯤은 대수롭잖았으니, 그녀야 늘 반쯤 조는 사람으로만 여기면 됐고, 에드먼드의 광고들도 여전히 더없이 요긴했던 것이다."]], [["“Well,”", "“자,”"], ["said Crawford, after a course of rapid questions and reluctant answers; “I am happier than I was, because I now understand more clearly your opinion of me.", "재빠른 물음과 마지못한 대답이 한바탕 오간 뒤 크로퍼드가 말했다. “나는 아까보다 더 행복해요, 이제 나에 대한 양의 생각을 더 또렷이 알았으니까요."], ["You think me unsteady: easily swayed by the whim of the moment, easily tempted, easily put aside.", "양은 나를 미덥지 못하다고, 그때그때의 변덕에 쉬 휘둘리고, 쉬 홀리고, 쉬 밀려나는 사람이라 여기시죠."], ["With such an opinion, no wonder that—But we shall see.—It is not by protestations that I shall endeavour to convince you I am wronged; it is not by telling you that my affections are steady.", "그런 생각이라면, 놀랄 것도 없이—하지만 두고 봅시다.—내가 억울하다는 걸 양한테 납득시키려 애쓰는 건 항변으로가 아니에요. 내 정이 굳건하다고 양한테 말함으로써가 아니고요."], ["My conduct shall speak for me; absence, distance, time shall speak for me.", "내 처신이 나를 대신해 말할 겁니다. 떨어져 있음이, 거리가, 시간이 나를 대신해 말할 거예요."], ["They shall prove that, as far as you can be deserved by anybody, I do deserve you.", "그것들이 증명할 겁니다, 누구에게든 양이 받을 만한 그 만큼은 나도 양을 받을 만함을."], ["You are infinitely my superior in merit; all that I know.", "양은 값어치에서 나보다 한없이 나은 사람이에요."], ["You have qualities which I had not before supposed to exist in such a degree in any human creature.", "그거야 다 알아요. 양한테는, 어떤 사람한테도 그만한 정도로 있으리라곤 여태 여기지 못한 자질들이 있어요."], ["You have some touches of the angel in you beyond what—not merely beyond what one sees, because one never sees anything like it—but beyond what one fancies might be.", "양한테는 천사 같은 데가 얼마간 있어요, 그저 눈에 보이는 것을 넘어서—그런 건 눈에 보이지도 않으니까요—아니, 사람이 있으려니 상상하는 것마저 넘어서요."], ["But still I am not frightened.", "하지만 그래도 나는 겁나지 않아요."], ["It is not by equality of merit that you can be won.", "양은 값어치가 대등해서 얻어질 사람이 아니에요."], ["That is out of the question.", "그건 어림없는 일이죠."], ["It is he who sees and worships your merit the strongest, who loves you most devotedly, that has the best right to a return.", "양의 값어치를 가장 세차게 알아보고 우러르는 이, 양을 가장 헌신적으로 사랑하는 이야말로 화답받을 가장 큰 권리를 지녔죠."], ["There I build my confidence.", "거기에 나는 내 확신을 세워요."], ["By that right I do and will deserve you; and when once convinced that my attachment is what I declare it, I know you too well not to entertain the warmest hopes.", "그 권리로 나는 양을 받을 만하고, 또 받을 만해질 겁니다. 그리고 내 애착이 내가 말한 그대로임을 일단 확신하게 되면, 나는 양을 너무 잘 알기에 더없이 뜨거운 바람을 품지 않을 수 없어요."], ["Yes, dearest, sweetest Fanny.", "그래요, 더없이 사랑하고 더없이 고운 패니."], ["Nay”", "아뇨”"], ["(seeing her draw back displeased), “forgive me.", "(그녀가 언짢아 물러나는 것을 보고), “용서해요."], ["Perhaps I have as yet no right; but by what other name can I call you?", "어쩌면 나한테 아직 그럴 권리는 없겠지요. 하지만 달리 무슨 이름으로 양을 부르겠어요?"], ["Do you suppose you are ever present to my imagination under any other?", "양이 내 상상 속에 다른 어떤 이름으로 떠오르리라 여기세요?"], ["No, it is ‘Fanny’ that I think of all day, and dream of all night.", "아니에요, 내가 온종일 생각하고 밤새 꿈꾸는 건 바로 ‘패니’예요."], ["You have given the name such reality of sweetness, that nothing else can now be descriptive of you.”", "양은 그 이름에 그토록 참된 고움을 입혀 놔서, 이제 다른 무엇으로도 양을 그려 낼 수가 없어요.”"]], [["Fanny could hardly have kept her seat any longer, or have refrained from at least trying to get away in spite of all the too public opposition she foresaw to it, had it not been for the sound of approaching relief, the very sound which she had been long watching for, and long thinking strangely delayed.", "패니는, 다가오는 구원의 소리가—그녀가 오래 기다려 온, 그리고 이상하게도 늦어진다 오래 여기던 바로 그 소리가 아니었더라면, 도무지 자리를 더 지키지도, 저가 내다본 그 온갖 너무 남의 눈에 띄는 반대를 무릅쓰고라도 자리를 뜨려 애써 보기를 삼가지도 못했으리라."]], [["The solemn procession, headed by Baddeley, of tea-board, urn, and cake-bearers, made its appearance, and delivered her from a grievous imprisonment of body and mind.", "배들리를 앞세운, 찻쟁반과 주전자와 케이크를 나르는 이들의 그 엄숙한 행렬이 모습을 드러내, 그녀를 몸과 마음의 그 괴로운 갇힘에서 풀어 주었다."], ["Mr. Crawford was obliged to move.", "크로퍼드는 하는 수 없이 자리를 옮겨야 했다."], ["She was at liberty, she was busy, she was protected.", "그녀는 놓여났고, 바빠졌고, 지켜졌다."]], [["Edmund was not sorry to be admitted again among the number of those who might speak and hear.", "에드먼드는 다시 말하고 들을 수 있는 이들 축에 끼게 된 것이 싫지 않았다."], ["But though the conference had seemed full long to him, and though on looking at Fanny he saw rather a flush of vexation, he inclined to hope that so much could not have been said and listened to without some profit to the speaker.", "그러나 그 대면이 그에게는 자못 길게 여겨졌음에도, 또 패니를 보니 오히려 언짢음의 홍조가 어렸음에도, 그는 그토록 많은 말이 오가고 들렸으니 그 말한 이에게 얼마간 득이 없지는 않았으리라 기꺼이 바랐다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXV", "# 제35장"]], [["Edmund had determined that it belonged entirely to Fanny to chuse whether her situation with regard to Crawford should be mentioned between them or not; and that if she did not lead the way, it should never be touched on by him; but after a day or two of mutual reserve, he was induced by his father to change his mind, and try what his influence might do for his friend.", "에드먼드는, 크로퍼드에 관한 제 처지를 둘 사이에 입에 올릴지 말지는 온전히 패니가 고를 몫이라고, 그리고 그녀가 먼저 물꼬를 트지 않으면 저는 결코 그것을 건드리지 않으리라 마음먹은 터였다. 그러나 하루 이틀 서로 삼가며 지낸 끝에, 그는 아버지의 권함에 마음을 바꾸어, 제 힘이 벗을 위해 무엇을 할 수 있을지 시험해 보게 되었다."]], [["A day, and a very early day, was actually fixed for the Crawfords’ departure; and Sir Thomas thought it might be as well to make one more effort for the young man before he left Mansfield, that all his professions and vows of unshaken attachment might have as much hope to sustain them as possible.", "크로퍼드 남매의 떠날 날이, 그것도 자못 이른 날이 실제로 정해졌다. 그리하여 토머스 경은, 그 젊은이가 맨스필드를 떠나기 전에 그를 위해 한 번 더 애써 두는 것이, 그 흔들림 없는 애착의 온갖 다짐과 맹세를 되도록 오래 떠받쳐 줄 바람이 되리라 여겼다."]], [["Sir Thomas was most cordially anxious for the perfection of Mr. Crawford’s character in that point.", "토머스 경은 그 점에서 크로퍼드 씨의 사람됨이 온전하기를 더없이 진심으로 바랐다."], ["He wished him to be a model of constancy; and fancied the best means of effecting it would be by not trying him too long.", "그는 그가 한결같음의 본보기이기를 바랐고, 그것을 이룰 가장 좋은 방편은 그를 너무 오래 시험하지 않는 것이려니 여겼다."]], [["Edmund was not unwilling to be persuaded to engage in the business; he wanted to know Fanny’s feelings.", "에드먼드는 이 일을 맡으라 설득당하는 것이 싫지 않았다. 그는 패니의 심경을 알고 싶었다."], ["She had been used to consult him in every difficulty, and he loved her too well to bear to be denied her confidence now; he hoped to be of service to her, he thought he must be of service to her; whom else had she to open her heart to?", "그녀는 온갖 어려움에서 그와 의논하는 데 익어 있었고, 그는 그녀를 너무 사랑해 이제 그녀의 속내를 못 듣는 것을 견딜 수 없었다. 그는 그녀에게 쓸모가 되기를 바랐고, 저가 그녀에게 쓸모가 되어야 한다 여겼다."], ["If she did not need counsel, she must need the comfort of communication.", "달리 누구에게 그녀가 마음을 열 수 있으랴? 조언이 필요치 않다면, 털어놓음의 위안이라도 필요할 터였다."], ["Fanny estranged from him, silent and reserved, was an unnatural state of things; a state which he must break through, and which he could easily learn to think she was wanting him to break through.", "저에게서 멀어져, 잠잠하고 삼가는 패니는 부자연스러운 형편이었다. 그가 깨뜨려야 할, 그리고 그녀가 깨뜨려 주기를 바라고 있으려니 쉬 여길 만한 그런 형편이었다."]], [["“I will speak to her, sir: I will take the first opportunity of speaking to her alone,”", "“제가 그 애한테 말해 보지요, 아버지. 단둘이 얘기할 첫 기회를 잡겠습니다.”"], ["was the result of such thoughts as these; and upon Sir Thomas’s information of her being at that very time walking alone in the shrubbery, he instantly joined her.", "이런 생각들의 결말이 그러했다. 그리고 그녀가 바로 그때 관목 산책로에서 혼자 거닐고 있다는 토머스 경의 일러 줌에, 그는 대번에 그녀에게 다가갔다."]], [["“I am come to walk with you, Fanny,”", "“너하고 걸으려고 왔다, 패니.” 그가 말했다."], ["said he. “Shall I?”", "“그래도 되지?”"], ["Drawing her arm within his.", "그녀의 팔을 제 팔에 끼며."], ["“It is a long while since we have had a comfortable walk together.”", "“우리가 함께 오붓하게 걸어 본 지가 오래됐구나.”"]], [["She assented to it all rather by look than word.", "그녀는 말보다는 낯빛으로 그 모두에 응했다."], ["Her spirits were low.", "그녀의 기운은 가라앉아 있었다."]], [["“But, Fanny,”", "“하지만, 패니,”"], ["he presently added, “in order to have a comfortable walk, something more is necessary than merely pacing this gravel together.", "그가 이내 덧붙였다. “오붓하게 걸으려면, 이 자갈길을 그저 함께 거니는 것 말고 무언가가 더 있어야 해."], ["You must talk to me.", "넌 나한테 얘기해야 한다."], ["I know you have something on your mind.", "네 마음에 뭔가 걸린 게 있는 걸 나는 알아."], ["I know what you are thinking of.", "네가 무슨 생각을 하는지 나는 알고."], ["You cannot suppose me uninformed.", "내가 아무것도 모르리라곤 여기지 못할 게다."], ["Am I to hear of it from everybody but Fanny herself?”", "나는 이걸 패니 자신 말고 다른 온갖 사람한테서 들어야 하겠니?”"]], [["Fanny, at once agitated and dejected, replied, “If you hear of it from everybody, cousin, there can be nothing for me to tell.”", "패니는, 마음 졸이면서도 풀 죽은 채 대답했다. “다른 온갖 사람한테서 들으신다면, 오빠, 제가 얘기할 건 아무것도 없겠네요.”"]], [["“Not of facts, perhaps; but of feelings, Fanny.", "“사실이야 아마 그렇겠지. 하지만 심경은, 패니."], ["No one but you can tell me them.", "그건 너 말고 아무도 나한테 못 얘기해."], ["I do not mean to press you, however.", "하지만 나야 너를 조를 뜻은 없다."], ["If it is not what you wish yourself, I have done.", "이게 네가 바라는 게 아니라면 나는 그만하마."], ["I had thought it might be a relief.”", "나는 이게 얼마간 위안이 되지 않을까 여겼거든.”"]], [["“I am afraid we think too differently for me to find any relief in talking of what I feel.”", "“아무래도 우리 생각이 너무 달라서, 제가 느끼는 걸 얘기해 봐야 아무 위안도 못 찾을 것 같아요.”"]], [["“Do you suppose that we think differently?", "“우리 생각이 다르다고 여기니?"], ["I have no idea of it.", "나는 그런 줄 몰랐다."], ["I dare say that, on a comparison of our opinions, they would be found as much alike as they have been used to be: to the point—I consider Crawford’s proposals as most advantageous and desirable, if you could return his affection.", "감히 말하건대, 우리 의견을 견줘 보면 늘 그래 왔던 만큼이나 서로 닮았을걸. 요점을 말하면—나는 크로퍼드의 청혼을, 네가 그 정에 화답할 수만 있다면, 더없이 이롭고 바람직한 것으로 여긴다."], ["I consider it as most natural that all your family should wish you could return it; but that, as you cannot, you have done exactly as you ought in refusing him.", "나는 네 온 식구가 네가 화답할 수 있기를 바라는 게 더없이 자연스럽다 여기고. 하지만 네가 그럴 수 없는 마당이니, 그를 마다한 것은 네가 마땅히 해야 할 바로 그대로 한 거야."], ["Can there be any disagreement between us here?”", "여기 우리 사이에 무슨 어긋남이 있을 수 있겠니?”"]], [["“Oh no!", "“어머, 아뇨!"], ["But I thought you blamed me.", "하지만 저는 오빠가 저를 나무라시는 줄 알았어요."], ["I thought you were against me.", "오빠가 저를 거스르시는 줄 알았고요."], ["This is such a comfort!”", "이거 참 위안이 돼요!”"]], [["“This comfort you might have had sooner, Fanny, had you sought it.", "“이 위안을 넌 더 일찍 얻을 수도 있었어, 패니, 네가 구했더라면."], ["But how could you possibly suppose me against you?", "하지만 어떻게 나를 너를 거스르는 사람이라 여길 수 있었니?"], ["How could you imagine me an advocate for marriage without love?", "어떻게 나를 사랑 없는 혼인을 지지하는 사람이라 여겼어?"], ["Were I even careless in general on such matters, how could you imagine me so where your happiness was at stake?”", "설령 내가 그런 일에 두루 무심하다 해도, 네 행복이 걸린 데서야 어떻게 나를 그러리라 여기겠니?”"]], [["“My uncle thought me wrong, and I knew he had been talking to you.”", "“삼촌은 제가 틀렸다 여기셨고, 저는 삼촌이 오빠하고 얘기하신 걸 알았거든요.”"]], [["“As far as you have gone, Fanny, I think you perfectly right.", "“네가 여태 한 데까지로 치면, 패니, 나는 네가 아주 옳다 여긴다."], ["I may be sorry, I may be surprised—though hardly that, for you had not had time to attach yourself—but I think you perfectly right.", "서운할지도, 놀랄지도 몰라—하기야 그건 아니지, 넌 정 붙일 겨를이 없었으니까—하지만 나는 네가 아주 옳다 여겨."], ["Can it admit of a question?", "여기에 무슨 물음의 여지가 있을 수 있니?"], ["It is disgraceful to us if it does.", "있다면 그건 우리한테 부끄러운 일이야."], ["You did not love him; nothing could have justified your accepting him.”", "너는 그를 사랑하지 않았어. 무엇으로도 네가 그를 받아들이는 게 정당화될 수 없었지.”"]], [["Fanny had not felt so comfortable for days and days.", "패니는 여러 날, 여러 날 동안 이토록 마음 편해 본 적이 없었다."]], [["“So far your conduct has been faultless, and they were quite mistaken who wished you to do otherwise.", "“여태 네 처신은 흠 없었어. 그리고 네가 달리하기를 바란 이들은 아주 잘못 짚은 거고."], ["But the matter does not end here.", "하지만 일이 여기서 끝나진 않아."], ["Crawford’s is no common attachment; he perseveres, with the hope of creating that regard which had not been created before.", "크로퍼드의 것은 여느 애착이 아니야. 그는 물러서지 않지, 앞서 생겨나지 않은 그 정을 자아내리라는 바람으로 말이야."], ["This, we know, must be a work of time. But”", "이건, 우리도 알다시피, 시간의 일일 수밖에 없어."], ["(with an affectionate smile) “let him succeed at last, Fanny, let him succeed at last.", "하지만” (애정 어린 미소와 함께) “끝내 그가 뜻을 이루게 해 줘라, 패니, 끝내 이루게 해 줘."], ["You have proved yourself upright and disinterested, prove yourself grateful and tender-hearted; and then you will be the perfect model of a woman which I have always believed you born for.”", "넌 저가 올곧고 사심 없음을 증명해 보였으니, 이제 저가 고맙고 정 많음을 증명해 보이렴. 그러면 넌, 내가 늘 네가 그러라고 태어났다 믿어 온 그 완벽한 여인의 본보기가 될 게다.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“어머!"], ["never, never, never!", "결코, 결코, 결코요!"], ["he never will succeed with me.”", "그이는 결코 저한테 뜻을 못 이룰 거예요.”"], ["And she spoke with a warmth which quite astonished Edmund, and which she blushed at the recollection of herself, when she saw his look, and heard him reply, “Never!", "그리고 그녀가 어찌나 뜨겁게 말했던지 에드먼드는 자못 놀랐고, 그녀는 그의 낯빛을 보고, 그리고 그가 “결코라니! 패니!—그토록 단단히 마음먹고 딱 잘라 말하다니!"], ["Fanny!—so very determined and positive!", "이건 너답지 않구나, 분별 있는 너답지가 않아”"], ["This is not like yourself, your rational self.”", "하고 대꾸하는 것을 듣고, 저 자신을 떠올리며 얼굴을 붉혔다."]], [["“I mean,”", "“제 말은,” 그녀가 서글피 저를 고치며 외쳤다."], ["she cried, sorrowfully correcting herself, “that I think I never shall, as far as the future can be answered for; I think I never shall return his regard.”", "“앞날을 장담할 수 있는 한, 저는 결코 그러지 않으리라 여긴다는 거예요. 저는 결코 그이의 정에 화답하지 않으리라 여겨요.”"]], [["“I must hope better things.", "“나는 더 나은 것을 바랄밖에 없다."], ["I am aware, more aware than Crawford can be, that the man who means to make you love him (you having due notice of his intentions) must have very uphill work, for there are all your early attachments and habits in battle array; and before he can get your heart for his own use he has to unfasten it from all the holds upon things animate and inanimate, which so many years’ growth have confirmed, and which are considerably tightened for the moment by the very idea of separation.", "나는 크로퍼드가 알 수 있는 것보다 더 잘 알아, 너로 하여금 저를 사랑하게 만들려는 사내는 (그 뜻을 네가 미리 알고 있으니) 자못 가파른 일을 해야 한다는 걸. 네 온갖 어릴 적 애착과 버릇이 진을 치고 맞서 있으니 말이야."], ["I know that the apprehension of being forced to quit Mansfield will for a time be arming you against him.", "그리고 그가 네 마음을 제 것으로 삼기 전에, 그는 그것을, 그토록 여러 해의 자라남이 굳혀 온, 그리고 헤어짐이라는 바로 그 생각에 지금 자못 단단히 죄인, 살아 있고 살아 있지 않은 온갖 것에 대한 그 붙듦에서 죄다 풀어내야 하거든. 나는 맨스필드를 떠나야 한다는 두려움이 한동안 너를 그에게 맞서 무장시키리라는 걸 안다."], ["I wish he had not been obliged to tell you what he was trying for.", "나는 그가 저가 뭘 노리는지 너한테 말해야 했던 게 아니었으면 싶어."], ["I wish he had known you as well as I do, Fanny.", "나는 그가 나만큼 너를 잘 알았으면 싶고, 패니."], ["Between us, I think we should have won you.", "우리 둘이 힘을 합쳤다면 우리가 너를 얻었으리라 나는 여긴다."], ["My theoretical and his practical knowledge together could not have failed.", "내 이론적인 앎과 그의 실전 앎이 합쳐졌으면 실패할 리 없었지."], ["He should have worked upon my plans.", "그가 내 계획대로 움직였어야 했어."], ["I must hope, however, that time, proving him (as I firmly believe it will) to deserve you by his steady affection, will give him his reward.", "하지만 나는 바랄밖에 없다, 시간이, 그가 (내가 굳게 믿듯) 그 굳건한 애정으로 너를 받을 만한 사람임을 증명해, 그에게 그 보답을 주기를."], ["I cannot suppose that you have not the wish to love him—the natural wish of gratitude.", "나는 네가 그를 사랑하고픈 바람이—고마움에서 우러나는 그 자연스러운 바람이 없으리라곤 여길 수 없다."], ["You must have some feeling of that sort.", "넌 그런 마음이 얼마간은 있을 게야."], ["You must be sorry for your own indifference.”", "넌 네 무심함이 미안할 게고.”"]], [["“We are so totally unlike,”", "“우리는 너무나 딴판이에요,”"], ["said Fanny, avoiding a direct answer, “we are so very, very different in all our inclinations and ways, that I consider it as quite impossible we should ever be tolerably happy together, even if I could like him.", "패니가 곧바른 대답을 피하며 말했다. “우리는 온갖 성향과 방식에서 너무나, 너무나 달라서, 저는 설령 제가 그이를 좋아할 수 있다 해도 우리가 함께 웬만큼이나마 행복할 수 있으리라곤 아주 어림없다 여겨요."], ["There never were two people more dissimilar.", "이보다 더 딴판인 두 사람은 없었을 거예요."], ["We have not one taste in common.", "우리한테는 통하는 취향이 하나도 없거든요."], ["We should be miserable.”", "우리는 비참해질 거예요.”"]], [["“You are mistaken, Fanny.", "“네가 잘못 짚은 거야, 패니."], ["The dissimilarity is not so strong.", "그 딴판임이 그리 세지 않아."], ["You are quite enough alike.", "너희는 충분히 닮았어."], ["You have tastes in common.", "너희한테는 통하는 취향이 있어."], ["You have moral and literary tastes in common.", "도덕적, 문학적 취향이 통하지."], ["You have both warm hearts and benevolent feelings; and, Fanny, who that heard him read, and saw you listen to Shakespeare the other night, will think you unfitted as companions?", "너희 둘 다 따뜻한 마음과 인정 어린 심경을 지녔고. 그리고, 패니, 요전 밤 그가 읽는 걸 듣고 네가 셰익스피어에 귀 기울이는 걸 본 이가, 너희가 짝으로 안 어울린다 여기겠니?"], ["You forget yourself: there is a decided difference in your tempers, I allow.", "넌 네 자신을 잊는구나. 너희 성미에 뚜렷한 다름이 있다는 건 나도 인정해."], ["He is lively, you are serious; but so much the better: his spirits will support yours.", "그는 발랄하고, 넌 진지하지. 하지만 그럴수록 더 좋아."], ["It is your disposition to be easily dejected and to fancy difficulties greater than they are.", "그의 신명이 네 것을 떠받쳐 줄 테니까. 쉬 풀 죽고 어려움을 실제보다 크게 여기는 게 네 성미야."], ["His cheerfulness will counteract this.", "그의 명랑함이 이걸 눅여 줄 거야."], ["He sees difficulties nowhere: and his pleasantness and gaiety will be a constant support to you.", "그는 어디서도 어려움을 안 보거든. 그리고 그의 유쾌함과 발랄함이 너한테 한결같은 떠받침이 될 게다."], ["Your being so far unlike, Fanny, does not in the smallest degree make against the probability of your happiness together: do not imagine it.", "너희가 그토록 딴판이라는 게, 패니, 함께 행복할 가망을 조금도 해치지 않아. 그렇게 여기지 마라."], ["I am myself convinced that it is rather a favourable circumstance.", "나 자신은 그게 오히려 좋은 사정이라 확신해."], ["I am perfectly persuaded that the tempers had better be unlike: I mean unlike in the flow of the spirits, in the manners, in the inclination for much or little company, in the propensity to talk or to be silent, to be grave or to be gay.", "나는 성미란 서로 다른 편이 낫다고 온전히 믿어. 내 말은, 신명의 흐름에서, 몸가짐에서, 사람 많은 데를 좋아하고 적은 데를 좋아하는 데서, 말하고 싶어 하고 잠잠하고 싶어 하는 데서, 근엄하고 싶어 하고 명랑하고 싶어 하는 데서 서로 다른 편이 낫다는 거야."], ["Some opposition here is, I am thoroughly convinced, friendly to matrimonial happiness.", "여기서 얼마간 반대되는 게, 나는 속속들이 확신하는데, 혼인의 행복에 이롭거든."], ["I exclude extremes, of course; and a very close resemblance in all those points would be the likeliest way to produce an extreme.", "물론 극단은 뺀 얘기고. 그 온갖 점에서 아주 바짝 닮으면 오히려 극단을 낳기 십상이야."], ["A counteraction, gentle and continual, is the best safeguard of manners and conduct.”", "부드럽고 끊임없는 눅임이야말로 몸가짐과 처신의 가장 좋은 방패지.”"]], [["Full well could Fanny guess where his thoughts were now: Miss Crawford’s power was all returning.", "패니는 이제 그의 생각이 어디에 가 있는지 넉넉히 짐작할 수 있었다. 크로퍼드 양의 힘이 죄다 되살아나고 있었다."], ["He had been speaking of her cheerfully from the hour of his coming home.", "그는 집에 돌아온 그 시각부터 그녀를 명랑하게 입에 올려 왔다."], ["His avoiding her was quite at an end.", "그녀를 피하던 것은 아주 끝났다."], ["He had dined at the Parsonage only the preceding day.", "그는 바로 그 전날 목사관에서 만찬을 든 참이었다."]], [["After leaving him to his happier thoughts for some minutes, Fanny, feeling it due to herself, returned to Mr.", "몇 분 그를 그 더 행복한 생각에 맡겨 둔 뒤, 패니는 저 자신을 위해 마땅한 일이다 여겨 크로퍼드 씨 얘기로 돌아가 말했다."], ["Crawford, and said, “It is not merely in temper that I consider him as totally unsuited to myself; though, in that respect, I think the difference between us too great, infinitely too great: his spirits often oppress me; but there is something in him which I object to still more.", "“제가 그이를 저한테 아주 안 어울린다 여기는 건 그저 성미에서만이 아니에요. 하기야 그 점에서도 저는 우리 사이의 다름이 너무 크다, 한없이 크다 여기지만요."], ["I must say, cousin, that I cannot approve his character.", "그이의 신명은 곧잘 저를 짓눌러요."], ["I have not thought well of him from the time of the play.", "하지만 그이한테는 제가 더더욱 못마땅해하는 무언가가 있어요."], ["I then saw him behaving, as it appeared to me, so very improperly and unfeelingly—I may speak of it now because it is all over—so improperly by poor Mr.", "저는 말해야겠어요, 오빠, 저는 그이의 사람됨을 좋게 볼 수가 없어요. 저는 그 연극 때부터 그이를 좋게 여기지 않았어요."], ["Rushworth, not seeming to care how he exposed or hurt him, and paying attentions to my cousin Maria, which—in short, at the time of the play, I received an impression which will never be got over.”", "그때 저는 그이가, 제 눈에는, 그토록 무척 온당찮고 몰인정하게 처신하는 걸 봤거든요—이제는 다 지난 일이니 말할 수 있어요—딱한 러시워스 씨한테 그토록 온당찮게, 그이를 어찌 망신 주고 아프게 하는지는 아랑곳 않는 듯, 그리고 사촌 마리아한테 마음을 쓰는 걸—요컨대, 그 연극 때 저는 결코 지워지지 않을 인상을 받았어요.”"]], [["“My dear Fanny,”", "“패니야,”"], ["replied Edmund, scarcely hearing her to the end, “let us not, any of us, be judged by what we appeared at that period of general folly.", "에드먼드가 그녀 말을 끝까지 듣지도 않다시피 하며 대꾸했다. “우리 누구도, 그 두루 어리석던 시절에 우리가 어떻게 비쳤는가로 재이지 말자꾸나."], ["The time of the play is a time which I hate to recollect.", "그 연극 때야말로 내가 떠올리기 싫은 때야."], ["Maria was wrong, Crawford was wrong, we were all wrong together; but none so wrong as myself.", "마리아도 틀렸고, 크로퍼드도 틀렸고, 우리 다 함께 틀렸어. 하지만 나만큼 틀린 이는 없었지."], ["Compared with me, all the rest were blameless.", "나에 견주면 나머지는 다 흠 없었어."], ["I was playing the fool with my eyes open.”", "나야말로 두 눈 멀쩡히 뜨고 바보짓을 하고 있었으니까.”"]], [["“As a bystander,”", "“곁에서 지켜보는 이로서,”"], ["said Fanny, “perhaps I saw more than you did; and I do think that Mr.", "패니가 말했다. “어쩌면 저는 오빠보다 더 많이 봤는지도 몰라요."], ["Rushworth was sometimes very jealous.”", "그리고 저는 정말 러시워스 씨가 이따금 무척 질투했다 여겨요.”"]], [["“Very possibly. No wonder.", "“그야 얼마든지 그랬겠지."], ["Nothing could be more improper than the whole business.", "놀랄 것도 없어. 그 온 일보다 더 온당찮은 건 없었으니까."], ["I am shocked whenever I think that Maria could be capable of it; but, if she could undertake the part, we must not be surprised at the rest.”", "나는 마리아가 그런 짓을 할 수 있었다는 걸 떠올릴 때마다 소름이 끼쳐. 하지만 그 애가 그 배역을 맡을 수 있었다면, 나머지야 놀랄 게 없지.”"]], [["“Before the play, I am much mistaken if Julia did not think he was paying her attentions.”", "“연극 전엔, 줄리아가 그이가 저한테 마음 쓴다 여긴 게 아니라면 제가 아주 잘못 본 거예요.”"]], [["“Julia!", "“줄리아라니!"], ["I have heard before from some one of his being in love with Julia; but I could never see anything of it.", "나도 전에 누군가한테서 그가 줄리아한테 반했다는 소릴 들은 적이 있어. 하지만 나는 그런 낌새를 통 못 봤거든."], ["And, Fanny, though I hope I do justice to my sisters’ good qualities, I think it very possible that they might, one or both, be more desirous of being admired by Crawford, and might shew that desire rather more unguardedly than was perfectly prudent.", "그리고, 패니, 나야 내 누이들의 좋은 점을 온당히 봐주고 싶다만, 그 애들이, 하나든 둘 다든, 크로퍼드한테 흠모받고 싶어 하고, 그 마음을 아주 신중한 것보다 다소 부주의하게 내비쳤을 수도 있다 여긴다. 나는 그 애들이 분명 그의 벗함을 좋아하던 걸 떠올릴 수 있어."], ["I can remember that they were evidently fond of his society; and with such encouragement, a man like Crawford, lively, and it may be, a little unthinking, might be led on to—there could be nothing very striking, because it is clear that he had no pretensions: his heart was reserved for you.", "그리고 그런 북돋움이 있으면, 크로퍼드 같은 사내, 발랄하고, 어쩌면 얼마간 생각 없는 사내가 이끌려—뭐 그리 눈에 띌 건 없었을 거야, 그가 아무 속셈이 없었던 게 뻔하니까. 그의 마음은 너를 위해 남겨져 있었거든."], ["And I must say, that its being for you has raised him inconceivably in my opinion.", "그리고 나는 말해야겠어, 그게 너를 위한 것이었다는 게 내 눈에 그를 이루 헤아릴 수 없이 높여 놨다고."], ["It does him the highest honour; it shews his proper estimation of the blessing of domestic happiness and pure attachment.", "그건 그에게 더없는 영광을 안겨. 그건 그가 가정의 행복과 순수한 애착의 복을 온당히 가늠함을 보여 주지."], ["It proves him unspoilt by his uncle.", "그건 그가 삼촌한테 물들지 않았음을 증명하고."], ["It proves him, in short, everything that I had been used to wish to believe him, and feared he was not.”", "요컨대 그건 그가, 내가 늘 그러리라 믿고 싶어 하면서도 아닐까 두려워하던 온갖 것임을 증명해.”"]], [["“I am persuaded that he does not think, as he ought, on serious subjects.”", "“저는 그이가 진지한 화제를 두고 마땅히 생각해야 할 만큼 생각하지 않는다 믿어요.”"]], [["“Say, rather, that he has not thought at all upon serious subjects, which I believe to be a good deal the case.", "“차라리 그가 진지한 화제를 두고 아예 생각해 본 적이 없다고 하려무나. 나는 그게 대체로 그렇다고 믿어."], ["How could it be otherwise, with such an education and adviser?", "그런 교육과 조언자로야 어찌 달랐겠니?"], ["Under the disadvantages, indeed, which both have had, is it not wonderful that they should be what they are?", "정말이지 둘 다 겪어 온 그 불리함 아래서, 그들이 지금 그만한 사람인 게 오히려 놀랍지 않으냐?"], ["Crawford’s feelings, I am ready to acknowledge, have hitherto been too much his guides.", "크로퍼드의 심경이 여태 너무 그의 길잡이 노릇을 해 왔다는 건 나도 기꺼이 인정한다."], ["Happily, those feelings have generally been good.", "다행히 그 심경이 대체로 좋은 것이긴 했지."], ["You will supply the rest; and a most fortunate man he is to attach himself to such a creature—to a woman who, firm as a rock in her own principles, has a gentleness of character so well adapted to recommend them.", "나머지야 네가 채워 줄 게고. 그리고 그런 사람한테—제 지조에서 바위처럼 굳으면서도, 그 지조를 천거하기에 그토록 잘 맞는 상냥한 성품을 지닌 여인한테 정 붙이니, 그는 더없이 복 받은 사내야."], ["He has chosen his partner, indeed, with rare felicity.", "그는 정말이지 드문 복으로 제 짝을 골랐어. 그는 너를 행복하게 해 줄 거다, 패니."], ["He will make you happy, Fanny; I know he will make you happy; but you will make him everything.”", "나는 그가 너를 행복하게 해 주리란 걸 알아. 하지만 너는 그를 온갖 것이게 만들어 줄 게야.”"]], [["“I would not engage in such a charge,”", "“저는 그런 임무를 맡진 않겠어요,”"], ["cried Fanny, in a shrinking accent; “in such an office of high responsibility!”", "패니가 움츠러드는 어조로 외쳤다. “그런 막중한 소임을요!”"]], [["“As usual, believing yourself unequal to anything!", "“여느 때처럼, 저는 아무것도 감당 못 한다 여기는구나!"], ["fancying everything too much for you!", "온갖 게 저한텐 너무 벅차다 여기고!"], ["Well, though I may not be able to persuade you into different feelings, you will be persuaded into them, I trust.", "자, 내가 널 다른 심경으로 설득하진 못할지언정, 넌 거기로 설득되고 말 게다, 나는 믿는다."], ["I confess myself sincerely anxious that you may.", "나는 정말이지 네가 그러기를 진심으로 바란다고 털어놓으마."], ["I have no common interest in Crawford’s well-doing.", "나는 크로퍼드가 잘되는 데 여느 관심이 아닌 관심을 갖고 있어."], ["Next to your happiness, Fanny, his has the first claim on me.", "네 행복 다음으로, 패니, 그의 행복이 나한테 으뜸가는 몫을 차지하거든."], ["You are aware of my having no common interest in Crawford.”", "내가 크로퍼드한테 여느 관심 아닌 관심이 있다는 걸 넌 알잖니.”"]], [["Fanny was too well aware of it to have anything to say; and they walked on together some fifty yards in mutual silence and abstraction.", "패니는 그것을 너무 잘 알기에 아무 할 말이 없었다. 그리하여 둘은 서로 잠잠히 상념에 잠겨 오십 야드쯤 함께 걸었다."], ["Edmund first began again—", "에드먼드가 먼저 다시 입을 열었다—"]], [["“I was very much pleased by her manner of speaking of it yesterday, particularly pleased, because I had not depended upon her seeing everything in so just a light.", "“나는 어제 그녀가 그것을 얘기하는 태도에 무척 기뻤어, 유난히 기뻤지, 그녀가 온갖 것을 그토록 온당한 눈으로 보리라곤 기대 못 했으니까."], ["I knew she was very fond of you; but yet I was afraid of her not estimating your worth to her brother quite as it deserved, and of her regretting that he had not rather fixed on some woman of distinction or fortune.", "나는 그녀가 너를 무척 좋아하는 줄이야 알았어. 하지만 그래도 그녀가 그 오라비에 대한 네 값어치를 마땅한 만큼 가늠 못 하고, 오히려 그가 지체 높거나 재산 있는 어떤 여자한테 마음 붙이지 않은 걸 서운해하지나 않을까 두려웠거든."], ["I was afraid of the bias of those worldly maxims, which she has been too much used to hear.", "그녀가 너무 익히 들어 온 그 세속적 격언들에 치우치지나 않을까 두려웠지."], ["But it was very different.", "하지만 그건 아주 딴판이었어."], ["She spoke of you, Fanny, just as she ought.", "그녀는 너를, 패니, 딱 마땅한 대로 얘기했어."], ["She desires the connexion as warmly as your uncle or myself.", "그녀는 이 인연을, 네 삼촌이나 나만큼이나 뜨겁게 바라."], ["We had a long talk about it.", "우리는 그것을 두고 오래 얘기했지."], ["I should not have mentioned the subject, though very anxious to know her sentiments; but I had not been in the room five minutes before she began introducing it with all that openness of heart, and sweet peculiarity of manner, that spirit and ingenuousness which are so much a part of herself.", "나야 그녀의 심경을 무척 알고 싶으면서도 그 화제를 먼저 꺼내진 않았을 텐데, 방에 든 지 오 분도 안 되어 그녀가 그 온갖 툭 트인 마음과, 그토록 그녀다운 그 고운 남다른 태도와, 그 기백과 꾸밈없음으로 그것을 꺼내기 시작하더구나."], ["Mrs. Grant laughed at her for her rapidity.”", "그랜트 부인이 그녀의 그 성급함을 두고 웃었어.”"]], [["“Was Mrs. Grant in the room, then?”", "“그럼 그랜트 부인도 방에 있었어요?”"]], [["“Yes, when I reached the house I found the two sisters together by themselves; and when once we had begun, we had not done with you, Fanny, till Crawford and Dr.", "“그래, 내가 그 집에 이르니 두 자매가 단둘이 함께 있더라."], ["Grant came in.”", "그리고 일단 시작하니, 크로퍼드하고 그랜트 박사가 들어올 때까지 우리는 네 얘기를 끝내지 못했어, 패니.”"]], [["“It is above a week since I saw Miss Crawford.”", "“제가 크로퍼드 양을 못 본 지 일주일이 넘었어요.”"]], [["“Yes, she laments it; yet owns it may have been best.", "“그래, 그녀도 그걸 서운해해. 하지만 그게 나았을지도 모른다고 인정하지."], ["You will see her, however, before she goes.", "아무튼 그녀가 떠나기 전에 넌 그녀를 보게 될 거야."], ["She is very angry with you, Fanny; you must be prepared for that.", "그녀는 너한테 무척 화가 나 있어, 패니. 넌 그 각오를 해야 해."], ["She calls herself very angry, but you can imagine her anger.", "자기가 무척 화났다고 말은 하지만, 그 화가 어떤 건지야 넌 짐작할 수 있잖니."], ["It is the regret and disappointment of a sister, who thinks her brother has a right to everything he may wish for, at the first moment.", "그건, 제 오라비가 바라는 온갖 것을 그 첫 순간에 얻을 권리가 있다 여기는 누이의 서운함과 실망이야."], ["She is hurt, as you would be for William; but she loves and esteems you with all her heart.”", "그녀는 상처받았어, 네가 윌리엄을 위해서라면 그럴 것처럼. 하지만 그녀는 온 마음으로 너를 사랑하고 존중해.”"]], [["“I knew she would be very angry with me.”", "“저는 그녀가 저한테 무척 화가 나 있으리란 걸 알았어요.”"]], [["“My dearest Fanny,”", "“패니야,”"], ["cried Edmund, pressing her arm closer to him, “do not let the idea of her anger distress you.", "에드먼드가 그녀의 팔을 저에게 더 바짝 끌어당기며 외쳤다. “그녀의 화라는 생각에 너무 마음 졸이지 마라."], ["It is anger to be talked of rather than felt.", "그건 느껴진다기보다 말로 하는 화야."], ["Her heart is made for love and kindness, not for resentment.", "그녀의 마음은 사랑과 친절을 위해 지어졌지, 원한을 위해 지어진 게 아니거든."], ["I wish you could have overheard her tribute of praise; I wish you could have seen her countenance, when she said that you should be Henry’s wife.", "나는 네가 그녀의 그 칭찬의 헌사를 엿들었더라면 싶어. 네가 반드시 헨리의 아내가 되어야 한다고 말할 때 그녀의 낯빛을 봤더라면 싶고."], ["And I observed that she always spoke of you as ‘Fanny,’ which she was never used to do; and it had a sound of most sisterly cordiality.”", "그리고 나는 그녀가 늘 너를 ‘패니’라 부르는 걸 눈여겨봤는데, 여태 안 그러던 그녀가 말이야. 그건 더없이 누이다운 정겨움의 울림이 있었어.”"]], [["“And Mrs.", "“그런데 그랜트 부인은, 뭐라고—뭐라 말했어요?"], ["Grant, did she say—did she speak; was she there all the time?”", "내내 거기 있었고요?”"]], [["“Yes, she was agreeing exactly with her sister.", "“그래, 그녀는 제 아우와 딱 같은 생각이었어."], ["The surprise of your refusal, Fanny, seems to have been unbounded.", "네가 마다한 데 대한 놀라움은, 패니, 끝이 없었던 모양이야."], ["That you could refuse such a man as Henry Crawford seems more than they can understand.", "네가 헨리 크로퍼드 같은 사내를 마다할 수 있었다는 게 그들로선 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 일인가 봐. 나는 너를 위해 할 수 있는 말을 했지."], ["I said what I could for you; but in good truth, as they stated the case—you must prove yourself to be in your senses as soon as you can by a different conduct; nothing else will satisfy them.", "하지만 정말이지, 그들이 그 일을 말하는 걸 들으니—너는 되도록 빨리 다른 처신으로 네가 제정신임을 증명해 보여야겠다. 그거 말곤 아무것도 그들을 흡족게 못 할 테니까."], ["But this is teasing you.", "하지만 이건 너를 놀리는 거구나."], ["I have done.", "나는 그만하마."], ["Do not turn away from me.”", "나한테서 돌아서진 마라.”"]], [["“I should have thought,”", "“저는 여겼어야 하지 않을까 싶어요,”"], ["said Fanny, after a pause of recollection and exertion, “that every woman must have felt the possibility of a man’s not being approved, not being loved by some one of her sex at least, let him be ever so generally agreeable.", "패니가 떠올리고 애쓰느라 잠깐 뜸을 들인 뒤 말했다. “한 사내가, 아무리 두루 상냥하다 해도, 적어도 제 여자들 가운데 누군가한테는 인정받지 못하고 사랑받지 못할 수도 있음을 여자라면 누구나 느꼈어야 하지 않을까 하고요."], ["Let him have all the perfections in the world, I think it ought not to be set down as certain that a man must be acceptable to every woman he may happen to like himself.", "그이가 세상 온갖 완벽함을 다 갖췄다 해도, 한 사내가 저 좋은 대로 마음에 든 여자마다에게 반드시 받아들여져야 한다고 못 박아 둬선 안 된다 여겨요."], ["But, even supposing it is so, allowing Mr. Crawford to have all the claims which his sisters think he has, how was I to be prepared to meet him with any feeling answerable to his own?", "하지만 설령 그렇다 쳐도, 크로퍼드 씨한테 그 누이들이 여기는 온갖 자격이 다 있다 쳐도, 제가 무슨 수로 그이의 것에 걸맞은 심경으로 그이를 맞을 채비가 돼 있었겠어요?"], ["He took me wholly by surprise.", "그이는 저를 아주 뜻밖에 덮쳤어요."], ["I had not an idea that his behaviour to me before had any meaning; and surely I was not to be teaching myself to like him only because he was taking what seemed very idle notice of me.", "저는 앞서 저한테 대한 그이의 처신에 무슨 뜻이 있으리라곤 생각도 못 했고요. 그리고 정말이지, 그이가 자못 실없어 보이는 눈길을 저한테 주었다 해서 제가 스스로 그이를 좋아하도록 다잡아야 할 건 아니잖아요."], ["In my situation, it would have been the extreme of vanity to be forming expectations on Mr. Crawford.", "제 처지에, 크로퍼드 씨한테 기대를 품는다는 건 극단의 허영일 테고요."], ["I am sure his sisters, rating him as they do, must have thought it so, supposing he had meant nothing.", "그이가 아무 뜻도 없었다 치면, 그이를 그렇게나 높이 치는 그 누이들도 분명 그리 여겼을 거예요."], ["How, then, was I to be—to be in love with him the moment he said he was with me?", "그런데 어떻게 제가—그이가 저를 사랑한다 말한 그 순간에 그이를 사랑하고 있어야 했겠어요?"], ["How was I to have an attachment at his service, as soon as it was asked for?", "어떻게 청받자마자 대령할 애착을 지니고 있어야 했겠어요?"], ["His sisters should consider me as well as him.", "그이의 누이들은 그이뿐 아니라 저도 헤아려야 해요."], ["The higher his deserts, the more improper for me ever to have thought of him.", "그이가 나을수록, 제가 언제고 그이를 마음에 둔다는 건 더 온당찮은 일이고요."], ["And, and—we think very differently of the nature of women, if they can imagine a woman so very soon capable of returning an affection as this seems to imply.”", "그리고, 그리고—여자가 이토록 이내 정에 화답할 수 있으리라 여긴다면, 이게 넌지시 그런 뜻인 듯한데, 우리는 여자의 본성을 아주 다르게 보는 거예요.”"]], [["“My dear, dear Fanny, now I have the truth.", "“패니야, 사랑하는 패니야, 이제 나는 진실을 얻었다."], ["I know this to be the truth; and most worthy of you are such feelings.", "나는 이게 진실임을 알아. 그리고 그런 심경이야말로 더없이 너다워."], ["I had attributed them to you before.", "나는 앞서 그것을 네 것으로 돌렸었어."], ["I thought I could understand you.", "나는 너를 알 수 있으려니 여겼거든."], ["You have now given exactly the explanation which I ventured to make for you to your friend and Mrs.", "너는 이제, 내가 감히 너를 대신해 네 벗과 그랜트 부인에게 해 준 그 설명을 딱 그대로 해 줬어."], ["Grant, and they were both better satisfied, though your warm-hearted friend was still run away with a little by the enthusiasm of her fondness for Henry.", "그리고 둘 다 더 흡족해했지, 하기야 네 따뜻한 벗은 헨리를 아끼는 그 열정에 여전히 얼마쯤 휩쓸리긴 했지만."], ["I told them that you were of all human creatures the one over whom habit had most power and novelty least; and that the very circumstance of the novelty of Crawford’s addresses was against him.", "나는 그들한테, 온갖 사람 가운데 버릇이 가장 큰 힘을 미치고 새것이 가장 작은 힘을 미치는 이가 바로 너라고, 그러니 크로퍼드의 구애가 새롭다는 바로 그 사정이 그에게 불리하다고 말했어."], ["Their being so new and so recent was all in their disfavour; that you could tolerate nothing that you were not used to; and a great deal more to the same purpose, to give them a knowledge of your character.", "그것이 그토록 새롭고 그토록 근래인 게 죄다 그에게 안 좋다고, 넌 익지 않은 것이라곤 아무것도 못 견딘다고 말이야. 그리고 그들한테 네 성품을 알려 주려고 같은 취지의 말을 잔뜩 더 했지."], ["Miss Crawford made us laugh by her plans of encouragement for her brother.", "크로퍼드 양은 제 오라비를 북돋울 계획으로 우리를 웃겼어."], ["She meant to urge him to persevere in the hope of being loved in time, and of having his addresses most kindly received at the end of about ten years’ happy marriage.”", "그녀는 그더러, 머잖아 사랑받게 되리라는 바람으로, 그리고 한 십 년쯤 행복한 혼인 끝에 그 구애가 더없이 살갑게 받아들여지리라는 바람으로 물러서지 말라 조를 작정이었거든.”"]], [["Fanny could with difficulty give the smile that was here asked for.", "패니는 여기서 청해진 그 미소를 좀처럼 짓기 어려웠다. 그녀의 심경은 온통 곤두서 있었다."], ["Her feelings were all in revolt.", "그녀는 제가 잘못하고 있었나 두려웠다."], ["She feared she had been doing wrong: saying too much, overacting the caution which she had been fancying necessary; in guarding against one evil, laying herself open to another; and to have Miss Crawford’s liveliness repeated to her at such a moment, and on such a subject, was a bitter aggravation.", "너무 많이 말하고, 필요하려니 여긴 그 조심을 지나치게 부린 건 아닌가, 한 화를 막다가 다른 화에 저를 내맡긴 건 아닌가 하고. 그리고 하필 이런 순간에, 이런 화제로 크로퍼드 양의 발랄함을 되풀이해 듣는 것은 쓰라린 보탬이었다."]], [["Edmund saw weariness and distress in her face, and immediately resolved to forbear all farther discussion; and not even to mention the name of Crawford again, except as it might be connected with what must be agreeable to her.", "에드먼드는 그녀의 낯에서 지침과 괴로움을 보고, 대번에 더는 아무 논의도 삼가기로, 그리고 반드시 그녀에게 흐뭇할 것과 얽힌 경우 말고는 크로퍼드의 이름조차 다시 입에 안 올리기로 마음먹었다."], ["On this principle, he soon afterwards observed—“They go on Monday.", "이 원칙에 따라 그는 이내 이렇게 뇌었다—“그들은 월요일에 떠나."], ["You are sure, therefore, of seeing your friend either to-morrow or Sunday.", "그러니 넌 틀림없이 내일이든 일요일이든 네 벗을 보게 될 거야."], ["They really go on Monday; and I was within a trifle of being persuaded to stay at Lessingby till that very day!", "그들은 정말 월요일에 가거든. 그리고 나는 바로 그날까지 레싱비에 머물도록 설득당할 뻔했지 뭐냐!"], ["I had almost promised it.", "거의 약속할 뻔했어."], ["What a difference it might have made!", "그랬으면 얼마나 달랐을까!"], ["Those five or six days more at Lessingby might have been felt all my life.”", "레싱비에서의 그 대엿새가 평생 사무쳤을지도 몰라.”"]], [["“You were near staying there?”", "“거기 머물 뻔했어요?”"]], [["“Very.", "“그럼."], ["I was most kindly pressed, and had nearly consented.", "무척 살갑게 조름받아 거의 승낙할 뻔했지."], ["Had I received any letter from Mansfield, to tell me how you were all going on, I believe I should certainly have staid; but I knew nothing that had happened here for a fortnight, and felt that I had been away long enough.”", "만일 맨스필드에서 너희가 다들 어찌 지내는지 알려 주는 편지라도 받았더라면, 나는 분명 머물렀을 거야. 하지만 나는 여기서 두 주 동안 무슨 일이 있었는지 아무것도 몰랐고, 이만하면 오래 떠나 있었다 느꼈거든.”"]], [["“You spent your time pleasantly there?”", "“거기서 즐겁게 지내셨어요?”"]], [["“Yes; that is, it was the fault of my own mind if I did not.", "“그래. 그러니까, 안 그랬다면 그건 내 마음 탓이지."], ["They were all very pleasant.", "그들은 다들 무척 유쾌했어."], ["I doubt their finding me so.", "그들이 나를 그리 여겼을지는 모르겠다만."], ["I took uneasiness with me, and there was no getting rid of it till I was in Mansfield again.”", "나야 마음의 불편을 지고 갔고, 다시 맨스필드에 오기까지는 그것을 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었으니까.”"]], [["“The Miss Owens—you liked them, did not you?”", "“그 오언 양들—좋으셨죠?”"]], [["“Yes, very well.", "“그래, 아주."], ["Pleasant, good-humoured, unaffected girls.", "유쾌하고 성미 좋고 꾸밈없는 아가씨들이야."], ["But I am spoilt, Fanny, for common female society.", "하지만 나는, 패니, 여느 여자들과의 사귐엔 버려진 몸이야."], ["Good-humoured, unaffected girls will not do for a man who has been used to sensible women.", "성미 좋고 꾸밈없는 아가씨들이야, 지각 있는 여자들에 익은 사내한텐 안 맞거든."], ["They are two distinct orders of being.", "그 둘은 딴 부류의 존재야."], ["You and Miss Crawford have made me too nice.”", "너하고 크로퍼드 양이 나를 너무 까다롭게 만들어 놨어.”"]], [["Still, however, Fanny was oppressed and wearied; he saw it in her looks, it could not be talked away; and attempting it no more, he led her directly, with the kind authority of a privileged guardian, into the house.", "그래도 패니는 여전히 짓눌리고 지쳐 있었다. 그는 그녀의 낯빛에서 그것을 보았고, 그것은 말로 씻어 낼 수 없었다. 그리하여 더는 그리 애쓰지 않고, 그는 그녀를, 그럴 자격을 지닌 보호자의 다정한 위엄으로, 곧장 집 안으로 이끌었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVI", "# 제36장"]], [["Edmund now believed himself perfectly acquainted with all that Fanny could tell, or could leave to be conjectured of her sentiments, and he was satisfied.", "에드먼드는 이제, 패니가 제 심경에 대해 말해 줄 수 있는, 또는 짐작에 맡겨 둘 수 있는 온갖 것을 저가 훤히 안다고 믿었고, 흡족했다."], ["It had been, as he before presumed, too hasty a measure on Crawford’s side, and time must be given to make the idea first familiar, and then agreeable to her.", "그것은, 그가 앞서 넘겨짚은 대로, 크로퍼드 편에서 너무 성급한 조처였고, 그 생각을 그녀에게 우선 익숙하게, 그다음 흐뭇하게 만들려면 시간이 주어져야 했다."], ["She must be used to the consideration of his being in love with her, and then a return of affection might not be very distant.", "그녀는 그가 저를 사랑한다는 것을 헤아리는 데 익어야 했고, 그러면 정으로 화답하기까지가 그리 멀지 않을지도 몰랐다."]], [["He gave this opinion as the result of the conversation to his father; and recommended there being nothing more said to her: no farther attempts to influence or persuade; but that everything should be left to Crawford’s assiduities, and the natural workings of her own mind.", "그는 이 소견을 그 대화의 결말로 아버지에게 전했다. 그리고 그녀에게 더는 아무 말도 안 하기를, 영향을 미치거나 설득하려는 시도를 더는 안 하기를, 다만 온갖 것을 크로퍼드의 부지런함과 그녀 자신의 마음의 자연스러운 움직임에 맡겨 두기를 권했다."]], [["Sir Thomas promised that it should be so.", "토머스 경은 그리하마고 약속했다. 패니의 성미에 대한 에드먼드의 이야기는 옳다 믿을 만했다."], ["Edmund’s account of Fanny’s disposition he could believe to be just; he supposed she had all those feelings, but he must consider it as very unfortunate that she had; for, less willing than his son to trust to the future, he could not help fearing that if such very long allowances of time and habit were necessary for her, she might not have persuaded herself into receiving his addresses properly before the young man’s inclination for paying them were over.", "그는 그녀에게 그 온갖 심경이 있으려니 여겼으나, 그것이 있다는 것을 자못 딱한 일로 여기지 않을 수 없었다. 아들만큼 앞날을 믿지는 못하던 그로서는, 그녀에게 그토록 오랜 시간과 버릇의 여유가 필요하다면, 그 젊은이가 구애하고픈 마음이 다하기 전에 그녀가 스스로 그 구애를 온당히 받아들이도록 설득되지 못할까 두렵지 않을 수 없었던 것이다."], ["There was nothing to be done, however, but to submit quietly and hope the best.", "그러나 조용히 따르고 잘되기를 바라는 것 말고는 달리 할 것이 없었다."]], [["The promised visit from “her friend,” as Edmund called Miss Crawford, was a formidable threat to Fanny, and she lived in continual terror of it.", "에드먼드가 “그녀의 벗”이라 부른 크로퍼드 양의 그 약속된 방문은 패니에게 무시무시한 위협이었고, 그녀는 끊임없는 두려움 속에 살았다."], ["As a sister, so partial and so angry, and so little scrupulous of what she said, and in another light so triumphant and secure, she was in every way an object of painful alarm.", "누이로서 그토록 치우치고 그토록 화나 있으며 제 말에 그토록 거리낌이 없는 데다, 또 다른 면으로는 그토록 의기양양하고 든든한 그녀는, 어느 모로 보나 괴로운 두려움의 대상이었다."], ["Her displeasure, her penetration, and her happiness were all fearful to encounter; and the dependence of having others present when they met was Fanny’s only support in looking forward to it.", "그녀의 언짢음도, 그녀의 꿰뚫어 봄도, 그녀의 행복도 죄다 맞닥뜨리기 두려운 것이었다. 그리하여 만날 때 다른 이들이 곁에 있으리라는 것에 기대는 것만이, 그것을 앞두고 패니가 지닌 유일한 버팀이었다."], ["She absented herself as little as possible from Lady Bertram, kept away from the East room, and took no solitary walk in the shrubbery, in her caution to avoid any sudden attack.", "그녀는 어떤 갑작스러운 공략도 피하려는 조심에서, 되도록 버트럼 부인 곁을 떠나지 않고, 동쪽 방을 멀리하고, 관목 산책로에서 혼자 걷지 않았다."]], [["She succeeded.", "그녀는 뜻을 이뤘다."], ["She was safe in the breakfast-room, with her aunt, when Miss Crawford did come; and the first misery over, and Miss Crawford looking and speaking with much less particularity of expression than she had anticipated, Fanny began to hope there would be nothing worse to be endured than a half-hour of moderate agitation.", "크로퍼드 양이 정말로 왔을 때 그녀는 이모와 함께 아침 식사방에 안전히 있었다. 그리고 첫 괴로움이 지나고, 크로퍼드 양이 그녀가 짐작한 것보다 훨씬 덜 노골적인 표정으로 보고 말하니, 패니는 웬만한 동요의 반 시간 말고는 견뎌 낼 더 나쁜 것이 없으려니 바라기 시작했다."], ["But here she hoped too much; Miss Crawford was not the slave of opportunity.", "그러나 여기서 그녀는 너무 많이 바랐다. 크로퍼드 양은 기회의 노예가 아니었다."], ["She was determined to see Fanny alone, and therefore said to her tolerably soon, in a low voice, “I must speak to you for a few minutes somewhere”; words that Fanny felt all over her, in all her pulses and all her nerves.", "그녀는 패니를 단둘이 보기로 굳게 마음먹은지라, 자못 이내 나직한 목소리로 그녀에게 말했다. “어디서든 몇 분 그쪽하고 얘기해야겠어요.”"], ["Denial was impossible.", "패니가 온몸으로, 온 맥박과 온 신경으로 느낀 말이었다."], ["Her habits of ready submission, on the contrary, made her almost instantly rise and lead the way out of the room.", "마다하기란 어림없었다. 오히려 얼른 따르는 그 버릇 탓에 그녀는 거의 대번에 일어나 앞장서 방을 나갔다."], ["She did it with wretched feelings, but it was inevitable.", "그녀는 비참한 심경으로 그리했으나, 피할 수 없는 일이었다."]], [["They were no sooner in the hall than all restraint of countenance was over on Miss Crawford’s side.", "현관홀에 나서기 무섭게 크로퍼드 양 편에서는 낯빛의 온갖 삼감이 다했다."], ["She immediately shook her head at Fanny with arch, yet affectionate reproach, and taking her hand, seemed hardly able to help beginning directly.", "그녀는 대번에 짓궂으면서도 정겨운 나무람으로 패니에게 고개를 젓고, 손을 잡더니, 좀처럼 곧바로 시작하지 않고는 못 배기는 듯했다."], ["She said nothing, however, but, “Sad, sad girl!", "그러나 그녀는 그저 “못된, 못된 사람 같으니!"], ["I do not know when I shall have done scolding you,”", "언제쯤 그쪽 잔소리를 다 할지 모르겠어요”"], ["and had discretion enough to reserve the rest till they might be secure of having four walls to themselves.", "하고 말할 뿐, 넉 벽이 온전히 저희만의 것이 될 때까지 나머지는 아껴 둘 만한 분별은 있었다."], ["Fanny naturally turned upstairs, and took her guest to the apartment which was now always fit for comfortable use; opening the door, however, with a most aching heart, and feeling that she had a more distressing scene before her than ever that spot had yet witnessed.", "패니는 자연스레 위층으로 향해, 손님을 이제 늘 편안히 쓸 만해진 그 방으로 데려갔다. 그러나 더없이 아픈 마음으로 문을 열며, 저 앞에 그곳이 여태 지켜본 그 어떤 것보다 더 괴로운 장면이 놓여 있음을 느끼면서였다."], ["But the evil ready to burst on her was at least delayed by the sudden change in Miss Crawford’s ideas; by the strong effect on her mind which the finding herself in the East room again produced.", "그러나 그녀에게 막 터지려던 그 화는, 크로퍼드 양의 생각이 문득 바뀐 덕에, 다시 동쪽 방에 있는 자신을 발견한 것이 그녀 마음에 미친 세찬 영향 덕에 적어도 미뤄졌다."]], [["“Ha!”", "“어머!”"], ["she cried, with instant animation, “am I here again?", "그녀가 대번에 생기를 띠며 외쳤다. “내가 여기 다시 왔네요?"], ["The East room!", "동쪽 방!"], ["Once only was I in this room before”; and after stopping to look about her, and seemingly to retrace all that had then passed, she added, “Once only before.", "전에 딱 한 번 이 방에 와 봤어요.” 그리고 둘레를 둘러보려, 그리고 그때 오간 온갖 것을 되짚어 보려는 듯 멈춰 섰다가, 그녀가 덧붙였다."], ["Do you remember it?", "“전에 딱 한 번. 기억나요?"], ["I came to rehearse.", "나는 연습하러 왔었죠."], ["Your cousin came too; and we had a rehearsal.", "그쪽 사촌도 왔고, 우리는 연습을 했어요."], ["You were our audience and prompter.", "그쪽은 우리 청중이자 대사 알려 주는 이였고요."], ["A delightful rehearsal.", "즐거운 연습이었어요."], ["I shall never forget it.", "나는 결코 못 잊을 거예요."], ["Here we were, just in this part of the room: here was your cousin, here was I, here were the chairs.", "우리가 여기 있었죠, 바로 이 자리에. 여기 그쪽 사촌이, 여기 내가, 여기 의자들이 있었어요."], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["why will such things ever pass away?”", "어째서 이런 것들이 다 지나가 버려야 하죠?”"]], [["Happily for her companion, she wanted no answer.", "그녀의 벗에게는 다행히도, 그녀는 아무 대답도 바라지 않았다."], ["Her mind was entirely self-engrossed.", "그녀의 마음은 온통 저에게만 쏠려 있었다."], ["She was in a reverie of sweet remembrances.", "그녀는 달콤한 추억의 몽롱함에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["“The scene we were rehearsing was so very remarkable!", "“우리가 연습하던 그 장면은 정말이지 어찌나 남다르던지!"], ["The subject of it so very—very—what shall I say?", "그 화제가 정말이지 그토록—그토록—뭐라 할까요?"], ["He was to be describing and recommending matrimony to me.", "그이는 나한테 혼인을 그려 보이고 권하기로 되어 있었죠."], ["I think I see him now, trying to be as demure and composed as Anhalt ought, through the two long speeches.", "나는 지금도 그이가 보이는 것 같아요, 그 두 긴 대사 내내 안할트답게 그토록 얌전하고 차분하려 애쓰던 모습이요."], ["‘When two sympathetic hearts meet in the marriage state, matrimony may be called a happy life.’ I suppose no time can ever wear out the impression I have of his looks and voice as he said those words.", "‘두 마음 맞는 이가 혼인의 상태에서 만나면, 혼인은 행복한 삶이라 불릴 만하지요.’ 그이가 그 말을 할 때의 그 낯빛과 목소리에 대한 내 인상은 어떤 시간으로도 결코 못 지울 거예요."], ["It was curious, very curious, that we should have such a scene to play!", "우리가 그런 장면을 연기하게 됐다는 게 참 묘했어요, 아주 묘했죠!"], ["If I had the power of recalling any one week of my existence, it should be that week—that acting week.", "만일 내가 내 삶의 어느 한 주라도 되불러 올 힘이 있다면, 그건 그 한 주여야 해요—그 연기하던 한 주."], ["Say what you would, Fanny, it should be that; for I never knew such exquisite happiness in any other.", "뭐라 하시든, 패니, 그건 그 주여야 해요. 나는 다른 어디서도 그토록 지극한 행복을 알아 본 적이 없거든요."], ["His sturdy spirit to bend as it did!", "그이의 그 뻣뻣한 기개가 그렇게나 꺾이다니!"], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["it was sweet beyond expression.", "그건 이루 말할 수 없이 달콤했어요."], ["But alas, that very evening destroyed it all.", "하지만 아아, 바로 그날 저녁이 그 모두를 무너뜨렸죠."], ["That very evening brought your most unwelcome uncle.", "바로 그날 저녁이 그쪽의 그 더없이 달갑잖은 삼촌을 데려왔으니까요."], ["Poor Sir Thomas, who was glad to see you?", "딱한 토머스 경, 그가 온 걸 반긴 이가 누구였겠어요?"], ["Yet, Fanny, do not imagine I would now speak disrespectfully of Sir Thomas, though I certainly did hate him for many a week.", "하지만, 패니, 내가 지금 토머스 경을 함부로 말하려니 여기진 마요, 하기야 나야 분명 몇 주씩이나 그를 미워했지만요."], ["No, I do him justice now.", "아니에요, 나는 이제 그를 온당히 봐요."], ["He is just what the head of such a family should be.", "그는 그런 집안의 가장이 마땅히 그래야 할 딱 그대로예요."], ["Nay, in sober sadness, I believe I now love you all.”", "아니, 차분한 서글픔으로 말하건대, 나는 이제 그쪽 식구 모두를 사랑하지 싶어요.”"], ["And having said so, with a degree of tenderness and consciousness which Fanny had never seen in her before, and now thought only too becoming, she turned away for a moment to recover herself.", "그리고 그리 말하고서, 패니가 여태 그녀에게서 본 적 없는, 그리고 이제 너무나 어울린다 여긴 그런 애틋함과 켕김을 담아, 그녀는 저를 추스르려 한순간 돌아섰다. “이 방에 들어온 뒤로 내가 잠깐 발작을 겪었죠, 보시다시피.”"], ["“I have had a little fit since I came into this room, as you may perceive,”", "그녀가 이내 장난스러운 미소로 말했다. “하지만 이제 지났어요."], ["said she presently, with a playful smile, “but it is over now; so let us sit down and be comfortable; for as to scolding you, Fanny, which I came fully intending to do, I have not the heart for it when it comes to the point.”", "그러니 우리 앉아서 편안히 있어요. 잔소리로 말하면, 패니, 나는 그걸 하려고 잔뜩 벼르고 왔지만, 막상 닥치니 도무지 그럴 마음이 안 나거든요.”"], ["And embracing her very affectionately, “Good, gentle Fanny!", "그리고 그녀를 자못 정겹게 끌어안으며, “착하고 상냥한 패니!"], ["when I think of this being the last time of seeing you for I do not know how long, I feel it quite impossible to do anything but love you.”", "이게 얼마나 오래일지 모를 그쪽을 보는 마지막이라는 걸 생각하면, 나는 그쪽을 사랑하는 것 말고는 아무것도 도무지 못 하겠어요.”"]], [["Fanny was affected.", "패니는 마음이 움직였다."], ["She had not foreseen anything of this, and her feelings could seldom withstand the melancholy influence of the word “last.”", "그녀는 이런 것을 조금도 내다보지 못했고, 그녀의 심경은 “마지막”이라는 낱말의 그 서글픈 힘을 좀처럼 못 버텼다. 그녀는 마치 도무지 그럴 수 없을 만큼 크로퍼드 양을 사랑하기라도 한 양 울었다."], ["She cried as if she had loved Miss Crawford more than she possibly could; and Miss Crawford, yet farther softened by the sight of such emotion, hung about her with fondness, and said, “I hate to leave you.", "그리고 그런 심경을 보고 한결 더 누그러진 크로퍼드 양은, 그녀에게 다정히 매달리며 말했다. “나는 그쪽을 두고 가는 게 싫어요."], ["I shall see no one half so amiable where I am going.", "내가 가는 데선 이 반만큼 상냥한 이도 못 볼 테니까요."], ["Who says we shall not be sisters?", "우리가 자매가 안 되리라고 누가 그래요?"], ["I know we shall.", "나는 우리가 그리되리란 걸 알아요."], ["I feel that we are born to be connected; and those tears convince me that you feel it too, dear Fanny.”", "나는 우리가 이어질 운명으로 태어났음을 느껴요. 그리고 그 눈물이, 그쪽도 그걸 느낀다는 걸 나한테 확신시켜 줘요, 사랑하는 패니.”"]], [["Fanny roused herself, and replying only in part, said, “But you are only going from one set of friends to another.", "패니는 정신을 차리고, 그저 절반만 대꾸하며 말했다. “하지만 크로퍼드 양은 그저 한 무리의 벗에게서 다른 무리의 벗에게로 가는 거잖아요."], ["You are going to a very particular friend.”", "아주 각별한 벗한테 가시는 거고요.”"]], [["“Yes, very true. Mrs.", "“그래요, 참 맞는 말이에요."], ["Fraser has been my intimate friend for years.", "프레이저 부인은 여러 해 내 절친한 벗이었죠."], ["But I have not the least inclination to go near her.", "하지만 나는 그이 곁에 갈 마음이 조금도 없어요."], ["I can think only of the friends I am leaving: my excellent sister, yourself, and the Bertrams in general.", "나는 오직 내가 두고 가는 벗들만 생각할 수 있거든요. 훌륭한 우리 언니, 그쪽, 그리고 버트럼가 사람들 다요."], ["You have all so much more heart among you than one finds in the world at large.", "그쪽들한테는 온 세상에서 찾을 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 마음이 있어요."], ["You all give me a feeling of being able to trust and confide in you, which in common intercourse one knows nothing of. I wish I had settled with Mrs.", "그쪽들 모두가 나한테, 그쪽들을 믿고 마음을 터놓을 수 있다는 느낌을 줘요, 여느 사귐에선 도무지 알 수 없는 그런 느낌 말이에요."], ["Fraser not to go to her till after Easter, a much better time for the visit, but now I cannot put her off.", "나는 프레이저 부인하고 부활절 뒤에나 가기로 약속해 뒀더라면 싶어요, 방문엔 훨씬 나은 때니까요. 하지만 이제 와서 미룰 순 없죠."], ["And when I have done with her I must go to her sister, Lady Stornaway, because she was rather my most particular friend of the two, but I have not cared much for her these three years.”", "그리고 그이하고 볼일이 끝나면 나는 그 언니 스토너웨이 부인한테 가야 해요, 그이가 둘 중에선 오히려 내 가장 각별한 벗이었으니까요. 하지만 이 삼 년간 나는 그이한테도 별 마음이 없었어요.”"]], [["After this speech the two girls sat many minutes silent, each thoughtful: Fanny meditating on the different sorts of friendship in the world, Mary on something of less philosophic tendency.", "이 말 뒤에 두 아가씨는 저마다 생각에 잠긴 채 여러 분 잠잠히 앉아 있었다. 패니는 세상의 갖가지 우정을 곰곰 헤아렸고, 메리는 좀 덜 철학적인 것을 헤아렸다."], ["She first spoke again.", "그녀가 먼저 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["“How perfectly I remember my resolving to look for you upstairs, and setting off to find my way to the East room, without having an idea whereabouts it was!", "“내가 그쪽을 위층에서 찾아보기로 마음먹고, 어디쯤인지도 모른 채 동쪽 방으로 가는 길을 찾아 나선 걸 어찌나 또렷이 기억하는지!"], ["How well I remember what I was thinking of as I came along, and my looking in and seeing you here sitting at this table at work; and then your cousin’s astonishment, when he opened the door, at seeing me here!", "걸어오면서 내가 무슨 생각을 했는지, 안을 들여다보고 그쪽이 이 탁자에 앉아 바느질하는 걸 본 걸, 그다음 그쪽 사촌이 문을 열고 여기 있는 나를 보고 얼마나 놀랐는지를 얼마나 또렷이 기억하는지!"], ["To be sure, your uncle’s returning that very evening!", "정말이지, 그쪽 삼촌이 바로 그날 저녁에 돌아오다니!"], ["There never was anything quite like it.”", "이런 일은 여태 도무지 없었어요.”"]], [["Another short fit of abstraction followed, when, shaking it off, she thus attacked her companion.", "또 한 번 잠깐의 상념이 뒤따랐고, 그것을 떨쳐 낸 그녀는 이렇게 제 벗을 공략했다."]], [["“Why, Fanny, you are absolutely in a reverie.", "“아니, 패니, 그쪽 아주 몽롱해 있네요."], ["Thinking, I hope, of one who is always thinking of you.", "나야 그쪽 생각을 늘 하는 이를 생각하고 있으려니 바라지만."], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["that I could transport you for a short time into our circle in town, that you might understand how your power over Henry is thought of there!", "잠시라도 그쪽을 시내의 우리 무리로 데려갈 수 있다면, 헨리에 대한 그쪽 힘이 거기서 어떻게 여겨지는지 그쪽이 알 수 있을 텐데!"], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["the envyings and heartburnings of dozens and dozens; the wonder, the incredulity that will be felt at hearing what you have done!", "수십 명이 넘는 이들의 그 시샘과 배 아파함, 그쪽이 한 일을 듣고 느낄 그 놀라움과 못 믿음이라니!"], ["For as to secrecy, Henry is quite the hero of an old romance, and glories in his chains.", "은밀함으로 말하면, 헨리는 아주 옛 이야기 속 사내 주인공이라, 제 사슬을 자랑스러워하거든요."], ["You should come to London to know how to estimate your conquest.", "그쪽은 런던에 와서 제 정복이 어떤 값어치인지 가늠해 봐야 해요."], ["If you were to see how he is courted, and how I am courted for his sake!", "그가 어떻게 떠받들리는지, 그리고 그이 덕에 내가 어떻게 떠받들리는지 그쪽이 본다면!"], ["Now, I am well aware that I shall not be half so welcome to Mrs. Fraser in consequence of his situation with you.", "이제 나는, 그이가 그쪽과 그런 처지가 된 탓에 내가 프레이저 부인한테 그 반만큼도 반갑지 않으리란 걸 잘 알아요."], ["When she comes to know the truth she will, very likely, wish me in Northamptonshire again; for there is a daughter of Mr.", "그이가 진실을 알게 되면, 아마 나를 다시 노샘프턴셔로 보내고 싶어 할걸요."], ["Fraser, by a first wife, whom she is wild to get married, and wants Henry to take.", "프레이저 씨한테 전처소생 딸이 하나 있는데, 그이가 그 애를 시집보내려고 안달이 나서 헨리더러 데려가라 하거든요."], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["she has been trying for him to such a degree.", "그이가 그이를 얻으려고 어찌나 애를 썼는지."], ["Innocent and quiet as you sit here, you cannot have an idea of the sensation that you will be occasioning, of the curiosity there will be to see you, of the endless questions I shall have to answer!", "여기 이토록 순진하고 조용히 앉아 있는 그쪽은, 그쪽이 일으킬 그 파장이, 그쪽을 보려는 그 궁금함이, 내가 답해야 할 그 끝없는 물음들이 어떨지 짐작도 못 할 거예요!"], ["Poor Margaret Fraser will be at me for ever about your eyes and your teeth, and how you do your hair, and who makes your shoes.", "딱한 마거릿 프레이저는 그쪽 눈이며 이며, 머리 어떻게 하는지, 신발은 누가 만드는지를 두고 영영 나를 들볶겠죠."], ["I wish Margaret were married, for my poor friend’s sake, for I look upon the Frasers to be about as unhappy as most other married people.", "나는, 딱한 내 벗을 위해 마거릿이 시집갔으면 싶어요. 프레이저네야말로 여느 부부만큼이나 불행하지 싶으니까요."], ["And yet it was a most desirable match for Janet at the time.", "하지만 그때야 재닛한테는 더없이 바람직한 짝이었죠."], ["We were all delighted.", "우리 다 기뻐했고요."], ["She could not do otherwise than accept him, for he was rich, and she had nothing; but he turns out ill-tempered and exigeant, and wants a young woman, a beautiful young woman of five-and-twenty, to be as steady as himself.", "그이는 그를 받아들일밖에 없었어요, 그가 부유하고 그이는 가진 게 없었으니까요. 그런데 그가 알고 보니 성미가 고약하고 까다로워서, 스물다섯의 젊은 여자, 아름다운 젊은 여자더러 저만큼이나 진득하라고 해요."], ["And my friend does not manage him well; she does not seem to know how to make the best of it.", "그리고 내 벗은 그를 잘 다루질 못해요. 그걸 어떻게 잘 넘겨야 할지 도무지 모르는 것 같아요."], ["There is a spirit of irritation which, to say nothing worse, is certainly very ill-bred.", "좋게 말해도 분명 무척 무례한, 짜증의 기운이 있죠. 그 집에서 나는 맨스필드 목사관의 부부 사이를 존경심으로 떠올리게 될 거예요."], ["In their house I shall call to mind the conjugal manners of Mansfield Parsonage with respect. Even Dr.", "그랜트 박사조차도 우리 언니를 온전히 믿고, 언니 판단을 얼마간 헤아려 줘서, 정말 애착이 있구나 느끼게 하거든요."], ["Grant does shew a thorough confidence in my sister, and a certain consideration for her judgment, which makes one feel there is attachment; but of that I shall see nothing with the Frasers.", "하지만 프레이저네선 그런 걸 조금도 못 보겠죠. 나는 영영 맨스필드에 마음이 가 있을 거예요, 패니."], ["I shall be at Mansfield for ever, Fanny.", "아내로선 우리 언니가, 남편으론 토머스 버트럼 경이 내 완벽함의 잣대예요."], ["My own sister as a wife, Sir Thomas Bertram as a husband, are my standards of perfection.", "딱한 재닛은 서글피 속아 넘어갔죠, 그러면서도 제 편에선 온당찮은 게 없었어요. 그이는 경솔하게 그 혼인에 뛰어든 게 아니거든요."], ["Poor Janet has been sadly taken in, and yet there was nothing improper on her side: she did not run into the match inconsiderately; there was no want of foresight.", "앞을 못 내다본 것도 아니고요. 그이는 그의 청혼을 사흘 두고 생각했고, 그 사흘 동안 제 지인들 가운데 의견 들을 만한 이는 죄다 붙잡고 조언을 구했어요."], ["She took three days to consider of his proposals, and during those three days asked the advice of everybody connected with her whose opinion was worth having, and especially applied to my late dear aunt, whose knowledge of the world made her judgment very generally and deservedly looked up to by all the young people of her acquaintance, and she was decidedly in favour of Mr.", "특히 돌아가신 우리 소중한 숙모한테 여쭸는데, 세상 물정에 밝아 그 판단이 지인인 젊은이들 사이에 두루, 또 마땅히 우러러졌던 분이죠. 그런데 그분이 딱 프레이저 씨 편이었어요."], ["Fraser. This seems as if nothing were a security for matrimonial comfort.", "이건 마치 무엇으로도 부부의 편안함을 장담할 수 없다는 것 같잖아요."], ["I have not so much to say for my friend Flora, who jilted a very nice young man in the Blues for the sake of that horrid Lord Stornaway, who has about as much sense, Fanny, as Mr.", "내 벗 플로라로 말하면 나는 그리 할 말이 없어요. 그 애는 근위 기병대의 아주 좋은 젊은이를 그 끔찍한 스토너웨이 경 때문에 차 버렸거든요."], ["Rushworth, but much worse-looking, and with a blackguard character.", "그자는, 패니, 러시워스 씨만큼이나 지각이 있는데 훨씬 더 못생겼고, 게다가 불한당 같은 사람이에요."], ["I had my doubts at the time about her being right, for he has not even the air of a gentleman, and now I am sure she was wrong.", "나야 그때 그 애가 옳은지 미심쩍었어요, 그자는 신사다운 풍채조차 없으니까요. 그리고 이제 나는 그 애가 틀렸다고 확신해요."], ["By the bye, Flora Ross was dying for Henry the first winter she came out.", "그건 그렇고, 플로라 로스는 사교계에 나온 첫 겨울에 헨리한테 죽고 못 살았죠."], ["But were I to attempt to tell you of all the women whom I have known to be in love with him, I should never have done.", "하지만 내가 알기로 그이한테 반한 여자들을 죄다 그쪽한테 말하려 든다면, 나는 영영 끝이 없을 거예요."], ["It is you, only you, insensible Fanny, who can think of him with anything like indifference.", "그이를 무언가 무심함 비슷한 걸로 생각할 수 있는 건 그쪽뿐이에요, 오직 그쪽, 무정한 패니뿐."], ["But are you so insensible as you profess yourself?", "하지만 그쪽, 정말 스스로 말하는 만큼 무정해요?"], ["No, no, I see you are not.”", "아뇨, 아뇨, 아닌 게 보이는걸요.”"]], [["There was, indeed, so deep a blush over Fanny’s face at that moment as might warrant strong suspicion in a predisposed mind.", "과연 그 순간 패니의 얼굴엔 자못 짙은 홍조가 어려, 미리 그리 여기는 마음에라면 강한 의심을 품을 만도 했다."]], [["“Excellent creature!", "“훌륭한 사람 같으니!"], ["I will not tease you.", "그쪽을 놀리진 않을게요."], ["Everything shall take its course.", "온갖 게 제 갈 길로 가겠죠."], ["But, dear Fanny, you must allow that you were not so absolutely unprepared to have the question asked as your cousin fancies.", "하지만, 사랑하는 패니, 그쪽 사촌이 여기는 만큼 그쪽이 그 물음을 받을 채비가 아주 안 돼 있던 건 아니었다는 건 인정해야 해요."], ["It is not possible but that you must have had some thoughts on the subject, some surmises as to what might be.", "그쪽이 이 일을 두고 얼마간 생각이, 어찌 될까 하는 얼마간의 짐작이 없었을 리 없어요."], ["You must have seen that he was trying to please you by every attention in his power.", "그이가 제 힘닿는 온갖 마음씀으로 그쪽 마음에 들려 애쓰는 걸 그쪽도 봤을 게 분명하고요."], ["Was not he devoted to you at the ball?", "무도회에서 그이가 그쪽한테 홀딱 빠져 있지 않았어요?"], ["And then before the ball, the necklace!", "그리고 무도회 전엔, 그 목걸이!"], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["you received it just as it was meant.", "그쪽은 그걸 딱 그 뜻대로 받았어요."], ["You were as conscious as heart could desire.", "그쪽은 바랄 나위 없이 다 알고 있었죠."], ["I remember it perfectly.”", "나는 그걸 아주 또렷이 기억해요.”"]], [["“Do you mean, then, that your brother knew of the necklace beforehand?", "“그럼 크로퍼드 양은, 오라버니가 그 목걸이를 미리 알고 있었다는 말씀이에요?"], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["Miss Crawford, that was not fair.”", "크로퍼드 양, 그건 온당치 않았어요.”"]], [["“Knew of it!", "“알고 있었냐고요!"], ["It was his own doing entirely, his own thought.", "그건 온전히 그이가 한 일, 그이 생각이었어요."], ["I am ashamed to say that it had never entered my head, but I was delighted to act on his proposal for both your sakes.”", "부끄럽지만 나야 그런 생각이 통 안 들었는데, 나는 그쪽들 둘 다를 위해 그이 제안대로 해서 기뻤죠.”"]], [["“I will not say,”", "“저는 말 안 하겠어요,” 패니가 대꾸했다."], ["replied Fanny, “that I was not half afraid at the time of its being so, for there was something in your look that frightened me, but not at first; I was as unsuspicious of it at first—indeed, indeed I was.", "“그때 저도 그런 게 아닐까 절반쯤 겁나지 않았다고는요. 크로퍼드 양의 낯빛에 저를 겁먹게 하는 무언가가 있었으니까요, 하지만 처음엔 아니었어요."], ["It is as true as that I sit here.", "처음엔 저는 그걸 조금도 의심 안 했어요—정말, 정말 그랬어요."], ["And had I had an idea of it, nothing should have induced me to accept the necklace.", "제가 여기 앉아 있는 것만큼이나 참이에요. 그리고 제가 그걸 짐작이라도 했다면, 무엇으로도 저는 그 목걸이를 받지 않았을 거예요."], ["As to your brother’s behaviour, certainly I was sensible of a particularity: I had been sensible of it some little time, perhaps two or three weeks; but then I considered it as meaning nothing: I put it down as simply being his way, and was as far from supposing as from wishing him to have any serious thoughts of me.", "오라버니의 처신으로 말하면, 분명 저는 어떤 각별함을 느끼긴 했어요. 얼마 전부터, 어쩌면 두세 주 전부터 그걸 느꼈죠."], ["I had not, Miss Crawford, been an inattentive observer of what was passing between him and some part of this family in the summer and autumn.", "하지만 그때 저는 그게 아무 뜻도 아니려니 여겼어요. 그저 그이의 방식이려니 여겼고, 그이가 저를 진지하게 생각하리라곤 바라기는커녕 짐작도 안 했어요."], ["I was quiet, but I was not blind. I could not but see that Mr.", "크로퍼드 양, 저는 여름과 가을에 그이와 이 집안 몇몇 사이에 오간 걸 무심히 보고만 있던 사람이 아니거든요."], ["Crawford amused himself in gallantries which did mean nothing.”", "저는 조용했지만 눈멀진 않았어요. 크로퍼드 씨가 아무 뜻도 없는 정겨움으로 재미 삼는 걸 저는 볼밖에 없었죠.”"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["I cannot deny it.", "나도 그건 부인 못 해요."], ["He has now and then been a sad flirt, and cared very little for the havoc he might be making in young ladies’ affections.", "그이는 이따금 딱한 바람둥이였고, 아가씨들 마음에 저지를지 모를 그 난장은 별로 아랑곳 안 했죠. 나도 곧잘 그걸 두고 그이를 나무랐어요."], ["I have often scolded him for it, but it is his only fault; and there is this to be said, that very few young ladies have any affections worth caring for.", "하지만 그게 그이의 유일한 흠이에요. 그리고 이런 말은 할 수 있죠, 마음 쓸 값어치 있는 정을 지닌 아가씨는 아주 드물다고요."], ["And then, Fanny, the glory of fixing one who has been shot at by so many; of having it in one’s power to pay off the debts of one’s sex!", "그리고, 패니, 그토록 많은 이의 표적이던 이를 붙잡는 그 영광이라니. 제 성(性)의 빚을 갚을 힘을 지닌다는 그 영광!"], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["I am sure it is not in woman’s nature to refuse such a triumph.”", "나는 그런 승리를 마다하는 건 여자의 본성에 없는 일이라 확신해요.”"]], [["Fanny shook her head.", "패니는 고개를 저었다."], ["“I cannot think well of a man who sports with any woman’s feelings; and there may often be a great deal more suffered than a stander-by can judge of.”", "“저는 어떤 여자의 마음이든 가지고 노는 사내를 좋게 볼 수가 없어요. 그리고 곁에서 지켜보는 이가 가늠할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 많이 겪어 내는 경우가 곧잘 있고요.”"]], [["“I do not defend him.", "“나는 그이를 편들지 않아요."], ["I leave him entirely to your mercy, and when he has got you at Everingham, I do not care how much you lecture him.", "그이를 온전히 그쪽 자비에 맡길게요. 그이가 그쪽을 에버링엄에 데려가면, 그쪽이 아무리 그이를 나무라도 나는 상관 안 해요."], ["But this I will say, that his fault, the liking to make girls a little in love with him, is not half so dangerous to a wife’s happiness as a tendency to fall in love himself, which he has never been addicted to.", "하지만 이건 말하겠어요, 그이의 흠, 곧 아가씨들이 저한테 얼마간 반하게 만들기를 좋아하는 그 흠은, 저 스스로 사랑에 빠지는 성향—그이가 여태 한 번도 빠진 적 없는 그 성향의 절반만큼도 아내의 행복에 위험하지 않다고요."], ["And I do seriously and truly believe that he is attached to you in a way that he never was to any woman before; that he loves you with all his heart, and will love you as nearly for ever as possible.", "그리고 나는 정말이지 진심으로 믿어요, 그이가 여태 어떤 여자한테도 그런 적 없는 방식으로 그쪽한테 애착하고 있음을, 그이가 온 마음으로 그쪽을 사랑하고, 되도록 영원에 가깝도록 그쪽을 사랑하리라는 걸."], ["If any man ever loved a woman for ever, I think Henry will do as much for you.”", "어떤 사내든 여자를 영원히 사랑한 적이 있다면, 나는 헨리가 그쪽한테 그만큼 할 거라 여겨요.”"]], [["Fanny could not avoid a faint smile, but had nothing to say.", "패니는 어렴풋한 미소를 피할 수 없었으나, 아무 할 말이 없었다."]], [["“I cannot imagine Henry ever to have been happier,”", "“나는 헨리가 그쪽 오라비의 임관을 얻어 내는 데 성공했을 때보다 더 행복한 적이 있었으리라곤 상상할 수 없어요.”"], ["continued Mary presently, “than when he had succeeded in getting your brother’s commission.”", "이내 메리가 말을 이었다."]], [["She had made a sure push at Fanny’s feelings here.", "그녀는 여기서 패니의 심경을 확실히 찔렀다."]], [["“Oh!", "“어머!"], ["yes.", "네."], ["How very, very kind of him.”", "어쩌면 그토록, 그토록 친절하세요.”"]], [["“I know he must have exerted himself very much, for I know the parties he had to move.", "“나는 그이가 무척 애썼을 게 분명하다는 걸 알아요, 그이가 움직여야 했던 이들을 아니까요."], ["The Admiral hates trouble, and scorns asking favours; and there are so many young men’s claims to be attended to in the same way, that a friendship and energy, not very determined, is easily put by.", "제독은 성가신 걸 싫어하고 청탁 넣는 걸 업신여기죠. 게다가 같은 식으로 봐줘야 할 젊은이들의 청이 하도 많으니, 그리 단단하지 못한 우정과 열의야 쉬 밀려나거든요."], ["What a happy creature William must be!", "윌리엄은 얼마나 행복한 사람일까!"], ["I wish we could see him.”", "우리가 그를 볼 수 있으면 싶네요.”"]], [["Poor Fanny’s mind was thrown into the most distressing of all its varieties.", "딱한 패니의 마음은 그 온갖 갈래 가운데 가장 괴로운 것에 던져졌다."], ["The recollection of what had been done for William was always the most powerful disturber of every decision against Mr.", "윌리엄을 위해 이뤄진 일을 떠올리는 것은 크로퍼드 씨에 대한 온갖 굳은 마음을 늘 가장 세차게 흔드는 것이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 그것을 골똘히 생각하며 앉아 있었는데, 처음엔 흐뭇이 그녀를 지켜보다가 그다음 딴것을 곰곰 헤아리던 메리가 문득 이렇게 말하며 그녀의 정신을 불러 세웠다."], ["Crawford; and she sat thinking deeply of it till Mary, who had been first watching her complacently, and then musing on something else, suddenly called her attention by saying: “I should like to sit talking with you here all day, but we must not forget the ladies below, and so good-bye, my dear, my amiable, my excellent Fanny, for though we shall nominally part in the breakfast-parlour, I must take leave of you here.", "“나는 여기서 온종일 그쪽하고 앉아 얘기하고 싶지만, 아래층 부인들을 잊어선 안 되겠죠. 그러니 잘 있어요, 사랑하는, 상냥한, 훌륭한 나의 패니."], ["And I do take leave, longing for a happy reunion, and trusting that when we meet again, it will be under circumstances which may open our hearts to each other without any remnant or shadow of reserve.”", "우리가 이름으로야 아침 식사방에서 헤어지겠지만, 나는 여기서 그쪽과 작별해야겠어요. 그리고 나는 정말 작별해요, 행복한 재회를 못내 바라며, 우리가 다시 만날 때는 삼감의 자취도 그림자도 없이 서로에게 마음을 열 수 있는 그런 사정에서이기를 믿으며.”"]], [["A very, very kind embrace, and some agitation of manner, accompanied these words.", "자못, 자못 다정한 포옹과 얼마간의 마음 졸이는 태도가 이 말에 뒤따랐다."]], [["“I shall see your cousin in town soon: he talks of being there tolerably soon; and Sir Thomas, I dare say, in the course of the spring; and your eldest cousin, and the Rushworths, and Julia, I am sure of meeting again and again, and all but you.", "“나는 머잖아 시내에서 그쪽 사촌을 볼 거예요. 그이가 제법 이내 거기 가겠다니까요."], ["I have two favours to ask, Fanny: one is your correspondence.", "그리고 토머스 경도, 아무래도 봄 동안에는요. 그리고 그쪽 맏사촌이랑 러시워스 내외랑 줄리아는 몇 번이고 다시 만날 게 분명해요, 그쪽만 빼고 다요."], ["You must write to me.", "나는 부탁이 둘 있어요, 패니."], ["And the other, that you will often call on Mrs.", "하나는 그쪽 편지예요. 나한테 꼭 편지해요."], ["Grant, and make her amends for my being gone.”", "그리고 다른 하나는, 그쪽이 곧잘 그랜트 부인을 찾아가, 내가 떠난 걸 그이한테 갚아 주는 거예요.”"]], [["The first, at least, of these favours Fanny would rather not have been asked; but it was impossible for her to refuse the correspondence; it was impossible for her even not to accede to it more readily than her own judgment authorised.", "이 부탁 가운데 적어도 첫째는 패니가 차라리 안 받았으면 싶은 것이었다. 그러나 편지 왕래를 마다하기란 그녀로선 어림없었다."], ["There was no resisting so much apparent affection.", "제 판단이 허락하는 것보다 더 선선히 그것에 응하지 않기조차 어림없었다."], ["Her disposition was peculiarly calculated to value a fond treatment, and from having hitherto known so little of it, she was the more overcome by Miss Crawford’s.", "그토록 짙어 보이는 애정을 거스를 길이 없었던 것이다. 그녀의 성미는 다정한 대접을 유난히 값지게 여기도록 되어 있었고, 여태 그런 것을 워낙 못 알고 지낸 터라, 크로퍼드 양의 그것에 더욱 사로잡혔다."], ["Besides, there was gratitude towards her, for having made their tête-à-tête so much less painful than her fears had predicted.", "게다가, 저희 단둘의 이야기를 제 두려움이 점친 것보다 훨씬 덜 괴롭게 해 준 데 대한 고마움도 있었다."]], [["It was over, and she had escaped without reproaches and without detection.", "그것은 끝났고, 그녀는 나무람도 들킴도 없이 벗어났다."], ["Her secret was still her own; and while that was the case, she thought she could resign herself to almost everything.", "그녀의 비밀은 여전히 저만의 것이었다. 그리고 그러한 한, 그녀는 거의 온갖 것에 저를 내맡길 수 있으리라 여겼다."]], [["In the evening there was another parting.", "저녁에 또 한 번의 작별이 있었다."], ["Henry Crawford came and sat some time with them; and her spirits not being previously in the strongest state, her heart was softened for a while towards him, because he really seemed to feel.", "헨리 크로퍼드가 와서 한동안 그들과 앉아 있었다. 그리고 그녀의 기운이 앞서 그리 굳센 상태가 아니던 터라, 그가 정말로 무언가 느끼는 듯 보여, 그녀의 마음은 한동안 그를 향해 누그러졌다."], ["Quite unlike his usual self, he scarcely said anything.", "여느 저와는 아주 딴판으로, 그는 좀처럼 아무 말도 안 했다."], ["He was evidently oppressed, and Fanny must grieve for him, though hoping she might never see him again till he were the husband of some other woman.", "그는 분명 짓눌려 있었다. 그리고 패니는, 그를 다시는 다른 여자의 남편이 되기 전엔 안 보기를 바라면서도, 그를 안쓰러워할밖에 없었다."]], [["When it came to the moment of parting, he would take her hand, he would not be denied it; he said nothing, however, or nothing that she heard, and when he had left the room, she was better pleased that such a token of friendship had passed.", "헤어질 순간이 오자, 그는 기어이 그녀의 손을 잡으려 했고, 그것을 마다하게 놔두지 않았다. 그러나 그는 아무 말도, 적어도 그녀가 들은 말은 아무것도 안 했다. 그리고 그가 방을 떠나자, 그녀는 그런 우정의 정표가 오간 것이 오히려 흐뭇했다."]], [["On the morrow the Crawfords were gone.", "이튿날 크로퍼드 남매는 떠났다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVII", "# 제37장"]], [["Mr. Crawford gone, Sir Thomas’s next object was that he should be missed; and he entertained great hope that his niece would find a blank in the loss of those attentions which at the time she had felt, or fancied, an evil.", "크로퍼드 씨가 떠나자, 토머스 경의 다음 목표는 그가 아쉬워지는 것이었다."], ["She had tasted of consequence in its most flattering form; and he did hope that the loss of it, the sinking again into nothing, would awaken very wholesome regrets in her mind.", "그리하여 그는, 그 무렵 조카딸이 나쁜 것으로 느꼈거나 여겼던 그 마음씀을 잃은 데서 그녀가 허전함을 찾아내리라 크게 바랐다. 그녀는 그 값어치를 더없이 우쭐한 꼴로 맛본 참이었다."], ["He watched her with this idea; but he could hardly tell with what success.", "그러니 그는, 그것을 잃음이, 다시 아무것도 아닌 데로 가라앉음이 그녀 마음에 자못 이로운 뉘우침을 일깨우기를 정말 바랐다."], ["He hardly knew whether there were any difference in her spirits or not.", "그는 이런 생각으로 그녀를 지켜보았다. 그러나 얼마나 성과가 있는지 그로선 좀처럼 알 수 없었다."], ["She was always so gentle and retiring that her emotions were beyond his discrimination.", "그녀의 기운에 무슨 다름이 있는지 없는지조차 그는 좀처럼 알지 못했다. 그녀는 늘 하도 상냥하고 물러나 있는지라 그 심경이 그의 분간을 넘어섰던 것이다."], ["He did not understand her: he felt that he did not; and therefore applied to Edmund to tell him how she stood affected on the present occasion, and whether she were more or less happy than she had been.", "그는 그녀를 헤아리지 못했다. 그리하여 그는 헤아리지 못함을 느끼고, 그런 까닭에 에드먼드에게, 그녀가 이번 일에서 어찌 느끼고 있는지, 예전보다 더 행복한지 덜 행복한지를 일러 달라 청했다."]], [["Edmund did not discern any symptoms of regret, and thought his father a little unreasonable in supposing the first three or four days could produce any.", "에드먼드는 뉘우침의 낌새라곤 조금도 알아채지 못했고, 처음 사나흘로 무슨 뉘우침이 생기리라 여기는 아버지가 다소 무리하다 여겼다."]], [["What chiefly surprised Edmund was, that Crawford’s sister, the friend and companion who had been so much to her, should not be more visibly regretted.", "에드먼드가 무엇보다 놀란 것은, 크로퍼드의 누이, 곧 그녀에게 그토록 큰 존재이던 그 벗이자 동무가 더 눈에 띄게 아쉬워지지 않는다는 것이었다."], ["He wondered that Fanny spoke so seldom of her, and had so little voluntarily to say of her concern at this separation.", "그는 패니가 그녀에 대해 그토록 드물게 말하고, 이 헤어짐에 대한 저의 마음을 자진해 얘기하는 것이 그토록 적은 것이 의아했다."]], [["Alas!", "아아!"], ["it was this sister, this friend and companion, who was now the chief bane of Fanny’s comfort.", "이제 패니의 편안함에 으뜸가는 골칫거리가 된 것이 바로 이 누이, 이 벗이자 동무였다."], ["If she could have believed Mary’s future fate as unconnected with Mansfield as she was determined the brother’s should be, if she could have hoped her return thither to be as distant as she was much inclined to think his, she would have been light of heart indeed; but the more she recollected and observed, the more deeply was she convinced that everything was now in a fairer train for Miss Crawford’s marrying Edmund than it had ever been before.", "만일 메리의 앞날의 운명이, 저가 그 오라비의 것은 그러하기를 굳게 마음먹은 만큼이나 맨스필드와 상관없다 믿을 수 있었더라면, 만일 그녀가 그리로 돌아올 날이, 저가 그의 것은 그러하리라 자못 여기고 싶은 만큼이나 멀다 바랄 수 있었더라면, 그녀는 정말이지 마음이 가벼웠으련만. 그러나 떠올리고 지켜볼수록, 크로퍼드 양이 에드먼드와 혼인하는 쪽으로 이제 온갖 것이 여태 없던 만큼이나 더 순조로운 궤도에 올랐음을 그녀는 더 깊이 확신했다."], ["On his side the inclination was stronger, on hers less equivocal.", "그의 편에서 그 마음이 더 굳세어졌고, 그녀의 편에서 덜 애매해졌다."], ["His objections, the scruples of his integrity, seemed all done away, nobody could tell how; and the doubts and hesitations of her ambition were equally got over—and equally without apparent reason.", "그의 반대, 곧 그 성실함의 거리낌은 어찌 된 영문인지 죄다 사라진 듯했다. 그리고 그녀의 야심의 미심쩍음과 머뭇거림 또한 마찬가지로—그리고 마찬가지로 이렇다 할 까닭 없이 걷혔다."], ["It could only be imputed to increasing attachment.", "그것은 오직 짙어 가는 애착에 돌릴밖에 없었다."], ["His good and her bad feelings yielded to love, and such love must unite them.", "그의 좋은 심경과 그녀의 나쁜 심경이 사랑에 굽혔고, 그런 사랑이라면 둘을 맺어 줄 수밖에 없었다. 그는 손턴 레이시에 관한 얼마간의 볼일이 끝나는 대로—어쩌면 두 주 안에 시내로 갈 참이었다."], ["He was to go to town as soon as some business relative to Thornton Lacey were completed—perhaps within a fortnight; he talked of going, he loved to talk of it; and when once with her again, Fanny could not doubt the rest.", "그는 갈 얘기를 했고, 그 얘기를 즐겨 했다. 그리고 다시 한번 그녀와 함께하면 나머지야 패니로선 의심할 나위 없었다."], ["Her acceptance must be as certain as his offer; and yet there were bad feelings still remaining which made the prospect of it most sorrowful to her, independently, she believed, independently of self.", "그녀의 승낙은 그의 청혼만큼이나 틀림없을 터였다. 그런데도 여전히 남은 나쁜 심경이 있어, 그것의 전망을 그녀에게 더없이 서글픈 것으로 만들었으니—그녀가 믿기로는, 저 자신과는 별개로 서글픈 것이었다."]], [["In their very last conversation, Miss Crawford, in spite of some amiable sensations, and much personal kindness, had still been Miss Crawford; still shewn a mind led astray and bewildered, and without any suspicion of being so; darkened, yet fancying itself light.", "바로 그 지난번 대화에서, 크로퍼드 양은 얼마간의 상냥한 심경과 자못 큰 인간적 친절에도 불구하고 여전히 크로퍼드 양이었다. 여전히 잘못 이끌려 갈피 못 잡으면서도 그런 줄은 조금도 의심 못 하는 마음을, 어두워졌으면서도 스스로 밝은 줄 여기는 마음을 드러냈다."], ["She might love, but she did not deserve Edmund by any other sentiment.", "그녀는 사랑할지언정, 다른 어떤 심경으로도 에드먼드를 받을 만하지 않았다."], ["Fanny believed there was scarcely a second feeling in common between them; and she may be forgiven by older sages for looking on the chance of Miss Crawford’s future improvement as nearly desperate, for thinking that if Edmund’s influence in this season of love had already done so little in clearing her judgment, and regulating her notions, his worth would be finally wasted on her even in years of matrimony.", "패니는 둘 사이에 통하는 두 번째 심경이 좀처럼 없다 믿었다. 그리고 그녀가, 크로퍼드 양이 앞으로 나아질 가망을 거의 가망 없다 보는 것을, 만일 이 사랑의 철에 에드먼드의 힘이 그녀의 판단을 맑히고 그 소견을 다잡는 데 이미 그토록 조금밖에 못 했다면 그 값어치가 혼인의 세월 속에서조차 끝내 그녀에게 헛되이 낭비되리라 여기는 것을, 더 나이 든 현인들은 용서해 줄지도 모른다."]], [["Experience might have hoped more for any young people so circumstanced, and impartiality would not have denied to Miss Crawford’s nature that participation of the general nature of women which would lead her to adopt the opinions of the man she loved and respected as her own.", "경험이라면 그런 처지의 젊은이들에게 더 많이 바랐을지도 모르고, 공평함이라면 크로퍼드 양의 천성에서, 저가 사랑하고 존경하는 사내의 의견을 제 것으로 받아들이게 할 그 여자의 두루 있는 천성의 몫을 부인하지 않았으리라."], ["But as such were Fanny’s persuasions, she suffered very much from them, and could never speak of Miss Crawford without pain.", "그러나 패니의 믿음이 그러했던지라, 그녀는 그것으로 무척 괴로워했고, 아픔 없이는 결코 크로퍼드 양을 입에 올릴 수 없었다."]], [["Sir Thomas, meanwhile, went on with his own hopes and his own observations, still feeling a right, by all his knowledge of human nature, to expect to see the effect of the loss of power and consequence on his niece’s spirits, and the past attentions of the lover producing a craving for their return; and he was soon afterwards able to account for his not yet completely and indubitably seeing all this, by the prospect of another visitor, whose approach he could allow to be quite enough to support the spirits he was watching.", "한편 토머스 경은 제 나름의 바람과 제 나름의 지켜봄을 이어 갔다. 여전히 인간 본성에 대한 그 온갖 앎으로, 힘과 값어치를 잃은 것이 조카딸의 기운에 미친 영향을, 그리고 그 사랑하던 이의 지난 마음씀이 그 되돌아옴에 대한 갈망을 자아내는 것을 볼 자격이 저에게 있다 느꼈다."], ["William had obtained a ten days’ leave of absence, to be given to Northamptonshire, and was coming, the happiest of lieutenants, because the latest made, to shew his happiness and describe his uniform.", "그리고 그는 이내, 이 모두를 아직 온전히, 틀림없이 못 보는 까닭을, 또 한 사람의 손님이 온다는 전망으로 풀어 낼 수 있게 되었으니, 그 다가옴은 그가 지켜보던 그 기운을 떠받치기에 아주 넉넉하다 여길 만한 것이었다. 윌리엄이 노샘프턴셔에서 쓸 열흘의 휴가를 얻어, 세상에 둘도 없이 행복한 중위—갓 임관된 중위인지라 가장 행복한 중위로서, 제 행복을 보이고 제 제복을 그려 보이러 오는 것이었다."]], [["He came; and he would have been delighted to shew his uniform there too, had not cruel custom prohibited its appearance except on duty.", "그는 왔다. 그리고 그는 여기서도 제 제복을 보이면 기뻤으련만, 잔인한 관례가 근무 중 말고는 그 차림을 금했다."], ["So the uniform remained at Portsmouth, and Edmund conjectured that before Fanny had any chance of seeing it, all its own freshness and all the freshness of its wearer’s feelings must be worn away.", "그리하여 제복은 포츠머스에 남았고, 에드먼드는 패니가 그것을 볼 낌새라도 있기 전에 그 온갖 새것 냄새며 그것을 입은 이의 온갖 새것 같은 심경이 다 닳아 없어지리라 넘겨짚었다."], ["It would be sunk into a badge of disgrace; for what can be more unbecoming, or more worthless, than the uniform of a lieutenant, who has been a lieutenant a year or two, and sees others made commanders before him?", "그것은 부끄러움의 표지로 가라앉을 터였다. 중위가 된 지 한두 해에, 남들이 저보다 먼저 함장이 되는 것을 보는 중위의 제복보다 더 안 어울리고 더 값없는 것이 무엇이랴?"], ["So reasoned Edmund, till his father made him the confidant of a scheme which placed Fanny’s chance of seeing the second lieutenant of H.M.S. Thrush in all his glory in another light.", "에드먼드는 이렇게 헤아렸으니, 그의 아버지가 그로 하여금, 국왕 폐하의 배 스러시호의 차석 중위가 그 온갖 영광에 싸인 것을 패니가 볼 낌새를 딴 눈으로 보게 한 한 계획의 속내를 그에게 털어놓기까지는 그러했다."]], [["This scheme was that she should accompany her brother back to Portsmouth, and spend a little time with her own family.", "이 계획인즉, 그녀가 오라비를 따라 포츠머스로 돌아가, 제 식구와 얼마간의 시간을 보낸다는 것이었다. 그것은, 위엄 어린 상념에 잠긴 어느 때에 토머스 경에게 옳고 바람직한 조처로 떠오른 것이었다."], ["It had occurred to Sir Thomas, in one of his dignified musings, as a right and desirable measure; but before he absolutely made up his mind, he consulted his son.", "그러나 아주 마음을 굳히기 전에 그는 아들과 의논했다. 에드먼드는 그것을 어느 모로나 헤아려 보고, 옳은 것 말고는 아무것도 보지 못했다."], ["Edmund considered it every way, and saw nothing but what was right.", "그 일은 그 자체로 좋았고, 이보다 나은 때에 할 수도 없었다."], ["The thing was good in itself, and could not be done at a better time; and he had no doubt of it being highly agreeable to Fanny.", "그리고 그는 그것이 패니에게 더없이 흐뭇한 일임을 의심치 않았다. 이것으로 토머스 경은 마음을 정하기에 넉넉했다."], ["This was enough to determine Sir Thomas; and a decisive “then so it shall be”", "그리하여 단호한 “그럼 그리하지”가 그 일의 그 단계를 매듭지었다."], ["closed that stage of the business; Sir Thomas retiring from it with some feelings of satisfaction, and views of good over and above what he had communicated to his son; for his prime motive in sending her away had very little to do with the propriety of her seeing her parents again, and nothing at all with any idea of making her happy.", "토머스 경은 얼마간의 흡족한 심경과, 아들에게 전한 것 이상의 좋은 속셈을 지닌 채 거기서 물러났다. 그녀를 떠나보내는 그의 으뜸가는 동기는 그녀가 다시 부모를 보는 것의 온당함과는 별로 상관이 없었고, 그녀를 행복하게 해 준다는 생각과는 조금도 상관이 없었던 것이다."], ["He certainly wished her to go willingly, but he as certainly wished her to be heartily sick of home before her visit ended; and that a little abstinence from the elegancies and luxuries of Mansfield Park would bring her mind into a sober state, and incline her to a juster estimate of the value of that home of greater permanence, and equal comfort, of which she had the offer.", "그는 분명 그녀가 기꺼이 가기를 바랐으나, 그에 못지않게 분명, 그 방문이 끝나기 전에 그녀가 집에 진저리 나기를 바랐다. 그리고 맨스필드 파크의 우아함과 호사를 얼마간 삼가는 것이 그녀의 마음을 차분한 상태로 만들어, 그녀에게 청해진, 더 오래가고 못지않게 편안한 그 보금자리의 값어치를 더 올바로 가늠하도록 기울게 하기를 바랐다."]], [["It was a medicinal project upon his niece’s understanding, which he must consider as at present diseased.", "그것은 조카딸의 슬기를 겨냥한 처방이었으니, 그는 그것을 지금으로선 병들었다 여길밖에 없었다."], ["A residence of eight or nine years in the abode of wealth and plenty had a little disordered her powers of comparing and judging.", "여덟아홉 해를 재물과 넉넉함의 거처에서 지낸 것이 그녀의 견주고 판단하는 힘을 얼마간 어지럽혀 놓은 것이다."], ["Her father’s house would, in all probability, teach her the value of a good income; and he trusted that she would be the wiser and happier woman, all her life, for the experiment he had devised.", "아버지의 집이 아마 그녀에게 넉넉한 수입의 값어치를 가르쳐 줄 터였다. 그리고 그는, 저가 궁리해 낸 이 시험 덕에 그녀가 평생 더 지혜롭고 더 행복한 여자가 되리라 믿었다."]], [["Had Fanny been at all addicted to raptures, she must have had a strong attack of them when she first understood what was intended, when her uncle first made her the offer of visiting the parents, and brothers, and sisters, from whom she had been divided almost half her life; of returning for a couple of months to the scenes of her infancy, with William for the protector and companion of her journey, and the certainty of continuing to see William to the last hour of his remaining on land.", "패니가 조금이라도 황홀에 빠지는 사람이었더라면, 무엇이 뜻해졌는지를 처음 알아들었을 때—삼촌이 처음 그녀에게, 거의 반평생 떨어져 지낸 그 부모와 형제자매를 만나 볼 것을, 윌리엄을 그 여정의 보호자이자 동무로 삼아 두어 달 어린 시절의 그 자리로 돌아갈 것을, 그리고 윌리엄이 뭍에 남아 있는 마지막 시각까지 계속 그를 볼 수 있으리라는 확실함을 내밀었을 때—그녀는 그 황홀에 세게 사로잡혔을 게 틀림없다. 그녀가 언제고 벅찬 기쁨에 몸을 내맡긴 적이 있다면 그것은 그때였을 게 틀림없으니, 그녀는 기뻤던 것이다."], ["Had she ever given way to bursts of delight, it must have been then, for she was delighted, but her happiness was of a quiet, deep, heart-swelling sort; and though never a great talker, she was always more inclined to silence when feeling most strongly.", "그러나 그녀의 행복은 조용하고 깊은, 가슴 벅찬 종류의 것이었다. 그리고 그녀는 원래 말이 많은 사람은 아니었으되, 가장 세차게 느낄 때면 늘 더 잠잠해지는 쪽이었다."], ["At the moment she could only thank and accept.", "그 순간 그녀는 그저 고마워하며 받아들일밖에 없었다."], ["Afterwards, when familiarised with the visions of enjoyment so suddenly opened, she could speak more largely to William and Edmund of what she felt; but still there were emotions of tenderness that could not be clothed in words.", "그 뒤로, 그토록 문득 열린 그 즐거움의 그림에 익숙해지자, 그녀는 제가 느낀 바를 윌리엄과 에드먼드에게 더 넉넉히 얘기할 수 있었다. 그러나 그래도 말로 옷 입힐 수 없는 애틋한 심경들이 있었다."], ["The remembrance of all her earliest pleasures, and of what she had suffered in being torn from them, came over her with renewed strength, and it seemed as if to be at home again would heal every pain that had since grown out of the separation.", "제 온갖 가장 어릴 적 즐거움에 대한 기억과, 그것에서 뜯겨 나며 겪은 것에 대한 기억이 새로운 힘으로 그녀를 덮쳐 왔고, 다시 집에 있으면 그 헤어짐에서 그 뒤로 자라난 온갖 아픔이 다 나을 것만 같았다."], ["To be in the centre of such a circle, loved by so many, and more loved by all than she had ever been before; to feel affection without fear or restraint; to feel herself the equal of those who surrounded her; to be at peace from all mention of the Crawfords, safe from every look which could be fancied a reproach on their account.", "그런 무리의 한가운데에 있는 것, 그토록 많은 이에게 사랑받고, 여태 있어 본 것보다 다들에게 더 사랑받는 것, 두려움도 삼감도 없이 정을 느끼는 것, 저 자신이 저를 둘러싼 이들과 대등함을 느끼는 것, 크로퍼드가에 대한 온갖 언급에서 놓여나 평온한 것, 그들 때문에 나무람이려니 여겨질 만한 온갖 눈길에서 벗어나 안전한 것."], ["This was a prospect to be dwelt on with a fondness that could be but half acknowledged.", "이것은 절반밖에 인정 못 할 애틋함으로 마음 두고픈 전망이었다."]], [["Edmund, too—to be two months from him (and perhaps she might be allowed to make her absence three) must do her good.", "에드먼드도—그에게서 두 달 떨어져 있는 것이 (어쩌면 그녀는 그 부재를 석 달로 늘리도록 허락받을 수도 있으리라) 그녀에게 이로울 게 틀림없었다."], ["At a distance, unassailed by his looks or his kindness, and safe from the perpetual irritation of knowing his heart, and striving to avoid his confidence, she should be able to reason herself into a properer state; she should be able to think of him as in London, and arranging everything there, without wretchedness.", "멀리 떨어져, 그의 낯빛이나 친절에 공략당하지 않고, 그의 마음을 아는 그 끊임없는 짜증과, 그의 속내를 피하려 애쓰는 것에서 안전한 곳에서라면, 그녀는 더 온당한 상태로 스스로를 다잡을 수 있으리라. 그녀는 그가 런던에서 온갖 것을 마련하고 있으려니, 비참함 없이 그를 생각할 수 있으리라."], ["What might have been hard to bear at Mansfield was to become a slight evil at Portsmouth.", "맨스필드에서라면 견디기 힘들었을 것이 포츠머스에서는 자그마한 화가 되려 하고 있었다."]], [["The only drawback was the doubt of her aunt Bertram’s being comfortable without her.", "단 하나 걸리는 것은 버트럼 이모가 그녀 없이 편안할까 하는 미심쩍음이었다. 그녀는 다른 누구에게도 쓸모가 없었다."], ["She was of use to no one else; but there she might be missed to a degree that she did not like to think of; and that part of the arrangement was, indeed, the hardest for Sir Thomas to accomplish, and what only he could have accomplished at all.", "그러나 거기서는 그녀가, 생각하기 싫을 만큼이나 아쉬워질지도 몰랐다. 그리고 그 채비의 그 대목이야말로 정말이지 토머스 경이 이뤄 내기에 가장 어려운 것이었고, 오직 그만이 조금이라도 이뤄 낼 수 있는 것이었다."]], [["But he was master at Mansfield Park.", "그러나 그는 맨스필드 파크의 주인이었다."], ["When he had really resolved on any measure, he could always carry it through; and now by dint of long talking on the subject, explaining and dwelling on the duty of Fanny’s sometimes seeing her family, he did induce his wife to let her go; obtaining it rather from submission, however, than conviction, for Lady Bertram was convinced of very little more than that Sir Thomas thought Fanny ought to go, and therefore that she must.", "어떤 조처든 정말로 마음을 굳히면 그는 늘 그것을 밀고 나갈 수 있었다. 그리하여 이제, 그 화제를 두고 오래 얘기하고, 패니가 이따금 제 식구를 보는 것의 도리를 설명하고 되풀이한 끝에, 그는 아내로 하여금 그녀를 보내게 만들었다."], ["In the calmness of her own dressing-room, in the impartial flow of her own meditations, unbiased by his bewildering statements, she could not acknowledge any necessity for Fanny’s ever going near a father and mother who had done without her so long, while she was so useful to herself.", "다만 확신에서라기보다 순종에서 얻어 낸 것이었으니, 버트럼 부인은 토머스 경이 패니가 가야 마땅하다 여기니 그리해야 한다는 것 말고는 좀처럼 확신하는 것이 없었기 때문이다. 제 옷방의 그 고요 속에서, 그의 어리둥절케 하는 말에 치우치지 않은 제 상념의 공평한 흐름 속에서라면, 그녀는 패니가, 저에게 그토록 쓸모 있으면서, 그토록 오래 저 없이 지내 온 아버지와 어머니 곁에 언제고 갈 무슨 필요가 있다고는 도무지 인정할 수 없었다."], ["And as to the not missing her, which under Mrs. Norris’s discussion was the point attempted to be proved, she set herself very steadily against admitting any such thing.", "그리고 그녀가 안 아쉬워지리라는 것—노리스 부인의 논의 아래 증명되려던 그 요점에 대해서는, 그녀는 그런 것을 인정하기를 아주 꿋꿋이 마다했다."]], [["Sir Thomas had appealed to her reason, conscience, and dignity.", "토머스 경은 그녀의 이성과 양심과 위엄에 호소했다."], ["He called it a sacrifice, and demanded it of her goodness and self-command as such.", "그는 그것을 희생이라 부르고, 그녀의 착함과 자제심에 그런 것으로서 그것을 요구했다."], ["But Mrs. Norris wanted to persuade her that Fanny could be very well spared—she being ready to give up all her own time to her as requested—and, in short, could not really be wanted or missed.", "그러나 노리스 부인은 그녀에게, 패니는 얼마든지 없어도 된다고—저 자신이 청받은 대로 제 온갖 시간을 그녀에게 다 바칠 채비가 돼 있으니—요컨대 정말로 필요하지도 아쉬워지지도 않으리라 설득하려 했다."]], [["“That may be, sister,”", "“그럴지도 모르지, 언니.”"], ["was all Lady Bertram’s reply.", "이것이 버트럼 부인의 대꾸의 전부였다."], ["“I dare say you are very right; but I am sure I shall miss her very much.”", "“아마 언니 말이 아주 옳겠죠. 하지만 나는 분명 그 애가 무척 아쉬울 거예요.”"]], [["The next step was to communicate with Portsmouth.", "다음 단계는 포츠머스에 알리는 것이었다."], ["Fanny wrote to offer herself; and her mother’s answer, though short, was so kind—a few simple lines expressed so natural and motherly a joy in the prospect of seeing her child again, as to confirm all the daughter’s views of happiness in being with her—convincing her that she should now find a warm and affectionate friend in the “mama”", "패니가 편지를 써서 저를 보내겠다 청했다. 그리고 어머니의 답장은, 비록 짧았으나 어찌나 다정하던지—제 아이를 다시 볼 전망에 그토록 자연스럽고 어머니다운 기쁨을 몇 마디 소박한 줄로 담아, 어머니와 함께한다는 것에 대한 딸의 온갖 행복의 그림을 죄다 굳혀 주었다—앞서 저에게 이렇다 할 애정을 보인 적이 분명 없던 그 “엄마”에게서 이제 따뜻하고 정겨운 벗을 찾으리라 그녀를 확신시켰다."], ["who had certainly shewn no remarkable fondness for her formerly; but this she could easily suppose to have been her own fault or her own fancy.", "그러나 이것은, 제 잘못이었거나 제 상상이었으려니 그녀는 쉬 여길 수 있었다."], ["She had probably alienated love by the helplessness and fretfulness of a fearful temper, or been unreasonable in wanting a larger share than any one among so many could deserve.", "그녀는 아마 겁 많은 성미의 그 무력함과 짜증으로 사랑을 멀어지게 했거나, 그토록 많은 이 가운데 누구도 받을 만한 것보다 더 큰 몫을 바라는 무리한 짓을 했으리라."], ["Now, when she knew better how to be useful, and how to forbear, and when her mother could be no longer occupied by the incessant demands of a house full of little children, there would be leisure and inclination for every comfort, and they should soon be what mother and daughter ought to be to each other.", "이제, 어떻게 쓸모가 되고 어떻게 참는지를 더 잘 알게 되었을 때, 그리고 어머니가 더는 어린것들로 가득한 집의 끊임없는 요구에 매이지 않게 되었을 때라면, 온갖 편안함을 위한 여유와 마음이 있을 터였고, 둘은 이내 어머니와 딸이 서로에게 마땅히 그래야 할 사이가 될 터였다."]], [["William was almost as happy in the plan as his sister.", "윌리엄은 그 계획에 제 누이 못지않게 행복했다."], ["It would be the greatest pleasure to him to have her there to the last moment before he sailed, and perhaps find her there still when he came in from his first cruise.", "그로서는, 출항 전 마지막 순간까지 그녀를 거기 두는 것, 그리고 어쩌면 첫 순항에서 돌아왔을 때 그녀를 여전히 거기서 보는 것이 더없는 즐거움일 터였다."], ["And besides, he wanted her so very much to see the Thrush before she went out of harbour—the Thrush was certainly the finest sloop in the service—and there were several improvements in the dockyard, too, which he quite longed to shew her.", "게다가 그는 스러시호가 항구를 나서기 전에 그녀가 그것을 몹시도 봤으면 싶었다—스러시호야말로 분명 해군에서 가장 훌륭한 슬루프함이었다—그리고 조선소에도 그가 그녀에게 무척 보여 주고픈 손질이 여럿 있었다."]], [["He did not scruple to add that her being at home for a while would be a great advantage to everybody.", "그는 그녀가 얼마간 집에 있는 것이 모두에게 큰 득이 되리라는 말을 덧붙이기를 서슴지 않았다."]], [["“I do not know how it is,”", "“어찌 된 영문인지 모르겠어.”"], ["said he; “but we seem to want some of your nice ways and orderliness at my father’s.", "그가 말했다. “하지만 아버지 댁엔 네 그 알뜰한 방식이랑 정연함이 얼마쯤 아쉬운 것 같아."], ["The house is always in confusion.", "집이 늘 어수선하거든."], ["You will set things going in a better way, I am sure.", "넌 분명 만사를 더 나은 식으로 굴러가게 해 놓을 거야."], ["You will tell my mother how it all ought to be, and you will be so useful to Susan, and you will teach Betsey, and make the boys love and mind you.", "넌 어머니한테 온갖 게 어때야 하는지 일러 줄 테고, 수전한테 그토록 쓸모가 될 테고, 벳시를 가르칠 테고, 사내애들이 널 사랑하고 따르게 만들 거야."], ["How right and comfortable it will all be!”", "그게 다 얼마나 반듯하고 편안할까!”"]], [["By the time Mrs. Price’s answer arrived, there remained but a very few days more to be spent at Mansfield; and for part of one of those days the young travellers were in a good deal of alarm on the subject of their journey, for when the mode of it came to be talked of, and Mrs.", "프라이스 부인의 답장이 이를 무렵엔, 맨스필드에서 보낼 날이 아주 며칠밖에 안 남아 있었다."], ["Norris found that all her anxiety to save her brother-in-law’s money was vain, and that in spite of her wishes and hints for a less expensive conveyance of Fanny, they were to travel post; when she saw Sir Thomas actually give William notes for the purpose, she was struck with the idea of there being room for a third in the carriage, and suddenly seized with a strong inclination to go with them, to go and see her poor dear sister Price.", "그리고 그 며칠 가운데 한 날의 얼마 동안, 젊은 나그네들은 제 여정을 두고 자못 마음을 졸였다. 그 방식이 얘기되기에 이르러, 형부의 돈을 아끼려던 제 온갖 안달이 헛됨을, 패니를 덜 비싼 편으로 데려가라는 제 바람과 넌지시 비침에도 불구하고 그들이 역마차로 가리라는 것을 노리스 부인이 알았을 때, 토머스 경이 실제로 그 비용으로 윌리엄에게 지폐를 주는 것을 그녀가 보았을 때, 그녀는 마차에 세 번째 자리가 있다는 생각에 사로잡혀, 문득 그들과 함께 가고픈, 딱한 소중한 동생 프라이스를 보러 가고픈 강한 마음에 휩싸인 것이다."], ["She proclaimed her thoughts.", "그녀는 제 생각을 떠벌렸다."], ["She must say that she had more than half a mind to go with the young people; it would be such an indulgence to her; she had not seen her poor dear sister Price for more than twenty years; and it would be a help to the young people in their journey to have her older head to manage for them; and she could not help thinking her poor dear sister Price would feel it very unkind of her not to come by such an opportunity.", "절반 넘게 젊은이들과 함께 가고픈 마음이 있노라 말해야겠다고, 저한테는 그것이 그런 호사일 테고, 딱한 소중한 동생 프라이스를 이십 년 넘게 못 봤으며, 저의 나이 든 지혜가 그들을 대신해 챙겨 주면 젊은이들 여정에 도움이 될 테고, 딱한 소중한 동생 프라이스가 이런 기회에 제가 안 오는 걸 무척 야박하게 여기지 않을까 여기지 않을 수 없다고."]], [["William and Fanny were horror-struck at the idea.", "윌리엄과 패니는 그 생각에 소름이 끼쳤다."]], [["All the comfort of their comfortable journey would be destroyed at once.", "그 오붓한 여정의 온갖 편안함이 대번에 무너질 판이었다."], ["With woeful countenances they looked at each other.", "애처로운 낯빛으로 둘은 서로를 바라보았다."], ["Their suspense lasted an hour or two.", "그 마음 졸임이 한두 시간 이어졌다."], ["No one interfered to encourage or dissuade. Mrs.", "아무도 부추기거나 말리려 끼어들지 않았다."], ["Norris was left to settle the matter by herself; and it ended, to the infinite joy of her nephew and niece, in the recollection that she could not possibly be spared from Mansfield Park at present; that she was a great deal too necessary to Sir Thomas and Lady Bertram for her to be able to answer it to herself to leave them even for a week, and therefore must certainly sacrifice every other pleasure to that of being useful to them.", "노리스 부인은 그 일을 저 혼자 정하도록 내버려졌다. 그리고 그것은, 조카와 조카딸의 이루 말할 수 없는 기쁨으로, 저가 지금 맨스필드 파크에서 도무지 빠질 수 없다는 것—토머스 경과 버트럼 부인에게 저가 너무나 없어선 안 될 사람이라, 한 주라도 그들을 떠나는 것은 스스로에게 떳떳할 수 없으니, 반드시 그들에게 쓸모가 되는 즐거움을 위해 다른 온갖 즐거움을 희생해야 한다는 것을 떠올리는 것으로 끝났다."]], [["It had, in fact, occurred to her, that though taken to Portsmouth for nothing, it would be hardly possible for her to avoid paying her own expenses back again.", "실은, 공짜로 포츠머스에 데려다진다 해도, 되돌아오는 제 비용은 도무지 안 치를 수 없으리라는 생각이 그녀에게 떠올랐던 것이다."], ["So her poor dear sister Price was left to all the disappointment of her missing such an opportunity, and another twenty years’ absence, perhaps, begun.", "그리하여 딱한 소중한 동생 프라이스는 그런 기회를 놓치는 온갖 실망에 내맡겨졌고, 어쩌면 또 이십 년의 못 봄이 시작되었다."]], [["Edmund’s plans were affected by this Portsmouth journey, this absence of Fanny’s.", "에드먼드의 계획도 이 포츠머스 여정에, 이 패니의 부재에 영향받았다."], ["He too had a sacrifice to make to Mansfield Park as well as his aunt.", "그 역시 이모와 마찬가지로 맨스필드 파크에 바칠 희생이 있었다. 그는 이맘때쯤 런던에 갈 작정이었다."], ["He had intended, about this time, to be going to London; but he could not leave his father and mother just when everybody else of most importance to their comfort was leaving them; and with an effort, felt but not boasted of, he delayed for a week or two longer a journey which he was looking forward to with the hope of its fixing his happiness for ever.", "그러나 그들의 편안함에 가장 중한 다른 온갖 이가 그들을 떠나는 바로 그때에 아버지와 어머니를 떠날 수는 없었다. 그리하여 자랑삼진 않았으되 느껴진 애씀으로, 그는 제 행복을 영영 굳혀 주리라는 바람으로 손꼽아 기다리던 그 여정을 한두 주 더 미뤘다."]], [["He told Fanny of it.", "그는 그것을 패니에게 말했다."], ["She knew so much already, that she must know everything.", "그녀가 이미 하도 많이 아는지라, 그녀는 온갖 것을 알밖에 없었다."], ["It made the substance of one other confidential discourse about Miss Crawford; and Fanny was the more affected from feeling it to be the last time in which Miss Crawford’s name would ever be mentioned between them with any remains of liberty.", "그것이 크로퍼드 양에 관한 또 한 번의 은밀한 이야기의 알맹이를 이뤘다. 그리고 패니는, 크로퍼드 양의 이름이 조금이나마 스스럼없이 둘 사이에 언제고 입에 오르는 마지막이려니 느껴 한결 더 마음이 움직였다."], ["Once afterwards she was alluded to by him.", "그 뒤 한 번 그가 그녀를 넌지시 입에 올렸다."], ["Lady Bertram had been telling her niece in the evening to write to her soon and often, and promising to be a good correspondent herself; and Edmund, at a convenient moment, then added in a whisper, “And I shall write to you, Fanny, when I have anything worth writing about, anything to say that I think you will like to hear, and that you will not hear so soon from any other quarter.”", "버트럼 부인이 저녁에 조카딸에게 이내 자주 편지하라 이르며 저 자신도 좋은 편지 벗이 되겠노라 약속하고 있었는데, 에드먼드가 알맞은 순간에 이렇게 속삭여 덧붙인 것이다. “그리고 내가 너한테 편지하마, 패니, 무언가 적을 값어치 있는 게, 네가 듣고 싶어 하려니 싶은, 그리고 다른 데선 그리 이내 못 들을 무언가 할 말이 생기면.”"], ["Had she doubted his meaning while she listened, the glow in his face, when she looked up at him, would have been decisive.", "듣는 동안 그의 뜻을 미심쩍어했다 한들, 그녀가 그를 올려다볼 제 그의 낯에 어린 그 달아오름이 그것을 못 박아 주었으리라."]], [["For this letter she must try to arm herself.", "이 편지를 위해 그녀는 저를 다잡으려 애써야 했다."], ["That a letter from Edmund should be a subject of terror!", "에드먼드의 편지가 두려움의 대상이 되다니!"], ["She began to feel that she had not yet gone through all the changes of opinion and sentiment which the progress of time and variation of circumstances occasion in this world of changes.", "그녀는, 이 바뀜의 세상에서 시간의 흐름과 사정의 달라짐이 자아내는 그 온갖 생각과 심경의 바뀜을 저가 아직 다 겪지 않았음을 느끼기 시작했다."], ["The vicissitudes of the human mind had not yet been exhausted by her.", "인간 마음의 그 부침은 그녀에게서 아직 다하지 않은 것이었다."]], [["Poor Fanny!", "딱한 패니!"], ["though going as she did willingly and eagerly, the last evening at Mansfield Park must still be wretchedness.", "그녀가 그토록 기꺼이, 못내 가는 것이었음에도, 맨스필드 파크에서의 마지막 저녁은 그래도 비참함일밖에 없었다. 헤어질 제 그녀의 마음은 온통 서글펐다."], ["Her heart was completely sad at parting.", "그녀는 집 안 방마다에 눈물을 흘렸고, 사랑하는 식구마다에는 훨씬 더 그러했다."], ["She had tears for every room in the house, much more for every beloved inhabitant.", "그녀는 이모에게 매달렸으니, 이모가 아쉬워질 것이기 때문이었다. 그녀는 삼촌의 손에 흐느낌을 삼키며 입 맞췄으니, 그를 언짢게 했기 때문이었다."], ["She clung to her aunt, because she would miss her; she kissed the hand of her uncle with struggling sobs, because she had displeased him; and as for Edmund, she could neither speak, nor look, nor think, when the last moment came with him; and it was not till it was over that she knew he was giving her the affectionate farewell of a brother.", "그리고 에드먼드로 말하면, 그와의 마지막 순간이 왔을 때 그녀는 말할 수도, 볼 수도, 생각할 수도 없었다. 그리고 그것이 지나고 나서야, 그녀는 그가 오라비의 정겨운 작별을 저에게 건네고 있었음을 알았다."]], [["All this passed overnight, for the journey was to begin very early in the morning; and when the small, diminished party met at breakfast, William and Fanny were talked of as already advanced one stage.", "이 모두가 밤새 오갔으니, 여정이 아침 아주 일찍 시작되기로 되어 있었기 때문이다. 그리하여 그 작고 단출해진 무리가 아침 식사에 모였을 때, 윌리엄과 패니는 이미 한 참(站)을 나아간 것으로 이야기되었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVIII", "# 제38장"]], [["The novelty of travelling, and the happiness of being with William, soon produced their natural effect on Fanny’s spirits, when Mansfield Park was fairly left behind; and by the time their first stage was ended, and they were to quit Sir Thomas’s carriage, she was able to take leave of the old coachman, and send back proper messages, with cheerful looks.", "여행의 새로움과 윌리엄과 함께한다는 행복은, 맨스필드 파크가 아주 뒤로 물러나자 이내 패니의 기운에 그 자연스러운 효험을 냈다. 그리하여 첫 참(站)이 끝나 토머스 경의 마차를 떠나게 될 무렵, 그녀는 늙은 마부에게 작별을 고하고 밝은 낯빛으로 온당한 인사말을 돌려보낼 수 있었다."]], [["Of pleasant talk between the brother and sister there was no end.", "오누이 사이의 정겨운 얘기에는 끝이 없었다. 온갖 것이 잔뜩 신난 윌리엄의 마음에 재밋거리가 되었고, 그는 좀 더 높은 결의 화제 사이사이에 장난과 농이 넘쳤다."], ["Everything supplied an amusement to the high glee of William’s mind, and he was full of frolic and joke in the intervals of their higher-toned subjects, all of which ended, if they did not begin, in praise of the Thrush, conjectures how she would be employed, schemes for an action with some superior force, which (supposing the first lieutenant out of the way, and William was not very merciful to the first lieutenant) was to give himself the next step as soon as possible, or speculations upon prize-money, which was to be generously distributed at home, with only the reservation of enough to make the little cottage comfortable, in which he and Fanny were to pass all their middle and later life together.", "그 화제들은, 시작이야 그렇지 않더라도, 죄다 스러시호에 대한 칭찬으로, 그것이 어찌 쓰일까 하는 짐작으로, 어떤 우세한 적과 한판 붙을 궁리로(선임 중위가 자리를 비운다 치면—윌리엄은 선임 중위에게 그리 인정을 베풀지 않았다—되도록 빨리 제 진급을 이룰 요량이었다), 아니면 나포 상금에 대한 억측으로 끝이 났다. 그 상금은 집에 넉넉히 나눠 주되, 그와 패니가 앞으로 중년과 노년을 죄다 함께 보낼 그 자그마한 오두막을 편안하게 꾸밀 만큼만 남겨 둘 참이었다."]], [["Fanny’s immediate concerns, as far as they involved Mr.", "패니의 당장의 걱정거리는, 크로퍼드 씨와 얽힌 한, 그들 대화의 아무 몫도 되지 않았다."], ["Crawford, made no part of their conversation.", "윌리엄은 무슨 일이 있었는지 알았고, 저가 인간의 됨됨이 가운데 으뜸으로 여길밖에 없는 사내에게 누이의 심경이 그토록 차가운 것을 진심으로 안타까워했다."], ["William knew what had passed, and from his heart lamented that his sister’s feelings should be so cold towards a man whom he must consider as the first of human characters; but he was of an age to be all for love, and therefore unable to blame; and knowing her wish on the subject, he would not distress her by the slightest allusion.", "그러나 그는 온통 사랑 편일 나이라 나무랄 줄을 몰랐다. 그리고 이 일에 대한 그녀의 바람을 알기에, 그는 조금도 넌지시 비쳐 그녀를 괴롭히지 않으려 했다."]], [["She had reason to suppose herself not yet forgotten by Mr. Crawford.", "그녀는 아직 크로퍼드 씨에게 잊히지 않았으려니 여길 만한 까닭이 있었다."], ["She had heard repeatedly from his sister within the three weeks which had passed since their leaving Mansfield, and in each letter there had been a few lines from himself, warm and determined like his speeches.", "맨스필드를 떠난 뒤 지난 세 주 동안 그녀는 그 누이에게서 여러 번 소식을 받았고, 편지마다 그 자신에게서 온 몇 줄이, 그의 말투처럼 뜨겁고 단호한 몇 줄이 들어 있었던 것이다. 그것은 패니가 두려워한 것 못지않게 언짢게 여긴 편지 왕래였다."], ["It was a correspondence which Fanny found quite as unpleasant as she had feared.", "크로퍼드 양의 그 발랄하고 정겨운 글투 자체가, 그녀가 이렇게 하는 수 없이 그 오라비의 펜에서 읽어야 하는 것과는 별개로 하나의 화였다."], ["Miss Crawford’s style of writing, lively and affectionate, was itself an evil, independent of what she was thus forced into reading from the brother’s pen, for Edmund would never rest till she had read the chief of the letter to him; and then she had to listen to his admiration of her language, and the warmth of her attachments.", "에드먼드가 그녀에게 그 편지의 알맹이를 제게 읽어 주기까지는 좀처럼 가만있지 않았던 까닭이다. 그러면 그녀는 그가 그녀의 말솜씨와 그 애착의 뜨거움을 감탄하는 것을 들어야 했다."], ["There had, in fact, been so much of message, of allusion, of recollection, so much of Mansfield in every letter, that Fanny could not but suppose it meant for him to hear; and to find herself forced into a purpose of that kind, compelled into a correspondence which was bringing her the addresses of the man she did not love, and obliging her to administer to the adverse passion of the man she did, was cruelly mortifying.", "실은, 편지마다 안부와 넌지시 비침과 추억이, 맨스필드에 대한 얘기가 어찌나 많던지, 패니는 그것이 그더러 들으라고 뜻해진 것이려니 여기지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 그런 종류의 목적에 억지로 끌려 들어가, 사랑하지 않는 사내의 구애를 저에게 가져다주고 사랑하는 사내의 어긋난 정을 저더러 시중들게 하는 편지 왕래에 떠밀린 자신을 발견하는 것은 모질게 무안한 노릇이었다."], ["Here, too, her present removal promised advantage.", "여기서도 그녀의 이번 떠남은 이로움을 약속했다."], ["When no longer under the same roof with Edmund, she trusted that Miss Crawford would have no motive for writing strong enough to overcome the trouble, and that at Portsmouth their correspondence would dwindle into nothing.", "더는 에드먼드와 한 지붕 아래 있지 않게 되면, 크로퍼드 양이 그 수고를 무릅쓸 만큼 강한 편지 쓸 동기가 없어지리라, 그리고 포츠머스에서는 그들 편지 왕래가 아무것도 아닌 것으로 시들어 버리리라 그녀는 믿었다."]], [["With such thoughts as these, among ten hundred others, Fanny proceeded in her journey safely and cheerfully, and as expeditiously as could rationally be hoped in the dirty month of February.", "이런 생각들을 천 가지 다른 생각들 틈에 품고서, 패니는 안전하고 밝게, 그리고 진창투성이 이월에 이치에 맞게 바랄 수 있는 만큼 빠르게 여정을 나아갔다."], ["They entered Oxford, but she could take only a hasty glimpse of Edmund’s college as they passed along, and made no stop anywhere till they reached Newbury, where a comfortable meal, uniting dinner and supper, wound up the enjoyments and fatigues of the day.", "그들은 옥스퍼드에 들어섰으나, 지나는 길에 그녀는 에드먼드의 대학을 그저 서둘러 흘깃 볼 수 있었을 뿐이었다. 그리고 뉴베리에 이르기까지 어디서도 멈추지 않았는데, 거기서 만찬과 저녁을 합친 편안한 한 끼가 그날의 즐거움과 고단함을 마무리 지었다."]], [["The next morning saw them off again at an early hour; and with no events, and no delays, they regularly advanced, and were in the environs of Portsmouth while there was yet daylight for Fanny to look around her, and wonder at the new buildings.", "이튿날 아침 그들은 다시 이른 시각에 길을 나섰다. 그리고 아무 일도, 아무 지체도 없이 꾸준히 나아가, 아직 패니가 둘레를 둘러보고 새 건물들을 신기해할 만한 대낮 빛이 남아 있는 동안 포츠머스 어귀에 이르렀다."], ["They passed the drawbridge, and entered the town; and the light was only beginning to fail as, guided by William’s powerful voice, they were rattled into a narrow street, leading from the High Street, and drawn up before the door of a small house now inhabited by Mr.", "그들은 도개교를 지나 시내에 들어섰다."], ["Price.", "그리고 빛이 겨우 스러지기 시작할 무렵, 윌리엄의 우렁찬 목소리에 이끌려, 그들은 하이 스트리트에서 갈라진 좁은 골목으로 덜컹거리며 들어가, 이제 프라이스 씨가 사는 자그마한 집 문 앞에 멎었다."]], [["Fanny was all agitation and flutter; all hope and apprehension.", "패니는 온통 마음 졸이며 설레었다. 온통 바람과 두려움이었다."], ["The moment they stopped, a trollopy-looking maidservant, seemingly in waiting for them at the door, stepped forward, and more intent on telling the news than giving them any help, immediately began with, “The Thrush is gone out of harbour, please sir, and one of the officers has been here to—”", "그들이 멎는 순간, 문간에서 그들을 기다리던 듯한, 헤벌쭉해 보이는 하녀 하나가 앞으로 나와, 무슨 거들 생각보다 소식 전할 마음이 더 앞선지, 대번에 이렇게 말문을 열었다. “스러시호는 항구를 나갔어요, 나리."], ["She was interrupted by a fine tall boy of eleven years old, who, rushing out of the house, pushed the maid aside, and while William was opening the chaise-door himself, called out, “You are just in time.", "그리고 사관 한 분이 여기 오셔서—” 그녀는 열한 살쯤 된 훤칠하고 잘생긴 사내아이에게 말이 잘렸으니, 그 아이가 집에서 뛰쳐나와 하녀를 밀치고, 윌리엄이 손수 마차 문을 열고 있는 사이에 소리친 것이다."], ["We have been looking for you this half-hour.", "“딱 맞춰 왔네. 우리가 반 시간이나 형을 기다렸어."], ["The Thrush went out of harbour this morning.", "스러시호는 오늘 아침에 항구를 나갔어."], ["I saw her.", "내가 봤어."], ["It was a beautiful sight.", "참 멋진 광경이었어."], ["And they think she will have her orders in a day or two. And Mr.", "그리고 하루 이틀이면 출항 명령이 떨어질 거래."], ["Campbell was here at four o’clock to ask for you: he has got one of the Thrush’s boats, and is going off to her at six, and hoped you would be here in time to go with him.”", "그리고 캠벨 씨가 네 시에 형을 찾으러 오셨어. 스러시호 보트를 하나 얻으셔서 여섯 시에 배로 나가는데, 형이 제때 와서 같이 가면 좋겠다고 하셨어.”"]], [["A stare or two at Fanny, as William helped her out of the carriage, was all the voluntary notice which this brother bestowed; but he made no objection to her kissing him, though still entirely engaged in detailing farther particulars of the Thrush’s going out of harbour, in which he had a strong right of interest, being to commence his career of seamanship in her at this very time.", "윌리엄이 그녀를 마차에서 내려 주는 동안, 이 아우가 자진해 보인 아는 체라곤 패니를 한두 번 빤히 쳐다본 것이 다였다. 그러나 그는 그녀가 제게 입 맞추는 것에는 반대하지 않았다. 하기야 여전히 온통, 저에게 강한 관심의 권리가 있는—바로 이때에 그 배에서 뱃사람 노릇을 시작할 참인 그 스러시호의 항구 나섬에 대해 더 낱낱이 늘어놓는 데 팔려 있긴 했지만."]], [["Another moment and Fanny was in the narrow entrance-passage of the house, and in her mother’s arms, who met her there with looks of true kindness, and with features which Fanny loved the more, because they brought her aunt Bertram’s before her, and there were her two sisters: Susan, a well-grown fine girl of fourteen, and Betsey, the youngest of the family, about five—both glad to see her in their way, though with no advantage of manner in receiving her.", "또 한순간이 지나 패니는 집의 그 좁은 현관 통로에 들어, 어머니의 품에 안겼다. 어머니는 참된 다정함의 낯빛으로, 그리고 버트럼 이모의 얼굴을 눈앞에 떠올려 주어 패니가 더욱 사랑한 그 이목구비로 거기서 그녀를 맞았다."], ["But manner Fanny did not want.", "그리고 거기 그녀의 두 누이가 있었다. 잘 자란 훤한 열넷 처녀 수전과, 집안의 막내로 다섯 살쯤 된 벳시였다—둘 다 저희 나름으로 그녀를 반가워했으나, 맞이하는 태도에 이렇다 할 좋은 데는 없었다."], ["Would they but love her, she should be satisfied.", "그러나 태도야 패니가 바라는 것이 아니었다. 그들이 그저 저를 사랑해 주기만 한다면 그녀는 흡족할 터였다."]], [["She was then taken into a parlour, so small that her first conviction was of its being only a passage-room to something better, and she stood for a moment expecting to be invited on; but when she saw there was no other door, and that there were signs of habitation before her, she called back her thoughts, reproved herself, and grieved lest they should have been suspected.", "그녀는 이내 어느 응접실로 안내되었는데, 하도 작아 처음엔 이것이 그저 더 나은 어디로 가는 통로 방이려니 확신하고, 한순간 더 안으로 청받기를 기다리며 서 있었다. 그러나 다른 문이 없는 것을, 그리고 눈앞에 사람 사는 흔적이 있는 것을 보고, 그녀는 생각을 거두고 저를 나무라며, 그들이 그것을 눈치챘을까 봐 서러워했다."], ["Her mother, however, could not stay long enough to suspect anything.", "그러나 어머니는 무엇을 눈치챌 만큼 오래 머물지 못했다. 그녀는 윌리엄을 맞으러 다시 거리 쪽 문으로 가 버린 것이다."], ["She was gone again to the street-door, to welcome William. “Oh!", "“아, 사랑하는 윌리엄, 널 보니 얼마나 반가운지."], ["my dear William, how glad I am to see you.", "그런데 스러시호 소식 들었니? 벌써 항구를 나갔단다."], ["But have you heard about the Thrush?", "우리가 생각도 못 한 사흘이나 앞당겨서 말이야."], ["She is gone out of harbour already; three days before we had any thought of it; and I do not know what I am to do about Sam’s things, they will never be ready in time; for she may have her orders to-morrow, perhaps.", "그래서 나는 샘 물건들을 어쩌면 좋을지 모르겠구나, 제때 도무지 채비가 안 될 텐데. 어쩌면 내일 출항 명령이 떨어질지도 모르니까."], ["It takes me quite unawares.", "아주 느닷없이 닥친 일이야."], ["And now you must be off for Spithead too.", "그리고 이제 너도 스핏헤드로 나가야 하고."], ["Campbell has been here, quite in a worry about you; and now what shall we do?", "캠벨이 여기 와서 너 때문에 잔뜩 안달하더라. 그래 이제 어쩌면 좋으냐?"], ["I thought to have had such a comfortable evening with you, and here everything comes upon me at once.”", "나는 너하고 그토록 오붓한 저녁을 보내려 했는데, 온갖 게 한꺼번에 나한테 닥치는구나.”"]], [["Her son answered cheerfully, telling her that everything was always for the best; and making light of his own inconvenience in being obliged to hurry away so soon.", "아들은 밝게 대답하며, 온갖 것은 늘 잘되게 마련이라고, 그리고 저가 이토록 이내 서둘러 떠나야 하는 제 불편을 대수롭잖게 넘겼다."]], [["“To be sure, I had much rather she had stayed in harbour, that I might have sat a few hours with you in comfort; but as there is a boat ashore, I had better go off at once, and there is no help for it.", "“물론 저야 그 배가 항구에 머물러서 어머니하고 몇 시간 편히 앉아 있었으면 훨씬 좋았겠죠. 하지만 뭍에 보트가 있으니 당장 나가는 게 낫겠어요, 어쩔 도리가 없죠."], ["Whereabouts does the Thrush lay at Spithead?", "스러시호가 스핏헤드 어디쯤에 있어요?"], ["Near the Canopus?", "캐노퍼스호 근처예요?"], ["But no matter; here’s Fanny in the parlour, and why should we stay in the passage?", "하지만 상관없어요. 여기 응접실에 패니가 있는데, 우리가 왜 통로에 서 있어야 해요?"], ["Come, mother, you have hardly looked at your own dear Fanny yet.”", "자, 어머니, 어머니의 그 소중한 패니를 아직 제대로 보지도 못하셨잖아요.”"]], [["In they both came, and Mrs. Price having kindly kissed her daughter again, and commented a little on her growth, began with very natural solicitude to feel for their fatigues and wants as travellers.", "둘이 함께 들어왔고, 프라이스 부인은 딸에게 다정히 다시 입 맞추고 그 자란 것을 조금 두고 말하더니, 자못 자연스러운 걱정으로 나그네인 그들의 고단함과 아쉬움을 헤아리기 시작했다."]], [["“Poor dears!", "“딱한 것들!"], ["how tired you must both be!", "둘 다 얼마나 지쳤겠니!"], ["and now, what will you have?", "자, 이제 뭘 좀 들려?"], ["I began to think you would never come.", "나는 너희가 영영 안 오려나 했다."], ["Betsey and I have been watching for you this half-hour.", "벳시하고 나는 반 시간이나 너희를 기다렸어."], ["And when did you get anything to eat?", "그래 언제 뭘 좀 먹었니?"], ["And what would you like to have now?", "그리고 이제 뭘 들고 싶어?"], ["I could not tell whether you would be for some meat, or only a dish of tea, after your journey, or else I would have got something ready.", "여행 뒤에 고기를 좀 들지, 아니면 그냥 차 한 잔이면 될지 알 수가 없어서 미리 뭘 채비 못 했다."], ["And now I am afraid Campbell will be here before there is time to dress a steak, and we have no butcher at hand.", "그리고 이제 스테이크를 구울 짬도 나기 전에 캠벨이 올까 봐 걱정이구나, 게다가 근처에 고깃간도 없고."], ["It is very inconvenient to have no butcher in the street.", "골목에 고깃간이 없으니 참 불편해."], ["We were better off in our last house.", "지난번 집이 그래도 나았지."], ["Perhaps you would like some tea as soon as it can be got.”", "아무래도 차가 되는 대로 좀 마시는 게 좋겠구나.”"]], [["They both declared they should prefer it to anything.", "둘 다 무엇보다도 그게 좋겠다 했다."], ["“Then, Betsey, my dear, run into the kitchen and see if Rebecca has put the water on; and tell her to bring in the tea-things as soon as she can.", "“그럼, 벳시야, 부엌에 가서 리베카가 물을 올렸는지 보렴. 그리고 되도록 빨리 찻그릇을 들여오라 일러라."], ["I wish we could get the bell mended; but Betsey is a very handy little messenger.”", "초인종을 고칠 수 있으면 좋겠는데, 그래도 벳시가 아주 야무진 자그마한 심부름꾼이지.”"]], [["Betsey went with alacrity, proud to shew her abilities before her fine new sister.", "벳시는 냉큼 갔으니, 훤한 새 언니 앞에서 제 재간을 보이는 것이 우쭐했던 것이다."]], [["“Dear me!”", "“어쩌나!”"], ["continued the anxious mother, “what a sad fire we have got, and I dare say you are both starved with cold.", "걱정 많은 어머니가 말을 이었다. “불이 이토록 시원찮으니, 너희 둘 다 추위에 얼었겠다."], ["Draw your chair nearer, my dear.", "의자를 좀 더 가까이 당기렴, 얘야."], ["I cannot think what Rebecca has been about.", "리베카가 대체 뭘 하고 있었는지 모르겠다."], ["I am sure I told her to bring some coals half an hour ago.", "분명 반 시간 전에 석탄을 좀 가져오라 일렀는데."], ["Susan, you should have taken care of the fire.”", "수전, 네가 불을 좀 봤어야지.”"]], [["“I was upstairs, mama, moving my things,”", "“저는 위층에서 제 물건을 옮기고 있었어요, 엄마.”"], ["said Susan, in a fearless, self-defending tone, which startled Fanny.", "수전이, 패니를 흠칫하게 하는 겁 없이 저를 두둔하는 투로 말했다."], ["“You know you had but just settled that my sister Fanny and I should have the other room; and I could not get Rebecca to give me any help.”", "“패니 언니하고 제가 저쪽 방을 쓰기로 방금 막 정하셨잖아요. 그리고 저는 리베카한테 도무지 거들어 달랄 수가 없었고요.”"]], [["Farther discussion was prevented by various bustles: first, the driver came to be paid; then there was a squabble between Sam and Rebecca about the manner of carrying up his sister’s trunk, which he would manage all his own way; and lastly, in walked Mr. Price himself, his own loud voice preceding him, as with something of the oath kind he kicked away his son’s portmanteau and his daughter’s bandbox in the passage, and called out for a candle; no candle was brought, however, and he walked into the room.", "더 실랑이할 새도 없이 여러 소동이 벌어졌다. 먼저 마부가 삯을 받으러 왔고, 그다음 누이의 여행 가방을 어떻게 위층으로 나를지를 두고 샘과 리베카 사이에 옥신각신이 있었는데, 샘은 죄다 제 뜻대로 하려 들었다. 그리고 끝으로, 프라이스 씨 자신이 걸어 들어왔으니, 그 우렁찬 목소리가 그보다 앞섰다. 무슨 욕지거리 비슷한 걸 내뱉으며 통로에서 아들의 여행 가방과 딸의 모자 상자를 걷어차고 초를 가져오라 소리친 것이다. 그러나 초는 안 나왔고, 그는 방으로 걸어 들어왔다."]], [["Fanny with doubting feelings had risen to meet him, but sank down again on finding herself undistinguished in the dusk, and unthought of.", "패니는 미심쩍은 심경으로 일어나 그를 맞으러 갔으나, 어둑함 속에 저가 눈에도 안 띄고 생각조차 안 됨을 알고 다시 주저앉았다."], ["With a friendly shake of his son’s hand, and an eager voice, he instantly began—“Ha!", "아들과 정겹게 악수를 나누고, 열띤 목소리로, 그는 대번에 말문을 열었다. “허!"], ["welcome back, my boy.", "잘 돌아왔다, 얘야."], ["Glad to see you.", "반갑구나."], ["Have you heard the news?", "소식 들었니?"], ["The Thrush went out of harbour this morning.", "스러시호가 오늘 아침 항구를 나갔어."], ["Sharp is the word, you see!", "서둘러야 한다, 알겠니!"], ["By G—, you are just in time!", "이런, 제기랄, 딱 맞춰 왔구나!"], ["The doctor has been here inquiring for you: he has got one of the boats, and is to be off for Spithead by six, so you had better go with him.", "군의관이 여기 와서 널 찾더라. 보트를 하나 얻어서 여섯 시엔 스핏헤드로 나간다니, 너도 그이랑 같이 가는 게 낫겠다."], ["I have been to Turner’s about your mess; it is all in a way to be done.", "네 식사비 때문에 터너네도 다녀왔다, 그럭저럭 될 판이야. 내일 출항 명령이 떨어져도 놀랄 것 없겠다."], ["I should not wonder if you had your orders to-morrow: but you cannot sail with this wind, if you are to cruise to the westward; and Captain Walsh thinks you will certainly have a cruise to the westward, with the Elephant.", "하지만 서쪽으로 순항할 거라면 이 바람으론 못 뜬다. 그리고 월시 함장은 네가 엘리펀트호랑 서쪽 순항을 분명 하게 되리라 여기더라."], ["By G—, I wish you may!", "이런, 제기랄, 그리됐으면 좋겠다!"], ["But old Scholey was saying, just now, that he thought you would be sent first to the Texel.", "하지만 늙은 숄리는 방금, 네가 먼저 텍설로 보내지지 싶다더라."], ["Well, well, we are ready, whatever happens.", "뭐, 됐어, 무슨 일이 있든 우리야 채비가 됐으니."], ["But by G—, you lost a fine sight by not being here in the morning to see the Thrush go out of harbour!", "하지만 이런 제기랄, 넌 아침에 여기 없어서 스러시호가 항구 나서는 그 멋진 광경을 놓쳤구나!"], ["I would not have been out of the way for a thousand pounds.", "천 파운드를 준대도 나는 그 자리를 안 비웠을 게다."], ["Old Scholey ran in at breakfast-time, to say she had slipped her moorings and was coming out, I jumped up, and made but two steps to the platform.", "늙은 숄리가 아침 먹을 참에 뛰어들어와 그 배가 닻줄을 풀고 나오고 있다길래, 나는 벌떡 일어나 두 걸음 만에 관측대로 갔지."], ["If ever there was a perfect beauty afloat, she is one; and there she lays at Spithead, and anybody in England would take her for an eight-and-twenty.", "물 위에 완벽한 미인이 떠 있었던 적이 있다면, 그 배가 바로 그거다. 그리고 그게 스핏헤드에 누워 있는데, 잉글랜드 사람이라면 누구든 28문함으로 볼 게다."], ["I was upon the platform two hours this afternoon looking at her.", "나는 오늘 오후 두 시간이나 관측대에서 그 배를 봤다."], ["She lays close to the Endymion, between her and the Cleopatra, just to the eastward of the sheer hulk.”", "그건 엔디미언호 바로 곁, 그 배랑 클레오파트라호 사이, 폐선 기중선 바로 동쪽에 누워 있어.”"]], [["“Ha!”", "“허!”"], ["cried William, “that’s just where I should have put her myself.", "윌리엄이 외쳤다. “바로 거기가 저라도 그 배를 뒀을 데예요."], ["It’s the best berth at Spithead.", "스핏헤드에서 제일 좋은 정박지죠."], ["But here is my sister, sir; here is Fanny,”", "그런데 여기 제 누이가 있어요, 아버지. 여기 패니예요.”"], ["turning and leading her forward; “it is so dark you do not see her.”", "하며 돌아서서 그녀를 앞으로 이끌었다. “너무 어두워서 안 보이시는군요.”"]], [["With an acknowledgment that he had quite forgot her, Mr.", "그녀를 아주 잊고 있었다는 걸 인정하며, 프라이스 씨는 이제 딸을 맞았다."], ["Price now received his daughter; and having given her a cordial hug, and observed that she was grown into a woman, and he supposed would be wanting a husband soon, seemed very much inclined to forget her again.", "그리고 다정히 한번 껴안고, 다 큰 여자가 됐구나, 아마 머잖아 남편이 필요하겠구나 하더니, 이내 다시 그녀를 잊으려 자못 마음이 기우는 듯했다. 패니는 그의 말투와 술 냄새에 서글피 아파진 심경으로 제자리로 움츠러들었다."], ["Fanny shrunk back to her seat, with feelings sadly pained by his language and his smell of spirits; and he talked on only to his son, and only of the Thrush, though William, warmly interested as he was in that subject, more than once tried to make his father think of Fanny, and her long absence and long journey.", "그리고 그는 오직 아들에게만, 오직 스러시호에 대해서만 얘기를 이어 갔다. 하기야 윌리엄이, 저 역시 그 화제에 뜨겁게 끌렸으면서도, 한 번 넘게 아버지가 패니를, 그 오랜 떠나 있음과 먼 여정을 생각하게 하려 애쓰긴 했지만."]], [["After sitting some time longer, a candle was obtained; but as there was still no appearance of tea, nor, from Betsey’s reports from the kitchen, much hope of any under a considerable period, William determined to go and change his dress, and make the necessary preparations for his removal on board directly, that he might have his tea in comfort afterwards.", "한동안 더 앉아 있으니 초가 하나 나왔다. 그러나 여전히 차는 나올 낌새가 없고, 벳시가 부엌에서 전하는 바로도 상당한 시간 안에는 차가 나올 가망이 별로 없던지라, 윌리엄은 가서 옷을 갈아입고 배로 옮겨 갈 채비를 곧바로 해 두어, 그 뒤에 차를 편히 마시기로 마음먹었다."]], [["As he left the room, two rosy-faced boys, ragged and dirty, about eight and nine years old, rushed into it just released from school, and coming eagerly to see their sister, and tell that the Thrush was gone out of harbour; Tom and Charles.", "그가 방을 나서자, 여덟아홉 살쯤 된, 누더기에 지저분한 두 볼그레한 사내아이가 방금 학교에서 풀려나 방으로 뛰어들었다. 누이를 얼른 보고 스러시호가 항구를 나갔다는 걸 전하러 온 것이었으니, 톰과 찰스였다."], ["Charles had been born since Fanny’s going away, but Tom she had often helped to nurse, and now felt a particular pleasure in seeing again.", "찰스는 패니가 떠난 뒤에 태어났으나, 톰은 그녀가 곧잘 어르며 돌봐 주었던지라, 이제 다시 보는 것이 자못 각별히 반가웠다."], ["Both were kissed very tenderly, but Tom she wanted to keep by her, to try to trace the features of the baby she had loved, and talked to, of his infant preference of herself.", "둘 다 무척 애틋하게 입맞춤을 받았으나, 톰은 저가 사랑하고 말도 걸어 주던 그 아기의 이목구비를 더듬어 보려, 그 아기가 어릴 적 저를 따르던 것을 얘기하려 곁에 붙들어 두고 싶었다. 그러나 톰은 그런 대접에 마음이 없었다."], ["Tom, however, had no mind for such treatment: he came home not to stand and be talked to, but to run about and make a noise; and both boys had soon burst from her, and slammed the parlour-door till her temples ached.", "그는 서서 말 들으려 온 것이 아니라 뛰어다니며 소란 피우러 온 것이었다. 그리하여 두 아이는 이내 그녀에게서 왈칵 벗어나, 그녀의 관자놀이가 지끈거리도록 응접실 문을 쾅 닫아 댔다."]], [["She had now seen all that were at home; there remained only two brothers between herself and Susan, one of whom was a clerk in a public office in London, and the other midshipman on board an Indiaman.", "이제 그녀는 집에 있는 이는 다 본 셈이었다. 저와 수전 사이엔 오라비 둘만 남았는데, 하나는 런던의 관청 서기였고, 다른 하나는 동인도 무역선의 사관후보생이었다."], ["But though she had seen all the members of the family, she had not yet heard all the noise they could make.", "그러나 그녀가 식구를 다 본 것이긴 해도, 그들이 낼 수 있는 온갖 소란을 아직 다 들은 것은 아니었다."], ["Another quarter of an hour brought her a great deal more.", "또 한 십오 분이 그녀에게 훨씬 더 많은 것을 안겨 주었다."], ["William was soon calling out from the landing-place of the second story for his mother and for Rebecca.", "윌리엄이 이내 이 층 층계참에서 어머니와 리베카를 불러 대기 시작했다."], ["He was in distress for something that he had left there, and did not find again.", "그가 거기 두고 온 무언가를 못 찾아 애를 태우고 있었던 것이다."], ["A key was mislaid, Betsey accused of having got at his new hat, and some slight, but essential alteration of his uniform waistcoat, which he had been promised to have done for him, entirely neglected.", "열쇠가 어디 놓였는지 모르게 됐고, 벳시가 그의 새 모자를 만졌다는 혐의를 받았으며, 해 주기로 약속받았던, 사소하나 요긴한 제복 조끼 손질이 아주 소홀히 되어 있었다."]], [["Mrs.", "프라이스 부인과 리베카와 벳시가 다들 저를 두둔하러 위층으로 올라가 죄다 한꺼번에 떠들었는데, 리베카가 가장 큰 소리였다. 그리고 그 일은 그 큰 서두름 속에 되도록 해내야 했다."], ["Price, Rebecca, and Betsey all went up to defend themselves, all talking together, but Rebecca loudest, and the job was to be done as well as it could in a great hurry; William trying in vain to send Betsey down again, or keep her from being troublesome where she was; the whole of which, as almost every door in the house was open, could be plainly distinguished in the parlour, except when drowned at intervals by the superior noise of Sam, Tom, and Charles chasing each other up and down stairs, and tumbling about and hallooing.", "윌리엄은 헛되이 벳시를 다시 아래로 보내거나, 있는 데서 성가시게 굴지 않게 하려 애썼다. 이 온갖 것이, 집 안 거의 모든 문이 열려 있던지라, 응접실에서 또렷이 분간되었다—샘과 톰과 찰스가 계단을 오르내리며 서로 쫓고, 뒹굴고, 고함치는 그 더 큰 소란에 이따금 묻힐 때만 빼면."]], [["Fanny was almost stunned.", "패니는 거의 얼이 빠질 지경이었다."], ["The smallness of the house and thinness of the walls brought everything so close to her, that, added to the fatigue of her journey, and all her recent agitation, she hardly knew how to bear it.", "집의 작음과 벽의 얇음이 온갖 것을 그토록 그녀에게 바짝 들이밀어, 여정의 고단함과 그 온갖 근래의 마음 졸임에 더해, 그녀는 도무지 그것을 어찌 견딜지 몰랐다."], ["Within the room all was tranquil enough, for Susan having disappeared with the others, there were soon only her father and herself remaining; and he, taking out a newspaper, the accustomary loan of a neighbour, applied himself to studying it, without seeming to recollect her existence.", "방 안은 웬만큼 고요했으니, 수전이 다른 이들과 함께 사라져, 이내 아버지와 그녀만 남았기 때문이다. 그리고 그는, 이웃에게서 늘 빌려 오는 신문을 꺼내, 그녀의 존재는 떠올리는 기색도 없이 그것을 읽는 데 몰두했다."], ["The solitary candle was held between himself and the paper, without any reference to her possible convenience; but she had nothing to do, and was glad to have the light screened from her aching head, as she sat in bewildered, broken, sorrowful contemplation.", "그 외로운 초 하나가 그와 신문 사이에, 그녀의 있을 법한 편의는 조금도 헤아리지 않은 채 놓여 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 할 일이 없었고, 얼떨떨하고 부서지고 서글픈 상념에 잠겨 앉은 채, 지끈거리는 머리에서 그 불빛이 가려진 것이 오히려 다행스러웠다."]], [["She was at home.", "그녀는 집에 있었다."], ["But, alas!", "그러나, 아아!"], ["it was not such a home, she had not such a welcome, as—she checked herself; she was unreasonable.", "그것은 그런 집이 아니었고, 그녀는 그런 환대를 받은 게 아니었다—그녀는 저를 다잡았다. 저가 무리한 것이다."], ["What right had she to be of importance to her family?", "저가 제 식구에게 중한 사람일 무슨 권리가 있단 말인가?"], ["She could have none, so long lost sight of!", "그토록 오래 잊혀 있었으니, 아무 권리도 없을밖에!"], ["William’s concerns must be dearest, they always had been, and he had every right.", "윌리엄의 일이 가장 소중한 게 마땅했다, 늘 그래 왔고, 그에게는 온갖 권리가 있었다."], ["Yet to have so little said or asked about herself, to have scarcely an inquiry made after Mansfield!", "그러나 저 자신에 대해서는 그토록 묻고 말하는 것이 적다니, 맨스필드에 대해서는 물음 하나 좀처럼 없다니!"], ["It did pain her to have Mansfield forgotten; the friends who had done so much—the dear, dear friends!", "맨스필드가 잊히는 것이 그녀는 정말 아팠다. 그토록 많은 것을 해 준 그 벗들이—사랑하는, 사랑하는 벗들이!"], ["But here, one subject swallowed up all the rest.", "그러나 여기서는 한 가지 화제가 나머지를 죄다 삼켜 버렸다."], ["Perhaps it must be so.", "어쩌면 그럴밖에 없으리라."], ["The destination of the Thrush must be now preeminently interesting.", "스러시호가 어디로 갈지가 이제 무엇보다 흥미로울밖에 없었다."], ["A day or two might shew the difference.", "하루 이틀이면 그 다름이 드러날지도 몰랐다."], ["She only was to blame.", "저 혼자 탓할 일이었다."], ["Yet she thought it would not have been so at Mansfield.", "그러나 그녀는 맨스필드에서라면 이렇지 않았으리라 여겼다."], ["No, in her uncle’s house there would have been a consideration of times and seasons, a regulation of subject, a propriety, an attention towards everybody which there was not here.", "아니, 삼촌 댁에서라면 때와 철에 대한 헤아림이, 화제의 다스림이, 온당함이, 여기엔 없는 뭇사람에 대한 마음씀이 있었으리라."]], [["The only interruption which thoughts like these received for nearly half an hour was from a sudden burst of her father’s, not at all calculated to compose them.", "이런 생각들이 거의 반 시간 동안 받은 유일한 끼어듦은, 그것들을 가라앉히기엔 도무지 알맞잖은, 아버지의 문득 터진 외침에서였다."], ["At a more than ordinary pitch of thumping and hallooing in the passage, he exclaimed, “Devil take those young dogs!", "통로에서 여느 때보다 더한 쿵쾅거림과 고함이 일자, 그가 소리친 것이다. “저 강아지 같은 것들을 악마가 잡아가라지!"], ["How they are singing out!", "어찌 저리 짖어 대는지!"], ["Ay, Sam’s voice louder than all the rest!", "아무렴, 샘 목소리가 나머지보다 다 크구나!"], ["That boy is fit for a boatswain.", "저 녀석은 갑판장 감이야."], ["Holla, you there!", "이봐, 거기!"], ["Sam, stop your confounded pipe, or I shall be after you.”", "샘, 그 망할 놈의 피리 소릴 멈춰라, 안 그러면 내가 쫓아간다.”"]], [["This threat was so palpably disregarded, that though within five minutes afterwards the three boys all burst into the room together and sat down, Fanny could not consider it as a proof of anything more than their being for the time thoroughly fagged, which their hot faces and panting breaths seemed to prove, especially as they were still kicking each other’s shins, and hallooing out at sudden starts immediately under their father’s eye.", "이 으름장이 어찌나 뻔히 무시되었던지, 오 분도 안 되어 세 아이가 다 함께 방으로 왈칵 들어와 앉기는 했으나, 패니는 그것을 그들이 한동안 아주 지쳤다는 것 이상의 무슨 증거로도 여길 수 없었다. 그들의 달아오른 얼굴과 헐떡이는 숨이 그것을 증명하는 듯했으니, 더구나 그들은 여전히 아버지 눈앞에서 서로의 정강이를 걷어차고 문득문득 고함을 질러 댔던 것이다."]], [["The next opening of the door brought something more welcome: it was for the tea-things, which she had begun almost to despair of seeing that evening. Susan and an attendant girl, whose inferior appearance informed Fanny, to her great surprise, that she had previously seen the upper servant, brought in everything necessary for the meal; Susan looking, as she put the kettle on the fire and glanced at her sister, as if divided between the agreeable triumph of shewing her activity and usefulness, and the dread of being thought to demean herself by such an office. “She had been into the kitchen,” she said, “to hurry Sally and help make the toast, and spread the bread and butter, or she did not know when they should have got tea, and she was sure her sister must want something after her journey.”", "다음번 문이 열릴 땐 좀 더 반가운 것이 들어왔다. 찻그릇이었으니, 그녀는 그날 저녁 그것을 볼 것을 거의 단념하기 시작한 참이었다. 수전과, 그 못한 행색으로 미루어 패니가 크게 놀라며 앞서 본 것이 위층 하녀였음을 알게 해 준 시중드는 계집아이가, 그 끼니에 필요한 온갖 것을 들여왔다. 수전은 주전자를 불에 올리고 누이를 슬쩍 보며, 제 부지런함과 쓸모를 보이는 흐뭇한 우쭐함과, 그런 일로 저를 낮춘다 여겨질까 하는 두려움 사이에서 갈리는 듯한 낯빛이었다. “부엌에 다녀왔어요,” 그녀가 말했다. “샐리를 재촉하고 토스트 만드는 걸 거들고 버터빵을 발라 놓으려고요. 안 그럼 언제 차가 될지 몰라서요. 그리고 언니가 여행 뒤에 뭐라도 들고 싶을 게 분명하니까요.”"]], [["Fanny was very thankful.", "패니는 무척 고마웠다."], ["She could not but own that she should be very glad of a little tea, and Susan immediately set about making it, as if pleased to have the employment all to herself; and with only a little unnecessary bustle, and some few injudicious attempts at keeping her brothers in better order than she could, acquitted herself very well.", "그녀는 차를 좀 마시면 무척 반갑겠다는 것을 인정할밖에 없었고, 수전은 마치 그 일거리를 온통 저 혼자 맡은 것이 기쁜 양 대번에 차 끓이는 데 나섰다. 그리고 얼마간의 쓸데없는 부산과, 제가 할 수 있는 것보다 오라비들을 더 잘 잡도리하려는 몇 번의 서투른 시도만 빼면, 자못 잘 해냈다."], ["Fanny’s spirit was as much refreshed as her body; her head and heart were soon the better for such well-timed kindness.", "패니의 기운은 몸만큼이나 되살아났다. 그녀의 머리와 마음은 그런 때맞은 친절 덕에 이내 나아졌다."], ["Susan had an open, sensible countenance; she was like William, and Fanny hoped to find her like him in disposition and goodwill towards herself.", "수전은 툭 트인, 지각 있는 낯빛이었다. 그녀는 윌리엄을 닮았고, 패니는 성미와 저에 대한 호의에서도 그녀가 그를 닮았기를 바랐다."]], [["In this more placid state of things William reentered, followed not far behind by his mother and Betsey.", "이렇게 좀 더 잔잔해진 형편에 윌리엄이 다시 들어왔고, 그 뒤 멀지 않게 어머니와 벳시가 뒤따랐다."], ["He, complete in his lieutenant’s uniform, looking and moving all the taller, firmer, and more graceful for it, and with the happiest smile over his face, walked up directly to Fanny, who, rising from her seat, looked at him for a moment in speechless admiration, and then threw her arms round his neck to sob out her various emotions of pain and pleasure.", "그는 중위 제복을 온전히 갖춰 입어, 그 덕에 더 훤칠하고 더 다부지고 더 우아해 보이고 움직이며, 더없이 행복한 미소를 낯에 띤 채 곧장 패니에게로 걸어왔다. 그녀는 자리에서 일어나 한순간 말없는 감탄으로 그를 바라보더니, 그의 목에 팔을 두르고 아픔과 기쁨의 갖가지 심경을 흐느껴 쏟아 냈다."]], [["Anxious not to appear unhappy, she soon recovered herself; and wiping away her tears, was able to notice and admire all the striking parts of his dress; listening with reviving spirits to his cheerful hopes of being on shore some part of every day before they sailed, and even of getting her to Spithead to see the sloop.", "불행해 보이지 않으려 애쓰며, 그녀는 이내 저를 추슬렀다. 그리고 눈물을 닦고서, 그의 차림에서 눈에 띄는 온갖 대목을 눈여겨보고 감탄할 수 있었다. 출항 전 날마다 얼마간 뭍에 있으리라는, 심지어 그녀를 스핏헤드로 데려가 그 슬루프함을 보여 주리라는 그의 밝은 바람을, 되살아나는 기운으로 들으면서."]], [["The next bustle brought in Mr.", "다음 소동은 스러시호의 군의관 캠벨 씨를 들여보냈다. 자못 반듯한 젊은이로, 제 벗을 부르러 온 것이었다."], ["Campbell, the surgeon of the Thrush, a very well-behaved young man, who came to call for his friend, and for whom there was with some contrivance found a chair, and with some hasty washing of the young tea-maker’s, a cup and saucer; and after another quarter of an hour of earnest talk between the gentlemen, noise rising upon noise, and bustle upon bustle, men and boys at last all in motion together, the moment came for setting off; everything was ready, William took leave, and all of them were gone; for the three boys, in spite of their mother’s entreaty, determined to see their brother and Mr.", "그를 위해 이리저리 궁리해 의자 하나가 마련되었고, 젊은 차 끓이는 이가 서둘러 씻은 잔과 받침이 마련되었다. 그리고 신사들 사이에 또 한 십오 분의 열띤 얘기가 오간 뒤—소란 위에 소란이, 부산 위에 부산이 일고, 사내들과 아이들이 마침내 다 함께 움직이는 가운데—떠날 순간이 왔다."], ["Campbell to the sally-port; and Mr.", "온갖 것이 채비되었고, 윌리엄이 작별을 고했으며, 그들 모두가 떠났다."], ["Price walked off at the same time to carry back his neighbour’s newspaper.", "세 아이가 어머니의 간청에도 아랑곳없이 오라비와 캠벨 씨를 성문까지 배웅하기로 굳게 마음먹었고, 프라이스 씨도 그때 함께 걸어 나가 이웃의 신문을 돌려주었던 것이다."]], [["Something like tranquillity might now be hoped for; and accordingly, when Rebecca had been prevailed on to carry away the tea-things, and Mrs.", "이제 고요 비슷한 것을 바랄 만도 했다."], ["Price had walked about the room some time looking for a shirt-sleeve, which Betsey at last hunted out from a drawer in the kitchen, the small party of females were pretty well composed, and the mother having lamented again over the impossibility of getting Sam ready in time, was at leisure to think of her eldest daughter and the friends she had come from.", "그리하여 리베카가 겨우 설득되어 찻그릇을 치우고, 프라이스 부인이 한동안 방을 이리저리 거닐며 셔츠 소매 한 짝을 찾았으나 벳시가 마침내 부엌 서랍에서 찾아내니, 그 자그마한 여자들의 무리는 웬만큼 가라앉았다. 그리고 어머니는 샘을 제때 채비시키기가 도무지 어렵다는 것을 다시 한번 한탄하고 나서야, 맏딸과 그녀가 떠나온 벗들을 생각할 여유가 생겼다."]], [["A few inquiries began: but one of the earliest—“How did sister Bertram manage about her servants?”", "몇 가지 물음이 시작되었다. 그러나 가장 이른 것 가운데 하나—“버트럼 언니는 하인들을 어떻게 건사하니?”"], ["“Was she as much plagued as herself to get tolerable servants?”—soon led her mind away from Northamptonshire, and fixed it on her own domestic grievances, and the shocking character of all the Portsmouth servants, of whom she believed her own two were the very worst, engrossed her completely.", "“쓸 만한 하인 구하기가 나만큼이나 애먹니?”—가 이내 그 마음을 노샘프턴셔에서 데려가, 제 살림의 시름에, 포츠머스 하인이란 하인은 죄다 고약하다는 데 붙박아 놓았다. 그리고 그중에서도 제 두 하인이 딱 최악이려니 여기는 그 마음이 그녀를 온통 사로잡았다."], ["The Bertrams were all forgotten in detailing the faults of Rebecca, against whom Susan had also much to depose, and little Betsey a great deal more, and who did seem so thoroughly without a single recommendation, that Fanny could not help modestly presuming that her mother meant to part with her when her year was up.", "리베카의 흠을 낱낱이 늘어놓는 데 버트럼가는 죄다 잊혔으니, 리베카에 대해서는 수전도 고할 것이 많고 어린 벳시는 훨씬 더 많았으며, 리베카는 정말이지 그토록 온전히 천거할 점 하나 없어 보이던지라, 패니는 어머니가 그 한 해가 차면 그녀를 내보낼 셈이려니 조심스레 넘겨짚지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Her year!” cried Mrs.", "“한 해라니!”"], ["Price; “I am sure I hope I shall be rid of her before she has staid a year, for that will not be up till November.", "프라이스 부인이 외쳤다. “정말이지 나는 그게 한 해가 차기 전에 그 애를 떼어 내면 좋겠다, 그 한 해는 십일월이나 돼야 차거든."], ["Servants are come to such a pass, my dear, in Portsmouth, that it is quite a miracle if one keeps them more than half a year.", "얘야, 포츠머스에선 하인이란 게 이 지경에 이르러서, 반년 넘게 데리고 있으면 그게 아주 기적이란다."], ["I have no hope of ever being settled; and if I was to part with Rebecca, I should only get something worse.", "나는 도무지 자리 잡을 가망이 없어. 그리고 리베카를 내보낸들 더 못한 것이나 얻을 뿐이지."], ["And yet I do not think I am a very difficult mistress to please; and I am sure the place is easy enough, for there is always a girl under her, and I often do half the work myself.”", "그래도 나는 내가 그리 비위 맞추기 어려운 안주인이라곤 여기지 않아. 그리고 이 자리야 분명 넉넉히 수월해, 늘 그 애 밑에 계집아이가 하나 있고, 나도 곧잘 일의 절반을 손수 하니까.”"]], [["Fanny was silent; but not from being convinced that there might not be a remedy found for some of these evils.", "패니는 잠잠했다. 그러나 이 화들 가운데 얼마간에는 고칠 길이 있을 수도 있음을 믿지 못해서가 아니었다."], ["As she now sat looking at Betsey, she could not but think particularly of another sister, a very pretty little girl, whom she had left there not much younger when she went into Northamptonshire, who had died a few years afterwards.", "이제 벳시를 바라보고 앉은 그녀는, 또 다른 누이를, 저가 노샘프턴셔로 갈 때 그리 어리지 않은 나이로 거기 두고 온, 자못 예쁜 어린 계집아이를, 몇 해 뒤에 죽은 그 아이를 각별히 생각하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["There had been something remarkably amiable about her.", "그 아이에게는 유난히 사랑스러운 데가 있었다."], ["Fanny in those early days had preferred her to Susan; and when the news of her death had at last reached Mansfield, had for a short time been quite afflicted.", "그 어릴 적 패니는 그 아이를 수전보다 더 좋아했다. 그리고 그 아이의 죽음 소식이 마침내 맨스필드에 이르렀을 때, 한동안 자못 애통해했었다."], ["The sight of Betsey brought the image of little Mary back again, but she would not have pained her mother by alluding to her for the world.", "벳시를 보니 어린 메리의 모습이 다시 떠올랐으나, 무엇을 준대도 그녀는 그 아이를 넌지시 입에 올려 어머니를 아프게 하지 않으려 했다."], ["While considering her with these ideas, Betsey, at a small distance, was holding out something to catch her eyes, meaning to screen it at the same time from Susan’s.", "이런 생각으로 그 아이를 헤아리는 동안, 벳시가 조금 떨어진 데서, 수전에게서는 가리려 하면서 무언가를 그녀의 눈에 띄게 내밀고 있었다."]], [["“What have you got there, my love?”", "“거기 뭘 들고 있니, 얘야?”"], ["said Fanny; “come and shew it to me.”", "패니가 말했다. “이리 와서 나한테 보여 주렴.”"]], [["It was a silver knife. Up jumped Susan, claiming it as her own, and trying to get it away; but the child ran to her mother’s protection, and Susan could only reproach, which she did very warmly, and evidently hoping to interest Fanny on her side. “It was very hard that she was not to have her own knife; it was her own knife; little sister Mary had left it to her upon her deathbed, and she ought to have had it to keep herself long ago. But mama kept it from her, and was always letting Betsey get hold of it; and the end of it would be that Betsey would spoil it, and get it for her own, though mama had promised her that Betsey should not have it in her own hands.”", "그것은 은칼이었다. 수전이 벌떡 일어나 제 것이라 우기며 빼앗으려 했으나, 아이는 어머니의 보호막으로 달아났고, 수전은 그저 나무랄밖에 없었는데, 자못 발끈하며 그리했다. 그러면서 분명 패니가 제 편이 되어 주기를 바라는 눈치였다. “제 칼을 못 갖다니 너무해요. 그건 제 칼이에요. 어린 메리 언니가 죽으면서 저한테 남긴 건데, 진작 제가 간직해야 마땅했다고요. 그런데 엄마가 그걸 저한테서 두고, 늘 벳시가 손대게 놔둬요. 그러다 끝내는 벳시가 그걸 망가뜨리고 제 것으로 삼을 거예요. 엄마가 벳시 손엔 안 쥐여 주겠다고 약속해 놓고선.”"]], [["Fanny was quite shocked.", "패니는 자못 충격받았다."], ["Every feeling of duty, honour, and tenderness was wounded by her sister’s speech and her mother’s reply.", "누이의 말과 어머니의 대답에, 도리와 명예와 애틋함의 온갖 심경이 상했다."]], [["“Now, Susan,” cried Mrs.", "“아니, 수전,”"], ["Price, in a complaining voice, “now, how can you be so cross?", "프라이스 부인이 투정 부리는 목소리로 외쳤다. “아니, 어쩜 그리 심술을 부리니?"], ["You are always quarrelling about that knife.", "넌 늘 그 칼을 두고 다투는구나."], ["I wish you would not be so quarrelsome.", "제발 그리 다투려 들지 마라."], ["Poor little Betsey; how cross Susan is to you!", "딱한 어린 벳시, 수전이 너한테 어찌 저리 심술을 부리는지!"], ["But you should not have taken it out, my dear, when I sent you to the drawer.", "하지만 너도 그걸 꺼내지 말았어야지, 얘야, 내가 서랍에 심부름을 보냈을 때 말이야."], ["You know I told you not to touch it, because Susan is so cross about it.", "손대지 말라 일렀잖니, 수전이 그걸 두고 하도 심술을 부리니까."], ["I must hide it another time, Betsey.", "다음번엔 그걸 숨겨 둬야겠다, 벳시."], ["Poor Mary little thought it would be such a bone of contention when she gave it me to keep, only two hours before she died.", "딱한 메리는 그걸 나한테 간직하라 주면서, 죽기 겨우 두 시간 전에 주면서, 그게 이런 다툼거리가 될 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐을 게다."], ["Poor little soul!", "딱한 것!"], ["she could but just speak to be heard, and she said so prettily, ‘Let sister Susan have my knife, mama, when I am dead and buried.’ Poor little dear!", "그 애는 겨우 들릴 만큼밖에 말을 못 하면서도, 어찌나 곱게 말했는지, ‘엄마, 내가 죽어 묻히면 수전 언니 칼 주세요’ 했단다."], ["she was so fond of it, Fanny, that she would have it lay by her in bed, all through her illness.", "딱하고 사랑스러운 것! 그 애가 그걸 어찌나 아꼈는지, 패니, 앓는 내내 잠자리 곁에 두려 했단다."], ["It was the gift of her good godmother, old Mrs. Admiral Maxwell, only six weeks before she was taken for death.", "그건 그 애의 착한 대모, 늙은 맥스웰 제독 부인이 준 선물이야, 그 애가 죽음에 붙들리기 겨우 여섯 주 전에 말이다."], ["Poor little sweet creature!", "딱하고 고운 어린것!"], ["Well, she was taken away from evil to come.", "뭐, 그 애는 앞으로 올 화를 피해 데려가진 게지."], ["My own Betsey”", "우리 벳시야”"], ["(fondling her), “you have not the luck of such a good godmother.", "(아이를 어루만지며), “너는 그런 좋은 대모의 복이 없구나."], ["Aunt Norris lives too far off to think of such little people as you.”", "노리스 이모야 너 같은 조그만 것들을 생각하기엔 너무 멀리 사시니.”"]], [["Fanny had indeed nothing to convey from aunt Norris, but a message to say she hoped that her god-daughter was a good girl, and learnt her book.", "패니가 정말이지 노리스 이모에게서 전할 것이라곤, 제 대녀가 착한 아이여서 책을 잘 배우기를 바란다는 안부의 말뿐이었다."], ["There had been at one moment a slight murmur in the drawing-room at Mansfield Park about sending her a prayer-book; but no second sound had been heard of such a purpose. Mrs.", "한순간 맨스필드 파크 응접실에서 그 애에게 기도서를 보내자는 얘기가 잠깐 나온 적은 있었으나, 그런 뜻이 두 번 다시 들리진 않았다."], ["Norris, however, had gone home and taken down two old prayer-books of her husband with that idea; but, upon examination, the ardour of generosity went off.", "그래도 노리스 부인은 그 생각으로 집에 가서 남편의 낡은 기도서 두 권을 내렸다. 그러나 살펴본 끝에 그 너그러움의 열의가 식었다."], ["One was found to have too small a print for a child’s eyes, and the other to be too cumbersome for her to carry about.", "하나는 아이 눈엔 활자가 너무 작았고, 다른 하나는 아이가 들고 다니기엔 너무 거추장스러웠던 것이다."]], [["Fanny, fatigued and fatigued again, was thankful to accept the first invitation of going to bed; and before Betsey had finished her cry at being allowed to sit up only one hour extraordinary in honour of sister, she was off, leaving all below in confusion and noise again; the boys begging for toasted cheese, her father calling out for his rum and water, and Rebecca never where she ought to be.", "패니는 지치고 또 지친지라, 잠자리에 들라는 첫 청을 고맙게 받아들였다. 그리고 언니를 기리는 뜻에서 딱 한 시간 더 앉아 있게 허락받은 것을 두고 벳시가 우는 것이 채 끝나기도 전에, 그녀는 자리를 떴다. 아래층을 다시 어수선함과 소란에 남겨 둔 채로였으니, 아이들은 구운 치즈를 달라 조르고, 아버지는 럼주에 물 탄 것을 내오라 소리치고, 리베카는 있어야 할 자리에 통 없었다."]], [["There was nothing to raise her spirits in the confined and scantily furnished chamber that she was to share with Susan.", "수전과 함께 쓰기로 된 그 옹색하고 세간 변변찮은 방에는 그녀의 기운을 돋울 것이 아무것도 없었다."], ["The smallness of the rooms above and below, indeed, and the narrowness of the passage and staircase, struck her beyond her imagination.", "정말이지 위아래 방들의 작음과, 통로와 계단의 좁음이 그녀의 상상을 넘어 사무쳤다."], ["She soon learned to think with respect of her own little attic at Mansfield Park, in that house reckoned too small for anybody’s comfort.", "그녀는 이내, 맨스필드 파크의 제 자그마한 다락방을—그 집에선 누구의 편안함에도 너무 작다 여겨지던 그 방을 존경심으로 떠올리게 되었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIX", "# 제39장"]], [["Could Sir Thomas have seen all his niece’s feelings, when she wrote her first letter to her aunt, he would not have despaired; for though a good night’s rest, a pleasant morning, the hope of soon seeing William again, and the comparatively quiet state of the house, from Tom and Charles being gone to school, Sam on some project of his own, and her father on his usual lounges, enabled her to express herself cheerfully on the subject of home, there were still, to her own perfect consciousness, many drawbacks suppressed.", "토머스 경이, 조카딸이 이모에게 첫 편지를 쓸 때의 그 온갖 심경을 다 볼 수 있었더라면, 그는 절망하지 않았으리라. 밤새 잘 쉰 것과, 유쾌한 아침과, 이내 다시 윌리엄을 볼 바람과, 톰과 찰스가 학교에 가고 샘이 제 무슨 일로 나가고 아버지가 여느 어슬렁거림에 나선 덕에 집이 견주어 조용해진 것이, 그녀로 하여금 집에 대해 밝게 저를 드러낼 수 있게 했으되, 그녀 자신은 훤히 아는 대로 여전히 눌러 둔 언짢은 대목이 많았던 것이다."], ["Could he have seen only half that she felt before the end of a week, he would have thought Mr. Crawford sure of her, and been delighted with his own sagacity.", "그가 한 주가 채 가기 전에 그녀가 느낀 것의 절반만이라도 볼 수 있었더라면, 그는 크로퍼드 씨가 그녀를 확실히 얻으리라 여기고, 제 슬기로움에 흐뭇해했으리라."]], [["Before the week ended, it was all disappointment.", "한 주가 가기도 전에 그것은 죄다 실망이었다."], ["In the first place, William was gone.", "우선, 윌리엄이 떠났다."], ["The Thrush had had her orders, the wind had changed, and he was sailed within four days from their reaching Portsmouth; and during those days she had seen him only twice, in a short and hurried way, when he had come ashore on duty.", "스러시호에 출항 명령이 떨어지고 바람이 바뀌어, 그는 그들이 포츠머스에 이른 지 나흘 만에 배를 타고 떠났다. 그리고 그 며칠 동안 그녀는 그가 근무차 뭍에 왔을 때 짧고 서두르는 식으로 딱 두 번밖에 그를 못 봤다."], ["There had been no free conversation, no walk on the ramparts, no visit to the dockyard, no acquaintance with the Thrush, nothing of all that they had planned and depended on.", "스스럼없는 얘기도, 성벽 위 산책도, 조선소 구경도, 스러시호와 낯익어지는 것도, 그들이 짜고 기대던 그 온갖 것이 하나도 없었다."], ["Everything in that quarter failed her, except William’s affection.", "윌리엄의 정 말고는 그 방면의 온갖 것이 그녀를 저버렸다."], ["His last thought on leaving home was for her.", "집을 떠날 제 그의 마지막 생각은 그녀를 위한 것이었다."], ["He stepped back again to the door to say, “Take care of Fanny, mother.", "그는 문간으로 다시 되걸어 와 말했다. “패니를 잘 돌봐 주세요, 어머니."], ["She is tender, and not used to rough it like the rest of us.", "그 애는 여려서, 우리 나머지처럼 거칠게 부대끼는 데 익지 않았어요."], ["I charge you, take care of Fanny.”", "제가 당부드려요, 패니를 잘 돌봐 주세요.”"]], [["William was gone: and the home he had left her in was, Fanny could not conceal it from herself, in almost every respect the very reverse of what she could have wished.", "윌리엄은 떠났다. 그리고 그가 그녀를 남겨 두고 간 그 집은, 패니가 저 자신에게 감출 수 없었듯, 거의 어느 모로 보나 저가 바랐을 것과 정반대였다."], ["It was the abode of noise, disorder, and impropriety.", "그것은 소란과 무질서와 온당찮음의 거처였다."], ["Nobody was in their right place, nothing was done as it ought to be.", "아무도 제자리에 있지 않았고, 아무것도 마땅한 대로 되지 않았다."], ["She could not respect her parents as she had hoped.", "그녀는 바라던 만큼 제 부모를 존경할 수가 없었다."], ["On her father, her confidence had not been sanguine, but he was more negligent of his family, his habits were worse, and his manners coarser, than she had been prepared for.", "아버지에 대해서는 그녀의 믿음이 그리 크지 않았으나, 그는 그녀가 각오한 것보다 제 식구에게 더 무심하고, 버릇이 더 나쁘고, 태도가 더 거칠었다."], ["He did not want abilities but he had no curiosity, and no information beyond his profession; he read only the newspaper and the navy-list; he talked only of the dockyard, the harbour, Spithead, and the Motherbank; he swore and he drank, he was dirty and gross.", "그에게 재간이 없는 것은 아니었으나, 호기심도, 제 직업 너머의 앎도 없었다. 그는 신문과 해군 명부만 읽었고, 조선소와 항구와 스핏헤드와 마더뱅크 얘기만 했다."], ["She had never been able to recall anything approaching to tenderness in his former treatment of herself.", "그는 욕을 했고 술을 마셨으며, 지저분하고 거칠었다. 그녀는 예전에 저를 대하던 그의 태도에서 애틋함 비슷한 것이라곤 좀처럼 떠올릴 수 없었다."], ["There had remained only a general impression of roughness and loudness; and now he scarcely ever noticed her, but to make her the object of a coarse joke.", "남은 것이라곤 거칢과 시끄러움의 두루뭉술한 인상뿐이었다. 그리고 이제 그는 좀처럼 그녀를 아는 체하지 않았으니, 거친 농의 과녁으로 삼을 때 말고는."]], [["Her disappointment in her mother was greater: there she had hoped much, and found almost nothing.", "어머니에 대한 실망은 더 컸다. 거기에 그녀는 많이 바랐으나, 거의 아무것도 못 찾았다."], ["Every flattering scheme of being of consequence to her soon fell to the ground. Mrs.", "어머니에게 중한 사람이 되려던 온갖 우쭐한 궁리가 이내 무너졌다."], ["Price was not unkind; but, instead of gaining on her affection and confidence, and becoming more and more dear, her daughter never met with greater kindness from her than on the first day of her arrival.", "프라이스 부인은 야박하진 않았다. 그러나 딸은, 그 애정과 믿음을 얻어 점점 더 소중한 사람이 되기는커녕, 도착한 첫날보다 더 큰 친절을 그녀에게서 받아 본 적이 없었다."], ["The instinct of nature was soon satisfied, and Mrs. Price’s attachment had no other source.", "천성의 본능은 이내 채워졌고, 프라이스 부인의 정에는 다른 샘이 없었다."], ["Her heart and her time were already quite full; she had neither leisure nor affection to bestow on Fanny.", "그녀의 마음도 시간도 이미 아주 가득 차 있었다. 그녀에게는 패니에게 베풀 여유도 정도 없었다."], ["Her daughters never had been much to her.", "딸들은 그녀에게 여태 큰 존재가 아니었다."], ["She was fond of her sons, especially of William, but Betsey was the first of her girls whom she had ever much regarded.", "그녀는 아들들을, 특히 윌리엄을 아꼈으나, 벳시야말로 그녀가 여태 크게 마음 쓴 첫 딸이었다."], ["To her she was most injudiciously indulgent.", "그 아이에게 그녀는 더없이 분별없이 무르게 굴었다."], ["William was her pride; Betsey her darling; and John, Richard, Sam, Tom, and Charles occupied all the rest of her maternal solicitude, alternately her worries and her comforts.", "윌리엄은 그녀의 자랑이요, 벳시는 그녀의 귀염둥이였다. 그리고 존과 리처드와 샘과 톰과 찰스가 그 나머지 온갖 어미로서의 마음씀을 차지해, 번갈아 그녀의 시름이자 위안이 되었다."], ["These shared her heart: her time was given chiefly to her house and her servants.", "이들이 그녀의 마음을 나눠 가졌다. 그녀의 시간은 주로 살림과 하인들에게 주어졌다."], ["Her days were spent in a kind of slow bustle; all was busy without getting on, always behindhand and lamenting it, without altering her ways; wishing to be an economist, without contrivance or regularity; dissatisfied with her servants, without skill to make them better, and whether helping, or reprimanding, or indulging them, without any power of engaging their respect.", "그녀의 나날은 일종의 굼뜬 부산함 속에 흘렀다. 온통 바쁘되 나아가는 것은 없고, 늘 뒤처지고 그것을 한탄하되 제 방식은 안 바꾸고, 알뜰해지고 싶어 하되 궁리도 규모도 없고, 하인들에게 못마땅해하되 그들을 낫게 할 재간은 없고, 거들든 나무라든 무르게 굴든 그들의 존경을 얻어 낼 힘이라곤 없었다."]], [["Of her two sisters, Mrs. Price very much more resembled Lady Bertram than Mrs.", "두 언니 가운데 프라이스 부인은 노리스 부인보다 버트럼 부인을 훨씬 더 닮았다."], ["Norris. She was a manager by necessity, without any of Mrs.", "그녀는 어쩔 수 없이 살림꾼이었을 뿐, 노리스 부인의 그런 성향도, 그런 부지런함도 조금도 없었다."], ["Norris’s inclination for it, or any of her activity.", "그녀의 성미는 천성으로 느긋하고 게을렀으니, 버트럼 부인처럼 말이다."], ["Her disposition was naturally easy and indolent, like Lady Bertram’s; and a situation of similar affluence and do-nothingness would have been much more suited to her capacity than the exertions and self-denials of the one which her imprudent marriage had placed her in.", "그리고 비슷하게 넉넉하고 아무것도 안 해도 되는 처지가, 그녀의 경솔한 혼인이 그녀를 밀어 넣은 그 처지의 애씀과 자기 부정보다 그녀의 깜냥에 훨씬 더 잘 맞았으리라."], ["She might have made just as good a woman of consequence as Lady Bertram, but Mrs. Norris would have been a more respectable mother of nine children on a small income.", "그녀도 버트럼 부인만큼이나 훌륭한 지체 높은 여자가 되었을 수 있고, 노리스 부인이라면 적은 수입으로 아홉 아이의 더 존경할 만한 어머니가 되었으리라."]], [["Much of all this Fanny could not but be sensible of.", "이 모두의 많은 것을 패니는 못 느낄 수 없었다."], ["She might scruple to make use of the words, but she must and did feel that her mother was a partial, ill-judging parent, a dawdle, a slattern, who neither taught nor restrained her children, whose house was the scene of mismanagement and discomfort from beginning to end, and who had no talent, no conversation, no affection towards herself; no curiosity to know her better, no desire of her friendship, and no inclination for her company that could lessen her sense of such feelings.", "그녀는 그 낱말을 쓰기를 거리꼈을지 모르나, 어머니가 치우치고 판단 그른 어버이임을, 자식을 가르치지도 잡도리하지도 않는 굼뜨고 지저분한 사람임을, 그 집이 처음부터 끝까지 잘못 건사됨과 불편함의 무대임을, 그리고 저를 향해 재간도 말솜씨도 정도 없는 사람임을—저를 더 알고픈 호기심도, 저의 우정을 바라는 마음도, 그런 심경을 덜어 줄 만한 벗함에 대한 기울음도 없는 사람임을 느낄밖에 없었고 또 느꼈다."]], [["Fanny was very anxious to be useful, and not to appear above her home, or in any way disqualified or disinclined, by her foreign education, from contributing her help to its comforts, and therefore set about working for Sam immediately; and by working early and late, with perseverance and great despatch, did so much that the boy was shipped off at last, with more than half his linen ready.", "패니는 몹시도 쓸모가 되고 싶었고, 제 집을 얕보는 티를 내거나 이국의 교육 탓에 그 편안함에 도움을 보태기에 어울리지 않거나 내키지 않는 티를 어떤 식으로든 안 내고 싶었다. 그리하여 그녀는 대번에 샘을 위해 바느질에 나섰고, 이르나 늦으나 물러섬 없이 바지런히 바느질해 하도 많이 해낸지라, 그 아이는 끝내 제 속옷 절반 넘게 채비된 채로 배에 실려 갔다."], ["She had great pleasure in feeling her usefulness, but could not conceive how they would have managed without her.", "그녀는 제 쓸모를 느끼는 것이 크게 기뻤으나, 저 없이 그들이 어찌 꾸려 갔을지 상상할 수 없었다."]], [["Sam, loud and overbearing as he was, she rather regretted when he went, for he was clever and intelligent, and glad to be employed in any errand in the town; and though spurning the remonstrances of Susan, given as they were, though very reasonable in themselves, with ill-timed and powerless warmth, was beginning to be influenced by Fanny’s services and gentle persuasions; and she found that the best of the three younger ones was gone in him: Tom and Charles being at least as many years as they were his juniors distant from that age of feeling and reason, which might suggest the expediency of making friends, and of endeavouring to be less disagreeable. Their sister soon despaired of making the smallest impression on them; they were quite untameable by any means of address which she had spirits or time to attempt. Every afternoon brought a return of their riotous games all over the house; and she very early learned to sigh at the approach of Saturday’s constant half-holiday.", "샘은 시끄럽고 제멋대로였으되, 떠날 때 그녀는 오히려 그를 아쉬워했다. 그가 영리하고 슬기롭고, 시내의 어떤 심부름이든 기꺼이 맡았기 때문이다. 그리고 그는, 그 자체로는 자못 이치에 맞으나 때 안 맞고 힘없는 열의로 건네지는 수전의 나무람은 뿌리치면서도, 패니의 시중과 상냥한 구슬림에는 영향받기 시작하던 참이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 세 어린 것 가운데 가장 나은 이가 그와 함께 가 버렸음을 알았다. 톰과 찰스는, 벗을 사귀고 덜 얄미워지려 애쓰는 것이 이롭겠다는 생각이 들 만한 그 감정과 이성의 나이에서, 적어도 그들이 그보다 어린 햇수만큼 멀었던 것이다. 누이는 이내 그들에게는 조금의 감화도 줄 수 없으리라 단념했다. 그들은 그녀가 시도할 기운이나 겨를이 있는 어떤 방식으로도 도무지 길들일 수 없었다. 오후마다 온 집 안에 그들의 난장 놀이가 되돌아왔다. 그리고 그녀는 아주 일찍부터 토요일의 그 어김없는 반공일이 다가오는 데 한숨짓기를 배웠다."]], [["Betsey, too, a spoiled child, trained up to think the alphabet her greatest enemy, left to be with the servants at her pleasure, and then encouraged to report any evil of them, she was almost as ready to despair of being able to love or assist; and of Susan’s temper she had many doubts.", "벳시 또한, 버릇없이 자라, 글자를 제 가장 큰 원수로 여기도록 길들여지고, 제 좋을 대로 하인들과 어울리게 내버려졌다가 그다음 그들의 무슨 나쁜 짓이든 일러바치라 부추김받은 그 아이라, 그녀는 사랑하거나 도와줄 수 있을까 하는 데서 거의 그만큼 단념할 지경이었다. 그리고 수전의 성미에 대해서는 미심쩍은 게 많았다."], ["Her continual disagreements with her mother, her rash squabbles with Tom and Charles, and petulance with Betsey, were at least so distressing to Fanny that, though admitting they were by no means without provocation, she feared the disposition that could push them to such length must be far from amiable, and from affording any repose to herself.", "어머니와의 끊임없는 다툼, 톰과 찰스와의 불끈하는 옥신각신, 벳시에 대한 심술이 적어도 패니에게는 어찌나 괴롭던지, 그것들이 결코 부아를 돋우는 일 없이 그런 것은 아님을 인정하면서도, 그녀는 그것들을 그토록 극단으로 밀어붙일 수 있는 그 성미가 상냥함과는, 그리고 저 자신에게 무슨 안식을 줌과는 거리가 멀지 않을까 두려웠다."]], [["Such was the home which was to put Mansfield out of her head, and teach her to think of her cousin Edmund with moderated feelings.", "이것이 바로, 맨스필드를 그녀 머리에서 몰아내고 사촌 에드먼드를 누그러진 심경으로 생각하도록 가르치기로 되어 있던 그 집이었다."], ["On the contrary, she could think of nothing but Mansfield, its beloved inmates, its happy ways.", "오히려 그녀는 맨스필드 말고는, 그 사랑하는 식구들 말고는, 그 행복한 방식들 말고는 아무것도 생각할 수 없었다."], ["Everything where she now was in full contrast to it.", "그녀가 지금 있는 데의 온갖 것이 그것과 온전한 대조를 이뤘다."], ["The elegance, propriety, regularity, harmony, and perhaps, above all, the peace and tranquillity of Mansfield, were brought to her remembrance every hour of the day, by the prevalence of everything opposite to them here.", "맨스필드의 그 우아함, 온당함, 정연함, 어우러짐, 그리고 어쩌면 무엇보다도 그 평온과 고요가, 여기서는 그와 정반대인 온갖 것이 판을 치는 탓에, 하루의 시각마다 그녀의 기억에 되불려 왔다."]], [["The living in incessant noise was, to a frame and temper delicate and nervous like Fanny’s, an evil which no superadded elegance or harmony could have entirely atoned for.", "끊임없는 소란 속에 사는 것은, 패니처럼 여리고 예민한 몸과 성미에는, 아무리 우아함이나 어우러짐을 더한들 온전히 갚아 줄 수 없는 화였다."], ["It was the greatest misery of all.", "그것이 무엇보다 큰 비참함이었다."], ["At Mansfield, no sounds of contention, no raised voice, no abrupt bursts, no tread of violence, was ever heard; all proceeded in a regular course of cheerful orderliness; everybody had their due importance; everybody’s feelings were consulted.", "맨스필드에서는 다툼의 소리도, 높아진 목소리도, 문득 터지는 소리도, 우악스러운 발소리도 결코 들리지 않았다. 온갖 것이 밝은 정연함의 규칙적인 흐름으로 나아갔다."], ["If tenderness could be ever supposed wanting, good sense and good breeding supplied its place; and as to the little irritations sometimes introduced by aunt Norris, they were short, they were trifling, they were as a drop of water to the ocean, compared with the ceaseless tumult of her present abode.", "다들 저마다의 마땅한 중함을 지녔고, 다들의 심경이 헤아려졌다. 애틋함이 모자란다 여겨질 만한 데가 언제고 있다면, 좋은 분별과 좋은 교양이 그 자리를 채웠다."], ["Here everybody was noisy, every voice was loud (excepting, perhaps, her mother’s, which resembled the soft monotony of Lady Bertram’s, only worn into fretfulness).", "그리고 노리스 이모가 이따금 일으키는 그 자잘한 짜증으로 말하면, 그것들은 짧았고, 사소했으며, 지금 거처의 그 그침 없는 소란에 견주면 바다에 물 한 방울이었다. 여기서는 다들 시끄러웠고, 목소리마다 컸다(어쩌면 어머니의 것만 빼고."], ["Whatever was wanted was hallooed for, and the servants hallooed out their excuses from the kitchen.", "그것은 버트럼 부인의 부드러운 단조로움을 닮았으되, 다만 짜증으로 닳아 있었다)."], ["The doors were in constant banging, the stairs were never at rest, nothing was done without a clatter, nobody sat still, and nobody could command attention when they spoke.", "무엇이 필요하든 고함쳐 불렀고, 하인들은 부엌에서 제 변명을 고함쳐 댔다. 문은 끊임없이 쾅쾅거렸고, 계단은 결코 가만있지 않았으며, 무엇 하나 달그락거림 없이 되지 않았고, 아무도 가만 앉아 있지 않았으며, 아무도 말할 때 남의 귀를 붙들 수 없었다."]], [["In a review of the two houses, as they appeared to her before the end of a week, Fanny was tempted to apply to them Dr. Johnson’s celebrated judgment as to matrimony and celibacy, and say, that though Mansfield Park might have some pains, Portsmouth could have no pleasures.", "한 주가 채 가기 전에 그녀에게 비친 대로 그 두 집을 견주어 보며, 패니는 혼인과 독신에 대한 존슨 박사의 그 이름난 판단을 거기에 갖다 대어, 맨스필드 파크에는 얼마간 고통이 있을지언정 포츠머스에는 아무 즐거움도 없다고 말하고픈 마음이 들었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XL", "# 제40장"]], [["Fanny was right enough in not expecting to hear from Miss Crawford now at the rapid rate in which their correspondence had begun; Mary’s next letter was after a decidedly longer interval than the last, but she was not right in supposing that such an interval would be felt a great relief to herself.", "이제 크로퍼드 양에게서, 그들 편지 왕래가 시작될 때의 그 빠른 속도로 소식이 오리라 기대하지 않은 것은 패니가 옳았다. 메리의 다음 편지는 지난번보다 확실히 더 긴 사이를 두고 왔다."], ["Here was another strange revolution of mind!", "그러나 그런 사이가 저에게 큰 안도로 느껴지리라 여긴 것은 옳지 않았다."], ["She was really glad to receive the letter when it did come.", "여기 또 한 번 마음의 야릇한 뒤집힘이 있었으니! 그녀는 그 편지가 정작 오니 정말로 반가웠던 것이다."], ["In her present exile from good society, and distance from everything that had been wont to interest her, a letter from one belonging to the set where her heart lived, written with affection, and some degree of elegance, was thoroughly acceptable.", "좋은 사교에서 이렇게 쫓겨나, 저를 흥미롭게 하곤 하던 온갖 것에서 이토록 멀리 떨어진 지금, 제 마음이 살고 있는 그 무리에 속한 이가 애정과 얼마간의 우아함을 담아 쓴 편지는 더없이 반가웠다. 더 일찍 못 쓴 것에 대한 변명으로 볼일이 많아진다는 그 흔한 핑계가 대어졌다."], ["The usual plea of increasing engagements was made in excuse for not having written to her earlier; “And now that I have begun,”", "“그리고 이제 시작하고 보니,” 그녀는 말을 이었다."], ["she continued, “my letter will not be worth your reading, for there will be no little offering of love at the end, no three or four lines passionnées from the most devoted H.", "“내 편지는 그쪽이 읽을 값어치가 없겠어요. 끝에 자그마한 사랑의 예물이, 세상에 둘도 없이 헌신적인 H."], ["C. in the world, for Henry is in Norfolk; business called him to Everingham ten days ago, or perhaps he only pretended the call, for the sake of being travelling at the same time that you were.", "C.에게서 온 열정 어린(passionnées) 서너 줄이 없을 테니까요."], ["But there he is, and, by the bye, his absence may sufficiently account for any remissness of his sister’s in writing, for there has been no ‘Well, Mary, when do you write to Fanny?", "헨리가 노퍽에 있거든요. 열흘 전에 볼일로 에버링엄에 불려 갔어요, 아니면 그저 볼일이 있는 척했는지도 모르죠, 그쪽이 여행할 그 무렵에 저도 여행하려고 말이에요."], ["Is not it time for you to write to Fanny?’ to spur me on.", "아무튼 그이는 거기 있고, 그건 그렇고, 그이가 없으니 그 누이의 편지 게으름도 넉넉히 설명이 되겠죠."], ["At last, after various attempts at meeting, I have seen your cousins, ‘dear Julia and dearest Mrs.", "나를 재촉할 ‘그래, 메리, 언제 패니한테 편지 쓸 거야? 이젠 패니한테 편지할 때 아니야?’ 하는 소리가 없었으니까요."], ["Rushworth’; they found me at home yesterday, and we were glad to see each other again.", "마침내, 몇 번의 만남 시도 끝에, 나는 그쪽 사촌들, ‘소중한 줄리아와 더없이 소중한 러시워스 부인’을 봤어요. 그들이 어제 집으로 나를 찾아왔고, 우리는 다시 만나 반가웠죠."], ["We seemed very glad to see each other, and I do really think we were a little.", "우리는 서로 만나 아주 반가운 듯했고, 나는 정말이지 우리가 얼마간은 반가웠다 여겨요."], ["We had a vast deal to say.", "우리는 나눌 얘기가 아주 많았어요."], ["Shall I tell you how Mrs. Rushworth looked when your name was mentioned?", "그쪽 이름이 나왔을 때 러시워스 부인이 어떤 낯빛이던지 얘기해 줄까요?"], ["I did not use to think her wanting in self-possession, but she had not quite enough for the demands of yesterday.", "나는 여태 그이가 침착함이 모자란 사람이라곤 여기지 않았는데, 어제의 그 시련엔 그게 좀 모자랐어요."], ["Upon the whole, Julia was in the best looks of the two, at least after you were spoken of.", "대체로 둘 중엔 줄리아가 더 나은 낯빛이었죠, 적어도 그쪽 얘기가 나온 뒤엔요."], ["There was no recovering the complexion from the moment that I spoke of ‘Fanny,’ and spoke of her as a sister should. But Mrs.", "내가 ‘패니’ 얘기를 꺼내고, 누이라면 마땅히 그럴 만한 투로 그 얘길 한 그 순간부터, 그 낯빛은 도무지 돌아오질 않더군요."], ["Rushworth’s day of good looks will come; we have cards for her first party on the 28th.", "하지만 러시워스 부인이 고운 낯빛을 뽐낼 날이 올 거예요. 우리는 28일 그이의 첫 모임 초대장을 받았거든요."], ["Then she will be in beauty, for she will open one of the best houses in Wimpole Street.", "그때는 그이도 아름다움에 싸이겠죠, 윔폴 스트리트에서도 손꼽히는 집 가운데 하나를 열 테니까요."], ["I was in it two years ago, when it was Lady Lascelle’s, and prefer it to almost any I know in London, and certainly she will then feel, to use a vulgar phrase, that she has got her pennyworth for her penny.", "나는 이 년 전 그게 래슬 부인 댁이던 시절에 거기 가 봤는데, 내가 런던에서 아는 거의 어느 집보다 더 좋아해요. 그리고 그때는 그이도 분명, 속된 표현을 쓰자면, 제 한 푼으로 한 푼어치를 제대로 얻었다 느끼겠죠."], ["Henry could not have afforded her such a house.", "헨리는 그이한테 그런 집을 마련해 줄 수 없었을 거예요."], ["I hope she will recollect it, and be satisfied, as well as she may, with moving the queen of a palace, though the king may appear best in the background; and as I have no desire to tease her, I shall never force your name upon her again.", "나는 그이가 그걸 떠올려, 왕(王)은 뒤편에 있을 때 가장 돋보일지언정 궁전의 여왕으로 나서는 데 되도록 흡족해하기를 바라요. 그리고 나는 그이를 괴롭힐 마음이 없으니, 다시는 그쪽 이름을 그이한테 억지로 들이밀지 않을게요."], ["She will grow sober by degrees.", "그이도 차츰 차분해지겠죠."], ["From all that I hear and guess, Baron Wildenheim’s attentions to Julia continue, but I do not know that he has any serious encouragement.", "내가 듣고 짐작하는 온갖 것으로 미루어, 빌덴하임 남작의 줄리아에 대한 마음씀은 이어지지만, 그이가 무슨 진지한 부추김을 받는지는 모르겠어요. 그 앤 더 잘해야 해요."], ["She ought to do better.", "가난한 귀족 자제(honourable)는 좋은 신랑감이 못 되죠."], ["A poor honourable is no catch, and I cannot imagine any liking in the case, for take away his rants, and the poor baron has nothing.", "그리고 나는 이 경우에 무슨 정 같은 건 도무지 상상이 안 돼요. 그이의 허풍(rants)을 빼면 딱한 그 남작한테는 아무것도 안 남거든요."], ["What a difference a vowel makes!", "모음 하나가 이런 차이를 내다니!"], ["If his rents were but equal to his rants!", "그이의 지대(rents)가 그 허풍(rants)만큼만 됐어도! 그쪽 사촌 에드먼드는 느릿느릿 움직여요."], ["Your cousin Edmund moves slowly; detained, perchance, by parish duties.", "어쩌면 교구 소임에 붙들렸겠죠. 손턴 레이시에 개종시킬 노파라도 하나 있나 봐요."], ["There may be some old woman at Thornton Lacey to be converted.", "나는 젊은 여자 때문에 내가 뒷전이 됐다곤 여기고 싶지 않네요. 안녕!"], ["I am unwilling to fancy myself neglected for a young one. Adieu!", "사랑하고 고운 나의 패니, 이건 런던에서 온 긴 편지예요."], ["my dear sweet Fanny, this is a long letter from London: write me a pretty one in reply to gladden Henry’s eyes, when he comes back, and send me an account of all the dashing young captains whom you disdain for his sake.”", "답장으로 헨리가 돌아왔을 때 그이 눈을 즐겁게 할 예쁜 편지 하나 써 보내 줘요. 그리고 그쪽이 그이를 위해 업신여기는 그 온갖 늠름한 젊은 함장들 얘기도 적어 보내 주고요.”"]], [["There was great food for meditation in this letter, and chiefly for unpleasant meditation; and yet, with all the uneasiness it supplied, it connected her with the absent, it told her of people and things about whom she had never felt so much curiosity as now, and she would have been glad to have been sure of such a letter every week.", "이 편지에는 곰곰 헤아릴 거리가, 주로 언짢게 헤아릴 거리가 많았다. 그러면서도 그것이 안겨 준 그 온갖 불편함에도 불구하고, 그것은 그녀를 떠나 있는 이들과 이어 주었고, 지금만큼 궁금해 본 적 없는 사람들과 일들을 그녀에게 일러 주었다."], ["Her correspondence with her aunt Bertram was her only concern of higher interest.", "그리하여 그녀는 이런 편지를 다달이가 아니라 매주 받으리라는 확신이 있었더라면 반가웠으련만. 버트럼 이모와의 편지 왕래가 그보다 더 큰 관심의 유일한 일이었다."]], [["As for any society in Portsmouth, that could at all make amends for deficiencies at home, there were none within the circle of her father’s and mother’s acquaintance to afford her the smallest satisfaction: she saw nobody in whose favour she could wish to overcome her own shyness and reserve.", "포츠머스에서 사귈 만한, 집의 부족함을 조금이나마 갚아 줄 만한 사교로 말하면, 아버지와 어머니의 지인 무리 안에는 그녀에게 조금의 흡족함이라도 줄 이가 하나도 없었다. 그녀가 저의 수줍음과 삼감을 무릅쓰고픈 마음이 들 만한 이는 아무도 안 보였다."], ["The men appeared to her all coarse, the women all pert, everybody underbred; and she gave as little contentment as she received from introductions either to old or new acquaintance.", "그녀 눈에 사내들은 죄다 거칠고, 여자들은 죄다 건방지고, 다들 교양 없어 보였다. 그리하여 옛 지인이든 새 지인이든 소개받아, 그녀는 받은 것만큼이나 준 흡족함도 적었다."], ["The young ladies who approached her at first with some respect, in consideration of her coming from a baronet’s family, were soon offended by what they termed “airs”; for, as she neither played on the pianoforte nor wore fine pelisses, they could, on farther observation, admit no right of superiority.", "처음에 준남작 집안 출신임을 헤아려 얼마간 존경을 담아 그녀에게 다가온 아가씨들은, 이내 그들이 “거드름”이라 부르는 것에 언짢아했다. 그녀가 피아노포르테를 치지도 고운 펠리스를 걸치지도 않으니, 더 지켜본 끝에 그들은 그녀에게 무슨 우위의 권리도 인정할 수 없었던 것이다."]], [["The first solid consolation which Fanny received for the evils of home, the first which her judgment could entirely approve, and which gave any promise of durability, was in a better knowledge of Susan, and a hope of being of service to her.", "패니가 집의 화를 두고 받은 첫 든든한 위안, 그녀의 판단이 온전히 찬동할 수 있는 첫 위안, 그리고 조금이나마 오래갈 조짐을 준 첫 위안은, 수전을 더 잘 알게 된 것과 그녀에게 쓸모가 되리라는 바람에 있었다."], ["Susan had always behaved pleasantly to herself, but the determined character of her general manners had astonished and alarmed her, and it was at least a fortnight before she began to understand a disposition so totally different from her own.", "수전은 늘 저에게 유쾌하게 굴었으나, 그 두루뭉술한 태도의 단호한 결이 그녀를 놀라게 하고 겁먹게 했던지라, 저와는 그토록 온전히 다른 성미를 헤아리기 시작하기까지 적어도 두 주가 걸렸다."], ["Susan saw that much was wrong at home, and wanted to set it right.", "수전은 집에 잘못된 게 많음을 보고, 그것을 바로잡고 싶어 했다."], ["That a girl of fourteen, acting only on her own unassisted reason, should err in the method of reform, was not wonderful; and Fanny soon became more disposed to admire the natural light of the mind which could so early distinguish justly, than to censure severely the faults of conduct to which it led.", "열넷 계집아이가, 오직 저 혼자의 도움 없는 이성만으로 움직이며, 그 고침의 방식에서 그른다는 것이야 놀랄 일이 아니었다. 그리하여 패니는 이내, 그것이 이끈 처신의 흠을 모질게 나무라기보다, 그토록 일찍부터 옳은 것을 분간할 줄 아는 그 마음의 타고난 빛을 감탄하는 쪽으로 더 기울었다."], ["Susan was only acting on the same truths, and pursuing the same system, which her own judgment acknowledged, but which her more supine and yielding temper would have shrunk from asserting.", "수전은 그저, 그녀 자신의 판단도 인정하나 좀 더 늘어지고 굽히는 그녀의 성미로는 감히 내세우기를 움츠렸을 그 똑같은 진리에 따라 움직이며, 그 똑같은 방식을 좇고 있을 뿐이었다."], ["Susan tried to be useful, where she could only have gone away and cried; and that Susan was useful she could perceive; that things, bad as they were, would have been worse but for such interposition, and that both her mother and Betsey were restrained from some excesses of very offensive indulgence and vulgarity.", "그녀라면 그저 자리를 떠 울밖에 없었을 데서 수전은 쓸모가 되려 애썼다. 그리고 수전이 쓸모가 됨을, 사정이 아무리 나빴어도 그런 끼어듦이 없었더라면 더 나빴으리라는 것을, 어머니와 벳시 둘 다 자못 거슬리는 무름과 상스러움의 몇몇 극단에서 억눌려 있음을 그녀는 알아볼 수 있었다."]], [["In every argument with her mother, Susan had in point of reason the advantage, and never was there any maternal tenderness to buy her off.", "어머니와의 어떤 다툼에서든 수전은 이치에서 우세했고, 그녀를 달래 물릴 어미로서의 애틋함이라곤 여태 없었다."], ["The blind fondness which was for ever producing evil around her she had never known.", "제 둘레에 늘 화를 낳는 그 눈먼 무름을 그녀는 한 번도 받아 본 적이 없었다."], ["There was no gratitude for affection past or present to make her better bear with its excesses to the others.", "남들에게 베풀어지는 그 지나침을 더 잘 참게 해 줄, 지난 또는 지금의 애정에 대한 고마움이라곤 없었던 것이다."]], [["All this became gradually evident, and gradually placed Susan before her sister as an object of mingled compassion and respect.", "이 모두가 차츰 뚜렷해지며, 차츰 수전을 언니 앞에 딱함과 존경이 뒤섞인 대상으로 세워 놓았다."], ["That her manner was wrong, however, at times very wrong, her measures often ill-chosen and ill-timed, and her looks and language very often indefensible, Fanny could not cease to feel; but she began to hope they might be rectified.", "그러나 그 태도가 그르다는 것, 이따금 자못 그르다는 것, 그 방편이 곧잘 잘못 골라지고 때 안 맞는다는 것, 그 낯빛과 말이 자못 흔히 두둔할 수 없다는 것을 패니는 못 느낄 수 없었다. 그러나 그녀는 그것들이 바로잡힐 수 있으리라 바라기 시작했다."], ["Susan, she found, looked up to her and wished for her good opinion; and new as anything like an office of authority was to Fanny, new as it was to imagine herself capable of guiding or informing any one, she did resolve to give occasional hints to Susan, and endeavour to exercise for her advantage the juster notions of what was due to everybody, and what would be wisest for herself, which her own more favoured education had fixed in her.", "수전이 저를 우러러보며 저의 좋은 평가를 바란다는 것을 그녀는 알았다. 그리고 권위의 소임 비슷한 것이 패니에게 아무리 낯설고, 저가 누구를 이끌거나 일깨울 수 있다고 여기는 것이 아무리 낯설었어도, 그녀는 수전에게 이따금 넌지시 일러 주기로, 그리고 저의 더 나은 교육이 저에게 박아 준 그 올바른 소견—누구에게나 마땅한 것이 무엇인지, 그리고 저 자신에게 가장 지혜로운 것이 무엇인지에 대한 소견을 그녀의 이로움을 위해 써 보기로 마음먹었다."]], [["Her influence, or at least the consciousness and use of it, originated in an act of kindness by Susan, which, after many hesitations of delicacy, she at last worked herself up to.", "그녀의 감화, 아니 적어도 그것의 자각과 씀은, 여러 번의 섬세한 망설임 끝에 마침내 그녀가 스스로를 다잡아 해낸 수전을 위한 친절의 처사에서 비롯했다."], ["It had very early occurred to her that a small sum of money might, perhaps, restore peace for ever on the sore subject of the silver knife, canvassed as it now was continually, and the riches which she was in possession of herself, her uncle having given her £10 at parting, made her as able as she was willing to be generous.", "이제 끊임없이 입에 오르내리는 그 은칼이라는 아픈 화제를 두고, 자그마한 돈이 어쩌면 영영 평화를 되찾아 줄지도 모른다는 생각이 그녀에게 아주 일찍 떠올랐고, 삼촌이 헤어질 때 준 10파운드로 저 자신이 가진 그 재물이, 너그러워지고픈 마음만큼이나 너그러워질 수 있게 해 주었던 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 아주 가난한 이 말고는 은혜를 베풀어 본 적이 하도 없고, 제 또래 사이에서 화를 없애거나 친절을 베푸는 데 하도 안 익고, 집에서 저를 대단한 마님인 양 치켜세우는 티를 낼까 봐 하도 두려운지라, 그런 선물을 하는 것이 저에게 안 어울리는 일은 아니라 마음먹기까지 얼마간이 걸렸다."], ["But she was so wholly unused to confer favours, except on the very poor, so unpractised in removing evils, or bestowing kindnesses among her equals, and so fearful of appearing to elevate herself as a great lady at home, that it took some time to determine that it would not be unbecoming in her to make such a present.", "그러나 마침내 그것은 이루어졌다. 벳시를 위해 은칼 하나를 샀고, 그것은 크나큰 기쁨으로 받아들여졌으니, 그 새것임이 바랄 만한 온갖 점에서 그것을 다른 것보다 낫게 했다."], ["It was made, however, at last: a silver knife was bought for Betsey, and accepted with great delight, its newness giving it every advantage over the other that could be desired; Susan was established in the full possession of her own, Betsey handsomely declaring that now she had got one so much prettier herself, she should never want that again; and no reproach seemed conveyed to the equally satisfied mother, which Fanny had almost feared to be impossible.", "수전은 제 것을 온전히 차지하게 되었고, 벳시는 이제 제가 훨씬 더 예쁜 걸 얻었으니 다시는 저것을 바라지 않겠다고 훤칠히 선언했다. 그리고 못지않게 흡족한 어머니에게도 아무 나무람이 전해지지 않은 듯했으니, 패니가 그것은 거의 어림없으려니 여기던 일이었다."], ["The deed thoroughly answered: a source of domestic altercation was entirely done away, and it was the means of opening Susan’s heart to her, and giving her something more to love and be interested in.", "그 처사는 속속들이 통했다. 집안 다툼의 한 샘이 아주 없어졌고, 그것은 수전이 그녀에게 마음을 여는 방편이 되어, 그녀에게 사랑하고 관심 둘 무언가를 더 안겨 주었다."], ["Susan shewed that she had delicacy: pleased as she was to be mistress of property which she had been struggling for at least two years, she yet feared that her sister’s judgment had been against her, and that a reproof was designed her for having so struggled as to make the purchase necessary for the tranquillity of the house.", "수전은 저에게 섬세함이 있음을 보여 주었다. 적어도 두 해나 다투어 온 그 소유물의 임자가 된 것이 기쁘면서도, 그녀는 언니의 판단이 저를 거슬렀을까 봐, 집의 고요를 위해 그 사들임이 필요해질 만큼 다툰 데 대한 꾸지람이 저에게 뜻해졌을까 봐 두려웠던 것이다."]], [["Her temper was open.", "그녀의 성미는 툭 트여 있었다."], ["She acknowledged her fears, blamed herself for having contended so warmly; and from that hour Fanny, understanding the worth of her disposition and perceiving how fully she was inclined to seek her good opinion and refer to her judgment, began to feel again the blessing of affection, and to entertain the hope of being useful to a mind so much in need of help, and so much deserving it.", "그녀는 제 두려움을 털어놓고, 그토록 발끈하며 다툰 것을 두고 저를 나무랐다. 그리하여 그 시각부터 패니는, 그녀의 성미의 값어치를 헤아리고, 그녀가 저의 좋은 평가를 구하고 저의 판단에 기대려 얼마나 온전히 기울어 있는지를 알아채고서, 다시금 애정의 복을 느끼기 시작했고, 그토록 도움이 아쉬우면서 그토록 도움을 받을 만한 마음에 쓸모가 되리라는 바람을 품기 시작했다."], ["She gave advice, advice too sound to be resisted by a good understanding, and given so mildly and considerately as not to irritate an imperfect temper, and she had the happiness of observing its good effects not unfrequently.", "그녀는 조언을 했으니, 좋은 슬기라면 거스를 수 없을 만큼 온당하되, 미흡한 성미를 돋우지 않도록 그토록 부드럽고 사려 깊게 건네진 조언이었다. 그리고 그녀는 그 좋은 효험을 드물잖게 지켜보는 행복을 누렸다."], ["More was not expected by one who, while seeing all the obligation and expediency of submission and forbearance, saw also with sympathetic acuteness of feeling all that must be hourly grating to a girl like Susan.", "굽히고 참는 것의 온갖 마땅함과 이로움을 보면서도, 수전 같은 아이에게 시시각각 거슬릴 온갖 것을 못지않게 정겹고 예민한 심경으로 본 이가 그보다 더 바라지는 않았다."], ["Her greatest wonder on the subject soon became—not that Susan should have been provoked into disrespect and impatience against her better knowledge—but that so much better knowledge, so many good notions should have been hers at all; and that, brought up in the midst of negligence and error, she should have formed such proper opinions of what ought to be; she, who had had no cousin Edmund to direct her thoughts or fix her principles.", "이 일에 대한 그녀의 가장 큰 놀라움은 이내—수전이 제 더 나은 앎을 거슬러 무례와 조바심으로 부아 돋음을 당했다는 것이 아니라, 그토록 더 나은 앎이, 그토록 많은 좋은 소견이 대체 그녀에게 있었다는 것이 되었다. 그리고 무심함과 그릇됨의 한복판에서 자라, 마땅히 어때야 하는가에 대한 그토록 올바른 소견을 지었다는 것—제 생각을 이끌거나 제 지조를 박아 줄 사촌 에드먼드 하나 없던 그녀가 말이다."]], [["The intimacy thus begun between them was a material advantage to each.", "이렇게 둘 사이에 시작된 친밀함은 저마다에게 큰 이로움이었다."], ["By sitting together upstairs, they avoided a great deal of the disturbance of the house; Fanny had peace, and Susan learned to think it no misfortune to be quietly employed.", "위층에서 함께 앉아 지냄으로써 그들은 집의 소란을 크게 피했다. 패니는 평온을 얻었고, 수전은 조용히 일하는 것이 불행이 아님을 배웠다."], ["They sat without a fire; but that was a privation familiar even to Fanny, and she suffered the less because reminded by it of the East room.", "그들은 불도 없이 앉아 있었다. 그러나 그것은 패니에게조차 익은 결핍이었고, 그것이 동쪽 방을 떠올려 준 덕에 그녀는 덜 괴로웠다."], ["It was the only point of resemblance.", "그것이 유일하게 닮은 점이었다."], ["In space, light, furniture, and prospect, there was nothing alike in the two apartments; and she often heaved a sigh at the remembrance of all her books and boxes, and various comforts there.", "넓이도, 빛도, 세간도, 내다보이는 경치도, 두 방에는 닮은 것이 하나도 없었다. 그리하여 그녀는 곧잘, 거기 있던 제 온갖 책과 상자와 갖가지 편안함을 떠올리며 한숨을 내쉬었다."], ["By degrees the girls came to spend the chief of the morning upstairs, at first only in working and talking, but after a few days, the remembrance of the said books grew so potent and stimulative that Fanny found it impossible not to try for books again.", "차츰 두 아가씨는 아침의 대부분을 위층에서 보내게 되었으니, 처음엔 그저 바느질하고 얘기하는 것뿐이었으나, 며칠 뒤엔 그 책들에 대한 기억이 하도 세차고 부추기는 것이 되어, 패니는 다시 책을 구해 보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["There were none in her father’s house; but wealth is luxurious and daring, and some of hers found its way to a circulating library.", "아버지의 집엔 책이 하나도 없었다. 그러나 재물이란 호사스럽고 대담한 것이라, 그녀의 재물 얼마가 대출 문고로 흘러 들어갔다."], ["She became a subscriber; amazed at being anything in propria persona, amazed at her own doings in every way, to be a renter, a chuser of books!", "그녀는 회원이 되었으니, 제 이름으로 어엿한 무언가가 된다는 데 놀라고, 어느 모로 보나 제가 하는 일에 놀라며—빌리는 이가, 책을 고르는 이가 되다니!"], ["And to be having any one’s improvement in view in her choice!", "게다가 그 고름에 누군가의 나아짐을 눈에 두고 있다니!"], ["But so it was.", "그러나 정말 그러했다."], ["Susan had read nothing, and Fanny longed to give her a share in her own first pleasures, and inspire a taste for the biography and poetry which she delighted in herself.", "수전은 아무것도 읽은 것이 없었고, 패니는 저의 그 첫 즐거움을 그녀에게 나눠 주어, 저 스스로 좋아하는 전기와 시에 대한 취향을 불어넣어 주고 싶었다."]], [["In this occupation she hoped, moreover, to bury some of the recollections of Mansfield, which were too apt to seize her mind if her fingers only were busy; and, especially at this time, hoped it might be useful in diverting her thoughts from pursuing Edmund to London, whither, on the authority of her aunt’s last letter, she knew he was gone.", "게다가 이 일로 그녀는 맨스필드에 대한 기억 얼마간을 묻어 두기를 바랐으니, 그 기억은 손가락만 바쁠 때면 너무 쉬 그녀의 마음을 사로잡곤 했던 것이다. 그리고 특히 이 무렵, 그녀의 생각이 에드먼드를 좇아 런던으로 가는 것을 돌려놓는 데 이것이 쓸모가 되기를 바랐다."], ["She had no doubt of what would ensue.", "이모의 지난번 편지를 근거로, 그녀는 그가 거기로 갔음을 알았던 것이다."], ["The promised notification was hanging over her head.", "그녀는 뒤이어 무슨 일이 생길지 의심치 않았다."], ["The postman’s knock within the neighbourhood was beginning to bring its daily terrors, and if reading could banish the idea for even half an hour, it was something gained.", "그 약속된 알림이 그녀의 머리 위에 걸려 있었다. 근방에 울리는 우체부의 문 두드림이 나날의 두려움을 가져오기 시작했고, 만일 책 읽기가 반 시간만이라도 그 생각을 몰아낼 수 있다면, 그것만으로도 얻는 바가 있는 셈이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLI", "# 제41장"]], [["A week was gone since Edmund might be supposed in town, and Fanny had heard nothing of him.", "에드먼드가 시내에 있으려니 여겨질 만한 때로부터 한 주가 지났으나, 패니는 그에 대한 아무 소식도 못 들었다."], ["There were three different conclusions to be drawn from his silence, between which her mind was in fluctuation; each of them at times being held the most probable.", "그의 잠잠함에서 이끌어 낼 만한 결론이 셋 있었으니, 그녀의 마음은 그 사이에서 오르내렸고, 저마다 때때로 가장 그럴싸한 것으로 여겨졌다."], ["Either his going had been again delayed, or he had yet procured no opportunity of seeing Miss Crawford alone, or he was too happy for letter-writing!", "그의 상경이 또 미뤄졌거나, 아직 크로퍼드 양을 단둘이 만날 기회를 못 얻었거나, 아니면 편지 쓰기엔 너무 행복하거나!"]], [["One morning, about this time, Fanny having now been nearly four weeks from Mansfield, a point which she never failed to think over and calculate every day, as she and Susan were preparing to remove, as usual, upstairs, they were stopped by the knock of a visitor, whom they felt they could not avoid, from Rebecca’s alertness in going to the door, a duty which always interested her beyond any other.", "이 무렵 어느 아침, 패니가 이제 맨스필드를 떠난 지 거의 네 주가 된 참—그녀가 날마다 어김없이 곱씹고 헤아리던 그 대목이었다—에, 그녀와 수전이 여느 때처럼 위층으로 올라가려 채비하는데, 손님의 문 두드림에 걸음이 멎었다. 리베카가 문으로 가는 데 어찌나 잽싸던지—그것은 여느 무엇보다도 늘 그녀의 관심을 끄는 소임이었다—그들은 그 손님을 피할 수 없으려니 느꼈다."]], [["It was a gentleman’s voice; it was a voice that Fanny was just turning pale about, when Mr.", "신사의 목소리였다."], ["Crawford walked into the room.", "패니가 막 창백해지려던 그 목소리였는데, 이내 크로퍼드 씨가 방으로 걸어 들어왔다."]], [["Good sense, like hers, will always act when really called upon; and she found that she had been able to name him to her mother, and recall her remembrance of the name, as that of “William’s friend,”", "그녀 같은 좋은 분별은 정말로 부름받으면 늘 움직이는 법이라, 그녀는 저가 그를 어머니에게 소개하며 “윌리엄의 벗”이라는 그 이름을 어머니에게 되떠올려 줄 수 있었음을 알았다."], ["though she could not previously have believed herself capable of uttering a syllable at such a moment.", "하기야 앞서라면 그런 순간에 한마디라도 뇔 수 있으리라곤 저 스스로 믿지 못했으련만."], ["The consciousness of his being known there only as William’s friend was some support.", "그가 여기서 그저 윌리엄의 벗으로만 알려져 있다는 자각이 얼마간 버팀이 되었다."], ["Having introduced him, however, and being all reseated, the terrors that occurred of what this visit might lead to were overpowering, and she fancied herself on the point of fainting away.", "그러나 그를 소개하고 다들 다시 자리에 앉고 나니, 이 방문이 무엇으로 이어질지에 대한 두려움이 밀려들어, 그녀는 저가 막 까무러칠 것만 같았다."]], [["While trying to keep herself alive, their visitor, who had at first approached her with as animated a countenance as ever, was wisely and kindly keeping his eyes away, and giving her time to recover, while he devoted himself entirely to her mother, addressing her, and attending to her with the utmost politeness and propriety, at the same time with a degree of friendliness, of interest at least, which was making his manner perfect.", "저를 다잡으려 애쓰는 동안, 처음엔 여느 때 못지않게 생기 어린 낯빛으로 그녀에게 다가왔던 그 손님은, 슬기롭고도 친절하게 눈길을 돌려 그녀에게 추스를 겨를을 주고는, 온통 그녀 어머니에게만 몸을 바쳐, 더없는 예의와 온당함으로, 그리고 동시에 그의 태도를 온전케 하는 얼마간의 다정함—적어도 관심을 담아 그녀에게 말을 걸고 시중을 들었다."]], [["Mrs. Price’s manners were also at their best.", "프라이스 부인의 태도 또한 더없이 좋았다."], ["Warmed by the sight of such a friend to her son, and regulated by the wish of appearing to advantage before him, she was overflowing with gratitude—artless, maternal gratitude—which could not be unpleasing.", "제 아들에게 그런 벗이 있음을 보고 데워지고, 그 앞에서 잘 보이려는 바람에 다잡혀, 그녀는 고마움이—꾸밈없는 어미의 고마움이 넘쳐흘렀는데, 그것은 밉지 않은 것이었다."], ["Mr. Price was out, which she regretted very much.", "프라이스 씨는 나가고 없었으니, 그녀는 그것을 무척 아쉬워했다."], ["Fanny was just recovered enough to feel that she could not regret it; for to her many other sources of uneasiness was added the severe one of shame for the home in which he found her.", "패니는 겨우 추슬러 저는 그것을 아쉬워할 수 없음을 느낄 만큼 되어 있었다. 그녀의 여러 다른 불편함의 샘에, 그가 저를 발견한 이 집에 대한 부끄러움이라는 모진 것이 더해진 까닭이다."], ["She might scold herself for the weakness, but there was no scolding it away.", "그녀는 그 여림을 두고 저를 나무랄 수야 있었으나, 나무란다고 그것이 없어지진 않았다."], ["She was ashamed, and she would have been yet more ashamed of her father than of all the rest.", "그녀는 부끄러웠고, 나머지 온갖 것보다 아버지가 훨씬 더 부끄러웠으리라."]], [["They talked of William, a subject on which Mrs. Price could never tire; and Mr.", "그들은 윌리엄 얘기를 했는데, 프라이스 부인은 그 화제라면 결코 물릴 줄 몰랐다."], ["Crawford was as warm in his commendation as even her heart could wish.", "그리고 크로퍼드 씨는 그녀의 마음이 바랄 만한 만큼이나 뜨겁게 그를 칭찬했다."], ["She felt that she had never seen so agreeable a man in her life; and was only astonished to find that, so great and so agreeable as he was, he should be come down to Portsmouth neither on a visit to the port-admiral, nor the commissioner, nor yet with the intention of going over to the island, nor of seeing the dockyard.", "그녀는 저가 평생 이토록 상냥한 사내를 본 적이 없다 느꼈다. 다만 그토록 훌륭하고 그토록 상냥한 그가 포츠머스에 내려온 것이 항구 제독을 뵈러 온 것도, 감독관을 뵈러 온 것도, 섬으로 건너갈 뜻에서도, 조선소를 보러 온 것도 아님을 알고 그저 놀랄 뿐이었다."], ["Nothing of all that she had been used to think of as the proof of importance, or the employment of wealth, had brought him to Portsmouth.", "그녀가 중함의 증거로, 또는 재물의 쓰임으로 여기곤 하던 그 온갖 것 가운데 무엇도 그를 포츠머스로 데려온 것이 아니었다."], ["He had reached it late the night before, was come for a day or two, was staying at the Crown, had accidentally met with a navy officer or two of his acquaintance since his arrival, but had no object of that kind in coming.", "그는 간밤 늦게 이르러, 하루 이틀 머물 참으로 크라운 여관에 묵고 있었고, 도착한 뒤 우연히 아는 해군 사관 한둘을 만났으나, 그런 종류의 목적으로 온 것은 아니었다."]], [["By the time he had given all this information, it was not unreasonable to suppose that Fanny might be looked at and spoken to; and she was tolerably able to bear his eye, and hear that he had spent half an hour with his sister the evening before his leaving London; that she had sent her best and kindest love, but had had no time for writing; that he thought himself lucky in seeing Mary for even half an hour, having spent scarcely twenty-four hours in London, after his return from Norfolk, before he set off again; that her cousin Edmund was in town, had been in town, he understood, a few days; that he had not seen him himself, but that he was well, had left them all well at Mansfield, and was to dine, as yesterday, with the Frasers.", "이 온갖 소식을 다 전할 무렵, 이제 패니가 눈길받고 말을 걸어져도 무리가 아니었다. 그리고 그녀는 그의 눈을 웬만큼 견디며, 그가 런던을 떠나기 전날 저녁 그 누이와 반 시간을 보냈다는 것을, 그녀가 더없이 다정한 사랑의 안부를 전했으나 편지 쓸 겨를은 없었다는 것을, 노퍽에서 돌아온 뒤 다시 길을 나서기까지 런던에서 채 스물네 시간도 못 보낸지라 메리를 반 시간이라도 본 것을 저는 운 좋게 여긴다는 것을, 그녀의 사촌 에드먼드가 시내에 있다는 것을—그가 알기로 며칠째 시내에 있다는 것을, 저는 그를 직접 보진 못했으나 그는 잘 있고 맨스필드의 온 식구를 잘 있는 채로 두고 왔으며, 어제처럼 프레이저네에서 만찬을 들기로 되어 있다는 것을 들었다."]], [["Fanny listened collectedly, even to the last-mentioned circumstance; nay, it seemed a relief to her worn mind to be at any certainty; and the words, “then by this time it is all settled,”", "패니는 차분히 들었으니, 마지막에 언급된 그 사정까지도 그러했다. 아니, 무엇에든 확실함에 이르는 것이 그녀의 지친 마음에 오히려 안도인 듯했다."], ["passed internally, without more evidence of emotion than a faint blush.", "그리하여 “그럼 이때쯤엔 죄다 정해졌겠구나” 하는 말이, 어렴풋한 홍조 말고는 이렇다 할 심경의 드러남 없이 속으로 지나갔다."]], [["After talking a little more about Mansfield, a subject in which her interest was most apparent, Crawford began to hint at the expediency of an early walk.", "맨스필드에 대해 조금 더 얘기하고 나서—그녀의 관심이 더없이 뚜렷한 화제였다—크로퍼드는 이른 산책이 좋겠다고 넌지시 비치기 시작했다."], ["“It was a lovely morning, and at that season of the year a fine morning so often turned off, that it was wisest for everybody not to delay their exercise”; and such hints producing nothing, he soon proceeded to a positive recommendation to Mrs.", "“고운 아침이고, 이맘때는 고운 아침이 하도 자주 흐려지니, 누구든 운동을 미루지 않는 게 가장 지혜롭다”는 것이었다. 그리고 그런 넌지시 비침이 아무것도 못 낳자, 그는 이내 프라이스 부인과 그 딸들에게 지체 없이 산책 나가라고 대놓고 권하기에 이르렀다."], ["Price and her daughters to take their walk without loss of time.", "이제 그들은 서로 통하게 되었다."], ["Now they came to an understanding. Mrs.", "프라이스 부인은, 보아하니 일요일 말고는 좀처럼 문밖에 나서지 않았다."], ["Price, it appeared, scarcely ever stirred out of doors, except of a Sunday; she owned she could seldom, with her large family, find time for a walk.", "대식구를 거느린지라 산책할 겨를을 좀처럼 못 낸다고 그녀는 인정했다. “그럼 딸들더러 이런 날씨를 누리라 이르고, 저에게 그들을 모시는 즐거움을 허락해 주지 않으시겠어요?”"], ["“Would she not, then, persuade her daughters to take advantage of such weather, and allow him the pleasure of attending them?”", "프라이스 부인은 크게 고마워하며 자못 선선히 응했다. “딸들이 자못 갇혀 지내요."], ["Mrs. Price was greatly obliged and very complying.", "포츠머스는 딱한 곳이라, 자주 못 나가거든요."], ["“Her daughters were very much confined; Portsmouth was a sad place; they did not often get out; and she knew they had some errands in the town, which they would be very glad to do.”", "그리고 그 애들이 시내에 볼일이 좀 있어, 기꺼이 하려던 참인 걸 나도 안답니다.”"], ["And the consequence was, that Fanny, strange as it was—strange, awkward, and distressing—found herself and Susan, within ten minutes, walking towards the High Street with Mr. Crawford.", "그리하여 그 결과로, 패니는—야릇하게도, 야릇하고 거북하고 괴롭게도—십 분도 안 되어 저와 수전이 크로퍼드 씨와 함께 하이 스트리트 쪽으로 걷고 있음을 알았다."]], [["It was soon pain upon pain, confusion upon confusion; for they were hardly in the High Street before they met her father, whose appearance was not the better from its being Saturday.", "이내 아픔 위에 아픔이, 얼떨떨함 위에 얼떨떨함이었다. 그들이 하이 스트리트에 채 들어서기도 전에 아버지와 마주쳤는데, 그 행색은 토요일인 탓에 나을 것이 없었던 것이다."], ["He stopt; and, ungentlemanlike as he looked, Fanny was obliged to introduce him to Mr.", "그가 멈춰 섰다. 그리고 그가 아무리 신사답잖아 보였어도, 패니는 하는 수 없이 그를 크로퍼드 씨에게 소개해야 했다."], ["Crawford. She could not have a doubt of the manner in which Mr.", "크로퍼드 씨가 어떤 인상을 받을지 그녀는 의심할 나위가 없었다."], ["Crawford must be struck.", "그는 아주 부끄럽고 역겨울 게 틀림없었다."], ["He must be ashamed and disgusted altogether.", "그는 이내 그녀를 단념하고 그 혼사에 대한 조금의 마음도 그칠 게 틀림없었다."], ["He must soon give her up, and cease to have the smallest inclination for the match; and yet, though she had been so much wanting his affection to be cured, this was a sort of cure that would be almost as bad as the complaint; and I believe there is scarcely a young lady in the United Kingdoms who would not rather put up with the misfortune of being sought by a clever, agreeable man, than have him driven away by the vulgarity of her nearest relations.", "그런데도, 그녀가 그토록 그의 정이 낫기를 바라 왔으면서도, 이것은 그 병만큼이나 나쁠 뻔한 그런 종류의 나음이었다. 그리고 나는 믿거니와, 영리하고 상냥한 사내에게 구애받는 불운을 참아 내느니 차라리 그이가 제 가장 가까운 피붙이의 상스러움에 쫓겨 가게 하고 싶은 아가씨는 온 연합왕국에 좀처럼 없으리라."]], [["Mr. Crawford probably could not regard his future father-in-law with any idea of taking him for a model in dress; but (as Fanny instantly, and to her great relief, discerned) her father was a very different man, a very different Mr.", "크로퍼드 씨는 아마 제 장래 장인을 옷차림의 본보기로 삼을 생각으로야 볼 수 없었으리라."], ["Price in his behaviour to this most highly respected stranger, from what he was in his own family at home.", "그러나 (패니가 대번에, 그리고 크게 안도하며 알아챈 대로) 그녀의 아버지는, 이 더없이 존경받는 낯선 이를 대하는 처신에서, 집에서 제 식구를 대하던 것과는 아주 딴사람, 아주 딴 프라이스 씨였다. 그의 태도는 이제, 세련되진 않았어도 웬만큼을 넘었다."], ["His manners now, though not polished, were more than passable: they were grateful, animated, manly; his expressions were those of an attached father, and a sensible man; his loud tones did very well in the open air, and there was not a single oath to be heard.", "고맙게 여기고, 생기 있고, 사내다웠다. 그의 말은 정 많은 아버지, 지각 있는 사내의 말이었다."], ["Such was his instinctive compliment to the good manners of Mr.", "그 우렁찬 목소리도 트인 데선 자못 어울렸고, 욕지거리 한마디 들리지 않았다."], ["Crawford; and, be the consequence what it might, Fanny’s immediate feelings were infinitely soothed.", "이것이 크로퍼드 씨의 좋은 태도에 대한 그의 본능적인 예의였다. 그리고 그 결과가 무엇이든 간에, 패니의 당장의 심경은 이루 말할 수 없이 다독여졌다."]], [["The conclusion of the two gentlemen’s civilities was an offer of Mr.", "두 신사의 예의차림의 끝은, 크로퍼드 씨를 조선소로 데려가겠다는 프라이스 씨의 청이었다."], ["Price’s to take Mr. Crawford into the dockyard, which Mr.", "크로퍼드 씨는, 조선소야 몇 번이고 봤으면서도, 그런 뜻으로 베풀어진 것을 은혜로 받고 싶고, 그만큼 더 오래 패니와 함께하기를 바라, 만일 프라이스 양들이 그 고단함을 겁내지만 않는다면 자못 고맙게 그것을 누리려 했다."], ["Crawford, desirous of accepting as a favour what was intended as such, though he had seen the dockyard again and again, and hoping to be so much the longer with Fanny, was very gratefully disposed to avail himself of, if the Miss Prices were not afraid of the fatigue; and as it was somehow or other ascertained, or inferred, or at least acted upon, that they were not at all afraid, to the dockyard they were all to go; and but for Mr.", "그리고 어찌어찌 그들이 조금도 안 겁낸다는 것이 확인되었는지 넘겨짚어졌는지, 아니면 적어도 그런 셈 치고, 조선소로 다들 가기로 되었다."], ["Crawford, Mr. Price would have turned thither directly, without the smallest consideration for his daughters’ errands in the High Street.", "그리고 크로퍼드 씨만 아니었다면, 프라이스 씨는 딸들의 하이 스트리트 볼일은 조금도 헤아리지 않고 곧장 그리로 돌아섰으리라."], ["He took care, however, that they should be allowed to go to the shops they came out expressly to visit; and it did not delay them long, for Fanny could so little bear to excite impatience, or be waited for, that before the gentlemen, as they stood at the door, could do more than begin upon the last naval regulations, or settle the number of three-deckers now in commission, their companions were ready to proceed.", "그러나 크로퍼드 씨는, 그들이 일부러 들르러 나온 가게에 들르게 되도록 마음 썼다. 그것이 그들을 오래 지체시키진 않았으니, 패니는 남을 조바심 나게 하거나 기다리게 하는 것을 하도 못 견뎌, 신사들이 문간에 서서 최근 해군 규정 얘기를 겨우 꺼내거나 지금 취역 중인 삼층 갑판함의 수를 채 셈하기도 전에, 그들의 일행은 나아갈 채비가 되었던 것이다."]], [["They were then to set forward for the dockyard at once, and the walk would have been conducted—according to Mr.", "그리하여 그들은 곧바로 조선소로 나서기로 했다. 그리고 크로퍼드 씨가 보기에, 만일 프라이스 씨에게 그 산책의 온전한 조율이 맡겨졌더라면 그것은 자못 별난 식으로 이뤄졌으리라."], ["Crawford’s opinion—in a singular manner, had Mr. Price been allowed the entire regulation of it, as the two girls, he found, would have been left to follow, and keep up with them or not, as they could, while they walked on together at their own hasty pace.", "두 아가씨는, 그가 보니, 뒤에 남겨져 저희 힘닿는 대로 따라오든 말든 하게 내버려지고, 그동안 그들 둘은 저희 서두르는 걸음으로 함께 걸어갈 판이었던 것이다."], ["He was able to introduce some improvement occasionally, though by no means to the extent he wished; he absolutely would not walk away from them; and at any crossing or any crowd, when Mr.", "그는 이따금 얼마간 손을 볼 수 있었으니, 바라는 만큼은 결코 못 됐지만. 그는 결코 그들에게서 앞서 걸어가려 하지 않았고, 어떤 건널목이나 어떤 인파에서, 프라이스 씨가 그저 “이리 와, 얘들아."], ["Price was only calling out, “Come, girls; come, Fan; come, Sue, take care of yourselves; keep a sharp lookout!”", "이리, 팬. 이리, 수, 조심들 해라."], ["he would give them his particular attendance.", "눈 똑바로 뜨고!” 하고 소리칠 뿐일 때, 그는 그들에게 각별히 시중을 들었다."]], [["Once fairly in the dockyard, he began to reckon upon some happy intercourse with Fanny, as they were very soon joined by a brother lounger of Mr.", "일단 조선소에 제대로 들어서자, 그는 패니와의 얼마간 행복한 어울림을 기대하기 시작했다."], ["Price’s, who was come to take his daily survey of how things went on, and who must prove a far more worthy companion than himself; and after a time the two officers seemed very well satisfied going about together, and discussing matters of equal and never-failing interest, while the young people sat down upon some timbers in the yard, or found a seat on board a vessel in the stocks which they all went to look at.", "이내 프라이스 씨의 어슬렁거리는 벗 하나가 그들과 합류했는데, 일이 어찌 돌아가는지 날마다 둘러보러 온 이로, 그 자신보다 훨씬 값진 말벗임이 틀림없었던 것이다. 그리하여 얼마 뒤 두 사관은 함께 돌아다니며 서로 대등하고 결코 물리지 않는 관심사를 논하는 데 자못 흡족해 보였고, 그동안 젊은이들은 뜰의 목재 위에 앉거나, 다들 보러 간, 선대에 오른 배 위에서 앉을 자리를 찾았다."], ["Fanny was most conveniently in want of rest.", "패니는 마침 쉬어야 할 참이었다."], ["Crawford could not have wished her more fatigued or more ready to sit down; but he could have wished her sister away.", "크로퍼드는 그녀가 그보다 더 지쳐 앉을 채비가 더 되어 있기를 바랄 수 없었으련만, 그녀 누이가 없어졌으면 하고는 바랄 만했다."], ["A quick-looking girl of Susan’s age was the very worst third in the world: totally different from Lady Bertram, all eyes and ears; and there was no introducing the main point before her.", "수전 또래의 눈치 빠른 계집아이는 세상에 둘도 없이 나쁜 세 번째였다. 버트럼 부인과는 아주 딴판으로, 온통 눈과 귀뿐이라, 그 앞에서 요점을 꺼낼 수가 없었다."], ["He must content himself with being only generally agreeable, and letting Susan have her share of entertainment, with the indulgence, now and then, of a look or hint for the better-informed and conscious Fanny.", "그는 그저 두루 상냥하게 굴며, 수전에게도 그 즐거움의 몫을 나눠 주고, 이따금 더 밝고 다 아는 패니에게 눈길이나 넌지시 비침을 던지는 것으로 만족할밖에 없었다."], ["Norfolk was what he had mostly to talk of: there he had been some time, and everything there was rising in importance from his present schemes.", "노퍽이 그가 주로 얘기할 것이었다. 거기서 그는 한동안 지냈고, 거기의 온갖 것이 그의 지금 궁리로 중해지고 있었다."], ["Such a man could come from no place, no society, without importing something to amuse; his journeys and his acquaintance were all of use, and Susan was entertained in a way quite new to her.", "그런 사내라면 어느 곳, 어느 사교에서도 즐길 거리를 얼마간 안 가져오고는 못 왔을 터라, 그의 여행과 그의 지인이 죄다 쓸모가 되었고, 수전은 저에게 아주 낯선 식으로 즐거워졌다."], ["For Fanny, somewhat more was related than the accidental agreeableness of the parties he had been in.", "패니에게는, 그가 어울린 무리의 우연한 유쾌함보다 얼마간 더한 것이 들려졌다."], ["For her approbation, the particular reason of his going into Norfolk at all, at this unusual time of year, was given.", "그녀의 찬동을 얻으려, 이 여느 때 아닌 철에 그가 대체 왜 노퍽에 갔는지 그 각별한 까닭이 주어졌다."], ["It had been real business, relative to the renewal of a lease in which the welfare of a large and—he believed—industrious family was at stake.", "그것은 정말 볼일이었으니, 크고 또—그가 믿기로—부지런한 한 집안의 안녕이 걸린 임대차의 갱신에 관한 것이었다."], ["He had suspected his agent of some underhand dealing; of meaning to bias him against the deserving; and he had determined to go himself, and thoroughly investigate the merits of the case.", "그는 제 관리인이 무슨 뒷거래를 하는 게, 저를 마땅한 이에게서 치우치게 할 셈인 게 미심쩍었다. 그리하여 몸소 가서 그 일의 옳고 그름을 속속들이 살피기로 마음먹었다."], ["He had gone, had done even more good than he had foreseen, had been useful to more than his first plan had comprehended, and was now able to congratulate himself upon it, and to feel that in performing a duty, he had secured agreeable recollections for his own mind.", "그는 갔고, 저가 내다본 것보다 더 큰 좋은 일을 했으며, 애초 계획이 아우른 것보다 더 많은 이에게 쓸모가 되었다. 그리고 이제 그것을 두고 스스로 뿌듯해하며, 한 도리를 다함으로써 제 마음에 흐뭇한 기억을 마련해 두었다 느낄 수 있었다."], ["He had introduced himself to some tenants whom he had never seen before; he had begun making acquaintance with cottages whose very existence, though on his own estate, had been hitherto unknown to him.", "그는 여태 한 번도 못 본 세입자 몇에게 저를 소개했고, 제 영지에 있으면서도 여태 그 존재조차 몰랐던 오두막들과 낯을 트기 시작했다. 이것은 패니를 겨냥한, 그것도 잘 겨냥한 것이었다."], ["This was aimed, and well aimed, at Fanny.", "그가 그토록 온당하게 말하는 것을 듣는 것은 흐뭇한 일이었다."], ["It was pleasing to hear him speak so properly; here he had been acting as he ought to do.", "여기서 그는 마땅히 해야 할 대로 처신하고 있었다. 가난하고 억눌린 이의 벗이 된다니!"], ["To be the friend of the poor and the oppressed!", "그녀에게 이보다 더 반가운 것은 없었다."], ["Nothing could be more grateful to her; and she was on the point of giving him an approving look, when it was all frightened off by his adding a something too pointed of his hoping soon to have an assistant, a friend, a guide in every plan of utility or charity for Everingham: a somebody that would make Everingham and all about it a dearer object than it had ever been yet.", "그리하여 그녀가 막 찬동하는 눈길을 그에게 주려는데, 그가 무언가 너무 노골적인 것을—머잖아 에버링엄을 위한 온갖 쓸모나 자선의 계획에서 조력자를, 벗을, 길잡이를 두기를 바란다는 것을, 에버링엄과 그 온갖 것을 여태보다 더 소중한 것으로 만들어 줄 누군가를 두기를 바란다는 것을 덧붙이는 바람에, 그 눈길이 죄다 겁먹어 달아나 버렸다."]], [["She turned away, and wished he would not say such things.", "그녀는 고개를 돌리고, 그가 그런 말을 안 했으면 싶었다."], ["She was willing to allow he might have more good qualities than she had been wont to suppose.", "그녀는 그에게 저가 여기던 것보다 더 많은 좋은 자질이 있을지도 모른다고 기꺼이 인정했다."], ["She began to feel the possibility of his turning out well at last; but he was and must ever be completely unsuited to her, and ought not to think of her.", "그녀는 그가 끝내 잘될 가망이 있음을 느끼기 시작했다. 그러나 그는 저에게 아주 안 어울렸고, 앞으로도 늘 그럴 게 틀림없었으며, 저를 마음에 두어선 안 되었다."]], [["He perceived that enough had been said of Everingham, and that it would be as well to talk of something else, and turned to Mansfield.", "그는 에버링엄 얘기는 넉넉히 했음을, 그리고 다른 얘기로 옮기는 게 나으리라는 것을 알아채고 맨스필드로 돌아섰다."], ["He could not have chosen better; that was a topic to bring back her attention and her looks almost instantly.", "이보다 더 잘 고를 수야 없었다. 그것은 거의 대번에 그녀의 관심과 낯빛을 되돌릴 화제였다."], ["It was a real indulgence to her to hear or to speak of Mansfield.", "맨스필드에 대해 듣거나 말하는 것은 그녀에게 정말이지 호사였다."], ["Now so long divided from everybody who knew the place, she felt it quite the voice of a friend when he mentioned it, and led the way to her fond exclamations in praise of its beauties and comforts, and by his honourable tribute to its inhabitants allowed her to gratify her own heart in the warmest eulogium, in speaking of her uncle as all that was clever and good, and her aunt as having the sweetest of all sweet tempers.", "이제 그곳을 아는 온갖 이와 그토록 오래 떨어져 있던 터라, 그가 그것을 입에 올리니 그녀는 그것을 자못 벗의 목소리로 느꼈다. 그리고 그가 그 아름다움과 편안함을 기리는 그녀의 애틋한 탄사로 이끌고, 그곳 식구들에 대한 그의 떳떳한 헌사로써 그녀로 하여금—삼촌을 온통 영리하고 착한 이로, 이모를 온갖 고운 성미 가운데 가장 고운 성미를 지닌 이로 말하며—더없이 뜨거운 찬사로 제 마음을 흐뭇하게 하도록 해 주었다."]], [["He had a great attachment to Mansfield himself; he said so; he looked forward with the hope of spending much, very much, of his time there; always there, or in the neighbourhood. He particularly built upon a very happy summer and autumn there this year; he felt that it would be so: he depended upon it; a summer and autumn infinitely superior to the last. As animated, as diversified, as social, but with circumstances of superiority undescribable.", "그 자신도 맨스필드에 크나큰 애착이 있었다. 그가 그리 말했다. 그는 제 시간의 많은, 아주 많은 부분을 거기서 보내기를 바라며 내다보았다. 늘 거기서든, 그 근방에서든. 그는 특히 올해 거기서의 자못 행복한 여름과 가을에 기대를 걸었다. 그리되리라 느꼈고, 그것에 기댔다. 지난번보다 한없이 나은 여름과 가을. 그만큼 생기 있고, 그만큼 다채롭고, 그만큼 어울리되, 이루 말할 수 없이 나은 사정을 갖춘."]], [["“Mansfield, Sotherton, Thornton Lacey,” he continued; “what a society will be comprised in those houses! And at Michaelmas, perhaps, a fourth may be added: some small hunting-box in the vicinity of everything so dear; for as to any partnership in Thornton Lacey, as Edmund Bertram once good-humouredly proposed, I hope I foresee two objections: two fair, excellent, irresistible objections to that plan.”", "“맨스필드, 소더턴, 손턴 레이시,” 그가 말을 이었다. “그 집들에 얼마나 대단한 사교가 담기겠어요! 그리고 미가엘 축일쯤엔, 어쩌면 넷째가 더해질지도 모르죠. 그토록 소중한 온갖 것의 근방에 자그마한 사냥 별장 하나가요. 언젠가 에드먼드 버트럼이 기분 좋게 내놨던 대로 손턴 레이시를 함께 쓰는 것으로 말하면, 나는 두 가지 반대를 미리 내다보지 싶거든요. 그 계획에 대한, 두 가지 어여쁘고, 훌륭하고, 뿌리칠 수 없는 반대를요.”"]], [["Fanny was doubly silenced here; though when the moment was passed, could regret that she had not forced herself into the acknowledged comprehension of one half of his meaning, and encouraged him to say something more of his sister and Edmund.", "패니는 여기서 두 겹으로 말문이 막혔다. 그러나 그 순간이 지나고 나니, 그의 뜻의 절반이나마 알아들었다고 스스로 억지로 인정해 그로 하여금 그 누이와 에드먼드에 대해 무언가 더 말하도록 북돋우지 못한 것이 아쉬웠다."], ["It was a subject which she must learn to speak of, and the weakness that shrunk from it would soon be quite unpardonable.", "그것은 그녀가 얘기하는 법을 익혀야 할 화제였고, 그것에서 움츠러드는 그 여림은 이내 아주 용서 못 할 것이 될 터였다."]], [["When Mr.", "프라이스 씨와 그 벗이 보고 싶은, 또는 볼 겨를이 있는 온갖 것을 다 보고 나자, 나머지도 돌아갈 채비가 되었다."], ["Price and his friend had seen all that they wished, or had time for, the others were ready to return; and in the course of their walk back, Mr.", "그리고 되걸어 돌아오는 길에, 크로퍼드 씨는 잠깐의 오붓함을 마련해 패니에게, 포츠머스에서 제 유일한 볼일은 그녀를 보는 것이라고, 오직 그녀 때문에, 오로지 그녀 때문에 하루 이틀 내려왔다고, 더 긴 온전한 헤어짐을 도무지 견딜 수 없어서라고 말했다. 그녀는 서운했다, 정말 서운했다."], ["Crawford contrived a minute’s privacy for telling Fanny that his only business in Portsmouth was to see her; that he was come down for a couple of days on her account, and hers only, and because he could not endure a longer total separation.", "그러면서도 이것과, 그가 안 했으면 싶은 두세 가지 다른 것에도 불구하고, 그녀는 그가 저번에 본 이래로 대체로 나아졌다 여겼다. 그는 맨스필드에서 여태 그러던 것보다 훨씬 더 상냥하고, 곰살맞고, 남의 심경에 자상했다."], ["She was sorry, really sorry; and yet in spite of this and the two or three other things which she wished he had not said, she thought him altogether improved since she had seen him; he was much more gentle, obliging, and attentive to other people’s feelings than he had ever been at Mansfield; she had never seen him so agreeable—so near being agreeable; his behaviour to her father could not offend, and there was something particularly kind and proper in the notice he took of Susan.", "그녀는 그가 이토록 상냥한 것을—이토록 상냥함에 가까운 것을 본 적이 없었다. 그녀의 아버지를 대하는 그의 처신은 거슬릴 수 없었고, 그가 수전을 아는 체하는 데는 유난히 친절하고 온당한 무언가가 있었다."], ["He was decidedly improved.", "그는 확실히 나아졌다. 그녀는 이튿날이 지나 버렸으면, 그가 딱 하루만 왔더라면 싶었다."], ["She wished the next day over, she wished he had come only for one day; but it was not so very bad as she would have expected: the pleasure of talking of Mansfield was so very great!", "그러나 그것은 그녀가 겁냈을 만큼 그리 나쁘진 않았다. 맨스필드 얘기를 하는 즐거움이 하도 컸던 것이다!"]], [["Before they parted, she had to thank him for another pleasure, and one of no trivial kind.", "헤어지기 전에, 그녀는 또 하나의 즐거움을, 그것도 사소하지 않은 즐거움을 두고 그에게 고마워해야 했다."], ["Her father asked him to do them the honour of taking his mutton with them, and Fanny had time for only one thrill of horror, before he declared himself prevented by a prior engagement.", "아버지가 그에게 함께 양고기 만찬을 드는 영광을 베풀어 달라 청하니, 패니는 딱 한 번의 소름 끼치는 전율을 느낄 겨를밖에 없었는데, 이내 그가 먼저 약속이 있어 못 하게 되었다고 밝혔다. 그는 그날과 이튿날 둘 다 이미 만찬 약속이 있었다."], ["He was engaged to dinner already both for that day and the next; he had met with some acquaintance at the Crown who would not be denied; he should have the honour, however, of waiting on them again on the morrow, etc., and so they parted—Fanny in a state of actual felicity from escaping so horrible an evil!", "크라운에서 몇몇 지인을 만났는데 도무지 마다할 수 없다는 것이었다. 그래도 이튿날 다시 그들을 뵈러 오는 영광을 누리겠노라 운운하고, 그리하여 그들은 헤어졌다—패니는 그토록 소름 끼치는 화를 면한 데서 온 정말이지 벅찬 기쁨에 잠긴 채!"]], [["To have had him join their family dinner-party, and see all their deficiencies, would have been dreadful!", "그가 그들의 집안 만찬 자리에 끼어 그들의 온갖 부족함을 다 보았더라면 끔찍했으리라!"], ["Rebecca’s cookery and Rebecca’s waiting, and Betsey’s eating at table without restraint, and pulling everything about as she chose, were what Fanny herself was not yet enough inured to for her often to make a tolerable meal.", "리베카의 요리와 리베카의 시중, 그리고 벳시가 식탁에서 거리낌 없이 먹으며 온갖 것을 제멋대로 잡아당기는 것은, 패니 자신조차 아직 웬만한 끼니를 곧잘 들 만큼은 익지 못한 것들이었다."], ["She was nice only from natural delicacy, but he had been brought up in a school of luxury and epicurism.", "그녀는 그저 타고난 섬세함에서 까다로울 뿐이었으나, 그는 호사와 미식의 학교에서 자란 사람이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLII", "# 제42장"]], [["The Prices were just setting off for church the next day when Mr. Crawford appeared again.", "이튿날 프라이스네가 막 교회로 나서려는데 크로퍼드 씨가 다시 나타났다."], ["He came, not to stop, but to join them; he was asked to go with them to the Garrison chapel, which was exactly what he had intended, and they all walked thither together.", "그는 머물러 있으려는 게 아니라 그들과 함께하러 온 것이었다. 그는 수비대 예배당까지 함께 가자 청받았는데, 그것이야말로 딱 그가 뜻하던 바였고, 그리하여 다들 함께 그리로 걸어갔다."]], [["The family were now seen to advantage.", "이제 그 집안이 돋보이게 보였다."], ["Nature had given them no inconsiderable share of beauty, and every Sunday dressed them in their cleanest skins and best attire.", "자연은 그들에게 적잖은 아름다움의 몫을 주었고, 일요일마다 그들을 가장 깨끗한 살결과 가장 좋은 옷차림으로 꾸며 놓았다."], ["Sunday always brought this comfort to Fanny, and on this Sunday she felt it more than ever.", "일요일은 늘 이 위안을 패니에게 안겨 주었고, 이 일요일에 그녀는 그것을 여느 때보다 더 느꼈다."], ["Her poor mother now did not look so very unworthy of being Lady Bertram’s sister as she was but too apt to look.", "그녀의 딱한 어머니는 이제, 자못 곧잘 그리 보이곤 하던 만큼 버트럼 부인의 동생답잖아 보이진 않았다."], ["It often grieved her to the heart to think of the contrast between them; to think that where nature had made so little difference, circumstances should have made so much, and that her mother, as handsome as Lady Bertram, and some years her junior, should have an appearance so much more worn and faded, so comfortless, so slatternly, so shabby.", "그녀는 둘 사이의 대조를 생각하면 곧잘 가슴이 저렸다. 자연이 그토록 작은 다름밖에 안 낸 데에 사정이 그토록 큰 다름을 냈다는 것을, 버트럼 부인만큼 고운 데다 몇 살 손아래인 어머니가 그토록 훨씬 더 닳고 바랜, 그토록 스산하고, 그토록 지저분하고, 그토록 초라한 행색이라는 것을 생각하면."], ["But Sunday made her a very creditable and tolerably cheerful-looking Mrs.", "그러나 일요일은 그녀를 자못 어엿하고 웬만큼 명랑해 보이는 프라이스 부인으로 만들었다."], ["Price, coming abroad with a fine family of children, feeling a little respite of her weekly cares, and only discomposed if she saw her boys run into danger, or Rebecca pass by with a flower in her hat.", "훤한 아이들 무리를 데리고 나서서, 한 주의 시름에서 잠깐 놓여난 것을 느끼며, 그저 제 아들들이 위험에 뛰어드는 것을 보거나 리베카가 모자에 꽃을 꽂고 지나가는 것을 볼 때만 심기가 흐트러지는."]], [["In chapel they were obliged to divide, but Mr.", "예배당에서 그들은 하는 수 없이 갈라져야 했으나, 크로퍼드 씨는 여자들 쪽에서 갈라지지 않도록 마음을 썼다."], ["Crawford took care not to be divided from the female branch; and after chapel he still continued with them, and made one in the family party on the ramparts.", "그리고 예배가 끝난 뒤에도 그는 여전히 그들과 함께해, 성벽 위 가족 무리의 한 사람이 되었다."]], [["Mrs. Price took her weekly walk on the ramparts every fine Sunday throughout the year, always going directly after morning service and staying till dinner-time.", "프라이스 부인은 한 해 내내 고운 일요일마다 성벽 위로 주간 산책을 했으니, 늘 아침 예배 직후에 가서 만찬 시각까지 머물렀다."], ["It was her public place: there she met her acquaintance, heard a little news, talked over the badness of the Portsmouth servants, and wound up her spirits for the six days ensuing.", "그것은 그녀의 사교 자리였다. 거기서 그녀는 지인을 만나고, 소식을 조금 듣고, 포츠머스 하인들의 고약함을 두고 얘기하고, 뒤이은 엿새를 위해 제 기운을 돋우었다."]], [["Thither they now went; Mr.", "이제 그들은 그리로 갔다."], ["Crawford most happy to consider the Miss Prices as his peculiar charge; and before they had been there long, somehow or other, there was no saying how, Fanny could not have believed it, but he was walking between them with an arm of each under his, and she did not know how to prevent or put an end to it.", "크로퍼드 씨는 프라이스 양들을 제 각별한 소임으로 여기는 것이 더없이 행복했다. 그리고 거기 이른 지 얼마 안 되어, 어찌 된 영문인지—어찌 그리됐는지 알 길이 없고 패니로선 믿을 수도 없었으나—그는 저마다의 팔을 제 팔에 낀 채 그들 사이를 걷고 있었고, 그녀는 그것을 막거나 끝낼 길을 몰랐다."], ["It made her uncomfortable for a time, but yet there were enjoyments in the day and in the view which would be felt.", "그것이 한동안 그녀를 거북하게 했으나, 그래도 그날에도 그 경치에도 느껴질 만한 즐거움이 있었다."]], [["The day was uncommonly lovely.", "그날은 유난히 고왔다. 정말 삼월이긴 했다."], ["It was really March; but it was April in its mild air, brisk soft wind, and bright sun, occasionally clouded for a minute; and everything looked so beautiful under the influence of such a sky, the effects of the shadows pursuing each other on the ships at Spithead and the island beyond, with the ever-varying hues of the sea, now at high water, dancing in its glee and dashing against the ramparts with so fine a sound, produced altogether such a combination of charms for Fanny, as made her gradually almost careless of the circumstances under which she felt them.", "그러나 그 포근한 공기와, 상쾌하고 부드러운 바람과, 이따금 잠깐씩 구름 낀 밝은 햇살에서는 사월이었다. 그리고 그런 하늘 아래 온갖 것이 하도 아름다워 보였다."], ["Nay, had she been without his arm, she would soon have known that she needed it, for she wanted strength for a two hours’ saunter of this kind, coming, as it generally did, upon a week’s previous inactivity.", "스핏헤드의 배들과 그 너머 섬 위로 서로 쫓는 그림자의 어우러짐이, 지금 만조에 이른 바다의 그 늘 바뀌는 빛깔과 함께, 흥에 겨워 춤추며 그토록 고운 소리로 성벽에 부딪히니, 그 모든 것이 어우러져 패니에게 그런 매력의 어우러짐을 자아내, 그녀로 하여금 그것을 느끼는 처지에 차츰 거의 무심해지게 했다. 아니, 그의 팔이 없었더라면 그녀는 이내 저에게 그것이 필요함을 알았으리라."], ["Fanny was beginning to feel the effect of being debarred from her usual regular exercise; she had lost ground as to health since her being in Portsmouth; and but for Mr.", "대개 그러하듯 한 주의 앞선 무기력 끝에 온 이런 종류의 두 시간 어슬렁 산책을 견딜 기력이 그녀에겐 없었던 것이다. 패니는 여느 규칙적인 운동을 못 하게 된 것의 여파를 느끼기 시작하고 있었다."], ["Crawford and the beauty of the weather would soon have been knocked up now.", "포츠머스에 온 이래 그녀는 건강이 나빠졌고, 크로퍼드 씨와 그 좋은 날씨가 아니었다면 이제 이내 나가떨어졌으리라."]], [["The loveliness of the day, and of the view, he felt like herself.", "그날과 그 경치의 고움을 그도 그녀처럼 느꼈다. 그들은 곧잘 같은 심경과 안목으로, 벽에 기댄 채 몇 분씩 멈춰 서서 바라보고 감탄했다."], ["They often stopt with the same sentiment and taste, leaning against the wall, some minutes, to look and admire; and considering he was not Edmund, Fanny could not but allow that he was sufficiently open to the charms of nature, and very well able to express his admiration.", "그리고 그가 에드먼드가 아님을 감안하면, 패니는 그가 자연의 매력에 웬만큼 열려 있고, 제 감탄을 자못 잘 나타낼 줄 안다는 것을 인정할밖에 없었다. 그녀는 이따금 얼마간 애틋한 상념에 젖었는데, 그는 이따금 그 틈을 타 들키지 않고 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다볼 수 있었다."], ["She had a few tender reveries now and then, which he could sometimes take advantage of to look in her face without detection; and the result of these looks was, that though as bewitching as ever, her face was less blooming than it ought to be.", "그리고 그 들여다봄의 결과인즉, 그녀의 얼굴이 여느 때 못지않게 홀리는 것이면서도, 마땅한 만큼 화사하진 않다는 것이었다. 그녀는 아주 잘 있노라 말했고, 달리 여겨지기를 싫어했다."], ["She said she was very well, and did not like to be supposed otherwise; but take it all in all, he was convinced that her present residence could not be comfortable, and therefore could not be salutary for her, and he was growing anxious for her being again at Mansfield, where her own happiness, and his in seeing her, must be so much greater.", "그러나 이 모두를 아울러 보면, 그는 그녀의 지금 거처가 편안할 리 없고 그러니 그녀에게 이로울 리 없다 확신했다. 그리하여 그녀가 다시 맨스필드에 있게 되기를 애타게 바라기 시작했으니, 거기서라면 그녀 자신의 행복도, 그녀를 보는 저의 행복도 훨씬 클 게 틀림없었다."]], [["“You have been here a month, I think?”", "“여기 온 지 한 달 됐죠?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“No; not quite a month.", "“아뇨, 한 달이 채 안 됐어요."], ["It is only four weeks to-morrow since I left Mansfield.”", "맨스필드를 떠난 지 내일이면 겨우 네 주예요.”"]], [["“You are a most accurate and honest reckoner.", "“참으로 정확하고 정직한 셈꾼이시네요."], ["I should call that a month.”", "나라면 그걸 한 달이라 부르겠는데.”"]], [["“I did not arrive here till Tuesday evening.”", "“저는 화요일 저녁에야 여기 이르렀는걸요.”"]], [["“And it is to be a two months’ visit, is not?”", "“그리고 두 달 방문이 될 참이죠, 아닌가요?”"]], [["“Yes.", "“네."], ["My uncle talked of two months.", "삼촌이 두 달을 얘기하셨어요."], ["I suppose it will not be less.”", "그보다 짧진 않을 것 같아요.”"]], [["“And how are you to be conveyed back again?", "“그리고 어떻게 다시 데려가지죠?"], ["Who comes for you?”", "누가 데리러 오나요?”"]], [["“I do not know.", "“모르겠어요."], ["I have heard nothing about it yet from my aunt.", "아직 이모한테서 그에 대해선 아무 말도 못 들었어요."], ["Perhaps I may be to stay longer.", "어쩌면 더 오래 머물게 될지도 모르죠."], ["It may not be convenient for me to be fetched exactly at the two months’ end.”", "딱 두 달 끝에 데리러 오는 게 형편에 안 맞을 수도 있고요.”"]], [["After a moment’s reflection, Mr.", "잠깐 헤아린 뒤, 크로퍼드 씨가 대답했다."], ["Crawford replied, “I know Mansfield, I know its way, I know its faults towards you.", "“나는 맨스필드를 알아요, 그 방식을 알고, 양을 향한 그 흠도 알아요."], ["I know the danger of your being so far forgotten, as to have your comforts give way to the imaginary convenience of any single being in the family.", "나는 양이 그토록 까맣게 잊혀, 양의 편안함이 그 집안 어느 한 사람의 상상 속 편의에 밀려나게 될 위험을 알아요."], ["I am aware that you may be left here week after week, if Sir Thomas cannot settle everything for coming himself, or sending your aunt’s maid for you, without involving the slightest alteration of the arrangements which he may have laid down for the next quarter of a year.", "나는 알아요, 토머스 경이 몸소 오거나 이모의 하녀를 양한테 보내는 일을, 제가 앞으로 석 달간 짜 둔 채비를 조금도 안 바꾸고서는 마련하지 못하면, 양이 이 주 저 주 여기 남겨질 수도 있다는 걸."], ["This will not do.", "이래선 안 돼요."], ["Two months is an ample allowance; I should think six weeks quite enough.", "두 달이면 넉넉한 몫이에요. 나라면 여섯 주면 아주 넉넉하다 여기겠어요."], ["I am considering your sister’s health,”", "나는 양의 누이 건강을 헤아리고 있어요.”"], ["said he, addressing himself to Susan, “which I think the confinement of Portsmouth unfavourable to.", "하며 그가 수전에게 몸을 돌려 말했다. “포츠머스에 갇혀 있는 게 그이 건강에 안 좋다 여겨서요."], ["She requires constant air and exercise.", "그이는 끊임없는 바람과 운동이 필요해요."], ["When you know her as well as I do, I am sure you will agree that she does, and that she ought never to be long banished from the free air and liberty of the country.", "나만큼 그이를 알게 되면, 그이가 그렇다는 것과, 그이가 결코 시골의 트인 바람과 자유에서 오래 쫓겨나 있어선 안 된다는 것에 그쪽도 분명 수긍할 거예요."], ["If, therefore”", "그러니 만일”"], ["(turning again to Fanny), “you find yourself growing unwell, and any difficulties arise about your returning to Mansfield, without waiting for the two months to be ended, that must not be regarded as of any consequence, if you feel yourself at all less strong or comfortable than usual, and will only let my sister know it, give her only the slightest hint, she and I will immediately come down, and take you back to Mansfield.", "(다시 패니에게 돌아서며) “양이 몸이 나빠지는 걸 느끼고, 두 달이 다 차기를 기다리지 않고 맨스필드로 돌아가는 데 무슨 어려움이 생기거든, 그건 조금도 대수로이 여겨선 안 돼요. 여느 때보다 조금이라도 덜 튼튼하거나 덜 편안하다 느껴지거든, 그저 내 누이한테만 알려요, 그이한테 아주 살짝만 넌지시 비치기만 하면, 그이하고 나는 대번에 내려와 양을 맨스필드로 데려갈 거예요."], ["You know the ease and the pleasure with which this would be done.", "이게 얼마나 손쉽고 즐겁게 이뤄질지 양도 알잖아요."], ["You know all that would be felt on the occasion.”", "그 자리에서 무엇이 느껴질지 양도 다 알고요.”"]], [["Fanny thanked him, but tried to laugh it off.", "패니는 그에게 고마워했으나, 그것을 웃어넘기려 했다."]], [["“I am perfectly serious,”", "“나는 아주 진지해요,”"], ["he replied, “as you perfectly know.", "그가 대꾸했다. “양도 아주 잘 알듯이."], ["And I hope you will not be cruelly concealing any tendency to indisposition.", "그리고 나는 양이 몸이 안 좋아지는 낌새를 모질게 감추지 않기를 바라요. 정말이지, 그리해선 안 돼요."], ["Indeed, you shall not; it shall not be in your power; for so long only as you positively say, in every letter to Mary, ‘I am well,’ and I know you cannot speak or write a falsehood, so long only shall you be considered as well.”", "그건 양의 힘에 닿는 일이 되지 못할 겁니다. 양이 메리한테 보내는 편지마다 대놓고 ‘나는 잘 있어요’ 하는 동안에만—그리고 나는 양이 거짓을 말하거나 쓸 수 없음을 아니까요—오직 그동안에만 양이 잘 있다 여겨질 테니까요.”"]], [["Fanny thanked him again, but was affected and distressed to a degree that made it impossible for her to say much, or even to be certain of what she ought to say.", "패니는 다시 그에게 고마워했으나, 어찌나 마음이 움직이고 괴로웠던지 많은 말을 하기도, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 가늠하기조차 어림없었다."], ["This was towards the close of their walk.", "이것은 그들 산책의 끝머리 무렵이었다."], ["He attended them to the last, and left them only at the door of their own house, when he knew them to be going to dinner, and therefore pretended to be waited for elsewhere.", "그는 마지막까지 그들에게 시중을 들다가, 그들이 만찬을 들러 간다는 것을 알자, 어디 다른 데서 저를 기다린다는 척하며, 그들 집 문간에서야 그들과 헤어졌다."]], [["“I wish you were not so tired,”", "“양이 그토록 지치지 않았으면 싶어요,”"], ["said he, still detaining Fanny after all the others were in the house—“I wish I left you in stronger health.", "다른 이들이 다 집에 든 뒤에도 여전히 패니를 붙들며 그가 말했다. “양을 더 튼튼한 채로 두고 가면 싶고요."], ["Is there anything I can do for you in town?", "시내에서 양을 위해 뭐 해 줄 게 없나요?"], ["I have half an idea of going into Norfolk again soon.", "나는 머잖아 다시 노퍽에 갈까 하는 마음이 절반쯤 있어요."], ["I am not satisfied about Maddison.", "매디슨이 못 미더워서요."], ["I am sure he still means to impose on me if possible, and get a cousin of his own into a certain mill, which I design for somebody else.", "그자는 여전히 되도록 나를 속여서 제 사촌을 어느 방앗간에 앉히려는 게 분명하거든요, 내가 딴 사람한테 주려는 방앗간에 말이에요."], ["I must come to an understanding with him.", "나는 그자와 담판을 지어야 해요."], ["I must make him know that I will not be tricked on the south side of Everingham, any more than on the north: that I will be master of my own property.", "나는 에버링엄 북쪽에서든 남쪽에서든 결코 속아 넘어가지 않으리라는 걸, 내가 내 재산의 주인이 되리라는 걸 그자한테 알려야 해요."], ["I was not explicit enough with him before.", "앞서 나는 그자한테 충분히 딱 잘라 말하지 못했어요."], ["The mischief such a man does on an estate, both as to the credit of his employer and the welfare of the poor, is inconceivable.", "그런 자가 영지에서 저지르는 해악은, 그 주인의 신망으로 보나 가난한 이의 안녕으로 보나 이루 헤아릴 수 없어요."], ["I have a great mind to go back into Norfolk directly, and put everything at once on such a footing as cannot be afterwards swerved from.", "나는 곧장 노퍽으로 돌아가, 나중에 어긋날 수 없는 발판 위에 온갖 것을 대번에 올려놓고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같아요. 매디슨은 영리한 자예요."], ["Maddison is a clever fellow; I do not wish to displace him, provided he does not try to displace me; but it would be simple to be duped by a man who has no right of creditor to dupe me, and worse than simple to let him give me a hard-hearted, griping fellow for a tenant, instead of an honest man, to whom I have given half a promise already.", "나는 그자가 나를 밀어내려 들지만 않는다면 그자를 밀어낼 마음은 없어요. 하지만 나를 속일 채권자의 권리도 없는 자한테 속아 넘어가는 건 어리석은 짓이고, 이미 절반쯤 약속을 준 정직한 사내 대신, 그자가 나한테 모질고 인색한 자를 세입자로 안기게 놔두는 건 어리석음보다 더한 짓이죠."], ["Would it not be worse than simple?", "어리석음보다 더한 짓 아니겠어요?"], ["Shall I go?", "갈까요?"], ["Do you advise it?”", "그리하라 권하시겠어요?”"]], [["“I advise!", "“제가 권하다니요!"], ["You know very well what is right.”", "크로퍼드 씨야말로 뭐가 옳은지 잘 아시잖아요.”"]], [["“Yes.", "“그럼요."], ["When you give me your opinion, I always know what is right.", "양이 내게 의견을 주면, 나는 늘 뭐가 옳은지 알거든요."], ["Your judgment is my rule of right.”", "양의 판단이 내 옳음의 잣대예요.”"]], [["“Oh, no! do not say so.", "“어머, 아녜요!"], ["We have all a better guide in ourselves, if we would attend to it, than any other person can be.", "그런 말씀 마세요. 우리 다, 마음만 기울인다면, 다른 누구보다 더 나은 길잡이를 저 자신 안에 지니고 있는걸요."], ["Good-bye; I wish you a pleasant journey to-morrow.”", "안녕히 가세요. 내일 즐거운 여행 되시길 빌어요.”"]], [["“Is there nothing I can do for you in town?”", "“시내에서 양을 위해 해 줄 게 정말 없어요?”"]], [["“Nothing; I am much obliged to you.”", "“없어요. 무척 감사드려요.”"]], [["“Have you no message for anybody?”", "“누구한테 전할 말도 없고요?”"]], [["“My love to your sister, if you please; and when you see my cousin, my cousin Edmund, I wish you would be so good as to say that I suppose I shall soon hear from him.”", "“누이한테 제 사랑을 전해 주세요, 그렇게 해 주시면. 그리고 제 사촌을, 사촌 에드먼드를 보시거든, 부디 이내 그이한테서 소식을 들으려니 여긴다고 좀 전해 주시면 좋겠어요.”"]], [["“Certainly; and if he is lazy or negligent, I will write his excuses myself.”", "“그럼요. 그리고 그가 게으르거나 소홀하거든, 그의 변명은 내가 직접 써 드리죠.”"]], [["He could say no more, for Fanny would be no longer detained.", "그는 더 말할 수 없었으니, 패니가 더는 붙들리려 하지 않았던 것이다."], ["He pressed her hand, looked at her, and was gone.", "그는 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐고, 그녀를 바라보고, 갔다."], ["He went to while away the next three hours as he could, with his other acquaintance, till the best dinner that a capital inn afforded was ready for their enjoyment, and she turned in to her more simple one immediately.", "그는 뒤이은 세 시간을 다른 지인들과 함께 되도록 흘려보내러 갔으니, 훌륭한 여관이 내놓을 수 있는 가장 좋은 만찬이 그들이 즐길 채비가 될 때까지였다. 그리고 그녀는 대번에 제 더 소박한 만찬으로 들어갔다."]], [["Their general fare bore a very different character; and could he have suspected how many privations, besides that of exercise, she endured in her father’s house, he would have wondered that her looks were not much more affected than he found them.", "그들의 여느 끼니는 자못 다른 빛깔이었다. 그리고 그가, 운동 말고도 그녀가 아버지 집에서 얼마나 많은 결핍을 견디는지 짐작할 수 있었더라면, 그녀의 낯빛이 저가 본 것보다 훨씬 더 축나지 않은 것을 의아해했으리라."], ["She was so little equal to Rebecca’s puddings and Rebecca’s hashes, brought to table, as they all were, with such accompaniments of half-cleaned plates, and not half-cleaned knives and forks, that she was very often constrained to defer her heartiest meal till she could send her brothers in the evening for biscuits and buns.", "그녀는 리베카의 푸딩과 리베카의 해시—죄다 반쯤 닦인 접시와, 절반도 안 닦인 나이프와 포크를 곁들여 식탁에 오르는 그것들을 도무지 감당 못 해, 자못 곧잘 제 가장 든든한 끼니를, 저녁에 오라비들을 시켜 비스킷과 빵을 사 올 수 있을 때까지 미룰밖에 없었다. 맨스필드에서 곱게 길러진 뒤라, 이제 와 포츠머스에서 단련되기엔 너무 늦은 것이다."], ["After being nursed up at Mansfield, it was too late in the day to be hardened at Portsmouth; and though Sir Thomas, had he known all, might have thought his niece in the most promising way of being starved, both mind and body, into a much juster value for Mr. Crawford’s good company and good fortune, he would probably have feared to push his experiment farther, lest she might die under the cure.", "그리고 토머스 경이, 온갖 것을 알았더라면, 조카딸이 몸도 마음도 굶주려 크로퍼드 씨의 좋은 벗함과 좋은 운수를 훨씬 더 올바로 값매기게 될 더없이 그럴듯한 길에 들어섰다 여겼을지언정, 그는 아마 그 처방으로 그녀가 죽을까 봐 그 시험을 더 밀어붙이기를 두려워했으리라."]], [["Fanny was out of spirits all the rest of the day.", "패니는 그날 나머지 내내 기운이 없었다. 크로퍼드 씨를 다시 안 볼 것을 웬만큼 확신하면서도, 그녀는 처지는 것을 어쩔 수 없었다."], ["Though tolerably secure of not seeing Mr.", "그것은 벗의 성질을 띤 누군가와 헤어지는 것이었다."], ["Crawford again, she could not help being low.", "그리고 한 면으로는 그가 간 것이 반가우면서도, 이제 저가 모두에게 버려진 것만 같았다."], ["It was parting with somebody of the nature of a friend; and though, in one light, glad to have him gone, it seemed as if she was now deserted by everybody; it was a sort of renewed separation from Mansfield; and she could not think of his returning to town, and being frequently with Mary and Edmund, without feelings so near akin to envy as made her hate herself for having them.", "그것은 일종의 새삼스러운 맨스필드와의 헤어짐이었다. 그리고 그가 시내로 돌아가 메리와 에드먼드와 곧잘 함께하리라는 것을, 그런 마음을 품은 저 자신이 미울 만큼 시샘에 아주 가까운 심경 없이는 그녀는 생각할 수 없었다."]], [["Her dejection had no abatement from anything passing around her; a friend or two of her father’s, as always happened if he was not with them, spent the long, long evening there; and from six o’clock till half-past nine, there was little intermission of noise or grog.", "그녀의 풀 죽음은 제 둘레에 벌어지는 무엇으로도 덜어지지 않았다. 아버지의 벗 한둘이, 그가 함께 있지 않을 때면 늘 그러듯, 거기서 그 길고 긴 저녁을 보냈다."], ["She was very low. The wonderful improvement which she still fancied in Mr.", "그리고 여섯 시부터 아홉 시 반까지, 소란과 그로그가 좀처럼 끊이지 않았다."], ["Crawford was the nearest to administering comfort of anything within the current of her thoughts.", "그녀는 자못 기운이 없었다. 그녀가 여전히 크로퍼드 씨에게서 그리는 그 놀라운 나아짐이, 그녀 생각의 흐름 안에서 위안을 줄 것에 가장 가까운 것이었다."], ["Not considering in how different a circle she had been just seeing him, nor how much might be owing to contrast, she was quite persuaded of his being astonishingly more gentle and regardful of others than formerly.", "저가 방금 얼마나 다른 무리에서 그를 봤는지도, 그것이 얼마나 대조에 힘입은 것일 수 있는지도 헤아리지 않은 채, 그녀는 그가 예전보다 놀랄 만큼 더 상냥하고 남을 더 헤아린다고 아주 믿어 버렸다."], ["And, if in little things, must it not be so in great?", "그리고 사소한 것에서 그렇다면, 큰 것에서도 그럴밖에 없지 않겠는가?"], ["So anxious for her health and comfort, so very feeling as he now expressed himself, and really seemed, might not it be fairly supposed that he would not much longer persevere in a suit so distressing to her?", "그녀의 건강과 편안함을 그토록 애타게 걱정하고, 이제 스스로 나타낸 대로, 또 정말로 그리 보인 대로 그토록 마음 깊은 사람이라면, 그가 그녀에게 그토록 괴로운 그 구애를 더는 오래 밀어붙이지 않으리라 여겨도 온당하지 않겠는가?"]], [["# CHAPTER XLIII", "# 제43장"]], [["It was presumed that Mr. Crawford was travelling back, to London, on the morrow, for nothing more was seen of him at Mr.", "크로퍼드 씨가 이튿날 런던으로 되돌아가는 여정에 올랐으려니 여겨졌으니, 프라이스 씨 댁에서 그의 모습이 더는 보이지 않았던 것이다."], ["Price’s; and two days afterwards, it was a fact ascertained to Fanny by the following letter from his sister, opened and read by her, on another account, with the most anxious curiosity:—", "그리고 이틀 뒤, 그것은 그 누이에게서 온 다음 편지로 패니에게 확인된 사실이 되었다. 그 편지는 다른 까닭에서 그녀가 더없이 애타는 궁금함으로 뜯어 읽은 것이었다—"]], [["“I have to inform you, my dearest Fanny, that Henry has been down to Portsmouth to see you; that he had a delightful walk with you to the dockyard last Saturday, and one still more to be dwelt on the next day, on the ramparts; when the balmy air, the sparkling sea, and your sweet looks and conversation were altogether in the most delicious harmony, and afforded sensations which are to raise ecstasy even in retrospect.", "“사랑하는 패니, 그쪽한테 알려야겠어요. 헨리가 그쪽을 보러 포츠머스에 내려갔다는 걸요."], ["This, as well as I understand, is to be the substance of my information.", "지난 토요일 그이가 그쪽하고 조선소까지 즐거운 산책을 했고, 이튿날엔 성벽 위에서 그보다 더 마음에 담아 둘 만한 산책을 했다는 걸요."], ["He makes me write, but I do not know what else is to be communicated, except this said visit to Portsmouth, and these two said walks, and his introduction to your family, especially to a fair sister of yours, a fine girl of fifteen, who was of the party on the ramparts, taking her first lesson, I presume, in love.", "그때 그 향긋한 공기와, 반짝이는 바다와, 그쪽의 고운 낯빛과 이야기가 다 함께 더없이 감미로운 어우러짐을 이루어, 되돌아 떠올리기만 해도 황홀을 자아낼 만한 심경을 안겨 주었다는 걸요. 이게, 내가 알아듣기로는, 내 알림의 알맹이가 될 참이에요."], ["I have not time for writing much, but it would be out of place if I had, for this is to be a mere letter of business, penned for the purpose of conveying necessary information, which could not be delayed without risk of evil.", "그이가 나더러 쓰게 하는데, 그이가 포츠머스에 다녀간 이 일과, 이 두 번의 산책과, 그이가 그쪽 식구한테 소개된 것—특히 그쪽의 어여쁜 누이, 성벽 위 무리에 낀 열다섯 살 훤한 아가씨, 짐작건대 사랑의 첫 가르침을 받던 그 아가씨한테 소개된 것 말고 달리 뭘 전해야 할지 나는 모르겠어요. 나는 길게 쓸 겨를이 없어요, 하지만 겨를이 있다 해도 어울리지 않을 거예요."], ["My dear, dear Fanny, if I had you here, how I would talk to you!", "이건 그저 볼일 편지, 미룰 경우 화의 위험이 있는 그 요긴한 알림을 전할 목적으로 쓰인 편지가 될 참이니까요. 사랑하는, 사랑하는 패니, 그쪽이 여기 있다면 내가 얼마나 그쪽한테 얘기할지!"], ["You should listen to me till you were tired, and advise me till you were still tired more; but it is impossible to put a hundredth part of my great mind on paper, so I will abstain altogether, and leave you to guess what you like.", "그쪽은 지칠 때까지 내 말을 들어야 하고, 그보다 더 지칠 때까지 나한테 조언해야 할 거예요. 하지만 내 그 대단한 마음의 백분의 일도 종이에 옮기는 건 어림없으니, 나는 아예 삼가고, 그쪽더러 좋을 대로 짐작하게 놔둘게요."], ["I have no news for you.", "그쪽한테 전할 소식이 없어요."], ["You have politics, of course; and it would be too bad to plague you with the names of people and parties that fill up my time.", "물론 그쪽엔 정치가 있죠. 그리고 내 시간을 채우는 사람과 무리의 이름으로 그쪽을 성가시게 하는 건 너무 못할 노릇이겠고요."], ["I ought to have sent you an account of your cousin’s first party, but I was lazy, and now it is too long ago; suffice it, that everything was just as it ought to be, in a style that any of her connexions must have been gratified to witness, and that her own dress and manners did her the greatest credit.", "그쪽 사촌의 첫 모임 얘기를 보냈어야 했는데, 게을렀고 이젠 너무 오래된 얘기예요. 이것만 알면 돼요, 온갖 게 딱 마땅한 대로였고, 그이의 인연이라면 누구든 지켜보며 흐뭇해했을 그런 격이었으며, 그이 자신의 옷차림과 태도가 그이한테 더없는 명예를 안겼다는 걸요."], ["My friend, Mrs. Fraser, is mad for such a house, and it would not make me miserable.", "내 벗 프레이저 부인은 그런 집이라면 사족을 못 쓰는데, 나라도 그런 집이 있으면 비참하진 않겠어요."], ["I go to Lady Stornaway after Easter; she seems in high spirits, and very happy.", "나는 부활절 뒤에 스토너웨이 부인한테 가요."], ["I fancy Lord S.", "그이는 자못 신나고 무척 행복해 보여요."], ["is very good-humoured and pleasant in his own family, and I do not think him so very ill-looking as I did—at least, one sees many worse.", "S 경은 제 식구 사이에선 자못 서글서글하고 유쾌한가 봐요. 그리고 나는 그이가 여기던 만큼 그리 못생겼다곤 여기지 않게 됐어요—적어도, 더 못한 이도 많으니까요."], ["He will not do by the side of your cousin Edmund.", "그이야 그쪽 사촌 에드먼드 곁에선 어림없죠."], ["Of the last-mentioned hero, what shall I say?", "방금 말한 그 주인공으로 말하면, 뭐라 할까요?"], ["If I avoided his name entirely, it would look suspicious.", "내가 그이 이름을 아예 피하면 미심쩍어 보일 테니."], ["I will say, then, that we have seen him two or three times, and that my friends here are very much struck with his gentlemanlike appearance. Mrs.", "그럼 이렇게 말하죠, 우리가 그이를 두세 번 봤고, 여기 내 벗들이 그이의 신사다운 풍채에 자못 감복했다고요."], ["Fraser (no bad judge) declares she knows but three men in town who have so good a person, height, and air; and I must confess, when he dined here the other day, there were none to compare with him, and we were a party of sixteen.", "프레이저 부인은(못 볼 눈이 아니에요) 시내에서 그만큼 좋은 인물과 키와 풍채를 지닌 남자를 셋밖에 모른다 단언해요. 그리고 나도 인정하건대, 요전에 그이가 여기서 만찬을 들었을 때, 그이에 견줄 이가 하나도 없었어요, 우리가 열여섯 명 무리였는데도요."], ["Luckily there is no distinction of dress nowadays to tell tales, but—but—but Yours affectionately.”", "다행히 요즘은 속내를 드러낼 옷차림의 차등이 없지만, 하지만—하지만—하지만 애정을 담아.”"]], [["“I had almost forgot (it was Edmund’s fault: he gets into my head more than does me good) one very material thing I had to say from Henry and myself—I mean about our taking you back into Northamptonshire.", "“깜빡 잊을 뻔했네요(에드먼드 탓이에요, 그이가 내게 이로운 것보다 더 자꾸 머릿속에 떠올라서요) 헨리와 나한테서 전할 자못 요긴한 것 하나를요—우리가 그쪽을 노샘프턴셔로 데려가는 것 말이에요."], ["My dear little creature, do not stay at Portsmouth to lose your pretty looks.", "사랑하는 어린것, 포츠머스에 남아 그 어여쁜 낯빛을 잃지 말아요."], ["Those vile sea-breezes are the ruin of beauty and health.", "그 고약한 바닷바람은 아름다움과 건강을 망쳐요."], ["My poor aunt always felt affected if within ten miles of the sea, which the Admiral of course never believed, but I know it was so.", "우리 딱한 숙모는 바다 십 마일 안이면 늘 몸이 안 좋았거든요, 제독이야 물론 결코 안 믿었지만, 나는 정말 그랬다는 걸 알아요."], ["I am at your service and Henry’s, at an hour’s notice.", "한 시간 전 통지면 나는 그쪽과 헨리를 위해 대령이에요."], ["I should like the scheme, and we would make a little circuit, and shew you Everingham in our way, and perhaps you would not mind passing through London, and seeing the inside of St.", "나는 그 계획이 마음에 들 거예요, 그리고 우리가 조금 에둘러, 가는 길에 그쪽한테 에버링엄을 보여 줄 거예요. 어쩌면 그쪽도 런던을 지나 하노버 스퀘어의 세인트 조지 교회 안을 구경하는 걸 마다하지 않겠죠."], ["George’s, Hanover Square.", "다만 그럴 때 그쪽 사촌 에드먼드는 내게서 떼어 놔요."], ["Only keep your cousin Edmund from me at such a time: I should not like to be tempted.", "나는 유혹받고 싶지 않으니까요."], ["What a long letter!", "편지가 어찌 이리 긴지!"], ["one word more.", "한마디만 더요."], ["Henry, I find, has some idea of going into Norfolk again upon some business that you approve; but this cannot possibly be permitted before the middle of next week; that is, he cannot anyhow be spared till after the 14th, for we have a party that evening.", "헨리가, 알고 보니, 그쪽이 찬동하는 무슨 볼일로 다시 노퍽에 갈까 하는 생각이 좀 있대요. 하지만 이건 다음 주 중반 전엔 도무지 허락될 수 없어요."], ["The value of a man like Henry, on such an occasion, is what you can have no conception of; so you must take it upon my word to be inestimable.", "그러니까, 그이는 14일 전엔 어떻게도 뺄 수 없거든요, 우리가 그날 저녁 모임이 있으니까요. 이런 자리에서 헨리 같은 남자의 값어치란 그쪽이 도무지 짐작도 못 할 것이니, 이루 헤아릴 수 없다는 내 말을 믿을밖에요."], ["He will see the Rushworths, which I own I am not sorry for—having a little curiosity, and so I think has he—though he will not acknowledge it.”", "그이가 러시워스 내외를 볼 텐데, 나야 그게 싫지 않다고 인정해요—얼마간 궁금하거든요, 그이도 그렇지 싶고요—그이야 인정 안 하겠지만.”"]], [["This was a letter to be run through eagerly, to be read deliberately, to supply matter for much reflection, and to leave everything in greater suspense than ever.", "이것은 못내 죽 훑어보고, 찬찬히 읽고, 곰곰 헤아릴 거리를 잔뜩 주며, 온갖 것을 여느 때보다 더 큰 마음 졸임 속에 남겨 두는 편지였다."], ["The only certainty to be drawn from it was, that nothing decisive had yet taken place.", "거기서 이끌어 낼 유일한 확실함은, 아직 결판나는 일이 아무것도 없었다는 것이었다."], ["Edmund had not yet spoken.", "에드먼드는 아직 말을 안 꺼낸 것이다."], ["How Miss Crawford really felt, how she meant to act, or might act without or against her meaning; whether his importance to her were quite what it had been before the last separation; whether, if lessened, it were likely to lessen more, or to recover itself, were subjects for endless conjecture, and to be thought of on that day and many days to come, without producing any conclusion.", "크로퍼드 양이 정말 어떻게 느끼는지, 어떻게 처신하려 하는지, 아니면 제 뜻과 상관없이 또는 그에 거슬러 어떻게 처신할지, 그가 그녀에게 중한 것이 지난번 헤어짐 전과 딱 같은지, 만일 덜해졌다면 더 덜해질 성싶은지 아니면 다시 회복될 성싶은지—이런 것이 끝없는 짐작거리였고, 그날에도 앞으로 올 여러 날에도 아무 결론도 못 낸 채 헤아려질 것이었다."], ["The idea that returned the oftenest was that Miss Crawford, after proving herself cooled and staggered by a return to London habits, would yet prove herself in the end too much attached to him to give him up.", "가장 자주 되돌아오는 생각은, 크로퍼드 양이 런던 생활로 돌아가 저가 식고 흔들렸음을 드러낸 끝에도, 끝내는 그를 단념하기엔 그에게 너무 애착함을 드러내리라는 것이었다."], ["She would try to be more ambitious than her heart would allow.", "그녀는 제 마음이 허락하는 것보다 더 야심 차려 애쓰리라."], ["She would hesitate, she would tease, she would condition, she would require a great deal, but she would finally accept.", "그녀는 머뭇거리고, 애태우고, 조건을 걸고, 많은 것을 요구하되, 끝내 받아들이리라."]], [["This was Fanny’s most frequent expectation.", "이것이 패니의 가장 잦은 짐작이었다."], ["A house in town—that, she thought, must be impossible.", "시내의 집—그것은, 그녀 생각에, 어림없을 게 틀림없었다."], ["Yet there was no saying what Miss Crawford might not ask.", "그러나 크로퍼드 양이 무엇을 요구 안 할지야 알 길이 없었다."], ["The prospect for her cousin grew worse and worse.", "사촌의 앞날이 점점 더 나빠졌다."], ["The woman who could speak of him, and speak only of his appearance!", "그를 두고, 오직 그의 풍채만을 말할 수 있는 여자라니!"], ["What an unworthy attachment!", "이 얼마나 값없는 애착인가!"], ["To be deriving support from the commendations of Mrs. Fraser!", "프레이저 부인의 칭찬에서 힘을 얻다니!"], ["She who had known him intimately half a year!", "그를 반년이나 가까이 알아 온 그녀가!"], ["Fanny was ashamed of her.", "패니는 그녀가 부끄러웠다."], ["Those parts of the letter which related only to Mr. Crawford and herself, touched her, in comparison, slightly.", "오직 크로퍼드 씨와 저 자신에 관한 편지의 그 대목들은, 견주어 보면 그녀를 얕게 건드렸다."], ["Whether Mr. Crawford went into Norfolk before or after the 14th was certainly no concern of hers, though, everything considered, she thought he would go without delay.", "크로퍼드 씨가 14일 전에 노퍽에 가든 뒤에 가든 분명 그녀가 상관할 바 아니었으나, 온갖 것을 헤아려 보면 그녀는 그가 지체 없이 가리라 여겼다."], ["That Miss Crawford should endeavour to secure a meeting between him and Mrs. Rushworth, was all in her worst line of conduct, and grossly unkind and ill-judged; but she hoped he would not be actuated by any such degrading curiosity.", "크로퍼드 양이 그와 러시워스 부인의 만남을 주선하려 애쓴다는 것은, 죄다 그녀의 가장 나쁜 처신의 결이었고, 몹시 야박하고 판단 그른 것이었다."], ["He acknowledged no such inducement, and his sister ought to have given him credit for better feelings than her own.", "그러나 그녀는 그가 그런 천한 궁금함에 움직이지 않기를 바랐다. 그는 그런 부추김을 인정하지 않았고, 그 누이는 제 것보다 더 나은 심경을 그에게 인정해 주었어야 했다."]], [["She was yet more impatient for another letter from town after receiving this than she had been before; and for a few days was so unsettled by it altogether, by what had come, and what might come, that her usual readings and conversation with Susan were much suspended.", "이 편지를 받고 나니 그녀는 앞서보다 더욱, 시내에서 또 한 통의 편지가 오기를 애타게 기다렸다. 그리고 며칠 동안은 그것으로, 온 것과 올지 모를 것으로 하도 마음이 어지러워, 여느 수전과의 책 읽기와 얘기가 자못 뜸해졌다."], ["She could not command her attention as she wished.", "그녀는 바라는 만큼 제 마음을 다잡을 수 없었다. 만일 크로퍼드 씨가 사촌한테 전하라던 제 말을 기억한다면, 그가 무슨 일이 있어도 저한테 편지하리라는 것이 자못 그럴싸하다, 더없이 그럴싸하다 그녀는 여겼다."], ["If Mr.", "그것이 그의 여느 친절과 더없이 걸맞을 터였다."], ["Crawford remembered her message to her cousin, she thought it very likely, most likely, that he would write to her at all events; it would be most consistent with his usual kindness; and till she got rid of this idea, till it gradually wore off, by no letters appearing in the course of three or four days more, she was in a most restless, anxious state.", "그리고 이 생각을 떨쳐 내기까지, 사나흘이 더 지나도 아무 편지가 안 나타나는 덕에 그것이 차츰 닳아 없어지기까지, 그녀는 더없이 안절부절, 애타는 상태에 있었다."]], [["At length, a something like composure succeeded.", "마침내, 평온 비슷한 것이 뒤이었다."], ["Suspense must be submitted to, and must not be allowed to wear her out, and make her useless.", "마음 졸임은 받아들여야 했고, 그것이 저를 지치게 해 쓸모없이 만들도록 놔둬선 안 되었다."], ["Time did something, her own exertions something more, and she resumed her attentions to Susan, and again awakened the same interest in them.", "시간이 얼마간 해 주었고, 저 자신의 애씀이 얼마간 더 해 주었다. 그리하여 그녀는 수전에 대한 마음씀을 다시 이어, 다시금 그 안에 같은 관심을 일깨웠다."]], [["Susan was growing very fond of her, and though without any of the early delight in books which had been so strong in Fanny, with a disposition much less inclined to sedentary pursuits, or to information for information’s sake, she had so strong a desire of not appearing ignorant, as, with a good clear understanding, made her a most attentive, profitable, thankful pupil.", "수전은 그녀를 무척 좋아하게 되어 갔다. 그리고 패니에게 그토록 강했던 어릴 적의 책에 대한 그 즐거움은 조금도 없고, 앉아서 하는 일이나 앎을 위한 앎에는 훨씬 덜 기우는 성미였으되, 그녀는 무지해 보이지 않으려는 마음이 하도 강해, 좋고 맑은 슬기와 더불어 더없이 마음 쏟고 얻는 것 많고 고마워하는 학생이 되었다."], ["Fanny was her oracle.", "패니는 그녀의 신탁이었다."], ["Fanny’s explanations and remarks were a most important addition to every essay, or every chapter of history.", "패니의 설명과 소견은 어느 논설이든, 어느 역사의 장이든 더없이 요긴한 보탬이었다."], ["What Fanny told her of former times dwelt more on her mind than the pages of Goldsmith; and she paid her sister the compliment of preferring her style to that of any printed author.", "패니가 옛날 얘기를 들려준 것이 골드스미스의 책장보다 그녀 마음에 더 오래 남았다. 그리고 그녀는 인쇄된 어느 작가의 문체보다 언니의 문체를 더 좋아하는 것으로 언니에게 찬사를 바쳤다."], ["The early habit of reading was wanting.", "어릴 적 책 읽는 버릇이 없었던 것이다."]], [["Their conversations, however, were not always on subjects so high as history or morals.", "그러나 그들의 이야기가 늘 역사나 도덕만큼 높은 화제인 것은 아니었다."], ["Others had their hour; and of lesser matters, none returned so often, or remained so long between them, as Mansfield Park, a description of the people, the manners, the amusements, the ways of Mansfield Park.", "다른 것들도 저마다의 때가 있었다. 그리고 좀 더 사소한 것 가운데, 맨스필드 파크—그 사람들, 그 태도, 그 즐길 거리, 맨스필드 파크의 방식에 대한 묘사만큼 자주 되돌아오거나 둘 사이에 오래 머무는 것은 없었다."], ["Susan, who had an innate taste for the genteel and well-appointed, was eager to hear, and Fanny could not but indulge herself in dwelling on so beloved a theme.", "점잖고 잘 갖춰진 것에 대한 타고난 취향이 있던 수전은 못내 듣고 싶어 했고, 패니는 그토록 사랑하는 화제에 마음 두는 데 저를 내맡길밖에 없었다."], ["She hoped it was not wrong; though, after a time, Susan’s very great admiration of everything said or done in her uncle’s house, and earnest longing to go into Northamptonshire, seemed almost to blame her for exciting feelings which could not be gratified.", "그녀는 그것이 그른 짓이 아니기를 바랐다. 하기야 얼마 뒤, 삼촌 집에서 말해지거나 이뤄진 온갖 것에 대한 수전의 그 자못 큰 감탄과, 노샘프턴셔에 가고픈 간절한 바람은, 채워질 수 없는 심경을 자아냈다며 거의 그녀를 나무라는 듯도 했지만."]], [["Poor Susan was very little better fitted for home than her elder sister; and as Fanny grew thoroughly to understand this, she began to feel that when her own release from Portsmouth came, her happiness would have a material drawback in leaving Susan behind.", "딱한 수전도 언니 못지않게 집에 어울리지 못했다. 그리고 패니가 이것을 속속들이 헤아리게 되면서, 저 자신이 포츠머스에서 풀려날 때가 오면 제 행복에 수전을 뒤에 두고 가는 것이 큰 흠이 되리라 느끼기 시작했다."], ["That a girl so capable of being made everything good should be left in such hands, distressed her more and more.", "온갖 좋은 것이 될 만한 그 재능 있는 아이가 그런 손에 남겨져야 한다는 것이 그녀를 점점 더 괴롭혔다."], ["Were she likely to have a home to invite her to, what a blessing it would be!", "만일 그녀가 그 애를 청할 집을 갖게 될 성싶다면, 그 얼마나 큰 복이랴!"], ["And had it been possible for her to return Mr. Crawford’s regard, the probability of his being very far from objecting to such a measure would have been the greatest increase of all her own comforts.", "그리고 만일 그녀가 크로퍼드 씨의 정에 화답하는 것이 있음 직한 일이었다면, 그가 그런 조처를 마다하기는커녕 아주 반길 성싶다는 것이 그녀 자신의 온갖 편안함에 가장 큰 보탬이 되었으리라."], ["She thought he was really good-tempered, and could fancy his entering into a plan of that sort most pleasantly.", "그녀는 그가 정말로 성미 좋은 사람이라 여겼고, 그가 그런 종류의 계획에 더없이 흔쾌히 나서는 것을 그려 볼 수 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLIV", "# 제44장"]], [["Seven weeks of the two months were very nearly gone, when the one letter, the letter from Edmund, so long expected, was put into Fanny’s hands.", "두 달 가운데 일곱 주가 거의 다 갔을 무렵, 그토록 오래 기다리던 그 한 통의 편지, 에드먼드의 편지가 패니의 손에 들어왔다."], ["As she opened, and saw its length, she prepared herself for a minute detail of happiness and a profusion of love and praise towards the fortunate creature who was now mistress of his fate.", "뜯어 그 길이를 보며, 그녀는 행복의 낱낱과, 이제 그의 운명의 임자가 된 그 운 좋은 이를 향한 넘치는 사랑과 칭찬에 대한 채비를 했다."], ["These were the contents—", "그 내용은 이러했다—"]], [["“My Dear Fanny,—Excuse me that I have not written before.", "“사랑하는 패니에게—진작 편지 못 한 걸 용서해 다오."], ["Crawford told me that you were wishing to hear from me, but I found it impossible to write from London, and persuaded myself that you would understand my silence.", "크로퍼드가 네가 내 소식을 듣고 싶어 한다고 일러 줬다만, 나는 런던에서는 도무지 쓸 수가 없더구나. 그리고 네가 내 잠잠함을 헤아려 주려니 스스로를 다독였지."], ["Could I have sent a few happy lines, they should not have been wanting, but nothing of that nature was ever in my power.", "행복한 몇 줄이라도 보낼 수 있었다면 그것을 아끼지 않았으련만, 그런 종류의 것은 아무것도 내 힘에 닿지 않았어."], ["I am returned to Mansfield in a less assured state than when I left it.", "나는 떠날 때보다 덜 확신하는 상태로 맨스필드에 돌아왔다."], ["My hopes are much weaker.", "내 바람은 자못 약해졌어."], ["You are probably aware of this already.", "너도 아마 이미 알고 있겠지."], ["So very fond of you as Miss Crawford is, it is most natural that she should tell you enough of her own feelings to furnish a tolerable guess at mine.", "크로퍼드 양이 너를 그토록 아끼니, 그녀가 제 심경을 너한테 넉넉히 얘기해 내 심경도 웬만큼 짐작하게 해 주는 게 더없이 자연스러운 일이니까."], ["I will not be prevented, however, from making my own communication.", "하지만 나는 그렇다 해서 내 얘기를 못 하진 않으련다."], ["Our confidences in you need not clash.", "너를 향한 우리 둘의 속내가 부딪힐 것 없어."], ["I ask no questions.", "나는 아무것도 안 물으마."], ["There is something soothing in the idea that we have the same friend, and that whatever unhappy differences of opinion may exist between us, we are united in our love of you.", "우리한테 같은 벗이 있다는 것, 그리고 우리 사이에 무슨 불행한 의견의 다름이 있을지언정 너를 사랑하는 데선 우리가 하나라는 그 생각에는 뭔가 위안이 있구나."], ["It will be a comfort to me to tell you how things now are, and what are my present plans, if plans I can be said to have.", "지금 형편이 어떤지, 그리고 내 지금 계획이—계획이랄 게 있다면—무엇인지 너한테 얘기하는 게 나한텐 위안이 될 게다."], ["I have been returned since Saturday.", "나는 토요일부터 돌아와 있어."], ["I was three weeks in London, and saw her (for London) very often.", "나는 런던에 세 주 있었고, (런던치고는) 그녀를 자못 자주 봤다."], ["I had every attention from the Frasers that could be reasonably expected.", "프레이저네에서 온당히 기대할 만한 온갖 마음씀을 다 받았고."], ["I dare say I was not reasonable in carrying with me hopes of an intercourse at all like that of Mansfield.", "정말이지 맨스필드에서 같은 어울림을 바라며 간 건 내가 온당치 못했나 보다."], ["It was her manner, however, rather than any unfrequency of meeting.", "하지만 그건 만남이 뜸해서라기보다 그녀의 태도였어."], ["Had she been different when I did see her, I should have made no complaint, but from the very first she was altered: my first reception was so unlike what I had hoped, that I had almost resolved on leaving London again directly.", "정작 봤을 때 그녀가 달랐다면 나야 아무 불평도 안 했겠지만, 맨 처음부터 그녀는 달라져 있었어. 첫 맞이가 내가 바라던 것과 하도 딴판이라, 나는 하마터면 곧장 런던을 다시 떠나기로 마음먹을 뻔했지."], ["I need not particularise.", "낱낱이 옮길 것도 없다."], ["You know the weak side of her character, and may imagine the sentiments and expressions which were torturing me.", "너는 그녀 성품의 약한 데를 알 테니, 나를 괴롭히던 그 말과 심경을 짐작할 수 있겠지."], ["She was in high spirits, and surrounded by those who were giving all the support of their own bad sense to her too lively mind.", "그녀는 자못 들떠 있었고, 저희 나쁜 분별의 온갖 뒷받침을 그녀의 너무 발랄한 마음에 대 주는 이들에 둘러싸여 있었어."], ["I do not like Mrs. Fraser.", "나는 프레이저 부인이 싫다."], ["She is a cold-hearted, vain woman, who has married entirely from convenience, and though evidently unhappy in her marriage, places her disappointment not to faults of judgment, or temper, or disproportion of age, but to her being, after all, less affluent than many of her acquaintance, especially than her sister, Lady Stornaway, and is the determined supporter of everything mercenary and ambitious, provided it be only mercenary and ambitious enough.", "그 여자는 냉정하고 허영에 찬 여자로, 온전히 편의로 시집간 데다, 제 혼인이 분명 불행하면서도 그 실망을 판단이나 성미의 흠이나 나이의 안 맞음이 아니라, 결국 저가 여러 지인, 특히 그 언니 스토너웨이 부인보다 덜 넉넉한 탓으로 돌리며, 돈만 되고 야심 차기만 하다면 무엇이든 그 돈 밝히고 야심 찬 것을 굳게 편드는 여자야."], ["I look upon her intimacy with those two sisters as the greatest misfortune of her life and mine.", "나는 그녀가 그 두 자매와 가깝게 지내는 것을 그녀 삶과 내 삶의 가장 큰 불운으로 여긴다."], ["They have been leading her astray for years.", "그들은 여러 해 그녀를 잘못 이끌어 왔어."], ["Could she be detached from them!—and sometimes I do not despair of it, for the affection appears to me principally on their side.", "그녀가 그들한테서 떨어질 수만 있다면!—그리고 나는 이따금 그것을 단념하지 않는데, 그 애정이 내가 보기엔 주로 그들 편에 있는 듯하기 때문이야. 그들은 그녀를 무척 아껴."], ["They are very fond of her; but I am sure she does not love them as she loves you.", "하지만 나는 그녀가 너를 사랑하는 만큼 그들을 사랑하진 않는다고 확신해."], ["When I think of her great attachment to you, indeed, and the whole of her judicious, upright conduct as a sister, she appears a very different creature, capable of everything noble, and I am ready to blame myself for a too harsh construction of a playful manner.", "정말이지 너를 향한 그녀의 그 큰 애착과, 누이로서 보인 그 온전히 사려 깊고 올곧은 처신을 떠올리면, 그녀는 온갖 고귀한 것을 할 수 있는 아주 딴사람으로 보이고, 나는 장난스러운 태도를 너무 모질게 새긴 나 자신을 나무랄 마음마저 든다."], ["I cannot give her up, Fanny.", "나는 그녀를 단념할 수 없어, 패니."], ["She is the only woman in the world whom I could ever think of as a wife.", "그녀는 이 세상에서 내가 아내로 여길 수 있는 유일한 여자야."], ["If I did not believe that she had some regard for me, of course I should not say this, but I do believe it.", "그녀가 나를 얼마간 마음에 두고 있다 믿지 않았다면 물론 이런 말은 안 했겠지만, 나는 정말 그리 믿는다."], ["I am convinced that she is not without a decided preference.", "나는 그녀가 뚜렷한 마음이 없진 않다고 확신해."], ["I have no jealousy of any individual.", "나는 어떤 한 사람을 두고 질투하는 게 아니야."], ["It is the influence of the fashionable world altogether that I am jealous of.", "내가 질투하는 건 그 유행하는 세상 전체의 물듦이야."], ["It is the habits of wealth that I fear.", "내가 두려운 건 재물의 버릇이고."], ["Her ideas are not higher than her own fortune may warrant, but they are beyond what our incomes united could authorise.", "그녀의 생각은 제 재산이 감당할 만한 것보다 높진 않아, 다만 우리 수입을 합친 것으로 뒷받침할 수 있는 것을 넘어서지."], ["There is comfort, however, even here.", "하지만 여기에도 위안은 있어."], ["I could better bear to lose her because not rich enough, than because of my profession.", "나는 그녀를 내 직업 탓에 잃느니, 넉넉지 못한 탓에 잃는 걸 차라리 더 잘 견딜 수 있거든."], ["That would only prove her affection not equal to sacrifices, which, in fact, I am scarcely justified in asking; and, if I am refused, that, I think, will be the honest motive.", "앞엣것은 그녀의 정이 희생만큼 크지 못함을 증명할 뿐일 텐데, 실은 내가 그런 희생을 청할 자격도 좀처럼 없고. 그리고 만일 내가 거절당한다면, 그게, 내 생각엔, 정직한 이유일 거야."], ["Her prejudices, I trust, are not so strong as they were.", "그녀의 편견은, 나는 믿는데, 예전만큼 세진 않아."], ["You have my thoughts exactly as they arise, my dear Fanny; perhaps they are sometimes contradictory, but it will not be a less faithful picture of my mind.", "사랑하는 패니, 너는 내 생각을 그 이는 그대로 다 받는 셈이다. 어쩌면 이따금 앞뒤가 안 맞겠지만, 그렇다 해서 내 마음의 덜 참된 그림은 아닐 게야."], ["Having once begun, it is a pleasure to me to tell you all I feel. I cannot give her up.", "일단 시작하고 보니, 내가 느끼는 걸 죄다 너한테 얘기하는 게 나한텐 즐겁구나."], ["Connected as we already are, and, I hope, are to be, to give up Mary Crawford would be to give up the society of some of those most dear to me; to banish myself from the very houses and friends whom, under any other distress, I should turn to for consolation.", "나는 그녀를 단념할 수 없어. 우리가 이미 이어져 있고, 또 그러기를 바라는 마당이니, 메리 크로퍼드를 단념한다는 건 내게 가장 소중한 몇몇의 사귐을 단념하는 것이요, 다른 어떤 괴로움에서든 위안을 얻으러 찾아갈 바로 그 집들과 벗들에게서 나 자신을 쫓아내는 일이 될 게다."], ["The loss of Mary I must consider as comprehending the loss of Crawford and of Fanny.", "나는 메리를 잃는 것을 크로퍼드와 패니를 잃는 것까지 아우르는 것으로 여길밖에 없어."], ["Were it a decided thing, an actual refusal, I hope I should know how to bear it, and how to endeavour to weaken her hold on my heart, and in the course of a few years—but I am writing nonsense.", "그게 결판난 일이라면, 정말 거절이라면, 나는 그것을 견디는 법을, 그리고 그녀가 내 마음을 붙든 힘을 약하게 하려 애쓰는 법을, 그리고 몇 해가 지나면—하지만 나는 실없는 소릴 쓰고 있구나. 거절당한다면 나는 견뎌야겠지."], ["Were I refused, I must bear it; and till I am, I can never cease to try for her.", "그리고 거절당하기까지, 나는 결코 그녀를 얻으려는 걸 그칠 수 없다."], ["This is the truth.", "이게 진실이야."], ["The only question is how?", "남은 물음은 오직 어떻게 하느냐지."], ["What may be the likeliest means?", "무엇이 가장 그럴싸한 방편일까?"], ["I have sometimes thought of going to London again after Easter, and sometimes resolved on doing nothing till she returns to Mansfield.", "나는 이따금 부활절 뒤 다시 런던에 갈까 생각했고, 이따금 그녀가 맨스필드로 돌아올 때까지 아무것도 안 하기로 마음먹었어."], ["Even now, she speaks with pleasure of being in Mansfield in June; but June is at a great distance, and I believe I shall write to her.", "지금도 그녀는 유월에 맨스필드에 있을 걸 즐겁게 얘기해. 하지만 유월은 아직 멀고, 아무래도 나는 그녀한테 편지를 쓸 것 같다."], ["I have nearly determined on explaining myself by letter.", "나는 편지로 내 뜻을 밝히기로 거의 마음을 정했어."], ["To be at an early certainty is a material object.", "일찍 확실함에 이르는 게 요긴한 일이니까."], ["My present state is miserably irksome.", "내 지금 처지는 비참하리만치 진력나."], ["Considering everything, I think a letter will be decidedly the best method of explanation.", "온갖 것을 헤아려 보면, 편지가 뜻을 밝히는 데 단연 가장 좋은 방편일 것 같다."], ["I shall be able to write much that I could not say, and shall be giving her time for reflection before she resolves on her answer, and I am less afraid of the result of reflection than of an immediate hasty impulse; I think I am.", "나는 말로는 못 할 것을 많이 쓸 수 있을 테고, 그녀가 답을 정하기 전에 곰곰 생각할 겨를을 줄 테니까. 그리고 나는 문득 서두르는 충동의 결말보다 곰곰 생각한 결말이 덜 두렵거든."], ["My greatest danger would lie in her consulting Mrs.", "그런 것 같아."], ["Fraser, and I at a distance unable to help my own cause.", "내 가장 큰 위험은 그녀가 프레이저 부인과 의논하고, 내가 멀리 떨어져 내 편을 돕지 못하는 데 있겠지."], ["A letter exposes to all the evil of consultation, and where the mind is anything short of perfect decision, an adviser may, in an unlucky moment, lead it to do what it may afterwards regret.", "편지란 의논의 온갖 화에 노출되는 것이고, 마음이 온전한 결심에 조금이라도 못 미치는 데선, 조언자가 하필 나쁜 순간에 나중에 뉘우칠 짓을 하도록 이끌 수도 있으니까."], ["I must think this matter over a little.", "이 일은 조금 더 곰곰 생각해 봐야겠다."], ["This long letter, full of my own concerns alone, will be enough to tire even the friendship of a Fanny.", "온통 내 걱정거리로만 가득한 이 긴 편지는 패니의 우정마저 지치게 하기에 넉넉하겠지."], ["The last time I saw Crawford was at Mrs. Fraser’s party.", "크로퍼드를 마지막 본 건 프레이저 부인의 모임에서였어."], ["I am more and more satisfied with all that I see and hear of him.", "나는 그에 대해 보고 듣는 온갖 것에 점점 더 흡족해."], ["There is not a shadow of wavering.", "흔들림의 그림자 하나 없어."], ["He thoroughly knows his own mind, and acts up to his resolutions: an inestimable quality.", "그는 제 마음을 속속들이 알고, 제 결심대로 처신해. 값을 매길 수 없는 자질이지."], ["I could not see him and my eldest sister in the same room without recollecting what you once told me, and I acknowledge that they did not meet as friends.", "나는 그와 내 맏누이가 한방에 있는 걸 보며, 언젠가 네가 나한테 한 말을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었어. 그리고 인정하건대, 둘은 벗으로 만나진 않더구나."], ["There was marked coolness on her side.", "그녀 편에 뚜렷한 냉랭함이 있었어."], ["They scarcely spoke.", "둘은 좀처럼 말을 안 했지."], ["I saw him draw back surprised, and I was sorry that Mrs. Rushworth should resent any former supposed slight to Miss Bertram.", "나는 그가 놀라 물러서는 걸 봤고, 러시워스 부인이 예전에 버트럼 양한테 있었으려니 여기는 무슨 무시를 원망한다는 게 나는 서운했다."], ["You will wish to hear my opinion of Maria’s degree of comfort as a wife.", "너는 아내로서 마리아가 얼마나 편안한지 내 생각을 듣고 싶겠지."], ["There is no appearance of unhappiness.", "불행해 보이는 데는 없어."], ["I hope they get on pretty well together.", "나는 둘이 제법 잘 지내기를 바란다."], ["I dined twice in Wimpole Street, and might have been there oftener, but it is mortifying to be with Rushworth as a brother.", "나는 윔폴 스트리트에서 두 번 만찬을 들었고, 더 자주 갈 수도 있었지만, 러시워스와 처남으로 함께 있는 건 무안한 노릇이야."], ["Julia seems to enjoy London exceedingly.", "줄리아는 런던을 몹시 즐기는 듯하고."], ["I had little enjoyment there, but have less here.", "나는 거기서 즐거움이 별로 없었는데, 여기선 더 없구나."], ["We are not a lively party.", "우리는 발랄한 무리가 못 돼."], ["You are very much wanted.", "네가 무척 아쉬워."], ["I miss you more than I can express.", "나는 이루 말할 수 없이 너를 그리워한다."], ["My mother desires her best love, and hopes to hear from you soon.", "어머니가 더없는 사랑을 전하시며, 이내 네 소식을 듣기를 바라셔."], ["She talks of you almost every hour, and I am sorry to find how many weeks more she is likely to be without you.", "어머니는 거의 매시간 네 얘기를 하시고, 앞으로 몇 주나 더 너 없이 지내실 성싶은지를 아니 나도 서글프구나."], ["My father means to fetch you himself, but it will not be till after Easter, when he has business in town.", "아버지가 몸소 너를 데리러 갈 참이지만, 그건 부활절 뒤, 아버지가 시내에 볼일이 있을 때라야 될 거야."], ["You are happy at Portsmouth, I hope, but this must not be a yearly visit.", "너는 포츠머스에서 행복하기를 바란다만, 이건 해마다 하는 방문이 되어선 안 돼."], ["I want you at home, that I may have your opinion about Thornton Lacey.", "나는 손턴 레이시에 대한 네 의견을 듣게 네가 집에 있으면 싶어."], ["I have little heart for extensive improvements till I know that it will ever have a mistress.", "나는 그곳이 언제고 안주인을 갖게 될지 알기 전엔 크게 손볼 마음이 별로 안 나."], ["I think I shall certainly write.", "아무래도 나는 분명 편지를 쓸 것 같다."], ["It is quite settled that the Grants go to Bath; they leave Mansfield on Monday.", "그랜트 부부가 바스로 가는 건 아주 정해졌어. 월요일에 맨스필드를 떠나."], ["I am glad of it.", "나야 그게 반갑다."], ["I am not comfortable enough to be fit for anybody; but your aunt seems to feel out of luck that such an article of Mansfield news should fall to my pen instead of hers.—Yours ever, my dearest Fanny.”", "나는 누구한테든 어울릴 만큼 마음이 편치 못하니까. 하지만 네 이모는, 맨스필드 소식의 이런 요긴한 대목이 당신 펜 대신 내 펜에 떨어진 걸 운수 없게 여기시는 눈치야.—언제나 네 것인, 사랑하는 패니.”"]], [["“I never will, no, I certainly never will wish for a letter again,”", "“나는 결코, 아니, 나는 분명 다시는 편지를 바라지 않을 거야.”"], ["was Fanny’s secret declaration as she finished this.", "이것이 이 편지를 다 읽으며 패니가 속으로 한 다짐이었다."], ["“What do they bring but disappointment and sorrow?", "“편지가 실망과 슬픔 말고 뭘 가져다준담?"], ["Not till after Easter!", "부활절 뒤에나라니!"], ["How shall I bear it?", "어떻게 견디지?"], ["And my poor aunt talking of me every hour!”", "게다가 딱한 이모가 매시간 내 얘기를 하신다니!”"]], [["Fanny checked the tendency of these thoughts as well as she could, but she was within half a minute of starting the idea that Sir Thomas was quite unkind, both to her aunt and to herself.", "패니는 이런 생각의 쏠림을 되도록 다잡았으나, 토머스 경이 이모에게도 저 자신에게도 자못 야박하다는 생각을 떠올리기 반 분 전까지 갔다."], ["As for the main subject of the letter, there was nothing in that to soothe irritation.", "편지의 주된 화제로 말하면, 거기엔 짜증을 다독일 것이 아무것도 없었다."], ["She was almost vexed into displeasure and anger against Edmund.", "그녀는 에드먼드에게 언짢음과 노여움이 일 만큼 거의 부아가 났다."], ["“There is no good in this delay,”", "“이 미룸엔 좋을 게 없어.”"], ["said she.", "그녀는 뇌었다."], ["“Why is not it settled?", "“왜 정하지 않는담?"], ["He is blinded, and nothing will open his eyes; nothing can, after having had truths before him so long in vain.", "그이는 눈이 멀었고, 무엇으로도 그 눈을 못 뜨게 해. 그토록 오래 진실을 헛되이 눈앞에 뒀는데도, 무엇으로도 못 하지."], ["He will marry her, and be poor and miserable.", "그이는 그녀와 혼인해 가난하고 비참해질 거야."], ["God grant that her influence do not make him cease to be respectable!”", "하느님, 그녀의 물듦이 그이를 어엿하지 못하게 만들지만 말게 하소서!”"], ["She looked over the letter again.", "그녀는 편지를 다시 훑어보았다."], ["“‘So very fond of me!’ ’tis nonsense all.", "“‘나를 그토록 아끼니!’ 죄다 실없는 소리야."], ["She loves nobody but herself and her brother.", "그녀는 저 자신과 제 오라비 말고는 아무도 사랑 안 해."], ["Her friends leading her astray for years!", "그녀의 벗들이 여러 해 그녀를 잘못 이끌었다니!"], ["She is quite as likely to have led them astray.", "그녀가 그들을 잘못 이끌었을 가망도 딱 그만큼 크지."], ["They have all, perhaps, been corrupting one another; but if they are so much fonder of her than she is of them, she is the less likely to have been hurt, except by their flattery.", "어쩌면 그들 다 서로를 물들여 왔을 테지만, 만일 그녀가 그들을 아끼는 것보다 그들이 그녀를 그토록 더 아낀다면, 그녀는 그들의 아첨 말고는 상처받았을 가망이 덜하지."], ["‘The only woman in the world whom he could ever think of as a wife.’ I firmly believe it.", "‘이 세상에서 그이가 아내로 여길 수 있는 유일한 여자.’ 나는 굳게 믿어."], ["It is an attachment to govern his whole life.", "그건 그이의 온 삶을 다스릴 애착이야."], ["Accepted or refused, his heart is wedded to her for ever.", "받아들여지든 거절당하든, 그이 마음은 영영 그녀와 맺어졌어."], ["‘The loss of Mary I must consider as comprehending the loss of Crawford and Fanny.’ Edmund, you do not know me.", "‘나는 메리를 잃는 것을 크로퍼드와 패니를 잃는 것까지 아우르는 것으로 여길밖에 없어.’ 에드먼드 오빠, 오빤 나를 몰라요."], ["The families would never be connected if you did not connect them!", "오빠가 잇지 않는다면 그 집안들은 결코 이어지지 않을 텐데!"], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["write, write.", "쓰세요, 쓰세요."], ["Finish it at once.", "당장 끝내 버려요."], ["Let there be an end of this suspense.", "이 마음 졸임을 끝내요."], ["Fix, commit, condemn yourself.”", "정하고, 매이고, 스스로에게 벌을 내리세요.”"]], [["Such sensations, however, were too near akin to resentment to be long guiding Fanny’s soliloquies.", "그러나 이런 심경은 너무나 원망에 가까운 것이라 패니의 혼잣말을 오래 이끌지 못했다."], ["She was soon more softened and sorrowful.", "그녀는 이내 한결 누그러지고 서글퍼졌다."], ["His warm regard, his kind expressions, his confidential treatment, touched her strongly.", "그의 뜨거운 마음, 그의 다정한 말, 그의 속내를 터놓는 대우가 그녀를 세차게 건드렸다."], ["He was only too good to everybody.", "그는 다들에게 너무 좋기만 한 사람이었다."], ["It was a letter, in short, which she would not but have had for the world, and which could never be valued enough.", "요컨대 그것은 무엇을 준대도 그녀가 안 받았을 리 없는 편지, 아무리 값매겨도 모자랄 편지였다."], ["This was the end of it.", "이것이 그 끝이었다."]], [["Everybody at all addicted to letter-writing, without having much to say, which will include a large proportion of the female world at least, must feel with Lady Bertram that she was out of luck in having such a capital piece of Mansfield news as the certainty of the Grants going to Bath, occur at a time when she could make no advantage of it, and will admit that it must have been very mortifying to her to see it fall to the share of her thankless son, and treated as concisely as possible at the end of a long letter, instead of having it to spread over the largest part of a page of her own.", "할 말은 별로 없으면서 편지 쓰기에 어지간히 빠진 이라면 누구나—적어도 여자들 세상의 큰 몫이 여기 들 텐데—버트럼 부인이, 그랜트 부부가 바스로 가는 것이 확실하다는 그런 요긴한 맨스필드 소식 한 대목이 하필 저가 그것을 써먹을 수 없는 때에 벌어진 것을 운수 없게 여긴 심정에 공감할 게 틀림없다. 그리고 그것이, 저 자신의 한 쪽의 가장 큰 부분에 펼쳐 쓸 거리가 되는 대신, 그 고마움 모르는 아들의 몫으로 떨어져 긴 편지 끝에 되도록 짤막하게 다뤄지는 것을 보는 것이 그녀에게 자못 무안한 노릇이었으리라는 것도 인정하리라."], ["For though Lady Bertram rather shone in the epistolary line, having early in her marriage, from the want of other employment, and the circumstance of Sir Thomas’s being in Parliament, got into the way of making and keeping correspondents, and formed for herself a very creditable, common-place, amplifying style, so that a very little matter was enough for her: she could not do entirely without any; she must have something to write about, even to her niece; and being so soon to lose all the benefit of Dr.", "버트럼 부인은 편지 쓰는 방면에서 오히려 좀 빛났으니, 혼인 초에, 달리 할 일이 없고 토머스 경이 의회에 있던 사정으로, 편지 벗을 만들고 지키는 데 들어, 저 나름의 자못 어엿하고 판에 박힌, 부풀리는 문체를 지어 내, 아주 자그마한 거리만으로도 넉넉했던 것이다. 그래도 그녀는 아무 거리 없이는 도무지 못 했다."], ["Grant’s gouty symptoms and Mrs.", "조카딸에게조차 쓸 무언가가 있어야 했다."], ["Grant’s morning calls, it was very hard upon her to be deprived of one of the last epistolary uses she could put them to.", "그런데 이내 그랜트 박사의 통풍 증세와 그랜트 부인의 아침 방문이라는 그 온갖 덕을 잃게 될 참이라, 그것들에 써먹을 수 있는 마지막 편지용 쓰임 하나를 빼앗기는 것이 그녀에겐 자못 딱한 노릇이었다."]], [["There was a rich amends, however, preparing for her.", "그러나 그녀에게는 넉넉한 보상이 마련되고 있었다."], ["Lady Bertram’s hour of good luck came.", "버트럼 부인의 운수 좋은 때가 온 것이다."], ["Within a few days from the receipt of Edmund’s letter, Fanny had one from her aunt, beginning thus—", "에드먼드의 편지를 받은 지 며칠 안 되어, 패니는 이모에게서 이렇게 시작하는 편지를 한 통 받았다—"]], [["“My Dear Fanny,—I take up my pen to communicate some very alarming intelligence, which I make no doubt will give you much concern”.", "“사랑하는 패니에게—나는 자못 놀라운 소식을 전하려 펜을 든다. 그게 너한테 큰 걱정을 안기리라는 걸 나는 의심치 않는다.”"]], [["This was a great deal better than to have to take up the pen to acquaint her with all the particulars of the Grants’ intended journey, for the present intelligence was of a nature to promise occupation for the pen for many days to come, being no less than the dangerous illness of her eldest son, of which they had received notice by express a few hours before.", "이것은 그랜트 부부가 잡은 여정의 온갖 낱낱을 알리려 펜을 드는 것보다 훨씬 나았다. 지금 소식은 앞으로 여러 날 펜에 일거리를 약속하는 성질의 것이었으니, 다름 아닌 맏아들의 위중한 병이었고, 몇 시간 전 속달로 그 기별을 받은 것이었다."]], [["Tom had gone from London with a party of young men to Newmarket, where a neglected fall and a good deal of drinking had brought on a fever; and when the party broke up, being unable to move, had been left by himself at the house of one of these young men to the comforts of sickness and solitude, and the attendance only of servants.", "톰은 젊은이 무리와 함께 런던에서 뉴마켓으로 갔는데, 거기서 소홀히 넘긴 낙마와 자못 많은 술이 열병을 불러온 것이다. 그리고 무리가 흩어질 때, 움직일 수 없던 터라, 이 젊은이들 가운데 하나의 집에 홀로 남겨져, 병과 외로움의 그 편안함과, 오직 하인들의 시중에 내맡겨졌다."], ["Instead of being soon well enough to follow his friends, as he had then hoped, his disorder increased considerably, and it was not long before he thought so ill of himself as to be as ready as his physician to have a letter despatched to Mansfield.", "그때 바란 대로 이내 나아 벗들을 뒤따르기는커녕, 그의 병은 자못 깊어졌고, 오래지 않아 스스로도 몹시 위중하다 여겨, 제 의사 못지않게 맨스필드로 편지를 부칠 채비가 되었다."]], [["“This distressing intelligence, as you may suppose,” observed her ladyship, after giving the substance of it, “has agitated us exceedingly, and we cannot prevent ourselves from being greatly alarmed and apprehensive for the poor invalid, whose state Sir Thomas fears may be very critical; and Edmund kindly proposes attending his brother immediately, but I am happy to add that Sir Thomas will not leave me on this distressing occasion, as it would be too trying for me. We shall greatly miss Edmund in our small circle, but I trust and hope he will find the poor invalid in a less alarming state than might be apprehended, and that he will be able to bring him to Mansfield shortly, which Sir Thomas proposes should be done, and thinks best on every account, and I flatter myself the poor sufferer will soon be able to bear the removal without material inconvenience or injury. As I have little doubt of your feeling for us, my dear Fanny, under these distressing circumstances, I will write again very soon.”", "“이 괴로운 소식은, 너도 짐작하다시피,” 마님이 그 알맹이를 전한 뒤 뇌었다. “우리를 몹시 마음 졸이게 했다. 그리고 우리는 그 딱한 병자 걱정에 크게 놀라고 두려워하지 않을 수 없구나. 토머스 경은 그 애 상태가 자못 위태로울까 두려워하신다. 그리고 에드먼드가 친절하게도 당장 제 형을 보살피러 가겠다는데, 그래도 나는 이 말을 기쁘게 덧붙이마, 토머스 경은 이 괴로운 때에 나를 두고 떠나진 않으신다는 걸, 그게 나한텐 너무 힘든 일일 테니까. 우리 자그마한 무리에서 에드먼드가 몹시 아쉽겠지만, 나는 그 애가 그 딱한 병자를 두려워하던 것보단 덜 놀라운 상태로 보게 되기를, 그리고 이내 그 애를 맨스필드로 데려올 수 있기를 믿고 바란다. 토머스 경도 그리하자 하시고, 어느 모로 보나 그게 낫다 여기시고, 나도 그 딱한 이가 이내 큰 불편이나 해 없이 그 옮김을 견딜 수 있으려니 스스로 좋게 여기고 있어. 사랑하는 패니, 이 괴로운 사정에 네가 우리를 안쓰러워하리라는 걸 나는 거의 의심치 않으니, 이내 다시 쓰마.”"]], [["Fanny’s feelings on the occasion were indeed considerably more warm and genuine than her aunt’s style of writing.", "이 일에 대한 패니의 심경은 정말이지 이모의 글투보다 훨씬 더 뜨겁고 참되었다. 그녀는 그들 모두를 진심으로 안쓰러워했다."], ["She felt truly for them all. Tom dangerously ill, Edmund gone to attend him, and the sadly small party remaining at Mansfield, were cares to shut out every other care, or almost every other.", "톰이 위중하고, 에드먼드가 그를 보살피러 가고, 서글피 단출해진 무리가 맨스필드에 남았다는 것이, 다른 온갖 걱정을, 아니 거의 온갖 걱정을 몰아낼 만한 근심거리였다."], ["She could just find selfishness enough to wonder whether Edmund had written to Miss Crawford before this summons came, but no sentiment dwelt long with her that was not purely affectionate and disinterestedly anxious.", "그녀는 겨우, 이 부름이 오기 전에 에드먼드가 크로퍼드 양에게 편지를 썼을지 궁금해할 만큼의 이기심을 낼 수 있었으나, 순전히 정겹고 사심 없이 애타는 것이 아닌 심경은 무엇도 그녀에게 오래 머물지 않았다. 이모는 그녀를 소홀히 하지 않았다."], ["Her aunt did not neglect her: she wrote again and again; they were receiving frequent accounts from Edmund, and these accounts were as regularly transmitted to Fanny, in the same diffuse style, and the same medley of trusts, hopes, and fears, all following and producing each other at haphazard.", "몇 번이고 다시 썼다. 그들은 에드먼드에게서 잦은 소식을 받고 있었고, 그 소식은 꼬박꼬박 패니에게로 전해졌으니, 같은 장황한 문체로, 같은 믿음과 바람과 두려움의 뒤범벅으로, 죄다 되는대로 서로 뒤따르고 서로를 낳으며 전해졌다."], ["It was a sort of playing at being frightened.", "그것은 일종의 겁먹은 척 놀이였다."], ["The sufferings which Lady Bertram did not see had little power over her fancy; and she wrote very comfortably about agitation, and anxiety, and poor invalids, till Tom was actually conveyed to Mansfield, and her own eyes had beheld his altered appearance.", "버트럼 부인이 못 본 그 고통은 그녀의 상상에 별 힘을 못 미쳤다. 그리하여 그녀는 마음 졸임과 애탐과 딱한 병자를 두고 자못 편안히 써 내려갔으니, 톰이 정말로 맨스필드로 옮겨져 저 자신의 눈이 그 달라진 행색을 보기까지는 그러했다."], ["Then a letter which she had been previously preparing for Fanny was finished in a different style, in the language of real feeling and alarm; then she wrote as she might have spoken.", "그러자 앞서 패니에게 쓰려고 채비하던 편지가 다른 문체로, 참된 심경과 놀라움의 말로 마무리되었다. 그러자 그녀는 말하듯 썼다."], ["“He is just come, my dear Fanny, and is taken upstairs; and I am so shocked to see him, that I do not know what to do.", "“그 애가 방금 왔다, 사랑하는 패니, 그리고 위층으로 옮겨졌어. 그런데 그 애를 보니 나는 하도 놀라 어찌할 바를 모르겠구나."], ["I am sure he has been very ill.", "그 애가 무척 아팠던 게 틀림없어."], ["Poor Tom!", "딱한 톰!"], ["I am quite grieved for him, and very much frightened, and so is Sir Thomas; and how glad I should be if you were here to comfort me.", "나는 그 애가 자못 서럽고, 몹시 두렵다. 토머스 경도 그러시고."], ["But Sir Thomas hopes he will be better to-morrow, and says we must consider his journey.”", "그리고 네가 여기 있어 나를 위로해 준다면 얼마나 좋을까. 하지만 토머스 경은 그 애가 내일이면 나아지리라 바라시고, 우리가 그 애 여정을 헤아려 봐야 한다 하신다.”"]], [["The real solicitude now awakened in the maternal bosom was not soon over.", "이제 어미의 가슴에 깨어난 그 참된 근심은 이내 지나가지 않았다."], ["Tom’s extreme impatience to be removed to Mansfield, and experience those comforts of home and family which had been little thought of in uninterrupted health, had probably induced his being conveyed thither too early, as a return of fever came on, and for a week he was in a more alarming state than ever.", "톰이 맨스필드로 옮겨져, 끊이지 않는 건강 속에선 별로 생각지 않던 집과 식구의 그 편안함을 누리기를 몹시도 조바심 낸 것이, 아마 그를 너무 일찍 그리로 옮기게 만들었으리라. 열병이 다시 도져, 한 주 동안 그는 여느 때보다 더 놀라운 상태에 있었던 것이다."], ["They were all very seriously frightened.", "다들 자못 심각하게 겁먹었다."], ["Lady Bertram wrote her daily terrors to her niece, who might now be said to live upon letters, and pass all her time between suffering from that of to-day and looking forward to to-morrow’s.", "버트럼 부인은 나날의 두려움을 조카딸에게 써 보냈고, 조카딸은 이제 편지에 기대어 산다 해도 좋을 지경이라, 온 시간을 오늘의 편지에 괴로워하는 것과 내일의 편지를 손꼽아 기다리는 것 사이에서 보냈다."], ["Without any particular affection for her eldest cousin, her tenderness of heart made her feel that she could not spare him, and the purity of her principles added yet a keener solicitude, when she considered how little useful, how little self-denying his life had (apparently) been.", "맏사촌에게 이렇다 할 정이 있는 것도 아니었으나, 마음의 애틋함이 그녀로 하여금 그를 잃을 수 없다 느끼게 했고, 그의 삶이 (겉보기에) 얼마나 쓸모없고 얼마나 자기 부정 없는 것이었나를 헤아리면 그 지조의 순수함이 한층 더 예리한 근심을 더했다."]], [["Susan was her only companion and listener on this, as on more common occasions.", "수전이, 이 일에서도 좀 더 예사로운 일에서와 마찬가지로, 그녀의 유일한 동무이자 들을 이였다."], ["Susan was always ready to hear and to sympathise.", "수전은 늘 기꺼이 들어 주고 함께 안타까워했다."], ["Nobody else could be interested in so remote an evil as illness in a family above an hundred miles off; not even Mrs.", "백 마일도 넘게 떨어진 집안의 병이라는 그토록 먼 화에 관심을 둘 이는 달리 아무도 없었다."], ["Price, beyond a brief question or two, if she saw her daughter with a letter in her hand, and now and then the quiet observation of, “My poor sister Bertram must be in a great deal of trouble.”", "프라이스 부인조차, 딸이 손에 편지를 든 것을 보면 짤막한 물음 한둘과, 이따금 “딱한 버트럼 언니가 큰 시름에 잠겼겠구나” 하는 잔잔한 소감 말고는 그러지 않았다."]], [["So long divided and so differently situated, the ties of blood were little more than nothing.", "그토록 오래 갈라지고 그토록 다르게 처한 터라, 핏줄의 정이란 아무것도 아닌 것이나 다름없었다."], ["An attachment, originally as tranquil as their tempers, was now become a mere name. Mrs.", "애초에 그들 성미만큼이나 잔잔하던 애착이 이제 한낱 이름이 되어 있었다."], ["Price did quite as much for Lady Bertram as Lady Bertram would have done for Mrs. Price.", "프라이스 부인이 버트럼 부인을 위해 한 것은 버트럼 부인이 프라이스 부인을 위해 했을 만큼과 딱 같았다."], ["Three or four Prices might have been swept away, any or all except Fanny and William, and Lady Bertram would have thought little about it; or perhaps might have caught from Mrs.", "프라이스네 서넛쯤은 쓸려 갔을지도, 패니와 윌리엄만 빼고 누구든 아니 죄다 그랬을지도 모르나, 버트럼 부인은 그것을 별로 마음에 두지 않았으리라."], ["Norris’s lips the cant of its being a very happy thing and a great blessing to their poor dear sister Price to have them so well provided for.", "아니 어쩌면, 그들이 그렇게 잘 건사되었으니 딱한 소중한 동생 프라이스한테는 자못 다행한 일이요 큰 복이라는 노리스 부인의 입버릇을 옮겨 받았을지도 모른다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLV", "# 제45장"]], [["At about the week’s end from his return to Mansfield, Tom’s immediate danger was over, and he was so far pronounced safe as to make his mother perfectly easy; for being now used to the sight of him in his suffering, helpless state, and hearing only the best, and never thinking beyond what she heard, with no disposition for alarm and no aptitude at a hint, Lady Bertram was the happiest subject in the world for a little medical imposition.", "맨스필드로 돌아온 지 한 주쯤 되었을 무렵, 톰의 당장의 위험은 지나갔고, 어머니를 아주 놓이게 할 만큼 그는 웬만큼 안전하다고 판정되었다. 이제 그의 그 앓는 무력한 모습을 보는 데 익고, 가장 좋은 소식만 듣고, 들은 것 너머로는 결코 생각지 않으며, 놀랄 기질도 없고 넌지시 비침을 알아챌 눈치도 없던 버트럼 부인은, 얼마간의 의술적 눈속임에 세상 둘도 없이 알맞은 대상이었던 것이다."], ["The fever was subdued; the fever had been his complaint; of course he would soon be well again.", "열이 잡혔다. 열이 그의 병이었다."], ["Lady Bertram could think nothing less, and Fanny shared her aunt’s security, till she received a few lines from Edmund, written purposely to give her a clearer idea of his brother’s situation, and acquaint her with the apprehensions which he and his father had imbibed from the physician with respect to some strong hectic symptoms, which seemed to seize the frame on the departure of the fever.", "그러니 물론 그는 이내 다시 나을 터였다. 버트럼 부인은 그보다 못한 것을 도무지 생각할 수 없었고, 패니도 이모의 안심을 함께 나누었다—에드먼드에게서, 그 형의 처지에 대한 더 또렷한 그림을 주고, 열이 물러갈 제 몸을 사로잡는 듯한 어떤 심한 소모열 증세에 대해 그와 그 아버지가 의사에게서 얻은 그 두려움을 그녀에게 알리려 일부러 쓴 몇 줄을 받기까지는."], ["They judged it best that Lady Bertram should not be harassed by alarms which, it was to be hoped, would prove unfounded; but there was no reason why Fanny should not know the truth.", "그들은, 근거 없는 것으로 드러나기를 바라야 할 그런 놀라움으로 버트럼 부인을 시달리게 하지 않는 것이 가장 낫다 여겼다. 그러나 패니가 진실을 몰라야 할 까닭은 없었다."], ["They were apprehensive for his lungs.", "그들은 그의 폐를 두려워했다."]], [["A very few lines from Edmund shewed her the patient and the sickroom in a juster and stronger light than all Lady Bertram’s sheets of paper could do.", "에드먼드의 아주 몇 줄이, 버트럼 부인의 온갖 종이보다 더 올바르고 더 세찬 빛으로 그 병자와 병실을 그녀에게 보여 주었다."], ["There was hardly any one in the house who might not have described, from personal observation, better than herself; not one who was not more useful at times to her son.", "집 안에서, 저 자신보다 눈으로 본 대로 더 잘 그려 낼 수 없을 이가 좀처럼 없었다. 이따금 그 아들에게 저보다 더 쓸모가 되지 않는 이가 하나도 없었다."], ["She could do nothing but glide in quietly and look at him; but when able to talk or be talked to, or read to, Edmund was the companion he preferred.", "그녀야 조용히 미끄러져 들어가 그를 바라보는 것 말고는 아무것도 못 했으리라. 그러나 말을 하거나 말을 걸어지거나 책을 읽어질 만할 때면, 에드먼드가 그가 좋아하는 동무였다."], ["His aunt worried him by her cares, and Sir Thomas knew not how to bring down his conversation or his voice to the level of irritation and feebleness.", "이모는 그 마음씀으로 그를 성가시게 했고, 토머스 경은 제 말이나 제 목소리를 그 짜증과 힘없음의 눈높이로 낮출 줄을 몰랐다."], ["Edmund was all in all.", "에드먼드가 전부였다."], ["Fanny would certainly believe him so at least, and must find that her estimation of him was higher than ever when he appeared as the attendant, supporter, cheerer of a suffering brother.", "패니는 적어도 그가 그러하리라 분명 믿었을 테고, 그가 앓는 형의 시중꾼이자 떠받침이자 북돋우는 이로 나선 모습을 보면 그에 대한 제 평가가 여느 때보다 더 높아짐을 알밖에 없었다. 도울 것이라곤 근래 병의 그 쇠약함만이 아니었다."], ["There was not only the debility of recent illness to assist: there was also, as she now learnt, nerves much affected, spirits much depressed to calm and raise, and her own imagination added that there must be a mind to be properly guided.", "그녀가 이제 알게 된 대로, 자못 상한 신경과, 다독이고 돋워야 할 자못 가라앉은 기운도 있었다. 그리고 그녀 자신의 상상은, 온당히 이끌어야 할 마음도 있으려니 하고 덧붙였다."]], [["The family were not consumptive, and she was more inclined to hope than fear for her cousin, except when she thought of Miss Crawford; but Miss Crawford gave her the idea of being the child of good luck, and to her selfishness and vanity it would be good luck to have Edmund the only son.", "그 집안은 폐병 내림이 아니었다. 그리하여 그녀는, 크로퍼드 양을 생각할 때만 빼면, 사촌을 두고 두려워하기보다 바라는 쪽으로 더 기울었다. 그러나 크로퍼드 양은 그녀에게 운수 좋은 아이라는 인상을 주었고, 그녀의 이기심과 허영에는 에드먼드가 외아들이 되는 것이 운수 좋은 일일 터였다."]], [["Even in the sick chamber the fortunate Mary was not forgotten.", "병실에서마저 그 운 좋은 메리는 잊히지 않았다."], ["Edmund’s letter had this postscript.", "에드먼드의 편지에는 이런 추신이 있었다."], ["“On the subject of my last, I had actually begun a letter when called away by Tom’s illness, but I have now changed my mind, and fear to trust the influence of friends.", "“지난번 편지의 그 일에 대해서는, 나는 정말로 편지를 쓰기 시작했다가 톰의 병으로 불려 갔어. 하지만 이제 마음을 바꿔, 벗들의 물듦에 맡기기가 두렵구나."], ["When Tom is better, I shall go.”", "톰이 나아지면, 내가 가마.”"]], [["Such was the state of Mansfield, and so it continued, with scarcely any change, till Easter.", "맨스필드의 형편은 이러했고, 부활절까지 좀처럼 아무 달라짐 없이 그대로 이어졌다."], ["A line occasionally added by Edmund to his mother’s letter was enough for Fanny’s information.", "에드먼드가 어머니의 편지에 이따금 덧붙인 한 줄이 패니의 소식으로 넉넉했다."], ["Tom’s amendment was alarmingly slow.", "톰의 나아짐은 놀랍도록 더뎠다."]], [["Easter came particularly late this year, as Fanny had most sorrowfully considered, on first learning that she had no chance of leaving Portsmouth till after it.", "올해는 부활절이 유난히 늦게 왔으니, 그 뒤라야 포츠머스를 떠날 낌새가 있음을 처음 알았을 때 패니가 더없이 서글피 헤아린 대로였다."], ["It came, and she had yet heard nothing of her return—nothing even of the going to London, which was to precede her return.", "그것이 왔으되, 그녀는 아직 제 돌아감에 대해서는 아무것도—돌아감에 앞설 런던행에 대해서조차 아무것도 듣지 못했다."], ["Her aunt often expressed a wish for her, but there was no notice, no message from the uncle on whom all depended.", "이모는 곧잘 그녀를 바라는 뜻을 밝혔으나, 온갖 것이 걸린 그 삼촌에게서는 아무 기별도, 아무 전갈도 없었다."], ["She supposed he could not yet leave his son, but it was a cruel, a terrible delay to her.", "그녀는 그가 아직 아들 곁을 못 떠나려니 여겼으나, 그것은 그녀에게 모진, 소름 끼치는 미룸이었다. 사월 말이 다가오고 있었다."], ["The end of April was coming on; it would soon be almost three months, instead of two, that she had been absent from them all, and that her days had been passing in a state of penance, which she loved them too well to hope they would thoroughly understand; and who could yet say when there might be leisure to think of or fetch her?", "이내 그녀가 그들 모두에게서 떠나 있은 지가 두 달이 아니라 거의 석 달이 될 참이었고, 그녀의 나날이, 그들이 속속들이 헤아려 주기를 바라기엔 그녀가 그들을 너무 사랑하는 그런 고행의 상태로 흘러온 지가 그리될 참이었다. 그런데 그녀를 생각하거나 데리러 올 겨를이 언제 있을지 아직 누가 말할 수 있으랴?"]], [["Her eagerness, her impatience, her longings to be with them, were such as to bring a line or two of Cowper’s Tirocinium for ever before her.", "그들과 함께하고픈 그녀의 못내 애타는 마음, 그 조바심, 그 그리움은, 쿠퍼의 「티로키니움」의 한두 행을 영영 그녀 눈앞에 데려올 만한 것이었다."], ["“With what intense desire she wants her home,” was continually on her tongue, as the truest description of a yearning which she could not suppose any schoolboy’s bosom to feel more keenly.", "“얼마나 간절한 열망으로 제 집을 그리는가”가 그녀 혀끝에 노상 맴돌았으니, 어떤 학생의 가슴인들 이보다 더 뼈저리게 느끼리라곤 여길 수 없는 그 그리움을 더없이 참되게 그린 것이었다."]], [["When she had been coming to Portsmouth, she had loved to call it her home, had been fond of saying that she was going home; the word had been very dear to her, and so it still was, but it must be applied to Mansfield.", "포츠머스로 오던 무렵, 그녀는 그것을 제 집이라 부르기를 좋아했고, 집에 간다고 말하기를 즐겼다. 그 낱말이 그녀에게 자못 소중했고, 지금도 여전히 그러했으나, 그것은 맨스필드에 갖다 붙여야 했다."], ["That was now the home.", "그곳이 이제 집이었다."], ["Portsmouth was Portsmouth; Mansfield was home.", "포츠머스는 포츠머스요, 맨스필드가 집이었다. 그녀의 은밀한 상념에 젖어 오래전부터 그렇게 정해져 있었다."], ["They had been long so arranged in the indulgence of her secret meditations, and nothing was more consolatory to her than to find her aunt using the same language: “I cannot but say I much regret your being from home at this distressing time, so very trying to my spirits.", "그리고 이모가 같은 말을 쓰는 것을 보는 것보다 그녀에게 더 위안이 되는 것은 없었다. “이 괴로운 때에, 내 기운에 그토록 힘겨운 이때에 네가 집을 떠나 있는 게 나는 자못 서운하다 안 할 수 없구나."], ["I trust and hope, and sincerely wish you may never be absent from home so long again,” were most delightful sentences to her.", "나는 네가 다시는 이토록 오래 집을 떠나 있지 않기를 믿고 바라고 진심으로 빈다”는 그녀에게 더없이 흐뭇한 문장이었다."], ["Still, however, it was her private regale.", "그래도 그것은 그녀 혼자만의 즐김이었다."], ["Delicacy to her parents made her careful not to betray such a preference of her uncle’s house.", "부모에 대한 섬세함이, 삼촌 집을 이렇게 더 좋아함을 드러내지 않도록 그녀를 조심시켰다."], ["It was always: “When I go back into Northamptonshire, or when I return to Mansfield, I shall do so and so.”", "늘 이랬다. “노샘프턴셔로 돌아가면, 아니 맨스필드로 돌아가면, 저는 이러이러하게 할 거예요.”"], ["For a great while it was so, but at last the longing grew stronger, it overthrew caution, and she found herself talking of what she should do when she went home before she was aware.", "오래도록 그러했으나, 마침내 그리움이 더 세져 조심을 뒤엎었고, 그녀는 저도 모르는 새 집에 가면 무엇을 하겠다는 얘기를 하고 있는 자신을 발견했다."], ["She reproached herself, coloured, and looked fearfully towards her father and mother.", "그녀는 저를 나무라며, 얼굴을 붉히고, 두려운 눈으로 아버지와 어머니를 바라보았다."], ["She need not have been uneasy.", "마음 졸일 것 없었다."], ["There was no sign of displeasure, or even of hearing her.", "언짢은 기색은커녕 그녀 말을 듣는 기색조차 없었다."], ["They were perfectly free from any jealousy of Mansfield.", "그들은 맨스필드에 대한 어떤 시샘에서도 아주 벗어나 있었다."], ["She was as welcome to wish herself there as to be there.", "그녀가 거기 있고 싶어 하는 것도 거기 있는 것만큼이나 반가운 일이었다."]], [["It was sad to Fanny to lose all the pleasures of spring.", "봄의 온갖 즐거움을 잃는 것이 패니에겐 서글펐다."], ["She had not known before what pleasures she had to lose in passing March and April in a town.", "그녀는 삼월과 사월을 도시에서 지내며 잃을 즐거움이 저에게 얼마나 있었는지 앞서 몰랐다."], ["She had not known before how much the beginnings and progress of vegetation had delighted her.", "초목이 돋고 자라는 것이 저를 얼마나 기쁘게 했는지 앞서 몰랐다."], ["What animation, both of body and mind, she had derived from watching the advance of that season which cannot, in spite of its capriciousness, be unlovely, and seeing its increasing beauties from the earliest flowers in the warmest divisions of her aunt’s garden, to the opening of leaves of her uncle’s plantations, and the glory of his woods.", "그 변덕에도 불구하고 사랑스럽지 않을 수 없는 그 철이 나아가는 것을 지켜보며, 이모 정원의 가장 볕 좋은 자락의 가장 이른 꽃부터 삼촌이 심은 나무들의 잎이 벌어짐과 그 숲의 영광에 이르기까지 그 더해 가는 아름다움을 보며 그녀가 몸과 마음 모두에서 얻어 낸 그 생기라니. 그런 즐거움을 잃는 것은 대수롭잖은 일이 아니었다."], ["To be losing such pleasures was no trifle; to be losing them, because she was in the midst of closeness and noise, to have confinement, bad air, bad smells, substituted for liberty, freshness, fragrance, and verdure, was infinitely worse: but even these incitements to regret were feeble, compared with what arose from the conviction of being missed by her best friends, and the longing to be useful to those who were wanting her!", "그것을, 갇힘과 소란 한복판에 있는 탓에 잃는다는 것, 자유와 싱그러움과 향기와 푸르름 대신 갇힘과 나쁜 공기와 나쁜 냄새를 안는다는 것은 한없이 더 나빴다. 그러나 이런 아쉬움을 부추기는 것들조차, 가장 좋은 벗들에게 아쉬워지고 있다는 확신에서, 그리고 저를 아쉬워하는 이들에게 쓸모가 되고픈 그리움에서 우러나는 것에 견주면 힘없는 것이었다!"]], [["Could she have been at home, she might have been of service to every creature in the house.", "집에 있을 수 있었다면, 그녀는 집 안 온갖 이에게 쓸모가 되었으리라."], ["She felt that she must have been of use to all.", "다들에게 쓸모가 되었으리라 그녀는 느꼈다."], ["To all she must have saved some trouble of head or hand; and were it only in supporting the spirits of her aunt Bertram, keeping her from the evil of solitude, or the still greater evil of a restless, officious companion, too apt to be heightening danger in order to enhance her own importance, her being there would have been a general good.", "다들에게 머리나 손의 수고를 얼마간 덜어 주었으리라. 그리고 그것이 그저 버트럼 이모의 기운을 떠받쳐, 이모를 외로움의 화에서, 아니 제 중함을 돋우려 위험을 키우기 십상인 안절부절 참견 많은 동무라는 더 큰 화에서 지켜 주는 것뿐이었대도, 그녀가 거기 있는 것이 두루 좋은 일이었으리라."], ["She loved to fancy how she could have read to her aunt, how she could have talked to her, and tried at once to make her feel the blessing of what was, and prepare her mind for what might be; and how many walks up and down stairs she might have saved her, and how many messages she might have carried.", "그녀는 어떻게 이모에게 책을 읽어 주고, 어떻게 이모와 얘기하고, 있는 것의 복을 느끼게 하는 동시에 있을지 모를 것에 대한 마음의 채비를 시키려 애쓸 수 있었을지, 그리고 얼마나 많은 계단 오르내림을 이모에게 덜어 주고 얼마나 많은 심부름을 대신했을지 그려 보기를 좋아했다."]], [["It astonished her that Tom’s sisters could be satisfied with remaining in London at such a time, through an illness which had now, under different degrees of danger, lasted several weeks.", "톰의 누이들이 하필 이런 때에, 이제 저마다 다른 정도의 위험 아래 여러 주째 이어진 병 내내 런던에 남아 있으면서 흡족할 수 있다는 것이 그녀는 놀라웠다. 그들이야 마음만 먹으면 맨스필드로 돌아올 수 있었다."], ["They might return to Mansfield when they chose; travelling could be no difficulty to them, and she could not comprehend how both could still keep away.", "그들에게 여행이야 어려울 게 없었다. 그런데 어떻게 둘 다 여전히 멀찍이 있을 수 있는지 그녀는 헤아릴 수 없었다."], ["If Mrs. Rushworth could imagine any interfering obligations, Julia was certainly able to quit London whenever she chose.", "러시워스 부인이야 무슨 발목 잡는 의무를 떠올릴 수 있다 쳐도, 줄리아는 분명 마음만 먹으면 언제든 런던을 떠날 수 있었다."], ["It appeared from one of her aunt’s letters that Julia had offered to return if wanted, but this was all.", "이모의 편지 하나로 미루어, 줄리아가 필요하다면 돌아오겠다 한 것 같았으나, 그것이 다였다."], ["It was evident that she would rather remain where she was.", "그녀가 있는 데 그대로 남고 싶어 하는 것이 뻔했다."]], [["Fanny was disposed to think the influence of London very much at war with all respectable attachments.", "패니는 런던의 물듦이 온갖 어엿한 애착과 자못 다투는 것이라 여기는 쪽으로 기울었다."], ["She saw the proof of it in Miss Crawford, as well as in her cousins; her attachment to Edmund had been respectable, the most respectable part of her character; her friendship for herself had at least been blameless.", "그녀는 그 증거를 사촌들에게서뿐 아니라 크로퍼드 양에게서도 보았다. 에드먼드에 대한 그녀의 애착은 어엿한 것이었고, 그 성품에서 가장 어엿한 대목이었다."], ["Where was either sentiment now?", "저에 대한 그녀의 우정도 적어도 흠 없는 것이었다."], ["It was so long since Fanny had had any letter from her, that she had some reason to think lightly of the friendship which had been so dwelt on.", "그런데 이제 그 어느 심경이 어디에 있는가? 패니가 그녀에게서 편지를 받은 지가 하도 오래라, 그토록 마음 두던 그 우정을 대수롭잖게 여길 만한 까닭이 얼마간 있었다."], ["It was weeks since she had heard anything of Miss Crawford or of her other connexions in town, except through Mansfield, and she was beginning to suppose that she might never know whether Mr.", "맨스필드를 통하지 않고서는 크로퍼드 양이나 시내의 다른 인연에 대해 무엇도 못 들은 지가 여러 주였다."], ["Crawford had gone into Norfolk again or not till they met, and might never hear from his sister any more this spring, when the following letter was received to revive old and create some new sensations—", "그리하여 그녀는, 크로퍼드 씨가 다시 노퍽에 갔는지 안 갔는지도 만나기 전엔 영영 모를지도 모르고, 그 누이에게서 이 봄엔 다시는 소식을 못 들을지도 모른다 여기기 시작하던 참에, 옛 심경을 되살리고 새 심경을 얼마간 자아내는 다음 편지를 받았다—"]], [["“Forgive me, my dear Fanny, as soon as you can, for my long silence, and behave as if you could forgive me directly.", "“사랑하는 패니, 내 오랜 잠잠함을 되도록 빨리 용서하고, 당장 용서할 수 있는 양 처신해 줘요. 이게 내 얌전한 청이자 기대예요."], ["This is my modest request and expectation, for you are so good, that I depend upon being treated better than I deserve, and I write now to beg an immediate answer.", "그쪽이 하도 착해서, 나는 내가 받을 만한 것보다 더 나은 대접을 받으려니 믿거든요. 그리고 나는 이제 당장의 답을 청하려 쓰는 거예요."], ["I want to know the state of things at Mansfield Park, and you, no doubt, are perfectly able to give it.", "나는 맨스필드 파크의 형편을 알고 싶은데, 그쪽이야 두말할 것 없이 그걸 아주 잘 전할 수 있겠죠."], ["One should be a brute not to feel for the distress they are in; and from what I hear, poor Mr.", "그들이 처한 그 괴로움을 안쓰러워하지 않는다면 짐승이나 다름없을 거예요."], ["Bertram has a bad chance of ultimate recovery.", "그리고 내가 듣기로, 딱한 버트럼 씨는 끝내 회복될 가망이 나쁘대요."], ["I thought little of his illness at first.", "나는 처음엔 그이 병을 대수롭잖게 여겼어요."], ["I looked upon him as the sort of person to be made a fuss with, and to make a fuss himself in any trifling disorder, and was chiefly concerned for those who had to nurse him; but now it is confidently asserted that he is really in a decline, that the symptoms are most alarming, and that part of the family, at least, are aware of it.", "그이를 무슨 사소한 탈에도 야단법석 떨어 주게 되고 저 스스로도 야단법석 떨 그런 부류로 봤고, 주로 그이를 돌봐야 할 이들이 안쓰러웠거든요. 하지만 이제 그이가 정말 쇠약해지고 있다고, 증세가 더없이 놀랍다고, 적어도 집안의 일부는 그걸 안다고 다들 자신 있게 말해요."], ["If it be so, I am sure you must be included in that part, that discerning part, and therefore entreat you to let me know how far I have been rightly informed.", "만일 그렇다면, 그쪽도 분명 그 일부에, 그 눈 밝은 일부에 들어 있을 게 틀림없으니, 부디 내가 얼마나 옳게 전해 들은 건지 알려 주기를 청해요."], ["I need not say how rejoiced I shall be to hear there has been any mistake, but the report is so prevalent that I confess I cannot help trembling.", "무슨 착오가 있었다는 걸 들으면 내가 얼마나 기쁠지야 말할 것도 없지만, 그 소문이 하도 파다해서, 솔직히 나는 떨지 않을 수 없어요."], ["To have such a fine young man cut off in the flower of his days is most melancholy.", "그토록 훤한 젊은이가 한창때에 꺾이다니 더없이 서글픈 일이죠."], ["Poor Sir Thomas will feel it dreadfully.", "딱한 토머스 경이 그걸 끔찍이 느끼실 거예요."], ["I really am quite agitated on the subject.", "나는 정말 이 일로 자못 마음이 졸여요."], ["Fanny, Fanny, I see you smile and look cunning, but, upon my honour, I never bribed a physician in my life.", "패니, 패니, 그쪽이 웃으며 짓궂은 낯을 하는 게 보이지만, 맹세코 나는 평생 의사한테 뇌물 준 적이 없어요."], ["Poor young man!", "딱한 젊은이!"], ["If he is to die, there will be two poor young men less in the world; and with a fearless face and bold voice would I say to any one, that wealth and consequence could fall into no hands more deserving of them.", "만일 그이가 죽는다면, 세상에 딱한 젊은이가 둘 줄어드는 거고요. 그리고 나는 겁 없는 얼굴과 대담한 목소리로 누구에게든 말하겠어요, 재물과 지체가 그보다 더 받을 만한 손에는 떨어질 수 없다고요."], ["It was a foolish precipitation last Christmas, but the evil of a few days may be blotted out in part.", "지난 크리스마스엔 어리석은 성급함이었지만, 며칠의 화쯤은 얼마간 지워질 수 있죠."], ["Varnish and gilding hide many stains.", "니스칠과 금박은 숱한 얼룩을 가리니까요."], ["It will be but the loss of the Esquire after his name.", "그건 그저 이름 뒤의 ‘에스콰이어(Esq.)’를 잃는 것뿐일 테고요."], ["With real affection, Fanny, like mine, more might be overlooked.", "내 것 같은 참된 정이 있으면, 패니, 더 많은 게 눈감아질 수 있어요."], ["Write to me by return of post, judge of my anxiety, and do not trifle with it.", "회신 우편으로 나한테 편지해서, 내 애탐을 헤아려 그것을 가벼이 여기지 말아 줘요."], ["Tell me the real truth, as you have it from the fountainhead.", "그쪽이 그 원천에서 얻은 대로, 참된 진실을 나한테 일러 줘요."], ["And now, do not trouble yourself to be ashamed of either my feelings or your own.", "그리고 이제, 내 심경이든 그쪽 심경이든 부끄러워하려 애쓰지 말아요. 내 말을 믿어요, 그건 자연스러울 뿐 아니라 박애롭고 덕스러운 거예요."], ["Believe me, they are not only natural, they are philanthropic and virtuous.", "그쪽 양심에 물어봐요, ‘에드먼드 경’이 다른 어떤 있을 법한 ‘경’보다 버트럼가의 온 재산으로 더 많은 좋은 일을 하지 않겠는지를요."], ["I put it to your conscience, whether ‘Sir Edmund’ would not do more good with all the Bertram property than any other possible ‘Sir.’ Had the Grants been at home I would not have troubled you, but you are now the only one I can apply to for the truth, his sisters not being within my reach. Mrs.", "그랜트 부부가 집에 있었으면 그쪽을 성가시게 안 했겠지만, 이제 진실을 물어볼 이는 그쪽뿐이에요, 그이 누이들은 내 손이 닿는 데 없으니까요."], ["R. has been spending the Easter with the Aylmers at Twickenham (as to be sure you know), and is not yet returned; and Julia is with the cousins who live near Bedford Square, but I forget their name and street.", "R 부인은 트위크넘의 에일머네에서 부활절을 보내는 중이고(물론 그쪽도 알겠지만) 아직 안 돌아왔어요."], ["Could I immediately apply to either, however, I should still prefer you, because it strikes me that they have all along been so unwilling to have their own amusements cut up, as to shut their eyes to the truth.", "줄리아는 베드퍼드 스퀘어 근처에 사는 사촌들과 있는데, 그 이름과 거리는 잊어버렸네요. 하지만 둘 중 누구에게든 당장 물어볼 수 있다 해도, 나는 여전히 그쪽이 더 좋아요."], ["I suppose Mrs. R.’s Easter holidays will not last much longer; no doubt they are thorough holidays to her.", "그들 다 저희 즐길 거리가 깨지는 게 하도 싫어 진실에 눈감아 온 것 같다는 생각이 들거든요."], ["The Aylmers are pleasant people; and her husband away, she can have nothing but enjoyment.", "R 부인의 부활절 휴가야 그리 오래는 안 가겠죠. 두말할 것 없이 그이한텐 온전한 휴가일 테고요."], ["I give her credit for promoting his going dutifully down to Bath, to fetch his mother; but how will she and the dowager agree in one house?", "에일머네는 유쾌한 사람들이에요. 게다가 남편이 없으니 그이야 즐거움밖에 없겠죠."], ["Henry is not at hand, so I have nothing to say from him.", "나는 그이가 남편더러 도리껏 바스로 내려가 제 어머니를 모셔 오게 부추긴 걸 갸륵히 봐요. 하지만 그이하고 그 노부인이 한집에서 어떻게 지낼까요?"], ["Do not you think Edmund would have been in town again long ago, but for this illness?—Yours ever, Mary.”", "헨리는 곁에 없어서, 그이한테서 전할 말은 없어요. 이 병만 아니었으면 에드먼드가 진작 다시 시내에 와 있었으리라 여기지 않아요?—언제나 그쪽의, 메리.”"]], [["“I had actually begun folding my letter when Henry walked in, but he brings no intelligence to prevent my sending it. Mrs.", "“막 편지를 접기 시작하는데 헨리가 걸어 들어왔지만, 그이가 이걸 못 부치게 할 소식을 가져오진 않네요."], ["R. knows a decline is apprehended; he saw her this morning: she returns to Wimpole Street to-day; the old lady is come.", "R 부인은 쇠약해지고 있다고 걱정한다는 걸 알아요."], ["Now do not make yourself uneasy with any queer fancies because he has been spending a few days at Richmond.", "그이가 오늘 아침 그이를 봤거든요. 그이는 오늘 윔폴 스트리트로 돌아가요."], ["He does it every spring.", "노부인이 왔고요."], ["Be assured he cares for nobody but you.", "자, 그이가 리치먼드에서 며칠 보냈다고 무슨 야릇한 공상으로 마음 졸이진 말아요. 그이는 봄마다 그래요."], ["At this very moment he is wild to see you, and occupied only in contriving the means for doing so, and for making his pleasure conduce to yours.", "그이가 그쪽 말고는 아무도 마음에 안 둔다는 걸 믿어요. 바로 이 순간에도 그이는 그쪽을 보고파 안달이고, 오직 그럴 방편을, 그리고 제 즐거움이 그쪽 즐거움에 보탬이 되게 할 방편을 궁리하는 데만 매여 있어요."], ["In proof, he repeats, and more eagerly, what he said at Portsmouth about our conveying you home, and I join him in it with all my soul.", "그 증거로, 그이는 포츠머스에서 우리가 그쪽을 집에 데려간다던 그 말을 되풀이해요, 더 못내 애타게요. 그리고 나도 온 넋으로 거기 함께해요."], ["Dear Fanny, write directly, and tell us to come.", "사랑하는 패니, 당장 편지해서 우리더러 오라고 해 줘요."], ["It will do us all good.", "그게 우리 다한테 좋을 거예요."], ["He and I can go to the Parsonage, you know, and be no trouble to our friends at Mansfield Park.", "그이하고 나는 목사관에 묵으면 되니까요, 알잖아요, 맨스필드 파크의 우리 벗들한테 폐 끼칠 것도 없고요."], ["It would really be gratifying to see them all again, and a little addition of society might be of infinite use to them; and as to yourself, you must feel yourself to be so wanted there, that you cannot in conscience—conscientious as you are—keep away, when you have the means of returning.", "그들 다 다시 보는 게 정말 흐뭇하겠고, 사교가 조금 보태지면 그들한테 한없이 쓸모가 될 거예요. 그리고 그쪽 자신으로 말하면, 거기서 저가 그토록 아쉬워지고 있음을 느낄 게 틀림없으니, 돌아갈 방편이 있는 마당에—그토록 양심 바른 그쪽이—양심상 멀찍이 있을 수 없을 거예요."], ["I have not time or patience to give half Henry’s messages; be satisfied that the spirit of each and every one is unalterable affection.”", "나는 헨리의 전할 말을 절반이라도 옮길 겨를도 참을성도 없어요. 그 하나하나의 알맹이가 변함없는 애정이라는 걸로 만족해 줘요.”"]], [["Fanny’s disgust at the greater part of this letter, with her extreme reluctance to bring the writer of it and her cousin Edmund together, would have made her (as she felt) incapable of judging impartially whether the concluding offer might be accepted or not.", "이 편지의 대부분에 대한 패니의 역겨움은, 그것을 쓴 이와 사촌 에드먼드를 한데 데려오는 것에 대한 그녀의 극심한 꺼림과 더불어, (그녀가 느끼기에) 그 맺음말의 청을 받아들여도 될지 아닐지를 공평하게 판단할 수 없게 만들었으리라."], ["To herself, individually, it was most tempting.", "저 자신으로만 치면, 그것은 더없이 마음 끌리는 것이었다."], ["To be finding herself, perhaps within three days, transported to Mansfield, was an image of the greatest felicity, but it would have been a material drawback to be owing such felicity to persons in whose feelings and conduct, at the present moment, she saw so much to condemn: the sister’s feelings, the brother’s conduct, her cold-hearted ambition, his thoughtless vanity.", "어쩌면 사흘 안에 맨스필드로 옮겨진 자신을 발견한다는 것은 더없는 행복의 그림이었으나, 그런 행복을, 그 심경과 처신에서 지금 이 순간 나무랄 것이 하도 많이 보이는 이들—누이의 그 심경, 오라비의 그 처신, 그녀의 냉정한 야심, 그의 생각 없는 허영—에게 빚진다는 것은 큰 흠이 되었으리라."], ["To have him still the acquaintance, the flirt perhaps, of Mrs. Rushworth!", "그가 여전히 러시워스 부인의 지인, 어쩌면 그 시시덕거리는 상대라니!"], ["She was mortified.", "그녀는 무안했다."], ["She had thought better of him.", "그녀는 그를 더 좋게 여겨 왔던 것이다."], ["Happily, however, she was not left to weigh and decide between opposite inclinations and doubtful notions of right; there was no occasion to determine whether she ought to keep Edmund and Mary asunder or not.", "그러나 다행히, 그녀는 서로 어긋난 마음과 옳음에 대한 미심쩍은 소견 사이에서 재고 정할 처지에 놓이지 않았다. 에드먼드와 메리를 떼어 놓아야 할지 말지를 정할 까닭이 없었다."], ["She had a rule to apply to, which settled everything.", "그녀에게는 온갖 것을 매듭지어 줄, 갖다 댈 규칙이 있었던 것이다."], ["Her awe of her uncle, and her dread of taking a liberty with him, made it instantly plain to her what she had to do.", "삼촌에 대한 경외와, 그에게 주제넘게 구는 데 대한 두려움이, 저가 무엇을 해야 할지를 대번에 뚜렷하게 해 주었다."], ["She must absolutely decline the proposal.", "그녀는 그 제안을 아주 마다해야 했다."], ["If he wanted, he would send for her; and even to offer an early return was a presumption which hardly anything would have seemed to justify.", "그가 원하면 저를 데리러 보낼 터였다. 그리고 일찍 돌아가겠다고 나서는 것조차, 무엇으로도 좀처럼 정당화될 성싶지 않은 주제넘음이었다."], ["She thanked Miss Crawford, but gave a decided negative.", "그녀는 크로퍼드 양에게 고마워하되, 딱 잘라 아니라고 했다."], ["“Her uncle, she understood, meant to fetch her; and as her cousin’s illness had continued so many weeks without her being thought at all necessary, she must suppose her return would be unwelcome at present, and that she should be felt an encumbrance.”", "“제가 알기로, 삼촌이 저를 데리러 오실 참이에요. 그리고 사촌의 병이 그토록 여러 주 이어지도록 제가 조금도 필요하다 여겨지지 않았으니, 제 돌아감이 지금으로선 반갑지 않으려니, 제가 짐스럽게 느껴지려니 여길밖에요.”"]], [["Her representation of her cousin’s state at this time was exactly according to her own belief of it, and such as she supposed would convey to the sanguine mind of her correspondent the hope of everything she was wishing for.", "이때 사촌의 상태에 대한 그녀의 전함은 저 자신이 믿는 그대로였고, 편지 벗의 그 낙천적인 마음에 그녀가 바라는 온갖 것에 대한 바람을 전할 만한 것이려니 그녀가 여기는 그대로였다."], ["Edmund would be forgiven for being a clergyman, it seemed, under certain conditions of wealth; and this, she suspected, was all the conquest of prejudice which he was so ready to congratulate himself upon.", "에드먼드는, 보아하니, 얼마간의 재물이라는 조건 아래서라면 성직자인 것을 용서받을 터였다. 그리고 이것이, 그녀가 짐작기에, 그가 그토록 선뜻 스스로 축하하던 그 편견의 극복이라는 것의 전부였다."], ["She had only learnt to think nothing of consequence but money.", "그녀는 그저 돈 말고는 아무것도 중하게 여기지 않는 법을 배웠을 뿐이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLVI", "# 제46장"]], [["As Fanny could not doubt that her answer was conveying a real disappointment, she was rather in expectation, from her knowledge of Miss Crawford’s temper, of being urged again; and though no second letter arrived for the space of a week, she had still the same feeling when it did come.", "제 답장이 정말 실망을 전하고 있음을 패니가 의심할 수 없던 터라, 그녀는 크로퍼드 양의 성미를 아는 만큼, 다시 조름받으려니 오히려 기대하는 쪽이었다. 그리고 한 주 동안 두 번째 편지가 안 왔으되, 그것이 정작 왔을 때에도 그녀는 여전히 같은 심경이었다."]], [["On receiving it, she could instantly decide on its containing little writing, and was persuaded of its having the air of a letter of haste and business.", "그것을 받자, 그녀는 그 안에 글이 별로 없음을 대번에 알 수 있었고, 그것이 서두르는 볼일 편지의 낌새를 띠고 있음을 짐작했다."], ["Its object was unquestionable; and two moments were enough to start the probability of its being merely to give her notice that they should be in Portsmouth that very day, and to throw her into all the agitation of doubting what she ought to do in such a case.", "그 목적은 의심할 나위 없었다. 그리고 두 순간이면, 그것이 그저 그들이 바로 그날 포츠머스에 오리라는 알림일 뿐이려니 하는 가망을 떠올려, 그런 경우 저가 무엇을 해야 할지 미심쩍어하는 온갖 마음 졸임에 그녀를 빠뜨리기에 넉넉했다."], ["If two moments, however, can surround with difficulties, a third can disperse them; and before she had opened the letter, the possibility of Mr. and Miss Crawford’s having applied to her uncle and obtained his permission was giving her ease.", "그러나 두 순간이 어려움으로 에워쌀 수 있다면, 세 번째 순간은 그것을 흩어 버릴 수 있는 법이라, 편지를 뜯기 전에, 크로퍼드 남매가 삼촌에게 청해 그 허락을 얻었을 가망이 그녀를 놓이게 했다."], ["This was the letter—", "편지는 이러했다—"]], [["“A most scandalous, ill-natured rumour has just reached me, and I write, dear Fanny, to warn you against giving the least credit to it, should it spread into the country.", "“더없이 망측하고 심술궂은 소문이 방금 나한테 이르렀어요. 그래서, 사랑하는 패니, 그게 시골까지 퍼지거든 조금도 그걸 곧이듣지 말라 그쪽한테 일러 두려 쓰는 거예요."], ["Depend upon it, there is some mistake, and that a day or two will clear it up; at any rate, that Henry is blameless, and in spite of a moment’s etourderie, thinks of nobody but you.", "믿어요, 무슨 착오가 있는 거고, 하루 이틀이면 밝혀질 거예요. 아무튼 헨리는 흠 없고, 한순간의 경솔(étourderie)에도 불구하고 그쪽 말고는 아무도 마음에 안 둬요."], ["Say not a word of it; hear nothing, surmise nothing, whisper nothing till I write again.", "이 얘긴 한마디도 마요. 내가 다시 쓸 때까지 아무것도 듣지도, 넘겨짚지도, 소곤거리지도 마요."], ["I am sure it will be all hushed up, and nothing proved but Rushworth’s folly.", "나는 이게 죄다 쉬쉬 덮이고, 러시워스의 어리석음 말고는 아무것도 드러나지 않으리라 확신해요."], ["If they are gone, I would lay my life they are only gone to Mansfield Park, and Julia with them.", "그들이 떠났다면, 나는 목숨을 걸고 그들이 그저 맨스필드 파크로, 줄리아와 함께 간 것뿐이라 장담해요."], ["But why would not you let us come for you?", "그런데 왜 우리더러 그쪽을 데리러 가게 안 했어요?"], ["I wish you may not repent it.—Yours, etc.”", "그쪽이 그걸 뉘우치지 않으면 싶네요.—그럼 이만.”"]], [["Fanny stood aghast.", "패니는 얼이 빠진 채 서 있었다."], ["As no scandalous, ill-natured rumour had reached her, it was impossible for her to understand much of this strange letter.", "아무런 망측하고 심술궂은 소문도 그녀에게 이르지 않은지라, 이 야릇한 편지를 많이 헤아리기란 어림없었다."], ["She could only perceive that it must relate to Wimpole Street and Mr. Crawford, and only conjecture that something very imprudent had just occurred in that quarter to draw the notice of the world, and to excite her jealousy, in Miss Crawford’s apprehension, if she heard it.", "그녀는 그것이 윔폴 스트리트와 크로퍼드 씨에 관한 것일 게 틀림없다는 것만 알아챌 수 있었고, 그 방면에서 방금 자못 경솔한 무슨 일이 벌어져 세상의 눈길을 끌고, 크로퍼드 양이 두렵기로는, 저가 그것을 들으면 저의 질투를 돋우려니 하는 것만 넘겨짚을 수 있었다."], ["Miss Crawford need not be alarmed for her.", "크로퍼드 양이 저 때문에 놀랄 것 없었다."], ["She was only sorry for the parties concerned and for Mansfield, if the report should spread so far; but she hoped it might not.", "그녀는 그저 그 관련된 이들이, 그리고 그 소문이 그토록 멀리 퍼진다면 맨스필드가 안쓰러울 뿐이었으나, 그러지 않기를 바랐다."], ["If the Rushworths were gone themselves to Mansfield, as was to be inferred from what Miss Crawford said, it was not likely that anything unpleasant should have preceded them, or at least should make any impression.", "크로퍼드 양의 말로 미루어질 대로 러시워스 내외 자신이 맨스필드로 갔다면, 무슨 언짢은 일이 그들에 앞섰거나, 적어도 무슨 인상을 남겼을 성싶진 않았다."]], [["As to Mr. Crawford, she hoped it might give him a knowledge of his own disposition, convince him that he was not capable of being steadily attached to any one woman in the world, and shame him from persisting any longer in addressing herself.", "크로퍼드 씨로 말하면, 그녀는 그것이 그에게 제 성미를 알게 해 주기를, 그가 세상 어떤 한 여자에게도 굳게 애착할 깜냥이 못 됨을 그에게 납득시키기를, 그리하여 그가 저에게 구애하기를 더는 못 밀어붙이도록 부끄럽게 하기를 바랐다."]], [["It was very strange!", "참 야릇했다!"], ["She had begun to think he really loved her, and to fancy his affection for her something more than common; and his sister still said that he cared for nobody else.", "그녀는 그가 정말로 저를 사랑한다 여기기 시작했고, 저에 대한 그의 정을 여느 것 이상의 무언가로 그려 보기 시작하던 참이었다. 그리고 그 누이도 여전히 그가 다른 누구도 마음에 안 둔다 했다."], ["Yet there must have been some marked display of attentions to her cousin, there must have been some strong indiscretion, since her correspondent was not of a sort to regard a slight one.", "그러나 사촌에게 무슨 뚜렷한 마음씀의 드러냄이 있었던 게 틀림없었고, 무슨 큰 경솔함이 있었던 게 틀림없었으니, 그녀의 편지 벗은 사소한 것을 대수로이 여길 부류가 아니었던 까닭이다."]], [["Very uncomfortable she was, and must continue, till she heard from Miss Crawford again.", "그녀는 크로퍼드 양에게서 다시 소식을 들을 때까지 자못 거북했고, 또 그럴밖에 없었다."], ["It was impossible to banish the letter from her thoughts, and she could not relieve herself by speaking of it to any human being.", "그 편지를 생각에서 몰아내기란 어림없었고, 그것을 어떤 사람에게든 얘기하여 저를 덜 수도 없었다."], ["Miss Crawford need not have urged secrecy with so much warmth; she might have trusted to her sense of what was due to her cousin.", "크로퍼드 양이 그토록 뜨겁게 은밀함을 조를 것 없었다. 사촌에게 마땅한 것을 헤아리는 그녀의 분별에 기대어도 됐을 것을."]], [["The next day came and brought no second letter.", "이튿날이 왔으나 두 번째 편지는 오지 않았다."], ["Fanny was disappointed.", "패니는 실망했다."], ["She could still think of little else all the morning; but, when her father came back in the afternoon with the daily newspaper as usual, she was so far from expecting any elucidation through such a channel that the subject was for a moment out of her head.", "그녀는 여전히 아침 내내 딴것은 거의 생각할 수 없었다. 그러나 아버지가 오후에 여느 때처럼 그날 신문을 들고 돌아왔을 때, 그녀는 그런 통로로 무슨 밝힘이 있으리라곤 하도 기대하지 않던 터라 그 화제가 한순간 머리에서 떠나 있었다."]], [["She was deep in other musing.", "그녀는 딴 상념에 깊이 잠겨 있었다."], ["The remembrance of her first evening in that room, of her father and his newspaper, came across her.", "그 방에서의 첫 저녁, 아버지와 그 신문에 대한 기억이 그녀를 스쳐 갔다."], ["No candle was now wanted.", "이제는 초가 필요 없었다."], ["The sun was yet an hour and half above the horizon.", "해가 아직 지평선 위 한 시간 반 남짓 있었다."], ["She felt that she had, indeed, been three months there; and the sun’s rays falling strongly into the parlour, instead of cheering, made her still more melancholy, for sunshine appeared to her a totally different thing in a town and in the country.", "그녀는 정말이지 저가 거기 석 달 있었음을 느꼈다. 그리고 응접실로 세차게 쏟아지는 햇살은, 명랑하게 하기는커녕 그녀를 한층 더 서글프게 했으니, 햇빛이 그녀에겐 도시에서와 시골에서 아주 딴것으로 보였던 까닭이다."], ["Here, its power was only a glare: a stifling, sickly glare, serving but to bring forward stains and dirt that might otherwise have slept.", "여기서 그 힘은 그저 눈부심일 뿐이었다. 숨 막히고 병약한 눈부심, 안 그랬으면 잠들어 있었을 얼룩과 때를 드러낼 뿐인 눈부심이었다."], ["There was neither health nor gaiety in sunshine in a town.", "도시의 햇빛에는 건강도 명랑함도 없었다. 그녀는 짓누르는 열기의 이글거림 속에, 떠도는 먼지의 구름 속에 앉아 있었고, 그녀의 눈은 그저, 아버지의 머리 자국이 밴 벽에서, 오라비들이 베고 새긴 탁자로 헤맬 수 있을 뿐이었다."], ["She sat in a blaze of oppressive heat, in a cloud of moving dust, and her eyes could only wander from the walls, marked by her father’s head, to the table cut and notched by her brothers, where stood the tea-board never thoroughly cleaned, the cups and saucers wiped in streaks, the milk a mixture of motes floating in thin blue, and the bread and butter growing every minute more greasy than even Rebecca’s hands had first produced it.", "거기엔 결코 속속들이 닦인 적 없는 찻쟁반과, 줄무늬로 훔쳐진 잔과 받침과, 묽은 푸른빛에 티끌이 떠도는 뒤범벅인 우유와, 리베카의 손이 처음 만들어 낸 것보다 시시각각 더 번드르르해지는 버터빵이 놓여 있었다. 아버지는 신문을 읽었고, 어머니는 차가 준비되는 동안 여느 때처럼 해진 양탄자를 두고 한탄하며 리베카가 그걸 좀 기웠으면 했다."], ["Her father read his newspaper, and her mother lamented over the ragged carpet as usual, while the tea was in preparation, and wished Rebecca would mend it; and Fanny was first roused by his calling out to her, after humphing and considering over a particular paragraph: “What’s the name of your great cousins in town, Fan?”", "그러다 패니는, 아버지가 어느 대목을 두고 흠흠거리며 곰곰 살핀 뒤 저에게 소리쳐 부르는 데 비로소 정신이 들었다. “시내에 사는 그 대단한 네 사촌 이름이 뭐냐, 팬?”"]], [["A moment’s recollection enabled her to say, “Rushworth, sir.”", "잠깐 떠올려 그녀는 말할 수 있었다. “러시워스예요, 아버지.”"]], [["“And don’t they live in Wimpole Street?”", "“그리고 그들이 윔폴 스트리트에 살지 않니?”"]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "“네, 아버지.”"]], [["“Then, there’s the devil to pay among them, that’s all!", "“그럼, 그들 사이에 큰 사달이 났구나, 그게 다야!"], ["There”", "자”"], ["(holding out the paper to her); “much good may such fine relations do you.", "(그녀에게 신문을 내밀며). “그런 대단한 친척들이 너한테 참 좋기도 하겠다."], ["I don’t know what Sir Thomas may think of such matters; he may be too much of the courtier and fine gentleman to like his daughter the less.", "토머스 경이 이런 일을 어찌 여길지야 모르겠다만, 그이야 너무 궁정 신하에 점잖은 신사라 제 딸을 덜 좋아하진 못하겠지. 하지만, 이런 제기랄!"], ["But, by G—! if she belonged to me, I’d give her the rope’s end as long as I could stand over her.", "그 애가 내 딸이었다면, 나는 지켜설 수 있는 한 밧줄 끝으로 매질을 했을 게다."], ["A little flogging for man and woman too would be the best way of preventing such things.”", "남자든 여자든 매질을 좀 하는 게 이런 일을 막는 가장 좋은 길이야.”"]], [["Fanny read to herself that “it was with infinite concern the newspaper had to announce to the world a matrimonial fracas in the family of Mr. R. of Wimpole Street; the beautiful Mrs. R., whose name had not long been enrolled in the lists of Hymen, and who had promised to become so brilliant a leader in the fashionable world, having quitted her husband’s roof in company with the well-known and captivating Mr. C., the intimate friend and associate of Mr. R., and it was not known even to the editor of the newspaper whither they were gone.”", "패니는 혼자 읽어 내려갔다, “윔폴 스트리트의 R 씨 집안에 혼인상의 불화(fracas)가 있었음을 세상에 알리게 되어 신문은 한없이 마음이 무겁다. 히멘의 명부에 그 이름이 오른 지 얼마 안 된, 그리고 유행하는 세상에서 그토록 빛나는 선도자가 되리라 여겨지던 아리따운 R 부인이, R 씨의 절친한 벗이자 동료인, 널리 알려진 매혹적인 C 씨와 함께 제 남편의 지붕을 떠났으며, 그들이 어디로 갔는지는 신문 편집자조차 알지 못한다.”"]], [["“It is a mistake, sir,” said Fanny instantly; “it must be a mistake, it cannot be true; it must mean some other people.”", "“착오예요, 아버지.” 패니가 대번에 말했다. “착오가 틀림없어요. 참일 리 없어요. 다른 누군가를 두고 하는 얘기가 틀림없어요.”"]], [["She spoke from the instinctive wish of delaying shame; she spoke with a resolution which sprung from despair, for she spoke what she did not, could not believe herself.", "그녀는 부끄러움을 미루려는 본능적인 바람에서 말했고, 절망에서 우러난 결연함으로 말했으니, 저 자신도 믿지 않는, 믿을 수 없는 것을 말했기 때문이다."], ["It had been the shock of conviction as she read.", "읽는 동안 그것은 확신의 충격이었다."], ["The truth rushed on her; and how she could have spoken at all, how she could even have breathed, was afterwards matter of wonder to herself.", "진실이 그녀에게 밀려들었다. 그리고 그녀가 대체 어떻게 조금이라도 말할 수 있었는지, 어떻게 숨이라도 쉴 수 있었는지가 뒷날 저 자신에게 놀라운 일이었다."]], [["Mr. Price cared too little about the report to make her much answer.", "프라이스 씨는 그 소문을 하도 대수롭잖게 여겨 그녀에게 이렇다 할 대꾸를 하지 않았다."], ["“It might be all a lie,”", "“죄다 거짓말일 수도 있지.”"], ["he acknowledged; “but so many fine ladies were going to the devil nowadays that way, that there was no answering for anybody.”", "그가 인정했다. “하지만 요즘 그토록 많은 대단한 부인들이 그런 식으로 마귀한테 끌려가니, 누구든 장담할 수가 없어.”"]], [["“Indeed, I hope it is not true,” said Mrs.", "“정말이지, 참이 아니면 좋겠어요.”"], ["Price plaintively; “it would be so very shocking!", "프라이스 부인이 하소연하듯 말했다. “그렇다면 너무나 망측하잖아요!"], ["If I have spoken once to Rebecca about that carpet, I am sure I have spoke at least a dozen times; have not I, Betsey?", "그 양탄자를 두고 리베카한테 한 번이라도 말했다면, 분명 적어도 열두 번은 말했을 거예요. 안 그러니, 벳시?"], ["And it would not be ten minutes’ work.”", "게다가 십 분도 안 걸릴 일인데.”"]], [["The horror of a mind like Fanny’s, as it received the conviction of such guilt, and began to take in some part of the misery that must ensue, can hardly be described.", "그런 죄에 대한 확신을 받아들이며, 뒤이을 비참함의 일부를 헤아리기 시작하는, 패니 같은 마음의 그 소름은 좀처럼 그려 낼 수 없다."], ["At first, it was a sort of stupefaction; but every moment was quickening her perception of the horrible evil.", "처음엔 일종의 얼떨떨함이었다. 그러나 매 순간이 그 소름 끼치는 화에 대한 그녀의 알아챔을 재우쳤다."], ["She could not doubt, she dared not indulge a hope, of the paragraph being false.", "그녀는 그 대목이 거짓이라는 것을 의심할 수도, 감히 바랄 수도 없었다."], ["Miss Crawford’s letter, which she had read so often as to make every line her own, was in frightful conformity with it.", "하도 자주 읽어 한 줄 한 줄 제 것으로 만든 크로퍼드 양의 편지가, 그것과 소름 끼치도록 들어맞았다."], ["Her eager defence of her brother, her hope of its being hushed up, her evident agitation, were all of a piece with something very bad; and if there was a woman of character in existence, who could treat as a trifle this sin of the first magnitude, who would try to gloss it over, and desire to have it unpunished, she could believe Miss Crawford to be the woman!", "그 오라비를 못내 두둔한 것, 그것이 쉬쉬 덮이기를 바란 것, 그 뻔한 마음 졸임이 죄다 무언가 자못 나쁜 것과 한 결이었다. 그리고 이 으뜸가는 크기의 죄를 대수롭잖게 여기고, 그것을 얼버무리려 애쓰고, 그것이 벌받지 않기를 바랄 수 있는, 됨됨이 있는 여자가 세상에 있다면, 크로퍼드 양이 바로 그 여자려니 그녀는 믿을 수 있었다!"], ["Now she could see her own mistake as to who were gone, or said to be gone. It was not Mr.", "이제 그녀는 누가 떠났는지, 아니 떠났다고 말해지는지에 대한 제 착각을 알 수 있었다."], ["and Mrs. Rushworth; it was Mrs.", "그것은 러시워스 내외가 아니었다."], ["Rushworth and Mr. Crawford.", "그것은 러시워스 부인과 크로퍼드 씨였다."]], [["Fanny seemed to herself never to have been shocked before.", "패니는 저 자신에게, 여태 한 번도 이토록 충격받은 적이 없는 것만 같았다."], ["There was no possibility of rest.", "쉴 가망이 없었다."], ["The evening passed without a pause of misery, the night was totally sleepless.", "그 저녁은 비참함의 멈춤 없이 지나갔고, 그 밤은 아주 잠 못 이루는 밤이었다."], ["She passed only from feelings of sickness to shudderings of horror; and from hot fits of fever to cold.", "그녀는 그저 메스꺼움의 심경에서 소름의 몸서리로, 열병의 뜨거운 발작에서 차가운 발작으로 오갈 뿐이었다."], ["The event was so shocking, that there were moments even when her heart revolted from it as impossible: when she thought it could not be.", "그 일은 하도 충격적이어서, 그녀의 마음이 그것을 어림없는 일로 여겨 거부하는 순간마저 있었으니, 그럴 리 없다 여기던 순간이었다."], ["A woman married only six months ago; a man professing himself devoted, even engaged to another; that other her near relation; the whole family, both families connected as they were by tie upon tie; all friends, all intimate together!", "시집간 지 겨우 여섯 달 된 여자, 스스로 다른 이에게 헌신한다, 심지어 약혼했다고까지 공언하는 사내, 그 다른 이가 그의 가까운 피붙이, 온 집안, 서로 얽히고설킨 두 집안, 죄다 벗이요 죄다 서로 가까운 사이라니!"], ["It was too horrible a confusion of guilt, too gross a complication of evil, for human nature, not in a state of utter barbarism, to be capable of!", "그것은, 아주 야만의 상태에 있지 않은 인간의 본성이 저지를 수 있기엔 너무나 소름 끼치는 죄의 뒤엉킴이요, 너무나 흉악한 화의 뒤얽힘이었다!"], ["yet her judgment told her it was so.", "그러나 그녀의 판단은 그것이 그러하다고 일러 주었다."], ["His unsettled affections, wavering with his vanity, Maria’s decided attachment, and no sufficient principle on either side, gave it possibility: Miss Crawford’s letter stampt it a fact.", "그의 그 미덥지 못한 정, 허영과 함께 흔들리는 그 정과, 마리아의 뚜렷한 애착과, 어느 편에도 넉넉한 지조가 없음이 그것을 있음 직하게 했다. 크로퍼드 양의 편지가 그것을 사실로 못 박았다."]], [["What would be the consequence?", "그 결말은 무엇일까?"], ["Whom would it not injure?", "그것이 누구를 안 다치게 할까?"], ["Whose views might it not affect?", "그것이 누구의 뜻에 영향 안 미칠까?"], ["Whose peace would it not cut up for ever?", "그것이 누구의 평온을 영영 도려내지 않을까?"], ["Miss Crawford, herself, Edmund; but it was dangerous, perhaps, to tread such ground.", "크로퍼드 양, 저 자신, 에드먼드. 그러나 어쩌면, 그런 땅을 밟는 것은 위험한 일이었다."], ["She confined herself, or tried to confine herself, to the simple, indubitable family misery which must envelop all, if it were indeed a matter of certified guilt and public exposure.", "그녀는, 만일 그것이 정말 확인된 죄요 세상에 드러남의 문제라면 다들을 에워쌀 그 단순하고 의심할 나위 없는 집안의 비참함에 저를 붙들었다, 아니 붙들려 애썼다."], ["The mother’s sufferings, the father’s; there she paused.", "어머니의 고통, 아버지의 고통. 거기서 그녀는 멈췄다."], ["Julia’s, Tom’s, Edmund’s; there a yet longer pause.", "줄리아의, 톰의, 에드먼드의. 거기서 한층 더 긴 멈춤이었다."], ["They were the two on whom it would fall most horribly.", "그것이 가장 소름 끼치게 떨어질 두 사람이 그들이었다."], ["Sir Thomas’s parental solicitude and high sense of honour and decorum, Edmund’s upright principles, unsuspicious temper, and genuine strength of feeling, made her think it scarcely possible for them to support life and reason under such disgrace; and it appeared to her that, as far as this world alone was concerned, the greatest blessing to every one of kindred with Mrs.", "토머스 경의 어버이로서의 근심과 드높은 명예심과 예절 관념이, 에드먼드의 올곧은 지조와 의심할 줄 모르는 성미와 참된 심경의 굳셈이, 그런 치욕 아래 그들이 목숨과 이성을 지탱하기란 좀처럼 어림없으려니 그녀에게 여겨지게 했다."], ["Rushworth would be instant annihilation.", "그리고 이 세상 하나만 놓고 보면, 러시워스 부인과 피붙이인 이 저마다에게 가장 큰 복은 당장의 없어짐이려니 그녀에게 보였다."]], [["Nothing happened the next day, or the next, to weaken her terrors.", "이튿날에도, 그 이튿날에도 그녀의 두려움을 약하게 할 아무 일도 일어나지 않았다."], ["Two posts came in, and brought no refutation, public or private.", "우편이 두 번 들어왔으나, 세상에든 사사로이든 아무 반박도 가져오지 않았다."], ["There was no second letter to explain away the first from Miss Crawford; there was no intelligence from Mansfield, though it was now full time for her to hear again from her aunt.", "크로퍼드 양의 첫 편지를 풀어 없앨 두 번째 편지가 없었다. 이제 이모에게서 다시 소식을 들을 때가 꽉 찼는데도, 맨스필드에서 아무 기별이 없었다."], ["This was an evil omen.", "이것은 나쁜 조짐이었다."], ["She had, indeed, scarcely the shadow of a hope to soothe her mind, and was reduced to so low and wan and trembling a condition, as no mother, not unkind, except Mrs. Price could have overlooked, when the third day did bring the sickening knock, and a letter was again put into her hands.", "정말이지 그녀는 마음을 다독일 바람의 그림자조차 좀처럼 없었고, 프라이스 부인 말고는 야박하지 않은 어떤 어머니라도 그냥 지나칠 수 없었을 만큼 그토록 기운 빠지고 파리하고 떨리는 상태로 내몰려 있었는데, 사흘째 되던 날 정말로 그 메스꺼운 문 두드림이 오고, 편지가 다시 그녀의 손에 놓였다."], ["It bore the London postmark, and came from Edmund.", "그것은 런던 소인이 찍혀 있었고, 에드먼드에게서 온 것이었다."]], [["“Dear Fanny,—You know our present wretchedness.", "“사랑하는 패니에게—너는 우리의 지금 비참함을 알겠지."], ["May God support you under your share!", "네 몫의 그것 아래서 하느님이 너를 붙들어 주시기를!"], ["We have been here two days, but there is nothing to be done.", "우리는 여기 이틀 있었지만, 할 수 있는 게 아무것도 없구나."], ["They cannot be traced.", "그들의 자취를 좇을 수가 없어."], ["You may not have heard of the last blow—Julia’s elopement; she is gone to Scotland with Yates.", "너는 아직 마지막 타격은 못 들었겠지—줄리아의 사랑의 도피 말이야. 그 애는 예이츠와 스코틀랜드로 갔다."], ["She left London a few hours before we entered it.", "우리가 런던에 들어서기 몇 시간 전에 런던을 떠났어."], ["At any other time this would have been felt dreadfully.", "다른 어느 때였다면 이건 끔찍이 사무쳤겠지."], ["Now it seems nothing; yet it is an heavy aggravation.", "지금은 아무것도 아닌 것 같지만, 그래도 무거운 보탬이야."], ["My father is not overpowered.", "아버지는 무너지진 않으셨다."], ["More cannot be hoped.", "그 이상은 바랄 수 없지."], ["He is still able to think and act; and I write, by his desire, to propose your returning home.", "아버지는 아직 생각하고 처신하실 수 있어. 그리고 나는, 아버지의 뜻으로, 네가 집에 돌아올 것을 청하려 쓴다."], ["He is anxious to get you there for my mother’s sake.", "아버지는 어머니를 위해 너를 거기 데려오고 싶어 하셔."], ["I shall be at Portsmouth the morning after you receive this, and hope to find you ready to set off for Mansfield.", "나는 네가 이걸 받은 이튿날 아침 포츠머스에 이를 테고, 네가 맨스필드로 떠날 채비가 돼 있기를 바란다."], ["My father wishes you to invite Susan to go with you for a few months.", "아버지는 네가 수전더러 몇 달 함께 가자 청하기를 바라셔."], ["Settle it as you like; say what is proper; I am sure you will feel such an instance of his kindness at such a moment!", "네 좋을 대로 정하고, 온당한 말을 하렴. 이런 순간에 이런 아버지의 친절을 너는 분명 느낄 게다!"], ["Do justice to his meaning, however I may confuse it.", "내가 아무리 그 뜻을 어지럽혀 전하더라도, 그 뜻만은 온당히 새겨 줘."], ["You may imagine something of my present state.", "너는 내 지금 상태를 얼마간 그려 볼 수 있겠지."], ["There is no end of the evil let loose upon us.", "우리에게 풀려난 화는 끝이 없어."], ["You will see me early by the mail.—Yours, etc.”", "너는 이른 우편마차로 오는 나를 곧 보게 될 게다.—그럼 이만.”"]], [["Never had Fanny more wanted a cordial.", "패니가 이토록 원기 돋울 것을 바란 적이 없었다."], ["Never had she felt such a one as this letter contained.", "이 편지가 담은 그런 것을 그녀가 느낀 적이 없었다."], ["To-morrow!", "내일!"], ["to leave Portsmouth to-morrow!", "내일 포츠머스를 떠난다니!"], ["She was, she felt she was, in the greatest danger of being exquisitely happy, while so many were miserable.", "그녀는, 그토록 많은 이가 비참한데, 저는 지극히 행복해질 크나큰 위험에 처해 있음을—그렇다고 느꼈다."], ["The evil which brought such good to her!", "그녀에게 그런 좋음을 가져다준 그 화라니!"], ["She dreaded lest she should learn to be insensible of it.", "그녀는 저가 그것에 무뎌지는 법을 배울까 두려웠다."], ["To be going so soon, sent for so kindly, sent for as a comfort, and with leave to take Susan, was altogether such a combination of blessings as set her heart in a glow, and for a time seemed to distance every pain, and make her incapable of suitably sharing the distress even of those whose distress she thought of most.", "그토록 이내 떠나게 된 것, 그토록 친절히 부름받은 것, 위안으로 부름받은 것, 게다가 수전을 데려가도 좋다는 허락까지—그것은 죄다, 그녀의 마음을 달아오르게 하고, 한동안 온갖 아픔을 멀리 밀어내는 듯, 그녀가 가장 마음 쓰는 이들의 괴로움조차 걸맞게 나눌 수 없게 만드는 그런 복의 어우러짐이었다."], ["Julia’s elopement could affect her comparatively but little; she was amazed and shocked; but it could not occupy her, could not dwell on her mind.", "줄리아의 사랑의 도피는 견주어 보면 그녀에게 별로 영향 못 미쳤다. 그녀는 놀라고 충격받았으나, 그것은 그녀를 사로잡을 수도, 그녀 마음에 머물 수도 없었다."], ["She was obliged to call herself to think of it, and acknowledge it to be terrible and grievous, or it was escaping her, in the midst of all the agitating pressing joyful cares attending this summons to herself.", "그녀는 억지로 저를 다잡아 그것을 생각하고, 그것이 끔찍하고 서러운 일임을 인정할밖에 없었으니, 안 그러면 저 자신을 향한 이 부름에 딸린 그 온갖 마음 졸이고 다급하고 기쁜 걱정거리 한복판에서 그것이 그녀에게서 새어 나가곤 했던 것이다."]], [["There is nothing like employment, active indispensable employment, for relieving sorrow.", "슬픔을 더는 데는 일거리, 활발하고 없어선 안 될 일거리만 한 것이 없다."], ["Employment, even melancholy, may dispel melancholy, and her occupations were hopeful.", "일거리는, 서글픈 일거리마저도 서글픔을 흩어 버릴 수 있으니, 그녀의 할 일은 바람에 찬 것이었다."], ["She had so much to do, that not even the horrible story of Mrs. Rushworth (now fixed to the last point of certainty), could affect her as it had done before.", "할 일이 하도 많아, 러시워스 부인의 그 소름 끼치는 사연(이제 확실함의 마지막 지점에 못 박힌)조차 앞서만큼 그녀에게 영향 미치지 못했다."], ["She had not time to be miserable.", "그녀는 비참해할 겨를이 없었다."], ["Within twenty-four hours she was hoping to be gone; her father and mother must be spoken to, Susan prepared, everything got ready.", "스물네 시간 안에 그녀는 떠나기를 바라고 있었다. 아버지와 어머니에게 말해야 했고, 수전을 채비시켜야 했고, 온갖 것을 준비해야 했다."], ["Business followed business; the day was hardly long enough.", "볼일에 볼일이 뒤따랐다. 하루가 도무지 넉넉지 않았다."], ["The happiness she was imparting, too, happiness very little alloyed by the black communication which must briefly precede it—the joyful consent of her father and mother to Susan’s going with her—the general satisfaction with which the going of both seemed regarded, and the ecstasy of Susan herself, was all serving to support her spirits.", "게다가 그녀가 나누어 주는 그 행복도—그에 앞서 짤막이 전해야 할 그 시커먼 소식으로도 거의 흐려지지 않는 행복도, 수전이 저와 함께 가는 데 대한 아버지와 어머니의 기쁜 승낙도, 둘의 떠남이 두루 흐뭇하게 여겨지는 듯한 것도, 수전 자신의 황홀함도, 죄다 그녀의 기운을 떠받쳐 주고 있었다."]], [["The affliction of the Bertrams was little felt in the family. Mrs.", "버트럼가의 괴로움은 그 집안에서 별로 느껴지지 않았다."], ["Price talked of her poor sister for a few minutes, but how to find anything to hold Susan’s clothes, because Rebecca took away all the boxes and spoilt them, was much more in her thoughts: and as for Susan, now unexpectedly gratified in the first wish of her heart, and knowing nothing personally of those who had sinned, or of those who were sorrowing—if she could help rejoicing from beginning to end, it was as much as ought to be expected from human virtue at fourteen.", "프라이스 부인은 몇 분 제 딱한 언니 얘기를 했으나, 리베카가 상자를 죄다 가져가 망쳐 놓은 탓에 수전의 옷을 담을 무언가를 어떻게 찾을까 하는 것이 그녀 생각에 훨씬 더 많이 있었다. 그리고 수전으로 말하면, 이제 뜻밖에 제 마음의 첫 바람이 채워진 데다, 죄지은 이들도 서러워하는 이들도 몸소 아는 바 없으니—처음부터 끝까지 기뻐하기를 억누를 수 있었다면, 그것만으로도 열넷의 인간의 덕에서 바랄 만한 것을 다한 셈이었다."]], [["As nothing was really left for the decision of Mrs. Price, or the good offices of Rebecca, everything was rationally and duly accomplished, and the girls were ready for the morrow.", "정말이지 프라이스 부인의 결정이나 리베카의 도움에 맡겨진 것이 아무것도 없던지라, 온갖 것이 이치에 맞게 마땅히 이뤄졌고, 두 아가씨는 이튿날을 위한 채비가 되었다."], ["The advantage of much sleep to prepare them for their journey was impossible.", "여정을 위해 그들을 다잡아 줄 넉넉한 잠이란 어림없었다."], ["The cousin who was travelling towards them could hardly have less than visited their agitated spirits—one all happiness, the other all varying and indescribable perturbation.", "그들을 향해 오고 있는 사촌이, 마음 졸이는 그들의 심경을—하나는 온통 행복이요, 다른 하나는 온통 변덕스럽고 이루 말할 수 없는 어지러움인 그 심경을 좀처럼 찾아들지 않았을 리 없었으니 말이다."]], [["By eight in the morning Edmund was in the house.", "아침 여덟 시에 에드먼드가 집에 들었다."], ["The girls heard his entrance from above, and Fanny went down.", "두 아가씨는 위에서 그가 들어오는 것을 들었고, 패니가 내려갔다."], ["The idea of immediately seeing him, with the knowledge of what he must be suffering, brought back all her own first feelings.", "그가 무엇을 겪고 있을지 아는 채로 그를 당장 본다는 생각이, 그녀 자신의 그 온갖 첫 심경을 되불러 왔다. 그가 그토록 저 가까이에, 그것도 비참함 속에 있다니."], ["He so near her, and in misery.", "그녀는 응접실에 들어서며 주저앉을 것만 같았다."], ["She was ready to sink as she entered the parlour.", "그는 혼자였고, 대번에 그녀를 맞았다. 그리고 그녀는, 겨우 또렷한 이 몇 마디만과 함께 그의 가슴에 꼭 안긴 자신을 발견했다."], ["He was alone, and met her instantly; and she found herself pressed to his heart with only these words, just articulate, “My Fanny, my only sister; my only comfort now!”", "“내 패니, 내 하나뿐인 누이. 이제 내 하나뿐인 위안!”"], ["She could say nothing; nor for some minutes could he say more.", "그녀는 아무 말도 할 수 없었다. 몇 분 동안은 그도 더 말할 수 없었다."]], [["He turned away to recover himself, and when he spoke again, though his voice still faltered, his manner shewed the wish of self-command, and the resolution of avoiding any farther allusion.", "그는 저를 추스르려 돌아섰다. 그리고 다시 말할 때, 그 목소리는 여전히 떨렸으되, 그 태도는 자제하려는 바람과, 더는 그 일을 넌지시 비치지 않으려는 결심을 보였다."], ["“Have you breakfasted?", "“아침 먹었니?"], ["When shall you be ready?", "언제 채비되겠어?"], ["Does Susan go?” were questions following each other rapidly.", "수전도 가고?”가 잇달아 재빨리 뒤따른 물음이었다."], ["His great object was to be off as soon as possible.", "그의 큰 목표는 되도록 빨리 떠나는 것이었다."], ["When Mansfield was considered, time was precious; and the state of his own mind made him find relief only in motion.", "맨스필드를 헤아리면 시간이 소중했다. 그리고 그 자신의 마음 상태가 그로 하여금 오직 움직임에서만 안도를 찾게 했다."], ["It was settled that he should order the carriage to the door in half an hour.", "그가 반 시간 안에 마차를 문으로 대게 하는 것으로 정해졌다."], ["Fanny answered for their having breakfasted and being quite ready in half an hour.", "패니는 그들이 아침을 먹고 반 시간 안에 아주 채비되어 있으리라 장담했다."], ["He had already ate, and declined staying for their meal.", "그는 이미 먹은 터라 그들의 끼니를 위해 머물기를 마다했다."], ["He would walk round the ramparts, and join them with the carriage.", "그는 성벽을 한 바퀴 돌다가 마차와 함께 그들과 합류하려 했다."], ["He was gone again; glad to get away even from Fanny.", "그는 다시 나갔다. 패니에게서마저 벗어나는 것이 반가운 채였다."]], [["He looked very ill; evidently suffering under violent emotions, which he was determined to suppress.", "그는 자못 아파 보였다. 분명 억누르기로 굳게 마음먹은 세찬 심경 아래 괴로워하고 있었다."], ["She knew it must be so, but it was terrible to her.", "그녀는 그럴밖에 없음을 알았으나, 그것이 그녀에겐 끔찍했다."]], [["The carriage came; and he entered the house again at the same moment, just in time to spend a few minutes with the family, and be a witness—but that he saw nothing—of the tranquil manner in which the daughters were parted with, and just in time to prevent their sitting down to the breakfast-table, which, by dint of much unusual activity, was quite and completely ready as the carriage drove from the door.", "마차가 왔다. 그리고 그가 바로 그 순간 다시 집에 들었으니, 마침 식구와 몇 분을 보내고, 딸들이 얼마나 잔잔한 태도로 떠나보내지는지의—다만 그는 아무것도 못 봤지만—증인이 되기에 딱 맞춰서였고, 또 그들이 아침 식탁에 앉는 것을 막기에 딱 맞춰서였다."], ["Fanny’s last meal in her father’s house was in character with her first: she was dismissed from it as hospitably as she had been welcomed.", "그 식탁은, 자못 유별난 부지런함으로, 마차가 문에서 떠날 제 아주 온전히 차려져 있었다. 아버지 집에서의 패니의 마지막 끼니는 그 첫 끼니와 한 결이었으니, 그녀는 맞아들여졌던 것만큼이나 살갑게 그 자리에서 내보내졌다."]], [["How her heart swelled with joy and gratitude as she passed the barriers of Portsmouth, and how Susan’s face wore its broadest smiles, may be easily conceived.", "포츠머스의 성문을 지날 제 그녀의 마음이 기쁨과 고마움으로 얼마나 벅찼는지, 수전의 얼굴이 얼마나 활짝 웃음을 띠었는지는 쉬 그려 볼 수 있다."], ["Sitting forwards, however, and screened by her bonnet, those smiles were unseen.", "그러나 앞쪽에 앉아 제 보닛에 가려, 그 웃음은 안 보였다."]], [["The journey was likely to be a silent one.", "그 여정은 잠잠한 것이 될 성싶었다."], ["Edmund’s deep sighs often reached Fanny.", "에드먼드의 깊은 한숨이 곧잘 패니에게 닿았다."], ["Had he been alone with her, his heart must have opened in spite of every resolution; but Susan’s presence drove him quite into himself, and his attempts to talk on indifferent subjects could never be long supported.", "그가 그녀와 단둘이 있었다면, 그의 마음은 온갖 결심에도 불구하고 열렸을 게 틀림없었다. 그러나 수전이 있어 그를 아주 제 속으로 몰아넣었고, 대수롭잖은 화제로 얘기해 보려는 그의 애씀은 결코 오래 이어지지 못했다."]], [["Fanny watched him with never-failing solicitude, and sometimes catching his eye, revived an affectionate smile, which comforted her; but the first day’s journey passed without her hearing a word from him on the subjects that were weighing him down.", "패니는 그침 없는 근심으로 그를 지켜보며, 이따금 그의 눈길과 마주쳐 정겨운 미소를 되살렸고, 그것이 그녀를 위로했다. 그러나 첫날 여정은, 그를 짓누르는 그 일에 대해 그에게서 한마디도 못 들은 채 지나갔다."], ["The next morning produced a little more.", "이튿날 아침이 조금 더 안겨 주었다."], ["Just before their setting out from Oxford, while Susan was stationed at a window, in eager observation of the departure of a large family from the inn, the other two were standing by the fire; and Edmund, particularly struck by the alteration in Fanny’s looks, and from his ignorance of the daily evils of her father’s house, attributing an undue share of the change, attributing all to the recent event, took her hand, and said in a low, but very expressive tone, “No wonder—you must feel it—you must suffer.", "옥스퍼드를 막 떠나기 전, 수전이 여관에서 한 대식구가 떠나는 것을 못내 지켜보며 창가에 서 있는 동안, 다른 둘은 난롯가에 서 있었다. 그리고 에드먼드는, 패니의 낯빛의 달라짐에 유난히 사무쳐, 그리고 그녀 아버지 집의 나날의 화를 모르는 탓에 그 달라짐의 지나친 몫을—죄다 그 근래의 일 탓으로 돌리며, 그녀의 손을 잡고 나직하나 자못 뜻 어린 투로 말했다."], ["How a man who had once loved, could desert you!", "“놀랄 것 없지—너는 사무칠밖에—너는 괴로울밖에."], ["But yours—your regard was new compared with——Fanny, think of me!”", "한때 사랑했던 사내가 어찌 너를 저버릴 수 있단 말이냐! 하지만 너의—너의 정은 저것에 견주면 새것이었지——패니, 나를 생각해 다오!”"]], [["The first division of their journey occupied a long day, and brought them, almost knocked up, to Oxford; but the second was over at a much earlier hour.", "그들 여정의 첫 자락은 긴 하루를 차지하며, 거의 나가떨어진 그들을 옥스퍼드에 데려다주었다. 그러나 둘째 자락은 훨씬 이른 시각에 끝났다."], ["They were in the environs of Mansfield long before the usual dinner-time, and as they approached the beloved place, the hearts of both sisters sank a little.", "그들은 여느 만찬 시각보다 한참 전에 맨스필드 어귀에 이르렀고, 그 사랑하는 곳에 다가갈 제 두 자매의 마음이 다 얼마간 내려앉았다."], ["Fanny began to dread the meeting with her aunts and Tom, under so dreadful a humiliation; and Susan to feel with some anxiety, that all her best manners, all her lately acquired knowledge of what was practised here, was on the point of being called into action.", "패니는 그런 소름 끼치는 치욕 아래 이모들과 톰을 마주하는 것이 두려워지기 시작했고, 수전은 얼마간의 마음 졸임으로, 제 온갖 가장 좋은 몸가짐이, 여기서 행해지는 것에 대해 근래 얻은 온갖 앎이 막 부려질 참임을 느끼기 시작했다."], ["Visions of good and ill breeding, of old vulgarisms and new gentilities, were before her; and she was meditating much upon silver forks, napkins, and finger-glasses.", "좋은 교양과 나쁜 교양의, 옛 상스러움과 새 점잖음의 그림이 그녀 눈앞에 있었고, 그녀는 은 포크와 냅킨과 핑거볼을 자못 곰곰 헤아리고 있었다."], ["Fanny had been everywhere awake to the difference of the country since February; but when they entered the Park her perceptions and her pleasures were of the keenest sort.", "패니는 이월 이래 어디서나 시골의 그 달라짐에 눈떠 있었다. 그러나 그들이 저택에 들어서니 그녀의 알아챔과 즐거움이 더없이 예리한 종류의 것이 되었다."], ["It was three months, full three months, since her quitting it, and the change was from winter to summer.", "그녀가 그곳을 떠난 지 석 달, 꼬박 석 달이었고, 그 바뀜은 겨울에서 여름으로였다."], ["Her eye fell everywhere on lawns and plantations of the freshest green; and the trees, though not fully clothed, were in that delightful state when farther beauty is known to be at hand, and when, while much is actually given to the sight, more yet remains for the imagination.", "그녀의 눈은 어디서나 더없이 싱그러운 초록의 잔디밭과 나무숲에 닿았다. 그리고 나무들은, 온전히 옷 입지는 않았으되, 더한 아름다움이 코앞에 있음을 알 만한, 그리고 눈에 실제로 많은 것이 주어지면서도 상상에 더 많은 것이 남아 있는 그 흐뭇한 상태에 있었다."], ["Her enjoyment, however, was for herself alone.", "그러나 그녀의 즐거움은 저 혼자만의 것이었다."], ["Edmund could not share it.", "에드먼드는 그것을 나눌 수 없었다."], ["She looked at him, but he was leaning back, sunk in a deeper gloom than ever, and with eyes closed, as if the view of cheerfulness oppressed him, and the lovely scenes of home must be shut out.", "그녀가 그를 바라보니, 그는 여느 때보다 더 깊은 침울함에 잠겨 뒤로 기댄 채, 마치 그 명랑한 경치가 저를 짓누르고 집의 그 사랑스러운 광경을 물리쳐야 하는 양 눈을 감고 있었다."]], [["It made her melancholy again; and the knowledge of what must be enduring there, invested even the house, modern, airy, and well situated as it was, with a melancholy aspect.", "그것이 그녀를 다시금 서글프게 했다. 그리고 거기서 무엇이 견뎌지고 있을지 아는 것이, 요즘풍에 바람 잘 통하고 자리 좋은 그 집마저도 서글픈 낯빛으로 뒤덮었다."]], [["By one of the suffering party within they were expected with such impatience as she had never known before.", "안에서 괴로워하는 무리 가운데 한 사람은 그녀를 여태 알던 적 없는 그런 못내 애타는 마음으로 기다리고 있었다."], ["Fanny had scarcely passed the solemn-looking servants, when Lady Bertram came from the drawing-room to meet her; came with no indolent step; and falling on her neck, said, “Dear Fanny!", "패니가 근엄해 보이는 하인들을 채 지나기도 전에, 버트럼 부인이 그녀를 맞으러 응접실에서 나왔다. 게으른 걸음이 아니게 나와, 그녀 목에 매달리며 말했다."], ["now I shall be comfortable.”", "“사랑하는 패니! 이제 나는 편안하겠구나.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XLVII", "# 제47장"]], [["It had been a miserable party, each of the three believing themselves most miserable.", "그것은 비참한 무리였으니, 셋이 저마다 저 자신을 가장 비참하다 여겼다."], ["Mrs. Norris, however, as most attached to Maria, was really the greatest sufferer.", "그러나 노리스 부인은 마리아에게 가장 애착했던지라 정말이지 가장 크게 괴로워하는 이였다."], ["Maria was her first favourite, the dearest of all; the match had been her own contriving, as she had been wont with such pride of heart to feel and say, and this conclusion of it almost overpowered her.", "마리아는 그녀의 첫째가는 귀염둥이, 온갖 이 가운데 가장 소중한 이였다. 그 혼사는, 그녀가 그런 뿌듯한 마음으로 곧잘 느끼고 말하곤 했듯, 저 자신이 꾸민 것이었고, 그것의 이런 결말이 그녀를 거의 무너뜨렸다."]], [["She was an altered creature, quieted, stupefied, indifferent to everything that passed.", "그녀는 달라진 사람이었으니, 잠잠해지고, 얼떨떨해지고, 벌어지는 온갖 것에 무심했다."], ["The being left with her sister and nephew, and all the house under her care, had been an advantage entirely thrown away; she had been unable to direct or dictate, or even fancy herself useful.", "아우와 조카와 함께 남겨져 온 집을 제 손에 맡은 것은 아주 헛되이 던져진 이점이었다. 그녀는 지휘하거나 지시할 수도, 심지어 저가 쓸모 있다 여길 수조차 없었던 것이다."], ["When really touched by affliction, her active powers had been all benumbed; and neither Lady Bertram nor Tom had received from her the smallest support or attempt at support.", "정말로 괴로움에 사무치니 그녀의 활발한 힘이 죄다 마비되었다. 그리하여 버트럼 부인도 톰도 그녀에게서 조금의 떠받침도, 떠받침의 시늉도 받지 못했다."], ["She had done no more for them than they had done for each other.", "그녀가 그들을 위해 한 것은 그들이 서로를 위해 한 것 이상이 아니었다."], ["They had been all solitary, helpless, and forlorn alike; and now the arrival of the others only established her superiority in wretchedness.", "그들은 다 함께 외롭고, 힘없고, 쓸쓸했다. 그리고 이제 다른 이들의 도착은 그저 비참함에서 그녀의 우위를 굳혀 놓을 뿐이었다."], ["Her companions were relieved, but there was no good for her.", "그녀의 동무들은 놓였으나, 그녀에게는 아무 좋음이 없었다."], ["Edmund was almost as welcome to his brother as Fanny to her aunt; but Mrs. Norris, instead of having comfort from either, was but the more irritated by the sight of the person whom, in the blindness of her anger, she could have charged as the daemon of the piece.", "에드먼드는 그 형에게 패니가 이모에게 그러한 것에 거의 못지않게 반가웠으나, 노리스 부인은 어느 쪽에서도 위안을 얻기는커녕, 제 노여움의 눈멂 속에 이 일의 악마라 몰아붙일 수도 있었을 그 사람을 보는 데 그저 더 부아만 돋을 뿐이었다."], ["Had Fanny accepted Mr. Crawford this could not have happened.", "패니가 크로퍼드 씨를 받아들였다면 이런 일은 일어날 수 없었으리라."]], [["Susan too was a grievance.", "수전 또한 성가심이었다."], ["She had not spirits to notice her in more than a few repulsive looks, but she felt her as a spy, and an intruder, and an indigent niece, and everything most odious.", "그녀는 그녀를 몇 번의 못마땅한 눈길 이상으로 아는 체할 기운이 없었으나, 그녀를 첩자로, 침입자로, 궁색한 조카딸로, 온갖 더없이 밉살스러운 것으로 느꼈다."], ["By her other aunt, Susan was received with quiet kindness.", "다른 이모에게서 수전은 조용한 친절로 맞아졌다."], ["Lady Bertram could not give her much time, or many words, but she felt her, as Fanny’s sister, to have a claim at Mansfield, and was ready to kiss and like her; and Susan was more than satisfied, for she came perfectly aware that nothing but ill-humour was to be expected from aunt Norris; and was so provided with happiness, so strong in that best of blessings, an escape from many certain evils, that she could have stood against a great deal more indifference than she met with from the others.", "버트럼 부인은 그녀에게 많은 시간이나 많은 말을 줄 수 없었으나, 그녀를 패니의 누이로서 맨스필드에 응당한 몫이 있다 느껴, 기꺼이 입 맞추고 좋아하려 했다. 그리고 수전은 그 이상으로 흡족했으니, 노리스 이모에게서는 심술 말고는 바랄 게 없음을 아주 잘 알고 온 데다, 하도 행복으로 채워지고, 그 온갖 복 가운데 으뜸인 것—숱한 확실한 화에서 벗어남에 하도 든든해, 다른 이들에게서 받은 것보다 훨씬 더한 무심함에도 맞설 수 있었을 것이기 때문이다."]], [["She was now left a good deal to herself, to get acquainted with the house and grounds as she could, and spent her days very happily in so doing, while those who might otherwise have attended to her were shut up, or wholly occupied each with the person quite dependent on them, at this time, for everything like comfort; Edmund trying to bury his own feelings in exertions for the relief of his brother’s, and Fanny devoted to her aunt Bertram, returning to every former office with more than former zeal, and thinking she could never do enough for one who seemed so much to want her.", "그녀는 이제 자못 저 혼자에 내맡겨져, 되도록 집과 그 땅과 낯을 트게 되었고, 그리하며 나날을 자못 행복하게 보냈다. 한편 안 그랬으면 그녀를 챙겼을 이들은 갇혀 있거나, 이 무렵 편안함 비슷한 온갖 것을 저에게 온전히 기대는 그 사람에게 저마다 온통 매여 있었다. 에드먼드는 형의 괴로움을 덜려는 애씀에 제 심경을 묻으려 애썼고, 패니는 버트럼 이모에게 몸을 바쳐, 예전의 온갖 소임에 예전 이상의 열의로 돌아가, 저를 그토록 많이 아쉬워하는 듯한 이를 위해서라면 아무리 해도 모자라다 여겼다."]], [["To talk over the dreadful business with Fanny, talk and lament, was all Lady Bertram’s consolation.", "그 소름 끼치는 일을 패니와 함께 얘기하고, 얘기하고 한탄하는 것이 버트럼 부인의 유일한 위안이었다."], ["To be listened to and borne with, and hear the voice of kindness and sympathy in return, was everything that could be done for her.", "귀 기울여지고 참아지며, 그 답례로 친절과 공감의 목소리를 듣는 것이 그녀를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 전부였다."], ["To be otherwise comforted was out of the question.", "달리 위로받기란 어림없었다."], ["The case admitted of no comfort.", "그 일은 아무 위안도 허락하지 않았다."], ["Lady Bertram did not think deeply, but, guided by Sir Thomas, she thought justly on all important points; and she saw, therefore, in all its enormity, what had happened, and neither endeavoured herself, nor required Fanny to advise her, to think little of guilt and infamy.", "버트럼 부인은 깊이 생각하지 못했으나, 토머스 경에게 이끌려 온갖 중요한 점에서 올바로 생각했다. 그리하여 그녀는 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 그 온갖 흉악함 그대로 보았고, 죄와 불명예를 대수롭잖게 여기려 스스로 애쓰지도, 패니더러 그리 이끌라 요구하지도 않았다."]], [["Her affections were not acute, nor was her mind tenacious.", "그녀의 정은 예리하지 않았고, 그 마음은 질기지 않았다."], ["After a time, Fanny found it not impossible to direct her thoughts to other subjects, and revive some interest in the usual occupations; but whenever Lady Bertram was fixed on the event, she could see it only in one light, as comprehending the loss of a daughter, and a disgrace never to be wiped off.", "얼마 뒤 패니는 그녀의 생각을 다른 화제로 돌려 여느 일거리에 얼마간 관심을 되살리는 것이 어림없지 않음을 알았다. 그러나 버트럼 부인이 그 일에 마음이 붙박일 때면 언제나, 그녀는 그것을 오직 한 빛으로만—딸을 잃음과, 결코 씻길 수 없는 치욕을 아우르는 것으로만 볼 수 있었다."]], [["Fanny learnt from her all the particulars which had yet transpired.", "패니는 그녀에게서, 아직 밝혀진 온갖 낱낱을 다 알게 되었다."], ["Her aunt was no very methodical narrator, but with the help of some letters to and from Sir Thomas, and what she already knew herself, and could reasonably combine, she was soon able to understand quite as much as she wished of the circumstances attending the story.", "이모는 자못 조리 있는 이야기꾼은 아니었으나, 토머스 경에게 오간 몇몇 편지의 도움과, 저 자신이 이미 아는 것과, 웬만큼 짜맞출 수 있는 것으로, 그녀는 이내 그 사연에 딸린 사정을 저가 바라는 만큼 훤히 헤아릴 수 있었다."]], [["Mrs. Rushworth had gone, for the Easter holidays, to Twickenham, with a family whom she had just grown intimate with: a family of lively, agreeable manners, and probably of morals and discretion to suit, for to their house Mr.", "러시워스 부인은 부활절 휴가 동안, 마침 가까워진 한 집안과 함께 트위크넘에 가 있었다."], ["Crawford had constant access at all times.", "발랄하고 상냥한 태도의 집안, 그리고 아마 거기 걸맞은 품행과 분별의 집안이었으니, 그들 집엔 크로퍼드 씨가 언제든 늘 드나들었던 것이다."], ["His having been in the same neighbourhood Fanny already knew. Mr.", "그가 같은 근방에 있었다는 것은 패니도 이미 알았다."], ["Rushworth had been gone at this time to Bath, to pass a few days with his mother, and bring her back to town, and Maria was with these friends without any restraint, without even Julia; for Julia had removed from Wimpole Street two or three weeks before, on a visit to some relations of Sir Thomas; a removal which her father and mother were now disposed to attribute to some view of convenience on Mr.", "러시워스 씨는 이때 바스에 가 있었으니, 며칠 제 어머니와 지내고 시내로 모셔 오려던 것이었고, 마리아는 이 벗들과 아무런 잡도리 없이, 줄리아조차 없이 있었다. 줄리아가 두세 주 전 윔폴 스트리트를 떠나 토머스 경의 어느 친척을 찾아간 것인데, 그 떠남을 이제 그 부모는 예이츠 씨에 얽힌 무슨 편의를 노린 것으로 돌리는 쪽이었다."], ["Yates’s account.", "러시워스 내외가 윔폴 스트리트로 돌아온 지 얼마 안 되어, 토머스 경은 런던의 오랜 더없이 각별한 벗에게서 편지를 한 통 받았다."], ["Very soon after the Rushworths’ return to Wimpole Street, Sir Thomas had received a letter from an old and most particular friend in London, who hearing and witnessing a good deal to alarm him in that quarter, wrote to recommend Sir Thomas’s coming to London himself, and using his influence with his daughter to put an end to the intimacy which was already exposing her to unpleasant remarks, and evidently making Mr. Rushworth uneasy.", "그는 그 방면에서 그를 놀라게 할 만한 것을 자못 많이 듣고 목격한지라, 토머스 경이 몸소 런던에 와서 딸에게 힘을 써, 이미 그녀를 언짢은 입길에 오르게 하고 분명 러시워스 씨를 거북하게 만들고 있는 그 가까움을 끝내라 권하려 쓴 것이었다."]], [["Sir Thomas was preparing to act upon this letter, without communicating its contents to any creature at Mansfield, when it was followed by another, sent express from the same friend, to break to him the almost desperate situation in which affairs then stood with the young people. Mrs. Rushworth had left her husband’s house: Mr. Rushworth had been in great anger and distress to him (Mr. Harding) for his advice; Mr. Harding feared there had been at least very flagrant indiscretion. The maidservant of Mrs. Rushworth, senior, threatened alarmingly. He was doing all in his power to quiet everything, with the hope of Mrs. Rushworth’s return, but was so much counteracted in Wimpole Street by the influence of Mr. Rushworth’s mother, that the worst consequences might be apprehended.", "토머스 경은 그 내용을 맨스필드의 어떤 사람에게도 알리지 않고 이 편지대로 움직이려 채비하던 참에, 같은 벗에게서 속달로 부쳐진 또 한 통이 뒤따랐다. 그때 두 젊은이 사이의 사정이 놓인 그 거의 절망적인 처지를 그에게 알리려는 것이었다. 러시워스 부인이 제 남편의 집을 떠났고, 러시워스 씨가 크게 노하고 괴로워하며 그이(하딩 씨)에게 조언을 구했다는 것, 하딩 씨가 적어도 자못 노골적인 경솔함이 있었으려니 두려워한다는 것이었다. 러시워스 노부인의 하녀가 놀랍게도 위협을 해 왔다. 그는 러시워스 부인의 돌아옴을 바라며 온갖 것을 잠재우려 제 힘닿는 데까지 하고 있었으나, 윔폴 스트리트에서 러시워스 씨 어머니의 영향에 하도 방해받아, 가장 나쁜 결말이 두려워질 만했다."]], [["This dreadful communication could not be kept from the rest of the family.", "이 소름 끼치는 소식은 나머지 식구에게 감출 수 없었다."], ["Sir Thomas set off, Edmund would go with him, and the others had been left in a state of wretchedness, inferior only to what followed the receipt of the next letters from London.", "토머스 경이 떠났고, 에드먼드가 함께 가려 했으며, 나머지는 비참함의 상태에 남겨졌으니, 런던에서 온 다음 편지들을 받은 뒤 뒤따른 것에만 못한 비참함이었다."], ["Everything was by that time public beyond a hope. The servant of Mrs.", "그때쯤엔 온갖 것이 바랄 나위 없이 세상에 드러나 있었다."], ["Rushworth, the mother, had exposure in her power, and supported by her mistress, was not to be silenced.", "어머니 러시워스 부인의 하인이 폭로할 힘을 쥐고 있었고, 제 안주인의 뒷받침을 받아 잠재워질 수 없었다."], ["The two ladies, even in the short time they had been together, had disagreed; and the bitterness of the elder against her daughter-in-law might perhaps arise almost as much from the personal disrespect with which she had herself been treated as from sensibility for her son.", "두 부인은 함께 있은 그 짧은 동안에조차 틀어졌다. 그리고 며느리에 대한 손위 쪽의 그 쓰라림은, 어쩌면 제 아들을 위하는 마음에서만큼이나, 저 자신이 받은 그 대접의 무시에서 우러난 것이었으리라."]], [["However that might be, she was unmanageable.", "그것이 어떠했든, 그녀는 다룰 수 없는 이였다."], ["But had she been less obstinate, or of less weight with her son, who was always guided by the last speaker, by the person who could get hold of and shut him up, the case would still have been hopeless, for Mrs.", "그러나 그녀가 덜 고집스럽거나, 늘 마지막에 말한 이에게, 저를 붙들어 가둘 수 있는 이에게 이끌리는 아들에게 무게가 덜했다 해도, 그 일은 여전히 가망 없었으리라."], ["Rushworth did not appear again, and there was every reason to conclude her to be concealed somewhere with Mr.", "러시워스 부인이 다시는 나타나지 않았고, 그녀가 어딘가에 크로퍼드 씨와 함께 숨어 있으려니 여길 온갖 까닭이 있었기 때문이다."], ["Crawford, who had quitted his uncle’s house, as for a journey, on the very day of her absenting herself.", "그는 바로 그녀가 몸을 감춘 그날, 여행이라도 가는 양 삼촌 집을 떠났던 것이다."]], [["Sir Thomas, however, remained yet a little longer in town, in the hope of discovering and snatching her from farther vice, though all was lost on the side of character.", "그래도 토머스 경은, 됨됨이의 면에서야 죄다 잃었을망정, 그녀를 찾아내어 더한 악덕에서 낚아채기를 바라며 시내에 얼마간 더 머물렀다."]], [["His present state Fanny could hardly bear to think of.", "그의 지금 상태를 패니는 좀처럼 생각을 견딜 수 없었다."], ["There was but one of his children who was not at this time a source of misery to him.", "그의 자식 가운데 이때 그에게 비참함의 샘이 아닌 이는 딱 하나뿐이었다."], ["Tom’s complaints had been greatly heightened by the shock of his sister’s conduct, and his recovery so much thrown back by it, that even Lady Bertram had been struck by the difference, and all her alarms were regularly sent off to her husband; and Julia’s elopement, the additional blow which had met him on his arrival in London, though its force had been deadened at the moment, must, she knew, be sorely felt.", "톰의 병은 누이의 처신이 준 충격으로 자못 깊어졌고, 그 나음이 그로 인해 자못 뒷걸음질 쳤던지라, 버트럼 부인조차 그 달라짐에 사무쳐, 그 온갖 놀라움을 꼬박꼬박 남편에게 부쳐 보냈다. 그리고 줄리아의 사랑의 도피, 곧 런던에 이르러 그를 맞은 그 보탠 타격은, 비록 그 순간엔 그 힘이 무디어졌을망정, 뼈저리게 사무칠 게 틀림없음을 그녀는 알았다."], ["She saw that it was.", "그녀는 그러함을 보았다."], ["His letters expressed how much he deplored it.", "그의 편지가 그가 그것을 얼마나 개탄하는지를 드러냈다."], ["Under any circumstances it would have been an unwelcome alliance; but to have it so clandestinely formed, and such a period chosen for its completion, placed Julia’s feelings in a most unfavourable light, and severely aggravated the folly of her choice.", "어떤 사정에서든 그것은 달갑잖은 인연이었으련만, 그것이 그토록 은밀히 맺어지고, 그것을 이루는 데 그런 때가 골라진 것은, 줄리아의 심경을 더없이 안 좋은 빛으로 놓았고 그 고름의 어리석음을 모질게 더했다."], ["He called it a bad thing, done in the worst manner, and at the worst time; and though Julia was yet as more pardonable than Maria as folly than vice, he could not but regard the step she had taken as opening the worst probabilities of a conclusion hereafter like her sister’s.", "그는 그것을 나쁜 일이라, 가장 나쁜 식으로, 가장 나쁜 때에 저질러진 것이라 불렀다. 그리고 줄리아가 아직, 악덕보다 어리석음이 그렇듯 마리아보다 더 용서할 만하긴 했으나, 그는 그녀가 밟은 그 걸음을 두고, 훗날 그 언니와 같은 결말의 가장 나쁜 가망을 여는 것으로 여길밖에 없었다."], ["Such was his opinion of the set into which she had thrown herself.", "그녀가 뛰어든 그 무리에 대한 그의 생각이 그러했다."]], [["Fanny felt for him most acutely.", "패니는 그를 더없이 뼈저리게 안쓰러워했다."], ["He could have no comfort but in Edmund.", "그에게는 에드먼드 말고는 아무 위안이 있을 수 없었다."], ["Every other child must be racking his heart.", "다른 자식마다 그의 마음을 쥐어뜯을 게 틀림없었다."], ["His displeasure against herself she trusted, reasoning differently from Mrs. Norris, would now be done away.", "저에 대한 그의 언짢음은, 노리스 부인과 다르게 헤아리건대, 이제 사라지려니 그녀는 믿었다."], ["She should be justified. Mr.", "그녀는 옳았음이 밝혀질 터였다."], ["Crawford would have fully acquitted her conduct in refusing him; but this, though most material to herself, would be poor consolation to Sir Thomas.", "크로퍼드 씨가 그를 마다한 그녀의 처신을 온전히 옳다 해 줬을 터였다. 그러나 이것은, 저 자신에게야 더없이 요긴할지언정, 토머스 경에게는 시원찮은 위안일 터였다."], ["Her uncle’s displeasure was terrible to her; but what could her justification or her gratitude and attachment do for him?", "삼촌의 언짢음이 그녀에겐 끔찍했으나, 그녀의 떳떳함이나 그녀의 고마움과 애착이 그를 위해 무엇을 할 수 있으랴?"], ["His stay must be on Edmund alone.", "그가 기댈 곳은 오직 에드먼드뿐일 게 틀림없었다."]], [["She was mistaken, however, in supposing that Edmund gave his father no present pain.", "그러나 에드먼드가 제 아버지에게 지금 아무 아픔도 안 준다 여긴 것은 그녀의 착각이었다."], ["It was of a much less poignant nature than what the others excited; but Sir Thomas was considering his happiness as very deeply involved in the offence of his sister and friend; cut off by it, as he must be, from the woman whom he had been pursuing with undoubted attachment and strong probability of success; and who, in everything but this despicable brother, would have been so eligible a connexion.", "그것은 다른 이들이 자아낸 것보다 훨씬 덜 저미는 성질의 것이었으나, 토머스 경은 그의 행복이 제 누이와 벗의 죄에 자못 깊이 얽혀 있다 여기고 있었다. 그가 그로 인해, 의심할 나위 없는 애착과 성공의 큰 가망으로 좇아 오던 그 여자에게서—이 천한 오라비만 빼면 자못 어울리는 인연이었을 그 여자에게서 떼어져야 할 게 틀림없었으니 말이다."], ["He was aware of what Edmund must be suffering on his own behalf, in addition to all the rest, when they were in town: he had seen or conjectured his feelings; and, having reason to think that one interview with Miss Crawford had taken place, from which Edmund derived only increased distress, had been as anxious on that account as on others to get him out of town, and had engaged him in taking Fanny home to her aunt, with a view to his relief and benefit, no less than theirs.", "그는, 그들이 시내에 있을 때 에드먼드가 그 온갖 나머지에 더해 제 몫으로 무엇을 겪고 있을지 알고 있었다. 그는 그의 심경을 보았거나 넘겨짚었고, 크로퍼드 양과의 한 번의 만남이 있었으며 에드먼드가 거기서 더한 괴로움만 얻었으려니 여길 만한 까닭이 있던지라, 다른 까닭 못지않게 그 까닭으로도 그를 시내에서 데리고 나오기를 애타게 바라, 그를 시켜 패니를 이모에게 데려가게 했으니, 그들의 안도와 이로움 못지않게 그의 안도와 이로움을 뜻해서였다."], ["Fanny was not in the secret of her uncle’s feelings, Sir Thomas not in the secret of Miss Crawford’s character.", "패니는 삼촌의 심경의 속내를 몰랐고, 토머스 경은 크로퍼드 양의 됨됨이의 속내를 몰랐다."], ["Had he been privy to her conversation with his son, he would not have wished her to belong to him, though her twenty thousand pounds had been forty.", "그가 그녀와 제 아들의 대화를 알았더라면, 그녀의 이만 파운드가 사만이었다 해도 그녀가 그의 사람이 되기를 바라지 않았으리라."]], [["That Edmund must be for ever divided from Miss Crawford did not admit of a doubt with Fanny; and yet, till she knew that he felt the same, her own conviction was insufficient.", "에드먼드가 크로퍼드 양과 영영 갈라져야 한다는 것은 패니에게 의심의 여지가 없었다. 그러면서도, 그가 같은 생각임을 알기까지는 저 자신의 확신이 넉넉지 않았다."], ["She thought he did, but she wanted to be assured of it.", "그가 그러려니 여겼으나, 그것을 확인받고 싶었다."], ["If he would now speak to her with the unreserve which had sometimes been too much for her before, it would be most consoling; but that she found was not to be.", "만일 그가 이제, 앞서 이따금 그녀에게 너무 벅차던 그 스스럼없음으로 저에게 얘기해 준다면 더없는 위안일 터였다. 그러나 그것은 안 될 일임을 그녀는 알았다."], ["She seldom saw him: never alone.", "그녀는 그를 좀처럼 못 봤고, 결코 단둘이서는 못 봤다."], ["He probably avoided being alone with her.", "그는 아마 그녀와 단둘이 있는 것을 피했으리라."], ["What was to be inferred?", "무엇을 미루어 알 수 있었으랴?"], ["That his judgment submitted to all his own peculiar and bitter share of this family affliction, but that it was too keenly felt to be a subject of the slightest communication.", "그의 판단은 이 집안 괴로움에서 제 몫인 그 온갖 남다르고 쓰라린 것을 다 받아들였으되, 그것이 조금이나마 얘기할 화제가 되기엔 너무 뼈저리게 사무친다는 것이었다."], ["This must be his state.", "이것이 그의 상태일 게 틀림없었다."], ["He yielded, but it was with agonies which did not admit of speech.", "그는 굽혔으되, 그것은 말을 허락하지 않는 고통과 함께였다."], ["Long, long would it be ere Miss Crawford’s name passed his lips again, or she could hope for a renewal of such confidential intercourse as had been.", "크로퍼드 양의 이름이 다시 그의 입에 오르거나, 그녀가 예전 같은 그런 속내를 터놓는 사귐의 되살아남을 바랄 수 있기까지는, 오래고 오랜 세월이 걸릴 터였다."]], [["It was long.", "정말이지 오래였다."], ["They reached Mansfield on Thursday, and it was not till Sunday evening that Edmund began to talk to her on the subject.", "그들은 목요일에 맨스필드에 이르렀는데, 에드먼드가 그 일을 두고 그녀에게 얘기하기 시작한 것은 일요일 저녁에야였다."], ["Sitting with her on Sunday evening—a wet Sunday evening—the very time of all others when, if a friend is at hand, the heart must be opened, and everything told; no one else in the room, except his mother, who, after hearing an affecting sermon, had cried herself to sleep, it was impossible not to speak; and so, with the usual beginnings, hardly to be traced as to what came first, and the usual declaration that if she would listen to him for a few minutes, he should be very brief, and certainly never tax her kindness in the same way again; she need not fear a repetition; it would be a subject prohibited entirely: he entered upon the luxury of relating circumstances and sensations of the first interest to himself, to one of whose affectionate sympathy he was quite convinced.", "일요일 저녁—비 오는 일요일 저녁, 곧 벗이 곁에 있으면 마음이 열려 온갖 것이 말해질밖에 없는, 온갖 것 가운데 바로 그런 때에 그녀와 함께 앉아, 방엔 그 어머니 말고 아무도 없이—그 어머니는 사무치는 설교를 들은 뒤 울다 잠들었으니—말하지 않기란 어림없었다. 그리하여, 무엇이 먼저였는지 좀처럼 좇을 수 없는 여느 서두와, 만일 그녀가 몇 분 저에게 귀 기울여 준다면 저는 자못 짤막하겠고 결코 다시는 같은 식으로 그녀의 친절을 부리지 않으리라는—되풀이는 두려워 말라는, 아주 금해진 화제가 되리라는 그 여느 다짐과 함께, 그는 저 자신에게 더없이 흥미로운 사정과 심경을, 그 정겨운 공감을 아주 확신하는 이에게 늘어놓는 호사에 들었다."]], [["How Fanny listened, with what curiosity and concern, what pain and what delight, how the agitation of his voice was watched, and how carefully her own eyes were fixed on any object but himself, may be imagined.", "패니가 어떤 궁금함과 걱정으로, 어떤 아픔과 어떤 기쁨으로 귀 기울였는지, 그의 목소리의 떨림이 어떻게 지켜졌는지, 그리고 그녀 자신의 눈이 어떻게 그가 아닌 다른 무엇에라도 애써 박혔는지는 그려 볼 수 있으리라."], ["The opening was alarming.", "그 서두가 놀라웠다."], ["He had seen Miss Crawford.", "그가 크로퍼드 양을 봤다는 것이었다."], ["He had been invited to see her.", "그녀를 보라 청받았다는 것이었다."], ["He had received a note from Lady Stornaway to beg him to call; and regarding it as what was meant to be the last, last interview of friendship, and investing her with all the feelings of shame and wretchedness which Crawford’s sister ought to have known, he had gone to her in such a state of mind, so softened, so devoted, as made it for a few moments impossible to Fanny’s fears that it should be the last.", "그는 스토너웨이 부인에게서 들러 달라는 쪽지를 받았고, 그것을 우정의 마지막, 마지막 만남으로 뜻해진 것으로 여기며, 크로퍼드의 누이라면 응당 알았어야 할 그 온갖 부끄러움과 비참함의 심경을 그녀에게 입혀, 그토록 누그러지고 그토록 헌신적인 마음 상태로 그녀에게 갔던지라, 패니의 두려움으로선 그것이 마지막이라는 것이 잠깐 어림없어졌다."], ["But as he proceeded in his story, these fears were over.", "그러나 그가 이야기를 이어 가자, 이 두려움은 걷혔다."], ["She had met him, he said, with a serious—certainly a serious—even an agitated air; but before he had been able to speak one intelligible sentence, she had introduced the subject in a manner which he owned had shocked him.", "그녀는 그를, 진지한—분명 진지한, 심지어 마음 졸이는 낯빛으로 맞았노라 그가 말했다. 그러나 그가 알아들을 만한 한 문장을 채 말하기도 전에, 그녀가 그 화제를, 저를 충격받게 했노라 그가 인정한 그런 식으로 꺼냈다."], ["“‘I heard you were in town,’ said she; ‘I wanted to see you.", "“‘시내에 오셨다는 걸 들었어요.’ 그녀가 말했어. ‘당신을 보고 싶었죠."], ["Let us talk over this sad business.", "이 서글픈 일을 함께 얘기해요."], ["What can equal the folly of our two relations?’ I could not answer, but I believe my looks spoke.", "우리 두 피붙이의 어리석음에 견줄 게 뭐가 있겠어요?’ 나는 대답할 수 없었지만, 내 낯빛이 말했으리라 믿는다."], ["She felt reproved.", "그녀는 나무람받았다 느꼈어."], ["Sometimes how quick to feel!", "이따금 어찌나 재빨리 느끼던지!"], ["With a graver look and voice she then added, ‘I do not mean to defend Henry at your sister’s expense.’ So she began, but how she went on, Fanny, is not fit, is hardly fit to be repeated to you.", "그녀는 이내 한결 근엄한 낯빛과 목소리로 덧붙였어. ‘당신 누이를 희생시켜 헨리를 두둔하려는 건 아니에요.’ 그렇게 시작했지만, 패니야, 그녀가 어떻게 이어 갔는지는 너한테 옮기기에 맞잖다, 좀처럼 맞잖아."], ["I cannot recall all her words.", "나는 그녀의 온갖 말을 다 떠올릴 수 없어."], ["I would not dwell upon them if I could.", "떠올릴 수 있다 해도 거기 마음 두고 싶지 않고."], ["Their substance was great anger at the folly of each.", "그 알맹이는 저마다의 어리석음에 대한 큰 노여움이었어."], ["She reprobated her brother’s folly in being drawn on by a woman whom he had never cared for, to do what must lose him the woman he adored; but still more the folly of poor Maria, in sacrificing such a situation, plunging into such difficulties, under the idea of being really loved by a man who had long ago made his indifference clear.", "그녀는, 저가 한 번도 마음 두지 않은 여자한테 끌려, 저가 흠모하던 여자를 잃을밖에 없는 짓을 저지른 제 오라비의 어리석음을 나무랐어. 하지만 그보다 더, 그토록 좋은 처지를 희생하고 그런 어려움에 뛰어든 딱한 마리아의 어리석음을—진작 제 무심함을 뚜렷이 밝힌 사내한테 정말로 사랑받고 있다는 생각에서 그리한 어리석음을 나무랐지."], ["Guess what I must have felt.", "내가 무엇을 느꼈을지 짐작해 보렴."], ["To hear the woman whom—no harsher name than folly given! So voluntarily, so freely, so coolly to canvass it!", "어리석음 말고는 더 모진 이름 하나 붙이지 않고서—그 여자를 그토록 스스로, 그토록 거리낌 없이, 그토록 차갑게 도마에 올리는 걸 듣는다는 걸!"], ["No reluctance, no horror, no feminine, shall I say, no modest loathings?", "아무 꺼림도, 아무 소름도, 아무 여자다운, 뭐랄까, 아무 얌전한 역겨움도 없이!"], ["This is what the world does.", "이게 세상이 하는 짓이야."], ["For where, Fanny, shall we find a woman whom nature had so richly endowed?", "패니, 자연이 그토록 넉넉히 갖춰 준 여자를 우리가 대체 어디서 찾겠니?"], ["Spoilt, spoilt!”", "망가졌어, 망가졌다니까!”"]], [["After a little reflection, he went on with a sort of desperate calmness.", "잠깐 헤아린 뒤, 그는 일종의 절망적인 차분함으로 말을 이었다."], ["“I will tell you everything, and then have done for ever.", "“너한테 죄다 얘기하고, 그러고 나면 영영 그만하마."], ["She saw it only as folly, and that folly stamped only by exposure.", "그녀는 그것을 오직 어리석음으로만, 그것도 오직 드러남으로만 못 박힌 어리석음으로만 봤어."], ["The want of common discretion, of caution: his going down to Richmond for the whole time of her being at Twickenham; her putting herself in the power of a servant; it was the detection, in short—oh, Fanny!", "여느 분별의, 조심의 모자람—그가 그녀가 트위크넘에 있는 내내 리치먼드로 내려간 것, 그녀가 저를 하인의 손아귀에 내맡긴 것. 요컨대 그건 들킴이었어—아, 패니!"], ["it was the detection, not the offence, which she reprobated.", "그녀가 나무란 건 들킴이었지, 죄가 아니었어."], ["It was the imprudence which had brought things to extremity, and obliged her brother to give up every dearer plan in order to fly with her.”", "일을 극단으로 몰아 그 오라비로 하여금 더 소중한 온갖 계획을 버리고 그녀와 함께 달아나게 만든 그 경솔함이었단 말이다.”"]], [["He stopt.", "그가 말을 멈췄다."], ["“And what,”", "“그래서,”"], ["said Fanny (believing herself required to speak), “what could you say?”", "패니가 (저더러 말하라는 줄 여기며) 말했다. “뭐라고—뭐라고 하셨어요, 오빠?”"]], [["“Nothing, nothing to be understood.", "“아무 말도, 알아들을 만한 아무 말도 못 했어."], ["I was like a man stunned.", "나는 얼이 빠진 사람 같았지."], ["She went on, began to talk of you; yes, then she began to talk of you, regretting, as well she might, the loss of such a—.", "그녀는 말을 이었고, 네 얘길 꺼내기 시작했어. 그래, 그때 그녀가 네 얘길 꺼내며, 마땅히 그럴 만하게, 그런—을 잃은 것을 서운해했어."], ["There she spoke very rationally.", "거기서 그녀는 자못 조리 있게 말했지."], ["But she has always done justice to you.", "하지만 그녀는 늘 너를 온당히 봐줬어."], ["‘He has thrown away,’ said she, ‘such a woman as he will never see again.", "‘그이는,’ 그녀가 말했어. ‘다시는 못 볼 그런 여자를 내던진 거예요."], ["She would have fixed him; she would have made him happy for ever.’ My dearest Fanny, I am giving you, I hope, more pleasure than pain by this retrospect of what might have been—but what never can be now.", "그 여자라면 그이를 붙들었을 거고, 그이를 영영 행복하게 해 줬을 거예요.’ 사랑하는 패니야, 나는 있을 수 있었던—그러나 이제 결코 있을 수 없는 것을 이렇게 되짚음으로써, 너한테 아픔보다 기쁨을 더 주고 있기를 바란다."], ["You do not wish me to be silent?", "내가 잠잠하기를 바라진 않지?"], ["If you do, give me but a look, a word, and I have done.”", "만일 그렇다면 눈길이든 말 한마디든 다오, 그럼 나는 그만하마.”"]], [["No look or word was given.", "아무 눈길도 말도 주어지지 않았다."]], [["“Thank God,” said he.", "“하느님 고맙게도,”"], ["“We were all disposed to wonder, but it seems to have been the merciful appointment of Providence that the heart which knew no guile should not suffer.", "그가 말했다. “우리 다 의아해할 만했지만, 아무 속임수도 모르던 그 마음이 괴로워하지 않은 건 아무래도 섭리의 자비로운 정하심이었나 봐."], ["She spoke of you with high praise and warm affection; yet, even here, there was alloy, a dash of evil; for in the midst of it she could exclaim, ‘Why would not she have him?", "그녀는 너를 드높은 칭찬과 따뜻한 정으로 얘기했어. 그러나 여기서마저 티가, 얼마간의 악의 섞임이 있었으니, 그 한복판에서 그녀가 이렇게 외칠 수 있었거든."], ["It is all her fault. Simple girl!", "‘왜 그 여자는 그이를 안 받았을까요?"], ["I shall never forgive her.", "죄다 그 여자 잘못이에요. 어리석은 애 같으니!"], ["Had she accepted him as she ought, they might now have been on the point of marriage, and Henry would have been too happy and too busy to want any other object.", "나는 결코 그 애를 용서 못 해요. 마땅히 그래야 할 대로 그이를 받아들였다면, 그들은 지금쯤 혼인을 코앞에 뒀을 테고, 헨리는 너무 행복하고 너무 바빠서 다른 어떤 대상도 바라지 않았을 거예요."], ["He would have taken no pains to be on terms with Mrs. Rushworth again.", "그이는 러시워스 부인과 다시 가까워지려 애쓰지 않았을 거고요."], ["It would have all ended in a regular standing flirtation, in yearly meetings at Sotherton and Everingham.’ Could you have believed it possible?", "그건 죄다 예사로운 붙박이 시시덕거림으로, 소더턴과 에버링엄에서의 해마다의 만남으로 끝났을 텐데.’ 이게 있을 수 있는 일이라 믿을 수 있었겠니?"], ["But the charm is broken.", "하지만 그 홀림은 깨졌어."], ["My eyes are opened.”", "내 눈이 뜨였다.”"]], [["“Cruel!”", "“모질어요!”"], ["said Fanny, “quite cruel.", "패니가 말했다. “정말 모질어요."], ["At such a moment to give way to gaiety, to speak with lightness, and to you!", "하필 그런 순간에 명랑함에 젖어, 가벼이 말하다니, 그것도 오빠한테!"], ["Absolute cruelty.”", "아주 모진 짓이에요.”"]], [["“Cruelty, do you call it?", "“모진 짓이라고?"], ["We differ there.", "거기선 우리 생각이 다르구나."], ["No, hers is not a cruel nature.", "아니야, 그녀의 천성은 모질지 않아."], ["I do not consider her as meaning to wound my feelings.", "나는 그녀가 내 심경을 다치게 할 뜻이었다곤 여기지 않는다."], ["The evil lies yet deeper: in her total ignorance, unsuspiciousness of there being such feelings; in a perversion of mind which made it natural to her to treat the subject as she did.", "그 화는 훨씬 더 깊은 데 있어. 그런 심경이 있다는 것을 그녀가 아주 모르고, 조금도 짐작 못 한다는 데, 그 화제를 그런 식으로 다루는 걸 그녀에게 자연스럽게 만든 그 뒤틀린 마음에 말이다."], ["She was speaking only as she had been used to hear others speak, as she imagined everybody else would speak.", "그녀는 그저 남들이 말하는 걸 익히 들어 온 대로, 다른 누구든 다 그리 말하리라 여기는 대로 말했을 뿐이야."], ["Hers are not faults of temper.", "그녀의 것은 성미의 흠이 아니야."], ["She would not voluntarily give unnecessary pain to any one, and though I may deceive myself, I cannot but think that for me, for my feelings, she would—.", "그녀는 누구에게든 쓸데없는 아픔을 자진해 주진 않을 거야. 그리고 내가 스스로를 속이는지 몰라도, 나로서는, 내 심경을 위해서라면 그녀가—라고 여길밖에 없어."], ["Hers are faults of principle, Fanny; of blunted delicacy and a corrupted, vitiated mind.", "그녀의 것은 지조의 흠이야, 패니. 무디어진 섬세함과, 물들고 상한 마음의 흠 말이다."], ["Perhaps it is best for me, since it leaves me so little to regret.", "어쩌면 나한텐 그게 가장 나은지도 모르지, 서운해할 게 그토록 적게 남으니까."], ["Not so, however.", "하지만 그렇지도 않아."], ["Gladly would I submit to all the increased pain of losing her, rather than have to think of her as I do.", "나는 그녀를 지금 이리 여겨야 하느니, 차라리 그녀를 잃는 온갖 더한 아픔에 기꺼이 굽히겠어."], ["I told her so.”", "나는 그녀한테 그리 말했다.”"]], [["“Did you?”", "“그러셨어요?”"]], [["“Yes; when I left her I told her so.”", "“그래. 그녀를 떠날 때 그리 말했어.”"]], [["“How long were you together?”", "“얼마나 함께 계셨어요?”"]], [["“Five-and-twenty minutes.", "“이십오 분."], ["Well, she went on to say that what remained now to be done was to bring about a marriage between them.", "자, 그녀는 이어서, 이제 남은 할 일은 둘을 혼인시키는 것이라 말했어."], ["She spoke of it, Fanny, with a steadier voice than I can.”", "그녀는 그것을, 패니, 나보다 더 진득한 목소리로 얘기했지.”"], ["He was obliged to pause more than once as he continued.", "그는 말을 이으며 한 번 넘게 멈춰야 했다."], ["“‘We must persuade Henry to marry her,’ said she; ‘and what with honour, and the certainty of having shut himself out for ever from Fanny, I do not despair of it.", "“‘우리는 헨리더러 그 여자와 혼인하라 설득해야 해요.’ 그녀가 말했어. ‘그리고 명예로 보나, 저가 패니한테서 영영 스스로를 막아 버린 게 확실한 걸로 보나, 나는 그걸 단념하지 않아요."], ["Fanny he must give up.", "패니는 그이가 단념할밖에 없죠."], ["I do not think that even he could now hope to succeed with one of her stamp, and therefore I hope we may find no insuperable difficulty.", "나는 그이조차 이제 그 애 같은 됨됨이의 여자한테 뜻을 이루기를 바랄 순 없으리라 여기니, 넘을 수 없는 어려움은 없으려니 바라요."], ["My influence, which is not small shall all go that way; and when once married, and properly supported by her own family, people of respectability as they are, she may recover her footing in society to a certain degree.", "작지 않은 내 영향력이 죄다 그리로 갈 거예요. 그리고 일단 혼인하고, 어엿한 사람들인 제 집안의 온당한 뒷받침을 받으면, 그 여자도 사교계에서 얼마간 제 발판을 되찾을 수 있어요."], ["In some circles, we know, she would never be admitted, but with good dinners, and large parties, there will always be those who will be glad of her acquaintance; and there is, undoubtedly, more liberality and candour on those points than formerly.", "어떤 무리에선, 우리도 알다시피, 결코 안 받아들여지겠지만, 좋은 만찬과 큰 모임이 있으면 늘 그 여자와 낯을 트고 반길 이들이 있기 마련이죠. 그리고 이런 점에선 두말할 것 없이 예전보다 더 너그럽고 트인 데가 있고요."], ["What I advise is, that your father be quiet.", "내가 권하는 건, 당신 아버지가 가만히 계시는 거예요."], ["Do not let him injure his own cause by interference.", "참견으로 제 일을 그르치게 놔두지 말아요."], ["Persuade him to let things take their course.", "일이 제 갈 길로 가게 두라고 설득해요."], ["If by any officious exertions of his, she is induced to leave Henry’s protection, there will be much less chance of his marrying her than if she remain with him.", "만일 그이가 무슨 부산한 애씀으로 그 여자를 헨리의 보호에서 떠나게 만들면, 그이가 그 여자와 혼인할 가망은 그 여자가 그이한테 남는 것보다 훨씬 줄어들어요."], ["I know how he is likely to be influenced.", "나는 그이가 어떻게 휘둘리기 쉬운지 알거든요."], ["Let Sir Thomas trust to his honour and compassion, and it may all end well; but if he get his daughter away, it will be destroying the chief hold.’”", "토머스 경더러 제 명예와 연민에 기대게 놔두면 다 잘 끝날 수도 있어요. 하지만 그이가 딸을 데려가면, 그건 그 으뜸가는 붙듦을 무너뜨리는 거예요.’”"]], [["After repeating this, Edmund was so much affected that Fanny, watching him with silent, but most tender concern, was almost sorry that the subject had been entered on at all.", "이것을 되풀이하고 나서 에드먼드가 하도 사무쳐하니, 잠잠하나 더없이 애틋한 근심으로 그를 지켜보던 패니는 이 화제를 아예 꺼낸 것이 거의 서러울 지경이었다."], ["It was long before he could speak again.", "그가 다시 말할 수 있기까지 한참이 걸렸다."], ["At last, “Now, Fanny,”", "마침내, “자, 패니야,”"], ["said he, “I shall soon have done.", "그가 말했다. “이제 이내 그만하마."], ["I have told you the substance of all that she said.", "나는 너한테 그녀가 한 온갖 말의 알맹이를 얘기했다."], ["As soon as I could speak, I replied that I had not supposed it possible, coming in such a state of mind into that house as I had done, that anything could occur to make me suffer more, but that she had been inflicting deeper wounds in almost every sentence.", "나는 겨우 말할 수 있게 되자, 내가 그런 마음 상태로 그 집에 들어와서, 나를 더 괴롭게 할 무슨 일이 생기리라곤 여기지 못했건만, 그녀가 거의 문장마다 더 깊은 상처를 입히고 있었노라 대꾸했어."], ["That though I had, in the course of our acquaintance, been often sensible of some difference in our opinions, on points, too, of some moment, it had not entered my imagination to conceive the difference could be such as she had now proved it.", "우리가 사귀는 동안 나도 곧잘 우리 의견에, 그것도 얼마간 중한 점들에서 얼마간 다름이 있음을 느끼긴 했으나, 그 다름이 그녀가 이제 증명한 그런 것일 수 있으리라곤 내 상상에 떠오르지 않았노라고."], ["That the manner in which she treated the dreadful crime committed by her brother and my sister (with whom lay the greater seduction I pretended not to say), but the manner in which she spoke of the crime itself, giving it every reproach but the right; considering its ill consequences only as they were to be braved or overborne by a defiance of decency and impudence in wrong; and last of all, and above all, recommending to us a compliance, a compromise, an acquiescence in the continuance of the sin, on the chance of a marriage which, thinking as I now thought of her brother, should rather be prevented than sought; all this together most grievously convinced me that I had never understood her before, and that, as far as related to mind, it had been the creature of my own imagination, not Miss Crawford, that I had been too apt to dwell on for many months past.", "제 오라비와 내 누이가 저지른 그 소름 끼치는 죄(누가 더 크게 꾀었는지야 나는 말하지 않으련다만)를 다루는 그 태도, 곧 그 죄 자체를 두고 말하며 온갖 나무람을 다 하되 옳은 나무람만은 안 하고, 그 나쁜 결말을, 오직 그름에서의 뻔뻔함과 예절 무시로써 무릅쓰거나 눌러 내야 할 것으로만 여기고, 그리고 무엇보다 맨 마지막으로, 이제 내가 그 오라비를 여기는 대로라면 오히려 막아야 할 혼인의 가망을 걸고, 그 죄가 이어지는 걸 순순히 받아들이고 타협하고 눈감으라 우리에게 권한 그 태도—이 모두가 함께, 나는 여태 그녀를 결코 헤아린 적이 없었음을, 그리고 마음에 관한 한, 내가 지난 여러 달 너무 쉬 마음 둔 것은 크로퍼드 양이 아니라 내 상상이 지어낸 이였음을 더없이 서럽게 나를 확신시켰노라고."], ["That, perhaps, it was best for me; I had less to regret in sacrificing a friendship, feelings, hopes which must, at any rate, have been torn from me now.", "어쩌면 나한텐 그게 가장 나은지도 모른다고, 아무튼 이제 나한테서 뜯겨 나갈밖에 없었던 우정과 심경과 바람을 희생하는 데 서운해할 게 덜하니까."], ["And yet, that I must and would confess that, could I have restored her to what she had appeared to me before, I would infinitely prefer any increase of the pain of parting, for the sake of carrying with me the right of tenderness and esteem.", "그러면서도, 만일 그녀를 앞서 나한테 비쳤던 그 모습으로 되돌릴 수 있다면, 애틋함과 존경의 권리를 지니고 가기 위해, 나는 헤어짐의 아픔이 아무리 더해져도 한없이 그편이 낫겠노라 인정할밖에, 인정하고 싶노라고. 이게 내가 한 말이야, 그 취지지."], ["This is what I said, the purport of it; but, as you may imagine, not spoken so collectedly or methodically as I have repeated it to you.", "하지만 짐작하다시피, 내가 너한테 되풀이한 것처럼 그리 차분하거나 조리 있게 말하진 못했어. 그녀는 놀랐어, 몹시 놀랐지—놀람 이상이었어."], ["She was astonished, exceedingly astonished—more than astonished.", "나는 그녀의 낯빛이 바뀌는 걸 봤다. 그녀는 몹시 붉어졌어."], ["I saw her change countenance. She turned extremely red.", "나는 여러 심경이 뒤섞인 걸 본 것 같았어."], ["I imagined I saw a mixture of many feelings: a great, though short struggle; half a wish of yielding to truths, half a sense of shame, but habit, habit carried it.", "크나큰, 그러나 짧은 몸부림, 진실에 굽히고픈 마음 절반, 부끄러움의 자각 절반이. 하지만 버릇이, 버릇이 이겼어."], ["She would have laughed if she could.", "그녀는 웃을 수 있었다면 웃었을 거야."], ["It was a sort of laugh, as she answered, ‘A pretty good lecture, upon my word.", "일종의 웃음 비슷한 것과 함께 그녀가 대답했지. ‘참으로 훌륭한 설교로군요, 정말."], ["Was it part of your last sermon?", "지난번 설교의 한 대목이었나요?"], ["At this rate you will soon reform everybody at Mansfield and Thornton Lacey; and when I hear of you next, it may be as a celebrated preacher in some great society of Methodists, or as a missionary into foreign parts.’ She tried to speak carelessly, but she was not so careless as she wanted to appear.", "이대로라면 당신은 이내 맨스필드와 손턴 레이시의 온갖 사람을 뜯어고치겠어요. 그리고 다음에 당신 소식을 듣거든, 그건 무슨 대단한 감리교 무리의 이름난 설교자로서든, 외국으로 가는 선교사로서든이겠죠.’ 그녀는 아무렇지 않게 말하려 했지만, 보이려던 만큼 아무렇지 않진 않았어."], ["I only said in reply, that from my heart I wished her well, and earnestly hoped that she might soon learn to think more justly, and not owe the most valuable knowledge we could any of us acquire, the knowledge of ourselves and of our duty, to the lessons of affliction, and immediately left the room.", "나는 그저 답으로, 진심으로 그녀가 잘되기를 빈다고, 그녀가 이내 더 올바로 생각하는 법을 배워, 우리 누구나 얻을 수 있는 가장 값진 앎—저 자신과 제 도리에 대한 앎을 괴로움의 가르침에 빚지지 않기를 간절히 바란다고 말하고, 대번에 방을 나섰어."], ["I had gone a few steps, Fanny, when I heard the door open behind me.", "몇 걸음 갔을 때, 패니야, 뒤에서 문이 열리는 소리가 들렸다."], ["‘Mr. Bertram,’ said she.", "‘버트럼 씨.’ 그녀가 말했어."], ["I looked back. ‘Mr.", "나는 돌아봤지."], ["Bertram,’ said she, with a smile; but it was a smile ill-suited to the conversation that had passed, a saucy playful smile, seeming to invite in order to subdue me; at least it appeared so to me.", "‘버트럼 씨.’ 그녀가 미소와 함께 말했어. 하지만 그건 방금 오간 대화엔 안 맞는 미소, 나를 굴복시키려 꾀는 듯한 앙큼하고 장난스러운 미소였어."], ["I resisted; it was the impulse of the moment to resist, and still walked on.", "적어도 나한텐 그리 보였다. 나는 뿌리쳤어."], ["I have since, sometimes, for a moment, regretted that I did not go back, but I know I was right, and such has been the end of our acquaintance.", "뿌리치는 게 그 순간의 충동이었고, 그대로 걸어갔어. 그 뒤로 이따금, 한순간, 돌아가지 않은 걸 서운해한 적도 있지만, 내가 옳았음을 나는 안다."], ["And what an acquaintance has it been!", "그리고 우리 사귐의 끝이 그러했어. 그런데 그게 대체 무슨 사귐이었더냐!"], ["How have I been deceived!", "내가 얼마나 속았는지!"], ["Equally in brother and sister deceived!", "오라비에게든 누이에게든 똑같이 속았어!"], ["I thank you for your patience, Fanny.", "네 참을성이 고맙다, 패니."], ["This has been the greatest relief, and now we will have done.”", "이게 더없이 큰 위안이었어. 자, 이제 그만하자.”"]], [["And such was Fanny’s dependence on his words, that for five minutes she thought they had done.", "그리고 그의 말에 대한 패니의 믿음이 하도 컸던지라, 오 분 동안 그녀는 그들이 정말 그만한 줄 여겼다."], ["Then, however, it all came on again, or something very like it, and nothing less than Lady Bertram’s rousing thoroughly up could really close such a conversation.", "그러나 그러다 그것이 죄다 다시 밀려왔으니, 아니 그와 자못 비슷한 것이 밀려왔으니, 버트럼 부인이 속속들이 깨어나는 것 말고는 그런 대화를 정말로 끝맺을 수 없었다."], ["Till that happened, they continued to talk of Miss Crawford alone, and how she had attached him, and how delightful nature had made her, and how excellent she would have been, had she fallen into good hands earlier.", "그리되기까지, 그들은 오직 크로퍼드 양만을, 그녀가 그를 어떻게 사로잡았는지, 자연이 그녀를 얼마나 흐뭇하게 지어냈는지, 좀 더 일찍 좋은 손에 들었더라면 그녀가 얼마나 훌륭했을지를 얘기해 갔다."], ["Fanny, now at liberty to speak openly, felt more than justified in adding to his knowledge of her real character, by some hint of what share his brother’s state of health might be supposed to have in her wish for a complete reconciliation.", "이제 터놓고 말할 수 있게 된 패니는, 톰의 건강 상태가 온전한 화해에 대한 그녀의 바람에 얼마나 몫을 했으려니 여겨질지를 얼마간 넌지시 비침으로써, 그녀의 참된 됨됨이에 대한 그의 앎에 보태는 것이 떳떳함 이상이라 느꼈다."], ["This was not an agreeable intimation.", "이것은 달가운 알림이 아니었다."], ["Nature resisted it for a while.", "천성이 한동안 그것에 맞섰다."], ["It would have been a vast deal pleasanter to have had her more disinterested in her attachment; but his vanity was not of a strength to fight long against reason.", "그녀의 애착이 좀 더 사심 없는 것이었더라면 훨씬 더 흐뭇했으련만. 그러나 그의 허영은 이치에 맞서 오래 싸울 만큼 세지 않았다."], ["He submitted to believe that Tom’s illness had influenced her, only reserving for himself this consoling thought, that considering the many counteractions of opposing habits, she had certainly been more attached to him than could have been expected, and for his sake been more near doing right.", "그는 톰의 병이 그녀에게 영향을 미쳤음을 믿는 데 굽혔으니, 다만 저 자신을 위해 이 위안 어린 생각만은 남겨 두었다—맞서는 버릇의 그 숱한 어긋냄을 헤아리면, 그녀는 분명 바랄 수 있었던 것보다 그를 더 애착했고, 그를 위해 옳은 일을 하는 데 더 가까이 갔었다는 생각을."], ["Fanny thought exactly the same; and they were also quite agreed in their opinion of the lasting effect, the indelible impression, which such a disappointment must make on his mind.", "패니도 딱 같은 생각이었다. 그리고 그런 실망이 그의 마음에 남길 게 틀림없는 그 오래가는 여파, 그 지워지지 않는 자국에 대한 소견에서도 둘은 아주 뜻이 같았다."], ["Time would undoubtedly abate somewhat of his sufferings, but still it was a sort of thing which he never could get entirely the better of; and as to his ever meeting with any other woman who could—it was too impossible to be named but with indignation.", "시간이 두말할 것 없이 그의 괴로움을 얼마간 덜어 줄 테지만, 그래도 그것은 그가 결코 아주 이겨 낼 수 없는 그런 종류의 것이었다. 그리고 그가 언제고—할 수 있는 다른 어떤 여자를 만나는 것으로 말하면—그것은 노여움 없이는 입에 담기엔 너무 어림없는 일이었다."], ["Fanny’s friendship was all that he had to cling to.", "패니의 우정이 그가 매달릴 수 있는 전부였다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLVIII", "# 제48장"]], [["Let other pens dwell on guilt and misery.", "죄와 비참함에는 다른 펜들이 마음을 두게 하자."], ["I quit such odious subjects as soon as I can, impatient to restore everybody, not greatly in fault themselves, to tolerable comfort, and to have done with all the rest.", "나는 그런 밉살스러운 화제는 되도록 빨리 접으련다, 저 자신에게 크게 잘못이 없는 이들을 죄다 웬만한 편안함으로 되돌리고 나머지는 죄다 끝내고 싶어 못내 조바심 나니 말이다."]], [["My Fanny, indeed, at this very time, I have the satisfaction of knowing, must have been happy in spite of everything.", "정말이지 내 패니는, 바로 이때에, 그 온갖 것에도 불구하고 행복했을 게 틀림없음을 나는 흡족히 안다."], ["She must have been a happy creature in spite of all that she felt, or thought she felt, for the distress of those around her.", "그녀는 제 둘레 사람들의 괴로움을 두고 저가 느낀, 아니 느낀다 여긴 그 온갖 것에도 불구하고 행복한 이였을 게 틀림없다."], ["She had sources of delight that must force their way.", "그녀에게는 스스로 뚫고 나올밖에 없는 즐거움의 샘들이 있었다."], ["She was returned to Mansfield Park, she was useful, she was beloved; she was safe from Mr.", "그녀는 맨스필드 파크로 돌아왔고, 쓸모가 되었고, 사랑받았다. 크로퍼드 씨에게서 안전했다."], ["Crawford; and when Sir Thomas came back she had every proof that could be given in his then melancholy state of spirits, of his perfect approbation and increased regard; and happy as all this must make her, she would still have been happy without any of it, for Edmund was no longer the dupe of Miss Crawford.", "그리고 토머스 경이 돌아왔을 때, 그녀는 그때의 그 서글픈 마음 상태에서 그가 줄 수 있는 온갖 증거로, 그의 온전한 찬동과 더해진 마음을 얻었다. 이 온갖 것이 그녀를 아무리 행복하게 했을지언정, 그녀는 그중 아무것도 없이도 여전히 행복했으리라, 에드먼드가 더는 크로퍼드 양의 속는 이가 아니었으니 말이다."]], [["It is true that Edmund was very far from happy himself.", "정말이지 에드먼드 자신은 행복과는 자못 거리가 멀었다."], ["He was suffering from disappointment and regret, grieving over what was, and wishing for what could never be.", "그는 실망과 서운함에 괴로워하며, 있었던 것을 슬퍼하고 결코 있을 수 없는 것을 바랐다."], ["She knew it was so, and was sorry; but it was with a sorrow so founded on satisfaction, so tending to ease, and so much in harmony with every dearest sensation, that there are few who might not have been glad to exchange their greatest gaiety for it.", "그녀는 그러함을 알았고 안쓰러웠다. 그러나 그것은, 흡족함에 그토록 바탕하고, 안도로 그토록 기울고, 온갖 더없이 소중한 심경과 그토록 어우러진 슬픔이라, 저의 가장 큰 명랑함을 그것과 바꾸어도 좋아하지 않을 이가 드물었으리라."]], [["Sir Thomas, poor Sir Thomas, a parent, and conscious of errors in his own conduct as a parent, was the longest to suffer.", "토머스 경은, 딱한 토머스 경은, 어버이로서, 그리고 어버이로서 제 처신의 잘못을 자각하는 이로서, 가장 오래 괴로워하는 이였다. 그는 그 혼사를 허락하지 말았어야 했다 느꼈다."], ["He felt that he ought not to have allowed the marriage; that his daughter’s sentiments had been sufficiently known to him to render him culpable in authorising it; that in so doing he had sacrificed the right to the expedient, and been governed by motives of selfishness and worldly wisdom.", "딸의 심경이 저에게 넉넉히 알려져 있었으니 그것을 승낙한 데 저의 죄가 있다고, 그리함으로써 저가 옳음을 편의에 희생하고 이기심과 세속적 슬기의 동기에 휘둘렸다고 느꼈다. 이것은 누그러지려면 얼마간의 시간이 걸릴 반성이었다."], ["These were reflections that required some time to soften; but time will do almost everything; and though little comfort arose on Mrs. Rushworth’s side for the misery she had occasioned, comfort was to be found greater than he had supposed in his other children.", "그러나 시간은 거의 온갖 것을 해내는 법이라, 러시워스 부인 쪽에서는 그녀가 자아낸 비참함에 대해 위안이 별로 일지 않았을망정, 다른 자식들에게서는 그가 여긴 것보다 더 큰 위안을 찾을 만했다."], ["Julia’s match became a less desperate business than he had considered it at first.", "줄리아의 혼사는 그가 처음에 여긴 것보다 덜 절망적인 일이 되었다."], ["She was humble, and wishing to be forgiven; and Mr.", "그녀는 겸손해져 용서받고 싶어 했다."], ["Yates, desirous of being really received into the family, was disposed to look up to him and be guided.", "그리고 예이츠 씨는, 정말로 집안에 받아들여지고 싶어, 그를 우러러보며 이끌려는 쪽이었다."], ["He was not very solid; but there was a hope of his becoming less trifling, of his being at least tolerably domestic and quiet; and at any rate, there was comfort in finding his estate rather more, and his debts much less, than he had feared, and in being consulted and treated as the friend best worth attending to.", "그는 자못 진득하지는 못했으나, 덜 실없어지고, 적어도 웬만큼은 가정적이고 조용해지리라는 바람이 있었다. 그리고 아무튼, 그의 재산이 그가 두려워한 것보다 오히려 좀 더 많고 그의 빚이 훨씬 적음을 알게 된 데, 그리고 귀 기울일 값어치가 가장 큰 벗으로 의논받고 대접받는 데 위안이 있었다."], ["There was comfort also in Tom, who gradually regained his health, without regaining the thoughtlessness and selfishness of his previous habits.", "톰에게도 위안이 있었으니, 그는 차츰 건강을 되찾되 예전 버릇의 그 생각 없음과 이기심은 되찾지 않았다."], ["He was the better for ever for his illness.", "그는 제 병 덕에 영영 나아졌다."], ["He had suffered, and he had learned to think: two advantages that he had never known before; and the self-reproach arising from the deplorable event in Wimpole Street, to which he felt himself accessory by all the dangerous intimacy of his unjustifiable theatre, made an impression on his mind which, at the age of six-and-twenty, with no want of sense or good companions, was durable in its happy effects.", "그는 괴로워했고, 생각하는 법을 배웠으니—여태 한 번도 알지 못한 두 이점이었다. 그리고 윔폴 스트리트의 그 개탄스러운 일—저가 두둔할 수 없는 그 연극의 온갖 위험한 가까움으로 저 자신이 한몫 거들었다 느낀 그 일에서 우러난 자책은, 지각이나 좋은 벗이 모자라지 않은 스물여섯의 나이에, 그 흐뭇한 여파가 오래가는 그런 자국을 그의 마음에 남겼다."], ["He became what he ought to be: useful to his father, steady and quiet, and not living merely for himself.", "그는 마땅히 되어야 할 사람이 되었다. 제 아버지에게 쓸모가 되고, 진득하고 조용하며, 그저 저 자신만을 위해 살지 않는 사람이."]], [["Here was comfort indeed!", "여기 정말 위안이 있었다!"], ["and quite as soon as Sir Thomas could place dependence on such sources of good, Edmund was contributing to his father’s ease by improvement in the only point in which he had given him pain before—improvement in his spirits.", "그리고 토머스 경이 그런 좋음의 샘들에 기댈 수 있게 되기 무섭게, 에드먼드는, 앞서 그에게 아픔을 주던 유일한 점—곧 그의 기운의 나아짐으로 아버지의 편안함에 보태고 있었다."], ["After wandering about and sitting under trees with Fanny all the summer evenings, he had so well talked his mind into submission as to be very tolerably cheerful again.", "여름 저녁 내내 패니와 함께 거닐고 나무 아래 앉아 있은 끝에, 그는 제 마음을 자못 잘 다독여 굽히게 하여 다시 웬만큼 명랑해졌다."]], [["These were the circumstances and the hopes which gradually brought their alleviation to Sir Thomas, deadening his sense of what was lost, and in part reconciling him to himself; though the anguish arising from the conviction of his own errors in the education of his daughters was never to be entirely done away.", "이런 것들이, 차츰 토머스 경에게 그 덜어짐을 가져다주어, 잃은 것에 대한 그의 사무침을 무디게 하고, 그를 얼마간 저 자신과 화해시킨 사정이자 바람이었다. 하기야 딸들의 교육에서 저 자신이 저지른 잘못에 대한 확신에서 우러난 그 고통은 결코 아주 없어질 수 없었지만."]], [["Too late he became aware how unfavourable to the character of any young people must be the totally opposite treatment which Maria and Julia had been always experiencing at home, where the excessive indulgence and flattery of their aunt had been continually contrasted with his own severity.", "너무 늦게야 그는, 마리아와 줄리아가 집에서 늘 겪던 그 아주 정반대인 대우—이모의 지나친 무름과 아첨이 저 자신의 엄함과 끊임없이 대비되던 그것이, 어떤 젊은이의 됨됨이에든 얼마나 안 좋을 게 틀림없는지를 깨달았다."], ["He saw how ill he had judged, in expecting to counteract what was wrong in Mrs.", "그는 노리스 부인에게 잘못된 것을 저 자신의 그 반대로 눅이려 여긴 것이 얼마나 그릇된 판단이었는지를 보았다."], ["Norris by its reverse in himself; clearly saw that he had but increased the evil by teaching them to repress their spirits in his presence so as to make their real disposition unknown to him, and sending them for all their indulgences to a person who had been able to attach them only by the blindness of her affection, and the excess of her praise.", "그가 딸들더러 제 앞에서 기운을 억누르게 가르쳐 그 참된 성미를 저에게 모르게 만들고, 오직 제 정의 눈멂과 제 칭찬의 지나침으로만 그들을 붙들 수 있던 사람에게 온갖 응석을 부리러 보냄으로써, 그저 그 화를 키웠을 뿐임을 또렷이 보았다."]], [["Here had been grievous mismanagement; but, bad as it was, he gradually grew to feel that it had not been the most direful mistake in his plan of education.", "여기 서러운 잘못된 건사가 있었다. 그러나 그것이 아무리 나빴어도, 그는 차츰 그것이 제 교육 계획에서 가장 끔찍한 잘못은 아니었음을 느끼게 되었다."], ["Something must have been wanting within, or time would have worn away much of its ill effect.", "무언가가 안에서 모자랐던 게 틀림없었으니, 안 그랬다면 시간이 그 나쁜 여파의 많은 것을 닳아 없앴으리라."], ["He feared that principle, active principle, had been wanting; that they had never been properly taught to govern their inclinations and tempers by that sense of duty which can alone suffice.", "그는 지조가, 살아 움직이는 지조가 모자랐던 게 아닌가 두려웠다. 그들이, 오직 그것만으로 넉넉한 그 도리의 자각으로 제 성향과 성미를 다스리도록 제대로 가르쳐진 적이 없었던 게 아닌가 두려웠다."], ["They had been instructed theoretically in their religion, but never required to bring it into daily practice.", "그들은 제 신앙을 이론으로야 배웠으되, 그것을 날마다 실천하도록 요구받은 적이 결코 없었다."], ["To be distinguished for elegance and accomplishments, the authorised object of their youth, could have had no useful influence that way, no moral effect on the mind.", "그들 젊음의 그 정해진 목표인, 우아함과 교양으로 돋보이는 것은, 그 방면에 아무 쓸모 있는 영향도, 마음에 아무 도덕적 효험도 못 미쳤으리라."], ["He had meant them to be good, but his cares had been directed to the understanding and manners, not the disposition; and of the necessity of self-denial and humility, he feared they had never heard from any lips that could profit them.", "그는 그들이 착하기를 뜻했으나, 그의 마음씀은 성미가 아니라 슬기와 몸가짐으로 쏠렸다. 그리고 자기 부정과 겸손의 필요에 대해서는, 그들이 저희에게 이로울 만한 어떤 입에서도 들어 본 적이 없는 게 아닌가 그는 두려웠다."]], [["Bitterly did he deplore a deficiency which now he could scarcely comprehend to have been possible.", "이제 그로서는 좀처럼 있을 수 있었으리라 헤아리기도 어려운 그 모자람을 그는 쓰라리게 개탄했다."], ["Wretchedly did he feel, that with all the cost and care of an anxious and expensive education, he had brought up his daughters without their understanding their first duties, or his being acquainted with their character and temper.", "애타고 값비싼 교육의 그 온갖 비용과 마음씀에도, 저가 딸들을 그 첫째가는 도리도 헤아리지 못하게, 그리고 저 자신은 그들의 됨됨이와 성미를 알지도 못하게 길렀음을 그는 비참하게 느꼈다."]], [["The high spirit and strong passions of Mrs. Rushworth, especially, were made known to him only in their sad result.", "특히 러시워스 부인의 그 드높은 기개와 세찬 열정은, 오직 그 서글픈 결말에서야 그에게 알려졌다."], ["She was not to be prevailed on to leave Mr. Crawford.", "그녀는 크로퍼드 씨를 떠나도록 구슬려지지 않았다."], ["She hoped to marry him, and they continued together till she was obliged to be convinced that such hope was vain, and till the disappointment and wretchedness arising from the conviction rendered her temper so bad, and her feelings for him so like hatred, as to make them for a while each other’s punishment, and then induce a voluntary separation.", "그녀는 그와 혼인하기를 바랐고, 둘은 함께 지냈다—그런 바람이 헛됨을 그녀가 하는 수 없이 확신하기까지, 그리고 그 확신에서 우러난 실망과 비참함이 그녀의 성미를 하도 나쁘게, 그에 대한 그녀의 심경을 미움과 하도 비슷하게 만들어, 한동안 둘이 서로의 벌이 되게 하고, 이내 자진한 헤어짐을 불러오기까지."]], [["She had lived with him to be reproached as the ruin of all his happiness in Fanny, and carried away no better consolation in leaving him than that she had divided them.", "그녀는 그와 살면서 패니를 향한 그의 온갖 행복을 무너뜨린 원흉으로 나무람받았고, 그를 떠나며 저가 둘을 갈라놓았다는 것 말고는 더 나은 위안을 가져가지 못했다."], ["What can exceed the misery of such a mind in such a situation?", "그런 처지의 그런 마음의 비참함에 견줄 것이 무엇이랴?"]], [["Mr. Rushworth had no difficulty in procuring a divorce; and so ended a marriage contracted under such circumstances as to make any better end the effect of good luck not to be reckoned on.", "러시워스 씨는 이혼을 얻어 내는 데 아무 어려움이 없었다."], ["She had despised him, and loved another; and he had been very much aware that it was so.", "그리하여, 더 나은 결말이라면 무엇이든 기대할 수 없는 요행의 덕일 만한 그런 사정 아래 맺어진 혼인이 끝났다."], ["The indignities of stupidity, and the disappointments of selfish passion, can excite little pity.", "그녀는 그를 업신여기고 다른 이를 사랑했으며, 그도 그러함을 자못 잘 알고 있었다. 어리석음의 그 수모와 이기적 열정의 그 실망은 좀처럼 딱함을 자아낼 수 없다."], ["His punishment followed his conduct, as did a deeper punishment the deeper guilt of his wife.", "그의 벌은 그의 처신을 뒤따랐고, 더 깊은 벌이 그 아내의 더 깊은 죄를 뒤따랐듯이. 그는 그 약속에서 풀려나, 무안하고 불행한 채로 있다가, 어떤 다른 예쁜 처녀가 다시 그를 혼인으로 꾈 수 있을 때까지—그리하여 그 상태에 대한 두 번째의, 그리고 바라건대 더 잘되는 시도에 나설 수 있을 때까지 그리 지낼 터였다."], ["He was released from the engagement to be mortified and unhappy, till some other pretty girl could attract him into matrimony again, and he might set forward on a second, and, it is to be hoped, more prosperous trial of the state: if duped, to be duped at least with good humour and good luck; while she must withdraw with infinitely stronger feelings to a retirement and reproach which could allow no second spring of hope or character.", "속더라도 적어도 좋은 기분과 좋은 운으로 속을 터였다. 반면 그녀는 한없이 더 세찬 심경을 안고, 바람이나 됨됨이의 두 번째 봄을 허락하지 않을 은둔과 나무람으로 물러날밖에 없었다."]], [["Where she could be placed became a subject of most melancholy and momentous consultation.", "그녀를 어디에 둘까가 더없이 서글프고 중대한 의논거리가 되었다."], ["Mrs. Norris, whose attachment seemed to augment with the demerits of her niece, would have had her received at home and countenanced by them all.", "노리스 부인은, 그 정이 조카딸의 흠과 함께 더해지는 듯한 이라, 그녀가 집에 받아들여져 다들의 두둔을 받게 했으리라."], ["Sir Thomas would not hear of it; and Mrs.", "토머스 경은 그것을 들으려 하지 않았다."], ["Norris’s anger against Fanny was so much the greater, from considering her residence there as the motive.", "그리고 노리스 부인의 패니에 대한 노여움은, 그녀가 거기 사는 것을 그 동기로 여겨 한층 더 커졌다. 그녀는 그의 거리낌을 그녀 탓으로 돌리기를 고집했다."], ["She persisted in placing his scruples to her account, though Sir Thomas very solemnly assured her that, had there been no young woman in question, had there been no young person of either sex belonging to him, to be endangered by the society or hurt by the character of Mrs. Rushworth, he would never have offered so great an insult to the neighbourhood as to expect it to notice her.", "하기야 토머스 경은, 만일 문제 될 젊은 여자가 없었더라면, 러시워스 부인의 사귐에 위험해지거나 그 됨됨이에 해를 입을 만한, 저에게 딸린 어떤 성(性)의 젊은 사람도 없었더라면, 그가 이웃더러 그녀를 아는 체하기를 바라는 그런 큰 모욕을 결코 이 고장에 안겼을 리 없다고 자못 엄숙히 그녀에게 다짐했지만."], ["As a daughter, he hoped a penitent one, she should be protected by him, and secured in every comfort, and supported by every encouragement to do right, which their relative situations admitted; but farther than that he could not go.", "딸로서, 그가 바라건대 뉘우치는 딸로서, 그녀는 그의 보호를 받고, 온갖 편안함을 누리고, 그들의 처지가 허락하는 한 옳게 처신하라는 온갖 북돋움의 뒷받침을 받을 터였다. 그러나 그 이상으로는 그가 나아갈 수 없었다."], ["Maria had destroyed her own character, and he would not, by a vain attempt to restore what never could be restored, by affording his sanction to vice, or in seeking to lessen its disgrace, be anywise accessory to introducing such misery in another man’s family as he had known himself.", "마리아는 제 됨됨이를 스스로 무너뜨렸고, 그는, 결코 되돌릴 수 없는 것을 되돌리려는 헛된 시도로써, 악덕에 제 승인을 보탬으로써, 아니 그 치욕을 덜려 애씀으로써, 저 자신이 겪었던 것 같은 그런 비참함을 다른 이의 집안에 들이는 데 어떤 식으로든 한몫 거들지 않으려 했다."]], [["It ended in Mrs.", "그것은 노리스 부인이 맨스필드를 떠나 제 불운한 마리아에게 몸을 바치기로 마음먹는 것으로, 그리고 그들을 위해 다른 고장, 멀고 외진 곳에 거처가 마련되는 것으로 끝났다."], ["Norris’s resolving to quit Mansfield and devote herself to her unfortunate Maria, and in an establishment being formed for them in another country, remote and private, where, shut up together with little society, on one side no affection, on the other no judgment, it may be reasonably supposed that their tempers became their mutual punishment.", "거기서 사교라곤 별로 없이 함께 갇혀, 한쪽엔 정이 없고 다른 쪽엔 분별이 없으니, 그 성미가 서로의 벌이 되었으려니 웬만큼 여길 만하다."]], [["Mrs. Norris’s removal from Mansfield was the great supplementary comfort of Sir Thomas’s life.", "노리스 부인이 맨스필드를 떠난 것은 토머스 경의 삶에 더해진 큰 위안이었다."], ["His opinion of her had been sinking from the day of his return from Antigua: in every transaction together from that period, in their daily intercourse, in business, or in chat, she had been regularly losing ground in his esteem, and convincing him that either time had done her much disservice, or that he had considerably over-rated her sense, and wonderfully borne with her manners before.", "그녀에 대한 그의 생각은 앤티가에서 돌아온 날부터 가라앉고 있었다. 그때부터 함께한 온갖 일에서, 나날의 사귐에서, 볼일에서든 잡담에서든, 그녀는 그의 존중에서 꼬박꼬박 자리를 잃으며, 그로 하여금 시간이 그녀에게 큰 해를 끼쳤거나, 아니면 그가 앞서 그녀의 지각을 자못 지나치게 값매기고 그녀의 태도를 놀랍도록 참아 왔던 것임을 확신하게 했다."], ["He had felt her as an hourly evil, which was so much the worse, as there seemed no chance of its ceasing but with life; she seemed a part of himself that must be borne for ever.", "그는 그녀를 시시각각의 화로 느껴 왔는데, 그것이 목숨과 함께라야 그칠 낌새밖에 없어 보이니 한층 더 나빴다. 그녀는 영영 견뎌야 할 저 자신의 일부인 듯했다."], ["To be relieved from her, therefore, was so great a felicity that, had she not left bitter remembrances behind her, there might have been danger of his learning almost to approve the evil which produced such a good.", "그러니 그녀에게서 놓이는 것이 하도 큰 기쁨이라, 만일 그녀가 쓰라린 기억을 뒤에 남기지 않았더라면, 그가 그런 좋음을 낳은 그 화를 거의 갸륵히 여기는 법을 배울 위험마저 있었으리라."]], [["She was regretted by no one at Mansfield.", "맨스필드에서 그녀를 아쉬워한 이는 아무도 없었다."], ["She had never been able to attach even those she loved best; and since Mrs.", "그녀는 저가 가장 사랑한 이들조차 붙들지 못했다."], ["Rushworth’s elopement, her temper had been in a state of such irritation as to make her everywhere tormenting.", "그리고 러시워스 부인의 사랑의 도피 이래로, 그녀의 성미는 하도 부아 돋은 상태라 어디서나 사람을 볶아 댔다."], ["Not even Fanny had tears for aunt Norris, not even when she was gone for ever.", "패니조차 노리스 이모를 위해 흘릴 눈물이 없었으니, 그녀가 영영 떠났을 때조차 그러했다."]], [["That Julia escaped better than Maria was owing, in some measure, to a favourable difference of disposition and circumstance, but in a greater to her having been less the darling of that very aunt, less flattered and less spoilt.", "줄리아가 마리아보다 더 잘 벗어난 것은, 얼마간은 성미와 사정의 다행한 다름 덕이었으나, 더 크게는 그녀가 바로 그 이모의 귀염둥이가 덜었던 것, 덜 아첨받고 덜 버릇 나빠진 것 덕이었다."], ["Her beauty and acquirements had held but a second place.", "그녀의 미모와 재주는 둘째 자리를 차지했을 뿐이었다."], ["She had been always used to think herself a little inferior to Maria.", "그녀는 늘 저를 마리아보다 조금 못하다 여기는 데 익어 있었다."], ["Her temper was naturally the easiest of the two; her feelings, though quick, were more controllable, and education had not given her so very hurtful a degree of self-consequence.", "그녀의 성미는 천성으로 둘 중 가장 느긋했고, 그 심경은, 재빠르되 더 다스릴 만했으며, 교육이 그녀에게 그토록 해로운 정도의 자부심을 주지는 않았다."]], [["She had submitted the best to the disappointment in Henry Crawford.", "그녀는 헨리 크로퍼드에 대한 실망에 가장 잘 굽혔다."], ["After the first bitterness of the conviction of being slighted was over, she had been tolerably soon in a fair way of not thinking of him again; and when the acquaintance was renewed in town, and Mr.", "무시당했다는 확신의 그 첫 쓰라림이 지나자, 그녀는 웬만큼 이내 그를 다시 생각지 않을 순탄한 길에 들어섰다."], ["Rushworth’s house became Crawford’s object, she had had the merit of withdrawing herself from it, and of chusing that time to pay a visit to her other friends, in order to secure herself from being again too much attracted.", "그리고 시내에서 그 사귐이 되살아나 러시워스 씨의 집이 크로퍼드의 목표가 되었을 때, 그녀는 거기서 저를 물리고, 다시 너무 끌리는 데서 저를 지키려 하필 그때를 잡아 다른 벗들을 찾아가는 갸륵함이 있었다."], ["This had been her motive in going to her cousin’s.", "이것이 그녀가 사촌에게 간 동기였다."], ["Mr. Yates’s convenience had had nothing to do with it.", "예이츠 씨의 편의야 그것과 아무 상관이 없었다."], ["She had been allowing his attentions some time, but with very little idea of ever accepting him; and had not her sister’s conduct burst forth as it did, and her increased dread of her father and of home, on that event, imagining its certain consequence to herself would be greater severity and restraint, made her hastily resolve on avoiding such immediate horrors at all risks, it is probable that Mr.", "그녀는 한동안 그의 마음씀을 받아 주고 있긴 했으나, 언제고 그를 받아들일 생각은 별로 없었다. 그리고 만일 그녀 언니의 처신이 그렇게 터지지 않았더라면, 그리고 그 일에 대한 아버지와 집에 대한 그녀의 더해진 두려움—그것이 저 자신에게 낳을 확실한 결말은 더한 엄함과 잡도리이려니 여겨, 무슨 위험을 무릅쓰고라도 그런 당장의 소름을 피하기로 서둘러 마음먹게 만든 그 두려움이 아니었더라면, 아마 예이츠 씨는 결코 뜻을 못 이뤘으리라."], ["Yates would never have succeeded. She had not eloped with any worse feelings than those of selfish alarm.", "그녀는 이기적인 놀람 말고는 더 나쁜 심경으로 도피한 것이 아니었다."], ["It had appeared to her the only thing to be done.", "그것이 그녀에겐 할 수 있는 유일한 일로 보였다."], ["Maria’s guilt had induced Julia’s folly.", "마리아의 죄가 줄리아의 어리석음을 불러온 것이다."]], [["Henry Crawford, ruined by early independence and bad domestic example, indulged in the freaks of a cold-blooded vanity a little too long.", "헨리 크로퍼드는, 이른 독립과 나쁜 집안의 본보기에 망가져, 냉혈의 허영의 그 변덕을 조금 너무 오래 부렸다."], ["Once it had, by an opening undesigned and unmerited, led him into the way of happiness.", "언젠가 그것은, 뜻하지도 받을 만하지도 않은 열림으로써, 그를 행복의 길로 이끈 적이 있었다."], ["Could he have been satisfied with the conquest of one amiable woman’s affections, could he have found sufficient exultation in overcoming the reluctance, in working himself into the esteem and tenderness of Fanny Price, there would have been every probability of success and felicity for him.", "만일 그가 한 상냥한 여자의 정을 얻은 것으로 흡족할 수 있었다면, 만일 패니 프라이스의 꺼림을 이겨 내어 그 존경과 애틋함으로 파고드는 데서 넉넉한 우쭐함을 찾을 수 있었다면, 그에게는 성공과 행복의 온갖 가망이 있었으리라."], ["His affection had already done something.", "그의 정은 이미 얼마간을 이룬 참이었다."], ["Her influence over him had already given him some influence over her.", "그에 대한 그녀의 힘이 이미 그에게 그녀에 대한 얼마간의 힘을 준 것이다."], ["Would he have deserved more, there can be no doubt that more would have been obtained, especially when that marriage had taken place, which would have given him the assistance of her conscience in subduing her first inclination, and brought them very often together.", "만일 그가 더 받을 만했더라면, 더 많은 것이 얻어졌으리라는 데 의심의 여지가 없다, 특히 그 혼인이 이뤄져 그가 그녀의 첫 성향을 눅이는 데 그녀 양심의 도움을 얻고, 둘을 자못 자주 함께 있게 했을 때는."], ["Would he have persevered, and uprightly, Fanny must have been his reward, and a reward very voluntarily bestowed, within a reasonable period from Edmund’s marrying Mary.", "만일 그가, 그것도 올곧게 물러서지 않았더라면, 패니는 그의 보답이 될밖에 없었으리라, 그것도 에드먼드가 메리와 혼인하고 얼마 안 되어 자못 자진해 주어진 보답이."]], [["Had he done as he intended, and as he knew he ought, by going down to Everingham after his return from Portsmouth, he might have been deciding his own happy destiny.", "만일 그가 뜻한 대로, 또 마땅히 해야 할 줄 안 대로, 포츠머스에서 돌아온 뒤 에버링엄으로 내려갔더라면, 그는 저 자신의 행복한 운명을 정하고 있었을지도 모른다. 그러나 그는 프레이저 부인의 모임을 위해 머물라 조름받았다."], ["But he was pressed to stay for Mrs.", "그의 머묾은 우쭐한 중함을 띠었고, 그는 거기서 러시워스 부인을 만나게 되어 있었다."], ["Fraser’s party; his staying was made of flattering consequence, and he was to meet Mrs. Rushworth there.", "궁금함과 허영이 둘 다 걸렸고, 당장의 즐거움의 유혹이, 옳음을 위해 무슨 희생도 하는 데 안 익은 마음에는 너무 셌다."], ["Curiosity and vanity were both engaged, and the temptation of immediate pleasure was too strong for a mind unused to make any sacrifice to right: he resolved to defer his Norfolk journey, resolved that writing should answer the purpose of it, or that its purpose was unimportant, and staid.", "그는 노퍽행을 미루기로, 편지가 그 목적을 대신하리라, 아니 그 목적이 대수롭잖으리라 마음먹고, 머물렀다. 그는 러시워스 부인을 봤고, 그녀에게서, 마땅히 그를 밀쳐 내고 둘 사이에 겉보기 무심함을 영영 세웠어야 할 그런 냉랭함으로 맞아졌다."], ["He saw Mrs.", "그러나 그는 무안했다."], ["Rushworth, was received by her with a coldness which ought to have been repulsive, and have established apparent indifference between them for ever; but he was mortified, he could not bear to be thrown off by the woman whose smiles had been so wholly at his command: he must exert himself to subdue so proud a display of resentment; it was anger on Fanny’s account; he must get the better of it, and make Mrs.", "그 미소가 그토록 온전히 제 손아귀에 있던 여자에게 내쳐지는 것을 그는 견딜 수 없었다. 그는 그토록 오만하게 드러난 원망을 눅이려 애써야 했다."], ["Rushworth Maria Bertram again in her treatment of himself.", "그것은 패니 때문에 난 노여움이었다. 그는 그것을 이겨 내어, 저 자신을 대하는 태도에서 러시워스 부인을 다시 마리아 버트럼으로 만들어야 했다."]], [["In this spirit he began the attack, and by animated perseverance had soon re-established the sort of familiar intercourse, of gallantry, of flirtation, which bounded his views; but in triumphing over the discretion which, though beginning in anger, might have saved them both, he had put himself in the power of feelings on her side more strong than he had supposed.", "이런 마음으로 그는 공략을 시작했고, 생기 어린 끈질김으로 이내, 제 뜻이 그은 그 한계인 그런 격의 없는 사귐, 정겨움, 시시덕거림을 다시 세워 놓았다. 그러나 노여움에서 비롯했으되 둘 다를 구할 수도 있었을 그 분별을 이기고 우쭐하는 사이에, 그는 저가 여긴 것보다 더 세찬 그녀 편 심경의 손아귀에 저를 내맡기고 말았다."], ["She loved him; there was no withdrawing attentions avowedly dear to her.", "그녀는 그를 사랑했다. 그녀에게 대놓고 소중한 그 마음씀을 거둘 길이 없었다."], ["He was entangled by his own vanity, with as little excuse of love as possible, and without the smallest inconstancy of mind towards her cousin.", "그는 되도록 사랑의 변명은 없이, 그리고 그녀 사촌을 향한 마음의 조금의 변덕도 없이, 저 자신의 허영에 얽혀 들었다."], ["To keep Fanny and the Bertrams from a knowledge of what was passing became his first object.", "무슨 일이 벌어지는지를 패니와 버트럼가가 모르게 하는 것이 그의 첫째가는 목표가 되었다."], ["Secrecy could not have been more desirable for Mrs. Rushworth’s credit than he felt it for his own.", "은밀함은 러시워스 부인의 명예를 위해서보다 저 자신을 위해 그가 더 바랄 만한 것이었다."], ["When he returned from Richmond, he would have been glad to see Mrs. Rushworth no more.", "리치먼드에서 돌아왔을 때, 그는 러시워스 부인을 다시는 안 봤으면 싶었으리라."], ["All that followed was the result of her imprudence; and he went off with her at last, because he could not help it, regretting Fanny even at the moment, but regretting her infinitely more when all the bustle of the intrigue was over, and a very few months had taught him, by the force of contrast, to place a yet higher value on the sweetness of her temper, the purity of her mind, and the excellence of her principles.", "그 뒤에 이어진 온갖 것은 그녀의 경솔함의 결과였다. 그리고 그는 끝내 그녀와 함께 달아났으니, 그럴밖에 없었기 때문이요, 그 순간에도 패니를 서운해하되, 그 밀회의 온갖 소란이 끝나고 아주 몇 달이 대조의 힘으로 그에게 그녀 성미의 고움과 그 마음의 순수함과 그 지조의 훌륭함을 한층 더 높이 값매기도록 가르쳤을 때 한없이 더 그녀를 서운해했다."]], [["That punishment, the public punishment of disgrace, should in a just measure attend his share of the offence is, we know, not one of the barriers which society gives to virtue.", "벌이, 곧 치욕이라는 세상의 벌이 그의 죄의 몫에 마땅한 만큼 따라야 한다는 것은, 우리가 알듯, 세상이 덕에 세워 주는 방벽 가운데 하나가 아니다."], ["In this world the penalty is less equal than could be wished; but without presuming to look forward to a juster appointment hereafter, we may fairly consider a man of sense, like Henry Crawford, to be providing for himself no small portion of vexation and regret: vexation that must rise sometimes to self-reproach, and regret to wretchedness, in having so requited hospitality, so injured family peace, so forfeited his best, most estimable, and endeared acquaintance, and so lost the woman whom he had rationally as well as passionately loved.", "이 세상에서 그 벌은 바랄 만한 것보다 덜 고르다. 그러나 훗날의 더 올바른 정하심을 감히 내다보려 들지 않더라도, 우리는 헨리 크로퍼드 같은 지각 있는 사내가 저 자신에게 적잖은 언짢음과 서운함을 마련해 두고 있으려니 온당히 여길 만하다—그토록 후한 대접을 그리 갚고, 집안의 평화를 그리 해치고, 저의 가장 좋고 가장 값지고 가장 정든 사귐을 그리 잃고, 저가 열정으로만큼이나 이치로도 사랑한 여자를 그리 잃음으로써, 때로 자책으로까지 치솟을밖에 없는 언짢음과 비참함으로까지 치솟을 서운함을."]], [["After what had passed to wound and alienate the two families, the continuance of the Bertrams and Grants in such close neighbourhood would have been most distressing; but the absence of the latter, for some months purposely lengthened, ended very fortunately in the necessity, or at least the practicability, of a permanent removal.", "두 집안을 다치게 하고 멀어지게 한 그 일이 있고 나서, 버트럼가와 그랜트가가 그토록 바짝 붙은 이웃으로 이어 지내는 것은 더없이 괴로웠으리라. 그러나 몇 달 일부러 늘어진 그랜트가의 부재가, 더없이 다행하게도 아주 옮겨 가는 것의 필요, 아니 적어도 그 될 만함으로 끝났다."], ["Dr. Grant, through an interest on which he had almost ceased to form hopes, succeeded to a stall in Westminster, which, as affording an occasion for leaving Mansfield, an excuse for residence in London, and an increase of income to answer the expenses of the change, was highly acceptable to those who went and those who staid.", "그랜트 박사는, 이제 거의 바람을 접다시피 한 어떤 연줄로, 웨스트민스터의 참사회원 자리를 이어받았는데, 그것은 맨스필드를 떠날 계기가 되고, 런던에 살 구실이 되고, 그 옮김의 비용을 감당할 수입의 늘음이 되는지라, 떠나는 이들에게나 남는 이들에게나 더없이 반가웠다."]], [["Mrs.", "그랜트 부인은, 사랑하고 사랑받을 성미라, 익숙하던 그 자리와 사람들을 얼마간 서운해하며 떠났을 게 틀림없다."], ["Grant, with a temper to love and be loved, must have gone with some regret from the scenes and people she had been used to; but the same happiness of disposition must in any place, and any society, secure her a great deal to enjoy, and she had again a home to offer Mary; and Mary had had enough of her own friends, enough of vanity, ambition, love, and disappointment in the course of the last half-year, to be in need of the true kindness of her sister’s heart, and the rational tranquillity of her ways.", "그러나 그 성미의 그 행복함은 어느 곳, 어느 사교에서든 그녀에게 누릴 것을 자못 많이 마련해 줄 게 틀림없었다. 그리고 그녀에게는 다시 메리에게 내밀 집이 있었다."], ["They lived together; and when Dr.", "그리고 메리는, 지난 반년 사이에 제 벗들에도, 허영과 야심과 사랑과 실망에도 진력난지라, 그 언니 마음의 참된 친절과 그 방식의 이치에 맞는 고요가 아쉬웠다. 둘은 함께 살았다."], ["Grant had brought on apoplexy and death, by three great institutionary dinners in one week, they still lived together; for Mary, though perfectly resolved against ever attaching herself to a younger brother again, was long in finding among the dashing representatives, or idle heir-apparents, who were at the command of her beauty, and her £20,000, any one who could satisfy the better taste she had acquired at Mansfield, whose character and manners could authorise a hope of the domestic happiness she had there learned to estimate, or put Edmund Bertram sufficiently out of her head.", "그리고 그랜트 박사가 한 주에 세 번의 큰 공식 만찬으로 졸중과 죽음을 불러왔을 때에도, 둘은 여전히 함께 살았다. 메리는, 다시는 결코 작은아들에게 정 붙이지 않기로 아주 굳게 마음먹었으면서도, 제 미모와 이만 파운드가 좌우할 수 있는 그 늠름한 명사들이나 한가한 상속자들 가운데서, 저가 맨스필드에서 얻은 그 더 나은 취향을 채워 줄 이, 그 됨됨이와 몸가짐이 저가 거기서 값매기는 법을 배운 그 가정의 행복에 대한 바람을 뒷받침해 줄 이, 아니 에드먼드 버트럼을 제 머리에서 넉넉히 몰아내 줄 이를 찾기까지 오래 걸렸던 것이다."]], [["Edmund had greatly the advantage of her in this respect.", "에드먼드는 이 점에서 그녀보다 자못 유리했다."], ["He had not to wait and wish with vacant affections for an object worthy to succeed her in them.", "그는 그녀를 이을 만한 대상을 텅 빈 정으로 기다리고 바랄 필요가 없었다."], ["Scarcely had he done regretting Mary Crawford, and observing to Fanny how impossible it was that he should ever meet with such another woman, before it began to strike him whether a very different kind of woman might not do just as well, or a great deal better: whether Fanny herself were not growing as dear, as important to him in all her smiles and all her ways, as Mary Crawford had ever been; and whether it might not be a possible, a hopeful undertaking to persuade her that her warm and sisterly regard for him would be foundation enough for wedded love.", "그가 메리 크로퍼드를 서운해하기를 겨우 마치고, 그런 다른 여자를 만나기란 얼마나 어림없는지를 패니에게 뇌기 무섭게, 자못 다른 종류의 여자도 딱 그만큼 잘, 아니 훨씬 더 잘 어울리지 않을까—패니 자신이, 그 온갖 미소와 그 온갖 방식에서, 메리 크로퍼드가 여태 그러했던 만큼이나 저에게 소중하고 중해지고 있지 않은가—그리고 그를 향한 그녀의 따뜻하고 누이다운 마음이 부부의 사랑의 넉넉한 바탕이 되리라 그녀를 설득하는 것이 있음 직한, 바람 있는 일이 아닐까 하는 생각이 그를 치기 시작한 것이다."]], [["I purposely abstain from dates on this occasion, that every one may be at liberty to fix their own, aware that the cure of unconquerable passions, and the transfer of unchanging attachments, must vary much as to time in different people.", "나는 이 자리에서 날짜를 일부러 안 밝히려는데, 이겨 낼 수 없는 열정의 나음과 변치 않는 애착의 옮김이 사람마다 그 시간이 자못 다를 게 틀림없음을 알기에, 다들 저 나름의 날짜를 정할 수 있도록이다."], ["I only entreat everybody to believe that exactly at the time when it was quite natural that it should be so, and not a week earlier, Edmund did cease to care about Miss Crawford, and became as anxious to marry Fanny as Fanny herself could desire.", "나는 그저 다들에게 이것만 믿기를 청한다, 딱 그리되는 게 지극히 자연스러운 그때에, 그보다 한 주도 이르지 않게, 에드먼드가 정말로 크로퍼드 양에 대한 마음을 그쳤고, 패니 자신이 바랄 만한 만큼이나 패니와 혼인하기를 못내 바라게 되었다는 것을."]], [["With such a regard for her, indeed, as his had long been, a regard founded on the most endearing claims of innocence and helplessness, and completed by every recommendation of growing worth, what could be more natural than the change?", "정말이지 그의 것처럼 오래된, 순진함과 힘없음이라는 더없이 정든 몫에 바탕하고, 자라 가는 값어치의 온갖 천거로 온전해진 그런 마음이라면, 그 바뀜보다 더 자연스러운 것이 무엇이랴?"], ["Loving, guiding, protecting her, as he had been doing ever since her being ten years old, her mind in so great a degree formed by his care, and her comfort depending on his kindness, an object to him of such close and peculiar interest, dearer by all his own importance with her than any one else at Mansfield, what was there now to add, but that he should learn to prefer soft light eyes to sparkling dark ones.", "그녀가 열 살이던 때부터 줄곧 해 온 대로 그녀를 사랑하고 이끌고 지켜 주며, 그녀의 마음이 그토록 크게 그의 마음씀으로 지어지고, 그녀의 편안함이 그의 친절에 기대고, 그에게 그토록 바짝하고 남다른 관심의 대상이며, 그녀와 얽힌 저 자신의 온갖 중함으로 하여 맨스필드의 다른 누구보다 소중했던 그녀이니, 이제 더할 것이 무엇이랴, 그가 반짝이는 검은 눈보다 부드러운 옅은 눈을 더 좋아하는 법을 배우는 것 말고는."], ["And being always with her, and always talking confidentially, and his feelings exactly in that favourable state which a recent disappointment gives, those soft light eyes could not be very long in obtaining the pre-eminence.", "그리고 늘 그녀와 함께 있고, 늘 속내를 터놓고 얘기하며, 그의 심경이 근래의 실망이 주는 딱 그 좋은 상태에 있으니, 그 부드러운 옅은 눈이 으뜸을 차지하기까지는 그리 오래 걸릴 수 없었다."]], [["Having once set out, and felt that he had done so on this road to happiness, there was nothing on the side of prudence to stop him or make his progress slow; no doubts of her deserving, no fears of opposition of taste, no need of drawing new hopes of happiness from dissimilarity of temper.", "일단 나서서, 저가 이 행복의 길에 나섰음을 느끼고 나니, 그를 멈추거나 그 나아감을 더디게 할 만한 것이 신중함 편에는 아무것도 없었다. 그녀가 받을 만함에 대한 미심쩍음도, 취향이 맞설까 하는 두려움도, 성미의 다름에서 행복의 새 바람을 끌어낼 필요도 없었다."], ["Her mind, disposition, opinions, and habits wanted no half-concealment, no self-deception on the present, no reliance on future improvement.", "그녀의 마음도, 성미도, 소견도, 버릇도, 절반의 감춤도, 지금에 대한 자기기만도, 앞날의 나아짐에 대한 기댐도 필요치 않았다."], ["Even in the midst of his late infatuation, he had acknowledged Fanny’s mental superiority.", "그 지난 홀림 한복판에서마저 그는 패니의 마음의 나음을 인정했었다."], ["What must be his sense of it now, therefore?", "그러니 이제 그것에 대한 그의 사무침이 어떠했으랴?"], ["She was of course only too good for him; but as nobody minds having what is too good for them, he was very steadily earnest in the pursuit of the blessing, and it was not possible that encouragement from her should be long wanting.", "그녀야 물론 그에게는 너무 과분한 사람일 뿐이었으나, 저에게 과분한 것을 갖는 걸 언짢아하는 이는 아무도 없는 법이라, 그는 자못 진득이 그 복을 좇는 데 진심이었고, 그녀에게서 오는 북돋움이 오래 모자랄 리 없었다."], ["Timid, anxious, doubting as she was, it was still impossible that such tenderness as hers should not, at times, hold out the strongest hope of success, though it remained for a later period to tell him the whole delightful and astonishing truth.", "그녀가 아무리 소심하고 애타고 미심쩍어했어도, 그녀 같은 그런 애틋함이 이따금 성공의 더없이 굳센 바람을 내비치지 않기란 여전히 어림없었다—하기야 그 온전히 흐뭇하고 놀라운 진실을 죄다 그에게 일러 주는 것은 좀 더 나중의 몫으로 남았지만."], ["His happiness in knowing himself to have been so long the beloved of such a heart, must have been great enough to warrant any strength of language in which he could clothe it to her or to himself; it must have been a delightful happiness.", "그토록 오래 그런 마음의 사랑받는 이였음을 아는 그의 행복은, 그가 그것을 그녀에게든 저 자신에게든 옷 입힐 수 있는 어떤 세찬 말이라도 마땅케 할 만큼 컸을 게 틀림없다. 그것은 흐뭇한 행복이었을 게 틀림없다."], ["But there was happiness elsewhere which no description can reach.", "그러나 어떤 묘사로도 닿을 수 없는 행복이 다른 데 있었다."], ["Let no one presume to give the feelings of a young woman on receiving the assurance of that affection of which she has scarcely allowed herself to entertain a hope.", "좀처럼 스스로 바람을 품도록 허락한 적조차 없는 그 정에 대한 다짐을 받는 젊은 여자의 심경을 감히 누구도 옮기려 들지 말라."]], [["Their own inclinations ascertained, there were no difficulties behind, no drawback of poverty or parent.", "저마다의 마음이 확인되니, 뒤에는 아무 어려움도, 가난이나 부모라는 아무 걸림도 없었다."], ["It was a match which Sir Thomas’s wishes had even forestalled.", "그것은 토머스 경의 바람이 오히려 미리 앞질러 놓은 혼사였다."], ["Sick of ambitious and mercenary connexions, prizing more and more the sterling good of principle and temper, and chiefly anxious to bind by the strongest securities all that remained to him of domestic felicity, he had pondered with genuine satisfaction on the more than possibility of the two young friends finding their natural consolation in each other for all that had occurred of disappointment to either; and the joyful consent which met Edmund’s application, the high sense of having realised a great acquisition in the promise of Fanny for a daughter, formed just such a contrast with his early opinion on the subject when the poor little girl’s coming had been first agitated, as time is for ever producing between the plans and decisions of mortals, for their own instruction, and their neighbours’ entertainment.", "야심 차고 돈 밝히는 인연에 진력나고, 지조와 성미라는 참된 좋음을 점점 더 값지게 여기며, 저에게 남은 가정의 온갖 행복을 가장 굳센 담보로 붙들어 매기를 무엇보다 애타게 바라던 그는, 그 두 젊은 벗이 저마다에게 닥친 온갖 실망에 대한 자연스러운 위안을 서로에게서 찾는 것의 그 있음 직함 이상의 것을 참된 흡족함으로 곰곰 헤아려 온 터였다. 그리하여 에드먼드의 청을 맞은 그 기쁜 승낙은, 패니를 며느리로 약속받음으로써 큰 얻음을 이뤘다는 그 드높은 사무침은, 딱한 어린 계집아이의 옴이 처음 논의되던 무렵 이 일에 대한 그의 첫 생각과 딱 그런 대조를 이뤘으니—시간이 인간의 계획과 결정 사이에, 저희 자신의 깨우침과 그 이웃의 재미를 위해 영영 지어내는 그런 대조를."]], [["Fanny was indeed the daughter that he wanted.", "패니는 정말이지 그가 바라던 딸이었다."], ["His charitable kindness had been rearing a prime comfort for himself.", "그의 자비로운 친절은 저 자신을 위한 으뜸가는 위안을 길러 온 셈이었다."], ["His liberality had a rich repayment, and the general goodness of his intentions by her deserved it.", "그의 너그러움은 넉넉한 갚음을 받았고, 그녀를 향한 그의 뜻의 두루 착함이 그럴 만했다."], ["He might have made her childhood happier; but it had been an error of judgment only which had given him the appearance of harshness, and deprived him of her early love; and now, on really knowing each other, their mutual attachment became very strong.", "그가 그녀의 어린 시절을 더 행복하게 해 줄 수도 있었으련만, 그에게 엄함의 겉모습을 씌우고 그녀의 어릴 적 사랑을 앗아 간 것은 오직 판단의 잘못이었을 뿐이었다. 그리고 이제, 서로를 정말로 알게 되니, 그들 서로의 애착은 자못 굳어졌다."], ["After settling her at Thornton Lacey with every kind attention to her comfort, the object of almost every day was to see her there, or to get her away from it.", "그녀를 손턴 레이시에 그 편안함에 대한 온갖 다정한 마음씀과 함께 자리 잡게 한 뒤, 거의 날마다의 목표가 거기서 그녀를 보거나, 그녀를 거기서 데려오는 것이었다."]], [["Selfishly dear as she had long been to Lady Bertram, she could not be parted with willingly by her.", "버트럼 부인에게 그녀가 오래도록 그토록 이기적으로 소중했던지라, 그녀로서는 그녀를 기꺼이 떠나보낼 수 없었다."], ["No happiness of son or niece could make her wish the marriage.", "아들이나 조카딸의 어떤 행복도 그녀로 하여금 그 혼인을 바라게 할 수 없었다."], ["But it was possible to part with her, because Susan remained to supply her place.", "그러나 그녀를 떠나보내는 것이 될 만했으니, 수전이 남아 그 자리를 채웠기 때문이다."], ["Susan became the stationary niece, delighted to be so; and equally well adapted for it by a readiness of mind, and an inclination for usefulness, as Fanny had been by sweetness of temper, and strong feelings of gratitude.", "수전이 붙박이 조카딸이 되었고, 그리되는 것이 기뻤다. 그리고 패니가 성미의 고움과 굳센 고마움의 심경으로 그에 알맞았던 것과 꼭 같이, 그녀도 재빠른 마음과 쓸모가 되려는 성향으로 그에 알맞았다."], ["Susan could never be spared.", "수전은 결코 없어선 안 될 이가 되었다."], ["First as a comfort to Fanny, then as an auxiliary, and last as her substitute, she was established at Mansfield, with every appearance of equal permanency.", "처음엔 패니의 위안으로, 다음엔 조력자로, 끝으로 그 자리를 잇는 이로, 그녀는 못지않게 붙박일 온갖 낌새와 함께 맨스필드에 자리 잡았다."], ["Her more fearless disposition and happier nerves made everything easy to her there.", "그녀의 더 겁 없는 성미와 더 행복한 신경이 거기서 그녀에게 온갖 것을 손쉽게 해 주었다."], ["With quickness in understanding the tempers of those she had to deal with, and no natural timidity to restrain any consequent wishes, she was soon welcome and useful to all; and after Fanny’s removal succeeded so naturally to her influence over the hourly comfort of her aunt, as gradually to become, perhaps, the most beloved of the two.", "상대해야 할 이들의 성미를 알아채는 재빠름과, 그에 따른 어떤 바람도 억누를 타고난 소심함이 없던지라, 그녀는 이내 다들에게 반가운, 쓸모 있는 이가 되었다. 그리고 패니가 떠난 뒤 이모의 시시각각의 편안함에 대한 그 힘을 하도 자연스레 이어받아, 차츰 어쩌면 둘 중 더 사랑받는 이가 되었다."], ["In her usefulness, in Fanny’s excellence, in William’s continued good conduct and rising fame, and in the general well-doing and success of the other members of the family, all assisting to advance each other, and doing credit to his countenance and aid, Sir Thomas saw repeated, and for ever repeated, reason to rejoice in what he had done for them all, and acknowledge the advantages of early hardship and discipline, and the consciousness of being born to struggle and endure.", "그녀의 쓸모에서, 패니의 훌륭함에서, 윌리엄의 이어지는 좋은 처신과 오르는 명성에서, 그리고 다들 서로를 밀어 주고 그의 보살핌과 도움에 값한, 다른 식구들의 두루 잘됨과 성공에서, 토머스 경은, 저가 그들 모두를 위해 한 일을 기뻐하고, 이른 고생과 단련의 이점을, 그리고 애쓰고 견디도록 태어났다는 자각의 이점을 인정할 까닭을 거듭, 영영 거듭 보았다."]], [["With so much true merit and true love, and no want of fortune and friends, the happiness of the married cousins must appear as secure as earthly happiness can be.", "그토록 많은 참된 값어치와 참된 사랑이 있고, 재산과 벗이 모자라지 않으니, 혼인한 두 사촌의 행복은 이 세상의 행복이 그럴 수 있는 만큼이나 든든해 보일밖에 없다."], ["Equally formed for domestic life, and attached to country pleasures, their home was the home of affection and comfort; and to complete the picture of good, the acquisition of Mansfield living, by the death of Dr.", "둘 다 똑같이 가정의 삶에 걸맞고 시골의 즐거움에 정든지라, 그들의 집은 애정과 편안함의 집이었다."], ["Grant, occurred just after they had been married long enough to begin to want an increase of income, and feel their distance from the paternal abode an inconvenience.", "그리고 그 좋음의 그림을 온전케 하듯, 그랜트 박사의 죽음으로 맨스필드 성직록을 얻은 것이, 그들이 수입의 늘음을 아쉬워하기 시작하고 아버지 집과의 거리를 불편으로 느낄 만큼 혼인한 지 오래된 바로 그때에 일어났다."]], [["On that event they removed to Mansfield; and the Parsonage there, which, under each of its two former owners, Fanny had never been able to approach but with some painful sensation of restraint or alarm, soon grew as dear to her heart, and as thoroughly perfect in her eyes, as everything else within the view and patronage of Mansfield Park had long been.", "그 일로 그들은 맨스필드로 옮겨 갔다. 그리고 거기 목사관은, 앞선 두 임자 저마다의 시절에 패니가 얼마간의 괴로운 잡힘이나 놀람의 심경 없이는 결코 다가갈 수 없던 그 목사관은, 이내 그녀의 마음에, 맨스필드 파크의 눈길과 두둔 안의 다른 온갖 것이 오래도록 그러했던 것만큼이나 소중하고, 그녀의 눈에 그만큼 속속들이 온전한 것이 되었다."]]];
